window.CARD_DATA = [
{"id":"wh-001","num":"1","category":"World · History","question":"Known only from what people left behind — tools, bones, hearths and cave art — <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers the human past before written records.","answer":"Prehistory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 3200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">until writing arrives locally</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Prehistory</b> is the stretch of the human past that comes before people in a given place began keeping written records of themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because there are no texts to read, archaeologists have to reconstruct it from the things people left behind — tools, bones, hearths and cave art — and from the ground those things were buried in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What makes a period prehistoric is simply the absence of writing, not how sophisticated its people were or how well they understood their world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a different way of dividing the past from the Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages, which are defined by the main material people used for tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two schemes can come apart: a society can be in the Iron Age and still count as prehistoric if it left no writing behind. <br><br> Prehistory is usually taken to begin with the first stone tools, made about 2.6 million years ago, although some researchers argue for an earlier starting point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has no single ending, because writing did not spread everywhere at once but reached different parts of the world at very different times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It appears in Mesopotamia and Egypt around 3200 BCE, considerably later in China, and in some regions only within the last few centuries, so the same calendar year can be prehistoric in one place and historical in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word itself can wrongly imply that people without writing had no history at all, when in fact they preserved and passed on their past through stories, songs and other spoken tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That implication was put to colonial use against the peoples it was applied to, and scholars who work on those histories now argue for retiring the word rather than applying it to anyone's ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Prehistory","questions":["Beginning with the first stone tools some 2.6 million years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no single ending, since writing reached Mesopotamia around 3200 BCE but other regions only recently.","Defined by the absence of writing rather than by any material, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can include an Iron Age society, and many who study those histories now argue the word should be retired altogether."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Gesa Mackenthun and Christen Mucher, “Introduction,” in <i>Decolonizing “Prehistory”: Deep Time and Indigenous Knowledges in North America</i>, ed. Gesa Mackenthun and Christen Mucher (Tucson: University of Arizona Press, 2021), 6–8, https://open.uapress.arizona.edu/projects/decolonizing-prehistory. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “Visible Language: The Earliest Writing Systems,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 15–17, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, “The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,” <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 2–3, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/PSM_V44_D647_Delineations_on_pieces_of_antler.jpg","title":"Prehistory","desc":"Delineations on pieces of antler. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V44_D647_Delineations_on_pieces_of_antler.jpg","alt":"PSM V44 D647 Delineations on pieces of antler"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why can the same calendar year be prehistoric in one place and historical in another?","a":"Because prehistory ends when a given place begins keeping written records, and writing did not spread everywhere at once: it appears in Mesopotamia and Egypt around 3200 BCE, considerably later in China, and in some regions only within the last few centuries."},{"q":"Why can a society be in the Iron Age and still count as prehistoric?","a":"Because the two schemes divide the past by different things. Prehistory is defined by the absence of writing, while the Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages are defined by the main material used for tools, so a people working iron who left no texts falls under both."},{"q":"Why do scholars now argue for retiring the word prehistory?","a":"Because it can wrongly imply that people without writing had no history at all, when in fact they preserved and passed on their past through stories, songs and other spoken tradition — an implication that was put to colonial use against the peoples it was applied to."}]},
{"id":"wh-002","num":"2","category":"World · History","question":"Sorting the deep past by the material used for tools, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arranges prehistory into three successive periods — Stone Age, Bronze Age, Iron Age.","answer":"Three-age system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1810s–1820s, by C. J. Thomsen</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Three-age system</b> is a framework that sorts the human past into three successive periods: the Stone Age, then the Bronze Age, then the Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each age takes its name from the material that dominated the making of cutting tools and weapons at the time, which makes technology rather than politics or dynasty the organising principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system is the whole ordering method, so the Stone Age is only its first period and not the system itself. What it delivers is a relative order — which finds came earlier and which came later — rather than exact calendar dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That limitation matters less than it sounds, because a sequence lets undated objects be slotted into place by what they are made of, so a store of unlabelled finds becomes a chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, a Danish curator, worked the scheme out in the early 1800s — one of three Scandinavian scholars whose relative orderings remade the subject — drawing his answer from the objects rather than from any written record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sorting a museum's collection, he saw that the oldest finds held stone tools but no metal, set the arrangement out in print in 1836, and his colleague Jens Jacob Worsaae then carried it across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient writers had imagined ages named for metals long before him, but Hesiod's races of gold, silver, bronze, heroes and iron are a tale of moral decline rather than a claim tested against evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the scheme was built from European and Near Eastern material, it does not fit everywhere, and John Lubbock, who extended it in English, wrote that he applied his own classification to Europe alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across much of Africa south of the Sahara iron appears early with little sign of bronze before it, and the term is largely missing from the archaeology of the Americas and Oceania too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Three-age system","questions":["Sorting a museum's collection, the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the objects themselves and set it out in print in 1836.","Because it gives only a relative order, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lets undated finds be placed by what they are made of, though it fits Europe far better than Africa or the Americas."],"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","science"],"sources":["Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, “Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,” in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 64–67, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]","Peter Rowley-Conwy, “The Three Age System in English: New Translations of the Founding Documents,” <i>Bulletin of the History of Archaeology</i> 14, no. 1 (2004): 4–15, esp. 4–5, https://doi.org/10.5334/bha.14102. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, “The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,” <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 2, 5, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Works and Days</i>, lines 109–201, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, in <i>Hesiod, the Homeric Hymns, and Homerica</i>, Project Gutenberg ebook 348, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/348/pg348.txt. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Julien Cooper and Yegor Grebnev, “Introduction: Bronze Age Civilizations,” <i>Old World: Journal of Ancient Africa and Eurasia</i> 3, no. 1 (2023): 3–4, https://doi.org/10.1163/26670755-20230009. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Thomsen.jpg","title":"Three-age system","desc":"Christian Jürgensen Thomsen (1777–1863) showing visitors around in the Danish National Museum. Contemporary drawing. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomsen.jpg","alt":"A contemporary drawing of Christian Jürgensen Thomsen holding up an object for a group of museum visitors, in a gallery lined with glass cases of arranged antiquities"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the three-age system give a relative order rather than calendar dates?","a":"Because it sorts finds by the material their cutting tools are made of, which says only what came earlier and what came later. That matters less than it sounds: a sequence lets undated objects be slotted into place by what they are made of."},{"q":"Why did Thomsen's scheme hold where Hesiod's ages of metal did not?","a":"Because Thomsen drew his answer from the objects rather than from any written record — sorting a museum's collection, he saw that the oldest finds held stone tools but no metal. Hesiod's races of gold, silver, bronze, heroes and iron are a tale of moral decline."},{"q":"Why does the system fit much of Africa poorly?","a":"Because it was built from European and Near Eastern material. Across much of Africa south of the Sahara iron appears early with little sign of bronze before it, so the middle age of the sequence has nothing to mark there."}]},
{"id":"wh-003","num":"3","category":"World · History","question":"First and longest of the three ages that archaeologists count as stone, bronze and iron, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the vast stretch of prehistory in which cutting tools were made chiefly from stone.","answer":"Stone Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 3300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Near East, later elsewhere</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Stone Age</b> is the earliest and longest period of human prehistory, the first of the three technological ages into which early material culture is traditionally sorted: stone, then bronze, then iron. It is a technological label rather than a fixed span of dates, defined by stone as the main material for tools with an edge, a point, or a striking surface. The scheme was devised in the 1820s by the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, who noticed while ordering the National Museum of Denmark that stone, bronze, and iron objects fell into a regular succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It therefore has no single worldwide beginning or end: it opened with the first shaped stone tools around 2.6 million years ago and closed only when a given people took up metalworking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That closing came at very different times from place to place: about 3300 BCE in the Near East, later across Europe and Asia, and, by the usual scheme, never in some regions. <br><br> Archaeologists divide it into three parts: the Paleolithic, the age of chipped stone, filling roughly 99 percent of it; the brief post-glacial Mesolithic; and the Neolithic, when farming, villages, pottery, and polished stone tools appeared. The names came later than the framework: John Lubbock coined 'Paleolithic' and 'Neolithic' in 1865, and 'Mesolithic' was added soon after to bridge the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its tools were the work of many kinds of human across almost 3 million years — early Homo, the far-ranging Homo erectus, the Neanderthals, and at last our own species. The great turning point near its end was the Neolithic Revolution, the shift to farming that began about 10,000 BCE in the Fertile Crescent and independently elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because it was built from European and Near Eastern finds, the scheme fits much of the world poorly — parts of Africa passed straight from stone to iron — and 'Stone Age' must never be used of any living people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Stone Age","questions":["Devised in the 1820s by the Danish curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first of the three technological ages, followed by bronze and then iron.","Archaeologists split the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the chipped-stone Paleolithic, the brief post-glacial Mesolithic, and the Neolithic, when farming, villages and polished stone tools first appeared."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Peter Rowley-Conwy, \"The Three Age System in English: New Translations of the Founding Documents,\" <i>Bulletin of the History of Archaeology</i> 14, no. 1 (2004): 4–15, esp. 4–5, https://doi.org/10.5334/bha.14102. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., \"3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,\" <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., \"Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under \"The Origins of Domestication,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, \"The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,\" <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 2–3, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Paulnabrone.jpg","title":"Stone Age","desc":"Poulnabrone Dolmen, County Clare, Ireland, downloaded from pdphoto.org, en:sr:Слика:Paulnabrone.jpg. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulnabrone.jpg","alt":"Paulnabrone"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why does the Stone Age close at a different date in different parts of the world?","a":"Because it is a technological label rather than a fixed span of dates: it closes only when a given people take up metalworking. That came about 3300 BCE in the Near East, later across Europe and Asia, and, by the usual scheme, never in some regions."},{"q":"Why does the three-age scheme fit much of the world poorly?","a":"Because it was built from European and Near Eastern finds, where stone gave way to bronze and then to iron. Parts of Africa passed straight from stone to iron, so the middle age of the sequence has nothing to mark there."},{"q":"Why does the Paleolithic take up almost the whole of the Stone Age?","a":"Because the Stone Age opened with the first shaped stone tools around 2.6 million years ago, and the Paleolithic, the age of chipped stone, fills roughly 99 percent of it — leaving the brief post-glacial Mesolithic and the Neolithic as the last of it."}]},
{"id":"wh-009","num":"9","category":"World · History","question":"Walking upright on two legs while still carrying a small, ape-sized brain, the African hominin <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> — the genus of 'Lucy' — lived from about 4.2 to 2 million years ago.","answer":"Australopithecus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4.2 – 2 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925, by Raymond Dart</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Australopithecus</b>, whose name means 'southern ape', was a genus of early hominins that lived across eastern and southern Africa from roughly 4.2 to 2 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Despite the name they were not apes in the ordinary sense, but members of our own evolutionary line, standing near its base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their defining feature was a striking blend of traits: they stood on two legs and regularly walked upright, yet kept small braincases of less than 500 cubic centimetres, about a third the size of ours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their arms stayed long and strong and their fingers curved, still adapted for climbing trees, so a single body carried a human-like lower half and an ape-like upper half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> This is why the genus matters so much: it shows that upright walking evolved long before large brains, overturning the old assumption that the brain came first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The anatomist Raymond Dart described and named the first of them in 1925 from the Taung child, a small skull found in South Africa the year before, whose braincase held only about 400 cubic centimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its most famous member is Lucy, a partial skeleton of Australopithecus afarensis some 3.2 million years old, unearthed at Hadar in Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Older still are the Laetoli footprints in Tanzania, about 3.66 million years old, a trail pressed into volcanic ash that catches these hominins walking plainly upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Small, with males averaging about 151 centimetres (4 ft 11 in) and females 105 (3 ft 5 in), and living mainly on leaves, fruit, seeds, roots, nuts and insects, they may be ancestral to Paranthropus, to later australopiths and to our own genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether any of them made stone tools is unresolved: flakes and cores from Lomekwi in Kenya are dated to 3.3 million years, older than any known Homo, but whether hominins shaped them at all has been contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Australopithecus","questions":["Named by Raymond Dart in 1925 for the Taung Child, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the genus whose long arms and curved fingers still suited climbing even as it walked upright.","Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> walked upright yet kept a brain a third the size of ours, it shows two-legged walking evolved long before large brains or stone tools."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Australopithecus anamensis</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-anamensis. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-africanus. [Open access]","Dean Falk et al., “Metopic Suture of Taung (<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>) and Its Implications for Hominin Brain Evolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 22 (2012): 8467–70, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3365196/. [Open access]","Fidelis T. Masao et al., “New Footprints from Laetoli (Tanzania) Provide Evidence for Marked Body Size Variation in Early Hominins,” <i>eLife</i> 5 (2016): e19568, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5156529/. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools and Butchery Traces? More Evidence Needed,” <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2016 (2016): 46–53, esp. 49 and 51, https://paleoanthro.org/media/journal/content/PA20160046.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Australopithecus_afarensisIMG_2930.JPG/1920px-Australopithecus_afarensisIMG_2930.JPG","title":"Australopithecus","desc":"A cast of the Australopithecus afarensis skull. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australopithecus_afarensisIMG_2930.JPG","alt":"Australopithecus afarensis skull"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why does Australopithecus matter so much to the study of human origins?","a":"Because it shows that upright walking evolved long before large brains. Its members walked habitually while keeping braincases under 500 cubic centimetres, about a third the size of ours, which overturned the old assumption that the brain came first."},{"q":"Why is the name southern ape misleading?","a":"Because they were not apes in the ordinary sense but members of our own evolutionary line, standing near its base — across eastern and southern Africa from roughly 4.2 to 2 million years ago."},{"q":"Why does one body seem to carry two different animals?","a":"Because the lower half is human-like and the upper half ape-like: they stood and walked on two legs, yet kept long strong arms and curved fingers still adapted for climbing trees."}]},
{"id":"wh-014","num":"14","category":"World · History","question":"In the badlands west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, the site of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded stone flakes struck about 3.3 million years ago, the oldest tools yet found.","answer":"Lomekwi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tools</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3.3 Mya</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lomekwi</b> is an archaeological site in the eroded badlands west of Lake Turkana in northern Kenya, where excavators uncovered the oldest stone tools yet known, struck about 3.3 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The spot catalogued as Lomekwi 3 yielded roughly 20 artefacts still sealed in the ground and about 130 more lying on the surface: cores, the sharp flakes struck from them, and heavy anvils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are not the neat, hand-sized pebble tools of later ages — the largest weighs some 15 kilograms (33 pounds), too heavy to swing, and was probably rested on the ground to hammer against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deposit was dated by its position between two layers of volcanic ash and by reversals of the Earth's magnetic field recorded in the sediment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That age pushed the origins of stone technology back roughly 700,000 years beyond the previous record from Gona in Ethiopia, and the excavators named the new industry the Lomekwian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The discovery was an accident: in July 2011 a team led by Sonia Harmand and Jason Lewis took a wrong turn while hunting for a fossil site and stopped on an unexplored hillside strewn with worked stone. One flake could be fitted back onto the core it was struck from, showing that a hominin had knapped stone on that very spot rather than the pieces washing in or breaking by chance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The workmanship looks experimental, battering a block against an anvil resting on the ground rather than using the controlled free-hand blow that defines the later Oldowan toolkit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since the oldest fossils of our own genus, Homo, are about half a million years younger, the makers were probably Australopithecus afarensis or Kenyanthropus platyops, whose flat-faced skull came from these same beds in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The claim is not settled: several archaeologists argue that the layers are dated less securely than reported and that natural forces can fracture stone in similar ways, so a second site of such age would settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lomekwi","questions":["The knapping at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks experimental, a block battered against an anvil resting on the ground rather than struck by the controlled free-hand blow of the later Oldowan.","Too old to be the work of our own genus, the giant cores of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were probably struck by Australopithecus afarensis or the flat-faced Kenyanthropus platyops."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","kenya"],"sources":["Sonia Harmand et al., \"3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,\" <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, \"3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools and Butchery Traces? More Evidence Needed,\" <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2016 (2016): 46–53, esp. 49 and 51, https://paleoanthro.org/media/journal/content/PA20160046.pdf. [Open access]","Thomas W. Plummer, Sonia Harmand, Emma M. Finestone, and Evan P. Wilson, \"The First Million Years of Technology: The Lomekwian and the Early Oldowan,\" <i>Annual Review of Anthropology</i> 54 (2025): 359–75, under \"Context and Characteristics of the Lomekwian Industry,\" https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10631785. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Kenyanthropus platyops</i>,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/kenyanthropus-platyops. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Kenyanthropus-Lomekwi.jpg","title":"Lomekwi","desc":"Lomekwi (Kenya), place of discovery of Kenyanthropus platyops. Chartep, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenyanthropus-Lomekwi.jpg","alt":"Kenyanthropus-Lomekwi"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Lomekwi","at":[35.7508,3.8742]},"why":[{"q":"Why does a flake that refits onto its core show more than the tools alone would?","a":"Because fitting a flake back onto the core it was struck from shows that a hominin knapped stone on that very spot, rather than the pieces washing in from elsewhere or breaking by chance."},{"q":"Why are the makers of these tools thought not to belong to our own genus?","a":"Because the oldest fossils of Homo are about half a million years younger than the tools. The likelier makers are Australopithecus afarensis or Kenyanthropus platyops, whose flat-faced skull came from these same beds in 1999."},{"q":"Why is the claim for the world's oldest stone tools not yet settled?","a":"Because several archaeologists argue that the layers are dated less securely than reported, and that natural forces can fracture stone in similar ways. A second site of such age would settle it."}]},
{"id":"wh-015","num":"15","category":"World · History","question":"Shaping stone by striking thin slices off it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> works only on glassy rocks such as flint and obsidian, which break in smooth curved surfaces so the crack follows the blow.","answer":"Knapping","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – present</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Knapping</b> is the craft of shaping stone by striking it so that thin slices, called flakes, break away from the core. The process is purely subtractive: material can only be removed, never put back, so one misjudged blow can ruin the whole piece. It works only on rocks such as flint, chert and obsidian, which are glassy or built from crystals far too small to see. Struck properly, these materials break in smooth, curved, shell-like surfaces, a pattern known as conchoidal fracture, in which the stone splits along the line of force rather than along any internal grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That predictability is what makes the craft possible: because the crack follows the blow, a knapper can aim, and can judge roughly where the next flake will come away, whereas coarse rock such as granite simply crumbles. <br><br> The hammer decides the result: a hard hammerstone drives off large, thick flakes and roughs out the shape, while a softer club of antler or bone delivers the thin, flat flakes that finish the edge of a handaxe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the most delicate work the hammer is set aside altogether: the knapper presses a pointed bone compressor against the edge until small flakes pop off, which is how points were given their saw-like teeth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A struck flake often carries a smooth swelling where the blow landed, and even the waste chips, known as debitage, usually betray a human hand rather than natural breakage. Grinding a stone edge smooth against another stone is a separate craft, far older than farming — edge-ground hatchets were being made in northern Australia 65,000 years ago — and it never replaced knapping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Modern experiments show that the skill takes years to learn, and it has never quite died out: gunflint makers were still striking flints for muskets in the 1800s, and archaeologists knap today to read the workshops of the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Knapping","questions":["In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a hard hammerstone drives off thick flakes to rough out the shape, while a softer antler billet delivers the thin flakes that finish an edge.","A struck flake bears a smooth swelling where the blow landed, and even the waste chips of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, called debitage, betray a human hand."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Li Li et al., “A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1290 and 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]","Antoine Muller, Ceri Shipton, and Chris Clarkson, “Stone Toolmaking Difficulty and the Evolution of Hominin Technological Skills,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 5883, under “Introduction,” https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-09914-2. [Open access]","Sydney B. J. Skertchly, <i>On the Manufacture of Gun-Flints, the Methods of Excavating for Flint, the Age of Palæolithic Man, and the Connexion between Neolithic Art and the Gun-Flint Trade</i>, Memoirs of the Geological Survey of England and Wales (London: Her Majesty’s Stationery Office, 1879), 5, https://archive.org/details/onmanufactureofg00skerrich. [Open access]","Veerle Rots et al., \"Pressure Flaking to Serrate Bifacial Points for the Hunt during the MIS5 at Sibudu Cave (South Africa),\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 12, no. 4 (2017): e0175151, under \"Evidence for Pressure Flaking,\" https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0175151. [Open access]","Elspeth H. Hayes et al., \"65,000-Years of Continuous Grinding Stone Use at Madjedbebe, Northern Australia,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 11747, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-15174-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Soft_Hammer.jpg/1920px-Soft_Hammer.jpg","title":"Knapping","desc":"An example of soft hammer percussion used in flintknapping. en:ZenTrowel. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soft_Hammer.jpg","alt":"Soft Hammer"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why can flint be knapped when a rock such as granite cannot?","a":"Because flint, chert and obsidian are glassy or built from crystals far too small to see, so a blow splits them along its own line of force in smooth, shell-like surfaces. Coarse rock such as granite simply crumbles instead."},{"q":"Why does a knapper change hammer part-way through making a handaxe?","a":"Because the hammer decides the result. A hard hammerstone drives off large, thick flakes and roughs out the shape, while a softer club of antler or bone delivers the thin, flat flakes that finish the edge."},{"q":"Why can a single misjudged blow ruin the whole piece?","a":"Because the process is purely subtractive: material can only be removed, never put back, so every flake is final. That is part of why modern experiments show the skill takes years to learn."}]},
{"id":"wh-016","num":"16","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a gorge in northern Tanzania, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest widespread stone tool industry, a kit of battered cores and razor-sharp flakes.","answer":"Oldowan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 – 1.7 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936, after Olduvai Gorge</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Oldowan</b> is the earliest widespread stone toolmaking tradition, a plain but effective technology that spread across Africa, later reached Eurasia, and lasted for roughly a million years. Its method was direct: a knapper held a rounded cobble in one hand and struck it with a hammerstone at a steep angle, driving off flakes with naturally razor-sharp edges. The resulting kit is modest — battered cores often called choppers, the flakes struck from them, the hammerstones themselves, and unworked stones carried in from elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Archaeologists still argue about which of these was the point: many now think the cutting flakes were the goal all along and the shaped cores merely the leftovers. In the shorthand scheme that Grahame Clark devised for the world's stone technologies, this whole tradition counts as Mode 1, the simplest of his five. <br><br> The industry takes its name from Olduvai Gorge in northern Tanzania, where Louis Leakey coined the term in 1936 and Mary Leakey later worked out the classification of tool types. The oldest securely dated examples come from Gona and Ledi-Geraru in Ethiopia at about 2.6 million years ago, though finds at Nyayanga in Kenya may push the tradition back towards 2.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Butchery is the clearest use: bones from these sites carry slicing cut marks and the crushing scars of a hammerstone swung to crack a shaft open for its marrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who did the striking is unsettled — Homo habilis was long assumed to be the maker, but teeth of the heavy-jawed Paranthropus lay among the tools at Nyayanga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By about 1.7 million years ago the teardrop hand axes of the Acheulean began to appear, and the simpler kit gradually gave way, having already travelled out of Africa to Dmanisi in Georgia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Oldowan","questions":["Counting as Mode 1 in Grahame Clark's five-part scheme, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is best shown at work by butchery — cut marks on bones and shafts cracked open for their marrow.","Tools from Gona in Ethiopia, dated to about 2.6 million years ago, are the oldest securely dated of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which may have been knapped by Homo habilis or heavy-jawed Paranthropus."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Thomas W. Plummer, Sonia Harmand, Emma M. Finestone, and Evan P. Wilson, “The First Million Years of Technology: The Lomekwian and the Early Oldowan,” <i>Annual Review of Anthropology</i> 54 (2025): 359–75, under “Context and Characteristics of the Early Oldowan Industry” and “Potential Manufacturers of Stone Tools 3.3–2.3 Ma,” https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10631785. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, under “The Hard Evidence for the Earliest Acheulean: The Current View,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Early Stone Age Tools,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/stone-tools/early-stone-age-tools. [Open access]","Fernando Diez-Martín et al., \"The Origin of The Acheulean: The 1.7 Million-Year-Old Site of FLK West, Olduvai Gorge (Tanzania),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 5 (2015): 17839, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep17839. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Canto_tallado_2-Guelmim-Es_Semara.jpg","title":"Oldowan","desc":"Canto tallado de tradición Olduvayense procedente de la región del Sáhara atlántico Guelmim-Es Semara (Museo Arqueológico Nacional de Madrid). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canto_tallado_2-Guelmim-Es_Semara.jpg","alt":"Canto tallado 2-Guelmim-Es Semara"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why do many archaeologists now think the flakes were the point?","a":"Because it is the flakes that carry naturally razor-sharp edges, driven off when a knapper struck a rounded cobble with a hammerstone at a steep angle. On that reading the battered cores often called choppers are merely the leftovers."},{"q":"Why is butchery the clearest use we can demonstrate?","a":"Because the bones from these sites carry the evidence directly: slicing cut marks, and the crushing scars of a hammerstone swung to crack a shaft open for its marrow."},{"q":"Why is it unsettled who made Oldowan tools?","a":"Because Homo habilis was long assumed to be the maker, but teeth of the heavy-jawed Paranthropus lay among the tools at Nyayanga — so the association of maker with tradition rests on what happens to be found beside what."}]},
{"id":"wh-017","num":"17","category":"World · History","question":"Cutting across the Serengeti Plain of Tanzania, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a ravine whose layered walls lay open 2 million years of deposits.","answer":"Olduvai Gorge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.03 Mya – 15,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1931, by the Leakeys</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Olduvai Gorge</b> is a steep-sided ravine cutting through the Eastern Serengeti Plains of northern Tanzania, inside the Ngorongoro Conservation Area, and now officially written Oldupai after the Maasai word behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The main gorge runs about 37 kilometres (23 miles) east across the plain before a side gorge joins it from the south, then another nine to the Balbal depression, and it is roughly 100 metres (330 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name comes from Duvai, the Maasai word for the sisal plant that grows there, which a European traveller first wrote down in 1892.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Early Pleistocene the basin had no outlet and held a shallow salt lake, fed by streams off a line of volcanoes to the west whose ash falls are now dated one by one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those dated ashes are what make the place so valuable, since the fossils and tools between them can be fixed in time; the oldest tools reach 2.03 million years, and most finds come from the numbered Beds I to IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Wilhelm Kattwinkel and his wife Martha came upon the gorge in 1911 and carried home to Munich the bones of a proboscidean, a three-toed horse, a hippopotamus and a sivathere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later generations remembered him as a butterfly collector who had stumbled on the place by accident, though his journey was a scientific expedition prompted by his medical interest in sleeping sickness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hans Reck dug there from October to December 1913 and came back with Louis Leakey's Third East African Archaeological Expedition of 1931 to 1932, after which the Leakeys worked the gorge for more than thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 17 July 1959 Mary Leakey uncovered the massive-jawed cranium the couple announced as Zinjanthropus, nicknamed Nutcracker Man and now called Paranthropus boisei, about 1.8 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 1960 and 1963 the same team found the remains named Homo habilis in 1964, its type specimen the jaw and hand bones called OH 7, and the gorge has yielded Homo erectus as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olduvai Gorge","questions":["Beds I to IV stack up the walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, interleaved with volcanic ash whose falls can be dated one by one, which is why its fossils sit firmly in time.","When Mary Leakey uncovered the massive-jawed skull nicknamed Nutcracker Man on 17 July 1959, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded a fossil of about 1.8 million years and made the Leakeys famous."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","tanzania"],"sources":["A. W. Gentry, Anthea Gentry and H. Mayr, “Rediscovery of Fossil Antelope Holotypes (Mammalia, Bovidae) Collected from Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania, in 1913,” <i>Mitteilungen der Bayerischen Staatssammlung für Paläontologie und historische Geologie</i> 35 (1995): 125–35, at 125–26, https://www.zobodat.at/pdf/Mitt-Bayer-Staatsslg-Pal-hist-Geol_35_0125-0135.pdf. [Open access]","Ngorongoro Conservation Area Authority, “Olduvai Gorge,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.ncaa.go.tz/olduvai-gorge/. [Open access]","Julio Mercader et al., “Earliest Olduvai Hominins Exploited Unstable Environments ~2 Million Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 12 (2021): 3, under “Stratigraphy and chronology” and “Early Oldowan ecology at ~2 Ma,” https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-20176-2. [Open access]","Julien Favreau et al., “Petrographic Characterization of Raw Material Sources at Oldupai Gorge, Tanzania,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 8 (2020): 158, abstract and “Geological Setting,” https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2020.00158. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “OH 5,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/oh-5. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo habilis</i>,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-habilis. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Olduvai_ast.jpg/1920px-Olduvai_ast.jpg","title":"Olduvai Gorge","desc":"Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania from space image description here Image Courtesy: NASA/GSFC/METI/ERSDAC/JAROS, and U.S./Japan ASTER Science Team. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olduvai_ast.jpg","alt":"Olduvai ast"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Olduvai Gorge","at":[35.3511,-2.9936]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Olduvai Gorge so valuable for dating the finds inside it?","a":"Because the basin was fed by streams off a line of volcanoes whose ash falls are now dated one by one, so the fossils and tools lying between those ashes can be fixed in time. The oldest tools reach 2.03 million years."},{"q":"Why is the butterfly-collector story about Kattwinkel wrong?","a":"Because his journey was a scientific expedition prompted by his medical interest in sleeping sickness. He and his wife Martha came upon the gorge in 1911 and carried home the bones of a proboscidean, a three-toed horse, a hippopotamus and a sivathere."},{"q":"Why is the gorge now written Oldupai?","a":"Because the name comes from Duvai, the Maasai word for the sisal plant that grows there, which a European traveller first wrote down in 1892; the official spelling now follows the Maasai word rather than that transcription."}]},
{"id":"wh-018","num":"18","category":"World · History","question":"Small-bodied, long-armed and with a brain about half the size of ours, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest widely accepted member of our own genus.","answer":"Homo habilis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.4 – 1.4 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1964, by Leakey, Tobias and Napier</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo habilis</b> is an extinct early human of eastern and southern Africa, living between about 2.4 and 1.4 million years ago, and is usually counted as the earliest widely accepted member of our own genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was small: the known individuals stand between roughly 100 and 135 centimetres (3 ft 3 in and 4 ft 5 in) and weighed around 32 kilograms (71 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its braincase was markedly larger than an australopith's, the average for early Homo running just short of 40 per cent above theirs, and its type specimen's volume is now put at 729 to 824 millilitres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The face was flatter and less projecting than in earlier hominins and the teeth were smaller, but below the neck the body stayed archaic, which is what makes the species so hard to place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Louis Leakey, Phillip Tobias and John Napier announced it in 1964, choosing a Latin word for 'handy' or 'skilful' because Oldowan stone tools lay in the same deposits at Olduvai Gorge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The type specimen, catalogued as OH 7 and nicknamed Jonny's Child, was found by Jonathan Leakey between 1960 and 1963: a lower jaw, pieces of the braincase and, crucially, hand bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Admitting it to Homo meant moving the goalposts, because Arthur Keith's cerebral Rubicon of 750 cubic centimetres was dropped to 600 to let the new species through, with manual dexterity and upright walking weighing more than brain size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That decision has been argued over ever since, and Bernard Wood and Mark Collard have held the species too unlike Homo in its whole way of life to stay in the genus at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Others hold that the fossils lumped under the name are really more than one species, and the record between 2 and 1.7 million years ago does show clear diversity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor did it neatly give rise to Homo erectus, which appears by 1.89 million years ago and lies alongside it in the upper beds at Olduvai, while the oldest Oldowan tools, at more than 2.58 million years, are older than any fossil of the genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo habilis","questions":["Announced in 1964 and given a Latin name for 'handy', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> won its place in our genus only after the brain-size threshold was lowered from 750 to 600 cubic centimetres.","A juvenile jaw and hand bones nicknamed Jonny's Child are the type specimen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, cast as the likely maker of the Oldowan flakes in the same Olduvai deposits."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo habilis</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-habilis. [Open access]","William H. Kimbel and Brian Villmoare, “From <i>Australopithecus</i> to <i>Homo</i>: The Transition That Wasn't,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150248, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920303/. [Open access]","Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, “Genera of the Human Lineage,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo erectus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Homo_habilis_IMG_2911.JPG/1920px-Homo_habilis_IMG_2911.JPG","title":"Homo habilis","desc":"On display at Lausanne Natural History Museum in Rumin palace Homo habilis. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_habilis_IMG_2911.JPG","alt":"On display at Lausanne Natural History Museum in Rumin palace Homo habilis"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why did admitting Homo habilis to our genus mean moving the goalposts?","a":"Because Arthur Keith's cerebral Rubicon of 750 cubic centimetres was dropped to 600 to let the new species through, with manual dexterity and upright walking weighing more than brain size."},{"q":"Why is Homo habilis so hard to place?","a":"Because it is a mixture: the braincase runs nearly 40 per cent above an australopith's on average and the face is flatter with smaller teeth, while below the neck the body stays archaic."},{"q":"Why can Homo habilis not be treated as the maker of the first stone tools?","a":"Because the oldest Oldowan tools, at more than 2.58 million years, are older than any fossil of the genus — and Homo erectus appears by 1.89 million years ago and lies alongside habilis in the upper beds at Olduvai."}]},
{"id":"wh-019","num":"19","category":"World · History","question":"Tall, long-legged and built much like us below the neck, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first human species to walk out of Africa, lasting nearly 2 million years.","answer":"Homo erectus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2 Mya – 110,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1894, by Eugène Dubois</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo erectus</b> is an extinct human species that appeared in Africa around 2 million years ago and survived until roughly 110,000 years ago, a span no other member of our genus has come close to matching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was the first of our relatives with long legs and short arms, built for walking and running across open country, though its trunk stayed deeper and wider than ours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Brain size climbed over that long career, from something like 600 cubic centimetres in the earliest African skulls to about 1,000 in later Asian ones, closing much of the gap to our own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The face stayed heavy, with a thick brow ridge above the eyes, a low forehead and no real chin. It was also the first human species to leave Africa, reaching the Caucasus by about 1.8 million years ago and eventually spreading as far as Java and northern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It was found long before anyone thought to look in Africa, when the Dutch army surgeon Eugène Dubois sailed east determined to dig up the missing link and struck a skullcap at Trinil in Java in 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He named it Pithecanthropus erectus, the upright ape-man, in 1894, and only in the 1950s was the species folded into our own genus as Homo erectus. The most complete skeleton is the Turkana Boy from Nariokotome in Kenya, a boy of about 11 or 12 who died some 1.5 million years ago, tall and long-limbed but broad through the chest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its handiwork includes the teardrop hand axes of the Acheulean, and a handful of sites such as Wonderwerk Cave and Gesher Benot Ya'aqov hint at the occasional use of fire. The famous Peking Man fossils from Zhoukoudian near Beijing belong to the same species, but the originals vanished in 1941 while being evacuated ahead of the Japanese advance, leaving only plaster casts behind.","citation":"","answerText":"Homo erectus","questions":["First dug up by Eugène Dubois at Trinil in Java in 1891, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was called Pithecanthropus, the upright ape-man, before being folded into our own genus in the 1950s.","Growing a brain from about 600 to 1,000 cubic centimetres across nearly two million years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried Acheulean hand axes and perhaps fire as far as China."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §§1–2 and table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Yan Rizal et al., “Last Appearance of <i>Homo erectus</i> at Ngandong, Java, 117,000–108,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 577, no. 7790 (2020): 381–85, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1863-2. [Open access]","Markus Bastir et al., “Rib Cage Anatomy in <i>Homo erectus</i> Suggests a Recent Evolutionary Origin of Modern Human Body Shape,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 4, no. 9 (2020): 1178–87, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-020-1240-4. [Open access]","Sabrina C. Curran et al., “Hominin Presence in Eurasia by at Least 1.95 Million Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 836, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-56154-9. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Homo_erectus_skull_cross-section.png","title":"Homo erectus","desc":"Horizontal cross-section through the ophryon–inion plane of Sinanthropus Skulls III (A) and XII (B), and Pithecanthropus Skull II (C). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_erectus_skull_cross-section.png","alt":"Homo erectus skull cross-section"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why was Homo erectus found in Java rather than in Africa?","a":"Because it was found long before anyone thought to look in Africa: the Dutch army surgeon Eugène Dubois sailed east determined to dig up the missing link and struck a skullcap at Trinil in 1891, naming it Pithecanthropus erectus in 1894."},{"q":"Why is the body of Homo erectus described as built for open country?","a":"Because it was the first of our relatives with long legs and short arms, built for walking and running across open ground, though its trunk stayed deeper and wider than ours."},{"q":"Why do only plaster casts survive of the Peking Man fossils?","a":"Because the originals vanished in 1941 while being evacuated from Zhoukoudian ahead of the Japanese advance, leaving the casts as the only record of them."}]},
{"id":"wh-020","num":"20","category":"World · History","question":"Proposed in 1975 for a lower jaw from Koobi Fora, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name under which some researchers keep Africa's earliest Homo erectus fossils apart from the Asian ones.","answer":"Homo ergaster","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.9 – 1.5 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975, by Groves and Mazák</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo ergaster</b> is the name given to a group of early human fossils from eastern and southern Africa, dating from roughly 1.9 to 1.5 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Colin Groves and Vratislav Mazák proposed it in 1975, and the specimen the name was built on is a lower jaw catalogued as KNM-ER 992, from Koobi Fora on the eastern shore of Lake Turkana in Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best skull attributed to the group, KNM-ER 3733, came out of the same deposits in the same year, found by Bernard Ngeneo and about 1.8 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is a mature female and one of the best preserved skulls of its time, and it carries the body plan that Homo erectus shows wherever it is found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the name describes a real species is the whole question, because the fossils are otherwise much like the Homo erectus long known from Java and China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Some scientists therefore keep the African fossils apart as Homo ergaster and the Asian ones as Homo erectus in the strict sense, while others lump them together as a single widespread species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two raise no difficulty about sitting in the same genus, and what is argued over is how finely a species should be cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brain size will not settle it, since the African and Georgian skulls run from 546 to 1,067 cubic centimetres, the Chinese from 855 to 1,225 and the Indonesian from 813 to 1,251, which is one broad range rather than two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor will age settle it, because later work on the rock sequences narrowed and in some cases wiped out the difference in time between the African and the Asian fossils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Turkana Boy, the near-complete skeleton of a youth from Nariokotome, is the best-known casualty of the disagreement, catalogued as Homo erectus by some museums and as Homo ergaster elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo ergaster","questions":["Neither brain size nor age can settle it, since the African and Asian skulls fall in one broad range, which is why sceptics call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> merely African Homo erectus.","The near-complete Turkana Boy is the best-known casualty of the dispute over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, labelled with this name in some museums and Homo erectus in others."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, “Genera of the Human Lineage,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §2 and table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo erectus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-ER 3733,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-er-3733. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-WT 15000,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-wt-15000. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/Homo_ergaster.jpg","title":"Homo ergaster","desc":"Skull of Homo ergaster (I took this photo in october 2005) Skull KNM-ER 3733 discovered by Bernard Ngeneo in 1975 (Kenya). No machine-readable author provided. Luna04~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright claims).,. CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_ergaster.jpg","alt":"Skull of Homo ergaster (I took this photo in october 2005) Skull KNM-ER 3733 discovered by Bernard Ngeneo in 1975 (Kenya)"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is it disputed whether Homo ergaster is a real species?","a":"Because the African fossils are otherwise much like the Homo erectus long known from Java and China. Some keep the African ones apart as ergaster and the Asian ones as erectus in the strict sense; others lump them as one widespread species."},{"q":"Why will brain size not settle the argument?","a":"Because the African and Georgian skulls run from 546 to 1,067 cubic centimetres, the Chinese from 855 to 1,225 and the Indonesian from 813 to 1,251 — which is one broad range rather than two."},{"q":"Why does the Turkana Boy carry two different labels?","a":"Because the near-complete skeleton from Nariokotome is the best-known casualty of the disagreement: it is catalogued as Homo erectus by some museums and as Homo ergaster elsewhere."}]},
{"id":"wh-021","num":"21","category":"World · History","question":"Found beside Lake Turkana in northern Kenya in 1984, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the most complete early human skeleton known, a boy who died about 1.6 million years ago.","answer":"Turkana Boy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.6 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1984, by Kamoya Kimeu</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Turkana Boy</b> is the skeleton of a boy who died beside a lake in northern Kenya about 1.6 million years ago, and it is the most complete early human ever recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kamoya Kimeu, a fossil hunter of long experience working with Richard Leakey's team, spotted a scrap of skull at Nariokotome, west of Lake Turkana, in 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Patient sieving of the slope above it produced most of the rest over several seasons, until about 40 per cent of the skeleton had been recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stood 1.6 metres (5 ft 3 in) and weighed 48 kilograms (106 pounds) when he died, and his braincase held 880 cubic centimetres, which would have reached about 909 had he grown up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The skeleton is catalogued as KNM-WT 15000, and museums label it either Homo erectus or Homo ergaster depending on where they stand in that dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was about eight or nine years old, and he had been growing at a rate closer to a living great ape's than to ours, so his kind reached adulthood faster than we do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His adult height has been revised in the same spirit: an early estimate of 185 centimetres (6 ft 1 in), which made him a strapping figure, is now put nearer 163.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For years he was also thought to be diseased, since an unusually narrow spinal canal was read as a rare inherited disorder of the skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A reassessment grouped his vertebrae with those of ordinary modern people and found the anomalies confined to a few bones of the chest and lower back, consistent with an injury rather than a developmental disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His rib cage turns out not to be modern either: reconstructed in three dimensions it is short, wide and deep, unlike the much shallower chest we carry, so fully modern body shape came later than his skeleton was long taken to show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Turkana Boy","questions":["Catalogued as KNM-WT 15000 and standing about 1.6 metres (5 ft 3 in) tall, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was spotted by the fossil hunter Kamoya Kimeu at Nariokotome while working with Richard Leakey's team.","A boy of about eight or nine at death who had been growing at a great ape's pace, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would have reached adulthood faster than we do."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","kenya"],"sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “KNM-WT 15000,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/knm-wt-15000. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, “<i>Homo erectus</i>,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Markus Bastir et al., “Rib Cage Anatomy in <i>Homo erectus</i> Suggests a Recent Evolutionary Origin of Modern Human Body Shape,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 4, no. 9 (2020): 1178–87, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-020-1240-4. [Open access]","Ronda R. Graves et al., “Just How Strapping Was KNM-WT 15000?,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 59, no. 5 (2010): 542–54, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2010.06.007. [Paywalled]","Regula Schiess et al., “Revisiting Scoliosis in the KNM-WT 15000 <i>Homo erectus</i> Skeleton,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 67 (2014): 48–59, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2013.12.009. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Chlapec_od_jezera_Turkana_03.jpg","title":"Turkana Boy","desc":"Lebka a spodní čelist turkanského chlapce. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chlapec_od_jezera_Turkana_03.jpg","alt":"Chlapec od jezera Turkana 03"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why would the Turkana Boy have reached adulthood sooner than we do?","a":"Because he had been growing at a rate closer to a living great ape's than to ours. He was about eight or nine when he died, standing 1.6 metres and weighing 48 kilograms, with a braincase of 880 cubic centimetres."},{"q":"Why is he no longer thought to have been diseased?","a":"Because a reassessment grouped his vertebrae with those of ordinary modern people and found the anomalies confined to a few bones of the chest and lower back, consistent with an injury rather than the inherited skeletal disorder first read into his narrow spinal canal."},{"q":"Why does his rib cage matter for when modern body shape appeared?","a":"Because reconstructed in three dimensions it is short, wide and deep, unlike the much shallower chest we carry — so fully modern body shape came later than his skeleton was long taken to show."}]},
{"id":"wh-023","num":"23","category":"World · History","question":"Beneath a medieval town in southern Georgia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has produced five skulls 1.8 million years old, the oldest human remains known outside Africa.","answer":"Dmanisi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hominins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">tools 1983, first jaw 1991</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dmanisi</b> is an archaeological site in southern Georgia where the ruins of a medieval town sit directly over the oldest human fossils known outside Africa, though an earlier presence in Eurasia is now claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Excavators digging beneath the medieval layers found stone tools in 1983 and the first hominin jawbone in 1991, and the deposits are dated to about 1.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The site has now yielded five skulls along with jaws and limb bones, an unusually rich haul for hominins of that age, since most are known from scraps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were small-bodied people, and the smallest of their braincases holds only about 546 cubic centimetres, closer to Homo habilis than to the Homo erectus they are usually assigned to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stone tools found with them are simple flakes and cores of Oldowan type, with no sign of the hand axe. <br><br> One of the skulls belonged to a man who had lost all but one of his teeth, the empty sockets long since closed over, meaning he lived for years without being able to chew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some read this as the earliest hint that the sick were looked after; others answer that soft foods and bone marrow would have kept him alive without anyone's help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The most complete of the five, published in 2013, set off a larger argument, because it pairs a jutting face with a braincase of only 546 cubic centimetres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its describers held that the variation among the five Dmanisi skulls is no wider than within a single population, and concluded that Homo habilis, Homo rudolfensis and Homo erectus may all be one evolving lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That has never been generally accepted, and a study of the tooth crowns published in 2025 came to the opposite view, arguing that two kinds of human lived at Dmanisi side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dmanisi","questions":["Scattered with the bones are thousands of simple Oldowan flakes and cores but no hand axe, and one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> skull belonged to a man who had lost all but one tooth.","A skull published in 2013 pairs a jutting face with a braincase of just 546 cubic centimetres, sparking the claim that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows several named humans as one species."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","georgia"],"sources":["Victor Nery, Walter Neves, Leticia Valota, and Mark Hubbe, “Testing the Taxonomy of Dmanisi Hominin Fossils through Dental Crown Area,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 11 (2025): e0336484, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12674552/. [Open access]","David Lordkipanidze, Marcia S. Ponce de León, Ann Margvelashvili, et al., “A Complete Skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, and the Evolutionary Biology of Early <i>Homo</i>,” <i>Science</i> 342, no. 6156 (2013): 326–31, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1238484. [Paywalled]","David Lordkipanidze, Abesalom Vekua, Reid Ferring, et al., “The Earliest Toothless Hominin Skull,” <i>Nature</i> 434, no. 7034 (2005): 717–18, https://doi.org/10.1038/434717b. [Paywalled]","Christoph P. E. Zollikofer, Vincent Beyrand, David Lordkipanidze, Paul Tafforeau, and Marcia S. Ponce de León, “Dental Evidence for Extended Growth in Early <i>Homo</i> from Dmanisi,” <i>Nature</i> 635 (2024): 906–11, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11602720/. [Open access]","Sabrina C. Curran, Virgil Drăgușin, Briana Pobiner, et al., “Hominin Presence in Eurasia by at Least 1.95 Million Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 836, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11747263/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Homo_Georgicus_IMG_2921.JPG/1920px-Homo_Georgicus_IMG_2921.JPG","title":"Dmanisi","desc":"Homo Georgicus. Rama. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_Georgicus_IMG_2921.JPG","alt":"Homo Georgicus"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Dmanisi","at":[44.2036,41.3317]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Dmanisi an unusually rich site for hominins of that age?","a":"Because most hominins of 1.8 million years ago are known from scraps, while Dmanisi has yielded five skulls along with jaws and limb bones — enough to see how much one population varies."},{"q":"Why is the toothless man's skull read two ways?","a":"Because he had lost all but one of his teeth, the sockets long since closed over, so he lived for years unable to chew. Some read this as the earliest hint that the sick were looked after; others answer that soft foods and bone marrow would have kept him alive unaided."},{"q":"Why did the fifth skull set off a larger argument?","a":"Because it pairs a jutting face with a braincase of only 546 cubic centimetres. Its describers held the variation among the five skulls no wider than within one population, and concluded that habilis, rudolfensis and erectus may all be a single evolving lineage."}]},
{"id":"wh-024","num":"24","category":"World · History","question":"Prised from a riverbank in eastern Java in 1891 and 1892, the skullcap, molar and thigh bone known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the first fossils of Homo erectus ever found.","answer":"Java Man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">830,000 – 773,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and 560,000 – 380,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1891–1892, by Eugène Dubois</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1894</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Java Man</b> is the name given to a skullcap, a molar and a thigh bone dug out of the bank of the Solo River at Trinil, in eastern Java, in 1891 and 1892.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were the first remains of Homo erectus ever found, and the Dutch physician who dug them up, Eugène Dubois, had crossed the world looking for exactly that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Convinced by Darwin that the fossil link between apes and humans lay somewhere in Asia, he enlisted as a medical officer in the colonial army of the Dutch East Indies, landing on Sumatra in December 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A molar came out of the riverbank in September 1891, the skullcap a month later from the same level barely a metre (3 feet) away, and the left thigh bone in August 1892, some 15 metres (49 feet) upstream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern measurement puts the braincase at about 940 cubic centimetres, far beyond any ape's and still well short of ours, though Dubois himself reckoned it at more than a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He named the creature Pithecanthropus erectus in 1894, 'the upright ape-man', taking the genus from a word Ernst Haeckel had coined in 1868 for a creature nobody had yet seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reception was cold, and the trouble was the thigh bone, essentially modern where the skullcap is low and heavily browed, so scholars argued whether the braincase belonged to the human family at all, or to an out-sized gibbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fieldwork published in 2023 reconstructed the old excavation and found younger river channels cutting through the spot where the thigh bone had lain, which would account for its modern build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The several genera then in use were folded into Homo erectus by Ernst Mayr, formalising what most researchers had already come to accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Dutch state commission found in 2025 that Dubois had dug with up to 50 forced labourers at a place he called Java's hell, and that December the three bones were handed over in Jakarta, first of more than 28,000 specimens returning to Indonesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Java Man","questions":["Hunted down by a Dutch army doctor who had joined the colonial forces purely to reach the Indies, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded a braincase of about 940 cubic centimetres, well beyond any ape's.","Because a thigh bone of almost modern build lay beside a low, heavy-browed skullcap, scholars asked whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one creature at all, or an out-sized gibbon."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","indonesia"],"sources":["Eugène Dubois, <i>Pithecanthropus erectus: eine menschenaehnliche Uebergangsform aus Java</i> (1894; repr. New York: G. E. Stechert, 1915), 1–2, 11, https://archive.org/details/Pithecanthropus00Dubo. [Open access]","Frank Huffman et al., “Geology and Discovery Record of the Trinil <i>Pithecanthropus erectus</i> Site, Java,” <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2022, no. 2, abstract and n. 4, https://doi.org/10.48738/2022.iss2.83. [Open access]","Eduard Pop et al., “Reconstructing the Provenance of the Hominin Fossils from Trinil (Java, Indonesia) through an Integrated Analysis of the Historical and Recent Excavations,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 176 (2023): 103312, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2022.103312. [Open access]","Xiujie Wu, Lynne A. Schepartz and Wu Liu, “A New <i>Homo erectus</i> (Zhoukoudian V) Brain Endocast from China,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 277, no. 1679 (2010): 337–44, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2842660/. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., “Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, §2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Commissie Koloniale Collecties, <i>Advies</i> ID-2025-1 [advice to the Dutch State Secretary for Education, Culture and Science on Indonesia's request for the return of the Dubois collection], 5 September 2025, 2, 5–6, 11, 20–21, https://commissie.kolonialecollecties.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2025/09/26/indonesie/id-2025-1-advies-collectie-dubois.pdf. [Open access]","Ministerie van Onderwijs, Cultuur en Wetenschap, “Topstukken Dubois-collectie overhandigd in Indonesië” [Key objects of the Dubois collection handed over in Indonesia], Rijksoverheid, 17 December 2025, https://www.rijksoverheid.nl/actueel/nieuws/2025/12/17/topstukken-dubois-collectie-overhandigd-in-indonesie. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/The_most_ancient_skeletal_remains_of_man_Plate_1.png/1920px-The_most_ancient_skeletal_remains_of_man_Plate_1.png","title":"Java Man","desc":"The Locality of the Pithecanthropus find, on the Bengavian River, Near Trinal, Java (After MME. SELENKA AND M. BLANKENHORN). The two white squares show where the femur (on left) and the skullcap (on right and more behind) were discovered. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_most_ancient_skeletal_remains_of_man_Plate_1.png","alt":"The most ancient skeletal remains of man Plate 1"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did Dubois go to the Dutch East Indies to look for a fossil?","a":"Because he was convinced by Darwin that the fossil link between apes and humans lay somewhere in Asia, and enlisted as a medical officer in the colonial army to get there, landing on Sumatra in December 1887."},{"q":"Why was the find received coldly?","a":"Because of the thigh bone, essentially modern where the skullcap is low and heavily browed. Scholars argued whether the braincase belonged to the human family at all, or to an out-sized gibbon."},{"q":"Why does later fieldwork account for that mismatch?","a":"Because work published in 2023 reconstructed the old excavation and found younger river channels cutting through the spot where the thigh bone had lain, which would explain its modern build."}]},
{"id":"wh-025","num":"25","category":"World · History","question":"Named in 1927 from a single tooth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the early human of a cave near Beijing whose entire original collection vanished in the winter of 1941.","answer":"Peking Man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750,000 – 230,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927, by Davidson Black</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1941</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Peking Man</b> is the name for the Homo erectus remains found at Zhoukoudian, a limestone hill about 50 kilometres (31 miles) southwest of Beijing, in deposits whose ages run from more than 750,000 to roughly 230,000 years ago by different dating methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1927 the Canadian anatomist Davidson Black named a whole new genus and species, Sinanthropus pekinensis, on the strength of a single lower molar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a reckless thing to do, and it was borne out two years later when Pei Wenzhong lifted the first skullcap out of the deposit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the late 1930s the hill had given up parts of more than 40 individuals, along with thousands of stone tools and layers of burnt material, making it the richest early human site anyone had dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The braincases run from about 915 to 1,225 cubic centimetres, far above any ape's and reaching into the lower part of our own range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Then it all disappeared, because as war closed in the most important of the fossils were packed into two large wooden footlockers late in 1941 and handed to the US military for shipment to New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Witnesses put the crates on a Marine vehicle, the cargo ship meant to carry them ran aground and never reached the port, and the gates of the Peking Union Medical College remain the last reliable sighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every theory since, from sinking at sea to burial near the college, is a guess, and no verifiable trace of the crates has ever been recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What saved the science was Franz Weidenreich, who had the skulls photographed, drawn, measured and cast before the war and the casts shipped to America, so the species is still studied largely from plaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not all of it is plaster, since digs from 1949 to 1959 and again in 1966 recovered six teeth and a jaw of original bone, while the burnt layers, doubted from the 1980s, have been partly restored by recent work on the cave floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Peking Man","questions":["A braincase of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may hold 1,225 cubic centimetres, which reaches into the lower part of the modern range and stands far above any ape's.","Because the skulls had all been cast, drawn and measured before the war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is still studied largely from plaster, the bones themselves having gone missing in 1941."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","china"],"sources":["Franz Weidenreich, <i>The Skull of</i> Sinanthropus pekinensis<i>: A Comparative Study on a Primitive Hominid Skull</i>, Palaeontologia Sinica, n.s. D, no. 10 (Pehpei, Chungking: Geological Survey of China, 1943), iii, 2–3, 190, https://archive.org/details/TheSkullOfSinanthropusPekinensisAComparativeStudyOnAPrimitive. [Open access]","Song Xing, María Martinón-Torres and José María Bermúdez de Castro, “The Fossil Teeth of the Peking Man,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 2066, introduction, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-20432-y. [Open access]","Xiujie Wu, Lynne A. Schepartz and Wu Liu, “A New <i>Homo erectus</i> (Zhoukoudian V) Brain Endocast from China,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 277, no. 1679 (2010): 337–44, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2842660/. [Open access]","Chao Huang, Jingshu Li and Xing Gao, “Evidence of Fire Use by <i>Homo erectus pekinensis</i>: An XRD Study of Archaeological Bones from Zhoukoudian Locality 1, China,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 9 (2022): 811319, introduction, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2021.811319. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger, Wu Liu and Xiujie Wu, “Investigation of a Credible Report by a US Marine on the Location of the Missing Peking Man Fossils,” <i>South African Journal of Science</i> 108, nos. 3–4 (2012), commentary, https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0038-23532012000200003. [Open access]","Xiaoya Ma, Guangxu Wang and Min Wang, “Impact of Chinese Palaeontology on Evolutionary Research,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 377, no. 1847 (2022): 20210029, background, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8819365/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Peking_Man_Weidenreich.png/1920px-Peking_Man_Weidenreich.png","title":"Peking Man","desc":"Franz Weidenreich's reconstruction of the Peking Man skull. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peking_Man_Weidenreich.png","alt":"Peking Man Weidenreich"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why was naming a genus on a single molar reckless?","a":"Because one tooth is thin ground for a whole new genus and species, which is what Davidson Black declared in 1927 as Sinanthropus pekinensis. It was borne out two years later when Pei Wenzhong lifted the first skullcap out of the deposit."},{"q":"Why is Peking Man still studied largely from plaster?","a":"Because the original fossils vanished in 1941 on their way out of the country. What saved the science was Franz Weidenreich, who had the skulls photographed, drawn, measured and cast before the war and the casts shipped to America."},{"q":"Why is every theory about the lost crates a guess?","a":"Because the gates of the Peking Union Medical College remain the last reliable sighting: witnesses put the crates on a Marine vehicle, the cargo ship meant to carry them ran aground, and no verifiable trace has ever been recorded since."}]},
{"id":"wh-026","num":"26","category":"World · History","question":"A limestone hill in the suburbs of Beijing, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds a record of occupation running from Homo erectus to people sewing skins with eyed bone needles.","answer":"Zhoukoudian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Layers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750,000 – 400,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1987</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zhoukoudian</b> is a limestone hill in the southwestern suburbs of Beijing, about 50 kilometres (31 miles) from the centre, known in Chinese as Dragon Bone Hill and riddled with separate fossil localities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most famous of them, known simply as Locality 1, is a karst deposit rather than an open cavern, and the debris filling it is divided into numbered layers running from the youngest at the top to the oldest at the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That filling took several hundred thousand years to gather, the topmost layers dated to about 400,000 years ago and the deepest reaching back beyond 750,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Out of it came the Peking Man fossils, parts of more than 40 individuals, along with thousands of stone tools and a rich Ice Age fauna of straight-tusked elephants, woolly rhinoceros, wolves and sabre-toothed cats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Weidenreich already knew that hyenas denned there and cracked the bones while they were fresh, and from the 1980s such arguments were pressed hard enough to cast doubt on the burnt layers as the hearths of a home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Higher up the same hill lies the Upper Cave, dug in the 1930s and sitting directly above the southwestern wall of Locality 1, which belongs to a wholly different world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people were modern humans, at least eight of them, and its cultural layers are dated to roughly 38,300 to 33,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They left ornaments of pierced fox, badger, deer, marten and tiger teeth, of carp and bird bone, of sea shell and of limestone, and three badger canines show they were sewn onto clothing rather than strung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cave's one formal bone tool is a needle with an eye at one end, and Pei Wenzhong found three complete skulls there, every one of them coated in ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Upper Cave material was packed up with the Peking Man collection in 1941 and vanished along with it, so its ornaments too are studied from casts and descriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhoukoudian","questions":["Locality 1 fills a limestone karst with layers reaching back beyond 750,000 years, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also holds an Upper Cave whose modern humans left an eyed bone needle.","Because hyenas denned in the same deposit and cracked the bones fresh, arguments from the 1980s recast <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a carnivore lair as much as a human home."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","china"],"sources":["Francesco d'Errico et al., “Zhoukoudian Upper Cave Personal Ornaments and Ochre: Rediscovery and Reevaluation,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 161 (2021): 103088, §§1–2 and fig. 1, https://shs.hal.science/hal-03445635/document. [Open access]","Song Xing, María Martinón-Torres and José María Bermúdez de Castro, “The Fossil Teeth of the Peking Man,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 2066, introduction, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-20432-y. [Open access]","Chao Huang, Jingshu Li and Xing Gao, “Evidence of Fire Use by <i>Homo erectus pekinensis</i>: An XRD Study of Archaeological Bones from Zhoukoudian Locality 1, China,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 9 (2022): 811319, introduction, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2021.811319. [Open access]","Franz Weidenreich, <i>The Skull of</i> Sinanthropus pekinensis<i>: A Comparative Study on a Primitive Hominid Skull</i>, Palaeontologia Sinica, n.s. D, no. 10 (Pehpei, Chungking: Geological Survey of China, 1943), 190, https://archive.org/details/TheSkullOfSinanthropusPekinensisAComparativeStudyOnAPrimitive. [Open access]","Peter Dodson, “China Shares Fossil Treasures with the World,” <i>The Anatomical Record</i> 308, no. 10 (2025): 2806–12, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12435128/. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger, Wu Liu and Xiujie Wu, “Investigation of a Credible Report by a US Marine on the Location of the Missing Peking Man Fossils,” <i>South African Journal of Science</i> 108, nos. 3–4 (2012), commentary, https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0038-23532012000200003. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Sinanthropus_Skull_V_%282%29.png","title":"Zhoukoudian","desc":"Sinanthropus Skull V. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinanthropus_Skull_V_(2).png","alt":"Sinanthropus Skull V (2)"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Zhoukoudian","at":[115.9461,39.6797]},"why":[{"q":"Why were the burnt layers at Locality 1 doubted as hearths?","a":"Because Weidenreich already knew that hyenas denned there and cracked the bones while they were fresh, and from the 1980s such arguments were pressed hard enough to cast doubt on the fire being anyone's home."},{"q":"Why does the Upper Cave belong to a wholly different world?","a":"Because its people were modern humans, at least eight of them, and its cultural layers date to roughly 38,300 to 33,500 years ago — hundreds of thousands of years after the deposit below it was filling."},{"q":"Why do we know the Upper Cave ornaments were sewn on rather than strung?","a":"Because three badger canines among them carry the wear of being attached to clothing, alongside pierced fox, deer, marten and tiger teeth, carp and bird bone, sea shell and limestone."}]},
{"id":"wh-027","num":"27","category":"World · History","question":"A teardrop-shaped stone worked on both faces to a continuous edge defines the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the longest-lived toolmaking tradition known, lasting over a million and a half years.","answer":"Acheulean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.76 Mya – 125,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1872, by Gabriel de Mortillet</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Acheulean</b> is the stone tool industry built around the hand axe, a slab of stone flaked on both faces into a teardrop or oval with a cutting edge running right round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cleavers with a broad chisel edge and heavy picks belong to the same kit, along with the flakes struck off in shaping them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Making one demands forethought of a kind the older Oldowan never required, since the knapper works towards a shape held in mind rather than simply striking off a useful edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest examples come from Kokiselei west of Lake Turkana in Kenya and from Konso in Ethiopia, both about 1.76 to 1.74 million years old, which pushed the tradition back some 300,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It then ran on until about 125,000 years ago, ending far earlier in some regions than in others, which makes it by far the longest-lived way of working stone that humans have ever kept up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Gabriel de Mortillet named it in 1872 after Saint-Acheul, a locality in the Somme valley near Amiens where such tools came out of the river gravels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hand axes are found across Africa, the Levant, Europe and the Indian subcontinent, and Homo erectus and later Homo heidelbergensis are credited with most of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hallam Movius noticed that they thin out towards eastern Asia, where simpler chopping tools prevail, and the boundary he drew still carries his name, though hand axes have since turned up in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What nobody agrees on is why they are so symmetrical, because butchery experiments suggest a neat outline cuts no better than a rough one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Suggestions run from the demands of the knapping itself to a proposal that a finely made axe advertised its maker's skill to prospective mates, an idea critics dismiss as untestable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Acheulean","questions":["Named in 1872 by Gabriel de Mortillet after Saint-Acheul in the Somme valley, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is credited mostly to Homo erectus and later Homo heidelbergensis.","Because a neat outline cuts no better than a rough one, why the hand axes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are so symmetrical is unsettled, with mate-display one contested idea."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, under “A Historical Context for the Acheulean” and “The Hard Evidence for the Earliest Acheulean: The Current View,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Alastair J. M. Key and Stephen J. Lycett, “Form and Function in the Lower Palaeolithic: History, Progress, and Continued Relevance,” <i>Journal of Anthropological Sciences</i> 95 (2017): 67–108, esp. 73–76 and 81–82, https://doi.org/10.4436/jass.95017. [Open access]","Fernando Diez-Martín et al., \"The Origin of The Acheulean: The 1.7 Million-Year-Old Site of FLK West, Olduvai Gorge (Tanzania),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 5 (2015): 17839, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep17839. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Early Stone Age Tools,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/stone-tools/early-stone-age-tools. [Open access]","Andy I. R. Herries, \"A Chronological Perspective on the Acheulian and Its Transition to the Middle Stone Age in Southern Africa: The Question of the Fauresmith,\" <i>International Journal of Evolutionary Biology</i> 2011 (2011): 961401, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3139141/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Acheuleanhandaxes.jpg","title":"Acheulean","desc":"Acheuleanhandaxes. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acheuleanhandaxes.jpg","alt":"Acheuleanhandaxes"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does a hand axe demand forethought the Oldowan never required?","a":"Because the knapper works towards a shape held in mind — a teardrop or oval flaked on both faces with a cutting edge running right round it — rather than simply striking off a useful edge."},{"q":"Why is the Acheulean the longest-lived way of working stone humans have kept up?","a":"Because it begins about 1.76 million years ago at Kokiselei and Konso and runs on until about 125,000 years ago, ending far earlier in some regions than in others."},{"q":"Why is the symmetry of a hand axe still unexplained?","a":"Because butchery experiments suggest a neat outline cuts no better than a rough one. Suggestions run from the demands of the knapping itself to a proposal that a fine axe advertised its maker's skill, which critics dismiss as untestable."}]},
{"id":"wh-028","num":"28","category":"World · History","question":"Long taken for a thunderbolt fallen from the sky, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a stone worked to an edge all round and gripped without any handle.","answer":"Hand axe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.76 Mya – under 200,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1800, by John Frere</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accepted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>hand axe</b> is a stone tool flaked on both faces into a teardrop, oval or pear shape, with a cutting edge running round most of its rim. It has no handle and no socket: the wide butt sits in the palm while the working edge is turned against the material, which is where the name comes from. Most are between 12 and 20 centimetres (4.7 and 7.9 inches) long, though British river gravels have yielded giants of more than 30, the largest a point from Furze Platt measuring 32.3 centimetres (12.7 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The maker roughs out the shape with a hammerstone and then finishes the edge with a softer billet of antler, bone or hardwood, which lifts thin flakes without shattering the piece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wear along the edges shows them used to butcher and skin carcasses and to work wood, and digging has been proposed as well, which is why they are often called the pocket knife of the Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For centuries in Europe they were taken for thunderstones, dropped where lightning struck, and were kept in houses as charms against storms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1800 the Society of Antiquaries of London printed John Frere's two-page account of pointed stone tools dug deep from a brick clay pit at Hoxne in Suffolk, beside the bones of giant animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He concluded that they came from a very remote period indeed, even beyond that of the present world, and was then ignored for sixty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jacques Boucher de Perthes gathered similar stones from the gravels at Abbeville and argued the same case, to much the same reception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1859 the geologist Joseph Prestwich, the antiquary John Evans and the geologist Charles Lyell went to see the Somme gravels at Amiens and Abbeville for themselves, all came away convinced, and the antiquity of humankind was settled at last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Hand axe","questions":["Roughed out with a hammerstone and finished with a soft antler billet, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> served to butcher carcasses, dig for roots and work wood — the pocket knife of the Stone Age.","Ignored when John Frere reported it from Hoxne in 1800, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> finally proved humankind's antiquity in 1859, when British visitors came to see the Somme gravels for themselves."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Alastair J. M. Key and Stephen J. Lycett, “Form and Function in the Lower Palaeolithic: History, Progress, and Continued Relevance,” <i>Journal of Anthropological Sciences</i> 95 (2017): 67–108, esp. 69–71 and 77–78, https://doi.org/10.4436/jass.95017. [Open access]","Kristiina Johanson, “The Changing Meaning of ‘Thunderbolts’,” <i>Folklore: Electronic Journal of Folklore</i> 42 (2009): 129–74, esp. 129–31, https://doi.org/10.7592/FEJF2009.42.johanson. [Open access]","James Sackett, \"Boucher de Perthes and the Discovery of Human Antiquity,\" <i>Bulletin of the History of Archaeology</i> 24 (2014): art. 2, https://doi.org/10.5334/bha.242. [Open access]","Li Li et al., \"A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]","Letty Ingrey, Martin Bates, Sarah Duffy and Matt Pope, \"A New Palaeolithic Giant Handaxe from Britain: Initial Results from Excavations at Maritime Academy, Medway, Kent,\" <i>Internet Archaeology</i> 61 (2023), under \"Description of the Maritime Academy 'Giant' Handaxe\" and table 2, https://doi.org/10.11141/ia.61.6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Vuistbijl_in_silex%2C_250_000_tot_38_000_BP%2C_vindplaats-_Meeuwen%2C_In_den_Damp%2C_21.10.1985.jpg/1920px-Vuistbijl_in_silex%2C_250_000_tot_38_000_BP%2C_vindplaats-_Meeuwen%2C_In_den_Damp%2C_21.10.1985.jpg","title":"Hand axe","desc":"Vuistbijl in silex, 250 000 tot 38 000 BP, vindplaats: Meeuwen, In den Damp, 21.10.1985, 5°31'25\" NB - 51°03'37\" OL, collectie Gallo-Romeins Museum Tongeren, IDD 4. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vuistbijl_in_silex,_250_000_tot_38_000_BP,_vindplaats-_Meeuwen,_In_den_Damp,_21.10.1985.jpg","alt":"Vuistbijl in silex, 250 000 tot 38 000 BP, vindplaats- Meeuwen, In den Damp, 21.10.1985"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why is a hand axe called an axe when it has no handle?","a":"Because the wide butt sits in the palm while the working edge is turned against the material — there is no handle and no socket, and the name records how it is held."},{"q":"Why were these tools kept in European houses as charms?","a":"Because for centuries they were taken for thunderstones, dropped where lightning struck, and were kept against storms rather than recognised as anything anyone had made."},{"q":"Why did it take sixty years for John Frere's conclusion to be accepted?","a":"Because he was simply ignored, as was Boucher de Perthes after him. It took Prestwich, Evans and Lyell going to the Somme gravels themselves in 1859 and all coming away convinced to settle the antiquity of humankind."}]},
{"id":"wh-029","num":"29","category":"World · History","question":"Warmth in the cold, light after sunset, safety from predators and food made softer and safer all followed from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose hardest step was kindling one at will.","answer":"control of fire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Contested traces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1.5 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wonderwerk Cave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Habitual use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 400,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>control of fire</b> covers three quite different achievements, and archaeologists are careful to keep them apart: using a blaze that nature lit, keeping one alive and carrying it about, and kindling a new one whenever it is wanted. Each step is harder than the one before, and only the last frees a group from waiting on lightning and bush fires. The rewards were enormous: warmth in cold latitudes, light after sunset, a defence against large predators, and food made softer, safer and quicker to digest. Richard Wrangham argues that cooking did more than feed people, and that the energy it released paid for the smaller gut and larger brain of early Homo from about 1.7 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The trouble is that scorched ground proves nothing by itself, because wildfires, lightning strikes and even smouldering dung leave much the same residue. <br><br> A convincing case needs burning that happened where it now lies, deep inside a cave or gathered into a single patch rather than smeared across a landscape. The oldest widely accepted are the ashed plants some 30 metres (100 feet) inside Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, about a million years old, and the burnt seed, wood and flint at Gesher Benot Ya'aqov in Israel, about 790,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa argued in 2011 that a handful of early sites is not the same as habit, and that fire becomes routine equipment only around 400,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even those sites showed people using fire rather than making it, and until recently the oldest proof of deliberate ignition was Neanderthal hand axes from France struck with pyrite some 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then in December 2025 a team at Barnham in Suffolk reported heated ground, fire-cracked hand axes and two lumps of iron pyrite, a mineral rare in that landscape and so carried in, all about 400,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"control of fire","questions":["Archaeologists split the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into three feats — using a blaze that nature lit, keeping one alive to carry about, and kindling a fresh flame whenever it is wanted.","Richard Wrangham holds that cooking made possible by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> released the energy for the smaller gut and larger brain of early Homo, from about 1.7 million years ago."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Francesco Berna et al., “Microstratigraphic Evidence of in situ Fire in the Acheulean Strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape Province, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 20 (2012): E1215–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3356665/. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","A. C. Sorensen, E. Claud, and M. Soressi, “Neandertal Fire-Making Technology Inferred from Microwear Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 10065, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-28342-9. [Open access]","Rob Davis et al., “Earliest Evidence of Making Fire,” <i>Nature</i> 649, no. 8097 (2025): 631–37, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09855-6. [Paywalled]","John A. J. Gowlett, \"The Discovery of Fire by Humans: A Long and Convoluted Process,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1696 (2016): 20150164, under \"Fire and the Cooking Hypothesis,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4874402/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why do archaeologists keep three achievements apart under one heading?","a":"Because using a blaze that nature lit, keeping one alive and carrying it about, and kindling a new one whenever it is wanted are each harder than the last — and only the third frees a group from waiting on lightning and bush fires."},{"q":"Why does scorched ground prove nothing by itself?","a":"Because wildfires, lightning strikes and even smouldering dung leave much the same residue. A convincing case needs burning that happened where it now lies, deep inside a cave or gathered into a single patch rather than smeared across a landscape."},{"q":"Why is a handful of early sites not the same as habit?","a":"Because Roebroeks and Villa argued in 2011 that scattered instances show fire used rather than relied on, and that it becomes routine equipment only around 400,000 years ago."}]},
{"id":"wh-030","num":"30","category":"World · History","question":"Reaching 140 metres (460 feet) into a hillside at the dry edge of the Kalahari, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds some 2 million years of deposits, one of the longest records of human life anywhere.","answer":"Wonderwerk Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire traces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 Mya</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Wonderwerk Cave</b> is a long, level tunnel running about 140 metres (460 feet) into a hillside of the Kuruman Hills in the Northern Cape of South Africa, at the dry edge of the Kalahari. Its Afrikaans name means 'miracle', and the deposits on its floor, dug so far to about 4 metres (13 feet), cover roughly 2 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That makes it one of the longest records of human life known anywhere, and unusually it is a single sheltered sequence rather than a scatter of open-air camps. Oldowan tools lie near the bottom, left about 1.8 million years ago, which is the oldest evidence anywhere of people living inside a cave rather than merely passing one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above them come Acheulean hand axes, then the tools of later ages, and near the top the engraved stones and painted walls left within the last 10,000 years, the paintings clustered in the first 40 metres (130 feet) where daylight still reaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> For all that depth of time, the cave is best known for one thing: fire. In 2012 a team examining undisturbed sediment under the microscope found ashed plant material and burnt bone about 30 metres (100 feet) inside, in Acheulean layers around a million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the burning had happened where it lay, and far enough in that a bush fire could not have reached it, this became the earliest solid evidence of fire used by human hands. Work published in 2026 pushed the traces back further still, to somewhere between 1.07 and 1.79 million years ago, using a new way of reading heat damage in fossil bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If that holds, the hominins carrying flame into this hillside were not our own kind but Homo erectus, tending a fire they had brought in from outside rather than kindled themselves.","citation":"","answerText":"Wonderwerk Cave","questions":["Oldowan tools left about 1.8 million years ago make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the oldest known place where people lived inside a cave rather than merely passing through one.","Ashed plants and burnt bone found 30 metres (100 feet) inside in 2012 made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the site of the earliest solid evidence of fire used by human hands."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","south africa"],"sources":["Liora Kolska Horwitz and Michael Chazan, “Past and Present at Wonderwerk Cave (Northern Cape Province, South Africa),” <i>African Archaeological Review</i> 32, no. 4 (2015): 595–612, esp. 596, 603–4 and 608, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10437-015-9208-5. [Open access]","Michael Chazan, “Technological Trends in the Acheulean of Wonderwerk Cave, South Africa,” <i>African Archaeological Review</i> 32, no. 4 (2015): 701–28, under “Context” and “Technological Development in the Wonderwerk Sequence,” https://hdl.handle.net/1807/90627. [Open access]","Francesco Berna et al., “Microstratigraphic Evidence of in situ Fire in the Acheulean Strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape Province, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 20 (2012): E1215–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3356665/. [Open access]","M. Dolores Marin-Monfort et al., “New Evidence for Early Pleistocene Use of Fire at Wonderwerk Cave (South Africa),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 6 (2026): e0347480, under “Wonderwerk Cave” and “Conclusions,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0347480. [Open access]","Andy I. R. Herries, \"A Chronological Perspective on the Acheulian and Its Transition to the Middle Stone Age in Southern Africa: The Question of the Fauresmith,\" <i>International Journal of Evolutionary Biology</i> 2011 (2011): 961401, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3139141/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Wonderwerk Cave","at":[23.5539,-27.845]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Wonderwerk Cave an unusually good record of human life?","a":"Because it is a single sheltered sequence rather than a scatter of open-air camps: about 4 metres of deposit dug so far, covering roughly 2 million years in one place."},{"q":"Why was the burnt material here accepted where scorched ground elsewhere is not?","a":"Because the burning had happened where it lay, and about 30 metres inside the cave — far enough in that a bush fire could not have reached it. The ashed plant material and burnt bone sit in Acheulean layers around a million years old."},{"q":"Why do the Oldowan tools near the bottom matter beyond their age?","a":"Because they were left about 1.8 million years ago inside the cave, which is the oldest evidence anywhere of people living in one rather than merely passing it."}]},
{"id":"wh-032","num":"32","category":"World · History","question":"Known almost entirely from one layer of a filled-in sinkhole in northern Spain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived some 800,000 years ago and startled everyone with a strikingly modern face.","answer":"Homo antecessor","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 860,000 – 780,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997, by Bermúdez de Castro</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo antecessor</b> is an extinct human species known almost entirely from one layer of one site: level TD6 of the Gran Dolina, a filled-in sinkhole laid open by a railway trench cut through the Sierra de Atapuerca in northern Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bones lie somewhere between 780,000 and 860,000 years old, which makes them among the oldest human remains anywhere in western Europe, from a time when the continent was only thinly and intermittently peopled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A team led by José María Bermúdez de Castro named the species in 1997, choosing the Latin word for 'a scout or forerunner', the one who goes ahead of the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What made the find famous was a partial face, catalogued ATD6-69, belonging to a boy of about 10, whose cheeks are hollowed and whose midface is flat in a way that looks startlingly like ours. No other human of that antiquity has a face like it, and later work on how the skull grew has confirmed that the adults kept those modern-looking features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The same layer holds something else: the oldest known evidence of cannibalism anywhere in the human record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The human bones there carry the same cut marks, the same smashing for marrow and the same casual discard as the deer and horse bones lying beside them, and most of the victims were children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The species was announced as the last common ancestor of ourselves and the Neanderthals, a claim that made it famous and that specialists doubted almost at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The question was settled in 2020 by a technique that reads ancient proteins locked in tooth enamel, which survive far longer than DNA and can be recovered where no genome could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those proteins place it as a close cousin of the line leading to us, the Neanderthals and the Denisovans, standing just outside that line rather than at its root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo antecessor","questions":["Named in 1997 for the Latin word meaning 'a scout or forerunner', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became famous for ATD6-69, the flat, modern-looking face of a boy of about 10.","The TD6 layer that yielded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also holds the oldest known evidence of cannibalism, its human bones cut and smashed for marrow like the deer beside them."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Frido Welker et al., “The Dental Proteome of <i>Homo antecessor</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 580, no. 7802 (2020): 235–38, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2153-8. [Open access]","I. Campaña et al., “New Interpretation of the Gran Dolina-TD6 Bearing <i>Homo antecessor</i> Deposits through Sedimentological Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 34799, under “The Gran Dolina Site” and “Results,” https://doi.org/10.1038/srep34799. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","James Cole, \"Assessing the Calorific Significance of Episodes of Human Cannibalism in the Palaeolithic,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7 (2017): 44707, table 1, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep44707. [Open access]","Marina Mosquera et al., \"Shedding Light on the Early Pleistocene of TD6 (Gran Dolina, Atapuerca, Spain): The Technological Sequence and Occupational Inferences,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 13, no. 1 (2018): e0190889, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0190889. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Homo_antecessor_reconstruccion.jpg","title":"Homo antecessor","desc":"Reconstruction of Homo antecessor skull at Museu d'Arqueologia de Catalunya (Barcelona, Spain) Reconstrucción del cráneo de Homo antecessor, Museo de Arqueología de Cataluña (Barcelona, España). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_antecessor_reconstruccion.jpg","alt":"Homo antecessor reconstruccion"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the boy's face make Homo antecessor famous?","a":"Because his cheeks are hollowed and his midface flat in a way that looks startlingly like ours, and no other human of that antiquity has a face like it. Later work on how the skull grew confirmed that the adults kept those features."},{"q":"Why is the cannibalism at Gran Dolina read as ordinary rather than ritual?","a":"Because the human bones carry the same cut marks, the same smashing for marrow and the same casual discard as the deer and horse bones lying beside them, and most of the victims were children."},{"q":"Why could ancient proteins settle what DNA could not?","a":"Because proteins locked in tooth enamel survive far longer than DNA and can be recovered where no genome could. Read in 2020, they place the species as a close cousin of our line rather than at its root."}]},
{"id":"wh-033","num":"33","category":"World · History","question":"A low limestone range in the province of Burgos, sliced open by a railway cutting, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold caves in which five different hominin species have been identified.","answer":"Atapuerca Mountains","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Record</span><span class=\"dt-v\">back to c. 1.4 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Atapuerca Mountains</b> are a low limestone range about 15 kilometres (9 miles) east of Burgos, in northern Spain, riddled with caves that filled with sediment over the past million and a half years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their sites have been known since the late 19th century, and what opened them was a railway cutting through the hill, the Trinchera del Ferrocarril, whose fossils were reported to the authorities in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Systematic excavation began in 1978 with the founding of the Atapuerca Project, and the range became a World Heritage Site in 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five hominin species are represented in its caves, among them Homo antecessor from the Gran Dolina, identified in 1994 and then the oldest species known in western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The strangest site is the Sima de los Huesos, the Pit of Bones, where the skulls of several people some 430,000 years old came to light in 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After three decades of digging, the number of individuals in that one deposit is put at 29, and the collection has nearly doubled since 2004, when about 4,000 specimens were known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are mostly teenagers and young adults, with ages at death of the kind a catastrophe leaves rather than a burial ground, and many of the fossils were destroyed by amateur cavers before proper excavation began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anatomy and ancient DNA read them not as a separate species but as a basal population of the Neanderthal line, and a paper of 2003 argued the pit holds the earliest possible evidence of funerary behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest find came out of the Sima del Elefante in 2022 and was published in 2025: most of the midface of a hominin, nicknamed Pink, from a level dated between 1.4 and 1.1 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the earliest human face known in western Europe, primitive enough to be set apart from the Gran Dolina's Homo antecessor and assigned instead to Homo affinis erectus, which its describers read as a population turnover at the end of the Early Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Atapuerca Mountains","questions":["Deep in the Pit of Bones at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the bones of 29 people, mostly teenagers and young adults, came out of a single deposit some 430,000 years old.","Published in 2025, the fossil nicknamed Pink came from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and is most of a hominin midface, 1.1 to 1.4 million years old and the oldest face in western Europe."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","spain"],"sources":["Fundación Atapuerca, “Los yacimientos de la sierra de Atapuerca,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://www.atapuerca.org/es/atapuerca/Yacimientos-de-Atapuerca. [Open access]","Fundación Atapuerca, “Proyecto Atapuerca,” accessed 1 August 2026, https://www.atapuerca.org/es/atapuerca/Proyecto-Atapuerca. [Open access]","José María Bermúdez de Castro et al., “The Sima de los Huesos Middle Pleistocene Hominin Site (Burgos, Spain): Estimation of the Number of Individuals,” <i>The Anatomical Record</i> 304, no. 7 (2021): 1463–77, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.24551. [Paywalled]","Rosa Huguet et al., “The Earliest Human Face of Western Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 640 (2025): 707–13, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08681-0. [Paywalled]","Alessandro Urciuoli et al., “Semicircular Canals Shed Light on Bottleneck Events in the Evolution of the Neanderthal Clade,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 972, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-56155-8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Sierra_de_Atapuerca.jpg/1920px-Sierra_de_Atapuerca.jpg","title":"Atapuerca Mountains","desc":"Overview of the Sierra de Atapuerca. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sierra_de_Atapuerca.jpg","alt":"Sierra de Atapuerca"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Atapuerca Mountains","at":[-3.5222,42.3667]},"why":[{"q":"Why do the Atapuerca caves hold so long a record?","a":"Because they filled with sediment over the past million and a half years, and a railway cutting through the hill laid the filling open. Five hominin species are represented in them."},{"q":"Why are the dead in the Pit of Bones read as a catastrophe rather than a cemetery?","a":"Because they are mostly teenagers and young adults — ages at death of the kind a catastrophe leaves rather than a burial ground. Twenty-nine individuals are now counted in that one deposit."},{"q":"Why is the face nicknamed Pink assigned away from Homo antecessor?","a":"Because it is primitive enough to be set apart from the Gran Dolina fossils, and comes from a level dated between 1.4 and 1.1 million years ago. Its describers assigned it to Homo affinis erectus and read it as a population turnover."}]},
{"id":"wh-034","num":"34","category":"World · History","question":"Named in 1908 from a single lower jaw dug out of a German sand pit, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was long cast as the common ancestor of the Neanderthals and ourselves.","answer":"Homo heidelbergensis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700,000 – 300,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1908, by Otto Schoetensack</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo heidelbergensis</b> is a proposed human species of the Middle Pleistocene, named in 1908 by Otto Schoetensack from a single lower jaw that quarrymen had dug out of a sand pit at Mauer, near Heidelberg, the year before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The jaw is oddly mixed: the bone is thick and heavy and the chin is missing altogether, yet the teeth are small and almost modern. Careful dating in 2010 put it at about 609,000 years old, give or take 40,000, which makes it one of the oldest human fossils anywhere in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Skulls later gathered under the name carry brains averaging around 1,200 cubic centimetres, not far short of ours, in bodies that were tall and powerfully built. For most of the 20th century the species was given the grandest job in the family tree: the common ancestor from which the Neanderthals grew in Europe and we grew in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The trouble is that the name has been made to carry almost anything that does not fit elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Middle Pleistocene fossils from three continents that are plainly not Homo erectus, not Neanderthal and not us have been swept into it, a habit specialists call the muddle in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA then removed its best European exhibit, showing that the people in the Pit of Bones at Atapuerca sit on the Neanderthal line rather than at the fork above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 2021 a group of researchers proposed abandoning the name outright, along with its African twin Homo rhodesiensis, moving the African fossils to a new species called Homo bodoensis and the European ones, Mauer included, to the Neanderthals, a suggestion that has not been widely taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most workers now use the term narrowly, for the Mauer jaw and a handful of European fossils like it, and treat the wider category as a filing cabinet rather than a species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo heidelbergensis","questions":["The Mauer jaw, thick and chinless yet with small, almost modern teeth and dated to about 609,000 years, is the type specimen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and one of Europe's oldest.","Because Middle Pleistocene fossils from three continents were all swept under it, the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a filing cabinet — the muddle in the middle — rather than one clear species."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Mirjana Roksandic et al., “Resolving the ‘Muddle in the Middle’: The Case for <i>Homo bodoensis</i> sp. nov.,” <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 31, no. 1 (2022): 20–29, https://doi.org/10.1002/evan.21929. [Open access]","Otto Schoetensack, <i>Der Unterkiefer des Homo heidelbergensis aus den Sanden von Mauer bei Heidelberg</i> (Leipzig: Wilhelm Engelmann, 1908), under “I. Geologisch-paläontologischer Teil,” https://archive.org/details/o-schoetensack-der-unterkiefer-des-homo-heidelbergensis. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Aurélien Mounier and Marta Mirazón Lahr, \"Deciphering African Late Middle Pleistocene Hominin Diversity and the Origin of Our Species,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 3406, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-019-11213-w. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., \"Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?\" <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Homo_heidelbergensis_%28Erstbeschreibung%29_04.jpg/1920px-Homo_heidelbergensis_%28Erstbeschreibung%29_04.jpg","title":"Homo heidelbergensis","desc":"Type specimen of Homo heidelbergenis from Mauer (near Heidelberg, Germany): radiography from the first description. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_heidelbergensis_(Erstbeschreibung)_04.jpg","alt":"Homo heidelbergensis (Erstbeschreibung) 04"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why do specialists call this species the muddle in the middle?","a":"Because the name has been made to carry almost anything that does not fit elsewhere: Middle Pleistocene fossils from three continents that are plainly not Homo erectus, not Neanderthal and not us have been swept into it."},{"q":"Why did ancient DNA weaken the species' claim to be our common ancestor?","a":"Because it removed the best European exhibit, showing that the people in the Pit of Bones at Atapuerca sit on the Neanderthal line rather than at the fork above it, where a common ancestor would have to stand."},{"q":"Why is the Mauer jaw itself described as oddly mixed?","a":"Because the bone is thick and heavy and the chin is missing altogether, yet the teeth are small and almost modern — one jaw carrying features from either side of the divide."}]},
{"id":"wh-035","num":"35","category":"World · History","question":"Blasted out of a German cave by quarrymen in 1856 and named as the first fossil human species ever recognised, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was drawn for a century as a stooping brute.","answer":"Neanderthal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Type fossil</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1856, Neander valley</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1864, by William King</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neanderthal</b> was a human species of Europe and western and central Asia, present from at least 400,000 years ago until about 40,000, and the closest relative our own species has ever had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name comes from the Neander valley near Düsseldorf, where quarrymen blasting limestone out of a cave in 1856 threw a skullcap and some limb bones onto the spoil heap. That valley had itself been named after Joachim Neander, a 17th-century hymn writer whose family had dressed their surname Neumann up in Greek, so the fossils are called, in effect, new man. William King proposed the species name Homo neanderthalensis in 1864, the first fossil human ever given one, though older finds from Engis and Gibraltar had already been dug up without anyone recognising what they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bones show a stocky, heavily muscled body built for cold, with a jutting midface, thick brow ridges, almost no chin, and a brain averaging slightly larger than ours. <br><br> They made Mousterian tools, glued stone points to shafts with birch tar, kept fire, hunted large animals at close range, and sometimes buried their dead. Deep in Bruniquel Cave in southern France, 336 metres (1,100 feet) from daylight, they broke stalagmites into two low rings about 176,500 years ago, which meant carrying fire far underground long before modern humans reached Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their brutish reputation traces to a single skeleton, the old man of La Chapelle-aux-Saints, whose spine was deformed by arthritis: Marcellin Boule reconstructed him in 1911 as a stooping, bent-kneed creature and took that for the whole species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 2010 a team led by Svante Pääbo published a draft Neanderthal genome and found what no bone could show, that Neanderthals and our own ancestors had interbred; everyone descended from those who left Africa carries a per cent or two of their DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They vanish around 40,000 years ago, and why is still argued over: competition, swinging climate, thin scattered populations, or simply being absorbed into far more numerous newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Neanderthal","questions":["One arthritic skeleton, the old man of La Chapelle-aux-Saints, was reconstructed in 1911 as a stooping <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and taken for the whole species, which is where the brutish image began.","Deep in Bruniquel Cave, 336 metres (1,100 feet) from daylight, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke stalagmites into rings about 176,500 years ago, long before modern humans reached Europe."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["William King, \"The Reputed Fossil Man of the Neanderthal,\" <i>Quarterly Journal of Science</i> 1 (1864): 88–97, https://archive.org/details/biostor-195152. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., \"A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,\" <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","Jacques Jaubert et al., \"Early Neanderthal Constructions Deep in Bruniquel Cave in Southwestern France,\" <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7605 (2016): 111–14, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature18291. [Paywalled]","Marcellin Boule, \"L'homme fossile de La Chapelle-aux-Saints,\" <i>Annales de Paléontologie</i> 6–8 (1911–13), https://archive.org/details/b22463355. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., \"The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,\" <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Krist Vaesen et al., \"Inbreeding, Allee Effects and Stochasticity Might Be Sufficient to Account for Neanderthal Extinction,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 11 (2019): e0225117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0225117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Neanderthal_at_AMNH.jpg/1920px-Neanderthal_at_AMNH.jpg","title":"Neanderthal","desc":"Neanderthal composite skeleton at the American Museum of Natural History. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neanderthal_at_AMNH.jpg","alt":"Neanderthal at AMNH"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why does the name mean, in effect, new man?","a":"Because the Neander valley was named after Joachim Neander, a 17th-century hymn writer whose family had dressed their surname Neumann up in Greek — so the fossils are called after the valley and the valley after him."},{"q":"Why does the brutish reputation rest on a mistake?","a":"Because it traces to one skeleton, the old man of La Chapelle-aux-Saints, whose spine was deformed by arthritis. Marcellin Boule reconstructed him in 1911 as a stooping, bent-kneed creature and took that for the whole species."},{"q":"Why did the 2010 genome show what no bone could?","a":"Because interbreeding leaves no skeletal mark but does leave DNA. Svante Pääbo's team found that Neanderthals and our own ancestors had interbred, and everyone descended from those who left Africa carries a per cent or two of their DNA."}]},
{"id":"wh-036","num":"36","category":"World · History","question":"Recognised from flints dug from gravel quarries near Paris in the 19th century, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shapes a core in advance so a flake of intended size and outline comes away in one blow.","answer":"Levallois technique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 40,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Levallois technique</b> is a way of working stone in which the core, the block being struck, is shaped in advance so that a flake of intended size and outline comes away in one blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It takes its name from Levallois-Perret, a suburb of Paris whose gravel quarries yielded such pieces during the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stoneworker trims around the edge of the block, works one face into a low dome, prepares a small flat surface to strike, and only then delivers the blow that frees the target flake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What is left is domed on one side, flat on the other and covered in the scars of that preparation, which is why such pieces are called tortoise cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The point of all this is predetermination: the flake exists in the maker's intention several steps before it exists in the stone, which is why it is read as evidence of planning ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some cores were set up to give one large flake, others a series of flakes, points or blades from the same prepared face, and a fresh round of trimming could make a core ready again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The method appears across Africa and Eurasia by about 300,000 years ago, and at Nor Geghi in Armenia it lies beside handaxes in layers dated to roughly 335,000 to 325,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because it turns up in widely separated regions that all had a long handaxe tradition behind them, many specialists argue it was worked out more than once rather than invented once and carried outwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both Neanderthals and early Homo sapiens used it, and the flakes alone rarely reveal which; one find at Umm el Tlel in Syria is unusually direct, a broken Levallois point lodged in the neck bone of a speared wild ass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Europe it holds on until roughly 40,000 years ago, when long narrow blades struck from tall cores take over, though in parts of Africa and Asia prepared cores were still worked long after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Levallois technique","questions":["Cores domed on one face and scarred by trimming are called tortoise cores, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reads as planning because the flake exists in the maker's mind first.","Appearing across Africa and Eurasia by about 300,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was used by both Neanderthals and early Homo sapiens, so its flakes rarely reveal the maker."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Éric Boëda et al., “A Levallois Point Embedded in the Vertebra of a Wild Ass (<i>Equus africanus</i>): Hafting, Projectiles and Mousterian Hunting Weapons,” <i>Antiquity</i> 73, no. 280 (1999): 394–402, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00088335. [Paywalled]","Metin I. Eren and Stephen J. Lycett, “Why Levallois? A Morphometric Comparison of Experimental ‘Preferential’ Levallois Flakes versus Debitage Flakes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 1 (2012): e29273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264556/. [Open access]","Daniel S. Adler et al., “Early Levallois Technology and the Lower to Middle Paleolithic Transition in the Southern Caucasus,” <i>Science</i> 345, no. 6204 (2014): 1609–13, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1256484. [Paywalled]","Sylvain Soriano and Paola Villa, “Early Levallois and the Beginning of the Middle Paleolithic in Central Italy,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 10 (2017): e0186082, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5650164/. [Open access]","James Blinkhorn, Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Michael D. Petraglia, and Simon Blockley, “Directional Changes in Levallois Core Technologies between Eastern Africa, Arabia, and the Levant during MIS 5,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11465, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8169925/. [Open access]","Li Li et al., “A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1290 and 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Levallois_core_%28FindID_967752%29.jpg/1920px-Levallois_core_%28FindID_967752%29.jpg","title":"Levallois technique","desc":"A flint levallois core of Middle Paleolithic date, c.250,000 BCE - 40,000. Description: The core is rounded on three sides and flat on the bottom where the final removal occurred. It has covering scaled semi-abrupt retouch. Kent County Council, Jo Ahmet, 2019-08-21 11:19:16, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Levallois_core_(FindID_967752).jpg","alt":"Levallois core"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Levallois technique read as evidence of planning ahead?","a":"Because the flake exists in the maker's intention several steps before it exists in the stone: the core is trimmed around the edge, domed on one face and given a striking surface, and only then is the target flake freed in one blow."},{"q":"Why do many specialists think the method was worked out more than once?","a":"Because it turns up in widely separated regions across Africa and Eurasia that all had a long handaxe tradition behind them, which is what an independently reached solution looks like rather than one invention carried outwards."},{"q":"Why can a Levallois flake rarely say who made it?","a":"Because both Neanderthals and early Homo sapiens used the technique, and the flakes alone do not distinguish them. One find at Umm el Tlel is unusually direct: a broken Levallois point lodged in the neck bone of a speared wild ass."}]},
{"id":"wh-037","num":"37","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a pair of rock shelters in the Dordogne, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the flake-tool industry the Neanderthals carried across Europe for more than a hundred thousand years.","answer":"Mousterian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1860s–1870s, Lartet and Christy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mousterian</b> is the stone tool industry of the Middle Paleolithic in Europe, western Asia and North Africa, current from roughly 300,000 to 40,000 years ago and, in Europe, the work of the Neanderthals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> It is named after Le Moustier, a pair of rock shelters above the Vézère in the Dordogne whose flint tools Lartet and Christy published in the 1860s and 1870s, and it is still the industry's type site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its assemblages are made of flakes rather than the great core tools of the earlier Acheulean: side scrapers above all, along with points and pieces notched or toothed along one edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The flakes come off prepared cores, worked by the Levallois method or by turning a disc-shaped core, and many were then mounted on wooden shafts with birch tar or natural bitumen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> What has kept the Mousterian argued over for a century is that its assemblages differ sharply from cave to cave, in ways no one has agreed how to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> François Bordes sorted the tools into 63 types and the assemblages into five recurring kinds, among them the scraper-heavy Quina and a variant that still made handaxes, and read each as the mark of a separate people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Lewis and Sally Binford answered in 1966 that one people doing different jobs at different places would leave exactly that pattern, and the Bordes–Binford debate became one of the defining arguments of 20th-century archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A later line of work suggested something plainer still: a scraper resharpened until it is worn down changes shape as it goes, so one type may simply be another further through its life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The makers were not the same everywhere: in Europe it is Neanderthal work, but in the Levant, at caves such as Skhul, Qafzeh and Tabun, Neanderthals and early modern humans left assemblages that cannot be told apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It ends around 40,000 years ago, as blade-based Upper Paleolithic industries spread and the Neanderthals disappear, though whether one caused the other is still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mousterian","questions":["Side scrapers, points and notched flakes struck from prepared cores and fixed to shafts with birch tar mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across Europe for over a hundred thousand years.","The 63 tool types were read as separate peoples until 1966, when the answer came that one people doing different jobs would leave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looking just as varied."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Édouard Lartet and Henry Christy, <i>Reliquiae Aquitanicae: Being Contributions to the Archaeology and Palaeontology of Périgord and the Adjoining Provinces of Southern France</i>, ed. Thomas Rupert Jones (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865–75), plates III and V, https://archive.org/details/reliquiaeaquitan00lartuoft. [Open access]","François Bordes, “Mousterian Cultures in France,” <i>Science</i> 134, no. 3482 (1961): 803–10, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.134.3482.803. [Paywalled]","Lewis R. Binford and Sally R. Binford, “A Preliminary Analysis of Functional Variability in the Mousterian of Levallois Facies,” <i>American Anthropologist</i> 68, no. 2 (1966): 238–95, https://doi.org/10.1525/aa.1966.68.2.02a001030. [Paywalled]","Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Marcel J. L. Th. Niekus et al., “Middle Paleolithic Complex Technology and a Neandertal Tar-Backed Tool from the Dutch North Sea,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 44 (2019): 22081–87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6825292/. [Open access]","Michael J. Shott, “Dibble’s Reduction Thesis: Implications for Global Lithic Analysis,” <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 7, no. 1 (2024): 12, https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41982-024-00178-y. [Open access]","Patrick Schmidt, Radu Iovita, Armelle Charrié-Duhaut, Gunther Möller, Abay Namen, and Ewa Dutkiewicz, “Ochre-Based Compound Adhesives at the Mousterian Type-Site Document Complex Cognition and High Investment,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 8 (2024): eadl0822, https://hal.science/hal-04484835v1/document. [Open access]","Jacopo Gennai, “The Mousterian in North-Western Tuscany: Publishing Fieldwork Documentation Leads to a New Stratigraphical Interpretation of the Piano di Mommio Sites,” <i>Open Research Europe</i> 4 (2024): 37, under “The Mousterian industries,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11109565/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Mousterian_Culture_and_Late_Stone_Age_Stone_Tools._Notch_for_sharpening_wood%2C_and_denticulate_for_sawing_wood_and_bone._Rosh_En_Mor_and_En_Aqev._250%2C000-22%2C000_BP._Israel.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Mousterian","desc":"Mousterian Culture and Late Stone Age Stone Tools. Notch for sharpening wood, and denticulate for sawing wood and bone. Rosh En Mor and En Aqev. 250,000-22,000 BP. Israel Israel Museum, Jerusalem, Israel. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mousterian_Culture_and_Late_Stone_Age_Stone_Tools._Notch_for_sharpening_wood,_and_denticulate_for_sawing_wood_and_bone._Rosh_En_Mor_and_En_Aqev._250,000-22,000_BP._Israel.jpg","alt":"Mousterian Culture and Late Stone Age Stone Tools. Notch for sharpening wood, and denticulate for sawing wood and bone. Rosh En Mor and En Aqev. 250,000-22,000 BP. Israel"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why has the Mousterian been argued over for a century?","a":"Because its assemblages differ sharply from cave to cave in ways no one has agreed how to explain. Bordes read each recurring kind as the mark of a separate people; the Binfords answered that one people doing different jobs at different places would leave exactly that pattern."},{"q":"Why might Bordes's tool types be fewer than they look?","a":"Because a scraper resharpened until it is worn down changes shape as it goes, so one type may simply be another further through its life rather than a different tool made by different hands."},{"q":"Why can the industry not stand for Neanderthals everywhere?","a":"Because the makers were not the same everywhere: in Europe it is Neanderthal work, but in the Levant, at caves such as Skhul, Qafzeh and Tabun, Neanderthals and early modern humans left assemblages that cannot be told apart."}]},
{"id":"wh-038","num":"38","category":"World · History","question":"Known first from one girl's finger bone and the genome read out of it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were an Asian people whose DNA still runs in New Guinea and Australia.","answer":"Denisovans","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lineage split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 400,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Identified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2010, from DNA</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Denisovans</b> are an extinct human population of Asia, and the first kind of human ever recognised from DNA rather than from the shape of its bones. They came to light in 2008 in Denisova Cave in the Altai mountains of southern Siberia, as a fragment of one girl's finger bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2010 a team led by Svante Pääbo sequenced it and found a lineage that was neither Neanderthal nor modern human, then named it after the cave, itself named after a hermit said to have sheltered there in the 18th century. They still have no agreed scientific name, because naming a species requires a described fossil and for years there was almost nothing to describe: a finger bone, a few teeth and some splinters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The genome put their split from the Neanderthal line at roughly 400,000 years ago and showed them spread across Asia, from Siberia to the Tibetan Plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One splinter from the same cave, from a girl who died about 90,000 years ago, turned out to have had a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father, the only first-generation cross between two kinds of human ever found. Their descendants are alive today: people in New Guinea and Aboriginal Australians carry roughly 4 to 6 per cent Denisovan DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tibetans carry a Denisovan version of a gene that helps the body cope with thin air, so the inheritance is still doing useful work high on the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A jawbone from Baishiya Karst Cave in Tibet, about 160,000 years old, was identified as Denisovan in 2019 from proteins surviving in its teeth, the first such fossil found outside Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then in 2025 the almost complete Harbin skull, once named Homo longi, was tied to them both by its proteins and by mitochondrial DNA taken from the hardened plaque on its teeth, giving a people known from a fingertip a face at last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Denisovans","questions":["Being the first humans known from DNA rather than bones, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still have no agreed scientific name, because naming a species needs a described fossil.","One splinter from Denisova Cave showed that a girl who died about 90,000 years ago had a Neanderthal mother and a father among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the only known first-generation cross."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","genetics"],"sources":["David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, abstract and “A Denisovan Individual,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Genome of the Offspring of a Neanderthal Mother and a Denisovan Father,” <i>Nature</i> 561, no. 7721 (2018): 113–16, abstract and opening paragraphs, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6130845/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, “Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, §4.2 “Who Were the Denisovans?,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Emilia Huerta-Sánchez et al., “Altitude Adaptation in Tibetans Caused by Introgression of Denisovan-Like DNA,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7513 (2014): 194–97, abstract, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4134395/. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu et al., “The Proteome of the Late Middle Pleistocene Harbin Individual,” <i>Science</i> 389, no. 6762 (2025): 704–7, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adu9677. [Paywalled]","Qiaomei Fu et al., “Denisovan Mitochondrial DNA from Dental Calculus of the >146,000-Year-Old Harbin Cranium,” <i>Cell</i> 188, no. 15 (2025): 3919–26, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2025.05.040. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Yuan_%282021%29_Fig._3-_Archaic_introgression_in_modern_humans_across_the_world_%28Denisovan_only%29.png","title":"Denisovans","desc":"Yuan (2021) Fig. 3- Archaic introgression in modern humans across the world (Denisovan only). 2021-10-29. Kai Yuan et al.,. CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuan_(2021)_Fig._3-_Archaic_introgression_in_modern_humans_across_the_world_(Denisovan_only).png","alt":"Yuan (2021) Fig. 3- Archaic introgression in modern humans across the world (Denisovan only)"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why do the Denisovans still have no scientific name?","a":"Because naming a species requires a described fossil, and for years there was almost nothing to describe: a finger bone, a few teeth and some splinters. They were recognised from DNA rather than from the shape of any bone."},{"q":"Why does Denisovan inheritance still do useful work today?","a":"Because Tibetans carry a Denisovan version of a gene that helps the body cope with thin air, and people in New Guinea and Aboriginal Australians carry roughly 4 to 6 per cent Denisovan DNA."},{"q":"Why was the Harbin skull such a significant identification?","a":"Because it tied an almost complete skull to them, by proteins and by mitochondrial DNA taken from the hardened plaque on its teeth — giving a people known from a fingertip a face at last."}]},
{"id":"wh-039","num":"39","category":"World · History","question":"Cool enough inside for ancient DNA to survive, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Altai mountains is the only place known to have housed Neanderthals, Denisovans and modern humans.","answer":"Denisova Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">back to c. 300,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1977, dug from 1982</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Finger bone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2008</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Denisova Cave</b> is a limestone cave above the Anuy river in the Altai mountains of southern Siberia, and the only place on earth known to have been lived in by three different kinds of human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its three chambers hold deposits reaching back some 300,000 years, sifted since 1977 and dug systematically from 1982 by archaeologists from Novosibirsk, and it takes its name from a hermit said to have sheltered in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cave is cool and its temperature steady, so ancient DNA survives here far better than at the warm sites where most human fossils turn up. That is why a scrap of a girl's finger bone found in 2008 could be sequenced at all, revealing the Denisovans, a human population nobody had suspected existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neanderthals lived here too, modern humans arrived by about 45,000 years ago, and the one known child of a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father came out of these same deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Working out who was present when took years of painstaking dating: optical readings on sand grains and radiocarbon on bone, published in 2019, traced occupation of the cave back about 300,000 years and put its oldest Denisovan fossil as early as 195,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The greater trick was pulling human DNA straight out of the dirt with no bone at all, shown in 2017 to work across four Eurasian caves including this one, and turned here in 2021 on 728 sediment samples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That sediment DNA sketched the order of tenants: Denisovans first, Neanderthals arriving later, a stretch when Denisovan traces vanish altogether, then both again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Among the objects left behind are a sewing needle of bird bone about 50,000 years old and a polished green stone bracelet with a neatly drilled hole, whose age and whose maker are both still argued over. The cave's lasting contribution is the method it proved: that a few scraps and a bucketful of sediment can hold the history of three kinds of human passing through one doorway.","citation":"","answerText":"Denisova Cave","questions":["From a girl's finger bone found here in 2008 came the first evidence of the Denisovans, because the cool, steady air of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let ancient DNA survive.","By pulling human DNA straight from the dirt of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, researchers traced its tenants — Denisovans first, then Neanderthals — across more than 700 sediment samples."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","russia"],"sources":["David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, under “Introduction,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “Neandertal and Denisovan DNA from Pleistocene Sediments,” <i>Science</i> 356, no. 6338 (2017): 605–8, abstract, author manuscript at https://eprints.bournemouth.ac.uk/29206/3/Revised_manuscript.pdf. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Genome of the Offspring of a Neanderthal Mother and a Denisovan Father,” <i>Nature</i> 561, no. 7721 (2018): 113–16, abstract, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6130845/. [Open access]","Elena I. Zavala et al., “Pleistocene Sediment DNA Reveals Hominin and Faunal Turnovers at Denisova Cave,” <i>Nature</i> 595, no. 7867 (2021): 399–403, abstract, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8277575/. [Open access]","Zenobia Jacobs et al., “Timing of Archaic Hominin Occupation of Denisova Cave in Southern Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 565, no. 7741 (2019): 594–99, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0843-2. [Paywalled]","Katerina Douka et al., “Age Estimates for Hominin Fossils and the Onset of the Upper Palaeolithic at Denisova Cave,” <i>Nature</i> 565, no. 7741 (2019): 640–44, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0870-z. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/%D0%98%D0%B7%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8C_%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0._01.jpg/1920px-%D0%98%D0%B7%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8C_%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0._01.jpg","title":"Denisova Cave","desc":"Денисова пещера: Солонешенский район, Алтайский край. Демин Алексей Барнаул, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%98%D0%B7%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%81%D1%8C_%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%94%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0._01.jpg","alt":"Известная на весь Мир Денисова пещера. 01"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Denisova Cave","at":[84.6761,51.3975]},"why":[{"q":"Why does ancient DNA survive so well at Denisova Cave?","a":"Because the cave is cool and its temperature steady, unlike the warm sites where most human fossils turn up. That is why a scrap of a girl's finger bone found in 2008 could be sequenced at all."},{"q":"Why was pulling DNA out of the sediment the greater trick?","a":"Because it needs no bone at all. Shown in 2017 to work across four Eurasian caves and turned here in 2021 on 728 samples, it sketched the order of tenants: Denisovans first, Neanderthals later, a stretch with no Denisovan trace, then both again."},{"q":"Why is this cave unique among human sites?","a":"Because it is the only place on earth known to have been lived in by three different kinds of human — Denisovans, Neanderthals and modern humans — and it yielded the one known child of a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father."}]},
{"id":"wh-040","num":"40","category":"World · History","question":"Standing about a metre (3 ft 3 in) tall with a brain the size of a chimpanzee's, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dug out of an Indonesian cave in 2003 and nicknamed the hobbit.","answer":"Homo floresiensis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Skeletons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100,000 – 60,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2003, at Liang Bua</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2004</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo floresiensis</b> is a very small human species from the island of Flores in Indonesia, known to almost everyone as the hobbit. A joint Indonesian and Australian team led by Mike Morwood found the first bones in 2003 in Liang Bua, a wide cave in the island's limestone hills, and named the species the following year. The type specimen is a partial female skeleton standing about a metre (3 ft 3 in) tall, and bones of several other individuals have come out of the same cave since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her braincase held about 426 cubic centimetres, close to a chimpanzee's and under a third of ours, yet stone tools lie in the very same deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first published dates put her at about 18,000 years ago, but a restudy of the cave's layers in 2016 moved the skeletons back to between 100,000 and 60,000 years, and the tools to around 50,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Many specialists refused to accept a new species and argued this was a modern human with a disease, proposing in turn microcephaly, a thyroid disorder, Laron syndrome and Down syndrome. Each was tested against the bones, and in the features that separate healthy people from sick ones the skeleton kept falling in with fossil humans rather than with either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where it came from is still open: a Homo erectus that shrank after reaching the island, or a smaller and older kind of human that left Africa, since the wrists and feet look strikingly primitive. Digging in the So'a basin has pushed the story back: jaws and teeth from about 700,000 years ago are as small or smaller, and an arm bone published in 2024 came from an adult smaller still, the shortest such bone ever reported for a fossil human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Flores lies beyond the deep strait Wallace drew as a line, so reaching it meant crossing open sea to an island that also held dwarf elephants and giant rats, and the hobbits vanish about 50,000 years ago, roughly when our own species arrives.","citation":"","answerText":"Homo floresiensis","questions":["First dated to about 18,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was pushed back to between 100,000 and 60,000 years when a 2016 restudy corrected the cave's layers.","Many refused a new species and read <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a diseased modern human, but tests for microcephaly, thyroid disorder and Down syndrome each fell in with fossils instead."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","indonesia"],"sources":["Peter Brown et al., “A New Small-Bodied Hominin from the Late Pleistocene of Flores, Indonesia,” <i>Nature</i> 431, no. 7012 (2004): 1055–61, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02999. [Paywalled]","Daisuke Kubo, Reiko T. Kono, and Yousuke Kaifu, “Brain Size of <i>Homo floresiensis</i> and Its Evolutionary Implications,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 280, no. 1760 (2013): 20130338, under “Endocranial Volume of LB1,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3652458/. [Open access]","Thomas Sutikna et al., “Revised Stratigraphy and Chronology for <i>Homo floresiensis</i> at Liang Bua in Indonesia,” <i>Nature</i> 532, no. 7599 (2016): 366–69, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature17179. [Paywalled]","Karen L. Baab et al., “A Critical Evaluation of the Down Syndrome Diagnosis for LB1, Type Specimen of <i>Homo floresiensis</i>,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 6 (2016): e0155731, abstract and “Conclusions,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0155731. [Open access]","Yousuke Kaifu et al., “Early Evolution of Small Body Size in <i>Homo floresiensis</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 6381, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-50649-7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Homo_floresiensis_skull_-_Naturmuseum_Senckenberg_-_DSC02091.JPG/1920px-Homo_floresiensis_skull_-_Naturmuseum_Senckenberg_-_DSC02091.JPG","title":"Homo floresiensis","desc":"Exhibit in Naturmuseum Senckenberg, Frankfurt am Main, Germany. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_floresiensis_skull_-_Naturmuseum_Senckenberg_-_DSC02091.JPG","alt":"Homo floresiensis skull - Naturmuseum Senckenberg"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why were the disease explanations tested and set aside?","a":"Because each was checked against the bones — microcephaly, a thyroid disorder, Laron syndrome, Down syndrome — and in the features that separate healthy people from sick ones the skeleton kept falling in with fossil humans rather than with either."},{"q":"Why is the origin of the hobbits still open?","a":"Because the wrists and feet look strikingly primitive, so it may be a Homo erectus that shrank after reaching the island, or a smaller and older kind of human that left Africa."},{"q":"Why does reaching Flores at all take some explaining?","a":"Because it lies beyond the deep strait Wallace drew as a line, so getting there meant crossing open sea — to an island that also held dwarf elephants and giant rats."}]},
{"id":"wh-041","num":"41","category":"World · History","question":"Hauled out of a South African cave chamber reachable only through a chimney 18 centimetres (7 inches) wide, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a brain under half the size of ours yet lived alongside the earliest Homo sapiens.","answer":"Homo naledi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">335,000 – 236,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2015, by Lee Berger</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo naledi</b> is a small-brained human species from the Rising Star cave system in South Africa, named in 2015 after the caves themselves, since <i>naledi</i> means 'star' in Sesotho. Two recreational cavers squeezed down a narrow chimney in September 2013 and came out into a chamber whose floor was littered with bones. That chimney drops about 12 metres (39 feet) and squeezes down to some 20 centimetres (8 inches), so the digging was done by six scientists slim enough to pass, recruited by Lee Berger through a notice on Facebook and all, as it happened, women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They brought up 1,550 pieces from at least 15 individuals, the richest haul of a single hominin species anywhere in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The anatomy is a mosaic: a brain of only 465 to 610 cubic centimetres, well under half of ours, a body about 1.44 metres (4 ft 9 in) tall, curved fingers and shoulders made for climbing, but wrists, legs and feet built for walking far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Everything about that skeleton suggested great antiquity, so the dates published in 2017 came as a shock: between 335,000 and 236,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That places this small-brained cousin in Africa at the same time as the earliest Homo sapiens, which means archaic bodies were not a phase the family left behind but a branch running alongside us. The harder question is how the bodies got so deep underground, since the bones carry no carnivore damage, arrived with almost no other animals, and show no sign of having been washed in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Berger argued they were carried in on purpose, and in 2023 his team went further, claiming deliberate burial and engravings cut into the cave walls, released as preprints alongside a Netflix documentary while review was still running. Both papers were published in 2025 with the journal's own verdict attached: one reviewer found the burial case convincing and another found it incomplete, while the engravings were judged an important but preliminary report, so the bones are certain and the story around them is not yet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo naledi","questions":["More than 1,550 bones of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from at least 15 individuals, were brought up by six slim scientists recruited through Facebook to pass a 12-metre chimney.","A 2023 claim that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had buried its dead and cut engravings into the cave walls was released alongside a Netflix film while peer review was still running."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","south africa"],"sources":["Lee R. Berger et al., “<i>Homo naledi</i>, a New Species of the Genus <i>Homo</i> from the Dinaledi Chamber, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 4 (2015): e09560, under “Introduction,” “Description” and “Body Size and Proportions,” https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.09560. [Open access]","Paul H. G. M. Dirks et al., “Geological and Taphonomic Context for the New Hominin Species <i>Homo naledi</i> from the Dinaledi Chamber, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 4 (2015): e09561, under “Cave Setting” and “Surface Modifications and Vertebrate–Bone Interactions,” https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.09561. [Open access]","Paul H. G. M. Dirks et al., “The Age of <i>Homo naledi</i> and Associated Sediments in the Rising Star Cave, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 6 (2017): e24231, abstract, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.24231. [Open access]","John Hawks et al., “New Fossil Remains of <i>Homo naledi</i> from the Lesedi Chamber, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 6 (2017): e24232, abstract, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.24232. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger et al., “Evidence for Deliberate Burial of the Dead by <i>Homo naledi</i>,” <i>eLife</i> 12 (2025): RP89106, eLife assessment, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.89106.3. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger et al., “An Initial Report of circa 241,000- to 335,000-Year-Old Rock Engravings and Their Relation to <i>Homo naledi</i> in the Rising Star Cave System, South Africa,” <i>eLife</i> 12 (2025): RP89102, eLife assessment, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.89102.3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Homo_naledi_skeletal_specimens.jpg/1920px-Homo_naledi_skeletal_specimens.jpg","title":"Homo naledi","desc":"Dinaledi skeletal specimens. The figure includes approximately all of the material incorporated in this diagnosis, including the holotype specimen, paratypes and referred material. These make up 737 partial or complete anatomical elements, many of which consist of several refitted specimens. Lee Roger Berger research team, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_naledi_skeletal_specimens.jpg","alt":"Homo naledi skeletal specimens"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did the dates published in 2017 come as a shock?","a":"Because everything about the skeleton suggested great antiquity — a brain of only 465 to 610 cubic centimetres and shoulders made for climbing — yet the bones date to between 335,000 and 236,000 years ago, alongside the earliest Homo sapiens."},{"q":"Why is how the bodies got so deep underground the harder question?","a":"Because the bones carry no carnivore damage, arrived with almost no other animals, and show no sign of having been washed in — so the ordinary ways a chamber fills with bone are all ruled out."},{"q":"Why are the bones certain while the story around them is not?","a":"Because the 2023 burial and engraving claims were released as preprints alongside a documentary while review was still running, and when published in 2025 one reviewer found the burial case convincing and another found it incomplete."}]},
{"id":"wh-042","num":"42","category":"World · History","question":"In the 1758 classification every other species got a list of distinguishing features, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> got the instruction to know itself and no bodily characters whatever.","answer":"Homo sapiens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 315,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1758, by Carl Linnaeus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homo sapiens</b> is the species every living person belongs to, and the only surviving member of a genus that for most of its history held several kinds of human at once. Carl Linnaeus named it in 1758, and where his other species each received a list of distinguishing features, ours received the instruction to know itself and no defining characters at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two centuries later a botanist tidied up the gap by designating Linnaeus himself as the type specimen, on the grounds that he was the one human the author had studied closely. The joke conceals a real difficulty, since a definition that cleanly separates our fossils from everybody else's has never been easy to write.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the bones do show is a high rounded braincase, a small face tucked beneath it, light brow ridges, and a chin, which is the one piece of bony anatomy no other human has ever had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Africa those features arrive piecemeal rather than together: Jebel Irhoud in Morocco carries some of them by about 315,000 years ago, Omo Kibish in Ethiopia by about 233,000, and no single site shows the whole package appearing at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the current picture is of a population spread across the continent and exchanging ancestry, rather than one cradle from which everything follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ventures beyond Africa began early and mostly failed, with a jaw from Misliya cave in Israel dated to roughly 180,000 years ago and later people at Skhul and Qafzeh, none of whom left descendants alive today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The expansion that held began around 60,000 years ago, reaching Australia by about 50,000 years ago, or 65,000 if the earliest Australian site is taken at face value, and Europe by about 45,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Along the way our ancestors met Neanderthals and Denisovans and had children with both, so being the only human left is a condition roughly 40,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homo sapiens","questions":["A chin is the one piece of bony anatomy no other human ever carried, marking <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart along with a high rounded braincase and a small tucked-in face.","The expansion that finally held began around 60,000 years ago and carried <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Australia by about 50,000 and Europe by 45,000, meeting Neanderthals along the way."],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"sources":["Carl Linnaeus, <i>Systema Naturae per Regna Tria Naturae</i>, 10th ed., vol. 1 (Stockholm: Laurentius Salvius, 1758), 20, https://archive.org/details/mobot31753000798873. [Open access]","Chris Stringer, \"The Origin and Evolution of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150237, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920294/. [Open access]","Simon Neubauer, Jean-Jacques Hublin, and Philipp Gunz, \"The Evolution of Modern Human Brain Shape,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 1 (2018): eaao5961, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5783678/. [Open access]","Céline M. Vidal et al., \"Age of the Oldest Known <i>Homo sapiens</i> from Eastern Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 601, no. 7894 (2022): 579–83, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8791829/. [Open access]","Andra Meneganzin, Telmo Pievani, and Giorgio Manzi, \"Pan-Africanism vs. Single-Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>: Putting the Debate in the Light of Evolutionary Biology,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 31, no. 4 (2022): 199–212, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9540121/. [Open access]","Israel Hershkovitz et al., \"The Earliest Modern Humans outside Africa,\" <i>Science</i> 359, no. 6374 (2018): 456–59, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aap8369. [Paywalled]","Chris Clarkson et al., \"Human Occupation of Northern Australia by 65,000 Years Ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 547, no. 7663 (2017): 306–10, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22968. [Paywalled]","Richard E. Green et al., \"A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,\" <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David Reich et al., \"Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,\" <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., \"The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,\" <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Homo_sapiens_-_Paleolithic_-_reconstruction_-_MUSE.jpg","title":"Homo sapiens","desc":"Ricostruzione di Homo sapiens del Paleolitico. Matteo De Stefano/MUSE This file was uploaded by MUSE - Science Museum of Trento in cooperation with Wikimedia Italia.,. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_sapiens_-_Paleolithic_-_reconstruction_-_MUSE.jpg","alt":"Homo sapiens - Paleolithic - reconstruction - MUSE"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why did Linnaeus leave our species without defining characters?","a":"Because where his other species each received a list of distinguishing features, ours received the instruction to know itself. A botanist later designated Linnaeus himself as the type specimen, on the grounds that he was the one human the author had studied closely."},{"q":"Why is the picture now a whole continent rather than one cradle?","a":"Because our features arrive piecemeal rather than together: Jebel Irhoud carries some by about 315,000 years ago, Omo Kibish by about 233,000, and no single site shows the whole package appearing at once."},{"q":"Why is being the only human left a recent condition?","a":"Because our ancestors met Neanderthals and Denisovans along the way and had children with both, so the state of having no other kind of human alive is roughly 40,000 years old."}]},
{"id":"wh-043","num":"43","category":"World · History","question":"A worked-out mine in Morocco whose fossils carry a modern face above a long low braincase nobody alive has, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pushed the age of our species back by about 100,000 years.","answer":"Jebel Irhoud","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 315,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Jebel Irhoud</b> is a worked-out barite mine about 100 kilometres (62 miles) west of Marrakesh in Morocco, and it holds the oldest fossils yet assigned to our own species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miners blasting the hillside in the early 1960s opened the deposits and turned up a human skull, which was read for a long time as an African Neanderthal and thought to be only a few tens of thousands of years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavations from 2004, led by Jean-Jacques Hublin and Abdelouahed Ben-Ncer, recovered 22 more fossils from at least five people, along with stone tools and the bones of the gazelles they ate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The deposits are full of flint that had been heated in ancient fires, and measuring the light stored in those burnt flakes put the layer at about 315,000 years ago, some 100,000 years older than anything previously called Homo sapiens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The faces are the first surprise, because they are small and tucked in, with jaws and teeth that would not draw a second glance today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The braincase behind those faces is the second surprise, since it is long and low rather than the globe that modern people carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Taken together the two say that our features were assembled piece by piece, with the modern face early and the rounded skull only later, and that brain shape went on changing well inside our own lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The location was a third surprise, because north-west Africa is about as far from the East African rift valleys as the continent allows, and the rift had long been treated as the cradle. That is part of why specialists moved from picturing one birthplace to picturing a population spread across the whole of Africa, with our features surfacing here and there and spreading between groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stone tools are among the earliest directly dated Middle Stone Age assemblages anywhere, which ties that toolkit to our species from close to its beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Jebel Irhoud","questions":["Heated flint from ancient fires dated the layer at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to about 315,000 years ago, which is roughly 100,000 years before any other fossil assigned to our species.","Sitting in the north-west corner of Africa, far from the East African rift long treated as our cradle, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helped replace one birthplace with a whole continent."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","morocco"],"sources":["Jean-Jacques Hublin et al., \"New Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the Pan-African Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 289–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22336. [Paywalled]","Andra Meneganzin, Telmo Pievani, and Giorgio Manzi, \"Pan-Africanism vs. Single-Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>: Putting the Debate in the Light of Evolutionary Biology,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 31, no. 4 (2022): 199–212, under \"Challenges and Implications of Jebel Irhoud,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9540121/. [Open access]","Daniel Richter et al., \"The Age of the Hominin Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, and the Origins of the Middle Stone Age,\" <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 293–96, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22335. [Paywalled]","Simon Neubauer, Jean-Jacques Hublin, and Philipp Gunz, \"The Evolution of Modern Human Brain Shape,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 1 (2018): eaao5961, table 1 and \"Gradual Globularization within <i>H. sapiens</i>,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5783678/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., \"Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?\" <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Jebel_Irhoud-1_NMNH.jpg","title":"Jebel Irhoud","desc":"Homo sapiens skull cast (Jebel Irhoud-1) on display at the Smithsonian's National Museum of Natural History. Jonathan Chen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jebel_Irhoud-1_NMNH.jpg","alt":"Jebel Irhoud-1 NMNH"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Jebel Irhoud","at":[-8.8725,31.855]},"why":[{"q":"Why was the first Jebel Irhoud skull misread for decades?","a":"Because it was taken for an African Neanderthal and thought to be only a few tens of thousands of years old. Measuring the light stored in burnt flint from the layer later put it at about 315,000 years."},{"q":"Why do the face and the braincase say different things?","a":"Because the faces are small and tucked in, with jaws and teeth that would not draw a second glance today, while the braincase behind them is long and low rather than the globe modern people carry."},{"q":"Why did the location surprise specialists?","a":"Because north-west Africa is about as far from the East African rift valleys as the continent allows, and the rift had long been treated as the cradle of our species."}]},
{"id":"wh-044","num":"44","category":"World · History","question":"Dug out of an Ethiopian riverbank in 1967, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were for four decades the oldest known members of our own species.","answer":"Omo remains","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 233,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967, by a team under Richard Leakey</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Omo remains</b> are two early human individuals, Omo I and Omo II, from the Kibish Formation on the lower Omo river in southwestern Ethiopia, and for forty years they were the oldest known members of our own species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A team from the Kenya National Museums under Richard Leakey found them in 1967, as part of a large international expedition working the valley. The two spots are named for the men who found them, Omo I coming from Kamoya's Hominid Site, after Kamoya Kimeu, the fossil hunter who would later find the Turkana Boy. Omo I is much of a skull vault with parts of the face and jaw and a good deal of the skeleton, and it is unmistakably modern, with a rounded braincase and a chin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Omo II is a nearly complete braincase, longer and lower and thicker in the wall, and it looks distinctly archaic, though it comes from beds of much the same age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That contrast is what makes the pair interesting, since two people from one formation refuse to look like a single tidy species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their age has moved three times: shells first suggested about 130,000 years, argon dating of crystals in volcanic ash gave 195,000 in 2005, and in 2022 the ash of a huge eruption lying above Omo I pushed it beyond 233,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each shift mattered because these bones were the yardstick against which the age of our species was measured. Jebel Irhoud in Morocco has since taken the record at about 315,000 years, but Omo I is still the earliest fossil that looks fully modern rather than partly so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nothing about the bones changed across those revisions; what changed was the reading of the rock around them, which is how a fossil can age by 100,000 years without anyone digging up anything new.","citation":"","answerText":"Omo remains","questions":["Made up of a modern-looking Omo I and an archaic Omo II from beds of one age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stubbornly refuse to look like a single tidy species.","Aged by shells, then by argon in 2005, then beyond 233,000 years by a 2022 eruption above them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew older each time the rock was reread."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","ethiopia"],"sources":["Ian McDougall, Francis H. Brown, and John G. Fleagle, \"Stratigraphic Placement and Age of Modern Humans from Kibish, Ethiopia,\" <i>Nature</i> 433, no. 7027 (2005): 733–36, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature03258. [Paywalled]","Céline M. Vidal et al., \"Age of the Oldest Known <i>Homo sapiens</i> from Eastern Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 601, no. 7894 (2022): 579–83, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8791829/. [Open access]","Simon Neubauer, Jean-Jacques Hublin, and Philipp Gunz, \"The Evolution of Modern Human Brain Shape,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 1 (2018): eaao5961, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5783678/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, \"Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., \"Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?\" <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Omo_Kibish_-_MCN_4152_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Omo_Kibish_-_MCN_4152_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Omo remains","desc":"Reproduction du crâne de l'Homme de Kibish. Musée des Civilisations Noires de Dakar (Sénégal). GuillaumeG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omo_Kibish_-_MCN_4152_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Omo Kibish - MCN 4152"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the pair of skulls more interesting than either alone?","a":"Because two people from one formation refuse to look like a single tidy species: Omo I is unmistakably modern, with a rounded braincase and a chin, while Omo II is longer, lower and thicker-walled and looks distinctly archaic."},{"q":"Why did the age of these bones matter so much each time it moved?","a":"Because they were the yardstick against which the age of our species was measured. Shells first suggested about 130,000 years, argon dating gave 195,000 in 2005, and in 2022 an ash layer above Omo I pushed it beyond 233,000."},{"q":"Why can a fossil age by 100,000 years without anything new being dug up?","a":"Because nothing about the bones changed across those revisions; what changed was the reading of the rock around them — the shells, the volcanic crystals and then the ash of a huge eruption lying above."}]},
{"id":"wh-045","num":"45","category":"World · History","question":"Not the first woman, not the only woman of her day, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name headline writers gave to the woman every living person descends from through an unbroken chain of mothers.","answer":"Mitochondrial Eve","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200,000 – 150,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1987, by Cann, Stoneking and Wilson</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mitochondrial Eve</b> is the name given to the woman from whom every person alive today descends through an unbroken chain of mothers, daughter after daughter, down to the present. The chain can be traced because mitochondria, the small structures that release energy in a cell, carry a scrap of DNA of their own, and a fertilised egg keeps only the mother's copy. That DNA is never shuffled with a father's either, so it passes down almost unchanged apart from the odd copying error, and those errors accumulate at a rough but measurable rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Comparing the mitochondrial DNA of living people therefore builds a single family tree of mothers, and any such tree, however many branches, must meet at one woman somewhere in the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She is that meeting point, and current estimates put her in Africa roughly 150,000 to 200,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Rebecca Cann, Mark Stoneking and Allan Wilson published the finding in 1987, after reading the mitochondrial DNA of 147 people and seeing that the deepest divisions in the tree all lay within Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their tree was built with a method they applied badly, as critics soon showed, but the two conclusions that mattered survived every better analysis since: the root is African, and the branches are short, which means recent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nickname was never in the paper, arriving instead with the commentary and the headlines, and a <i>Newsweek</i> cover the following January put a genetic Adam and Eve on newsstands worldwide. It has misled readers ever since, because she was not the first woman, not the only woman of her time, and not everyone's mother in any simple sense, since thousands of her contemporaries also have living descendants, just not through daughters all the way down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor is the title permanently hers, because every time the last woman in some maternal line dies childless the meeting point slides forward, which makes Mitochondrial Eve a moving position rather than a person history could ever name.","citation":"","answerText":"Mitochondrial Eve","questions":["Because a fertilised egg keeps only its mother's mitochondrial DNA, comparing that DNA across 147 people in 1987 let researchers trace <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Africa some 150,000 years ago.","Every time the last woman in some maternal line dies childless, the meeting point slides forward, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a moving position rather than any person history could name."],"tags":["concept","genetics","evolution","science"],"sources":["Rebecca L. Cann, Mark Stoneking, and Allan C. Wilson, \"Mitochondrial DNA and Human Evolution,\" <i>Nature</i> 325, no. 6099 (1987): 31–36, https://doi.org/10.1038/325031a0. [Paywalled]","Candela L. Hernández, \"Mitochondrial DNA in Human Diversity and Health: From the Golden Age to the Omics Era,\" <i>Genes</i> 14, no. 8 (2023): 1534, https://doi.org/10.3390/genes14081534. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu et al., \"A Revised Timescale for Human Evolution Based on Ancient Mitochondrial Genomes,\" <i>Current Biology</i> 23, no. 7 (2013): 553–59, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5036973/. [Open access]","Teresa Rito et al., \"The First Modern Human Dispersals across Africa,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 11 (2013): e80031, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0080031. [Open access]","Aaron P. Ragsdale et al., \"A Weakly Structured Stem for Human Origins in Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 617, no. 7962 (2023): 755–63, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06055-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/MacaulayRichardsmtDNAtree.jpg","title":"Mitochondrial Eve","desc":"A detailed phylogeny of the human mitochondrial DNA haplogroups. Vincent Macaulay and Martin Richards, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacaulayRichardsmtDNAtree.jpg","alt":"A phylogenetic tree of human mitochondrial DNA haplogroups"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why can a chain of mothers be traced at all?","a":"Because mitochondria carry a scrap of DNA of their own, a fertilised egg keeps only the mother's copy, and that DNA is never shuffled with a father's — so it passes down almost unchanged apart from copying errors that accumulate at a measurable rate."},{"q":"Why does such a woman have to exist?","a":"Because comparing the mitochondrial DNA of living people builds a single family tree of mothers, and any such tree, however many branches, must meet at one woman somewhere in the past. She is that meeting point."},{"q":"Why is the nickname misleading?","a":"Because she was not the first woman, not the only woman of her time, and not everyone's mother in any simple sense: thousands of her contemporaries also have living descendants, just not through daughters all the way down."}]},
{"id":"wh-046","num":"46","category":"World · History","question":"For roughly 99 percent of the human story, people lived in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the age of chipped-stone tools, hunting and gathering, fire and the first cave art.","answer":"Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 9700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Paleolithic</b>, or <b>Old Stone Age</b>, is the earliest and longest stretch of human prehistory, the first great division of the Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name comes from the Greek for 'old stone', and John Lubbock coined it in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The age is defined above all by tools of chipped stone, shaped by knapping — the controlled striking of one stone against another to detach sharp flakes — which first appear about 2.6 million years ago, or 3.3 million if disputed finds are counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ended with the last Ice Age, about 11,700 years ago — spanning, by common reckoning, some 99 percent of the human story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through all of it people lived by hunting wild game, fishing, and gathering plants, in small nomadic bands sheltering in caves and camps, without farms, herds, or settled villages. <br><br> The age was the work not of one people but of many, its toolmakers ranging from early Homo and the far-roaming Homo erectus to the Neanderthals and, from about 300,000 years ago, our own Homo sapiens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The stoneworking grew steadily finer across that span, from crude Oldowan choppers to the shapely Acheulean hand-axe, the flake tools of the Neanderthals, and at last the slender blades of the late Paleolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its makers mastered fire for warmth, cooking, and safety, and over successive ages spread out of Africa to people almost the whole earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In its later phase came a flowering of the mind — vivid cave paintings of animals, carved figurines, beads, bone flutes, and the deliberate burial of the dead — signs that point to art, belief, and language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> All of this is known to us only through archaeology and fossils, for the Paleolithic lies wholly before writing; it closed when, in several regions, people began to domesticate plants and animals, the turn to farming that opened the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Paleolithic","questions":["Coined in 1865 from the Greek for 'old stone', the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marks the earliest and longest division of the Stone Age, also called the Old Stone Age.","From early Homo and the far-roaming Homo erectus to Neanderthals and our own species, the toolmakers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mastered fire and spread out of Africa."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Li Li et al., “A Synthesis of the Dibble et al. Controlled Experiments into the Mechanics of Lithic Production,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 30, no. 4 (2023): 1284–1325, esp. 1290 and 1301–2, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-022-09586-2. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., “Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,” <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–23, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Jean-Jacques Hublin et al., “New Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the Pan-African Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 289–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22336. [Paywalled]","Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., “Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under “The Origins of Domestication,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/api/collection/v1/iiif/754048/1814306/main-image","title":"Nine Acheulean bifaces (700,000–200,000 BCE)","desc":"Suitable for a variety of tasks, including digging, chopping, and butchering, Paleolithic hand axes are the earliest and longest-used kind of tool made by diverse species of hominids, with documented usage over a period of 1.7 million years. These nine examples, which were all discovered within a small geographic area in Aisne, France, are remarkable for their variety and the high quality of their workmanship. They are a potent manifestation of an early and continuous human interest in perfection of form, and aesthetics in general, as demonstrated by their makers' efforts to endow them with symmetrical faces and edges, well beyond practical requirements. The largest bifaces in this group are so substantial that they would have been of little to no use as tools. Although their specific functions remain a matter of speculation, it is clear that they must have been prized for their appearance rather than utility.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/754048","alt":"Nine flaked stone hand axes of varying size arranged in rows against a plain ground"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why does the Paleolithic take up almost the whole human story?","a":"Because it runs from the first chipped stone tools about 2.6 million years ago to the end of the last Ice Age about 11,700 years ago — by common reckoning some 99 per cent of it."},{"q":"Why is the Paleolithic not the work of one people?","a":"Because its toolmakers range from early Homo and the far-roaming Homo erectus to the Neanderthals and, from about 300,000 years ago, our own Homo sapiens — several kinds of human across one technological age."},{"q":"Why is the whole of it known only through archaeology and fossils?","a":"Because the Paleolithic lies wholly before writing. What can be recovered is what people left behind — chipped stone, hearths, paintings, carved figurines, beads and burials — and the ground it was buried in."}]},
{"id":"wh-047","num":"47","category":"World · History","question":"The earliest and longest phase of the Old Stone Age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from the first chipped-stone tools to about 300,000 years ago, defined by the Oldowan chopper and the Acheulean hand-axe.","answer":"Lower Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.6 Mya – 300,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lower Paleolithic</b> is the earliest and by far the longest division of the Old Stone Age, the opening chapter in the human record of stone tools, and it alone accounts for nearly nine-tenths of that whole period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It begins with the first chipped-stone tools, conventionally dated to about 2.6 million years ago, and it closes roughly 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Disputed finds from Lomekwi in Kenya may push toolmaking back toward 3.3 million years ago, so the opening date is a convention rather than a fixed boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word Paleolithic comes from the Greek for 'old stone' and was coined by John Lubbock in 1865; prehistorians later divided its immense span into a Lower, a Middle and an Upper phase, this being the lowest and oldest of the three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Across the whole of it people lived as small, mobile bands of hunter-gatherers, without farms, villages, pottery, metal or writing of any kind. <br><br> The older of its two great toolkits is the Oldowan, named for Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania, where it was first recognised, and usually credited to Homo habilis, an early human who struck sharp flakes from simple pebble cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The later Acheulean, named in the same way for Saint-Acheul in France, produced the symmetrical teardrop hand-axe, one of the longest-lived tool designs ever made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Homo erectus above all carried that design out of Africa, and its fossils at Dmanisi in Georgia are the oldest yet found beyond the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether these early humans could control fire is still genuinely uncertain, and widely accepted traces of hearths appear only in the later part of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The age ends not with a sudden break but with a new skill: shaping a core in advance so that a flake of planned size and shape breaks away, the prepared-core method that opens the Middle Paleolithic and the world of the Neanderthals.","citation":"","answerText":"Lower Paleolithic","questions":["It was Homo erectus who carried the teardrop hand-axe of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of Africa, leaving at Dmanisi in Georgia the oldest human fossils yet found beyond the continent.","The older Oldowan toolkit, credited to Homo habilis, gave way within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Acheulean hand-axe, one of the longest-lived tool designs ever made."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["David R. Braun et al., “Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Sonia Harmand et al., “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools from Lomekwi 3, West Turkana, Kenya,” <i>Nature</i> 521, no. 7552 (2015): 310–15, https://hal.science/hal-04379924. [Open access]","Manuel Domínguez-Rodrigo and Luis Alcalá, “3.3-Million-Year-Old Stone Tools and Butchery Traces? More Evidence Needed,” <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2016 (2016): 46–53, esp. 49 and 51, https://paleoanthro.org/media/journal/content/PA20160046.pdf. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Ignacio de la Torre, “The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","David Lordkipanidze, Marcia S. Ponce de León, Ann Margvelashvili, et al., “A Complete Skull from Dmanisi, Georgia, and the Evolutionary Biology of Early <i>Homo</i>,” <i>Science</i> 342, no. 6156 (2013): 326–31, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1238484. [Paywalled]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Museum_of_Anatolian_Civilizations_Lower_Paleolithic_finds_in_2014_03.jpg/1920px-Museum_of_Anatolian_Civilizations_Lower_Paleolithic_finds_in_2014_03.jpg","title":"Lower Paleolithic","desc":"Tools in several varieties. From Lower Paleolithic, 1.000.000-200.000 BCE Excavated in Dursunlu (Konya province), which is the oldest dated archeological site in Turkey (up to approximately 900.000 BCE). Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_of_Anatolian_Civilizations_Lower_Paleolithic_finds_in_2014_03.jpg","alt":"Museum of Anatolian Civilizations Lower Paleolithic finds in 2014 03"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the opening date a convention rather than a fixed boundary?","a":"Because it is set at the first chipped-stone tools, conventionally about 2.6 million years ago, while disputed finds from Lomekwi in Kenya may push toolmaking back towards 3.3 million."},{"q":"Why does this one division account for nearly nine-tenths of the Old Stone Age?","a":"Because it runs from about 2.6 million years ago to roughly 300,000, while the Middle and Upper phases together fill only the last quarter-million years of that span."},{"q":"Why does the period end with a skill rather than an event?","a":"Because what closes it is shaping a core in advance so that a flake of planned size and shape breaks away — the prepared-core method that opens the Middle Paleolithic and the world of the Neanderthals."}]},
{"id":"wh-048","num":"48","category":"World · History","question":"Between the age of the hand-axe and the age of the cave painters, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the middle stretch of the Old Stone Age, the time of the Neanderthals.","answer":"Middle Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 50,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Middle Paleolithic</b> is the central of the three divisions of the Old Stone Age, the long chapter between the earlier hand-axe cultures and the later blade-and-art ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the usual convention it ran from about 300,000 to 50,000 years ago, and the Neanderthals were gone from Europe by about 40,000, though both edges are gradual and shift from region to region rather than marking any single moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its opening is defined by a technique rather than a date: the prepared-core or Levallois method, in which a knapper, a stoneworker striking flakes from stone, shapes a core in advance so that a flake of planned size and shape comes off in one blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> This lighter, standardised kit replaced the older Acheulean hand-axe, and its flakes could be fitted onto shafts to make composite tools such as stone-tipped spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Europe and western Asia this is above all the world of the Neanderthals, an early kind of human whose toolkit, rich in scrapers and points, is called Mousterian after the rock-shelter of Le Moustier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup> <br><br> Yet the Neanderthals were not alone: Denisovans ranged across Asia, and early members of our own species, Homo sapiens, met Neanderthals in the Near East at Skhul and Qafzeh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Because both used the same Levallois and Mousterian methods, the tools alone rarely reveal which kind of human made them, and Africa's parallel record, which belongs to Homo sapiens, carries its own name, the Middle Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> That African record holds the earliest strong hints of symbolic life, among them ochre engraved with patterns and pierced shell beads from Blombos Cave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Firm evidence shows these populations mastered fire and cooking, hunted large game, and glued stone points to shafts with tar, though deliberate burial, art and ritual remain debated and far less certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Upper Paleolithic follows from about 50,000 years ago, overlapping Europe's last Neanderthals by several thousand years; the famous figurative cave paintings belong to that later age, not to this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Middle Paleolithic","questions":["Opening with the Levallois method of shaping a core in advance, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from about 300,000 to 50,000 years ago and produced the Mousterian toolkit of scrapers and points.","In Africa the parallel record of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, known there as the Middle Stone Age, holds the earliest hints of symbolic life, such as the engraved ochre of Blombos Cave."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Christopher S. Henshilwood et al., “Emergence of Modern Human Behavior: Middle Stone Age Engravings from South Africa,” <i>Science</i> 295, no. 5558 (2002): 1278–80, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1067575. [Paywalled]","Lyn Wadley, Tamaryn Hodgskiss, and Michael Grant, “Implications for Complex Cognition from the Hafting of Tools with Compound Adhesives in the Middle Stone Age, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 24 (2009): 9590–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2700998/. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, “On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","Francesco d’Errico and Chris B. Stringer, “Evolution, Revolution or Saltation Scenario for the Emergence of Modern Cultures?,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 366, no. 1567 (2011): 1060–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3049097/. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren and Stephen J. Lycett, “Why Levallois? A Morphometric Comparison of Experimental ‘Preferential’ Levallois Flakes versus Debitage Flakes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 1 (2012): e29273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264556/. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Jean-Jacques Hublin et al., “New Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco and the Pan-African Origin of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 289–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22336. [Paywalled]","Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., “Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Xiujie Wu, “Making Sense of Eastern Asian Late Quaternary Hominin Variability,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9479, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11531466/. [Open access]","Patrick Schmidt, Radu Iovita, Armelle Charrié-Duhaut, Gunther Möller, Abay Namen, and Ewa Dutkiewicz, “Ochre-Based Compound Adhesives at the Mousterian Type-Site Document Complex Cognition and High Investment,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 8 (2024): eadl0822, https://hal.science/hal-04484835v1/document. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Stone_tools%2C_Neanderthal%2C_Bad_Urach%2C_Wittlingen%2C_c._50%2C000_to_70%2C000_years_old_-_Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_-_Stuttgart%2C_Germany_-_DSC02685.jpg/1920px-Stone_tools%2C_Neanderthal%2C_Bad_Urach%2C_Wittlingen%2C_c._50%2C000_to_70%2C000_years_old_-_Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_-_Stuttgart%2C_Germany_-_DSC02685.jpg","title":"Middle Paleolithic","desc":"Stone tools, Neanderthal, Bad Urach, Wittlingen, c. 50,000 to 70,000 years old - Landesmuseum Württemberg - Stuttgart, Germany. Daderot. 2015-12-06 05:46:54. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_tools,_Neanderthal,_Bad_Urach,_Wittlingen,_c._50,000_to_70,000_years_old_-_Landesmuseum_W%C3%BCrttemberg_-_Stuttgart,_Germany_-_DSC02685.jpg","alt":"Stone tools, Neanderthal, Bad Urach, Wittlingen, c. 50,000 to 70,000 years old - Landesmuseum Württemberg - Stuttgart, Germany"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is this division defined by a technique rather than a date?","a":"Because it opens with the prepared-core or Levallois method, in which a knapper shapes a core in advance so that a flake of planned size and shape comes off in one blow — a lighter, standardised kit replacing the Acheulean hand-axe."},{"q":"Why do the tools rarely reveal which kind of human made them?","a":"Because Neanderthals and early Homo sapiens both used the same Levallois and Mousterian methods, and they met in the Near East at Skhul and Qafzeh, where the assemblages cannot be told apart."},{"q":"Why does Africa's parallel record carry a different name?","a":"Because it belongs to Homo sapiens and is called the Middle Stone Age. It holds the earliest strong hints of symbolic life, among them ochre engraved with patterns and pierced shell beads from Blombos Cave."}]},
{"id":"wh-049","num":"49","category":"World · History","question":"The last of the three divisions of the Old Stone Age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the age when modern humans spread across Eurasia, struck slender blade tools and left a burst of animal art.","answer":"Upper Paleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50,000 – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Upper Paleolithic</b> is the third and final division of the Old Stone Age, following the Middle Paleolithic. By the usual convention it ran from about 50,000 to 11,700 years ago, ending as the last Ice Age did, though both edges are gradual and shift from region to region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is above all the age of modern humans, Homo sapiens, who spread across Eurasia and on into Australia and, later, the Americas; the Neanderthals, an older kind of human with whom they overlapped and interbred, faded from the record around 40,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its technological signature is the blade, a long, straight-edged flake struck in series from a carefully prepared core, which yielded far more cutting edge from each lump of stone than earlier methods. Alongside such blades came a rich craft in bone, antler, and ivory — eyed needles for sewn, fitted clothing, barbed harpoons, and the spear-thrower that launched darts with new force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The period is famous for its wealth of art, ornament, music, and elaborate burial, a flowering once cast as a sudden \"creative explosion\" unique to Europe. Most researchers now doubt that reading, arguing that such art and symbols built up slowly, and far earlier, in Africa. The oldest known cave paintings of real animals come not from Europe but from the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, at least 45,500 years old, with a narrative scene nearby dated to 51,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Europe's own record is nonetheless spectacular, from the ivory figurines and bone flutes of the Swabian caves to the painted animals of Chauvet, Lascaux, and Altamira and the ivory-bead burials of Sungir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The cultures used to order it — Aurignacian, Gravettian, Solutrean, Magdalenian — form a Western European scheme rather than a worldwide one; when the glaciers at last drew back, the Upper Paleolithic gave way to the Mesolithic and, in time, to farming.","citation":"","answerText":"Upper Paleolithic","questions":["The eyed needle, the barbed harpoon and the spear-thrower all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Homo sapiens worked bone, antler and ivory alongside slender stone blades.","The painted caves of Chauvet, Lascaux and Altamira date from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the oldest known animal paintings come earlier still, from the Indonesian island of Sulawesi."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Mike Walker et al., “Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,” <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–23, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Ian Gilligan, Francesco d’Errico, Luc Doyon, Wei Wang, and Yaroslav V. Kuzmin, “Paleolithic Eyed Needles and the Evolution of Dress,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 26 (2024): eadp2887, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11212769/. [Open access]","Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., “Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim et al., “Narrative Cave Art in Indonesia by 51,200 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 631 (2024): 814–18, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07541-7. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), paras. 7 and 13, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Anita Quiles, Hélène Valladas, Hervé Bocherens et al., “A High-Precision Chronological Model for the Decorated Upper Paleolithic Cave of Chauvet-Pont d’Arc, Ardèche, France,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–75, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Erik Trinkaus and Alexandra P. Buzhilova, “Diversity and Differential Disposal of the Dead at Sunghir,” <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 361 (2018): 7–21, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.223. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/Venus_de_Brassempouy.jpg","title":"Upper Paleolithic","desc":"Venus of Brassempouy, photographed at the Musée d'Archéologie Nationale. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_de_Brassempouy.jpg","alt":"Venus de Brassempouy"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did the blade yield more from each lump of stone?","a":"Because a blade is a long, straight-edged flake struck in series from a carefully prepared core, so the same block gives far more cutting edge than the flake and core-tool methods before it."},{"q":"Why do most researchers now doubt the creative-explosion reading?","a":"Because they argue such art and symbols built up slowly, and far earlier, in Africa. The oldest known cave paintings of real animals come from Sulawesi in Indonesia, at least 45,500 years old, with a narrative scene nearby dated to 51,200."},{"q":"Why are the Aurignacian and Magdalenian not worldwide labels?","a":"Because the cultures used to order this period — Aurignacian, Gravettian, Solutrean, Magdalenian — form a Western European scheme, built from the sequence found there rather than from the record of the whole world."}]},
{"id":"wh-050","num":"50","category":"World · History","question":"The geological epoch of the great ice ages, running from about 2.58 million to 11,700 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saw ice sheets advance and retreat dozens of times.","answer":"Pleistocene","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epoch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2.58 Mya – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Pleistocene</b> is a geological epoch, a formally defined slice of deep time within the Quaternary Period of the Cenozoic Era. It ran from roughly 2.58 million years ago to about 11,700 years ago, when the warmer Holocene, our current epoch, began. Its start was pushed back from about 1.8 million years ago to 2.58 million only in 2009, so that the epoch now opens near the onset of major Northern Hemisphere glaciation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crucially, it is defined by the planet's climate and rock record rather than by anything people did, and its boundaries are fixed by 'golden spikes' in Sicilian strata and a Greenland ice core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This makes it a different kind of period from the archaeological Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, which is defined by human stone tools; though the two overlap across almost the same vast span of time, their boundaries do not line up. <br><br> It is popularly called the Ice Age, but that name flattens the picture: the epoch held dozens of swings between cold glacial phases, when vast ice sheets spread, and warm interglacials like today. These swings were paced by the Milankovitch cycles, slow and regular changes in Earth's orbit and the tilt of its axis that alter how sunlight falls across the globe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the ice was at its greatest, so much water was locked up that the sea fell by as much as 130 metres (430 feet), opening land bridges such as Beringia between Siberia and Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of human evolution unfolded within it, for Homo erectus, the Neanderthals, the Denisovans and our own species all lived through the epoch, and humans first crossed such bridges into Australia and the Americas, though the exact dates are debated. It was also the age of the megafauna, from woolly mammoths to sabre-toothed cats, most of which died out near its close for reasons — climate, human hunting, or both — that scholars still dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pleistocene","questions":["Defined by the rock and climate record rather than by human tools, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is fixed by golden spikes in Sicilian strata and a Greenland ice core.","When ice locked up the seas during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the water fell as much as 120 metres (390 feet) and opened land bridges such as Beringia between Siberia and Alaska."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature"],"sources":["Philip L. Gibbard and Martin J. Head, \"The Newly-Ratified Definition of the Quaternary System/Period and Redefinition of the Pleistocene Series/Epoch, and Comparison of Proposals Advanced Prior to Formal Ratification,\" <i>Episodes</i> 33, no. 3 (2010): 152–158, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2010/v33i3/002. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","J. D. Hays, John Imbrie, and N. J. Shackleton, \"Variations in the Earth’s Orbit: Pacemaker of the Ice Ages,\" <i>Science</i> 194, no. 4270 (1976): 1121–32, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.194.4270.1121. [Paywalled]","Rachel M. Spratt and Lorraine E. Lisiecki, \"A Late Pleistocene Sea Level Stack,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 12, no. 4 (2016): 1079–92, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-12-1079-2016. [Open access]","Martin Jakobsson et al., \"Post-Glacial Flooding of the Bering Land Bridge Dated to 11 cal ka BP Based on New Geophysical and Sediment Records,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 13, no. 8 (2017): 991–1005, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-13-991-2017. [Open access]","Jens-Christian Svenning et al., \"The Late-Quaternary Megafauna Extinctions: Patterns, Causes, Ecological Consequences and Implications for Ecosystem Management in the Anthropocene,\" <i>Cambridge Prisms: Extinction</i> 2 (2024): e5, https://doi.org/10.1017/ext.2024.4. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/EarlyPleistoceneAnimals.png","title":"Pleistocene","desc":"Animals of the early Pleistocene, from an illustration of 1921. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EarlyPleistoceneAnimals.png","alt":"EarlyPleistoceneAnimals"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Pleistocene a different kind of period from the Paleolithic?","a":"Because it is defined by the planet's climate and rock record rather than by anything people did, with its boundaries fixed by golden spikes in Sicilian strata and a Greenland ice core. The two overlap across almost the same span, but their boundaries do not line up."},{"q":"Why does calling it the Ice Age flatten the picture?","a":"Because the epoch held dozens of swings between cold glacial phases, when vast ice sheets spread, and warm interglacials like today — not one long cold spell."},{"q":"Why did land bridges open when the ice was at its greatest?","a":"Because so much water was locked up in ice that the sea fell by as much as 130 metres, laying bare crossings such as Beringia between Siberia and Alaska."}]},
{"id":"wh-051","num":"51","category":"World · History","question":"Rather than a slice of time, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a condition in which Earth keeps permanent ice sheets on land — and we are in one right now.","answer":"Ice age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Quaternary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2.6 Mya</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>ice age</b> is not a slice of time but a climate condition — a long interval, usually lasting millions of years, during which Earth keeps permanent ice sheets on its land. The term carries two meanings that are easily muddled: to scientists it is that whole ice-sheet interval, while in everyday speech it means a single cold spell, the mammoth world of the most recent glaciation. Within one, the climate swings between colder glacial phases, when the ice advances, and warmer interglacial ones, when it retreats, as in our own time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The counterintuitive result is that we are still in an ice age today, the Quaternary, because permanent ice still caps Antarctica and Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Our comfortable Holocene is simply its current interglacial, a warm pause rather than the finish line, which is what sets an ice age apart from a named geological epoch like the Pleistocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Earth has passed through several such intervals since at least 2.4 billion years ago, the most dramatic being the Cryogenian, roughly 720 to 635 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During it the ice may have reached almost to the equator, an idea known as 'Snowball Earth', though how completely the planet froze is still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What switches an ice age on works over millions of years: the drifting of continents into polar positions, shifting ocean currents, and a fall in the carbon dioxide that traps the Sun's heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Milankovitch cycles, slow changes in Earth's orbit and tilt, only set the beat of the glacials within an ice age, being far too weak to start one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The famous Ice Age of popular imagination was merely the latest glacial, peaking about 20,000 years ago, when great ice sheets buried the northern lands and the sea fell by roughly 130 metres (430 feet), opening land bridges such as Beringia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ice age","questions":["Earth has passed through at least five, the fiercest being the Cryogenian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 720 to 635 million years ago, when ice may have reached almost to the equator.","Drifting continents, shifting currents and falling carbon dioxide switch an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on over millions of years, while the Milankovitch cycles only pace the cold spells within it."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature","climate"],"sources":["Past Interglacials Working Group of PAGES, \"Interglacials of the Last 800,000 Years,\" <i>Reviews of Geophysics</i> 54, no. 1 (2016): 162–219, under \"Defining Interglacials,\" https://hal.science/hal-03245836. [Open access]","Paul F. Hoffman et al., \"Snowball Earth Climate Dynamics and Cryogenian Geology-Geobiology,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 11 (2017): e1600983, abstract and table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5677351/. [Open access]","Rachel M. Spratt and Lorraine E. Lisiecki, \"A Late Pleistocene Sea Level Stack,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 12, no. 4 (2016): 1079–92, esp. 1080, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-12-1079-2016. [Open access]","James Hansen et al., \"Climate Sensitivity, Sea Level and Atmospheric Carbon Dioxide,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 371, no. 2001 (2013): 20120294, under \"Cenozoic Climate Change,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3785813/. [Open access]","Philip L. Gibbard and Martin J. Head, \"The Newly-Ratified Definition of the Quaternary System/Period and Redefinition of the Pleistocene Series/Epoch, and Comparison of Proposals Advanced Prior to Formal Ratification,\" <i>Episodes</i> 33, no. 3 (2010): 152–58, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2010/v33i3/002. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Ice_Age_Human_Footprints_%28a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff%29.JPG/1920px-Ice_Age_Human_Footprints_%28a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff%29.JPG","title":"Ice age","desc":"Seven human footprints preserved in Ice Age gypsum cement. Ice Age footprints from adolescemt female estimated to 23,000-21,000 years ago. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ice_Age_Human_Footprints_(a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff).JPG","alt":"Ice Age Human Footprints (a45c5031-d519-43f0-8d64-7b81d653c3ff)"},"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why are we still in an ice age today?","a":"Because an ice age is a climate condition rather than a slice of time: it lasts as long as Earth keeps permanent ice sheets on its land, and ice still caps Antarctica and Greenland. The Holocene is simply the current interglacial."},{"q":"Why can the Milankovitch cycles not start an ice age?","a":"Because they are far too weak. They only set the beat of the glacials within one; what switches an ice age on works over millions of years — continents drifting into polar positions, shifting ocean currents, and falling carbon dioxide."},{"q":"Why is the term easily muddled?","a":"Because it carries two meanings: to scientists it is the whole ice-sheet interval, usually lasting millions of years, while in everyday speech it means a single cold spell — the mammoth world of the most recent glaciation."}]},
{"id":"wh-052","num":"52","category":"World · History","question":"The most recent great cold of the current ice age and the world of mammoths, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what popular culture simply calls 'the Ice Age'.","answer":"Last Glacial Period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">115,000 – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Last Glacial Period</b> was the most recent cold stage of the current ice age — the specific span, roughly 115,000 to 11,700 years ago, that popular culture loosely calls 'the Ice Age'. Sitting within the Late Pleistocene, it followed the last warm interglacial and reached its frozen peak, the Last Glacial Maximum, around 20,000 years ago. Geologists give this one period a different name in each region — the Würm in the Alps, the Weichselian in northern Europe, the Wisconsin in North America, the Devensian in Britain — yet all describe the same glacial. At its coldest, great ice sheets buried roughly a third of the land: the vast Laurentide over Canada, the Fennoscandian over northern Europe, and smaller ones over the mountains and islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So much water was locked into ice that the sea fell by some 130 metres (430 feet), laying bare land bridges such as Beringia between Siberia and Alaska, and Doggerland between Britain and mainland Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Across the cold, dry, dusty north spread the grassy 'mammoth steppe', grazed by woolly mammoths, woolly rhinoceroses, cave bears and giant deer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For people this was the Upper Palaeolithic: modern humans spread across the planet, and it was in these millennia that they painted the caves at Chauvet, some 36,000 years ago, and Lascaux, about 17,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Crossing the newly exposed land, they reached Australia and, later, the Americas, though exactly when they first arrived in each is still debated. From about 20,000 years ago the ice began to melt and the seas to rise, but the thaw was jolted by the Younger Dryas, a sharp return to near-glacial cold roughly 12,900 to 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The period finally closed about 11,700 years ago as the world warmed into our own Holocene, a transition that also saw many of the great cold-loving animals die out, for reasons scholars still dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Last Glacial Period","questions":["Known as the Würm in the Alps, the Weichselian in northern Europe and the Wisconsin in North America, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached its frozen peak about 20,000 years ago.","As modern humans painted Chauvet and Lascaux and crossed into Australia and the Americas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laid bare land bridges like Beringia and Doggerland."],"tags":["era","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Christine L. Batchelor et al., \"The Configuration of Northern Hemisphere Ice Sheets through the Quaternary,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 3713, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-019-11601-2. [Open access]","Rachel M. Spratt and Lorraine E. Lisiecki, \"A Late Pleistocene Sea Level Stack,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 12, no. 4 (2016): 1079–92, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-12-1079-2016. [Open access]","Anita Quiles et al., \"A High-Precision Chronological Model for the Decorated Upper Paleolithic Cave of Chauvet-Pont d’Arc, Ardèche, France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–75, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","Yucheng Wang et al., \"Late Quaternary Dynamics of Arctic Biota from Ancient Environmental Genomics,\" <i>Nature</i> 600, no. 7887 (2021): 86–92, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04016-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Global_sea_levels_and_vegetation_during_the_last_Ice_Age_%28South_Asia%29.jpg","title":"Last Glacial Period","desc":"Global sea levels and vegetation during the last Ice Age (South Asia). Sea levels: National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration Vegetation: Locoluis. 2020-03-22. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Global_sea_levels_and_vegetation_during_the_last_Ice_Age_(South_Asia).jpg","alt":"Global sea levels and vegetation during the last Ice Age (South Asia)"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does one glacial period carry four different names?","a":"Because geologists name it regionally: the Würm in the Alps, the Weichselian in northern Europe, the Wisconsin in North America and the Devensian in Britain — all describing the same glacial."},{"q":"Why did Doggerland and Beringia exist at all?","a":"Because so much water was locked into ice that the sea fell by some 130 metres, laying bare land between Britain and mainland Europe and between Siberia and Alaska."},{"q":"Why was the thaw not a steady warming?","a":"Because it was jolted by the Younger Dryas, a sharp return to near-glacial cold roughly 12,900 to 11,700 years ago, before the world warmed into our own Holocene."}]},
{"id":"wh-053","num":"53","category":"World · History","question":"With ice nearly four kilometres (2.5 miles) thick over Canada and the sea some 125 metres (410 feet) lower, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of roughly 21,000 years ago left the planet colder, drier and dustier than now.","answer":"Last Glacial Maximum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 26,000 – 19,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ice greatest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 21,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Last Glacial Maximum</b> is the coldest phase of the last Ice Age, running from roughly 26,000 to 19,000 years ago, with the world's ice at its greatest volume about 21,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ice sheets buried northern North America, Scandinavia and the northern half of Britain, and the Laurentide sheet over what is now Canada stood almost 4 kilometres (2.5 miles) thick at its dome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Locking that much water into ice pulled the sea about 125 metres (410 feet) below its present level, so the continental shelves emerged as dry land and Beringia joined Siberia to Alaska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Global temperatures averaged about 6 degrees Celsius colder than today, and atmospheric carbon dioxide fell to roughly 190 parts per million, the lowest of the entire glacial cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A cold world is also a dry one, since colder air holds less moisture, so deserts spread, forests shrank into pockets, and the dust off the bare ground still shows in ice cores as a dirty band. <br><br> For people the effect was a squeeze, and northern Europe emptied as its inhabitants contracted into southern refugia in Iberia, Italy and the Balkans. The Cantabrian coast of northern Spain was one of the richest of these, and the Solutrean toolmakers belong to exactly this window, which is why fitted clothing and the eyed needle matter as much as their stone points. Ancient DNA from a man buried in southern Iberia about 23,000 years ago links the people of western Europe before the cold peak to those who came after it, which is the sort of evidence a refugium predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The retreat began around 19,000 years ago as the Earth's orbit turned the sunlight back up, and meltwater raised the sea over the following ten thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Doggerland was drowned by about 8,000 years ago, and the coastlines we take for granted are the recent product of that thaw rather than anything permanent.","citation":"","answerText":"Last Glacial Maximum","questions":["Northern Europe emptied of people during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose survivors crowded south into refuges in Iberia, Italy and the Balkans until the ice began pulling back.","By dropping atmospheric carbon dioxide to about 180 parts per million and baring the continental shelves, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned Beringia into dry land linking Siberia to Alaska."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature","climate"],"sources":["Peter U. Clark et al., \"The Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Science</i> 325, no. 5941 (2009): 710–14, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1172873. [Paywalled]","Daniel Moreno-Parada et al., \"Simulating the Laurentide Ice Sheet of the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>The Cryosphere</i> 17, no. 6 (2023): 2139–56, https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-17-2139-2023. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney et al., \"Glacial Cooling and Climate Sensitivity Revisited\" (preprint), <i>EarthArXiv</i> (2019), https://doi.org/10.31223/osf.io/me5uj. [Open access]","Bernhard Bereiter et al., \"Revision of the EPICA Dome C CO2 Record from 800 to 600 kyr before Present,\" <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 42, no. 2 (2015): 542–49, Antarctic composite CO2 data at https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/paleo/icecore/antarctica/antarctica2015co2composite.txt. [Open access]","Vanessa Villalba-Mouco et al., \"A 23,000-Year-Old Southern Iberian Individual Links Human Groups That Lived in Western Europe before and after the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 7 (2023): 597–609, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10089921/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Aridity_ice_age_vs_early_holocene_vs_modern.jpg","title":"Last Glacial Maximum","desc":"During the Last Glacial Maximum 14000 years ago, thermal-driven evaporation from the oceans onto continental landmasses was low, causing large areas of extreme desert, including polar deserts (cold but with low rates of precipitation). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aridity_ice_age_vs_early_holocene_vs_modern.jpg","alt":"Aridity ice age vs early holocene vs modern"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is a cold world also a dry one?","a":"Because colder air holds less moisture, so deserts spread, forests shrank into pockets, and the dust off the bare ground still shows in ice cores as a dirty band."},{"q":"Why did northern Europe empty during the cold peak?","a":"Because its inhabitants contracted into southern refugia in Iberia, Italy and the Balkans. The Cantabrian coast of northern Spain was one of the richest, and the Solutrean toolmakers belong to exactly this window."},{"q":"Why does ancient DNA support the refugium picture?","a":"Because a man buried in southern Iberia about 23,000 years ago links the people of western Europe before the cold peak to those who came after it — which is the continuity a refugium predicts."}]},
{"id":"wh-054","num":"54","category":"World · History","question":"Living on wild food rather than crops or herds, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> way of life was the only one humans knew until farming began about 12,000 years ago.","answer":"Hunter-gatherer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Only way of life</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until c. 12,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hunter-gatherers</b>, also called foragers, live on wild food — hunting, fishing and gathering plants — rather than growing crops or keeping herds. The term names a subsistence strategy rather than a period of the past: some peoples were still living this way in the 20th century, and a few do today. Until farming began about 12,000 years ago, every human on earth ate this way, which covers almost the whole of the human story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was never a single forager diet, since what a group ate followed what its own landscape offered: Arctic peoples lived almost entirely on animals, while in warm places gathered plants supplied most of the food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The familiar picture is a small nomadic band of a few dozen people, moving camp with the seasons, sharing what they bring in rather than storing it up, and owning little beyond what can be carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That is only one form the life took: on the Pacific Northwest Coast, the Tlingit and Haida built large permanent villages of cedar plank houses. They grew no crops, living instead on salmon they caught, dried and stored in bulk, and their societies were steeply ranked, with noble titles inherited within families and slaves held as property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So permanent villages, stored food and sharp differences of rank did not need farming to arise; on several continents they grew up among peoples who lived entirely on wild food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Peoples who still forage today, such as the Hadza and the San, are modern people living modern lives, and they are not a window into the deep past. Most live on land nobody else wanted, and they trade, work for wages and go to school like anyone else.","citation":"","answerText":"Hunter-gatherer","questions":["On the Pacific Northwest Coast the Tlingit and Haida built permanent plank-house villages on stored salmon, showing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> life could raise ranked societies without any farming.","Peoples who still forage today, such as the Hadza and San, follow a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> economy yet are modern people, not a living window into the deep past."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Greger Larson et al., \"Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under \"The Origins of Domestication,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Dan Zhu, Eric D. Galbraith, Victoria Reyes-García, and Philippe Ciais, \"Global Hunter-Gatherer Population Densities Constrained by Influence of Seasonality on Diet Composition,\" <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 5, no. 11 (2021): 1536–45, under \"Seasonality Constrains Diet Composition,\" https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC7611941. [Open access]","Eric Alden Smith and Brian F. Codding, \"Ecological Variation and Institutionalized Inequality in Hunter-Gatherer Societies,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 13 (2021): e2016134118, under \"Introduction\" and \"Discussion,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8020663/. [Open access]","Hannah M. Lewis, Lucio Vinicius, Janis Strods, Ruth Mace, and Andrea Bamberg Migliano, \"High Mobility Explains Demand Sharing and Enforced Cooperation in Egalitarian Hunter-Gatherers,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 5 (2014): 5789, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms6789. [Open access]","Andrea B. Migliano et al., \"Hunter-Gatherer Multilevel Sociality Accelerates Cumulative Cultural Evolution,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 9 (2020): eaax5913, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax5913. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Kalina_hunter_gatherer.jpg","title":"Hunter-gatherer","desc":"A Kali'na hunter with a woman gatherer. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalina_hunter_gatherer.jpg","alt":"Kalina hunter gatherer"},"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does hunter-gatherer name a strategy rather than a period?","a":"Because it describes living on wild food rather than growing crops or keeping herds, and some peoples were still living this way in the 20th century while a few do today — even though every human ate this way until farming began."},{"q":"Why does the Pacific Northwest Coast upset the familiar picture?","a":"Because the Tlingit and Haida built large permanent villages of cedar plank houses, grew no crops, lived on salmon they dried and stored in bulk, and were steeply ranked, with inherited noble titles and slaves held as property."},{"q":"Why are peoples who forage today not a window into the deep past?","a":"Because they are modern people living modern lives: most live on land nobody else wanted, and they trade, work for wages and go to school like anyone else."}]},
{"id":"wh-055","num":"55","category":"World · History","question":"Named in the 1920s because Europe's labels fitted the African record badly, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the long middle chapter of the African Stone Age, belonging to our own species.","answer":"Middle Stone Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1928, by John Goodwin</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Middle Stone Age</b> is the middle division of the African Stone Age, running from roughly 300,000 until about 40,000 years ago, though both edges are gradual and shift from region to region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name goes back to a 1928 paper by the archaeologist John Goodwin, elaborated the next year in <i>The Stone Age Cultures of South Africa</i>, his joint work with the engineer Clarence van Riet Lowe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That book laid out an Earlier, a Middle and a Late Stone Age for the continent, in place of early schemes that leaned on European frameworks and imported French terminology wholesale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meeting at Livingstone in 1955, the Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory spent over three hours framing a resolution that pressed the scheme on the whole continent, and carried it against two votes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Despite the name it is not the Mesolithic, a word Goodwin's term was chosen to stand apart from; in time it corresponds roughly to the Middle Paleolithic of Europe and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The record is ours: the oldest known fossils of Homo sapiens, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, lay among one of the earliest directly dated Middle Stone Age toolkits, about 315,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its toolmakers worked by the prepared-core method, shaping a block so that a flake of planned size and edge came away in one blow, then trimming those flakes into points and scrapers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two southern African phases show how quickly things could move: the Still Bay, dated to a window between about 71,900 and 71,000 years ago, and the Howiesons Poort that followed between 64,800 and 59,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Still Bay layers at Blombos Cave hold the earliest accepted signs of symbolic life, among them pierced shell beads and two pieces of ochre engraved with crosshatched patterns, dated to about 75,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From about 40,000 years ago the Late Stone Age takes over, its earliest phase still mixing prepared cores with newer bipolar and microlithic work, the fully microlithic industries arriving thousands of years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Middle Stone Age","questions":["Set out in 1929 by John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded the oldest known Homo sapiens fossils, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, about 315,000 years old.","The Blombos layers, roughly 75,000 years old, hold the earliest widely accepted signs of symbolic life, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries pierced shell beads and crosshatched ochre."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","africa"],"sources":["Emily Hallinan, “The Southern African Stone Age Site Index (SASSI): A Spatial, Chronological and Contextual Resource for Archaeological Research,” <i>Journal of Open Archaeology Data</i> 14 (2026): 2, under “Overview” and table 1, https://doi.org/10.5334/joad.186. [Open access]","B. D. Malan, “The Term ‘Middle Stone Age’,” in <i>Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory, Livingstone 1955</i>, ed. J. Desmond Clark and Sonia Cole (London: Chatto and Windus, 1957), 223–27, https://archive.org/details/in.gov.ignca.6340. [Open access]","“Resolutions of the Congress,” in <i>Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory, Livingstone 1955</i>, ed. J. Desmond Clark and Sonia Cole (London: Chatto and Windus, 1957), resolution 6 and the closing session, https://archive.org/details/in.gov.ignca.6340. [Open access]","Daniel Richter et al., “The Age of the Hominin Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, Morocco, and the Origins of the Middle Stone Age,” <i>Nature</i> 546, no. 7657 (2017): 293–96, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature22335. [Paywalled]","Zenobia Jacobs et al., “Ages for the Middle Stone Age of Southern Africa: Implications for Human Behavior and Dispersal,” <i>Science</i> 322, no. 5902 (2008): 733–35, esp. 733, https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstreams/3e6b212a-1edc-45c8-a0b6-067293fb544c/download. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, esp. 9964, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren and Stephen J. Lycett, “Why Levallois? A Morphometric Comparison of Experimental ‘Preferential’ Levallois Flakes versus Debitage Flakes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 1 (2012): e29273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3264556/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Blombos_point.JPG","title":"Middle Stone Age","desc":"Bifacial silcrete point from M1 phase (71,000 BCE) layer of Blombos Cave, South Africa; scale bar = 5 cm. Vincent Mourre / Inrap, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blombos_point.JPG","alt":"Blombos point"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why was a separate African scheme thought necessary?","a":"Because the early schemes leaned on European frameworks and imported French terminology wholesale. Goodwin and van Riet Lowe laid out an Earlier, a Middle and a Late Stone Age for the continent instead."},{"q":"Why must the Middle Stone Age not be read as the Mesolithic?","a":"Because the name was chosen precisely to stand apart from it. In time it corresponds roughly to the Middle Paleolithic of Europe and Asia, not to the post-glacial Mesolithic."},{"q":"Why is this record described as ours?","a":"Because the oldest known fossils of Homo sapiens, from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, lay among one of the earliest directly dated Middle Stone Age toolkits, about 315,000 years old."}]},
{"id":"wh-057","num":"57","category":"World · History","question":"Scraped from iron-rich earth and in use from at least 300,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the red and yellow pigment that colours painted cave walls and Ice Age burials.","answer":"Ochre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from at least c. 300,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ochre</b> is a natural earth coloured by iron oxide, running from pale yellow through orange to deep blood red, and the oldest pigment people are known to have used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its red comes from the mineral hematite and its yellow from goethite, mixed in the ground with clay and sand, so a piece of ochre is a rock rather than a made paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It forms wherever iron-rich rock weathers, so it lies almost everywhere, and it can be rubbed on like a crayon or crushed to powder on a grindstone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heating yellow ochre drives the water out of the goethite and turns it red, and burnt crumbs at some Stone Age sites are read as people doing that on purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ground fine and worked into fat, marrow, plant gum or water, it yields a paint that grips skin, hide, bone and stone and holds its colour for tens of thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Lumps carried into caves and scraped for powder appear by about 300,000 years ago at Twin Rivers in Zambia and in the Kapthurin beds of Kenya, and claims for older use are argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Blombos Cave in South Africa a paste of ochre, bone, charcoal and fat was ground and stored in two abalone shells about 100,000 years ago, and lumps engraved with cross-hatched patterns lie in its 77,000-year-old layers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the ochre in a Stone Age cave, though, was probably working material: powdered into resin it makes a stronger glue for setting a stone point into a shaft, it helps in tanning hides, and it may have guarded skin against sun and insects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Archaeologists therefore disagree about how much of it stands for art or identity and how much is workshop debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What is harder to explain away is the red powder heaped over the dead, in Ice Age Europe and in many later societies, and the fact that people carried good ochre tens of kilometres when duller earth lay closer to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ochre","questions":["Ground with bone, charcoal and fat, a paste of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was mixed and stored in two abalone shells at Blombos Cave in South Africa about 100,000 years ago.","Powdered into tree resin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made a stronger glue for setting a stone point into its shaft, so much of it in a cave was workshop debris rather than art."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","geology"],"sources":["Christopher S. Henshilwood et al., “Emergence of Modern Human Behavior: Middle Stone Age Engravings from South Africa,” <i>Science</i> 295, no. 5558 (2002): 1278–80, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1067575. [Paywalled]","Lyn Wadley, Tamaryn Hodgskiss, and Michael Grant, “Implications for Complex Cognition from the Hafting of Tools with Compound Adhesives in the Middle Stone Age, South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 24 (2009): 9590–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2700998/. [Open access]","Christopher S. Henshilwood et al., “A 100,000-Year-Old Ochre-Processing Workshop at Blombos Cave, South Africa,” <i>Science</i> 334, no. 6053 (2011): 219–22, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1211535. [Paywalled]","Paola Villa et al., “A Milk and Ochre Paint Mixture Used 49,000 Years Ago at Sibudu, South Africa,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 6 (2015): e0131273, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4488428/. [Open access]","Daniela Eugenia Rosso, Francesco d’Errico, and Alain Queffelec, “Patterns of Change and Continuity in Ochre Use during the Late Middle Stone Age of the Horn of Africa: The Porc-Epic Cave Record,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0177298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5443497/. [Open access]","Rimtautas Dapschauskas, Matthias B. Göden, Christian Sommer, and Andrew W. Kandel, “The Emergence of Habitual Ochre Use in Africa and Its Significance for the Development of Ritual Behavior during the Middle Stone Age,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 35, nos. 3–4 (2022): 233–319, https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10963-022-09170-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Links_gebrannte_Siena%2C_rechts_nat%C3%BCrliche.JPG/1920px-Links_gebrannte_Siena%2C_rechts_nat%C3%BCrliche.JPG","title":"Ochre","desc":"Burnt sienna (darker) and raw sienna (paler). Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Links_gebrannte_Siena,_rechts_nat%C3%BCrliche.JPG","alt":"Links gebrannte Siena, rechts natürliche"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is a piece of ochre a rock rather than a paint?","a":"Because it is a natural earth coloured by iron oxide — hematite for red, goethite for yellow — mixed in the ground with clay and sand. It becomes paint only when ground fine and worked into fat, marrow, plant gum or water."},{"q":"Why do archaeologists disagree about what ochre in a cave means?","a":"Because much of it was probably working material: powdered into resin it makes a stronger glue for hafting, it helps in tanning hides, and it may have guarded skin against sun and insects. So art and workshop debris look much alike."},{"q":"Why are the burials and the carrying distances harder to explain away?","a":"Because red powder was heaped over the dead in Ice Age Europe and many later societies, and people carried good ochre tens of kilometres when duller earth lay closer to hand — neither of which a working material alone accounts for."}]},
{"id":"wh-058","num":"58","category":"World · History","question":"Dug into an old sea cliff above the Indian Ocean, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has given up engraved ochre and shell beads, pushing symbolic behaviour back beyond 70,000 years ago.","answer":"Blombos Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 101,000 – 71,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1991, by Christopher Henshilwood</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Blombos Cave</b> is a small cave cut into an ancient wave-cut cliff on the southern Cape coast of South Africa, standing 34.5 metres (113 feet) above the sea and barely 100 metres (330 feet) from the Indian Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reaches only six or seven metres (20 to 23 feet) from the drip line to the back wall, yet the excavated Middle Stone Age sequence beneath its floor runs more than four metres (13 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its deepest phase dates by luminescence and uranium series to between about 101,000 and 94,000 years ago, and a sterile band of sand seals it off from the Late Stone Age layers above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Christopher Henshilwood began excavating in 1991 and has directed the work with Karen van Niekerk ever since, recovering among much else nine human teeth and dental fragments from at least five people, perhaps seven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The upper levels belong to the Still Bay, a short-lived southern African tradition of slender leaf-shaped points flaked on both faces, dated elsewhere to between about 71,900 and 71,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> What made the cave famous is what those upper layers held, finds that carried the origins of symbolic behaviour deep into the African Middle Stone Age. In 2002 Henshilwood's team published two pieces of red ochre engraved with cross-hatched designs, from a phase dated to about 75,000 years ago and then the oldest deliberate patterns known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same sediments held 41 tick shells of the sea snail Nassarius kraussianus, pierced on purpose and bearing traces of use, alongside worked bone tools and 400 bifacial Still Bay points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A deeper layer preserved a pigment workshop from around 100,000 years ago, where an ochre-rich compound was mixed with bone, charcoal and crushed stone and stored in two abalone shells, one still holding its quartzite grinder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 2018 the excavators announced a cross-hatched pattern drawn with an ochre crayon on a ground silcrete flake about 73,000 years ago, at least 30,000 years older than any drawing then known, though what it meant is beyond recovery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Blombos Cave","questions":["About 73,000 years old, a cross-hatched pattern drawn on a silcrete flake with an ochre crayon made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the source of the oldest drawing then known.","A layer around 100,000 years old preserved a whole pigment workshop at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where ochre paste was ground and stored in two abalone shells."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","south africa"],"sources":["Katja Douze, Sarah Wurz, and Christopher S. Henshilwood, “Techno-Cultural Characterization of the MIS 5 (c. 105–90 Ka) Lithic Industries at Blombos Cave, Southern Cape, South Africa,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 10, no. 11 (2015): e0142151, under “Research background on Blombos Cave,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0142151. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, esp. 9964, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Daniela Eugenia Rosso, Africa Pitarch Martí and Francesco d’Errico, “Middle Stone Age Ochre Processing and Behavioural Complexity in the Horn of Africa: Evidence from Porc-Epic Cave, Dire Dawa, Ethiopia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 11 (2016): e0164793, under “Introduction,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0164793. [Open access]","Christopher S. Henshilwood, Francesco d'Errico, Karen L. van Niekerk, et al., “An Abstract Drawing from the 73,000-Year-Old Levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa,” <i>Nature</i> 562, no. 7725 (2018): 115–18, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0514-3. [Paywalled]","Frederick E. Grine and Christopher S. Henshilwood, “Additional Human Remains from Blombos Cave, South Africa: (1999–2000 Excavations),” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 42, no. 3 (2002): 293–302, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.2001.0525. [Paywalled]","Zenobia Jacobs et al., “Ages for the Middle Stone Age of Southern Africa: Implications for Human Behavior and Dispersal,” <i>Science</i> 322, no. 5902 (2008): 733–35, esp. 733, https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstreams/3e6b212a-1edc-45c8-a0b6-067293fb544c/download. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/BBC-strat-date-.jpg","title":"Blombos Cave","desc":"Stratigraphy and dates of the west section of Blombos Cave. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BBC-strat-date-.jpg","alt":"BBC-strat-date"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Blombos Cave","at":[21.2225,-34.4144]},"why":[{"q":"Why did such a small cave become so important?","a":"Because it reaches only six or seven metres from the drip line to the back wall, yet the excavated Middle Stone Age sequence beneath its floor runs more than four metres deep — a long record in a tiny space."},{"q":"Why did the engraved ochre matter when it was published?","a":"Because the two pieces, from a phase dated to about 75,000 years ago, were then the oldest deliberate patterns known anywhere, carrying the origins of symbolic behaviour deep into the African Middle Stone Age."},{"q":"Why is the pigment workshop remarkable as a workshop?","a":"Because around 100,000 years ago an ochre-rich compound was mixed with bone, charcoal and crushed stone and stored in two abalone shells, one still holding its quartzite grinder — a recipe, a container and a tool together."}]},
{"id":"wh-059","num":"59","category":"World · History","question":"Small crescents of stone, blunted along one edge so glue and binding could hold them in a wooden shaft, are the signature of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a southern African tradition.","answer":"Howiesons Poort","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 64,800 – 59,500 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Howiesons Poort</b> is a distinctive toolmaking tradition of the southern African Middle Stone Age, recognised at rock shelters and caves across South Africa, Lesotho and Namibia. Its knappers struck long, narrow blades from carefully prepared cores of fine-grained stone such as silcrete and chert, then snapped them into short lengths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One edge of each piece was blunted by steep retouch, a treatment archaeologists call backing, which left the opposite edge razor-sharp while giving glue and binding a blunt curve to grip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The result was a small crescent or trapeze, often no longer than a fingernail, made to sit in a wooden shaft as a replaceable part rather than be held in the hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Composite weapons of that kind are ordinary in far later prehistory, so the industry has long been read as a sign of thoroughly modern technical thinking. <br><br> The name comes from a small shelter above the pass on the road into Grahamstown, now Makhanda, in the Eastern Cape, dug in 1927 and 1928 by John Hewitt of the Albany Museum and Father Stapleton, a teacher at St Aidan's College; they named it after the pass, which carried the name of a Mr Howison, misspelled that name in print, and the error has been repeated ever since. Single-grain luminescence dating published by Zenobia Jacobs, Richard Roberts and colleagues in 2008 placed the industry between roughly 64,800 and 59,500 years ago at sites hundreds of kilometres apart, a shared style lasting some 5,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later thermoluminescence work at Diepkloof Rock Shelter has returned much older ages, so the true span is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same layers have yielded 270 fragments of ostrich eggshell engraved with repeated hatched bands about 60,000 years old, and a bone point from Sibudu Cave that its excavators read as an arrow tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most puzzling is the ending, because backed segments largely drop out of the levels above, and whether cooling climate, thinning populations or changed hunting ranges explains their loss is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Howiesons Poort","questions":["Single-grain luminescence dating puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 64,800 and 59,500 years ago, a single style shared across sites hundreds of kilometres apart.","Named after a pass on the road into Grahamstown that carried a Mr Howison's misspelled name, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dug at a small shelter in 1927 and 1928."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","south africa"],"sources":["Zenobia Jacobs et al., \"Ages for the Middle Stone Age of Southern Africa: Implications for Human Behavior and Dispersal,\" <i>Science</i> 322, no. 5902 (2008): 733–35, esp. 733, https://openresearch-repository.anu.edu.au/bitstreams/3e6b212a-1edc-45c8-a0b6-067293fb544c/download. [Open access]","Pierre-Jean Texier et al., \"A Howiesons Poort Tradition of Engraving Ostrich Eggshell Containers Dated to 60,000 Years Ago at Diepkloof Rock Shelter, South Africa,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 14 (2010): 6180–85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851956/. [Open access]","Lucinda Backwell, Francesco d’Errico, and Lyn Wadley, \"Middle Stone Age Bone Tools from the Howiesons Poort Layers, Sibudu Cave, South Africa,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 35, no. 6 (2008): 1566–80, esp. 1573–76, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jas.2007.11.006. [Paywalled]","Sylvain Soriano et al., \"The Still Bay and Howiesons Poort at Sibudu and Blombos: Understanding Middle Stone Age Technologies,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0131127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0131127. [Open access]","Martin Ziegler et al., \"Development of Middle Stone Age Innovation Linked to Rapid Climate Change,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 4 (2013): 1905, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms2897. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Annals_of_the_South_African_Museum_-_Annale_van_die_Suid-Afrikaanse_Museum_%281929%29_%2818394935036%29.jpg/1920px-Annals_of_the_South_African_Museum_-_Annale_van_die_Suid-Afrikaanse_Museum_%281929%29_%2818394935036%29.jpg","title":"Howiesons Poort","desc":"Title : Annals of the South African Museum = Annale van die Suid-Afrikaanse Museum Identifier : annalsofsouthafr27192sout (find matches) Year : 1898 (1890s) Authors : South African Museum Subjects : Natural history Publisher : Cape Town : The Museum Contributing Library : Smithsonian Libraries… Public domain (No restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annals_of_the_South_African_Museum_-_Annale_van_die_Suid-Afrikaanse_Museum_(1929)_(18394935036).jpg","alt":"Annals of the South African Museum - Annale van die Suid-Afrikaanse Museum (1929)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Howiesons Poort","at":[26.5267,-33.31],"kind":"region","area":[[16.3,-28.6],[16.9,-26.6],[19.9,-26.4],[22.5,-26],[25.5,-25.8],[28.4,-25.7],[31.3,-26.5],[32.4,-27.8],[30.5,-30.6],[28.3,-32.6],[25.6,-34.2],[22.3,-34.6],[19,-34.5],[17.3,-32.4]]},"why":[{"q":"Why was one edge of each blade deliberately blunted?","a":"Because backing leaves the opposite edge razor-sharp while giving glue and binding a blunt curve to grip, so the piece could sit in a wooden shaft as a replaceable part rather than be held in the hand."},{"q":"Why is the industry read as modern technical thinking?","a":"Because composite weapons of that kind — a small crescent or trapeze set into a shaft — are ordinary in far later prehistory, so their appearance here puts the idea tens of thousands of years earlier."},{"q":"Why is the name itself a mistake?","a":"Because Hewitt and Stapleton named the shelter after the pass above it, which carried the name of a Mr Howison, misspelled that name in print, and the error has been repeated ever since."}]},
{"id":"wh-060","num":"60","category":"World · History","question":"Named for a site in eastern Algeria and known by the narrow stem worked into the base of its stone points, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread across North Africa and a Sahara that was then green.","answer":"Aterian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 145,000 – 30,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">both ends debated</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aterian</b> is a Middle Stone Age toolkit found across North Africa and the Sahara, named after Bir el Ater in eastern Algeria, where the collection that defined it was gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What sets it apart is the tang, a narrow stem worked into the base of a point or scraper so that the tool could be seated in a split shaft and bound tight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A tang is the clearest early sign of a stone tool designed from the outset to be mounted rather than held, which is why these points are often called the oldest arrowheads, though that goes further than the evidence allows. Alongside them the assemblages hold leaf-shaped bifaces, scrapers and flakes struck by the Levallois method, which shapes a core in advance so that a flake of the wanted form comes off in a single blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Luminescence dating puts the earliest Aterian at about 145,000 years ago, and the tradition ran on until roughly 30,000, though both dates are debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its makers were Homo sapiens, settled by skulls and jaws from Dar es-Soltane, Contrebandiers and El Harhoura on the Moroccan coast that lay in Aterian layers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Taforalt in eastern Morocco people pierced sea snail shells, rubbed them with red ochre and wore them, and at roughly 82,000 years old these are among the oldest ornaments known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bone was worked into shaped tools at Contrebandiers between about 120,000 and 90,000 years ago, probably for hides and fur, and the caves hold hearths and traces of built structures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The spread of the toolkit followed the Sahara's mood, since the desert filled with grassland and lakes during humid spells, opening corridors that closed again when it dried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Specialists now hesitate over the word itself, because the tanged points vary greatly from place to place and the assemblages shade into the rest of the North African Middle Stone Age, so the Aterian may be a habit shared across a wide region rather than one people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aterian","questions":["Made by Homo sapiens and dated at Ifri n'Ammar in Morocco to about 145,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tanged its points to seat them in split shafts.","At Taforalt in eastern Morocco its people pierced sea-snail shells and rubbed them with ochre, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left ornaments among the oldest known, roughly 82,000 years old."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","africa"],"sources":["Inga Bergmann, Jean-Jacques Hublin, Abdelouahed Ben-Ncer, Fatima Zohra Sbihi-Alaoui, Philipp Gunz, and Sarah E. Freidline, “The Relevance of Late MSA Mandibles on the Emergence of Modern Morphology in Northern Africa,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 8841, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9133045/. [Open access]","Emilie Campmas, Matthieu Lebon, et al., “Aterian Shell Beads from the Coastal Site of El Mnasra Cave (Rabat-Témara, Morocco): Specificities of the North African MSA Personal Ornaments,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21 (2026): e0338785, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13016355/. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Emily Y. Hallett, Curtis W. Marean, Teresa E. Steele, et al., “A Worked Bone Assemblage from 120,000–90,000 Year Old Deposits at Contrebandiers Cave, Atlantic Coast, Morocco,” <i>iScience</i> 24, no. 9 (2021): 102988, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8478944/. [Open access]","Yassine Ait Brahim, Lijuan Sha, Jasper A. Wassenburg, et al., “The Spatiotemporal Extent of the Green Sahara during the Last Glacial Period,” <i>iScience</i> 26, no. 7 (2023): 107018, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10320408/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/At%C3%A9rien_%28Djelfa%29.JPG","title":"Aterian","desc":"Atérien, région de Djelfa (Zaccar), Algérie. Michel-georges bernard, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:At%C3%A9rien_(Djelfa).JPG","alt":"Atérien (Djelfa)"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does the tang matter more than the shape of the point?","a":"Because a narrow stem worked into the base is the clearest early sign of a stone tool designed from the outset to be mounted in a split shaft and bound tight, rather than held in the hand."},{"q":"Why is calling these the oldest arrowheads going too far?","a":"Because a tang shows only that the tool was meant to be hafted — it does not show what it was hafted to, and the evidence does not reach as far as a bow."},{"q":"Why do specialists now hesitate over the word Aterian?","a":"Because the tanged points vary greatly from place to place and the assemblages shade into the rest of the North African Middle Stone Age, so it may be a habit shared across a wide region rather than one people."}]},
{"id":"wh-061","num":"61","category":"World · History","question":"The traits archaeologists once treated as the mark of a fully human mind — art, ornament, burial, language — go under the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and where they arose is still argued.","answer":"Behavioural modernity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolution model</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gradual model</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 300,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Behavioural modernity</b> is the name archaeologists give to the bundle of habits and abilities taken to mark a mind like our own, not merely a body like our own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual list runs to art and ornament, deliberate burial, music, fishing and specialised hunting, tools of bone and antler, blades struck from prepared cores, goods carried far from home, and above all symbols and language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None leaves a fossil of thought, so each is read as a proxy: a pierced shell bead is taken as proof that its wearer meant something by it and expected others to understand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea took shape around how abruptly cave paintings, figurines and beads appear in the European Upper Paleolithic, after about 45,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On one reading that suddenness is real, some neurological change around 50,000 years ago having made the modern mind possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Against it stands the African record: pigment working, blades, bone points, shell beads and engraved ochre are known there from between 300,000 and 70,000 years ago, scattered and intermittent rather than sudden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That makes the European burst a late local flourish rather than the birth of the mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Population size may explain the flickering, since dense and connected groups keep hard-won skills alive where thin ones lose them, so the record may track numbers rather than intelligence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neanderthals used pigment and pierced shells at Cueva de los Aviones in Spain about 115,000 years ago, which makes the trait list a poor boundary between them and us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> So the checklist is now widely set aside in favour of what people actually did, the traits turning up patchily across a subdivided African population, though the term stays in wide use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Behavioural modernity","questions":["A paper of 2000 titled 'The revolution that wasn't' scattered the trait list of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across African sites tens of thousands of years before Europe's cave art.","One account ties <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a neurological change around 50,000 years ago, reading the sudden burst of Upper Paleolithic art and beads as a real event."],"tags":["concept","archaeology","prehistory","evolution"],"sources":["Sally McBrearty and Alison S. Brooks, “The Revolution That Wasn’t: A New Interpretation of the Origin of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 39, no. 5 (2000): 453–563, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.2000.0435. [Paywalled]","Richard G. Klein, “Out of Africa and the Evolution of Human Behavior,” <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 17, no. 6 (2008): 267–81, https://doi.org/10.1002/evan.20181. [Paywalled]","Adam Powell, Stephen Shennan, and Mark G. Thomas, “Late Pleistocene Demography and the Appearance of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Science</i> 324, no. 5932 (2009): 1298–1301, https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/168654/. [Open access]","João Zilhão et al., “Symbolic Use of Marine Shells and Mineral Pigments by Iberian Neandertals,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 3 (2010): 1023–28, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2824307/. [Open access]","Francesco d’Errico and Chris B. Stringer, “Evolution, Revolution or Saltation Scenario for the Emergence of Modern Cultures?,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 366, no. 1567 (2011): 1060–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3049097/. [Open access]","Dirk L. Hoffmann, Diego E. Angelucci, Valentín Villaverde, Josefina Zapata, and João Zilhão, “Symbolic Use of Marine Shells and Mineral Pigments by Iberian Neandertals 115,000 Years Ago,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 2 (2018): eaar5255, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5833998/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., “Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?,” <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/VenusHohlefels2.jpg","title":"Behavioural modernity","desc":"Paleolithic figure Venus of Hohlefels (of mammoth ivory). Ramessos, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VenusHohlefels2.jpg","alt":"The Venus of Hohle Fels"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is every item on the list read as a proxy?","a":"Because none of them leaves a fossil of thought. A pierced shell bead is taken as proof that its wearer meant something by it and expected others to understand — the object stands in for a mind that cannot be dug up."},{"q":"Why does the African record undercut the sudden-change reading?","a":"Because pigment working, blades, bone points, shell beads and engraved ochre are known there from between 300,000 and 70,000 years ago, scattered and intermittent rather than sudden — which makes the European burst a late local flourish."},{"q":"Why might the flickering track numbers rather than intelligence?","a":"Because dense and connected groups keep hard-won skills alive where thin ones lose them, so a technique can appear, vanish and reappear without anything changing about the minds that hold it."}]},
{"id":"wh-064","num":"64","category":"World · History","question":"Built on a real Sumatran supereruption, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that our species was nearly wiped out 74,000 years ago, until diggers found people carrying on through the ash.","answer":"Toba catastrophe theory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eruption</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 74,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1998, by Stanley Ambrose</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Toba catastrophe theory</b> is the proposal that a volcanic eruption in Sumatra about 74,000 years ago brought our species close to extinction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eruption itself is not in doubt: Toba expelled something like 5,300 cubic kilometres of magma, the largest eruption known in the last two million years, and laid a bed of ash from the Arabian Sea to the South China Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1998 Stanley Ambrose argued that a volcanic winter had ushered in a thousand years of the coldest temperatures of the later Pleistocene, around 71,000 to 70,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He tied that to a puzzle in our own biology, since human genetic diversity is oddly low for so widespread a species, which had been read as the mark of a crash to a few thousand breeding individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Put together, the two made an irresistible story: a mountain in Indonesia nearly ended us, and everyone alive descends from the handful who scraped through. <br><br> The evidence has not been kind to it. In India, stone tools lie both below and above thick beds of Toba ash — at Jwalapuram in the south and at Dhaba in the Son valley — and look much the same in each, so whoever was making them carried straight on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cores drilled from Lake Malawi contain glass shards from the eruption, and the mud around them records a cooling of only about 1.5 degrees, nothing like a volcanic winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Pinnacle Point in South Africa the same ash falls in the middle of an occupation that not only continues but grows busier afterwards, with new kinds of tool appearing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Newer climate models still give a sharp global chill but leave Africa and India among the mildest places on the map, so the eruption stands as one of the largest in the history of the planet while the near-extinction it was supposed to have caused has quietly gone away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Toba catastrophe theory","questions":["Stanley Ambrose proposed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1998, arguing a volcanic winter opened a thousand years of extreme cold after a blast that laid ash from the Arabian Sea to the South China Sea.","At Jwalapuram in India, tools lie little changed below and above the Toba ash, one of the finds that quietly undid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its near-extinction."],"tags":["theory","geology","science","prehistory"],"sources":["Stanley H. Ambrose, “Late Pleistocene Human Population Bottlenecks, Volcanic Winter, and Differentiation of Modern Humans,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 34, no. 6 (1998): 623–51, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.1998.0219. [Paywalled]","Michael Petraglia et al., “Middle Paleolithic Assemblages from the Indian Subcontinent Before and After the Toba Super-Eruption,” <i>Science</i> 317, no. 5834 (2007): 114–16, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1141564. [Paywalled]","Chris Clarkson et al., “Human Occupation of Northern India Spans the Toba Super-Eruption ~74,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 961, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7042215/. [Open access]","Christine S. Lane, Ben T. Chorn, and Thomas C. Johnson, “Ash from the Toba Supereruption in Lake Malawi Shows No Volcanic Winter in East Africa at 75 ka,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 20 (2013): 8025–29, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3657767/. [Open access]","Eugene I. Smith et al., “Humans Thrived in South Africa through the Toba Eruption about 74,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 555, no. 7697 (2018): 511–15, https://oasis.library.unlv.edu/geo_fac_articles/145. [Open access]","Benjamin A. Black et al., “Global Climate Disruption and Regional Climate Shelters after the Toba Supereruption,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 29 (2021): e2013046118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8307270/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Map_of_Lake_Toba.jpg/1920px-Map_of_Lake_Toba.jpg","title":"Toba catastrophe theory","desc":"Lake Toba - Danau Toba. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Lake_Toba.jpg","alt":"Map of Lake Toba"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why was the theory irresistible when it was proposed?","a":"Because it joined two things: the largest eruption known in two million years, and the puzzle that human genetic diversity is oddly low for so widespread a species, which had been read as a crash to a few thousand breeding individuals."},{"q":"Why do the Indian stone tools weigh against it?","a":"Because at Jwalapuram and at Dhaba the tools lie both below and above thick beds of Toba ash and look much the same in each, so whoever was making them carried straight on."},{"q":"Why has the near-extinction quietly gone away while the eruption has not?","a":"Because the eruption is not in doubt, while Lake Malawi cores record a cooling of only about 1.5 degrees, Pinnacle Point grows busier after the ashfall, and newer models leave Africa and India among the mildest places on the map."}]},
{"id":"wh-065","num":"65","category":"World · History","question":"Long blamed on our own arrival, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now dated to between 41,000 and 39,000 years ago, and may owe more to tiny inbred populations.","answer":"Neanderthal extinction","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 41,000 – 39,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Redating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neanderthal extinction</b> is the disappearance of the Neanderthals from Europe and western Asia around 40,000 years ago, after a run of several hundred thousand years. A dating programme published in 2014 rebuilt the chronology of 40 sites from Spain to Russia with cleaner radiocarbon methods and placed the end of their toolkit between about 41,000 and 39,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a single moment: they went from different regions at different times, so the map empties patchily rather than all at once. Where both peoples are found, the same work put their overlap with arriving modern humans at roughly 2,600 to 5,400 years, long enough to meet and to interbreed, far too long for a single conquest. An earlier study had already undercut the claim that late bands held out in southern Iberia long after the north, since redating with better purification pushed those young ages back beyond the reach of the method.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Three explanations are argued over, and none excludes the others. Competition is the oldest: modern humans arrived in larger groups with wider networks for exchanging materials, and may simply have worked the same country harder. Climate is the second, since the period is full of violent cold swings, and a vast Italian eruption falls near the same window, though its worst effects were brief and largely missed western Europe. The third is demography, and the genomes have made it the strongest: a Siberian cave held a father and his daughter in a band of perhaps a couple of dozen, and a late individual from France came from a line isolated for 50,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A modelling study in 2019 found that inbreeding, the difficulty of finding mates in tiny groups and plain bad luck could have finished them within 10,000 years with nobody else involved, so most specialists now expect several pressures at once, and no clean ending, since their DNA is still in us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Neanderthal extinction","questions":["A 2014 dating programme across 40 sites placed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 41,000 and 39,000 years ago and showed it happened patchily, region by region, not all at once.","A 2019 model found that inbreeding, the trouble of finding mates in tiny bands and plain bad luck could have driven the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with no one else involved."],"tags":["event","palaeontology","prehistory","evolution"],"sources":["Tom Higham et al., “The Timing and Spatiotemporal Patterning of Neanderthal Disappearance,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–9, abstract, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]","Rachel E. Wood et al., “Radiocarbon Dating Casts Doubt on the Late Chronology of the Middle to Upper Palaeolithic Transition in Southern Iberia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 8 (2013): 2781–86, abstract and “Results and Discussion,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3581959/. [Open access]","Laurits Skov et al., “Genetic Insights into the Social Organization of Neanderthals,” <i>Nature</i> 610, no. 7932 (2022): 519–25, abstract and “Genomic Diversity,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581778/. [Open access]","Ludovic Slimak et al., “Long Genetic and Social Isolation in Neanderthals before Their Extinction,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 4, no. 9 (2024): 100593, summary, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11480857/. [Open access]","Krist Vaesen et al., “Inbreeding, Allee Effects and Stochasticity Might Be Sufficient to Account for Neanderthal Extinction,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 11 (2019): e0225117, abstract and “Materials and Methods,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0225117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Neanderthal_distribution.jpg","title":"Neanderthal extinction","desc":"Neanderthal distribution. 2019-05-05. Berria, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neanderthal_distribution.jpg","alt":"Neanderthal distribution"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why was the disappearance not a single moment?","a":"Because they went from different regions at different times, so the map empties patchily rather than all at once. The 2014 dating programme placed the end of their toolkit between about 41,000 and 39,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why does the length of the overlap rule out a conquest?","a":"Because where both peoples are found the overlap runs roughly 2,600 to 5,400 years — long enough to meet and to interbreed, far too long for a single conquest."},{"q":"Why have the genomes made demography the strongest explanation?","a":"Because they show how few and how isolated the groups were: a Siberian cave held a father and his daughter in a band of perhaps a couple of dozen, and a late French individual came from a line isolated for 50,000 years."}]},
{"id":"wh-066","num":"66","category":"World · History","question":"If Neanderthals really made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of France and northern Spain, with its curved backed blades, bone awls and pierced teeth, then ornament was never ours alone.","answer":"Châtelperronian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 44,000 – 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">dates disputed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1938, by Dorothy Garrod</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Châtelperronian</b> is a stone tool industry of central and south-western France and northern Spain, made at the moment the Middle Paleolithic was giving way to the Upper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Henri Breuil defined it in 1909 from the Grotte des Fées at Châtelperron, a cave in the Allier, filing it under the Aurignacian as that industry's earliest phase, and the name we use is Dorothy Garrod's, from 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its signature tool is the Châtelperron knife, a blade blunted down one curved edge so that it could be gripped or hafted without cutting the hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> With it come bone awls and pierced animal teeth, the sort of worked bone and ornament that archaeologists had long treated as the mark of modern humans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits between the Mousterian below and the Protoaurignacian above, roughly 44,000 to 40,000 years ago, though the dates are argued over as fiercely as everything else about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The long quarrel is about who made it, and the answer decides whether ornament and blade-making were ever ours alone. A Neanderthal skeleton dug out at Saint-Césaire in 1979 lay in a Châtelperronian level, and teeth from the Grotte du Renne at Arcy-sur-Cure pointed the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 2016 the proteins in bone scraps from that cave were read as archaic and the ancient DNA in them as Neanderthal, which their authors took as settling that the tools and the bones belonged together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The doubts have not gone: dates from the Grotte du Renne scatter as though the layers had been churned, the Saint-Césaire association was challenged in 2018, and in 2023 the hip bone of a newborn modern human was identified in the same collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even granting that Neanderthals made it, whether they worked it out themselves or copied the modern humans arriving in Europe is unsettled, and the whole question rests on a handful of disturbed cave floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Châtelperronian","questions":["Henri Breuil named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1906 after the Grotte des Fées at Châtelperron, having at first filed the very same material under the Aurignacian.","A Neanderthal skeleton found at Saint-Césaire in 1979 lay in a level of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but churned dates and a 2023 newborn modern human keep who made it disputed."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","france"],"sources":["Igor Djakovic, Morgan Roussel, and Marie Soressi, \"Stone tools in shifting sands: past, present, and future perspectives on the Châtelperronian stone tool industry,\" <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 7 (2024): 29, https://hdl.handle.net/1887/4108948. [Open access]","Frido Welker, Mateja Hajdinjak, Sahra Talamo, et al., \"Palaeoproteomic evidence identifies archaic hominins associated with the Châtelperronian at the Grotte du Renne,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 40 (2016): 11162–11167, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5056053/. [Open access]","Arthur Gicqueau, Alexandra Schuh, Juliette Henrion, et al., \"Anatomically modern human in the Châtelperronian hominin collection from the Grotte du Renne (Arcy-sur-Cure, northeast France),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 12682, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403518/. [Open access]","Thomas Higham, Roger Jacobi, Michèle Julien, et al., \"Chronology of the Grotte du Renne (France) and implications for the context of ornaments and human remains within the Châtelperronian,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 47 (2010): 20234–20239, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2996711/. [Open access]","François Caron, Francesco d'Errico, Pierre Del Moral, et al., \"The reality of Neandertal symbolic behavior at the Grotte du Renne, Arcy-sur-Cure, France,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e21545, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3126825/. [Open access]","Brad Gravina, François Bachellerie, Solène Caux, et al., \"No reliable evidence for a Neanderthal–Châtelperronian association at La Roche-à-Pierrot, Saint-Césaire,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 15134, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6181958/. [Open access]","Igor Djakovic, Alastair Key, and Marie Soressi, \"Optimal linear estimation models predict 1400–2900 years of overlap between <i>Homo sapiens</i> and Neandertals prior to their disappearance from France and northern Spain,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 15000, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9561710/. [Open access]","Davide Delpiano, Brad Gravina, and Marco Peresani, \"Back(s) to basics: the concept of backing in stone tool technologies for tracing hominins' technical innovations,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 33, no. 6 (2024): e22045, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11624295/. [Open access]","Karl J. Narr, \"On the stratigraphy of Upper Palaeolithic types and type groups,\" <i>DEUQUA Special Publications</i> 3 (2021): 27–34, https://doi.org/10.5194/deuquasp-3-27-2021. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Pointes_de_chatelperron.jpg","title":"Châtelperronian","desc":"Pointes de chatelperron. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pointes_de_chatelperron.jpg","alt":"Pointes de chatelperron"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does it matter so much who made the Châtelperronian?","a":"Because the answer decides whether ornament and blade-making were ever ours alone: the industry carries bone awls and pierced animal teeth, the sort of work archaeologists had long treated as the mark of modern humans."},{"q":"Why have the doubts not gone away?","a":"Because dates from the Grotte du Renne scatter as though the layers had been churned, the Saint-Césaire association was challenged in 2018, and in 2023 the hip bone of a newborn modern human was identified in the same collection."},{"q":"Why would granting Neanderthal authorship still leave a question open?","a":"Because whether they worked it out themselves or copied the modern humans arriving in Europe is unsettled — and the whole question rests on a handful of disturbed cave floors."}]},
{"id":"wh-067","num":"67","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a rock shelter in the Pyrenean foothills, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Europe its first figurines and flutes as the last Neanderthals disappeared.","answer":"Aurignacian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 43,000 – 33,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1906, by Breuil and Cartailhac</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aurignacian</b> is the first Upper Paleolithic culture to spread across Europe, and it is generally attributed to modern humans rather than to the Neanderthals it replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its name comes from a rock shelter at Aurignac in the Pyrenean foothills of south-western France, where in 1852 a workman broke through a slab and found seventeen skeletons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mayor had them reburied in the parish cemetery, where they were promptly lost, and when Édouard Lartet dug the shelter in 1860 he found something far older beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Worked flint and bone lay among the remains of mammoth, cave bear and reindeer, and Émile Cartailhac proposed the site's name for the industry in 1906, with Henri Breuil defining it the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is recognised by steep-fronted carinated scrapers, long blades with a distinctive nibbled retouch, and slim antler points split at the base to take a shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What sets it apart is less the toolkit than what came with it, because the Aurignacian is where the European record stops being a matter of stone alone and fills with art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> From the caves of the Swabian Jura in southern Germany come ivory figurines, among them a lion-headed man and a small full-bodied woman, the latter carved at least 35,000 years ago and perhaps nearer 40,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> From the same caves come flutes cut from bird bone and mammoth ivory, lying in layers dated to between about 42,500 and 37,000 years ago, among the oldest musical instruments known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The painted lions and rhinoceroses of Chauvet in the Ardèche are often placed here too, though that attribution is disputed, and what is striking is how accomplished they already are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup> All of this appears in the few thousand years around 40,000 years ago, the same window in which the Neanderthals vanish, and whether that timing is cause or coincidence is argued over still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"12\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aurignacian","questions":["At Aurignac in 1852 a workman broke through a slab onto seventeen skeletons that were reburied and lost, before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the site's name in 1906.","From the Swabian caves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left ivory figurines and bird-bone flutes, one about 42,000 years old and the oldest musical instruments known anywhere."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Charles Lyell, <i>The Geological Evidences of the Antiquity of Man</i>, 2nd American ed. (Philadelphia: George W. Childs, 1863), 182–186, https://archive.org/details/geologicaleviden00lyeluoft. [Open access]","Édouard Lartet and Henry Christy, <i>Reliquiae Aquitanicae: Being Contributions to the Archaeology and Palaeontology of Périgord and the Adjoining Provinces of Southern France</i>, ed. Thomas Rupert Jones (London: Williams & Norgate, 1875), https://archive.org/details/reliquiaeaquitan00lartuoft. [Open access]","Keiko Kitagawa and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Split-based points from the Swabian Jura highlight Aurignacian regional signatures,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0239865, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0239865. [Open access]","Benjamin Schürch, Svenja Schray, and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Reconstructing flexible pathways of Aurignacian blade and bladelet production at Vogelherd,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0331921, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0331921. [Open access]","Ana B. Marín-Arroyo, Joseba Rios-Garaizar, Lawrence G. Straus et al., \"Chronological reassessment of the Middle to Upper Paleolithic transition and Early Upper Paleolithic cultures in Cantabrian Spain,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 4 (2018): e0194708, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0194708. [Open access]","Jonathan A. Haws, Michael M. Benedetti, Sahra Talamo, et al., \"The early Aurignacian dispersal of modern humans into westernmost Eurasia,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 41 (2020): 25414–25422, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2016062117. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Conard and Veerle Rots, \"Rope making in the Aurignacian of central Europe more than 35,000 years ago,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 5 (2024): eadh5217, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adh5217. [Open access]","Sara E. Rhodes, Britt M. Starkovich and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Did climate determine Late Pleistocene settlement dynamics in the Ach Valley, SW Germany?,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 5 (2019): e0215172, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0215172. [Open access]","Jean Bouyssonie, \"L'Aurignacien,\" <i>Bulletin de la Société préhistorique française</i> 51, no. 8 (1954): 49–53, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bspf_0249-7638_1954_hos_51_8_12409. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Conard, \"A female figurine from the basal Aurignacian of Hohle Fels Cave in southwestern Germany,\" <i>Nature</i> 459, no. 7244 (2009): 248–252, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07995. [Paywalled]","Paul Pettitt and Paul Bahn, \"An alternative chronology for the art of Chauvet Cave,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 89, no. 345 (2015): 542–553, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2015.21. [Paywalled]","Tom Higham, Katerina Douka, Rachel Wood, et al., \"The timing and spatiotemporal patterning of Neanderthal disappearance,\" <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7514 (2014): 306–309, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature13621. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Pendants%2C_Aurignacian_culture%2C_31000-24000_BC%2C_various_materials_-_Cinquantenaire_Museum_-_Brussels%2C_Belgium_-_DSC08704.jpg/1920px-Pendants%2C_Aurignacian_culture%2C_31000-24000_BC%2C_various_materials_-_Cinquantenaire_Museum_-_Brussels%2C_Belgium_-_DSC08704.jpg","title":"Aurignacian","desc":"Exhibit in the Cinquantenaire Museum - Brussels, Belgium. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pendants,_Aurignacian_culture,_31000-24000_BC,_various_materials_-_Cinquantenaire_Museum_-_Brussels,_Belgium_-_DSC08704.jpg","alt":"Pendants, Aurignacian culture, 31000-24000 BCE, various materials - Cinquantenaire Museum - Brussels, Belgium"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Aurignacian set apart by more than its toolkit?","a":"Because it is where the European record stops being a matter of stone alone and fills with art: ivory figurines from the Swabian Jura, a lion-headed man, and flutes cut from bird bone and mammoth ivory."},{"q":"Why were the first skeletons found at Aurignac of no use to science?","a":"Because the mayor had the seventeen skeletons reburied in the parish cemetery, where they were promptly lost. What Lartet found in 1860 was something far older beneath them."},{"q":"Why is the timing of the Aurignacian argued over?","a":"Because all of it appears in the few thousand years around 40,000 years ago, the same window in which the Neanderthals vanish, and whether that is cause or coincidence cannot be read off the dates."}]},
{"id":"wh-068","num":"68","category":"World · History","question":"Railway workers in the Dordogne uncovered five skeletons in 1868 at the shelter that gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name, long the word for every early modern human in Europe.","answer":"Cro-Magnon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 32,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1868</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cro-Magnon</b> is the name long given to the early modern humans of Ice Age Europe, and for a century it meant, in ordinary speech, the first people who looked like us. It comes from a rock shelter in the Vézère valley of south-western France, where in March 1868 workmen digging a road struck flints, animal bones and human remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The geologist Louis Lartet took charge and recovered four adults and an infant, laid in the shelter with pierced sea shells and worked reindeer antler, though later study found several more newborns among the bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is local and plain: in the region's Occitan a <i>cros</i> is 'a hollow' or 'a cave', while <i>Magnon</i> is either a personal name or an old word for 'large'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The find mattered because it placed people whose skeletons look like living Europeans in the same deposits as Ice Age animals, which at the time was still contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  Cro-Magnon 1, the skull of a middle-aged man, is among the most familiar fossils of its kind, and its owner was ill: a lesion had eaten into his forehead, read by some as a tumour, by others as a fungal infection. These were people of the Upper Paleolithic, striking long slender blades, carving bone and antler, and stringing shells and teeth into ornaments, and more than 300 perforated sea shells were laid in the graves with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A pierced shell from the burials has since been radiocarbon dated to about 32,000 years ago, later than anyone had assumed, which places them in the early Gravettian rather than the Aurignacian their tools had suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Like everyone whose ancestors left Africa they carried a little Neanderthal DNA, but Europeans today draw only part of their ancestry from such hunters, the rest arriving later with farmers and herders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word has quietly left the scientific literature, which prefers European early modern humans and keeps Cro-Magnon for the shelter and its fossils; it survives in museums, textbooks and the popular imagination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cro-Magnon","questions":["From the Occitan, where a cros is a hollow or cave, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took a plain local name for people whose skeletons already looked like living Europeans.","Because a pierced shell from the burials dates to about 32,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now sits in the early Gravettian rather than the Aurignacian its tools suggested."],"tags":["people","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","france"],"sources":["Dominique Henry-Gambier, \"Les fossiles de Cro-Magnon (Les Eyzies-de-Tayac, Dordogne): nouvelles données sur leur position chronologique et leur attribution culturelle,\" <i>Bulletins et Mémoires de la Société d'Anthropologie de Paris</i> 14, no. 1–2 (2002): 89–112, https://journals.openedition.org/bmsap/459. [Open access]","Dominique Henry-Gambier, Roland Nespoulet, and Laurent Chiotti, \"An early Gravettian cultural attribution for the human fossils from the Cro-Magnon rock shelter (Les Eyzies-de-Tayac, Dordogne),\" <i>PALEO</i> 24 (2013): 121–138, https://doi.org/10.4000/paleo.2859. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu, Cosimo Posth, Mateja Hajdinjak, et al., \"The genetic history of Ice Age Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7606 (2016): 200–205, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4943878/. [Open access]","Wolfgang Haak, Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, et al., \"Massive migration from the steppe was a source for Indo-European languages in Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 522, no. 7555 (2015): 207–211, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5048219/. [Open access]","Erik Trinkaus, \"European early modern humans and the fate of the Neandertals,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 18 (2007): 7367–7372, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1863481/. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Abri de Cro-Magnon,\" https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/en/a-propos/abri-cro-magnon. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Cro-Magnon-male-skull.png","title":"Cro-Magnon","desc":"Cro-Magnon-male-skull. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cro-Magnon-male-skull.png","alt":"Cro-Magnon-male-skull"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why did the 1868 find matter at the time?","a":"Because it placed people whose skeletons look like living Europeans in the same deposits as Ice Age animals, which was still contested then."},{"q":"Why did a radiocarbon date on a shell move the burials?","a":"Because a pierced shell from them dated to about 32,000 years ago, later than anyone had assumed, which places the burials in the early Gravettian rather than the Aurignacian their tools had suggested."},{"q":"Why has the word left the scientific literature?","a":"Because specialists prefer European early modern humans and keep Cro-Magnon for the shelter and its fossils. It survives in museums, textbooks and the popular imagination rather than in the papers."}]},
{"id":"wh-069","num":"69","category":"World · History","question":"Carved from a mammoth tusk about 40,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Swabian Jura is the oldest sculpture anyone has made of a creature that never existed.","answer":"Lion-man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reassembled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1969</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lion-man</b> is a standing figure with a lion's head, cut from a single mammoth tusk about 40,000 years ago and now the most famous object of the European Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Otto Völzing found it on 25 August 1939 in the Stadel cave of the Hohlenstein cliff, in the Lone valley of southern Germany, on the last day of the dig.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany invaded Poland a week later, the dig was abandoned, and the boxed splinters lay unstudied for thirty years, reaching the Museum Ulm only in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joachim Hahn opened it in 1969, recognised what he had, and fitted some 200 pieces together into a figure with a human body and the head of a cave lion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later digging in the cave returned hundreds more fragments, and the sculpture was taken apart and rebuilt once more in 2012 and 2013, reaching its present height of 31.1 centimetres (12.2 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nothing about it was useful, and that is what has kept it interesting. An experiment carving a replica with flint tools of the kind its maker had ran to hundreds of hours, an enormous outlay for a small band living hand to mouth. Whoever spent them was not carving a lion or a person but something that has never walked the earth, and no older sculpture of an imagined creature is known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether it is a god, a spirit, a person in a lion skin or a figure from a story nobody can now hear, there is no way to tell. Its sex was argued over for decades, and the rebuilt figure is now read as male on the evidence of a triangular sliver at the groin, while a second, much smaller lion-headed figure from the nearby cave of Hohle Fels shows the idea was not one carver's alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lion-man","questions":["Otto Völzing found it on the dig's last day in 1939, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sat in a box for thirty years until Joachim Hahn fitted its 200 ivory pieces together.","Carving a replica of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with flint tools took hundreds of hours, a huge outlay for a small band to spend on a creature that never walked the earth."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory","germany"],"sources":["Museum Ulm, “Löwenmensch,” Archäologie, inv. no. P 1939/2013, museum-digital:baden-württemberg, accessed 31 July 2026, https://bawue.museum-digital.de/object/2497. [Open access]","Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren, “Hohlenstein,” Welterbe-Fundstellen, accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.urmu.de/welterbe/fundstellen/hohlenstein. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), paras. 4 and 7, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Conard, “Palaeolithic Ivory Sculptures from Southwestern Germany and the Origins of Figurative Art,” <i>Nature</i> 426, no. 6968 (2003): 830–32, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02186. [Paywalled]","Welterbe Höhlen und Eiszeitkunst der Schwäbischen Alb, “UNESCO-Welterbe,” accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.iceageart.de/unesco-welterbe. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Loewenmensch1.jpg/1920px-Loewenmensch1.jpg","title":"Lion-man","desc":"The lion man from the Stadel Cave in Hohlenstein, Lonetal. Dagmar Hollmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loewenmensch1.jpg","alt":"Loewenmensch1"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why has the fact that it was useless kept the figure interesting?","a":"Because an experiment carving a replica with flint tools of the kind its maker had ran to hundreds of hours — an enormous outlay for a small band living hand to mouth, spent on something that does nothing."},{"q":"Why is the subject more remarkable than the craftsmanship?","a":"Because whoever carved it was not carving a lion or a person but something that has never walked the earth, and no older sculpture of an imagined creature is known anywhere."},{"q":"Why did the figure lie unstudied for thirty years?","a":"Because Völzing found it on the last day of the dig in August 1939; Germany invaded Poland a week later, the dig was abandoned, and the boxed splinters reached the Museum Ulm only in 1962."}]},
{"id":"wh-070","num":"70","category":"World · History","question":"Named from a Dordogne rock shelter, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the Ice Age its Venus figurines, its first fired clay and its first woven cloth.","answer":"Gravettian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 33,000 – 24,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1938, by Dorothy Garrod</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Gravettian</b> is the Upper Paleolithic culture that followed the Aurignacian, and from about 33,000 years ago it reached from the Atlantic coast to the Russian plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It takes its name from La Gravette, a rock shelter at Bayac in the Dordogne, and the label is credited to Dorothy Garrod, who in 1939 became the first woman to hold a professorial chair at Oxford or Cambridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Its marker is the Gravette point, a narrow blade blunted down one straight edge so that it could be set into a shaft as a spear tip or a knife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The people who made it hunted across open cold steppe, and on the Russian plain they raised circular structures framed from mammoth bone, among the oldest of them at Kostenki as the ice reached its greatest extent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its last few thousand years run into the coldest stretch of the Ice Age, when much of northern Europe emptied and people drew back towards the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br>  The carved figures a reader pictures as Ice Age art belong to this culture, though the great painted caves do not, Chauvet being older and Lascaux and Altamira younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Venus figurines are Gravettian, among them the one from Willendorf in Austria and the one from Dolní Věstonice in Moravia, and the second of those is not stone or ivory but fired clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At roughly 31,000 to 29,000 years old the Moravian figures are among the oldest fired ceramics known, made thousands of years before anyone thought to fire a pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup> Clay also caught what does not normally survive, since impressions on fired lumps at nearby Pavlov preserve cord, netting and woven fibre, among the earliest evidence of textile technology anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Ancient DNA has since sprung a surprise: the Gravettians of France and Spain and those of central, eastern and southern Europe carried distinct ancestries while sharing one toolkit, and only the western line is traceable after the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Gravettian","questions":["Dorothy Garrod named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1938 after La Gravette, the year before she became the first woman to hold a professorial chair at Oxford or Cambridge.","Ancient DNA has revealed that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was shared by two distinct populations, the French-Spanish and the central European, of whom only the western group survived the coldest ice."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Cosimo Posth, He Yu, Ayshin Ghalichi, et al., \"Palaeogenomics of Upper Palaeolithic to Neolithic European hunter-gatherers,\" <i>Nature</i> 615, no. 7950 (2023): 117–126, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9977688/. [Open access]","Rebecca Farbstein and April Nowell, \"Children at play: the role of novices in the production of Europe's earliest Upper Paleolithic ceramics,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 10 (2024): e0309107, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0309107. [Open access]","Rebecca Farbstein, Dinko Radić, Dejana Brajković, and Preston T. Miracle, \"First Epigravettian ceramic figurines from Europe (Vela Spila, Croatia),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 7 (2012): e41437, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3404104/. [Open access]","Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., \"The microstructure and the origin of the Venus from Willendorf,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Colin D. Wren and Ariane Burke, \"Habitat suitability and the genetic structure of human populations during the Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) in western Europe,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0217996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0217996. [Open access]","Andreas Maier, Robin John, Florian Linsel et al., \"Analyzing trends in material culture evolution—a case study of Gravettian points from Lower Austria and Moravia,\" <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 6 (2023): 15, https://doi.org/10.1007/s41982-023-00145-z. [Open access]","Wei Chu, Pavlo Shydlovskyi, and Andreas Maier, \"A revised radiocarbon chronology for the mammoth bone structures and associated features at Mezhyrich, Ukraine,\" <i>Open Research Europe</i> 5 (2025): 198, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12639289/. [Open access]","Alexander J. E. Pryor, David G. Beresford-Jones, Alexander E. Dudin, et al., \"The chronology and function of a new circular mammoth-bone structure at Kostenki 11,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 94, no. 374 (2020): 323–341, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2020.7. [Paywalled]","Pamela Jane Smith, \"Dorothy Garrod, first woman Professor at Cambridge,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 74, no. 283 (2000): 131–136, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00066230. [Paywalled]","James M. Adovasio, Olga Soffer and Bohuslav Klíma, \"Upper Palaeolithic fibre technology: interlaced woven finds from Pavlov I, Czech Republic, c. 26,000 years ago,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 70, no. 269 (1996): 526–34, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X0008368X. [Paywalled]","Pamela B. Vandiver, Olga Soffer, Bohuslav Klíma, and Jiří Svoboda, \"The origins of ceramic technology at Dolni Věstonice, Czechoslovakia,\" <i>Science</i> 246, no. 4933 (1989): 1002–1008, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.246.4933.1002. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/V%C3%A9nus_de_Lespugue_Gravettien_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27Homme_04022018_3.jpg/1920px-V%C3%A9nus_de_Lespugue_Gravettien_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27Homme_04022018_3.jpg","title":"Gravettian","desc":"Venus of Lespugue (back view), mammoth ivory, found in the Grotte des Rideaux (Lespugue, Haute-Garonne, France). Dated from the Gravettian, (Upper Paleolithic, 23 000 years). Currently in the Musée de l'Homme, in Paris. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V%C3%A9nus_de_Lespugue_Gravettien_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27Homme_04022018_3.jpg","alt":"Vénus de Lespugue Gravettien Musée de l'Homme 3"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are the great painted caves not Gravettian?","a":"Because the culture runs from about 33,000 years ago, which puts Chauvet before it and Lascaux and Altamira after it. What belongs to it are the carved figures — the Venus figurines of Willendorf and Dolní Věstonice."},{"q":"Why are the Moravian figurines remarkable beyond their subject?","a":"Because at roughly 31,000 to 29,000 years old they are fired clay rather than stone or ivory — among the oldest fired ceramics known, made thousands of years before anyone thought to fire a pot."},{"q":"Why is the ancient DNA a surprise?","a":"Because the Gravettians of France and Spain and those of central, eastern and southern Europe carried distinct ancestries while sharing one toolkit, and only the western line is traceable after the ice."}]},
{"id":"wh-071","num":"71","category":"World · History","question":"Found from the Pyrenees to Siberia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nearly all share the same swollen hips, thin arms and small blank head.","answer":"Venus figurines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mostly c. 30,000 – 23,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Venus figurines</b> are small carvings of women made across Ice Age Europe and Siberia, and they are among the most familiar objects the period has left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They come from a band of country running from the French Pyrenees to Lake Baikal, some 6,000 kilometres (3,700 miles), and most were made between roughly 30,000 and 23,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were cut from whatever lay to hand: mammoth ivory, limestone, calcite, steatite and antler, and at one Moravian site from clay that was then fired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What is strange is not that people carved women, but that they carved them the same way across that whole distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The breasts, belly, hips and thighs are swollen, the arms are thin or missing, the hands and feet trail away to nothing, and the head is usually small, bare and without a face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The exceptions are the most interesting objects in the set. The oldest of them all is a little ivory woman from Hohle Fels in Germany, carved at least 35,000 years ago and older by several thousand years than the Gravettian series that follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The figurines from Mal'ta in Siberia are slender rather than heavy, and several seem to be wearing fitted fur clothing, which is what a Siberian winter would demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 2022 a scan of the Willendorf figure matched its stone to an outcrop near Lake Garda, on the far side of the Alps, so either the carving or its owners had travelled a very long way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the figures were for is unknown, but conventions held in common over such distances mean the people who made them were in touch, and were handing on more than a way of working flint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Venus figurines","questions":["The oldest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a little ivory woman from Hohle Fels in Germany, carved at least 35,000 years ago, thousands of years before the rest.","Carved from ivory, limestone, steatite and even fired clay, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include the Willendorf figure, whose stone a 2022 scan matched to an outcrop across the Alps."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["Gillian Morriss-Kay, “A New Hypothesis on the Creation of the Hohle Fels “Venus” Figurine,” <i>Palethnologie</i> 5 (2013), paras. 1–3, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.5135. [Open access]","Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., “The Microstructure and the Origin of the Venus from Willendorf,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, under “Introduction” and “Discussion,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), paras. 4 and 7, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal’ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]","Rebecca Farbstein and April Nowell, “Children at Play: The Role of Novices in the Production of Europe’s Earliest Upper Paleolithic Ceramics,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 10 (2024): e0309107, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11508051/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Venus_figurine_of_Grimaldi_MAN_35308.jpg/1920px-Venus_figurine_of_Grimaldi_MAN_35308.jpg","title":"Venus figurines","desc":"Venus figurine of Grimaldi MAN 35308. Marie-Lan Taÿ Pamart. 2026-05-13. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_figurine_of_Grimaldi_MAN_35308.jpg","alt":"Venus figurine of Grimaldi MAN 35308"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the sameness of the figurines stranger than the subject?","a":"Because they were carved the same way across some 6,000 kilometres, from the French Pyrenees to Lake Baikal: swollen breasts, belly, hips and thighs, thin or missing arms, hands and feet trailing to nothing, and a small bare head without a face."},{"q":"Why are the exceptions the most interesting objects in the set?","a":"Because they break the pattern in ways that say something: the Hohle Fels woman is several thousand years older than the Gravettian series, and the Mal'ta figures are slender and seem to wear fitted fur clothing, which is what a Siberian winter would demand."},{"q":"Why do these carvings show more than a shared way of working stone?","a":"Because conventions held in common over such distances mean the people who made them were in touch — and the Willendorf figure's stone matches an outcrop on the far side of the Alps."}]},
{"id":"wh-072","num":"72","category":"World · History","question":"Cut from limestone that outcrops 730 kilometres (450 miles) away across the Alps, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has bands wound round her head and no face beneath them.","answer":"Venus of Willendorf","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 30,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1908</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Venus of Willendorf</b> is a woman 11.1 centimetres (4.4 inches) tall, cut from oolitic limestone and still tinted here and there with red ochre, which probably once covered her entirely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Josef Szombathy's excavation above the Danube at Willendorf in Lower Austria turned her up on 7 August 1908, lifted by a workman named Johann Veran, and she has been in Vienna ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was carved about 30,000 years ago, in the Gravettian, and she has become one of the best-known objects in the whole of prehistory, reproduced past counting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her arms are thin and laid across heavy breasts, her belly and hips are enormous, and her legs taper to nothing where the feet should be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The carver had to work around hard iron-oxide lumps scattered through the stone, and the cavity at her navel may be where one of them broke away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The head is the strangest part of her, wrapped in bands that run right round it and leave no face at all, not an eye and not a mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some read those bands as braided or coiled hair, others as a woven cap or a netted snood, which would make her a picture of a textile that rotted away long ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reading is not idle, because carvers far to the east at Mal'ta in Siberia marked their figures with what look like fitted fur coveralls and hoods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stone she is cut from does not occur anywhere near Willendorf, and in 2022 a scan of her interior found its closest match at an outcrop by Lake Garda, some 730 kilometres (450 miles) off on the far side of the Alps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether she was carried north over the mountains or her owners walked that road themselves, she is evidence that the Ice Age world was wider and better connected than a palm-sized carving suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Venus of Willendorf","questions":["A workman named Johann Veran picked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of Josef Szombathy's excavation above the Danube in Lower Austria on 7 August 1908.","Traces of red ochre once covered the whole of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an 11-centimetre limestone woman whose stone comes from an outcrop far south of the Alps."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory","austria"],"sources":["Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., “The Microstructure and the Origin of the Venus from Willendorf,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, under “Introduction,” “Results” and “Discussion,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Naturhistorisches Museum Wien, “Venus-Forschung,” Prähistorische Abteilung, accessed 31 July 2026, https://www.nhm.at/en/research/prehistory/research/venus-research. [Open access]","Gillian Morriss-Kay, “A New Hypothesis on the Creation of the Hohle Fels “Venus” Figurine,” <i>Palethnologie</i> 5 (2013), para. 1, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.5135. [Open access]","Harald Floss, “The Oldest Portable Art: The Aurignacian Ivory Figurines from the Swabian Jura (Southwest Germany),” <i>Palethnologie</i> 7 (2015), para. 4, https://doi.org/10.4000/palethnologie.888. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal’ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, under “Clothing,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Venus_of_Willendorf_-_All_sides.jpg/1920px-Venus_of_Willendorf_-_All_sides.jpg","title":"Venus of Willendorf","desc":"Venus of Willendorf as shown at the Naturhistorisches Museum in Vienna, Austria, in January 2020. Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_of_Willendorf_-_All_sides.jpg","alt":"Venus of Willendorf - All sides"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the head the strangest part of the figure?","a":"Because it is wrapped in bands that run right round it and leave no face at all, not an eye and not a mouth — where the rest of the body is carved in heavy detail."},{"q":"Why is reading those bands as a textile not idle speculation?","a":"Because carvers far to the east at Mal'ta in Siberia marked their figures with what look like fitted fur coveralls and hoods, so clothing was something Ice Age carvers did represent."},{"q":"Why does her stone make the Ice Age world look wider than it seems?","a":"Because the oolitic limestone does not occur anywhere near Willendorf, and a 2022 scan found its closest match at an outcrop by Lake Garda, some 730 kilometres off on the far side of the Alps."}]},
{"id":"wh-073","num":"73","category":"World · History","question":"Laurel-leaf blades 35 centimetres (14 inches) long and barely a centimetre (0.4 inches) thick make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the high point of stoneworking in Ice Age Europe.","answer":"Solutrean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 25,000 – 19,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1869, by Gabriel de Mortillet</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Solutrean</b> is an Upper Paleolithic industry of France, Spain and Portugal, made between roughly 25,000 and 19,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gabriel de Mortillet gave it the name in 1869, after Solutré in eastern Burgundy, which lies well away from the south-western country where most of its sites are found. It falls across the coldest stretch of the last Ice Age, when northern Europe emptied and people were pressed back into the south-western corner of the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its knappers thinned the blanks by percussion and finished them by pressure flaking, pressing the last flakes off with a point of antler, and some pieces had been heated beforehand to make the flint work more sweetly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The result is the laurel leaf, a blade worked on both faces and thinned to a few millimetres, and the finest of them are among the most beautiful things the Stone Age produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br>  A hoard found at Volgu holds laurel leaves over 30 centimetres (12 inches) long and barely a centimetre (0.4 inches) thick, worked far beyond anything hunting requires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Solutrean also brings the eyed needle to western Europe, where finer sewing answered the cold of the glacial maximum, though fitted clothing had been made with awls and burins long before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two things commonly said about it are worth knowing precisely because both of them are wrong. The horse bones heaped at Solutré were long explained as herds driven off the rock above, a story that held until the 1950s, but they are now read as the leavings of hunters ambushing horses along their migration route at the foot of the rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The other is the claim that Solutrean people crossed the Atlantic ice to become the Clovis hunters, which almost every archaeologist and geneticist rejects: the one Clovis genome ever read is closer to living Native Americans than to anybody in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Solutrean","questions":["Gabriel de Mortillet named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1869 after Solutré in Burgundy, and it spans the coldest stretch of the Ice Age between 22,000 and 17,000 years ago.","Working by pressure flaking with an antler point, and heating the flint first to soften it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knappers thinned blades on both faces to a few millimetres."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., \"The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,\" <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren, Robert J. Patten, Michael J. O'Brien, and David J. Meltzer, \"Refuting the technological cornerstone of the Ice-Age Atlantic crossing hypothesis,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 7 (2013): 2934–2941, https://cladistics.coas.missouri.edu/assets/pdf_articles/Eren_et_al_2013.pdf. [Open access]","João Cascalheira and Nuno Bicho, \"On the chronological structure of the Solutrean in southern Iberia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 9 (2015): e0137308, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0137308. [Open access]","João Cascalheira, \"Territoriality and the organization of technology during the Last Glacial Maximum in southwestern Europe,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0225828, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0225828. [Open access]","Ian Gilligan, Francesco d'Errico, Luc Doyon, Wei Wang, and Yaroslav V. Kuzmin, \"Paleolithic eyed needles and the evolution of dress,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 26 (2024): eadp2887, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11212769/. [Open access]","Julie Bachellerie, \"Chapitre 10. Outillage « solutréen » et schémas de production associés,\" <i>PALEO</i>, hors-série (2025): 150–193, https://doi.org/10.4000/14tzv. [Open access]","Elaine Turner, \"Horse, reindeer and bison hunters at Solutré: an archaeozoological analysis of a Magdalenian bone assemblage,\" <i>Bonner zoologische Beiträge</i> 50 (1999): 143–182, https://archive.org/details/biostor-136046. [Open access]","Thierry Aubry, Bruce Bradley, Miguel Almeida, et al., \"Solutrean laurel leaf production at Maîtreaux: an experimental approach guided by techno-economic analysis,\" <i>World Archaeology</i> 40, no. 1 (2008): 48–66, https://doi.org/10.1080/00438240701843538. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Biface_feuille_de_laurier.JPG","title":"Solutrean","desc":"Biface en forme de feuille de laurier. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biface_feuille_de_laurier.JPG","alt":"Biface feuille de laurier"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are the finest laurel leaves not tools at all?","a":"Because they are worked far beyond anything hunting requires: a hoard found at Volgu holds laurel leaves over 30 centimetres long and barely a centimetre thick, thinned to the point of fragility."},{"q":"Why is the horse-drive story at Solutré no longer told?","a":"Because the heaped bones, long explained as herds driven off the rock above, are now read as the leavings of hunters ambushing horses along their migration route at the foot of the rock."},{"q":"Why is the Solutrean-to-Clovis crossing rejected?","a":"Because almost every archaeologist and geneticist rejects it, and the one Clovis genome ever read is closer to living Native Americans than to anybody in Europe."}]},
{"id":"wh-074","num":"74","category":"World · History","question":"Toolmakers who worked antler as readily as flint and depended so completely on reindeer gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, named after a Dordogne rock shelter, its old label as the age of the reindeer.","answer":"Magdalenian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 21,000 – 14,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Magdalenian</b> is the last Upper Paleolithic industry of western Europe, made from roughly 21,000 to 14,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It takes its name from the rock shelter of La Madeleine in the Dordogne, and it was Gabriel de Mortillet, who also named the Solutrean, who attached the label to the whole epoch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its people leaned heavily on reindeer, so much so that scholars long called this the age of the reindeer, though bison and horse were at first more abundant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Where the Solutreans had put their skill into stone, the Magdalenians put it into antler and bone, turning out barbed harpoon heads, eyed needles, fine awls and spear-throwers that added range and force to a thrown shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The barbs and shapes of those harpoon heads change steadily through the sequence, which is why archaeologists have long used them to slot a site into its part of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> This is also the great age of cave painting, and the polychrome bison crowded across the ceiling at Altamira in northern Spain are Magdalenian work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So is much of the finest portable art, including spear-throwers carved into animals and the pair of swimming reindeer carved from ivory found at Montastruc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Burial could be elaborate, and a woman laid in El Mirón cave in Cantabria was covered in red ochre, with the engraved block beside her stained the same colour. As the ice pulled back, Magdalenian groups pushed north and east into Belgium, Germany and Poland, reaching Britain by about 14,800 years ago, where Gough's Cave at Cheddar records a short seasonal stay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes tie these scattered groups together and trace their ancestry back through the Gravettian populations of western Europe to the much earlier Aurignacian, setting them apart from the Gravettians of central and southern Europe, and the whole way of life gave way around 14,000 years ago to people spreading from the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Magdalenian","questions":["The polychrome bison crowding the ceiling at Altamira are the work of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose people also carved spear-throwers and engraved a pair of swimming reindeer on mammoth ivory.","As the ice pulled back the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread north and east into Belgium, Germany and Poland, reaching Britain by about 14,700 years ago for short seasonal stays."],"tags":["industry","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Qiaomei Fu, Cosimo Posth, Mateja Hajdinjak, et al., \"The genetic history of Ice Age Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7606 (2016): 200–205, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4943878/. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth, He Yu, Ayshin Ghalichi, et al., \"Palaeogenomics of Upper Palaeolithic to Neolithic European hunter-gatherers,\" <i>Nature</i> 615, no. 7950 (2023): 117–126, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9977688/. [Open access]","M. García-Diez, D. L. Hoffmann, J. Zilhão, et al., \"Uranium series dating reveals a long sequence of rock art at Altamira Cave (Santillana del Mar, Cantabria),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 11 (2013): 4098–4106, https://cir.cenieh.es/handle/20.500.12136/420. [Open access]","Silvia M. Bello, Lucile Crété, Julia Galway-Witham, and Simon A. Parfitt, \"Knapping tools in Magdalenian contexts: new evidence from Gough's Cave (Somerset, UK),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 12 (2021): e0261031, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0261031. [Open access]","Andy Needham, Izzy Wisher, Andrew Langley, Matthew Amy, and Aimée Little, \"Art by firelight? Using experimental and digital techniques to explore Magdalenian engraved plaquette use at Montastruc (France),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266146, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0266146. [Open access]","Pere Gelabert, Victoria Oberreiter, Lawrence Guy Straus, et al., \"A sedimentary ancient DNA perspective on human and carnivore persistence through the Late Pleistocene in El Mirón Cave, Spain,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 107, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11696082/. [Open access]","Henri Breuil, \"Le Magdalénien,\" <i>Bulletin de la Société préhistorique française</i> 51, no. 8 (1954): 59–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bspf_0249-7638_1954_hos_51_8_12411. [Open access]","Gabriel de Mortillet, <i>Le Préhistorique: Antiquité de l'homme</i>, 2nd ed. (Paris: C. Reinwald, 1885), chap. 4, https://archive.org/details/laprhistoirean00mort. [Open access]","Andreas Maier, Carmen Liebermann, and Sebastian J. Pfeifer, \"Beyond the Alps and Tatra Mountains—the 20–14 ka repopulation of the northern mid-latitudes as inferred from palimpsests deciphered with keys from western and central Europe,\" <i>Journal of Paleolithic Archaeology</i> 3 (2020): 398–452, https://doi.org/10.1007/s41982-019-00045-1. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Atlatls%2C_17-12_kya%2C_upper_from_La_Madeleine_rockshelter%2C_lower_from_Le_Mas_d%27Azil%2C_France_-_Houston_Museum_of_Natural_Science_-_DSC02033.JPG/1920px-Atlatls%2C_17-12_kya%2C_upper_from_La_Madeleine_rockshelter%2C_lower_from_Le_Mas_d%27Azil%2C_France_-_Houston_Museum_of_Natural_Science_-_DSC02033.JPG","title":"Magdalenian","desc":"Exhibit in the Houston Museum of Natural Science, Houston, Texas, USA. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlatls,_17-12_kya,_upper_from_La_Madeleine_rockshelter,_lower_from_Le_Mas_d%27Azil,_France_-_Houston_Museum_of_Natural_Science_-_DSC02033.JPG","alt":"Atlatls, 17-12 kya, upper from La Madeleine rockshelter, lower from Le Mas d'Azil, France - Houston Museum of Natural Science"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did archaeologists use harpoon heads to date a site?","a":"Because the barbs and shapes change steadily through the sequence, so a harpoon head slots a site into its part of the period in a way the stone tools do not."},{"q":"Why is the Magdalenian a story about antler and bone rather than stone?","a":"Because where the Solutreans had put their skill into stone, these people put it into barbed harpoon heads, eyed needles, fine awls and spear-throwers that added range and force to a thrown shaft."},{"q":"Why does the term age of the reindeer overstate the case?","a":"Because although its people leaned heavily on reindeer, bison and horse were at first more abundant — so the label describes the later part of the period better than the whole."}]},
{"id":"wh-075","num":"75","category":"World · History","question":"Made with ochre and charcoal on walls deep underground, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as the bison of Altamira or the horses of Chauvet usually shows large game animals rather than people.","answer":"cave painting","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 45,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Leang Tedongnge, Sulawesi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 40,800 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cave painting</b> is an image applied to the wall or ceiling of a cave or rock shelter, and archaeologists file it, along with engravings and reliefs, under the heading of parietal art. The palette is narrow and earthy, with reds and yellows coming from ochre, an iron-rich earth, and blacks from charcoal or ground manganese oxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Painters spread the colour with a finger, a pad of moss or fur, or a brush of animal hair, and they also blew a fine mist of it through a hollow bone or reed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Blowing pigment around a hand held flat against the rock leaves a stencil, and those pale hand shapes turn up in caves from France to Indonesia and Argentina. Large game animals dominate the subjects — horses, bison, aurochs, deer, mammoths and lions — while people are rare and sketchy, often outnumbered by dots, grids and other abstract signs. <br><br> The oldest securely dated painting is a Sulawesi warty pig at Leang Tedongnge in Indonesia, made at least 45,500 years ago, and a scene of pigs and human-like figures in a nearby cave has been dated to 51,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Europe's earliest date so far is a red disk at El Castillo in northern Spain, at least 40,800 years old, though the lions and rhinoceroses of Chauvet, drawn around 36,000 years ago, are better known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lascaux, painted between about 21,500 and 21,000 years ago and stumbled into by four teenagers in 1940, holds some 600 animal images, horses far outnumbering the stags, aurochs, ibexes and bison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the polychrome bison of Altamira came to light in 1879, the leading specialists refused to believe that anything so accomplished could be so old and accused their finder of forgery. One of them, Émile Cartailhac, published a famous apology in 1902, but what the paintings actually meant is still open, with hunting magic, a symbolic order and the visions of trance all proposed and none of them fitting every cave.","citation":"","answerText":"cave painting","questions":["Blowing a mist of pigment around a hand pressed to the rock leaves the pale stencils that mark a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in sites from France to Indonesia and Argentina.","The oldest securely dated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a warty pig at Leang Tedongnge in Indonesia, made at least 45,500 years ago, older than anything so far known from Europe."],"tags":["practice","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, et al., \"Oldest cave art found in Sulawesi,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abd4648. [Open access]","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim et al., \"Narrative cave art in Indonesia by 51,200 years ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 631 (2024): 814–818, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07541-7. [Open access]","A. W. G. Pike, D. L. Hoffmann, M. García-Díez, et al., \"U-Series Dating of Paleolithic Art in 11 Caves in Spain,\" <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6087 (2012): 1409–1413, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1219957. [Paywalled]","Anita Quiles, Hélène Valladas, Hervé Bocherens et al., \"A high-precision chronological model for the decorated Upper Paleolithic cave of Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, Ardèche, France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–4675, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Themes\" and \"Dating the Figures at Lascaux,\" <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"The Raw Materials,\" <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 3 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/raw-materials. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"The Techniques,\" <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 3 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/techniques. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Lubang_Jeriji_Sal%C3%A9h_cave_painting_of_Bull.jpg","title":"cave painting","desc":"Representation of a wild bovid, the Banteng, made in ochre, discovered in the Lubang Jeriji Saléh cave, East Kalimantan, Borneo, Indonesia, dated to 40 ka. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lubang_Jeriji_Sal%C3%A9h_cave_painting_of_Bull.jpg","alt":"Lubang Jeriji Saléh cave painting of Bull"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why is the palette of cave painting so narrow?","a":"Because it comes from what the ground offers: reds and yellows from ochre, an iron-rich earth, and blacks from charcoal or ground manganese oxide, spread with a finger, a pad of moss or fur, or a brush of animal hair."},{"q":"Why were the Altamira paintings first taken for a forgery?","a":"Because when the polychrome bison came to light in 1879 the leading specialists refused to believe anything so accomplished could be so old, and accused their finder of faking them. Cartailhac published an apology in 1902."},{"q":"Why is what the paintings meant still open?","a":"Because hunting magic, a symbolic order and the visions of trance have all been proposed, and none of them fits every cave — the subjects, the signs and the placings differ too much from site to site."}]},
{"id":"wh-076","num":"76","category":"World · History","question":"Sealed by a rockfall for some 20,000 years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Ardèche region of southern France preserved charcoal lions and rhinoceroses that three cavers found in December 1994.","answer":"Chauvet Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 37,000 – 33,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and c. 31,000 – 28,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chauvet Cave</b> is a limestone cavern in the Ardèche of southern France, near the natural arch of Pont-d'Arc, and it holds some of the oldest and best preserved figurative art known. Three cavers, Jean-Marie Chauvet, Éliette Brunel Deschamps and Christian Hillaire, felt cool air seeping from a narrow gap in the cliff on 18 December 1994 and dug their way through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A rockfall had closed the original entrance some 20,000 years earlier, sealing the interior so completely that hearths, torch smears and the footprints of animals and of a child survived on the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates on charcoal from the black drawings point to two periods of use, one about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago and another roughly 31,000 to 28,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those dates upset the accepted sequence of Paleolithic styles, and a minority of specialists still argue from style and stratigraphy that the art is thousands of years younger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Roughly a thousand images have been catalogued, more than 400 of them animals of at least 14 species, among them cave lions, mammoths, woolly rhinoceroses, cave bears, aurochs, ibex and an owl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The artists scraped the rock pale for a background, cut outlines with a flint edge, smudged charcoal with a finger to shade, and blew pigment around a flattened hand to leave a stencil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Overlapping heads suggest movement and depth, above all on the Panel of the Horses, where four charcoal muzzles lean from the wall beside two rhinoceroses locking horns. Bears occupied the cave far longer than people did, scooping about 300 nests into the clay floor and leaving several thousand bones, among them about 190 skulls, one set on a block of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cave has never been open to visitors, only researchers, so most people meet it through Werner Herzog's 2010 film <i>Cave of Forgotten Dreams</i> or the full-size replica opened nearby in 2015.","citation":"","answerText":"Chauvet Cave","questions":["Radiocarbon dates on its charcoal drawings, some as old as 37,000 years, made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> far older than experts expected and upset the accepted sequence of Paleolithic styles.","Closed to all but researchers, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the public mainly through Werner Herzog's 2010 film <i>Cave of Forgotten Dreams</i> and the full-size replica opened nearby in 2015."],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","france"],"sources":["Anita Quiles, Hélène Valladas, Hervé Bocherens et al., \"A high-precision chronological model for the decorated Upper Paleolithic cave of Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, Ardèche, France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–4675, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1523158113. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Discovery,\" <i>La grotte Chauvet-Pont d'Arc</i>, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/chauvet/en/discovery-3. [Open access]","Benjamin Sadier, Jean-Jacques Delannoy, Lucilla Benedetti, et al., \"Further constraints on the Chauvet cave artwork elaboration,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 21 (2012): 8002–8006, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1118593109. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Bear hollows,\" <i>La grotte Chauvet-Pont d'Arc</i>, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/chauvet/en/bear-hollows. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"Aurignacian Cave Art,\" <i>La grotte Chauvet-Pont d'Arc</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/chauvet/en/aurignacian-cave-art. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Paintings_from_the_Chauvet_cave_%28museum_replica%29.jpg","title":"Chauvet Cave","desc":"Replica of the painting from the Chauvet cave, in the Anthropos museum, Brno. The original art is approximately 31,000 years old, probably Aurignacien. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paintings_from_the_Chauvet_cave_(museum_replica).jpg","alt":"Paintings from the Chauvet cave (museum replica)"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Chauvet Cave","at":[4.4142,44.3875]},"why":[{"q":"Why is so much more than the paintings preserved at Chauvet?","a":"Because a rockfall closed the original entrance some 20,000 years ago, sealing the interior so completely that hearths, torch smears and the footprints of animals and of a child survived on the floor."},{"q":"Why did the radiocarbon dates upset the accepted sequence?","a":"Because they place accomplished figurative art at about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago, far earlier than the styles then thought to belong there — and a minority still argue from style and stratigraphy that it is thousands of years younger."},{"q":"Why did bears leave more of a mark on the cave than people?","a":"Because they occupied it far longer, scooping about 300 nests into the clay floor and leaving several thousand bones, among them about 190 skulls, one of them set on a block of stone."}]},
{"id":"wh-077","num":"77","category":"World · History","question":"Painted in the Dordogne some 21,000 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closed to visitors in 1963 after green algal films spread across walls that had been safe for twenty millennia.","answer":"Lascaux","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 21,500 – 21,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lascaux</b> is a cave near Montignac in the Dordogne, in south-western France, whose density and confidence of drawing earned it the nickname Sistine Chapel of prehistory. Four boys aged 15 to 17 found it on 12 September 1940 — Marcel Ravidat, Georges Agniel, Simon Coencas and Jacques Marsal — and two of them later worked there as guides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cave is traditionally divided into seven sectors, among them the Hall of the Bulls, the Axial Gallery, the Nave and the Shaft, and its floor drops 19 metres (62 feet) from the entrance to the foot of the Shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 600 animals are painted or drawn on the walls, dominated by horses, with stags and aurochs equal behind them and then ibex and bison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is one human being in the whole cave, in the Shaft Scene, where a bird-headed figure lies beside a wounded bison in one of the very few narrative images Palaeolithic art attempts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Radiocarbon dates measured on reindeer bone from the main sectors put the occupations between about 21,500 and 21,000 years ago, at the turn from the Badegoulian to the Magdalenian and older than the 17,000 years long quoted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those reindeer are part of the puzzle, since the animal the dating rests on is one the painters left off the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Working in the dark meant carrying fire, and over a hundred lamps have been found, among them a polished red sandstone bowl with a handle and an engraved decoration, from the foot of the Shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Figures set where wall meets ceiling raise the question of how they were reached, and André Glory reported encrusted sockets for interlocked beams along the Axial Gallery, though the cave's record warns against generalising from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tourism then ruined the air: green algal films spread in the 1960s, the cave closed in 1963, and a fungus raised white stains in 2001 and black ones later that year, so visitors go to the facsimiles Lascaux II and Lascaux IV, of 1983 and 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lascaux","questions":["Discovered on 12 September 1940 by four boys aged 15 to 17, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds some 600 painted and drawn animals, among which the horse far outnumbers every other.","The one human being in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a bird-headed figure in the Shaft lying beside a wounded bison, in one of Paleolithic art's rare scenes."],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","france"],"sources":["Ministère de la Culture, “The Four Discoverers,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/four-discoverers. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “The Lascaux Cave,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lascaux-cave. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Themes,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/themes-0. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Dating the Figures at Lascaux,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/dating-figures-lascaux. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Lighting,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lighting. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Access to the Walls,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/access-walls. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Lascaux II and III,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lascaux-ii-and-iii. [Open access]","Ministère de la Culture, “Lascaux IV,” <i>La grotte de Lascaux</i>, accessed 1 August 2026, https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/lascaux/en/lascaux-iv. [Open access]","Zélia Bontemps et al., “Microbial Diversity and Secondary Metabolism Potential in Relation to Dark Alterations in Paleolithic Lascaux Cave,” <i>npj Biofilms and Microbiomes</i> 10 (2024): 121, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41522-024-00589-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Lascaux-IV_01.jpg/1920px-Lascaux-IV_01.jpg","title":"Lascaux","desc":"Lascaux 4, Montignac, Dordogne, France. Pictures takes in the part called atelier. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lascaux-IV_01.jpg","alt":"Lascaux-IV 01"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Lascaux","at":[1.17,45.0536]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the reindeer at Lascaux part of the puzzle?","a":"Because the radiocarbon dates rest on reindeer bone, and reindeer are the one animal the painters left off the walls — so the dating depends on a species the art does not depict."},{"q":"Why is the Shaft Scene unusual for Palaeolithic art?","a":"Because it holds the only human being in the whole cave, a bird-headed figure lying beside a wounded bison, in one of the very few narrative images Palaeolithic art attempts."},{"q":"Why can nobody visit the cave any more?","a":"Because tourism ruined the air: green algal films spread in the 1960s, the cave closed in 1963, and a fungus raised white stains in 2001 and black ones later that year. Visitors go to the facsimiles instead."}]},
{"id":"wh-078","num":"78","category":"World · History","question":"Dismissed as a forgery for over twenty years after 1879, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Cantabria holds a crowded ceiling of life-sized bison whose shoulders and flanks are the rock's own bulges.","answer":"Cave of Altamira","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 36,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Entered again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1985</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cave of Altamira</b> lies on a hillside above Santillana del Mar in Cantabria, in northern Spain, and its painted ceiling was the first Paleolithic art the world had to argue about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A local man, Modesto Cubillas, came upon the blocked entrance around 1868 while out hunting and told the landowner Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola, who first looked inside in 1875. Sautuola went back in 1879 with his young daughter María, and it was she, small enough to look up in comfort, who noticed the animals crowding the low ceiling. He published the find in 1880 in a short pamphlet arguing that it was Paleolithic, at which the leading prehistorians called it a fraud and pointed to a painter who had been staying with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sautuola died discredited in 1888, and only after similar caves turned up in France did Émile Cartailhac recant, in an 1902 article he titled <i>Mea culpa d'un sceptique</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Polychrome Hall carries about 25 large figures in red, black and violet, mostly bison between 125 and 170 centimetres (4 ft 1 in and 5 ft 7 in) long, with a hind of two metres (6 ft 7 in) among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Volume came from the rock itself: painters chose swellings in the limestone for a bison's shoulder and flank, then engraved and outlined over ochre, charcoal and iron oxide. A rockfall later sealed the cave mouth, and uranium-series dates on the calcite crusts run from a red sign at least 36,000 years old to Magdalenian work near the end, spanning more than 20,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Breath and lamps did more damage than the intervening millennia, so Spain closed the cave in 1977, let in 8,500 people a year from 1982, then shut it again in 2002 over green mould.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A full-size replica opened at the museum next door in 2001, and from 2014 a conservation study drew five visitors a week by lot to see the original ceiling in protective suits.","citation":"","answerText":"Cave of Altamira","questions":["Found blocked around 1868 by a hunter and reopened by Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried a ceiling his young daughter María first noticed while looking up.","Damaged more by breath and lamps than by the millennia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was closed in 1977 and again in 2002, and a full-size replica opened next door in 2001."],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","spain"],"sources":["M. García-Díez, D. L. Hoffmann, J. Zilhão, et al., \"Uranium series dating reveals a long sequence of rock art at Altamira Cave (Santillana del Mar, Cantabria),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 11 (2013): 4098–4106, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jas.2013.05.011. [Open access]","Émile Cartailhac, \"Les cavernes ornées de dessins. La grotte d'Altamira, Espagne. Mea culpa d'un sceptique,\" <i>L'Anthropologie</i> 13 (1902): 348–354, https://archive.org/details/Cartailhac1902. [Open access]","Oscar Moro Abadía, \"The reception of Palaeolithic art at the turn of the twentieth century: between archaeology and art history,\" <i>Journal of Art Historiography</i> 12 (June 2015), https://arthistoriography.wordpress.com/12-jun-2015/. [Open access]","Vicente Bayarri, Alfredo Prada, and Francisco García, \"A Multimodal Research Approach to Assessing the Karst Structural Conditions of the Ceiling of a Cave with Palaeolithic Cave Art Paintings: Polychrome Hall at Altamira Cave (Spain),\" <i>Sensors</i> 23, no. 22 (2023): 9153, https://doi.org/10.3390/s23229153. [Open access]","Alfredo Prada and Vicente Bayarri, \"Methodology for the Monitoring and Control of the Alterations Related to Biodeterioration and Physical-Chemical Processes Produced on the Paintings on the Ceiling of the Polychrome Hall at Altamira,\" <i>Conservation</i> 4, no. 4 (2024): 703–30, https://doi.org/10.3390/conservation4040042. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/Altamira%2C_bison.jpg","title":"Cave of Altamira","desc":"A modern interpretation of the bison from the Altamira cave ceiling, one of the most famous paintings from there. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altamira,_bison.jpg","alt":"Altamira, bison"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Cave of Altamira","at":[-4.1203,43.3825]},"why":[{"q":"Why did a child notice the ceiling when the adults had not?","a":"Because María was small enough to look up in comfort. Her father had first entered in 1875 and gone back in 1879; it was she who saw the animals crowding the low ceiling."},{"q":"Why did Sautuola die discredited?","a":"Because when he published the find in 1880 as Paleolithic, the leading prehistorians called it a fraud and pointed to a painter who had been staying with him. Cartailhac recanted only in 1902, after similar caves turned up in France."},{"q":"Why do the bison seem to swell out of the wall?","a":"Because the volume came from the rock itself: painters chose swellings in the limestone for a bison's shoulder and flank, then engraved and outlined over ochre, charcoal and iron oxide."}]},
{"id":"wh-079","num":"79","category":"World · History","question":"Pecked, incised or abraded into stone rather than painted onto it, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in the open air, as at Murujuga in Australia or the Côa Valley in Portugal.","answer":"petroglyph","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Côa Valley</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 30,000 – 12,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>petroglyph</b> is an image cut into rock, made by taking stone away from the surface rather than adding pigment to it, and that difference gives the form its extraordinary durability. The commonest technique in the older phases was pecking, striking the surface again and again with a hard hammerstone to leave a broad line of fresh, pale stone. Engravers also incised fine grooves with a sharp point and abraded whole areas smooth, and many panels carry all three treatments on one surface. In arid country the effect depends on desert varnish, a thin dark skin of clay and manganese that builds slowly on exposed rock, so a new cut reads as a bright line against a near-black ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That contrast fades as varnish reforms inside the cut, which is why the most weathered images on a panel are usually its oldest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Dating them absolutely is genuinely hard, because varnish does not thicken at a steady rate and no laboratory method works across every rock type, so a credible age rests on several converging arguments rather than one measurement. The open-air Palaeolithic engravings of the Côa Valley in northern Portugal came to light during survey work for a dam that would have drowned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologists and journalists campaigned under the slogan that petroglyphs cannot swim, a change of government cancelled the dam in 1995, and UNESCO listed the valley three years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Murujuga, on the Burrup Peninsula in Western Australia, holds the densest concentration known anywhere, and its Traditional Owners won World Heritage listing for it in 2025 after decades of campaigning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Industrial plants stand inside that rock-art landscape, and whether their emissions are damaging the engravings is the sharpest question hanging over the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"petroglyph","questions":["In arid country a fresh <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows as a bright line against the dark skin of desert varnish, and as that varnish reforms the oldest images slowly fade back.","Campaigners saved the Côa Valley engravings from a dam under the slogan that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot swim, and Portugal cancelled the project in 1995 before UNESCO listed the valley."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["Usha F. Lingappa, Chris M. Yeager, Ajay Sharma, et al., \"An ecophysiological explanation for manganese enrichment in rock varnish,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 25 (2021): e2025188118, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2025188118. [Open access]","Thierry Aubry and Jorge Davide Sampaio, \"Fariseu: New Chronological Evidence for Open-air Palaeolithic Art in the Côa Valley (Portugal),\" <i>Antiquity</i> 81, no. 312 (2007), https://antiquity.ac.uk/ProjGall/aubry316/. [Open access]","K. A. Evans et al., \"Impacts of Industrial Emissions on Rock Art at Murujuga, Western Australia,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 13805, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-44180-6. [Open access]","Fundação Côa Parque, \"The Côa Battle: World Heritage,\" accessed 1 August 2026, https://arte-coa.pt/en/the-coa-battle/. [Open access]","Jolam T. Neumann et al., \"Artificial Weathering of Rock Types Bearing Petroglyphs from Murujuga, Western Australia,\" <i>Heritage Science</i> 10 (2022): 77, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-022-00706-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Negev_camel_petroglyph.jpg/1920px-Negev_camel_petroglyph.jpg","title":"petroglyph","desc":"Petroglyph of a camel; Negev, southern Israel. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Negev_camel_petroglyph.jpg","alt":"Negev camel petroglyph"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is a petroglyph so much more durable than a painting?","a":"Because it is made by taking stone away from the surface rather than adding pigment to it, so there is no layer to flake, fade or wash off — the image is the rock."},{"q":"Why does a fresh cut show up at all in arid country?","a":"Because of desert varnish, a thin dark skin of clay and manganese that builds slowly on exposed rock, so a new cut reads as a bright line against a near-black ground."},{"q":"Why is dating petroglyphs so hard?","a":"Because varnish does not thicken at a steady rate and no laboratory method works across every rock type, so a credible age rests on several converging arguments rather than one measurement."}]},
{"id":"wh-081","num":"81","category":"World · History","question":"By adding a forearm's length of leverage to the throw, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> launched a light spear far faster and further than any bare arm could manage.","answer":"spear-thrower","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest hooks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>spear-thrower</b> is a rod or board with a hook or spur at one end, which engages the butt of a light spear and works as an extension of the thrower's arm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hunter grips it near the far end, lays the spear along it, and whips the whole assembly forward, so the hook travels through a far longer arc than the hand alone could describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That extra leverage turns into speed at the tip, and controlled tests give darts launched this way roughly two-thirds more velocity than javelins thrown by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gain in force is enough to drive a point through the hide of large game from a distance that keeps the hunter clear of hooves and horns. One study also found that it evens out the gap between men's and women's throwing speeds, which hand-throwing does not, so the weapon widens who can hunt effectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The device is far older than the names we know it by, which come from peoples who were still using it when Europeans arrived: atlatl from Nahuatl in Mexico, woomera from Australia. In Europe the oldest definite hooks are Magdalenian, from about 20,000 years ago, and the reindeer-antler piece from Combe Saunière in south-west France, often called the earliest, comes from a layer whose dates are disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Magdalenian carvers turned these hooks into sculpture, and the best known, from the cave of Mas d'Azil in the Pyrenees, shows a young animal turning to look at two birds perched on something emerging from its body, traditionally read as a dropping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bow displaced it across most of the world, being easier to aim and needing a far lighter projectile, but it held on in Australia and among Arctic hunters, who throw from a kayak where a bow would be unwieldy. It was still a weapon of war in Mexico when the Spanish arrived, which is why the Nahuatl word is the one that stuck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"spear-thrower","questions":["Known as the atlatl to the Nahuatl of Mexico and the woomera in Australia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was still in use by both peoples when Europeans first arrived.","The oldest securely dated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a reindeer-antler hook from Combe Saunière in south-west France, made about 17,500 years ago in the last stretch of the Ice Age."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Michelle R. Bebber, Briggs Buchanan, Metin I. Eren, Robert S. Walker, and Dexter Zirkle, \"Atlatl use equalizes female and male projectile weapon velocity,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 13349, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10432391/. [Open access]","Pierre Cattelain and Jean-Marc Pétillon, \"« Type 2a », the earliest Palaeolithic spear-thrower: a new Middle Magdalenian object from Isturitz (Pyrénées-Atlantiques, France) and its implications,\" <i>PALEO</i> 26 (2015): 17–32, https://doi.org/10.4000/paleo.3025. [Open access]","Pierre Cattelain, \"Un crochet de propulseur solutréen de la grotte de Combe-Saunière 1 (Dordogne),\" <i>Bulletin de la Société préhistorique française</i> 86, no. 7 (1989): 213–216, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bspf_0249-7638_1989_num_86_7_9386. [Open access]","Justin Coppe, Noora Taipale, and Veerle Rots, \"Terminal ballistic analysis of impact fractures reveals the use of spearthrower 31 ky ago at Maisières-Canal, Belgium,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 18305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600151/. [Open access]","Paule Steiner, \"Un petit poucet magdalénien ?,\" <i>Cahiers de littérature orale</i> 67–68 (2010): 85–94, https://doi.org/10.4000/clo.904. [Open access]","Stephanie Wood, ed., <i>Online Nahuatl Dictionary</i>, s.v. \"atlatl\" (Eugene, OR: Wired Humanities Projects, University of Oregon, 2000–), https://nahuatl.wired-humanities.org/content/atlatl. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Silver_Atlatl_Spear_Thrower_MET_VS1987_394_244.jpeg/1920px-Silver_Atlatl_Spear_Thrower_MET_VS1987_394_244.jpeg","title":"spear-thrower","desc":"Chimú; Spear thrower; Metal-Implements. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_Atlatl_Spear_Thrower_MET_VS1987_394_244.jpeg","alt":"Silver Atlatl Spear Thrower 394 244"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does a spear-thrower add so much force?","a":"Because the hook travels through a far longer arc than the hand alone could describe, working as an extension of the arm. Controlled tests give darts launched this way roughly two-thirds more velocity than javelins thrown by hand."},{"q":"Why does the weapon widen who can hunt effectively?","a":"Because one study found it evens out the gap between men's and women's throwing speeds, which hand-throwing does not — the leverage does work the arm would otherwise have to do."},{"q":"Why did the bow not displace it everywhere?","a":"Because it held on in Australia and among Arctic hunters, who throw from a kayak where a bow would be unwieldy; it was still a weapon of war in Mexico when the Spanish arrived."}]},
{"id":"wh-082","num":"82","category":"World · History","question":"Storing the archer's slow effort in a bent stave and releasing it in an instant, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let one hunter shoot from cover, kneeling, and alone.","answer":"bow and arrow","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest evidence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 64,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Sibudu Cave, South Africa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest bows</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>bow and arrow</b> is a system for storing muscular effort and letting it go in an instant, since the archer bends a springy stave, the wood holds the energy, and releasing the string drives much of it into a light shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is a different trick from throwing, where the speed of the projectile can never exceed what the arm itself can generate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because the work is done slowly and stored, a bow can be drawn kneeling, from behind cover, or held at full draw while the target walks into range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Arrows are lighter than the darts a spear-thrower needs, so a hunter carries a dozen or more and can shoot several in the time it takes to retrieve one dart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest claimed evidence comes from Sibudu Cave in South Africa, where stone segments the size of arrowheads are about 64,000 years old and a bone point close to 62,000, though the excavators call the reading tentative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br>  Outside Africa the earliest clear case was long Fa-Hien Lena in Sri Lanka, where bone arrow points made between about 48,000 and 34,000 years ago show people hunting in dense rainforest, until impact damage at Grotte Mandrin in France pushed it back to 54,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The weapon itself almost never survives: wood and sinew rot away unless they lie waterlogged, frozen or desert-dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest known arrow shafts were pine, found with thousands of reindeer bones at Stellmoor near Hamburg and roughly 11,500 years old; they were destroyed in an air raid in 1944, though a few fragments turned up among the excavator's effects in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest complete bows are the elm staves from Holmegaard in Denmark, roughly 10,000 to 8,500 years old and cut, like every prehistoric European bow, from a single stave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Everything since refines the same principle, from Ötzi's unfinished yew stave to composite bows of horn and sinew, and the weapon remade hunting and war by letting one person kill at a distance, from cover and alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"bow and arrow","questions":["The oldest evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from Sibudu Cave in South Africa, where stone segments the size of arrowheads, some scarred by impact, are about 64,000 years old.","Because arrows weigh far less than the darts a spear-thrower needs, a hunter with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could carry a dozen and loose several while retrieving a single dart."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Michelle C. Langley, Noel Amano, Oshan Wedage, et al., \"Bows and arrows and complex symbolic displays 48,000 years ago in the South Asian tropics,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 24 (2020): eaba3831, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7292635/. [Open access]","John Meadows, Carl Heron, Matthias Hüls, Bente Philippsen, and Mara-Julia Weber, \"Dating the lost arrow shafts from Stellmoor (Schleswig-Holstein, Germany),\" <i>Quartär</i> 65 (2018): 105–114, https://quartaer.obermaier-gesellschaft.de/pdfs/2018/2018_05_meadows.pdf. [Open access]","Jürgen Junkmanns, Johanna Klügl, Werner Schoch, Giovanna Di Pietro, and Albert Hafner, \"Neolithic and Bronze Age archery equipment from Alpine ice-patches: a review on components, construction techniques and functionality,\" <i>Journal of Neolithic Archaeology</i> 21 (2019): 283–314, https://doi.org/10.12766/jna.2019.10. [Open access]","B. W. Kooi and C. A. Bergman, \"An approach to the study of ancient archery using mathematical modelling,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 71, no. 271 (1997): 124–134, https://www.bio.vu.nl/thb/users/kooi/kobe97.pdf. [Open access]","John J. Shea and Matthew L. Sisk, \"Complex projectile technology and <i>Homo sapiens</i> dispersal into western Eurasia,\" <i>PaleoAnthropology</i> 2010 (2010): 100–122, https://paleoanthro.net/media/journal/content/PA20100100.pdf. [Open access]","Matthew L. Sisk and John J. Shea, \"The African origin of complex projectile technology: an analysis using tip cross-sectional area and perimeter,\" <i>International Journal of Evolutionary Biology</i> 2011 (2011): 968012, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3132613/. [Open access]","Laure Metz, Jason E. Lewis, and Ludovic Slimak, \"Bow-and-arrow, technology of the first modern humans in Europe 54,000 years ago at Mandrin, France,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 8 (2023): eadd4675, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946345/. [Open access]","Lucinda Backwell, Francesco d'Errico, and Lyn Wadley, \"Middle Stone Age bone tools from the Howiesons Poort layers, Sibudu Cave, South Africa,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 35, no. 6 (2008): 1566–1580, https://in-africa.org/wp-content/uploads/2012/12/Backwell-et-al-2008-JAS-MSA-bone-tools-from-HP-layers-Sibudu.pdf. [Open access]","Lucinda Backwell, Justin Bradfield, Kristian J. Carlson, et al., \"The antiquity of bow-and-arrow technology: evidence from Middle Stone Age layers at Sibudu Cave,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 362 (2018): 289–303, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2018.11. [Paywalled]","Marlize Lombard and Laurel Phillipson, \"Indications of bow and stone-tipped arrow use 64 000 years ago in KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 84, no. 325 (2010): 635–648, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00100134. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Indian_with_bow_%26_arrow_001.jpg","title":"bow and arrow","desc":"An archer drawing a bow, from the Massengill Postcard Collection. Public domain (No restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_with_bow_%26_arrow_001.jpg","alt":"Indian with bow & arrow 001"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why is a bow a different trick from throwing?","a":"Because the archer bends a springy stave and the wood stores the effort, releasing it in an instant — where in throwing the speed of the projectile can never exceed what the arm itself can generate."},{"q":"Why does storing the work change how a hunter can shoot?","a":"Because it is done slowly, so a bow can be drawn kneeling, from behind cover, or held at full draw while the target walks into range. Arrows are light too, so a hunter carries a dozen and shoots several in the time one dart takes to retrieve."},{"q":"Why does the weapon itself almost never survive?","a":"Because wood and sinew rot away unless they lie waterlogged, frozen or desert-dry. The oldest known shafts, from Stellmoor, were destroyed in an air raid in 1944."}]},
{"id":"wh-083","num":"83","category":"World · History","question":"Rarely more than 3 centimetres (1.2 inches) long, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not meant to be used alone but glued in rows into a wooden shaft to arm an arrow or a knife.","answer":"microlith","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest bladelets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 71,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Backed pieces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 65,000 – 59,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>microlith</b> is a small retouched stone tool struck from a slender bladelet rather than shaped down from a core, and there is no agreed ceiling on its size, with published limits running from about 25 to 50 millimetres (1 to 2 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word is built from the Greek roots for 'small' and 'stone', and the smallness is the whole point, because a tool this size is useless held in the hand. It was made to sit in a groove cut along a shaft of wood, bone or antler, fixed with birch-bark tar or resin, so that several of them worked together as a single edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Knappers sort them into backed forms, blunted along one edge so the other stays keen, and geometric forms trimmed into triangles, crescents and trapezes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Many were produced by a set procedure in which a notch is cut into the side of a blade and the blade snapped across it, yielding a fragment of predictable shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The advantage of building a weapon this way is repair, since a shaft armed with a row of small pieces can be made good in the field, whereas a single large point that snaps is finished. It also stretches scarce good stone a long way, and one standard blank can serve as a barb, a tip or a cutting edge depending on how it is mounted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A bog find at Rönneholm in southern Sweden preserves the arrangement directly, with four flint microliths still held by birch-bark tar in a groove cut down a hazel arrow tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest are far older, since backed pieces dominate the Howiesons Poort of southern Africa between about 65,000 and 59,000 years ago, and bladelet making at Pinnacle Point reaches back some 71,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> After the Ice Age they became the standard kit across most of Europe, Africa and South Asia, which is why the little blade is the signature of the Mesolithic and of the African Late Stone Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"microlith","questions":["Backed examples reach back roughly 70,000 years in the Howiesons Poort of southern Africa, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is far older than the Mesolithic it came to define.","Because a shaft armed with a row of them can be mended in the field, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let hunters repair a weapon that a single snapped point would have ruined."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"sources":["Lars Larsson, Arne Sjöström, and Carl Heron, \"The Rönneholm arrow: a find of a wooden arrow-tip with microliths in the bog Rönneholms Mosse, central Scania, southern Sweden,\" <i>Lund Archaeological Review</i> 22 (2016): 7–20, https://journals.lub.lu.se/lar/article/view/21616. [Open access]","Lars Larsson and Arne Sjöström, \"Early Mesolithic flint-tipped arrows from Sweden,\" <i>Antiquity</i> Project Gallery 85, no. 330 (2011), https://antiquity.ac.uk/projgall/larsson330. [Open access]","Amy M. Way, Paloma de la Peña, Eduardo de la Peña, and Lyn Wadley, \"Howiesons Poort backed artifacts provide evidence for social connectivity across southern Africa during the Final Pleistocene,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 9227, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9184481/. [Open access]","Manuel Will and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Regional patterns of diachronic technological change in the Howiesons Poort of southern Africa,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 9 (2020): e0239195, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0239195. [Open access]","Oshan Wedage, Andrea Picin, James Blinkhorn et al., \"Microliths in the South Asian rainforest ~45–4 ka: new insights from Fa-Hien Lena Cave, Sri Lanka,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 10 (2019): e0222606, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0222606. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski, Hong Chen, Cheng Liu, et al., \"Versatile use of microliths as a technological advantage in the miniaturization of Late Pleistocene toolkits: the case study of Neve David, Israel,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 6 (2020): e0233340, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7269238/. [Open access]","Nicolò Fasser and Federica Fontana, \"Exploring the 'microburin blow': an insight into the variability of the microburin blow method for the production of Sauveterrian geometrics in the site of Mondeval de Sora (N-E, Italy),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 33, no. 2 (2026): art. 21, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10816-025-09755-z. [Open access]","Kyle S. Brown, Curtis W. Marean, Zenobia Jacobs, et al., \"An early and enduring advanced technology originating 71,000 years ago in South Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 491, no. 7425 (2012): 590–593, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature11660. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Kebaran_culture_microliths_22000-18000_BP.jpg","title":"microlith","desc":"Kebaran culture microliths 22000-18000 BP. Gary Todd. 2019-05-05. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kebaran_culture_microliths_22000-18000_BP.jpg","alt":"Kebaran culture microliths 22000-18000 BP"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the smallness of a microlith the whole point?","a":"Because a tool this size is useless held in the hand. It was made to sit in a groove cut along a shaft of wood, bone or antler, fixed with birch-bark tar or resin, so that several worked together as a single edge."},{"q":"Why is a weapon armed this way better than one with a single point?","a":"Because it can be repaired: a shaft carrying a row of small pieces can be made good in the field, whereas a single large point that snaps is finished. It also stretches scarce good stone a long way."},{"q":"Why does the Rönneholm find matter more than the microliths themselves?","a":"Because it preserves the arrangement directly — four flint microliths still held by birch-bark tar in a groove cut down a hazel arrow tip, which is what everywhere else has to be inferred."}]},
{"id":"wh-084","num":"84","category":"World · History","question":"Insulated by small ears, a thick layer of fat and outer hairs over 70 centimetres (28 inches) long, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grazed northern grasslands until its last island herds died out about 4,000 years ago.","answer":"woolly mammoth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Split off</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800,000 – 600,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mainland end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wrangel Island</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>woolly mammoth</b> was a cold-adapted elephant of the northern hemisphere, and it split from the steppe mammoth in northeastern Siberia somewhere between 800,000 and 600,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A big bull stood 2.7 to 3.4 metres (8 ft 10 in to 11 ft 2 in) at the shoulder and weighed between 5.5 and 8 tonnes (6 and 8.8 tons), which is roughly the build of an African elephant today, though the legs were shorter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything about the body fought the cold: small ears and a short tail lost less heat, and up to 8 centimetres (3 inches) of fat lay beneath the skin. Over a woolly undercoat about 2.5 centimetres (1 inch) thick grew guard hairs longer than 70 centimetres (28 inches), and a fatty hump rose over the shoulders. The trunk ended in two finger-like tips, and curving tusks reached 4.2 metres (13 ft 9 in) in the largest bulls, used for display, for fighting and for shovelling snow off the grass below. <br><br> Its range ran from Iberia across Eurasia and over the Bering land bridge deep into North America, on a dry, open grassland that has no real equivalent anywhere today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> People lived alongside it for tens of thousands of years, painting it on cave walls, carving its ivory into figurines and flutes, and building huts out of its bones. Siberian permafrost has given back whole carcasses with hair, skin and stomach contents intact, and mammoth teeth held the oldest DNA yet sequenced from any animal, one about 1.2 million years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mainland herds were gone by roughly 10,500 years ago as their grassland shrank and hunters pressed them, and genetics shows the last islanders were inbred without being doomed by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rising seas left those remnants behind, on St Paul until about 5,600 years ago and on Wrangel until about 4,000, by which time the pyramids at Giza had stood for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"woolly mammoth","questions":["Split from the steppe mammoth in Siberia perhaps 800,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried curving tusks up to 4.2 metres (13 ft 9 in), used for fighting and shovelling snow.","Preserved whole in Siberian permafrost with hair and stomach intact, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded the oldest DNA yet sequenced, from an animal about 1.2 million years old."],"tags":["animal","palaeontology","prehistory","nature"],"sources":["Tom van der Valk et al., \"Million-Year-Old DNA Sheds Light on the Genomic History of Mammoths,\" <i>Nature</i> 591, no. 7849 (2021): 265–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7116897/. [Open access]","Asier Larramendi, \"Shoulder Height, Body Mass, and Shape of Proboscideans,\" <i>Acta Palaeontologica Polonica</i> 61, no. 3 (2016): 537–74, https://doi.org/10.4202/app.00136.2014. [Open access]","Glen M. MacDonald et al., \"Pattern of Extinction of the Woolly Mammoth in Beringia,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 3 (2012): 893, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms1881. [Open access]","Marianne Dehasque et al., \"Temporal Dynamics of Woolly Mammoth Genome Erosion Prior to Extinction,\" <i>Cell</i> 187, no. 14 (2024): 3531–40, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2024.05.033. [Open access]","Russell W. Graham et al., \"Timing and Causes of Mid-Holocene Mammoth Extinction on St. Paul Island, Alaska,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 33 (2016): 9310–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4995940/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Woolly_mammoth_with_downward_spiralling_tusk.jpg/1920px-Woolly_mammoth_with_downward_spiralling_tusk.jpg","title":"woolly mammoth","desc":"Woolly mammoth from Bzianka, Poland, with one downward spiralling tusk. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woolly_mammoth_with_downward_spiralling_tusk.jpg","alt":"Woolly mammoth with downward spiralling tusk"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why did every part of the body fight the cold?","a":"Because small ears and a short tail lost less heat, up to 8 centimetres of fat lay beneath the skin, guard hairs longer than 70 centimetres grew over a woolly undercoat, and a fatty hump rose over the shoulders."},{"q":"Why has Siberia given back so much more than bones?","a":"Because permafrost preserves whole carcasses with hair, skin and stomach contents intact — and mammoth teeth held the oldest DNA yet sequenced from any animal, one about 1.2 million years old."},{"q":"Why did the last mammoths outlive the mainland herds by thousands of years?","a":"Because rising seas left those remnants behind on islands: St Paul until about 5,600 years ago and Wrangel until about 4,000, by which time the pyramids at Giza had stood for centuries."}]},
{"id":"wh-085","num":"85","category":"World · History","question":"Because it took mammoths, ground sloths and Diprotodon while sparing small animals, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is unusual among extinction events in having selected almost entirely by body size.","answer":"Quaternary extinction event","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Concentrated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50,000 – 11,700 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Australia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 45,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Americas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 – 11,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Quaternary extinction event</b> is the loss of most of the world's very large land animals, concentrated in the last 50,000 years and continuing in places into recorded history. What marks it out is that it selected by size: almost everything over about 44 kilograms (97 pounds) was at risk, while small animals came through largely untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gone are the mammoths and mastodons, the woolly rhinoceros, the giant ground sloths and glyptodonts, the cave lion, the giant short-faced bear and Diprotodon, the largest marsupial known. The damage was very unevenly spread: Africa lost about a fifth of its large genera, Eurasia around a third, North America close to three-quarters, South America more than four-fifths and Australia nearly nine in ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That gradient tracks how long each continent's animals had lived alongside hominins, which is the most striking pattern in the whole event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two explanations have been argued over for decades: hunting and other pressure from arriving modern humans, and the violent climate swings at the end of the last glacial period. Dung-fungus spores, which fall away as large herbivores disappear, drop before the vegetation changes in records from the eastern United States and northern Australia, and that order fits animals going first with habitat following.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against it, some analyses of North American records find the declines match falling temperatures better than they match human numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A 2025 review of 360 papers found the field still divided, and divided along disciplinary lines, so anyone who tells you the question is settled is ahead of the evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What is not in doubt is the aftermath: fruits left with nothing large enough to carry their seeds, nutrients no longer walked inland from the coasts, and the mammoth steppe giving way to tundra and forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Quaternary extinction event","questions":["Sparing Africa but taking nearly nine in ten of Australia's large genera, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hit each continent in step with how long its animals had known hominins.","Argued for decades between human hunting and violent climate swings, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remains unsettled, a 2025 review of 360 papers finding the field still divided."],"tags":["event","palaeontology","prehistory","nature"],"sources":["Paul L. Koch and Anthony D. Barnosky, \"Late Quaternary Extinctions: State of the Debate,\" <i>Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics</i> 37 (2006): 215–50, https://ib.berkeley.edu/labs/barnosky/Koch%20%20and%20Barnosky%202006.pdf. [Open access]","Christopher Sandom et al., \"Global Late Quaternary Megafauna Extinctions Linked to Humans, Not Climate Change,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 281, no. 1787 (2014): 20133254, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2013.3254. [Open access]","Jacquelyn L. Gill et al., \"Pleistocene Megafaunal Collapse, Novel Plant Communities, and Enhanced Fire Regimes in North America,\" <i>Science</i> 326, no. 5956 (2009): 1100–1103, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1179504. [Paywalled]","Susan Rule et al., \"The Aftermath of Megafaunal Extinction: Ecosystem Transformation in Pleistocene Australia,\" <i>Science</i> 335, no. 6075 (2012): 1483–86, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1214261. [Paywalled]","Jack M. Broughton and Elic M. Weitzel, \"Population Reconstructions for Humans and Megafauna Suggest Mixed Causes for North American Pleistocene Extinctions,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 5441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-07897-1. [Open access]","Mathew Stewart et al., \"The State of the Late Quaternary Megafauna Extinction Debate: A Systematic Review and Analysis,\" <i>Frontiers in Mammal Science</i> 4 (2025): 1678231, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmamm.2025.1678231. [Open access]","Paulo R. Guimarães Jr., Mauro Galetti, and Pedro Jordano, \"Seed Dispersal Anachronisms: Rethinking the Fruits Extinct Megafauna Ate,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 3, no. 2 (2008): e1745, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0001745. [Open access]","Christopher E. Doughty et al., \"Global Nutrient Transport in a World of Giants,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 4 (2016): 868–73, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1502549112. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg/1920px-Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg","title":"Quaternary extinction event","desc":"Woolly mammoths were driven to extinction by climate change and human impacts. The image depicts a late Pleistocene landscape in northern Spain with woolly mammoths (Mammuthus. primigenius ), equids , a woolly rhinoceros ( Coelodonta antiquitatis ), and European cave lions ( Panthera leo spelaea )… Mauricio Antón, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ice_age_fauna_of_northern_Spain_-_Mauricio_Ant%C3%B3n.jpg","alt":"Ice age fauna of northern Spain - Mauricio Antón"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the extinction described as having selected by size?","a":"Because almost everything over about 44 kilograms was at risk while small animals came through largely untouched — mammoths, woolly rhinoceros, giant ground sloths, the cave lion and Diprotodon all gone."},{"q":"Why is the continent-by-continent gradient the most striking pattern?","a":"Because it tracks how long each continent's animals had lived alongside hominins: Africa lost about a fifth of its large genera, Eurasia a third, North America near three-quarters, South America over four-fifths and Australia nearly nine in ten."},{"q":"Why is anyone who calls the question settled ahead of the evidence?","a":"Because a 2025 review of 360 papers found the field still divided, and divided along disciplinary lines — dung-fungus spores fitting animals going first, and some North American records fitting falling temperatures better."}]},
{"id":"wh-087","num":"87","category":"World · History","question":"Buried in two caves in what is now Israel, one child with deer antlers on the chest, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> record an early departure from Africa that left almost no trace in anyone alive today.","answer":"Skhul and Qafzeh hominins","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 120,000 – 90,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Skhul dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1931–1932, by Theodore McCown</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Skhul and Qafzeh hominins</b> are the human remains from two caves in what is now Israel, one on Mount Carmel and one above Nazareth, and they are among the earliest of our species known outside Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Skhul was dug in 1931 and 1932 by Theodore McCown, part of Dorothy Garrod's work on Carmel, Qafzeh by René Neuville from 1933 and Bernard Vandermeersch from 1965, the second cave alone yielding 27 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their skulls are a mixture: the braincase is globular with a high forehead like ours, while Skhul 5 has an unbroken bar of bone above the eyes and the most jutting face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both were dated by heating flints from the deposits and measuring the light released, Qafzeh in 1988 and Skhul in 1993, putting them at 120,000 to 90,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was far older than assumed, since the caves had been read as a late stage in a tidy local sequence. <br><br> The dates turned that sequence around, because the Neanderthals buried at Kebara and Amud, in the same corridor, proved far younger, at 70,000 to 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two populations were moving in and out of the same corridor rather than one replacing the other, and the Levant looks less like a staircase than a doorway used repeatedly from both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Several of the dead were buried on purpose, among the earliest deliberate burials known for our species: a child of 12 or 13 at Qafzeh lay on its back in a pit, arms bent, deer antlers on the chest, and a man at Skhul held a boar's jaw in his arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sea shells carried inland, holed by nature but worn from stringing and rubbed with colour, turn up at both caves with red ochre, among the oldest evidence anywhere for ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Genetics has since given them a melancholy role, because people outside Africa today descend overwhelmingly from a later dispersal around 50,000 years ago, so these families look like an earlier attempt that ended without issue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Skhul and Qafzeh hominins","questions":["Dated by heating buried flints to roughly 120,000 to 90,000 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proved far older than a tidy local sequence had assumed.","Carrying a high rounded braincase like ours yet an unbroken bar of bone above the eyes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> look to early students like a puzzling mixture of two kinds of human."],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","prehistory","hominin","israel"],"sources":["Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Hélène Valladas et al., “Thermoluminescence Dating of Mousterian ‘Proto-Cro-Magnon’ Remains from Israel and the Origin of Modern Man,” <i>Nature</i> 331, no. 6157 (1988): 614–16, https://doi.org/10.1038/331614a0. [Paywalled]","Rachel Sarig et al., “Malocclusion in Early Anatomically Modern Human: A Reflection on the Etiology of Modern Dental Misalignment,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 11 (2013): e80771, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3835570/. [Open access]","Hélène Coqueugniot et al., “Earliest Cranio-Encephalic Trauma from the Levantine Middle Palaeolithic: 3D Reappraisal of the Qafzeh 11 Skull,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 7 (2014): e102822, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0102822. [Open access]","Daniella E. Bar-Yosef Mayer et al., “On Holes and Strings: Earliest Displays of Human Adornment in the Middle Palaeolithic,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 7 (2020): e0234924, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0234924. [Open access]","Yossi Zaidner et al., “Evidence from Tinshemet Cave in Israel Suggests Behavioural Uniformity across <i>Homo</i> Groups in the Levantine Mid-Middle Palaeolithic circa 130,000–80,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 9, no. 5 (2025): 886–901, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12106087/. [Open access]","Rasmus Nielsen et al., “Tracing the Peopling of the World through Genomics,” <i>Nature</i> 541, no. 7637 (2017): 302–10, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5772775/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Qafzeh_11_child_skull_cast.jpg/1920px-Qafzeh_11_child_skull_cast.jpg","title":"Skhul and Qafzeh hominins","desc":"A skull cast of the Qafzeh 11 child. Natural History Museum, London. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qafzeh_11_child_skull_cast.jpg","alt":"Qafzeh 11 child skull cast"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the new dates turn the local sequence around?","a":"Because the caves had been read as a late stage in a tidy sequence, and heating flints put them at 120,000 to 90,000 years ago — while the Neanderthals buried at Kebara and Amud proved far younger, at 70,000 to 50,000."},{"q":"Why does the Levant look like a doorway rather than a staircase?","a":"Because two populations were moving in and out of the same corridor rather than one replacing the other, with our species there early and Neanderthals there later."},{"q":"Why does genetics give these families a melancholy role?","a":"Because people outside Africa today descend overwhelmingly from a later dispersal around 50,000 years ago, so these look like an earlier attempt that ended without issue."}]},
{"id":"wh-091","num":"91","category":"World · History","question":"Neither the first man nor the only one of his day, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the nickname for the man from whom every living man descends in an unbroken line of fathers.","answer":"Y-chromosomal Adam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 – 200,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">2015 estimate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">254,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Y-chromosomal Adam</b> is the name given to the man from whom every living man descends through an unbroken line of fathers, son after son, down to the present. The Y chromosome makes that line traceable: it passes only from father to son, and almost all of it escapes the shuffling that mixes a person's other chromosomes each generation. It therefore travels down the male line nearly intact, picking up copying errors that serve as markers, and those markers sort all living men into one branching tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Any such tree has to converge somewhere, and he is where it converges, a statement about one stretch of DNA rather than about ancestry in general. Current estimates place him in Africa somewhere between about 200,000 and 300,000 years ago, though that figure has moved further and more often than almost any number in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The largest jump came in 2013, when a sample sent to a consumer ancestry service by the family of an African-American man named Albert Perry matched no known branch of the tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Researchers at the University of Arizona traced the same rare lineage to a few Mbo men in western Cameroon, and adding this new deepest branch pushed the common ancestor back by roughly two hundred thousand years in a single paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Critics argued the new date was too old and later analyses trimmed it, and the episode is a fair reminder that a date built from living samples can be overturned by one unusual donor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nickname misleads in the same way its female counterpart does, because he was not the first man, not the only man of his time, and not everyone's father in any ordinary sense, since countless of his contemporaries have living descendants through lines that pass through daughters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He never met the woman his name is paired with either, since the two are worked out from different pieces of DNA with separate histories, and nothing requires them to be contemporaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Y-chromosomal Adam","questions":["In 2013 a sample from an African-American man named Albert Perry matched no known branch, and tracing it to Mbo men in Cameroon pushed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back roughly a hundred thousand years.","Worked out from a different stretch of DNA with its own history, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> need never have met the woman his name is paired with, nor lived in her time."],"tags":["concept","genetics","evolution","science"],"sources":["Fernando L. Mendez et al., \"An African American Paternal Lineage Adds an Extremely Ancient Root to the Human Y Chromosome Phylogenetic Tree,\" <i>American Journal of Human Genetics</i> 92, no. 3 (2013): 454–59, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3591855/. [Open access]","Monika Karmin et al., \"A Recent Bottleneck of Y Chromosome Diversity Coincides with a Global Change in Culture,\" <i>Genome Research</i> 25, no. 4 (2015): 459–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4381518/. [Open access]","Pille Hallast et al., \"The Y-Chromosome Tree Bursts into Leaf: 13,000 High-Confidence SNPs Covering the Human Y-Chromosome Phylogeny,\" <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 32, no. 3 (2015): 661–73, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msu327. [Open access]","Aaron P. Ragsdale et al., \"A Weakly Structured Stem for Human Origins in Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 617, no. 7962 (2023): 755–63, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06055-y. [Open access]","Teresa Rito et al., \"The First Modern Human Dispersals across Africa,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 11 (2013): e80031, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0080031. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/World_Map_of_Y-DNA_Haplogroups.png/1920px-World_Map_of_Y-DNA_Haplogroups.png","title":"Y-chromosomal Adam","desc":"World Map of Y-Chromosome Haplogroups - Dominant Haplogroups in Pre-Colonial Populations with Possible Migrations Routes Behance page Notes: The Y-DNA haplogroup. (s) with the highest % in that area (or is notable) Population/language/region name in which the haplogroup is the majority or the… Chakazul, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:World_Map_of_Y-DNA_Haplogroups.png","alt":"World Map of Y-DNA Haplogroups"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does the Y chromosome make a line of fathers traceable?","a":"Because it passes only from father to son, and almost all of it escapes the shuffling that mixes a person's other chromosomes each generation — so it travels down the male line nearly intact, picking up copying errors that serve as markers."},{"q":"Why did one unusual donor move the date by two hundred thousand years?","a":"Because a sample from an African-American man named Albert Perry matched no known branch of the tree. Tracing the same rare lineage to a few Mbo men in Cameroon added a new deepest branch, pushing the common ancestor back in a single paper."},{"q":"Why need he not have been a contemporary of Mitochondrial Eve?","a":"Because the two are worked out from different pieces of DNA with separate histories, and nothing requires their meeting points to fall at the same time."}]},
{"id":"wh-093","num":"93","category":"World · History","question":"Dug at the foot of the Arnhem Land escarpment, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up more than 10,000 stone artefacts dated to about 65,000 years ago, which would make it Australia's oldest human site.","answer":"Madjedbebe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 65,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">still disputed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Madjedbebe</b> is a sandstone rock shelter at the foot of the Arnhem Land escarpment in the far north of Australia, on the country of the Mirarr people and ringed by Kakadu National Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Older publications call it Malakunanja II, and it stands inside the Jabiluka mineral lease, a uranium deposit the Mirarr have long fought to keep unmined. The apron of sand under the overhang has been dug four times, in 1973, in 1989, and again in 2012 and 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recent work was led by Chris Clarkson under an agreement with the Gundjeihmi Aboriginal Corporation that gave the Mirarr control of where the trenches went and of what came out of them. Those two seasons produced more than 10,000 stone artefacts from the lowest dense band alone, and sand grains from that band were dated by optically stimulated luminescence, which measures how long a grain has lain buried in the dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The result, published in 2017, put the first occupation at about 65,000 years ago, 5,000 to 15,000 years earlier than the oldest dates from any other Australian site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same layer holds the earliest known hatchet heads with ground edges, the earliest seed-grinding stones in Australia, and quantities of ground ochre along with glittering mica, apparently used as pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charred plant remains show what was eaten there between 65,000 and 53,000 years ago, pandanus nuts and yams among them, so the shelter records a way of living rather than a single visit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The date is not settled, because the deposit is loose sand worked over by termites, and critics led by Jim Allen and James O'Connell argue that rocks and artefacts could have sunk into the older sand beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Molecular clocks have been read as pointing to settlement nearer 50,000 years ago, though the split from Eurasians is put at about 58,000 in a range wide enough to hold 65,000, which on the excavators' reckoning leaves people among the giant marsupials for over 20,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Madjedbebe","questions":["Australia's oldest ground-edge hatchet heads and its earliest seed-grinding stones came from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a rock shelter on Mirarr country whose lowest band also held ground ochre and glittering mica.","Because its loose sand is riddled by termites and later pits, critics argue heavy tools sank into older layers, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its 65,000-year date stay disputed."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","australia"],"sources":["Chris Clarkson, Zenobia Jacobs, Ben Marwick, et al., \"Human occupation of northern Australia by 65,000 years ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 547, no. 7663 (2017): 306–310, http://faculty.washington.edu/bmarwick/PDFs/Clarkson_Jacobs_Marwick_2017.pdf. [Open access]","Chris Clarkson, Richard G. Roberts, Zenobia Jacobs, et al., \"Reply to comments on Clarkson et al. (2017) 'Human occupation of northern Australia by 65,000 years ago,'\" <i>Australian Archaeology</i> 84, no. 1 (2018): 84–89, https://anthropology.washington.edu/sites/anthropology/files/documents/research/clarkson-roberts-jacobs-marwick-et-al-2018-aa-reply.pdf. [Open access]","James F. O'Connell, Jim Allen, Martin A. J. Williams, et al., \"When did <i>Homo sapiens</i> first reach Southeast Asia and Sahul?,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 34 (2018): 8482–8490, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6112744/. [Open access]","Chris Clarkson, Mike Smith, Ben Marwick, et al., \"The archaeology, chronology and stratigraphy of Madjedbebe (Malakunanja II): a site in northern Australia with early occupation,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 83 (2015): 46–64, https://faculty.washington.edu/bmarwick/PDFs/Clarkson_et_al._2015_MJB_1989.pdf. [Open access]","S. Anna Florin, Andrew S. Fairbairn, May Nango, et al., \"The first Australian plant foods at Madjedbebe, 65,000–53,000 years ago,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 924, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7026095/. [Open access]","Anna-Sapfo Malaspinas, Michael C. Westaway, Craig Muller, et al., \"A genomic history of Aboriginal Australia,\" <i>Nature</i> 538, no. 7624 (2016): 207–214, https://www.eva.mpg.de/documents/Nature/Malaspinas_Genomic_Nature_2016_2351078.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Madjedbebe","at":[132.8833,-12.5]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the shelter record a way of living rather than a single visit?","a":"Because the layer holds ground-edge hatchet heads, seed-grinding stones, quantities of ochre and mica, and charred plant remains — pandanus nuts and yams among them — showing what was eaten there between 65,000 and 53,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why is the 65,000-year date not settled?","a":"Because the deposit is loose sand worked over by termites, and critics led by Jim Allen and James O'Connell argue that rocks and artefacts could have sunk into the older sand beneath."},{"q":"Why did the Mirarr have a say in how the site was dug?","a":"Because the 2012 and 2015 seasons ran under an agreement with the Gundjeihmi Aboriginal Corporation that gave them control of where the trenches went and of what came out of them."}]},
{"id":"wh-094","num":"94","category":"World · History","question":"Burnt and crushed in one case, laid under red ochre in the other, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are two graves about 40,000 years old from a dry lake bed in New South Wales.","answer":"Lake Mungo remains","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mungo Lady 1968, Mungo Man 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1981</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lake Mungo remains</b> are two burials that weathered out of a sand dune beside a lake bed in south-western New South Wales, dry for more than 10,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The geologist Jim Bowler found the first in 1968: a woman whose body had been burnt, her bones then crushed and buried in a shallow pit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Six years later, a few hundred metres away, he came upon a man laid out full length on his back with his hands folded and red ochre scattered over him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> They are known as Mungo Lady and Mungo Man, and they lie in the country of the Mutthi Mutthi, Barkindji and Ngiyampaa peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Willandra Lakes around them, a chain of basins that held water through the last ice age and dried as it ended, became a World Heritage site in 1981. <br><br> Their age has been revised more than once, first put near 30,000 years, then claimed at 62,000 in 1999, and settled in 2003 at about 40,000 by a team that included Bowler himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That still leaves the ochre burial the oldest ritual burial known anywhere and the burnt woman the earliest burnt human remains on record, though the burning is incomplete and no pyre survives, so specialists now hedge the word 'cremation'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 2001 a study of mitochondrial DNA taken from Mungo Man reported a lineage carried by no living person, which its authors read as evidence that one modern human lineage in Australia had been replaced by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Re-sequencing in 2016 showed those readings to be contamination, five modern European sequences in the bone and a sixth in the original extracts matching the first author of the 2001 study, and the claim collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both were eventually returned to their Traditional Owners, Mungo Man in 2017, and after years of disagreement over whether to keep them accessible for study, the reburial of the Willandra ancestors was approved in 2022 and has since gone ahead.","citation":"","answerText":"Lake Mungo remains","questions":["Found weathering out of a dune in 1968, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include the earliest burnt human remains on record, a woman whose bones were crushed and buried in a shallow pit.","A 2001 study of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> claimed a DNA lineage no living person carries, until re-sequencing in 2016 exposed the readings as modern contamination and the claim collapsed."],"tags":["fossil","archaeology","prehistory","australia"],"sources":["James M. Bowler, Harvey Johnston, Jon M. Olley, et al., \"New ages for human occupation and climatic change at Lake Mungo, Australia,\" <i>Nature</i> 421, no. 6925 (2003): 837–840, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01383. [Paywalled]","Alan Thorne, Rainer Grün, Graham Mortimer, et al., \"Australia's oldest human remains: age of the Lake Mungo 3 skeleton,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 36, no. 6 (1999): 591–612, https://doi.org/10.1006/jhev.1999.0305. [Paywalled]","Gregory J. Adcock, Elizabeth S. Dennis, Simon Easteal, et al., \"Mitochondrial DNA sequences in ancient Australians: implications for modern human origins,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 98, no. 2 (2001): 537–542, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC14622/. [Open access]","Tim H. Heupink, Sankar Subramanian, Joanne L. Wright, et al., \"Ancient mtDNA sequences from the First Australians revisited,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 25 (2016): 6892–6897, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4922152/. [Open access]","Jessica I. Cerezo-Román, Elizabeth Sawchuk, Flora Schilt, et al., \"Earliest evidence for intentional cremation of human remains in Africa,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 1 (2026): eadz9554, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12757068/. [Open access]","Joanne L. Wright, Sally Wasef, Tim H. Heupink, et al., \"Ancient nuclear genomes enable repatriation of Indigenous human remains,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaau5064, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6300400/. [Open access]","Kathryn E. Fitzsimmons, Nicola Stern, Colin V. Murray-Wallace, et al., \"The Mungo mega-lake event, semi-arid Australia: non-linear descent into the last ice age, implications for human behaviour,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 6 (2015): e0127008, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0127008. [Open access]","Richard Gillespie, \"Dating the First Australians,\" <i>Radiocarbon</i> 44, no. 2 (2002): 455–472, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0033822200031830. [Open access]","Adam Brumm and Mark W. Moore, \"Symbolic revolutions and the Australian archaeological record,\" <i>Cambridge Archaeological Journal</i> 15, no. 2 (2005): 157–175, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0959774305000089. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Uomo_di_Mungo.jpg","title":"Lake Mungo remains","desc":"Uomo di Mungo. Benutzer:Schomynv de:Benutzer:Schomynv. 2011-05-19. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uomo_di_Mungo.jpg","alt":"Uomo di Mungo"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why do specialists now hedge the word cremation?","a":"Because the burning of the woman's body is incomplete and no pyre survives, so what can be said is that her bones were burnt, then crushed and buried in a shallow pit."},{"q":"Why did the 2001 DNA claim collapse?","a":"Because re-sequencing in 2016 showed the readings to be contamination: five modern European sequences in the bone and a sixth in the original extracts matching the first author of the 2001 study."},{"q":"Why does the ochre burial matter beyond its age?","a":"Because the man was laid out full length on his back with his hands folded and red ochre scattered over him, which makes it the oldest ritual burial known anywhere."}]},
{"id":"wh-096","num":"96","category":"World · History","question":"A four-year-old boy buried by the Belaya made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> matter far beyond Siberia, because his genome ties these mammoth hunters to the first Americans.","answer":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mal'ta burial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 24,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1928, by Mikhail Gerasimov</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mal'ta-Buret' culture</b> is named for two Upper Paleolithic camps on the rivers west of Lake Baikal, in south-central Siberia, far beyond the eastern edge of Gravettian Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mikhail Gerasimov began digging at Mal'ta in 1928 and came back to it, on and off, for thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The camp's floors held the remains of dwellings, and it is inside them that most of the carved ivory was found, some of it in what look like ritual corners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They carved about thirty small female figures from mammoth ivory, and these are unlike the Venus figurines further west: slender rather than heavy, and marked in a way that looks like fitted fur clothing with a hood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They also carved birds with their wings out as though in flight, and flat bone plaques cut with zigzag patterns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What has made the culture famous, though, is neither a house nor a carving but a child buried at Mal'ta. Gerasimov found a grave holding two children, one known only from a few teeth and the other a boy of about four, laid in the ground with a necklace of 120 beads, a bird pendant and an ivory plaque and dusted with ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His genome was published in 2013, and at about 24,000 years old it was then the oldest genome of a modern human that anyone had read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It held two surprises: he was related to the ancestors of western Eurasians, and not at all closely to East Asians, although he had lived in Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also shares a great deal with living Native Americans, who draw a large part of their ancestry from people like him, and that solved an old puzzle about why their genes had never looked purely East Asian, as the geography alone would lead you to expect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","questions":["Unlike the heavy Venus figures further west, the ivory women of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are slender and marked as if wearing fitted fur clothing with a hood.","Mikhail Gerasimov, who first dug the camps of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1928, found most of its carved ivory lying inside the dwellings themselves."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","russia"],"sources":["Maanasa Raghavan, Pontus Skoglund, Kelly E. Graf, et al., “Upper Palaeolithic Siberian Genome Reveals Dual Ancestry of Native Americans,” <i>Nature</i> 505, no. 7481 (2014): 87–91, https://escholarship.org/uc/item/79h679zc. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal’ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, under “The Mal’ta burial,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]","Gerhard W. Weber, Alexander Lukeneder, Mathias Harzhauser, et al., “The Microstructure and the Origin of the Venus from Willendorf,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 2926, under “Introduction,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8885675/. [Open access]","Junzo Uchiyama, J. Christopher Gillam, Alexander Savelyev, and Chao Ning, “Populations Dynamics in Northern Eurasian Forests: A Long-Term Perspective from Northeast Asia,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e16, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427466/. [Open access]","J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben A. Potter, Lasse Vinner, et al., “Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan Genome Reveals First Founding Population of Native Americans,” <i>Nature</i> 553, no. 7687 (2018): 203–7, https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/7887/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Malta_artefacts_%28reworked%29.jpg","title":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","desc":"Malta artefacts (reworked). Лапоть. 2023-02-25. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malta_artefacts_(reworked).jpg","alt":"Malta artefacts (reworked)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Mal'ta-Buret' culture","at":[103.5174,52.85],"kind":"region","area":[[102.75,52.6],[102.95,53.1],[103.6,53.3],[104.25,53.1],[104.35,52.7],[103.85,52.35],[103.1,52.4]]},"why":[{"q":"Why do the Mal'ta figures look unlike the Venus figurines further west?","a":"Because they are slender rather than heavy, and marked in a way that looks like fitted fur clothing with a hood — which is what a Siberian winter would demand."},{"q":"Why was the boy's genome such a surprise?","a":"Because he was related to the ancestors of western Eurasians and not at all closely to East Asians, although he had lived in Siberia."},{"q":"Why did that genome solve an old puzzle about Native American ancestry?","a":"Because he shares a great deal with living Native Americans, who draw a large part of their ancestry from people like him — which explains why their genes had never looked purely East Asian."}]},
{"id":"wh-097","num":"97","category":"World · History","question":"Whenever the sea fell far enough to bare the floor of the Bering Strait, Siberia and Alaska became one land called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, grazed by mammoth.","answer":"Beringia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Last land bridge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 35,700 – 11,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1937, by Eric Hultén</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Beringia</b> is the name for the land that joined Siberia to Alaska whenever the sea fell far enough to lay bare the shallow floor of the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Swedish botanist Eric Hultén coined the word in 1937, after Vitus Bering, the navigator in Russian service who sailed through the strait in 1728.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was no narrow causeway but a plain hundreds of kilometres across, so that anyone crossing had no sense of leaving one continent for another. Too little snow fell there for glaciers to form, so while ice sheets buried Canada and Scandinavia this corner of the Arctic stayed open grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That cold steppe carried mammoth, horse, bison and saiga antelope, and it is known today as the mammoth steppe, after its largest grazer, a dry grassland running west into Siberia and east into the Yukon, though the low bridge itself may have been wetter tundra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Seafloor sediments analysed in 2022 suggest the bridge appeared surprisingly late, around 35,700 years ago, while separate work on the flooding puts its final drowning nearer 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people who became Native Americans passed through that window, and their genetics carry a set of mutations that sets them apart from their nearest living relatives in Asia and turns up in indigenous populations throughout the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The standstill hypothesis explains those mutations by a long pause: a population cut off in Beringia for thousands of years, unable to go further east while ice sheets blocked the way, and spreading south only after about 16,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Not everyone accepts the word, since the founding population seems not to have sheltered in a refuge but to have spread across an immense arctic plain, which is a poor fit for a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How they travelled south is likewise unsettled, since the corridor between the two Canadian ice sheets seems to have opened too late to have been the first route, which has turned attention to boats and the Pacific coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Beringia","questions":["The Swedish botanist Eric Hultén coined <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1937, naming it for Vitus Bering, the navigator in Russian service who sailed through the strait in 1728.","The standstill hypothesis holds that the ancestors of Native Americans were penned for thousands of years in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, spreading south only after about 16,000 years ago once the ice retreated."],"tags":["place","geography","prehistory","migration"],"sources":["Jesse R. Farmer, Tamara Pico, Ona M. Underwood et al., \"The Bering Strait was flooded 10,000 years before the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 1 (2023): e2206742119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2206742119. [Open access]","Martin Jakobsson, Christof Pearce, Thomas M. Cronin, et al., \"Post-glacial flooding of the Bering Land Bridge dated to 11 cal ka BP based on new geophysical and sediment records,\" <i>Climate of the Past</i> 13 (2017): 991–1005, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-13-991-2017. [Open access]","John F. Hoffecker, Scott A. Elias, G. Richard Scott, and Dennis H. O'Rourke, \"Beringia and the peopling of the Western Hemisphere,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 290, no. 1990 (2023): 20222246, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9832545/. [Open access]","Erika Tamm, Toomas Kivisild, Maere Reidla, et al., \"Beringian standstill and spread of Native American founders,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 2, no. 9 (2007): e829, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0000829. [Open access]","Bastien Llamas, Lars Fehren-Schmitz, Guido Valverde, et al., \"Ancient mitochondrial DNA provides high-resolution time scale of the peopling of the Americas,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 4 (2016): e1501385, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4820370/. [Open access]","Kari B. Schroeder, Theodore G. Schurr, Jeffrey C. Long, et al., \"A private allele ubiquitous in the Americas,\" <i>Biology Letters</i> 3, no. 2 (2007): 218–223, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2375964/. [Open access]","C. R. Harington and Jacques Cinq-Mars, \"Radiocarbon dates on saiga antelope (<i>Saiga tatarica</i>) fossils from Yukon and the Northwest Territories,\" <i>Arctic</i> 48, no. 1 (1995): 1–7, https://doi.org/10.14430/arctic1218. [Open access]","Mikkel W. Pedersen, Anthony Ruter, Charles Schweger, et al., \"Postglacial viability and colonization in North America's ice-free corridor,\" <i>Nature</i> 537, no. 7618 (2016): 45–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature19085. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Global_sea_levels_during_the_last_Ice_Age.jpg/1920px-Global_sea_levels_during_the_last_Ice_Age.jpg","title":"Beringia","desc":"Global sea levels during the last Ice Age. National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration. 2008-03-28. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Global_sea_levels_during_the_last_Ice_Age.jpg","alt":"Global sea levels during the last Ice Age"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why would nobody crossing have felt they were leaving one continent for another?","a":"Because it was no narrow causeway but a plain hundreds of kilometres across — a grassland, not a bridge in any sense a traveller would notice."},{"q":"Why did this corner of the Arctic stay open while Canada lay under ice?","a":"Because too little snow fell there for glaciers to form, so it remained cold steppe carrying mammoth, horse, bison and saiga antelope."},{"q":"Why is the standstill hypothesis not universally accepted?","a":"Because the founding population seems not to have sheltered in a refuge but to have spread across an immense arctic plain, which is a poor fit for a standstill."}]},
{"id":"wh-098","num":"98","category":"World · History","question":"Because ice sealed the inland corridor, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> most likely ran down the Pacific shore, small groups out of Siberia spreading into two empty continents.","answer":"Settlement of the Americas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founding split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 36,000 – 25,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">South of the ice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 16,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>settlement of the Americas</b> was the last great continental expansion of our species, carrying people out of north-eastern Asia into two continents on which no human being had ever set foot. Their ancestors began separating from their East Asian relatives somewhere between roughly 36,000 and 25,000 years ago, a division still written into the DNA of indigenous American populations today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The road ran through Beringia, the cold grassland plain that joined Siberia to Alaska whenever glacial seas fell far enough to lay bare the floor of the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genetics suggests the travellers were held up for thousands of years in the north, unable to press on while the ice sheets of the Last Glacial Maximum sealed the way south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That long pause is called the standstill hypothesis, and though not everyone accepts it, by about 16,000 years ago people were certainly living far beyond the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> For most of the 20th century archaeologists held that the first arrivals were the Clovis hunters, named for their fluted stone points and dated tightly to between about 13,050 and 12,750 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That model collapsed as older sites accumulated: a camp at Cooper's Ferry in Idaho at roughly 16,000 years, preserved human faeces in the Paisley Caves of Oregon at a little over 12,000 radiocarbon years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fossil footprints at White Sands push the story much further back, to between 23,000 and 21,000 years ago, a range upheld by a second dating programme in 2023 and still being tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The corridor between the two Canadian ice sheets cannot have been the first road south, since the plants and animals a traveller needed returned to it only around 12,600 years ago, which points instead to boats and the Pacific coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Individual dates at the earliest end remain fiercely contested, but the Asian origin is not: in 2014 the genome of a Clovis-era boy buried in Montana showed his people ancestral to many indigenous Americans living today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Settlement of the Americas","questions":["Long credited to the Clovis hunters, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been pushed back by fossil footprints at White Sands dated to between 23,000 and 21,000 years ago.","A Clovis-era boy buried in Montana yielded a 2014 genome tying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Asia, his people ancestral to indigenous Americans living today."],"tags":["event","archaeology","prehistory","migration","north america"],"sources":["J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben A. Potter, Lasse Vinner, et al., \"Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan genome reveals first founding population of Native Americans,\" <i>Nature</i> 553, no. 7687 (2018): 203–207, https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/7887/. [Open access]","John F. Hoffecker, Scott A. Elias, G. Richard Scott, and Dennis H. O'Rourke, \"Beringia and the peopling of the Western Hemisphere,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 290, no. 1990 (2023): 20222246, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9832545/. [Open access]","Erika Tamm, Toomas Kivisild, Maere Reidla, et al., \"Beringian standstill and spread of Native American founders,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 2, no. 9 (2007): e829, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1952074/. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr., and David L. Carlson, \"The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Loren G. Davis, David B. Madsen, David A. Sisson, et al., \"Dating of a large tool assemblage at the Cooper's Ferry site (Idaho, USA) to ~15,785 cal yr B.P. extends the age of stemmed points in the Americas,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 51 (2022): eade1248, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9788777/. [Open access]","Lisa-Marie Shillito, Helen L. Whelton, John C. Blong, et al., \"Pre-Clovis occupation of the Americas identified by human fecal biomarkers in coprolites from Paisley Caves, Oregon,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 29 (2020): eaba6404, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7363456/. [Open access]","Matthew R. Bennett, David Bustos, Jeffrey S. Pigati, et al., \"Evidence of humans in North America during the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Science</i> 373, no. 6562 (2021): 1528–1531, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abg7586. [Paywalled]","Vance T. Holliday, Jason D. Windingstad, Jordon Bright, et al., \"Paleolake geochronology supports Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) age for human tracks at White Sands, New Mexico,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 25 (2025): eadv4951, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12175891/. [Open access]","Alia J. Lesnek, Jason P. Briner, Charlotte Lindqvist, et al., \"Deglaciation of the Pacific coastal corridor directly preceded the human colonization of the Americas,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 5 (2018): eaar5040, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5976267/. [Open access]","Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., \"The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,\" <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Footprints_of_Human_Adolescent_and_Teen_%28b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e%29.JPG/1920px-Footprints_of_Human_Adolescent_and_Teen_%28b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e%29.JPG","title":"Settlement of the Americas","desc":"12 human footprints belonging to one adolecent and possibly female teenager. The size of the set of footprints indicates that it was left by a human child. The second set of tracks were left by a possibly female teenager. Keywords: Ice Age; Footprint; Size; Adolescent. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Footprints_of_Human_Adolescent_and_Teen_(b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e).JPG","alt":"Footprints of Human Adolescent and Teen (b47488a1-0f52-4d94-9da3-b7b8fea91d0e)"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did the Clovis First model collapse?","a":"Because older sites accumulated: a camp at Cooper's Ferry in Idaho at roughly 16,000 years, human faeces in the Paisley Caves of Oregon, and footprints at White Sands pushing the story back to between 23,000 and 21,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why can the ice-free corridor not have been the first road south?","a":"Because the plants and animals a traveller needed returned to it only around 12,600 years ago — too late — which points instead to boats and the Pacific coast."},{"q":"Why is the Asian origin not in dispute even where the dates are?","a":"Because the ancestry is written into the DNA: their ancestors separated from their East Asian relatives between roughly 36,000 and 25,000 years ago, and a Clovis-era boy's genome showed his people ancestral to many indigenous Americans today."}]},
{"id":"wh-099","num":"99","category":"World · History","question":"A waterlogged bog in southern Chile preserved wood, cordage and seaweed, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the site that broke the belief that Clovis hunters were the first Americans.","answer":"Monte Verde","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Main layer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975, dug from 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Endorsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Monte Verde</b> is an archaeological site on the bank of Chinchihuapi Creek in southern Chile, a little inland from Puerto Montt and more than 10,000 kilometres (6,200 miles) from the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local people came upon bones there in the mid-1970s, and from 1977 Tom Dillehay and Mario Pino uncovered a settlement buried and sealed under waterlogged peat. That peat is why the site matters: it shut out the oxygen, so wood, cordage, knotted rushes and plant remains survived instead of rotting away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavators found the wooden frame of a long hide-covered shelter, hearths, digging sticks, chewed medicinal leaves, wild potato and seaweed carried up from the distant coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A human footprint was pressed into the clay beside it, and there were gomphothere bones, but the stone tools are mostly expedient pebble flakes and bola stones, with no fluted points at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The dating put the main layer at about 14,500 years ago, roughly 1,500 years before Clovis and at the far southern end of two continents people were supposed to have entered from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because that could not be squared with Clovis hunters arriving first, the site was resisted for twenty years and its stratigraphy repeatedly questioned. In 1997 a panel of well-known archaeologists visited the excavation and accepted in print that the main layer was about 12,500 radiocarbon years old, and the Clovis First model did not recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In March 2026 a paper in <i>Science</i> argued the opposite, that a layer of volcanic ash beneath the finds is only 11,000 years old and the deposit is thousands of years younger than claimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dillehay and others answered in May with three critiques, objecting that the new samples came from cutbank exposures away from the site rather than from the excavation itself, so the matter is now open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Monte Verde","questions":["Buried under peat on Chinchihuapi Creek, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> preserved a hide-covered shelter frame, chewed medicinal leaves and a child's footprint pressed into the clay beside it.","A 2026 paper in <i>Science</i> argued that ash beneath the finds is only 11,000 years old, making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> thousands of years younger than its excavators had long claimed."],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","chile"],"sources":["Mario Pino and Tom D. Dillehay, \"Monte Verde II: an assessment of new radiocarbon dates and their sedimentological context,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 393 (2023): 524–540, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.32. [Open access]","Tom D. Dillehay, Carlos Ocampo, José Saavedra, et al., \"New archaeological evidence for an early human presence at Monte Verde, Chile,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 11 (2015): e0141923, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0141923. [Open access]","Todd A. Surovell, César Méndez, Juan-Luis García, et al., \"A mid-Holocene age for Monte Verde challenges the timeline of human colonization of South America,\" <i>Science</i> 391, no. 6791 (2026): 1283–1288, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adw9217. [Paywalled]","Tom D. Dillehay, Mario Pino, Luis Lara, et al., \"Geomorphological and archaeological evidence at Monte Verde II, Chile supports the claim of human occupation 14,500 years ago,\" eLetter, <i>Science</i>, May 4, 2026, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2026/05/Three-Critiques.pdf. [Open access]","David J. Meltzer, J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Thomaz Pinotti, et al., \"Genetic evidence and the peopling of the Americas: reply to Surovell et al. 2026,\" eLetter, <i>Science</i>, May 4, 2026, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2026/05/Three-Critiques.pdf. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Jessi J. Halligan, Rolfe D. Mandel, et al., \"Geoarchaeological assessment of the suggested middle Holocene age for Monte Verde II, Chile,\" eLetter, <i>Science</i>, May 5, 2026, https://fundacionmonteverde.cl/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Waters-et-al.-Rebuttal-to-Surovell-et-al.-2026.pdf. [Open access]","David J. Meltzer, Donald K. Grayson, Gerardo Ardila, et al., \"On the Pleistocene antiquity of Monte Verde, southern Chile,\" <i>American Antiquity</i> 62, no. 4 (1997): 659–663, https://doi.org/10.2307/281884. [Paywalled]","Tom D. Dillehay, C. Ramírez, M. Pino, et al., \"Monte Verde: seaweed, food, medicine, and the peopling of South America,\" <i>Science</i> 320, no. 5877 (2008): 784–786, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1156533. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Monte_Verde_2012.jpg/1920px-Monte_Verde_2012.jpg","title":"Monte Verde","desc":"Vista del sitio de Monte Verde. Geología Valdivia, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Verde_2012.jpg","alt":"Monte Verde 2012"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Monte Verde","at":[-73.2044,-41.5047]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the peat matter as much as anything found in it?","a":"Because it shut out the oxygen, so wood, cordage, knotted rushes and plant remains survived instead of rotting away — a hide-covered shelter frame, digging sticks, chewed medicinal leaves and seaweed carried from the coast."},{"q":"Why was the site resisted for twenty years?","a":"Because a main layer at about 14,500 years ago, roughly 1,500 years before Clovis and at the far southern end of two continents, could not be squared with Clovis hunters arriving first."},{"q":"Why is the dating open again?","a":"Because a 2026 paper argued that a layer of volcanic ash beneath the finds is only 11,000 years old, and Dillehay and others answered that the new samples came from cutbank exposures away from the site rather than the excavation itself."}]},
{"id":"wh-100","num":"100","category":"World · History","question":"Chasing mammoth and giant bison across a thawing continent, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left fluted stone points from Alaska to Patagonia before the Archaic way of life began.","answer":"Paleo-Indians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until 8000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Clovis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,050 – 12,750 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Paleo-Indians</b> were the first peoples of the Americas, the hunters and foragers who spread through two empty continents at the close of the last Ice Age. The archaeologist Frank H. H. Roberts Jr. used the name in a 1940 survey without ever defining it, and it has since come to cover everyone in the Americas before about 8000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After that date the climate settles, the great animals are gone, and archaeologists speak instead of the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Archaic_period_(North_America)\">Archaic period</span>. Their world was colder, drier and stranger than ours, grazed by mammoth, mastodon, horse, camel and a bison half again the size of the living kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They moved in small bands and carried little, yet worked fine stone quarried hundreds of kilometres away, which is among the clearest signs of how far and how often they travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their signature is the fluted point, a spear tip with a channel flake struck from the base up each face so that it could be seated in a socket of bone or ivory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The best known is the Clovis point, named after the town of Clovis in eastern New Mexico and dated to about 13,050 to 12,750 years ago; the Folsom point that follows it on the Plains is smaller and made for bison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Folsom point found in 1927 in place among the ribs of an extinct bison settled a long argument, proving beyond doubt that people had been in the Americas while Ice Age animals still lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much of their food those animals supplied is still disputed: some sites yield plants, small game and fish, while isotopes published in 2024 from a Clovis child buried in Montana suggest mammoth made up much of his mother's diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within a few thousand years the mammoth and the giant bison were gone, whether from hunting or from the violent climate swings at the end of the Pleistocene, and their descendants became the Archaic peoples of the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Paleo-Indians","questions":["The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was used in a 1940 survey without ever being defined, and it now covers everyone in the Americas before about 8000 BCE.","A fluted Folsom point found in 1927 lodged in the ribs of an extinct bison proved the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had hunted while Ice Age animals still lived."],"tags":["people","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["Frank H. H. Roberts Jr., \"Developments in the problem of the North American Paleo-Indian,\" in <i>Essays in Historical Anthropology of North America</i>, Smithsonian Miscellaneous Collections 100 (Washington, DC: Smithsonian Institution, 1940), 51–116, https://archive.org/details/smithsonianmisce1001940smit. [Open access]","James C. Chatters, Ben A. Potter, Stuart J. Fiedel, et al., \"Mammoth featured heavily in western Clovis diet,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 49 (2024): eadr3814, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adr3814. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr., and David L. Carlson, \"The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Paul L. Koch and Anthony D. Barnosky, \"Late Quaternary extinctions: state of the debate,\" <i>Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics</i> 37 (2006): 215–250, https://ib.berkeley.edu/labs/barnosky/Koch%20%20and%20Barnosky%202006.pdf. [Open access]","Linda S. Cordell, \"The Folsom site in retrospect,\" in <i>Vermejo Park</i>, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 83–86, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.83. [Open access]","Jeff M. Martin, Jim I. Mead and Perry S. Barboza, \"Bison body size and climate change,\" <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 8, no. 9 (2018): 4564–4574, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.4019. [Open access]","Alan M. Slade, \"To haft and to hold: evidence for the hafting of Clovis fluted points,\" <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 3 (2021): 133–151, https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.3033. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Eden_points_and_Dalton_projectile_points_-_Paleo-Indian_collection_-_Robbins_Museum_-_Middleborough%2C_Massachusetts_-_DSC03772.jpg/1920px-Eden_points_and_Dalton_projectile_points_-_Paleo-Indian_collection_-_Robbins_Museum_-_Middleborough%2C_Massachusetts_-_DSC03772.jpg","title":"Paleo-Indians","desc":"Archaeological exhibit in the Robbins Museum, 17 Jackson Street, Middleborough, Massachusetts, USA. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eden_points_and_Dalton_projectile_points_-_Paleo-Indian_collection_-_Robbins_Museum_-_Middleborough,_Massachusetts_-_DSC03772.jpg","alt":"Eden points and Dalton projectile points - Paleo-Indian collection - Robbins Museum - Middleborough, Massachusetts"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does the term cover everyone before about 8000 BCE?","a":"Because Roberts used it in 1940 without ever defining it, and after that date the climate settles and the great animals are gone, so archaeologists speak instead of the Archaic period."},{"q":"Why is fine stone from far away one of the clearest signs about these people?","a":"Because they moved in small bands and carried little, yet worked stone quarried hundreds of kilometres away — which shows how far and how often they travelled."},{"q":"Why did a point found among a bison's ribs settle a long argument?","a":"Because the Folsom point found in place in 1927 proved beyond doubt that people had been in the Americas while Ice Age animals still lived."}]},
{"id":"wh-101","num":"101","category":"World · History","question":"Every one of its points carries a channel flake struck from the base, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread that single design across a continent in barely 300 years.","answer":"Clovis culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Type site found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Clovis</b> is a North American toolmaking tradition of the very end of the Ice Age, named after Blackwater Draw, a dry lake bed near the town of Clovis in New Mexico. Its signature is a lance-shaped stone point with a flute, a channel flake driven off the base up each face so that the point could be seated in a bone or ivory socket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That last removal was the risky one, since a flute struck badly wrecks a point already close to finished, and broken examples litter the workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The knappers also struck overshot flakes that ran right across the piece to carry off part of the far edge, though whether they meant to, and whether it sets Clovis apart at all, is now disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their points turn up right across North America, from coast to coast and south into Mexico and Central America. <br><br> The startling thing is the timing: careful redating puts the whole tradition between 13,050 and 12,750 years ago, and it is not the work of the first Americans, who were already there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Clovis people also buried caches of tools, at East Wenatchee, Fenn, Simon and elsewhere, some of the pieces far larger and finer than working gear needs to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Anzick site in Montana holds the only Clovis burial known, an infant boy covered in red ochre with more than 100 stone and bone objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At about a dozen sites their points lie among mammoth or mastodon bones, though whether every one is a kill rather than a scavenged carcass is now questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tradition ends as the Younger Dryas cold sets in, giving way on the Plains to the smaller Folsom point and elsewhere to a scatter of regional styles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Clovis culture","questions":["Named after Blackwater Draw in New Mexico, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left more than 10,000 fluted points at some 1,500 places from coast to coast.","The only burial known from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Anzick infant in Montana, covered in red ochre with more than 100 stone and bone objects."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr., and David L. Carlson, \"The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., \"The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,\" <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]","Donald K. Grayson, David J. Meltzer, and Ryan P. Breslawski, \"Overkill and the North American archaeological record—not guilty by association? A comment on Wolfe and Broughton (2020),\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 128 (2021): 105312, https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/people.smu.edu/dist/5/554/files/2021/08/Grayson-Meltzer-and-Breslawski-2021-JAS-Overkill-and-the-North-American-archaeological-record%E2%80%94not-guilty-by-association.pdf. [Open access]","Brett A. Story, Metin I. Eren, Kaitlyn Thomas, Briggs Buchanan, and David J. Meltzer, \"Why are Clovis fluted points more resilient than non-fluted lanceolate points? A quantitative assessment of breakage patterns between experimental models,\" <i>Archaeometry</i> 61, no. 1 (2019): 1–13, https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/people.smu.edu/dist/5/554/files/2019/01/Story-et-al.-2019-ARCHAEOMETRY-Why-are-Clovis-fluted-points-more-resilient-than-non-fluted-lanceolate-points-2205ct4.pdf. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren, David J. Meltzer, and Brian N. Andrews, \"Is Clovis technology unique to Clovis?,\" <i>PaleoAmerica</i> 4, no. 3 (2018): 202–218, https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/people.smu.edu/dist/5/554/files/2018/12/Eren-Meltzer-Andrews-2018-PALEOAMERICA-Is-Clovis-Technology-Unique-to-Clovis-1zq67w7.pdf. [Open access]","Metin I. Eren, Robert J. Patten, Michael J. O'Brien, and David J. Meltzer, \"Refuting the technological cornerstone of the Ice-Age Atlantic crossing hypothesis,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 40, no. 7 (2013): 2934–2941, https://cladistics.coas.missouri.edu/assets/pdf_articles/Eren_et_al_2013.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Clovis_spearpoints_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg/1920px-Clovis_spearpoints_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg","title":"Clovis culture","desc":"Clovis spearpoints on display at the Cleveland Museum of Natural History in Cleveland, Ohio. Named for Clovis, New Mexico, where such uniquely designed spearpoints were first found by archeologists, these spearpoints date from 13,500 to 13,000 years ago. Tim Evanson, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clovis_spearpoints_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Natural_History.jpg","alt":"Clovis spearpoints - Cleveland Museum of Natural History"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Clovis culture","at":[-103.3246,34.2779],"kind":"region","area":[[-123,45],[-120,49],[-105,49],[-90,48],[-78,45],[-72,42],[-75,36],[-81,29],[-97,25],[-104,29],[-114,32],[-121,37]]},"why":[{"q":"Why was the flute the risky part of making a Clovis point?","a":"Because a channel flake struck badly wrecks a point already close to finished, and the broken examples litter the workshops. It is the last removal on a piece that has already taken all the work."},{"q":"Why is the timing of the tradition startling?","a":"Because careful redating puts the whole of it between 13,050 and 12,750 years ago, and it is not the work of the first Americans, who were already there."},{"q":"Why are the buried caches more than working gear?","a":"Because at East Wenatchee, Fenn, Simon and elsewhere some of the pieces are far larger and finer than anything a hunter would need to use."}]},
{"id":"wh-102","num":"102","category":"World · History","question":"Knapped from stone carried hundreds of kilometres, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was resharpened and rebased until it wore out, and how it was actually used is still argued.","answer":"Clovis point","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Clovis point</b> is a leaf-shaped stone spear tip, worked on both faces and thinned to a shallow lens, with its edges ground blunt near the base where it met the shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They range from a few centimetres to more than twenty, and the largest known, from the East Wenatchee cache in Washington state, is an agate point 24.5 centimetres (9.6 inches) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stone matters: knappers chose fine cherts, jaspers and obsidians that flake predictably, and carried them extraordinary distances, in some cases more than 900 kilometres (560 miles) from the quarry. The blunted base was set into a foreshaft, probably a socket of bone or ivory though the method is still argued, and the flutes thinned it so the point could be bound in without a bulky joint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A worn point was not thrown away but resharpened at the tip and sometimes rebased and refluted, so an old one can be much shorter than it began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> How it was used is the open question, and three answers have long been on the table: a dart thrown with a spear-thrower, a hand-thrown javelin, and a spear driven in close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is still no consensus on which of them the design suits best, and the low frequency of impact fractures on Clovis points has been read as evidence both for and against hunting big game.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A 2024 study added a fourth possibility, a braced pike planted in the ground so that a charging animal drove itself onto the point and its own weight did the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Impact damage and the way the points break give some support to each idea, which is part of why an argument about a simple stone tip has run for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because collectors pay heavily for them, a flourishing market in fakes and replicas surrounds them, and a point with no recorded findspot is worth very little as evidence.","citation":"","answerText":"Clovis point","questions":["Worked on both faces to a shallow lens, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> usually ran 4 to 20 centimetres (1.6 to 7.9 inches), though a white agate giant from the East Wenatchee cache reached 23.","The base was ground blunt to sit in a split foreshaft, and the flutes thinned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so it could be bound in without a bulky joint."],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["R. Scott Byram, Kent G. Lightfoot, and Jun Ueno Sunseri, \"Clovis points and foreshafts under braced weapon compression: modeling Pleistocene megafauna encounters with a lithic pike,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 8 (2024): e0307996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0307996. [Open access]","David K. Thulman, Michael J. Shott, Alan M. Slade, and Justin P. Williams, \"Clovis point allometry, modularity, and integration: exploring shape variation due to tool use with landmark-based geometric morphometrics,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 8 (2023): e0289489, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0289489. [Open access]","Alan M. Slade, \"To haft and to hold: evidence for the hafting of Clovis fluted points,\" <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 3 (2021): 133–151, https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.3033. [Open access]","Richard B. Waitt, \"Megafloods and Clovis cache at Wenatchee, Washington,\" <i>Quaternary Research</i> 85 (2016): 430–444, https://fop.cascadiageo.org/pacific_northwest_cell/2018/Background_Literature/Waitt2016WenatcheeMegafloods.pdf. [Open access]","Briggs Buchanan et al., \"A Morphometric Assessment of the Intended Function of Cached Clovis Points,\" <i>PLoS ONE</i> 7, no. 2 (2012): e30530, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0030530. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Clovis_point%2C_11500-9000_BC%2C_Sevier_County%2C_Utah%2C_chert_-_Natural_History_Museum_of_Utah_-_DSC07376.JPG/1920px-Clovis_point%2C_11500-9000_BC%2C_Sevier_County%2C_Utah%2C_chert_-_Natural_History_Museum_of_Utah_-_DSC07376.JPG","title":"Clovis point","desc":"Exhibit in the Natural History Museum of Utah, Salt Lake City, Utah, USA. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clovis_point,_11500-9000_BC,_Sevier_County,_Utah,_chert_-_Natural_History_Museum_of_Utah_-_DSC07376.JPG","alt":"Clovis point, 11500-9000 BCE, Sevier County, Utah, chert - Natural History Museum of Utah"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did knappers carry stone hundreds of kilometres?","a":"Because the stone matters: they chose fine cherts, jaspers and obsidians that flake predictably, and carried them in some cases more than 900 kilometres from the quarry."},{"q":"Why can an old point be much shorter than it began?","a":"Because a worn point was not thrown away but resharpened at the tip, and sometimes rebased and refluted — so its length records its working life rather than its design."},{"q":"Why has an argument about a simple stone tip run for decades?","a":"Because how it was used is open: a dart thrown with a spear-thrower, a hand-thrown javelin, a spear driven in close, or a braced pike planted in the ground. Impact damage and the way the points break give some support to each."}]},
{"id":"wh-103","num":"103","category":"World · History","question":"Fluting almost the whole face of each point and wrecking up to three in four in the attempt, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent extraordinary skill on tips for hunting bison on the Plains.","answer":"Folsom tradition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,900 – 12,300 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Type find</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927, Wild Horse Arroyo</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Folsom tradition</b> is a hunting culture of the Great Plains and the American Southwest, named after the village of Folsom in New Mexico, where the bones came out of Wild Horse Arroyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> George McJunkin, a Black cowboy born into slavery, noticed bones washing out of the arroyo wall in 1908 and spent years trying to get anyone to come and look. He died in 1922, four years before the Colorado Museum of Natural History finally dug the place under Jesse Figgins, so he never learned what he had found. On 29 August 1927 a point turned up wedged between the ribs of an extinct bison, and Figgins stopped the work and had scientists travel out to see it lying there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That was the moment the long argument ended, since a worked spear tip inside an animal dead for thousands of years cannot be explained away as a coincidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The point itself is small, seldom more than 8 centimetres (3 inches), and fluted almost from base to tip on both faces, which is far harder than the shallower Clovis flute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Estimates of how many were ruined in the fluting run from about 10 to 60 percent, so the design cost a great deal of good stone and skill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Why they took that risk is still argued, one reading being that the thinned faces let the wooden haft grip the point like a splint against snapping. These were specialists in <i>Bison antiquus</i>, and at Wild Horse Arroyo the remains of some 32 of them lay with 19 points, an animal trap rather than single kills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the Cooper site in Oklahoma a bison skull painted with a red zigzag in hematite sat in the middle of one such kill, the first Paleoindian art known from the Americas outside a burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Folsom tradition","questions":["George McJunkin, a Black cowboy born into slavery, saw the bones eroding from Wild Horse Arroyo in 1908, years before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its name from the nearby village.","At the Cooper site the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a bison skull painted with a red zigzag in hematite, the oldest painted object known anywhere in North America."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","north america"],"sources":["Briggs Buchanan, J. David Kilby, Marcus J. Hamilton, et al., \"Bayesian revision of the Folsom age range using IntCal20,\" <i>PaleoAmerica</i> 7, no. 2 (2021): 133–144, http://marcusjhamilton.weebly.com/uploads/2/5/5/3/25533140/buchanan_et_al_2021.pdf. [Open access]","Lawrence J. Jackson, \"Carl Schwachheim and the Folsom site,\" <i>Current Research in the Pleistocene</i> 14 (1997): 42–44, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2023/07/CRP-14-1997.pdf. [Open access]","Leland C. Bement, Marian Hyman, Michael E. Zolensky, and Brian J. Carter, \"A painted skull from the Cooper site: a Folsom bison kill in NW Oklahoma,\" <i>Current Research in the Pleistocene</i> 14 (1997): 6–8, https://liberalarts.tamu.edu/csfa/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2023/07/CRP-14-1997.pdf. [Open access]","J. D. Figgins, \"The antiquity of man in America,\" <i>Natural History</i> 27, no. 3 (1927): 229–239, https://archive.org/details/sim_natural-history_may-june-1927_27_3. [Open access]","Linda S. Cordell, \"The Folsom site in retrospect,\" in <i>Vermejo Park</i>, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 83–86, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.83. [Open access]","Kaitlyn A. Thomas, Brett A. Story, Metin I. Eren, et al., \"Explaining the origin of fluting in North American Pleistocene weaponry,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 81 (2017): 23–30, https://cladistics.coas.missouri.edu/assets/pdf_articles/2017-clovis-fluting.pdf. [Open access]","David T. Kirkpatrick, \"The prehistory of northeastern New Mexico,\" in <i>Vermejo Park</i>, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 77–82, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.77. [Open access]","David J. Meltzer, Lawrence C. Todd, and Vance T. Holliday, \"The Folsom (Paleoindian) type site: past investigations, current studies,\" <i>American Antiquity</i> 67, no. 1 (2002): 5–36, https://doi.org/10.2307/2694875. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Soapstone-LindenmeierSite.JPG","title":"Folsom tradition","desc":"The arroyo surrounding the Lindenmeier archaeological site (Folsom culture) on Soapstone Prairie Natural Area near Fort Collins, Colorado. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soapstone-LindenmeierSite.JPG","alt":"Soapstone-LindenmeierSite"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the 1927 find end the long argument?","a":"Because a worked spear tip lodged between the ribs of an animal dead for thousands of years cannot be explained away as a coincidence — and Figgins stopped work and had scientists travel out to see it lying in place."},{"q":"Why was the Folsom flute so much more demanding than the Clovis one?","a":"Because it runs almost from base to tip on both faces. Estimates of how many points were ruined in the fluting run from about 10 to 60 per cent, so the design cost a great deal of good stone and skill."},{"q":"Why is Wild Horse Arroyo read as a trap rather than single kills?","a":"Because the remains of some 32 bison lay there with 19 points — a number of animals killed together rather than one at a time."}]},
{"id":"wh-104","num":"104","category":"World · History","question":"Named after an arctic flower whose pollen fills the layer, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> threw the northern world back into near-glacial cold for about 1,200 years, ending as abruptly as it began.","answer":"Younger Dryas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,900 – 11,700 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Younger Dryas</b> was a sharp return to near-glacial cold across the northern hemisphere, running from roughly 12,900 to 11,700 years ago and lasting about 1,200 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It takes its name from <i>Dryas octopetala</i>, an arctic flower whose pollen crowds the Scandinavian sediments laid down while it lasted. The world had been warming for nearly two thousand years when it struck, and the interruption undid a great deal of that gain inside a single human lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Central Greenland was perhaps 10 to 14 degrees colder than today, European forests gave way again to tundra and steppe, and dust in the ice cores rose several times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The exit was faster still, with the cores recording the closing warm-up in 40 to 50 years, and that boundary is the formal start of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The standard explanation is a flood of meltwater from the shrinking North American ice sheet, which freshened the North Atlantic and slowed the current that carries warmth north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the water went out is still argued: the old answer was the St Lawrence, but a good deal of evidence now favours the Mackenzie and the Arctic Ocean instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also unclear how one flood could hold that circulation down for more than a thousand years, so the mechanism is not fully settled. A comet or asteroid airburst has been proposed as the trigger, but the markers claimed for it have largely failed independent replication and most specialists do not accept it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For people the timing mattered: the cold overlaps the end of Clovis toolmaking in North America and the last stretch before farming in the Levant, though whether it pushed anyone into agriculture is disputed.","citation":"","answerText":"Younger Dryas","questions":["Blamed on a flood of meltwater that freshened the North Atlantic and slowed the current carrying warmth north, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began about 12,900 years ago.","A warm-up of forty to fifty years in the ice cores closes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and marks the start of the Holocene, which overlaps the end of Clovis toolmaking."],"tags":["era","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","Christo Buizert et al., \"Greenland Temperature Response to Climate Forcing during the Last Deglaciation,\" <i>Science</i> 345, no. 6201 (2014): 1177–80, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1254961. [Paywalled]","Jerry F. McManus et al., \"Collapse and Rapid Resumption of Atlantic Meridional Circulation Linked to Deglacial Climate Changes,\" <i>Nature</i> 428, no. 6985 (2004): 834–37, https://www.whoi.edu/cms/files/mcmanus04nat_100524.pdf. [Open access]","Julian B. Murton et al., \"Identification of Younger Dryas Outburst Flood Path from Lake Agassiz to the Arctic Ocean,\" <i>Nature</i> 464, no. 7289 (2010): 740–43, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature08954. [Paywalled]","David J. Meltzer et al., \"Chronological Evidence Fails to Support Claim of an Isochronous Widespread Layer of Cosmic Impact Indicators Dated to 12,800 Years Ago,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 21 (2014): E2162–71, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4040610/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Younger_Dryas_and_Holocene_temperature_changes.png","title":"Younger Dryas","desc":"Air temperature changes, determined as proxy temperatures, taken from the central region of Greenland's ice sheet during the Late Pleistocene and the Holocene. Chart in natural, left to right, time axis, with modern temperature indicated for clarity. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Younger_Dryas_and_Holocene_temperature_changes.png","alt":"Younger Dryas and Holocene temperature changes"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the period named after a flower?","a":"Because the pollen of Dryas octopetala, an arctic flower, crowds the Scandinavian sediments laid down while it lasted — the plant returning as the cold did."},{"q":"Why is the standard meltwater explanation not fully settled?","a":"Because it is unclear how one flood could hold the North Atlantic circulation down for more than a thousand years, and where the water went out is argued — the old answer was the St Lawrence, but much evidence now favours the Mackenzie."},{"q":"Why do most specialists reject the airburst trigger?","a":"Because the markers claimed for a comet or asteroid impact have largely failed independent replication."}]},
{"id":"wh-105","num":"105","category":"World · History","question":"Every farm, city, state and written word belongs to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the warm epoch that began when the Younger Dryas ended and that we are still living in.","answer":"Holocene","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epoch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11,700 BP – present</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Holocene</b> is the epoch we are living in, the second and far shorter half of the Quaternary Period, and it began 11,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its name joins the Greek holos and kainos to mean 'entirely recent', and the French naturalist Paul Gervais introduced it in the 1860s, before the geological congresses took it up. The boundary is not a guess but a marked point: nearly 1,493 metres (4,898 feet) down in the NGRIP ice core from Greenland, at the sharpest signal of the warming that ended the Younger Dryas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because ice preserves annual layers, that base can be counted rather than estimated, and it is quoted as 11,700 years before 2000 with an error of only 99 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strictly it is an interglacial like others before it, a warm pause inside the ice age that still holds Greenland and Antarctica, and not the end of the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What makes it exceptional is steadiness, since the Pleistocene lurched between glacial and warm phases while Holocene climate has stayed mild enough for people to build on it. Geologists split it in 2018 into three ages, each beginning at a climate shock: the Greenlandian, then the Northgrippian at the cold snap 8,200 years ago, then the Meghalayan at the drought of 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That last boundary is defined in a stalagmite from Krem Mawmluh cave in Meghalaya, north-eastern India, whose layers record an Asian monsoon that faltered at a time when several early civilisations came apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost everything usually called history sits inside this epoch: farming taken up independently in several regions, the first villages and cities, writing, states, and a population grown from a few million to eight billion. That weight of human activity is why the Anthropocene was proposed as a new epoch beginning in 1952, but stratigraphers voted the proposal down in March 2024, so the Holocene remains, formally, our own time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Holocene","questions":["Introduced by Paul Gervais in the 1860s, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has its base fixed nearly 1,493 metres (4,898 feet) down in a Greenland ice core at the sharpest warming signal.","Split in 2018 into the Greenlandian, Northgrippian and Meghalayan ages, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived a 2024 vote that would have ended it at the proposed Anthropocene."],"tags":["era","geology","science","nature"],"sources":["Subcommission on Quaternary Stratigraphy, International Commission on Stratigraphy, \"Major Divisions,\" https://quaternary.stratigraphy.org/major-divisions. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,\" <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–223, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Subcommission on Quaternary Stratigraphy, International Commission on Stratigraphy, \"Working Group on the ‘Anthropocene’,\" https://quaternary.stratigraphy.org/working-groups/anthropocene. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Definition and Dating of the GSSP (Global Stratotype Section and Point) for the Base of the Holocene Using the Greenland NGRIP Ice Core, and Selected Auxiliary Records,\" <i>Journal of Quaternary Science</i> 24, no. 1 (2009): 3–17, https://timescalefoundation.org/references/HoloceneGSSP_JQuatSci090.pdf. [Open access]","Past Interglacials Working Group of PAGES, \"Interglacials of the Last 800,000 Years,\" <i>Reviews of Geophysics</i> 54, no. 1 (2016): 162–219, under \"Defining Interglacials,\" https://hal.science/hal-03245836. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Global_temperature_relative_to_peak_Holocene_temperature%2C_based_on_ocean_cores_%28NASA%29.png/1920px-Global_temperature_relative_to_peak_Holocene_temperature%2C_based_on_ocean_cores_%28NASA%29.png","title":"Holocene","desc":"These two graphs show global temperatures for two different time periods (Hansen and Sato, 2011). The top graph shows global surface temperature for the past 5.3 million years as inferred from cores of ocean sediments taken all around the global ocean. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Global_temperature_relative_to_peak_Holocene_temperature,_based_on_ocean_cores_(NASA).png","alt":"Global temperature relative to peak Holocene temperature, based on ocean cores (NASA)"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why can the start of the Holocene be counted rather than estimated?","a":"Because the boundary is a marked point nearly 1,493 metres down in the NGRIP ice core, and ice preserves annual layers — so it is quoted as 11,700 years before 2000 with an error of only 99 years."},{"q":"Why is steadiness what makes the epoch exceptional?","a":"Because the Pleistocene lurched between glacial and warm phases, while Holocene climate has stayed mild enough for people to build on it. Strictly it is an interglacial like others before it."},{"q":"Why is the Anthropocene not the name of our epoch?","a":"Because stratigraphers voted the proposal down in March 2024, so the Holocene remains formally our own time despite the weight of human activity that prompted it."}]},
{"id":"wh-106","num":"106","category":"World · History","question":"Squeezed between the Old and New Stone Ages, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers the hunter-gatherers of the warming world after the Ice Age, armed with tiny blades set in wooden shafts.","answer":"Mesolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9700 – 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Coined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1866, by Hodder Westropp</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mesolithic</b>, or Middle Stone Age, is the phase of the Stone Age belonging to the hunter-gatherers of the early Holocene, who lived after the last Ice Age but before farming reached them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its name comes from the Greek for 'middle' and 'stone', and Hodder Westropp proposed it in 1866, a year after John Lubbock named the Paleolithic and the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Westropp's own three terms that year ran Palaeolithic, Mesolithic and Kainolithic; by 1872 he had swapped the last for Lubbock's Neolithic, and Grahame Clark's 1932 division of the age is still the backbone of the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Europe the age opens around 9700 BCE and closes wherever farming arrived, early in the southeast and only about 4000 BCE in Britain and southern Scandinavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is defined by a way of life rather than by dates, which is why the Levant's equivalent is called the Epipalaeolithic, and why researchers in Southeast Asia found early on that the label did not fit their evidence at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The warming remade the land: tundra gave way to dense forest, rising seas swallowed coastal plains, and the great herds of open country thinned or moved north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Hunters answered with the microlith, a blade rarely longer than 3 centimetres (1.2 inches), glued in rows into a shaft of wood or antler to arm arrows, harpoons and knives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Diets broadened to fish, shellfish, waterfowl, small game and hazelnuts, a shift Kent Flannery named the broad spectrum revolution in 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Star Carr in North Yorkshire, in use between about 9300 and 8500 BCE, gave up barbed antler points, a lake-edge platform of split timbers, and 33 red deer skullcaps worked into headdresses, of which only nine were pierced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"12\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"13\"></sup> Water dominated the richest sites: the Pesse canoe of the Netherlands, a hollowed log just under 3 metres (10 feet) long, is reckoned the oldest boat known, and Denmark's shell middens hold the oysters of coastal foragers who lived off the sea long before any crop was sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"14\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"15\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mesolithic","questions":["Coined by Hodder Westropp in 1866 alongside a third term he later dropped, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took its modern shape from Grahame Clark’s division of the age in 1932.","At Star Carr in North Yorkshire the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left barbed antler points and 33 red deer skullcaps worked into headdresses, though only nine of them were pierced."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Hodder M. Westropp, “On the Analogous Forms of Implements among Early and Primitive Races,” <i>Memoirs Read before the Anthropological Society of London</i> 2 (1866): 288–93, at 291, https://archive.org/details/memoirsreadbefor02anth. [Open access]","Hodder M. Westropp, <i>Pre-historic Phases; or, Introductory Essays on Pre-historic Archæology</i> (London: Bell and Daldy, 1872), 65, https://archive.org/details/cu31924029915240. [Open access]","John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Ben Elliott and Seren Griffiths, “Living Mesolithic Time: Narratives, Chronologies and Organic Material Culture,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 31, no. 3 (2018): 347–65, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-018-9119-x. [Open access]","Mike Walker et al., “Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,” <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–23, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under “The Origins of Domestication,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Podjanok Kanjanajuntorn, “The Three-Age System: A Struggle for Southeast Asian Prehistoric Periodisation,” <i>SPAFA Journal</i> 4 (2020): 5, https://doi.org/10.26721/spafajournal.v4i0.623. [Open access]","Heikki Seppä et al., “Last Nine-Thousand Years of Temperature Variability in Northern Europe,” <i>Climate of the Past</i> 5 (2009): 523–35, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-5-523-2009. [Open access]","Lars Larsson, Arne Sjöström, and Carl Heron, “The Rönneholm Arrow: A Find of a Wooden Arrow-Tip with Microliths in the Bog Rönneholms Mosse, Central Scania, Southern Sweden,” <i>Lund Archaeological Review</i> 22 (2016): 7–20, https://journals.lub.lu.se/lar/article/view/21616. [Open access]","Mary C. Stiner, “Thirty Years on the ‘Broad Spectrum Revolution’ and Paleolithic Demography,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 98, no. 13 (2001): 6993–96, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC34611/. [Open access]","Jessica Bates et al., “Spatial Organisation within the Earliest Evidence of Post-Built Structures in Britain,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 7 (2024): e0306908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249238/. [Open access]","Ben Elliott, Becky Knight, and Aimée Little, “Antler Frontlets,” in <i>Star Carr Volume 2: Studies in Technology, Subsistence and Environment</i>, ed. Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller, and Barry Taylor (York: White Rose University Press, 2018), 297–333, at 297 and 321, https://doi.org/10.22599/book2.l. [Open access]","Aimée Little et al., “Technological Analysis of the World’s Earliest Shamanic Costume: A Multi-Scalar, Experimental Study of a Red Deer Headdress from the Early Holocene Site of Star Carr, UK,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0152136, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4830440/. [Open access]","Cultural Heritage Agency of the Netherlands, “Pesse, kano van (-7510),” Maritime Stepping Stones database, accessed 31 July 2026, https://mass.cultureelerfgoed.nl/pesse. [Open access]","Harry K. Robson et al., “The Effects of Mid-Holocene Foragers on the European Oyster in Denmark,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 48 (2024): e2411692121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11573498/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Stone_Age_Stone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Kebaran_culture%2C_22000-18000_BP.jpg/1920px-Stone_Age_Stone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Kebaran_culture%2C_22000-18000_BP.jpg","title":"Mesolithic","desc":"En Gev Epipaleolithic period Basalt Israel Antiquities Authority H: 29 cm; Diam: 25-28 cm; Weight: 11 kg Prehistoric Periods The Israel Museum, Jerusalem Archaeology. Stone Age Stone Mortar & Pestle, Kebaran culture, 22000-18000 BP Israel Museum, Jerusalem, Israel. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_Age_Stone_Mortar_%26_Pestle,_Kebaran_culture,_22000-18000_BP.jpg","alt":"Stone Age Stone Mortar & Pestle, Kebaran culture, 22000-18000 BP"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Mesolithic defined by a way of life rather than by dates?","a":"Because it opens around 9700 BCE in Europe and closes wherever farming arrived — early in the southeast and only about 4000 BCE in Britain and southern Scandinavia."},{"q":"Why did hunters turn to the microlith?","a":"Because the warming remade the land: tundra gave way to dense forest, rising seas swallowed coastal plains, and the great herds of open country thinned or moved north. Small blades glued in rows armed arrows, harpoons and knives for that world."},{"q":"Why does the label not travel everywhere?","a":"Because the Levant's equivalent is called the Epipalaeolithic, and researchers in Southeast Asia found early on that the label did not fit their evidence at all."}]},
{"id":"wh-107","num":"107","category":"World · History","question":"Where the end of the Ice Age changed little and farming followed soon after, archaeologists prefer the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Mesolithic, a name stressing continuity with what came before.","answer":"Epipaleolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 23,000 – 11,600 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Epipaleolithic</b> is the last stretch of the Paleolithic in the Levant and the wider Near East, the hunter-gatherers who lived through the end of the Ice Age and the millennia after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name is built on the Greek <i>epi</i>, meaning 'upon': this is the age laid on top of the Old Stone Age rather than a break from it, and that emphasis is why the word exists. It covers the same ground as the Mesolithic, between the Upper Paleolithic and the Neolithic, but the two names lean opposite ways, one stressing what carried on and the other what was coming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists reach for it where the retreat of the ice altered the landscape comparatively little, and where farming followed too closely for any long middle age. It runs from roughly 23,000 to 11,600 years ago in the southern Levant, and the label reaches North Africa too, where the Iberomaurusian of Taforalt belongs to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> The Levantine sequence runs from the Kebaran through the Geometric Kebaran, whose microliths are trimmed into neat trapezes and triangles, to the Natufian of about 15,000 to 11,650 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Natufians are why the period matters: at Ain Mallaha they cut curved houses into the slope and walled them with undressed limestone, over some 2,000 square metres that may have held 50 to 100 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> They cut wild cereals with flint-bladed sickles in a harvest that ran from mid-spring to late, ground them on stone, stored food, and kept the dog, their only domesticated animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Shubayqa 1 in north-eastern Jordan a team recovered charred flatbread of wild einkorn and club-rush tubers, baked about 14,400 years ago, the oldest bread known and some 4,000 years older than farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> None of this was agriculture, yet the huts, the graves and the heavy ground stone show hunter-gatherers already settling down, and the first farming villages of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic grew out of exactly these camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Epipaleolithic","questions":["Natufians cut curved houses into the slope at Ain Mallaha, where 50 to 100 people may have lived, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows foragers settling down before any crop was sown.","At Shubayqa 1 in Jordan the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left charred flatbread of wild einkorn baked about 14,400 years ago, the oldest bread known and older than farming."],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history"],"sources":["Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Laurent Davin et al., “Modeling Identities among the First-Sedentary Communities: Emergence of Clay Personal Ornaments in Epipaleolithic Southwest Asia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12 (2026): eaea2158, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12998513/. [Open access]","Ceri Shipton et al., “The Natufian Epipalaeolithic and Pre-Pottery Neolithic in the Desert of Northern Arabia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 16842, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13226711/. [Open access]","Gil Haklay and Avi Gopher, “A New Look at Shelter 131/51 in the Natufian Site of Eynan (Ain-Mallaha), Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0130121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4496039/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Simon J. M. Davis and François R. Valla, “Evidence for Domestication of the Dog 12,000 Years Ago in the Natufian of Israel,” <i>Nature</i> 276 (1978): 608–10, https://doi.org/10.1038/276608a0. [Paywalled]","Amaia Arranz-Otaegui et al., “Archaeobotanical Evidence Reveals the Origins of Bread 14,400 Years Ago in Northeastern Jordan,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 31 (2018): 7925–30, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6077754/. [Open access]","Jonathan Santana et al., “Multi-Isotope Evidence of Population Aggregation in the Natufian and Scant Migration during the Early Neolithic of the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11857, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8178372/. [Open access]","Zineb Moubtahij et al., “Isotopic Evidence of High Reliance on Plant Food among Later Stone Age Hunter-Gatherers at Taforalt, Morocco,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8 (2024): 1035–45, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11090808/. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, under “The Origins of Domestication,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Lovers_9000BC_british_museum.jpg","title":"Epipaleolithic","desc":"Photo of an 11 thousand year old sculpture believed to depict a couple having sex. Now in the British Museum. Originates from Ain Sakhri, Judea. Geni, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lovers_9000BC_british_museum.jpg","alt":"Lovers 9000 BCE british museum"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the period named for what was laid on top rather than what came next?","a":"Because epi means upon: this is the age laid on top of the Old Stone Age rather than a break from it, where Mesolithic leans towards what was coming."},{"q":"Why do archaeologists reach for this word rather than Mesolithic here?","a":"Because the retreat of the ice altered the landscape comparatively little in the Near East, and farming followed too closely for any long middle age."},{"q":"Why do the Natufians make the period matter?","a":"Because they show hunter-gatherers already settling down: curved houses cut into the slope at Ain Mallaha, wild cereals cut with flint sickles, ground stone, stored food and the dog — and the first farming villages grew out of exactly these camps."}]},
{"id":"wh-108","num":"108","category":"World · History","question":"Named after a sandbank and mapped from oil-industry seismic data, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the drowned country that joined Britain to the continent until the sea closed over it.","answer":"Doggerland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dry land</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 16,000 – 8,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Storegga tsunami</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,150 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1998, by Bryony Coles</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Doggerland</b> is the name for the country that lay where the southern North Sea is now, joining Britain to the Netherlands, Germany and Denmark by dry land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With so much water locked up in ice, the sea stood far lower, and from roughly 16,000 years ago this was a plain of rivers, lakes, marsh and open woodland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Rhine, the Meuse, the Thames and the Scheldt ran together across it, and its wetlands would have been some of the richest hunting and fishing country in Europe. Bryony Coles gave it the name in 1998, after the Dogger Bank, which is itself named for the doggers, the old Dutch fishing boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it is under water, almost everything known about it has been dredged up, washed ashore or read off seismic surveys made by the oil industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The find that started it came up in a North Sea trawler's net in 1931, a lump of peat that broke open to reveal a barbed antler point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since then bones of mammoth, lion, deer and aurochs have come up with the fish, along with worked flint, human remains and Mesolithic tools of bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Seismic data gathered to look for gas has been reworked into maps of the buried rivers and coastlines, covering tens of thousands of square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sea took it gradually and then, about 8,150 years ago, a huge landslip off Norway sent a tsunami across whatever was left of the low ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A study in 2020 argued that even so it was not finished at a stroke, and that the slow rise of the sea rather than the wave is what took the last of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Doggerland","questions":["The story began in 1931 when a Norfolk trawler struck a lump of peat and pulled up a barbed antler point, the first sign that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had ever been dry land.","Where the Rhine, Meuse, Thames and Scheldt once ran together, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a plain of marsh and woodland that made some of Europe's richest hunting and fishing country."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","prehistory"],"sources":["James Walker et al., \"A Great Wave: The Storegga Tsunami and the End of Doggerland?\" <i>Antiquity</i> 94, no. 378 (2020): 1409–25, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2020.49. [Open access]","Vincent Gaffney, Kenneth Thomson, and Simon Fitch, eds., <i>Mapping Doggerland: The Mesolithic Landscapes of the Southern North Sea</i> (Oxford: Archaeopress, 2007), https://www.archaeopress.com/Archaeopress/DMS/F9B49C6FC7E04728B2346A365BC405F1/9781905739141-sample.pdf. [Open access]","Bernhard Weninger et al., \"The Catastrophic Final Flooding of Doggerland by the Storegga Slide Tsunami,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 35 (2008): 1–24, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.35.1. [Open access]","Vincent Gaffney et al., \"Multi-Proxy Characterisation of the Storegga Tsunami and Its Impact on the Early Holocene Landscapes of the Southern North Sea,\" <i>Geosciences</i> 10, no. 7 (2020): 270, https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences10070270. [Open access]","J. H. M. Peeters and G. Momber, \"The Southern North Sea and the Human Occupation of Northwest Europe after the Last Glacial Maximum,\" <i>Netherlands Journal of Geosciences</i> 93, no. 1–2 (2014): 55–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/njg.2014.3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Doggerbank.jpg","title":"Doggerland","desc":"Doggerbank. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doggerbank.jpg","alt":"Doggerbank"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Doggerland","at":[3,52],"kind":"region","area":[[0.9,53],[1.1,55.3],[2.6,56.5],[5.2,57],[7.6,55.9],[8.3,54.4],[6.2,53],[4.2,52.2],[2.6,51.9],[1,52.3]]},"why":[{"q":"Why would this have been rich country to live in?","a":"Because the Rhine, the Meuse, the Thames and the Scheldt ran together across a plain of rivers, lakes, marsh and open woodland, and its wetlands would have been some of the best hunting and fishing in Europe."},{"q":"Why is almost everything known about it accidental?","a":"Because it is under water, so the finds have been dredged up, washed ashore or read off seismic surveys made by the oil industry — the first was a lump of peat in a trawler's net in 1931 holding a barbed antler point."},{"q":"Why is the tsunami not the whole story of its drowning?","a":"Because a study in 2020 argued that even after the Norwegian landslip about 8,150 years ago it was not finished at a stroke, and that the slow rise of the sea took the last of it."}]},
{"id":"wh-113","num":"113","category":"World · History","question":"Warmest around 6,500 years ago and driven by a wobble in Earth's orbit, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lifted northern summers above today's, while the tropics stayed cooler.","answer":"Holocene climatic optimum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Interval</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 – 5,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Warmest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,500 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Holocene climatic optimum</b> is the warm interval of the early and middle Holocene, running roughly from 9,000 to 5,000 years ago and warmest near 6,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries a crowd of names: the Hypsithermal, the Altithermal, the Holocene Thermal Maximum and the Mid-Holocene Warm Period, a fair sign of how loosely its edges are drawn. The warmth came from the slow wobble of Earth's orbit, since precession brought the northern summer nearer the Sun and raised the sunlight falling on high northern latitudes. That forcing peaked before the warmth did, because the Laurentide ice sheet still lay over North America, and its cold and its whiteness held temperatures down until it had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the optimum is no single moment shared by the planet: early and strong at high northern latitudes, later and fainter in the tropics, and dated at some sites anywhere between 11,000 and 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its evidence lies in living things and ice, for the northern treeline stood further north than now, Arctic glaciers shrank, and warm-water molluscs lived on Canadian coasts too cold for them today. The most striking change was in Africa, where a stronger summer monsoon watered much of the Sahara, filling it with grassland, rivers and lakes that drew herders and their cattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That African humid period ended about 5,500 years ago, though the drying came neither everywhere at once nor everywhere abruptly, running earlier in the north than the south. The warmth was seasonal and regional rather than global, gathered into northern summers, while the tropics and parts of the Southern Hemisphere sat cooler than they do now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That makes it a weak analogue for present warming: one reconstruction puts its warmest centuries 0.7 degrees above the 19th century but 0.3 degrees below the average of 2011 to 2019, and the IPCC holds with medium confidence that the recent decade is the warmer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Holocene climatic optimum","questions":["When roughly 9 million square kilometres (3.5 million square miles) of the Sahara held grassland, rivers and lakes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood at its height, and that green ended about 5,500 years ago.","Known also as the Hypsithermal and the Altithermal, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> warmed northern summers through orbital precession, yet it makes a poor analogue for the even warming of today."],"tags":["era","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Darrell S. Kaufman et al., \"Holocene Global Mean Surface Temperature, a Multi-method Reconstruction Approach,\" <i>Scientific Data</i> 7 (2020): 201, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0530-7. [Open access]","Olivier Cartapanis et al., \"Complex Spatio-temporal Structure of the Holocene Thermal Maximum,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 13 (2022): 5662, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-33362-1. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata, and Peter B. deMenocal, \"Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","IPCC, \"Summary for Policymakers,\" in <i>Climate Change 2021: The Physical Science Basis</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), para. A.2.2, https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg1/chapter/summary-for-policymakers/. [Open access]","Olivier Marchal et al., \"Apparent Long-Term Cooling of the Sea Surface in the Northeast Atlantic and Mediterranean during the Holocene,\" <i>Quaternary Science Reviews</i> 21, no. 4–6 (2002): 455–83, https://www.whoi.edu/cms/files/marchal02qsr_42274.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/202006_KaufmanEtAl_Holocene_Temp_NH_Bond.png","title":"Holocene climatic optimum","desc":"Holocene temperature anomalies in latitudes 30°-60° and 60°-90° N and Bond-events. DeWikiMan, based on work by Kaufman, D., McKay, N., Routson, C. et al.,. CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:202006_KaufmanEtAl_Holocene_Temp_NH_Bond.png","alt":"KaufmanEtAl Holocene Temp NH Bond"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the warmth peak later than the sunlight that caused it?","a":"Because the Laurentide ice sheet still lay over North America, and its cold and its whiteness held temperatures down until it had gone."},{"q":"Why is the optimum no single moment shared by the planet?","a":"Because it was early and strong at high northern latitudes, later and fainter in the tropics, and is dated at some sites anywhere between 11,000 and 4,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why is it a weak analogue for present warming?","a":"Because the warmth was seasonal and regional, gathered into northern summers, while the tropics and parts of the Southern Hemisphere sat cooler than now — and one reconstruction puts its warmest centuries 0.3 degrees below the average of 2011 to 2019."}]},
{"id":"wh-114","num":"114","category":"World · History","question":"About 8,200 years ago the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chilled the northern hemisphere for some 160 years, the sharpest cold snap of the Holocene, before the warmth of the epoch returned.","answer":"8.2-kiloyear event","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Event</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,200 BP, or 6,200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">some 160 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>8.2-kiloyear event</b> was an abrupt turn to cold, dry conditions about 8,200 years ago, and it stands as the sharpest climate reversal of the Holocene, the warm epoch we still live in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greenland ice cores register it as a plunge in oxygen isotopes that arrived within a couple of decades and lasted roughly 160 years, with temperatures over the ice falling by some 3 degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the northern hemisphere cooled by 1 to 3 degrees, a drop legible in lake muds, pollen, cave formations and ocean sediments across four continents. The usual explanation begins with a flood, for Lakes Agassiz and Ojibway, vast sheets of meltwater ponded against the shrinking Laurentide ice, burst through Hudson Bay into the Labrador Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A vast pulse of fresh water reached the ocean in a few years at most, and that lid of light water slowed the Atlantic overturning circulation that carries warmth north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Not everyone accepts so simple a chain, and some argue that the collapse of the ice saddle over Hudson Bay, rather than the lakes alone, delivered the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whatever the trigger, the consequences were global, since the tropical rain belts shifted south, the Asian and African monsoons weakened and the Middle East turned arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The outburst also raised the sea by a metre or two (3 to 7 feet), leaving Doggerland a shrinking archipelago, and the Storegga landslide off Norway sent a tsunami across it during the coldest decades of the event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Near East the tightly linked world of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B came apart within these centuries, its great sites emptying across the Levant, Syria and Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much of that the cold caused is still argued, but the event is firm enough in the record that geologists made it a boundary, its Greenland horizon of 8,236 years before 2000 opening the Northgrippian, the middle age of the Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"8.2-kiloyear event","questions":["When Lakes Agassiz and Ojibway burst through Hudson Bay into the Labrador Sea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed, because that flood of fresh water slowed the Atlantic's overturning circulation.","Geologists cut the Holocene in two at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Greenland ice-core boundary at 8,236 years before 2000 opens the Northgrippian, the middle age of the epoch."],"tags":["event","geology","science","climate"],"sources":["Richard B. Alley et al., \"Holocene Climatic Instability: A Prominent, Widespread Event 8200 yr Ago,\" <i>Geology</i> 25, no. 6 (1997): 483–86, https://doi.org/10.1130/0091-7613%281997%29025%3C0483:HCIAPW%3E2.3.CO;2. [Open access]","Ilkka S. O. Matero et al., \"The 8.2 ka Cooling Event Caused by Laurentide Ice Saddle Collapse,\" <i>Earth and Planetary Science Letters</i> 473 (2017): 205–14, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.epsl.2017.06.011. [Open access]","E. R. Thomas et al., \"The 8.2 ka Event from Greenland Ice Cores,\" <i>Quaternary Science Reviews</i> 26, no. 1–2 (2007): 70–81, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.quascirev.2006.07.017. [Paywalled]","James Walker et al., \"A Great Wave: The Storegga Tsunami and the End of Doggerland?\" <i>Antiquity</i> 94, no. 378 (2020): 1409–25, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2020.49. [Open access]","Bernhard Weninger et al., \"Climate Forcing Due to the 8200 cal yr BP Event Observed at Early Neolithic Sites in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>Quaternary Research</i> 66, no. 3 (2006): 401–20, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.yqres.2006.06.009. [Paywalled]","Mike Walker et al., \"Formal Ratification of the Subdivision of the Holocene Series/Epoch (Quaternary System/Period): Two New Global Boundary Stratotype Sections and Points (GSSPs) and Three New Stages/Subseries,\" <i>Episodes</i> 41, no. 4 (2018): 213–223, https://doi.org/10.18814/epiiugs/2018/018016. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Climate_Relapse_around_6250_BC.png","title":"8.2-kiloyear event","desc":"This is the most prominent temperature fallback (regression) of the Holocene immediately preceding the Atlantic temperature peak. Hans J.J.G. Holm, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Climate_Relapse_around_6250_BC.png","alt":"Climate Relapse around 6250 BCE"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did a flood cool the northern hemisphere?","a":"Because Lakes Agassiz and Ojibway burst through Hudson Bay into the Labrador Sea, and that lid of light fresh water slowed the Atlantic overturning circulation that carries warmth north."},{"q":"Why did geologists make this event a formal boundary?","a":"Because it is firm enough in the record: its Greenland horizon of 8,236 years before 2000 opens the Northgrippian, the middle age of the Holocene."},{"q":"Why is the sharpest reversal of the Holocene still a modest one?","a":"Because it lasted roughly 160 years and cooled much of the northern hemisphere by 1 to 3 degrees — sharp against a mild epoch, but nothing like the swings of the Pleistocene before it."}]},
{"id":"gr-001","num":"1","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Running from about 3100 to 1050 BCE across Crete, the Cyclades and the Greek mainland, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is divided into Minoan, Cycladic and Helladic sequences.","answer":"Aegean Bronze Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aegean Bronze Age</b> runs from about 3100 to 1050 BCE, and three regional traditions divide it, Minoan on Crete, Cycladic in the islands and Helladic on the Greek mainland, each cut into Early, Middle and Late phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islanders built first, and by about 2650 BCE the Cyclades held towns of two-storeyed houses, while the long ships they drew on clay pans needed crews of up to fifty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Crete followed with the palaces, which rose at Knossos and Phaistos around 1900 BCE and called for trained masons and workforces far larger than anything the island had assembled before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Metal explains much of the traffic between them, since Crete is badly supplied with copper, tin, silver and gold, and that shortage pushed Cretans to sea to find what their smiths needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Crete produced the first literate high civilisation in Europe, and its palaces reached out to Egypt, the Levant and Cyprus, their finest pottery passing to foreign courts as gifts between ruling families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From about 1750 BCE Minoan ways spread through the islands, and Cretan pottery, Linear A tablets and frescoes turn up at Phylakopi, Ayia Irini and Akrotiri, whose towns fortified themselves while Crete did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The eruption that buried the Theran town of Akrotiri is the age's sharpest quarrel, and Sturt Manning dates it to between 1609 and 1560 BCE, shifting the landmarks of the whole period with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The mainland rose later and differently, its lords at Mycenae buried in shaft graves heavy with gold, much of it worked to their own taste by Cretan smiths living among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After about 1470 BCE the balance turned towards the mainland, and Knossos alone went on as a palace, keeping its records in Linear B, the script shown in 1952 to record Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That made Greek the oldest language in Europe still spoken; the mainland palaces burned around 1200 BCE, a drying climate ensuring none rose again, but the age itself runs on to about 1050.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aegean Bronze Age","questions":["Crete's shortage of copper, tin, silver and gold sent its people to sea, and that search for metal shaped trade across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from its earliest centuries.","The mainland palaces burned around 1200 BCE and no central authority returned, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran on to about 1050 before giving way to the Greek Early Iron Age."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 4: The Early Cycladic Period,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-4/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 2, 5–8, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, \"Second Intermediate Period Date for the Thera (Santorini) Eruption and Historical Implications,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 9 (2022): e0274835, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0274835. [Open access]","Martin Finné et al., \"Late Bronze Age Climate Change and the Destruction of the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 12 (2017): e0189447, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0189447. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Aegean_Civilization%2C_H._G._Wells%27_Outline_of_History%2C_page_95.jpg","title":"Aegean Bronze Age","desc":"Illustration from page 95 of The outline of history; being a plain history of life and mankind, the definitive edition revised and rearranged by the author, by H.G. Wells, illustrated by J. F. Horrabin, showing \"Aegean Civilization.\".","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegean_Civilization,_H._G._Wells%27_Outline_of_History,_page_95.jpg","alt":"Aegean Civilization, H. G. Wells' Outline of History, page 95"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did Crete's shortage of metal push its people to sea?","a":"Because the island is badly supplied with copper, tin, silver and gold, so what its smiths needed had to be found abroad, which is much of the traffic between Crete and its neighbours."},{"q":"Why does the Theran eruption's date unsettle the whole period?","a":"Because it is the age's sharpest quarrel and shifts the landmarks of the period with it — Manning dates it to between 1609 and 1560 BCE."},{"q":"Why did the decipherment of Linear B matter beyond one script?","a":"Because it showed the tablets record Greek, which made Greek the oldest language in Europe still spoken."}]},
{"id":"gr-002","num":"2","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Farming hamlets, cemeteries of 20 graves or fewer, and towns whose houses had upper floors all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the central Aegean islands.","answer":"Cycladic civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grotta-Pelos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Keros-Syros</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2650 – 2400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phylakopi I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1850 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cycladic civilisation</b> is the Early Bronze Age culture of the Cyclades, the islands of the central Aegean, and one of the three that make up the Greek Bronze Age beside Minoan Crete and the Helladic mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people began as farmers in scattered hamlets, each holding one kin group for three or four generations and each with its own small cemetery outside the houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those burial grounds usually held 20 graves or fewer, the dead laid on their sides with the knees drawn up, and very often nothing was buried with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By about 2650 BCE some islanders were living in real towns, and at Skarkos on Ios the neat masonry still stands almost three metres (10 feet) high, most houses having had an upper floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the islands had to offer the wider Aegean was metal: lead and silver from Siphnos, copper from Kythnos, and all three from the Laurion ores across the water in Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> None of it moved without boats, and the long ships incised on Cycladic clay pans needed crews of up to fifty, more than most single settlements could raise, so neighbours had to combine to put one to sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They sailed a long way, and while the obsidian used on Keros came mostly from Melos, pieces came in from the Dodecanese and central Anatolia, along the routes that brought Anatolian tin and gold into the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Keros became the meeting point of those routes, a barren island where islanders gathered to lay marble figures and vessels in the ground, every one broken somewhere else and carried in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Late in the 3rd millennium BCE small walled forts went up on Syros, Naxos and Delos and were abandoned within a few generations, after which the hamlets drew together into single large towns and marble carving died away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through all of it the islanders were not newcomers, since Early Bronze Age Aegean genomes are closely alike and descend mostly from the Neolithic farmers already living there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cycladic civilisation","questions":["Lead and silver from Siphnos, copper from Kythnos and Melian obsidian all moved by sea, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> depended on boats crewed by more men than one village held.","The most prosperous phase of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closes late in the 3rd millennium BCE, when the small walled forts on Syros, Naxos and Delos were abandoned within a few generations."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 4: The Early Cycladic Period,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-4/. [Open access]","Florian Clemente et al., \"The Genomic History of the Aegean Palatial Civilizations,\" <i>Cell</i> 184, no. 10 (2021): 2565–86, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2021.03.039. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew et al., \"The Sanctuary at Keros in the Aegean Early Bronze Age: From Centre of Congregation to Centre of Power,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 1–36, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1709. [Open access]","Tristan Carter et al., \"Characterizing Centrality: Obsidian Consumption, Supra-Regional Connectivity, and Social Reproduction at the Early Bronze Age Sanctuary of Keros (Cyclades, Greece),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0325218, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0325218. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Marble_seated_harp_player_MET_gr47.100.1.R.jpg","title":"Cycladic civilisation","desc":"Cycladic; Statuette of a seated harp player; Stone.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_seated_harp_player_MET_gr47.100.1.R.jpg","alt":"Marble seated harp player"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Cyclades","at":[25.1667,37],"kind":"region","area":[[24.1,37.3],[24.55,37.95],[25.3,38.05],[25.95,37.2],[25.9,36.45],[25.2,36.2],[24.3,36.5],[24.05,36.95]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did islanders have to combine to put a ship to sea?","a":"Because the long ships incised on Cycladic clay pans needed crews of up to fifty, more than most single settlements could raise."},{"q":"Why did the islands matter to the wider Aegean?","a":"Because of metal: lead and silver from Siphnos, copper from Kythnos, and all three from the Laurion ores across the water in Attica."},{"q":"Why is Keros an odd place for people to have gathered?","a":"Because it is barren, and islanders came there to lay marble figures and vessels in the ground, every one broken somewhere else and carried in."}]},
{"id":"gr-003","num":"3","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Carved from island marble and found mostly in tombs, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were made to lie on their backs, since the feet and knees are rendered for a reclining figure.","answer":"Cycladic figurines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cycladic figurines</b> are the marble human figures carved in the islands of the central Aegean during the Early Bronze Age, and most of them came out of graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are not one thing: the earliest have prominent ears and kneecaps with hands meeting across the stomach, others hold stubby arms out at shoulder level, and the plainest are barely more than ovoid pebbles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The famous type, arms folded across the chest, belongs to the centuries around 2600 BCE, and was never meant to stand, since the feet and knees are carved for a figure lying on its back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor are they all women, for among the earliest the sexes are plainly marked and the males are almost as numerous as the females.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside them the islanders carved people doing things, harpists seated with their instruments, pipers standing to play, warriors wearing the strap that held a dagger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Carving one cost a practised hand 20 to 30 hours with nothing but stone tools, and the workshop at Skarkos on Ios held emery, obsidian and pumice beside half-finished figures and pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pigment is the point, because the white marble we admire is an accident of survival: these figures were painted, black and blue for eyes, eyebrows and hair, red for necklaces, bracelets and diadems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the paint has gone it often leaves a paint ghost, a smoother patch standing in faint relief because the stone beneath weathered differently from the bare stone around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What those patterns meant is genuinely open — body paint for particular occasions, marks of status, attributes of the figure, badges of a shared identity — and the graves hold the colour kits too, stained bowls with pestles and bone tubes for pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not all stayed whole, for at the sanctuary on Keros they arrive already broken, one piece carried over the sea and the rest left behind, and because so many others were dug for the market rather than recorded, most have no findspot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cycladic figurines","questions":["Black and blue once marked their eyes and hair and red their necklaces, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> we admire as white marble are stripped of the colour they once carried.","Harpists, pipers and warriors with a dagger-strap were carved alongside the folded-arm figures, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show far more than one repeated female form."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 4: The Early Cycladic Period,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-4/. [Open access]","\"The Use of Colour in Cycladic Art,\" Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, https://cycladic.gr/en/essay/i-chrisi-tou-chromatos-stin-kykladiki-te/. [Open access]","\"Manufacture of Cycladic Figurines,\" Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, https://cycladic.gr/en/essay/technikes-kataskevis-ton-kykladikon-e/. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew et al., \"The Sanctuary at Keros in the Aegean Early Bronze Age: From Centre of Congregation to Centre of Power,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 1–36, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1709. [Open access]","David W. J. Gill and Christopher Chippindale, \"Material and Intellectual Consequences of Esteem for Cycladic Figures,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 97, no. 4 (1993): 601–59, https://doi.org/10.2307/506716. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Cycladic_female_figurine_2.jpg","title":"Cycladic figurines","desc":"Cycladic female figurine, from Museum of Cycladic Art at Athens - Canonical type, early work of the Spedos variety, 17.3 cm, #104a in the 1968 catalogue.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cycladic_female_figurine_2.jpg","alt":"Cycladic female figurine 2"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the white marble a false impression?","a":"Because the figures were painted — black and blue for eyes, eyebrows and hair, red for necklaces, bracelets and diadems — and the bare stone is an accident of survival."},{"q":"Why can lost paint still be seen?","a":"Because it often leaves a paint ghost, a smoother patch standing in faint relief because the stone beneath weathered differently from the bare stone around it."},{"q":"Why do most of them tell us nothing about where they came from?","a":"Because so many were dug for the market rather than recorded, so they have no findspot."}]},
{"id":"gr-004","num":"4","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cycladic islanders sailed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for centuries carrying single pieces of figurines and bowls broken at home, and left them in deposits whose fragments never join up.","answer":"Keros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2750 – 2250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Keros</b> is a small, rugged and now uninhabited island at the centre of the Cyclades, and for five hundred years of the Early Bronze Age it was among the best-connected places in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That is the strange part, because the island has poor soil, little farmland and almost nothing worth quarrying, so nearly everything on it had to be carried there across open water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Facing its western tip across a narrow strait stands the islet of Dhaskalio, which a natural causeway joined to Keros while the place was in use, giving visiting ships good shelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its builders cut the steep slopes into terraces to make level ground and raised their walls from marble ferried 10 kilometres (6 miles) from Naxos, more than 7,000 tonnes (7,700 tons) of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stairways, massive entrances and a drainage system running across the whole islet mark it out as a planned settlement, on a scale its excavator compares with Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Yet almost nobody lived there: kitchens and rubbish heaps are missing, the food came from neighbouring islands, and the buildings look made to receive crowds rather than to house them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What those crowds came to do is why Keros is famous, for at Kavos on that western tip they laid marble figures and stone vessels in the ground already broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pieces do not fit together, most objects being present as a single fragment whose remainder is always missing, so the breaking was done on the islands the visitors had sailed from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dhaskalio worked metal beyond anything else known in the Early Bronze Age Cyclades, casting axes, chisels, spearheads and daggers, and its obsidian came from Melos, the Dodecanese and central Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Radiocarbon places its three phases between roughly 2750 and 2250 BCE, after which the island was left and, unusually for the Aegean, never built on again, so it stands as the oldest maritime sanctuary yet known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Keros","questions":["More than 7,000 tonnes (7,700 tons) of marble were shipped 10 kilometres (6 miles) from Naxos to build Dhaskalio, the terraced settlement joined by a causeway to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its two special deposits.","With poor soil and almost nothing worth quarrying, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imported even its building stone, yet its metalworkers cast more bronze than anywhere else in the Early Bronze Age Cyclades."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Colin Renfrew et al., \"The Sanctuary at Keros in the Aegean Early Bronze Age: From Centre of Congregation to Centre of Power,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 1–36, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1709. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew, \"The Sanctuary at Keros: Questions of Materiality and Monumentality,\" <i>Journal of the British Academy</i> 1 (2013): 187–212, https://doi.org/10.5871/jba/001.187. [Open access]","Colin Renfrew, Michael Boyd, and Christopher Bronk Ramsey, \"The Oldest Maritime Sanctuary? Dating the Sanctuary at Keros and the Cycladic Early Bronze Age,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 86, no. 331 (2012): 144–60, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00062517. [Paywalled]","\"Evidence for Advanced Architectural Planning at the Early Prehistoric Site of Dhaskalio in the Aegean,\" British School at Athens, 6 May 2019, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/2019/05/06/evidence-for-advanced-architectural-planning-at-the-early-prehistoric-site-of-dhaskalio-in-the-aegean/. [Open access]","\"Keros Project,\" Department of Archaeology, University of Cambridge, https://www.arch.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/current-projects/keros-project. [Open access]","Tristan Carter et al., \"Characterizing Centrality: Obsidian Consumption, Supra-Regional Connectivity, and Social Reproduction at the Early Bronze Age Sanctuary of Keros (Cyclades, Greece),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0325218, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0325218. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Figurilla_de_mujer%2C_Cultura_Keros-Silos_%28British_Museum%29.jpg/1920px-Figurilla_de_mujer%2C_Cultura_Keros-Silos_%28British_Museum%29.jpg","title":"Keros","desc":"A Cycladic marble figurine of a woman, carved with folded arms and a plain tilted-back head in the Spedos manner of the Keros-Syros culture, about 2500 to 2300 BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figurilla_de_mujer,_Cultura_Keros-Silos_(British_Museum).jpg","alt":"Figurilla de mujer, Cultura Keros-Silos (British Museum)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Keros","at":[25.65,36.89]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the island's importance strange?","a":"Because it has poor soil, little farmland and almost nothing worth quarrying, so nearly everything on it had to be carried across open water."},{"q":"Why is Dhaskalio read as built for crowds rather than residents?","a":"Because kitchens and rubbish heaps are missing and the food came from neighbouring islands, while stairways, massive entrances and a drainage system run across the whole islet."},{"q":"Why must the figures have been broken elsewhere?","a":"Because the pieces do not fit together, most objects being present as a single fragment whose remainder is always missing."}]},
{"id":"gr-005","num":"5","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"More than 70 round tholos tombs cluster around the Mesara plain in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each taking hundreds of bodies over centuries with no regard paid to the burials already inside.","answer":"Early Minoan Crete","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">EM I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EM II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2650 – 2150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EM III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2150 – 2000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Early Minoan Crete</b> is the island in the Early Bronze Age, running from about 3100 to 2000 BCE, the long stretch of Cretan life before any palace was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> People lived in small settlements of a few families, and the fullest recovered, Myrtos Fournou Korifi on the south coast, packs some 65 rooms and yards into a sixth of a hectare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That warren was first read as one complex run by a central authority, until Todd Whitelaw took it apart into five or six family houses of much the same size, holding perhaps fifty people between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sameness is what matters: kitchens, storerooms and rooms of looms and washtubs repeat house by house instead of being shared, and nothing in the buildings or the finds puts anyone above anyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not poor for it, and Mochlos in the north-east grew into a large village of houses and streets, with workshops turning out gold jewellery, stone vases and obsidian blades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their dead went into communal tombs, in the south the round stone tholos, four to thirteen metres (13 to 43 feet) across and almost always entered from the east, more than 70 of them known from over 45 sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hundreds of bodies went into most of them with no regard for the burials already there, each laid down with personal belongings and with food and drink for whatever came after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where a settlement had two such tombs they are much of a size and hold much the same goods, the best evidence that Mesara society was one of rough equals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The north-east buried differently, in rectangular house tombs, and at Mochlos two unusually large and isolated ones held gold diadems and a silver vessel, marks of rank that may belong to chiefly families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Myrtos and Vasiliki both burned near the end of the period, yet no incomers explain it, since ancient genomes give Minoans at least three-quarters of their ancestry from the first farmers of western Anatolia and the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Early Minoan Crete","questions":["Once the warren of 65 rooms at Myrtos was taken apart into five or six ordinary family houses rather than one ruler's complex, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looked strikingly egalitarian.","Houses of much the same size, each with its own kitchen, storerooms and looms, are why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks like a society with nobody standing above anyone else."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 5: The Early Minoan Period: The Settlements,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-5-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Mochlos,\" INSTAP Study Center for East Crete, https://instapstudycenter.net/archaeological-projects/ascsa-projects/mochlos/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis et al., \"Genetic Origins of the Minoans and Mycenaeans,\" <i>Nature</i> 548, no. 7666 (2017): 214–18, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature23310. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Minoan_-_Sealstone_of_a_Bull_-_Walters_42769.jpg","title":"Early Minoan Crete","desc":"Carved sealstones were popular ornaments among Bronze Age Minoans and Mycenaeans and served as insignia of rank and social status. They were influenced by the elaborate traditions of stamp and cylinder seals developed in the Near East and Egypt.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_-_Sealstone_of_a_Bull_-_Walters_42769.jpg","alt":"Minoan - Sealstone of a Bull"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why was Myrtos Fournou Korifi re-read as several houses?","a":"Because Todd Whitelaw took the warren of 65 rooms apart into five or six family houses of much the same size, holding perhaps fifty people between them, rather than one complex under a central authority."},{"q":"Why is the repetition of rooms house by house significant?","a":"Because kitchens, storerooms and rooms of looms and washtubs repeat rather than being shared, and nothing in the buildings or the finds puts anyone above anyone else."},{"q":"Why do the Mesara tombs suggest a society of rough equals?","a":"Because where a settlement had two such tombs they are much of a size and hold much the same goods."}]},
{"id":"gr-006","num":"6","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Small rival states in separate valleys, palaces built above all for storage, and an undeciphered script are what make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Bronze Age culture of Crete.","answer":"Minoan civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prepalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1720 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Postpalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1470 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan civilisation</b> is the Bronze Age culture of Crete, running from about 3100 to 1050 BCE and named after Minos, the king in whom the Greeks summed up the age before their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crete was never one kingdom, for its mountains cut the island into separate valleys and plains, each with its own town, so Minoans lived among small rival states more like Renaissance Italy than Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That made close communities where everyone knew everyone, the opposite of the Near Eastern kingdoms with their distant ruler and vast peasantry, and Cretan neighbours repeatedly destroyed each other's settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around 1900 BCE the palaces appeared and changed how the island worked, each a mass of rooms around a paved central court with long storage magazines in its west wing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Storage is the heart of them: Malia perhaps gives more of its ground floor to magazines than any other palace, and at three of the four the outer wall of those magazines is the building's most carefully built face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They fed and entertained too, with banquet halls upstairs at the north end, served by one stair from the kitchens and another from the courts, and shallow stepped areas where a crowd could stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Along the west side of the court lay cult rooms whose pillars are cut with double axes and tridents, read by some as places where a god blamed for earthquakes was appeased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their script was Linear A, written from about 1800 to 1450 BCE and chiefly on Crete, undeciphered still across fewer than two thousand surviving documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Minoan goods and habits travelled with their ships, and pottery, Linear A tablets, frescoes and terracotta women in Cretan dress turn up at Phylakopi, Ayia Irini and Akrotiri, while Kastri on Kythera looks settled outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around 1470 BCE destructions ended that order, and the richly armed graves appearing near Knossos soon after were long read as an incoming Mycenaean military caste, though the newest study argues for Minoans of three ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan civilisation","questions":["Linear A, the undeciphered script of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was written from about 1800 to 1450 BCE and survives on fewer than two thousand documents, most of them from Crete.","Around 1470 BCE a wave of destructions ended the palace order of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and richly armed graves appeared near Knossos soon afterwards, long read as an incoming Mycenaean caste."],"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 2, 5–7, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]","Ester Salgarella and Simon Castellan, \"SigLA: The Signs of Linear A. A Palæographical Database,\" in <i>Grapholinguistics and Its Applications: Proceedings of Grapholinguistics in the 21st Century, Part II</i> (Brest: Fluxus Editions, 2020), https://doi.org/10.36824/2020-graf-salg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Knossos_women_fresco.jpg/1920px-Knossos_women_fresco.jpg","title":"Minoan civilisation","desc":"A restored Minoan wall painting from Knossos showing women in profile, their dark hair worked into heavy curls and their dress picked out in blue and white.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_women_fresco.jpg","alt":"Knossos women fresco"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Minoan civilisation","at":[25.1631,35.2981],"kind":"region","area":[[23.35,35.75],[24.5,35.95],[25.3,36.6],[25.6,36.6],[25.75,36.15],[26.55,35.45],[26.45,34.8],[25.5,34.75],[24.3,34.75],[23.35,35.05]]},"why":[{"q":"Why was Crete never one kingdom?","a":"Because its mountains cut the island into separate valleys and plains, each with its own town, so Minoans lived among small rival states that repeatedly destroyed each other's settlements."},{"q":"Why is storage read as the heart of a palace?","a":"Because Malia perhaps gives more of its ground floor to magazines than any other palace, and at three of the four the outer wall of those magazines is the building's most carefully built face."},{"q":"Why is the meaning of the graves near Knossos after 1470 BCE disputed?","a":"Because their rich armament was long read as an incoming Mycenaean military caste, and the newest study argues instead for Minoans of three ranks."}]},
{"id":"gr-007","num":"7","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Chasing a script he had spotted on Cretan seal stones, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travelled the island from 1894 and reported a pre-Phoenician writing in two phases, one pictorial and one linear.","answer":"Arthur Evans","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 July 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 July 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ashmolean</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Keeper, 1884 – 1908</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Excavated 1900 – 1931</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sir Arthur Evans</b>, son of the prehistorian Sir John Evans, gave Europe a Bronze Age civilisation nobody had known was there, and gave it the name it still carries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to Crete after a script rather than a palace, having met in Greece in 1893 small perforated stones cut with symbols that convinced him the Aegean had written long before the Greek alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Travelling the centre and east of the island from 1894, he was soon able to report a pre-Phoenician writing in two phases, one pictorial and one linear, which grew into the three scripts he named Cretan Hieroglyphic, Linear A and Linear B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The culture those scripts belonged to he called Minoan, after the legendary king Minos, and the word has framed every account of Bronze Age Crete since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From 1900 to 1931 he dug at Knossos, uncovering the largest palace site then known in the Aegean and opening a new chapter in the archaeology and art of the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He did not work alone, for Duncan Mackenzie ran the field archaeology, Theodore Fyfe and Christian Doll were his architects, and Piet de Jong drew the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His account of it, <i>The Palace of Minos</i>, ran to six volumes and an index, 3,108 pages and 2,433 images, a publisher's feat and by modern standards not an archaeological publication at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He worked in a Crete that had just become an autonomous state, and employed Christian and Muslim Cretans together at a time when the island was divided between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He paid for much of the digging himself, and in 1924 gave the site to the British School at Athens by deed of gift, with £6,800 endowed to keep a curator on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What most visitors see at Knossos now is his restoration work, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial, and which is argued over still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Arthur Evans","questions":["It was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who named the culture Minoan after the legendary king Minos, a word that has framed every account of Bronze Age Crete since.","The restorations that most visitors to Knossos take for the palace itself are the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Oxford museum he once kept calls them extensive and controversial."],"tags":["person","archaeology","history","greece"],"sources":["\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","\"The Sir Arthur Evans and Knossos Archives: Overview and Structure,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/archive/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Cretan Pictographs and Prae-Phoenician Script</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1895), 5–6, https://archive.org/details/cretanpictograph00evan. [Open access]","Arthur Evans, <i>The Palace of Minos: A Comparative Account of the Successive Stages of the Early Cretan Civilization as Illustrated by the Discoveries at Knossos</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Neolithic and Early and Middle Minoan Ages</i> (London: Macmillan, 1921), v–vi, https://archive.org/details/palaceofminoscom01evanuoft. [Open access]","\"Notable Donations and Legacies to the British School at Athens,\" British School at Athens, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/Legacy-and-Donation-Case-Studies.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Margaret_and_Arthur_Evans_22_June_1888.jpg","title":"Arthur Evans","desc":"Arthur Evans (1851 - 1941) with his wife Margaret (nee Freeman, 1848 - 1893) in 1888.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_and_Arthur_Evans_22_June_1888.jpg","alt":"Margaret and Arthur Evans 22 June 1888"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did Evans come to Crete in the first place?","a":"Because of a script rather than a palace: perforated stones cut with symbols, met in Greece in 1893, convinced him the Aegean had written long before the Greek alphabet."},{"q":"Why is The Palace of Minos not an archaeological publication by modern standards?","a":"Because although it runs to six volumes, 3,108 pages and 2,433 images, it is a work of synthesis and display rather than a record of the excavation."},{"q":"Why is what visitors see at Knossos argued over?","a":"Because most of it is his restoration work, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial."}]},
{"id":"gr-008","num":"8","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Standing on the low hill of Kephala six kilometres (four miles) inland from the north coast of Crete, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the largest Minoan palace and governed the whole of north-central Crete.","answer":"Knossos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6900 – 6600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Knossos</b> is the largest of the Minoan palaces, standing on the low hill of Kephala about six kilometres (four miles) inland from the north coast of Crete, above the stream Evans knew as the Kairatos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a mound as much as a palace, since an extensive Neolithic settlement lies beneath it, and modelled radiocarbon puts the first people on the hill between 6900 and 6600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the Bronze Age it governed north-central Crete, one of a set of separate states each holding its own valley or plain, with Mount Jouktas rising to the south as the peak sanctuary that served it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The palace covers about 13,000 square metres measured at one level, much of that carried up through two or more storeys, and everything in it is arranged around a paved central court 54 by 27 metres (177 by 89 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Along the west side of that court lay the cult rooms, and behind the west front the long storage magazines, so the two things the building most plainly did were worship and keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Above the magazines, on the floor later writers call the piano nobile, lay halls of state reached by broad stairs leading up from the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A banquet hall stood at the north end of the building, served by one staircase from the kitchens below and another from the courts, so dining was kept well away from audience and residence alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the southeast quarter were the residential quarters, suites of hall, forehall and lightwell with a more private room beyond, a sunken lustral basin and often a toilet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Outside the west front lay a paved court crossed by raised limestone causeways, with three great stone-lined pits sunk into it, and at the northwest corner the stepped theatral area where a crowd could stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The site draws over a million visitors a year, and the walls they walk between are largely Evans's restoration, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Knossos","questions":["Storage magazines behind the west front and cult rooms along the court are the two things <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> most plainly did, under halls of state reached by broad stairs.","Beneath the palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies a Neolithic settlement whose first occupation modelled radiocarbon dates to between 6900 and 6600 BCE, one of the earliest in the Aegean and in Europe."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 3–70, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Katerina Douka et al., \"Dating Knossos and the Arrival of the Earliest Neolithic in the Southern Aegean,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 91, no. 356 (2017): 304–21, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.29. [Paywalled]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 5–7, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Knossos_%E2%80%94_Throne_room_grass_fresco.jpg/1920px-Knossos_%E2%80%94_Throne_room_grass_fresco.jpg","title":"Knossos","desc":"Knossos — Throne room grass fresco. Gsimonov.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_%E2%80%94_Throne_room_grass_fresco.jpg","alt":"Knossos — Throne room grass fresco"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Knossos","at":[25.1631,35.2981]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the site a mound as much as a palace?","a":"Because an extensive Neolithic settlement lies beneath it, with modelled radiocarbon putting the first people on the hill between 6900 and 6600 BCE."},{"q":"Why do worship and storage stand out among what the building did?","a":"Because the cult rooms lie along the west side of the central court and the long storage magazines behind the west front, the two most plainly identifiable functions."},{"q":"Why was the banquet hall placed at the north end?","a":"Because it was served by one staircase from the kitchens below and another from the courts, keeping dining well away from audience and residence alike."}]},
{"id":"gr-009","num":"9","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Storage magazines filling the ground floor, halls of state above them and an empty paved court in the middle are the shape of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from about 1900 BCE.","answer":"Minoan palace","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1720 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kommos palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50 years in use</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Minoan palace</b> is a building set around a rectangular central court, the type that defines Crete from about 1900 BCE, the classic four being Knossos, Phaistos, Malia and Zakros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stand roughly one to a region, Knossos over north-central Crete, Phaistos over the Mesara plain, Chania in the west, the eastern valleys each with their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What a palace mostly did was store, since all four give much of their ground floor to magazines, and at three of them that magazine wall is the building's most carefully built face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above them lay halls of state reached by broad stairs from the court, and at the north end a banquet hall with separate stairs from the kitchens and from the public courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Suites of hall, forehall and lightwell, each with a private room and a sunken lustral basin beyond, recur in the same form at Knossos, Phaistos and Malia, and in villas and townhouses outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The court is usually laid north to south, and why is disputed: to light the colonnades along it, to aim its long axis at a sacred mountain, or to face the west-side cult rooms towards the sunrise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all daily activity happened in the rooms around that court rather than out on it, so the plan works outwards from an empty middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the middle was for is the live question, and the plastered court at Sissi, 450 square metres and holding ritual fittings, is read as ground where crowds ate and drank together at festivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There are also more of them than the handbooks suggest, on far more scales and not all founded or abandoned together, the courts at Kommos, Galatas, Gournia, Petras and Palaikastro running from 1,102 square metres down to 78, and the Kommos palace lasting fifty years at most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word palace is an inheritance rather than a description, because a Minoan court-centred building was not the monumental residence of a powerful ruler, and its excavators increasingly say so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan palace","questions":["Why the central court of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was usually laid north to south is disputed, whether to light its colonnades, aim at a sacred mountain, or face the cult rooms towards sunrise.","Excavators now often write court-centred building instead of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the ritual eating and drinking found at Sissi's court is the newest evidence for what such courts were for."],"tags":["building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jan Driessen, \"Revisiting the Minoan Palaces: Ritual Commensality at Sissi,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 95, no. 381 (2021): 686–704, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2021.30. [Paywalled]","Jan Driessen, \"Site History,\" Sissi Archaeological Project, Université catholique de Louvain, https://sarpedon.be/site/site-history/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, <i>Minoan Crete: An Introduction</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2021), 2, 5–7, https://assets.cambridge.org/97811084/40493/excerpt/9781108440493_excerpt.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Phaistos_Palace_Layout_Scan.jpg","title":"Minoan palace","desc":"This is a scanned picture of the layout of the Phaistos palace from the book, Crete: The Forerunner of Greece by Charles Henry Hawes (1867-1943) and Harriet Boyd Hawes (1871-1945). The book (fourth edition) was published in 1922 by Harper & Brothers. The first edition was published in 1909.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaistos_Palace_Layout_Scan.jpg","alt":"Phaistos Palace Layout Scan"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the word palace an inheritance rather than a description?","a":"Because a Minoan court-centred building was not the monumental residence of a powerful ruler, and its excavators increasingly say so."},{"q":"Why is the orientation of the central court disputed?","a":"Because the usual north-south layout has been explained as lighting the colonnades, as aiming the long axis at a sacred mountain, and as facing the west-side cult rooms towards the sunrise."},{"q":"Why do the handbooks understate how many there were?","a":"Because more exist on far more scales and not all founded or abandoned together — the courts at Kommos, Galatas, Gournia, Petras and Palaikastro run from 1,102 square metres down to 78."}]},
{"id":"gr-010","num":"10","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A gypsum seat 1.06 metres (3 ft 6 in) tall, hollowed to fit a body and raised 21 centimetres (8 inches) above the benches around it, is what gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name.","answer":"Throne Room at Knossos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Throne Room at Knossos</b> is a small chamber on the west side of the palace's central court, at its north end, among the rooms given over to cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against one wall stands a gypsum seat with a high back of undulating leaf-shaped outline, 1.06 metres (3 ft 6 in) tall in all, its seat hollowed to fit a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gypsum benches run round the other walls with their tops 21 centimetres (8 inches) below that seat, so whoever sat in the chair sat just above everyone else in the room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Facing it across the floor is a sunken tank of closely fitted gypsum slabs, nearly three metres (10 feet) long, reached by six steps and with no outlet at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Painted walls closed the room in, a landscape of running water, rushes, hills and palms, and on either side of the inner doorway two couchant griffins facing outward in the position of guardians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The room is later than the palace around it, belonging to the last palace epoch, which opens as the Neopalatial age closes about 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That is the age in which Knossos alone went on working as a palace, those at Phaistos, Malia and Zakros having been destroyed and never rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who held it then is not settled, the candidates being a Mycenaean conqueror, a viceroy installed by one, and a Minoan lord keeping his place with Mycenaean mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Richly furnished graves appear around Knossos at the same time, their men buried with weapons and hoards of bronze vessels in a manner new to Crete and long familiar on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The griffin fresco belongs to that period, and its heraldic stiffness is among the art read as evidence of Mainland Greeks at Knossos, though much of what a visitor sees in the room now is Evans's restoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Throne Room at Knossos","questions":["Two couchant griffins faced outward beside the inner doorway of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the position of guardians, over walls painted with water, rushes and palms.","The whole arrangement of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to the latest palace epoch at Knossos, and its griffin fresco is read as evidence of Mainland Greeks ruling on Crete."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 35–41, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Arthur Evans, <i>The Palace of Minos: A Comparative Account of the Successive Stages of the Early Cretan Civilization as Illustrated by the Discoveries at Knossos</i>, vol. 1, <i>The Neolithic and Early and Middle Minoan Ages</i> (London: Macmillan, 1921), 204, https://archive.org/details/palaceofminoscom01evanuoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Knossos_07.JPG/1920px-Knossos_07.JPG","title":"Throne Room at Knossos","desc":"The throne room of the palace at Knossos, uncovered in April 1900, with restored red and white wall painting running round it and a sunken basin cut into the floor in front.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_07.JPG","alt":"Knossos 07"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Knossos","at":[25.1631,35.2981]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the seat put its occupant above the room?","a":"Because the gypsum benches round the other walls have their tops 21 centimetres below the seat, so whoever sat in the chair sat just above everyone else."},{"q":"Why is the room later than the palace around it?","a":"Because it belongs to the last palace epoch, opening as the Neopalatial age closes about 1470 BCE, when Knossos alone went on working as a palace."},{"q":"Why is it disputed who used it?","a":"Because the candidates are a Mycenaean conqueror, a viceroy installed by one, and a Minoan lord keeping his place with Mycenaean mercenaries."}]},
{"id":"gr-011","num":"11","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The builders of the New Palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set its west façade far east of the old one, which is why an Old Palace façade, west court and theatral area still lie open.","answer":"Phaistos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-2nd century BCE, by Gortyn</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phaistos</b> is a Minoan palace on a hill above the great valley of the Mesara in southern Crete, with the sea and the ancient harbour lying away to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its central court is the longest of any Minoan palace at 63 metres (207 feet) from north to south, though at 22.5 metres (74 feet) across it holds slightly less ground than the court at Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavators stress that the building never had the plan usually credited to Cretan palaces, a court closed on four sides: it was a western block, a southern retaining wall and separate buildings round the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> West of the main façade lies a large paved court crossed by causeways of limestone slabs raised a little above the cobbles, with a theatral area of steps at its north end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A grand flight of well-joined limestone slabs about 13.5 metres (44 feet) wide climbs into the building,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the main storerooms run north to south either side of a broad corridor just inside the monumental west entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first palace was built in Middle Minoan IB, about 1900 BCE, and the Protopalatial period closed at the end of Middle Minoan IIB in a destruction horizon probably caused by an earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What sets the site apart is how much of that first palace is still visible, because the builders of the New Palace put their western façade well east of the old western margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The old west façade, the west court before it, the theatral area and much of the west wing were left in the open rather than buried under the later building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By far the richest assortment of polychrome Kamares ware comes from the palace here, and the Mesara is arguably where that pottery reached its technical and aesthetic height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second palace was destroyed at the close of the Neopalatial age and never rebuilt, though the hill was lived on almost without a break into the Hellenistic age, until Gortyn destroyed the city in the mid-2nd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phaistos","questions":["At 63 metres (207 feet) north to south, the central court of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the longest of any Minoan palace, though its area falls just short of the court at Knossos.","By far the richest assortment of polychrome Kamares ware comes from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the Mesara plain of southern Crete is arguably where that style reached its technical height."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 45–46, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","\"Festòs,\" Centro di Archeologia Cretese, Università degli Studi di Catania, https://www.cac.unict.it/festos. [Open access]","Keith Branigan, \"Phaistos,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=phaistos. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Archaeological_site_Phaistos_detail.jpg/1920px-Archaeological_site_Phaistos_detail.jpg","title":"Phaistos","desc":"Detail of the archaeological site of Phaistos, Crete, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_site_Phaistos_detail.jpg","alt":"Archaeological site Phaistos detail"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Phaistos","at":[24.8136,35.0514]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the palace never have the plan usually credited to Cretan palaces?","a":"Because it was a western block, a southern retaining wall and separate buildings round the court, rather than a court closed on four sides."},{"q":"Why is so much of the first palace still visible here?","a":"Because the builders of the New Palace put their western façade well east of the old western margin, leaving the old west façade, its court and the theatral area in the open."},{"q":"Why is the Mesara associated with Kamares ware?","a":"Because by far the richest assortment of the polychrome pottery comes from this palace, and the plain is arguably where that ware reached its technical and aesthetic height."}]},
{"id":"gr-012","num":"12","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 300 metres (980 feet) west of the palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood Quartier Mu, the largest complex known on Crete from the age of the first palaces, sealed by a violent fire.","answer":"Malia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Quartier Mu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Malia</b> is a Minoan palace and the town about it, on the north coast of Crete, where people were living from the Early Minoan II period long before any palace stood there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its central court measures 48 by 23 metres (157 by 75 feet) and the palace covers some 7,600 square metres (81,800 square feet), the largest of the Cretan palaces after Knossos, though very few frescoes have been found in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More of its ground floor goes to storage than in any other palace, with magazines inside the west façade, in the east wing and in the north-east quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eight shallow kouloures in two rows of four sit in a walled enclosure recessed between the west court and the southern end of the west façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The central court was laid out for ceremony, with a pit dug at its middle, perhaps for sacrifices, a limestone ball bearing one cup-mark planted to the north-west, and a polished stone with cup-marks set into the southern paving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first palace was raised at the opening of the Protopalatial period, about 1900 BCE, and was destroyed at the end of Middle Minoan IIB by a cause that is still unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A genuine town grew up around it, and 300 metres (980 feet) to the west stood Quartier Mu, the largest complex known on Crete from the age of the first palaces, more than 3,000 square metres (32,000 square feet) of it uncovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two large buildings stood there among five small craftsmen's houses — a seal-cutter's, a potter's, a smelter's — and a violent fire about 1700 BCE sealed the ruins with their contents inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those two are among the few buildings outside the palaces to yield archives in Cretan hieroglyphic, and some 150 soapstone seals from the workshop define a manner of engraving called the Malia style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second palace was built over the first, destroyed in Late Minoan I and never raised again, while the monumental enclosure at Chrysolakkos, perhaps the tomb of the palace's occupants, produced the famous gold bee pendant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Malia","questions":["More of the ground floor is given over to storage at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than at any other Cretan palace, and eight shallow kouloures sit in a walled enclosure beside its west court.","The central court at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a pit dug at its middle, a limestone ball planted to the north-west and a polished stone with cup-marks set into the paving at its south."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"5a. Malia – The Palace,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-palais-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Malia_Palace%2C_Crete%2C_Sept_2019_a.jpg/1920px-Malia_Palace%2C_Crete%2C_Sept_2019_a.jpg","title":"Malia","desc":"Minoan Palace at Malia, Crete, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malia_Palace,_Crete,_Sept_2019_a.jpg","alt":"Malia Palace, Crete, Sept 2019 a"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Malia","at":[25.4647,35.2833]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the central court look laid out for ceremony?","a":"Because a pit was dug at its middle, perhaps for sacrifices, with a cup-marked limestone ball planted to the north-west and a polished stone with cup-marks set into the southern paving."},{"q":"Why is Quartier Mu so informative?","a":"Because a violent fire about 1700 BCE sealed its ruins with their contents inside, preserving two large buildings among five craftsmen's houses — a seal-cutter's, a potter's, a smelter's."},{"q":"Why do those buildings matter for the study of writing?","a":"Because they are among the few outside the palaces to yield archives in Cretan hieroglyphic."}]},
{"id":"gr-013","num":"13","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Nearly 500 clay nodules stamped with seal impressions, most bearing two or three faces, came out of one room of a house on the shore at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the easternmost Minoan palace.","answer":"Zakros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1470 BCE, in Late Minoan IB</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zakros</b> is a Minoan palace and harbour town at the eastern end of Crete, on a delta where the Zakro river escapes from a gorge about 8 kilometres (5 miles) long, beside a natural port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That port was reckoned the last on the directest sea route from the Aegean to the shore of Cyrenaica in North Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palace is much the smallest of the four, covering about 2,800 square metres (30,000 square feet) where Knossos covers some 13,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its central court measures 29 by 12 metres (95 by 39 feet) and is aligned north-east to south-west, where the courts of the others run north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Major magazines are not a feature of it, only a group of lesser storerooms in the north-west corner, and the halls usually put on an upper floor sit here at ground level in the east half of the west wing, fronting straight onto the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Rooms west of the central court seem to have held the cult equipment, and the settlement belongs to the small class of Minoan towns with a palace at the middle ringed by large houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the spur above the shore stood a building its excavator called perhaps the most significant domestic structure then uncovered on any Aegean site, and took for the residence of a local chief or governor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of its rooms held nearly 500 well-preserved clay nodules stamped with seal impressions, most of them bearing two or three faces and plainly once attached to documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With them lay a small tablet of fine red clay inscribed in the linear script of Knossos, and two countersigns pressed into its rim with a signet showing wild goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper came ashore as oxhide ingots, Zakros being one of the Cretan findspots for them in a trade reaching Cyprus and the Syrian coast, and the palace was destroyed about 1470 BCE in the Late Minoan IB horizon that took virtually every Minoan site of consequence, and never rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zakros","questions":["The smallest of the Minoan palaces, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a central court of 29 by 12 metres (95 by 39 feet) aligned north-east to south-west where the others run north to south.","Copper came ashore as oxhide ingots at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose port was reckoned the last on the directest sea route from the Aegean to the North African shore."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["D. G. Hogarth, \"Excavations at Zakro, Crete,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 121–49, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Palace_of_Zakros_ruins.jpg/1920px-Palace_of_Zakros_ruins.jpg","title":"Zakros","desc":"Palace of Zakros ruins, Eastern Crete.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_of_Zakros_ruins.jpg","alt":"The excavated palace at Zakros seen from above, its central court and the rooms around it"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Zakros","at":[26.2614,35.0981]},"why":[{"q":"Why did so small a palace matter?","a":"Because its port was reckoned the last on the directest sea route from the Aegean to the shore of Cyrenaica, and copper came ashore there as oxhide ingots in a trade reaching Cyprus and the Syrian coast."},{"q":"Why is its plan unusual among the four?","a":"Because its court runs north-east to south-west where the others run north to south, major magazines are absent, and the halls usually put upstairs sit at ground level fronting straight onto the court."},{"q":"Why do the clay nodules from the house above the shore matter?","a":"Because nearly 500 of them are stamped with seal impressions and were plainly once attached to documents, found with a tablet in the linear script of Knossos."}]},
{"id":"gr-014","num":"14","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Some fifty well-preserved houses along cobbled streets, with a central court and a small palace among them, make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most extensively cleared Neopalatial town on Crete.","answer":"Gournia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Minoan IB, c. 1490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Gournia</b> is a Minoan town on the north coast of the Isthmus of Ierapetra in eastern Crete, and the most extensively cleared town of the Neopalatial period on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some fifty well-preserved houses stand along a system of cobbled streets, with a central court, a small palace and a cemetery among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A large open court lies on the south side of the hill's crest, resembling the central courts of the great palaces, and north of it stands a much-eroded building with palatial features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its principal façade faces west onto a small paved court and is built of cut blocks of poros limestone, with storage magazines within and setbacks along its front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Near the summit of the hill is a small rectangular shrine of a single room, approached from the west by a cobbled street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The place belongs to the class of Minoan settlements built around one central main building, sometimes in the form of a palace, with a town of small buildings about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came out of its houses is the furniture of ordinary life: household goods, tools and weapons, vessels, jewellery and objects of cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town was found in May 1901 and cleared in three seasons by a workforce of about a hundred local men and ten women, and what came out of it is of Middle Minoan III to Late Minoan I date, roughly 1750 to 1490 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the shore below, later work has identified a monumental shipshed, two sets of fortification walls with towers, a cobbled street running up to the town, a gate by the river, two dams at its mouth and stretches of Minoan agricultural terracing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town went down in the Late Minoan IB destructions, the horizon that took virtually every Minoan residential site of consequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Gournia","questions":["North of the open court at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood a building faced west in cut poros limestone, and near the summit a shrine of one room reached by a cobbled street.","A monumental shipshed, walls with towers and two dams at a river mouth belong to the shore below <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Minoan town on the Isthmus of Ierapetra in eastern Crete."],"tags":["place","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Excavation History,\" Gournia Excavation Project, University at Buffalo, https://www.gournia.org/excavation-history.php. [Open access]","\"Gournia Excavations,\" Gournia Excavation Project, University at Buffalo, https://www.gournia.org/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","L. Vance Watrous, \"The Harbor Complex of the Minoan Town at Gournia,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 116, no. 3 (2012): 521–41, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.116.3.0521. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Gournia_024.jpg/1920px-Gournia_024.jpg","title":"Gournia","desc":"A paved street of the Minoan town of Gournia in eastern Crete, running between house walls that survive to about knee height.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gournia_024.jpg","alt":"Gournia 024"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Gournia","at":[25.7889,35.1056]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Gournia so valuable for understanding Minoan life?","a":"Because it is the most extensively cleared town of the Neopalatial period, some fifty well-preserved houses along cobbled streets, holding the household goods, tools, vessels and cult objects of ordinary life."},{"q":"Why is the town read as belonging to a wider Minoan pattern?","a":"Because it is built around one central main building, here in the form of a small palace, with a town of small buildings about it."},{"q":"Why has the shore below changed the picture of the site?","a":"Because later work found a monumental shipshed, two sets of fortification walls with towers, a street up to the town, a gate, two dams and Minoan agricultural terracing."}]},
{"id":"gr-015","num":"15","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The rise of the first Cretan palaces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as redistribution, as élites herding people off their land, as farmers insuring each other, and as the wealth of trade.","answer":"Protopalatial period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1925 – 1720 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Protopalatial period</b> is the age of the first Cretan palaces, running from Middle Minoan IB to the end of Middle Minoan IIB, roughly 1900 to 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its absolute dates are soft at both ends, given as 1925 or 1900 for the opening and 1750 or 1720 for the close, and individual excavations work with figures of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first certain palaces are built at Knossos and Phaistos, and at Malia a genuine town grows up around the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Potters take up the fast wheel and thin their vessel walls until the best Kamares ware earns the name eggshell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing appears with it: a pictographic or hieroglyphic script from Middle Minoan IA, and Linear A from about Middle Minoan IB to IIA, read from left to right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hilltops and caves show unambiguous evidence of cult for the first time, and close contacts with the eastern Peloponnese now extend on a similar scale to the central Aegean islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Away from the palaces the record is best at Quartier Mu in Malia, where two large buildings stood among five craftsmen's houses holding seal-cutters, potters and a smelter, and where hieroglyphic archives were kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why palaces appeared at all is argued rather than settled: Renfrew read them as redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and wine, and Gamble as the work of élites driving people into large settlements away from their fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Halstead saw instead an outgrowth of farmers trading surplus grain to insure one another against bad years, needing no central authority at all, while van Andel and Runnels put the weight on trade, shipping and metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period ends at Knossos and Phaistos in a major destruction horizon, probably an earthquake, and that horizon is what defines the close of the Old Palace age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Protopalatial period","questions":["The fast wheel, eggshell-thin Kamares ware and the first Cretan writing all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which runs from Middle Minoan IB to the end of Middle Minoan IIB.","A major destruction horizon at Knossos and Phaistos, probably an earthquake, is what defines the close of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the age of the first Cretan palaces."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/MM_II_relief-_and_applique-decorated_cup_from_Malia_-_Herakleion_AM_19814_-_01.jpg/1920px-MM_II_relief-_and_applique-decorated_cup_from_Malia_-_Herakleion_AM_19814_-_01.jpg","title":"Protopalatial period","desc":"A pale Minoan clay cup from Malia with no handles, its surface worked in relief and applied ornament, of the Middle Minoan II period.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MM_II_relief-_and_applique-decorated_cup_from_Malia_-_Herakleion_AM_19814_-_01.jpg","alt":"MM II relief- and applique-decorated cup from Malia - Herakleion AM 19814 - 01"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the reason palaces appeared still argued rather than settled?","a":"Because Renfrew read them as redistributive centres, Gamble as élites driving people into large settlements, Halstead as an outgrowth of farmers insuring one another with surplus grain, and van Andel and Runnels as a matter of trade and metals."},{"q":"Why are the period's absolute dates soft?","a":"Because the opening is given as 1925 or 1900 and the close as 1750 or 1720, and individual excavations work with figures of their own."},{"q":"Why is Quartier Mu the best record away from the palaces?","a":"Because two large buildings stood there among five craftsmen's houses holding seal-cutters, potters and a smelter, and hieroglyphic archives were kept in them."}]},
{"id":"gr-016","num":"16","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The villa, a large and sumptuously appointed building that is neither a palace nor an ordinary house, is new in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and has no clear Protopalatial counterpart.","answer":"Neopalatial period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1720 – 1490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neopalatial period</b> is the age of the second Cretan palaces, running from Middle Minoan IIIA to the end of Late Minoan IB, roughly 1750 to 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its dates are given variously, from 1750 or 1720 for the opening to 1490 or 1470 for the close, and a single excavation may work with figures of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> New palaces are raised over the ruins of the old at Knossos, Phaistos and Malia, each still built around a central court with a paved west court outside its main façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A class of building appears that the age before it had not: the villa, large and sumptuously appointed, neither a palace nor an ordinary house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Villas stand alone in the countryside at Vathypetro and Sklavokampos, at the centre of villages, in clumps of their own, and among the mansions ringing the great palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pier-and-door partitions, lustral basins and the paired halls of a residential quarter recur through this architecture from one end of the island to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same forms travel: pottery above all, whether imported or copied, but also Linear A, frescoes, bronze statuettes and those very partitions and basins turn up across the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thera, Melos and Kea are the most thoroughly Minoanised of the islands, Phylakopi on Melos having yielded both frescoes and Linear A inscriptions, while Kythera and Trianda on Rhodes look like colonies settled by Minoans and their descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether that reach was colonisation, a sea empire or simply trade and imitation is argued, and it need not imply any Minoan army at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The age ends in the Late Minoan IB destructions, which took virtually every Minoan residential site of consequence, palaces and villa complexes and towns and isolated villas alike, and left Knossos alone still working as a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Neopalatial period","questions":["Pottery, Linear A, frescoes, bronze statuettes, pier-and-door partitions and lustral basins carry the forms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the Aegean, to Thera, Melos, Kea, Kythera and Rhodes.","The Late Minoan IB destructions close the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taking palaces, villa complexes, towns and isolated villas alike, and leaving Knossos alone still working as a palace."],"tags":["era","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/The_Parisian%2C_fresco%2C_Knossos%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Neopalatial period","desc":"The \"Parisian\", fresco, Knossos, Greece. Neopalatial period 1350 BCE. Herakleion Archaeological Museum. Height: 25 cm.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Parisian,_fresco,_Knossos,_Greece.jpg","alt":"The Parisian, fresco, Knossos, Greece"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does the villa mark this age off from the one before?","a":"Because it is a class of building the earlier period had not: large and sumptuously appointed, neither a palace nor an ordinary house, standing alone in the countryside, in villages and among the mansions ringing the palaces."},{"q":"Why is the Minoan reach across the Aegean disputed?","a":"Because whether it was colonisation, a sea empire or simply trade and imitation is argued, and it need not imply any Minoan army at all."},{"q":"Why is the Late Minoan IB horizon so sharp a break?","a":"Because it took virtually every Minoan residential site of consequence — palaces, villa complexes, towns and isolated villas alike — leaving Knossos alone still working as a palace."}]},
{"id":"gr-017","num":"17","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Rectangular cists lined with lead sheeting lie sunk in the floors among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Knossos, one range under another in the eighth magazine, and every one was found empty.","answer":"Palace storerooms and pithoi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"Minoan <b>palace storerooms and pithoi</b> go together: through both palace ages, from about 1900 to 1470 BCE, every Cretan palace gives much of its ground floor to magazines filled with rows of huge jars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Knossos they open west off a long north-south corridor in the west wing, kept almost entirely to the strip inside the west façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That corridor, Evans's Long Gallery, runs 53 metres (174 feet) and is about 3.4 metres (11 feet) wide, with limestone door-jambs down its western side and thirteen magazines opening off it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The jars stood thickly: over twenty in the fifth magazine, twelve of them more or less whole, nine in a row along the south wall of the fourth, and twelve from the third with burnt grain still in some.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Phaistos runs its magazines either side of a broad corridor, Malia gives more ground-floor space to storage than any other palace and keeps rooms in the east wing and north-east quarter besides, and Zakros has no major magazines at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Sunk into the paved floors at Knossos are rectangular cists lined with lead sheeting, six in a row down the fourth magazine, six more in the fifth and five in the seventh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the eighth the arrangement is doubled, one range of cists beneath another, the lower reachable only by lifting the whole pavement above, the two together 1.3 metres (4 feet 3 inches) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans found them empty, and concluded that receptacles hidden so carefully, under a pavement solid enough to carry the jars standing on it, were meant for the safe-keeping of things of value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Outside the magazines stand kouloures, large half-sunken cylinders of rubble, several at Malia keeping a central base to carry a raised floor and so keep dry the grain they probably held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What all that capacity was for is argued rather than settled: Renfrew's reading of the palaces as redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and wine makes the storerooms its machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palace storerooms and pithoi","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Knossos open west off a Long Gallery 53 metres (174 feet) long, and over twenty jars were counted in the fifth magazine alone.","Every Cretan palace devotes a large part of its ground floor to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but Malia gives over more of it than any other and Zakros keeps almost no major magazines at all."],"tags":["object","building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 19–25, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","\"5a. Malia – The Palace,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-palais-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg","title":"Palace storerooms and pithoi","desc":"Orientalizing pithos. Found in Arcades (?), Crete, ca. 675 BCE. Terracotta, stamped and incised decoration.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg","alt":"Pithos Louvre CA4523"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did Evans think the lead-lined cists held valuables?","a":"Because they were hidden so carefully — sunk under a pavement solid enough to carry the jars standing on it, one range beneath another under the eighth magazine — that safe-keeping was the plausible purpose."},{"q":"Why do the kouloures at Malia keep a central base?","a":"Because it carried a raised floor, which would keep dry the grain those half-sunken cylinders probably held."},{"q":"Why is all that storage capacity still argued over?","a":"Because it is the machinery of Renfrew's reading of the palaces as redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and wine, and that reading is not settled."}]},
{"id":"gr-018","num":"18","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Copper reached Crete under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as oxhide ingots weighing about 20 kilograms (45 pounds) apiece, cast from ore that was mined on Cyprus.","answer":"Minoan palace economy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In operation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan palace economy</b> is what the Cretan palaces did with goods: they gathered staples in bulk, kept a record of them, put craftsmen to work and brought in what the island could not supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every palace gives much of its ground floor to magazines, and beside them stand kouloures, large half-sunken cylinders of rubble that probably held grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What could not be grown or dug at home was bought: gold, silver and electrum, ivory from both elephant and hippopotamus, ostrich eggs, tin, and the spices that went into perfumed oils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Copper arrived as oxhide ingots averaging 0.60 by 0.45 metres (24 by 18 inches) and about 4 centimetres (1.6 inches) thick, each weighing some 20 kilograms (45 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They turn up at Palaikastro, Zakros, Mochlos, Knossos, Kommos and Ayia Triada, and the metal in those tested was mined on Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Craft was organised as well as supplied, and at Quartier Mu in Malia two large buildings stood among five workshop houses, one kept by a seal-cutter, one by a potter, one by a smelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some 150 soapstone seals from that workshop are enough to define a manner of engraving of their own, and the buildings beside it held hieroglyphic archives, so writing and accounting were not the palaces' alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The accounting itself survives in clay: nearly 500 nodules stamped with seal impressions lay in one room of a house at Zakros, most bearing two or three faces and plainly once fixed to documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Knossos a file of inscribed tablets lay on the floor of the eighth magazine, the remains, it seems, of a gypsum coffer, and the figures added to them appear to refer to bronze single-edged axes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the whole adds up to is argued rather than settled, one reading making the palaces redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and vine, another putting the weight on trade and on wool, cheese and hides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan palace economy","questions":["Nearly 500 clay nodules at Zakros and a file of inscribed tablets at Knossos are the surviving paperwork of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which counted what it stored.","Five workshop houses at Quartier Mu, one each for a seal-cutter, a potter and a smelter, show how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> organised craft as well as supplying it."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 24–25, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","D. G. Hogarth, \"Excavations at Zakro, Crete,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 132–34, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Palace_of_Malia_-_Pillar_Crypt.jpg/1920px-Palace_of_Malia_-_Pillar_Crypt.jpg","title":"Minoan palace economy","desc":"The Pillar Crypt in the Palace of Malia, Crete, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palace_of_Malia_-_Pillar_Crypt.jpg","alt":"Palace of Malia - Pillar Crypt"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the palaces have to buy so much?","a":"Because gold, silver, electrum, ivory, ostrich eggs, tin and the spices for perfumed oils could not be grown or dug on Crete, and the copper arriving as oxhide ingots was mined on Cyprus."},{"q":"Why is writing not shown to be a palace monopoly?","a":"Because Quartier Mu at Malia, a quarter of workshops, held hieroglyphic archives alongside a seal-cutter's, a potter's and a smelter's house."},{"q":"Why is the shape of the economy disputed?","a":"Because one reading makes the palaces redistributive centres for a countryside specialising in oil and vine, another puts the weight on trade and on wool, cheese and hides."}]},
{"id":"gr-019","num":"19","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The signary of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs to 135 signs, 45 of them known only as cut seal types, 43 only in scratched forms on clay, and 45 in both.","answer":"Cretan hieroglyphic script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appears</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IA, c. 2050 – 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IIIB, c. 1700 – 1675 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cretan hieroglyphic script</b> is the earlier of the two writing systems of Bronze Age Crete, a pictorial one that appears in Middle Minoan IA, about 2050 to 2000 BCE, and runs on into Middle Minoan IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its life therefore covers the whole age of the first palaces and the opening of the second, ending about 1700 to 1675 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The signs are pictures and the script has an overall glyptic character, which is to say it was made to be cut into stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest examples are on Middle Minoan I seals with three or four sides, and later texts appear on clay bars, medallions, cones, noduli and sealings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surviving texts are few and very short, and they come from Knossos, Phaistos and Malia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> There is no uniformity in the direction of writing, and beyond the numerals, which run on a decimal system, the script is undeciphered and likely to remain so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arthur Evans came to it in 1889, when a four-sided cornelian bead-seal cut with figures on every face reached the Ashmolean Museum, wrongly said to be from Sparta, and he read it at once as a conventionalised system of picture-writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1909 he could set out a signary of 135 signs, of which 45 were known only as cut seal types, 43 only in the scratched forms on clay, and 45 in both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The elongated bead-seals, cut as three- or four-sided prisms, could take whole lines of inscription, and both they and the small signets fell out of use as the script ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was no palace monopoly either: the buildings of Quartier Mu at Malia, a quarter of workshops that included a seal-cutter's, are among the few outside a palace to have yielded hieroglyphic archives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cretan hieroglyphic script","questions":["Appearing in Middle Minoan IA and running on into Middle Minoan IIIB, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut above all on seal-stones of three or four sides.","About a third of Linear A's signs closely resemble those of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and beyond its decimal numerals that older system is undeciphered and likely to remain so."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 5, 235, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","\"5b. Malia – The Mu Quarter,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/malia-le-quartier-mu-2/?lang=en. [Open access]","\"4. Malia – Historical Discussion,\" École française d'Athènes, https://www.efa.gr/presentation-historique-3/?lang=en. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/The_palace_of_Minos_-_a_comparative_account_of_the_successive_stages_of_the_early_Cretan_civilization_as_illustrated_by_the_discoveries_at_Knossos_%281921%29_%2814771199524%29.jpg","title":"Cretan hieroglyphic script","desc":"A printed table of Cretan hieroglyphic signs, drawn up for Arthur Evans's The Palace of Minos and published in 1921.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_palace_of_Minos_-_a_comparative_account_of_the_successive_stages_of_the_early_Cretan_civilization_as_illustrated_by_the_discoveries_at_Knossos_(1921)_(14771199524).jpg","alt":"The palace of Minos - a comparative account of the successive stages of the early Cretan civilization as illustrated by the discoveries at Knossos (1921)"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the script called glyptic in character?","a":"Because its signs are pictures made to be cut into stone, the earliest examples being three- and four-sided Middle Minoan I seals."},{"q":"Why is it likely to remain undeciphered?","a":"Because the surviving texts are few and very short, there is no uniformity in the direction of writing, and beyond the decimal numerals nothing can be read."},{"q":"Why could Evans build a signary of 135 signs?","a":"Because he could set 45 known only as cut seal types beside 43 known only in scratched forms on clay and 45 appearing in both."}]},
{"id":"gr-020","num":"20","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Of Linear B's syllabic signs, 45 have close equivalents in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and ten more have doubtful parallels, so many can be given a sound.","answer":"Linear A","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appears</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IIA, perhaps MM IB, c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Minoan I, to c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Linear A</b> is the writing of the Cretan palaces, and the early texts called Proto-Linear from the first palace at Phaistos push its appearance back to Middle Minoan IIA or perhaps to Middle Minoan IB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It never appears on seals, has a general graphic character, reads uniformly from left to right, and makes extensive use of ligatures, which are signs combined into one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About a third of its signs closely resemble the older pictographic forms, though the two scripts now look virtually contemporary rather than one growing out of the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Texts occur most often on clay tablets, but also on six stone libation tables, spoon-shaped mortars, a doorjamb, a gold ring, a miniature gold axe, silver and gold pins, a bronze tablet and a fair number of bronze ingots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two cups from Knossos carry inscriptions written round the inside in cuttlefish ink with a reed pen, before the clay was fired, and a house at Zakros held a small tablet of fine red clay inscribed in the same script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It turns up at some twenty sites, and only three places outside Crete have produced true texts: Ayia Irini on Kea, Phylakopi on Melos and Akrotiri on Thera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The latest inscriptions are no later than Late Minoan I, which closes about 1470 BCE, so the script goes down with the palaces it served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The name is Arthur Evans's: in 1909 he set out the linear writing of Crete as two classes, A and B, and the labels have stuck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of Linear B's syllabic signs, 45 have close equivalents in the older script and ten more have doubtful parallels, so a great many of its signs can be given a sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is as far as it goes, because Linear B was derived from Linear A in order to write a different, non-Minoan language, which is why deciphering the younger script in 1952 did not unlock the elder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Linear A","questions":["Six stone libation tables, a doorjamb, a gold ring, silver and gold pins and cuttlefish ink inside a cup all carry <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is found mostly on clay tablets.","Crete's linear writing falls into two classes, and it is the older of them, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that appears at some twenty sites across the island and is still unread."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 28–29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","D. G. Hogarth, \"Excavations at Zakro, Crete,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 133, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Linear_A_cup.png","title":"Linear A","desc":"Linear A. Ink-witten inscriptions round the inner surface of cup (Third Middle Minoan).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_A_cup.png","alt":"Linear A cup"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did deciphering Linear B not unlock Linear A?","a":"Because the younger script was derived from the elder in order to write a different, non-Minoan language, so the sound values carry across while the words do not."},{"q":"Why can many Linear A signs nevertheless be given a sound?","a":"Because 45 of Linear B's syllabic signs have close equivalents in the older script and ten more have doubtful parallels."},{"q":"Why does the script end when it does?","a":"Because the latest inscriptions are no later than Late Minoan I, closing about 1470 BCE, so it goes down with the palaces it served."}]},
{"id":"gr-021","num":"21","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Every sign was pressed into the wet clay of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a punch, the same stamp each time, so just 45 stamps produced all 241 characters on it.","answer":"Phaistos Disc","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IIIB, c. 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Phaistos Disc</b> is a fired clay disc stamped on both faces with pictographic signs, from the palace at Phaistos in southern Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not perfectly round, its diameter running from 158 to 165 millimetres (6.2 to 6.5 inches) and its thickness from 16 to 21 millimetres (0.6 to 0.8 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every character was stamped in relief with a punch while the clay was still wet, each variety of sign always by the same stamp, so that 45 stamps were used in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That makes it in effect a printed inscription, the punches taken for hard wood or ivory by one of its first students and for metal cast in matrices of engraved steatite by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Face A carries 31 sign-groups and 123 signs, face B 30 groups and 118, making 241 characters in 61 groups, the inscription spiralling between rim and centre on each face and closed on each side by a line of five punctuations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The signs stand in groups of two to seven divided by incised lines, and the spiral is read from the outside inward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It lay in a small room at the north-eastern edge of the site, in dark earth mixed with ash, charcoal and potsherds, having fallen from a floor above with a fragment of a clay tablet in the Minoan linear script beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The context is Middle Minoan IIIB, so the disc belongs to the years around 1700 BCE, and one of the excavators judged its clay not to be Cretan at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phaistos itself was a palace of two lives, the first raised in Middle Minoan IB and thrown down by an earthquake at the end of MM IIB, the second rebuilt in MM IIIA, with a settlement of some 50 hectares (124 acres) around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its 45 signs suggest a syllabary rather than an alphabet, it has no close parallel on Crete and is probably an import, and it is undeciphered, an approximation to printing that one scholar called 'immense in potentiality but null in effect, a freak'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phaistos Disc","questions":["The inscription on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spirals between rim and centre on each face, 61 sign-groups in all, each face closed off by a line of five punctuations.","With 45 different signs and no close parallel on Crete, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is probably a syllabary, probably an import, and still undeciphered."],"tags":["object","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Luigi Pernier, \"Il disco di Phaestos con caratteri pittografici,\" <i>Ausonia</i> 3 (1908): 255ff, https://archive.org/details/ausonia03sociuoft. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 22–24, 273–74, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Festòs,\" Centro di Archeologia Cretese, Università degli Studi di Catania, https://www.cac.unict.it/festos. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Phaistos_Disc_%E2%80%94_Side_A.jpg/1920px-Phaistos_Disc_%E2%80%94_Side_A.jpg","title":"Phaistos Disc","desc":"Phaistos Disc — Side A. Gleb Simonov.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaistos_Disc_%E2%80%94_Side_A.jpg","alt":"Phaistos Disc — Side A"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the disc in effect a printed inscription?","a":"Because every character was stamped in relief with a punch while the clay was still wet, each variety of sign always by the same stamp, so 45 stamps were used in all."},{"q":"Why is it thought to be an import?","a":"Because it has no close parallel on Crete, and one of its excavators judged its clay not to be Cretan at all."},{"q":"Why is a syllabary rather than an alphabet suspected?","a":"Because it uses 45 distinct signs, far more than an alphabet needs and about what a syllabary requires."}]},
{"id":"gr-022","num":"22","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Men are painted red and women white in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a convention perhaps taken from Egypt, and every known figured example is of Neopalatial date.","answer":"Minoan frescoes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan frescoes</b> are the wall paintings of Bronze Age Crete, and every known figured example belongs to the Neopalatial age, roughly 1750 to 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of the surviving corpus comes from Knossos and Ayia Triada, while Malia and Phaistos have produced very few — though painted plaster was not kept for grand rooms alone, and the storeroom walls at Knossos carried a dado of red and blue or grey bands rising to about the height of the jars standing against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The subjects run to bull-leaping, boxing and wrestling, processions of figures carrying vessels, heraldic griffins and lions, and scenes of the natural world with cats stalking birds, monkeys and flowers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men are painted red and women white, a convention that may have been taken over from Egyptian painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where a painter wanted relief he made it physically, modelling the figure out in the plaster itself, though only on a large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A miniature style exists beside the large one, small-figured and crowded, in the Grandstand and Sacred Grove paintings from Knossos and the Boxers from Tylissos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans found the Cup-bearer in 1900 lying face upwards on a passage floor, two large pieces making up most of a life-sized youth in a close-fitting embroidered loin-cloth and an ornamental belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His head and face survived, with a silver necklace and earrings, and on his left wrist an agate lentoid seal whose banding the painter had taken care to show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He carries a pointed cup of silver with gold mountings, of the same funnel-shaped kind held by the Keftiu chieftains in Egyptian tomb paintings of the time of Thutmose III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Frescoes travelled with the rest of Minoan practice, turning up at Phylakopi on Melos and Akrotiri on Thera, and much of what a visitor sees at Knossos today is Evans's restoration, which the museum holding his archive calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan frescoes","questions":["The Cup-bearer, a life-sized youth wearing an agate seal on his wrist and carrying a silver cup, is one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which come mostly from Knossos and Ayia Triada.","Where a painter of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wanted relief he modelled it out in the plaster itself, and most of the surviving corpus comes from Knossos and Ayia Triada."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 15–16, 20, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Akrotiri_Antelopes_fresco.jpg/1920px-Akrotiri_Antelopes_fresco.jpg","title":"Minoan frescoes","desc":"Akrotiri Antelopes fresco. Gleb Simonov.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akrotiri_Antelopes_fresco.jpg","alt":"Akrotiri Antelopes fresco"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is painted plaster not confined to grand rooms?","a":"Because the storeroom walls at Knossos carried a dado of red and blue or grey bands rising to about the height of the jars standing against them."},{"q":"Why did a painter model relief physically?","a":"Because where he wanted it he built the figure out in the plaster itself, though only on a large scale."},{"q":"Why does the Cup-bearer's vessel connect Crete to Egypt?","a":"Because he carries a pointed silver cup with gold mountings of the same funnel-shaped kind held by the Keftiu chieftains in Egyptian tomb paintings of the time of Thutmose III."}]},
{"id":"gr-023","num":"23","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A performer in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grips a charging bull by the horns and lets them pass beneath her armpits, while another turns a somersault over its back.","answer":"Bull-leaping fresco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>bull-leaping fresco</b> is the Minoan wall painting from Knossos showing acrobats vaulting a charging bull, the most familiar image of a sport its art returns to again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its fragments lay in a basement chamber built against the eastern terrace wall, having fallen from an upper room, and were found about 1.5 metres (5 feet) above the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parts of two galloping bulls were put back together from the broken stucco, each about a quarter of natural size and drawn to a much larger scale than the people beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over the back of one bull a figure turns a somersault; another stands between the horns of a charging bull, the horns passing under her armpits as she grasps them higher up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every performer wears the same loin-cloth, narrow metallic girdle, striped socks and slippers, and what separates them is skin colour, the hues of the costume, blue and red diadems and rather curlier hair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The convention across Minoan painting is red skin for men and white for women, perhaps taken over from Egypt, so the white-skinned leapers here are taken for women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Maidens as well as youths were therefore trained for the arena, and the later Greek story of Athenian youths and maidens sent as tribute to the Minotaur at Knossos may keep a memory of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The subject was no one-off: at the south-west portico three successive layers of plaster each carried a large painting whose central figure was a life-sized bull, spotted and in violent action, probably grappled with by a man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bull-leaping and bull-catching were painted flat and modelled in stucco relief at several sizes, and the boxer rhyton from Ayia Triada carries a miniature version in which the jumper is gored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every figured Minoan fresco known is Neopalatial, of the years between about 1750 and 1470 BCE, and much of what stands at Knossos today is restoration the Ashmolean Museum calls extensive and controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Bull-leaping fresco","questions":["Both sexes performed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Athenian tribute of youths and maidens to the Minotaur at Knossos may remember the Cretan sport.","Every performer in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wears the same loin-cloth, girdle, striped socks and slippers, and it is skin colour, hair and diadems that tell the women from the men."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 94–96, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 13, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg/1920px-Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg","title":"Bull-leaping fresco","desc":"Knossos bull leaping fresco. Gleb Simonov.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg","alt":"Knossos bull leaping fresco"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why are some of the leapers taken for women?","a":"Because the convention across Minoan painting is red skin for men and white for women, perhaps taken over from Egypt, and the performers otherwise wear the same costume."},{"q":"Why is the bull drawn so much larger than the people?","a":"Because the two are painted to different scales, each bull about a quarter of natural size and much larger relative to the figures beside it."},{"q":"Why is the subject not read as a single commission?","a":"Because at the south-west portico three successive layers of plaster each carried a large painting whose central figure was a life-sized bull in violent action."}]},
{"id":"gr-024","num":"24","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"One of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands only 20 centimetres (8 inches) to the neck, having lost her head altogether, her left arm wanting and her right holding out a small snake, tail upwards.","answer":"Snake Goddess figurines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM III, c. 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Snake Goddess figurines</b> are faience figures from the Temple Repositories at Knossos, stone cists sunk under a floor of the sanctuary west of the central court, filled with Middle Minoan III material of around 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two shallow empty cists lay above two larger and deeper ones, whose upper fill held Middle Minoan III pottery and whose lowest part held broken cult equipment, greasy earth with charred plant remains, and stag horns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three largely preserved figures came out of them together with fragments of others, the tallest usually counted a goddess and the smaller ones votaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The goddess as reconstituted stands 34.2 centimetres (13.5 inches) high in a purplish-brown tiara, an embroidered jacket with a laced bodice and a skirt with a double apron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her eyes and raised eyebrows are black and her ears abnormally large, and the ground colour is a milky white with details picked out in purple and black.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three snakes with greenish bodies spotted purple-brown coil about her: one held out in her right hand, two interlaced round her hips, one running up the jacket and over an ear to the tiara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The votary is smaller, 20 centimetres (8 inches) to the neck, and had lost her head altogether; her left arm is wanting and her right holds out a small snake, tail upwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both figures are part modern: the goddess's left forearm and the head of the snake rising from her tiara are restored, as are her apron and most of her skirt, made good from a better-preserved one of the same pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The repositories also held miniature votive robes in faience, plaques of a cow and a wild goat nursing their young, shells, crystal and ivory inlays, stone tables of offering, a marble cross, gold foil and a clay tablet in Linear A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the figures represent is unsettled: the snake goddess is usually taken for a household divinity and does not appear on seals, and Minoan figures plausibly divine rather than mortal are overwhelmingly female.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Snake Goddess figurines","questions":["Stone cists sunk under a floor at Knossos, filled with Middle Minoan III material of about 1700 BCE, held the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> along with miniature votive robes in faience.","Three snakes with greenish bodies spotted purple-brown coil about the larger of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one held out in her right hand and two interlaced round her hips."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 74–78, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"Sir Arthur Evans: Biography,\" Sir Arthur Evans Archive, Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, https://sirarthurevans.ashmus.ox.ac.uk/biography/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Minoan_-_Snake_Goddess_-_Walters_23196.jpg","title":"Snake Goddess figurines","desc":"This statue of a snake goddess has been carved with a drill and flat chisel. The upper part is dowelled into the lower. The goddess wears a tall headdress with a snake wound around it. Her hands are pierced to hold snakes. The headdress is broken off and the upper part has been repaired.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_-_Snake_Goddess_-_Walters_23196.jpg","alt":"Minoan - Snake Goddess"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is part of what a visitor sees modern?","a":"Because the goddess's left forearm and the head of the snake rising from her tiara are restored, as are her apron and most of her skirt, made good from a better-preserved one."},{"q":"Why is the taller figure called a goddess and the others votaries?","a":"Because that is how the three largely preserved figures from the repositories are usually distinguished, by size rather than by anything the objects state."},{"q":"Why is what the figures represent unsettled?","a":"Because the snake goddess is usually taken for a household divinity and does not appear on seals, and Minoan figures plausibly divine rather than mortal are overwhelmingly female."}]},
{"id":"gr-025","num":"25","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Named after a cave above a village in the chain of Mount Ida, visible from the palace at Phaistos, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the fine polychrome pottery of the first Cretan palaces.","answer":"Kamares ware","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM IB – IIB, c. 2000 – 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Kamares ware</b> is the fine polychrome pottery of the first Cretan palaces, and it takes its name from the cave above the village of Kamares where it was first found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That cave lies in the chain of Mount Ida, and its entrance can be made out with the naked eye from the palace at Phaistos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ware belongs to Middle Minoan IB through IIB, the age of the Old Palaces, from about 2000 to 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It arrives with the fast potter's wheel and with vessel walls thinned until the finest pieces earn the name eggshell ware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its decoration is light on dark, abundant white with reds, oranges and yellows on a black ground, in complex abstract patterns and now and then stylised plants, animals or human beings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Mosso thought the potters worked with bronze vessels in front of them, reproducing in clay the bosses and folds of the metal and even the way the handles were attached, and giving the surface a metallic sheen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some pieces carry their ornament in relief, one kind with clay applied as bunches of grapes, leaves or ears of corn, and a jug from Ayia Triada that looks like an imitation of repoussé work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sometimes the black ground gives way to the natural brick red of the clay, with the design laid on in white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By far the richest assortment comes from the palace at Phaistos, and the Mesara of southern Crete is arguably where the art reached its technical and aesthetic height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ranked with seals and ceremonial weaponry among the luxury goods the palaces had made, and at Knossos one tall Kamares jar survived because a later floor cut across its top, a smaller spouted vase, a pedestalled cup and obsidian still inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Kamares ware","questions":["The fast wheel and walls thinned to what is called eggshell ware belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose decoration is white, red, orange and yellow on a black ground.","The makers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seem to have had bronze vessels before them, copying in clay the bosses and folds of metal and even the way the handles were attached."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 44, 54, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 21, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 11: The First Palaces in the Aegean,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-11-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Decorated_cups_Phaistos_archmus_Heraklion.jpg/1920px-Decorated_cups_Phaistos_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","title":"Kamares ware","desc":"Various cups decorated with white and polychrome Kamares Ware motifs. Phaistos, 1800 - 1700 BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Decorated_cups_Phaistos_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","alt":"Decorated cups Phaistos archmus Heraklion"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did Mosso think the potters worked with bronze in front of them?","a":"Because they reproduced in clay the bosses and folds of metal vessels and even the way the handles were attached, giving the surface a metallic sheen."},{"q":"Why does the ware arrive when it does?","a":"Because it comes with the fast potter's wheel, which let vessel walls be thinned until the finest pieces earn the name eggshell ware."},{"q":"Why is the Mesara singled out?","a":"Because by far the richest assortment comes from the palace at Phaistos, and southern Crete is arguably where the art reached its technical and aesthetic height."}]},
{"id":"gr-026","num":"26","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Rhyta, bridge-spouted jugs and stirrup jars carry <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and because so many of them are fillers it has been suggested the vases were made for religious use.","answer":"Marine Style pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IB, c. 1600 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IB to c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Marine Style pottery</b> is the Cretan ware of Late Minoan IB, decorated with densely packed sea creatures drawn as though from life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The motifs are the murex or whorl-shell, the nautilus or argonaut, the octopus, the dolphin, seaweed, the sea anemone and the star.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shapes are specialised ones: rhyta of conical and piriform kinds, bridge-spouted jugs and stirrup jars, with very few cups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since rhyta are fillers, and so common in this style, it has been suggested that the vases were made for religious use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The style may derive from a branch of fresco painting particularly at home in the Aegean islands, especially Thera, and may even have been developed on Crete by Cycladic artists who fled the eruption of the Santorini volcano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Mosso watched Pernier dig one such vessel at Phaistos in 1906 and set down what it looked like: corals interlacing their branches in fantastic shapes, floating seaweeds, and the argonaut stretching out arms covered with suckers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The painter had sketched it freehand in sepia, repeating the same motive with slight variations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The vase is double, a cone descending from the neck nearly to the bottom and a small opening below, so that wine poured in stayed put until the vessel was turned over at the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Evans met the same taste at Knossos, where fragments of good vases carrying naturalistic designs of plants and marine objects had fallen from an upper chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Late Minoan IB is dated on one reckoning to about 1600 to 1500 BCE and on another to a close about 1470, and it is the horizon in which nearly every Minoan site of consequence was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Marine Style pottery","questions":["The murex, the nautilus, the octopus, the dolphin, seaweed, sea anemone and star are the creatures packed across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Cretan ware of Late Minoan IB.","One reading traces <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to island fresco painting and to Cycladic artists who came to Crete after the Santorini eruption, though the case is not settled."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 261–63, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 94, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/AMI_-_Oktopusvase.jpg","title":"Marine Style pottery","desc":"Archaeological Museum in Herakleion. Minoan clay bottle showing an Octopus (1500 BCE).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMI_-_Oktopusvase.jpg","alt":"AMI - Oktopusvase"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why has the style been thought to be for religious use?","a":"Because its shapes are specialised — rhyta above all, which are fillers, with bridge-spouted jugs and stirrup jars and very few cups."},{"q":"Why has the style been connected with fresco painting?","a":"Because it may derive from a branch of it particularly at home in the Aegean islands, and may even have been developed on Crete by Cycladic artists who fled the Santorini eruption."},{"q":"Why does a double vessel hold its wine until it is turned over?","a":"Because a cone descends from the neck nearly to the bottom with only a small opening below, so what is poured in stays put until the rhyton is inverted at the altar."}]},
{"id":"gr-027","num":"27","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Laid over a container or a door, a clay lump stamped by one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not be got past without destroying both the lump and the impression on it.","answer":"Minoan sealstones","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prism seals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lentoids, rings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan sealstones</b> are small portable objects, mostly of stone but also of bone, ivory, metal or paste, engraved with a design and made to be worn on the body, as ornament, amulet or badge of rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first true Aegean seals belong to the second phase of the Early Bronze Age, and more than 10,000 seals and impressions survive, the most important source for Aegean imagery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A seal secured, first of all: laid over a container or a door, the clay lump it was pressed into could not be got past without destroying the mark on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It marked, the lump hanging from an object by a string or the stamp going straight onto a pot, to say what was inside or who owned it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And it authorised, stamped on a free-standing lump perhaps used as a token or a receipt; one lump from Chania carries the marks of twelve different seals and, on its back, the impression of a folded leather sheet tied with string.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest Cretan seals are almost exclusively of soft stone or ivory, though the pear-shaped signet was now and then cut from rock crystal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the age of the first palaces, from about 1900 BCE, the three-sided prism was a characteristic shape, and a workshop in Quartier Mu at Malia was given over to seal-cutters who specialised in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their flat faces suited whole lines of Cretan hieroglyphic writing, and that use was given up once the linear script came in, since Linear A never appears on seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Engravers turned instead to the bossed oval field of the lentoid bead-seal and the bezel of the signet ring, where bold pictures replaced calligraphy, a change Evans set at about 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A basement room at Knossos held a mass of broken sealings stamped mostly with animals, the clay nodules prepared to make more, and a matrix taken from a real seal that could stamp counterfeits — used, Evans thought, to forge a royal signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan sealstones","questions":["Cut as three-sided prisms in the age of the first palaces and later as lentoids and the bezels of signet rings, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the most important source there is for Aegean imagery.","A clay lump from Chania carries the marks of twelve different <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and, on its back, the impression of a folded leather sheet tied with string."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Seals &amp; sealings — About,\" Corpus der minoischen und mykenischen Siegel, Institute of Classical Archaeology, Heidelberg University, https://www.uni-heidelberg.de/fakultaeten/philosophie/zaw/cms/seals/sealsAbout.html. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 37–38, 120–21, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 17–19, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Agate_lentoid_seal_MET_DP141656_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Agate_lentoid_seal_MET_DP141656_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Minoan sealstones","desc":"Minoan; Seal, lentoid; Gems.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agate_lentoid_seal_MET_DP141656_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Agate lentoid seal"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does a seal secure a container without a lock?","a":"Because the clay lump it was pressed into could not be got past without destroying the mark on it."},{"q":"Why did the three-sided prism fall out of use?","a":"Because its flat faces suited whole lines of Cretan hieroglyphic writing, and that use ended once the linear script came in, since Linear A never appears on seals."},{"q":"Why is one Knossos find read as a forger's kit?","a":"Because a basement room held broken sealings, clay nodules prepared to make more, and a matrix taken from a real seal that could stamp counterfeits."}]},
{"id":"gr-028","num":"28","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Deep layers of ash carrying no animal bones, and great quantities of clay figurines, are what mark out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Cretan cult places on prominent local hills.","answer":"Peak sanctuaries","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from MM I, c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buildings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from MM III, c. 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Decline</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after LM I, c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Peak sanctuaries</b> are Cretan cult places at or just below the tops of prominent local hills, not necessarily true mountain peaks, marked by deep layers of ash and great quantities of clay figurines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ash holds no animal bones, so it is read as bonfire debris rather than blood sacrifice; metal objects are exceptional and pottery, apart from miniature vases, is rare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of some fifty hilltops claimed as peak sanctuaries at least 25 are generally accepted, and at none does the evidence for cult go back before Middle Minoan I, about the time the first palaces were built around 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They and the cult caves are virtually the only places besides the palaces to yield the finest Kamares pottery, tables of offerings and Linear A inscriptions off clay tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the human figurines are single arms, legs and heads, modelled separately and pierced for suspension, probably the parts of whole dolls once held together by string.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Petsofa above Palaikastro is one of the two excavated and published in full, and its key layer is black earth 17 to 20 centimetres (7 to 8 inches) thick, crowded with figurines, many whole and still holding their paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Myres read it as a bonfire into which figurines were thrown, raked over from time to time to clear the larger ones, then levelled for fresh fuel and fresh offerings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its male figures wear a loin-cloth, a white wrapper and a knotted girdle, with close-fitting white boots, and several carry a broad triangular dagger worn nearly horizontally outside the belt, as long as the wearer's forearm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The votive arms run 8 to 12 centimetres (3 to 5 inches) from shoulder to fingertip, painted white, probably for women, or brown-red and black for men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Juktas, at 811 metres (2,660 feet) and taken to have served Knossos and Archanes, Evans found the summit ringed by a wall of great rough blocks and no buildings inside it; in Middle Minoan III three terraces went up, with a stepped altar over cracks in the bedrock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Peak sanctuaries","questions":["Single arms, legs and heads, modelled separately and pierced for suspension, turn up in numbers at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and were probably parts of whole dolls held together by string.","Only Petsofa above Palaikastro and Juktas above Knossos, of the 25 or so generally accepted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, have been excavated and published in full."],"tags":["place","practice","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","John L. Myres, \"Excavations at Palaikastro. II. §13. The Sanctuary-Site of Petsofà,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 356–58, 374–75, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 23–24, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Petsophas_Gipfelheiligtum_17.jpg/1920px-Petsophas_Gipfelheiligtum_17.jpg","title":"Peak sanctuaries","desc":"The remains of the Minoan peak sanctuary of Petsophas, a scatter of low walls on a bare rocky summit above Palaikastro in eastern Crete, with the sea beyond.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petsophas_Gipfelheiligtum_17.jpg","alt":"Petsophas Gipfelheiligtum 17"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the ash read as bonfire debris rather than sacrifice?","a":"Because it holds no animal bones, and metal objects are exceptional while pottery apart from miniature vases is rare."},{"q":"Why are so many of the figurines single limbs?","a":"Because arms, legs and heads were modelled separately and pierced for suspension, probably the parts of whole dolls once held together by string."},{"q":"Why does Myres read the Petsofa layer as repeatedly used?","a":"Because he takes it for a bonfire into which figurines were thrown, raked over from time to time to clear the larger ones, then levelled for fresh fuel and fresh offerings."}]},
{"id":"gr-029","num":"29","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"First lived in during the Neolithic and later used as cemeteries, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only became places of worship early in the Middle Minoan period, at about the time the first palaces went up.","answer":"Cretan cult caves","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM I, c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM III, to c. 1075 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cretan cult caves</b> are caves on Crete used for worship in the Bronze Age, having served first as dwellings in the Neolithic and then, from the end of that period, widely as cemeteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cult use begins early in the Middle Minoan period, around 1900 BCE, when the first palaces were being built, and the two may well be connected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The evidence is pottery, animal figurines and now and then bronze, and several caves hold quantities of animal bones, mostly deer, ox and goat, from some form of sacrifice, where the hilltop sanctuaries' ash holds none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are fewer than the hilltops — Amnisos, the Idaean cave, Juktas, Kamares, Psychro, Skoteino and Stavromyti — and at none of them does cult go back before Middle Minoan I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The richest votive deposits come from Kamares high on Mount Ida, the Dictaean or Psychro cave above the Lasithi plain, the Idaean cave, and Arkalochori, which gave up an astonishing array of bronze swords, daggers and double axes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Psychro cave is double, water from the east having cut an abyss that falls sheer and then slopes some 60 metres (200 feet) to an icy pool out of which rises a forest of stalactites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The innermost recess of the upper grot was walled off and roughly paved with squared blocks, an enclosure Hogarth took for a temenos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beneath it lay ashes 0.7 metres (2 feet 3 inches) thick, then black mould yielding bronze weapons, then more ashes, then black earth full of painted pottery, to a total depth of 2.1 metres (7 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the lower grot bronze blades, fibulae and votive double axes stood edgeways in the crevices of the stalactite pillars, some chinks holding ten apiece and many so closed over by lime they could not be got out without breaking the stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nearly 500 bronze pieces came out of the cave, along with 550 unbroken little wheel-made cups, all plainly new when set down, the same pale cups found in votive deposits in the caves of Dikte and Ida.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cretan cult caves","questions":["Bronze blades, fibulae and votive double axes stood edgeways in the stalactite pillars of Psychro, one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some chinks holding as many as ten apiece.","Deer, ox and goat bones in quantity, left from some form of sacrifice, are found in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and not on the hilltop sanctuaries, whose ash holds no bones at all."],"tags":["place","practice","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","David G. Hogarth, \"The Dictaean Cave,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 96, 99–101, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 24, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Cave_Dikti12%28js%29.jpg/1920px-Cave_Dikti12%28js%29.jpg","title":"Cretan cult caves","desc":"The lit interior of the Psychro cave on Mount Dikte in Crete, a wide chamber hung with stalactites and grown with stalagmites.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cave_Dikti12(js).jpg","alt":"Cave Dikti12(js)"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why do the caves differ from the hilltop sanctuaries in their remains?","a":"Because several hold quantities of animal bones, mostly deer, ox and goat, from some form of sacrifice, where the hilltop sanctuaries' ash holds none."},{"q":"Why could some Psychro votives not be recovered?","a":"Because bronze blades, fibulae and axes were stood edgeways in the crevices of stalactite pillars, and many were so closed over by lime they could not be got out without breaking the stone."},{"q":"Why are the cups from the cave taken for offerings?","a":"Because 550 unbroken little wheel-made cups came out plainly new when set down, the same pale cups found in votive deposits in the caves of Dikte and Ida."}]},
{"id":"gr-030","num":"30","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A base or impost ending at each side in two horn-like projections, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> served as stands for double axes and libation jugs, or as crowning members on altars and roofs.","answer":"Horns of consecration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">On frescoes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos pair</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Horns of consecration</b> are a piece of Minoan ritual furniture: a base or impost ending at each side in a horn-like projection, at times like real ox horns, more often a conventional imitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They survive as objects of stone or terracotta, often stuccoed, and are painted or carved on murals, altars, vases, seals and larnakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They serve either as stands for a narrow range of cult implements, double axes, libation jugs and branches, or as crowning members on altars and roofs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is Evans's, and what they originally meant is uncertain: stylised bulls' horns, the crescent moon, the rising sun, or an odd form of pot support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Evans met them everywhere in Minoan imagery: at the foot of sacred trees and pillars, at the foot of the double axe, above an archway, on the roof of a shrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On a signet impression from Knossos they stand before the columns of a shrine and again on its entablature, which told Evans that those columns were a made pillar form of the cult object, against the natural peak the goddess stands on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Four pairs are visible above the wall of another sanctuary on a fresco fragment from the palace, and every known figured Minoan fresco is Neopalatial, between about 1750 and 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Actual examples survive, among them a painted terracotta pair from the votive cave at Patso, with a conical stem and two curved objects rising between the horns, broken away above and of lost meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A limestone pair from a house at Knossos, 20 centimetres (8 inches) wide and 19 (7.5 inches) high, stood near the east wall of a small room, on a platform of potsherds rounded as though in running water, during the building's last partial occupation, after about 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That pair had no socket between the horns, where those on the raised base of the Shrine of the Double Axes did: their sockets held the handles of the axes that stood in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Horns of consecration","questions":["The sockets between the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the raised base of the Shrine of the Double Axes at Knossos held the handles of the axes that stood in them.","Stylised bulls' horns, the crescent moon and the rising sun have all been proposed as the origin of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and so has an odd form of pot support."],"tags":["object","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 37–39, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 12, 91, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 30, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Horns_of_concesration%2C_Clay_model%2C_1500-1450_BC%2C_AMH%2C_145010_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Horns_of_concesration%2C_Clay_model%2C_1500-1450_BC%2C_AMH%2C_145010_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Horns of consecration","desc":"Clay model of the horns of concesration,1500-1450 BCE. Archaeological Museum of Heraklion.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horns_of_concesration,_Clay_model,_1500-1450_BC,_AMH,_145010_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Horns of concesration, Clay model, 1500-1450 BCE, AMH"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is what they meant uncertain?","a":"Because the name is Evans's and the object has been read as stylised bulls' horns, the crescent moon, the rising sun, or an odd form of pot support."},{"q":"Why did a signet impression tell Evans about shrine columns?","a":"Because horns stand before the columns of a shrine and again on its entablature, which told him those columns were a made pillar form of the cult object, against the natural peak the goddess stands on."},{"q":"Why do some pairs carry a socket between the horns and others not?","a":"Because the sockets on the raised base of the Shrine of the Double Axes held the handles of the axes that stood in them, where the limestone pair from a Knossos house has none."}]},
{"id":"gr-031","num":"31","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch reports that the Carian word for the double axe was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a word probably connected with labyrinthos, the name given to Minos's palace at Knossos.","answer":"Labrys","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid Early Minoan, before 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM III, to c. 1075 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>labrys</b> is the double axe, the commonest of all Minoan religious symbols, made from the middle of the Early Minoan period onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some large bronze examples were working tools, but miniatures in gold, silver, lead, steatite and terracotta, and bronze ones too fragile to swing, can only be symbolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In pictures it is usually set in a stone base or between horns of consecration, and what it stood for is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Near East such axes are wielded by male divinities and read as thunderbolts, but in Crete no male divinity holds one, which favours the view that it owes its frequency to being the instrument of sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is not Minoan: Plutarch, in the 2nd century CE, reports that the Carian word for a double axe was labrys, probably connected with labyrinthos, the palace's later name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Knossos the sign is cut into the gypsum of two pillar rooms, three sides of the eastern pillar and all four of the western, and on the principal jambs besides, which led Evans to call the palace the House of the Double Axe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He tied the name to the Carian Zeus Labraundos, and argued that the axe was itself an object of worship, a material shape the divinity's essence could enter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bronze votive axes were laid up in the cave sanctuaries of Ida and Dikte, 18 in the lower grot at Psychro, almost every one still standing in a stalactite niche.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All were too small or thin to be of use; two kept their shafts, and the largest, 280 millimetres (11 inches) long, carries fine oblique lines across the blades like those Evans had seen on axes engraved on gems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clearest arrangement to survive is the Shrine of the Double Axes at Knossos, abandoned with its furniture in place, where two stuccoed clay horns of consecration stand on a bench, a round socket between each pair to take an axe and a small steatite one resting against the left-hand pair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Labrys","questions":["Eighteen bronze examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of the lower grot at Psychro, nearly all still standing in the stalactite niches, and every one too thin to have been used.","Incised on three sides of one pillar at Knossos and on all four of another, the sign of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave that building the name House of the Double Axe."],"tags":["object","symbol","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"Knossos: Summary Report of the Excavations in 1900. I. The Palace,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 32–34, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","David G. Hogarth, \"The Dictaean Cave,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900): 108–9, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 9, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Minoan_Labrys1.jpg","title":"Labrys","desc":"Votive double ax Early Aegean, MinoanBronze Age, Late Minoan IA Period, about 1550–1500 BCE. Gold double axe composed of thin sheet gold mounted on a hollow gold shaft and outlined by bands of five incised lines. On left blade, inscription of four signs in undeciphered Linear A.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_Labrys1.jpg","alt":"Minoan Labrys1"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are many of the axes taken as symbolic rather than tools?","a":"Because miniatures in gold, silver, lead, steatite and terracotta, and bronze ones too fragile to swing, could never have been used — 18 at Psychro were all too small or thin to be of use."},{"q":"Why is the Near Eastern reading of the axe rejected for Crete?","a":"Because there such axes are wielded by male divinities and read as thunderbolts, while in Crete no male divinity holds one, which favours its being the instrument of sacrifice."},{"q":"Why is the name not a Minoan word?","a":"Because it comes from Plutarch in the 2nd century CE, reporting that the Carian word for a double axe was labrys."}]},
{"id":"gr-032","num":"32","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Because Linear A is undeciphered there is effectively no contemporary text about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is known instead from the places of cult, the pictures of it and its furniture.","answer":"Minoan religion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Minoan Crete</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 1075 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult places</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from MM I, c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan religion</b> is known almost entirely from things rather than words, Linear A being undeciphered and no contemporary text about it surviving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usable evidence falls into three classes: the places where cult happened, pictures of cult on seals, rings, murals, larnakes and pottery, and the furniture of cult itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The places are cult caves, hilltop sanctuaries and shrines in towns, palaces and houses, the last sorted into bench sanctuaries, lustral basins and pillar crypts, of which only the bench goes back to the Prepalatial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Working from the pictures, Evans read the sacred trees and pillars that recur in them as aniconic forms of the divinity, worshipped as a material shape the god could enter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The figures plausibly divine rather than mortal are overwhelmingly female, and several distinct goddesses can be told apart by the attributes they carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Snake Goddess, known from the faience figures of the Temple Repositories at Knossos, is usually taken for a household divinity and never appears on seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Mistress of Animals stands on her rocky peak between two lions on a signet impression from Knossos, a shrine on one side and a saluting man on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Male divinities are rare and often drawn smaller than the women beside them: one descends through the air with a spear, another stands beardless between horns of consecration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Animal sacrifice is not in doubt, and two finds suggest it reached people: a sanctuary at Anemospilia burned at the end of Middle Minoan II, about 1750 BCE, held an 18-year-old trussed on a platform with a bronze dagger 0.4 metres (16 inches) long among his bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His bones were white on the upper side and black on the lower, which suggested to the anthropologist who saw them that he had bled to death; and in the North House at Knossos 327 children's bones carried fine knife marks like the marks butchery leaves on animal bone, though later analysis put them at four children at most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan religion","questions":["Overwhelmingly female divinities, several of them distinguishable by their attributes, are what the art of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows, with male gods rare and often drawn smaller than the women beside them.","A trussed 18-year-old with a bronze dagger among his bones, at a sanctuary that burned at Anemospilia, is the sharpest evidence that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may have included human sacrifice."],"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 29–30, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 74–78, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 9–10, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Epiphany_cycle_%E2%80%94_Sacred_Conversation.jpg","title":"Minoan religion","desc":"Epiphany cycle — Sacred Conversation. Gsimonov.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epiphany_cycle_%E2%80%94_Sacred_Conversation.jpg","alt":"Epiphany cycle — Sacred Conversation"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is this religion known almost entirely from things?","a":"Because Linear A is undeciphered and no contemporary text about it survives, so the evidence is the places of cult, the pictures of it, and its furniture."},{"q":"Why can separate goddesses be distinguished?","a":"Because several can be told apart by the attributes they carry — a Snake Goddess known from the faience figures, a Mistress of Animals standing on her peak between two lions."},{"q":"Why is the evidence for human sacrifice hedged?","a":"Because the Anemospilia burial is one case read from the colour of the bones, and the 327 children's bones from the North House at Knossos were put at four children at most by later analysis."}]},
{"id":"gr-033","num":"33","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"On the front of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> women pour liquid into a krater between two double axes with birds perched on them, to the music of a lyre.","answer":"Ayia Triada sarcophagus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IIIA, c. 1400 – 1350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ayia Triada sarcophagus</b> is a limestone burial chest painted on lime plaster, found in a looted tomb of the early 14th century BCE in southern Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Burial in Late Minoan III Crete usually meant a chamber tomb with the body on the floor or in a terracotta larnax or pithos, so a stone chest is rare and its painted scenes are unique in Aegean funerary art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the front, women pour liquid into a krater standing between two double axes with birds perched on them, to the music of a lyre, the mixing read as wine and water for a goddess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans had already argued that a double axe could be the divinity's own material shape, which lets the birds settling on them read as the deity arriving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beside it a motionless figure with concealed arms stands before a building, taken for the spirit of the dead man watching his own obsequies, with a boat and cattle brought to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the back a bull lies strapped to a table with a conical rhyton fixed in the ground beneath it to take the blood, a flute player behind and a procession of women approaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The women hold their palms downward and the blood drains into the earth, which marks the sacrifice as one to a power of the underworld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each end carries a chariot, one drawn by two agrimia, the Cretan wild goats, the other by winged griffins, the pairs of women riding in them read as divine escorts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the whole is one theme is disputed: some separate the front as a cult of the dead from the back as the cult of a god, others deny the funerary reading and make the standing figure a deity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nilsson resolved it by supposing the dead man deified, a Mycenaean overlord rather than a Minoan, and Burke reads the chest as a Mycenaean elite claiming power by dressing itself in the Minoan past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ayia Triada sarcophagus","questions":["A bull strapped to a table with a rhyton below to take its blood fills the back of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, marking the sacrifice as one to a power of the earth.","Chariots drawn by agrimia at one end and by winged griffins at the other carry pairs of women across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, read as divine escorts for the dead man's journey."],"tags":["object","art","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 9, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Brendan Burke, \"Materialization of Mycenaean Ideology and the Ayia Triada Sarcophagus,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 109, no. 3 (2005): 403–22, https://ajaonline.org/article/107/. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Sarcophagus_archmus_Heraklion.jpg/1920px-Sarcophagus_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","title":"Ayia Triada sarcophagus","desc":"One of the lateral faces of the \"Agia Triada\" sarcophagus, Crete, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcophagus_archmus_Heraklion.jpg","alt":"Sarcophagus archmus Heraklion"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is a painted stone chest so unusual?","a":"Because burial in Late Minoan III Crete usually meant a chamber tomb with the body on the floor or in a terracotta larnax or pithos, so a stone chest is rare and its painted scenes unique in Aegean funerary art."},{"q":"Why do the birds on the double axes read as a deity arriving?","a":"Because Evans had argued that a double axe could be the divinity's own material shape, so a bird settling on one marks the god's presence."},{"q":"Why is the sacrifice on the back read as one to an underworld power?","a":"Because the women hold their palms downward and the blood drains into the earth through a conical rhyton fixed in the ground beneath the bull."}]},
{"id":"gr-034","num":"34","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Set on four feet with drain-holes in the bottom, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> repeats in painted clay the framing and sunk panels of an ordinary wooden chest.","answer":"Larnax","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM III, c. 1470 – 1075 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>larnax</b> is a terracotta chest used to hold the dead in Bronze Age Crete, the commonest container of a Late Minoan burial, and most of the tombs holding them are of the Post-Palatial period after about 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Burial then usually meant a chamber tomb, with the body laid on the floor or set inside a larnax or a pithos, and the two practices were at times followed side by side in one tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Evans held that these chests were not models of houses but translations into painted clay of the wooden chests of household furniture, and that some were made for burial from the start, since they have drain-holes in the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their narrow capacity suggested to some that they were ossuaries filled once the flesh had gone, but a larnax from Zapher Papoura and another from Artsa held skeletons lying on their backs with the knees drawn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The painted stone chest from Ayia Triada is a rare version in stone of this otherwise ordinary object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The painted larnax Bosanquet published from Palaikastro is of well-baked dark red clay under a creamy slip, 1.22 metres (4 feet) long and about a metre (3 feet) high with its gabled lid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands on four feet, carries four vertical handles a side and one at each end, and has two holes bored near the edge of the lid so that lid and body could be laced together for carrying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its body faithfully repeats the framing and six sunk panels of a wooden chest rather than the gabled roof of a house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One panel carries a slender column with a moulded capital supporting a pair of sacred horns and a double axe, springing from between two flowers; the others a winged griffin, a fish drawn upside down, and a bird.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Larnakes are so common around Palaikastro that Bosanquet found them serving as a wash-tub beside a well, a huge flower-pot, and the receiver of a wine-press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Larnax","questions":["A column with a moulded capital carrying sacred horns and a double axe fills one panel of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Palaikastro, with a griffin, a fish and a bird on the rest.","The narrow capacity of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suggested it held bones alone, until examples were found with the skeleton laid on its back and the knees drawn up."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Robert C. Bosanquet, \"Excavations at Palaikastro. I. §3. The Painted Larnax, and Some Other Larnax-Burials,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 297–300, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Prehistoric Tombs of Knossos</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1906), 6–10, 30, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictombs00evan. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Minoan_larnax%2C_brids%2C_carriage%2C_seascape%2C_Crete%2C_AMH%2C_145315.jpg/1920px-Minoan_larnax%2C_brids%2C_carriage%2C_seascape%2C_Crete%2C_AMH%2C_145315.jpg","title":"Larnax","desc":"Minoan larnax with painting of brids, carriage and seascape. Crete, probably Postpalatial period. Archaeological Museum of Heraklion.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_larnax,_brids,_carriage,_seascape,_Crete,_AMH,_145315.jpg","alt":"Minoan larnax, brids, carriage, seascape, Crete, AMH"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did Evans read the chests as furniture rather than houses?","a":"Because their bodies repeat the framing and sunk panels of a wooden chest rather than the gabled roof of a house, and some have drain-holes showing they were made for burial from the start."},{"q":"Why is the ossuary reading not general?","a":"Because although their narrow capacity suggested chests filled once the flesh had gone, examples from Zapher Papoura and Artsa held skeletons lying on their backs with the knees drawn up."},{"q":"Why does the Palaikastro larnax have holes in its lid?","a":"Because two were bored near the edge so that lid and body could be laced together for carrying."}]},
{"id":"gr-035","num":"35","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Circular chambers of unworked fieldstone bonded with clay, built above ground and entered from the east through a doorway barely a metre square, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> housed hundreds of burials each.","answer":"Mesara tholos tombs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">EM I, c. 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into LM IIIA, after c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesara tholos tombs</b> are circular stone burial chambers of southern Crete, between 4 and 13 metres (13 and 43 feet) across, first built in Early Minoan I from about 3000 BCE and some of them still in use more than a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their thick walls are of unworked fieldstones bonded with clay, founded on bedrock and standing above ground rather than sunk into it, and the doorway, rarely over a metre (3 feet) square and closed by a slab, is almost always on the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most corbel slightly inward, and whether the larger ones ever carried a complete stone vault was for decades the central question of the subject; a full vault is now allowed to the smaller tombs at least.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans, who saw the ruins himself, called them true beehive tombs raised on the principle of the horizontal arch, and took the 23 cubic metres of fallen stone inside one Platanos ossuary as proof of solid building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The larger Platanos tholos measures 13.10 metres (43 feet) inside, only a little less than the Tomb of Clytemnestra at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hundreds of inhumations went into most of them with no regard for what was already there, so the interiors are a confused mass of bones and grave goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Traces of great fires on the rock floor, and layers of clean white sand between one burial stratum and the next, are read as periodic fumigation and tidying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tholos at Hagia Triada gave up miniature figures of terracotta and steatite and more than a hundred seals of ivory, bone and steatite, every one pierced to hang from a cord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the form came from is unsettled: derivation from Syria, from Nubia and Egypt and from Cycladic prototypes have all been argued and all meet objections, while Branigan takes them for free-standing imitations of caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are not the ancestors of the Mycenaean tholos in any simple way, since the mainland tombs are sunk into hillsides under an earthen mound, fully vaulted, and entered down a long dromos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mesara tholos tombs","questions":["Whether the biggest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever carried a complete stone vault was for decades the central question of the subject, and only the smaller ones are now allowed one.","The mainland tombs are sunk into hillsides under an earthen mound and entered down a long dromos, where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stand above ground with a small door facing east."],"tags":["building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Stephanos Xanthoudides, <i>The Vaulted Tombs of Mesará</i> (London: Hodder and Stoughton, 1924), xi–xii, https://archive.org/details/vaultedtombsofme00xant. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 249, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was the roofing of these tombs the central question for decades?","a":"Because most corbel only slightly inward, so whether the larger ones ever carried a complete stone vault was disputed; a full vault is now allowed to the smaller tombs at least."},{"q":"Why are their interiors a confused mass?","a":"Because hundreds of inhumations went into most of them with no regard for what was already there, though fires on the floor and clean sand layers are read as periodic fumigation and tidying."},{"q":"Why are they not simply ancestral to the Mycenaean tholos?","a":"Because the mainland tombs are sunk into hillsides under an earthen mound, fully vaulted, and entered down a long dromos, where these stand above ground with a small east doorway."}]},
{"id":"gr-036","num":"36","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"At Knossos a portico of twelve pillars flanked one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for some 20 metres, and a guard-house faced a bastion where it met the paved road running west.","answer":"Minoan roads","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan roads</b> are known from the causeways, roadways and cobbled streets that survive at and around the palaces and towns of Bronze Age Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Knossos a roadway ran north from the palace's northern entrance carrying its own main drain, and for some 20 metres (65 feet) it was flanked by a portico of twelve pillars set in a double row.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it met a second paved road running west stood a tower or guard-house facing a massive bastion, so that the chief avenue of public access to the palace was jealously watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the West Court ran a narrow causeway more carefully paved than the rest and raised a little above it, striking diagonally north-west from the West Portico to avoid the angle of the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phaistos has a precisely similar diagonal causeway across its own west court, leading to a stepway that climbs the tiers of seats above, and that parallel is what settled the Knossos strip as a path rather than the base of a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> North-west of the palace the paved ways branch, one causeway running west to east towards the northern entrance and a narrower one turning north-east towards the pillar hall that faces it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where they fork lies the stepped Theatral Area, a paved space bisected by a causeway and overlooked on two sides by tiers of stone steps, eighteen of them on the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The causeway coming down from the west cuts the southern flight diagonally, which is why that flight falls from six tiers to three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside a town the roads are streets: at Palaikastro and Pseira cobbled streets define the blocks of living units in the Neopalatial period, from about 1750 BCE, as they do in contemporary settlements of the Cyclades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The paved ground had been walked a long time: a test pit through the West Court found Mycenaean and then Kamares sherds beneath it, and nearer the west wall Kamares sherds ran two metres (6 feet) down to the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan roads","questions":["A narrow strip across the West Court at Knossos was read as a wall until Phaistos produced the same thing, which settled it as one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, raised and carefully paved.","At Palaikastro and Pseira cobbled streets define the blocks of living units, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> look like inside a town rather than between them."],"tags":["building","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1902,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 5–6, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 5–6, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report for the Year 1903,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 9 (1902–1903): 99–100, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1902-1903_9. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Malia_Minoan-site_processional-way_20230608_101244.jpg/1920px-Malia_Minoan-site_processional-way_20230608_101244.jpg","title":"Minoan roads","desc":"North side of the Minoan palace of Malia; processional way from the north entrance to the east.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malia_Minoan-site_processional-way_20230608_101244.jpg","alt":"Malia Minoan-site processional-way"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was the Knossos causeway settled as a path rather than a wall base?","a":"Because Phaistos has a precisely similar diagonal causeway across its own west court, leading to a stepway that climbs the tiers of seats above."},{"q":"Why was the northern approach to the palace so heavily watched?","a":"Because a tower or guard-house faced a massive bastion where the roadway met a second paved road, so the chief avenue of public access was jealously guarded."},{"q":"Why does one flight of the Theatral Area drop from six tiers to three?","a":"Because the causeway coming down from the west cuts the southern flight diagonally."}]},
{"id":"gr-037","num":"37","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A stone conduit under flat slabs, a metre high and half that wide, runs beneath the Domestic Quarter at Knossos, the backbone of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and wide enough to walk along.","answer":"Minoan water management","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Later Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan water management</b> is best seen at Knossos, where an elaborate drainage system runs through the later palace's Domestic Quarter, from about 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stone shafts descend from the upper floors to a stone conduit under flat covering slabs, about a metre (3 feet) high and half that wide for most of its course, coated inside with cement and easy for a man to walk along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first purpose was to carry off surface water: one shaft took the rain off the Central Court and another the drainage from the neighbouring halls' roofs, both descending gradually with an occasional step down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cretan rains are torrential, so the conduits were flushed hard from time to time, and the architect turned that force to account by tying the sanitary arrangements into the same system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lightwell, one of the features that make Minoan architecture its own, is where the water is caught: at the Hall of the Colonnades the open floor is cemented rather than paved and slopes to a drain hole in the corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> From there the drain runs along that hall's southern wall and across the angle of the Hall of the Double Axes, gaining capacity with every drainage area until a man can crawl along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the floor of the Corridor of the Draught Board, 1.45 metres (5 feet) down, ran terracotta pipes 76 centimetres (30 inches) long, socketed one into the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each mouthpiece carries a stop-ridge solidly backed behind, which met the butt of the next tube and resisted the pressure that would otherwise have wedged the two together to splitting point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The latrine off the Room of the Plaster Couch had a wooden seat at the back with a stone foot-rest before it, about 57 centimetres (22 inches) from the ground, within a centimetre of the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mosso, a physiologist by trade, said he admired that alabaster-lined room more than the throne of Minos, and after a downpour at Hagia Triada watched water run from sewers a man could walk upright in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan water management","questions":["Under a corridor at Knossos <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> included terracotta pipes 76 centimetres long, socketed one into the next, each mouthpiece carrying a stop-ridge to stop them wedging together.","The rains of Crete are torrential, so the conduits of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were flushed hard from time to time, and the sanitary arrangements were tied into that same system."],"tags":["building","technology","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1902,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 13–14, 81–86, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos: Provisional Report of the Excavations for the Year 1901,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 7 (1900–1901): 109–10, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1900-1901_7. [Open access]","Angelo Mosso, <i>The Palaces of Crete and Their Builders</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 80, 123, https://archive.org/details/palacesofcreteth00moss. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Tuyaux_de_poterie_et_jonctions_sur_le_r%C3%A9seau_d%27eau_sous_pression_%C3%A0_Knossos.jpg/1920px-Tuyaux_de_poterie_et_jonctions_sur_le_r%C3%A9seau_d%27eau_sous_pression_%C3%A0_Knossos.jpg","title":"Minoan water management","desc":"Terracotta pipes and junctions on the pressurized water network at Knossos.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuyaux_de_poterie_et_jonctions_sur_le_r%C3%A9seau_d%27eau_sous_pression_%C3%A0_Knossos.jpg","alt":"Tuyaux de poterie et jonctions sur le réseau d'eau sous pression à Knossos"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the architect tie the sanitary arrangements into the storm drains?","a":"Because Cretan rains are torrential, so the conduits were flushed hard from time to time, and that force could be turned to account."},{"q":"Why does each terracotta pipe carry a stop-ridge?","a":"Because it met the butt of the next tube and resisted the pressure that would otherwise have wedged the two together to splitting point."},{"q":"Why is the lightwell where the water is caught?","a":"Because its open floor is cemented rather than paved and slopes to a drain hole in the corner, from which the drain runs on gaining capacity."}]},
{"id":"gr-038","num":"38","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 180 metres off the north coast of eastern Crete, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rises in cliffs on one side and slopes to the sea on the other, where the Minoan town lay.","answer":"Mochlos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rich graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Early Minoan, before c. 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Neopalatial, c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mochlos</b> is a circular mass of limestone about 180 metres (200 yards) off the north coast of eastern Crete, rising in cliffs some 60 metres (200 feet) on its north side and sloping to sea level on its south, where the town lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water between island and mainland is very shallow at one point, and Seager reasoned that if the subsidence seen elsewhere in Crete happened here, the island was once joined to it and had the best harbour on that coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Minoan house walls survive on the shore opposite, so the town lay on both sides of that neck whether or not it stood dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cemetery is a hillside of small tombs plus six large chamber tombs along a narrow ledge, a precipice overhead and a 30-metre (100-foot) drop to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five kinds of tomb were used: ossuaries closed by upright slabs, cist graves of Cycladic type, tombs walled in small stones like houses, burials in holes in the rock, and, from Middle Minoan III, bodies laid in pithoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The house tomb is better represented here than anywhere, its slab walls sometimes standing 2.5 metres (8 feet), sometimes taking the cliff for a back wall, and roofed flat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly 150 gold ornaments came out of the graves, besides eleven necklaces of rock crystal, gold, shell, carnelian, amethyst, steatite, breccia and faience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The gold is worked two ways, beaten over a mould or pricked through from behind into raised dots, and most was worn in life rather than made for the grave: the diadems carry pin holes where their owners jabbed them tight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That upset the picture of a poor Early Minoan Crete before about 1900 BCE: Seager asked what the capitals must have held, if so small a settlement had precious metal in such abundance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether the tombs also show a ranked society is argued, Soles reading size, position and grave goods that way and Watrous explaining them otherwise; in the Neopalatial period the houses stand large and separate along the waterfront.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mochlos","questions":["Nearly 150 gold ornaments and eleven necklaces came out of the graves at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is what proved that Early Minoan Crete was not a poor place.","Six large ossuaries stand on a narrow ledge at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a precipice overhead and a drop of 30 metres to the sea below."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Richard B. Seager, \"Excavations on the Island of Mochlos, Crete, in 1908,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 13, no. 3 (1909): 273–74, https://archive.org/details/jstor-497023. [Open access]","Richard B. Seager, <i>Explorations in the Island of Mochlos</i> (Boston: American School of Classical Studies at Athens, 1912), 13–15, 104–6, https://archive.org/details/explorationsinis00seaguoft. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/%CE%9C%CF%8C%CF%87%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_05.jpg/1920px-%CE%9C%CF%8C%CF%87%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_05.jpg","title":"Mochlos","desc":"The excavated Minoan settlement at Mochlos on the north coast of eastern Crete, low walls on the shore with the sea and the hills of the mainland behind.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9C%CF%8C%CF%87%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_05.jpg","alt":"Μόχλος 05"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Mochlos","at":[25.9075,35.1875]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Seager think the island had once been joined to the coast?","a":"Because the water between is very shallow at one point, and if the subsidence seen elsewhere in Crete happened here the island would have had the best harbour on that coast."},{"q":"Why is most of the gold read as worn in life?","a":"Because the diadems carry pin holes where their owners jabbed them tight, rather than being made for the grave."},{"q":"Why did the finds upset the picture of Early Minoan Crete?","a":"Because so small a settlement held precious metal in such abundance that Seager asked what the capitals must have contained."}]},
{"id":"gr-039","num":"39","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cliffs rise sheer 240 metres from the sea on one side of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the other slopes to three sandy coves, on the middle one of which the Minoan town stood.","answer":"Pseira","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">MM I – LM I, c. 1900 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pseira</b> is a barren mass of rock about 3 kilometres (2 miles) off eastern Crete, roughly that long itself and nowhere more than 1.6 kilometres (1 mile) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its west coast is cliffs rising sheer 240 metres (800 feet) out of the sea, while the east slopes to three sandy coves divided by tongues of land, the middle one running out some 180 metres (200 yards).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that point stood a Minoan town, already important in Middle Minoan I from about 1900 BCE, which Seager thought owed its existence to the sheltered cove on its south side, open only to the east, whence gales rarely come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island offered little else: terrace walls show the slopes were cultivated, but the ground was too small and thin to feed a farming population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seager set that beside Gournia, which had no harbour and never reached the same wealth or such close dealings with Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The town is a huddle of streets and houses over the top and sides of the point, with long flights of steps dropping to the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two roads climb from the head of the cove and a stepway divides near the summit into four or five ways across the hilltop; the level roads are unpaved, floored with natural rock, while every side alley down to the sea has a paved stepway still in good repair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The walls are heavy, sometimes roughly squared blocks in the Late Minoan I manner over lighter Middle Minoan I work, with no ashlar and no house to match the palace at Gournia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brick seems not to have been used at all, unlike Gournia and Vasiliki: it would have come from the mainland, while the island's stratified limestone breaks off ready to build with and a soft slaty stone splits into slabs for upper floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No well or cistern has been found in the town, and its early graves held clay and stone vases with little metal, the mark of a poorer community than the one buried at Mochlos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pseira","questions":["The level roads of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are unpaved and floored with natural rock, while every side alley running down to the sea has a paved stepway still in good repair.","Brick seems not to have been used at all at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since it would have had to come from the mainland and the island's limestone breaks off ready to build with."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Richard B. Seager, <i>Excavations on the Island of Pseira, Crete</i> (Philadelphia: University Museum, 1910), 5–9, https://archive.org/details/excavationsonisl00seag. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Richard B. Seager, <i>Explorations in the Island of Mochlos</i> (Boston: American School of Classical Studies at Athens, 1912), 13, https://archive.org/details/explorationsinis00seaguoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Psira_island.jpg","title":"Pseira","desc":"Island of Psira north of Crete in the Gulf of Mirabella.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psira_island.jpg","alt":"Psira island"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Pseira","at":[25.8597,35.1903]},"why":[{"q":"Why did a town stand on so barren an island?","a":"Because Seager thought it owed its existence to the sheltered cove on the point's south side, open only to the east, whence gales rarely come."},{"q":"Why was no brick used here?","a":"Because it would have had to come from the mainland, while the island's stratified limestone breaks off ready to build with and a soft slaty stone splits into slabs for upper floors."},{"q":"Why is the community judged poorer than Mochlos?","a":"Because its early graves held clay and stone vases with little metal, and there is no ashlar and no house to match the palace at Gournia."}]},
{"id":"gr-040","num":"40","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Both fists held against the chest and the left foot slightly advanced, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands about 50 centimetres high, its body of hippopotamus ivory on a wooden frame.","answer":"Palaikastro Kouros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">LM IB, c. 1500 – 1450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Palaikastro Kouros</b> is a Minoan statuette of a young man, made of several materials at once and recovered in some hundreds of fragments from an urban sanctuary in eastern Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands about 50 centimetres (20 inches) high and 18 (7 inches) wide, both fists held against the chest in the attitude of the Minoan worshipper and the left foot slightly advanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The body is hippopotamus ivory built on a wooden armature, the eyes are rock crystal, and thin sheets of gold render the loincloth and the sandals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Faure adds plaques of Egyptian blue, and reads both the method of assembly and the proportions as Egyptian, of a kind met in other Minoan divine figures at Knossos, Malia and Archanes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the head is cut from is not agreed: the excavators call it steatite and their reviewer serpentine, from the country north of Ida.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It is the most complete Minoan male figure to survive apart from the ivory acrobat from Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pieces came up over several seasons, the torso and an arm first in 1987 and the legs last in 1990, and only the middle of the body, probably of wood, and a little of the face are still missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town at Palaikastro reached its greatest extent in the Neopalatial period, with substantial houses and wide streets in blocks, and many of its buildings burned about 1450 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The statuette comes from that Late Minoan IB destruction, and its final publication argues that the figure is the young god of nearby Mount Petsofas, an ancestor of the Diktaian Zeus named in Greek inscriptions a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Faure refuses the link on three grounds: the Linear A dedications from Petsofas name three apparently female powers, the name Dikte was given to sacred mountains all over Crete and beyond it, and the equation with the Linear B address to the Diktaian god is not established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palaikastro Kouros","questions":["Only the middle of the body, probably of wood, and a little of the face are missing from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was recovered in several hundred fragments.","The final publication makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the young god of nearby Mount Petsofas and an ancestor of the Diktaian Zeus, which one reviewer flatly refuses."],"tags":["object","art","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jean-Claude Poursat, review of <i>The Palaikastro Kouros: A Minoan Chryselephantine Statuette and Its Aegean Bronze Age Context</i>, <i>Topoi. Orient-Occident</i> 11, no. 2 (2001): 707–10, https://www.persee.fr/doc/topoi_1161-9473_2001_num_11_2_1961. [Open access]","Paul Faure, review of <i>The Palaikastro Kouros: A Minoan Chryselephantine Statuette and Its Aegean Bronze Age Context</i>, <i>Revue des Études Grecques</i> 114, no. 1 (2001): 284–85, https://www.persee.fr/doc/reg_0035-2039_2001_num_114_1_4448_t1_0284_0000_2. [Open access]","Gilles Touchais, \"Palaikastro,\" <i>Bulletin de Correspondance Hellénique</i> 120, no. 3 (1996): 1320, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1996_num_120_3_14084. [Open access]","\"Chiona Beach Project,\" British School at Athens, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/research-2/chiona-beach-project-2/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Palaikastro_Kouros_12.jpg/1920px-Palaikastro_Kouros_12.jpg","title":"Palaikastro Kouros","desc":"The Palaikastro Kouros displayed in a museum case: a standing male figure built up from ivory, gold and stone, made between about 1480 and 1425 BCE and found in a house at Roussolakkos.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palaikastro_Kouros_12.jpg","alt":"Palaikastro Kouros 12"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the statuette take several seasons to recover?","a":"Because it came up in some hundreds of fragments, the torso and an arm first in 1987 and the legs last in 1990."},{"q":"Why is its identification as a young god disputed?","a":"Because Faure objects that the Linear A dedications from Petsofas name three apparently female powers, that the name Dikte was given to sacred mountains all over Crete, and that the equation with the Linear B Diktaian god is not established."},{"q":"Why can the material of its head not be stated flatly?","a":"Because the excavators call it steatite and their reviewer serpentine, from the country north of Ida."}]},
{"id":"gr-041","num":"41","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The central court of the first palace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measures 13 by 6 metres, where the court at Knossos runs to 54 by 27.","answer":"Petras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Neolithic on, before 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemetery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">EM II – MM IIB, to c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Petras</b> is a Minoan settlement on a hill east of modern Siteia, looking out over Siteia Bay, with a palatial building at its centre and a town around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its palace is among the smallest of the court-centred Cretan buildings: the central court of the first phase measures 13 by 6 metres (43 by 20 feet), where the court at Knossos runs to 54 by 27.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Occupation goes back much further, a Late Neolithic settlement on the eastern slope and an extensive Early Minoan I one on the north, and long afterwards a Late Minoan III wall was thrown round the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Sector I, north-east of the palace, House I.1 went up in Middle Minoan IIIB, was last lived in during Late Minoan IA, and was occupied again in Late Minoan II to III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the neighbouring hill stands a cemetery of thirteen house tombs, used from Early Minoan II to Middle Minoan IIB, with an undisturbed rock shelter beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Only six have been dug out completely; corridors run between them and two ceremonial areas were laid out among them, across four phases of the cemetery's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are built much as the house tombs at Mochlos are, in several phases with several rooms apiece, and fragments of marble figurines point to the Cyclades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The shelter on the western side held a secondary deposit a metre deep, probably emptied out of one or more of the tombs, and seems to have gone out of use when the palace was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It gave up at least 95 skulls and some 7,000 human bones, with 100 clay vases and five of stone, 10 silver and seven bronze objects including daggers, four gold bands and five seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bones were carried there once the flesh was gone, traces of burning suggest fumigation where they first lay, and one woman shows what may be artificial deformation of the skull and trephination shortly before she died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Petras","questions":["A rock shelter beside the house tombs at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held at least 95 skulls and some 7,000 human bones, moved there after the flesh had gone.","Thirteen house tombs of Early Minoan II to Middle Minoan IIB stand on the hill beside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with corridors between them and two ceremonial areas laid out among them."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["R. Angus K. Smith, review of <i>Petras, Siteia II: A Minoan Palatial Settlement in Eastern Crete. Late Bronze Age Pottery from Houses I.1 and I.2</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2023.08.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.08.04/. [Open access]","Ilaria Caloi, review of <i>Petras, Siteia: The Pre- and Proto-Palatial Cemetery in Context</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2017.10.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.10.45. [Open access]","\"KEPHALA, Petras — 2009,\" <i>Chronique des fouilles en ligne</i>, no. 1793, École française d'Athènes, https://chronique.efa.gr/?kroute=report&id=1793. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 12: Minoan Architecture: The Palaces,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-12-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Petras_Ausgrabungsst%C3%A4tte_50.jpg/1920px-Petras_Ausgrabungsst%C3%A4tte_50.jpg","title":"Petras","desc":"The Minoan site of Petras near Sitia in eastern Crete, its excavated walls spread over a low hill above the bay.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petras_Ausgrabungsst%C3%A4tte_50.jpg","alt":"Petras Ausgrabungsstätte 50"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Petras","at":[26.1,35.2]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the bones in the rock shelter a secondary deposit?","a":"Because they were carried there once the flesh was gone, probably emptied out of one or more of the house tombs, with traces of burning suggesting fumigation where they first lay."},{"q":"Why is the palace unusual among the court-centred buildings?","a":"Because it is among the smallest: its first central court measures 13 by 6 metres where the court at Knossos runs to 54 by 27."},{"q":"Why do fragments of marble figurines matter here?","a":"Because they point to the Cyclades, so the cemetery's house tombs sit inside a wider Aegean network."}]},
{"id":"gr-042","num":"42","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"No bodies and hardly any metal were found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its people had warning, and a first thin fall of pumice was probably the warning they took.","answer":"Akrotiri","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late LM IA, c. 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1607 BCE, by radiocarbon</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Akrotiri</b> is a Bronze Age town on Thera, buried late in Late Minoan IA, around 1500 BCE, by the eruption of the island's volcano and preserved under metres of debris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The caldera it left measures as much as 83 square kilometres (32 square miles) and reaches 480 metres (1,570 feet) below sea level inside cliffs standing 300 metres (1,000 feet) above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earthquake had wrecked much of the town beforehand, and its people set about pulling down what was unsafe, levelling the rubble and rebuilding, work unfinished when the volcano went off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Large grooved ellipsoidal stones on that levelled rubble served much as a wrecking ball does, and the pottery and the scale of the repairs argue years, probably decades, between earthquake and eruption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An upstairs bedroom in the West House was still being plastered and painted when work stopped, two vessels of dried plaster and one of paint left standing in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> No bodies were found and hardly any metal or portable valuables, so the people had warning: the first thin fall of pumice, 3 centimetres (1 inch) deep and weathered as though open for months, was probably it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of 8,000 animal bones shows goats and sheep kept far more than pigs and cattle used mostly for draught, and two of the richest buildings were each raised from one quarry's stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fava beans in the West House jars were infested with the bean weevil, and a radiocarbon date on the beetles gives a midpoint of 1607 BCE, range 1744 to 1538, well before the pottery's date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same beetles date the season: they lay eggs in green pods in the field and emerge once the crop is stored, and adults had emerged here, so the eruption came in summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The frescoes survive in larger pieces than anywhere else and can be set back on their own walls, so whole rooms can be reconstructed: the fleet crossing between two towns, the blue monkeys, the boxing boys, and the crocus-gatherers bringing flowers to a seated goddess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Akrotiri","questions":["Bean weevils in the storage jars of the West House give <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a radiocarbon midpoint of 1607 BCE, and their life cycle puts the eruption in summer.","An earthquake wrecked much of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> years before the eruption, and the demolition, levelling and rebuilding that followed were still unfinished when the volcano went off."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","\"THERA — 2011,\" <i>Chronique des fouilles en ligne</i>, no. 2761, École française d'Athènes, https://chronique.efa.gr/?id=2761&kroute=report. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","\"THERA. — Akrotiri. — 1976,\" <i>Chronique des fouilles en ligne</i>, no. 21919, École française d'Athènes, https://chronique.efa.gr/?id=21919&r=report. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Fresco_of_a_fisherman%2C_Akrotiri%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Akrotiri","desc":"Fresco of a fisherman holding dolphinfish, Akrotiri, Santorini, Greece. Height: 1.10 m.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_of_a_fisherman,_Akrotiri,_Greece.jpg","alt":"Fresco of a fisherman, Akrotiri, Greece"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Akrotiri","at":[25.4036,36.3514]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the town read as having had warning?","a":"Because no bodies were found and hardly any metal or portable valuables, and the first thin fall of pumice was weathered as though open for months."},{"q":"Why can years be put between the earthquake and the eruption?","a":"Because the pottery and the scale of the repairs argue years, probably decades — the people had pulled down what was unsafe, levelled the rubble and were rebuilding when the volcano went off."},{"q":"Why do bean weevils date the season of the eruption?","a":"Because they lay eggs in green pods in the field and emerge once the crop is stored, and adults had emerged here, so it came in summer."}]},
{"id":"gr-043","num":"43","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tephra from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies in Late Minoan IA levels on Rhodes, Melos and Crete, well below the burnt horizon that ends the Cretan palaces.","answer":"Thera eruption","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Erupted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1627 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caldera filled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">within 2 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Thera eruption</b>, which vulcanologists call the Minoan eruption, is among the largest of the Holocene and emptied a Cycladic island of its people late in Late Minoan IA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The island was already a ring of land round a flooded bay, left by the Cape Riva eruption 22,000 years ago, with a lava island growing at its centre ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four phases are read off the deposits: a plinian pumice fall, surges thrown up where seawater reached the vent, a coarse ignimbrite 55 metres (180 feet) thick carrying blocks 10 metres (33 feet) across, and a finer, hotter one from several vents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third phase blew the central island apart and the fourth ran with the collapse of the caldera, a hollow 10 by 7 kilometres (6 by 4 miles) across and the youngest of at least four there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The erupted mass may reach 117 to 129 cubic kilometres (28 to 31 cubic miles), a ceiling rather than a settled figure, since the offshore deposits are poorly measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ash lies over 5 metres (16 feet) deep at Akrotiri and up to 50 metres (160 feet) elsewhere, and vulcanologists agree the sequence was rapid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prevailing winds carried the pumice south-east, sparing the mainland and western Crete and leaving eastern Crete under 10 centimetres (4 inches) at most, thin enough, some argue, for wind and rain to clear within a year or two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new caldera was sealed off from the sea while it collapsed, and only afterwards did water break in through a channel 3 kilometres (2 miles) long and fill it in under two days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Collapse therefore cannot have raised the waves that reached Crete; pyroclastic flows running into the sea did, and debris at Palaikastro records a run-up of at least 9 metres (30 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon has long put it near 1600 BCE and a Therasia shrub now favours the mid-16th century, but on either date its tephra lies in Late Minoan IA levels on Rhodes and Melos as well as Crete, before the Late Minoan IB fires that ended the Cretan palaces, so no direct link can be shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Thera eruption","questions":["Four phases carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a plinian pumice fall through wet surges to an ignimbrite 55 metres thick, and the caldera collapsed at the end of it.","The caldera left by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sealed from the sea while it formed, so the waves that reached Crete came from pyroclastic flows running into the water instead."],"tags":["event","geology","nature","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Dávid Karátson et al., \"Towards Reconstruction of the Lost Late Bronze Age Intra-Caldera Island of Santorini, Greece,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 7026, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5935677/. [Open access]","Paraskevi Nomikou et al., \"Post-Eruptive Flooding of Santorini Caldera and Implications for Tsunami Generation,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 7 (2016): 13332, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5105177/. [Open access]","Global Volcanism Program, \"Santorini,\" <i>Volcanoes of the World</i>, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=212040. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Kostas Sbonias, Iris Tzachili and Timothy J. Heaton, \"Olive Shrub Buried on Therasia Supports a Mid-16th Century BCE Date for the Thera Eruption,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 6994, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10147620/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Santorini_ASTER.jpg","title":"Thera eruption","desc":"Santorini island, Greece - EOS photo NASA.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santorini_ASTER.jpg","alt":"Santorini ASTER"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why can the caldera collapse not have raised the waves that reached Crete?","a":"Because the new caldera was sealed off from the sea while it collapsed, and water broke in only afterwards; pyroclastic flows running into the sea raised the waves instead."},{"q":"Why did the ash spare much of Crete?","a":"Because prevailing winds carried the pumice south-east, leaving eastern Crete under 10 centimetres at most, thin enough that some argue wind and rain cleared it within a year or two."},{"q":"Why can the eruption not be linked directly to the fall of the palaces?","a":"Because its tephra lies in Late Minoan IA levels on Rhodes, Melos and Crete, before the Late Minoan IB fires that ended them."}]},
{"id":"gr-044","num":"44","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A fleet moving between two towns fills the south wall of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and warriors come ashore under tower shields and boars'-tusk helmets on the north.","answer":"Flotilla fresco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1627 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Flotilla fresco</b> is the miniature frieze that ran round the top of Room 5, on the upper storey of the West House at Akrotiri on Thera, above the niches in its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is arguably the most complex single work of Aegean Bronze Age art yet recovered in anything like its entirety, and it answers to three names: 'the flotilla', 'the naval battle' and 'the river landscape'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The south wall, 40 centimetres (16 inches) high, carries a fleet of ships moving between two towns, and the north wall the same height, with a ceremony on a hilltop and warriors coming ashore under tower shields and boars'-tusk helmets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The east wall, half as tall, shows a river running through a landscape; the frieze went along the west wall too, of which little survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below it two life-sized nude fishermen stood in narrow panels, and under each window a painted band imitated the veining of coloured marble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Marinatos dug the fragments out in 1971 and 1972, and the frieze has drawn an enormous literature, some of it treating the fleet as the record of a particular voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who the ships belong to, who the warriors are and where the two towns stood are all disputed, and it is doubtful whether the evidence now available can settle any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Caution is owed on general grounds: Minoan painting offers no unmistakably particularised scene, historical or mythological, and one study of Aegean architectural painting argues that a painted town is built from an idea rather than from observation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the ships and towns do have is company: the Town Mosaic from Knossos before them, the silver Siege Rhyton from Grave Circle A at Mycenae beside them, and boats incised on Cycladic frying pans a thousand years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men carry more of this frieze than of most Minoan painting, the fishermen, the warriors and the ships' captains all being male, and the whole of it went under the ash late in Late Minoan IA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Flotilla fresco","questions":["Two life-sized nude fishermen stood in narrow panels below the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and under each window a painted band imitated the veining of coloured marble.","The ships of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> share features with boats incised on Cycladic frying pans a thousand years before, and its towns with the Town Mosaic from Knossos."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Alexandre Farnoux, \"Image et paysage: l'exemple des fresques de la Maison Ouest de Théra,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 15 (1990): 133–42, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ktema_0221-5896_1990_num_15_1_2013. [Open access]","Catherine Lyon Crawford, review of <i>Aegean Wall Painting: A Tribute to Mark Cameron</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.12.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.12.18/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Museum_of_Prehistoric_Thera_city_detail.jpg","title":"Flotilla fresco","desc":"City detail from the flotilla fresco. Museum of Prehistoric Thera.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museum_of_Prehistoric_Thera_city_detail.jpg","alt":"Museum of Prehistoric Thera city detail"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is caution owed to readings of the frieze as a particular voyage?","a":"Because Minoan painting offers no unmistakably particularised scene, historical or mythological, and a painted town has been argued to be built from an idea rather than from observation."},{"q":"Why does the fresco answer to three names?","a":"Because its walls carry three different subjects: a fleet moving between two towns, a hilltop ceremony with warriors coming ashore, and a river running through a landscape."},{"q":"Why does it stand out among Aegean painting for its figures?","a":"Because men carry more of this frieze than of most Minoan painting — the fishermen, the warriors and the ships' captains are all male."}]},
{"id":"gr-045","num":"45","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Thera lay empty for as much as a century or two under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the same ash has since been traced in lake beds in western Anatolia.","answer":"Theran tephra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Laid down</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-16th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ice-core spikes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1611, 1562 – 1555 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Theran tephra</b> is the ash and pumice thrown out by the Late Bronze Age eruption on Thera, and the layer by which that eruption is traced and dated across the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It buried its own island so deeply that Thera was uninhabitable for as much as a century or two, and Akrotiri was never lived in again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deep-sea cores from the southern Aegean and cuttings on Crete map where it fell, and a layer of it turned up in lake sediments in western Anatolia, further north and east than anyone had supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it landed thinly, on eastern Crete, its effect is argued over: one side holds that even a few centimetres would have wrecked the harvests, the other that so thin a layer enriches the soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument matters because one long-standing view made the eruption the cause of the fires that ended the Cretan palaces, through failed harvests or a fleet lost to the waves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The opposing view holds that it had nothing to do with them and puts the destructions down to local earthquakes, or to Mycenaeans arriving from the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tephra decides between them: it sits in Late Minoan IA levels at Trianda on Rhodes and Phylakopi on Melos as well as on Crete, so the eruption comes well before the Late Minoan IB destructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For its size the eruption put comparatively little sulfate into the air, so its power to cool the climate was smaller than its bulk suggests, though its columns rose more than 30 kilometres (19 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sulfate spikes in the polar ice at 1611 BCE and at 1562 to 1555 BCE are the leading candidates, and which is right turns on the eruption's own date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That date is still argued: radiocarbon on buried material has long pointed near 1600 BCE, a shrub buried on Therasia raises the odds on the middle of the 16th century, and dating of Egyptian objects puts the eruption before the reign of Ahmose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Theran tephra","questions":["One camp blamed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for ruining the farmland of eastern Crete, another read the same thin layer as harmless, or even as good for the soil.","The eruption that laid down <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put comparatively little sulfate into the air, so its effect on climate was smaller than its size would suggest."],"tags":["object","geology","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Kostas Sbonias, Iris Tzachili and Timothy J. Heaton, \"Olive Shrub Buried on Therasia Supports a Mid-16th Century BCE Date for the Thera Eruption,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 6994, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10147620/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Hendrik J. Bruins and Johannes van der Plicht, \"The Minoan Thera Eruption Predates Pharaoh Ahmose: Radiocarbon Dating of Egyptian 17th to Early 18th Dynasty Museum Objects,\" <i>PLOS One</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0330702, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12422448/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Akrotiri-Santorini_volcanic-pumice_Aegean-Sea_Greece.jpg","title":"Theran tephra","desc":"Two layers of pumice, long time eroded, mined in quarries, and in one layer small scale rock intrusions of the great minoan, Late-Bronze-Age (3,5 ka) volcanic eruption of the caldera island Thera/Santorini (Minoan Eruption).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akrotiri-Santorini_volcanic-pumice_Aegean-Sea_Greece.jpg","alt":"Akrotiri-Santorini volcanic-pumice Aegean-Sea Greece"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does where the tephra lies settle an old argument?","a":"Because it sits in Late Minoan IA levels at Trianda on Rhodes and Phylakopi on Melos as well as on Crete, so the eruption comes well before the Late Minoan IB destructions."},{"q":"Why is the effect of a thin ash fall disputed?","a":"Because one side holds that even a few centimetres would have wrecked the harvests and the other that so thin a layer enriches the soil."},{"q":"Why did so large an eruption cool the climate less than its bulk suggests?","a":"Because for its size it put comparatively little sulfate into the air, even though its columns rose more than 30 kilometres."}]},
{"id":"gr-046","num":"46","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cretan pottery lying at Kahun and Harageh in levels of the early 19th century BCE is the firmest early evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the palaces were new when it arrived.","answer":"Minoan trade with Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest finds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 19th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palatial era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1930 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan trade with Egypt</b> ran through most of the Bronze Age and is known almost entirely from objects found out of place: Cretan pottery in the Nile valley, Egyptian goods in the Cretan palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest contacts were indirect, with Levantine trading centres standing between the two, and only later did Crete and Egypt deal with one another face to face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The firmest early evidence is Cretan pottery of Middle Minoan IB to IIA date from Kahun and Harageh, in levels of the early 19th century BCE, when the first palaces at Knossos and Phaistos were new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What went out was Kamares ware, the eggshell-thin polychrome tableware of the palaces, whose use was confined to the palaces that made it, to the peak and cave sanctuaries, and to a few centres abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last group is the point: the foreign holdings look less like a market than like gifts passing between one ruling class and another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Coming the other way, a cache of 24 Neopalatial stone vessels fell from an upper room at Knossos, twelve of them rhyta and several of them Egyptian imports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Figurines, amulets, stone bowls and scarabs from the Mesara tombs were once read as direct Egyptian imports and used to derive the tholos tomb from Egypt or Nubia, an argument the dating has since undercut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ostrich eggs were mounted as Minoan rhyta from shells that can only have reached Crete from Egypt or Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egyptian religion left a mark as well: the Minoan genius, the animal-headed figure that carries a libation jug in Cretan art, is a reworking of Ta-weret, the hippopotamus goddess who in Egypt is a kindly spirit rather than a great divinity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Long after the palaces fell, the harbour town of Kommos on the south coast was taking in vases from Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy at once, the exchange by then plain commerce rather than a traffic between courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan trade with Egypt","questions":["Because Kamares ware went only to the palaces, the mountain sanctuaries and a few courts abroad, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks in its early phase like gift-giving between rulers rather than a market.","Ostrich-egg rhyta, Egyptian stone vases fallen from an upper room at Knossos and the jug-carrying Minoan genius are all marks left by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the island."],"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","greece","egypt","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 6: The Early Minoan Period: The Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-6-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Cretans_Bringing_Gifts%2C_Tomb_of_Rekhmire_MET_DT10883.jpg/1920px-Cretans_Bringing_Gifts%2C_Tomb_of_Rekhmire_MET_DT10883.jpg","title":"Minoan trade with Egypt","desc":"Facsimile, Rekhmire (TT 100), foreigners, Keftiu.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cretans_Bringing_Gifts,_Tomb_of_Rekhmire_MET_DT10883.jpg","alt":"Cretans Bringing Gifts, Tomb of Rekhmire"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does Kamares ware abroad look like gift rather than trade?","a":"Because its use was confined to the palaces that made it, to the peak and cave sanctuaries, and to a few centres abroad — which reads as goods passing between one ruling class and another."},{"q":"Why was the Egyptian derivation of the tholos tomb undercut?","a":"Because the figurines, amulets, stone bowls and scarabs from the Mesara tombs, once read as direct imports and used to argue it, have since been undercut by the dating."},{"q":"Why is the Minoan genius evidence of religious borrowing?","a":"Because the animal-headed figure carrying a libation jug in Cretan art is a reworking of Ta-weret, who in Egypt is a kindly spirit rather than a great divinity."}]},
{"id":"gr-047","num":"47","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Standing for the west beside a man of Punt, a Nubian and a Syrian, the man of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> personifies one quarter of the world in the Theban tombs.","answer":"Keftiu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb paintings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1479 – 1425 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Keftiu</b> is the name Egyptian texts and tomb paintings give to a land and a people beyond the sea, taken since the early 20th century to be Crete and the Aegean islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Theban tombs of the 18th Dynasty the four quarters of the world are personified in turn, and the man of Keftiu stands for the west, beside a man of Punt for the east, a Nubian for the south and a Syrian for the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is drawn as a people apart, unrelated to the Syrian who represents the north, and wears a waistcloth rather than Semitic robes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egyptian usage put Keftiu among the isles, at the far end of the ring of coasts a ship followed to reach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The identification was long resisted because Ptolemaic scribes used Keftiu to render the Greek name for Phoenicia, which made the earlier placing in the west look like a blunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What tells against that is the freight: the tombs of Puamra, Senenmut, Rekhmire and Menkheperraseneb show the Keftiu bringing metal vessels, and one long one-handled vase is drawn just like the jug held by the Cup-Bearer at Knossos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Rekhmire's tomb a man with a drawn sword turns back to speak to the next in the file while carrying a great white vase whose curved handles match those of an alabaster vase from Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Metropolitan Museum, which holds Nina de Garis Davies's full-size copies of the scene, files it both as gifts from the Keftiu and as Cretans bringing gifts, and puts the tomb at about 1479 to 1425 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name stuck to a pot as well: the plain straight-sided cup of Minoan Crete, the commonest drinking vessel of the Middle Minoan III period, is still called a Keftiu cup, or a Vapheio cup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Behind the ceremony lies ordinary traffic, Egyptian goods in the Cretan palaces and Cretan pottery in the Nile valley, which is what a procession of gift-bearers turns into ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Keftiu","questions":["Ptolemaic scribes used <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to render the Greek name for Phoenicia, which is why the earlier Egyptian placing of it in the west was long read as a blunder.","A long one-handled vase carried by the men of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Theban tombs is drawn just like the jug held by the Cup-Bearer at Knossos."],"tags":["people","history","archaeology","greece","egypt","bronze age"],"sources":["H. R. Hall, \"Keftiu and the Peoples of the Sea,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 157–89, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","\"Gifts from the Keftiu, Tomb of Rekhmire,\" facsimile by Nina de Garis Davies, accession 30.4.85, collection record, Metropolitan Museum of Art, https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/544611. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Minoan_-_Rhyton_in_the_Form_of_a_Bull%27s_Head_-_Walters_23194_-_Side.jpg","title":"Keftiu","desc":"This rhyton is carved in the round with details incised. It is interior drilled with a large hole in the top of the head, ending in a small hole through the mouth. The eyes are to be inlaid. There is a double axe on the forehead. One horn is broken and has been repaired.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_-_Rhyton_in_the_Form_of_a_Bull%27s_Head_-_Walters_23194_-_Side.jpg","alt":"Minoan - Rhyton in the Form of a Bull's Head - Side"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was the identification with Crete long resisted?","a":"Because Ptolemaic scribes used Keftiu to render the Greek name for Phoenicia, which made the earlier placing in the west look like a blunder."},{"q":"Why does the freight in the tomb paintings argue for the Aegean?","a":"Because the Keftiu bring metal vessels, and one long one-handled vase is drawn just like the jug held by the Cup-Bearer at Knossos."},{"q":"Why is the procession less exotic than it looks?","a":"Because behind the ceremony lies ordinary traffic — Egyptian goods in the Cretan palaces and Cretan pottery in the Nile valley — which is what a gift-bearing scene turns into ritual."}]},
{"id":"gr-048","num":"48","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The whole basis of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one sentence of Thucydides, saying that Minos was the first man tradition records as having built a navy.","answer":"Minoan thalassocracy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neopalatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or later</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan thalassocracy</b> is the sea-empire that later Greeks credited to Minos of Crete, and the question is how much Bronze Age reality lies behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says Minos is the first man known by tradition to have built a navy, that he made himself master of what the Greeks called their own sea and ruled the Cyclades, and that he put piracy down there to secure his revenues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that Minos sent the first colonies into most of those islands, driving out the Carians and setting his own sons over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere he says that once the navy existed the sea became easier to cross, the coasts turned to getting rich, and some towns grew strong enough to build walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether any of this describes the 2nd millennium BCE is unsettled, and the argument has run for decades against a backdrop of real Minoan expansion across the southern Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Such a power would have to belong either to the Neopalatial period, roughly 1750 to 1500 BCE, or to the century after it when Knossos alone still had a working palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kastri on Kythera, and probably Trianda on Rhodes, do look to have held Minoans and nobody else in the earliest Late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Ayia Irini, Phylakopi and Akrotiri, though, where Minoan influence runs deepest and Linear A was written, the evidence will not show Minoans in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Warships certainly existed, since one Theran frieze shows a fleet under way and a cluster of them lying offshore, but those ships need not be Cretan, nor kept up to police the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked whether Minoan influence across the Aegean, Cyprus, the Levant and Egypt can be explained by trade alone, together with Cretan craftsmen working abroad and no Minoan navy at all, Rutter answers yes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan thalassocracy","questions":["The trouble with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that Kastri on Kythera looks to have held Minoans and nobody else, while at Ayia Irini and Phylakopi no evidence puts them in charge.","The weak joint in any argument for a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that warships plainly existed in the Bronze Age Aegean but need not have been Cretan."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.4, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.8, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros%2C_Crete.jpg","title":"Minoan thalassocracy","desc":"Minoan copper ingot from Zakros, Crete.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minoan_copper_ingot_from_Zakros,_Crete.jpg","alt":"Minoan copper ingot from Zakros, Crete"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why will the islands where Minoan influence runs deepest not prove Minoan rule?","a":"Because at Ayia Irini, Phylakopi and Akrotiri, where Linear A was written, the evidence cannot show Minoans in charge — where Kastri and probably Trianda do look to have held Minoans and nobody else."},{"q":"Why do the Theran warships not settle the question?","a":"Because those ships need not be Cretan, nor kept up to police the sea, even though a fleet is plainly shown under way."},{"q":"Why can the whole reach be explained without a navy?","a":"Because asked whether trade alone with Cretan craftsmen working abroad would account for it, Rutter answers yes."}]},
{"id":"gr-049","num":"49","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In the earliest Late Bronze Age <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks to have held Cretans and nobody else, which cannot be said of any Cycladic town.","answer":"Minoan Kythera","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Minoan phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Minoan Kythera</b> is the Cretan settlement on the island lying between Crete and the southern Peloponnese, and the clearest case anywhere of Minoans living outside Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre was Kastri, on the east coast, probably first settled permanently by colonists from Crete in Middle Minoan IA, around 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the earliest phases of the Late Bronze Age the place looks to have held Minoans and nobody else, which cannot be said of the Cycladic towns where Minoan influence is strongest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its dead were laid in chamber tombs, and both the tombs and the goods put in them are thoroughly Minoan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is where an old theory of Evans's comes apart: he made the lords of the Shaft Graves at Mycenae Cretan conquerors, and Kythera shows what Minoans abroad actually did with their dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Above the settlement, on the hill called Vouno, stood a peak sanctuary of the Cretan kind, one of a small group whose worshippers left stone libation tables and ladles cut with Linear A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Peak sanctuaries were a Cretan institution and few of them lasted: only nine of the 39 known survive into Late Minoan I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other signs incised on objects from the island have been claimed as Linear A as well, and are not confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The island sat on the way to Laconia, where Minoan stone-workers were after the green-flecked porphyry called lapis Lacedaemonius by early Late Minoan IA, and where a Minoan potter may have been living, out of Crete or out of Kythera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kastri stayed in step with Crete to the end of the palaces, the two Late Minoan IB painting styles turning up together in its deposits as they do at Ayia Irini on Keos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Minoan Kythera","questions":["A peak sanctuary of the Cretan kind stood on the hill above the settlement of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its worshippers left stone libation tables cut with Linear A.","The lords of the Shaft Graves were once made Cretan conquerors, but the chamber tombs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show what Minoans abroad really did with their dead."],"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Thomas Palaima and Aren Wilson-Wright, review of Brent Davis, <i>Minoan Stone Vessels with Linear A Inscriptions</i>, Aegaeum 36, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.11.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.11.36/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Kythera","at":[22.9975,36.2575]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Kythera the clearest case of Minoans abroad?","a":"Because in the earliest phases of the Late Bronze Age Kastri looks to have held Minoans and nobody else, which cannot be said of the Cycladic towns where Minoan influence is strongest."},{"q":"Why does the island undercut an old theory of Evans's?","a":"Because he made the lords of the Shaft Graves at Mycenae Cretan conquerors, and Kythera shows what Minoans abroad actually did with their dead — chamber tombs whose goods are thoroughly Minoan."},{"q":"Why does its peak sanctuary matter?","a":"Because peak sanctuaries were a Cretan institution and few lasted — only nine of the 39 known survive into Late Minoan I — so one on Vouno with Linear A libation tables marks the settlement as Cretan."}]},
{"id":"gr-050","num":"50","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Mallia, Phaistos and Zakros never held a court again after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only Knossos went on, under someone nobody can name.","answer":"destruction of the Minoan palaces","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1490 – 1470 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>destruction of the Minoan palaces</b> is the wave of burning that ended the Neopalatial period on Crete, at some point around 1490 to 1470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It reached virtually every Minoan settlement of any consequence: the palaces at Mallia, Phaistos and Zakros, the villa complexes at Ayia Triada and Tylissos, the town of Gournia, the village at Myrtos Pyrgos and the lone villa at Sklavokampos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the destructions involved fire, and things were left where they lay: the Sanctuary Rhyton, which shows a peak sanctuary in considerable detail, came out of the burnt level at Zakros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mallia, Phaistos and Zakros were never rebuilt as palaces, and only Knossos went on, though it is far from clear who was in charge of it afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earlier horizon had done something similar two and a half centuries before, when Knossos and Phaistos were wrecked at the end of Middle Minoan IIB, probably by an earthquake, and Quartier Mu at Mallia burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What caused the later wave is not agreed, and the argument now runs between human agency and natural, with a few sites read as having suffered both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor has anyone settled which natural disaster is meant: earthquake is the usual answer and still wants proving, since buildings and layers cannot always show when a tremor turned into a social collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Thera eruption was long a candidate, but its ash lies in Late Minoan IA levels well before this horizon, and a post-eruption Late Minoan IA phase is now clear at Mochlos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the burning was one event is unsettled too, ceramic work having read it as a generation or more of unquiet years while the current feeling among specialists is that the destructions fell relatively close together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is certain is that they cannot be lined up across the island, since the last fires at Chania in the west and Zakros in the east will not correlate easily with what happened at Knossos in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"destruction of the Minoan palaces","questions":["Earthquake is the usual answer to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and still wants proving, since buildings and layers cannot show when a tremor turned into a social collapse.","The last fires at Chania in the west and at Zakros in the east will not line up with Knossos in the middle, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may be several events."],"tags":["event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Luca Girella, review of Thomas M. Brogan and Erik Hallager, eds., <i>LM IB Pottery: Relative Chronology and Regional Differences</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2013.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.05.08/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 10: Middle Minoan Crete,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-10-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Knossos_-_North_Portico_02.jpg/1920px-Knossos_-_North_Portico_02.jpg","title":"destruction of the Minoan palaces","desc":"The North Portico in Knossos, Crete, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_-_North_Portico_02.jpg","alt":"Knossos - North Portico 02"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Thera eruption no longer a candidate cause?","a":"Because its ash lies in Late Minoan IA levels well before this horizon, and a post-eruption Late Minoan IA phase is now clear at Mochlos."},{"q":"Why does earthquake as a cause still want proving?","a":"Because buildings and layers cannot always show when a tremor turned into a social collapse, so the usual answer is not demonstrated."},{"q":"Why can the burning not be lined up across the island?","a":"Because the last fires at Chania in the west and Zakros in the east will not correlate easily with what happened at Knossos in the middle."}]},
{"id":"gr-051","num":"51","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Shaft-niche graves found nowhere else on the island, and chamber tombs with dromoi leaning inward in the mainland manner, cluster round <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the palaces burn.","answer":"Mycenaean Knossos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Final Palatial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1490 – 1375 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Knossos</b> is the century or so after the Cretan palaces burned, when Knossos alone still worked as a palace and the question is who was running it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period, Late Minoan II to IIIA2 and roughly 1490 to 1375 BCE, is now called Final Palatial Crete, and it is when a Knossian hegemony rises over the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around the palace a set of richly furnished tombs appears, the so-called Warrior Graves, in cemeteries at Zapher Papoura, Ayios Ioannis and Sellopoulo, and further afield at Katsambas, Archanes, Phaistos and Chania in the west of the island, most of them newly opened in Late Minoan II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their forms are not Minoan: shaft-niche graves, a small group found nowhere else on Crete, and chamber tombs with long dromoi whose walls lean inward in the mainland manner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their contents are not either, with weapons buried beside the dead, hoards of bronze vessels, quantities of jewellery, mainland shapes such as the two-handled kylix, and the first amber to reach the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three readings have long been on offer: a Mycenaean king who conquered Crete around 1500 BCE, a viceroy installed by one, or a Minoan lord keeping Mycenaean mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The graves are the evidence everyone reaches for, and the field is split down the middle over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alberti takes them for the burials of Mycenaean conquerors, while allowing that the mainland parallels are stronger in the pottery than in the jewellery, the bronzes or the weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Preston writes about the same tombs without using the words conqueror or warrior grave at all, reading them as elite burials, and an earlier study of the grave goods sorted the dead into three ranks and argued they were Minoans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pottery cuts both ways: the mainland shapes are made at Knossos rather than imported, and at Knossos this pottery comes only out of the tombs, while elsewhere on Crete the same wares turn up only in settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Knossos","questions":["One scholar reads the Warrior Graves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the tombs of conquerors while another describes the same burials without using the word conqueror once.","Weapons beside the dead, hoards of bronze vessels and the first amber to reach Crete mark out the rich tombs around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from anything Minoan before them."],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","J. D. Muhly, review of Gerald Cadogan, Eleni Hatzaki and Antonis Vasilakis, eds., <i>Knossos: Palace, City, State</i>, BSA Studies 12, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.09.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.09.17/. [Open access]","Sean Hemingway, review of Nicoletta Momigliano, ed., <i>Knossos Pottery Handbook: Neolithic and Bronze Age (Minoan)</i>, BSA Studies 14, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.05.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.05.21/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Antiquities_from_the_cemetery_of_Zafer_Papoura.png/1920px-Antiquities_from_the_cemetery_of_Zafer_Papoura.png","title":"Mycenaean Knossos","desc":"Tripod Plaster Hearth and group of Bronze Vessels found in south-west corner of Chamber-Tomb (No. 14).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antiquities_from_the_cemetery_of_Zafer_Papoura.png","alt":"Antiquities from the cemetery of Zafer Papoura"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Knossos","at":[25.1631,35.2981]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the Warrior Graves the evidence everyone reaches for?","a":"Because their forms and contents are not Minoan — shaft-niche graves, mainland-style chamber tombs, weapons beside the dead, bronze hoards and the first amber on the island."},{"q":"Why does the field split over what they mean?","a":"Because Alberti takes them for the burials of Mycenaean conquerors while Preston reads the same tombs as elite burials without using the word conqueror, and an earlier study sorted the dead into three ranks and called them Minoans."},{"q":"Why does the pottery cut both ways?","a":"Because the mainland shapes are made at Knossos rather than imported, and at Knossos this pottery comes only out of the tombs where elsewhere on Crete the same wares turn up only in settlements."}]},
{"id":"gr-052","num":"52","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Where Pylos kept its records in one archive room, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of deposits scattered through the palace, and not all of them need be of one date.","answer":"Knossos Linear B archive","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chariot Tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1390 – 1370 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Knossos Linear B archive</b> is the body of clay tablets found in the palace, written in an early dialect of Greek and preserved because fire baked them hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unlike Pylos, Knossos has no central archive room: its tablets came out of deposits scattered through the building, and those deposits need not all be of one date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are more broken than the Pylos texts and carry fewer signs apiece, which makes reading across them hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Something like 66 to 75 scribal hands have been counted in the corpus, most of them working inside one sector of the palace economy, on one commodity or place or group of people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A statistical study drew 1,331 personal names out of 49 of those hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hand 117 is the sheep scribe, and his tablets usually set a second man above the shepherd, a 'collector' read by some as the flock's owner and by others as an official answerable to the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shepherds may themselves have been supervisors, contracting others to do the day's pasturing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hands 103 and 105 keep the textile records, while the Room of the Chariot Tablets stands apart from the rest of the corpus, sharing fewer names with it than chance would give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When any of it was written is the oldest quarrel about it: the tablets were long tied to the palace's fall early in Late Minoan IIIA2, and Palmer, Hallager and others move most of them to the middle of the 13th century BCE, while Driessen has redated even the Chariot Tablets by twenty years and the specialists in a single volume can be found using two rival chronologies at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The standard edition of the Linear B texts, Ventris and Chadwick's <i>Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i> of 1956 and 1973, was replaced only in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Knossos Linear B archive","questions":["Sheep are counted by one scribal hand and cloth by two others in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of its scribes keeping to a single commodity, place or group of people.","Above each shepherd in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands a second man, read by some as the flock's owner and by others as an official answerable to the palace."],"tags":["text","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Stavroula Nikoloudis, review of Hedvig Landenius Enegren, <i>The People of Knossos: Prosopographical Studies in the Knossos Linear B Archives</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.05.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.05.10/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of John Killen, ed., <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, eds., <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Linear_B_tablet_KN_Ra_1548_%28photo_and_tablet_drawing%29.png","title":"Knossos Linear B archive","desc":"Linear B tablet KN Ra 1548.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_KN_Ra_1548_(photo_and_tablet_drawing).png","alt":"Linear B tablet KN Ra 1548 (photo and tablet drawing)"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is this archive harder to read than the one at Pylos?","a":"Because Knossos has no central archive room, its tablets coming from deposits scattered through the building that need not all be of one date, and they are more broken with fewer signs apiece."},{"q":"Why can individual scribes be traced through the corpus?","a":"Because 66 to 75 hands have been counted, most working inside one sector of the palace economy, on one commodity or place or group of people."},{"q":"Why is the date of the tablets the oldest quarrel about them?","a":"Because they were long tied to the palace's fall early in Late Minoan IIIA2 while Palmer, Hallager and others move most of them to the mid-13th century, and specialists in one volume can be found using two rival chronologies at once."}]},
{"id":"gr-053","num":"53","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"No single centre ruled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> again after the fire at Knossos, and the story from there is fragmentation and a material culture growing more regional.","answer":"Postpalatial Crete","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1385 – 1375 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Linear B in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 1250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Postpalatial Crete</b> is the island after the fire that gutted Knossos early in Late Minoan IIIA2, about 1385 to 1375 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Everyone takes that fire for human work, since the signs of a Mycenaean military presence around Knossos vanish with it, and the lead in the southern Aegean passes to the mainland kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independent polities appear elsewhere on the island for the first time since the Late Minoan IB destructions, and the story from there is fragmentation and a material culture growing more regional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Mesara a huge megaron-like building went up at Ayia Triada over the two rich villas that had run the region before, with an outsize two-storeyed stoa 75 metres (82 yards) north-east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two thick-walled chamber tombs stand above ground nearby, of the form that becomes the island's commonest in these centuries, and the better preserved held the painted stone sarcophagus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> At Kommos, the harbour 16 kilometres (10 miles) south, ashlar from the old Neopalatial building was re-used to raise Building P, a six-gallery shed that is the largest structure of its period on Crete and seems to have housed ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ruins gave thousands of fragments of a plain transport amphora made for the job, and vases from Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy, none of which reached Ayia Triada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second kingdom shows in Linear B tablets from 13th-century levels at Chania in the west, ancient Kydonia, where the Bronze Age town lies beneath five later cities and the small finds outshine the buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> East Crete probably had polities of its own, its pottery being unlike that of the centre and west, while the wares of these centuries lose their lustrous dark-on-light decoration and turn largely plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Worship shrank to fit: the shrine of the double axes at Knossos is a metre and a half (5 feet) square and was left with its furniture standing, and the town shrine at Gournia belonged to no palace but stood alone near the top of the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Postpalatial Crete","questions":["A six-gallery shed at Kommos, the largest building raised anywhere on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was assembled from the cut ashlar of the palace it stood over and seems to have housed ships.","Linear B tablets from 13th-century levels at Chania mark out a second kingdom in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Bronze Age town lies beneath five later cities."],"tags":["era","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 15: Minoan Religion,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-15-narrative/. [Open access]","Sean Hemingway, review of Nicoletta Momigliano, ed., <i>Knossos Pottery Handbook: Neolithic and Bronze Age (Minoan)</i>, BSA Studies 14, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.05.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.05.21/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 13: Minoan Domestic and Funerary Architecture of the Neopalatial and Post-Palatial Periods,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-13-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Clay_male_figurine%2C_hands_on_chest%2C_Crete%2C_Postpalatial%2C_AMH%2C_145258.jpg/1920px-Clay_male_figurine%2C_hands_on_chest%2C_Crete%2C_Postpalatial%2C_AMH%2C_145258.jpg","title":"Postpalatial Crete","desc":"Clay male figurine with hands on chest wearing an apron. Crete, Palaikastro, around 1300-1200 BCE, Postpalatial period. Archaeological Museum of Heraklion.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_male_figurine,_hands_on_chest,_Crete,_Postpalatial,_AMH,_145258.jpg","alt":"Clay male figurine, hands on chest, Crete, Postpalatial, AMH"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Knossos fire taken for human work?","a":"Because the signs of a Mycenaean military presence around Knossos vanish with it, and the lead in the southern Aegean passes to the mainland kingdoms."},{"q":"Why is Kommos read as having housed ships?","a":"Because Building P is a six-gallery shed, the largest structure of its period on Crete, whose ruins gave thousands of fragments of a plain transport amphora made for the job."},{"q":"Why is the island read as fragmenting?","a":"Because independent polities appear for the first time since the Late Minoan IB destructions, the material culture grows more regional, and east Cretan pottery is unlike that of the centre and west."}]},
{"id":"gr-054","num":"54","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Praisos, the town of the people who spoke <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is archaeologically ordinary: a handful of inscriptions apart, nothing there distinguishes it from any other Cretan city.","answer":"Eteocretan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscriptions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Eteocretan</b> is the name given to a pre-Greek language of eastern Crete, known from a handful of inscriptions cut in the Greek alphabet between the 6th and the 3rd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word is Homer's: in a line Strabo quotes, Crete holds a mixture of tongues, and among its peoples are Achaeans, Eteocretans proud of heart, Cydonians, Dorians and Pelasgians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Staphylus gave the Dorians the east of the island, the Cydonians the west and the Eteocretans the south, and put the other peoples in the plains because they were the stronger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo thought it reasonable that the Eteocretans and the Cydonians were the island's own and the rest incomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their town was Praisos, which held the temple of Dictaean Zeus, lay 60 stadia above the sea between Samonium and the Cherronesus, and was razed to the ground by the men of Hierapytna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Halbherr found the first of the inscriptions at the foot of the Altar Hill at Praisos in 1884, and another came out of the same hill in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are written in Greek letters and are not Greek, so every attempt on them has had to guess at the language behind the script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Conway, publishing the 1901 stone, worked on the hypothesis that the language was Indo-European, on the ground that its clusters of vowels and consonants and its word endings were all ordinary in such languages around 500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hardest modern finding about the Eteocretans is negative: apart from those few texts, nothing in the material remains of Praisos marks it out from any other Cretan city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A survey of its region has traced occupation at the town from about 1200 to 500 BCE and a refuge settlement in the hills nearby, so a people Homer names are visible in what they wrote and almost nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Eteocretan","questions":["The people whose language we call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the south of Crete in Staphylus' division, which gave the east to the Dorians and the west to the Cydonians.","One hypothesis about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, tried on a stone from the Altar Hill at Praisos, makes it an Indo-European language, judging by its endings and its letter clusters."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.6, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.12, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D12. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Post-Minoan Crete: Proceedings of the First Colloquium on Post-Minoan Crete</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2000.05.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.05.17/. [Open access]","R. S. Conway, \"The Pre-Hellenic Inscriptions of Praesos,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 125–56, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]","R. C. Bosanquet, \"Excavations at Praesos. I,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 8 (1901–1902): 231–70, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1901-1902_8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Eteocretan_inscritpion_in_alphabet%2C_Praisos%2C_4_th_c_BC%2C_AM_Herakleon%2C_258462.jpg/1920px-Eteocretan_inscritpion_in_alphabet%2C_Praisos%2C_4_th_c_BC%2C_AM_Herakleon%2C_258462.jpg","title":"Eteocretan","desc":"Eteocretan inscritpion. Minoan language in the alphabet. Praisos, 4 th century BCE. Archaeological museum Iraklio (Herakleon).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eteocretan_inscritpion_in_alphabet,_Praisos,_4_th_c_BC,_AM_Herakleon,_258462.jpg","alt":"Eteocretan inscritpion in alphabet, Praisos, 4 th c BCE, AM Herakleon"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why has every attempt on the inscriptions had to guess?","a":"Because they are written in Greek letters and are not Greek, so the language behind the script has to be supposed before anything can be read."},{"q":"Why did Conway assume an Indo-European language?","a":"Because he judged that the clusters of vowels and consonants and the word endings were all ordinary in such languages around 500 BCE."},{"q":"Why is the hardest modern finding a negative one?","a":"Because apart from those few texts, nothing in the material remains of Praisos marks it out from any other Cretan city."}]},
{"id":"gr-055","num":"55","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Minos went up to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> every ninth year, Strabo reports, and came back with written commandments he said were the god's own.","answer":"Idaean Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Idaean Cave</b> is a sanctuary on Mount Ida, the loftiest mountain in Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo puts Ida in the widest part of the island, roughly circular, with a circuit of six hundred stadia and the best of the Cretan cities around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It should not be confused with Dicte, though the ancients did: Strabo corrects Aratus for setting Dicte close to Ida when it lies a thousand stadia east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cave was the cave of Zeus, and Strabo reports that Minos would go up to it every ninth year and come back with commandments in writing that he said were the god's own ordinances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the same mountain Strabo has the Idaean Dactyls, said to be the first settlers of its lower slopes, whom every version of the story credits with the first working of iron, though he records that others put them in Phrygia instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Worship there was old: Kamares ware, the eggshell pottery of the first Cretan palaces, has come from the mountain, which puts people on it by about 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Votive bronze double axes, the special emblem of the Cretan Zeus, have been found in cave sanctuaries of the god on Ida and on Dicte alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Caves were used for worship across the Greek world, and because a cave's rock contains everything that happens inside it, such places keep unusually direct traces of how people once moved and where they set objects down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A recent study of this cave, written by a member of its own excavation team, reads what was found there together with what the place itself does to anyone standing in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What the mountain kept in the end was its names: the smiths who were said to have struck the first iron on its slopes, and the god whose laws a king was said to have fetched down out of its cave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Idaean Cave","questions":["Votive bronze double axes, the special emblem of the Cretan Zeus, turn up in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and in the cave on Dicte alike, and Kamares ware puts people on the mountain by 1900 BCE.","Aratus set Dicte close to Ida and Strabo corrected him, which is one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is so often confused with the other cave of the Cretan Zeus."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.4, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D4. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.8, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D8. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.4.12, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D12. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.3.22, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D22. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, \"The Palace of Knossos,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 6 (1899–1900), https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1899-1900_6. [Open access]","Carolyn Laferrière, review of Stella Katsarou and Alexander Nagel, eds., <i>Cave and Worship in Ancient Greece: New Approaches to Landscape and Ritual</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2022.10.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.03/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Idaean_Cave_panorama.jpg/1920px-Idaean_Cave_panorama.jpg","title":"Idaean Cave","desc":"Idaean Cave (Ideon Antron) or Cave of Zeus, Mount Ida (Psiloritis), Crete, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idaean_Cave_panorama.jpg","alt":"Idaean Cave panorama"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Idaean Cave","at":[24.7725,35.2267]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the ancients confuse this mountain with Dicte?","a":"Because the two were run together, and Strabo has to correct Aratus for setting Dicte close to Ida when it lies a thousand stadia east of it."},{"q":"Why do caves keep unusually direct traces of worship?","a":"Because a cave's rock contains everything that happens inside it, so how people moved and where they set objects down survives in place."},{"q":"Why is the cave's use known to be old?","a":"Because Kamares ware, the eggshell pottery of the first Cretan palaces, has come from the mountain, which puts people on it by about 1900 BCE."}]},
{"id":"gr-056","num":"56","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The palaces of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept their accounts in Linear B, an early form of Greek, and stood behind boulder walls of a kind the palaces of Crete never had.","answer":"Mycenaean civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shaft graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean civilisation</b> is the Late Bronze Age culture of the Greek mainland, named after Mycenae in the Argolid; specialists use the label interchangeably with Late Helladic, the mainland's own pottery sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens in a burst of wealth, the shaft graves at Mycenae holding gold and silver vessels, weapons, amber and stone vases richer than anything else found in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of that metalwork is Minoan in shape and probably Cretan in making, so the first mainland princes seem to have risen through a close relationship with the palaces of Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their heirs were laid in tholos tombs, round chambers roofed by corbelled vaults and entered down a long passage, the earliest in Messenia and then in the Argolid, Laconia and Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Palaces followed from about 1400 BCE at Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos, Thebes, Gla, Orchomenos and Athens, each built around a megaron of porch, vestibule and throne room with a hearth ringed by four columns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Unlike Crete's, these palaces stood behind fortifications finished about 1250 BCE, of boulders so large that Strabo reports Tiryns as walled by the Cyclopes; the same rulers drained a whole Boeotian lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their clerks wrote Greek in Linear B, and archives survive at Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos and Thebes and at Knossos and Chania on Crete, 3,369 tablets at Knossos alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> They record a state under a <i>wanax</i>, or king, with a <i>lawagetas</i> whose title means 'leader of the people', landholding <i>telestai</i>, and the <i>damos</i>, a village body that held land in common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its reach ran far beyond Greece, Baltic amber coming south from about 1700 BCE and the ship wrecked off Cape Gelidonya carrying copper and tin ingots, bronze scrap, pottery and weights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Then it ended in fire around 1200 BCE, Mycenae, Tiryns, Thebes and Pylos all burning, and specialists blame some combination of raiding, drought, revolt, lost trade and changed warfare rather than one cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean civilisation","questions":["Beginning in the shaft graves of the Argolid, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread across the mainland and reached Crete, where the Knossos archive was kept in Greek rather than in the island's older script.","Every mainland palace of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned around 1200 BCE, and no single cause among raiding, drought, revolt, lost trade and changing warfare has been made to account for all of it."],"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Chronology Overview,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/chronology/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.11, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D6%3Asection%3D11. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Facsimile_of_a_Mycenaean_fresco_%28Tiryns%29%2C_Woman_Carrying_an_Ivory_Pyxis%2C_by_Emile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_p%C3%A8re%2C_1912.jpg","title":"Mycenaean civilisation","desc":"Woman Carrying an Ivory Pyxis, Facsimile of reconstructed fresco from Tiryns, Greece. Source of identification.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facsimile_of_a_Mycenaean_fresco_(Tiryns),_Woman_Carrying_an_Ivory_Pyxis,_by_Emile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_p%C3%A8re,_1912.jpg","alt":"Facsimile of a Mycenaean fresco (Tiryns), Woman Carrying an Ivory Pyxis, by Emile Gilliéron père, 1912"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Mycenaean civilisation","at":[22.7575,37.7303],"kind":"region","label":[22.25,37.85],"area":[[20.45,38.85],[21.2,39.45],[22.3,39.75],[23.4,39.65],[23.55,39.05],[24.65,38.65],[24.35,37.65],[23.45,37.4],[23.35,36.4],[24.5,35.85],[26.55,35.65],[26.35,34.75],[23.35,34.85],[22.45,36.25],[21.6,36.65],[21,37.55],[20.75,38.3]]},"why":[{"q":"Why do the first mainland princes look tied to Crete?","a":"Because much of the shaft-grave metalwork is Minoan in shape and probably Cretan in making, so their wealth seems to have risen through a close relationship with the Cretan palaces."},{"q":"Why do these palaces differ from the Cretan ones in plan and setting?","a":"Because each is built around a megaron of porch, vestibule and throne room with a hearth ringed by four columns, and unlike Crete's they stand behind fortifications finished about 1250 BCE."},{"q":"Why is no single cause given for their end?","a":"Because Mycenae, Tiryns, Thebes and Pylos all burned around 1200 BCE, and specialists blame some combination of raiding, drought, revolt, lost trade and changed warfare."}]},
{"id":"gr-057","num":"57","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A merchant who had made his fortune in indigo and tea, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut a great trench through Hisarlik in the 1870s and told the world he had found Priam's Troy.","answer":"Heinrich Schliemann","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 January 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 December 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Excavated 1870 – 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mycenae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Excavated from 1876</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Heinrich Schliemann</b>, born on 6 January 1822 at Neubukow in Mecklenburg-Schwerin, the son of a Protestant clergyman, was the merchant who put Troy and Mycenae into the historical record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He learned languages as a clerk in Amsterdam after a shipwreck stranded him in 1841, traded indigo and tea from St Petersburg, and was out of business altogether by 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In his autobiography he said that as a boy he had settled on digging up Troy, a story his modern biographers read as a piece of staging rather than a memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He turned the first sod at Hisarlik in April 1870 and drove a huge trench down through the mound, demolishing the upper strata, by his own account, to reach the city he expected below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1873 he cut a hoard of gold out of the wall with a knife while his workmen rested and carried it off in his wife's shawl; the Ottoman government was owed half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He called it 'Priam's Treasure', the largest building 'Priam's Palace' and the entrance 'the Scaean Gate', and those three names were enough for most scholars to dismiss the whole book.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Mycenae from July 1876 he opened graves inside the citadel and lifted out gold masks, diadems and breastplates, taking the dead for Agamemnon and the companions murdered with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both identifications were out by centuries: those graves hold burials of about 1650 to 1500 BCE, and the second city he took for Priam's long predates the Troy VIIa or VIh that specialists now prefer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He dug on at Tiryns, sinking shafts in 1876 and clearing the palace from 1884, and at Orchomenos, latterly with Wilhelm Dörpfeld, and he died suddenly at Naples on 26 December 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His reputation has never settled: work by Calder and Traill made him a pathological liar for many, while others answer that his mistakes were a pioneer's and that before him the Bronze Age Aegean was unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Heinrich Schliemann","questions":["At Mycenae in 1876 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lifted gold masks, diadems and breastplates out of graves inside the citadel and took the dead for Agamemnon and the companions murdered with him.","Cutting a hoard out of the wall with a knife while his workmen rested, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried it off in his wife's shawl, keeping a find the Ottoman government was owed half of."],"tags":["person","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Henry Schliemann, <i>Ilios: The City and Country of the Trojans</i> (London: John Murray, 1880), 1–4, 40–41, 59, https://archive.org/details/ilioscitycountry1880schl. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 1–14, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Henry Schliemann, <i>Mycenae: A Narrative of Researches and Discoveries at Mycenae and Tiryns</i> (London: John Murray, 1878), preface by W. E. Gladstone, https://archive.org/details/mycenaenarrative00schl. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of Stefanie Samida, <i>Heinrich Schliemann</i>, UTB Profile, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2012.12.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.12.25/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Daheim_Kalender_1892_-_Johann_Ludwig_Heinrich_Julius_Schliemann.png","title":"Heinrich Schliemann","desc":"An illustration of Heinrich Schliemann, from a German almanac of 1892.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daheim_Kalender_1892_-_Johann_Ludwig_Heinrich_Julius_Schliemann.png","alt":"Daheim Kalender 1892 - Johann Ludwig Heinrich Julius Schliemann"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why did most scholars dismiss his Troy book?","a":"Because he called the gold hoard 'Priam's Treasure', the largest building 'Priam's Palace' and the entrance 'the Scaean Gate' — three names that assumed what the excavation was meant to test."},{"q":"Why were both of his great identifications wrong?","a":"Because the Mycenae graves hold burials of about 1650 to 1500 BCE, centuries before any Agamemnon, and the second city he took for Priam's long predates the Troy VIIa or VIh specialists now prefer."},{"q":"Why has his reputation never settled?","a":"Because work by Calder and Traill made him a pathological liar for many, while others answer that his mistakes were a pioneer's and that before him the Bronze Age Aegean was unknown."}]},
{"id":"gr-058","num":"58","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rich in gold and makes it Agamemnon's seat, and its walls of boulders were credited by later Greeks to the Cyclopes.","answer":"Mycenae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tholos tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1525 – 1275 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Razed by Argos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">468 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenae</b> is the citadel above the Argive plain that gave its name to the civilisation of Late Bronze Age Greece, and which Homer makes Agamemnon's seat and calls rich in gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greeks made Perseus its founder, and Pausanias gives two accounts of the name, the cap that fell from his scabbard there, and the mushroom he drank from, both called <i>myces</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its walls are of boulders so large that the Greeks credited them to the Cyclopes, first raised late in the 14th century BCE and finished in their final form around 1250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Inside them a palace stood on the summit, dominating the highest point of the citadel and sprawling over artificial terraces, above the shaft graves of much earlier kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nine great tholos tombs lie around the citadel, built between about 1525 and 1275 BCE, the largest 14.5 metres (48 feet) across and faced with sculpture in relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> A destruction level inside the walls marks the end of its palatial age around 1200 BCE, when Tiryns, Thebes and Pylos burned as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> People stayed on, and when Xerxes invaded, Mycenae alone in the Argolid sent men to fight beside the Spartans at Thermopylae, eighty of them by Pausanias's count, while Argos held back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Argos had refused to march unless given a share of the command, and it feared that a Mycenae trading on its ancient prestige would come to dispute the leadership of the Argolid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 468 BCE the Argives besieged and stormed the town, sold its people into slavery, dedicated a tenth of them to the god and razed it, though the walls were beyond removing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias still saw them six centuries later, and the gate with the lions; Thucydides had already warned that Mycenae was small, and that a city's power is not read off its external show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenae","questions":["Alone in the Argolid, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent eighty men to fight beside the Spartans at Thermopylae, and Argos, which had held back, besieged and razed it in 468 BCE.","Pausanias gives two accounts of how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> got its name, one from the cap that fell from Perseus's scabbard there and one from the mushroom he drank from, both called <i>myces</i>."],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 7.180, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D7%3Acard%3D181. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.3–6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.65, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0084%3Abook%3D11%3Achapter%3D65. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.10, trans. Thomas Hobbes, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0247%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Eberzahnhelm_auf_Fresko_aus_Mykene_140.jpg/1920px-Eberzahnhelm_auf_Fresko_aus_Mykene_140.jpg","title":"Mycenae","desc":"Boar tusk helmets on fresco from Mycenae.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eberzahnhelm_auf_Fresko_aus_Mykene_140.jpg","alt":"Eberzahnhelm auf Fresko aus Mykene 140"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Mycenae","at":[22.7575,37.7303]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Argos destroy the town in 468 BCE?","a":"Because it feared that a Mycenae trading on its ancient prestige would come to dispute the leadership of the Argolid, Mycenae having sent men to Thermopylae while Argos held back."},{"q":"Why did the Greeks credit the walls to the Cyclopes?","a":"Because they are built of boulders so large that no ordinary builder seemed able to have raised them."},{"q":"Why does Thucydides warn against reading the site as it stands?","a":"Because Mycenae was small, and a city's power is not to be read off its external show."}]},
{"id":"gr-059","num":"59","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Two beasts with their forepaws on altars flank a column in the triangle above the door of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the main entrance to the citadel of Mycenae.","answer":"Lion Gate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still standing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lion Gate</b> is the main entrance to the citadel of Mycenae, named for the two opposing animals carved in relief and set in the triangle above its door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek ministry that keeps the site calls it the symbol of the Mycenaean rulers' power, the acropolis is entered through it, and the ramp up to the palace begins there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relieving triangle it fills is a typical feature of Mycenaean architecture, an opening left above the lintel so that the wall above does not bear on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two beasts stand with their forepaws on altars either side of a column, which carries a capital and the beam-ends of a roof above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their heads are gone, and the closest study of the tool marks reconstructs them as having turned backward, out of the face of the relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That study, by Nicholas Blackwell, reads tubular drills and saws in the surface, a large pendulum saw and a smaller convex blade, and finds the methods paralleled in central Anatolia, which raises the question of Hittite contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The gate belongs with the final fortifications, finished about 1250 BCE, and where Tiryns and Gla built ramps up to their gates Mycenae used a fold of its own ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its approach is a narrow passage between wall and tower, so an attacker at the door could be shot at from both sides; the inner gate at Tiryns is virtually identical in plan and elevation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it means has long been argued: the lions have been read as the military might of those holding the walls, and Evans took them for the sacred animals of a deity whose image the column was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was never lost, unlike almost all else Mycenaean, for Pausanias saw the gate with the lions still standing in the ruined wall, and put it down, like the wall, to the Cyclopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lion Gate","questions":["Pausanias saw the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still standing in the ruined wall in the 2nd century CE and put it down, like the wall itself, to the Cyclopes.","The heads on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are missing, and a study of its tool marks reconstructs them as having turned backward out of the face of the relief."],"tags":["building","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 58–60, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Nicholas G. Blackwell, \"Making the Lion Gate Relief at Mycenae: Tool Marks and Foreign Influence,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 118, no. 3 (2014): 451–88, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.118.3.0451. [Paywalled]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 140–41, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Underwood_%26_Underwood_-_The_Lion_Gate_at_Mycenae_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Underwood_%26_Underwood_-_The_Lion_Gate_at_Mycenae_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Lion Gate","desc":"Underwood & Underwood - The Lion Gate at Mycenae. Underwood & Underwood Details on Google Art Project.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Underwood_%26_Underwood_-_The_Lion_Gate_at_Mycenae_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"Underwood & Underwood - The Lion Gate at Mycenae"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is there a triangle above the lintel?","a":"Because a relieving triangle is left open so that the weight of the wall above does not bear on the lintel — a typical feature of Mycenaean architecture."},{"q":"Why does the study of tool marks raise the question of Hittite contact?","a":"Because Blackwell reads tubular drills and saws in the surface, a large pendulum saw and a smaller convex blade, and finds the methods paralleled in central Anatolia."},{"q":"Why is the approach to the gate so narrow?","a":"Because it is a passage between wall and tower, so an attacker at the door could be shot at from both sides."}]},
{"id":"gr-060","num":"60","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The slabs ringing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut a corner off two of its six shaft graves, which proves the ring was raised after the graves were already there.","answer":"Grave Circle A","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Grave Circle A</b> is the ring of upright slabs standing inside the citadel of Mycenae, a little south of the Lion Gate, around six shaft graves cut into the rock beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The graves are the older part of it, dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE, and they held gold and silver vessels, weapons, amber, stone vases and seals, with gold masks laid over the faces of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ring is a double row of slabs about a metre (3 feet) apart, capped with horizontal slabs and packed between with stone and earth, so what looks like a fence is the facing of a solid wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An entrance was left at the point nearest the Lion Gate, and in the fill above the graves stood carved stelae and a round altar, set precisely over the centre of the fourth grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ring is later than the graves it rings, and the proof is that it cuts a corner off two of them, which it could not have done had it stood there first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The citadel's final walls were finished about 1250 BCE, and the circle stands inside them, where Grave Circle B, the other and slightly older cemetery, lies outside to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the Mycenaeans of the palace age kept an old burial ground inside their fortress and raised a monument over it, centuries after the last body had gone in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias wrote that Agamemnon and those murdered with him lay within the wall, while Clytemnestra and Aegisthus were buried a little outside it, thought unworthy of a place inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was that passage, on Schuchhardt's account, that convinced Schliemann the graves of the house of Atreus lay inside the citadel rather than outside it, and sent him digging there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was right about the place and out by three centuries or more about the people; what the circle shows is mainland lords buried in gold long before Mycenae had a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Grave Circle A","questions":["Pausanias put Agamemnon's grave inside the wall of Mycenae and Clytemnestra's outside it, and that sentence sent the first excavators digging where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to light.","Dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE, the six graves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held gold and silver vessels, weapons, amber and stone vases, with gold masks laid over the faces of the dead."],"tags":["building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 5, 138, 152–57, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6–7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/The_Grave_Circle_A_%2816th_cent._B.C.%29_in_the_citadel_of_Mycenae_on_26_March_2019.jpg/1920px-The_Grave_Circle_A_%2816th_cent._B.C.%29_in_the_citadel_of_Mycenae_on_26_March_2019.jpg","title":"Grave Circle A","desc":"Grave Circle A in the citadel of Mycenae, the double ring of upright slabs enclosing the shaft graves. George E.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Grave_Circle_A_(16th_cent._B.C.)_in_the_citadel_of_Mycenae_on_26_March_2019.jpg","alt":"Grave Circle A at Mycenae seen from above: a double ring of upright stone slabs enclosing a sunken area of shaft graves, with the citadel wall behind and the hillside beyond"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the ring later than the graves it encloses?","a":"Because it cuts a corner off two of them, which it could not have done had it stood there first."},{"q":"Why is what looks like a fence in fact a wall?","a":"Because it is a double row of slabs about a metre apart, capped with horizontal slabs and packed between with stone and earth."},{"q":"Why did Pausanias send Schliemann digging inside the citadel?","a":"Because he wrote that Agamemnon and those murdered with him lay within the wall, while Clytemnestra and Aegisthus were buried a little outside it."}]},
{"id":"gr-061","num":"61","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Fourteen shaft graves lie inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the older of Mycenae's two circles, which was left outside the fortification walls west of the Lion Gate.","answer":"Grave Circle B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Grave Circle B</b> is the older of the two shaft-grave cemeteries at Mycenae, lying outside the fortification walls west of the Lion Gate and enclosing fourteen shaft graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was in use from about 1650 to 1550 BCE, a generation or two ahead of Grave Circle A, though one chronology compresses both into the same span of 1600 to 1500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both were the resting place of the Mycenaean elite and yielded impressive burial goods, so the two are the same elite's burying ground a few generations apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One man, buried in Grave Gamma, wore a face-mask of electrum, the natural alloy of gold and silver, earlier than any of the gold masks from the circle inside the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From Tomb N there survives a very degraded dark fabric that still shows patterns woven in with an extra weft, in the tapestry technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The features that mark the latest graves here, the carved stelae, the masks, the signs of chariots, are the features of Circle A, so the one cemetery runs on into the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It sits in the lower town among four of Mycenae's nine tholos tombs, and about fifty metres (160 feet) from the houses whose Linear B tablets record workers, oil and perfumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is the contrast with Circle A: the citadel wall keeps that one inside, while this older cemetery was left outside among the tombs and the workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its skeletons have twice been used to ask whether those buried together were relatives: seven skulls were rebuilt as faces in 1995, which found the man in the electrum mask to resemble the woman beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then in 2008 ancient DNA was typed from the same cemetery, and that study too reported kinship between the burials, a question the gold and the masks could never settle on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Grave Circle B","questions":["A man buried in Grave Gamma at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wore a face-mask of electrum, earlier than any of the gold masks from the circle inside the citadel wall.","Seven skulls from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were rebuilt as faces in 1995 and ancient DNA was typed from it in 2008, both asking whether those buried together were relatives."],"tags":["building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Stella Spantidaki, \"Elite Fabrics from the Grave Circles of Mycenae,\" <i>CHS Research Bulletin</i> (Washington, DC: Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 2022), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2022/08/29/elite-fabrics-from-the-grave-circles-of-mycenae/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","J. H. Musgrave, R. A. H. Neave and A. J. N. W. Prag, \"Seven Faces from Grave Circle B at Mycenae,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 90 (1995): 107–36, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400016105. [Paywalled]","Abigail S. Bouwman, Keri A. Brown, A. John N. W. Prag and Terence A. Brown, \"Kinship between Burials from Grave Circle B at Mycenae Revealed by Ancient DNA Typing,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 35, no. 9 (2008): 2580–84, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jas.2008.04.010. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Findings_from_Grave_%CE%91_of_Grave_Circle_B_1.JPG/1920px-Findings_from_Grave_%CE%91_of_Grave_Circle_B_1.JPG","title":"Grave Circle B","desc":"Archaeological Museum of Mycenae: Type A bronze sword from shaft grave Alpha of grave circle B in Mycenae. About 1625-1600 BCE (MH III B).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Findings_from_Grave_%CE%91_of_Grave_Circle_B_1.JPG","alt":"Findings from Grave Α of Grave Circle B 1"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is this cemetery read as running on into the other?","a":"Because the features marking its latest graves — the carved stelae, the masks, the signs of chariots — are the features of Grave Circle A."},{"q":"Why is its position outside the walls the telling contrast?","a":"Because the citadel wall keeps Circle A inside, while this older cemetery was left outside among the tholos tombs and the workshops."},{"q":"Why could the grave goods never settle who was buried together?","a":"Because gold and masks say nothing about kinship, which is why seven skulls were rebuilt as faces in 1995 and ancient DNA typed from the cemetery in 2008."}]},
{"id":"gr-062","num":"62","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Found over the face of a man in Shaft Grave V at Mycenae, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.","answer":"Mask of Agamemnon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mask of Agamemnon</b> is the gold funerary mask found over the face of a man in Shaft Grave V of Grave Circle A at Mycenae, and now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He lay at the south end of the grave, and the face on the mask is well formed, with the eyes set close together, a long delicate nose and thin lips.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eyes are ringed by a double rim, and the line running across them, marking where the lids meet, shows that they are shut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eyebrows and the full beard are raised in relief and then worked over with sunk lines to make them more lifelike, though the eyelashes were left unmarked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Schuchhardt took the closed eyes as proof that these masks were portraits of the men they covered, and found the gold plate too thick to have been simply moulded over one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The other mask from the same grave is beardless and almost spherical, and the cutting of its brows and mouth he put down to a want of skill or care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that he concluded the two were not made by the same hand, and that the bodies can scarcely have been buried at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The graves of Circle A were dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE, and the man in the mask went into the ground with a gold breastplate, amber beads and a gold band still round his arm bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a 19th-century attribution, from Schliemann taking the Circle A dead for Agamemnon and his murdered companions, and it is out by some three centuries against any date for a Trojan War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Schuchhardt, writing in 1891, still called it simply 'the bearded mask'; the other name stuck, and the face has become the image of the Aegean Bronze Age, the <i>Mona Lisa</i> of prehistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mask of Agamemnon","questions":["The closed eyes and the full beard worked over with sunk lines set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart from the beardless mask found at the other end of the same grave.","Schliemann's name for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is out by some three centuries, since the graves of Circle A were dug about 1600 to 1500 BCE."],"tags":["object","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 5, 225–26, 256–59, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","James P. Holoka, review of Cathy Gere, <i>The Tomb of Agamemnon</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.12.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.12.22/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg/1920px-Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg","title":"Mask of Agamemnon","desc":"Athens — Mask of Agamemnon. Gleb Simonov.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_%E2%80%94_Mask_of_Agamemnon.jpg","alt":"Athens — Mask of Agamemnon"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did Schuchhardt take these masks for portraits?","a":"Because the line across the eyes shows the lids meeting, so the eyes are shut, and he found the gold plate too thick to have been simply moulded over a face."},{"q":"Why did he conclude the two masks from one grave were not made together?","a":"Because the other is beardless and almost spherical with its brows and mouth cut carelessly, which he took for a different hand and a different occasion."},{"q":"Why is the mask's name a misattribution?","a":"Because it comes from Schliemann taking the Circle A dead for Agamemnon and his murdered companions, and is out by some three centuries against any date for a Trojan War."}]},
{"id":"gr-063","num":"63","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a deep rectangular pit cut straight down into rock, the dead laid at the bottom under a roof of slabs and the shaft filled in above them.","answer":"shaft grave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>shaft grave</b> is a deep rectangular pit cut straight down into rock, with the dead laid at the bottom and the shaft filled in over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The twenty at Mycenae, six in Grave Circle A and fourteen in Circle B, are the ones the type is known by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their sides stand 3 to 5 metres (10 to 16 feet) high, and the plan runs from about 2.7 by 3 metres (9 by 10 feet) in the smallest to 5 by 6.7 in the largest, which held one body and five respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The walls were lined with small quarry-stones and clay, and the bodies bedded in river pebbles among their ornaments, tools and weapons, then covered over with fine clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lid of slate slabs rested on one or two heavy beams laid across the shaft, whose ends were shod in sheet copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the beams rotted the lid fell in, and that is what disturbed the contents; it had been read as the disorder of a hurried burial until someone asked where the fallen slabs had been lying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above ground each grave was marked by a limestone stele about 1.5 metres (5 feet) high, some carved in low relief and others left as plain polished slabs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Schliemann read the smoke-blackened walls, the ashes and the unburnt wood as cremation carried out inside the tomb, but parts of the bodies came through, so they were not burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The graves belong to about 1650 to 1500 BCE and the type then gives way to the tholos tomb, whose earliest examples are in Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The shafts filled up and were forgotten, so that the graves Pausanias was shown as Agamemnon's were not these but the tholos tombs still standing above ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"shaft grave","questions":["The lid of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was slate slabs on one or two heavy beams, and when those rotted it fell in, which is what disturbed the contents rather than a hurried burial.","Each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Mycenae was marked above ground by a limestone stele about 1.5 metres (5 feet) high, some carved in low relief and others left as plain polished slabs."],"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 157–61, 167–70, 176, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6–7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae.jpg/1920px-Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae.jpg","title":"shaft grave","desc":"The Grave Circle A, and the main entrance of the citadel (left), at Mycenae.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae.jpg","alt":"Grave-Circle-A-Mycenae"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why were the contents of these graves found disordered?","a":"Because the slate lid rested on heavy beams across the shaft, and when the beams rotted the lid fell in — read as the disorder of a hurried burial until someone asked where the fallen slabs had been lying."},{"q":"Why was Schliemann wrong about cremation in the tomb?","a":"Because parts of the bodies came through, so whatever the smoke-blackened walls and ashes mean, the dead were not burned."},{"q":"Why were these not the graves Pausanias was shown?","a":"Because the shafts filled up and were forgotten, so what he was shown as Agamemnon's were the tholos tombs still standing above ground."}]},
{"id":"gr-064","num":"64","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a round burial chamber roofed by a corbelled vault, built into a hillside and entered along an open passage called a dromos.","answer":"tholos tomb","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1525 – 1275 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>tholos tomb</b> is a round burial chamber roofed by a corbelled vault, built of stone into a hillside and entered along an open passage called a dromos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Corbelling means laying each course of stone a little further in than the one below until the courses meet overhead, and Mycenaean builders carried it further than anyone: spans of eight metres (26 feet) are ordinary and the largest tombs pass fourteen (46 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The doorway is monumental, framed by mouldings cut with a saw rather than a chisel, with half-columns standing on low plinths either side of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine of them stand at Mycenae, built between about 1525 and 1275 BCE, and Alan Wace, who dug the site, sorted them into three groups by their masonry and their ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four lie near Grave Circle B, the tombs of the Lions, of Aegisthus and of Clytemnestra, with the Treasury of Atreus further south, which the ministry that keeps the site calls the most perfect example of the type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest are in Messenia, and the form spreads from there to the Argolid, Laconia and Attica, staying rare in Boeotia and reaching the Cyclades once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were built for the uppermost rank of Mycenaean society, and as fewer sites go on raising them they mark where power has gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They take over from the shaft grave, and standing above ground rather than under it they stayed open, which is why the Treasury of Atreus was rifled long before anyone thought to record what was in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias calls them the underground chambers of Atreus and his children, in which their treasures were stored, and the name treasury stayed on them for as long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tomb at Menidi in Attica settled it in 1879, when a building of exactly this kind was opened north of Athens and found to hold six bodies lying undisturbed with their ornaments about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"tholos tomb","questions":["Pausanias called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Mycenae underground chambers where the treasures of Atreus were stored, and the name treasury stuck until Menidi in 1879 proved them tombs.","Pausanias called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Mycenae underground chambers where the treasures of Atreus were stored, and the name treasury stuck until one opened at Menidi in Attica in 1879 held six undisturbed bodies."],"tags":["building","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), xxiv, 143–48, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","W. G. Cavanagh and R. R. Laxton, \"The Structural Mechanics of the Mycenaean Tholos Tomb,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 76 (1981): 109–40, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400019493. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Schatzhaus_des_Atreus%2C_Querschnitt.jpg","title":"tholos tomb","desc":"Schatzhaus des Atreus, Querschnitt. Treasure of Atreus.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schatzhaus_des_Atreus,_Querschnitt.jpg","alt":"Schatzhaus des Atreus, Querschnitt"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does a corbelled vault stand without an arch?","a":"Because each course of stone is laid a little further in than the one below until the courses meet overhead, a technique Mycenaean builders carried past fourteen metres."},{"q":"Why were these tombs robbed so early?","a":"Because standing above ground rather than under it they stayed open, which is why the Treasury of Atreus was rifled long before anyone thought to record its contents."},{"q":"Why did the Menidi tomb settle what these buildings were?","a":"Because one of exactly this kind was opened north of Athens in 1879 and found to hold six bodies lying undisturbed with their ornaments about them."}]},
{"id":"gr-065","num":"65","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The largest tholos tomb at Mycenae, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is entered along a 35-metre (115-foot) passage and roofed by 33 courses of stone closed at the top by a single slab.","answer":"Treasury of Atreus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1275 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Treasury of Atreus</b> is the biggest and best preserved of the nine tholos tombs at Mycenae, half-way down the eastern slope of the hill, and it is popularly called the Tomb of Agamemnon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dromos 35 metres (115 feet) long and 6 wide runs level into the hillside between walls of ashlar masonry and ends at a façade about 14 metres (46 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doorway is 5.4 metres (17 feet 9 inches) high, framed by three bands cut with a saw, with half-columns of dark grey alabaster either side, their shafts thickening upward and carved with spirals, triangles and lozenges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evans traced the fluted foliation of their capitals and the inlay that went with it to contemporary Egypt, comparing capitals from Akhenaten's palace at Amarna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Over the door a relieving triangle with sides 3 metres (10 feet) long was closed by courses of red porphyry cut with spirals, and dowel holes show the whole façade was faced in something further, marbles or bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The inner lintel is a single block of about 120 tonnes (120 tons), which had to be quarried, moved to the spot, worked accurately and laid to a close fit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside, the chamber is about 15 metres (50 feet) across and as high, built in 33 horizontal courses each stepped in from the one below and closed at the top by a single slab.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stones are cut to the curve and polished, so the line runs unbroken from floor to apex, and bronze nails still sit in holes across the vault, which carried scattered ornaments rather than the metal sheathing once imagined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs to the last of the three groups Wace sorted the Mycenae tholoi into, which run to about 1275 BCE, and it was empty when opened, rifled long before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its façade is now split between London and Athens, and Pausanias knew the building as a chamber where the treasure of Atreus was kept, which is the name it still carries although the ministry that keeps the site calls it a tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Treasury of Atreus","questions":["A single block of about 120 tonnes (120 tons) spans the doorway of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and dowel holes show its façade once carried marble or bronze on top of the carving.","Pausanias knew the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a chamber where the treasure of Atreus was kept, and the name has stayed on it although the building is a tomb."],"tags":["building","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 143–48, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 19: Mycenaean Tholos Tombs and Early Mycenaean Settlements,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-19-narrative/. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>The Mycenaean Tree and Pillar Cult and Its Mediterranean Relations</i> (London: Macmillan, 1901), 32–34, https://archive.org/details/mycenaeantreepil00evanuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/The_tholos_tomb_Treasury_of_Atreus_or_Tomb_of_Agamemnon_in_Mycenae_on_March_26%2C_2019.jpg/1920px-The_tholos_tomb_Treasury_of_Atreus_or_Tomb_of_Agamemnon_in_Mycenae_on_March_26%2C_2019.jpg","title":"Treasury of Atreus","desc":"The tholos tomb of Agamemnon in Mycenae. Argolis, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_tholos_tomb_Treasury_of_Atreus_or_Tomb_of_Agamemnon_in_Mycenae_on_March_26,_2019.jpg","alt":"The walled approach, doorway and relieving triangle of the Treasury of Atreus, with its mound behind"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the chamber's line unbroken from floor to apex?","a":"Because the 33 horizontal courses are each stepped in from the one below and the stones cut to the curve and polished, closing at the top with a single slab."},{"q":"Why did Evans look to Egypt for the half-columns?","a":"Because he traced the fluted foliation of their capitals and the inlay that went with it to contemporary Egypt, comparing capitals from Akhenaten's palace at Amarna."},{"q":"Why is the building still called a treasury?","a":"Because Pausanias knew it as a chamber where the treasure of Atreus was kept, and the name stayed on it although the ministry that keeps the site calls it a tomb."}]},
{"id":"gr-066","num":"66","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Greeks credited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Cyclopes: Strabo says Proetus walled Tiryns with the help of seven of them, called 'Bellyhands' and brought in from Lycia.","answer":"Cyclopean masonry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cyclopean masonry</b> is walling built of huge unworked limestone boulders roughly fitted together, with the gaps between them packed out by smaller stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the ordinary fortification masonry of Mycenaean Greece, at Mycenae, Tiryns, Argos, Krisa and the Athenian acropolis, with somewhat smaller stones at Midea and large slabs at Gla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The walls it makes are enormously thick: at Mycenae 3 to 7 metres (10 to 23 feet) where both faces can still be traced, and as much as 14 (46 feet) along two stretches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Tiryns, where the circuit stands best, the broadest stretches hold galleries inside them like casemates, and traces of the same have since been found in the north wall at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cut stone is kept for the gateways, and a third masonry of polygonal blocks with very close joints appears at particular points of the circuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest of these circuits go up late in the 14th century BCE, and the final systems were finished around 1250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is not a modern coinage but a Greek explanation, since the stones were held to be past the strength of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strabo says Proetus used Tiryns as a base and walled it with the help of the Cyclopes, seven of them, called 'Bellyhands' because they got their food by their craft, and fetched in from Lycia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias says the same at Mycenae of the surviving wall and of the gate with the lions on it, pointing back to the Cyclopes who had built for Proetus at Tiryns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The technique reaches well beyond the Argolid: at Troy a stretch of wall is of great unwrought blocks with the interstices filled by smaller stones, exactly as at Tiryns and Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cyclopean masonry","questions":["<span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is walling of huge unworked limestone boulders roughly fitted together, the gaps packed with smaller stones, and it is the ordinary fortification masonry of Mycenaean Greece.","At Tiryns the broadest stretches of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold galleries inside them like casemates, and at Mycenae the walls run 3 to 7 metres (10 to 23 feet) thick and as much as 14 in places."],"tags":["practice","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 54, 136–38, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.11, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D6%3Asection%3D11. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/The_Lion_Gate_on_26_March_2019.jpg/1920px-The_Lion_Gate_on_26_March_2019.jpg","title":"Cyclopean masonry","desc":"The Lion Gate. Mycenae. Argolis, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Lion_Gate_on_26_March_2019.jpg","alt":"The Lion Gate on 26 March 2019"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the name not a modern coinage?","a":"Because it is a Greek explanation: the stones were held to be past the strength of men, and Strabo and Pausanias both credit the walls to the Cyclopes."},{"q":"Why is cut stone kept for the gateways?","a":"Because the boulder walling cannot frame an opening, so dressed blocks — and at points a polygonal masonry with very close joints — are used where precision is needed."},{"q":"Why do the broadest stretches at Tiryns hold galleries?","a":"Because the walls are thick enough to carry passages inside them like casemates, and traces of the same have since been found in the north wall at Mycenae."}]},
{"id":"gr-067","num":"67","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer's Catalogue of Ships calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> famed for its walls, and Pausanias found that the wall was all of it that still stood.","answer":"Tiryns","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tiryns</b> is a fortified citadel on a low limestone rock in the Argive plain, which Homer's Catalogue of Ships names alongside Argos and calls famed for its walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rock is about 300 metres (328 yards) long and 100 (109 yards) wide, standing 18 metres (59 feet) above the plain and isolated on every side, and it falls from south to north in three terraces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The palace stood on the upper terrace at the south, filling the whole space inside the walls, while the middle citadel carried small dwellings and the lower one was left undug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Found in excellent preservation, its plan is the Homeric house: a forecourt with colonnades and an altar, a megaron with hearth and columns and an ante-room, and a bathroom close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is also a second, smaller set of the same rooms, reached from the first only with difficulty, which its excavators took for the women's quarters, an arrangement Homer knows nothing of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The walls are the thing here, standing better than anywhere else, with galleries running inside the broadest stretches; the palace within kept its wall-paintings and an alabaster frieze along the vestibule of the men's hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its first circuit belongs to the later 14th century and the last was complete by about 1250 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Strabo makes the place the base of operations of Proetus, the king for whom the Cyclopes were said to have raised the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias, who came to the ruins, reports the wall as the only part still remaining, a work of the Cyclopes of unwrought stones so large that a pair of mules could not move the smallest of them; and he notices that small stones were set in to bind the large blocks together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tirynthians themselves were removed by the Argives, who wanted Argos larger, so the town went the way of Mycenae and only the walls that had made it famous stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tiryns","questions":["A Mycenaean palace stands on the upper terrace at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a forecourt, a megaron and a bathroom laid out as Homer describes a great house.","Pausanias said the stones of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were so big that a pair of mules could not shift the smallest, and noticed the small stones set in to bind the big blocks."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.559, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D2%3Acard%3D546. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.25.8, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.11, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D6%3Asection%3D11. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), xxi–xxii, 14, 96–97, 107, 117, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Walls_of_Tiryns_-_Dodwell_Edward_-_1834.jpg","title":"Tiryns","desc":"Edward Dodwell. Views and Descriptions of Cyclopian, or, Pelasgic Remains, in Greece and Italy; with Constructions of a later Period, London, Adolphus Richter, MDCCCXXXIV (1834).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walls_of_Tiryns_-_Dodwell_Edward_-_1834.jpg","alt":"Walls of Tiryns - Dodwell Edward - 1834"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Tiryns","at":[22.8,37.5994]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the palace's plan called the Homeric house?","a":"Because it has a forecourt with colonnades and an altar, a megaron with hearth and columns and an ante-room, and a bathroom close by."},{"q":"Why does one part of the palace sit oddly with Homer?","a":"Because a second smaller set of the same rooms, reached from the first only with difficulty, was taken for women's quarters — an arrangement Homer knows nothing of."},{"q":"Why did Pausanias notice the small stones in the wall?","a":"Because they were set in to bind the huge unwrought blocks together, blocks so large he says a pair of mules could not move the smallest of them."}]},
{"id":"gr-068","num":"68","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer puts Nestor at Pylos, the well-built citadel of Neleus, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Ano Englianos in Messenia is the Mycenaean palace that carries his name.","answer":"Palace of Nestor","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Palace of Nestor</b> is the Mycenaean palace at Ano Englianos in Messenia, which the Greek ministry responsible for it calls the best preserved of them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homer puts Nestor at Pylos, the well-built citadel of Neleus, where Telemachus comes ashore to ask the old king for news of his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a complex rather than one building, four main structures — a south-west block, a central building, a north-east block and a wine store — with 105 ground-floor rooms between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its heart is the throne room, a great rectangle whose central hearth is ringed by four columns, with the ruler's throne standing against one wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Near it are a room holding a clay bath tub and storerooms full of vessels, and the walls throughout carried fine paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its archive holds 1,107 clay tablets written in Linear B by 32 different scribes, the most substantial collection anywhere on the mainland, and it is what tells us what the place did and who worked for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site was located by Konstantinos Kourouniotis in 1939 and dug by Carl Blegen of the University of Cincinnati, the work broken off almost at once by the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The palace reached the height of its prosperity in the 13th century BCE and was destroyed by fire in the early years of the 12th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exactly when is not settled: the burning is put either late in Late Helladic IIIB or somewhere early in IIIC, and the pottery will not decide between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What followed is not in doubt, for the emptying of Messenia in the century after is more striking than in any other part of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palace of Nestor","questions":["The archive of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds 1,107 tablets in Linear B written by 32 different scribes, and its throne room has a circular hearth ringed by four columns.","A complex of 105 ground-floor rooms in four main buildings, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned early in the 12th century BCE, after which Messenia emptied more strikingly than anywhere else."],"tags":["building","place","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: History,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh351.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Odyssey</i> 3.4–20, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0136%3Abook%3D3%3Acard%3D1. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg/1920px-%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","title":"Palace of Nestor","desc":"Παλάτι του Νέστορα - Νέο στέγαστρο προστασίας.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A0%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%AC%CF%84%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%85_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B1_-_%CE%9D%CE%AD%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%AD%CE%B3%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%80%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%83%CF%84%CE%B1%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","alt":"Παλάτι του Νέστορα - Νέο στέγαστρο προστασίας"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Palace of Nestor","at":[21.695,37.0272]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the archive what tells us most about the place?","a":"Because its 1,107 Linear B tablets, written by 32 scribes, are the most substantial collection on the mainland and record what the palace did and who worked for it."},{"q":"Why is the date of its burning unsettled?","a":"Because it is put either late in Late Helladic IIIB or early in IIIC, and the pottery will not decide between them."},{"q":"Why is what followed not in doubt?","a":"Because the emptying of Messenia in the century after is more striking than in any other part of Greece."}]},
{"id":"gr-069","num":"69","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 250 clay tablets came out of the Armoury at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1993, which makes it one of only four mainland sites to have produced a Linear B archive.","answer":"Mycenaean Thebes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Thebes</b> is the palace centre on the Kadmeia, the low citadel of Thebes in Boeotia, and one of the most important administrative centres of the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also the least recoverable of them, since the town has been lived in without a break and a modern one stands over the ancient remains, which the ministry says makes a full investigation of the site impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is known has come instead from rescue digs scattered through the streets, and the palace is known in parts rather than as a plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Called the palace of Kadmos, it stood near the middle of the citadel, its painted annexes and departments covering a great part of the hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the parts recovered are a Room of the Treasure and an Armoury, and the site has given up inscribed stirrup jars as well as tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> About 250 clay tablets came out of the Armoury between 1993 and 1995, and Thebes is one of only four places on the mainland to have produced a Linear B archive, beside Mycenae, Tiryns and Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The palace belongs to the 14th and 13th centuries BCE, and the bulk of it was probably destroyed by fire late in Late Helladic IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the seven gates the tradition gives Thebes, only the entrance between the two round towers of the Electran Gates still stands, though stretches of the Mycenaean wall survive where two others were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The epic may remember something about the place: Homer's Catalogue of Ships runs through the Boeotian towns and Thebes is not among them, only lower Thebe, the well-built citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the hills at Kastellia stands a monumental chamber tomb known as the tomb of the sons of King Oedipus, a Mycenaean grave carrying the name of the city's own myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Thebes","questions":["Homer's Catalogue of Ships runs through the Boeotian towns without naming Thebes, listing only lower Thebe, while the palace of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned late in the 13th century BCE.","A modern town sits over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its palace is known in parts — painted annexes, a Room of the Treasure and an Armoury — rather than as a plan."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Thebes: History,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2459. [Open access]","\"Thebes: Monuments,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh351.jsp?obj_id=2459. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.505, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D2%3Acard%3D494. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_%28GR03%29_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg/1920px-Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_%28GR03%29_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg","title":"Mycenaean Thebes","desc":"The modern town of Thebes in Boeotia seen from the north, built over the Mycenaean citadel, whose remains lie beneath the houses.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_(GR03)_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg","alt":"Vue Nord Ville - Thèbes (GR03) - 2022-03-21 - 3"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Thebes","at":[23.3178,38.3208]},"why":[{"q":"Why is this the least recoverable of the mainland palaces?","a":"Because the town has been lived in without a break and a modern one stands over the ancient remains, which the ministry says makes a full investigation impossible."},{"q":"Why is the palace known in parts rather than as a plan?","a":"Because what is known has come from rescue digs scattered through the streets rather than from an open excavation of the whole."},{"q":"Why does the Catalogue of Ships sit oddly with the site?","a":"Because it runs through the Boeotian towns and Thebes is not among them, only lower Thebe, the well-built citadel."}]},
{"id":"gr-070","num":"70","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The third fortified citadel of the Argolid, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a Cyclopean wall like those of Mycenae and Tiryns but built of somewhat smaller stones.","answer":"Midea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Midea</b> is the third of the fortified Mycenaean citadels of the Argolid, standing behind Mycenae and Tiryns in fame and holding a Cyclopean wall like theirs, though built of somewhat smaller stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its circuit has two gates, one east and one west, and the excavation divides the acropolis between those two areas and the lower terraces below them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A megaron on those terraces shows two successive phases within Late Helladic IIIB, the second of them with a large hearth set slightly off centre and four columns standing round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A shrine area lies hard against the Cyclopean wall, as the religious centre does at Mycenae, and it gave up a miniature tripod offering table and a naturalistic female figure in terracotta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery imported from western Anatolia turned up on the site as well, along with four sword pommels of early Mycenaean date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Late Helladic IIIB at Midea was ended by a destruction caused by an earthquake and the fires that came with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mycenae, Tiryns and Thebes burned at about the same point, around 1200 BCE, and the wider pattern is what the argument is now about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Explanations by one coordinated cause have largely been given up in favour of a confluence of disasters, read site by site rather than across the whole map at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pausanias makes Midea part of the share of Proetus, who took the Heraeum, Midea, Tiryns and the Argive coast when the sons of Abas divided the kingdom between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He passed the place himself on the road from Argos and found nothing of it left but the foundations, adding that Electryon, the father of Alcmena, had once been its king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Midea","questions":["Late Helladic IIIB at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ended by an earthquake and the fires that came with it, and Pausanias later found nothing of the place but its foundations.","A shrine against the wall at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up a miniature tripod offering table and a terracotta figure of a woman, and its megaron had a hearth ringed by four columns."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of Gisela Walberg, <i>Midea: The Megaron Complex and Shrine Area. Excavations on the Lower Terraces 1994–1997</i>, Prehistory Monographs 20, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.03.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.03.43/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.16.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.25.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Trevor Van Damme, review of Reinhard Jung and Eleftheria Kardamaki, eds., <i>Synchronizing the Destructions of the Mycenaean Palaces</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.04.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.04.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Midea.JPG/1920px-Midea.JPG","title":"Midea","desc":"The citadel of Midea in the Argolid, its Cyclopean wall of huge unworked blocks following the crest of the hill.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Midea.JPG","alt":"Midea archeological site"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Midea","at":[22.8414,37.6497]},"why":[{"q":"Why has a single coordinated cause for the destructions been given up?","a":"Because the pattern is now read site by site rather than across the whole map at once, as a confluence of disasters — here an earthquake and the fires that came with it."},{"q":"Why is the shrine's position worth noticing?","a":"Because it lies hard against the Cyclopean wall, as the religious centre does at Mycenae."},{"q":"Why did Pausanias find nothing of the place?","a":"Because passing it on the road from Argos he found only the foundations left, though he still knew it as part of the share of Proetus."}]},
{"id":"gr-071","num":"71","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Standing on a limestone island in the drained Kopais basin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is unusual among Mycenaean fortresses in having four major gates at roughly the cardinal points.","answer":"Gla","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Gla</b> is a Mycenaean fortress on a low limestone island rising from the floor of the Kopais basin in Boeotia, near its north-east corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a fortified administrative centre and military strongpoint set there to protect the drainage network that had turned a lake into farmland, and a building of the palace kind stood inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its walls are of large limestone slabs rather than the boulders used in the Argolid, and they went up all at once early in Late Helladic IIIB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is unusual in having four major gates at roughly the cardinal points, where an ordinary Mycenaean fortress has one main entrance and a postern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each of them is reached by a long, fairly steep, artificially built ramp, as at Tiryns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The scale of the undertaking, this citadel and the draining of the basin together, dwarfs any other Mycenaean building project yet known, the northern dyke alone running up to 66 metres (217 feet) thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The basin was remembered in the epic geography, since Homer's Catalogue of Ships lists Copae among the Boeotian towns, the name the lake itself carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who did the work is argued over: the standard account attaches Gla and the Kopais to Orchomenos, a major rival of Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kramer-Hajos has argued instead for a joint venture between the two cities, pointing to a system of fortifications along the northern Kopais with nothing at all facing Thebes to the south; her reviewer objects that this leaves the destructions that follow soon after at Thebes and at Gla unexplained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gla was destroyed, and presumably the drainage with it, well before the end of the 13th century BCE, earlier than the burning of the Argive citadels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Gla","questions":["Whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belonged to Orchomenos alone or to Orchomenos and Thebes together is argued, since its fortifications run along the northern Kopais with nothing facing Thebes.","The walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are of large limestone slabs rather than boulders and went up all at once, part of a project that dwarfs any other Mycenaean building work known."],"tags":["place","building","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Alex R. Knodell, review of Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, <i>Mycenaean Greece and the Aegean World: Palace and Province in the Late Bronze Age</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2017.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.04.13. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.502, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D2%3Acard%3D494. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cd/Plan_of_the_Palace_of_Gla.jpg","title":"Gla","desc":"A plan of the palace at Gla.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_of_the_Palace_of_Gla.jpg","alt":"Plan of the Palace of Gla"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Gla","at":[23.1822,38.4833]},"why":[{"q":"Why was a fortress built on this island at all?","a":"Because it was set there to protect the drainage network that had turned the Kopais lake into farmland, as an administrative centre and military strongpoint."},{"q":"Why is the fortress unusual in its gates?","a":"Because it has four major ones at roughly the cardinal points, where an ordinary Mycenaean fortress has one main entrance and a postern."},{"q":"Why is Kramer-Hajos's joint-venture reading objected to?","a":"Because her reviewer holds that it leaves the destructions that follow soon after at Thebes and at Gla unexplained."}]},
{"id":"gr-072","num":"72","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Porch, vestibule and throne room in a line, with a great round hearth and four columns around it, are the three parts of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a Mycenaean palace.","answer":"megaron","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palatial form</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>megaron</b> is the great hall at the heart of a Mycenaean palace, divided into three along one axis: a columned porch, a vestibule the Greeks called the <i>prodomos</i>, and beyond it the <i>domos</i>, the main chamber that held the ruler's throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A large circular hearth stands at the middle of that chamber with four columns set square around it, and the throne against the middle of the right-hand wall at Pylos, at Tiryns and probably at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The floors of all three parts were plastered and painted with patterns, and frescoes ran over the walls throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form is not an import: the throne room with its central hearth is a monumental version of the ordinary private house of the Early and Middle Helladic periods, grown grand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the Mycenaeans took from Crete they took selectively, and the plan they built is the opposite of a Minoan one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Minoan palace works outward from its central court, while a Mycenaean palace is centripetal, funnelling anyone who enters towards a hall left standing isolated and dominant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A palace usually holds more than one: Tiryns, Pylos and Mycenae each have a smaller second unit of the same shape, sometimes called the queen's megaron, and at Tiryns the smaller one is hard to reach from the greater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gla has several smaller and plainer ones, none of them in a central position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form reaches past the great palaces as well, for the megaron on the lower terraces at Midea has the same large hearth, set a little off centre, with its four columns round it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Homer's great houses have the same parts exactly reproduced, the forecourt with its colonnades and altar, the hall with pillars and hearth and ante-room, and the bathroom beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"megaron","questions":["A Minoan palace works outward from its court, but a Mycenaean one funnels whoever enters towards the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a hall left standing isolated and dominant at the centre.","The throne room of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a monumental version of the ordinary house of the Early and Middle Helladic periods, so the form is inherited rather than borrowed from Crete."],"tags":["building","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 107–17, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Walter Leaf, introduction to Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i> (London: Macmillan, 1891), xxi–xxii, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","\"Mycenae,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2573. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of Gisela Walberg, <i>Midea: The Megaron Complex and Shrine Area. Excavations on the Lower Terraces 1994–1997</i>, Prehistory Monographs 20, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.03.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.03.43/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/MiceneMegaron.jpg/1920px-MiceneMegaron.jpg","title":"megaron","desc":"Mycenae, Megaron.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MiceneMegaron.jpg","alt":"MiceneMegaron"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the form not an import from Crete?","a":"Because the throne room with its central hearth is a monumental version of the ordinary private house of the Early and Middle Helladic periods, grown grand."},{"q":"Why is a Mycenaean palace the opposite of a Minoan one in plan?","a":"Because a Minoan palace works outward from its central court, while a Mycenaean one funnels anyone who enters towards a hall left standing isolated and dominant."},{"q":"Why does the form reach beyond the great palaces?","a":"Because Gla has several smaller and plainer ones and the megaron on the lower terraces at Midea has the same large hearth with its four columns round it."}]},
{"id":"gr-073","num":"73","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The same motifs turn up in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1250 BCE and in Cretan painting five centuries older, a tenacity of tradition compared with that of oral formulaic poetry.","answer":"Mycenaean fresco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pylos set</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Mycenaean fresco</b> is a wall painting from one of the mainland palaces, and substantial sets survive from Mycenae, Orchomenos, Thebes, Tiryns and Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest belong to Late Helladic IIA, the late 16th or early 15th century BCE, and the fullest set, from Pylos, to about 1250 to 1200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Painting ran right through the great hall, over the walls of porch, vestibule and throne room alike, above floors plastered and painted with patterns of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The subjects are processions on a large scale and, on a small one, hunting, preparation for battle, sacrifice and feasting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Animals appear in human settings or as emblems of royal power, lions and wingless griffins among them, while sea creatures were put on floors rather than walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Tiryns the masterpiece is a bull galloping at full speed on a blue ground, its body yellow with many red spots, a man balanced on its back with one leg thrown high and a hand on its horn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The strangest thing about this painting is how little it changes: the same motifs recur from 1250 BCE back to the Crete of 1750 to 1375, a tenacity of tradition that has been compared with that of oral formulaic poetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One consequence is practical, that a Mycenaean mural cannot be dated by its subject matter alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other is a doubt about what the pictures show, since Cretan scenes depict things really done where the mainland versions may be formulas repeated after the sense of them was lost, and that bull-leaper may be exactly the case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Pylos the lyre player, the feasting figures and the bull of the throne room have been read as one composition, a bull led into the palace to be sacrificed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean fresco","questions":["Because the subjects hardly change, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot be dated by what it shows, and the mainland versions may be formulas repeated after their sense was lost.","The masterpiece among the Tiryns examples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a bull galloping on a blue ground, yellow with red spots, a man balanced on its back holding a horn."],"tags":["art","object","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 24: Mycenaean Pictorial Art and Pottery,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-24-narrative/. [Open access]","Carl Schuchhardt, <i>Schliemann's Excavations: An Archaeological and Historical Study</i>, trans. Eugénie Sellers (London: Macmillan, 1891), 118–20, https://archive.org/details/schliemannsexca00strogoog. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 20: Mycenaean Residential Architecture: Palaces and Ordinary Housing,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-20-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","\"Thebes: Monuments,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh351.jsp?obj_id=2459. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Gillieron_Shield_Fresco_Tiryns.png","title":"Mycenaean fresco","desc":"Reproduction of the \"Shield Frieze\" from the Mycenaean palace at Tiryns, painted by Émile Gilliéron and acquired by the Metropolitan Museum in New York before 1912.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gillieron_Shield_Fresco_Tiryns.png","alt":"Gillieron Shield Fresco Tiryns"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why can a Mycenaean mural not be dated by its subject alone?","a":"Because the same motifs recur from 1250 BCE back to the Crete of 1750 to 1375, a tenacity of tradition compared with that of oral formulaic poetry."},{"q":"Why does that raise a doubt about what the pictures show?","a":"Because Cretan scenes depict things really done, where the mainland versions may be formulas repeated after the sense of them was lost — the bull-leaper may be exactly that."},{"q":"Why were sea creatures put on floors rather than walls?","a":"Because the walls carried processions on a large scale and, on a small one, hunting, battle preparation, sacrifice and feasting, while marine subjects belonged underfoot."}]},
{"id":"gr-074","num":"74","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A principally syllabic script of some 89 signs with over a hundred ideograms beside them, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut into clay at six palaces and turned out to record Greek.","answer":"Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Most tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Linear B</b> is the writing of the Mycenaean palaces, a principally syllabic script of some 89 signs standing for vowels and for consonant-vowel combinations, with over a hundred ideograms alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was pressed into unbaked clay, and what survives survives because the fires that ended the palaces hardened the tablets that recorded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Archives are known from six places, Mycenae, Tiryns, Pylos and Thebes on the mainland and Knossos and Chania on Crete, and most of the tablets belong to about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arthur Evans set Crete's linear writing out as two classes, A and B, in 1909, and 45 of Linear B's syllabic signs have close equivalents in the older of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The older script has never been read, so the sound of many signs is known while the language behind them is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The language behind Linear B is Greek, and the Greek ministry calls these texts the proof that it is the earliest known Greek script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading it pushed the earliest examples of written Greek back to more than 3,000 years ago and made Greek the oldest known European language still spoken today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it was used for is narrow: the tablets record land tenure, the assignment of people to tasks, military organisation and economic transactions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything surviving in it is administration, so a script that turns out to preserve the oldest Greek preserves not a line of literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That the palaces of Greece kept their accounts in Greek is what the reading of it settled, and that mattered as much to Indo-European linguistics as to Aegean archaeology, altering the understanding of the early civilisations of Greece and Crete completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Linear B","questions":["Deciphering <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1952 pushed the earliest written Greek back more than 3,000 years and made Greek the oldest European language still spoken today.","Everything surviving in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is administrative, from land tenure to military organisation, so a script that records Greek preserves no literature at all."],"tags":["concept","object","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 28–29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Linear_B_tablet_An_661.png","title":"Linear B","desc":"Linear B tablet An 661.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_An_661.png","alt":"Linear B tablet An 661"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does anything of this script survive at all?","a":"Because it was pressed into unbaked clay, and what survives does so because the fires that ended the palaces hardened the tablets."},{"q":"Why is the sound of many Linear A signs known while the language is not?","a":"Because 45 of Linear B's syllabic signs have close equivalents in the older script, so the values carry across, but the older script has never been read."},{"q":"Why does the oldest Greek preserve no literature?","a":"Because everything surviving in the script is administration — land tenure, the assignment of people to tasks, military organisation and economic transactions."}]},
{"id":"gr-075","num":"75","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came in 1952, when a young British architect showed the script recorded Greek, and a Cambridge philologist became the first scholar to accept it.","answer":"decipherment of Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deciphered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1952</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Confirmed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953 and 1956</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>decipherment of Linear B</b> was achieved in 1952 by Michael Ventris, a young British architect, working on the clay tablets Arthur Evans had sorted into two classes of Cretan linear writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What he showed was that the script of the Mycenaean palaces, some 89 syllabic signs and a hundred ideograms, recorded Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within weeks he was in touch with John Chadwick, a philologist just appointed to a lectureship at Cambridge, who had been given the work by Sir John Myres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chadwick was the first scholar to accept the decipherment as correct, and his first letter, of 13 July 1952, offered whatever 'a mere philologist' could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In it he supplied the sound-value <i>pu</i>, which let the name Pylos itself be read; Ventris answered the same day that he had been feeling the need of a mere philologist to keep him on the right lines, and asked him to collaborate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their first scholarly publication came out in the <i>Journal of Hellenic Studies</i> in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dramatic confirmation came on 7 April 1955, when Chadwick, joining fragments in the Herakleion Museum, put together the horse tablet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It carried <i>i-qo</i>, the Mycenaean <i>hikkwos</i>, which is the later Greek <i>hippos</i>, a horse, and their letters call it a dramatic confirmation of the correctness of the decipherment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two worked closely together for four years, until Ventris died in 1956 within weeks of the publication of their joint <i>Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The original letters are kept in the Mycenaean Epigraphy Room at Cambridge and have been digitised, so the whole of it can be followed letter by letter, from July 1952 to shortly before Ventris's death in September 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"decipherment of Linear B","questions":["The dramatic confirmation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a tablet joined in 1955 carrying <i>i-qo</i>, the Mycenaean <i>hikkwos</i>, which is the later Greek <i>hippos</i>, a horse.","Chadwick's first letter about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered whatever 'a mere philologist' could do and supplied the sound-value <i>pu</i>, which let the name Pylos be read."],"tags":["event","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Ventris–Chadwick Correspondence,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/archive/correspondence. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","\"Palace of Nestor: Description,\" Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Arthur J. Evans, <i>Scripta Minoa: The Written Documents of Minoan Crete</i>, vol. 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1909), 28–29, https://archive.org/details/scriptaminoawrit01evanuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Linear_B_tablet_An_657.png","title":"decipherment of Linear B","desc":"Linear B tablet An 657.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_An_657.png","alt":"Linear B tablet An 657"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did Ventris ask a philologist to collaborate?","a":"Because Chadwick supplied the sound-value pu, which let the name Pylos be read, and Ventris answered that he had been feeling the need of a mere philologist to keep him on the right lines."},{"q":"Why is the horse tablet called a dramatic confirmation?","a":"Because joining fragments in the Herakleion Museum produced i-qo, the Mycenaean hikkwos, which is the later Greek hippos, a horse, beside the picture of one."},{"q":"Why can the whole decipherment be followed letter by letter?","a":"Because the original correspondence is kept in the Mycenaean Epigraphy Room at Cambridge and has been digitised, from July 1952 to shortly before Ventris's death."}]},
{"id":"gr-076","num":"76","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Told apart by their handwriting, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> number 32 at Pylos and 100 at Knossos, and nothing they wrote describes their own work or their training.","answer":"Linear B scribes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Script in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tablets fired</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1180 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Linear B scribes</b> are the writers of the palace records of Bronze Age Crete and Greece, in a script used there between about 1400 and 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word scribe is a convention that claims nothing about their standing: they may have been palace administrators, writers assisting them, or workers whose own job included recording what they produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are told apart by their handwriting, chiefly by palaeographic analysis of the writing itself, though subject matter, formatting, orthography and language all play a part; much of it now read from mid-20th-century photographs that preserve details no longer visible on the tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Counted that way, 1,107 tablets at Pylos are the work of 32 hands and 3,369 at Knossos the work of 100, while a recent study of the Pylos writers puts them at 30 to 40.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing they wrote describes their own work or their training, and no certain school text or practice text has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What stands in for that is the writing itself: the same conventions of spelling and record-keeping hold across every site, so all of them were trained in one shared tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within it variation was in certain cases permissible and entirely normal, even within the work of a single writer, so the training set conventions rather than fixing every form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Teaching may have been informal, since learning of this kind can happen through interaction with others as much as through formal instruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clay was worked by rolling out a cylinder and flattening it with the palms, or by folding up a sheet, into long narrow palm-leaf tablets of a line or two, or rectangular page-shaped ones carrying more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether a writer made the tablet they wrote on is unknown, palmprints identifying only four makers across more than one tablet, and none of it was meant to keep: the clay was air-dried, and fired only by the fire that ended the palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Linear B scribes","questions":["The clay the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote on was rolled into a cylinder and flattened with the palms, or folded up from a sheet, and only ever air-dried.","Spelling varied even within one writer's work, so the training that produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set shared conventions rather than fixing every form, and may have been informal."],"tags":["people","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Anna P. Judson, \"Learning to Spell in Linear B: Orthography and Scribal Training in Mycenaean Pylos,\" <i>The Cambridge Classical Journal</i> 68 (2022): 133–63, https://doi.org/10.1017/S1750270522000057. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, \"The Tablet-Makers of Pylos: An Experimental Investigation into the Production of Linear B Tablets,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 118 (2023): 147–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245423000059. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"Research and Archival Collections,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/archive. [Open access]","Mycenaean Epigraphy Group, \"The Decipherment of Linear B: Introduction,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Cambridge, https://www.classics.cam.ac.uk/research/projects/mycep/decipherment. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Clay_Tablet_inscribed_with_Linear_B_script.jpg/1920px-Clay_Tablet_inscribed_with_Linear_B_script.jpg","title":"Linear B scribes","desc":"The clay tablet KN Fp 13, dated to 1450-1375 BCE, is Minoan and was found at Knossos by Arthur Evans. It records, in Greek written in Linear B, quantities of oil apparently offered to various deities.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_Tablet_inscribed_with_Linear_B_script.jpg","alt":"Clay Tablet inscribed with Linear B script"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the word scribe a convention here?","a":"Because it claims nothing about their standing: they may have been palace administrators, writers assisting them, or workers whose own job included recording what they produced."},{"q":"Why is a shared tradition inferred when no school text survives?","a":"Because the same conventions of spelling and record-keeping hold across every site, so all of them were trained in one tradition even though nothing describes that training."},{"q":"Why was none of this writing meant to last?","a":"Because the clay was air-dried, and fired only by the fire that ended the palace."}]},
{"id":"gr-077","num":"77","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"About 80 per cent of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay in two small rooms immediately inside the palace's main entrance, the rest scattered through storerooms and two outlying buildings.","answer":"Pylos tablets","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Pylos tablets</b> are the Linear B records kept at the Palace of Nestor in Messenia, the largest such archive from the Greek mainland, in which the place calls itself PU-RO, capital of a kingdom covering Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 80 per cent of them lay in the Archives Complex, rooms 7 and 8, two small rooms immediately inside the palace's main entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest were scattered through the site, in a pottery storeroom, three rooms holding oil, and two buildings outside the palace proper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> All of it is firmly dated by the burnt destruction contexts in which it was found, to the end of the 13th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation ran from 1939 into the 1960s, and every text has since been published twice over, as a transcription and as a corpus giving each tablet a drawing, a colour photograph, its size, its fragments and its find-spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their subject is administration, and there is enough of it for major portions of the administrative system of a Mycenaean kingdom to be reconstructed from this one archive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Land takes up the largest and most argued-over part: a complex system of tenure evidently existed in the Pylian kingdom, the wanax, the lawagetas and the telestai stand out as major landholders, and the palace is interested in all land in the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beside it run narrower sets: the Jn tablets on bronze, twenty of them reconstructed from 34 pieces and over a hundred unnumbered fragments, and the o-ka series, listing bodies of troops called watchers of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The military tablets account for some 800 coast watchers and 600 rowers, and others record textile work, land taxation and religious dedications, among them banquet materials for Poseidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the archive gives, then, is not a history but a kingdom caught working: its officials, its landholders, its bronze, its cloth, its gods and the men it had posted along its shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pylos tablets","questions":["Among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the military sets account for some 800 coast watchers and 600 rowers, while the o-ka series lists bodies of troops called watchers of the sea.","So much of a Mycenaean kingdom's administration survives in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that major portions of its system can be reconstructed, with the wanax and the lawagetas among its landholders."],"tags":["object","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Silvia Ferrara, review of <i>Les archives du roi Nestor: Corpus des inscriptions en linéaire B de Pylos, I–II</i>, by Louis Godart and Anna Sacconi, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2020.12.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.12.28/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, \"The Tablet-Makers of Pylos: An Experimental Investigation into the Production of Linear B Tablets,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 118 (2023): 147–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245423000059. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Eleanor Dickey, review of <i>The Pylos Tablets Transcribed</i>, 2nd ed., by Jean-Pierre Olivier and Maurizio Del Freo, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2022.12.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.12.05/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Linear_B_tablet_PY_Ta_716_%28photo%29.png","title":"Pylos tablets","desc":"Photograph of Linear B tablet PY Ta 716.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_B_tablet_PY_Ta_716_(photo).png","alt":"Linear B tablet PY Ta 716"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why can a whole kingdom's administration be reconstructed from one archive?","a":"Because there is enough of it: the tablets cover landholding, bronze, cloth, taxation, religious dedications and the men posted along the shore."},{"q":"Why are the tablets so firmly dated?","a":"Because all of them were found in burnt destruction contexts, which puts the archive at the end of the 13th century BCE."},{"q":"Why is land the most argued-over part of the record?","a":"Because a complex system of tenure evidently existed, with the wanax, the lawagetas and the telestai standing out as major landholders and the palace interested in all land in the kingdom."}]},
{"id":"gr-078","num":"78","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Written WA-NA-KA in the tablets, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held land three times the size of the lawagetas', and the word survives in the <i>Iliad</i> as an archaic term for a king.","answer":"wanax","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>wanax</b> is the ruler at the head of a Mycenaean kingdom, written WA-NA-KA in the Linear B tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word outlived the palaces that used it, surviving in the <i>Iliad</i> as an archaic form beside the ordinary <i>basileus</i> for a king, and Agamemnon is <i>anax andron</i>, lord of men, in the poem's seventh line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which man held the office is nearly recoverable at Pylos, where it has been suggested, on evidence its proposers call inconclusive, that his name was Enkhelyawon or something like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was the kingdom's greatest landholder, the lawagetas below him controlling land one third the size of his, though three telestai together hold as much as he does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Land was held from him as well as by him, the telestai holding theirs in return for their service, <i>telos</i>, to the wanax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The adjective <i>wanakteros</i>, royal, marks what belonged to his household, and a potter, a fuller and possibly an armourer are named by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Painted stirrup jars from Thebes, Eleusis, Tiryns and Chania carry the same word in paint, and presumably held wine or oil from royal vineyards or olive groves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The title reaches into religion as well, since in some tablets wanax appears to be used as a divine title, though that does not by itself make him a priest-king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His pairing with the goddess Potnia in one Pylos series has been read as making the two human substitutes for some kind of divine pair, in what one contributor calls a theocratic system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Feasting belonged to the office too, in festivals organised by the ruler and high officials for political purposes, and the wanax is placed by modern accounts across religion and ritual, the economy, the military sphere and the social hierarchy at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"wanax","questions":["The adjective <i>wanakteros</i> marks what belonged to the household of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and painted stirrup jars from four sites carry the same word in paint.","Paired with the goddess Potnia in one Pylos series, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as a human substitute for a divine pair, though the tablets do not make him a priest-king."],"tags":["title","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 2, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 116, no. 3 (2012), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/1142/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 1.7, ed. D. B. Monro and T. W. Allen, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0133%3Abook%3D1%3Acard%3D1. [Open access]","Silke Knippschild, review of <i>Ancient Greece: From the Mycenaean Palaces to the Age of Homer</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Irene S. Lemos, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.08.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.39/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Ceramic_fragment_with_WANAKTI_inscription.jpg","title":"wanax","desc":"Ancient Greek ceramic fragment depicting a horse with rider. Votive pinax from the sanctuary of Poseidon at Penteskouphia, Corinth (Berlin, Antikensammlung F.552;. BishkekRocks.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceramic_fragment_with_WANAKTI_inscription.jpg","alt":"Ceramic fragment with WANAKTI inscription"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the word known to have outlived the palaces?","a":"Because it survives in the Iliad as an archaic form beside the ordinary basileus, and Agamemnon is anax andron, lord of men, in the poem's seventh line."},{"q":"Why does the adjective wanakteros matter?","a":"Because it marks what belonged to his household — a potter, a fuller and possibly an armourer are named by it, and painted stirrup jars carry the word in paint."},{"q":"Why does his appearance as a divine title not make him a priest-king?","a":"Because the word is used that way in some tablets without the office itself being shown to be priestly, though his pairing with Potnia has been read as a divine pair."}]},
{"id":"gr-079","num":"79","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Written ra-wa-ke-ta in the tablets, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes second after the wanax among the Pylos landholders, with an estate a third the size of the king's.","answer":"lawagetas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>lawagetas</b> is the second man of a Mycenaean kingdom, written ra-wa-ke-ta in Linear B, attested at both Pylos and Knossos and apparently one man at either place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The etymology is transparent, <i>laos</i> and <i>agein</i>, leader of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the <i>laos</i> was is less so, and one reading takes it to mean not an army but any weak group of followers in need of protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Pylos list of major landholders he stands immediately after the wanax, with an estate a third the size of the king's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> People are attached to him as they are to the king: landholders can be named by their membership in his household, an adjective built from his title, <i>lawagesios</i>, qualifies certain personnel, and two Pylos land tablets, Ea 421 and Ea 809, record men holding land from his wheelwright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What he actually did is the open question of the office, since the name suggests a war leader and the tablets do not confirm one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The comparison usually drawn is with the Germanic societies Tacitus describes, where a king directs the leader who commands his fighting men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that stands the plain fact that no solid evidence from Mycenaean archaeology shows him filling such a role, and he may have been a crown prince, or something else altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Military associations do cling to the title even so, and one suggestion gives him a part in bringing new peoples into the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word for the people he led outlasted the palaces that employed him: <i>laos</i> is Homer's word for the host, and it is the <i>laoi</i> who begin to perish when Apollo sends his pestilence through the camp in the tenth line of the <i>Iliad</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"lawagetas","questions":["The name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means leader of the people, but no solid evidence makes him a war leader, and he may have been a crown prince instead.","Two Pylos tablets record men holding land from the wheelwright of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and an adjective formed from his title qualifies certain personnel."],"tags":["title","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"Thinking Comparatively about Greek Mythology XIII, with a Focus on the Role of Hēraklēs as Kingmaker,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 18 October 2019, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/thinking-comparatively-about-greek-mythology-xiii-with-a-focus-on-the-role-of-herakles-as-kingmaker/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 1.10, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D1%3Acard%3D8. [Open access]","Rachele Pierini and Thomas G. Palaima, eds., \"MASt@CHS — Fall Seminar 2020 (Friday, November 6): Summaries of Presentations and Discussion,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 23 December 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mast-chs-friday-november-6-2020-summaries-of-presentations-and-discussion/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is what the office actually did the open question?","a":"Because the name means leader of the people and suggests a war leader, and the tablets do not confirm one."},{"q":"Why is the Germanic comparison not decisive?","a":"Because although Tacitus describes a king directing the leader who commands his fighting men, no solid evidence from Mycenaean archaeology shows him filling such a role."},{"q":"Why can people be identified as his?","a":"Because landholders can be named by their membership in his household, an adjective built from his title qualifies certain personnel, and two Pylos tablets record men holding land from his wheelwright."}]},
{"id":"gr-080","num":"80","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In the tablets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no grander than the chief of a small group, in one case a group of bronzesmiths, and heads work groups at Pylos, Knossos and Thebes.","answer":"qa-si-re-u","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>qa-si-re-u</b> is a minor official of the Mycenaean palaces, known at Pylos, Knossos and Thebes, and the ancestor of the ordinary Greek word for a king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the tablets he is nothing grander than some kind of chief or leader of a small group, in one case a group of bronzesmiths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is the head of work groups, and one Pylos tablet gives a whole section of seven entries the heading qa-si-re-wi-jo-te, men performing as <i>qa-si-re-u</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another lists him beside the ko-re-te and the po-ro-ko-re-te, officials connected with the kingdom's second-order centres, where the koreter had charge of one of the sixteen major administrative units of the Pylian kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In some contexts he may have run small outlying districts, though on an extreme view the word never occurs outside contexts involving craftsmen and means no more than the man in charge of a manufacturing unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The connection with Homer's <i>basileus</i>, meaning king, is undeniable, and the metamorphosis from a Mycenaean village headman to a Homeric chieftain is explicable in view of the chaotic conditions that followed the collapse of the palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came through that collapse because his power had never been the palace's to give: officials of this kind derived it from local communities, which accounts both for their survival and for their rise in status once the centre was gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>geronsia</i>, whose duties are not fully understood, came through the same way and ended as the classical <i>gerousia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The line has been traced beyond Greek proper as well, as far as the <i>pa-si-le-wo-se</i> of the Cypriot syllabary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the ninth line of the <i>Iliad</i> Apollo is angry at the <i>basileus</i>, two lines after Agamemnon is <i>anax andron</i>, so that a king's word and a foreman's have come to mean the same man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"qa-si-re-u","questions":["Because his power came from local communities rather than the palace, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlived the collapse and rose in status, ending as the ordinary Greek word for a king.","Beside the ko-re-te and the po-ro-ko-re-te, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears on a Pylos tablet among officials connected with the kingdom's second-order centres, far below the wanax."],"tags":["title","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Rachele Pierini and Thomas G. Palaima, eds., \"MASt@CHS — Fall Seminar 2020 (Friday, November 6): Summaries of Presentations and Discussion,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 23 December 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mast-chs-friday-november-6-2020-summaries-of-presentations-and-discussion/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 1.9, ed. D. B. Monro and T. W. Allen, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0133%3Abook%3D1%3Acard%3D8. [Open access]","Silke Knippschild, review of <i>Ancient Greece: From the Mycenaean Palaces to the Age of Homer</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Irene S. Lemos, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.08.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.39/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did this official survive the collapse of the palaces?","a":"Because his power had never been the palace's to give: officials of this kind derived it from local communities, which accounts for their survival and their later rise."},{"q":"Why is the shift from foreman to king thought explicable?","a":"Because the metamorphosis is set against the chaotic conditions that followed the collapse, in which a local headman's standing could grow."},{"q":"Why does the extreme view narrow the title so far?","a":"Because on it the word never occurs outside contexts involving craftsmen and means no more than the man in charge of a manufacturing unit."}]},
{"id":"gr-081","num":"81","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Best translated as village, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can mean either the people of a community or the land they hold, and it is an entity that allocates landholdings.","answer":"damos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>damos</b> is the local community of a Mycenaean kingdom, written da-mo in the tablets, and an entity that can allocate landholdings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is perhaps best translated as village, an English word that can refer either to the people of a community or to the land that community holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Linear B evidence strongly suggests it is nothing more than a group of individual landholders, that is, a collective landholding body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are the individual communities found within palatial territory, and each of them maintained a significant degree of independence from the palace, against an older view that saw them as subject to its political and economic control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Records of the kind that name it were used to calculate taxes, which is one reason so much of the archive turns on who held what.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The land is described by two words, ki-ti-me-na and ke-ke-me-na, which Ventris and Chadwick read as private plots against communal ones belonging to the damos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Work through the 1980s and 1990s overturned that reading, showing that both terms describe land associated with the damos, and rendering one private and the other common is now called idiosyncratic and not generally accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plots called ki-ti-me-na at Pylos are never said to be held from a woman, and women there have severely restricted access to land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The collective could also be argued with, and one Pylos tablet records the priestess e-ri-ta in dispute with it over a holding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it did not have was politics: nothing suggests the word carried in the Bronze Age the political sense it took on in the Archaic and Classical periods, so the term that would later mean the people is here the name of a body that holds fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"damos","questions":["Nothing suggests the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried political weight in the Bronze Age, though the word took on exactly that sense in the Archaic and Classical periods.","One Pylos tablet records the priestess e-ri-ta in dispute with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over a holding, which shows the collective could be argued with."],"tags":["institution","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]","Susan Lupack, \"A View from Outside the Palace: The Sanctuary and the <i>Damos</i> in Mycenaean Economy and Society,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 207–17, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0207. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the reading of the two land words change?","a":"Because Ventris and Chadwick took them as private against communal plots, and work through the 1980s and 1990s showed both terms describe land associated with the damos."},{"q":"Why is the older picture of a subject village rejected?","a":"Because each of these communities maintained a significant degree of independence from the palace, against the view that saw them under its political and economic control."},{"q":"Why does the word not yet mean what it later meant?","a":"Because nothing suggests it carried in the Bronze Age the political sense it took on in the Archaic and Classical periods; here it names a body that holds fields."}]},
{"id":"gr-082","num":"82","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Managers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collected dispersed raw materials and gave them to specialists, who added value by skilled labour and made goods the palace then handed to a chosen few.","answer":"Mycenaean palace economy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palatial era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean palace economy</b> is what the mainland palaces did with goods, and it worked by allocation: managers collected dispersed raw materials and gave them out to specialists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those specialists added value to them through skilled labour and produced composite artefacts, which palatial authorities then redistributed to an exclusive group of recipients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The industries run this way were chariots, perfumed oils and textiles, so what the system turned out was not daily bread but prestige.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The allocation has a name in the tablets, <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i>, and a Pylos record of bronzesmiths can list them instead as <i>a-ta-ra-si-jo</i>, men without one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Landholding records were used to calculate taxes, so the archive turns on who held what quite as much as on who made what.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What the palace did not do matters as much: the concepts brought to bear are staple and wealth finance, and the argument is that palatial control worked largely as a wealth finance system meant to raise the prestige of the rulers and secure the allegiance of local elites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That control was partial rather than absolute, and the live question has become the mechanisms and strategies by which a palace exerted it over some areas but not others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Redistribution is not one thing either, since several forms of it may occur at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The offerings the tablets record could not have fully supported the sanctuaries, which removes the main argument for treating those sanctuaries as subject to palatial authority, and they cannot be interpreted as part of a real system of redistribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At least three spheres of economic influence can be posited in a Mycenaean state, the palace, the sanctuaries and the damos, which is a very different picture from one centre directing everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean palace economy","questions":["Control under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was partial rather than absolute, and the live question is how a palace exerted it over some areas and not others.","Beside the palace, sanctuaries and the damos were spheres of influence in their own right, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was never one centre directing everything."],"tags":["concept","institution","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Susan Lupack, \"A View from Outside the Palace: The Sanctuary and the <i>Damos</i> in Mycenaean Economy and Society,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 207–17, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0207. [Open access]","Robert Schon, \"By Appointment to His Majesty the Wanax: Value-Added Goods and Redistribution in Mycenaean Palatial Economies,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 219–27, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0219. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Rethinking Mycenaean Palaces II</i>, rev. and expanded 2nd ed., ed. Michael L. Galaty and William A. Parkinson, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.07.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.57. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]","Rachele Pierini and Thomas G. Palaima, eds., \"MASt@CHS — Fall Seminar 2020 (Friday, November 6): Summaries of Presentations and Discussion,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 23 December 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mast-chs-friday-november-6-2020-summaries-of-presentations-and-discussion/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg/1920px-Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","title":"Mycenaean palace economy","desc":"The great round hearth at the centre of the megaron of the palace at Pylos, its plastered rim ringed by the sockets of four columns.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","alt":"Hearth of the megaron of Pylos"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the system turn out prestige rather than staples?","a":"Because the industries run this way were chariots, perfumed oils and textiles, whose products went back out to an exclusive group of recipients."},{"q":"Why is palatial control described as partial?","a":"Because the live question is by what mechanisms a palace exerted it over some areas but not others, and at least three spheres of economic influence can be posited — the palace, the sanctuaries and the damos."},{"q":"Why are the sanctuaries not read as subject to the palace?","a":"Because the offerings the tablets record could not have fully supported them, which removes the main argument for treating them that way."}]},
{"id":"gr-083","num":"83","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Every female producer at Knossos whose duties can be identified worked in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, largely as corvée labour rather than as slaves, and one tablet records their monthly rations.","answer":"Mycenaean textile industry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean textile industry</b> is the largest single operation the Linear B archives record, and it ran on the <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i>, the distribution or allocation of raw material by the palace to specialist workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behind the cloth stood the flocks, and the archives carry flock records at Pylos and shearing and lambing records at Knossos, where the sheep of the D-tablets have been counted as population statistics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The people who worked the wool were women: at Knossos every female producer whose duties can be identified is occupied with textiles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were largely corvée labourers rather than slaves, and one Knossos tablet, E 777, records monthly rations issued to a group of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across Knossos and Pylos together the evidence has been gathered for some 2,000 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the palace wanted from them is set out in the Lc series of tablets, which records textile production targets for central and western Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cloths named are <i>te-pa</i>, <i>pa-we-a</i> and <i>tu-na-no</i>, and the targets differ according to the groups assigned to fulfil them, groups designated by ethnicity and occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Comparing the total target for non-collector workers in central Crete with the individual targets suggests that the annual planned production was not their sum but a predefined amount of cloth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That in turn answers why only some places in Crete were put under the obligation at all, and it suggests the target was only part of what a Cretan work group owed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cloth was one of the industries whose products left the palace again as value added by skilled labour, redistributed to a restricted group of recipients, and the craft marked the language as well, in the conceptual links drawn between weaving, sewing, devising plots and musical performance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean textile industry","questions":["The Lc tablets set targets for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in central and western Crete, for cloths called <i>te-pa</i>, <i>pa-we-a</i> and <i>tu-na-no</i>, differing by the group assigned to meet them.","Raw material went out to specialist workers under the <i>ta-ra-si-ja</i>, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was organised, and the flocks behind it fill the shearing and lambing records."],"tags":["practice","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Marie-Louise B. Nosch, \"The Mycenaean Administration of Textile Production in the Palace of Knossos: Observations on the Lc(1) Textile Targets,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 4 (2011): 495–505, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.4.0495. [Paywalled]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, by John Killen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]","Robert Schon, \"By Appointment to His Majesty the Wanax: Value-Added Goods and Redistribution in Mycenaean Palatial Economies,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 219–27, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0219. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Mycenaean_stone_tools%2C_1clay_loom_weights%2C_AM_Ag_Andreas%2C_Sifnos%2C_53530.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_stone_tools%2C_1clay_loom_weights%2C_AM_Ag_Andreas%2C_Sifnos%2C_53530.jpg","title":"Mycenaean textile industry","desc":"Mycenaean stone tools (pounders, polishers, grinders) - and clay loom weights. Archaeological Museum of Agios Andreas on Sifnos.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_stone_tools,_1clay_loom_weights,_AM_Ag_Andreas,_Sifnos,_53530.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean stone tools, 1clay loom weights, AM Ag Andreas, Sifnos, 53530"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the workforce known to have been women?","a":"Because at Knossos every female producer whose duties can be identified is occupied with textiles, and across Knossos and Pylos the evidence has been gathered for some 2,000 of them."},{"q":"Why are they read as corvée rather than slaves?","a":"Because they were largely corvée labourers, and one Knossos tablet records monthly rations issued to a group of them."},{"q":"Why is the annual target read as a predefined amount?","a":"Because comparing the total for non-collector workers in central Crete with the individual targets suggests the planned production was not their sum."}]},
{"id":"gr-084","num":"84","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The E- series at Pylos records assessed lands and amounts on one reading to a cadastre, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most complex thing the tablets preserve.","answer":"Mycenaean land tenure","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean land tenure</b> is the most complex thing the Linear B texts record, and the system that evidently existed in the Pylian kingdom is known almost entirely from one run of tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These are the E- series at Pylos, which set down assessed lands and on one reading amount to a cadastre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Holdings come in named kinds: the wanax, the lawagetas and the telestai hold <i>temenoi</i>, while <i>ka-ma</i> land differs from those in some as yet undetermined way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One recent argument makes <i>ka-ma</i> parts of the community's own land set aside for intensive cultivation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The telestai hold theirs in return for their service, their <i>telos</i>, and three of them together hold as much as the king and on average as much as the second man of the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether any of this amounts to feudalism is old ground: some have argued the system at least partly feudal, with lands held from the king in return for services rendered to him, and others deny that there is evidence for feudalism at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One comparison drawn is with the economy typical of medieval monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The purpose of the records is clearer than their vocabulary, since landholding texts were used to calculate taxes in kind precisely, and the round figures in them may reflect lands measured in exact units rather than rough reckoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are local as well as fiscal: the E- records are taken to concern places close to Pylos, perhaps within one administrative district, where the flax assessments and the miscellaneous tribute of other series reach further out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the best-known episode in the run is contested, the tablet on which a priestess appears to argue with the community over what she holds having had its reading as a dispute questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean land tenure","questions":["Some read <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as at least partly feudal, lands held from the king for services rendered, while others deny any evidence for feudalism at all.","Records of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were used to calculate taxes in kind, and their round figures may reflect lands measured in exact units rather than rough reckoning."],"tags":["concept","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Pylos_5.jpg/1920px-Pylos_5.jpg","title":"Mycenaean land tenure","desc":"Pylos (ruins of Mycenaean city, containing so-called Palace of Nestor).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pylos_5.jpg","alt":"Pylos 5"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is so much of what is known drawn from one run of tablets?","a":"Because the E- series at Pylos sets down assessed lands and on one reading amounts to a cadastre, and the system is known almost entirely from it."},{"q":"Why is the feudal reading contested?","a":"Because some argue the system at least partly feudal, with lands held from the king for services rendered, and others deny there is evidence for feudalism at all."},{"q":"Why may the round figures not be rough reckoning?","a":"Because the records were used to calculate taxes in kind precisely, so the round numbers may reflect lands measured in exact units."}]},
{"id":"gr-085","num":"85","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Among <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Poseidon is the most important at Pylos, while two names, Enyalios and Paiawon, survive only as later epithets of Ares and Apollo.","answer":"Mycenaean gods in Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean gods in Linear B</b> are the divine names the palace tablets record, and most of them are Olympians, some centuries before Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poseidon stands out: he is named at Knossos and at Pylos, where he is evidently the most important deity to judge from the frequency with which he is mentioned, and one tablet assigns him banquet materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Zeus appears at Knossos as <i>di-wo</i>, with a female counterpart Diwia; Hera stands beside him on one Pylos tablet, and Artemis appears as <i>a-ti-mi-te</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two names arrive as gods and survive only as epithets, Enyalios, later attached to Ares, and Paiawon, later Paian and attached to Apollo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysos is present as <i>di-wo-nu-so-jo</i>, though the name may belong to a person rather than a god, and Potnia, the mistress, is named at both palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The hierarchy is not the later one: in the Knossos oil allocations Zeus does not get the most, having not yet the pre-eminent place he occupied later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor are the old Aegean powers present, since none of the goddesses generally taken for them is mentioned in these texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the gods received was modest and bloodless, a pot of honey, spices such as fennel and coriander, jugs of oil, with honey going to all the gods on one Knossos tablet and a Priestess of the Winds having honey of her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oddities are what a reader remembers: perfumed oil goes to an Aswian Potnia and to a Divine Mother, and a Theban wine record has been read as an offering to the Snakes, beside the terracotta snakes from the cult centre at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How much of this the palace controlled is open, since the offerings recorded could not have fully supported the sanctuaries, and the standard edition now spreads texts once filed together as tribute and ritual offerings across land tenure, taxation and religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean gods in Linear B","questions":["Offerings to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were bloodless and modest, a pot of honey, spices such as fennel and coriander, and jugs of oil, with Zeus getting far from the most.","None of the goddesses usually taken for old Aegean powers appears among <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a Theban wine record has been read as an offering to the Snakes."],"tags":["concept","deity","religion","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 26: Mycenaean and Late Cycladic Religion and Religious Architecture,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-26-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]","Susan Lupack, \"A View from Outside the Palace: The Sanctuary and the <i>Damos</i> in Mycenaean Economy and Society,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011): 207–17, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.2.0207. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, by John Killen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Mycenaean_figurines%2C_Bird_Goddesses%2C_Psi_type%2C_202185.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_figurines%2C_Bird_Goddesses%2C_Psi_type%2C_202185.jpg","title":"Mycenaean gods in Linear B","desc":"Late Helladic figurines, so called Bird Goddesses, 1400-1300 BCE. Archaeological Museum of Nafplio: (22) Female figurine, Psi type. Nafplion, Evangelistria, 1350-1300 BCE. (23, 24) Female figurine, Psi type. Nafplion, Evangelistria, chamber tomb ΛΓ, 1400-1350 BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_figurines,_Bird_Goddesses,_Psi_type,_202185.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean figurines, Bird Goddesses, Psi type"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the hierarchy of gods not the later one?","a":"Because in the Knossos oil allocations Zeus does not get the most, not yet having the pre-eminent place he occupied later, while Poseidon is the deity most frequently mentioned at Pylos."},{"q":"Why does the record surprise on what the gods received?","a":"Because the offerings are modest and bloodless — a pot of honey, spices such as fennel and coriander, jugs of oil — rather than the sacrifices later cult implies."},{"q":"Why is palatial control of the sanctuaries open?","a":"Because the offerings recorded could not have fully supported them, so the tablets do not show the palace maintaining the cult."}]},
{"id":"gr-086","num":"86","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A major deity at both Knossos and Pylos, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a name that means simply mistress, and at Knossos she is the mistress of the Labyrinth.","answer":"Potnia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Potnia</b> is a major deity of the Linear B tablets, worshipped at both Knossos and Pylos, and her name is simply the word for mistress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She rarely stands alone: at Knossos she is <i>da-pu-ri-to-jo po-ti-ni-ja</i>, Potnia of the Labyrinth, and <i>a-ta-na po-ti-ni-ja</i>, which may be Athena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Pylos the additions are mostly places, <i>pa-ki-ja-ni-ja</i> and <i>a-si-wi-ja</i> among them, with <i>i-qe-ja</i>, Hippeia, perhaps meaning of horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether that leaves one goddess with many local faces or many goddesses sharing a title is the question those names raise, and the evidence has been weighed for a monotheistic and a polytheistic reading alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One line drawn through it is grammatical: alone, Potnia is a goddess, but when the name is modified or associated with a place she may be a queen instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What she received was of a piece with the rest of the cult, and honey on one Knossos tablet goes to all the gods and to Potnia of <i>da-pu-ri-to</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sheep are connected with her, though not as offerings, and Amnisos is often named as the site of an offering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Perfumed oil reaches an Aswian Potnia, one of a set of recipients at Pylos whose associations lie outside the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The women who served such cults held a standing they held nowhere else, since most women appearing in prominent roles at Pylos are religious officials and religion has been called the one institution that could trump gender, though at Knossos it made little difference to the hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word outlived the palaces that wrote it down, for Homer still calls Artemis <i>potnia theron</i>, mistress of wild beasts, in book 21 of the <i>Iliad</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Potnia","questions":["Alone <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a goddess, but when the name is modified or attached to a place she may be a queen, which is why the epithets matter.","The word for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlived the palaces, since Homer still calls Artemis <i>potnia theron</i>, mistress of wild beasts, in book 21 of the <i>Iliad</i>."],"tags":["deity","religion","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 26: Mycenaean and Late Cycladic Religion and Religious Architecture,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-26-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>KE-RA-ME-JA: Studies Presented to Cynthia W. Shelmerdine</i>, ed. Dimitri Nakassis, Joann Gulizio and Sarah A. James, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.12.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.12.03/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 21.470, ed. D. B. Monro and T. W. Allen, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0133%3Abook%3D21%3Acard%3D468. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Women in Mycenaean Greece: The Linear B Tablets from Pylos and Knossos</i>, by Barbara A. Olsen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.03.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.03.45/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/NAMA_Art%C3%A9mis_Orthia.jpg","title":"Potnia","desc":"Relief plate, of a bronze fibula. Ivory, 8 cm. Sparta, sanctuary of Artemis Orthia. Initial Archaic, 660 AEC. National Archaeological Museum, Athens (A15502).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Art%C3%A9mis_Orthia.jpg","alt":"NAMA Artémis Orthia"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why do her many titles raise a problem?","a":"Because the name is simply the word for mistress and rarely stands alone, so whether the tablets show one goddess with local faces or many goddesses sharing a title is open."},{"q":"Why does grammar help draw a line through the question?","a":"Because alone she is a goddess, while when the name is modified or associated with a place she may be a queen instead."},{"q":"Why did serving such a cult raise a woman's standing?","a":"Because most women appearing in prominent roles at Pylos are religious officials, religion having been called the one institution that could trump gender — though at Knossos it made little difference."}]},
{"id":"gr-087","num":"87","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Scenes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are common on the carved stelae over the Shaft Graves, though whether they show warfare, hunting or races at funeral games is open.","answer":"Mycenaean chariot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">On stelae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean chariot</b> is the light horse-drawn vehicle of Late Bronze Age Greece, and the earliest sign of it stands over the Shaft Graves at Mycenae, carved on their stelae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chariot scenes are quite common on those stelae, though what they show is open, whether illustrations of warfare, of hunting, or of races at funeral games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing of the kind existed in northern or central Europe at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The animal that pulled it appears in the tablets as <i>i-qo</i>, from the Indo-European <i>ekwos</i>, and one Knossos tablet sets horses and asses down together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In palatial art the horses are shown hitched to chariots at both Tiryns and Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest evidence for the vehicle is the ground it ran on: the Argolid carried a road network linking the major Mycenaean sites, reaching north into the Corinthia and east across the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was built with bridges across ravines and with drainage culverts of limestone boulders, corbel-vaulted beneath the roadbed, and the only rationale for such constructions is that they were designed to accommodate vehicular traffic in the form of chariots and wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Traces of the same thing turn up in Messenia, in Attica and, it is claimed, in Phocis, and carefully constructed roads are not a feature of later Greek civilisation until the 5th century BCE at the earliest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The vehicles themselves were the palace's work, the North-Eastern Building at Pylos having been argued to be a secondary workshop for their final assembly, and they belong with armour, leather goods and furniture among the state-controlled industries that supplied the elite with wealth and prestige.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their uses, which include warfare and status display, are held ultimately to have served the ideological and material interests of the palatial elite, while who drove them is less settled, one suggestion taking the chariot tablets to record a Mycenaean version of a Near Eastern warrior-charioteer caste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean chariot","questions":["At Pylos the North-Eastern Building has been argued to be a secondary workshop for the final assembly of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of the palace's controlled industries.","Who drove the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would be settled by one suggestion, which takes the chariot tablets to record a Mycenaean version of a Near Eastern warrior-charioteer caste."],"tags":["object","technology","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 24: Mycenaean Pictorial Art and Pottery,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-24-narrative/. [Open access]","Dimitri Nakassis, review of <i>Rethinking Mycenaean Palaces II</i>, rev. and expanded 2nd ed., ed. Michael L. Galaty and William A. Parkinson, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2008.07.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.57. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>The People of Knossos: Prosopographical Studies in the Knossos Linear B Archives</i>, by Hedvig Landenius Enegren, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 114, no. 2 (2010), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/682/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Mycenaean_potery_krater%2C_horse-drawn_chariot%2C_1350-1300_BC%2C_Enkomi%2C_BM_Cat_Vases_C340%2C_142835.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_potery_krater%2C_horse-drawn_chariot%2C_1350-1300_BC%2C_Enkomi%2C_BM_Cat_Vases_C340%2C_142835.jpg","title":"Mycenaean chariot","desc":"Mycenaean potery krater decorated with a horse-drawn chariot and riders, 1350-1300 BCE (LH IIIa2). Found in Tomb 70, Enkomi, Cyprus. British Museum, GR 1897.4-1.1113. BM Cat Vases C340.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_potery_krater,_horse-drawn_chariot,_1350-1300_BC,_Enkomi,_BM_Cat_Vases_C340,_142835.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean potery krater, horse-drawn chariot, 1350-1300 BCE, Enkomi, BM Cat Vases C340"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are the roads the clearest evidence for the vehicle?","a":"Because bridges across ravines and corbel-vaulted drainage culverts beneath the roadbed have no rationale except to accommodate vehicular traffic in the form of chariots and wagons."},{"q":"Why is what the grave stelae show uncertain?","a":"Because the chariot scenes on them may be illustrations of warfare, of hunting, or of races at funeral games."},{"q":"Why is the chariot grouped with armour and furniture?","a":"Because it belongs among the state-controlled industries supplying the elite with wealth and prestige, and served the ideological and material interests of the palatial elite."}]},
{"id":"gr-088","num":"88","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Buried in the Cuirass Tomb in the Argolid, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one of the oldest complete suits of armour from the European Bronze Age, its surviving bronze weighing about 18 kilograms (40 pounds).","answer":"Dendra panoply","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dendra panoply</b> is a suit of bronze plate armour from a tomb near the village of Dendra in the Argolid, one of the oldest complete suits of armour from the European Bronze Age and the example reached for when the look of Mycenaean metal armour has to be shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came out of the Cuirass Tomb, tomb 12, a chamber tomb of the ordinary Mycenaean kind, an underground room reached by a long entrance corridor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tombs of that sort were cut into rock rather than built and normally hold several burials, so they are generally considered family tombs, which places this warrior among his relatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The suit is about 3,500 years old, its plates edged with leather strips and joined with leather thongs, and its head was covered by plaques imitating a boar's tusk helmet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is estimated to weigh about 18 kilograms (40 pounds), one handguard and parts of the greaves being lost and the bronze oxidised over three and a half millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether a man could fight in it has been doubted almost since it came to light, museum captions calling it too cumbersome to be worn except on ceremonial occasions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others restricted it to a man riding rather than walking, and Drews put that plainly, holding the corslet armour for a chariot rider and inappropriate for an infantryman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study published in 2024 tested the question instead of arguing it, putting thirteen volunteers from a Hellenic Army marine brigade into a replica weighing 23.32 kilograms (51 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of them completed an eleven-hour simulation of Late Bronze Age combat, which its authors take as evidence that the armour was entirely compatible with use in extended combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The written record is less help than it might be, since the armour tablets at Pylos describe nothing similar to this suit, in spite of a logogram among them that looks exactly like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dendra panoply","questions":["Museum captions once called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> too cumbersome to wear except on ceremonial occasions, and its corslet has been held inappropriate for an infantryman to fight in.","Thirteen volunteers from a marine brigade fought an eleven-hour simulation in a 23.32-kilogram (51-pound) replica of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and every one of them finished it."],"tags":["object","technology","warfare","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Andreas D. Flouris et al., \"Analysis of Greek Prehistoric Combat in Full Body Armour Based on Physiological Principles: A Series of Studies Using Thematic Analysis, Human Experiments, and Numerical Simulations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301494, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301494. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","David Gill, review of <i>The End of the Bronze Age: Changes in Warfare and the Catastrophe ca. 1200 B.C.</i>, by Robert Drews, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 1994.01.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.01.09/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Brill's Companion to Warfare in the Bronze Age Aegean</i>, ed. Lynne A. Kvapil and Kim Shelton, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2025.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Mycenaean_armour_from_chamber_tomb_12_of_Dendra_1.JPG/1920px-Mycenaean_armour_from_chamber_tomb_12_of_Dendra_1.JPG","title":"Dendra panoply","desc":"Mycenaean armour from chamber tomb 12 of Dendra 1.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_armour_from_chamber_tomb_12_of_Dendra_1.JPG","alt":"Mycenaean armour from chamber tomb 12 of Dendra 1"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was the suit long thought unwearable in battle?","a":"Because museum captions called it too cumbersome for anything but ceremony, and Drews held the corslet armour for a chariot rider and inappropriate for an infantryman."},{"q":"Why did the 2024 study change that?","a":"Because thirteen volunteers in a replica weighing 23.32 kilograms all completed an eleven-hour simulation of Late Bronze Age combat."},{"q":"Why is the written record little help here?","a":"Because the armour tablets at Pylos describe nothing similar to this suit, in spite of a logogram among them that looks exactly like it."}]},
{"id":"gr-089","num":"89","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Homer describes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as wrought of hide, stiffened within by tight-stretched thongs, faced without with the white teeth of a boar and lined with felt.","answer":"boar's tusk helmet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In art from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>boar's tusk helmet</b> is the characteristic headgear of Mycenaean warriors, a cap of hide faced with plates cut from the tusks of wild boar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fullest description of one is in the tenth book of the <i>Iliad</i>, where Meriones gives Odysseus a helmet wrought of hide and made stiff within by many a tight-stretched thong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without, the white teeth of a boar of gleaming tusks were set thick on this side and that, well and cunningly, and within was fixed a lining of felt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homer gives it a pedigree as well: Autolycus stole it from Eleon after breaking into the house of Amyntor, and gave it to Amphidamas, who passed it to Molus as a guest-gift, and Molus gave it to his son to wear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four owners stand between the theft and the man who puts it on for a night raid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The helmet runs through Mycenaean art, and warriors wearing them crowd the scene of combat worked in relief on the silver Battle Krater from the Shaft Graves at Mycenae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are shown away from the mainland too, on the north wall of the miniature fresco in the West House at Akrotiri, where the warriors carry tower shields as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who those warriors are is unresolved, and the evidence presently available will not decide it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They may even appear in Egypt, since a painted papyrus from el-Amarna in the British Museum shows soldiers in helmets and short-cropped oxhide tunics that can be paralleled in Aegean representations, which has been read as evidence for direct rather than indirect contact between the two cultures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The shape was still worth copying in metal, and when a full reconstruction of the Dendra armour was built its helmet plaques imitated a boar's tusk helmet over an inner cap of leather, as Homer describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"boar's tusk helmet","questions":["Warriors wearing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crowd the relief on the silver Battle Krater from the Shaft Graves, and appear again on the north wall of a fresco at Akrotiri.","Soldiers on a papyrus from el-Amarna wear helmets and oxhide tunics paralleled in Aegean art, which may put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in an Egyptian painting."],"tags":["object","technology","warfare","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 10.260–71, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D10%3Acard%3D254. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]","Andreas D. Flouris et al., \"Analysis of Greek Prehistoric Combat in Full Body Armour Based on Physiological Principles: A Series of Studies Using Thematic Analysis, Human Experiments, and Numerical Simulations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301494, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301494. [Open access]","Louise Schofield and R. B. Parkinson, \"Of Helmets and Heretics: A Possible Egyptian Representation of Mycenaean Warriors on a Papyrus from el-Amarna,\" <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 89 (1994): 157–70, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0068245400015343. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Boars%27s_tusk_helmet_NAMA6568_Athens_Greece1.jpg/1920px-Boars%27s_tusk_helmet_NAMA6568_Athens_Greece1.jpg","title":"boar's tusk helmet","desc":"Boar’s tusk helmet with cheek-guards and a double bone hook on top. Mycenae, chamber Tomb 515, 14th – 13th cents. BCE. N°6568.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boars%27s_tusk_helmet_NAMA6568_Athens_Greece1.jpg","alt":"Boars's tusk helmet NAMA6568 Athens Greece1"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does Homer give the helmet a pedigree?","a":"Because four owners stand between Autolycus's theft of it and the man who puts it on for a night raid, which is how the poem marks an heirloom worth naming."},{"q":"Why are the warriors of the Akrotiri fresco unresolved?","a":"Because they wear the helmets and carry tower shields, and the evidence presently available will not decide who they are."},{"q":"Why was the shape still worth imitating in metal?","a":"Because when a full reconstruction of the Dendra armour was built its helmet plaques imitated a boar's tusk helmet over an inner cap of leather, as Homer describes."}]},
{"id":"gr-090","num":"90","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Named from the outline of its two lobes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was painted as a whole frieze on the second floor of the Residential Quarters at Knossos.","answer":"figure-of-eight shield","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In art</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>figure-of-eight shield</b> is the waisted body shield of Bronze Age Aegean art, named from the outline its two lobes make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shields on that scale are what Homer gives his greatest fighters, and making one was a craftsman's work in hide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ajax carries a shield like a city wall, of seven hides of sturdy bulls with an eighth layer of bronze wrought over them, made by Tychius, far best of workers in hide, whose home was in Hyle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another such shield in the poem reaches to the feet and is called a defence against javelins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It could be a liability as easily as a protection: Periphetes, turning to flee, tripped upon the rim of the shield he himself bore, fell backward, and Hector killed him where he lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In art the shape is everywhere, and at Knossos a whole Frieze of Figure-of-Eight Shields was painted on the second floor of the Residential Quarters, the centrepieces worked with parallel chevrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Painters used it on pottery as well, one of the motifs of the Alternating Style, in a set that runs double axe, sacral knot, sea anemone and trefoil rockwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fighting is a subject the Shaft Grave workshops returned to, a silver krater from them carrying a scene of crowded combat worked in relief and a silver rhyton the attack on a fortified town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other body shield of Aegean art is the tower shield, rectangular rather than waisted, and it is one of those that the disputed warriors of the Akrotiri flotilla fresco carry ashore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both shapes are shields a man stands behind rather than holds up, which is what the poem means by one reaching to the feet and by one likened to a city wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"figure-of-eight shield","questions":["Beside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the other Aegean body shield is the tower shield, rectangular rather than waisted, carried by the warriors on the West House fresco at Akrotiri.","Painters set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among their Alternating Style motifs, in a set running double axe, sacral knot, sea anemone and trefoil rockwork."],"tags":["object","technology","warfare","art","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 7.219–23, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D7%3Acard%3D206. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 15.645–47, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D15%3Acard%3D638. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 17: Akrotiri on Thera, the Santorini Volcano and the Middle and Late Cycladic Periods in the Central Aegean Islands,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-17-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Wall_Painting_of_Figure-of-Eight_Shield_%283400853679%29.jpg/1920px-Wall_Painting_of_Figure-of-Eight_Shield_%283400853679%29.jpg","title":"figure-of-eight shield","desc":"Represents an original made of cow's hide. Defensive weapon and symbol of a war goddess. Mycenaean, 13th century BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wall_Painting_of_Figure-of-Eight_Shield_(3400853679).jpg","alt":"Wall Painting of Figure-of-Eight Shield"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is such a shield a liability as well as a protection?","a":"Because Periphetes, turning to flee, tripped upon the rim of the shield he himself bore, fell backward, and Hector killed him where he lay."},{"q":"Why is it a shield a man stands behind rather than holds up?","a":"Because the poem describes one reaching to the feet and likens another to a city wall, which is a body shield rather than a held one."},{"q":"Why does Ajax's shield take a named craftsman?","a":"Because it is seven hides of sturdy bulls with an eighth layer of bronze wrought over them, made by Tychius, far best of workers in hide."}]},
{"id":"gr-091","num":"91","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Most examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were made in Crete, and analysis of their clay separates three main production areas chemically and 24 fabrics under the microscope.","answer":"stirrup jar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>stirrup jar</b> is the characteristic closed pot of the Late Bronze Age Aegean, a shape whose origins can be traced to Middle Minoan III and Late Minoan IA in Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the mainland it first appears in appreciable quantity in Late Helladic IIIA1, and in the phase after that the stirrup jar, the piriform jar and the alabastron are the shapes most frequently found in tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its form was conspicuous enough to be copied elsewhere, at Akrotiri among other sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The version that travelled is the coarse-ware transport jar, and preliminary results recognise perfumed oil, and probably wine, as the commodities it most often carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most were produced in Crete, and analysis of their clay separates three main production areas chemically and 24 fabrics petrographically, though only 15 of those fabrics are shared by more than one vessel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some carry Linear B painted on before firing, up to about 200 such inscriptions being known, and a recent supplement added 48 more to the published corpus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they say is usually a personal name, a place name, or both, and not one of the inscribed transport jars was made in mainland Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the writing was for is disputed, the jars being taken by some for gifts marked as sent from one palace to another and by others for documents of a Cretan administration, their appearance on the mainland then being secondary re-use in trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A few are painted <i>wanaktero</i>, royal, and those turn up at Thebes, Eleusis, Tiryns and Chania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where a jar came from can now be told from its fabric, and those at Tiryns are mostly from Chania and the western Messara, arriving after the palace at Knossos was destroyed, which points to a new exchange network between the mainland and Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"stirrup jar","questions":["Up to about 200 Linear B inscriptions were painted on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before firing, usually a personal name, a place name, or both.","The examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found at Tiryns come mostly from Chania and the western Messara, arriving after the palace at Knossos was destroyed."],"tags":["object","technology","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Transport Stirrup Jars of the Bronze Age Aegean and East Mediterranean</i>, by Halford W. Haskell, Richard E. Jones, Peter M. Day and John T. Killen, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 117, no. 3 (2013), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/1617/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>Supplemento al Corpus delle iscrizioni vascolari in lineare B</i>, by Louis Godart and Anna Sacconi, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.10.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.41/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 24: Mycenaean Pictorial Art and Pottery,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-24-narrative/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Maritime Transport Containers in the Bronze–Iron Age Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean</i>, ed. Stella Demesticha and A. Bernard Knapp, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 122, no. 3 (2018), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/3691/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Mycenaean_-_Stirrup_Jar_-_Walters_482084_-_Three_Quarter_Top.jpg","title":"stirrup jar","desc":"This jar is named for its handle, which resembles a stirrup. Originally created in Crete in the 16th century BCE, the stirrup jar was extremely popular throughout the Aegean region for both storage and transport and became the most characteristic Mycenaean vessel type.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_-_Stirrup_Jar_-_Walters_482084_-_Three_Quarter_Top.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean - Stirrup Jar - Three Quarter Top"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why are the inscribed transport jars evidence about Crete?","a":"Because not one of them was made in mainland Greece, so the writing on them belongs to Cretan production whatever it means."},{"q":"Why is the purpose of the painted inscriptions disputed?","a":"Because they are taken by some for gifts marked as sent from one palace to another and by others for documents of a Cretan administration, re-used secondarily in trade."},{"q":"Why do the jars at Tiryns point to a new network?","a":"Because they are mostly from Chania and the western Messara and arrive after the palace at Knossos was destroyed."}]},
{"id":"gr-092","num":"92","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"It has been argued that of the two gold <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one was the work of a Minoan craftsman and the other came out of a Mycenaean workshop.","answer":"Vapheio cups","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Vapheio cups</b> are a pair of gold cups from the tholos tomb at Vapheio in Laconia, and the question they raise is who made them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ellen Davis, whose study of them is the standard one, proposed that one was the work of a Minoan craftsman and the other the product of a Mycenaean workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shape is Minoan and the name has stuck to it: a straight-sided Vapheio cup, inspired by Cretan prototypes, is common in the Cycladic repertoire too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the quiet cup a bull stands hobbled, and it is often cited to show that Minoan pictorial art chooses the moment immediately following violent action rather than the violence itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same eye picks the moment before, as in the cat stalking the bird from Ayia Triadha, though the instant of maximum violence is depicted occasionally as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Attributing precious metalwork on the mainland is an old difficulty, and the Cretan origin of such objects is still called uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Shaft Graves show why: of their 28 vessels of solid gold most are rather clumsily made, with technical features atypical of Minoan craftsmanship, and are almost without exception taken for the work of local craftsmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The 42 silver vessels from the same graves are far more carefully made, matching silver plate from Crete, and are read either as imports or as Minoan work done to order for mainland patrons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of that plate is of Minoan shapes, Vapheio cups and ewers and rhyta among them, while mainland taste shows in the goblets and kantharoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a pair of cups was for has an answer of its own: they have been read as instruments of diplomacy, tools in the cultivation of host-guest relations in the tradition of <i>xenia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Vapheio cups","questions":["A bull stands hobbled on the quiet one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, cited to show Minoan art choosing the moment immediately after violent action rather than the violence itself.","Paired vessels like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been read as instruments of diplomacy, tools in the cultivation of host-guest relations in the tradition of <i>xenia</i>."],"tags":["object","art","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jessica Doyle, review of <i>Studies in Aegean Art and Culture: A New York Aegean Bronze Age Colloquium in Memory of Ellen N. Davis</i>, ed. Robert B. Koehl, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2017.06.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.06.28. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 14: Late Minoan Painting and Other Representational Art: Pottery, Frescoes, Steatite Vases, Ivories, and Bronzes,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-14-narrative/. [Open access]","Philip P. Betancourt, review of <i>The Manufacture of Minoan Metal Vessels: Theory and Practice</i>, by Christina F. Clarke, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 121, no. 1 (2017), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/3368/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Goldbecher_von_Vafio.JPG","title":"Vapheio cups","desc":"Mycenaen golden cup from Vafeio, Laconia. Approximately first half of 15th century BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goldbecher_von_Vafio.JPG","alt":"Goldbecher von Vafio"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is attributing mainland gold work so difficult?","a":"Because the Cretan origin of such objects is still called uncertain, and Davis proposed that of this very pair one is Minoan work and the other Mycenaean."},{"q":"Why do the Shaft Grave vessels divide by metal?","a":"Because most of the 28 gold ones are rather clumsily made with features atypical of Minoan craftsmanship, while the 42 silver ones match silver plate from Crete."},{"q":"Why is the quiet cup so often cited?","a":"Because its hobbled bull shows Minoan pictorial art choosing the moment immediately following violent action rather than the violence itself."}]},
{"id":"gr-093","num":"93","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"What moved in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> falls into three kinds: raw materials like ivory and ostrich eggs, manufactured goods like seals and textiles, and produce like wine and wool.","answer":"Mycenaean trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First amber</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1725 – 1675 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gelidonya wreck</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean trade</b> is the movement of goods between the Greek mainland and the rest of the Bronze Age Mediterranean, and what moved falls into three kinds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The raw materials are precious metals, gold and silver and electrum, ivory from both elephants and hippopotami, ostrich eggs, stones for architecture and jewellery and tools, and the spices used in perfumed oils, coriander and frankincense and myrrh among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The manufactured goods are pottery, seals, carved ivories, textiles, furniture, stone and metal vessels, and weaponry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The agricultural produce is wine, olive oil, flax, hides and wool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Baltic amber came south as well, almost exclusively as beads and multiply perforated rectangular plaques, the first of it reaching Greece about 1725 to 1675 BCE, possibly in only three consignments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One ship is known in detail: the wreck off Cape Gelidonya went down around 1200 BCE or a little later, nine or ten metres (30 or 33 feet) long, and probably served both as a carrier of raw materials and as a sort of itinerant smithy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whose it was is not settled, Bass having argued the ship Phoenician or Canaanite and Muhly and others Mycenaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far the goods travelled is measurable in pottery, since Aegean-type ware from 103 sites on the Italian mainland makes up about a fifth of the Mycenaean ceramics circulating in the central Mediterranean, and Italian potters were making it themselves as well as taking it in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Transport stirrup jars carried oil and wine eastward, Cyprus being a major market for them, and who ran that traffic is argued, since private traders not controlled by palatial authorities seem not only possible but probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the top it was diplomacy, and the Mycenaean pottery reaching Egypt has been read as a highly organised system engaging the ruling elite at Mycenae and the pharaonic court, with olives and olive oil a crucial part of the connection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean trade","questions":["Aegean-type pottery from 103 sites in Italy shows how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached, and local potters there were making it as well as importing it.","Private traders outside palace control seem probable in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while at the top it was diplomacy, olives and oil going to Egypt as royal greeting gifts."],"tags":["concept","practice","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Mycenaean Greece, Mediterranean Commerce, and the Formation of Identity</i>, by Bryan E. Burns, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/891/. [Open access]","Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Transport Stirrup Jars of the Bronze Age Aegean and East Mediterranean</i>, by Halford W. Haskell, Richard E. Jones, Peter M. Day and John T. Killen, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 117, no. 3 (2013), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/1617/. [Open access]","Christine Johnston, review of <i>Italo-Mycenaean Pottery: The Archaeological and Archaeometric Dimensions</i>, by Richard Jones, Sara T. Levi, Marco Bettelli and Lucia Vagnetti, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.02.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.42. [Open access]","Jorrit M. Kelder, \"Royal Gift Exchange between Mycenae and Egypt: Olives as 'Greeting Gifts' in the Late Bronze Age Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 3 (2009): 339–52, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.113.3.339. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mycenaean_stirrup_vase_Louvre_AO19201.jpg/1920px-Mycenaean_stirrup_vase_Louvre_AO19201.jpg","title":"Mycenaean trade","desc":"Mycenaean stirrup vase, 14th-13th centuries BCE, imported to Ugarit. Found in the acropolis of Ras Shamra (ancient Ugarit), tomb 37.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_stirrup_vase_Louvre_AO19201.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean stirrup vase Louvre AO19201"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Gelidonya ship read as a smithy as well as a carrier?","a":"Because it went down around 1200 BCE carrying raw materials and probably served both as a carrier of them and as a sort of itinerant smithy."},{"q":"Why is pottery a good measure of how far the goods travelled?","a":"Because Aegean-type ware from 103 Italian sites makes up about a fifth of the Mycenaean ceramics circulating in the central Mediterranean, and Italian potters were making it themselves."},{"q":"Why is palatial control of the traffic doubted?","a":"Because private traders not controlled by palatial authorities seem not only possible but probable, whatever the diplomatic exchange at the top."}]},
{"id":"gr-094","num":"94","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The bulk cargo of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was copper and tin in the ten-to-one ratio that makes bronze, with 354 of the copper ingots of the oxhide type.","answer":"Uluburun shipwreck","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Uluburun shipwreck</b> is a Late Bronze Age vessel that went down off Uluburun near Kaş on the Turkish coast, found in 1982 by a sponge diver who described metal biscuits with ears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was excavated over 11 seasons between 1984 and 1994, in more than 22,000 dives, at depths beyond 46 metres (150 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hull was Lebanese cedar, pegged mortise-and-tenon, with a proto-keel rather than a true one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bulk cargo was copper and tin in the ten-to-one ratio that makes bronze, 354 of the copper ingots being of the oxhide shape, and its tin came as oxhide ingots of metallic tin, in far greater quantities than any earlier find.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around that lay carved ivory containers, gold jewellery set with carnelian and agate, glass ingots, elephant tusks, ostrich eggshells and faience beads, with at least 149 Canaanite jars of terebinth resin and a gold scarab naming Nefertiti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The wreck is dated to about 1320 BCE, give or take fifteen years, older than the ship at Cape Gelidonya, until then the earliest ever excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its tin has since been traced: 105 ingots, 91 per cent of that cargo and about a tonne of metal, were put through lead isotope, trace element and tin isotope analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two-thirds proved to come from the Taurus mountains of Turkey and a third, 35 ingots, from deposits in Tajikistan and Uzbekistan more than 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) away, which the authors read as a culturally diverse, multiregional and multivector system rather than one under central control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not everyone is confident of such sourcing, since these ingots carry more lead and antimony and less indium than others, and their lead isotopes probably suffered contamination in corroding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For Greece the question is who was aboard: the excavator proposes two men of Aegean origin and a ship bound for the Aegean, and the cargo mirrors in many respects the palatial gift exchange of the Amarna Letters, raising a diplomatic voyage tied to Mycenaean palatial enterprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Uluburun shipwreck","questions":["Analysis of the tin from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put two-thirds of it in the Taurus mountains and a third in Tajikistan and Uzbekistan, over 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) away.","The excavator of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposes that two men of Aegean origin were aboard and that the ship was sailing to the Aegean."],"tags":["event","object","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["\"Uluburun Late Bronze Age Shipwreck Excavation,\" Institute of Nautical Archaeology, https://nauticalarch.org/projects/uluburun-late-bronze-age-shipwreck-excavation/. [Open access]","Wayne Powell et al., \"Tin from Uluburun Shipwreck Shows Small-Scale Commodity Exchange Fueled Continental Tin Supply across Late Bronze Age Eurasia,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 48 (2022): eabq3766, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq3766. [Open access]","Christoph Bachhuber, \"Aegean Interest on the Uluburun Ship,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 110, no. 3 (2006): 345–63, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.110.3.345. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Isotope Systematics and Chemical Composition of Tin Ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and Other Late Bronze Age Sites in the Eastern Mediterranean Sea: An Ultimate Key to Tin Provenance?,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Uluburun1.jpg","title":"Uluburun shipwreck","desc":"Information Description: Wooden model from the Shipwreck from Uluburun. Source: own work Date: June 2006 Author: Martin Bahmann.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uluburun1.jpg","alt":"Uluburun1"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the tin cargo read as a multiregional system?","a":"Because two-thirds of the analysed ingots came from the Taurus mountains and a third from deposits in Tajikistan and Uzbekistan more than 3,000 kilometres away."},{"q":"Why is that sourcing not universally accepted?","a":"Because these ingots carry more lead and antimony and less indium than others, and their lead isotopes probably suffered contamination in corroding."},{"q":"Why has a diplomatic voyage been proposed?","a":"Because the cargo mirrors in many respects the palatial gift exchange of the Amarna Letters, and the excavator proposes two men of Aegean origin aboard a ship bound for the Aegean."}]},
{"id":"gr-095","num":"95","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Written ah-hi-ya-wa-a in the Hittite syllabary, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now almost unanimously identified with an Aegean Late Bronze Age and apparently palatial power.","answer":"Ahhiyawa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hittite texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ahhiyawa</b> is a land named in the Hittite records, and whether the name means the Greek world has been argued since the 1920s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is written <i>ah-hi-ya-wa-a</i> in the Hittite syllabary, with an older form <i>a-ah-hi-ya</i> behind it, and one reconstruction derives both the Hittite name and the Greek <i>Akhaioi</i> from a proto-form <i>akhai-wi-a</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between about 1400 and 1200 BCE the Hittite state had several encounters with Ahhiyawans on Anatolian soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The documents that record them are the famous ones: the Tawagalawa letter, the Indictment of Madduwatta, the Sausgamuwa Treaty, the Annals of Mursili, the Alaksandu Treaty and the Milawata letter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One runs the other way, for the fragmentary letter KUB 26.91, found at Hattusa, was sent by a king of Ahhiyawa, and messages were conveyed in writing to the frontier outposts of each kingdom in its own language and script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where the land lay has been put on the Greek mainland or among the Aegean islands, and also in Pamphylia, in Cyprus and on Rhodes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The boldest reading places it at Mycenae itself, on the ground that no other site on the Helladic mainland fits the references the texts make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The present consensus is wide but shallower, since Ahhiyawa is now almost unanimously identified with an Aegean and apparently palatial power, with friction between Hatti and Ahhiyawa around Millawanda, later Miletus, and Hiyawa-men read as Mycenaean-Anatolian mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that stands the view that the equation is hypothetical and largely a matter of faith, the earliest Greek attestation of Achaeans being Homer, with the disputes running on to linguistic proof, geography, the chronology of fragmentary texts and whether contact was direct or ran through Arzawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is not in doubt is that the texts record conflicts in western Anatolia, and one reading takes those conflicts for the historical basis of Homer's account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ahhiyawa","questions":["Sceptics hold that equating <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the Greek world is hypothetical and largely a matter of faith, since the earliest attestation of Achaeans in Greek is Homer.","The fragmentary letter KUB 26.91, found at Hattusa, was sent by a king of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which makes the correspondence run both ways."],"tags":["place","concept","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jorrit M. Kelder, review of <i>Die Ahhijawa-Frage: Mit einer kommentierten Bibliographie</i>, by Robert Fischer, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.10.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.10.32. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Brill's Companion to Warfare in the Bronze Age Aegean</i>, ed. Lynne A. Kvapil and Kim Shelton, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2025.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.08/. [Open access]","H. Craig Melchert, \"Mycenaean and Hittite Diplomatic Correspondence: Fact and Fiction,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 6 May 2020, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/mycenaean-and-hittite-diplomatic-correspondence-fact-and-fiction/. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"East of the Achaeans: Making Up for a Missed Opportunity while Reading Hittite Texts,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 22 July 2015, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/east-of-the-achaeans-making-up-for-a-missed-opportunity-while-reading-hittite-texts/. [Open access]","Steven Karacic, review of <i>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed</i>, by Eric H. Cline, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2015.08.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/La_Dame_de_Myc%C3%A8nes%2C_fresco.jpg/1920px-La_Dame_de_Myc%C3%A8nes%2C_fresco.jpg","title":"Ahhiyawa","desc":"A fragment of Mycenaean wall painting known as the Lady of Mycenae, a woman shown in profile in a white and red bodice, about 53 cm high.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Dame_de_Myc%C3%A8nes,_fresco.jpg","alt":"La Dame de Mycènes, fresco"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the identification called a matter of faith by its critics?","a":"Because the equation is hypothetical, the earliest Greek attestation of Achaeans being Homer, with disputes running on to linguistic proof, geography and the chronology of fragmentary texts."},{"q":"Why is one letter from the archive unlike the rest?","a":"Because KUB 26.91 was sent by a king of Ahhiyawa rather than received, and messages were conveyed to each kingdom's frontier outposts in its own language and script."},{"q":"Why is the present consensus described as wide but shallow?","a":"Because Ahhiyawa is now almost unanimously identified with an Aegean and apparently palatial power, without agreement on where exactly it lay."}]},
{"id":"gr-096","num":"96","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one sign covers a family of consonants, k standing for g and k and kh, r for both l and r, and q for the labiovelars.","answer":"Mycenaean Greek","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Greek</b> is the language of the Linear B tablets, an early dialect of Greek and the oldest form of the language on record, and the new standard edition gives it a chapter of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading it is not simply reading Greek, because the writing that carries it was never designed for the sounds it had to represent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A single sign stands for a whole family of consonants: k covers g and k and kh, p covers b and p and ph, t covers t and th, r covers both l and r, and q covers the labiovelars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Final stops were probably lost by Mycenaean times and are therefore not noted, which is why honey is written <i>me-ri</i> rather than with the consonant it ends in later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aspiration is not systematically noted either, though a special sign is used for it when the vowel is <i>a</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What follows is ambiguity a reader has to resolve from context, since <i>pa-te</i> can be either father or all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It leaves modern transcriptions disagreeing among themselves as well, one word being rendered <i>doelos</i> by some scholars and <i>dohelos</i> by others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some questions cannot be settled from spelling at all, and the conventions of Mycenaean word formation are firm enough that handling them loosely is a fault reviewers name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the dialect sits among the others is a live question, and one innovation, a preposition governing the dative rather than the genitive, is shared with Arcadian, though whether that is real or an illusion has been doubted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Individual words show the language on the move: <i>i-qo</i>, horse, comes from the Indo-European <i>ekwos</i> and turns up aspirated in later Greek as <i>hippos</i>, perhaps through hypercorrection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Greek","questions":["Because final stops are not written in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, honey appears as <i>me-ri</i>, and <i>pa-te</i> has to be read from context as either father or all.","The word for horse in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is <i>i-qo</i>, from Indo-European <i>ekwos</i>, which turns up aspirated in later Greek as <i>hippos</i>, perhaps by hypercorrection."],"tags":["concept","language","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 3, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 119, no. 3 (2015), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/2170/. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 113, no. 4 (2009), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/639/. [Open access]","José Miguel Jiménez Delgado, review of <i>Introducción al griego micénico: Gramática, selección de textos y glosario</i>, 2nd ed., by Alberto Bernabé and Eugenio R. Luján, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2021.07.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.36/. [Open access]","Anna P. Judson, review of <i>The New Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>, by John Killen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2024.12.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.20/. [Open access]","Michael F. Lane, review of <i>A Companion to Linear B: Mycenaean Greek Texts and Their World</i>, vol. 1, ed. Yves Duhoux and Anna Morpurgo Davies, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.03.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.33. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is reading the language not simply reading Greek?","a":"Because the writing was never designed for the sounds it had to represent: one sign covers a whole family of consonants, so k stands for g, k and kh alike."},{"q":"Why must a reader resolve words from context?","a":"Because the spelling is ambiguous — pa-te can be either father or all — and aspiration and final stops are not systematically noted."},{"q":"Why do modern transcriptions disagree with each other?","a":"Because the script underdetermines the sounds, so one word is rendered doelos by some scholars and dohelos by others."}]},
{"id":"gr-097","num":"97","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"After Knossos fell about 1375 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows in settlements at Trianda, Ayia Irini and Phylakopi that had been Minoan in character until then.","answer":"Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominance from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean</b> is the spread of mainland material culture and influence through the islands and along the Anatolian coast once Crete's dominance had ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the destruction at Knossos about 1375 BCE, mercantile and probably political dominance within the southern Aegean passes from Crete to the kingdoms of the southern Greek mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The change shows in the settlements themselves, since places Minoan in character until the end of Late Minoan IB are by this time Mycenaean, at Trianda on Rhodes, Ayia Irini on Keos, Phylakopi on Melos, and Miletus and Iasos in Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It shows abroad as well, the almost complete absence of Minoan pottery at Amarna being one indication of Mycenaean mercantile dominance within the Aegean by then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Late Helladic IIIA, sites in the Dodecanese, coastal Anatolia, Cyprus, the Levant and the Cyclades were Mycenaeanised to varying degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What that amounts to is carefully limited: Mycenaeans may have lived at some of these places, but none seems to have been a colony or under mainland control, except perhaps Phylakopi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vectors were trade, diplomacy and raiding or warfare, with pottery the most visible export and textiles and perfumed oil beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the seafaring behind it was local rather than imperial, working through maritime small worlds of neighbouring coastscapes held together by regular contact, with Kalamianos serving as Mycenae's own major harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Minoan intermediaries had acted in the earlier exchange networks, and private traders working outside palatial control seem not only possible but probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The last movement is the sharpest, exported pottery falling away in Late Helladic IIIB and rising steeply in IIIC at Emporio, Enkomi, Kition, Ashdod and Tel Miqne-Ekron, while Cyprus took at least two incursions of Mycenaean refugees, the first reaching Enkomi, Kition, Palaeokastro Maa and Sinda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean","questions":["Trade, diplomacy and raiding were the vectors of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though none of the Mycenaeanised sites seems to have been a colony or under mainland control.","Cyprus took at least two incursions of refugees at the end of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first reaching Enkomi, Kition, Palaeokastro Maa and Sinda."],"tags":["concept","event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jennifer Moody, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to the Aegean Bronze Age</i>, ed. Cynthia W. Shelmerdine, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 114, no. 4 (2010), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/717/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Jason W. Earle, review of <i>Maritime Networks in the Mycenaean World</i>, by Thomas F. Tartaron, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.08.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.08.34/. [Open access]","Andrea Vianello, review of <i>Mycenaean Greece, Mediterranean Commerce, and the Formation of Identity</i>, by Bryan E. Burns, <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 2 (2011), https://ajaonline.org/book-review/891/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Mycenaean_World_en.png","title":"Mycenaean expansion in the Aegean","desc":"Map of Mycenaean Greece 1400-1200 BCE: Palaces, main cities and other settlements. User:Alexikoua, User:Panthera tigris tigris.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_World_en.png","alt":"Mycenaean World en"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the change of dominance visible in the settlements?","a":"Because places Minoan in character until the end of Late Minoan IB — Trianda, Ayia Irini, Phylakopi, Miletus, Iasos — are Mycenaean by this time."},{"q":"Why is the word colony withheld from almost all of them?","a":"Because Mycenaeans may have lived at some of these places, but none seems to have been a colony or under mainland control, except perhaps Phylakopi."},{"q":"Why does the Late Helladic IIIC pottery pattern stand out?","a":"Because exports fall away in IIIB and rise steeply in IIIC at Emporio, Enkomi, Kition, Ashdod and Tel Miqne-Ekron, while Cyprus took at least two incursions of Mycenaean refugees."}]},
{"id":"gr-098","num":"98","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Linear A appears at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Late Minoan IA vessels, at least five of which were made on the spot, in a town that was Minoan before it was Greek.","answer":"Mycenaean Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Minoan from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Miletus</b> is the Bronze Age settlement on the Anatolian coast that later Greeks knew as Miletos, and it was Minoan long before it was Mycenaean, the site going back to about 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the New Palace period it looks like a settlement colony, with abundant Minoan domestic pottery, evidence of Minoan cult, and frescoes and walls executed in Minoan technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Linear A appears there on Late Minoan IA vessels, at least five of which were produced at Miletus itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first of those phases ended in a destruction associated with the eruption of the Theran volcano, tephra in the level showing as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town was rebuilt and destroyed again at the end of Late Minoan IB or during Late Minoan II, probably along with the other destructions that mark the end of Minoan power in the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From Late Helladic IIIA the Mycenaeans dominate, and the place is one of those whose Minoan character has by then given way to a mainland one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The evidence for it is an overwhelming proportion of Mycenaean pottery and ritual figurines, with a Corridor House and Mycenaean burial rites in the phase that follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavator identifies the town in this period with Millawanda, known from Hittite sources, and a careful weighing of those sources concludes that the evidence is sufficient to support the link.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That matters because Millawanda is where the friction between Hatti and Ahhiyawa is placed, and one of the Ahhiyawa documents is the Milawata letter itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What became of the people is a separate question, and one reading has the Mycenaean population staying where it was with only the aristocracy changing, since Mycenaean remains continue in sufficient quantity from the Mycenaean into the Hittite period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean Miletus","questions":["The excavator identifies <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Millawanda of the Hittite records, and a careful review of the sources finds the evidence sufficient to support the link.","One reading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has the population staying put with only the aristocracy changing, since Mycenaean remains continue in quantity into the Hittite period."],"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>The Greeks in the East</i>, ed. Alexandra Villing, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.07.65, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.07.65/. [Open access]","P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Jorrit M. Kelder, review of <i>Die Ahhijawa-Frage: Mit einer kommentierten Bibliographie</i>, by Robert Fischer, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.10.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.10.32. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Brill's Companion to Warfare in the Bronze Age Aegean</i>, ed. Lynne A. Kvapil and Kim Shelton, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2025.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.08/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Miletus","at":[27.2783,37.5303]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the earlier settlement read as a Minoan colony?","a":"Because it has abundant Minoan domestic pottery, evidence of Minoan cult, frescoes and walls in Minoan technique, and Linear A on vessels at least five of which were made there."},{"q":"Why does the identification with Millawanda matter?","a":"Because Millawanda is where the friction between Hatti and Ahhiyawa is placed, and one of the Ahhiyawa documents is the Milawata letter itself."},{"q":"Why has continuity of population been proposed?","a":"Because Mycenaean remains continue in sufficient quantity from the Mycenaean into the Hittite period, so only the aristocracy need have changed."}]},
{"id":"gr-099","num":"99","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Mycenaean engineers led two rivers into one course running east to the natural sinkholes, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned a shallow Boeotian lake into farmland until the water came back.","answer":"drainage of Lake Copais","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reflooded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>drainage of Lake Copais</b> was the Mycenaean scheme that emptied a shallow seasonal lake in Boeotia by leading the two rivers that fed it to the sinkholes at the basin's north-eastern corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The basin has no outlet to the sea, so the Boeotian Kephisos pooled there, and Strabo could still write that the lake's outlets were nowhere to be seen except the fissure that admitted the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That fissure was one of the katavothres, crevices worn in the limestone through which the water sank and came up again on the Euboean Gulf, and the height of them set the level the lake stood at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The engineers turned the greater part of the Kephisos out of its own bed into that of the Melas a few kilometres from Orchomenos, so that the two ran east together towards those sinkholes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A levee some 22 kilometres (14 miles) long then held the combined flow off the reclaimed ground, faced along its outer sides with retaining walls of Cyclopean masonry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those walls were set in a foundation trench packed with clay and small stones, built up in rough courses of boulders, and backed by a core of yellowish lake clay chosen for its plasticity and its waterproofing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stand today to 2.3 metres (7 feet 6 inches), and about 6 kilometres (4 miles) east of Orchomenos the levee and its two walls together measure 30 metres (98 feet) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the sinkholes silted up and needed clearing, a tunnel was begun beside them at Kephalari, planned to run over 2.2 kilometres (1.4 miles) with 16 shafts sunk to keep it on line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only about a fifth of it was ever cut, the bore stops dead against solid rock, and some hold the work Hellenistic rather than Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery from the levee places its building in Late Helladic IIIB, the water came back near the end of the Mycenaean period once the sinkholes were plugged, and Pausanias still found a lake over most of the Orchomenian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"drainage of Lake Copais","questions":["At Kephalari a tunnel meant to carry water 2.2 kilometres (1.4 miles) to the sea was part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but only a fifth was cut before it stopped against solid rock.","Strabo has Crates of Chalcis clearing the choked channels to renew the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when party strife among the Boeotians stopped him, though many places, he wrote, had already been drained."],"tags":["building","event","technology","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","E. Kountouri et al., \"The Mycenaean Drainage Works of North Kopais, Greece: A New Project Incorporating Surface Surveys, Geophysical Research and Excavation,\" <i>Water Science and Technology: Water Supply</i> 13, no. 3 (2013): 710–18, preprint, https://www.itia.ntua.gr/en/getfile/1360/2/documents/2013WST_WS_MycenaeanDrainage_pp1.pdf. [Open access]","N. Mamassis, S. Moustakas, and N. Zarkadoulas, \"Representing the Operation of Ancient Reclamation Works at Lake Copais in Greece,\" <i>Water History</i> (2015), preprint, https://www.itia.ntua.gr/en/getfile/1539/1/documents/Copais_WH10_9_14.pdf. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.2.18–19, trans. H. L. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D2%3Asection%3D18. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 9.38.6–7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the lake exist at all?","a":"Because the basin has no outlet to the sea, so the Boeotian Kephisos pooled there, its level set by the height of the sinkholes through which water escaped."},{"q":"Why was a tunnel begun beside the sinkholes?","a":"Because they silted up and needed clearing, so a bore of over 2.2 kilometres with 16 shafts was planned — of which only about a fifth was ever cut."},{"q":"Why did the water come back?","a":"Because near the end of the Mycenaean period the sinkholes were plugged, and Pausanias still found a lake over most of the Orchomenian land."}]},
{"id":"gr-100","num":"100","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Bridges of Cyclopean boulders on the line from Tiryns to Epidauros are the best-preserved pieces of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and two of them still carry farm traffic today.","answer":"Mycenaean road network","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1190 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mycenaean road network</b> is the system of built roads that joined the citadels of the Argive plain and ran on beyond it, carried over broken ground on terraces and crossing ravines on bridges of Cyclopean boulders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its best-known stretch runs from Tiryns towards Epidauros, where four such structures survive near Arkadiko, two of them still crossed today for local farm work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are built of unwrought limestone blocks laid without mortar, each opening roofed by courses stepped inward until they meet, a corbel rather than a true arch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The floor of an opening is a laid slab that steadied its walls and kept the stream from washing them out, which argues for real hydraulic experience in the builders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An abutment near Mycenae stands 4.7 metres (15 feet) above a dry bed some 40 metres (130 feet) wide, so that crossing probably took several spans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The same abutment measures 5 metres (16 feet) across, leaving a usable roadway of about 3.8 metres (12 feet) once side parapets are allowed for, enough for two vehicles to pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smaller streams were taken under the road instead of over it, through stone channels roofed in the same stepped fashion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Boeotia may show the engineering in another form, since the retaining walls of the Copais levee created an artificial plateau along which a road could have run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pausanias, going from Argos past the ruins of Tiryns towards Epidauros, still called that route the highway, and he described the Tirynthian walls as the work of the Cyclopes, stones so large a pair of mules could not stir the smallest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nothing so deliberately built appears in Greece again for centuries, though a later network of cart roads across the Peloponnese, known from wheel ruts cut in bedrock to a gauge of about 1.4 metres (4 feet 7 inches), has been placed by some in the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mycenaean road network","questions":["An abutment near Mycenae stands 4.7 metres (15 feet) above a dry bed 40 metres (130 feet) wide, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> probably included a bridge of several spans.","Pausanias still called the way from Argos past Tiryns to Epidauros the highway, roughly the line taken by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> more than a thousand years before him."],"tags":["building","object","technology","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Sławomir Karaś and Nien-Tsu Tuan, \"The World's Oldest Bridges: Mycenaean Bridges,\" <i>American Journal of Civil Engineering and Architecture</i> 5, no. 6 (2017): 237–44, https://doi.org/10.12691/ajcea-5-6-3. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.25.7–9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","D. Graham J. Shipley, review of <i>Verkehrswege der Argolis: Rekonstruktion und historische Bedeutung</i>, by Klaus Tausend, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.03.40. [Open access]","E. Kountouri et al., \"The Mycenaean Drainage Works of North Kopais, Greece: A New Project Incorporating Surface Surveys, Geophysical Research and Excavation,\" <i>Water Science and Technology: Water Supply</i> 13, no. 3 (2013): 710–18, preprint, https://www.itia.ntua.gr/en/getfile/1360/2/documents/2013WST_WS_MycenaeanDrainage_pp1.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Arkadiko_bridge%2C_mycenaean%2C_Late_Heladic_age%2C_202418.jpg/1920px-Arkadiko_bridge%2C_mycenaean%2C_Late_Heladic_age%2C_202418.jpg","title":"Mycenaean road network","desc":"Arkadiko (or Kazarma) bridge, Argolis, Peloponnesos, Greece. Mycenaean (Achaean) construction, Late Bronze age, Late Helladic III (probably III B), 1340/1300 to 1190/1100 BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arkadiko_bridge,_mycenaean,_Late_Heladic_age,_202418.jpg","alt":"Arkadiko bridge, mycenaean, Late Heladic age"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does a bridge's laid floor slab argue for hydraulic experience?","a":"Because it steadied the opening's walls and kept the stream from washing them out, which is a builder answering a problem water makes."},{"q":"Why is a roadway of about 3.8 metres significant?","a":"Because once side parapets are allowed for it is enough for two vehicles to pass."},{"q":"Why does nothing comparable appear again for centuries?","a":"Because carefully constructed roads are not a feature of later Greek civilisation until much later, though a network of cart roads known from wheel ruts has been placed by some in the archaic period."}]},
{"id":"gr-101","num":"101","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Four small towers stand eight or nine metres (26 to 31 feet) apart on the northern face of the best-preserved stretch of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the side from which its builders expected attack.","answer":"Isthmian wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Isthmian wall</b> is a fortification of Cyclopean masonry laid across the Isthmus of Corinth, the only land approach to the Peloponnese, late in the Mycenaean palace period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its line can be followed about two kilometres (1.2 miles) inland from the Saronic Gulf to a deep gully; beyond that nothing certain survives, and it has never been traced to the Corinthian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is two faces of large unworked stones packed out with smaller ones around a rubble core, 3.6 to 4 metres (12 to 13 feet) thick and standing in one place nearly 2.5 metres (8 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The longest unbroken run measures 45.5 metres (149 feet) and carries four small towers projecting about 0.7 metres (2 feet 4 inches) from the face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every tower is on the north side, and that face is the higher and better built, so its builders expected attack from the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its date rests on the masonry, which is that of the Mycenaean citadels, and on some seventy Mycenaean sherds, a stirrup jar and a pitcher among them dating to the turn from Late Helladic IIIB to IIIC, about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the generation in which Mycenae, Tiryns and Athens finished their own circuits and drove passages to water outside them, the first clear sign of a fear of siege in the Bronze Age Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No settlement in the Corinthia was large enough to have paid for such a barrier, so Oscar Broneer, who cleared it, took it for a joint work of the Argolid kingdoms, a first line of defence before their citadels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence is thinner than that argument needs: only one stretch yielded Mycenaean pottery wedged among its own stones, and a study of Cyclopean building holds the stretches differ too much to be one structure, dating the finest after the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Broneer himself allowed good reason to doubt it was ever finished, on a line others tried again: a wall against the Persians in 480 BCE, and at last the Hexamilion, barring the way south for a thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Isthmian wall","questions":["The only land route into the Peloponnese was fortified late in the Bronze Age by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a barrier of unworked boulders traced for about two kilometres (1.2 miles).","Because no Corinthian settlement was large enough to have raised it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as evidence that the kingdoms of the Argolid could still act together around 1200 BCE."],"tags":["building","warfare","technology","archaeology","history","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Oscar Broneer, \"The Cyclopean Wall on the Isthmus of Corinth and Its Bearing on Late Bronze Age Chronology,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 35, no. 4 (1966): 346–62, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147564.pdf. [Open access]","Oscar Broneer, \"The Cyclopean Wall on the Isthmus of Corinth, Addendum,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 37, no. 1 (1968): 25–35, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147536.pdf. [Open access]","Nancy Claire Loader, \"The Definition of Cyclopean: An Investigation into the Origins of the LH III Fortifications on Mainland Greece\" (PhD diss., Durham University, 1995), 166–67, https://etheses.durham.ac.uk/id/eprint/5374/. [Open access]","William R. Caraher and Timothy E. Gregory, \"Fortifications of Mount Oneion, Corinthia,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 75, no. 3 (2006): 327–56, https://doi.org/10.2972/hesp.75.3.327. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 21: Mycenaean Public and Funerary Architecture: Fortifications, Drainage Projects, Roads, and Chamber Tombs,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-21-narrative/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.71, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Korinth_Isthmus_de.png","title":"Isthmian wall","desc":"A map of the Isthmus of Corinth, the neck of land joining the Peloponnese to central Greece, with the Gulf of Corinth to the north and the Saronic Gulf to the south.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korinth_Isthmus_de.png","alt":"Korinth Isthmus de"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the wall read as facing an attack from the mainland?","a":"Because every tower is on the north side, and that face is the higher and better built."},{"q":"Why did Broneer take it for a joint work of the Argolid kingdoms?","a":"Because no settlement in the Corinthia was large enough to have paid for such a barrier, so he read it as a first line of defence before their citadels."},{"q":"Why is the evidence thinner than that argument needs?","a":"Because only one stretch yielded Mycenaean pottery wedged among its own stones, and a study of Cyclopean building holds the stretches differ too much to be one structure."}]},
{"id":"gr-102","num":"102","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Nine cities stand one on top of another on the mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from a settlement of about 3000 BCE up to the Roman town on the summit.","answer":"Troy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE – 500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy VI from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Troy</b> is the city on the mound of Hisarlık in the north-west corner of Anatolia, called Ilios in Homer and Ilion by the Greeks and Romans after them, and lived on for some 4,500 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was destroyed and rebuilt so often that the site is a stack of cities rather than one, each levelling the wreckage of the last; excavators count nine, Troy I at the bottom and Roman Troy IX on the summit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first was a village of about 3000 BCE, the second a small walled citadel that burned, the Burnt City that Schliemann took for Homer's Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three poorer settlements followed, and the hill seems to have stood empty for about 110 years after the third, the fourth city beginning around 2060 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From about 1750 BCE the sixth city rose over them, its sloping walls of great stones the most conspicuous thing on the mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What that city amounted to is still argued: one side reads the evidence as the capital of a significant regional power in western Anatolia, the other holds it cannot even be proven to have been a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mound had probably been identified as the Troy of legend by the beginning of the Archaic period, and that identity proved worth more than the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the 7th century BCE the Lokrians sent two aristocratic daughters a year to serve in the sanctuary of Athena Ilias, in atonement for Ajax's rape of Kassandra, and the townspeople might kill them if they were caught outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Athens planted its first colony in the eastern Aegean at Sigeion nearby, beside the mounds it took for the graves of Achilles, Patroklos and Ajax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Xerxes climbed the citadel of Priam on his march into Greece and sacrificed a thousand cattle to Athena of Ilion, and it was the Greek and Roman town that finished the Bronze Age city off, the terrace cut for Athena's temple destroying the middle of the old citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Troy","questions":["The Lokrians sent two aristocratic daughters a year to serve Athena at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in atonement for Ajax's rape of Kassandra at the fall of the city.","Xerxes turned aside on his march into Greece to climb the citadel of Priam at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and sacrifice a thousand cattle to Athena of Ilion."],"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","greece","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Wilhelm Dörpfeld, <i>Troja und Ilion: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen in den vorhistorischen und historischen Schichten von Ilion, 1870–1894</i> (Athens: Beck &amp; Barth, 1902), 26–35, https://archive.org/details/trojaundilionerg00drpf. [Open access]","Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Donald Easton and Bernhard Weninger, \"Troy III–V: New Radiocarbon Dates Confirm a Gap in Blegen's Sequence,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 50 (2023): 396–419, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.50.14. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","C. Brian Rose, \"Separating Fact from Fiction in the Aiolian Migration,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 77, no. 3 (2008): 399–430, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/40205756.pdf. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 7.43, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Legendary_walls_of_Troy_%288708672267%29.jpg/1920px-Legendary_walls_of_Troy_%288708672267%29.jpg","title":"Troy","desc":"The legendary walls of Troy.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Legendary_walls_of_Troy_(8708672267).jpg","alt":"The stone city walls at the site of Troy, with visitors on the path beside them"},"difficulty":1,"locator":{"name":"Troy","at":[26.2389,39.9575]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the site a stack of cities rather than one?","a":"Because it was destroyed and rebuilt so often, each settlement levelling the wreckage of the last, and excavators count nine from Troy I to Roman Troy IX."},{"q":"Why did the site's identity outlast the city?","a":"Because it had probably been identified as the Troy of legend by the Archaic period, after which the Lokrians sent daughters to Athena Ilias and Xerxes sacrificed a thousand cattle there."},{"q":"Why did the classical town damage the Bronze Age one?","a":"Because the terrace cut for Athena's temple destroyed the middle of the old citadel."}]},
{"id":"gr-103","num":"103","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The houses crowded inside the patched walls of the old citadel, each with storage jars sunk below its floor, belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which then burned.","answer":"Troy VIIa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy VI</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy VIIa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1180 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Troy VIIa</b> is the settlement raised in the ruins of Troy VI at Hisarlık in north-west Anatolia, and the layer most often put forward as the city Homer's poems besiege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troy VI, standing from about 1750 BCE, was the grandest thing built on the mound: a horseshoe circuit of dressed limestone over 4 metres (13 feet) thick and 9 metres (30 feet) high in places, carried higher in mudbrick, with five gates and projecting towers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its foundations were bedded on a cushion of earth rather than rock, perhaps against earthquakes, and if so it did not work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end of phase VIh the walls came down in what most authorities call an earthquake, collapsed stonework everywhere and no convincing sign of fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reading moved Homer's city one layer up, and some take the wooden horse of the story for a memory of the earthquake, the horse being the animal of Poseidon, who shakes the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The survivors rebuilt inside the patched circuit far more tightly: sturdy houses of one to three rooms sharing party walls, set in places against the inner face of the wall, which Troy VI kept clear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their floors are honeycombed with pits holding storage jars capped by stone slabs, often read as stocking up for a siege, though these houses stood one storey high and had nowhere else to put the stores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Troy VIIa then burned in a general fire that took the buildings inside the citadel and out, leaving human bones unburied in the wreckage, so its end is credited to violence rather than the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The date is argued: Blegen first said about 1240 BCE and later raised it to 1270, others bring it down to 1200 or 1190 on the latest Mycenaean pottery, and the present excavators end it about 1175.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor does everyone accept the promotion: to its critics Troy VIIa is a poor village in the ruins of a great city, and the Greeks put the war anywhere between 1334 and 1184 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Troy VIIa","questions":["An earthquake rather than an army is argued to have brought Troy VI down, and the city Homer sang of was then <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the crowded settlement raised in its ruins.","Human bones lay unburied in the fire-scarred ruins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why its end, unlike the fall of the city beneath it, is credited to violence."],"tags":["place","city","era","warfare","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 23: Troy VI,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-23-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","George E. Mylonas, \"Priam's Troy and the Date of Its Fall,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 33, no. 4 (1964): 352–80, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147283.pdf. [Open access]","Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Donald Easton and Bernhard Weninger, \"Troy III–V: New Radiocarbon Dates Confirm a Gap in Blegen's Sequence,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 50 (2023): 396–419, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.50.14. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Mycenaean_Troy_p034-Plan_II.jpg","title":"Troy VIIa","desc":"Mycenaean Troy, Plan II - Citadel of II Stratum.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_Troy_p034-Plan_II.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean Troy p034-Plan II"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Troy","at":[26.2389,39.9575]},"why":[{"q":"Why was Homer's city moved one layer up?","a":"Because Troy VI's walls came down in what most authorities call an earthquake, with collapsed stonework everywhere and no convincing sign of fire."},{"q":"Why is the end of Troy VIIa credited to violence?","a":"Because it burned in a general fire that took buildings inside the citadel and out, leaving human bones unburied in the wreckage."},{"q":"Why are the storage pits not proof of a siege?","a":"Because these houses stood one storey high and had nowhere else to put the stores, so the jars need mean no more than ordinary storage."}]},
{"id":"gr-104","num":"104","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A rock-cut ditch traced for 600 metres (about 660 yards) south of the citadel is the best evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its size is still fought over.","answer":"lower city of Troy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inner ditch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silted up by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1175 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>lower city of Troy</b> is the settlement outside the citadel walls at Hisarlık, and the argument over its size is the argument over whether Bronze Age Troy was a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The citadel is small, 2 to 3 hectares (5 to 7 acres), climbing in concentric terraces whose buildings are set along radii of the circle rather than parallel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Large free-standing houses stand on the lowest and best preserved terrace, but no palace survives: the crown of the mound was shaved away in Hellenistic and Roman times below the Bronze Age levels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blegen took a town outside the walls for granted, writing that an extramural lower town of undetermined size certainly existed, and Troy VIIa's fire took buildings beyond the citadel as well as within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nobody tested it until magnetic survey and trial trenches crossed the plateau to the south from 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> About 400 metres (440 yards) out they found a ditch cut into the rock, 4 metres (13 feet) wide and 1 to 2 metres (3 to 7 feet) deep, traced for over 600 metres (660 yards) without reaching an end, with a second ditch further out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line breaks in places, and at one break the rock is cut for the posts of a wooden palisade behind a causeway, which is a gate, hard to explain otherwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pottery and radiocarbon put the inner ditch in the 15th century BCE, silting up towards the end of Troy VI and through Troy VIIa, and a cemetery lies between the two, so the town had spread over its own graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the excavators' reading that gives a defended lower city of 25 to 35 hectares (62 to 86 acres) and 5,000 to 6,000 people, ten times the citadel and the size of Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their chief critic reads the same ground as some 11 hectares (27 acres) and at most 2,000 people, takes the ditches for quarries or water channels, and notes that Mycenae's acropolis is nearly twice the size of Troy's — to which the answer is that water runs neither uphill nor over the gaps in a ditch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"lower city of Troy","questions":["Magnetic survey and trial trenches from 1992 found the two ditches and the palisade gate that are now the case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, long assumed but never tested.","Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covered 35 hectares (86 acres) and held 6,000 people, or 11 hectares (27 acres) and held 2,000, decides whether the place deserves to be called a city."],"tags":["place","city","building","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 23: Troy VI,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-23-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 27: Troy VII and the Historicity of the Trojan War,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-27-narrative/. [Open access]","Wilhelm Dörpfeld, <i>Troja und Ilion: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen in den vorhistorischen und historischen Schichten von Ilion, 1870–1894</i> (Athens: Beck &amp; Barth, 1902), 33, 107–82, https://archive.org/details/trojaundilionerg00drpf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Troy","at":[26.2389,39.9575]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the size of the lower town decide what Troy was?","a":"Because the citadel is only 2 to 3 hectares, so whether the place was a city at all turns on how much settlement lay outside its walls."},{"q":"Why is one break in the ditch read as a gate?","a":"Because the rock there is cut for the posts of a wooden palisade behind a causeway, which is hard to explain otherwise."},{"q":"Why is the quarry-or-channel reading answered as it is?","a":"Because water runs neither uphill nor over the gaps in a ditch."}]},
{"id":"gr-105","num":"105","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Because the current runs out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the wind blows down it from the north-east, a sailing ship bound for the Black Sea had to wait at the mouth.","answer":"Dardanelles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Beşik Bay graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1360 – 1320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dardanelles</b> is the narrow strait between the Aegean and the Sea of Marmara, the only sea route from the Mediterranean to the Black Sea, and Troy stands at its mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Troy's site is a crossroads: a shallow bay beside it, its hinterland's largest river reaching the sea there, and beyond that the water dividing Europe from Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs the wrong way for anyone going north, its outflow of Black Sea water exceeding every river of the northern Aegean put together, and the winds prevail from the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A sailing ship therefore cannot simply go in; it waits at the mouth for a day that allows it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beşik Bay, 8 kilometres (5 miles) south-west of Troy, is the first natural harbour below the entrance and the obvious place to wait, with a cemetery of the late 14th century BCE beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Manfred Korfmann built a large claim on that delay: crews held up for weeks or months had to be fed, and Troy grew rich on them as a hub of trade between the Aegean, Anatolia, Thrace and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His colleagues put it more carefully, calling Troy a gateway settlement at the edge of the Bronze Age urban world, where two economies met under a local power's protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what they cite lies at the far end of the route: 150 stone anchors of Aegean and Near Eastern type off the Bulgarian coast, oxhide ingots from Bulgaria and the Marmara shores, and a ceremonial axe of Pontic origin aboard the Uluburun ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their chief critic answers that nothing on the mound points to the Black Sea, that no Bronze Age stone anchor has turned up at Beşik Bay or at Troy, and that no harbour settlement has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He would as soon send goods overland across the Thracian peninsula, which beats waiting months for a wind, and a year later still wrote that Mycenaean pottery is absent from the Black Sea and Black Sea imports from Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dardanelles","questions":["Beşik Bay is the first natural harbour below the entrance to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why Troy's excavators made it the anchorage where ships waited for a favourable wind.","Troy was said to have grown rich on crews delayed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though its chief critic replies that nothing dug up on the mound points to the Black Sea at all."],"tags":["place","geography","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 23: Troy VI,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-23-narrative/. [Open access]","Vassiliki H. Kourafalou and Kostas Tsiaras, \"A Nested Circulation Model for the North Aegean Sea,\" <i>Ocean Science</i> 3, no. 1 (2007): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.5194/os-3-1-2007. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to P. Jablonka and C. B. Rose,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 109, no. 1 (2005), https://ajaonline.org/wp-content/uploads/2011/08/1091_Kolb.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Graphic_map_of_the_Dardanelles.JPG/1920px-Graphic_map_of_the_Dardanelles.JPG","title":"Dardanelles","desc":"Map of the Dardanelles drawn by G.F. Morrell, 1915. The map shows the Gallipoli peninsula and west coast of Turkey, and the location of front line troops and landings.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graphic_map_of_the_Dardanelles.JPG","alt":"Graphic map of the Dardanelles"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"locator":{"name":"Dardanelles","at":[26.4,40.2]},"why":[{"q":"Why can a sailing ship not simply enter the strait?","a":"Because its outflow of Black Sea water exceeds every river of the northern Aegean put together and the winds prevail from the north-east, so a ship waits at the mouth for a day that allows it."},{"q":"Why did Korfmann build a claim about Troy's wealth on that delay?","a":"Because crews held up for weeks or months had to be fed, which would make the city a hub of trade between the Aegean, Anatolia, Thrace and the Black Sea."},{"q":"Why does his chief critic reject it?","a":"Because nothing on the mound points to the Black Sea, no Bronze Age stone anchor has turned up at Beşik Bay or Troy, no harbour settlement has been found, and Mycenaean pottery is absent from the Black Sea."}]},
{"id":"gr-106","num":"106","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Hittite treaty of the 13th century BCE binds a ruler named Alaksandu, whose land <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> occupied the Troad, to the great king at Hattusa.","answer":"Wilusa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Hittite texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alaksandu treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Wilusa</b> is a land named in the Hittite archives at Hattusa, one of the four vassal states into which the kingdom of Arzawa in western Anatolia was divided, and the one that held the Troad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its neighbours were Mira, which covered Ionia, the Seha River Land running from Adramyttion to Smyrna and taking in Lesbos, and Hapalla inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hittites counted the people of Arzawa as allies but not as equals, and the whole coast was fought over between Hatti and the kingdom their scribes called Ahhiyawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wilusa sits at the centre of that quarrel: a letter from the Hittite king to the king of Ahhiyawa implies that the two had at last settled a dispute over who held it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fullest document is a treaty of the 13th century BCE between Muwattalli II and Alaksandu, ruler of Wilusa, in which the Hittite king takes for granted that Alaksandu's income comes from vineyards, threshing floors, fields and stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What makes Wilusa matter to Greek history is a pair of names: Wilusa answers to Wilios, the older form of Ilios, and Alaksandu to Alexandros, the other name of the Trojan prince Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most scholars now take Wilusa for the state whose capital stood at Hisarlık, which would make Bronze Age Troy a real kingdom with a named king corresponding with an empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Hittite evidence puts that land firmly in north-western Anatolia, which is the standing answer to attempts to move Homer's Troy to Cilicia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Against it stands the fact that Wilusa is never named outside an Anatolian context, and that nothing written has been dug up at Troy: since Hittite kings exchanged letters with Wilusa's rulers an archive must have existed there, and finding it is the one thing that would settle the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The names are meanwhile a pattern rather than a proof, since history is turned into myth as readily as myth is turned into history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Wilusa","questions":["The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answers to Wilios, the older form of Ilios, and its ruler Alaksandu to Alexandros, which is the other name Homer gives the Trojan prince Paris.","Hatti and Ahhiyawa quarrelled over who held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a letter between their kings implies that the dispute was settled in the end."],"tags":["place","state","history","archaeology","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["C. Brian Rose, \"Separating Fact from Fiction in the Aiolian Migration,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 77, no. 3 (2008): 399–430, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/40205756.pdf. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"East of the Achaeans: Making Up for a Missed Opportunity while Reading Hittite Texts,\" <i>Classical Inquiries</i>, Center for Hellenic Studies, Harvard University, 22 July 2015, https://classical-inquiries.chs.harvard.edu/east-of-the-achaeans-making-up-for-a-missed-opportunity-while-reading-hittite-texts/. [Open access]","Peter Jablonka and C. Brian Rose, \"Late Bronze Age Troy: A Response to Frank Kolb,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 615–30, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.615. [Open access]","Frank Kolb, \"Troy VI: A Trading Center and Commercial City?\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 108, no. 4 (2004): 577–613, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.108.4.577. [Open access]","Catalin Pavel, review of <i>Lag Troia in Kilikien? Der aktuelle Streit um Homers Ilias</i>, ed. Christoph Ulf and Robert Rollinger, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.10.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.10.03/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Mycenaean_Troy_p040-Plan_III.jpg","title":"Wilusa","desc":"Mycenaean Troy, Plan III - Citadel of Mycenaean Troy.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mycenaean_Troy_p040-Plan_III.jpg","alt":"Mycenaean Troy p040-Plan III"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does this land matter to Greek history?","a":"Because Wilusa answers to Wilios, the older form of Ilios, and its ruler Alaksandu to Alexandros, the other name of the Trojan prince Paris."},{"q":"Why would finding an archive at Troy settle the question?","a":"Because Hittite kings exchanged letters with Wilusa's rulers, so one must have existed there — and nothing written has been dug up at the site."},{"q":"Why are the matching names a pattern rather than a proof?","a":"Because history is turned into myth as readily as myth is turned into history, and Wilusa is never named outside an Anatolian context."}]},
{"id":"gr-107","num":"107","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Destructions spread over 150 years and 6 million square kilometres (2.3 million square miles) are what historians gather under the name the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though no single cause explains them.","answer":"Late Bronze Age collapse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destructions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1150 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Late Bronze Age collapse</b> is the run of destructions, abandonments and political failures that ended the palace states of the eastern Mediterranean between the middle of the 13th century BCE and the end of the 12th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not one event: the changes fall at different times over some 6 million square kilometres (2.3 million square miles), from Greece in the west to the borders of Mesopotamia, and from Anatolia down to Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Greece the palaces at Mycenae, Tiryns, Midea, Thebes and Pylos burned late in Late Helladic IIIB or early in IIIC, roughly between 1200 and 1150 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the east Ugarit ended altogether, its last cuneiform letters reporting enemy ships off the coast and begging neighbours for help, and Hittite Anatolia was among the regions worst hit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whatever else happened, international trade fell away deeply, and it would be folly to pretend that did not matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The explanations offered are legion: migration, attack from outside, political struggle within the palaces or among them, the gap between centres and peripheries, climate change, earthquakes and plague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strongest scientific case is the climatic one, since pollen and isotopes from Cyprus and Syria record an abrupt dry episode, and one study reads famine, then seaborne invasion, then political and economic collapse in sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that, a proposed earthquake storm of 1225 to 1175 BCE is unsupported by the study of ancient faults, and the dates themselves are unsettled, radiocarbon from northern Greece running 70 to 100 years earlier than the conventional chronology of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Societies survive droughts, so the reading now favoured makes climate a catalyst rather than a trigger, with collapse coming out of several stresses cascading together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself is doing too much work: Cyprus, several Levantine towns and parts of Crete survived or flourished, so what happened is better called a decline with clear cultural continuity in many places, and the popular framing of a single year is a place to start an argument, not a date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Late Bronze Age collapse","questions":["Because societies survive droughts, the dry episode recorded in Cyprus and Syria is now read as a catalyst of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than its trigger.","Cyprus, several Levantine towns and parts of Crete came through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and even flourished, which is why the word itself is thought to claim too much."],"tags":["event","era","history","archaeology","climate","greece","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Steven Karacic, review of <i>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed</i>, by Eric H. Cline, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.08.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.37/. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg/1920px-Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg","title":"Late Bronze Age collapse","desc":"Resignature of the \"war of sea peoples\" on the northeast wall of the temple of the dead Ramses III in Medinet Habu, Egypt.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg","alt":"Medinet Habu Ramses III. Tempel Nordostwand Abzeichnung 01"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the collapse not one event?","a":"Because the changes fall at different times over some 6 million square kilometres, from Greece to the borders of Mesopotamia and from Anatolia down to Egypt."},{"q":"Why is climate now read as a catalyst rather than a trigger?","a":"Because societies survive droughts, so the favoured reading has collapse coming out of several stresses cascading together."},{"q":"Why is the word collapse doing too much work?","a":"Because Cyprus, several Levantine towns and parts of Crete survived or flourished, so what happened is better called a decline with clear cultural continuity in many places."}]},
{"id":"gr-108","num":"108","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Ramesses III's temple at Medinet Habu names five of the groups gathered under the modern label the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and shows Egypt beating them on land and at sea.","answer":"Sea Peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramesses III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">year 8 of his reign</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sea Peoples</b> is the modern label for a set of groups named in Egyptian, Hittite and Ugaritic records between the 14th and the 11th centuries BCE, long treated as the destroyers of the Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fullest account is Ramesses III's inscription at Medinet Habu, which names the Peleset, Tjeker, Shekelesh, Denyen and Weshesh and says they laid waste Hatti, Arzawa, Qode, Alashiya and Carchemish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its text has northerners in their isles disturbed and swept away at once, nobody standing before them, and a camp set up in Amurru; the temple walls then show Egypt beating them on land and at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other names turn up elsewhere and earlier: the Sherden, Ekwesh, Shekelesh, Teresh and Lukka in Merneptah's fifth year, the Lukka and Sherden already in the Amarna letters, and the Shikila who live on ships in a letter from Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The names are spread over four centuries and several archives, not confined to one invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Egyptian texts are propaganda before they are chronicles, and Egyptologists have questioned the date and the historicity of the Medinet Habu account alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One study of how foreigners are shown in those reliefs concludes that their purpose was to record the king's own actions rather than an invasion, and doubts that 150 years of scholarship built on them can establish one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor does anyone know how far the movements reached or whether these peoples ever acted as one force, their proposed origins running from Sardinia to the Balkans, Anatolia and the steppe beyond Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The confident equations of the older books, making the Peleset Cretans and forerunners of the Philistines, the Tjeker Sicilians and the Denyen the Danaans of Greek epic, were read off the shapes of the names, and such groups are still seldom identified rigorously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is a real pattern of seaborne violence and displaced people, now read as one strand of a wider crisis rather than its cause, and tied by some to famine after an abrupt drought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Sea Peoples","questions":["The Sherden and Lukka appear in the Amarna letters and the Peleset in Ramesses III's eighth year, so the names collected as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are spread over four centuries.","Because their proposed origins run from Sardinia to the steppe beyond Mesopotamia, nobody can say whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever acted together as a single force."],"tags":["people","event","warfare","history","archaeology","migration","bronze age"],"sources":["A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Twentieth to the Twenty-sixth Dynasties</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), §§59–64, https://archive.org/details/ancient-records-of-egypt-vol-4_202012. [Open access]","The Epigraphic Survey, <i>Medinet Habu</i>, vol. 1, <i>Earlier Historical Records of Ramses III</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 8 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1930), https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip8.pdf. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Steven Karacic, review of <i>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed</i>, by Eric H. Cline, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.08.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Champollion%27s_description_of_the_peoples_named_on_the_Second_Pylon_at_Medinet_Habu.png","title":"Sea Peoples","desc":"Champollion's description of the peoples named on the Second Pylon at Medinet Habu. w:Jean-François Champollion. 1832.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Champollion%27s_description_of_the_peoples_named_on_the_Second_Pylon_at_Medinet_Habu.png","alt":"Champollion's description of the peoples named on the Second Pylon at Medinet Habu"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why must the Egyptian account be handled carefully?","a":"Because its texts are propaganda before they are chronicles, and Egyptologists have questioned the date and the historicity of the Medinet Habu account alike."},{"q":"Why does the label cover more than one invasion?","a":"Because the names are spread over four centuries and several archives — the Lukka and Sherden already in the Amarna letters, the Shikila in a letter from Ugarit."},{"q":"Why were the confident older equations unsound?","a":"Because making the Peleset Cretans, the Tjeker Sicilians and the Denyen the Danaans was read off the shapes of the names, and such groups are still seldom identified rigorously."}]},
{"id":"gr-109","num":"109","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Burnt cattle bones from a feast held shortly before it lay in the Archives Complex when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended the Palace of Nestor for good.","answer":"destruction of Pylos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palace burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1180 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruins reused</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to the 10th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>destruction of Pylos</b> is the fire that ended the Palace of Nestor in Messenia, in the south-west of the Peloponnese, and the palace was never rebuilt after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pottery from the destruction levels dates it closely to the turn from Late Helladic IIIB to IIIC, and a stalagmite from a cave nearby puts it at roughly 3,150 to 3,130 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The building burned hot enough to leave hard red mudbrick and a black char across its floors, and its upper storey came down on top of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a corner of room 7 of the Archives Complex lay the burnt bones of at least ten cattle, the remains of one sacrifice and feast held not long before the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The kingdom's own records show its bronze-workers short of copper, and lists of men posted as watchers by the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those watchers have been read as a kingdom bracing for attack from the water, and the palace as having fallen to a surprise raid by the people it was watching for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is offered as an interpretation rather than a finding, since the tablets record an administration going about its year and nothing in them announces an emergency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Climate supplies no trigger either: the cave record shows no clear match in time between the destruction and drier conditions, though the aridity that followed probably cut yields and made any recovery harder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came after was emptier here than anywhere else in southern Greece, Messenia losing more of its people in the following century than any other region, and the Aegean as a whole was among the worst hit parts of the Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ridge was not quite abandoned, though: rooms of the main building still standing were used again between the fire and the early 10th century BCE, and people settled on and around the ruins afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"destruction of Pylos","questions":["Lists of men posted as watchers by the sea are why some read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a surprise raid from the water, though nothing in the archive announces an emergency.","A stalagmite from a cave near the palace shows no match in time between drier conditions and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which removes climate as its trigger."],"tags":["event","place","warfare","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Shannon LaFayette Hogue, \"New Evidence of Post-Destruction Reuse in the Main Building of the Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 151–57, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0151. [Open access]","Martin Finné et al., \"Late Bronze Age Climate Change and the Destruction of the Mycenaean Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 12 (2017): e0189447, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0189447. [Open access]","Sharon R. Stocker and Jack L. Davis, \"Animal Sacrifice, Archives, and Feasting at the Palace of Nestor,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 73, no. 2 (2004): 179–95, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/4134892.pdf. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Griffin%2C_Golden_Seal_from_Pylos.jpg","title":"destruction of Pylos","desc":"Griffin from a golden signet bead, unearthed in a tomb in Pylos, Messinia, by american archaeologist Carl Blegen in 1953 (NAMA 7986 - National Archaeological Museum of Athens, Hellenic Ministry of Culture).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Griffin,_Golden_Seal_from_Pylos.jpg","alt":"Griffin, Golden Seal from Pylos"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Pylos","at":[21.695,37.0272]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the surprise-raid reading offered as an interpretation rather than a finding?","a":"Because the tablets record an administration going about its year and nothing in them announces an emergency, whatever the watchers by the sea suggest."},{"q":"Why does climate supply no trigger here?","a":"Because the cave record shows no clear match in time between the destruction and drier conditions, though the aridity that followed probably cut yields."},{"q":"Why is the site not simply abandoned?","a":"Because rooms of the main building still standing were used again between the fire and the early 10th century BCE, and people settled on and around the ruins."}]},
{"id":"gr-110","num":"110","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Houses were rebuilt inside the walls of Mycenae and the lower town at Tiryns went up again during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but no palace was ever raised in either place.","answer":"Postpalatial Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mycenae again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 – 1125 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Postpalatial Greece</b> is the 12th century BCE on the Greek mainland, between the burning of the palaces and the Dark Age, and is called Late Helladic IIIC.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palaces were never rebuilt, but the citadels were not abandoned: at Mycenae houses went up again inside the walls, on old foundations and new, and the Granary beside the Lion Gate stayed in use well into the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Tiryns the lower town was rebuilt after the flood that wrecked its predecessor, and a large hall with a central hearth and three columns in a row went up outside the walls late in the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A life-sized painted woman from Mycenae shows fresco was still a major art somewhere, and substantial building of the period is known at Lefkandi, Asine, Korakou and Teichos Dymaion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What did not come back was the palace itself, with its writing and its accounting; Pylos shows the pattern in miniature, rooms still standing lived in a while, then people settled on the ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The century was violent: Mycenae burned again about 1150 to 1125 BCE, the great hall outside Tiryns went at much the same time, and Lefkandi's levels record destruction after destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Messenia and Laconia are nearly empty of it, and by the end the citadels of Athens and Mycenae were burial grounds, which is what abandonment looks like on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two funeral customs arrive in its second half: cremation, at Perati in Attica and Ialysos on Rhodes, and burial singly in cist graves, now taken for a revival of older mainland practice rather than an import.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end of Mycenaean civilisation was therefore not the fall of the palaces but a slide of a century or more after it, and its date is unsettled, radiocarbon from northern Greece running 70 to 100 years earlier than the southern chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lasting change is regional: the uniform Mycenaean culture of the 14th and 13th centuries broke into local styles, and Greece stayed local for centuries after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Postpalatial Greece","questions":["Cremation at Perati and burial singly in cist graves both spread through the second half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the century after the palaces burned.","Because the uniform Mycenaean culture broke into local styles during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Greece stayed a patchwork of regions for the next seven hundred years."],"tags":["era","event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Shannon LaFayette Hogue, \"New Evidence of Post-Destruction Reuse in the Main Building of the Palace of Nestor at Pylos,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 151–57, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0151. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/AGM_Ancient_Greek_Pair_of_Terracotta_Boots.jpg/1920px-AGM_Ancient_Greek_Pair_of_Terracotta_Boots.jpg","title":"Postpalatial Greece","desc":"Ancient Greek pair of terracotta boots. Early geometric period cremation burial of a woman, 900 BCE. Ancient Agora Museum in Athens.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGM_Ancient_Greek_Pair_of_Terracotta_Boots.jpg","alt":"AGM Ancient Greek Pair of Terracotta Boots"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the fall of the palaces not the end of Mycenaean civilisation?","a":"Because the citadels were not abandoned — houses went up again at Mycenae, Tiryns rebuilt its lower town — and the end came as a slide of a century or more after."},{"q":"Why do the citadels of Athens and Mycenae read as abandoned by the century's end?","a":"Because they had become burial grounds, which is what abandonment looks like on the ground."},{"q":"Why is the lasting change described as regional?","a":"Because the uniform Mycenaean culture of the 14th and 13th centuries broke into local styles, and Greece stayed local for centuries after."}]},
{"id":"gr-111","num":"111","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Greece kept no written records at all through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so what is known of those centuries comes almost entirely from graves and pottery.","answer":"Greek Dark Ages","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protogeometric</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Greek Dark Ages</b> is the name long given to the centuries between the end of Mycenaean civilisation and the Archaic period, running roughly from 1050 to 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The darkness is partly the period's own and partly ours: writing, palace buildings and the economies that ran on them had ceased around 1200 BCE, and Greece kept no records again for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is left is mostly graves and pots, and the data remain so limited that almost anything said beyond a single site rests on theory rather than evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The label is contested as well, borrowed as it was from medieval Europe: specialists argue over Dark Age, Early Iron Age and naming the period after its pottery instead, and none of the three has carried the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the dates shift, radiocarbon from northern Greece running 70 to 100 years earlier than the conventional chronology of the south, and the Protogeometric phase has been argued to open about 1020 rather than 1050 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It is not three and a half blank centuries, though.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The material sorts into phases, Submycenaean first, then Protogeometric from about 1050 to 900 and Geometric after it, which means the pottery kept changing steadily enough to be followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade across the sea continued at a lower level: amphoras made in the Thessalian and Euboean area between about 800 and 675 BCE reached Troy, Antissa on Lesbos, and coastal sites in the Chalkidike, Thessaly and Euboea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cult places were kept up too, one of them at Troy built against the ruined Bronze Age wall, where 28 stone-paved circles were laid out about 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The period was therefore a poorer, more local and far less visible Greece rather than an empty one, and how dark it looks still depends a good deal on which regions have been dug and published.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek Dark Ages","questions":["Trade across the sea did not stop in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: amphoras made in Thessaly and Euboea between about 800 and 675 BCE reached Troy and Lesbos.","Specialists dispute the very name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, borrowed from medieval Europe, and offer Early Iron Age or a pottery phase in its place."],"tags":["era","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]","Paul D. Scotton, review of <i>The Aegean from Bronze Age to Iron Age: Continuity and Change Between the Twelfth and Eighth Centuries BC</i>, by O. T. P. K. Dickinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.62, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.62/. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]","C. Brian Rose, \"Separating Fact from Fiction in the Aiolian Migration,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 77, no. 3 (2008): 399–430, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/40205756.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Early_Geometric_Jewelry_from_the_Areopagus_of_Athens._Ancient_Agora_Museum_of_Athens.jpg/1920px-Early_Geometric_Jewelry_from_the_Areopagus_of_Athens._Ancient_Agora_Museum_of_Athens.jpg","title":"Greek Dark Ages","desc":"14-16: Broad gold finger rings. 17-19: Gold finger rings 20: Pair of gold earrings with trapezoid endings Finds are part of an early Geometric Cremation Burial of a Pregnant Wealthy Woman (from the N.W. of the Areopagus). About 850 BCE. Ancient Agora Museum (Athens).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Geometric_Jewelry_from_the_Areopagus_of_Athens._Ancient_Agora_Museum_of_Athens.jpg","alt":"Early Geometric Jewelry from the Areopagus of Athens. Ancient Agora Museum of Athens"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the darkness partly ours?","a":"Because writing and the economies that ran on it ceased around 1200 BCE, so Greece kept no records for centuries and what is left is mostly graves and pots."},{"q":"Why is the name of the period contested?","a":"Because it was borrowed from medieval Europe, and specialists argue over Dark Age, Early Iron Age and naming the period after its pottery, with none carrying the day."},{"q":"Why is it not three and a half blank centuries?","a":"Because the pottery sorts into phases that keep changing, trade across the sea continued at a lower level, and cult places were kept up."}]},
{"id":"gr-112","num":"112","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Athenians of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up the family chamber tomb for single graves cut in earth or lined with stone slabs, and began burying iron beside bronze.","answer":"Submycenaean period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1070 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protogeometric</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1020 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Submycenaean period</b> is the phase of Greek pottery and burial between the last Mycenaean styles and the Protogeometric, known above all from the cemeteries of Athens and of Salamis across the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is the Kerameikos, where graves dug near the Pompeion and the Dipylon Gate since the 1960s were published as tombs 115 to 148.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burial had changed shape: where Mycenaean families lay together in rock-cut chamber tombs, the dead now went singly into pits, into boxes lined with stone slabs, or into wooden coffins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few were burnt instead and their ashes gathered into an amphora, and what went in with them is modest rather than poor: weapons and tools, pins and fibulae, beads of glass, faience and amber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Early Iron Age graves of the Athenian Agora hold a different gathering of people from the Kerameikos, most of the adults being women, with infants and children of every age unusually well represented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Iron had at first served only for rings and other small ornaments, and it is in the decades closing the phase that iron swords, daggers and a knife appear in Athenian graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taken as the moment the metal displaced bronze for tools, that is where the Greek Iron Age begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Athens took to burning its dead about the same time, a change the Argolid never followed, so cremation is an Attic habit of these years rather than a Greek one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several pot shapes come from outside Attica, amphorae with handles from shoulder to lip and vases in the form of birds being Cypriot ideas, while handmade wares point north towards Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth opens the phase around 1070 and closes it near 1000 BCE, and because almost every pot comes from a grave rather than a house some doubt it is a period at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Submycenaean period","questions":["The Attic pottery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sorts into four stages, the last of them transitional to the Protogeometric style that followed it.","Radiocarbon from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth ends the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 1020 BCE, leaving the style far less time than the whole century once allowed it."],"tags":["era","object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"The Last Mycenaeans at Corinth,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48, no. 4 (1979): 348–92, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147841.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Lekythos_in_protogeometric_style%2C_Submycenaean_Greece%2C_c._1050_BC%2C_H_5057_-_Martin_von_Wagner_Museum_-_W%C3%BCrzburg%2C_Germany_-_DSC05623.jpg/1920px-Lekythos_in_protogeometric_style%2C_Submycenaean_Greece%2C_c._1050_BC%2C_H_5057_-_Martin_von_Wagner_Museum_-_W%C3%BCrzburg%2C_Germany_-_DSC05623.jpg","title":"Submycenaean period","desc":"A small dark clay jug of the Submycenaean period, about 1050 BCE, with a plain body and a single handle.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lekythos_in_protogeometric_style,_Submycenaean_Greece,_c._1050_BC,_H_5057_-_Martin_von_Wagner_Museum_-_W%C3%BCrzburg,_Germany_-_DSC05623.jpg","alt":"Lekythos in protogeometric style, Submycenaean Greece, c. 1050 BCE, H 5057 - Martin von Wagner Museum - Würzburg, Germany"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did burial change shape in this phase?","a":"Because where Mycenaean families lay together in rock-cut chamber tombs, the dead now went singly into pits, stone-lined boxes or wooden coffins, and a few were cremated."},{"q":"Why is cremation called an Attic habit rather than a Greek one?","a":"Because Athens took to burning its dead in these years and the Argolid never followed."},{"q":"Why do some doubt it is a period at all?","a":"Because almost every pot assigned to it comes from a grave rather than a house, so the phase may be a burial fashion rather than a stage."}]},
{"id":"gr-113","num":"113","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The concentric circles that give the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name were drawn with a multiple brush turning on a pivot rather than by a steady hand.","answer":"Protogeometric period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiocarbon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1020 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Protogeometric period</b> takes its name from its pottery, the style that follows the last Mycenaean wares in central and southern Greece and runs to about 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its signature is the ring of concentric circles and semicircles, laid down not by a steady hand but with a multiple brush turning on a pivot, a technique worked out by experiment and long missing from the standard accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New shapes entered the Athenian repertoire across the transition into it, among them ring vases, bird-shaped askoi and amphorae with handles from shoulder to lip, all on Cypriot models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stirrup jar, the workhorse container of Mycenaean Greece, appears for the last time in the graves of that same transition and then goes out of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Iron displaced bronze as the metal for tools over the same decades, which on the usual definition is where the Iron Age in Greece begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A cremation from the Athenian Agora shows the period's end on the ground: the burned bones of a warrior in a neck amphora half a metre high, its mouth shut with a field stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His iron sword, spearheads, knives and a horse's bit had gone on the pyre with him and were gathered up afterwards in cloth whose weave still shows on the rusted metal, and the pyre sweepings held carbonised figs and grapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When any of this happened is unsettled: radiocarbon from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth supports the conventional scheme, which opens the style around 1020 to 1000 rather than 1050 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 21 dates from the mound at Sindos near Thessaloniki push it far higher, placing the late phase in the first half of the 11th century, and its authors note that almost the whole Protogeometric is dated by scholarly intuition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The name is contested as well, since parts of the Aegean have no recognisable Protogeometric pottery at all, so calling the age after one central Greek workshop tradition makes those regions a periphery by definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Protogeometric period","questions":["A warrior cremated in the Athenian Agora at the close of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was buried with iron weapons still wrapped in the cloth that carried them from the pyre.","21 radiocarbon dates from Sindos in northern Greece would push the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> higher than the conventional scheme allows, which rests largely on a handful of eastern sherds."],"tags":["era","object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Carl W. Blegen, \"Two Athenian Grave Groups of About 900 B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 21, no. 4 (1952): 279–94, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/146860.pdf. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Amphora_protogeometric_BM_A1123.jpg","title":"Protogeometric period","desc":"Late protogeometric belly-handled amphora with chequer-board design on the shoulder and parallel wavy lines in a panel at the handle zone. Made in Athens, from Athens?.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphora_protogeometric_BM_A1123.jpg","alt":"Amphora protogeometric BM A1123"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the style's signature ornament a matter of technique?","a":"Because the rings of concentric circles were laid down not by a steady hand but with a multiple brush turning on a pivot, a method worked out by experiment."},{"q":"Why is the period's chronology unsettled?","a":"Because 21 dates from Sindos push it far higher than the conventional scheme, and their authors note that almost the whole Protogeometric is dated by scholarly intuition."},{"q":"Why is the name itself contested?","a":"Because parts of the Aegean have no recognisable Protogeometric pottery at all, so naming the age after one central Greek workshop tradition makes those regions a periphery by definition."}]},
{"id":"gr-114","num":"114","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The six stages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are dated by a few sherds from Tel Abu Hawam and a handful more from Megiddo and Samaria.","answer":"Geometric period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Geometric</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 760 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Geometric period</b> is the last phase of the Greek Early Iron Age, about 900 to 700 BCE, and is named after its pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is cut into six stages, Early, Middle and Late Geometric each divided in two, of a generation or so apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those dates rest on very little: the move into Early Geometric hangs on a couple of sherds from one layer at Tel Abu Hawam, and the later divisions on a handful from Megiddo and Samaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Radiocarbon from the mound at Sindos in northern Greece would push the whole scheme higher, so the conventional figures are a working frame rather than a settled one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Real wealth appears early: a woman cremated on the Areopagus slope at Athens about 850 BCE was buried with granulated and filigreed gold jewellery, ivory stamp seals, and beads of faience and glass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Her ashes lay in an urn stopped with a cup, and beside it stood a terracotta chest with five model granaries on its lid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The century after her is the one that remakes Greece, and its tempo of social and material change has never been satisfactorily explained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The usual answer is that the population grew, and the Athenian Agora seems to show it: of some thirty wells of the period, fourteen were dug between 900 and 760 BCE and sixteen in the fifty years that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The graves that carry the same argument are less safe than they look, because many burials may mean that more people were dying rather than living, and the Agora has 28 of them from the second half of the 8th century against only four from the first half of the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every well in use around 700 BCE was filled in at much the same moment, which has been read as a long drought, and either way the Attic record goes quiet for a generation just as the Greek world was sending out its first colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Geometric period","questions":["Sixteen of the thirty Geometric wells in the Athenian Agora were dug in the last fifty years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is usually read as a growing population.","A woman cremated on the Areopagus slope early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was buried with gold jewellery, ivory stamp seals and beads of faience and glass."],"tags":["era","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Evelyn Lord Smithson, \"The Tomb of a Rich Athenian Lady, ca. 850 B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 37, no. 1 (1968): 77–116, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147539.pdf. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Lynn Roller, review of <i>Greece in the Making, 1200–479 BC</i>, by Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.02.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.09/. [Open access]","John McK. Camp II, \"A Drought in the Late Eighth Century B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48 (1979): 397–411, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147843.pdf. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Clay_amphora%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_Period%2C_800-760_BC_%2827931792873%29.jpg/1920px-Clay_amphora%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_Period%2C_800-760_BC_%2827931792873%29.jpg","title":"Geometric period","desc":"Ancient Greek Art Gallery, Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, Greece. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_amphora,_Middle_Geometric_II_Period,_800-760_BC_(27931792873).jpg","alt":"Clay amphora, Middle Geometric II Period, 800-760 BCE"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why do the period's dates rest on very little?","a":"Because the move into Early Geometric hangs on a couple of sherds from one layer at Tel Abu Hawam, and the later divisions on a handful from Megiddo and Samaria."},{"q":"Why are graves a poor measure of population growth?","a":"Because many burials may mean that more people were dying rather than living — the Agora has 28 from the second half of the 8th century against four from the first half of the 7th."},{"q":"Why does the Athenian record go quiet around 700 BCE?","a":"Because every well in use then was filled in at much the same moment, which has been read as a long drought."}]},
{"id":"gr-115","num":"115","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pollen from a Messenian lagoon records the least human impact in 4,000 years, the clearest sign of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed the Mycenaean palaces.","answer":"depopulation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces burn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pollen recovers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Depopulation</b> after the palaces is the steep fall in occupied sites and burials across southern Greece in the century following the destructions of about 1200 BCE, when the centres at Mycenae, Pylos, Thebes, Tiryns and elsewhere burned and sites across the Argolid, the Corinthia, Laconia, Messenia, Attica and Boeotia were given up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The destruction and the emptying run together along a rough north-south axis through those same regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Messenia shows it more starkly than anywhere else in southern Greece, its palace never rebuilt and its countryside largely deserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pollen cored from the Osmanaga Lagoon beside the Bay of Navarino agrees from a wholly different direction: oak spread, olive fell away, and through the Early Iron Age the Messenian landscape carried the least intensive human impact of the last 4,000 years, a trend reversed only around 800 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That independent line matters, because the rest of the evidence is thin enough that almost any claim reaching beyond a single site rests on theory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The emptying was also uneven, and in places it runs the other way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Achaea and the Ionian islands, Kephallenia above all, have more tombs in these years rather than fewer, which is the reverse of what Messenia, Laconia and even the Argolid record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cyprus received two separate waves of Aegean arrivals, the first early in the 12th century at Enkomi, Kition, Maa and Sinda, the second perhaps two generations later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Crete people moved rather than vanished, up to Karphi on its peak above the Lasithi plain and to ridges and ledges such as Kavousi-Kastro and Katalimata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the safe statement is that far fewer people can be seen, which is not quite the same as far fewer people: some left, some died, and some lived in ways that leave less behind, while the largest migration accounts are judged implausible on the sheer numbers and shipping they would need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"depopulation","questions":["Achaea and the Ionian islands show more tombs rather than fewer after 1200 BCE, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that emptied Messenia was far from universal.","Cyprus took two waves of Aegean arrivals in the 12th century BCE, often read as one destination of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that emptied parts of southern Greece."],"tags":["concept","event","history","archaeology","greece","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]","Susan E. Alcock, Andrea M. Berlin, Ann B. Harrison, Sebastian Heath, Nigel Spencer, and David L. Stone, \"Pylos Regional Archaeological Project, Part VII: Historical Messenia, Geometric through Late Roman,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 74, no. 2 (2005): 147–209, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/25067953.pdf. [Open access]","Paul D. Scotton, review of <i>The Aegean from Bronze Age to Iron Age: Continuity and Change Between the Twelfth and Eighth Centuries BC</i>, by O. T. P. K. Dickinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.62, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.62/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why does the Messenian pollen record matter so much?","a":"Because it agrees from a wholly different direction — oak spreading, olive falling away, the least intensive human impact of the last 4,000 years — where the rest of the evidence is thin."},{"q":"Why was the emptying uneven?","a":"Because Achaea and the Ionian islands have more tombs in these years rather than fewer, and on Crete people moved up to Karphi and other high places rather than vanishing."},{"q":"Why is far fewer people visible not the same as far fewer people?","a":"Because some left, some died, and some lived in ways that leave less behind — while the largest migration accounts are judged implausible on the numbers and shipping they would need."}]},
{"id":"gr-116","num":"116","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The cemeteries at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run from about 1050 to 825 BCE, the very stretch of centuries that used to be called the Greek Dark Age.","answer":"Lefkandi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xeropolis phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemeteries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 825 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lefkandi</b> is a coastal site on the western side of Euboea, on the narrow strait that separates the island from the Greek mainland, and people lived there from about 2100 BCE down into the Geometric period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The settlement mound is called Xeropolis, walled and continuously occupied from the Middle Helladic period onwards, with the Mycenaean palace age barely represented and the 12th century spreading over most of the hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three building stages of that century have been separated there, the first of them with two-storey houses whose upper floors were used for dining while clay storage bins and the cooking stood below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the first settlement burned down the second was laid out on a tidy grid of squares about five metres across, and the dead were now buried under the floors of rooms still lived in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crucible fragments and moulds show metal being worked on site, and an iron knife from that phase is among the earliest in the Aegean, probably brought in from Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its excavators read the community as decentralised but comfortable, letting the old fortification wall go, spreading valuables and skilled crafts through the settlement rather than concentrating them, and burying its dead privately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cemeteries are what made the site famous, running from about 1050 to 825 BCE, which is exactly the stretch of centuries once written off as the Greek Dark Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Graves in its Skoubris cemetery correspond to the Attic phase transitional between Submycenaean and Protogeometric, and radiocarbon taken here helped place that change at about 1020 to 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The site has since anchored the argument that Euboea set the pace for the Aegean in these centuries, its pottery style reaching Boeotia, Thessaly, the Cyclades and further still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That case rests almost wholly on pottery, and it has been objected that a dominant style is not a dominant state, so the importance of the site is agreed while its supremacy is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lefkandi","questions":["An iron knife from the settlement mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is among the earliest in the Aegean and was probably brought there from Cyprus.","The claim that Euboea led the Aegean rests largely on pottery from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a dominant style is not the same as a dominant state."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Lefkandi IV: The Bronze Age. The Late Helladic IIIC Settlement at Xeropolis</i>, ed. R. D. G. Evely, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.06.15. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]","Lefkandi Excavation Project, \"Excavations at Lefkandi,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Oxford, https://lefkandi.classics.ox.ac.uk/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Centaur_Chiron_of_Lefkandi%2C_Archaeological_museum_of_Eretria%2C_Greece.jpg/1280px-Centaur_Chiron_of_Lefkandi%2C_Archaeological_museum_of_Eretria%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Lefkandi","desc":"Centaur of Lefkandi, 1050–900 BCE, clay. Identified as Chiron, found in two pieces in two different tombs at the site of Toumba. Archaeological Museum of Eretria.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centaur_Chiron_of_Lefkandi,_Archaeological_museum_of_Eretria,_Greece.jpg","alt":"A painted terracotta centaur, a bearded man’s upper body on a horse’s hindquarters, standing on a museum plinth"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Lefkandi","at":[23.6753,38.4125]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the site's cemeteries make it famous?","a":"Because they run from about 1050 to 825 BCE, exactly the stretch of centuries once written off as the Greek Dark Age."},{"q":"Why is the community read as decentralised but comfortable?","a":"Because it let the old fortification wall go, spread valuables and skilled crafts through the settlement rather than concentrating them, and buried its dead privately."},{"q":"Why is Euboean supremacy disputed while the site's importance is not?","a":"Because the case rests almost wholly on pottery, and it has been objected that a dominant style is not a dominant state."}]},
{"id":"gr-117","num":"117","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A linen cloth folded inside a Cypriot bronze urn survived three thousand years in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, protected by the metal around it.","answer":"Toumba building at Lefkandi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 950 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze urn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 BCE or earlier</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Toumba building at Lefkandi</b> is a monumental hall of about 50 by 13.8 metres (164 by 45 feet), raised in the middle of the 10th century BCE on the eastern side of the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is divided into an east room, a central room and an apse, with a row of wooden columns down its axis, a curved western end, and a wooden veranda that is the earliest known in Greek architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two shafts were cut into the floor of the central room: one held four horses, the other a man whose body had been burned and a woman who had not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His ashes were placed in a bronze amphora made in Cyprus, already an heirloom at least a century old when it went into the ground, and an iron sword, a spearhead and a whetstone were laid beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She wore gold, and among it a necklace made in Syria around 1700 BCE, some seven centuries before her own funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Folded carefully inside the urn was a large rectangular linen cloth, woven in plain tabby with fringes and bands of geometric pattern, which the bronze around it preserved for three thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the most nearly complete early Greek garment known, restored at the National Archaeological Museum in Athens and put on show there for the first time in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Radiocarbon on the textile returned about 1126 to 1001 BCE, while a horse bone from the same complex had lost its collagen and could not be dated at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hall was destroyed not long after the funeral and then deliberately covered by a mound, and the ground in front of its eastern door became a cemetery for people who were probably of the same household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The find was disruptive as well as spectacular, since the emergency excavation drew staff and time away from the settlement dig, whose final publication then waited decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Toumba building at Lefkandi","questions":["Four horses lay in one shaft and a cremated man and an unburnt woman in the other beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was pulled down soon afterwards.","A necklace made in Syria about 1700 BCE was worn by the woman buried in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, seven centuries after the thing was made."],"tags":["building","place","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Lefkandi Excavation Project, \"Early Excavations at Lefkandi: The Protogeometric Building and the Cemetery of Toumba,\" Faculty of Classics, University of Oxford, https://lefkandi.classics.ox.ac.uk/Toumba.html. [Open access]","British School at Athens, \"Lefkandi Textile on Display in the National Archaeological Museum,\" 24 January 2019, https://www.bsa.ac.uk/2019/01/24/lefkandi-textile-on-display-in-the-national-archaeological-museum/. [Open access]","ARTEX, Hellenic Centre for Research and Conservation of Archaeological Textiles, \"Project Lefkandi,\" https://artextiles.org/en/content/project-lefkandi. [Open access]","Michael B. Toffolo et al., \"Towards an Absolute Chronology for the Aegean Iron Age: New Radiocarbon Dates from Lefkandi, Kalapodi and Corinth,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e83117, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0083117. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Lefkandi IV: The Bronze Age. The Late Helladic IIIC Settlement at Xeropolis</i>, ed. R. D. G. Evely, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.06.15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Lefkandi_Heroon_from_E.jpg/1920px-Lefkandi_Heroon_from_E.jpg","title":"Toumba building at Lefkandi","desc":"Lefkandi, Toumba. Protogeometric \"Heroon\" in 1991, from east.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lefkandi_Heroon_from_E.jpg","alt":"Lefkandi Heroon from E"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Lefkandi","at":[23.6753,38.4125]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the objects in the shaft older than the funeral?","a":"Because the bronze amphora holding his ashes was made in Cyprus and already an heirloom at least a century old, and her necklace was made in Syria some seven centuries earlier."},{"q":"Why did a linen cloth survive three thousand years?","a":"Because it was folded carefully inside the bronze urn, whose metal preserved it — the most nearly complete early Greek garment known."},{"q":"Why was the find disruptive as well as spectacular?","a":"Because the emergency excavation drew staff and time away from the settlement dig, whose final publication then waited decades."}]},
{"id":"gr-118","num":"118","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A hall of 13.6 by 8 metres with a pit hearth and an apse at one end stood at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it is among the largest buildings known from Dark Age Greece.","answer":"Nichoria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dark Age II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dark Age III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 850 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nichoria</b> is a flat-topped ridge about 500 metres (1,600 feet) long in Messenia, some 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) from the northwestern coast of the Messenian Gulf, and it carries one of the few Greek settlements that can be followed right through the Dark Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Mycenaean village there was destroyed late in the 13th century BCE, along with the palaces and much else in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What followed is set out in phases, the second falling in the 10th century and perhaps running into the middle of the 9th, the third from about 850 to 750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most striking building is Unit IV-1, an apsidal hall of 13.60 by 8 metres (45 by 26 feet) not counting its courtyard, which its excavators call one of the largest Dark Age apsidal structures in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wooden posts set 1.80 metres (6 feet) apart carried its roof, a line of column bases ran down the long axis, and a pit hearth about a metre across was sunk near the middle of the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The hall was rebuilt at least once, its south wall moved outward and a new floor laid, and a small walled courtyard added in front of the eastern door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was dropped on those floors is domestic rather than grand: spindle whorls, finger rings, a whetstone, an iron spike and axe blade, coarse cooking ware, charred seeds, and the bones of sheep, goat, pig and cattle bearing knife marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that evidence the excavators read it as a dwelling, perhaps a chieftain's house, rather than a temple, while noting that a Mycenaean hall had combined sacred and domestic uses too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another apsidal building went up directly over the tip of its apse, so little time passed between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ridge was given up before the middle of the 8th century, though Late Geometric graves show the land was not abandoned, and later surveyors put its quiet down to lying in a buffer zone facing Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Nichoria","questions":["Spindle whorls, a whetstone, cooking pots and butchered animal bones on the floors at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed its excavators to a dwelling rather than a temple.","The ridge at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was given up before the middle of the 8th century BCE, though Late Geometric graves show the land around it was still used."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]","William A. McDonald, \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1969–71,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 41, no. 2 (1972): 218–73, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147682.pdf. [Open access]","Susan E. Alcock, Andrea M. Berlin, Ann B. Harrison, Sebastian Heath, Nigel Spencer, and David L. Stone, \"Pylos Regional Archaeological Project, Part VII: Historical Messenia, Geometric through Late Roman,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 74, no. 2 (2005): 147–209, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/25067953.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","A. Bernard Knapp and Sturt W. Manning, \"Crisis in Context: The End of the Late Bronze Age in the Eastern Mediterranean,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 120, no. 1 (2016): 99–149, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.120.1.0099. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Entrance_Tholos_Tomb_at_Nichoria.jpg/1920px-Entrance_Tholos_Tomb_at_Nichoria.jpg","title":"Nichoria","desc":"Entrance of the Tholos Tomb at the Mycenaean archaeological site of Nichoria, located in Messinia, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entrance_Tholos_Tomb_at_Nichoria.jpg","alt":"Entrance Tholos Tomb at Nichoria"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Nichoria","at":[21.9128,37.0033]},"why":[{"q":"Why do the excavators read the great hall as a dwelling?","a":"Because what was dropped on its floors is domestic — spindle whorls, finger rings, a whetstone, cooking ware, charred seeds and butchered animal bones — rather than temple furniture."},{"q":"Why is the site so valuable for the Dark Age?","a":"Because it is one of the few Greek settlements that can be followed right through it, in phases running from the 10th century into the 8th."},{"q":"Why is the ridge's later quiet explained by its position?","a":"Because later surveyors put it down to lying in a buffer zone facing Sparta, though Late Geometric graves show the land was not abandoned."}]},
{"id":"gr-119","num":"119","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"No spring or well was found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its flat roofs were built to catch the rain its people had to live on.","answer":"Zagora on Andros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiocarbon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1015 – 925 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zagora on Andros</b> is an Early Iron Age town on a headland on the south-western coast of that Cycladic island, spread over about 6.7 hectares (17 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was lived in through the 9th and 8th centuries BCE, with signs that people were already there late in the 10th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fortification wall with an elaborate gate closed the landward side, and it is among the more important pieces of Geometric military architecture to survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Australian excavations of the late 1960s and early 1970s opened about a tenth of the town and found 55 stone-built rooms making up at least 25 houses, grouped in clusters much like the island villages of today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No spring or well has been found on the headland, so the flat roofs were built to catch rainwater and store it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the place so valuable is that almost nothing was built over it afterwards, so an Early Iron Age town survives as a plan rather than as fragments under later walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people left at the end of the 8th century, in the very decades when the Greek world was changing fastest, and why they went is still unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> All that was raised there later was a small temple of the 6th century, built mostly of schist, with the altar standing in a square room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Work resumed in 2010 as a joint project of the University of Sydney and the Archaeological Society at Athens, and a trench dug then produced a stratified radiocarbon sequence: its earliest levels fall in 1015 to 925 BCE and its Late Geometric ones in 935 to 850 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those dates would begin the Late Geometric at least a century before the conventional 760 BCE, the same direction in which dates from northern Greece push, and they are among the very few absolute sequences the period has.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zagora on Andros","questions":["Because almost nothing was built over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after it was abandoned, an Early Iron Age town survives there as a plan rather than as fragments.","Radiocarbon from a stratified trench at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would start the Late Geometric a century before the conventional date of 760 BCE."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Zagora Archaeological Project, \"About Zagora,\" University of Sydney and the Australian Archaeological Institute at Athens, https://zagoraarchaeologicalproject.org/the-site/about-zagora/. [Open access]","Rudolph Alagich, Lorena Becerra-Valdivia, Margaret C. Miller, Katerina Trantalidou, and Colin Smith, \"Mediterranean Early Iron Age Chronology: Assessing Radiocarbon Dates from a Stratified Geometric Period Deposit at Zagora (Andros), Greece,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 398 (2024): 454–69, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.16. [Open access]","Australian Archaeological Institute at Athens, <i>AAIA Bulletin</i> 4 (2006/2007), https://aaia.gr/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Bulletin-4-web.pdf. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Lynn Roller, review of <i>Greece in the Making, 1200–479 BC</i>, by Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.02.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.09/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Zagora%2C_Andros%2C_224941x.jpg/1920px-Zagora%2C_Andros%2C_224941x.jpg","title":"Zagora on Andros","desc":"Near perpendicular rocks had been in the 9th to 7th century capital of Andros, Zagora. A little further north (left in image) was a late Neolithic settlement Strofilias.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zagora,_Andros,_224941x.jpg","alt":"Zagora, Andros, 224941x"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Zagora","at":[24.9,37.8333]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the town survive as a plan rather than as fragments?","a":"Because almost nothing was built over it afterwards, only a small 6th-century temple."},{"q":"Why were the roofs built flat?","a":"Because no spring or well has been found on the headland, so they had to catch rainwater and store it."},{"q":"Why do its radiocarbon dates matter beyond the site?","a":"Because they would begin the Late Geometric at least a century before the conventional 760 BCE, and they are among the very few absolute sequences the period has."}]},
{"id":"gr-120","num":"120","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A shaft furnace, charcoal and air at about 1,200 °C gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a spongy bloom rather than liquid metal, and the rest was done with a hammer.","answer":"iron metallurgy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron over bronze</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest smithy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Iron metallurgy</b> takes over in Greece across the transition from the Submycenaean to the Protogeometric, when iron displaced bronze as the ordinary metal for tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method was the bloomery: ore mixed with charcoal in a shaft furnace one to two metres (3 to 7 feet) high and 40 to 80 centimetres (16 to 31 inches) across inside, with air driven in at the bottom by bellows or natural draught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the working temperature of around 1,200 °C (2,200 °F) the slag ran liquid but the iron did not, so what came out was a solid sponge, or bloom, shot through with slag and charcoal that had to be hammered out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek smiths could not melt and cast iron at all, so every object was shaped by long hammering, and the carbon that turns iron into steel was taken up in the smithing hearth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Steel could be hardened by quenching and the resulting brittleness eased by tempering, and blades were often made by welding a hard steel edge into a softer iron body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The technology reached the Aegean from the east, from Anatolia, the Levant and Cyprus, and one of the earliest iron objects in Greece is a knife from Lefkandi that was probably brought in from Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was in ordinary hands soon enough, and the floors of the Dark Age hall at Nichoria gave up an iron spike, an axe blade and pieces of another blade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Evidence for making the metal is far thinner than evidence for using it, and the earliest excavated smithy on the Aegean coast, at Phokaia, belongs to the 11th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A little smelting waste from the cemeteries at Kastri on Thasos points to local production in small quantities, which fits the trinket-sized iron finds of those years, mostly knives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The craft kept improving long afterwards: blades from Zone in Thrace were made by welding low-carbon to high-carbon steel and twisting the two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"iron metallurgy","questions":["The earliest excavated blacksmith's workshop on the Aegean coast belongs to the 11th century BCE, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is known far better from objects than from workshops.","Welding a hard steel edge into a softer iron body was routine, which shows how well <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> understood the materials it was working."],"tags":["practice","technology","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Thilo Rehren, \"Metallurgy, Greece and Rome,\" in <i>The Encyclopedia of Ancient History</i>, ed. Andrew Erskine, David B. Hollander, and Arietta Papaconstantinou (Wiley, 2021), https://repository.cyi.ac.cy/bitstream/CyI/1462/1/Rehren%202021%20Metallurgy%20%20Greece%20and%20Rome%20EncAncHist.pdf. [Open access]","Nikos Nerantzis, Nathan Arrington, Domna Terzopoulou, and Maria Tasaklaki, \"Iron Working in Aegean Thrace during Antiquity: New Evidence for Smelting Activity of the 4th Century BC at the City on the Molyvoti Peninsula,\" <i>Scientific Culture</i> 8, no. 3 (2022): 105–23, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6640260. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Lefkandi IV: The Bronze Age. The Late Helladic IIIC Settlement at Xeropolis</i>, ed. R. D. G. Evely, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.06.15. [Open access]","William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Bas_fourneau.png","title":"iron metallurgy","desc":"An engraving of a bloomery furnace at work, with smiths hammering the hot iron bloom and the tools of the trade drawn below.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas_fourneau.png","alt":"Bas fourneau"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why was every iron object shaped by hammering?","a":"Because Greek smiths could not melt and cast iron at all: the bloomery ran the slag liquid while the iron stayed a solid sponge that had to be worked."},{"q":"Why were blades made of two metals welded together?","a":"Because a hard steel edge could be set into a softer iron body, and quenching and tempering managed the brittleness hardening brings."},{"q":"Why is evidence for making the metal thinner than for using it?","a":"Because the earliest excavated smithy on the Aegean coast belongs to the 11th century BCE, and only a little smelting waste from Kastri on Thasos points to local production."}]},
{"id":"gr-121","num":"121","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Burning the dead on a pyre and gathering the bones into a jar, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches Greece in the later 12th century BCE and displaces inhumation at Athens over the centuries after.","answer":"cremation burial","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First appears</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Usual at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cremation burial</b> arrives in Greece in the second half of the 12th century BCE and is generally thought to have come from the east, where the cremation cemetery of Troy VI and Hittite funeral custom offer precedents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It spread unevenly and never became universal: at Athens it displaced burial of the body over the following centuries, while the Argolid kept to inhumation throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Kerameikos it becomes common only in the phase transitional to the Protogeometric, where the burned bones were gathered into an amphora and set in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The pyres there stood close to the graves, in structures built for the occasion and not meant to last, and the ash, the earth beneath it, the burned sherds and what was left of the jewellery and weapons were swept together into the grave pit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At some sites the ash holds seashell and smooth beach pebbles, so at least some bodies were burned on the shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Burned bone is unusually informative, because fire records the conditions it was burned under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bone calcined white has passed above 700 °C (1,300 °F), and where one fragment shows several colours at once the body was burned with the flesh still on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The quantities are the puzzle: a fully cremated adult leaves about 2,400 grams (5 pounds) of bone, and urns at Lefkandi average about 1,660 grams while those at the Kerameikos hold 595 and at Torone 301.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whole cemeteries fall lower still, the unurned deposits at Voulokalyva in Thessaly averaging 73 grams (2.6 ounces) apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 71 pyres dug at Lefkandi, 33 held no burned bone at all, which raises the question whether the remains were routinely collected or whether burning and burying were separate acts with separate meanings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"cremation burial","questions":["Bone calcined white in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has passed 700 °C, and several colours in one fragment mean the body burned with its flesh still on.","Athens took up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in place of burying the body, while the Argolid kept to inhumation, so the custom never became a Greek rule."],"tags":["practice","archaeology","history","greece","iron age","bronze age"],"sources":["Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Vatya_culture_cremation_urn_burial.jpg","title":"cremation burial","desc":"Vatya culture cremation urn burial. Cavazzuti, C., Arena, A., Cardarelli, A. et al.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vatya_culture_cremation_urn_burial.jpg","alt":"Vatya culture cremation urn burial"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why is burned bone unusually informative?","a":"Because fire records the conditions it was burned under: bone calcined white has passed 700 °C, and a fragment showing several colours at once means the body was burned with the flesh still on it."},{"q":"Why are the quantities in the urns a puzzle?","a":"Because a fully cremated adult leaves about 2,400 grams of bone, while urns at Lefkandi average 1,660, the Kerameikos 595 and Torone 301."},{"q":"Why do the Lefkandi pyres raise a question about the rite?","a":"Because 33 of the 71 dug held no burned bone at all, which asks whether the remains were routinely collected or whether burning and burying were separate acts."}]},
{"id":"gr-122","num":"122","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The sides of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are usually upright stone slabs, though boulders, cut bedrock or even small rubble walling were all used instead.","answer":"cist grave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revival</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Common at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cist grave</b> is a rectangular box sunk into the ground to hold a single body, and in Greece its sides are most often upright stone slabs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other builds are known: walls of large boulders capped by a rough course or two of smaller stones, sides cut straight into earth or bedrock, and, rarely, proper rubble walling carried up from the floor, the kind called a Mauerkiste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of that rare sort was found by the sanctuary of Demeter and Kore at Corinth, its south wall standing for 1.10 metres (3 feet 7 inches) and the box small enough that whoever lay in it was probably a child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form is very old in Greece and never went away entirely, since burial of one person in a cist continued quietly through the centuries of the Mycenaean palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It returns to prominence in the later 12th century BCE, alongside cremation, as the family chamber tomb falls out of use on the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Desborough read that return as an intrusion from the north, brought south from Epirus, but most scholars now take it for a revival of Middle Helladic practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One explanation offered for the change is practical: cutting a chamber tomb for a whole family is heavy work to begin when nobody knows how long they will be staying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cists turn up in the ruins of the old centres, on the Acropolis at Athens and inside the citadel at Mycenae, where they mark walled towns given over to the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Submycenaean cemetery of the Kerameikos they are much the commonest grave, 17 of the 34 tombs published from the Pompeion area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small slab-built cists held children at Nichoria, and at Lefkandi cremation urns were set inside cists, so the box outlived the rite it had been built for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"cist grave","questions":["A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was once thought to have come south with newcomers from Epirus, but its return is now read as a revival of much older Greek practice.","17 of the 34 Submycenaean tombs published from the Kerameikos take the form of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which makes it the commonest grave of that cemetery."],"tags":["practice","object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, \"The Last Mycenaeans at Corinth,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48, no. 4 (1979): 348–92, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147841.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]","William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]","Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the form's return read as a revival rather than an intrusion?","a":"Because Desborough took it for a practice brought south from Epirus, and most scholars now take it for a revival of Middle Helladic custom that had never entirely gone away."},{"q":"Why might people have stopped cutting chamber tombs?","a":"Because cutting one for a whole family is heavy work to begin when nobody knows how long they will be staying."},{"q":"Why does the box outlive the rite it was built for?","a":"Because at Lefkandi cremation urns were set inside cists, so the form served a body and then ashes alike."}]},
{"id":"gr-123","num":"123","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A fourth-century boundary stone found at the Agora's north-west corner shows the civic centre stood inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the potters' quarter.","answer":"Kerameikos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron Age graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Boundary stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kerameikos</b> is the district in the north-west of ancient Athens named after its potters, and it was very large, taking in the city centre and reaching out beyond the walls as far as the Academy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The convenient labels Inner and Outer Kerameikos are inventions of modern topographers: ancient writers say simply the Kerameikos, and none of them uses the word to mean the cemetery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A boundary stone of the 4th century BCE was found where it had stood at the north-west corner of the Classical Agora, facing the road in from the Dipylon Gate, which places the civic centre inside the potters' quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Potters worked there for a practical reason: the ground is low and prone to flooding, watered by the Eridanos, by many wells and by natural lines of drainage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their workshops of the 6th and 5th centuries cluster just inside and around the Dipylon Gate and run on to the north-west, and there is almost no trace of potteries anywhere else within the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What made the name famous is the burial ground, whose first excavation volume of 1939 defined the transitional phase between Submycenaean and Protogeometric that specialists still use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its oldest graves are plain ones, and of the thirty-odd Submycenaean tombs in the newest volume of the excavation most carried few offerings at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not everyone lay poor, and one woman of that phase was sent off richly enough to stand apart from her neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the Protogeometric the pyres were burning close to the graves in structures put up for the day, and the urns hold on average 595 grams (21 ounces) of burnt bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its dead are not a cross-section of Athens either: the graves under the later Agora hold many infants and children and mostly adult women, unlike the Kerameikos, so where a body went was a decision somebody made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Kerameikos","questions":["Potters settled in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because the ground there was low and well watered, by the Eridanos, by many wells and by natural drainage.","The graves under the later Agora hold many children and mostly adult women, unlike those of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so burial there was a matter of choice."],"tags":["place","site","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John K. Papadopoulos, \"The Original Kerameikos of Athens and the Siting of the Classical Agora,\" <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 37, no. 2 (1996): 107–28, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/download/2841/5837/15737. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Kerameikos: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen, Bd. XVIII. Die submykenische Nekropole: Neufunde und Neubewertung</i>, by Florian Ruppenstein, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.16. [Open access]","Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]","Florian Ruppenstein, \"The Transitional Phase from Submycenaean to Protogeometric: Definition and Comparative Chronology,\" in <i>LH III C Chronology and Synchronisms III: LH III C Late and the Transition to the Early Iron Age</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Anna Elisabeth Bächle (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2009), 327–43, https://www.austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572_0x0022a02b.pdf. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Kerameikos%2C_excavations_1915.jpg/1920px-Kerameikos%2C_excavations_1915.jpg","title":"Kerameikos","desc":"The German excavation of the Kerameikos cemetery at Athens in 1914 and 1915, photographed from the Dipylon gate across the cleared ground.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerameikos,_excavations_1915.jpg","alt":"Kerameikos, excavations 1915"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Kerameikos","at":[23.7189,37.9783]},"why":[{"q":"Why did potters work in this district?","a":"Because the ground is low and prone to flooding, watered by the Eridanos, by many wells and by natural lines of drainage."},{"q":"Why are Inner and Outer Kerameikos modern inventions?","a":"Because ancient writers say simply the Kerameikos, and none of them uses the word to mean the cemetery."},{"q":"Why are its dead not a cross-section of Athens?","a":"Because the graves under the later Agora hold many infants and children and mostly adult women, unlike this cemetery, so where a body went was a decision somebody made."}]},
{"id":"gr-124","num":"124","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The band between the handles of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a prothesis, the dead laid out on a couch among mourners with their arms raised.","answer":"Dipylon Amphora","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 760 – 750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dipylon Amphora</b> is a monumental Athenian vase of about 760 to 750 BCE, the name piece of the painter known as the Dipylon Master, and it was made to stand over a grave as its marker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs to a group of 13 figure-decorated kraters and three monumental amphorae named after the Dipylon Gate, near which their burial plot lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The band between its handles carries a prothesis, the ceremonial laying out of the corpse among mourners, which is much the commonest scene on these vases, while three of them show the ekphora instead, the body carried to the cemetery on a chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pictures of this kind broke a silence of centuries, since Greek art had shown almost no human figures since the fall of Mycenaean culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The markers are usually taken to be gendered, kraters standing over men and monumental belly-handled amphorae over women, an assumption that has never been tested against the bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Which grave it stood on is not known, since the plot was dug in 1871 and 1872 by an art dealer and the vase could afterwards be tied only to one of two burials, both adult inhumations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One marker alone was found still in place there, a krater 0.95 metres (3 feet 1 inch) high above a trench holding a bronze urn with the ashes of a youth or a girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That plot gave up more than 231 graves in three or four layers, running from the Middle Geometric down to the 4th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burials multiply across these decades, 28 in the Agora and 50 in the Kerameikos in the second half of the century alone, so the marked graves are a small minority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was the choice uniform: the Agora graves hold many children and mostly adult women where the Kerameikos does not, and by the end of the century figured vases were going into graves as offerings rather than standing above them in public view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dipylon Amphora","questions":["Kraters are thought to have marked men's graves and vases like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> those of women, though nobody has tested that against the bones beneath them.","Nobody knows which of two graves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood over, because the plot was dug by an art dealer in 1871 and 1872."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Alexandra Alexandridou, \"The 'Dipylon' Vases and Their Graves: The End of Exclusivity in Early Iron Age Athens,\" <i>Revista M. Estudos sobre a morte, os mortos e o morrer</i> 7, no. 14 (2022): 342–61, https://doi.org/10.9789/2525-3050.2022.v7i14.342-361. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, \"The Original Kerameikos of Athens and the Siting of the Classical Agora,\" <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 37, no. 2 (1996): 107–28, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/download/2841/5837/15737. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","John McK. Camp II, \"A Drought in the Late Eighth Century B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48 (1979): 397–411, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147843.pdf. [Open access]","Anna Lagia, Anastasia Papathanasiou, Zoi Malakasioti, and Foteini Tsiouka, \"Cremations of the Early Iron Age from Mound 36 at Voulokalyva (Ancient Halos) in Thessaly: A Bioarchaeological Appraisal,\" in <i>Brandbestattungen von der mittleren Donau bis zur Ägäis zwischen 1300 und 750 v. Chr.</i>, ed. Michaela Lochner and Florian Ruppenstein (Vienna: Verlag der Österreichischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, 2013), 197–219, https://austriaca.at/0xc1aa5572%200x002debf5.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Grave_amphora._8th_cent._B.C.jpg/1920px-Grave_amphora._8th_cent._B.C.jpg","title":"Dipylon Amphora","desc":"Monumental Attic grave amphora. From the Cerameicus cemetery. The main scene shows the ‘prothesis’ and mourning of the dead. Over the bier is the shroud. Men, women, and a child lament with the hands on their heads, in the usual mourning gesture. Work by the “Dipylon Painter”. George E.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_amphora._8th_cent._B.C.jpg","alt":"Grave amphora. 8th cent. B.C"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why do vases like this break a silence?","a":"Because Greek art had shown almost no human figures since the fall of Mycenaean culture, and these carry the laying out of the corpse among mourners."},{"q":"Why is the gendering of the markers an untested assumption?","a":"Because kraters are taken to stand over men and belly-handled amphorae over women, and that has never been checked against the bones."},{"q":"Why is this vase's own grave unknown?","a":"Because the plot was dug in 1871 and 1872 by an art dealer, and the vase could afterwards be tied only to one of two adult inhumations."}]},
{"id":"gr-125","num":"125","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The measured zigzags and evenly hatched maeanders of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave way late on to quicker work, painted with a flick of a practised wrist.","answer":"Geometric pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Style</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attic lead ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Geometric pottery</b> is the painted ware of Greece between about 900 and 700 BCE, and it is what the whole period is named after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ornament is drawn in dark glaze on the pale clay: banded bodies, measured zigzags, hatched maeanders and panels of pattern set between the handles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Middle Geometric painters worked with excruciating care, where the later ones drew quickly and with a practised wrist, being potters with many orders to fill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Late Geometric brings panels rather than continuous friezes, ornament by the unit, and birds and other animals in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its grandest pieces are the figured vases that stood over graves, from the workshop of the Dipylon Master and then that of the Hirschfeld Painter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the ware is not grand at all, and the wells of the Athenian Agora are full of the ordinary sort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Banded utility amphorae are among the commonest things in them, the cheap pots used to haul water, lost whenever a rope broke or the jar struck the side of the shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kitchen ware ran on its own track: the micaceous cooking jugs are coarse and heavy in the earlier phases, and in the 8th century some change of technique, still not understood, made them light-walled and shapely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What people buried was not what they used, since flat pyxides are everywhere in the graves and almost absent from the wells, having been made for the grave alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Attic work led Greece until the middle of the 8th century, after which Athenian potters stopped setting the fashion and their pots are rarely found outside Attica, though the style as a whole still dates the Iron Age across the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Geometric pottery","questions":["Flat pyxides are everywhere in the graves and almost absent from the wells, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not all made for the same purposes.","Attic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led the whole of Greece until about 750 BCE, after which Athenian pots are rarely found outside Attica at all."],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Eva Brann, \"Late Geometric Well Groups from the Athenian Agora,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 30, no. 2 (1961): 93–146, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147205.pdf. [Open access]","Alexandra Alexandridou, \"The 'Dipylon' Vases and Their Graves: The End of Exclusivity in Early Iron Age Athens,\" <i>Revista M. Estudos sobre a morte, os mortos e o morrer</i> 7, no. 14 (2022): 342–61, https://doi.org/10.9789/2525-3050.2022.v7i14.342-361. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","John McK. Camp II, \"A Drought in the Late Eighth Century B.C.,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 48 (1979): 397–411, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147843.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Clay_skyphos%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_-_Late_Geometric_IA_Period%2C_775-750_BC_%2828469156951%29.jpg/1920px-Clay_skyphos%2C_Middle_Geometric_II_-_Late_Geometric_IA_Period%2C_775-750_BC_%2828469156951%29.jpg","title":"Geometric pottery","desc":"Ancient Greek Art Gallery, Museum of Cycladic Art, Athens, Greece. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clay_skyphos,_Middle_Geometric_II_-_Late_Geometric_IA_Period,_775-750_BC_(28469156951).jpg","alt":"Clay skyphos, Middle Geometric II - Late Geometric IA Period, 775-750 BCE"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does the pottery of the graves differ from that of the wells?","a":"Because what people buried was not what they used: flat pyxides are everywhere in the graves and almost absent from the wells, having been made for the grave alone."},{"q":"Why is later Geometric drawing quicker than earlier?","a":"Because Middle Geometric painters worked with excruciating care where the later ones drew with a practised wrist, being potters with many orders to fill."},{"q":"Why does Attic work stop leading Greece in the mid-8th century?","a":"Because Athenian potters stopped setting the fashion and their pots are rarely found outside Attica after it, though the style as a whole still dates the Iron Age across the Mediterranean."}]},
{"id":"gr-126","num":"126","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Geometric pots left in Mycenaean chamber tombs are the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though whether the visitors meant worship is disputed.","answer":"hero cult at Bronze Age tombs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Offerings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Menidi until</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hero cult at Bronze Age tombs</b> is the practice, visible from the 8th century BCE, of leaving offerings at Mycenaean chamber and tholos tombs that were centuries old and long closed by then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chamber tombs had gone out of use as a mainland burial form before the Bronze Age ended, so the people who came back to them were visiting somebody else's monuments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carl Blegen was the first to notice and publish the pattern, at Prosymna below the Argive Heraion, where many of the 53 chamber tombs he dug had been frequented again in the Geometric and Archaic periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He read the pottery and bronze bowls as continuous family veneration running through the Dark Age, but that continuity cannot be supported: the gap at Prosymna is real.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual explanation instead is political, that 8th-century Argos wanted conspicuous Mycenaean ancestors to validate its claims and found them in these tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The evidence is more mixed than either story allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Post-Bronze Age burials were also made in such tombs across the Argolid, at Argos, Berbati, Asine, Mycenae and Dendra, at dates running from the Submycenaean period to the Roman, which speaks less of worshipping heroes than of ordinary funerals held among the ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One specialist accordingly prefers to call it tomb cult, and to treat the visits as part of Geometric funerary ritual rather than as a cult of epic figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attica shows the same thing: at the Menidi tholos, the best preserved in the region and built in the 14th or 13th century BCE, finds in its corridor suggest honour paid to the ancestors as late as the 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Messenia it went on intermittently through the centuries of Spartan rule, and the best-explored case there, a tholos at Nichoria whose Bronze Age use ended late in the 13th century, has been published as a hero cult and read by others as a tomb reused for shelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hero cult at Bronze Age tombs","questions":["Burials of Submycenaean to Roman date inside the same tombs complicate <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a funeral among the ancestors is not the same as worship.","Argos is thought to have wanted Mycenaean forebears to back its claims, which is the political reading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 8th century."],"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Carla M. Antonaccio, \"Terraces, Tombs, and the Early Argive Heraion,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 61, no. 1 (1992): 85–105, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/148184.pdf. [Open access]","Susan E. Alcock, Andrea M. Berlin, Ann B. Harrison, Sebastian Heath, Nigel Spencer, and David L. Stone, \"Pylos Regional Archaeological Project, Part VII: Historical Messenia, Geometric through Late Roman,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 74, no. 2 (2005): 147–209, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/25067953.pdf. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Ephorate of Antiquities of West Attica, \"Tholos Tomb of Acharnai, Municipality of Acharnes,\" Hellenic Ministry of Culture and Sports, https://www.efada.gr/en-us/Archaeological-Sites-Monuments/-Catalogue-of-Sites-Monuments/tholos-tomb-of-acharnai-municipality-of-acharnes. [Open access]","William A. McDonald et al., \"Excavations at Nichoria in Messenia: 1972–1973,\" <i>Hesperia</i> 44, no. 1 (1975): 69–141, https://www.ascsa.edu.gr/uploads/media/hesperia/147426.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Hero_cult_Louvre_CA308.jpg/1920px-Hero_cult_Louvre_CA308.jpg","title":"hero cult at Bronze Age tombs","desc":"Hero cult (?); epitaph dedicated to Oedipus on the stele. Side A of a lucanian red-figure pseudo-panathenaic amphora, ca. 380–370 BCE. From Apulia.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hero_cult_Louvre_CA308.jpg","alt":"Hero cult Louvre CA308"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why can Blegen's reading of continuous family veneration not stand?","a":"Because chamber tombs had gone out of use before the Bronze Age ended and the gap at Prosymna is real, so the visitors were coming to somebody else's monuments."},{"q":"Why do some specialists prefer the term tomb cult?","a":"Because post-Bronze Age burials were made in such tombs across the Argolid at dates from the Submycenaean to the Roman, which speaks of funerals held among the ancestors rather than a cult of epic figures."},{"q":"Why is the political explanation attractive?","a":"Because 8th-century Argos wanted conspicuous Mycenaean ancestors to validate its claims, and found them in these tombs."}]},
{"id":"gr-127","num":"127","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Phemius and Demodocus are the <i>Odyssey</i>'s two portraits of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the singer who made his tale as he performed it rather than reciting one learnt by heart.","answer":"aoidos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Odyssey</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>aoidos</b> was the singer of early Greece, who performed narrative song to a stringed instrument and composed as he sang rather than reciting a text learnt by heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four appear in the <i>Odyssey</i>: Phemius in Ithaca, the blind Demodocus among the Phaeacians, and two unnamed, one attached to Agamemnon at Mycenae and one to Menelaus at Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Agamemnon left his singer watching over Clytemnestra when he sailed for Troy, and Aegisthus had him killed, a use of a singer found nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of the four lives in a house of his own: the singer belongs to somebody else's hall, and above all to a palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is as unthinkable without his instrument as a warrior without arms, and the <i>Odyssey</i> gives its singers the lyre and the kithara, while in the <i>Iliad</i> the phorminx is in the hands of Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The instrument had a long past behind it, since Mycenaean Greece already had a large concert lyre of seven strings, known from ivory fragments and painted on a wall at Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The songs were short and chosen for the moment: Demodocus sings a quarrel of Achilles and Odysseus, then the loves of Ares and Aphrodite, then the wooden horse, each on request and none near the length of the poem they sit inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is an improviser rather than a reciter, able to lengthen or shorten a tale to suit the room, and Telemachus says outright that people most applaud the newest song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The singers explained the gift by the gods: the Muse loved Demodocus, took his eyes and gave him sweet song, while Phemius, pleading for his life, called himself self-taught and said a god had planted songs of every kind in his mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those two claims are the halves of an oral poetics, a tradition received and an occasion answered by the singer alone, though the portraits are a poet's own and the later rhapsodes, reciting fixed poems, were self-taught in no such sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"aoidos","questions":["Begging for his life, Phemius called himself self-taught and said a god had planted songs in his mind, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explained his gift.","Demodocus sings a quarrel, then the loves of Ares and Aphrodite, then the wooden horse, each on request, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked at a feast."],"tags":["person","practice","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","Mauricio Vélez Upegui, \"Cantores palaciegos: un estudio sobre la figura del aedo en la Odisea,\" <i>Folios</i> 50 (2019): 83–96, https://doi.org/10.17227/folios.50-7457. [Open access]","John G. Younger, \"The Aegean Bard: Evidence for Sound and Song,\" in <i>EPOS: Reconsidering Greek Epic and Aegean Bronze Age Archaeology</i>, ed. Sarah P. Morris and Robert Laffineur, Aegaeum 28 (Liège and Austin: Université de Liège, 2007), 71–78, https://hdl.handle.net/1808/5716. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/OdysseyDemodokos.png/1920px-OdysseyDemodokos.png","title":"aoidos","desc":"A line engraving of a singer seated with his lyre before a listening company, an imagined scene of the bard Demodocus in the Odyssey.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OdysseyDemodokos.png","alt":"OdysseyDemodokos"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the singer of the Odyssey an improviser rather than a reciter?","a":"Because Demodocus sings a different short song on each request, none near the length of the poem they sit inside, lengthening or shortening a tale to suit the room."},{"q":"Why does the singer belong to a household rather than to himself?","a":"Because none of the four in the poem lives in a house of his own — the singer belongs to somebody else's hall, and above all to a palace."},{"q":"Why are the singers' two claims about their gift the halves of an oral poetics?","a":"Because Demodocus has his song from the Muse and Phemius calls himself self-taught with a god's planting — a tradition received and an occasion answered by the singer alone."}]},
{"id":"gr-128","num":"128","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Iliad and the Odyssey were credited in antiquity to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a poet about whom nothing is established, no dated life and no certain birthplace and no word from anyone who knew him.","answer":"Homer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Poems composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not much before 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pre-eminent by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">end of 6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homer</b> is the name Greek tradition attached to the <i>Iliad</i> and the <i>Odyssey</i>, and nothing about the man is established: no dated life, no certain birthplace, no word from anyone who could have known him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The poems themselves point to Ionia: their language is a mélange of Ionic, Aeolic, Mycenaean and Attic with Ionic predominant, which is where ancient testimony put his birth and his work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also give a rough date, since the objects and customs they describe mean they cannot have been composed much before 700 BCE, and they address an eighth-century audience with tales of a more confident past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The verse shows what kind of poetry this is, its constant formulae and repeated scenes coming out of a long process of oral composition and recomposition in performance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The diction was shaped by the metre, obsolete and dialect forms surviving wherever they gave a poet a useful alternative, so the Greek of the poems belongs to no one place or time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> About the man the Greeks had stories rather than records, and the surviving Lives are built from the motifs that shape other early poets' lives: exile, a strange death, a cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The longest passes itself off as the work of Herodotus, and one branch of the tradition, going back to Alcidamas around 400 BCE, has Homer beaten in a contest by Hesiod, who wrote about the works of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such biographies may have been made by schools of rhapsodes like the Homeridae on Chios, and what they record is less the poet than how widely he was read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name also covered more poetry than it does now, since the epic cycle was sometimes given to Homer, though most sources assigned those poems to younger men counted as his pupils or continuers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was settled late: by the end of the 6th century BCE the two epics had a standing the rest did not, and everything else sank to a secondary place, surviving only in fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homer","questions":["The surviving Lives of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are built from motifs shared with other early poets, exile and a strange death and a cult, rather than from anything remembered.","The epic cycle was sometimes credited to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in antiquity, though most sources gave those poems to younger men counted as his pupils or continuers."],"tags":["person","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Mary R. Lefkowitz, review of <i>Early Greek Poets' Lives: The Shaping of the Tradition</i>, by Maarit Kivilo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.38/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Townley_Homer.jpg","title":"Homer","desc":"A page of the Townley Homer, a Byzantine manuscript of the Iliad written in a small hand with the scholia crowded round the text.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Townley_Homer.jpg","alt":"Townley Homer"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why does the language of the poems belong to no one place or time?","a":"Because the diction was shaped by the metre, obsolete and dialect forms surviving wherever they gave a poet a useful alternative, in a mélange of Ionic, Aeolic, Mycenaean and Attic."},{"q":"Why are the ancient Lives of Homer poor evidence?","a":"Because they are built from the motifs that shape other early poets' lives — exile, a strange death, a cult — and the longest passes itself off as the work of Herodotus."},{"q":"Why did the name once cover more poetry than it does now?","a":"Because the epic cycle was sometimes given to Homer, and only by the end of the 6th century BCE did the two epics gain a standing that sank the rest to a secondary place."}]},
{"id":"gr-129","num":"129","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Wolf's Prolegomena of 1795 opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Parry's fieldwork among Yugoslav singers a century and a half later changed the terms it was argued in.","answer":"Homeric Question","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prolegomena</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fieldwork</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1933 – 1935</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Homeric Question</b> is the long argument over how the <i>Iliad</i> and the <i>Odyssey</i> came to be: whether one poet made them, how much of them was inherited, and how they became fixed texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its prehistory is at Alexandria, where Zenodotus, Aristophanes of Byzantium and above all Aristarchus worked over the poems in the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, marking lines they judged spurious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some ancient scholars separated the two, giving the <i>Odyssey</i> an author other than the <i>Iliad</i>'s, and Aristarchus already treated its last book and part of its underworld scene as later additions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antiquity told of a gathering of the poems at Athens: the spurious Platonic <i>Hipparchus</i> credits a son of Peisistratus with putting them into the Panathenaia and having them written down, and Diogenes Laertius credits Peisistratus or Solon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those say more about the founding of that recitation than about how the text came down, and the modern argument opens with Friedrich August Wolf, whose <i>Prolegomena ad Homerum</i> of 1795 put the author in question and saw that the epic began with singers, not writers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Analysts of the 19th century took that method further, looking for the seams between the shorter poems they believed had been stitched together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The terms changed with Milman Parry, who joined a study of Homeric diction to fieldwork on the oral epic of Yugoslavia in 1933–5, and with Albert Lord, whose <i>Singer of Tales</i> set out the theory and carried composition in performance well beyond classics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The singers showed that each performance is an act of making rather than of reproduction: the same song, never the same words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That settled most of the question, the poems being song made and remade in performance, and few doubt their conventions were formed where nobody wrote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is unsettled is how they became texts, proposals running from a singer dictating to a slow hardening over generations, one seating that singer in 6th-century Athens on evidence reviewers found too confident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homeric Question","questions":["Aristarchus at Alexandria already marked the last book of the <i>Odyssey</i> as a later addition, which is an ancient anticipation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its analysts.","Nobody now doubts that the poems come out of an oral tradition, so what is left of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is how they ever became fixed written texts."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","Minna Skafte Jensen, review of <i>The Singer of Tales</i>, 3rd ed., by Albert Bates Lord, ed. David F. Elmer, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.09/. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part II,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 3, no. 1–2 (1988): 11–60, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/3i-ii/3_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Jonathan Burgess, review of <i>Tales of Heroes: The Origins of the Homeric Texts</i>, by Ione Mylonas Shear, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.10.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.10.18/. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_061.jpg/1920px-Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_061.jpg","title":"Homeric Question","desc":"One of two surviving painting of a series of seven by Rembrandt, Guercino, Mattia Preti, Salvator Rosa, and Giacinto Brandi. Parts were removed from all four sides as a result of fire damage, following an earthquake that struck Antonio Ruffo's palace in Messina in 1783.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_061.jpg","alt":"Rembrandt Harmensz. van Rijn 061"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did fieldwork on Yugoslav singers change the terms of the question?","a":"Because it showed each performance to be an act of making rather than reproduction — the same song, never the same words — which settled that the poems' conventions were formed where nobody wrote."},{"q":"Why are the ancient stories of an Athenian gathering poor evidence?","a":"Because they say more about the founding of that recitation at the Panathenaia than about how the text came down."},{"q":"Why does part of the question remain unsettled?","a":"Because how the poems became texts is still argued, proposals running from a singer dictating to a slow hardening over generations."}]},
{"id":"gr-130","quote":{"book":"homer-iliad","n":"1","cite":"Book 1.1-7","text":"<p>The wrath sing, goddess, of Peleus' son, Achilles, that destructive wrath which brought countless woes upon the Achaeans, and sent forth to Hades many valiant souls of heroes, and made them themselves spoil for dogs and every bird; thus the plan of Zeus came to fulfillment, from the time when first they parted in strife Atreus' son, king of men, and brilliant Achilles.</p>"},"num":"130","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The first word names the anger of Achilles, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follows that anger through a quarrel, a friend's death and a duel, stopping short of Troy's fall.","answer":"Iliad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not much before 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book division</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Iliad</b> is the older of the two Homeric epics, and its subject is not the Trojan war but the anger of one man, <i>menis</i> being its first word and the theme that word names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its spine is the quarrel of the chiefs in the first book, the defeat of the Greeks in the eleventh, the killing of Patroclus in the sixteenth and the death of Hector in the twenty-second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Achilles withdraws from the fighting after that quarrel, and it is his grief at the death of Patroclus, his closest friend, that brings him back to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end of the war lies outside the poem altogether, with no wooden horse and no fall of Troy, that matter belonging to the other epics of the Trojan cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its narrator keeps an even, objective tone throughout, where the <i>Odyssey</i> hands whole books to its hero to tell his own adventures like a bard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The division into 24 books is not the poet's but Alexandrian, one book to each letter of the Greek alphabet, and papyri older than Aristarchus carry no sign of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Readers have long felt other joints in it, and Oliver Taplin reads the poem as three long movements, each about the length of one evening's performance, treating book 10 as a later intrusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Analysts before him suspected that same book, with the embassy to Achilles and one of the battles, of having been added to something shorter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What nobody disputes is the length, which is hard to account for by the festival occasions a singer would ordinarily have had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was learnt by heart as well as heard, since Xenophon has a rich Athenian say his father made him memorise both poems to turn him into a man of worth, and Simonides could quote a line of it and expect it recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Iliad","questions":["The division of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into 24 books is Alexandrian rather than the poet's, and papyri older than Aristarchus carry no sign of it.","Xenophon has a rich Athenian say his father made him learn the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its companion by heart so that he would become a man of worth."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","S. Douglas Olson, review of <i>Homeric Soundings: The Shaping of the Iliad</i>, by Oliver Taplin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.01.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.01.21/. [Open access]","Michael Lynn-George, review of <i>The Anger of Achilles: Menis in Greek Epic</i>, by Leonard Charles Muellner, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.02.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.02.10/. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Achilles_weapons_MNA_Naples.jpg/1920px-Achilles_weapons_MNA_Naples.jpg","title":"Iliad","desc":"Thetis at Hephaestos' forge waiting to receive Achilles' new weapons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achilles_weapons_MNA_Naples.jpg","alt":"Achilles weapons MNA Naples"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why is the poem's subject not the Trojan war?","a":"Because menis, anger, is its first word and its theme, and the end of the war lies outside it altogether — no wooden horse and no fall of Troy."},{"q":"Why is the division into 24 books not the poet's?","a":"Because it is Alexandrian, one book to each letter of the Greek alphabet, and papyri older than Aristarchus carry no sign of it."},{"q":"Why is the poem's length hard to account for?","a":"Because it far exceeds what the festival occasions a singer would ordinarily have had could hold."}]},
{"id":"gr-131","num":"131","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Four books follow Telemachus before his father appears, and in the middle of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the hero takes over the telling himself for four books more.","answer":"Odyssey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not much before 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book division</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Odyssey</b> is the second of the two Homeric epics and tells the return of one man from Troy, a subject it shares with a whole family of poems, since the cycle's <i>Nostoi</i> gave the returns of the other Greeks and left Odysseus out as meriting an epic to himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into halves, books 1 to 12 carrying the hero out to a world of wonders and 13 to 24 bringing him home to Ithaca, each half in three movements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Four books follow Telemachus before his father appears at all, and the last four are revenge, reunion and reconciliation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the middle the hero takes the telling over, books 9 to 12 being his own account of the wanderings given to the Phaeacians, so that the narrator's voice mixes with his and he recounts his exploits like a bard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The poem draws a distinction there, since its own singers sing traditional matter where Odysseus draws on what he says happened to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Once home he tells further tales that are deliberately false, the longest to the swineherd Eumaeus, a story assembled out of 48 narrative elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The poem also revises itself as it goes, laying Agamemnon's murder at first on Aegisthus alone, and only the two scenes among the dead, in books 11 and 24, bring Clytemnestra into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ending was doubted early, Aristarchus treating the last book and part of the first of those scenes as later additions, and the Analysts doubting the opening four books as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They took it, on the whole, for the work of a single poet other than the <i>Iliad</i>'s, more unified in structure than that poem and yet fuller of internal inconsistency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The story did not stop with it, for the cycle's <i>Telegony</i> opened on the burial of the suitors, sent Odysseus off again to make the sacrifices Teiresias had ordered, and married him in Thesprotia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Odyssey","questions":["Odysseus tells the swineherd Eumaeus a long and deliberate lie, one of several false tales that make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a poem about storytelling as well as homecoming.","The cycle's <i>Nostoi</i> told the returns of every Greek but Odysseus, who was held to merit the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a monumental epic of his own."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","Silvio Bär, review of <i>Reading Homer's Odyssey</i>, by Kostas Myrsiades, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.05/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Odyssey-crop.jpg/1920px-Odyssey-crop.jpg","title":"Odyssey","desc":"Homer, Odyssey manuscript. Date: 3rd quarter of the 15th century.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odyssey-crop.jpg","alt":"Odyssey-crop"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why does the poem hand four books to its own hero?","a":"Because books 9 to 12 are his account of the wanderings given to the Phaeacians, so he recounts his exploits like a bard and the narrator's voice mixes with his."},{"q":"Why does the poem draw a line between its singers and its hero?","a":"Because its own singers sing traditional matter where Odysseus draws on what he says happened to him — and once home he tells tales that are deliberately false."},{"q":"Why does the poem appear to revise itself as it goes?","a":"Because it lays Agamemnon's murder at first on Aegisthus alone, and only the two scenes among the dead bring Clytemnestra into it."}]},
{"id":"gr-132","num":"132","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Six feet to the line, each a dactyl or a spondee, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the verse Greek epic was made in and elegy and drama borrowed afterwards.","answer":"dactylic hexameter","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epic verse from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cola described</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dactylic hexameter</b> is the verse of Greek epic, six feet to the line, each a dactyl, one heavy syllable and two light, or a spondee, two heavy ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is quantitative rather than accentual, built on the length of syllables and not on stress, and so hard to hear for speakers whose own verse runs on beats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since Hermann Fränkel's study of 1926 the line has been read not as six feet but as a run of cola, the word-groups that are its real compositional units, separated by sense-breaks or caesurae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He found four cola and three caesurae in every Homeric verse, the first break having four possible positions and the other two having two each, so the parts come out in different shapes from line to line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That variety is the point, as two lines of Longfellow's <i>Hiawatha</i> run to about the same length with four identical cola, three fixed breaks and almost no flexibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A word's metrical shape therefore decides where in the line it can go: 'Achaeans' sits mostly in the second half and takes an epithet to help it fit, where 'Trojans', 'Argives' and 'Danaans' are more accommodating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditional phrases are cut to the same measure, each shaped to fill a particular stretch of the line, which is what let a singer build his verses at speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a listener hears comes as much from the placing of words as from the feet: Dionysius of Halicarnassus praised the line where Sisyphus's rock rolls back for holding nothing but dactyls, though about one Homeric line in five does, and missed the four word-breaks splitting its pairs of short syllables.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the metre came from is still argued, derivations having been proposed from a lyric pattern expanded by dactyls, from an Indo-European ancestor shared with Vedic and Iranian verse, and from the dactylo-epitrite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It long outlived the epic, since Hesiod, the elegists, Attic drama, Apollonius, Theocritus and the Greek Christian poets all wrote in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"dactylic hexameter","questions":["One influential reading takes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as four word-groups divided by three sense-breaks rather than as six feet, which changed how Homeric verse is analysed.","A word's metrical shape decides where it can sit in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why 'Achaeans' keeps to the second half of the line."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Margalit Finkelberg, review of <i>Struttura e storia dell'esametro greco</i>, ed. Marco Fantuzzi and Roberto Pretagostini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.07/. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part II,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 3, no. 1–2 (1988): 11–60, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/3i-ii/3_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","John Gibert, review of <i>Hesperos: Studies in Ancient Greek Poetry Presented to M. L. West on his Seventieth Birthday</i>, ed. Patrick Finglass, Christopher Collard and Nicholas Richardson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/IliadScansion.png","title":"dactylic hexameter","desc":"\"The Fight for the Body of Patroclus\" by John Flaxman, with superimposed scansion marks representing the last half of a line of dactylic hexameter, and incidentally reflecting the 7 characters in the image.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IliadScansion.png","alt":"IliadScansion"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the line read as cola rather than as six feet?","a":"Because Fränkel found four cola and three caesurae in every Homeric verse, with the breaks falling in different positions, so the parts come out in different shapes from line to line."},{"q":"Why does a word's shape decide where it can stand?","a":"Because the verse is quantitative, so 'Achaeans' sits mostly in the second half and takes an epithet to help it fit where 'Trojans' and 'Argives' are more accommodating."},{"q":"Why did Dionysius's praise of one famous line miss something?","a":"Because he praised it for holding nothing but dactyls, though about one Homeric line in five does, and he missed the four word-breaks splitting its pairs of short syllables."}]},
{"id":"gr-133","num":"133","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A singer could build a line fast because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave him a phrase of the right length for every hero and every action.","answer":"formulaic composition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Homeric epic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1928</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Formulaic composition</b> is the technique Milman Parry found in Homer, a formula being a group of words used regularly under the same metrical conditions to express one essential idea, and the formulas together making a system a singer could compose in without writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its clearest case is a name with its epithet: Parry counted four ways of saying Odysseus in the nominative, <i>dios Odysseus</i>, 'godlike Odysseus', 60 times, <i>polymetis Odysseus</i>, 'of many wiles', 81, and two longer phrases 38 and 4 times, each of a different length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Swap in another figure whose phrase is the same length, <i>phaidimos Hektor</i> or <i>Pallas Athene</i>, and the line still scans, so one pattern serves a whole cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The system is at once extensive and thrifty, covering a great range of ideas with little duplication between entries of the same sense and length, which one recent account restates as a nucleus with a peripheral element attached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the epithets are called ornamental: they are chosen for the metre rather than for the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Parry set it out in his monograph of 1928, with tables of the name-and-epithet phrases falling after the mid-verse break and after the break in the fourth foot, for eleven gods and heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second unit sits above the formula, the type-scene, identified by a scholar who did not share Parry's view of oral composition at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The strength of the case was its thoroughness: the whole system of formulas and the use of epithets can already be made out in the master's thesis he wrote in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later work loosened the definition, Hainsworth dropping the metrical conditions and Hoekstra showing that linguistic innovation flexed the diction, the first proof that a late-looking form need not be a later insertion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That cost the formula some of its force as evidence, since a definition vague enough to cover everything stops indicating oral origin, and metricians have called Parry's own inexact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"formulaic composition","questions":["Parry counted four nominative phrases for Odysseus, each a different length, which is the clearest evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Homeric poems.","The repetitions that irritated the Alexandrians and the analysts turn out to be the technical basis of the verse, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part II,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 3, no. 1–2 (1988): 11–60, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/3i-ii/3_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","James Bradley Wells, review of <i>Pointing at the Past: From Formula to Performance in Homeric Poetics</i>, by Egbert J. Bakker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.06.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.06.28/. [Open access]","Margalit Finkelberg, review of <i>Struttura e storia dell'esametro greco</i>, ed. Marco Fantuzzi and Roberto Pretagostini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.07/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are the epithets called ornamental?","a":"Because they are chosen for the metre rather than for the moment — Parry counted four ways of saying Odysseus in the nominative, each of a different length."},{"q":"Why does one metrical pattern serve a whole cast?","a":"Because swapping in another figure whose phrase is the same length, phaidimos Hektor or Pallas Athene, leaves the line still scanning."},{"q":"Why did later work cost the formula some of its force as evidence?","a":"Because Hainsworth dropped the metrical conditions and a definition vague enough to cover everything stops indicating oral origin."}]},
{"id":"gr-134","num":"134","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pindar calls the Homeridai singers of stitched verses, which is a clue both to what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was and to what the name may mean.","answer":"rhapsode","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Syracuse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 504 – 501 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>rhapsode</b> was the professional reciter of Homer in archaic and classical Greece, a performer of poems already settled rather than a singer making his tale as he went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name may come from the verb for stitching, and Pindar calls the Homeridai singers of 'stitched verses' who begin most often from Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those Homeridai of Chios were in effect a guild of such professionals, said at first to be Homer's own descendants and later merely performers with no claim of blood, though everything in their repertoire was still put down to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scholiast on that passage of Pindar names Cynaethus of Chios among them, credited with composing many verses and slipping them into Homer's poetry, and with the first recitation of Homer at Syracuse around the 69th Olympiad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Martin West went further and suggested that such a company could have invented the ancestral Homer, the blindness a plausible touch and the name perhaps formed from their own rather than the other way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They performed in succession, one man taking up the poetry where the last had left it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also travelled, so whatever local version a school of them carried did not stay fixed in one place for long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their delivery seems to have been closer to recitative than to song, on one account of how the performance of epic differed from that of lyric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What they had lost was the older singer's freedom, since once writing had settled the texts, memorising and reciting took the place of improvising, and no rhapsode called himself self-taught as Phemius does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are nonetheless part of the answer to how the poems reached us at all, repeated recomposition by rhapsodes being one of the explanations offered for their fixing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"rhapsode","questions":["Cynaethus of Chios is said to have been the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to recite Homer at Syracuse, around the 69th Olympiad, and to have inserted verses of his own.","Once writing had settled the poems, memorising and reciting them replaced making them up, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was no longer self-taught as the old singers claimed to be."],"tags":["title","practice","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","Mary R. Lefkowitz, review of <i>Early Greek Poets' Lives: The Shaping of the Tradition</i>, by Maarit Kivilo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.38/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","Ralph Rosen, review of <i>Pindar's Homer: The Lyric Possession of an Epic Past</i>, by Gregory Nagy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1991.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1991/1991.01.13/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/%28Venice%29_Rhapsode_by_Giandomenico_Tiepolo.jpg/1920px-%28Venice%29_Rhapsode_by_Giandomenico_Tiepolo.jpg","title":"rhapsode","desc":"(Venice) Rhapsode by Giandomenico Tiepolo. Didier Descouens. Taken on 8 May 2024.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Rhapsode_by_Giandomenico_Tiepolo.jpg","alt":"(Venice) Rhapsode by Giandomenico Tiepolo"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why had these performers lost the older singer's freedom?","a":"Because once writing had settled the texts, memorising and reciting took the place of improvising, and no rhapsode called himself self-taught as Phemius does."},{"q":"Why did West suggest the Homeridai may have invented Homer?","a":"Because a company of this kind could have produced the ancestral figure, the blindness a plausible touch and the name perhaps formed from their own rather than the other way about."},{"q":"Why does their travelling matter to the text?","a":"Because whatever local version a school of them carried did not stay fixed in one place for long."}]},
{"id":"gr-135","num":"135","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Households, gifts, assemblies and chiefs make up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and scholars have dated that world anywhere from Mycenae to the poet's own century.","answer":"Homeric society","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Poems' date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World depicted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Bronze Age to 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Homeric society</b> is the world the poems describe, its exchange of gifts and its ranks of chiefs and followers, and the standing question about it is whose world it actually is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The poems hold memories of the Bronze Age, yet the objects and customs they take for granted cannot be much older than 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scholars have accordingly placed that society in the Mycenaean age, in the Dark Age, in the 8th century and in the 7th, and the editors of one conference volume on the subject admitted outright that their contributors took sharply different views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of those papers argued for the 7th century, one of them from the peoples and places the poems name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third observed that the <i>Iliad</i> gives the Mycenaean period no historical date at all, and treats the age of the heroes as a splendour cut off from the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The strongest case for the Bronze Age has been built from the furniture of the poems: houses compared with Mycenaean architecture, weapons and clothing, the Catalogue of Ships, and the Phaeacians read as a hierarchy of the Linear B kind rather than as an early polis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has not carried the field, its reviewer finding the arguments well short of their confident rhetoric, though he granted that opinion had swung a long way against Bronze Age origins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Part of the trouble is that an oral tradition carries the old and the new along together, so a late-looking feature need not be a late addition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The poet is also speaking to his own listeners about a past he presents as better ordered and more confident than their present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lefkandi is the whole difficulty in miniature, its great building assigned by turns to the early Mycenaean period, to the earlier Iron Age and to the late 8th century, and each of the three claimed as decisive for the poems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Homeric society","questions":["The <i>Iliad</i> gives the Mycenaean age no historical date at all, which is one reason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is so hard to place in time.","An oral tradition carries old and new together, so a late-looking detail in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> need not be a late addition to the poems."],"tags":["concept","history","literature","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]","Jonathan Burgess, review of <i>Tales of Heroes: The Origins of the Homeric Texts</i>, by Ione Mylonas Shear, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.10.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.10.18/. [Open access]","Carmine Pisano, review of <i>Homer</i>, by Barbara Graziosi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.12.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.12.16/. [Open access]","Gustavo Frade, \"Homero e a questão homérica,\" <i>Em Tese</i> 23, no. 3 (2017): 209–36, https://doi.org/10.17851/1982-0739.23.3.209-236. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why can the poems' society not be dated straightforwardly?","a":"Because they hold memories of the Bronze Age while the objects and customs they take for granted cannot be much older than 700 BCE."},{"q":"Why does an oral tradition make the question harder?","a":"Because it carries the old and the new along together, so a late-looking feature need not be a late addition."},{"q":"Why is Lefkandi the difficulty in miniature?","a":"Because its great building has been assigned by turns to the early Mycenaean period, the earlier Iron Age and the late 8th century, and each of the three claimed as decisive."}]},
{"id":"gr-136","num":"136","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The whole of the <i>Odyssey</i> turns on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the household held together in its master's absence and taken back from the suitors by force.","answer":"oikos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>oikos</b> was the household, the basic unit of Greek social and economic life, meaning at once the house, the land that fed it, the property it worked and the people in it, free and enslaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What mattered in practice were the relationships inside it, husband and wife, parent and child, master and slave, rather than the long descent lines that older scholarship put first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A holding of about 12 hectares (30 acres) was reckoned enough to support a slave in Aristotle's ideal city, though most Greek states were made up of small farmers who had none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A household that hired its labour instead counted as incomplete, on the reasoning that a slave belonged to the master where a hired man did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Homer the oikos is the thing at stake, since the <i>Odyssey</i> is the story of one household kept together through its master's absence and taken back by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The suitors eat their way through it for years, and their killing and burial are the last thing the poem describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its second half moves between the hut of the swineherd and the hall of the king, two households of the same kind at very different scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wealth also moved between households as gifts, a traffic long thought to be what held early Greek society together, though the assumption has been re-examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The old story that the rise of the city swept the household aside has been refuted, since the family stayed fundamental throughout and the polis could not have emerged without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two were held together rather than opposed, and households were expected to reproduce in miniature the constitution of the city they belonged to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"oikos","questions":["What mattered inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the ties of husband and wife, parent and child, master and slave, rather than any long line of descent.","The rise of the polis did not sweep the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> away, and the city could not have emerged without the household's continued strength."],"tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Tim Parkin, review of <i>The Family in Greek History</i>, by Cynthia Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.06.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.06.07/. [Open access]","Cathal Woods, review of <i>The Household as the Foundation of Aristotle's Polis</i>, by D. Brendan Nagle, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.11.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.11.14/. [Open access]","Silvio Bär, review of <i>Reading Homer's Odyssey</i>, by Kostas Myrsiades, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.05/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Ancient_Olynthos_Chalkidiki_-_Greece_-_048.jpg","title":"oikos","desc":"Reconstructed remains of ancient city of Olynthos, Chalkidiki, Greece (northern hill of ancient town). Hippodamic town layout (rectangular streets and housing blocks) visible.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Olynthos_Chalkidiki_-_Greece_-_048.jpg","alt":"Ancient Olynthos Chalkidiki - Greece - 048"},"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why was a household that hired its labour counted incomplete?","a":"Because a slave belonged to the master where a hired man did not, so the household lacked a member rather than a worker."},{"q":"Why is the Odyssey a poem about the oikos?","a":"Because it is the story of one household kept together through its master's absence and taken back by force, its second half moving between a swineherd's hut and a king's hall."},{"q":"Why has the old story of the household giving way to the city been refuted?","a":"Because the family stayed fundamental throughout and the polis could not have emerged without it, households being expected to reproduce the city's constitution in miniature."}]},
{"id":"gr-137","num":"137","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A stranger at the door was owed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the <i>Odyssey</i> uses that obligation to judge the moral worth of nearly everyone in it.","answer":"xenia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xenia</b> was the guest-friendship binding Greek households to one another, a code of hospitality owed to the stranger who arrived at the door and answered in gifts that passed between the two houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the <i>Odyssey</i> it is one of the three themes that hold the poem together, beside homecoming and identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also works as a touchstone, a standard by which almost every figure in the epic can be judged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Telemachus's journey is accordingly a tour of houses, the good order of Pylos set against the chaos he left at home, and Menelaus and Helen receiving their guest and recognising him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The suitors are placed by the same measure, since they prepare to welcome Telemachus with an ambush, and the killing of them is where the poem ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Phaeacians are harder to place, unusual in the prominence they give to Arete and ambiguous in their attitude to strangers even as they carry Odysseus home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The worst breach of all is the Cyclops, whose meeting with Odysseus has been read as a contest between civilisation and something older and cruder, a division the poem sharpens with similes drawn from shipbuilding and the smith's craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That scene had a long afterlife, and Euripides made a satyr play of it in which hospitality is among the words to watch, the Cyclops turning from monster into gourmand and philosopher while Odysseus comes off as a cynical agnostic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gifts moving between houses were long treated as the mechanism that held such a society together, an assumption more recent work has questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The swineherd's hut shows that the code needed no wealth, since four books of the poem pass there and the loyalty shown a beggar is the point of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"xenia","questions":["By the standard of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the poem settles what it thinks of the suitors, who prepare to welcome Telemachus home with an ambush.","Euripides turned the Cyclops into a satyr play in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a word to watch and the monster becomes a gourmand and a philosopher."],"tags":["practice","concept","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["David Pollio, review of <i>A Companion to Homer's Odyssey</i>, by James V. Morrison, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.07.04/. [Open access]","Ian C. Storey, review of <i>Euripides: Cyclops. A Satyr Play</i>, by Carl A. Shaw, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.24/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]","Silvio Bär, review of <i>Reading Homer's Odyssey</i>, by Kostas Myrsiades, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.05/. [Open access]","Ingrid Holmberg, \"The Creation of the Ancient Greek Epic Cycle,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 13, no. 2 (1998): 456–478, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/13ii/10_holmberg.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does the code work as a touchstone in the poem?","a":"Because almost every figure in the Odyssey can be judged by how they treat a guest — Pylos against the chaos at home, the suitors preparing an ambush, the Cyclops at the far end."},{"q":"Why does the swineherd's hut matter to the theme?","a":"Because four books pass there and the loyalty shown a beggar is the point of them, so the code needed no wealth."},{"q":"Why are the Phaeacians harder to place than the rest?","a":"Because they are unusual in the prominence they give to Arete and ambiguous in their attitude to strangers even as they carry Odysseus home."}]},
{"id":"gr-138","num":"138","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A hero risked his life in battle for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the fame that outlives him, and it exists only for as long as somebody goes on singing it.","answer":"kleos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Kleos</b> is the fame a Greek hero won and left behind him, the return for which a man would put his life at risk in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word means something heard, and it is fixed in the epic formula <i>kleos aphthiton</i>, 'imperishable fame'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gregory Nagy matched that phrase with a Vedic expression, <i>śravas akṣitam</i>, and traced both to an Indo-European ancestor, which would make the idea older than the Greek language, though the correspondence has not convinced everyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heroism itself is measured in the poems by lineage, by era and by behaviour, and risking one's life in a fight is its defining act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Odysseus is a hero of another kind, tricky rather than simply brave, which is part of why the <i>Odyssey</i> reads so differently from the poem of Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The catch is that kleos exists only in the singing of it, and the Muse stirs Demodocus to sing the glories of men, a tale whose fame goes up into the broad sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phemius makes the point plainly when he pleads for his life, since alive he can go on singing the praises of a godlike Odysseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hero's immortality is therefore in the singer's keeping, which is a good argument for not killing the singer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later poetry took the idea over, and epic and Pindar's victory odes are both fundamentally concerned with kleos, the odes justifying their praise of a living athlete by reference to the praise of dead heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That praise poetry sets out to close the gap between the heroic past and the historic present, and even Herodotus has been read as carrying on the same business of conveying fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"kleos","questions":["The formula for imperishable fame has been matched with a Vedic phrase, which would make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> older than the Greek language itself.","Epinician and epic are both about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since Pindar's victory odes praise a living athlete by way of the dead heroes of the past."],"tags":["concept","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mark W. Edwards, \"Homer and Oral Tradition: The Formula, Part I,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 1, no. 2 (1986): 171–230, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/1ii/2_edwards.pdf. [Open access]","William C. Scott, \"Oral Verse-Making in Homer's Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 4, no. 3 (1989): 382–412, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/4iii/6_scott.pdf. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","David Pollio, review of <i>A Companion to Homer's Odyssey</i>, by James V. Morrison, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.07.04/. [Open access]","Ralph Rosen, review of <i>Pindar's Homer: The Lyric Possession of an Epic Past</i>, by Gregory Nagy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1991.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1991/1991.01.13/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does a hero's immortality depend on the singer?","a":"Because kleos exists only in the singing of it, which is why Phemius, pleading for his life, argues that alive he can go on praising a godlike Odysseus."},{"q":"Why has the phrase been traced beyond Greek?","a":"Because Nagy matched kleos aphthiton with the Vedic śravas akṣitam and traced both to an Indo-European ancestor, though the correspondence has not convinced everyone."},{"q":"Why do victory odes reach back to dead heroes?","a":"Because praise poetry justifies its praise of a living athlete by reference to theirs, setting out to close the gap between the heroic past and the historic present."}]},
{"id":"gr-139","num":"139","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"In the Linear B tablets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> names men well down the hierarchy, and only after the palaces fell did the word come to mean the leading man.","answer":"basileus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Iron Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Basileus</b> is the Greek word that later means king, but in the Bronze Age tablets it belongs to people a long way down the hierarchy, the word for the king himself being wanax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Oswyn Murray set that single Mycenaean wanax against the many basileis found at the end of the Dark Age, heads of noble families rather than monarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The contrast is not as clean as it looks, since the tablets themselves may imply almost as many wanaktes in Mycenaean times as there were basileis afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word's descent has been followed from the Mycenaean qa-si-re-u through Archaic Cyprus and the Assyrian records, which is one of the few continuous threads across the collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the title meant once the palaces were gone was settled locally, as leaders reinvented themselves in the break between the Submycenaean and the Protogeometric.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Such a man's standing rested on more than fighting, and keeping trade open, with it the supply of metal and fine objects, and giving banquets have been argued to count for as much as prowess in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Access to the sea and the means of travelling it were central to how these elites formed, in the Euboean Gulf above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Religion belonged to the same structure of power, with hero cult in the Early Iron Age tied to it and the mound at Toumba standing at the centre of the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Homer's world is full of such men, and the poems have even been read as commissioned by 8th-century kings to support the idea of kingship itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether the society they head is an early polis or a hierarchy of the older Linear B kind is precisely what remains in dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"basileus","questions":["Keeping trade open and giving banquets are argued to have mattered as much as fighting to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Early Iron Age.","The single Mycenaean wanax set against the many later heads of noble families is the usual way of introducing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Early Iron Age."],"tags":["title","institution","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Silke Knippschild, review of <i>Ancient Greece: From the Mycenaean Palaces to the Age of Homer</i>, ed. Sigrid Deger-Jalkotzy and Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.39/. [Open access]","Edmund Bloedow, review of <i>Early Greece</i>, by Oswyn Murray, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.01/. [Open access]","David Pollio, review of <i>A Companion to Homer's Odyssey</i>, by James V. Morrison, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.07.04/. [Open access]","Jonathan Burgess, review of <i>Tales of Heroes: The Origins of the Homeric Texts</i>, by Ione Mylonas Shear, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.10.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.10.18/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Homer's World: Fiction, Tradition, Reality</i>, ed. Øivind Andersen and Matthew Dickie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.05.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.05.02/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the contrast between one wanax and many basileis not as clean as it looks?","a":"Because the tablets themselves may imply almost as many wanaktes in Mycenaean times as there were basileis afterwards."},{"q":"Why did such a man's standing rest on more than fighting?","a":"Because keeping trade open, and with it the supply of metal and fine objects, and giving banquets have been argued to count for as much as prowess in war."},{"q":"Why does the word matter beyond its own meaning?","a":"Because its descent from the Mycenaean qa-si-re-u through Archaic Cyprus and the Assyrian records is one of the few continuous threads across the collapse."}]},
{"id":"gr-140","num":"140","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Almost everything antiquity knew about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was read back out of his own verse, including the Muses teaching him song on Helicon.","answer":"Hesiod","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hesiod</b> is the first Greek poet to say much about himself, and almost everything later writers knew of his life was read back out of his own verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He tells how the Muses taught him fine song beneath Helicon while he was tending his flocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also claims a victory in a poetic contest, lines that later readers took as a boast of having beaten Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That grew into the <i>Certamen</i>, a text of the 2nd century CE whose sources go back at least to Alcidamas around 400 BCE, and in which Hesiod wins because he had written about the works of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The opposition of war to farming is the one stable element across the many versions of that contest, though they differ over whether the judge is a king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His death was told as a Locrian legend, murder by two brothers who believed he had seduced their sister, and another tradition made him twice young and twice buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later writers built him elaborate genealogies linking him to Orpheus or to Atlas, filling in an ancestry his poems had left blank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What antiquity actually took from him was authority, since he is quoted on justice and the city, on moderation and on dealings between people, and his standing rests on knowledge rather than on inspiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is a paradox in that reception, because Greek tradition made him the poet of farming and the seasons while quoting his farming almanac and his calendar of days comparatively little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Studies of the archaic hexameter treat the <i>Iliad</i> and his two poems as one body of verse, and some scholars doubt that an individual Hesiod ever existed at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Hesiod","questions":["War against farming is the one stable element across the versions of the contest in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> defeats Homer before a judge.","Greek tradition made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the poet of farming and the seasons, and then quoted his farming almanac and his calendar of days remarkably little."],"tags":["person","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mary R. Lefkowitz, review of <i>Early Greek Poets' Lives: The Shaping of the Tradition</i>, by Maarit Kivilo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.38/. [Open access]","Ruth Scodel, review of <i>Hesiod: The Other Poet. Ancient Reception of a Cultural Icon</i>, by Hugo H. Koning, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.10.05/. [Open access]","Carol Dougherty, \"Phemius' Last Stand: The Impact of Occasion on Tradition in the Odyssey,\" <i>Oral Tradition</i> 6, no. 1 (1991): 93–103, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/6i/9_dougherty.pdf. [Open access]","Margalit Finkelberg, review of <i>Struttura e storia dell'esametro greco</i>, ed. Marco Fantuzzi and Roberto Pretagostini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.07/. [Open access]","Hugo Koning, review of <i>Hesiodic Voices: Studies in the Ancient Reception of Hesiod's Works and Days</i>, by Richard Hunter, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.10.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.10.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Hesiod_and_the_Muse.jpg","title":"Hesiod","desc":"Gustave Moreau's painting of Hesiod and the Muse, the poet walking with a winged Muse at his shoulder.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hesiod_and_the_Muse.jpg","alt":"Hesiod and the Muse"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is almost everything known of his life circular?","a":"Because later writers read it back out of his own verse — the Muses on Helicon, the contest victory — and built genealogies to fill an ancestry his poems left blank."},{"q":"Why did the contest story settle on the works of peace?","a":"Because the opposition of war to farming is the one stable element across its many versions, though they differ over whether the judge is a king."},{"q":"Why is his reception paradoxical?","a":"Because Greek tradition made him the poet of farming and the seasons while quoting his farming almanac and his calendar of days comparatively little."}]},
{"id":"gr-141","num":"141","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Chaos, Gaia and Eros stand at the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose line of divine rule then passes by castration and swallowing until Zeus settles it.","answer":"Theogony","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,022 lines</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Theogony</b> is the hexameter poem that gives the Greek gods a single family tree, running from the earliest powers down to the settled kingship of Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its narrator does not merely call on the Muses but is given something by them, a god-like voice and a staff, together with a riddling claim about truth that has troubled readers ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the head of it stand Chaos, Gaia and Eros, and what Chaos means is still argued over, whether a gap between heaven and earth, a being that itself gives rise to further gods, or both at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tartaros belongs among those first beings too, and later fathers Typhon on Gaia, so that the poem carries an agent of disorder inside its own order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rule then changes hands by violence, Kronos castrating Ouranos and swallowing his own children until Rhea contrives to save Zeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Jenny Strauss Clay argued that theology trumps chronology in how the material is arranged, and that the moments where a genealogy is anticipated or held back are where the poet's choices show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Gaia's revenge the poem doubles back to catalogue the children of Night and of Strife, underlining its cycles of generation, violence and deception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zeus's manoeuvring reads as an example set for human kings, and the whole has been taken as an intensely political poem about the city rather than a compendium of inherited theology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Babylonian <i>Enuma Elish</i> tells a story of the same shape, ending in a storm god's victory that ties order to kingship, and castration, absent from the Ugaritic texts but present in the Hurro-Hittite Kumarbi cycle, has been used to argue an Anatolian route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How far any of that was really transmitted is unproven, and the text is unsettled in smaller ways as well, several verses being judged spurious, the ending deleted by some editors and defended by others, and the date of composition pulled later than it once was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Theogony","questions":["Because the Babylonian <i>Enuma Elish</i> tells a story of much the same shape, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has long been read against the theogonies of the Near East.","Theology is argued to trump chronology in the arrangement of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose held-back genealogies are where the poet's own choices show most clearly."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Cashman Kerr Prince, review of <i>Hesiod's Cosmos</i>, by Jenny Strauss Clay, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.23/. [Open access]","Wm. Blake Tyrrell, review of <i>The Narrative Voice in the Theogony of Hesiod</i>, by Kathryn Stoddard, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005-01-13.html. [Open access]","Carolina López Ruiz, review of <i>Chaos, Cosmos and Creation in Early Greek Theogonies: An Ontological Exploration</i>, by Olaf Almqvist, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.16/. [Open access]","Carolina López-Ruiz, review of <i>Cosmologies et cosmogonies dans la littérature antique</i>, ed. Pascale Derron, Entretiens sur l'Antiquité classique 61, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.05.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.05.39/. [Open access]","Mehmet-Ali Ataç, review of <i>When the Gods Were Born: Greek Cosmogonies and the Near East</i>, by Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.12.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.12.11. [Open access]","Richard Janko, review of <i>Hesiod: Theogony, Works and Days, Testimonia</i>, ed. and trans. Glenn W. Most, Loeb Classical Library 57, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.03.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.31/. [Open access]","R. B. Rutherford, review of <i>Hellenica: Selected Papers on Greek Literature and Thought, Volume I: Epic</i>, by M. L. West, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.11.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.11.33. [Open access]","Robert Lamberton, review of <i>Hesiod's 'Theogony', From Near Eastern Creation Myths to 'Paradise Lost'</i>, by Stephen Scully, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.05.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.05.25/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Hesiod%2C_Theogony%2C_Venice%2C_Gr._464.jpg","title":"Theogony","desc":"Hesiod, Theogony (lines 566-586, detailing the creation of Pandora as punishment for Prometheus stealing fire for mankind), with scholia, in ms. Venice, Biblioteca Marciana, Gr. 464, fol. 158v.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hesiod,_Theogony,_Venice,_Gr._464.jpg","alt":"Hesiod, Theogony, Venice, Gr. 464"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does the poem carry disorder inside its own order?","a":"Because Tartaros stands among the first beings and later fathers Typhon on Gaia, so an agent of disorder belongs to the genealogy the poem is building."},{"q":"Why did Clay say theology trumps chronology in it?","a":"Because the arrangement of the material is what the poet chooses, and the moments where a genealogy is anticipated or held back are where those choices show."},{"q":"Why is the Near Eastern parallel not a demonstrated borrowing?","a":"Because although the Enuma Elish tells a story of the same shape and castration appears in the Hurro-Hittite Kumarbi cycle, how far any of it was really transmitted is unproven."}]},
{"id":"gr-142","num":"142","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Three parts make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a stretch of myth, then a long section on justice and farming, and finally a list of days.","answer":"Works and Days","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">828 lines</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Works and Days</b> is Hesiod's poem of instruction, and it falls into three parts: myth as far as line 212, justice and farming to line 694, and then a run of days hedged about with taboos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is addressed to his brother Perses, who had taken the larger share of their father's estate with the gift-eating chiefs on his side, and the word Hesiod sets against them is dike, the right of a case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of the quarrel the poem builds a contrast, the virtuous and self-sufficient poet against the idle dependent brother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At line 40 he tells them that fools do not know how much more the half is than the whole, one of several sayings still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prometheus and Pandora's jar belong to the same opening section, and why Hope alone stayed inside it has plagued interpreters, while the succession of races named after metals is told just as obliquely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The ideal held up in the working half is running one's own household without needing anybody else's, which one study calls the Iron Age ideal of the poem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A small set of ideas keeps returning, strife and emulation, hope and shame, and the teaching is thought to work by making the hearer reason for himself rather than by handing down a rule, though the abstract words for self-sufficiency never occur in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The farm work is pinned to the risings and settings of stars rather than to any civil calendar, which makes it a rudimentary solar calendar and the best evidence for how Greeks reckoned time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether it would serve a farmer is doubted, one reading holding that it contains no genuine agricultural instruction and is art meant to move rather than to teach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that reading the year's round teaches the will of Zeus and justice is a recognition that hardship is necessary, while the chiefs the <i>Theogony</i> praises are condemned here, a tension running through Hesiod's work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Works and Days","questions":["Because its farming advice is pinned to the risings and settings of stars, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the best evidence there is for how the Greeks reckoned their year.","Hesiod addressed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a brother who had taken the larger share of their inheritance and had the gift-eating chiefs of the place on his side."],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["James Holoka, review of <i>Hesiod's Works and Days</i>, ed. and trans. David W. Tandy and Walter C. Neale, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.05.11/. [Open access]","John Lewis, review of <i>Farming and Poetry in Hesiod's Works and Days</i>, by Maria S. Marsilio, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.10.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.10.16. [Open access]","Brendon Reay, review of <i>God and the Land: The Metaphysics of Farming in Hesiod and Vergil</i>, by Stephanie A. Nelson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.08.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.08.16/. [Open access]","J. J. Mulhern, review of <i>Hesiod's Works and Days: How to Teach Self-Sufficiency</i>, by Lilah Grace Canevaro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.17. [Open access]","Chad Matthew Schroeder, review of <i>Theogony; and, Works and Days</i>, trans. Catherine Schlegel and Henry Weinfield, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.09.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.09.38/. [Open access]","Anne-Marie Lewis, review of <i>Greek and Roman Calendars: Constructions of Time in the Classical World</i>, by Robert Hannah, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.10.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.10.04/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Werke_und_Tage.jpg","title":"Works and Days","desc":"Initial page of Hesiod’s poem Works and Days, with the Greek original text on the left side, and a Latin translation on the right side.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Werke_und_Tage.jpg","alt":"Werke und Tage"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the poem's farming calendar tied to the stars?","a":"Because the work is pinned to the risings and settings of stars rather than to any civil calendar, which makes it a rudimentary solar calendar and the best evidence for how Greeks reckoned time."},{"q":"Why is it doubted that the poem would serve a farmer?","a":"Because one reading holds it contains no genuine agricultural instruction and is art meant to move rather than to teach."},{"q":"Why is there a tension between Hesiod's two poems?","a":"Because the chiefs the Theogony praises are condemned here as gift-eaters, and this poem sets dike, the right of a case, against them."}]},
{"id":"gr-143","num":"143","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Field survey put only slight Protogeometric and Geometric finds at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so Hesiod's village was a small place at the dawn of the Archaic age.","answer":"Ascra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hesiod's village</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ascra</b> is the Boeotian village where Hesiod farmed, set in the Valley of the Muses under Mount Helicon, and his father had come there from Aeolic Cumae across the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A survey run from Bradford and Cambridge identified the site, and its Protogeometric and Geometric finds are slight, so the place of about 700 BCE was a very small community at the dawn of the Archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did not stay small, reaching some 11 hectares (27 acres) and perhaps 1,300 people by Classical times, which made it larger than several Boeotian towns that counted as cities in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its neighbour Thespiae grew far more, to about 100 hectares (250 acres) and maybe 13,000 people, and traces of a defence wall of Archaic or Early Classical style at Ascra may be a mark of trouble between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What kind of place it was in Hesiod's own day is the harder question, and the same evidence is read both ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Anthony Edwards argued that it governed itself, the chiefs of Thespiae judging Ascran quarrels only when a quarrel was brought to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He rested that on what Hesiod does not say, since the poet complains of lawsuits, of false swearing and of his brother's gifts to those chiefs, but never of rents, taxes, land shortage or debts owed outside the village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading it was thinly settled and roughly equal, its borrowing a matter of food and neighbours rather than of markets, and a bad year could turn anybody into a debtor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The survey reads the poem the other way, as showing a village already dependent on Thespiae by the close of the Geometric period, and a recent study has it subordinated to that city while lording it over the households inside itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Edwards's own reviewer was not persuaded, and behind the whole argument sits the difficulty that the village is being reconstructed out of the poems written in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ascra","questions":["Hesiod complains of lawsuits and false swearing but never of rents or taxes, which is the argument that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still governed itself in his day.","By Classical times <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covered some 11 hectares and held perhaps 1,300 people, while its neighbour Thespiae had grown to roughly a hundred."],"tags":["place","geography","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Timothy Howe, review of <i>Hesiod's Ascra</i>, by Anthony T. Edwards, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.09.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.09.13/. [Open access]","John Bintliff, \"The Archaeological Survey of the Valley of the Muses and Its Significance for Boeotian History,\" in <i>La montagne des muses</i>, Recherches et Rencontres 7 (Geneva: Droz, 1996), 193–217, https://revues.droz.org/RR/article/view/RR_7_193-217. [Open access]","Olivier Mariaud, review of <i>Finding People in Early Greece</i>, by Carol G. Thomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.07.63, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.07.63. [Open access]","Ruth Scodel, review of <i>City, Countryside, and the Spatial Organization of Value in Classical Antiquity</i>, ed. Ralph M. Rosen and Ineke Sluiter, Mnemosyne Supplements 279, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.07.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.07.23/. [Open access]","Samuel Ortencio Flores, review of <i>Voiceless, Invisible, and Countless in Ancient Greece: The Experience of Subordinates, 700-300 BCE</i>, by Samuel D. Gartland and David W. Tandy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.11/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Map_of_Boeotia.jpg/1920px-Map_of_Boeotia.jpg","title":"Ascra","desc":"An antique map of ancient Boeotia, the region of central Greece in which Ascra lay, with its lakes, rivers and towns marked.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Boeotia.jpg","alt":"Map of Boeotia"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Ascra","at":[23.0742,38.327]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Edwards argue the village governed itself?","a":"Because of what Hesiod does not say: he complains of lawsuits, false swearing and his brother's gifts to the chiefs, but never of rents, taxes, land shortage or debts owed outside the village."},{"q":"Why is the same evidence read the other way?","a":"Because the survey takes the poem to show a village already dependent on Thespiae by the close of the Geometric period, and a recent study has it subordinated to that city."},{"q":"Why is the whole argument awkwardly founded?","a":"Because the village is being reconstructed out of the poems written in it."}]},
{"id":"gr-144","num":"144","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The innovation that makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> different from its Phoenician model is that four Semitic signs became vowels, with a fifth split off the consonant wau.","answer":"Greek alphabet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE, still argued</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Greek alphabet</b> is the Phoenician letters turned to writing Greek, and what makes it more than a borrowing is that the vowels get signs of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The adapter took four Semitic letters whose names opened on sounds Greek had no consonant for — alf, he, yod and ain — and drew a fifth vowel off the consonant wau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is agreement that this was done once and not repeatedly, and none about the circumstances, imagined both as a chance meeting of traders in some harbour and as a formal occasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The letters phi, chi and psi have no Phoenician counterpart and are argued over separately, as the adapter's own inventions or as a later second borrowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One case puts the work in Cyprus, among scribes already writing Greek in the Cypriot syllabary, and rests on the single letters for ks and zd, exactly the two clusters that syllabary wrote with one sign; against it stands the absence of any early alphabetic writing on Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The date keeps moving earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was argued from the want of anything before 700 BCE that it could be no older, and later finds took the earliest evidence back some seventy years, while radiocarbon dates from Carthage and two Italian sites have been used to argue for the 9th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was no single Greek alphabet at first but a crowd of local ones, and the regional groupings long drawn on the map, tested by network modelling, came out less tidy than that map suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its uses ran from marks cut into pots before firing and scratched in afterwards to verse and to short functional notes, which is why one study prefers to speak of local literacies rather than of Greek literacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The boldest claim is that it was devised to take down hexameter and Homer in particular; a reviewer thought that rested on willingness to believe rather than on demonstrable probability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek alphabet","questions":["It was argued from the want of anything earlier that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be no older than 700 BCE, and later finds took that back about seventy years.","Single letters for the clusters ks and zd are the argument for putting the making of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Cyprus, among scribes who already wrote Greek in a syllabary."],"tags":["concept","technology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Mabel Lang, review of <i>Homer and the Origin of the Greek Alphabet</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1991.05.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1991/1991.05.15/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]","Natalia Elvira Astoreca, review of <i>Panhellenes at Methone: Graphê in Late Geometric and Protoarchaic Methone</i>, ed. Jenny Strauss Clay, Irad Malkin and Yannis Z. Tzifopoulos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 44, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.32. [Open access]","Megan Daniels, review of <i>Connecting Communities in Archaic Greece: Exploring Economic and Political Networks through Data Modelling</i>, by Michael Loy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.01.28/. [Open access]","Eleanor Dickey, review of <i>Greek Writing from Knossos to Homer: A Linguistic Interpretation of the Origin of the Greek Alphabet and the Continuity of Ancient Greek Literacy</i>, by Roger D. Woodard, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.04.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.04.04/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Dipylon_Inscription.JPG/1920px-Dipylon_Inscription.JPG","title":"Greek alphabet","desc":"One of the four oldest known samples of the use of the Greek alphabet. It has been dated to ca.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dipylon_Inscription.JPG","alt":"Dipylon Inscription"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why is the alphabet more than a borrowing?","a":"Because the vowels get signs of their own: four Semitic letters whose names opened on sounds Greek had no consonant for were taken over, and a fifth vowel drawn off the consonant wau."},{"q":"Why has Cyprus been proposed as the place it was made?","a":"Because Greek was already being written there in a syllabary, and the single letters for ks and zd match exactly the two clusters that syllabary wrote with one sign — though no early alphabetic writing has been found on the island."},{"q":"Why does one study prefer to speak of local literacies?","a":"Because there was no single Greek alphabet at first but a crowd of local ones, put to uses running from marks on pots to verse and short functional notes."}]},
{"id":"gr-145","num":"145","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A set of 22 letters, every one of them a consonant, makes up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves a reader to supply the vowels from the sense.","answer":"Phoenician alphabet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phoenician coast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Phoenician alphabet</b> is the writing of the Levantine coast, 22 letters and every one of them a consonant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ancestry runs back to the Proto-Sinaitic inscriptions of the early 2nd millennium BCE, where Egyptian signs were turned to the writing of a West Semitic language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The simplification is argued to have come from Egypt rather than Mesopotamia, out of the Egyptian one-consonant signs, and one account puts the first adaptation down to the practical needs of mercenary soldiering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Writing of this kind is known at Ugarit by about 1400 BCE and on the Phoenician coast by about 1000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people who used it there did not call themselves by that name, which is a modern label for a coast that took its shape after the collapse of about 1200 to 1150 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Whether the thing is an alphabet at all is disputed, and it has been called instead a consonantal syllabary, on the argument that speech falls into syllables and not into separate consonants and vowels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He doubts the acrophonic principle too, the idea that each letter is named for an object whose word begins with that sound, since most of the letters carry no such association.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where one word ends and the next begins was marked, though not in one manner: some texts divide what a speaker would treat as a single word and others the whole phrase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The surviving material is not thin, about ten thousand Phoenician and Punic inscriptions having been counted by 2018, with more turning up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aramaic came to be written in a consonantal alphabet of the same 22 signs, thought to descend from this one, and the Greeks made their own use of it, which is why a recent treatment would have the alphabet read as a technology shaped by its circumstances rather than as a miracle that explains itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phoenician alphabet","questions":["It has been proposed that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better called a consonantal syllabary, since speech falls into syllables rather than into separate consonants and vowels.","The line behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches to Sinai in the early 2nd millennium BCE, where Egyptian signs were first put to work on a Semitic language."],"tags":["concept","technology","history","bronze age","iron age"],"sources":["L. R. Siddall, review of <i>Writing: Theory and History of the Technology of Civilization</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.04.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.04.24/. [Open access]","Russell Clark, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of the Phoenician and Punic Mediterranean</i>, ed. Carolina López-Ruiz and Brian R. Doak, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.08.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.08.13/. [Open access]","D. M. Goldstein and Dieter C. Gunkel, review of <i>A Companion to the Ancient Greek Language</i>, ed. Egbert J. Bakker, Blackwell Companions to the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.07/. [Open access]","Michela Piccin, review of <i>Avventure della scrittura: documenti dal Mediterraneo orientale antico</i>, ed. Raffaella Pierobon Benoit, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.09.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.09.41/. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Barth%C3%A9l%C3%A9my_Alphabet_ph%C3%A9nicien.jpg","title":"Phoenician alphabet","desc":"Barthélémy Alphabet phénicien. Jean-Jacques Barthélémy, 1758.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barth%C3%A9l%C3%A9my_Alphabet_ph%C3%A9nicien.jpg","alt":"Barthélémy Alphabet phénicien"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is it disputed whether this is an alphabet at all?","a":"Because it has been called a consonantal syllabary instead, on the argument that speech falls into syllables rather than into separate consonants and vowels."},{"q":"Why is the acrophonic principle doubted?","a":"Because most of the letters carry no association with an object whose word begins with that sound."},{"q":"Why do the people who used it not carry the modern name?","a":"Because Phoenician is a modern label for a coast that took its shape after the collapse of about 1200 to 1150 BCE."}]},
{"id":"gr-146","num":"146","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A complete line of hexameter scratched on a wine jug makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the earliest pieces of writing in the Greek alphabet.","answer":"Dipylon inscription","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wine jug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 740 BCE, conventional</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dipylon inscription</b> is a complete line of hexameter scratched on a Late Geometric wine jug from Athens, put at about 740 BCE and among the earliest writing in the Greek alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The jug is number 192 in the National Archaeological Museum, which bought it from Ioannis Palaiologos, an Athenian dealer who had dug it up himself in the Outer Kerameikos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Palaiologos had learned to dig on the Archaeological Society's own excavations at the Kerameikos in the early 1860s and then worked for the trade until about 1900, arrested and fined more than once without stopping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So one of the oldest Greek inscriptions there is has no recorded grave behind it, which is a fact about the 19th century rather than about the 8th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ypsilon and chi are already on the pot, and those are among the letters Greek added rather than took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Names begin to be scratched on Athenian pottery about the middle of the 8th century, and only in the 7th are inscriptions painted on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That this line scans at all puts verse on a pot within a generation or so of the alphabet's arrival, which one study places about 775 BCE, roughly half a century earlier than used to be supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same study reads that arrival together with the sudden appearance of figured scenes in vase painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The date of 740 is less solid than it looks, since the conventional chronology of the Geometric periods hangs on a handful of sherds from Megiddo and Samaria dated by destruction layers there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates from Sindos put the start of Late Geometric I at 870 rather than 760 or 750, an offset of fifty to eighty years running across centuries, which its authors say bears directly on when the alphabet was taken up; other radiocarbon work has been used to push the earliest inscriptions into the 9th century as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dipylon inscription","questions":["The museum bought the jug carrying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from an Athenian dealer who had dug it up himself, so it has no recorded grave behind it.","Radiocarbon dates from Sindos unsettle the conventional 740 BCE given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, putting the start of Late Geometric I a century or so earlier."],"tags":["text","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Yannis Galanakis, \"Of Grave Hunters and Earth Contractors: A Look at the 'Private Archaeology' of Greece,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i>, 15 November 2012, https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2012/11/15/of-grave-hunters-earth-contractors-a-look-at-the-private-archaeology-of-greece/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]","Barry B. Powell, review of <i>Image and Myth: A History of Pictorial Narration in Greek Art</i>, by Luca Giuliani, trans. Joseph O'Donnell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.04.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.35. [Open access]","Catalin Pavel, \"Art and the Alphabet in the Times of the Dipylon Master,\" <i>GAIA: Revue interdisciplinaire sur la Grèce ancienne</i> 19 (2016): 25–56, https://doi.org/10.3406/gaia.2016.1697. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Natalia Elvira Astoreca, review of <i>Panhellenes at Methone: Graphê in Late Geometric and Protoarchaic Methone</i>, ed. Jenny Strauss Clay, Irad Malkin and Yannis Z. Tzifopoulos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 44, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.32. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/NAMA_Dipylon_Oinochoe.JPG","title":"Dipylon inscription","desc":"The Dipylon oinochoe in a museum case with a drawing of its inscription beside it, a late Geometric wine jug carrying one of the earliest Greek alphabetic texts.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NAMA_Dipylon_Oinochoe.JPG","alt":"NAMA Dipylon Oinochoe"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does one of the oldest Greek inscriptions have no recorded grave?","a":"Because the jug was dug up by an Athenian dealer who worked for the trade, so its context is a fact about the 19th century rather than about the 8th."},{"q":"Why does a scanning line on a pot matter?","a":"Because it puts verse on pottery within a generation or so of the alphabet's arrival, which one study places about 775 BCE."},{"q":"Why is the date of 740 less solid than it looks?","a":"Because the Geometric chronology hangs on a handful of sherds from Megiddo and Samaria, and radiocarbon from Sindos would push it fifty to eighty years earlier."}]},
{"id":"gr-147","num":"147","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Three lines scratched on a small clay cup from Ischia make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the earliest pieces of Greek writing and, to many, the first epigram.","answer":"Nestor's Cup","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cup inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 730 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nestor's Cup</b> is a drinking cup out of the cemeteries at the foot of Monte Vico on Ischia, cut with three lines of Greek about 730 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The verses run, in one rendering, \"I am the goblet of Nestor, fine to drink from, but whoever drinks from this goblet will instantly be seized by desire for the well-crowned Aphrodite\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The joke measures that against the <i>Iliad</i>, where Nestor's cup is so heavy that only he can lift it when full, while the thing itself is a modest piece of clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is among the earliest Greek writing there is and is commonly counted the earliest epigram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the cup was made is not agreed, one account calling it Rhodian and another placing it in northern Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It has been taken as showing that Greeks in the west already had Homer in their heads by the third quarter of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was taken apart in turn, on the ground that the cup has nothing in common with the shape of the vessel Homer describes, and that nothing guarantees its owners drank from it at a symposium, could read Greek, or caught the allusion at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He added that Nestor was linked with remarkable cups in other traditions too, so the <i>Iliad</i> need not be the thing referred to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A friendlier reading takes the metre as a sign that the cup went round a company of drinkers, and so that a developed drinking culture was already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The grave was re-examined in 2021, and what had been read for decades as one child of ten to fourteen proved to be at least three people, with sheep and bird burnt alongside them, which unsettles the picture of a boy buried with a wine cup.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Nestor's Cup","questions":["The joke in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns on the <i>Iliad</i>, where the old king's cup is so heavy that only he can lift it when full.","A 2021 study of the burial that held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found at least three people and burnt animal bone where one child had long been assumed."],"tags":["object","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Melania Gigante, Alessia Nava, Robert R. Paine, et al., \"Who Was Buried with Nestor's Cup? Macroscopic and Microscopic Analyses of the Cremated Remains from Tomb 168 (Second Half of the 8th Century BCE, Pithekoussai, Ischia Island, Italy),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257368, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257368. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","Adam Rabinowitz, review of <i>The Rise of the Greek Aristocratic Banquet</i>, by Marek Węcowski, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.42/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Coupe_de_Nestor_-_mus%C3%A9e_Arch%C3%A9ologique_de_Pithecusae.jpg","title":"Nestor's Cup","desc":"Coupe de Nestor - musée Archéologique de Pithecusae. Shonagon.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coupe_de_Nestor_-_mus%C3%A9e_Arch%C3%A9ologique_de_Pithecusae.jpg","alt":"Coupe de Nestor - musée Archéologique de Pithecusae"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the inscription funny?","a":"Because it measures a modest piece of clay against the Iliad, where Nestor's cup is so heavy that only he can lift it when full."},{"q":"Why is the claim that its readers knew Homer contested?","a":"Because the cup has nothing in common with the vessel Homer describes, nothing guarantees its owners could read Greek or caught the allusion, and Nestor was linked with remarkable cups in other traditions too."},{"q":"Why did the 2021 re-examination unsettle the picture?","a":"Because what had been read for decades as one child of ten to fourteen proved to be at least three people, with sheep and bird burnt alongside them."}]},
{"id":"gr-148","num":"148","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The earliest lasting Greek settlement in the western Mediterranean was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, made by Eretrians and Chalcidians on Ischia off the Bay of Naples.","answer":"Pithekoussai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek goods by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 780 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Large settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pithekoussai</b> stood on Ischia, off the Bay of Naples, and is the earliest lasting Greek settlement in the western Mediterranean, made by Eretrians and Chalcidians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest Greek pottery belongs to the first half of the 8th century BCE, but it comes from a dump and may mean nothing more than trade running ahead of settlement, somebody selling Greek goods to Campanians by about 780.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By about 750 the built-up ground ran at least a kilometre (half a mile) along its axis, three times the size of Smyrna at that date, with four to five hundred households.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 493 graves have been dug, reckoned at no more than a twentieth of a cemetery that filled between 750 and 700, and the population behind them has been put as low as about 900 and as high as nearly ten thousand; the dead were burnt, at times with sheep or birds burnt beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were not all Greeks, since Levantine amphorae, north Syrian face jugs and Phoenician lamps came up with them, and one amphora over a baby's grave carried a label in Aramaic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The settlers worked metal, bringing iron ore across from Elba, and sent out Late Geometric pottery of their own making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three Corinthian potters lived among them and shipped their small perfume flasks to Campania, Caere and Bologna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Analysis of the clays now supports that pattern more widely, since potters and their methods travelled the Euboean routes rather than finished pots being carried out from Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What to call the place is unsettled: David Ridgway had it for an emporion, a planned commercial venture in which hundreds of families lived by selling their skills, while a later contributor puts it past an emporion and short of a proper colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people went on to found Cumae on the mainland, which was a city of the ordinary kind, and its cemeteries at the foot of Monte Vico are where the inscribed cup called Nestor's was buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pithekoussai","questions":["Levantine amphorae, north Syrian face jugs and Phoenician lamps came out of the graves at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its people were plainly not all Greeks.","Whether to call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an emporion or something nearer a colony is unsettled, since it was a planned commercial venture with hundreds of households."],"tags":["place","archaeology","history","greece","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19. [Open access]","Claudio Vacanti, review of <i>The Emporion in the Ancient Western Mediterranean: Trade and Colonial Encounters from the Archaic to the Hellenistic Period</i>, ed. Éric Gailledrat, Michael Dietler and Rosa Plana-Mallart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.09.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.09.45/. [Open access]","Daniel Frank, review of <i>Greek Iron Age Pottery in the Mediterranean World: Tracing Provenance and Socioeconomic Ties</i>, ed. Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.06.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.33/. [Open access]","Melania Gigante, Alessia Nava, Robert R. Paine, et al., \"Who Was Buried with Nestor's Cup? Macroscopic and Microscopic Analyses of the Cremated Remains from Tomb 168 (Second Half of the 8th Century BCE, Pithekoussai, Ischia Island, Italy),\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257368, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257368. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B12_085-ISCHIA_Isola_%2C_olim_AENARIA.jpeg/1920px-Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B12_085-ISCHIA_Isola_%2C_olim_AENARIA.jpeg","title":"Pithekoussai","desc":"The map of the Italian island Ischia, near the coast of Napels, was published in 1640 by Joan Blaeu (1598-1673) and Cornelius Blaeu (1610-1642) in the third volume of the Atlas Novus.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B12_085-ISCHIA_Isola_,_olim_AENARIA.jpeg","alt":"Atlas Van der Hagen-KW1049B12 085-ISCHIA Isola, olim AENARIA"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Pithekoussai","at":[13.8957,40.7312]},"why":[{"q":"Why may the earliest Greek pottery there not date the settlement?","a":"Because it comes from a dump and may mean nothing more than trade running ahead of settlement, somebody selling Greek goods to Campanians by about 780."},{"q":"Why is the place not read as purely Greek?","a":"Because Levantine amphorae, north Syrian face jugs and Phoenician lamps came up with the burials, and one amphora over a baby's grave carried a label in Aramaic."},{"q":"Why is what to call the settlement unsettled?","a":"Because Ridgway had it for an emporion, a planned commercial venture of hundreds of families living by their skills, while a later contributor puts it past an emporion and short of a proper colony."}]},
{"id":"gr-149","num":"149","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"At the mouth of the Orontes in northern Syria stood <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose buildings held more Greek pottery than any other site in the region.","answer":"Al Mina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Euboean pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Al Mina</b> is a site at the mouth of the Orontes in northern Syria, where Leonard Woolley uncovered warehouse-like buildings holding large amounts of Greek pottery, Euboean in the 8th century and Attic later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those finds nourished the idea of Greek ascendancy in the Early Iron Age Levant, and the place was made a Greek settlement holding some sort of charter from the Syrian kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His case rests on there being a greater proportion of Greek pottery here than anywhere else in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against it stands a tiny sounding at Tyre, which produced more Greek pottery than all of Al Mina in the same years, and nobody questions that Tyre was Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The alternative offered is a Syrian town with a few Euboean merchants working in it, or a Syrian commercial enclave whose traders dealt in Greek wares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pottery cannot be counted as people in any case, and much of the undecorated ware was thrown away as the site was dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A study of the Syrian and Phoenician pottery that was kept found a significant Phoenician presence and came out inconclusive about the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground under all of it is softer than it looks, since the strata here and at other Levantine sites are now reckoned less well defined than they once were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chemical testing of the later pottery, from the years between 539 and 301 BCE, showed that much of what had been taken for local work came in from western Asia Minor, Miletos above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A further reading makes the settlement a mercenary one rather than a commercial one, and its author and Boardman published in the same volume without either mentioning the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Al Mina","questions":["A tiny sounding at Tyre produced more Greek pottery than all of contemporary <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and nobody questions that Tyre was Phoenician.","Because much undecorated ware was thrown away as it was dug, the pottery from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot be counted as a census of who lived there."],"tags":["place","archaeology","history","syria","iron age"],"sources":["Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19. [Open access]","Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>The Greeks in the East</i>, ed. Alexandra Villing, British Museum Research Publication 157, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.07.65, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.07.65/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Ancient Greeks West and East</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Mnemosyne Supplements 196, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.20/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume One</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","David Gill, review of <i>Archaic Cyprus: A Study of the Textual and Archaeological Evidence</i>, by A. T. Reyes, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.34. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Al Mina","at":[35.9871,36.0804]},"why":[{"q":"Why does Tyre undercut the case for a Greek settlement here?","a":"Because a tiny sounding there produced more Greek pottery than all of Al Mina in the same years, and nobody questions that Tyre was Phoenician."},{"q":"Why is the pottery evidence weaker than the counts suggest?","a":"Because pottery cannot be counted as people, and much of the undecorated ware was thrown away as the site was dug."},{"q":"Why did chemical testing change what was known about the later phases?","a":"Because much of what had been taken for local work came in from western Asia Minor, Miletos above all."}]},
{"id":"gr-150","num":"150","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Chalcis and Eretria, the two cities behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, colonised jointly for much of the 8th century before falling out over the Lelantine plain.","answer":"Euboean trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trade and colonies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lelantine War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 710 – 650 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Euboean trade</b> is the reach of two cities on one island, Chalcis and Eretria, across the Mediterranean in the 9th and 8th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chalcis was settled in the 11th century in a favourable spot and had a large part in the metal trade, its ruling families named in a well known passage of Hesiod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretria was founded in the 9th where there was an excellent harbour, took its name from the word for a rower, and held Andros, Tenos and Keos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xeropolis at Lefkandi commanded the eastern half of the plain of the Lelas and Chalcis the western, and one reading makes Eretria the harbour of Lefkandi rather than a rival to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two colonised together for a good part of the 8th century, in Chalcidice well before 700, at Pithekoussai in its second quarter and then at Cumae, with Rhegion the last of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both were in the Levant by the second half of the century, which Al Mina is cited to show, and the exchange they joined had been running through the Early Iron Age before they entered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Northward, the port of Methone speaks a dialect and writes an alphabet tied to Euboea and to Eretria in particular, with imported pots from a great many Greek cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the west somebody was selling Greek goods to Campanians by about 780, ahead of any settlement there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What moved along these routes was not only cargo, since analysis of the clays indicates that potters and their methods travelled rather than finished pots being shipped out from Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war between the two cities over the plain between them, put by one study at roughly 710 to 650, closed the golden age of both, a war without winners after which neither kept the impulse it had shown before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Euboean trade","questions":["Analysis of clays suggests that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried potters and their methods westward rather than finished pots being shipped out from Greece.","The Lelantine War, put at roughly 710 to 650 BCE, closed the golden age of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and left neither city with the impulse it had shown before."],"tags":["practice","history","greece","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of <i>Untersuchungen zum Lelantischen Krieg und verwandten Problemen der frühgriechischen Geschichte</i>, by Victor Parker, Historia Einzelschriften 109, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.06.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.06.12/. [Open access]","Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19. [Open access]","Daniel Frank, review of <i>Greek Iron Age Pottery in the Mediterranean World: Tracing Provenance and Socioeconomic Ties</i>, ed. Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.06.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.33/. [Open access]","Natalia Elvira Astoreca, review of <i>Panhellenes at Methone: Graphê in Late Geometric and Protoarchaic Methone</i>, ed. Jenny Strauss Clay, Irad Malkin and Yannis Z. Tzifopoulos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 44, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.32. [Open access]","Joanna Smith, review of <i>Italy and Cyprus in Antiquity, 1500-450 BC</i>, ed. Larissa Bonfante and Vassos Karageorghis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.41/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did more travel these routes than cargo?","a":"Because analysis of the clays indicates that potters and their methods travelled, rather than finished pots being shipped out from Greece."},{"q":"Why is Methone useful evidence for Euboean reach?","a":"Because it speaks a dialect and writes an alphabet tied to Euboea and to Eretria in particular, with imported pots from a great many Greek cities."},{"q":"Why did the golden age of both cities close?","a":"Because the war between them over the plain between, put at roughly 710 to 650, was a war without winners after which neither kept the impulse it had shown before."}]},
{"id":"gr-151","num":"151","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A tripillar shrine of Phoenician type built inside a Cretan temple is the firmest evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than merely for their goods.","answer":"Phoenicians in the Aegean","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kommos imports from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Levantine visits end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phoenicians in the Aegean</b> are known far better from what they left than from where they lived: metal bowls, ivories, glass beads, faience, scarabs and the techniques of jewellery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carolina López-Ruiz carries the debt further, to the mortise-and-tenon method of building ships, to the antecedents of the Ionic and Aeolic capitals, and to Greek monumental sculpture, which she has arriving by Phoenician adaptation rather than from Egypt direct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The winged sphinx, everywhere in Phoenician art of the Iron Age, is the badge of that traffic, and Herodotus has a Tyrian Cadmus bringing the Greeks their letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The firmest sign of people rather than objects is at Kommos on the south coast of Crete, where a shrine of the Phoenician or Punic three-pillar kind was built inside the temple, with Phoenician pottery from the late 10th or early 9th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its excavators read those contexts as showing not merely goods passed along a chain but the actual presence of Phoenician traders, if not residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A recent study of the same site is colder about it: there was no Phoenician colonisation of Crete, it is not even known whether a settlement stood there, and Levantine visits had stopped before the end of the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elsewhere the traces are of workshops rather than of homes, with Phoenician art-wares made on Rhodes and at Carthage, the factories put wherever the materials or the customers were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside the Aegean the case is easier, since at Pithekoussai in the west somebody painted a Semitic funerary symbol on an amphora over a baby's grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the northern Aegean, rich in timber and metals, early Greek and Phoenician dealings run alongside Trojans, Phrygians and the people already there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether 'the Phoenicians' are a people at all is now itself argued, López-Ruiz rejecting what she calls Phoenicoskepticism and holding them a real group with shared ritual and cultural traits, while a contributor to the same volume declines to call the famous metal bowls Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phoenicians in the Aegean","questions":["Metal bowls, ivories, glass beads and faience mark <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though objects travel a great deal more easily than the people who made them.","Workshops making Phoenician art-wares on Rhodes are part of the case for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a workshop is not the same thing as a settlement."],"tags":["people","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Hélène Sader, review of <i>Phoenicians and the Making of the Mediterranean</i>, by Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.06.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.06.11/. [Open access]","Jérémy Lamaze, review of <i>Thirsty Seafarers at Temple B of Kommos: Commercial Districts and the Role of Crete in Phoenician Trading Networks in the Aegean</i>, by Judith Muñoz Sogas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.10.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.10.25/. [Open access]","Jean Turfa, review of <i>Phoenicians</i>, by Glenn Markoe, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.05.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.05.09/. [Open access]","Grace Erny, review of <i>The Connected Iron Age: Interregional Networks in the Eastern Mediterranean, 900-600 BCE</i>, ed. Jonathan M. Hall and James F. Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.08/. [Open access]","Donald Haggis, review of <i>Kommos IV: The Greek Sanctuary</i>, ed. Joseph W. Shaw and Maria C. Shaw, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.06.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.06.18/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is Kommos the firmest sign of people rather than goods?","a":"Because a shrine of the Phoenician or Punic three-pillar kind was built inside the temple there, with Phoenician pottery from the late 10th or early 9th century."},{"q":"Why is a recent study colder about that reading?","a":"Because it holds there was no Phoenician colonisation of Crete, that it is not even known whether a settlement stood there, and that Levantine visits had stopped before the end of the 7th century."},{"q":"Why is it argued whether the Phoenicians are a people at all?","a":"Because López-Ruiz rejects what she calls Phoenicoskepticism and holds them a real group with shared ritual and cultural traits, while a contributor to the same volume declines to call the famous metal bowls Phoenician."}]},
{"id":"gr-152","num":"152","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Twelve cities on the western coast of Asia Minor traced themselves to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with Athens as the mother city in most of the stories.","answer":"Ionian migration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ionian migration</b> is the tradition that Greeks crossed from the mainland to settle the middle stretch of the western coast of Asia Minor, in the twelve cities of the dodekapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Athens is the mother city in those stories, and the founders they hang on are Neileus at Miletos and Androklos at Ephesos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo and Pausanias preserve a whole variety of such tales, which between them supplied a base for a shared Ionianness rather than one agreed account of what happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Something can be said for the Athenian claim: the evidence of social institutions and of the calendar has been found consistent with Athens as the mother city of Miletos, putting the settlement at about 1050 BCE from the likeness of Milesian and Athenian pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miletos was old ground by then, Minoan from about 1700 BCE and Mycenaean afterwards, so whoever arrived was arriving somewhere rather than nowhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The trouble lies in the middle, since the argument welds pre-Archaic archaeology from Ionia to post-Archaic literature from Athens with nothing said about the centuries in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Local Ionian identities may have grown up on their own and only later been fastened to the migration story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The case against the historical reliability of this tradition has been set out along with those of the Trojan war and the Dorian invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor does the material read as an Athenian transplant: at Klazomenai local potters adopted or adapted Greek shapes while working several different clays, which points to an overlapping manufacture rather than to one Ionian style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And the stories counted for most late, rising in significance from the reign of Hadrian, when the Panhellenion made Doric, Aeolic and Ionic into categories worth claiming and the cities of Asia were competing with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ionian migration","questions":["Milesian and Athenian pottery are alike enough to put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 1050 BCE, on a site already Minoan and then Mycenaean.","Arguments for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> weld pre-Archaic archaeology from Ionia to post-Archaic literature from Athens with almost nothing said about the centuries in between."],"tags":["event","history","migration","greece","iron age"],"sources":["P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]","Dies van der Linde, review of <i>Roman Ionia: Constructions of Cultural Identity in Western Asia Minor</i>, by Martin Hallmannsecker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.09.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.09.15/. [Open access]","Lee E. Patterson, review of <i>The Land of Ionia: Society and Economy in the Archaic Period</i>, by Alan M. Greaves, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.17/. [Open access]","Manolis Mikrakis, review of <i>Collapse and Transformation: The Late Bronze Age to Early Iron Age in the Aegean</i>, ed. Guy D. Middleton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.04.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.04.25/. [Open access]","Daniel Frank, review of <i>Greek Iron Age Pottery in the Mediterranean World: Tracing Provenance and Socioeconomic Ties</i>, ed. Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.06.33, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.06.33/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does the argument for an Athenian origin have a hole in the middle?","a":"Because it welds pre-Archaic archaeology from Ionia to post-Archaic literature from Athens with nothing said about the centuries in between."},{"q":"Why does the pottery not read as an Athenian transplant?","a":"Because at Klazomenai local potters adopted or adapted Greek shapes while working several different clays, which points to an overlapping manufacture rather than one Ionian style."},{"q":"Why did the migration stories matter most late?","a":"Because they rose in significance from the reign of Hadrian, when the Panhellenion made Doric, Aeolic and Ionic into categories worth claiming and the cities of Asia were competing."}]},
{"id":"gr-153","num":"153","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Lesbian, Thessalian and Boeotian all descend from a common Aeolic, which is the linguistic fact any account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to explain.","answer":"Aeolian migration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">By tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Aeolian migration</b> is the tradition of a movement out of mainland Greece into the north-west of Asia Minor and the islands off it, Lesbos above all, in the early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is certainly an Aeolic dialect group behind the name, Lesbian and Thessalian and Boeotian, and all three descend from a common Aeolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For Greek settlement in Asia Minor at large, the literary record has been found generally to accord with the span of years covered by the Greek pottery along the coast of western Turkey, though the process was neither momentary nor short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Samothrace the Aeolian elements outweigh the Ionian, and the first settlers are thought to have come from Aeolis, from the nearby Troad or from Lesbos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How Lesbian itself took shape is a separate question, and the part played in it by the Ionic of Asia Minor is held not to be exaggerated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The difficulty is the archaeology, since it has been argued that there is no archaeological evidence at all for Aeolian Greeks moving from Balkan Hellas to Anatolia in the early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roger Woodard takes that argument as a working model and follows where it goes, which is backwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If no such crossing happened, then common Aeolic was already being spoken in Anatolia in the Bronze Age, as the language of the Ahhiyawa, the Mycenaean community of the western Anatolian coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lesbian would then be the dialect of that community's descendants, sitting where it had always sat, and Thessalian and Boeotian would belong to Aeolians who went the other way, out of Anatolia into Balkan Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archaeology does not rule that out, and a wider reading of the Protogeometric Aegean sees peaceful development by communities settled in one place rather than people on the move as nomads or invaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aeolian migration","questions":["One account turns the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around, proposing that Aeolic was already spoken in Anatolia and that its speakers moved the other way, into Greece.","Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happened at all is doubted, since it has been argued that no archaeology supports Greeks crossing from Balkan Hellas to Anatolia."],"tags":["event","history","migration","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Roger Woodard, \"Aeolian Origins,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i> 7 (2019), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2019/03/15/aeolian-origins/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume One</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume Two</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Mnemosyne Supplements 193, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Marina Veksina, review of <i>Studies in Ancient Greek Dialects: From Central Greece to the Black Sea</i>, ed. Georgios K. Giannakis, Emilio Crespo and Panagiotis Filos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 49, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.02.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.02.12. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does Woodard follow the migration argument backwards?","a":"Because if no crossing happened, common Aeolic was already spoken in Anatolia in the Bronze Age, and Thessalian and Boeotian would belong to Aeolians who went the other way."},{"q":"Why is the archaeology the difficulty here?","a":"Because it has been argued that there is no archaeological evidence at all for Aeolian Greeks moving from Balkan Hellas to Anatolia in the early Iron Age."},{"q":"Why does a wider reading of the period support that doubt?","a":"Because the Protogeometric Aegean now reads as peaceful development by communities settled in one place rather than people on the move."}]},
{"id":"gr-154","num":"154","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Handmade burnished ware was the best candidate for newcomers behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until it turned out to appear well before the palaces were destroyed.","answer":"Dorian invasion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces fall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dorian invasion</b> is the idea that newcomers came down into the Peloponnese and the south of Greece once the Mycenaean palaces had fallen, and it has been given up, put back where it came from among the myths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The palaces went in the closing years of the 13th century, and life for the Mycenaeans by then must have been very insecure and unstable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is offered in place of invaders is internal strife, natural disaster and the collapse of a system, and historians now prefer several causes at once to any single one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The migration stories are doubted on their own ground, since they show the marks of different traditions run together into one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where regions turn out to share a language, an institution or a custom, contact and adoption will account for it without anybody needing to invade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The best material candidate for an incoming people was handmade burnished ware, pottery built by hand and polished, quite unlike the wheel-made Mycenaean pottery it lies beside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is a great deal of it, rising at Tiryns to 30.6% of the whole assemblage early in the last Mycenaean phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It arrives well before the palace there was destroyed, however, which takes it out of the event it was called on to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor does it point north: up to 82% of the locally made ware has parallels in southern Italy, and the makers have been read as craftsmen from there, a small minority working outside the palace and its elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The invasion has been set beside the Trojan war and the Ionian migration among legends whose reliability does not survive examination, and the Protogeometric Aegean now reads as communities developing peacefully in fixed places rather than as populations in motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dorian invasion","questions":["Doubt is cast on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because the migration stories show different traditions run together, and contact would explain the shared features anyway.","Internal strife, natural disasters and systems collapse have replaced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the explanation for why the Mycenaean palaces fell in the first place."],"tags":["event","history","migration","greece","bronze age","iron age"],"sources":["Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>The Mycenaeans</i>, by Louise Schofield, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.07.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.07.56. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Margaretha Kramer-Hajos, review of <i>Die handgemachte geglättete Keramik mykenischer Zeitstellung</i>, by Klaus Kilian and Tobias Mühlenbruch, Tiryns 15, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.09.59, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.09.59. [Open access]","Manolis Mikrakis, review of <i>Collapse and Transformation: The Late Bronze Age to Early Iron Age in the Aegean</i>, ed. Guy D. Middleton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.04.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.04.25/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>The Protogeometric Aegean: The Archaeology of the Late Eleventh and Tenth Centuries B.C.</i>, by Irene S. Lemos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.40. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does shared language or custom not require an invasion?","a":"Because where regions turn out to share a language, an institution or a custom, contact and adoption will account for it without anybody arriving."},{"q":"Why does handmade burnished ware fail as evidence of newcomers?","a":"Because it arrives well before the palace at Tiryns was destroyed, which takes it out of the event it was called on to explain."},{"q":"Why does that pottery not point north?","a":"Because up to 82% of the locally made ware has parallels in southern Italy, and the makers have been read as craftsmen from there."}]},
{"id":"gr-155","num":"155","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Sparta's kings drew their standing from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which made them founders of the same kind as the men who led out colonies.","answer":"Return of the Heracleidae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Laconian genealogy by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Return of the Heracleidae</b> is the story that the descendants of Heracles came back to the Peloponnese to take what was already theirs, a right recovered rather than a country conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were worked into the Laconian genealogy perhaps as early as the beginning of the 7th century, and what the story presses is exactly that right of return to Laconian ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sparta's kings took their legitimacy from descent among them, which put them in a class with the founders who led out colonies elsewhere in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The story did harder work as well, since the Spartan case for taking Messenia is implicit in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a separate tradition from the Dorian invasion, and the Spartans themselves had to find some way of holding the two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Neither the archaeology nor the language will support one coherent account of where the Spartans came from, and it is not until the 7th century that a Sparta appears which is not legendary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lakonia in the 8th century was thinly settled, almost wholly at Sparta and Amyclae in the valley of the Eurotas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside Sparta the story was fought over too, and giving Lykourgos the credit for the constitution was a win for the factions that wanted the kings stripped of their standing as founders of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kinship myths of this kind were made and remade for use between cities, and Greeks took to obviously new stories far more readily than might be expected, so that invention turned into tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Athens eventually turned the family against Sparta, Euripides putting the children of Heracles on stage as suppliants under Athenian protection, an answer to Spartan claims to be liberating cities early in the Peloponnesian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Return of the Heracleidae","questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a different story from the Dorian invasion, the Spartans themselves had to find some way of reconciling the two accounts.","Euripides answered Spartan claims by putting the children of Heracles under Athenian protection, turning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the city it had served."],"tags":["event","mythology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Naoíse Mac Sweeney, review of <i>Kinship Myth in Ancient Greece</i>, by Lee E. Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.08.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.08.26. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Kõiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Timothy Doran, review of <i>Myth, Text, and History at Sparta</i>, ed. Thomas Figueira, Gorgias Studies in Classical and Late Antiquity 18, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.16/. [Open access]","Sophie Mills, review of <i>City of Suppliants: Tragedy and the Athenian Empire</i>, by Angeliki Tzanetou, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.01.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.01.27/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does the story insist on return rather than conquest?","a":"Because it presses a right of return to Laconian ground already theirs, from which Sparta's kings took their legitimacy."},{"q":"Why did Sparta need to reconcile two traditions?","a":"Because this is a separate tradition from the Dorian invasion, and the Spartans themselves had to find some way of holding the two together."},{"q":"Why did giving Lykourgos the credit for the constitution matter?","a":"Because it was a win for the factions that wanted the kings stripped of their standing as founders of the city."}]},
{"id":"gr-156","num":"156","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The usual sorting of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is fourfold: Ionic, Doric or West Greek, Aeolic, and the Achaean pair of Arcadian and Cypriot.","answer":"Greek dialects","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 8th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Koine from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Greek dialects</b> are the local forms of the language before a common Greek came in, and they are known above all from what was cut in stone rather than from what was written in books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual sorting is fourfold: Ionic, Doric or West Greek, Aeolic, and the Achaean pair of Arcadian and Cypriot, with Pamphylian attached to the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeolic covers Lesbian, Thessalian and Boeotian, all three of them descended from a common Aeolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Behind the lot lies Mycenaean, and the Linear B tablets already preserve two forms of it, conventionally called Normal and Special Mycenaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the language first came apart, and how the groups should be divided below the top level, is still open, and a good deal more has been written about the Greek that came before them than about their own internal history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The tidy map misleads, since a dialect is really a matter of one city at a time and it travels with the people who speak it, so that certain forms at Byzantium point to a community of Asiatic Aeolic speakers in the colony there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether a group of dialects marks off a people is doubted: shared language, institutions and customs are more readily explained by contact and adoption than by anybody's arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In literature a dialect goes with a genre rather than with a place, and the ancient grammarians heard temperaments in them, Ionic poetic and soft against Doric austere and masculine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One argument holds that the odd forms of tragic language are not borrowings from Doric or Ionic at all, but everyday Attic deliberately made weightier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How any one dialect came to be is its own problem, and the part played by the Ionic of Asia Minor in the shaping of Lesbian is held not to be exaggerated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek dialects","questions":["Ancient grammarians heard temperaments in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, calling Ionic poetic and soft while Doric was austere and masculine, as though a dialect carried a character.","The features shared across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may be explained by contact and adoption rather than by any people arriving from somewhere else."],"tags":["language","history","literature","greece","iron age"],"sources":["M. J. C. Scarborough, review of <i>Ἑλληνικὲς διάλεκτοι στὸν ἀρχαῖο κόσμο</i>, ed. Anna Panayotou and Giovanbattista Galdi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.28/. [Open access]","Lucien van Beek, review of <i>Formes et fonctions des langues littéraires en Grèce ancienne</i>, ed. Andreas Willi, Entretiens sur l'Antiquité classique 65, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.07.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.07.24. [Open access]","Marina Veksina, review of <i>Studies in Ancient Greek Dialects: From Central Greece to the Black Sea</i>, ed. Georgios K. Giannakis, Emilio Crespo and Panagiotis Filos, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 49, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.02.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.02.12. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Daniel Kölligan, review of <i>The Linguistic Roots of Ancient Greek</i>, by Don Ringe, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.04.18/. [Open access]","Roger Woodard, \"Aeolian Origins,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i> 7 (2019), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2019/03/15/aeolian-origins/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Decree_Triphylians_Louvre_Br4069.jpg/1920px-Decree_Triphylians_Louvre_Br4069.jpg","title":"Greek dialects","desc":"Decree of the Triphylians granting citizenship and immunity from all obligations to three named persons (Pyladas, Gnathon and Pyrus) and their issue. Bronze disc bearing a clockwise spiral inscription beginning at the circumference in Elean dialect.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Decree_Triphylians_Louvre_Br4069.jpg","alt":"Decree Triphylians Louvre Br4069"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does a dialect map mislead?","a":"Because a dialect is really a matter of one city at a time and travels with its speakers — certain forms at Byzantium point to a community of Asiatic Aeolic speakers in the colony."},{"q":"Why is a dialect group doubted as the mark of a people?","a":"Because shared language, institutions and customs are more readily explained by contact and adoption than by anybody's arrival."},{"q":"Why does a literary dialect not indicate a place?","a":"Because in literature a dialect goes with a genre, and one argument holds tragic language to be everyday Attic deliberately made weightier rather than borrowed."}]},
{"id":"gr-157","num":"157","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Arcadian in the middle of the Peloponnese and Cypriot on Cyprus are the two halves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which specialists call the Achaean dialects.","answer":"Arcadocypriot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cypriot to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Arcadocypriot</b> is the group that puts together Arcadian, spoken in the middle of the Peloponnese, and Cypriot, spoken on Cyprus, which specialists call the Achaean dialects and to which Pamphylian is usually joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rupert Thompson has asked whether the prepositional usage that Arcado-Cypriot shares with Mycenaean is an isogloss of Bronze Age date, a feature held in common since before the palaces went down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Linear B itself already carries two forms of Mycenaean, conventionally called the Normal and the Special.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cyprus is where the case for something surviving is strongest, since Greek culture there stayed extremely conservative and kept a great deal that was Mycenaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greeks of Cyprus are also held to have escaped the destructions of about 1200 BCE that put an end to the Mycenaean kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The writing points the same way, since Cyprus in the 1st millennium used a different verb for the act of writing from the rest of the Greek world, which implies a practice carried on in a culture still using a syllabic script of the older kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That script served Cypriot Greek throughout the 1st millennium BCE, and it served Eteocypriot as well, a language that is not Greek at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two lived side by side: four bilingual inscriptions survive, Greek names are written out in Eteocypriot, and the kings of Amathous bore Greek names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island was mixed in another direction too, with substantial Phoenician settlements there from the middle of the 9th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What any of it proves about descent is a separate question, since features shared between dialects can come of contact and adoption as readily as of common ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Arcadocypriot","questions":["Rupert Thompson asked whether the prepositions shared by Mycenaean and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are an isogloss going back to before the palaces of the Bronze Age fell.","Cypriot, the eastern half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was written in a syllabic script that also served Eteocypriot, a language that is not Greek."],"tags":["language","history","greece","cyprus","iron age"],"sources":["M. J. C. Scarborough, review of <i>Ἑλληνικὲς διάλεκτοι στὸν ἀρχαῖο κόσμο</i>, ed. Anna Panayotou and Giovanbattista Galdi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.28/. [Open access]","John Bennet, review of <i>Donum Mycenologicum: Mycenaean Studies in Honour of Francisco Aura Jorro</i>, ed. Alberto Bernabé and Eugenio R. Luján, BCILL 131, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.04.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.04.25/. [Open access]","Eleanor Dickey, review of <i>Greek Writing from Knossos to Homer: A Linguistic Interpretation of the Origin of the Greek Alphabet and the Continuity of Ancient Greek Literacy</i>, by Roger D. Woodard, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.04.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.04.04/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>A Linguistic History of Ancient Cyprus: The Non-Greek Languages and their Relations with Greek, c. 1600-300 BC</i>, by Philippa M. Steele, Cambridge Classical Studies, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.01.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.01.24/. [Open access]","Roger Woodard, \"Aeolian Origins,\" <i>Center for Hellenic Studies Research Bulletin</i> 7 (2019), https://research-bulletin.chs.harvard.edu/2019/03/15/aeolian-origins/. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Panoses_stele.jpg/1920px-Panoses_stele.jpg","title":"Arcadocypriot","desc":"Cypriot syllabary script stele, with the name Panoses (𐠞𐠜𐠩𐠩 - pa-no-se-se), found in Ormideia (in Larnaca district), Cypro-classical period, now in Larnaca District Archaeological Museum.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoses_stele.jpg","alt":"Panoses stele"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is Cyprus where the case for Bronze Age survival is strongest?","a":"Because Greek culture there stayed extremely conservative and kept a great deal that was Mycenaean, its Greeks having escaped the destructions of about 1200 BCE."},{"q":"Why does the word for writing point the same way?","a":"Because Cyprus used a different verb for the act of writing from the rest of the Greek world, which implies a practice carried on in a culture still using a syllabic script."},{"q":"Why does a shared feature not prove common descent?","a":"Because features shared between dialects can come of contact and adoption as readily as of a common ancestor."}]},
{"id":"gr-158","num":"158","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Paphos, Amathous, Salamis, Idalion, Marion and Kition were among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the separate states into which the island was divided in the 1st millennium BCE.","answer":"Cypriot city-kingdoms","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 8th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abolished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">312 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cypriot city-kingdoms</b> were the separate states into which Cyprus was divided in the 1st millennium BCE, among them Paphos, Amathous, Salamis, Idalion, Marion and Kition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When they formed is unknown, and it is one of the sharper arguments in the archaeology of the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One reading finds no general break at the turn of the Bronze Age, between the phases called Late Cypriot IIC and IIIA in the 13th and 12th centuries BCE, some areas breaking down economically while others flourished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other holds that even at Paphos, Kition and Enkomi-Salamis, the cases usually cited, the evidence for a political system carrying through to the 8th century BCE is meagre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reading Amathous may be the earliest of them all, founded not in the 11th century but in the 9th or 8th under Phoenician influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Kition had been a substantial town since the 13th century BCE, with its temples and a massive wall along the sea, and in the 9th century it passed to the Phoenicians, who rebuilt the Bronze Age temple complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Copper is what made an inland kingdom worth taking, and is read as the reason Idalion became a desirable target and was in the end annexed by Kition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Assyria dealt with the island as a set of kings, and a cuneiform inscription records the gifts of seven of them to Sargon II in about 707 BCE, although the text has no secure findspot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How far a kingdom faced outward could differ inside its own borders: at Amathous the town tombs are full of Greek and Levantine pottery while the Vasilikos and Maroni valleys behind yielded almost none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That looks like a deliberate want of interest in imported pottery rather than isolation, the inland dead being given iron weapons instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cypriot city-kingdoms","questions":["Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew out of the Bronze Age or followed a break turns on the 12th century, where one reading finds continuity across it and another finds none.","Copper made an inland member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worth taking, and is read as the reason Idalion was in the end annexed by Kition."],"tags":["state","history","archaeology","greece","cyprus","iron age"],"sources":["Christian Körner, review of <i>Les royaumes de Chypre à l'épreuve de l'histoire: transitions et ruptures de la fin de l'âge du Bronze au début de l'époque hellénistique</i>, ed. Anna Cannavò and Ludovic Thély, Bulletin de correspondance hellénique Supplément 60, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.39, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.39/. [Open access]","Christian Körner, review of <i>La naissance des cités-royaumes cypriotes</i>, by Thierry Petit, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.06.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.06.02/. [Open access]","Gerald Cadogan, review of <i>Kition de Chypre</i>, by Marguerite Yon, Guides archéologiques de l'Institut français d'archéologie du Proche-Orient 4, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.64, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.64/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>The Topography of Ancient Idalion and its Territory</i>, by Stephan G. Schmid and Will M. Kennedy, Studia Cyprologica Berolinensia 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.03.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.03.36/. [Open access]","David Gill, review of <i>Archaic Cyprus: A Study of the Textual and Archaeological Evidence</i>, by A. T. Reyes, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.34. [Open access]","Grace Erny, review of <i>The Connected Iron Age: Interregional Networks in the Eastern Mediterranean, 900-600 BCE</i>, ed. Jonathan M. Hall and James F. Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.08/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Cyprus","at":[33,35],"zoom":5},"why":[{"q":"Why is the date the kingdoms formed one of the sharper arguments?","a":"Because one reading finds no general break at the turn of the Bronze Age, while the other holds the evidence for a political system carrying through to the 8th century meagre even at the sites usually cited."},{"q":"Why did copper make an inland kingdom worth taking?","a":"Because it is read as the reason Idalion became a desirable target and was in the end annexed by Kition."},{"q":"Why is the pottery gap behind Amathous read as a choice?","a":"Because the town tombs are full of Greek and Levantine wares while the valleys behind yielded almost none, the inland dead being given iron weapons instead."}]},
{"id":"gr-159","num":"159","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Greek was written on Cyprus in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the 8th century BCE into Hellenistic times, an Aegean script akin to Linear B and read since 1872.","answer":"Cypriot syllabary","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cypriot syllabary</b> is the script in which Greek was written on Cyprus from the 8th century BCE well into Hellenistic times, an Aegean syllabic script akin to Linear B.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was read from 1872 onwards, far earlier than the Aegean scripts it resembles, so that Greek in a syllabary was legible while the Bronze Age tablets were still shut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind it stands Cypro-Minoan, the Late Bronze Age writing of the island, whose signary runs to 112 identified signs with their variants and which no attempt has yet succeeded in reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That earlier script was in use between about 1650 and 1050 BCE, largely for trade, cut into vessel handles, weights and oxhide ingots, and it turns up at Enkomi and across the water at Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The link forward is argued from signs that share identical or close form and phonetic values with Linear B, which points to some genetic connection between the Aegean systems and the Cypriot ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Linear B survives only as administration, where the syllabary was never so confined: it is attested for all the usual epigraphic genres, and well over a thousand texts are now known against the 456 in Masson's corpus of 1983.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It served languages besides Greek, Eteocypriot among them, and at Golgoi an unread local tongue called Golgian appears in syllabic inscriptions as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The alphabet reached the island in the 6th century BCE and the two ran together for centuries, so that a grave marker from Golgoi names the dead man twice, as <i>ka-ru-xe</i> in syllabic Greek and as <i>ΚΑΡΥΞΕΜΙ</i> in alphabetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Kafizin more than 300 dedications to the Nymphs, dated between 225 and 218 BCE, carry both scripts on the same pot, while at Idalion nothing but the syllabary has turned up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nicocles, the last king of Paphos, reformed his own signary into a deliberately archaic form, and the script and the dialect it carried were still in use at the end of the 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cypriot syllabary","questions":["A grave marker from Golgoi names the dead man twice, once in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and once in the Greek alphabet, the two scripts running side by side for centuries.","Behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands Cypro-Minoan, the Bronze Age writing of the island, which has 112 identified signs and which nobody has yet succeeded in reading."],"tags":["concept","technology","history","greece","cyprus","iron age"],"sources":["Andreas Willi, review of <i>Le dialecte grec ancien de Chypre. Tome I: Grammaire; Tome II: Répertoire des inscriptions en syllabaire chypro-grec</i>, by Markus Egetmeyer, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/le-dialecte-grec-ancien-de-chypre.tome-i-grammaire-tome-ii-repertoire-des-inscriptions-en-syllabaire-chypro-grec/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>Cypro-Minoan and its Writers: At Home and Overseas</i>, by Cassandra M. Donnelly, Cambridge Elements: Writing in the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.11.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.11.24/. [Open access]","Vassilis Petrakis, review of <i>Syllabic Writing on Cyprus and its Context</i>, ed. Philippa M. Steele, Cambridge Classical Studies, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.04.53, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.53/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>Inscriptiones Graecae XV: Inscriptiones Cypri, Pars II Inscriptiones Cypri alphabeticae, Fasc. 1</i>, ed. Maria Kantiréa and Daniela Summa, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.12.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.12.26/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>The Topography of Ancient Idalion and its Territory</i>, by Stephan G. Schmid and Will M. Kennedy, Studia Cyprologica Berolinensia 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.03.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.03.36/. [Open access]","Beatrice Pestarino, review of <i>Classical Cyprus: Proceedings of the Conference, University of Graz, 21-23 September 2017</i>, ed. Maria Christidis, Antoine Hermary, Gabriele Koiner and Anja Ulbrich, Kypriaka 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.43/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Idalion_bilingual_and_the_decipherment_of_the_the_Cypriot_syllabary.jpg/1920px-Idalion_bilingual_and_the_decipherment_of_the_the_Cypriot_syllabary.jpg","title":"Cypriot syllabary","desc":"Idalion bilingual and the decipherment of the the Cypriot syllabary. George Smith. 1871.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idalion_bilingual_and_the_decipherment_of_the_the_Cypriot_syllabary.jpg","alt":"Idalion bilingual and the decipherment of the the Cypriot syllabary"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was Greek in this script legible before Linear B was?","a":"Because the syllabary was read from 1872 onwards, far earlier than the Aegean scripts it resembles, so Cypriot Greek could be read while the Bronze Age tablets were still shut."},{"q":"Why is the script's range wider than Linear B's?","a":"Because Linear B survives only as administration, where this is attested for all the usual epigraphic genres, with well over a thousand texts now known."},{"q":"Why can one grave marker be read twice over?","a":"Because the alphabet reached the island in the 6th century BCE and the two ran together for centuries, so a Golgoi marker names the dead man in both scripts."}]},
{"id":"gr-160","num":"160","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Athenian festival of the Synoikia gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name, and the Greek word means, quite literally, 'the coming together of houses'.","answer":"synoecism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Synoecism</b> is several communities becoming one state, and the word is more literal than it looks: the Athenian festival of the Synoikia means 'the coming together of houses'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it does not say is whether anybody moved, since it covers a political union among communities that stay where they are as readily as a physical gathering of them into one town, and the two have to be kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Argos a synoecism of the 8th century BCE, which gave that city the mastery of the Argolid, is beyond doubt, while the place itself took an urban shape only centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind the distinction sits the question the whole subject turns on, whether anywhere can be counted a full polis when it has no town at its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenians told the story as Theseus', and four possibilities for the date and the nature of that synoecism have been laid out without any being settled on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One account goes further and holds that the Athenians misread their own festival, a celebration of a synoecism being properly a Synoikisteria, and that the Attic state was assembled out of phratry lodges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A reviewer is unpersuaded, unwilling to write off the 6th-century events Herodotus records as fictions projected backwards out of ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cases that can be checked are the late ones, and they do not always do what the sources say: Diodorus and Strabo have Elis made a city about 471 BCE, where the digging and the inscriptions show a nucleated settlement already working as a polis, so it reads as a reinforcement rather than a creation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rhodes is the clearest instance of the other kind, Ialysos, Cameiros and Lindos becoming one state in 408/7 BCE, after which the priest of Helios gave the island's year its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It could also be done to people rather than by them, Mausolos forcing half a dozen small native settlements together into a new and much enlarged Halikarnassos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"synoecism","questions":["Marcel Piérart would keep two things apart under the heading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a political union of communities that stay put, and their physical gathering into one town.","In 408/7 BCE Ialysos, Cameiros and Lindos made one state by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the priest of Helios became the magistrate the Rhodian year was named after."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Bob Develin, review of <i>Festivals and Legends: The Formation of Greek Cities in the Light of Public Ritual</i>, by Noel Robertson, Phoenix Supplementary Volume 31, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.03.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.03.11/. [Open access]","Marek Wecowski, review of <i>La naissance de la ville dans l'antiquité</i>, ed. Michel Reddé et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.18/. [Open access]","Jon Mikalson, review of <i>Athenian Religion: A History</i>, by Robert Parker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.05.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.05.01/. [Open access]","Matthew Dillon, review of <i>Even More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 6, Historia Einzelschriften 162, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.14. [Open access]","Gaia De Luca, review of <i>Religious Life in Late Classical and Hellenistic Rhodes</i>, by Juliane Zachhuber, Oxford Classical Monographs, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.10.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.10.04/. [Open access]","Gary Reger, review of <i>The Hellenistic Settlements in Europe, the Islands, and Asia Minor</i>, by Getzel M. Cohen, Hellenistic Culture and Society 17, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.11.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.11.19/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why must two kinds of synoecism be kept apart?","a":"Because the word covers a political union among communities that stay where they are as readily as a physical gathering of them into one town."},{"q":"Why does Argos show the distinction so clearly?","a":"Because a synoecism of the 8th century BCE gave the city mastery of the Argolid, while the place itself took an urban shape only centuries afterwards."},{"q":"Why do the checkable late cases not always match the sources?","a":"Because Diodorus and Strabo have Elis made a city about 471 BCE, where the digging and the inscriptions show a nucleated settlement already working as a polis."}]},
{"id":"gr-161","num":"161","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A town and the body of citizens whose town it was are the two senses the Greeks ran together in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which one inventory lists 1,035.","answer":"polis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Known poleis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 1,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>polis</b> is the form of community most of the Greek world organised itself into, and the word carries two senses at once, an urban centre and the body of citizens whose centre it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Copenhagen Polis Centre set out to establish what the Greeks themselves meant by it, by listing every settlement contemporary sources explicitly call a polis, and its inventory discusses 1,035 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A place earns its entry on what it was called and what it left behind, tested against toponymy and ethnics, territory, constitution, dependent status, civic subdivisions, walls, coins, religious organisation and urban features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city-ethnic, the name borne by a person of the place rather than by the place itself, is the most dependable sign of the status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence turns out not to be required at all, since a dependent polis was still a polis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Autonomia is self-government of a limited sort rather than anything like the modern notion of autonomy, and a settlement Greek writers declined to call a polis was a kome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the thing arose is the argument: one case puts a structural revolution in 8th-century Greece, tying the rise of the polis to the enormous changes in material culture of that same century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Against it the shift is called far less drastic than usually posited and the 6th century the one that matters, buildings with specialised political functions appearing only then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The word's own history is turned round with it, the linguistic weight falling on the polis as a place, with the community of citizens looking like a secondary meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On that account the gathering of people into towns began after the Bronze Age collapse and was still unfinished in the classical period, and the places that became poleis first were those with a Mycenaean past behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"polis","questions":["A structural revolution in the 8th century BCE is put behind the rise of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, against the answer that the 6th century matters more.","Independence was never required of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a dependent one was still counted as such, and the surest sign of the status is the name borne by its people."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of 'Poleis'</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Historisk-Filosofiske Meddelelser 74, and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Kurt Raaflaub, Historia Einzelschriften 108, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Frederick Naerebout, review of <i>An Inventory of Archaic and Classical Poleis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.02.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.02.29/. [Open access]","Simon Goldhill, review of <i>Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Kurt A. Raaflaub, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1996.10.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.10.11/. [Open access]","Sarah C. Murray, review of <i>An Age of Experiment: Classical Archaeology Transformed (1976-2014)</i>, ed. Lisa Nevett and James Whitley, McDonald Institute Monographs, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.40/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>Archaic Greece: New Approaches and New Evidence</i>, ed. N. R. E. Fisher, Hans van Wees and Deborah Dickmann Boedeker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.11.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.11.27/. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200-479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg/1920px-Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg","title":"polis","desc":"View from Philopappos Hill in Athens (Attica, Greece) — Acropolis of Athens. A.Savin.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attica_06-13_Athens_50_View_from_Philopappos_-_Acropolis_Hill.jpg","alt":"Attica 06-13 Athens 50 View from Philopappos - Acropolis Hill"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is independence not required for the status?","a":"Because a dependent polis was still a polis, and autonomia means self-government of a limited sort rather than anything like the modern notion of autonomy."},{"q":"Why is the city-ethnic the most dependable sign?","a":"Because it is the name borne by a person of the place rather than by the place itself, so it records how the community was reckoned rather than how big it was."},{"q":"Why is the date of the polis's rise argued?","a":"Because one case ties it to the enormous changes in material culture of the 8th century, while another calls the shift far less drastic and points to the 6th, when buildings with specialised political functions appear."}]},
{"id":"gr-162","num":"162","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Greek people spread over a region called itself by its own ethnic when it acted as a state, so an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears in the record as 'the Aetolians' or 'the Boeotians'.","answer":"ethnos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>ethnos</b> is a Greek people settled across a region rather than gathered into one town: the Thessalians, Boeotians, Aetolians, Acarnanians, Phocians, Arcadians and Achaeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Acting as a state such a people called itself by its own ethnic, 'the Aetolians' or 'the Boeotians', where scholars say koinon, an ancient word used for a community, for the body that passed decrees and for a federal assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Religion did much of the work of making one, shared sanctuaries and festivals building a sense of community beyond the single polis in Boeotia, while in Aetolia it came before the polis grew at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The leagues worked as interest groups competing for resources, with federal coinage, taxation and arbitration between members, the Aetolian League arising in the 4th century BCE against territorial loss and outside aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How tight the union was is another matter, since a third of the Aetolian and Peloponnesian coins tested fall short of 90% silver, and a member city kept its own standards so long as the coin carried the same message.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Whether an ethnos was ever one thing is doubted, the Aeolians having been called a phantom one, since togetherness need not translate into political expression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reading ethnic claims were pressed by cities only when it served their own purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thessaly refuses the choice between a full federation and a loose ethnic affiliation, internal competition keeping the region going until Philip II ended its independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The upland peoples of Phocis, Aetolia, Acarnania and Arcadia mounted sophisticated defence through the 5th and 4th centuries BCE with no central government, giving up ground and striking without warning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Phocians are said to have whitened themselves with chalk about 490 BCE and fallen on the enemy camp to kill four thousand Thessalians, and in 426 the Aetolians abandoned their land, turned, and killed 300 Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"ethnos","questions":["The Aeolians have been called a phantom <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a sense of ethnic togetherness need not translate into any political expression at all.","The upland peoples who made up an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Phocis, Aetolia, Acarnania and Arcadia defended themselves ably with no central federal government at all."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Elizabeth A. Meyer, review of <i>Greek Federal Terminology</i>, by Jacek Rzepka, Akanthina 12, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.05.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.05.27/. [Open access]","Roy van Wijk, review of <i>Ethnos and Koinon: Studies in Ancient Greek Ethnicity and Federalism</i>, ed. Hans Beck, Kostas Buraselis and Alex McAuley, Heidelberger Althistorische Beiträge und Epigraphische Studien 61, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.11.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.11.11/. [Open access]","Jacek Rzepka, review of <i>Creating a Common Polity: Religion, Economy, and Politics in the Making of the Greek Koinon</i>, by Emily Mackil, Hellenistic Culture and Society 55, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.58, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.58/. [Open access]","Nicholas Cross, review of <i>Greek Warfare beyond the Polis: Defense, Strategy, and the Making of Ancient Federal States</i>, by David A. Blome, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.03.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.03.08/. [Open access]","Roy van Wijk, review of <i>The \"Koina\" of Southern Greece: Historical and Numismatic Studies in Ancient Greek Federalism</i>, ed. Catherine Grandjean, Numismatica antiqua 12, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.05/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why did religion do so much of the work of making a people?","a":"Because shared sanctuaries and festivals built a sense of community beyond the single polis in Boeotia, and in Aetolia came before the polis grew at all."},{"q":"Why is it doubted that an ethnos was ever one thing?","a":"Because togetherness need not translate into political expression — the Aeolians have been called a phantom one — and ethnic claims were pressed by cities when it served their purposes."},{"q":"Why could such peoples fight effectively without a central government?","a":"Because the upland peoples mounted sophisticated defence by giving up ground and striking without warning, as the Aetolians did in 426 by abandoning their land and turning."}]},
{"id":"gr-163","num":"163","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Every four years from 776 BCE by tradition, games were held at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the sanctuary of Zeus in the western Peloponnese that grew out of a cult place of about 900 BCE.","answer":"Olympia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Games from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">776 BCE, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Olympia</b> is the great sanctuary of the western Peloponnese where the Olympic games were held, and the ground under it was in use long before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Beneath the later Pelopion lies a mound ringed and covered with stones, of Early Helladic II date rather than Mycenaean, no tomb but a focus of rituals, its top demonstrably still visible in the Early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The earliest objects from the site are Late Neolithic, while the oldest cult activity that can be reliably ascribed, ash from the altar with terracotta and bronze votives, runs from 900 to 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The famous black layer holds kylikes of the late 11th century BCE and protogeometric jugs at its lowest and material of the advanced 7th at its top, and it was not laid down gradually but in a few brief grading operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That bears on the traditional date of the games, since the earliest wells belong to the early 7th century BCE, substantially later than 776.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The offerings have been read in three stages, of which the first, before 800 BCE, has Olympia as a meeting place for the petty chiefs of the west, with some Arcadian jewellery and small bronzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Through the 8th century the figurines stay small and simple while the tripods grow larger and more elaborate and are mostly brought from workshops elsewhere, the elites of emerging poleis competing beyond their own borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 7th century pottery appears with a settlement at Elis beside the sanctuary, and tripods fall away as dedications here while becoming civic symbols in other places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> András Patay-Horváth would derive the games from elite hunting of wild cattle in the region, the tripods and animal figurines being its traces and a ritual chase its enactment, which his reviewer calls innovative but speculative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A rural sanctuary served as a rallying point where neighbouring communities exchanged hospitality, which is what this was long before Elis held it, as it did from at least 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olympia","questions":["The offerings at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been read as marking a meeting place for the petty chiefs of the west before 800 BCE, with Arcadian jewellery and small bronzes.","A mound of the 3rd millennium BCE lies beneath the Pelopion at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, no tomb but a focus of rituals, its top still visible in the Early Iron Age."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>Olympia: Archäologie eines Heiligtums</i>, by Helmut Kyrieleis, Zaberns Bildbände zur Archäologie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.06.15. [Open access]","William Childs, review of <i>Olympia 1875-2000: 125 Jahre deutsche Ausgrabungen</i>, ed. Helmut Kyrieleis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.02.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.02.26. [Open access]","Dina Guth, review of <i>The Origins of the Olympic Games</i>, by András Patay-Horváth, Archaeolingua Series Minor 36, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.47, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.47/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","Nicola Giaccone, review of <i>Olympia: A Cultural History</i>, by Judith M. Barringer, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.21/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Restored_ruins_of_the_Temple_of_Hera%2C_ancient_Doric_Greek_temple_at_Olympia%2C_Greece_%2816187889508%29.jpg/1920px-Restored_ruins_of_the_Temple_of_Hera%2C_ancient_Doric_Greek_temple_at_Olympia%2C_Greece_%2816187889508%29.jpg","title":"Olympia","desc":"Restored ruins of the Temple of Hera, ancient Doric Greek temple at Olympia, Greece. 11:06. Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Restored_ruins_of_the_Temple_of_Hera,_ancient_Doric_Greek_temple_at_Olympia,_Greece_(16187889508).jpg","alt":"Restored ruins of the Temple of Hera, ancient Doric Greek temple at Olympia, Greece"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Olympia","at":[21.63,37.6383]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the black layer bear on the traditional date of the games?","a":"Because it was laid down in a few brief grading operations rather than gradually, and the earliest wells belong to the early 7th century BCE, substantially later than 776."},{"q":"Why do the changing offerings show competition between cities?","a":"Because through the 8th century the figurines stay small and simple while the tripods grow larger and are mostly brought from workshops elsewhere, elites competing beyond their own borders."},{"q":"Why is the wild-cattle theory of the games' origin only speculative?","a":"Because it derives them from elite hunting with the tripods and animal figurines as traces, which its reviewer calls innovative but speculative."}]},
{"id":"gr-164","num":"164","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"On the flank of Parnassus stood <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the sanctuary of Apollo whose oracle was the most famous in the Greek world, with cult there from the 8th century BCE.","answer":"Delphi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Delphi</b> is the sanctuary of Apollo on the flank of Parnassus that housed the most famous oracle of the Greek world, and there is evidence of settlement in the area from the Mycenaean period to the end of antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cult is late by comparison, since the earliest evidence is of the 8th century rather than the 10th, and here the settlement comes first and is eventually displaced by the cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In that it differs from Olympia, which it otherwise resembles in being geographically remote and in holding large numbers of early votives, competitive dedications by the elite among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle, on Morgan's reading, is panhellenic from the start, consulted on community questions probably beyond the collective wisdom of the local elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others would rather take it for a discussion-inciting force, a catalyst for negotiation instead of a unilateral authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The bronzes of the late 8th and 7th centuries BCE are where the sanctuary's reach shows, tripods and cauldrons and vessels with griffin, siren and bull protomai fixed to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their provenance tables count 24 pieces from north Syria, 15 griffins from Samos, 14 from Crete, 13 from Cyprus and 12 more Levantine but unplaced, numbers that reflect the importance of the East and the processes of Orientalization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The building came later than the giving, the earliest historic temple of Apollo being put at about 600 BCE, a date inferred from reused blocks of Sikyonian poros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before Apollo the shrine is said to have belonged to the Python, and the springs of Castalia and Kassotis rise on the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cult did carry through the end of the Bronze Age elsewhere in Phocis, at Kalapodi, where intensive activity is securely dated from the second half of the 9th century BCE, although it is still unknown whether that sanctuary is Artemis of Hyampolis or the oracle of Apollo at Abai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Delphi","questions":["Settlement comes first and cult eventually displaces it at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the earliest evidence is of the 8th century rather than the 10th.","The oracle at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is held to have been panhellenic from the start, consulted on questions probably beyond the collective wisdom of the local elite."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>Das antike Delphi</i>, by Michael Maass, Beck'sche Reihe 2431, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.06.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.06.24/. [Open access]","Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World, Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Bronzes du haut-archaïsme à Delphes: Trépieds, chaudrons et vaisselle de bronze (fin VIIIe-VIIe siècle)</i>, by Hélène Aurigny, Fouilles de Delphes 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.08/. [Open access]","Nassos Papalexandrou, review of <i>Kalapodi: Ergebnisse der Ausgrabungen im Heiligtum der Artemis und des Apollon von Hyampolis in der antiken Phokis</i>, ed. Rainer C. S. Felsch et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.09.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.09.23. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/%27Delphi%27_by_Edward_Lear%2C_watercolor%2C_12_by_19_cm..jpg","title":"Delphi","desc":"'Delphi' by Edward Lear, watercolor, 12 by 19 cm. Edward Lear.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%27Delphi%27_by_Edward_Lear,_watercolor,_12_by_19_cm..jpg","alt":"'Delphi' by Edward Lear, watercolor, 12 by 19 cm."},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Delphi","at":[22.5013,38.4823]},"why":[{"q":"Why does this sanctuary differ from Olympia in its sequence?","a":"Because here the settlement comes first and is eventually displaced by the cult, whose earliest evidence is of the 8th century rather than the 10th."},{"q":"Why do the bronzes show the sanctuary's reach?","a":"Because the provenance tables count 24 pieces from north Syria, 15 griffins from Samos, 14 from Crete and 13 from Cyprus, numbers reflecting the importance of the East."},{"q":"Why is the oracle's role disputed?","a":"Because Morgan reads it as panhellenic from the start, consulted on questions beyond the local elite's wisdom, while others take it for a catalyst for negotiation rather than a unilateral authority."}]},
{"id":"gr-165","num":"165","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Bronze Age settlement of some 35,000 m² (8.6 acres) inside a circuit wall, one of the largest in the Aegean, lies beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Hera was later worshipped.","answer":"Heraion of Samos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 BCE, by its altars</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th century BCE, perhaps</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Altar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Heraion of Samos</b> is the sanctuary of Hera on that island, and it stands on ground that was crowded long before any god was worshipped there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beneath it lies a settlement with a nearly continuous sequence running from the Middle Chalcolithic, the second half of the 5th millennium BCE, into the Middle Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the late Early Bronze Age it was one of the largest settlements in the Aegean and western Anatolia, some 35,000 m² (8.6 acres) enclosed in a circuit wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sanctuary's own rise is traced from the first Greek settlers at the end of the Bronze Age, shown by its early altars, with a monumental temple standing there perhaps already in the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two much larger replacements followed in the 6th, together with the Great Altar and sculptural dedications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Telling a temple from a temple-like building is the difficulty of the place, ten of them standing in the archaic period, and many are argued to have been oriented towards the great altar of Hera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others sit along the main routes through the sanctuary beside votive sculpture, which marks them out as treasuries, and the thrice life-size Isches kouros is the grandest thing given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was brought there is what makes the site remarkable, and Kyrieleis puts it plainly: no other excavation in Greece has produced as many imports, among them Egyptian and Near Eastern reliefs and figurines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of 1,105 dedications reads Hera through them, finding her tied to agriculture and vegetation and receiving military offerings, with 35 building models taken for her care of home and family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The local can be set against all that traffic, since the myths, dedications and festivals of Samian Hera, the Toneia and the Heraia among them, carry a flavour of their own and a maritime cast, the cult shifting from global to local over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Heraion of Samos","questions":["No other excavation in Greece is said to have produced as many imports as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them Egyptian and Near Eastern reliefs and figurines.","Ten temple-like buildings stood at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the archaic period, many oriented towards the great altar and others marked out as treasuries by the sculpture beside them."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>Sanctuaries and the Sacred in the Ancient Greek World</i>, by John Griffiths Pedley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.10.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.10.20/. [Open access]","Nassos Papalexandrou, review of <i>The Significance of Votive Offerings in Selected Hera Sanctuaries in the Peloponnese, Ionia and Western Greece</i>, by Jens David Baumbach, BAR International Series 1249, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.05.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.05.55/. [Open access]","Daniel J. Pullen, review of <i>Ausgrabungen in der frühbronzezeitlichen Siedlung im Heraion von Samos 1966</i>, by Hans Peter Isler, Samos 30, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.01.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.01.34/. [Open access]","Ranjani Atur, review of <i>The Local Horizon of Ancient Greek Religion</i>, ed. Hans Beck and Julia Kindt, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.05.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.05.16/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Kore_Heraion_Samos_Louvre_Ma686.jpg","title":"Heraion of Samos","desc":"Kore (archaic statue of a young maiden) from the Heraion of Samos. Bears an inscription in Ionian alphabet: \"Cheramyes dedicated me to Hera, as a gift\". Marble, made in Samos, ca. 570–560 BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kore_Heraion_Samos_Louvre_Ma686.jpg","alt":"Kore Heraion Samos Louvre"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Heraion of Samos","at":[26.8856,37.6719]},"why":[{"q":"Why is telling a temple from a temple-like building the site's difficulty?","a":"Because ten of them stood there in the archaic period, and while many are argued to face the great altar of Hera, others sit along the main routes beside votive sculpture, which marks them as treasuries."},{"q":"Why is the sanctuary remarkable for what was brought to it?","a":"Because no other excavation in Greece has produced as many imports, among them Egyptian and Near Eastern reliefs and figurines."},{"q":"Why can a local Hera be set against all that traffic?","a":"Because the myths, dedications and festivals of Samian Hera carry a flavour of their own and a maritime cast, the cult shifting from global to local over time."}]},
{"id":"gr-166","num":"166","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The sanctuary of Hera Akraia stood at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than in Corinth itself, and the children of Medea are argued to have been buried there.","answer":"Perachora","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Expansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Perachora</b> is a sanctuary of Hera on the far side of the gulf from Corinth, one of the places through which that city ordered its sacred landscape, along with Isthmia and Temple Hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Real changes in the material expression of religious belief appear across the Corinthia in the 8th century BCE, and a period of great expansion at Perachora comes with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her study of where wealth was put down, in graves against sanctuaries, has the two changing places about 750 BCE, when it moves out of the grave and into cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> De Polignac had brought the region into his account as early as 1984, mentioning Perachora and listing Isthmia as a border sanctuary in the 8th century and the site of a major temple in the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Which Hera stood here has been argued over, and B. Menadier puts the sanctuary of Hera Akraia at Perachora rather than at Corinth itself, on the archaeology and on the texts together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the same reading the children of Medea were buried here rather than in the middle of Corinth, and this was the only seat of the rites owed them, the cenotaph Pausanias saw in the city being a Roman monument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The place is rich in small valuable things and in imports, and a study of Hera's sanctuaries counts 3,417 votive offerings from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among them are four terracotta building models, bone pipes of the 6th century BCE whose purpose is still argued over, and Egyptian or Egyptianizing figures of Bes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across the Geometric and Archaic periods the Hera worshipped here stays the same kind of goddess, concerned with growing up, with home and family, and with agriculture and vegetation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of its buildings has been read as a hestiatorion, a hall for ritual dining, although the identification has never been settled, and much of what is said about the site still rests on a publication of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Perachora","questions":["A study of Hera's sanctuaries counts 3,417 votive offerings from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them four terracotta building models and Egyptian figures of Bes.","Corinth ordered its sacred landscape through Isthmia, Temple Hill and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last of which expanded greatly in the 8th century BCE."],"tags":["place","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","Gina Salapata, review of <i>Peloponnesian Sanctuaries and Cults: Proceedings of the Ninth International Symposium at the Swedish Institute at Athens</i>, Acta Instituti Atheniensis Regni Sueciae Series 4, 48, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.04.30. [Open access]","Nassos Papalexandrou, review of <i>The Significance of Votive Offerings in Selected Hera Sanctuaries in the Peloponnese, Ionia and Western Greece</i>, by Jens David Baumbach, BAR International Series 1249, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.05.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.05.55/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>Ancient Greek Cult Practice from the Archaeological Evidence</i>, ed. Robin Hägg, Acta Instituti Atheniensis Regni Sueciae 8°, 15, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.08.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.08.16/. [Open access]","Paul Scotton, review of <i>The Archaeology of Ancient Greece</i>, by James Whitley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.09.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.09.41/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/%CE%A0%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%B1%CF%87%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%B1_%CE%9A%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BD%CE%B8%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg/1920px-%CE%A0%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%B1%CF%87%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%B1_%CE%9A%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BD%CE%B8%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","title":"Perachora","desc":"The plain of Perachora in the Corinthia seen from the air, the modern village spread across it and the Heraion lying on the headland beyond.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A0%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%B1%CF%87%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%B1_%CE%9A%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BD%CE%B8%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","alt":"Περαχώρα Κορινθίας"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Perachora","at":[22.95,38.0333]},"why":[{"q":"Why does wealth appear to change places about 750 BCE?","a":"Because the study of where it was put down finds it moving out of the grave and into cult across the Corinthia, with a period of great expansion at this sanctuary."},{"q":"Why does Menadier place Hera Akraia here rather than at Corinth?","a":"Because the archaeology and the texts together put the sanctuary here, making this the only seat of the rites owed Medea's children, the cenotaph Pausanias saw being a Roman monument."},{"q":"Why is much of what is said about the site provisional?","a":"Because it still rests on a publication of 1940, and identifications such as the hall for ritual dining have never been settled."}]},
{"id":"gr-167","num":"167","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An object given to a god and left in a sanctuary is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its inscription, where it has one, names the giver and the god.","answer":"votive dedication","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>votive dedication</b> is an object given to a god and left in a sanctuary, and where it carries an inscription that inscription says who gave it and to whom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 154 metrical inscriptions survive on Attic offerings of the 7th to 5th centuries BCE, most of them to Athena but others to Hermes, Poseidon, Aphrodite, Demeter and Kore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their dedicators run from the public offering to the individual benefactor, and take in women sustaining households and foreign craftsmen such as Euphron of Paros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The object is made to speak, and one of them answers a passer-by outright: 'To all men alike who ask I answer'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a text is a concise presentation of the act and ritual of dedication, built from a handful of basic elements and reactivated whenever the object is seen and the words are read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The gift on that account is a charis, an exchange between human and divine that renews itself at each reading, the epigram written for reading aloud and meant to be repeated over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The inscribing is itself a material act, one that sets the offering inside a network of people rather than simply labelling it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Olympia and on the Athenian Acropolis, where arms and armour were given in quantity, the inscribed and the uninscribed sit together in no tidy system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not everything given was bought, since wreaths of greenery, cakes shaped into figures and votives woven or cut from wood were made by the giver, though how that making felt is hard to recover from evidence so patchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was every dedication devout, those set up in the gymnasia answering to conventions political and institutional rather than to religious feeling, and 25 documents survive in which a Greek community regulates what may be placed in its sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"votive dedication","questions":["The epigram on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a concise presentation of the ritual, reactivated whenever the object is seen and the words are read aloud.","Inscribed and uninscribed examples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sit together at Olympia and on the Athenian Acropolis, where arms and armour were given in quantity, in no tidy system."],"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Alcorac Alonso Déniz, review of <i>Archaic and Classical Attic Dedicatory Epigrams: An Epigraphic, Literary and Linguistic Commentary</i>, by Sara Kaczko, Trends in Classics Supplementary Volumes 33, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.13/. [Open access]","Eleonora Santin, review of <i>Archaic Greek Epigram and Dedication: Representation and Reperformance</i>, by Joseph W. Day, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.01.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.01.22. [Open access]","Signe Barfoed, review of <i>The Stuff of the Gods: The Material Aspects of Religion in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Matthew Haysom, Maria Mili and Jenny Wallensten, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.08.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.08.09/. [Open access]","Vinciane Pirenne-Delforge, review of <i>Personal Experience and Material Devotion in Ancient Greek Religion</i>, by K. A. Rask, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.12.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.12.31/. [Open access]","Paraskevi Martzavou, review of <i>Dediche sacre nel mondo greco-romano: diffusione, funzioni, tipologie</i>, ed. John Bodel and Mika Kajava, Acta Instituti Romani Finlandiae 35, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.38/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/-11032901_Etruscan_Male_Votive_Head_III_cen._BC.jpg/1920px--11032901_Etruscan_Male_Votive_Head_III_cen._BC.jpg","title":"votive dedication","desc":"An Etruscan terracotta votive head, 3rd century BCE, 28 centimetres high.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-11032901_Etruscan_Male_Votive_Head_III_cen._BC.jpg","alt":"Etruscan male votive head, 3rd century BCE"},"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is an inscribed offering said to renew itself?","a":"Because the gift is a charis, an exchange reactivated whenever the object is seen and the words are read, the epigram written for reading aloud and meant to be repeated."},{"q":"Why is inscribing a material act rather than a label?","a":"Because it sets the offering inside a network of people rather than simply naming what it is."},{"q":"Why was not every dedication devout?","a":"Because those set up in the gymnasia answered to conventions political and institutional rather than to religious feeling."}]},
{"id":"gr-168","num":"168","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The three-legged bronze vessels called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as cooking and heating gear before becoming the most prestigious dedications in Greek sanctuaries.","answer":"bronze tripod cauldrons","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Prized from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In decline</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>bronze tripod cauldron</b> is a three-legged bronze vessel, and it began as a thing for cooking and heating before it became anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the Bronze Age some classes of tripod had passed beyond that use into status symbols, princely gifts and prizes, and through the Geometric and Early Archaic periods they were the most prestigious dedications made in Greek sanctuaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are the votives that embody prestige and tradition above all others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tripod has been read as the symbol above all of authoritative discourse, and so of political power and of holding ground, tied as well to truthful revelation and to the consulting of Apollo at Delphi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reading is worth having, though it has been warned against for theoretical overreach and for speculation set down as established fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They could be enormous, the monumental cauldrons of the 8th century BCE from the Athenian Acropolis reaching more than 2 m (6 ft 7 in) in height, with one gorgon-decorated ring handle reconstructed at 77 cm (30 inches) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those handles were too fragile to lift anything and served mainly as decoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Figures were fastened to them from the 9th century BCE, at first bulls' heads and birds on a minority of examples, then horses through the 8th and human forms from its middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Towards the end of that century the spear-brandishing warrior gives way to handle-holders, pairs of figurines naked but for their helmets grasping opposite sides of the ring, the community's act of dedication acted out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 7th century orientalising cauldrons arrive with griffin and siren attachments, most of them made on Samos and the same protomai fixed to the bronzes at Delphi, while at Olympia tripods fall away as offerings and become civic symbols elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"bronze tripod cauldrons","questions":["One ring handle from a monumental example of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reconstructed at 77 cm (30 inches) across, and was too fragile to lift anything.","Pairs of helmeted figurines grasping the ring handles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replace the spear-brandishing warrior towards the end of the 8th century BCE, standing for the community's dedication."],"tags":["object","technology","religion","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["M. Jones, review of <i>The Visual Poetics of Power: Warriors, Youths, and Tripods in Early Greece</i>, by Athanasios Christou Papalexandrou, Greek Studies: Interdisciplinary Approaches, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.08.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.08.23/. [Open access]","Helle Hochscheid, review of <i>Hephaistus on the Athenian Acropolis: Current Approaches to the Study of Artifacts Made of Bronze and Other Metals</i>, ed. Nassos Papalexandrou and Amy Sowder Koch, Selected Papers on Ancient Art and Architecture 7, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.01.08/. [Open access]","Richard Hamilton, review of <i>Greek Sanctuaries: New Approaches</i>, ed. Nanno Marinatos and Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.03.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.03.17. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Bronzes du haut-archaïsme à Delphes: Trépieds, chaudrons et vaisselle de bronze (fin VIIIe-VIIe siècle)</i>, by Hélène Aurigny, Fouilles de Delphes 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.08/. [Open access]","Stefanie A. H. Kennell, review of <i>Olympia: Archäologie eines Heiligtums</i>, by Helmut Kyrieleis, Zaberns Bildbände zur Archäologie, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.06.15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Bronze_tripod_cauldron_%282%29%2C_Late_Haelladic_III_C%2C_AM_of_Mycenae%2C_201680.jpg/1920px-Bronze_tripod_cauldron_%282%29%2C_Late_Haelladic_III_C%2C_AM_of_Mycenae%2C_201680.jpg","title":"bronze tripod cauldrons","desc":"Bronze tripod cauldron (2), Late Haelladic III C, 1180 - 1050 BCE. Archaeological Museum of Mycenae.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_tripod_cauldron_(2),_Late_Haelladic_III_C,_AM_of_Mycenae,_201680.jpg","alt":"Bronze tripod cauldron (2), Late Haelladic III C, AM of Mycenae"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did a cooking vessel become the grandest of dedications?","a":"Because by the Bronze Age some classes had passed beyond use into status symbols, princely gifts and prizes, and through the Geometric and Early Archaic periods they were the most prestigious offerings made."},{"q":"Why were the great ring handles decorative rather than functional?","a":"Because they were too fragile to lift anything, one gorgon-decorated example reconstructed at 77 centimetres across."},{"q":"Why do the handle-holders act out the dedication?","a":"Because towards the end of the 8th century the spear-brandishing warrior gives way to pairs of figurines grasping opposite sides of the ring, the community's own act shown on the object."}]},
{"id":"gr-169","num":"169","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A grave is called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because weapons were put in it, and at Fortetsa on Crete that is the whole of the reasoning.","answer":"warrior burial","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>warrior burial</b> is a grave taken for a fighting man's because weapons were put into it, and the identification is usually made on that ground alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At chamber tomb P at Fortetsa near Knossos, among the most important tombs on Crete in the Early Iron Age, a bronze girdle and a quiver accompanied two burials in pithoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> D'Acunto takes both to be warriors mainly because of the weapons standing beside the cinerary urns, which is the reasoning the whole category rests on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the sample is large the pattern does hold, and a study of 1,146 burials from Macedonia between 550 and 300 BCE finds weapons the purview of men, with elite men often presented in death as warriors, though not as Homeric ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same study finds little sign of women's part in war, since weapons are not usually found in their graves, while elite women reached for other marks of standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Reading a person off their goods is riskier than it looks, and the Athenian Agora cemeteries make the point: 83 tombs were catalogued and 53 bodies examined, and the burial long called the 'Young Lady' turns out to be probably male.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Weapons were also not the only way to be conspicuous in death, since 11 Athenian graves of the Middle and Late Geometric use bronze cauldrons as cremation urns, which Simona Dalsoglio reads as an exceptionally elite display bound up with feasting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Several contributors to the same volume argue for kinship groupings within the cemeteries, so a grave may say as much about a family as about the person laid in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the hinterland of Amathous on Cyprus the choice ran the other way, iron weapons serving as the display where the town's own tombs took imported pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A warrior burial therefore names a way of being buried rather than a proven occupation, and what the weapons announce may be rank, family or the manner of a funeral as much as a life spent fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"warrior burial","questions":["Behind every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies the finding of a study of 1,146 Macedonian graves, that weapons were the purview of men and elite men were presented in death as warriors.","The burial long called the 'Young Lady' in the Athenian Agora is probably male, a reminder that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read from its goods rather than its bones."],"tags":["practice","warfare","archaeology","history","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos, The Athenian Agora 36, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]","Vyron Antoniadis, review of <i>Kreta in der geometrischen und archaischen Zeit</i>, ed. W.-D. Niemeier, O. Pilz and I. Kaiser, Athenaia 2, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.29. [Open access]","Sabine Müller, review of <i>Age, Gender and Status in Macedonian Society, 550-300 BCE: Intersectional Approaches to Mortuary Archaeology</i>, by Elina M. Salminen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.08.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.08.30/. [Open access]","Nathan Arrington, review of <i>Rethinking Athens before the Persian Wars</i>, ed. Constanze Graml, Annarita Doronzio and Vincenzo Capozzoli, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.11.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.11.12/. [Open access]","Grace Erny, review of <i>The Connected Iron Age: Interregional Networks in the Eastern Mediterranean, 900-600 BCE</i>, ed. Jonathan M. Hall and James F. Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.08/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does the category rest on thin reasoning?","a":"Because a grave is called a warrior's because weapons were put in it, which is the identification made on that ground alone."},{"q":"Why do the Athenian Agora cemeteries warn against reading a person off their goods?","a":"Because 83 tombs were catalogued and 53 bodies examined, and the burial long called the 'Young Lady' turns out to be probably male."},{"q":"Why may a grave say as much about a family as a person?","a":"Because several contributors argue for kinship groupings within the cemeteries, so the display belongs to a group rather than to the one laid in it."}]},
{"id":"gr-170","num":"170","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A graph of burials rising steeply in Athens, Attica and the Argolid is the best-known evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it has since been taken apart.","answer":"eighth-century revival","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>eighth-century revival</b> is the name for the burst of change that closes the Greek Dark Age, and its best-known evidence is a graph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anthony Snodgrass published in 1980 a count of burials from Athens, Attica and the Argolid climbing steeply in the late 8th century BCE, and the climb was read as a rise in population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The count was taken apart in 1987, when adult and child burials were shown to have been run together and the 7th century left out, after which population rise ceased to be a plausible, or even a possible, explanation of the figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later estimates put it lower still: the yearly increase in Attica through the 8th century cannot pass 0.3 to 0.4 per cent, and cannot reach the 3 to 4 per cent hitherto assumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that view the sample is extremely small and not necessarily representative, and few conclusions drawn from it statistically deserve confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That the material changes is not in doubt, and the massive change in material culture of the 8th century BCE has been set beside the rise of the polis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The densification of settlement in Attica has been read as a mark of political unification, on the ground that such growth in the archaeological visibility of settlements must raise political questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Visibility is the word doing the work there, and a reviewer answers that the growth rates do not bear the reading, and that bronze tripod dedications on the acropolis do not begin only in the late 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even the century may be wrong, since the standard chronology for the Iron Age Aegean faces a stiff challenge from radiocarbon at Sindos and Sidon, which would move everything between about 1150 and 735 upward, in the earlier phases by over a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On that revision the invention of the alphabet would have to be raised well into the 9th century, although a dating of Homer to about 780 to 750 BCE is thought likely to remain cogent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"eighth-century revival","questions":["Attica's yearly population increase during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now held to be no more than 0.4 per cent, far below the 3 to 4 per cent once assumed.","Radiocarbon from Sindos and Sidon would move the whole chronology upward, so even the century named in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may turn out to be the wrong one."],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","greece","iron age"],"sources":["Robin Osborne, review of <i>A History of Greece: 1300 to 30 BC</i>, by Victor Parker, Blackwell History of the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.11/. [Open access]","Helen Hatzimavroudi, review of <i>Griechische Archaik: interne Entwicklungen - externe Impulse</i>, ed. Robert Rollinger and Christoph Ulf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.08.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.08.12. [Open access]","Maximilian Rönnberg, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World, Vol. II: Athens and Attica</i>, by Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.09.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.09.41/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>Archaic Greece: New Approaches and New Evidence</i>, ed. N. R. E. Fisher, Hans van Wees and Deborah Dickmann Boedeker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.11.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.11.27/. [Open access]","Richard Janko, review of <i>Homer and his Iliad</i>, by Robin Lane Fox, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.03.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.03.03/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the burial graph stop meaning a population rise?","a":"Because in 1987 adult and child burials were shown to have been run together and the 7th century left out, after which population rise ceased to be a possible explanation of the figures."},{"q":"Why is the word visibility doing the work in the settlement argument?","a":"Because growth in the archaeological visibility of settlements is what is measured, and a reviewer answers that the growth rates do not bear a reading of political unification."},{"q":"Why might even the century be wrong?","a":"Because radiocarbon at Sindos and Sidon would move everything between about 1150 and 735 upward, in the earlier phases by over a century."}]},
{"id":"gr-171","num":"171","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Greek cities built their earliest laws into sanctuary walls, and the block from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was set on the east wall of the temple of Apollo Delphinios.","answer":"Dreros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctuary from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dreros</b> is a Cretan city whose temple wall carried the oldest law surviving from anywhere in the Greek world, and the first text in which the word 'polis' appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The block stood on the east wall of the temple of Apollo Delphinios, and it rules that a man who has held the office of kosmos may not hold it again for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kosmos was the most important office the city had, and the law sets out punishments for anyone found breaking the rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Three bodies swore the oath that confirmed it, the kosmos, the damioi and the ikati of the polis, and the last two are offices nobody can now identify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is dated between 650 and 600 BCE, with the earliest legal inscriptions from Gortyn, and belongs to a turn by Greek cities towards inscribing law and displaying it in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The publication of such a law has been read as a stage in the growth of a communal self-awareness, one that pushed people to identify with the polis of Dreros itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Written law appears about 650 BCE and quickly becomes a fixture of civic life in many Greek cities though not all, most of it built into a sanctuary wall, the letters big, painted and up to 25 centimetres (10 inches) tall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lawgiving is attested for 53 Greek communities from Argos to Zancle, Dreros among them, and the famous law from Chios is later, at about 575 to 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Neither Dreros nor Gortyn was a democracy and both published their laws regardless, while the three swearing bodies have been read as authority that was multiple, overlapping and sometimes in conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Digging on the west acropolis has found three phases in the sanctuary, the offerings of the 7th-century phase richer than the deposit beneath them and perhaps the mark of a smaller and more powerful elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dreros","questions":["The temple wall at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the oldest Greek law that survives, forbidding a man to hold the office of kosmos again for ten years.","The word polis is first found in writing on a block from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the kosmos, the damioi and the ikati swore to keep the law."],"tags":["place","politics","history","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Alex Gottesman, review of <i>Lawmaking and Adjudication in Archaic Greece</i>, by Zinon Papakonstantinou, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.08.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.08.28. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>Writing Greek Law</i>, by Michael Gagarin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.06.49, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.49/. [Open access]","Vyron Antoniadis, review of <i>Cretan Cities: Formation and Transformation</i>, ed. Florence Gaignerot-Driessen and Jan Driessen, Aegis 7, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.17. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>Cité, démocratie et écriture: histoire de l'alphabétisation d'Athènes à l'époque classique</i>, by Christophe Pébarthe, Culture et cité 3, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.05.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.05.17. [Open access]","Eric Robinson, review of <i>Schiedsrichter, Gesetzgeber und Gesetzgebung im archaischen Griechenland</i>, by Karl-Joachim Hölkeskamp, Historia-Einzelschriften 131, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.04.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.04.16. [Open access]","Maria Ilaria De Rossi, “Dreros Law,” <i>Axon</i> 1, no. 2 (2017), https://doi.org/10.14277/2532-6848/Axon-1-2-17-01. [Open access]","Federica Carugati, Gillian K. Hadfield and Barry R. Weingast, “Building Legal Order in Ancient Athens,” <i>Journal of Legal Analysis</i> 7, no. 2 (2015): 291–324, https://doi.org/10.1093/jla/lav003. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Dreros_12.jpg/1920px-Dreros_12.jpg","title":"Dreros","desc":"Archaeological site of Dreros (Δρῆρος), municipality of Agios Nikolaos, Crete, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dreros_12.jpg","alt":"Dreros 12"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Dreros","at":[25.6283,35.2567]},"why":[{"q":"Why is a rule about holding office again worth cutting in stone?","a":"Because kosmos was the most important office the city had, so barring a man from repeating it for ten years is a limit on the city's own power."},{"q":"Why has publishing such a law been read as more than administration?","a":"Because it is taken as a stage in the growth of a communal self-awareness, one that pushed people to identify with the polis itself."},{"q":"Why does publishing law not imply democracy?","a":"Because neither Dreros nor Gortyn was one and both published their laws regardless, the three swearing bodies reading as authority that was multiple and sometimes in conflict."}]},
{"id":"gr-172","num":"172","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A Greek city's farms, tombs and country shrines lay in its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the land outside the town, and the inventory of poleis gives the size of each.","answer":"chora","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>chora</b> is the land a Greek city held outside its town, and the great inventory of archaic and classical poleis states the size of one for each of the 1,035 places it discusses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town itself is the astu, and the same introduction sets that word beside polis and polisma, each with its own meaning and reference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word 'polis' will not stay in the town: it is found used for the political community, for the urban centre and for the hinterland or territory alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been pressed that chora and polis cannot be separated, and that field survey is what shows where the people of a city actually lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her picture of the countryside has rural dwellings in it, and tombs and shrines with them, the Southern Argolid standing as the worked example.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The archaic city walled its town and not its land, and the forts and towers standing out in the territory are few and far between before 480 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> De Polignac made the rural sanctuary a rallying point that articulated a city's territory, some of them standing on the frontier between the inhabited world and the empty one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Carter's work at Metaponto denies it, holding that the rural shrines there mark no boundary, while Cole finds sanctuaries of Demeter in the town, beside it and out at the borders alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A chora could be laid out on a grid, the territory of Chersonesos organised on orthogonal lines and the European part of the Bosporan kingdom cut into rectangular plots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sparta drew the sharpest line of the lot, Cartledge finding town and chora divided by status and by obligation, and its greatest festival held out at Amyklai rather than in the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"chora","questions":["Sparta drew an exceptionally sharp line between the town and its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with lower status and different obligations for those who lived out there.","On the Black Sea the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Chersonesos was organised on orthogonal lines, and the European part of the Bosporan kingdom was likewise cut into rectangular plots."],"tags":["concept","geography","history","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Frederick Naerebout, review of <i>An Inventory of Archaic and Classical Poleis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.02.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.02.29/. [Open access]","Jacek Rzepka, review of <i>The Return of the Polis: The Use and Meanings of the Word Polis in Archaic and Classical Sources</i>, by Mogens Herman Hansen, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 8, Historia Einzelschriften 198, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.02.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.02.46/. [Open access]","Lise Hannestad and Sidsel Westh-Hansen, review of <i>Classical Archaeology</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Blackwell Studies in Global Archaeology 10, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.05.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.05.42/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Greek City Walls of the Archaic Period, 900–480 BC</i>, by Rune Frederiksen, Oxford Monographs on Classical Archaeology, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.05.08/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Surveying the Greek Chora: The Black Sea Region in a Comparative Perspective</i>, ed. Pia Guldager Bilde and Vladimir F. Stolba, Black Sea Studies 4, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.05.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.05.06/. [Open access]","Graham Shipley, review of <i>Sparta in Laconia: The Archaeology of a City and its Countryside</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh, BSA Studies Series 4, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.02. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why will the word polis not stay in the town?","a":"Because it is used for the political community, for the urban centre and for the hinterland or territory alike."},{"q":"Why is field survey what shows where a city's people lived?","a":"Because chora and polis cannot be separated, and the countryside holds rural dwellings with tombs and shrines among them."},{"q":"Why is de Polignac's frontier sanctuary disputed?","a":"Because Carter's work at Metaponto holds that the rural shrines there mark no boundary, and Cole finds sanctuaries of Demeter in the town, beside it and at the borders alike."}]},
{"id":"gr-173","num":"173","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Every Arkadian city but Mantineia built its walls around a patch of defended high ground, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> giving its name to a whole type of fortification.","answer":"acropolis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>acropolis</b> is the high ground at the heart of a Greek town, and the word 'polis' is held to have widened from it to the settlement around it and then to a whole territorial state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It names a kind of fortification as well as a place, the acropolis type leaning on natural defences where a horizontal type and an uneven one do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every Arkadian polis but Mantineia worked a naturally protected height into its circuit, 19 of them catalogued in detail with 14 more besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among archaic walls, Emporio on Chios encloses 2.4 hectares (5.9 acres) with nobody living inside it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Athens the walls Kylon's men were besieged behind in about 632 BCE were presumably Mycenaean, the Pelargikon among them, and no archaic circuit there has ever been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Athenian hill is the one everybody knows, in use from Neolithic times to the closing of the ancient sanctuary in the late 5th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A height carried cult as well as walls: the West Acropolis at Dreros holds a sanctuary of three phases, opening on offerings and animal bones of the late 8th or early 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Assos a temple of Athena went up on the acropolis about 540 BCE, built of andesite quarried from that same rock and mixing Doric, Ionic and Aeolic in one building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Which god held a height is not always recoverable, the sanctuary at Stymphalos having been given to Athena Polias on an inscription, to Artemis on its sculpture and to Artemis Eileithyia on the animal bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its temple is a plain house-like building with an altar and a court of five aniconic stone pillars beside it, and the offerings run to an archaic kore and over 325 pieces of jewellery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"acropolis","questions":["Athena Polias, Artemis and Artemis Eileithyia have all been argued for as the goddess of the sanctuary on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Stymphalos, and its excavators disagree.","Kylon's men were besieged at Athens in about 632 BCE behind walls on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that were presumably Mycenaean, no archaic circuit having ever been found there."],"tags":["place","warfare","religion","archaeology","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]","Matthias R. Nöth, review of <i>The Fortifications of Arkadian City States in the Classical and Hellenistic Periods</i>, by Matthew P. Maher, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.52, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.52/. [Open access]","John K. Papadopoulos, review of <i>Greek City Walls of the Archaic Period, 900–480 BC</i>, by Rune Frederiksen, Oxford Monographs on Classical Archaeology, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.05.08/. [Open access]","Susan I. Rotroff, review of <i>The Athenian Acropolis: History, Mythology, and Archaeology from the Neolithic Era to the Present</i>, by Jeffrey M. Hurwit, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.07.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.07.17. [Open access]","Vyron Antoniadis, review of <i>Cretan Cities: Formation and Transformation</i>, ed. Florence Gaignerot-Driessen and Jan Driessen, Aegis 7, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.17. [Open access]","Tuna Şare, review of <i>The Temple of Athena at Assos</i>, by Bonna Daix Wescoat, Oxford Monographs on Classical Archaeology, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.05.40/. [Open access]","Jeannette Marchand, review of <i>Stymphalos: The Acropolis Sanctuary, Volume 1</i>, ed. Gerald P. Schaus, Phoenix Supplementary Volumes 54, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.08.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.08.02. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg/1920px-Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg","title":"acropolis","desc":"The village and castle (acropolis) of Lindos, island of Rhodes, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lindos_Rhodes_3.jpg","alt":"Lindos Rhodes 3"},"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does the word for a high place stand behind the word for a state?","a":"Because 'polis' is held to have widened from the citadel to the settlement around it and then to a whole territorial state."},{"q":"Why is Emporio on Chios an odd walled place?","a":"Because its circuit encloses 2.4 hectares with nobody living inside it at all."},{"q":"Why is the deity of a height not always recoverable?","a":"Because the sanctuary at Stymphalos was given to Athena Polias on an inscription, to Artemis on its sculpture and to Artemis Eileithyia on the animal bones."}]},
{"id":"gr-174","num":"174","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Before it was a square it was a meeting, and the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belonged to the assembly of all a community's adult men, who sat under a council of elders.","answer":"agora","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>agora</b> is a gathering before it is a place: the word names the assembly of all a community’s adult men, sitting under a council of elders drawn from the noble families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Homer the council, the assembly and the law court together are the government, and across the archaic period the council gained ground as the assembly lost it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name then passed from the meeting to the ground it met on, so an agora is a political institution and an architectural type at once; some 70 are known across the mainland and the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stoas, council houses, temples, shrines, gateways, concert halls, market halls and libraries stood around one, and legal business and public proclamation went on there beside the trading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What an agora looked like therefore depended on what a city had put around its edges rather than on any one plan; what they shared was the open ground and the uses it was put to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two routes led to that ground: a new foundation could reserve it from the start, and a city that grew had to clear it of what stood there already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Megara Hyblaia in Sicily is the first kind, keeping ground clear from the 8th century with its earliest foundations already respecting the line between domestic and public, then filling it with religious and public building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens is the second and among the latest: its ground was a potters’ field and a burial ground, pottery was still being made there in the mid-7th century BCE, and it was not laid out as an agora until the end of the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The most famous agora is therefore not the typical one, but a late example of a form already old when Athens took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Greek west the type runs from archaic Megara Hyblaia to Halaesa in the 2nd century BCE, the agora at Soluntum holding a stoa, porticoes, cisterns and an odeon that doubled as a council house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"agora","questions":["Megara Hyblaia in Sicily kept ground clear for its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the 8th century, and religious and public building filled that reserved space over the next two hundred years.","Pottery was still being made on the ground of the Athenian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the mid-7th century BCE, and it was a potters' field and a cemetery long before it was anything civic."],"tags":["place","politics","archaeology","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Edmund F. Bloedow, review of <i>Early Greece</i>, by Oswyn Murray, 2nd ed., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.01/. [Open access]","Mischa Meier, review of <i>Ancient Greece: A Political, Social, and Cultural History</i>, by Sarah B. Pomeroy et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.07.08/. [Open access]","Samantha L. Martin-McAuliffe, review of <i>On the Agora: The Evolution of a Public Space in Hellenistic and Roman Greece (c. 323 BC – 267 AD)</i>, by Christopher P. Dickenson, Mnemosyne Supplements 398, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.02.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.02.06/. [Open access]","David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Oxford University School of Archaeology Monograph 57, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28. [Open access]","Patrick Thomas, review of <i>Ceramicus Redivivus: The Early Iron Age Potters' Field in the Area of the Classical Athenian Agora</i>, by John K. Papadopoulos, Hesperia Supplement 31, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.03.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.03.21/. [Open access]","Laura Pfuntner, review of <i>Die Agora von Solunt: öffentliche Gebäude und öffentliche Räume des Hellenismus im griechischen Westen</i>, by Markus Wolf, Sonderschriften des Deutschen Archäologischen Instituts Rom 16, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.11.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.11.35/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Stoa_in_Athens.jpg","title":"agora","desc":"The colonnade of the Stoa of Attalos in the Athenian agora, a long double row of columns rebuilt on its ancient foundations.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stoa_in_Athens.jpg","alt":"Stoa in Athens"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is an agora a gathering before it is a place?","a":"Because the word names the assembly of all a community's adult men, and only later passed from the meeting to the ground it met on."},{"q":"Why did no two agoras look alike?","a":"Because what one looked like depended on what a city had put around its edges rather than on any one plan; what they shared was the open ground and its uses."},{"q":"Why is the most famous agora not the typical one?","a":"Because Athens's ground was a potters' field and a burial ground where pottery was still made in the mid-7th century, and was not laid out as an agora until the end of the archaic period."}]},
{"id":"gr-175","num":"175","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Archaic Greeks called a man of Athens an Athēnaios and had no abstract noun for what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possessed, which is why defining early citizenship is so contested.","answer":"polites","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>polites</b> is a member of a polis, and the two are inseparable by definition: a town that is also a community of citizens, and a community of citizens that is also a town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clearest mark of the standing is the city-ethnic, the name a person of the place bore, which denotes membership of a polis and is only primarily political.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the archaic period there is little evidence that the Greeks had a clear concept of citizenship, or even a word for it: they had ethnics like Athēnaios and no abstract noun behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the standing rested on is disputed, whether descent from the founders, a performance rather than a grant, or a distinguished behaviour before ever a legal position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Legal regulation was stricter than had once been thought, with formal military obligations on the elite, while the thētes at the bottom sit in an ambiguous place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Against the performance case stands the plain fact that women, metics and slaves all did essential civic work and none of them was institutionalised as a citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle marks off citizenship in its fullest sense, the male prerogative, from the more fragmented forms held by women and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A range of middling positions lies between the two, women holding functions of their own distributed by criteria that probably included gender, and metics keeping their ties home while travelling freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of the citizen body was poor: the Athenian working class made up 90 per cent of it in the early 6th century BCE and about half by the classical period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> For women and minors the standing could be precarious, since even a distant male relative might threaten it, and with it their protection from being enslaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"polites","questions":["Being a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is argued to have been something performed rather than granted, and answered with the case that it was a legal status and not a civic function.","The working class made up 90 per cent of Athens' citizen body in the early 6th century, so the ordinary <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laboured for a living."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Jacek Rzepka, review of <i>The Return of the Polis: The Use and Meanings of the Word Polis in Archaic and Classical Sources</i>, by Mogens Herman Hansen, Papers from the Copenhagen Polis Centre 8, Historia Einzelschriften 198, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.02.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.02.46/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of 'Poleis'</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Historisk-Filosofiske Meddelelser 74, and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Kurt Raaflaub, Historia Einzelschriften 108, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","J. J. Mulhern, review of <i>Defining Citizenship in Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Alain Duplouy and Roger W. Brock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.03.14/. [Open access]","Christopher J. Joyce, review of <i>Citizenship Practised, Citizenship Imagined: Multiple Ways of Experiencing Citizenship in the Greek World</i>, ed. Lucia Cecchet and Chiara Lasagni, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.02.23/. [Open access]","Anna Accettola, review of <i>Citizenship in Antiquity: Civic Communities in the Ancient Mediterranean</i>, ed. Jakub Filonik, Christine Plastow and Rachel Zelnick-Abramovitz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.05.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.05.20/. [Open access]","Samuel Ortencio Flores, review of <i>Voiceless, Invisible, and Countless in Ancient Greece: The Experience of Subordinates, 700–300 BCE</i>, by Samuel D. Gartland and David W. Tandy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.11/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is it doubted that archaic Greeks had a concept of citizenship?","a":"Because there is little evidence they had a clear one or even a word for it: they had ethnics like Athēnaios and no abstract noun behind them."},{"q":"Why does the performance account of citizenship run into trouble?","a":"Because women, metics and slaves all did essential civic work and none of them was institutionalised as a citizen."},{"q":"Why could the standing be precarious for women and minors?","a":"Because even a distant male relative might threaten it, and with it their protection from being enslaved."}]},
{"id":"gr-176","num":"176","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Kyrene had three, Korinth eight and the Aeolic cities none at all, so the number of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a Greek city divided its citizens into was nothing like standard.","answer":"phyle","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>phyle</b> is the largest of the divisions a Greek city cut its citizens into, and it did the work of taking newcomers in, building solidarity and keeping tyranny off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The number varied from city to city: three at Kyrene, four at Sikyon, eight at Korinth and three at Argos until a fourth was added about 460 BCE, while the Aeolic cities had none at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Chios the council sat fifty men to the phyle, at Miletus six of them coordinated the picking of magistrates, and Spartan phylai fought together in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleisthenes of Sikyon gave his city's phylai the names Archelaoi, Hyatai, Oneatai and Choireatai, marking Sikyon off from its rival Argos and stiffening its army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kyrene reformed its own after a defeat that followed 565 BCE, the work of Demonax, and at Athens Cleisthenes built ten phylai on top of trittyes and demes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Civic subdivisions are standard enough to be one of the headings under which all 1,035 poleis of the archaic and classical periods are described, across 45 regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where a phyle came from is disputed: one account derives them from bands of settlers who later attached to places, named by who arrived first, against an older view that they were artificial creations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below the phyle a citizen belonged to a phratry, organised by kinship, and to the gene, oikos, orgeones and thiasos below that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a unit of the army as well, the tribal taxis being raised from one, though whether that or the men of a single deme formed the hoplite's primary group is argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Military service of this kind bought citizens a share in the political decisions of the polis, in Sparta and Boiotia as much as at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"phyle","questions":["One account has each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beginning as a band of settlers who later attached to a place, against the older view that they were artificial creations.","Spartan units of this kind fought together, and the tribal taxis, the unit raised from one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is argued to have been the Athenian hoplite's primary group."],"tags":["concept","politics","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["James Kierstead, review of <i>Die griechischen Phylen: Funktion – Entstehung – Leistungen</i>, by Oliver Grote, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.09.47, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.09.47/. [Open access]","Frederick Naerebout, review of <i>An Inventory of Archaic and Classical Poleis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen and Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.02.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.02.29/. [Open access]","Susan Lape, review of <i>The Associations of Classical Athens: The Response to Democracy</i>, by Nicholas F. Jones, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.04.18. [Open access]","Christopher Matthew, review of <i>The Psychology of the Athenian Hoplite: The Culture of Combat in Classical Athens</i>, by Jason Crowley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.25/. [Open access]","Adam Schwartz, review of <i>The Spear, the Scroll, and the Pebble: How the Greek City-State Developed as a Male Warrior-Citizen Collective</i>, by Richard A. Billows, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.15/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why did a city cut its citizens into such divisions?","a":"Because they did the work of taking newcomers in, building solidarity and keeping tyranny off."},{"q":"Why did Cleisthenes of Sikyon rename his city's phylai?","a":"Because the new names marked Sikyon off from its rival Argos and stiffened its army."},{"q":"Why is the origin of the phyle disputed?","a":"Because one account derives them from bands of settlers later attached to places and named by who arrived first, against an older view that they were artificial creations."}]},
{"id":"gr-177","num":"177","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Draco's homicide law is argued to imply that every Athenian belonged to a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a case that has never been universally accepted.","answer":"phratry","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>phratry</b> is one of the associations a Greek citizen belonged to below the phyle, organised by kinship and on principles that were not democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cult practice was uniform enough to build a common Athenian identity, and generic phratry monuments stood in the agora for city dwellers cut off from their ancestral centres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below it came the gene, the oikos, the orgeones and the thiasos, sorted by kinship and by wealth rather than by any democratic rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twelve are counted at Athens, three to each of the revised Ionic phylai, so that a group of three made up the unit called a trittys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Solonian legislation opened citizenship to men outside the elite through the phratries, and through them the ideas of Athenian descent and citizenship were in the end mapped onto territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their vitality fell away through periods of warfare and political upheaval, some deme groups absorbing phratry cults outright, and the fit between the two was never a tidy one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decree of the Demotionidai is the main document for the Attic phratry, and its reading is contested down to whether the Dekeleis named in it are a subgroup of the Demotionidai or something else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost all of what is known is Attic, and whether every Athenian belonged to one in the archaic period is unsettled, one argument for it resting on what Draco's homicide law implies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Attic polis has been explained as a combination of phratry lodges scattered across Attica, an account that asks the Athenians to have misunderstood their own institutional language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What counted in daily life was the household and the ties inside it rather than any extended bloodline, whatever the wider groups a man was enrolled in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"phratry","questions":["Organised by kinship rather than by democratic principle, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built a common Athenian identity out of cult practice that was the same from one to the next.","The decree of the Demotionidai is the centrepiece of the standard collection of inscriptions for the Attic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its reading is contested."],"tags":["concept","politics","religion","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Susan Lape, review of <i>The Associations of Classical Athens: The Response to Democracy</i>, by Nicholas F. Jones, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.04.18. [Open access]","Antony Keen, review of <i>The Phratries of Attica</i>, by S. D. Lambert, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.04.13/. [Open access]","Bob Develin, review of <i>Festivals and Legends: The Formation of Greek Cities in the Light of Public Ritual</i>, by Noel Robertson, Phoenix Supplementary Volume 31, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.03.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.03.11/. [Open access]","Christopher Smith, review of <i>Kinship in Ancient Athens: An Anthropological Analysis</i>, by Sarah C. Humphreys, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.12.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.12.24/. [Open access]","Tim Parkin, review of <i>The Family in Greek History</i>, by Cynthia B. Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.06.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.06.07/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why did generic phratry monuments stand in the agora?","a":"Because city dwellers were cut off from their ancestral centres, and the cult practice was uniform enough to build a common Athenian identity."},{"q":"Why did these associations lose their vitality?","a":"Because periods of warfare and political upheaval wore them down, some deme groups absorbing phratry cults outright."},{"q":"Why is what is known about them so narrow?","a":"Because almost all of it is Attic, and the main document, the decree of the Demotionidai, is contested down to who the Dekeleis in it are."}]},
{"id":"gr-178","num":"178","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not found in the sources with the sense of 'clan' until the 4th century BCE, though modern accounts apply it to much earlier families.","answer":"genos","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>genos</b> is one of the groups an Athenian phratry divided into, sorted with the oikos, the orgeones and the thiasos by kinship and by wealth rather than by any democratic rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What such a group chiefly did was transmit a priestly office, passing that right down among its members, and there is little evidence it ever did anything else at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Athenian gene look geographical in origin rather than fundamentally aristocratic, taking their character from where their members lived rather than from descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Belonging to one placed a man inside a phratry and so inside the phyle above it, which is how a Greek city reached down as far as the household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What counted in practice was that household and the ties inside it, between husband and wife, parent and child, master and slave, rather than any extended bloodline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The word is not found in the sources with the sense of 'clan' until the 4th century BCE, though modern accounts routinely apply it to families centuries older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The standing of a genos moved rather than holding still, and at Athens one that had been privileged at first was later limited in what it could claim and then repudiated altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two studies of 1976 concluded independently that the evidence will not carry more than the priestly function, and the older picture has not recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether institutions sharing one name across different periods hold any fundamental identity is doubted, and the same doubt has been pressed on the Roman gens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The groups themselves were real enough, since a phratry did subdivide and did so by kinship and standing; it is the ancient clan behind them that has gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"genos","questions":["Two studies of 1976 concluded independently that there is little evidence the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ever anything but a group that passed on priestly office.","The Athenian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks geographical in origin rather than fundamentally aristocratic, taking its character from where its members lived rather than from descent."],"tags":["concept","politics","religion","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Susan Lape, review of <i>The Associations of Classical Athens: The Response to Democracy</i>, by Nicholas F. Jones, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.04.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.04.18. [Open access]","Mischa Meier, review of <i>Ancient Greece: A Political, Social, and Cultural History</i>, by Sarah B. Pomeroy et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.07.08/. [Open access]","Ayelet Haimson Lushkov, review of <i>The Roman Clan: The Gens from Ancient Ideology to Modern Anthropology</i>, by Christopher John Smith, W. B. Stanford Memorial Lectures, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.11.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.11.06/. [Open access]","Antony Keen, review of <i>The Phratries of Attica</i>, by S. D. Lambert, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.04.13/. [Open access]","Tim Parkin, review of <i>The Family in Greek History</i>, by Cynthia B. Patterson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.06.07, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.06.07/. [Open access]","Christopher Smith, review of <i>Kinship in Ancient Athens: An Anthropological Analysis</i>, by Sarah C. Humphreys, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.12.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.12.24/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why did the older picture of the ancient clan collapse?","a":"Because two studies of 1976 concluded independently that the evidence will not carry more than the priestly function, and it has not recovered."},{"q":"Why is the word's own history a warning?","a":"Because it is not found with the sense of 'clan' until the 4th century BCE, though modern accounts routinely apply it to families centuries older."},{"q":"Why do these groups look geographical rather than aristocratic?","a":"Because the Athenian ones take their character from where their members lived rather than from descent."}]},
{"id":"gr-179","num":"179","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Medieval and modern images of nobility have distorted what is written about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the term itself is now held to be only rarely useful.","answer":"archaic aristocracy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>archaic aristocracy</b> is the elite of Greece in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, and standing in it was not fixed at birth but had to be earned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Access to the higher ranks was open to enterprising men, and the standing was tied not to lineage but to wealth and its display.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had to be won and won again, so that a man who stopped competing for prestige lost it whatever his birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six ways of being recognised run through the record, claimed descent, marriage, funerals, luxury goods, where a statue was dedicated and how original it was, across the 10th to 5th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Honour was at first tied to holding property, and only later, once civic offices were held unpaid, could it be kept apart from material wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The drinking party was the arena where a fluid and contested standing was settled, open on equal terms to anyone with the right training whatever his background.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its practice is traced by pottery from about 800 BCE, reclining came in later rather than being the defining mark, and the form looks Greek rather than borrowed from the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Constant pressure to pile up wealth and perform the part drove men into ruinous behaviour, which is what the mobility of the period looked like from inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Archaic tyrants were less a new thing than a newly unacceptable one, elite power-seeking having gone on all along and been condemned as law rose between about 750 and 650 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether a unified nobility existed in archaic Greece at all is doubted, a structured one being placed only in 5th-century Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"archaic aristocracy","questions":["Standing in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was something won and re-won rather than inherited, so a man could lose it whatever his birth.","The drinking party was open to anyone with the right training, which made it a way into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a fence around it."],"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Massimiliano Di Fazio, review of <i>'Aristocracy' in Antiquity: Redefining Greek and Roman Elites</i>, ed. Nick Fisher and Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.11.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.11.31/. [Open access]","Timothy Power, review of <i>Le Prestige des élites: Recherches sur les modes de reconnaissance sociale en Grèce entre les Xe et Ve siècles avant J.-C.</i>, by Alain Duplouy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.08.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.08.22/. [Open access]","Adam Rabinowitz, review of <i>The Rise of the Greek Aristocratic Banquet</i>, by Marek Wecowski, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2015.10.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2015/2015.10.42/. [Open access]","Evan Vance, review of <i>From Homer to Solon: Continuity and Change in Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Johannes C. Bernhardt and Mirko Canevaro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.03.14/. [Open access]","Christian Mann, review of <i>“Adel” und gesellschaftliche Differenzierung im archaischen und frühklassischen Griechenland</i>, by Jan Bernhard Meister, Historia Einzelschriften 263, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.03.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.03.43/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Statue_of_Nicandre_NAMA1_Athens_Greece.jpg","title":"archaic aristocracy","desc":"Female statue. Island marble. Found in Delos, in a sanctuary of Artemis. One of the earliest monumental statues in stone, it probably represents the goddess Artemis. To the left thigh of the statue is an inscription, dedication to Apollo by the Naxian Nikandre. Daedalic style. Ca.650 BCE. N°1.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Nicandre_NAMA1_Athens_Greece.jpg","alt":"Statue of Nicandre NAMA1 Athens Greece"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did a man's standing have to be won again?","a":"Because it was tied not to lineage but to wealth and its display, so one who stopped competing for prestige lost it whatever his birth."},{"q":"Why did that pressure drive men into ruin?","a":"Because the constant need to pile up wealth and perform the part is what the mobility of the period looked like from inside it."},{"q":"Why were archaic tyrants less a new thing than a newly unacceptable one?","a":"Because elite power-seeking had gone on all along and came to be condemned as law rose between about 750 and 650 BCE."}]},
{"id":"gr-180","num":"180","category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The middle-class army of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now called a myth resting on one isolated ancient generalisation, these being ill-trained amateurs rather than a settled middle rank.","answer":"hoplite","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Equipment from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Superseded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>hoplite</b> is the heavily armed infantryman of the Greek city, and the middle-class hoplite army is now called a myth resting on one isolated and ill-founded ancient generalisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These were amateurs: archaic and classical land armies were made up of ill-trained non-specialists, with no place to train but the gymnasia, which the wealthy kept to themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Battle descriptions hardened the divisions between ranks rather than levelling them, and the citizen militias behind them were haphazard about command, mobilisation, supply and discipline alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bronze bell cuirass of the Argos panoply weighs 3.360 kilograms (7.4 pounds), a figure hard to place as either impossibly heavy or unexpectedly light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not every cuirass was bronze, compound versions in fabric being lighter and presumably cheaper, and the shapes flattered: a bell cuirass narrowed the waist and broadened the chest, while a muscle cuirass exaggerated the musculature of chest and stomach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Greek armies were never hoplites alone, cavalry, slingers and archers serving beside them and never getting the credit in antiquity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Light infantry beat them in the field more than once, Chalcidaeans at Megara in 458 BCE and at Spartolus in 429, and Athenian missile troops on Sphacteria in 425.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What held a man in the line is argued over, the men of his own deme on one account and the tribal regiment raised from his phyle on another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Below the hoplites stood the thetes, whose invisibility in the record is put down to stigma and to land-based census classes falling out of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Skirmishers, slaves and mercenaries stood at the centre of Greek fighting, and their absence from the record is blamed on an elite taste for the heavily armed and the face-to-face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hoplite","questions":["Chalcidaean light infantry beat them at Spartolus in 429 BCE, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not own the battlefield the way antiquity's writers suggest.","The bronze bell cuirass of the Argos panoply worn by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> weighs 3.360 kilograms, a figure hard to place as either heavy or light."],"tags":["concept","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Kevin Daly, review of <i>Greek Warfare: Myths and Realities</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.66, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.66/. [Open access]","Stewart Flory, review of <i>Reinstating the Hoplite: Arms, Armour and Phalanx Fighting in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Adam Schwartz, Historia Einzelschriften 207, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.23/. [Open access]","Nadya Williams, review of <i>Combined Arms Warfare in Ancient Greece: From Homer to Alexander the Great and His Successors</i>, by Graham Wrightson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.04.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.04.55/. [Open access]","Christopher Matthew, review of <i>The Psychology of the Athenian Hoplite: The Culture of Combat in Classical Athens</i>, by Jason Crowley, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.25/. [Open access]","Samuel Ortencio Flores, review of <i>Voiceless, Invisible, and Countless in Ancient Greece: The Experience of Subordinates, 700–300 BCE</i>, by Samuel D. Gartland and David W. Tandy, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.11/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Amphora_phalanx_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1429.jpg/1920px-Amphora_phalanx_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1429.jpg","title":"hoplite","desc":"Phalanx. Side A of an Attic black-figure Tyrrhenic amphora, ca. 560 BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphora_phalanx_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1429.jpg","alt":"Amphora phalanx Staatliche Antikensammlungen 1429"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the middle-class hoplite army called a myth?","a":"Because it rests on one isolated and ill-founded ancient generalisation, while the armies themselves were ill-trained non-specialists with no place to train but the gymnasia."},{"q":"Why did battle harden the divisions between ranks?","a":"Because the descriptions of it did, and the citizen militias were haphazard about command, mobilisation, supply and discipline alike."},{"q":"Why are light troops, slaves and mercenaries missing from the record?","a":"Because an elite taste for the heavily armed and the face-to-face kept them out, though light infantry beat hoplites in the field more than once."}]},
{"id":"gr-181","num":"","category":"","question":"Each man's shield covered the flank of the neighbour on his left, which is why a whole battle line of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drifted rightward as it advanced.","questions":["A general could not pass an order down a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once it was moving, so where the contingents stood before the fighting began was the whole of Greek tactical thought.","The bloodiest part of a Greek battle came after a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke, when men threw away shield and armour to run and cavalry rode them down."],"answer":"phalanx","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze panoply</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 725 – c. 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>phalanx</b> is the massed close-order formation in which Greek heavy infantry fought, drawn up in files and ranks, and the word itself carries an older sense of 'beam' or 'roller'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>aspis</i> shaped it: about 90 centimetres (three feet) across and some 6.8 kilograms (15 pounds), it locked to the elbow, leaving its bearer's right side bare while reaching well past his left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That lopsidedness pulled whole classical battle lines rightward, each man edging in behind the shield of the neighbour on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the contingents stood before the fighting began was the substance of Greek tactical thought, since a general could not pass an order down a moving line and few of his men had ever drilled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The right end is usually called the post of honour, yet in more than half the documented battles another part held it, and the recorded depths ignore the neat multiples of eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nor was battle the ritual meeting on an open plain it is often taken for: two thirds of the classical engagements recorded turned on deception, surprise or a bid for better ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the ranks then physically shoved, the <i>othismos</i> or mass push that Greek historians name without explaining, has been argued over for close to a century, as has the date when a citizen formation replaced looser fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reconstruction and computer modelling of the archaic bronze panoply of bell cuirass, Corinthian helmet and <i>aspis</i> suggest it was built to withstand crushing pressure, putting massed pushing at 700 BCE at the latest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A line broke by degrees rather than at a stroke, and the rout that followed was the bloodiest part of the day, fleeing men shedding shield and armour as cavalry rode them down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cavalry fought in the line as well as after it and stayed decisive into the Hellenistic age; of the fields themselves only a handful still carry a mound or trophy where a phalanx stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"phalanx","tags":["concept","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]","Kyle Fingerson, review of <i>Classical Greek Tactics: A Cultural History</i>, by Roel Konijnendijk, Mnemosyne Supplements 409, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.11.48, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.11.48/. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Clemens Koehn, review of <i>Understanding Greek Warfare</i>, by Matthew A. Sears, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.02.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.02.24/. [Open access]","Emil Nankov, review of <i>Battles and Battlefields of Ancient Greece: A Guide to Their History, Topography and Archaeology</i>, by C. Jacob Butera and Matthew A. Sears, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.02.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.02.25/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Stele_of_the_vultures_%28phalanx%29.jpg/1920px-Stele_of_the_vultures_%28phalanx%29.jpg","title":"phalanx","desc":"Stele of the vultures (phalanx). Ernest de Sarzec - Choquin de Sarzec, Ernest (1832-1901).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_vultures_(phalanx).jpg","alt":"Stele of the vultures (phalanx)"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why did whole battle lines drift rightward?","a":"Because the shield locked to the elbow left its bearer's right side bare while reaching past his left, so each man edged in behind the shield of the neighbour on that side."},{"q":"Why was the pre-battle deployment the substance of Greek tactical thought?","a":"Because a general could not pass an order down a moving line and few of his men had ever drilled, so where the contingents stood had to be settled beforehand."},{"q":"Why is the picture of a ritual meeting on an open plain wrong?","a":"Because two thirds of the classical engagements recorded turned on deception, surprise or a bid for better ground."}]},
{"id":"gr-182","num":"","category":"","question":"Locked to the forearm by a bronze band at its centre, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not be shifted across to cover its bearer's right side, nor slung on his back in flight.","questions":["Held straight on, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stuck out well past its bearer's left while leaving his right side bare, wasting protection and weight in the same movement.","The deep bowl of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> let its rim sit on the cuirass rather than the bare shoulder, so pressure went into the armour and not the collarbone."],"answer":"aspis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Agora shield</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE fill</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>aspis</b> is the round shield of the Greek heavy infantryman, about 90 centimetres (three feet) across and around 6.8 kilograms (15 pounds) for its robust construction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It hung on two grips rather than one: a band at the centre, the porpax, through which the left forearm passed to the elbow, and a handle at the rim, the antilabe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That arrangement locked the shield to the arm and cost it its reach, so it could not be shifted across to cover the right side as a single-handled shield could, nor slung on the back in flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Held straight on it left its bearer's right side bare and stuck out well past his left, wasting protection and weight at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bowl was deep enough for the rim to sit on the cuirass where front and back plates overlap rather than on the bare shoulder, so pressure passed into the armour instead of the collarbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That bowl also kept the press of a crowd off the chest, and the rim would strike its bearer's own nose and mouth unless he tucked it hard against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How heavy it really was is argued over, one side defending the old estimates of a considerable burden and the other asking whether they had ever been checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it did is not in question: it was a defensive and an offensive weapon at once, and the plain tactics of the massed line followed from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also outlasted the rest of the bronze panoply, staying in common use long after the bell cuirass and the Corinthian helmet had gone out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One survives largely complete from a cistern filling in the Athenian Agora, bent and chipped, 0.97 metres across at its widest, its narrow rim worked in a fine guilloche and its plain centre carrying an inscription punched in letters eight centimetres high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"aspis","tags":["object","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]","Debra Hamel, review of <i>Warfare in Ancient Greece: A Sourcebook</i>, by Michael M. Sage, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.06.01/. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Stewart Flory, review of <i>Reinstating the Hoplite: Arms, Armour and Phalanx Fighting in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Adam Schwartz, Historia Einzelschriften 207, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.23/. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Agora Object B 262,\" <i>Athenian Agora Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.agathe.gr/id/agora/object/b%20262. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Hoplitodromos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1471.jpg","title":"aspis","desc":"Hoplitodromos (armoured race); on the right some tripods as winning prizes. Side A of an Attic black-figure neck-amphora, ca. 550 BCE. From Vulci.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoplitodromos_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_1471.jpg","alt":"Hoplitodromos Staatliche Antikensammlungen 1471"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the two-grip arrangement cost the shield its reach?","a":"Because the forearm passed through a band at the centre to a handle at the rim, locking it to the arm, so it could not be shifted across to cover the right side nor slung on the back in flight."},{"q":"Why does the bowl matter as much as the face?","a":"Because it let the rim sit on the cuirass where front and back plates overlap rather than on the bare shoulder, passing pressure into the armour and keeping the press of a crowd off the chest."},{"q":"Why is its weight argued over?","a":"Because one side defends the old estimates of a considerable burden and the other asks whether they had ever been checked."}]},
{"id":"gr-183","num":"","category":"","question":"The thickest bronze on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> guards the nose and cheeks, which a weapon rarely reached, and the thinnest sits on the crown, where a blow was likeliest to land.","questions":["Forward-angled cheekpieces left the throat open to a careless man, but let the wearer of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pull his neck in and brace his head as the lines met.","Covering the whole head and most of the face, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave more protection than anything else a Greek wore and took his sight, hearing and movement in exchange."],"answer":"Corinthian helmet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – c. 475 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Corinthian helmet</b> is the bronze helmet of the Greek heavy infantryman, covering the whole head and most of the face, and it was made far beyond the city it is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Surviving examples stand about 23 centimetres (nine inches) high, and much of the hoplite equipment that survives at all was dedicated at sanctuaries such as Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One was probably cast first and then hammered to its final shape, and the crest it often carried was of horsehair or the like, which survives on none of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gave more protection than anything else a Greek wore, and took vision, hearing and movement in exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Replica testing bears the first of those out: a helmet of this type genuinely restricts what its wearer can see.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its bronze is deliberately uneven: one taken from the plain of Marathon weighs 1,193 grams and runs to 10 millimetres at the nasal and 2 or 3 across the face, while its crown is under a millimetre thick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is the reverse of what the damage suggests: the crown is where a blow was likeliest to land and is barely thick enough for one, while the nose and face were unlikely to be struck at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The explanation offered is the shield, since as two lines collided the rim of a man's own <i>aspis</i> came at his nose and mouth, so the thickest metal on his head was armour against his own equipment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cheekpieces angle forward, which leaves the front of the throat open to a careless man but lets him pull his neck in and brace his head at the moment of impact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smiths learned to work-harden the bronze in the late 6th century and made the helmets thinner as they hardened, and within a generation the type had all but left the vases, worn by more than four hoplites in five painted between 525 and 500 BCE and by fewer than one in five painted after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Corinthian helmet","tags":["object","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Cleveland Museum of Art, \"Corinthian Helmet,\" accession 2024.63, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.clevelandart.org/art/2024.63. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Stewart Flory, review of <i>Reinstating the Hoplite: Arms, Armour and Phalanx Fighting in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Adam Schwartz, Historia Einzelschriften 207, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.23/. [Open access]","Robert Mason, \"Weapon Wednesday: The Nugent Marathon Corinthian Helmet,\" Royal Ontario Museum, 19 February 2014, https://www.rom.on.ca/blogs/weapon-wednesday-nugent-marathon-corinthian-helmet. [Open access]","H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Corinthian_helmet_BM_2838.jpg","title":"Corinthian helmet","desc":"Corinthian helmet BM 2838. Marie-Lan Nguyen. 2006.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corinthian_helmet_BM_2838.jpg","alt":"Corinthian helmet BM 2838"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the metal thickest where a blow was least likely?","a":"Because as two lines collided the rim of a man's own shield came at his nose and mouth, so the thickest metal on his head was armour against his own equipment."},{"q":"Why do the cheekpieces angle forward?","a":"Because although that leaves the front of the throat open to a careless man, it lets him pull his neck in and brace his head at the moment of impact."},{"q":"Why did the type all but vanish from the vases within a generation?","a":"Because smiths learned to work-harden the bronze in the late 6th century and made helmets thinner as they hardened, and the type fell from four hoplites in five to fewer than one in five."}]},
{"id":"gr-184","num":"","category":"","question":"Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever happened turns on the double-grip shield, which is supposed to have brought a sudden revolution in tactics early in the archaic period and then in politics.","questions":["The modern case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rests on a remark of Aristotle's, that more people gained a share in government once the phalanx displaced the aristocrats and their horses.","Against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it is argued that the equipment was lighter than supposed and that the middling farmer at the heart of the case did not exist in numbers."],"answer":"hoplite reform","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Argued over</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since the 19th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Orthodox from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>hoplite reform</b> is the proposed change by which the double-grip shield brought a sudden revolution in Greek tactics early in the archaic period, and with it a great shift in society and politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea descends from Aristotle, who wrote that aristocrats and their horses had ruled the battlefield and the state alike until the phalanx and its greater numbers gave more people a share in government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In its fullest modern form the farmers who filled that phalanx are credited with remaking the polis, laying the ground for political equality and for citizenship itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the standard account in English after an influential article of 1947, and it has been held in one form or another for more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind it stood a 19th-century model of Greek battle as a ritual: armies meeting by arrangement on an open plain, always eight ranks deep, the general on the right, and no part for cavalry or light troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From the 1960s the study of the arms dedicated at sanctuaries such as Olympia began turning that sudden revolution into a gradual one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later work held that the equipment was lighter than supposed, so men could fight outside the densest formations, and that the fighting in the <i>Iliad</i> was not clearly different from the archaic phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The middling farmer at the heart of the case came under attack as well, the middle-class hoplite army being called a myth resting on a single ill-founded ancient generalisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On one full alternative the early polis gave equal rights to about 15% of its people and the broader citizen phalanx appeared only in the very late 6th century, while field survey tells against the spread of farming onto poor land the older case needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing has settled it: reconstruction of the bronze panoply has since been used to argue that a mass phalanx must be as old as the equipment, and the question is still described as a vexed one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hoplite reform","tags":["theory","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Kyle Fingerson, review of <i>Classical Greek Tactics: A Cultural History</i>, by Roel Konijnendijk, Mnemosyne Supplements 409, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.11.48, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.11.48/. [Open access]","Kevin Daly, review of <i>Greek Warfare: Myths and Realities</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.66, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.66/. [Open access]","H. W. Lanphier, \"The Bronze Hoplite Panoply and the <i>Othismos</i> in the Early Phalanx,\" <i>Greece &amp; Rome</i> 72, no. 2 (2025): 292–310, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0017383525100375. [Open access]","Clemens Koehn, review of <i>Understanding Greek Warfare</i>, by Matthew A. Sears, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.02.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.02.24/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Vix_crater_hoplite_circa_500_BCE.jpg","title":"hoplite reform","desc":"Vix crater hoplite circa 500 BCE. Oblomov2.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vix_crater_hoplite_circa_500_BCE.jpg","alt":"Vix crater hoplite circa 500 BCE"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did the sudden revolution become a gradual one?","a":"Because from the 1960s the study of the arms dedicated at sanctuaries such as Olympia turned it into a slow change rather than a break."},{"q":"Why did the middling farmer at the heart of the case come under attack?","a":"Because the middle-class hoplite army was called a myth resting on a single ill-founded ancient generalisation, and field survey tells against the spread of farming onto poor land the older case needs."},{"q":"Why is the question still unsettled?","a":"Because reconstruction of the bronze panoply has since been used to argue that a mass phalanx must be as old as the equipment."}]},
{"id":"gr-185","num":"","category":"","question":"The top frieze carries the earliest surviving picture of massed hoplites, which puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the centre of the argument over how that formation arose.","questions":["Under the handle of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest known picture of the Judgement of Paris, damaged, with inscriptions naming two of the goddesses and Alexander.","Every warrior on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries two spears, gripped along with the shield handle, and the pair being taken up behind them are painted with throwing loops."],"answer":"Chigi vase","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 640 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Chigi vase</b> is a Protocorinthian olpe of about 640 BCE, 26.2 centimetres high, painted with three friezes of human figures and among the most ambitious pots ever made at Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is probably the earliest surviving example of the olpe, a shape that became one of the commonest Corinth produced, and it is a jug for pouring wine at a feast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It ended up in Etruria, in the main chamber of a princely tomb under a mound 60 metres across outside Veii, among some 230 objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its friezes run from a hare hunt at the foot to a lion hunt and a procession of a chariot and four horsemen in the middle band, and hoplites closing at the top; read upward they have been taken for the career of a Corinthian nobleman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the handle is the Judgement of Paris, the oldest known picture of it, damaged but carrying inscriptions that name two of the goddesses and Alexander, with Hermes gone except for the tip of his herald's staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The top frieze holds the earliest surviving picture of massed hoplites, and a piper with them: piping is named twice in Homer and never in war, and the cheek-strap that holds the pipes cannot be traced before the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That frieze is treated as the canonical image of the phalanx in archaic art, and it is central material evidence in the case against the older account of how the formation arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What unsettles it is that the men carry two spears each, gripped along with the shield handle, and that the pair being taken up in the arming scene behind them are painted with throwing loops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A reading published in 1947 explained the second shafts away as an unpainted rank standing behind, which fails because the hands close on shaft and handle together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of the painted evidence is ambiguous in this way, which is why flatly opposed accounts of archaic warfare have both survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chigi vase","tags":["object","art","warfare","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Tom Rasmussen, \"Paris on the Chigi Vase,\" <i>Cedrus</i> 1 (2013): 55–64, https://doi.org/10.13113/cedrus/20131675. [Open access]","Thierry Petit, \"Les sphinx sur le Vase François et l'Olpè Chigi : l'héroïsation des élites,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome — Antiquité</i> 131, no. 2 (2019), https://doi.org/10.4000/mefra.9092. [Open access]","Wawrzyniec Miścicki, \"Notes on Representations of Hoplites with Double Spears in Archaic Greek Art and the Usage of Javelins and Spears by the Archaic Phalanx,\" <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilisation</i> 17 (2013): 113–20, https://doi.org/10.12797/SAAC.17.2013.17.09. [Open access]","Matthew A. Sears, review of <i>Men of Bronze: Hoplite Warfare in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Donald Kagan and Gregory F. Viggiano, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.70, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.70/. [Open access]","Kevin Daly, review of <i>Greek Warfare: Myths and Realities</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.66, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.66/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Detail_from_the_Chigi-vase.jpg/1920px-Detail_from_the_Chigi-vase.jpg","title":"Chigi vase","desc":"Hoplites go to battle at the sounds of music.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_from_the_Chigi-vase.jpg","alt":"Detail from the Chigi-vase"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the top frieze central to the argument about the phalanx?","a":"Because it holds the earliest surviving picture of massed hoplites and is treated as the canonical image of the formation in archaic art."},{"q":"Why does the same frieze unsettle that reading?","a":"Because the men carry two spears each, gripped along with the shield handle, and the pair being taken up in the arming scene behind them are painted with throwing loops."},{"q":"Why have flatly opposed accounts of archaic warfare both survived?","a":"Because most of the painted evidence is ambiguous in this way, and the 1947 explanation of the second shafts fails since the hands close on shaft and handle together."}]},
{"id":"gr-186","num":"","category":"","question":"Both held unlimited power for a time, which is why a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is hard to tell from a lawgiver, and each usually rose in answer to a crisis in his own city.","questions":["Justifying himself as the corrector of injustice, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could point to oracles like those gathered around Cypselus, which present his rule as the answer to unjust leaders.","On one reading a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was no innovator at all but the culmination of aristocratic rule, and the men he oppressed were other aristocrats rather than the people at large."],"answer":"tyrannos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Age of tyrants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>tyrannos</b> is a ruler who took sole power in a Greek city outside its ordinary order, and the tyrants of the archaic age are a varied group that resists easy definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyranny grew out of the economic and political crisis many communities went through, and a tyrant commonly rose in answer to a local one, often holding an office of the city while he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line between him and a lawgiver is thin enough to look artificial, since both held unlimited power for a time, and his rule was usually limited too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was not necessarily an innovator: on one reading archaic tyranny is the culmination of aristocratic rule rather than a break with it, and the men it oppressed were other aristocrats rather than the people at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tyrants tended to be benefactors of their cities, strengthening their position by patronage and by networks among the elite, and they commissioned works of art that advertised them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> They justified themselves as correctors of injustice, and the oracles gathered around Cypselus of Corinth present his rule as the answer to unjust leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What legitimised a ruler was excellence shown in public, in distinguished or divine descent, in war, in the games, or in founding a city; wealth counted because it displayed that rather than by itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As support fell away they dressed themselves as founders or liberators to prolong their power, while the justice they dispensed came to look arbitrary and in need of correction of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The neat sequence in which hereditary kings gave way to tyrants grown rich is doubted, and so is the sharp line ancient writers drew between king and tyrant: tyrants founded dynasties, and three generations was enough to count as well established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether a man was called a tyrant at all could turn on the politics of whoever wrote about him, and rule of this kind faded as it grew more repressive and fell out of step with the cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"tyrannos","tags":["title","concept","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]","Lynette Mitchell, review of <i>A Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Kurt A. Raaflaub and Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.45/. [Open access]","Philip Davies, review of <i>The Heroic Rulers of Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Lynette Mitchell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.30/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Marcaline J. Boyd, review of <i>Tyranny: New Contexts</i>, ed. Sian Lewis, Dialogues d'histoire ancienne supplement 21, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.10.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.10.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Tiranicidas_04.JPG/1920px-Tiranicidas_04.JPG","title":"tyrannos","desc":"Naples. National Archaeological Museum. Harmodius and Aristogeiton.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiranicidas_04.JPG","alt":"Tiranicidas 04"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the line between a tyrant and a lawgiver thin?","a":"Because both held unlimited power for a time, and a tyrant's rule was usually limited too, often exercised while he held an office of the city."},{"q":"Why has archaic tyranny been read as the culmination of aristocratic rule?","a":"Because a tyrant need not have been an innovator, and the men his rule oppressed were other aristocrats rather than the people at large."},{"q":"Why is the sharp ancient line between king and tyrant doubted?","a":"Because tyrants founded dynasties and three generations was enough to count as well established, and whether a man was called a tyrant could turn on the politics of whoever wrote about him."}]},
{"id":"gr-187","num":"","category":"","question":"A prophecy of usurpation sent the Bacchiadae to kill the infant <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was hidden from them in a ceramic beehive, or so Herodotus tells it.","questions":["Having ended the rule of the Bacchiadae, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have needed neither bodyguard nor mercenaries, and to have lived as a highly esteemed aristocrat.","The oracles gathered around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> present his rule as the correction of an injustice done by the men before him, which is the standard justification a tyrant offered."],"answer":"Cypselus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 580 BCE, or as late as 540</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cypselus</b> ended the rule of the Bacchiadae at Corinth and made himself sole ruler of the city, founding a dynasty that is one of the standard examples of archaic tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His father Eetion came from outside the Bacchiad clan and his mother was Labda, and what is told of the family is fictive beyond reasonable doubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest telling is Herodotus's: a prophecy of usurpation sends the Bacchiadae to kill the child, who is hidden from them in a ceramic beehive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is his mother whom Herodotus makes lame, not him, though the story is often retold the other way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oracles gathered around him too, presenting his rule as the correction of an injustice done by the men before him, which is the standard justification a tyrant offered for taking power at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Once the Bacchiadae were gone he seems to have been accepted by most Corinthians, the aristocracy among them, since they were the men the Bacchiadae had shut out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is said to have needed neither bodyguard nor mercenaries, to have had no rival left to fear, and to have lived as a highly esteemed aristocrat rather than as anything new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that stands Herodotus, who has him drive many Corinthians into exile and kill many more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His son Periander met a different generation of nobles who wanted power for themselves, and held his by keeping a bodyguard and dealing ruthlessly with his enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the dynasty ended is not settled: the traditional date falls in the 580s BCE, and a case has been made for bringing it down as late as 550 or 540, the ancient figures behind both coming from Herodotus, Thucydides and the later chronographers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cypselus","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Christopher J. Joyce, review of <i>The Tyrants of Corinth: Legends of Cypselus and Periander</i>, by Daniel Ogden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.50/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Alden Mosshammer, review of <i>Il POxy. 664 di Eraclide Pontico e la cronologia dei Cipselidi</i>, by Walter Lapini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.11/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the story of his childhood not evidence?","a":"Because what is told of the family is fictive beyond reasonable doubt, down to the beehive he was hidden in — and it is his mother whom Herodotus makes lame, not him."},{"q":"Why might the aristocracy have accepted him?","a":"Because they were the men the Bacchiadae had shut out, and he is said to have needed neither bodyguard nor mercenaries."},{"q":"Why is that acceptance not the whole picture?","a":"Because Herodotus has him drive many Corinthians into exile and kill many more."}]},
{"id":"gr-188","num":"","category":"","question":"Cut down whatever stands tallest: in Aristotle's telling that is the advice <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Thrasybulus, a lesson in reducing one's political opponents.","questions":["Facing nobles who wanted power for themselves, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept his by a bodyguard and by ruthless measures, turning his father's tyranny towards coercion.","The ghost of his dead wife Melissa would not tell <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> where the treasure lay until her burial rites were made good, or so the story runs."],"answer":"Periander","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cypselid rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ends c. 580 BCE, or as late as 540</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Periander</b> is the second of the Cypselid tyrants of Corinth, the son of Cypselus, and in the tradition that grew up around him one of the Seven Sages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He met a different generation of nobles from his father's, men who wanted power for themselves and disputed his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He held on to it by keeping a bodyguard and dealing ruthlessly with his enemies, turning the tyranny towards coercion while remaining bound to the aristocratic world he had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The parable of the ears of corn belongs to him: in Aristotle's telling it is Periander who advises Thrasybulus, and the advice to cut down whatever stands tallest is a lesson in reducing one's political opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyrants of this kind were benefactors of their cities as well, strengthening their position by patronage and by networks among the elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What is told of his household is grim and mostly invention: he is said to have consulted the ghost of his dead wife Melissa, and for selfish reasons, to learn where treasure was hidden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She would not answer him until her burial rites were made good, which he saw to by defrauding the women of Corinth of their clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His son Lycophron stands in that tradition as the counter to Cypselus: where the grandfather moves from the margin to the centre, the grandson is condemned to wander without ever reaching home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An oracle that the Cypselid line would fail in a later generation ties the two stories together, and they are one strand of the long Corinthian narrative through which tyranny is presented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All of it is enveloped in fiction, and even the dynasty's dates are unsettled, the ancient reckonings resting on Herodotus, Thucydides and the later chronographers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Periander","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Christopher J. Joyce, review of <i>The Tyrants of Corinth: Legends of Cypselus and Periander</i>, by Daniel Ogden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.50/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Leone Porciani, review of <i>Reading Herodotus: A Guided Tour through the Wild Boars, Dancing Suitors, and Crazy Tyrants of The History</i>, by Debra Hamel, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.01.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.01.23/. [Open access]","Alden Mosshammer, review of <i>Il POxy. 664 di Eraclide Pontico e la cronologia dei Cipselidi</i>, by Walter Lapini, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.11/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg","title":"Periander","desc":"Bust of Periander bearing the inscription “Periander, son of Cypselus, Corinthian”. Marble, Roman copy after a Greek original from the 4th century.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg","alt":"Periander Pio-Clementino"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did he rule differently from his father?","a":"Because he met a different generation of nobles, men who wanted power for themselves, so he held his by keeping a bodyguard and dealing ruthlessly with his enemies."},{"q":"Why is the parable of the corn a lesson in politics?","a":"Because cutting down whatever stands tallest is advice on reducing one's political opponents."},{"q":"Why is the story of his household worth little as history?","a":"Because it is grim and mostly invention, all of it enveloped in fiction, down to consulting his dead wife's ghost to learn where treasure was hidden."}]},
{"id":"gr-189","num":"","category":"","question":"The seven monolithic columns still standing above <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belong to a temple built in the middle of the 6th century BCE to replace a destroyed predecessor.","questions":["For three centuries the Potters' Quarter at the western edge of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned out pots, figurines, painted plaques and votive shields from the white marl clay of the place.","The rock of Acrocorinth stands 575 metres high above <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and commanded the road across the Isthmus, the pass south towards Argos and the coastal road west to Sicyon."],"answer":"Corinth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Apollo temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Potters' Quarter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Corinth</b> lies below Acrocorinth, a rock 575 metres (1,886 feet) high that controlled the road across the Isthmus, the pass south towards Cleonae and Argos, and the coastal road west to Sicyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Water made the place as much as the rock did: at the spring of Peirene there is activity from the Neolithic onwards, the first works to manage the water date from the Geometric period, and improvement goes on from the archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city's great archaic building is the temple of Apollo, put up in the middle of the 6th century BCE to replace a 7th-century predecessor that had been destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carried six columns at each end and 15 along each side, all of them monolithic, and its great length against its width is a mark of its date; seven of those columns are standing still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That it belonged to Apollo is a deduction rather than a record, resting on the description of the city Pausanias left and on a small dedicatory plaque found in the area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At the western edge of the walled city lay the Potters' Quarter, a complex of workshops and living quarters that worked for three centuries, from the 7th to the 4th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its potters used the white marl clay of the place, and the moulds they left show they turned out figurines, painted plaques and votive shields as well as pots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wasters, pieces melted by accident in the firing, came out of the ground there, and two shrines with standing stones were found in it by later excavators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rock above the city went on being fortified long after all this: the earliest walls surviving on Acrocorinth are of the later 4th century, and what stands over them is the work of Byzantine, Ottoman, Venetian and modern hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archaic city underneath was held by the Bacchiadae until Cypselus broke their grip, after which he was accepted by most Corinthians, the aristocracy among them, whom the Bacchiadae had shut out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Corinth","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Temple of Apollo,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/temple%20of%20apollo. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Potters' Quarter,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/potters%27%20quarter. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Acrocorinth,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/acrocorinth. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Peirene,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/peirene. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-58-09.jpg/1920px-Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-58-09.jpg","title":"Corinth","desc":"Greece, Ancient Corinth, Temple of Apollo.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-58-09.jpg","alt":"Korinth BW 2017-10-10 10-58-09"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Corinth","at":[22.8802,37.9053]},"why":[{"q":"Why did water make the place as much as the rock?","a":"Because at the spring of Peirene there is activity from the Neolithic onwards, the first works to manage the water date from the Geometric period, and improvement goes on from the archaic age."},{"q":"Why is the great temple's dedication a deduction?","a":"Because it rests on Pausanias's description of the city and on a small dedicatory plaque found in the area rather than on any inscription of the building's own."},{"q":"Why do wasters and moulds matter in the Potters' Quarter?","a":"Because the moulds show the potters turned out figurines, painted plaques and votive shields as well as pots, and wasters are pieces melted by accident in the firing."}]},
{"id":"gr-190","num":"","category":"","question":"Ships were moored and emptied of their cargo before being moved on a wheeled vehicle along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which spared them the voyage round the Peloponnese.","questions":["About 8 kilometres long and 3.5 to 6 metres wide, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is associated with Periander and was in use from at least the 5th century BCE to the 1st.","One argument holds that no constant flow of ships and cargoes crossed on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that the commercial life of the isthmus lay in its emporion instead."],"answer":"Diolkos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Diolkos</b> is the paved trackway that carried boats overland across the Isthmus of Corinth, sparing them the long and dangerous voyage round the Peloponnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That crossing is the route the rock of Acrocorinth commanded, together with the pass south towards Argos and the coastal road west to Sicyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The road ran about 8 kilometres (5 miles) and was 3.5 to 6 metres (11 to 20 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built in the 6th century BCE and is associated with Periander, tyrant of Corinth from 625 to 585.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A ship was moored and emptied of its cargo before being moved with the help of a wheeled vehicle, and the road was particularly useful for warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was in use from at least the 5th century to the 1st, and had fallen out of use after Corinth was destroyed in 146 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Part of the paved road was dug out in 1956 and 1960, and the beachrocks at its western end record a subsidence of some 35 centimetres (14 inches) after that destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the middle of the 19th century the road was taken as the key to Corinth's fortunes and to the commercial usefulness of its isthmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A re-examination of the archaeological, logistical and textual evidence has argued instead that it carried no constant flow of ships and cargoes, and that it served the communication, transport, travel and strategic ends of Corinth and her allies rather than the goods of other states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that account the commercial life of the isthmus lay in its emporion for exchange rather than in the road, hauling ships across was rare and attested only in military contexts, and the trackway served the supply of Isthmia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Diolkos","tags":["building","object","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Giannis Saitis, Anna Karkani, Niki Evelpidou, and Hampik Maroukian, \"Palaeogeographical Reconstruction of Ancient Diolkos Slipway by Using Beachrocks as Proxies, West Corinth Isthmus, Greece,\" <i>Quaternary</i> 5, no. 1 (2022): art. 7, https://doi.org/10.3390/quat5010007. [Open access]","David K. Pettegrew, \"The Diolkos of Corinth,\" <i>American Journal of Archaeology</i> 115, no. 4 (2011): 549–74, https://doi.org/10.3764/aja.115.4.0549. [Paywalled]","Michael Kleu, review of <i>The Isthmus of Corinth: Crossroads of the Mediterranean World</i>, by David K. Pettegrew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.07.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.07.36/. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Diolkos,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/diolkos. [Open access]","American School of Classical Studies at Athens, \"Corinth Monument: Acrocorinth,\" <i>Corinth Excavations</i>, accessed 7 August 2026, https://ascsa.net/id/corinth/monument/acrocorinth. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Istmo_de_Corinto_ESC_large_ISS011_ISS011-E-13188.JPG/1920px-Istmo_de_Corinto_ESC_large_ISS011_ISS011-E-13188.JPG","title":"Diolkos","desc":"KORINTHOS, KHILIOMODHION Camera Camera Tilt: 24 Camera Focal Length: 400mm Camera: E4: Kodak DCS760C Electronic Still Camera Film: 3060E : 3060 x 2036 pixel CCD, RGBG array.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istmo_de_Corinto_ESC_large_ISS011_ISS011-E-13188.JPG","alt":"Istmo de Corinto ESC large ISS011 ISS011-E-13188"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was such a road worth building?","a":"Because it carried boats overland across the Isthmus, sparing them the long and dangerous voyage round the Peloponnese."},{"q":"Why has its commercial importance been argued down?","a":"Because a re-examination holds it carried no constant flow of ships and cargoes, hauling ships across being rare and attested only in military contexts."},{"q":"Why can subsidence be measured at its western end?","a":"Because the beachrocks there record a drop of some 35 centimetres after the destruction of Corinth in 146 BCE."}]},
{"id":"gr-191","num":"","category":"","question":"Winning the four-horse chariot race at the Pythian games in 582/81 was one way <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> displayed the excellence by which a ruler of his kind justified holding power.","questions":["The Dorian tribes of Sicyon were renamed Hyatai, Oneatai and Choireatai by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Herodotus says the names stayed in use for 60 years after his death.","Agariste, the daughter of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, married Megacles of the Alcmaeonid house at Athens, and the Spartans later deposed those descended from her father."],"answer":"Cleisthenes of Sicyon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – c. 569 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Orthagorids</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 620 – c. 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cleisthenes of Sicyon</b> is an Orthagorid, of the dynasty that held Sicyon for about a century, its rule reckoned to run from around 620 or 610 to around 520 or 510 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own reign is put at about 600 to about 569, though the ancient figures are approximate and the canonical hundred years of the tyranny cannot be taken exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took part in the First Sacred War, traditionally dated about 590, and won the four-horse chariot race at the Pythian games in 582/81.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A victory of that kind was one of the ways a ruler showed the excellence that justified his rule, along with distinguished or divine descent, success in war, and the founding of a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His daughter Agariste married Megacles, of the Alcmaeonid house at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He gave the Dorian tribes of Sicyon new names, Hyatai, Oneatai and Choireatai, and Herodotus says those names stayed in use for 60 years after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Orthagorids were fiercely hostile to Argos, which is the setting for the renaming and for the rest of what Herodotus reports of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rule of this kind was aristocratic in character rather than a break with aristocracy, on one account its culmination, and the men it pressed on were other aristocrats rather than the people at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tyrants were benefactors of their cities as well, strengthening their position by patronage and by networks among the elite, and a tyrant's rule was usually a limited one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dynasty ended when Sparta deposed those descended from Cleisthenes of Sicyon, and where to put that turns on Herodotus's 60 years and on the later chronographers, giving anything from about 520 down to about 505.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cleisthenes of Sicyon","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Victor Parker, \"The Dates of the Orthagorids of Sicyon,\" <i>Tyche</i> 7 (1992): 165–75, https://doi.org/10.15661/tyche.1992.007.20. [Open access]","Philip Davies, review of <i>The Heroic Rulers of Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, by Lynette Mitchell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.30/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Lynette Mitchell, review of <i>A Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. Kurt A. Raaflaub and Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.45/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did a chariot victory matter to a ruler?","a":"Because success in the games was one of the ways he showed the excellence that justified his rule, along with descent, success in war and the founding of a city."},{"q":"Why did he rename the Dorian tribes?","a":"Because the Orthagorids were fiercely hostile to Argos, and the new names marked Sicyon off from it."},{"q":"Why is the end of the dynasty hard to date?","a":"Because it turns on Herodotus's 60 years and on the later chronographers, giving anything from about 520 down to about 505."}]},
{"id":"gr-192","num":"","category":"","question":"Told to throw away his most valued possession to appease the gods, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cast a ring into the sea, and a fisherman brought it back to him inside a fish.","questions":["Penteconters, triremes and samainae made up the fleet of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and tradition credits him with inventing the last of those three, as Greek writers often credit famous men with famous inventions.","Taunted for never having added Samos to the king's dominions, the Persian governor at Sardis baited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with an offer of wealth and had him killed at Magnesia."],"answer":"Polycrates of Samos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">522 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Magnesia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Polycrates of Samos</b> is the tyrant whose navy was said to be one of the greatest in archaic Greece, and whose fleet Herodotus's <i>Histories</i> describe more fully than any other surviving source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those accounts conflict over how the fleet was used, how it was made up, and what part it played in his wider strategy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ships were penteconters, triremes and samainae, the last a type whose invention tradition ascribes to him, though Greek historiography habitually credits famous inventions to famous men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How he paid to keep so large a navy is a further question, and one answer canvassed is that he turned to pillaging and privateering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lavish dipteral temple of Hera on the island was commissioned under his rule, and Herodotus reckoned him more magnificent than Pisistratus on the ground that the greatest of Greek temples stood at Samos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Amasis, pharaoh of Egypt from 570 to 526 BCE, made an agreement with him that suited both, since the pharaoh sought connections to reinforce his own sovereignty and the tyrant needed manpower and support to hold his island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus tells how Amasis grew uneasy at his run of luck and urged him to throw away his most valued possession, a ring, which a fisherman then brought back inside a fish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He broke the agreement and sent the triremes, most likely built at Samos with Egyptian money, to Cambyses instead, and they arrived late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Without them the pharaoh's successor Psammetichus III could not check the Persians beyond his frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end came through Oroetes, the Persian governor at Sardis, who had been taunted for never adding Samos to the king's dominions and who baited the tyrant with an offer of the wealth his projects needed; he sailed to Magnesia at the start of 522 despite his daughter's dream, and was killed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Polycrates of Samos","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Jakub Kuciak, \"The Fleet as the Basis for Polycrates of Samos' Thalassocracy,\" <i>Electrum</i> 27 (2020): 45–66, https://doi.org/10.4467/20800909el.20.003.12793. [Open access]","Olga Constanza Ojeda Sánchez, \"Polícrates onírico: el sueño en la obra de Heródoto,\" <i>Espacio, Tiempo y Forma. Serie II, Historia Antigua</i> 35 (2022): 41–54, https://doi.org/10.5944/etfii.35.2022.30839. [Open access]","Hannah L. Ringheim, \"Hera and the Sea. Decoding Dedications at the Samian Heraion,\" <i>Studia Hercynia</i> 23, no. 1 (2019): 11–25, https://studiahercynia.ff.cuni.cz/wp-content/uploads/sites/79/2020/02/Hannah_L_Ringheim_11-25.pdf. [Open access]","Anthony J. Papalas, review of <i>Trouble in the West: Egypt and the Persian Empire, 525–332 BCE</i>, by Stephen Ruzicka, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.03.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.03.21/. [Open access]","Joel Alden Schlosser, review of <i>Sophist Kings: Persians as Other in Herodotus</i>, by Vernon L. Provencal, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.50/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/The_crucifixion_of_Polycrates_the_tyrant_after_his_capture_by_the_Persians_MET_DP837539.jpg/1920px-The_crucifixion_of_Polycrates_the_tyrant_after_his_capture_by_the_Persians_MET_DP837539.jpg","title":"Polycrates of Samos","desc":"The crucifixion of Polycrates the tyrant after his capture by the Persians. Salvator Rosa.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_crucifixion_of_Polycrates_the_tyrant_after_his_capture_by_the_Persians_MET_DP837539.jpg","alt":"The crucifixion of Polycrates the tyrant after his capture by the Persians"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the invention of the samaina doubtfully his?","a":"Because Greek historiography habitually credits famous inventions to famous men, whatever tradition ascribes."},{"q":"Why did the agreement with Amasis suit both men?","a":"Because the pharaoh sought connections to reinforce his own sovereignty and the tyrant needed manpower and support to hold his island."},{"q":"Why did Oroetes want him dead?","a":"Because the Persian governor had been taunted for never adding Samos to the king's dominions, and baited him with an offer of the wealth his projects needed."}]},
{"id":"gr-193","num":"","category":"","question":"Herodotus records that the digging of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began at two portals at once, and names the engineer as Eupalinos, son of Naustrophus, of Megara.","questions":["About 270 metres into the mountain the straight line of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was abandoned, its digger swinging west in search of sounder rock before turning back towards the original course.","Water from a spring at Agiades ran through more than 4,000 pipes laid in a deep trench along the floor of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, delivering some 400 cubic metres a day."],"answer":"Tunnel of Eupalinos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,040 m</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bore</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.8 by 1.8 m</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Tunnel of Eupalinos</b> is the aqueduct bore cut through the Kastro mountain on Samos in the middle of the 6th century BCE, and Herodotus is the first to describe it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He names the engineer as Eupalinos, son of Naustrophus, of Megara, and records what makes the work remarkable, that the digging began at two portals at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It runs about 1,040 metres (3,400 feet), 612 from the north and 424 from the south, the two headings meeting beneath a summit rising 225 metres (738 feet) above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cross-section is roughly 1.8 metres (5 ft 11 in) square, and the floor is almost perfectly level, the fall being carried instead by a deep trench along it holding the pipes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water came from a spring at Agiades north of the city, ran some 3 kilometres (1.9 miles) through more than 4,000 pipes, and delivered an estimated 400 cubic metres a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The work probably took more than a decade, and went wrong early, the rock in the north proving so weak that walls and roof collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For 165 metres (541 feet) the bore had to be lined with dry masonry walls and vaults, and about 270 metres (890 feet) in, Eupalinos abandoned the straight line and swung west in search of sounder rock before turning back towards it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One reading of the zigzag holds that the straight line was the only figure he planned to use, that an equal-legged triangle was improvised for the detour, and that a later remeasurement corrected the errors it introduced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The route as a whole has been read as evidence that he understood the theory of errors and could grade rock by its mechanical strength, a rough forerunner of modern classification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Masons left graffiti along the way, and the letters HP on slabs used in it suggest the sanctuary of Hera controlled the quarries at Agiades that supplied them; Megara itself had a water system of tunnels and fountains, though tying its own aqueduct to Eupalinos on the masonry alone has been called rash.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tunnel of Eupalinos","tags":["building","object","technology","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Åke Olson, \"How Eupalinos navigated his Way through the Mountain (An empirical approach to the geometry of Eupalinos' tunnel on Samos),\" <i>Anatolia Antiqua</i> 20 (2012): 25–34, https://doi.org/10.3406/anata.2012.1323. [Open access]","Stathis Stiros and Villy Kontogianni, \"Selection of the Path of the Eupalinos Aqueduct at Ancient Samos on the Basis of Geodetic and Geological / Geotechnical Criteria,\" <i>Bulletin of the Geological Society of Greece</i> 43, no. 2 (2010): 866–75, https://doi.org/10.12681/bgsg.11252. [Open access]","Georgios Angistalis, Georgios Dounias, Grigorios Tsokas, and Costas Zambas, \"The Walls of Eupalinos Aqueduct, Samos Island, Greece. Description, Pathology and Proposed Restoration Measures,\" <i>Bulletin of the Geological Society of Greece</i> 53, no. 1 (2018): 193–228, https://doi.org/10.12681/bgsg.18710. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]","Eleni Tziligkaki and Michael Stamatakis, \"Underground quarries in the area of Agiades, Samos Island, Greece: Notes on historical topography and chronology,\" <i>Bulletin of the Geological Society of Greece</i> 53, no. 1 (2018): 161–92, https://doi.org/10.12681/bgsg.18835. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Eupalinian_aqueduct.JPG","title":"Tunnel of Eupalinos","desc":"Inside the Tunnel of Eupalinos (Eupalinian aqueduct) in Samos, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eupalinian_aqueduct.JPG","alt":"Eupalinian aqueduct"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the floor level when the water had to fall?","a":"Because the fall is carried instead by a deep trench along the bore holding the pipes, so the tunnel itself could be cut flat."},{"q":"Why did the engineer abandon the straight line?","a":"Because the rock in the north proved so weak that walls and roof collapsed, so about 270 metres in he swung west in search of sounder rock."},{"q":"Why is the sanctuary of Hera thought to have controlled the quarries?","a":"Because the letters HP appear on slabs used in the work, which points to the quarries at Agiades being hers."}]},
{"id":"gr-194","num":"","category":"","question":"Aristotle describes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a hereditary king who turned himself into a tyrant, which makes him the earliest example of archaic tyranny anyone can name.","questions":["Weights, measures, coinage and the iron spits called obeliskoi are all credited by ancient writers to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the traditions about him are late and hard to reconcile.","A late tale of the Corinthian Actaeon's death ties <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Corinth and to the founding of Syracuse, doubling as part of a foundation myth for the Corinthian state."],"answer":"Pheidon of Argos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the tradition disagrees</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pheidon of Argos</b> is the ruler whom Aristotle describes as a hereditary king who made himself a tyrant, which makes his the earliest example of archaic tyranny that can be named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the reckoning of many scholars that would put him near in time to the poets Archilochus and Semonides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of the surviving detail about him goes back to the fourth-century historian Ephorus rather than to anything contemporary, which is the root of the difficulty he presents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He and Pittacus of Mytilene are war-leaders rather than demagogues, and the rise of Cypselus at Corinth has been connected with the pressure Argos was putting on that city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A late tale in which the Corinthian Actaeon dies ties him to Corinth again, and through it to the founding of Syracuse, so that the story doubles as part of a foundation myth for the Corinthian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What he is chiefly remembered for is a set of standards: weights, measures, coinage and the iron spits called obeliskoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pheidonian measures were in use abroad early, but the tradition tying him to the silver coins of Aegina has been challenged, one argument being that those coins were struck to a weight standard already bearing his name and that the link to the coinage itself came later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is also given a hand in the running of Olympia, where Elis and its neighbour Pisa contested control of the sanctuary, and an Olympic celebration of his is bound up with what is called the Pisan anolympiad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His date is the hardest question about him, since Herodotus puts him relatively late while other accounts synchronise him with Lycurgus of Sparta, or with a break at Olympia and the end of the Corinthian monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the phalanx wants a first inventor, he has been proposed for that too, on the argument that hoplite arms were made for aristocratic duelling and only afterwards suited a formation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pheidon of Argos","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Kõiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08/. [Open access]","Brian Lavelle, review of <i>Die archaische Tyrannis</i>, by Loretana de Libero, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.18/. [Open access]","Ben Brown, review of <i>Elis: Internal Politics and External Policy in Ancient Greece</i>, by Graeme Bourke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.02.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.02.36/. [Open access]","Adam Schwartz, review of <i>The Spear, the Scroll, and the Pebble: How the Greek City-State Developed as a Male Warrior-Citizen Collective</i>, by Richard A. Billows, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.15/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is he the earliest nameable archaic tyrant?","a":"Because Aristotle describes him as a hereditary king who made himself a tyrant, which is the transition the word names."},{"q":"Why is the detail about him unreliable?","a":"Because much of it goes back to the fourth-century historian Ephorus rather than to anything contemporary."},{"q":"Why is his link to the Aeginetan coinage challenged?","a":"Because one argument holds those coins were struck to a weight standard already bearing his name, and that the link to the coinage itself came later."}]},
{"id":"gr-195","num":"","category":"","question":"The genealogies of Phoroneus, Danaos and Perseus all lean towards <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the 6th century BCE, and the handling of its Heraclid founders goes back further still.","questions":["Asine was destroyed and its people moved to Korone in Messenia, perhaps helped by Sparta, an enemy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a likely ally of the Asineans.","Three phylai are reckoned for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a fourth mentioned only from about 460 BCE, and its votive-rich sanctuary on the Aspis hill belongs mostly to the 7th century."],"answer":"Archaic Argos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rises</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phylai</span><span class=\"dt-v\">three, a fourth from c. 460 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Archaic Argos</b> is the city of the Argive plain in the centuries after the Bronze Age, and the rise of its power in the north-east Argolid is dated to the second half of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cult of Apollo Pythaieus spread through the Peloponnese around then, and a league of Argos, Sparta and Epidaurus may have been founded on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Argive Heraion, the great sanctuary of the plain, is taken with that evidence to show a city already holding some hegemony in its own region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genealogies of Phoroneus, Argos, Melampous, Proitos, Danaos and Perseus all show marked pro-Argive leanings by the 6th century, and some by the 7th or late 8th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition of the Heraclid founders of the city was handled the same way, and that handling can be traced back to the second half of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its neighbours felt the change: Asine was destroyed and its people moved to Korone in Messenia, perhaps helped by Sparta, an Argive enemy and a likely ally of the Asineans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Argos and Sparta then fought border wars for centuries, in the Thyreatis or in the Argolid, and most of those conflicts fall in the archaic age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stories told about them were not fixed: the traditions of Hysiae, the Battle of the Champions and Sepeia were still being shaped in the 5th century by whatever course Argive foreign policy was taking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three phylai are reckoned for the city in this period, with a fourth mentioned only from about 460.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the Aspis hill the votives run from the Late Geometric period to the Early Hellenistic and most belong to the 7th and early 6th centuries, confirming a sanctuary there, while a 6th-century building inscription from the city has been read as evidence that such work was made deliberately anonymous, so that no family could claim the credit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Archaic Argos","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Kõiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08/. [Open access]","Anna Philippa-Touchais and Alexandra Alexandridou, \"A New Heraion (?) on the Aspis hilltop at Argos,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 47–64, https://doi.org/10.4000/16637. [Open access]","Evan Vance, \"Archaic Construction and the Public Sphere: The Contribution of the Epigraphic Sources from Aegina and Argos,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 65–88, https://doi.org/10.4000/1663c. [Open access]","Elena Franchi, \"Memory of the Past in Classical Argos: The Archaic Border Wars with Sparta and Argos' 5th-c. Foreign Policy,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 163–90, https://doi.org/10.4000/16638. [Open access]","James Kierstead, review of <i>Die griechischen Phylen: Funktion – Entstehung – Leistungen</i>, by Oliver Grote, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.09.47, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.09.47/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Argos_-_Coronelli_Vincenzo_-_1688.jpg","title":"Archaic Argos","desc":"Vincenzo Coronelli - Repubblica di Venezia p. IV. Citta, Fortezze, ed altri Luoghi principali dell' Albania, Epiro e Livadia, e particolarmente i posseduti da Veneti descritti e delineati dal p. Coronelli, Venice, 1688.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Argos_-_Coronelli_Vincenzo_-_1688.jpg","alt":"Argos - Coronelli Vincenzo - 1688"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Argos","at":[22.7167,37.6167]},"why":[{"q":"Why do the city's genealogies count as evidence of its ambitions?","a":"Because those of Phoroneus, Argos, Melampous, Proitos, Danaos and Perseus all show marked pro-Argive leanings by the 6th century, and some earlier."},{"q":"Why were the stories of its wars with Sparta not fixed?","a":"Because the traditions of Hysiae, the Battle of the Champions and Sepeia were still being shaped in the 5th century by whatever course Argive foreign policy was taking."},{"q":"Why has a building inscription been read as deliberately anonymous?","a":"Because it presents the work without naming anyone, which is taken as evidence that no family was to claim the credit."}]},
{"id":"gr-196","num":"","category":"","question":"One reading of the archaic history of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has Argive domination, then Epidaurian rule under the tyrant Procles, then an oligarchy of its own from about 618 BCE.","questions":["The weights, measures and coins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named a standard used far beyond it, and the story that Solon replaced that system at Athens is anachronistic on the archaeology.","The quarrel with Athens was almost wholly naval, and Corinth is said to have sold the Athenians twenty ships to use against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the years before Salamis."],"answer":"Aegina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 618 – 613 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple of Aphaia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late archaic, 13 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Aegina</b> is the island polis of the Saronic Gulf, whose harbour mound at Kolonna was occupied from the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Aegina Treasure, a hoard of gold jewellery, probably came from one or more Middle Helladic tombs there, and its pieces were made in different parts of the eastern Mediterranean at somewhat different dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation in the eastern suburb of Bronze Age Kolonna has uncovered the destruction deposit of two early Mycenaean buildings of the 16th century BCE, the older of them read as a purple-dye works from its pigment-bearing sherds and its heaps of mollusc shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island's archaic political history has been set out as a sequence of dominations: Argive, then Epidaurian under the tyrant Procles, and then an oligarchy of its own from about 618 or 613.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dating independence that late puts the first war with Athens, the origin of the ancient enmity Herodotus reports between them, correspondingly late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That quarrel was almost wholly naval, and Corinth is said to have sold the Athenians twenty ships to use against the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its weights, measures and coins gave their name to a standard used far beyond it, and the tradition that Solon replaced that system at Athens is anachronistic on the archaeological evidence, though it makes sense as a symbol of Athens setting itself apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sanctuary of Aphaia is the great building of the island, and the cost of its late archaic temple has been reckoned at 13 talents, or perhaps 6.3 without the sculpture in its pediments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An inscription commemorating that temple names a priest where a city would name its eponymous magistrate, and presents the finished monument without giving anyone the credit, which has been read as deliberate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the Persians burnt the older temple on the site, and whether the sculptures of its successor honour Aeginetan prowess at Salamis in 480, have been argued over for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aegina","tags":["place","city","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["William Loomis, review of <i>Excursions in Epichoric History: Aiginetan Essays</i>, by Thomas J. Figueira, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.01.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.01.01/. [Open access]","Evan Vance, \"Archaic Construction and the Public Sphere: The Contribution of the Epigraphic Sources from Aegina and Argos,\" in <i>Argos Through the Argive Lens</i>, ed. Clémence Weber-Pallez and Evan Vance (Athens: École française d'Athènes, 2025), 65–88, https://doi.org/10.4000/1663c. [Open access]","John G. Younger, review of <i>The Aigina Treasure: Aegean Bronze Age Jewellery and a Mystery Revisited</i>, ed. J. Lesley Fitton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.02.64, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.02.64/. [Open access]","Lydia Berger, Gerhard Forstenpointner, Peter Frühauf, and Fabian Kanz, \"More than just a color: Archaeological, analytical, and procedural aspects of Late Bronze Age purple-dye production at Cape Kolonna, Aegina,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 6 (2024): e0304340, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0304340. [Open access]","Hansgeorg Bankel and Andrew Stewart, \"New observations on the pediments of the early Classical temple of Aphaia on Aegina,\" <i>Journal of Greek Archaeology</i> 7 (2022): 173–212, https://doi.org/10.32028/jga.v7i.1714. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Temple_of_Apollo_Aegina_Greece.jpg/1920px-Temple_of_Apollo_Aegina_Greece.jpg","title":"Aegina","desc":"Temple of Apollo in Ægina, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Apollo_Aegina_Greece.jpg","alt":"Temple of Apollo Aegina Greece"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Aegina","at":[23.49,37.73]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the date of the island's independence matter?","a":"Because putting it as late as about 618 or 613 puts the first war with Athens, the origin of the enmity Herodotus reports, correspondingly late."},{"q":"Why is the tradition that Solon replaced its standard anachronistic?","a":"Because the archaeological evidence will not carry it, though the story makes sense as a symbol of Athens setting itself apart."},{"q":"Why is one Bronze Age building read as a purple-dye works?","a":"Because the destruction deposit of that 16th-century structure held pigment-bearing sherds along with heaps of mollusc shells, which are the residue of making the dye."}]},
{"id":"gr-197","num":"","category":"","question":"The names of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are read as the city of the bronzeworkers and the city of the rowers, and the two of them colonised together rather than separately.","questions":["Both Pithekoussai and Cumae are accepted as joint foundations of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Corcyra's Eretrian origin seems sure and Rhegion was the last of the western settlements.","A treaty between the two cities of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reported to have regulated which weapons might be used, and one of them was governed by aristocrats called Hippobotai."],"answer":"Chalcis and Eretria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chalcis founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Joint colonies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Strait bridged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 425 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chalcis and Eretria</b> are the two cities of the Lelantine plain on Euboea, and their names are read as the city of the bronzeworkers and the city of the rowers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chalcis was established in the 11th century BCE in a very favourable place, on the strait that has been bridged since the late 5th century and makes Euboea one of only two Greek islands a traveller can reach on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is nevertheless not a well known city, and Eretria is much the better known of the two, though its excavations have run for decades without being fully published.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretria is one of the main archaeological sites of the Greek Early Iron Age, and its heroon at the West Gate, its House of the Mosaics and its sanctuary of Apollo Daphnephoros are standard illustrations in the handbooks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earlier phases of that sanctuary were a long-awaited publication of their own, and the ground beneath the city goes back to the Late Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The two colonised together rather than separately, in Chalcidice, in the Levant around Al Mina, and in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are accepted as founders of Pithekoussai and of Cumae, and the Eretrian origin of Corcyra seems sure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Naxos, Zankle and Rhegion complete the western list, and Rhegion, the last of them, belongs to the closing years of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chalcis was governed by aristocrats called Hippobotai, horse-feeders, whose horses carried noble warriors to the field rather than serving as a weapon, and a treaty between the two cities is reported to have regulated which weapons might be used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tie between Eretria and the sea is the constant in it: a votive inscription found near its theatre records a body called the Aeinautai, the ever-sailors, and carries the earliest trace of the cult of Hermes in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chalcis and Eretria","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of <i>Untersuchungen zum Lelantischen Krieg und verwandten Problemen der frühgriechischen Geschichte</i>, by Victor Parker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.06.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.06.12/. [Open access]","Olivier Mariaud, review of <i>Eretria: A Guide to the Ancient City</i>, by Pierre Ducrey, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.05.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.05.24/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Le sanctuaire d'Apollon Daphnéphoros à l'époque géométrique</i>, by Samuel Verdan, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.11.59, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.11.59/. [Open access]","David Michael Smith, review of <i>An Island between Two Worlds: The Archaeology of Euboea from Prehistoric to Byzantine Times</i>, ed. Žarko Tankosić, Fanis Mavridis and Maria Kosma, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.10.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.10.42/. [Open access]","Barbara Leone, \"Dedication by the <i>Aeinautae</i> in Eretria,\" <i>Axon</i> 1, no. 1 (2017), https://doi.org/10.14277/2532-6848/Axon-1-1-17-9. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Chalcis","at":[23.595,38.4625]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the better-dug of the two still incompletely known?","a":"Because Eretria's excavations have run for decades without being fully published, so even its sanctuary of Apollo Daphnephoros was a long-awaited publication of its own."},{"q":"Why did the two cities colonise together rather than separately?","a":"Because both are accepted as founders of Pithekoussai and of Cumae, and their joint ventures reach Chalcidice, the Levant around Al Mina and the west."},{"q":"Why are the Hippobotai horse-feeders rather than cavalry?","a":"Because their horses carried noble warriors to the field rather than serving as a weapon."}]},
{"id":"gr-198","num":"","category":"","question":"Three brief ancient notices of the fighting survive, and not one of them names the plain that gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the modern title scholars know it by.","questions":["Samos, Corinth and Thessaly are named on the Chalcidian side of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with Miletus and Megara on the Eretrian, which is what makes it look panhellenic.","Thucydides took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the most notable quarrel among the Greeks before his own, and said the rest of Greece took sides in it."],"answer":"Lelantine War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lefkandi burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chalcis taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">506 BCE, by Athens</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lelantine War</b> is the conflict between Chalcis and Eretria over the plain that lies between them on Euboea, and its title is a modern one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three brief notices of hostilities survive, in Herodotus, Thucydides and Aristotle, and not one of them names that plain as the thing being fought over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fighting on Euboea is mentioned four times in poetry, by Hesiod, Archilochus and Theognis and in an anonymous elegiac fragment, while the fuller accounts come from Strabo and Plutarch drawing on older writers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides took the quarrel for the most notable among the Greeks before his own war, and his remark that the rest of Greece took sides in it makes it a panhellenic tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the later writers preserve is strange enough: that the legendary Curetes fought endlessly for the plain, and that the Chalcidians took up the custom of pederasty after watching a lover die gloriously against the Eretrians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The archaeological support usually offered for a real war is the burning of Lefkandi-Xeropolis at about 700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allies are named on both sides, Samos, Corinth and Thessaly with Chalcis and Miletus and Megara with Eretria, and the Thessalian part is put down to ties between the Hippobotai, the horse-feeding aristocrats of Chalcis, and the Thessalian nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hoplite tactics were already known to Archilochus, whose best-dated poems fall between 655 and 645, which places the fighting he mentions near the middle of the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Euboean settlers led the colonising of the north until the war ended their power there, after which Chalcis worked with the Andrians, hostile to Eretria, to plant colonies where its own were threatened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the war actually was remains open: one account makes both cities outgrown offshoots of Lefkandi that came to blows over a fertile plain, another turns the fighting into ritual combat between young aristocrats until Athens took Chalcis in 506, and it has become the standard case of an archaic subject whose sources will not carry a narrative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lelantine War","tags":["event","history","greece","archaic","warfare"],"sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of <i>Untersuchungen zum Lelantischen Krieg und verwandten Problemen der frühgriechischen Geschichte</i>, by Victor Parker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1998.06.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.06.12/. [Open access]","Natasha Bershadsky, \"Impossible Memories of the Lelantine War,\" in <i>Dossier: Place aux objets!</i> (Paris: Éditions de l'École des hautes études en sciences sociales, 2018), 191–213, https://doi.org/10.4000/books.editionsehess.5679. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200–479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>Αρχαϊκή Ελλάδα και πόλεμος</i>, by Katerina S. Meidani, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.10.48, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.10.48/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Lelantine_War.png/1920px-Lelantine_War.png","title":"Lelantine War","desc":"Jonathan M. Hall, A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200–479 BCE, 2nd Edition, Chichester, Wiley Blackwell, 2014, pp. 2, 3. ISBN: 9781118301272.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lelantine_War.png","alt":"Lelantine War"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the war's name a modern one?","a":"Because the three brief ancient notices of hostilities, in Herodotus, Thucydides and Aristotle, do not name that plain as the thing being fought over."},{"q":"Why is it the standard case of a subject whose sources will not carry a narrative?","a":"Because what the later writers preserve includes the Curetes fighting endlessly for the plain and the Chalcidians taking up pederasty after watching a lover die gloriously."},{"q":"Why does Archilochus help date the fighting?","a":"Because hoplite tactics were already known to him and his best-dated poems fall between 655 and 645, which places the fighting he mentions near the middle of the 7th century."}]},
{"id":"gr-199","num":"","category":"","question":"Workshops making the pottery of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been identified in the Hellespont, around the Black Sea and in the Nile Delta, which are the very regions it was settling.","questions":["The oracle of Apollo at Didyma, joined by a Sacred Way, was the great extra-urban sanctuary of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its temple ranked with those of Ephesus and Samos.","The name sits close to Milatos on Crete, and the Hittite records that call the Greeks Ahhiyawa may name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Millawanda."],"answer":"Archaic Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pottery abroad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 494 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Archaic Miletus</b> is the Greek city on the Anatolian coast, standing on the cusp between the Greek world and that of the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its foundation stories are many, its name sits close to Milatos on Crete, and the Hittite records that call the Greeks Ahhiyawa may name the place Millawanda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its heyday came before the Classical age, which is part of why so little survives about it, the Greeks not yet writing on any large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sanctuary that mattered most to the city stood outside it, the oracle of Apollo at Didyma, joined to it by a Sacred Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That temple ranked with the temples of Artemis at Ephesus and Hera on Samos among the largest and most spectacular cult buildings of the archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city's own pottery has been placed by decades of excavation, and Miletus holds a central position in the development of its painting style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the middle of the 7th century BCE that pottery was wanted from the Black Sea to Egypt, and workshops making it have been identified in the Hellespont, around the Black Sea and in the Nile Delta, which are the very regions the city was settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One argument sets it in a colonising alliance with Megara, the two sharing the oligarchic office of the aisymnetai and an elite kinship claimed to reach back to the Lelantine War, in which both had sided with Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Apollo Didymeos had his part in those ventures, while within the walls the sanctuary of Aphrodite on the Zeytintepe has supplied much of the material by which archaic Milesian pottery is dated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its archaic career closes with the Ionian Revolt, where Herodotus has Aristagoras call his rule over the city a kingship, and where the help Athens sent the rebels became the beginning of troubles for Greeks and barbarians alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Archaic Miletus","tags":["city","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]","Thomas G. Schattner, review of <i>La céramique milésienne et apparentée à l'époque archaïque</i>, ed. Anne Coulié, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.04.21/. [Open access]","Udo Schlotzhauer, review of <i>Der archaische Heiligtumsbefund vom Taxiarchis-Hügel in Didyma</i>, by Helga Bumke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.04/. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]","David J. DeVore, review of <i>Reading Herodotus: A Study of the Logoi in Book 5 of Herodotus' Histories</i>, by Elizabeth Irwin and Emily Greenwood, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.08.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.08.05/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Miletus%2C_Illustration_for_La_Terre-Sainte_et_les_lieux_illustr%C3%A9s_par_les_ap%C3%B4tres%2C_by_Adrien_Egron%2C_1837_%2839%29.jpg","title":"Archaic Miletus","desc":"Miletus, Illustration for La Terre-Sainte et les lieux illustrés par les apôtres, by Adrien Egron, 1837 (39). published on 1837.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miletus,_Illustration_for_La_Terre-Sainte_et_les_lieux_illustr%C3%A9s_par_les_ap%C3%B4tres,_by_Adrien_Egron,_1837_(39).jpg","alt":"Miletus, Illustration for La Terre-Sainte et les lieux illustrés par les apôtres, by Adrien Egron, 1837 (39)"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Miletus","at":[27.2783,37.5303]},"why":[{"q":"Why does so little survive of the city's greatest period?","a":"Because its heyday came before the Classical age, when the Greeks were not yet writing on any large scale."},{"q":"Why does its pottery trace the city's reach?","a":"Because after the middle of the 7th century it was wanted from the Black Sea to Egypt, and workshops making it are identified in the very regions the city was settling."},{"q":"Why is a colonising alliance with Megara proposed?","a":"Because the two shared the oligarchic office of the aisymnetai and an elite kinship claimed to reach back to the Lelantine War, in which both had sided with Eretria."}]},
{"id":"gr-200","num":"","category":"","question":"Running between the Hermos and the Maiandros rivers and taking in Samos and Chios, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had Lydians and Karians for neighbours as well as Dorians and Aeolians.","questions":["Herodotus counted twelve cities in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the dodekapolis, though later writers use the name loosely enough to mean the Greeks of Asia Minor at large.","Serving as mercenaries in Egypt and the Near East and trading into the Black Sea left the identity of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> soft and open to its neighbours."],"answer":"Ionia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12, the dodekapolis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian Wars end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ionia</b> is the stretch of the western seaboard of Asia Minor held by the Ionian Greeks, running between the Hermos and the Maiandros rivers and taking in the islands of Samos and Chios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus counted twelve cities there, the dodekapolis, and the name stayed slippery afterwards, meaning in some later writers the cities of the Ionian League and in others the Greeks of Asia Minor at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Miletus, the greatest of them, stood between the Greek world and that of the Near East, and its heyday fell before the Classical age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The region's pottery was made chiefly in its south, where decades of excavation have placed the workshops, while Chios and the northern cities went their own ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its neighbours were not only Dorians and Aeolians but Lydians and Karians, and its people served as mercenaries in Egypt and the Near East while trading into the Mediterranean and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ionian identity was accordingly soft and permeable, open to the cultures around it rather than fenced off from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persian invasion is the point past which that could not return, and where a Greek identity built on a sense of separateness begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The region's foundation stories and traditions were themselves inventions of some date, and fixing that date means reading the literary sources against what the ground holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ionia has usually been valued for what it wrote, philosophy and poetry and history, much of it later than the archaic period, though those writers were a tiny minority of the people who lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beginning instead with the landscape, then the archaeology, and only then the texts, gives a region shaped by Anatolian influences as much as by Greek ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ionia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Lee E. Patterson, review of <i>The Land of Ionia: Society and Economy in the Archaic Period</i>, by Alan M. Greaves, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.17/. [Open access]","Omar Coloru, review of <i>Ethnic Constructs in Antiquity: The Role of Power and Tradition</i>, ed. Ton Derks and Nico Roymans, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.07.71, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.07.71/. [Open access]","Naoíse Mac Sweeney, review of <i>Accustomed to Obedience? Classical Ionia and the Aegean World, 480–294 BCE</i>, by Joshua P. Nudell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.46/. [Open access]","Thomas G. Schattner, review of <i>La céramique milésienne et apparentée à l'époque archaïque</i>, ed. Anne Coulié, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.04.21/. [Open access]","P. J. Smith, review of <i>Miletos, the Ornament of Ionia: A History of the City to 400 B.C.E.</i>, by Vanessa B. Gorman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.06.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.06.17/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Priene_2013-03-24t.jpg/1920px-Priene_2013-03-24t.jpg","title":"The plain of the Maeander from Priene","desc":"The flat valley of the Maeander seen from the hillside ruins of Priene, one of the twelve cities of the Ionian League, with cut blocks of the ancient city in the foreground and mountains closing the far side of the plain.","alt":"A wide green river plain with cultivated fields and lines of trees, seen from a hillside strewn with cut ancient blocks, a long mountain ridge rising in haze beyond","credit":"Rabe!, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Priene_2013-03-24t.jpg"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Ionia","at":[27.5,38.2],"kind":"region","area":[[26.65,38.75],[27.45,38.72],[27.85,38.45],[27.9,38.05],[27.75,37.7],[27.35,37.45],[26.85,37.55],[26.3,37.95],[25.9,38.55],[26.15,38.8]]},"why":[{"q":"Why was Ionian identity soft and permeable?","a":"Because its neighbours were Lydians and Karians as well as Dorians and Aeolians, and its people served as mercenaries in Egypt and the Near East while trading into the Black Sea."},{"q":"Why does the Persian invasion mark a turn?","a":"Because it is the point past which that openness could not return, and where a Greek identity built on a sense of separateness begins."},{"q":"Why does beginning with the texts distort the region?","a":"Because it has usually been valued for what it wrote, much of that later than the archaic period, by a tiny minority of the people who lived there."}]},
{"id":"gr-201","num":"","category":"","question":"Poseidon Helikonios is the god of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cities across Ionia and its Black Sea colonies kept shrines of their own to him.","questions":["A site on Otomatik Tepe was long taken for the archaic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a newer identification places it in the Mycale beside the Carian town of Melia.","The festival called the Panionia was held either at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself or in one of the member states, and repeated attendance is what kept their bonds alive."],"answer":"Panionion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian League</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Member states</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12, later 13</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Panionion</b> is the common sanctuary of the Ionian Greeks, the place of the festival called the Panionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its god is Poseidon Helikonios, one of three divinities counted as distinctively Ionian alongside Dionysos Phleus and Apollo Delphinios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Individual Ionian cities kept shrines of their own to that god, and so did the Ionian colonies on the shores of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The festival was held either at the sanctuary itself or in one of the member states, and taking part in it again and again is what kept the bonds between them alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It belongs to the class of supra-regional sanctuary, the kind that binds states together without being the property of any one of them, and the coastal life of the region may itself lie behind the choice of a sea god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where the archaic sanctuary stood has been argued over, since a site on Otomatik Tepe was long taken for it and that identification proved hard to maintain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A newer one places the archaic Panionion, together with the Carian town of Melia, in the Mycale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If it holds, the dates of the finds there would put the founding of the Ionian League at about 600 BCE, and the League itself may have been formed in answer to conflict between Ionian Greeks and Carians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How many cities belonged is not fixed either, Herodotus counting twelve while the later association has thirteen member states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Poseidon serves elsewhere as the god of leagues asserting a past that is not Dorian, and what this sanctuary held together was an identity grounded less in the geography of Ionia than in things done in common, and open to its neighbours until the Persian invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Panionion","tags":["building","place","practice","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Denise Demetriou, review of <i>Landscape, Ethnicity and Identity in the Archaic Mediterranean Area</i>, ed. Gabriele Cifani and Simon Stoddart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.11.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.11.34/. [Open access]","Floris van den Eijnde, review of <i>Kult-Politik-Ethnos: Überregionale Heiligtümer im Spannungsfeld von Kult und Politik</i>, ed. Klaus Freitag, Peter Funke and Matthias Haake, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.10.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.10.23/. [Open access]","Dies van der Linde, review of <i>Roman Ionia: Constructions of Cultural Identity in Western Asia Minor</i>, by Martin Hallmannsecker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.09.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.09.15/. [Open access]","Lee E. Patterson, review of <i>The Land of Ionia: Society and Economy in the Archaic Period</i>, by Alan M. Greaves, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.17/. [Open access]","Omar Coloru, review of <i>Ethnic Constructs in Antiquity: The Role of Power and Tradition</i>, ed. Ton Derks and Nico Roymans, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.07.71, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.07.71/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Panionium_theatre.jpg/1920px-Panionium_theatre.jpg","title":"Panionion","desc":"Panionium theatre near Güzelçamlı, on the north slope of Mt. Mycale, on the top of a low hill called Otomatik Tepe.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panionium_theatre.jpg","alt":"Panionium theatre"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Panionion","at":[27.235,37.7127]},"why":[{"q":"Why is repeated attendance at the festival what mattered?","a":"Because taking part in it again and again is what kept the bonds between the member cities alive, the sanctuary belonging to none of them in particular."},{"q":"Why is the League's founding date tied to a disputed site?","a":"Because a newer identification places the archaic sanctuary and the Carian town of Melia in the Mycale, and if it holds the finds there put the founding at about 600 BCE."},{"q":"Why is the identity it held together not a matter of geography?","a":"Because it was grounded less in the geography of Ionia than in things done in common, and stayed open to the region's neighbours until the Persian invasion."}]},
{"id":"gr-202","num":"","category":"","question":"Counted among the seven wonders in Hellenistic times, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands over an archaic predecessor whose remains were uncovered beneath the 4th-century building.","questions":["The mysteries for which the goddess became famous are unattested at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the 4th century BCE, appearing first amid the rivalry of Alexander's successors.","Ranking with the temple of Hera on Samos and the temple of Apollo at Didyma, the archaic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one of the great cult buildings of its age."],"answer":"Artemision at Ephesus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 775 – 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rediscovered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1870 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Artemision at Ephesus</b> is the sanctuary of Artemis outside that city, counted in Hellenistic times among the seven wonders of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath its 4th-century temple lies an archaic predecessor, and the early growth of the sanctuary is bound up with a natural site that changed shape over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That archaic building ranked with the temple of Hera on Samos and the temple of Apollo at Didyma among the largest and most spectacular cult buildings of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city it served lay on the Kaystros, and its founder hero was Androklos, as Miletus had Neileus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mysteries for which the goddess later became famous are unattested before the 4th century BCE, appearing first amid the competition of Alexander's successors for the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The temple was lost for centuries and found again only in 1870, after a seven-year search, and what survived of the 4th-century building and of its archaic predecessor was uncovered then and in a second campaign a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The community that raised it is far less well understood than the sanctuary itself, and the reason is the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Ephesus, as along that whole coast, the earlier levels lie under substantial Hellenistic and Roman ruins and can be reached only in keyholes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so, 74 pieces of Geometric and Archaic pottery from one such excavation, set beside notes made in the 1920s, are enough to show that the northern slope of Panayır Dağ was a settlement quarter before the Hellenistic period, while the earliest pottery from another deposit on the site belongs to the second quarter of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is archaeological rather than merely literary evidence for where the people who built the Artemision lived, though the Greek and Greek-style pottery there cannot be weighed properly against the non-Greek styles of the same date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Artemision at Ephesus","tags":["building","place","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Frederick G. Naerebout, review of <i>Die Archäologie der ephesischen Artemis: Gestalt und Ritual eines Heiligtums</i>, ed. Ulrike Muss, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.05.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.05.31/. [Open access]","Alan M. Greaves, review of <i>Archäologische Forschungen zur Siedlungsgeschichte von Ephesos in geometrischer, archaischer und klassischer Zeit</i>, by Martin Steskal, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.17/. [Open access]","Udo Schlotzhauer, review of <i>Der archaische Heiligtumsbefund vom Taxiarchis-Hügel in Didyma</i>, by Helga Bumke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.04/. [Open access]","Dies van der Linde, review of <i>Roman Ionia: Constructions of Cultural Identity in Western Asia Minor</i>, by Martin Hallmannsecker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.09.15, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.09.15/. [Open access]","Heidi Wendt, review of <i>The Mysteries of Artemis of Ephesos: Cult, Polis, and Change in the Graeco-Roman World</i>, by Guy MacLean Rogers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.10.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.10.05/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Philips_Galle%2C_Dianatemplet_i_Efesos%2C_1572%2C_KKSgb10001-5%2C_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Philips_Galle%2C_Dianatemplet_i_Efesos%2C_1572%2C_KKSgb10001-5%2C_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Artemision at Ephesus","desc":"A 16th-century engraving of the temple of Artemis at Ephesus, one of a series on the wonders of the ancient world, showing the building as the engraver imagined it.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philips_Galle,_Dianatemplet_i_Efesos,_1572,_KKSgb10001-5,_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst_(cropped).jpg","alt":"Philips Galle, Dianatemplet i Efesos, 1572, KKSgb10001-5, Statens Museum for Kunst"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Artemision at Ephesus","at":[27.3639,37.9497]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the community that built the sanctuary less well known than the building?","a":"Because along that whole coast the earlier levels lie under substantial Hellenistic and Roman ruins and can be reached only in keyholes."},{"q":"Why are 74 sherds worth so much here?","a":"Because set beside notes made in the 1920s they show the northern slope of Panayır Dağ was a settlement quarter before the Hellenistic period — archaeological rather than merely literary evidence for where the builders lived."},{"q":"Why are the goddess's famous mysteries treated as late?","a":"Because they are unattested before the 4th century BCE, appearing first amid the competition of Alexander's successors for the region."}]},
{"id":"gr-203","num":"","category":"","question":"Reaching from southern Italy and Sicily to the Propontis and the Black Sea, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> planted settlements overseas from the 8th century BCE onward.","questions":["The western and eastern halves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have usually been studied by different specialists, so comparing the two is a recent undertaking.","The regions Miletus was settling during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are where its pottery workshops turn up: the Hellespont, the shores of the Black Sea and the Nile Delta."],"answer":"Greek colonisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlements</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 6th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Corcyra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">733 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Greek colonisation</b> is the name given to the settlements Greeks planted overseas from the 8th century BCE onward, reaching from southern Italy and Sicily to the Propontis and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the north Aegean the Euboeans led, planting the Chalcidice, until the Lelantine War put an end to their power there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chalcis afterwards worked with the Andrians, who were hostile to Eretria, to plant colonies where its own were threatened, and those Andrian foundations belong to the middle of the 7th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Megarians took the Propontis and the shores of the Black Sea, in what one argument calls a colonising alliance with Miletus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Milesian pottery workshops turn up in the Hellespont, around the Black Sea and in the Nile Delta, which are the very regions that city was settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Poteidaea was founded by Corinthians at a time when the Euboeans were no longer a great power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient sources give Corcyra an early Eretrian settlement, which many reject for want of archaeological evidence to match it, and the next stage there is the Corinthian foundation of 733 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The western and eastern halves of the phenomenon have usually been worked on by different specialists, so that comparing them is recent, and it raises the question whether the movements of the 8th century were a break with what went before or one more stretch of a migration that never stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pull-factors have been sought in Magna Graecia and Sicily, mobility and the settling-down that follows are treated as two poles of one analysis, and a line is drawn between colonies founded from the homeland and those founded from other colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditional picture has been questioned over the last two decades, particularly what organisation these ventures had, if any, and how the settlers stood towards the people already there, and a survey of the whole field is now reckoned beyond any one person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek colonisation","tags":["event","practice","history","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 1</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","Dominik Delp, review of <i>Comparing Greek Colonies: Mobility and Settlement Consolidation from Southern Italy to the Black Sea (8th–6th Century BC)</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.38/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 2</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Ancient_colonies.PNG/1920px-Ancient_colonies.PNG","title":"Greek colonisation","desc":"Map of the colonies of Ancient Greece and Phoenicia — circa 550 BCE. Also other regional 'dynasties' of the Mediterranean region.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_colonies.PNG","alt":"Ancient colonies"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why has comparing the eastern and western ventures come only recently?","a":"Because the two halves have usually been worked on by different specialists, so setting them side by side is new."},{"q":"Why is the early Eretrian settlement of Corcyra rejected by many?","a":"Because the ancient sources give it while no archaeological evidence matches, the next stage there being the Corinthian foundation of 733 BCE."},{"q":"Why does the comparison raise a question about the 8th century itself?","a":"Because it asks whether those movements were a break with what went before or one more stretch of a migration that never stopped."}]},
{"id":"gr-204","num":204,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Where the line falls between an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and an emporion is much argued over, since Herodotus applies the second word to Naukratis, Oinoussai and places on the Black Sea.","questions":["Olbia was laid out from the first with cult buildings among its houses and grew from an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a state that had absorbed neighbouring Berezan by the end of the Archaic period.","Settlers in an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed citizens of their mother city by origin while building identities of their own, holding on to only part of what they had brought."],"answer":"apoikia","answerText":"apoikia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Main period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"An <b>apoikia</b> is a settlement the Greeks planted away from the community that sent it out, read as an organised emigration whose emphasis falls on departure from the oikos, the household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first settlers were apoikoi, and those despatched later to strengthen a foundation already standing were epoikoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those who went remained citizens of their metropolis by origin, and in the new place built identities and memories of their own, holding on to only part of what they had brought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As the Greek population grew such a settlement could become a full polis, and Olbia on the Black Sea, laid out from the first with cult buildings among its houses, turned into a state that had absorbed neighbouring Berezan by the end of the Archaic period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A foundation need not answer to a single mother city: Locri Epizephyrii in southern Italy may have been a joint venture of East and West Locris, or of that people as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The word stands beside emporion, which Herodotus applies to Oinoussai, Naukratis and several places around the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the line between the two falls is much argued over, and one study makes the emporion a proto-settlement rather than the trading station usually understood, leaving the apoikia a regular Greek city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vocabulary shifted in the 4th century BCE, when nearly all Athenian foundations became cleruchies and their settlers cleruchs, so that Plutarch later used the newer words of far older ventures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether any of it should be called colonisation is disputed, and one position rejects the word for these early ventures, traces the modern habit to notions of cultural superiority, and prefers the neutral migration, since colonisation implies missions organised by a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other defends organised colonisation as a continuum running from private enterprises to politically organised communities already in the 8th century BCE, while a recent study takes apoikismos, expatriation, through migration theory and sets aside explanations by overpopulation, shortage and trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","D. Erciyas, review of <i>Hellenic Colonization in Euxeinos Pontos: Penetration, Early Establishment and the Problem of the “Emporion” Revisited</i>, by Elias K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.05.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.05.24/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]","Gregory Crane, review of <i>Athens and Aigina in the Age of Imperial Colonization</i>, by Thomas J. Figueira, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1993.01.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.01.09/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Zwischen Ansässigkeit und Mobilität: die sogenannte Grosse Kolonisation der Griechen aus migrationstheoretischer Perspektive</i>, by Dominick Delp, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.08.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.08.18/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does the word emphasise departure rather than arrival?","a":"Because it is built on the oikos, the household, so it names an organised emigration away from one rather than the planting of a new place."},{"q":"Why did the vocabulary shift in the 4th century?","a":"Because nearly all Athenian foundations became cleruchies and their settlers cleruchs, so Plutarch later used the newer words of far older ventures."},{"q":"Why do some scholars refuse the word colonisation here?","a":"Because colonisation implies missions organised by a state, and the habit is traced to notions of cultural superiority, so migration is preferred as neutral."}]},
{"id":"gr-205","num":205,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Herodotus was told by the Sybarites that Dorieus, the Spartan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, came to grief because he had failed to follow the instructions Delphi gave him.","questions":["The privileges Cyrene's colonists granted their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed to his descendants, and the tomb of Battus stood in the agora, where no ordinary Greek was buried.","Homer's Skherie is laid out by an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who divides the farmland and builds a monumental agora with fixed stone seating, a temple beside it, city walls and a water supply."],"answer":"oikist","answerText":"oikist","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"An <b>oikist</b> is the leader of a Greek expedition sent out to found a settlement overseas, a part that fell to members of the elite whose motives lay in the antagonism of political life at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among his duties as founder was the introduction of religious and political institutions brought from the homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homer's Skherie is laid out by such a man, who divides the farmland and raises a monumental agora with fixed stone seating, a temple beside it, city walls and a water supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was invested with sacral authority by Delphi, and it was probably he who marked out the temenoi, the precincts of the gods, in the new place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sybarites told Herodotus that the Spartan Dorieus came to grief because he had not followed the instructions Delphi gave him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Miltiades was designated oikist of the Thracian Chersonese by the Pythia rather than by himself, and the Dolonkoi and the colonists who went with him recognised him before he ever sailed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was then chosen tyrannos by his fellow citizens, and his prerogatives passed intact to Stesagoras and to the younger Miltiades after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A founder was honoured after death with a cult of his own and buried where no ordinary Greek was buried, in the middle of the city, so that the tomb of Battus stood in the agora at Cyrene while the royal privileges his colonists had conceded him went on to his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A new settlement had no old monuments to hang a memory on, and a founder cult was one of the ways it made itself a past, and with that past a new social reality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Such a cult could keep alive what the man had actually been, since the honours paid to Miltiades recalled his autocratic rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Zwischen Ansässigkeit und Mobilität: die sogenannte Grosse Kolonisation der Griechen aus migrationstheoretischer Perspektive</i>, by Dominick Delp, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.08.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.08.18/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","Corinne Pache, review of <i>Ancient Greek Hero Cult</i>, ed. Robin Hägg, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.12.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.12.17/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why was a founder buried where no ordinary Greek was?","a":"Because he was honoured after death with a cult of his own, so the tomb of Battus stood in the agora at Cyrene rather than outside the walls."},{"q":"Why did a new settlement need such a cult?","a":"Because it had no old monuments to hang a memory on, and a founder cult was one of the ways it made itself a past, and with that past a new social reality."},{"q":"Why did the role carry religious authority?","a":"Because he was invested with it by Delphi and probably marked out the precincts of the gods himself — the Sybarites told Herodotus that Dorieus came to grief for not following his instructions."}]},
{"id":"gr-206","num":206,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The medicinal and culinary plant silphium became emblematic of the wealth and identity of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the largest and most prosperous Greek city in Libya.","questions":["Power at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay first with the Battiad monarchy, was restructured in the 6th century BCE by Demonax of Mantinea, and returned briefly to the dynasty with Persian support.","The beginnings of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew poets from Pindar to Callimachus, and Pindar's fourth Pythian, for king Arcesilas IV, ties them to the Argonauts' voyage to Lake Tritonis."],"answer":"Cyrene","answerText":"Cyrene","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cyrene</b> is the Greek city planted inland in Libya in the 7th century BCE, and on both the written and archaeological evidence it was the largest and most prosperous Greek city there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The part played by the island of Thera in the original foundation is attested by many writers over a long period and may be taken as historically true, but Thera was only a starting point, and other parts of the Greek world shared in the foundation or in the expansion that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aegean connections show in the ground from the start, with an unusual quantity of Cretan material beside the commoner East Greek and Rhodian wares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stood at the head of a group of Greek towns in Cyrenaica, among them Tauchira, Barca, Euesperides and the coastal Apollonia, about which the sources say almost nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its economy was tied to the exploitation of silphium, the medicinal and culinary plant emblematic of the city's wealth and identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Power lay at first with the Battiad monarchy, was restructured in the 6th century BCE by Demonax of Mantinea, and returned briefly to the dynasty with Persian support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No other Greek foundation is related in such detail by the ancient writers, chiefly Herodotus, though their accounts begin only in the 5th century BCE and see the whole region from this one city's side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He gives two versions of the departure, one told on Thera and one by the Cyreneans, independent of each other, running the same events in another order, and the Delphic oracle runs through his telling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He seems to have gone to Libya himself, but his informants came from elite families for whom the fall of the Battiads was a recent memory, and what they told him leans against the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The foundation drew poets from Pindar to Callimachus and Apollonius, beside them stands the inscription known as the Oath of the Founders, and recent excavation has worked on structures in and around the sanctuary of Apollo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World. Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 2</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]","Evangelia Anagnostou-Laoutides, review of <i>Myth and History in Ancient Greece: The Symbolic Creation of a Colony</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.11.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.11.34/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominguez, review of <i>Mythe et histoire dans l'Antiquité grecque: la création symbolique d'une colonie</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.02.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.02.25/. [Open access]","Arthur Thourson Jones, review of <i>For the Preservation of the Cultural Heritage in Libya</i>, ed. Savino di Lernia and Davide Zampetti, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.09.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.09.27/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Archaeological_Site_of_Cyrene-109025.jpg","title":"Cyrene","desc":"Archaeological Site of Cyrene (Libya). Giovanni Boccardi.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_Site_of_Cyrene-109025.jpg","alt":"Archaeological Site of Cyrene"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why do the ancient accounts lean against the Battiad dynasty?","a":"Because Herodotus's informants came from elite families for whom the fall of the Battiads was a recent memory."},{"q":"Why is Thera only a starting point in the foundation?","a":"Because other parts of the Greek world shared in the foundation or the expansion that followed, and the ground shows an unusual quantity of Cretan material beside East Greek and Rhodian wares."},{"q":"Why is so little known of the other Greek towns of Cyrenaica?","a":"Because the sources say almost nothing about them, seeing the whole region from this one city's side and beginning only in the 5th century BCE."}]},
{"id":"gr-207","num":207,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Cyreneans acknowledged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as founder and ruler, buried him in the agora, and let the privileges they had conceded him pass to his descendants.","questions":["Pindar's fourth Pythian gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an Argonaut ancestor named Euphemus and ties the founding of Cyrene to the voyage of the Argonauts to the Libyan lake Tritonis.","Callimachus, himself a Cyrenean, has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raise a temple of Apollo Carneius at Aziris, where Greeks first danced the Carneia with Libyan women."],"answer":"Battus","answerText":"Battus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded Cyrene</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Battus</b> is the Theran who led the settlers to Cyrene and was acknowledged by the Cyreneans as founder and ruler, holding both roles at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No other individual founder receives as much attention in the ancient literary sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus's account of the business reaches far back for its causes, and its focus moves from Sparta to Thera and then to Cyrene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The unflattering picture of him as a stuttering bastard comes from Cyrenean informants of Herodotus whose families remembered the overthrow of the dynasty, and whose telling leans against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His tomb stood in the agora, and the royal privileges the first colonists conceded him passed to his descendants, one of whom later used them to claim as much for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pindar's fourth Pythian, written for king Arcesilas IV, gives him an Argonaut ancestor named Euphemus and ties the founding to the voyage of the Argonauts to the Libyan lake Tritonis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A clod of earth handed over as a token of friendship is lost at sea and washed up on Thera, and comes home generations later when the Therans under his leadership settle in Libya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fifth Pythian sets the Theban Aegids and the Trojan Antenorids together at the site, so that the Theban and Trojan wars coincide and the distance between the fall of Troy and his own act closes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hero cult he was given at Cyrene is presented as matching the cults established for the great heroes of the Mycenaean past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Making him a founding hero was also a way of making Arcesilas one, and Callimachus, himself a Cyrenean, has him raise a temple of Apollo Carneius at Aziris, where Greeks first danced the Carneia with Libyan women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["B. M. Lavelle, review of <i>Tyranny and Political Culture in Ancient Greece</i>, by James F. McGlew, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.10.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.10.21/. [Open access]","Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World. Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","Evangelia Anagnostou-Laoutides, review of <i>Myth and History in Ancient Greece: The Symbolic Creation of a Colony</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.11.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.11.34/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominguez, review of <i>Mythe et histoire dans l'Antiquité grecque: la création symbolique d'une colonie</i>, by Claude Calame, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.02.25, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.02.25/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas, Volume 2</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.03.14, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.03.14/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the unflattering portrait of him suspect?","a":"Because it comes from Cyrenean informants of Herodotus whose families remembered the overthrow of the dynasty, and whose telling leans against it."},{"q":"Why does Pindar tie the founding to the Argonauts?","a":"Because it gives him an Argonaut ancestor and a clod of earth that comes home generations later, which makes the settlement the fulfilment of an older promise."},{"q":"Why did making him a founding hero serve a living king?","a":"Because the royal privileges the first colonists conceded him passed to his descendants, so honouring the founder was a way of making Arcesilas one."}]},
{"id":"gr-208","num":208,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The expedition that planted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Sicily around 733 BCE was led out from Corinth by Archias, a Bacchiad, according to Thucydides.","questions":["The nymph Arethusa of the island of Ortygia is prominent on the coins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE, beside votive deposits going back to the 7th.","Because the Peloponnesian river Alpheos was held to feed the spring on Ortygia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could claim a tie to Olympia and the wider Greek world."],"answer":"Syracuse","answerText":"Syracuse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 733 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Syracuse</b> is the Greek city Corinth planted in Sicily around 733 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides names Archias, a Bacchiad, as the man who led the expedition out from Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch's account of him makes violence at home the cause of the departure, which sits beside Thucydides' picture of early settlement as violent towards the people already on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sicily lies at the geographical centre of the Mediterranean and carried a dense network of Greek, Punic and native Sikel, Sikan and Elymian settlements, so that the island was synonymous with conflict and competition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Megara Hyblaea was planted by Megarians shortly after Syracuse itself, and in turn founded Selinus, which brought the Greeks to the edge of Carthage's sphere of influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The island of Ortygia carries the spring Arethusa, whose nymph is prominent on the city's coins of the late 6th and early 5th centuries, and votive deposits there go back to the 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Artemis is associated with the island, and her cult is at once uniquely Syracusan and a link back to Corinth, from which it was imported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Peloponnesian river Alpheos, which runs past Olympia, was held to be the source of that spring, tying the city to the wider Greek world; the association is carried by the foundation oracle Pausanias cites and hinted at earlier in Ibycus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Gelon refounded the city in the 5th century BCE he mingled new peoples and groups into it, and the mixing of Alpheos with Arethusa served ancient writers as the figure for what he had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides' account of the peopling and settlement of Sicily is written to look forward to the Athenian expedition, and his Syracuse comes to resemble Athens itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Parrish Elizabeth Wright, review of <i>Myth, Locality, and Identity in Pindar's Sicilian Odes</i>, by Virginia M. Lewis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.27/. [Open access]","Vivienne Gray, review of <i>A Commentary on Thucydides. Volume III: Books 5.25-8.109</i>, by Simon Hornblower, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.01.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.01.24/. [Open access]","Mark Edwards, review of <i>The Poetics of Colonization: From City to Text in Archaic Greece</i>, by Carol Dougherty, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.05.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.05.04/. [Open access]","Tim Sorg, review of <i>The Fight for Greek Sicily: Society, Politics, and Landscape</i>, ed. Melanie Jonasch, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.03.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.03.51/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/The_Greek_theatre_of_Syracuse%2C_Sicily.jpg/1920px-The_Greek_theatre_of_Syracuse%2C_Sicily.jpg","title":"Syracuse","desc":"The Greek theatre of Syracuse lies on the south slopes of the Temenite hill, overlooking the modern city of Syracuse in southeastern Sicily.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Greek_theatre_of_Syracuse,_Sicily.jpg","alt":"The Greek theatre of Syracuse, Sicily"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why does the spring on Ortygia tie the city to the Peloponnese?","a":"Because the river Alpheos, which runs past Olympia, was held to be its source, an association carried by the foundation oracle Pausanias cites and hinted at earlier in Ibycus."},{"q":"Why did that image serve writers describing Gelon's refoundation?","a":"Because he mingled new peoples and groups into the city, and the mixing of Alpheos with Arethusa was the figure for what he had done."},{"q":"Why does Thucydides' Syracuse come to resemble Athens?","a":"Because his account of the peopling and settlement of Sicily is written to look forward to the Athenian expedition."}]},
{"id":"gr-209","num":209,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The story of friendly help given the settlers by the native king Hyblon belongs to the founding of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, planted in eastern Sicily shortly after Syracuse.","questions":["Gelon destroyed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 483 or 482 BCE and absorbed its people, ending a quarrel with Syracuse that had begun as an alliance.","Eighth-century foundations keeping domestic and public ground apart show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a town plan from its earliest years, as does the early separation of its cemeteries."],"answer":"Megara Hyblaea","answerText":"Megara Hyblaea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 733 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">483 or 482 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Megara Hyblaea</b> is the Greek city planted in eastern Sicily by Megarians shortly after Syracuse, and it in turn sent out Selinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It arrived among the well-developed and widespread native Pantalica and Thapsos culture, and the story of friendly help given the settlers by the native king Hyblon belongs in that setting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had a town plan from its earliest years, shown by 8th-century foundations that keep domestic and public ground apart and by the early separation of the cemeteries from the settlement, the 61 hectares later enclosed by the 6th-century wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavation of the archaic town shows a planned and apparently egalitarian settlement in place around the last quarter of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the 7th to the 6th century religious and public building filled in the space set aside earlier for public use, which may have been more than half the site if the agora area is anything to go by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> House sizes and styles begin to differ in the 7th century, which points to an early start for social stratification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was governed by an oligarchy broad enough to stop any of its members setting up as tyrant, allied with neighbouring Syracuse at first and later its enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That quarrel ended the place: Gelon destroyed it in 483 or 482 BCE and absorbed its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the very few early colonial settlements that is well excavated and published, most of the others being known chiefly from their cemeteries, and if its urban development began at the end of the 8th century and was in part complete in the 7th, Greek urbanisation may have begun at what looks like the periphery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It stands with Syracuse, Naxos, Gela and others as evidence that the western Greeks worked out their town planning for themselves, dividing land into lots and attending to orientation, precincts and civic centres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis; More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Irad Malkin, review of <i>Difference</i>, ed. Sian Lewis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.11.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.11.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Monnaie_-_Argent%2C_Sicile%2C_Megara_Hyblaea_-_btv1b103174640_%282_of_2%29.jpg","title":"Megara Hyblaea","desc":"Appartient à l’ensemble documentaire : MonnGre Langue : grec ancien.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Argent,_Sicile,_Megara_Hyblaea_-_btv1b103174640_(2_of_2).jpg","alt":"Monnaie - Argent, Sicile, Megara Hyblaea"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is this site so valuable for studying early colonies?","a":"Because it is one of the very few that is well excavated and published, most of the others being known chiefly from their cemeteries."},{"q":"Why does it suggest Greek urbanisation began at the periphery?","a":"Because its urban development began at the end of the 8th century and was in part complete in the 7th, which is early against anything in the homeland."},{"q":"Why do the houses point to early social stratification?","a":"Because their sizes and styles begin to differ in the 7th century, in a settlement whose earliest plan looks egalitarian."}]},
{"id":"gr-210","num":210,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Crete and Rhodes planted the first of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 688 BCE, and the second of the pair has no named founders at all in the tradition.","questions":["The crab on the coins of the younger of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands for the river of the same name, which the odes written before Theron came to power already dwell on.","A Thesmophorion at Bitalemi served the elder of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the middle of the 7th century BCE until the Carthaginians destroyed that city in 405."],"answer":"Gela and Acragas","answerText":"Gela and Acragas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gela founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">688 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gela destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">405 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Gela and Acragas</b> are two Greek cities of Sicily, and what is known of how each began is very unequal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gela was planted by settlers from Crete and Rhodes in 688 BCE, some 45 years after Corinth founded Syracuse, and by the 680s the Sicilian colonies were sending out daughters of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the Sicilian sites carrying evidence for early town planning, Gela is the one studied in most detail, and it proves a preconceived urban structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The small sanctuary at Bitalemi just outside the city, probably a Thesmophorion, was founded in the middle of the 7th century BCE, some time after the city itself, and was rearranged in the mid-6th century in a way that closed off the earlier stratigraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sanctuary was mostly, though not completely, abandoned after the Carthaginians destroyed Gela in 405 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Acragas, by contrast, has no named founders at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That left Pindar free to put the ancestors of its tyrant Theron into those legendary roles, and to build the family a Panhellenic heroic genealogy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The crab on Akragantine coins stands for the river Akragas, which the odes written before Theron came to power already dwell on, so that the later emphasis tied the tyrant to a symbol the city already had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heraclea Minoa was established, perhaps between 580 and 570 BCE, as an attempt to check the expansion of Acragas under Phalaris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its temples are famous enough to distort the study of it, the splendour of the Valley of the Temples drawing attention from the ground between and around them, while less grand remains have been assigned, as so many in Sicily are, to Demeter and Persephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis; More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Vladimir Stissi, review of <i>Gela: il Thesmophorion di Bitalemi. La fase arcaica, scavi P. Orlandini 1963-1967</i>, ed. Marina Albertocchi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.01.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.01.05/. [Open access]","Dominic Dalglish, review of <i>The Akragas Dialogue: New Investigations on Sanctuaries in Sicily</i>, ed. Natascha Sojc and Monica de Cesare, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.09.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.09.32/. [Open access]","Parrish Elizabeth Wright, review of <i>Myth, Locality, and Identity in Pindar's Sicilian Odes</i>, by Virginia M. Lewis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.27/. [Open access]","David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why could Pindar invent a heroic ancestry for Acragas?","a":"Because the city has no named founders at all, which left those legendary roles free for the ancestors of its tyrant Theron."},{"q":"Why does the crab on its coins matter to that story?","a":"Because it stands for the river Akragas, which the odes written before Theron came to power already dwell on, so the later emphasis tied him to a symbol the city already had."},{"q":"Why do the famous temples distort the study of the city?","a":"Because the splendour of the Valley of the Temples draws attention from the ground between and around them."}]},
{"id":"gr-211","num":211,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Planted in western Sicily by Megara Hyblaea in the second half of the 7th century, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought the Greeks up to the edge of Carthage's sphere.","questions":["Between the late 7th century and 460 BCE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built no fewer than seven temples of more than 112,000 tons of stone, one of the greatest building programmes of the ancient world.","Hemmed in by Phoenicians to the west, Greeks to the east and Elymians to the north, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a position that governed its foreign policy and its internal development alike."],"answer":"Selinus","answerText":"Selinus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temples built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 7th century – 460 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Selinus</b> is the Greek city planted in western Sicily by Megara Hyblaea in the second half of the 7th century BCE, and it brought the Greeks up to the edge of Carthage's sphere of influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It found itself among the complex and dynamic native Sant'Angelo Muxaro and Polizzello, Punic and Elymian cultures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within decades of settlement its centre covered all the ground it would still occupy in the 5th century, some 100 hectares bounded by cemeteries and by the lie of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its town plan had certainly taken shape by 580 to 570 BCE and may go back in part to the 7th century, when the sanctuary of Demeter Malophoros was first laid out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is among the Sicilian sites that give conclusive evidence for early town planning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Between the late 7th century and 460 BCE it carried through one of the greatest building programmes of the ancient world, no fewer than seven temples of more than 112,000 tons of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The metopes of Temples Y and C are read not as sculpture standing on its own but by where they sat within the building, the sanctuary and the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beside the Malophoros sanctuary lay an independent precinct of Zeus Meilichios, given over largely to unofficial, family cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city was hemmed in by Phoenicians to the west, Greeks to the east and Elymians and other native peoples to the north, and that position governed its foreign policy and its internal development alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its wealth and its position together brought the tyrant Theron to power in the mid-6th century, after which nothing more is heard of fighting with its non-Greek neighbours until the 5th, which suggests that its later pro-Punic stance had roots this early.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["David Smith, review of <i>Megara Hyblaia and Selinous: The Development of Two Greek City-States in Archaic Sicily</i>, by Franco De Angelis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.28/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis; More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Lela M. Urquhart, review of <i>Zeus Meilichios a Selinunte</i>, by Cristoforo Grotta, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.12.49, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.12.49/. [Open access]","Barbara A. Barletta, review of <i>Temple Decoration and Cultural Identity in the Archaic Greek World: The Metopes of Selinus</i>, by Clemente Marconi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.11.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.11.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Selinunte_-_Templi_Orientali_%28Temple_E%29_18.JPG/1920px-Selinunte_-_Templi_Orientali_%28Temple_E%29_18.JPG","title":"Selinus","desc":"Temple E - Selinunte, Italy.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selinunte_-_Templi_Orientali_(Temple_E)_18.JPG","alt":"Selinunte - Templi Orientali (Temple E) 18"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the city's position the key to its history?","a":"Because it was hemmed in by Phoenicians to the west, Greeks to the east and Elymians to the north, and that governed its foreign policy and its internal development alike."},{"q":"Why are its metopes not read as sculpture standing alone?","a":"Because they are read by where they sat within the building, the sanctuary and the city."},{"q":"Why is its later pro-Punic stance thought to have early roots?","a":"Because after Theron came to power in the mid-6th century nothing more is heard of fighting with its non-Greek neighbours until the 5th."}]},
{"id":"gr-212","num":212,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An expedition from Achaia planted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in southern Italy towards the end of the 8th century BCE, and it later founded Metapontion and Poseidonia in its turn.","questions":["Croton took Herakles for an ancestor and then took over the cult of Philoktetes, in an expansion that finished with the destruction of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and left an enormous ruined site.","Houses of the final phase at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are rectangular, one large room and two small ones, and were found stripped bare, as though emptied by people being forced out."],"answer":"Sybaris","answerText":"Sybaris","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 725 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">444 – 443 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sybaris</b> is the Greek city planted in southern Italy by an expedition from Achaia, south of the gulf of Corinth, towards the end of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country around it, the Sybaritide, was already settled, and its people were part of a widening network by the late 8th century as Greek settlements went up across southern Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It sent out daughters of its own, founding Metapontion about 630 BCE with help from Corinth and Poseidonia a generation later without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Siris lost its independence about 570 BCE and was drawn into its orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It shared with Croton, Metapontion and Poseidonia a set of conspicuous extra-urban sanctuaries, each with a temple of Hera standing in the chora, which may have been read as marks of a common Achaean identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its end came from Croton, whose expansion, carried out under the cult of a hero taken over from the peoples in between, finished with a victory in 510 BCE and the destruction of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Enough of the city has survived that destruction to show it covered an enormous area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Houses of the final phase are rectangular, one large room and two small ones, and were found stripped of everything, as though those living in them had emptied them on being forced out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site was settled again as Thurii, a Panhellenic foundation of 444 to 443 BCE that is as good a marker as any for the end of the colonising movement in Magna Graecia and Sicily, on a territory smaller than the one Sybaris had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among the gold Orphic tablets, three from Thurii stand apart in showing the dead moving easily through the underworld before being granted a solution to their problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis, review of <i>Conceptualising Early Colonisation</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.43/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","William M. Balco, review of <i>Contexts of Early Colonization</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.42/. [Open access]","Kirsten Bedigan, review of <i>Mystic Cults in Magna Graecia</i>, ed. Giovanni Casadio and Patricia A. Johnston, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.04.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.04.13/. [Open access]","Simon Trépanier, review of <i>Plato and Pythagoreanism</i>, by Phillip Sidney Horky, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.05.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.05.18/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg/1920px-Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg","title":"Sybaris","desc":"The excavated ruins on the site of Sybaris in southern Italy, walls and streets laid bare on the coastal plain.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg","alt":"Sibari-scavi-teatro"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why do its houses look emptied rather than sacked?","a":"Because those of the final phase were found stripped of everything, as though the people living in them had cleared them on being forced out."},{"q":"Why might the extra-urban Hera temples mark a shared identity?","a":"Because this city shared them with Croton, Metapontion and Poseidonia, each with a temple standing in its chora, which may have been read as marks of a common Achaean descent."},{"q":"Why is the refoundation as Thurii a useful marker?","a":"Because that Panhellenic foundation of 444 to 443 BCE is as good a marker as any for the end of the colonising movement in Magna Graecia and Sicily."}]},
{"id":"gr-213","num":213,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An expedition from Achaia planted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in southern Italy towards the end of the 8th century BCE, and its great sanctuary stood on Cape Lacinium to the south.","questions":["Pythagoras of Samos emigrated to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and founded there a puritanical way of life built on the doctrine of reincarnation, being a charismatic guru rather than a philosopher.","Taking Herakles for an ancestor and then the cult of Philoktetes, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> expanded until it destroyed Sybaris, over which it won a victory in 510 BCE."],"answer":"Croton","answerText":"Croton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 725 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sybaris defeated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Croton</b> is one of the two cities that settlers from Achaia founded in southern Italy at the close of the 8th century BCE, and it was to destroy the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its great sanctuary, the Heraion on Cape Lacinium to the south of the city, has been set beside the Heraion on Samos, and in a colonial city such a cult stands outside the walls, turning the field of competition away from the citizens and towards the indigenous peoples around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It claimed an antiquity of its own by taking Herakles for an ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It then took over the cult of Philoktetes, a hero who had belonged to the non-Greek communities lying between it and Sybaris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That was an expansionary move, and it ended in the destruction of Sybaris, over which the city won its victory in 510 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pythagoras of Samos, who lived from about 570 to 490 BCE, emigrated to the city and founded there a puritanical way of life built on the doctrine of reincarnation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He appears to have been a charismatic guru rather than a philosopher, and still less a mathematician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the victory over Sybaris the city seems to have held a dominant position in the region under a community led by him, and Pythagorean membership seems to have been extended to the leaders of neighbouring states as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Philolaus of Croton, who lived from about 470 to 400 BCE, is one of only three mathematical Pythagoreans who can be made out at all through the mists of the period before Plato, the others being Archytas of Tarentum and Hippasus of Metapontum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city also produced the athlete Milo, who for all his fame never received worship and so never attained the standing of a hero, where Theagenes of Thasos did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","Simon Trépanier, review of <i>Plato and Pythagoreanism</i>, by Phillip Sidney Horky, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.05.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.05.18/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Placing the Gods: Sanctuaries and Sacred Space in Ancient Greece</i>, ed. Susan E. Alcock and Robin Osborne, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.06.01/. [Open access]","William Morison, review of <i>Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Stephen G. Miller, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.12.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.12.10/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Capo_Colonna_-_Colonna.jpg/1920px-Capo_Colonna_-_Colonna.jpg","title":"Croton","desc":"Capo Colonna (Crotone, Calabria, Italy) - The column remaining from the temple of Hera Lacinia.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capo_Colonna_-_Colonna.jpg","alt":"Capo Colonna - Colonna"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the city take over a hero who was not Greek?","a":"Because Philoktetes had belonged to the non-Greek communities lying between it and Sybaris, so adopting his cult was an expansionary move."},{"q":"Why does a colonial city put its great sanctuary outside the walls?","a":"Because that turns the field of competition away from the citizens and towards the indigenous peoples around them."},{"q":"Why did a famous athlete not become a hero?","a":"Because Milo for all his fame never received worship, where Theagenes of Thasos did, and worship is what the standing required."}]},
{"id":"gr-214","num":214,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The traditions about the founding of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are tied to the Messenian war and turn on the Partheniai, surviving in variant forms both there and in Sparta.","questions":["Archytas presided over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the mid-380s to the mid-350s BCE, elected general seven times, and under him the city led the Italian League.","Hostilities with Rome broke out in 282 and 281 BCE and ended with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seeking military help from Pyrrhus of Epirus against them."],"answer":"Taras","answerText":"Taras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archytas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 385 – 355 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War with Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">282 – 281 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Taras</b> is the Greek city of southern Italy founded from Sparta, and the traditions of its foundation are tied to the Messenian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They turn on the Partheniai, and they survive in variant forms at both ends, in the colony and in Sparta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those traditions are initiatory in nature, and yet they also corroborate the 8th-century archaeological evidence for the foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legends of the Partheniai have been read as showing that anything resembling colonisation beyond Lakonia, the honours given to Brasidas among such things, could look like a threat to the patrilineal rights of the Spartan royal houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own expansion was felt by its neighbours, and Metapontion is said to have taken Nestor for a founder in the face of the Spartan city pushing towards Sybaris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Archytas presided over the city from the mid-380s to the mid-350s BCE in what seems to have been a form of democracy, being said to have been elected general seven times, though not necessarily one year after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was a most successful general, and under him the city led the Italian League, though for all that precious little is really known about him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He gave considerable thought to political harmony between rich and poor, balancing the privileges of wealth against the duties of the well-off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hostilities with Rome broke out in 282 and 281 BCE, and they ended with the city seeking military help from Pyrrhus of Epirus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ancient accounts of that outbreak turn on odd episodes and flamboyant individuals: a demagogue who talked the citizens into attacking a Roman fleet passing by, a drunk who fouled a Roman envoy's toga before the assembled citizenry, and a man who warned them in vain against inviting Pyrrhus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Massimo Nafissi and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08/. [Open access]","Timothy Doran, review of <i>Myth, Text, and History at Sparta</i>, ed. Thomas J. Figueira, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.16/. [Open access]","Erwin Cook, review of <i>The Returns of Odysseus: Colonization and Ethnicity</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.22/. [Open access]","John Dillon, review of <i>Archytas of Tarentum: Pythagorean, Philosopher and Mathematician King</i>, by Carl A. Huffman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.34/. [Open access]","Michael Fronda, review of <i>Images and Insults: Ancient Historiography and the Outbreak of the Tarentine War</i>, by Christopher L. H. Barnes, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.04.37/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Gioielli_dall%27antica_Taras_%28Taranto%29.jpg/1920px-Gioielli_dall%27antica_Taras_%28Taranto%29.jpg","title":"Taras","desc":"Tre orecchini d'oro, una collana ed un anello provenienti dall'antica Taras (Taranto). IV-III secolo a.C.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gioielli_dall%27antica_Taras_(Taranto).jpg","alt":"Gioielli dall'antica Taras (Taranto)"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why do the foundation traditions survive in variant forms?","a":"Because they were told at both ends, in the colony and in Sparta itself, and are initiatory in nature — while still corroborating the 8th-century archaeological evidence."},{"q":"Why could colonising look like a threat at Sparta?","a":"Because anything of the kind beyond Lakonia, the honours given to Brasidas among such things, could look like a threat to the patrilineal rights of the royal houses."},{"q":"Why are the ancient accounts of the war with Rome unsatisfying?","a":"Because they turn on odd episodes and flamboyant individuals — a demagogue, a drunk who fouled an envoy's toga, a man who warned against Pyrrhus in vain."}]},
{"id":"gr-215","num":215,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Archaeology puts <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first Greek colony on the Italian mainland, no later than 730 to 720 BCE, with an earlier date of about 750 not disproved.","questions":["A long gallery cut into the rock at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has for many years been shown as the Sibyl’s grotto, though as it now looks it began as a covered way for troops.","Statuettes from the votive deposit on the acropolis at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were every one found without its head, which probably means the heads were taken off as part of the rite."],"answer":"Cumae","answerText":"Cumae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">421 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cumae</b> is the first Greek colony on the Italian mainland, and the archaeological evidence puts it no later than 730 to 720 BCE, with an earlier date of about 750 not disproved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was founded a little after Pithekoussai, the island settlement off the Bay of Naples whose people went on to make it, and after Cumae came a flood of colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation here has turned up Greek burial ritual and Greek sherds in a possible proto-urban setting as early as the earliest material from the island, which blurs the order of the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archival records of the old digs at both places are being gone through again, since they bear on the Early Iron Age chronology of the whole Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city is where the Cumaean Sibyl gave her prophecies, and a long gallery cut into the rock has for many years been shown as her grotto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The gallery as it now looks is dated to the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, when it began as a covered way for troops climbing to the upper defences of the acropolis, its floor being lowered only later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A votive deposit from the lower terrace of that acropolis held terracotta feet, hands, heads, masks, statuettes, body parts and small altars, with a relief plaque and a few bronzes among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Statuettes make up about half of what survives and body parts the rest, among them forty feet, four hands, 58 pieces of finger and four male genitalia, most of them made in a mould.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every one of the statuettes was found without its head, which probably means the heads were taken off as part of the rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The native Campanians took the city in 421 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Jean Turfa, review of <i>The First Western Greeks</i>, by David Ridgway, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1994.02.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1994/1994.02.19/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Ancient Greeks West and East</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.03.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.03.20/. [Open access]","Ingrid Edlund-Berry, review of <i>Il deposito dall'Acropoli di Cuma</i>, by Marina Catucci and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.09.29, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.09.29/. [Open access]","William M. Balco, review of <i>Contexts of Early Colonization</i>, ed. Lieve Donnellan, Valentino Nizzo and Gert-Jan Burgers, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.05.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.05.42/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Charles-Louis_Cl%C3%A9risseau%2C_Ancient_Gate_at_Cumae_near_Naples%2C_1769%2C_private_collection.jpg","title":"Cumae","desc":"Charles-Louis Clérisseau, Ancient Gate at Cumae near Naples, 1769, private collection. Chalres-Louis Clérisseau. 1769.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles-Louis_Cl%C3%A9risseau,_Ancient_Gate_at_Cumae_near_Naples,_1769,_private_collection.jpg","alt":"Charles-Louis Clérisseau, Ancient Gate at Cumae near Naples, 1769, private collection"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the order of the two earliest western settlements blurred?","a":"Because excavation here has turned up Greek burial ritual and Greek sherds in a possible proto-urban setting as early as the earliest material from Pithekoussai."},{"q":"Why is the Sibyl's grotto not what it is shown as?","a":"Because the gallery as it now looks is dated to the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, beginning as a covered way for troops climbing to the upper defences."},{"q":"Why are the headless statuettes read as part of a rite?","a":"Because every one of them was found without its head, which probably means the heads were taken off deliberately rather than lost."}]},
{"id":"wh-004","num":4,"category":"Human origins","question":"Traced through fossil bones, stone tools and lately ancient DNA, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the long process by which people descended from apelike ancestors, beginning in Africa millions of years ago.","questions":["Upright walking came first and big brains much later in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why the earliest members of our lineage stood on two legs with brains no bigger than an ape's.","Often drawn as a single file marching towards a modern man, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better pictured as a bush, with 15 to 20 early human species known and several overlapping in time."],"answer":"human evolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ape split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8 – 6 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Our species</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 300,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"human evolution","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Human evolution</b> is the long process of change by which people descended from apelike ancestors, and the science that reconstructs that process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not mean that people descended from any living ape: humans and the great apes of Africa share a common ancestor that lived between about 8 and 6 million years ago, and the two lines have gone their own ways ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of what followed happened in Africa, which is where the earliest members of the human line are found and where our own species later appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The evidence is of three kinds: fossil bones and teeth, the stone tools their makers left in ground that can be dated, and, for the most recent stretch, DNA recovered from ancient bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its central lesson is that the traits we think of as human did not arrive together as a package but were assembled piecemeal, over millions of years and in no obvious order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Walking upright came first, and by about 4 million years ago Australopithecus was doing it habitually.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its brain nevertheless stayed small, usually under 500 cubic centimetres, roughly a third the size of a modern one, so an upright body cannot have been a consequence of a clever mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Flaked stone tools follow much later, the systematic making of sharp edges being known from about 2.6 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Brains then enlarged, a rise that ran within species rather than in jumps between them and that quickened towards the present, reaching an average near 1,300 cubic centimetres in Homo sapiens, which appears in Africa around 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nothing about this was a march in single file: some 15 to 20 early human species are recognised, several were alive at the same time, and the last of them interbred with ours before dying out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Walking Upright,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/human-characteristics/walking. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Thomas A. Püschel et al., \"Hominin Brain Size Increase Has Emerged from within-Species Encephalization,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 49 (2024): e2409542121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11626186/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-sapiens. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., \"A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,\" <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David R. Braun et al., \"Earliest Known Oldowan Artifacts at >2.58 Ma from Ledi-Geraru, Ethiopia, Highlight Early Technological Diversity,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 24 (2019): 11712–17, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6575601/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","evolution","palaeontology","prehistory","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Fossil_hominids.jpg/1920px-Fossil_hominids.jpg","title":"human evolution","desc":"Fossil Hominid Skull Display at The Museum of Osteology. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fossil_hominids.jpg","alt":"Fossil hominids"},"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does human evolution not mean that people descended from any living ape?","a":"Because humans and the great apes of Africa share a common ancestor that lived between about 8 and 6 million years ago, and the two lines have gone their own ways ever since."},{"q":"Why can an upright body not have been a consequence of a clever mind?","a":"Because walking upright came first. By about 4 million years ago Australopithecus was doing it habitually, while its brain stayed under 500 cubic centimetres — roughly a third the size of a modern one."},{"q":"Why is human evolution not a march in single file?","a":"Because some 15 to 20 early human species are recognised, several were alive at the same time, and the last of them interbred with ours before dying out."}]},
{"id":"wh-005","num":5,"category":"Human origins","question":"Marked out above all by habitual upright walking, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is any member of the human branch of the family tree, a group taking in Homo, Australopithecus, Paranthropus and Ardipithecus.","questions":["Older books call the same creatures hominids, but once the great apes were gathered into one family that word had to widen, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took over its old meaning.","Brain size is no guide to whether a fossil is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: the oldest candidates carry braincases no larger than a chimpanzee's, and the edge of the group is argued over."],"answer":"hominin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7 Mya – present</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest claimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sahelanthropus, c. 7 – 6 Mya</span></div>","answerText":"hominin","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>hominin</b> is any member of Hominini, the branch of the family tree holding modern humans, the extinct human species and our immediate ancestors — Homo, Australopithecus, Paranthropus and Ardipithecus among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hominini is a tribe, a rank between subfamily and genus, which is why the name ends in -ini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word is recent in this sense, and it exists because molecular work rearranged the apes: humans, chimpanzees and gorillas turned out to be more closely related to one another than any of them is to the orangutan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That gathered the great apes into a single family, Hominidae, so 'hominid' — which had meant roughly what 'hominin' means now — widened to take in chimpanzees, gorillas and orangutans as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A narrower word was then needed for the human branch alone, which is the work 'hominin' does; older books using 'hominid' of the same creatures are out of date rather than wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What marks a hominin out is not the brain but the way it moves: habitual upright walking as the chief means of getting about, with teeth of essentially human form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest candidates bear this out, carrying braincases no larger than a chimpanzee's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oldest of them is Sahelanthropus tchadensis from Chad, dated between 7 and 6 million years ago, whose foramen magnum, the opening where the spinal cord leaves the skull, sits further forward than in any primate but humans — the mark a head balanced on an upright spine leaves behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where the edge of the group falls is a question of classification rather than of fact, and it moves: the accepted genera went from three to seven in the years to 2003, and one review left Sahelanthropus <i>incertae sedis</i>, of uncertain position.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 15 to 20 early human species are recognised in all,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and several were alive at once: around 1.9 million years ago Homo erectus shared East Africa with Homo habilis, Homo rudolfensis and Paranthropus boisei, sometimes at the same sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Australian Museum, \"Hominid and Hominin — What’s the Difference?,\" last updated 2 December 2020, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/hominid-and-hominin-whats-the-difference/. [Open access]","Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, \"Genera of the Human Lineage,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]","Morris Goodman and Kirstin N. Sterner, \"Phylogenomic Evidence of Adaptive Evolution in the Ancestry of Humans,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, suppl. 2 (2010): 8918–23, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3024020/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/sahelanthropus-tchadensis. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Evidence_as_to_Man%27s_Place_in_Naturep2.jpg","title":"hominin","desc":"SKELETONS OF THE Gibbon. Orang. Chimpanzee. Gorilla. Man. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evidence_as_to_Man%27s_Place_in_Naturep2.jpg","alt":"Evidence as to Man's Place in Naturep2"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why was a new word needed when hominid already existed?","a":"Because molecular work showed that humans, chimpanzees and gorillas are more closely related to one another than any of them is to the orangutan. That gathered the great apes into one family, so hominid widened to take them in and the human branch alone needed a narrower word."},{"q":"Why does the name Hominini end in -ini?","a":"Because Hominini is a tribe, a rank between subfamily and genus, and -ini is the ending that rank carries. Hominidae, one rank above it, is the family — which since the rearrangement holds the chimpanzees, gorillas and orangutans as well."},{"q":"Why can a creature with a chimpanzee-sized braincase still be a hominin?","a":"Because what marks a hominin out is not the brain but the way it moves: habitual upright walking as the chief means of getting about, with teeth of essentially human form. The earliest candidates bear this out, carrying braincases no larger than a chimpanzee's."}]},
{"id":"wh-006","num":6,"category":"Human origins","question":"Known from a crushed cranium nicknamed Toumaï, recovered in the Djurab Desert of northern Chad, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest creature yet proposed as a member of the human branch.","questions":["At roughly 7 million years old, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits close to the parting of the human and chimpanzee lines, which is why the argument over whether it walked upright matters so much.","A forward-set foramen magnum and small canines put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the human side for some, while its chimp-sized brain and chimp-like limb bones leave others unconvinced."],"answer":"Sahelanthropus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7.2 – 6.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Toros-Menalla, Chad</span></div>","answerText":"Sahelanthropus","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sahelanthropus</b> tchadensis is the oldest creature yet proposed as a member of the human branch, known from fossils recovered in the Djurab Desert of northern Chad and named in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best of them is a nearly complete but badly crushed cranium nicknamed Toumaï, 'hope of life' in the local language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cosmogenic dating of the beds that held it brackets the animal between 6.8 and 7.2 million years old, with a weighted mean near 7.04 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That sits close to the time the human and chimpanzee lines are thought to have parted, which is what makes the fossil so valuable and so fiercely argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its brain was small, roughly 320 to 380 cubic centimetres, much like a chimpanzee's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What first suggested it belonged on the human side was the skull: the foramen magnum, where the spinal cord leaves the braincase, sits further forward than in apes or any other primate but humans, which is where a head balanced on an upright spine would put it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The teeth pointed the same way, the canines and incisors being relatively small, though a heavy brow ridge sets the face apart from anything among the australopithecines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A thigh bone and two forearm bones from the same locality were later brought into the argument, and they cut both ways: in size and shape both limb bones sit closest to chimpanzees, while features of the femur have been read as the hip and knee of a habitual walker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The literature is accordingly polarised, one group finding enough here for the earliest hominin and another dismissing that evidence and doubting the animal belongs on our branch at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Formal classifications have hedged to match, one review leaving the genus <i>incertae sedis</i>, of uncertain position, so whether this is an ancestor, a cousin or an ape of some other line is still open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/sahelanthropus-tchadensis. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/sahelanthropus-tchadensis/. [Open access]","Anne-Elisabeth Lebatard et al., \"Cosmogenic Nuclide Dating of <i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i> and <i>Australopithecus bahrelghazali</i>: Mio-Pliocene Hominids from Chad,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 9 (2008): 3226–31, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265126/. [Open access]","Scott A. Williams et al., \"Earliest Evidence of Hominin Bipedalism in <i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 1 (2026): eadv0130, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12758524/. [Open access]","Camilo J. Cela-Conde and Francisco J. Ayala, \"Genera of the Human Lineage,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 100, no. 13 (2003): 7684–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC164648/. [Open access]"],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Sahelanthropus_tchadensis_-_TM_266_location.jpg","title":"Sahelanthropus","desc":"A map of Chad marking TM 266, where the first Sahelanthropus tchadensis was found, with the inferred maximum extent of Holocene Lake Mega Chad outlined. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sahelanthropus_tchadensis_-_TM_266_location.jpg","alt":"Sahelanthropus tchadensis - TM 266 location"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is Sahelanthropus both so valuable and so fiercely argued over?","a":"Because cosmogenic dating of the beds that held it brackets the animal between 6.8 and 7.2 million years old, with a weighted mean near 7.04 million — close to the time the human and chimpanzee lines are thought to have parted."},{"q":"Why did the skull first suggest that Sahelanthropus belonged on the human side?","a":"Because the foramen magnum, where the spinal cord leaves the braincase, sits further forward than in apes or any other primate but humans, which is where a head balanced on an upright spine would put it. The small canines and incisors pointed the same way."},{"q":"Why do the limb bones cut both ways in the argument?","a":"Because in size and shape the thigh bone and the two forearm bones sit closest to chimpanzees, while features of the femur have been read as the hip and knee of a habitual walker."}]},
{"id":"wh-007","num":7,"category":"Human origins","question":"Older than stone tools, large brains or any other trait of our branch, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is habitual movement on two legs, and the change that rebuilt the skeleton from skull to foot.","questions":["Three individuals crossed wet volcanic ash at Laetoli 3.6 million years ago, leaving in about 70 prints the earliest direct evidence of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> anywhere in the record.","Walking costs a human roughly 75 per cent less energy than moving about costs a chimpanzee, which is the leading explanation for why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was worth evolving at all."],"answer":"bipedalism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Evident from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Footprints</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3.6 Mya, Laetoli</span></div>","answerText":"bipedalism","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Bipedalism</b> is habitual movement on two legs, and in its human form it means not merely standing upright but walking as the only way of getting about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the oldest of the traits that mark the human branch, evolving over 4 million years ago, long before large brains, stone tools or anything else distinctively human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reaching it rebuilt the skeleton from head to foot. The opening where the spinal cord leaves the skull moved forward, so that the head balances on the spine rather than hanging from it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the lower back gained the forward curve that absorbs the shock of each step, a curve found in no other animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hip bones broadened, the thigh bones lengthened, and the knee grew strong enough to carry the whole body on one leg at a time — the arrangement Homo erectus had by 1.9 million years ago, climbing having been given up for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest direct evidence is not a bone but a set of footprints: at Laetoli in Tanzania three individuals crossed wet volcanic ash 3.6 million years ago, leaving about 70 prints along nearly 27 metres (89 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their big toes lie in line with the rest of the foot, and the tracks record a heel strike followed by a push off the toes — how a modern human walks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Why it began is less settled than that it did, and the leading answer is economy: human walking costs about 75 per cent less than either walking or knuckle-walking costs a chimpanzee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That saving is not automatic, since in living chimpanzees the two gaits cost much the same; what matters is that individuals with longer hind limbs and a straighter hip were cheaper on two legs, which is where selection could take hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Other advantages have been proposed and none excludes the rest: an upright animal has its hands free for food, tools or infants, reaches fruit on low branches, and looks larger to whatever is watching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Walking Upright,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/human-characteristics/walking. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Laetoli Footprint Trails,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/laetoli-footprint-trails. [Open access]","Michael D. Sockol, David A. Raichlen, and Herman Pontzer, \"Chimpanzee Locomotor Energetics and the Origin of Human Bipedalism,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 30 (2007): 12265–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1941460/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/sahelanthropus-tchadensis. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","evolution","palaeontology","prehistory","anatomy"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does bipedalism show that large brains were not what made us human?","a":"Because it is the oldest of the traits marking the human branch, evolving over 4 million years ago — long before large brains, stone tools or anything else distinctively human."},{"q":"Why do the Laetoli footprints count as evidence of a human kind of walk?","a":"Because the big toes lie in line with the rest of the foot, and the tracks record a heel strike followed by a push off the toes, which is how a modern human walks. Three individuals left about 70 prints across wet volcanic ash 3.6 million years ago."},{"q":"Why is economy only part of the answer to why two legs won out?","a":"Because the saving is not automatic: in living chimpanzees walking and knuckle-walking cost much the same. What mattered is that individuals with longer hind limbs and a straighter hip were cheaper on two legs, which is where selection could take hold."}]},
{"id":"wh-008","num":8,"category":"Human origins","question":"Named from the Afar word for 'ground' joined to the Greek for 'ape', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a genus of early Ethiopian hominins whose best-known member is the skeleton called Ardi.","questions":["The pelvis of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> combines climbing with upright walking while the big toe still splays out to grasp, which is why it is called a mosaic rather than a halfway house.","Small unhoned canines and a foot that both grasps and pushes off make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the fossil behind the argument that our line never descended from a knuckle-walking ape."],"answer":"Ardipithecus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5.8 – 4.4 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Middle Awash, Ethiopia</span></div>","answerText":"Ardipithecus","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ardipithecus</b> is a genus of early hominins from Ethiopia, its name joining the Afar word for 'ground' or 'floor' to the Greek for 'ape'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The older of its two species, Ardipithecus kadabba, comes from the Middle Awash and lived between about 5.8 and 5.2 million years ago, known from a modest set of teeth, jaws and a few bones of the hand, foot and arm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The younger and far better known is Ardipithecus ramidus, at about 4.4 million years ago, first reported in 1994 and now represented by over 100 specimens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among them is the partial female skeleton nicknamed Ardi, announced in 2009; the remains of well over 100 further individuals have since confirmed that her anatomy is typical of the species rather than peculiar to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her brain was small, roughly 300 to 350 cubic centimetres, about the size of a female chimpanzee's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the genus matter is that it is a mosaic rather than a halfway house: the pelvis carries features for climbing alongside features for walking upright, while the big toe still splays out to grasp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The teeth say the same thing from the other end, the canines being less projecting and smaller than in any other known ape, with no sign of the honing that sharpens an ape's upper canine against a lower premolar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earlier species carries the signal in miniature, a single toe bone from 5.2 million years ago having been read as showing the push-off of an upright stride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From all this its discoverers argue that the ancestor we share with chimpanzees was no chimpanzee but a generalised ape, so that our line never descended from a knuckle-walker — the claim the genus is famous for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is disputed: a re-examination of the foot reads it instead as evidence that what preceded hominin walking was ordinary African-ape quadrupedalism and climbing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/ardipithecus-ramidus. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Ardipithecus kadabba</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/ardipithecus-kadabba. [Open access]","Tim D. White et al., \"Neither Chimpanzee nor Human, <i>Ardipithecus</i> Reveals the Surprising Ancestry of Both,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 16 (2015): 4877–84, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4413341/. [Open access]","Thomas Cody Prang, \"The African Ape-like Foot of <i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i> and Its Implications for the Origin of Bipedalism,\" <i>eLife</i> 8 (2019): e44433, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6491036/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Ardipithecus ramidus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/ardipithecus-ramidus/. [Open access]"],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Ardipithecus_%28finger_bones%29.jpg/1920px-Ardipithecus_%28finger_bones%29.jpg","title":"Ardipithecus","desc":"Finger bones of \"Ardi\" (casts). Gerbil, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardipithecus_(finger_bones).jpg","alt":"Ardipithecus (finger bones)"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is Ardipithecus called a mosaic rather than a halfway house?","a":"Because its features are not blended but combined whole: the pelvis carries traits for climbing alongside traits for walking upright, while the big toe still splays out to grasp. It is two working anatomies in one animal, not one anatomy part-way to another."},{"q":"Why do the teeth support putting Ardipithecus on the human branch?","a":"Because the canines are less projecting and smaller than in any other known ape, with no sign of the honing that sharpens an ape's upper canine against a lower premolar."},{"q":"Why is the claim that our line never descended from a knuckle-walker disputed?","a":"Because it rests on reading Ardi's anatomy as a generalised ape rather than a chimpanzee-like one, and a re-examination of the foot reads it instead as evidence that ordinary African-ape quadrupedalism and climbing preceded hominin walking."}]},
{"id":"wh-010","num":10,"category":"Human origins","question":"Catalogued as AL 288-1 and found at Hadar in Ethiopia in 1974, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the partial skeleton that showed upright walking had come long before any growth of the brain.","questions":["Several hundred fragments make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the most complete early hominin skeletons known, enough of the pelvis, leg and spine surviving to say how she moved.","Also called Dinkinesh, Amharic for 'you are marvellous', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood about 1.05 metres tall and carried a walker's hips beneath a braincase the size of a chimpanzee's."],"answer":"Lucy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3.2 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Hadar, Ethiopia</span></div>","answerText":"Lucy","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lucy</b> is a partial skeleton of Australopithecus afarensis, catalogued as AL 288-1 and found in 1974 at Hadar in the Afar region of Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several hundred fragments survive, making her one of the most complete early hominin skeletons known and leaving enough of the pelvis, leg and spine to say how she moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She stood about 1.05 metres (3 ft 5 in) tall and lived some 3.2 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her species ranged across eastern Africa between about 3.85 and 2.95 million years ago, lived mainly on plants, and may be ancestral to later Australopithecus, Paranthropus and Homo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was nicknamed after a Beatles song playing in camp the night she was found, and is also known as Dinkinesh, Amharic for 'you are marvellous'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What made her decisive is the combination she preserves: a short broad pelvis and thigh bones angled inward to bring the knees under the body, which is the arrangement of a habitual walker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her braincase was nevertheless small, her species averaging about 446 cubic centimetres, close to a chimpanzee's and roughly a third of a modern human's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That pairing is the whole significance of her, since walking upright evolved over 4 million years ago while large and complex brains came much later, and here both facts sit in one skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> She had not left the trees, either: her species kept long strong arms and curved fingers throughout, so the animal to picture is at home on the ground and above it alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Her own kind left the direct proof a few hundred thousand years before her, in the footprint trails at Laetoli in Tanzania, which are attributed to A. afarensis on the strength of bones from the same beds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"AL 288-1 ('Lucy'),\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/al-288-1. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Ashleigh L. A. Wiseman, \"Three-Dimensional Volumetric Muscle Reconstruction of the <i>Australopithecus afarensis</i> Pelvis and Limb, with Estimations of Limb Leverage,\" <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 10, no. 6 (2023): 230356, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10265029/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Ian Tattersall, \"Endocranial Volumes and Human Evolution,\" <i>F1000Research</i> 12 (2023): 565, table 1, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10517302/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Introduction to Human Evolution,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/education/introduction-human-evolution. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/australopithecus-afarensis/. [Open access]"],"tags":["fossil","hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Lucy_cast_front_view_CMNH.jpg","title":"Lucy","desc":"Lucy (Australopithecus) skeletal cast in its new display at the Cleveland Museum of Natural History. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucy_cast_front_view_CMNH.jpg","alt":"Lucy cast front view CMNH"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why was Lucy decisive where an isolated skull or jaw would not have been?","a":"Because several hundred fragments survive, leaving enough of the pelvis, leg and spine to say how she moved: a short broad pelvis and thigh bones angled inward to bring the knees under the body, which is the arrangement of a habitual walker."},{"q":"Why is the pairing of traits in one skeleton the whole significance of Lucy?","a":"Because walking upright evolved over 4 million years ago while large and complex brains came much later, and here both facts sit in one body: a walker's pelvis and legs under a braincase averaging about 446 cubic centimetres, close to a chimpanzee's."},{"q":"Why should Lucy not be pictured as an animal that had left the trees?","a":"Because her species kept long strong arms and curved fingers throughout, so the animal to picture is at home on the ground and above it alike."}]},
{"id":"wh-011","num":11,"category":"Human origins","question":"Set in wet volcanic ash and sealed by the next eruption, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> record a gait no bone can give directly: heel down first, then a push off the toes.","questions":["The roughly 70 impressions called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were left when three individuals crossed the same Tanzanian ash surface 3.66 million years ago, the smallest treading in the largest one's tracks.","Because the big toe lies in line with the others rather than splaying to grasp, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the earliest direct evidence that upright walking had become habitual."],"answer":"Laetoli footprints","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3.66 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Laetoli, Tanzania</span></div>","answerText":"Laetoli footprints","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Laetoli footprints</b> are trails of hominin prints preserved in hardened volcanic ash at Laetoli in northern Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They survive by an accident of timing: a nearby volcano laid down a fine ash, rain wetted it, animals and hominins walked across it, and a later eruption buried the surface before it could be destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best-known trail runs almost 27 metres (88 feet) and holds about 70 impressions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three individuals made it, moving in the same direction, the smallest treading in the tracks of the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surface has been dated to about 3.66 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their value is that they record a gait, which no bone can give directly: the big toe lies in line with the rest of the foot rather than splaying out to grasp, and each step lands on the heel and pushes off from the toes, the way a modern human walks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That makes them the earliest direct evidence that upright walking had become habitual rather than occasional, and the close spacing of the prints suggests short legs and correspondingly short strides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are attributed to Australopithecus afarensis, whose fossils lie nearby in the same sediment layer and whose bones independently show a body that walked upright,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> though the case rests on that association rather than on any skeleton found in the tracks themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A second set of trackways on the same surface carries the prints of a much larger walker, put at about 1.65 metres (5 ft 5 in), which has been read as a sign of marked size difference between the sexes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prints at another spot, first noticed in 1976, were left by a small hominin with an odd cross-stepping walk, and have been argued to record a second kind of hominin crossing the same landscape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Laetoli Footprint Trails,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/behavior/laetoli-footprint-trails. [Open access]","Fidelis T. Masao et al., \"New Footprints from Laetoli (Tanzania) Provide Evidence for Marked Body Size Variation in Early Hominins,\" <i>eLife</i> 5 (2016): e19568, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5156529/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Ellison J. McNutt et al., \"Footprint Evidence of Early Hominin Locomotor Diversity at Laetoli, Tanzania,\" <i>Nature</i> 600, no. 7889 (2021): 468–71, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8674131/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-afarensis. [Open access]","Australian Museum, \"<i>Australopithecus afarensis</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://australian.museum/learn/science/human-evolution/australopithecus-afarensis/. [Open access]"],"tags":["fossil","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa","tanzania"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Homo_footprints.jpg","title":"Laetoli footprints","desc":"Interpretation of a diagramatics o Human footprint in Laetoli (3.7 My) thought to be from A. afarensis; Ileret (1.5 My), from H. erectus, and H. sapiens. The green lines represent the points under the same pressure/weight. As in Science, vol 323, pg 1197. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_footprints.jpg","alt":"Homo footprints"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why do the footprints tell us something no bone can?","a":"Because they record a gait rather than an anatomy. The big toe lies in line with the rest of the foot rather than splaying out to grasp, and each step lands on the heel and pushes off from the toes, the way a modern human walks."},{"q":"Why did the trails survive at all?","a":"By an accident of timing: a nearby volcano laid down a fine ash, rain wetted it, animals and hominins walked across it, and a later eruption buried the surface before it could be destroyed."},{"q":"Why is the attribution to Australopithecus afarensis less certain than the prints themselves?","a":"Because no skeleton was found in the tracks. The case rests on association — afarensis fossils lie nearby in the same sediment layer, and their bones independently show a body that walked upright."}]},
{"id":"wh-012","num":12,"category":"Human origins","question":"Blasted out of lime workings in South Africa in 1924, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a young australopith's skull whose braincase filled with limestone and set into a natural cast of the brain.","questions":["Milk teeth and erupting first molars put the age at death at three or four, and a forward-set foramen magnum made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first fossil evidence that hominins walked upright.","Punctures at the bottom of the eye sockets, matching the damage crowned eagles leave on monkeys, suggest the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed and eaten by a large bird of prey."],"answer":"Taung Child","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.8 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Taung, South Africa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died aged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 or 4 years</span></div>","answerText":"Taung Child","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Taung Child</b> is the fossilised skull of a young Australopithecus africanus, blasted out of lime workings at Taung in South Africa in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It preserves the face, much of the braincase and the jaws, with the milk teeth and the first molars still in place, which puts the child's age at death at three or four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Limestone had filled the empty braincase and hardened, so the fossil carries a natural cast of the brain's outer surface, made by the ground rather than by anyone's hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That cast gives a cranial capacity now put at between 382 and 407 cubic centimetres, which falls within the range of the great apes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The specimen is about 2.8 million years old, and its species ranged across southern Africa between roughly 3.3 and 2.1 million years ago, walking upright while keeping shoulders and hands still suited to climbing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What made the skull decisive is where the spinal cord left it: the foramen magnum sits beneath the braincase rather than towards the back, which is the placement of a head carried on an upright body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was the first fossil evidence of hominin bipedality, and the description published in 1925 made the skull the type specimen of a new species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was also the first fossil support for Darwin's prediction of 1871 that the human lineage had begun in Africa rather than in Asia or Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How the child died has a likely answer of its own: punctures at the bottom of the eye sockets, with gouges and scratches around the orbits and across the front of the skull, match the damage crowned eagles leave on the monkeys they kill and eat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The deposit around the skull holds eggshells and the bones of many small animals, which is what gathers below a large raptor's nest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"Taung Child,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/taung-child. [Open access]","Dean Falk et al., \"Metopic Suture of Taung (<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>) and Its Implications for Hominin Brain Evolution,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 22 (2012): 8467–70, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3365196/. [Open access]","John Rowan and Bernard Wood, \"Dart and the Taung Juvenile: Making Sense of a Century-Old Record of Hominin Evolution in Africa,\" <i>Biology Letters</i> 20, no. 7 (2024): 20240185, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11267397/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Australopithecus africanus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/australopithecus-africanus. [Open access]","Lee R. Berger and W. Scott McGraw, \"Further Evidence for Eagle Predation of, and Feeding Damage on, the Taung Child,\" <i>South African Journal of Science</i> 103, nos. 11–12 (2007): 496–98, https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0038-23532007000600013. [Open access]"],"tags":["fossil","hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Tobias_and_Taung.jpg/1920px-Tobias_and_Taung.jpg","title":"Taung Child","desc":"Dr. Philip V. Tobias and the Taung Child. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tobias_and_Taung.jpg","alt":"Tobias and Taung"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why was the Taung Child decisive when its brain was ape-sized?","a":"Because of where the spinal cord left the skull. The foramen magnum sits beneath the braincase rather than towards the back, which is the placement of a head carried on an upright body — the first fossil evidence of hominin bipedality."},{"q":"Why does the skull carry a cast of the brain nobody made?","a":"Because limestone had filled the empty braincase and hardened, so the ground itself moulded the brain's outer surface. That natural cast gives a cranial capacity now put at between 382 and 407 cubic centimetres."},{"q":"Why is an eagle the likely cause of the child's death?","a":"Because punctures at the bottom of the eye sockets, with gouges and scratches around the orbits and across the front of the skull, match the damage crowned eagles leave on the monkeys they kill, and the deposit holds eggshells and many small animals' bones."}]},
{"id":"wh-013","num":13,"category":"Human origins","question":"Flaring cheekbones, a crest along the top of the skull and cheek teeth four times the size of a modern human's make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the robust australopithecines.","questions":["Carbon isotopes show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived largely on grasses and sedges rather than nuts, which overturned the reading its massive jaws had suggested for decades.","The name means 'beside man', which is roughly where most scholars still put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: a side branch of the human tree that led nowhere."],"answer":"Paranthropus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2.7 – 1.2 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">eastern and southern Africa</span></div>","answerText":"Paranthropus","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Paranthropus</b> is an extinct genus of African hominins, the robust australopithecines, whose skulls are built around the machinery of chewing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three species are recognised: P. aethiopicus in the Turkana basin from about 2.7 to 2.3 million years ago,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> P. boisei in eastern Africa from about 2.3 to 1.2 million,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and P. robustus in southern Africa from about 1.8 to 1.2 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The genus name means 'beside man', which is where it has stood since 1938: a side branch rather than an ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Robust describes the face and teeth rather than the body, a male P. boisei standing about 1.37 metres (4 ft 6 in) and weighing some 49 kilograms (108 lb).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chewing anatomy is what defines the group: a crest along the midline of the skull to anchor the jaw muscles, wide cheekbones to carry them, and cheek teeth four times the size of a modern human's beneath the thickest enamel of any early human.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known skull, OH 5 from Olduvai Gorge, was nicknamed Nutcracker Man for exactly that apparatus,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and for decades the reading was that a head built like this existed to crush hard objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The teeth themselves say otherwise. Microwear on the molars of P. boisei resembles a fruit-eater's rather than a hard-object specialist's, so tough foods like roots and nuts were probably fallbacks, eaten when better ones ran short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbon isotopes go further, showing a diet dominated by C4 plants such as grasses and sedges, sustained over more than half a million years and across the Turkana, Baringo, Natron and Olduvai regions, more heavily than in any other hominin yet measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The southern species fed more widely, on nuts, seeds, roots and tubers alongside soft fruit, insects and meat,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> but the eastern specialist may have been undone by its own narrowness: C4 grasslands contracted as woodland spread around a million years ago, sharpening competition among the animals that lived on them, and P. boisei disappears just before that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Paranthropus boisei</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/paranthropus-boisei. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Paranthropus aethiopicus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/paranthropus-aethiopicus. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Paranthropus robustus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/paranthropus-robustus. [Open access]","Thure E. Cerling et al., \"Diet of <i>Paranthropus boisei</i> in the Early Pleistocene of East Africa,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 23 (2011): 9337–41, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3111323/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Rhonda L. Quinn and Christopher J. Lepre, \"Contracting Eastern African C4 Grasslands during the Extinction of <i>Paranthropus boisei</i>,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 7164, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8009881/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"OH 5,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/fossils/oh-5. [Open access]"],"tags":["hominin","palaeontology","evolution","prehistory","africa"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi.JPG/1920px-Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi.JPG","title":"Paranthropus","desc":"The skull of Paranthropus boisei, known as KNM ER 406, photographed at the Nairobi National Museum in August 2012. Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi.JPG","alt":"Paranthropus-boisei-Nairobi"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does robust describe the face rather than the body?","a":"Because the build is in the chewing machinery: a crest along the midline of the skull to anchor the jaw muscles, wide cheekbones to carry them, and cheek teeth four times a modern human's. A male P. boisei stood about 1.37 metres and weighed some 49 kilograms."},{"q":"Why is the Nutcracker Man reading of Paranthropus now doubted?","a":"Because the teeth say otherwise. Microwear on the molars of P. boisei resembles a fruit-eater's rather than a hard-object specialist's, so tough foods like roots and nuts were probably fallbacks, eaten when better ones ran short."},{"q":"Why may the eastern species have been undone by its own diet?","a":"Because carbon isotopes show P. boisei living on C4 plants such as grasses and sedges more heavily than any other hominin measured, and those grasslands contracted as woodland spread around a million years ago — just before it disappears."}]},
{"id":"wh-022","num":22,"category":"Human origins","question":"Long before our own species existed, the dispersal called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried hominins from Africa into western Asia, and eventually as far as China and Indonesia.","questions":["The spread known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put hominins at Dmanisi in Georgia by 1.8 million years ago and in Java before 1.5 million.","The travellers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not big-brained pioneers: the smallest braincase among them holds 546 cubic centimetres, barely above an australopith's."],"answer":"Out of Africa I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest claim</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.95 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dmanisi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.8 Mya</span></div>","answerText":"Out of Africa I","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Out of Africa I</b> is the name given to the first spread of hominins beyond Africa, an event more than a million and a half years older than our own species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The species associated with it is Homo erectus, which appears about 1.89 million years ago and whose range runs from northern, eastern and southern Africa through western Asia to Georgia, China and Indonesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best-dated early site outside the continent is Dmanisi in Georgia, where hominins were present by 1.8 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cut-marked animal bones at Grăunceanu in Romania have been dated to more than 1.95 million years ago, which would push the crossing earlier still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the other direction the record reaches Java by more than 1.5 million years ago, at the far end of a route across most of the breadth of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What is striking about the travellers is how unremarkable they were, judged by the traits usually taken to mark the human career. Skulls of this period from Africa and Georgia hold brains ranging from about 546 to 1,067 cubic centimetres, so the smallest sits barely above an australopith's, and the spread within one region is nearly twofold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor did they carry the newest toolkit out with them: the Acheulean hand axe appears in Africa only around 1.76 million years ago, by which time hominins were already at Dmanisi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Homo erectus went on to become possibly the longest-lived of all early human species, persisting in Indonesia until roughly 110,000 years ago, long after it had gone from Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The numeral in the name matters, because Homo sapiens did not appear in Africa until about 300,000 years ago: the spread that eventually carried modern people across the world is a separate event, and a far later one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Sabrina C. Curran et al., \"Hominin Presence in Eurasia by at Least 1.95 Million Years Ago,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 836, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11747263/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]","Yan Rizal et al., \"Last Appearance of <i>Homo erectus</i> at Ngandong, Java, 117,000–108,000 Years Ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 577, no. 7790 (2020): 381–85, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1863-2. [Open access]","Susan C. Antón et al., \"Morphological Variation in <i>Homo erectus</i> and the Origins of Developmental Plasticity,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150236, table 1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920293/. [Open access]","Ignacio de la Torre, \"The Origins of the Acheulean: Past and Present Perspectives on a Major Transition in Human Evolution,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1698 (2016): 20150245, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4920301/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo sapiens</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-sapiens. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","migration","hominin","palaeontology","prehistory","evolution"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Out_of_Africa_I_and_II_hypothesis.jpg","title":"Out of Africa I","desc":"A map of the Out of Africa I and II hypotheses. Gianreali, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Out_of_Africa_I_and_II_hypothesis.jpg","alt":"Out of Africa I and II hypothesis"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is it striking how unremarkable the first travellers out of Africa were?","a":"Because skulls of this period from Africa and Georgia hold brains ranging from about 546 to 1,067 cubic centimetres, so the smallest sits barely above an australopith's, and the spread within one region is nearly twofold."},{"q":"Why did they not carry the newest toolkit out with them?","a":"Because the Acheulean hand axe appears in Africa only around 1.76 million years ago, by which time hominins were already at Dmanisi in Georgia — so the crossing came first and the hand axe after."},{"q":"Why does the numeral in the name matter?","a":"Because Homo sapiens did not appear in Africa until about 300,000 years ago, so the spread that eventually carried modern people across the world is a separate event and a far later one."}]},
{"id":"wh-031","num":31,"category":"Human origins","question":"Heat breaks food down before it is swallowed, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposes that this is what paid for the small gut and the large brain of early Homo.","questions":["The trouble with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is chronology: it needs fire around 1.7 million years ago, and burnt material genuinely in place goes back only about a million.","Evidence from jaws and teeth alone cannot settle the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because cutting and pounding with stone tools soften food as well as heat does."],"answer":"cooking hypothesis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Change dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1.7 Mya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire in place</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1 Mya</span></div>","answerText":"cooking hypothesis","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>cooking hypothesis</b> holds that the control of fire for cooking was a driving force behind the adaptations that define early Homo, above all a reduced digestive system and an increased brain volume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its logic is energetic rather than culinary. Heat breaks food down before it is swallowed, so more of a meal's energy is recovered and less gut is needed to recover it, which is what would make a costly brain affordable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Homo erectus, appearing about 1.89 million years ago, is the species in which those changes show most clearly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The hypothesis accordingly dates the shift to somewhere around 1.7 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One line of support comes from the jaws: the ability to generate molar bite force falls away at the very origin of the genus, Homo habilis producing less of it at the molars than the australopiths before it, which implies that changes in diet or in food processing mattered from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That evidence does not require fire, though, since cutting and pounding with stone tools soften food as well, and the jaws cannot tell the two apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The harder problem is the dating. Burnt material genuinely in place goes back about a million years at Wonderwerk Cave in South Africa, and fire becomes routine equipment only around 400,000 years ago, which is a long way short of 1.7 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A bioenergetic analysis presses further still, finding that cooking meat improves its digestibility by only about 8 per cent, some 1,200 calories an hour, too little to repay the cost of keeping a fire alight, and proposing that preserving meat and fat and keeping predators off were the likelier first reasons for Homo erectus to burn anything at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["John A. J. Gowlett, \"The Discovery of Fire by Humans: A Long and Convoluted Process,\" <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 371, no. 1696 (2016): 20150164, under \"Fire and the Cooking Hypothesis,\" https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4874402/. [Open access]","Miki Ben-Dor and Ran Barkai, \"A Bioenergetic Approach Favors the Preservation and Protection of Prey, Not Cooking, as the Drivers of Early Fire,\" <i>Frontiers in Nutrition</i> 12 (2025): 1585182, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12122349/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Justin A. Ledogar et al., \"Bite Force Production and the Origin of <i>Homo</i>,\" <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 12, no. 4 (2025): 241879, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12014231/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Wil Roebroeks and Paola Villa, \"On the Earliest Evidence for Habitual Use of Fire in Europe,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 13 (2011): 5209–14, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3069174/. [Open access]","Francesco Berna et al., \"Microstratigraphic Evidence of in situ Fire in the Acheulean Strata of Wonderwerk Cave, Northern Cape Province, South Africa,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 20 (2012): E1215–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3356665/. [Open access]","Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History, Human Origins Program, \"<i>Homo erectus</i>,\" accessed 7 August 2026, https://humanorigins.si.edu/evidence/human-fossils/species/homo-erectus. [Open access]"],"tags":["theory","evolution","prehistory","science","archaeology"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the cooking hypothesis energetic rather than culinary?","a":"Because heat breaks food down before it is swallowed, so more of a meal's energy is recovered and less gut is needed to recover it — which is what would make a costly brain affordable."},{"q":"Why can the jaws not settle whether fire was involved?","a":"Because cutting and pounding with stone tools soften food as well, and the jaws cannot tell the two apart. Molar bite force falls away at the very origin of the genus, which shows only that food processing mattered from the beginning."},{"q":"Why is the dating the harder problem for the hypothesis?","a":"Because the shift is placed around 1.7 million years ago, while burnt material genuinely in place goes back about a million years at Wonderwerk Cave and fire becomes routine equipment only around 400,000."}]},
{"id":"wh-056","num":56,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"Last of the three divisions of the African Stone Age, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from roughly 40,000 years ago into historical times, far outlasting the last Ice Age.","questions":["The names were chosen in 1929 so that the African sequence would not be read as the European one, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not the continent's Upper Palaeolithic.","Small tools are its signature, and in eastern Senegal the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was in use at the same time as Middle Stone Age technology, by separate populations."],"answer":"Late Stone Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 BP – historical times</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Africa</span></div>","answerText":"Late Stone Age","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Late Stone Age</b> is the last of the three divisions into which the African Stone Age is sorted, running from about 40,000 years ago into historical times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scheme was laid out by John Goodwin and Clarence van Riet Lowe in 1929, and its names were chosen so that the African sequence would not be read as the European one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That caution still matters, because this is not the African Upper Palaeolithic: it begins later, lasts very much longer, and ends not with the Ice Age but whenever a given people took up metal or entered the written record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its beginning is usually placed at a strong technological change at Border Cave in KwaZulu-Natal, around 44,000 to 42,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The early phase is informal, mixing prepared cores with bipolar work and small tools, and fully microlithic industries such as the Wilton appear only much later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What its toolkits have in common is smallness: cores worked down to yield flakes, bladelets and blades, with segments dominating and backed bladelets and end-scrapers beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The label is applied right across the continent rather than in the south alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two sites in the Falémé valley of eastern Senegal, dated between about 17,000 and 12,000 years ago, are the oldest such assemblages known in that country and add to a very small number for West Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They also show that the divisions are not clean slices of time, since through the Final Pleistocene there Middle and Late Stone Age technologies were in contemporaneous use by separate populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What people ate can now be read directly from their bones: at Taforalt in Morocco, hunter-gatherers of 15,000 to 13,000 years ago relied heavily on plant foods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Emily Hallinan, “The Southern African Stone Age Site Index (SASSI): A Spatial, Chronological and Contextual Resource for Archaeological Research,” <i>Journal of Open Archaeology Data</i> 14 (2026): 2, under “Overview” and table 1, https://doi.org/10.5334/joad.186. [Open access]","B. D. Malan, “The Term ‘Middle Stone Age’,” in <i>Third Pan-African Congress on Prehistory, Livingstone 1955</i>, ed. J. Desmond Clark and Sonia Cole (London: Chatto and Windus, 1957), 223–27, https://archive.org/details/in.gov.ignca.6340. [Open access]","Paola Villa et al., “Border Cave and the Beginning of the Later Stone Age in South Africa,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 33 (2012): 13208–13, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1202629109. [Paywalled]","Zineb Moubtahij et al., “Isotopic Evidence of High Reliance on Plant Food among Later Stone Age Hunter-Gatherers at Taforalt, Morocco,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8, no. 5 (2024): 1035–45, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11090808/. [Open access]","Matar Ndiaye et al., “Two New Later Stone Age Sites from the Final Pleistocene in the Falémé Valley, Eastern Senegal,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0294346, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10977785/. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","africa","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Pieza_foli%C3%A1cea_africana.jpg","title":"Late Stone Age","desc":"African biface artifact dated in Late Stone Age period. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieza_foli%C3%A1cea_africana.jpg","alt":"Pieza foliácea africana"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is this not simply the African Upper Palaeolithic?","a":"Because it begins later, lasts very much longer, and ends not with the Ice Age but whenever a given people took up metal or entered the written record."},{"q":"Why do the Senegal sites show the divisions are not clean slices of time?","a":"Because through the Final Pleistocene in the Falémé valley, Middle and Late Stone Age technologies were in contemporaneous use by separate populations — so the labels overlap rather than succeed one another."},{"q":"Why can what people ate now be read directly?","a":"Because it can be read from their bones: at Taforalt in Morocco, hunter-gatherers of 15,000 to 13,000 years ago relied heavily on plant foods."}]},
{"id":"wh-062","num":62,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"Saying nothing about food or shelter and everything about display, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the oldest personal ornaments whose purpose is not in doubt.","questions":["A deliberate hole, and the polish where one shell rubbed against the next on a string, are what separate worn <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from shells that were merely gathered.","At Taforalt in Morocco, pierced and ochre-stained <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about 82,000 years old carry wear showing they had been suspended and worn."],"answer":"shell beads","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Collected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 240,000 – 160,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pierced and worn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 120,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"shell beads","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shell beads</b> are the oldest personal ornaments whose purpose is not in doubt, and their interest is that they say nothing about food or shelter and everything about display.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An ornament only works if it is seen: it must carry a meaning understood between groups, and be worn where others can look at it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What shows that a shell was worn rather than merely gathered is a deliberate perforation together with the wear that stringing leaves behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Misliya Cave in Israel, shells from between about 240,000 and 160,000 years ago carry no trace of human handling at all, so they were collected and not displayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Qafzeh, around 120,000 years ago, the shells are pierced and show fine striations beside the hole and polish where one valve rubbed against the next on a string.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both of those assemblages are of Glycymeris, a bivalve broad enough to be noticed on a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In North Africa the beads are sea snails instead: pierced Nassarius shells from Taforalt in Morocco, dated to roughly 82,000 years ago, worn enough to show they had been suspended and coated in red ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Similar Tritia shells run through the Aterian sites of the Moroccan coast, so the practice is regional rather than one site's peculiarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oldest ornament known to have been buried with a person is a perforated Conus shell laid with an infant of four to six months at Border Cave in South Africa, in layers dated to 74,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later assemblages reach far past shell: the Upper Cave at Zhoukoudian yielded ornaments of badger, fox, deer, marten and tiger teeth, of fish and bird bone, of shell, limestone and a perforated pebble, and three badger canines among them had been sewn onto clothing rather than hung round a neck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Daniella E. Bar-Yosef Mayer et al., “On Holes and Strings: Earliest Displays of Human Adornment in the Middle Palaeolithic,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 7 (2020): e0234924, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0234924. [Open access]","Abdeljalil Bouzouggar, Nick Barton, Marian Vanhaeren, et al., “82,000-Year-Old Shell Beads from North Africa and Implications for the Origins of Modern Human Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 104, no. 24 (2007): 9964–69, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1891266/. [Open access]","Francesco d'Errico and Lucinda Backwell, “Earliest Evidence of Personal Ornaments Associated with Burial: The Conus Shells from Border Cave,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 93 (2016): 91–108, esp. 91, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2016.01.002. [Paywalled]","Emilie Campmas, Matthieu Lebon, et al., “Aterian Shell Beads from the Coastal Site of El Mnasra Cave (Rabat-Témara, Morocco): Specificities of the North African MSA Personal Ornaments,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21 (2026): e0338785, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13016355/. [Open access]","Francesco d'Errico et al., “Zhoukoudian Upper Cave Personal Ornaments and Ochre: Rediscovery and Reevaluation,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 161 (2021): 103088, §§1–2, https://shs.hal.science/hal-03445635/document. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","archaeology","prehistory","art","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Shell_bead_necklace.webp","title":"shell beads","desc":"This is a reconstruction of a shell bead necklace, including 10 shell beads from Cro-Magnon, France, (bottom part of necklace, in color) and 10 facsimile beads (top part of necklace, in white). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shell_bead_necklace.webp","alt":"Shell bead necklace"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why are shell beads the ornaments whose purpose is not in doubt?","a":"Because they say nothing about food or shelter and everything about display: an ornament only works if it is seen, carries a meaning understood between groups, and is worn where others can look at it."},{"q":"Why does a gathered shell not count as an ornament?","a":"Because what shows a shell was worn is a deliberate perforation together with the wear that stringing leaves. The shells at Misliya carry no trace of human handling at all, so they were collected and not displayed."},{"q":"Why is the Moroccan evidence read as a regional practice?","a":"Because similar Tritia shells run through the Aterian sites of the Moroccan coast alongside the pierced, ochre-coated Nassarius shells from Taforalt — so it is a habit shared across a region rather than one site's peculiarity."}]},
{"id":"wh-063","num":63,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"An infant of four to six months laid in a cut pit at Border Cave with a pierced shell is the oldest African <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> known to include an ornament.","questions":["At Sungir near Vladimir, some 13,000 mammoth ivory beads went into one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is more than 2,500 hours of work laid in the ground.","Two children at Mal'ta lay in a slab-lined structure floored with red ochre, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> furnished with an ivory tiara and a necklace of 120 bone beads."],"answer":"Paleolithic burial","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Border Cave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 74,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sungir</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 34,000 cal BP</span></div>","answerText":"Paleolithic burial","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Paleolithic burial</b> is the deliberate interment of a body during the Old Stone Age, and the oldest in Africa known to include an ornament holds an infant of four to six months at Border Cave in South Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pit was cut into Howiesons Poort layers dated to 74,000 years, give or take four thousand, and a perforated Conus shell lay with the child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Far to the north, at Mal'ta beside the Belaya in Siberia, two children were laid in a slab-lined structure whose floor was covered with red ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With them went a tiara of mammoth ivory, a necklace of 120 bone beads, a large oval pendant, a bone plaque cut with a zigzag, and a carving of a bird in flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Goods of that kind could hardly have been the property of a child of three or four, which is the excavator's reason for reading them as given rather than owned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The most elaborate Upper Paleolithic burial yet known is at Sungir near Vladimir in Russia, dated to about 34,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One adult lay beneath some 3,000 mammoth ivory beads, with twelve pierced fox canines across the forehead and 25 ivory arm bands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two others, buried head to head, were adolescents of roughly ten and twelve, and 13,000 ivory beads in all were associated with the interments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a cost rather than a decoration: the beads alone stand for more than 2,500 hours of work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA has since changed how the group is read, since all of them were genetically male and none was a close relative of any other, so the labour was not spent on a single family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Francesco d'Errico and Lucinda Backwell, “Earliest Evidence of Personal Ornaments Associated with Burial: The Conus Shells from Border Cave,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 93 (2016): 91–108, esp. 91, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhevol.2016.01.002. [Paywalled]","Erik Trinkaus and Alexandra P. Buzhilova, “Diversity and Differential Disposal of the Dead at Sunghir,” <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 361 (2018): 7–21, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.223. [Open access]","April Nowell, “Adolescence and Innovation in the European Upper Palaeolithic,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e36, under “Box 1,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427464/. [Open access]","Martin Sikora, Andaine Seguin-Orlando, Vitor C. Sousa, et al., “Ancient Genomes Show Social and Reproductive Behavior of Early Upper Paleolithic Foragers,” <i>Science</i> 358, no. 6363 (2017): 659–62, https://escholarship.org/uc/item/7vx791kv. [Open access]","Liudmila Lbova, “The Siberian Paleolithic Site of Mal'ta: A Unique Source for the Study of Childhood Archaeology,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 3 (2021): e9, under “The Mal'ta burial,” https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10427291/. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","religion","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Dolni_Vestonice_burial_DV13-DV14-DV15.jpg/1920px-Dolni_Vestonice_burial_DV13-DV14-DV15.jpg","title":"Paleolithic burial","desc":"The triple burial of Dolní Věstonice, Moravia, dated to around 31,000 years before present. From left to right: DV 13, DV 15, DV 14. Alissa Mittnik, Chuan-Chao Wang, Jiří Svoboda, Johannes Krause, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolni_Vestonice_burial_DV13-DV14-DV15.jpg","alt":"Dolni Vestonice burial DV13-DV14-DV15"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why are the Mal'ta goods read as given rather than owned?","a":"Because an ivory tiara, a necklace of 120 bone beads, a large pendant, an engraved plaque and a carved bird could hardly have been the property of a child of three or four."},{"q":"Why are the Sungir beads a cost rather than a decoration?","a":"Because 13,000 ivory beads were associated with the interments, and the beads alone stand for more than 2,500 hours of work."},{"q":"Why did ancient DNA change how the Sungir group is read?","a":"Because all of them were genetically male and none was a close relative of any other, so the labour was not spent on a single family."}]},
{"id":"wh-080","num":80,"category":"The Palaeolithic","question":"The securest evidence there is of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a vulture wing bone 21.7 centimetres long with four surviving finger holes, lifted from Hohle Fels.","questions":["The Swabian caves were made a World Heritage property in 2017 for holding the oldest portable art and instruments of humankind, which is where the case for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chiefly rests.","A pierced cave bear thigh bone from Slovenia would push <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back to the Neanderthals, if the holes in it were cut rather than bitten."],"answer":"Paleolithic music","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest secure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 – 35,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Swabian Jura, Germany</span></div>","answerText":"Paleolithic music","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"Sound leaves nothing behind, so <b>Paleolithic music</b> is known almost entirely from the instruments that survive, and the oldest accepted without dispute are the flutes of the Swabian Jura in southern Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The best preserved is 21.7 centimetres (8.5 inches) long, cut from the wing bone of a griffon vulture, and was lifted from the cave of Hohle Fels in 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four finger holes survive along it, and the lower end appears to have broken away at a fifth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mouthpiece is a bevelled edge cut into the shaft to leave a notch, which is where the breath is directed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is between 35,000 and 40,000 years old, and came from the same cave and the same years as a small ivory figure of a woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Six caves across two valleys were inscribed together as a World Heritage property in 2017, on the grounds that they hold the oldest portable art and musical instruments of humankind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A much older claim comes from Divje Babe in western Slovenia: the thigh bone of a cave bear cub, about 11 centimetres (4.3 inches) long, pierced by two clean round holes in a line, from a layer of 60,000 to 50,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> If those holes were cut on purpose the instrument is Neanderthal, because in Europe at that date there was nobody else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The objection is that a carnivore's canine punches a neat round hole through thin bone, and one study reads the piece as a bear cub gnawed by spotted hyenas in a cave bear den.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its finders answer that no bite aligns holes down a shaft, the museum in Ljubljana displays it as the oldest musical instrument in the world, and the question is not settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren, “Gänsegeierflöte,” Digitale Sammlung, inv. no. 1977-0119-0089-9000, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.urmu.de/digitale-sammlung/objekt/gaensegeierfloete-1977-0119-0089-9000. [Open access]","Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren, “Hohle Fels,” Welterbe-Fundstellen, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.urmu.de/welterbe/fundstellen/hohle-fels. [Open access]","Welterbe Höhlen und Eiszeitkunst der Schwäbischen Alb, “UNESCO-Welterbe,” accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.iceageart.de/unesco-welterbe. [Open access]","Matija Turk, Ivan Turk, and Marcel Otte, “The Neanderthal Musical Instrument from Divje Babe I Cave (Slovenia): A Critical Review of the Discussion,” <i>Applied Sciences</i> 10, no. 4 (2020): 1226, https://doi.org/10.3390/app10041226. [Open access]","Cajus G. Diedrich, “‘Neanderthal Bone Flutes’: Simply Products of Ice Age Spotted Hyena Scavenging Activities on Cave Bear Cubs in European Cave Bear Dens,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 2, no. 4 (2015): 140022, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4448875/. [Open access]","National Museum of Slovenia, “Neanderthal Flute,” Collection Highlights, inv. no. D. b. 652, accessed 7 August 2026, https://www.nms.si/en/collections/highlights/343-Neanderthal-flute. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","art","archaeology","prehistory"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Divje_Babe_flute_%28Late_Pleistocene_flute%29.jpg/1920px-Divje_Babe_flute_%28Late_Pleistocene_flute%29.jpg","title":"Paleolithic music","desc":"Divje Babe flute (alleged Late Pleistocene flute) dating to 43100 ± 700 BP. National Museum of Slovenia. Measurements: 133.6 mm (5.26 in) long. Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Divje_Babe_flute_(Late_Pleistocene_flute).jpg","alt":"Divje Babe flute (Late Pleistocene flute)"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is Paleolithic music known almost entirely from instruments?","a":"Because sound leaves nothing behind. What survives is the object that made it — the flutes of the Swabian Jura, the best preserved cut from the wing bone of a griffon vulture."},{"q":"Why would the Divje Babe bone be Neanderthal if it is an instrument?","a":"Because it comes from a layer of 60,000 to 50,000 years ago, and in Europe at that date there was nobody else to have made it."},{"q":"Why is that piece still disputed?","a":"Because a carnivore's canine punches a neat round hole through thin bone, and one study reads it as a bear cub gnawed by spotted hyenas. Its finders answer that no bite aligns holes down a shaft."}]},
{"id":"wh-086","num":86,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made by mitochondrial DNA from 147 people, all of it stemming from one woman who had lived in Africa.","questions":["Where multiregional evolution had Homo erectus becoming modern in place right across the Old World, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has our species arising on one continent and spreading from it.","Neanderthal DNA in everyone descended from those who left the continent is why the strict replacement form of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no longer stands, though its core does."],"answer":"recent African origin of modern humans","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest fossils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Jebel Irhoud, Morocco</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Genetic case</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1987</span></div>","answerText":"recent African origin of modern humans","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>recent African origin of modern humans</b> is the model holding that our species arose in Africa and spread out of it, so that every person alive descends from that African population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chief rival was multiregional evolution, in which Homo erectus turned by degrees into archaic and then modern humans in place across the Old World, the regions kept in step by gene flow between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In China that was taken to mean that people living there today trace their ancestry directly back to the Homo erectus of Zhoukoudian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finding that turned the argument came in 1987: mitochondrial DNA from 147 people, all of it stemming from one woman who is reckoned to have lived about 200,000 years ago, probably in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fossils point the same way, the earliest members of our lineage yet recognised being those from Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, dated to about 300,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What did not survive is the strict form of the model, and the thing that broke it was the draft Neanderthal genome of 2010.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It showed Neanderthals sharing more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa, which points to gene flow from Neanderthals into the ancestors of those who left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Replacement was therefore not total, and the reading now taken as the most economical combines the two models: dispersal out of Africa in several waves, with regular mixing among the smaller populations already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Africa itself has been rethought as well, because the early fossils show a striking range of forms over a wide spread of country rather than a straight march towards ourselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What has replaced the idea of a single birthplace is one of strongly subdivided populations living across the continent and joined by occasional gene flow, a picture some call African multiregionalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Christopher J. Bae and Xiujie Wu, “Making Sense of Eastern Asian Late Quaternary Hominin Variability,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9479, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11531466/. [Open access]","Rebecca L. Cann, Mark Stoneking, and Allan C. Wilson, “Mitochondrial DNA and Human Evolution,” <i>Nature</i> 325, no. 6099 (1987): 31–36, https://doi.org/10.1038/325031a0. [Paywalled]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, “Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]","Eleanor M. L. Scerri et al., “Did Our Species Evolve in Subdivided Populations across Africa, and Why Does It Matter?,” <i>Trends in Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 33, no. 8 (2018): 582–94, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6092560/. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., “A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,” <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]"],"tags":["theory","evolution","genetics","palaeontology","prehistory","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_%282023%29.png/1920px-Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_%282023%29.png","title":"recent African origin of modern humans","desc":"This map shows the migration routes of modern humans within and Out of Africa, based on archaeogenetic data and environmental factors. The map was designed and created by me, Jayasinghe Sevan, in 2023. Jayasinghe23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Migration_routes_of_modern_humans_(2023).png","alt":"Migration routes of modern humans (2023)"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did the 2010 Neanderthal genome break the strict form of the model?","a":"Because it showed Neanderthals sharing more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa, which points to gene flow into the ancestors of those who left. Replacement was therefore not total."},{"q":"Why has Africa itself been rethought along with the model?","a":"Because the early fossils show a striking range of forms over a wide spread of country rather than a straight march towards ourselves — so the picture is strongly subdivided populations joined by occasional gene flow."},{"q":"Why did the 1987 mitochondrial study count against multiregional evolution?","a":"Because mitochondrial DNA from 147 people all stemmed from one woman reckoned to have lived about 200,000 years ago, probably in Africa — not from separate lineages evolving in place across the Old World."}]},
{"id":"wh-088","num":88,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"Crossing at the Strait of Bab-el-Mandeb and following the shores of Arabia and India eastward is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposes, against a road through Egypt and Sinai.","questions":["Rainfall modelling gives a window near 65,000 years ago, when the northern road closed for some 40,000 years and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reopened for a long spell.","Backed microliths were once offered as its signature and did not hold up, so lithic miniaturization is now proposed instead to track the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> eastward from Africa."],"answer":"southern dispersal route","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Climate window</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 65,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Genetic estimate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">75,000 – 55,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"southern dispersal route","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>southern dispersal route</b> is the proposed path of modern humans out of Africa across the Strait of Bab-el-Mandeb at the foot of the Red Sea, rather than overland by the Nile and the Sinai land bridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two are set against each other as the southern and the northern route, and which of them carried the people who went on to fill Eurasia is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genetics gives the departure a date, putting the migration out of Africa between about 75,000 and 55,000 years ago and the arrival across Eurasia and Australia between 55,000 and 45,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rainfall decides whether either road can be walked at all, and modelling it opens a window near 65,000 years ago, the point after which the northern route shut for some 40,000 years and the southern one reopened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The southern road carries an obstacle the northern one does not, since Bab-el-Mandeb has to be crossed by water, and the archaeological support for anyone doing so is thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> People had reached the Levant long before, the caves of Skhul and Qafzeh holding our species at roughly 130,000 to 90,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those families left no descendants among us, so the question is not whether anybody went early but which road the later dispersal took, in a picture of several waves that mixed with the populations already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Stone tools were expected to settle it, and small backed pieces were once put forward as the route's signature, being found in southern Africa and in the Indian subcontinent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That case has not held, because the type was invented repeatedly rather than carried, the ground between the two regions is blank, and east of India it is missing at the dates required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proposal now is miniaturization itself, a shift to very small tools that can be followed west to east from Panga ya Saidi on the Kenyan coast to Sri Lanka and on to Timor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Robert M. Beyer, Mario Krapp, Anders Eriksson, and Andrea Manica, “Climatic Windows for Human Migration out of Africa in the Past 300,000 Years,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 12 (2021): 4889, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8384873/. [Open access]","Ceri Shipton, “Lithic Miniaturization Provides a Signature of an MIS4-3 Southern Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i>,” <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 1 (2026): e70027, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12965195/. [Open access]","James Blinkhorn, Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Michael D. Petraglia, and Simon Blockley, “Directional Changes in Levallois Core Technologies between Eastern Africa, Arabia, and the Levant during MIS 5,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11465, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8169925/. [Open access]","Huw S. Groucutt, Eleanor M. L. Scerri, Chris Stringer, and Michael D. Petraglia, “Skhul Lithic Technology and the Dispersal of <i>Homo sapiens</i> into Southwest Asia,” <i>Quaternary International</i> 515 (2019): 30–52, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:26de242d-8c79-44a8-9dea-5631a7400664. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Xiujie Wu, “Making Sense of Eastern Asian Late Quaternary Hominin Variability,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9479, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11531466/. [Open access]"],"tags":["theory","migration","archaeology","prehistory","genetics","geography"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does rainfall decide whether either road can be walked?","a":"Because the country between is desert when it is dry. Modelling opens a window near 65,000 years ago, the point after which the northern route shut for some 40,000 years and the southern one reopened."},{"q":"Why is the southern road the harder case to make?","a":"Because Bab-el-Mandeb has to be crossed by water rather than walked, and the archaeological support for anyone doing so is thin."},{"q":"Why did small backed tools fail as the route's signature?","a":"Because the type was invented repeatedly rather than carried, the ground between southern Africa and the Indian subcontinent is blank, and east of India it is missing at the dates required."}]},
{"id":"wh-089","num":89,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The clearest sign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that Neanderthals share more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa, which points to gene flow.","questions":["A girl from Denisova Cave with a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father shows that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran between archaic populations too, not only into our own.","Tibetans carry a version of a hypoxia gene explicable only by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, inherited from Denisovans and helping the body cope with thin mountain air."],"answer":"archaic human admixture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Denisova 11</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 90,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">a first-generation hybrid</span></div>","answerText":"archaic human admixture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Archaic human admixture</b> is the interbreeding between our own species and the older human populations it met on leaving Africa, and it is read not from bones but from the DNA of people alive now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The draft Neanderthal genome of 2010 is where it was first shown, in a pattern nobody had expected: Neanderthals share more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That asymmetry is hard to explain except by gene flow from Neanderthals into the ancestors of those who left the continent, and everyone descended from them carries a per cent or two of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same year a finger bone from Denisova Cave in southern Siberia gave up a genome belonging to a group that shares a common origin with Neanderthals and had never been suspected from any fossil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those Denisovans contributed 4 to 6 per cent of the genetic material of present-day Melanesians, which suggests they were once widespread across Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The two legacies are not spread alike, Neanderthal DNA turning up in everyone outside Africa while the Denisovan share is concentrated far to the east and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor did the mixing run only into us: a girl from the same Siberian cave had a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father, which is as close to the act as the evidence gets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her parents' populations had separated more than 390,000 years before, so what the cave records is two long-parted lineages meeting again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of what was inherited still works: Tibetans carry a version of a gene governing the response to low oxygen that is found in Denisovans and almost nowhere else, and no explanation but introgression fits it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Denisovans remain the harder group to place, since most of what is assigned to them is isolated teeth or undiagnostic fragments and they have no agreed species name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Richard E. Green et al., “A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,” <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Genome of the Offspring of a Neanderthal Mother and a Denisovan Father,” <i>Nature</i> 561, no. 7721 (2018): 113–16, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6130845/. [Open access]","Emilia Huerta-Sánchez et al., “Altitude Adaptation in Tibetans Caused by Introgression of Denisovan-Like DNA,” <i>Nature</i> 512, no. 7513 (2014): 194–97, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4134395/. [Open access]","Katerina Harvati and Hugo Reyes-Centeno, “Evolution of <i>Homo</i> in the Middle and Late Pleistocene,” <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 173 (2022): 103279, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703123/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","genetics","evolution","prehistory","palaeontology","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Le_Moustier_skull_in_Berlin_reconstitution.jpg/1920px-Le_Moustier_skull_in_Berlin_reconstitution.jpg","title":"archaic human admixture","desc":"Le Moustier skull in Berlin reconstitution. Gary Todd. 2019-05-12 06:53:46. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Moustier_skull_in_Berlin_reconstitution.jpg","alt":"Le Moustier skull in Berlin reconstitution"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is admixture read from living people rather than from bones?","a":"Because interbreeding leaves no skeletal mark. What shows it is an asymmetry in the DNA of people alive now: Neanderthals share more genetic variants with living Eurasians than with people in sub-Saharan Africa."},{"q":"Why are the two archaic legacies not spread alike?","a":"Because Neanderthal DNA turns up in everyone outside Africa, while the Denisovan share is concentrated far to the east and south — 4 to 6 per cent in present-day Melanesians."},{"q":"Why is the Denisovan oxygen gene evidence that cannot be explained another way?","a":"Because Tibetans carry a version of a gene governing the response to low oxygen that is found in Denisovans and almost nowhere else, and no explanation but introgression fits it."}]},
{"id":"wh-090","num":90,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"Fragments 30 to 100 base pairs long, chemically damaged and few in number, are what a laboratory has to work with when it recovers <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from bone or sediment.","questions":["The petrous bone is sampled before any other because it gives up to a hundred times more <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than a long bone does, being the densest in the body.","Cytosine deamination, showing as changes at the ends of fragments, is how genuine <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is told apart from the modern kind that contaminates a laboratory."],"answer":"ancient DNA","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First genomes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2010</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Neanderthal and Denisovan</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fragments</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 – 100 base pairs</span></div>","answerText":"ancient DNA","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ancient DNA</b> is genetic material recovered from the remains of the past, and it is the technique behind most of what has lately been learned about prehistoric populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is never a clean sample: it is fragmented, chemically altered and present in very small quantities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bonds of the molecule break by reaction with water, oxygen attacks the bases, and cytosine decays into uracil, so the pieces a laboratory gets are typically 30 to 100 base pairs long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That damage is also the proof, because the decay of cytosine shows up as a distinctive pattern of changes at the ends of each fragment, which is what separates genuinely old material from modern contamination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contamination is the standing danger of the field, met with cleanrooms, bleach and ultraviolet light, dedicated reagents and blank controls; early work on Egyptian mummies was later admitted to have been spoiled by the researchers' own DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where the sample is taken from matters as much as how it is handled, and the petrous bone behind the ear, the densest in the body, can yield up to a hundred times more human DNA than a long bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method changed with the machines: copying single stretches gave way to sequencing on a massive scale, which can assemble a whole genome out of badly broken material, and newer library preparation recovers the very shortest fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The results reshaped prehistory rather than decorating it, the draft Neanderthal genome of 2010 showing that Neanderthals and our own ancestors had interbred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same year a finger bone from a Siberian cave yielded a genome belonging to a population nobody had suspected, the Denisovans, who are known first from their DNA and only afterwards from their bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its reach is still widening at one end, since DNA can now be read from cave sediments with no skeleton present at all, and hard-stopped at the other, where nothing genetic survives and only ancient proteins still speak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jay Silverstein and Amber Chapman, “Recovery and Analysis of Ancient DNA: Challenges, Methods, and Applications in Forensic and Archaeological Science,” <i>Journal of Genetic Engineering and Biotechnology</i> 24, no. 2 (2026): 100702, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13156627/. [Open access]","Richard E. Green et al., “A Draft Sequence of the Neandertal Genome,” <i>Science</i> 328, no. 5979 (2010): 710–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5100745/. [Open access]","David Reich et al., “Genetic History of an Archaic Hominin Group from Denisova Cave in Siberia,” <i>Nature</i> 468, no. 7327 (2010): 1053–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4306417/. [Open access]","Pere Gelabert et al., “A Sedimentary Ancient DNA Perspective on Human and Carnivore Persistence through the Late Pleistocene in El Mirón Cave, Spain,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 107, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11696082/. [Open access]","Frido Welker et al., “The Dental Proteome of <i>Homo antecessor</i>,” <i>Nature</i> 580, no. 7802 (2020): 235–38, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2153-8. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","genetics","science","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Neanderthal_DNA_extraction.jpg/1920px-Neanderthal_DNA_extraction.jpg","title":"ancient DNA","desc":"Working in a clean room, researchers at the Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology in Leipzig, Germany, took extensive precautions to avoid contaminating Neanderthal DNA samples - extracted from bones like this one - with DNA from any other source, including modern humans. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neanderthal_DNA_extraction.jpg","alt":"Neanderthal DNA extraction"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is the damage to ancient DNA also the proof of its age?","a":"Because the decay of cytosine into uracil shows up as a distinctive pattern of changes at the ends of each fragment, which is what separates genuinely old material from modern contamination."},{"q":"Why does it matter which bone the sample is taken from?","a":"Because the petrous bone behind the ear, the densest in the body, can yield up to a hundred times more human DNA than a long bone."},{"q":"Why is contamination the standing danger of the field?","a":"Because what survives is fragmented and present in very small quantities, so a researcher's own DNA can swamp it — early work on Egyptian mummies was later admitted to have been spoiled that way."}]},
{"id":"wh-092","num":92,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"Australia, New Guinea and Tasmania stood as one landmass called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while the sea was low, and it lasted until about nine thousand years ago.","questions":["Drift modelling gives under a five per cent chance of anyone reaching <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by accident, which is why the first voyages there are read as deliberate.","A long chronology putting arrival at 60,000 to 65,000 years ago and a short one at 47,000 to 51,000 are the two positions on when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reached."],"answer":"Sahul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrival debated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">65,000 – 47,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Landmass ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"Sahul","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sahul</b> is the name for the single landmass that New Guinea, Australia and Tasmania formed while the sea stood low, a continent that lasted until roughly 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To its west lay Sunda, the exposed shelf of Southeast Asia, and between the two ran Wallacea, a belt of islands that was never joined to either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sahul could therefore only ever be reached across open water, by a series of crossings through those islands of which at least one approached 100 kilometres (60 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modelling how currents carry a drifting party, and working out which islands can actually be seen from which, puts the chance of arriving there by accident below five per cent unless forty or more adults were swept off at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conclusion drawn is that the voyages were meant, since establishing a population that could survive required repeated and coordinated crossings by hundreds of people within a few centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When this happened is the most contested question in Australian prehistory, and the two positions have names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The long chronology puts the first substantial arrivals at 60,000 to 65,000 years ago, resting on the rock shelter of Madjedbebe in Arnhem Land, where occupation begins about 65,000 years ago with grinding stones, ground ochres and hatchet heads with ground edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The short chronology puts them at 47,000 to 51,000 instead, on the argument that any age beyond 50,000 for that shelter is unlikely to stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An analysis of 2,456 mitochondrial genomes published in 2025 came down on the longer side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes also show the continent was not uniform before it drowned: the ancestors of Papuans and of Aboriginal Australians had diverged from one another 25,000 to 40,000 years ago, while both had parted from Eurasians 51,000 to 72,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Michael I. Bird et al., “Early Human Settlement of Sahul Was Not an Accident,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 8220, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6579762/. [Open access]","Francesca Gandini et al., “Genomic Evidence Supports the ‘Long Chronology’ for the Peopling of Sahul,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11 (2025): eady9493, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12662211/. [Open access]","James F. O'Connell et al., “When Did <i>Homo sapiens</i> First Reach Southeast Asia and Sahul?,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 34 (2018): 8482–90, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1808385115. [Paywalled]","Chris Clarkson et al., “Human Occupation of Northern Australia by 65,000 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 547, no. 7663 (2017): 306–10, http://faculty.washington.edu/bmarwick/PDFs/Clarkson_Jacobs_Marwick_2017.pdf. [Open access]","Anna-Sapfo Malaspinas et al., “A Genomic History of Aboriginal Australia,” <i>Nature</i> 538, no. 7624 (2016): 207–14, https://www.eva.mpg.de/documents/Nature/Malaspinas_Genomic_Nature_2016_2351078.pdf. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","geography","migration","prehistory","archaeology","genetics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Map_of_Sunda_and_Sahul_2.png","title":"Sahul","desc":"Modified map of Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) Source=Base map derived from File:Karte von Sunda und Sahul.png. Work of Map of Sunda and Sahul and the Wallace Line, the Lydekker Line and the Weber Line. Altaileopard, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Sunda_and_Sahul_2.png","alt":"Map of Sunda and Sahul 2"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why could Sahul only ever be reached across open water?","a":"Because Wallacea, the belt of islands between it and Sunda, was never joined to either — so getting there meant a series of crossings of which at least one approached 100 kilometres."},{"q":"Why are the voyages read as deliberate rather than accidental?","a":"Because modelling currents and inter-island visibility puts the chance of arriving by accident below five per cent unless forty or more adults were swept off at once, and a surviving population required repeated coordinated crossings by hundreds of people."},{"q":"Why do the genomes show the continent was not uniform before it drowned?","a":"Because the ancestors of Papuans and of Aboriginal Australians had diverged from one another 25,000 to 40,000 years ago, while both had parted from Eurasians 51,000 to 72,000 years ago."}]},
{"id":"wh-095","num":95,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The earliest evidence of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is three people from Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, dated between 45,930 and 42,580 years ago and found with Initial Upper Palaeolithic tools.","questions":["Western hunter-gatherers, ancient north Eurasians and early farmers of largely Near Eastern origin are the three populations the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left behind in living genomes.","Corded Ware people in Germany drawing three-quarters of their ancestry from Yamnaya herders is the last great wave of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, about 4,500 years ago."],"answer":"peopling of Europe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First arrivals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 45,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Steppe migration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,500 BP</span></div>","answerText":"peopling of Europe","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>peopling of Europe</b> happened in waves rather than once, and the ancestry of Europeans alive today was laid down over some 45,000 years by populations that arrived long apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modern humans were present by at least 45,000 years ago, and the Neanderthals they found there had gone by about 40,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest yet recovered are three individuals from Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, dated between 45,930 and 42,580 years ago and lying with an assemblage of Initial Upper Palaeolithic tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not every early arrival has descendants: people of much the same age from Romania and from Siberia contributed nothing detectable to later populations, so those lines simply stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neanderthal ancestry was heavier at the start and has thinned, falling from 3 to 6 per cent in the first Europeans to around 2 per cent now, which is read as selection working against those variants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From roughly 37,000 to 14,000 years ago the sampled dead all descend from a single founder population, and that one does form part of the ancestry of Europeans today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ice Age pressed those people into refuges, and a man buried in southern Iberia 23,000 years ago links the western European groups living before the glacial maximum with those after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second layer came with farming, closely related groups of early farmers appearing in Germany, Hungary and Spain between about 8,000 and 7,000 years ago, quite distinct from the hunter-gatherers already in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The third came off the grasslands: around 4,500 years ago the Corded Ware people of Germany drew some three-quarters of their ancestry from the Yamnaya herders of the east, and that steppe ancestry is now everywhere in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The upshot is that most Europeans descend from at least three sharply different populations — western hunter-gatherers, ancient north Eurasians, and early farmers of largely Near Eastern origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mateja Hajdinjak et al., “Initial Upper Palaeolithic Humans in Europe Had Recent Neanderthal Ancestry,” <i>Nature</i> 592, no. 7853 (2021): 253–57, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8026394/. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu et al., “The Genetic History of Ice Age Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 534, no. 7606 (2016): 200–205, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature17993. [Paywalled]","Vanessa Villalba-Mouco et al., “A 23,000-Year-Old Southern Iberian Individual Links Human Groups That Lived in Western Europe before and after the Last Glacial Maximum,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 7 (2023): 597–609, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10089921/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis et al., “Ancient Human Genomes Suggest Three Ancestral Populations for Present-Day Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 513, no. 7518 (2014): 409–13, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4170574/. [Open access]","Wolfgang Haak et al., “Massive Migration from the Steppe Was a Source for Indo-European Languages in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 522, no. 7555 (2015): 207–11, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature14317. [Paywalled]"],"tags":["concept","migration","genetics","prehistory","archaeology","geography"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Expansion_of_early_modern_humans_from_Africa.jpg","title":"peopling of Europe","desc":"Maps showing the distribution of Neanderthals and after the dispersal of anatomically modern humans out of Africa, and the locations of major archeological sites for each group. Berria, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expansion_of_early_modern_humans_from_Africa.jpg","alt":"Expansion of early modern humans from Africa"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did some of the earliest arrivals leave no trace in living people?","a":"Because those lines simply stopped: people of much the same age as the Bacho Kiro individuals, from Romania and from Siberia, contributed nothing detectable to later populations."},{"q":"Why has Neanderthal ancestry thinned since the first Europeans?","a":"Because it has fallen from 3 to 6 per cent in the first Europeans to around 2 per cent now, which is read as selection working against those variants."},{"q":"Why do most Europeans descend from at least three different populations?","a":"Because the ancestry was laid down in waves: western hunter-gatherers and ancient north Eurasians, then early farmers of largely Near Eastern origin, then steppe ancestry arriving with the Yamnaya herders around 4,500 years ago."}]},
{"id":"wh-109","num":109,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"The earliest post-built structure known in Britain is a platform of large split timbers laid along the lake edge at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in use between about 9300 and 8500 BCE.","questions":["About 90 per cent of all the red deer headdresses known from early prehistoric Europe come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where 33 have been found and only nine are pierced.","The only engraved pendant known from Mesolithic Britain came out of the lake-edge deposits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 2015, mistaken at first for an ordinary piece of stone."],"answer":"Star Carr","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9300 – 8500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Headdresses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">33, nine of them pierced</span></div>","answerText":"Star Carr","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Star Carr</b> is an Early Mesolithic site on the shore of a vanished lake in North Yorkshire, in use between about 9300 and 8500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a stopping place but one returned to for something like 800 years, which is what makes it worth as much as it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Along the water's edge its people laid a platform of large split timbers, the earliest post-built structures known anywhere in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wood of that age almost never survives, so what is ordinary carpentry elsewhere is here a rare glimpse of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its most striking finds are red deer skulls worked into headdresses, each made from the upper part of a stag's cranium with the antlers left attached, the lower jaw and the surrounding bone cut away and the plate behind pierced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Star Carr holds more than 90 per cent of every such object known from prehistoric Europe, so a whole class of artefact is effectively one site's evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The original excavations yielded 21 and later work recovered twelve more, which makes 33 in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only nine of the 33 are pierced, and the antlers were cut down by grooving and splintering and the remaining beams hollowed out, so these were worked for wearing rather than merely kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2015 a piece of shale came out of the lake-edge mud that had been taken for a natural stone, its drilled hole packed with sediment and its engraved lines invisible until the mud fell away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sub-triangular, about 31 by 35 millimetres (1.2 by 1.4 inches) and 3 thick, it is the only engraved pendant known from Mesolithic Britain, and no other in Europe is made of shale rather than amber, antler or bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jessica Bates et al., “Spatial Organisation within the Earliest Evidence of Post-Built Structures in Britain,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 7 (2024): e0306908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11249238/. [Open access]","Aimée Little et al., “Technological Analysis of the World's Earliest Shamanic Costume: A Multi-Scalar, Experimental Study of a Red Deer Headdress from the Early Holocene Site of Star Carr, UK,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0152136, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4830440/. [Open access]","Ben Elliott, Becky Knight, and Aimée Little, “Antler Frontlets,” in <i>Star Carr Volume 2: Studies in Technology, Subsistence and Environment</i>, ed. Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller, and Barry Taylor (York: White Rose University Press, 2018), 297–333, https://doi.org/10.22599/book2.l. [Open access]","Nicky Milner et al., “A Unique Engraved Shale Pendant from the Site of Star Carr: The Oldest Mesolithic Art in Britain,” <i>Internet Archaeology</i> 40 (2016), https://doi.org/10.11141/ia.40.8. [Open access]","Nicky Milner, Chantal Conneller, and Barry Taylor, eds., <i>Star Carr Volume 1: A Persistent Place in a Changing World</i> (York: White Rose University Press, 2018), https://doi.org/10.22599/book1. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","practice","art","europe"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Star_Carr_headdress_cropped.JPG/1920px-Star_Carr_headdress_cropped.JPG","title":"Star Carr","desc":"Star Carr collection at Yorkshire museum - mesolithic headdress made from deer skull. From the earliest known post glacial settlement in England. Jonathan Cardy, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Star_Carr_headdress_cropped.JPG","alt":"Star Carr headdress cropped"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Star Carr","at":[-0.4233,54.2142]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Star Carr worth as much as it is?","a":"Because it was not a stopping place but one returned to for something like 800 years, between about 9300 and 8500 BCE — so it records a way of living rather than a single visit."},{"q":"Why is ordinary carpentry a rare glimpse here?","a":"Because wood of that age almost never survives. The platform of large split timbers along the water's edge is the earliest post-built structure known anywhere in Britain."},{"q":"Why is a whole class of artefact effectively one site's evidence?","a":"Because Star Carr holds more than 90 per cent of every red deer antler headdress known from prehistoric Europe — 33 in all, of which only nine are pierced."}]},
{"id":"wh-111","num":111,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cultivated crops and domesticated animals rather than wild food alone are what define the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last phase of the Stone Age before metal.","questions":["Because farming was taken up independently in at least eleven separate centres, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no single span and begins thousands of years apart around the world.","Villages of a few hundred growing into settlements of thousands, with land tenure and hardening rank, are how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> changed the way people lived together."],"answer":"Neolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 – 3300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Near East; later elsewhere</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic</b>, or New Stone Age, is the last phase of the Stone Age, the one in which people came to live on cultivated crops and domesticated animals rather than on wild food alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Lubbock coined the name in 1865, in a scheme of four ages with no Mesolithic in it, and said plainly at the time that he was applying it to Europe only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Domestication for food began around the world between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age released its grip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began independently in at least eleven separate centres, each working on the plants and animals it had to hand rather than on any shared stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The changes did not start with the period itself, since the Epipalaeolithic of 23,000 to 12,000 years ago already shows them gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The largest early farming villages held populations in the hundreds, and as the Neolithic wore on settlements in the thousands arose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With that size came land ownership and rights of tenure attached to emerging elites, so rank hardened into something inherited rather than earned within a lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its dead were often laid in graves built of massive stones, whether roofed chambers, passage graves or long galleries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the last third of the 5th millennium BCE those tombs could be opened again, and bodies were added to them across centuries, which makes them monuments to a line rather than to a person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole change was called a revolution in 1936, on an analogy with the industrial one, though it is now read as a transition slow enough that nobody living through it need have noticed a thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["John Lubbock, <i>Pre-historic Times, as Illustrated by Ancient Remains, and the Manners and Customs of Modern Savages</i> (London: Williams and Norgate, 1865), 2–3, https://archive.org/details/prehistorictime07lubbgoog. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]","Trevor Watkins, “From Pleistocene to Holocene: The Prehistory of Southwest Asia in Evolutionary Context,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 39, no. 3 (2017): 22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5556129/. [Open access]","Bettina Schulz Paulsson, “Radiocarbon Dates and Bayesian Modeling Support Maritime Diffusion Model for Megaliths in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 9 (2019): 3460–65, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6397522/. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","agriculture","history","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Neolithic_Stone_Spades_%26_Axes.jpg/1920px-Neolithic_Stone_Spades_%26_Axes.jpg","title":"Neolithic","desc":"Xinzheng City Museum, Henan Province, China. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neolithic_Stone_Spades_%26_Axes.jpg","alt":"Neolithic Stone Spades & Axes"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why did domestication begin in at least eleven separate centres?","a":"Because each worked on the plants and animals it had to hand rather than on any shared stock, as the last ice age released its grip between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why did rank harden as settlements grew?","a":"Because with size came land ownership and rights of tenure attached to emerging elites, so rank became something inherited rather than earned within a lifetime."},{"q":"Why are the great stone tombs monuments to a line rather than a person?","a":"Because from the last third of the 5th millennium BCE they could be opened again, and bodies were added to them across centuries."}]},
{"id":"wh-112","num":112,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Sedentism, cemeteries and architecture appear in the Levant before farming does, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better described as a transition than an event.","questions":["Happening independently in at least eleven separate centres, each with its own plants and animals, is what makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a repeated event rather than a single one.","A livestock disease splitting from its cattle-infecting relative about 9,800 years ago is among the prices of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, paid in penned animals and crowded settlements."],"answer":"Neolithic Revolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Transition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 10,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 11, independent</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic Revolution","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic Revolution</b> is the shift from hunting and gathering to farming and settled village life, a social, economic and technological transformation rather than merely a change of diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Settling down came first, though: the Natufian of the southern Levant, between about 14,500 and 11,600 years ago, already had sedentism, cemeteries and architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had begun on animals too, since the Natufian already shows food production including animal domestication, and at 'Ain Mallaha an adult woman was buried with a juvenile domestic dog, with a second such burial from Hayonim Terrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The traits we file under the word Neolithic emerged and developed across the Epipalaeolithic, between 23,000 and 12,000 years ago, instead of arriving with the crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cultivation for food followed between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age let go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It then happened again and again, independently, in at least eleven separate centres, each working the plants and animals of its own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it took hold the settlements grew, the largest early farming villages holding populations in the hundreds across the whole Old World, and later ones running into the thousands as the period wore on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Land ownership and rights of tenure came with them, attached to emerging elites, so that rank began to be inherited rather than won inside a single life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was a price, and part of it was disease: the bacterium behind brucellosis in sheep and goats split from its cattle-infecting relative about 9,800 years ago, exactly as livestock economies were consolidating and spreading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The settlement itself supplied the mechanism, since penning and confinement, thin hygiene among new herders, denser herds and the mixing of species that had never met all favour a pathogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4035915/. [Open access]","Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]","Trevor Watkins, “From Pleistocene to Holocene: The Prehistory of Southwest Asia in Evolutionary Context,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 39, no. 3 (2017): 22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5556129/. [Open access]","Louis L'Hôte et al., “An 8000 Years Old Genome Reveals the Neolithic Origin of the Zoonosis <i>Brucella melitensis</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 6132, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11271283/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","prehistory","agriculture","archaeology","history","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Egyptian_Domesticated_Animals.jpg/1920px-Egyptian_Domesticated_Animals.jpg","title":"Neolithic Revolution","desc":"Old Egyptian hieroglyphic painting showing an early instance of a domesticated animal (cow being milked). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_Domesticated_Animals.jpg","alt":"Egyptian Domesticated Animals"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why is settling down not simply a consequence of farming?","a":"Because settling down came first. The Natufian of the southern Levant, between about 14,500 and 11,600 years ago, already had sedentism, cemeteries and architecture, while cultivation for food followed only between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why did the shift to farming bring new diseases with it?","a":"Because the settlement itself supplied the mechanism: penning and confinement, thin hygiene among new herders, denser herds and the mixing of species that had never met all favour a pathogen. The bacterium behind brucellosis split from its cattle-infecting relative about 9,800 years ago."},{"q":"Why is this not a single event with one birthplace?","a":"Because it happened again and again, independently, in at least eleven separate centres, each working the plants and animals of its own country — and the traits filed under the word emerged across the Epipalaeolithic rather than arriving with the crops."}],"leadsTo":[{"id":"wh-133","how":"Numbers rose after the crops arrived rather than before them, driven by fertility rather than by survival."},{"id":"wh-170","how":"Childe made the farming surplus the engine of the first cities, though later evidence has complicated that sequence."}]},
{"id":"wh-115","num":115,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Curving from the Mediterranean round to the Persian Gulf, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a cultivable fringe with mountains on one side and desert on the other.","questions":["Ancient genomes show the first farmers of the southern Levant and of the Zagros each descended from local hunter-gatherers, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took up farming from within.","The local Epipalaeolithic runs from about 23,000 years ago, so people had been living in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for ten millennia before anyone there planted a crop."],"answer":"Fertile Crescent","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Farming begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 11,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and more than once within it</span></div>","answerText":"Fertile Crescent","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Fertile Crescent</b> is the cultivable fringe of the western Asian desert, a borderland between desert and mountains with the mountains on one side of it and the dry country on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs as an approximate semicircle, its western end at the southeast corner of the Mediterranean, its centre directly north of Arabia and its eastern end at the head of the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a modern invention made because there was no other: no geographical or political term covered the whole semicircle, so one was coined for it, lower-case in 1914 and capitalised as a proper name two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> People had been living there a very long time before they farmed it, the Epipalaeolithic of the southern Levant running from roughly 23,000 to 11,600 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its final phase, the Natufian of about 14,500 to 11,600 years ago, already had sedentism, cemeteries and architecture, so the settling came before the sowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Domestication for food then began between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age released its hold on the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> This was one of at least eleven separate centres where that happened independently, so the crescent is a first among several rather than the single place farming was invented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes have since been read from 44 people of the region spanning roughly 12,000 to 1,400 BCE, running from Natufian hunter-gatherers through to farmers of the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They show that the first farmers of the southern Levant and the first farmers of the Zagros mountains in Iran each descended from the hunter-gatherers of their own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Farming here was therefore taken up by the people already living in the land rather than carried in by newcomers, and taken up more than once inside the crescent itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["James Henry Breasted and James Harvey Robinson, <i>Outlines of European History, Part I</i> (Boston: Ginn, 1914), 56–57, https://archive.org/details/outlinesofeurope01robi. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Times: A History of the Early World</i> (Boston: Ginn, 1916), 100–101, https://archive.org/details/ancienttimeshist00brea_2. [Open access]","Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1323964111. [Paywalled]","Iosif Lazaridis et al., “Genomic Insights into the Origin of Farming in the Ancient Near East,” <i>Nature</i> 536, no. 7617 (2016): 419–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/nature19310. [Paywalled]"],"tags":["place","geography","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/NLI_Quarta_Asie_tabula.jpg/1920px-NLI_Quarta_Asie_tabula.jpg","title":"Fertile Crescent","desc":"Map of the levant by Ptolemy, 1486 reproduction by Johann Reger. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NLI_Quarta_Asie_tabula.jpg","alt":"NLI Quarta Asie tabula"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Fertile Crescent","at":[40,36],"kind":"region","area":[[34.2,31.3],[35,33.5],[36,36],[38.5,37.4],[41.5,37.6],[44.5,36.5],[46.5,33.5],[48.3,30.4],[47.3,30.3],[45.3,33.3],[43.3,35.5],[40.5,36.3],[38,36.2],[36.6,34.5],[36,32],[35.4,31.2]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the name have to be invented?","a":"Because no geographical or political term covered the whole semicircle, so one was coined for it — lower-case in 1914 and capitalised as a proper name two years later."},{"q":"Why is the crescent a first among several rather than the birthplace of farming?","a":"Because domestication for food began independently in at least eleven separate centres between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, each working on the plants and animals it had to hand."},{"q":"Why do the ancient genomes show farming was not carried in?","a":"Because the first farmers of the southern Levant and the first farmers of the Zagros mountains each descended from the hunter-gatherers of their own country — so it was taken up by the people already living there, and more than once inside the crescent."}]},
{"id":"wh-116","num":116,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"People of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built semi-subterranean round houses of undressed limestone into the slope at 'Ain Mallaha, where 50 to 100 of them lived for 3,000 years.","questions":["Use-wear on flint sickle blades shows the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> harvesting cereals through a long spring, some 4,500 years before any of those cereals was domesticated.","Settling down came before farming, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the phase in which it happened: cemeteries, architecture and stone houses, with the crops still wild."],"answer":"Natufian culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Culture</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,500 – 11,600 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ain Mallaha</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 15,000 – 12,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"Natufian culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Natufian culture</b> is the last phase of the Epipalaeolithic in the Levant, running from about 14,500 to 11,600 years ago, and it is where people settled down before they took up farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is read as a transitional entity, marking the beginning of the change away from the mobile foraging bands of the Palaeolithic, and one study brackets it slightly wider, at 15,000 to 11,650 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clearest of its villages is 'Ain Mallaha in the north of what is now Israel, occupied for some 3,000 years between roughly 15,000 and 12,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It covered about 2,000 square metres (half an acre) and is estimated to have held between 50 and 100 people at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its houses were semi-subterranean and curved rather than square, made of undressed limestone, cut back into the slope with retaining walls to hold the hillside off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They were reaping cereals long before anyone had changed them, since wear on the flint blades of their sickles, from el-Wad Terrace and from Salibiya I, shows the cutting of grain both semi-ripe and ripe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pattern is of a protracted harvest beginning in mid-spring and running to the very end of it, which is a season worked through rather than a windfall gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is cultivation roughly 4,500 years before those same cereals were domesticated, so the crops were being tended long before they were altered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Staying put brought the rest with it: cemeteries among the houses, architecture worth maintaining, and a beginning made on the domestication of animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor did it end when the period did, because at 'Ain Mallaha the occupation runs on unbroken, with settlement deepening and finds carrying over from the late Natufian into the Neolithic that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lisa A. Maher et al., “A Unique Human-Fox Burial from a Pre-Natufian Cemetery in the Levant (Jordan),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 1 (2011): e15815, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3027631/. [Open access]","Laurent Davin et al., “Modeling Identities among the First-Sedentary Communities: Emergence of Clay Personal Ornaments in Epipaleolithic Southwest Asia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12 (2026): eaea2158, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12998513/. [Open access]","Gil Haklay and Avi Gopher, “A New Look at Shelter 131/51 in the Natufian Site of Eynan (Ain-Mallaha), Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0130121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4496039/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Jonathan Santana et al., “Multi-Isotope Evidence of Population Aggregation in the Natufian and Scant Migration during the Early Neolithic of the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 11857, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8178372/. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","agriculture","history","practice"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Basalt_%26_Limestone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Natufian_Culture.jpg/1920px-Basalt_%26_Limestone_Mortar_%26_Pestle%2C_Natufian_Culture.jpg","title":"Natufian culture","desc":"Basalt & Limestone Mortar & Pestle, Natufian Culture Israel Museum, Jerusalem, Israel. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basalt_%26_Limestone_Mortar_%26_Pestle,_Natufian_Culture.jpg","alt":"Basalt & Limestone Mortar & Pestle, Natufian Culture"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Natufian culture","at":[35.0436,31.9819],"kind":"region","area":[[34.3,31.2],[34.6,32.6],[35.2,33.6],[36,34.8],[37.2,35.6],[38.3,35],[38,33.8],[36.9,32.6],[36.2,31.3],[35.3,30.2],[34.6,30.4]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Natufian read as a transitional culture?","a":"Because it marks the beginning of the change away from the mobile foraging bands of the Palaeolithic: cemeteries among the houses, architecture worth maintaining, and a beginning made on the domestication of animals."},{"q":"Why does the wear on their sickle blades matter?","a":"Because it shows the cutting of grain both semi-ripe and ripe, in a protracted harvest running from mid-spring to the end of it — a season worked through rather than a windfall gathered."},{"q":"Why does the settling come before the sowing?","a":"Because they were reaping cereals roughly 4,500 years before those same cereals were domesticated, so the villages, the cemeteries and the stone houses all precede any altered crop."}]},
{"id":"wh-117","num":117,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Tameness, coat colour changes, floppy ears, smaller teeth and smaller brains are the suite of traits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaves on a mammal, whatever the species.","questions":["Cereals were reaped for some 4,500 years before they changed, which is the clearest sign that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a protracted process rather than a decision anyone took.","Loss of seed shattering, larger seeds, less branching and reduced dormancy make up the plant side of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and crops from unrelated centres converge on it."],"answer":"domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">For food, from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 11,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plant species</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 – 2,500, in 120 – 160 families</span></div>","answerText":"domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Domestication</b> is the process by which a wild species is reshaped under human keeping, and the first thing to understand about it is that it is typically protracted, shaped more by unconscious selection than by any intentional breeding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nobody set out to do it, in other words, and the gap between using a species and changing it can be enormous: Natufian sickles were reaping wild cereals some 4,500 years before those grains were domesticated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Domestication for food began between 12,000 and 11,000 years ago, as the last ice age ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began independently in at least eleven separate centres, each working on whatever grew or grazed nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1,000 and 2,500 plant species have been at least partly domesticated since, drawn from something like 120 to 160 plant families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What makes it a single subject is that it leaves the same marks on unrelated creatures, a pattern noticed by Darwin and still called the domestication syndrome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In mammals that means tameness and docility, changes of coat colour, smaller teeth, altered skull shape, floppy ears and tails, breeding out of season, juvenile behaviour carried into adulthood, and a reduction in brain size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parts of the same suite turn up in domesticated birds and fish, which is why it is read as a property of the process rather than of any one lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants have their own version of it: loss of seed shattering, larger seeds, less branching, reduced dormancy and shifted flowering time, all of which make a crop easier to harvest and useless at sowing itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Crops from centres with no contact between them converge on that suite, often through the very same genes, and what began in these fields ended in the first cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Adam S. Wilkins, Richard W. Wrangham, and W. Tecumseh Fitch, “The ‘Domestication Syndrome’ in Mammals: A Unified Explanation Based on Neural Crest Cell Behavior and Genetics,” <i>Genetics</i> 197, no. 3 (2014): 795–808, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4096361/. [Open access]","Yu Cao, Jianbing Yan, Jeffrey Ross-Ibarra, and Ning Yang, “Plant Domestication Revisited: Genomic Insights into Origins, Mechanisms, and Convergent Evolution,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 1 (2026): 114062, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12799793/. [Open access]","Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1323964111. [Paywalled]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","evolution","science","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Wheat_domestication_illustrated_-_Vavilov_Institute_of_Plant_Industry.jpg","title":"domestication","desc":"Wheat domestication illustrated. Luigi Guarino from Rome, Italy, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wheat_domestication_illustrated_-_Vavilov_Institute_of_Plant_Industry.jpg","alt":"Wheat domestication illustrated - Vavilov Institute of Plant Industry"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why is domestication described as unconscious rather than intended?","a":"Because it is typically protracted and shaped more by unconscious selection than by any deliberate breeding — nobody set out to do it, and the gap between using a species and changing it can run to thousands of years."},{"q":"Why is the domestication syndrome read as a property of the process?","a":"Because the same marks turn up on unrelated creatures — tameness, changed coat colour, smaller teeth, floppy ears, breeding out of season, reduced brain size — and parts of the suite appear in domesticated birds and fish too."},{"q":"Why does a domesticated plant make a good crop and a poor wild plant?","a":"Because it loses seed shattering, grows larger seeds, branches less, has reduced dormancy and shifts its flowering time — all of which make it easier to harvest and useless at sowing itself."}]},
{"id":"wh-118","num":118,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cereals, winter pulses and one oil plant travelled together out of the Fertile Crescent as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, rather than spreading one species at a time.","questions":["Einkorn and emmer wheat most likely come from the Karaca Dağ range in southeast Turkey, which is one corner of where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were first brought under cultivation.","Flax is the odd member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, being grown for oil and fibre rather than for food, and taken first for the oil."],"answer":"Neolithic founder crops","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in and around the Fertile Crescent</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic founder crops","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic founder crops</b> are the small group of plants first brought under cultivation in and around the Fertile Crescent, and what makes them a group rather than a list is that they travelled as a package, turning up together wherever farming spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cereals are its core: by the early Neolithic, about 10,000 years ago, three wheats had been domesticated there, the diploid einkorn, the tetraploid emmer and hexaploid bread wheat, with barley beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Einkorn and emmer most likely came from the Karaca Dağ range in what is now southeast Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Barley may have more than one origin, with centres both in the Fertile Crescent and on the Iranian plateau, and emmer may have been domesticated a second time in the southern Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The package is therefore not one event in one valley but a set of overlapping ones across a wide arc of country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Pulses went with the grain, lentils and peas making up the winter half of the package as it moved east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pea has two wild relatives, one spread across the Mediterranean and one confined to the Levant, so even a single crop can carry a complicated parentage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Flax is the odd member, grown not for food but for oil and fibre, and domesticated about 10,000 years ago from pale flax, its wild progenitor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The genetic work suggests it was taken first for the oil rather than for the thread, which is the reverse of what the plant is now known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of it appeared from nothing, since the wild cereals of the same country had been reaped with flint sickles for roughly 4,500 years before any of them was altered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Yu Cao, Jianbing Yan, Jeffrey Ross-Ibarra, and Ning Yang, “Plant Domestication Revisited: Genomic Insights into Origins, Mechanisms, and Convergent Evolution,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 1 (2026): 114062, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12799793/. [Open access]","Jonas Gregorio de Souza, Javier Ruiz-Pérez, Carla Lancelotti, and Marco Madella, “Environmental Effects on the Spread of the Neolithic Crop Package to South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 7 (2022): e0268482, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9273075/. [Open access]","Yong-Bi Fu, “Flax Domestication Processes as Inferred from Genome-Wide SNP Data,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 8731, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11906640/. [Open access]","Oldřich Trněný et al., “Molecular Evidence for Two Domestication Events in the Pea Crop,” <i>Genes</i> 9, no. 11 (2018): 535, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6265838/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","nature","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Triticum_monococcum_illustration_%2801f%29.png/1920px-Triticum_monococcum_illustration_%2801f%29.png","title":"Neolithic founder crops","desc":"Triticum monococcum (Einkorn wheat) from Icones et descriptiones Graminum austriacorum Author: Host, Nikolaus Thomas Publisher: Vindobonae : A. Schmidt, 1801-1809. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Triticum_monococcum_illustration_(01f).png","alt":"Triticum monococcum illustration (01f)"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are the founder crops treated as a group rather than a list?","a":"Because they travelled as a package, turning up together wherever farming spread — the cereals, the pulses and flax move as a set rather than each spreading on its own."},{"q":"Why does the package not point to a single valley of origin?","a":"Because the origins overlap across a wide arc: einkorn and emmer point to the Karaca Dağ range, barley may have centres in both the Fertile Crescent and the Iranian plateau, and emmer may have been domesticated a second time in the southern Levant."},{"q":"Why is flax the odd member of the package?","a":"Because it was grown not for food but for oil and fibre, and the genetic work suggests it was taken first for the oil rather than the thread, which is the reverse of what the plant is now known for."}]},
{"id":"wh-119","num":119,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Wild wheat and barley break up along the spike and scatter their grain, so the first thing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to undo was the plant's own way of sowing itself.","questions":["Two genes called Brittle rachis 1 and 2 were the target of the mutations that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible, and they are found only in the wheat and barley tribe.","One region does not fit the slow picture of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since in southeast Anatolia the wheat appears to have changed faster than that model predicts."],"answer":"cereal domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Key genes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Brittle rachis 1 and 2</span></div>","answerText":"cereal domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cereal domestication</b> turned plants that scatter their own seed into plants that hold it, and the loss of that natural mode of grain dispersal is perhaps the single most critical event in the whole process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A wild wheat or barley breaks up progressively along the spike as it ripens and dries, dropping its grain on the ground where the next generation can grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two genes govern that shattering in the wild progenitors, Brittle rachis 1 and Brittle rachis 2, and both were the target of the mutations that domesticated the crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are found only in the tribe that contains wheat and barley, and are thought to have arisen by a gene duplication whose copy then took on a new job.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tough spike sits alongside larger seeds and reduced dormancy in the plant side of the domestication syndrome, and it leaves the crop unable to sow itself at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Harvesting is what did the selecting, and it had been going on a long time, flint sickles reaping wild cereals for some 4,500 years before any of them changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The usual picture is accordingly a slow one, shaped more by unconscious selection than by any deliberate breeding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where it happened is plural too, einkorn and emmer pointing to the Karaca Dağ range in southeast Turkey while barley may have a second origin on the Iranian plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One region does not fit the slow picture: grain measurements at an early Neolithic site in southeast Anatolia, set beside the plant remains and the dates, suggest wheat there changed faster, with no long stage of cultivating it wild beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The explanation offered is a social one, that cereals were mattering more at feasts in settlements whose architecture grew steadily more elaborate across the 11th millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mohammad Pourkheirandish and Takao Komatsuda, “Grain Disarticulation in Wild Wheat and Barley,” <i>Plant and Cell Physiology</i> 63, no. 11 (2022): 1584–91, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9680857/. [Open access]","Yu Cao, Jianbing Yan, Jeffrey Ross-Ibarra, and Ning Yang, “Plant Domestication Revisited: Genomic Insights into Origins, Mechanisms, and Convergent Evolution,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 1 (2026): 114062, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12799793/. [Open access]","Iris Groman-Yaroslavski et al., “Natufian Sickle Blades (ca. 15,000–11,700 cal. BP) Reveal Cereal Cultivation ca. 4.5 Millennia before Domestication,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-54740-5. [Open access]","Ceren Kabukcu, Eleni Asouti, Nadja Pöllath, Joris Peters, and Necmi Karul, “Pathways to Plant Domestication in Southeast Anatolia Based on New Data from Aceramic Neolithic Gusir Höyük,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11, no. 1 (2021): 2112, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7822836/. [Open access]","Jonas Gregorio de Souza, Javier Ruiz-Pérez, Carla Lancelotti, and Marco Madella, “Environmental Effects on the Spread of the Neolithic Crop Package to South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 7 (2022): e0268482, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9273075/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","science","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Various_grains.jpg/1920px-Various_grains.jpg","title":"cereal domestication","desc":"Oats, barley, and some products made from them. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Various_grains.jpg","alt":"Various grains"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the loss of natural seed dispersal the critical change?","a":"Because a wild spike breaks up as it ripens and drops its grain where the next generation can grow; a tough spike holds the seed for the harvester and leaves the crop unable to sow itself at all."},{"q":"Why is cereal domestication usually pictured as slow?","a":"Because harvesting is what did the selecting and it had been going on a long time, with flint sickles reaping wild cereals for some 4,500 years before any of them changed, shaped more by unconscious selection than deliberate breeding."},{"q":"Why does one Anatolian site not fit that slow picture?","a":"Because grain measurements there, set beside the plant remains and the dates, suggest wheat changed faster with no long stage of cultivating it wild — explained socially, by cereals mattering more at feasts in increasingly elaborate settlements."}]},
{"id":"wh-120","num":120,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Because a herder shows up in the age and sex of the animals killed rather than in the shape of their bones, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is hard to date from a skeleton alone.","questions":["The oldest of the three independent cradles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Southwest Asia, where sheep, goats, pigs and cattle all followed the cereals and pulses into settled farming villages.","Pigs on the Lower Yangtze were eating cooked food and human waste, a commensal route into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which the animal moves towards the settlement rather than being driven in."],"answer":"animal domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Livestock</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,500 – 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Llama, alpaca</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 – 8,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Horse, camel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,000 – 2,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"animal domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Animal domestication</b> is a coevolutionary relationship rather than a single act, and the globally important livestock were taken up inside settled farming communities, in three independent cradles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Southwest Asia is the oldest: sheep, goats, pigs and cattle all followed the cereals and pulses there, between about 10,500 and 10,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cattle descend from the aurochs, a wild ox now extinct, in two lineages: the taurine cattle of Southwest Asia and the humped zebu traced to a South Asian population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> China is the second cradle, where pigs followed millet and rice, and the Andes the third, where llamas and alpacas were taken between 9,000 and 8,000 years ago, with the turkey added in North America later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A further wave was about muscle rather than meat, the donkey, horse and camel arriving between 5,000 and 2,000 years ago and transforming transport, trade and the scale of warfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dating the start is hard, because a herder shows up first in the age and sex of the animals killed rather than in the shape of their bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Ganj Dareh in the Zagros only about a fifth of the goats lived past four years, and 60 to 70 per cent of males were killed before two and a half while as many females survived beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomes from those animals are already distinct from wild goats by around 8200 BCE, the oldest livestock genomes yet recovered, and visible change in the skeleton follows roughly a thousand years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aşıklı Höyük in central Anatolia gives the same lesson from the other end: a millennium of managing sheep and goats, from catching wild lambs and kids to grow on through to large captive herds, produced no reduction in body size at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor was there one route in, since the pigs at two early sites on the Lower Yangtze were eating cooked food and human waste, a commensal path along which the animal moves towards the settlement rather than being driven in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Thomas Cucchi and Benjamin Arbuckle, “Animal Domestication: From Distant Past to Current Development and Issues,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 6–9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214435/. [Open access]","Mary C. Stiner, Natalie D. Munro, Hijlke Buitenhuis, Güneş Duru, and Mihriban Özbaşaran, “An Endemic Pathway to Sheep and Goat Domestication at Aşıklı Höyük (Central Anatolia, Turkey),” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 4 (2022): e2110930119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8795544/. [Open access]","Kevin G. Daly et al., “Herded and Hunted Goat Genomes from the Dawn of Domestication in the Zagros Mountains,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 25 (2021): e2100901118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8237664/. [Open access]","Benjamin S. Arbuckle and Theo M. Kassebaum, “Management and Domestication of Cattle (<i>Bos taurus</i>) in Neolithic Southwest Asia,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 10–19, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214434/. [Open access]","Jiajing Wang et al., “Early Evidence for Pig Domestication (8,000 cal. BP) in the Lower Yangtze, South China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 24 (2025): e2507123122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12184640/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","nature","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Haustiere_III_in_Brockhaus_1937.jpg","title":"animal domestication","desc":"Haustiere III in Brockhaus 1937. scan: Julo. 1937, scan: 2010. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haustiere_III_in_Brockhaus_1937.jpg","alt":"Haustiere III in Brockhaus 1937"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why is the start of animal domestication hard to date?","a":"Because a herder shows up first in the age and sex of the animals killed rather than in the shape of their bones — at Ganj Dareh most males died before two and a half while females survived, long before any skeletal change."},{"q":"Why does Aşıklı Höyük show that body size is a poor test?","a":"Because a millennium of managing sheep and goats there, from catching wild lambs to keeping large captive herds, produced no reduction in body size at all."},{"q":"Why is there no single route into domestication?","a":"Because the pigs at two early Lower Yangtze sites were eating cooked food and human waste, a commensal path along which the animal moves towards the settlement rather than being driven into it."}]},
{"id":"gr-216","num":216,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Settlers from Phocaea founded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about 600 BCE, and the cults gathered there point back to several places in Ionia rather than to their own mother city alone.","questions":["Two boats built about 550 BCE were left on the shore at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one hull sewn together and the other joined by mortise and tenon.","Pytheas, who sailed north as far as Britain and wrote a lost book <i>On the Ocean</i>, was a citizen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Greek window on the Gallic world."],"answer":"Massalia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell to Caesar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">49 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Massalia</b> is the Greek city on the site of Marseille, founded about 600 BCE by settlers from Phocaea in Ionia, and the most important Greek city of the far west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its character was Ionian before it was Phocaean: the cults gathered there point back to particular places in Ionia — Athena from Phocaea, Apollo Delphinios from Miletus, Artemis of Ephesus — and Phocaean traits of its own surface only in the second half of the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not one of its temples has been found, so the sanctuaries are placed only from what Strabo and Justin wrote long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town lay on a strip of land between a sheltered inlet and three hills, the highest 42 metres (140 feet) above the sea and probably the acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its foundation story has been told so often that scholars now say it has blinded the historiography of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It lived by trade, the Greek window on the Gallic world and a port for tin, slaves and wine, one of a chain of Phocaean stations reaching Gallic and Tartessian resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Wine and the vessels that came with it changed the peoples inland, who had a hand in the change themselves, taking imported and hybrid wares into pottery of their own in shapes that suited existing habits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor was it the territory-less trading post it was long taken for: vineyards were laid out east of the city on a planned system, one at Saint-Jean-du-Désert about 5 kilometres (3 miles) from the old harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The harbour had a quay by the 6th century BCE, and two boats built about the middle of it were left on the shore, one hull sewn and the other joined by mortise and tenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle thought the place worth a <i>Constitution of the Massaliots</i>, now lost, the city kept a treasury at Delphi, and its citizen Pytheas sailed north to Britain and beyond, writing a lost book <i>On the Ocean</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Massalia","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","france","geography"],"sources":["Christine Davidson, review of <i>The Oxford History of the Archaic Greek World. Vol. IV: Cyrene to Metapontion</i>, ed. Paul Cartledge and Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.03.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.03.24/. [Open access]","Ulrike Krotscheck, review of <i>Greek Marseille and Mediterranean Celtic Region</i>, ed. Sophie Bouffier and Dominique Garcia, H-France Review 18, no. 204 (2018), https://h-france.net/vol18reviews/vol18no204krotscheck.pdf. [Open access]","Institut national de recherches archéologiques préventives, “L’Antiquité grecque à Marseille,” Atlas archéologique de Marseille, https://multimedia.inrap.fr/atlas/marseille/synthese-periodes/antiquite-grecque. [Open access]","Timothy Teeter, review of <i>Ancient Greek France</i>, by A. Trevor Hodge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1999.11.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.11.17/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]","Tamar Hodos, review of <i>Greek Colonisation: An Account of Greek Colonies and Other Settlements Overseas</i>, vol. 1, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.08.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.08.38/. [Open access]","Jerker Blomqvist, review of <i>Pythéas: Explorateur du Grand Nord</i>, by François Herbaux, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.01.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.01.38/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Marseille_-_Le_jardin_des_vestiges.JPG/1920px-Marseille_-_Le_jardin_des_vestiges.JPG","title":"Massalia","desc":"The Jardin des Vestiges at Marseille, where the quays and fortifications of the harbour of ancient Massalia stand in the middle of the modern city.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marseille_-_Le_jardin_des_vestiges.JPG","alt":"Marseille - Le jardin des vestiges"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Massalia","at":[5.37,43.2964]},"why":[{"q":"Why is its character called Ionian before Phocaean?","a":"Because the cults gathered there point back to particular places in Ionia — Athena from Phocaea, Apollo Delphinios from Miletus, Artemis of Ephesus — and Phocaean traits surface only later."},{"q":"Why are its sanctuaries placed only from literature?","a":"Because not one of its temples has been found, so what is known of them comes from Strabo and Justin writing long afterwards."},{"q":"Why was it not the territory-less trading post it was taken for?","a":"Because vineyards were laid out east of the city on a planned system, one at Saint-Jean-du-Désert about 5 kilometres from the old harbour."}]},
{"id":"gr-217","num":217,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Strabo says the Greeks and the Iberians at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to share one walled town divided by an inner wall, under a legal code made of barbarian and Greek laws.","questions":["A lead sheet from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, written in Ionian Greek about 500 BCE, sets out a shipping deal a Phocaean merchant was handing to his agent there.","The Greeks at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began on an island the residents later called the old city, and moved across to the mainland after the middle of the 6th century BCE."],"answer":"Emporion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 575 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neapolis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman landing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Emporion</b> is the Greek trading town on the Bay of Roses in Catalonia, the most important Greek settlement on the Mediterranean coast of Iberia and the best known of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phocaean merchants working west out of Massalia settled there for good in the second quarter of the 6th century BCE, on a promontory local people had held since the late Bronze Age, where Phoenician goods had been arriving for a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo calls that first site the Palaia Polis, +m[1]+, and after mid-century a second nucleus grew on the mainland across the port hollow, now called the Neapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town stayed small — barely three or four hectares (7 to 10 acres) — and commercial, though it struck its own silver from the 5th century BCE, in fractions stamped +m[1]+ or +m[1]+.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even a rival Greek port lived off Etruscan goods: its 6th- and 5th-century houses gave up more Etruscan amphorae and bucchero than anyone expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo also describes an Iberian settlement beside the Greek one, later taken into a single town divided by an inner wall, under laws made of barbarian and Greek together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lead sheet from the Neapolis, in Ionian Greek of about 500 BCE, sets out a shipping deal a Phocaean merchant handed to his agent, part of it to be done near Sagunt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iberian names appear among the parties, and Iberian sgraffiti on pots and lead run through the town, so its inscriptions show Greeks and Iberians cooperating far more than the Roman-period writers do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It fed Attic pottery to the Iberian interior, though the Iberians may have decided who received it, and it was one of a string of small coastal ports rather than a bridgehead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its cemeteries, the one Phocaean case where burials survive in quantity, keep Greek and Iberian apart where the houses do not, and in 218 BCE its harbour was where a Roman army first landed in Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Emporion","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","spain","geography"],"sources":["Maite Miró and Marta Santos, “The Greek Presence on the East Coast of the Iberian Peninsula: Colonial Establishments and Rhythms of Trade with Iberian Societies,” <i>Catalan Historical Review</i> 7 (2014): 9–28, https://doi.org/10.2436/20.1000.01.95. [Open access]","Generalitat de Catalunya, “Empúries,” Cultural Heritage, https://patrimoni.gencat.cat/en/collection/empuries. [Open access]","Claudio Vacanti, review of <i>The Emporion in the Ancient Western Mediterranean: Trade and Colonial Encounters from the Archaic to the Hellenistic Period</i>, ed. Éric Gailledrat, Michael Dietler and Rosa Plana-Mallart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.09.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.09.45/. [Open access]","Andy Fear, review of <i>Colonial Encounters in Ancient Iberia: Phoenician, Greek, and Indigenous Relations</i>, ed. Michael Dietler and Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.05.43, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.05.43/. [Open access]","Jean MacIntosh Turfa, review of <i>Gli Etruschi da Genova ad Ampurias</i>, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.05.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.05.09/. [Open access]","Michael Eisman, review of <i>Greek Identity in the Western Mediterranean</i>, ed. Kathryn Lomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.36/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Greek_mosiac_at_Emp%C3%BAries.jpg/1920px-Greek_mosiac_at_Emp%C3%BAries.jpg","title":"Emporion","desc":"Ηδύκοιτος--\"The pleasure of lying down\"--can be seen at the top of the photograph.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek_mosiac_at_Emp%C3%BAries.jpg","alt":"Greek mosiac at Empúries"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Emporion","at":[3.1206,42.1347]},"why":[{"q":"Why do the town's inscriptions differ from the Roman-period writers?","a":"Because Iberian names appear among the parties to a shipping deal and Iberian sgraffiti run through the town, so they show Greeks and Iberians cooperating far more than the later accounts do."},{"q":"Why is the cemetery evidence important here?","a":"Because it is the one Phocaean case where burials survive in quantity, and it keeps Greek and Iberian apart where the houses do not."},{"q":"Why is even a Greek port's Etruscan pottery surprising?","a":"Because its 6th- and 5th-century houses gave up more Etruscan amphorae and bucchero than anyone expected of a rival's town."}]},
{"id":"gr-218","num":218,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The literary and the archaeological dates for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disagree by decades, Eusebius putting the first settlement on Berezan in 647 BCE where the earliest strata are of the last quarter of that century.","questions":["Miletus planted most of the cities of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the exception being Mesambria, the one Dorian polis on those shores, settled from Megara, Byzantium and Chalcedon.","The earliest material for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is very thin, about ten sherds for the second quarter of the 7th century BCE and ten more for the third."],"answer":"Black Sea colonisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Colonised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 6th centuries BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Berezan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">647 BCE in Eusebius, c. 625 by finds</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bosporan kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 5th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Black Sea colonisation</b> is the planting of Greek settlements round the Pontos, mostly in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, from Apollonia and Histria in the west to Phasis and Dioscurias in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miletus founded most of them, the exception being Mesambria, the one Dorian polis on those shores, settled from Megara, Byzantium and Chalcedon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The written sources are thin and mostly much later — Strabo, Arrian, Pseudo-Scylax, Stephanus of Byzantium — and they tell us as much about the world that wrote them as about the foundations they report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pseudo-Scymnus has Sinope founded by Thessalian heroes in the 8th century BCE and taken over by Milesians afterwards, which is the shape a good many of these traditions have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Berezan, ancient Borysthenes, between the mouths of the Dnieper and the Bug, was probably the first, and Eusebius dates it to 647 BCE where its earliest houses and graves are of the last quarter of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The material from before that is very thin, about ten sherds for the second quarter of the 7th century and ten more for the third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the last quarter pottery is plentiful at Berezan, at least three permanent settlements stood on the northern shore, and their people lived at first in dugouts and half-sunken huts before building above ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land was not empty: hand-made pottery shows people at Berezan before the Greeks came, Greek pots travelled inland to the hill fort of Nemirov, and on the Taman peninsula Maeotian groups from the Kuban camped beside a Greek settlement at harvest and worked its fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the settlers were after is argued over, the older answer being trade and the newer one land and population pressure at home, though neither alone covers the range of motives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the 5th century BCE the Bosporan kingdom grew up around Panticapaeum and its grain became vital to Athens, while Berezan itself declined as Olbia took the lead in its own region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Black Sea colonisation","tags":["event","practice","history","greece","archaic","migration"],"sources":["Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Greek Colonies in the Black Sea</i>, ed. Dimitrios V. Grammenos and Elias K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.09.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.09.01/. [Open access]","Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Berezan: The Architecture, History and Culture of the First Greek Colony in the Northern Black Sea</i>, by S. L. Solovyov, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.01/. [Open access]","D. Erciyas, review of <i>Hellenic Colonization in Euxeinos Pontos: Penetration, Early Establishment and the Problem of the “Emporion” Revisited</i>, by E. K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.05.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.05.24/. [Open access]","Dominik Delp, review of <i>Comparing Greek Colonies: Mobility and Settlement Consolidation from Southern Italy to the Black Sea (8th–6th Century BC)</i>, ed. Camilla Colombi and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.38/. [Open access]","Sara Owen, review of <i>The Greek Colonisation of the Black Sea Area: Historical Interpretation of Archaeology</i>, ed. Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.02.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.02.06/. [Open access]","Gocha R. Tsetskhladze, review of <i>Ancient Sacral Monuments in the Black Sea</i>, ed. Elias K. Petropoulos and Alexander A. Maslennikov, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.05.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.05.08/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/PontosGreekColonies.jpg","title":"Black Sea colonisation","desc":"A map of the Black Sea with the Greek colonies planted around its shores marked along the coast.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PontosGreekColonies.jpg","alt":"PontosGreekColonies"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why must the written sources be handled carefully?","a":"Because they are thin and mostly much later — Strabo, Arrian, Pseudo-Scylax, Stephanus — and tell us as much about the world that wrote them as about the foundations they report."},{"q":"Why is the earliest material at Berezan so hard to read?","a":"Because it is very thin, about ten sherds for the second quarter of the 7th century and ten more for the third, before the pottery becomes plentiful."},{"q":"Why is the land not to be thought of as empty?","a":"Because hand-made pottery shows people at Berezan before the Greeks came, Greek pots travelled inland to Nemirov, and Maeotian groups camped beside a Greek settlement at harvest and worked its fields."}]},
{"id":"gr-219","num":219,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The earliest coins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show a cow stepping over a dolphin, read as its claim to be where Io crossed the strait, a claim Chalcedon opposite made for itself.","questions":["A tenth on shipping was levied at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the 430s BCE, broke off when the city revolted against Athens in 411, and was reimposed by Alcibiades across the water in 409.","Currents and winds let ships through the Bosporus only at certain times, so they gathered in convoys near <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose wealth came of standing where they had to wait."],"answer":"Byzantium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE, from Megara</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First coins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rhodian war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">220 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 May 330 CE, by Constantine</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Byzantium</b> is the Greek city on the European side of the Bosporus, founded from Megara in the 7th century BCE and refounded by Constantine as his capital nearly a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Megara and Miletus appear to have colonised the Propontis and the Pontos together, since given where their foundations lie neither set of cities could have worked on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Byzantium became a mother city in its turn, joining Megara and Chalcedon in settling Mesambria, the one Dorian polis on the Black Sea coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What made the place was the strait: currents and winds let ships through only at certain times, so they gathered in convoys to wait, especially at Hieron on the Asian shore under the sanctuary of Zeus Ourios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest coins show a cow stepping over a dolphin, read as a claim to be where Io crossed, which Chalcedon opposite made for itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The city sits very high on the Athenian tribute lists, and that is now taken less as a measure of its wealth than as Athens making it the hinge of its control over the strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tenth on shipping was levied there from the 430s BCE, broke off when the city revolted in 411, and was put back by Alcibiades at Chrysopolis across the water in 409.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Athens it guaranteed passage itself, and when the Galatians of Tylis demanded sums four times its highest tribute assessment it reimposed the tenth, which brought Rhodes to war in 220 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its other wealth swam past it, the tunny and mackerel migrating between the Black Sea and the Aegean, netted and cured in shore works the city leased out under a monopoly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of what is known of the strait's places and stories comes from the <i>Anaplus Bospori</i> of Dionysius of Byzantium, whose Greek survives only in part, the rest read through a 16th-century Latin translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Byzantium","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","politics"],"sources":["Paul Kimball, review of <i>Byzantium and the Bosporus: A Historical Study, from the Seventh Century BC until the Foundation of Constantinople</i>, by Thomas Russell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.05.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.05.51/. [Open access]","Margarit Damyanov, review of <i>Mégarika: nouvelles recherches sur Mégare, les cités de la Propontide et du Pont-Euxin</i>, ed. Adrian Robu and Iulian Bîrzescu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.02.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.02.32/. [Open access]","Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Greek Colonies in the Black Sea</i>, ed. Dimitrios V. Grammenos and Elias K. Petropoulos, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.09.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.09.01/. [Open access]","Tommaso Braccini, review of <i>Dionysios von Byzanz, Anaplus Bospori: Die Fahrt auf dem Bosporos</i>, by Margarethe Billerbeck, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.07.05, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.07.05/. [Open access]","Kevin Feeney, review of <i>From Byzantium to Constantinople: An Urban History</i>, by John Matthews, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2026.01.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2026/2026.01.19/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/1784_Bocage_Map_of_The_Bosphorus_and_the_City_of_Byzantium_-_Istanbul_-_Constantinople_-_Geographicus_-_Bosphorus-white-1793.jpg/1920px-1784_Bocage_Map_of_The_Bosphorus_and_the_City_of_Byzantium_-_Istanbul_-_Constantinople_-_Geographicus_-_Bosphorus-white-1793.jpg","title":"Byzantium","desc":"This lovely map, depicting the Bosphorus and the city of Byzantium, was prepared by Jean-Denis Barbié du Bocage in 1784 for the “Travels of Anarcharsis”. The map shows the narrow naval passage from the Propontis (Sea of Marmara) to the Pontus Euxinus (Black Sea).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1784_Bocage_Map_of_The_Bosphorus_and_the_City_of_Byzantium_-_Istanbul_-_Constantinople_-_Geographicus_-_Bosphorus-white-1793.jpg","alt":"1784 Bocage Map of The Bosphorus and the City of Byzantium - Istanbul - Constantinople - Geographicus - Bosphorus-white-1793"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Byzantium","at":[28.9847,41.0153]},"why":[{"q":"Why did ships gather rather than pass straight through?","a":"Because currents and winds let them through only at certain times, so they waited in convoys, especially at Hieron under the sanctuary of Zeus Ourios."},{"q":"Why is the city's high tribute assessment read differently now?","a":"Because it is taken less as a measure of its wealth than as Athens making it the hinge of its control over the strait."},{"q":"Why did the city reimpose a toll centuries later?","a":"Because the Galatians of Tylis demanded sums four times its highest tribute assessment, and the tenth on shipping brought Rhodes to war in 220 BCE."}]},
{"id":"gr-220","num":220,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The bronze dolphins and arrowheads found in quantity at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are now read as small offerings to Apollo before they were ever used as money.","questions":["The island settlement of Berezan grew, spread its farmland along the Bug, and gave rise to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in time became a state and absorbed its own parent.","One reading of Greek trade with the Scythians inland from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Milesian city on the Bug estuary, puts slaves rather than grain at its centre."],"answer":"Olbia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 500 BCE, from Miletus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctuary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in use c. 600 BCE – c. 100 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Olbia</b> is the Milesian city on the estuary of the Bug in what is now southern Ukraine, and it became the leading Greek settlement of the northern Black Sea as Berezan out to seaward declined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of Berezan itself: as that island settlement worked its crafts and pushed its farmland up the river, Olbia was laid out on the mainland with cult buildings and houses from the start, and in time became a state that absorbed its own parent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands on the Bug rather than the larger Dnieper, and the people already living there and the networks they used are what shaped where the Greeks could settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its earliest houses were largely dugouts, a form that may be local rather than Greek, and indigenous pottery, crafts and building run right through the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its western religious precinct was in use from the 6th century BCE until the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What drew the Greeks is not settled: a fishing station, a farming settlement and a trading post have all been proposed, and so has the idea that the place was planted to supply Miletus with metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What went the other way is disputed as well, one account putting slaves rather than grain at the centre of Greek dealings with the Scythians in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The small bronze dolphins and arrowheads found in quantity here and along the western coast have long been taken for an early form of money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They fit no known pattern of how coinage develops, and are better read as small offerings to Apollo, the dolphin to Delphinios and the arrowhead to Ietros, which took on a second life as tokens of exchange once real coin arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Out in the fields the settlers were Greek: the countryside gives them as the dominant part of the population in the chora as in the city, with at most a few of the surrounding peoples among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olbia","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","ukraine","geography"],"sources":["Stanley Burstein, review of <i>The Cauldron of Ariantas: Studies Presented to A. N. Sceglov on the Occasion of His 70th Birthday</i>, ed. Pia Guldager Bilde, Jakob Munk Højte and Vladimir F. Stolba, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.11.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.11.06/. [Open access]","Emma Buckingham, review of <i>Greek Colonization in Local Contexts: Case Studies in Colonial Interactions</i>, ed. Jason Lucas, Carrie Ann Murray and Sara Owen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2020.10.57, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2020/2020.10.57/. [Open access]","John Brendan Knight, review of <i>Advances in Ancient Black Sea Studies: Historiography, Archaeology and Religion</i>, ed. Victor Cojocaru and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.03.02/. [Open access]","Elias K. Petropoulos, review of <i>Surveying the Greek Chora: The Black Sea Region in a Comparative Perspective</i>, ed. Pia Guldager Bilde and Vladimir F. Stolba, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.05.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.05.06/. [Open access]","Balbina Baebler, review of <i>Ancient Berezan: The Architecture, History and Culture of the First Greek Colony in the Northern Black Sea</i>, by S. L. Solovyov, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.01, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.01/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Olbia","at":[31.9036,46.6925]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the bronze dolphins and arrowheads not early coinage?","a":"Because they fit no known pattern of how coinage develops, and read better as offerings to Apollo — the dolphin to Delphinios, the arrowhead to Ietros — that later served as tokens of exchange."},{"q":"Why did the city rise as its parent settlement fell?","a":"Because Berezan worked its crafts and pushed its farmland up the river, and the mainland foundation laid out with cult buildings and houses from the start in time absorbed it."},{"q":"Why is what drew the Greeks there unsettled?","a":"Because a fishing station, a farming settlement, a trading post and a supply of metals for Miletus have all been proposed."}]},
{"id":"gr-221","num":221,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The earliest pottery showing firm occupation at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is dated about 615 BCE, forty years before the pharaoh Herodotus credits with founding it.","questions":["Greeks from nine cities kept a shared sanctuary at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and 22 dedications from it are addressed to the gods of the Hellenes rather than to any one city's god.","Amasis gave the Greeks who settled in Egypt a place of their own at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with its own internal organisation, though they remained subject to the king."],"answer":"Naucratis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 615 – 610 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amasis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">570 – 526 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Naucratis</b> is the Greek trading settlement in the Nile Delta, the first Greek town in Egypt and where Greeks from many cities lived and traded under an Egyptian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says Amasis gave it to them: somewhere to live for those who settled in Egypt, and land for shrines and altars for the merchants who did not want to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He calls it a polis, but that word can mean no more than a town, and what he describes is a separate Greek community with its own organisation, still subject to the king, as the Phoenicians at Memphis and the Jews at Elephantine were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The date is the standing problem: the earliest pottery showing firm occupation is put at about 615 to 610 BCE, forty years before Amasis, and one answer is that archaic Greek pottery is dated too early rather than that Herodotus is wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dedication from Priene by a mercenary who served under Psammetichus I keeps open the possibility that the place began under him instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its greatest sanctuary was the Hellenion, founded by nine cities together in Herodotus's list, which counts Rhodes as one where its three cities were still separate, so the founders may have been eleven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The site carries 22 dedications addressed simply to the gods of the Hellenes, far too many for a phrase used loosely, and a mark of one shared cult place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what makes it remarkable: it is the one archaic settlement where the idea of Hellenism is explicit, and the suggestion is that Egyptians saw Greeks from many cities as one kind of stranger, and the Greeks took that up as their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same pull worked a level down, the three cities of Rhodes counting as Rhodian here and carrying that back home with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Greeks kept their own gods in public, but the houses tell another story from the temples, Egyptian household religion having got well inside private life while the institutions held the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Naucratis","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","egypt","religion"],"sources":["Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis</i> and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Edward Harris, review of <i>La cité marchande</i>, by Alain Bresson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.09.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.09.40/. [Open access]","Marek Wecowski, review of <i>La naissance de la ville dans l’antiquité</i>, ed. Michel Reddé and others, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.18, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.18/. [Open access]","Meritxell Ferrer-Martín, review of <i>Negotiating Identity in the Ancient Mediterranean: The Archaic and Classical Greek Multiethnic Emporia</i>, by Denise Demetriou, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.08.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.08.23/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, ed. Jeffrey Spier, Timothy F. Potts and Sara E. Cole, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","Danielle L. Kellogg, review of <i>A Small Greek World: Networks in the Ancient Mediterranean</i>, by Irad Malkin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.12.60, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.12.60/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg/1920px-Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg","title":"Naucratis","desc":"Seated sphinx. Plate, Eastern Greek Orientalizing, 6th century BCE. From Naukratis in Egypt.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg","alt":"Plate sphinx BM GR1965.9-30.705"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Naucratis","at":[30.5925,30.9011]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Herodotus's word polis not enough to make it a city?","a":"Because that word can mean no more than a town, and what he describes is a separate Greek community with its own organisation, still subject to the king, as the Phoenicians at Memphis were."},{"q":"Why is the date of the settlement a standing problem?","a":"Because the earliest pottery showing firm occupation is put at about 615 to 610 BCE, forty years before Amasis, and one answer is that archaic Greek pottery is dated too early."},{"q":"Why is this the one archaic settlement where Hellenism is explicit?","a":"Because 22 dedications there are addressed simply to the gods of the Hellenes, far too many for a phrase used loosely, and the Hellenion was founded by nine cities together."}]},
{"id":"gr-222","num":222,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The best-known record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is five lines cut into the leg of a colossus at Abu Simbel by two men of Greek and Carian descent on campaign in Nubia.","questions":["Evidence for the Carians in Egypt is in fact fuller than the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the ancient literary sources leave the opposite impression.","Egypt gained new battlefield methods along with the men, since <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought the hoplite way of fighting with them from the reign of Psammetichus I onwards."],"answer":"Greek mercenaries in Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Psammetichus I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">664 – 610 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abu Simbel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">593 – 592 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Greek mercenaries in Egypt</b> are attested from the reign of Psammetichus I, and many of the Greek settlers who first appear in the country served in the armies of the pharaohs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The pharaohs of the 26th dynasty were among the three great employers of hired Greek soldiers in the archaic age, along with the tyrants of mainland Greece and those of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greeks, Carians and Cypriots turn up in Egypt as traders, settlers and soldiers alike, and Memphis became a city of Syrians, Phoenicians, Jews, Carians and Ionians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Carians who served beside them are in fact better documented than the Greeks, which is the reverse of the impression the written sources give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus is the chief of those sources and is often stretched further than he goes: he does not mention Assyrian rule over Egypt at all, and does not say Greeks fought for Persia against the last Saite king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A dedication at Priene by a man who had served under Psammetichus I shows how early the traffic ran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its best-known trace is at Abu Simbel, where Archon son of Amoibichos and Peleqos son of Eudamos cut five lines into the leg of one of the colossi of Ramesses II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They record the expedition Psammetichus II sent against the Nubians in 593 and 592 BCE, and the letter-forms and dialects of the graffiti around theirs show men of several different origins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What Egypt was buying was not only manpower, since the hoplite way of fighting was new and the pharaohs had reason to want it, whatever the economic terms of the arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek itself had no word for a mercenary, and the words used shifted as coined money spread, from <i>epikouros</i>, +m[1]+, to <i>misthophoros</i>, +m[1]+, and <i>xenos misthophoros</i>, +m[1]+.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Greek mercenaries in Egypt","tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic","egypt","warfare"],"sources":["Stefano Struffolino, “Inscription of Greek Mercenaries to Abu Simbel,” <i>Axon</i> 2, no. 1 (2018): 9–30, https://doi.org/10.30687/Axon/2532-6848/2018/01/001. [Open access]","Ian Worthington, review of <i>Greek Mercenaries: From the Late Archaic Period to Alexander</i>, by Matthew Trundle, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.04/. [Open access]","Nino Luraghi, review of <i>Mercenaries in the Classical World: To the Death of Alexander</i>, by Stephen English, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.12.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013.12.50/. [Open access]","Maria Cannata, review of <i>Beyond the Nile: Egypt and the Classical World</i>, ed. Jeffrey Spier, Timothy F. Potts and Sara E. Cole, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.04.37, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.04.37/. [Open access]","Angelos Chaniotis, review of <i>Introduction to an Inventory of Poleis</i> and <i>More Studies in the Ancient Greek Polis</i>, ed. Mogens Herman Hansen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.07.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.07.16/. [Open access]","Peter Nadig, review of <i>‘Because I am Greek’: Polyonymy as an Expression of Ethnicity in Ptolemaic Egypt</i>, by Sandra Coussement, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.04.21/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is Herodotus often stretched further than he goes?","a":"Because he does not mention Assyrian rule over Egypt at all, and does not say Greeks fought for Persia against the last Saite king."},{"q":"Why do the Abu Simbel graffiti show more than one expedition's names?","a":"Because the letter-forms and dialects of the graffiti around them show men of several different origins."},{"q":"Why did the pharaohs want these men?","a":"Because what Egypt was buying was not only manpower: the hoplite way of fighting was new, and the pharaohs had reason to want it."}]},
{"id":"rm-001","num":"1","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Strabo saw <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a triangle with the Alps for its base and the strait of Sicily for its vertex, cut down its length by the Apennines.","answer":"Ancient Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 1 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Population</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 – 16 million, disputed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ancient Italy</b> names the peninsula and its peoples before Rome made one country of them, and it was a geographical expression long before it was a political one. Strabo describes a triangle running south, the Alps for its base and the strait of Sicily for its vertex, six thousand stadia from the Adriatic to the heel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Apennines run its length, so the workable country comes in pockets between the ridges and the two coasts rather than in one broad expanse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The one great lowland lies to the north between those mountains and the Alps: the Po plain, today among the most densely populated and productive country in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For most of this period it was not reckoned part of Italy at all, and the name had begun far to the south, where Antiochus of Syracuse used <i>Italia</i> of the toe alone before it spread north and took in the Cisalpine Gauls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How many people the peninsula held is disputed, and the argument turns on two readings of the same census totals and the same scatter of settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A low count gives about 6 million around 1 CE after two centuries of slow decline, where a high count gives about 16 million after rapid growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost nothing survives in which these peoples describe themselves at length — Umbrian is known chiefly from one document of about 4,500 words — so they reach us through Greek and Roman authors writing after Rome absorbed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology has carried the weight since the 19th century and ancient DNA since the 2010s: 82 people buried in Etruria and the south show a gene pool holding steady through the 1st millennium BCE, then shifting abruptly under the empire to about half eastern Mediterranean ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The frame has moved with the evidence: 'Romanization' is contested enough that some would drop the word, the spread of Roman ways being explained through a common elite culture rather than as something done to the conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ancient Italy","questions":["Read the Roman census totals one way and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held about 6 million people; read the same figures the other way and it held nearer 16 million.","Because its peoples left almost nothing about themselves, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches us through Greek and Roman authors, through archaeology, and lately through DNA from its graves."],"tags":["place","history","geography","archaeology","genetics","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.1.1–3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5A*.html. [Open access]","Michele Livani et al., \"Subsurface Geological and Geophysical Data from the Po Plain and the Northern Adriatic Sea,\" <i>Earth System Science Data</i> 15 (2023): 4261–93, https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-4261-2023. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 6.1.4, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/6A*.html. [Open access]","Saskia Hin and Emilio Zagheni, \"Population Dynamics in Historical Roman Italy: The Impact of Warfare\" (conference paper, International Union for the Scientific Study of Population), https://iussp.org/sites/default/files/event_call_for_papers/Hin%26Zagheni_PopulationModeling.pdf. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth et al., \"The Origin and Legacy of the Etruscans through a 2000-Year Archeogenomic Time Transect,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7 (2021): eabi7673, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi7673. [Open access]","Kathryn Lomas, review of <i>Italy and the West: Comparative Issues in Romanization</i>, ed. Simon Keay and Nicola Terrenato, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2002.07.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.07.23/. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Umbrian: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=58&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg/1920px-Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg","title":"Ancient Italy","desc":"Samnite soldiers from a tomb fresco from Nola, 4th century BCE. Museo archeologico nazionale di Napoli (inv. nr. 9363). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg","alt":"Samnite soldiers from a tomb frieze in Nola 4th century BCE"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why do these peoples reach us mainly through Roman writers?","a":"Because almost nothing survives in which they describe themselves at length — Umbrian is known chiefly from one document of about 4,500 words — so Greek and Roman authors writing after the conquest carry the account."},{"q":"Why can the same census totals give two population figures?","a":"Because a low reading gives about 6 million around 1 CE after two centuries of slow decline, and a high one about 16 million after rapid growth, from the same totals and the same scatter of settlement."},{"q":"Why is the word 'Romanization' now contested?","a":"Because the spread of Roman ways is explained through a common elite culture rather than as something done to the conquered, and some would drop the term outright."}]},
{"id":"rm-002","num":"2","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Running about 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) from north-west to south-east, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divide the Italian peninsula lengthwise and leave its flat ground in strips along two facing coasts.","answer":"Apennines","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Apennines</b> are the mountain spine of Italy, a set of parallel ranges running about 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) from north-west to south-east down the length of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are conventionally cut into three sectors, northern, central and southern, and the highest ground stands in the centre, where the Gran Sasso reaches 2,912 metres (9,554 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain divides the country without closing it, and the flat land lies to either side, sloping away west towards the Tyrrhenian Sea and east towards the Adriatic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain falls unevenly across that divide, the relief gathering the wet on one flank and leaving the Adriatic side of the range drier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rock changes along the chain, and at its southern front it is clay-rich flysch whose slopes fail readily: one sector in northern Apulia, 1,460 square kilometres (564 square miles) in extent, has been mapped for its landslides alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The mountains are still rising, by as much as 1 millimetre (0.04 inches) a year in the central Apennines, and the fastest uplift follows the highest ground and the drainage divide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Extensional faults take up that movement, and earthquakes along them reach magnitudes between 5.8 and 6.9, so this is unquiet ground rather than a settled backdrop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Along the western flank the peninsula turns volcanic, and the Smithsonian's Global Volcanism Program files Vesuvius and Campi Flegrei together among the Italian Peninsula provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Vesuvius is a stratovolcano of 1,281 metres (4,203 feet) that last erupted in 1944; Campi Flegrei is a caldera reaching 458 metres (1,503 feet), last in eruption in 1538.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the range leaves is a country whose workable ground runs in strips along two coasts with a barrier between them,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and that is the shape the rest of Roman history is written across.","citation":"","answerText":"Apennines","questions":["Still rising by up to a millimetre a year and shaken by earthquakes of magnitude 5.8 to 6.9, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the unquiet backbone of the Italian peninsula.","The Gran Sasso reaches 2,912 metres (9,554 feet) in the central sector of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the chain conventionally divided into northern, central and southern parts."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","history","italy"],"sources":["Vincenzo Capozzi et al., \"Historical Snow Measurements in the Central and Southern Apennine Mountains: Climatology, Variability, and Trend,\" <i>The Cryosphere</i> 19 (2025): 565–595, https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-19-565-2025. [Open access]","Patience A. Cowie et al., \"Orogen-Scale Uplift in the Central Italian Apennines Drives Episodic Behaviour of Earthquake Faults,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7 (2017): 44858, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep44858. [Open access]","Francesca Ardizzone et al., \"Geomorphological Landslide Inventory Map of the Daunia Apennines, Southern Italy,\" <i>Earth System Science Data</i> 15 (2023): 753–767, https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-15-753-2023. [Open access]","\"Vesuvius,\" Global Volcanism Program, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=211020. [Open access]","\"Campi Flegrei,\" Global Volcanism Program, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=211010. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Monte_Pollino_%28P.N.P.%29.jpg/1920px-Monte_Pollino_%28P.N.P.%29.jpg","title":"Apennines","desc":"Il versante nord di Monte Pollino. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Pollino_(P.N.P.).jpg","alt":"Monte Pollino (P.N.P.)"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"locator":{"name":"Apennines","at":[13.5656,42.4692],"kind":"range","spine":[[8.45,44.35],[9.55,44.45],[10.3,44.3],[11.2,44.1],[11.85,43.85],[12.6,43.45],[13.1,42.8],[13.55,42.47],[14.05,41.95],[15,41.3],[15.55,40.8],[16.05,40.15],[16.2,39.55],[16.35,39],[16.05,38.4],[15.9,38.15]]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the chain divide the peninsula without closing it?","a":"Because the flat land lies to either side of it, sloping away west towards the Tyrrhenian and east towards the Adriatic, so the workable ground runs in strips along two coasts."},{"q":"Why is one flank of the range drier than the other?","a":"Because the relief gathers the rain on one side and leaves the Adriatic side of the chain drier, so the divide is a climatic one as well as a physical one."},{"q":"Why is this unquiet ground rather than a settled backdrop?","a":"Because the central Apennines are still rising by as much as a millimetre a year, extensional faults take up the movement, and earthquakes along them reach magnitudes between 5.8 and 6.9."}]},
{"id":"rm-003","num":"3","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Rising in the Apennines and reaching the Tyrrhenian Sea beside Rome, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Italy's third-longest river and was the city's road to the sea before it was anything else.","answer":"Tiber","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Tiber</b> is the third-longest river in Italy, running about 405 kilometres (250 miles) from a source in the Apennines to the Tyrrhenian Sea beside Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It drains a catchment of roughly 17,400 square kilometres (6,700 square miles), which takes about 95 centimetres (37 inches) of rain a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its flow is anything but steady: mean discharge runs at around 225 cubic metres a second, the highest annual figures pass 1,500 and the lowest fall to 60, a range wide enough to change what the river could carry from one year to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The valley it occupies was cut tens of metres below present sea level at the Last Glacial Maximum, and it has been filling ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Along the middle valley that filling has left a staircase of four aggradational terraces standing above the modern alluvial plain, beneath an older tabular plateau that reaches 250 to 270 metres (820 to 890 feet) above sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Three phases of aggradation brought the plain at Rome to a relatively stable 6 to 8 metres (20 to 26 feet) above sea level by the late 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of them was abrupt, laying 4 to 6 metres (13 to 20 feet) of alluvium in roughly a century around 2,600 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tiber Island, which splits the channel in the middle of the city, owes its existence to tectonic movement rather than to silt alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The river was Rome's route to the sea before the city had any other, and its earliest harbour lay upstream at the Forum Boarium, on the bank within the city itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mouth was the difficulty: a sandbank grounded the ship carrying the Magna Mater in 205–204 BCE, Strabo called the harbour there unsheltered, and laden vessels had to tranship into lighters and river boats to reach Rome at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tiber","questions":["Laden ships could not pass the sandbank at the mouth of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and had to tranship into lighters, which is how cargo from the sea reached Rome at all.","Three phases of silting raised the floodplain of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a stable 6 to 8 metres (20 to 26 feet) above sea level by the late 3rd century BCE."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","history","italy"],"sources":["Silvia Fornasaro et al., \"Total Mercury Mass Load from the Paglia–Tiber River System: The Contribution to Mediterranean Sea Hg Budget,\" <i>Toxics</i> 10 (2022): 395, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics10070395. [Open access]","Jaime Pitarch et al., \"Linking Flow-Stream Variability to Grain Size Distribution of Suspended Sediment from a Satellite-Based Analysis of the Tiber River Plume,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 19729, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-56409-8. [Open access]","Fabrizio Marra et al., \"Rome in Its Setting: Post-Glacial Aggradation History of the Tiber River Alluvial Deposits and Tectonic Origin of the Tiber Island,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13 (2018): e0194838, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0194838. [Open access]","Ferréol Salomon et al., \"The Development and Characteristics of Ancient Harbours: Applying the PADM Chart to the Case Studies of Ostia and Portus,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11 (2016): e0162587, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0162587. [Open access]","Fabrizio Marra, Fabio Florindo, and Carmelo Petronio, \"Quaternary Fluvial Terraces of the Tiber Valley: Geochronologic and Geometric Constraints on the Back-Arc Magmatism-Related Uplift in Central Italy,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7 (2017): 2517, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-02437-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Hendrik_Frans_van_Lint_-_Rome_-_A_View_of_the_Tiber_-_WGA13056.jpg","title":"Tiber","desc":"Hendrik Frans van Lint - Rome - A View of the Tiber. Hendrik Frans van Lint. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hendrik_Frans_van_Lint_-_Rome_-_A_View_of_the_Tiber_-_WGA13056.jpg","alt":"Hendrik Frans van Lint - Rome - A View of the Tiber"},"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"locator":{"name":"Tiber","at":[12.2334,41.7405],"kind":"river","zoom":22},"why":[{"q":"Why did the river's flow matter as much as its length?","a":"Because the discharge ranges from 60 to over 1,500 cubic metres a second against a mean of about 225, wide enough to change what the river could carry from one year to the next."},{"q":"Why does the plain at Rome stand where it does?","a":"Because three phases of aggradation raised it to a relatively stable 6 to 8 metres above sea level by the late 3rd century BCE, one laying 4 to 6 metres of alluvium in roughly a century."},{"q":"Why was the mouth of the river the difficulty rather than the route?","a":"Because a sandbank grounded the ship carrying the Magna Mater, Strabo called the harbour there unsheltered, and laden vessels had to tranship into lighters and river boats to reach the city."}]},
{"id":"rm-004","num":"4","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"South of the Tiber on Italy's Tyrrhenian side, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is built on a thick plateau of volcanic rock laid down by two districts of dormant volcanoes that flank Rome.","answer":"Latium","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Latium</b> is the coastal country of west-central Italy lying south of the Tiber, and the ground under it is volcanic. Two districts built that ground: Monti Sabatini north of Rome and the Alban Hills to the south, erupting from about 600,000 years ago in a sequence punctuated by pulses of tectonic uplift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their eruptions laid a thick pyroclastic plateau, and the tuffaceous hills Rome was founded on are cut from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither district is extinct, only dormant: the last eruptions came about 70,000 years ago at Monti Sabatini and 36,000 at the Alban Hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the wider province that activity has been waning from north-west to south-east, from northern Latium towards the bay of Naples, and it crossed the site of Rome on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> People were on this ground long before any of it settled, and Acheulian and early Middle Palaeolithic assemblages at Torre in Pietra put them here hundreds of millennia back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The coast is a line of shallow lagoons behind dunes, with a wide plain running south of the Anzio promontory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Inland the limestone of the Monti Lepini and Monti Ausoni holds caves that were used for burial through the Copper and Bronze Ages, among them La Sassa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On an island in the Caprolace lagoon a Middle Bronze Age workshop made salt by briquetage, boiling brine down until a cake of it was left, a technique in use across Europe from the Neolithic to Roman times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The evidence there points to an elite-driven workshop rather than a household one, so this coast carried a specialised industry well before Rome amounted to anything, and the marshy ground behind the dunes was not drained until the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Latium","questions":["In the Middle Bronze Age a workshop on a lagoon island in coastal <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> boiled brine into cakes of salt, a specialised industry running there long before Rome existed.","Acheulian assemblages at Torre in Pietra show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was occupied for hundreds of millennia before any of the peoples Rome would later contend with arrived."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","history","italy"],"sources":["Fabrizio Marra et al., \"Monti Sabatini and Colli Albani: The Dormant Twin Volcanoes at the Gates of Rome,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 8666, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-65394-2. [Open access]","Paola Villa et al., \"The Acheulian and Early Middle Paleolithic in Latium (Italy): Stability and Innovation,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11 (2016): e0160516, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0160516. [Open access]","Luca Alessandri et al., \"Salt or Fish (or Salted Fish)? The Bronze Age Specialised Sites along the Tyrrhenian Coast of Central Italy: New Insights from Caprolace Settlement,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14 (2019): e0224435, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0224435. [Open access]","Marco Romboni et al., \"La Sassa Cave: Isotopic Evidence for Copper Age and Bronze Age Population Dynamics in Central Italy,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18 (2023): e0288637, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0288637. [Open access]","Fabrizio Marra et al., \"The Archaeological Ensemble from Campoverde (Agro Pontino, Central Italy): New Constraints on the Last Interglacial Sea Level Markers,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 17837, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-36111-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/I_Colli_Albani_visti_dal_parco_degli_acquedotti_Roma.JPG/1920px-I_Colli_Albani_visti_dal_parco_degli_acquedotti_Roma.JPG","title":"Latium","desc":"The Alban Hills seen from the Park of the Aqueducts, Rome. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Colli_Albani_visti_dal_parco_degli_acquedotti_Roma.JPG","alt":"The Alban Hills rising beyond the flat plain south-east of Rome"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Latium","at":[12.7167,41.9],"kind":"region","area":[[12.24,41.83],[12.75,42],[13.1,41.85],[13.35,41.55],[13.1,41.25],[12.85,41.25],[12.45,41.55],[12.23,41.75]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the ground Rome was built on volcanic?","a":"Because Monti Sabatini and the Alban Hills laid a thick pyroclastic plateau from about 600,000 years ago, and the tuffaceous hills of the city are cut from it."},{"q":"Why are those districts called dormant rather than extinct?","a":"Because the last eruptions came about 70,000 years ago at Monti Sabatini and 36,000 at the Alban Hills, the activity waning north-west to south-east across the province."},{"q":"Why is the salt workshop in the lagoon read as an elite enterprise?","a":"Because the evidence there points to a specialised industry rather than a household one, boiling brine down by briquetage on this coast well before Rome amounted to anything."}]},
{"id":"rm-005","num":"5","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"About 20 kilometres (12 miles) south-east of Rome, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are a volcanic caldera whose eruptions helped lay the plateau the city's own hills are cut from.","answer":"Alban Hills","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Alban Hills</b>, the Colli Albani of Italian geology, are a volcanic caldera about 20 kilometres (12 miles) south-east of Rome, rising to 949 metres (3,114 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With Monti Sabatini to the north of the city they laid the thick pyroclastic plateau that Rome's own hills are cut from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their eruptions began about 600,000 years ago and ran on in a long series of ignimbrites and ash-falls, with people living in the region while they continued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Geologists divide the volcano's life into three periods, named Vulcano Laziale, Tuscolano-Artemisio-Faete and Via dei Laghi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its youngest vents are maars, craters blasted open where rising magma met groundwater, and the largest of them holds Lake Albano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> When it last erupted is genuinely unsettled: one study dates the last magmatic eruption to 36,000 years ago, another to about 23,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second reports phreatic activity, steam blasts driven without fresh magma, going on through the Holocene and into historical times, while the Smithsonian's catalogue records the last known eruption simply as uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is agreed is that the volcano is quiescent rather than extinct, and that across the last 600,000 years its average dormancy has run to some 38,500 years, about as long as the interval since it last erupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twenty years of satellite radar show the ground above the youngest vents inflating by more than 2 millimetres (0.08 inches) a year, which is read as magma recharging at depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gas still reaches the surface: at Cava dei Selci, a neighbourhood of 4,000 people on the flank of the volcano, carbon dioxide carrying about 1 per cent hydrogen sulphide has killed animals, caused blowouts and road collapses, and forced houses to be evacuated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Alban Hills","questions":["Satellite radar shows the ground above the youngest vents of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inflating by more than 2 millimetres (0.08 inches) a year, read as magma recharging at depth.","At Cava dei Selci a neighbourhood of 4,000 people sits on gas rising from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, carbon dioxide with hydrogen sulphide in it that has killed animals and forced evacuations."],"tags":["place","geography","geology","nature","italy"],"sources":["Fabrizio Marra et al., \"Monti Sabatini and Colli Albani: The Dormant Twin Volcanoes at the Gates of Rome,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 8666, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-65394-2. [Open access]","Mónica Ágreda-López et al., \"The Crystal Cargo Provides a Chronicle of Pre-Caldera Dynamics in Mafic Volcanic Systems: Insights from Colli Albani,\" <i>Bulletin of Volcanology</i> 87 (2025): 78, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00445-025-01865-6. [Open access]","\"Colli Albani,\" Global Volcanism Program, Smithsonian Institution, https://volcano.si.edu/volcano.cfm?vn=211004. [Open access]","Maria Luisa Carapezza et al., \"Health Impact of Natural Gas Emission at Cava dei Selci Residential Zone,\" <i>Environmental Geochemistry and Health</i> 45 (2023): 707–729, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10653-022-01244-6. [Open access]","Paola Villa et al., \"The Acheulian and Early Middle Paleolithic in Latium (Italy): Stability and Innovation,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11 (2016): e0160516, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0160516. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Louis_Gurlitt_-_Albaner_Berge.jpg/1920px-Louis_Gurlitt_-_Albaner_Berge.jpg","title":"Alban Hills","desc":"Louis Gurlitt - Albaner Berge. Louis Gurlitt. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Gurlitt_-_Albaner_Berge.jpg","alt":"Louis Gurlitt - Albaner Berge"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Alban Hills","at":[12.7,41.73],"kind":"region","zoom":120,"area":[[12.56,41.83],[12.68,41.87],[12.8,41.85],[12.88,41.77],[12.86,41.66],[12.74,41.61],[12.6,41.64],[12.55,41.73]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the volcano called quiescent rather than extinct?","a":"Because over the last 600,000 years its average dormancy has run to some 38,500 years, about as long as the interval since it last erupted."},{"q":"Why is the date of the last eruption unsettled?","a":"Because one study puts the last magmatic eruption 36,000 years ago and another about 23,000, while the Smithsonian's catalogue records the last known eruption simply as uncertain."},{"q":"Why is the ground above the youngest vents watched?","a":"Because twenty years of satellite radar show it inflating by more than 2 millimetres a year, which is read as magma recharging at depth."}]},
{"id":"rm-006","num":"6","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Po plain filled with large defended centres, Sardinia raised its drystone towers, and copper from the southern Alps travelled as far as the Balkans.","answer":"Bronze Age Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nuraghi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Bronze Age Italy</b> was never one society, and the regions of the peninsula and its islands went their own ways at their own speeds. In the north the communities of the Po plain settled progressively through the 2nd millennium BCE, ending in the large defended centres that stood at the turn of the Middle and Late Bronze Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people in them were drawing on networks that reached into continental Europe and the eastern Mediterranean, and isotopes taken from their dead show movement into those settlements as well as goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the metal behind all this came out of the southern Alps, and analysis of 57 objects found that copper matching those ores was almost omnipresent in the western and central Balkans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italy was therefore exporting raw material into Europe rather than only receiving it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Sardinia the age built something no other part of Italy did, the nuraghi, drystone towers put up between about 1700 and 1100 BCE and still the island's most prominent Bronze Age monument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Far to the south, mining near the Sila Plateau made protohistoric Calabria one of the significant metallurgical territories of Italy, tying it into exchange along the Tyrrhenian coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Middle Bronze Age burial in the karst cave of Grotta della Monaca, 600 metres (2,000 feet) up near the river Esaro, has yielded the first Bronze Age mitogenomes recovered from that region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the centre, the Final Bronze Age settlement of Monte Croce Guardia in the Marche ground its grain on tools cut from volcanic rock, chosen because lava wears well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tracing that rock back to the outcrops it came from puts people and heavy goods on the move across central Italy some three thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Bronze Age Italy","questions":["The first mitogenomes from a Middle Bronze Age cave burial in Calabria were recovered to study <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a period whose southern communities lived beside some of the peninsula's richest ore.","Sardinia raised thousands of drystone towers between about 1700 and 1100 BCE, the most prominent monument left anywhere by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and unmatched on the mainland."],"tags":["era","history","italy","archaeology","technology"],"sources":["Claudio Cavazzuti et al., \"Flows of People in Villages and Large Centres in Bronze Age Italy through Strontium and Oxygen Isotopes,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14 (2019): e0209693, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0209693. [Open access]","Mario Gavranović et al., \"Emergence of Monopoly: Copper Exchange Networks during the Late Bronze Age in the Western and Central Balkans,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17 (2022): e0263823, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0263823. [Open access]","Davide Schirru and Alessandro Vanzetti, \"Climbing the Giara: A Quantitative Reassessment of Movement and Visibility in the Nuragic Landscape of the Gesturi Plateau,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18 (2023): e0289023, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0289023. [Open access]","Francesco Fontani et al., \"First Bronze Age Human Mitogenomes from Calabria (Grotta della Monaca, Southern Italy),\" <i>Genes</i> 12 (2021): 636, https://doi.org/10.3390/genes12050636. [Open access]","Patrizia Santi et al., \"Tracing the Human Movements of Three Thousand Years Ago by Volcanic Grinding Tools in the Final Bronze Age Settlement of Monte Croce Guardia,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 7022, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-34033-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Nuraghe_village.JPG","title":"Bronze Age Italy","desc":"Nuraghe et son environnement. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuraghe_village.JPG","alt":"Nuraghe village"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is it wrong to speak of one Bronze Age society here?","a":"Because the regions went their own ways at their own speeds — defended centres on the Po plain, drystone towers in Sardinia, mining near the Sila Plateau in Calabria."},{"q":"Why is Italy read as exporting metal rather than only receiving it?","a":"Because much of the copper came out of the southern Alps, and analysis of 57 objects found ores matching those sources almost omnipresent in the western and central Balkans."},{"q":"Why does a grinding tool show people on the move?","a":"Because the volcanic rock was chosen for wearing well, and tracing it back to its outcrops puts people and heavy goods crossing central Italy three thousand years ago."}]},
{"id":"rm-007","num":"7","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Sub-quadrangular, banked and ditched, the villages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood on the ridges of the Po plain and carried the densest population Bronze Age Italy reached.","answer":"Terramare culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Terramare culture</b> is the Middle and Recent Bronze Age culture of the Po plain, its banked villages the nearest thing Bronze Age Italy had to a landscape of towns. The name is modern: it comes from <i>terra marna</i>, a local word for the organically rich earth of the mounds the villages left behind, which was quarried as fertiliser in the 19th century and so brought them to notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They were sub-quadrangular, raised on alluvial ridges and old river terraces, and laid out to a plan rather than grown at random: the huts stood either on the ground or on built platforms, aligned to the perimeter works that enclosed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their sizes ran from small to substantial: Sant'Eurosia grew from 1.5 to 2.5 hectares (3.7 to 6.2 acres) over its life, while large ones such as Fondo Paviani reached 15 to 20 hectares (37 to 49 acres), and the economy under all of it was farming and herding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Oppeano near Verona the floors of two houses of 1650 to 1550 BCE hold laminated dung from small livestock stalled indoors, so people and their sheep or goats were sharing a roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What they ate was changing across the same centuries, as broomcorn and foxtail millet, rye, spelt and new legumes spread through the peninsula alongside the older cereals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were not, however, a mobile people: strontium and oxygen isotopes from more than a hundred individuals at three sites put most movement inside a radius of about 50 kilometres (30 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They could build well beyond the scale of a household, and the clearest case is Noceto, where a great tank of oak was sunk into a terrace at the plain's southern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tree rings and radiocarbon date its lower tank to 1444 BCE and the upper one twelve years later, to 1432, each to within about four years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The whole system then collapsed around 1150 BCE, and the explanation offered is a combination of land worked past what it could bear and a shift in the climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Terramare culture","questions":["At Noceto a tank of oak sunk into a terrace of the Po plain belongs to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its timbers dated by tree rings to 1444 BCE and a second tank to 1432.","Around 1150 BCE the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collapsed, an end laid to land worked past what it could bear together with a shift in the climate."],"tags":["culture","history","italy","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["Claudio Cavazzuti et al., \"Flows of People in Villages and Large Centres in Bronze Age Italy through Strontium and Oxygen Isotopes,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14 (2019): e0209693, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0209693. [Open access]","Giacomo Capuzzo et al., \"Highly Diverse Bronze Age Population Dynamics in Central-Southern Europe and Their Response to Regional Climatic Patterns,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13 (2018): e0200709, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0200709. [Open access]","Cristiano Nicosia et al., \"High-Resolution Sediment Analysis Reveals Middle Bronze Age Byre-Houses at the Site of Oppeano,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17 (2022): e0272561, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272561. [Open access]","Mauro Cremaschi et al., \"Dating the Noceto Vasca Votiva, a Unique Wooden Structure of the 15th Century BCE, and the Timing of a Major Societal Change in the Bronze Age of Northern Italy,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16 (2021): e0251341, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0251341. [Open access]","Alessandra Varalli, Jacopo Moggi-Cecchi, and Gwenaëlle Goude, \"A Multi-Proxy Bioarchaeological Approach Reveals New Trends in Bronze Age Diet in Italy,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 12203, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-15581-0. [Open access]","Mauro Cremaschi, Paolo Storchi, and Alessandro Perego, \"Geoarchaeology in Urban Context: The Town of Reggio Emilia and River Dynamics during the Last Two Millennia in Northern Italy,\" <i>Geoarchaeology</i> 33 (2018): 52–66, author copy at https://air.unimi.it/bitstream/2434/530920/5/CREMASCHI%20GEOARCH.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi%2C_1877.jpg/1920px-Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi%2C_1877.jpg","title":"Terramare","desc":"Fotografia tratta da L. Pigorini, Terramara dell’età del Bronzo situata in Castione dei Marchesi (territorio parmigiano), in “Atti Reale Accademia dei Lincei”, s. III, VIII, 1882-83. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scavi_alla_Terramara_di_Castione_Marchesi,_1877.jpg","alt":"Scavi alla Terramara di Castione Marchesi, 1877"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Terramare culture","at":[10.93,44.62],"kind":"region","area":[[9.45,45.15],[10.4,45.45],[11.2,45.42],[11.95,45.2],[12.05,44.8],[11.6,44.45],[10.6,44.5],[9.7,44.72],[9.4,44.95]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the culture named after fertiliser?","a":"Because terra marna is a local word for the organically rich earth of the mounds the villages left, which was quarried in the 19th century and so brought them to notice."},{"q":"Why are these villages said to be planned rather than grown?","a":"Because they were sub-quadrangular, raised on alluvial ridges and old river terraces, with huts on the ground or on built platforms aligned to the perimeter works."},{"q":"Why is this settled culture not also a mobile one?","a":"Because strontium and oxygen isotopes from more than a hundred individuals at three sites put most movement inside a radius of about 50 kilometres."}]},
{"id":"rm-008","num":"8","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Burning its dead and sealing them in necked urns under in-turned bowls, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathered central Italy onto plateaus that would become the Etruscan cities.","answer":"Villanovan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Villanovan culture</b> is the culture of Italy's Early Iron Age, running from the end of the 10th century to the 8th BCE across the Tyrrhenian side of central Italy and the southern Po plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known above all from how it buried its dead: cremated bone in a vase with a distinctive neck, covered by an in-turned rim bowl, both worked in a geometric style, the urn set in a pit and packed round with the ashes of the pyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bronze helmets and disc fibulae recur in the graves often enough to be diagnostic of the whole group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burning was so much the rule that the exceptions stand out: at Tarquinia twenty people were laid unburnt in the sacred heart of the settlement while their contemporaries went to cemeteries on its edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The living changed as much as the dead, the scattered villages of the preceding period giving way to large settlements spread over plateaus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The scale is what startles: principal sites covered 120 to 200 hectares (300 to 490 acres) and secondary ones 25 to 90 hectares (62 to 220 acres), each ringed by its own cemeteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The process behind it was nucleation, and one reading is that bands of descent groups agreed to coalesce on a defensible site rather than being driven there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What came out has been interpreted as a form of early state formation, which is to say that these are the cities of Etruria in their first shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The countryside kept pace, since livestock in Italy grew larger through the Iron Age, earlier than the same change reached the western provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The culture also travelled: Pontecagnano in Campania was founded early in the 9th century BCE by Villanovan groups out of southern Etruria and grew in time into an Etruscan city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Villanovan culture","questions":["Principal sites of 120 to 200 hectares (300 to 490 acres), each ringed by cemeteries, mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose gathering onto plateaus has been read as early state formation.","Pontecagnano in Campania was founded early in the 9th century BCE by groups of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> coming out of southern Etruria, and became an Etruscan city."],"tags":["culture","history","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Carmen Esposito et al., \"Intense Community Dynamics in the Pre-Roman Frontier Site of Fermo (Ninth–Fifth Century BCE, Marche, Central Italy) Inferred from Isotopic Data,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 3632, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-29466-3. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Angela Trentacoste et al., \"Pre-Roman Improvements to Agricultural Production: Evidence from Livestock Husbandry in Late Prehistoric Italy,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13 (2018): e0208109, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0208109. [Open access]","Roberto Germano et al., \"Health and Lifestyle in the Iron Age Italian Community of Pontecagnano,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21 (2026): e0338448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0338448. [Open access]","Giovanna Bagnasco et al., \"Bioarchaeology Aids the Cultural Understanding of Six Characters in Search of Their Agency (Tarquinia, Ninth–Seventh Century BC, Central Italy),\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 11895, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-61052-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Villanovan_Horse_Bit_LACMA_AC1992.152.17.jpg/1920px-Villanovan_Horse_Bit_LACMA_AC1992.152.17.jpg","title":"Villanovan culture","desc":"Tools and Equipment; horse trappings Bronze Overall length: 9 7/16 in. (24 cm); Height of bit: 2 7/16 in. (6.2 cm) Gift of Varya and Hans Cohn (AC1992.152.17) Greek, Roman and Etruscan Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Villanovan_Horse_Bit_LACMA_AC1992.152.17.jpg","alt":"Villanovan Horse Bit LACMA AC1992.152.17"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Villanovan culture","at":[11.46,44.5],"kind":"region","area":[[10.15,43.95],[10.45,44.35],[11.3,44.68],[11.85,44.58],[12.05,44.2],[11.85,43.75],[12.25,43.2],[12.45,42.8],[12.55,42.35],[12.5,42.05],[12.35,41.9],[11.95,41.8],[11.3,42.25],[10.5,42.85],[10.15,43.5]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the culture known chiefly from its graves?","a":"Because the rite is distinctive and consistent: cremated bone in a necked vase under an in-turned rim bowl, set in a pit and packed round with the ashes of the pyre."},{"q":"Why do twenty unburnt bodies at Tarquinia stand out?","a":"Because burning was so much the rule that these exceptions, laid in the sacred heart of the settlement while their contemporaries went to cemeteries on its edge, mark something apart."},{"q":"Why is the change in settlement read as early state formation?","a":"Because scattered villages gave way to plateau settlements of 120 to 200 hectares, each ringed by its own cemeteries, which is Etruria's cities in their first shape."}]},
{"id":"rm-009","num":"9","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Speakers of one branch of Indo-European spread across the peninsula, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were never a nation and never included every community living in Italy.","answer":"Italic peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Italic peoples</b> are the communities of ancient Italy who spoke a language of the Italic branch of Indo-European, which makes them a linguistic category rather than a nation and leaves a good deal of the peninsula outside it. The branch falls in two: Latin together with Faliscan on one side, and on the other the Sabellic or Osco-Umbrian languages. Oscan ran through the middle and the south, and the peoples associated with it include the Peligni, Vestini, Marrucini, Marsi and Volsci, with the Aequi a possible sixth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> South Picene, spoken in what are now the Marche and Abruzzo, is plainly Italic and shows considerable affinity with Umbrian, though whether it belongs inside the Osco-Umbrian group or branched away earlier is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survives in 23 inscriptions of the 6th to 3rd centuries BCE, together with two inscribed helmets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Faliscan sat on the right bank of the Tiber in an enclave among Etruscan neighbours, beside Veii and Capena, and of all these languages it is the closest to Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How close is contested: a peripheral dialect of Latin to some scholars, a separate language within the same taxon to others, a fully independent Italic language to a third view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of these identities was as ancient as the peoples holding them liked to claim, since distinct Italic ethnicities emerged only in the Late Bronze Age and hardened into well-defined cultural and linguistic identities during the Iron Age of the 10th to 3rd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Umbri, whom ancient writers counted among the most ancient Italic populations of all, are a case in point, their origins still debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Almost every mitochondrial lineage recovered from the pre-Roman cemetery at Plestia can still be found in Umbria today, which argues for continuity in the population whatever happened to the label.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Italic peoples","questions":["Latin and Faliscan on one side, the Sabellic tongues on the other: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are grouped by the branch of Indo-European they spoke rather than by any shared state.","Distinct ethnicities among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> emerged only in the Late Bronze Age and hardened during an Iron Age running from the 10th to the 3rd century BCE."],"tags":["people","history","italy","language","iron age"],"sources":["Anna Marinetti, \"South Picene: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=69&lang=en. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Faliscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=66&lang=en. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Oscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=56&lang=en. [Open access]","Francesco Ravasini et al., \"The Genomic Portrait of the Picene Culture Provides New Insights into the Italic Iron Age and the Legacy of the Roman Empire in Central Italy,\" <i>Genome Biology</i> 25 (2024): 292, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-024-03430-4. [Open access]","Alessandra Modi et al., \"The Mitogenome Portrait of Umbria in Central Italy as Depicted by Contemporary Inhabitants and Pre-Roman Remains,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 10700, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-67445-0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Ancient_Samnium.png","title":"Italic peoples","desc":"Reference map for Samnium. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Samnium.png","alt":"Ancient Samnium"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does this category leave much of the peninsula out?","a":"Because it is a linguistic one, taking in only those who spoke a language of the Italic branch — Latin and Faliscan on one side, the Sabellic or Osco-Umbrian languages on the other."},{"q":"Why were these identities not as ancient as the peoples claimed?","a":"Because distinct Italic ethnicities emerged only in the Late Bronze Age and hardened into well-defined cultural and linguistic identities during the Iron Age."},{"q":"Why can a label change while the population does not?","a":"Because almost every mitochondrial lineage recovered from the pre-Roman cemetery at Plestia can still be found in Umbria today, which argues for continuity whatever happened to the name."}]},
{"id":"rm-010","num":"10","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Sharing a material culture, a burial rite and a language but never a single state, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled the country between the Tiber and the Liri.","answer":"Latins","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 950 – 509 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Latins</b> were the people of Latium vetus, the country lying between the rivers Tiber and Liri, and they spoke the language that carries their name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were not a state but a set of communities holding a material culture in common, the Latial culture I to IV, along with similar burial customs and a similar political organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kinship of their language with Umbrian, Sabellian and Oscan is plain but unexplained, scholars still divided over whether it descends from a shared ancestor or grew out of long contact between neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its oldest surviving evidence is a handful of inscriptions of the 7th and 6th centuries BCE, among them the Duenos inscription and the Lapis Niger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people were no more primordial than the language, having emerged as a distinct Italic ethnicity in the Late Bronze Age and hardened into a defined cultural identity across the Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Their settlements were many and closely tied together, numbering between 78 and 107 across the stretch from about 950 to 509 BCE and linked by an unusually dense web of roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Power among them was never evenly held, and by the earlier 9th century BCE one of those settlements, Rome, had become by far the largest in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That imbalance is what most distinguishes Latium from Etruria over the river, where the proto-urban centres stood on a more equal footing with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> These were not isolated communities either, since goods from far outside the peninsula reach Italic sites of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> And though Latins and Etruscans spoke unrelated languages, DNA from Latins at Rome and its environs shows a local genetic profile shared with their Etruscan neighbours, so what separated the two was culture rather than descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Latins","questions":["By the earlier 9th century BCE Rome had become the largest of the settlements of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose power was never evenly held among them.","DNA from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Rome shows a genetic profile shared with their Etruscan neighbours, so what divided the two peoples was culture rather than descent."],"tags":["people","history","italy","language","iron age"],"sources":["Francesca Fulminante et al., \"Coordinated Decisions and Unbalanced Power: How Latin Cities Shaped Their Terrestrial Transportation Network,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 4 (2017): 4, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2017.00004. [Open access]","Filippo Battistoni, revised by Mirko Donninelli, \"Latin: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=36&lang=en. [Open access]","Francesco Ravasini et al., \"The Genomic Portrait of the Picene Culture Provides New Insights into the Italic Iron Age and the Legacy of the Roman Empire in Central Italy,\" <i>Genome Biology</i> 25 (2024): 292, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-024-03430-4. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth et al., \"The Origin and Legacy of the Etruscans through a 2000-Year Archeogenomic Time Transect,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7 (2021): eabi7673, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi7673. [Open access]","Francesca Fulminante, \"Editorial: Where Do Cities Come From and Where Are They Going To? Modelling Past and Present Agglomerations to Understand Urban Ways of Life,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 7 (2021): 633838, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2020.633838. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why were these communities a people without being a state?","a":"Because they held a material culture in common, the Latial culture I to IV, along with similar burial customs and a similar political organisation, rather than a single government."},{"q":"Why is their region distinguished from Etruria over the river?","a":"Because power among them was never evenly held, one settlement having become by far the largest in the region, where the Etruscan proto-urban centres stood on a more equal footing."},{"q":"Why is culture rather than descent said to separate them from the Etruscans?","a":"Because although the two spoke unrelated languages, DNA from Latins at Rome and its environs shows a local genetic profile shared with their Etruscan neighbours."}]},
{"id":"rm-011","num":"11","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Modern historians speak of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the singular, where the ancient writers describe at least three separate assemblies of Latin communities, each meeting at a different sanctuary.","answer":"Latin League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 6th century – 338 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Latin League</b> is a modern name for the alliances binding the communities of Latium, and the singular flatters the evidence: the ancient writers describe assemblies at three different sanctuaries, which have almost always been run together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One gathered at the spring and grove of Ferentina, where Livy and Dionysius of Halicarnassus have the peoples of Latium meet, and where terms between Rome and those cities were settled more than once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its site is now placed near the via Appia, some 25 kilometres (16 miles) from Rome, at a small lake once called the Laghetto di Turno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ground beside it has produced a terracotta warrior’s head of the very beginning of the 5th century BCE, a fragment of roof sculpture and so the trace of a sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second met at the grove of Diana in the wood of Aricia, whose founding inscription Cato copied out: it was dedicated by Egerius Baebius of Tusculum, dictator of the Latins, for eight named peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The third and longest-lived met on the Alban Mount for the feriae Latinae, the festival of Jupiter Latiaris, which Dionysius says 47 cities shared, bringing lambs, cheeses or milk to a bull sacrificed for all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a movable feast, and alone among such festivals its date was set by the consuls rather than the priests, which made it a moment of consular authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The words the Roman writers use for the meat give the arrangement away: Rome distributed it and the cities asked for their share, a ceremony under Roman direction rather than a rite among equals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Power in Latium had long been unequal, Rome having grown much the largest settlement in the region,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> so several of these alliances look less like instruments of Roman policy than like attempts to resist it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Rome broke the Latins in 338 BCE it dissolved the league, its federal army and its courts, and kept the festival, fixing its date thereafter while leaving the member states to preside in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Latin League","questions":["At the festival on the Alban Mount, one gathering of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some of the 47 cities brought lambs, some cheeses, and some a measure of milk.","Rome distributed the sacrificial meat and the Latin cities asked for their share, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was never the gathering of equals its name suggests."],"tags":["institution","history","italy","religion","iron age"],"sources":["Rianne A. M. Hermans, \"Latin Cults through Roman Eyes: Myth, Memory and Cult Practice in the Alban Hills\" (PhD diss., University of Amsterdam, 2017), chap. 4, https://hdl.handle.net/11245.1/cd8d636c-def7-44a4-9043-231c0d428d28. [Open access]","Alexandre Grandazzi, \"Identification d'une déesse: Ferentina et la ligue latine archaïque,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 140, no. 1 (1996): 273–94, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1996.15581. [Open access]","Filippo Coarelli, \"Le sanctuaire de Diana Nemorensis: nouvelles découvertes,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 156, no. 1 (2012): 555–69, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2012.93450. [Open access]","Bernadette Liou-Gille, \"Naissance de la ligue latine: mythe et culte de fondation,\" <i>Revue belge de philologie et d'histoire</i> 74, no. 1 (1996): 73–97, https://doi.org/10.3406/rbph.1996.4096. [Open access]","Francesca Fulminante et al., \"Coordinated Decisions and Unbalanced Power: How Latin Cities Shaped Their Terrestrial Transportation Network,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 4 (2017): 4, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2017.00004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Ligue-latine-carte.png","title":"Latin League","desc":"Map of Latium with the cities of the Latin League. Cassius Ahenobarbus, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ligue-latine-carte.png","alt":"Ligue-latine-carte"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Latin League","at":[12.7,41.71],"kind":"region","label":[12.72,41.78],"area":[[12.3,41.95],[12.6,42.03],[12.85,41.99],[13.02,41.86],[13.06,41.66],[13.15,41.4],[13.05,41.2],[12.8,41.28],[12.45,41.52],[12.25,41.72]]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the singular name flatter the evidence?","a":"Because the ancient writers describe assemblies at three different sanctuaries — Ferentina, the grove of Diana at Aricia and the Alban Mount — which have almost always been run together."},{"q":"Why does the wording about the sacrificial meat matter?","a":"Because Rome distributed it and the cities asked for their share, which makes the rite a ceremony under Roman direction rather than one among equals."},{"q":"Why did Rome keep the festival after dissolving the league?","a":"Because the league's army and courts were instruments the Latins could use, where the feast was a moment of consular authority whose date Rome then fixed."}]},
{"id":"rm-012","num":"12","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Latin never formed a place name on their name, as it did Samnium and Latium, which is one sign of how loosely the country of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was defined.","answer":"Sabines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Iron Age – 290 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sabines</b> were the people of the ager Sabinus, the hill country north-east of Rome, never a political unit, its borders vague enough that ancient authors disagree over which towns lay in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cures, Reate and Amiternum are always counted Sabine, and the looseness shows in a fact of language: Latin has no place name formed on the ethnic, where the Samnites have Samnium and the Latins Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their settlements stood on heights commanding the routes of the Tiber valley, a pattern set in the Final Bronze Age and carried into the Iron Age at Magliano Sabina, Foglia and Poggio Sommavilla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the 7th century BCE the scattered nuclei drew together into single centres on the Etruscan and Latial model, their cemeteries on the heights immediately around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eretum spread over some 20 hectares (49 acres) on urban lines by the later 7th century, passing from a family grouping under one leader to something more complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its cemetery at Colle del Forno was in use from the end of the 7th century until the end of the 4th, chamber tombs with loculi cut into the walls, holding whole generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they spoke is far less certain than where they lived: the corpora disagree over which inscriptions are Sabine at all, and most writing found there is on pottery — a flask 5.7 centimetres (2.2 inches) long need not have been made where it was buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That leaves 39 words the ancient grammarians label Sabine, and the sound-changes behind them do not agree, some being characteristic of Latin, some of Faliscan and some of one or another Sabellic language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome took the country in 290 BCE, and in 271 a channel some 2,200 metres (7,200 feet) long drained the Lacus Velinus, a lake of over 90 square kilometres (35 square miles) whose plain around Reate became farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cures, from which Roman tradition drew the city’s second king, contracted and was gradually abandoned, a decline read against the earthquake of 174 BCE that Livy reports from Sabine country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Sabines","questions":["The ancient grammarians label 39 surviving words as belonging to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the sound-changes behind them point to several different languages rather than to one.","Rome took the country of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 290 BCE, and in 271 a channel some two kilometres long drained the lake at its heart, turning the plain around Reate into farmland."],"tags":["people","history","italy","language","iron age"],"sources":["Annie Cecilia Burman, \"De Lingua Sabina: A Reappraisal of the Sabine Glosses\" (PhD diss., University of Cambridge, 2017), https://doi.org/10.17863/CAM.18502. [Open access]","Adriana Emiliozzi, Paola Moscati and Paola Santoro, \"The Princely Cart from Eretum,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i>, Supplemento 1 (2007): 143–62, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/Suppl_1/10_Emiliozzi.pdf. [Open access]","Francesca Colosi et al., \"Sabina Tiberina: modellizzazione della distribuzione delle presenze archeologiche,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 10 (1999): 275–87, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF10/10_19_Colosi-G_Espa-S_Espa-Gabrielli-Ricci-Verga.pdf. [Open access]","Carlo Carrara et al., <i>The Rieti Intermountain Basin and S. Francesco d’Assisi</i>, Field Trip Guide Book D07, 32nd International Geological Congress, Florence, 20–28 August 2004 (Rome: APAT, 2004), https://www.isprambiente.gov.it/contentfiles/00006500/6589-d07.pdf. [Open access]","Antonio Colapietro, Mauro Lucarini and Gennaro Maria Monti, \"Cures Sabini: indagine geo-archeologica preliminare,\" <i>Bollettino della Società Geografica Italiana</i>, ser. 14, 1, no. 1 (2018): 201–13, https://doi.org/10.13128/bsgi.v1i1.100. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/The_Intervention_of_the_Sabine_Women_%28Vincent%29.png","title":"Sabines","desc":"1781 painting by François-André Vincent. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Intervention_of_the_Sabine_Women_(Vincent).png","alt":"The Intervention of the Sabine Women (Vincent)"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Sabina","at":[12.8667,42.4],"kind":"region","area":[[12.45,42.3],[12.66,42.12],[12.95,42.1],[13.2,42.2],[13.45,42.42],[13.3,42.72],[13,42.8],[12.7,42.62],[12.48,42.48]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the extent of this people's country vague?","a":"Because they were never a political unit, ancient authors disagree over which towns lay in it, and Latin has no place name formed on the ethnic as Samnium and Latium are."},{"q":"Why is their language less certain than their territory?","a":"Because the corpora disagree over which inscriptions are theirs at all, most writing found there is on portable pottery, and the 39 words the grammarians label theirs show inconsistent sound-changes."},{"q":"Why did draining a lake change the country?","a":"Because a channel some 2,200 metres long emptied the Lacus Velinus, over 90 square kilometres of water, and the plain around Reate became farmland."}]},
{"id":"rm-013","num":"13","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"A country full of hillforts, sanctuaries and scattered farms but with few if any cities is what set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart from the Latins and the Etruscans.","answer":"Samnites","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hillforts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th – 3rd century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samnite Wars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 343 – 290 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Samnites</b> were the people of the central and southern Apennines, and their country looked nothing like the Italy of the plains: full of hillforts, sanctuaries and dispersed farms and small clusters, with few if any cities of the kind the Latins and Etruscans built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek and Roman writers turned that into a moral fact rather than a settlement one, calling them rough highlanders and village dwellers and sometimes likening them to wild beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their own tradition explained their spread by the ver sacrum or 'sacred spring', in which a community facing overpopulation sent part of itself away to seek land and become another people, often following an animal that carried a god's will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A lidar survey of 15,300 square kilometres (5,900 square miles) across five modern regions found 305 suspected hillforts, 299 of them recorded in no published material at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several command the tratturi, the drove roads of transhumance, the largest of them set on a spur above the route from Lucera to Castel di Sangro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What those walls enclosed is now being reopened: at Monte Santa Croce-Cognolo, an 18-hectare (44-acre) site long assumed to be inhabited throughout, magnetometry found nothing suggesting permanent housing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it did find were alignments of postholes and trenches more consistent with timber enclosures for livestock, so a monumental circuit need not mean a town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Public life ran instead through the sanctuaries, and Pietrabbondante, the great sanctuary of the Pentri, is taken for their political centre, the focus of the people under arms, where captured weapons including Roman armour were dedicated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Oscan inscriptions there name a meddix tuticus, chief magistrate of a touto, though the tidy pyramid of pagus, touto and Samnite League that handbooks build from such words rests on thinner evidence than it appears to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether Samnium had a hereditary aristocracy or any state-level administration before the wars is not clear from what survives, since that kind of evidence begins to appear only in the 2nd century BCE, after Rome had taken the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Samnites","questions":["A lidar survey of the central Apennines found 305 suspected hillforts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and 299 of them appeared in no published record at all.","A tradition in which part of a community breaks away to seek land elsewhere and becomes another people is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explained their spread across the mountains."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Giacomo Fontana, \"Italy's Hidden Hillforts: A Large-Scale Lidar-Based Mapping of Samnium,\" <i>Journal of Field Archaeology</i> 47, no. 4 (2022): 245–61, https://doi.org/10.1080/00934690.2022.2031465. [Open access]","Giacomo Fontana and Wieke de Neef, \"Italy's Empty Hillforts: Reassessing Urban-Centric Biases through Combined Non-Invasive Prospection Methods on a Samnite Site (Fourth–Third Centuries BC),\" <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 402 (2024): 1558–75, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.157. [Open access]","Rafael Scopacasa, \"The Negotiation of Social Hierarchies in a Mountain Community of Pre-Roman Samnium, Central Italy,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 255–72, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/94264. [Open access]","Massimiliano Di Fazio, \"Herdsmen into Warriors: An Overview on Pastoralism and War in Ancient Central Italy,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 297–314, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/94264. [Open access]","Tesse D. Stek, <i>Cult Places and Cultural Change in Republican Italy</i> (Amsterdam: Amsterdam University Press, 2009), https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/34581. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Samnite_Bronze_Cuirass_Body_Armor%2C_South_Italy%2C_400-300_BC_%2810458801043%29.jpg/1920px-Samnite_Bronze_Cuirass_Body_Armor%2C_South_Italy%2C_400-300_BC_%2810458801043%29.jpg","title":"Samnites","desc":"Getty Villa Museum, Los Angeles, California: Roman, Greek, and Etruscan Antiquities. Complete indexed photo collection at WorldHistoryPics.com. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samnite_Bronze_Cuirass_Body_Armor,_South_Italy,_400-300_BC_(10458801043).jpg","alt":"Samnite Bronze Cuirass Body Armor, South Italy, 400-300 BCE"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Samnium","at":[14.2397,41.6028],"kind":"region","area":[[13.95,41.88],[14.45,41.95],[15,41.55],[15.55,41.05],[15.3,40.72],[14.85,40.85],[14.45,41.05],[14.2,41.35]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Greek and Roman writers make their settlement a moral fact?","a":"Because a country of hillforts, sanctuaries and dispersed farms had few cities of the kind they knew, so they called these people rough highlanders and village dwellers."},{"q":"Why need a monumental circuit not mean a town?","a":"Because at Monte Santa Croce-Cognolo magnetometry found nothing suggesting permanent housing, only alignments of postholes and trenches more consistent with timber enclosures for livestock."},{"q":"Why is the tidy scheme of pagus, touto and league doubted?","a":"Because it is built from a few Oscan words on thinner evidence than it appears to rest on, and state-level administration begins to show only in the 2nd century BCE, after Rome took the country."}]},
{"id":"rm-014","num":"14","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Where Etruscan culture predominated it was written in an Etruscan-derived alphabet and where Greek culture did in a Greek-derived one, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one language written several ways.","answer":"Oscan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE – 79 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Oscan</b> was the language of much of southern Italy, found across Campania, Samnium, Hirpinia, Lucania and Bruttium and as far as Messina, in some 800 inscriptions running from the 6th century BCE to the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the Sabellic languages, with Umbrian and several smaller traditions, and Sabellic sits inside Italic beside Latin and Faliscan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it was ever one tongue or a koiné drawn together from local traditions is argued, and the writing is the strongest evidence either way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where Etruscan culture predominated, in Campania and around it, Oscan was written in an alphabet developed from the Etruscan one; where Greek culture did, in Lucania and Bruttium, in an alphabet developed from the Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later documents use the Latin alphabet instead, so a single language is written three ways according to whose script was to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the texts are short: votive dedications, marks of ownership, epitaphs and boundary stones, with a handful of long legal and religious documents such as the Tabula Bantina and the law from Roccagloriosa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nothing of an Oscan literature survives, though the Ludi Osci and the Atellan farces carried from Campania to Rome imply there was one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most public moment was the Social War, where the rebels' first coins carried ITALIA in Latin, the one language they all shared, and Oscan became the commoner legend only after the Umbrians and Etruscans left the revolt in 90 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the war it was never used in an official text again, and how much longer it was written at all turns on one Pompeian inscription, long read as an imperial copy of a 2nd-century BCE original and argued to be that original itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corpus can now be questioned as a whole, its inscriptions annotated alongside Umbrian, Messapic, Venetic and early Latin in one database of more than 36,000 words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Oscan","questions":["Some 800 inscriptions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive from Campania, Samnium, Lucania and Bruttium, running from the 6th century BCE to graffiti at Pompeii in 79 CE.","After the Social War no official text was written in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> again, though graffiti at Pompeii show it was still being written when the town was buried."],"tags":["language","history","italy","iron age"],"sources":["\"The Oscan Language,\" <i>Lingue e culture dell'Italia antica: linguistica storica e modelli digitali</i> (PRIN project, University of Florence), https://www.prin-italia-antica.unifi.it/vp-133-the-oscan-language.html. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Oscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=56&lang=en. [Open access]","Katherine McDonald, \"The Testament of Vibius Adiranus,\" <i>Journal of Roman Studies</i> 102 (2012): 40–55, https://katherinemcdonald.net/wp-content/uploads/2015/08/mcdonald-testament-of-vibius-adiranus.pdf. [Open access]","Lucia Carbone, \"The First ITALIA on Coinage,\" <i>Pocket Change</i> (American Numismatic Society), 13 October 2020, https://numismatics.org/pocketchange/italia/. [Open access]","Reuben J. Pitts, \"Corpus of the Epigraphy of the Italian Peninsula in the 1st Millennium BCE (CEIPoM),\" <i>Journal of Open Humanities Data</i> 8 (2022): 1, https://doi.org/10.5334/johd.65. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg/1920px-OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg","title":"Oscan","desc":"Stele bearing an inscription in Oscan (British Museum, London). Around 300 - 100 BCE.C. Found in the inner arch of the Nola Gate in Pompeii. Reads from right to left: \"Vibius Popidius, son of Vibius, chief magistrate, was responsible for this work and approved it\". Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg","alt":"OsqueBritishMuseum"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is one language written in three alphabets?","a":"Because where Etruscan culture predominated it was written in an alphabet from the Etruscan one, where Greek culture did in one from the Greek, and later documents used the Latin script."},{"q":"Why did the rebels of the Social War first strike coins in Latin?","a":"Because ITALIA in Latin was the one language they all shared, and Oscan became the commoner legend only after the Umbrians and Etruscans left the revolt in 90 BCE."},{"q":"Why is it hard to say how long the language went on being written?","a":"Because the question turns on one Pompeian inscription, long read as an imperial copy of a 2nd-century BCE original and argued to be that original itself."}]},
{"id":"rm-015","num":"15","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Pliny called them the oldest people of Italy, and by the 9th century BCE the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had one of the first well-defined identities in central Italy.","answer":"Umbrians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Identity formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th – 8th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 260 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Umbrians</b> were the people of ancient Umbria, larger than the modern region: it ran east and west of the Tiber to the edge of the Sabina and over the Apennines into the modern Marche and Emilia-Romagna, leaving out Etruscan places such as Perusia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pliny called them the oldest people of Italy and passed on a Greek explanation of the name, that they had survived the floods; their origins are still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the Early Iron Age of the 9th and 8th centuries BCE they were among the first communities of central Italy with a well-defined identity, beside the Etruscans, the Picentes and the Samnites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They began on the left bank of the Tiber and spread west, until around the 6th century BCE the Etruscans took that ground and the river became the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their settlements gathered in the Apennine basins at Todi, Terni, Gubbio and Ameria and on the Colfiorito plateau, where villages of huts stood a regular 500 metres (1,600 feet) apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Their sacred places were mostly open air, sometimes no more than a pit in bedrock, and of nine fully excavated Umbrian sanctuaries only three had a structure marking the holy ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Above Gubbio the summits of Monte Ingino and Monte Ansciano carried such places, used seasonally — sheep and pig teeth point to spring — and one took offerings again after a gap of three centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Umbrian is a Sabellic language written two ways, in an alphabet derived from the Etruscan and later in the Latin one, its epigraphy running from the 4th century BCE to the 1st.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corpus is badly lopsided: one long ritual text from Gubbio and some thirty short inscriptions, the weightiest of those the bronze Mars of Todi and a helmet from Bologna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rome reached Umbria in the 4th century BCE and held it by the middle of the 3rd, though whether the Umbrians fought at Sentinum in 295 is doubtful: Livy and Polybius report them absent and the triumphal record omits them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Umbrians","questions":["One long ritual text from Gubbio and about thirty short inscriptions are nearly all that survives of what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote in their own language.","Starting on the left bank of the Tiber, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread west until the Etruscans took that ground and the river became the border between the two."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Alessandra Modi et al., \"The Mitogenome Portrait of Umbria in Central Italy as Depicted by Contemporary Inhabitants and Pre-Roman Remains,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 10700, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-67445-0. [Open access]","Arianna Zapelloni Pavia, <i>Continuity and Change in Ancient Umbrian Cult Places</i>, Mnemosyne Supplements 485 (Leiden: Brill, 2024), https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/98984. [Open access]","\"The Umbrian Language,\" <i>Lingue e culture dell'Italia antica: linguistica storica e modelli digitali</i> (PRIN project, University of Florence), https://www.prin-italia-antica.unifi.it/vp-151-the-umbrian-language.html. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Umbrian: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=58&lang=en. [Open access]","Marianna Negro et al., \"Revisiting Gubbio: Settlement Patterns and Ritual from the Middle Palaeolithic to the Roman Era,\" <i>Land</i> 13, no. 9 (2024): 1369, https://doi.org/10.3390/land13091369. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Typological_distribution_of_votive_figurines_across_Umbrian_sanctuaries.png","title":"Umbrians","desc":"Typological distribution of votive figurines across Umbrian sanctuaries. Arianna Pavia. 2024. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Typological_distribution_of_votive_figurines_across_Umbrian_sanctuaries.png","alt":"Typological distribution of votive figurines across Umbrian sanctuaries"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Umbria","at":[12.5793,43.353],"kind":"region","area":[[12.3,43.5],[12.5,44],[12.75,44.08],[13.35,43.65],[13.15,43.28],[12.95,42.85],[12.72,42.55],[12.45,42.6],[12.25,43.05]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Tiber become this people's border?","a":"Because they began on its left bank and spread west until around the 6th century BCE the Etruscans took that ground."},{"q":"Why are their sanctuaries hard to recognise on the ground?","a":"Because they were mostly open air, sometimes no more than a pit in bedrock, and of nine fully excavated ones only three had a structure marking the holy ground."},{"q":"Why is their language corpus called lopsided?","a":"Because it is one long ritual text from Gubbio against some thirty short inscriptions, the weightiest of those a bronze Mars and a helmet."}]},
{"id":"rm-016","num":"16","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Seven bronze tablets from Gubbio, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry the longest ritual text to survive from ancient Italy, with no Latin or Greek liturgy approaching their detail.","answer":"Iguvine Tables","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 1st century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Iguvine Tables</b> are seven bronze tablets from Iguvium, the modern Gubbio, and they carry the longest ritual text surviving from ancient Italy: no liturgy in Latin or Greek approaches them for detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They came to light near the Roman theatre, traditionally in 1444, though the earliest secure document is the deed of 1456 by which the seven passed to the commune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were inscribed at different times between the 3rd and 1st centuries BCE, first in the alphabet the Umbrians had taken from the Etruscans and later in the Latin one, though the rites themselves are older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are also nearly the whole of Umbrian: about 4,000 words here against some thirty short inscriptions everywhere else, which makes them the longest text in any non-Latin language of ancient Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What they record are the proceedings and prayers of the Atiedian Brothers, a priestly association who in early times were probably also the political leaders of the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rites are purifications and sacrifices, some to be performed when the omens came out wrong, others fixed to feasts or to points in the farming year, and in places the words of the prayer itself are written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before a lustration of the people the officiant is told to go out and watch the birds, and only on returning to put on his vestment and drive the victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One fire ritual has the fire kindled on the way out to the field and set on the altar in the grove, carried there in a vessel the text names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same ceremony is set out twice over, once in the native script and once in Latin letters, which is what allows the older alphabet to be read against the newer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The text also instructs the community to shun its neighbours, so the liturgy is as much a statement of who Iguvium was not as a record of what it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Iguvine Tables","questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set out the same ceremony in the native Umbrian script and again in Latin letters, the older alphabet can be read against the newer.","The prayers and proceedings of a priestly brotherhood at Iguvium are set down in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which tell the officiant to go out and watch the birds before purifying the people."],"tags":["text","history","italy","religion","language"],"sources":["\"Room with the Iguvine Tables,\" Museo Civico di Palazzo dei Consoli, Gubbio, https://www.palazzodeiconsoli.it/en/room-with-the-iguvine-tables/. [Open access]","\"The Iguvine Tables,\" Museo Civico Palazzo dei Consoli, Gubbio, <i>MeTU: Musei e Territorio dell'Umbria</i>, https://www.umbriametu.it/museums/museo-civico-palazzo-dei-consoli-gubbio/the-iguvine-tables/?lang=en. [Open access]","Gregory Nagy, \"The Fire Ritual of the Iguvine Tables: Facing a Central Problem in the Study of Ritual Language,\" <i>Classical World</i> 100, no. 2 (2007): 151–57; 2nd ed. online 2015, https://chs.harvard.edu/curated-article/gregory-nagy-the-fire-ritual-of-the-iguvine-tables-facing-a-central-problem-in-the-study-of-ritual-language/. [Open access]","Arianna Zapelloni Pavia, <i>Continuity and Change in Ancient Umbrian Cult Places</i>, Mnemosyne Supplements 485 (Leiden: Brill, 2024), https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/98984. [Open access]","\"The Umbrian Language,\" <i>Lingue e culture dell'Italia antica: linguistica storica e modelli digitali</i> (PRIN project, University of Florence), https://www.prin-italia-antica.unifi.it/vp-151-the-umbrian-language.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Tavole_iguvine%2C_in_lingua_umbra_III-I_secolo_ac._ca.%2C_00.JPG/1920px-Tavole_iguvine%2C_in_lingua_umbra_III-I_secolo_ac._ca.%2C_00.JPG","title":"Iguvine Tables","desc":"Iguvine Tablets. Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tavole_iguvine,_in_lingua_umbra_III-I_secolo_ac._ca.,_00.JPG","alt":"Tavole iguvine, in lingua umbra III-I secolo ac. ca., 00"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why do these tablets carry so much weight for one language?","a":"Because their 4,000 words stand against some thirty short inscriptions everywhere else, which makes them the longest text in any non-Latin language of ancient Italy."},{"q":"Why does one ceremony appear on them twice?","a":"Because it is set out once in the native script and once in Latin letters, which is what allows the older alphabet to be read against the newer."},{"q":"Why is the liturgy also a statement about neighbours?","a":"Because the text instructs the community to shun them, so the rites say as much about who Iguvium was not as about what it did."}]},
{"id":"rm-017","num":"17","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Roman writers set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside the Aequi among the enemies who pressed hardest on the young city, coming down from the Apennines towards the Pontine plain.","answer":"Volsci","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Took Satricum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">488 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Their graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th – early 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Volsci</b> were a people of the hills and plains south-east of Rome, and Roman writers set them beside the Aequi among the enemies who pressed hardest on the young city. The historical narrative makes them aggressive: a community that came down from the Apennine country towards the Pontine plain during the 5th century BCE, until it occupied Satricum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their speech was Sabellic, and Volscian is counted among the minor Italic traditions that with Paelignian, Vestinian, Marrucinian, Marsian and perhaps Aequian make up a Middle-Italic linguistic area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Satricum lay about 60 kilometres (37 miles) south of Rome on the river Astura, grown from a hamlet of huts in the 9th century BCE to nearly 40 hectares by the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1980 a burial ground of at least 200 graves came to light in its south-west corner, its pottery of the 5th and early 4th centuries BCE, and those graves have been given to the Volsci.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Among them were about twenty children's graves, some of the richest in the cemetery, and one held a fine miniature lead axe inscribed in a non-Latin language identified as Volscian, in an alphabet unknown in Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The burials sit awkwardly with the warlike picture: 16 weapons in almost 200 graves, mostly with older people, which reads as a mark of rank rather than a working armoury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isotope work on 43 third molars from three of the presumed Volscian cemeteries found every individual local, and the excavators' own account calls these Volsci an indigenous community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Aequi held the mountain country further east, and Rome's answer to them stands at Alba Fucens, on a hill of 950 to 1,000 metres (3,100 to 3,300 feet) above the Fucine plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That Latin colony was planted in 303 BCE in Aequian territory, on the line of the Via Tiburtina Valeria, and is now the largest archaeological area in the Apennines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Volsci","questions":["A miniature lead axe from a child's grave at Satricum carries an inscription in the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, written in an alphabet unknown in Latium.","Only 16 weapons turned up in almost 200 graves at Satricum, which fits badly with the warlike picture Roman writers drew of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who took the town."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","language"],"sources":["Massimiliano Di Fazio, \"Herdsmen into Warriors. An Overview on Pastoralism and War in Ancient Central Italy,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 297–314, https://doi.org/10.1515/9783111558417-012. [Open access]","\"Satricum,\" \"Excavations Southwest Necropolis 1980–1986\" and \"Epigraphic Finds,\" Satricum Project, Department of Archaeology, University of Amsterdam, https://www.uva.nl/en/discipline/archaeology/research/satricum/southwest-necropolis/southwest-necropolis.html. [Open access]","Maria Alicandro et al., \"Alba Fucens Archaeological Site: Multiscale and Multidisciplinary Approach for Risk Assessment and Conservation,\" <i>International Archives of the Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing and Spatial Information Sciences</i> XLII-2/W11 (2019): 47–53, https://doi.org/10.5194/isprs-archives-XLII-2-W11-47-2019. [Open access]","Alessia Ventriglia, \"Oscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=56&lang=en. [Open access]","Amanda Sengeløv et al., \"Understanding the Post-Archaic Population of Satricum, Italy: A Bioarchaeological Approach,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports</i> 31 (2020): 102285, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jasrep.2020.102285. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/John_Harris_Valda_-_A_Roman_battle_with_the_Volscians.jpg","title":"Volsci","desc":"John Harris Valda - A Roman battle with the Volscians. John Harris Valda. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Harris_Valda_-_A_Roman_battle_with_the_Volscians.jpg","alt":"John Harris Valda - A Roman battle with the Volscians"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Satricum","at":[12.755083,41.513128],"zoom":9},"why":[{"q":"Why do the graves at Satricum sit awkwardly with the Roman picture?","a":"Because there are 16 weapons in almost 200 graves, mostly with older people, which reads as a mark of rank rather than a working armoury."},{"q":"Why do the excavators call this an indigenous community?","a":"Because isotope work on 43 third molars from three of the presumed cemeteries found every individual local, where the narrative has the people coming down from the Apennines."},{"q":"Why does one child's grave carry so much weight?","a":"Because it held a miniature lead axe inscribed in a non-Latin language identified as Volscian, in an alphabet unknown in Latium."}]},
{"id":"rm-018","num":"18","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Living in hilltop forts behind dry-stone walls, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the country from modern Piedmont and Liguria across into southern France before Rome came.","answer":"Ligurians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">180 – 179 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ligurians</b> were the Iron Age people of the north-west, settled across what are now Liguria, Piedmont, western Lombardy and Emilia and on into southern France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They took shape between the Middle Bronze Age, about 1600 BCE, and the opening of the Iron Age around 900, and are counted a people of the 9th to the 2nd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They lived in castellari, hilltop forts ringed with dry-stone walls and set where the ground already defended them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their graves are those of fighters: the chief buried at Pietra Ligure went down with weapons including an antenna sword of central European type, and at Savignone a sword was folded back on itself before burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greek and Roman writers set them down as tribes of barbarians — illiterate, treacherous and lying, but daring and brave in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The charge of lying can be traced to one man: Cato wrote in his <i>Origines</i> that they were unlettered and untruthful, and he wrote it to explain why he could not establish where they had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had defended them in the senate in 190 BCE, and what turned him was the risings of 187 to 175 and the welcome they had given Hannibal's brother Mago in 205; the slur stayed rare, picked up by Virgil and Cicero and almost nobody else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome's own answer to those risings was deportation: about 47,000 Apuan Ligurians were moved from the Magra and Vara valleys to Samnium in two operations, in 180 and 179 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Planted on land confiscated from the Samnites, they became the Ligures Baebiani, named for the consul who organised the transfer, and magnetic survey at Macchia di Circello has traced their main street and a market building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A bronze plate of 117 BCE, found in 1506 in the Polcevera valley, shows the settlement of it: two Roman senators fixing what Genoa and the neighbouring Ligurian tribes might each take from the public land they had grazed for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ligurians","questions":["About 47,000 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were moved from the Magra and Vara valleys to Samnium in two operations of 180 and 179 BCE, and settled on confiscated land.","Cato wrote that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were unlettered and untruthful, which was less an observation than a way of explaining why he could not establish where they came from."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"sources":["\"The Metal Ages,\" Museo Diffuso del Finale, Istituto Internazionale di Studi Liguri – Sezione Finalese, https://mudifinale.com/en/percorso-tematico/the-metal-ages/. [Open access]","\"The Ligurians and the Birth of Genoa\" and \"The Ligurians and Rome,\" Museo di Archeologia Ligure, Musei di Genova, https://www.museidigenova.it/en/ligurians-and-birth-genoa. [Open access]","Maciej Piegdoń, \"Sententia Minuciorum/Tabula Polcevera from 117 BCE – Arbitration as an Instrument for Resolving Disputes among Allies during the Republican Period and a Source to Analyse Rome's Policy Regarding Subordinate Lands,\" <i>In Gremium: Studies in History, Culture and Politics</i>, no. 18 (2025): 77–91, https://doi.org/10.61826/ig.vi18.449. [Open access]","Sina Clara Lehnig and Nikola Babucic, \"Forced Resettlement as an Imperial Strategy: Evidence from the Ligures Baebiani in Roman Samnium,\" <i>Antiquity</i>, First View (2026): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2026.10391. [Open access]","Michel Dubuisson, \"Caton et les Ligures: l'origine d'un stéréotype,\" <i>Revue belge de Philologie et d'Histoire</i> 68, no. 1 (1990): 73–83, https://www.persee.fr/doc/rbph_0035-0818_1990_num_68_1_3700. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Celtas_ligures_iberos.jpg","title":"Ligurians","desc":"Map of the distribution of prerroman tribes in the Iberian Peninsula. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Celtas_ligures_iberos.jpg","alt":"Celtas ligures iberos"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Liguria","at":[8.9339,44.4072],"kind":"region","area":[[6.9,43.62],[7.1,44.3],[7.3,44.95],[8.2,45.35],[9.3,45.15],[10.35,44.72],[10.25,44.1],[9.85,44.05],[8.95,44.28],[7.6,43.79]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Cato call these people untruthful?","a":"Because he wrote it in his Origines to explain why he could not establish where they had come from, having himself defended them in the senate twenty years earlier."},{"q":"Why did the slur spread so little?","a":"Because it was picked up by Virgil and Cicero and almost nobody else, so it stayed rare rather than becoming the standard Roman view."},{"q":"Why were tens of thousands of them moved to Samnium?","a":"Because Rome answered the risings of 187 to 175 BCE by deporting about 47,000 of them from the Magra and Vara valleys onto land confiscated from the Samnites."}]},
{"id":"rm-019","num":"19","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"At the sanctuary of Reitia at Este the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left bronze writing tablets and styluses as votives, which is how we know how they taught the alphabet.","answer":"Veneti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE – 50 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Veneti</b> lived in north-eastern Italy, in what is now the Veneto, and spoke an Indo-European language slightly similar to Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It survives on several hundred brief inscriptions scattered across the region and dating between about 550 BCE and 50 CE, some of them still obscure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hierarchy and proto-urban settlement are visible from the 8th century BCE, and Padua and Este may have had a full urban layout by the 6th, when many of the great sanctuaries were flourishing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They wrote in an alphabet taken from the Etruscans in two phases: a northern Etruscan model through the 6th century, then a southern one that split into distinct local varieties at Este, Padua and Vicenza.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Situla art, the figured bronze vessels of the region, ran from about 650 to 275 BCE, and it and writing alike spread first in elite hands through deep contact with Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The sanctuary of the goddess Reitia at Este holds the best evidence in ancient Italy for how writing was taught, because the tablets and styluses used to teach it were offered there as votives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bronze tablets carry a grid of five rows of sixteen boxes holding a list of fifteen consonants and the five vowels repeated, and the styluses, 13 to 26 centimetres (5 to 10 inches) long, are large enough to have been working tools rather than models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their inscribed grave markers are arranged in strikingly different ways, some running round a cylinder, some beside figured panels, some with the second line upside down against the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cremation was the usual rite and inhumation may have been reserved for low-ranking people; isotopes from nineteen inhumed individuals at Padua's CUS-Piovego cemetery show a narrow diet leaning heavily on millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They kept their independence through the Celtic invasion of the Po plain in the 4th century BCE and the first Roman advance north; Roman policy hardened in the 2nd, and the region was annexed in the 1st.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Veneti","questions":["Holding out through the Celtic invasion of the Po plain and the first Roman advance north, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not annexed until the 1st century BCE.","Several hundred short inscriptions in an Indo-European language close to Latin, running from about 550 BCE to 50 CE, are what survive of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and their speech."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","language"],"sources":["Laura Montagnaro, \"Venetic,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Scrittura&id=31&lang=en. [Open access]","Elisa Perego, \"Magic and Ritual in Iron Age Veneto, Italy,\" <i>Papers from the Institute of Archaeology</i> 20 (2010): 67–96, https://doi.org/10.5334/pia.342. [Open access]","Elisa Perego, \"The Other Writing: Iconic Literacy and Situla Art in Pre-Roman Veneto (Italy),\" in <i>Writing as Material Practice: Substance, Surface and Medium</i> (London: Ubiquity Press, 2013), 253–70, https://doi.org/10.5334/bai.m. [Open access]","Giusy Capasso et al., \"Dietary Perspectives on Social Asymmetry in a Full Iron Age Community of Northern Italy: Stable Isotope Evidence from the Patavine CUS-Piovego Necropolis,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 15208, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-51643-3. [Open access]","Ruth D. Whitehouse, \"'Tombstones' in the North Italian Iron Age: Careless Writers or Athletic Readers?,\" in <i>Writing as Material Practice: Substance, Surface and Medium</i> (London: Ubiquity Press, 2013), 271–88, https://doi.org/10.5334/bai.n. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Panel_of_Este%2C_Venetic_Inscription.jpg","title":"Veneti","desc":"The Panel of Este (VI sec. b.c.). An ancient instrument for teaching the Venetic language. The inscription represents the possible articulations of the same word. Giorgio Roncolato, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panel_of_Este,_Venetic_Inscription.jpg","alt":"Panel of Este, Venetic Inscription"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Venetia","at":[11.8733,45.4078],"kind":"region","area":[[11.05,45.4],[11.35,45.85],[12.05,46.05],[12.75,45.95],[13.05,45.75],[12.75,45.4],[12.35,45.05],[11.75,44.95],[11.1,45.05]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is one sanctuary the best evidence for teaching writing in ancient Italy?","a":"Because the tablets and styluses used to teach it were offered there as votives, the tablets carrying a grid of boxes with the consonants and the repeated vowels."},{"q":"Why are the styluses thought to be working tools?","a":"Because at 13 to 26 centimetres long they are large enough to have been used rather than made only as models for dedication."},{"q":"Why did the alphabet split into local varieties?","a":"Because it was taken from the Etruscans in two phases, a northern model through the 6th century and then a southern one that diverged at Este, Padua and Vicenza."}]},
{"id":"rm-020","num":"20","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"One of the three Iapygian cultures of Iron Age Apulia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the heel of Italy and wrote in an alphabet modelled on the Greek of nearby Taranto.","answer":"Messapians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Writing ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hannibalic war, c. 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Messapians</b> held the heel of Italy, and were one of the three cultures — with the Peucetians and the Daunians — that made up the Iapygian civilisation of Iron Age Apulia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> All three names reach us from Greek documents rather than from anything the peoples themselves wrote about who they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their own writing survives as Messapic, a corpus running from the middle of the 6th century to the end of the 2nd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard collection, published in 2002, registered 545 epigraphic items with 48 more counted doubtful and about 30 coins, and almost a hundred further texts have appeared since at Vaste, Castro and Ostuni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nearest model for their alphabet is the Laconian one of Taranto, the Greek city on their border, and some locally made pieces carry Greek letters outright, probably the work of resident Greek craftsmen living among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the corpus is funerary; a smaller group is religious, and a scarce few public inscriptions on stone carry the longest texts of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trozzella is theirs and nobody else's: a vase with a funerary function chiefly linked to high-ranking women, its modern name taken from the little wheels set on its handles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The burial grounds are substantial — the necropolis of Monte d'Elia at Alezio belongs to one of the most important ancient settlements in the Salento peninsula, and geophysics is now used to trace how far it runs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their landscape held towns behind major walls and lines of intervisible towers, and at Muro Leccese the largest Archaic house yet found in Messapia has been dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That house ended in arrowheads, catapult balls and fire, and the writing stops with the wars against Hannibal, after which the region blends into the Italiote world Rome was assembling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Messapians","questions":["The trozzella, a wheel-handled vase buried mainly with high-ranking women, is a form made by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and by nobody else in ancient Italy.","Some 545 inscriptions running from the mid-6th to the late 2nd century BCE are what remains of the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them from graves."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","language"],"sources":["Simona Marchesini, \"Messapic,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore, updated December 2022), https://doi.org/10.25429/sns.it/lettere/mnamon044. [Open access]","Serena Aneli et al., \"The Genetic Origin of Daunians and the Pan-Mediterranean Southern Italian Iron Age Context,\" <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 39, no. 2 (2022): msac014, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8826970/. [Open access]","Angela Falezza and Francesco Iacono, \"Archaeological Discoveries and New Data from South Italy,\" <i>Archaeological Reports</i> 70 (2024): 139–72, https://doi.org/10.1017/S057060842400005X. [Open access]","\"The Apulian Ceramics,\" Museum of Archaeological Sciences and Art, University of Padua, https://archeologia.musei.unipd.it/en/discover-the-museum/the-apulian-ceramics/. [Open access]","Lara De Giorgi et al., \"Electromagnetic Survey at the Messapian Necropolis in Alezio (Lecce, Italy),\" in <i>IMEKO TC4 International Conference on Metrology for Archaeology and Cultural Heritage</i> (Rome, 2023), https://doi.org/10.21014/tc4-ARC-2023.189. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Map_of_Ancient_Italy%2C_Southern_Part.jpg","title":"Messapians","desc":"Reference Map of Ancient Italy, Southern Part. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ancient_Italy,_Southern_Part.jpg","alt":"Map of Ancient Italy, Southern Part"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Messapia","at":[18.1667,40.35],"kind":"region","area":[[17.25,40.47],[17.6,40.68],[17.95,40.72],[18.35,40.55],[18.52,40.15],[18.36,39.79],[18,39.98],[17.75,40.25]]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the names of these cultures not their own?","a":"Because all three reach us from Greek documents rather than from anything the peoples themselves wrote about who they were."},{"q":"Why does their alphabet point to one neighbouring city?","a":"Because its nearest model is the Laconian alphabet of Taranto, the Greek city on their border, and some local pieces carry Greek letters outright."},{"q":"Why is most of what survives funerary?","a":"Because the corpus is chiefly grave texts, with a smaller religious group and a scarce few public inscriptions on stone that carry the longest texts of all."}]},
{"id":"rm-021","num":"21","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Settling the Po valley from about 400 BCE with La Tène gear, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> absorbed the older Lepontic-speaking communities of the lake country.","answer":"Cisalpine Gauls","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th – 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cisalpine Gauls</b> were the transalpine Celtic communities established on the Italian peninsula between the 4th and the 1st centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lake country between Lugano, Maggiore and Como had belonged before them to the Lepontii, whose speech is reckoned the oldest attested Continental Celtic and is known from about 150 short inscriptions running from the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those inscriptions fall in two phases: an earlier one belonging to the last part of the Golasecca culture, and a second opening around 400 BCE with the invasion of the Po valley by Gauls carrying the material of La Tène.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaulish itself was written here in an Etruscan alphabet, the oldest of the three ways the language was ever set down, with half a dozen inscriptions concentrated around Lugano.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two of that corpus's three stone inscriptions, at Todi and Vercelli, are bilingual in Gaulish and Latin, and belong at least to the second part of the 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the Apennines behind Bologna, Monte Bibele is a village with a necropolis and a votive deposit of the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, an Etruscan foundation of some 7,000 square metres (1.7 acres) sealed by a sudden fire late in the 3rd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It belongs to a wider reordering of those hills, small settlements set on the passes and held by Italic peoples and transalpine Celts in alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strontium and dental evidence from 38 burials at Monterenzio Vecchio, across the valley, found locals and incomers together, the incomers sharing dental affinities with Celts from Yorkshire, and no measurable difference in grave goods between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Cenomani cemetery at Verona gives a different answer: of 49 individuals of the 3rd to 1st centuries BCE, one is clearly not local and five more may not be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The migration was real and the communities it made were mixed and settled quickly, and Lepontic was absorbed first into Gaulish and then, once Rome held the north, into Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cisalpine Gauls","questions":["At Monterenzio Vecchio the graves of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held both local people and incomers, and the grave goods do not tell the two apart.","Bilingual stones at Todi and Vercelli, cut in Gaulish and Latin, are among the few stone inscriptions the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left in Italy."],"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","migration"],"sources":["Francesca Ciurli, \"Celtic, Lepontic,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Scrittura&id=59&lang=en. [Open access]","Coline Ruiz Darasse, \"Celtic, Gaulish,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore, updated April 2022), https://doi.org/10.25429/sns.it/lettere/mnamon027. [Open access]","Rita Sorrentino et al., \"Unravelling Biocultural Population Structure in 4th/3rd Century BC Monterenzio Vecchio (Bologna, Italy) through a Comparative Analysis of Strontium Isotopes, Non-metric Dental Evidence, and Funerary Practices,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0193796, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0193796. [Open access]","Zita Laffranchi et al., \"'Celts' up and down the Alps: Insights on Mobility Patterns in the Pre-Roman/Celtic Population from Verona (NE Italy, 3rd–1st c. BCE),\" <i>American Journal of Biological Anthropology</i> 178, no. 3 (2022): 513–29, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9544713/. [Open access]","Annachiara Penzo, Federica Proni and Antonio Gottarelli, \"The Archaeological Settlement of Monte Bibele (Bologna),\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 571–80, https://doi.org/10.19282/AC.28.2.2017.47. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Gallia_cisalpina_-_Shepherd_png.png","title":"Cisalpine Gauls","desc":"Territori della Gallia cisalpina attorno agli inizi del I secolo a.C. Historical Atlas by William R. Shepherd, 1911. Courtesy of the University of Texas Libraries, The University of Texas at Austin. From The Historical Atlas by William R. Shepherd, 1911 edition. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gallia_cisalpina_-_Shepherd_png.png","alt":"Gallia cisalpina - Shepherd png"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why do the inscriptions of the lake country fall into two phases?","a":"Because the earlier belongs to the last part of the Golasecca culture and the second opens around 400 BCE with the invasion of the Po valley by Gauls carrying the material of La Tène."},{"q":"Why is the migration read as producing mixed and settled communities?","a":"Because at Monterenzio Vecchio locals and incomers were buried together with no measurable difference in grave goods, the incomers sharing dental affinities with Celts from Yorkshire."},{"q":"Why does the Verona cemetery give a different answer?","a":"Because of 49 individuals of the 3rd to 1st centuries BCE only one is clearly not local and five more may not be."}]},
{"id":"rm-022","num":"22","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Rome's great neighbour to the north, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled the country between the Arno, the Tiber and the sea with rival city-states and never became an empire.","answer":"Etruscan civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Nucleation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Etruscan civilisation</b> was that of the rich communities of Tyrrhenian central Italy in the 1st millennium BCE, between the Arno, the Tiber and the sea, and was absorbed at last into the Roman empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its political landscape was set by a gathering of population in the 10th century BCE running alongside the making of an identity; it went furthest in the south and on the coast, less far north and inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scattered sites of 2 or 3 hectares (5 to 7 acres) were left for centres on plateaus of 50 to 185 hectares (120 to 460 acres), and by the Orientalising and Archaic periods these were full city-states, spaced on average 15 to 25 kilometres (9 to 16 miles) apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two kinds of town stood side by side: robust ones that held together for four or five centuries with practices for handing on authority, and delicate ones that lasted a few generations and then dispersed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No Etruscan empire ever came of it, because rivalry between descent group and community, and between one centre and the next, kept the whole in equilibrium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those two loyalties were built in stone, the community in encircling city walls and the descent groups in cemeteries outside them so large that they were settlements in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing was distributed as unevenly as power: in the south literacy was held tightly by the great centres, while in the north it reached far down into the small ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Etruscan inscriptions run from the 8th century BCE to the 1st, and the language was spoken well beyond Etruria — south around Capua and Pontecagnano, north between Bologna and Verucchio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their reach was commercial too: amphorae shipped to Lattara in southern France about 525 to 475 BCE held wine resinated with pine and flavoured with rosemary, basil and thyme, and the trade ended in vines planted there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prosperity had a price, and at Pontecagnano five of 29 children buried between 730 and 580 BCE show scurvy, read as the cost of an agriculture leaning ever harder on cereals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan civilisation","questions":["Robust towns that lasted five centuries stood beside delicate ones that dispersed after a few generations, and both belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Tyrrhenian central Italy.","Amphorae of resinated wine reached a port in southern France about 500 BCE from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and vines were planted there soon afterwards."],"tags":["culture","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Simon Stoddart et al., \"Patterns of Etruscan Urbanism,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 7 (2020): 1, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2020.00001. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart, \"Power and Place in Etruria,\" in <i>Eurasia at the Dawn of History: Urbanization and Social Change</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2017), 304–18, https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/handle/1810/260822. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]","Rachele Simonit et al., \"Infantile Scurvy as a Consequence of Agricultural Intensification in the 1st Millennium BCE Etruria Campana,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 21396, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-48455-0. [Open access]","Patrick E. McGovern et al., \"Beginning of Viniculture in France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 25 (2013): 10147–52, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3690885/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bronze_chariot_inlaid_with_ivory_MET_DP137936.jpg/1920px-Bronze_chariot_inlaid_with_ivory_MET_DP137936.jpg","title":"Etruscan civilisation","desc":"Etruscan; Chariot; Bronzes. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_chariot_inlaid_with_ivory_MET_DP137936.jpg","alt":"Bronze chariot inlaid with ivory"},"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"Etruria","at":[11.7561,42.2492],"kind":"region","area":[[10.15,43.95],[10.95,44.05],[11.55,43.85],[11.95,43.5],[12.25,43.2],[12.45,42.8],[12.55,42.35],[12.5,42.05],[12.35,41.9],[11.95,41.8],[11.3,42.25],[10.5,42.85],[10.15,43.5]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did no Etruscan empire come out of so much wealth?","a":"Because rivalry between descent group and community, and between one centre and the next, kept the whole in equilibrium."},{"q":"Why are the cemeteries as telling as the city walls?","a":"Because the two loyalties were built in stone, the community in encircling walls and the descent groups in cemeteries outside them so large that they were settlements in their own right."},{"q":"Why did prosperity leave scurvy in children's bones?","a":"Because five of 29 children buried at Pontecagnano show it, read as the cost of an agriculture leaning ever harder on cereals."}]},
{"id":"rm-023","num":"23","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The argument over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is as old as Herodotus, who derived them from Anatolia, and Dionysius of Halicarnassus, who held they had always been where they were.","answer":"Etruscan origins","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Local roots</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Genomes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 BCE – 1000 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan origins</b> have been disputed since antiquity, and the argument has always had two sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek writers Herodotus and Hellanicus of Lesbos derived the Etruscans from Anatolia or the Aegean, a reading the Greek elements arriving in Etruria during the Orientalising period of the 8th to 6th centuries BCE did nothing to discourage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus, writing in the 1st century BCE, held the opposite, that they had grown up where they stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists now favour Dionysius, tracing the civilisation to the late Bronze Age Proto-Villanovan communities of about 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That consensus is a modern achievement as much as an ancient one, argued after Fascism from continuities in material culture across the turn of the 2nd and 1st millennia BCE and from unbroken occupation of the same places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What keeps the question alive is the language, a non-Indo-European isolate surrounded by Italic speakers and still not fully understood, its inscriptions running from the 8th century BCE to the 1st and consisting mostly of names and repeating formulas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not quite alone: Etruscan, Lemnian and Raetic were grouped in 1998 into a family called Tyrsenian, and the stele from Kaminia on Lemnos shares Etruscan's case endings and words — <i>nefts</i> for a descendant, <i>avils</i> for year, <i>sealchvis</i> for forty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> But the split between the three is reckoned prehistoric and probably older than the Bronze Age, so the family points far behind any voyage of the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomes from 82 people buried in Etruria and southern Italy between 800 BCE and 1000 CE found Indo-European steppe ancestry among the Etruscans and no recent Anatolian admixture at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The local gene pool held across the 1st millennium BCE even while taking in individuals of central European, north African and Near Eastern descent, and shifted abruptly only under the Roman empire, when about half the ancestry became eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan origins","questions":["Genomes from 82 people buried between 800 BCE and 1000 CE bear on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, finding steppe ancestry and no recent Anatolian admixture in the Iron Age.","Because Etruscan, Lemnian and Raetic split prehistorically, the family bears on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by pointing much further back than any voyage from the Aegean."],"tags":["concept","history","italy","genetics","language"],"sources":["Cosimo Posth et al., \"The Origin and Legacy of the Etruscans through a 2000-Year Archeogenomic Time Transect,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 39 (2021): eabi7673, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi7673. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Lemnian: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=26&lang=en. [Open access]","Simona Marchesini, \"Rhaetic: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=41&lang=en. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart, \"Power and Place in Etruria,\" in <i>Eurasia at the Dawn of History: Urbanization and Social Change</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2017), 304–18, https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/handle/1810/260822. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Etruscan_funerary_stone_inscription_%28CIE_5071%29.png/1920px-Etruscan_funerary_stone_inscription_%28CIE_5071%29.png","title":"Etruscan origins","desc":"Drawing of Etruscan funerary stone inscription from Orvieto (CIE 5071). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_funerary_stone_inscription_(CIE_5071).png","alt":"Etruscan funerary stone inscription (CIE 5071)"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why has the question been argued since antiquity?","a":"Because Herodotus and Hellanicus derived these people from Anatolia or the Aegean, while Dionysius of Halicarnassus held that they had grown up where they stood."},{"q":"Why does the language not settle it?","a":"Because although it is a non-Indo-European isolate, its family with Lemnian and Raetic split prehistorically and probably before the Bronze Age, pointing far behind any voyage of the 6th century."},{"q":"Why does the genetic evidence favour the local account?","a":"Because 82 genomes found Indo-European steppe ancestry and no recent Anatolian admixture, the local gene pool holding across the 1st millennium BCE."}]},
{"id":"rm-024","num":"24","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Each of the four great <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the south — Veii, Tarquinia, Caere and Vulci — covered between 125 and 175 hectares (310 and 430 acres) of volcanic plateau.","answer":"Etruscan cities","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plateau sites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Perugia's gates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan cities</b> stood on volcanic plateaus, and the four great ones of the south — Veii, Tarquinia, Caere and Vulci — each covered between 125 and 175 hectares (310 and 430 acres), a scale nothing before them approached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were probably not built up all over: the evidence points to nuclei of people occupying separate parts of each plateau, with perhaps some common identity across the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The countryside around them filled and then thinned, 549 dwelling sites being known from the Iron Age and Orientalising, 1,248 from the Archaic and 914 from the Post-Archaic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fortification mattered, though how much is argued: there was large-scale effort to wall the new sites, while the early defences at Veii's Piazza d'Armi are now thought to be medieval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What certainly survives is later and unmistakable, the gates of Perugia, built between the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE and now all but swallowed by the houses grown around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Not every city grew by accretion: Marzabotto, in the Apennines above Bologna, was laid out at the end of the 6th century BCE on a regular plan of insulae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its layout is thought to have been set by astronomical and solar events, so that the streets on the ground repeated a pattern read in the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside one of those regular blocks stood a peripteral temple to Tinia, the highest god of the Etruscan pantheon, and a Tuscanic temple of about 500 BCE was found beside it in 2014 and 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city was abandoned during the 4th century BCE, when Celtic migration reached the Po plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smaller places keep the same shape of plateau and cemetery, and at San Giuliano an Etruscan town and its rock-cut necropolis are being dug to follow how settlement there shifted from about 800 BCE onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan cities","questions":["Unlike most <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Marzabotto was laid out at once on a regular plan of insulae at the end of the 6th century BCE, and abandoned when the Celts came.","The gates of Perugia, built between the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, are among the best-preserved remains of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, now swallowed by the houses around them."],"tags":["place","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","\"The Etruscan City of Kainua-Marzabotto,\" Department of History and Cultures, University of Bologna, https://disci.unibo.it/en/research/archaeological-missions/archaeological-missions-italy/etruscan-city-kainua-marzabotto. [Open access]","Fabio Radicioni et al., \"The Etruscan City Gates of Perugia: Geomatic Techniques for the Documentation and Study of an Urban History Heritage,\" in <i>Proceedings of ARQUEOLÓGICA 2.0</i> (Valencia: Universitat Politècnica de València, 2021), https://doi.org/10.4995/arqueologica9.2021.12058. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart et al., \"Patterns of Etruscan Urbanism,\" <i>Frontiers in Digital Humanities</i> 7 (2020): 1, https://doi.org/10.3389/fdigh.2020.00001. [Open access]","Davide Zori et al., \"From Etruscan Urban Centre to Medieval Fortified Village: San Giuliano Archaeological Research Project,\" <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 363 (2018): e6, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2018.99. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Etruscan_civilization_map.png","title":"Etruscan cities","desc":"A map showing the extent of Etruria and the Etruscan civilization. The map includes the 12 cities of the Etruscan League and notable cities founded by the Etruscans. The dates on the map are an approximation. NormanEinstein, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_civilization_map.png","alt":"Etruscan civilization map"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is it doubted that these plateaus were built up all over?","a":"Because the evidence points to nuclei of people occupying separate parts of each one, with perhaps some common identity across the whole."},{"q":"Why is one city's plan unlike the rest?","a":"Because Marzabotto was laid out at the end of the 6th century BCE on a regular plan of insulae, thought to have been set by astronomical and solar events."},{"q":"Why is the early fortification at Veii no longer good evidence?","a":"Because the defences at Piazza d'Armi are now thought to be medieval, so what certainly survives is later work like the gates of Perugia."}]},
{"id":"rm-025","num":"25","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Ancient authors put twelve peoples at one shared sanctuary, which is what modern scholars mean by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though no list of members has ever been agreed.","answer":"Etruscan league","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctuary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE – 4th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statue base</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Etruscan league</b> is what modern scholars call the association of twelve peoples that ancient writers describe meeting at a shared sanctuary — and the word is a modern one, where Livy writes <i>concilium</i> or <i>foedus</i>, terms of far more transitory meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He does attest a league of twelve cities, but no list of members has ever been agreed, the membership may have shifted over time, and candidate cities cannot always be matched against physical remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The harder difficulty is that no evidence from the Etruscans themselves says they were organised in any such way, a point most accounts pass over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their own titles do not settle it either: <i>zilath mechl rasnal</i> was long read as head of a national confederation, but if <i>rasnal</i> stands for something like a commonwealth rather than 'the Etruscans', the phrase is a city magistracy and no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What can be said is that the southern cities had a federal structure for some purposes, while whether the northern ones did is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nor were the cities alike: each had its own political environment and history, and territory sizes, settlement ranks and frontier formation all varied across Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The meeting place itself, however, has been found, at Campo della Fiera on the plain below Orvieto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Excavation there since 2000 has uncovered more than five hectares (12 acres) holding several temples, a <i>Via Sacra</i> and a broad expanse given over to offerings, which is taken beyond serious doubt for the Fanum Voltumnae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Religious use of the ground begins in the 6th century BCE and runs unbroken to the end of the 4th century CE, the cult place having been made grand — tradition credits Porsenna — with altars set along the sacred way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among the finds is a moulded trachyte statue base about 84 centimetres (33 inches) high, inscribed in the late 6th century BCE to record a gift by a woman, perhaps a freedwoman named Kanuta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan league","questions":["At Campo della Fiera below Orvieto, five hectares of temples and a sacred way are taken for the meeting place of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Fanum Voltumnae.","No inscription of their own says the Etruscans were organised as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the title once read as its head may only be a city magistracy."],"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Miriam Gillett, \"The 'Etruscan League' Reconsidered,\" <i>Australasian Society for Classical Studies 31st Annual Conference Proceedings</i> (2010), http://ascs.org.au/news/ascs31/Gillett.pdf. [Open access]","Simonetta Stopponi, \"Un grand site étrusque: les fouilles de Campo della Fiera à Orvieto,\" Auditorium Michel Laclotte, Musée du Louvre, 13 February 2025, https://api-www.louvre.fr/sites/default/files/2025-02/20250123_MDA_ORVIETO_bat%20web.pdf. [Open access]","\"The Celestial Place: Etruscans and Their Gods — The Federal Sanctuary of Orvieto,\" Musée national d'histoire et d'art, Luxembourg, 2018, https://www.nationalmusee.lu/en/exhibitions/the-celestial-place-etruscans-and-their-gods. [Open access]","Simon Stoddart, \"Power and Place in Etruria,\" in <i>Eurasia at the Dawn of History: Urbanization and Social Change</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2017), 304–18, https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/handle/1810/260822. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the standard name for this association a modern imposition?","a":"Because Livy writes concilium or foedus, terms of far more transitory meaning, and no evidence from the Etruscans themselves says they were organised in any such way."},{"q":"Why does an Etruscan title fail to settle the question?","a":"Because zilath mechl rasnal was long read as head of a national confederation, but if rasnal stands for a commonwealth rather than 'the Etruscans' the phrase is a city magistracy and no more."},{"q":"Why is the meeting place identified with confidence when the league is not?","a":"Because excavation at Campo della Fiera has uncovered more than five hectares of temples, a Via Sacra and an expanse of offerings, taken beyond serious doubt for the Fanum Voltumnae."}]},
{"id":"rm-026","num":"26","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Written from the 8th century BCE to the 1st, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pruned the Greek alphabet it borrowed of the letters it had no use for, among them beta, delta and the vowel o.","answer":"Etruscan language","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 1st century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liber Linteus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 390 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Etruscan language</b> was written from the 8th century BCE to the 1st, in what is the oldest writing system of the pre-Roman peoples of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The alphabet came with Greek colonists, spreading from Pithekoussai and then Cumae through direct contact between Etruscan aristocrats and the colonial elite, alongside the symposium and the Orientalising taste for the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was adopted entire at first, letters the language had no use for included, and then pruned: <i>beta</i> and <i>delta</i> went with the voiced plosives Etruscan lacked, and so did the vowel /o/.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Signs had to be found for what Greek did not write, a second sibilant taken from Phoenician and an /f/ set down first as a digraph and then with a new letter shaped like an 8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There was no single Etruscan alphabet: north and south of Vulci and Orvieto the two sibilant letters were used the opposite way round, and Caere wrote a sigma of four strokes all its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The language inflects nouns for four cases with a locative besides, does not inflect the verb for person or number, and builds family names with the endings <i>-na</i> and <i>-ra</i> from a father's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the 5th century BCE unstressed vowels weaken until they stop being written, so that <i>Menerva</i> becomes <i>Menrva</i> and <i>turuce</i> becomes <i>turce</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its isolation is not total: it shares with Raetic and Lemnian the missing /o/, the absence of voiced plosives, and the habit of building words by agglutination where Indo-European languages inflect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what survives is names and repeated formulas, and the longest continuous text is the Liber Linteus, a linen book of about 390 BCE that reached us cut into strips as the wrappings of an Egyptian mummy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the end of the 1st millennium BCE the language was largely extinguished and the literature written in it lost, leaving stone, bronze and pottery to carry what remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan language","questions":["The longest continuous text in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a linen book of about 390 BCE that survived cut into strips as the wrappings of an Egyptian mummy.","Sharing a lack of the vowel o and of voiced plosives with Raetic and Lemnian, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> builds its words by agglutination where Indo-European languages inflect."],"tags":["language","history","italy","iron age","archaeology"],"sources":["Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore, updated September 2022), https://doi.org/10.25429/sns.it/lettere/mnamon048. [Open access]","Igor Uranić, \"Liber Linteus and the Zagreb Mummy,\" in <i>Recikliraj, ideje iz prošlosti / Recycle, Ideas from the Past</i> (Zagreb: University of Zagreb, Faculty of Humanities and Social Sciences, Department of Archaeology, 2019), https://doi.org/10.17234/9789531757232-07. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Simona Marchesini, \"Rhaetic: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=41&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Aulus-metellus-142BB03F76F7CD37B2E.jpg","title":"Etruscan language","desc":"Bronze sculpture of The Orator. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aulus-metellus-142BB03F76F7CD37B2E.jpg","alt":"Aulus-metellus-142BB03F76F7CD37B2E"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why was the borrowed alphabet pruned after being taken entire?","a":"Because the language had no voiced plosives and no /o/, so beta, delta and omicron went, while signs had to be found for a second sibilant and for /f/."},{"q":"Why can there be no single alphabet for the language?","a":"Because north and south of Vulci and Orvieto the two sibilant letters were used the opposite way round, and Caere wrote a sigma of four strokes all its own."},{"q":"Why does the longest surviving text owe its survival to Egypt?","a":"Because the Liber Linteus, a linen book of about 390 BCE, reached us cut into strips and used as the wrappings of a mummy."}]},
{"id":"rm-027","num":27,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are three gold sheets from the harbour sanctuary of Caere, two in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, recording a dedication Thefarie Velianas made to Uni-Astarte around 500 BCE.","questions":["Rolled up and buried beside the temples they had been nailed to, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive intact, and their Phoenician sheet measures how close Caere stood to Carthage.","Students of Etruscan hoped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would serve as a Rosetta Stone for their language, but the Phoenician and Etruscan versions of the dedication do not match word for word."],"answer":"Pyrgi Tablets","answerText":"Pyrgi Tablets","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple B</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 510 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Pyrgi Tablets</b> are three inscribed sheets of gold, two in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, found in July 1964 in the sanctuary at Pyrgi, the harbour town of the Etruscan city of Caere on the Tyrrhenian coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pyrgi stood at the seaward end of a broad road from Caere, and its monumental sanctuary of Uni-Astarte has been dug by the Sapienza University of Rome since 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sheets had been rolled up and hidden inside a container, and gold nails beside them, with holes punched along their edges, show they had once been fixed to a door frame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That careful burial is why they survive unmarked after 2,500 years, while the sea has been eating steadily into the temples they came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Phoenician was a Semitic language of the Levantine coast, known almost entirely from inscriptions, whose western form, spoken at Carthage and in the colonies it controlled, is the one scholars call Punic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> All three sheets record a dedication by Thefarie Velianas, whom the Phoenician text calls king over Cisra, an old name for Caere, and whom the two Etruscan texts leave untitled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He gives a sacred place, and by the Phoenician account a statue with it, to Astarte, whom the Etruscan sheets call Uni-Astre, Uni being the goddess the Romans would later know as Juno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lettering, language and archaeological context together date the dedication to about 500 BCE, at the latest to the years 500 to 490, and Thefarie had monumentalised the sanctuary around 510.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Students of Etruscan first hoped the Phoenician sheet would unlock their language, but the versions do not match word for word, the Punic one dating by the Phoenician calendar, so the tablets are no Rosetta Stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their weight is historical instead: among surviving inscriptions they are the oldest direct source for pre-Roman Italy, and a Punic dedication in Caere's own port measures how close the city stood to Carthage a generation after Etruscan and Carthaginian fleets met the Phocaeans off Corsica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["object","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","language","religion","etruscan"],"sources":["Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, \"Pyrgi Tablets,\" <i>Museum's Masterpieces</i>, https://www.museoetru.it/masterpieces/lamine-doro-da-pyrgi. [Open access]","Jacques Heurgon, \"Les inscriptions de Pyrgi et l'alliance étrusco-punique autour de 500 av. J.-C.,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 109, no. 1 (1965): 89–104, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1965.11813. [Open access]","Maria Paola Baglione, Barbara Belelli Marchesini, Claudia Carlucci and Laura Maria Michetti, \"Pyrgi, Harbour and Sanctuary of Caere: Landscape, Urbanistic Planning and Architectural Features,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 201–10, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF28.2/14_Baglione_et_al.pdf. [Open access]","Paolo Merlo, \"Phoenician: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=15&lang=en. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Pyrgi_tablets_A.jpg","title":"Pyrgi Tablets","desc":"The \"Pyrgi tablets\". Laminated sheets of gold with a treatise both in Etruscan and Phoenician languages. From Etruscan Museum in Rome (Museo di Villa Giulia). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrgi_tablets_A.jpg","alt":"Pyrgi tablets A"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why did these sheets survive unmarked when their temples did not?","a":"Because they had been rolled up and hidden inside a container, while the sea has been eating steadily into the buildings they were once nailed to."},{"q":"Why did they not unlock the Etruscan language?","a":"Because the versions do not match word for word, the Punic one dating by the Phoenician calendar, so they are no Rosetta Stone."},{"q":"Why does a Punic dedication in this harbour matter historically?","a":"Because it measures how close Caere stood to Carthage a generation after Etruscan and Carthaginian fleets met the Phocaeans off Corsica."}]},
{"id":"rm-028","num":28,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Because its religious literature is lost, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is known mainly from what practice left behind: sanctuaries, painted terracotta temples, inscribed votive offerings and the debris of sacrifice.","questions":["In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a votive column at Poggio Colla could be taken down and buried upside down beside two statue bases, with a piglet killed and a bronze bowl laid in after it.","Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a temple facade was dressed in painted terracotta rather than carved marble, one sanctuary could take dedications to several gods, and thunder and entrails were read for signs."],"answer":"Etruscan religion","answerText":"Etruscan religion","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Votive words</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Portonaccio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 530 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"Very little of what the Etruscans believed was written down in a form that has reached us, so <b>Etruscan religion</b> is known mostly from what its practice left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest offerings were made in caves, in water and in the ground, and only later are they joined by cult buildings, altars, sacrificial debris, dedicated objects and pictures of gods and rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Words start appearing on votive objects from about the second quarter of the 1st millennium BCE, and although Etruscan often resists translation, terms for giving, dedication, the gods and sanctity can be picked out of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Portonaccio, the sanctuary outside Veii, a foundation deposit of roughly 600 to 530 BCE preserves the inscribed names of the people who dedicated there, several of them from known aristocratic families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman habits must not be read back into any of this: the bargain of <i>do ut des</i> cannot be assumed, and one sanctuary could take dedications to several different gods at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A great sanctuary was a district rather than a building, and at Pyrgi two monumental temples stood beside a second cult place, with votive deposits, a fire altar and ceremonial buildings along the road serving them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Temple facades were dressed not in carved marble but in painted terracotta, worked up into figures as large as life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of them, a windswept head of Thesan, the Etruscan goddess of the dawn whom Greek writers called Leucothea, shows how readily an Etruscan god could be matched with a Greek one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Excavation can recover a whole sequence of ritual: at Poggio Colla a votive column was taken down and set upside down in a pit beside two statue bases, a piglet was killed, and a bronze bowl and gold wire were laid in after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deposits of that kind are sorted into acts of propitiation, foundation, celebration and obliteration, and beside them stood the reading of divine will from thunder, from lots and from the entrails of sacrificed animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["practice","religion","history","italy","iron age","archaeology","etruscan"],"sources":["Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Tesse D. Stek, review of <i>The Archaeology of Sanctuaries and Ritual in Etruria</i>, ed. Nancy T. de Grummond and Ingrid Edlund-Berry, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2012.04.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.04.42/. [Open access]","Liza Cleland, review of <i>Votives, Places and Rituals in Etruscan Religion</i>, ed. Margarita Gleba and Hilary Becker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.06.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.55/. [Open access]","Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, \"Head of Leucotea,\" <i>Museum's Masterpieces</i>, https://www.museoetru.it/masterpieces/head-of-leucotea. [Open access]","Maria Paola Baglione, Barbara Belelli Marchesini, Claudia Carlucci and Laura Maria Michetti, \"Pyrgi, Harbour and Sanctuary of Caere: Landscape, Urbanistic Planning and Architectural Features,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 201–10, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF28.2/14_Baglione_et_al.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Etruscan_votive_heads_IV-II_century_BC.jpg/1920px-Etruscan_votive_heads_IV-II_century_BC.jpg","title":"Etruscan religion","desc":"Etruscan votive heads IV-II cen BC. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_votive_heads_IV-II_century_BC.jpg","alt":"Etruscan votive heads IV-II century BCE"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is this religion known from practice rather than doctrine?","a":"Because very little of what these people believed was written down in a form that has reached us, so it is read from offerings, altars, sacrificial debris and pictures of rites."},{"q":"Why must Roman habits not be read back into it?","a":"Because the bargain of do ut des cannot be assumed, and one sanctuary could take dedications to several different gods at once."},{"q":"Why is a great sanctuary called a district rather than a building?","a":"Because at Pyrgi two monumental temples stood beside a second cult place, with votive deposits, a fire altar and ceremonial buildings along the road serving them."}]},
{"id":"rm-029","num":29,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The Etruscan art of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> read the will of the gods from the entrails of a sacrificed animal, above all from the head of the liver, and Rome took the practice over.","questions":["A bronze liver found near Piacenza, its face cut into cells named for gods, is the best surviving guide to how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was actually carried out in Etruria.","Rome ended up with an order of sixty public practitioners of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the Senate still summoned grandees out of Etruria whenever it wanted a prodigy explained."],"answer":"haruspicy","answerText":"haruspicy","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman responsa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Piacenza liver</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Haruspicy</b> is divination by inspecting a sacrificed animal, and it is the branch of Etruscan religious learning the Romans took over most completely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organs read were the <i>exta</i>, and the liver came to matter more than any other, ahead of the gall bladder, heart and lungs, with stomach and kidneys counting for little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the haruspex watched above all was the <i>caput iecinoris</i>, the head of the liver: whether it was there at all, how large it was, and how it was split.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The books governing it sat inside the wider learning Romans called the <i>Etrusca disciplina</i>, beside its books on lightning and ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How old the practice is cannot be settled easily, and it may owe something to the older extispicy of Assyria and Babylonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Clay models of a sacrificial sheep's liver, lungs or spiral gut were made across the ancient Near East, more than a hundred of them published and a whole series recovered at Mari on the Euphrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They took hold in Anatolia and along the Levantine coast more firmly than in Mesopotamia itself, and were probably carried onward to Italy from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What survives from Etruria is the bronze liver of Piacenza, made around 100 BCE, found in the territory of Gossolengo and still kept in the city's Palazzo Farnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its flat face is cut into compartments carrying 41 names, 27 of them gods, with 16 round the rim fixing the orientation and the sun and moon on the rounded side, and a <i>pars familiaris</i> is marked off from a <i>pars hostilis</i>, so where a mark fell decided whether the sign was good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Rome the reading became business of state: from the early 3rd century BCE the answers ran steadily in favour of senatorial expansion, and imperial inscriptions name an order of sixty public haruspices, thought by some to be lesser men than the grandees the Senate summoned out of Etruria to explain a prodigy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["practice","religion","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","rome"],"sources":["Charles Guittard, \"Signes du destin et pouvoir dans les pratiques étrusco-italiques,\" <i>Collection de l'Institut des Sciences et Techniques de l'Antiquité</i> 999 (2006): 71–82, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ista_0000-0000_2006_act_999_1_3429. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Jean Nougayrol, \"La langue des haruspices babyloniens à propos d'un foie d'argile inédit,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 110, no. 1 (1966): 193–203, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1966.11961. [Open access]","Musei di Palazzo Farnese, \"Museo Archeologico,\" Comune di Piacenza, https://palazzofarnese.piacenza.it/it/page/museo-archeologico-1. [Open access]","Yann Berthelet, \"Les haruspices publics au service de Rome : des aristocrates convoqués d'Étrurie ? Quels aristocrates ?,\" <i>Collection de l'Institut des Sciences et Techniques de l'Antiquité</i> 1505 (2020): 133–53, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ista_0000-0000_2020_act_1505_1_3820. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Diagram_of_the_bronze_liver_of_Piacenza.jpg","title":"haruspicy","desc":"Diagram of the inscriptions of the bronze liver of Piacenza. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diagram_of_the_bronze_liver_of_Piacenza.jpg","alt":"Diagram of the bronze liver of Piacenza"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did one organ come to matter above the rest?","a":"Because the liver was read ahead of gall bladder, heart and lungs, and the haruspex watched its caput iecinoris above all — whether it was there, how large, and how it was split."},{"q":"Why does the bronze liver of Piacenza work as an instrument?","a":"Because its flat face is cut into compartments carrying 41 names with 16 round the rim fixing the orientation, and a pars familiaris marked off from a pars hostilis decides whether a sign was good."},{"q":"Why did the practice become business of state at Rome?","a":"Because from the early 3rd century BCE the answers ran steadily in favour of senatorial expansion, and imperial inscriptions name an order of sixty public haruspices."}]},
{"id":"rm-030","num":30,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Romans gave the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Etruscan learning that recognised and read the signs the gods sent, set down in books on entrails, on lightning and on ritual.","questions":["A child called Tages, come up out of a ploughed furrow, and a nymph called Vegoia were held to have revealed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which great Etruscan families then wrote down and kept.","Sixteen regions of the sky, each the seat of a god, belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as does the doctrine that allotted the Etruscan people ten ages and no more."],"answer":"Etrusca disciplina","answerText":"Etrusca disciplina","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Latin version</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 56 – 44 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cited at Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st century BCE – 5th century CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Etrusca disciplina</b> is the Roman name for the body of Etruscan religious learning, the rules by which signs sent by the gods were recognised and read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls the Etruscans a people given to religious observance beyond all others, and skilled beyond all others in the art of cultivating the gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What stands behind that reputation is a habit of mind rather than a set of rites, since anything out of the ordinary was a message: a comet, an earthquake, an odd plant, a deformed birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seneca put the difference sharply, writing that where a Roman thought lightning was thrown because clouds had collided, an Etruscan thought clouds collided in order that lightning be thrown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The learning was written down in books the Romans sorted into three classes: the <i>libri haruspicini</i> on entrails, the <i>fulgurales</i> on lightning, and the <i>rituales</i> on ritual, which laid down among much else the rite for founding a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its origin was told as a revelation made to the Etruscans by Tages, a child who came up out of a ploughed furrow near Tarquinia, and by the nymph Vegoia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Great families kept the books and handed them down, and Etruscan sarcophagi show their owners holding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome read them in translation: Tarquitius Priscus made a Latin <i>Ostentarium Tuscum</i> and an <i>Ostentarium arborarium</i>, a list of unlucky trees, both still being quoted by Macrobius centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One piece survives inside the corpus of the Roman land surveyors, Vegoia warning that men will shift boundary stones near the end of the eighth <i>saeculum</i> and be damned for it, which fits the doctrine that the Etruscan people were allotted ten such ages and no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sky was divided into sixteen regions, each the seat of a god, a scheme listed in full only by Martianus Capella in the 5th century CE and marked out on the bronze liver of Piacenza, and modern reconstructions of it still disagree about where north lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","religion","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","text"],"sources":["Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]","Charles Guittard, \"Macrobe et l'Etrusca disciplina : ostentarium Tuscum et ostentarium arborarium,\" <i>Collection de l'Institut des Sciences et Techniques de l'Antiquité</i> 1356 (2016): 13–26, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ista_0000-0000_2016_act_1356_1_3083. [Open access]","Jean Peyras, \"Religion étrusque et droit romain d'après le bureau central des géomètres de la dynastie valentino-théodosienne,\" <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 38, no. 1 (2012): 163–76, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.2012.3458. [Open access]","Gérard Capdeville, \"Les dieux de Martianus Capella,\" <i>Revue de l'histoire des religions</i> 213, no. 3 (1996): 251–99, https://doi.org/10.3406/rhr.1996.1203. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Etruscan_bronze_mirror_with_Menrva_and_Lasa_Vecu.jpg/1920px-Etruscan_bronze_mirror_with_Menrva_and_Lasa_Vecu.jpg","title":"Etrusca disciplina","desc":"Etruscan bronze mirror with Menrva and Lasa Vecu (Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, Rome). Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_bronze_mirror_with_Menrva_and_Lasa_Vecu.jpg","alt":"Etruscan bronze mirror with Menrva and Lasa Vecu"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why does Seneca's remark capture the difference in outlook?","a":"Because he says a Roman thought lightning was thrown because clouds had collided, where an Etruscan thought clouds collided in order that lightning be thrown."},{"q":"Why was anything unusual treated as a message?","a":"Because the learning was a habit of mind rather than a set of rites, so a comet, an earthquake, an odd plant or a deformed birth all had to be read."},{"q":"Why is the scheme of the sky still argued over?","a":"Because its sixteen regions are listed in full only by Martianus Capella in the 5th century CE, and modern reconstructions still disagree about where north lay."}]},
{"id":"rm-031","num":31,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the patterns on ceilings and upper walls copy woven cloth, standing not for a domestic interior but for the tents raised over a funeral.","questions":["Small blue-green trees with black berries punctuate the walls in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a ring of them is thought to have made the chamber a kind of sacred precinct.","Charun, hook-nosed and green as a corpse and carrying a heavy mallet, belongs to the later <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the underworld crowds out the banquet."],"answer":"Etruscan tomb painting","answerText":"Etruscan tomb painting","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shrub motif</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until the 4th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">François Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 350 – 325 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Etruscan tomb painting</b> covers the walls of rock-cut chamber tombs, and the painted tombs of Tarquinia, with the tumuli of Caere, are the part of Etruscan archaeology best known outside the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The painted room is not a gallery of pictures but a space built out of them, and the small trees punctuating the walls, almost constant until the 4th century BCE, are what mark it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trees are stereotyped, with lanceolate blue-green leaves and black berries, usually read as laurels, and birds perch in their branches or fly towards them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ring of shrubs is thought to have made the chamber a kind of <i>templum</i>, the inaugurated space Roman practice also marked with trees, and the painted columns in the corners of the Tomb of the Lionesses do similar work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The patterns on ceilings and upper walls copy woven cloth, and their point is not a domestic interior but the tents and canopies raised for a funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The subjects are the funeral itself: games, dances and above all the banquet, whose arrangement and place in the tomb settle from the late 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Banqueters hold out fillets, wreaths and eggs, and a false door, first painted in the Tomb of the Augurs, becomes a common way of closing a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they show of eating and drinking is partly borrowed, the wine, the furniture and the sports that went with a feast, though the presence of freeborn Etruscan women at the banquet struck Greek writers as scandalous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The later tombs turn towards the underworld, and Charun appears in them: hook-nosed, with pointed animal ears and wild hair, blue or green as a corpse should be, and always carrying a heavy mallet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the François Tomb at Vulci a Greek composition of Achilles killing Trojan prisoners has a winged demon and a grimacing Charun added to it, drawn more flatly than the borrowed figures and there to say the gods were present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["art","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","religion"],"sources":["Anne-Marie Adam, \"« Horror vacui » et détails inutiles (?) dans la peinture funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 37, no. 1 (2012): 239–54, https://doi.org/10.3406/ktema.2012.1663. [Open access]","Anne-Marie Adam, \"Végétation et paysage dans la peinture funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 15, no. 1 (1990): 143–50, https://doi.org/10.3406/ktema.1990.2014. [Open access]","Jean-René Jannot, \"Charon et Charun : à propos d'un démon funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 135, no. 2 (1991): 443–64, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1991.14997. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Tomb_of_the_augurs_detail.jpg","title":"Etruscan tomb painting","desc":"Tomba degli auguri, Tarquinia, Italia. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_the_augurs_detail.jpg","alt":"Tomb of the augurs detail"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are the painted trees more than decoration?","a":"Because a ring of shrubs is thought to have made the chamber a kind of templum, the inaugurated space Roman practice also marked with trees."},{"q":"Why do the ceiling patterns copy woven cloth?","a":"Because their point is not a domestic interior but the tents and canopies raised for a funeral, which is the occasion the whole room depicts."},{"q":"Why does the François Tomb draw its demons more flatly than its heroes?","a":"Because the Achilles composition is borrowed Greek work and the winged demon and Charun are added to it, there to say the gods were present."}]},
{"id":"rm-032","num":32,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The town on the Civita plateau and the painted cemeteries on the ridge opposite are the two halves of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Etruscan city that gave Rome the Tarquins.","questions":["Tarchon founded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Tages came up out of a furrow in its fields, and Demaratus of Corinth settled there before his descendants took the throne at Rome.","The port of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Gravisca, whose sanctuary drew Greek traders and yielded more than 200,000 catalogued finds in eleven seasons of digging."],"answer":"Tarquinia","answerText":"Tarquinia","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gravisca votives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 – 470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gravisca colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">181 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Tarquinia</b> was one of the great cities of southern Etruria, its town standing on the Civita plateau and its cemeteries on the Monterozzi ridge across the valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers gave it a founder to match, Tarchon, eponym of the city and in some accounts the brother or lieutenant of the Lydian Tyrrhenus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tarchon was also the man said to have written down the revelation of Tages, the child who came up out of a furrow near Tarquinia and gave the Etruscans their religious learning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Roman tradition tied the city to Rome's own kings, since Demaratus of Corinth was said to have settled there and his descendants to have ruled Rome as the Tarquins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It claimed Servius Tullius as well, the king the Etruscan tradition identified with Mastarna and whom Cicero reports to have been born of a Tarquinian slave woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its great temple was the Ara della Regina on the plateau, and when a new terrace was laid in front of it in the 4th century BCE care was taken to mark the position of an archaic chest and precinct wall below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The excavators suggest that chest may have been taken for the tomb of Tarchon himself, which would make the terrace an act of remembering rather than of building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Old geophysical surveys, read again, are producing a plan of the town to match those already available for Veii and Vulci.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its port was Gravisca, whose sanctuary drew Greek traders: eleven seasons of excavation from 1969 produced more than 200,000 catalogued finds, the votive offerings heaviest between 510 and 470 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across the valley the Monterozzi cemetery holds the painted chambers the city is best known for, and Rome absorbed the place in stages, planting a colony at Gravisca in 181 BCE and making Tarquinia a municipium of the tribe Stellatina, run by a board of four, after the Social War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","religion"],"sources":["Maria Mainardi and Giovanna Bagnasco Gianni, \"Tarquinia,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 20 (2011): 237–316, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2011_num_20_1_4644. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Francesca Boitani and Mario Torelli, \"Un nuovo santuario dell'emporion di Gravisca,\" <i>Publications de l'École française de Rome</i> 251 (1999): 93–102, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_1999_act_251_1_5391. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]","Anne-Marie Adam, \"« Horror vacui » et détails inutiles (?) dans la peinture funéraire étrusque,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 37, no. 1 (2012): 239–54, https://doi.org/10.3406/ktema.2012.1663. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Monterozzi_necropolis_%E2%80%94_Tomb_of_the_Leopards.jpg/1920px-Monterozzi_necropolis_%E2%80%94_Tomb_of_the_Leopards.jpg","title":"Tarquinia","desc":"Monterozzi necropolis — Tomb of the Leopards. Gleb Simonov. 2024-01-03. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monterozzi_necropolis_%E2%80%94_Tomb_of_the_Leopards.jpg","alt":"Monterozzi necropolis — Tomb of the Leopards"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Tarquinia","at":[11.7561,42.2492]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Roman tradition tie its own kings to this city?","a":"Because Demaratus of Corinth was said to have settled there and his descendants to have ruled Rome as the Tarquins, while Servius Tullius was reported born of a Tarquinian slave woman."},{"q":"Why is the 4th-century terrace read as an act of remembering?","a":"Because care was taken to mark the position of an archaic chest and precinct wall below it, and that chest may have been taken for the tomb of the city's founder."},{"q":"Why does its port matter as much as its temple?","a":"Because the sanctuary at Gravisca drew Greek traders, producing more than 200,000 catalogued finds with the offerings heaviest between 510 and 470 BCE."}]},
{"id":"rm-033","num":33,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Greeks called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Agylla, an Ionian workshop there made the painted hydriae named after it, and Rome sent its sacred objects there when the Gauls came.","questions":["After its ships helped beat the Phocaeans, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stoned the prisoners to death and was told by the Delphic oracle to found a sanctuary in expiation.","Romans held that the people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the first made citizens without the vote, though Rome later took half their land as public property."],"answer":"Caere","answerText":"Caere","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardinian Sea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman truce</span><span class=\"dt-v\">353 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Caere</b> stood on a plateau where Cerveteri now stands, and the Greeks who traded there knew it as Agylla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo passes on a tradition that Pelasgians out of Thessaly founded it, one of several Greek stories that fastened themselves to the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the most active centre of hellenisation in Etruria: a small colony of Athenians was settled there by about 600 BCE, and between 540 and 510 an Ionian workshop in the city turned out the painted water jars still called Caeretan hydriae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its cemetery at the Banditaccia was full of the finest Attic black-figure and early red-figure pottery, which makes Caere one of the chief markets for Athenian vases in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of that made its dealings with Greeks peaceful, and about 540 BCE Caeretan ships joined Carthaginian ones against the Phocaeans in the Sardinian Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Caeretans stoned their prisoners to death, and after what they took for divine punishment the Delphic oracle set them a sanctuary in expiation; Strabo also records a Caeretan treasury standing at Delphi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo indeed says the city was honoured among Greeks for its justice, since for all its power it kept clear of piracy, which the facts contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its port at Pyrgi carried a monumental sanctuary of Uni-Astarte, and Dionysius of Syracuse sacked the place in 384 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Rome the city was long on unusual terms: Tarquinius Superbus took refuge there in 509 BCE, and when the Gauls held Rome in 390 the Roman sacred objects were sent to Caere for safekeeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romans held that the Caerites were the first people made citizens without the vote, a tradition disputed in antiquity and argued over still, and the friendship did not save them: Rome granted a truce in 353 BCE, took half the territory as public land, and Strabo found the city in decline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Mauro Cristofani, \"Cerveteri,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 5 (1987): 251–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1987_num_5_1_3380. [Open access]","Jacques Heurgon, \"Les inscriptions de Pyrgi et l'alliance étrusco-punique autour de 500 av. J.-C.,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 109, no. 1 (1965): 89–104, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1965.11813. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Michel Humbert, \"L'incorporation de Caere dans la civitas Romana,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 84, no. 1 (1972): 231–68, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1972.923. [Open access]","Maria Paola Baglione, Barbara Belelli Marchesini, Claudia Carlucci and Laura Maria Michetti, \"Pyrgi, Harbour and Sanctuary of Caere: Landscape, Urbanistic Planning and Architectural Features,\" <i>Archeologia e Calcolatori</i> 28, no. 2 (2017): 201–10, https://www.archcalc.cnr.it/indice/PDF28.2/14_Baglione_et_al.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Herakles_Kerberos_Eurystheus_Louvre_E701.jpg/1920px-Herakles_Kerberos_Eurystheus_Louvre_E701.jpg","title":"Caere","desc":"Side A: Herakles, Cerberus and Eurystheus. Side B: two eagles flying and a hare. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herakles_Kerberos_Eurystheus_Louvre_E701.jpg","alt":"Herakles Kerberos Eurystheus Louvre E701"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Caere","at":[12.105,42.0075]},"why":[{"q":"Why is this city called the most hellenised in Etruria?","a":"Because a small colony of Athenians was settled there by about 600 BCE, an Ionian workshop made painted hydriae in the city, and its cemetery was full of the finest Attic pottery."},{"q":"Why does Strabo's praise of its justice sit badly with the record?","a":"Because he says the city kept clear of piracy, yet its ships joined Carthaginian ones against the Phocaeans and its people stoned the prisoners to death."},{"q":"Why did friendship with Rome not preserve it?","a":"Because Rome granted a truce in 353 BCE, took half the territory as public land, and Strabo found the city in decline."}]},
{"id":"rm-034","num":34,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Rock-cut chambers under earthen mounds, laid out along streets like a town, make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Caere, dug into a plateau across the valley from the city.","questions":["At the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a tomb chamber imitates the rooms of a house, and an altar outside is often joined to the mound by a small bridge.","Cippi, stelae and stone lions marked graves at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while most of the writing that honoured the dead sat inside the chambers where almost nobody could read it."],"answer":"Banditaccia necropolis","answerText":"Banditaccia necropolis","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb types</span><span class=\"dt-v\">develop to c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sunken roads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 4th century BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Banditaccia necropolis</b> is the great cemetery of Caere, spread over a plateau across a valley from the town, and its tumuli are among the most recognisable things Etruscan archaeology has produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Burial moved onto the plateau from an area to the north in the late Villanovan period and spread across it from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tombs are rock-cut chambers under earthen mounds, some of them satellites of a larger one, and outside there is often an altar joined to the mound by a small bridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside they imitate the rooms of a house, and their plans go on developing until about the middle of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After that the cemetery is regulated: tombs are set out along laid roads and given a uniform outside, cut as a cube, as a small palace, or behind a single façade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same regulation holds from the 4th century BCE, when the façades stop and tombs are cut along roads sunk into the tuff, the great family hypogea excepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tomb was meant to hold the memory of the dead before the family and sometimes before the whole community, and cippi, stelae and stone lions did that work above ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing came early to Etruscan burial, but the great majority of the inscriptions are inside the chambers, where almost nobody could read them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What those chambers held made Caere one of the chief markets for Attic pottery in the west, and it also made the cemetery a quarry: between 1845 and 1856 the marchese Giampietro Campana worked it hard and took the finds into a private museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When that collection was broken up, some 12,000 works were divided between the Louvre and nearly ninety French provincial towns, others going to the Hermitage and to Florence, and many lost the record of where they had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["place","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","religion"],"sources":["Mauro Cristofani, \"Cerveteri,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 5 (1987): 251–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1987_num_5_1_3380. [Open access]","Catherine Cousin, \"Une commémoration originale du mort en Étrurie,\" <i>Actes des congrès nationaux des sociétés historiques et scientifiques</i> 134, no. 1 (2010): 17–33, https://www.persee.fr/doc/acths_1764-7355_2010_act_134_1_1813. [Open access]","Jacques Heurgon, \"Les inscriptions de Pyrgi et l'alliance étrusco-punique autour de 500 av. J.-C.,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 109, no. 1 (1965): 89–104, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1965.11813. [Open access]","Laurent Haumesser, \"Les statues en terre cuite de la collection Campana. La multiplication des Atlantes,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 162, no. 1 (2018): 365–86, https://www.persee.fr/doc/crai_0065-0536_2018_num_162_1_96474. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Deceased_with_funeral_procession%2C_Cerveteri%2C_Banditaccia_necropolis%2C_Tomb_of_the_Sarcophagi%2C_2nd_quarter_of_the_4th_century_BC%2C_Circeo_marble%2C_inv._14949_-_Museo_Gregoriano_Etrusco_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC01156.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Banditaccia necropolis","desc":"Exhibit in the Museo Gregoriano Etrusco - Vatican Museums. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deceased_with_funeral_procession,_Cerveteri,_Banditaccia_necropolis,_Tomb_of_the_Sarcophagi,_2nd_quarter_of_the_4th_century_BC,_Circeo_marble,_inv._14949_-_Museo_Gregoriano_Etrusco_-_Vatican_Museums_-_DSC01156.jpg","alt":"Deceased with funeral procession, Cerveteri, Banditaccia necropolis, Tomb of the Sarcophagi, 2nd quarter of the 4th century BCE, Circeo marble, - Museo Gregoriano Etrusco - Vatican Museums"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Banditaccia necropolis","at":[12.105,42.0075],"within":"Caere"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the cemetery change shape in the middle of the 6th century BCE?","a":"Because from then it is regulated: tombs set out along laid roads and given a uniform outside, cut as a cube, as a small palace, or behind a single façade."},{"q":"Why is it odd that most of the inscriptions are inside the chambers?","a":"Because a tomb was meant to hold the memory of the dead before the family and sometimes the whole community, and almost nobody could read the texts where they were cut."},{"q":"Why did the 19th-century excavations damage more than the ground?","a":"Because the Campana collection was broken up and some 12,000 works divided between the Louvre, nearly ninety French towns, the Hermitage and Florence, many losing the record of where they came from."}]},
{"id":"rm-035","num":35,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Dionysius called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most powerful city in Etruria and equal in extent to Athens, and Rome stormed it in 396 BCE after a long siege.","questions":["Walls running some 9 kilometres (5.6 miles) around 190 hectares (470 acres) enclosed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose small acropolis on the Piazza d'Armi had older defences of its own.","After Rome took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the tribunes proposed moving half the city's people into it, and a golden krater went to Delphi as a tithe of the plunder."],"answer":"Veii","answerText":"Veii","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Piazza d'Armi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – early 5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken by Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Veii</b> was Rome's nearest Etruscan neighbour, and Dionysius of Halicarnassus calls it the most powerful city in Etruria in the days of Romulus and equal in extent to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its city walls enclosed 190 hectares (470 acres) and ran about 9 kilometres (5.6 miles), while the small acropolis plateau of the Piazza d'Armi covered barely 6.9 hectares (17 acres) behind roughly a kilometre of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those acropolis walls are among the oldest Etruscan military architecture known, and settlement there was heaviest between the 8th century BCE and the middle of the 6th before dying away in the early 5th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city walls are later, put at the end of the 5th century BCE by their excavators and probably not much before its middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beyond them a ring of sanctuaries marked out the city's territory, and the greatest of them, Portonaccio, drew gifts from across Etruria: a foundation deposit of about 600 to 530 BCE holds fragments inscribed with the names of the men who gave them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Veii's history in Roman sources is one long quarrel with Rome, beginning under Romulus with the loss of the <i>septem pagi</i> and a truce of a hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A twenty-year war is placed at the start of Servius Tullius's reign, and in 479 BCE the Fabii fought a private war of their own against Veii and were destroyed at the Cremera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end came in 396 BCE when Camillus stormed the city after a long siege; the Romans sent a golden krater to Delphi as a tithe of the plunder, and the tribunes of the plebs proposed moving half of Rome into the captured town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site did not simply die with the city, since excavation shows it going on at lower intensity, picking up again between 350 and 250 BCE and then tailing away into villas and perhaps a healing sanctuary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman poets nonetheless made Veii the type of the vanished city, and in 46 BCE its land went to Caesar's veterans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan","warfare"],"sources":["Francesca Boitani and Iefke van Kampen, \"Veio,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 553–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4754. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Paul Fontaine, \"Véies. Les remparts et la porte de la Piazza d'Armi,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 105, no. 1 (1993): 221–39, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1993.1799. [Open access]","Françoise Gaultier, \"Sanctuaires et territoires en pays étrusque : le cas de Véies,\" <i>Publications de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 21 (2010): 115–39, https://www.persee.fr/doc/keryl_1275-6229_2010_act_21_1_1418. [Open access]","Liza Cleland, review of <i>Votives, Places and Rituals in Etruscan Religion</i>, ed. Margarita Gleba and Hilary Becker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.06.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.55/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1e/Map_of_Veii.jpg","title":"Veii","desc":"Map of Veii. George Dennis. 1848. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Veii.jpg","alt":"Map of Veii"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Veii","at":[12.3897,42.0233]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the acropolis walls older than the city's own?","a":"Because settlement on the small plateau was heaviest from the 8th century to the middle of the 6th, while the city walls are put at the end of the 5th century BCE."},{"q":"Why did sanctuaries stand outside the walls?","a":"Because a ring of them marked out the city's territory, the greatest at Portonaccio drawing gifts from across Etruria."},{"q":"Why is the poets' picture of a vanished city misleading?","a":"Because excavation shows occupation going on at lower intensity, picking up again between 350 and 250 BCE before tailing away into villas and perhaps a healing sanctuary."}]},
{"id":"rm-036","num":36,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"Claudius told the senate that Mastarna, companion of the Vibenna brothers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, went on to rule Rome under the name Servius Tullius.","questions":["Rome stripped <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of its coastal strip in 280 BCE, which made room for the Latin colony at Cosa and for the prefectures of Saturnia and Forum Aurelii.","Uni, Vei, Fufluns and Thufltha were worshipped at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose personification stands beside those of Tarquinia and Vetulonia on a relief from Caere."],"answer":"Vulci","answerText":"Vulci","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">280 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">François Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 350 – 325 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Vulci</b> stood above the river Fiora in southern Etruria, its people the <i>Velc</i> of their own inscriptions, the Olkietai of Polybius and the <i>Volcentani</i> that Pliny knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ranked with the greatest cities, its personification carved beside those of Tarquinia and Vetulonia on a relief from Caere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gods worshipped there were Uni, Vei, Fufluns, Suri and Thufltha, and the names of its leading families survive on a mass of inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city's largest claim on Roman history is a pair of brothers, Aulus and Caelius Vibenna, whom the emperor Claudius named in the speech preserved on a bronze tablet at Lyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Claudius said their companion Mastarna went on to rule Rome as Servius Tullius, which puts an Etruscan account beside the Roman one of a Roman king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The François Tomb, painted in the third quarter of the 4th century BCE, belonged to one of those families, and a bronze statuette may name the man buried in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 280 BCE Vulci lost its coastal strip and part of its hinterland to Rome, which made room for the Latin colony at Cosa and for the prefectures of Saturnia and Forum Aurelii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Social War the city was a Roman municipium of the tribe Sabatina, run by a board of four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its modern history begins in October 1828, when Lucien Bonaparte, prince of Canino, opened the cemeteries with his wife Alexandrine de Bleschamps driving the work, dividing the ground into 'family districts' from the inscriptions cut on funerary cippi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His collection was shown in five rooms of the castle at Musignano and then sold and scattered, and two Attic amphorae from those digs surfaced at a Paris auction in 2005, while geophysics has lately been recovering the plan of the town they came out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["city","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Anna Maria Moretti Sgubini, \"Vulci,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 1082–1154, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4783. [Open access]","Clara Berrendonner, \"Les Tetnie à Vulci,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 118, no. 1 (2006): 21–34, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.2006.10966. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"Réapparition de deux vases provenant des fouilles de Lucien Bonaparte à Vulci,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 151, no. 3 (2007): 1415–33, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2007.91372. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Dominique Briquel, \"« Gens ante alias magis dedita religionibus » : les Étrusques et le sacré,\" <i>Bulletin de l'Association Guillaume Budé</i> 1, no. 3 (1989): 247–62, https://doi.org/10.3406/bude.1989.1404. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Gold_fibula_from_Vulci_-_British_Museum_%282%29.jpg","title":"Vulci","desc":"Gold fibula with a procession of gold lions in gold granulation, while more lions, sphinxes and heads of lions and horses decorate the bow, as well as the tip and butt of the catchplate. From Vulci, Italy British Museum (675-650 BCE). Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_fibula_from_Vulci_-_British_Museum_(2).jpg","alt":"Gold fibula from Vulci - British Museum (2)"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Vulci","at":[11.6317,42.4189]},"why":[{"q":"Why does this city carry a claim on Roman history?","a":"Because Claudius named the brothers Aulus and Caelius Vibenna in his Lyon speech and said their companion Mastarna went on to rule Rome as Servius Tullius."},{"q":"Why is that claim worth weighing against the Roman account?","a":"Because it puts an Etruscan version beside the Roman one of a Roman king, preserved on a bronze tablet by an emperor who studied the subject."},{"q":"Why did the 1828 excavations organise the ground by families?","a":"Because the digging was divided into 'family districts' read off the inscriptions cut on funerary cippi."}]},
{"id":"rm-037","num":37,"category":"Italy before Rome","question":"The duck-billed jugs called Schnabelkannen carried <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into Switzerland, the Rhine valley and Gaul, about a hundred of them turning up outside Italy.","questions":["Engraved mirrors are the most closely studied products of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the ancient sources put mirrors among the instruments by which the will of the gods was read.","No tin slag has ever been found in Tuscany, so the tin that went into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must have been brought in from somewhere else."],"answer":"Etruscan bronzework","answerText":"Etruscan bronzework","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Schnabelkannen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">525 – 400 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Etruscan bronzework</b> travelled further than anything else the Etruscans made, and bronze vessels are the bulk of what reached Iron Age Gaul from Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The commonest of them by a wide margin was the <i>Schnabelkanne</i>, a jug with a duck-billed spout, most of them made between 525 and 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Corded cistae, situlae and stamnoi went the same way, and the trade reached Switzerland, the Rhine valley and Gaul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their Etruscan origin is well established and their dating fairly tight, which makes them the clearest measure there is of trade between Etruria and the peoples beyond the Alps: about 150 of these jugs are known from Italy and more than a hundred from outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Etruscan bronzes also turn up as gifts and offerings in other people's sanctuaries across the western Mediterranean and western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> At home the most closely studied bronzes are the engraved mirrors, gathered into a corpus published collection by collection, with fresh pieces still emerging from private hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A mirror could be more than a mirror, since the ancient sources put mirrors among the instruments by which the will of the gods was read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin, and where Etruria found its tin is a harder question than where it found its copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1876 a mining engineer reported cassiterite in an ancient working near Campiglia Marittima, and the mine has been called Etruscan ever since, though its galleries took only the upper part of the deposit, where tin ore lay mixed with iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There is no real evidence for that attribution: the earliest published find from those mines is Roman, no tin slag has ever been found in Tuscany, only one of 28 Tuscan ore samples reaches the tin content that Bronze Age Britain and Portugal were smelting, and modern quarrying has destroyed enough of the workings that their date may never be settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["object","technology","archaeology","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Bernard Bouloumié, \"Les œnochoés en bronze du type Schnabelkanne en France et en Belgique,\" <i>Gallia</i> 31, no. 1 (1973): 1–35, https://doi.org/10.3406/galia.1973.2623. [Open access]","Marco Tizzoni, \"Etruscan Tin: A Legend?,\" <i>Pallas</i> 50, no. 1 (1999): 89–100, https://doi.org/10.3406/palla.1999.1538. [Open access]","Roger Lambrechts, \"Deux miroirs étrusques inédits,\" <i>Revue belge de philologie et d'histoire</i> 76, no. 1 (1998): 159–76, https://doi.org/10.3406/rbph.1998.4259. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Liza Cleland, review of <i>Votives, Places and Rituals in Etruscan Religion</i>, ed. Margarita Gleba and Hilary Becker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2009.06.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.06.55/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Sleep_and_Death_Carrying_off_the_Slain_Sarpedon_%28cista_handle%29%2C_400-380_BC%2C_Etruscan%2C_bronze_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC08259.JPG/1920px-Sleep_and_Death_Carrying_off_the_Slain_Sarpedon_%28cista_handle%29%2C_400-380_BC%2C_Etruscan%2C_bronze_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC08259.JPG","title":"Etruscan bronzework","desc":"Etruscan bronze statuette — in the Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, Ohio, USA. Photography was permitted in the museum without restriction. This artwork is old enough so that it is in the public domain. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sleep_and_Death_Carrying_off_the_Slain_Sarpedon_(cista_handle),_400-380_BC,_Etruscan,_bronze_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC08259.JPG","alt":"Sleep and Death Carrying off the Slain Sarpedon (cista handle), 400-380 BCE, Etruscan, bronze - Cleveland Museum of Art"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why are these jugs the clearest measure of trade across the Alps?","a":"Because their Etruscan origin is well established and their dating fairly tight, and about 150 are known from Italy against more than a hundred from outside it."},{"q":"Why is the famous 'Etruscan' tin mine doubted?","a":"Because the earliest published find there is Roman, no tin slag has ever been found in Tuscany, and only one of 28 Tuscan ore samples reaches the tin content Bronze Age Britain and Portugal smelted."},{"q":"Why can a mirror be more than a mirror?","a":"Because the ancient sources put mirrors among the instruments by which the will of the gods was read."}]},
{"id":"rm-038","num":"38","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The lustrous black surface of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from burnishing alone before firing, with no slip or glaze, which is what separates it from Greek and Roman black-glazed pottery.","questions":["Fired in a reducing atmosphere at 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F), <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is black right through rather than coated, and 19th-century scholars called it the national pottery of the Etruscans.","Production of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began around Caere in the early 7th century BCE and intensified in the relief-figured workshops of inland Chiusi and Orvieto in the 6th."],"answer":"bucchero","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later ware</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th – 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Bucchero</b> is the black pottery of Etruria, black right through rather than coated, and by the 19th century scholars were calling it the national pottery of the Etruscans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What strikes a viewer first is the uniformly black lustre, and then the range of shapes and ornament, which mixes Greek borrowings with the Etruscans' own strongly orientalising inventions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Technically it belongs among the primitive ceramics: the firing and the process after it are exclusively reducing, at a low 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F), probably in clamp or pit kilns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shine comes from burnishing before firing, and there is no sintered clay coating and no slip, which is the essential technical break from the black-glazed wares of Greece and Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That places it in a firing tradition reaching back into prehistory, from which it differs more in artistic than technical qualities, at the fine end of an industry that also made coarse ware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Finely turned, polished and decorated pieces appear in the first decades of the 7th century BCE in southern Etruria, chiefly around Caere, whose early <i>bucchero sottile</i> has high-quality fabrics and fine, lustrous surfaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Southern Etruria is notable for the variety and richness of both its own and its imported pottery, and the innovations of its workshops intensified between the late 8th and the late 6th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During the 6th century production grew in central and inland Etruria, above all in the relief-figured vases of Chiusi and Orvieto, before fading rapidly at that century's end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shapes run from open forms such as the kotyle, kylix, chalice, kantharos and kyathos to closed amphorae and jugs, and ornament on impasto and bucchero is among the largest bodies of Etruscan imagery, though on these monochrome wares complex scenes are rare and mythological compositions exceptional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inscribed bucchero perfume flasks and outsize kyathoi passed between aristocratic families as gifts, and a sherd from Poggio Colla carries a stamp that may be the earliest scene of childbirth in European art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"bucchero","tags":["object","technology","archaeology","art","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Jean Gran-Aymerich, Christian Lahanier, Jeanne Gauthier, Alain Duval, Jean-Michel Malfoy, and Catherine Boyer, \"Sur deux groupes de bucchero examinés au Louvre,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome. Antiquité</i> 97, no. 2 (1985): 611–38, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1985.1472. [Open access]","Jean Gran-Aymerich, \"Images et mythes sur les vases noirs d'Étrurie (VIIIe–VIe siècle av. J.-C.),\" <i>Publications de l'École française de Rome</i> 253 (1999): 383–404, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_1999_act_253_1_5433. [Open access]","Jean Gran-Aymerich, \"Production et circulation des céramiques d'Étrurie méridionale : l'exemple du site littoral de La Castellina, entre Cerveteri et Tarquinia,\" <i>Publications de l'École française de Rome</i> 389 (2007): 101–8, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_2007_act_389_1_9067. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Robinson Peter Krämer, \"Plebeian Rituals and Peasant Cults? Religious Transformations in Etruria during the Sixth and Early Fifth Centuries BCE and their Socio-Political Context,\" in <i>Dependency and Social Inequality in Pre-Roman Italy</i>, ed. Martin Bentz and Patrick Zeidler (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2024), 121–56, https://doi.org/10.1515/9783111558417-006. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Bucchero_kantharos_Terme.jpg/1920px-Bucchero_kantharos_Terme.jpg","title":"bucchero","desc":"Bucchero kantharos, 3rd or 4th Latial Period. From tomb no. 62 at the cemetery of Osteria dell'Osa, near Rome. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucchero_kantharos_Terme.jpg","alt":"Bucchero kantharos Terme"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is the ware black all the way through?","a":"Because the firing and the process after it are exclusively reducing, at a low 600 to 700 °C, so the colour is in the fabric rather than in a coating."},{"q":"Why is its shine not a glaze?","a":"Because it comes from burnishing before firing, with no sintered clay coating and no slip, which is the essential technical break from the black-glazed wares of Greece and Rome."},{"q":"Why are complex scenes rare on it?","a":"Because the ware is monochrome, so ornament runs to shape and relief rather than to pictures, and mythological compositions are exceptional."}]},
{"id":"rm-039","num":"39","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still carries its paint, brown on the bare flesh and white on the black-hemmed clothing, so it shows that ancient sculpture was coloured rather than white.","questions":["Herakles has just seized the golden-horned hind in the roof group the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to, which is why the god strides towards him with one arm thrust out.","Taken down with its temple around 300 BCE and ritually buried, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of the ground almost intact, in pieces someone had laid in a careful row."],"answer":"Apollo of Veii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dismantled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Apollo of Veii</b> is a terracotta statue made for the roof of a temple in the Portonaccio sanctuary at Veii at the end of the 6th century BCE, and it still carries its paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colouring shows as a contrast between the brown of the bare flesh and the white of the clothing hemmed in black — ancient sculpture was brightly coloured, not white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The god strides barefoot to the left with the tight archaic smile, his left arm thrust forward in threat and the other lowered, perhaps once holding a bow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pose makes sense only against the statue facing him: Herakles, who has just seized the golden-horned hind sacred to Artemis as one of his labours, with Apollo coming for his sister's animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artemis herself and Mercury as peacemaker belonged to the same scene, and Latona carrying the infant Apollo stood among the figures ranged along the roof ridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Some Etruscan bronzes show the hero in eastern dress just after that capture, an episode Pindar placed among the Hyperboreans, neighbours of the Scythians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The temple under it was the first of Tuscan type found in Etruria, three cellae on a high square podium, and its decoration points to Delphi, so it was probably dedicated to Herakles and Apollo — though the sanctuary's own dedications name Menerva above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its upper parts carried terracottas so intricate that a system of syllabic marks was used to get each piece into its place, and about twenty acroteria stood on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Veii's workshops were at their height then, and Rome's kings sent to them for modellers in clay: Vulca, the only Etruscan artist the ancient sources name, was called in for the cult statue of the Capitoline temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around 300 BCE the temple was taken down and its sculpture ritually buried, as buildings elsewhere in Etruria were dismantled and their parts given to the gods like an offering; the Apollo came out of that ground almost intact in 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Apollo of Veii","tags":["object","art","archaeology","religion","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["\"Apollo di Veio,\" Museo Nazionale Etrusco di Villa Giulia, https://www.museoetru.it/capolavori/apollo-di-veio. [Open access]","Françoise Gaultier, \"Sanctuaires et territoires en pays étrusque : le cas de Véies,\" <i>Publications de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 21 (2010): 115–39, https://www.persee.fr/doc/keryl_1275-6229_2010_act_21_1_1418. [Open access]","Francesca Boitani and Iefke van Kampen, \"Veio,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 553–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4754. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Françoise Gaultier, \"Héraclès à Cerveteri : sur quelques terres cuites étrusques du musée du Louvre,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 152, no. 4 (2008): 1465–93, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2008.92238. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Apollo_di_Veio_-_Museo_di_Villa_Giulia_2.jpg","title":"Apollo of Veii","desc":"Apollo di Veio - Santuario di Portonaccio - Museo di Villa Giulia. Vulca (?), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apollo_di_Veio_-_Museo_di_Villa_Giulia_2.jpg","alt":"Apollo di Veio - Museo di Villa Giulia 2"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does the figure's pose only make sense with another beside it?","a":"Because he strides towards Herakles, who has just seized the golden-horned hind sacred to Artemis, with Artemis herself and Mercury as peacemaker in the same group."},{"q":"Why were syllabic marks cut into the roof sculpture?","a":"Because the terracottas were intricate enough that a system of marks was needed to get each piece into its place."},{"q":"Why did the statue survive nearly intact?","a":"Because around 300 BCE the temple was taken down and its sculpture ritually buried, as buildings elsewhere in Etruria were dismantled and their parts given to the gods."}]},
{"id":"rm-040","num":"40","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The two clusters of Etruscan speakers in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay around Capua and Pontecagnano, the southern counterpart of the Etruscan language area in the Po valley.","questions":["Pliny wrote that the ager Picentinus had once belonged to the Etruscans, one of the ancient claims for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and graves of Villanovan character later dug there bore him out.","In 438 BCE the Campanians swore themselves into a people of equals, which cut against the clientship <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on, and Capua fell to the Samnites soon after."],"answer":"Etruscan Campania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Writing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Campanian oath</span><span class=\"dt-v\">438 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan Campania</b> is the Etruscan settlement, language and rule that reached south of Latium into the plain behind the Bay of Naples, from the early Iron Age to the middle of the 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The earliest movement was a Villanovan one: groups from the Capenate and Veientine districts into the plain around Capua, and from coastal Etruria into the <i>ager Picentinus</i> south of the Sarno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It came in small waves rather than one conquest, and its dealings with the Ausones already there were more complicated than war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Etruscan-speaking communities are attested in two clusters, one around Capua and one around Pontecagnano, the southern prolongation of the Etruscan language area, matching the northern one in the Po valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Capua was the chief of them, called Volturnum before the Romans renamed it, and Roman writers made its foundation Etruscan and remembered an Etruscan lordship over Campania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For the southern cluster the ancient testimony is Pliny, who says the <i>ager Picentinus</i> had once belonged to the Etruscans, and Stephanus of Byzantium, who calls Picentia a city of Tyrrhenia; graves of Villanovan character later dug there bore them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Etruscan writing spread in Campania later than in southern Etruria, becoming common only in the 6th century, and in tombs and sanctuaries it works above all as a marker of the family name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inland cities grew with it — Nola and Nocera, with Pompeii serving as their port at the mouth of the Sarno — and Capuan bronze vessels were still being recommended to Roman farmers centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What kind of political control to imagine behind all this is much disputed, and no convincing narrative yet fits the archaeological material to the literary claim of Etruscan rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ended in the later 5th century: Greek settlement checked the expansion, and in 438 BCE the Campanians constituted themselves as a people, an oath among equals that cut against the clientship the Etruscan cities were built on, with Capua falling to the Samnites soon after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan Campania","tags":["place","history","archaeology","italy","iron age","etruscan","migration","language"],"sources":["Maria Bonghi-Jovino, \"Capua,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 4 (1985): 455–76, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1985_num_4_1_4613. [Open access]","Françoise-Hélène Massa-Pairault, \"La Campanie avant Rome. Recherches actuelles et problèmes,\" <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 22, no. 1 (1996): 33–61, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.1996.2262. [Open access]","Bruno d'Agostino, \"Pontecagnano,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 14 (1996): 187–98, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_1996_num_14_1_4037. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Daniele F. Maras, \"Etruscan: Language Overview,\" <i>Mnamon: Ancient Writing Systems in the Mediterranean</i> (Pisa: Scuola Normale Superiore), https://mnamon.sns.it/index.php?page=Lingua&id=35&lang=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Etruscan-Corinthian_Kotyle_%28cup%29_with_Gorgon%2C_from_Pontecagnano%2C_beginning_6th_century_BC%2C_Monsters._Fantastic_Creatures_of_Fear_and_Myth_Exhibition%2C_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme%2C_Rome_%2812818177694%29.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Etruscan Campania","desc":"Etruscan-Corinthian kotyle by the Painter of the Bad Wolf; figures emerge from the background among which that of the Gorgon; from Pontecagnano, tomb 856; beginning 6th century BCE, Pontecagnano, Museo Nazionale dell' Agro Picentino (inv. 35272). Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan-Corinthian_Kotyle_(cup)_with_Gorgon,_from_Pontecagnano,_beginning_6th_century_BC,_Monsters._Fantastic_Creatures_of_Fear_and_Myth_Exhibition,_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme,_Rome_(12818177694).jpg","alt":"Etruscan-Corinthian Kotyle (cup) with Gorgon, from Pontecagnano, beginning 6th century BCE, Monsters. Fantastic Creatures of Fear and Myth Exhibition, Palazzo Massimo alle Terme, Rome"},"difficulty":5,"locator":{"name":"Campania","at":[14.95,40.75],"kind":"region","area":[[13.75,41.25],[14.02,41.48],[14.48,41.5],[15,41.44],[15.58,41.4],[15.72,41.1],[15.6,40.8],[15.8,40.55],[15.75,40.24],[15.58,40.04],[15.3,40.02],[15,40.2],[14.9,40.42],[14.72,40.58],[14.45,40.62],[14.33,40.57],[14.2,40.65],[14.25,40.85],[14.02,41.05],[13.85,41.15]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the literary claim of Etruscan rule hard to test?","a":"Because no convincing narrative yet fits the archaeological material to it, and what kind of political control to imagine behind the settlement is much disputed."},{"q":"Why is the writing there read as a marker of family?","a":"Because it spread later than in southern Etruria, becoming common only in the 6th century, and in tombs and sanctuaries it works above all to carry the family name."},{"q":"Why did the oath of 438 BCE cut against Etruscan practice?","a":"Because the Campanians constituted themselves as a people by an oath among equals, where the Etruscan cities were built on clientship."}]},
{"id":"gr-223","num":223,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Latin name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> renders a Greek phrase whose meaning shrank over time, until under Rome it stood for the Greek-settled coasts of Italy from Cumae to Tarentum.","questions":["The cities of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shared an Italiote League, yet there is little reliable evidence that their inhabitants felt any communal regional identity, which may have been a Roman perception.","Greek settlement in the region later called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began at Pithekoussai on Ischia about the middle of the 8th century BCE and closed with Thurii in 444 BCE."],"answer":"Magna Graecia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 443 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Magna Graecia</b>, or <i>Megale Hellas</i>, is the name for the Greek-settled coasts of southern Italy, and it progressively shrank in meaning until in the Roman period it indicated the Greek inhabited areas from Cumae to Tarentum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settlement began in the mid-8th century BCE at Pithekoussai on Ischia and ran through Cumae, Poseidonia, Sybaris, Kroton, Metapontion and Taras, closing with the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii in 444 to 443 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sicily is often counted in with them, so that Naxos, Syracuse, Katane, Gela, Akragas and Selinus are surveyed in the same volumes as the mainland foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older model of a single founding act has been questioned for Greek settlements from southern Italy to the Black Sea, since it sits poorly with archaeology that points instead to continuous immigration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They shared a common Italiote League, yet there is little reliable evidence of what sense of communal regional identity the inhabitants had, and the region may be more a Roman perception than a local claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the archaic silver of Sybaris a bull stands out against the background on both faces, in relief on the obverse and carved inward into the flan on the reverse, before double-relief striking took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Greek cities lived among Oscan speakers, and a Lucanian pot of about 410 to 390 BCE carries <i>Posdan</i>, a variant of the theonym Poseidon whose syncope explains the Latin name Paestum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Italic newcomers took Poseidonia the <i>chora</i> was divided among them, yet Greek artistic forms, methods and materials continued there, and the sanctuary of Santa Venera stayed in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE Greek and Italic communities behaved within one framework of local rivalries and aristocratic competition, and Hannibal's arrival was an opportunity in a struggle that was essentially local.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Rome absorbed the region from about 350 BCE, with the Hannibalic War the decisive turn, after which Greek magistracies survived chiefly as vehicles for elite benefaction and public display.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Magna Graecia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","italy","archaic"],"sources":["William Owens, review of <i>Rome and the Western Greeks, 350 BC–AD 200: Conquest and Acculturation in Southern Italy</i>, by Kathryn Lomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.12/. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","Dominik Delp, review of <i>Comparing Greek Colonies: Mobility and Settlement Consolidation from Southern Italy to the Black Sea (8th–6th Century BC)</i>, ed. Camilla Colombi et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.06.38, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.06.38/. [Open access]","Jessica Brocchieri, Rosa Vitale and Carlo Sabbarese, “Double-Relief Silver Coins Minted in the Greek Colonies (444–390/340–280/270 BC) of Southern Italy Analysed by XRF,” <i>Quantum Beam Science</i> 8, no. 1 (2024): 2, https://doi.org/10.3390/qubs8010002. [Open access]","María José Estarán Tolosa, review of <i>Alloglossoi: Multilingualism and Minority Languages in Ancient Europe</i>, ed. Albio Cesare Cassio and Sara Kaczko, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.09.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.09.02/. [Open access]","John Pedley, review of <i>Ritorno a Santa Venera: storia del santuario di Afrodite Urania-Venere Iovia di Paestum</i>, by Mario Torelli, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.04.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.04.22/. [Open access]","Mark Thatcher, review of <i>Between Rome and Carthage: Southern Italy during the Second Punic War</i>, by Michael P. Fronda, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.09.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.09.22/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG/1920px-Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","title":"Magna Graecia","desc":"Temple of Concordia - Agrigento, Italy.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","alt":"Agrigento-Tempio della Concordia01"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the region perhaps more a Roman perception than a local claim?","a":"Because there is little reliable evidence of what sense of communal regional identity the inhabitants had, and the name progressively shrank in meaning until the Roman period fixed it."},{"q":"Why has the single founding act been questioned?","a":"Because it sits poorly with archaeology that points instead to continuous immigration, from southern Italy to the Black Sea."},{"q":"Why is Hannibal's arrival read as a local opportunity?","a":"Because by the 4th and 3rd centuries Greek and Italic communities behaved within one framework of local rivalries and aristocratic competition."}]},
{"id":"gr-224","num":224,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The stamp that turned a weighed lump of metal into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added an issuing authority's guarantee, so that a payment could be counted out instead of weighed and tested.","questions":["Seventh-century Anatolia is where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins, and it begins in electrum, a pale alloy of gold and silver rather than in either metal on its own.","Most explanations of the invention of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now settle on the state, which needed to make acceptable payments and then wanted to standardise and protect what it was paying in."],"answer":"coinage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Invented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 525 – 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Coinage</b> is metal issued in standardised pieces under a stamp, and what the stamp added to weighed bullion was an authority's guarantee, so that a payment could be counted instead of weighed and tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began in seventh-century Anatolia, and it began in electrum, a pale alloy of gold and silver rather than in either metal on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Lydia the series probably opens in the reign of Gyges, with Gyges and his son Alyattes the likeliest issuers of the earliest Lydian electrum, though the evidence falls short of proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The alloy was regulated rather than natural: the old view that these coins were struck from electrum panned out of the Pactolus has been discarded, and lead content points to gold from several sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Separate gold and silver coinages followed almost certainly under Croesus, and silver coinage was spreading through the Greek world around the middle of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What Greece used before is unsettled, and one case is that weighed silver was already the preferred medium of exchange there, so that coin only formalised a practice the Greeks had long had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that, the absence of Greek <i>Hacksilber</i>, silver chopped up and weighed, has been read as showing that spits, tripods and cauldrons were the recognised medium instead, and that the move to coin was direct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state is where most explanations now settle: coin came out of the need to make acceptable payments, and the motive shifted from the fiscal towards a wish to standardise and protect the currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early electrum staters look like issues struck to make high-value payments, and the invention has been read as driven by state military priorities rather than by everyday trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wide variation in the weights of the smallest denominations implies local monetisation rather than production for foreign trade, and Athens took up coinage about 525 to 500 BCE, amid a wider fiscal restructuring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"coinage","tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","technology"],"sources":["Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]","Elizabeth Kosmetatou, review of <i>Hacksilber to Coinage: New Insights into the Monetary History of the Near East and Greece</i>, ed. Miriam S. Balmuth, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.03.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.03.16. [Open access]","John H. Kroll, review of <i>Money and its Uses in the Ancient Greek World</i>, ed. Andrew Meadows and Kirsty Shipton, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.07.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.07.24/. [Open access]","Ben Akrigg, review of <i>Agoranomia: Studies in Money and Exchange Presented to John H. Kroll</i>, ed. Peter G. van Alfen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.12.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.12.04/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","Andrew Monson, review of <i>Ships and Silver, Taxes and Tribute: A Fiscal History of Archaic Athens</i>, by Hans van Wees, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.08.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.08.42/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Izmir_Archaeology_museum_Greek_coins_5810.jpg/1920px-Izmir_Archaeology_museum_Greek_coins_5810.jpg","title":"coinage","desc":"From a hoard found in Clazomenae, a number of \"staters\" (a standard measure) from the 6th century BCE. The central \"lion and bull\" one is thought to come from Lydia. This puts them with the first coins ever struck, the invention is thought to be Lydian.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Izmir_Archaeology_museum_Greek_coins_5810.jpg","alt":"Izmir Archaeology museum Greek coins 5810"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why did coinage begin in electrum rather than gold or silver?","a":"Because the earliest Anatolian issues are of that pale alloy, and its composition was regulated rather than natural — lead content points to gold from several sources."},{"q":"Why is what Greece used before coin unsettled?","a":"Because one case has weighed silver already the preferred medium, while the absence of Greek Hacksilber has been read as showing that spits, tripods and cauldrons served instead."},{"q":"Why is the invention now credited to the state rather than to trade?","a":"Because the early electrum staters look like issues struck to make high-value payments, and wide weight variation in the smallest denominations implies local monetisation rather than production for foreign trade."}]},
{"id":"gr-225","num":225,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The lion's head in profile is the commonest type on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and six of those coins carry the Lydian inscription WALWET, taken to name the king the Greeks called Alyattes.","questions":["Analysis has shown that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was artificially debased to about 54 per cent gold, so its composition was regulated rather than panned out of the Pactolus as it came.","The ladder of fractions in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs right down to a ninety-sixth of a stater, a tiny coin stamped with the type of a lion's paw rather than the usual head."],"answer":"Lydian electrum coinage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Struck</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alyattes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 610 – 560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Croesus reform</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lydian electrum coinage</b> is the earliest coinage of the Lydian kingdom, and the commonest coins from the Ephesus excavations are those stamped with a lion's head in profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was struck in graduated denominations, the largest a third of a Lydian weight stater at 4.7 grams (0.17 ounces), running down through a ladder of fractions to a forty-eighth at 0.29 grams (0.010 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smaller still is the ninety-sixth of a stater, which carries the type of a lion's paw, weighs 0.15 grams (0.0053 ounces) and measures only 4 millimetres (0.16 inches) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A twelfth-stater from the Central Basis of the Artemision sets its inscription between confronting lion heads with a single punch on the reverse, and about 155 coins with that legend are known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Six of the lion-head coins bear the Lydian inscription WALWET, which many take to record the king the Greeks knew as Alyattes, and a few others carry KUKALIM, 'of Gyges'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The dating rests on die links: the Kukalim coins share dies with the Walwet issues, and a coin from the foundations of the second shrine at the Artemision, no later than about 640 to 620 BCE, was struck from a punch used for Walwet coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The metal is not what it looks like, for analysis shows the lion-head coins were artificially debased to about 54 per cent gold, 44 per cent silver and one or two per cent copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Electrum does occur naturally in the streams of the region, but the old view that these coins were panned straight out of the Pactolus has been discarded, the composition being regulated rather than natural.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Simple refining installations stood outside the city walls near the Pactolus in the first half of that century, parting gold from silver with salt at about 800 °C (1,470 °F) and recovering the silver by cupellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around the middle of the 6th century BCE Croesus called in the electrum coins of the realm and exchanged them for a bimetallic coinage of pure gold and pure silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lydian electrum coinage","tags":["object","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","technology"],"sources":["John H. Kroll, “The Coins of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-kroll-coins-of-sardis. [Open access]","Crawford H. Greenewalt, Jr., with an addition by Nicholas Cahill, “Gold and Silver Refining at Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-greenewalt-gold-silver-refining. [Open access]","“Electrum Twelfth-Stater with Lydian Inscription ‘walwet’,” cat. 20 in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Istanbul Archaeological Museum IAM 5974, Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/artifacts/latw-20. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","Ben Akrigg, review of <i>Agoranomia: Studies in Money and Exchange Presented to John H. Kroll</i>, ed. Peter G. van Alfen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.12.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.12.04/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the coinage not what its metal looks like?","a":"Because analysis shows the lion-head coins were artificially debased to about 54 per cent gold, 44 per cent silver and one or two per cent copper."},{"q":"Why was the panning theory discarded?","a":"Because although electrum does occur naturally in the region's streams, the composition of these coins is regulated rather than natural, and refining installations stood near the Pactolus in the same century."},{"q":"Why can the issues be dated at all?","a":"Because of die links: the Kukalim coins share dies with the Walwet issues, and a coin from foundations no later than about 640 to 620 BCE was struck from a punch used for Walwet coins."}]},
{"id":"gr-226","num":226,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cyrus sent camels from his baggage train against the cavalry of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the best in the world at that time, and the horses took fright and threw the line into disarray.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Lydian empire reached its greatest extent, running east as far as the river Halys, with all the Greeks in Asia paying him tribute.","The reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended when Sardis fell after a fourteen-day siege, a Mardian having climbed an escarpment of the acropolis the Lydians thought too sheer to need a guard."],"answer":"Croesus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 547 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardis sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">547 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Croesus</b> is the last king of Lydia, fifth of the Mermnad line after Gyges, Ardys, Sadyattes and Alyattes, who ruled in patrilinear succession from about 680 BCE, and he held the throne from about 560 into the 540s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The western Anatolian empire those kings had built reached its greatest extent under him, running east as far as the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He attacked Ephesus and then made war on the other Aeolian and Ionian cities, and by the end of it all the Greeks in Asia were paying him tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lydian wealth in gold and silver became proverbial, and it came from secondary gold carried down by the mountain streams on the north side of Mount Tmolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was he who called in the electrum coinage of the realm and exchanged it for a bimetallic coinage of pure gold and pure silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When Cyrus revolted from Astyages, king of the Medes, Croesus prepared for war, and the two armies met in Cappadocia at the city of Pteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His cavalry was the best in the world at that time and the strongest arm of his army, and it was thrown into disarray when Cyrus sent camels from his baggage train into the fray, terrifying the horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Lydians were driven within their walls and besieged, and after fourteen days a Mardian named Hyroiades climbed an escarpment of the acropolis so sheer that they had thought it needed no guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sack is dated to 547 BCE on the evidence of the Nabonidus Chronicle, the fortification wall was burned along with houses at its foot and elsewhere in the city, and Croesus himself was taken alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His dedications at Delphi, his dealings with Greek oracles and his alliances with Greek states were still being argued over in Herodotus's day, and what is handed down of the dynasty's rise and his legendary wealth is largely tales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Croesus","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","archaic","anatolia"],"sources":["Crawford H. Greenewalt, Jr., “Introduction,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-greenewalt-introduction. [Open access]","Nicholas Cahill, “The Persian Sack of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-cahill-persian-sack-sardis. [Open access]","John H. Kroll, “The Coins of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-kroll-coins-of-sardis. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>The Mother of the Gods, Athens, and the Tyranny of Asia: A Study of Sovereignty in Ancient Religion</i>, by Mark H. Munn, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2006.10.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.10.27. [Open access]","Catherine M. Draycott, review of <i>The Lydians and Sardis</i>, by Hasan Dedeoglu, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.56/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg/1920px-Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg","title":"Croesus","desc":"Croesus at the stake. Side A from an Attic red-figure amphora, ca. 500–490 BCE. From en:Vulci.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kroisos_stake_Louvre_G197.jpg","alt":"Kroisos stake Louvre G197"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did his best arm fail him at Pteria?","a":"Because his cavalry was the strongest part of his army and was thrown into disarray when Cyrus sent camels from his baggage train into the fray, terrifying the horses."},{"q":"Why did the acropolis fall after only fourteen days?","a":"Because a Mardian named Hyroiades climbed an escarpment so sheer that the Lydians had thought it needed no guard."},{"q":"Why can the sack be dated firmly to 547 BCE?","a":"Because the Nabonidus Chronicle supplies the date, and the fortification wall was burned along with houses at its foot and elsewhere in the city."}]},
{"id":"gr-227","num":227,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A city that adopted its neighbour's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bought compatibility with that neighbour's coinage, which is usually explained by the wish for ready exchangeability in trade.","questions":["The sources do not state a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so it is inferred from the coins, by finding the heaviest surviving examples of a denomination and taking those to approximate it.","Much of the early electrum clings to the Lydo-Milesian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Attic one came in time to dominate the coinage of the whole Greek world."],"answer":"weight standard","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>weight standard</b> is the weight a coinage's denominations are struck to, and it is what decides whether one city's silver can be paid out against another's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not stated in the sources but inferred from the coins, by finding the heaviest examples of a denomination and taking those to approximate the standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second method reads the 80th percentile of a large sample, and on the early electrum the whole coinage shows a remarkable conformity to a single standard, matching a twelfth of a Cyzicene stater at the higher denomination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the early electrum clings closely to what is called the Lydo-Milesian standard, whose stater weighs about 14.1 grams (0.50 ounces), and Lydia's own lion-head coins were cut as fractions of a weight stater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Aeginetan standard puts its stater at about 12.4 grams (0.44 ounces), and the Attic standard came in time to dominate the coinage of the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first coins of Anatolia were struck on highly localised weight systems, and the use of electrum made exchange complicated on top of that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A city that took up a neighbour's standard bought compatibility with that neighbour's coinage, and the wish for ready exchangeability within trade networks is the usual explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tension between local coinages and the attempt to lock them into larger networks of common standards is one of the marks of ancient Mediterranean connectivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reading a standard off a table of weights is inference, and the same figures bear more than one reading: Bosporan drachms taken for Aeginetan imply a stater of 11.7 grams (0.41 ounces), nearer a Persian standard than an Aeginetan one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A full range of denominations of silver was present in northern Greece from the start, so the standard governed not just the stater but every fraction struck beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"weight standard","tags":["concept","history","greece","archaic","anatolia","technology"],"sources":["Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Beginning of Coinage in the Cimmerian Bosporus (A Hoard from Phanagoria)</i>, by Vladimir D. Kuznetsov and Mikhail G. Abramzon, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.46/. [Open access]","William E. Metcalf, review of <i>Money in Classical Antiquity</i>, by Sitta von Reden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.10.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.10.06/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, “Tout ce qui brille… Electrum and the Origins of Western Coinage,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 178 (2021): 443–70, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7859603. [Open access]","John H. Kroll, “The Coins of Sardis,” in <i>The Lydians and Their World</i> (2010), Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, https://sardisexpedition.org/en/essays/latw-kroll-coins-of-sardis. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]","Ben Akrigg, review of <i>Agoranomia: Studies in Money and Exchange Presented to John H. Kroll</i>, ed. Peter G. van Alfen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.12.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.12.04/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why must a standard be inferred rather than read?","a":"Because it is not stated in the sources, so it is found from the heaviest examples of a denomination, or from the 80th percentile of a large sample."},{"q":"Why would a city take up a neighbour's standard?","a":"Because it bought compatibility with that neighbour's coinage, the wish for ready exchangeability within trade networks being the usual explanation."},{"q":"Why can the same weights bear more than one reading?","a":"Because Bosporan drachms taken for Aeginetan imply a stater of 11.7 grams, nearer a Persian standard than an Aeginetan one."}]},
{"id":"gr-228","num":228,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew Greeks from many cities rather than one, and the four best known held the crown games at Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia and Nemea.","questions":["The idea that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood for Greek unity sits badly with the evidence, these being places wars were fought over and places that fuelled disunity through confrontational dedication.","At Dodona the sacred oak of Zeus, the percussive cauldrons and the flight of doves gave the oracle its voice, one shape a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could take beyond the games."],"answer":"Panhellenic sanctuary","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Circuit of four</span><span class=\"dt-v\">complete by 573 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Panhellenic sanctuary</b> is a sanctuary used by Greeks from many cities rather than by one, and the four best known are Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia and Nemea, which held the crown games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The label reaches wider than those four, and Dodona, Delos and the sanctuary of Zeus on Mount Lykaion in Arcadia are counted among them too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The games and the site grew together, the evolution of the games being directly related to the history of the sanctuary that housed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Olympia's history has been traced through nine chronological stages, running from the 3rd millennium BCE down to the abandonment of the site in the 7th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Delphi is the odd one out, a settlement as well as a ritual site, with little physical evidence of athletic building there before the 4th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The standard idea of Panhellenism does not tally well with the facts, and these were places wars were fought over, places that fuelled disunity through conspicuous and regularly confrontational dedication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were governed differently: Olympia is better thought of as an extra-urban sanctuary under the control of Elis, while at Delphi dedicators had a greater say in where their votives stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A monument was read against its neighbours rather than in a vacuum, so putting one up could be an act of one-upmanship with the structure beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The four festivals were not equal in standing, since catalogues of victories typically list Nemea last, and victory poetry has the laudatory compounds Olympionikos, Pythionikos and Isthmionikos but never Nemeonikos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The setting was part of the experience, wind, water, animals and people sounding in conditions imposed by natural features of oracular significance, as at Dodona with its sacred oak, its percussive cauldrons and its doves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Panhellenic sanctuary","tags":["place","concept","history","greece","archaic","religion"],"sources":["Chris Eckerman, review of <i>Delphi and Olympia: The Spatial Politics of Panhellenism in the Archaic and Classical Periods</i>, by Michael Scott, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.55. [Open access]","Paul Christesen, review of <i>Games and Sanctuaries in Ancient Greece: Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia, Nemea, Athens</i>, by Panos Valavanis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.20. [Open access]","Manolis Mikrakis, review of <i>Soundscape and Landscape at Panhellenic Greek Sanctuaries</i>, ed. Erica Angliker and Angela Bellia, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.11.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.11.35/. [Open access]","Roman Ivanov, review of <i>Pindar's Poetry, Patrons, and Festivals: From Archaic Greece to the Roman Empire</i>, ed. Simon Hornblower and Catherine Morgan, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.10.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.10.45/. [Open access]","David Lunt, review of <i>Two Studies in the History of Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.51. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/The_Temple_of_Apollo_%28Delphi%2C_Sanctuary_of_Apollo%29_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg/1920px-The_Temple_of_Apollo_%28Delphi%2C_Sanctuary_of_Apollo%29_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg","title":"Panhellenic sanctuary","desc":"The Temple of Apollo (Delphi, Sanctuary of Apollo) on October 4, 2020. George E.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Temple_of_Apollo_(Delphi,_Sanctuary_of_Apollo)_on_October_4,_2020.jpg","alt":"The Temple of Apollo (Delphi, Sanctuary of Apollo) on October 4, 2020"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does the standard idea of Panhellenism not tally with the facts?","a":"Because these were places wars were fought over, and places that fuelled disunity through conspicuous and regularly confrontational dedication."},{"q":"Why was putting up a monument an act against its neighbours?","a":"Because it was read against them rather than in a vacuum, so a dedication could be one-upmanship with the structure beside it."},{"q":"Why are the four festivals known not to have been equal?","a":"Because catalogues of victories typically list Nemea last, and victory poetry has Olympionikos, Pythionikos and Isthmionikos but never Nemeonikos."}]},
{"id":"gr-229","num":229,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The traditional date of 776 BCE for the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was settled on because that year synchronised Iphitus with Lycurgus, not from any written record of the festival.","questions":["Coming second or third at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted for nothing, and the amateur-and-professional framing once used to describe the competitors is now thought misleading.","Numbering successive Olympiads from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became a standard way of assigning dates to everything of significance in Greek history, long after the festival itself had begun."],"answer":"Olympic Games","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">776 BCE traditionally</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Victor list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 BCE, by Hippias</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">385 CE at least</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Olympic Games</b> are the athletic festival held at the sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia, which is better thought of as an extra-urban sanctuary under the control of Elis than as neutral ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are the senior of the four crown games: catalogues of victories typically list Nemea last, and of nearly 200 Olympic monuments recorded by Pausanias not one commemorates a victory from outside that circuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The events fall into running, combat, pentathlon and equestrian classes, though the programme can be reconstructed only hypothetically, on the basis of fragmentary literary sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only winning counted, coming second or third counting for nothing, and the amateur-and-professional framing that used to organise accounts of the games is now thought misleading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The cases usually cited for female participation, the Hera festival at Olympia, the Arcteia at Brauron and the Spartan model of education, relate not to women but to girls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The traditional date of 776 BCE rests on Hippias of Elis, who compiled the first complete list of Olympic victors around 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had no such list to work from and was forced to compile it himself, out of family reports and inscriptions that were not easily datable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He settled on 776 because that year synchronised Iphitus with Lycurgus, and not because of any written record of the games or of their victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the names he gives for the early victors can be trusted far more than the dates he assigned to their victories, or even than the order he put them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Numbering successive Olympiads became a standard way of assigning dates to everything of significance in Greek history, and the games were held until at least 385 CE, though when the last was held is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olympic Games","tags":["event","practice","history","greece","archaic","religion"],"sources":["Stephen Brunet, review of <i>Olympic Victor Lists and Ancient Greek History</i>, by Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.67, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.67/. [Open access]","David Lunt, review of <i>Two Studies in the History of Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.51. [Open access]","David Gilman Romano, review of <i>Ancient Greek Athletics: Primary Sources in Translation</i>, by Charles H. Stocking and Susan A. Stephens, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2023.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2023/2023.02.13/. [Open access]","Paul Christesen, review of <i>Games and Sanctuaries in Ancient Greece: Olympia, Delphi, Isthmia, Nemea, Athens</i>, by Panos Valavanis, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.01.20, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.01.20. [Open access]","Chris Eckerman, review of <i>Delphi and Olympia: The Spatial Politics of Panhellenism in the Archaic and Classical Periods</i>, by Michael Scott, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.55. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>The Ancient Olympic Games</i>, 2nd ed., by Judith Swaddling, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.24. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Ancient_Olympic_Starting_Line.JPG/1920px-Ancient_Olympic_Starting_Line.JPG","title":"Olympic Games","desc":"Part of the starting line at the stadium at Olympia.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Olympic_Starting_Line.JPG","alt":"Ancient Olympic Starting Line"},"difficulty":1,"why":[{"q":"Why is 776 BCE a synchronism rather than a record?","a":"Because Hippias of Elis had no list to work from and settled on that year because it synchronised Iphitus with Lycurgus, not because of any written record of the games."},{"q":"Why can his victors be trusted further than his dates?","a":"Because he compiled the names from family reports and inscriptions that were not easily datable, so the order and the years he assigned are the weakest part."},{"q":"Why are the usual cases for female participation set aside?","a":"Because the Hera festival at Olympia, the Arcteia at Brauron and the Spartan model of education relate not to women but to girls."}]},
{"id":"gr-230","num":230,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"During the games arms were not laid down anywhere in the Greek world, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> guaranteed a safe trip for athletes and spectators and nothing wider.","questions":["Modern commentators keen on peace and brotherhood have made much of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in no way sought to promote peace and cannot be linked to the brotherhood of humanity.","The secession of Mytilene was planned during the games of 428 BCE, and mercenaries used the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to go looking for employment."],"answer":"Olympic truce","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Games from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">776 BCE, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">393 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Olympic truce</b>, the <i>ekecheiria</i>, is the arrangement that surrounded the four-yearly games at the sanctuary of Zeus at Olympia, and it did a great deal less than its modern reputation suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The finding that has displaced the older picture is blunt: during the Olympic Games arms were not laid down in the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the truce actually guaranteed was a temporary safe trip for athletes, coaches and spectators, and it reached no further than the people travelling to the festival and home again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contemporary commentators wanting to stress the ideals of peace, unity and brotherhood claimed by the modern Olympics have made much of this so-called sacred truce, which in no way sought to promote peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word covers a suspension of hostilities against those on the road, not the suspension of a war, and it cannot be linked to any notion of the brotherhood of humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It served as a political instrument like any other: the secession of Mytilene was planned during the Olympic Games of 428 BCE, and mercenaries made use of it when they were looking for employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 420 BCE the Eleans flogged the Spartan Lichas, which intensified the tensions of the moment and brought Argos into the anti-Spartan alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sanctuary of Olympia and the agonistic festivals have a bellicose character of their own, and these were places wars were fought over rather than places that suspended them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The circuit the truce served was a competitive hierarchy rather than a fellowship, catalogues of victories typically listing Nemea last of the four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athletics excluded non-Greeks and women and may even have reinforced divisions within Greek society, and the games ran on regardless, numbered by Olympiads, until at least 385 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Olympic truce","tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic","religion","warfare"],"sources":["Christian Mann, review of <i>War-Peace and Panhellenic Games</i>, ed. Nikos Birgalias, Kostas Buraselis, Paul Cartledge et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.09.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.09.04. [Open access]","Michael Carter, review of <i>Onward to the Olympics: Historical Perspectives on the Olympic Games</i>, ed. Gerald P. Schaus and Stephen R. Wenn, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.56. [Open access]","Stephen Brunet, review of <i>Olympic Victor Lists and Ancient Greek History</i>, by Paul Christesen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.67, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.67/. [Open access]","David Lunt, review of <i>Two Studies in the History of Ancient Greek Athletics</i>, by Thomas Heine Nielsen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.06.51, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.06.51. [Open access]","Chris Eckerman, review of <i>Delphi and Olympia: The Spatial Politics of Panhellenism in the Archaic and Classical Periods</i>, by Michael Scott, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.55, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.55. [Open access]","Zinon Papakonstantinou, review of <i>The Ancient Olympic Games</i>, 2nd ed., by Judith Swaddling, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2000.06.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2000/2000.06.24. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did the truce not stop wars?","a":"Because it guaranteed only a temporary safe trip for athletes, coaches and spectators, and during the games arms were not laid down in the Greek world."},{"q":"Why was it used as a political instrument?","a":"Because the secession of Mytilene was planned during the games of 428 BCE, and mercenaries made use of it when looking for employment."},{"q":"Why has its modern reputation grown so far past it?","a":"Because commentators wanting to stress the ideals of peace, unity and brotherhood claimed by the modern Olympics have made much of a truce that in no way sought to promote peace."}]},
{"id":"gr-231","num":231,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Several villages together with outlying Amyklai made up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had no city wall at all and held a territory of roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles).","questions":["Every citizen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a hoplite, where in other cities the hoplites were only the wealthiest 30 to 50 per cent, and all of them dined in their messes every night.","The archaeology of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a central settlement of no great monumental character in the archaic and classical periods, with streets not laid on a grid until the imperial period."],"answer":"Sparta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leading power</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 371 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leuctra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">371 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sparta</b> is the Greek city of Laconia, made up of several villages together with outlying Amyklai rather than a single walled town, and it held a territory of roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It had no city wall, and the explanations offered for that run to Spartan conservatism, the presence of the <i>perioikoi</i>, and the sheer difficulty of enclosing the four central villages and Amyklai together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was like an armed camp whose citizens were required to dine in their messes every night, and all Spartiates were hoplites, where in other cities the hoplites were the wealthiest 30 to 50 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archaeology does not match that picture: the central settlement had no significantly monumental character in the archaic and classical periods, and its streets were not laid on a rectangular grid before the imperial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Material culture was no simple reflection of the development of a military ethos, and at the sanctuary of Artemis Orthia the votive lead figurines begin to increase well before the supposed 6th-century date of austerity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The city rested on the helots, enslaved as a nation to the Spartan state and given brutal treatment by their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Laconian and Messenian helots were not one group: the Messenians laid claim to an Achaean heritage older than the Dorian, and Laconian helotage outlasted Messenian liberation by centuries, ending only under Roman domination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their numbers are argued over, with 7 to 1 proposed as an upper limit on the ratio of helots to Spartans, below the higher figures once current.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The institutions attributed to the lawgiver Lycurgus include the <i>gerousia</i>, the <i>agoge</i>, the <i>krypteia</i> and the <i>syssitia</i>, and the kingship was dual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Unlocking any of it is hard, because there are very few direct Spartan sources and what survives is mostly non-Spartan and severely influenced by the Spartans' own manipulation of their image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Sparta","tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","warfare","politics"],"sources":["Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Graham Shipley, review of <i>Sparta in Laconia: The Archaeology of a City and its Countryside</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh and S. E. C. Walker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.02. [Open access]","Review of <i>Spartan Reflections</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.02.13/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Ancient_Sparta_columns.jpg/1920px-Ancient_Sparta_columns.jpg","title":"Sparta","desc":"Fallen column drums and architrave blocks lying where they were excavated on the site of ancient Sparta, along a path running under olive trees.","credit":"Photograph by Peulle, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Sparta_columns.jpg","alt":"Two large fluted column drums lying on bare earth in the foreground, a row of long rectangular stone blocks behind them, and more scattered masonry running away up a path between old olive trees, with a few visitors in the distance"},"difficulty":1,"locator":{"name":"Sparta","at":[22.4236,37.0819]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the archaeology not match the armed-camp picture?","a":"Because the central settlement had no significantly monumental character in the archaic and classical periods, and the votive lead figurines at Artemis Orthia begin to increase well before the supposed 6th-century austerity."},{"q":"Why were the Laconian and Messenian helots not one group?","a":"Because the Messenians laid claim to an Achaean heritage older than the Dorian, and Laconian helotage outlasted Messenian liberation by centuries."},{"q":"Why is the city so hard to study?","a":"Because there are very few direct Spartan sources, and what survives is mostly non-Spartan and severely influenced by the Spartans' own manipulation of their image."}]},
{"id":"gr-232","num":232,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The region Sparta ruled was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its heartland the Eurotas valley, framed by Taygetos on the west and Parnon on the east and running south to Malea and Tainaron.","questions":["Survey east of Sparta found very little long-term continuity in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each broad period laying down anew its own pattern and hierarchy of settlement.","A sudden proliferation of 87 sites appears in the mid-6th century BCE in the surveyed part of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, followed by widespread abandonment in the classical period."],"answer":"Laconia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spartan from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lost Messenia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">369 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Laconia</b> is the region of the southern Peloponnese that Sparta ruled, its heartland the Eurotas valley, the Spartan plain, with the neighbouring rural landscapes running out from it on either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains frame it, Taygetos along the west and Parnon along the east, and it runs south to the peninsulas of Malea and Tainaron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its road network divides into four zones, north, east, south and west, and a route ran into and probably across Taygetos, separate from the roads to Gytheion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The perioikic towns had territories of their own, and in the territory of Geronthrai, the modern Geraki, no fewer than 13 routes have been identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> People were on the land early, the surveyed country having been first colonised in late and final neolithic times, though dense occupation appears only from Early Helladic II onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta's territory ran to roughly 8,000 square kilometres (3,100 square miles), and a survey of 70 square kilometres (27 square miles) east of the city set out to examine how the heartland stood to the country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it found was very little long-term continuity, each broad period of time laying down anew a unique pattern and hierarchy of settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the mid-6th century BCE there is a sudden proliferation of 87 sites in the surveyed area, and in the classical period a widespread abandonment of many of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who was doing the farming is a question the survey raised and could not settle: Spartiates, perioikoi, helots, or some inferior Spartan group working the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Laconian helots were not the same people as the Messenians, and Laconian helotage outlasted Messenian liberation by centuries, coming to an end only under Roman domination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Laconia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","archaic","geography"],"sources":["David Pettegrew, review of <i>Continuity and Change in a Greek Rural Landscape: The Laconia Survey</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh et al., Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.09.42, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.09.42/. [Open access]","Graham Shipley, review of <i>Sparta in Laconia: The Archaeology of a City and its Countryside</i>, ed. William G. Cavanagh and S. E. C. Walker, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.03.02, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.03.02. [Open access]","Review of <i>Spartan Reflections</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.02.13/. [Open access]","D. Graham J. Shipley, review of <i>The Road-Network of Lakonike</i>, by Giannes A. Pikoulas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.16. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Eurotas.JPG/1920px-Eurotas.JPG","title":"Laconia","desc":"Rivière Eurotas, quelques kilomètres en aval de Mistra.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eurotas.JPG","alt":"Eurotas"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Laconia","at":[22.5,37],"kind":"region","area":[[22.05,37.3],[22.45,37.45],[22.85,37.35],[23.1,37.05],[23.2,36.75],[23.05,36.45],[22.75,36.7],[22.55,36.4],[22.3,36.65],[22.2,37]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the survey find so little long-term continuity?","a":"Because each broad period of time laid down anew a unique pattern and hierarchy of settlement, rather than building on the one before."},{"q":"Why could the survey not say who farmed the land?","a":"Because Spartiates, perioikoi, helots or some inferior Spartan group are all possible, and the sites themselves do not distinguish between them."},{"q":"Why do the perioikic towns matter to the region's roads?","a":"Because they had territories of their own — in that of Geronthrai alone no fewer than 13 routes have been identified."}]},
{"id":"gr-233","num":233,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The towns of Laconia and Messenia together with Sparta itself constituted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the state, where Sparta was only the city at the head of it.","questions":["Aristotle wrote that there are six so-called <i>morai</i> and that all the citizens of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are divided into them, which is where the shape of the state shows.","Of the 292 men taken alive from the garrison on Sphakteria, picked by lot from all the divisions of the army of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, roughly 120 were <i>Spartiatai</i>."],"answer":"Lacedaemon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 195 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lacedaemon</b> is the state where Sparta is the city: the free inhabitants of the many towns of Laconia and Messenia, along with Sparta itself, constituted the state known as Lakedaimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two names run in parallel, so <i>Spartiatai</i> and <i>Lakedaimonioi</i> can each be used of the <i>homoioi</i>, or more broadly of the citizens of the Spartan <i>polis</i> and of the Lakedaimonian state respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The towns are the perioikic communities, and they seem to have been <i>poleis</i> in their own right, albeit ones politically dependent on Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is disputed: one case classifies them as villages whose people were citizens of the Spartan <i>polis</i> in no sense at all, another defends the traditional view that they were citizens without a Spartiate's rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The national festival, the Hyakinthia, reinforced the hierarchical divide between city and territory, where the Athenian equivalent united <i>polis</i> and <i>chora</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The army is where the arrangement shows, and Aristotle wrote that there are six so-called <i>morai</i> and that all the Lakedaimonians are divided into them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The garrison caught on Sphakteria had been picked by lot from all the divisions of the Lakedaimonian army, and of the 292 taken alive roughly 120 were <i>Spartiatai</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One reading is that as the <i>homoioi</i> declined in number the <i>morai</i> were filled mostly from the <i>hypomeiones</i>, Spartans short of full citizenship, while most <i>perioikoi</i> went on fighting in contingents of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading the <i>morai</i> were the civic contingent of Sparta itself within the larger Lakedaimonian army, an arrangement that strained: some perioikic soldiers refused call-up orders and others deserted once called.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below both orders stood the helots, enslaved as a nation to the Spartan state, and none of it is easy to read, since very few sources are Spartan and what survives is shaped by the Spartans' management of their own image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lacedaemon","tags":["state","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Review of <i>Spartan Reflections</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2002.02.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2002/2002.02.13/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/The_Ancient_Theatre_of_Sparta_on_May_15%2C_2019.jpg/1920px-The_Ancient_Theatre_of_Sparta_on_May_15%2C_2019.jpg","title":"Lacedaemon","desc":"The ancient theatre of Sparta on the southern slope of the acropolis hill, a site ancient authors connect with the celebration of the Gymnopaidiae. George E.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ancient_Theatre_of_Sparta_on_May_15,_2019.jpg","alt":"The curved stone seating of the ancient theatre of Sparta"},"difficulty":4,"locator":{"name":"Lacedaemon","at":[22.4236,37.0819],"kind":"region","area":[[21.5,37.2],[21.9,37.48],[22.2,37.52],[22.45,37.45],[22.85,37.35],[23.1,37.05],[23.2,36.75],[23.05,36.45],[22.75,36.7],[22.55,36.4],[22.3,36.65],[21.9,36.7],[21.6,36.75],[21.5,37]]},"why":[{"q":"Why do two names run in parallel for the same people?","a":"Because Spartiatai and Lakedaimonioi name the citizens of the Spartan polis and of the wider state respectively, and the state included the towns of Laconia and Messenia."},{"q":"Why is the standing of the perioikic towns disputed?","a":"Because one case classifies them as villages whose people were citizens of Sparta in no sense at all, and another defends them as citizens without a Spartiate's rights."},{"q":"Why does the national festival matter to that hierarchy?","a":"Because the Hyakinthia reinforced the divide between city and territory, where the Athenian equivalent united polis and chora."}]},
{"id":"gr-234","num":234,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Sparta's kings claimed descent from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the line of Herakles, and that claim is the charter on which Dorian Sparta rested its hold on the Peloponnese.","questions":["Tyrtaeus gives the earliest mention of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, writing of ancestors who came under the guidance of kings who were descendants of Herakles.","The genealogy of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was invented in Argos and developed further in Sparta, where it made conquest look like rightful inheritance rather than seizure."],"answer":"Herakleidai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the genealogy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Herakleidai</b> are the descendants of Herakles from whom the kings of Sparta claimed to come, and that claim is the charter of Dorian Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Spartan kingship was dual, and both of its royal houses traced themselves back along the same line to the same ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyrtaeus gives the earliest mention, writing of ancestors who came under the guidance of kings who were descendants of Herakles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pindar, Herodotus, Isocrates and Ephorus carry it forward, Herodotus making Aristodemus the great-grandson of Hyllus and Ephorus working the story into its dominant version.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The royal descent is a secondary reading of the older story of the Return of the Herakleidai, and the genealogy was invented in Argos before being developed further in Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is genealogical fiction serving a political purpose rather than a record of anything, and it belongs with the rest of what modern work has tried to see past under the name of the Spartan mirage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it did was legitimise Dorian settlement, since territorial right came down from Herakles, and conquest could then be presented as rightful inheritance rather than as seizure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Heraklids were brought into the local genealogy perhaps as early as the beginning of the 7th century BCE, while the fundamental formation of Spartan statehood is put in the 8th, on literary and archaeological evidence together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state the claim underwrote was Lakedaimon, the free inhabitants of the towns of Laconia and Messenia along with Sparta itself, and not the city on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> None of it is easy to check, since very few sources are Spartan and what survives is mostly non-Spartan and severely influenced by the Spartans' management of their own image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Herakleidai","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Alexey L. Darvin, “Origin of Spartan Kings from Heracles,” <i>RUDN Journal of World History</i> 10, no. 3 (2018): 237–49, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8127-2018-10-3-237-249. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Hercules_Nessus_MAN_Napoli_Inv9001.jpg/1920px-Hercules_Nessus_MAN_Napoli_Inv9001.jpg","title":"Herakleidai","desc":"Hercules carrying his son Hyllus looks at the centaur Nessus, who is about to carry Deianira across the river on his back.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hercules_Nessus_MAN_Napoli_Inv9001.jpg","alt":"Hercules Nessus MAN Napoli"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was descent from Herakles worth claiming?","a":"Because territorial right came down from him, so conquest could be presented as rightful inheritance rather than as seizure."},{"q":"Why is the royal descent a secondary reading?","a":"Because it grows out of the older story of the Return of the Herakleidai, a genealogy invented in Argos before being developed further in Sparta."},{"q":"Why is the claim called genealogical fiction?","a":"Because it serves a political purpose rather than recording anything, and belongs with the rest of what is studied under the name of the Spartan mirage."}]},
{"id":"gr-235","num":235,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"By the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Sparta took Messenia, and the people of the conquered country were reduced to the status of helots in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.","questions":["For fifth-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides there was only one archaic conflict, and the pair of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> everyone now counts was established only in the 4th century.","When Sparta expanded into Messenia, at the time of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, there was no Messenian ethnic identity, and the region became a coherent one only under Spartan control."],"answer":"Messenian Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 460 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liberation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">370 – 369 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Messenian Wars</b> are the wars by which Sparta took Messenia, whose people were reduced to the status of helots after the conquest of their country in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The towns of Messenia thereafter counted, with those of Laconia and with Sparta itself, in the state known as Lakedaimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their historicity does not let itself be easily reconstructed, and the number of them turns out to belong to the tradition rather than to be a fact standing behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word Messene named a smaller country then than the classical region does, and for 5th-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides there was only one archaic Messenian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An account with two archaic wars was established in the 4th century and developed afterwards, elaborated by Myron of Priene and Rhianus, whose work Pausanias adapted freely into a highly idiosyncratic and colourful version.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Sparta expanded into Messenia there was no Messenian ethnic identity, and Messenia was not perceived as a coherent geographical region, becoming one only under Spartan control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaic Messenia had no obviously predominant central settlement, and it was not developing as other Greek regions were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Messenian identity emerged in the 5th century, and the rebellion against the Spartans in the 460s was critical to it, the rebels choosing the name of Messenians for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those exiled at the end of that war and settled by the Athenians at Naupaktos proclaimed the identity in very visible dedications at Delphi and Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The liberation of 370 to 369 BCE created a free Messenian state, and none of the story is easy to check, since very few sources are Spartan and what survives is shaped by Sparta's management of its own image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Messenian Wars","tags":["event","history","greece","archaic","warfare","politics"],"sources":["James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Yves Lafond, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.30/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/63/Aristomenes_fighting_his_way_out_of_Ira.jpg","title":"Messenian Wars","desc":"Aristomenes fighting his way out of Ira. Edmund Ollier Publication date 1882.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristomenes_fighting_his_way_out_of_Ira.jpg","alt":"Aristomenes fighting his way out of Ira"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does the number of wars belong to the tradition?","a":"Because for 5th-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides there was only one archaic Messenian war, and the two-war account was established in the 4th century."},{"q":"Why was there no Messenian identity to conquer?","a":"Because when Sparta expanded there was none, and Messenia was not perceived as a coherent geographical region, becoming one only under Spartan control."},{"q":"Why did that identity emerge when it did?","a":"Because the rebellion of the 460s was critical to it, the rebels choosing the name for themselves and the exiles settled at Naupaktos proclaiming it in dedications at Delphi and Olympia."}]},
{"id":"gr-236","num":236,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tyrtaeus wrote that the fighting of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran nineteen years, and that in the twentieth the enemy left their rich lands and fled from the great uplands of Ithome.","questions":["Pausanias dated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to 743 to 724 BCE, but those figures are Hellenistic, resting on Spartan kinglists where one generation was reckoned at forty years.","Because Messene then meant a limited area rather than the classical region, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to the plain of the upper Pamisus and the lands about Mount Ithome."],"answer":"First Messenian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional</span><span class=\"dt-v\">743 – 724 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lower dating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 690 – 670 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>First Messenian War</b> is the earlier of the two archaic wars in which Sparta made Messenia its own and its people helots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tyrtaeus is the nearest thing to a contemporary witness, naming King Theopompus, writing of the spearmen fathers of his hearers' fathers, and giving the fighting a length of twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his lines the war ran nineteen years, and in the twentieth the foeman left his rich lands and fled from the great uplands of Ithome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For Tyrtaeus, as for Homer, Messene meant a limited area rather than the classical region, and the fighting belongs to the plain of the upper Pamisus, the lands about that mountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The long narrative everyone knows is Pausanias's, a text of the 2nd century CE and the product of a very creative engagement with previous works, and it is hardly any history at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He dates the war to 743 to 724 BCE, but those figures are Hellenistic and rest on genealogies taken from the Spartan kinglists, where one generation was reckoned at forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Olympic victor list, which carries seven Messenian winners between 768 and 736 BCE and none after, has been read as support for those dates and proves nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lower chronology rests instead on Epaminondas, who at his trial said he had freed Messenia after 230 years of subjection, putting the second war's end near 600 BCE and this one about 690 to 670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the number of wars belongs to the tradition rather than standing behind it, since 5th-century historians such as Antiochus of Syracuse and Thucydides knew only one archaic Messenian war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tyrtaeus himself wrote in the second half of the 7th century BCE, and his <i>Eunomia</i>, taken with the Great Rhetra, suggests that unequal distribution of land may be the most cogent explanation for the annexation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"First Messenian War","tags":["event","warfare","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","J. M. Edmonds, ed. and trans., <i>Elegy and Iambus</i>, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), Tyrtaeus fr. 3, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0479:volume=1:text=2:section=2. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Yves Lafond, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.30/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Ithome_east_summit.jpg/1920px-Ithome_east_summit.jpg","title":"First Messenian War","desc":"Mount Ithome (Messene): eastern summit. Mark Landon (photographed in 1990.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ithome_east_summit.jpg","alt":"Ithome east summit"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is Pausanias's long narrative hardly history?","a":"Because it is a text of the 2nd century CE and the product of a very creative engagement with previous works."},{"q":"Why are the traditional dates of 743 to 724 BCE unsound?","a":"Because they are Hellenistic and rest on genealogies taken from the Spartan kinglists, where one generation was reckoned at forty years."},{"q":"Why does Tyrtaeus not describe the region a reader expects?","a":"Because for him, as for Homer, Messene meant a limited area rather than the classical region — the plain of the upper Pamisus about Ithome."}]},
{"id":"gr-237","num":237,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tyrtaeus rallied the Spartans in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his own date puts that fighting firmly in the second half of the 7th century BCE rather than in Pausanias's 685 to 668.","questions":["Aristomenes is the Messenian hero of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, winning several victories before he was betrayed and forced to withdraw to Mount Eira, though whether he was a real person cannot be known.","Pausanias took his account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Rhianus of Crete, who had King Laotychidas commanding, though two Spartan kings bore that name and only the later was known to him."],"answer":"Second Messenian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional</span><span class=\"dt-v\">685 – 668 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lower dating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 635 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Second Messenian War</b> is the later of the two archaic wars, and Pausanias makes it a revolt of Messenians already under Spartan rule rather than a fresh conquest, breaking out in the reign of Anaxander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tyrtaeus is the contemporary evidence, since it was he who rallied the Spartans to defeat Messenia in this second war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His vocabulary and the hortatory character of his poetry put him later than Archilochus and Callinus, and there is no reason to date any poem of his earlier than 640 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That lodges the war firmly in the second half of the 7th century, unless higher dates are wanted for Archilochus, Callinus, Gyges and the Assyrian inscriptions of Assurbanipal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias dates it to 685 to 668 BCE, figures rejected out of hand because they cannot be squared with his own dates for the earlier war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A lower chronology puts its end near 600 BCE, on Epaminondas's word at his trial that he had freed Messenia after 230 years of subjection, and its beginning about 635 to 625.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias took his account from Rhianus of Crete, who had King Laotychidas commanding; two Spartan kings bore that name and Pausanias knew only the later, whose reign began in 491 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristomenes is the war's Messenian hero, winning several victories before he was betrayed and forced to withdraw to Mount Eira, and it is impossible to know whether he was a real person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tyrtaeus gives a fair summary of the Spartan constitution as it stood in this war and knows nothing of the ephors, whom a story has appointed to run the state while the kings were away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For all its fame, Sparta's hold on Messenia was no anomaly, but the most spectacular and best attested instance of something Greek states did to one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Second Messenian War","tags":["event","warfare","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Barbara McCauley, review of <i>Aristomenes of Messene: Legends of Sparta's Nemesis</i>, by Daniel Ogden, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.07.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.07.46. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Franc_Kav%C4%8Di%C4%8D_-_Dekle_re%C5%A1i_Aristomena_iz_ujetni%C5%A1tva.jpg","title":"Second Messenian War","desc":"Franc Kavčič - Dekle reši Aristomena iz ujetništva. Franz Caucig.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franc_Kav%C4%8Di%C4%8D_-_Dekle_re%C5%A1i_Aristomena_iz_ujetni%C5%A1tva.jpg","alt":"Franc Kavčič - Dekle reši Aristomena iz ujetništva"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why are Pausanias's dates for the war rejected outright?","a":"Because they cannot be squared with his own dates for the earlier war, so the two accounts contradict each other."},{"q":"Why does his source create a further problem?","a":"Because he took it from Rhianus, who had a King Laotychidas commanding, and Pausanias knew only the later king of that name, whose reign began in 491 BCE."},{"q":"Why is Sparta's hold on Messenia called no anomaly?","a":"Because it is the most spectacular and best attested instance of something Greek states did to one another."}]},
{"id":"gr-238","num":238,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Each man on a Spartan campaign was to give one of the songs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once the paean had been sung after supper, which is the custom Athenaeus records.","questions":["The ancient life makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an Athenian, a lame schoolmaster of little sense, sent when the Delphic oracle bade the Spartans take a counsellor from Athens.","Aristotle cites the <i>Eunomia</i> of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as evidence that civil strife breaks out when some are in want while others are rich, men pressed by the war demanding a redistribution of land."],"answer":"Tyrtaeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 633 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 books in antiquity</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tyrtaeus</b> is the Spartan elegiac poet of the Second Messenian War, and his verse is the nearest thing to a contemporary account of Sparta's conquest of Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexandrian scholars had five books of him, holding a poem on citizenship, exhortations in elegiac verse and war songs, among them the <i>Embateria</i>, or songs of the battle charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Suidas puts his flourishing in the 35th Olympiad and Jerome in the 36th, and his vocabulary and hortatory manner place him after Archilochus and Callinus, so no poem of his need be earlier than 640 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The ancient life makes him an Athenian, a lame schoolmaster thought to have little sense, sent when the Delphic oracle bade the Spartans take a counsellor from Athens, though Suidas calls him a Laconian or a Milesian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athenaeus records that after the Spartans had beaten the Messenians it became their custom on campaign that, once the paean had been sung after supper, each man should give one of his songs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His lines on the earlier war name King Theopompus and the fathers of his hearers' fathers, run to twenty years, and end with the enemy fleeing the great uplands of Ithome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his usage Messene named a smaller country than the classical region, as it does in Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Eunomia</i> was a hortatory poem carrying an oracle brought from Delphi and the return of the Heracleidae, on which the Spartan kings rested their right to Laconia, and in it the people are urged to obey the kings and the elders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle cites the poem as evidence that civil strife breaks out when some are in want while others are rich, since men pressed by the war demanded a redistribution of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias has him talking the citizens out of that quarrel, and he has been read as a Spartan Solon, though whether the <i>Eunomia</i> has anything to do with the Great Rhetra is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tyrtaeus","tags":["person","literature","history","greece","archaic","warfare"],"sources":["J. M. Edmonds, ed. and trans., <i>Elegy and Iambus</i>, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), Tyrtaeus, life and fragments, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0479:volume=1:text=2. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Franz_Kollar%C5%BE_-_Tyrtaeus_inspires_the_Spartans_with_his_war_songs.jpg","title":"Tyrtaeus","desc":"Franz Kollarž - Tyrtaeus inspires the Spartans with his war songs. Franz Kollarž. c. 1872.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Kollar%C5%BE_-_Tyrtaeus_inspires_the_Spartans_with_his_war_songs.jpg","alt":"Franz Kollarž - Tyrtaeus inspires the Spartans with his war songs"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is his verse the nearest thing to a contemporary account?","a":"Because he rallied the Spartans in the second war himself, and his vocabulary and hortatory manner place him after Archilochus and Callinus, so no poem of his need be earlier than 640 BCE."},{"q":"Why does Aristotle cite the Eunomia as social evidence?","a":"Because it shows civil strife breaking out when some are in want while others are rich, men pressed by the war demanding a redistribution of the land."},{"q":"Why did his songs outlive the war?","a":"Because Athenaeus records that after the Messenians were beaten it became the Spartan custom on campaign that each man give one of his songs after the paean."}]},
{"id":"gr-239","num":239,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The enslaved population of Laconia and Messenia were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who worked Spartiate estates and rendered their masters a proportional share of the crop, put at half of it.","questions":["The status of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was double, since they were the private property of individual Spartiates and at the same time subjects of a significant communal domination.","An average Spartiate estate held about five families of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a ratio of seven of them to each Spartiate has been proposed as an upper limit for their numbers."],"answer":"helots","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enslaved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Messenia freed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">370 – 369 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Helots</b> are the enslaved population of Laconia and Messenia who worked the land for the Spartiates, reduced to that status after the conquest of their country in the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They rendered their masters a proportional share of what they grew on Spartiate estates, put at half the crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their status was double: the private property of individual Spartiates, and at the same time subjects of a significant communal domination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the second half of that meant in practice is that the state freed helots and killed them without reference to the Spartiates who owned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They normally stayed attached to the holdings they cultivated rather than to particular Spartiates, an arrangement that safeguarded their subsistence and made landowner and cultivator dependent on one another over the long term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What to call the arrangement is argued: one account has them enslaved as a nation to the Spartan state, and another holds that plain slavery best describes their condition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A third has them individually owned, with restrictions on how they could be disposed of, and insists that they were not chattel slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Numbers are argued over as well: an average estate held about five families of them, an ordinary citizen's holding ran to some 18 hectares (45 acres), and a ratio of seven helots to each Spartiate has been proposed as an upper limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Laconian and the Messenian helots were not one people, the Messenians appearing to have claimed an Achaean heritage older than the Dorian, and their revolt in the 460s BCE was critical to the emergence of a Messenian identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Laconian helotage outlasted the liberation of Messenia by centuries, and the familiar modern picture of the whole institution rests less on the ancient sources than on an idiosyncratic selection from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"helots","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Ellen Millender, review of <i>Property and Wealth in Classical Sparta</i>, by Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.07.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.07.26/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Yves Lafond, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2009.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2009/2009.04.30/. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Fernand_Sabatt%C3%A9_-_Un_Spartiate_montre_%C3%A0_ses_fils_un_ilote_ivre_%281900%29.jpg","title":"helots","desc":"Fernand Sabatté - Un Spartiate montre à ses fils un ilote ivre (1900). Fernand Sabatté.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fernand_Sabatt%C3%A9_-_Un_Spartiate_montre_%C3%A0_ses_fils_un_ilote_ivre_(1900).jpg","alt":"Fernand Sabatté - Un Spartiate montre à ses fils un ilote ivre (1900)"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is their status called double?","a":"Because they were the private property of individual Spartiates and at the same time subjects of a communal domination — the state freed them and killed them without reference to their owners."},{"q":"Why did attachment to the land rather than to a master matter?","a":"Because it safeguarded their subsistence and made landowner and cultivator dependent on one another over the long term."},{"q":"Why is the familiar modern picture of the institution suspect?","a":"Because it rests less on the ancient sources than on an idiosyncratic selection from them."}]},
{"id":"gr-240","num":240,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The free people of the towns spread across Laconia and Messenia were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, members of the state of Lakedaimon alongside Sparta itself.","questions":["Herodotus has Demaratos tell Xerxes that Sparta holds about 8,000 men, all equals of those who fell, and that the other Lakedaimonians, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, are not their equals but are good men.","The communities of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were small, of 400 to 600 people, and looked much like domestic colonies scattered through both Laconia and Messenia."],"answer":"perioikoi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 7th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Freed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">195 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Perioikoi</b> are the free people of the towns spread across Laconia and Messenia that made up the state of Lakedaimon alongside Sparta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They enjoyed a degree of local autonomy and their communities went on being thought of as poleis in their own right, yet they were politically dependent on the Spartans and could neither hold office in Sparta nor sit in its assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether those settlements were really city-states or only villages has been argued both ways, and with it whether their people belonged to the Spartan <i>polis</i> at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their communities were small, of 400 to 600 people, and looked much like domestic colonies scattered through both regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They farmed on their own behalf and on the kings', with whom they had special ties, and their towns had territories of their own: in that of Geronthrai, the modern Geraki, 13 routes have been identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Archaeologically they are very hard to tell apart from the helots, though the larger buildings and the more valuable offerings may be assumed to be theirs, and perioikoi in Messenia took part in the rebellion of the 460s BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus gives the Spartan account of them through the exiled king Demaratos: there is in Lakedaimon a city, Sparta, of about 8,000 men, and the other Lakedaimonians are not their equals, but they are good men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made the Spartans better, on that account, was their <i>nomos</i>, which let them fight together in a phalanx exclusively their own, and the claim legitimated their monopoly of privilege within the Lakedaimonian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How they actually fought is argued: one case has most of them in contingents of their own, another has them homogeneous with the Spartans and standing beside them in the phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The arrangement strained as full Spartan citizens grew fewer, and in the crisis of 370 to 369 BCE some refused call-up orders while whole communities went over to Sparta's enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"perioikoi","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"sources":["Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","D. Graham J. Shipley, review of <i>The Road-Network of Lakonike</i>, by Giannes A. Pikoulas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2014.06.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.06.16. [Open access]","James Roy, review of <i>The Ancient Messenians: Constructions of Ethnicity and Memory</i>, by Nino Luraghi, <i>Scripta Classica Israelica</i> 29 (2010), https://scriptaclassica.org/index.php/sci/article/view/3171. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Spartan_Territory_Before_371_BC.png","title":"perioikoi","desc":"Map of Sparta during the Classical era, before the Battle of Leuctra (371 BCE). The blue dots show the location of perioikoi cities. Sources: - D. Graham J. Shipley, \"Sparta and its perioikic neighbours: a century of reassessment\", Hermathena, 181, 2006, pp 51–82.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spartan_Territory_Before_371_BC.png","alt":"Spartan Territory Before 371 BCE"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why are they hard to distinguish archaeologically?","a":"Because they are very hard to tell apart from the helots, though the larger buildings and the more valuable offerings may be assumed to be theirs."},{"q":"Why does Demaratos's account serve Spartan interests?","a":"Because it makes the Spartans better by their nomos, which let them fight together in a phalanx exclusively their own, legitimating their monopoly of privilege."},{"q":"Why did the arrangement strain in the 4th century?","a":"Because full Spartan citizens grew fewer, and in the crisis of 370 to 369 BCE some refused call-up orders while whole communities went over to Sparta's enemies."}]},
{"id":"gr-241","num":241,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Full standing among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on monthly contributions of produce to the common messes, and a man who could not make them dropped into the <i>hypomeiones</i>.","questions":["Aristotle has the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fall from 10,000 in the 5th century to fewer than 1,000 in the 4th, though the territory could ostensibly have supported 30,000 infantry.","Land in Sparta could be alienated and passed with daughters inheriting alongside sons, so the equality of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ideology rather than fact in a plutocratic society."],"answer":"Spartiates","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8,000 in 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fallen to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,000 by 371 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartiates</b> are the citizens of Sparta itself, and the word is used narrowly of those who enjoyed full political rights, the <i>homoioi</i> or 'equals'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Used broadly it takes in anyone who belonged to the Spartan <i>polis</i> by birth, whatever rights he held: Xenophon calls the mercenary Drakontios a Spartiate although, exiled as a boy and never through the Spartan upbringing, he can hardly have been one of the equals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Full standing rested on the monthly contributions of produce to the common messes, and a man who could not make them dropped into the <i>hypomeiones</i>, the 'lesser men'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cowardice in battle and failure to pass through the upbringing were possibly other ways down, though the word itself is attested exactly once, in Xenophon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the rights amounted to was a voice: those in the assembly could speak and vote on what the elders put before them, and the final decision lay with the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Aristotle has them fall from 10,000 in the 5th century to fewer than 1,000 in the 4th, though the territory could ostensibly have supported 30,000 infantry and 1,500 cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He blamed whatever fragmented the landed estates, singling out widespread property ownership by women and policies that encouraged large families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The equality was ideology rather than fact, since land could be alienated and passed by partible inheritance with daughters inheriting alongside sons, and classical Sparta was a plutocratic society whose inequality of wealth undermined the very ideal it lived by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shortage told on the battlefield, and the Spartans drew back from a suicidal ideal once they saw they could not be profligate with the lives of soldiers they could not replace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even the kings were not strictly <i>homoioi</i>, calling themselves Achaeans rather than Dorians and standing outside the upbringing, and the myth of Sparta is slowly crumbling into a picture more fragmentary and complex than the old one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartiates","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","politics"],"sources":["Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Property and Wealth in Classical Sparta</i>, by Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.07.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.07.26/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah Pomeroy, review of <i>Sparta: Beyond the Mirage</i>, ed. Anton Powell and Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2003.08.06, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.08.06. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians%2C_excavation.jpg/1920px-Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians%2C_excavation.jpg","title":"Spartiates","desc":"The German excavation of the Tomb of the Lacedaemonians in the Kerameikos at Athens in 1914, the front of the monument cleared and photographed during the dig.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians,_excavation.jpg","alt":"Tomb of the Lacedaemonians, excavation"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why could a man lose his standing among the equals?","a":"Because full standing rested on the monthly contributions of produce to the common messes, and a man who could not make them dropped into the lesser men."},{"q":"Why was the equality ideology rather than fact?","a":"Because land could be alienated and passed by partible inheritance with daughters inheriting alongside sons, so inequality of wealth undermined the very ideal the city lived by."},{"q":"Why did the falling number of citizens change how Sparta fought?","a":"Because the Spartans drew back from a suicidal ideal once they saw they could not be profligate with the lives of soldiers they could not replace."}]},
{"id":"gr-242","num":242,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plato's <i>Laws</i> gives the first mention of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, listing it among the things that instilled endurance, the men going barefoot in winter and roving the countryside by night and by day.","questions":["Aristotle, preserved by Plutarch, has the magistrates send the most discreet young men out with daggers in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, to hide by day and by night kill every helot they caught.","Modern readings divide the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> three ways, as an initiation rite, as a policing service and as a military unit, and no consensus has been reached on what its name conceals."],"answer":"krypteia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lycurgus, c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Described by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Plato, 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>krypteia</b> is a Spartan institution whose name means 'hidden thing', and it is among those the ancient sources attribute to the lawgiver Lycurgus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word appears in no literature before the 4th century BCE, its first mention coming in Plato's <i>Laws</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There a Spartan speaker lists it among the things a lawgiver set up for war and for instilling <i>karteresis</i>, or 'endurance': the men go barefoot in winter, sleep without coverlets, keep no attendants and rove the whole countryside by night and by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of killing he says nothing at all, and he does not mention the helots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle, preserved by Plutarch, has the magistrates send out the most discreet of the young men with daggers and such supplies as were necessary, to hide by day and by night come down into the highways and kill every helot they caught, and sometimes to cross the fields and slay the sturdiest and best of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch adds that the ephors declared war on the helots each year so that killing them carried no impiety, and then refuses to believe Lycurgus capable of so abominable a measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern readings divide it three ways, as an initiation rite, as a policing service and as a military unit, and calling it a secret police imports an image of modern agencies the evidence will not carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even what was hidden is unsettled: the men themselves, or what they were doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its victims are the helots, whose status, treatment and revolt are the three points current scholarship turns on, and Sparta had unrest inside its own citizen body too: the one attested mention of its 'lesser men' comes in Xenophon's account of a conspiracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind all of it lies the evidence: Plato was an Athenian writing a legislative project rather than a description of Sparta, and what was credited to Lycurgus went on shifting for centuries after he was supposed to have lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"krypteia","tags":["practice","institution","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"sources":["Julie Rayanne Newman, “Krypteia and Xenelasia: Reassessing the Notion of Spartan Secrecy” (MA thesis, University of Calgary, 2021), https://prism.ucalgary.ca/bitstreams/f878c7ac-5275-42d4-85e9-d11e85a26046/download. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Helots and Their Masters in Laconia and Messenia: Histories, Ideologies, Structures</i>, ed. Nino Luraghi and Susan E. Alcock, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.06.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.06.26/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why do the two main sources describe different things?","a":"Because Plato's Spartan lists it among trainings for endurance and says nothing of killing or of helots, where Aristotle has young men sent out with daggers to kill every helot they caught."},{"q":"Why is calling it a secret police misleading?","a":"Because that imports an image of modern agencies the evidence will not carry, and modern readings divide it three ways — initiation rite, policing service, military unit."},{"q":"Why is Plato weak evidence for Spartan practice?","a":"Because he was an Athenian writing a legislative project rather than a description of Sparta, and what was credited to Lycurgus went on shifting for centuries."}]},
{"id":"gr-243","num":243,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch presents the Great Rhetra as a Delphic oracle received by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Diodorus has the Pythia deliver it to him directly.","questions":["Where other lawgivers got a hero cult at home, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was honoured as a hero or rather as a god, and like a colony's founder he received oracles from Delphi.","Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added the ephorate to the constitution he had inherited, so the ancients did not credit <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with quite everything."],"answer":"Lycurgus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tradition from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lycurgus</b> is the lawgiver to whom the Spartans credited their constitution and their whole way of living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where other lawgivers received a hero cult in their own cities, he was honoured as a hero or rather as a god, and like the founder of a colony he received oracles from Delphi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch presents the Great Rhetra as one of those oracles, received by Lycurgus, and Diodorus has the Pythia deliver it to him directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything Spartan was put down to him, down to the number of the elders: Plutarch cites Aristotle that thirty of the leading men set out with Lycurgus and two of them lost their nerve and withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the krypteia is his on Aristotle's authority, which Plutarch reports and then refuses to believe, judging Lycurgus by the mildness and justice he shows everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> His date was pushed back into the age of the Heracleidae, a placing that makes sense as a founding story rather than as a chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ancients did not credit him with quite everything: Plato made the ephorate a third stage under a third saver, and Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added it to the constitution he had inherited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern work sets the biography aside as unrecoverable and asks instead what the institutions attributed to him can be dated to, reading the constitution as a development over time rather than one man's single reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tradition is not a late invention, though: an independent and contemporaneous 7th-century tradition already ascribed the Rhetra's institutions to a Lycurgus, and those institutions preserve details of real change by the archaic period at the latest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What moved over the centuries was the man himself, whose character, laws and customs were redefined by additions and subtractions, so that reading him means reading the problems of the ancient material as much as the material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lycurgus","tags":["person","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]","Julie Rayanne Newman, “Krypteia and Xenelasia: Reassessing the Notion of Spartan Secrecy” (MA thesis, University of Calgary, 2021), https://prism.ucalgary.ca/bitstreams/f878c7ac-5275-42d4-85e9-d11e85a26046/download. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Statue_of_Lycurgus_of_Sparta%2C_at_the_Law_Courts_of_Brussels%2C_December_30%2C_2013.jpg/1920px-Statue_of_Lycurgus_of_Sparta%2C_at_the_Law_Courts_of_Brussels%2C_December_30%2C_2013.jpg","title":"Lycurgus","desc":"Statue of Lycurgus, Lawgiver of Sparta, at the Law Courts of Brussels, Belgium on December 30, 2013.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Lycurgus_of_Sparta,_at_the_Law_Courts_of_Brussels,_December_30,_2013.jpg","alt":"Statue of Lycurgus of Sparta, at the Law Courts of Brussels, December 30, 2013"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why was almost everything Spartan credited to him?","a":"Because he was honoured as a hero or rather as a god and received oracles from Delphi like a colony's founder, so the institutions were attached to him down to the number of the elders."},{"q":"Why is his date in the age of the Heracleidae telling?","a":"Because such a placing makes sense as a founding story rather than as a chronology."},{"q":"Why is the tradition nevertheless not a late invention?","a":"Because an independent and contemporaneous 7th-century tradition already ascribed the Rhetra's institutions to a Lycurgus, and those institutions preserve details of real change."}]},
{"id":"gr-244","num":244,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A shrine to Zeus Syllanius and a council of thirty including the kings are ordered by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves the main decision and power to the <i>damos</i>.","questions":["Plutarch takes the rider to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a later addition by the kings Polydorus and Theopompus, though it is probably part of the document from the start.","Dates proposed for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run across most of a century, from 699 to 675 BCE at one end to 650 to 600 at the other."],"answer":"Great Rhetra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed dates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">699 – 675 or 650 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Great Rhetra</b> is one of the earliest constitutional documents of archaic Greece, a charter of the Spartan state sanctioning its cults, the divisions of its citizens, its governing bodies and how it made decisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A <i>rhetra</i> is the Spartan word for an enactment or decree, and Sparta largely did without written legislation, so even this one may not have been written down before the late archaic period or the classical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It survives through Plutarch's <i>Life of Lycurgus</i> and through Diodorus, who quotes the verses Tyrtaeus made of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It orders a shrine to Zeus Syllanius and Athena Syllania, the division of the people into <i>phylai</i> and <i>obai</i>, and a council of thirty including the <i>archagetai</i>, by which the kings are meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assembly is then to be called from time to time between Babyca and Cnacion, proposals put there and withdrawn, and the main decision and power given to the <i>damos</i>, the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two provisions pull against that supremacy, the first being the council's power to introduce proposals and withdraw them, which sets the agenda before the people ever meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second is the rider: if the <i>damos</i> chose crookedly the elders and the kings could annul it, a clause Plutarch takes for a later addition by the kings Polydorus and Theopompus but which is probably original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle rules out the assembly ever having held a right to amend what was put to it, that being a mark of an advanced democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its date is argued across most of a century, from 699 to 675 BCE at one end to 650 to 600 at the other, and the setting with it: after the first war with Messenia, during the second, or after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On one reading it handed the hoplites the political power they were pressing for and so kept Sparta from tyranny; on another it belongs to a constitution that grew rather than one that was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Great Rhetra","tags":["text","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Noreen Humble, review of <i>The Spartans: The World of the Warrior-Heroes of Ancient Greece</i>, by Paul Cartledge, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.07.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.07.40/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why may so early a charter never have been written down?","a":"Because Sparta largely did without written legislation, so even this one may not have been put in writing before the late archaic period or the classical."},{"q":"Why do two provisions pull against the people's supremacy?","a":"Because the council could introduce proposals and withdraw them, setting the agenda before the people met, and the rider let the elders and kings annul a crooked decision."},{"q":"Why does Aristotle deny the assembly a right to amend?","a":"Because that would be a mark of an advanced democracy, which the archaic constitution was not."}]},
{"id":"gr-245","num":245,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Two hereditary kings ruled at once under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one from each royal house and constitutionally equal in authority, so that each was a check upon the other's power.","questions":["The kings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not strictly among the <i>homoioi</i>, identifying themselves as Achaeans rather than Dorians and not passing through the Spartan upbringing.","Under the Great Rhetra the two holders of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sat in the council of thirty as ordinary members, and Aristotle limited their importance to leading the army on campaign."],"answer":"Spartan dual kingship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In place by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 – 192 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Spartan dual kingship</b> gave the state two hereditary kings at once, one from each of two royal families, constitutionally equal in authority so that each was a check upon the other's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One house was traditionally the senior on Herodotus's telling, though that seniority carried no constitutional weight and neither king outranked the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Great Rhetra the kings sat in the council of thirty as two of its members, included with no special privileges, so that the charter of the state diminished their constitutional power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle limited their importance to the leadership of the army on campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they were not was ordinary Spartans: strictly speaking they were not among the <i>homoioi</i>, they identified themselves as Achaeans rather than Dorians, and they did not pass through the Spartan upbringing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They had special ties with the perioikoi, the free people of the outlying towns, who farmed on the kings' behalf as well as on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato made the double kingship the work of a god, setting it apart from the council, which he gave to a human being blended with divine power, and from the ephorate later still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A recent account calls the institution a divine dyarchy and traces it through Spartan history, its religious side bound up with the hierarchical structure of the society it stood at the head of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two kings meant two claims and two followings: Herodotus has the exiled king Demaratos advising Xerxes, and Cleomenes secured the accession of the second Laotychidas in 491 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two lines also carry the chronology of early Sparta, since dating it means counting reigns in the two kinglists, which leads at best to vague results and is the bane of dating by generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan dual kingship","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Manolis Pagkalos, review of <i>A Companion to Sparta</i>, ed. Anton Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2019.07.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.07.34/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why were two kings a check rather than a duplication?","a":"Because the two houses were constitutionally equal in authority, so each was a check upon the other's power, and neither outranked the other whatever the traditional seniority."},{"q":"Why did the Great Rhetra diminish the kings?","a":"Because it seated them in the council of thirty as two of its members, included with no special privileges."},{"q":"Why are the kinglists the bane of early Spartan chronology?","a":"Because dating that period means counting reigns in the two lists, which leads at best to vague results."}]},
{"id":"gr-246","num":246,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Tradition has the twin sons of Aristodemus, Eurysthenes and Procles, becoming Sparta's first co-rulers, though the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> take their names from Agis and Eurypon instead.","questions":["Both of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> claimed descent from Heracles through Hyllus, who had been adopted by Aegimius, the eponym of the Dorians.","Dating early Sparta means counting reigns in the lists of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Pausanias's genealogy knew only one Laotychidas where the line in fact held two."],"answer":"Agiads and Eurypontids","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In place by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 – 192 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Agiads and Eurypontids</b> are the two royal houses of Sparta, each supplying one of the two kings who ruled together, so that the succession ran down two lines at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tradition has Aristodemus, the grandson of Heracles' son Hyllus, conquering Laconia and dying, after which his twin sons Eurysthenes and Procles became Sparta's first co-rulers and the double kingship began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The houses are not named for those twins, though, but for Agis and Eurypon, mythical kings standing further down each line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the two the Agiads were traditionally the senior on Herodotus's telling, a precedence that carried no constitutional weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both lines claimed the same ancestry, and Hyllus himself had been adopted by Aegimius, the eponym of the Dorians, which tied the Heraclid claim to the Dorian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two lists are how early Sparta gets dated at all: Theopompus stands four reigns above the first Laotychidas in the Eurypontid line, and the Agiad line yields similar results.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antiquity got the lists wrong as well, since Pausanias's genealogy of the Spartan kings knew only one Laotychidas where the Eurypontid line held two, four generations apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the lists preserve is argued: the institutions credited to the tradition seem to keep details of real change by the archaic period, even where the biographies attached to them cannot be recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The claim underneath was a construction rather than a record, and it bought three things: hereditary right to the kingship, the priestly functions that went with it, and a title to dominion beyond Sparta's borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both houses also set themselves a little apart from the people they ruled, calling themselves Achaeans rather than Dorians and standing outside the upbringing every other Spartan went through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Agiads and Eurypontids","tags":["people","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Alexey L. Darvin, “Origin of Spartan Kings from Heracles,” <i>RUDN Journal of World History</i> 10, no. 3 (2018): 237–49, https://doi.org/10.22363/2312-8127-2018-10-3-237-249. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","David W. Madsen, review of <i>Spartans: A New History</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.08.10, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.10/. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_%28Jacques-Louis_David%29.PNG","title":"Agiads and Eurypontids","desc":"Léonidas aux Thermopyles (Jacques-Louis David). Jacques-Louis David.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9onidas_aux_Thermopyles_(Jacques-Louis_David).PNG","alt":"Léonidas aux Thermopyles (Jacques-Louis David)"},"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why are the houses not named for the first pair of kings?","a":"Because they take their names from Agis and Eurypon, mythical kings standing further down each line, rather than from the twins Eurysthenes and Procles."},{"q":"Why did the shared ancestry claim buy so much?","a":"Because it secured hereditary right to the kingship, the priestly functions that went with it, and a title to dominion beyond Sparta's borders."},{"q":"Why did antiquity get the lists wrong?","a":"Because Pausanias's genealogy knew only one Laotychidas where the Eurypontid line held two, four generations apart."}]},
{"id":"gr-247","num":247,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Sparta's council of elders, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was thirty strong, 28 <i>gerontes</i> with the two kings making up the number, and a man had to be past sixty to be chosen.","questions":["Candidates for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed through the assembly one by one in silence while judges shut in a nearby room marked each shout by its loudness without knowing whose it was.","Aristotle called the way men were elected to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> childish, and objected as well that a candidate had to put himself forward, a rule fitter for the ambitious than the virtuous."],"answer":"gerousia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">From the Rhetra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Members</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30, with the two kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60 and over</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>gerousia</b> is Sparta's council of elders, thirty strong, 28 <i>gerontes</i> with the two kings making up the number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A <i>geron</i> had to be past sixty when he was chosen, and he held the office for the rest of his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the Great Rhetra a council of powerful aristocrats had advised the kings by custom rather than by institution, and the Rhetra put it on a new basis, integrated with the kings and answering the aspirations of the leading families, which is real archaic change whatever is made of the lawgiver credited with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its power was to prepare: it introduced proposals and withdrew them, so that the assembly could approve what the council put to it but could promote no law of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a court as well, the only Spartan one that could pronounce death or exile, and it heard the serious cases, homicide among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Election was by shouting: the people were called together, judges shut in a room where they could neither see nor be seen but could hear, and the candidates passed through one by one in an order drawn by lot, in silence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The judges marked each shout by its loudness without knowing whose it was, only that it came first, second, third, and the man with the most and loudest acclamations was declared elected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle called the procedure childish, and objected as well that a man had to put himself forward, a rule fitter for the ambitious than for the virtuous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has since been read as designed for impartiality rather than as primitive, the lot securing spontaneity and the sealed judges anonymity, and as an early form of approval voting, since a voter could acclaim as many candidates as he liked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who could stand was another matter, since it was probably only aristocrats, and rich Spartiates used their wealth to build patronal relationships that reached into the nominally equal dealings of the <i>homoioi</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"gerousia","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Charles Girard, “Acclamation Voting in Sparta: An Early Use of Approval Voting,” in <i>Handbook on Approval Voting</i>, ed. Jean-François Laslier and M. Remzi Sanver (Heidelberg: Springer, 2010), 15–18, https://hal.science/hal-03005701v1/document. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Ellen Millender, review of <i>Property and Wealth in Classical Sparta</i>, by Stephen Hodkinson, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2001.07.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2001/2001.07.26/. [Open access]","Stephanie Larson, review of <i>Ancient Tradition and Early Greek History: The Origins of States in Early-Archaic Sparta, Argos and Corinth</i>, by Mait Koiv, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2004.06.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.06.08. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was the council's power to prepare business so great?","a":"Because it introduced proposals and withdrew them, so the assembly could approve what was put to it but could promote no law of its own."},{"q":"Why did Aristotle call the election procedure childish?","a":"Because a candidate was chosen by which man drew the loudest shouting, and because a man had to put himself forward, a rule fitter for the ambitious than for the virtuous."},{"q":"Why has that procedure since been defended?","a":"Because it has been read as designed for impartiality — the lot securing spontaneity and the sealed judges anonymity — and as an early form of approval voting."}]},
{"id":"gr-248","num":248,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could fine whom they chose and enact immediate payment, deprive magistrates of office, imprison them and prefer a capital charge against them.","questions":["Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added the office of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the constitution he had inherited, and from the middle of the 4th century that view prevailed.","It was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who dealt with Kinadon's conspiracy, choosing not to arrest him inside Sparta and hand-picking six or seven of the <i>hippeis</i> to take him."],"answer":"ephors","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">By tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 754 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 a year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abolished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">227 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>ephors</b> are the magistrates who oversaw the Spartan state, and the sources put the beginning of the office at very different points in the city's history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato made it a third stage, after the double kingship given by a god and the council given by a human being blended with divine power, and ascribed it to a third saver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle said outright that King Theopompus added the ephorate to the constitution he had inherited, and from the middle of the 4th century that view prevailed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One story has them appointed to run the state while the kings were away at war, and Tyrtaeus, who gives a fair summary of the constitution of his own day, knows nothing of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A modern account puts the office later still, in the second half of the 6th century BCE and out of the needs of Sparta's league, with the social origin of the ephors in well-placed aristocratic families rather than in the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon has the powerful citizens setting the office up themselves, having concluded that obedience is a very great blessing whether in a state, an army or a household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they got was power: the ephors could fine whom they chose and enact immediate payment, deprive magistrates of office, imprison them and prefer a capital charge against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They did not leave men elected to office to rule as they liked through the year, but punished a lawbreaker the moment they saw one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They could set business before the assembly as well, bringing a motion without the council having to agree among itself, so that the preparing of business was divided between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The executive work was theirs too: it was the ephors who dealt with Kinadon's conspiracy, choosing not to arrest him inside Sparta and hand-picking six or seven of the <i>hippeis</i> to take him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"ephors","tags":["institution","people","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</i> 8.3–4, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, trans. E. C. Marchant and G. W. Bowersock (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0210:text=Const.%20Lac.:chapter=8. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Victor Parker, “The Dates of the Messenian Wars,” <i>Chiron</i> 21 (1991): 25–48, https://publications.dainst.org/journals/chiron/1125/5492. [Open access]","Cameron Hawkins, “Spartans and Perioikoi: The Organization and Ideology of the Lakedaimonian Army in the Fourth Century B.C.E.,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51 (2011): 401–34, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12941. [Open access]","Alberto Esu, “Divided Power and Εὐνομία: Deliberative Procedures in Ancient Sparta,” <i>The Classical Quarterly</i> 67, no. 2 (2017): 353–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0009838817000544. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Sparta_Ephoren_%28WMH_11-1861-62_S_48_LLoeffler%29.jpg","title":"ephors","desc":"A 19th-century engraving imagining the five ephors of Sparta seated together in council, each with a staff.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sparta_Ephoren_(WMH_11-1861-62_S_48_LLoeffler).jpg","alt":"Sparta Ephoren (WMH 11-1861-62 S 48 LLoeffler)"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why do the sources disagree about when the office began?","a":"Because Plato made it a third stage under a third saver, Aristotle said King Theopompus added it to an inherited constitution, and Tyrtaeus, summarising the constitution of his own day, knows nothing of them."},{"q":"Why was the office so powerful in practice?","a":"Because the ephors could fine whom they chose and enact immediate payment, deprive magistrates of office, imprison them and prefer a capital charge."},{"q":"Why did they not wait out a magistrate's year?","a":"Because they did not leave men elected to office to rule as they liked, but punished a lawbreaker the moment they saw one."}]},
{"id":"gr-249","num":249,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch says the verb <i>apellazein</i> means to assemble the people, with a reference to Apollo, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met between Babyca and Cnacion with neither halls nor any other building.","questions":["When the multitude of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was assembled no one of them was permitted to make a motion, and what the elders and kings laid before them could only be accepted or rejected.","Even the name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is doubtful, since no ancient writer but Plutarch uses it, and the word goes back to Apollo, Apellon in the Doric dialect."],"answer":"apella","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>apella</b> is the assembly of the Spartan citizens, and even the name is doubtful, since no ancient writer but Plutarch uses it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took it in the plural, and the word goes back to Apollo, Apellon in the Doric dialect, whom Plutarch calls the source and author of the polity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Rhetra's verb <i>apellazein</i> means to assemble the people, and they assembled between Babyca and Cnacion, a bridge and a river on Aristotle's reading of the names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They met in the open, with neither halls nor any other kind of building for the purpose, because Lycurgus thought good counsel was not promoted by such things but discouraged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The serious purposes of an assembly were rendered foolish and futile, on that account, by men gazing at statues and paintings, scenic embellishments and the extravagantly decorated roofs of council halls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How often it met is not recoverable: the Rhetra says only from time to time, a phrase read as from season to season and as once a year, a period or a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its principal innovation was to be institutionalised at all, since its meetings no longer depended on the will of the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the multitude was assembled no one of them was permitted to make a motion, and what the elders and the kings laid before them could be accepted or rejected and nothing more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One reading has it enacting or rejecting what the council and the ephors put to it without deliberating on anything left open, so that it could promote no law of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It decided by shouting, as it decided most affairs of moment, which is how a body that could put nothing forward still had the last word on what was put to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"apella","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 6, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0047:chapter=6. [Open access]","Mary Fragkaki, “The Great Rhetra,” <i>Rosetta</i> 17 (2015): 35–51, https://rosetta.bham.ac.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Fragkaki_Great-Rhetra_Rosetta17.pdf. [Open access]","Adolfo Dominquez, review of <i>Lakedaimonion Politeia: Die Entstehung der spartanischen Verfassung</i>, by Lukas Thommen, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 98.2.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1998/1998.02.12/. [Open access]","Charles Girard, “Acclamation Voting in Sparta: An Early Use of Approval Voting,” in <i>Handbook on Approval Voting</i>, ed. Jean-François Laslier and M. Remzi Sanver (Heidelberg: Springer, 2010), 15–18, https://hal.science/hal-03005701v1/document. [Open access]","Alberto Esu, “Divided Power and Εὐνομία: Deliberative Procedures in Ancient Sparta,” <i>The Classical Quarterly</i> 67, no. 2 (2017): 353–73, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0009838817000544. [Paywalled]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why is even the name of the assembly doubtful?","a":"Because no ancient writer but Plutarch uses it, and he takes it in the plural, deriving it from Apollo, Apellon in the Doric dialect."},{"q":"Why did it meet in the open?","a":"Because good counsel was held not to be promoted by halls but discouraged, serious purposes being rendered futile by men gazing at statues, paintings and decorated roofs."},{"q":"Why is its principal innovation that it existed at all?","a":"Because being institutionalised meant its meetings no longer depended on the will of the kings."}]},
{"id":"gr-250","num":250,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Instead of leaving each father to appoint a slave as tutor, Lycurgus gave the boys of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a Warden with a staff of youths carrying whips.","questions":["Plutarch has boys taken by the state at seven and enrolled in the companies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the one best in judgement and bravest in fighting was made captain.","Over each company of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood an eiren, who after supper asked who was the best man in the city and punished a faulty answer with a bite on the thumb."],"answer":"agoge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lycurgus, c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Boys entered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at 7</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at 30</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>agoge</b> is the public upbringing that took a Spartan boy out of his father's hands and put him in the state's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon says that instead of leaving each father to appoint a slave as tutor, Lycurgus gave charge of the boys to a member of the class from which the highest offices are filled, and gave him a staff of youths carrying whips.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead of softening their feet with sandals the boys went unshod, and instead of changes of clothing they wore one garment through the year, to be readier for heat and cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their food was kept moderate enough that they would never suffer repletion and would know what it was to go with their hunger unsatisfied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were allowed to relieve that hunger by stealing, and a boy who was caught was beaten with many stripes, since men punish a learner for not carrying out properly what he is taught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch has them taken by the state at seven and enrolled in companies, where the one who excelled in judgement and was most courageous in fighting was made captain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of reading and writing they learned only enough to serve their turn, and all the rest was calculated to make them obey commands well, endure hardships and conquer in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over each company stood an eiren, two years out of the class of boys, who after supper asked who was the best man in the city and punished a faulty answer with a bite on the thumb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says he had seen youths expiring under the lash at the altar of Artemis Orthia, where a priestess stood holding a wooden image that grew heavy if the scourgers spared a lad for his beauty or rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The word itself belongs to the Hellenistic and Roman periods and is not used before the middle of the 3rd century BCE, so how far what he describes continued the classical system is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"agoge","tags":["practice","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</i> 2, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, trans. E. C. Marchant and G. W. Bowersock (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0210:text=Const.%20Lac.:chapter=2. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 16–18, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0047:chapter=16. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.16.10–11, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=16. [Open access]","Edmond Lévy, “Remarques préliminaires sur l'éducation spartiate,” <i>Ktèma</i> 22 (1997): 151–60, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ktema_0221-5896_1997_num_22_1_2189. [Open access]","Antony Keen, review of <i>The Gymnasium of Virtue: Education and Culture in Ancient Sparta</i>, by Nigel M. Kennell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 96.9.28, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1996/1996.09.28. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why were the boys allowed to steal food?","a":"Because their rations were kept short enough that they would know hunger, and a boy caught stealing was beaten for not carrying out properly what he had been taught."},{"q":"Why did they learn so little reading and writing?","a":"Because they learned only enough to serve their turn, all the rest being calculated to make them obey commands well, endure hardships and conquer in battle."},{"q":"Why is it disputed how far Plutarch describes the classical system?","a":"Because the word itself belongs to the Hellenistic and Roman periods and is not used before the middle of the 3rd century BCE."}]},
{"id":"wh-110","num":110,"category":"Peopling the planet","question":"More than 700 natural shelters weathered out of the Vindhyan hills of central India make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, over 400 of them painted and their floors full of stone tools.","questions":["A single shelter among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can carry five layers of painting one over another, and 16 layers have been told apart across the whole site.","The last painting done in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a conch-shell script of the 6th and 7th centuries, which always overlies the older pictures and is never overlain by them."],"answer":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100,000 years ago onwards</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mesolithic to c. 7th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shelters</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 700, over 400 painted</span></div>","answerText":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Bhimbetka rock shelters</b> are hollows and caves weathered out of the Vindhyan hills of Madhya Pradesh, in central India, about 45 kilometres (28 miles) south-east of Bhopal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They run along a stretch some 10 kilometres (6 miles) long and 3 wide, and number more than 700, of which over 400 carry paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rock is sandstone, weathering easily under heat, rain and wind, and several hundred million years of that opened shelters from small niches to large caves, in forest fed by perennial springs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their floors hold stone tools from the Lower Palaeolithic on: handaxes and cleavers, then smaller Middle Palaeolithic scrapers, then Upper Palaeolithic blades and burins, and finally a Mesolithic industry that drops quartzite for chalcedony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One shelter's deposit runs nearly four metres (13 feet) deep through all four of those stages, and the site's Acheulean bifaces are unusually refined, its cleavers strikingly symmetrical in section.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> At one end of the Auditorium shelter cup-shaped depressions have been hammered into the rock, some beneath Acheulean levels and dated to nearly 100,000 years, which would make them among the earliest anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of the paintings are red or white, the colour taken from haematite nodules that turn up in the habitation deposits, many with facets ground smooth where pigment was worked off them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Green, red and white colouring stones came out of the Upper Palaeolithic and Mesolithic layers and green out of none later, which is why green figures are read as the oldest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later painters worked over earlier work, so one shelter may show five layers and 16 have been told apart across the site, the topmost a conch-shell script of the 6th and 7th centuries that nothing overlies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wild animals dominate the older pictures, the ceiling of Zoo Rock crowded with them, alongside hunts, dances with drummers and masked men, while the latest phases turn instead to horse and elephant riders and battle scenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Archaeological Survey of India, “Prehistoric Rock Shelters of Bhimbetka (2003), Madhya Pradesh,” accessed August 10, 2026, https://asi.nic.in/pages/WorldHeritageBhimbetka. [Open access]","V. N. Misra, “The Prehistoric Rock Art of Bhimbetka, Central India,” <i>NCPA Quarterly Journal</i> 10, no. 1 (1981), https://www.sahapedia.org/sites/default/files/The%20Prehistoric%20Rock%20Art%20of%20Bhimbetka,%20Central%20India.pdf. [Open access]","Meenakshi Dubey-Pathak, “The Rock Art of the Bhimbetka Area in India,” <i>Adoranten</i> (2014): 5–22, https://www.rockartscandinavia.com/images/articles/a14pathak.pdf. [Open access]","Meenakshi Dubey Pathak, “Green Images in Indian Rock Art,” <i>Arts</i> 14, no. 2 (2025): 36, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts14020036. [Open access]","Ceri Shipton, “Hierarchical Organization in the Acheulean to Middle Palaeolithic Transition at Bhimbetka, India,” <i>Cambridge Archaeological Journal</i> 26, no. 4 (2016): 601–18, https://doi.org/10.1017/S095977431600041X. [Paywalled]"],"tags":["place","art","archaeology","prehistory","india"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Rock_Shelter_8%2C_Bhimbetka_02.jpg/1920px-Rock_Shelter_8%2C_Bhimbetka_02.jpg","title":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","desc":"Paintings in Rock Shelter 8, Bhimbetka, India. Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_Shelter_8,_Bhimbetka_02.jpg","alt":"Rock Shelter 8, Bhimbetka"},"locator":{"name":"Bhimbetka rock shelters","at":[77.6131,22.9384]},"why":[{"q":"Why did so many shelters form in this one range?","a":"Because the rock is sandstone, weathering easily under heat, rain and wind, and several hundred million years of that opened more than 700 hollows and caves in forest fed by perennial springs."},{"q":"Why are green figures read as the oldest paintings?","a":"Because green, red and white colouring stones came out of the Upper Palaeolithic and Mesolithic layers and green out of none later — so the pigment itself dates the phase."},{"q":"Why can one shelter show sixteen phases of painting?","a":"Because later painters worked over earlier work rather than choosing fresh rock, so the layers stack: one shelter may show five, and the topmost across the site is a conch-shell script of the 6th and 7th centuries that nothing overlies."}]},
{"id":"wh-121","num":121,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Grey wolves became the first domestic animal well before any crop was sown, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the only one to predate farming altogether.","questions":["Canids from Goyet, Předmostí and Razboinichya Cave were all published as evidence of early <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until nuclear genomes showed each of them to be a now-extinct wolf.","By 14,300 years ago one genetically uniform dog population had spread across Europe and Anatolia, so whatever <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as, the animals were soon passing between unrelated peoples."],"answer":"dog domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest dogs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 17,000 – 14,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest dog DNA</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,200 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lineages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">five by c. 11,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"dog domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Dog domestication</b> made grey wolves into the first domestic animal, and the only one taken up before farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it happened, when, and which people were involved are all still unknown, and published estimates of the date have ranged from 135,000 to 15,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest bones that look like a dog's are European, from 17,000 to 14,000 years ago, but younger candidates turn up in East Asia, the Levant and the Americas, so the archaeology points to no one region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bone shape alone has misled repeatedly: canids from Goyet, Předmostí, Razboinichya Cave and Eliseevichi were each published as an early dog, and nuclear genomes have since shown every one of them to be a now-extinct wolf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clearest early case is a jaw of about 14,300 years ago from Bonn-Oberkassel in Germany, buried with two people and carrying damage the animal could not have survived without long human care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest dog DNA yet recovered is from a 14,200-year-old animal at Kesslerloch in Switzerland, and it already shares ancestry with dogs worldwide, so Europe's Palaeolithic dogs were not a separate line that failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One genetically uniform dog population was spread across Europe and Anatolia by then, among Magdalenian, Epigravettian and Anatolian hunter-gatherers alike, so the animals were passing between human groups quite unlike one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 11,000 years ago at least five major lineages had diverged, and all dogs share an ancestry distinct from every living wolf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes from 72 wolves spanning 100,000 years put the main source in eastern Eurasia, while dogs in the Near East and Africa draw up to half their ancestry from a second, southwest Eurasian source that no sampled wolf matches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gene flow afterwards ran mostly the other way, from dogs into wolves, though almost two-thirds of breed dogs carry a low but detectable wolf ancestry acquired roughly a thousand generations ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Anders Bergström et al., “Genomic History of Early Dogs in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 651, no. 8107 (2026): 986–94, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10112-7. [Open access]","William A. Marsh et al., “Dogs Were Widely Distributed across Western Eurasia during the Palaeolithic,” <i>Nature</i> 651, no. 8107 (2026): 995–1003, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10170-x. [Open access]","Anders Bergström et al., “Grey Wolf Genomic History Reveals a Dual Ancestry of Dogs,” <i>Nature</i> 607, no. 7918 (2022): 313–20, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-04824-9. [Open access]","Anders Bergström et al., “Origins and Genetic Legacy of Prehistoric Dogs,” <i>Science</i> 370, no. 6516 (2020): 557–64, https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/168236/1/aba9572_revision2_1_.pdf. [Open access]","Audrey T. Lin et al., “A Legacy of Genetic Entanglement with Wolves Shapes Modern Dogs,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 48 (2025): e2421768122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2421768122. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","evolution","genetics","archaeology","nature"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Hund_Doppelgrab_Oberkassel.jpg/1920px-Hund_Doppelgrab_Oberkassel.jpg","title":"Dog domestication","desc":"Jaw, maxilla fragment and teeth of the dog from the Bonn-Oberkassel double grave. Thilo Parg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hund_Doppelgrab_Oberkassel.jpg","alt":"Jaw, maxilla fragment and teeth of the dog from the Bonn-Oberkassel double grave"},"why":[{"q":"Why has bone shape repeatedly misled the search for early dogs?","a":"Because canids from Goyet, Předmostí, Razboinichya Cave and Eliseevichi were each published as an early dog, and nuclear genomes have since shown every one of them to be a now-extinct wolf."},{"q":"Why is the Bonn-Oberkassel jaw the clearest early case?","a":"Because the animal was buried with two people and carries damage it could not have survived without long human care, which is evidence of a relationship rather than of a skeleton's shape."},{"q":"Why do genomes suggest more than one wolf source?","a":"Because ancient genomes from 72 wolves put the main source in eastern Eurasia, while dogs in the Near East and Africa draw up to half their ancestry from a second, southwest Eurasian source no sampled wolf matches."}]},
{"id":"wh-122","num":122,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Farming and settled villages were both well established in Southwest Asia before anyone made a pot, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named for.","questions":["Round houses give way to square ones part way through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a change so consistent that squared building is treated as the trademark of its second phase.","Skulls detached from the body and modelled over in plaster are a mortuary practice of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, known from Jericho, 'Ain Ghazal and other sites across the Levant."],"answer":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">PPNA</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 11,700 – 10,500 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">PPNB</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,500 – 8,250 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Pre-Pottery Neolithic</b> is the first part of the Neolithic in Southwest Asia, the stretch in which people took up farming and settled villages before anyone had made pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It follows the Natufian and falls into two, the PPNA of roughly 11,700 to 10,500 years ago and the PPNB of roughly 10,500 to 8,250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two were first distinguished at Jericho, and what separates them is a change in stone tools, in house building and in how people fed themselves, the PPNB being marked by its naviform blade cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no clear evidence of domestic animals in the PPNA, while sheep and goats are plainly domesticated in the levels above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its people stayed put: of 44 individuals sampled at Jericho, only two had grown up anywhere else, which is a settled community rather than one built by migration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The most visible change is architectural, the round buildings of the Natufian and the PPNA giving way to square ones, which is the trademark of the PPNB.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not abrupt, since at Jerf el Ahmar round and square houses stand in the same levels and some are square with rounded corners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later PPNB settlements swelled into mega-sites, mobility fell as people leaned harder on domesticated food, and obsidian and other exotic materials travelled long distances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A mortuary practice runs through the PPNB in which skulls were detached from the body, modelled over in plaster and kept, known from Jericho, Tell Ramad, Beisamoun, Kfar HaHoresh, Tell Aswad, 'Ain Ghazal and Nahal Hemar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its reach ran well past the Fertile Crescent, since tools of the kinds that define the Natufian and the Pre-Pottery Neolithic turn up at Sahout in the northern Arabian desert between 13,500 and 8,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Ceri Shipton et al., “The Natufian Epipalaeolithic and Pre-Pottery Neolithic in the Desert of Northern Arabia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 16842, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-40541-3. [Open access]","Andrea L. Balbo et al., “Squaring the Circle: Social and Environmental Implications of Pre-Pottery Neolithic Building Technology at Tell Qarassa (South Syria),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 7 (2012): e42109, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0042109. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Plastered Skulls from the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B Site of Yiftahel (Israel): A Computed Tomography-Based Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): e89242, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0089242. [Open access]","Xiaoran Wang et al., “Isotopic and Proteomic Evidence for Communal Stability at Pre-Pottery Neolithic Jericho in the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 16360, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-43549-1. [Open access]","Xiaoran Wang et al., “Isotopic and DNA Analyses Reveal Multiscale PPNB Mobility and Migration across Southeastern Anatolia and the Southern Levant,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2210611120, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2210611120. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","agriculture","practice","history"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Plastered_skull._Such_skulls_were_buried_in_houses_and_probably_represent_ancestral_cults._Pre-Pottery_Neolithic_B%2C_8800-6900_BCE._From_Jericho%2C_in_modern-day_Palestinian_Territories._The_Jordan_Museum%2C_Amman.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Pre-Pottery Neolithic","desc":"Plastered skull. Such skulls were buried in houses and probably represent ancestral cults. Pre-Pottery Neolithic B, 8800-6900 BCE. From Jericho, in modern-day Palestinian Territories. The Jordan Museum, Amman, Jordan. Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plastered_skull._Such_skulls_were_buried_in_houses_and_probably_represent_ancestral_cults._Pre-Pottery_Neolithic_B,_8800-6900_BCE._From_Jericho,_in_modern-day_Palestinian_Territories._The_Jordan_Museum,_Amman.jpg","alt":"Plastered skull from Jericho, Jordan Museum"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the period named for the absence of pottery?","a":"Because it is the stretch in which people took up farming and settled villages before anyone had made pottery, so the missing craft is what marks the first part of the Neolithic in Southwest Asia off from what follows."},{"q":"Why are the PPNA and PPNB distinguished from one another?","a":"Because stone tools, house building and how people fed themselves all change between them: the PPNB is marked by its naviform blade cores, by square houses, and by sheep and goats that are plainly domesticated where the PPNA shows none."},{"q":"Why is the shift from round to square houses called gradual?","a":"Because at Jerf el Ahmar round and square houses stand in the same levels, and some are square with rounded corners, so the trademark of the PPNB arrives by degrees rather than at a stroke."}]},
{"id":"wh-123","num":123,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Rings of T-shaped limestone pillars carved with foxes, boars and cranes were raised at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 10th millennium BCE, when nearly everyone alive still hunted and gathered.","questions":["More than 7,000 grinding slabs, handstones and mortars have now been analysed at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is hard to square with the old view that nobody lived there.","The two central pillars of Enclosure D at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stand 5.5 metres (18 feet) tall and wear carved belts with fox-pelt loincloths, so the T is a human figure."],"answer":"Göbekli Tepe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9600 – 8800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Göbekli Tepe","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Göbekli Tepe</b> is a Pre-Pottery Neolithic mound on a bare limestone ridge in south-eastern Turkey, one of a cluster of sites around Şanlıurfa where T-shaped stone pillars have been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits about 15 kilometres (9 miles) north-east of that city, its deposits up to 15 metres (49 feet) deep over some nine hectares (22 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest Neolithic horizon dates to roughly 9600 to 8800 BCE, and to it belong all the major circular buildings, from 10 to 30 metres (33 to 98 feet) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each is ringed by ten or twelve T-shaped limestone pillars set into walls of quarry stone, with a taller pair at the centre, and the two in Enclosure D reach 5.5 metres (18 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The T is read as a human head, since several pillars carry arms and hands in relief and the central pair in Enclosure D wear belts with buckles and a loincloth of fox pelts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Reliefs across the pillars show foxes, boars, bulls, cranes, ducks, gazelles, snakes, spiders and scorpions, all of them wild and, where they can be sexed, male.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those images were not fixed once carved, since pillars in Building F were erased and re-worked repeatedly, several losing more than 10 centimetres (4 inches) of surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The excavator found no residential buildings and read the place as a group of sanctuaries where scattered communities gathered for rites, a reading that has since been qualified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation has turned up smaller rectangular buildings raised around the monumental ones, partly at the same time and partly afterwards, and more than 7,000 grinding slabs, handstones, mortars and pestles, a concentration unusual for the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seasonal peaks in that food processing are read as large work feasts, and how many people could have been permanently present is limited by water, since no spring is known nearby and the cisterns found so far hold 153 cubic metres (5,400 cubic feet) between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Laura Dietrich et al., “Cereal Processing at Early Neolithic Göbekli Tepe, Southeastern Turkey,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 5 (2019): e0215214, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0215214. [Open access]","Klaus Schmidt, “Göbekli Tepe – the Stone Age Sanctuaries: New Results of Ongoing Excavations with a Special Focus on Sculptures and High Reliefs,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 37 (2010): 239–56, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.37.21. [Open access]","Christopher Claudio Caletti, “Göbekli Tepe and the Sites around the Urfa Plain (SE Turkey): Recent Discoveries and New Interpretations,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica</i> 2 (2020): 95–123, https://doi.org/10.13128/asiana-679. [Open access]","Oliver Dietrich and Julia Wagner, “Early Neolithic Imagery in Flux: A Case Study on the Reliefs of Building F at Göbekli Tepe,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 50 (2023): 222–36, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.50.11. [Open access]","Bahattin Çelik, “New Neolithic Cult Centres and Domestic Settlements in the Light of Urfa Region Surveys,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 42 (2015): 353–64, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.42.25. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","religion","turkey","history"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe%2C_Urfa.jpg/1920px-G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe%2C_Urfa.jpg","title":"Göbekli Tepe","desc":"Excavated circular enclosures ringed with T-shaped stone pillars on the mound at Göbekli Tepe. Teomancimit, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe,_Urfa.jpg","alt":"Excavated circular enclosures ringed with T-shaped stone pillars on the mound at Göbekli Tepe"},"locator":{"name":"Göbekli Tepe","at":[38.9217,37.2236]},"why":[{"q":"Why are the T-shaped pillars read as human figures?","a":"Because several carry arms and hands in relief, and the central pair in Enclosure D wear belts with buckles and a loincloth of fox pelts, so the T reads as a head above a body."},{"q":"Why has the reading of the site as pure sanctuary been qualified?","a":"Because excavation has since found smaller rectangular buildings raised around the monumental ones, partly at the same time, along with more than 7,000 grinding slabs, handstones, mortars and pestles."},{"q":"Why could only a limited number of people have lived there permanently?","a":"Because water constrains it: no spring is known nearby, and the cisterns found so far hold 153 cubic metres between them, which is why the seasonal peaks in food processing are read as work feasts."}]},
{"id":"wh-124","num":124,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"A stone tower over 8 metres (26 feet) high with a staircase built inside it was raised at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 8300 BCE, beside a wall about half its height.","questions":["Strontium in the teeth of 44 Neolithic dead from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found only two who had grown up elsewhere, which is a community that stayed put rather than one built by migration.","Ten skulls with faces modelled over them in plaster and shells set in for eyes have come out of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where occupation runs from the Natufian to the Ottomans."],"answer":"Jericho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 10,500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tower</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze Age fall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1520 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Jericho","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Jericho</b> is the settlement at Tell es-Sultan in the rift valley north of the Dead Sea, and it records more than 10,000 years of occupation, from the Late Natufian to the Ottoman period, with no clear break in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Pre-Pottery Neolithic levels are among the best known anywhere, and the terms PPNA and PPNB were themselves first defined from its stratigraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What sets it apart from other sites of its day in the southern Levant is a stone tower over 8 metres (26 feet) high and a wall beside it, both raised around 8300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tower is conical, built of undressed stone, plastered inside and out, and holds a staircase within it, the wall standing about half its height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been read as a fortification, as flood defence, as a ritual centre and as a claim on territory, and none of those readings has settled the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Strontium in the teeth of 44 of its Neolithic dead identifies only two who grew up elsewhere, so the place was held by a community that stayed put rather than one built by incomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the earlier phase its people buried skulls apart from their bodies, grouped together and unadorned, and in the later one they began modelling faces over them in plaster, with shells set in for eyes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ten plastered skulls are known from the site in all, three of them lifted from a single square on the western side of the mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town went on being rebuilt for thousands of years afterwards, and the walled Bronze Age city was burnt at the end of the Middle Bronze Age, radiocarbon on charred grain from that fire giving a date between about 1600 and 1520 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those measurements also rule out a destruction as late as about 1400 BCE, a date that had been proposed for the end of the fortified city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Xiaoran Wang et al., “Isotopic and Proteomic Evidence for Communal Stability at Pre-Pottery Neolithic Jericho in the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 16360, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-43549-1. [Open access]","Roy Liran and Ran Barkai, “Casting a Shadow on Neolithic Jericho,” <i>Antiquity Project Gallery</i> 85, no. 327 (2011): 1–5, https://doi.org/10.15184/S2978855200902282. [Open access]","Eugen Strouhal, “Five Plastered Skulls from Pre-Pottery Neolithic B Jericho: Anthropological Study,” <i>Paléorient</i> 1, no. 2 (1973): 231–47, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1973.4169. [Open access]","Viviane Slon et al., “The Plastered Skulls from the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B Site of Yiftahel (Israel): A Computed Tomography-Based Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): e89242, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0089242. [Open access]","Hendrik J. Bruins and Johannes van der Plicht, “Tell es-Sultan (Jericho): Radiocarbon Results of Short-Lived Cereal and Multiyear Charcoal Samples from the End of the Middle Bronze Age,” <i>Radiocarbon</i> 37, no. 2 (1995): 213–20, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0033822200030666. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","city","prehistory","archaeology","history","palestine"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Tower_of_Jericho.jpg/1920px-Tower_of_Jericho.jpg","title":"Jericho","desc":"The Neolithic stone tower of Jericho, a drum of undressed stone standing in an excavation trench at Tell es-Sultan. Reinhard Dietrich, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tower_of_Jericho.jpg","alt":"The Neolithic stone tower of Jericho, a drum of undressed stone standing in an excavation trench at Tell es-Sultan"},"locator":{"name":"Jericho","at":[35.46,31.8561]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Jericho central to how the Neolithic is divided?","a":"Because the terms PPNA and PPNB were themselves first defined from its stratigraphy, its Pre-Pottery Neolithic levels being among the best known anywhere."},{"q":"Why is the purpose of its tower and wall still disputed?","a":"Because they have been read as a fortification, as flood defence, as a ritual centre and as a claim on territory, and none of those readings has settled the argument."},{"q":"Why does strontium in the teeth matter for reading the site?","a":"Because of 44 of its Neolithic dead only two grew up elsewhere, so the place was held by a community that stayed put rather than one built by incomers."}]},
{"id":"wh-125","num":125,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Houses at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were packed together with no streets and no doors, so people walked over their neighbours' roofs and climbed down a ladder to get in.","questions":["At least 741 people were buried beneath the floors at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and ancient genomes show that those under one house were rarely close relatives.","A wall painting from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been read as a town plan below an erupting volcano, which would make it the oldest map known."],"answer":"Çatalhöyük","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7100 – 6000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Çatalhöyük","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Çatalhöyük</b> is a Neolithic town on the Konya Plain in central Anatolia, occupied from about 7100 to 6000 BCE and known for its size, its well-preserved mudbrick architecture and its wall paintings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its rectangular houses stand packed against one another with no streets between them and no doorways, so people crossed their neighbours' roofs and climbed down a ladder to get indoors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of how many lived there run from several hundred to perhaps a few thousand at the height of the settlement, and the figure rose and fell over the centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every building excavated shows signs of being lived in, no communal architecture or generally accessible space has been found below the topmost levels, and a study weighing many kinds of evidence at once found no sign of lasting or institutionalised inequality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Walls were plastered and painted inside, most often with red panels, geometric designs and handprints, and a minority of houses displayed complete sets of cattle horns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The dead were buried beneath the floors, and at least 741 people have been recovered from the excavated buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient genomes show that close relatives are rare among those laid under one house, so a household was not simply one biological family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pigment went into the graves as well as onto the walls, red ochre most often, with cinnabar found mainly on men and blue and green with women, and the more people a building held the more times its walls had been repainted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plant remains are dominated by wheat and barley, then lentils, peas and chickpeas, with acorns, pistachios and a wide range of wild bulbs and seasonal plants alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One painting has been read as a plan of the town below an erupting volcano, and a summit eruption of Hasan Dağı has been dated to about 6960 BCE, though a leopard skin over a geometric pattern remains the rival reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Katheryn C. Twiss et al., “‘But Some Were More Equal than Others:’ Exploring Inequality at Neolithic Çatalhöyük,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 9 (2024): e0307067, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0307067. [Open access]","Reyhan Yaka et al., “Variable Kinship Patterns in Neolithic Anatolia Revealed by Ancient Genomes,” <i>Current Biology</i> 31, no. 11 (2021): 2455–68, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2021.03.050. [Open access]","Eline M. J. Schotsmans et al., “New Insights on Commemoration of the Dead through Mortuary and Architectural Use of Pigments at Neolithic Çatalhöyük, Turkey,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12, no. 1 (2022): 4055, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-07284-3. [Open access]","Axel K. Schmitt et al., “Identifying the Volcanic Eruption Depicted in a Neolithic Painting at Çatalhöyük, Central Anatolia, Turkey,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 1 (2014): e84711, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084711. [Open access]","Carlos G. Santiago-Marrero et al., “A Microbotanical and Microwear Perspective to Plant Processing Activities and Foodways at Neolithic Çatalhöyük,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252312, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0252312. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","city","prehistory","archaeology","history","turkey"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/%C3%87atalh%C3%B6y%C3%BCk_after_the_first_excavations_by_James_Mellaart_and_his_team..jpg","title":"Çatalhöyük","desc":"Excavated mudbrick rooms at Çatalhöyük standing wall against wall with no streets between them. Omar hoftun, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%87atalh%C3%B6y%C3%BCk_after_the_first_excavations_by_James_Mellaart_and_his_team..jpg","alt":"Excavated mudbrick rooms at Çatalhöyük standing wall against wall with no streets between them"},"locator":{"name":"Çatalhöyük","at":[32.8281,37.6667]},"why":[{"q":"Why did people at Çatalhöyük enter their houses through the roof?","a":"Because the rectangular houses stand packed against one another with no streets between them and no doorways, so people crossed their neighbours' roofs and climbed down a ladder to get indoors."},{"q":"Why is the town read as lacking institutionalised inequality?","a":"Because every building excavated shows signs of being lived in, no communal architecture or generally accessible space has been found below the topmost levels, and a study weighing many kinds of evidence found no sign of lasting inequality."},{"q":"Why was a household there not simply one biological family?","a":"Because ancient genomes show close relatives are rare among the people laid beneath any one house, of the at least 741 recovered from the excavated buildings."}]},
{"id":"wh-126","num":126,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Two caches of lime-plaster statues, each figure built around a bundle of reeds bound with twine, went into the ground at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beneath the floors of houses already abandoned.","questions":["By the end of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B nearly every settlement in the central and southern Levant had been abandoned, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was still growing past 12 hectares (30 acres).","The final aceramic phase of the southern Levant, the PPNC, was defined from the deposits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which went on to be occupied again by Yarmoukian potters."],"answer":"'Ain Ghazal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At its height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8000 – 6600 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"'Ain Ghazal","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>'Ain Ghazal</b> is a Neolithic settlement beside the Zarqa river on the northern edge of Amman, in Jordan, and one of the largest known anywhere in the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began as a modest village and grew through the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B into a mega-site of the kind that reached up to 20 hectares (49 acres) and held hundreds or even a few thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its growth outlasted its neighbours: by the end of that phase nearly every known Pre-Pottery Neolithic B settlement in the central and southern Levant had been abandoned, while it went on spreading past 12 hectares (30 acres) on both banks of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two further phases follow, a final aceramic one defined from the site itself and named the PPNC, and then a Yarmoukian occupation using pottery whose remains cover well over 13 hectares (32 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavator argued that the ground itself gave out, the clearing of woodland and the grazing of herds stripping thin soils off the steep slopes of the southern Levant in a way the flatter north escaped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the site is best known for is two caches of plaster statuary, buried beneath the floors of buildings that had already stood empty for a long time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first held about ten figures 80 to 90 centimetres (31 to 35 inches) tall along with eight or more busts, each built around a bundle of reeds and twigs bound with twine and moulded over in plaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their eyes are outlined in green pigment with black irises, probably bitumen, and one face carries three vertical red stripes on the brow and three diagonal ones on each cheek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Skulls got the same attention, buried in the corners of rooms, and two crania have been shown to be modelled and painted red, one with plaster still adhering and one with a black modelling substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strontium in the teeth of 22 of its dead places all but one within the local range, so whatever drew people together into these mega-sites, its people were almost all born nearby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Gary O. Rollefson, “The Aceramic Neolithic of the Southern Levant: The View from ’Ain Ghazal,” <i>Paléorient</i> 15, no. 1 (1989): 135–40, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1989.4491. [Open access]","Gary O. Rollefson, “Ritual and Ceremony at Neolithic Ain Ghazal (Jordan),” <i>Paléorient</i> 9, no. 2 (1983): 29–38, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1983.4339. [Open access]","Gary O. Rollefson, “’Ain Ghazal (Jordan): Ritual and Ceremony III,” <i>Paléorient</i> 24, no. 1 (1998): 43–58, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.1998.4668. [Open access]","Michelle Bonogofsky, “Cranial Modeling and Neolithic Bone Modification at ’Ain Ghazal: New Interpretations,” <i>Paléorient</i> 27, no. 2 (2001): 141–46, https://doi.org/10.3406/paleo.2001.4734. [Open access]","Jonathan Santana et al., “Multi-Isotope Evidence of Population Aggregation in the Natufian and Scant Migration during the Early Neolithic of the Southern Levant,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11, no. 1 (2021): 11857, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-90795-2. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","jordan","art"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Ain_Ghazal_Statues_Jordan_Archaeological_Museum_Amman_Jordan0822.jpg/1280px-Ain_Ghazal_Statues_Jordan_Archaeological_Museum_Amman_Jordan0822.jpg","title":"'Ain Ghazal","desc":"A two-headed plaster figure from 'Ain Ghazal, the eyes of both heads outlined in black, in the Jordan Archaeological Museum. Michael Gunther, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ain_Ghazal_Statues_Jordan_Archaeological_Museum_Amman_Jordan0822.jpg","alt":"A two-headed plaster figure from 'Ain Ghazal, the eyes of both heads outlined in black, in the Jordan Archaeological Museum"},"why":[{"q":"Why did 'Ain Ghazal outlast its neighbours?","a":"By the end of the Pre-Pottery Neolithic B nearly every known settlement in the central and southern Levant had been abandoned, while it went on spreading past 12 hectares on both banks of the Zarqa."},{"q":"Why did the excavator think the settlement eventually failed?","a":"Because the ground itself gave out: clearing woodland and grazing herds stripped thin soils off the steep slopes of the southern Levant in a way the flatter north escaped."},{"q":"Why does strontium in the teeth complicate the idea of a gathering place?","a":"Because of 22 of its dead all but one fall within the local range, so whatever drew people together into these mega-sites, its people were almost all born nearby rather than drawn in from far off."}]},
{"id":"wh-127","num":127,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Two huts of about 20,000 years ago at Kharaneh IV in Jordan push <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back thousands of years before the Natufian stone houses usually credited with it.","questions":["The house mouse took up living with people in large hunter-gatherer settlements in the Levant about 14,500 years ago, which makes it a marker of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than of farming.","Villages in north-east China appear about 7,900 years ago just as the climate turned wetter, which suggests <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began there under plenty rather than under pressure."],"answer":"sedentism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Levant huts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">House mice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">North-east China</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,900 BP</span></div>","answerText":"sedentism","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sedentism</b> is a way of living with limited or no residential movement, and it is not a rung on a ladder of social evolution but one option among several.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is easier to define than to detect, since words like prolonged or regular occupation are hard to turn into measurements, and the argument over where the line falls has not closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it is read from is permanent structures, grinding stones too heavy to carry, occupation deposits deep enough to imply long repeated stays, and the animals that move in to live alongside people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Southwest Asia it came well before farming: two huts of about 20,000 years ago at Kharaneh IV in eastern Jordan, with caches of shell and red ochre, stand thousands of years before the Natufian stone houses usually credited with the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> House mice make the same point from the other end, taking up life with people in large late hunter-gatherer settlements in the Levant around 14,500 years ago, before any crop was sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In north-east China villages and cultivation appear together about 7,900 years ago, at the moment the climate turned wetter, which argues that people settled under plenty rather than under pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There the domestication of plants followed only half a millennium after cultivation began, and even then domesticated plants and animals were rare, so settling down did not wait on farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Tanegashima in southern Japan the degree of settlement tracks what the forest offered, and stable sedentism arrives only with the Incipient Jomon, when the island's population rose as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the Caribbean, strontium and oxygen in the teeth of 44 people from Canímar Abajo in Cuba describe a coastal community living on local resources, not the highly mobile foragers the island's early groups were long taken to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Staying put also made a niche for other species, and the house mouse spread out of it with farming across the Near East and much later with Europe's towns and trade routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lisa A. Maher et al., “Twenty Thousand-Year-Old Huts at a Hunter-Gatherer Settlement in Eastern Jordan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 2 (2012): e31447, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0031447. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi et al., “Tracking the Near Eastern Origins and European Dispersal of the Western House Mouse,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10, no. 1 (2020): 8276, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-64939-9. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach-Lavi et al., “Sedentism and Plant Cultivation in Northeast China Emerged during Affluent Conditions,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 7 (2019): e0218751, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218751. [Open access]","Yadira Chinique de Armas et al., “Earliest Evidence of Sedentism in the Antilles: Multiple Isotope Data from Canímar Abajo, Cuba,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 1 (2025): e2413963121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2413963121. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Limestone_%26_basalt_mortars_from_Eynan%2C_early_Natufian_circa_12500_BC.jpg/1920px-Limestone_%26_basalt_mortars_from_Eynan%2C_early_Natufian_circa_12500_BC.jpg","title":"Sedentism","desc":"Two large limestone and basalt mortars from Natufian Eynan, each too heavy to be carried about, in the Israel Museum. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limestone_%26_basalt_mortars_from_Eynan,_early_Natufian_circa_12500_BC.jpg","alt":"Two large limestone and basalt mortars from Natufian Eynan, each too heavy to be carried about, in the Israel Museum"},"why":[{"q":"Why is sedentism easier to define than to detect?","a":"Because words like prolonged or regular occupation are hard to turn into measurements, so it has to be read indirectly from structures, heavy grinding stones, deep occupation deposits and the animals that move in alongside people."},{"q":"Why does sedentism not depend on farming?","a":"Because in Southwest Asia it came first: huts of about 20,000 years ago at Kharaneh IV stand thousands of years before the Natufian houses usually credited with the change, and house mice took up life with people around 14,500 years ago."},{"q":"Why is north-east China read as settling under plenty?","a":"Because villages and cultivation appear together about 7,900 years ago at the moment the climate turned wetter, and domesticated plants and animals stayed rare for centuries afterwards."}]},
{"id":"wh-128","num":128,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"The oldest dates for fired clay vessels anywhere come from caves in southern China, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an invention of hunter-gatherers rather than of farmers.","questions":["Fats soaked into 528 vessels from around the Baltic show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was put to regionally different uses, fish in the east and animal fat further south and west.","Reaching the Baltic from the Caspian in three or four centuries, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread across Europe several times faster than farming did, and among foragers rather than farmers."],"answer":"pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest pots</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 – 17,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 11,000 – 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 5900 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"pottery","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pottery</b> is fired clay shaped into vessels, and its history cuts across the usual account of the Neolithic, because it was often invented by hunter-gatherers rather than by farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest dates for pots anywhere are from caves in southern China, about 20,000 to 17,000 years ago, though other evidence from the same deposits has been read as putting them nearer the end of the Ice Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foragers on Tanegashima in southern Japan were making it by roughly 14,000 to 12,800 years ago, mostly on the island itself, though a few vessels were carried there across open water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Africa's early pottery belongs to hunting, gathering and fishing peoples too, appearing across the Sahara and the Nile valley between about 11,000 and 10,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modelling the radiocarbon dates there favours two or three separate inventions rather than one, with the likely centres in the Central Sahara, the Nile valley and West Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Europe it reached hunter-gatherers before farming did, appearing near the northern shore of the Caspian shortly before 5900 BCE and then running west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It covered more than 3,000 kilometres (1,900 miles) to the Baltic in three or four centuries, six to ten kilometres a year, several times the speed at which farming spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is too fast to be people carrying it as they moved, so what travelled was the knowledge, passed along networks of kin and neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the pots were for can be read from the fats soaked into their walls, and 528 vessels from around the Baltic show regional habits rather than regional environments, with fish in the east, non-ruminant animal fat in the south-east and a mixture including dairy in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Near East the order is reversed: at the Mazandaran caves a Pre-Pottery Neolithic lies beneath the Pottery Neolithic, and the oldest pottery of north-eastern Iran begins only at the start of the 7th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Fumie Iizuka et al., “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Rocco Rotunno and Enrico R. Crema, “Bayesian Analyses of Radiocarbon Dates Suggest Multiple Origins of Ceramic Technology in Early Holocene Africa,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 8819, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63887-0. [Open access]","Ekaterina Dolbunova et al., “The Transmission of Pottery Technology among Prehistoric European Hunter-Gatherers,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 7, no. 2 (2023): 171–83, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-022-01491-8. [Open access]","Blandine Courel et al., “Organic Residue Analysis Shows Sub-Regional Patterns in the Use of Pottery by Northern European Hunter-Gatherers,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 7, no. 4 (2020): 192016, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.192016. [Open access]","Rahmat Abbasnejad Seresti et al., “The Oldest Pottery Neolithic (PN) Culture of Northeastern Iran: First Absolute Dating from Eastern Mazandaran Plains,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0322478, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0322478. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","technology","prehistory","archaeology","history"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg/1920px-Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg","title":"Pottery","desc":"Two deep Incipient Jomon cooking pots with pointed bases and banded decoration, in the Tokyo National Museum. Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg","alt":"Two deep Incipient Jomon cooking pots with pointed bases and banded decoration, in the Tokyo National Museum"},"why":[{"q":"Why does pottery cut across the usual account of the Neolithic?","a":"Because it was often invented by hunter-gatherers rather than farmers — the oldest pots are from southern Chinese caves, Africa's early pottery belongs to hunting, gathering and fishing peoples, and in Europe it reached foragers before farming did."},{"q":"Why must what spread across Europe have been knowledge rather than people?","a":"Because pottery covered more than 3,000 kilometres to the Baltic in three or four centuries, six to ten kilometres a year — several times the speed at which farming spread, and too fast for people carrying it as they moved."},{"q":"Why are the fats in a pot's walls worth measuring?","a":"Because they say what the vessel was used for: 528 vessels around the Baltic show fish in the east, non-ruminant fat in the south-east and a mixture including dairy in the west — regional habits rather than regional environments."}]},
{"id":"wh-129","num":129,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Non-shattering types are only about a tenth of the rice impressed in 10,000-year-old pottery clay at Shangshan, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is called a protracted process.","questions":["Use-wear and rice phytoliths on 52 flaked stone tools from the Lower Yangtze show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was driven by harvesting, first with finger knives and later with sickles.","Most functional mutations in japonica landraces turn out to predate the Holocene, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked largely on variation already present in the wild plant rather than on new mutations."],"answer":"rice domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 4,400 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reached Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 2,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"rice domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Rice domestication</b> turned a wetland grass into the crop that feeds more than half the world, and its hallmark is the loss of seed shattering, which leaves a plant unable to sow itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asian rice, <i>Oryza sativa</i>, was first domesticated in China as the japonica type, beginning about 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its wild ancestor <i>Oryza rufipogon</i> grows in swampy wetland, ripens unevenly and drops its seed into the water, which makes it a hard plant to gather and a slow one to change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The change was protracted, starting in the Shangshan culture of the Lower Yangtze about 10,000 years ago and running for several millennia to the Liangzhu culture of 5,300 to 4,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice spikelets impressed in Shangshan pottery clay of the 10th millennium BP are 12 per cent non-shattering, and at Huxi, 9,000 to 8,400 years ago, the figure is 8.7 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Harvesting is what did the selecting, and 52 flaked stone tools from Shangshan and Hehuashan carry the traces, 30 of them worn by cutting plants and 28 of those holding rice phytoliths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two methods can be told apart, reaping the heads with a finger knife and cutting the stalks near the base with a sickle, and sickles took over as water management made the crop ripen together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice was drunk as well as eaten: vessels from the earliest phase at Shangshan held a fermented drink made with a starter of <i>Monascus</i> mould and yeast, the oldest brewing method known in East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of 76 known functional mutations in japonica landraces arose in the Pleistocene, more than 11,700 years ago, so farmers were largely selecting variation the wild plant already carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> West Africa domesticated a rice of its own from the wild <i>Oryza barthii</i>, and Asian rice reached West Asia and Europe by the close of the 1st millennium BCE, by the Silk Road and by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jiajing Wang et al., “New Evidence for Rice Harvesting in the Early Neolithic Lower Yangtze River, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 12 (2022): e0278200, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0278200. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Identification of 10,000-Year-Old Rice Beer at Shangshan in the Lower Yangzi River Valley of China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 51 (2024): e2412274121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2412274121. [Open access]","Ornob Alam et al., “Evolutionary Histories of Functional Mutations during the Domestication and Spread of <i>japonica</i> Rice in Asia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 43 (2025): e2514614122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2514614122. [Open access]","Babatunde O. Kehinde et al., “African Cultivated, Wild and Weedy Rice (<i>Oryza</i> spp.): Anticipating Further Genomic Studies,” <i>Biology</i> 13, no. 9 (2024): 697, https://doi.org/10.3390/biology13090697. [Open access]","Robert N. Spengler et al., “A Journey to the West: The Ancient Dispersal of Rice Out of East Asia,” <i>Rice</i> 14, no. 1 (2021): 83, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12284-021-00518-4. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Zhejiang_Sheng_Bowuguan_2014.09.28_15-14-33.jpg/1920px-Zhejiang_Sheng_Bowuguan_2014.09.28_15-14-33.jpg","title":"Rice domestication","desc":"Waterlogged rice grains and rice leaves from the Hemudu site, dark with age, laid out in a dish in the Zhejiang Provincial Museum. Zhangzhugang, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhejiang_Sheng_Bowuguan_2014.09.28_15-14-33.jpg","alt":"Waterlogged rice grains and rice leaves from the Hemudu site, dark with age, laid out in a dish in the Zhejiang Provincial Museum"},"why":[{"q":"Why was rice a slow plant to domesticate?","a":"Because its wild ancestor grows in swampy wetland, ripens unevenly and drops its seed into the water, which makes it hard to gather and slow to change — the process ran for several millennia from Shangshan to Liangzhu."},{"q":"Why does harvesting explain the loss of shattering?","a":"Because 52 flaked stone tools from Shangshan and Hehuashan carry the traces, 30 worn by cutting plants and 28 of those holding rice phytoliths, so reaping is what selected the plants that held their seed."},{"q":"Why were early farmers largely not creating new variation?","a":"Because most of 76 known functional mutations in japonica landraces arose in the Pleistocene, more than 11,700 years ago, so they were selecting variation the wild plant already carried."}]},
{"id":"wh-130","num":130,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Foxtail and broomcorn were the crops of the Yellow River basin, and their non-shattering and grain-size changes put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 8300 and 4300 BCE.","questions":["Directly dating 29 charred grains at Dadiwan put the oldest secure evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 7,900 BP, against the ages assumed for the layers they came out of.","Yielding far less per hectare than wet rice, the crops produced by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread northern China's Neolithic cultures over much more ground than their Yangtze neighbours."],"answer":"millet domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8300 – 4300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dadiwan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,900 BP, directly dated</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reached Iberia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"millet domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Millet domestication</b> gave northern China its staple crops, foxtail millet and broomcorn millet, and made the Yellow River basin one of the two centres of farming in early China alongside the rice country of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The changes that mark it — the loss of shattering, larger grains and denser heads — are placed between 8300 and 4300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wild relatives, green foxtail and the ruderal broomcorn group, now grow mostly as weeds of cultivated ground, so where they are found says little about where they were first taken in hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early finds cluster in a semi-arid belt of the loess country, where monsoon rainfall runs between 400 and 700 millimetres (16 and 28 inches) and woodland gives way to steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 4500 to 3500 BCE millet-centred farming was established across that basin and the Loess Plateau above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Millets yield much less per hectare than wet rice, which is why the northern Neolithic cultures covered far more ground than their Yangtze counterparts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dating 29 charred grains from Dadiwan directly gives about 7,900 BP as the oldest secure date for the crop, and most of those dates disagree with the ages assumed for the layers the grains came out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the corrected chronology four waves carried dry-land farming west along the eastern Silk Road, at roughly 7800, 6500, 5500 and 4500 BP, and millet was being eaten on the Atlantic coast of Iberia by about 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was never a one-crop system: adzuki beans directly dated to 8985 to 8645 years ago at Xiaogao in Shandong sat in a field system with millet, rice and soybean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomes from 74 Middle Neolithic individuals show the Yellow and Yangtze farmers as distinct populations exchanging in both directions, which is what farming spread by people rather than by ideas looks like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Haiming Li et al., “Multiple Waves of Westward Dry-Land Agriculture Expansions along the East Silk Road during the Neolithic Age,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 6, no. 3 (2026): 1321–31, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2025.12.013. [Open access]","Xuexiang Chen et al., “The Discovery of Adzuki Bean (<i>Vigna angularis</i>) in Eastern China during the 9th Millennium BP and Its Domestication in East Asia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 39 (2025): e2510835122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2510835122. [Open access]","Jianxue Xiong et al., “The Genomic History of East Asian Middle Neolithic Millet- and Rice-Agricultural Populations,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 5, no. 10 (2025): 100976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12791001/. [Open access]","Borja González-Rabanal et al., “The Arrival of Millets to the Atlantic Coast of Northern Iberia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12, no. 1 (2022): 18589, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-23227-4. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg/1920px-Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg","title":"Millet domestication","desc":"A stand of ripening foxtail millet, the bristly heads bent over under the weight of the grain, in the botanical garden in Warsaw. Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg","alt":"A stand of ripening foxtail millet, the bristly heads bent over under the weight of the grain, in the botanical garden in Warsaw"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the wild relatives say little about where millet was domesticated?","a":"Because green foxtail and the ruderal broomcorn group now grow mostly as weeds of cultivated ground, so where they are found today reflects farming rather than the plants' original range."},{"q":"Why did northern Neolithic cultures cover far more ground than those of the Yangtze?","a":"Because millets yield much less per hectare than wet rice, so the same number of people needed a much larger area of the loess country to feed themselves."},{"q":"Why did dating the grains directly change the chronology?","a":"Because most of the dates on 29 charred grains from Dadiwan disagree with the ages assumed for the layers they came out of, which put the oldest secure date at about 7,900 BP and reset the westward waves."}]},
{"id":"wh-131","num":131,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Teosinte, a wiry Mexican grass carrying a handful of hard-cased seeds, is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began from in the Balsas region about 9,000 years ago.","questions":["Cobs from Paredones in Peru are already domesticated at 6,700 years old while older Mexican ones are still intermediate, which is why the course of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is argued over.","At El Gigante in Honduras tree fruits and squash appear 6,000 years before maize does, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a long way from making it anybody's staple."],"answer":"maize domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 years ago, in Mexico</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In South America</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 7,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A staple after</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"maize domestication","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Maize domestication</b> turned teosinte, a wiry Mexican grass bearing a handful of hard-cased seeds, into the most productive staple crop in the world, and it began in south-western Mexico about 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wild ancestor is <i>Zea mays</i> ssp. <i>parviglumis</i>, and starch grains and phytoliths from the Balsas region put the crop in use there by about 8,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Comparing a maize landrace with a population of its ancestor across 18 domestication traits finds that the intensity of selection was weak for every one of them, weaker than natural selection often is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of those traits the total weight of grain per plant is the one selection does not appear to have improved at all, so this was not, at the start, a crop being bred for yield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It travelled quickly for all that, reaching South America by at least 7,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether it arrived finished is the live argument: on one reading it went south as a partial domesticate, and South America became a second centre of improvement where the domestication syndrome was fixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against that, three cobs from Paredones on the Peruvian coast, 6,700 to 5,000 years old, come from the same domestication event as Mexican maize and are already domesticated, where the older Mexican specimens from Guilá Naquitz and San Marcos are intermediate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Primitive-looking cobs did survive a long way out, in the Peruaçu valley of Brazil between 1,010 and 570 years ago, the furthest from the centre of origin and the longest such characters are known to have lasted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from El Gigante in Honduras, 2,300 to 1,900 years old, are closest to South American maize, so a later wave carried the crop back north again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was slow to matter: at that same shelter tree fruits and squash appear about 11,000 years ago and maize only about 4,400, with the turn to field crops coming after 4,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Logan Kistler et al., “Archaeological Central American Maize Genomes Suggest Ancient Gene Flow from South America,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 52 (2020): 33124–29, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2015560117. [Open access]","Chin Jian Yang et al., “The Genetic Architecture of Teosinte Catalyzed and Constrained Maize Domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 12 (2019): 5643–52, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1820997116. [Open access]","Miguel Vallebueno-Estrada et al., “Domestication and Lowland Adaptation of Coastal Preceramic Maize from Paredones, Peru,” <i>eLife</i> 12 (2023): e83149, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.83149. [Open access]","Flaviane Malaquias Costa et al., “Archaeological Findings Show the Extent of Primitive Characteristics of Maize in South America,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 36 (2024): eadn1466, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adn1466. [Open access]","Douglas J. Kennett et al., “Trans-Holocene Bayesian Chronology for Tree and Field Crop Use from El Gigante Rockshelter, Honduras,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 6 (2023): e0287195, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0287195. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","mexico"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Maize-teosinte.jpg","title":"Maize domestication","desc":"Three ears laid side by side: a slender teosinte ear carrying about a dozen hard-cased seeds, a maize-teosinte hybrid, and a full maize cob many times the size of either. John Doebley, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maize-teosinte.jpg","alt":"Three ears laid side by side: a slender teosinte ear carrying about a dozen hard-cased seeds, a maize-teosinte hybrid, and a full maize cob many times the size of either"},"why":[{"q":"Why is maize not, at the start, a crop bred for yield?","a":"Because across 18 domestication traits the intensity of selection was weak for every one, and the total weight of grain per plant is the trait selection does not appear to have improved at all."},{"q":"Why is it disputed whether maize arrived in South America finished?","a":"Because one reading has it going south as a partial domesticate improved there, while three cobs from Paredones, 6,700 to 5,000 years old, are already domesticated where the older Mexican specimens are intermediate."},{"q":"Why did maize take so long to become a staple?","a":"Because at El Gigante tree fruits and squash appear about 11,000 years ago and maize only about 4,400, with the turn to field crops coming after 4,000 — thousands of years of use before dominance."}]},
{"id":"wh-132","num":132,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Carved stone and manufactured pestles at Waim are evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, putting a Neolithic in the New Guinea highlands a thousand years before any migrants arrived.","questions":["Where a crop varies most is where it is oldest, or so ran the reasoning behind the phrase centre of origin, which gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its vocabulary.","Indigenous peoples of eastern North America domesticated annual crops of their own, one of the least famous cases of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and several of those crops later fell out of use."],"answer":"independent origins of agriculture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Centres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 11, independent</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 11,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Guinea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a Neolithic by c. 5,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"independent origins of agriculture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>independent origins of agriculture</b> are the finding that farming was not invented once and handed on but arose separately in at least eleven centres, each working whatever grew or grazed nearby, as the last ice age let go 12,000 to 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nikolai Vavilov gave the idea its vocabulary, coining the phrase centre of origin for the place where a crop's diversity is greatest, on the reasoning that a crop is oldest where it varies most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The method was fallible: his proposal that emmer, barley, oats and lentils were independently domesticated in Abyssinia has been disproven, though coffee, enset, t'ef and finger millet do appear to come from the Ethiopian highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scholars have never agreed what domestication is, how it happens, or even which organisms it applies to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What has given way is the idea of a single core area and a single event, in favour of protracted and largely unconscious processes running along several parallel lineages at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The New Guinea highlands are the clearest case outside the famous centres, with wetland farming from the middle of the Holocene and, at Waim, the earliest figurative stone carving and manufactured pestles in Oceania, of 5,050 to 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those finds put a Neolithic there about a thousand years before Lapita migrants reached the region from South-East Asia, which is what makes it an invention rather than an import.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eastern North America is another, where Indigenous peoples domesticated a set of annual crops of their own, several of which fell out of cultivation around the time of European colonization and survive only in the archaeological record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Calling those crops lost is the older habit; with Indigenous food sovereignty movements taking the practice up again they are better described as sleeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The record there is unusually full because flotation, which lifts charred seeds out of excavated soil in water, became standard practice in the 1960s and has since been applied to thousands of sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Greger Larson et al., “Current Perspectives and the Future of Domestication Studies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 111, no. 17 (2014): 6139–46, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1323964111. [Open access]","Rosalind Emma Gillis et al., “Unravelling Domestication: Introduction to the Theme Issue,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240187, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2024.0187. [Open access]","Robert N. Spengler and Li Tang, “Seeking Consensus on the Domestication Concept,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240188, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2024.0188. [Open access]","Ben Shaw et al., “Emergence of a Neolithic in Highland New Guinea by 5000 to 4000 Years Ago,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 13 (2020): eaay4573, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aay4573. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The Sleeping Crops of Eastern North America: A New Synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2024.0192. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","evolution"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Centres_of_origin_and_spread_of_agriculture_labelled.svg/1280px-Centres_of_origin_and_spread_of_agriculture_labelled.svg.png","title":"Independent origins of agriculture","desc":"A world map marking centres where farming began — the Fertile Crescent, the Yangtze and Yellow River basins, New Guinea, Central Mexico, north-western South America, eastern North America and the Sahel — with arrows for its spread and question marks over the least certain. Ian Alexander, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centres_of_origin_and_spread_of_agriculture_labelled.svg","alt":"A world map marking centres where farming began — the Fertile Crescent, the Yangtze and Yellow River basins, New Guinea, Central Mexico, north-western South America, eastern North America and the Sahel — with arrows for its spread and question marks over the least certain"},"why":[{"q":"Why is a crop thought to be oldest where it varies most?","a":"That was Vavilov's reasoning behind the phrase centre of origin, and it was fallible — his claim that emmer, barley, oats and lentils were domesticated in Abyssinia has been disproven, though coffee, enset, t'ef and finger millet do come from there."},{"q":"Why does New Guinea count as an invention rather than an import?","a":"Because the wetland farming and the Waim carvings and pestles of 5,050 to 4,200 years ago put a Neolithic there about a thousand years before Lapita migrants reached the region from South-East Asia."},{"q":"Why are eastern North America's domesticated crops better called sleeping than lost?","a":"Because several fell out of cultivation around the time of European colonization and survive in the archaeological record, and Indigenous food sovereignty movements are taking the practice up again."}]},
{"id":"wh-133","num":133,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cemeteries hold a higher share of children after farming arrives, and that rising share is the signal by which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first identified.","questions":["A 420-year rise followed by some 840 years of decline is the shape the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes in the radiocarbon record, and what caused the falling half is still disputed.","Because it puts the growth after farming rather than before it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> undid the older claim that a crowded Mesolithic drove people to take up crops."],"answer":"Neolithic demographic transition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Follows farming</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 9,000 BP in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Boom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">420 years, at 0.17 per cent a year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bust</span><span class=\"dt-v\">some 840 years after that</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic demographic transition","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neolithic demographic transition</b> is the surge in human numbers that followed the arrival of farming, and its engine was fertility rather than survival: people had more children, while a new regime of higher mortality ran alongside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is read from cemeteries, where the share of skeletons that are children rises after farming reaches a region, a measure known as the juvenility index.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because farming arrived in Europe over four thousand years, the clock is set to zero at the local arrival date, which is what lets regions a millennium apart be compared at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That evidence overturned an older claim that a crowded Mesolithic had pushed people into farming: the growth comes after the crops, not before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reading is only as good as the bones, and in small samples the arithmetic behind it carries a large error, which is why the standard equation is now fitted per sample rather than taken off the shelf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A second and quite separate proxy counts radiocarbon dates from archaeological sites, on the reasoning that more people leave more datable rubbish, and the two curves correlate closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together they give the transition its shape: a 420-year rise, then some 840 years of decline, a whole cycle of about 1,260 years, with the boom running at 0.17 per cent a year and the bust at a quarter of that in reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What caused the falling half is unsettled, and three answers are in play.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The European cemetery study found no correlation with climate, dated radiocarbon series from Britain and Ireland put the downturns alongside abrupt North Atlantic climate changes, and modelling of the periods and amplitudes finds climate cannot produce them where density-driven conflict can.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Northern China shows the same rise between 5000 and 2000 BCE, and there it is credited less to domestication itself than to the improvement of crops and animals afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Sean S. Downey et al., “The Neolithic Demographic Transition in Europe: Correlation with Juvenility Index Supports Interpretation of the Summed Calibrated Radiocarbon Date Probability Distribution (SCDPD) as a Valid Demographic Proxy,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 8 (2014): e105730, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0105730. [Open access]","Patrik Galeta and Anna Pankowská, “A New Method for Estimating Growth and Fertility Rates Using Age-at-Death Ratios in Small Skeletal Samples,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 6 (2023): e0286580, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0286580. [Open access]","Dániel Kondor et al., “Explaining Population Booms and Busts in Mid-Holocene Europe,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 9310, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-35920-z. [Open access]","Andrew Bevan et al., “Holocene Fluctuations in Human Population Demonstrate Repeated Links to Food Production and Climate,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 49 (2017): E10524–31, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1709190114. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","science"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/GBM_-_Linearbandkeramik_1_Grab.jpg/1920px-GBM_-_Linearbandkeramik_1_Grab.jpg","title":"Neolithic demographic transition","desc":"A crouched Linear Pottery culture burial from the cemetery at Aiterhofen-Odmuhle, laid out with shell beads and stone adzes, on display in the Gaubodenmuseum at Straubing. Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GBM_-_Linearbandkeramik_1_Grab.jpg","alt":"A crouched Linear Pottery culture burial from the cemetery at Aiterhofen-Odmuhle, laid out with shell beads and stone adzes, on display in the Gaubodenmuseum at Straubing"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the clock set to the local arrival of farming rather than to a calendar date?","a":"Because farming reached Europe over four thousand years, so zeroing at each region's own arrival date is what allows regions a millennium apart to be compared at all."},{"q":"Why did this evidence overturn the older account of why farming was adopted?","a":"Because the growth in numbers comes after the crops rather than before them, which refutes the claim that a crowded Mesolithic pushed people into farming."},{"q":"Why is the cause of the declining half still unsettled?","a":"Because three answers are in play: the European cemetery study found no correlation with climate, British and Irish radiocarbon series put downturns alongside abrupt North Atlantic changes, and modelling finds climate cannot produce them where density-driven conflict can."}]},
{"id":"wh-134","num":134,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Screening 1,313 ancient Eurasians turned up the microbes behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only from about 6,500 years ago, peaking as livestock keeping spread five millennia back.","questions":["At four cemeteries by Lake Baikal 39 per cent of the dead carry plague from 5,500 years ago, and they are hunter-gatherers, which complicates the idea that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived with farming.","Whether the earliest strains of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> caused flea-borne plague or food-borne enteritis is unsettled, and with it how much the Neolithic decline is owed to infection."],"answer":"zoonotic disease","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zoonoses appear</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,500 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bubonic plague</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3,800 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"zoonotic disease","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zoonotic disease</b> is illness that passes between animals and people, and taking animals into the household changed the ecology of both, opening ways for pathogens to invade and persist in each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Screening 1,313 ancient Eurasians across 37,000 years of remains turned up 5,486 hits from 492 microbial species, of which 3,384 are known human pathogens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most kinds of microbe run through that whole span, and the zoonotic ones do not: they appear from about 6,500 years ago and peak around 5,000, which is when livestock keeping spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is direct evidence for something long argued and thinly supported, a first epidemiological transition in which settling down beside animals raised the burden of infectious disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their spread grew over the millennia after, alongside the pastoralist migrations out of the Eurasian steppe, and the weight of what that meant is still visible as late as 1850, when about half of all children died before the age of fifteen and more than half of those deaths were from infection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plague is the test case, and it complicates the story rather than confirming it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <i>Yersinia pestis</i> lacked the machinery for the bubonic form until about 3,800 years ago, so what the earlier strains did to a person cannot be read off the genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At four cemeteries by Lake Baikal it is in 39 per cent of the dead from about 5,500 years ago, within small family groups and killing children of 8 to 11 — and those people were hunter-gatherers, far outside Neolithic Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Others read the early strains as food-borne enteritis rather than flea-borne plague, and doubt that epidemics emptied Neolithic Scandinavia at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Bury in the Paris Basin, 132 genomes either side of a break in burial show two largely unrelated populations, plague and relapsing fever in the record between them, and forest growing back over the fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Martin Sikora et al., “The Spatiotemporal Distribution of Human Pathogens in Ancient Eurasia,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8073 (2025): 1011–19, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09192-8. [Open access]","Ruairidh Macleod et al., “Lethal Plague Outbreaks in Lake Baikal Hunter-Gatherers 5,500 Years Ago,” <i>Nature</i> 654, no. 8119 (2026): 697–705, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10540-5. [Open access]","Hamadou Oumarou Hama et al., “Commentary on Seersholm et al.: <i>Yersinia pestis</i> Infection Is Not Synonymous with Deadly Plague in Neolithic Scandinavia,” <i>American Journal of Biological Anthropology</i> 189, no. 1 (2026): e70200, https://doi.org/10.1002/ajpa.70200. [Open access]","Frederik V. Seersholm et al., “Population Discontinuity in the Paris Basin Linked to Evidence of the Neolithic Decline,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 10, no. 4 (2026): 677–88, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-026-03027-z. [Open access]","Robin Bendrey et al., “Conceptualizing Emergent Animal Farming and Infectious Diseases: A One Health Framework,” <i>Evolution, Medicine, and Public Health</i> 13, no. 1 (2025): 344–54, https://doi.org/10.1093/emph/eoaf029. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","science"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg/1920px-Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg","title":"Zoonotic disease","desc":"Rod-shaped Yersinia pestis bacteria massed in the foregut of a flea, in a scanning electron micrograph with the instrument's own scale bar burnt in along the foot. NIAID, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg","alt":"Rod-shaped Yersinia pestis bacteria massed in the foregut of a flea, in a scanning electron micrograph with the instrument's own scale bar burnt in along the foot"},"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does the ancient microbial screen support a first epidemiological transition?","a":"Because most kinds of microbe run through the whole 37,000-year span while the zoonotic ones appear only from about 6,500 years ago and peak around 5,000 — which is when livestock keeping spread."},{"q":"Why can early plague not be read off its genome?","a":"Because Yersinia pestis lacked the machinery for the bubonic form until about 3,800 years ago, so what the earlier strains did to a person cannot be inferred from the sequence alone."},{"q":"Why does the Lake Baikal evidence complicate the farming story?","a":"Because plague is in 39 per cent of the dead there from about 5,500 years ago, within small family groups and killing children — and those people were hunter-gatherers, far outside Neolithic Europe."}]},
{"id":"wh-135","num":135,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Milk residues sit on European pots from the Neolithic onward, yet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed rare for another three thousand years, which is the puzzle every explanation of it must answer.","questions":["The variants behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sit not in the lactase gene but in an enhancer inside a neighbouring one, and five of them have been shown to work.","Central Asian herders live on dairy products and only about 12 per cent of them show <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because fermenting the milk answers the same problem culturally."],"answer":"lactase persistence","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Selection began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,500 BP, between the Balkans and central Europe</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First carrier</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,960 BP, Eneolithic Ukraine</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Common in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 3,000 BP</span></div>","answerText":"lactase persistence","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lactase persistence</b> is the continued production of lactase into adulthood, which lets a person digest the sugar in milk; losing the enzyme after weaning is the ancestral condition for humans and for every other mammal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 35 per cent of people worldwide keep it, and the variants responsible sit not in the lactase gene itself but in an enhancer inside the neighbouring gene upstream of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Five have been shown to work, the European one by binding a transcription factor more tightly than the ancestral sequence does and so keeping the gene switched on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Selection on it has been put at between 3 and 19 per cent in northern Europe, which makes it one of the strongest signals of recent natural selection anywhere in the human genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also a case of convergent evolution, separate mutations in the same short stretch of DNA doing the same job in eastern and western Africa, in Arabia and in South Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Milk itself came first, and by a long way: fat residues on some 7,000 potsherds from more than 550 sites show milk in use across Europe from the beginning of the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The allele stayed rare for millennia after that, turning up reliably 5,960 years ago, standing at about 1 per cent between 5,000 and 4,000 years ago, and reaching 31 per cent only after 3,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modelling allele frequencies against farming dates puts the start of selection around 7,500 years ago between the central Balkans and central Europe, and finds no support for the old northern-sunlight explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Why it rose at all is unsettled, since how much milk a region used explains its trajectory no better than steady selection does, and living carriers show no consistent advantage in health or fertility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Famine and infection are the current proposal, milk being dangerous without the enzyme to anyone already weak — while Central Asia, heavily pastoral and barely persistent because its milk is fermented, resists any simpler account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Richard P. Evershed et al., “Dairying, Diseases and the Evolution of Lactase Persistence in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 608, no. 7922 (2022): 336–45, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/7615474. [Open access]","Laure Ségurel et al., “Why and When Was Lactase Persistence Selected For? Insights from Central Asian Herders and Ancient DNA,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 6 (2020): e3000742, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000742. [Open access]","Jay T. Stock and Jonathan C. K. Wells, “Dairying and the Evolution and Consequences of Lactase Persistence in Humans,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 13, no. 3 (2023): 7–13, https://doi.org/10.1093/af/vfad022. [Open access]","Céleste E. Cohen, Dallas M. Swallow, and Catherine Walker, “The Molecular Basis of Lactase Persistence: Linking Genetics and Epigenetics,” <i>Annals of Human Genetics</i> 89, no. 5 (2025): 321–32, https://doi.org/10.1111/ahg.12575. [Open access]","Yuval Itan et al., “The Origins of Lactase Persistence in Europe,” <i>PLOS Computational Biology</i> 5, no. 8 (2009): e1000491, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1000491. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","evolution","genetics","science","agriculture"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Lactase_persistence_in_the_Old_World.svg","title":"Lactase persistence","desc":"An interpolated map of Africa, Europe and Asia shading the percentage of adults carrying a lactase-persistence genotype, darkest across north-western Europe and in patches of East Africa and Arabia, with small circles marking the sampled populations. Joe Roe, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lactase_persistence_in_the_Old_World.svg","alt":"An interpolated map of Africa, Europe and Asia shading the percentage of adults carrying a lactase-persistence genotype, darkest across north-western Europe and in patches of East Africa and Arabia, with small circles marking the sampled populations"},"why":[{"q":"Why is lactase persistence called one of the strongest selection signals in our genome?","a":"Because selection on it has been put at between 3 and 19 per cent in northern Europe, an unusually large coefficient for a recent human trait, and it arose convergently in Africa, Arabia and South Asia."},{"q":"Why did milk drinking not require the allele?","a":"Because fat residues on some 7,000 potsherds from more than 550 sites show milk in use across Europe from the beginning of the Neolithic, while the allele stayed at about 1 per cent between 5,000 and 4,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why is the reason for its rise still unsettled?","a":"Because how much milk a region used explains its trajectory no better than steady selection does, living carriers show no consistent advantage, and heavily pastoral Central Asia is barely persistent because its milk is fermented."}]},
{"id":"wh-136","num":136,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Cattle were pulling loads in Southwest Asia by about 8200 BCE and milk sat in European pots from the first farmers, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer read as one horizon.","questions":["Milk, wool, traction and transport are the four uses gathered together by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, every one of them taken from a living animal rather than from a carcass.","A yoke from a village on Lake Constance and plough furrows sealed under a Wiltshire barrow are the 4th-millennium evidence the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built on, and both still stand."],"answer":"secondary products revolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1981, for the 4th millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest yoke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3384–3370 BCE, Lake Constance</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 3900 BCE, Carpathians</span></div>","answerText":"secondary products revolution","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>secondary products revolution</b> is a proposal that livestock were first kept for what can only be had by killing them, and that the uses of a living animal followed thousands of years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those uses are milk, wool, traction and transport, and as set out in 1981 they arrived together as a package in the 4th millennium BCE, spreading into Europe from Southwest Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The claim mattered because each use changes what an animal is worth: one that ploughs opens heavy ground, one that pulls a cart moves bulk, and a fleece or a milking comes again next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wool is the hardest to date, fleece almost never surviving, so it is tracked through the sheep instead, whose spread across Europe was reshaped by a Bronze Age movement off the Pontic steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its 4th-millennium evidence is solid — a cattle yoke from a village on Lake Constance, dated 3384–3370 BCE, ard furrows sealed under a Wiltshire barrow — and the wheel may belong there too, one argument placing its invention among Carpathian copper miners by 3900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What has gone is the single horizon, because each of the four turns out to be far older somewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fat residues on pottery show milk in use across Europe from the beginning of the Neolithic, so the first farmers were already dairying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cattle were worked as early as about 8200 BCE in Southwest Asia, by 6000 BCE in the Balkans and by 5300 BCE in Catalonia, which leaves no room for a single transfer of know-how westward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Timings, reasons and process differ region by region, and what travelled was a set of separable practices taken up where local conditions rewarded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the 4th millennium does hold it holds sharply: north of the Caucasus the early steppe herders milked sheep and nothing else, keeping cattle back for work, while at Kilshane in Ireland a herd was bred to raise large oxen for the yoke as megalith building grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Fabienne Pigière and Jessica Smyth, “First Evidence for Cattle Traction in Middle Neolithic Ireland: A Pivotal Element for Resource Exploitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0279556, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0279556. [Open access]","Richard P. Evershed et al., “Dairying, Diseases and the Evolution of Lactase Persistence in Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 608, no. 7922 (2022): 336–45, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/7615474. [Open access]","Ashley Scott et al., “Emergence and Intensification of Dairying in the Caucasus and Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 6, no. 6 (2022): 813–22, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-022-01701-6. [Open access]","Lee R. Alacoque, Richard W. Bulliet, and Kai A. James, “Reconstructing the Invention of the Wheel Using Computational Structural Analysis and Design,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 11, no. 10 (2024): 240373, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.240373. [Open access]","Damla Kaptan et al., “The Population History of Domestic Sheep Revealed by Paleogenomes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 41, no. 10 (2024): msae158, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msae158. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","technology"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/0854_Ein_Krug_aus_Bronocice%2C_3.550_v._Chr..JPG/1920px-0854_Ein_Krug_aus_Bronocice%2C_3.550_v._Chr..JPG","title":"The Bronocice pot","desc":"A burnished brown clay vessel with a sharply angled lower body and a flaring rim, standing on a small metal support in a museum case; incised round its upper zone are schematic four-wheeled wagons, each a rectangle with a circle at every corner and a peaked outline above, beside a fir-tree motif and a zigzag band. Silar, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0854_Ein_Krug_aus_Bronocice,_3.550_v._Chr..JPG","alt":"A burnished brown clay vessel with a sharply angled lower body and a flaring rim, standing on a small metal support in a museum case; incised round its upper zone are schematic four-wheeled wagons, each a rectangle with a circle at every corner and a peaked outline above, beside a fir-tree motif and a zigzag band"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the uses of a living animal matter so much?","a":"Because each changes what an animal is worth: one that ploughs opens heavy ground, one that pulls a cart moves bulk, and a fleece or a milking comes again next year rather than only once."},{"q":"Why has the single 4th-millennium horizon been abandoned?","a":"Because each of the four uses turns out to be far older somewhere — milk from the beginning of the Neolithic, cattle worked by about 8200 BCE in Southwest Asia and 5300 BCE in Catalonia — leaving no room for one transfer of know-how westward."},{"q":"Why is wool the hardest of the four to date?","a":"Because fleece almost never survives, so it is tracked through the sheep instead, whose spread across Europe was reshaped by a Bronze Age movement off the Pontic steppe."}]},
{"id":"wh-137","num":137,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Post-framed timber longhouses on loess soil and pots banded with incised lines are the marks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first farming society of central Europe.","questions":["Genomes from 250 people show that the households of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were patrilocal, women more often being the ones who had come from somewhere else.","Talheim, Vrable and Asparn-Schletz are massacre sites of the last century of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose radiocarbon counts fall away around 5000 BCE."],"answer":"Linear Pottery culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5500–5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second wave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">several centuries after 5500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000–4900 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Linear Pottery culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Linear Pottery culture</b> is the first farming society of central Europe, running from about 5500 to 5000 BCE and named for the incised bands that wrap its fine pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It grew out of the Starčevo culture of western Hungary and the Körös of the Hungarian plain, and it spread in two waves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, from about 5500 BCE, reached Slovakia, Austria, Moravia, Bohemia and Germany; the second, several centuries later, ran west to the Paris basin and Normandy and east into Poland, Ukraine, Moldova and Romania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its settlements cluster on the loess soils of river plains, its houses are post-framed timber longhouses, and across that whole range the material culture is strikingly uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cattle dominate the animal bones, and the ages at which they were killed across 19 sites show a husbandry of milk and meat together, with mature beasts kept on, while milk fats in perforated Polish vessels point to cheese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The people descend chiefly from Anatolian farmers and carry, on average, about 5 per cent of the ancestry of Europe's own hunter-gatherers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That mixing began at once: at Brunn in Austria, among the oldest of these sites, two of three burials are of mixed descent and one is roughly half hunter-gatherer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Data from the genomes of 250 individuals show more structure down the male line than the female and more male relatives within a site, pointing to patrilocal households, as strontium had already suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eastern communities carried more hunter-gatherer ancestry than the western ones, so behind the shared pots lie two different histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ends around 5000 BCE in what the radiocarbon counts read as a collapse, and its last century leaves massacres: at least 26 people killed at Schöneck-Kilianstädten with clubs and arrows, their lower legs deliberately broken, and at Asparn-Schletz a group of dead too unrelated to have come from one village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Pere Gelabert et al., “Social and Genetic Diversity in First Farmers of Central Europe,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 9, no. 1 (2025): 53–64, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-024-02034-z. [Open access]","János Jakucs et al., “Between the Vinča and Linearbandkeramik Worlds: The Diversity of Practices and Identities in the 54th–53rd Centuries cal BC in Southwest Hungary and Beyond,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 29, no. 3 (2016): 267–336, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-016-9096-x. [Open access]","Rosalind E. Gillis et al., “The Evolution of Dual Meat and Milk Cattle Husbandry in Linearbandkeramik Societies,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 284, no. 1860 (2017): 20170905, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2017.0905. [Open access]","Alexey G. Nikitin et al., “Interactions between Earliest Linearbandkeramik Farmers and Central European Hunter Gatherers at the Dawn of European Neolithization,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 19544, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-56029-2. [Open access]","Christian Meyer et al., “The Massacre Mass Grave of Schöneck-Kilianstädten Reveals New Insights into Collective Violence in Early Neolithic Central Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 36 (2015): 11217–22, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/4568710. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","migration"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Linear_Pottery_001.jpg/1920px-Linear_Pottery_001.jpg","title":"A Linear Pottery vessel","desc":"A pale grey, almost spherical Neolithic bowl reassembled from its sherds with the joins still showing, incised with arcs that hang from the rim, rows of short jabbed dashes running beside them, and ladder-like bars of parallel lines across the belly. Willow, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linear_Pottery_001.jpg","alt":"A pale grey, almost spherical Neolithic bowl reassembled from its sherds with the joins still showing, incised with arcs that hang from the rim, rows of short jabbed dashes running beside them, and ladder-like bars of parallel lines across the belly"},"locator":{"name":"Linear Pottery culture","at":[8.9564,48.9328],"kind":"region","area":[[3,49.3],[4.5,50.9],[8,51.8],[12,52.4],[16.5,52.3],[21,51.5],[25.5,50.4],[28,48.8],[26,47.2],[22,46.6],[18,46.9],[14,47.4],[10.5,47.9],[7,48.3],[4,48.4]]},"why":[{"q":"Why do genomes point to patrilocal households?","a":"Because data from 250 individuals show more structure down the male line than the female and more male relatives within a site, which is what strontium had already suggested."},{"q":"Why do two different histories lie behind the shared pottery?","a":"Because the eastern communities carried more hunter-gatherer ancestry than the western ones, even though the material culture is strikingly uniform across the whole range."},{"q":"Why is the culture's end read as a collapse?","a":"Because the radiocarbon counts fall sharply around 5000 BCE, and its last century leaves massacres — at least 26 killed at Schöneck-Kilianstädten with their lower legs deliberately broken, and unrelated dead at Asparn-Schletz."}]},
{"id":"wh-138","num":138,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"A dolmen, a passage grave, a standing stone and a stone circle are all built from the same thing, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name joins the Greek words for big and stone.","questions":["Modelling 2,410 radiocarbon dates puts the first European tombs built from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in northwest France in the second half of the 5th millennium BCE.","The Shamir field on the Golan Heights holds over 400 tombs each roofed by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is one reminder that the tradition is not only European."],"answer":"megalith","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4800–4000 BCE, northwest France</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Passage graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 4000 BCE, along the Atlantic</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Scandinavia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">second half of the 4th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"megalith","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>megalith</b> is a large stone raised or set into a structure, from the Greek for big and for stone, and some 35,000 still stand in Europe, mostly near a coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They recur in a few forms across the whole range: dolmens, passage and gallery graves, single stones alone or set in circles and rows, and megalithic buildings, with the graves facing east or southeast toward the rising sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where they begin was settled by modelling 2,410 radiocarbon dates, which put the first megalithic graves in the second half of the 5th millennium BCE, and only northwest France has monumental building before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Passy graves in the Paris basin run to 280 metres (920 feet) with no stone chamber at all, and the tumulus of Saint-Michel at Carnac was begun between 4782 and 4594 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From there the idea travelled by sea: thousands of passage graves went up along the Atlantic coasts in the first half of the 4th millennium, and the tradition reached Scandinavia in the second half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That reversed a long argument: a single origin spreading out of the Near East gave way to a picture of several independent centres, which has now given way again to one origin and a spread by sea, running the opposite way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genomes from 24 people buried in five northwestern tombs show close kin among them and more men than women, which points to patrilineal use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rule is not general: at Panoría in southern Spain 44 individuals were sexed by bone, DNA and protein together, and there are twice as many women as men however the count is cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor was the rite fixed: a chamber at Los Milanes held 28,740 burnt bone fragments from at least 21 people, cremated elsewhere in the 28th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor is any of it only European: such structures run from Ireland to Korea, and over 400 dolmens stand in the Shamir field on the Golan, one roofed by a basalt capstone of about 50 tonnes (55 tons).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["B. Schulz Paulsson, “Radiocarbon Dates and Bayesian Modeling Support Maritime Diffusion Model for Megaliths in Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 9 (2019): 3460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1813268116. [Open access]","Federico Sánchez-Quinto et al., “Megalithic Tombs in Western and Northern Neolithic Europe Were Linked to a Kindred Society,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 19 (2019): 9469–74, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1818037116. [Open access]","Marta Díaz-Zorita Bonilla et al., “Female Sex Bias in Iberian Megalithic Societies through Bioarchaeology, aDNA and Proteomics,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 21818, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-72148-x. [Open access]","Gonen Sharon et al., “Monumental Megalithic Burial and Rock Art Tell a New Story about the Levant Intermediate Bronze ‘Dark Ages’,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 3 (2017): e0172969, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0172969. [Open access]","Paula Becerra Fuello et al., “Funerary Practices of Cremation at the Megalithic Societies of South-Eastern Iberia: The Cemetery of Los Milanes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0330771, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0330771. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","prehistory","archaeology","history","religion","building"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Straight_lines_of_stones_%2843201526792%29.jpg/1920px-Straight_lines_of_stones_%2843201526792%29.jpg","title":"Megalith","desc":"The Carnac stones are an exceptionally dense collection of megalithic sites around the village of Carnac in Brittany, consisting of alignments, dolmens, tumuli and single menhirs. Carol, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Straight_lines_of_stones_(43201526792).jpg","alt":"Rows of standing stones receding across rough grassland at Carnac in Brittany"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the account of where megaliths began reverse twice?","a":"A single Near Eastern origin gave way to several independent centres, and modelling 2,410 radiocarbon dates has since given way again to one origin — northwest France — spreading by sea, running the opposite way."},{"q":"Why can megaliths not be said to record a patrilineal rule?","a":"Because although 24 people in five northwestern tombs are close kin with more men than women, at Panoría in southern Spain there are twice as many women as men however the count is cut."},{"q":"Why is the rite behind them not a fixed one?","a":"Because a chamber at Los Milanes held 28,740 burnt bone fragments from at least 21 people cremated elsewhere in the 28th century BCE, rather than the inhumations found in other tombs."}]},
{"id":"wh-139","num":139,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"Fifty of the 52 surviving sarsens at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> share a chemistry matching West Woods, about 25 kilometres (15 miles) north on the Marlborough Downs.","questions":["The Altar Stone at the centre of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Old Red Sandstone from northeast Scotland, at least 750 kilometres (470 miles) away and most likely carried by sea.","Durrington Walls, a settlement lived in for perhaps a decade around 2500 BCE, stands 2.8 kilometres (1.7 miles) from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was probably where its builders camped."],"answer":"Stonehenge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3180–2965 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sarsens raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-3rd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Builders' camp</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2535–2475 BCE, Durrington Walls</span></div>","answerText":"Stonehenge","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Stonehenge</b> is a stone circle on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, built and rebuilt over some six centuries, and it is made of two quite different kinds of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The great sarsens are blocks of silcrete: a typical upright runs 6 to 7 metres (20 to 23 feet) including the buried part and weighs about 20 tonnes (22 tons), the tallest standing 9.1 metres (30 feet); of an original 80 or so, 52 remain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifty of those 52 share one chemistry, which matches the sarsens of West Woods in the Marlborough Downs, about 25 kilometres (15 miles) north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smaller bluestones were raised earlier and come from the Preseli Hills of west Wales, over 200 kilometres (125 miles) away, some of them traced to particular outcrops there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Altar Stone lying at the centre is odder still: a shaped block of 6 tonnes (6.6 tons) whose grains match the Old Red Sandstone of northeast Scotland, at least 750 kilometres (470 miles) off and almost certainly brought by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was also a graveyard, and from early on: the cremated remains of up to 58 people were dug out of it, which makes it one of the largest Late Neolithic burial sites known in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those burials date between 3180–2965 and 2565–2380 BCE, and of the 25 individuals whose bone could be measured at least 10 had not lived on the chalk, west Wales being the likeliest home — the bluestone country again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The people who raised the sarsens seem to have camped at Durrington Walls, 2.8 kilometres (1.7 miles) away, a settlement of 2535 to 2475 BCE that may have stood for no more than a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its animal bones and the residues in its pots make it a place of feasting, and especially in winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was the ground empty before any of this, since hunter-gatherers had long gathered at Blick Mead on the edge of it, and whether the monument went up in closed forest or in a clearing that already mattered is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["David J. Nash et al., “Origins of the Sarsen Megaliths at Stonehenge,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 31 (2020): eabc0133, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abc0133. [Open access]","Anthony J. I. Clarke et al., “A Scottish Provenance for the Altar Stone of Stonehenge,” <i>Nature</i> 632, no. 8025 (2024): 570–75, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-07652-1. [Open access]","Christophe Snoeck et al., “Strontium Isotope Analysis on Cremated Human Remains from Stonehenge Support Links with West Wales,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 10790, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-28969-8. [Open access]","Piers D. Mitchell et al., “Intestinal Parasites in the Neolithic Population Who Built Stonehenge (Durrington Walls, 2500 BCE),” <i>Parasitology</i> 149, no. 8 (2022): 1027–33, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0031182022000476. [Open access]","Samuel M. Hudson et al., “Life before Stonehenge: The Hunter-Gatherer Occupation and Environment of Blick Mead Revealed by sedaDNA, Pollen and Spores,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266789, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0266789. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","religion","building"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Stonehenge_Closeup_July_2018.jpg/1920px-Stonehenge_Closeup_July_2018.jpg","title":"Stonehenge","desc":"Standing sarsen uprights with several lintels still resting across them and other stones fallen among drought-browned grass, under a hazy summer sky, a bird perched on one upright and visitors small in the distance. Mball93, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stonehenge_Closeup_July_2018.jpg","alt":"Standing sarsen uprights with several lintels still resting across them and other stones fallen among drought-browned grass, under a hazy summer sky, a bird perched on one upright and visitors small in the distance"},"locator":{"name":"Stonehenge","at":[-1.8261,51.1789]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the Altar Stone stand apart from the rest of the monument?","a":"Because its grains match the Old Red Sandstone of northeast Scotland, at least 750 kilometres off and almost certainly brought by sea, where the sarsens come from 25 kilometres away and the bluestones from west Wales."},{"q":"Why is Stonehenge described as a graveyard as well as a circle?","a":"Because the cremated remains of up to 58 people were dug out of it, dating between about 3180 and 2380 BCE, which makes it one of the largest Late Neolithic burial sites known in Britain."},{"q":"Why does Durrington Walls matter for reading the sarsen phase?","a":"Because the people who raised the sarsens seem to have camped there, 2.8 kilometres away, and its animal bones and pot residues make it a place of feasting, especially in winter."}]},
{"id":"wh-140","num":140,"category":"The Neolithic transition","question":"An arrowhead between his rib cage and left shoulder blade is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died, high in the Ötztal Alps some 5,300 years ago.","questions":["The copper blade of the axe found with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from ore in southern Tuscany, though Alpine copper was known and worked at the time.","Sequenced at high coverage, the genome of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows no Steppe ancestry at all and the most Anatolian farmer ancestry of any European of his day."],"answer":"Ötzi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3350–3120 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the early Copper Age, 4th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Ötzi","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ötzi</b> is a man who died high in the Ötztal Alps, on what is now the Italian–Austrian border, between 3350 and 3120 BCE, and whose body froze into a glacier — one of the oldest such mummies known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His gear went into the ice with him, including a hand axe whose wooden shaft can be radiocarbon dated, which makes it one of the few copper objects firmly fixed to the early Copper Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lead isotopes in that blade point to ore from southern Tuscany, though Alpine copper was known and worked then, so metal was moving a long way and the mountains divided one metal circuit from another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His genome, read at 15-fold coverage, carries no detectable Steppe ancestry and the highest Anatolian-farmer ancestry of any European of his time, which points to a rather isolated Alpine population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also says he had darker skin than Europeans today and carried the variants for male-pattern baldness, which the mummy itself bears out in its pigmentation and bare head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He was killed, and the manner is still being worked out: an arrowhead sits between his rib cage and left shoulder blade, under a deep wound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first reading was that he bled out from a torn artery under the collarbone; a fresh study of the scans finds a pooled clot of about 110 millilitres (4 fluid ounces), so the bleeding need not have killed him at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same work reconstructs the moment: a straight arrow path fits both the notch in the shoulder blade and the tear in the artery, fixing how he stood when it struck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ice that kept him is not much older than he is: a dome on a summit near where he lay has been frozen about 5,900 years, so the high Alps were coming out of nearly ice-free conditions just before his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even his gut survives, holding anaerobic bacteria whose nearest matches today are the gut communities of people living outside the industrialised world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Ke Wang et al., “High-Coverage Genome of the Tyrolean Iceman Reveals Unusually High Anatolian Farmer Ancestry,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 3, no. 9 (2023): 100377, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xgen.2023.100377. [Open access]","Gilberto Artioli et al., “Long-Distance Connections in the Copper Age: New Evidence from the Alpine Iceman's Copper Axe,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0179263, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0179263. [Open access]","Chiara Villa et al., “Ötzi the Iceman: Forensic 3D Reconstructions of a 5300-Year-Ago Murder Case,” <i>International Journal of Legal Medicine</i> 139, no. 5 (2025): 2263–71, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00414-025-03510-5. [Open access]","Pascal Bohleber et al., “New Glacier Evidence for Ice-Free Summits during the Life of the Tyrolean Iceman,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 20513, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-77518-9. [Open access]","Mohamed S. Sarhan et al., “The Iceman's Microbiome: Unveiling Millennia of Microbial Diversity and Continuity,” <i>Microbiome</i> 14 (2026): 135, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-026-02417-6. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","prehistory","archaeology","history","genetics","technology"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/%C3%96tztal_Alps.jpg/1920px-%C3%96tztal_Alps.jpg","title":"The Ötztal Alps","desc":"A wide view across the Ötztal Alps from a high ridge: broad glaciers filling the valleys on the left and right, dark rock spurs rising between them, snow-streaked peaks running to the horizon and heaped cumulus overhead. Tiia Monto, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%96tztal_Alps.jpg","alt":"A wide view across the Ötztal Alps from a high ridge: broad glaciers filling the valleys on the left and right, dark rock spurs rising between them, snow-streaked peaks running to the horizon and heaped cumulus overhead"},"why":[{"q":"Why is Ötzi's axe unusually valuable for dating the Copper Age?","a":"Because its wooden shaft can be radiocarbon dated, which makes it one of the few copper objects firmly fixed to the early Copper Age rather than dated by association."},{"q":"Why does his genome point to an isolated population?","a":"Because it carries no detectable Steppe ancestry and the highest Anatolian-farmer ancestry of any European of his time, which is what a rather isolated Alpine group looks like."},{"q":"Why is the manner of his death still being worked out?","a":"Because the first reading was that he bled out from a torn artery, while a fresh study of the scans finds a pooled clot of about 110 millilitres, so the bleeding need not have killed him at once."}]},
{"id":"wh-141","num":141,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Two routes carried farming across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: one inland through the Balkans and up the Danube, the other along the northern Mediterranean coast by sea.","questions":["More than 90 per cent of the ancestry of the first farmers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> traces to a single Anatolian source, so the change was a movement of people.","Around 3000 BCE the farming societies of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> faltered: megalith building stopped, numbers fell, and at Bury near Paris one population gave way to another."],"answer":"Neolithic Europe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spread</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6600 – 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Decline</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Neolithic Europe","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Neolithic Europe</b> begins with the movement of farmers out of western Anatolia and the Aegean about 8,600 years ago, into a continent where everyone still lived by hunting and gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming travelled by two routes: an inland one through the Balkans and up the Danube and the Rhine, and a sea route along the northern Mediterranean coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two moved at different speeds, pioneering households shifting about 50 kilometres (31 miles) a generation inland against 70 (43 miles) along the coast, and the whole spread ran until roughly 4000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a movement of people rather than of ideas: over 90 per cent of the ancestry of Europe's early farmers traces to one Anatolian source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor was the advance steady, since it stalled for centuries at several frontiers, Britain, Sweden and the middle Danube among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Farmers and foragers then lived alongside each other for a long time — more than 3,000 years between the Balkans and Denmark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mixing was rarer than that overlap suggests: on both routes alike about 3.6 per cent of farmers took a hunter-gatherer partner or brought one into the farming life, so geography changed how far people moved but not how they behaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did grow with time, the local share of hunter-gatherer ancestry rising at each stage of the inland advance, and the incomers outnumbered the foragers about five to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 10 and 15 per cent of the ancestry of Europeans today is hunter-gatherer, the residue of that slow absorption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Numbers also rose and crashed repeatedly, on a rhythm climate change cannot account for and crowding-driven conflict can;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> around 3000 BCE megalith building stopped, and at Bury near Paris one population gave way to another amid forest regrowth and plague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Alexandros Tsoupas et al., “Local Increases in Admixture with Hunter-Gatherers Followed the Initial Expansion of Neolithic Farmers across Continental Europe,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 34 (2025): eadq9976, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adq9976. [Open access]","Joaquim Fort and Joaquim Pérez-Losada, “Interbreeding between Farmers and Hunter-Gatherers along the Inland and Mediterranean Routes of Neolithic Spread in Europe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 7032, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-51335-4. [Open access]","Troy M. LaPolice, Matthew P. Williams, and Christian D. Huber, “Modeling the European Neolithic Expansion Suggests Predominant Within-Group Mating and Limited Cultural Transmission,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 7905, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63172-0. [Open access]","Dániel Kondor et al., “Explaining Population Booms and Busts in Mid-Holocene Europe,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 9310, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-35920-z. [Open access]","Frederik Valeur Seersholm et al., “Population Discontinuity in the Paris Basin Linked to Evidence of the Neolithic Decline,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 10 (2026): 677–88, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-026-03027-z. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","genetics","migration"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Expansion_of_farming_in_western_Eurasia%2C_9600%E2%80%934000_BCE.png/1920px-Expansion_of_farming_in_western_Eurasia%2C_9600%E2%80%934000_BCE.png","title":"The expansion of farming in western Eurasia, 9600–4000 BCE","desc":"A relief map from the Levant to Ireland, coloured in bands that spread outwards from a dark red core in the Fertile Crescent through orange in the Balkans to green across central Europe, blue along the Mediterranean and yellow in Britain, Ireland and Denmark, with dashed arrows tracing the routes and a calendar year against each band. Gronenborn, Horejs, Börner and Ober, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expansion_of_farming_in_western_Eurasia,_9600%E2%80%934000_BCE.png","alt":"A relief map from the Levant to Ireland, coloured in bands that spread outwards from a dark red core in the Fertile Crescent through orange in the Balkans to green across central Europe, blue along the Mediterranean and yellow in Britain, Ireland and Denmark, with dashed arrows tracing the routes and a calendar year against each band"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the spread of farming read as a movement of people?","a":"Because over 90 per cent of the ancestry of Europe's early farmers traces to one Anatolian source, rather than to the foragers who were already there taking up a new practice."},{"q":"Why does the long overlap with foragers not imply much mixing?","a":"Because on both routes alike only about 3.6 per cent of farmers took a hunter-gatherer partner, even though the two ways of life ran alongside each other for more than 3,000 years."},{"q":"Why does geography not explain how the two peoples behaved?","a":"Because the inland and coastal routes moved at different speeds — about 50 kilometres a generation against 70 — yet the rate of mixing was the same on both."}]},
{"id":"wh-142","num":142,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The stone dresser, the slabbed beds and the hearth still standing inside the houses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survive because the fittings there were built of stone rather than of wood.","questions":["Burnt vole and mouse bones in the household waste at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the earliest evidence in Europe of Neolithic people eating rodents or keeping them down as pests.","Founded around 2900 BCE and settled again in the 28th century, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands on the Bay of Skaill in Orkney with its houses banked about by their own midden."],"answer":"Skara Brae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2900 – 2400 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Skara Brae","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Skara Brae</b> is a village of stone houses on the Bay of Skaill in Orkney, lived in through the late 4th and early 3rd millennia BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The houses are joined to one another and banked about with thick midden — the settlement's own household waste — which held the walls and made the whole village one low mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ways between them were roofed with lintels and buried under that same midden, so people passed from house to house under cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fittings inside were built of stone rather than wood and have therefore survived: a dresser of shelves, beds framed in slabs, and a hearth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Drains run beneath the floors, laid to keep rainwater and soakage out of the houses rather than to carry sewage away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The bones in those middens are almost all food: cattle above all, sheep in quantity, pig rarely and red deer hardly at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people also took the small mammals living among them, and burnt vole and mouse bones lie in the household waste in numbers that only deliberate collection explains — the earliest evidence in Europe of Neolithic people eating rodents or keeping them down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both animals had been carried to the islands by people, and the vole, cut off there for more than 5,000 years, grew to an exceptional size on a genome that lost most of its diversity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The village belongs to an Orkney that from about 3200 BCE was the most crowded and inventive corner of Britain, sending new monuments and pottery styles south through Britain and Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Founded in the centuries around 2900 BCE, it seems to have been left after a short time and settled again in the 28th century BCE; around 2800 BCE the heart of Neolithic Orkney fell quiet, and its villages were finally abandoned in the 24th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Alex Bayliss et al., “Islands of History: The Late Neolithic Timescape of Orkney,” <i>Antiquity</i> 91, no. 359 (2017): 1171–88, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.140. [Open access]","V. Gordon Childe, D. M. S. Watson, and A. Robinson, “Final Report on the Operations at Skara Brae,” <i>Proceedings of the Society of Antiquaries of Scotland</i> 65 (1931): 27–77, https://doi.org/10.9750/PSAS.065.27.77. [Open access]","Andrzej A. Romaniuk et al., “Rodents: Food or Pests in Neolithic Orkney,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 3, no. 10 (2016): 160514, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.160514. [Open access]","Katharina Dulias et al., “Ancient DNA at the Edge of the World: Continental Immigration and the Persistence of Neolithic Male Lineages in Bronze Age Orkney,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 8 (2022): e2108001119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2108001119. [Open access]","Xuejing Wang and Gerald Heckel, “Genome-Wide Relaxation of Selection and the Evolution of the Island Syndrome in Orkney Voles,” <i>Genome Research</i> 34, no. 6 (2024): 851–62, https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.278487.123. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","building","nature"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Skara_Brae_house_1.jpg/1920px-Skara_Brae_house_1.jpg","title":"House 1 at Skara Brae","desc":"An excavated drystone house seen from above: curving walls of thin flat slabs standing chest-high, two low square openings into side cells, upright slabs framing bed spaces on the sand floor, grass along the rim and visitors' feet at the top edge. Wknight94, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skara_Brae_house_1.jpg","alt":"An excavated drystone house seen from above: curving walls of thin flat slabs standing chest-high, two low square openings into side cells, upright slabs framing bed spaces on the sand floor, grass along the rim and visitors' feet at the top edge"},"locator":{"name":"Skara Brae","at":[-3.3417,59.0487]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the village's own rubbish become part of its architecture?","a":"Because the houses were banked about with thick midden, which held the walls and made the whole settlement one low mound, with the roofed ways between houses buried under the same material."},{"q":"Why do the fittings inside survive where those of other villages do not?","a":"Because the dresser, the beds and the hearth were built of stone rather than wood, so they did not rot away as timber furniture elsewhere has."},{"q":"Why are the burnt vole and mouse bones significant?","a":"Because they lie in the household waste in numbers only deliberate collection explains, which is the earliest evidence in Europe of Neolithic people eating rodents or keeping them down."}]},
{"id":"wh-143","num":143,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The passage at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is aligned on the solstice sunrise, and the chamber it lights is a cruciform one of three recesses inside a mound of 200,000 tonnes.","questions":["A skull fragment from the chamber of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from a man whose parents were full siblings or a parent and child, was read in 2020 as evidence of a dynastic elite.","Built above the River Boyne about 3200 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one of the developed Irish passage tombs raised well after the first farmers' timber houses."],"answer":"Newgrange","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3200 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chamber bone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3340 – 3020 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Newgrange","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Newgrange</b> is a passage tomb above the River Boyne in eastern Ireland, built and used in the later Neolithic, about 3200 to 3000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A passage tomb is a stone-lined passage running in to a chamber at its end under a round mound, usually ringed by a kerb; this one holds more than 200,000 tonnes of earth and stone, and its chamber is cruciform, with three recesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The passage is aligned on the solstice sunrise, and the chamber it lights would have held only a few people at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming had reached the island by sea centuries earlier, in an influx that left the first Irish farmers with genomes of chiefly Near Eastern origin, and its opening burst — a horizon of rectangular timber houses — falls between 3750 and 3600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Newgrange belongs to the developed passage tombs raised after 3400 BCE, so the great monuments come well after that boom rather than with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Genomes from megaliths along the Atlantic coast show close kin buried together, more men than women in the British Isles, and the same male lines recurring, which points to patrilineal kindreds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Ireland that kinship reaches between monuments as well as within them, so people buried in one tomb were related to those in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A fragment of skull from the Newgrange chamber, dated 3340 to 3020 BCE, is that of an adult man whose parents were either full siblings or a parent and child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such unions are recorded almost only among ruling families, so his genome was read as evidence of a dynastic elite; that reading is now disputed, one case being too little to show whether the practice was routine or even known of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chamber was dug over for treasure after its rediscovery in 1699 and the layer the bone came from held at least five people, and the critics read the shared ancestry as a reordering of social relations after 3600 BCE rather than a line of rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lara M. Cassidy et al., “A Dynastic Elite in Monumental Neolithic Society,” <i>Nature</i> 582, no. 7812 (2020): 384–88, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-020-2378-6. [Open access]","Jessica Smyth et al., “The ‘King’ of Newgrange? A Critical Analysis of a Neolithic Petrous Fragment from the Passage Tomb Chamber,” <i>Antiquity</i> 99, no. 405 (2025): 672–88, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2025.63. [Open access]","T. Rowan McLaughlin et al., “The Changing Face of Neolithic and Bronze Age Ireland: A Big Data Approach to the Settlement and Burial Records,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 29 (2016): 117–53, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-016-9093-0. [Open access]","Federico Sánchez-Quinto et al., “Megalithic Tombs in Western and Northern Neolithic Europe Were Linked to a Kindred Society,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 19 (2019): 9469–74, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1818037116. [Open access]","Lara M. Cassidy et al., “Neolithic and Bronze Age Migration to Ireland and Establishment of the Insular Atlantic Genome,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 2 (2016): 368–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4720318/. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","prehistory","archaeology","history","genetics","religion"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Newgrange_entrance_stone.jpg/1920px-Newgrange_entrance_stone.jpg","title":"The entrance stone at Newgrange","desc":"A long kerbstone lying on its side, its whole face pecked with linked spirals and lozenges, a vertical groove dividing it, with the dark opening of the passage and its lintel in the wall behind. Jal74, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newgrange_entrance_stone.jpg","alt":"A long kerbstone lying on its side, its whole face pecked with linked spirals and lozenges, a vertical groove dividing it, with the dark opening of the passage and its lintel in the wall behind"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the great passage tombs come well after farming reached Ireland?","a":"Because the opening burst of rectangular timber houses falls between 3750 and 3600 BCE, while developed passage tombs like Newgrange were raised only after 3400 BCE."},{"q":"Why was one skull fragment read as evidence of a dynastic elite?","a":"Because the adult man it came from had parents who were either full siblings or a parent and child, and such unions are recorded almost only among ruling families."},{"q":"Why is that reading disputed?","a":"Because one case is too little to show whether the practice was routine or even known of, the chamber was dug over for treasure in 1699, and the critics read the shared ancestry as a reordering of social relations after 3600 BCE."}]},
{"id":"wh-144","num":144,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"A third of all the gold from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from one grave, number 43, and another third from five rich burials that held no body at all.","questions":["Computer tomography has shown that the celebrated “mask graves” of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held no masks but clay figures about half life size, crushed flat by the earth.","In use for some 250 years between 4590 and 4340 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is usually read as the first clear evidence of ranked society in Europe."],"answer":"Varna necropolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4590 – 4340 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Complex ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4250 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Varna necropolis","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Varna necropolis</b> is a Copper Age cemetery on the Black Sea coast of Bulgaria, in use for about 250 years between 4590 and 4340 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 270 burials are known, and what was put in them is richer than anything else of that date in the Old World.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its gold is very unevenly spread: a third of all the gold objects come from a single grave, number 43, and a further third from five rich symbolic graves and deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A great many of the graves held no body at all, and there are so many of these that the cemetery probably served several settlements rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three of them contained a human head modelled in clay, and computer tomography has shown that the celebrated “mask graves” are nothing of the kind: what was buried was a figure about half life size, crushed flat by the weight of earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The people buried there belonged to the Kodžadermen-Gumelniţa-Karanovo VI complex, which at its widest around 4500 BCE reached from southern Ukraine to northern Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their genomes are those of the local Copper Age population, so the wealth was not carried in by newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metal was being pushed hard across the same region at the same time, and crucibles from the Lower Danube show lead ore smelted around 4400 to 4300 BCE, the earliest such experiment in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Varna is usually read as the first clear evidence of ranked society in Europe;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> that reading is argued with, since across five millennia in the Carpathian Basin the potential for inequality was rarely realised for long, and putting wealth in a grave is itself a way of taking it out of circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The complex thinned and then vanished around 4250 BCE, its settlements abandoned, and the cemetery had gone out of use a century or so before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Raiko Krauß et al., “Chronology and Development of the Chalcolithic Necropolis of Varna I,” <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 44 (2017): 282–305, https://doi.org/10.4312/dp.44.17. [Open access]","Sandra Penske et al., “Early Contact between Late Farming and Pastoralist Societies in Southeastern Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 620, no. 7973 (2023): 358–65, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06334-8. [Open access]","Svend Hansen et al., “The Earliest Lead Ore Processing in Europe: 5th Millennium BC Finds from Pietrele on the Lower Danube,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 4 (2019): e0214218, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0214218. [Open access]","Aurélien Tafani et al., “Tracing Chalcolithic Population Mobility Using Strontium Isotopes and Proteomics at Gumelniţa Site, Romania,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 23002, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-05671-0. [Open access]","Paul R. Duffy et al., “Five Thousand Years of Inequality in the Carpathian Basin,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 32 (2025): eadu0323, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adu0323. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","technology","art"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Varna_Chalcolithic_necropolis.jpg/1920px-Varna_Chalcolithic_necropolis.jpg","title":"Grave 1 of the Varna necropolis, a symbolic burial","desc":"A museum case holding the contents of one grave: gold rings, beads, crescents and a beaten gold sheet pinned out on a white board in the position they were worn, two heavy gold bowls and a gold-hafted axe below, strings of shell beads, flint blades, and two trays of excavated earth at the left. Ivano Giambattista, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varna_Chalcolithic_necropolis.jpg","alt":"A museum case holding the contents of one grave: gold rings, beads, crescents and a beaten gold sheet pinned out on a white board in the position they were worn, two heavy gold bowls and a gold-hafted axe below, strings of shell beads, flint blades, and two trays of excavated earth at the left"},"locator":{"name":"Varna necropolis","at":[27.8644,43.2131]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the cemetery thought to have served more than one settlement?","a":"Because a great many of its graves held no body at all, and there are so many of these that a single community is unlikely to account for them."},{"q":"Why are the famous mask graves not masks?","a":"Because computer tomography has shown that what was buried was a figure about half life size, crushed flat by the weight of earth rather than a face laid on a body."},{"q":"Why is Varna's status as the first ranked society in Europe argued with?","a":"Because across five millennia in the Carpathian Basin the potential for inequality was rarely realised for long, and putting wealth in a grave is itself a way of taking it out of circulation."}]},
{"id":"wh-145","num":145,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The oldest known copper smelting, slag from Belovode in eastern Serbia about 5000 BCE, falls early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the phase between the Neolithic and the Bronze Age.","questions":["The tin bronze of the Balkan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was nobody's invention: it came out of smelting ores that already carried copper and tin together.","Because the label follows local sequences, the centuries called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Balkans are still called Neolithic in southern Scandinavia, which the copper reached from Serbian mines."],"answer":"Chalcolithic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Balkans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6200 – 3700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest smelting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Chalcolithic","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Chalcolithic</b>, or Copper Age, is the phase between the Neolithic and the Bronze Age in which copper was worked; in the Balkans, where it is best studied, it runs from the first use of copper minerals about 6200 BCE to about 3700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a stage every society passed through, since metallurgy followed no single path, so the name is used where copper mattered and its dates differ by thousands of years from region to region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest known copper smelting is slag from Belovode in eastern Serbia, dated to about 5000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tin bronze of those centuries was nobody's invention: it came out of smelting ores that already carried copper and tin together, rather than from deliberately mixing two metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the southern Levant the earliest metal is a copper awl of the late 6th millennium BCE, buried at Tel Tsaf and made from ore that probably came from the Caucasus, so the technique arrived by exchange long before anyone there made metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rich hoards that make that region a centre of early metallurgy, Nahal Mishmar above all, belong to the second half of the 5th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lost-wax casting is older still and further east: the earliest object made that way is a copper amulet some 6,000 years old from Mehrgarh in Baluchistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Metal travelled a long way from its sources, and copper from Serbian mines reached the north European plain and southern Scandinavia between about 4100 and 3300 BCE, where those same objects are called Neolithic rather than Chalcolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether metalworking was invented once and carried outwards or arrived several times over was argued for a century, and radiocarbon dating undid the older answer by showing early European sites to be older than the Near Eastern ones they were derived from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The idea that metal made the elite has weakened with it, since in the Balkans metal production seems to follow how communities were organised rather than to have built a hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]","Christopher P. Thornton and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Introduction: The Beginnings of Metallurgy in Global Perspective,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 22 (2009): 181–84, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-009-9026-2. [Open access]","Yosef Garfinkel et al., “The Beginning of Metallurgy in the Southern Levant: A Late 6th Millennium CalBC Copper Awl from Tel Tsaf, Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): e92591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0092591. [Open access]","Mathieu Thoury et al., “High Spatial Dynamics-Photoluminescence Imaging Reveals the Metallurgy of the Earliest Lost-Wax Cast Object,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 7 (2016): 13356, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms13356. [Open access]","Jan Piet Brozio et al., “The Origin of Neolithic Copper on the Central Northern European Plain and in Southern Scandinavia: Connectivities on a European Scale,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 5 (2023): e0283007, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0283007. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","technology","science"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Copper_from_Hoard_of_Ritual_Objects%2C_Judean_Desert%2C_4500-3500_BC_%2828346798417%29.jpg/1920px-Copper_from_Hoard_of_Ritual_Objects%2C_Judean_Desert%2C_4500-3500_BC_%2828346798417%29.jpg","title":"Copper objects from the Judean Desert hoard, 4500–3500 BCE","desc":"Two large limestone and basalt mortars from Natufian Eynan, each too heavy to be carried about, in the Israel Museum. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copper_from_Hoard_of_Ritual_Objects,_Judean_Desert,_4500-3500_BC_(28346798417).jpg","alt":"Eight small dark copper vessels and mace-heads standing on a museum shelf against pale marble, each cast in a different shape — spiralled, ribbed, plain-necked, one with a flat disc collar — their surfaces smooth and glinting"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Chalcolithic not a stage every society passed through?","a":"Because metallurgy followed no single path, so the name is used where copper mattered and its dates differ by thousands of years from region to region."},{"q":"Why was the earliest tin bronze nobody's invention?","a":"Because it came out of smelting ores that already carried copper and tin together, rather than from anyone deliberately mixing two metals."},{"q":"Why did radiocarbon dating undo the older account of metallurgy's spread?","a":"Because it showed early European sites to be older than the Near Eastern ones they had been derived from, which removed the ground for a single invention carried outwards."}]},
{"id":"wh-146","num":146,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The first step in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs no fire at all: native copper and gold can be picked up as nuggets and hammered cold into shape.","questions":["Smelting is the threshold in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since it makes metal that was never metal in nature and wants about 1100 °C (2000 °F), which five or six blowpipes can reach.","Cold-hammered gold in a burial at Jiskairumoko shows that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began among low-level food producers, without hereditary elites or a farming surplus behind it."],"answer":"early metallurgy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Copper beads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 6200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Andean gold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2155 – 1936 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"early metallurgy","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Early metallurgy</b> begins before metal does: the first use of copper in the Balkans, from about 6200 BCE, is malachite and azurite ground into beads, minerals picked for their green and blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Working metal itself comes next and needs no fire either, since copper and gold occur native and can be hammered cold into shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest worked gold in the Americas is exactly that: cold-hammered native gold from a burial at Jiskairumoko in the Lake Titicaca basin, dated between 2155 and 1936 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its owners were low-level food producers, and the finding that matters there is a negative one — neither hereditary elites nor a farming surplus is needed before metalworking begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smelting is the real threshold, because it makes metal that was never metal in nature, and it wants around 1100 to 1200 °C (2000 to 2200 °F), which five or six blowpipes will reach without any furnace of the later kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The two uses of copper stayed apart for a long time, and in the Balkans the minerals chosen for beads are distinct from those chosen for smelting, a separation not yet identified in Anatolia or the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What early metal is mostly found doing is marking somebody out: the earliest metal object in the southern Levant, a small copper awl, was buried with a woman whose grave set her apart from her neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lead came early too, and an object of the late 5th millennium BCE from a cave in the Negev is almost pure lead, smelted from ore in the Taurus mountains and probably used as a spindle whorl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of these steps follows from the one before, and metallurgy took no single developmental path from one society to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole chain is now studied together — mining, fuel, smelting, casting, use and recycling — and the old question of whether metalworking was invented once has given way to asking how many times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mark Aldenderfer et al., “Four-Thousand-Year-Old Gold Artifacts from the Lake Titicaca Basin, Southern Peru,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 13 (2008): 5002–5, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0710937105. [Open access]","Miljana Radivojević and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Early Balkan Metallurgy: Origins, Evolution and Society, 6200–3700 BC,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 34 (2021): 195–278, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-021-09155-7. [Open access]","Yosef Garfinkel et al., “The Beginning of Metallurgy in the Southern Levant: A Late 6th Millennium CalBC Copper Awl from Tel Tsaf, Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): e92591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0092591. [Open access]","Naama Yahalom-Mack et al., “The Earliest Lead Object in the Levant,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 12 (2015): e0142948, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0142948. [Open access]","Christopher P. Thornton and Benjamin W. Roberts, “Introduction: The Beginnings of Metallurgy in Global Perspective,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 22 (2009): 181–84, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10963-009-9026-2. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","prehistory","archaeology","history","technology","science"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/1280px-NatCopper.jpg","title":"Native copper","desc":"A knobbly mass of bright metallic copper photographed against white, grown into branching lumps and rounded buds with holes through it, its surface pitted and reflecting warm orange highlights. Jonathan Zander, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NatCopper.jpg","alt":"A knobbly mass of bright metallic copper photographed against white, grown into branching lumps and rounded buds with holes through it, its surface pitted and reflecting warm orange highlights"},"why":[{"q":"Why is smelting called the real threshold rather than working metal?","a":"Because copper and gold occur native and can be hammered cold into shape, where smelting makes metal that was never metal in nature, needing around 1100 to 1200 °C."},{"q":"Why does the Jiskairumoko gold matter as a negative finding?","a":"Because its owners were low-level food producers, which shows that neither hereditary elites nor a farming surplus is needed before metalworking begins."},{"q":"Why has the old question about metallurgy's invention been replaced?","a":"Because none of its steps follows from the one before and it took no single developmental path between societies, so the question is now how many times it was invented rather than whether it was once."}]},
{"id":"wh-147","num":147,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Henan alone holds more than 3,000 sites of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose villages, painted pottery and millet fields spread from the middle Yellow River into Gansu, Hubei, Sichuan and beyond.","questions":["Genomes from twelve burials at Zhanmatun match those of the core region, which is why the spread of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read as a movement of people rather than of ideas alone.","Raised nitrogen in millet grains shows that the fields of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were manured, most likely with pig dung, on loess soils that carry very little nitrogen of their own."],"answer":"Yangshao culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Yangshao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3500 – 3000 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Yangshao culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Yangshao culture</b> is the great Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River, flourishing roughly between 5000 and 3000 BCE across what are now the provinces of Henan, Shaanxi and Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its scale is the first thing to grasp: Henan alone holds more than 3,000 Yangshao sites, and its reach crossed the middle Yellow River into Hebei, Gansu, Qinghai, Hubei, Ningxia and Sichuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What marks it out is a settled way of living — villages that stayed put, painted pottery, domestic pigs and dogs, and dryland farming of broomcorn and foxtail millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The farming was not everything at first, and carbon isotopes from early sites such as Banpo point to a mixed diet in which wild plants still counted for a good deal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pigs were fed on millet, and on more of it as the centuries passed, until at Xipo it dominated their diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Loess carries very little nitrogen of its own, so the raised nitrogen in millet grains of 5500 to 3500 years ago is evidence that the fields were manured, most likely with dung from those same pigs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Villages grew with the yields: the largest covered 10 to 12 hectares (25 to 30 acres) before 5000 BCE and as much as 70 (170 acres) by about 4000, and by the late Yangshao of 3500 to 3000 BCE regional centres stood over lesser settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Drink belonged to it too: vessels carry the residues of a fermentation starter made with Monascus mould, and people dancing in a ring are painted on a house floor at Dadiwan and on pottery basins further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from twelve burials at Zhanmatun, a site of one of its regional variants, are indistinguishable from Yangshao people of the core, which makes the spread a movement of people and not only of ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What came of it outlasts the pottery: Yangshao ancestry is one of the roots of East Asians today, and the first splitting of the Sino-Tibetan languages falls with the culture's push west and southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lei Sun et al., “The Demic Expansion of Yangshao Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 186, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12218936/. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens, Yijie Zhuang and Dorian Q. Fuller, “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Xin Wang et al., “Millet Manuring as a Driving Force for the Late Neolithic Agricultural Expansion of North China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 5552, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-23315-4. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Archaeological Evidence for Initial Migration of Neolithic Proto Sino-Tibetan Speakers from Yellow River Valley to Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 51 (2022): e2212006119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9907151/. [Open access]","Quan Zhang et al., “Stable Isotopes Reveal Intensive Pig Husbandry Practices in the Middle Yellow River Region by the Yangshao Period (7000–5000 BP),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257524, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257524. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","china"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/%E4%BA%BA%E9%9D%A2%E9%B1%BC%E7%BA%B9%E5%BD%A2%E5%BD%A9%E9%99%B6%E7%9B%8608737.jpg/1920px-%E4%BA%BA%E9%9D%A2%E9%B1%BC%E7%BA%B9%E5%BD%A2%E5%BD%A9%E9%99%B6%E7%9B%8608737.jpg","title":"Painted basin with human faces and fish, Banpo, Yangshao culture","desc":"Looking down into a wide orange-red earthenware basin, mended from sherds with pale fills, painted inside in black with two round human faces in pointed caps, a small fish at either side of each mouth, and two whole fish drawn between them. Augusthaiho, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%BA%E9%9D%A2%E9%B1%BC%E7%BA%B9%E5%BD%A2%E5%BD%A9%E9%99%B6%E7%9B%8608737.jpg","alt":"Looking down into a wide orange-red earthenware basin, mended from sherds with pale fills, painted inside in black with two round human faces in pointed caps, a small fish at either side of each mouth, and two whole fish drawn between them"},"locator":{"name":"Yangshao culture","at":[109.1,36.3],"kind":"region","area":[[105,35.6],[107,37.85],[110.5,38.5],[113.5,38],[115.5,36],[115,33.8],[112,32.8],[108.5,33.4],[105.5,34.2]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the raised nitrogen in Yangshao millet evidence of manuring?","a":"Because loess carries very little nitrogen of its own, so grains of 5500 to 3500 years ago enriched in it point to fields being fertilised, most likely with pig dung."},{"q":"Why is the culture's spread read as a movement of people?","a":"Because genomes from twelve burials at Zhanmatun, a site of one of its regional variants, are indistinguishable from Yangshao people of the core."},{"q":"Why did farming not mean the end of wild food there?","a":"Because carbon isotopes from early sites such as Banpo point to a mixed diet in which wild plants still counted for a good deal."}]},
{"id":"wh-148","num":148,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Cattle reached the Central Plains during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their shoulder blades were soon being heated until they cracked so that the cracks could be read.","questions":["Thin black pots turned on the wheel and walled towns such as Pingliangtai mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran across the Yellow River plains from about 5,000 to 4,000 years ago.","Diets across northern China turned from millet alone to a mixture during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and tooth decay rose sharply with it, at about the time of an abrupt global climate event."],"answer":"Longshan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cattle arrive</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2250 – 1950 BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Longshan culture","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Longshan culture</b> is the name given to the late Neolithic of the Yellow River plains, the period running from about 5,000 to 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is known first by its pots: thin black wares, polished and standardised and carrying the marks of the wheel, made alongside the grey and brown vessels of daily use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its settlements were walled, and Pingliangtai in Henan is one of them, standing among seasonally flooded wetlands at the close of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were also numerous: in the Songshan hills, a core area of early Chinese civilisation, 660 sites belong to these thousand years against 563 for the whole of the much longer Yangshao period before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cattle reached the Central Plains only now, between about 4,200 and 3,900 years ago, and brought with them a new use for a shoulder blade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The bone was heated until it cracked and the cracks were read as an answer, and scapulae worked that way have been found at Taosi and Zhoujiazhuang, long before the inscribed oracle bones of the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whole cattle were buried at Shantaisi and at Pingliangtai, so the animal carried a weight beyond meat across the plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What people ate was changing too: across northern and northwestern China the diet turned from millet alone to a mixture that included wheat, barley or rice at around 4,500 to 4,000 years ago, and tooth decay rose sharply thereafter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That turn falls on the abrupt global climate event of 4,200 years ago, which is why it is read as a response to a changing world rather than a change of taste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The largest settlement of these centuries stands outside the Longshan heartland: Shimao, on the Loess Plateau, a stone-walled town of some 4 square kilometres (1.5 square miles) whose tombs give pedigrees four generations deep down the male line, and whose east gate covered more than 80 human skulls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Linyu Xia et al., “Compositional Analysis of Longshan Period Pottery and Ceramic Raw Materials in the Yongcheng Region, Henan Province,” <i>Materials</i> 18, no. 12 (2025): 2681, https://doi.org/10.3390/ma18122681. [Open access]","Christina Cheung et al., “Stable Isotope and Dental Caries Data Reveal Abrupt Changes in Subsistence Economy in Ancient China in Response to Global Climate Change,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 7 (2019): e0218943, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218943. [Open access]","Xiaochen Pei et al., “The Dynamics of Bovini Exploitation Strategies on the Central Plains of China from the Middle Neolithic to the Bronze Age,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29616, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-81065-y. [Open access]","Zehui Chen et al., “Ancient DNA from Shimao City Records Kinship Practices in Neolithic China,” <i>Nature</i> 648, no. 8094 (2025): 659–67, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09799-x. [Open access]","Lijie Yan et al., “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Clustering Patterns of Settlements from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age (9000–3000 BP) in the Songshan Mountain Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0351644, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0351644. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","technology"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Cup_with_high_handle%2C_China%2C_Shandong_province%2C_Longshan_culture%2C_c._2500-2000_BC%2C_earthenware%2C_openwork_%28eggshell_type%29_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco%2C_CA_-_DSC07691.jpg/1920px-Cup_with_high_handle%2C_China%2C_Shandong_province%2C_Longshan_culture%2C_c._2500-2000_BC%2C_earthenware%2C_openwork_%28eggshell_type%29_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco%2C_CA_-_DSC07691.jpg","title":"Eggshell-ware stemmed cup, Shandong, Longshan culture, c. 2500–2000 BCE","desc":"A tall dark goblet standing against a grey wall: a wide flaring bowl on a slender neck above a swelling barrel-shaped stem cut through with rows of narrow vertical slits, on a spreading foot, its near-black surface patched with pale encrustation. Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cup_with_high_handle,_China,_Shandong_province,_Longshan_culture,_c._2500-2000_BC,_earthenware,_openwork_(eggshell_type)_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco,_CA_-_DSC07691.jpg","alt":"A tall dark goblet standing against a grey wall: a wide flaring bowl on a slender neck above a swelling barrel-shaped stem cut through with rows of narrow vertical slits, on a spreading foot, its near-black surface patched with pale encrustation"},"locator":{"name":"Longshan culture","at":[117.3542,36.7358],"kind":"region","area":[[108.5,34.5],[109.5,37.5],[112,38.5],[115,38],[118,37.8],[121,37.3],[122.3,36.4],[121,35.5],[118,34.5],[115,33.5],[112,33.2],[110,33.5]]},"why":[{"q":"Why does a cattle shoulder blade matter for the period?","a":"Because cattle reached the Central Plains only now, and the bone was heated until it cracked and the cracks read as an answer — divination at Taosi and Zhoujiazhuang long before the inscribed Shang oracle bones."},{"q":"Why is the change in diet read as a response rather than a preference?","a":"Because the turn from millet alone to a mixture including wheat, barley or rice falls on the abrupt global climate event of 4,200 years ago."},{"q":"Why is Shimao awkward for the usual account of the period?","a":"Because the largest settlement of these centuries stands outside the Longshan heartland — a stone-walled town of some 4 square kilometres on the Loess Plateau, with pedigrees four generations deep and over 80 skulls at its east gate."}]},
{"id":"wh-149","num":149,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted more than ten thousand years without farming, and ended only when rice and millet growers crossed from Korea about 3,000 years ago.","questions":["Shell mounds ring the coasts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and at Kawaji the share of sea food in the diet climbs through its later phases before falling away under the newcomers.","The lineage of a man buried at Ikawazu during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> parts from mainland East Asians earlier than East Asians and Native Americans part from one another."],"answer":"Jōmon period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 16,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Farming arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,000 years ago</span></div>","answerText":"Jōmon period","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Jōmon period</b> is the long hunter-fisher-gatherer age of the Japanese archipelago, beginning more than 16,000 years ago and running for over ten thousand years without farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its people are read as the direct descendants of those who carried Upper Palaeolithic stone tools into the islands some 38,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genome of a man buried at Ikawazu bears that out: his lineage parts from mainland East Asians earlier than East Asians and Native Americans part from each other, which is put at about 26,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sea was the mainstay, and shell mounds ring the coasts; in the burials at Kawaji on the Atsumi peninsula the share of marine food climbs through the later Jomon and falls away again in the centuries after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those same people knocked teeth out deliberately as a rite, and which teeth went changed from one phase to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Plants counted too: proteins from a soybean and from a nut of the walnut family survive in the charred crust inside a pot from Shimoyakebe, dated to about 2,900 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By then the population was large and settled, with hunting, foraging and cultivation all long established, so the farmers who crossed from Korea from about 3,000 years ago met a full country rather than an empty one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fats soaked into 260 pots say the meeting changed cooking only slowly: a marker for broomcorn millet turns up in the Korean vessels and in none of the Japanese ones, and food from the water went on being cooked in the new continental shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jomon bones are hard to sequence, because a warm wet climate and acid volcanic soils destroy DNA everywhere but the far north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people did not vanish with the period: the ancestry of Japanese today is modelled as three strands, one of them Jomon, and how much of it a person carries varies across the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]","Oliver E. Craig et al., “Lipid Residue Analysis Reveals Divergent Culinary Practices in Japan and Korea at the Dawn of Intensive Agriculture,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 30 (2025): e2504414122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2504414122. [Open access]","Soichiro Kusaka and Yasuhiro Yamada, “Temporal Changes in Diet and Mortuary Practices among the Jomon People Based on Human Skeletal Remains Excavated from the Kawaji Shell Mound in Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 133, no. 2 (2025): 79–88, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.250522. [Open access]","Takumi Nishiuchi et al., “Paleoproteomics of Pottery Food Residues Reveals Plant Cupin Superfamily Proteins Preserved for Thousands of Years,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 5 (2026): 115637, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115637. [Open access]","Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","japan","genetics"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Spouted_vessel_with_cord_marks%2C_Japan%2C_Hokkaido%2C_final_Jomon_period%2C_c._1000_BC%2C_earthenware_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07535.jpg/1920px-Spouted_vessel_with_cord_marks%2C_Japan%2C_Hokkaido%2C_final_Jomon_period%2C_c._1000_BC%2C_earthenware_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07535.jpg","title":"Spouted vessel with cord marks, Hokkaido, final Jōmon period, c. 1000 BCE","desc":"A tall dark goblet standing against a grey wall: a wide flaring bowl on a slender neck above a swelling barrel-shaped stem cut through with rows of narrow vertical slits, on a spreading foot, its near-black surface patched with pale encrustation. Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spouted_vessel_with_cord_marks,_Japan,_Hokkaido,_final_Jomon_period,_c._1000_BC,_earthenware_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07535.jpg","alt":"A squat brown earthenware pot on a pale museum surface, a narrow upright neck rising from a broad shouldered body covered in raised curling bands, with a short tubular spout at one side and a row of knobbed studs around the lower edge"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Jōmon lineage described as parting early from other East Asians?","a":"Because the genome of a man buried at Ikawazu splits from mainland East Asians earlier than East Asians and Native Americans split from each other, which is put at about 26,000 years ago."},{"q":"Why did the arrival of farmers change cooking only slowly?","a":"Because the fats in 260 pots show a broomcorn millet marker in the Korean vessels and none of the Japanese ones, with food from the water still cooked in the new continental shapes."},{"q":"Why are Jōmon bones hard to sequence?","a":"Because a warm wet climate and acid volcanic soils destroy DNA everywhere in the archipelago but the far north."}]},
{"id":"wh-150","num":150,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Radiocarbon on tooth enamel from 23 burials puts the aceramic cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 5200 and 4900 BCE, three thousand years later than the chronology it replaces.","questions":["Barley is more than nine tenths of the grain found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where it was taken in hand locally while the wheat beside it came from the west.","Zebu cattle were domesticated from the Indian aurochs quite separately from the cattle of the Fertile Crescent, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Balochistan is the reference point for that second event."],"answer":"Mehrgarh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemetery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5200 – 4900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pottery from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-5th millennium BCE</span></div>","answerText":"Mehrgarh","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mehrgarh</b> is a Neolithic complex on the Bolan River in Balochistan, Pakistan, and the only site in the north-west Indian subcontinent whose Neolithic deposits have been dug on any scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is usually named as the earliest evidence of farming in the subcontinent, and it stands on an alluvial fan in the hilly flanks rather than out on a river plain, water being what decided where people could live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its farming was half brought in and half its own: sheep, goats and wheat came from the west, while barley and zebu cattle were taken in hand locally, and barley is more than nine tenths of the grain recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That second half matters far beyond one site, because cattle were domesticated twice from two different wild oxen — taurine cattle in the Fertile Crescent and zebu from the Indian aurochs — and Mehrgarh is the reference point for the zebu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The imported crops came a long way, and a model of the eastward spread of wheat and barley slows most at the Zagros mountains, where the ground and the climate made the crossing hardest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The settlement itself is nine or ten metres (30 to 33 feet) of stacked mudbrick, houses built and abandoned, their ruins used for rubbish and for burial, then built over again, nine times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its dates have lately been overturned: radiocarbon on tooth enamel from 23 burials puts the aceramic cemetery between 5200 and 4900 BCE, lasting two to five centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronology it replaces began about 8000 BCE and ran for three thousand years, so the place has lost most of its depth in time while keeping every metre of its depth in earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the new dates farming reached the Indus valley late and by farmers moving in rather than beginning there, and pottery in what is now Pakistan starts no earlier than the middle of the 5th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing found so far ties the Neolithic of Mehrgarh to the Neolithic of China or of northern India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Benjamin Mutin et al., “New Radiocarbon Dates of Human Tooth Enamel Reveal a Late Appearance of Farming Life in the Indus Valley,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 11345, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92621-5. [Open access]","Kavita Gangal, Graeme R. Sarson and Anvar Shukurov, “The Near-Eastern Roots of the Neolithic in South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 5 (2014): e95714, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0095714. [Open access]","J. Bates et al., “Early to Mid-Holocene Land Use Transitions in South Asia: A New Archaeological Synthesis of Potential Human Impacts,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0313409, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0313409. [Open access]","Jonas Gregorio de Souza et al., “Environmental Effects on the Spread of the Neolithic Crop Package to South Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 7 (2022): e0268482, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0268482. [Open access]","Yiduan Liu et al., “The Genomic Landscape of Cattle: Domestication, Dispersal, and Adaptive Evolution,” <i>Animals</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 776, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani16050776. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","pakistan"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Mehrgarh.jpg/1920px-Mehrgarh.jpg","title":"The mound at Mehrgarh, Balochistan","desc":"A pale eroded cliff of mudbrick and silt at an archaeological mound, cut into blocks and deep recesses with horizontal bedding lines running through it, cobbles washed out at its foot and sparse scrub along the top under a bleached sky. mhtoori, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mehrgarh.jpg","alt":"A pale eroded cliff of mudbrick and silt at an archaeological mound, cut into blocks and deep recesses with horizontal bedding lines running through it, cobbles washed out at its foot and sparse scrub along the top under a bleached sky"},"locator":{"name":"Mehrgarh","at":[67.6167,29.3833]},"why":[{"q":"Why is Mehrgarh the reference point for zebu cattle?","a":"Because cattle were domesticated twice from two different wild oxen — taurine cattle in the Fertile Crescent and zebu from the Indian aurochs — and the zebu were taken in hand locally here, alongside barley."},{"q":"Why has the site lost most of its depth in time but none in earth?","a":"Because radiocarbon on tooth enamel from 23 burials puts the aceramic cemetery between 5200 and 4900 BCE, replacing a chronology that began about 8000 BCE, while the nine or ten metres of stacked mudbrick remain."},{"q":"Why do the new dates change how farming reached the Indus valley?","a":"Because on them farming arrives late and with farmers moving in rather than beginning there, and pottery in what is now Pakistan starts no earlier than the middle of the 5th millennium BCE."}]},
{"id":"gr-251","num":251,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristotle thought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> should have been paid for at public cost as in Crete, since a Spartan too poor to contribute lost his rights of citizenship.","questions":["Plutarch has candidates for one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> balloted on with pellets of soft bread dropped into a bowl, a pellet squeezed flat counting as a vote against.","Xenophon says the Spartans had once eaten privately at home, and that Lycurgus dragged them into the open by inventing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the ages were deliberately blended."],"answer":"syssitia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lycurgus, c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mess size</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 15 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>syssitia</b> were the compulsory common meals at which the adult male citizens of Sparta ate together, and Herodotus lists them among the institutions of war that Lycurgus was said to have founded, beside the <i>enomotiai</i> and <i>triekades</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon says the Spartans had once messed privately at home, and that Lycurgus, tracing more than half their misdemeanours to that custom, dragged them out of holes and corners into the broad daylight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His ordinance allowed food enough to guard against want without inducing repletion, and stopped unnecessary drinking while leaving men free to quench thirst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ages were blended so that the young learned from their elders, and the talk turned by custom on honourable acts done for the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the meal broke up they walked home in the dark without a torch, having to move as freely by night as by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch says the Cretans called them <i>andreia</i> and the Spartans <i>phiditia</i>, and that Lycurgus brought them in against luxury, so that a rich man could not display his means when he ate beside a poor one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They met in companies of fifteen, a few more or less, each man contributing monthly a set quota of barley meal, wine, cheese and figs, with a little money for relishes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A candidate was balloted on with pellets of soft bread dropped into a bowl, one squeezed flat counting against him, and a single such pellet kept him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle thought the arrangement badly made, since the meals should have been funded publicly as in Crete, whereas at Sparta the very poor could scarcely take part, and by ancient custom a man who could not contribute lost his rights of citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Phylarchus, quoted by Athenaeus, says that by the 3rd century BCE the Spartans had given up assembling for the <i>phiditia</i>, meeting instead over costly couches, foreign wines and foreign perfumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"syssitia","tags":["practice","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.65–66, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707.txt. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 5, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 10, 12, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9–2.10, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists</i> 4.141–142, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://www.attalus.org/old/athenaeus4.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why were the meals held in public rather than at home?","a":"Because more than half the Spartans' misdemeanours were traced to messing privately, so the ordinance dragged them out of holes and corners into the broad daylight."},{"q":"Why did a single squeezed pellet keep a man out?","a":"Because a candidate was balloted on with pellets of soft bread dropped into a bowl, and one flattened counted against him."},{"q":"Why did Aristotle think the arrangement badly made?","a":"Because the meals should have been funded publicly as in Crete, whereas here the very poor could scarcely take part and a man who could not contribute lost his rights of citizenship."}]},
{"id":"gr-252","num":252,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristotle reports a law that shamed selling one's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but allowed a man to give or bequeath it, so the land passed into the hands of a few.","questions":["Plutarch says each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielded seventy measures of barley a year for its holder and twelve for his wife, the helots tilling it and handing over the produce.","Agis IV proposed cutting Laconia into 4,500 shares, one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for each Spartan, with 15,000 more outside for the provincials, three centuries after Lycurgus was said to have done it first."],"answer":"kleros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lycurgus, c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>kleros</b> was the allotment of land that kept a Spartan citizen, worked for him by helots whose produce paid the dues that kept him in his mess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Lycurgus redistributed the land because wealth had gathered in a few hands, cutting the country of the <i>perioikoi</i> into 30,000 lots and that of Sparta itself into 9,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each was reckoned large enough to yield seventy measures of barley a year for its holder and twelve for his wife, with wine and oil in proportion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the helots owed is described two ways, as that fixed render and, in Pausanias, as half of all the produce of their fields, which he supports with Tyrtaeus on men worn like asses under great burdens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The holder did not work it himself, being forbidden every mechanical art, and Plutarch adds that the leisure this bought him was counted among the blessings Lycurgus had provided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius took the equal division of landed property, with the common diet, for one of the two things that kept Sparta free of civil strife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle, writing nearly two centuries before him, describes the opposite: some citizens holding very little and others a great deal, so that the land had passed into the hands of a few.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He blames a law that shamed the sale of an inheritance while letting anyone give or bequeath it, and reports that nearly two-fifths of the country was held by women through heiresses and large dowries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The consequence he draws is that a land able to maintain 1,500 cavalry and 30,000 hoplites had fewer than 1,000 citizens, so the city sank under a single defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Agis IV proposed dividing the land again in the 3rd century BCE, into 4,500 lots for Spartans and 15,000 outside them, Plutarch says most of Sparta's wealth was by then in the hands of its women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"kleros","tags":["concept","institution","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 8, 12, 24, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.14, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.48, vol. 3, trans. W. R. Paton (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Agis</i> 7–8, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Agis*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the holder of an allotment do no work on it?","a":"Because helots worked it and he was forbidden every mechanical art, the leisure that bought him being counted among the blessings Lycurgus had provided."},{"q":"Why do Polybius and Aristotle describe opposite systems?","a":"Because Polybius took equal division of land for one of the two things that kept Sparta free of civil strife, while Aristotle describes the land already passed into the hands of a few."},{"q":"Why does Aristotle blame the law of inheritance?","a":"Because it shamed the sale of an inheritance while letting anyone give or bequeath it, so that nearly two-fifths of the country was held by women through heiresses and large dowries."}]},
{"id":"gr-253","num":253,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says Lycurgus set slaves to make the clothes and ordered running and trials of strength for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, holding that a free woman's highest work was bearing children.","questions":["Aristotle complains that many things at Sparta were managed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and asks what difference it makes whether women rule or the rulers are ruled by them.","A foreign woman told Gorgo that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone rule their men, and Plutarch says she answered that they alone give birth to men."],"answer":"Spartan women","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Married at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 18</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2/5 by 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan women</b> of the citizen class were reared, married and endowed under rules unlike those of any other Greek city, and it is those rules rather than any individual life that the sources describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon says that elsewhere a girl was kept on the plainest food and set to work wool sitting still, and asks how women reared so could bear splendid children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lycurgus, he says, left the making of clothes to slaves and ordered bodily training for women as for men, with contests in running and strength, on the view that strong parents made stronger offspring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has the maidens exercise in running, wrestling, casting the discus and hurling the javelin, and go in processions and dance in tunics only while the young men watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marriage came at full growth rather than in childhood, and both writers describe an elderly husband bringing a younger man to his wife, or a man raising children by another's wife with that husband's consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Aristophanes put a Spartan woman on the Athenian stage in 411 BCE, greeted as handsome and strong enough to strangle a bull, which she says comes of doing gymnastics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato has an Athenian speaker call the Spartan lawgiver half a man for taking the utmost care of the males while letting the female sex live softly with no order of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aristotle is harsher, saying that many things at Sparta were managed by the women, and asking what difference it makes whether women rule or the rulers are ruled by them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds that they were useless when Thebes invaded and caused more confusion than the enemy, and that nearly two-fifths of the country was held by women through heiresses and dowries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch denies the charge outright, and gives the exchange in which a foreign woman tells Gorgo that Spartan women alone rule their men and Gorgo answers that they alone give birth to men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan women","tags":["people","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 1, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 14–15, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Lysistrata</i>, in <i>Aristophanes: The Eleven Comedies</i>, vol. 1 (London: printed for the Athenian Society, 1912), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Aristophanes:_The_Eleven_Comedies/Lysistrata. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i>, book 7, trans. Benjamin Jowett, Project Gutenberg ebook 1750, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1750/pg1750.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Spartan_running_girl_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Spartan_running_girl_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Spartan women","desc":"Bronze figure of a running girl, 520–500 BCE, Spartan, found at Prizren. The short chiton baring one breast matches the outfit Pausanias says was worn by athletes competing in the Heraean Games. British Museum 1876,0510.1.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spartan_running_girl_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A bronze figure of a girl in mid-stride, wearing a short chiton that bares one breast, mounted on a museum stand"},"why":[{"q":"Why were girls trained in the body rather than kept at the wool?","a":"Because the making of clothes was left to slaves and bodily training ordered for women as for men, on the view that strong parents made stronger offspring."},{"q":"Why do the sources disagree so sharply about their standing?","a":"Because Aristotle says many things at Sparta were managed by the women and asks what difference it makes whether women rule, while Plutarch denies the charge outright."},{"q":"Why does Plato's speaker call the lawgiver half a man?","a":"Because he took the utmost care of the males while leaving the female sex to live softly with no order of life."}]},
{"id":"gr-254","num":254,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Thucydides says the rich at Sparta dressed like the poor, an early sign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a city built as villages rather than adorned with temples and public buildings.","questions":["A rhetra of Lycurgus enforced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by ordaining that every house have its roof fashioned by the axe and its doors by the saw, and by no other tool.","Herodotus has Pausanias set a Persian banquet beside a Laconian meal to show his fellow commanders what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looked like against the fare Mardonius had enjoyed."],"answer":"Spartan austerity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Supposed from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan austerity</b> is the plainness of living that other Greeks took for the mark of Sparta, and Thucydides put it as a paradox: were Lacedaemon deserted, its remains would make posterity doubt its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city, he says, was neither built compactly nor adorned with magnificent temples and public buildings, but composed of villages after the old fashion of Hellas, though the Spartans held two-fifths of the Peloponnese and led the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that a modest style of dress was first adopted at Sparta, the rich doing their best to assimilate their way of life to that of the common people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says a rhetra of Lycurgus ordained that every house have its roof fashioned by the axe and its doors by the saw and by no other tool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nobody, he adds, carries silver-footed couches and gold cups into a house like that, so a man must proportion his couch to his house and the rest to the couch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon says money-making was barred outright, the coinage being such that ten minas needed a waggon to carry them, while gold and silver were liable to search and their holder to a penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At table the plainness was the black broth, which the elders prized so much that they left the meat to the young men, and which a king of Pontus is said to have bought a Spartan cook to make and then disliked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells how <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span>, after Plataea, had Mardonius' cooks lay out the Persian dinner and set his own servants to prepare a Laconian one beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pointing at the two, he asked the Greek commanders to observe the senselessness of a leader who had such fare and came to take away theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athenaeus preserves Phylarchus on how the thing ended, with the Spartans no longer assembling for the common meals and taking instead to costly couches, foreign wines and foreign perfumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan austerity","tags":["concept","practice","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.6, 1.10, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 12–13, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 7, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 9.82, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists</i> 4.141–142, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://www.attalus.org/old/athenaeus4.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"quote":{"book":"thucydides-peloponnesian-war","n":"1","cite":"Book 1.10","text":"<p>For I suppose if Lacedaemon were to become desolate, and the temples and the foundations of the public buildings were left, that as time went on there would be a strong disposition with posterity to refuse to accept her fame as a true exponent of her power. And yet they occupy two-fifths of Peloponnese and lead the whole, not to speak of their numerous allies without.</p>"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Thucydides think the city's remains would mislead posterity?","a":"Because it was composed of villages after the old fashion of Hellas, neither built compactly nor adorned with magnificent temples, though the Spartans held two-fifths of the Peloponnese."},{"q":"Why would a rule about axes and saws limit a man's furniture?","a":"Because nobody carries silver-footed couches and gold cups into a house roofed by the axe, so a man must proportion his couch to his house and the rest to the couch."},{"q":"Why did Pausanias set two dinners side by side after Plataea?","a":"Because he wanted the Greek commanders to observe the senselessness of a leader who had such fare and came to take away theirs."}]},
{"id":"gr-255","num":255,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Thucydides says that when a king was in the field every command passed down through the ranks, so that almost the whole <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> consisted of officers under officers.","questions":["At Mantinea the Argives came on with haste and fury while the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> advanced slowly to the music of many flute-players, a standing institution meant to keep the line even.","Xenophon credits Lycurgus with giving the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a crimson uniform and a heavy bronze shield, as the least womanish equipment and the quickest to burnish."],"answer":"Spartan army","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,000 Spartiates, 479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Broken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">371 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Spartan army</b> was the hoplite force of a city that trained for nothing else, and Herodotus has the exiled king Demaratus tell Xerxes that the Lacedaemonians are the best of all men when fighting in a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Though free, he says, they are not free in all things: Law is set over them as a master they fear more than the Persians fear their king, and it bids them never flee whatever the numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says every command proceeded from the king in the field and passed down the ranks, so that almost the whole army consisted of officers under officers, the care of what must be done falling upon many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that their numbers were unknown, the government being secret, so he reckons the force at Mantinea from the depth and frontage of its ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There the Argives came on with haste and fury while the Lacedaemonians advanced slowly to the music of many flute-players, a standing institution with nothing to do with religion, meant to keep the line even.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon credits Lycurgus with the crimson uniform and the heavy bronze shield, as an equipment with nothing womanish about it, quickest burnished and least readily soiled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that men past early manhood were allowed to wear their hair long, since it would make them look taller, freer and more terrible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says the young men's discipline was relaxed on campaign, so that they were the only men in the world for whom war brought a respite from training for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before battle the king sacrificed a she-goat and had the men set garlands on their heads while the pipers played the hymn to Castor and he led off a marching paean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle's verdict is that the whole constitution regarded one part of virtue only, the soldier's, so their power held while they were at war and fell when they gained empire, knowing nothing of the arts of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan army","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.66, 5.68, 5.70, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 11, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.104, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 22, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides have to estimate the army's size?","a":"Because its numbers were unknown, the government being secret, so he reckons the force at Mantinea from the depth and frontage of its ranks."},{"q":"Why did the flute-players march with the line?","a":"Because it was a standing institution with nothing to do with religion, meant to keep the line even as the men advanced slowly."},{"q":"Why does Aristotle judge the constitution a failure?","a":"Because it regarded one part of virtue only, the soldier's, so their power held while they were at war and fell when they gained empire, knowing nothing of the arts of peace."}]},
{"id":"gr-256","num":256,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says Lycurgus divided his citizen soldiers into six of the unit called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each under one polemarch, with four lochagoi and sixteen enomotarchs below him.","questions":["Plutarch says the size of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was disputed, at five hundred men according to Ephorus, seven hundred according to Callisthenes, and nine hundred according to Polybius.","When peltasts under Iphicrates destroyed one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outside Lechaeum, Xenophon put the death-roll at about 250 men, counting the second fight and the final flight."],"answer":"mora","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Six morai</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>mora</b> was the largest division of the classical Spartan army, and the two fullest ancient accounts of the units below it do not agree with each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus credits Lycurgus with establishing what appertained to war, naming enomoties and triecads and the common meals, so the smallest unit is the one the earliest source names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides gives the ladder as it worked at Mantinea, the king giving the word to the polemarchs, they to the lochages, these to the pentecostyes, these to the enomotarchs, and these to the enomoties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reckons the army's size from that ladder because its numbers were kept secret, counting seven lochoi in the field besides the Sciritae, four pentecostyes to a lochos and four enomoties to a pentecosty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The front rank of an enomoty was four men and the depth generally eight, though each captain chose for himself, which gave a front line of 448 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon instead has Lycurgus divide the citizen soldiers into six morai of cavalry and heavy infantry, each with one polemarch, four lochagoi, eight penteconters and sixteen enomotarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His lochos is therefore a quarter of a mora, where Thucydides names no mora at all and puts the lochos at the head of everything below it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon adds that the arrangement was far simpler than outsiders supposed, since the front-rank men are all leaders and the enomotarch passes the word that makes the line deeper or shallower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How many men a mora held was already disputed in antiquity, Plutarch reporting five hundred according to Ephorus, seven hundred according to Callisthenes and nine hundred according to Polybius among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Xenophon shows one in the field outside Lechaeum, caught by peltasts, its polemarch sending out first the ten-years-service men and then the fifteen, and closes with about 250 dead and the destruction of the Lacedaemonian mora.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"mora","tags":["institution","concept","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.66, 5.68, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 11, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.65–66, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pelopidas</i> 17, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pelopidas*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 4.5, trans. H. G. Dakyns, Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1174/pg1174.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why do the two fullest accounts of the army's units not agree?","a":"Because Xenophon makes the lochos a quarter of a mora, where Thucydides names no mora at all and puts the lochos at the head of everything below it."},{"q":"Why was the size of a mora disputed in antiquity itself?","a":"Because Plutarch reports five hundred according to Ephorus, seven hundred according to Callisthenes and nine hundred according to Polybius."},{"q":"Why did Xenophon think the arrangement simpler than outsiders supposed?","a":"Because the front-rank men are all leaders and the enomotarch passes the word that makes the line deeper or shallower."}]},
{"id":"gr-257","num":257,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xenophon says the ephors chose three hippagretai, each of whom picked a hundred men for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and had to explain whom he preferred in honour and whom he passed over.","questions":["Thucydides puts the three hundred <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as they were called, fighting round King Agis in the centre of the line at Mantinea while the cavalry held the wings.","Three hundred of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> escorted Themistocles as far as the Tegean border, and Herodotus says he was the only man to whom the Spartans ever gave such an escort."],"answer":"hippeis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>hippeis</b> were the three hundred picked Spartans who formed the king's guard in the field, and the word is the ordinary Greek for horsemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon says the ephors select three men in the prime of life called hippagretai, and that each chooses a hundred others, being bound to explain whom he prefers in honour and whom he disapproves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who fail are then at open war with the men who rejected them and with those chosen instead, each side watching for a slip against the code of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides shows them at Mantinea in the centre of the line, where the three hundred knights, as he calls them, fought round King Agis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His account of the same battle posts the cavalry on the two wings, so the three hundred stood among the hoplites whatever their name meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus has three hundred picked Spartans, the same who are called the horsemen, escort Themistocles out of Lacedaemon as far as the boundaries of Tegea, after they had honoured him with an olive wreath and their best chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds that Themistocles is the only man he knows of to whom the Spartans ever gave an escort on his way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The body turned over each year, since the five eldest passing out of it became the Well-doers, bound for that year to go wherever the state sent them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of them, Lichas, is the man Herodotus credits with finding the bones of Orestes at Tegea, having come to a forge there under truce and watched iron being worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The guard served at home as well as in the field, and when the ephors moved against the conspirator Cinadon they told their agent to get six or seven men from the eldest of the hippagretai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"hippeis","tags":["institution","people","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 4, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.67, 5.72, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 8.124, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.67–68, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2707/pg2707.txt. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 3.3, trans. H. G. Dakyns, Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1174/pg1174.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does a body called horsemen fight on foot?","a":"Because at Mantinea the three hundred stood in the centre of the line round King Agis, while the cavalry was posted on the two wings, whatever the name meant."},{"q":"Why did the selection leave the rejected at odds with the chosen?","a":"Because each hippagretes was bound to explain whom he preferred in honour and whom he disapproved, so both sides watched for a slip against the code."},{"q":"Why did the body turn over each year?","a":"Because the five eldest passing out of it became the Well-doers, bound for that year to go wherever the state sent them."}]},
{"id":"gr-258","num":258,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch says Lycurgus withdrew gold and silver and gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so trifling a value for its weight that ten minas' worth needed a store-room and a yoke of cattle to shift.","questions":["Polybius says that once Sparta campaigned outside the Peloponnese neither <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nor the barter of crops would serve, so the Spartans had to beg from the Persians.","Plutarch supposed that all the earliest money was like <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some peoples using spits for coins, which is why six obols, or 'spits', still make a drachma."],"answer":"iron money","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use until</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Iron money</b> was the currency of Sparta while the rest of Greece coined silver, and Plutarch credits Lycurgus with withdrawing gold and silver from use and ordaining it alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To a great weight of it he gave a trifling value, so that ten minas' worth wanted a large store-room in the house and a yoke of cattle to move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The iron was quenched red-hot in vinegar, which robbed it of its temper and left it brittle and worthless for any other purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon gives the same sum from the other side, saying ten minas could not come into a house without the master or one of his household noticing, since it would need a waggon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that gold and silver were themselves liable to search with a penalty on whoever was caught, and that freeborn citizens were forbidden to have anything to do with money-making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch draws the consequences: the money could not be carried into the rest of Greece nor valued there, so no merchant brought freight into their harbours and the luxury trades left with the old coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He supposed elsewhere that all the earliest money was of this kind, some peoples using iron spits, which is why six obols, or 'spits', still make a drachma, a 'handful'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius says the arrangement served while Sparta ruled its neighbours and failed the moment it campaigned abroad, which called for a currency in universal circulation and supplies drawn from outside Laconia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lysander then filled the city with wealth and the love of wealth, and the ephors, debating whether to purge it of gold and silver, resolved that the state might use coin while any private person holding it should die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle's verdict on the whole is that there was no money in the treasury though great wars had to be fought, and that the lawgiver had made his city poor and his citizens greedy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"iron money","tags":["object","concept","history","greece","archaic","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 9, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 7, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lysander</i> 2, 16–17, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lysander*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.49, vol. 3, trans. W. R. Paton (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why was the iron quenched in vinegar?","a":"Because it robbed the metal of its temper and left it brittle and worthless for any other purpose, so the money could not be turned into tools."},{"q":"Why did the arrangement isolate the city's trade?","a":"Because the money could not be carried into the rest of Greece nor valued there, so no merchant brought freight into their harbours and the luxury trades left."},{"q":"Why did the system fail once Sparta campaigned abroad?","a":"Because that called for a currency in universal circulation and supplies drawn from outside Laconia, which iron could not buy."}]},
{"id":"gr-259","num":259,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Athenaeus quotes Polycrates that the Lacedaemonians kept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for three days, wearing no garlands and eating no bread at the feasts because they were mourning Hyacinthus.","questions":["The alliance Athens swore with Sparta was to be renewed each year by Athenians coming to Lacedaemon for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with their pillar set beside the statue of Apollo at Amyclae.","Herodotus says the Spartans were keeping the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the envoys arrived, and were accused of disporting themselves while their allies were left to destruction."],"answer":"Hyacinthia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hyacinthia</b> was the festival of Apollo at Amyclae, the Laconian town whose sanctuary the Spartans kept as their own and named in their treaties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias calls the image there old and uncouth, a bronze pillar except for its face, feet and hands, wearing a helmet and holding a spear and a bow, and guesses its height at thirty cubits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its pedestal was fashioned in the shape of an altar, and they said Hyacinthus was buried in it, offerings going to him as to a hero through a bronze door on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The throne that carried the image was made by Bathycles of Magnesia, and Pausanias, who saw it, says that describing its reliefs one by one would only have bored his readers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the altar Hyacinthus is shown bearded and being carried up to heaven with his sister Polyboea, though a painter had given him instead the prime of youthful beauty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Athenaeus quotes Polycrates for the shape of the festival: three days, and for the mourning of Hyacinthus no garlands and no bread at the feasts, cheesecakes given out instead, and no paean sung to the god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The middle day was a spectacle instead, with boys playing the harp girt up in their tunics, riders crossing the theatre, choruses singing native poems, and girls carried in curved wooden chariots called <i>canathra</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Victims were sacrificed all that day, every citizen feasted his friends and his own slaves, and the city emptied itself to the show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus has the Spartans keeping the festival on the eve of Plataea, holding it of the greatest consequence to see to the god, while Athenian envoys accused them of disporting themselves and leaving their allies to destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon says the men of Amyclae came home for the sacred paean whatever campaign they were on, a custom no service abroad was allowed to interrupt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Hyacinthia","tags":["festival","practice","history","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.18–19, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists, or Banquet of the Learned</i> 4.139, vol. 1, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor01athe. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 9.7, 9.11, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.23, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 4.5, trans. H. G. Dakyns, Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1174/pg1174.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did the festival keep a day of mourning inside a feast?","a":"Because Hyacinthus was mourned first — no garlands, no bread, no paean to the god — before the middle day turned to spectacle and sacrifice."},{"q":"Why did the Athenians accuse the Spartans of disporting themselves?","a":"Because they were keeping the festival on the eve of Plataea, holding it of the greatest consequence to see to the god while their allies faced destruction."},{"q":"Why did no campaign interrupt it for the men of Amyclae?","a":"Because they came home for the sacred paean whatever service they were on, a custom no expedition was allowed to break."}]},
{"id":"gr-260","num":260,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Isocrates says the Spartans were still sacrificing at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his own day to Menelaus and Helen together, and doing it not as to heroes but as to gods.","questions":["Polybius says the hills the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands on are rocky and hard to climb, and command the strip of level ground between the city and the Eurotas.","A nurse carried an ugly child daily to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where a woman stroked her head and said she would be the fairest woman in Sparta, and so she became."],"answer":"Menelaion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze Age site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shrine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Menelaion</b> was the sanctuary of Menelaus and Helen at Therapne in Laconia, standing on high ground across the Eurotas from Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias records a temple of Menelaus there, says the place takes its name from a daughter of Lelex, and reports that Menelaus and Helen were buried in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gives the Rhodian version as well, in which Helen was driven out after Menelaus died and hanged on a tree by handmaidens dressed as Furies, which is why the Rhodians have a sanctuary of Helen of the Tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isocrates says the Spartans were still offering holy and traditional sacrifices to the pair at Therapne in his own day, and offering them not as to heroes but as to gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Homer had already given Menelaus a destiny apart, Proteus telling him that he would not die in Argos but be taken to the Elysian plain because he had married Helen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus puts a temple of Helen at Therapne above the temple of Phoebus, and tells of a nurse who carried an ugly child there day after day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She set her before the image and prayed the goddess to rid her of her unshapeliness, until a woman appeared, stroked the child's head and said she would be the fairest woman in Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From that day her aspect changed; she married Agetos, was afterwards taken from him by King Ariston through a trick of gifts, and became the mother of Demaratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius, who calls it the Menelaïum, says the hills it stands on are rocky, hard to climb and of considerable height, and command the level strip between the city and the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Philip V came down on Sparta, Lycurgus took post round it with not less than two thousand men and dammed the river to flood that strip, leaving one narrow passage under the hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Menelaion","tags":["place","building","history","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.19, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.61–63, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.21–22, vol. 3, trans. W. R. Paton (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Helen</i> 63, in <i>Isocrates</i>, vol. 3, trans. La Rue Van Hook (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1945), https://archive.org/details/isocrateswitheng03isocuoft. [Open access]","Homer, <i>The Odyssey</i> 4, trans. Samuel Butler, Project Gutenberg ebook 1727, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1727/pg1727.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Therapne%2C_Menelaion_from_NE_%281988%29_01.jpg/1920px-Therapne%2C_Menelaion_from_NE_%281988%29_01.jpg","title":"Menelaion","desc":"Therapne (Sparta). View of the Menelaion from the northeast, with Taygetos in the background.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Therapne,_Menelaion_from_NE_(1988)_01.jpg","alt":"Therapne, Menelaion from the north-east"},"locator":{"name":"Menelaion","at":[22.4541,37.0661],"within":"Sparta"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the cult at Therapne unusual for a hero shrine?","a":"Because Isocrates says the Spartans offered sacrifices to the pair not as to heroes but as to gods."},{"q":"Why does Homer already set Menelaus apart?","a":"Because Proteus tells him he will not die in Argos but be taken to the Elysian plain, because he had married Helen."},{"q":"Why did the hills matter in war as well as in cult?","a":"Because they are rocky, hard to climb and command the level strip between the city and the river, which is why Lycurgus took post there against Philip V."}]},
{"id":"gr-261","num":261,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pausanias says the priestess stood by holding the image of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which grew too heavy to carry whenever the scourgers spared the lash for a lad's beauty or high rank.","questions":["Xenophon says stealing as many cheeses as possible off the shrine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was encouraged, while others were told at the same moment to scourge whoever was caught.","Plutarch says he has seen youths expiring under the lash at the altar of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Cicero heard while at Sparta that boys were sometimes whipped to death there."],"answer":"Artemis Orthia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman theatre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Artemis Orthia</b> had her sanctuary at the place the Spartans called Limnaeum, 'the Marshy', and Pausanias renders the epithet Orthia as 'Upright'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says the image was called Lygodesma, 'willow-bound', as well, because it was found in a thicket of willows whose encircling withies made it stand upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias holds it to be the image Orestes and Iphigenia stole out of the Tauric land, and offers as evidence that Astrabacus and Alopecus went out of their minds the moment they found it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a second proof he gives the day the Limnatians, Cynosurians and the people of Mesoa and Pitane fell to quarrelling as they sacrificed, so that many were killed at the altar and the rest died of disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An oracle then told them to stain the altar with human blood, and a man taken by lot was sacrificed until Lycurgus changed the custom to a scourging of the lads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon, writing centuries earlier, describes something looser: stealing as many cheeses as possible off the shrine of Orthia while others were told to scourge the thief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Pausanias the priestess stands by holding the wooden image, which is small and light but grows too heavy to carry if the scourgers spare the lash for a lad's beauty or high rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he has himself seen youths expiring under the lash at the altar, and tells it as what makes credible the boy who let a stolen fox tear out his bowels rather than be caught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His Spartan sayings call the thing the Flagellation, held each year, the boys lashed all day and vying over who could bear the most blows, the winner held in especial repute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cicero, who had been to Sparta, says blood followed the lash in abundance and that he used to hear there they were sometimes whipped to death, and yet not one was ever heard to cry out or groan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Artemis Orthia","tags":["place","practice","history","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.16, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 2, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 18, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Instituta Laconica</i> 40, in <i>Moralia</i>, vol. 3, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Instituta_Laconica*.html. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Tusculan Disputations</i> 2.14, trans. C. D. Yonge, Project Gutenberg ebook 14988, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14988/pg14988.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/The_remains_of_the_Temple_of_Artemis_Orthia_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg/1920px-The_remains_of_the_Temple_of_Artemis_Orthia_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","title":"Artemis Orthia","desc":"The remains of the Temple of Artemis Orthia in Sparta. Laconia, Greece. George E.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_remains_of_the_Temple_of_Artemis_Orthia_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","alt":"Remains of the temple of Artemis Orthia in Sparta"},"locator":{"name":"Artemis Orthia","at":[22.435,37.083],"within":"Sparta"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the two chief accounts of the rite describe different things?","a":"Because Xenophon, writing centuries earlier, describes stealing cheeses off the shrine while others scourge the thief, where Pausanias and Plutarch describe a flagellation at the altar."},{"q":"Why does the priestess hold the image during the scourging?","a":"Because it is small and light but grows too heavy to carry if the scourgers spare the lash for a lad's beauty or high rank."},{"q":"Why does Pausanias offer proofs that the image is the Tauric one?","a":"Because the claim needs support: he cites Astrabacus and Alopecus going out of their minds on finding it, and the killing at the altar that led to an oracle demanding blood."}]},
{"id":"gr-262","num":262,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Critias praised the kothon, a shape in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as the cup most convenient on an expedition, because a soldier cannot easily see what he is drinking in it.","questions":["The finest cups of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were found chiefly in Etruria and later at Naukratis and on Samos, and were long grouped instead under the name Cyrenaic.","Polemo says the Lacedaemonians used vessels of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, drinking from them at festivals and victory feasts and at the marriage-feasts of their maidens."],"answer":"Laconian pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Finest phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 580 – 530 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Laconian pottery</b> is the earthenware of Sparta and its region, whose finest phase is a black-figure ware of the 6th century BCE made up mostly of cups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The shapes are slender kylikes owing much to metal originals, which shows in the palmettes set at the ends of their handles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Designs were painted in black on a slip running from deep buff to a pale cream, with firmly drawn incised lines and a plentiful use of purple for details.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A cup of about 550 BCE in New York, worked in the manner of the Arkesilas Painter, carries birds, bulls and Herakles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That painter is named from the Arkesilas cup in Paris, which shows the king of Kyrene aboard ship superintending the weighing of a cargo, with the figures labelled and a monkey, a lizard and a panther above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The plainer ware is what the written sources notice: Critias praised the Lacedaemonian kothon as the cup most convenient on an expedition and the easiest carried in a knapsack, its whole point being that a soldier must often drink water that is not clean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One cannot easily see what one is drinking in it, and its brim curves inward so as to hold back what is foul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch repeats him and adds that common and necessary things such as bedsteads, chairs and tables were excellently made at Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polemo says the Lacedaemonians used earthenware vessels, drinking from earthenware cups at festivals, at feasts in honour of victory and at the marriage-feasts of their maidens, and using small casks at other banquets and at the phiditia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two-handled lekaina, a shape a New York example dates to the 6th century BCE, took its name from the clay it was made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Laconian pottery","tags":["object","culture","history","greece","art","archaic"],"sources":["Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists, or Banquet of the Learned</i> 11.66, vol. 2, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor02athe. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 9, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 341–43, 460, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta kylix, Greek, Laconian, ca. 550 BCE, accession 59.15, https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/255020. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta lekaina, Greek, Laconian, 6th century BCE, accession 1986.11.7, https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/255861. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Laconian_black_figure_kylix_-_cocks_and_sphinxes_-_Roma_MCPCPN_-_01.jpg/1920px-Laconian_black_figure_kylix_-_cocks_and_sphinxes_-_Roma_MCPCPN_-_01.jpg","title":"Laconian pottery","desc":"Laconian black-figure kylix, ca. 550–530 BCE, with an animal frieze of two confronted cocks between sphinxes; Musei Capitolini, Rome.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laconian_black_figure_kylix_-_cocks_and_sphinxes_-_Roma_MCPCPN_-_01.jpg","alt":"Laconian black-figure kylix with cocks and sphinxes, Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the finest cups show their debt to metalwork?","a":"Because their slender shapes owe much to metal originals, which shows in the palmettes set at the ends of their handles."},{"q":"Why was the plain kothon prized on campaign?","a":"Because a soldier must often drink water that is not clean, and one cannot easily see what one is drinking in it while its inward-curving brim holds back what is foul."},{"q":"Why do the written sources notice the plain ware rather than the painted?","a":"Because they are interested in use — the campaign cup, the earthenware at festivals and weddings, the casks at the common messes — rather than in decoration."}]},
{"id":"gr-263","num":263,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Two continents dispute in Antipater's epigram whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a Lydian or a Spartan, since the tomb is simple to look on but holds the bones of a great man.","questions":["The Suda calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a Laconian from Messoa, though Crates made him a Lydian out of Sardis, and says his parents were slaves and he wrote six books.","Sosibius says the naked choruses at the Gymnopaidiae sang songs of Thaletas and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose maiden-song praises Agido and Hagesichora as they carry a robe to Orthia."],"answer":"Alcman","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 books in antiquity</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Alcman</b> was an archaic lyric poet who composed at Sparta, and the Suda credits him with six books, lyric poetry and a poem called <i>Diving Women</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same entry calls him a Laconian from Messoa while recording that Crates made him a Lydian out of Sardis, names his father as Damas or Titaros, and says his parents were slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It puts him in the 27th Olympiad, when Ardys was king of Lydia, and makes him the inventor of love poetry and the first to sing in metres other than the hexameter, though that chronology is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Velleius Paterculus denies the Spartan claim flatly, and two epigrams in the Greek Anthology turn the quarrel into a compliment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antipater writes that the stone is plain to look on but holds the bones of a great man, and that two continents dispute him, since minstrels have many mothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Alexander of Aetolia answers in the poet's own voice: reared in ancient Sardis he would have been a eunuch beating pretty tambourines, where instead he is a citizen of Sparta of the many tripods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What survives at length is a maiden-song on a first-century papyrus, sung by a chorus of girls who praise their leader Hagesichora and a second girl, Agido, as they carry a robe to Orthia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They call the two racehorses grazing among the cattle of dreams, set Hagesichora's hair beside pure gold, say she shines like Sirius, and name eight more singers who cannot help them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other pieces show a poet of the countryside and the table: peak and ravine asleep with the creeping things, the beasts, the bees and the monsters of the brine, and Alcman himself waiting on a pot of common fare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sosibius records that the naked choruses of the Gymnopaidiae still sang his songs, and Pausanias was shown his tomb at Sparta, its charm unspoilt by the Laconian dialect, the least musical of them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Alcman","tags":["person","history","greece","literature","art","archaic"],"sources":["Suda On Line, s.v. \"Alkman, Alcman,\" adler number alpha 1289, trans. and vetted by the Suda On Line project, https://www.cs.uky.edu/~raphael/sol/sol-entries/alpha/1289. [Open access]","J. M. Edmonds, ed. and trans., <i>Lyra Graeca</i>, vol. 1 (London: William Heinemann, 1922), 45–51, 79–89, https://archive.org/details/lyragraecabeing00edmogoog. [Open access]","<i>The Greek Anthology</i> 7.18, 7.709, vol. 2, trans. W. R. Paton (London: William Heinemann, 1917), https://archive.org/details/greekanthologyii0002pato. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists, or Banquet of the Learned</i> 15.22–23, vol. 3, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor03atheuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.15, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Mosaic_Portrait_of_the_Poet_Alcman.jpg/1920px-Mosaic_Portrait_of_the_Poet_Alcman.jpg","title":"Alcman","desc":"Imaginary portrait of Alcman in a floor mosaic of the late 2nd or 3rd century CE from Jerash, Jordan, made some eight centuries after the poet and set in a border with other Greek authors, the Seasons and the Muses.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_Portrait_of_the_Poet_Alcman.jpg","alt":"Roman mosaic portrait head of the poet Alcman, Jerash"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the poet's own origin disputed?","a":"Because the Suda calls him a Laconian from Messoa while recording that Crates made him a Lydian out of Sardis, and Velleius denies the Spartan claim flatly."},{"q":"Why did two epigrams turn that quarrel into a compliment?","a":"Because Antipater answers that two continents dispute him since minstrels have many mothers, and Alexander has him say that reared in Sardis he would have been a eunuch with a tambourine."},{"q":"Why did his songs outlast him at Sparta?","a":"Because Sosibius records that the naked choruses of the Gymnopaidiae still sang them."}]},
{"id":"gr-264","num":264,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Historians call it <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: Thucydides warned that were Lacedaemon deserted, posterity would refuse to believe her fame matched her power, the city being a set of villages.","questions":["One ancient admission behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Xenophon's: he would not maintain that the laws of Lycurgus were still obeyed, since Spartans now boasted of gold they had once dreaded.","Aristotle sharpens <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by reckoning that a country able to maintain 30,000 hoplites had under a thousand citizens, so the city sank under a single defeat."],"answer":"Spartan mirage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Modern term</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan mirage</b> is the name modern scholarship gives to the distance between Sparta's reputation and the Sparta the evidence will bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides saw the trap coming: were Lacedaemon deserted and only its temples and foundations left, posterity would be strongly disposed to refuse her fame as a true measure of her power, the city being neither compact nor adorned but a collection of villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He could not even count the Spartan army at Mantinea, because the secrecy of their government kept the numbers unknown and nobody trusted their opponents' guess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch, writing some seven centuries after the event, opens his life of Lycurgus by admitting that nothing can be said of the lawgiver which is not disputed, his birth, his travels, his death and above all his date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon, who lived at Sparta and admired it, would not venture to maintain that the laws of Lycurgus were obeyed in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Spartans had once dreaded to be caught with gold, he says, and now made a boast of it, and men who had preferred moderate means at home now wanted nothing so much as to end their days as governors abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle is harsher: the licence of the Spartan women defeats the intention of the constitution, so that half the city may be reckoned to have no laws at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He adds that property had fallen into few hands, that two-fifths of the land was held by women, and that a country able to maintain 1,500 cavalry and 30,000 hoplites had fewer than a thousand citizens, so that it sank under a single defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Spartans had also never found the secret of managing their subject population, whose risings he treats as a standing weakness rather than an accident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Modern work therefore assigns much of the traditional picture to an imagined Sparta, with customs credited to Lycurgus introduced later for the needs of the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan mirage","tags":["concept","history","greece","classical","archaic"],"sources":["Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 19, 308–9, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.10, 5.68, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 1, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 14, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.9, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Ancient_Sparta_ruins_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Ancient_Sparta_ruins_%281%29.jpg","title":"Spartan mirage","desc":"Ruins on the site of ancient Sparta, the low remains Thucydides had in mind when he wrote that a deserted Lacedaemon would leave posterity refusing to believe her fame matched her power.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Sparta_ruins_(1).jpg","alt":"Low stone footings among grass and trees on the site of ancient Sparta"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Thucydides expect posterity to misjudge the city?","a":"Because were it deserted, its temples and foundations would make it look far weaker than it was, being a collection of villages rather than a compact and adorned city."},{"q":"Why could even a contemporary not count the Spartan army?","a":"Because the secrecy of their government kept the numbers unknown, and nobody trusted their opponents' guess."},{"q":"Why does Xenophon's admiration make his testimony sharper?","a":"Because although he lived at Sparta and admired it, he would not maintain that the laws of Lycurgus were obeyed in his own day."}]},
{"id":"gr-265","num":265,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The ancient name for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was simply the Lacedaemonians and their allies, and Thucydides says Sparta took no tribute from them but established oligarchies instead.","questions":["Corinth was told she broke her oaths in refusing the peace of 421, since a majority of the allies bound the rest of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unless gods or heroes stood in the way.","Xenophon shows the congress of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resolving on ten thousand men against Olynthus, and letting a state pay three Aeginetan obols a day rather than send them."],"answer":"Peloponnesian League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">366 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Peloponnesian League</b> is the modern name for the system of alliances by which Sparta led the Peloponnese from the 6th century BCE, and whose name in antiquity was simply the Lacedaemonians and their allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus dates the ascendancy behind it: once the Spartans had the better of the Tegeans they had subdued the greater part of the Peloponnese besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of that century the alliance took in Corinth, Sicyon, probably Megara and Aegina, and the cities of the Argolis, with Sparta's own territory two-fifths of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its members were bound to Sparta rather than to one another, each by a separate treaty with the leader, who was to come to an attacked ally's aid and to be aided in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says the policy of Lacedaemon was to exact no tribute but to secure the subservience of the allies to her interests by establishing oligarchies among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta settled its own mind first and then summoned the rest: in 432 BCE the Lacedaemonians heard every argument, took the vote of all the allied states present in order, great and small alike, and the majority was for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That majority bound the others, and Corinth was told she had added the breaking of her oaths to her refusal of the peace, since it had been expressly agreed that the decision of the majority should stand unless the gods or heroes stood in the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon shows the congress at work again over Olynthus, the Lacedaemonians asking the allies for their joint advice and the states resolving to raise ten thousand men between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same meeting let any state that wished send money instead, at three Aeginetan obols a day for each man, with a horseman reckoned at four foot soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus closes the account: after holding the supremacy of Greece for almost five hundred years, the Lacedaemonians were beaten in one great battle and lost it irretrievably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Peloponnesian League","tags":["institution","history","greece","warfare","classical","archaic"],"sources":["Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 16–17, 175–76, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.68, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.19, 1.125, 5.30, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 5.2, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1174/1174-h/1174-h.htm. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 15.50, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1952), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/15C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Bronze_warrior_Laconian_from_Sparta_6th_century_BCE_British_Museum.jpg/1920px-Bronze_warrior_Laconian_from_Sparta_6th_century_BCE_British_Museum.jpg","title":"Peloponnesian League","desc":"Bronze warrior, Laconian, from Sparta, 6th century BCE. British Museum.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_warrior_Laconian_from_Sparta_6th_century_BCE_British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A small bronze warrior in a crested helmet, carrying a round shield with his spear arm raised"},"why":[{"q":"Why did each ally treat with Sparta rather than with one another?","a":"Because the members were bound to Sparta separately, each by its own treaty with the leader, who was to come to an attacked ally's aid and to be aided in turn."},{"q":"Why could Corinth be accused of breaking her oaths by refusing the peace?","a":"Because it had been expressly agreed that the decision of the majority should stand unless the gods or heroes stood in the way, and the majority had voted for war."},{"q":"Why did Sparta exact no tribute from the states she led?","a":"Because her policy, Thucydides says, was to secure the subservience of the allies to her interests by establishing oligarchies among them instead."}]},
{"id":"gr-266","num":266,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Herodotus has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Olympia when the cauldrons of Hippocrates boiled without fire, advising him not to take a wife, or to disown any son he had.","questions":["Diodorus says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> engraved three maxims on a column at Delphi: know thyself, nothing overmuch, and a pledge, and ruin is nigh.","The tradition has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> die at Pisa of excess of joy, having just congratulated his son on winning the Olympic crown for boxing."],"answer":"Chilon of Sparta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ephor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">556/555 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chilon of Sparta</b> was a Lacedaemonian of the 6th century BCE counted among the Seven Sages, whom Diogenes Laertius makes the son of Damagetas and the author of an elegiac poem some 200 lines long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was made ephor in the 55th Olympiad, or the 56th by another reckoning, and was said to have first proposed that ephors be joined to the kings as their auxiliaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last claim is doubted: the writer Diogenes leans on seems to have recorded Chilon's first term of office rather than the first ephorate, and other accounts give the office to Lycurgus, to Theopompus or to the Messenian-war kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus puts him at Olympia when the cauldrons of Hippocrates boiled without fire beneath them, advising him not to bring a wife into his house, and to divorce her and disown any son he had already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hippocrates would not be persuaded, and the son afterwards born to him was Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diodorus says his life agreed with his teaching, a thing one rarely finds, and has him engrave three maxims on a column at Delphi: know thyself, nothing overmuch, and a pledge, and ruin is nigh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tradition does not agree about them, since Diogenes gives only the pledge as Chilon's own apophthegm and reports 'know thyself' claimed for Thales and for the prophetess Phemonoë.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The precepts kept under his name are plain and mostly about restraint: control the tongue, especially at a banquet; use threats to nobody, for that is womanish; visit friends in adversity sooner than in prosperity; prefer a loss to a dishonest gain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He spoke so briefly that Aristagoras of Miletus called the manner Chilonean, and Herodotus has Demaratus tell Xerxes that Chilon thought Cythera a greater gain to Sparta sunk under the sea than left above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is said to have died at Pisa of excess of joy after congratulating his son on an Olympic victory in boxing, and Pausanias was shown a hero-shrine of Chilon at Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chilon of Sparta","tags":["person","history","greece","concept","archaic"],"sources":["Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of the Eminent Philosophers</i> 1.40, 1.68–73, trans. R. D. Hicks, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Lives_of_the_Eminent_Philosophers/Book_I. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.59, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.235, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 9.9–10, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/9*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 94–95, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.16, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Chilon_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg","title":"Chilon of Sparta","desc":"Imaginary portrait of Chilon, engraved for Thomas Stanley's History of Philosophy of 1655 and dressing him in the fancy costume of that century rather than anything Spartan.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilon_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg","alt":"Engraved full-length figure of a bearded man in robe and turban, captioned CHILO"},"why":[{"q":"Why is Chilon's founding of the ephorate doubted?","a":"Because the writer Diogenes leans on seems to have recorded Chilon's own first term of office rather than the first ephorate, and other accounts give the office to Lycurgus, to Theopompus or to the Messenian-war kings."},{"q":"Why does the tradition disagree about which maxims are his?","a":"Because Diodorus has him engrave all three at Delphi while Diogenes gives only the pledge as Chilon's own, and reports 'know thyself' claimed for Thales and for the prophetess Phemonoë."},{"q":"Why did his manner of speaking acquire a name of its own?","a":"Because he spoke so briefly that Aristagoras of Miletus called the manner Chilonean, as in his judgement that Cythera would be a greater gain to Sparta sunk under the sea than left above it."}]},
{"id":"gr-267","num":267,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristagoras of Miletus showed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a bronze tablet engraved with a map of the whole earth, and was sent out of Sparta before sunset on admitting the king lay three months inland.","questions":["Told the burning grove was sacred to Argos, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> groaned that Apollo had greatly deceived him in promising he would take the city of that name.","Maeandrius of Samos offered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> his gold and silver cups, and Herodotus says the king refused them and had the ephors expel him from the Peloponnese."],"answer":"Cleomenes I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agiad king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 521 – c. 490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cleomenes I</b> was Agiad king of Sparta from about 521 to about 490 BCE, the son Anaxandrides had by a second wife taken when the ephors pressed him over the first’s barrenness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says he was not quite in his right senses but on the verge of madness, and that the Spartans followed custom anyway and gave the throne to the eldest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His half-brother Dorieus, whom Pausanias calls sounder in judgement and the better soldier, would not stay to be his subject and sailed off to found a colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At home Herodotus calls him the most upright of men: Maeandrius of Samos pressed gold and silver cups on him, and he refused and had the ephors expel the man lest he corrupt some other Spartan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He heard Aristagoras of Miletus out over a bronze tablet engraved with a map of the whole earth, then told him to be gone before sunset on learning the Persian king lay three months inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Aristagoras followed him home and raised his offer to fifty talents, at which the king’s daughter Gorgo, then eight or nine, said the stranger would do him hurt if he did not leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the field he is another man: after beating the Argives he called about fifty of those sheltering in the sacred grove out by name, pretending their ransom was paid, and killed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then had the helots fire the grove, and on learning it was sacred to the hero Argos he groaned that Apollo had deceived him in promising he would take Argos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the Athenian acropolis the priestess told him no Dorian might enter, and he answered that he was no Dorian but an Achaean; Thucydides has him drive the accursed out of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Brought back to Sparta he struck every Spartan he met in the face with his staff until his kinsmen bound him in the stocks, where he got a knife from his helot warder and cut himself to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cleomenes I","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 3.148, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.42, 5.49–51, 5.72, 6.75–80, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.3–3.4, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 178–82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why did Cleomenes inherit the throne despite Herodotus's verdict on him?","a":"Because although Herodotus says he was not quite in his right senses but on the verge of madness, the Spartans followed custom anyway and gave the throne to the eldest."},{"q":"Why can Herodotus call the same king the most upright of men?","a":"Because when Maeandrius of Samos pressed gold and silver cups on him he refused them, and had the ephors expel the man lest he corrupt some other Spartan."},{"q":"Why did he groan on learning what he had burned at Argos?","a":"Because the grove he had the helots fire was sacred to the hero Argos, so Apollo's promise that he would take Argos had been fulfilled deceitfully and not by the city falling."}]},
{"id":"gr-268","num":268,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Xerxes was told by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the Lacedaemonians are free but not free in all things, since Law is set over them as a master they fear more than his men fear him.","questions":["Ariston reckoned the months on his fingers before the ephors and denied on oath that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be his son, which Cleomenes used years later to depose him.","From Susa <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent word of the invasion on a folding tablet scraped bare and waxed over again, and Gorgo worked out how to read it."],"answer":"Demaratus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eurypontid king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 515 – 491 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">With Xerxes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Demaratus</b> was Eurypontid king of Sparta from about 515 to 491 BCE, and stood in the shadow of his Agiad colleague Cleomenes for nearly the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus explains his name and then his ruin in the same breath: the Spartans had prayed in a body for a son to Ariston, but the boy came in less than ten months, and Ariston, reckoning on his fingers as he sat with the ephors, denied on oath that the child could be his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He repented and took the words back, and the ephors thought nothing of them then, but they were remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Demaratus abandoned Cleomenes at Eleusis a law was laid down that the two kings should never again take the field together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleomenes then set up Leotychides to claim the throne on that old oath, and had Cobon of Delphi persuade the prophetess Perialla to declare Demaratus no son of Ariston; when it came out, both were disgraced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias gives the flat verdict that he was deposed not rightfully but because Cleomenes hated him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Holding a lesser office afterwards, he was asked by Leotychides at the Gymnopaidiae what it was like to be a magistrate after being king, and he veiled his head, went home, sacrificed an ox and asked his mother who his father was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fled to Persia and marched with Xerxes, who asked whether Greeks would really stand, and was told that the Lacedaemonians are free and yet not free in all things, since Law is set over them as a master they fear far more than his own men fear him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From Susa he had already sent warning of the invasion on a folding tablet scraped bare and waxed over again, which Gorgo worked out how to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus leaves open whether he did that in friendship or in malice, and his descendants still governed Pergamum and two cities beside it in Xenophon's day, on land the Persian king had given him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Demaratus","tags":["person","ruler","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.75, 6.61–70, 7.104, 7.239, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.4, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 3.1, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), Project Gutenberg ebook 1174, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1174/1174-h/1174-h.htm. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.6, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/11A*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 178–82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why did an oath sworn at Demaratus's birth ruin him decades later?","a":"Because the boy came in less than ten months and Ariston, reckoning on his fingers before the ephors, denied on oath that the child could be his; he took the words back, but they were remembered."},{"q":"Why is his deposition called unrightful rather than merely unlucky?","a":"Because Cleomenes had Cobon of Delphi persuade the prophetess Perialla to declare him no son of Ariston, and when it came out both were disgraced; Pausanias says he was deposed because Cleomenes hated him."},{"q":"Why is the warning he sent from Susa hard to read as loyalty?","a":"Because Herodotus leaves open whether he sent it in friendship or in malice, having marched with Xerxes and held land the Persian king had given him."}]},
{"id":"gr-269","num":269,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The Lacedaemonians came to Sardis to buy gold for the image of Apollo on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Croesus gave it to them as a gift instead.","questions":["Pausanias says the Spartans spent the gold Croesus sent for the Apollo of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on adorning the Amyclaean image instead, that cult being more distinguished.","Pausanias reaches <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the ruins of Sellasia and says the city lies further on, and he knew a second hill of the name in the Argolid."],"answer":"Thornax","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Image made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Thornax</b> was a place in Laconia on the road north of Sparta, carrying an image of Apollo Pythaeus made after the style of the one at Amyclae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias reaches it going along the highway past the ruins of Sellasia, and says the city lies further on from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus explains why it mattered: the Lacedaemonians had sent to Sardis and were buying gold there to use for that image, and Croesus, when they wished to buy, gave it them as a gift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That service is why they were pleased when his messengers came asking an alliance, saying the god had bidden him join with himself the Hellene as a friend and that he had heard they were the chiefs of Hellas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They exchanged oaths of friendship and alliance, a pact commonly dated to about 555 BCE and read as a clear affront to Argos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> They also had a bronze mixing-bowl made, covered outside with figures round the rim and large enough to hold three hundred amphorae, and it never reached Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lacedaemonians said the Samians sailed out and took it; the Samians said the carriers, hearing Sardis had fallen, sold it in Samos, where it was dedicated in the temple of Hera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the herald came asking help against Cyrus the Spartans had a quarrel with Argos over Thyrea in hand, and their ships were ready before word arrived that Croesus was a prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pausanias closes the account against Herodotus: the gold went on adorning the Amyclaean image after all, that cult being in Spartan eyes the more distinguished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its exact hill has not been securely identified, Strabo's text being damaged where the name may have stood and the conjecture that restores it doubtful, and Pausanias knew a second Thornax in the Argolid, renamed Coccygium after Zeus turned cuckoo there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Thornax","tags":["place","mountain","history","geography","greece","religion","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.69–70, 1.82–83, 1.152–153, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.10–3.11, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.36, vol. 1, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/pausaniasgreece01pausuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.5.3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), note 254, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/8E*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 174–76, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was Sparta buying gold at Sardis in the first place?","a":"Because the Lacedaemonians wanted it for an image of Apollo Pythaeus at Thornax, made after the style of the one at Amyclae, and Croesus gave them the gold as a gift when they wished to buy."},{"q":"Why did Croesus's gift matter to the alliance that followed?","a":"Because that service is why the Spartans were pleased when his messengers came asking an alliance, saying the god had bidden him join the Hellene with himself as a friend."},{"q":"Why do the accounts disagree about where the gold ended up?","a":"Because Pausanias closes against Herodotus, saying the gold went on adorning the Amyclaean image after all, that cult being in Spartan eyes the more distinguished."}]},
{"id":"gr-270","num":270,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Told that Sparta meant to restore Hippias, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that heaven would sooner lie below the earth and fishes dwell where men had lived before.","questions":["The envoy of Corinth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, told the congress to set up a despot in Sparta itself first, and then go establishing them for others.","Herodotus says the other allies had kept silence until <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spoke freely, after which every one of them adjured Sparta to do no violence to a Greek city."],"answer":"Socles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spoke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 504 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Socles</b> was the envoy of Corinth who spoke against Sparta at the congress of the allies about 504 BCE, the first meeting of that alliance on record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What he was answering was a reputation Thucydides states flatly: the tyrants of Athens and the far older tyrannies of the rest of Hellas were, Sicily excepted, once and for all put down by Lacedaemon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides grounds it in Sparta's own case, an unbroken freedom from tyrants and one form of government kept for more than four hundred years, which put her in a position to arrange the affairs of other states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle repeats it in a sentence, that the Lacedaemonians put down most of the tyrannies, and Herodotus supplies instances, chief among them a forty-day siege of Polycrates at Samos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus also undercuts it, since the Lacedaemonians said themselves that they sailed against Samos less to help the exiles than to avenge a mixing-bowl the Samians had robbed them of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> At Athens the lever was Delphi, the exiled Alcmaeonids having paid the Pythian prophetess to tell every Spartan who consulted her, privately or publicly, to set Athens free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta obeyed and drove out the sons of Peisistratus, though bound to them by guest-friendship, holding that the concerns of the god should be preferred to those of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Years afterwards Cleomenes brought home oracles foretelling injury from Athens, so Sparta summoned Hippias from Sigeum, told the allies plainly that it had erred, and proposed to put him back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of them disapproved and kept silence, and Socles alone spoke, telling the Corinthian story of Cypselus and Periander at length and challenging Sparta to set up a despot at home first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest then spoke out and adopted his opinion, and modern accounts read the whole policy narrowly, as hostility to particular tyrants whom an oligarchy friendly to Sparta could replace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Socles","tags":["person","history","greece","concept","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.63–65, 5.90–93, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.18, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.12, trans. Benjamin Jowett, in <i>Aristotle's Politics</i>, with an introduction, analysis and index by H. W. C. Davis (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/aristotlespoliti00arisuoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 3.46–48, 3.54–56, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 179–81, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"why":[{"q":"Why was Sparta thought the state that put down tyrannies?","a":"Because Thucydides says the tyrants of Athens and the far older tyrannies of the rest of Hellas were, Sicily excepted, once and for all put down by Lacedaemon, and Aristotle repeats it."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus undercut that reputation even while supplying its instances?","a":"Because the Lacedaemonians said themselves that they sailed against Samos less to help the exiles than to avenge a mixing-bowl the Samians had robbed them of."},{"q":"Why did Socles' speech carry a congress that had been silent?","a":"Because most of the allies disapproved of restoring Hippias and kept silence until he spoke, telling the Corinthian story of Cypselus and Periander and challenging Sparta to set up a despot at home first."}]},
{"id":"gr-271","num":271,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes its name from the irons the Spartans carried to Tegea, in which the captured among them were then set to till the Tegean plain.","questions":["Delphi refused Sparta the whole of Arcadia before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, offering instead a plain to measure with line and divide, which the losers measured as prisoners.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Lichas read an oracle in a Tegean smithy, taking the bellows for winds and the hammer and anvil for stroke and counterstroke."],"answer":"Battle of the Fetters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Battle of the Fetters</b> is the modern name for a defeat the Spartans suffered at Tegea around the middle of the 6th century BCE, which the ancient accounts describe without naming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus places it after Lycurgus, when Sparta had grown prosperous, was no longer content to keep still, and consulted Delphi about conquering the whole of Arcadia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pythian prophetess refused Arcadia, telling them that many acorn-eating men were there to prevent them, and offered instead Tegea to dance in and a fair plain to measure with line and divide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They marched on Tegea with fetters in their hands, trusting a deceitful oracle and expecting to make slaves of the Tegeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Worsted in the encounter, those taken alive worked the Tegean plain wearing the fetters they had brought, and Herodotus says the irons still hung about the temple of Athena Alea in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta went on losing until Delphi told them to bring home the bones of Orestes, a second oracle setting the grave where two winds blow by compulsion, stroke answers stroke, and trouble lies upon trouble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lichas, one of the five Agathoergoi sent about on state business each year, read the riddle in a Tegean smithy: the bellows the winds, the hammer and anvil the strokes, the iron the trouble laid upon trouble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smith had dug up a coffin seven cubits long while sinking a well, Sparta drove Lichas out on a pretended charge, and he hired the yard, took the bones home, and from then on the Lacedaemonians had much the better of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias keeps the whole story and adds a reader's caution, that in the age of heroes the god would have called bronze the woe to man, setting beside it Cimon's finding of the bones of Theseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modern accounts read the bones as policy rather than piety, marking the point at which Sparta stopped annexing land, claimed the Achaean inheritance, and turned to a hegemony held by treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of the Fetters","tags":["event","battle","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.65–68, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.3, 3.7, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 9.36, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/9*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 165–66, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Natasha Bershadsky, “The Border of War and Peace: Myth and Ritual in Argive-Spartan Dispute over Thyreatis,” in <i>Maintaining Peace and Interstate Stability in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, ed. Julia Wilker (Mainz: Verlag Antike, 2012), 56–57, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/43876. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Tegea_Temple_of_Athena_Alea_from_SW.jpg/1920px-Tegea_Temple_of_Athena_Alea_from_SW.jpg","title":"Temple of Athena Alea, Tegea","desc":"Temple of Athena Alea at Tegea, from southwest.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tegea_Temple_of_Athena_Alea_from_SW.jpg","alt":"Tegea, temple of Athena Alea from the south-west"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Spartans march on Tegea carrying fetters?","a":"Because they trusted a deceitful oracle that offered them Tegea to dance in and a fair plain to measure with line and divide, and expected to make slaves of the Tegeans."},{"q":"Why did the irons hang in the temple of Athena Alea?","a":"Because the Spartans were worsted, and those taken alive worked the Tegean plain wearing the fetters they had brought; Herodotus says they still hung there in his own day."},{"q":"Why do modern accounts read the bones of Orestes as policy?","a":"Because bringing them home marks the point at which Sparta stopped annexing land, claimed the Achaean inheritance, and turned to a hegemony held by treaty."}]},
{"id":"gr-272","num":272,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Three hundred a side fought for Thyrea in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and by nightfall only Alcenor and Chromios of Argos and the Spartan Othryades were still standing.","questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Argives bound themselves by a curse to wear their hair short until Thyrea was won back, and Sparta took the opposite law.","In 420 BCE Argos won a clause letting either state challenge the other over Cynuria and settle it as in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Sparta thought mere folly."],"answer":"Battle of the Champions","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 546 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Battle of the Champions</b> is the modern name for a fight of three hundred picked men a side over the border district of Thyrea, dated from Herodotus to about 546 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sparta was the aggressor, having cut Thyrea off from the Argive possessions, and when the Argives came to save their territory the two sides agreed to settle the ground by champions alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both armies were to withdraw home, for fear lest one side seeing its countrymen suffer defeat should come up to their support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chosen men proved equally matched, and by nightfall three were left of the six hundred: Alcenor and Chromios for Argos, Othryades for Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two Argives ran home reckoning themselves the victors, while Othryades stripped the Argive dead, carried their arms to his own camp and stayed where he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Next day each side claimed the win, the one on the greater number surviving and the other because those men had fled, and the dispute became a general battle that Sparta took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Argives cut their hair short and made a law with a curse attached that no man should grow it long nor any woman wear gold until Thyrea was won back, and the Lacedaemonians laid down for themselves the opposite law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus ends with Othryades killing himself there, ashamed to return alone; Argos told it otherwise, and a monument in its theatre showed Perilaus son of Alcenor killing him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The quarrel outlasted them all, and in 420 BCE the Argives won a clause allowing either state to challenge the other and decide Cynuria by battle as before, which Thucydides says Sparta thought mere folly before agreeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One modern reading takes the story as the trace of ritual combats fought for the district through the archaic period, each settling possession until the next, until Sparta broke the practice by annexing it outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of the Champions","tags":["event","battle","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 1.82, vol. 1, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2707, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2707/2707-h/2707-h.htm. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.41, trans. Richard Crawley (London, 1874), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.20, 2.38, vol. 1, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/pausaniasgreece01pausuoft. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 176–77, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]","Natasha Bershadsky, “The Border of War and Peace: Myth and Ritual in Argive-Spartan Dispute over Thyreatis,” in <i>Maintaining Peace and Interstate Stability in Archaic and Classical Greece</i>, ed. Julia Wilker (Mainz: Verlag Antike, 2012), 49–77, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/43876. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Battle_between_Agravers_and_Spartians%2C_546_BC.jpg/1920px-Battle_between_Agravers_and_Spartians%2C_546_BC.jpg","title":"Battle of the Champions, engraved 1832","desc":"Battle between Argives and Spartans (Battle of the 300 Champions), 546 BCE. Line engraving from Vorzeit und Gegenwart, vol. 2 (Augsburg: Schlosser, 1832).","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_between_Agravers_and_Spartians,_546_BC.jpg","alt":"Line engraving of hoplites fighting hand to hand among the fallen, a temple beyond"},"why":[{"q":"Why were both armies made to withdraw before the champions fought?","a":"Because of the fear lest one side, seeing its countrymen suffer defeat, should come up to their support, so the two sides agreed to settle the ground by champions alone."},{"q":"Why could each side claim the victory after the fight?","a":"Because the Argives claimed it on the greater number surviving, and the Spartans because those two men had fled while Othryades stripped the dead and stayed where he was."},{"q":"Why did the two states change how their people wore their hair?","a":"Because the Argives cut theirs short under a law with a curse attached, that no man grow it long nor any woman wear gold until Thyrea was won back, and Sparta laid down the opposite."}]},
{"id":"gr-273","num":273,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Cleomenes had the Spartan herald call breakfast at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and attacked while the Argives, who were copying every proclamation, sat down to eat.","questions":["Pausanias says the poetess Telesilla armed the women of Argos after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and set them on the wall, and that the Lacedaemonians gave way rather than kill them.","So many Argives fell at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Herodotus says slaves ruled the state until the sons of the dead were grown men."],"answer":"Battle of Sepeia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Battle of Sepeia</b> is the name given to the destruction of the Argive army by Cleomenes of Sparta near Tiryns, usually dated to 494 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Delphi had told him he would take Argos, and when the sacrifices at the river Erasinos refused him a crossing he said he admired it for not betraying its countrymen, then shipped the army from Thyrea to Tiryns and Nauplia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Argives came down to meet him and camped close by, afraid not of open fighting but of being beaten by craft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So they took to doing whatever the Spartan herald proclaimed, and Cleomenes, seeing it, had the herald call breakfast and attacked while they were eating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many fell and many more fled into the grove sacred to Argos, where they were called out by name for ransom and killed until the rest saw it, and the grove was fired with them inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The dead are counted differently by everyone: Herodotus reports six thousand on the Argives' own account, Pausanias puts five thousand in the grove, and Plutarch names 7,777 only to dismiss it as a fabulous story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias and Plutarch add what Herodotus does not, that the city was saved by its women under the poetess Telesilla, who armed those of fighting age with what was left in the houses and the sanctuaries and set them on the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Lacedaemonians gave way, reckoning that to destroy the women would be an invidious success and to lose to them a shameful disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus quotes an oracle about the female conquering the male, which Pausanias applies to that fight while allowing that Herodotus may not have understood it so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Argos was left so bare of men that its slaves held the state until the sons of the fallen grew up, while Cleomenes sacrificed at the Heraion and went home to be tried for taking bribes not to storm the city, and was acquitted on an omen he had seen there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of Sepeia","tags":["event","battle","history","greece","warfare","archaic"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.76–83, 7.148, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 2.20, vol. 1, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/pausaniasgreece01pausuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.4, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Plutarch, “Bravery of Women” 245c–f, in <i>Moralia</i>, vol. 3, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), https://archive.org/details/plutarchs-moralia-vol.-3-loeb-245. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 181–82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Argos_Heraion_Plain.jpg/1920px-Argos_Heraion_Plain.jpg","title":"The Argive plain from the Heraion","desc":"View from the Heraion of Argos into the Inachos plain, Argolis, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Argos_Heraion_Plain.jpg","alt":"Terraces and fallen column drums in the foreground, a wide cultivated plain running to mountains beyond"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Argives' caution deliver them to Cleomenes?","a":"Because they feared being beaten by craft and so took to doing whatever the Spartan herald proclaimed, and Cleomenes had the herald call breakfast and attacked while they were eating."},{"q":"Why is the number of the Argive dead reported so differently?","a":"Because Herodotus reports six thousand on the Argives' own account, Pausanias puts five thousand in the grove, and Plutarch names 7,777 only to dismiss it as a fabulous story."},{"q":"Why did the Lacedaemonians give way before the women on the wall?","a":"Because they reckoned that to destroy the women would be an invidious success and to lose to them a shameful disaster, so they withdrew from the city."}]},
{"id":"gr-274","num":274,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the dead were laid away in a scarlet robe and olive leaves with nothing else, and mourning ended on the twelfth day with a sacrifice to Demeter.","questions":["Plutarch credits Lycurgus with allowing burial inside the city as part of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the young grew used to the sight and feared no pollution from a corpse.","A name was cut on a tomb under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only for a man fallen in war, or for a woman whose case the manuscripts and their editors describe differently."],"answer":"Spartan burial custom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lycurgus, c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Spartan burial custom</b> is known chiefly from Plutarch, who credits Lycurgus with doing away with the fear of the dead by letting Spartans bury within the city and keep memorials near the sacred places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The point, he says, was to make the young familiar with such sights, so that they had no horror of death and did not count a corpse or a graveyard as polluting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing went into the grave with the body, which was covered with a scarlet robe and olive leaves and laid away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No name might be cut on a tomb except that of a man who had fallen in war or of a woman who had died in sacred office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mourning was fixed at eleven days, and on the twelfth they sacrificed to Demeter and had done with grief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A king's death was another matter: Herodotus says horsemen carried the news through Laconia while women went about the city striking a copper kettle, and two free persons of every household had to go into mourning under heavy penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fixed numbers of perioikoi were compelled to attend along with helots and Spartans, thousands beating their foreheads and crying that the king last dead was the best of all, and for ten days there was no assembly and no election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A king killed in war was carried out in effigy, and Xenophon says the laws plainly mean that the kings of Lacedaemon are honoured not as men but as heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias saw the tombs of Leonidas and Pausanias opposite the theatre and a slab naming those who endured Thermopylae with their fathers' names; the rule is visible still in the Kerameikos at Athens, over the Spartans who fell there in 403 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One clause is disputed: the manuscripts of Plutarch say a woman in sacred office, while a much-adopted emendation makes it a woman dead in childbirth, a symmetry with death in battle that recent work calls an erroneous conjecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Spartan burial custom","tags":["practice","history","greece","religion","archaic","classical"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 27, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, vol. 1, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.58–59, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay, Project Gutenberg ebook 2456, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/2456/2456-h/2456-h.htm. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>The Polity of the Lacedaemonians</i> 15, trans. H. G. Dakyns, in <i>The Works of Xenophon</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890–97), Project Gutenberg ebook 1178, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/1178/1178-h/1178-h.htm. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.14, vol. 2, trans. W. H. S. Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://archive.org/details/descriptionofgre0002paus. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 129, 236, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians_7.JPG/1920px-Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians_7.JPG","title":"Tomb of the Lacedaemonians, Kerameikos","desc":"Kerameikos, Athens, Greece: View from north-east on the Tomb of the Lacedaemonians.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_the_Lacedaemonians_7.JPG","alt":"A long low wall of dressed limestone blocks in an excavated cemetery, an earth bank behind it"},"why":[{"q":"Why were Spartans buried inside the city and near the sacred places?","a":"Because Lycurgus meant to do away with the fear of the dead by making the young familiar with such sights, so that they had no horror of death and counted no corpse or graveyard polluting."},{"q":"Why was a king's death mourned so differently from anyone else's?","a":"Because Xenophon says the laws plainly mean that the kings of Lacedaemon are honoured not as men but as heroes, so perioikoi, helots and Spartans alike were compelled to mourn."},{"q":"Why is the rule about naming a woman's tomb disputed?","a":"Because Plutarch's manuscripts say a woman in sacred office, while a much-adopted emendation makes it a woman dead in childbirth, a symmetry with death in battle that recent work calls an erroneous conjecture."}]},
{"id":"gr-275","num":275,"category":"Sparta","question":"Sacrifice to Zeus the Leader at home, another at the border and a she-goat in the battle line all belonged to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which could turn a whole army back unfought.","questions":["Each Spartan king kept two envoys to Delphi at the public table, and every army carried a seer, because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was state business rather than a matter of private piety.","Thucydides records two full mobilisations abandoned at the border in one summer because the crossing victims came out wrong, which shows how much weight <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried in war."],"answer":"Spartan divination","answerText":"Spartan divination","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Spartan divination</b> was the reading of signs from sacrificial animals and from oracles, and Sparta built it into the machinery of kingship and war rather than leaving it to private piety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus lists among a king's privileges the right to choose two Pythioi, envoys sent to consult Apollo at Delphi who ate with him at public charge; the kings kept the oracles that came back, and the Pythioi knew them too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every army carried a seer, and the most celebrated was Tisamenus of Elis, of the Iamid family of prophets, whom the Spartans wanted so badly that he raised his price until they made him a citizen. He then demanded the same for his brother Hegias and got it, and Herodotus says the two were the only men ever made fellow citizens of Sparta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon describes a campaign opening with sacrifice at home to Zeus Agetor, the Leader, after which a fire-bearer took fire from that altar and carried it ahead of the army, never letting it go out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the boundary the king sacrificed again, to Zeus and Athena, and crossed only when both accepted; the Greeks called this rite the diabateria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That was no formality: in a single summer Thucydides records two full Spartan mobilisations abandoned at the frontier, one under Agis at Leuctra and another at Caryae, because the crossing sacrifices came out wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sacrifice went on into the fighting itself, with an offering to the Muses before combat and, once the men were drawn up, a goat killed to Artemis Agrotera while they put on garlands and the pipers played the hymn to Castor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Plataea the offerings would not come favourable while Persian arrows fell among the Spartans, until <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span> fixed his gaze on the temple of Hera and prayed, the Tegeans went forward, and the victims turned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A king on campaign was therefore priest as much as general, and the sign he read could commit an army or send it home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 6.57, 9.33–35, 9.61–62, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Lacedaemonians</i> 13, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, trans. E. C. Marchant and G. W. Bowersock (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0210:text=Const.%20Lac.:chapter=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.54–55, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lycurgus</i> 21–22, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Lycurgus*.html. [Open access]","Ryszard Kulesza, <i>Sparta: History, State and Society</i> (Berlin: Peter Lang, 2022), 82, https://library.oapen.org/handle/20.500.12657/87764. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why was Tisamenus of Elis made a citizen of Sparta?","a":"Because the Spartans wanted the seer so badly that he raised his price until they granted it, and he then demanded the same for his brother Hegias and got that too."},{"q":"Why could a sacrifice at the frontier stop a whole campaign?","a":"Because the king crossed only when Zeus and Athena both accepted the diabateria, and Thucydides records two full Spartan mobilisations abandoned at the border in a single summer when they came out wrong."},{"q":"Why does divination make the Spartan king a priest as much as a general?","a":"Because he chose the Pythioi and kept the oracles they brought back, sacrificed at the frontier and before the fighting, and the sign he read could commit an army or send it home."}]},
{"id":"gr-276","num":276,"category":"Athens","question":"Thucydides argued that the thin soil of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was why it escaped the faction that ruined richer parts of Greece, and so never changed its inhabitants.","questions":["Pentelic marble, Hymettus honey and the silver at Laurion all came out of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose thin soil Thucydides thought too poor to be worth invading.","Strabo knew <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by its older name Acte, because most of it lies flat along the sea, and said its countryside held demes too numerous to list."],"answer":"Attica","answerText":"Attica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One polis by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Attica</b> is the peninsula at the south-eastern corner of central Greece, sea on two sides and Athens set in its largest plain; Strabo records that the older name for it was Acte, because most of it lies below the mountains and stretches flat and narrow along the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo lists its mountains as Hymettus, Brilessus, Lycabettus, Parnes and Corydallus, and traces its coast from the Isthmus round to the cape at Sounion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias sums up what each range was for: quarries on Pentelicus, hunting for wild boar and bear on Parnes, and on Hymettus the most suitable pasture for bees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The soil between them is thin, and Thucydides turns that poverty into the explanation of Athens' whole early history: because Attica was not worth taking it escaped the faction that ruined richer lands, and never changed its inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strongest men driven out of the rest of Greece took refuge there and were naturalised, until the region could not hold them and Athens began sending colonies to Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What Attica had instead of good farmland was stone and metal, and Strabo calls the Hymettian and Pentelic quarries the finest near the city and Hymettus honey the best there is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his own day the silver at Laurion in the south-east was worked out, and the miners were re-smelting the old slag heaps for what earlier and clumsier furnaces had left in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the Persian invasion it had been rich enough that the Athenians proposed to share a windfall out at ten drachmas a head, until Themistocles talked them into building 200 ships with it instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That fleet was meant for a war with Aegina, and Herodotus says it proved the salvation of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo thought the demes of the Attic interior too many to recount, and when Sparta invaded year after year in the Peloponnesian War that whole country population moved inside the walls of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.2, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.144, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.1.1–23, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D1%3Asection%3D23. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.32.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Geoff Bakewell, review of Nicholas F. Jones, <i>Rural Athens under the Democracy</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2005.04.03, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.03/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/As_seen_from_Cape_Sounion_Greece.jpg/1920px-As_seen_from_Cape_Sounion_Greece.jpg","title":"Attica","desc":"Coastline toward north from Cape Sounion, Greece.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:As_seen_from_Cape_Sounion_Greece.jpg","alt":"As seen from Cape Sounion, Greece"},"locator":{"name":"Attica","at":[23.7,38],"kind":"region","area":[[23.4,38.12],[23.58,38.28],[23.82,38.32],[24.02,38.24],[24.08,38.06],[24.06,37.86],[24.03,37.64],[23.88,37.72],[23.74,37.84],[23.56,37.94],[23.42,37.99]]},"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides make Attica's poor soil the key to its history?","a":"Because the land was not worth taking, so the region escaped the faction that ruined richer lands and never changed its inhabitants, taking in the strongest men driven out of the rest of Greece."},{"q":"Why did Attica's wealth lie in stone and metal rather than farmland?","a":"Because the soil between its mountains is thin, while the Hymettian and Pentelic quarries were the finest near the city and the silver at Laurion lay in the south-east."},{"q":"Why did a windfall of silver become two hundred ships?","a":"Because Themistocles talked the Athenians out of sharing it at ten drachmas a head; the fleet was meant for a war with Aegina, and Herodotus says it proved the salvation of Greece."}]},
{"id":"gr-277","num":277,"category":"Athens","question":"Thucydides says that before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Attica was a set of independent townships that consulted the king at Athens only in danger and sometimes made war on him.","questions":["Athenians kept their land and their country homes after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but their council-chambers and magistracies were abolished and merged into the single town hall of Athens.","The festival of the Synoecia, paid for by the state, kept the memory of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Strabo says joined the twelve towns of Cecrops under Theseus."],"answer":"Synoecism of Attica","answerText":"Synoecism of Attica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Theseus, before 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Complete by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>synoecism of Attica</b> is the tradition that the region's separate communities were merged into one state centred on Athens, and the fullest ancient account of it is Thucydides'. He writes that from Cecrops down to Theseus, Attica had always consisted of independent townships, each with its own council-chamber and magistrates, which consulted the king at Athens only in danger and sometimes made war on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Theseus, being a king of equal intelligence and power, abolished those councils and magistracies and merged them in the single council-chamber and town hall of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Private property was untouched — people kept their land and went on living where they had — but they now had one political centre, and every inhabitant of Attica counted as an Athenian citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides adds that the union was still commemorated in his own day by the Synoecia, a feast paid for by the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo, following Philochorus, says Cecrops first settled the people in twelve cities and that Theseus later united the twelve into one; he names eleven — Cecropia, Tetrapolis, Epacria, Deceleia, Eleusis, Aphidna, Thoricus, Brauron, Cytherus, Sphettus and Cephisia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch has Theseus canvassing them township by township and clan by clan, offering the powerful a state without a king in which he would be only commander in war and guardian of the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Aristotelian Constitution of the Athenians counted his settlement as the second of the eleven constitutions Athens had had, and the first that involved a constitutional point at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much of this is history is another matter: the Athenians dated it to the heroic age, and excavation in the Agora finds no radical change in where Athenians lived between the Bronze Age and the Early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What Thucydides describes is in any case a political union rather than a move, which is why he can say a few lines later that most Athenians still found it hard to leave their country homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.14–16, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.1.20, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D1%3Asection%3D20. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Theseus</i> 24, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41.2, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Maximilian F. Rönnberg, review of John K. Papadopoulos and Evelyn Lord Smithson, <i>The Early Iron Age: The Cemeteries. The Athenian Agora 36</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.09.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.09.09/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides describe the union as political rather than a move?","a":"Because private property was untouched and people kept their land and went on living where they had; what changed was that the townships' councils and magistracies were merged into one at Athens."},{"q":"Why did the townships need merging at all?","a":"Because from Cecrops to Theseus each had its own council-chamber and magistrates, consulted the king at Athens only in danger, and sometimes made war on him."},{"q":"Why is the tradition hard to test against the archaeology?","a":"Because the Athenians dated it to the heroic age, and excavation in the Agora finds no radical change in where Athenians lived between the Bronze Age and the Early Iron Age."}]},
{"id":"gr-278","num":278,"category":"Athens","question":"The claim of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was that its people had sprung from the soil and never come from anywhere else, and Pericles opened his funeral speech with it.","questions":["Erichthonius, whom Pausanias says had no human father but was born of Hephaestus and Earth, stands in the line of early kings that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built on.","The Periclean law of 451 BCE that confined citizenship to those with two Athenian parents gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> political force, since Athenians came to justify their status by birth and descent."],"answer":"Athenian autochthony","answerText":"Athenian autochthony","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Claim current</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Citizenship law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">451/450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Athenian autochthony</b> was the claim that Athenians had sprung from the soil of Attica itself and had lived there ever since, never having arrived from anywhere else. Herodotus has Athenian envoys make the boast to Gelon of Syracuse: theirs is the most ancient race of all, and alone among the Greeks they have never moved from one land to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides gives the same fact a cause, saying that Attica's thin soil made it not worth taking, so it escaped the faction that emptied richer lands and kept its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pericles opens his funeral speech with it — the ancestors dwelt in the country without break in the succession from generation to generation and handed it down free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditions behind the claim run through a line of early kings, and Pausanias gives them: Actaeus first, then his son-in-law Cecrops, then Cranaus, whose daughter Atthis gave the land the name Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Cranaus was deposed by Amphictyon and Amphictyon by Erichthonius, of whom Pausanias says that he had no human father but was born of Hephaestus and Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus knew a temple on the Acropolis of Erechtheus, said to have been born of the Earth, and in it the olive and the salt spring that Athena and Poseidon set as witnesses when they contended for the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Persians burned the temple the olive put out a shoot a cubit long the next day, and those sent up to sacrifice reported it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus also records the sequence of names the Athenians gave themselves — Cranaoi as Pelasgians, Cecropidae under Cecrops, Athenians under Erechtheus, Ionians after Ion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The claim did political work: Pericles' law of 451 BCE confined citizenship to those with two Athenian parents, and it has been argued that this pushed Athenians to define themselves by birth and descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 7.161, 8.44, 8.55, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.2, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.36, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.2.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Bruce LaForse, review of Susan Lape, <i>Race and Citizen Identity in the Classical Athenian Democracy</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2011.03.46, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.03.46/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg/1280px-Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg","title":"Athenian autochthony","desc":"Birth of Erichthonius: Athena receives the baby Erichthonius from the hands of the earthmother Gaia. Hephaestus is watching the scene. Side A of an Attic red-figure stamnos, 470–460 BCE.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg","alt":"Birth of Erichthonios on an Attic red-figure stamnos, Staatliche Antikensammlungen 2413"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides give the claim of unbroken descent a cause?","a":"Because Attica's thin soil made it not worth taking, so the region escaped the faction that emptied richer lands and kept the same people in it."},{"q":"Why were the olive and the salt spring kept on the Acropolis?","a":"Because Athena and Poseidon had set them as witnesses when they contended for the land, and when the Persians burned the temple the olive put out a shoot a cubit long the next day."},{"q":"Why is the claim read as doing political work rather than antiquarian?","a":"Because Pericles' law of 451 BCE confined citizenship to those with two Athenian parents, and it has been argued that this pushed Athenians to define themselves by birth and descent."}]},
{"id":"gr-279","num":279,"category":"Athens","question":"When Cylon seized the Acropolis, Thucydides says, most political business lay with the nine men holding the office of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in the classical city lasted one year.","questions":["A candidate for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was asked before the Council and a jury who his grandfather was, where his family tombs lay, and whether he had done his military service.","Herodotus dates the Persian arrival in Attica by naming the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of that year, Calliades, which is how Athenians fixed a date."],"answer":"Archon","answerText":"Archon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annual from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">683/682 BCE, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nine archons by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 580 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chosen by lot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">487/486 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"An <b>archon</b> was one of the chief magistrates of Athens, and in the classical city there were nine of them, holding office for a single year. The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the greatest and oldest offices were three: the King, who was ancestral, the War-lord, added because some kings proved cowardly in war, and the Archon, instituted last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were filled by birth and wealth, held first for life and then for a ten-year term, and the six Lawgivers were added many years later, once elections had become yearly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That text also says the archonship only became great late, since the Archon administered none of the ancestral rites but only duties added afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Solon's laws were set up in the Royal Colonnade, and there the nine swore at the Stone that if they broke one they would dedicate a golden statue of a man, an oath still taken in the text's own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Their power in the archaic city was real: Thucydides says that when Cylon seized the Acropolis, most political functions were discharged by the nine archons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the archonship of Telesinus, in 487/486 BCE, they were chosen by lot, tribe by tribe, from a list of five hundred put up by the demesmen, where before they had all been elected by vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the classical system a candidate faced a scrutiny before the Council and again before a jury, and was asked his father's name and deme, his grandfather's, his mother's, whether he had family tombs and a Family Apollo, whether he treated his parents well and had done his military service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the nine gave his name to the year, so Herodotus can say that the Persians reached Attica when Calliades was archon of the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Modern work is not bound to follow the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> on any of this, and Ste. Croix argued that selection by lot from a preselected list was not Solon's at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 55, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 8.51, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","David Whitehead, review of G. E. M. de Ste. Croix, <i>Athenian Democratic Origins and Other Essays</i>, ed. David Harvey and Robert Parker, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2004.08.16, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.08.16/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Stoa_Basileios.jpg/1280px-Stoa_Basileios.jpg","title":"Archon","desc":"Stoa Basileios.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stoa_Basileios.jpg","alt":"Foundations of the Stoa Basileios in the Athenian Agora"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the Archon instituted last of the three oldest offices?","a":"Because the King was ancestral and the War-lord was added when some kings proved cowardly in war; the Constitution of the Athenians also says the archonship only became great late."},{"q":"Why did selection by lot replace election from 487 BCE?","a":"Because from the archonship of Telesinus the nine were drawn by lot, tribe by tribe, from a list of five hundred put up by the demesmen, where before they had all been elected by vote."},{"q":"Why was a candidate asked about his tombs and his parents?","a":"Because the scrutiny before the Council and a jury tested descent and conduct together — his father's name and deme, his grandfather's, his mother's, his Family Apollo, and his military service."}]},
{"id":"gr-280","num":280,"category":"Athens","question":"Trials for deliberate murder, for wounding, for causing death by poison and for arson were held on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while unintentional killing went to other courts.","questions":["Members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were appointed from men who had been archons, which is why it was the one Athenian office still held for life.","In the archonship of Conon, Ephialtes stripped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the powers that made it the safeguard of the constitution and handed them to the Five Hundred, the People and the jury-courts."],"answer":"Areopagus","answerText":"Areopagus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Guardian of laws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until 462/461 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Powers cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">462/461 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Areopagus</b> was a council at Athens named after the hill of Ares on which it met, and its members were men who had held the archonship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says its official function was to guard the laws, but that it administered the greatest number and the most important of the affairs of state, and fined and punished offenders against public order without appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the archons were chosen by birth and wealth and the Areopagites were appointed from them, this alone of the offices was still held for life when that text was written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the time before Solon the Council summoned and chose on its own authority the man suitable for each office and commissioned him to hold it for a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Solon set it to guard the laws, left it watching over the greatest affairs of state, let it render its accounts to the Acropolis without saying why the money had gone, and gave it the trial of men conspiring to put down the democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The constitution stayed under the leadership of the Areopagites for about seventeen years after the Persian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then Ephialtes prosecuted many of them over their conduct in office and, in the archonship of Conon in 462/461 BCE, stripped the Council of the added powers that made it the safeguard of the constitution, handing them to the Five Hundred, the People and the jury-courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was murdered not long afterwards by Aristodicus of Tanagra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it kept was the gravest homicide: trials for deliberate murder and wounding, for causing death by poison and for arson were held on the Areopagus, while unintentional killing and the killing of a slave or a foreigner went elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hill carried its own stories: Pausanias says Ares was tried there first, that Orestes was later tried for killing his mother and dedicated an altar on being acquitted, and that the unhewn stones where accused and accuser stood were called Outrage and Ruthlessness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 57, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Areopagus._Mars_Hill._Temple_of_Theseus_-_D._Constantin._LCCN94513428.jpg/1280px-Areopagus._Mars_Hill._Temple_of_Theseus_-_D._Constantin._LCCN94513428.jpg","title":"Areopagus","desc":"Title: Areopagus. Mars Hill. Temple of Theseus / D. Constantin. Abstract/medium: 1 photographic print : albumen.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Areopagus._Mars_Hill._Temple_of_Theseus_-_D._Constantin._LCCN94513428.jpg","alt":"The rock of the Areopagus at Athens in a 19th-century photograph, with the Temple of Theseus beyond"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the Areopagus alone still held for life?","a":"Because its members were appointed from men who had held the archonship, and the archons were chosen by birth and wealth, so the qualification carried the tenure with it."},{"q":"Why did Ephialtes' reform not abolish the Council outright?","a":"Because he stripped only the added powers that made it the safeguard of the constitution, handing those to the Five Hundred, the People and the jury-courts."},{"q":"Why did the gravest killings stay on the hill after 462 BCE?","a":"Because trials for deliberate murder and wounding, for causing death by poison and for arson were the Council's own jurisdiction, while unintentional killing and the killing of a slave or foreigner went elsewhere."}]},
{"id":"gr-281","num":281,"category":"Athens","question":"After Damasias was driven from office the Athenians elected ten archons instead of nine, five of them from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, three from the rustics and two from the artisans.","questions":["Plutarch says Theseus gave the noblemen of Athens, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the care of religious rites, the supply of magistrates and the interpretation of the will of Heaven.","Before Solon the supreme offices at Athens went by birth and wealth, which kept them in the hands of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and filled the Areopagus from the same families."],"answer":"Eupatridae","answerText":"Eupatridae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled before</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Board of ten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 580 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Eupatridae</b> were the nobility of Athens before Solon, when appointment to the supreme offices went by birth and wealth and those offices were held first for life and afterwards for a term of ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says Theseus was the first to separate the people into noblemen, husbandmen and handicraftsmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To the noblemen he gave the care of religious rites, the supply of magistrates, the teaching of the laws and the interpretation of the will of Heaven, the three orders being thought to excel respectively in dignity, in usefulness and in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The arrangement fed itself, because the Council of the Areopagus was appointed from the men who had held the archonship, so the body that guarded the laws was filled from the families that supplied the magistrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beneath them the constitution was oligarchical in every respect: the poor and their wives and children were in slavery to the rich, were called Clients and Sixth-part-tenants, and could be seized with their children if the rent went unpaid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When the people rose against the notables the two parties jointly chose Solon as arbitrator and archon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was of the first rank by birth and reputation but belonged by wealth and position to the middle class, which is a reminder that the two qualifications could come apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The archonship went on being fought over after him: in the fifth year after his own archonship, in 590/589 BCE, no archon was appointed because of party strife, and none again in 586/585 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Damasias was then elected and held the office two years and two months until he was driven out of it by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The quarrel was settled by electing ten archons instead of nine — five from the Eupatridae, three from the Agroikoi or rustics and two from the Demiourgoi or artisans — a board dated to 580 BCE, with the office shared among the three orders rather than kept by one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Constitution of Athens</i> 13, trans. E. Poste (London: Macmillan, 1891), https://archive.org/details/aristotleonconst00arisiala. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 13, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Theseus</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 2, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the arrangement of offices feed the nobility's own power?","a":"Because the Council of the Areopagus was appointed from men who had held the archonship, so the body that guarded the laws was filled from the families that supplied the magistrates."},{"q":"Why is Solon a reminder that birth and wealth could come apart?","a":"Because he was of the first rank by birth and reputation but belonged by wealth and position to the middle class, while appointment to office went by both qualifications."},{"q":"Why were ten archons elected in 580 BCE instead of nine?","a":"Because party strife had twice left the office unfilled and Damasias had to be driven out of it by force, so the board was shared among the three orders — five Eupatridae, three rustics, two artisans."}]},
{"id":"gr-282","num":282,"category":"Athens","question":"Told at Delphi to seize the Acropolis at the grand festival of Zeus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> struck during the Olympic festival in the Peloponnese and made himself master of the citadel.","questions":["Pausanias could not say why the Athenians set up a bronze statue of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> despite his plotting a tyranny, and guessed it was for his beauty and his Olympic victory.","The men who killed the suppliants left behind by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called accursed, they and their descendants, and the Athenians drove them out of the city."],"answer":"Cylon","answerText":"Cylon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attempted coup</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 632 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cylon</b> was an Athenian of good birth and powerful position who had won at the Olympic games and married a daughter of Theagenes, tyrant of Megara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inquiring at Delphi, he was told by the god to seize the Acropolis of Athens on the grand festival of Zeus, so he took a force from Theagenes, persuaded his friends to join him and struck when the Olympic festival came round in the Peloponnese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says he never thought to ask whether the festival meant was in Attica or elsewhere, the Athenians having a grand festival of Zeus of their own, the Diasia, celebrated outside the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians flocked in from the country and laid siege to the citadel, and when most of them wearied of the blockade and left, the watch was handed to the nine archons with full powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus tells it otherwise, saying the suppliants were taken from the altar by the presidents of the naucraries, who at that time administered affairs at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Cylon and his brother escaped by Thucydides' account, while Plutarch has Megacles the archon coax the besieged down to stand trial holding a braided thread tied to the goddess's image, which broke of its own accord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those caught outside the sacred precincts were stoned, those at the altars were killed where they sat, and only the men who supplicated the archons' wives were spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From that deed the killers were called accursed and guilty against the goddess, they and their descendants, and the Athenians drove them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were later tried before three hundred jurors chosen for noble birth, Myron prosecuting; the guilty were sentenced to everlasting banishment and their bodies cast out of their tombs, after which Epimenides of Crete purified the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias saw a bronze statue of Cylon on the Acropolis and could not say why a man who had plotted a tyranny was honoured, guessing it was for his beauty and his Olympic win in the double foot-race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The History of Herodotus</i> 5.71, vol. 2, trans. G. C. Macaulay (London: Macmillan, 1890), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2456/pg2456.txt. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Cylon choose the wrong festival to strike on?","a":"Because Thucydides says he never thought to ask whether the grand festival of Zeus meant one in Attica or elsewhere, the Athenians having their own, the Diasia, celebrated outside the city."},{"q":"Why did killing the suppliants leave a curse on their killers' descendants?","a":"Because the men were taken from the altars and killed where they sat, so the killers were called accursed and guilty against the goddess, they and their line, and were driven out."},{"q":"Why could Pausanias not explain Cylon's statue on the Acropolis?","a":"Because he could not say why a man who had plotted a tyranny was honoured, and guessed it was for his beauty and his Olympic win in the double foot-race."}]},
{"id":"gr-283","num":283,"category":"Athens","question":"Solon repealed the laws of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, all except those concerning homicide, because they were too severe and their penalties too heavy.","questions":["Asked why death was the penalty for most offences, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have answered that the lesser ones deserved it and for the greater none heavier could be found.","Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> legislated for a constitution that already existed, and that nothing in his laws is worth mentioning except their severity in imposing heavy punishment."],"answer":"Draco","answerText":"Draco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Law code</span><span class=\"dt-v\">621/620 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Repealed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE, except homicide</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Draco</b> was the lawgiver under whom, as the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts it, a code of laws was first published at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That text dates his ordinances to the archonship of Aristaechmus, which is 621/620 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sets out a whole system under him: citizenship for those who provided themselves with arms, the nine archons and the treasurers drawn from men holding an unencumbered estate worth at least ten minae, and the generals and masters of the horse from men worth a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A council of 401 was to be chosen by lot from the citizens over thirty, nobody holding office twice until the whole number had been gone through, with fines for missing a meeting graded from three drachmae down to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> puts it differently, saying that Draco legislated for a constitution that already existed and that nothing in his laws is worth mentioning except their severity in imposing heavy punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch says one penalty was assigned to almost all transgressions, namely death, so that men convicted of idleness were put to death and those who stole salad or fruit were punished like those who committed sacrilege or murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked why he had made death the penalty for most offences, Draco is said to have answered that in his opinion the lesser ones deserved it, and that for the greater no heavier penalty could be found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The orator Demades said in later times that Draco's laws were written not with ink but with blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Solon established his own constitution the Athenians ceased to observe Draco's ordinances, all except those relating to homicide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch gives the reason for the repeal plainly — the laws were too severe and their penalties too heavy — so what Athens kept of Draco was the law on killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 4, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1274b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the ancient accounts of Draco's work disagree so widely?","a":"Because the Constitution of the Athenians sets out a whole constitution under him, while Aristotle's Politics says he legislated for one that already existed and that nothing in his laws is notable except their severity."},{"q":"Why was death the penalty for almost every offence?","a":"Because Draco is said to have answered that in his opinion the lesser transgressions deserved it, and that for the greater no heavier penalty could be found."},{"q":"Why did the Athenians keep only the law on killing?","a":"Because Plutarch gives the reason for the repeal plainly — the laws were too severe and their penalties too heavy — so the homicide ordinances alone survived Solon's constitution."}]},
{"id":"gr-284","num":284,"category":"Athens","question":"In 409/8 BCE the Athenians had <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut on a stone stele and set up in front of the Royal Stoa, which is why its wording survives.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a man who killed without forethought was exiled, and the 51 ephetai decided whether the killing had been unwilling.","Reconciliation under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to be granted by the dead man's father, brother and sons together, since a single objector among them prevailed over the rest."],"answer":"Draco's homicide law","answerText":"Draco's homicide law","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enacted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">621/620 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">409/408 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Draco's homicide law</b> was the one part of his code the Athenians kept when Solon repealed the rest, and its wording is known because they had it cut in stone again two centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 409/8 BCE, when Diokles was archon, the Council and the People decided that the inscribers of the laws should take the law over from the King and set it on a stele in front of the Royal Stoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives opens on the first axon: even if a man kills another not from forethought, he shall be exiled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kings were to pronounce a man responsible for homicide, whether he had killed or had planned it, and the ephetai, the appeal judges, were to decide the case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reconciliation could be granted by the dead man's father, brother or sons, but only by all of them together, since one objector prevailed; failing them the right passed outward as far as cousins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where a killer had no such kin, 51 ephetai decided whether the killing was unwilling, and ten men of a phratry, chosen by the 51 for their excellence, could let him back in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The law bound men who had killed before it, required a proclamation against the killer in the agora, and named who joined the prosecution: cousins, cousins' sons, brothers-in-law, fathers-in-law and phratry members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also protected the exile, since anyone who killed him while he kept away from frontier markets, Amphiktyonic contests and rites was liable as for killing an Athenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Demosthenes quotes a law giving the Council of the Areopagus cognizance of homicide, intentional wounding, arson, and poisoning where a man kills by giving poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those charges were still tried on the Areopagus in the classical city, while unintentional killing went to the court at the Palladium, which is the division the first axon already draws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>Decree to Republish Draco's Law on Homicide</i> (OR 183A; IG I<sup>3</sup> 104), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/104. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 57, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Aristocrates</i> 22, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0074%3Aspeech%3D23%3Asection%3D22. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Protos_Axon_%28Draco%29.png","title":"Draco's homicide law","desc":"Drawing of the Greek phrase \"ΠΡΟΤΟΣ ΑΧΣΟΝ\" (\"first axon\"), taken from an inscription containing Draco's law on homicide.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protos_Axon_%28Draco%29.png","alt":"Drawing of the words ΠΡΟΤΟΣ ΑΧΣΟΝ, 'first axon', from the inscription of Draco's homicide law"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the wording of this law known when the rest is not?","a":"Because in 409/8 BCE the Council and the People decided that the inscribers of the laws should take it over from the King and set it on a stele in front of the Royal Stoa."},{"q":"Why could one relative block a reconciliation with a killer?","a":"Because the dead man's father, brother and sons could grant it only all together, since one objector prevailed; failing them the right passed outward as far as cousins."},{"q":"Why did the law protect the exile it had banished?","a":"Because anyone who killed him while he kept away from frontier markets, Amphiktyonic contests and rites was liable as for killing an Athenian."}]},
{"id":"gr-285","num":285,"category":"Athens","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked the land of the rich in Attica and handed over a sixth of what it produced, which is where their name comes from.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who failed to pay could be arrested along with their children, since down to Solon's time all borrowing at Athens was on the security of the debtor's person.","Solon says he removed the boundary-posts implanted in the Black Earth, freeing the land the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had worked while paying a sixth of its produce to others."],"answer":"Hektemoroi","answerText":"Hektemoroi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested before</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abolished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>hektemoroi</b> were the dependent farmers of Attica before Solon, so called because they tilled the land of the rich and paid over a sixth of what it produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls them Sixth-part-tenants and Clients, says the whole of the country was in few hands, and adds that the poor, their wives and their children were in slavery to the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If they ever failed to pay their rents they and their children were liable to arrest, and down to the time of Solon all borrowing was on the security of the debtor's person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says a man who had pledged himself could be seized by his creditor, some becoming slaves at home and others being sold into foreign countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many were driven to sell their own children, there being no law against it, or to leave the country altogether because of the cruelty of the money-lenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The most grievous and bitter thing in public affairs for the masses was this slavery, though the same text says they were discontented about everything else too, finding themselves virtually without a share in anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The many being enslaved to the few, the people rose against the notables, and after a long and violent struggle the two parties jointly chose Solon as arbitrator and archon, who freed them by prohibiting loans secured on the person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Solon's own poetry, quoted in the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i>, is where his account of the arrangement survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He calls the Black Earth to witness that it was he who removed the many boundary-posts implanted in her, so that she who had been a slave was free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He says he brought home men sold abroad, some unjustly and some justly, and others who had fled from dire need until they no longer spoke their Attic tongue, and freed those suffering base slavery at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 2, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 6, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could a debtor's children be seized along with him?","a":"Because down to the time of Solon all borrowing was on the security of the debtor's person, so failure to pay the rents made the farmer and his children liable to arrest."},{"q":"Why did some of those sold abroad lose their own language?","a":"Because they had been out of Attica long enough that, as Solon's verse says of the men he brought home, they no longer spoke their Attic tongue."},{"q":"Why does Solon call the Black Earth to witness what he did?","a":"Because it was he who removed the many boundary-posts implanted in her, so that she who had been a slave was free — the mortgage stones being the visible mark of the arrangement."}]},
{"id":"gr-286","num":286,"category":"Athens","question":"Both sides of a long and violent party struggle jointly chose <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as arbitrator and archon, and put the government of Athens into his hands.","questions":["The laws of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were written up on the Boards and set in the Royal Colonnade, where everyone swore to observe them and the nine archons swore at the Stone.","Because the settlement disappointed both factions, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> might have made himself tyrant by taking either side, and chose to incur the enmity of both instead."],"answer":"Solon","answerText":"Solon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 – c. 560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abroad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Solon</b> was the Athenian whom both parties to a long and violent struggle jointly chose as arbitrator and archon, entrusting the government to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was of the first rank by birth and reputation but belonged by wealth and position to the middle class, which the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the other authorities admit and his own poems confirm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He laid the blame for the civil strife wholly on the rich, exhorting them to restrain their stubborn moods and to moderate their pride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the wisest of the Athenians turned to him because he was least implicated in the errors of the time, neither joined with the rich in their injustice nor caught in the necessities of the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Solon himself said that he entered public life reluctantly, fearing one party's greed and the other party's arrogance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> He established a constitution and made other laws, and from then on the Athenians ceased to observe Draco's ordinances except those relating to homicide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The laws were written up on the Boards and set in the Royal Colonnade, where all swore to observe them and the nine archons swore at the Stone to dedicate a golden statue if they broke one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He fixed them to stay unaltered for a hundred years, and when people kept coming to criticise and question them he refused to stay and explain, holding that everybody should carry out their provisions for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his own verse he says that nothing pleased his mind about compassing power by tyrannic force, nor that good and bad men should have equal portion in the fatherland's fertile soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The settlement disappointed both factions, so that he might have been tyrant had he taken either side, and he chose instead to incur the enmity of both and go abroad for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Solon acceptable to both sides of the quarrel?","a":"Because he was least implicated in the errors of the time, neither joined with the rich in their injustice nor caught in the necessities of the poor, though of the first rank by birth."},{"q":"Why did he refuse to explain his own laws to those who asked?","a":"Because he had fixed them to stay unaltered for a hundred years and held that everybody should carry out their provisions for himself, so he would not stay and interpret them."},{"q":"Why did a settlement that disappointed both factions leave him free of tyranny?","a":"Because he might have been tyrant had he taken either side, and chose instead to incur the enmity of both and go abroad for ten years."}]},
{"id":"gr-287","num":287,"category":"Athens","question":"Solon's cancellation of debts was called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a word meaning a shaking-off of burdens, since the people shook off the load they had been carrying.","questions":["Androtion held that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> relieved the poor not by cancelling debts but by reducing the interest on them, and by an increase in measures and in the value of money.","Three of Solon's intimates, told in confidence what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would do, borrowed heavily and bought estates before it was published, then kept the land and refused their creditors."],"answer":"Seisachtheia","answerText":"Seisachtheia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enacted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>seisachtheia</b> was Solon's relief of debt at Athens, a name meaning the shaking-off of burdens, since the people shook off their load.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he made the people free both at the time and for the future by prohibiting loans secured on the person, and enacted cancellations of debts both private and public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Solon was the first to soften an ugly thing with a kindly name, the Athenians afterwards calling harlots companions, taxes contributions and the prison a chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His first public measure, by that account, was that existing debts should be remitted and that in future nobody should lend money on the person of a borrower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his own verse Solon says he took away the record-stones that were planted everywhere in the mortgaged land, so that the Earth, before in bondage, was free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the measure actually did was disputed in antiquity: Androtion and others held that the poor were relieved not by a cancelling of debts but by a reduction of the interest on them, together with an increase in measures and in the purchasing power of money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that account the mina was made to hold a hundred drachmas where it had held 73, so a debtor discharged the same sum in money of lesser value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch reports that most writers took it for a removal of all debt, and that Solon's own poems accord better with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three of his intimates, told in confidence that he meant to cancel debts but not touch the land, borrowed heavily and bought estates, then kept them and refused their creditors, which brought him into great odium until it emerged that he had remitted five talents of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He pleased neither party, the rich vexed at losing their securities and the poor more so because he did not redistribute the land, and both turned against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 6, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the name itself part of the measure's history?","a":"Because it means the shaking-off of burdens, and Plutarch says Solon was the first to soften an ugly thing with a kindly name, as the Athenians later called taxes contributions and the prison a chamber."},{"q":"Why did ancient writers disagree about what the relief actually did?","a":"Because Androtion and others held the poor were relieved by a reduction of interest and a change in the coinage rather than a cancelling of debts, while most writers took it for a removal of all debt."},{"q":"Why did Solon's own friends bring him into odium over it?","a":"Because three intimates, told in confidence that he meant to cancel debts but not touch the land, borrowed heavily and bought estates, then kept them and refused their creditors."}]},
{"id":"gr-288","num":288,"category":"Athens","question":"The four ranks into which citizens were sorted by the yearly yield of their land are called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they fixed which offices each man could hold.","questions":["Plutarch says the lowest rank in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held no office at all but sat in the assembly and the law-courts, a privilege that afterwards proved of the highest importance.","It was already disputed in antiquity whether the cavalry rank of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was measured by yearly produce or simply by a man's ability to keep a horse."],"answer":"Solon's property classes","answerText":"Solon's property classes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Introduced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Classes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Solon's property classes</b> were four ranks into which the Athenians were divided by assessment, as they had been divided before, and which fixed what part each man took in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> names them Five-hundred-measure man, Horseman, Teamster and Labourer, the first three requiring 500, 300 and 200 measures of dry and liquid produce together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the Greek names Pentakosiomedimnoi, Hippada Telountes, Zeugitai and Thetes, and says the second were those able to keep a horse or reaching 300 measures, who paid a knight's tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The offices were held from the top three classes alone: the nine archons, the treasurers, the vendors of contracts, the Eleven and the paymasters, each assigned in proportion to a class's assessment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those rated in the labourer class were admitted to membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone, a share Plutarch says seemed at first of no moment and afterwards proved of the very highest importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As proof that offices went by assessment the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> points to the law on the treasurers, which orders them to be chosen by lot from the Five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> says Solon appointed all the offices from the notable and the wealthy, leaving the people only the power of electing the magistrates and calling them to account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How the second class was measured was already disputed when the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> was written, some saying it went by produce, others by the ability to keep a horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those on the second side adduced a statue on the Acropolis inscribed for Anthemion son of Diphilus, who had exchanged the labourer rating for the cavalry, with a horse standing beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch's account of Solon's valuations reckons a sheep and a bushel of grain at a drachma apiece, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> adds that long afterwards no man drawing lots for an office would admit to a labourer's rating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 23, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a share in the assembly and courts alone so consequential?","a":"Because the labourer class was admitted to nothing else, a share Plutarch says seemed at first of no moment and afterwards proved of the very highest importance."},{"q":"Why is the law on the treasurers cited as proof of the system?","a":"Because it orders them to be chosen by lot from the Five-hundred-measure men, which shows that offices were assigned in proportion to a class's assessment."},{"q":"Why was the second class's qualification already disputed in antiquity?","a":"Because some said it went by produce and others by the ability to keep a horse, the latter citing Anthemion's statue on the Acropolis, inscribed for a man who exchanged the labourer rating for the cavalry."}]},
{"id":"gr-289","num":289,"category":"Athens","question":"The richest of Solon's four classes were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose land yielded 500 measures of dry and liquid produce together, and from whom the treasurers of Athena were drawn.","questions":["Plutarch says those whose land gave a yearly increase of 500 measures wet and dry were placed in the first class and called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, above the horsemen and the teamsters.","Although the treasurers of Athena had to be drawn from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> adds that the man on whom the lot fell held office even if quite poor."],"answer":"Pentakosiomedimnoi","answerText":"Pentakosiomedimnoi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Introduced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Threshold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 measures a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Pentakosiomedimnoi</b> were the first and richest of the four classes into which Solon divided the Athenians by assessment, the name meaning five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says a man had to be rated in the class if the produce of his estate came to 500 dry and liquid measures jointly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below them came the horsemen at 300 measures and the teamsters at 200, while the rest were rated in the labourer class and admitted to no office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the same figure, placing in the first class those who enjoyed a yearly increase of 500 measures wet and dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The offices were shared out among the top three classes in proportion to the amount of their assessment, so the highest rating carried the highest posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One office was reserved to this class alone, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> cites it as proof that office went by assessment: the law ordering the treasurers to be chosen by lot from the Five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those treasurers were the ten Treasurers of Athena, one drawn by lot from each tribe, who took over the custody of the statue of Athena, the Victories, the other monuments and the funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Describing the Athens of its own day, the same work says that Solon's law about them was still in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It adds in the same breath that the man on whom the lot fell held office even though he was quite a poor man, so the qualification stood in the law without being applied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch's account of Solon's valuations reckons a bushel of grain at a drachma, which gives some measure of what 500 a year meant, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says that nobody drawing lots for an office would admit to a labourer's rating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 47, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 23, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the law on the treasurers cited as proof about office?","a":"Because it ordered the ten Treasurers of Athena to be drawn by lot from the five-hundred-measure men alone, showing that office was shared out in proportion to a man's assessment."},{"q":"Why did the highest qualification stand in the law without being applied?","a":"Because the Constitution of the Athenians says Solon's law about the treasurers was still in force in its own day, and yet the man on whom the lot fell held office even though he was quite a poor man."},{"q":"Why does a bushel of grain help measure what the rating meant?","a":"Because Plutarch's account of Solon's valuations reckons a bushel at a drachma, which gives some sense of what an estate yielding 500 measures a year amounted to."}]},
{"id":"gr-290","num":290,"category":"Athens","question":"The third of Solon's classes were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose land yielded 200 measures wet and dry together, and who held the ordinary offices but not at first the archonship.","questions":["Five years after the death of Ephialtes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became eligible for the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot, and Mnesitheides was the first to hold it.","Plutarch calls the class whose yearly increase amounted to 200 measures the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ranking them below the horsemen and above the labourers who held no office at all."],"answer":"Zeugitai","answerText":"Zeugitai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Introduced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Threshold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 measures a year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archonship open</span><span class=\"dt-v\">457/456 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Zeugitai</b> were the third of the four classes into which Solon divided the Athenians by assessment, ranking below the five-hundred-measure men and the horsemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says men had to be rated in the class who made 200 measures, wet and dry together, from their land, the lowest of the three thresholds Solon set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the same figure and the Greek name, placing in the class those whose yearly increase amounted to 200 measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both accounts define the class by produce alone, and neither attaches any military duty or equipment to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below them were the labourers, who were admitted to no office and took part only as members of the assembly and the law-courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Offices were distributed among the top three classes in proportion to the amount of their assessment: the nine archons, the treasurers, the vendors of contracts, the Eleven and the paymasters, so a rating decided not merely whether a man could serve but where.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> counts them among the notable and the wealthy from whom Solon appointed all the offices, the fourth class alone being admitted to none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One post was closed to them outright, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> citing the law that ordered the treasurers to be chosen by lot from the five-hundred-measure men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archonship was closed to them as well: all the archons hitherto had come from the horsemen and the five-hundred-measure men, while the teamsters held the ordinary offices, unless some provision of the laws was ignored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Five years after the death of Ephialtes, in 457/456 BCE, eligibility for the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot was extended to the class, and the first of them to hold the office was Mnesitheides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is this class defined by produce and nothing else?","a":"Because both the Constitution of the Athenians and Plutarch set it at 200 measures wet and dry from the land, and neither attaches any military duty or equipment to the rating."},{"q":"Why did the rating decide where a man served rather than whether?","a":"Because offices were distributed among the top three classes in proportion to their assessment, so the teamsters held the ordinary offices while the treasurership and archonship went higher."},{"q":"Why does the year 457/456 BCE matter for the class?","a":"Because five years after the death of Ephialtes eligibility for the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot was extended to it, and Mnesitheides was the first of them to hold the office."}]},
{"id":"gr-291","num":291,"category":"Athens","question":"Everyone whose land fell short of 200 measures was rated among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who held no office at all but sat in the assembly and the law-courts.","questions":["Plutarch says the share in the courts given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seemed at first of no moment, but afterwards proved of the very highest importance as most disputes came before those jurors.","When Athens broke into factions while Solon was abroad, the multitude of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood with the Hill-men under Peisistratus, being the bitter enemies of the rich."],"answer":"Thetes","answerText":"Thetes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Introduced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Threshold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under 200 measures</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Thetes</b> were the fourth and lowest of the classes into which Solon divided the Athenians, taking in everyone whose land fell short of the 200 measures that made a teamster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says they were admitted to no office at all, having a share in the membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the same, and adds that this last privilege seemed at first of no moment but afterwards proved of the very highest importance, since most disputes finally came into the hands of these jurors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He explains why: even in cases that Solon assigned to the magistrates for decision, he allowed an appeal to a popular court whenever anyone wanted one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> goes further, saying Solon founded the democracy by constituting the jury-courts from all the citizens, while bestowing on the people only the power of electing the magistrates and calling them to account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The word was older than the rating, and Plutarch uses it earlier in the same life for the men who tilled the land of the rich for a sixth of the increase, called Hectemorioi and Thetes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As a census class it was a residue rather than a threshold, since a man fell into it by failing to reach the one above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rating was not enforced for long, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says that when the presiding official asked a man about to draw lots for an office what rate he paid, nobody whatever would say he was rated a labourer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The class could also act together, and Plutarch says that when Athens broke into factions while Solon was abroad, the multitude of Thetes stood with the Hill-men under Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He calls them the bitter enemies of the rich, and says that although the city still observed the new laws, everyone was by then expecting a revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 29, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a share in the courts alone turn out to matter so much?","a":"Because Solon allowed an appeal to a popular court from the magistrates' decisions whenever anyone wanted one, so most disputes finally came into the hands of these jurors."},{"q":"Why is this class a residue rather than a threshold?","a":"Because a man fell into it by failing to reach the 200 measures that made a teamster, where the three classes above were each defined by a figure of their own."},{"q":"Why would nobody admit to the rating when drawing lots for office?","a":"Because the class was barred from every office, so a man asked by the presiding official what rate he paid had reason not to name the one that disqualified him."}]},
{"id":"gr-292","num":292,"category":"Athens","question":"Solon set up a second council beside the Areopagus, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a hundred men drawn from each of the four old tribes of Athens.","questions":["Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was to deliberate on public matters before the people did, and to let nothing come before the assembly without such deliberation first.","Plutarch calls the Areopagus the upper council and says that with it and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the city rode as it were at double anchor, less tossed by the surges."],"answer":"Council of Four Hundred","answerText":"Council of Four Hundred","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">From Solon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Replaced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Council of Four Hundred</b> was the second council of Solon's settlement at Athens, made up of a hundred men from each of the four tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he made it and gave the Council of the Areopagus the duty of guarding the laws, as it had overseen the constitution before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he set it up after noticing that the common people had grown uneasy and bold in consequence of their release from debt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its business was to deliberate on public matters before the people did, and to let no matter come before the popular assembly without such deliberation first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With two councils, Plutarch says, the city rode as it were at double anchor, and would be less tossed by the surges and keep its populace in greater quiet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The four tribes were the old ones, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> noting that there were four tribes as before and four tribal kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A council was not itself new, since the same work's account of Draco's ordinances describes a Council of 401 chosen by lot from the citizens over thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That earlier body fined a councillor who missed a sitting according to his rating: 3 drachmas from a five-hundred-measure man, 2 from a knight and 1 from a teamster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The graver business stayed with the Areopagus, which corrected offenders with sovereign power to fine and punish and tried men who conspired to put down the democracy, under a law of impeachment Solon laid down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch adds that the council took a joint oath to ratify the laws, while the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> counts Solon's settlement third among eleven changes of constitution and the Four Hundred of a later oligarchy eighth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 4, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Solon think Athens needed a second council?","a":"Because he had noticed that the common people had grown uneasy and bold in consequence of their release from debt, and with two councils the city rode as it were at double anchor."},{"q":"Why could no matter reach the assembly undeliberated?","a":"Because the Council's business was to deliberate on public matters before the people did, and to let nothing come before the popular assembly without such deliberation first."},{"q":"Why did the graver business stay with the Areopagus?","a":"Because it corrected offenders with sovereign power to fine and punish and tried men who conspired to put down the democracy, under a law of impeachment Solon laid down."}]},
{"id":"gr-293","num":293,"category":"Athens","question":"The popular court at Athens was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose jurors were ordinary citizens and whose right to hear appeals the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls the chief basis of the people's power.","questions":["Plutarch says Solon's laws were reported to be obscurely and ambiguously worded on purpose, so as to enhance the power of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its jurors.","In Aristophanes' comedy the old man Philocleon is of all men the fondest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cannot sleep for thinking of the water-clock."],"answer":"Heliaia","answerText":"Heliaia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">From Solon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Central court by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">462/461 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Heliaia</b> was the popular court of Athens, a tribunal of jurors drawn from the citizens, which in Aristophanes' comedy the old man Philocleon loves above all things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> counts the right of appeal to the jury-court as one of the three most democratic features of Solon's constitution, and the one said to be the chief basis of the power of the multitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its explanation is that the people, having the power of the vote, becomes sovereign in the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the same of the arrangement, that even in cases Solon assigned to the magistrates for decision he allowed an appeal to a popular court whenever anyone wanted one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lowest class gained most, since the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the labourers were admitted to membership of the assembly and the law-courts alone and to no office at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The two accounts then disagree about the intention behind it, Plutarch reporting that Solon's laws were said to be obscurely and ambiguously worded on purpose, to enhance the power of the popular courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> knows that opinion and rejects it as unlikely, putting the obscurity down to his not being able to define the ideal in general terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grants the effect all the same, saying that because the laws are not drafted simply or clearly many disputes arise and the jury-court is the umpire in all business both public and private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> makes the courts the whole of Solon's democratic legacy, saying he founded the democracy by constituting the jury-courts from all the citizens, for which some blamed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By Aristophanes' day, where the name is spelled Heliaea, the court had a pay and a temper of its own, and Philocleon swears by his Heliast's pay and wakes with his mind on the water-clock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 9, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>The Wasps</i>, in <i>The Eleven Comedies</i>, vol. 2, trans. the Athenian Society, Project Gutenberg ebook 8689, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8689. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D2%3Asection%3D1274a. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/AGMA_Klepsydra_-_Water_Clock_1.JPG/1280px-AGMA_Klepsydra_-_Water_Clock_1.JPG","title":"Heliaia","desc":"Klepsydra - Water Clock: reconstruction of a clay original of the late 5th century BCE. Ancient Agora Museum in Athens.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGMA_Klepsydra_-_Water_Clock_1.JPG","alt":"Klepsydra, a water clock, in the Ancient Agora Museum, Athens"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the right of appeal called the basis of the people's power?","a":"Because the people, having the power of the vote, becomes sovereign in the government, and Solon allowed an appeal to a popular court even from cases he had assigned to the magistrates."},{"q":"Why do the sources disagree about Solon's obscure drafting?","a":"Because Plutarch reports it was said to be deliberate, to enhance the power of the popular courts, while the Constitution of the Athenians rejects that as unlikely and blames his inability to define the ideal in general terms."},{"q":"Why does the effect hold whether or not the obscurity was intended?","a":"Because both accounts agree that since the laws are not drafted simply or clearly many disputes arise, and the jury-court is the umpire in all business public and private."}]},
{"id":"gr-294","num":294,"category":"Athens","question":"Both ancient accounts of the lawgiver quote <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at length, since the verses are where he justified what he had done and rebuked those who wanted more.","questions":["Plutarch says the Salamis elegy, one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was recited from the herald's stone by a man feigning madness to evade a law forbidding the subject.","Answering those who had wanted the land divided, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> say he stood at guard on every side, a wolf at bay among a pack of hounds."],"answer":"Solon's poems","answerText":"Solon's poems","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 594 – 560 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Solon's poems</b> are the verses in which the Athenian lawgiver argued his own case, and they survive because later writers quoted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says he composed poetry at first with no serious end in view, as amusement and diversion in his hours of leisure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later he put philosophic maxims into verse and interwove political teachings in his poems, not simply to record them but because they contained justifications of his acts, with exhortations, admonitions and rebukes for the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some said he even attempted to reduce his laws to heroic verse before publishing them, opening with a prayer that Zeus give those laws success and fame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing of wealth, he says two men are alike wealthy, one with much silver and gold and wide domains, the other with only enough for food and clothes and the enjoyment of child and wife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the parties chose him as arbitrator only after he had composed the elegy in which he marks with sorrow that Ionia's oldest land is being done to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same poem he tells the wealthy to refrain their stubborn moods and moderate their pride, and he attaches the blame for the civil strife wholly to the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A whole chapter of that work is given over to quoting him, including the lines where he says he gave the people grace enough and stood protecting both sides with a strong shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Answering those who had wanted the land divided, he says nothing pleased his mind about compassing power by tyrannic force, and that he stood at guard on every side, a wolf at bay among a pack of hounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch tells how he feigned madness to evade a law forbidding any proposal about Salamis, put a cap on his head, climbed the herald's stone and recited an elegy of a hundred verses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 3, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 12, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e5/Papyrus131%28f.6v%29-The-Aristotelian-Constitution-of-Athens.jpg","title":"Solon's poems","desc":"Papyrus 131 (f.6v) - Aristotle's Constitution of the Athenians, British Library.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papyrus131%28f.6v%29-The-Aristotelian-Constitution-of-Athens.jpg","alt":"Papyrus 131, verso, a leaf of Aristotle's Constitution of the Athenians in the British Library"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Solon turn from amusement to putting politics in verse?","a":"Because the later poems contained justifications of his acts, with exhortations, admonitions and rebukes for the Athenians, rather than simply recording philosophic maxims."},{"q":"Why is the elegy about Ionia's oldest land part of his appointment?","a":"Because the Constitution of the Athenians says the parties chose him as arbitrator only after he had composed it, marking with sorrow that the land was being done to death."},{"q":"Why did he feign madness to speak about Salamis?","a":"Because a law forbade any proposal about it, so he put a cap on his head, climbed the herald's stone and recited an elegy of a hundred verses."}]},
{"id":"gr-295","num":295,"category":"Athens","question":"After making his laws, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took him to Egypt and Cyprus for ten years, since he would not stay to explain the laws or to see them altered.","questions":["The reason Herodotus gives for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that he could then not be compelled to repeal a law, the Athenians having sworn to keep them ten years.","One stop on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Cyprus, where he persuaded a king to move his city down to the plain and was honoured when it was renamed Soli."],"answer":"Solon's travels","answerText":"Solon's travels","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Abroad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 years, from 593 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Solon's travels</b> were the ten years he spent abroad after making his laws, and the ancient accounts explain them in three slightly different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says people kept coming to worry him about the laws, so rather than alter them or stay and incur enmity he went abroad to Egypt, both to trade and to see the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He said he would not come back for ten years, thinking it unfair that he should stay and explain his laws instead of everybody carrying out their provisions for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he made his ownership of a vessel an excuse for foreign travel and obtained leave of absence for ten years, hoping the Athenians would grow accustomed to the laws in that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He quotes Solon's own remark that in great affairs it is difficult to please all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus gives a firmer reason, that he sailed forth to see the world so as not to be compelled to repeal any of his laws, the Athenians being bound by solemn oaths to abide by them for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Egypt, Plutarch has him living where the Nile pours forth his floods near the Canobic shore, and studying with the learned priests Psenophis of Heliopolis and Sonchis of Sais.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From them he is said to have heard the story of the lost Atlantis, and to have tried to introduce it in a poetical form to the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Cyprus he persuaded the king Philocyprus to move his city down to the fair plain below it, stayed to help lay the new one out, and was honoured when it was renamed Soli after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His interview with Croesus at Sardis some thought fictitious on chronological grounds, but Plutarch refused to reject a story so famous and well attested, and so worthy of Solon's magnanimity and wisdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 26, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 27, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.29, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Solon leave Athens for exactly ten years?","a":"Because the Athenians were bound by solemn oaths to abide by his laws for that term, and he would not stay to be compelled to repeal any of them."},{"q":"Why did he refuse to remain and explain his own laws?","a":"Because he thought it unfair that he should stay and interpret them instead of everybody carrying out their provisions for himself, and people kept coming to worry him about them."},{"q":"Why did Plutarch keep the meeting with Croesus despite the chronology?","a":"Because although some thought it fictitious on chronological grounds, he refused to reject a story so famous and well attested, and so worthy of Solon's magnanimity and wisdom."}]},
{"id":"gr-296","num":296,"category":"Athens","question":"In the course of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no archon was appointed at all in two separate years, and Damasias, once elected, had to be driven out of the office by force.","questions":["Three parties formed during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, named from where their farms lay: the Men of the Coast, the Men of the Plain and the Hillmen.","Where the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> finds three parties in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Herodotus finds two and has Peisistratus raise a third because he coveted the sovereign power."],"answer":"Athenian party strife after Solon","answerText":"Athenian party strife after Solon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Anarchy years</span><span class=\"dt-v\">590/89 and 586/585 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Factions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, by c. 560 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Athenian party strife after Solon</b> broke out once he had gone abroad, and it fills the space the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> leaves between his settlement, third in its list of constitutions, and the tyranny of Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That work measures the trouble by the archonship: for four years the city kept the peace, but in the fifth year after Solon's archonship, in 590/589 BCE, the strife was such that no archon was appointed, and none again in 586/585 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Damasias was then elected and held the office for two years and two months, until he was driven out of it by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians next chose ten archons at once, five from the nobles, three from the farmers and two from the artisans, which that work takes as proof of the office's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It says elsewhere that the trouble began because the settlement had disappointed both sides, the people having expected a general redistribution of property and the notables a return to the old system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Three parties formed, named from the districts where their farms lay: the Men of the Coast under Megacles son of Alcmaeon, the Men of the Plain under Lycurgus, and the Hillmen under Peisistratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their aims differed as much as their ground: the coast was thought to want a middle form of constitution, the plain an oligarchy, and the hill had chosen an extreme advocate of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the Hillmen stood those who had lost the debts owed to them and, out of fear, those not of pure descent, which the same work proves from the revision of the citizen roll after the tyrants fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch adds that the multitude of labourers was among them as bitter enemies of the rich, and that although the city still observed the new laws everyone was by then expecting a revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells it differently, making the feud a quarrel between coast and plain into which Peisistratus raised up a third faction because he coveted the sovereign power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 13, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 29, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Solon*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 11, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the settlement leave both sides ready to quarrel?","a":"Because it had disappointed them equally, the people having expected a general redistribution of property and the notables a return to the old system."},{"q":"Why were the three parties named after districts?","a":"Because they took their names from where their farms lay — coast, plain and hill — and their aims differed as much as their ground, wanting a middle constitution, an oligarchy and popular rule."},{"q":"Why did men of impure descent side with the Hillmen out of fear?","a":"Because their citizenship was insecure, which the Constitution of the Athenians proves from the revision of the citizen roll carried out after the tyrants fell."}]},
{"id":"gr-297","num":297,"category":"Athens","question":"When <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was born his father had been warned by Chilon at Olympia to disown any son he had, after vessels of meat and water boiled over without fire.","questions":["Summoned to the Areopagus on a charge of murder, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appeared in person to make his defence, and the man who had issued the summons took fright and left.","The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nineteen years of rule out of 33, where the <i>Politics</i> gives seventeen out of the same total."],"answer":"Peisistratus","answerText":"Peisistratus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 528/527 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tyrant from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">561/560 BCE, in three spells</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">528/527 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Peisistratus</b> held Athens as tyrant for the last stretch of a long life, and Herodotus introduces him as the son of Hippocrates, ruling an Attica divided into factions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That father, still a private man, was at Olympia for the games when vessels standing full of meat and water boiled over without fire, and Chilon the Lacedaemonian, who saw the marvel, advised him not to take a wife who could bear children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hippocrates refused the advice, and the son afterwards born to him raised up a third faction while the coast and the plain were at feud, coveting the sovereign power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had a reputation to raise it on: the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he had won great fame in the war against Megara, and Herodotus that he had taken Nisaea and performed other great exploits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Solon is said to have opposed his request for a bodyguard, calling himself wiser than those who did not know he was aiming at tyranny and braver than those who knew and held their tongues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus says that once in power he disturbed neither the order of offices nor the laws, but governed the city according to its established constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> agrees, calling him kindly and mild in everything, merciful to offenders, and willing to administer everything according to the laws without giving himself an advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both it and the <i>Politics</i> record that he was once summoned to the Areopagus on a charge of murder and appeared to make his defence, whereupon the man who had issued the summons took fright and left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He grew old in office and died of disease in the archonship of Philoneos, in 528/527 BCE, leaving two sons by his wedded wife, Hippias and Hipparchus, and two by Timonassa of Argos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two works disagree about how much of that time he ruled: both count 33 years from his first establishment, but one gives nineteen years in office and the other seventeen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 16, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 14, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 17, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.1315b, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D5%3Asection%3D1315b. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could Peisistratus raise a third faction at all?","a":"Because he had won great fame in the war against Megara, taking Nisaea and performing other exploits, so he had a reputation to build a party on while coast and plain were at feud."},{"q":"Why do the sources call his rule mild despite it being a tyranny?","a":"Because Herodotus says he disturbed neither the order of offices nor the laws, and the Constitution of the Athenians calls him merciful to offenders and willing to administer everything according to the laws."},{"q":"Why did the charge of murder before the Areopagus come to nothing?","a":"Because he appeared to make his defence, whereupon the man who had issued the summons took fright and left, so the case was never heard."}]},
{"id":"gr-298","num":298,"category":"Athens","question":"The first of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began with a self-inflicted wound blamed on his enemies, which persuaded the people to vote him a bodyguard of club-bearers.","questions":["The second of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made with a tall woman dressed in full armour and driven into the city in a chariot, heralds proclaiming that Athena was bringing him back.","The last of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was won by force at the temple of Pallenian Athena, after money from Pangaeus, hired soldiers, and help from Thebes and Lygdamis of Naxos."],"answer":"Peisistratus' three bids for power","answerText":"Peisistratus' three bids for power","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First bid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">561/560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second bid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 556 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Third bid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">546 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Peisistratus' three bids for power</b> were the attempts by which he seized Athens, was driven out twice, and came back at last by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the first he wounded himself, gave out that the opposite factions had done it, and so won a vote for a bodyguard, with whose help he seized the Acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus tells it with a wagon, saying he wounded his mules too and drove into the marketplace claiming to have escaped enemies, and that his guard carried wooden clubs instead of spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the sixth year the partisans of Megacles and Lycurgus made common cause and expelled him before his government had taken root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second was Megacles' doing: harried by faction, he offered his daughter in marriage and brought him back by dressing a tall woman as the goddess and driving her into the city beside him, heralds proclaiming that Athena was bringing Peisistratus back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus thought the device exceptionally foolish in men setting out to deceive the subtlest of the Greeks, but reports that the townsfolk took the woman for the goddess and welcomed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That hold lasted about seven years and broke on the marriage: the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he was unwilling to live with the daughter as his wife, Herodotus that he would not have children by a house under a curse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the third he left the country, gathered money near Pangaeus and hired soldiers, and in the eleventh year went to Eretria, backed by the Thebans, by Lygdamis of Naxos and by the knights there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Landing at Marathon he was joined by countrymen who loved the rule of one more than freedom, and met the Athenians at the temple of Pallenian Athena, a diviner prophesying that the net was spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Winning that battle he held the tyranny firmly, and disarmed the people at a muster at the Temple of Theseus, speaking so quietly that they came up the hill to hear while his men gathered the arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 14, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 15, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.60, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.61, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D61. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.62, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D62. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Return_of_Peisistratus_to_Athens_with_the_false_Minerva.jpg","title":"Peisistratus' three bids for power","desc":"Return of Peisistratus to Athens with the false Minerva. M. A. Barth, Vorzeit und Gegenwart, Augsburg, 1832.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Return_of_Peisistratus_to_Athens_with_the_false_Minerva.jpg","alt":"Engraving of a woman in armour standing beside a bearded man in a chariot driving into a Greek city, a crowd looking on"},"why":[{"q":"Why did a self-inflicted wound win him the Acropolis?","a":"Because he gave out that the opposite factions had done it and so won a vote for a bodyguard, with whose help he seized the citadel."},{"q":"Why did Herodotus think the second return exceptionally foolish?","a":"Because dressing a tall woman as Athena and proclaiming that the goddess was bringing him back was a crude device in men setting out to deceive the subtlest of the Greeks — and the townsfolk believed it."},{"q":"Why did the alliance with Megacles break over a marriage?","a":"Because he would not live with the daughter as his wife, Herodotus saying he refused to have children by a house under a curse."}]},
{"id":"gr-299","num":299,"category":"Athens","question":"The tithe on produce that funded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> paid for loans to poor farmers and for local justices who went on circuit so that men need not leave their farms.","questions":["Thucydides says the tyrants of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exacted no more than a twentieth of income, adorned the city splendidly and left its laws in force, keeping only the offices in the family.","Men were often heard saying that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the elder tyrant was the Golden Age of Cronos, and that the government grew much harsher once his sons had succeeded him."],"answer":"Peisistratid Athens","answerText":"Peisistratid Athens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tyranny secure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">546 – 510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sons ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">528/7 – 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Peisistratid Athens</b> was the city under Peisistratus and then under his sons, who the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says carried on affairs in the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says he disturbed neither the order of offices nor the laws, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> calls his administration moderate and more constitutional than tyrannic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He advanced loans to the poor so that they might support themselves by farming, with two objects: to keep them scattered about the country, and to leave them neither the desire nor the time to attend to public business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thoroughly cultivated land increased his revenues, for he levied a tithe from the produce, and Herodotus adds money collected from the district of the river Strymon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He organised the Local Justices and often went on circuit himself, inspecting and settling disputes, so that men might not neglect their farms by coming into the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On one such journey he found a man digging mere rocks on Hymettus, who said the farm grew all the aches and pains there are, of which Peisistratus took the tithe; pleased by the free speech, he made the farm tax-free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says their government was not grievous to the multitude nor odious in practice, that they cultivated wisdom and virtue, exacted no more than a twentieth of income, adorned the city splendidly and provided sacrifices for the temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That twentieth stands against the tithe, and he adds that the city kept its existing laws, except that care was taken to have the offices in the hands of one of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A grandson named after the tyrant dedicated the altar of the twelve gods in the market-place during his own archonship, and the altar in the Pythian precinct, whose faded inscription Thucydides had read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus has him hold Athenian sons hostage in Naxos and purify Delos of the dead buried within sight of the temple, while men were often heard calling his rule the Golden Age of Cronos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 16, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D64. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 17, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Peisistratos_aqueduct_Syntagma_Athens.jpg/1920px-Peisistratos_aqueduct_Syntagma_Athens.jpg","title":"Peisistratid Athens","desc":"Part of the aqueduct constructed by Peisistratos and exposed during construction of the Athens metro, now on display at Syntagma square.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peisistratos_aqueduct_Syntagma_Athens.jpg","alt":"A line of terracotta water pipes with inspection holes, laid in a trench and exposed in an open archaeological area"},"why":[{"q":"Why did lending to the poor serve the tyranny as well as the farms?","a":"Because it kept them scattered about the country and left them neither the desire nor the time to attend to public business, while cultivated land increased the revenue he tithed."},{"q":"Why did he send justices on circuit and go himself?","a":"Because disputes could then be inspected and settled in the country, so that men might not neglect their farms by coming into the city."},{"q":"Why do the sources disagree about what he took from the land?","a":"Because the Constitution of the Athenians says he levied a tithe from the produce, while Thucydides says the family exacted no more than a twentieth of income."}]},
{"id":"gr-300","num":300,"category":"Athens","question":"Guiding the Persians to Marathon in old age, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost a tooth in the sand and said the land was not theirs, his own share being whatever held it.","questions":["Thucydides argues that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the eldest son, since the pillar on the Acropolis names five of his children and none of either brother's.","After his brother's murder <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had the marchers set their arms aside, then picked out everyone found carrying a dagger rather than the shield and spear of a procession."],"answer":"Hippias","answerText":"Hippias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tyrant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">528/7 – 510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Exiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Hippias</b> was the son of Peisistratus who held Athens after him, the elder of the two brothers in whose hands affairs lay and, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says, statesmanlike and wise by nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides insists that he and not Hipparchus was the eldest and the reigning tyrant, a thing he says he had more exact accounts of than others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His evidence is the pillar set up on the Acropolis to record the crime of the tyrants, which names five children of Hippias and none of his brothers, and puts his name first after his father's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that the tyranny could not have passed so easily to a younger brother unused to authority, where Hippias had long been accustomed to overawe the citizens and be obeyed by the mercenaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Hipparchus was killed at the Panathenaic festival he was outside the city arranging the procession, and composed his features so as not to betray himself, sending the armed marchers to a spot without their weapons and picking out every man found with a dagger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> After that the tyranny pressed harder: he put many citizens to death, and his executions and sentences of exile in revenge for his brother left him suspicious of everybody and embittered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About four years later things stood so badly that he began fortifying Munychia, meaning to move his establishment there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also turned his eyes abroad, marrying his daughter Archedice to a man of Lampsacus whose family had great influence with Darius, and her tomb there carries verses naming her daughter, wife and sister to the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deposed in the fourth year by the Lacedaemonians and the banished Alcmaeonidae, he went under safe conduct to Sigeum, then to Lampsacus, then to the Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Twenty years afterwards he guided the invaders to Marathon, where he dreamt of his mother, lost a loose tooth in the sand, and said the land was not theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 18, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.55, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.58, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 19, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.59, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.107, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D107. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides insist Hippias was the elder brother?","a":"Because the pillar on the Acropolis recording the tyrants' crime names five children of Hippias and none of his brothers, and puts his name first after his father's."},{"q":"Why does he add an argument from the succession itself?","a":"Because the tyranny could not have passed so easily to a younger brother unused to authority, where Hippias had long been accustomed to overawe the citizens and be obeyed by the mercenaries."},{"q":"Why did the rule grow harsher after his brother's murder?","a":"Because his executions and sentences of exile in revenge left him suspicious of everybody and embittered, and about four years later he began fortifying Munychia to move his establishment there."}]},
{"id":"gr-301","num":301,"category":"Athens","question":"Where Thucydides makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the man who solicited Harmodius and then insulted his sister, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives both the love and the insult to a younger brother.","questions":["It was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who brought the poets Anacreon and Simonides to Athens, being fond of amusement and love-making and having literary tastes, while his elder brother controlled the government.","Thucydides says it was the sad fate of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that got him the credit with posterity of having been tyrant, when in truth his elder brother reigned."],"answer":"Hipparchus","answerText":"Hipparchus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">514 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Hipparchus</b> was a son of Peisistratus and the brother of Hippias, with whom affairs lay by station and age, though the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the government was controlled by the elder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That work calls him fond of amusement and love-making, with literary tastes, and credits him with bringing poets such as Anacreon and Simonides to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides is blunter about his standing: it was the sad fate which made him famous that got him the credit with posterity of having been tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his account Hipparchus solicited Harmodius, was refused, and resolved to insult him covertly, first inviting his sister to bear a basket in a procession and then turning her away as unworthy of the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives that love and that insult to the youngest brother, Thettalus, bold and insolent in his mode of life, whose conduct it calls the source of all their misfortunes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In that version Thettalus stopped Harmodius's sister from carrying a basket in the Panathenaic procession by saying something insulting about Harmodius as effeminate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both accounts end alike: at the festival the conspirators saw one of their number talking with Hippias, feared discovery, and killed Hipparchus by the Leocoreum as the procession was being formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the night before he dreamt that a tall and handsome man stood over him and told him in riddling verses to endure the unendurable, since no man does wrong without paying the penalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He put the vision from his mind and led the procession in which he met his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thucydides tells the whole story at length to show that the Athenians are no more accurate than anybody else about their own tyrants and their own history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 18, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.55, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.56, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.57, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.56, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides say his death is what made him famous?","a":"Because it was the sad fate which made him famous that got him the credit with posterity of having been tyrant, when Hippias was the elder and really supreme."},{"q":"Why do the accounts differ over which brother insulted Harmodius's sister?","a":"Because Thucydides gives the solicitation and the insult to Hipparchus, while the Constitution of the Athenians gives them to the youngest brother Thettalus, whose conduct it calls the source of all their misfortunes."},{"q":"Why does Thucydides tell the story at such length?","a":"Because he wants to show that the Athenians are no more accurate than anybody else about their own tyrants and their own history."}]},
{"id":"gr-302","num":302,"category":"Athens","question":"Thucydides says the Panathenaic festival was chosen for the deed of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it was the one day citizens in the procession could meet together in arms without suspicion.","questions":["Seeing one of their accomplices talking familiarly with Hippias, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took fright and rushed within the gates to kill Hipparchus by the Leocoreum before they could be arrested.","One of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed on the spot and the other tortured, until he taunted Hippias with giving his right hand to his brother's murderer and was killed in a rage."],"answer":"Harmodius and Aristogeiton","answerText":"Harmodius and Aristogeiton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The killing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">514 BCE, at the Panathenaia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tyranny fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Harmodius and Aristogeiton</b> were the two Athenians who killed Hipparchus, son of Peisistratus, at the Panathenaic festival, and whom Herodotus describes as men of Gephyraean descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides makes their action the consequence of a love affair, Harmodius being in the flower of youthful beauty and Aristogeiton a citizen of the middle rank who was his lover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the sister of Harmodius was invited to bear a basket in a procession and then turned away as unworthy, Aristogeiton grew more exasperated than ever and the two arranged everything with those who would join them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They waited for the great feast of the Panathenaea, which Thucydides calls the sole day on which the citizens forming part of the procession could meet together in arms without suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conspirators were few, for better security, in the hope that those not in the plot would be carried away by the example and use the arms in their hands to recover their liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On the day they saw one of their accomplices talking familiarly with Hippias, took fright, and rushed within the gates to be revenged first on the man who had wronged them, killing Hipparchus by the Leocoreum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Harmodius was killed on the spot and Aristogeiton escaped through the crowd running up, but was afterwards taken and dispatched in no merciful way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he was tortured a long time and named men of distinguished families who were friends of the tyrants, either to make his captors destroy their own innocent friends or because they truly were his accomplices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Unable to die whatever he did, he offered more names, induced Hippias to give his right hand as a pledge, then taunted him with giving it to his brother's murderer, and was killed on the spot in a rage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That work also denies the story that Hippias disarmed the marchers and searched them, saying men did not walk armed in processions then, which is exactly what Thucydides reports him doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.56, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.57, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 18, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.55, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.58, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the Panathenaea the day chosen for the attempt?","a":"Because Thucydides calls it the sole day on which the citizens forming part of the procession could meet together in arms without suspicion."},{"q":"Why were the conspirators kept few?","a":"Because that was better security, and they hoped those not in the plot would be carried away by the example and use the arms in their hands to recover their liberty."},{"q":"Why did they strike at Hipparchus rather than at Hippias?","a":"Because they saw one of their accomplices talking familiarly with Hippias, took fright, and rushed within the gates to be revenged first on the man who had wronged them."}]},
{"id":"gr-303","num":303,"category":"Athens","question":"The statues of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were carried off from Athens by Xerxes among the spoils, and afterwards restored to the city by Antiochus.","questions":["Athenian drinking songs about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised to wreathe a sword in a myrtle bough, and told Harmodius he was not dead but dwelling in the isles of the blessed.","Thucydides complains that the Athenian public credited the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with killing a tyrant, when Hipparchus was not the tyrant and the city stayed unfree for four years more."],"answer":"Tyrannicides","answerText":"Tyrannicides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First group</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried off</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Replacement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">477/476 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Tyrannicides</b> is the name Athens gave Harmodius and Aristogeiton after they killed Hipparchus, and the honours the city paid them were those of men who had set it free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Statues of the pair stood in the city, and Pausanias says some of the figures were made by Critius, the old ones being the work of Antenor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Xerxes took Athens after the citizens had abandoned it he carried those statues off among the spoils, and they were afterwards restored to the Athenians by Antiochus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two were buried in the public cemetery, in the company Pausanias lists there of Conon and Timotheus, Zeno and Chrysippus, Ephialtes and Lycurgus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts memorial offerings to them among the standing duties of the War-lord, beside the funeral games for those who had fallen in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Athenaeus preserves the drinking songs sung about them, one promising to wreathe a sword in a myrtle bough, the sword that laid the tyrant low, when patriots burning to be free gave Athens equality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Another tells Harmodius that though reft of breath he shall never feel the stroke of death, the happy heroes' isles being the bright abode allotted him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides set himself against the whole of it, writing that the general Athenian public fancy Hipparchus was tyrant when he fell, not knowing that Hippias, the eldest son, was really supreme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He puts the error down to the way most men deal with traditions, even those of their own country, receiving them all alike as they are delivered without applying any critical test whatever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor did the killing free the city: the tyranny pressed harder afterwards, and Hippias was deposed in the fourth year by the Lacedaemonians and the banished Alcmaeonidae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.8.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8%3Asection%3D5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.29.15, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29%3Asection%3D15. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 58, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>The Deipnosophists</i> 15.50, trans. C. D. Yonge, Bohn's Classical Library, vol. 3 (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1854), https://archive.org/details/deipnosophistsor03athe. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.20, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.59, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg/1920px-National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg","title":"Tyrannicides","desc":"Statue of Harmodius and Aristogeiton, Roman copy of Greek bronze, Naples National Archaeological Museum.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Archaeological_Museum_of_Naples_-_Tyrannicide_group_-_Harmodius_and_Aristogeiton.jpg","alt":"Two marble statues in a museum gallery, a bearded man with a cloak over his arm beside a younger man with his arm raised to strike"},"why":[{"q":"Why were their statues taken away and then given back?","a":"Because Xerxes carried them off among the spoils when he took an Athens the citizens had abandoned, and they were afterwards restored to the Athenians by Antiochus."},{"q":"Why does Thucydides set himself against the whole tradition?","a":"Because the Athenian public fancied Hipparchus was tyrant when he fell, not knowing that Hippias, the eldest son, was really supreme, and he blames a public that receives traditions without applying any critical test."},{"q":"Why is calling them liberators wrong on the events as well?","a":"Because the killing did not free the city: the tyranny pressed harder afterwards, and Hippias was deposed in the fourth year by the Lacedaemonians and the banished Alcmaeonidae."}]},
{"id":"gr-304","num":304,"category":"Athens","question":"Given as much gold as he could carry at one time on his person, one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled his boots, his tunic and his mouth, and strewed gold-dust in his hair.","questions":["Herodotus refuses to believe that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held up a shield as a signal to the Persians, calling them tyrant-haters as much as Callias or even more so.","The marriage of Agariste of Sicyon into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced Cleisthenes, who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy, and in two more generations the mother of Pericles."],"answer":"Alcmaeonids","answerText":"Alcmaeonids","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cursed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 632 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reforms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Alcmaeonids</b> were an Athenian family whom Herodotus calls men of renown even in the old days, their standing rising from the time of Alcmaeon and then Megacles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Croesus sent Lydians to consult the Delphic oracle, Alcmaeon helped them so zealously that the king summoned him to Sardis and gave him as much gold as he could carry away at one time on his person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came prepared, in a wide tunic and the most spacious boots he could find, packed both with gold-dust, strewed more in his hair and took as much again into his mouth, coming out swollen and hardly able to drag the weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Croesus burst out laughing and gave him all that and as much again, on which the family grew very rich, kept four-horse chariots and won with them at Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next generation raised the house higher, when Cleisthenes of Sicyon proclaimed that any Greek who thought himself worthy to be his son-in-law should come and be tried for the hand of his daughter Agariste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Megacles won her, and from that marriage came the Cleisthenes who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy, and two generations later the Agariste who dreamt she had given birth to a lion and bore Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against all that stood a curse, Herodotus noting that the family was said to be under one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides gives its origin: Cylon's companions were raised from the altar on a promise of safety and killed, so those who did it and their descendants were called accursed, driven out, and driven out again by Cleomenes, the bones of their dead taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Banished under the tyranny, they contracted to build the temple at Delphi and made it finer than the model showed, fronting in Parian marble what they had agreed to build in tufa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herodotus refuses to believe the story that they signalled to the Persians with a shield, calling them tyrant-haters as much as Callias or more so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.125, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D125. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.126, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.131, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D131. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.61, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D61. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.62, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D62. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.121, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D121. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Croesus double Alcmaeon's reward?","a":"Because he had been offered as much gold as he could carry on his person and came in a wide tunic and spacious boots, packing his hair and his mouth too, so that Croesus burst out laughing."},{"q":"Why did a curse follow the family for generations?","a":"Because Cylon's companions were raised from the altar on a promise of safety and killed, so those who did it and their descendants were called accursed, driven out, and driven out again by Cleomenes."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus refuse the story of the shield signal?","a":"Because he calls the family tyrant-haters as much as Callias or more so, having been banished under the tyranny and helped to end it."}]},
{"id":"gr-305","num":305,"category":"Athens","question":"The Spartans might never have taken the citadel in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the sons of the family were caught being smuggled out of the country and their parents came to terms.","questions":["The first Spartan force sent to bring about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> landed at Phalerum and was destroyed by a thousand Thessalian horsemen, its commander Anchimolius killed and buried in Attica.","Herodotus reckons the rule that ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 36 years, where the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> makes it 49 in all, seventeen of them after Peisistratus died."],"answer":"Fall of the Peisistratids","answerText":"Fall of the Peisistratids","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tyranny ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Fall of the Peisistratids</b> came four years after Hipparchus was killed, and it was brought about by a Spartan army rather than by any Athenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exiles headed by the Alcmaeonids could not manage it themselves: they fortified Leipsydrion on the slopes of Parnes, were besieged and dislodged by the tyrants, and were remembered afterwards in a drinking catch that mourned the good men the place threw away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Failing in everything else, they took the contract to build the temple at Delphi, which gave them money to spend on the Spartans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus reports what the Athenians say happened next, that they bribed the Pythian priestess to tell any Spartan who consulted her, on private business or public, to set Athens free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says only that she kept commanding it until she brought the Spartiates to the point, although the Peisistratids were their friends and strangers to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A first force under Anchimolius went by sea and put in at Phalerum, where the Thessalian king Cineas met it with a thousand horse, killed the commander and drove the survivors to their ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cleomenes then came by land with a larger army, routed the Thessalian horsemen with more than forty slain, and shut the family up within the Pelasgic wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus thought the siege would have failed, since the Spartans meant to stay only a few days and the defenders were well supplied with food and drink.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What settled it was that the sons of the house were caught while being secretly carried out of the country, on which their people agreed to quit Attica within five days on Athenian terms and went to Sigeum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two reckonings of what had ended differ: Herodotus makes it 36 years of rule, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> about seventeen after Peisistratus died, or 49 years in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 19, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.63, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D63. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D64. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.65, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D65. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.59, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the exiles take the contract to build the temple at Delphi?","a":"Because they had failed in everything else — dislodged from Leipsydrion and besieged — and the contract gave them money to spend on the Spartans."},{"q":"Why did the siege of the Pelasgic wall succeed at all?","a":"Because the sons of the house were caught while being secretly carried out of the country; Herodotus thought it would otherwise have failed, the Spartans meaning to stay only a few days."},{"q":"Why did a Spartan army rather than Athenians end the tyranny?","a":"Because the Pythian priestess kept commanding every Spartan who consulted her to set Athens free, until she brought the Spartiates to the point although the Peisistratids were their friends."}]},
{"id":"wh-151","num":151,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Lakes, rivers and grassland covered what is now the Sahara during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when a stronger summer monsoon carried rain far enough north to water a desert.","questions":["Saharan sites stood empty for roughly a millennium after 8,200 years ago, a dry interruption inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the people who returned afterwards kept livestock.","The Sahara has greened and dried many times over as Earth's orbit wobbled, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is only the most recent of them, ending as northern summers dimmed."],"answer":"African humid period","answerText":"African humid period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Interval</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 15,000 – 5,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 11,000 – 5,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arid pause</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,200 – 7,200 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>African humid period</b> is the stretch of the late glacial and early Holocene when a strengthened summer monsoon watered the Sahara, turning the world's largest hot desert into grassland, lakes and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began about 15,000 years ago and had closed by roughly 5,500, driven by precession, the slow wobble that brings northern summers nearer the Sun, and that dragged the monsoon rain belt north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not a one-off: more than 230 such green phases have been counted in the last eight million years, paced by the changing shape of Earth's orbit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plant waxes in sea-floor mud off West Africa put rainfall at a median of 640 millimetres (25 inches) a year, roughly ten times what that coast gets now, with the wet zone reaching 31 degrees north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water left its shape on the map: river channels linked the desert basins, and Lake Megachad covered some 361,000 square kilometres (139,000 square miles), carrying fish and hippopotamus across ground that is now sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> People followed the water, and hunter-fisher-gatherers with barbed bone harpoons settled beside the lakes; the dune field at Gobero in Niger holds about 200 burials from two quite different populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between them lies a gap of roughly a thousand years from about 8,200 years ago, an arid interruption that empties sites across the central Sahara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who came back kept livestock, and by 7,000 years ago herders were burying their dead at Takarkori in the Libyan mountains, having taken up herding from neighbours rather than been replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> How it ended is contested — abruptly within a century or two, or gradually and at different times in different places — and one Ethiopian record reads it as gradual but broken by decades-long droughts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> What the herders did to it is argued over too: grazing and burning have been proposed as an accelerant of the drying and, on one climate model, as what delayed the collapse by centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata and Peter B. deMenocal, “Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","Juan Cruz Larrasoaña, “A Review of West African Monsoon Penetration during Green Sahara Periods; Implications for Human Evolution and Dispersals over the Last Three Million Years,” <i>Oxford Open Climate Change</i> 1, no. 1 (2021): kgab011, https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfclm/kgab011. [Open access]","Juan C. Larrasoaña, Andrew P. Roberts and Eelco J. Rohling, “Dynamics of Green Sahara Periods and Their Role in Hominin Evolution,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 10 (2013): e76514, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0076514. [Open access]","Nick A. Drake et al., “Ancient Watercourses and Biogeography of the Sahara Explain the Peopling of the Desert,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 2 (2011): 458–62, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1012231108. [Open access]","Paul C. Sereno et al., “Lakeside Cemeteries in the Sahara: 5000 Years of Holocene Population and Environmental Change,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 3, no. 8 (2008): e2995, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0002995. [Open access]","Martin H. Trauth et al., “Early Warning Signals of the Termination of the African Humid Period(s),” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 3697, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-47921-1. [Open access]","David K. Wright, “Humans as Agents in the Termination of the African Humid Period,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 5 (2017): 4, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2017.00004. [Open access]","Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]","Chris Brierley, Katie Manning and Mark Maslin, “Pastoralism May Have Delayed the End of the Green Sahara,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 4018, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-06321-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Mega-Chad.jpg/1920px-Mega-Chad.jpg","title":"African humid period","desc":"Present-day Lake Chad and Lake Mega-Chad. Joshua Stevens, NASA Earth Observatory. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mega-Chad.jpg","alt":"Present-day Lake Chad and Lake Mega-Chad"},"why":[{"q":"Why is a green Sahara not a one-off event?","a":"Because more than 230 such phases have been counted in the last eight million years, paced by the changing shape of Earth's orbit, with precession dragging the monsoon rain belt north."},{"q":"Why does Gobero record two different populations?","a":"Because a gap of roughly a thousand years from about 8,200 years ago, an arid interruption, empties sites across the central Sahara, and those who came back kept livestock."},{"q":"Why is the end of the humid period contested?","a":"Because it is read either as abrupt within a century or two or as gradual and staggered by region, with one Ethiopian record showing a gradual decline broken by decades-long droughts."}]},
{"id":"wh-152","num":152,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Herds of cattle with their herders fill the middle phase of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, painted and engraved on massifs that are now among the driest places on Earth.","questions":["Radiocarbon on the pigments has failed again and again, so the chronology of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rests on style, on which figure is drawn over which, and on the deposits below.","Round-headed human figures, then cattle, then chariots, then camels: the phases of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follow the desert's own history from grassland down to sand."],"answer":"Saharan rock art","answerText":"Saharan rock art","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Round Heads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,900 – 7,400 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pastoral</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,500 – 4,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Horse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 – 2,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Camel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2,300 BP</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Saharan rock art</b> is the tens of thousands of paintings and engravings on the cliffs, boulders and rockshelters of the desert's mountain massifs, and the most visible thing its prehistory left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The great concentrations lie in the central massifs, the Tadrart Acacus in Libya and the Tassili n'Ajjer in Algeria above all; the Acacus alone holds 707 recorded sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figures are sorted into styles taken to follow one another in order: Wild Fauna, then Round Heads, then Pastoral, then Horse, then Camel, with Tifinagh, the Tuareg alphabet, last of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Round Heads are painted human figures whose heads are round and blank, set among Barbary sheep and antelope in hunts, and in dances nobody can now read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pastoral is the largest body of it, cattle drawn in outline with their herders; people really were keeping livestock in these mountains 7,000 years ago, and the engravings show their tethering stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Dating it is the hard part: radiocarbon on the pigments has failed repeatedly in both the Acacus and the Tassili, the samples too degraded and too polluted to trust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the chronology is built sideways, from the style itself, from which figure is drawn over which, from the varnish on the rock, from luminescence dates on sediment burying a panel, and from the layers below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An engraved stone built into something datable gives a firm answer, and cattle in the Pastoral style stand on ceremonial platforms at Messak raised about 5,430 to 5,150 years ago, some over buried cattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the art records is a desert that was not one: its earliest animals are elephant, rhinoceros, giraffe and hippopotamus, and the excavated bones agree, catfish and crocodile at Takarkori while the water lasted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then come the cattle, then the horse and the chariot, then the camel, each phase a record of the living the ground could still support, until it could support almost none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Marina Gallinaro, “Saharan Rock Art: Local Dynamics and Wider Perspectives,” <i>Arts</i> 2, no. 4 (2013): 350–82, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts2040350. [Open access]","Savino di Lernia and Marina Gallinaro, “The Date and Context of Neolithic Rock Art in the Sahara: Engravings and Ceremonial Monuments from Messak Settafet (South-West Libya),” <i>Antiquity</i> 84, no. 326 (2010): 954–75, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00067016. [Paywalled]","Savino di Lernia et al., “Inside the ‘African Cattle Complex’: Animal Burials in the Holocene Central Sahara,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 2 (2013): e56879, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0056879. [Open access]","Marina Gallinaro and Savino di Lernia, “Trapping or Tethering Stones (TS): A Multifunctional Device in the Pastoral Neolithic of the Sahara,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 1 (2018): e0191765, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0191765. [Open access]","Wim Van Neer et al., “Aquatic Fauna from the Takarkori Rock Shelter Reveals the Holocene Central Saharan Climate and Palaeohydrography,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 2 (2020): e0228588, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0228588. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata and Peter B. deMenocal, “Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Daily_scene_with_cattle.jpg/1920px-Daily_scene_with_cattle.jpg","title":"Saharan rock art","desc":"Daily scene with cattle in Tassili n'Ajjer park in Djanet, Algeria. The description and the title are just interpretation. IssamBarhoumi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daily_scene_with_cattle.jpg","alt":"Daily scene with cattle in Tassili n'Ajjer, Algeria"},"why":[{"q":"Why is Saharan rock art dated sideways rather than directly?","a":"Because radiocarbon on the pigments has failed repeatedly in both the Acacus and the Tassili, the samples too degraded and too polluted to trust."},{"q":"Why does an engraved stone built into a structure give a firmer answer?","a":"Because it can be dated by what it is built into: Pastoral-style cattle stand on ceremonial platforms at Messak raised about 5,430 to 5,150 years ago."},{"q":"Why do the animals depicted record a changing landscape?","a":"Because the earliest are elephant, rhinoceros, giraffe and hippopotamus, then cattle, then the horse and chariot, then the camel — each phase what the ground could still support."}]},
{"id":"wh-153","num":153,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Herds were kept in Africa long before any crop was sown there, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the older half of food production on the continent and the reverse of the Near Eastern order.","questions":["The first domestic animals in sub-Saharan Africa turn up near Lake Turkana about 5,000 years ago, brought down the White Nile as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread out of a drying Sahara.","Eastern Africans carry their own mutations for digesting milk as adults, quite separate from the European one, which is what thousands of years of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did to their genomes."],"answer":"African cattle pastoralism","answerText":"African cattle pastoralism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Sahara</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 7,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">East Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 5,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Southern Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 2,000 BP</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>African cattle pastoralism</b> is the keeping of cattle as a way of life, and it is the older half of food production in Africa, herds having been kept here long before any crop was sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the cattle came from is argued over: archaeologists have long held that Saharan foragers tamed the African aurochs, while the genetics reads them as Fertile Crescent cattle later crossed with wild African aurochs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 7,000 years ago people were keeping livestock in the Libyan mountains, and herding seems to have spread among the Saharans already there rather than arriving with newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rock art is full of them, cattle drawn with their herders, and they counted for more than meat: whole cattle lie buried under stone platforms at Messak, about 6,080 to 5,120 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the Sahara dried the herds moved, and the first domestic animals in sub-Saharan Africa appear near Lake Turkana about 5,000 years ago, most likely brought down the White Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Ancient DNA shows people moving with them rather than animals travelling alone, in two rounds of mixing: one in north-eastern Africa around 6,000 to 5,000 years ago, another near 4,000 as herders met local foragers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herding reached southern Africa only about 2,000 years ago, carried from eastern Africa by a migration the genomes show was heavily male-biased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming trailed far behind: crops and iron entered eastern Africa around 2,500 years ago, so for millennia herders and foragers lived alongside one another instead of one replacing the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Milk is much of why the cattle mattered, and eastern Africans carry their own mutations for digesting it as adults, quite separate from the European one and under strong recent selection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Cattle became wealth and sacrifice as much as food, an attachment already plain in those Saharan graves, where the burials were deliberate and the animals whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mary E. Prendergast et al., “Ancient DNA Reveals a Multistep Spread of the First Herders into Sub-Saharan Africa,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6448 (2019): eaaw6275, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6827346/. [Open access]","Daniel Pitt et al., “Domestication of Cattle: Two or Three Events?,” <i>Evolutionary Applications</i> 12, no. 1 (2019): 123–36, https://doi.org/10.1111/eva.12674. [Open access]","Mário Vicente et al., “Male-Biased Migration from East Africa Introduced Pastoralism into Southern Africa,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 19 (2021): 259, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-021-01193-z. [Open access]","Savino di Lernia et al., “Inside the ‘African Cattle Complex’: Animal Burials in the Holocene Central Sahara,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 2 (2013): e56879, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0056879. [Open access]","Nada Salem et al., “Ancient DNA from the Green Sahara Reveals Ancestral North African Lineage,” <i>Nature</i> 641, no. 8061 (2025): 144–50, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08793-7. [Open access]","Marina Gallinaro, “Saharan Rock Art: Local Dynamics and Wider Perspectives,” <i>Arts</i> 2, no. 4 (2013): 350–82, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts2040350. [Open access]","Alessia Ranciaro et al., “Genetic Origins of Lactase Persistence and the Spread of Pastoralism in Africa,” <i>American Journal of Human Genetics</i> 94, no. 4 (2014): 496–510, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajhg.2014.02.009. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Maasai_boy_herding_cattle.jpg/1920px-Maasai_boy_herding_cattle.jpg","title":"African cattle pastoralism","desc":"Maasai boy herding cattle, photographed in 2017. Helen Amirian, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maasai_boy_herding_cattle.jpg","alt":"Maasai boy herding cattle"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the origin of African cattle argued over?","a":"Because archaeologists have long held that Saharan foragers tamed the African aurochs, while the genetics reads them as Fertile Crescent cattle later crossed with wild African aurochs."},{"q":"Why is herding called the older half of African food production?","a":"Because herds were kept long before any crop was sown — livestock in the Libyan mountains 7,000 years ago, while crops and iron entered eastern Africa only around 2,500 years ago."},{"q":"Why does ancient DNA show people moving rather than animals alone?","a":"Because it records two rounds of mixing, one in north-eastern Africa around 6,000 to 5,000 years ago and another near 4,000 as herders met local foragers."}]},
{"id":"wh-154","num":154,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"A complete young cow lies in a clay-lined chamber under a stone mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of seven cattle tumuli dug at a basin that is now waterless desert.","questions":["The earliest claimed domestic cattle in Africa come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Bir Kiseiba, though both the dates and the animals' domestic status are still disputed.","Nine sandstone blocks stand in a line 100 metres (110 yards) apart at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a small circle of slabs beyond them that may have marked the sky."],"answer":"Nabta Playa","answerText":"Nabta Playa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,800 – 6,200 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wells and huts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,100 – 8,900 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Megaliths</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,500 – 6,200 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nabta Playa</b> is a large internally drained basin near the south-eastern edge of Egypt's Western Desert, 100 kilometres (60 miles) west of Abu Simbel and 30 kilometres (19 miles) north of the Sudanese border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is bone-dry now, but for several thousand years the summer monsoon filled it season by season, and people camped on its floor from about 10,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest sites are scatters of stone tools with no houses, wells or storage, left by groups who came when the water stood and went when it failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 9,100 years ago that had changed: settlements held large oval huts and smaller round ones, bell-shaped storage pits and deep walk-in wells, and the wells are the point, since they made it possible to stay in the desert all year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sheep or goats arrived from south-west Asia around 8,400 to 8,000 years ago, probably up the Nile, and sheep in time displaced gazelle as the main meat, while cattle bones stayed rare throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What made the place famous is the claim that its cattle were domestic as early as 11,000 years ago, which would make them the oldest in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That claim is contested on both counts: the dates and the domestic status are each disputed, the animal bones were never fully published, and no neighbouring area has produced anything so old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeozoologists now take undoubted domestic cattle there only from about 8,000 years ago, and the genetics reads African herds as Fertile Crescent stock later crossed with local aurochs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a dry century around 7,600 years ago the basin was settled again, and this time its people built: nine sandstone blocks in a line 100 metres (110 yards) apart, a small circle of slabs beyond them that may have marked the sky, and stone mounds over buried cattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seven mounds have been dug, one holding a whole young cow in a clay-lined chamber, and the excavators read the ground as a ceremonial centre showing rank centuries before anything comparable along the Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Fred Wendorf and Romuald Schild, “Nabta Playa and Its Role in Northeastern African Prehistory,” <i>Journal of Anthropological Archaeology</i> 17, no. 2 (1998): 97–123, https://doi.org/10.1006/jaar.1998.0319. [Paywalled]","Veerle Linseele et al., “New Archaeozoological Data from the Fayum ‘Neolithic’ with a Critical Assessment of the Evidence for Early Stock Keeping in Egypt,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 10 (2014): e108517, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0108517. [Open access]","Daniel Pitt et al., “Domestication of Cattle: Two or Three Events?,” <i>Evolutionary Applications</i> 12, no. 1 (2019): 123–36, https://doi.org/10.1111/eva.12674. [Open access]","Jessica E. Tierney, Francesco S. R. Pausata and Peter B. deMenocal, “Rainfall Regimes of the Green Sahara,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 1 (2017): e1601503, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601503. [Open access]","Martin H. Trauth et al., “Early Warning Signals of the Termination of the African Humid Period(s),” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 3697, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-47921-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Calendar_aswan.JPG/1920px-Calendar_aswan.JPG","title":"Nabta Playa","desc":"Nabta Playa Calendar Circle, reconstructed at Aswan Nubia museum. Raymbetz, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calendar_aswan.JPG","alt":"Nabta Playa calendar circle, reconstructed at the Nubia Museum in Aswan"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the wells change what was possible at Nabta Playa?","a":"Because the earliest sites are scatters of stone tools left by groups who came when the water stood and went when it failed, while deep walk-in wells made it possible to stay in the desert all year."},{"q":"Why is the claim for the oldest domestic cattle in Africa contested?","a":"Because the dates and the domestic status are each disputed, the animal bones were never fully published, and no neighbouring area has produced anything so old."},{"q":"Why is the later occupation read as a ceremonial centre?","a":"Because its people built nine sandstone blocks in a line, a small circle of slabs that may have marked the sky, and stone mounds over buried cattle, one holding a whole young cow in a clay-lined chamber."}]},
{"id":"wh-155","num":155,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Banana phytoliths and taro starch put independent farming in the New Guinea highlands at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where mounds were being raised almost seven thousand years ago.","questions":["Six phases of drainage over ten thousand years are stacked in the peat at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from mounded plots to ditch networks cut across a highland wetland.","Agriculture began without cereals and without herds in New Guinea, and the wetland at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is where that separate beginning was first shown to be real."],"answer":"Kuk Swamp","answerText":"Kuk Swamp","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cultivated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,220 – 9,910 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,950 – 6,440 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ditched</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,350 – 3,980 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Kuk Swamp</b> is a wetland in the upper Wahgi valley of the Papua New Guinea highlands, about 1,550 metres (5,090 feet) up and 12 kilometres (7 miles) north-east of Mount Hagen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It matters because the highlands were long taken to have received farming late and from outside, and the peat here holds a sequence of cultivation ten thousand years deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest phase, about 10,220 to 9,910 years ago, is plant use and some cultivation along the wetland margin, with the forest above already being cleared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 6,950 to 6,440 years ago people were raising mounds on the swamp edge, dry ground built up out of wet, and growing bananas on them in quantity, with the rainforest above turned to grassland at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 4,350 to 3,980 years ago they were cutting ditches instead, draining the wetland in regular networks, and the digging goes on through three later phases until about 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The crops are the point: taro, bananas, yams, sugarcane and pitpit, all of them roots, stems and fruit rather than grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> New Guinea made agriculture without cereals and without livestock, which is why it is counted a separate invention rather than something borrowed from Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence for it is microscopic, since crops like these rot and leave no seed: banana phytoliths, taro starch on stone tools, and pollen recording forest turned to grass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bananas are the clearest case of all, because the wild plant behind most cultivated bananas grows in New Guinea, so the crop was taken in hand here before travelling as far as Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Taro went the other way, east into the Pacific, where it was being grown on Efate in Vanuatu by about 2,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jack Golson et al., eds., <i>Ten Thousand Years of Cultivation at Kuk Swamp in the Highlands of Papua New Guinea</i>, Terra Australis 46 (Canberra: ANU Press, 2017), chap. 1, https://press-files.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/n2540/html/ch01.xhtml. [Open access]","Tim Denham, Jack Golson and Philip Hughes, “Phase 2: Mounded Cultivation during the Mid Holocene,” in <i>Ten Thousand Years of Cultivation at Kuk Swamp in the Highlands of Papua New Guinea</i>, Terra Australis 46 (Canberra: ANU Press, 2017), https://press-files.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/n2540/html/ch12.xhtml. [Open access]","Tim P. Denham et al., “Origins of Agriculture at Kuk Swamp in the Highlands of New Guinea,” <i>Science</i> 301, no. 5630 (2003): 189–93, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1085255. [Paywalled]","Xavier Perrier et al., “Multidisciplinary Perspectives on Banana (<i>Musa</i> spp.) Domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 28 (2011): 11311–18, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1102001108. [Open access]","Giorgia Camperio et al., “Sedimentary Biomarkers of Human Presence and Taro Cultivation Reveal Early Horticulture in Remote Oceania,” <i>Communications Earth & Environment</i> 5 (2024): 667, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-024-01831-8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Kuk_New_Guinea_2002.jpg/1920px-Kuk_New_Guinea_2002.jpg","title":"Kuk Swamp","desc":"The Kuk Early Agricultural Site consists of 116 ha of swamps in the western highlands of New Guinea 1,500 meters above sea-level. Satellite image acquired 7 May 2002. NASA/JPL, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuk_New_Guinea_2002.jpg","alt":"Satellite view of the Kuk Early Agricultural Site in the western highlands of New Guinea"},"why":[{"q":"Why is New Guinea counted a separate invention of agriculture?","a":"Because it made agriculture without cereals and without livestock — taro, bananas, yams, sugarcane and pitpit, roots, stems and fruit rather than grain — rather than borrowing a package from Asia."},{"q":"Why does the evidence for it have to be microscopic?","a":"Because crops like these rot and leave no seed, so the record is banana phytoliths, taro starch on stone tools, and pollen showing forest turned to grass."},{"q":"Why are bananas the clearest case of local domestication?","a":"Because the wild plant behind most cultivated bananas grows in New Guinea, so the crop was taken in hand here before travelling as far as Africa."}]},
{"id":"wh-156","num":156,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Nine of the ten primary branches of a single language family are spoken on one island, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reckoned to have started from Taiwan.","questions":["Madagascar was reached from southern Borneo and Rapa Nui from the opposite edge of the Pacific, the two far ends of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its 1,250 languages.","Rice and millet farmers crossed to Taiwan about five thousand years ago, and roughly a thousand years later the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, taking their descendants south into the Philippines."],"answer":"Austronesian expansion","answerText":"Austronesian expansion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Into Taiwan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,500 – 4,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Out of Taiwan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,200 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Remote Oceania</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,000 – 2,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">East Polynesia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1025 – 1290 CE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Austronesian expansion</b> is the spread of Austronesian-speaking people out of Taiwan across half the world, carrying a family of about 1,250 languages that some 300 million people speak today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before European ships crossed the same water five centuries ago it was the most widely spread family on earth, running from Madagascar in the west to Rapa Nui in the east and from Taiwan south to Aotearoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The case for Taiwan is linguistic: nine of the ten primary branches are spoken on the island, and every Austronesian language beyond it belongs to the tenth, Malayo-Polynesian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology supplies the dates, since rice and millet farmers crossed from south-eastern China to Taiwan about 5,000 years ago and their descendants began moving south to the Philippines around 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlement then ran through Island Southeast Asia between 4,000 and 3,500 years ago, reached the islands east of New Guinea by about 3,250, and crossed into Remote Oceania, which nobody had ever reached, by 2,800.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The advance went in bursts with long stops between them, and the last stretch took people to the last habitable places on earth, the archipelagos of eastern Polynesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> High-precision dating puts the Society Islands at about 1025 to 1120 CE and then one rapid pulse to every remaining island, Aotearoa among them, by about 1290.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Westward the reach was as long: Malagasy descends from the South East Barito languages of southern Borneo, and its speakers crossed the Indian Ocean about 1,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The usual explanation is farming spreading through country held thinly by hunter-gatherers, and the exception argues for it, since New Guinea developed agriculture of its own and kept its own languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genes tell a rougher story than the languages do: every Austronesian-speaking group sampled carries ancestry closest to the Indigenous peoples of Taiwan, but western Indonesians also carry mainland Austroasiatic ancestry, and two-thirds of Malagasy ancestry is Bantu-related.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Matthew Spriggs, Paul Geraghty and Yue-Chen Liu, “Austronesian Archaeolinguistics,” in <i>The Oxford Handbook of Archaeology and Language</i>, ed. Martine Robbeets and Mark Hudson (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2025), 466–92, https://reich.hms.harvard.edu/sites/reich.hms.harvard.edu/files/inline-files/Chapter25_OHAL_AustronesianArchaeolinguistics.pdf. [Open access]","Dang Liu, Albert Min-Shan Ko and Mark Stoneking, “The Genomic Diversity of Taiwanese Austronesian Groups: Implications for the ‘Into- and Out-of-Taiwan’ Models,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 2, no. 5 (2023): pgad122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10187666/. [Open access]","Janet M. Wilmshurst et al., “High-Precision Radiocarbon Dating Shows Recent and Rapid Initial Human Colonization of East Polynesia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 108, no. 5 (2011): 1815–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3033267/. [Open access]","Denis Pierron et al., “Genomic Landscape of Human Diversity across Madagascar,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 32 (2017): E6498–E6506, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5559028/. [Open access]","Mark Lipson et al., “Reconstructing Austronesian Population History in Island Southeast Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 5 (2014): 4689, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4143916/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Chronological_dispersal_of_Austronesian_people_across_the_Pacific_%28per_Benton_et_al%2C_2012%2C_adapted_from_Bellwood%2C_2011%29.svg/1920px-Chronological_dispersal_of_Austronesian_people_across_the_Pacific_%28per_Benton_et_al%2C_2012%2C_adapted_from_Bellwood%2C_2011%29.svg.png","title":"Austronesian expansion","desc":"Map outlining migratory paths of Austronesian speaking populations, including estimated dates. Adapted from Benton et al., 2012. Kikilipse, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chronological_dispersal_of_Austronesian_people_across_the_Pacific_(per_Benton_et_al,_2012,_adapted_from_Bellwood,_2011).svg","alt":"Map of Austronesian migration routes from Taiwan across the Pacific and to Madagascar, with dates"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the linguistic evidence point to Taiwan?","a":"Because nine of the ten primary branches are spoken on the island, and every Austronesian language beyond it belongs to the tenth, Malayo-Polynesian."},{"q":"Why does New Guinea support the farming explanation of the spread?","a":"Because the usual account is farming spreading through country held thinly by hunter-gatherers, and New Guinea, which developed agriculture of its own, kept its own languages instead."},{"q":"Why do the genes tell a rougher story than the languages?","a":"Because every Austronesian-speaking group sampled carries ancestry closest to Taiwan's Indigenous peoples, yet western Indonesians also carry mainland Austroasiatic ancestry and two-thirds of Malagasy ancestry is Bantu-related."}]},
{"id":"wh-157","num":157,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Fine toothed stamps pressed into wet clay in elaborate repeating designs are the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose potters carried them from the Bismarck Archipelago to Tonga.","questions":["The first people to reach Vanuatu, Fiji and Tonga belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their ancient DNA carries almost none of the Papuan ancestry their descendants have today.","Obsidian quarried in New Britain turns up in Fiji and even in Borneo, which shows that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept its sea routes open long after landfall."],"answer":"Lapita culture","answerText":"Lapita culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Bismarcks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,450 – 3,350 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dispersal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3,250 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Remote Oceania</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,000 – 2,800 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lapita culture</b> is the archaeological culture that carried the first people into the empty Pacific, beginning about 3,450 years ago, and it is known above all for its pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its potters pressed fine toothed stamps into wet clay in elaborate repeating designs, and the style appears fully formed in the earliest sites of the Bismarck Archipelago rather than growing up there by degrees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest of the kit is as distinctive: polished stone adzes, shell ornaments cut from many different species, and hamlets of rectangular houses, some of them raised on stilts over the reef.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pottery is there by 3,450 to 3,350 years ago, across the whole island group within a century of that, and the move south and east begins around 3,250.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 3,000 and 2,800 years ago its makers crossed into Remote Oceania, reaching Vanuatu, New Caledonia, Fiji and at last Tonga and Samoa, none of which any human had seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Obsidian quarried in the Bismarck Archipelago turns up as far off as New Caledonia and Fiji and as far west as Borneo, so the voyaging did not stop once a landfall was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Tonga and Samoa the advance halted for centuries, and the speech there drifted away from Fijian into Proto-Polynesian, which makes this culture the ancestor of Polynesian ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient DNA has rewritten who the voyagers were: the first Remote Oceanians descend almost undiluted from the population that spread Austronesian languages out of Taiwan, having passed the long-settled Papuans of New Guinea and the Solomons with hardly any mixing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Papuan-related people then pushed east from about 2,500 years ago into islands where Lapita had already ended, and most ni-Vanuatu today carry 80 to 88 per cent Papuan ancestry where the earliest arrivals carried at most 3.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The languages did not follow the genes, since Austronesian survived that near-total replacement, a pairing its investigators call extremely rare and perhaps unprecedented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jim Specht, “Small Islands in the Big Picture: The Formative Period of Lapita in the Bismarck Archipelago,” in <i>Oceanic Explorations: Lapita and Western Pacific Settlement</i>, ed. Stuart Bedford, Christophe Sand and Sean P. Connaughton, Terra Australis 26 (Canberra: ANU Press, 2007), chap. 3, https://press-files.anu.edu.au/downloads/press/p61051/pdf/ch0327.pdf. [Open access]","Matthew Spriggs, Paul Geraghty and Yue-Chen Liu, “Austronesian Archaeolinguistics,” in <i>The Oxford Handbook of Archaeology and Language</i>, ed. Martine Robbeets and Mark Hudson (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2025), 466–92, https://reich.hms.harvard.edu/sites/reich.hms.harvard.edu/files/inline-files/Chapter25_OHAL_AustronesianArchaeolinguistics.pdf. [Open access]","Pontus Skoglund et al., “Genomic Insights into the Peopling of the Southwest Pacific,” <i>Nature</i> 538, no. 7626 (2016): 510–13, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5515717/. [Open access]","Mark Lipson et al., “Population Turnover in Remote Oceania Shortly after Initial Settlement,” <i>Current Biology</i> 28, no. 7 (2018): 1157–65, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5882562/. [Open access]","Cosimo Posth et al., “Language Continuity despite Population Replacement in Remote Oceania,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 2, no. 4 (2018): 731–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5868730/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Lapita_potsherd_from_Fiji_with_face_design.jpg","title":"Lapita culture","desc":"Anthropomorphic designs on Lapita potsherds are rarely found in Fiji. Patrick Nunn, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lapita_potsherd_from_Fiji_with_face_design.jpg","alt":"Potsherd stamped with a face design, Lapita culture, Fiji"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the pottery style said to appear fully formed?","a":"Because its elaborate stamped designs are already complete in the earliest sites of the Bismarck Archipelago rather than growing up there by degrees."},{"q":"Why does obsidian show the voyaging did not stop at landfall?","a":"Because stone quarried in the Bismarck Archipelago turns up as far off as New Caledonia and Fiji and as far west as Borneo."},{"q":"Why is the pairing of languages and genes in Vanuatu called extremely rare?","a":"Because most ni-Vanuatu today carry 80 to 88 per cent Papuan ancestry where the earliest arrivals carried at most 3, and yet Austronesian speech survived that near-total replacement."}]},
{"id":"wh-158","num":158,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Squash, sunflower, marshelder and goosefoot were all brought under cultivation beside the rivers of what is now the eastern United States, which is what makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an independent invention.","questions":["Maize arrived late and was folded into the fields of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than sweeping it away, and several of its crops survived until European colonisation.","Seeds that grew larger and coats that grew thinner are the marks of domestication in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose oldest dated crop is a squash of about five thousand years ago."],"answer":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","answerText":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Squash</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,025 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sunflower</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,840 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A crop complex</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 3,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Maize integrated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,100 – 500 BP</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Eastern Agricultural Complex</b> is the name for the crops that Indigenous people domesticated in eastern North America, one of about ten places on earth where farming was worked out rather than borrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four species carry the case — pepo squash, sunflower, marshelder and goosefoot — each showing the marks of cultivation in seeds that grew larger or in coats that grew thinner as people sowed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Direct dating of the seeds themselves puts squash first at about 5,025 years ago, sunflower at 4,840, marshelder at 4,400 and goosefoot at 3,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 3,800 years ago these were no longer scattered single crops but a set: at Riverton in Illinois at least five domesticated seed plants were being grown together in one short occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The starchy seeds joined later, erect knotweed and maygrass and little barley among them, and through the Middle and Late Woodland centuries the group grows steadily more visible and more consistent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Why people began is argued over, but the population of the region rose sharply in the thousand years before the first domesticates, which suggests pressure on wild foods rather than leisurely tinkering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That the work happened here at all was contested, since squash, sunflower and goosefoot were each proposed as early arrivals from Mexico, and the question has been settled in eastern North America's favour for all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sequencing has since gone straight to the evidence: sunflower seeds 3,100 years old from a rockshelter in Arkansas carry organelle DNA matching modern cultivated sunflowers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Maize came late and did not sweep the rest away, being folded into the existing fields after about 1,100 years ago and only gradually becoming the more important crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Several of these crops fell out of cultivation only around European colonisation, and the domesticated forms of marshelder, goosefoot and erect knotweed are extinct, which is why they are increasingly called sleeping rather than lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Bruce D. Smith, “Eastern North America as an Independent Center of Plant Domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 103, no. 33 (2006): 12223–28, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1567861/. [Open access]","Bruce D. Smith and Richard A. Yarnell, “Initial Formation of an Indigenous Crop Complex in Eastern North America at 3800 B.P.,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 16 (2009): 6561–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2666091/. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The Sleeping Crops of Eastern North America: A New Synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12079124/. [Open access]","Elic M. Weitzel and Brian F. Codding, “Population Growth as a Driver of Initial Domestication in Eastern North America,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 3, no. 8 (2016): 160319, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5108960/. [Open access]","Nathan Wales et al., “Ancient DNA Reveals the Timing and Persistence of Organellar Genetic Bottlenecks over 3,000 Years of Sunflower Domestication and Improvement,” <i>Evolutionary Applications</i> 12, no. 1 (2019): 38–53, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6304678/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Chenopodium_berlandieri_NPS-1.jpg/1920px-Chenopodium_berlandieri_NPS-1.jpg","title":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","desc":"Chenopodium berlandieri at Wind Cave National Park, South Dakota, USA. Jim Pisarowicz, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chenopodium_berlandieri_NPS-1.jpg","alt":"A goosefoot plant, Chenopodium berlandieri, growing wild in South Dakota"},"why":[{"q":"Why is eastern North America counted as an independent centre?","a":"Because squash, sunflower and goosefoot were each proposed as early arrivals from Mexico and the question has been settled in eastern North America's favour for all three."},{"q":"Why might people there have begun cultivating at all?","a":"Because the population of the region rose sharply in the thousand years before the first domesticates, which suggests pressure on wild foods rather than leisurely tinkering."},{"q":"Why are these crops increasingly called sleeping rather than lost?","a":"Because several fell out of cultivation only around European colonisation, and the domesticated forms of marshelder, goosefoot and erect knotweed are extinct rather than never having existed."}]},
{"id":"wh-159","num":159,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Six earthen ridges wrap a plaza in northeastern Louisiana at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where foragers moved a million cubic metres (1.3 million cubic yards) of earth without farming, draft animals or wheels.","questions":["Mound A rose in no more than 90 days, the second-largest earthen mound in North America and the centrepiece of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose builders never farmed.","Soapstone from the Appalachians and stone carried 1,100 kilometres (700 miles) turn up at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose exchange network reached across half a continent."],"answer":"Poverty Point","answerText":"Poverty Point","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,700 – 3,100 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earthworks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,300 – 3,200 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mound A</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 90 days or less</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2014</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Poverty Point</b> is a vast earthwork complex on a bluff above the Mississippi floodplain in northeastern Louisiana, raised by people who did not farm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Six concentric, semi-elliptical ridges wrap a flat plaza across roughly 200 hectares (500 acres), the outermost of them about 1.14 kilometres (0.7 miles) from end to end, and people lived on the ridges for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Five mounds belong to the main period and a sixth was added about 700 CE; the largest, Mound A, is the second-biggest earthen mound in North America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mound A alone holds some 238,500 cubic metres (312,000 cubic yards) of earth and the whole landscape about a million, reckoned at roughly five million hours of work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It went up in no more than 90 days, which is a burst of concentrated work rather than the slow accumulation such a mound is usually taken to record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The builders hunted, fished and gathered, and had neither draft animals nor wheels to move any of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is what makes the site awkward, since building on this scale was long held to require farming and a ranked society to command the labour, and no sign of such ranking has been found here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stone came from a very long way off: soapstone from the southern Appalachians, novaculite from central Arkansas about 280 kilometres (175 miles) away, and material from sources up to 1,100 kilometres (700 miles) distant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Survey has since found 36 circles of timber posts, some 62 metres (203 feet) across, buried beneath the plaza, and the baked clay objects the site is named for turn up across the region as its signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronology is being rewritten as it is redated: the earthworks now look like short bursts around 3,300 to 3,200 years ago at the end of an occupation of a few centuries, and the place reads less as the source of a culture than as where several traditions converged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Seth B. Grooms, Grace M. V. Ward and Tristram R. Kidder, “Convergence at Poverty Point: A Revised Chronology of the Late Archaic Lower Mississippi Valley,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 396 (2023): 1453–69, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.155. [Open access]","Tristram R. Kidder and Seth B. Grooms, “Chronological Hygiene and Bayesian Modeling of Poverty Point Sites in the Lower Mississippi Valley, circa 4200 to 3200 cal BP,” <i>American Antiquity</i> 89, no. 1 (2024): 98–118, https://doi.org/10.1017/aaq.2023.85. [Open access]","Diana M. Greenlee, “Poverty Point,” <i>64 Parishes</i>, Louisiana Endowment for the Humanities, last updated 26 February 2024, https://64parishes.org/entry/poverty-point. [Open access]","Diana M. Greenlee et al., “Dendrogeomorphological Analysis of Earthwork Stability at Poverty Point SHS, Louisiana,” National Center for Preservation Technology and Training, National Park Service, 30 September 2013, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/dendrogeomorphological-analysis-of-earthwork-stability-at-poverty-point-shs-louisiana.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Poverty Point National Monument,” accessed 12 August 2026, https://www.nps.gov/popo/index.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Mound_A_at_the_Monumental_Earthworks_Poverty_Point_World_Heritage_Site.jpg/1920px-Mound_A_at_the_Monumental_Earthworks_Poverty_Point_World_Heritage_Site.jpg","title":"Poverty Point","desc":"Mound A at the Monumental Earthworks of Poverty Point World Heritage Site. Jennifer R. Trotter, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mound_A_at_the_Monumental_Earthworks_Poverty_Point_World_Heritage_Site.jpg","alt":"Mound A at Poverty Point seen from the air through low cloud"},"why":[{"q":"Why is Poverty Point awkward for the usual account of monument building?","a":"Because building on this scale was long held to require farming and a ranked society to command the labour, and its builders hunted, fished and gathered with no sign of such ranking found."},{"q":"Why does Mound A's construction time matter?","a":"Because it went up in no more than 90 days, which is a burst of concentrated work rather than the slow accumulation such a mound is usually taken to record."},{"q":"Why is the site now read as a convergence rather than a source?","a":"Because redating makes the earthworks short bursts around 3,300 to 3,200 years ago at the end of an occupation of a few centuries, so several traditions met there rather than one radiating out."}]},
{"id":"wh-160","num":160,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Potatoes, quinoa, llamas and guinea pigs were all taken from wild ancestors on the same slopes, which is what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an invention rather than a borrowing.","questions":["Starch ground into stone tools at a village 3,890 metres (12,760 feet) up beside Lake Titicaca is some of the best evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and for where the potato began.","The animal half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the only large herd animals in the Americas from the guanaco and the vicuña on the high puna."],"answer":"Andean domestication","answerText":"Andean domestication","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Camelid herding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 – 8,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Size change</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,100 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Guinea pig</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 4,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Potato in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3400 – 1600 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Andean domestication</b> is the independent working out of farming and herding along the western spine of South America, one of a handful of places on earth where both were invented rather than borrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The crops came from wild relatives growing on the same slopes: potato, oca and ullucu among the tubers, quinoa, cañihua and amaranth grown for their seed, and beans, peppers, squashes, cotton and coca beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seed crops and root crops both mattered, and it is the tubers, grown for the starch-rich organs they store underground, that set the region apart from the cereal cradles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The potato was taken in somewhere in the high south-central Andes, and starch grains off grinding stones at Jiskairumoko, a village 3,890 metres (12,760 feet) up in the Titicaca basin, put it in use there between about 3400 and 1600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fifty of the 141 grains taken from fourteen manos and metates were consistent with cultivated potato, which is direct evidence of early use inside the plant's own homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The animals are the other half and the rarer achievement, this being the only cradle in the Americas to produce large herd animals: the llama from the guanaco, and the alpaca entangled with the vicuña.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When is argued over, since a broad reckoning puts camelid herding between 9,000 and 8,000 years ago while the first size changes visible in the bones come around 7,100, with larger animals spreading widely between 5,800 and 4,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The gap is not a contradiction, because a herding relationship runs well ahead of anything a skeleton is able to record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The guinea pig was being kept by roughly 4,000 years ago, and ancient DNA has since pointed to a second and separate centre for it in the eastern Colombian highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the package could carry shows in the Titicaca basin, where quinoa and tubers fed growing communities for two thousand years and the llama supplied transport, wool and dung as well as meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Barbara Pickersgill, “Domestication of Plants in the Americas: Insights from Mendelian and Molecular Genetics,” <i>Annals of Botany</i> 100, no. 5 (2007): 925–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2759216/. [Open access]","Claudia U. Rumold and Mark S. Aldenderfer, “Late Archaic–Early Formative Period Microbotanical Evidence for Potato at Jiskairumoko in the Titicaca Basin of Southern Peru,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 48 (2016): 13672–77, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5137686/. [Open access]","Hugo D. Yacobaccio, “The Domestication of South American Camelids: A Review,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 43–51, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214430/. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi and Benjamin Arbuckle, “Animal Domestication: From Distant Past to Current Development and Issues,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 6–9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214435/. [Open access]","Edana Lord et al., “Ancient DNA of Guinea Pigs (<i>Cavia</i> spp.) Indicates a Probable New Center of Domestication and Pathways of Global Distribution,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 8901, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7264122/. [Open access]","Melanie J. Miller et al., “Quinoa, Potatoes, and Llamas Fueled Emergent Social Complexity in the Lake Titicaca Basin of the Andes,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 49 (2021): e2113395118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8670472/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Types_of_Potatoes_in_Peru.jpg/1280px-Types_of_Potatoes_in_Peru.jpg","title":"Andean domestication","desc":"Types of potatoes in Peru. Cathetovar, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Types_of_Potatoes_in_Peru.jpg","alt":"Many varieties of native Andean potato in baskets and bowls on a table, with guinea pigs on straw beneath it"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the tubers set the Andes apart from the cereal cradles?","a":"Because potato, oca and ullucu are grown for the starch-rich organs they store underground, alongside seed crops, where the other cradles built their farming on grain."},{"q":"Why is the Andean case rare among the Americas?","a":"Because it is the only cradle there to produce large herd animals — the llama from the guanaco and the alpaca entangled with the vicuña."},{"q":"Why is the gap between herding dates and bone changes not a contradiction?","a":"Because a herding relationship runs well ahead of anything a skeleton can record: a broad reckoning puts camelid herding at 9,000 to 8,000 years ago while size changes appear around 7,100."}]},
{"id":"wh-161","num":161,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Bodies taken apart, put back together and finished in clay and pigment make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the oldest deliberate mummification anywhere in the Americas.","questions":["In a desert where nothing rots, the dead were already preserving themselves, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> improved on what the dryness was doing on its own.","Infants and children were the first to be given the treatment that produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and adults joined them only later in a practice that ran for millennia."],"answer":"Chinchorro mummies","answerText":"Chinchorro mummies","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chinchorro</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 – 4,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mummification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8,000 – 4,400 BP</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chinchorro mummies</b> are the artificially preserved dead of a people who fished and gathered along the coast of the Atacama Desert, in what is now northern Chile and southern Peru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Making one meant taking a body apart and putting it back together again: complete disassembly and reassembly, the anatomy rebuilt, and the outside finished with clay, pigments and other materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The practice appears about 7,000 to 8,000 years ago and had all but gone by 4,400, which makes it the oldest deliberate mummification anywhere in the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was long called the oldest anywhere at all, and a study of 54 pre-Neolithic burials published in 2025 argues instead that bodies were being smoke-dried in Southeast Asia and southern China thousands of years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the early period only infants and children were treated this way, which suggests they were counted full members of the community, and adults were mummified afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The setting explains a good deal, because in a hyperarid desert bodies do not rot: every corpse mummifies on its own, and the dead pile up in the landscape rather than disappearing from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rising freshwater and a productive sea let the population grow, and a demographic model puts a typical person in sight of several thousand naturally preserved dead, so the custom improved on something the desert was doing already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second suggestion is arsenic, the water at Camarones carrying over 200 micrograms per litre against a safe level of 10, with the miscarriages that causes proposed as the spur — though the study modelling the practice says its contribution cannot be weighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mummies were not buried and left alone but taken up and handled again and again, which blurs the line between being dead and being gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing else these people made comes near the mummies for complexity, and none of it rested on farming; the practice faded as the highlands dried and warm-water years thinned the fisheries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Pablo A. Marquet et al., “Emergence of Social Complexity among Coastal Hunter-Gatherers in the Atacama Desert of Northern Chile,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 37 (2012): 14754–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3443180/. [Open access]","Tom D. Dillehay, “Selective Complexity and Adaptive Mortuary Behavior,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 37 (2012): 14722–23, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3443118/. [Open access]","Gonzalo M. Rojas-Costa et al., “Intracranial Volume Variation in Chinchorro Mummies: A Comparative Study with Pre-Hispanic Farmers and Contemporary Populations,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 41301, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12638747/. [Open access]","Hsiao-chun Hung et al., “Earliest Evidence of Smoke-Dried Mummification: More Than 10,000 Years Ago in Southern China and Southeast Asia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 38 (2025): e2515103122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12478042/. [Open access]","Mario Apata and Susanne P. Pfeifer, “Recent Population Genomic Insights into the Genetic Basis of Arsenic Tolerance in Humans,” <i>Heredity</i> 124, no. 2 (2020): 253–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6972707/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Momia_Chinchorro_Negra.jpg/1280px-Momia_Chinchorro_Negra.jpg","title":"Chinchorro mummies","desc":"Chinchorro mummy of an infant, black type, Museo Arqueológico San Miguel de Azapa. WeHaKa, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Momia_Chinchorro_Negra.jpg","alt":"An infant mummy of the black Chinchorro type lying on sand in a museum case, the sticks of its internal frame showing at the shoulders and legs"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the desert setting explain part of the practice?","a":"Because in a hyperarid desert bodies do not rot: every corpse mummifies on its own, so a typical person was in sight of several thousand naturally preserved dead and the custom improved on what the ground was doing already."},{"q":"Why is the claim to be the world's oldest mummification now qualified?","a":"Because a study of 54 pre-Neolithic burials published in 2025 argues that bodies were being smoke-dried in Southeast Asia and southern China thousands of years earlier; it remains the oldest in the Americas."},{"q":"Why is arsenic proposed as a spur, and why only tentatively?","a":"Because the water at Camarones carries over 200 micrograms per litre against a safe level of 10, and the miscarriages that causes have been suggested as the cause — though the modelling study says its contribution cannot be weighed."}]},
{"id":"wh-162","num":162,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Fractured skulls, points lodged in bone and pits full of the dead are most of the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since nobody who fought one left a written account.","questions":["A cemetery on the Nile where two thirds of the dead carry wounds, many of them healed, is the oldest good evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as something that came back year after year.","Roughly a tenth of 2,363 Neolithic skulls from northwestern Europe carry trauma, which is the strongest measure anyone has of how common <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was."],"answer":"prehistoric warfare","answerText":"prehistoric warfare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lethal violence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 430,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jebel Sahaba</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 13,400 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Potočani</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4200 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Prehistoric warfare</b> is organised violence between groups before anybody was writing about it, so it has to be read off bodies and settlements: broken skulls, points lodged in bone, mass graves, weapons meant for people, and defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Killing is far older than any of that: a skull from the Sima de los Huesos in northern Spain, about 430,000 years old, carries two fractures above the brow made by the same object, and two blows imply an intention to kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is a homicide rather than a war, and organised conflict between groups shows itself only where a number of the dead are found together. The oldest place that does is Jebel Sahaba, a cemetery beside the Nile in Sudan at least 13,400 years old, where 41 of the 61 people examined carry an injury, healed or unhealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Re-examination found that many carry both kinds at once, so the site records raids and ambushes returning over years rather than the single battle it was long read as.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Among Europe's early farmers violence was ordinary: cranial trauma across 2,363 skeletons runs at about 10.9 per cent, of which 3.2 per cent are unhealed wounds nobody survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a genuine rise on the foragers who came before, and at sites of mass killing the figure is nearer half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Potočani in Croatia, about 4200 BCE, 41 people of both sexes and every age from two years old were killed and thrown into a pit, 13 of them with cracked or pierced skulls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sequencing nearly all of them found that about 70 per cent had no close relative among the dead, so this was an unrelated slice of a large farming population and not a feud between families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Farming is not the whole story, since on the Atacama coast 288 adults from fishing communities show violence holding steady across ten thousand years, between local groups, with combat scenes in the rock art and no defences in the settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Linda Fibiger, Torbjörn Ahlström, Christian Meyer and Martin Smith, “Conflict, Violence, and Warfare among Early Farmers in Northwestern Europe,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2209481119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9942812/. [Open access]","Nohemi Sala et al., “Lethal Interpersonal Violence in the Middle Pleistocene,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0126589, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446311/. [Open access]","Isabelle Crevecoeur et al., “New Insights on Interpersonal Violence in the Late Pleistocene Based on the Nile Valley Cemetery of Jebel Sahaba,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 9991, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8159958/. [Open access]","Mario Novak et al., “Genome-Wide Analysis of Nearly All the Victims of a 6200 Year Old Massacre,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0247332, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7946188/. [Open access]","Vivien G. Standen et al., “Violence in Fishing, Hunting, and Gathering Societies of the Atacama Desert Coast: A Long-Term Perspective,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 9 (2023): e0290690, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10511140/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Morella_%28combate-de-arquero.png","title":"prehistoric warfare","desc":"Rock painting of a battle between archers, Cueva del Roure, Morella la Vella, Castellón, Spain, from Eduardo Hernández Pacheco's survey of 1924. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morella_(combate-de-arquero.png","alt":"A traced copy of a rock painting in which several archers with drawn bows run and shoot at one another"},"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is the Sima de los Huesos skull a homicide rather than a war?","a":"Because organised conflict between groups shows itself only where a number of the dead are found together, and two fractures made by one object on one skull imply an intention to kill a single person."},{"q":"Why was Jebel Sahaba re-read as raiding rather than one battle?","a":"Because re-examination found that many of the 41 injured people carry healed and unhealed wounds at once, which records raids and ambushes returning over years."},{"q":"Why does Potočani rule out a feud between families?","a":"Because sequencing nearly all 41 of the dead found about 70 per cent had no close relative among them, so this was an unrelated slice of a large farming population."}]},
{"id":"wh-163","num":163,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The main measure archaeologists have for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the size of the houses people lived in, compared within a single settlement.","questions":["The awkward fact at the centre of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that a hundred generations passed after the first farming before house sizes show any marked difference.","Land that limits production, growing populations and ranked settlements track the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> far better than the mere existence of a surplus does."],"answer":"origins of social inequality","answerText":"origins of social inequality","answerDate":"","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Origins of social inequality</b> is the question of how societies came to be divided into people with more and people with less, and archaeologically it is measured by comparing the sizes of the houses people lived in within a single settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That proxy is a conservative one: residence size tracks income closely and sets a floor under wealth inequality rather than a ceiling, so what it reports is the least that was there, and a figure taken at one settlement is not the same figure taken across a region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The largest attempt at it scores Gini coefficients across 1,173 sites and 47,019 residential structures spanning more than 10,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central finding is that persistent wealth inequality typically lags the onset of plant cultivation by more than a millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the first 2,000 years of the Neolithic, a hundred generations, the global sample shows no marked difference in house sizes, medians sitting between 0.2 and 0.3, and neither farming nor herding nor animal traction changed that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A surplus by itself, then, did not produce lasting inequality, which is the reverse of the usual account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What does track it is population growth, land-limited production, where land rather than labour is the binding constraint, and hierarchies of settlement size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reading offered is that polities growing one to two millennia after farming became common interfered with traditional levelling mechanisms, the enforcement of egalitarian norms among them, while ranked settlements let households in the top places pull further ahead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inheritance is the other half of it: across 21 societies material wealth passes to children strongly under farming and herding and hardly at all among foragers, and wealth Ginis run about 0.48 and 0.42 against 0.25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of it is a ratchet, since governance practices mediate how far inequality goes, iron smelting is associated with lower rather than higher house-size disparity, and neither the size nor the productivity of a settlement predicts it well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Timothy A. Kohler et al., “Economic Inequality Is Fueled by Population Scale, Land-Limited Production, and Settlement Hierarchies across the Archaeological Record,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400691122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12037049/. [Open access]","Tim Kerig et al., “100 Generations of Wealth Equality after the Neolithic Transitions,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400697122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036991/. [Open access]","Scott G. Ortman et al., “Changes in Agglomeration and Productivity Are Poor Predictors of Inequality across the Archaeological Record,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400693122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12037055/. [Open access]","Monique Borgerhoff Mulder et al., “Intergenerational Wealth Transmission and the Dynamics of Inequality in Small-Scale Societies,” <i>Science</i> 326, no. 5953 (2009): 682–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2792081/. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Toward Multiscalar Measures of Inequality in Archaeology,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400700121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12037059/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg/1280px-Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","title":"origins of social inequality","desc":"Maidanetske mega-settlement, Cucuteni-Trypillia culture, 3D model. René Ohlrau, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","alt":"A 3D model of a prehistoric mega-settlement, hundreds of houses laid out in concentric rings with a few larger buildings among them"},"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is house size a conservative measure of inequality?","a":"Because residence size tracks income closely and sets a floor under wealth inequality rather than a ceiling, so what it reports is the least that was there."},{"q":"Why does a surplus by itself not explain lasting inequality?","a":"Because for the first 2,000 years of the Neolithic the global sample shows no marked difference in house sizes, and neither farming nor herding nor animal traction changed that."},{"q":"Why is inequality not a ratchet once it appears?","a":"Because governance practices mediate how far it goes, iron smelting is associated with lower rather than higher house-size disparity, and neither settlement size nor productivity predicts it well."}]},
{"id":"wh-164","num":164,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Because fibre rots, most of what survives of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a scrap of cord stuck to a stone tool and microscopic threads in tool residues.","questions":["A three-ply cord of conifer inner bark, found stuck to a stone flake in France, is the oldest direct trace of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> anybody has found.","The wheel-and-axle principle sits inside the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because over a hundred perforated pebbles from a village of 12,000 years ago are read as spindle whorls."],"answer":"fibre technology","answerText":"fibre technology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Neanderthal cord</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 52,000 – 41,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dyed bast fibres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 28,000 – 18,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spindle whorls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Woven, Iberia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Fibre technology</b> is the making of cord, net, thread and cloth out of plant and animal fibres, among the most fundamental and complex things people do and the least often found, because fibre rots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is mostly indirect: a scrap stuck to a stone tool, microscopic threads left in tool residues, impressions in clay, and the weights and whorls used to spin and weave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest direct trace is a cord of conifer inner bark lying on a stone flake at the Abri du Maras in France, between 41,000 and 52,000 years old and therefore Neanderthal, a scrap 6.2 millimetres (a quarter of an inch) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is three bundles twisted one way and plied together the other, which takes knowing which trees to strip and when, how to ret them, and a grasp of sets and pairs that amounts to numeracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twisted fibre underlies clothing, rope, bags, nets, mats and boats, so once it exists it does not go away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Shizitan on the loess plateau of north China, tools used between 28,000 and 18,000 years ago carry 546 bast microfibres, mostly hemp, with the whole sequence of cutting, retting, pounding and scraping behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of those fibres 46 are dyed, in blue, pink, black, grey, green and red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spinning leaves harder evidence: more than a hundred perforated pebbles from the 12,000-year-old village of Nahal Ein-Gev II in Israel are read as spindle whorls, which puts the wheel-and-axle principle thousands of years before any cart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Weaving on a loom is later again, and in Iberia the first loom-woven cloth appears in the second half of the 4th millennium BCE, at the same moment loom weights do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The labour was enormous: on the Peruvian coast cultivated cotton replaced gathered wild bast and made larger fishing nets possible, and fibre work may have taken more time than any other single technology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Bruce L. Hardy et al., “Direct Evidence of Neanderthal Fibre Technology and Its Cognitive and Behavioral Implications,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 4889, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7145842/. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Unveiling Bast Fiber Production in Upper Paleolithic North China: Microfibers and Usewear Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 4 (2026): e0346767, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13075717/. [Open access]","Talia Yashuv and Leore Grosman, “12,000-Year-Old Spindle Whorls and the Innovation of Wheeled Rotational Technologies,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 11 (2024): e0312007, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11559986/. [Open access]","David Beresford-Jones et al., “Refining the <i>Maritime Foundations of Andean Civilization</i>: How Plant Fiber Technology Drove Social Complexity during the Preceramic Period,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Method and Theory</i> 25, no. 2 (2018): 393–425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5953975/. [Open access]","Margarita Gleba et al., “Multidisciplinary Investigation Reveals the Earliest Textiles and Cinnabar-Coloured Cloth in Iberia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11, no. 1 (2021): 21918, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8578544/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Sandalias_procedentes_de_la_Cueva_de_los_Murci%C3%A9lagos%2C_Albu%C3%B1ol._Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a.jpg/1280px-Sandalias_procedentes_de_la_Cueva_de_los_Murci%C3%A9lagos%2C_Albu%C3%B1ol._Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a.jpg","title":"fibre technology","desc":"Esparto sandals from the Cueva de los Murciélagos, Albuñol, in the Museo Arqueológico Nacional de España. Dorieo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandalias_procedentes_de_la_Cueva_de_los_Murci%C3%A9lagos,_Albu%C3%B1ol._Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_Nacional_de_Espa%C3%B1a.jpg","alt":"Two sandals plaited from esparto grass, their soles built up from coiled and braided cord"},"why":[{"q":"Why is fibre technology so rarely found?","a":"Because fibre rots, so what survives is mostly indirect — a scrap on a stone tool, threads in tool residues, impressions in clay, and the weights and whorls used to spin and weave."},{"q":"Why does the Abri du Maras cord imply more than dexterity?","a":"Because three bundles twisted one way and plied the other takes knowing which trees to strip and when, how to ret them, and a grasp of sets and pairs that amounts to numeracy."},{"q":"Why do the Nahal Ein-Gev whorls matter beyond textiles?","a":"Because more than a hundred perforated pebbles read as spindle whorls put the wheel-and-axle principle thousands of years before any cart."}]},
{"id":"wh-165","num":165,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The wooden body has almost never survived, so what is usually found of an early <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a criss-cross of long thin scratches cut into a buried ground surface.","questions":["Because an ox pulls a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a person swings a hoe, one household could suddenly break far more ground than it could ever prepare by hand.","Soil creeping downhill behind a field edge piles up into a lynchet, which is one of the few landforms a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> builds rather than merely scratches."],"answer":"plough","answerText":"plough","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ard marks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Belderg lynchet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3100 – 2900 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>plough</b> is dragged through soil to open a furrow for seed, and the oldest kind, the ard, cuts a shallow groove without turning the earth over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because it does not invert the sod, a field was worked twice at right angles, so an ard leaves a criss-cross of long thin scratches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tool was wood and has almost never survived, so those scratches are the evidence, cut into an old ground surface and sealed under the mid-4th-millennium BCE long barrow at South Street in Wiltshire, or under an Early Neolithic house site at Ballygalley in Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also builds landforms: soil creeping downhill behind a field edge piles into a bank called a lynchet, and one at Belderg Beg in County Mayo lay under peat of 3100 to 2900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two Irish traces say opposite things: a scratch beside a house is a garden plot, the lynchet part of a field system across a hillside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What separates a plough from a hoe is the ox rather than the blade: animal traction multiplies labour, letting one household break far more ground than it could prepare by hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is a change of kind, because the first farming in central Europe was the opposite: small plots dug by hand and heavily manured, which shows in the weeds among the crop and the nitrogen in charred grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Extensive farming is land-hungry, and only wealthier households could keep draught animals and hire their labour out, so ploughing is argued to have widened the gap between them and a growing landless class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is offered as the reason Old World wealth gaps outran American ones, no animal there being any use in a yoke, but a survey of house sizes finds no break in inequality where ploughing appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its mark on bodies is plainer: across 5,400 years of farming in central Europe the arm bones of women changed more than those of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Alison A. Macintosh, Ron Pinhasi, and Jay T. Stock, “Divergence in Male and Female Manipulative Behaviors with the Intensification of Metallurgy in Central Europe,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 11 (2014): e112116, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4229139/. [Open access]","Fabienne Pigière and Jessica Smyth, “First Evidence for Cattle Traction in Middle Neolithic Ireland: A Pivotal Element for Resource Exploitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0279556, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9879418/. [Open access]","Amy Bogaard et al., “Combining Functional Weed Ecology and Crop Stable Isotope Ratios to Identify Cultivation Intensity: A Comparison of Cereal Production Regimes in Haute Provence, France and Asturias, Spain,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 25, no. 1 (2016): 57–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4709954/. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler et al., “Greater Post-Neolithic Wealth Disparities in Eurasia than in North America and Mesoamerica,” <i>Nature</i> 551, no. 7682 (2017): 619–22, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5714260/. [Open access]","Tim Kerig et al., “100 Generations of Wealth Equality after the Neolithic Transitions,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400697122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036991/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Aratro_Lavagnone.jpg/1280px-Aratro_Lavagnone.jpg","title":"plough","desc":"The Lavagnone ard, an oak plough in the Museo civico archeologico Giovanni Rambotti. 65cla, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratro_Lavagnone.jpg","alt":"A prehistoric wooden ard in a museum case, its dark oak sole and curved beam rising towards a reconstructed yoke, before a rock carving of a man ploughing with oxen"},"why":[{"q":"Why are criss-crossed scratches the evidence for early ploughing?","a":"Because the ard was wood and has almost never survived, and since it does not invert the sod a field was worked twice at right angles, leaving long thin grooves in the old ground surface."},{"q":"Why is the ox rather than the blade what separates a plough from a hoe?","a":"Because animal traction multiplies labour, letting one household break far more ground than it could prepare by hand — a change of kind from the small hand-dug, heavily manured plots of the first central European farming."},{"q":"Why is the claim that ploughing widened wealth gaps not settled?","a":"Because it is offered as the reason Old World wealth gaps outran American ones, and yet a survey of house sizes finds no break in inequality where ploughing appears."}]},
{"id":"wh-166","num":166,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The hard part was never the disc but the joint at its centre, which is why the earliest surviving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a square hole and turned with its axle.","questions":["Logs laid under a load are useless once it has passed over them, and in a mine tunnel they cannot be carried to the front, which may be why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began underground.","More than 150 clay drinking mugs shaped as four-wheeled wagons, made no later than 3600 BCE, are the earliest known pictures of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in use."],"answer":"wheel","answerText":"wheel","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wagon models</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 3600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest wheel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3350 – 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spoked chariots</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2100 – 1700 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>wheel</b> is easy to picture and hard to build, because the difficulty is not the disc but the joint at its centre, which must bear a load and turn freely at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One proposed route runs from free rollers, logs laid across a path for a load to ride over, to grooved rollers held in sockets under the cargo, and then to two discs on a slender axle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rollers are useless once the load has passed over them, and in a narrow mine trench they cannot be carried back to the front, which is what makes fixing them under the cart pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the argument for copper miners in the Carpathians inventing it by 3900 BCE, whose culture left over 150 clay drinking mugs shaped as four-wheeled wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest surviving wheel comes from the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia, and the square hole at its centre shows it was fixed to its axle and turned with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Wheels and axles turn up in the Middle East and in Europe at much the same moment in the second half of the 4th millennium BCE, and differ enough to look separate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wooden wheels and axles have come out of waterlogged ground in Switzerland, Germany and the Netherlands, wheel tracks run beside a tomb at Flintbek, and a pot from Bronocice carries wagons with drawbars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A wheel is no use without something to pull it: the first vehicles were heavy, ran on four solid discs, and were drawn by oxen and then by the ass-onager hybrids that hauled Mesopotamia’s war wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No reconstruction of a steppe wagon and no clay model from the Carpathians or Anatolia has a driver’s seat, so the driver walked beside the team in a roadless world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The spoked wheel was a second invention: the light chariots of Sintashta took steppe travel from 3 to 16 kilometres (2 to 10 miles) an hour, and a wheeled dog figurine of the 8th century CE shows the principle reached Mesoamerica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Lee R. Alacoque, Richard W. Bulliet, and Kai A. James, “Reconstructing the Invention of the Wheel Using Computational Structural Analysis and Design,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 11, no. 10 (2024): 240373, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11529624/. [Open access]","Weronika Klecel and Elżbieta Martyniuk, “From the Eurasian Steppes to the Roman Circuses: A Review of Early Development of Horse Breeding and Management,” <i>Animals</i> 11, no. 7 (2021): 1859, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8300240/. [Open access]","E. Andrew Bennett et al., “The Genetic Identity of the Earliest Human-Made Hybrid Animals, the Kungas of Syro-Mesopotamia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 2 (2022): eabm0218, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8759742/. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann, and Volker Heyd, “Horse Genetics, Archaeology, and the Beginning of Riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13170673/. [Open access]","Fabienne Pigière and Jessica Smyth, “First Evidence for Cattle Traction in Middle Neolithic Ireland: A Pivotal Element for Resource Exploitation,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0279556, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9879418/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Ljubljana_Marshes_Wheel_with_axle_%28oldest_wooden_wheel_yet_discovered%29.jpg/1280px-Ljubljana_Marshes_Wheel_with_axle_%28oldest_wooden_wheel_yet_discovered%29.jpg","title":"wheel","desc":"Ljubljana Marshes Wheel with axle, the oldest wooden wheel yet discovered. Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ljubljana_Marshes_Wheel_with_axle_(oldest_wooden_wheel_yet_discovered).jpg","alt":"A prehistoric wooden wheel and its axle in a museum case, the wheel made of two planks joined by battens with a square hole at its centre"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the joint at the centre harder than the disc?","a":"Because it must bear a load and turn freely at once, which is what makes a wheel easy to picture and hard to build."},{"q":"Why would a mine trench reward fixing rollers under a cart?","a":"Because rollers are useless once the load has passed over them, and in a narrow trench they cannot be carried back to the front — which is the argument for Carpathian copper miners inventing the wheel by 3900 BCE."},{"q":"Why is it thought the driver walked beside the team?","a":"Because no reconstruction of a steppe wagon and no clay model from the Carpathians or Anatolia has a driver's seat."}]},
{"id":"wh-167","num":167,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"A canal silts up and a wet field dries, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is often inferred from the sedges and reeds that grew among the crop rather than from the works themselves.","questions":["Lidar over the Teotihuacan Valley found 16.9 kilometres of river course deliberately rerouted, and a city built over the earlier <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had watered its fields.","Intensive farming and states go together everywhere, but the stronger link runs from having a state to practising <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, rather than the other way about."],"answer":"irrigation","answerText":"irrigation","answerDate":"","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Irrigation</b> is bringing water to a crop rather than waiting for rain, and it frees farming from where and when the rain falls. It is also hard to see in the ground, so it is often inferred from the plants: the water-loving sedges and reeds of a wet field leave phytoliths, tiny silica bodies, among the crop’s own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Read that way, rice grown in South India between about 1000 BCE and 500 CE looks rainfed rather than irrigated, which upsets the assumption that rice implies paddy fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the works are big enough they survive as landscape: lidar over the Teotihuacan Valley in Mexico shows 16.9 kilometres (10 miles) of river course deliberately rerouted, with the city built over earlier irrigation systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Bolivian Amazon the Casarabe threw causeways and canals across savanna between 400 and 1400 CE, a network that drained and watered the ground for intensive maize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Not all irrigation is a canal: Byzantine farmers in the Negev steered runoff off bare hillsides into terraced plots and grew wine grapes in a desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modelling one of their watersheds shows more runoff raising yields only to a point, and a long drought cutting production so far that recovery runs past a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Irrigation is one of the practices meant by intensive agriculture, alongside manuring and crop rotation, and states almost everywhere rest on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mapped across 868 cultures on a language tree the two are indeed tightly coupled, but the stronger relationship runs the other way: having a state makes intensive farming much more likely, rather than the reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> States were also far less likely to give the practice up once they had it, so their part looks like sustaining irrigation rather than being made by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Christopher Opie and Quentin D. Atkinson, “State Formation across Cultures and the Role of Grain, Intensive Agriculture, Taxation and Writing,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 10, no. 1 (2026): 156–63, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12846917/. [Open access]","Eleanor Kingwell-Banham, “Dry, Rainfed or Irrigated? Reevaluating the Role and Development of Rice Agriculture in Iron Age-Early Historic South India Using Archaeobotanical Approaches,” <i>Archaeological and Anthropological Sciences</i> 11, no. 12 (2019): 6485–6500, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6890616/. [Open access]","Nawa Sugiyama et al., “Humans as Geomorphic Agents: Lidar Detection of the Past, Present and Future of the Teotihuacan Valley, Mexico,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 9 (2021): e0257550, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8452071/. [Open access]","Barak Garty et al., “Wine Economy in Byzantine Shivta (Negev, Israel): Exploring the Role of Runoff Agriculture and Droughts through Agent-Based Modeling,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 7 (2025): e0325204, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12244680/. [Open access]","Joseph Hirst et al., “Constraining the Population Size Estimates of the Pre-Columbian Casarabe Culture of Amazonian Bolivia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0325104, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12124758/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Ipuy_shaduf.jpg/1280px-Ipuy_shaduf.jpg","title":"irrigation","desc":"Scene of a gardener using a shaduf, Tomb of Ipuy at Deir el-Medina, west bank of Thebes, TT217. Norman de Garis Davies, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ipuy_shaduf.jpg","alt":"A painted scene of a gardener working a counterweighted lever to lift a bucket of water from a lily pool towards trees and flowers"},"why":[{"q":"Why is irrigation often inferred from plants rather than from works?","a":"Because it is hard to see in the ground, so the water-loving sedges and reeds of a wet field are read from the phytoliths they leave among the crop's own."},{"q":"Why does South Indian rice upset an assumption?","a":"Because read through those phytoliths, rice grown there between about 1000 BCE and 500 CE looks rainfed rather than irrigated, so rice does not imply paddy fields."},{"q":"Why is the state read as sustaining irrigation rather than made by it?","a":"Because across 868 cultures the stronger relationship runs from state to intensive farming rather than the reverse, and states were far less likely to give the practice up once they had it."}]},
{"id":"wh-168","num":168,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Because there are no records, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read backwards from objects found far from the outcrop or the shore their material came from.","questions":["The longest journey a stone tool has yet been shown to make through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took chert from south-west France 600 kilometres into central Iberia during the ice age.","Amounts fall away with distance from the source in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because goods can pass from band to band without anyone ever travelling far."],"answer":"prehistoric trade","answerText":"prehistoric trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iberian network</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 25,300 – 23,900 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Keros obsidian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Únětice collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Prehistoric trade</b> is the movement of goods between people before anyone wrote anything down, so with no records it is read backwards from objects found far from where their material came from. The method is provenance: many raw materials carry a chemical signature of the seam they were quarried at, so a single blade or bead can be matched to one source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the Cycladic sanctuary of Keros, 207 pieces of obsidian measured by portable X-ray fluorescence came mostly from Melos, with a few from Giali in the Dodecanese and from central Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It runs far deeper than farming: chert tools left at Peña Capón in central Iberia were quarried in south-west France, 600 to 700 kilometres (370 to 430 miles) off, the longest such journey yet proved for a stone tool in Palaeolithic Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is exceptional, most stone having moved locally, and the network behind it held from about 25,300 to 23,900 years ago, through the coldest part of the ice age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A far-travelled object does not prove anybody made the journey, since goods pass down the line from band to band in short hops, which shows in the amounts: most near the source and least far from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same caution holds later, and Baltic amber reaching Iberia in the last quarter of the 2nd millennium BCE looks like the far end of Mediterranean networks rather than direct dealing with the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exchange also works where nothing is for sale: at Baño Nuevo 1 in Patagonia, 100 kilometres (60 miles) from the Pacific and 320 (200 miles) from the Atlantic, hunter-gatherers wore beads cut from sea shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Routes shift, and 550 metal analyses show southern Scandinavia drawing copper first from the Austrian Alps and Slovakia through hubs in central Germany, with high-tin British metal alongside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When those hubs collapsed around 1600 BCE the supply failed and smiths melted down what they had, until a new eastern route opened to the Carpathians that also carried amber to the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Marta Sánchez de la Torre et al., “Far-Reaching Hunter-Gatherer Networks during the Last Glacial Maximum in Western Europe,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 4 (2026): eadz7697, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12822654/. [Open access]","Mercedes Murillo-Barroso et al., “Amber in Prehistoric Iberia: New Data and a Review,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 8 (2018): e0202235, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6114727/. [Open access]","Heide W. Nørgaard, Ernst Pernicka, and Helle Vandkilde, “Shifting Networks and Mixing Metals: Changing Metal Trade Routes to Scandinavia Correlate with Neolithic and Bronze Age Transformations,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252376, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8208583/. [Open access]","Tristan Carter et al., “Characterizing Centrality: Obsidian Consumption, Supra-Regional Connectivity, and Social Reproduction at the Early Bronze Age Sanctuary of Keros (Cyclades, Greece),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0325218, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12425306/. [Open access]","Heidi Hammond et al., “Middle through Late Holocene Long-Distance Transport of Exotic Shell Personal Adornments in Central West Patagonia (Southern South America): The Archaeomalacological Assemblage of Baño Nuevo 1,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0304454, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11125563/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Obsidian_from_Milos_in_Crete%2C_3000-2300_BC%2C_AMH%2C_144704.jpg/1280px-Obsidian_from_Milos_in_Crete%2C_3000-2300_BC%2C_AMH%2C_144704.jpg","title":"prehistoric trade","desc":"Blades, cores, flakes and waste chips of obsidian imported from Melos, found at Poros, Heraklion, 3000–2300 BCE. Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Obsidian_from_Milos_in_Crete,_3000-2300_BC,_AMH,_144704.jpg","alt":"A museum case of black obsidian blades, cores and flakes laid out beside three bowls heaped with working waste"},"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why can prehistoric trade be traced at all?","a":"Because many raw materials carry a chemical signature of the seam they were quarried at, so a single blade or bead can be matched to one source by provenance analysis."},{"q":"Why does a far-travelled object not prove a long journey?","a":"Because goods pass down the line from band to band in short hops, which shows in the amounts — most near the source and least far from it."},{"q":"Why did Scandinavian smiths start melting down what they had?","a":"Because when the central German hubs drawing copper from the Alps and Slovakia collapsed around 1600 BCE the supply failed, until a new eastern route opened to the Carpathians."}]},
{"id":"wh-169","num":169,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"Because the rivers whose silt made the plain habitable went on burying it, the settlements of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are reached only in deep soundings or where sedimentation was slight.","questions":["Four sherds imported from north Syria or Mesopotamia, lying beside a shell from the Nile at a site in Israel, show how far the pottery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travelled.","Settlements stayed small, scattered and unranked through the two thousand years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so the public temples and social ranking that appear at its close stand out."],"answer":"Ubaid period","answerText":"Ubaid period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6000 – 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spread beyond the plain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 4500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ubaid period</b>, roughly 6000 to 4000 BCE, is the earliest attested phase of permanent habitation on the plain of southern Mesopotamia, and it lasted some two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the hardest of that plain's phases to reach, because the rivers whose silt made settlement possible went on burying it, so its levels are known from deep soundings or from the few sectors where sedimentation happened to be slight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What comes up is a scattered, non-hierarchical spread of relatively small settlements whose people farmed, herded, fished and hunted, with craft specialisation only beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Irrigation was used at a local scale, and is inferred indirectly from the plant remains rather than read off any surviving canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The period is tracked across a wide area by its painted pottery, dated closely enough that its absence can date a site: a settlement on Ghagha Island in the Gulf is placed before 5550 BCE partly because no Mesopotamian ware had yet reached it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From about 4500 BCE its cultural elements turn up well outside the plain, along the coasts of the Persian Gulf, in northern Mesopotamia and in western Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Northern sites could be substantial: Tell Zeidan, on the Syrian floodplain, covered 12 hectares (30 acres) between 5300 and 3850 BCE, with public buildings, private houses and specialised craft quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four sherds imported from north Syria or Mesopotamia reached Tel Tsaf in Israel, where they lay among Anatolian obsidian beads, a clay sealing and a shell from the Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The copper awl found at that site, the earliest metal object in the southern Levant, matches awls from 5th-millennium BCE sites at Tepe Gawra and Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only late in the period do social stratification and public temples appear, with some sites passing 10 hectares (25 acres); the largest, set in a depression sheltered by higher ground, was the temple-town of Eridu, which the Sumerian King List remembers as one of five cities before the flood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Kevin Lidour et al., “Exploring the Early Neolithic in the Arabian Gulf: A Newly Discovered 8,400-Year-Old Stone-Built Architecture on Ghagha Island, United Arab Emirates,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0326259, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0326259. [Open access]","Amy K. Styring et al., “Urban Form and Scale Shaped the Agroecology of Early ‘Cities’ in Northern Mesopotamia, the Aegean and Central Europe,” <i>Journal of Agrarian Change</i> 22, no. 4 (2022): 831–54, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9580239/. [Open access]","Yosef Garfinkel et al., “The Beginning of Metallurgy in the Southern Levant: A Late 6th Millennium CalBC Copper Awl from Tel Tsaf, Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): e92591, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0092591. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Earliest Cotton Fibers and Pan-Regional Contacts in the Near East,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 1045554, https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpls.2022.1045554/full. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ubaid_III_pottery_5300-4700_BC_Louvre_Museum.jpg/1280px-Ubaid_III_pottery_5300-4700_BC_Louvre_Museum.jpg","title":"Ubaid period","desc":"Ubaid III pottery, 5300–4700 BCE, Louvre Museum AO 29598. ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ubaid_III_pottery_5300-4700_BC_Louvre_Museum.jpg","alt":"A broad buff pottery jar with a tall flaring neck, its shoulder painted in dark brown with fine bands and a row of zigzags"},"why":[{"q":"Why is this the hardest phase of the southern plain to reach?","a":"Because the rivers whose silt made settlement possible went on burying it, so its levels are known only from deep soundings or the few sectors where sedimentation was slight."},{"q":"Why can its pottery date a site by being absent?","a":"Because the painted ware is dated closely enough that a settlement on Ghagha Island is placed before 5550 BCE partly because no Mesopotamian ware had yet reached it."},{"q":"Why is the period's social character described as changing only late?","a":"Because it begins as a scattered, non-hierarchical spread of small settlements, and only late do social stratification and public temples appear, with some sites passing 10 hectares."}]},
{"id":"wh-170","num":170,"category":"Neolithic worlds","question":"The marks originally set down for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were craft specialists released from growing their own food, metallurgy, long-distance trade and monumental public buildings.","questions":["A settlement can be large and dense without the ranking and public works the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> implies, which is why Çatalhöyük's doorless houses, entered across the roofs, complicate it.","Settlements of many thousands appeared in Ukraine and Moldova hundreds of years before Uruk, which is one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer told as a single event in one place."],"answer":"urban revolution","answerText":"urban revolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First proto-urban</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3950 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936, by V. Gordon Childe</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>urban revolution</b> is the name given in 1936 by V. Gordon Childe to the second of two thresholds between hunter-gatherers and the first civilisations, the first being the shift to farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its marks were craft specialisation and metallurgy, the trade of craft goods over long distances, and the accumulation of surplus and wealth in cities where ruling classes lived and monumental public buildings were raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The engine proposed was the agricultural surplus, which freed resident specialists from producing their own food to work metal, weave at scale, and write down and administer what the new arrangements required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence since has pulled at that in two directions, the first being that a settlement can be large and dense without being ranked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Çatalhöyük in Anatolia held several hundred to a few thousand people in mudbrick houses packed so tightly that many crossed their neighbours' roofs to descend by ladder into doorless homes, and shows no unified trajectory of inequality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The second is that urbanism arose in more places, and more shapes, than a single revolution allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The world's first proto-urban settlements were the Trypillia mega-sites of Ukraine and Moldova, reaching 320 hectares (790 acres) and some 15,000 inhabitants from about 3950 BCE, hundreds of years before Uruk, with houses set along a ring corridor around an empty centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Amazonia the Casarabe culture spread across roughly 4,500 square kilometres (1,700 square miles) of seasonally flooded Bolivian savannah between 500 and 1400 CE, in a four-tier hierarchy of monumental mounds joined by canals and causeways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mesoamerica produced one of the world's original urbanisation episodes with no beasts of burden, no wheeled transport and only limited and late use of metal tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What holds up best is that density as much as size decided how a city fed itself: high-density urbanism in northern Mesopotamia and the Aegean entailed radical agricultural extensification, a low-input strategy limited by land rather than labour, while lower-density settlement in south-west Germany allowed more intensive management of arable land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Dorian Q. Fuller and Chris J. Stevens, “Between Domestication and Civilization: The Role of Agriculture and Arboriculture in the Emergence of the First Urban Societies,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 28, no. 3 (2019): 263–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6499764/. [Open access]","Frank Schlütz et al., “Isotopes Prove Advanced, Integral Crop Production, and Stockbreeding Strategies Nourished Trypillia Mega-Populations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 52 (2023): e2312962120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756257/. [Open access]","Katheryn C. Twiss et al., “‘But Some Were More Equal than Others:’ Exploring Inequality at Neolithic Çatalhöyük,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 9 (2024): e0307067, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0307067. [Open access]","Umberto Lombardo et al., “Maize Monoculture Supported Pre-Columbian Urbanism in Southwestern Amazonia,” <i>Nature</i> 639, no. 8053 (2025): 119–23, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11882439/. [Open access]","Diane Z. Chase et al., “Mesoamerican Urbanism Revisited: Environmental Change, Adaptation, Resilience, Persistence, and Collapse,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 31 (2023): e2211558120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10400939/. [Open access]","Amy K. Styring et al., “Urban Form and Scale Shaped the Agroecology of Early ‘Cities’ in Northern Mesopotamia, the Aegean and Central Europe,” <i>Journal of Agrarian Change</i> 22, no. 4 (2022): 831–54, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9580239/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg/1280px-Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","title":"urban revolution","desc":"Maidanetske mega-settlement, Cucuteni-Trypillia culture, 3D model. René Ohlrau, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maidanetske_3D_model_long.jpg","alt":"A 3D model of a prehistoric settlement in which hundreds of small houses stand in concentric rings around a large empty centre, laid over a terrain model of the site today"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Çatalhöyük pull against Childe's account?","a":"Because it held several hundred to a few thousand people in tightly packed doorless houses and shows no unified trajectory of inequality — a settlement can be large and dense without being ranked."},{"q":"Why does a single revolution not fit the evidence?","a":"Because urbanism arose in more places and shapes than that allows: the Trypillia mega-sites reached 15,000 people centuries before Uruk, and Mesoamerica urbanised with no beasts of burden, no wheeled transport and little metal."},{"q":"Why did density rather than size decide how a city fed itself?","a":"Because high-density urbanism in northern Mesopotamia and the Aegean entailed radical agricultural extensification limited by land, while lower-density settlement in south-west Germany allowed more intensive management of arable."}]},
{"id":"wh-171","num":171,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The farming year and the river year are badly out of step in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so the flood-recession farming of Egypt and the Indus was not available and the water had to be engineered.","questions":["Rain falling on the Taurus and Zagros keeps the two rivers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flowing all year, which is what made a plain between two deserts habitable at all.","Enamel from a third-millennium BCE town in the far south of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a diet of cereals, land animals and hardly any marine fish, despite the sea being close."],"answer":"Mesopotamia","answerText":"Mesopotamia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plain settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 6000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Large canals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesopotamia</b> is the land of the Tigris and the Euphrates, and the vast low-gradient plain of its south was built out of their own sediment, filling a foreland basin as Arabia converged on Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain on the Taurus and Zagros headwaters keeps rivers that size flowing all year, leaving the region far less dependent on its own weather than a harsh arid zone between two deserts would otherwise be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The catch is timing: a long lag between the farming year and the river year hindered the flood-recession farming Egypt and the Indus Valley could rely on, a mismatch that had to be engineered around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That meant irrigating while the rivers ran low and defending against them while they ran high, and cuneiform texts and radiocarbon-dated canals attest to large-scale hydraulic works from about 2500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rivers also move: below Ramadi and Samarra they sweep across the plain as they lay down sediment, and the avulsion node at Kut has carried the Tigris along several historically attested courses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the plain grew was set by that water: its winter cereals were wheat and barley, and a summer crop became possible only once summer grains had been carried in from Africa and East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Broomcorn millet has now been identified from phytoliths at second-millennium BCE Khani Masi on the Upper Diyala, the earliest evidence yet for cereal multi-cropping in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Abu Tbeirah in the far south, zinc isotopes in third-millennium BCE tooth enamel give an omnivorous diet of cereals, land animals probably including pigs, and a little freshwater fish, but little or no marine fish despite the nearby sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Far to the north, villages baked dough in domed ovens on ceramic trays between 6400 and 5900 BCE, and residues on a few point to breads enriched with lard or oil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plain itself is now going: the central floodplain has been transformed within the last five decades, many of its ancient sites destroyed outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Elise Jakoby Laugier, Jesse Casana, and Dan Cabanes, “Phytolith Evidence for the Pastoral Origins of Multi-Cropping in Mesopotamia (Ancient Iraq),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8748697/. [Open access]","Matteo Giaccari et al., “When Collagen Fails: Zinc Isotopes Unlock Sumerian Lifeways in Southern Mesopotamia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 11 (2026): e2526276123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12993939/. [Open access]","Alessandro Pistola et al., “AI-ming Backwards: Vanishing Archaeological Landscapes in Mesopotamia and Automatic Detection of Sites on CORONA Imagery,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0330419, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0330419. [Open access]","Sergio Taranto et al., “Unveiling the Culinary Tradition of ‘Focaccia’ in Late Neolithic Mesopotamia by Way of the Integration of Use-Wear, Phytolith and Organic-Residue Analyses,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 26805, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11538533/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Mesopotamia_9_October_2020.jpg/1920px-Mesopotamia_9_October_2020.jpg","title":"Mesopotamia","desc":"Mesopotamia 9 October 2020. NASA. 2020-10-09. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mesopotamia_9_October_2020.jpg","alt":"A satellite view in which the Tigris and Euphrates run southeast across a tan desert plain as narrow green threads, converging near the head of the Persian Gulf, with mountains along the north and east"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the region less dependent on its own weather than its aridity suggests?","a":"Because rain on the Taurus and Zagros headwaters keeps rivers of that size flowing all year, so the water arrives from mountains far upstream rather than from local rainfall."},{"q":"Why could Mesopotamia not simply farm the flood as Egypt did?","a":"Because a long lag between the farming year and the river year hindered flood-recession farming, so people had to irrigate while the rivers ran low and defend against them while they ran high."},{"q":"Why did a summer crop become possible only later?","a":"Because the plain's own cereals, wheat and barley, are winter crops, and summer grains had to be carried in from Africa and East Asia — broomcorn millet is now identified at second-millennium BCE Khani Masi."}]},
{"id":"wh-172","num":172,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Ur, Uruk, Kish, Lagash and Umma competed for water and irrigable land across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, made alliances with one another, and in the end went to war over them.","questions":["Under the Third Dynasty of Ur, merchants in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked about half their time for the state and pursued their own interests with the other half.","Arid ground, salt and bitumen destroy the collagen that isotope studies rely on, so the bodies of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are far harder to read than its hundreds of thousands of texts."],"answer":"Sumer","answerText":"Sumer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">City-states</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Third Dynasty of Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2112 – 2004 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sumer</b> is the collective name for the earliest network of city-states, which emerged about 5,000 years ago in southern Mesopotamia, knit together by shared cultural traditions and economic interests rather than by any single crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was agrarian, sustained by large-scale irrigation, and control over labour was institutionalised and structured around the hydraulic works that irrigation demanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ur, Uruk, Kish, Lagash and Umma competed for control of water and irrigable land, developed alliances with one another, and ultimately entered conflicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After an Akkadian imperial interlude the city-state dynasties revived, but the fall of the Third Dynasty of Ur ended the primacy of Sumer as a dominant political network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under that dynasty, which ruled from 2112 to 2004 BCE, merchants worked about half their time for the state and pursued their own interests with the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The written record it left is enormous: hundreds of thousands of cuneiform texts have been recovered over the past two centuries, most of them in Sumerian and Akkadian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its kings put their names into the building material, stamping or incising bricks in those languages before firing, so that a wall can be dated to a single reign; one assemblage carries twelve kings, Shulgi of Ur among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Weighing was invented around 3000 BCE between Mesopotamia and Egypt, and the Mesopotamian shekel it settled into ran about 8.2 to 8.8 grams (0.29 to 0.31 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bodies are the hard part, because arid ground, high soil salinity and widespread bitumen destroy the collagen that isotope studies of diet depend on: at Abu Tbeirah only three of 48 samples in earlier work yielded any.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Enough survives for the ordinary to show through, from children weaned onto animal milk and cereals to cattle at Ur and Abu Tbeirah foddered under at least two distinct regimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Gai Gutherz et al., “Translating Akkadian to English with Neural Machine Translation,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 2, no. 5 (2023): pgad096, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10153418/. [Open access]","Matthew D. Howland et al., “Exploring Geomagnetic Variations in Ancient Mesopotamia: Archaeomagnetic Study of Inscribed Bricks from the 3rd–1st Millennia BCE,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 52 (2023): e2313361120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756309/. [Open access]","Matteo Giaccari et al., “When Collagen Fails: Zinc Isotopes Unlock Sumerian Lifeways in Southern Mesopotamia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 11 (2026): e2526276123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12993939/. [Open access]","Nicola Ialongo, Raphael Hermann, and Lorenz Rahmstorf, “Bronze Age Weight Systems as a Measure of Market Integration in Western Eurasia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 27 (2021): e2105873118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8271817/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Standard_of_Ur_-_War.jpg/1280px-Standard_of_Ur_-_War.jpg","title":"Sumer","desc":"Standard of Ur, 26th century BCE, the 'War' panel. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_Ur_-_War.jpg","alt":"A mosaic panel of shell and lapis lazuli in three registers: four-wheeled battle wagons drawn by equids, a file of cloaked infantry carrying spears, and prisoners led before a taller figure"},"why":[{"q":"Why is Sumer called a network rather than a state?","a":"Because its city-states were knit together by shared cultural traditions and economic interests rather than by any single crown, and Ur, Uruk, Kish, Lagash and Umma competed, allied and fought."},{"q":"Why can a Sumerian wall be dated to a single reign?","a":"Because its kings stamped or incised their names into bricks before firing; one assemblage carries twelve kings, Shulgi of Ur among them."},{"q":"Why are Sumerian bodies hard to study?","a":"Because arid ground, high soil salinity and widespread bitumen destroy the collagen isotope studies of diet depend on — at Abu Tbeirah only three of 48 samples in earlier work yielded any."}]},
{"id":"wh-173","num":173,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"For a thousand years the southern plain carried no more than 15 widely dispersed villages, and then <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew to over 250 hectares and at least 25,000 people.","questions":["At Susa, tokens lay beside bevelled-rim bowls and clay sealings in step with developments at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across what is now the Iraqi border.","Pastoralism, trade and resource diversity all played parts in the rise of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but where the agricultural surplus behind its prosperity came from is still uncertain."],"answer":"Uruk","answerText":"Uruk","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Uruk period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 – 3250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3550 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Uruk</b>, the modern Tell al-Warka in southern Iraq, underwent full-fledged urbanisation in the 4th millennium BCE, reaching over 250 hectares (620 acres) at its peak and accommodating at least 25,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What came before makes that stranger: for a thousand years the southern plain carried no more than 15 autonomous villages, widely and fairly evenly dispersed, at an extremely low density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From about the end of the 5th millennium BCE the population of the alluvium grew roughly eightfold over a thousand years, and the region developed two, then three, then four levels of settlement hierarchy, with marked social stratification and institutions of bureaucratic governance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through the period named after the city, settlement grew denser and more clustered around urban institutions, and some sites reached several tens of hectares apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New techniques were introduced or improved in agriculture, animal husbandry, fishing, textiles, ceramics and metal, architecture and art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By about 3550 BCE, Uruk-related settlements and cultural influences had spread north into upper Mesopotamia and beyond, and east onto the Susiana plain in what is now Khuzestan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Susa the clay tokens turn up among workshops and stores alongside bevelled-rim bowls and cylinder-seal impressions, in step with what was happening at Uruk across the modern border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery changed with it across Mesopotamia as painted decoration disappeared, and at Arslantepe in eastern Anatolia roughly 15 per cent of the assemblage is mass-produced plain bowls, production turning from the symbolic towards efficiency and serialisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Precedence is not settled, and the north has a claim of its own: Tell Brak was a dispersed settlement of 55 hectares (140 acres) with monumental buildings between 4200 and 3900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What none of it explains is the basis: pastoralism, trade and resource diversity all mattered, but the origin of the agricultural surplus behind Uruk's prosperity is uncertain, and irrigation in this period is only indirectly inferred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]","Amy K. Styring et al., “Urban Form and Scale Shaped the Agroecology of Early ‘Cities’ in Northern Mesopotamia, the Aegean and Central Europe,” <i>Journal of Agrarian Change</i> 22, no. 4 (2022): 831–54, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9580239/. [Open access]","Pamela Fragnoli, “Re-assessing the Notion(s) of Craft Standardization through Diversity Statistics: A Pilot Study on Late Chalcolithic Pottery from Arslantepe in Eastern Anatolia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 1 (2021): e0245660, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0245660. [Open access]","David S. Sandeford, “Organizational Complexity and Demographic Scale in Primary States,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 5, no. 5 (2018): 171137, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5990841/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Pergamon_Museum_Cone_mosaics_from_temple_district_facade_of_Eanna_in_Uruk_8330.jpg/1920px-Pergamon_Museum_Cone_mosaics_from_temple_district_facade_of_Eanna_in_Uruk_8330.jpg","title":"Uruk","desc":"Cone mosaics from the temple-district facade of Eanna in Uruk, Pergamon Museum. Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pergamon_Museum_Cone_mosaics_from_temple_district_facade_of_Eanna_in_Uruk_8330.jpg","alt":"A mud-brick wall and a row of engaged half-columns whose surfaces are covered in tens of thousands of small clay cones, their flat ends set in zigzag and lozenge patterns in buff and dark red"},"why":[{"q":"Why does what came before make Uruk's growth stranger?","a":"Because for a thousand years the southern plain carried no more than 15 autonomous villages, widely and evenly dispersed at extremely low density, before the population grew roughly eightfold."},{"q":"Why is the city's precedence not settled?","a":"Because the north has a claim of its own: Tell Brak was a dispersed settlement of 55 hectares with monumental buildings between 4200 and 3900 BCE."},{"q":"Why is the basis of Uruk's prosperity still uncertain?","a":"Because pastoralism, trade and resource diversity all mattered, but the origin of the agricultural surplus behind it is unknown and irrigation in this period is only indirectly inferred."}]},
{"id":"wh-174","num":174,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A scribe pressed the signs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into moist clay with a stylus and then left the tablet to dry in the open air, firing it only occasionally.","questions":["Because a sign is either impressed or it is not, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no bold and no underline, and scribes reached instead for red, white and yellow pigment.","Salts drawn to the surface of a buried tablet cover the inscription, so text in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that no eye can read is now being recovered with X-ray fluorescence."],"answer":"cuneiform","answerText":"cuneiform","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 BCE – c. 100 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Akkadian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2700 BCE – 75 CE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cuneiform</b> is the wedge-shaped writing of the ancient Near East, and it was a common medium there from the late 4th millennium BCE to the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scribe impressed the signs into moist clay with a stylus and let the tablet dry in the open air, and only occasionally was the clay fired to make it more durable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ancestor is the proto-cuneiform of Uruk, and by about 3000 BCE the impression or incision of styli on soft clay had become a system complex enough to be reasonably called writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is a script rather than a language: Akkadian, a Semitic tongue attested from about 2700 BCE to 75 CE, is one of the languages it carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Akkadian is rarely written entirely syllabically, and almost all of it uses Sumerian logograms, so profusely in divination literature that many sentences are made of nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Salt is what threatens the tablets now, drawn to the surface over millennia of burial by swings of temperature and humidity until it covers the inscription and the text cannot be read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Firing them or treating them with a consolidant makes the writing legible again and alters the object irreversibly, where X-ray fluorescence at a synchrotron can recover the text without altering it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The script's own logic is binary, a sign being either impressed or not impressed, which leaves no way to emphasise a word by weight or shade as a printer would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scribes found another way: analysis of tablets from Kish of the mid-3rd millennium BCE has recovered red, white and yellow pigment in the impressed signs, some of it mixed with quartz crystals, and on one literary tablet the brilliant yellow sits in a single sign, <i>dingir</i>, the logograph marking a deity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it is still untranslated, for want of readers rather than of texts: the Neo-Assyrian corpus alone runs to just over a million words, comparable to a standard corpus covering a thousand years of English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Mirko Surdi et al., “Synchrotron XRF Spectroscopy for Reading Salt-Encrusted Cuneiform Tablets,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 3283, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11770115/. [Open access]","Daniele Chiriu et al., “Raman Identification of Cuneiform Tablet Pigments: Emphasis and Colour Technology in Ancient Mesopotamian Mid-Third Millennium,” <i>Heliyon</i> 3, no. 3 (2017): e00272, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5367865/. [Open access]","Gai Gutherz et al., “Translating Akkadian to English with Neural Machine Translation,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 2, no. 5 (2023): pgad096, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10153418/. [Open access]","Juha M. Lahnakoski et al., “Embodied Emotions in Ancient Neo-Assyrian Texts Revealed by Bodily Mapping of Emotional Semantics,” <i>iScience</i> 27, no. 12 (2024): 111365, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11700638/. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/List_of_workers_and_rations_Kish_ED_Ashmolean.jpg/1920px-List_of_workers_and_rations_Kish_ED_Ashmolean.jpg","title":"Cuneiform","desc":"Cuneiform tablet, list of workers and rations. From Kish, Early Dynastic period. Ashmolean Museum. Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:List_of_workers_and_rations_Kish_ED_Ashmolean.jpg","alt":"A square terracotta clay tablet, chipped along its top edge, its face ruled into a grid of boxes and filled with rows of wedge-shaped impressed signs"},"why":[{"q":"Why is cuneiform described as a script rather than a language?","a":"Because it carried several languages: Akkadian, a Semitic tongue attested from about 2700 BCE to 75 CE, is one of them, and it is written largely with Sumerian logograms."},{"q":"Why does salt threaten the tablets?","a":"Because swings of temperature and humidity over millennia of burial draw it to the surface until it covers the inscription and the text cannot be read."},{"q":"Why did scribes use pigment in the impressed signs?","a":"Because the script's logic is binary — a sign is either impressed or not — leaving no way to weight a word, so red, white and yellow were used instead, one yellow marking the sign for a deity."}]},
{"id":"wh-175","num":175,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica are the four places where a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was invented from nothing, and the scripts of Crete and Anatolia are counted as secondary.","questions":["The earliest signs of any <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are pictures, but a picture is rarely used for the thing it shows, one entity standing instead for another.","Rongorongo survives on fewer than 30 wooden objects, and dating one of them to the 15th century suggests the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Rapa Nui was its islanders' own invention."],"answer":"writing system","answerText":"writing system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First invented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oracle bones</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rongorongo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the 15th century CE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>writing system</b> is a graphic code, a set of standardized pairings between symbols and meanings in which the symbols take the form of images that can endure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One was invented from nothing only a handful of times, in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica, and a few more, among them the scripts of Crete and Anatolia, count as secondary inventions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of them lean on iconicity, since the earliest signs are pictures and their shapes are chosen for what they look like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A picture is rarely used for the thing it shows, though, and what turns one into a sign is metaphor, with its subcategories of pars pro toto and metonymy, whereby one entity stands for another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strategies are local and culturally marked: proto-cuneiform uses human heads and genitalia as pars pro toto, where the Egyptian script uses human body parts as measurement indices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Writing in Mesopotamia and western Iran came out of accounting: first tablets bearing only number signs, then tablets adding one or two signs for the things counted, such as herded animals or dairy products.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oracle bone inscriptions of the late Shang run to some 4,000 signs built from about 170 basic shapes, and at least half the corpus is still undeciphered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the count of primary inventions is four is open, and Rapa Nui is the test: a script called Rongorongo survives there on fewer than 30 wooden objects, and nobody can read it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything turns on whether it was devised before European ships reached the island in the 1720s: four tablets were radiocarbon-dated directly, one gave a secure mid-15th-century date and the rest the 19th, which places its use before any outside influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beyond the famous cases the world's scripts are now being counted and compared, from over 47,000 characters measured across more than 133 of them to the Dongba pictographic writing of the Naxi people of China, catalogued a manuscript at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Ludovica Ottaviano et al., “Metaphors and the Invention of Writing,” <i>Topics in Cognitive Science</i> 18, no. 1 (2026): 245–63, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12831613/. [Open access]","Silvia Ferrara et al., “The Invention of Writing on Rapa Nui (Easter Island): New Radiocarbon Dates on the Rongorongo Script,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 2794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10837134/. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]","Helena Miton and Olivier Morin, “Graphic Complexity in Writing Systems,” <i>Cognition</i> 214 (2021): 104771, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8346946/. [Open access]","Yuqi Ma et al., “Dataset for Single Character Detection in Dongba Manuscripts,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12, no. 1 (2025): 1075, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12217708/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Rongorongo_B-r_Aruku-Kurenga.jpg/1920px-Rongorongo_B-r_Aruku-Kurenga.jpg","title":"Writing system","desc":"Recto of rongorongo Tablet B, or Aruku Kurenga, 43 by 16 centimetres (17 by 6 inches), carrying 1,135 signs across its two faces. Photograph from the Trocadéro Museum, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rongorongo_B-r_Aruku-Kurenga.jpg","alt":"A long wooden tablet with rounded ends, its dark surface covered in close ruled rows of small incised glyphs, many of them stylised human and bird figures"},"why":[{"q":"Why is a picture rarely used for the thing it shows?","a":"Because what turns a picture into a sign is metaphor, with pars pro toto and metonymy, whereby one entity stands for another — and those strategies are local and culturally marked."},{"q":"Why did writing in Mesopotamia grow out of accounting?","a":"Because the sequence runs from tablets bearing only number signs to tablets adding one or two signs for the things counted, such as herded animals or dairy products."},{"q":"Why does Rapa Nui test the count of primary inventions?","a":"Because everything turns on whether Rongorongo was devised before European ships arrived in the 1720s, and one of four directly dated tablets gave a secure mid-15th-century date."}]},
{"id":"wh-176","num":176,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Carved in reverse so that rolling it across damp clay left a raised frieze, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closed doors, jars and sacks, and was rolled across tablets to vouch for what they said.","questions":["No example harder than quartz has been found, because a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut with sand abrasive and hand, palm, pump or bow drills rather than with metal alone.","Susa has yielded scenes of potters at work, files of figures and men fighting on clay sealed with a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an interest in daily life the Near East never matched again."],"answer":"cylinder seal","answerText":"cylinder seal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stamp seals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 6000 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cylinder seal</b> is a small pierced stone, engraved in reverse so that rolling it across damp clay leaves a raised frieze, and it is attested in Iran and Mesopotamia from about 3500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did not arrive on empty ground, since stamp seals had been pressed into clay in Iran from at least 6000 BCE, the earliest excavated example coming from the Neolithic site of Khaleseh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roughly 2,000 survive from the six centuries between 3300 and 2300 BCE, and they have been called Sumerian art in miniature, the most characteristic object the Sumerians made and the most numerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clay it marked was more often a fastening than a document: a door closure, a pot mouth, the tied neck of a sack, the knotted wrapping of a reed basket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the apparatus was really for is argued about: on one reading it let institutions dress extractive debt up as balanced reciprocity, a moral cover under which elites could begin charging interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They were cut without metal for most of that history, with abrasive slurries of sand or emery worked by hand, palm, pump and bow drills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No seal or bead harder than quartz has been found, which is the limit those tools imposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavations at four sites in the Diyala region east of Baghdad published them in strict order of depth and position, from the Jamdat Nasr period down to the Old Babylonian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The images repay the trouble: Susa has yielded potters at work, files of figures and men in conflict, an interest in daily activities called unmatched in any later period, while one seal shows the little-known goddess Ninishkun interceding with Ishtar for its owner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bore may yet betray a forgery, since electron microscopy of a silicone impression found concentric abrasion marks in all four genuine bores examined and, bar one exception, none in the fakes, though the authors want many more tested first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Leonard Gorelick and A. John Gwinnett, “The Origin and Development of the Ancient Near Eastern Cylinder Seal,” <i>Expedition</i> 23, no. 4 (1981), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-origin-and-development-of-the-ancient-near-eastern-cylinder-seal/. [Open access]","Roger Matthews et al., “Information Revolutions and Information Transitions: Counting, Sealing, Writing in Iran 10,000–300 BC,” <i>Journal of Ancient Near Eastern History</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 215–305, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12645803/. [Open access]","Henri Frankfort, <i>Stratified Cylinder Seals from the Diyala Region</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 72 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1955), https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/oip-72-stratified-cylinder-seals-diyala-region. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures, “Highlights from the Collection: Mesopotamia,” University of Chicago, https://isac.uchicago.edu/collections/highlights/highlights-collection-mesopotamia. [Open access]","Leonard Gorelick and A. John Gwinnett, “Ancient Seals and Modern Science,” <i>Expedition</i> 20, no. 2 (1978), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/ancient-seals-and-modern-science/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression._Worshiper_pouring_a_libation_before_a_seated_god%2Cca._2112%E2%80%932004_B.C._Neo-Sumarian_Mesopotamia_Nippur.jpg/1920px-Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression._Worshiper_pouring_a_libation_before_a_seated_god%2Cca._2112%E2%80%932004_B.C._Neo-Sumarian_Mesopotamia_Nippur.jpg","title":"Cylinder seal","desc":"Cylinder seal and modern impression: a worshipper pouring a libation before a seated god, c. 2112–2004 BCE, Neo-Sumerian, from Nippur. Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression._Worshiper_pouring_a_libation_before_a_seated_god,ca._2112%E2%80%932004_B.C._Neo-Sumarian_Mesopotamia_Nippur.jpg","alt":"A small green stone cylinder carved with two standing figures, and beside it the wider clay impression it makes when rolled out, showing figures approaching a seated god beneath a crescent"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the clay a seal marked usually not carry a text?","a":"Because it was more often a fastening than a document — a door closure, a pot mouth, the tied neck of a sack, the knotted wrapping of a reed basket."},{"q":"Why is no seal harder than quartz found?","a":"Because they were cut without metal for most of their history, with abrasive slurries of sand or emery worked by hand, palm, pump and bow drills, which set that limit."},{"q":"Why might the bore reveal a forgery?","a":"Because electron microscopy of silicone impressions found concentric abrasion marks in all four genuine bores examined and, bar one exception, none in the fakes — though the authors want many more tested."}]},
{"id":"wh-177","num":177,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The core of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is unbaked brick and only its thick skin is fired, which is why weeper-holes were left through the facing to drain the winter rain.","questions":["The longer sides were built slightly convex rather than straight, so that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would seem to gain strength toward the centre, where the building stood highest.","Nabonidus, last king of Babylon, set three more stages on the old base of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Ur, but respected the ancient building and did not alter the design of its stairways."],"answer":"ziggurat","answerText":"ziggurat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>ziggurat</b> is a stepped platform of brick raised over a temple, and the best preserved of them stands at Ur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its lowest stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet) and the tower rose some 28 metres (92 feet) above the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside it is crude unbaked brick; outside, a thick facing of baked brick laid in pitch for mortar, with reed mats dipped in pitch between courses at intervals to bond the mass together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The long sides were built slightly convex rather than straight, which makes them read as stronger, and weeper-holes were left through the facing so that winter rain could drain instead of swelling the core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nabonidus, last king of Babylon, recorded on clay cylinders that he had repaired and completed a tower begun and left unfinished by two earlier kings of Ur, setting three more stages on the old base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Eridu had one too: survey there reconstructs the temple complex of the Third Dynasty of Ur as a three-stepped artificial hill about 12 metres (39 feet) high, on a site occupied from the 6th to the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A king began such a work in person, at least in ceremony, since a foundation figurine buried in a box beneath a tower of Enlil's temple shows Ur-Nammu carrying on his head the basket of clay for its first brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Firing a brick locks the direction and strength of the Earth's magnetic field into it, so a wall whose bricks name the reign that laid them records the field of that moment as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fabric survives remarkably well, and much of the wall face at Ur was described on excavation as looking as clean and new as when it was first built, though the tower had been wrecked in the 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What a visitor sees there now is partly modern, because the ziggurat was re-faced with new brick in the 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["C. Leonard Woolley, “The Ziggurat of Ur,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> 15, no. 2 (1924): 107–14, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/1235/. [Open access]","William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]","Philippe Quenet et al., “Archaeological Mission at Eridu: The 2022 Campaign of the University of Strasbourg at Abu Shahrayn. Preliminary Report Part 1,” <i>Iraq</i> 87 (2025): 263–312, https://doi.org/10.1017/irq.2025.10035. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures, “Highlights from the Collection: Mesopotamia,” University of Chicago, https://isac.uchicago.edu/collections/highlights/highlights-collection-mesopotamia. [Open access]","Matthew D. Howland et al., “Exploring Geomagnetic Variations in Ancient Mesopotamia: Archaeomagnetic Study of Inscribed Bricks from the 3rd–1st Millennia BCE,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 52 (2023): e2313361120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756309/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Ziggurat_of_Ur_Site_in_Nasiriyah_16.jpg/1280px-Ziggurat_of_Ur_Site_in_Nasiriyah_16.jpg","title":"Ziggurat","desc":"The ziggurat of Ur, near Nasiriyah, Iraq, whose lowest stage was re-faced with modern brick in the 1960s. Alli Khalil, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggurat_of_Ur_Site_in_Nasiriyah_16.jpg","alt":"A vast brick platform rising in stages from flat desert, its sloping facade broken by a long central stairway climbing to the upper terrace, with visitors standing small at its foot"},"why":[{"q":"Why were weeper-holes left through the facing?","a":"Because the core is crude unbaked brick, so winter rain had to drain out rather than swelling the mass inside the baked-brick shell."},{"q":"Why were the long sides built slightly convex?","a":"Because a curved face reads as stronger than a straight one, which is a matter of how the tower looks rather than how it stands."},{"q":"Why does a dated brick record more than a reign?","a":"Because firing locks the direction and strength of the Earth's magnetic field into the clay, so a wall whose bricks name the reign that laid them records the field of that moment too."}]},
{"id":"wh-178","num":178,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A deep sounding through the temple mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found 18 building levels stacked one above another, the lowest of them numbered XVIII.","questions":["After the kingship descended from heaven it was in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the list gives Alulim a reign of 28,800 years and his successor one of 36,000.","A Sumerian hymn calls the shrine at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an artfully built mountain floating on the water, its silver and lapis lazuli shining like daylight."],"answer":"Eridu","answerText":"Eridu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6000 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple levels</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18, lowest XVIII</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Eridu</b>, the modern Abu Shahrayn, lies southwest of Nasiriyah in southern Iraq, in a depression cut off from the Euphrates plain by a ridge, and it was occupied from the 6th to the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sumerian tradition put the beginning there: after the kingship descended from heaven, the king list says, the kingship was in Eridug, where Alulim reigned 28,800 years and Alaljar 36,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then Eridug fell and the kingship was taken to Bad-tibira, and after five cities and eight kings the flood swept over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reigns after it are ordinary lengths, so the list divides a primordial age from a historical one at that point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city belonged to Enki, god of fresh water and wisdom, and a hymn calls his shrine an artfully built mountain floating on the water, built of silver and decorated with lapis lazuli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What made the site famous is a deep sounding cut through the temple, which found 18 architectural levels one above another, the lowest numbered XVIII.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sherds through that sequence are what the chronology of the Ubaid period was built on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest shrine is a small building of medium rectangular mud bricks, a long central sanctuary with smaller rooms either side, an altar and an offering podium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its facades already alternate buttresses with recesses and its inner walls were finished in white painted mud plaster, features that match the temples of two thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The whole was eventually the E-abzu, reconstructed for the Third Dynasty of Ur as a three-stepped artificial hill some 12 metres (39 feet) high, standing over a basin that the plain's own drainage still carries the name of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Philippe Quenet et al., “Archaeological Mission at Eridu: The 2022 Campaign of the University of Strasbourg at Abu Shahrayn. Preliminary Report Part 1,” <i>Iraq</i> 87 (2025): 263–312, https://doi.org/10.1017/irq.2025.10035. [Open access]","“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Samuel Noah Kramer, “Iraqi Excavations During the War Years,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> 13, no. 2 (1948): 2–29, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/3023/. [Open access]","Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","“Enki's Journey to Nibru,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.1.4 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr114.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Ziggurat_at_Eridu_%2830809118442%29.jpg","title":"Eridu","desc":"Baked bricks from the ziggurat at Eridu, southwest of Nasiriyah, Iraq, some of them stamped with the name of King Ur-Nammu. David Stanley, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggurat_at_Eridu_(30809118442).jpg","alt":"A few surviving courses of mudbrick emerging from a low rubble-strewn mound, with empty grey desert running flat to the horizon behind it"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the king list divide a primordial age from a historical one at Eridu?","a":"Because its first kings there reign 28,800 and 36,000 years, while the reigns after the flood are ordinary lengths, so the flood marks the break in the list's own scale."},{"q":"Why did a single deep sounding matter so much?","a":"Because it cut through the temple and found 18 architectural levels one above another, and the sherds through that sequence are what the chronology of the Ubaid period was built on."},{"q":"Why is the earliest shrine there significant for later architecture?","a":"Because its facades already alternate buttresses with recesses and its inner walls were finished in white painted mud plaster — features that match temples of two thousand years later."}]},
{"id":"wh-179","num":179,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Drone survey at Lagash has found monumental walls cutting a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into separate quarters, each with its own workshops rather than one centre serving the whole.","questions":["At the usual estimate of 100 to 200 people per hectare, the 500 hectares of Lagash make a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of somewhere between 60,000 and 120,000 inhabitants.","The Sumerian king list is a list of cities as much as of men, since it hands the kingship from one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the next as each in turn falls."],"answer":"Sumerian city-state","answerText":"Sumerian city-state","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2900 – 2334 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lagash</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 hectares</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>Sumerian city-state</b> was a city with its own ruler, its own god and its own irrigated hinterland, and the king list treats the arrangement as the natural order, handing the kingship from one city to the next as each in turn falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the water came from changed what they were: as the head of the Persian Gulf silted up and the tides that had reached far inland withdrew, the cities came to depend on river irrigation instead, and the canal networks of the Early Dynastic period followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Freshwater marshes had been established at Ur and Uruk by about 7000 years ago, so the first settlers on the plain were living beside water rather than digging for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cuneiform texts and radiocarbon-dated canals attest to large-scale hydraulic works from about 4500 years ago; before that, irrigation is only indirectly inferred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The engineering is what the competition was about, since a city upstream of a rival held its water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Lagash shows what one of them looked like on the ground: roughly 500 hectares (1,240 acres), dense across hundreds of them, and cut by monumental walls into distinct walled quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the usual estimate of 100 to 200 people per hectare, that is a population of 60,000 to 120,000 in the later 3rd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Production was scattered rather than central: pottery kilns concentrated in one area of 75 by 150 metres (246 by 492 feet), with separate zones for metal, stone and shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Housing survives too, and the largest continuous stretch of it ever uncovered anywhere is at Ur, from the Old Babylonian period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Eridu shows the other end of the same story, occupied from the 6th to the 2nd millennium BCE and still being rebuilt on its own mound long after the cities that shared the plain with it had risen and gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Augusta McMahon et al., “Dense Urbanism and Economic Multi-centrism at Third-Millennium BC Lagash,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 393 (2023): 596–615, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.36. [Open access]","Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Philippe Quenet et al., “Archaeological Mission at Eridu: The 2022 Campaign of the University of Strasbourg at Abu Shahrayn. Preliminary Report Part 1,” <i>Iraq</i> 87 (2025): 263–312, https://doi.org/10.1017/irq.2025.10035. [Open access]","William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_%28Stele_of_the_Vultures%29%2C_c._2450_BC_%2828008189230%29.jpg/1280px-Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_%28Stele_of_the_Vultures%29%2C_c._2450_BC_%2828008189230%29.jpg","title":"Sumerian city-state","desc":"Limestone victory stele of Eannatum of Lagash, known as the Stele of the Vultures, c. 2450 BCE, in the Ancient Near East gallery of the Louvre. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limestone_Victory_Stele_of_King_Eannatum_of_Lagash_(Stele_of_the_Vultures),_c._2450_BC_(28008189230).jpg","alt":"Three joined limestone fragments forming a broad gable, carved in low relief with helmeted soldiers, fallen bodies and a standing figure beside a tree, over a band of cuneiform signs"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the cities come to depend on river irrigation?","a":"Because as the head of the Persian Gulf silted up and the tides that had reached far inland withdrew, the marshes gave way and the canal networks of the Early Dynastic period followed."},{"q":"Why was hydraulic engineering what the cities competed over?","a":"Because a city upstream of a rival held its water, so control of the canal network was control of a neighbour's harvest."},{"q":"Why is production at Lagash described as scattered rather than central?","a":"Because its pottery kilns concentrated in one area of 75 by 150 metres, with separate zones for metal, stone and shell, across a city cut by monumental walls into distinct quarters."}]},
{"id":"wh-180","num":180,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Twelve seasons of digging at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 1922 and 1934 employed as many as 300 workers at a time and ran four or five months a year.","questions":["The moon god Nanna held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the freshwater marshes beside it had been there since about 7,000 years ago, long before anyone cut a canal.","Sixteen spectacular tombs and thousands of plainer graves make up the royal cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose wealth caused a great stir among archaeologists and the public alike."],"answer":"Ur","answerText":"Ur","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1922 – 1934</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ur</b> was the city of Nanna, the Sumerian moon god, and its mound holds some 5,000 years of occupation, the earliest habitation reaching back to about 5500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sat where the land met the water: freshwater marshes had been established there by about 7,000 years ago, before any of the plain's great canals were cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its best-known monument is the ziggurat, whose lowest stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet) and which still rises above the flat country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The royal cemetery is what made it famous, with 16 spectacular tombs and thousands more ordinary graves, and the wealth of Queen Puabi's largely intact tomb caused a great stir among archaeologists and the public alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Penn Museum and the British Museum dug there jointly from 1922 to 1934, twelve seasons of four or five months, with as many as 300 workers at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Third Dynasty of Ur the city was the head of a state that ran most of the plain, and a tablet found beside a tomb under one of its house floors is dated to the reign of Shulgi, the dynasty's second king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading the people of the southern plain has been harder than reading their documents, because arid ground, salt and bitumen destroy the collagen that isotope studies of diet rely on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Zinc in tooth enamel survives where collagen does not, and work at nearby Abu Tbeirah found milk still in the diet before weaning was complete, with cattle there and at Ur kept under at least two feeding regimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Area AH holds the largest continuous expanse of Old Babylonian housing ever uncovered, so the ordinary streets are as well known here as the tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Work has begun again since 2015, and the ziggurat a visitor climbs today was re-faced with modern brick in the 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]","C. Leonard Woolley, “The Ziggurat of Ur,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> 15, no. 2 (1924): 107–14, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/1235/. [Open access]","Matteo Giaccari et al., “When Collagen Fails: Zinc Isotopes Unlock Sumerian Lifeways in Southern Mesopotamia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 11 (2026): e2526276123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12993939/. [Open access]","Liviu Giosan and Reed Goodman, “Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329084, https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0329084. [Open access]","William B. Hafford and Richard L. Zettler, “The Thrill of Discovery,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-thrill-of-discovery/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Ram_Caught_in_a_Thicket%2C_from_ancient_Mesopotamia_-_British_Museum_%2842636807315%29.jpg/1280px-Ram_Caught_in_a_Thicket%2C_from_ancient_Mesopotamia_-_British_Museum_%2842636807315%29.jpg","title":"Ur","desc":"The Ram in a Thicket, from the royal cemetery at Ur, on display in the British Museum. Nate Loper, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ram_Caught_in_a_Thicket,_from_ancient_Mesopotamia_-_British_Museum_(42636807315).jpg","alt":"A goat figure of gold leaf, lapis lazuli and shell seen through museum glass, its horned head raised and its forelegs resting among the golden branches of a stylised plant"},"why":[{"q":"Why has reading the people of Ur been harder than reading their documents?","a":"Because arid ground, salt and bitumen destroy the collagen that isotope studies of diet rely on, so the tablets survive far better than the bodies."},{"q":"Why does zinc in tooth enamel help where collagen fails?","a":"Because it survives when collagen does not, and work at nearby Abu Tbeirah used it to find milk still in the diet before weaning was complete."},{"q":"Why is Area AH as valuable as the royal tombs?","a":"Because it holds the largest continuous expanse of Old Babylonian housing ever uncovered, so the ordinary streets are as well known here as the burials."}]},
{"id":"wh-181","num":181,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Of more than 2,000 graves in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, only 16 had the structure, the wealth and the sacrificed attendants that convinced the excavators they were royal.","questions":["Skulls from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went through a hospital CT scanner in 2007, the first step in overturning the story that its victims drank poison from clay cups.","Three gold pins at the shoulder of a woman buried in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> each carried a lapis cylinder seal, and one of them read Puabi, Queen."],"answer":"Royal Cemetery at Ur","answerText":"Royal Cemetery at Ur","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 2500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemetery dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1926 – 27</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Royal Cemetery at Ur</b> is a burial ground of more than 2,000 graves, dug into waste land below the city walls where rubbish had been tipped for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only 16 had the structure, the wealth and the evidence of human sacrifice that convinced the excavators they were royal, and those 16 gave the whole ground its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name does not really fit: the ground was in use for about a thousand years, many graves are older than the royal tombs, and their occupants belong to no recorded dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seal impressions and tablets in the rubbish above and below put the royal tombs in the century between about 2600 and 2500 BCE, the period archaeologists call Early Dynastic IIIA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They came in two kinds: graves with a built tomb chamber, and graves where only the approach pit survived, called death-pits after the bodies laid out in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The largest was the Great Death Pit, which held 74 bodies, most of them young women in elaborate dress, while the King's Grave held a man in a copper helmet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The one clearly royal body lay on a bier in a chamber of its own, its whole upper part covered in beads of gold, silver, lapis lazuli, carnelian and agate, with three gold pins at the shoulder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each pin carried a lapis cylinder seal, and one read Puabi, Queen; her name is in no king list, and because the seal names no husband she may have ruled in her own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Standard of Ur came out of this ground, and the equids drawing its war wagons are probably kungas, which ancient DNA from a Syrian burial identifies as donkey and wild-ass hybrids, the first animals people bred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Woolley had the attendants walk down the ramp and drink poison from clay cups; CT scans of two crushed skulls point instead to victims felled with a sharp instrument, heated, embalmed with mercury and laid in rows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William B. Hafford, “A Spectacular Discovery: Burials Simple and Splendid,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-spectacular-discovery/. [Open access]","P. R. S. Moorey, “What Do We Know About the People Buried in the Royal Cemetery?,” <i>Expedition</i> 20, no. 1 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/what-do-we-know-about-the-people-buried-in-the-royal-cemetery/. [Open access]","Aubrey Baadsgaard, “Imaging Ur's Sacrificial Dead: An Archaeological CAT Scan,” <i>Expedition</i> 49, no. 2 (2007), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/imaging-urs-sacrificial-dead-an-archaeological-cat-scan/. [Open access]","Aubrey Baadsgaard et al., “Human Sacrifice and Intentional Corpse Preservation in the Royal Cemetery of Ur,” <i>Antiquity</i> 85, no. 327 (2011): 27–42, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00067417. [Paywalled]","E. Andrew Bennett et al., “The Genetic Identity of the Earliest Human-Made Hybrid Animals, the Kungas of Syro-Mesopotamia,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 2 (2022): eabm0218, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8759742/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Standard_of_Ur_-_Peace_Panel_-_Sumer.jpg/1280px-Standard_of_Ur_-_Peace_Panel_-_Sumer.jpg","title":"Royal Cemetery at Ur","desc":"Detail of the 'peace' panel of the Standard of Ur, found in the Royal Cemetery at Ur and inlaid in shell, lapis lazuli and red limestone. Juan Carlos Fonseca Mata, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_Ur_-_Peace_Panel_-_Sumer.jpg","alt":"Three horizontal registers of small figures inlaid in pale shell against deep blue lapis lazuli, with seated men raising cups at a banquet above and files of attendants leading rams, cattle and a laden ass below"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the name Royal Cemetery not really fit?","a":"Because the ground was in use for about a thousand years, many graves are older than the royal tombs, and their occupants belong to no recorded dynasty — only 16 of more than 2,000 graves gave it the name."},{"q":"Why might Puabi have ruled in her own right?","a":"Because her lapis cylinder seal reads Puabi, Queen and names no husband, and her name is in no king list."},{"q":"Why has Woolley's account of the attendants' deaths been revised?","a":"Because he had them walk down the ramp and drink poison, while CT scans of two crushed skulls point instead to victims felled with a sharp instrument, heated, embalmed with mercury and laid in rows."}]},
{"id":"wh-182","num":182,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The Sumerian king list makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the fifth king of Uruk's first dynasty, gives his father as a phantom, and credits him with a reign of 126 years.","questions":["Asked whether to submit to Kish, the elders of Uruk said yes and the young men said fight, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the advice of the young men.","Enlil grants <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kingship but not eternal life, and in the poem on his death he is made governor of the netherworld and judge among its ghosts."],"answer":"Gilgamesh","answerText":"Gilgamesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign in the list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">126 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">List compiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Gilgamesh</b> is the king of Uruk in Sumerian tradition, and the Sumerian king list makes him the city's fifth ruler, lord of Kullab, with a father it calls a phantom and a reign of 126 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That list is not history as we understand it but a mixture of myth, legend and historical information, opening on fantastically long reigns that shorten as it reaches rulers who can be checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fullest copy is a clay prism written around 1800 BCE, and it seems to have been composed to argue that only one city at a time could hold the kingship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kings of Isin, in power when it was written, stand at the end of it, which makes the whole document an argument for their own legitimacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A drier text, the history of the Tummal, is the better evidence: it has him build the Numunbura in Enlil's temple at Nippur, and his son Ur-lugal bring the goddess Ninlil into the Tummal after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It puts him third in a line of builders, after Enmebaragesi of Kish and his son Aga, and after Mesannepada of Ur and his, which is a plainer claim than a reign of 126 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Sumerian poems about him are separate from the Akkadian epic, and in one of them envoys of Aga arrive from Kish and he puts the question of submission to two assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The elders tell him to submit and the able-bodied men tell him to fight; he trusts the young men, Aga besieges Uruk, and Gilgamesh takes him captive and then sets him free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Enkidu is his servant there rather than his companion, and his radiance on the rampart overwhelms his own city's young and old before the besiegers see him at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the poem on his death Enlil grants him kingship but not eternal life, his household is laid down with him inside the palace at Uruk, and he is made governor of the netherworld, passing judgment among its ghosts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Ashmolean Museum, “Sumerian King List,” University of Oxford, https://www.ashmolean.org/sumerian-king-list. [Open access]","“The History of the Tummal,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.3 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr213.htm. [Open access]","“Gilgameš and Aga,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.8.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr1811.htm. [Open access]","“The Death of Gilgameš,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.8.1.3 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr1813.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Sumerian_King_List%2C_1800_BC%2C_Larsa%2C_Iraq.jpg/1280px-Sumerian_King_List%2C_1800_BC%2C_Larsa%2C_Iraq.jpg","title":"Gilgamesh","desc":"The Weld-Blundell Prism, the fullest surviving copy of the Sumerian king list, written on clay at about 1800 BCE and now in the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford. Gts-tg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumerian_King_List,_1800_BC,_Larsa,_Iraq.jpg","alt":"A tall four-sided clay prism standing on a clear mount in a museum gallery, its faces ruled into columns and covered from top to bottom in small cuneiform signs"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Sumerian king list not read as history?","a":"Because it mixes myth, legend and historical information, opening on fantastically long reigns that shorten as it reaches rulers who can be checked, and it seems composed to argue that only one city at a time could hold the kingship."},{"q":"Why is the Tummal history the better evidence for Gilgamesh?","a":"Because it makes a plainer claim: that he built the Numunbura in Enlil's temple at Nippur, third in a line of builders, rather than that he reigned 126 years."},{"q":"Why do the Sumerian poems differ from the Akkadian epic?","a":"Because they are separate compositions: in one, Enkidu is his servant rather than his companion, and the story is of Aga's envoys and two assemblies of Uruk rather than a quest for eternal life."}]},
{"id":"wh-183","num":183,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Copied on twelve tablets from the 8th to the 2nd centuries BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Babylonian poem that tradition credits to a scholar called Sin-leqi-unninni.","questions":["Enkidu, Humbaba in the Cedar Forest and the Bull of Heaven all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose hero fails the test set him and goes home to Uruk empty-handed.","A ship resting on a mountain and a dove sent out that found no resting place stand on the eleventh tablet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, read in the British Museum in 1872."],"answer":"Epic of Gilgamesh","answerText":"Epic of Gilgamesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest copies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Standard version</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 100 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Epic of Gilgamesh</b> is the long Babylonian poem about the king of Uruk, and the Babylonians referred to it by its own opening line, <i>ša nagba imuru</i>, 'He that has seen all things'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mature form, known as the Standard Babylonian version, sets some 3,000 lines out on twelve tablets, and was still being copied between the 8th and the 2nd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tradition credits that edition to a scholar named Sin-leqi-unninni, supposedly working late in the 2nd millennium BCE, but the poem was old long before he touched it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ten oldest surviving manuscripts were written in southern Iraq between about 1800 and 1500 BCE, and behind them lie separate Sumerian poems about the same hero, which are not the epic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fullest copies come from the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, and even those are late copies of a much older original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Gilgamesh oppresses Uruk until the gods send the wild man Enkidu to distract him, and the two become friends, travel to the Cedar Forest and kill its guardian Humbaba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Gilgamesh spurns the goddess Ishtar she sends the Bull of Heaven against his city, the pair kill that too, and she answers with a long, lingering death for Enkidu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The last third of the poem has him searching alone for everlasting life, finding the flood survivor Uta-napishtim at the ends of the earth, failing the test set him and going home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The eleventh tablet carries Uta-napishtim's own account of that flood, and it was that tablet, pieced together from fragments in the British Museum and announced in December 1872, which made the poem known again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even now it is incomplete: the standard edition works from 184 fragments making up some 73 manuscripts, no full set survives in any one hand, and between a third and a fifth of the text has never been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Stephen Langdon, “The Epic Of Gilgamish,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> VIII, no. 1 (1917): 29–38, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/550/. [Open access]","Eleanor Robson, review of <i>The Babylonian Gilgamesh Epic: Introduction, Critical Edition and Cuneiform Texts</i>, by A. R. George, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2004.04.21, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2004/2004.04.21/. [Open access]","“Gilgameš and Ḫuwawa (Version A),” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.1.8.1.5 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr1815.htm. [Open access]","Morris Jastrow Jr. and Albert T. Clay, <i>An Old Babylonian Version of the Gilgamesh Epic</i>, Yale Oriental Series, Researches IV.3 (New Haven: Yale University Press, 1920), https://www.gutenberg.org/files/11000/11000-h/11000-h.htm. [Open access]","George Smith, <i>The Chaldean Account of Genesis</i>, ed. A. H. Sayce, new ed. (London: Sampson Low, 1880), 4–5, https://archive.org/stream/chaldeanaccounto00smituoft/chaldeanaccounto00smituoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/British_Museum_Flood_Tablet_1.jpg/1280px-British_Museum_Flood_Tablet_1.jpg","title":"Epic of Gilgamesh","desc":"The Flood Tablet, the eleventh tablet of the Epic of Gilgamesh, from the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh and now in the British Museum. Mike Peel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Flood_Tablet_1.jpg","alt":"A broken rectangular clay tablet held on a clear mount, its face ruled into two columns and packed from edge to edge with rows of small wedge-shaped cuneiform signs, the lower right corner lost"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the poem's authorship credited only by tradition?","a":"Because Sin-leqi-unninni is supposed to have made the Standard Babylonian edition late in the 2nd millennium BCE, and the poem was old long before he touched it — its oldest manuscripts date from about 1800 to 1500 BCE."},{"q":"Why did the eleventh tablet make the poem known again?","a":"Because it carries Uta-napishtim's own account of the flood, and its piecing together from British Museum fragments was announced in December 1872."},{"q":"Why is the epic still called incomplete?","a":"Because the standard edition works from 184 fragments making up some 73 manuscripts, no full set survives in any one hand, and between a third and a fifth of the text has never been found."}]},
{"id":"wh-184","num":184,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"The Sumerian king list calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the son of a gardener and the cupbearer of Ur-Zababa, says he built Agade, and gives him a reign of 56 years.","questions":["Sent to Uruk with a tablet asking for his own murder in one legend, and set adrift on the Euphrates in another, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded the dynasty of Agade.","Lugal-zage-si of Uruk was led a prisoner through the gate of Enlil by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose daughter Enheduanna became priestess of the moon god at Ur."],"answer":"Sargon of Akkad","answerText":"Sargon of Akkad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2334 – 2279 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the king list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">56 years</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sargon of Akkad</b> founded the dynasty of Agade, and the Sumerian king list introduces him as the man 'whose father was a gardener, the cupbearer of Ur-Zababa', who built the city and ruled for 56 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Louvre dates his reign to about 2334–2279 BCE on the conventional chronology, and holds a fragment of one of his stelae, made at Nippur and found at Susa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king list has the kingship pass to Agade from Lugal-zage-si of Uruk; copies of his own monuments say what that meant, for he beat Lugal-zage-si and led him a prisoner through the gate of Enlil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ur, Lagash, Umma and Adab surrendered after further fighting and had their walls thrown down, and the empire that resulted reached from the lower sea to the upper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His daughter Enheduanna was made priestess of the moon god at Ur, where an alabaster disk showing her at the ritual survives from the goddess Ningal's courtyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Those monuments mostly reach us at second hand: a scribe at Nippur copied their inscriptions off the stelae and statues in the temple courts, and his tablet survives where the stones do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the rest comes through omen texts, where episodes of his reign hang off the markings on a sacrificial liver, and one was long read as saying he crossed the Mediterranean when the chronicle behind it says the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two legends grew around him, centuries apart and in different languages: in the Sumerian one Ur-Zababa, frightened by a dream of Inana drowning him in a river of blood, sends his cupbearer to Lugal-zage-si with a tablet asking for the bearer's own murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That text pauses to explain that in those days writing words on tablets existed but putting tablets into envelopes did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the Akkadian one his mother sets him adrift on the Euphrates, Akki the irrigator draws him out and raises him as a gardener, and Ishtar loves him and gives him the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Stèle de Sargon d'Akkad,” inv. SB 1, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010123451. [Open access]","Leon Legrain, “The Inscriptions of the Kings of Agade,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> XIV, no. 3 (1923), https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/9818/. [Open access]","Leon Legrain, “Sargon of Agade,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> X, no. 3–4 (1944), https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/2639/. [Open access]","L. W. King, <i>Chronicles Concerning Early Babylonian Kings</i>, vol. 1 (London: Luzac, 1907), 11, 28–29, 63, https://archive.org/stream/chroniclesconcer01king/chroniclesconcer01king_djvu.txt. [Open access]","“The Sargon legend,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.4 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr214.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Sargon_victory_stele_three-quarter_view.jpg/1280px-Sargon_victory_stele_three-quarter_view.jpg","title":"Sargon of Akkad","desc":"The carved upper register of the fragmentary stele of Sargon of Akkad, made at Nippur, found at Susa and now in the Louvre. ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_victory_stele_three-quarter_view.jpg","alt":"The worn top of a dark stone block carved in low relief, showing a file of figures in long fringed garments standing shoulder to shoulder beneath a band of upright spear-like shapes"},"why":[{"q":"Why do Sargon's own monuments reach us at second hand?","a":"Because a scribe at Nippur copied their inscriptions off the stelae and statues in the temple courts, and his tablet survives where the stones do not."},{"q":"Why are omen texts an awkward source for his reign?","a":"Because episodes hang off the markings on a sacrificial liver, and one was long read as saying he crossed the Mediterranean when the chronicle behind it says the Persian Gulf."},{"q":"Why does the Sumerian legend pause to explain writing?","a":"Because its plot turns on a tablet asking for its own bearer's murder, and in those days writing words on tablets existed but putting tablets into envelopes did not."}]},
{"id":"wh-185","num":185,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Ruled from Agade by Sargon and his descendants, and given 11 kings and 181 years by the Sumerian king list, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached from the lower sea to the upper.","questions":["Naram-Sin's two-metre stele shows him in a horned tiara climbing a wooded mountain over his enemies, and it is the great surviving monument of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, later carried off to Susa.","A Sumerian poem blames the fall of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Naram-Sin taking axes to Enlil's temple, for which the god sent the Gutians down from the mountains."],"answer":"Akkadian Empire","answerText":"Akkadian Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sargon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2334 – 2279 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naram-Sin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2254 – 2218 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the king list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 kings, 181 years</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Akkadian Empire</b> was the state ruled from Agade by Sargon and his descendants, and the Sumerian king list gives its dynasty 11 kings and 181 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copies of those kings' own monuments put its limits at Elam and the Persian Gulf in the south and the Lebanon, the Taurus and the Hittite land in the north-west, from the lower sea to the upper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same inscriptions have the boats of Magan, Meluhha and Dilmun lining the quays in front of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Sumerian poem remembers Agade at its height, with Elam and Subir loading themselves with goods for it like packasses, and monkeys, elephants and water buffalo jostling in its public squares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sargon's grandson Naram-Sin, whom the king list also gives 56 years, left a two-metre limestone stele of his victory over the Lullubi on which he climbs a wooded mountain wearing the horned tiara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That stele stood at Sippar until an Elamite king carried it off to Susa as booty in the 12th century BCE and cut his own inscription into its carved mountain, which is where it was found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The poem blames Naram-Sin for everything that followed: unable to get a favourable omen, he took axes to Enlil's temple at Nippur and shipped its gold, silver and copper away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enlil answered by bringing the Gutians down from the mountains, 'an unbridled people, with human intelligence but canine instincts', after which grain sold at half a litre for a shekel and the dead lay unburied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is an explanation rather than a record: nearly all surviving Sumerian literature reaches us in Old Babylonian copies of about 1900 to 1600 BCE, written out by schoolboys centuries after Agade fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The king list is barer and stranger, breaking off after Sargon's line to ask 'Then who was king?' twice over, and answering with four men who ruled three years between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["“The Sumerian King List,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.1 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr211.htm. [Open access]","Leon Legrain, “The Inscriptions of the Kings of Agade,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> XIV, no. 3 (1923), https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/9818/. [Open access]","“The cursing of Agade,” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.1.5 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section2/tr215.htm. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Stèle de Naram-Sin,” inv. SB 4, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010123450. [Open access]","Martin Worthington, review of <i>The Literature of Ancient Sumer</i>, by Jeremy A. Black et al., <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2006.01.27, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2006/2006.01.27/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Stele_Naram_Sim_Louvre_Sb4.jpg/1280px-Stele_Naram_Sim_Louvre_Sb4.jpg","title":"Akkadian Empire","desc":"Detail of the victory stele of Naram-Sin, king of Akkad, celebrating his victory over the Lullubi of the Zagros; limestone, c. 2250 BCE, brought from Sippar to Susa as spoils of war in the 12th century BCE and now in the Louvre. Jastrow. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_Naram_Sim_Louvre_Sb4.jpg","alt":"Pink limestone carved in relief: a large bearded figure in a horned helmet strides forward with a bow and axe over the bodies of smaller fallen men, while one enemy kneels and another stands with hands raised beside a panel of cuneiform"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the poem's account of Agade's fall an explanation rather than a record?","a":"Because it blames Naram-Sin for taking axes to Enlil's temple at Nippur and has the god answer with the Gutians, and nearly all surviving Sumerian literature reaches us in Old Babylonian schoolboy copies centuries later."},{"q":"Why was Naram-Sin's victory stele found at Susa?","a":"Because an Elamite king carried it off from Sippar as booty in the 12th century BCE and cut his own inscription into its carved mountain."},{"q":"Why is the king list's treatment of the empire's end strange?","a":"Because it breaks off after Sargon's line to ask 'Then who was king?' twice over, and answers with four men who ruled three years between them."}]},
{"id":"wh-186","num":186,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Founded by Ur-Nammu after the Gutians were driven out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Sumer for over a century and built the ziggurat that still dominates the ruins of Ur.","questions":["Shulgi's copper foundation pegs at Susa and the smashed stela of his father belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when the Elamites carried Ibbi-Sin away.","Nabonidus dug into the old brickwork and left cylinders saying he had finished the tower begun by the kings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fifteen centuries before his own reign."],"answer":"Third Dynasty of Ur","answerText":"Third Dynasty of Ur","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shulgi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2094 – 2047 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Third Dynasty of Ur</b> took the kingship after Utu-hegal of Uruk drove the Gutians out, and for over a century Ur-Nammu and his successors ruled from Ur as kings of Sumer and Akkad, claiming the four corners of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ur-Nammu began and his son Shulgi finished a building programme: the temple of Nanna and Ningal with its brick tower on an artificial platform, a hall of justice, a treasury, a palace and a house for the high priestess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That tower's lower stage measures about 40 by 59 metres (130 by 195 feet) and it stands 28 metres (92 feet) high, with three flights of a hundred steps meeting at a gateway in the parapet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shulgi, whose reign the Louvre dates to about 2094–2047 BCE, buried copper pegs shaped as a man carrying a builder's basket at Susa and a bronze one at Girsu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ur was a school town as well as a capital: a branch of the Edubba or Tablet House stood there, over 800 pieces of Sumerian literary text were found, and its kings are credited with a Sumerian renaissance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Shulgi raised a stela to his father in the courtyard below the hall of justice, 3 metres (10 feet) of limestone in five registers front and back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It shows Ur-Nammu shouldering the mason's tools to lay the foundation brick while workers climb ladders with baskets of mud and figures pour rain from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the top are the crescent moon below the radiating sun, one band lists the canals opened by royal command, and on the back a bull is sacrificed while two men beat huge drums.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Ibbi-Sin the Elamites and their allies from Mari plundered city and temple and carried the king and the statue of Nanna off to Susa, leaving the stela smashed across the pavement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifteen centuries later Nabonidus, the last king of Babylon, drove a hole into the brickwork hunting the old foundation records, and left cylinders saying he had completed the tower they began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Leon Legrain, “The Third Dynasty of Ur. The Stela of Ur-Nammu,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> X, no. 3–4 (1944): 56–57, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/2657/. [Open access]","C. Leonard Woolley, “The Ziggurat of Ur,” <i>The Museum Journal</i> XV, no. 2 (1924): 107–14, https://www.penn.museum/sites/journal/1235/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Clou de fondation,” inv. SB 2879, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010176957. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Figurine de fondation,” inv. AO 27907, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010152187. [Open access]","Samuel Noah Kramer, “The Ur Excavations and Sumerian Literature,” <i>Expedition</i> 20, no. 1 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-ur-excavations-and-sumerian-literature/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Ur-Nassiriyah.jpg/1280px-Ur-Nassiriyah.jpg","title":"Third Dynasty of Ur","desc":"The ziggurat at Ur, begun by Ur-Nammu, rising behind the excavated mud-brick walls of the city near Nasiriyah in southern Iraq. M. Lubinski, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur-Nassiriyah.jpg","alt":"A broad stepped tower of mud brick with a straight staircase climbing its front, standing above a flat desert plain beyond a field of low excavated brick walls laid out in rooms and corridors"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Ur-Nammu's stela show him carrying a mason's tools?","a":"Because it depicts him laying the foundation brick himself while workers climb ladders with baskets of mud — a king beginning a building work in person, at least in ceremony."},{"q":"Why did Nabonidus drive a hole into the tower's brickwork?","a":"Because fifteen centuries later he was hunting the old foundation records, and he left cylinders saying he had completed the tower those kings began."},{"q":"Why is Ur called a school town as well as a capital?","a":"Because a branch of the Edubba stood there, over 800 pieces of Sumerian literary text were found, and its kings are credited with a Sumerian renaissance."}]},
{"id":"wh-187","num":187,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A broken Nippur tablet in Istanbul carries the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose prologue promises that the orphan shall not fall a prey to the wealthy nor the widow to the powerful.","questions":["Where a later code would take an eye for an eye, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sets a fine of ten silver shekels on a man who has cut off another's foot.","Promulgated by the king who founded the Third Dynasty of Ur, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the title of oldest law collection known, three centuries older than Hammurabi's."],"answer":"Code of Ur-Nammu","answerText":"Code of Ur-Nammu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ur-Nammu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reigned from 2112 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Code of Ur-Nammu</b> is the oldest collection of laws yet recovered, written in Sumerian in the name of the king who founded an independent state centred on Ur in 2112 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His line, the third dynasty to rule that city, held Sumer until the Elamites sacked Ur in 2004 BCE, and its second king Shulgi reigned from 2094 to 2047 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The code survives on a broken tablet in the Istanbul Museum of the Ancient Orient, number 3191 of its Nippur collection, joined from two fragments by a curator there and read in 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is sun-baked and light brown, 20 by 10 centimetres (8 by 4 inches), ruled into eight columns with about 45 lines each, and more than half the writing is destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a copy rather than the original, of the kind students at Nippur and Ur wrote out to learn Sumerian, made some centuries after Ur-Nammu's own reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The obverse carries a long prologue in which An and Enlil make the moon-god Nanna king of Ur, and Nanna in turn chooses Ur-Nammu as his earthly representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The king defeats the rival city of Lagash and kills its ruler Namhani, drives out the 'grabbers' of the citizens' oxen, sheep and donkeys, and fixes honest and unchangeable weights and measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He claims that under him the orphan 'did not fall a prey to the wealthy', the widow not to the powerful, and 'the man of one shekel' not to 'the man of one mina'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The laws began on the reverse and are so damaged that only five can be restored, of which three set silver fines where later codes take an eye for an eye: ten shekels for a severed foot, a whole mina for bones broken with a weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The title of oldest code had already passed twice in the five years before, from Hammurabi's stele in the Louvre to a Sumerian code of Lipit-Ishtar and then to a Babylonian one dug up at Tell Harmal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Samuel Noah Kramer, “Law and Love, A Hymn, A Prayer, and a Word to the Wise,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> XVII, no. 2 (1952): 23–42, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/3637/. [Open access]","Richard Zettler, “The Empire of Ur-Nammu under His Descendants,” <i>Expedition</i> 29, no. 1 (1987), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-empire-of-ur-nammu-under-his-descendants/. [Open access]","Richard L. Zettler, “From Beneath the Temple: Inscribed Objects from Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 28, no. 3 (1986), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-beneath-the-temple/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Ur_Nammu_code_Istanbul_cropped.jpg","title":"Code of Ur-Nammu","desc":"The broken clay tablet from Nippur carrying the Code of Ur-Nammu, in the Istanbul Archaeology Museums. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur_Nammu_code_Istanbul_cropped.jpg","alt":"A dark, badly broken rectangular clay tablet, its surface ruled into narrow vertical columns of small wedge-shaped cuneiform signs, with much of the upper right corner and the lower edge missing"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the surviving tablet not the original code?","a":"Because it is a copy of the kind students at Nippur and Ur wrote out to learn Sumerian, made some centuries after Ur-Nammu's own reign."},{"q":"Why can only five of its laws be restored?","a":"Because the laws began on the reverse of a broken tablet more than half of whose writing is destroyed, ruled into eight columns of about 45 lines each."},{"q":"Why do its penalties look unlike the later codes?","a":"Because three of the five set silver fines where later codes take an eye for an eye — ten shekels for a severed foot, a whole mina for bones broken with a weapon."}]},
{"id":"wh-188","num":188,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A Sumerian schoolroom poem copied in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a boy caned for speaking Akkadian, which says plainly enough which of the two languages he grew up with.","questions":["More than fifty houses on narrow winding lanes stood at Ur in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the dead of those households were buried beneath their own floors.","Amorite dynasties out of Syria ruled the rival cities of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them Mari in the north and the Babylon where Hammurabi came to the throne in 1792 BCE."],"answer":"Old Babylonian period","answerText":"Old Babylonian period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ur sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2004 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hammurabi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Old Babylonian period</b> runs from about 2000 to 1500 BCE, the centuries after the Elamites sacked Ur in 2004 BCE and left southern Mesopotamia with no single ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Power passed to dynasties founded by Amorites, a West Semitic people who had been moving east out of Syria and whose earliest rulers the Assyrian king list remembers as 'kings who lived in tents'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They took the old cities for their own and made rivals of them, Shamshi-Adad building an Assyrian realm in the north while the diplomatic correspondence kept at Mari records how these kings dealt with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The one who came out on top was Hammurabi, himself of Amorite descent, who ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE and whose stele of laws now stands in the Louvre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Akkadian was by now the language people spoke, and Sumerian survived as the language of scholarship, taught in school to men who had grown up speaking something else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nearly all the Sumerian literature that can be read today was copied out in the scribal schools, most of it at Nippur, by students working a curriculum that began with sign lists and ended with poetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Schooling took place in ordinary houses rather than in institutions, and one house at Ur held so many exercise tablets that it can only have been a schoolroom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Sumerian poem about school days, itself one of the set texts, has a boy beaten by the keeper of the gate, by the keeper of the beer jug, and by the Sumerian teacher who demands to know why he spoke Akkadian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Ur more than fifty houses stood along narrow winding lanes, and they remain the largest continuous stretch of Old Babylonian housing known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dead were buried beneath the floors of those houses, in or near the room the family kept as its chapel, and 172 graves of the Isin-Larsa and Old Babylonian levels were recorded in that quarter alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]","Richard Zettler, “The Empire of Ur-Nammu under His Descendants,” <i>Expedition</i> 29, no. 1 (1987), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-empire-of-ur-nammu-under-his-descendants/. [Open access]","Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","William B. Hafford, “City of the Moon: New Excavations at Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 59, no. 1 (2017), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/city-of-the-moon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/CBS7847_Ninmeshara_Penn_Museum.jpg","title":"Old Babylonian period","desc":"An Old Babylonian copy of the Sumerian hymn known as the Exaltation of Inanna, found at Nippur and now in the Penn Museum. Masha Stoyanova, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CBS7847_Ninmeshara_Penn_Museum.jpg","alt":"A pale, chipped rectangular clay tablet standing upright, its face covered in close-set horizontal rows of small cuneiform signs divided into ruled columns"},"why":[{"q":"Why was Sumerian taught to people who did not speak it?","a":"Because Akkadian was by now the spoken language and Sumerian survived as the language of scholarship, so it was learned in school by men who had grown up speaking something else."},{"q":"Why does nearly all Sumerian literature come from schoolrooms?","a":"Because it was copied out by students working a curriculum that began with sign lists and ended with poetry, most of it at Nippur — and schooling took place in ordinary houses, one at Ur holding so many exercise tablets it can only have been a schoolroom."},{"q":"Why did power pass to Amorite dynasties?","a":"Because the Elamite sack of Ur in 2004 BCE left southern Mesopotamia with no single ruler, and the Amorites moving east out of Syria took the old cities for their own."}]},
{"id":"wh-189","num":189,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Though his people spoke Akkadian, the scribes made praise poems in Sumerian for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, calling him the good shepherd who restored the rites at the moon god's shrine.","questions":["An Amorite king who rose to challenge the Assyrian realm to his north, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Babylon for 42 years, and a stele carrying his name was later carried off to Susa as booty.","A dark stone vase carved on the Iranian Plateau was inscribed again more than 800 years later in the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which shows what such eastern goods were still worth."],"answer":"Hammurabi","answerText":"Hammurabi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Babylon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st dynasty</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hammurabi</b> ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE as a king of its first dynasty, and he came of Amorite stock, the West Semitic people who had been moving east into Mesopotamia out of Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his earlier years the greater power lay north of him, where Shamshi-Adad had built an Assyrian realm reaching from the Khabur to the Euphrates, and he emerged as a serious rival about ten years before that king died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How the kings of the age dealt with one another is known above all from the diplomatic correspondence kept at Mari.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His court had poetry made for him in Sumerian although Akkadian was what people spoke, and one praise poem calls him the good shepherd, reverent, eloquent and skilled in precious plans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same poem has him daily before Nanna and Ningal, restoring the purification rites and the divine powers at their shrine E-kish-nu-gal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A prayer written for him asks Enki to prolong the years of his life and to grant him lordship over every one of the foreign lands, which is as plain a statement of ambition as the period leaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A high official of Larsa named Lu-Nanna had a small kneeling figure of copper and gold made and dedicated it, in Sumerian, to the Amorites' own god Amurru, for the life of Hammurabi, king of Babylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His name travelled as far as his trade did: a dark stone vase carved on the Iranian Plateau about 2400 BCE was inscribed for him more than 800 years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He is remembered above all for a stele of laws whose relief shows him standing before the sun god Shamash, a meeting that legitimises his sovereignty and the judgements cut into the stone below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stele did not stay in Babylonia: where Ur-Nammu's was smashed by invaders, this one was carried off whole as booty, and it came out of the ground at Susa in Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","“A praise poem of Ḫammu-rābi (Ḫammu-rābi C),” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.8.2.3 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.2.8.2.3. [Open access]","“A prayer to Enki for Ḫammu-rābi (Ḫammu-rābi B),” in J. A. Black et al., <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i>, c.2.8.2.2 (Oxford, 1998–2006), https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.2.8.2.2. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “L'Adorant de Larsa,” inv. AO 15704, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010140480. [Open access]","Holly Pittman, “Highlands and Lowlands: The Interaction of Communities,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/highlands-and-lowlands/. [Open access]","Jeanny Vorys Canby, “A Monumental Puzzle,” <i>Expedition</i> 29, no. 1 (1987), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-monumental-puzzle/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Worshiper_Larsa_Mp3h9123.jpg","title":"Hammurabi","desc":"The Worshipper of Larsa, a copper and gold figure dedicated to the god Amurru for the life of Hammurabi, king of Babylon. Louvre AO 15704. Rama, CC BY-SA 3.0 FR, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Worshiper_Larsa_Mp3h9123.jpg","alt":"A small metal figure of a bearded man in a high-brimmed cap, half kneeling with one hand raised before his mouth in prayer, his face and hand covered in gold, set on a base beside a reclining ram"},"why":[{"q":"Why was Hammurabi not the dominant king of his early years?","a":"Because the greater power lay north of him, where Shamshi-Adad had built an Assyrian realm reaching from the Khabur to the Euphrates, and he emerged as a rival only about ten years before that king died."},{"q":"Why does his stele's relief matter as much as its laws?","a":"Because it shows him standing before the sun god Shamash, a meeting that legitimises his sovereignty and the judgements cut into the stone below."},{"q":"Why did his stele survive whole where Ur-Nammu's did not?","a":"Because it was carried off as booty rather than smashed by invaders, and came out of the ground at Susa in Iran."}]},
{"id":"wh-190","num":190,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"A surgeon who saves an eye earns ten shekels under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and one whose knife kills the patient has his hands cut off for it.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes an eye for an eye between men of equal standing, but settles the very same injury in silver when it is done to a freed man.","Cut into a basalt stele over two metres tall and carried off to Susa in antiquity, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is numbered to 282, though 34 of its paragraphs no longer survive."],"answer":"Code of Hammurabi","answerText":"Code of Hammurabi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hammurabi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Laws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">282, of which 34 lost</span></div>","difficulty":2,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Code of Hammurabi</b> is a collection of laws set out in Old Babylonian Akkadian on a basalt stele 2.25 metres (7 feet 5 inches) tall, made for the king who ruled Babylon from 1792 to 1750 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stone was found not in Babylonia but at Susa in Iran, dug out of the ground there over the winter of 1901–02, and it stands in the Louvre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A relief across its top shows the king receiving the symbols of kingship from Shamash, the sun god, in a scene that underscores his divinely given right to establish the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The laws are framed by a prologue in which Anu and Bel call Hammurabi by name to bring about the rule of righteousness in the land, and by an epilogue thick with curses on anyone who would alter them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are numbered to 282 and run the gamut of a man's possible deeds and misdeeds, though 34 paragraphs between the sixty-fifth and the hundredth no longer survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known provision is talion: a man who puts out another's eye loses his own, and one who breaks another's bone has his bone broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That holds only between men of equal standing, since the same injuries done to a freed man are settled in silver, so what a crime costs depends on the rank of the person it was done to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A surgeon who opens a tumour and saves the eye earns ten shekels, while one whose knife kills the patient has his hands cut off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A builder whose house collapses and kills the owner is put to death, and if it kills the owner's son then the builder's son is put to death in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A clay tablet in the Louvre carries the prologue in 120 lines, and its shorter list of the cities Hammurabi had annexed suggests it was written before the stele, so the text grew as his conquests did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Musée du Louvre, “Code de Hammurabi,” inv. SB 8, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010174436. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “The Uses of Writing: Inscribed Objects and Texts in the Middle East Galleries,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-uses-of-writing/. [Open access]","<i>The Code of Hammurabi</i>, trans. L. W. King, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/ancient/hamcode.asp. [Open access]","Samuel Noah Kramer, “Law and Love, A Hymn, A Prayer, and a Word to the Wise,” <i>Museum Bulletin</i> XVII, no. 2 (1952): 23–42, https://www.penn.museum/sites/bulletin/3637/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Tablette: prologue du code de Hammurabi,” inv. AO 10237, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010134970. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Code_of_Hammurabi_10.jpg","title":"Code of Hammurabi","desc":"The top of the stele in the Louvre, with Hammurabi standing before the enthroned sun god Shamash, beside the gallery's own panel on the code. Chad and Steph, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Code_of_Hammurabi_10.jpg","alt":"The rounded top of a tall dark stone pillar carved in relief with a seated god holding out a ring and rod to a standing bearded king, the stone below them covered in columns of cuneiform, with a lit museum wall panel to the left"},"why":[{"q":"Why does what a crime costs depend on rank?","a":"Because talion holds only between men of equal standing: a man who puts out an equal's eye loses his own, while the same injuries done to a freed man are settled in silver."},{"q":"Why does the Louvre's prologue tablet suggest the text grew?","a":"Because its shorter list of the cities Hammurabi had annexed suggests it was written before the stele, so the text expanded as his conquests did."},{"q":"Why is the stele framed by a prologue and curses?","a":"Because the prologue has Anu and Bel call Hammurabi by name to bring about the rule of righteousness, and the epilogue lays curses on anyone who would alter the laws."}]},
{"id":"wh-191","num":191,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Hammurabi’s laws close by asking that his name be repeated for ever in Esagila, the temple of Marduk at the heart of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that any wronged man read them there.","questions":["The gods gave Marduk dominion over earthly man and then called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by his name, so that city and god rose together and could not afterwards be told apart.","Eleven centuries after Hammurabi, Nebuchadnezzar II was still stamping his foundation bricks at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the title provider of Esagila, the temple at its heart."],"answer":"Babylon","answerText":"Babylon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hammurabi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nebuchadnezzar II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">604 – 562 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken by Cyrus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":1,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Babylon</b> was the city on the Euphrates, in the south of Mesopotamia, that an Amorite dynasty raised into a capital, and its rise is announced in the prologue to Hammurabi’s laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There the gods give Marduk dominion over earthly man, and then call Babylon by his name, make it great on earth and found an everlasting kingdom in it whose foundations are laid as solidly as those of heaven and earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> City and god rose together, so that the king could style himself the sun of Babylon whose rays shed light over the land of Sumer and Akkad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same prologue takes care to present him as the patron of far older places, re-establishing Eridu, enriching Ur, laying again the foundations of Sippar and giving back to the city of Ashur its protecting god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marduk’s own temple at the centre of Babylon was Esagila, and the laws close by asking that the king’s name be repeated for ever there, and that any wronged man come, stand before the royal image and read what was just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Eleven centuries later Nebuchadnezzar II was still stamping his foundation bricks with the title provider of Esagila, first-born son of Nabopolassar, king of Babylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His is the Babylon that can be seen, rebuilt on a scale nothing before it had matched, with a processional way walled in glazed and moulded brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lions in coloured glaze paced along those walls about 600 BCE, and it was such bright fragments lying loose on the ground that first drew excavators to the mound at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By then Babylon had inherited an empire, the Babylonians of southern Mesopotamia having conquered Assyria in 612 BCE and taken over the territories it had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its own turn came in 539 BCE, when Cyrus the Great of Persia took the city, and Babylon passed into somebody else’s empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["<i>The Code of Hammurabi</i>, trans. L. W. King, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/ancient/hamcode.asp. [Open access]","Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Brique de fondation de Nabuchodonosor II,” inv. AO 32450, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010156663. [Open access]","Robert H. Dyson, Jr., “A Babylonian Lion in Toronto,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 2 (1963), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-babylonian-lion-in-toronto/. [Open access]","Virginia R. Herrmann, “From Trash to Historical Treasure: Stamped Handles from Gibeon in Judah,” <i>Expedition</i> 64, no. 3 (2023), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-trash-to-historical-treasure/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Processional_Way%2C_Babylon_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Processional_Way%2C_Babylon_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Processional Way, Babylon","desc":"A striding lion in glazed and moulded brick from the Processional Way, dated to the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II and re-erected in the Pergamon Museum in Berlin, where the surviving glazed pieces stand among plain brick. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Processional_Way,_Babylon_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A lion walking to the left, modelled in relief in brick and glazed in white, ochre and orange against a field of blue brick, with banded borders above and below, much of the surrounding brickwork plain and unglazed"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Babylon's rise and Marduk's rise go together?","a":"Because the prologue to Hammurabi's laws has the gods give Marduk dominion over earthly man and then call Babylon by his name, founding an everlasting kingdom in it."},{"q":"Why does that prologue name Eridu, Ur, Sippar and Ashur?","a":"Because it presents the king as the patron of far older places — re-establishing Eridu, enriching Ur, laying again the foundations of Sippar, giving Ashur back its protecting god."},{"q":"Why did excavators first come to the mound?","a":"Because bright fragments of the glazed and moulded brick of Nebuchadnezzar's processional way, its lions pacing in coloured glaze about 600 BCE, were lying loose on the ground."}]},
{"id":"wh-192","num":192,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because its worshippers left hymns, dedications and inscriptions buried under temple walls, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be read from the inside as well as dug up, city god by city god.","questions":["Since the temple was the god's own house, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made building one the king's work, and he carried in the first basket of bricks himself.","Because the great temples were not open to everyone at all times, much of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happened at neighbourhood shrines and in the private chapels many houses kept."],"answer":"Mesopotamian religion","answerText":"Mesopotamian religion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Votive statue</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Offering stand</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2120 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple at Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shulgi, 2094 – 2047 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesopotamian religion</b> was the worship of the gods of the cities between the Tigris and Euphrates, and it can be read from the inside, in hymns, dedications and building inscriptions worshippers wrote down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each city had a patron god of its own: at Ur it was Nanna, the moon god, and at Nippur, Enlil, chief of all Mesopotamian deities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hymn has all the gods of the earth bow down to Enlil on his holy dais in Nippur, the lofty bond between heaven and earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The temple was that god's house, and the same hymn gives it a daily festival, everlasting hand-washing rites, and graded priests: the <i>lagar</i> expert in blessing, the <i>gudug</i> in lustration rites, the <i>nueš</i> in the holy prayers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Priests and priestesses ran the cult of a deity, and the most powerful at Ur was the <i>entu</i> priestess, who kept the rituals of Nanna and his consort Ningal, a post usually held by the king's daughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Building for a god was the king's work: he brought in the first basket of bricks at the groundbreaking and had his name stamped into them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the walls of Nimintabba's temple at Ur, boxes of baked brick set in bitumen each held a steatite tablet and a statuette of a man with a filled basket on his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their eight lines of Sumerian say whose house it was: for the goddess Nimintabba, his lady, Shulgi, mighty man, king of Ur, king of Sumer and Akkad, her house, built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Others reached the gods by giving: a scribe dedicated a diorite statue of a worshipper with joined hands to the god Erra for the life of Naram-Sin, and Ninalla gave Bawu an offering stand for the life of her husband Gudea of Lagash and her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most worship happened closer to home, at neighbourhood shrines and in the private chapels, altar and incense chimney and all, that many houses kept, since the great temples were not always open to everyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William B. Hafford, “Mesopotamian City Life: Four Thousand Years Ago,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/mesopotamian-city-life/. [Open access]","“Enlil in the E-kur (Enlil A),” <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i> t.4.05.1, Faculty of Oriental Studies, University of Oxford, https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.4.05.1. [Open access]","Richard L. Zettler, “From Beneath the Temple: Inscribed Objects from Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 28, no. 3 (1986), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-beneath-the-temple/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Fragment d’une statue votive de Naram-Sin,” inv. SB 53, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010121488. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Support d'offrandes ou brûle parfum voué à Gudea,” inv. AO 29931, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010154298. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Foundation_figurine_of_Ur-Nammu%2C_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._21st_century_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg/1280px-Foundation_figurine_of_Ur-Nammu%2C_from_Nippur%2C_Iraq._21st_century_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg","title":"Foundation figurine of Ur-Nammu","desc":"A bronze figure buried with an inscribed stone tablet beneath the E-kur of Enlil at Nippur, showing the king carrying a basket of clay on his head as the builder of the temple. Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foundation_figurine_of_Ur-Nammu,_from_Nippur,_Iraq._21st_century_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg","alt":"A small bronze figure of a bearded man in a long skirt, both arms raised to steady a wide basket balanced on his head, the lower part of the skirt covered in cuneiform and tapering towards a peg"},"why":[{"q":"Why can this religion be read from the inside?","a":"Because it survives in hymns, dedications and building inscriptions that worshippers wrote down themselves, rather than only in outsiders' descriptions."},{"q":"Why was building a temple the king's own work?","a":"Because he brought in the first basket of bricks at the groundbreaking and had his name stamped into them — the foundation deposits at Ur show a man with a filled basket on his head."},{"q":"Why did most worship happen away from the great temples?","a":"Because those were not always open to everyone, so people used neighbourhood shrines and the private chapels, altar and incense chimney and all, that many houses kept."}]},
{"id":"wh-193","num":193,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Man is made from the blood of Kingu, and heaven from half of Tiamat, in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the seven-tablet poem the Babylonians called after its own first two words.","questions":["Europe knew the story only from a Greek summary until the tablets of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were read, and found the sea cut in two much as the summary had said.","The seventh and last tablet is given over to proclaiming the fifty names of Marduk, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ends on the god it has spent six tablets raising above the rest."],"answer":"Enuma Elish","answerText":"Enuma Elish","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Nineveh copies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reign of Ashurbanipal, c. 670 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Enuma Elish</b> is the Babylonian poem of the creation, called after its own first two words, “When in the height”, and written out on seven tablets running to some 994 lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens before anything has a name: heaven not yet named, the earth beneath bearing no name, Apsu who begat them and Tiamat the mother of them both, their waters mingled and no field yet formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gods come forth from that chaos, Ea overthrows Apsu, and Tiamat sets about avenging him, promoting the god Kingu to lead her host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only after Ea and Anu have each failed to appease her does Anshar appeal to Marduk to become the champion of the gods, who confer their power on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marduk spreads out his net and catches her, looses the evil wind in her face, then splits her up like a flat fish into two halves and makes one a covering for heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Man was made last, out of a god’s blood, though whose stayed uncertain until tablets from Ashur closed the gap in the sixth: not Marduk’s own, as had been conjectured, but Kingu’s, condemned as the one who created warfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The seventh tablet ends by proclaiming Marduk’s fifty names, of which the recovered text gives half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before a line of it could be read again, Europe knew the story only from a priest of Babylon writing in Greek, who told how Belus cut a woman asunder to make the earth of one half of her and the heavens of the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every copy then known had been inscribed for the library at Nineveh in the reign of Ashurbanipal, and none was an original, the Assyrians themselves copying something older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its order of events runs as Genesis does, primeval chaos, light, sky, dry land and the astral bodies, with man last of all, and the likeness reaches even the syntax of the opening verses, “When … at which time … then …”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["L. W. King, <i>The Seven Tablets of Creation</i>, vol. 1 (London: Luzac, 1902), https://archive.org/details/the-seven-tablets-of-creation.-vol.-1. [Open access]","D. D. Luckenbill, “The Ashur Version of the Seven Tablets of Creation,” <i>The American Journal of Semitic Languages and Literatures</i> 38, no. 1 (1921): 12–35, https://archive.org/details/ashurversionofse00luck. [Open access]","E. A. Speiser, “Mesopotamian Motifs in the Early Chapters of Genesis,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 1 (1962), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/mesopotamian-motifs-in-the-early-chapters-of-genesis/. [Open access]","George Smith, <i>The Chaldean Account of Genesis</i> (New York: Scribner, Armstrong, 1876), https://archive.org/details/chaldeanaccount00smitrich. [Open access]","Isaac Preston Cory, <i>Ancient Fragments</i>, 2nd ed. (London: William Pickering, 1832), https://archive.org/details/CoryIPAncientFragmentsContainingWhatRemainsOfTheWritingsOfSanchoniathoBerossusAb. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Enuma_Elish_K.3473.jpg/1280px-Enuma_Elish_K.3473.jpg","title":"A tablet of Enuma Elish","desc":"Tablet K.3473, a fragment of the poem from the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, broken and pinned back together in the British Museum. Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enuma_Elish_K.3473.jpg","alt":"A tall clay tablet broken into several pieces and mounted with small clips against a green cloth panel, its face covered in closely ruled lines of cuneiform"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the source of the blood that made man stay uncertain?","a":"Because the sixth tablet had a gap, and it was conjectured to be Marduk's own until tablets from Ashur showed it was Kingu's, condemned as the one who created warfare."},{"q":"Why was the story known in Europe before the poem could be read?","a":"Because a priest of Babylon writing in Greek told how Belus cut a woman asunder to make the earth of one half of her and the heavens of the other."},{"q":"Why is no surviving copy an original?","a":"Because every copy then known had been inscribed for the library at Nineveh in Ashurbanipal's reign, and the Assyrians were themselves copying something older."}]},
{"id":"wh-194","num":194,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because a square with its diagonals drawn in was a school exercise, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knew what would be called Pythagoras’ theorem more than a thousand years before Pythagoras.","questions":["Counting in sixties and writing with only two symbols, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled thousands of tablets with tables, problems and calculations that scribes copied out at school.","A table of whole numbers at Columbia shows that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a method for making right-angled triangles out of whole numbers, a millennium and a half before Euclid."],"answer":"Babylonian mathematics","answerText":"Babylonian mathematics","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Babylonian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">School at Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first half of the 18th century BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Babylonian mathematics</b> is known from thousands of clay tablets of the Old Babylonian period, roughly 1900 to 1700 BCE, which carry tables, problems and calculations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its numbers are sexagesimal, counted in sixties, and written in a notation built entirely out of two symbols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the tablets come from the scribal schools, and the commonest of them are the arithmetical tables young scribes copied out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One from Nippur is small and narrow, its script practised and elegant, the end of the text ruled off with a neat horizontal line and signed “long tablet of Gan-Gal”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A private house at Ur gave up over two thousand texts, administrative, lexical, mathematical and literary, those that can be dated coming from the first half of the 18th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A round school tablet at Yale carries nothing but a diagram, a square with both its diagonals drawn in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Along one diagonal is an accurate approximation of the square root of 2 in sexagesimal notation, with one side labelled with its length and the product of the two written there as well, so the scribe is using what would be called Pythagoras’ theorem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tablet known as Plimpton 322 goes further, showing that a method existed for generating Pythagorean triples, sets of three whole numbers in which the square of one is the sum of the squares of the other two, a millennium and a half before Euclid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What that tablet was for is still argued over, with no agreement on how its numbers were worked out or on whether it belonged to scribal education or to something more like research.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the advanced work was harder than anything a scribe would meet in a career, and reads as knowledge pursued for its own sake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Before Pythagoras: The Culture of Old Babylonian Mathematics,” exhibition, New York University, 2010, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/index.html. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Tablet Illustrating Pythagoras’ Theorem and the Square Root of 2,” YBC 7289, Yale Babylonian Collection, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/ybc-7289/. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Table of Whole-Number Sides of Pythagorean Triangles,” Plimpton 322, Columbia University, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/plimpton-322/. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “School Tablet with Multiplication Table,” B6063, University of Pennsylvania Museum, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/before-pythagoras/items/b-6063/. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg/1280px-YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg","title":"Tablet YBC 7289","desc":"The obverse of a round school tablet in the Yale Babylonian Collection, a square with both its diagonals drawn in, photographed with the numbers written along them transcribed alongside. A. Urcia, Yale Peabody Museum of Natural History, and Theodor Langhorne Franklin, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg","alt":"A round reddish clay tablet, chipped at the edges, incised with a square and both of its diagonals, cuneiform numbers cut along one side and one diagonal, with modern numerals printed beside each group"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the Yale round tablet show a scribe using Pythagoras' theorem?","a":"Because its square with both diagonals drawn carries an accurate sexagesimal approximation of the square root of 2, with one side labelled and the product of the two written there as well."},{"q":"Why is the purpose of Plimpton 322 still argued over?","a":"Because there is no agreement on how its numbers were worked out, nor on whether it belonged to scribal education or to something more like research."},{"q":"Why does some of this mathematics read as pursued for its own sake?","a":"Because much of the advanced work was harder than anything a scribe would meet in a career, so it cannot be explained as training for the job."}]},
{"id":"wh-195","num":195,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because a diary was kept night after night for seven centuries, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a record in which a comet of 164 BCE can still be identified as Halley’s.","questions":["Watching the sky was never separable from reading it, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded that when Jupiter appears in Elul the land will eat good food.","Setting the twelve signs against the twelve months of the year, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave every month its own stones, its own plants and its own trees."],"answer":"Babylonian astronomy","answerText":"Babylonian astronomy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Diaries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 BCE – 75 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zodiac tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Seleucid, 305 – 64 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Babylonian astronomy</b> was the systematic watching of the sky, written down in cuneiform over more than seven centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The astronomical diaries run from about 750 BCE to perhaps as late as 75 CE, and what survives of them shows the sky mapped carefully and the heavenly bodies observed day by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Assyrian kings were sent reports by named observers, and one of them admits that two or three times during these days we have looked for Mars, but could not see it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seven moving bodies, the sun and moon among them, had names of their own, Sin for the moon and Shamash for the sun, Dilbat for Venus and Kaimanu for Saturn, and their places were noted against the constellations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eclipses were watched with the greatest possible care and their duration and extent recorded, the moon’s disc being read as four parts standing for four countries, so that a total eclipse was called a darkness of the countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Observing was inseparable from divining: when Jupiter appears in Elul the land will eat good food, and when the moon occults Jupiter a great king will die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tablet from Uruk sets the twelve signs of the zodiac against the twelve months of the year, giving each month its own stones, plants and trees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another from the same city records the phases of the moon, then divides the sign of the Ram into twelve parts of its own, then casts horoscopes on the agreement between events and the movement of the planets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 164 BCE the diaries noted a comet that had appeared in the east in the path of Anu, near the Pleiades and Taurus, and passed west along the path of Ea, which has since been identified as Halley’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greeks and Romans later divided the day into hours by adapting what they took from Babylonia and from Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["R. Campbell Thompson, <i>The Reports of the Magicians and Astrologers of Nineveh and Babylon</i>, vol. 2, English Translation and Transliteration (London: Luzac, 1900), https://archive.org/details/reportsofmagicia02thom. [Open access]","Christina Griffith, “Halley’s Comet: A Frequent Guest in Earth’s Cosmic Backyard,” <i>Expedition</i> 61, no. 1 (2019), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/halleys-comet/. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Calendrier zodiacal du signe de la vierge,” inv. AO 6448, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010167087. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Tablette : observations astronomiques suivies d’horoscopes,” inv. AO 6483, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010167126. [Open access]","Institute for the Study of the Ancient World, “Time and Cosmos in Greco-Roman Antiquity,” exhibition, New York University, 2016, https://isaw.nyu.edu/exhibitions/time-cosmos/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/British_Museum%2C_Babylon_Astronomical_Diary%2C_331-330_BC.jpg/1280px-British_Museum%2C_Babylon_Astronomical_Diary%2C_331-330_BC.jpg","title":"An astronomical diary from Babylon","desc":"Part of a diary of 331–330 BCE recording a lunar eclipse, and the earliest contemporary record of the battle of Gaugamela, in the British Museum. Brigade Piron, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum,_Babylon_Astronomical_Diary,_331-330_BC.jpg","alt":"A broken clay tablet mounted on a metal stand against an amber background, its face ruled into close lines of small, sharply cut cuneiform"},"why":[{"q":"Why was observing the sky inseparable from divining?","a":"Because the records read the sky for consequences: when Jupiter appears in Elul the land will eat good food, and when the moon occults Jupiter a great king will die."},{"q":"Why was a total lunar eclipse called a darkness of the countries?","a":"Because the moon's disc was read as four parts standing for four countries, so an eclipse covering all of it darkened all of them."},{"q":"Why do the diaries matter beyond their own time?","a":"Because they record more than seven centuries of day-by-day observation — in 164 BCE a comet in the path of Anu near the Pleiades, since identified as Halley's."}]},
{"id":"wh-196","num":196,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because a pupil's practice tablet went back into the water bucket to be softened and used again, most of what survives from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is there by accident.","questions":["Teaching in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began not with words but with wedges: repeated verticals, then corners, pressed into clay with a reed stylus until the hand made them without thinking.","Since few schoolrooms have been identified, much teaching in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thought to have gone on in private houses, one of which at Ur gave up over two thousand texts."],"answer":"edubba","answerText":"edubba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Babylonian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">School at Ur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nippur exercise</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1730 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>edubba</b>, or tablet house, was the Sumerian school where a scribe learned to write, and the exercise tablets left in it are how that teaching is known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Few schoolrooms are known, and much of the work went on in private houses: the house at 7 Quiet Street in Ur gave up over two thousand texts, the datable ones from the first half of the 18th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A pupil began with the stylus itself, a trimmed reed pressed into clay to leave repeated vertical wedges, then corner wedges, and only afterwards combinations that made signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Syllables came next, about 80 enough to spell anything a scribe needed, then lists of personal names and of words grouped by subject, the trees and wooden objects running past 800 entries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Literature came last, and its range was wide: myths, praises of kings, city laments, hymns to gods and temples, disputations between animals, seasons and tools, proverbs, and comic pieces about school life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The commonest exercise tablet at Nippur carries two columns, the teacher's example on the left and on the right a scratch pad written and erased until it wore thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small round tablets called lentils took a single line from the teacher for copying underneath; one, from a hymn to Lipit-Eshtar, reads “She guided your finger on the clay”, the 'she' being Nisaba, goddess of writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most tablets went back into a bucket of water to be used again, and a brick-lined box of exercise fragments was found sunk into the floor of one house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In one Sumerian dialogue a supervisor passes on his teacher's rules, that zeal is proper for a task and time-wasting is taboo, and the young scribe answers that he has run the household and worked the fields and resents being lectured as a shirker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing stayed the property of that class, and a hymn copied long afterwards called the scribal art the mother of orators and father of masters, delightful and never satiating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Steve Tinney, “Texts, Tablets, and Teaching: Scribal Education in Nippur and Ur,” <i>Expedition</i> 40, no. 2 (1998), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/texts-tablets-and-teaching/. [Open access]","“The advice of a supervisor to a younger scribe (E-dub-ba-a C),” <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i> t.5.1.3, Faculty of Oriental Studies, University of Oxford, https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.5.1.3. [Open access]","Stephen J. Tinney, “From Clay to Computers,” <i>Expedition</i> 50, no. 3 (2008), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/from-clay-to-computers/. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “The Uses of Writing: Inscribed Objects and Texts in the Middle East Galleries,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-uses-of-writing/. [Open access]","Ann Guinan, Gary Oller, and Dorothy Ormsby, “Nippur Rebaked: The Conservation of Cuneiform Tablets,” <i>Expedition</i> 18, no. 3 (1976), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/nippur-rebaked/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Cuneiform_tablet_with_schoolwork_BM_104096.jpg/1280px-Cuneiform_tablet_with_schoolwork_BM_104096.jpg","title":"School tablet with a proverb","desc":"A small round school tablet of the Old Babylonian period carrying a proverb: the teacher's version is on this face and the pupil's copy on the other. British Museum 104096. Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuneiform_tablet_with_schoolwork_BM_104096.jpg","alt":"A palm-sized disc of pale clay held upright on a clear museum mount, its face ruled into four bands filled with wedge-shaped signs and the lowest part of the disc left blank"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the teaching known from exercise tablets rather than from schools?","a":"Because few schoolrooms are known and much of the work went on in private houses — 7 Quiet Street in Ur gave up over two thousand texts."},{"q":"Why did a pupil begin with wedges rather than with signs?","a":"Because the training ran from pressing repeated vertical wedges into clay, to corner wedges, and only afterwards to the combinations that made signs."},{"q":"Why do so few exercise tablets survive whole?","a":"Because most went back into a bucket of water to be used again, and the two-column pads were written and erased until they wore thin."}]},
{"id":"wh-197","num":197,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Since the southern alluvium had mud, reeds and dates but no metal, no timber and no hard coloured stone, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was how the cities got everything else.","questions":["Cuneiform texts name three lands beyond the Lower Sea, Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha, always in that order, which is the clearest map anyone has of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by sea.","Wool worked into cloth was the surplus southern Mesopotamia had to spend, which made textiles the main thing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to offer for the metals and stone the south lacked."],"answer":"Mesopotamian trade","answerText":"Mesopotamian trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Textile trade</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sargon of Akkad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2334 – 2279 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assur caravans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 2nd millennium BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesopotamian trade</b> was the exchange that kept the southern cities supplied, since almost everything an urban life needed had to be brought in from somewhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The alluvium gave mud and marsh reeds to build with, bitumen oozing from the ground to waterproof boats and containers, poor stone in accessible outcrops and the date palm; beyond those it gave nothing, no metals, no tall hardwood timbers and no hard, colourful stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the south did have was water and land, and pasture on the uplands either side of the rivers for great herds of sheep and goats, whose wool the cities spun and wove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of the 4th millennium BCE textiles had become its chief manufactured commodity, and cloth, with some specially prepared foods and ointments, was what it paid the highlands with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lapis lazuli came from Afghanistan, more than 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles) off across mountain and desert, and since Sumer controlled neither the source nor the plateau between, the supply ran through independent middlemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The texts name three lands beyond the Lower Sea, and always in that order or its reverse, which reads as a sequence in space: Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dilmun was Bahrain and the Arabian shore beside it, Magan a land of copper in what is now Oman, and Meluhha the Indus civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sargon of Akkad boasted that ships from all three docked at his capital, and a hymn has Enki order the Dilmun boats loaded with timber and the boats of Meluhha to carry gold and silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What came back was copper and other metals, wood, pearls and animals, and the traffic left its mark: Indus seals at Ur, carnelian beads, probably Indian, on the cloak in Queen Puabi's tomb, and Shu-ilishu, whose cylinder seal names him a translator of the Meluhhan language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Trade also went overland, and merchants from Assur carried tin north into central Anatolia early in the 2nd millennium BCE and left their tablets at Kanesh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Holly Pittman, “Highlands and Lowlands,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/highlands-and-lowlands/. [Open access]","Gregory L. Possehl, “The Middle Asian Interaction Sphere,” <i>Expedition</i> 49, no. 1 (2007), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-middle-asian-interaction-sphere/. [Open access]","Maurizio Tosi and Marcello Piperno, “Lithic Technology Behind the Ancient Lapis Lazuli Trade,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 1 (1973), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/lithic-technology-behind-the-ancient-lapis-lazuli-trade/. [Open access]","“Enki and the world order,” <i>The Electronic Text Corpus of Sumerian Literature</i> t.1.1.3, Faculty of Oriental Studies, University of Oxford, https://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/cgi-bin/etcsl.cgi?text=t.1.1.3. [Open access]","Robert Maddin, Tamara Stech Wheeler, and James D. Muhly, “Tin in the Ancient Near East,” <i>Expedition</i> 19, no. 2 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/tin-in-the-ancient-near-east/. [Open access]","Gregory L. Possehl, “Shu-ilishu’s Cylinder Seal,” <i>Expedition</i> 48, no. 1 (2006), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/shu-ilishus-cylinder-seal/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_Shu-ilishu%2C_interpreter_of_the_Meluhhan_language%2C_Louvre_Museum_AO_22310.jpg/1280px-Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_Shu-ilishu%2C_interpreter_of_the_Meluhhan_language%2C_Louvre_Museum_AO_22310.jpg","title":"The cylinder seal of Shu-ilishu","desc":"A rolled impression of the cylinder seal of Shu-ilishu, whose inscription names him a translator of the Meluhhan language. Louvre AO 22310, photographed for the 19th-century catalogue of the Collection de Clercq. Louis de Clercq, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akkadian_cylinder_seal_with_inscription_Shu-ilishu,_interpreter_of_the_Meluhhan_language,_Louvre_Museum_AO_22310.jpg","alt":"A wide black-and-white photograph of a clay seal impression showing a row of standing figures in fringed robes, one carrying a vessel and another an animal, with columns of cuneiform signs at each end"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the southern cities have to import so much?","a":"Because the alluvium gave mud, reeds, bitumen, poor stone and the date palm, and beyond those no metals, no tall hardwood timbers and no hard, colourful stones."},{"q":"Why did textiles become the chief thing the south made?","a":"Because what it did have was water, land and upland pasture for great herds of sheep and goats, whose wool the cities spun and wove — and cloth is what it paid the highlands with."},{"q":"Why did lapis lazuli reach Sumer through middlemen?","a":"Because it came from Afghanistan, more than 1,900 kilometres off, and Sumer controlled neither the source nor the plateau between."}]},
{"id":"wh-198","num":198,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Because their kings replaced the old habit of naming each year after some great event, dating by a ruler's regnal years is one lasting thing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Babylon.","questions":["The carved boundary stone called a kudurru, which put a royal grant of land under the protection of the gods, first appears under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and is what they are best known for.","The conquest of Babylonia was gradual rather than sudden, since the Sea-Country kings in the far south went on ruling independently after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had taken the north."],"answer":"Kassites","answerText":"Kassites","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kassite Nippur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kassites</b> were the people whose kings ruled Babylonia through much of the later 2nd millennium BCE, in a period that ends about 1150.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their conquest was not one blow: it succeeded quickly over much of the country and slowly in the south, where the Sea-Country kings kept their independence, and only after further fighting did the administration centre on Babylon again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They ruled as a caste rather than as a migration, keeping their own names while gradually taking on the civilisation of Babylon, which their coming left largely as it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much about them is still not known, and the administrative tablets dug out of their levels at Nippur are among the few things that speak directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their most durable gift was a way of counting time: in place of the old system, under which each year was known by an elaborate title taken from some great event, they dated by the years of the king's reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their capital was Dur-Kurigalzu near Babylon, where copper oxhide ingots of the kind that circulated all over the Bronze Age Mediterranean have been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Nippur they put up a building resembling their temple of Ningal at Ur, and a Kassite ration document survives dated by the reign of King Kurigalzu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A seal impression from those levels carries stars and lozenges, elements taken as characteristically Kassite, along with foxes, which are not common on seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What they are best remembered for is the <i>kudurru</i>, a conical block of stone engraved with a royal grant of land and guarded by divine emblems and a string of curses; the real title-deed was a sealed clay tablet, and cutting the stone was optional, adding the gods' protection to the owner's rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dynasty's fall is tied to Elamite aggression, and the monuments carried off as spoil lay in the mounds of Susa until a French expedition brought them up again: the stele of Naram-Sin, Hammurabi's laws and a series of Kassite kudurrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["L. W. King, <i>A History of Babylon: From the Foundation of the Monarchy to the Persian Conquest</i> (London: Chatto and Windus, 1915), 214–58, https://archive.org/details/historyofbabylon00kinguoft. [Open access]","McGuire Gibson, “Nippur 1972–1973,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 1 (1973), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/nippur-1972-1973/. [Open access]","McGuire Gibson, “The Twelfth Season at Nippur,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 4 (1974), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-twelfth-season-at-nippur/. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “Archaeometry and Shipwrecks,” <i>Expedition</i> 53, no. 1 (2011), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/archaeometry-and-shipwrecks/. [Open access]","A. John Gwinnett and Leonard Gorelick, “Ancient Lapidary,” <i>Expedition</i> 22, no. 1 (1979), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/ancient-lapidary/. [Open access]","Ann Guinan, Gary Oller, and Dorothy Ormsby, “Nippur Rebaked: The Conservation of Cuneiform Tablets,” <i>Expedition</i> 18, no. 3 (1976), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/nippur-rebaked/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23.jpg","title":"The kudurru of Melishipak","desc":"The upper part of a Kassite boundary stone recording a royal land grant, carrying the star, crescent and sun-disc of Ishtar, Sin and Shamash above a seated deity receiving the king and his daughter. Excavated at Susa by Jacques de Morgan; Louvre Sb 23. Jastrow, released into the. public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kudurru_Melishipak_Louvre_Sb23.jpg","alt":"The rounded top of a dark stone stele carved in relief: an eight-pointed star, a crescent and a rayed sun-disc in a row, and below them a deity seated on a throne facing a standing bearded king who raises one hand and leads a smaller figure by the wrist"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Kassite conquest leave Babylonian civilisation largely as it was?","a":"Because they ruled as a caste rather than as a migration, keeping their own names while gradually taking on the civilisation of the country they governed."},{"q":"Why was their way of dating years a durable change?","a":"Because it replaced a system in which each year was known by an elaborate title taken from some great event with dating by the years of the king's reign."},{"q":"Why was cutting a kudurru optional?","a":"Because the real title-deed was a sealed clay tablet, so the engraved stone added the gods' protection to the owner's rights rather than conveying them."}]},
{"id":"wh-199","num":199,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Although the name comes from a word for highland, and the Babylonians used it for the country east of them, the chief city of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood out in the plain.","questions":["The accounting tablets from Susa are the earliest writing of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they stay undeciphered because number signs and logograms give no idea at all of the language behind them.","Rulers titled kings of Anshan and Susa built the empire that let <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> compete with Assyria and Babylonia, reaching its greatest political and military successes between about 1300 and 1100 BCE."],"answer":"Elam","answerText":"Elam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proto-Elamite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2900 – 2600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Middle Elamite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Susa destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":3,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Elam</b> was the civilisation of what is now south-western Iran; its name comes from a word for highland: the Sumerians wrote of the country NIM and the Babylonians of <i>elamtu</i>, the land east and north-east of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet Susa, its chief city, stood out in the plain, miles from the mountains behind it, on rich fields that helped keep it settled without a break from the 4th millennium BCE to the 12th or 13th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Proto-Elamite, an accounting script, is known from over 1,400 tablets and fragments at Susa and from Tepe Sialk and Tall-i-Ghazir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seal impressions date them to about 2900 to 2600 BCE, they are read from right to left and top to bottom, and their figures are decimal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are still undeciphered, because they seem to be written with number signs and logograms alone, which stand for words rather than sounds, so nothing can be recovered of the language behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Elamite language, written later in Mesopotamian cuneiform, remains largely enigmatic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elamite rule runs from the end of the 3rd millennium BCE to about 640 BCE, when Ashurbanipal's army destroyed Susa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a long stretch rank was a ladder of offices, not a single crown, and the business documents of Susa appear to record the climb: the <i>sukkal</i> of the city rose to <i>sukkal</i> of Elam and Simash, then to <i>sukkalmah</i> at the top, while the bare title of king counted for little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between about 1300 and 1100 BCE a line titled kings of Anshan and Susa built the Middle Elamite empire, when Elam competed for power with Assyria and Babylonia and achieved the greatest political and military successes of its long history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anshan is Tall-i Malyan in Fars, nearly 200 hectares (500 acres) of mounds 46 kilometres (29 miles) north of Shiraz, where inscribed bricks bore out the identification; and one of them, Untash-Napirisha, replaced his first ziggurat at Chogha Zanbil, his new lowland city, with the largest ever built in the ancient Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Edith Porada, “Aspects of Elamite Art and Archaeology,” <i>Expedition</i> 13, no. 3-4 (1971), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/aspects-of-elamite-art-and-archaeology/. [Open access]","Christopher L. Hamlin, “A Proto-Elamite Account Tablet from Susa,” <i>Expedition</i> 10, no. 4 (1968), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-proto-elamite-account-tablet-from-susa/. [Open access]","George G. Cameron, <i>History of Early Iran</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1936), 71–72, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.183377. [Open access]","Elizabeth Carter and Matthew W. Stolper, “Middle Elamite Malayan,” <i>Expedition</i> 18, no. 2 (1976), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/middle-elamite-malayan/. [Open access]","Philip Jones, “An Elamite Inscribed Brick,” <i>Expedition</i> 56, no. 3 (2014), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/an-elamite-inscribed-brick/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Zigorat_Chogha_Zanbil_Temple.jpg/1280px-Zigorat_Chogha_Zanbil_Temple.jpg","title":"The ziggurat at Chogha Zanbil","desc":"The face and central staircase of the ziggurat Untash-Napirisha raised at his new Elamite city, in what is now Khuzestan in south-western Iran; the outer skin is of baked brick over a mud-brick core. Atsed, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zigorat_Chogha_Zanbil_Temple.jpg","alt":"A broad wall of pale mud brick rising in stepped terraces under a clear sky, with a wide flight of brick steps climbing to a narrow doorway at its centre and a paved court in the foreground"},"why":[{"q":"Why is Proto-Elamite still undeciphered?","a":"Because it seems to be written with number signs and logograms alone, which stand for words rather than sounds, so nothing can be recovered of the language behind them."},{"q":"Why does the name of the country sit oddly with its chief city?","a":"Because the name comes from a word for highland, and yet Susa stood out in the plain, miles from the mountains behind it, on rich fields."},{"q":"Why did the bare title of king count for little for a long stretch?","a":"Because rank was a ladder of offices: the business documents of Susa record the climb from sukkal of the city to sukkal of Elam and Simash and then to sukkalmah at the top."}]},
{"id":"wh-200","num":200,"category":"Mesopotamia","question":"Letters 17 to 30 of the Amarna archive are the surviving half of a correspondence between Egypt and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose kings addressed the pharaoh as their brother.","questions":["A vassal treaty found at Hattusa put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Hittite lordship, and the gods called to witness it include four whose names are Indo-European.","The ruling house of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was of Indo-European descent, which shows in royal names like Artatama and Tushratta, in a horse-breeding vocabulary, and in a word for its military aristocracy."],"answer":"Mitanni","answerText":"Mitanni","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Power grows</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amarna Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suppiluliuma I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1355 – 1320 BCE</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mitanni</b> was the kingdom of northern Mesopotamia and Syria that was amassing power by about 1500 BCE and stood with Assyria, Babylon, Egypt and Hatti at the centre of the civilised world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the centuries before it rose, Hurrian names had already spread across the whole northern arc from the Syrian coast to the borders of Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tell al-Rimah in the Sinjar may have lain in a political no-man's-land as the fortunes of Assyria and Mitanni to either side waxed and waned, and its cylinder seals of that date are mostly Mitannian and Syrian in style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hoard of frit from the same site includes a green-glazed cosmetic capsule imitating a Mitannian seal design, two stags either side of a palmette tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ruling class was of Indo-European descent, which shows in royal names such as Artatama and Tushratta, in the horse-breeding terminology of its texts, and in the word <i>mairya-</i> used of its military aristocracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By the Amarna Age, roughly 1400 to 1300 BCE, it had consolidated itself and was taking northern Syria, while Assyria broke free of its rule and made itself a great power in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its letters are numbers 17 to 30 of the archive found at Tell el-Amarna, which survives almost entirely as what Egypt received, not what it sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In it the great kings call one another brother where a vassal writes to my lord, my sun, and Akkadian is the language of negotiation, though letter 24 is Hurrian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tushratta addresses the pharaoh as a son-in-law he loves and signs himself the father-in-law who loves him; the commonest complaint in the archive is Egyptian gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A vassal treaty brought the kingdom under Hittite lordship, Suppiluliuma I recognising Shattiwaza as its future king and Shattiwaza recognising him as his lord; of the gods called to witness it four names are Indo-European, the oldest such list known, and one long read as the god Mitra may instead be a class of treaty gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Theresa Howard Carter, “Early Assyrians in the Sinjar,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/early-assyrians-in-the-sinjar/. [Open access]","Priscila Scoville, “The ancient Near East in contact: an introduction to the Egypt-Mitanni affairs in the Amarna Letters,” <i>Estudos Internacionais</i> 6, no. 2 (2018): 65–78, https://doi.org/10.5752/P.2317-773X.2018v6n2p65. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “The Hittites and the Aegean World,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 2 (1974), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-hittites-and-the-aegean-world/. [Open access]","David Crownover, “Some Frit from Northern Mesopotamia,” <i>Expedition</i> 7, no. 1 (1964), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/some-frit-from-northern-mesopotamia/. [Open access]","Jaan Lahe and Vladimir Sazonov, “Mitra esmamainimine Hetiidi kuninga Šuppiluliuma I ja Mitanni kuninga Šattiwaza lepingus?,” <i>Mäetagused</i> 73 (2019): 5–14, https://doi.org/10.7592/MT2019.73.lahe_sazonov. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amarna_letter._Letter_from_Tushratta_king_of_Mitanni_to_Amenhotep_III._From_Tell_el-Amarna%2C_Egypt._1st_half_of_the_14th_century_BCE._British_Museum.jpg/1280px-Amarna_letter._Letter_from_Tushratta_king_of_Mitanni_to_Amenhotep_III._From_Tell_el-Amarna%2C_Egypt._1st_half_of_the_14th_century_BCE._British_Museum.jpg","title":"A letter from Tushratta","desc":"Tushratta of Mitanni writes to Amenhotep III of Egypt, negotiating a diplomatic marriage and asking for much gold as a bride-price; from Tell el-Amarna, first half of the 14th century BCE, in the British Museum. Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amarna_letter._Letter_from_Tushratta_king_of_Mitanni_to_Amenhotep_III._From_Tell_el-Amarna,_Egypt._1st_half_of_the_14th_century_BCE._British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A thick rectangular clay tablet standing upright in a museum mount, its face covered edge to edge with close-packed lines of cuneiform divided by ruled horizontal bands"},"why":[{"q":"Why is its ruling class described as Indo-European in descent?","a":"Because of royal names such as Artatama and Tushratta, the horse-breeding terminology of its texts, and the word mairya- used of its military aristocracy."},{"q":"Why is the Amarna archive a one-sided source?","a":"Because it survives almost entirely as what Egypt received rather than what it sent, so the correspondence is read from the incoming letters alone."},{"q":"Why does the treaty with Suppiluliuma matter beyond politics?","a":"Because four of the gods called to witness it bear Indo-European names, the oldest such list known — though one long read as Mitra may instead be a class of treaty gods."}]},
{"id":"rm-041","num":"41","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy wrote that the two seas around Italy carry Etruscan names, the Tuscan and the Adriatic, a memory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Roman readers took for granted.","questions":["A wreck off Giens went down about 500 BCE with a thousand Etruscan wine amphorae in six lashed layers, showing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as cargo rather than as the reputation Greek writers gave it.","Hieron of Syracuse sent triremes to Cumae in 474 BCE and humbled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a great sea fight, after which the Tyrrhenian passed steadily into other hands."],"answer":"Etruscan sea power","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Alalia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 – 535 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gravisca texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 520 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand Ribaud F</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 475 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cumae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">474 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan sea power</b> is the shipping, trade and naval force the cities of Etruria exercised over the Tyrrhenian and the Adriatic in the archaic centuries, and the reputation that came with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy took it for granted that before Rome's supremacy Etruscan power reached widely by sea and land, and that Italy's two seas bore Etruscan names, the Tuscan and the Adriatic after the colony of Atria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo supplied the darker half: the Tyrrheni had been strong under one ruler and took to the sea as pirates only once that union dissolved, though acting together they could make long expeditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One fleet action is securely attested, the ships of Caere fighting beside the Carthaginians against Phocaean Greeks off Alalia in Corsica between 540 and 535 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Diodorus still calls the Tyrrhenians masters of the sea in 474 BCE, in the sentence describing the triremes Hieron of Syracuse sent to Cumae, which humbled them in a great sea fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> How much of that tradition holds is open, since the piracy was a reputation spread and orchestrated by writers in Greek, and whether the ships amounted to a thalassocracy is asked rather than settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ports are not in doubt: Pyrgi and Gravisca served Caere and Tarquinia on the Tyrrhenian coast, Spina and Adria stood on the Adriatic, and Genoa held a resident Etruscan-speaking community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sanctuary at Gravisca has yielded 119 Greek inscriptions, most from about 550 to 520 BCE, one of them a dedication to Apollo cut into a stone anchor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A century before Marseille was founded, Etruscan merchants were already working the coasts of Provence and Languedoc, and it is to them that the arrival of wine in Gaul is credited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One cargo shows the scale: the wreck at Grand Ribaud off Giens went down about 500 to 475 BCE with about a thousand Etruscan wine amphorae lashed in five or six layers, some 40 tonnes (44 tons), though whether Etruscans or Greeks from Marseille sailed it is argued still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan sea power","tags":["concept","history","archaeology","italy","etruscan","iron age","warfare"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.2.2, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5B*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.51, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 4 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/11C*.html. [Open access]","Jean-René Jannot, \"Les navires étrusques, instruments d'une thalassocratie ?,\" <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 139, no. 3 (1995): 743–78, https://www.persee.fr/doc/crai_0065-0536_1995_num_139_3_15514. [Open access]","Stéphane Bourdin, \"Fréquentation ou intégration : les présences allogènes dans les emporia étrusques et ligures (VIe–IVe siècles av. J.-C.),\" in <i>Espaces d'échanges en Méditerranée : Antiquité et Moyen Âge</i>, ed. François Clément, John Tolan and Jérôme Wilgaux (Rennes: Presses universitaires de Rennes, 2006), 19–39, https://books.openedition.org/pur/7832. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8546786. [Open access]","Luc Long, Lucien-François Gantés and Michel Rival, \"L'épave Grand Ribaud F : un chargement de produits étrusques du début du Ve siècle avant J.-C.,\" in <i>Gli Etruschi da Genova ad Ampurias: Atti del XXIV Convegno di Studi Etruschi ed Italici, Marseille–Lattes, 26 settembre – 1 ottobre 2002</i>, vol. 2 (Pisa: Istituti editoriali e poligrafici internazionali, 2006), 455–95, https://www.studietruschi.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/ATTISE24_28_LONG-ET-ALII.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Etruscan_Ship.JPG/1920px-Etruscan_Ship.JPG","title":"Etruscan sea power","desc":"Tomb of the Ship, Tarquinia (detail). Chiar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etruscan_Ship.JPG","alt":"Painted ships with masts, rigging and dark hulls on a wall of the Tomb of the Ship at Tarquinia, about 450 BCE"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why is the reputation for piracy read as a Greek construction?","a":"Because it was a reputation spread and orchestrated by writers in Greek, and Strabo has these people turn pirate only once their union dissolved, acting together on long expeditions before."},{"q":"Why is one anchor at Gravisca worth more than its size suggests?","a":"Because it is inscribed as a dedication to Apollo, among 119 Greek inscriptions from the sanctuary, most of about 550 to 520 BCE."},{"q":"Why does a wreck off Giens not settle who was carrying the wine?","a":"Because it went down with about a thousand Etruscan amphorae in five or six layers, and whether Etruscans or Greeks from Marseille sailed it is argued still."}]},
{"id":"rm-042","num":"42","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Herodotus says the Phocaeans won the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yet lost forty of their sixty ships, a Cadmean victory that cost them Corsica and sent them to found Velia instead.","questions":["The men of Agylla stoned their share of the prisoners taken at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and were told by the Pythia to honour the dead with rites and horse races.","An archaic temple of Athena at Velia has given up two helmets that its excavators read as spoils dedicated after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of them of Italic Negau type."],"answer":"Battle of Alalia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 541 – 535 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Velia founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athena temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">540 – 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Etruscan Aléria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Alalia</b> is the sea fight of about 540 BCE in which a Phocaean fleet based on Corsica met an allied Etruscan and Carthaginian one, and Caere’s part in it is among the best attested facts of archaic Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells it: Phocaeans fleeing the Persian conquest of their city sailed for Cyrnus, where at an oracle’s command they had founded Alalia twenty years earlier, and stayed five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They plundered all their neighbours, so the Tyrrhenians and Carthaginians made common cause and came at them with sixty ships each, against the Phocaeans’ sixty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the sea he calls Sardonian the Phocaeans won, yet it was a Cadmean victory: forty ships lost, and the twenty left useless with their rams twisted awry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So they took their families off Alalia and sailed to Rhegium, and later founded Hyele, now Velia, the Cyrnus the oracle meant being a hero and not the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The captured crews were divided by lot and the men of Agylla — Caere — stoned theirs; when everything passing the spot was crippled in turn, the Pythia told them to honour the dead with rites and horse races, kept up in Herodotus’s day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He never calls the battle Alalia, which is the colony’s name rather than the fight’s, and the place is unknown; the tradition rests on seven passages, not on his account alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle later cites the Etruscans and Carthaginians as peoples bound to one another by commercial agreements, the footing such an alliance stood on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Corsica passed to Etruria: the burial ground at Aléria, in use from the late 6th century BCE, is the only necropolis of Etruscan character recorded in France, its Etruscan occupation put at about 540 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Velia a temple of Athena of 540–530 BCE has given up painted pottery, vases inscribed ‘sacred’ and arms and armour, among them a Chalcidian helmet and one of Italic Negau type, which the excavators take for offerings after the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of Alalia","tags":["event","history","italy","etruscan","iron age","warfare","greece"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.166–67, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D166. [Open access]","Michel Gras, \"Marseille, la bataille d'Alalia et Delphes,\" <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 13 (1987): 161–81, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_1987_num_13_1_1755. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8546786. [Open access]","Federica Sacchetti et al., \"Aléria – Les nécropoles : analyse spatiale d'une occupation diachronique,\" <i>ADLFI. Archéologie de la France – Informations</i>, Corse (2024), https://journals.openedition.org/adlfi/155947. [Open access]","Ministero della Cultura, \"MiC, archeologia: rinvenuti i resti del tempio arcaico di Atena sull'acropoli di Velia,\" comunicato stampa, 1 February 2022, https://cultura.gov.it/comunicato/22137. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1280a, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D3%3Asection%3D1280a. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/30/Battle_of_Alalia_%28Hutchinson%27s_story_of_the_nations%29.jpg","title":"Battle of Alalia","desc":"The battle as imagined by Ambrose Dudley for <i>Hutchinson's Story of the Nations</i> in 1915; no ancient depiction of it survives. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Alalia_(Hutchinson%27s_story_of_the_nations).jpg","alt":"A 1915 painting of the sea fight off Alalia: a beaked galley grappled against a larger ship, its decks crowded with armed men"},"difficulty":4,"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus call the Phocaean win a Cadmean victory?","a":"Because they lost forty ships of sixty and the twenty left were useless with their rams twisted awry, so they abandoned the colony and sailed away."},{"q":"Why did Caere have to institute games for the men it killed?","a":"Because everything passing the spot where the prisoners were stoned was crippled in turn, and the Pythia told them to honour the dead with rites and horse races."},{"q":"Why is the battle's usual name not the one its earliest source uses?","a":"Because Alalia is the colony's name rather than the fight's, the place is unknown, and Herodotus calls the water the Sardonian sea."}]},
{"id":"rm-043","num":"43","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Strabo says the Greeks grew so strong that they gave the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to this part of Italy together with Sicily, though by his own day nearly all of it had been barbarised.","questions":["Polybius calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Greater Hellas, and says that when the club-houses of the Pythagoreans were burnt the leading men of every city there perished at once.","Settlement in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened at Pithekoussai in the mid-8th century BCE and closed with the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii in 444 to 443 BCE, some three centuries later."],"answer":"Magna Graecia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 443 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taras leads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 385 – 355 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Magna Graecia</b>, in Greek <i>Megale Hellas</i>, is the name for the Greek cities planted along the coasts of southern Italy from the middle of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Settlement opened at Pithekoussai on Ischia, then Cumae, and a flood followed: Sybaris and Croton from Achaia at the end of the century, Metapontion and Poseidonia from Sybaris, Elea from Phocaea in 540 BCE, and last the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii in 444 to 443 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once they prospered the settlers raised some of the largest temples in the Greek world, in a country whose own peoples were often unfriendly and pressing outwards themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo says the Greeks grew so strong that they gave this part of Italy, together with Sicily, the name Magna Graecia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius calls it Greater Hellas and shows a working political world: when the club-houses of the Pythagoreans were burnt the leading men of every city perished at a stroke and sedition ran through all the Greek towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was the Achaeans who were trusted to settle it, and Croton, Sybaris and Caulonia then made a league with a common temple of Zeus Amarius and took the laws of Achaia, until Dionysius of Syracuse and their neighbours forced them to give it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A later league was led by Taras under Archytas, who is said to have been elected general seven times between the mid-380s and the mid-350s BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name itself was elastic: it once took in the whole Greek world, and shrank until in the Roman period it meant the Greek-inhabited parts of Italy from Cumae to Tarentum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How far its cities felt themselves one region is doubtful, and local quarrels and Italian neighbours weighed more with them than Rome until Roman consolidation of the south after 320 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strabo’s verdict is the end of it: by his day everything but Taras, Rhegium and Neapolis had been barbarised, held by Lucanians, Bruttians and Campanians who were Campanian in name and Roman in fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Magna Graecia","tags":["place","region","history","greece","italy","geography","migration"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 6.1.2, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/6A*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.39, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]","William Owens, review of <i>Rome and the Western Greeks, 350 BC–AD 200: Conquest and Acculturation in Southern Italy</i>, by Kathryn Lomas, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1995.03.12, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1995/1995.03.12/. [Open access]","John Dillon, review of <i>Archytas of Tarentum: Pythagorean, Philosopher and Mathematician King</i>, by Carl A. Huffman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.03.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.34/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG/1920px-Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","title":"Magna Graecia","desc":"The Temple of Concordia at Agrigento, the Greek Akragas, which the ancient name took in along with the rest of Sicily. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrigento-Tempio_della_Concordia01.JPG","alt":"A Doric temple of honey-coloured stone standing complete on a ridge, its columns and pediment intact"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why is the name an elastic one?","a":"Because it once took in the whole Greek world and shrank until in the Roman period it meant the Greek-inhabited parts of Italy from Cumae to Tarentum."},{"q":"Why does the burning of the Pythagorean club-houses matter politically?","a":"Because the leading men of every city perished at a stroke, and sedition then ran through all the Greek towns."},{"q":"Why is it doubtful that these cities felt themselves one region?","a":"Because local quarrels and Italian neighbours weighed more with them than Rome did until Roman consolidation of the south after 320 BCE."}]},
{"id":"rm-044","num":"44","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says the Aricians broke at the first charge until cohorts from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wheeled on the Etruscan rear, and its tyrant later sheltered Tarquinius Superbus until his death.","questions":["Strabo calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the oldest of all the Sicilian and Italiote cities, founded by men who agreed it should count as a colony of Chalcis while bearing another town's name.","Tyrrhenians, Umbrians and Daunians marched on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 524 BCE and were beaten, a defeat that helped erode the Etruscan hold on southern Italy."],"answer":"Cumae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attack repulsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">524 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aricia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 504 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tarquin dies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">495 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cumae</b> is the oldest Greek city on the Italian mainland, in the ground no later than 730 to 720 BCE and settled from Pithekoussai, the island off the Bay of Naples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Strabo calls it the oldest of all the Sicilian and Italiote cities, led out by Hippocles of Cumae and Megasthenes of Chalcis, who agreed it should be a colony of Chalcis under the other's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius gives a different pair of founders, Eretrians and Chalcidians, and a city celebrated throughout Italy for its wealth, holding the best of the Campanian plain and the havens around Misenum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Sibyl prophesied there, and a long gallery cut through the tuff below the acropolis has long been shown as her grotto, though as it stands it began as a covered way for troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Rome's share in that oracle is the Sibylline Books, which Dionysius says a foreign woman sold to Tarquinius, three of nine after she had burnt the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 524 BCE Tyrrhenians, Umbrians and Daunians marched on the city envying its prosperity and were beaten, a defeat which with the sea fight off the same coast in 474 BCE eroded the Etruscan presence in southern Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Twenty years later the Aricians begged help against Aruns, son of Porsena, and Livy says the Cuman cohorts let the Etruscans press past them in pursuit, wheeled on their rear and cut them to pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius names the commander Aristodemus, sent out with the poorest citizens in ten unseaworthy ships by aristocrats who hoped he would die, and who killed the Tyrrhenian general himself and came home to seize the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tarquinius Superbus died there in 495 BCE, having gone to that tyrant once the Latins were broken, and Roman envoys buying corn in a famine lost their money and pack animals to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Campanians afterwards became masters of the place and used its people badly, yet Strabo still found many traces of Greek decorum and usage preserved there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cumae","tags":["city","history","italy","greece","iron age","religion","warfare"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.4.4, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5D*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 7.2–12, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 4 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1943), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/7A*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.62, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8546786. [Open access]","James Evans, review of <i>The Greek Cities of Magna Graecia and Sicily</i>, by Luca Cerchiai, Lorena Jannelli and Fausto Longo, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.04.56, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.04.56/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Cuma_-_Antro_della_Sibilla.JPG/1920px-Cuma_-_Antro_della_Sibilla.JPG","title":"Cumae","desc":"The approach to the Antro della Sibilla, Parco archeologico di Cuma. DanieleDaniele1, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuma_-_Antro_della_Sibilla.JPG","alt":"A deep passage cut straight through pale tuff rock at Cumae, ending at the dark trapezoidal mouth of the Sibyl's gallery"},"difficulty":3,"locator":{"name":"Cumae","at":[14.0536,40.8486]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Sibyl's grotto not quite what it is shown as?","a":"Because the long gallery cut through the tuff below the acropolis, as it stands, began as a covered way for troops."},{"q":"Why did the victory of 474 BCE matter beyond the city?","a":"Because with the defeat of 524 it eroded the Etruscan presence in southern Italy, the sea fight off that coast humbling a power Diodorus still called master of the sea."},{"q":"Why did sending a commander out with the poorest citizens backfire?","a":"Because the aristocrats who gave Aristodemus ten unseaworthy ships hoped he would die, and he killed the Tyrrhenian general and came home to seize the city."}]},
{"id":"rm-045","num":"45","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy's twelve lictors, the curule chair and the toga praetexta are the plainest signs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the number twelve coming from the custom of twelve sovereign cities.","questions":["Workmen summoned from all Etruria to finish the Capitoline temple, and soothsayers called in to read public prodigies, are cases of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Livy states outright.","The tradition that Roman boys once learnt Etruscan literature as they later learnt Greek is the boldest claim made for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Livy half doubts it himself."],"answer":"Etruscan influence on Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Veii stormed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caere absorbed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Etruria and Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 89 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Etruscan influence on Rome</b> is the debt the Romans acknowledged to their northern neighbours in religion, insignia, building and letters, together with the question of how deep it really went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Etruria began just across the Tiber, its heartland roughly between Rome and Florence, so the two were neighbours long before either was a power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome's own tradition brought its Tarquin kings from Tarquinii, the first of them a rich man who left because, as the son of a Corinthian refugee, he could rise no higher among Etruscans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Livy derives the twelve lictors, the curule chair and the toga praetexta from the Etruscans, and the number twelve from the custom by which their twelve sovereign cities each gave one lictor to a jointly elected king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tarquinius Superbus sent for workmen from every part of Etruria to finish the temple of Jupiter on the Capitol, and it was Etruscan soothsayers whom Rome employed to read prodigies affecting the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Latin alphabet itself was taken over from the Greek through Etruscan, which is why its 23 letters run in much the same order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy also reports, and half doubts, a tradition that Roman boys were once taught Etruscan literature as they were taught Greek in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How far all this made Rome an Etruscan city is disputed,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the Roman picture of these neighbours is itself suspect, since Greek and Roman writers dwell on their wealth and their decadence, distortions aimed less at Etruscan life than at their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The traffic then reversed: Rome stormed Veii in 396 BCE, and Caere was absorbed to become the first people remembered as Roman citizens without the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Etruria was taken piece by piece thereafter, by roads, colonies and alliances with its ruling families,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and its sanctuaries went on being used through the expansion that ended its independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Etruscan influence on Rome","tags":["concept","history","italy","etruscan","iron age","religion","language"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Winfred P. Lehmann and Jonathan Slocum, \"Latin Online: Series Introduction,\" Linguistics Research Center, University of Texas at Austin, https://lrc.la.utexas.edu/eieol/latol. [Open access]","Charlotte R. Potts and Christopher J. Smith, \"The Etruscans: Setting New Agendas,\" <i>Journal of Archaeological Research</i> 30 (2022): 597–644, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10814-021-09169-x. [Open access]","Valeria Riedemann Lorca, review of <i>A Companion to the Etruscans</i>, ed. Sinclair Bell and Alexandra A. Carpino, Blackwell Companions to the Ancient World, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2018.04.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.04.41/. [Open access]","Michel Humbert, \"L'incorporation de Caere dans la civitas Romana,\" <i>Mélanges de l'École française de Rome</i> 84, no. 1 (1972): 231–68, https://doi.org/10.3406/mefr.1972.923. [Open access]","Francesca Boitani and Iefke van Kampen, \"Veio,\" <i>Bibliografia topografica della colonizzazione greca in Italia e nelle isole tirreniche</i> 21 (2012): 553–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/btcgi_0000-0009_2012_num_21_1_4754. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg/1920px-Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg","title":"Etruscan influence on Rome","desc":"Relief from Iulia Concordia showing three lictors with their fasces, Museo Nazionale Concordiese, Portogruaro. Following Hadrian, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors,_National_Museum_of_Concordia,_Portogruaro,_Italy.jpg","alt":"A limestone relief of three men in togas walking abreast, each carrying a bundle of rods over one shoulder"},"difficulty":3,"why":[{"q":"Why does the Latin alphabet run in the Greek order?","a":"Because it was taken over from the Greek through Etruscan, which is why its 23 letters keep much the same sequence."},{"q":"Why is the Roman picture of these neighbours itself suspect?","a":"Because Greek and Roman writers dwell on their wealth and their decadence, distortions aimed less at Etruscan life than at their own."},{"q":"Why does Livy trace the twelve lictors to Etruria?","a":"Because he derives the number from the custom by which twelve sovereign cities each gave one lictor to a jointly elected king."}]},
{"id":"rm-046","num":"46","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy counted 244 years from the founding of the city to its liberation, the whole span of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when two consuls replaced its last king.","questions":["After the last king went, a priest called the king for sacrifices took over the religious duties of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was placed under the Pontifex Maximus.","Romans did not begin writing their own history until about 200 BCE, so the whole account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a reconstruction made centuries after it ended."],"answer":"Roman Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditionally</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 – 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regal years</span><span class=\"dt-v\">244, by Livy's count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Forum levelled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman Kingdom</b> is the period Rome put at the start of its own history, running from the founding of the city to the expulsion of its last king, which Livy reckoned at 244 years in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The throne was remembered as elective rather than hereditary: when Romulus died the hundred senators held power five days each in rotation, a year they called the interregnum, after which the people chose a king and the senate ratified the choice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Romulus is credited with those first hundred senators, the Patres whose descendants were the patricians,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Servius Tullius with grading the citizens by property into classes and centuries, the first class alone counting eighty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has the last king reign 25 years and be driven out, after which two consuls were elected in the very assembly of centuries Servius was said to have made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the kings had done in religion passed to a king for sacrifices, placed under the Pontifex Maximus so that he should be king in nothing but name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> How much of that is history is a real question, since Romans did not begin writing their own until about 200 BCE and the regal narrative mixes fact and fiction to an uncertain degree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On that reading the tradition grows gradually more reliable only from the middle of the 4th century BCE, long after the last king is supposed to have gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Excavation gives the same centuries another shape: the marshy Forum valley was filled and levelled in the 7th century BCE, the first move to bind the hills around a common space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> From about that time come the first Regia, the huts of the burial ground beside it, and the monumental cult building by the river at Sant’Omobono.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The temple of Jupiter on the Capitol went up at the end of the period, on a scale that invites comparison with the great sanctuaries of Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Roman Kingdom","tags":["era","history","italy","politics","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.17, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Filippo De Rossi, review of <i>A Critical History of Early Rome: From Prehistory to the First Punic War</i>, by Gary Forsythe, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2005.08.40, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2005/2005.08.40/. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg/1920px-C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","title":"Roman Kingdom","desc":"Silver denarius of C. Marcius Censorinus, its obverse showing the jugate heads of Numa Pompilius and Ancus Marcius. Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","alt":"Both faces of a worn silver coin: two overlapping bearded heads facing right, and a rider standing across two galloping horses"},"difficulty":2,"why":[{"q":"Why did the kingship not pass from father to son?","a":"Because it was remembered as elective: on a king's death the hundred senators held power five days each in rotation, and then the people chose and the senate ratified."},{"q":"Why is the tradition about this period hard to use as history?","a":"Because Romans did not begin writing their own history until about 200 BCE, and the regal narrative mixes fact and fiction to an uncertain degree."},{"q":"Why does the Forum valley matter more than the king list?","a":"Because filling and levelling that marshy ground in the 7th century BCE was the first move to bind the hills around a common space."}]},
{"id":"rm-048","num":"48","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Strabo has the first founders wall three of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Ancus take in two more, and Servius add the last two behind a trench, a mound and a wall.","questions":["Livy puts the earliest Romans on one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Sabines with their citadel on another, the Albans on a third, and newcomers on a fourth.","Rome kept a festival called the Septimontium whose own seven places were not the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a sign that the familiar list came later than the ground."],"answer":"Seven Hills of Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Forum levelled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Seven and wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 525 BCE, on one reading</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Seven Hills of Rome</b> are the Palatine, Capitoline, Aventine, Caelian, Esquiline, Viminal and Quirinal, the high ground the city grew across on the left bank of the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has them taken up by different people: the earliest Romans on the Palatine, the Sabines on the Capitoline with its citadel, the Albans on the Caelian, and the Aventine handed to newcomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancus is said to have joined the Aventine to the Palatine by settling the ground round the altar of Venus Murcia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also brought the Janiculum across the river inside the city's bounds, not because the room was wanted but to keep an enemy off so strong a position, and reached it by the first bridge thrown over the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Servius added the Quirinal and the Viminal, then the Esquiline, where he lived himself to give the place importance, and ringed the whole with mound, moat and wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo describes that work: a deep trench, its earth heaped on the inner side, a mound some six stadia long, and a wall with towers running from the Colline Gate to the Esquiline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What Servius is said to have extended was the pomerium, the strip the Etruscans consecrated by augury and marked with boundary stones when they founded a city, rather than the wall alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The census that prompted the enlargement counted 80,000 men who could bear arms, on the authority of the oldest Roman historian Livy knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The seven as a fixed list came later than the ground itself, and Rome kept a festival called the Septimontium whose own seven were places rather than these hills, on a list handed down under the name of Antistius Labeo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One reconstruction puts the traditional seven and their wall in the third quarter of the 6th century BCE and reads that festival as the memory of it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while the marshy Forum valley between the hills had been filled and levelled a century earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Seven Hills of Rome","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.7, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Linda Zollschan, review of <i>From “Roma quadrata” to “la grande Roma dei Tarquini”: A Study of the Literary Tradition on Rome's Territorial Growth under the Kings</i>, by Adam Ziółkowski, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.04.36, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.04.36/. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"locator":{"name":"seven hills of Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"kind":"region","within":"Rome","area":[[12.474,41.884],[12.478,41.897],[12.487,41.906],[12.503,41.904],[12.508,41.893],[12.504,41.88],[12.491,41.876],[12.479,41.878]]},"why":[{"q":"Why was the Janiculum brought inside the city's bounds?","a":"Because the room was not wanted; it was taken to keep an enemy off so strong a position, and reached by the first bridge thrown over the Tiber."},{"q":"Why is the pomerium not the same thing as the wall?","a":"Because it is the strip the Etruscans consecrated by augury and marked with boundary stones when founding a city, and it is that which Servius is said to have extended."},{"q":"Why is the list of seven later than the ground it names?","a":"Because Rome kept a festival called the Septimontium whose own seven were places rather than these hills, on a list handed down under another name."}]},
{"id":"rm-049","num":"49","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has Romulus take the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as his station for the augury while Remus took the Aventine, and fortify it first of all once he was sole ruler.","questions":["The Lupercalia was run on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name Livy traces to an Arcadian city called Pallantium and whose cliffs and marshy valleys made it a stronghold.","Because the emperors lived on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its Latin name passed first to their own residence and afterwards to any palace at all."],"answer":"Palatine Hill","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wall remains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">perhaps 378 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Palatine Hill</b> is the centremost of Rome's seven, an irregular quadrilateral about 2 kilometres (1.2 miles) round whose highest point stands 43 metres (141 feet) above the level of the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is flat-topped, with two summits called the Palatium and the Cermalus, ringed by cliffs far more formidable than they now look and almost surrounded by marshy valleys traversed by winding streams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only the narrow ridge of the Velia joined it to the Esquiline, so it was a position of great natural strength commanding the one permanent crossing of the lower Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient tradition is unanimous in putting the earliest nucleus of Rome here, and modern scholars have generally agreed with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy has Romulus take the hill as his station for the augury while Remus took the Aventine, and says his first work as sole ruler was to fortify the Palatine, where he had been brought up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy also reports that the hill was first called Pallantium, after a city of that name in Arcadia, and that the name afterwards became Palatium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lupercalia was celebrated there, a festival he traces to the Arcadian Evander, in which young men ran about naked in honour of the Lycaean Pan, whom the Romans called Inuus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo counts the Palatine among the three hills the first founders walled, though the surviving defences are later: a 6th-century BCE stretch at the north-west corner, and one on the west and south known as the wall of Romulus, perhaps a fort of 378 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As the hill came into favour it filled with the houses of the aristocracy, the oldest on record built in 330 BCE, and every sanctuary on it that can be placed belongs to the later republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Augustus lived on it and his successors built palaces across it, and because they did the hill's own name passed first to the imperial residence and then to any palace at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Palatine Hill","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.5, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Palatinus,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 374–80, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Palatinus.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.7, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Circus_Maximus_et_Domus_Augustana_-_Rome_111001.JPG/1280px-Circus_Maximus_et_Domus_Augustana_-_Rome_111001.JPG","title":"Palatine Hill","desc":"Central part of the Circus Maximus and ruins of the Domus Augustana on Palatine Hill. Jean-Pol GRANDMONT, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Circus_Maximus_et_Domus_Augustana_-_Rome_111001.JPG","alt":"A long grassed hollow in the foreground and a hill rising behind it, its crest carrying tiers of ruined brick arches and vaults"},"locator":{"name":"Palatine Hill","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was this the hill the earliest settlement stood on?","a":"Because it was flat-topped and ringed by cliffs, almost surrounded by marshy valleys and joined to the Esquiline only by a narrow ridge, commanding the one permanent crossing of the lower Tiber."},{"q":"Why does the word for the hill now mean any grand residence?","a":"Because Augustus lived on it and his successors built palaces across it, so its name passed first to the imperial residence and then to any palace at all."},{"q":"Why do its surviving defences not support the founding story?","a":"Because the walls that remain are later — a 6th-century BCE stretch at the north-west corner, and one called the wall of Romulus that may be a fort of 378 BCE."}]},
{"id":"rm-050","num":"50","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says a human head came to light in the foundations of Jupiter's temple on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the soothsayers read as portending the head of all the world.","questions":["Geese sacred to Juno are said to have saved the citadel on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the Gauls climbed its cliff by night without waking the sentries or the dogs.","Smallest of Rome's hills, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the citadel on one summit and the temple of Jupiter, Juno and Minerva on the other, with the Asylum in the dip between."],"answer":"Capitoline Hill","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gauls on the Arx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">390 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Capitoline Hill</b> is the smallest of Rome's hills, some 460 metres (500 yards) long and 180 metres (200 yards) wide on average, lying between the Forum and the Campus Martius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Steep cliffs ring it on every side but the south-east, where it was reached from the forum valley, and it falls into three parts: a summit at either end and a dip between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern summit held the Arx, the citadel; on the southern, Tarquin established the worship of Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, which marked that point as the religious centre of the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That dip was called the Asylum, on the story that Romulus received there the refugees of other towns, and the hill's official name was <i>Arx et Capitolium</i>, for its double nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only later did Capitolium spread to the whole hill, and the word could also stand for the temple of Jupiter alone, as its most significant part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Livy reports that while the foundations were dug a human head came to light with the face perfect, which soothsayers from the city and from Etruria read as portending that the spot would be the head of all the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman antiquarians derived the name Capitolium from <i>caput</i>, 'head', on the strength of that story, though the hill was also called Tarpeius and Saturnius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The precipice at its south-west corner, from which those convicted of capital crimes were thrown, was the <i>saxum Tarpeium</i>, where Livy has a commander's daughter open the citadel to the Sabines and die crushed beneath their shields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same rock carries his account of the Gallic attack of 390 BCE, when the enemy climbed it at night without waking the sentries or the dogs and were betrayed by the geese sacred to Juno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temple was dedicated in the first year after the kings were driven out, by a consul who finished the prayer with his hand on the door-post after being told his son was dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Capitoline Hill","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","religion","archaeology"],"sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Capitolinus Mons,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 95–98, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Capitolinus.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.47, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Parte_della_Rupe_Tarpea_-_Li_Monumenti_Pi%C3%BA_Celebri_Di_Roma_Antica_E_Moderna_Disegnati_Da_Vero_Ed_Incisi_Dai_Migliori_Artisti_Rome_Presso_Po_-_1850.jpg","title":"Capitoline Hill","desc":"Parte della Rupe Tarpea, from Li monumenti piú celebri di Roma antica e moderna, 1850. Pietro Datri, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parte_della_Rupe_Tarpea_-_Li_Monumenti_Pi%C3%BA_Celebri_Di_Roma_Antica_E_Moderna_Disegnati_Da_Vero_Ed_Incisi_Dai_Migliori_Artisti_Rome_Presso_Po_-_1850.jpg","alt":"An engraving of a high rock face with houses standing along its crest and two figures walking on the road below"},"locator":{"name":"Capitoline Hill","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the hill's official name a double one?","a":"Because the northern summit held the citadel and the southern the temple of Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, so Arx et Capitolium named its two natures."},{"q":"Why did Romans derive the name from the word for a head?","a":"Because Livy reports that a human head with the face perfect came to light while the temple foundations were dug, and soothsayers read it as portending that the spot would be the head of all the world."},{"q":"Why did the geese matter more than the sentries in 390 BCE?","a":"Because the Gauls climbed the rock at night without waking either the sentries or the dogs, and it was the geese sacred to Juno that gave them away."}]},
{"id":"gr-306","num":306,"category":"Athens","question":"Seven hundred Athenian households were banished as accursed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on a list of names handed to Cleomenes by Isagoras, who had been on the losing side of the faction-strife.","questions":["One expedition of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke up at Eleusis when the Corinthians concluded they were acting wrongly and went home, and the other king Demaratus followed them.","So little came of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Sparta afterwards summoned Hippias from Sigeum and proposed restoring him, only for Socles of Corinth to talk the allies out of it."],"answer":"Spartan intervention at Athens","answerText":"Spartan intervention at Athens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Expelled Hippias</span><span class=\"dt-v\">510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Backed Isagoras</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Spartan intervention at Athens</b> was the run of expeditions Sparta sent into Attica after helping to expel the tyrants, part of the four Dorian comings Herodotus counts, twice as invaders and twice as helpers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Isagoras, on the losing side of the faction-strife with Cleisthenes, invited the aid of Cleomenes, who had been his friend since the besieging of the Peisistratidae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Cleomenes first sent a herald demanding the banishment of Cleisthenes and many others as the Accursed, at Isagoras's instruction, since the guilt was held to lie with the Alcmaeonids and not with his friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleisthenes secretly departed, and Cleomenes appeared with no great force and banished seven hundred Athenian households named for him by Isagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then tried to dissolve the Council and hand the offices of government to Isagoras's faction, which the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> numbers at three hundred friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> <br><br> The Council resisted, Cleomenes and his partisans seized the acropolis, and the rest of the Athenians united and besieged them for two days, the Lacedaemonians leaving under truce on the third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> The prisoners left behind were put to death, the Athenians brought back Cleisthenes and the seven hundred households, and envoys were sent to Sardis who gave Darius earth and water and were blamed for it at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cleomenes mustered an army from the whole Peloponnesus without declaring its purpose, which was to set Isagoras up as tyrant, and broke in as far as Eleusis while the Boeotians took Oenoe and Hysiae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It broke up before any battle when the Corinthians decided they were acting wrongly and left and Demaratus, the other king, did the same, after which Sparta forbade both kings to go out with one army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later the Lacedaemonians sent for Hippias from Sigeum and asked their allies to help restore him, but Socles of Corinth spoke against it and the allies entreated them not to harm a Greek city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.70, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D70. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.73, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.74, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D74. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.75, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D75. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.76, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D76. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.91, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D91. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.93, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D93. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 20, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the charge of being Accursed fall only on one side?","a":"Because the guilt was held to lie with the Alcmaeonids, so demanding the banishment of the Accursed removed Cleisthenes and his people and left Isagoras's friends untouched."},{"q":"Why did Cleomenes fail to hand the government to Isagoras?","a":"Because the Council resisted the dissolution, and when he and his partisans seized the acropolis the rest of the Athenians united and besieged them until they left under truce on the third day."},{"q":"Why were the two Spartan kings forbidden to campaign together?","a":"Because the army mustered against Athens broke up before any battle when the Corinthians decided they were acting wrongly and left, and Demaratus, the other king, did the same."}]},
{"id":"gr-307","num":307,"category":"Athens","question":"Herodotus cannot give the lineage of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, saying only that the man's house was notable and that his kinsfolk sacrifice to Zeus of Caria.","questions":["It was on the instruction of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Cleomenes demanded the banishment of the Accursed, a charge that fell on the Alcmaeonids and spared his own friends.","Winning in the Comradeships only to see his rival take the commons into his party, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was archon in the very year the ten tribes were made."],"answer":"Isagoras","answerText":"Isagoras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Archon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Isagoras</b> son of Tisandrus was, with Cleisthenes the Alcmaeonid, one of the two principal holders of power at Athens once the tyrants had been removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls him a man of a notable house but says he cannot give his lineage, remarking only that his kinsfolk sacrifice to Zeus of Caria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> is blunter about where he stood, calling him a friend of the tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the faction-strife that followed he had the better of it among the Comradeships, and Cleisthenes answered by enlisting the people and offering to hand the government over to the multitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once his rival had drawn in the Athenian people, which was then debarred from all rights, Herodotus says he was stronger by far than the opposing faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Isagoras was nonetheless eponymous archon in the year the reforms were carried, which the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts in the fourth year after the deposition of the tyrants, in 508/507 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Losing power, he devised a counter-plot and invited the aid of Cleomenes, a friend since the besieging of the Peisistratidae, of whom it was said that he regularly went to see Isagoras's wife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At his instruction the Spartan demanded the banishment of Cleisthenes and many other Athenians as the Accursed, a charge that fell on the Alcmaeonids and left his own friends untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seven hundred households were then banished on a list he supplied, and the Council was to be dissolved and the offices of government handed to him and three hundred of his partisans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Council resisted, the citizens besieged the acropolis for two days, and he came out under truce with Cleomenes while those left behind were put to death, the army mustered afterwards having as its undeclared purpose to set him up as tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.70, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D70. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.73, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.74, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D74. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 20, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Isagoras lose a contest he had been winning?","a":"Because he had the better of it among the Comradeships, and Cleisthenes answered by enlisting the people, then debarred from all rights, which made him stronger by far."},{"q":"Why did he call in a Spartan king against his own city?","a":"Because he had lost power and devised a counter-plot, inviting the aid of Cleomenes, a friend since the besieging of the Peisistratidae."},{"q":"Why was the army raised after his flight still his affair?","a":"Because Cleomenes mustered it from the whole Peloponnesus without declaring its purpose, which was to set him up as tyrant of Athens."}]},
{"id":"gr-308","num":308,"category":"Athens","question":"Herodotus says that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named none of the ten new tribes after the sons of Ion, and added Aias only because the hero was a neighbour and an ally.","questions":["Demanded as one of the Accursed and secretly departing, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was sent for again by the Athenians once Cleomenes had left the acropolis under truce.","Herodotus traces the descent of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a marriage into the house of a Sicyonian tyrant, and on to a brother's daughter who bore Pericles."],"answer":"Cleisthenes of Athens","answerText":"Cleisthenes of Athens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reforms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10, replacing 4</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cleisthenes of Athens</b> was an Alcmaeonid, son of Megacles and of Agariste, whose father was the tyrant Cleisthenes of Sicyon, and Herodotus says he was named after his mother's father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same passage calls him the man who gave the Athenians their tribes and their democracy, and traces the line on to his brother's daughter Agariste, the mother of Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After the tyrants were removed he and Isagoras were the two principal holders of power, and Herodotus adds that he was reputed to have bribed the Pythian priestess.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Getting the worst of it in the political clubs, he took the commons into his party, offering, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says, to hand the government over to the multitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Cleomenes came at Isagoras's asking and demanded the banishment of the Accursed he secretly departed, and the Athenians sent to bring him back once the siege of the acropolis was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He divided the Athenians into ten tribes instead of four, calling none after the sons of Ion but taking names from other heroes, all native except Aias, added as a neighbour and an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus supposes he followed the lead of his grandfather and namesake out of contempt for the Ionians, and says that once he had won the people over he was stronger by far than the rival faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> credits him with a Council of five hundred instead of four hundred, and with making men known officially by their demes rather than by their fathers' names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It says the tribal heroes were picked by the Pythian priestess from a list of a hundred, and that he enacted the law about ostracism, making the constitution much more democratic than Solon's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> With the people in control of affairs he was their leader and head of the people, and he carried the changes in the archonship of Isagoras, in 508/507 BCE, the fourth year after the tyrants fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.73, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.131, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D131. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 20, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Cleisthenes take the commons into his party?","a":"Because he was getting the worst of it in the political clubs, so he offered to hand the government over to the multitude and was then stronger by far than the rival faction."},{"q":"Why were the new tribes not named after the sons of Ion?","a":"Because he took names from other heroes instead, all native except Aias, added as a neighbour and an ally; Herodotus supposes he followed his grandfather out of contempt for the Ionians."},{"q":"Why did naming men by their demes matter?","a":"Because it made them known officially by where they lived rather than by their fathers' names, which the Constitution of the Athenians counts among the changes that made the constitution more democratic than Solon's."}]},
{"id":"gr-309","num":309,"category":"Athens","question":"Geleon, Aegicores, Argades and Hoples were the four the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced, and the Pythia picked the new heroes from a list of a hundred.","questions":["Pausanias saw statues of the heroes who named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and could not tell which Cecrops or which Pandion of the several kings so called was meant.","Making the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ten rather than twelve kept the old Thirds out of the reckoning, since using them would have left the multitude unmixed."],"answer":"Cleisthenic tribes","answerText":"Cleisthenic tribes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10, replacing 4</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Cleisthenic tribes</b> were the ten phylai into which Cleisthenes divided the Athenians in place of the four they had had before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus names the old four after the sons of Ion, Geleon, Aegicores, Argades and Hoples, and says the new names were taken from other heroes, all native to the country except Aias, added because he was a neighbour and an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> gives the purpose as mixing the citizens up so that more might take part in the government, and traces to it the saying that one should not draw distinctions between tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It explains why he did not make twelve: that would have used the existing Thirds of the four old tribes, and the multitude would not have been mixed up at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each tribe instead drew on all three regions, the land being portioned into thirty parts, ten in the suburbs, ten on the coast and ten inland, assigned by lot three to a tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The heroes the tribes were named after were picked by the Pythian priestess from a previously chosen list of a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias saw their statues standing near the Council Chamber and lists them as Hippothoon, Antiochus, Ajax, Leos, Erechtheus, Aegeus, Oeneus and Acamas, with Cecrops and Pandion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Of the last two he says plainly that he does not know which of the men bearing each name was the one honoured, there having been an earlier and a later Cecrops and two kings called Pandion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Herodotus counts the change in officers as well as in names, ten tribe-wardens in place of four, with ten districts assigned to each tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribe then became the unit the state was organised by, fifty men from each sitting on the Council of Five Hundred and the generals coming to be elected one from every tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.5.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5%3Asection%3D1. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.5.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5%3Asection%3D2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.5.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D5%3Asection%3D3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Monument_of_the_Eponymous_Heroes.jpg/1920px-Monument_of_the_Eponymous_Heroes.jpg","title":"Cleisthenic tribes","desc":"Monument of the Eponymous Heroes on the Agora in Athens.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_of_the_Eponymous_Heroes.jpg","alt":"A long low stone base with a row of upright posts along its edge, standing in an archaeological park among olive trees"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Cleisthenes make ten tribes rather than twelve?","a":"Because twelve would have used the existing Thirds of the four old tribes, and the multitude would not have been mixed up at all, which was the purpose of the rearrangement."},{"q":"Why was each tribe drawn from three separate regions?","a":"Because the land was portioned into thirty parts — ten suburban, ten coastal, ten inland — assigned three to a tribe by lot, so that every tribe had a share in all the districts."},{"q":"Why could Pausanias not say who two of the heroes were?","a":"Because there had been an earlier and a later Cecrops and two kings called Pandion, so he does not know which man bearing each name was the one honoured."}]},
{"id":"gr-310","num":310,"category":"Athens","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made the official designation of an Athenian so that nobody should call attention to the citizens newly enfranchised by Cleisthenes.","questions":["Registered on the roll of his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at eighteen, an Athenian was voted on by its members on oath, and could be sold by the state if he lost his appeal.","Some took their names from their localities and others from their founders, since a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no longer always corresponded to the place it was named for."],"answer":"deme","answerText":"deme","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 140</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"A <b>deme</b> was a local unit of the Athenian countryside, and Herodotus says Cleisthenes assigned ten districts to each of his ten tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says he named some of them from their localities and others from their founders, the demes no longer all corresponding to the places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He made all the inhabitants of each one fellow-demesmen, so that men should be designated officially by their demes rather than by their fathers' names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The purpose given is that nobody should call attention to the newly enfranchised citizens, and the same work adds that Athenians came to use their demes as surnames in private life as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Demarchs were appointed with the duties the ship-commissioners had had, the demes taking the place of the ship-commissions, while the clans, brotherhoods and priesthoods of each were left on the ancestral plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides describes the Athenians as having long lived scattered over Attica in independent townships, and says most of them still kept house in the country when the Peloponnesian War began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their trouble at abandoning those houses and the hereditary temples was deep, each man feeling that he was bidding farewell to his own native city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the developed constitution the deme was where a man became a citizen, those of citizen parentage on both sides being registered on its roll at eighteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The demesmen voted on oath whether he had reached the lawful age and was a freeman of legitimate birth, and one who lost could appeal, the deme electing five of its own to plead against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If he lost again the state sold him and if he won the demesmen were compelled to register him, and the examination of the Nine Archons opened by asking to what deme a man's father belonged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 2.16, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 42, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 55, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Remains_of_the_ancient_road_and_the_ancient_city_of_Rhamnus_on_July_22%2C_2020.jpg/1920px-Remains_of_the_ancient_road_and_the_ancient_city_of_Rhamnus_on_July_22%2C_2020.jpg","title":"Deme","desc":"The ruins of the ancient deme of Rhamnous, of the Aiantis tribe, in northeastern Attica. George E.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_ancient_road_and_the_ancient_city_of_Rhamnus_on_July_22,_2020.jpg","alt":"A stony ancient road running down a scrub-covered hillside towards ruined walls on a headland, with the sea and mountains beyond"},"why":[{"q":"Why were Athenians to be named by their demes rather than their fathers?","a":"Because nobody was then to call attention to the newly enfranchised citizens, whose fathers' names would have marked them out; Athenians came to use their demes as surnames in private life too."},{"q":"Why did leaving the country houses cut so deep in the war?","a":"Because most Athenians still kept house in the country and had their hereditary temples there, so each man felt he was bidding farewell to his own native city."},{"q":"Why did the deme decide who was a citizen?","a":"Because its demesmen voted on oath at a man's registration whether he had reached the lawful age and was a freeman of legitimate birth, with an appeal if he lost."}]},
{"id":"gr-311","num":311,"category":"Athens","question":"Ten belonged to the suburbs, ten to the coast and ten inland, and each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was assigned to a tribe by lot so no tribe lacked a share in all three.","questions":["Twelve ship-boards were assigned to each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the four old Athenian tribes, and over the ship-boards stood the commissioners appointed for the levies and the expenditure.","Because each of the four Ionian tribes already held three, twelve tribes would have kept the older <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> system in use and left the Athenian multitude unmixed."],"answer":"trittys","answerText":"trittys","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30, three to a tribe</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"abstract":"A <b>trittys</b> was a third of an Athenian tribe, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says Cleisthenes portioned out the land among the demes into thirty such parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ten of them belonged to the suburbs, ten to the coast and ten to the inland district, and he gave these parts the name of Thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were assigned among the tribes by lot, three to each, in order that every tribe might have a share in all the districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same work explains why he did not arrange the citizens in twelve tribes: that would have meant using the division already in place, the four old tribes having contained twelve Thirds between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Had he done so the multitude would not have been mixed up at all, which was the purpose the whole rearrangement was for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The older Thirds are described under Solon, when there were four tribes and four tribal kings, three Thirds assigned from each tribe and twelve ship-boards to each Third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Over the ship-boards stood the office of ship-commissioners, appointed for the levies and the expenditure, and clauses ordering them to levy or to spend out of the ship-commission fund recurred often in Solon's laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regions the new Thirds were cut from had been the ground of Athenian faction, since the three parties of the years after Solon derived their names from the places where their farms were situated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were the Men of the Coast under Megacles son of Alcmaeon, the Men of the Plain who desired the oligarchy, and the Hillmen who had appointed Peisistratus over them as an extreme advocate of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus, the earlier of the two sources, does not use the word at all, reporting only that Cleisthenes made ten tribe-wardens in place of four and assigned ten districts to each tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.66, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D66. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D69. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 13, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the Thirds assigned to tribes by lot?","a":"Because each tribe had to take three of the thirty parts, one from each region, so that every tribe might have a share in the suburbs, the coast and the inland district alike."},{"q":"Why were the old Thirds an obstacle to a twelve-tribe scheme?","a":"Because the four old tribes already contained twelve Thirds between them, so twelve new tribes would have reused that division and mixed nobody up."},{"q":"Why did the regions the Thirds cut across matter politically?","a":"Because the three parties of the years after Solon took their names from where their farms lay — the coast, the plain and the hill — so region was the ground of Athenian faction."}]},
{"id":"gr-312","num":312,"category":"Athens","question":"Cleomenes tried to dissolve the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and hand the offices to Isagoras' faction, and it resisted him, after which he seized the acropolis and was besieged.","questions":["No measure could be put to the Athenian people that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had not prepared and published in advance, its proposer being liable to indictment if he carried one.","Eumelides rescued Lysimachus from the executioner, saying no citizen ought to die unsentenced, and the people stripped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the power to fine, imprison and kill."],"answer":"Council of Five Hundred","answerText":"Council of Five Hundred","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Members</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500, 50 a tribe</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Council of Five Hundred</b> was the body Cleisthenes made in place of the council of four hundred, taking fifty men from each of his ten new tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its members were appointed by lot, and the presidency was filled by each tribe in turn in an order settled by lot, the first four selected holding it for 36 days and the latter six for 35.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those serving as presidents dined together in the Round-house at the state's expense and convened the meetings of both the Council and the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Council met on every day but holidays and the assembly four times in each presidency, with the business, each day's agenda and the place of meeting put up in writing beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The presidents gave the first audience to heralds and to ambassadors, and dispatches were delivered to them by their bearers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Council prepared the resolutions the people voted on, and no measure could be passed that it had not prepared and published in writing in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had once held sovereign power to fine, imprison and put to death, and lost it when Eumelides of Alopece rescued Lysimachus from the executioner, saying that no citizen ought to die without the sentence of a jury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Afterwards every verdict and penalty it passed had to be brought before the jury-court, and its scrutiny of the incoming councillors and of the Nine Archons became subject to appeal in the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its members drew five obols a day and those acting as president an obol more for food, and a man might sit on it twice where the other offices could be held once only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus shows it acting on its own account within a few years of its founding, resisting Cleomenes when he tried to dissolve it and entrust the government to Isagoras' faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.72, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 45, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 62, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D62. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Remains_of_the_Old_Bouleuterion_and_Metroon_in_the_Ancient_Agora_of_Athens_on_March_23%2C_2021.jpg/1920px-Remains_of_the_Old_Bouleuterion_and_Metroon_in_the_Ancient_Agora_of_Athens_on_March_23%2C_2021.jpg","title":"Council of Five Hundred","desc":"Remains of the Old Bouleuterion and Metroon in the Ancient Agora of Athens. George E.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Old_Bouleuterion_and_Metroon_in_the_Ancient_Agora_of_Athens_on_March_23,_2021.jpg","alt":"Low courses of ancient stone foundations and wall blocks running across a grassy archaeological site, with trees and a scrubby hillside behind under an overcast sky"},"why":[{"q":"Why could nothing reach the assembly the Council had not prepared?","a":"Because it drew up the resolutions the people voted on, and no measure could be passed that it had not prepared and published in writing in advance."},{"q":"Why did the Council lose its power to put men to death?","a":"Because Eumelides of Alopece rescued Lysimachus from the executioner, saying no citizen ought to die without the sentence of a jury, after which its verdicts went before the jury-court."},{"q":"Why might a man sit on it twice when other offices were held once?","a":"Because five hundred places had to be filled every year from ten tribes, and the rule for the Council alone allowed a second term where the other offices could be held once only."}]},
{"id":"gr-313","num":313,"category":"Athens","question":"Otanes told the Persian conspirators that the rule of the multitude has the loveliest name of all, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and does none of the things a monarch does.","questions":["Determining offices by lot, holding power accountable and deliberating in public are the three things Herodotus has Otanes name as what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually does.","Maeandrius proclaimed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Samians while reserving six talents and a hereditary priesthood for himself, and was told he had better account for the money instead."],"answer":"isonomia","answerText":"isonomia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Current from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 525 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Isonomia</b> is the Greek word for equality under the law, and Herodotus puts it at the head of the case for government by the many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the debate he sets among the Persian conspirators, Otanes says that the rule of the multitude has in the first place the loveliest name of all, isonomia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then defines it by what it does: it determines offices by lot, it holds power accountable, and it conducts all deliberating publicly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each is the opposite of what a ruler answerable to nobody does, since such a man upsets the ancestral ways, kills indiscriminately and is the best confidant of slander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Otanes was outvoted and asked in return only that neither he nor his descendants be subject to any of the others, a house Herodotus says was still free in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Maeandrius of Samos set up an altar to Zeus the Liberator on learning of Polycrates' death, called an assembly and proclaimed isonomia to the citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He reserved six talents of Polycrates' wealth for himself and the priesthood of that cult for his descendants, and a Samian rose to say he was unfit to rule and had better account for the monies he had handled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristagoras made a pretence of giving up his tyranny and gave Miletus isonomia so that the Milesians might readily join his revolt from Darius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides has it among the fair professions of the Greek civil wars, the popular leaders crying political equality and the oligarchs a moderate aristocracy while each sought prizes for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In none of these passages is it applied to Athens, and where Herodotus explains why the Athenians grew great once rid of their tyrants he writes instead of isegoria, equal speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.80, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D80. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.83, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D83. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.142, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D142. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.37, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D37. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.78, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D78. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 3.82, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D82. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Otanes define the word by what a government does?","a":"Because he takes the loveliest name of all and gives it three practices — offices by lot, power held accountable, and all deliberating conducted publicly — each the opposite of what a ruler answerable to nobody does."},{"q":"Why does the word appear in the mouths of tyrants proclaiming it?","a":"Because Maeandrius proclaimed it while reserving six talents and a priesthood for himself, and Aristagoras gave Miletus isonomia so that the Milesians might readily join his revolt from Darius."},{"q":"Why is it striking that the word is not applied to Athens?","a":"Because where Herodotus explains why the Athenians grew great once rid of their tyrants he writes instead of isegoria, equal speech, and no passage here applies isonomia to the city."}]},
{"id":"gr-314","num":314,"category":"Athens","question":"Fewer than six thousand sherds and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was void, and the man whose name appeared most often left Athens for ten years with the enjoyment of his estate.","questions":["The first man banished by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Hipparchus son of Charmus, a relative of the tyrants, whom Cleisthenes is said to have made the law chiefly to be rid of.","An illiterate countryman handed Aristides a sherd at an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and asked him to write Aristides on it, being tired of hearing the man called the Just."],"answer":"ostracism","answerText":"ostracism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">487 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">417 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Quorum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 votes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Ostracism</b> was the Athenian procedure for banishing a citizen for ten years by vote, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> credits the law to Cleisthenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That work says it was enacted out of the suspicion felt against the men in the positions of power, because Peisistratus had set himself up as tyrant while leader of the people and general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not put in force until the archonship of Phaenippus in 487 BCE, two years after Marathon, when the first man banished was a relative of the tyrants, the son of Charmus, of the deme Collytus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For three years the Athenians went on ostracizing the friends of the tyrants, at whom the legislation had been aimed, and then in the fourth used it to remove anyone else who seemed too great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xanthippus son of Ariphron was the first man unconnected with the tyranny to go, and Aristides followed, until the expedition of Xerxes brought all the exiles home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The assembly voted in the sixth presidency of each year on whether an ostracism should be held at all, the question being put with the other business of that meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch describes the vote itself: each man wrote on a sherd the name of the citizen he wanted removed and carried it to a part of the market-place fenced round with wooden rails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The magistrates counted the sherds in gross first, since with fewer than six thousand the ostracism was void, and then the man written by the most was banished for ten years with the enjoyment of his estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch tells of an illiterate countryman who handed Aristides his sherd and asked him to write Aristides on it, being tired of hearing the man everywhere called the Just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides has Themistocles ostracized and living at Argos when the Spartans came for him, and Herodotus has Aristides cross from Aegina before Salamis, banished by the people and yet the most just man in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i>, in <i>Plutarch's Lives</i>, rev. Arthur Hugh Clough, 1859, Wikisource, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Plutarch%27s_Lives_%28Clough%29/Aristides. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.79, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.135, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D135. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Athen_Stoa_Ostrakismos_2.jpg/1920px-Athen_Stoa_Ostrakismos_2.jpg","title":"Ostracism","desc":"These ostraka from 482 BCE were recovered from a well near the Acropolis. The ostraca in the upper row read 'THEMISTHOKLES PHREARIOS', while the others read 'THEMISTHOKLES NEOKLEOS'.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athen_Stoa_Ostrakismos_2.jpg","alt":"Twelve broken pot bases mounted in rows on a museum wall, each with a Greek name scratched around the ring through the black glaze"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the law made against men in positions of power?","a":"Because Peisistratus had set himself up as tyrant while leader of the people and general, so the suspicion fell on those who held such positions."},{"q":"Why did its use change in its fourth year?","a":"Because for three years the Athenians ostracized the friends of the tyrants, at whom the legislation had been aimed, and then began using it to remove anyone else who seemed too great."},{"q":"Why were the sherds counted in gross before the names were read?","a":"Because with fewer than six thousand in all the ostracism was void, so the total had to be established before the man written by the most could be banished."}]},
{"id":"gr-315","num":315,"category":"Athens","question":"The presidents of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Athens at that time, says Herodotus, where Thucydides gives the same charge to the nine archons with full powers.","questions":["Twelve of them went to each of the four old tribes, and Cleisthenes replaced every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a deme and its officer with a demarch.","Clauses ordering an officer to levy or to spend out of the fund of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recurred in laws of Solon no longer in force when the Constitution of the Athenians was written."],"answer":"naukrary","answerText":"naukrary","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested before</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">48 by tradition</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"abstract":"A <b>naukrary</b> was one of the units the Athenians were divided into before Cleisthenes, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> assigns twelve of them to each of the four old tribes, beside three Thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over them was established an office whose holders were appointed for the levies and the expenditures that were made, the same passage setting the arrangement under Solon, when there were still four tribes and four tribal kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That work says clauses reading 'the ship-commissioner to levy' and 'to spend out of the ship-commission fund' occurred frequently in the laws of Solon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It adds that those laws were no longer in force in its own day, so it knows the clauses as quotations rather than as anything still used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cleisthenes put the demes in the place of the naukraries and appointed demarchs with the same duties their officers had had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The unit is best known from a disagreement between two historians about who was governing Athens when Cylon tried to make himself tyrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the men who raised Cylon's followers from the goddess's statue were the presidents of the naukraroi, the rulers of Athens at that time, and that the suppliants were subject to any penalty save death and were slain all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says the watch was left to the nine archons with full powers, adds that at that time most political functions were discharged by the nine archons, and gives the killing to those Athenians who were charged with the duty of keeping guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Godley renders the phrase as the presidents of the naval boards and Rackham has ship-commissioners for the same office, the Greek in Herodotus reading naukraroi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither passage explains what the unit was for, and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> describes only its levies and its fund.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 21, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.71, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Historiae</i> 5.71, ed. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0125%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D126. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the unit known chiefly from a disagreement between historians?","a":"Because Herodotus says the presidents of the naukraroi were the rulers of Athens when Cylon's followers were killed, while Thucydides says most political functions were then discharged by the nine archons."},{"q":"Why does the Constitution of the Athenians quote Solon's clauses rather than cite the law?","a":"Because those laws were no longer in force in its own day, so it knows phrases like 'to spend out of the ship-commission fund' as quotations rather than as anything still used."},{"q":"Why did the demes take the unit's place so neatly?","a":"Because Cleisthenes put them where the naukraries had been and appointed demarchs with the same duties their officers had had."}]},
{"id":"gr-316","num":316,"category":"Athens","question":"Aristotle lists the temple of Olympian Zeus among the works of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside the pyramids of Egypt, as a device for keeping subjects poor and busy.","questions":["The spring below the acropolis was Fairwater while it lay open and Nine Pipes after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> altered it, and Pausanias says Peisistratus embellished it.","Hadrian dedicated the temple and its gold and ivory statue centuries after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had begun the building and left it standing unfinished."],"answer":"Peisistratid building programme","answerText":"Peisistratid building programme","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 546 – 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Peisistratid building programme</b> is the work the tyrants of Athens put up in the city, and Aristotle names its largest piece outright, the building of the temple of Olympian Zeus by the Peisistratids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He lists it with the pyramids in Egypt, the votive offerings of the Cypselids and the temples at Samos raised by Polycrates, all as instances of one device of tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The device is to make the subjects poor so that no guard need be kept against them, and to leave a people busy with their daily affairs no leisure to plot against their ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The temple was left standing unfinished, and Pausanias saw it complete only because Hadrian dedicated the building and a gold and ivory statue exceeding every other in size but the colossi at Rhodes and Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Athenians told him the ancient sanctuary there had been built by Deucalion, and pointed to a grave near the temple as their evidence that Deucalion had lived at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides says the spring below the citadel went by the name of Callirhoe, or Fairwater, while it lay open, and was called Enneacrunos, or Nine Pipes, after the alteration the tyrants made to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that it stood near enough the old city to be used for the most important offices, and that the old fashion of taking its water before marriage was still kept up in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias saw it centuries later, says Peisistratus had embellished it, and remarks that although there were cisterns all over Athens this was the only fountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides had also read the faded inscription on the altar in the Pythian precinct, which a grandson of the tyrant dedicated during his own archonship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The precinct of the temple was about four stades round and full of statues when Pausanias walked it, every city having dedicated a likeness of Hadrian and the Athenians a colossus behind the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 2.15, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.1313b, trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0058%3Abook%3D5%3Asection%3D1313b. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.14.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D14%3Asection%3D1. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.18, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Constantinou_Dimitrios_-_The_Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus_and_the_Acropolis_in_the_background_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Constantinou_Dimitrios_-_The_Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus_and_the_Acropolis_in_the_background_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The temple of Olympian Zeus and the Acropolis","desc":"An early photograph of the columns of the temple of Olympian Zeus at Athens standing on open ground, with the rock of the Acropolis rising beyond them.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantinou_Dimitrios_-_The_Temple_of_Olympian_Zeus_and_the_Acropolis_in_the_background_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A sepia photograph of a group of tall fluted columns with leafy Corinthian capitals standing on open ground at the right, two more columns alone in the middle distance, a low town behind them and the rock of the Acropolis rising beyond it"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Aristotle list the temple with the Egyptian pyramids?","a":"Because both are instances of one device of tyranny: to make the subjects poor so that no guard need be kept against them, and to leave them no leisure to plot against their ruler."},{"q":"Why did Pausanias see a finished temple where the tyrants left one unfinished?","a":"Because Hadrian dedicated the building and a gold and ivory statue exceeding every other in size but the colossi at Rhodes and Rome."},{"q":"Why did the spring change its name under the tyrants?","a":"Because it was called Callirhoe, or Fairwater, while it lay open, and Enneacrunos, or Nine Pipes, after the alteration they made to it."}]},
{"id":"gr-317","num":317,"category":"Athens","question":"Xenophon dates a year by an eclipse of the moon one evening and by the burning of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Athens, when Callias was archon.","questions":["The foundations that carry the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stand between the Erechtheion and the Parthenon, on the rock the Athenians of Pausanias' day called the Acropolis and once called the Polis.","A shoot a cubit long sprang the next day from the olive burnt with the rest of the sanctuary, on the acropolis rock where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands."],"answer":"Old Temple of Athena","answerText":"Old Temple of Athena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 525 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Old Temple of Athena</b> is the name given to a large set of foundations on the Athenian acropolis, standing between the Erechtheion and the Parthenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Xenophon dates a year by an eclipse of the moon and by the burning of the old temple of Athena at Athens, when Pityas was ephor at Sparta and Callias archon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus, writing earlier, says the fetters of the Chalcidian and Boeotian prisoners could still be seen in his own time hanging from acropolis walls that the Persians' fire had charred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He places them opposite the temple which faces west, and says a tenth of the ransom bought a four-horse chariot standing to the left of the entrance into the outer porch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Persians reached the town in 480 BCE, the archonship of Calliades, it was deserted, and the few holding the sacred precinct fenced it with doors and logs, believing the oracle's wooden wall meant that and not the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some of the barbarians climbed the sheer cliff near the precinct of Cecrops' daughter Aglaurus, opened the gates, murdered the suppliants, plundered the sacred precinct and set fire to the entire acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In that acropolis, Herodotus adds, is a shrine of Erechtheus, and in the shrine an olive tree and a pool of salt water set there by Poseidon and Athena as tokens when they contended for the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The olive was burnt with the rest of the sacred precinct, and on the day after the burning the Athenians ordered up to sacrifice saw a shoot about a cubit long sprung from the stump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias saw a double building there centuries later, with sea-water in a cistern that sent forth the noise of waves when a south wind blew, and the outline of a trident on the rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He calls the most holy symbol the image of Athena on what is now the Acropolis but in early days the Polis, or 'City', and reports a legend that it fell from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.77, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D77. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.51, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D51. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.53, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 8.55, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 1.6.1, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0206%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.26, trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]","Jane E. Harrison and Margaret de G. Verrall, <i>Mythology and Monuments of Ancient Athens</i> (London: Macmillan, 1890), 414, https://archive.org/details/mythologymonumen00paus. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Foundations_of_the_Old_Athena_Temple_%28foreground%29.jpg/1280px-Foundations_of_the_Old_Athena_Temple_%28foreground%29.jpg","title":"Foundations of the Old Temple of Athena","desc":"Foundations of the Old Athena Temple (foreground). Plate from Martin Luther D'Ooge, The Acropolis of Athens, 1909.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foundations_of_the_Old_Athena_Temple_(foreground).jpg","alt":"A grainy photograph looking down on the Athenian acropolis, with long rectangular lines of squared stone blocks laid out on the ground in the foreground and the porch of the Erechtheion standing behind them"},"why":[{"q":"Why were the Persians able to take the acropolis at all?","a":"Because the few holding the sacred precinct had fenced it with doors and logs, believing the oracle's wooden wall meant that and not the ships, and some barbarians climbed the sheer cliff and opened the gates."},{"q":"Why did the olive shoot matter so much to the Athenians?","a":"Because the tree was one of the tokens Athena and Poseidon set when they contended for the land, and on the day after the burning those sent up to sacrifice saw a shoot about a cubit long sprung from the stump."},{"q":"Why could Herodotus still see the fetters of prisoners on the walls?","a":"Because the walls opposite the west-facing temple had been charred by the Persians' fire and the Chalcidian and Boeotian fetters hung there still in his own time."}]},
{"id":"gr-318","num":318,"category":"Athens","question":"Xenophon says nobody even attempts to date the beginning of mining at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that the dumps are small beside the virgin silver-laden hills.","questions":["Alcibiades told the Spartans that fortifying Decelea would deprive the Athenians of their revenues from the silver mines at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and of their gains from land and law courts.","Nicias son of Niceratus owned a thousand men in the mines at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and let them out to a Thracian for an obol a day each, net."],"answer":"Laurion","answerText":"Laurion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Worked from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Bronze Age, c. 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great strike</span><span class=\"dt-v\">483/482 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Laurion</b> is the mining district of Attica, and Xenophon calls the silver in its soil a gift of divine providence, since of the many states near Athens by land and sea not one has even a thin vein of the ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He puts beside it the land that yields no fruit if sown and yet, when quarried, feeds many times the number it could support if it grew corn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mines had been worked so many generations, he says, that nobody even attempts to date the beginning of the work, and the dumps are small beside the virgin silver-laden hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The silver-yielding ground was continually being found to extend further rather than to shrink, and where other trades slump when more men enter them, more diggers here only multiply the ore discovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor is silver like furniture, which a man stops buying once he has enough for his house, since nobody has ever owned so much of it as to want no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon had heard that Nicias son of Niceratus once owned a thousand men in the mines and let them to Sosias the Thracian for an obol a day each, net, with every vacancy filled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hipponicus had 600 slaves let out on the same terms for a mina a day and Philemonides 300 for half a mina, and others owned numbers in proportion to their capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts a great strike in the archonship of Nicomedes, in 483/482 BCE, when the working of the mines at Maronea had given the state a profit of 100 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Alcibiades advised the Spartans that fortifying Decelea would deprive the Athenians at once of their revenues from the silver mines at Laurium, and of their gains from the land and the law courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says that occupation cost them their whole country and more than 20,000 slaves, a great part of them craftsmen, who deserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 1, trans. E. C. Marchant, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 4, trans. E. C. Marchant, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 6.91, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D91. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 7.27, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D27. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Greece_Lavrion_Mines_1903.jpg/1280px-Greece_Lavrion_Mines_1903.jpg","title":"Miners at Lavrion, 1903","desc":"Greek miners—just going down—at the famous Mines of Ancient Laurion, Greece. From stereograph card.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece_Lavrion_Mines_1903.jpg","alt":"A sepia photograph of a large crowd of miners in caps on a bare hillside, many of them holding up oil lamps, with pit buildings and a winding tower on the ridge behind"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Xenophon call the silver a gift of divine providence?","a":"Because of the many states near Athens by land and sea not one has even a thin vein of the ore, and the ground that yields no fruit if sown feeds many when quarried."},{"q":"Why does he say more diggers do not spoil the trade?","a":"Because where other trades slump when men crowd into them, more diggers here only multiply the ore discovered, the silver-yielding ground being continually found to extend further."},{"q":"Why did fortifying Decelea strike at the mines?","a":"Because it deprived the Athenians at once of their revenues from the silver, and cost them more than 20,000 slaves, a great part of them craftsmen, who deserted."}]},
{"id":"gr-319","num":319,"category":"Athens","question":"Every judge desires them above all, says the chorus of the <i>Birds</i>, promising that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> will nest in their money-bags and lay coins for them.","questions":["The uniformity of style and type is remarkable, since the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keep an archaic head of Athena and an incuse square reverse through class after class.","The reverse of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a bird to the right with its head facing, wings closed, an olive-spray and a crescent behind it, and three letters."],"answer":"Athenian owls","answerText":"Athenian owls","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Struck from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Main coin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 4-drachma tetradrachm</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Athenian owls</b> are the silver coins of Athens, and their reverse gives them the name: an owl to the right with its head facing and its wings closed, an olive-spray behind it, and the letters ΑΘΕ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The obverse carries the head of Athena in a close-fitting crested helmet, rude and archaic on the earliest pieces, with a large prominent eye and each lock of hair ending in a twisted curl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the later coins the helmet is adorned in front with three olive-leaves and a floral scroll behind, and a crescent-moon joins the olive-spray behind the owl on the tetradrachm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole series is remarkable for its uniformity of style and type, and the scale ran from the decadrachm down through the tetradrachm at about 17.5 grams to fractions of a single obol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> credits Solon with raising the standard of the measures, the weights and the coinage, the mina going from 70 drachmas to the full hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon says that at most other ports a merchant is compelled to ship a return cargo, because the local currency has no circulation in other states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Athens he may export silver instead, and wherever he sells it he is sure to make a profit on the capital invested, which Xenophon counts among the advantages of the city as a commercial centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chorus of Aristophanes' <i>Birds</i> promises the judges that the owls of Laurium will never be wanting to them, homing with them, building their nests in their money-bags and laying coins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the <i>Frogs</i> the chorus calls the old coins not counterfeit at all but the finest of all coins, the only ones of the proper stamp, of resounding metal among Greeks and foreigners everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It sets them against the base bronze minted yesterday or the day before, which the city uses instead, as it uses worthless men in place of the good ones it despises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 3, trans. E. C. Marchant, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Birds</i> 1101–1117, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr. (New York: Random House, 1938), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0026%3Acard%3D1102. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 10, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0046%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Frogs</i> 718–737, trans. Matthew Dillon, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0032%3Acard%3D718. [Open access]","Barclay V. Head, <i>Historia Numorum: A Manual of Greek Numismatics</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1887), 310–312, https://archive.org/details/historianumorumm00headuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Tetradrachm_Athens_450_reverse_CdM_Paris.jpg","title":"Reverse of an Athenian tetradrachm, about the 450s BCE","desc":"Silver tetradrachm issued by Athens, about the 450s BCE, reverse: owl, olive spray and crescent moon, with the inscription ΑΘΕ.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tetradrachm_Athens_450_reverse_CdM_Paris.jpg","alt":"A worn silver coin showing an owl standing to the right with its head turned to face the viewer, an olive spray and a crescent above its shoulder, and three Greek letters down the right edge"},"why":[{"q":"Why could an Athenian merchant export silver where others could not?","a":"Because at most ports the local currency has no circulation elsewhere and a merchant must ship a return cargo, while Athenian silver sells at a profit wherever it is taken."},{"q":"Why does Aristophanes call the old coins the only ones of proper stamp?","a":"Because they were of resounding metal among Greeks and foreigners everywhere, set against the base bronze minted yesterday, as good men are set against the worthless ones the city uses instead."},{"q":"Why is the uniformity of the type worth remarking on?","a":"Because the whole series holds one style and one pair of images, the helmeted Athena and the owl with olive-spray and ΑΘΕ, from the decadrachm down to fractions of an obol."}]},
{"id":"gr-320","num":320,"category":"Athens","question":"A request from Epidaurus for Athenian olive wood is where Herodotus begins the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the trees given on condition of yearly dues to Athena and Erechtheus.","questions":["An oracle bade the Athenians wait thirty years and then mark out a precinct for Aeacus, one turn in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that they could not stomach.","During the fighting that came out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Corinth sold Athens twenty ships at five drachmas apiece, its law forbidding an outright gift."],"answer":"enmity between Athens and Aegina","answerText":"enmity between Athens and Aegina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Open war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 506 – 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">481 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>enmity between Athens and Aegina</b> is traced by Herodotus to the barren land of Epidaurus, whose people were told by the priestess at Delphi to set up images of Damia and Auxesia in the wood of the cultivated olive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They asked the Athenians for the trees, it being said that at that time there were no olives anywhere save at Athens, and got them on condition of yearly dues to Athena and Erechtheus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Aeginetans, still subject to Epidaurus and crossing there even for their own lawsuits, began to build ships, revolted, and carried the images off to a place of their own called Oea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians sent one trireme to fetch them back, and their own account has the crew driven mad by a thunderstorm and an earthquake until they killed one another and a single man returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Aeginetans and the Argives say instead that the Argives crossed secretly from Epidaurus, fell on the Athenians unawares and cut them off from their ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> All three accounts agree that one man came home, and the wives of the dead gathered round him and stabbed him with the brooch-pins of their garments, each asking him where her husband was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Athenians thought that more dreadful than the disaster itself and could punish the women only by changing their dress to a linen tunic that needed no pins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Argos and Aegina made brooches the chief thing offered to the two goddesses and forbade anything Attic in the temple, even pottery, and their women wore longer pins ever after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When it came to war an oracle bade Athens wait thirty years, mark out a precinct for Aeacus and begin in the thirty-first, which the Athenians could not stomach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Aeginetans then ambushed the sacred ship at Sunium with many leading Athenians aboard, and Corinth, a close friend at that time, sold Athens twenty ships at five drachmas apiece because its law forbade a free gift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.82–83, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D82. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.85–86, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D85. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.87–88, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D87. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.89, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D89. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.87, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D87. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 6.89, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D89. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Epidaurus have to ask Athens for the olive wood?","a":"Because the priestess told them to set up images of Damia and Auxesia in the wood of the cultivated olive, and it was said that at that time there were no olives anywhere save at Athens."},{"q":"Why did Athenian women have to change how they dressed?","a":"Because the wives of the dead stabbed the one returning man with their brooch-pins, and the Athenians, finding that more dreadful than the disaster, could punish them only by imposing a linen tunic that needed no pins."},{"q":"Why did Corinth charge five drachmas apiece for its ships?","a":"Because its law forbade a free gift, so a close friend of Athens sold the twenty triremes at a nominal price instead of giving them."}]},
{"id":"gr-321","num":321,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Tyrannies established almost everywhere in place of hereditary monarchy, and Hellas beginning to fit out fleets, is how Thucydides describes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which closes with the Persian Wars.","questions":["Lacedaemon put down the tyrants at last, having obtained good laws very early, near the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Marathon and the armada close.","The 8th century is commonly taken as the breaking-point at which the polis emerged, near the start of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though that shift may be less drastic."],"answer":"Archaic period","answerText":"Archaic period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Archaic period</b> is the age of Greek history between the Dark Age and the Classical, and Thucydides opens his sketch of these centuries with a Hellas still removing and settling, unable to attain the quiet which must precede growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The late return of the Hellenes from Ilium caused many revolutions, he writes, and it was the citizens driven into exile by the factions that followed who founded the cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many years had to elapse before Hellas could send out colonies, as Athens did to Ionia and most of the islands and the Peloponnesians to most of Italy and Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As the power of Hellas grew and the acquisition of wealth became more an object, tyrannies were established almost everywhere in place of the old hereditary monarchy with its definite prerogatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hellas began to fit out fleets, the Corinthians being the first to approach the modern style of naval architecture, and the earliest sea-fight in history was fought between Corinth and Corcyra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Corinth, planted on an isthmus, had from time out of mind been a commercial emporium, and the epithet 'wealthy' given it by the old poets marks the money that bought her a navy and put down piracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tyrants were at last put down by Lacedaemon, which had obtained good laws at a very early period and kept the same form of government for more than four hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not many years after that deposition the battle of Marathon was fought, and ten years afterwards the barbarian returned with the armada, which is where the period is conventionally closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 8th century is commonly taken as the breaking-point at which the polis emerged, though a longer perspective makes that shift less drastic and the 6th century more important than is often thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tyranny itself is now read as a response to economic and political crisis, and the tyrants and lawgivers of these centuries as a varied group that resists easy definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.12, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.18, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Kostas Vlassopoulos, review of <i>A History of the Archaic Greek World, ca. 1200–479 BCE</i>, by Jonathan M. Hall, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.01.41, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.01.41/. [Open access]","Angela Kühr, review of <i>The Cambridge Companion to Archaic Greece</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2008.01.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2008/2008.01.13/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides make the return from Troy a cause of colonies?","a":"Because the late return of the Hellenes caused many revolutions, and it was the citizens driven into exile by the factions that followed who founded the cities abroad."},{"q":"Why did tyrannies replace the old hereditary monarchies?","a":"Because as the power of Hellas grew and the acquisition of wealth became more an object, tyrannies were established almost everywhere in place of a kingship with definite prerogatives."},{"q":"Why do modern accounts make the 6th century more important than the 8th?","a":"Because a longer perspective makes the emergence of the polis less drastic a shift than the conventional 8th-century breaking-point suggests, and tyranny is read as a response to a later crisis."}]},
{"id":"gr-322","num":322,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Hybrid monsters, lions, lotus-flowers and rosettes strewn as filling ornament mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Greek workshops took up eastern motifs across pottery, ivory and bronze.","questions":["Griffin, siren and bull protomes fixed to cauldrons at Delphi are among the most characteristic bronzes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which runs through the 7th century.","A kit of Levantine motifs, ivory work, monumental sculpture and alphabetic writing is what Greeks took up in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, largely through Phoenician contact."],"answer":"Orientalising period","answerText":"Orientalising period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Orientalising period</b> is the phase of Greek art that runs through the 7th century BCE, the change setting in at the end of the 8th, when workshops across the Greek world took up eastern motifs and techniques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the East came hybrid monsters such as the sphinx and the siren, animals such as the lion, isolated motifs like the lotus-flower and the rosette, and a habit of laying decoration in long bands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground between the figures was strewn with rosettes and other filling ornament, a manner that reads as an imitation of woven textiles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The change is put down to the commercial activity of the Phoenicians, scattered by Assyria in the 8th century, and was artistic and industrial rather than political.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The borrowings have been gathered into an orientalising kit: Levantine decorative motifs, new pottery and metalwork technologies, oriental-style monumental sculpture, ivory work, masonry techniques, burial and banqueting customs, and alphabetic writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus says the Phoenicians who came with Cadmus brought the alphabet to Hellas, unknown to the Greeks before, and that the Ionians learned the letters from them and named them after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Delphi shows the period in bronze: rod stands and tripods of Cypro-Cretan type, and appliqués in the form of griffin, siren and bull protomes, among the most characteristic pieces of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The winged sphinx began in Egypt and reached Greece in the winged and female version of the Levant, where it had become a standard feature of Phoenician work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Imports and imitations are distinguished from orientalising art proper, which is Greek in essence and manufacture and oriental only in inspiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One reading makes the innovation inseparable from the crystallisation of the polis, the new art serving elites in the forming communities as a mode of social recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.58, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 333, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Hélène Sader, review of <i>Phoenicians and the Making of the Mediterranean</i>, by Carolina López-Ruiz, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.06.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.06.11/. [Open access]","John Papadopoulos, review of <i>Bronzes du haut-archaïsme à Delphes: Trépieds, chaudrons et vaisselle de bronze (fin VIIIe-VIIe siècle)</i>, by Hélène Aurigny, Fouilles de Delphes 5, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.07.08, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.07.08/. [Open access]","Carolina López-Ruiz, review of <i>Un art citoyen: recherches sur l'orientalisation des artisanats en Grèce proto-archaïque</i>, by Thomas Brisart, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2012.11.45, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2012/2012.11.45/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Protom_d%27un_grif%C3%B3%2C_bronze%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_VII_aC._Museu_Arqueol%C3%B2gic%2C_Ol%C3%ADmpia-2.jpg","title":"Bronze griffin protome from Olympia, before the middle of the 7th century BCE","desc":"Protome of a griffin. Hammered bronze. The eyes are encrusted with bone, and the now lost iris was made of a semi-precious stone. Before the middle of the 7th century BCE. National Archaeological Museum of Olympia.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protom_d%27un_grif%C3%B3,_bronze,_segona_meitat_del_segle_VII_aC._Museu_Arqueol%C3%B2gic,_Ol%C3%ADmpia-2.jpg","alt":"A hammered bronze head and long curving neck of a griffin, its beak open, a knobbed spike rising from the crown and the eye socket set with bone"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the change put down to Phoenician commerce?","a":"Because the Phoenicians were scattered by Assyria in the 8th century and their trade carried the motifs and techniques west, so the change was artistic and industrial rather than political."},{"q":"Why is the filling ornament thought to imitate textiles?","a":"Because ground-ornaments of that kind — rosettes and other filling strewn between the figures — are almost unknown in eastern art itself and read as an imitation of woven cloth."},{"q":"Why are imports kept separate from orientalising art proper?","a":"Because orientalising work is Greek in essence and manufacture and oriental only in inspiration, where an import or a copy is the eastern object itself."}]},
{"id":"gr-323","num":323,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Pear-shaped lekythoi, aryballoi and skyphoi strewn with rosettes and cut with incised scale-patterns are the stock of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Athens took the market from by about 550 BCE.","questions":["A jug bearing the name of Pyrrhos, no later than 700 BCE and the earliest known signature on a pot, belongs to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found across Sicily and Italy.","Shapes, figures and above all the incised line passed from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Athenian workshops, whose black-figure then drove it off the market."],"answer":"Corinthian pottery","answerText":"Corinthian pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protocorinthian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 650 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Corinthian pottery</b> is the painted ware of Corinth, and it falls into five classes: the Protocorinthian of about 750 to 650 BCE, then vases with incised scale-patterns, vases with floral ornament and unincised figures, the same with the figures incised, and finally large animal friezes of about 600 to 550.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dominating form is the alabastron or lekythos, a pear-shaped vase with a flat round lip and a flat handle, beside the aryballos, the skyphos, the pyxis and a small krater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Protocorinthian vases turn up at Syracuse, a Corinthian foundation of 735 BCE, at Megara Hyblaea, in Italian trench-tombs of about 750 to 650, and at the Argive Heraion, on Aegina and more rarely at Tiryns, Athens and Eleusis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of them carries the name of Pyrrhos in an inscription that cannot be much later than 700 BCE, which makes it the earliest known signature on a pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides puts commerce behind all this: Corinth was planted on an isthmus and had from time out of mind been an emporium, wealthy enough in the old poets' epithet to buy itself a navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The incised line arrives with a class of vases covered in scale-patterns, cut with a compass whose graving-tool left the centre points of the arcs visible, a technique long familiar in metalwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Towards the end of the 8th century an influx of eastern motifs transformed the style, bringing conventionalised rosettes, leaves and flowers strewn all over the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ground-ornaments of that kind are almost unknown in eastern art itself and seem to be drawn from embroidered textiles, and purple accessories become common alongside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The finest work is miniature: some twenty pear-shaped lekythoi survive with minute figures in an advanced style, the Macmillan lekythos at their head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Corinthian shapes, figures and above all incision shaped Athenian black-figure, and by about 550 BCE Attic had taken the market, for reasons nobody has settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>The Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent; New York: E. P. Dutton, 1910), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0200%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 306, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 307–309, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 311–312, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Aryballos_Macmillan.JPG","title":"The Macmillan aryballos, attributed to the Chigi Painter","desc":"Perfume bottle, aryballos, with lion-head mouth, attributed to the Chigi Painter; British Museum GR 1889.4-18.1.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aryballos_Macmillan.JPG","alt":"A small Protocorinthian perfume flask whose mouth is modelled as a lion's head, its body banded with narrow friezes of tiny figures and animals"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the incised line thought to come from metalwork?","a":"Because it arrives with vases covered in scale-patterns cut with a compass whose graving-tool left the centre points of the arcs visible, a technique long familiar in metal."},{"q":"Why does one pot carry more weight than the rest of the class?","a":"Because it bears the name of Pyrrhos in an inscription that cannot be much later than 700 BCE, which makes it the earliest known signature on a pot."},{"q":"Why does Corinth's position explain the ware's distribution?","a":"Because it was planted on an isthmus and had from time out of mind been an emporium, so its vases turn up at Syracuse, Megara Hyblaea and in Italian tombs."}]},
{"id":"gr-324","num":324,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Air let in, then shut off, then let in again is how the kiln makes the colour of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose figures are silhouettes with details cut through them.","questions":["Athens took the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Corinth along with its shapes, and held the market with it until red-figure reversed the scheme.","Magnetite crystals under 200 nanometres across colour the glassy slip of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the unslipped clay beside it cannot match because oxygen reaches it."],"answer":"black-figure technique","answerText":"black-figure technique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Incision from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attic heyday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 520 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>black-figure technique</b> paints its figures as silhouettes in a slip of fine clay and cuts their details through it with a graver, an adjunct to figure-decoration that was entirely new and was probably taken from metalwork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colour comes from the kiln rather than the brush, since one clay serves for both pot and slip and three stages of firing are what separate them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first, air is let in and every surface turns brighter shades of red and orange, the iron compound formed being mostly hematite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the second the air is shut off, every surface goes black, and a thin glassy layer forms on the slipped areas alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the third, oxygen works back into the unslipped clay and returns it to red, while the slipped surfaces cannot take it through that vitreous layer and stay black.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The black is a highly resistant potash-alumino-silicate glass coloured by magnetite crystals under 200 nanometres across, made from an illitic Attic clay worked down to a fine particle size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beyond incision the painters added accessory red and white for expressive purposes, laid on before the pot went into the fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No Greek kiln has been found, so the ovens are known from the painted vases themselves: tall conical furnaces fed by fires from beneath, with one door for putting the pots in and another for watching them bake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Corinthian pinakes are the best of that evidence, and the potters' hymn ascribed to Homer names the demons who spoil a firing, the Unquenchable, the Crasher, the Smasher and the Savage Conqueror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Corinthian shapes and above all incision passed to Athens, where the technique held the market until red-figure reversed the scheme and the Andokides Painter began the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 311, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 215–216, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Sanchita Balachandran, \"Bringing Back the (Ancient) Bodies: The Potters' Sensory Experiences and the Firing of Red, Black and Purple Greek Vases,\" <i>Arts</i> 8, no. 2 (2019): 70, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts8020070. [Open access]","Lars Lühl, Bernhard Hesse, Ioanna Mantouvalou, Max Wilke, Sammia Mahlkow, Eleni Aloupi-Siotis, and Birgit Kanngiesser, \"Confocal XANES and the Attic Black Glaze: The Three-Stage Firing Process through Modern Reproduction,\" <i>Analytical Chemistry</i> 86, no. 14 (2014): 6924–30, https://doi.org/10.1021/ac500990k. [Paywalled]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Amphora_with_equestrian_scenes%2C_Pointed_Nose_Painter%2C_Tyrrhenian_group%2C_Greek%2C_Attic%2C_c._550_BC%2C_black-figure_technique_-_Krannert_Art_Museum%2C_UIUC_-_DSC06540.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Attic black-figure amphora, Pointed Nose Painter, about 550 BCE","desc":"Amphora with equestrian scenes, Pointed Nose Painter, Tyrrhenian group, Greek, Attic, about 550 BCE, black-figure technique. Exhibit in the Krannert Art Museum, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphora_with_equestrian_scenes,_Pointed_Nose_Painter,_Tyrrhenian_group,_Greek,_Attic,_c._550_BC,_black-figure_technique_-_Krannert_Art_Museum,_UIUC_-_DSC06540.jpg","alt":"A tall two-handled jar whose body carries bands of black horsemen and animals on the orange clay, their manes, harness and muscles picked out by fine lines cut through the black"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the black produced by the kiln rather than by the brush?","a":"Because one clay serves for both pot and slip, and three stages of firing separate them: oxygen reddens everything, its absence blackens everything and glazes the slip, and its return reddens only what is unslipped."},{"q":"Why does the slipped surface stay black in the last stage?","a":"Because a thin glassy layer formed on it in the reducing stage, and oxygen cannot work back through that vitreous layer as it does into the bare clay."},{"q":"Why are Greek kilns known only from painted pictures of them?","a":"Because no Greek kiln has been found, so the tall conical furnaces with their fires beneath and their two doors are known from the vases themselves, the Corinthian pinakes above all."}]},
{"id":"gr-325","num":325,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"The finest work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Vatican amphora on which Ajax and Achilles bend over a board, one calling four and the other three.","questions":["Ten vases carry the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and twice the signature says he both painted and made the pot rather than only made it.","The inside of a Munich cup by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is filled by Dionysos sailing in a ship shaped like a fish, its mast overgrown by a vine."],"answer":"Exekias","answerText":"Exekias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vases dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 – 530 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Exekias</b> is among the most accomplished of the Athenian black-figure artists, and ten vases carry his name: four amphorae, four cups and two fragments, with a handful of unsigned pieces given to him besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most Attic artists sign with a word meaning that they made the pot, and he twice writes that he both painted and made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His finest is an amphora in the Vatican with Ajax and Achilles bent over a board game, the one calling four and the other three, and the Dioskouroi with Tyndareus and Leda on the reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries his signature in iambic form and the name Onetorides praised as beautiful, while the other three signed amphorae are in London, Paris and Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cup in Munich is a masterpiece of its kind: inside, Dionysos sails in a ship shaped like a fish with the mast and yard overgrown by a vine, and outside are large eyes and groups of warriors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His subjects come from the ordinary stock of the day, and what marks them out is the care in every detail, the delicacy and minuteness of the incising, the sparing use of accessory colour and the naming of almost every figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wide-bowled cup with a low foot, which the eye-cups of Ionia had already made popular, seems to have been brought to Athens by him and stayed in favour right through the red-figure period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where other painters set palmettes and lotus-buds under the handles he puts an elaborate system of spirals, a pattern descended from Mycenaean art by way of the Melian amphorae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A neck-amphora of about 540 BCE in New York, 47 centimetres (18 inches) high and still carrying its lid and knob, is painted with a chariot wedding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He stands midway between Kleitias of the François vase and the transitional work that came after, and narrative reached its full development with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 379–381, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 357, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 375, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Jenifer Neils, review of <i>How to Read Greek Vases</i>, by Joan R. Mertens, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.07.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.07.23/. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta neck-amphora with lid and knob, Greek, Attic, attributed to Exekias, ca. 540 BCE, accession 17.230.14a, b, https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/250551. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP218568.jpg","title":"Neck-amphora with lid, attributed to Exekias, about 540 BCE","alt":"A tall lidded two-handled jar painted with a four-horse chariot carrying a bride and groom, a lyre-player beside the team and attendants at either end, with a large black spiral under each handle and bands of meander below","desc":"A terracotta neck-amphora with its lid and knob, Attic black-figure work attributed to Exekias, about 540 BCE and 47 cm (18 inches) high. A four-horse chariot carries a bride and groom, with a lyre-player beside the team and attendants at either end, over a band of meander.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/250551"},"why":[{"q":"Why is his signature unusual among Attic painters?","a":"Because most Attic artists sign with a word meaning only that they made the pot, and he twice writes that he both painted and made it."},{"q":"Why is his work distinguished when his subjects are the common stock?","a":"Because what marks it out is the care in every detail, the delicacy and minuteness of the incising, the sparing use of accessory colour and the naming of almost every figure."},{"q":"Why do his handle ornaments stand out from other painters'?","a":"Because where others set palmettes and lotus-buds under the handles he puts an elaborate system of spirals, a pattern descended from Mycenaean art by way of the Melian amphorae."}]},
{"id":"gr-326","num":326,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Ergotimos made me and Kleitias painted me are each written twice on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, once in the wedding procession and once beside Theseus on the rim.","questions":["Almost every figure on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named, and so are an altar, a seat, a hydria, a fountain-house and the stone in a centaur's hand.","Pygmies fight cranes round the foot of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with six figured registers above them and Ajax carrying the dead body of Achilles on each handle."],"answer":"François Vase","answerText":"François Vase","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>François Vase</b> is an Attic black-figure volute krater of about 570 BCE, signed by Ergotimos as potter and Kleitias as painter, and now in Florence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is 66 centimetres (26 inches) high with a rim 57 centimetres (22 inches) across, and carries six figured registers, with Ajax lifting the dead Achilles on the handles and pygmies fighting cranes round the foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The potting is as inventive as the drawing, the neck rising in two degrees with no offset lip and the handles owing something, perhaps, to the volutes of monumental architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two friezes run round the neck, the hunt for the Calydonian boar above the chariot race at the funeral games of Patroklos, and Theseus with the Athenian youths above the battle of Lapiths and centaurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wedding of Peleus and Thetis runs unbroken round the shoulder, a subject Sophilos had signed on a dinos a decade earlier, and beneath it Achilles chases Troilos while Hephaistos rides back to Olympus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Almost every figure is named, and so are an altar, a seat, a hydria, a fountain-house and the stone in a centaur's hand, the alphabet of the lettering leaving no doubt that the work is Athenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The names include spellings found nowhere else, Poseipon for Poseidon and Aphrogite for Aphrodite among them, and both signatures are written twice, once in the wedding procession and once beside Theseus on the rim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysos steps out of the procession in full frontal view with an amphora on his shoulder, probably the golden jar that Homer says held the bones of Achilles and Patroklos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The glaze inside fired red and is worn by metal dippers, and the holes drilled round the handles proved to be an ancient repair in lead, so the krater was used and mended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fragments came from a plundered tomb near Chiusi, gathered in the 1840s by the painter Alessandro François, and whether the scenes make one programme is argued still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The François Vase: New Perspectives</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Mario Iozzo and Adrienne Lezzi-Hafter, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.32/. [Open access]","“Florence 4209 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Florence+4209&object=Vase. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 73, 370, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 23.83–92, trans. A. T. Murray, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0134%3Abook%3D23%3Acard%3D62. [Open access]","“London 1971.11-1.1 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=London+1971.11-1.1&object=Vase. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Vaso_fran%C3%A7ois%2C_firenze%2C_03.JPG","title":"The François Vase in Florence","desc":"The François Vase, an Attic black-figure volute krater signed by the potter Ergotimos and the painter Kleitias, in the National Archaeological Museum of Florence.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaso_fran%C3%A7ois,_firenze,_03.JPG","alt":"One side of a large two-handled clay pot, its breaks and joins plainly visible, painted in horizontal bands: winged sphinxes and fighting figures at the top, a chariot race below them with Greek names written beside the drivers, a procession of gods and horses round the widest part, and a low decorated foot"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the vase certainly Athenian rather than imported?","a":"Because almost every figure and several objects are named, and the alphabet of the lettering leaves no doubt that the work is Athenian."},{"q":"Why do the lead holes round the handles matter?","a":"Because they proved to be an ancient repair, which with the glaze worn inside by metal dippers shows the krater was used and mended rather than made only for a tomb."},{"q":"Why are both signatures written twice?","a":"Because Ergotimos as potter and Kleitias as painter each signed once in the wedding procession and again beside Theseus on the rim."}]},
{"id":"gr-327","num":327,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"A stand in New York barely 9 centimetres (3.5 inches) across and the great krater in Florence are both signed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, working with the potter Ergotimos.","questions":["A chariot race at the funeral games puts Odysseus first, though he does not compete in Homer, which is one reason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thought to have worked from a recital.","Only Exekias, Nearchos and the Amasis Painter are reckoned to rival <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Attic black-figure, and his style has been called dry, precise and careful."],"answer":"Kleitias","answerText":"Kleitias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 560 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Kleitias</b> was an Athenian vase-painter working about 570 BCE, and he is known by name because he wrote it on his own work, in the form that reads Kleitias painted me.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worked with the potter Ergotimos, who signed beside him to say that he had made it, which was not the usual thing: most early Attic artists used one formula implying that the same man made and painted the pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two signed pieces that survive could hardly differ more in size: the François Vase in Florence stands 66 centimetres (26 inches) high, the other is a stand in New York 5.7 centimetres (2 inches) high and 9 centimetres (3.5 inches) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That small disc carries a single Gorgon's face, staring out with its tongue between its teeth and its beard drawn in fine incised lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His achievement in black-figure is reckoned to be rivalled only by Exekias, Nearchos and the Amasis Painter, and his style has been called dry, precise and careful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He labelled copiously, and 130 names are painted on the krater, naming not only the gods and the heroes but the odd inanimate object as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The names do not always follow the poems, and his chariot race at the funeral games puts Odysseus first, though Odysseus does not compete in the <i>Iliad</i>, and Diomedes third, though Homer makes him the winner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He is unlikely to have owned a written copy of the poem and probably worked from a recital he had heard, giving the drivers plausible names and leaning, perhaps, on a better-read friend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Theseus appears on the krater in festal dress, among the earliest pictures of the hero in Athenian art, which begins to show him about 575 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Elsewhere he took a shape that painters were already using and made it a story by naming it, so that a warrior carrying a dead comrade off the field becomes Ajax and Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Florence 4209 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Florence+4209&object=Vase. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 370, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 379, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta stand, Greek, Attic, signed by Ergotimos as potter and by Kleitias, ca. 570 BCE, accession 31.11.4, https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253342","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>Tradition and Originality: A Study of Exekias</i>, by E. Anne Mackay, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2010.08.34, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.08.34/. [Open access]","Barry B. Powell, review of <i>Image and Myth: A History of Pictorial Narration in Greek Art</i>, by Luca Giuliani, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.35, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.35/. [Open access]","Sarah Morris, review of <i>Homer and the Artists: Text and Picture in Early Greek Art</i>, by Anthony M. Snodgrass, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 1999.10.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1999/1999.10.32/. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The François Vase: New Perspectives</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Mario Iozzo and Adrienne Lezzi-Hafter, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.32/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP-12501-003.jpg","title":"Terracotta stand signed by Ergotimos and Kleitias","alt":"A small round clay disc seen from above, painted with a single staring face: round eyes, curled hair, tusks at the corners of the mouth and a tongue hanging between the teeth, the beard drawn as a dense fringe of fine incised lines","desc":"Terracotta stand, Greek, Attic, about 570 BCE, signed by Ergotimos as potter and by Kleitias, 5.7 cm high and 9 cm across. Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 31.11.4, Fletcher Fund, 1931. Public domain (Open Access), via the Metropolitan Museum of Art.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253342"},"why":[{"q":"Why is this painter's own name known when most are not?","a":"Because he wrote it on his work in the form that reads Kleitias painted me, while the potter Ergotimos signed separately to say he had made it."},{"q":"Why is his chariot race thought to come from a recital rather than a text?","a":"Because the names do not follow the poem — Odysseus is put first though he does not compete in the Iliad, and Diomedes third though Homer makes him the winner."},{"q":"Why does naming a scene amount to making it a story?","a":"Because he took shapes painters were already using and labelled them, so that a warrior carrying a dead comrade off the field becomes Ajax and Achilles."}]},
{"id":"gr-328","num":328,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Where other black-figure painters gave women's flesh a coat of added white, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would sometimes draw them in outline against the bare clay instead.","questions":["A lekythos in New York by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows two women at an upright loom, the warp hanging in weighted threads and the woven band forming above.","Vases signed Amasis made me are given by museums to the potter of that name and their painting to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who left no name of his own."],"answer":"Amasis Painter","answerText":"Amasis Painter","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 515 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Amasis Painter</b> is the name given to an Athenian black-figure painter at work from about 560 to 515 BCE, and it is borrowed rather than his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The vases he decorated carry the signature Amasis made me, running along the edge of the picture, which is a potter's formula, so he is called after the man who threw the pots while his own name goes unrecorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Few vases carry that signature, but his manner is individual enough that a good many unsigned ones have been given to him as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His drawing is accurate and his detail careful and delicate, close to Exekias in that, though his subjects are more monotonous and his figures a good deal more rigid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much in his work suggests a connection with Ionia, and since his name is Egyptian more than one writer has thought him a foreigner, from Naukratis, from Daphnae, or from somewhere in Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He was fond of the olpe, a jug carrying its picture in a panel to the right of the handle only, and four of his signed vases take that shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also painted one of the earliest Attic alabastra, a small oil flask whose form came from Egyptian models and reached Athens about the middle of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where other painters gave women's flesh a coat of added white, he would sometimes leave them in outline against the bare clay, which has been read as an experiment and as an economy alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A lekythos of his in New York shows two women at an upright loom, the warp hanging in weighted threads and the finished band forming along the beam above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On a neck amphora in Boston he set Herakles and Apollo hauling at the Delphic tripod with Hermes stepping between them, hand raised, his name misspelt above his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 382–383, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","“Boston 01.8027 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Boston+01.8027&object=Vase. [Open access]","Metropolitan Museum of Art, terracotta lekythos, Greek, Attic, attributed to the Amasis Painter, ca. 550–530 BCE, accession 31.11.10, https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253348","J. Michael Padgett, review of <i>The Colors of Clay: Special Techniques in Athenian Vases</i>, by Beth Cohen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.03.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.09/. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>Corpus vasorum antiquorum. Deutschland, Bd 99; Berlin, Antikensammlung, Bd. 16: Attische Salbgefässe</i>, by Nina Zimmermann-Elseify, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2016.04.50, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.04.50/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/gr/original/DP-32672-005.jpg","title":"Women at an upright loom, on a lekythos by the Amasis Painter","alt":"Detail of a clay pot: two women in patterned dresses stand either side of a tall loom, its warp hanging in a curtain of straight threads ending in a row of small weights, with a woven band and a decorated beam across the top","desc":"Detail of a terracotta lekythos, Greek, Attic, about 550 to 530 BCE, attributed to the Amasis Painter: women weaving at a warp-weighted loom. Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 31.11.10, Fletcher Fund, 1931. Public domain (Open Access), via the Metropolitan Museum of Art.","credit":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/253348"},"why":[{"q":"Why is this painter known by another man's name?","a":"Because the vases carry the signature Amasis made me, which is a potter's formula, so he is called after the man who threw the pots while his own name goes unrecorded."},{"q":"Why has he been thought a foreigner?","a":"Because much in his work suggests a connection with Ionia and the name on the pots is Egyptian, so writers have placed him at Naukratis, at Daphnae or somewhere in Asia Minor."},{"q":"Why is his treatment of women's flesh read two ways?","a":"Because where other painters gave it a coat of added white he would sometimes leave the figures in outline against the bare clay, which has been called an experiment and an economy alike."}]},
{"id":"gr-329","num":329,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Nine-tenths of the painted vases recovered in Etruria came from Vulci alone, one measure of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that sent Athenian pots west across the Mediterranean.","questions":["Merchants in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> scratched notes under the feet of the pots they shipped, one recording 34 lekythoi for 37 obols and another a batch of six kraters.","Between 60 and 80 per cent of Etruscan tombs of the later 6th and 5th centuries held Athenian pottery, carried there by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that fed Vulci and Cerveteri."],"answer":"Attic pottery trade","answerText":"Attic pottery trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reaches Etruria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 620 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In most tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Attic pottery trade</b> carried Athenian painted vases across the Mediterranean, and it is why the pots found in Greece itself are few and small beside the crowds of them taken from the cemeteries of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vases by the Nettos Painter, working late in the 7th century BCE, were the first Attic pieces to reach Etruria, and the numbers rose sharply in the opening decades of the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athenian pottery turns up in every Etruscan city of any size, from Hadria at the mouth of the Po down to the gates of Rome, and nine-tenths of all the painted vases recovered in Etruria came from Vulci alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between 60 and 80 per cent of Etruscan tombs of the later 6th and 5th centuries held Attic pottery, most often cups, kraters, amphorae and lekythoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not bought for the grave alone, since the same wares appear in houses and in some forty sanctuaries, in large centres and small, in richer and poorer houses alike, and many pots carry ancient repairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The merchants who moved them left their mark under the foot, scratching commercial signs into the clay and sometimes a note of the batch with its price.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One krater now in London lists six kraters, twelve cups, twenty vinegar-cups and twenty saucers with figures in drachmas and obols beside them, and a shorter note gives 34 lekythoi for 37 obols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristophanes has Dionysos tell Euripides that he can buy the offending oil-flask for an obol, the sort of sum those lists imply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At such prices Attic pottery in Etruria cannot be called a luxury ware, and it made up only a small part of the crockery in an Etruscan house, exactly as fine pottery did in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some have argued from this that the vases travelled as little more than ballast, though the numbers tell against it, and the potters plainly knew their customers, sending west the revels, warriors, athletes and Herakles the Etruscan buyer wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Adolfo Domínguez, review of Christoph Reusser, <i>Vasen für Etrurien: Verbreitung und Funktionen attischer Keramik im Etrurien des 6. und 5. Jahrhunderts vor Christus</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2003.06.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2003/2003.06.11/. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 70, 76–77, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 2 (London: John Murray, 1905), 238–41, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient02walt. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The François Vase: New Perspectives</i>, ed. H. A. Shapiro, Mario Iozzo and Adrienne Lezzi-Hafter, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2014.04.32, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2014/2014.04.32/. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Frogs</i> 1236, ed. Matthew Dillon, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0032%3Acard%3D1225. [Open access]","Mariachiara Franceschini, review of Sheramy D. Bundrick, <i>Athens, Etruria, and the Many Lives of Greek Figured Pottery</i>, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2019.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2019/2019.10.30/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Eracle%2C_Atena_e_Dioniso%2C_550-530_aC%2C_anfora_attica_da_Vulci_-FG.jpg/1920px-Eracle%2C_Atena_e_Dioniso%2C_550-530_aC%2C_anfora_attica_da_Vulci_-FG.jpg","title":"An Athenian amphora found at Vulci","desc":"Herakles, Athena and Dionysos on an Attic black-figure amphora of about 550 to 530 BCE found at Vulci, now in the Museo Gregoriano Etrusco of the Vatican Museums, inv. 16590.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eracle,_Atena_e_Dioniso,_550-530_aC,_anfora_attica_da_Vulci_-FG.jpg","alt":"A two-handled clay jar standing in a museum case, its upper body painted with black figures on an orange ground: a draped figure at the left, a figure wearing a spotted lion skin in the middle and a draped figure at the right, under a band of palmettes round the neck, with a black lower body on a small foot"},"why":[{"q":"Why are more Attic vases found in Italy than in Greece?","a":"Because the trade carried them across the Mediterranean into the Etruscan cemeteries, and nine-tenths of all the painted vases recovered in Etruria came from Vulci alone."},{"q":"Why is the ware not to be called a luxury?","a":"Because the merchants' scratched price notes give 34 lekythoi for 37 obols and a batch of kraters and cups in drachmas and obols, sums no more than Aristophanes' obol for an oil-flask."},{"q":"Why do the pots show that they were not bought only for graves?","a":"Because the same wares appear in houses and in some forty sanctuaries, in richer and poorer houses alike, and many of them carry ancient repairs."}]},
{"id":"gr-330","num":330,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"The figures on a pot in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not painted at all: they are left in the colour of the clay while the black slip is brushed over everything around them.","questions":["An amphora in Boston of about 530 BCE shows Achilles and Ajax twice over, once in black-figure and once in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that was then new at Athens.","Detail in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is drawn with a brush rather than cut with a graver, in relief lines for the firm contours and dilute gloss for the softer markings."],"answer":"red-figure technique","answerText":"red-figure technique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Invented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>red-figure technique</b> reverses the older scheme: the figures are left in the colour of the clay and the black slip is carried over everything around them, so the figures are never painted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was an invention rather than a development, no intermediate stage between the two schemes being possible, and it appears at Athens in the last quarter of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The contours were drawn round each figure with a brush, a fine one at first and then a broader that laid a band about 3 millimetres (0.1 inches) wide; filling in the rest of the surface was mechanical work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inner detail was put in with a brush rather than cut with a graver, in relief lines for the firm markings, gloss thinned to a warm brown for the softer ones, and masses of solid black for hair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A fragment at Sèvres catches the sequence half done, its figures finished and bordered in black while the ground was never filled in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What it bought was a subtlety of drawing no graver could reach, and the painters who took it up were draughtsmen before anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some workshops hedged for a while, and bilingual vases carry one technique on each side: an amphora in Boston of about 530 BCE has Achilles and Ajax at their game in red-figure on one face and in black-figure on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rivalry the new drawing set off is written on the pots themselves, and an amphora signed by Euthymides carries beside its revellers the boast that Euphronios never managed anything like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Black-figure went on being made in quantity into the early 5th century, but the younger painters preferred the new method, and the old one was left to a few conservative uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Red-figure pots were still being made and exported in the 4th century, by which time Athens was no longer the centre it had been and the busiest workshops lay in the Greek cities of southern Italy and Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 221–23, 400–402, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Mary B. Moore, review of <i>The History of Greek Vases: Potters, Painters and Pictures</i>, by John Boardman, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.04.23, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.04.23/. [Open access]","J. Michael Padgett, review of <i>The Colors of Clay: Special Techniques in Athenian Vases</i>, by Beth Cohen, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2007.03.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2007/2007.03.09/. [Open access]","“Boston 01.8037 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Boston+01.8037&object=Vase. [Open access]","“Munich 2307 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Munich+2307&object=Vase. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Kylix_with_crowned_athlete%2C_Antiphon_painter%2C_Greek%2C_Attic%2C_c._485_BC%2C_red-figure_technique_on_earthenware_-_Krannert_Art_Museum%2C_UIUC_-_DSC06524.jpg/1920px-Kylix_with_crowned_athlete%2C_Antiphon_painter%2C_Greek%2C_Attic%2C_c._485_BC%2C_red-figure_technique_on_earthenware_-_Krannert_Art_Museum%2C_UIUC_-_DSC06524.jpg","title":"Attic red-figure kylix, Antiphon Painter, about 485 BCE","desc":"Kylix with crowned athlete, Antiphon Painter, Greek, Attic, about 485 BCE, red-figure technique on earthenware. Exhibit in the Krannert Art Museum, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kylix_with_crowned_athlete,_Antiphon_painter,_Greek,_Attic,_c._485_BC,_red-figure_technique_on_earthenware_-_Krannert_Art_Museum,_UIUC_-_DSC06524.jpg","alt":"A shallow two-handled drinking cup seen from above on a museum stand, its inside black except for a round panel where a young man stands in the orange colour of the clay, a cloak over one arm and a staff in his hand, his body and drapery drawn in fine dark lines, the panel ringed by a meander border"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the technique called an invention rather than a development?","a":"Because no intermediate stage between the two schemes is possible: either the figures are painted on the clay or the ground is filled and the figures left in it."},{"q":"Why did painters take it up despite the extra filling work?","a":"Because it bought a subtlety of drawing no graver could reach, inner detail being brushed in relief lines and thinned gloss rather than cut through the slip."},{"q":"Why does the Sèvres fragment matter?","a":"Because it catches the sequence half done, its figures finished and bordered in black while the ground was never filled in, which shows the order the work was carried out in."}]},
{"id":"geo-001","num":1,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"California","answerText":"California","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 September 1850</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31st state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"California"},"facts":[["Capital","Sacramento"],["Population","39.4M (2024)"],["Largest city","Los Angeles"],["Area","423,967 km² (163,695 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Flag_of_California.svg","credit":"Donald Graeme Kelley (design), vectorised by Devin Cook, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_California.svg)","alt":"The flag of California: a brown grizzly bear walking on a patch of green grass, with a red star above it at the hoist, the words CALIFORNIA REPUBLIC beneath, and a red band along the bottom."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>California</b> is a Pacific coast state of the United States and the most populous of the fifty, with a resident population the Census Bureau put at 39.4 million in July 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is more than eight million ahead of Texas, the next largest, and roughly one American in eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its total area of 423,967 square kilometres (163,695 square miles) makes it the third largest state by extent, behind only Alaska and Texas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground runs from a long Pacific shoreline through the farmed floor of the Central Valley to the granite of the Sierra Nevada, and drops away in the south-east into the desert basin of Death Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That spread of landscapes shows in what the federal government protects there, from the Channel Islands off the southern coast to Joshua Tree in the deserts and Lassen Volcanic among the northern peaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Gold was found in the Sierra foothills in the late 1840s, and the rush that followed brought migrants from across the country and from overseas in numbers that reshaped the territory within a few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> California was admitted to the Union as the thirty-first state on 9 September 1850, having skipped the long territorial apprenticeship most states served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the early 1960s it had overtaken New York to become the most populous state, a position it has held ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its economy grew unusually broad rather than resting on one staple, taking in diversified agriculture, fisheries, forestry and mining alongside aircraft plants, shipyards, tourism, the film industry and the technology sector now identified with Silicon Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sacramento, in the northern Central Valley, is the seat of state government, while Los Angeles is by a wide margin the largest city at about 3.9 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"California: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/california-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"California,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ca/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Tunnel_View%2C_Yosemite_Valley%2C_Yosemite_NP_-_Diliff.jpg/1920px-Tunnel_View%2C_Yosemite_Valley%2C_Yosemite_NP_-_Diliff.jpg","title":"Yosemite Valley, California","desc":"Yosemite Valley.","credit":"Diliff, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tunnel_View,_Yosemite_Valley,_Yosemite_NP_-_Diliff.jpg","alt":"A view of Yosemite Valley."}},
{"id":"geo-002","num":2,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Texas","answerText":"Texas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 December 1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Texas"},"facts":[["Capital","Austin"],["Population","31.3M (2024)"],["Largest city","Houston"],["Area","695,662 km² (268,596 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Flag_of_Texas.svg","credit":"Paul B. Joiner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Texas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Texas: a blue vertical band at the hoist bearing a single white star, beside a white bar above a red bar."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Texas</b> is the largest state of the United States outside Alaska, covering 695,662 square kilometres (268,596 square miles) in the south-centre of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also the second most populous, with 31.3 million residents in July 2024 and a gap of some eight million to California above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its border with Mexico runs along the Rio Grande, whose great bend the National Park Service protects as Big Bend National Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land falls from the Guadalupe Mountains and the high plains of the west, through the hill country of the centre, to the barrier islands of the Gulf coast at Padre Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Houston, with about 2.4 million people, is the largest city, ahead of San Antonio and Dallas, while the seat of government is at Austin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Texas declared independence from Mexico in 1836 and then spent nearly a decade as a sovereign nation, the Republic of Texas, with its own president, its own currency and its own foreign legations, before it joined the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was admitted as the twenty-eighth state on 29 December 1845, the only one to enter the Union directly from independent statehood rather than by graduating from a federal territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ceremonial transfer of government followed on 19 February 1846, when Anson Jones, the last president of the republic, handed authority to James Pinckney Henderson, the first governor of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That short national history left marks the state has never given up, from the lone star of its flag to the standing of the Alamo in its public memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cattle, cotton, oil and gas built the modern economy in turn, and the Gulf coast around Houston remains one of the great concentrations of refining and petrochemical industry anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Clinton Drake, \"Texas: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 28 November 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/texas-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Texas,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/tx/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Canyon%2C_Rio_Grande%2C_Texas.jpeg/1920px-Canyon%2C_Rio_Grande%2C_Texas.jpeg","title":"Big Bend National Park, Texas","desc":"Big Bend National Park.","credit":"Michael from Minnesota (@Michael), CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canyon,_Rio_Grande,_Texas.jpeg","alt":"A view of Big Bend National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-003","num":3,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Florida","answerText":"Florida","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 March 1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Florida"},"facts":[["Capital","Tallahassee"],["Population","23.4M (2024)"],["Largest city","Jacksonville"],["Area","170,312 km² (65,758 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Flag_of_Florida.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Florida.svg)","alt":"The flag of Florida: a red diagonal cross on a white field, with the state seal at the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Florida</b> is the long peninsula at the south-eastern corner of the United States, dividing the Atlantic Ocean from the Gulf of Mexico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the twenty-seventh state on 3 March 1845 and is now the most populous of the south-eastern states and the second most populous southern state after Texas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Census Bureau counted 23.4 million residents in July 2024, and the state has for years ranked among those with the fastest-growing immigrant populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its total area of 170,312 square kilometres (65,758 square miles) includes an unusually large share of water, some 12,000 square kilometres (4,700 square miles) more than its land area alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital is Tallahassee, in the north-western panhandle, a long way from the peninsula where most of the population lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Low, flat and wet, the southern half of the peninsula drains slowly southward through the sawgrass of the Everglades, which the National Park Service protects together with the neighbouring swamp of Big Cypress and its endangered panthers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Offshore the Dry Tortugas and Biscayne parks take in reefs, keys and shallows that are far more water than land, and the barrier beaches of Canaveral and Gulf Islands run along either coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Spanish settlers came early and left the oldest European masonry in the country: the fort of Castillo de San Marcos still stands at St. Augustine, with Fort Matanzas guarding the inlet to its south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The climate and the scenery of the Sunshine State have long drawn visitors in enormous numbers, and tourism has overtaken agriculture and manufacturing as the main component of the economy, with the growing service sector drawing migrants in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jacksonville, at just over a million people, is the largest city by population, while Miami anchors the far larger metropolitan area at the peninsula's south-eastern tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Florida: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/florida-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Florida,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/fl/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sunset_over_the_River_of_Grass%2C_NPSphoto%2C_G.Gardner_%289255157507%29.jpg/1920px-Sunset_over_the_River_of_Grass%2C_NPSphoto%2C_G.Gardner_%289255157507%29.jpg","title":"the Everglades, Florida","desc":"Everglades National Park.","credit":"Everglades NPS from Homestead, Florida, United States, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_over_the_River_of_Grass,_NPSphoto,_G.Gardner_(9255157507).jpg","alt":"A view of Everglades National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-004","num":4,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Rhode Island","answerText":"Rhode Island","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 May 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Rhode Island"},"facts":[["Capital","Providence"],["Population","1.11M (2024)"],["Largest city","Providence"],["Area","4,001 km² (1,545 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Rhode_Island.svg","credit":"Xrmap flag collection 2.7, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rhode_Island.svg)","alt":"The flag of Rhode Island: a gold anchor on white above a blue ribbon reading HOPE, ringed by thirteen gold stars."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Rhode Island</b> is the smallest state of the United States, tucked into the New England coast between Connecticut and Massachusetts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its total area is 4,001 square kilometres (1,545 square miles), of which only 2,678 square kilometres (1,034 square miles) is land — the rest is Narragansett Bay and the coastal waters that give the Ocean State its nickname.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 1.11 million people live there, so that despite its size it is a long way from being the least populous state, and it is among the most densely settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bay cuts deep inland from the south, breaking the state into a mainland and a scatter of islands and peninsulas, and putting a great deal of shoreline into a very small compass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Providence stands at the head of that bay and is both the capital and the largest city, with roughly 195,000 residents of the state's 1.11 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The colony began as a refuge rather than as a venture: Roger Williams, banished from the Massachusetts Bay colony for his opinions and sheltered by the region's Native peoples, founded Providence in 1636.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He argued that to molest any person for professing a doctrine or practising a worship merely religious is to persecute them, and the town was built to put that principle into practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tolerance that followed drew others, and Touro Synagogue at Newport, which the National Park Service maintains as a historic site, is the oldest surviving synagogue building in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rhode Island was the last of the thirteen original states to ratify the federal constitution, holding out until 29 May 1790 over its suspicion of central power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the 19th century the fast water of the Blackstone valley turned the first American textile mills, and the National Park Service treats that corridor as the birthplace of the country's industrial revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Rhode Island: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/rhode-island-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Rhode Island,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ri/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Breakers_01.jpg","title":"The Breakers, Rhode Island","desc":"The Breakers.","credit":"User:Itub, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breakers_01.jpg","alt":"A view of The Breakers."}},
{"id":"geo-504","num":504,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Providence","answerText":"Providence","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1636</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1900</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Rhode Island","dot":"Providence"},"facts":[["State","Rhode Island"],["Population","195k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","134th largest"],["Population growth","+2.0% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Flag_of_Providence%2C_Rhode_Island.png","credit":"City of Providence, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Providence,_Rhode_Island.png)","alt":"The flag of Providence: the city seal in gold and blue on a dark blue field, showing a scene captioned WHAT CHEER? with figures greeting a canoe."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Providence</b> is the capital of Rhode Island and its largest city, standing at the head of Narragansett Bay where the Providence River meets the tidewater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roughly 195,000 people live within the city itself<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, about a sixth of the state's 1.11 million, in a state small enough that almost all of it lies within an hour's drive of here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town was founded in 1636 by Roger Williams, who had been banished from the Massachusetts Bay colony for his religious opinions and was sheltered by the region's Native peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He settled at a place the Narragansett called Moshassuck and renamed it Providence, in acknowledgement of what he took to be a merciful deliverance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The National Park Service keeps a memorial on the site of the freshwater spring around which the first houses of Providence Plantations gathered, a four-and-a-half acre greenspace at the foot of College Hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Williams held that to molest any person, Jew or Gentile, for professing a doctrine or practising a worship merely religious is to persecute them, and he made the place a shelter for those distressed in conscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That principle, radical in 17th-century New England, drew dissenters of many kinds and shaped the colony's later reputation for religious liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For most of its history the state had no single capital at all, and its legislature moved between several towns before Providence took the role permanently at the opening of the present State House in 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 19th-century city grew on textiles, jewellery and silverware, trades fed by the water power of the Blackstone valley just to the north, which the National Park Service treats as the birthplace of American industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its harbour and its universities have kept it the commercial and cultural centre of the state as those mills have closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Rhode Island: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/rhode-island-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"History &amp; Culture,\" Roger Williams National Memorial, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/rowi/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Rhode Island,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ri/index.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Aerial_view_of_downtown_Providence%2C_September_2015.JPG/1920px-Aerial_view_of_downtown_Providence%2C_September_2015.JPG","title":"Providence","desc":"Providence, Rhode Island, seen from a distance.","credit":"Pi.1415926535, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_downtown_Providence,_September_2015.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Providence, Rhode Island."}},
{"id":"cnh-001","num":"1","category":"China · History","question":"Because it never produced an epic, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives only in pieces — a few lines in a geography here, a passing allusion in a ritual compendium there.","answer":"Chinese mythology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief sources</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 BCE – 100 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pangu myth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"中國神話","hanzi":"中国神话","pinyin":"Zhōngguó shénhuà","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chinese mythology</b> was never handed down as a single work: it survives as material scattered through texts written for other purposes, among them geographies, ritual compendia and philosophical treatises. Chinese literature is unusual in having no epic or narrative genre at its origin, and mythology appears in it only sporadically and in an entirely fragmentary state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sources are infinitely divided and dispersed, so a figure may be known from a few lines in one book and an allusion in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The richest single repertory is the <i>Shanhaijing</i>, or <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i>, one of several compilations made as imperial China took shape, around the turn of the era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It alone is given over entirely to cosmography; the <i>Huainanzi</i>, the <i>Lüshi chunqiu</i> and the <i>Liji</i> carry their myth alongside ritual, statecraft and natural philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What they have least of is a creation story: a world made by a creator standing outside it never took hold in China, and the poverty of early accounts of the world’s origin has long puzzled scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pangu, the giant from whose body the world is formed, is the best known of the few and a late one, first written down in the 3rd century CE, after most of the figures he appears to precede.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That poverty is disputed, though: a silk manuscript from a Chu tomb at Changsha has Fuxi and Nüwa beget four gods who part heaven from earth and set the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The flood is the best-attested early story, and no deluge: at least six versions can be told apart in the <i>Book of Documents</i> alone, local traditions of a great inundation and of setting the world in order, none saying where the water came from or laying blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In China proper Nüwa fights the water and mends the world, while among peoples of the south a brother and sister survive to found a new humanity; of ancient Chinese belief little else reaches us but through tomb painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chinese mythology","questions":["The giant Pangu, from whose body the world is formed, is a latecomer to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, his earliest written versions dating only from the 3rd century CE.","No angry god sends the flood in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: the water is simply there, and Nüwa is remembered for fighting it and repairing the world afterwards."],"tags":["concept","mythology","religion","literature","china","text"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Léon Vandermeersch, “Écriture et littérature en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 8 (1986): 53–66, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1986.927. [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Diény, “Mythologie et sinologie,” <i>Études chinoises</i> 9, no. 1 (1990): 129–50, https://doi.org/10.3406/etchi.1990.1120. [Open access]","Vera V. Dorofeeva-Lichtmann, “Conception of Terrestrial Organization in the Shan hai jing,” <i>Bulletin de l’École française d’Extrême-Orient</i> 82 (1995): 57–110, https://doi.org/10.3406/befeo.1995.2297. [Open access]","Marc Kalinowski, “Mythe, cosmogénèse et théogonie dans la Chine ancienne,” <i>L’Homme</i> 36, no. 137 (1996): 41–60, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1996.370035. [Open access]","Max Kaltenmark, “La légende de la ville immergée en Chine,” <i>Cahiers d’Extrême-Asie</i> 1 (1985): 1–10, https://doi.org/10.3406/asie.1985.855. [Open access]","Rémi Mathieu, “Le corbeau dans la mythologie de l’ancienne Chine,” <i>Revue de l’histoire des religions</i> 201, no. 3 (1984): 281–309, https://doi.org/10.3406/rhr.1984.4312. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Anonymous-Fuxi_and_N%C3%BCwa.jpg","title":"Chinese mythology","desc":"Fuxi and Nüwa. Nüwa is on the left. Fuxi is her brother. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous-Fuxi_and_N%C3%BCwa.jpg","alt":"Fuxi and Nüwa"},"why":[{"q":"Why does this material survive only in fragments?","a":"Because Chinese literature has no epic or narrative genre at its origin, so myth appears sporadically inside geographies, ritual compendia and philosophical treatises written for other purposes."},{"q":"Why is the shortage of creation stories argued over?","a":"Because a world made by a creator standing outside it never took hold, and the best-known giant is a late figure of the 3rd century CE, while a Chu silk manuscript has Fuxi and Nüwa beget gods who part heaven from earth."},{"q":"Why is the flood story the best attested of them?","a":"Because at least six versions can be told apart in the Book of Documents alone, local traditions of a great inundation and of setting the world in order, none saying where the water came from."}]},
{"id":"cnh-002","num":"2","category":"China · History","question":"Chinese tradition sets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the first dynasty, and no two ancient accounts agree on which culture heroes belong on the list.","answer":"Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yao’s accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2357 BCE or 2145 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanhuang annals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 8th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"三皇五帝","hanzi":"三皇五帝","pinyin":"Sān Huáng Wǔ Dì","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors</b> are the rulers Chinese tradition sets before the first dynasty, and no two ancient accounts agree on who they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian’s <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i> opens not with the sovereigns but with five emperors — Huangdi, Zhuanxu, Ku, Yao and Shun — his rule being to accept only what a trusted book did not contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By that rule he kept two treatises on the five emperors, which the <i>Chunqiu</i> and the <i>Guoyu</i> leave standing, and refused the <i>Shanhaijing</i> outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book, compiled between the 4th and 1st centuries BCE, holds more myth by itself than all the other classics together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Three Sovereigns were set in front of his work eight centuries later by Sima Zhen, whose annals credit Fuxi with marriage rites, with nets for hunting and fishing, and with officials named after dragons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The eight-figure arrangement came out of five-phases cosmology: once the elements were held to generate one another rather than conquer, Huangdi could no longer stand first and three sovereigns were placed before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Zhen had to move Nüwa into Huangdi’s old place to manage it, and admitted the triad fitted the scheme badly, she and Fuxi both answering to wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two belong together in any case, and appear on 2nd-century-CE reliefs in Shandong as a man and a woman with their serpent tails entwined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Traditional chronologies put Yao’s accession at 2357 BCE and at 2145, and every disagreement between them falls before 841 BCE, the earliest year Chinese dating fixes securely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the reigns preserve is the mythic content of ancestral legend embedded in the Confucian classics,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and a conception of legitimate rule rooted in myth ran on from them through the whole of Chinese history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors","questions":["Sima Qian opened his history with only five of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a Tang commentator prefixed the other three to the book eight centuries afterwards.","Five-phases cosmology reshaped the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the elements were held to generate one another, so Huangdi lost first place and three predecessors were found for him."],"tags":["people","mythology","history","china","text"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxiii–clxxxv, cxcii and ccxv–ccxvi, and Annales des Trois Souverains, 3–7, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Vérité historique et langage de l’histoire en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 13–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.931. [Open access]","Jean Levi, review of Rémi Mathieu, <i>Étude sur la mythologie et l’ethnologie de la Chine ancienne</i>, <i>L’Homme</i> 27, no. 101 (1987): 146–48, https://www.persee.fr/doc/hom_0439-4216_1987_num_27_101_368774. [Open access]","Françoise Lauwaert, “Le saint, le boiteux et l’héritier. À propos de la fonction impériale en Chine,” <i>L’Homme</i> 38, no. 148 (1998): 79–98, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1998.370576. [Open access]","Marc Kalinowski, “Mythe, cosmogénèse et théogonie dans la Chine ancienne,” <i>L’Homme</i> 36, no. 137 (1996): 41–60, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1996.370035. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Yellow_emperor_Wu_Family_Shrine.jpg","title":"Three Sovereigns and Five Emperors","desc":"Rubbing of the relief on stone walls in Wu Family Shrine (ACE2nd century). The Yellow Emperor, one of the mythical Five Sovereigns. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_emperor_Wu_Family_Shrine.jpg","alt":"Yellow Emperor, Wu Family Shrine"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Sima Qian begin with five rulers and not eight?","a":"Because his rule was to accept only what a trusted book did not contradict, so he kept the treatises the Chunqiu and the Guoyu leave standing and refused the Shanhaijing outright."},{"q":"Why were three sovereigns placed in front of the emperors later?","a":"Because once the five phases were held to generate one another rather than conquer, Huangdi could no longer stand first, so the arrangement had to be extended backwards."},{"q":"Why did the compiler admit his own triad fitted badly?","a":"Because he had to move Nüwa into Huangdi's old place to make the scheme work, and she and Fuxi both answered to wood."}]},
{"id":"cnh-003","num":3,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Heaven and earth lay mingled like a hen's egg until <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew inside it for 18,000 years, pushing the sky up by one <i>zhang</i> every day.","questions":["Chinese tradition holds that the sacred peaks were the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the sun and moon his eyes, and the human race the creatures that had lived on his body.","No text of the Han period or earlier mentions <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose creation story first appears in the 3rd century CE and was doubted by the very source that records it."],"answer":"Pangu","answerText":"Pangu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chaos endured</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18,000 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest witness</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wei Zhao, c. 204 – 273 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fullest account</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Xu Zheng, 3rd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"盤古","hanzi":"盘古","pinyin":"Pángǔ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Pangu</b> is the giant of Chinese myth whose growth forced heaven and earth apart and whose body, at his death, became the features of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No surviving text of the Warring States, Qin or Han periods names him, and the first written versions belong to the 3rd century CE, more than a thousand years after Chinese records become continuously datable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest witness is a fragment of Wei Zhao's lost <i>Dongji</i>, preserved in a Tang Buddhist commentary, which reports what people commonly said and dismisses it as resting on no historical record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fullest early account is Xu Zheng's <i>Sanwu liji</i>: heaven and earth lay mingled like a hen's egg, Pangu grew inside for 18,000 years, and sky, earth and giant each gained a <i>zhang</i> a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That egg is the image of the <i>huntian</i> cosmology of his own century, which held that heaven encloses the earth as a shell encloses the yolk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second strand, known best from the <i>Shuyi ji</i>, tells what became of him: his head made the sacred peaks, his eyes the sun and moon, his blood the rivers, and the creatures on his body the human race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its age is unsettled, and one work long quoted for it looks instead like a Daoist compilation assembled centuries after Xu Zheng.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Daoists of the Six Dynasties made Pangu a god in his own right, identifying him with Yuanshi tianzun, one of the three highest Daoist deities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His origin is disputed: the name has been read as a form of Panhu, a dog ancestor honoured in the south, and as an Indian import, while oral versions among Miao and Yao communities point to a life outside the written record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Official history kept its distance, since Sima Qian began with the Five Emperors and the Tang supplement on the Three Sovereigns passes him over, but private histories from the Song made him the first ruler, and his temples stand from Lingnan to Hebei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]","Wu Xiaodong, “The Rhinoceros Totem and Pangu Myth: An Exploration of the Archetype of Pangu,” <i>Oral Tradition</i> 16, no. 2 (2001): 364–80, https://journal.oraltradition.org/wp-content/uploads/files/articles/16ii/Wu.pdf. [Open access]","Marc Kalinowski, “Mythe, cosmogénèse et théogonie dans la Chine ancienne,” <i>L’Homme</i> 36, no. 137 (1996): 41–60, https://doi.org/10.3406/hom.1996.370035. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxiii–clxxxv, and Annales des Trois Souverains, 3–7, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Vérité historique et langage de l’histoire en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 13–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.931. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Pangu.jpg","title":"Pangu","desc":"A portrait of Pangu from the Sancai Tuhui, an illustrated encyclopedia compiled by Wang Qi and printed in 1609. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pangu.jpg","alt":"Woodblock portrait of Pangu, headed with his name in Chinese"},"why":[{"q":"Why is this figure treated as a late arrival?","a":"Because no surviving text of the Warring States, Qin or Han names him, and the first written versions belong to the 3rd century CE."},{"q":"Why does the earliest witness dismiss what it reports?","a":"Because the fragment records what people commonly said and holds that it rests on no historical record."},{"q":"Why does the egg image tie the story to its own century?","a":"Because it is the picture of the huntian cosmology of that time, which held that heaven encloses the earth as a shell encloses the yolk."}]},
{"id":"cnh-004","num":4,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Carved on shrine walls and coffin lids across Han China, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Fuxi appear with human faces and the bodies of snakes, their tails twined together.","questions":["When the sky broke it was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who patched it, and she who is said to have made the first people by moulding them out of yellow earth.","To complete the Three Sovereigns a Tang commentator slipped <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between Fuxi and Shennong, then admitted the triad fitted the five phases badly."],"answer":"Nüwa","answerText":"Nüwa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Han tomb murals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">86 – 49 BCE, at Luoyang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wu Liang Shrine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd century CE, in Shandong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buddhist jewel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">435 CE, at Pingcheng</span></div>","traditional":"女媧","hanzi":"女娲","pinyin":"Nǚwā","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Nüwa</b> is the goddess of Chinese myth who made the first people by moulding them out of yellow earth, and who patched the sky when it broke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is almost always shown beside Fuxi, the two with human faces and the bodies of snakes, their tails sometimes twined together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pair are among the figures most often carved in Han funerary art, on the walls of ancestral shrines, on stone coffins and across the domed ceilings of tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the Wu Liang Shrine in Shandong, Fuxi holds a carpenter's square for the order he brings the human world, and a small boy between them stands for the children the two give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet the two began apart: the texts that first name them keep them independent, and the <i>Huainanzi</i> is where they are first named in one passage, without their relationship being described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their pairing is credited to a change in Han religion that gave female deities male companions, and the couple were also recorded as the gods of the sun and the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In China proper she alone fights the flood and repairs the world, while among peoples of the south a brother and sister survive it to found a new humanity, and that pair is often Fuxi and Nüwa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A silk manuscript from a Chu tomb at Changsha has the two of them beget four gods who part heaven from earth, set the seasons and lay out the mountains and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Her standing rose as she was written into the dynastic past: Ban Gu made her Fuxi's subordinate, Zheng Xuan raised her to one of the Three Sovereigns, and Sima Zhen slipped her between Fuxi and Shennong to complete that triad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Her image outlasted the Han, travelling to the tombs of Gansu and Turfan, and by 435 CE it had reached Pingcheng, where she and Fuxi hold a Buddhist wish-granting jewel between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Jinchao Zhao, “Integration and Transformation: A Study of the Sun and the Moon Depicted in the Imagery of Fuxi and Nüwa,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 13–45, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.13-45. [Open access]","Fan Zhang, “Chinese-Buddhist Encounter: Synthesis of Fuxi-Nüwa and Cintamani in Early Medieval Chinese Art,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 87–111, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.87-111. [Open access]","Max Kaltenmark, “La légende de la ville immergée en Chine,” <i>Cahiers d’Extrême-Asie</i> 1 (1985): 1–10, https://doi.org/10.3406/asie.1985.855. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, ccxiv–ccxv, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Xiaoyuncong_nvwa.jpg","title":"Nüwa","desc":"Nüwa, from Xiao Yuncong's illustrations to the Heavenly Questions, a Qing woodblock imprint held by the National Palace Museum, Taipei. Xiao Yuncong, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xiaoyuncong_nvwa.jpg","alt":"Woodblock print of Nüwa, serpent-tailed, rising among flames and broken rock"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the famous pairing not original to either figure?","a":"Because the texts that first name them keep them independent, and the Huainanzi is where they first appear in one passage without their relationship being described."},{"q":"Why did the two come to be shown together so constantly?","a":"Because the pairing is credited to a change in Han religion that gave female deities male companions, and they were also recorded as the gods of the sun and the moon."},{"q":"Why did her standing rise over the centuries?","a":"Because she was written into the dynastic past — made Fuxi's subordinate by Ban Gu, raised to the Three Sovereigns by Zheng Xuan, and slipped between Fuxi and Shennong by Sima Zhen."}]},
{"id":"cnh-005","num":5,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Looking up at the sky and down at the earth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have drawn the eight trigrams, three stacked lines each unbroken for yang or broken for yin.","questions":["A Tang commentator gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a snake's body, writing to replace knotted cords, the rite of marriage, and officials named after the dragon whose omen he received.","Tradition hands the 64 hexagrams to King Wen of Zhou and hands the eight trigrams under them to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who made the figures but not the book."],"answer":"Fuxi","answerText":"Fuxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 3322 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Han carvings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st – 2nd century CE, in Henan and Shandong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang supplement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sima Zhen, early 8th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"伏羲","hanzi":"伏羲","pinyin":"Fúxī","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Fuxi</b> is the culture hero of Chinese myth who is credited with the eight trigrams, and the figure with whom the received account of the Chinese past effectively begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> has him look up at the forms in the sky, look down at the patterns on the earth, and study the markings of birds and beasts before he draws the figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A trigram is three stacked lines, each of them either unbroken for yang or broken for yin, and the eight combinations were taken to hold the workings of the whole world in shorthand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage credits him with knotting cords into nets for hunting and fishing, which is one of the explanations offered for his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Tang commentator gathers much more: he replaced knotted cords with writing, set the rite by which a man and a woman married, and named his officials after the dragon whose omen he had received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That commentator also makes him the son of a woman who conceived after walking in a giant's footprints, gives him a snake's body and a human head, and has him succeed the fire-bringer Suiren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition that hands him the trigrams stops short of handing him the book: the 64 hexagrams and the words hung on them went to King Wen of Zhou and his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He could also stand where Pangu stands, and one Han apocryphon opens its account of the parting of heaven and earth with him and never mentions Pangu at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Han funerary carving he holds a carpenter's square for the order he brings the human world, and he is nearly always shown beside Nüwa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two were separate in the texts that name them earliest, so the couple is a creation of the Han, whose image later travelled west to the tombs of Gansu and Turfan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 11–12, 382–83, 423, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, 5–7, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Jinchao Zhao, “Integration and Transformation: A Study of the Sun and the Moon Depicted in the Imagery of Fuxi and Nüwa,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 13–45, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.13-45. [Open access]","Fan Zhang, “Chinese-Buddhist Encounter: Synthesis of Fuxi-Nüwa and Cintamani in Early Medieval Chinese Art,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 7, no. 2 (2019): 87–111, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2019.7.2.87-111. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/%E4%BC%8F%E7%BE%B2%E6%B0%8F%E7%95%AB%E5%85%AB%E5%8D%A6%EF%BC%88%E5%BB%BF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%B2%E9%80%9A%E4%BF%97%E8%A1%8D%E7%BE%A9%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"Fuxi draws the eight trigrams","desc":"Fuxi draws the eight trigrams, an illustration from the Nianyishi tongsu yanyi, a popular retelling of the dynastic histories printed in 1736. Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BC%8F%E7%BE%B2%E6%B0%8F%E7%95%AB%E5%85%AB%E5%8D%A6%EF%BC%88%E5%BB%BF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%B2%E9%80%9A%E4%BF%97%E8%A1%8D%E7%BE%A9%EF%BC%89.jpg","alt":"Woodblock print of Fuxi at a table bearing the eight-trigram diagram, a dragon-horse rising from the river below"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the trigrams stop short of the book they belong to?","a":"Because the tradition that hands him the eight figures gives the 64 hexagrams and the words hung on them to King Wen of Zhou and his son."},{"q":"Why are his inventions described as read off the world?","a":"Because the Great Appendix has him look up at the forms in the sky, look down at the patterns on the earth, and study the markings of birds and beasts before drawing the figures."},{"q":"Why is his pairing with Nüwa credited to the Han?","a":"Because the two were separate in the texts that name them earliest, and the couple only afterwards travelled west to the tombs of Gansu and Turfan."}]},
{"id":"cnh-006","num":6,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Wood cut into a share and bent into a handle: <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> taught ploughing and weeding to all under heaven, and set the market at midday so that each got what he wanted.","questions":["The herbal that bears the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lists 365 drugs, one for each day of the year, though its contents show it was compiled under the Han.","With the body of a man and the head of an ox, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reigned by the power of fire and rules the three months of summer in the ritual calendar."],"answer":"Shennong","answerText":"Shennong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 BCE or earlier, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">120 years, then buried at Changsha</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Herbal ascribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">365 drugs, compiled under the Han</span></div>","traditional":"神農","hanzi":"神农","pinyin":"Shénnóng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Shennong</b>, the Divine Husbandman, is the culture hero of Chinese myth who taught farming, and he stands second among the Three Sovereigns in the commonest reckoning of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> says that after Fuxi died he arose, cut wood into a ploughshare and bent wood into a plough-handle, and taught ploughing and weeding to all under heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage gives him the midday market, where people brought their goods together, made their exchanges and withdrew, each having got what he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It presents both as ideas taken from the hexagrams rather than as inventions of his own wit, which is how that text accounts for most of what the early sovereigns did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Tang commentator supplies the rest of the portrait: he had the body of a man and the head of an ox, reigned by the power of fire, and named his officials after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That account has him whip the plants and trees with a red lash, test the hundred kinds of plant himself, and so find the first drugs that cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that tasting comes the <i>Shennong bencao jing</i>, the herbal bearing his name, which set out 365 drugs, one for each day of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The book is not his: its own contents show it was put together under the Han, and what is known of its drugs survives only in quotation inside a much later materia medica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ritual calendar sets him in the shape of the year as well, making him the divine ruler of the three months of summer, the season whose element is fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He is the one figure every version of the Three Sovereigns keeps, whether the other two are Fuxi and Huangdi, or Suiren and Fuxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, 13–15, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 383, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","Emil Bretschneider, <i>Botanicon Sinicum</i>, part 3, <i>Botanical Investigations into the Materia Medica of the Ancient Chinese</i> (Shanghai: Kelly and Walsh, 1895), 1–3, https://archive.org/details/botaniconsinicu01bretgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Lî Kî</i>, Sacred Books of the East 27 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1885), 268, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0027unse. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Xu_Jetian_-_Shennong.jpg","title":"Shennong","desc":"Shennong chewing a sprig of herbs, an eighteenth-century ink painting on silk held by the Bibliothèque nationale de France; the inscription names him Yandi, king by the virtue of fire. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xu_Jetian_-_Shennong.jpg","alt":"Ink painting of a horned Shennong, dressed in leaves, chewing a sprig of herbs"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the Book of Changes present his plough as an idea rather than an invention?","a":"Because that text accounts for most of what the early sovereigns did as ideas taken from the hexagrams, the midday market included."},{"q":"Why is the herbal that bears his name not his?","a":"Because its own contents show it was put together under the Han, and what is known of its drugs survives only in quotation inside a much later materia medica."},{"q":"Why does the ritual calendar place him in summer?","a":"Because he reigned by the power of fire, and summer is the season whose element is fire."}]},
{"id":"cnh-007","num":7,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"Bears, leopards, panthers, lynxes and tigers were trained by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who beat Yandi at Banquan in three engagements before the lords made him Son of Heaven.","questions":["Sima Qian opened his history on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than anyone older, saying that what the hundred schools told of him was not fit for a gentleman to repeat.","The ritual calendar puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the centre between the four seasons, ruling by the power of earth, whose colour is the one his name carries."],"answer":"Yellow Emperor","answerText":"Yellow Emperor","answerDate":"","traditional":"黃帝","hanzi":"黄帝","pinyin":"Huángdì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Yellow Emperor</b>, Huangdi, is the figure with whom Chinese history was made to begin: Sima Qian opened the <i>Shiji</i> on the Annals of the Five Emperors, and the first of the five is he.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He is given as a son of Shaodian, of the family Gongsun, and called Xuanyuan after the hill where his mother bore him, having conceived on seeing lightning circle the Great Bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his time the house of Shennong had grown weak and the lords fought one another, so they took refuge with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He trained bears, leopards, panthers, lynxes and tigers, beat Yandi at Banquan in three engagements and destroyed Chiyou at Zhuolu, and the lords then raised him to Son of Heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name is a colour: the omen of his reign was the power of earth, whose colour is yellow, and he named his officials after the clouds as Fuxi had named his after dragons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The ritual calendar sets him at the centre, between the four seasons, where the emperor wears yellow robes, rides yellow horses and eats from round vessels made to resemble the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Great Appendix passes over him quickly, granting him with Yao and Shun only the upper and lower garments, the canoe and the oar, the ox and the horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian left out the inventions the legends heap on his reign, and said why: the <i>Book of Documents</i> records only from Yao onward, and what the hundred schools told of Huangdi was not fit for a gentleman to repeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Every ruler after him in that chapter is his descendant, Zhuanxu his grandson and Ku his great-grandson, then Yao and Shun, so the whole line was made to begin in one man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of it is dated, and Sima Qian knew it: he let his exact chronology reach back only to 841 BCE and counted by generations before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxvii–cxc, and 25–39, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 383–85, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Lî Kî</i>, Sacred Books of the East 27 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1885), 280–81, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0027unse. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Vérité historique et langage de l’histoire en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 13–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.931. [Open access]","呂宗力 (Lü Zongli), “盤古與中國的開闢神話,” <i>文化雜誌 / Revista de Cultura</i>, no. 105 (Macao: Instituto Cultural, 2019), 72–95, https://www.icm.gov.mo/rc/viewer/pdfViewerParts/10105/2556. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Yellow_Emperor_%28Huangdi%29.jpg","title":"The Yellow Emperor","desc":"Portraits of Famous Men: Yellow Emperor (Huangdi), ink and colour on silk mounted as an album leaf, by an unknown Chinese artist, held by the Philadelphia Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Yellow_Emperor_(Huangdi).jpg","alt":"Album-leaf portrait of the Yellow Emperor in the flat imperial crown with beaded tassels"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Sima Qian leave out the inventions credited to this reign?","a":"Because the Book of Documents records only from Yao onward, and what the hundred schools told of him was not fit for a gentleman to repeat."},{"q":"Why is his name a colour?","a":"Because the omen of his reign was the power of earth, whose colour is yellow, and he named his officials after the clouds as Fuxi had after dragons."},{"q":"Why does the whole line of emperors begin in one man?","a":"Because every ruler after him in that chapter is made his descendant, Zhuanxu his grandson and Ku his great-grandson, then Yao and Shun."}]},
{"id":"cnh-008","num":8,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"The <i>Book of Documents</i> says that, according to the teachings of antiquity, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first to produce disorder, which spread until the quiet people became robbers and murderers.","questions":["Sima Qian allows only four lines to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was the most terrible and remained invincible until the Yellow Emperor seized him and put him to death at Zhuolu.","A lost book quoted by a Tang scholar gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 81 brothers with beasts' bodies, heads of bronze and foreheads of iron, who ate sand and forged the first weapons."],"answer":"Chiyou","answerText":"Chiyou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2700 – 2600 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Sima Qian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 lines, then killed at Zhuolu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In legend</span><span class=\"dt-v\">81 brothers with heads of bronze</span></div>","traditional":"蚩尤","hanzi":"蚩尤","pinyin":"Chīyóu","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Chiyou</b> is the rebel the Yellow Emperor destroyed at Zhuolu, and the oldest name the <i>Book of Documents</i> records: it says that, according to the teachings of antiquity, he was the first to produce disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That disorder spread among the quiet, orderly people until all became robbers and murderers, and among the people of Miao the five punishments were turned into engines of oppression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> James Legge noted that no name older than Yao is mentioned anywhere else in that collection, and that so cursory a reference is itself evidence it is a genuine record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian allows him four lines: while Xuanyuan drilled the lords with shield and lance, Chiyou was the most terrible and remained invincible, until he made trouble, disobeyed the imperial orders, and was seized and killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he was is never said, and the <i>Rites of Dai the Elder</i> calls him an ambitious man sprung from the people, where Chavannes read Sima Qian's own wording to mean a lord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Everything monstrous about him is later, and reaches us in fragments, Chinese myth having survived sporadically and in pieces rather than as epic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tang scholar Zhang Shoujie quotes a lost book for 81 brothers all called Chiyou, with beasts' bodies and human voices, heads of bronze and foreheads of iron, who ate sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They made the five kinds of weapon, swords and lances and great crossbows, and killed without reason, where the Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> gives the bow and arrow to the sages as a benefit producing awe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In that same account the Yellow Emperor could not put him down by goodness and justice, so he sighed at heaven, which sent down a dark woman with a sealed writing on war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stayed useful after death: his image was drawn to frighten the empire into obedience, and Tang, founder of Shang, named his rebellion as the precedent for punishing lords who would not serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 27–29, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 27 and 255, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 384–85, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), 190, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Écriture et littérature en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 8 (1986): 53–66, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1986.927. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C%E7%9C%81%E6%B2%82%E5%8D%97%E5%8E%BF_%E6%B1%89%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F%E7%9F%B3_%E8%9A%A9%E5%B0%A4%E4%BA%94%E5%85%B5.jpg","title":"Chiyou and the five weapons","desc":"Rubbing of a Han pictorial stone from Yinan, Shandong, showing Chiyou with the five weapons: a bow and arrows above his head, blades in both hands and swords sprouting from his limbs. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C%E7%9C%81%E6%B2%82%E5%8D%97%E5%8E%BF_%E6%B1%89%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F%E7%9F%B3_%E8%9A%A9%E5%B0%A4%E4%BA%94%E5%85%B5.jpg","alt":"Rubbing of a Han stone relief: a squat beast-faced figure with a bow and arrows above his head and blades sprouting from his arms and legs"},"why":[{"q":"Why does a cursory mention count as evidence of a genuine record?","a":"Because no name older than Yao is mentioned anywhere else in that collection, so the brevity reads as an old notice rather than an invention."},{"q":"Why is everything monstrous about him late?","a":"Because the earliest notices say only that he made disorder and was killed, and the bronze heads and 81 brothers reach us in fragments quoted by a Tang scholar."},{"q":"Why was he useful to the state after his defeat?","a":"Because his image was drawn to frighten the empire into obedience, and Tang named his rebellion as the precedent for punishing lords who would not serve."}]},
{"id":"cnh-009","num":9,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the lords raised Xuanyuan to Son of Heaven in place of the house of Shennong, so the fight is what makes the Yellow Emperor emperor.","questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Yellow Emperor had already beaten Yandi in three engagements at Banquan, a field the old accounts put only a few li away.","Sima Qian gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two lines, saying only that Chiyou disobeyed, that the lords were called to arms, and that he was seized there and put to death."],"answer":"Battle of Zhuolu","answerText":"Battle of Zhuolu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in a reign of c. 2697 – 2599 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Two battles</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 rounds at Banquan, then Zhuolu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named as an omen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the Zuo Commentary, Duke Xi, year 25</span></div>","traditional":"涿鹿之戰","hanzi":"涿鹿之战","pinyin":"Zhuōlù zhī zhàn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Zhuolu</b> is the fight in which the Yellow Emperor destroyed Chiyou, and in the <i>Shiji</i> it is the act that makes him emperor: the lords raised Xuanyuan to Son of Heaven after it, in place of the house of Shennong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian allows it two lines: Chiyou made trouble and disobeyed the imperial orders, so Huangdi called the lords to arms, gave battle in the country of Zhuolu, seized him and put him to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the second of a pair, easily run together, since he had already beaten Yandi in three engagements at Banquan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fields are neighbours, Banquan being the spring of Mount Ban, one li from Zhuolu by some accounts and five by others, and Zhuolu itself was Huangdi's capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commentators place it south of Baoan in Xuanhua, in what is now Hebei, on a headwater of the Yongding river, in a reign some chronologies put between 2697 and 2599 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Neither the <i>Book of Documents</i> nor the <i>Book of Songs</i> so much as names Huangdi, and what the Documents does notice is the loser, saying that according to the teachings of antiquity Chiyou was the first to produce disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting is old in the record all the same: a diviner in the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> consults the tortoise-shell for the marquis of Jin and calls the answer auspicious, being the omen of the Yellow Emperor's battle at Banquan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was therefore famous long before Sima Qian wrote, and Chavannes concluded that the legends of this sovereign are ancient whatever the classics omit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Legge observed that other writers tell the struggle quite differently, with dragons, mists and the invention of the compass playing conspicuous parts, which is the sort of material Chinese myth preserves only in fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Victory brought no rest: Huangdi hunted down whoever would not submit, cut passages through the mountains, opened roads, and went east to the sea, west to Kongtong and north against the Xunyu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 27–31, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 27 and 255, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ew, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), 195–96, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.60723. [Open access]","Térence Billeter, “Un ancêtre légendaire au service du nationalisme chinois,” <i>Perspectives chinoises</i> 47 (1998): 46–53, https://doi.org/10.3406/perch.1998.2298. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “Écriture et littérature en Chine,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 8 (1986): 53–66, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1986.927. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/%E6%B2%B3%E5%8C%97%E6%B6%BF%E9%B9%BF%E2%80%94%E9%BB%84%E5%B8%9D%E5%9F%8E_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The plain at Zhuolu","desc":"The plain and hills at Zhuolu in Hebei, where the battle is traditionally placed, seen from the terrace of the modern memorial hall built there to the Yellow Emperor. 中国郑开亮, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B2%B3%E5%8C%97%E6%B6%BF%E9%B9%BF%E2%80%94%E9%BB%84%E5%B8%9D%E5%9F%8E_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A paved terrace with a bronze incense burner and rows of banners, looking out over a wooded plain to a long line of hills"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the fight known to be older than the history that records it?","a":"Because a diviner in the Zuo Commentary calls a tortoise-shell answer auspicious, being the omen of the Yellow Emperor's battle at Banquan, so it was famous long before Sima Qian wrote."},{"q":"Why are the two battles of that reign easily confused?","a":"Because the fields are neighbours, Banquan being the spring of Mount Ban, one li from Zhuolu by some accounts and five by others, and Zhuolu was the emperor's own capital."},{"q":"Why do the classics not support the account we have?","a":"Because neither the Book of Documents nor the Book of Songs so much as names the victor, and what the Documents notices is the loser, called the first to produce disorder."}]},
{"id":"cnh-010","num":10,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"The <i>Book of Documents</i> opens on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who is called reverential, intelligent, accomplished and thoughtful, naturally and without effort, his bright influence reaching all four quarters of the land.","questions":["Told by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a round year is 366 days, the Xi and He brothers were to square it with the four seasons by an intercalary month.","Seventy years on the throne, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put aside his own son as insincere and quarrelsome and took Shun, an unmarried man among the lower people, in his place."],"answer":"Yao","answerText":"Yao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Received date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">accession 2357 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70 years before seeking a successor</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Year length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">366 days, with an intercalary month</span></div>","traditional":"堯","hanzi":"尧","pinyin":"Yáo","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Yao</b> is the sage-king with whom the <i>Book of Documents</i> begins, and the oldest ruler it describes: its first chapter, the Canon of Yao, opens by examining into antiquity and finding him styled Fangxun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is reverential, intelligent, accomplished and thoughtful, naturally and without effort, the bright influence of those qualities felt through the four quarters and above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He harmonised the nine classes of his kindred, polished his own people and united the myriad states, which is rule by example: the Great Appendix to the <i>Book of Changes</i> says he and Shun simply wore their robes and good order followed everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sima Qian's portrait is more concrete: goodness like heaven's, knowledge a god's, near at hand like the sun and from afar like a cloud, in a yellow cap and a plain robe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The one office the Canon gives him is the calendar: he set the Xi and He brothers at the four quarters to watch the sun, moon and stars, and told them a round year is 366 days, squared by an intercalary month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Then the flood, whose waters in their vast extent embrace the hills and overtop the great heights, with Gun employed over Yao's own objection, laboured nine years without accomplishing the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seventy years on the throne, Yao sought a successor, put his own son aside as insincere and quarrelsome, and took Shun, an unmarried man among the lower people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That refusal is what the tradition kept: Confucius called him great as a sovereign, matched only by heaven, his virtue so vast the people could find no name for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> denies it was Yao's to give, answering that the sovereign cannot give the throne to another and that heaven gave it to Shun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The one testable thing in the Canon is the stars, since precession was unknown there until long after Yao's supposed date and the four culminating stars he names could not have been computed backwards; they fit the 24th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 24–28 and 32–39, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 42–74, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 383–84, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 78, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 354, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Ma_Lin_-_Emperor_Yao.jpg/1280px-Ma_Lin_-_Emperor_Yao.jpg","title":"Emperor Yao","desc":"Yao as imagined by the Song painter Ma Lin, a hanging scroll in colour on silk in the National Palace Museum, Taipei; the inscription calls his virtue lofty and says he governed by letting his robes hang down. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ma_Lin_-_Emperor_Yao.jpg","alt":"Painted hanging scroll of a bearded man in a red and dark robe and a small black cap, one hand raised, with an inscription in the silk above him"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the calendar the one office his canon describes?","a":"Because he set the Xi and He brothers at the four quarters to watch the sun, moon and stars, and told them a round year is 366 days, squared by an intercalary month."},{"q":"Why is passing over his own son what the tradition kept?","a":"Because after seventy years he judged the son insincere and quarrelsome and took Shun, an unmarried man among the lower people, which is what Confucius praised as virtue past naming."},{"q":"Why are the stars the only testable thing in the canon?","a":"Because precession was unknown there until long after his supposed date, so the four culminating stars he names could not have been computed backwards, and they fit the 24th century BCE."}]},
{"id":"cnh-011","num":11,"category":"Chinese mythology","question":"The <i>Book of Documents</i> gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a canon of his own, opening on a man styled Chonghua who was profound, wise, accomplished and intelligent, mild and courteous and truly sincere.","questions":["Sent to plaster the granary roof, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came down on two broad rush hats when his father set fire to it beneath him, and later escaped a filled-in well by a side passage.","Confucius asked whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> might not be instanced as having governed efficiently without exertion, doing nothing but gravely and reverently occupying his imperial seat."],"answer":"Shun","answerText":"Shun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Received date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">accession 2255 BCE, by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Called to office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at 30, then 30 years with Yao</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Banished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 criminals, to the four frontiers</span></div>","traditional":"舜","hanzi":"舜","pinyin":"Shùn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Shun</b> is the commoner Yao raised to the throne, and the <i>Book of Documents</i> gives him a canon of his own, opening on a man styled Chonghua, profound, wise, accomplished and intelligent, mild and courteous and truly sincere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian makes the household murderous: his father Gusou blind, his mother dead, his half-brother Xiang arrogant, and Gusou forever seeking to kill him, while Shun served all three scrupulously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twice they tried in earnest: they fired the granary roof he was plastering, and he came down on two broad rush hats; they filled in a well he was digging, and he left by a side passage he had cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is also the man things gathered around: wherever he settled, a village formed in a year, a town in two and a city in three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yao tested him with his own two daughters, and in the great plains at the foot of the mountains, where the tempests of wind, thunder and rain did not make him go astray.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The canon then reads like an inventory of government: he regulated the pitch-pipes and the measures of length, capacity and weight, divided the land into twelve provinces, and toured the four quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He banished four criminals to the frontiers, among them Gun, who had failed for nine years against the flood and was held prisoner till death, and all under heaven acknowledged the justice of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He appointed the ministers the tradition remembers by name, Yu over the works, Qi over the fields, Gao Yao over crime and Kui over the music, and had them examined every three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confucius made him the model of effortless rule, asking whether Shun might not be instanced as governing efficiently without exertion, doing nothing but gravely occupying his seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> says plainly that he was born near the wild tribes of the east, and the Bamboo Books, recovered from a tomb in the 3rd century CE, date his succession to Yao's 73rd year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 38–44, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 12 and 72–83, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 159, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 316, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fb/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Shun.jpg","title":"Emperor Shun","desc":"Portraits of Famous Men: Emperor Shun, an album leaf by an unknown Chinese artist, held by the Philadelphia Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Shun.jpg","alt":"Album-leaf portrait bust of Shun in the flat imperial crown with beaded tassels, in blue and green robes"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the tradition dwell on his murderous household?","a":"Because he served a father who sought to kill him, a dead mother's place and an arrogant half-brother scrupulously, escaping a fired granary and a filled-in well."},{"q":"Why does his canon read like an inventory of government?","a":"Because it has him regulate the pitch-pipes and the measures, divide the land into twelve provinces, tour the four quarters, banish four criminals and appoint ministers examined every three years."},{"q":"Why did Confucius make him the model of effortless rule?","a":"Because he asks whether Shun might not be instanced as governing efficiently without exertion, doing nothing but gravely occupying his seat."}]},
{"id":"cnh-012","num":12,"category":"Mythology","question":"The tradition credits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with carriages on land, boats on water, sledges in icy places and spiked shoes for the hills, and with passing his own door three times without going in.","questions":["Shun is said to have handed the throne to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose survey divided the land into nine provinces and set each one's tribute by the quality of its soil.","When the princes went to the son of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than to the minister he had named, the sequence of abdications ended and the throne began passing from father to son."],"answer":"Yu the Great","answerText":"Yu the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Received date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Xia opens 2205 BCE, or 1989 BCE by another reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17, in both received systems</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Shû King</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 provinces surveyed and graded by soil</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Succeeded by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">his son Qi, not by Yi, who served 7 years</span></div>","traditional":"大禹","hanzi":"大禹","pinyin":"Dà Yǔ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Yu the Great</b> is the figure on whom the Chinese mythical sequence closes and the dynastic record is said to open: the man who drained the flood, surveyed what was left and passed the throne to his son. The <i>Shû King</i> has him describing his own labours, in which he mounted four conveyances — carriages on land, boats on water, sledges in icy places and shoes spiked for the hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He opened passages for the streams throughout the nine provinces and led them to the sea, while one colleague taught the people to take fish and birds and another to sow grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is quoted saying that he stayed with his wife four days after marrying, and heard his infant son wailing without turning aside; the tradition adds that he passed his own door three times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confucius could find no flaw in him: coarse food, poor clothing and a low house, with all his strength spent on the ditches and water channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The survey credited to him is the Tribute of Yü, which divides the land into nine provinces, grades the soil of each and fixes what it owes accordingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Zuo Commentary adds that at the height of Xia virtue the provinces sent in their metal, nine great cauldrons were cast, and the strange creatures of each region were figured on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then comes the break: Shun had presented Yu to Heaven and Yu presented his minister Yi, but when Yi withdrew after the mourning the princes went to Qi, saying he was their sovereign's son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Asked whether that meant Yu fell short of Yao and Shun, the <i>Mencius</i> answers that it did not — Yi had served seven years where the others served decades, Qi was able, and the change was Heaven's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The received chronologies open the Xia in 2205 or 1989 BCE and agree on seventeen kings, and Chavannes judged the whole stretch prehistory rather than history, rebuilt from scattered fragments with more art than science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 16–18 and 64–75, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 79, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Seuen, third year, 293, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 358–59, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), clxvi and cxc, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/King_Yu_of_Xia.jpg/1280px-King_Yu_of_Xia.jpg","title":"Yu the Great","desc":"Standing Portrait of King Yu of Xia, hanging scroll, colour on silk, by Ma Lin, 13th century. National Palace Museum, Taipei. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Yu_of_Xia.jpg","alt":"A standing figure in a long robe and a flat-topped ceremonial cap, holding a tablet, painted on silk."},"locator":{"name":"Xia dynasty","at":[113.0253,34.4553],"kind":"region","area":[[109.5,34],[110,36],[111.5,37],[113.5,36.5],[115,35.4],[115.3,34.2],[114,33.3],[112,33],[110.5,33.4]]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the survey credited to him grade the soil province by province?","a":"Because the Tribute of Yü divides the land into nine provinces and fixes what each owes according to the grade of its ground."},{"q":"Why did the succession pass to a son when it had twice passed to a stranger?","a":"Because his minister withdrew after the mourning and the princes went to Qi, saying he was their sovereign's son — a change the Mencius attributes to Heaven rather than to any falling off."},{"q":"Why does Chavannes call the whole stretch prehistory?","a":"Because it was rebuilt from scattered fragments with more art than science, and the received chronologies cannot even agree whether the dynasty opens in 2205 or 1989 BCE."}]},
{"id":"cnh-013","num":13,"category":"Mythology","question":"Snakes and dragons held the land in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and people nested in trees on the low ground and dug caves on the high until the channels were opened.","questions":["Gun laboured nine years without success in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was banished to a mountain to die, after which his son took up the same task and finished it.","No ark appears in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: the waters are not a punishment to be survived but a disorder of the river channels, to be dug open and led away to the sea."],"answer":"Chinese flood myth","answerText":"Chinese flood myth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Set in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the reigns of Yao and Shun, from 2357 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gun's attempt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 years, unaccomplished</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yu's labour</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 years by one account, 13 by another</span><span class=\"dt-k\">His own door</span><span class=\"dt-v\">passed 3 times, never entered</span></div>","traditional":"洪水神話","hanzi":"洪水神话","pinyin":"hóngshuǐ shénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Chinese flood myth</b> is the calamity that fills the reigns of Yao and Shun and closes the age of the sage rulers: a rising of the waters fought for a generation and at last drained away. The Canon of Yao has the sovereign asking for a man to correct it, describing waters that embrace the hills, overtop the great heights and threaten the heavens while the lower people groan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> adds what the land was like beneath them: snakes and dragons held it, and people nested in trees on the low ground and dug caves on the high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gun was put to the work over Yao's own objection that he rejected orders and was baleful to his colleagues, and after nine years the waters had not stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shun, inspecting, found his works unworthy of praise, banished him to Mount Yu to die there, and set his son to the same task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The remedy is not an ark but drainage: Yu dug open the obstructed channels and conducted the waters to the sea, driving the snakes and dragons into the marshes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Legge noted that the calamity is nowhere presented as a deluge or a sudden accumulation of water, but as the river channels being broken up and disordered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How long the work took is told differently: the <i>Mencius</i> has him eight years away from home, while the <i>Shiji</i> quotes the books of Xia for thirteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> 19th-century missionaries pressed an identification with Noah's flood that Legge himself accepted only as an imperfect echo and that Bunsen dismissed outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the tradition builds on the draining is administrative rather than moral — the survey of the land, its division into nine provinces and the fixing of tribute — and Confucius remembered the man who did it as one who spent all his strength on ditches and water channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 32–35 and 64–75, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 250–51 and 279–80, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), cxxx and 99–100, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 2, 73–74, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 79, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg/1280px-%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg","title":"Yu ordering the water works","desc":"Woodblock illustration from the Ming novel Youxia zhizhuan, 17th century, digitised in the Guben xiaoshuo jicheng. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A6%B9%E7%8E%8B%E5%88%86%E4%BB%98%E8%A1%86%E5%B0%87%E6%B2%BB%E6%B0%B4.jpg","alt":"Line woodcut in a circular frame: a seated ruler under a draped canopy gestures toward three kneeling officials, the panel title running down each side in characters."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the remedy drainage rather than escape?","a":"Because the calamity is not a deluge but the river channels being broken up and disordered, so Yu dug open the obstructed channels and conducted the waters to the sea."},{"q":"Why was the first man set to the work dismissed?","a":"Because Yao had objected that he rejected orders and was baleful to his colleagues, and after nine years the waters had not stopped, so Shun banished him to Mount Yu."},{"q":"Why did the identification with Noah's flood not hold?","a":"Because Legge himself accepted it only as an imperfect echo and Bunsen dismissed it outright, the Chinese account describing no sudden accumulation of water."}]},
{"id":"cnh-014","num":14,"category":"Mythology","question":"Relying on his archery, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> neglected the business of the people and gave himself to hunting, until the minister he had raised up turned the palace against him.","questions":["Two archers bear the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the record, one in the service of a legendary emperor and one who seized the throne of the Xia and then lost it.","Confucius's questioner named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a man skilful at archery who did not die a natural death, setting him against Yu and Ji, who worked the fields and won the empire."],"answer":"Houyi","answerText":"Houyi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ku from 2436 BCE, then Tai Kang from 2188 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held the throne</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 years, by one account</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Put away</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 good officers, for 1 flatterer</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Confucius names</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 men who did not die naturally</span></div>","traditional":"后羿","hanzi":"后羿","pinyin":"Hòu Yì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Houyi</b> is the archer of Chinese legend, and the name is more a title than a person: it is borne by a bowman serving the legendary emperor Ku and again by a descendant under Yao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is that descendant who is remembered for shooting into the sky, freeing the moon from an eclipse and scattering the false suns that were spoiling the crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The classics carry another of the name, the prince of Qiong, who in the Songs of the Five Sons blocks the return of the Xia king Tai Kang from a hundred-day hunt beyond the Lo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Zuo Commentary tells it at length: he took advantage of the people's discontent to supersede the line of Xia, then relied on his archery, neglected their business and gave himself to the beasts of the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He put away four good officers and made his chief minister Zhuo of Han, a slanderous man whose own house had cast him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Zhuo flattered within the palace and bribed without it, cajoled the people and encouraged the hunting, until inside and outside all were ready to have him instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Returning from the field one day he was killed by his own servants, who boiled him and gave his flesh to his sons; they could not eat it and died in the gate of Qiong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Zhuo took his wife and had by her the strong man Ao, and it ended only when Shao Kang was raised to the throne: the princes of Qiong perished, the commentary says, because they had lost the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Confucius made no reply when a questioner set that archery against the husbandry of Yu and Ji and observed that neither he nor Ao died a natural death, though he praised the man for esteeming virtue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Mencius</i> gives the killing instead to Feng Meng, a pupil who had learned the whole science and reckoned only his teacher above him — and holds the teacher partly to blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 268–69, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 78–79, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Seang, fourth year, 424, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 141, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 328, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_%28%E5%90%8E%E7%BE%BFcrop%29.jpg","title":"An archer in the hunt","desc":"Detail from an 18th-century painted album of exemplary imperial episodes, Recueil Historique des Principaux Traits de la Vie des Empereurs Chinois, Bibliotheque nationale de France. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_%28%E5%90%8E%E7%BE%BFcrop%29.jpg","alt":"Painted in colour on silk: a rider in a red robe on a white horse draws a bow and aims upward, a quiver at his back, with trees and other riders behind him."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this name more a title than a person?","a":"Because it is borne by a bowman serving the emperor Ku, by a descendant under Yao who shot the false suns, and by the prince of Qiong who usurped the Xia."},{"q":"Why did the prince of Qiong lose the throne he had taken?","a":"Because he relied on his archery, neglected the people's business and gave himself to hunting, so his own servants killed him and the commentary says his house perished for losing the people."},{"q":"Why does the Mencius hold the archer partly to blame for his death?","a":"Because he had taught Feng Meng the whole science, and the pupil then reckoned only his teacher above him."}]},
{"id":"cnh-015","num":15,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"The oldest surviving telling of the story, in Zhang Heng's <i>Ling Xian</i>, has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> steal the drug of immortality from her husband Houyi and flee to the moon.","questions":["A naming taboo under two Tang emperors turned Heng into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the name by which the woman in the moon has been known ever since.","Round cakes sold at the mid-autumn festival were painted with a white rabbit and a lunar beauty, whom buyers named for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, said to have fled to the moon."],"answer":"Chang'e","answerText":"Chang'e","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ku from 2436 BCE, as the wife of Houyi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Set down by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Zhang Heng, 78 – 139 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Name changed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under 2 Tang emperors who bore Heng</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Festival</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th month, from the 11th day to the 15th</span></div>","traditional":"嫦娥","hanzi":"嫦娥","pinyin":"Cháng'é","translations":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Chang'e</b> is the woman who lives in the moon, and the oldest surviving account of how she came to be there makes her a thief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The astronomer Zhang Heng, who lived from 78 to 139 CE, wrote in his <i>Ling Xian</i> that when Houyi begged the drug of immortality from the Queen Mother of the West, Heng E stole it and fled for refuge to the mansion of the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Houyi was her husband and the drug was his, so the story turns on a theft inside a marriage rather than on any quest of her own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arriving, she was not rewarded but transformed, becoming the chanchu, the toad whose outline Chinese observers traced on the face of the moon and which was thought to swallow it during an eclipse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same legend is carried in the <i>Huainanzi</i>, so more than one Han work preserved it. <br><br> Her name was not always Chang'e: it was Heng, taken from a line in the ancient odes praising a lady as being like the moon advancing to the full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was altered because two Tang emperors bore Heng as a personal name, and a naming taboo then put the character out of ordinary use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around her the moon filled with other tenants: a hare squatting at the foot of a cassia tree and pounding drugs for the immortals, and a man named Wu Kang condemned to hew that tree for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cassia was said to be most visible at mid-autumn, so taking a degree in the examinations held at that season was described as plucking a leaf from the cassia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Fuzhou in the 1860s the festival of the middle of autumn ran from the eleventh to the fifteenth of the eighth month, and the round cakes baked for it were painted with a white rabbit and a lunar beauty, whom people named after an ancient woman said to have visited the moon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 94, 30–31, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, 288–89, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), nos. 55 and 140, 22 and 53–54, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Religious Portions of the Shih King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 248, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 300, 94–95, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 64–66, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/%E5%8F%A4%E4%BB%8A%E7%99%BE%E7%BE%8E%E5%9C%96%E4%B9%8B%E5%AB%A6%E5%A8%A5%EF%BC%88One_hundred_beauties_Chang%27e%EF%BC%89.png/1280px-%E5%8F%A4%E4%BB%8A%E7%99%BE%E7%BE%8E%E5%9C%96%E4%B9%8B%E5%AB%A6%E5%A8%A5%EF%BC%88One_hundred_beauties_Chang%27e%EF%BC%89.png","title":"Chang'e in the moon","desc":"Drawing by Wu Youru, died about 1893, from the album Gujin baimei tu, published by Hunan Fine Arts Publishing House. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%A4%E4%BB%8A%E7%99%BE%E7%BE%8E%E5%9C%96%E4%B9%8B%E5%AB%A6%E5%A8%A5%EF%BC%88One_hundred_beauties_Chang%27e%EF%BC%89.png","alt":"Line drawing: a woman in flowing robes stands among banked clouds inside the disc of the full moon, beside a flowering tree and a railed terrace, with an inscription in characters at the upper left."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the oldest account make her a thief?","a":"Because the drug of immortality had been begged from the Queen Mother of the West by her husband, and she stole it and fled to the moon."},{"q":"Why was her name changed?","a":"Because two Tang emperors bore Heng as a personal name, and a naming taboo then put that character out of ordinary use."},{"q":"Why did examination success come to be called plucking a cassia leaf?","a":"Because the moon's cassia tree was said to be most visible at mid-autumn, which is the season the examinations were held in."}]},
{"id":"cnh-016","num":16,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"A giant who raced the sun, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drank the Yellow River and the Wei dry, set out north for the Great Marsh, and died of thirst on the road before he reached it.","questions":["The staff that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> threw down as he died became Deng Forest, which another chapter calls the Peach Forest, three hundred li around and full of horses.","Three characters added in a later chapter, that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not measure his strength, turn a giant keeping pace with the sun into one racing it and losing."],"answer":"Kuafu","answerText":"Kuafu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Yellow Emperor's age, c. 2697 BCE by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drank dry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 rivers, the Yellow River and the Wei</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deng Forest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">also called the Peach Forest, 300 li around</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descended from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Gonggong, 3 generations back</span></div>","traditional":"夸父","hanzi":"夸父","pinyin":"Kuāfù","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Kuafu</b> is a giant of Chinese myth who chased the sun and died of thirst, and his name describes rather than names him: kua means large and fu is a word for a man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> tells it in a few dozen characters: he ran with the sun and entered it, drank the Yellow River and the Wei dry, made north for the Great Marsh, died on the road, and his cast-off staff became Deng Forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same book makes him neither solitary nor obscure: he descends from Houtu, three generations below Gonggong, wears a yellow snake in each ear, and belongs to a country of large men east of Nie'er.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deng Forest is the Peach Forest that another chapter puts on Mount Kuafu, three hundred li around and full of horses, in the far west of modern Henan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yugu, where he overtakes the sun, is also Yuyuan, and the <i>Huainanzi</i>, assembled under the Han prince Liu An, who died in 122 BCE, says the sun reaching Yuyuan is what dusk means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The chapters of the great wilderness open the story with three characters the first telling lacks — that he did not measure his strength — which turn a man keeping pace with the sun into one racing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the usual reading now, though the older wording says only that he ran alongside the sun as far as Yugu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those chapters end it differently again: the winged dragon Yinglong, having killed Chiyou, killed Kuafu too, so the giant falls in the Yellow Emperor's war rather than to thirst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Liezi</i> carries the story as well, adding that the staff was soaked in the fat and flesh of the corpse before it grew, and that Deng Forest spread thousands of li.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book's own standing is disputed: Herbert Giles called it a forgery of the 1st or 2nd century CE, while his son Lionel held that a genuine core lay under later accretions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Zhao Kuifu 赵逵夫, “‘夸父逐日’神话的历史文化内涵” [The historical and cultural content of the ‘Kuafu chases the sun’ myth], <i>文学遗产</i> [Literary Heritage] 2020, no. 5, https://ir.nwnu.edu.cn/bitstream/39RV6HYL/61950/1/%E2%80%9C%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E9%80%90%E6%97%A5%E2%80%9D%E7%A5%9E%E8%AF%9D%E7%9A%84%E5%8E%86%E5%8F%B2%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%86%85%E6%B6%B5.pdf. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 412, 132–33, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Sun Shulin 孫樹林, “从古籍中的黄帝神话传说记载管窥古人之宇宙观” [The cosmology of the ancients seen through records of the Yellow Emperor myths in the old books], <i>島根大学外国語教育センタージャーナル</i> 10 (2015): 67–78, https://ir.lib.shimane-u.ac.jp/31500. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1901), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/cu31924086054776. [Open access]","Lionel Giles, trans., <i>Taoist Teachings from the Book of Lieh Tzŭ</i> (London: John Murray, 1912), 13–15, https://archive.org/details/taoistteachingsf00unse. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Spirits_and_the_Supernatural_-_pic47_-_%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%96.png/1280px-Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Spirits_and_the_Supernatural_-_pic47_-_%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%96.png","title":"Kuafu striding after the sun","desc":"Vectorised from an illustration in the Gujin Tushu Jicheng, the imperial encyclopaedia compiled between 1700 and 1725, in its section on spirits and the supernatural. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Spirits_and_the_Supernatural_-_pic47_-_%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%96.png","alt":"Line drawing: a barefoot giant strides through banked clouds towards the disc of the sun at the upper left, a snake threaded through his ears and another held in his raised hand, the title in characters down the right edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the story change from a run to a race?","a":"Because the chapters of the great wilderness open it with three characters the first telling lacks, that he did not measure his strength, where the older wording says only that he ran alongside the sun."},{"q":"Why is his death told two ways in one book?","a":"Because one chapter has him die of thirst on the road while another has the winged dragon Yinglong kill him after killing Chiyou, which puts him in the Yellow Emperor's war."},{"q":"Why is the fullest version of the tale the least secure?","a":"Because the Liezi's own standing is disputed, Herbert Giles calling it a forgery of the 1st or 2nd century CE where his son held that a genuine core lay under later accretions."}]},
{"id":"cnh-017","num":17,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"The daughter of Yandi drowned in the Eastern Sea and came back as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a small bird that carries wood and stones from the western hills to dam it up.","questions":["Tao Yuanming wrote that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries small twigs meaning to fill the vast sea, which turned a drowned girl's bird into a figure for holding on.","On Mount Fajiu lives a bird shaped like a crow, with a patterned head, a white beak and red feet, called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because that is what its cry sounds like."],"answer":"Jingwei","answerText":"Jingwei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Her father</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yandi, placed from 2737 BCE in 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The mountain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fajiu, where 1 river also rises</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the western hills to 1 sea, in the east</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Praised by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tao Yuanming, 365 – 427 CE</span></div>","traditional":"精衛","hanzi":"精卫","pinyin":"Jīngwèi","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Jingwei</b> is a small bird of Chinese myth that carries twigs and pebbles out over the water and drops them in, meaning to fill up the sea, and she was a girl before she was a bird.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> finds her on Mount Fajiu, where the zhe trees grow: a bird shaped like a crow, with a patterned head, a white beak and red feet, whose cry is its own name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The passage calls her the youngest daughter of Yandi: she went wandering on the Eastern Sea, drowned there and did not come back, and so became Jingwei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ever since, the passage says, she has carried wood and stones from the western hills to dam up the Eastern Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The entry then closes like the geography it belongs to rather than like a story, noting that the Zhang River rises on that mountain and runs east into the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her father Yandi is Shennong the Divine Husbandman, who first shaped timber into ploughs and taught the people farming, and whose reign one traditional reckoning places from 2737 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is called Yandi, the Blazing Emperor, because he took fire as the emblem of his government, and a second fire sign was added to his name to say so twice over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tao Yuanming, who lived from 365 to 427 CE, read the book and wrote that Jingwei carries small twigs meaning to fill the vast sea, which made her a figure for holding on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The older gloss is less kind: a 19th-century dictionary explains the saying the story left behind as being like the tomtit carrying pebbles, in the sense of wasting one's powers on a hopeless task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both readings are still in use, and Hong Kong sets the story for primary schools as an example of knowing a thing cannot be done and doing it anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), no. 401, 157, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Education Bureau, Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, “精衞填海,” in 《郁文華章》（小學篇）：「選篇分析」, no. 22, https://www.edb.gov.hk/attachment/tc/curriculum-development/kla/chi-edu/resources/primary/lang/culture/03_022.pdf. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 609, 187, and pt. III, 366, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 238–39, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1901), 128–29, https://archive.org/details/cu31924086054776. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic130_-_%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E5%9C%96.png/1280px-Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic130_-_%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E5%9C%96.png","title":"The Jingwei bird","desc":"Vectorised from an illustration in the Gujin Tushu Jicheng, the imperial encyclopaedia compiled between 1700 and 1725, in its section on the animal kingdom. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic130_-_%E7%B2%BE%E8%A1%9B%E5%9C%96.png","alt":"Line drawing of a long-tailed bird in flight seen from above, wings spread and streaming tail feathers, the plumage worked in fine scale-like strokes."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the entry about her read like geography rather than a story?","a":"Because it closes by noting that the Zhang River rises on that mountain and runs east into the Yellow River, the book being a classic of mountains and seas."},{"q":"Why do two opposite readings of the tale survive together?","a":"Because Tao Yuanming made her a figure for holding on, while a 19th-century dictionary glosses the saying as wasting one's powers on a hopeless task."},{"q":"Why is the bird's cry part of the story?","a":"Because it is her own name, so the creature announces what she was, a drowned girl who did not come back."}]},
{"id":"cnh-018","num":18,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"Chinese legend blames the tilt of heaven to the north-west on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who struck Mount Buzhou with his head after losing a fight and brought the sky-pillar down.","questions":["The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is really the title of an office, something like Minister of Works, and the classics have Yao refuse to employ him and Shun banish him.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised a flood that drove people up the hills and into the trees, Nüwa smelted five-coloured stones to mend the sky and propped its corners with a turtle's legs."],"answer":"Gonggong","answerText":"Gonggong","traditional":"共工","hanzi":"共工","pinyin":"Gònggōng","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fuxi, from 2852 BCE by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shennong, or Zhuanxu, 2 generations after Huangdi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Broke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mount Buzhou, 1 of 8 sky-pillars, in the north-west</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Banished by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shun, as 1 of the 4 great criminals</span></div>","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Gonggong</b> is a figure of Chinese legend remembered above all as a rebel who raised a flood across the world, and whom later ages turned into a god of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name is not really a personal name but the title of an office, roughly Minister of Works or overseer of the waters, and one reading has that office name passing to the family that held it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The old books cannot agree where to place him, calling him a minister of Fuxi, a vassal of Shennong, or a lord in the days of Zhuanxu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Huainanzi</i> says he stirred the flood waters until they beat against Kongsang, the four seas swirled together, and the people all climbed the hills and took to the trees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> makes him a descendant of Yandi and the father of Houtu, setting him in the line that Kuafu also comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What every version keeps is his defeat: he contended for the throne, lost, and in his rage struck Mount Buzhou with his head until it came down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That mountain was the sky-pillar of the north-west, so heaven has tilted that way ever since, carrying the sun, moon and stars towards it, while the earth falls short in the south-east and every river runs there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Who beat him differs, for the <i>Liezi</i> and the <i>Huainanzi</i> name Zhuanxu and Sima Zhen names Zhurong, and Édouard Chavannes noted that tradition puts Gonggong generations earlier and so gives the fight to his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nüwa then smelted five-coloured stones to mend the sky and cut the legs from a giant sea turtle to prop its corners, and by one account she raised an army and killed him herself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The classics historicise all of it, for Yao refused to employ him, calling him a fine talker whose work turned out badly, and Shun banished him as one of four great criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 284, 89, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), 23–24 and 39, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 49–50 and 67, and Annales des trois souverains, 11, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 393 and 601, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Sun Shulin 孫樹林, “从古籍中的黄帝神话传说记载管窥古人之宇宙观” [The cosmology of the ancients seen through records of the Yellow Emperor myths in the old books], <i>島根大学外国語教育センタージャーナル</i> 10 (2015): 69 and 72, https://ir.lib.shimane-u.ac.jp/31500. [Open access]","Lionel Giles, trans., <i>Taoist Teachings from the Book of Lieh Tzŭ</i> (London: John Murray, 1912), 85, https://archive.org/details/taoistteachingsf00unse. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/%E5%85%B1%E5%B7%A5%E6%B0%8F%E9%A0%AD%E8%A7%B8%E4%B8%8D%E5%91%A8%E5%B1%B1.jpg/1280px-%E5%85%B1%E5%B7%A5%E6%B0%8F%E9%A0%AD%E8%A7%B8%E4%B8%8D%E5%91%A8%E5%B1%B1.jpg","title":"Gonggong butts Mount Buzhou","desc":"A woodblock illustration from the lower volume of the Ming vernacular chronicle 按鑑演義帝王御世盤古至唐虞傳, whose artist is not named. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%85%B1%E5%B7%A5%E6%B0%8F%E9%A0%AD%E8%A7%B8%E4%B8%8D%E5%91%A8%E5%B1%B1.jpg","alt":"Woodblock print in a circular frame: a bearded figure in a robe leans forward and drives his head against a tall cracked peak at the left, with bare branches, a small sun disc and banked cloud around him, and the title in characters down both side panels."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this name not really a personal one?","a":"Because it is the title of an office, roughly Minister of Works or overseer of the waters, and one reading has that title passing to the family that held it."},{"q":"Why does the sky tilt in Chinese cosmology?","a":"Because in his rage he struck Mount Buzhou, the sky-pillar of the north-west, until it came down, so the stars run that way and every river runs south-east."},{"q":"Why do the accounts name different victors over him?","a":"Because the Liezi and Huainanzi name Zhuanxu and Sima Zhen names Zhurong, and tradition puts him generations earlier, which gives the fight to his own descendants."}]},
{"id":"cnh-019","num":19,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"In the Tribute of Yu the western tribes bring hair-cloth and skins from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the cosmogonists later made the central mountain of the earth.","questions":["The <i>Huainanzi</i> piles the walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in nine gradations and grows a pearl tree, a jade tree and a tree of immortality on its terraces in the far west.","King Mu of Zhou is said to have driven his eight steeds to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and met the Queen Mother of the West there, a journey Sima Qian left out of his annals."],"answer":"Kunlun","answerText":"Kunlun","traditional":"崑崙","hanzi":"昆仑","pinyin":"Kūnlún","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Tribute of Yu, 1 of the Shu King's documents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Visited by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">King Mu of Zhou, who reigned from 1001 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Measured at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,090 li round and 11,000 li high, in 1 account</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walled in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 gradations, with 12 towers of five-coloured jade</span></div>","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Kunlun</b> is the mountain at the centre of the world in Chinese tradition, standing somewhere in the far west, with the great rivers of the earth rising at its foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name begins as a plain geographical one: in the Tribute of Yu the wild tribes of the west bring hair-cloth and skins from Kunlun, and the document says nothing else about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> then have King Mu of Zhou lead a punitive expedition there in his 17th year and see the Western Wangmu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sima Qian left that journey out of his annals of Zhou altogether, and Édouard Chavannes observed that if the oldest texts are to be believed the figure Mu met is a barbarian chief rather than a queen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the cosmogonists made of the name is another thing entirely, for they raised it into the central mountain of the earth and the source of the four great rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> gives it 10,090 li round and 11,000 li high, with a blue, a white, a red and a black river running at its base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Huainanzi</i> piles its walls in nine gradations and grows on it a pearl tree, a jade tree, a star-gem tree and a tree of immortality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its foot runs the Yellow Water, which winds three times and comes back to its source, and whoever drinks it escapes death, while the Yellow River rises there and the Weak Water issues from a hollow rock into the Moving Sands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twelve towers of five-coloured jade stand on its terraces, where the immortals grow sesame and coriander and the Queen Mother of the West keeps her court beside the Lake of Gems, feasting her guests on peaches that ripen once in three thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The <i>Erya</i> calls the mountain Sumi in the west, which is the Chinese name for Sumeru, and 19th-century sinologists therefore read the whole paradise as Indian cosmology transplanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 380, 108–9, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), 127, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, King Mu, 17th year, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 272 and 594, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 265, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 16 and 137, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Kunlun_Mountains.jpg/1280px-Kunlun_Mountains.jpg","title":"A snow peak on the western frontier","desc":"Photographed near Tashkurgan in Xinjiang in 2010 by Preston Rhea, who titles the view the Kunlun Mountains. CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunlun_Mountains.jpg","alt":"A snow-covered peak stands far off beyond a wall of bare dark ridges, with a wide river running through green pasture in the foreground and a few animals and low buildings along the far bank."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the name start out unremarkable?","a":"Because in the Tribute of Yu it is only where the western tribes bring hair-cloth and skins from, and the document says nothing else about it."},{"q":"Why did the cosmogonists make it the centre of the world?","a":"Because they raised it into the central mountain of the earth and the source of the four great rivers, giving it measurements, jade towers and a tree of immortality."},{"q":"Why did 19th-century sinologists read the paradise as Indian?","a":"Because the Erya calls the mountain Sumi in the west, which is the Chinese name for Sumeru."}]},
{"id":"cnh-020","num":20,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"In the <i>Bamboo Annals</i> King Mu of Zhou drives west to Kunlun and is entertained by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the Lake of Gems, where the peaches of immortality grow.","questions":["The image of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives above all in the relief carvings of Han grave chambers, and Taoist writers gave her a palace on Kunlun walled in gold.","A consort was invented for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Dongwanggong, the Eastern King Lord, whose name openly imitates her own and who is the male principle to her female."],"answer":"Queen Mother of the West","answerText":"Queen Mother of the West","traditional":"西王母","hanzi":"西王母","pinyin":"Xīwángmǔ","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Entertained</span><span class=\"dt-v\">King Mu of Zhou, about 985 BCE by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Han grave chambers, 206 BCE to 220 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Her palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 li round, ramparted in gold</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Her peaches</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ripen once in 3,000 years, by 1 account</span></div>","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Queen Mother of the West</b> is the goddess of the Chinese west, who keeps her court on Kunlun at the head of the immortals and admits a favoured mortal from time to time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole legend grew by accretion over centuries from the slender basis of a name that turns up in a few very early traditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those notices are thin: the apocryphal Books of Zhou and the <i>Bamboo Annals</i> both have King Mu entertained by her in the west, and the <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i> carries an obscure mention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Édouard Chavannes read the oldest texts to mean a barbarian chief rather than a queen, and the sober suggestion of the 19th century was that Wangmu named either a region or a sovereign in the ancient west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Liezi</i> built an allegorical tale on those hints, and the credulity of Han Wu Di produced a whole literature of her visits to him with her fairy troop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Taoist writers then furnished her mountain: a palace a thousand li round, ramparted in massive gold with battlements of precious stones, above the Lake of Gems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beside that lake grows the peach tree of the immortals, whose fruit confers immortality on whoever she allows to taste it, and the accounts differ, one giving fruit once in three thousand years and another three thousand years of leaf before three thousand more of ripening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ripening fixed her birthday, kept as the Feast of Peaches and served with bears' paws, monkeys' lips, dragon liver and phoenix marrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She sends azure birds out as her messengers, and a consort was eventually found for her in Dongwanggong, the Eastern King Lord, whose name openly imitates her own and who was paired with her as the male principle to her female.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Long before any of that was written she was carved everywhere in Han funerary art, surviving above all in the relief work on the stones and bricks of grave chambers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 572, 178–79, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 272, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 136–37, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 265, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, King Mu, 17th year, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Nataša Vampelj Suhadolnik, “Image and Role of the Queen Mother of the West in Han Grave Art,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 3, no. 2 (2015): 197–212, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2015.3.2.197-212. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West%2C_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg/1280px-Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West%2C_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg","title":"Peach Festival of the Queen Mother of the West","desc":"A handscroll in ink and colour on silk, Ming or Qing dynasty, 17th or 18th century, by an anonymous painter once credited to Fang Chunnian. Freer Gallery of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peach_Festival_of_the_Queen_Mother_of_the_West,_Freer_Gallery_of_Art.jpg","alt":"A blue-and-green landscape painting: a terraced palace of tiled halls and pavilions stands on a stone platform among pines, small figures on its balconies and crossing a bridge below, with green and blue peaks and banked gold cloud behind and an orchard at the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why is her cult called an accretion?","a":"Because the whole legend grew over centuries from the slender basis of a name that turns up in a few very early traditions."},{"q":"Why did the peach tree fix a date in the calendar?","a":"Because its ripening was taken to set her birthday, kept as the Feast of Peaches, though the accounts differ over how the three thousand years are counted."},{"q":"Why was a consort found for her so late?","a":"Because Dongwanggong's name openly imitates her own, and he was paired with her as the male principle to her female."}]},
{"id":"cnh-021","num":21,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"In 219 BCE a magician from Qi told the First Emperor of the isles in the Eastern Sea and sailed for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with several thousand young men and women.","questions":["The immortals on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are said to feed on the gems scattered along its shores and on a fountain of life that never stops running.","Sima Qian had no patience with the magicians who ran the sea after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, setting them down as charlatans who lived on a sovereign's credulity."],"answer":"Penglai","answerText":"Penglai","traditional":"蓬萊","hanzi":"蓬莱","pinyin":"Pénglái","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sought from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">219 BCE, under the First Emperor of Qin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Its neighbours</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fangzhang and Yingzhou, 2 more isles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in a book of the 5th or 4th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Its fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">several 1,000 young men and women, driven back</span></div>","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Penglai</b> is the first of the three isles of the immortals, which the Chinese of the Qin placed in the Eastern Sea, out of sight but not far off, opposite their own coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its neighbours are Fangzhang and Yingzhou, and the same island goes under the further names Penghu and Pengqiu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three are peopled by immortals whose shining bodies are fed on the gems lying scattered along their shores and on the fountain of life that runs there for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest writer to put islands of the immortals out in the ocean is the <i>Liezi</i>, of the 5th or 4th century BCE, who is also the first to describe the fruit of immortality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By that time the princes of the coastal states of Qi and Yan were already sending ships out to look for them, talked into it by a magician called Song Wuji.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Anqi Sheng, a drug-seller on the eastern shore who was said to have lived a thousand years, was summoned by the First Emperor and talked with him for three days and nights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On taking his leave he told the emperor to look for him again in the isle of the immortals, and it was on that word that the great expedition was launched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 219 BCE a professor of magic arts from Qi named Xu Shi, otherwise Xu Fu, announced the isles to the emperor as he toured his new empire and begged leave to sail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He went at the head of several thousand young men and women, and the story has the fleet sight the islands and then be driven back by contrary winds, which has been read since as a memory of some attempt to settle Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sima Qian, writing a century later, had no patience with any of it and set the men who ran the sea after enchanted isles down as charlatans living on a sovereign's credulity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 559, 174, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, 163–64, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 3, 309 and 703, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 72, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 647, 197, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, LIII and XCV, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/La_expedici%C3%B3n_de_Xu_Fu%2C_por_Utagawa_Kuniyoshi.jpg/1280px-La_expedici%C3%B3n_de_Xu_Fu%2C_por_Utagawa_Kuniyoshi.jpg","title":"Xu Fu's fleet sets out for Penglai","desc":"A woodblock triptych of about 1843 by Utagawa Kuniyoshi, whose title has the First Emperor build ten great ships and send the court magician Xu Fu after the herbs of long life. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_expedici%C3%B3n_de_Xu_Fu,_por_Utagawa_Kuniyoshi.jpg","alt":"A colour woodblock print across three sheets: three great junks under patterned sails, their prows carved as beasts and their decks crowded with small figures, ride a blue sea, with a robed man standing on the leading prow pointing ahead and a snow-capped peak on the far horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the great expedition launched when it was?","a":"Because a drug-seller said to have lived a thousand years told the First Emperor to look for him again in the isle of the immortals, and it was on that word that the fleet sailed."},{"q":"Why do the voyages predate the emperor who is remembered for them?","a":"Because the princes of the coastal states of Qi and Yan were already sending ships out, talked into it by a magician called Song Wuji."},{"q":"Why does the earliest historian give the search no credit?","a":"Because Sima Qian set the men who ran the sea after enchanted isles down as charlatans living on a sovereign's credulity."}]},
{"id":"cnh-022","num":22,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"Chinese vases, fans and teapots are crowded with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Taoist group whose members stand between them for youth and age, wealth and poverty, and both sexes.","questions":["No source earlier than the Yuan dynasty knows the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a fixed set, though several of them had been celebrated separately in Taoist legend for centuries before.","Only three of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were real people, and the histories that mention those three say nothing whatever about the magical powers the later stories give them."],"answer":"Eight Immortals","answerText":"Eight Immortals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grouped in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Yuan dynasty, 1280 to 1368</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or as early as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1250 to 1279, in the last Southern Song years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Historical</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 of the 8, the other 5 being fable</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lists differ</span><span class=\"dt-v\">across 4 early works, 1 sharing almost no names</span></div>","traditional":"八仙","hanzi":"八仙","pinyin":"Bāxiān","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Eight Immortals</b> are a group of Taoist figures so constantly painted that few subjects in Chinese art rival them, turning up on vases, teapots, fans, scrolls and embroidery alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Between them they stand for the whole of humanity: an old man and a youth, a soldier and a scholar, a nobleman and a beggar, a cripple and a woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each is known by an emblem rather than a face, so that Zhang Guolao rides his donkey, Lan Caihe plays his flute, Li Tieguai leans on an iron crutch and Lü Dongbin carries a sword.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So much luck attaches to them that the number itself took some of it on, and China has an Eight Genii table, an Eight Genii bridge and an Eight Genii vermicelli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One series of pictures shows them crossing the sea, each riding a monster of his own, and another sets them in pairs with their symbols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> As a fixed set they are late, for the Qing scholar Zhao Yi could find no trace of them as a group before the Yuan dynasty, and the most generous reading pushes them back only to the closing decades of the Southern Song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Several had been celebrated singly as immortals long before that, so what those years supplied was the assembly rather than the figures gathered into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor was the membership settled: four early works give four lists, one of which shares almost no names with the rest, and the single woman is absent from two of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only three were real people — Zhongli Quan, Zhang Guolao and Lü Dongbin — and Lü took his degree about 770 before withdrawing to live as a recluse on Mount Hua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The sober records of those three credit them with no supernatural power whatever, and the tales do not keep their own chronology, handing He Xiangu a peach from a man born after her death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 251, 338, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 207 and 562–63, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 288–89, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 35–36, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 36–38, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 63–64, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Octagonal_vase_depicting_the_Eight_Great_Immortals_of_Daoism%2C_China%2C_Longquan_ware%2C_Yuan_dynasty%2C_1271-1368%2C_mold-stamped_ceramic_under_glaze_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco%2C_CA_-_DSC07490.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"The Eight Immortals on a Yuan celadon vase","desc":"An octagonal Longquan celadon vase of the Yuan dynasty, whose eight faces are mould-stamped with the Eight Immortals in unglazed panels. Asian Art Museum, San Francisco. Photograph by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octagonal_vase_depicting_the_Eight_Great_Immortals_of_Daoism,_China,_Longquan_ware,_Yuan_dynasty,_1271-1368,_mold-stamped_ceramic_under_glaze_-_Asian_Art_Museum_-_San_Francisco,_CA_-_DSC07490.jpg","alt":"A pale green celadon vase with eight flat sides, its shoulder and foot moulded with flower panels and its waist carrying a row of unglazed orange panels, each holding a standing bearded figure in long robes among curling clouds."},"why":[{"q":"Why is each of them known by an object rather than a face?","a":"Because the group stands for the whole of humanity — old and young, soldier and scholar, nobleman and beggar — and an emblem is what tells one from another."},{"q":"Why is the set called late when several of its members are old?","a":"Because no trace of them as a group is found before the Yuan, and the most generous reading pushes it only to the closing decades of the Southern Song, so what those years supplied was the assembly."},{"q":"Why do the tales sit badly with the records of the three real men?","a":"Because the sober records credit them with no supernatural power whatever, and the stories do not keep their own chronology, handing He Xiangu a peach from a man born after her death."}]},
{"id":"cnh-023","num":23,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"A monster to be killed in Europe, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a bringer of rain and the essence of the yang principle, and turns evil only in the legends Buddhism carried in from India.","questions":["Chinese writers give the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nine resemblances, among them the horns of a deer, the head of a camel and the claws of a hawk, and they do not agree on all nine.","The opening hexagram of the <i>Book of Changes</i> is a ladder of them, from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lying hid in the deep to the one on the wing in the sky."],"answer":"Chinese dragon","answerText":"Chinese dragon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First seen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Fuxi, from 2852 BCE by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 360 scaly creatures, in a Han dictionary</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Graded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 5 colours by an emperor, 1101 to 1126</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Imperial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 claws to a foot, 4 for lesser dragons</span></div>","traditional":"龍","hanzi":"龙","pinyin":"lóng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Chinese dragon</b>, the long, is chief of the four divinely constituted creatures and a spirit of the waters, a bringer of rain rather than a monster to be killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the essence of the yang principle, and can transform itself, shrinking to the size of a silkworm or swelling until it fills the space between heaven and earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the spring equinox it ascends into the sky, and at the autumn equinox it buries itself in rivers or in the depths of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chinese writers give it nine resemblances — a deer's horns, a camel's head, a snake's neck, a hawk's claws — but do not agree on all nine, and one has it hear through its horns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A pearl of magic power goes with it, on its forehead by one account and beneath its chin by another, and a Han dictionary makes it chief of the 360 scaly creatures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It is the emblem of the emperor, whose throne is the dragon seat, and only imperial robes carried a beast with five claws to a foot where lesser dragons have four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first hexagram of the <i>Book of Changes</i> runs from the dragon lying hid in the deep to the one on the wing in the sky, the whole a figure for the great man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four kinds are listed: the celestial dragon holding up the mansions of the gods, the spiritual one that makes wind and rain, the earth dragon that lays out the rivers, and one guarding hidden treasure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dragon kings of the seas came the other way about, being the nagas of India brought in by Buddhism, and an emperor of the 12th century canonised five coloured dragon spirits as kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Belief held firm because the creature was constantly seen, any cloud with a serpentine tail being a dragon, and in drought a paper one was carried through the streets to ask for rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Yî King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 16 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1882), 57–59, https://archive.org/details/mlbd.sacredbooksofeas0000fmax.vol.16. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 451, 141–43, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 94, 307, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 208–10, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 677–85, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 409–12, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Nine_Dragons%2C_detail%2C_Song_Dynasty.jpg","title":"Two of Chen Rong's Nine Dragons","desc":"A detail of the handscroll <i>Nine Dragons</i>, painted in ink by Chen Rong in 1244, under the Southern Song. Reproduced from Michael Sullivan's <i>The Arts of China</i>. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nine_Dragons,_detail,_Song_Dynasty.jpg","alt":"An ink painting on a pale ground: two scaled, whiskered dragons coiling through banks of wash-laid cloud, one turning its horned head back over its shoulder and the other reaching forward with a splayed clawed foot, with a red seal impression at the top right."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this creature a bringer of rain rather than a monster?","a":"Because it is a spirit of the waters and the essence of the yang principle, ascending at the spring equinox and burying itself in rivers or the sea at the autumn one."},{"q":"Why could only the emperor's robes carry the full beast?","a":"Because it is the emblem of the emperor, whose throne is the dragon seat, and only imperial robes bore a dragon with five claws to a foot where lesser ones have four."},{"q":"Why did belief in it hold so firm?","a":"Because the creature was constantly seen, any cloud with a serpentine tail being a dragon, and in drought a paper one was carried through the streets to ask for rain."}]},
{"id":"cnh-024","num":24,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"English calls it the Chinese phoenix, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an omen bird that comes only when a kingdom is tranquil and its ruler fond of peace, and leaves when it is not.","questions":["The male of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the feng and the female the huang, and the pair's inseparable fellowship is Chinese poetry's covert way of speaking about lovers.","A regent of the Han told his boy emperor that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been seen on the coast, and an official was sent off at once to make it offerings."],"answer":"Fenghuang","answerText":"Fenghuang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First came</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Huangdi, from 2697 BCE by 1 annal</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Danced for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shun, at the 9 parts of his music</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Worshipped from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">84 BCE, under Zhaodi of the Han</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last seen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 1368, as the Ming took the throne</span></div>","traditional":"鳳凰","hanzi":"凤凰","pinyin":"fènghuáng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Fenghuang</b> is a bird of wondrous form and mystic nature, one of the four supernatural creatures, and English calls it the Chinese phoenix on many writers' authority rather than on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The male is the feng and the female the huang, and the compound of the two names is what Chinese itself calls the bird.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Accounts of its shape differ and none settles on one animal, giving it a hen's head, a man's eye, a serpent's neck, a swallow's brow, a tortoise's back and the outward semblance of a dragon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its fish-like tail carries twelve feathers, or thirteen in a year with an intercalary month, its song has five harmonious notes, and its plumage the five colours, each standing for a cardinal virtue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is king of the 360 kinds of feathered creature, comes out of the East, alights on no tree but the wutong, eats the fruit of the bamboo and drinks only from clear streams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> What made it matter is that it appears only where a kingdom is tranquil and its ruler fond of peace, and leaves a state that is disordered and warlike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> have it come to Huangdi by the Luo and nest in the courts at Shun's accession, and the <i>Shu King</i> has it dance into the hall when the nine parts of Shun's music had been played.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That made a sighting worth manufacturing, and the first official worship of the bird was got up in 84 BCE by the regent Huo Guang, who told a boy emperor one had appeared on the eastern coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An officer was sent to make it offerings; at the next accession a flock obliged in Shandong, and the emperor marked the event with a general amnesty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Ming historiographer wrote that a scholar would never have committed such a blunder, and the last recorded coming was in 1368, on the tomb of the father of the man then taking the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), 88, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, Hwang-ti, 50th year, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 184, 40–41, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 76, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 666–69, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 669–71, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic001_-_%E9%B3%B3%E5%87%B0%E5%9C%96.png","title":"The fenghuang in the Imperial Encyclopaedia","desc":"A plate from the Gujin Tushu Jicheng, the imperial encyclopaedia compiled under Chen Menglei and printed in the 1720s, in its section on the animal kingdom. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Encyclopaedia_-_Animal_Kingdom_-_pic001_-_%E9%B3%B3%E5%87%B0%E5%9C%96.png","alt":"A woodblock plate in black line on a pale ground: a long-tailed bird with a crested head and streaming tail plumes standing among rocks and foliage, with two columns of Chinese characters at the right edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the English name not the bird's own?","a":"Because Chinese calls it by the compound of the male feng and the female huang, and 'phoenix' rests on many writers' authority rather than on the bird itself."},{"q":"Why was a sighting of it worth manufacturing?","a":"Because it appears only where a kingdom is tranquil and its ruler fond of peace, so its arrival testified to the government under which it came."},{"q":"Why is the first official worship of it dated to 84 BCE?","a":"Because the regent Huo Guang told a boy emperor one had appeared on the eastern coast and had an officer sent to make it offerings."}]},
{"id":"cnh-025","num":25,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"Confucius wept when hunters caught a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his own state, saying the creature comes only when there is an intelligent king, and the <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i> stop at that entry.","questions":["A picture of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bringing a child hangs in bedrooms and rides before the bridal chair, and a boy of unusual promise is called the gift of the unicorn.","An emperor of the Han thought he had caught a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and renamed the period of his reign after it, the beast being an omen that a wise ruler is on the throne."],"answer":"Qilin","answerText":"Qilin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the parks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">of Huangdi, from 2697 BCE by 1 annal</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Again under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yao, in the 7th year of his reign</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caught</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 14th year of Duke Ai of Lu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed a reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 121 BCE, after a Han emperor's supposed catch</span></div>","traditional":"麒麟","hanzi":"麒麟","pinyin":"qílín","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Qilin</b> is one of the four supernatural creatures of Chinese tradition, beside the dragon, the phoenix and the tortoise, and English calls it the Chinese unicorn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The male is the qi and the female the lin, and the compound of the two is what Chinese calls the creature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The authorities do not agree: an early glossary gives it a deer’s body, an ox’s tail and a single horn, though later pictures give it a pair, while the dictionary of about 100 CE makes it a benevolent beast with a horse’s body and a horn of flesh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The fleshy tip marks a creature able for war that wills to have peace, and later writers add that it eats nothing living, and never treads on growing grass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What mattered was never its shape but its timing: it comes only where a wise ruler reigns or a sage is about to be born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> That made a sighting worth arranging: the <i>Bamboo Annals</i> put them in Huangdi’s parks and one in the seventh year of Yao, and a Han emperor who thought he had caught one renamed his reign period after the catch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The famous appearance went wrong: in Duke Ai of Lu’s fourteenth year hunters took a lin west of the capital and broke its foreleg, and the man given it threw it out as unlucky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Confucius went to look, knew it at once, and wept, because the creature comes only when there is an intelligent king and this one had come at the wrong time and been hurt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i> stop at that entry, which is why the capture is famous, though the translator who put them into English doubted the line was Confucius’s and thought the beast an antelope uncommon in Lu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What outlived the omen is a domestic hope: the qilin bringing a child is painted in bedrooms and carried before the bridal chair, and a bright boy is called the gift of the unicorn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Gae, fourteenth year, 834, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), Prolegomena, 85–86, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 389, 127, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 109, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 43, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 672–76, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 322, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Tuhui_zongyi_%28Principles_of_Painting%29_Qilin.jpeg","title":"The qilin in a Ming painting manual","desc":"A woodblock plate from the <i>Tuhui zongyi</i>, the painting manual edited by Yang Erzeng and printed in 1607; the copy reproduced is in the Philadelphia Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuhui_zongyi_(Principles_of_Painting)_Qilin.jpeg","alt":"A woodblock plate in black line on aged paper: a scaled, cloven-hoofed beast with a bearded dragon-like head, a pair of horns swept back over its shoulders and a bushy flame-shaped tail, bounding through grass beneath a pine, with its name in two Chinese characters at the upper right."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the creature's timing matter more than its shape?","a":"Because it comes only where a wise ruler reigns or a sage is about to be born, so a sighting was a verdict on the government and worth arranging."},{"q":"Why did Confucius weep at the one that was caught?","a":"Because the beast comes only when there is an intelligent king, and this one had come at the wrong time and had its foreleg broken."},{"q":"Why does a fleshy horn belong to a benevolent beast?","a":"Because the soft tip marks a creature able for war that wills to have peace, as later writers add that it eats nothing living and never treads on growing grass."}]},
{"id":"cnh-026","num":26,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divide the Chinese sky into quarters, dragon east, bird south, tiger west and tortoise north, with seven of the 28 lunar mansions to each.","questions":["The <i>Book of Rites</i> has an army march under banners of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Red Bird in front, the Dark Warrior behind, the Azure Dragon left and the White Tiger right.","A tiger's coat was said to turn white at five hundred years, which is why the western quarter of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the White Tiger rather than an ordinary one."],"answer":"Four Symbols","answerText":"Four Symbols","answerDate":"","traditional":"四象","hanzi":"四象","pinyin":"Sìxiàng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Four Symbols</b> are the four quarters of the Chinese sky, each named for a creature: the Azure Dragon in the east, the Vermilion Bird in the south, the White Tiger in the west and the Dark Warrior in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are a way of dividing the stars rather than a bestiary, since the 28 lunar mansions are shared out seven to a quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are also not the four supernatural creatures — the qilin, phoenix, tortoise and dragon — which the same manual lists as a set of its own, and only the dragon and the tortoise belong to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each quarter carries a season as well as a direction, the dragon standing for spring and the east together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The tiger is white because a tiger's coat was said to turn white at five hundred years, and its name came to mean the western quarter and then the west in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest use is military: the <i>Book of Rites</i> directs that on the march the banner of the Red Bird go in front, the Dark Warrior behind, the Azure Dragon on the left and the White Tiger on the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its translator's note explains that each of those names covers the seven constellations of that quarter, so an army moved under a diagram of the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The northern figure is really a tortoise, and Dark Warrior is the name of an emperor who in one legend beat a demon king that had turned into one, the victor's name sticking to the quarter the tortoise stands for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scheme was old enough to keep time by, and a commentator on the Canon of Yao read the middle of spring off it, from the seven southern constellations showing towards evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It outlived the astronomy, since officials and wealthy men were still carried to the grave under catafalque hangings worked with a dragon and a tiger, for the eastern and western quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Lî Kî</i>, Sacred Books of the East 27 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1885), the Khü Lî, sect. i, pt. iv, 8, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0027unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Les Cinq Empereurs, 44, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Les Cinq Empereurs, 47, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 91, 307, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, no. 187, 61, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II, no. 94, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 681, 684, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Soochow_Planisphere_%28Detail_2%29.jpg/1920px-Soochow_Planisphere_%28Detail_2%29.jpg","title":"The Suzhou planisphere","desc":"A rubbing of the star chart engraved on stone at Suzhou in about 1247, from a map drawn by Huang Shang around 1190 and cut by Wang Zhiyuan; the rubbing is of the 16th century. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soochow_Planisphere_(Detail_2).jpg","alt":"A star chart in gold on a near-black ground: a great disc of the sky crossed by a band for the Milky Way, its stars joined into constellations by fine lines, cut into unequal wedges by radial lines that run from an inner circle of circumpolar stars out to a rim of small labelled panels."},"why":[{"q":"Why are these four not the four supernatural creatures?","a":"Because the same manual lists that set separately, and only the dragon and the tortoise belong to both; these four divide the sky, sharing the 28 lunar mansions seven to a quarter."},{"q":"Why did an army march under their banners?","a":"Because the Book of Rites places each at its own side of the column, so the order of march reproduced the diagram of the sky."},{"q":"Why does the northern figure carry an emperor's name?","a":"Because Dark Warrior names an emperor who in one legend beat a demon king that had turned into a tortoise, and the victor's name stuck to the quarter the tortoise stands for."}]},
{"id":"cnh-027","num":27,"category":"Myth and origins","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the mask on Shang and Zhou bronzes, two great eyes over curved tusks, and its name means glutton after a man the Zuo Commentary says Shun banished.","questions":["The oldest text to use the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means a man rather than a mask, one of four wicked ones cast out to the four distant regions for greed beyond measure.","Cataloguers of Chinese bronzes proposed the tiger, the storm god and the Tibetan mastiff for the face of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and none of the three carried the day."],"answer":"Taotie","answerText":"Taotie","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Duke Wan's 18th year, in the Zuo Commentary</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Banished with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 other wicked ones, by Shun</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On bronzes of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Shang, c. 2000 – 1001 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"饕餮","hanzi":"饕餮","pinyin":"tāotiè","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Taotie</b> is the monster face that stares out of early Chinese bronzes, and its name means glutton after a man rather than a beast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Zuo Commentary, under the eighteenth year of Duke Wan, sets out four wicked men whom Shun banished to the four distant regions: Chaos, Monster, Block and Glutton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Glutton was the worthless descendant of an officer of Huangdi, greedy of eating and drinking, craving money and property, insatiable in his exactions and sparing neither the orphan nor the widow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Grand Historian tells the same story, and later commentators matched those four one for one against the four criminals the Canon of Shun says he put away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The equations are not worth much in themselves, as their reporter admits, but the two lists are plainly two forms of one legend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The mask is the thing a reader is likelier to have seen: a face with enormous eyes and heavy jaws armed with curved tusks, moulded in relief on ritual wine jars and food vessels of the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits on a ground of squared spirals that the cataloguers read as cloud and thunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One explanation reached for the tradition that Yu the Great cast the first bronze vessels and moulded them with the shapes of the storm gods of the hills and waters, so that people might know their dreaded features and keep clear of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the face actually meant was not settled: the tiger, the all-devouring storm god and the Tibetan mastiff were each proposed within a few years of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The scholar who gathered those readings declined to choose between them, regretting that no evidence had been given for any, and asked only that the thing be treated as a deity rather than as ornament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Wan, eighteenth year, 283, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Les Cinq Empereurs, 78, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), 40, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Berthold Laufer, <i>Jade: A Study in Chinese Archaeology and Religion</i>, Field Museum of Natural History Publication 154 (Chicago, 1912), 184–85, https://archive.org/details/jadestudyinchin00lauf. [Open access]","Stephen W. Bushell, <i>Chinese Art</i>, vol. 1, Victoria and Albert Museum Art Handbook (London: His Majesty's Stationery Office, 1904), 90, https://archive.org/details/chineseart1bush. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Late_Shang_bronze_lei_with_turquoise_taotie_design.jpg/1920px-Late_Shang_bronze_lei_with_turquoise_taotie_design.jpg","title":"A taotie face laid in turquoise","desc":"Photographed in the National Museum of China, where the photographer records it as a late Shang bronze lei with a turquoise taotie design. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Shang_bronze_lei_with_turquoise_taotie_design.jpg","alt":"A small bronze vessel faced with a mosaic of turquoise tesserae in blue and brown: a symmetrical monster mask laid across its front, with a raised vertical flange down the middle for the nose, a great curled eye and arched brow to either side of it, and a domed lid above set with a small horned head."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the mask named after a man?","a":"Because the Glutton was one of four wicked men Shun banished, greedy of eating and drinking, insatiable in his exactions and sparing neither the orphan nor the widow."},{"q":"Why was the face thought to be a warning rather than a decoration?","a":"Because one explanation reached for the tradition that Yu cast the first vessels with the shapes of the storm gods, so that people might know their dreaded features and keep clear of them."},{"q":"Why did the scholar who collected the readings refuse to choose?","a":"Because the tiger, the all-devouring storm god and the Tibetan mastiff were each proposed within a few years and no evidence had been given for any."}]},
{"id":"cnh-028","num":28,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"Traditionally said to describe the charts on Yu the Great's nine bronze vessels, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> surveys hills and waters and fills them with gods, omen beasts and impossible peoples.","questions":["Kuafu, Jingwei, the Queen Mother of the West and a winged dragon that killed Chiyou are all described in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, distrusted as geography and indispensable as myth.","A geographical compilation of the Zhou by one reckoning, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was already ancient when a 4th-century scholar edited it, along with the dictionary of ancient terms and the <i>Elegies of Chu</i>."],"answer":"Classic of Mountains and Seas","answerText":"Classic of Mountains and Seas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou at latest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1122 – 250 BCE, by 1 reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edited by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Guo Pu, 276 – 324 CE</span></div>","traditional":"山海經","hanzi":"山海经","pinyin":"Shānhǎi jīng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b><i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i></b> is the oldest Chinese geography to survive apart from the Tribute of Yu, and it is a geography of a strange kind: it walks from hill to hill, naming at each the trees, the ores, the water and the creature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese bibliographers file it with the geographies, though it has long been read with distrust, and its 19th-century defenders concluded only that it is at least as old as the Zhou and probably older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tradition made it a description of the charts engraved on the nine bronze vessels Yu the Great cast after draining the flood, which would set it at the beginning of the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A French compiler in Shanghai a century ago called it a compilation of the Zhou, full of strange statements the scholars of his day disbelieved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Guo Pu, who lived from 276 to 324, edited it along with the old dictionary of terms and the <i>Elegies of Chu</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What has kept it in hand is its second half, where the survey passes beyond the seas and the countries stop being possible. There are headless people whose eyes sit in the breast, people with one arm or three faces, pygmies who walk hand in hand against birds that would carry them off, and giants whose eyebrows hang over their carts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its animals are omens as much as animals: a beast is described, then what its appearance brings, which is why a red pheasant of the Xiaohua hills was painted on doors against fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A winged dragon lives in the far south, and having killed Chiyou it could not rise again and hid beneath the earth, so drought follows wherever it lies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kuafu chasing the sun, Jingwei filling the sea and a Mount Kunlun 11,000 li high are all in it, and a modern study calls the book the wellspring of the country's ancient myths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 35, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 408–9, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 386–90, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 683 and 707, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 380, 108–9, and pt. II no. 56, 301, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Zhao Kuifu 赵逵夫, “‘夸父逐日’神话的历史文化内涵” [The historical and cultural content of the ‘Kuafu chases the sun’ myth], 文学遗产 [Literary Heritage] 2020, no. 5, https://ir.nwnu.edu.cn/bitstream/39RV6HYL/61950/1/%E2%80%9C%E5%A4%B8%E7%88%B6%E9%80%90%E6%97%A5%E2%80%9D%E7%A5%9E%E8%AF%9D%E7%9A%84%E5%8E%86%E5%8F%B2%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E5%86%85%E6%B6%B5.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Wu-Renchen-Shanhai-jing-guangzhu-Kui.jpg","title":"A page of the book","desc":"Kui, described in Commons as a one-legged bull-shaped creature, from Wu Renchen's annotated <i>Shanhaijing guangzhu</i> printed in 1786; reproduced from <i>Ritual and Scripture in Chinese Popular Religion</i>, edited by David Johnson. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu-Renchen-Shanhai-jing-guangzhu-Kui.jpg","alt":"A woodblock page in black line within a heavy ruled border: a hornless ox-like beast with long ears and a bushy tail crouches on the empty page, its name and four short columns of Chinese text ranged down the right-hand margin."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the book filed as a geography despite its contents?","a":"Because it walks from hill to hill naming the trees, the ores, the water and the creature at each, which is the form of a survey however strange the countries become."},{"q":"Why are its animals inseparable from omens?","a":"Because a beast is described and then what its appearance brings, which is why a red pheasant of the Xiaohua hills was painted on doors against fire."},{"q":"Why does tradition put it at the very beginning of the record?","a":"Because it was taken for a description of the charts engraved on the nine bronze vessels Yu cast after draining the flood."}]},
{"id":"cnh-029","num":29,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"Chiefly the work of a minister of Chu who drowned himself in the Miluo, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds the <i>Li Sao</i> and the <i>Nine Songs</i> and stands as a class of its own.","questions":["Wild, irregular and so allegorical that much of it would be unintelligible without the commentary, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first gathered in the 1st century BCE and survives in an edition of 17 books.","The poet is said to have clasped a stone and gone into the river on the fifth of the fifth moon, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands behind the Dragon Boat Festival."],"answer":"Chuci","answerText":"Chuci","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Its poet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qu Yuan, 332 – 295 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collected by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Liu Xiang, in the 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"楚辭","hanzi":"楚辞","pinyin":"Chǔcí","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b><i>Chuci</i></b>, the Elegies of Chu, is a collection of southern poetry given a class of its own at the head of Chinese belles-lettres, a place it has held since the Sui history's bibliography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is chiefly the work of Qu Yuan, a minister of the state of Chu who lived from 332 to 295 BCE and stood in high favour with Prince Huai until a jealous rival denounced him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Degraded and disregarded, he went to the bank of the Miluo, told a fisherman that the world was foul and he alone clean, then clasped a stone to his chest and went under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His chief piece is the <i>Li Sao</i>, Falling into Trouble, nearly 400 lines in which he leaves the world in a phoenix car drawn by dragons and is refused at the palace of God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Poems by his nephew Song Yu and by Jing Cha, both nearly contemporary, make up the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The pieces were first gathered by Liu Xiang in the 1st century BCE, and the earliest form now surviving is Wang Yi's edition in 17 books, which appends work by eight later hands and carries his commentary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhu Xi of the Song cut a selection to 8 books and annotated it again, and though later editors took liberties with the poems the old commentary came down uncorrupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The verse itself is unlike anything before it, wild and irregular in metre, and so allusive that without the commentary much of it would now be unintelligible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Among the Nine Songs inside the collection is the Genius of the Mountain, clad in wistaria and girdled with ivy, riding a red pard through a grove no daylight reaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Qu Yuan went into the river on the fifth of the fifth moon, and the people of Chu cast rice in after him, which is the origin of the Dragon Boat Festival and of the rice cake in water-flag leaves eaten on the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 181, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1901), 50–53, https://archive.org/details/cu31924086054776. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 200–201, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 466, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 326, 107, and pt. I no. 642, 196, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Li_sao_illustr%C3%A9_3_10.png","title":"The Mountain Spirit, with her poem","desc":"The ninth of the Nine Songs in an annotated edition of the <i>Chuci</i> published under the title <i>Li sao</i>, with illustrations by Xiao Yuncong, 1645. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_sao_illustr%C3%A9_3_10.png","alt":"An open woodblock book: on the left the poem printed in columns of Chinese characters, on the right a framed cut of a woman in leaf robes with a flowering staff, riding a spotted leopard through cloud, a small attendant beside her and a second beast below."},"why":[{"q":"Why would much of this verse be unintelligible without its commentary?","a":"Because the poetry is wild and irregular in metre and so allusive that the old commentary, which came down uncorrupted, is what makes the allusions readable."},{"q":"Why does one poet's death fix a date in the Chinese year?","a":"Because he went into the Miluo on the fifth of the fifth moon and the people of Chu cast rice in after him, which is the origin of the Dragon Boat Festival."},{"q":"Why does the collection carry work by men who never knew its chief poet?","a":"Because Wang Yi's edition in 17 books, the earliest form now surviving, appends work by eight later hands along with his commentary."}]},
{"id":"cnh-030","num":30,"category":"Myth and legend","question":"Jade stands for purity, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bears that name, and a Song emperor who had signed a humiliating peace put him on the list of gods in 1012.","questions":["Born to a queen who dreamed of Lord Lao on a dragon, and later a hermit who healed the sick, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is in legend a king's son who gave his throne away.","Called the high god of the Taoists and, a century ago, the most popular divinity in China, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> issues writs of pardon that are bought at paper shops and burnt."],"answer":"Jade Emperor","answerText":"Jade Emperor","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Birthday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 9th of the 1st moon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First listed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 10th month of 1012</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statue cast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 1013, by Zhenzong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 1116 by 1 manual</span></div>","traditional":"玉皇大帝","hanzi":"玉皇大帝","pinyin":"Yùhuáng Dàdì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Jade Emperor</b> is the high god of the Taoists and was, a century ago, the most popular divinity in China, jade standing for purity in a name also given as Yuhuang Shangdi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Legend makes him the son of a queen of Guangyan Miaole who, past her prime and childless, dreamed that Lord Lao came riding a dragon with a boy in his arms and gave her the child.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was born at noon on the ninth day of the first moon, gave the palace wealth to the poor, took the throne at his father's death and resigned it days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He lived as a hermit at Puming in Shaanxi and on Mount Xinyan in Yunnan, attained perfection, and spent the rest of his life healing the sick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Buddhists and Taoists both claim him, the former identifying him with Indra, and a sinner could buy a printed writ of his pardon at a paper shop, to be filled in and burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His place in the record is later and stranger: in 1005 the Song emperor Zhenzong signed a humiliating peace with the Khitan, and to prop up his throne began announcing messages from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A pennant of yellow silk twenty feet long flew from a gate of the capital with a letter tied to it, praising his virtue and promising his house seven hundred generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the tenth month of 1012 he told his ministers that an Immortal had brought him a letter from the Jade Emperor, and the official history notes the name entering the list of divinities then for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had a statue cast in 1013, and bought the silence of the minister likeliest to object with a cask said to hold wine and found to hold pearls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 19th-century manual dates the worship instead to 1116 under Huizong and the Taoist Lin Lingsu, while a biographical dictionary makes the god a magician who raced another to heaven on dragons and won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 130–32, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 510–11 and 524, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 9–12, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 391, 127–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 957, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Jade_Emperor._Ming_Dynasty.jpg","title":"The Jade Emperor enthroned","desc":"A 16th-century hanging scroll in the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, catalogued there as the Daoist deity the Jade Emperor. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jade_Emperor._Ming_Dynasty.jpg","alt":"A painting on silk, worn and torn at the upper right: a bearded god in wide white robes with dark embroidered borders sits cross-legged on a lotus throne, hands folded in his lap, a small jewelled crown on his head and a plain gold halo behind it, with scrolled clouds in pink, green and ochre filling the ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did an emperor announce messages from heaven in 1005?","a":"Because he had just signed a humiliating peace with the Khitan, and the announcements were meant to prop up his throne."},{"q":"Why does the god's name enter the official record so late?","a":"Because the history notes it entering the list of divinities for the first time in 1012, after the emperor told his ministers an Immortal had brought him a letter."},{"q":"Why do the accounts of when his worship began disagree?","a":"Because a 19th-century manual dates it instead to 1116 under Huizong and the Taoist Lin Lingsu, where the official history points to the earlier reign."}]},
{"id":"cnh-031","num":31,"category":"Myth and legend","difficulty":3,"question":"Buddhism brought the Sanskrit Naraka to China, where it became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an earth-prison whose sentences end, since a soul that has served its term is reborn among the six classes of living beings.","questions":["A Taoist handbook put the entrance to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a mountain near Fengdu in Sichuan, the Indian hell being reckoned far too deep underground to lie anywhere near China.","Under the Song, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reorganised into ten courts under the Jade Emperor, so the underworld came to be run like a Chinese magistracy, with a register of lives kept by a clerk."],"answer":"Diyu","answerText":"Diyu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reshaped under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Huizong, 1101 – 1126</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descent told of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Wanli reign, 1573 – 1620</span></div>","traditional":"地獄","hanzi":"地狱","pinyin":"dìyù","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Diyu</b>, written with characters meaning \"earth-prison\", is the Chinese underworld, the term chosen to render the Sanskrit Naraka when Buddhism brought a place of punishment for the wicked into China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a final destination but a phase of transmigration, closer to a purgatory: the shortest term in any one hell is five hundred years, each of its days worth fifty above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A soul that leaves it is reborn among the six classes of living beings, which are gods, men, asuras, animals, hungry ghosts and those still under torment below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ruler is Yanluo, the Sanskrit Yama, whom Buddhism took from Brahmanism, and who by one legend was a king of Vaisali sworn to be reborn in hell for victory in battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He won, and now rules in a palace where devils pour molten copper down his throat three times a day, his generals serving as jailers and his sister Yami taking the women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Sanskrit hell lay some 450,000 kilometres (280,000 miles) underground, too far from China, and a Taoist handbook to the underworld, the <i>Yuli chaozhuan</i>, put its entrance in a mountain near Fengdu in Sichuan instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A story set in the Ming reign of Wanli has a governor lowered down the shaft in a box, walking to an iron gate and taking tea with Yanluo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How many hells there are was never settled: Buddhism counted eight, the northern school added eight cold ones, another reckoning gives 136 with two more for women and for suicides, and one sutra 180.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under the Song the underworld was reorganised as ten courts and placed under the Jade Emperor, giving it the shape of a Chinese magistracy rather than an Indian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fittings followed: a life-register kept by a clerk who once lengthened an emperor's by altering a numeral, a bridge only the good cross, and a pond of blood for women dead in childbirth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 250–59, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 179, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 247, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 207, 327, and pt. II no. 18, 355, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 245 and 462, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 197, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin01dool. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 105, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Jin_Chushi._Yama_the_Fifth_King_of_Hell%2C_late_12_century._Metmuseum.jpg/1920px-Jin_Chushi._Yama_the_Fifth_King_of_Hell%2C_late_12_century._Metmuseum.jpg","title":"A king of hell holds court","desc":"One of a set of hanging scrolls of the Ten Kings of Hell, painted in ink and colour on silk by Jin Chushi before 1195 and now in the Metropolitan Museum of Art; the file's uploader identifies this king as Yama. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_Chushi._Yama_the_Fifth_King_of_Hell,_late_12_century._Metmuseum.jpg","alt":"A tall silk scroll in a gold brocade mount, read from top to bottom. A red-robed king in a winged official's cap sits scowling behind a plain wooden desk while a clerk in green, in a black scholar's cap, leans in holding out an open document. Below them blue-skinned demons drag a barefoot man in white towards a large round mirror on a stand, and at the foot of the scroll an armoured demon swings two swords among snakes and thorn trees where small figures are being tormented."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this place closer to a purgatory than to a hell?","a":"Because it is a phase of transmigration rather than a destination: a soul that leaves it is reborn among the six classes of living beings."},{"q":"Why was the entrance moved to a mountain in Sichuan?","a":"Because the Sanskrit hell lay some 450,000 kilometres underground, too far from China, so a Taoist handbook put the way in near Fengdu instead."},{"q":"Why did the underworld end up shaped like a Chinese magistracy?","a":"Because under the Song it was reorganised as ten courts and placed under the Jade Emperor, and the fittings followed — registers, a bridge, a clerk keeping the life-register."}]},
{"id":"cnh-032","num":32,"category":"Myth and legend","difficulty":4,"question":"A soul whose merits outweigh its faults passes from the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> straight to the tenth, where it is reborn, and never sees the mirror of guilt at all.","questions":["Most of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hold sixteen small hells apiece, each with its own speciality, its own list of offences, and its own list of the good deeds that excuse a soul.","The whole scheme of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from a Taoist monk's handbook rather than from canonical Buddhism, and it is arranged throughout like a Chinese magistrate's court."],"answer":"ten courts of hell","answerText":"ten courts of hell","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in a Southern Song album, 1200 – 1279</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A judge of it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Bao Zheng, died 1062</span></div>","traditional":"十殿閻羅","hanzi":"十殿阎罗","pinyin":"Shí Diàn Yánluó","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>ten courts of hell</b> are the received popular account of Diyu, the Chinese underworld: ten halls, each with a presiding king, through which a dead soul is passed in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The whole scheme descends from one Taoist book, a handbook to the underworld by a monk named Tan Chi who claimed to have gone down and come back, rather than from canonical Buddhism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is arranged throughout like a Chinese magistrate's court, with registers, warrants, lictors and the rest of a yamen's paraphernalia, and its officials are promoted or demoted on their record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The First Court keeps the register of the living and the dead, and a soul whose merits outweigh its faults goes straight from it to the Tenth to be reborn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A guilty soul is dragged instead before a mirror inscribed \"no good man comes before this mirror of guilt\", and then to the Second Court, where the punishments begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Each court between them holds sixteen small hells with their own specialities, among them knives, ice, copper cauldrons and wild beasts, and its own list of the offences it exists to punish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those lists read like a legal code rather than a theology: maiming, denying a deposit left in trust, kidnapping children to sell to monasteries, prescribing for patients without knowing medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each court also lists what exempts a soul from it, and the living could petition the ten kings on a dead parent's behalf in hope of a lighter penalty or none at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its judges are dead Chinese officials: Bao Zheng, the Song judge said to be no more bribable than the king of the dead himself, was given the Fifth Court by popular opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tenth Court settles where a soul is reborn, and before it goes Granny Meng gives it a broth of oblivion, a measure taken after escaped souls began telling the living what happens below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 262–301, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 179, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 618, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 197, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin01dool. [Open access]","Cleveland Museum of Art, “Album of Daoist and Buddhist Themes: Kings of Hells: Leaf 29,” accession 2004.1.29, https://clevelandart.org/art/2004.1.29. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/China%2C_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-China%2C_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif.jpg","title":"A king of hell in his hall","desc":"Leaf 29 of a Chinese album of Daoist and Buddhist themes, painted in the 1200s and belonging to the album's series on the Ten Kings of Hells; the Cleveland Museum of Art, which holds it, says the king's identity is unclear. Made available under CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China,_Southern_Song_dynasty_-_Album_of_Daoist_and_Buddhist_Themes-_Kings_of_Hells-_Leaf_29_-_2004.1.29_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif","alt":"An ink outline drawing on tan paper, with no colour but a red seal at the foot. A bearded official in a winged cap sits on a throne at the head of a flight of steps, holding a narrow plaque across his chest; a hanging scroll beside him shows a plan of walled buildings. Attendants stand below the dais with trays and staves, an armoured demon waits at the left and a beaked demon at the right, and two old men in long robes bow with their hands clasped at the foot of the steps."},"why":[{"q":"Why does this scheme rest on one book rather than on scripture?","a":"Because it descends from a handbook to the underworld by a monk who claimed to have gone down and come back, rather than from canonical Buddhism."},{"q":"Why do the courts' lists read like a legal code?","a":"Because each names the offences it exists to punish — maiming, denying a deposit left in trust, kidnapping children, prescribing without knowing medicine — and what exempts a soul from it."},{"q":"Why is a broth of oblivion given at the last court?","a":"Because escaped souls had begun telling the living what happens below, so the drink was a measure taken against that."}]},
{"id":"cnh-033","num":33,"category":"Myth and legend","difficulty":3,"question":"The god of the kitchen, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, keeps account of the good and evil done by the family he lodges with, and goes up at the year's end to report on them.","questions":["Honey or sticky rice is fed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before he leaves for heaven, so that his lips will stick together and he can say little about the household's year.","With the ancestral tablet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one of the two things worshipped in every Chinese house, his printed image pasted up on the wall beside the cooking range."],"answer":"Zao Jun","answerText":"Zao Jun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Goes up</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the 24th of the 12th moon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Birthday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 3rd of the 8th moon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First sacrifice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Han Wudi, 140 – 86 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"灶君","hanzi":"灶君","pinyin":"Zàojūn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Zao Jun</b>, the god of the kitchen, is the household deity of Chinese popular religion, his image pasted up over the cooking range in what was reckoned to be every family in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With the ancestral tablet he was one of the two objects of worship a Chinese house was never without, honoured with incense and candles on the first and fifteenth of every month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is a Taoist figure and goes by several names, among them the god of the stove and Zhang Dan, which means the one who makes out the bill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His office is not cooking but watching: he keeps account of the good and evil done by the family he lodges with, and Taoist books make him the chief of heaven's secret service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the end of every year he goes up and reports on the household's conduct to the Jade Emperor, and is away over the turn of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> His picture is torn down and burnt with mock-money, which is how he travels, and beans and straw are thrown on the roof so the family may hear his footsteps or his horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before he leaves he is feasted, and the prudent give him honey or balls of glutinous rice so that his lips stick together and he can say little up above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A printed address bought at a shop is read out before the image by the head of the house and then burnt to carry it up, like a memorial sent to a magistrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A new picture is pasted up for his return, showing an old man and, in most modern versions, his wife the goddess of the hearth, among almanac tables and painted animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The legend of his worship has it beginning with a Han magician who claimed the god had freed him from age and hunger, and who persuaded Emperor Wu to offer the first imperial sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 44 and 166–67, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 597 and 622, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 81–85, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. D. J. Finn, second part, vol. 9 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1931), 6, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch09doruoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 458–59, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/%E5%8F%B8%E5%91%BD%E7%AB%88%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E4%B8%89%E6%95%99%E6%BA%90%E6%B5%81%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E5%A4%A7%E5%85%A8%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"The god of the stove and his clerk","desc":"A woodblock illustration which the file's own record identifies as the Stove God of the Sanjiao yuanliu soushen daquan, a Chinese compendium of the gods of the three religions, in a 1909 edition. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%B8%E5%91%BD%E7%AB%88%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E4%B8%89%E6%95%99%E6%BA%90%E6%B5%81%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E5%A4%A7%E5%85%A8%EF%BC%89.jpg","alt":"A black and white woodblock line drawing. A bearded man in a tall official's cap and long belted robes stands on a terrace beneath a banana plant, his hands folded inside his sleeves; a smaller attendant in a cap stands at his shoulder holding a bound volume against his chest, with a table and a railing behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why is his office watching rather than cooking?","a":"Because he keeps account of the good and evil done by the family he lodges with, and Taoist books make him the chief of heaven's secret service."},{"q":"Why is his picture burnt rather than stored?","a":"Because burning it with mock-money is how he travels to report on the household at the year's end, and a new picture is pasted up for his return."},{"q":"Why is he given honey before he goes?","a":"Because the prudent mean his lips to stick together, so that he can say little when he makes his report above."}]},
{"id":"cnh-034","num":34,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"Legend has the Yellow Emperor hang two spirits' portraits on peach-wood above his doors, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are still painted or pasted on doors across China.","questions":["Two generals who sat up armed outside a sick Tang emperor's bedroom were later painted on his palace gates, and so joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> guarding ordinary houses.","Renewed every New Year and set one to each leaf of a double door, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were once to be seen on almost every house front in China."],"answer":"Menshen","answerText":"Menshen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Quoted by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wang Chong, 27 – 97 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Told again in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Fengsu tongyi, late 2nd century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New pair under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tang Taizong, 627 – 650 CE</span></div>","traditional":"門神","hanzi":"门神","pinyin":"ménshén","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Menshen</b> are the guardian gods of the door, a pair of armed figures set one to each leaf of a Chinese double door and renewed at the New Year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name is plural and behaves more like an office than a person: several different pairs have held it, and one household's pair need not be its neighbour's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are counted among a standard set of six household spirits invoked in charms, alongside the ancestral tablets and the god of the stove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The oldest pair are two spirits of a legend which the <i>Lunheng</i>, written by Wang Chong in the 1st century, quotes from the <i>Classic of Mountains and Seas</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a mountain in the eastern sea stood a peach tree three thousand li round, and the door by which the ghosts came and went lay between its north-eastern boughs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Shen Shu and Yu Lu had charge of that gate, binding with cords of reed any ghost that had done harm and feeding it to the tigers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yellow Emperor, hearing of it, is said to have raised a peach-wood figure and had the two painted with tigers and cords on house doors, and the custom is traced from that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second-century study of custom, the <i>Fengsu tongyi</i>, of which only a third survives, has officials cut peach-wood figures on the year's last night and paint a tiger on the doorway; by the 19th century many households pasted up the two names instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That pair were displaced in popular favour by two generals of the Tang emperor Taizong, Qin Shubao and Yuchi Gong, who watched armed outside his door through a sickness and were then painted on his palace gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The minister Wei Zheng took the back gates, and it has been argued that the pictures came first and the gods after them, an image put up to keep harm out ending as a god in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Wang Ch'ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part I: Philosophical Essays of Wang Ch'ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz; London: Luzac; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1907), 243, https://archive.org/details/lunheng01wang. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 728, 220–21, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 131, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 172–74, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), 151–52, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 710–11, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 3 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 261, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch03doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/%E9%96%80%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E6%96%B0%E5%88%BB%E5%87%BA%E5%83%8F%E5%A2%9E%E8%A3%9C%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E8%A8%98%EF%BC%89.jpg/1280px-%E9%96%80%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E6%96%B0%E5%88%BB%E5%87%BA%E5%83%8F%E5%A2%9E%E8%A3%9C%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E8%A8%98%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"The door gods on a pair of doors","desc":"A woodblock illustration which the file's own record identifies as the door gods of the Xinke chuxiang zengbu Soushen ji, an illustrated Chinese compendium of the gods, and dates to 1600. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%96%80%E7%A5%9E%EF%BC%88%E6%96%B0%E5%88%BB%E5%87%BA%E5%83%8F%E5%A2%9E%E8%A3%9C%E6%90%9C%E7%A5%9E%E8%A8%98%EF%BC%89.jpg","alt":"A black and white woodblock line drawing of a pair of closed double doors studded with rows of round bosses, a bearded warrior in armour painted on each leaf, each holding a long-hafted weapon upright and turning his head towards the other; the two characters for door god are cut into the top right corner."},"why":[{"q":"Why does this name behave like an office rather than a person?","a":"Because several different pairs have held it, and one household's pair need not be its neighbour's."},{"q":"Why is peach wood part of the custom?","a":"Because the oldest pair guarded the ghost-gate between the north-eastern boughs of a peach tree three thousand li round, so peach-wood figures were cut and set at doors."},{"q":"Why has it been argued that the pictures came before the gods?","a":"Because two generals painted on a sick emperor's gates displaced the older pair, an image put up to keep harm out ending as a god in its own right."}]},
{"id":"cnh-035","num":35,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":1,"question":"Because the twelve earthly branches also number the double hours, an animal of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> governs a time of day and a compass point as well as a year of birth.","questions":["A fortune-teller may rule that guests born under one animal of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must stay away from a wedding, lest some influence fall on them at the wrong hour.","Asking why the rat does not drive off the horse if water beats fire, a 1st-century sceptic attacked the elemental logic behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its twelve animals."],"answer":"Chinese zodiac","answerText":"Chinese zodiac","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest witness</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wang Chong, born 27 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In use by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 1st or 2nd century BCE, perhaps</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Animal years by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 2nd century CE, on one view</span></div>","traditional":"生肖","hanzi":"生肖","pinyin":"shēngxiào","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Chinese zodiac</b> is a cycle of twelve animals — rat, ox, tiger, hare, dragon, serpent, horse, goat, monkey, cock, dog and pig — one to each of the twelve earthly branches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The branches are a counting series rather than a band of constellations, and pairing them with ten heavenly stems gives the sixty combinations of the sexagenary cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those sixty pairs named days from great antiquity and were applied to years only under the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same branches divide the day into twelve double hours and mark twelve points of the compass, so an animal governs an hour and a direction as well as a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is not a zodiac in the astronomical sense: the twelve divisions of the ecliptic, where the sun and moon meet, are a separate set and the nearer analogue of the western signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A birth is written as eight characters, a stem and a branch each for the year, month, day and hour, and the year's branch is what makes a person belong to an animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A fortune-teller comparing those characters with the five elements may find that a certain animal must be kept away from a wedding, a coffining or a house-raising, so those born in its year stay at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Charms were printed for each of the twelve to cure a person born in that branch's year, and every one of the sixty years had a god of its own with an image in the temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The scheme invited that reasoning, since each animal takes an element from its branch and the five elements conquer one another in a fixed order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wang Chong took it apart in the 1st century, asking why the rat does not drive off the horse if water beats fire; he is also the oldest Chinese witness to the twelve, whose origin, Turkic or Chinese, was long disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Wang Ch'ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part I: Philosophical Essays of Wang Ch'ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz; London: Luzac; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1907), 6 and 105–06, https://archive.org/details/lunheng01wang. [Open access]","Wang Ch'ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part II: Miscellaneous Essays of Wang Ch'ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke, 2nd ed. (1911; repr. New York: Paragon Book Gallery, 1962), appendix II, 479–80, https://archive.org/details/lunheng02wang. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 296, 348–49, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II nos. 302–03, 351–52, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 340–42, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 176–77, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 3 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 262, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch03doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Tang_Pottery_Zodiac_Figures_%289948253544%29.jpg/1920px-Tang_Pottery_Zodiac_Figures_%289948253544%29.jpg","title":"Pottery figures of the twelve animals","desc":"A row of Tang pottery figures with human bodies and animal heads, which the file's own record identifies as zodiac figures in the Shaanxi Provincial Museum, Xi'an. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_Pottery_Zodiac_Figures_(9948253544).jpg","alt":"Four standing pottery figures in a museum case, each with a human body in a long belted robe and its hands folded inside its sleeves, and each with the head of an animal: from the left a rodent, a horned ox, a broad-faced cat and a long-eared hare, all of them looking upward and standing on ringed plinths."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this not a zodiac in the astronomical sense?","a":"Because the twelve branches are a counting series rather than a band of constellations, and the twelve divisions of the ecliptic are a separate set."},{"q":"Why does an animal govern an hour and a direction as well as a year?","a":"Because the same branches divide the day into twelve double hours and mark twelve points of the compass, the animals being attached to the branches."},{"q":"Why did Wang Chong think the scheme did not work?","a":"Because each animal takes an element from its branch and the elements conquer one another in a fixed order, so he asked why the rat does not drive off the horse if water beats fire."}]},
{"id":"cnh-036","num":36,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"Because the river of heaven runs full to the brim, one drop of rain sweeps away the bridge of birds and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lose their one night together that year.","questions":["Girls once tried to thread a needle in the dark before a stick of incense on the night the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meet, counting a success as an omen of skill to come.","A single line in a Han treatise, saying magpies fill the Milky Way on the seventh night of the seventh month, is the oldest notice of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> anywhere."],"answer":"Cowherd and Weaver Girl","answerText":"Cowherd and Weaver Girl","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest notice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a Han treatise, by 122 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">They meet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the 7th night of the 7th month</span></div>","traditional":"牛郎織女","hanzi":"牛郎织女","pinyin":"Niúláng Zhīnǚ","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Cowherd and Weaver Girl</b> are two stars on opposite banks of the Milky Way, whom Chinese tradition makes a husband and wife kept apart and allowed to meet one night a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Weaver Girl is Vega, in Lyra; the Cowherd was less firmly placed, some putting him in Aquila and others in a group drawn from Capricorn and Sagittarius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oldest notice is one line in the <i>Huainanzi</i>, put together in 21 books under Liu An, a prince who died in 122 BCE: the two are parted all year except on the seventh night of the seventh month, when magpies fill the Milky Way and let her cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> From that obscure sentence a literature grew, its most famous stanza by Cao Pi, first emperor of Wei, on the two gazing at each other across the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the fuller telling she is a god's daughter, kept at her loom until her father married her to the herdsman on the far bank, whereupon she gave up weaving altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He separated them in anger, sending the husband across the river of stars and allowing one meeting a year, for which myriads of magpies make a bridge she walks over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything therefore turns on the weather, since the river of heaven runs full to the brim and one extra drop of rain floods it and sweeps the bird-bridge away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It attached itself to a real journey, a Han envoy's search for the source of the Yellow River, held to be the Milky Way continued on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sailed upstream to a city where a woman sat spinning and a young man watered an ox; she gave him her shuttle, and an astronomer at home found a wandering star had crossed between the two at that hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The festival is women's: melons, fruit, cakes, flowers and incense are set out for the two stars, and girls thread a needle in the dark, a success being an omen of skill to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 311, 97–98, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 189–91, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 60–61, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin02dool. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, first part, vol. 5 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1914–1938), 593–94, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch05doruoft. [Open access]","Alexander Wylie, <i>Notes on Chinese Literature</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1867), 126, https://archive.org/details/notesonchineseli00wyliuoft. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 412, 132, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Niulang_and_Zhinv_%28Long_Corridor%29.JPG/1920px-Niulang_and_Zhinv_%28Long_Corridor%29.JPG","title":"The meeting on the bridge of magpies","desc":"A painted panel in the Long Corridor of the Summer Palace in Beijing, which the file's own record identifies as the reunion of the Weaver Girl and the Cowherd on the bridge of magpies and dates to about the end of the 19th century. The painter is not named. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niulang_and_Zhinv_(Long_Corridor).JPG","alt":"A wide painted panel on a wooden beam, framed by scrolling clouds. On the left a woman in green and blue robes strides forward with long yellow and blue ribbons streaming behind her; on the right a man in a broad conical hat turns towards her, and between them two small children reach out with their arms. Three magpies fly overhead."},"why":[{"q":"Why does everything in the story turn on the weather?","a":"Because the river of heaven runs full to the brim, so one extra drop of rain floods it and sweeps the bird-bridge away."},{"q":"Why did the tale attach itself to a real journey?","a":"Because the Milky Way was held to be the Yellow River continued in the sky, so a Han envoy's search for its source could end at a city where a woman spun and a young man watered an ox."},{"q":"Why do girls thread a needle in the dark at the festival?","a":"Because the feast is the weaver's, so success at it is an omen of skill to come."}]},
{"id":"cnh-037","num":37,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"Realgar wine drunk at the summer festival turns the wife back into a giant serpent in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the shock of the sight frightens her husband to death.","questions":["The oldest clear ancestor of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a Southern Song tale in which three women met at West Lake prove to be an otter, a white snake and a black chicken.","A late Ming collection of stories gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its settled form, in which the snake is a menace and her husband ends the story as a monk."],"answer":"Legend of the White Snake","answerText":"Legend of the White Snake","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled form</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a woodblock print of 1624</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compiler</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Feng Menglong, 1574 – 1645</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reshaped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the mid-18th century</span></div>","traditional":"白蛇傳","hanzi":"白蛇传","pinyin":"Báishé zhuàn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Legend of the White Snake</b> is one of the best known stories in China: a snake spirit takes a woman's form, marries a young man of Hangzhou, and is shut under a lakeside pagoda by a monk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its oldest clear ancestor is a Southern Song tale of three women met at West Lake, who prove under exorcism to be a black chicken, a white snake and an otter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Behind that lies a plainer skeleton: a young man passes a night at a beautiful stranger's mansion, sickens as his vital essence drains, and finds on returning that the house is a graveyard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The settled form is late Ming, a tale known from a woodblock print of 1624 in Feng Menglong's <i>Stories to Caution the World</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There the snake is a menace rather than a wife: she possesses her husband, who is twice arrested over goods she gave him, and the monk Fahai buries her and her maid Little Green under the pagoda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From the middle of the 18th century the retellings turn on the marriage instead, and she takes on the virtues of a female immortal as a good wife and mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The episodes now known everywhere were added then: the meeting in the rain on West Lake, the borrowed umbrella, and the realgar wine of the summer festival, which turns her back into a giant serpent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sight frightens her husband to death, so she goes to Mount Kunlun for the herb that revives him, and when Fahai keeps him at the Golden Mountain monastery she leads an army of sea creatures against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is imprisoned under the pagoda once her son is born, and the son frees her by his filial devotion and by placing first in the civil examinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A recent comparative study links these tales, and the European stories of marriage with a demon woman, to the taming of a shape-shifting empousa in a 3rd-century life of Apollonius of Tyana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aaron Balivet, “The Many Transformations of White Snake: Gender, Ritual, and Performance in Late Imperial China” (PhD diss., University of Wisconsin–Madison, 2020), 5–8, https://asset.library.wisc.edu/1711.dl/MKNK5FP5V6YM79E/R/file-f7383.pdf. [Open access]","Whalen Lai, “From Folklore to Literate Theater: Unpacking Madame White Snake,” <i>Asian Folklore Studies</i> 51, no. 1 (1992): 51–66, https://web.archive.org/web/20250512083239/https://asianethnology.org/article/1066/download. [Open access]","A. B. Starostina, “On the Question of the Origin of Legends about Melusine and Madame White Snake,” <i>Shagi/Steps</i> 9, no. 3 (2023): 87–107, https://doi.org/10.22394/2412-9410-2023-9-3-87-107. [Open access]","Feng Menglong, comp., <i>Jingshi tongyan</i> [Stories to caution the world], juan 28, “Bai niangzi yongzhen Leifengta,” Chinese Text Project, https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?if=gb&chapter=730335. [Open access]","<i>Qingping shantang huaben</i> [Plain tales from Peaceful Mountain Hall], “Xihu san ta ji,” Chinese Text Project, https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?if=gb&chapter=185607. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Peking_Opera_The_Legend_of_the_White_Snake_%286986544727%29.jpg/1920px-Peking_Opera_The_Legend_of_the_White_Snake_%286986544727%29.jpg","title":"The tale on the Peking opera stage","desc":"Three of the tale's characters in a performance the photographer records as given at Zhenjiang in December 1985 by a touring Peking opera troupe. Just some dust, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peking_Opera_The_Legend_of_the_White_Snake_(6986544727).jpg","alt":"Three costumed opera performers before a pale curtain. On the left a figure in a green embroidered robe, with a sword at the hip and a jewelled headdress, gestures with one hand; in the centre a figure in white with a pearled headdress holds a tasselled whisk; on the right a figure in a blue scholar's robe and a soft blue cap looks down."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the snake change from a menace into a wife?","a":"Because from the middle of the 18th century the retellings turn on the marriage instead of the exorcism, and she takes on the virtues of a female immortal as a good wife and mother."},{"q":"Why does the festival wine matter to the plot?","a":"Because the realgar drunk at midsummer turns her back into a giant serpent, and the sight frightens her husband to death, which sends her to Kunlun for the herb that revives him."},{"q":"Why does the son's examination success end the story?","a":"Because she is imprisoned under the pagoda once he is born, and he frees her by his filial devotion and by placing first in the civil examinations."}]},
{"id":"cnh-038","num":38,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"A ball of fire seen climbing the mast meant that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was leaving the ship, and sailors who saw it come down again believed she would see them home.","questions":["Weaving at her loom, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dreamt of her father and brothers in a storm, and lost her father's junk when she opened her mouth to answer her own mother.","Two figures called Favourable-wind-ear and Thousand-mile-eye stand one on each side of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in her temples, listening for a fair breeze and watching a thousand li off."],"answer":"Mazu","answerText":"Mazu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cult began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the Song, 960 – 1280 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ningbo temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded in the 12th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UNESCO listing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 2009</span></div>","traditional":"媽祖","hanzi":"妈祖","pinyin":"Māzǔ","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mazu</b> is the Chinese goddess of the sea, worshipped by sailors, fishermen and traders alike, whose cult began in Fujian under the Song, the dynasty of 960 to 1280.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> She was born in a fishing village on Meizhou Island in Putian, and was known in her life for filial piety, for healing, for divining as a village medium, and for saving people from disaster at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the telling written down at Fuzhou in the 1860s she fell asleep over her weaving, head on the loom, and dreamt of her father and two brothers caught in a storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She took her father's junk in her mouth and her brothers' in her hands, and when her mother called she opened her mouth to answer, being an obedient girl, and let him go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Days later the news came that the brothers had been saved and the father drowned; emperors afterwards honoured her 36 times, with 16 official titles and more than a hundred popular ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In her temples an assistant stands on each side of her own image, Favourable-wind-ear, who catches the least breath of a fair breeze, and Thousand-mile-eye, who sees a thousand li away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sailors carried embers from the censer before her image in a small red bag or hung them aboard, and in a storm knelt at the bow with incense calling on her by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A ball of fire on the mast was taken for the goddess herself: going up it meant she was leaving and disaster was near, and coming down it promised she would see the junk in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Merchants carried the cult with them: the grandest temple in Fuzhou's southern suburbs was built by traders from Ningbo, and the finest building in Ningbo was a Mazu temple founded by Fujian men in the 12th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Emigration took her to Taiwan and to the Chinese towns of Southeast Asia, and in 2009 UNESCO put Mazu belief and customs on its list of intangible cultural heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Justus Doolittle, <i>Social Life of the Chinese</i>, vol. 1 (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1865), 262–64, https://archive.org/details/sociallifeofchin01dool. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>The Middle Kingdom</i>, vol. 1 (London: W. H. Allen, 1883), 123, https://archive.org/details/b29352897_0001. [Open access]","Dingying Lin, Zhiming Zhou, Xiaobin Zhang and Zhanhong Wu, “Analysis of the Factors Influencing the Dissemination of Mazu Culture in Southeast Asia during the Qing Dynasty,” <i>PLOS One</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0325164, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0325164. [Open access]","Suastiwi Triatmodjo, Ted Tsung-te Tsai, M. Agus Burhan, Hanggar Budi Prasetya and Endah Budiarti, “Mythologization of the Goddess Mazu: Readings through Spacial Setting and Activity in the Interior of Cu An Kiong Temple,” <i>Harmonia</i> 22, no. 2 (2022): 381–91, https://doi.org/10.15294/harmonia.v22i2.40627. [Open access]","Hsiao-Ming Chang and Ching-Hui Lin, “Mazu Belief and Happiness: A Sequential Mediation Model Involving Mental Health and Positive Emotions among Taiwanese Followers,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 16 (2025): 1571796, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1571796. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 288, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Wood_Statue_of_Mazu_Late_19th_century_CE_Qing_Dynasty_%281644-1911_CE%29_China.jpg/1920px-Wood_Statue_of_Mazu_Late_19th_century_CE_Qing_Dynasty_%281644-1911_CE%29_China.jpg","title":"A Qing image of the goddess","desc":"A gilded and painted wooden figure of the goddess enthroned, which the photographer's own record dates to the late 19th century, under the Qing. No holding museum is named. Mary Harrsch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wood_Statue_of_Mazu_Late_19th_century_CE_Qing_Dynasty_(1644-1911_CE)_China.jpg","alt":"A carved wooden figure seated square-on behind museum glass. She wears a flat-topped crown with a fringed headdress falling over each ear, her face painted a reddish brown; a heavy gilded robe carved with dragons and clouds covers her shoulders and knees, and both hands hold a broad curved tablet across her front. The pedestal below is carved with a lotus."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the dream in her legend end badly for her father?","a":"Because she held his junk in her mouth and her brothers' in her hands, and when her mother called she opened her mouth to answer, being an obedient girl."},{"q":"Why did her cult travel so far from one island?","a":"Because merchants carried it with them, the grandest temple in Fuzhou's southern suburbs being built by Ningbo traders and Ningbo's finest by Fujian men."},{"q":"Why was a light on the mast read two ways?","a":"Because a ball of fire going up the mast meant she was leaving and disaster was near, and coming down it promised she would see the junk in."}]},
{"id":"cnh-039","num":39,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":3,"question":"A general beheaded in 219 was made a duke, then a prince, and in 1594 a god, after which the empire worshipped him as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with about 1,600 official temples.","questions":["Though his shrines are called temples of the military god, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is honoured by scholars too, on a legend that he could recite the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> from beginning to end.","After crediting him with their victories over the Taiping rebels, the Qing in 1856 named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a sage and made him the equal of Confucius in the eyes of the state."],"answer":"Guandi","answerText":"Guandi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">219 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made a god</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1594, by the Wanli emperor</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named a sage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1856, under the Qing</span></div>","traditional":"關帝","hanzi":"关帝","pinyin":"Guāndì","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guandi</b> is the Chinese god of war, the deified form of Guan Yu, a general beheaded in 219 CE in the wars that broke the Han empire into three kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was born at Jieliang in Hedong, the later Jiezhou in Shanxi, and changed his given name twice in early life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shut in a back room by his parents, he broke out through the window, heard a girl and her father weeping, and killed the magistrate and the uncle who were forcing her into concubinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fleeing west with his description in every officer's hands, he stooped to wash at a brook and found his face turned a dark ruddy colour; asked his name at the barrier he said Guan, which had never been his family name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Zhuozhou he lifted the stone a butcher named Zhang Fei had rolled over his well, a straw-shoe seller called Liu Bei parted their fight, and in a peach orchard the three swore to die on one day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He kept that oath: taken prisoner with two of his lord's wives and shut up with them for a night, he stood at the door until morning holding a lantern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sun Quan took and beheaded him, and the deification then ran through four centuries of imperial decrees: duke early in the 12th century, prince in 1128, and a Mongol title around 1330.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1594 the Ming emperor Wanli raised him to the rank of <i>di</i>, meaning 'god', and from that year the state worshipped him officially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Qing credited him with their victories over the Taiping and in 1856 named him a sage, the equal of Confucius; he had by then about 1,600 official temples, in the capital and in every prefectural and district city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is a god of literature as well, on the strength of a legend that he could recite the <i>Zuo Commentary</i> from end to end, and Buddhists made him a guardian of their monasteries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 297, 92–93, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 113–17, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 6 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1920), 71–72, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch06doruoft. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 6 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1920), 80–81, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch06doruoft. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), no. 1009, 383–84, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Henry Doré, <i>Researches into Chinese Superstitions</i>, trans. M. Kennelly, second part, vol. 7 (Shanghai: T'usewei Printing Press, 1922), 315–16, https://archive.org/details/researchesintoch07doruoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Anonymous_-_Guan_Yu_-_2001.442_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg/1920px-Anonymous_-_Guan_Yu_-_2001.442_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg","title":"The god on a Qing scroll","desc":"A hanging scroll of the god in ink, colour and gold on silk, which the Metropolitan Museum of Art catalogues as the work of an unidentified artist of about 1700. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Guan_Yu_-_2001.442_-_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg","alt":"A tall painted silk scroll mounted in gold brocade. A bearded figure in a green embroidered robe stands on banks of green and pink cloud with a golden disc behind his head, one hand gripping a long halberd hung with a red pennant. A dark-faced attendant in armour stands close behind him and a younger figure in red, hands joined, at his left. A gold cartouche of Chinese characters sits in the top right corner."},"why":[{"q":"Why is his surname not the one he was born with?","a":"Because fleeing with his description circulated he washed at a brook, found his face turned a dark ruddy colour, and gave the name Guan at the barrier."},{"q":"Why did his deification take four centuries?","a":"Because it ran through imperial decrees — duke early in the 12th century, prince in 1128, a Mongol title around 1330 — until Wanli raised him to the rank of god in 1594."},{"q":"Why is a god of war also a god of literature?","a":"Because of a legend that he could recite the Zuo Commentary from end to end."}]},
{"id":"cnh-040","num":40,"category":"Chinese mythology","difficulty":4,"question":"Standard tables open the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Fuxi in 2852 BCE and carry it down through Shennong, the Yellow Emperor and Yao, giving each an exact year of accession.","questions":["Behind the genealogies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies the theory of the five elements, its emperors being symbols of five natural forces that succeed one another by destroying one another.","Compiling in the 1st century BCE, the Grand Historian drew up a table by generations running back through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Yellow Emperor, but set down exact years only from 841 BCE."],"answer":"Chinese legendary period","answerText":"Chinese legendary period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Opens with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fuxi, 2852 BCE by one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Table ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Shun, 2255 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Exact years from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">841 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"傳說時代","hanzi":"传说时代","pinyin":"chuánshuō shídài","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Chinese legendary period</b> is the division of early history that tradition furnishes with named rulers and exact years of accession, marked off from the purely mythical epochs placed before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A standard table opens with Fuxi, whose reign is placed in 2852 BCE, and runs through Shennong at 2737, the Yellow Emperor at 2697, Yao at 2356 and Shun at 2255.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A note under it observes that numerous dissimilar schemes were propounded by historical compilers, and that their mutually antagonistic views are themselves the evidence against every date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian said of the three dynasties that they are ancient and their chronology cannot be made precise, so his own exact years begin only with the Gonghe regency, in 841 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two rival systems of dates were still in roughly equal credit long afterwards, and every difference between them falls before that year, which is read as proof that his limit holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The organising idea behind the earliest genealogies seems to have been the theory of the five elements, its five emperors being symbols of natural forces that succeed one another by destroying one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That doctrine was held to have been taught by Confucius himself, and the short text recording it is among the works Sima Qian says he drew his own table from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had critics inside the tradition: in the 1st century CE Wang Chong tested it against the animals of the cycle, asking why the rat does not drive off the horse if water beats fire, or the cock eat the hare if metal beats wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Myth also grew forward into the record, since a man is not made a god without some cause being assigned, and that cause tends to take the form of a marvellous narrative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against all of it stands the annalist, whose very title means an impartial recorder, and whose office was taken seriously enough that the historians of Qi died in 547 BCE rather than falsify their chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. III, 365–66, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxliii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxviii–cxc, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]","Wang Ch’ung, <i>Lun-hêng, Part I: Philosophical Essays of Wang Ch’ung</i>, trans. Alfred Forke (Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz; London: Luzac; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1907), 105–07, https://archive.org/details/lunheng01wang. [Open access]","E. T. C. Werner, <i>Myths and Legends of China</i> (London: George G. Harrap, 1922), 63, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.38703. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/%E6%AD%A6%E6%A2%81%E7%A5%A0%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E7%9F%B3.jpg","title":"A carved stone of the Wu Liang shrine","desc":"An ink rubbing of one of the carved stones of the Wu Liang shrine in Shandong, a Han family shrine of the 2nd century CE, reproduced in Édouard Chavannes's archaeological survey of northern China. The record names the stone and not the figures carved on it. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A6%E6%A2%81%E7%A5%A0%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E7%9F%B3.jpg","alt":"An ink rubbing printed white on dark grey, of a stone slab with a gabled top. The gable carries a frieze of animals and small figures above a band of scrollwork. Below it runs a row of standing robed figures, each framed in its own narrow upright compartment under a scalloped canopy. Two further bands beneath hold seated figures, horses and carriages."},"why":[{"q":"Why are the disagreements between the chronologies the argument against them?","a":"Because numerous dissimilar schemes were propounded and every difference between them falls before 841 BCE, which is where Sima Qian's own exact years begin."},{"q":"Why does the five-element theory shape the earliest genealogies?","a":"Because its five emperors are symbols of natural forces that succeed one another by destroying one another, and the text recording that doctrine is among those the historian drew his table from."},{"q":"Why does myth grow forward into the record as well as backward?","a":"Because a man is not made a god without some cause being assigned, and that cause tends to take the form of a marvellous narrative."}]},
{"id":"gr-331","num":331,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is anonymous and takes his name from the potter whose signature is cut into the foot of the pots, saying he made rather than painted them.","questions":["Fewer than twenty vases survive from the hand of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, generally thought one of the first to practise the red-figure technique and possibly the artist who invented it.","A bilingual amphora in Munich shows Herakles feasting twice over, its red-figure side given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its black-figure side to the Lysippides Painter."],"answer":"Andokides Painter","answerText":"Andokides Painter","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 515 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bilinguals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Andokides Painter</b> is an anonymous Athenian vase-painter of the later 6th century BCE, named after the potter Andokides, who employed him and whose signature his pots carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reads <i>epoiesen</i>, 'made it', and is cut into the foot: it names the man who shaped and fired the pot, not the one who drew the figures on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fewer than twenty vases survive from his hand, mostly amphorae with a few cups, their scenes drawn from the stories of Herakles and Dionysos and the tale of Ajax and Achilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is generally thought the first painter to work regularly in the red-figure technique after its invention at Athens around 530 to 520 BCE, and possibly its inventor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best known for bilingual vases, which carry the same design twice, once in red-figure and once in the older black-figure, so that the two techniques stand side by side on one pot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> An amphora in Berlin, signed on the foot, shows Herakles making off with the Delphic tripod while Apollo hauls at one of its legs and Athena watches; wrestlers fill the other side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A bilingual amphora in Munich has Herakles reclining at a feast with Athena beside him on both faces, the red-figure side given to this painter and the black-figure side to the Lysippides Painter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether those black-figure faces are a second man's work or the same painter in his older manner has never been settled, and the two have been proposed as one artist working in both techniques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A third amphora, in the Louvre, is decorated in a special technique like red-figure but on a white ground, with Amazons arming on one side and women bathing on the other, one swimming and another diving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His date is fixed from outside the pottery, by the securely dated reliefs of the Siphnian Treasury at Delphi, and it has been argued that Psiax, a finer and bolder draughtsman, realised what red-figure could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["“Andokides Painter,” Union List of Artist Names, ID 500030596, Getty Research Institute, http://vocab.getty.edu/page/ulan/500030596. [Open access]","“Andokides Painter (red-figure),” Kerameikos.org, https://kerameikos.org/id/andokides_painter_red_figure. [Open access]","“Berlin F 2159 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Berlin+F+2159&object=Vase. [Open access]","“Munich 2301 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Munich+2301&object=Vase. [Open access]","“Louvre F 203 (Vase),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Louvre+F+203&object=Vase. [Open access]","Amanda Herring Brendle, review of <i>Bilingual Vases: The Andokides Painter and the Lysippides Painter</i>, by Rui Morais, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 2018.05.17, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2018/2018.05.17/. [Open access]","H. B. Walters, <i>History of Ancient Pottery: Greek, Etruscan, and Roman</i>, vol. 1 (London: John Murray, 1905), 386–387, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient01walt. [Open access]","Jenifer Neils, review of <i>The Art of Vase-Painting in Classical Athens</i>, by Martin Robertson, <i>Bryn Mawr Classical Review</i> 1993.02.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1993/1993.02.11/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Andokides_Painter_ARV_3_1_Herakles_Apollon_tripod_-_wrestlers_%2801%29.jpg/1920px-Andokides_Painter_ARV_3_1_Herakles_Apollon_tripod_-_wrestlers_%2801%29.jpg","title":"Amphora signed by the potter Andokides, painted by the Andokides Painter, about 525 BCE","desc":"Attic red-figure amphora type A with lid. Side A: Athena, Herakles and Apollon struggling for the tripod, Artemis. Side B: young coach, two wrestlers. Athens, late archaic, about 525 BCE. Potter: Andokides, signed on the foot. Painter: Andokides Painter. Findspot Vulci; Berlin, Altes Museum (Antikensammlung) F 2159.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andokides_Painter_ARV_3_1_Herakles_Apollon_tripod_-_wrestlers_(01).jpg","alt":"A tall two-handled jar with a lid, standing on a museum plinth and black all over except for a band of figures in the orange colour of the clay: a bearded man in a lion skin strides away carrying a three-legged stand while a long-haired figure catches at it, a helmeted woman with spear and shield stands behind him and another woman watches from the right, and a signature is cut into the black foot"},"why":[{"q":"Why is this painter named after somebody else?","a":"Because the signature cut into the foot reads epoiesen, 'made it', and names the potter Andokides who shaped and fired the vase, not the man who drew the figures."},{"q":"Why are the bilingual vases the hardest part of his work to settle?","a":"Because it has never been settled whether the black-figure faces are a second man's work or the same painter in his older manner, and the two have been proposed as one artist."},{"q":"Why is his date fixed from outside the pottery?","a":"Because it rests on the securely dated reliefs of the Siphnian Treasury at Delphi rather than on anything the vases themselves record."}]},
{"id":"gr-332","num":332,"category":"Archaic Greece","difficulty":3,"question":"Archaic Greek sculptors carved the standing nude male <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the left foot advanced and the arms held at the sides, setting it over graves and in sanctuaries.","questions":["Older museum catalogues called the standing archaic youth an Apollo, but the modern name for the type is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since very few of the statues can be shown to be gods.","Cut from Naxian or Parian marble, painted, and sometimes over three metres tall, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood on Attic graves and as a dedication in Greek sanctuaries."],"answer":"kouros","answerText":"kouros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last example</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>kouros</b> is a free-standing statue of a young man, carved in the round and nude, standing frontally with the left foot advanced, the arms held down at the sides and the hands clenched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The example in New York is over life-size at 1.93 metres (6 feet 4 inches), cut from island marble, and keeps that scheme exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself is a modern label: nineteenth-century catalogues called such a figure an Apollo, though few can be shown to be gods, and the Greeks had instead put the start of free sculpture under Daedalus, a legendary Cretan said to have first opened statues' eyes and freed their arms from their sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus records that Egyptian sculptors worked to a measured canon, dividing the body into 21 parts and a quarter, and that an early Samian statue stood 'with the arms stretched stiffly down the sides and the legs separated in a stride', much as Egyptian work did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly every archaic male statue in Greece follows that same scheme, so the type is a convention held in common rather than the invention of a single workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The statues had two settled uses, standing over graves and serving as dedications in sanctuaries, and free sculpture in Greece grew out of those two habits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Marble came from Naxos and Paros, and the finished surface was painted; red survives on the hair, the eyebrows, the nipples and inside the nostrils of the best-preserved pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scale ranged from figures well under life-size to the colossal kouros dedicated to Poseidon at Sounion, which stands 3.05 metres (10 feet) tall, and the Dipylon head belongs to a statue of about 3 metres (10 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An unfinished kouros from Naxos survives still worked with the point, caught at the stage before any surface was smoothed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The series closes with the funerary Aristodikos of about 500 to 490 BCE, whose arms come a little away from the thighs and whose hair is cropped short, and who is reckoned the last of the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“New York 32.11.1 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=New+York+32.11.1&object=sculpture. [Open access]","“Athens, NM 3938 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+NM+3938&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>The Library of History</i> 1.98.5–9, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1933), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 36, 93–94, 100, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","“Archaic Period,” National Archaeological Museum, Athens, https://www.namuseum.gr/en/collection/archaiki-periodos/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Marble_statue_of_a_kouros_%28youth%29_MET_DT263.jpg/1920px-Marble_statue_of_a_kouros_%28youth%29_MET_DT263.jpg","title":"Marble kouros in the Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","desc":"Marble statue of a kouros (youth), Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 32.11.1, Fletcher Fund, 1932.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_statue_of_a_kouros_(youth)_MET_DT263.jpg","alt":"A nude marble statue of a standing young man, facing forward with the left foot advanced, arms straight down at the sides and hands closed, long hair falling behind the shoulders."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the modern label preferred to the older one?","a":"Because nineteenth-century catalogues called such a figure an Apollo, and few of them can be shown to be gods; the Greeks themselves put the start of free sculpture under Daedalus instead."},{"q":"Why is the type read as a shared convention rather than one workshop's invention?","a":"Because nearly every archaic male statue in Greece follows the same scheme, and Diodorus records an early Samian statue standing in the Egyptian manner, arms down and legs in a stride."},{"q":"Why does free sculpture in Greece look the way it does?","a":"Because it grew out of the two settled uses of these statues, standing over graves and serving as dedications in sanctuaries."}]},
{"id":"gr-333","num":333,"category":"Archaic Greece","difficulty":3,"question":"Standing frontally in a richly patterned chiton, with one hand drawing the skirt tight against the legs, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the draped female counterpart of the kouros.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the statue type found in numbers on the Athenian Acropolis, thrown down when the Persians sacked the city and buried where the returning Athenians found it.","Analysis of one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from an Attic grave recovered gold and lead foil and eleven pigments, so the type as first made was painted rather than white."],"answer":"kore","answerText":"kore","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 630 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last examples</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 490 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>kore</b> is a free-standing archaic Greek statue of a standing young woman, carved in the round and always clothed, the draped counterpart of the nude kouros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type varies little: all stand erect, looking straight to the front, the left foot slightly advanced, the right arm bent forward at the elbow to hold an offering while the left hand pulls the skirt tight across the legs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most wear a chiton pinned along the upper arm to make sleeves and, over it, a heavier garment folded into a diplois, whose deep folds are where the Attic sculptors spent their skill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whom they represent has never been settled: they can hardly be Athena, whose attributes could not have vanished without a trace, so the likeliest reading is that they are worshippers, though some call them priestesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One inscription records the offering of 'a maiden' to Poseidon by a fisherman, so such a figure could be a conventional gift rather than a portrait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest is the Nikandre, dedicated to Apollo on Delos about 640 to 630 BCE, life-size at 1.90 metres (6 feet 3 inches) but only 0.17 metres (7 inches) deep and so nearly flat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The largest group came out of the Athenian Acropolis, many together in a pit north-west of the Erechtheum, thrown down when the Persians sacked the city and buried where the returning Athenians found them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They stood over graves as well, and the Phrasikleia from Myrrhinous in Attica survives entire, cut from Parian marble by Aristion of Paros, 1.79 metres (5 feet 10 inches) tall and wearing a wreath, a necklace, earrings and bracelets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Re-examination of her surface recovered gold and lead foil and eleven pigments, and paint on an Acropolis figure 0.55 metres (1 foot 10 inches) high made the chiton blue and edged the outer garment red and blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The series runs out about 500 to 490 BCE with statues whose folds fall with real weight and whose faces are solemn and no longer smiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 164–165, 167, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","“Archaic Period,” National Archaeological Museum, Athens, https://www.namuseum.gr/en/collection/archaiki-periodos/. [Open access]","“Athens, NM 1 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+NM+1&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Amanda Claridge, “Looking for Colour on Greek and Roman Sculpture,” <i>Journal of Art Historiography</i> 5 (December 2011): 4, https://arthistoriography.files.wordpress.com/2011/12/claridge.pdf. [Open access]","“Athens, Acropolis 675 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Acropolis+675&object=sculpture. [Open access]","“Athens, Acropolis 674 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Acropolis+674&object=sculpture. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Phrasikleia_Kore%2C_Atenas%2C_Grecia%2C_2019_01.jpg/1280px-Phrasikleia_Kore%2C_Atenas%2C_Grecia%2C_2019_01.jpg","title":"The Phrasikleia Kore, National Archaeological Museum, Athens","desc":"Statue of a kore in Parian marble that stood on the grave of Phrasikleia at Merenda, ancient Myrrhinous, in Attica, made by the sculptor Aristion of Paros; accession 4889, National Archaeological Museum of Athens.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phrasikleia_Kore,_Atenas,_Grecia,_2019_01.jpg","alt":"A marble statue of a standing young woman in a long close-fitting chiton, facing forward, wearing a wreath and a necklace, her hair in beaded braids over the shoulders, holding a bud against her chest with the right hand and drawing the skirt with the left."},"why":[{"q":"Why is it doubted that these statues are the goddess?","a":"Because they can hardly be Athena, whose attributes could not have vanished without a trace, so the likeliest reading is that they are worshippers, though some call them priestesses."},{"q":"Why does one inscription weigh against reading them as portraits?","a":"Because it records the offering of 'a maiden' to Poseidon by a fisherman, which makes such a figure a conventional gift rather than a likeness of anybody."},{"q":"Why was the largest group of them found buried together?","a":"Because they were thrown down when the Persians sacked the city and buried in a pit north-west of the Erechtheum, where the returning Athenians left them."}]},
{"id":"gr-334","num":334,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Greek sculptors of the 6th century BCE turned almost every mouth up at the corners, so a warrior fighting or dying on a pediment wears the same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a goddess.","questions":["The ends of the lips were the hardest thing an early sculptor had to cut, and Attic workshops spent a century refining the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into something like an expression.","Statues carved just before the Persian sack have mouths that no longer turn up, and one droops at the corners, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> helps date a sculpture."],"answer":"archaic smile","answerText":"archaic smile","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aristion stele</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 510 – 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>archaic smile</b> is the upturned mouth that Greek sculptors gave to almost every figure they carved between about 600 and 480 BCE, whatever that figure was doing. Its simplest form is a single curve, as on the colossal head of Hera from Olympia, whose mouth is cut as one arc and so produces the smile in its most primitive form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not there from the start: on an early statue from Eleutherna in Crete the mouth is merely a straight line, with no attempt at expression of any kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ends of the lips were the hardest part of the face for an early carver, who was content to stop them with a vertical cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Attic workshops complicated the shape until the mouth was divided into three curves and the lips were worked off into the cheek by an imperceptible transition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the smile is not is a mood, and the west pediment of the temple at Aegina proves it: those warriors wear the same conventional smile whether they are fighting or dying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their limbs and muscles are drawn up to ease the pain of their wounds while their faces keep the archaic smile hardly modified at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the east pediment, carved a little later, the fallen warrior does not smile without reason: the same form is transformed by clenched teeth and drawn lips into an expression of anguish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same shape carries a different feeling on the grave stele of Aristion, signed by Aristokles about 510 to 500 BCE, where the form of the smile is preserved and its effect entirely changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On the latest Acropolis statues, made shortly before 480 BCE, one mouth has lost the convention and another actually droops at the corners, and the severe sculpture of the next generation leaves the smile behind altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 138, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 133, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 172–174, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Frank Bigelow Tarbell, <i>A History of Greek Art</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1922), 156–157, https://archive.org/details/historyofgreekar00tarbuoft. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 204–205, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 179, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","“Athens, NM 29 (Sculpture),” Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+NM+29&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Frank Bigelow Tarbell, <i>A History of Greek Art</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1922), 151–152, https://archive.org/details/historyofgreekar00tarbuoft. [Open access]","Ali Abd AL Mohsen Ali, “Characteristics of The Transitional Phase In Greek Sculpture Between Archaic And Classical,” <i>Al-Academy</i>, no. 114 (December 2024): 293–308, https://jcofarts.uobaghdad.edu.iq/index.php/jcofarts/article/view/1518. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Aphaia_pediment_head_W-III_Glyptothek_Munich_75.jpg/1280px-Aphaia_pediment_head_W-III_Glyptothek_Munich_75.jpg","title":"Head of a warrior from the temple at Aegina","desc":"Marble head of a warrior from the west pediment of the temple at Aegina, identified by the file's record as warrior III and dated to about 505–500 BCE; Glyptothek, Munich.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aphaia_pediment_head_W-III_Glyptothek_Munich_75.jpg","alt":"A marble head of a young man in a pushed-back crested helmet, tilted a little backwards, with almond-shaped eyes, a band of small snail curls across the brow, and a mouth clearly turned up at both corners."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the smile not to be read as a mood?","a":"Because the west pediment at Aegina gives the same conventional smile to warriors fighting and dying alike, their limbs drawn up in pain while their faces are hardly modified."},{"q":"Why did the corners of the mouth take so long to master?","a":"Because the ends of the lips were the hardest part of the face for an early carver, who was content to stop them with a vertical cut before Attic workshops worked them off into the cheek."},{"q":"Why can the same shape carry anguish rather than a smile?","a":"Because on the later east pediment at Aegina the form is transformed by clenched teeth and drawn lips, and on the stele of Aristion its effect is entirely changed while the form is preserved."}]},
{"id":"gr-335","num":335,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Under life size and cut from Parian marble about 530 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named for a heavy garment that recent readings of her paint call a god's robe.","questions":["The overfall that gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> her modern name is divided along one arm and unbroken along the other, so the garment may never have been a peplos at all.","A heavy fall of cloth turns the lower half of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a pillar, and that stiffness once made her look like a late imitation of much older work."],"answer":"Peplos Kore","answerText":"Peplos Kore","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 525 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.18 m (3 ft 10 in)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">February 1886, on the Acropolis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Peplos Kore</b> is a marble statue of a young woman set up on the Athenian Acropolis about 530 to 525 BCE, named in modern times for the heavy garment she seems to wear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was found west of the Erechtheum in February 1886, is cut from Parian marble, and stands 1.18 metres (3 feet 10 inches) without her plinth, well under life size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She is nearly whole, missing only the separately carved left forearm and the lower right corner of the skirt with the front of the feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The left arm reached forward to hold an offering; the right hung at her side, closed on something thin enough to need a hole bored through the fist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The garment will not behave: the overfall is divided along the left arm but unbroken on the right, sewn to nothing, though pin holes show it was fastened at both shoulders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her paint was still vivid when she came out of the ground, and a watercolour made for the first report records more of it than survives now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That scheme ran a border of palmettes and lotus on a red ground beneath a green meander, set three green stripes on the girdle, and made her hair dark red and her lips red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A bronze wreath was fixed into holes cut round her head, some nails still in place, and the shaft of a <i>meniskos</i> stood broken off level with the crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her pillar-like lower half brought a charge of archaism, a later carver imitating an older manner, which her early cataloguers refused, matching her instead to the Moschophoros and placing her among the earliest Acropolis figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A reading of the colour now turns that round, taking the garment for an <i>ependytes</i>, the robe of the gods, and the stiff form for a cult image copied in stone, though much of any such reconstruction is guesswork.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Perseus Digital Library, “Athens, Acropolis 679 (Sculpture),” Perseus Art and Archaeology Artifact Browser, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens,+Acropolis+679&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Guy Dickins, <i>Catalogue of the Acropolis Museum</i>, vol. 1, <i>Archaic Sculpture</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1912), 223–25, https://archive.org/details/catalogueacropo00cassgoog. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 174, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gard. [Open access]","Guy Dickins, <i>Catalogue of the Acropolis Museum</i>, vol. 1, <i>Archaic Sculpture</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1912), 16, https://archive.org/details/catalogueacropo00cassgoog. [Open access]","Amanda Claridge, “Looking for Colour on Greek and Roman Sculpture,” <i>Journal of Art Historiography</i> 5 (December 2011): 4, http://arthistoriography.files.wordpress.com/2011/12/claridge.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Watercolor_of_Acropolis_kore_679_by_%C3%89mile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_%28ArchEph_1887%2C_pl_9%29.jpg/1280px-Watercolor_of_Acropolis_kore_679_by_%C3%89mile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_%28ArchEph_1887%2C_pl_9%29.jpg","title":"Peplos Kore","desc":"The Peplos Kore as her colour survived at the moment of discovery, drawn for the excavation report of 1887. Watercolour by Emile Gillieron, lithograph by K. Grundman.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Watercolor_of_Acropolis_kore_679_by_%C3%89mile_Gilli%C3%A9ron_(ArchEph_1887,_pl_9).jpg","alt":"A colour plate showing the same statue twice, front and back: a standing young woman in a long skirt and a belted overfall, her hair in red braids, with painted borders of palmettes and meander on the garment."},"why":[{"q":"Why does her garment resist being identified?","a":"Because the overfall is divided along the left arm but unbroken on the right and sewn to nothing, though pin holes show it was fastened at both shoulders."},{"q":"Why is a watercolour part of the evidence for her?","a":"Because her paint was still vivid when she came out of the ground, and the one made for the first report records more of the scheme than survives on the marble now."},{"q":"Why was she once suspected of being a deliberate archaism?","a":"Because her pillar-like lower half looks like a later carver imitating an older manner, a charge her cataloguers refused by matching her to the Moschophoros among the earliest Acropolis figures."}]},
{"id":"gr-336","num":336,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Broken into ten pieces to be smuggled abroad and recovered from a Paris dealer in 1937, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a life-size Attic kouros whose base epigram names a dead warrior.","questions":["Heavier and rounder in the flesh than any kouros before it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeps the old frontal stance while its thighs, calves and buttocks swell out of the type.","An epigram on the base usually read with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asks a passer-by to stop and pity Kroisos, whom fierce Ares destroyed among the front ranks."],"answer":"Anavysos Kouros","answerText":"Anavysos Kouros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 520 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.94 m (6 ft 4 in)</span></div>","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Anavysos Kouros</b> is a life-size marble statue of a naked young man, carved in Attica about 530 to 520 BCE and now in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands 1.94 metres (6 feet 4 inches) high, is cut from Parian marble, and holds the kouros scheme exactly: frontal, the weight even on both feet with the left one slightly advanced, arms down the flanks, fists closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What separates it from the type is the flesh: the chest is broad, the waist narrow, and the abdomen, thighs, calves and buttocks swell into contours its first publisher called almost feminine and in marked contrast to other kouroi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hair is set in ten spiral curls across the forehead, bound by a narrow fillet, and falls behind in twenty parallel tresses whose divisions curve rather than forming the continuous rectangular grid of earlier statues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Red paint survives on that fillet, on the hair of the head and pubes and on the irises, and the almond eyes with their prominent balls probably carried painted pupils too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The statue marked a grave, and it is usually read with an inscribed base carrying two lines of verse: 'Stand and pity by the <i>sema</i> of Kroisos, dead, whom fierce Ares once destroyed among the front ranks.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That name is why the figure is also called the Kroisos Kouros, and a <i>sema</i> is the grave marker itself, the thing the passer-by is asked to stop beside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether base and statue belong together has been doubted, and most accounts keep the pity the verse asks for well away from the smiling figure above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was smuggled out of Greece by sea, broken into ten pieces for packing, and recovered by Greek police in Paris from a dealer, reaching the museum in three cases early in August 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Andreas Panayotakis reassembled it with plaster in the left arm and leg, and it went on show in the archaic gallery, the missing arm pieces arriving later from Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Perseus Digital Library, “Athens, NM 3851 (Sculpture),” Perseus Art and Archaeology Artifact Browser, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens,+NM+3851&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Alex. Philadelpheus, “The Anavysos Kouros,” <i>Annual of the British School at Athens</i> 36 (1935–1936): 1–4, https://archive.org/details/annual-of-the-british-school-at-athens_1935-1936_36. [Open access]","Seth Nathaniel Estrin, “Objects of Pity: Art and Emotion in Archaic and Classical Greece” (PhD diss., University of California, Berkeley, 2016), 1, https://escholarship.org/uc/item/54d8k6q1. [Open access]","Paul Lemerle, “Musée National,” <i>Bulletin de correspondance hellénique</i> 61 (1937): 441–42, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1937_num_61_1_2741. [Open access]","Paul Lemerle, “Musée National,” <i>Bulletin de correspondance hellénique</i> 62 (1938): 443–46, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1938_num_62_1_2717. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","archaic greece","sculpture","attica"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Kouros_from_Anabyssos_Kroisos_NAMA_3851_102565.jpg/1280px-Kouros_from_Anabyssos_Kroisos_NAMA_3851_102565.jpg","title":"Anavysos Kouros","desc":"Kouros in Parian marble, 1.94 metres (6 feet 4 inches) tall, from about 530 BCE; found at Anavysos in Attica, where it stood at the grave of Kroisos. National Archaeological Museum of Athens 3851.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kouros_from_Anabyssos_Kroisos_NAMA_3851_102565.jpg","alt":"A life-size marble statue of a naked young man seen from slightly to his left, standing with the left foot advanced, arms held down along the flanks and both fists closed, long braided hair falling to the shoulders, and a paler plaster join across the lower left leg."},"why":[{"q":"Why does this figure stand out among statues of its type?","a":"Because the flesh departs from the scheme: a broad chest, a narrow waist, and abdomen, thighs, calves and buttocks swelling into contours its first publisher called almost feminine."},{"q":"Why is the pairing with the inscribed base uncertain?","a":"Because whether base and statue belong together has been doubted, and most accounts keep the pity the verse asks for well away from the smiling figure above it."},{"q":"Why did it reach the museum in pieces?","a":"Because it was smuggled out of Greece by sea, broken into ten pieces for packing, and recovered by Greek police in Paris from a dealer, arriving in three cases."}]},
{"id":"gr-337","num":337,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Cut from Hymettian marble about 570 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a bearded Athenian carrying a calf across his shoulders to Athena, the animal's legs gathered in his crossed hands.","questions":["An inscription running right to left on the poros base of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> records that Rhombos, son of Palos, dedicated the statue, and the figure is generally read as Rhombos himself.","The beard was left as a smooth raised surface for paint and the eyes hollowed for inlays now lost, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeps the kouros stance while carrying its offering."],"answer":"Moschophoros","answerText":"Moschophoros","difficulty":4,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Moschophoros</b>, or calf-bearer, is a marble statue of a bearded man carrying a calf across his shoulders, dedicated on the Athenian Acropolis about 570 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cut from Hymettian marble and 1.65 metres (5 feet 5 inches) high without its base, it keeps the kouros pose, frontal with the left leg advanced and both feet flat, while the beard, the cloak and the animal mark the man out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A thin himation hangs from both shoulders nearly to the knees, leaving the chest bare, and is told from the flesh only by its flatter surface and the two incised lines of its border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The eyes were hollowed for inlays now lost, the beard left as a smooth raised surface for paint rather than carved, and green colour survives behind the calf, whose forelegs are twisted impossibly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Crossed arms and crossed legs bind man and animal into one group, a motif common in small bronzes but used here, as far as is known, on a large marble statue for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The greater part of the figure was found in 1864 in digging the foundations of the old museum on the Acropolis, and the base with the feet in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two were joined, and the inscription on the poros block below runs from right to left to say that Rhombos, son of Palos, dedicated it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first letter of the name is broken away, so it is a restoration: a K was proposed when the base was first published, the reading Rh in 1890, and later opinion took the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The statue was at first called a Hermes Kriophoros, an Apollo Nomios or Theseus with the Marathonian bull, and is now read as the dedicator himself, not a portrait but a type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Conservation in 2014 replaced the plaster in-fillings standing for the missing lower legs with six steel supports, uncovered the original attachment to the base, and redefined the height from 1.61 to 1.65 metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Perseus Digital Library, “Athens, Acropolis 624 (Sculpture),” Perseus Art and Archaeology Artifact Browser, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens,+Acropolis+624&object=sculpture. [Open access]","Guy Dickins, <i>Catalogue of the Acropolis Museum</i>, vol. 1, <i>Archaic Sculpture</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1912), 156–59, https://archive.org/details/catalogueoftheac005891mbp. [Open access]","Acropolis Museum, “Male statue with its base. The ‘Calf-bearer’,” Explore Collections, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/male-statue-its-base-calf-bearer. [Open access]","Acropolis Museum, “‘Calf-Bearer’,” Conservation of Antiquities, 2014, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/conservation-antiquities/calf-bearer. [Open access]","Henri Lechat, <i>La sculpture attique avant Phidias</i> (Paris: Alphonse Picard et fils, 1904), 106, https://archive.org/details/lasculptureattiq92lech. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","art","archaeology","greece","archaic greece","sculpture","attica","religion"],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.65 m (5 ft 5 in)</span></div>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/ACMA_Moschophoros.jpg","title":"Moschophoros","desc":"The Moschophoros photographed in the old Acropolis Museum in 2005, before the statue was remounted in 2014 and the in-fillings standing for its missing lower legs were removed. Acropolis Museum 624.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_Moschophoros.jpg","alt":"A marble statue of a bearded man standing frontally with a calf laid across his shoulders, its four legs gathered in his crossed hands at his chest, a thin cloak hanging from his shoulders and leaving the torso bare, the eye sockets empty, and the legs ending in restored blocks below the knees."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the dedicator's name only a restoration?","a":"Because the first letter is broken away on the poros block, so a K was proposed when the base was first published, the reading Rh in 1890, and later opinion took the second."},{"q":"Why is the group's design remarkable for a large marble?","a":"Because crossed arms and crossed legs bind man and animal into one, a motif common in small bronzes but used here, as far as is known, on a large marble statue for the first time."},{"q":"Why do the beard and eyes look unfinished?","a":"Because the eyes were hollowed for inlays now lost and the beard left as a smooth raised surface for paint rather than carved, as the surviving green colour behind the calf shows was intended."}]},
{"id":"gr-338","num":338,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Excavation at Dreros, Isthmia and Thermos shows how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> developed over two centuries from a one-room hall into a colonnaded building roofed in painted terracotta.","questions":["Since the burnt offering was made at an altar standing in front of it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> served chiefly as a house for the god's image and for the gifts left beside it.","Built of mudbrick on stone footings with wooden columns, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried its ornament in painted terracotta, and at Olympia its wooden posts were replaced one by one in stone."],"answer":"archaic temple","answerText":"archaic temple","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dreros temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 775 – 750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Isthmia temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thermos temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 – 610 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Olympia Heraion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["building","religion","art","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"abstract":"The <b>archaic temple</b> was a roofed house built for a god's image, and the sacrifice that gave it its purpose was burnt outside, at an altar before its door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest one known stands at Dreros on Crete, a single walled room about eleven metres by seven of small dressed stones, dated to the second quarter of the 8th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its plan is a hall rather than a shrine, with a stone-lined hearth in the middle of the floor, one or two axial columns carrying the roof, and a bench in a corner for offerings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beside that bench a later hand built a box of upright slabs packed with goats' horns, and on this altar of horns probably stood three figures of hammered bronze over wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing about it was monumental, and what the next two centuries added was scale, a colonnade and a roof of baked clay. <br><br> By about 700 BCE the temple of Poseidon at Isthmia had stone walls painted with panels outside and a surround of seven by nineteen wooden columns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> East of it lay the sacrificial ground, still strewn with ash, burnt bones and the smooth pebbles worshippers brought to share symbolically in the killing, and later a stone altar forty metres long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Thermos in Aetolia a temple of mudbrick and timber on stone foundations went up about 630 to 610 BCE, dressed in terracotta roof tiles, gutters, moulded antefixes, a sphinx for the gable and ten painted metope plaques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under it lay a long Geometric hall ringed by an oval colonnade whose thick ash layers, holding burnt bone and bronze statuettes, show it too had served a god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Heraion at Olympia, of about 600 BCE and the earliest monumental temple in Greece, stood on wooden columns exchanged for stone as the timbers rotted, so its capitals differ by date<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>; at Corinth a stone temple of about 540 replaced one of about 625.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 8, 297-301, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","D. J. Blackman, \"Dreros,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=dreros. [Open access]","Oscar Broneer, \"Isthmia,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=isthmia. [Open access]","Nancy Bookidis, \"Thermos,\" in <i>The Princeton Encyclopedia of Classical Sites</i>, ed. Richard Stillwell (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1976), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0006:entry=thermos. [Open access]","\"Olympia, Temple of Hera,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Olympia%2C+Temple+of+Hera&object=Building. [Open access]","\"Corinth, Temple of Apollo,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Corinth%2C+Temple+of+Apollo&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg/1920px-Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg","title":"Temple of Apollo, Corinth","desc":"The archaic Doric temple of Apollo at Corinth, built about 540 BCE on the site of an earlier temple of about 625.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korinth_BW_2017-10-10_10-55-28.jpg","alt":"Seven weathered limestone Doric columns standing on a stepped platform, five of them still carrying a stretch of architrave, with a mountain ridge behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the sacrifice burnt outside the building?","a":"Because the temple was a roofed house built for the god's image, while the sacrifice that gave it its purpose was made at an altar before its door."},{"q":"Why is the earliest example called a hall rather than a shrine?","a":"Because it is a single walled room of small dressed stones with a hearth in the middle of the floor, axial columns carrying the roof, and a bench in a corner for offerings."},{"q":"Why do the capitals of the Heraion at Olympia differ by date?","a":"Because it stood on wooden columns that were exchanged for stone as the timbers rotted, so each replacement was cut in the manner of its own time."}]},
{"id":"gr-339","num":339,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"A baseless column of twenty shallow flutes, capped by a convex echinus under a square abacus, is the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the older of the two systems Greek temples were built in.","questions":["Triglyphs were explained in antiquity as boards nailed over sawn beam ends, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was read as carpentry turned to stone, with mutules under the cornice standing for rafter ends.","A triglyph must sit over each column and another must close the frieze, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met at every corner a conflict solved only by a wide metope or a narrowed bay."],"answer":"Doric order","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Aphaia, Aegina</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parthenon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vitruvius</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Doric order</b> is one of the two systems of column and entablature that Greek architects built their temples in, and Vitruvius, writing in the 1st century BCE, held it the first to arise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Doric column stands straight on the platform with no base of its own, and its shaft carries twenty shallow flutes meeting in sharp edges rather than the flat fillets of the Ionic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital is a spreading convex moulding, the echinus, under a square slab called the abacus, and abacus, echinus, necking and the shaft top were cut from one block.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shaft was usually built of drums pegged together with wood, though at the temple of Aphaia on Aegina, of about 500 to 480 BCE, all but three columns were cut in one piece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Archaic shafts taper strongly from foot to summit, which lent an air of stability, and the flutes were cut after the column stood so the edges came out sharp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Above the columns lies the architrave, a plain band closed by a fillet with short strips and rows of peg-like guttae under it; the fillet was painted red, the strips and guttae blue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The frieze above alternates triglyphs, blocks cut with vertical channels, with metopes, the roughly square panels between them, which were often filled with sculpture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vitruvius derived it from carpentry: triglyphs stood for boards fixed over the sawn ends of the ceiling beams and painted with blue wax, metopes for the gaps between, and the sloping mutules under the cornice for the rafter ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He also records a rule the order cannot keep, since a triglyph should stand over the centre of every column and another should close the frieze at the corner, and at the corner the two collide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Either the corner metope runs half a triglyph too wide or the end bay is narrowed instead, and the narrowing was built: on the Parthenon the corner bays measure 3.68 metres (12 feet) against 4.29 (14 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Doric order","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.1, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 47-48, 79-80, 87-88, 104, 184-85, 218, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","\"Aegina, Temple of Aphaia,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Aegina%2C+Temple+of+Aphaia&object=Building. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.2, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.3, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","\"Athens, Parthenon,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Parthenon&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/Doric-order-labeled.jpg","title":"The parts of the Doric order","desc":"A rendering of the corner of a Doric temple with the parts named: the architrave with its taenia, regulae and guttae, the frieze of triglyphs and metopes, and the mutules, geison and sima above. J.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doric-order-labeled.jpg","alt":"A grey rendering of the corner of a Greek temple, its fluted columns carrying a plain band, a row of channelled blocks alternating with blank panels, and a projecting cornice, with the parts labelled in English."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Vitruvius derive the order from carpentry?","a":"Because he read triglyphs as boards fixed over the sawn ends of the ceiling beams and painted with blue wax, metopes as the gaps between, and the sloping mutules as rafter ends."},{"q":"Why can the frieze not obey its own rule at a corner?","a":"Because a triglyph should stand over the centre of every column and another should close the frieze at the corner, and at the corner those two demands collide."},{"q":"Why were the flutes cut only after the column stood?","a":"Because cutting them in place made the edges where they meet come out sharp, which is what distinguishes them from the flat fillets of the other order."}]},
{"id":"gr-340","num":340,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Named for the Greek cities of the Asia Minor coast, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives its columns a moulded base of their own and a capital wound into a pair of spiral volutes.","questions":["A column of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands about nine diameters high on 24 flutes, and the architrave above it is stepped back into three overlapping bands rather than left as one plain face.","Builders in Asia Minor set dentils straight on the architrave while Athens kept a continuous sculpted frieze, two regional forms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Doric entablature kept one fixed arrangement."],"answer":"Ionic order","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ephesos temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">columns c. 560 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erechtheion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">421 – 406 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Proportions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 diameters, 24 flutes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ionic order</b> is one of the two systems of column and entablature Greek architects built their temples in, and it takes its name from the Ionian cities of the Asia Minor coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vitruvius held it younger than the Doric and read it as a woman's figure, the base standing in place of a shoe, the volutes hanging like curled ringlets, and the flutes falling like the folds of a matron's robe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An Ionic column rises from a moulded base of its own, in two families: the Asiatic, a torus set over a hollow trochilos, and the Attic, two tori separated by a scotia, which reached its settled form at the Erechtheion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its shaft carries 24 flutes, deeper than the Doric and parted by flat fillets rather than sharp edges, though archaic builders used other numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital's mark is a pair of volutes, spirals wound out from a small circle called the eye, set out by Vitruvius as quarter-circles each half the eye's width smaller than the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It is the slenderer of the two: Vitruvius allows the Ionic column nine diameters against the Doric's seven, and the north porch of the Erechtheion stands 7.64 metres (25 feet) high on a lower diameter of 0.817 metres (2 feet 8 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The architrave is broken into three overlapping bands, the fasciae, where the Doric presents one plain face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The frieze runs unbroken, taking the name zophoros when it carries figure sculpture, where the Doric frieze is cut into triglyphs and metopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The entablature is not one thing, though: in Asia Minor a jutting course of dentils could sit straight on the architrave with no frieze, as at Priene, while Athenian builders kept the frieze and left the dentils out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor was any of it fixed at the start: in the archaic temple of Artemis at Ephesos the flutes do not space evenly under one capital, and the variation of parts there has been called as marked as in a Gothic building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ionic order","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.1, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 83-84, 101, 103, 108, 139, 184-85, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 3.5, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","\"Athens, Erechtheion,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Athens%2C+Erechtheion&object=Building. [Open access]","W. R. Lethaby, <i>Greek Buildings Represented by Fragments in the British Museum</i> (London: B. T. Batsford, 1908), 22-23, 33, https://archive.org/details/greekbuildingsre00leth. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Vitruvius_the_Ten_Books_on_Architecture_-_The_Ionic_Order_according_to_Vitruvius.png","title":"The Ionic order, drawn to Vitruvius's proportions","desc":"A plate from Morris Hicky Morgan's 1914 translation of Vitruvius, captioned \"The Ionic order according to Vitruvius compared with the order of the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus\" and noting the difference between the Roman and the Greek relation of the baluster-side of the capital to the echinus.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vitruvius_the_Ten_Books_on_Architecture_-_The_Ionic_Order_according_to_Vitruvius.png","alt":"A line drawing of two Ionic orders side by side, each a fluted column on a moulded base under a capital of spiral volutes carrying an architrave of three overlapping bands; the left is closed by a plain sculptured frieze, the right by a row of small blocks, with a compass diagram of the volute's spiral between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Vitruvius read the order as a woman's figure?","a":"Because the base stands in place of a shoe, the volutes hang like curled ringlets, and the flutes fall like the folds of a matron's robe."},{"q":"Why can the entablature not be described as one thing?","a":"Because in Asia Minor a jutting course of dentils could sit straight on the architrave with no frieze, as at Priene, while Athenian builders kept the frieze and left the dentils out."},{"q":"Why is the archaic temple at Ephesos evidence against a fixed system?","a":"Because its flutes do not space evenly under one capital, and the variation of parts there has been called as marked as in a Gothic building."}]},
{"id":"gr-341","num":341,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"A Greek temple built on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a single ring of columns right round its walls, leaving a covered walk between that colonnade and the enclosed building inside it.","questions":["Doubling the surrounding colonnade of a temple laid out on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes it dipteral, while engaging those columns against the cella wall instead makes the building pseudoperipteral.","The same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was set round cellas of quite different shapes, which is one reason for thinking the ring was applied to the temple rather than grown out of it."],"answer":"peripteral plan","answerText":"peripteral plan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple C</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zeus, Olympia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 470 – 456 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vitruvius</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 by 11 columns</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stylobate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3:1 archaic to 2:1 later</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>peripteral plan</b> sets a single ring of columns right round the walls of a Greek temple, so that what a worshipper met was a colonnade with a house inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vitruvius lists it among seven elementary forms, the temple in antis, the prostyle, the amphiprostyle, the peripteral, the pseudodipteral, the dipteral and the hypaethral, each named for what its columns do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His peripteral temple carries six columns in front and six behind with eleven along each flank counting the corners, standing one intercolumniation clear of the walls to leave a walk right round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls that walk the pteroma, and the house within keeps porches of its own, a pronaos in front and usually an opisthodomos behind, the cella between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Doubling the ring makes a temple dipteral, as at Ephesos; engaged columns suggesting a ring that is not there make it pseudoperipteral, as at Akragas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It was not the natural end of a series: had the temple grown from in antis through double in antis to peripteral, cella and surround would have agreed from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They do not, and the colonnade is better read as deliberately applied as a sign of religious distinction, a considerable time passing before it fitted the cella within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same ring was laid round quite different buildings: a cella in antis at Temple D at Selinus, one doubly in antis at the temple of Zeus at Olympia, a prostyle at Temple C, an amphiprostyle at the Parthenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its proportions moved: archaic stylobates often approach three to one, classical ones about two and a half, Hellenistic ones about two, though exceptions are frequent enough to make the plan weak evidence of date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Temple C at Selinus, of the first half of the 6th century BCE, is six by seventeen, its early date attested by that elongated plan, where the temple of Zeus at Olympia, of about 456 BCE, is six by thirteen on a stylobate of 64.12 by 27.68 metres (210 by 91 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 3.2, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 4.4, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 127, 308-13, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","\"Selinus, Temple C,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Selinus%2C+Temple+C&object=Building. [Open access]","\"Olympia, Temple of Zeus,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Olympia%2C+Temple+of+Zeus&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Peripteros_1.1_-_Anthony_Rich%2C_Illustrated_Companion_to_the_Latin_Dictionary%2C_and_Greek_Lexicon%2C_p._491.jpg","title":"Peripteros","desc":"Peripteros. Woodcut from Anthony Rich, The Illustrated Companion to the Latin Dictionary and Greek Lexicon (1849), p. 491.","credit":"Anthony Rich, Illustrated Companion to the Latin Dictionary and Greek Lexicon (1849), p. 491, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peripteros_1.1_-_Anthony_Rich,_Illustrated_Companion_to_the_Latin_Dictionary,_and_Greek_Lexicon,_p._491.jpg","alt":"Ground plan of a temple: a single ring of columns runs right round a rectangular walled building with a porch at each end."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the colonnade read as applied rather than grown?","a":"Because had the temple developed from in antis through double in antis, cella and surround would have agreed from the start; they do not, so the ring reads as a deliberate sign of religious distinction."},{"q":"Why can the plan's proportions not date a temple?","a":"Because although archaic stylobates often approach three to one and later ones grow squarer, the exceptions are frequent enough to make the plan weak evidence of date."},{"q":"Why does the same ring appear round quite different buildings?","a":"Because the colonnade was laid round whatever cella the builders had — in antis at Temple D at Selinus, doubly in antis at Olympia, prostyle at Temple C, amphiprostyle at the Parthenon."}]},
{"id":"gr-342","num":342,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Oak columns at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were exchanged for stone one at a time as the timbers rotted, so its Doric capitals differ in profile according to when each was replaced.","questions":["Every fourth year the Sixteen Women wove a robe for Hera and held foot races for girls at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose track was the Olympic stadium shortened by about a sixth.","Pausanias found the quoit of Iphitus and a table by Colotes inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and was told a wounded soldier’s body had turned up in its decayed roof."],"answer":"Heraion of Olympia","answerText":"Heraion of Olympia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Doric peripteral, 6 by 16 columns</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stylobate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18.75 by 50.01 m (62 by 164 feet)</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Heraion of Olympia</b>, Hera's temple in the sanctuary of Zeus, dates from about 600 BCE and is the earliest monumental temple in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is Doric and peripteral, six columns by sixteen on a two-stepped platform, its stylobate 18.75 by 50.01 metres (62 by 164 feet), with an east-opening cella between a pronaos and a deeper opisthodomos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its walls were sun-dried brick and its columns and entablature wood, the materials of a Mycenaean palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oaken columns were exchanged for stone one at a time as the timbers rotted, so the capitals run from archaic through developed to decadent by the date of each replacement, and one oak still stood in the rear chamber in Pausanias's day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Buttresses ran along the cella walls, four a side, with a later colonnade of fourteen Doric columns in front of them making two narrow aisles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias gives it 169 feet long, 63 wide and not short of 50 high, credits it to the people of Scillus about eight years after Oxylus took the throne of Elis, and names no architect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every fourth year the Sixteen Women wove Hera a robe and held the Heraea, foot races for unmarried girls in three age classes, hair down and right shoulder bared, over the Olympic track shortened by a sixth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Winners took olive crowns, a portion of the cow sacrificed to Hera, and the right to set up inscribed statues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside stood a table of ivory and gold by Colotes holding the victors' crowns, the quoit of Iphitus with the Olympic truce lettered round it in a circle, and a small ivory couch called Hippodameia's toy; Polemo of Ilium, in the earlier 2nd century BCE, listed 33 silver cups and gold vessels kept there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its age was the point: Polemo called it the old temple of Hera, and the guide Aristarchus told Pausanias that repairing the decayed roof turned up a wounded soldier's body between the tiles and the ceiling, from the battle in the Altis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","archaeology","religion","greece","archaic","place"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.16, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.20, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","\"Olympia, Temple of Hera,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Olympia%2C+Temple+of+Hera&object=Building. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 17, 21, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Studies in Greek Scenery, Legend and History</i> (London: Macmillan, 1917), 134, 138, 144, https://archive.org/details/studiesingreeksc00frazuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Curtius_Olympia_3_t_02.jpg","title":"The Heraion of Olympia under excavation","desc":"The Heraion seen from the south-east during the German excavations, with workmen standing at points of the plan. Photograph by Gebr. Romaidis of Patras, published in Ernst Curtius, ed., Die Ausgrabungen zu Olympia, vol. 3 (Berlin, 1877-78).","credit":"Gebr. Romaidis, Patras, in Ernst Curtius, ed., Die Ausgrabungen zu Olympia, vol. 3 (Berlin, 1877-78), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curtius_Olympia_3_t_02.jpg","alt":"A temple reduced to its lowest courses and stumps of columns, with drums and capitals lying on the ground and men standing among them on a hillside."},"locator":{"name":"Olympia","at":[21.63,37.6383]},"why":[{"q":"Why do the capitals of one temple run from archaic to decadent?","a":"Because the oaken columns were exchanged for stone one at a time as the timbers rotted, so each was cut in the manner of the date of its replacement."},{"q":"Why is the building said to use the materials of a Mycenaean palace?","a":"Because its walls were sun-dried brick and its columns and entablature wood, which is what makes it the earliest monumental temple in Greece rather than a fully stone one."},{"q":"Why did repairing the roof turn up a body?","a":"Because a wounded soldier from the battle in the Altis had got between the tiles and the ceiling, and the decayed roof was not opened again until Pausanias's guide told him of it."}]},
{"id":"gr-343","num":343,"category":"Archaic Greece","question":"Built about 580 BCE on the island of Korkyra, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries the earliest sculptured pediment surviving from a Greek temple, a limestone gable filled by a colossal winged Gorgon.","questions":["Doric and pseudo-dipteral, eight columns by seventeen on a platform of 47.9 by 22.4 metres (157 by 73 feet), the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided its cella into three aisles with two interior rows of ten columns.","Filling a shallow gable by size rather than depth, the sculptor of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the centre to a Gorgon and her beasts and squeezed Zeus and a giant into one corner."],"answer":"Temple of Artemis at Corfu","answerText":"Temple of Artemis at Corfu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 580 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 by 17 columns</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pediment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22.16 m wide</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Temple of Artemis at Corfu</b>, on the island the Greeks called Korkyra, went up about 580 BCE and carries the earliest sculptured pediment that survives from a Greek temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is Doric and pseudo-dipteral, eight columns by seventeen on a platform of 47.9 by 22.4 metres (157 by 73 feet), with an east-opening cella between a pronaos and an opisthodomos, each of them carrying two columns between the wall ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two interior rows of ten columns apiece divided the cella into three aisles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its west pediment is limestone, 22.16 metres (73 feet) wide and 3.15 metres (10 feet 4 inches) high at the apex, and at the centre stands a colossal winged Gorgon, snakes in her hair and eyes staring, in the kneeling posture archaic art used for running flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pegasus and Chrysaor stand at her shoulders, and a great cat fills the breadth on either side, head turned to the front and body in profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Hesiod has the two of them spring from Medusa at the moment Perseus cut off her head, so the pediment does not show that moment but sets all three together and whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The corners work on a much smaller scale: Zeus swings his thunderbolt at a giant at one end, and at the other a seated woman sits before a bearded man lying dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A shallow triangle is awkward to fill, and this sculptor filled it by size rather than by depth, giving the Gorgon and the cats the height and squeezing the gods into the ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The figures are square in build and conceived from front and side at once, chest full-face above legs in profile, with much of the detail incised rather than modelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Corinth held Corcyra and the staring Gorgon was a Corinthian motif; the same group stood at both ends of the roof, which later temples did not repeat, and in the Archaeological Museum of Corfu, which holds the pediment, the monster is taken for Artemis herself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","art","archaeology","religion","greece","archaic"],"sources":["\"Corcyra, Temple of Artemis,\" building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Corcyra%2C+Temple+of+Artemis&object=Building. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 159, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","\"Corcyra, Temple of Artemis, west pediment,\" cast 12, Museum of Classical Archaeology, University of Cambridge, https://museum.classics.cam.ac.uk/collections/casts/corcyra-temple-artemis-west-pediment. [Open access]","\"Archaeological Museum of Corfu,\" Ministry of Culture and Sports, Odysseus, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/1/eh151.jsp?obj_id=3420. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Theogony</i> 270-283, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0130%3Acard%3D270. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg/1920px-West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg","title":"West pediment of the Temple of Artemis at Corfu","desc":"The reconstructed west pediment, with the winged Gorgon at the centre, a great cat on either side and small figures in the corners. Dr.K.","credit":"Dr.K., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_pediment_from_the_Temple_of_Artemis_in_Corfu.jpg","alt":"A wide shallow stone gable. A crouching winged figure with a grimacing face fills the centre, a long cat lies on each side of her, and tiny figures stand in the narrow corners."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the pediment not show the moment of the beheading?","a":"Because Hesiod has Pegasus and Chrysaor spring from Medusa when Perseus cut off her head, and the sculptor instead sets all three together and whole at the centre."},{"q":"Why did the sculptor fill the triangle by size rather than depth?","a":"Because a shallow pediment is awkward to fill, so the Gorgon and the flanking cats take the height while the gods are squeezed into the corners at a much smaller scale."},{"q":"Why is a staring Gorgon expected on a Corfu temple?","a":"Because Corinth held Corcyra and the staring Gorgon was a Corinthian motif, so the island's sculpture follows its mother city."}]},
{"id":"gr-344","num":344,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Herodotus says the islanders of Siphnos were the richest of their day and built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Delphi from a tenth of the yield of their gold and silver mines.","questions":["In place of the two columns that would ordinarily stand between its wall ends, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Delphi carried draped women wearing a basket-shaped head-dress, among the earliest caryatids in Greek building.","A carved frieze runs round all four sides of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its gods and heroes labelled in letters once painted red, and its north face shows the giants fighting the Olympians."],"answer":"Siphnian Treasury","answerText":"Siphnian Treasury","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 - 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ionic, 2 columns in antis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Footprint</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8.90 by 6.35 m (29 by 21 feet)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Frieze</span><span class=\"dt-v\">0.64 m (2 feet 1 inch) high</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Siphnian Treasury</b> is a small marble building put up at Delphi by the islanders of Siphnos towards the end of the 6th century BCE, and it is the most richly decorated structure the sanctuary has yielded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It stands on the south side of the Sacred Way a few paces west of the Sicyonian treasury, raised on a tower-like foundation of tufa that lifts it clear of the road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that platform rose an Ionic building in antis with its front to the west, 8.90 by 6.35 metres (29 by 21 feet), its entablature of Parian marble and its mouldings cut with a depth and clearness matched almost nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two supports between the projecting wall ends were not columns but draped women wearing the basket-shaped head-dress called a polos, among the earliest free-standing caryatids in Greek architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the Siphnians were then the richest of the islanders, from gold and silver mines worked on the island, and that they made the treasury out of a tenth of their income.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias adds that the god had ordered the tithe, and that when greed made them withhold it the sea flooded the mines and hid them from sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A sculptured frieze 0.64 metres (2 feet 1 inch) high ran round all four sides and has been recovered almost entire, its figures named by inscriptions once picked out in red paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The north side shows the gods fighting the giants and is the finest of the four, with the Mother of the Gods driving into the battle in a car drawn by lions that savage a giant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The east side sets a Homeric combat before an assembly of seated deities, Menelaus, Hector and Aeneas among the warriors whose names are cut beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its pediment carries the struggle of Heracles and Apollo for the Delphic tripod with Athena standing between them, and the building is called Siphnian chiefly because Pausanias mentions that treasury immediately after the Sicyonian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","archaeology","history","art","greece","archaic period","delphi"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.57, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.11.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Pausanias's Description of Greece, Translated with a Commentary</i>, vol. 5, <i>Commentary on Books IX., X.</i> (London: Macmillan, 1898), 272-76, https://archive.org/details/pausaniassdescri05pausuoft. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 135-40, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","Harold North Fowler and James Rignall Wheeler, <i>A Handbook of Greek Archaeology</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1909), 133, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeka00fowluoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Siphniens_-_2_-_%28Gl.III.A.21%29.jpg/1920px-Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Siphniens_-_2_-_%28Gl.III.A.21%29.jpg","title":"The reconstructed facade of the Siphnian Treasury","desc":"Adolphe Giraudon's photograph of about 1910, showing the pediment, the sculptured frieze and the carved capitals of the treasury as reassembled at Delphi. The printed caption below calls it the treasury of the Cnidians, one of the identifications proposed after the excavation.","credit":"Adolphe Giraudon, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Siphniens_-_2_-_(Gl.III.A.21).jpg","alt":"A triangular pediment carved with standing figures and a horse, set over a long sculptured band of seated figures, chariots and galloping horses, the whole resting on an entablature carried by two carved capitals."},"why":[{"q":"Why could a small island afford so rich a building?","a":"Because Herodotus says the Siphnians were then the richest of the islanders, from gold and silver mines worked on the island, and made the treasury out of a tenth of their income."},{"q":"Why did the mines stop paying, on Pausanias's account?","a":"Because the god had ordered the tithe, and when greed made the Siphnians withhold it the sea flooded the mines and hid them from sight."},{"q":"Why is the building attributed to Siphnos at all?","a":"Because Pausanias mentions that treasury immediately after the Sicyonian one, and this building stands a few paces west of the Sicyonian on the Sacred Way."}]},
{"id":"gr-345","num":345,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Pausanias found no treasure left in any of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the small stone houses that single cities built along the Sacred Way to hold what they had dedicated to Apollo.","questions":["The Thebans built one from the spoils of Leuctra and the Athenians one from Marathon, two of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Pausanias passed in order as he climbed towards the temple.","Herodotus says the six golden bowls of Gyges stood in one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that went by a city's name although a tyrant, not the city, had paid for it."],"answer":"treasuries at Delphi","answerText":"treasuries at Delphi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicyonian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 - 601 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Syracusan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 413 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Theban</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 371 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>treasuries at Delphi</b> were small stone houses put up inside the sanctuary of Apollo by single cities, and by single men, to hold the offerings they had dedicated to the god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Votive offerings, weapons and other valuables were stored in them, and they probably served too as gathering places for the city that had built them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They lined the Sacred Way, the paved road climbing from the south-eastern entrance to the temple, whose course has been likened to a great S reversed and whose stones are grooved against slipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias described the monuments of the sanctuary in strict topographical order, and the French excavations laid them bare in that same succession, which is how most of the treasuries are identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He gives their reasons: the Thebans built from the spoils of Leuctra, the Athenians from Datis's army at Marathon, the Syracusans from the great Athenian disaster, the Potidaeans out of piety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Others paid for a building instead: the god ordered the Siphnians to give a tenth of the revenue of their mines, while of the Cnidians Pausanias cannot tell whether they built for a victory or to show their wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus records six golden bowls of Gyges weighing thirty talents in the treasury of the Corinthians, which he says was in truth the treasury of Cypselus, and a gold lion of Croesus stood there too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sicyonians built twice, an earlier round house of the 7th century BCE whose fluted columns and curved architrave were buried in the foundations of the 6th-century building that replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Athenian treasury, about 10 by 6 metres (33 by 20 feet) and Doric with two columns between its wall ends, carried thirty marble metopes of Heracles and Theseus worked with the precision of fine Attic vases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A step of it still reads 'Athenians' and 'Marathon', and one of the hymns to Apollo is cut on its walls with the musical notation;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> yet Pausanias found no treasure left in any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","archaeology","history","art","religion","greece","archaic period","delphi"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.11, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.14, 1.50-51, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Pausanias's Description of Greece, Translated with a Commentary</i>, vol. 5, <i>Commentary on Books IX., X.</i> (London: Macmillan, 1898), 259, 270-82, https://archive.org/details/pausaniassdescri05pausuoft. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 203-4, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","Harold North Fowler and James Rignall Wheeler, <i>A Handbook of Greek Archaeology</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1909), 154, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeka00fowluoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Vue_de_Delphes_avec_le_Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Ath%C3%A9niens_et_le_Mus%C3%A9e._-_%28Gl.III.A.3%29.jpg/1920px-Vue_de_Delphes_avec_le_Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Ath%C3%A9niens_et_le_Mus%C3%A9e._-_%28Gl.III.A.3%29.jpg","title":"The Athenian treasury on its terrace at Delphi","desc":"A photograph of about 1910 by the Alinari brothers looking across the sanctuary of Apollo, with the Athenian treasury on its terrace at the right, the foundations and scattered blocks of other buildings running down the slope below it, and the museum in the valley beyond. The printed caption reads 'DELPHES - Grece. Tresor des Atheniens et vue du Musee'.","credit":"Fratelli Alinari, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_de_Delphes_avec_le_Tr%C3%A9sor_des_Ath%C3%A9niens_et_le_Mus%C3%A9e._-_(Gl.III.A.3).jpg","alt":"A small stone building with two columns in its front standing on a terrace on a bare mountainside, with courses of ruined walls and scattered blocks below it and a white-roofed building in the valley beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why are most of these buildings identifiable at all?","a":"Because Pausanias described the monuments of the sanctuary in strict topographical order, and the French excavations laid them bare in that same succession along the Sacred Way."},{"q":"Why did cities have different reasons for building one?","a":"Because the Thebans built from the spoils of Leuctra and the Athenians from Marathon, while the Siphnians were ordered by the god to give a tenth of their mines and the Potidaeans built out of piety."},{"q":"Why is nothing left of what they were built to hold?","a":"Because although Herodotus records Gyges' six golden bowls of thirty talents and a gold lion of Croesus among them, Pausanias found no treasure left in any of the buildings."}]},
{"id":"gr-346","num":346,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Whoever carved <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to fit one group into a long triangle whose centre seems to want a colossal figure and whose angles have barely room for a man lying down.","questions":["Sculptors solved the corners of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with fallen warriors, reclining river gods, or the sun rising from the sea at one end and the moon sinking at the other.","The quiet scene went on the front gable and the violent one at the back, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Olympia sets a still chariot team against a fight of Lapiths and Centaurs."],"answer":"pediment sculpture","answerText":"pediment sculpture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Poros gables</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aegina</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pediment sculpture</b> filled the triangular field between the horizontal cornice of a Greek temple and the raking cornices of its gable, a field whose back wall is the tympanum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the frieze it is one of only two places the orders prescribe for sculpture, and acroteria were set on the three angles of every pediment besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The field is the difficulty: its height runs from a centre wanting a colossal figure to angles with barely room for a reclining one, and a single group may not vary much in the size of its figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among the earliest that survive are the poros gables of the Athenian Acropolis, almost all combats, which in every case give the antagonist a snake’s or a fish’s tail to fill the angles with its coils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One, around 570 BCE, is 14.1 metres (46 feet) long and 1.44 metres (4 feet 9 inches) high at the centre: lions devour a bull in the middle, Heracles wrestles Triton on the left, and on the right stands a winged three-bodied creature whose identity is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Relief gave way to figures worked almost in the round and merely set against the background, which the architectural frame’s strong projection all but forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A chariot was the commonest device: the length of car and horses takes up the narrowing field and separates the middle group from the smaller figures at the sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corners were filled with the dying, or with reclining river gods: at Olympia, where Pausanias notes that the pediment narrows again, they are the Alpheius and the Cladeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Symmetry became the rule: at Aegina, where an earlier set was discarded and replaced, twelve figures besides Athena stood in the west gable and at least ten in the east, each answering one across the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> And the front took the quieter scene and the back the violent one, so Olympia set the still preparation for a chariot race against Lapiths and Centaurs, and the Parthenon the birth of Athena against her contest with Poseidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","art","sculpture","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D24. [Open access]","Harold North Fowler and James Rignall Wheeler, <i>A Handbook of Greek Archaeology</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1909), 121, 202–04, 215–16, 223–26, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeka00fowluoft. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 39–42, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","“Hekatompedon. West pediment,” Ακρ. 3, Acropolis Museum, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/hekatompedon-west-pediment. [Open access]","“Aegina, Temple of Aphaia,” building record, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Aegina,+Temple+of+Aphaia&object=Building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Olimpyamuseum002.jpg/1920px-Olimpyamuseum002.jpg","title":"The west pediment of the Temple of Zeus at Olympia","desc":"Sculptures of the west pediment of the Temple of Zeus at Olympia, arranged as they stood in the gable, in the Archaeological Museum of Olympia.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olimpyamuseum002.jpg","alt":"Marble figures from a temple gable set out in a long triangle, tapering from a tall standing figure at the centre to figures lying down at each end."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the triangular field so hard to fill?","a":"Because its height runs from a centre wanting a colossal figure to angles with barely room for a reclining one, while a single group may not vary much in the size of its figures."},{"q":"Why was a chariot the commonest device at the centre?","a":"Because the length of car and horses takes up the narrowing field and separates the middle group from the smaller figures at the sides."},{"q":"Why did the sculpture come away from the background over time?","a":"Because the architectural frame's strong projection all but forces it, so relief gave way to figures worked almost in the round and merely set against the tympanum."}]},
{"id":"gr-347","num":347,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Because the square field of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> practically limits a group to two or three figures, Doric temples favoured subjects that divided neatly into separate scenes.","questions":["Where the Ionic frieze ran as one unbroken band above the architrave, the Doric frieze was interrupted, and its sculpture went into the square field of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between projecting triglyphs.","At Selinus, Perseus beheading the Gorgon fills one square <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Heracles carrying the Cercopes slung from a pole fills another, each figure turning full front above legs in profile."],"answer":"metope","answerText":"metope","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Selinus Temple C</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest metopes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","sculpture","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"abstract":"A <b>metope</b> is the roughly square panel between two triglyphs in the frieze of a Doric building, and its character follows from that: the Ionic frieze runs unbroken, while the Doric frieze wants decoration interrupted into separate fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many were left undecorated, a plain white surface of stucco or marble, but the square face was an inviting field for a decorator, and the projecting triglyphs either side framed whatever went into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some were given individual frames besides, broad bands above and below and narrow ones at the sides, as on the box-like Perseus, Heracles and Apollo panels from Selinus now at Palermo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Depth of recess governed the carving: a deep triangular gable took figures nearly in the round where the shallower panel took half relief, and blue triglyphs projected clearly from surfaces of red or white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The square field practically limits a composition to groups of two figures, or sometimes three, and a third is in most cases cramped close against the margin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br> The favourite subjects are naturally those that divide easily into separate scenes: the labours of Heracles and of Theseus, or combats of gods with giants and of Greeks with centaurs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A single scene is occasionally spread over two panels or more, but such a treatment could only be made tolerable by accepted convention, and to that it never attained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Temple C at Selinus, put up on the highest point of the acropolis in the first half of the 6th century BCE, carried carved panels on its triglyph frieze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its earliest set, little later than the beginning of that century, shows Perseus beheading the Gorgon before Athena and Heracles carrying the Cercopes slung head downwards from a pole, chests full to the front above legs in profile, the heads disproportionately large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What such a field carried is not always recoverable: the tufa slabs found at the Sicyonian treasury at Delphi were read as metopes by one scholar and as parts of a frieze by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 148-49, 221-22, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 42-44, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","Ernest A. Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i>, 2nd ed. (London: Macmillan, 1915), 162-63, https://archive.org/details/handbookofgreeks00gardrich. [Open access]","\"Selinus, Temple C (Building),\" Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/artifact?name=Selinus,+Temple+C&object=Building. [Open access]","J. G. Frazer, <i>Pausanias's Description of Greece, Translated with a Commentary</i>, vol. 5, <i>Commentary on Books IX., X.</i> (London: Macmillan, 1898), 270, https://archive.org/details/pausaniassdescri05pausuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Metopa_decapita_Meduca_-_Flickr_-_Rino_Porrovecchio.jpg/1920px-Metopa_decapita_Meduca_-_Flickr_-_Rino_Porrovecchio.jpg","title":"metope","desc":"Metope with Perseus beheading Medusa, watched by Athena, from Temple C at Selinunte, in the Museo archeologico regionale Antonio Salinas, Palermo.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metopa_decapita_Meduca_-_Flickr_-_Rino_Porrovecchio.jpg","alt":"Carved stone panel showing Perseus driving a sword into the neck of Medusa, with Athena standing at the left edge"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the Doric frieze want separate scenes where the Ionic does not?","a":"Because the Ionic frieze runs unbroken while the Doric is cut into panels by projecting triglyphs, so its decoration is interrupted into separate framed fields."},{"q":"Why do the favourite subjects run to labours and combats?","a":"Because the square field practically limits a composition to two figures or sometimes three, so subjects that divide easily into separate scenes suit it best."},{"q":"Why did spreading one scene across two panels never take hold?","a":"Because such a treatment could only be made tolerable by accepted convention, and to that it never attained."}]},
{"id":"gr-348","num":348,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Archaic builders in Aeolis and on Lesbos raised the volutes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> vertically out of the shaft and set a palmette in the triangle between them.","questions":["Strictly two-faced and unornamented on its narrow sides, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was hard to place at a building's corner and was probably used indoors or on votive columns standing alone.","Made only in the second half of the 6th century BCE and largely confined to the north-east Aegean, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a short career that its awkward two-sided form helps explain."],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"Aeolic capital","answerText":"Aeolic capital","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 500 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Aeolic capital</b> is an Archaic column head on which two spiral volutes rise vertically out of the shaft and then spring outward, leaving a triangle between them that is filled by a great palmette.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the volutes a leaf-carved torus, or a group of smaller mouldings, binds the two spirals together, and below that hangs a girdle of overhanging leaves whose serrated lower edge is cut off from the shaft by a deep cavetto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Examples come from temples at Neandria in the Troad and at Nape on Lesbos, and from finds at Mitylene, at Larissa near Smyrna and at Aegae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were made in the second half of the 6th century BCE, across an area covering roughly the north-eastern quarter of the Aegean basin, from Lesbos and Aeolis as far as eastern Thrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How the form developed is not clear: some examples are very plain while others carry subsidiary volutes and decorative detail, and nobody has managed to order those traits chronologically or fit them into a coherent stemma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Ionic capital differs in each of these respects, since its volutes lie horizontally and are joined by a cushion, while the girdle of hanging leaves has hardened into the egg-and-tongue echinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the Aeolic form is strictly two-faced and carries no ornament on its narrow sides, it is harder to fit into a building than an Ionic one, and the usual Ionic solution at a corner was impracticable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That has led scholars to suppose it was used by preference in interior colonnades, or on isolated votive columns standing on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The difficulties it posed probably explain both its slight success in the Greek world and the brevity of its career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its origin is generally traced to the Levant, and the very word 'proto-Aeolic', coined for the Levantine and Cypriot pieces treated as prototypes, already encodes a view about which way the influence ran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","building","archaeology","greece","archaic"],"sources":["William J. Anderson and R. Phené Spiers, <i>The Architecture of Ancient Greece</i>, rev. and rewritten by William Bell Dinsmoor (London: B. T. Batsford, 1927), 71-72, 101, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.209411. [Open access]","Allan Marquand, <i>Greek Architecture</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), 94, 202, https://archive.org/details/GreekArchitecture. [Open access]","Jacques des Courtils, \"Alâzeytin Kalesi et l'architecture éolique,\" <i>Anatolia Antiqua</i> 19 (2011): 385-92, https://www.persee.fr/doc/anata_1018-1946_2011_num_19_1_1108. [Open access]","Antoine Hermary, \"Chypre et la Carie à la fin de l'époque archaïque,\" <i>Anatolia Antiqua</i> 19 (2011): 371-75, https://www.persee.fr/doc/anata_1018-1946_2011_num_19_1_1106. [Open access]","Thierry Petit, \"La forêt qui cache l'arbre. De l'‘Arbre-de-la-Vie’ levantin aux diptères ioniques et à l'ordre corinthien,\" <i>Ktèma</i> 33 (2008): 309-27, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ktema_0221-5896_2008_num_33_1_1110. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Aeolic_Capital.jpg/1920px-Aeolic_Capital.jpg","title":"Aeolic capital from Old Smyrna","desc":"An Aeolic capital from the temple of Athena at Old Smyrna, in the Izmir Museum of History and Art.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aeolic_Capital.jpg","alt":"A limestone capital whose two large spiral volutes spring outward from a central block, with a small carved palmette in the triangle between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this form harder to build with than the Ionic capital?","a":"Because it is strictly two-faced and carries no ornament on its narrow sides, so the usual Ionic solution at a corner was impracticable and it suited interior colonnades or isolated votive columns."},{"q":"Why can its development not be set in order?","a":"Because some examples are very plain while others carry subsidiary volutes and decorative detail, and nobody has managed to order those traits chronologically or fit them into a coherent stemma."},{"q":"Why does the term 'proto-Aeolic' carry an argument in it?","a":"Because it was coined for the Levantine and Cypriot pieces treated as prototypes, so the word already encodes a view about which way the influence ran."}]},
{"id":"gr-349","num":349,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Greek foundries built a clay core, coated it in wax, modelled the surface on the wax and then melted it out, a hollow method called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that made life-size statues possible.","questions":["Solid bronze statues wasted costly metal and were unmanageably heavy, so sixth-century Greek workshops turned to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves only a thin skin of metal around a fireproof core.","Greek smiths once beat bronze into plates and riveted them together limb by limb, a laborious method that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced for anything larger than a statuette."],"answer":"lost-wax casting","answerText":"lost-wax casting","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hollow casting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in Greece, c. 600 – 501 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samian founders</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Rhoikos and Theodoros, fl. c. 540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","technology","art","sculpture","greece","archaic"],"abstract":"<b>Lost-wax casting</b> makes a hollow bronze object by modelling it in wax, encasing the wax in clay, and melting the wax away so that molten metal can fill the space it leaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything turns on the core, a mass of fireproof material shaped like the finished piece but standing back from its surface by the thickness of the metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wax may be laid straight onto that core and modelled there, or a clay original moulded first and a wax skin brushed inside the empty mould before the core is packed behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bronze rods driven through the wax hold the core steady once it has run out, tubes let the wax escape and the metal in, and vents release the trapped air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mould and crucible were always clay, and melting a bronze of nine parts copper to one of tin sometimes needed more than 1,000 °C (1,830 °F) while clay turns unstable beyond 1,100 °C (2,010 °F), leaving the founder a narrow band and no instrument to measure it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Greeks worked bronze long before they cast statues in it, and the earliest large figures were cast solid or built up from hammered plates, a technique they called <i>sphyrelaton</i>, 'beaten with the hammer'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias saw one at Sparta, a Zeus he took for the oldest bronze anywhere, made not in a single piece but of limbs hammered separately and fastened with nails rather than solder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both methods seem to have lasted into the 6th century BCE, when hollow casting arrived and spared the waste of costly metal and the dead weight of a solid statue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias credited the discovery to two Samians, Rhoikos and Theodoros, a tradition read since as the Greek adoption of a far older craft rather than its invention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Little survives, since bronze was always worth melting down, and the workshops are known mainly from casting pits such as one found on the Athenian Agora in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["H. B. Walters, <i>Catalogue of the Bronzes, Greek, Roman, and Etruscan, in the Department of Greek and Roman Antiquities, British Museum</i> (London: Printed by Order of the Trustees, 1899), xxix–xxxiii, xl, https://archive.org/details/catalogueofbronz00brit. [Open access]","Ernest Arthur Gardner, <i>A Handbook of Greek Sculpture</i> (London: Macmillan, 1911), 23–26, https://archive.org/details/rshandbookofgree00garduoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.17.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.14.8, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0160%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Gerhard Zimmer, “Les ateliers de fabrication des grands bronzes,” in <i>L’artisanat métallurgique dans les sociétés anciennes en Méditerranée occidentale: techniques, lieux et formes de production</i>, Publications de l’École française de Rome 332 (Rome: École française de Rome, 2004), 341–51, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_2004_act_332_1_7755. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Terracotta_casting_mould_for_a_statue_of_Apollo._AGMA.jpg/1920px-Terracotta_casting_mould_for_a_statue_of_Apollo._AGMA.jpg","title":"Clay casting mould for a bronze Apollo","desc":"Terracotta casting mould for the lower half of a bronze statue of Apollo, found in a casting pit near the temple of Apollo; middle of the 6th century BCE, Ancient Agora Museum, Athens.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_casting_mould_for_a_statue_of_Apollo._AGMA.jpg","alt":"Fragments of a thick clay mould, reassembled around a modern support into the shape of a standing figure, in a museum case beside a panel diagramming a casting pit and furnace."},"why":[{"q":"Why was casting a large bronze a matter of narrow margins?","a":"Because melting a bronze of nine parts copper to one of tin sometimes needed more than 1,000 °C while clay turns unstable beyond 1,100 °C, leaving the founder a narrow band and no instrument to measure it."},{"q":"Why did hollow casting displace the older techniques?","a":"Because it spared both the waste of costly metal and the dead weight of a solid statue, where the earliest large figures had been cast solid or built up from hammered plates."},{"q":"Why is so little of this work left to study?","a":"Because bronze was always worth melting down, so the craft is known mainly from casting pits such as the one found on the Athenian Agora in 1936."}]},
{"id":"gr-350","num":350,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Ancient critics preferred to call it <i>melic</i>, verse sung to a musical accompaniment, where the modern catch-all name for it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, points instead at the lyre.","questions":["Alexandrian scholars drew up a canon of nine poets working in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Alkman to Bacchylides, whose songs were made either for a single voice or for a trained chorus.","Because so little survives except where later grammarians and scholiasts quote it, most <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read in scraps, and before 1897 only Pindar could be judged from substantial remains."],"answer":"lyric poetry","answerText":"lyric poetry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First fixed date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">676 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Terpander's victory at Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 melic poets, Alkman to Bacchylides</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Lyric poetry</b> is the modern name for the Greek verse that was neither epic nor drama, and the Greeks of the classical age preferred <i>melic</i>, a term covering all poetry that was sung to a musical accompaniment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their word <i>melos</i> held three things together at once, the words, the melody and the rhythm, and Plato has Socrates say exactly that of song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The catch-all use of 'lyric' for everything outside epic and drama is modern and inaccurate, since it implies a lyre, while epic itself was once sung and elegy and iambus soon dropped their music.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle, setting out the kinds of poetry, names epic, tragedy, comedy and dithyramb, and has no such general category at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Alexandrian scholars instead fixed a canon of nine melic poets, running from Alkman and Alkaios through Sappho, Stesichoros, Ibykos, Anakreon and Simonides to Pindar and Bacchylides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The songs divide into monody, sung by one voice to the lyre or the pipe and given over to the deepest feelings of the individual, and choral melic, performed by a body of singers who danced as they sang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Choral song was public and ceremonial, a solemn expression of the united voice of the state, and it was built in the triad of strophe, antistrophe and epode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Such a performance required a professional who wrote the words, composed the music and drilled the dancers, usually for one occasion only, whether a victory, a harvest, a holy day, a birth, a death or a marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Almost none of this immense body of song survives entire, and what is left is preserved in quotation, scattered through the manuscripts of more than eighty later authors, grammarians, scholiasts and geographers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The great exception arrived in 1896, when a papyrus reached the British Museum carrying enough of Bacchylides to be edited the following year, for until then Pindar alone could be judged from considerable remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xviii–xix, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 3.398d, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0168:book=3:section=398d. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 76, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1447a, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1447a. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xx, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xxi–xxiii, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 95–96, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), vii–viii, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Richard C. Jebb, ed., <i>Bacchylides: The Poems and Fragments</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1905), v, https://archive.org/details/bacchylidespoems00bacciala. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Terracotta_amphora_%28jar%29_MET_DT272.jpg/1920px-Terracotta_amphora_%28jar%29_MET_DT272.jpg","title":"A singer with the kithara","desc":"Attic red-figure amphora attributed to the Berlin Painter, about 490 BCE. The front shows a young man singing and playing the kithara. Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_amphora_%28jar%29_MET_DT272.jpg","alt":"A tall black Greek vase painted in orange with a young man in a long robe, head tilted back in song, playing a large stringed kithara."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Greeks call this poetry melic rather than lyric?","a":"Because melos held the words, the melody and the rhythm together at once, while 'lyric' implies a lyre, and epic was once sung while elegy and iambus soon dropped their music."},{"q":"Why did a choral song need a professional?","a":"Because one man wrote the words, composed the music and drilled the dancers, usually for a single occasion — a victory, a harvest, a holy day, a birth, a death or a marriage."},{"q":"Why did the discovery of 1896 change what could be judged?","a":"Because until a papyrus of Bacchylides reached the British Museum, Pindar alone could be judged from considerable remains, the rest surviving only in quotation."}]},
{"id":"wh-201","num":"201","category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"The farming year of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned on a summer flood that rose at Aswan in June, peaked from August to September, and receded by the end of October, when sowing began.","questions":["Historians divide <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into an Old, a Middle and a New Kingdom, each a stretch of unified rule, separated by Intermediate Periods in which central authority came apart.","The tombs and temples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were built of stone at the desert edge and stand, while its towns were mudbrick on the floodplain and lie buried under Nile silt."],"answer":"Ancient Egypt","answerText":"Ancient Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2686 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2181 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ptolemaic rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">305 – 30 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ancient Egypt</b> was the civilisation of the lower Nile, and for some 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) from the Mediterranean south into Nubia the valley is still dense with its great buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Nile runs about 6,825 kilometres (4,240 miles), fed from Africa's equatorial plateau by the White Nile and from the Ethiopian highlands by the Blue Nile and the Atbara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before twentieth-century damming its summer flood was driven by monsoon rain over those highlands, rising at Aswan as early as June, peaking from August to September, and receding by the end of October, when sowing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prosperity was tied overwhelmingly to that cycle, and a weak flood is associated with serious human consequences right through the recorded history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surviving record is skewed by the same river: tombs and temples were stone at the desert edge and still stand, while settlements from capitals to villages were mudbrick on the floodplain and lie buried under silt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Out of the Naqada farming culture, which spread through Upper Egypt and from its middle phase into the north, grew a state organised around a divine king whose office was held to keep chaos out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kings of the 1st and 2nd Dynasties, roughly 3050 to 2750 BCE, were buried at Abydos in the desert cemetery now called Umm el-Qa'ab.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What followed is divided into an Old, a Middle and a New Kingdom, each a long stretch of unified rule separated by Intermediate Periods in which central authority came apart, then a Late Period, Persian rule and the Ptolemaic dynasty, ending in 30 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those dates are a construct rather than a record, resting on astronomical observations, king-lists and documents open to more than one reading, so that rival High and Low chronologies a century apart have long been argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A set of 48 new radiocarbon dates published in 2025 ruled the Low Chronology out for the Old and Middle Kingdoms, though a standard teaching chronology still warns that Egyptian dates are only certain after 664 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["culture","history","archaeology","egypt","africa","bronze age"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Joseph G. Manning, Francis Ludlow, Alexander R. Stine, William R. Boos, Michael Sigl, and Jennifer R. Marlon, “Volcanic Suppression of Nile Summer Flooding Triggers Revolt and Constrains Interstate Conflict in Ancient Egypt,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 900, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-00957-y. [Open access]","Pınar Erdil et al., “Resolution of the High versus Low Debate for Old and Middle Kingdom Egypt,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0314612, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314612. [Open access]","Josef Wegner, “Excavating the Residence of an Ancient Egyptian Mayor,” <i>Expedition</i> 41, no. 3 (1999): 8–24, https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/excavating-the-residence-of-an-ancient-egyptian-mayor/. [Open access]","Matthew Adams, “The Search for Egypt’s First Kings,” <i>Expedition</i> 65, no. 3 (2024), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-search-for-egypts-first-kings/. [Open access]","Branislav Anđelković, “The Molding Power of Ideology: Political Transformations of Predynastic Egypt,” <i>Etnoantropološki problemi</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): 713–22, https://doi.org/10.21301/EAP.v9i3.9. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Egyptian Chronology,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Ancient_Egypt_map-en.svg","title":"Ancient Egypt","desc":"Map of ancient Egypt, showing the Nile up to the fifth cataract and the major cities and sites of the Dynastic period, c. 3150 to 30 BCE; Cairo and Jerusalem are shown as reference cities. Jeff Dahl, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Egypt_map-en.svg","alt":"A map of the Nile running from the Mediterranean south to the fifth cataract, with the major cities and sites of dynastic Egypt marked along the valley"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the surviving record skewed towards tombs and temples?","a":"Because those were stone at the desert edge and still stand, while settlements from capitals to villages were mudbrick on the floodplain and lie buried under silt."},{"q":"Why were Egyptian dates argued over for so long?","a":"Because the chronology is a construct resting on astronomical observations, king-lists and documents open to more than one reading, which produced rival High and Low schemes a century apart."},{"q":"Why was the timing of the flood decisive for the country?","a":"Because it rose at Aswan as early as June, peaked from August to September and receded by the end of October, when sowing began — and a weak flood carried serious human consequences."}]},
{"id":"wh-202","num":"202","category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Sown in autumn on ground the flood had just drained, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reaped between February and May and then pounded spikelet by spikelet to free the grain.","questions":["Egypt struck no coinage, so wages, pensions and taxes were reckoned in food, and the bread and beer that stood for money were milled from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> above all.","Grown in the Levant from about 9700 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived in the Nile valley already domesticated, and the traits that made it a crop were not acquired in Egypt."],"answer":"emmer wheat","answerText":"emmer wheat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 9700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated in Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1130 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Emmer wheat</b> is a hulled wheat, its grains locked in husks that threshing alone will not break, and it was the staple bread grain of ancient Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was among the earliest crops taken into cultivation, grown in the Levant from about 9700 BCE, and it reached the Nile valley already domesticated rather than being tamed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egypt grew it almost without rain: the summer flood spread water and fresh silt across the valley floor, and what the fields yielded followed from how high that water rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A low flood left ground dry and unworked, a high one swept the banks away, and either meant a short harvest, so the height was watched from gauges such as the nilometer on Elephantine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The water was caught in natural depressions and led on by canals, later by dykes and reservoirs, the oldest known being the Sadd el-Kafara near Helwan, built in the 27th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Because the flood came in late summer, the crop had to go in during autumn and grow through the cool months, which ruled out anything needing heat and wet together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground was broken with a hoe or an ard once the water had drained, in October or November, and the seed was scattered and trodden in by animals to keep it from birds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Six months on, between February at the earliest and May at the latest, the ears were cut with sickles, then threshed, winnowed and sieved to break the spikelets apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came out was issued rather than sold: Egypt struck no coinage, and wages, pensions and taxes were reckoned in kind, most often as bread and beer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The scale was institutional: the mortuary temple of Ramesses III is recorded turning out over 2,000 loaves and 144 jars of beer a day, and when the flood failed that same accounting drove farmers to sell land to meet their taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["plant","agriculture","archaeology","history","egypt","bronze age"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Michael F. Scott, Laura R. Botigué, Selina Brace, Chris J. Stevens, Victoria E. Mullin and Alice Stevenson, “A 3,000-Year-Old Egyptian Emmer Wheat Genome Reveals Dispersal and Domestication History,” <i>Nature Plants</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): 1120–28, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-019-0534-5. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Food Production in Egypt,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foodproduction/index.html. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Cereal Production,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foodproduction/cereal.html. [Open access]","Mohamed Megahed and Hana Vymazalová, “Vysoká přehrada: projekt a jeho důsledky,” <i>Pražské egyptologické studie</i> 14 (2015): 10–16, https://sites.ff.cuni.cz/pes/wp-content/uploads/sites/13/2015/11/Mohamed_Megahed-Hana_Vymazalova_10-16.pdf. [Open access]","Vanessa Smith, “Food Fit for the Soul of a Pharaoh: The Mortuary Temple’s Bakeries and Breweries,” <i>Expedition</i> 48, no. 2 (2006), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/food-fit-for-the-the-soul-of-a-pharaoh/. [Open access]","Joseph G. Manning, Francis Ludlow, Alexander R. Stine, William R. Boos, Michael Sigl, and Jennifer R. Marlon, “Volcanic Suppression of Nile Summer Flooding Triggers Revolt and Constrains Interstate Conflict in Ancient Egypt,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 900, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-017-00957-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Agricultural_Scenes%2C_Tomb_of_Nakht_MET_DT306954.jpg/1920px-Agricultural_Scenes%2C_Tomb_of_Nakht_MET_DT306954.jpg","title":"Agricultural scenes in the tomb of Nakht","desc":"Facsimile by Norman de Garis Davies of the agricultural scenes on the east wall of the offering chapel in the Theban tomb of Nakht (TT 52), 18th Dynasty, c. 1400–1390 BCE. Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agricultural_Scenes,_Tomb_of_Nakht_MET_DT306954.jpg","alt":"A painted facsimile of a tomb wall; in the lowest band men plough with a pair of oxen, hoe, break clods with mallets and scatter seed among trees, while above them others reap with sickles and measure a heap of grain"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the timing of the flood decide what Egypt could grow?","a":"Because it came in late summer, so the crop had to go in during autumn and grow through the cool months, which ruled out anything needing heat and wet together."},{"q":"Why was the height of the flood watched so closely?","a":"Because a low flood left ground dry and unworked and a high one swept the banks away, and either meant a short harvest — hence gauges such as the nilometer on Elephantine."},{"q":"Why was grain issued rather than sold?","a":"Because Egypt struck no coinage, so wages, pensions and taxes were reckoned in kind, most often as bread and beer."}]},
{"id":"wh-203","num":203,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Sequence-dated by its pottery into three phases running from about 4000 to 3000 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Upper Egyptian culture out of which the first Egyptian state grew.","questions":["Black-topped pots in shallow pits, then rich tombs with copper and cylinder jars, mark the successive phases of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the 4th-millennium culture of Upper Egypt.","Long argued to be either a single state or a set of rival chiefdoms, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread from Upper Egypt into the Delta, where its pottery displaced the local tradition."],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"Naqada culture","answerText":"Naqada culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Naqada I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 – 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naqada II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 – 3200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naqada III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3200 – 3000 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Naqada culture</b> is the archaeological culture of Upper Egypt through the 4th millennium BCE, the period in which scattered farming villages along the Nile became one of the world's earliest states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is named after a cemetery near the town of Naqada, where Flinders Petrie plotted the objects in nearly three thousand graves during a single season of digging in 1895.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No calendar dates were available, so he ordered the graves by their pottery into a relative sequence, and Werner Kaiser rebuilt that scheme in 1957 into the numbered phases still in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They run from about 4000 to 3500 BCE, from about 3500 to 3200, and from about 3200 to 3000, and each is defined chiefly by its pots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first phase the dead lay in shallow oval pits much as in the Badarian period before it, and black-topped red ware is the characteristic vessel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The second brought marl and rough wares, copper, stone vessels and superbly worked flint knives, along with obsidian and lapis lazuli carried in from outside Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some tombs are now large and well furnished while others stay plain, and the goods in them are mostly everyday objects, as though the afterlife were a copy of life on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the third, cemeteries in Upper Egypt hold extremely wealthy burials, cylinder jars are a standard offering and the first writing appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Delta had a Lower Egyptian culture of its own, and only in that last phase did Naqadan pottery, and then Naqadan potters, displace it, while a settlement at Tell el-Farkha burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Whether all this was one state from the start or a set of rival chiefdoms around Abydos, Hierakonpolis and Naqada is still argued, and the evidence is largely pots, graves and imports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"sources":["Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “The Naqada Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/background.html. [Open access]","W. M. Flinders Petrie and J. E. Quibell, <i>Naqada and Ballas. 1895</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1896), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/cu31924028748261. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Chronology of the Naqada Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/chronology.html. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Burial customs of Predynastic Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/burialcustoms/predynastic.html. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Naqada Period: foreign relations,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2001), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/foreign.html. [Open access]","Jade Bajeot, “Technical Traditions and Social Boundaries. The Case of Predynastic Egypt,” <i>Études et Travaux</i> 38 (2025): 35–59, https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.38.002. [Open access]","Krzysztof M. Ciałowicz, “New Discoveries at Tell el-Farkha and the Beginnings of the Egyptian State,” <i>Études et Travaux</i> 30 (2017): 231–50, https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.30.011. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “The Political Unification of Egypt in the Naqada Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities (London: University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/inegypt.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","history","egypt","pottery"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Jar_decorated_with_painted_figures_of_boats_%28D-ware%29%2C_baked_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_413_p02.jpg/1920px-Jar_decorated_with_painted_figures_of_boats_%28D-ware%29%2C_baked_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_413_p02.jpg","title":"Painted Naqada pottery","desc":"Painted clay vessels of the Naqada culture in the Museo Egizio, Turin, which identifies the boat-decorated jar as S 413 and dates the group to about 3700–3300 BCE. Museo Egizio, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jar_decorated_with_painted_figures_of_boats_%28D-ware%29,_baked_clay_-_Museo_Egizio,_Turin_S_413_p02.jpg","alt":"Four clay vessels against a dark ground: a tall plain red jar, a pale jar painted with rows of many-oared boats carrying cabins, a jar painted with nested arcs and hatched panels, and a shallow bowl painted with spirals"},"locator":{"name":"Naqada culture","at":[32.7167,25.9],"kind":"region","area":[[32.5,23.9],[33.2,24.2],[33,25.4],[32.4,26.3],[31.6,27.4],[30.8,27.9],[30.4,27.5],[31.1,26.6],[31.9,25.5],[32.3,24.5]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Petrie order the graves by their pottery?","a":"Because no calendar dates were available, so he built a relative sequence from the objects in nearly three thousand graves, a scheme Kaiser rebuilt in 1957 into the numbered phases still used."},{"q":"Why are the goods in these graves mostly everyday objects?","a":"Because the afterlife appears to have been imagined as a copy of life on earth, so what went into a tomb is what a person used above ground."},{"q":"Why is the political shape of the period still argued over?","a":"Because the evidence is largely pots, graves and imports, which cannot settle whether this was one state from the start or rival chiefdoms around Abydos, Hierakonpolis and Naqada."}]},
{"id":"wh-204","num":204,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Carved on a slab of greywacke with a paint-grinding hollow still cut into one face, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a king wearing both crowns of Egypt and striking down his enemies.","questions":["Hundreds of plain slate slabs ground eye-paint in Naqada graves, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that same object made enormous, given to a temple at Hierakonpolis and carved on both faces.","Two long-necked leopards twine their necks around the grinding hollow of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose top register writes a catfish and a chisel as the name of an early Egyptian king."],"answer":"Narmer Palette","answerText":"Narmer Palette","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign of Narmer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2686 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Narmer Palette</b> is a shield-shaped slab of dark greywacke, 63 cm (25 inches) long and carved in low relief on both faces, and now in the Egyptian Museum in Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Palettes of this kind were ordinary in prehistoric Egypt: hundreds of plain slate slabs have come from Naqada cemeteries, found in front of the face among beads, combs and hairpins and stained green where malachite was ground into eye-paint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This one keeps that working hollow, a shallow circle framed by the entwined necks of two long-necked leopards, so it is a real palette made monumental rather than a plaque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavators, who lifted it from a heap of votive objects buried beneath the temple at Hierakonpolis, supposed it was given there to paint the god's image or the king before a ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both faces are cut into horizontal registers, and the figures already follow conventions Egyptian art would keep for three thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At the top of each face a rectangular frame flanked by two cow-eared Hathor heads holds a catfish and a chisel, read since the excavation as the king's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The catfish is secure; the chisel has never had a convincing explanation, so the reading is conventional rather than certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the face with the hollow the king walks in the red crown of Lower Egypt behind four standard-bearers towards ten enemies whose severed heads lie between their legs, and below, drawn as a bull, he breaks down a walled town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the other face he wears the white crown of Upper Egypt and swings a mace at a kneeling captive, while a falcon draws a rope through the lip of a human head sprouting papyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egypt's antiquities ministry calls the palette a record of unification while stating that unification was in reality gradual, and the king's own place is still argued: first of the First Dynasty, or of a 'Dynasty 0' before it, and perhaps the Menes of later lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["J. E. Quibell and F. W. Green, <i>Hierakonpolis, Part II</i>, Egyptian Research Account Fifth Memoir (London: Bernard Quaritch, 1902), 41-43. https://archive.org/details/hierakonpolis00petrgoog [Open access]","\"Narmer,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/narmer.html [Open access]","\"Art in Archaic Egypt,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/art/archaicegyptart-index.htm [Open access]","Josep Cervelló-Autuori, \"Was King Narmer Menes?,\" <i>Archeo-Nil</i> 15 (2005): 31-46. https://doi.org/10.3406/arnil.2005.896 [Open access]","Gabor Takacs, \"Note on the name of king Narmer,\" <i>Linguistica</i> 37, no. 1 (1997): 53-58. https://doi.org/10.4312/linguistica.37.1.53-58 [Open access]","\"Early Dynastic Period,\" Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt. https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/early-dynastic-period [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Narmer_Palette_serpopard_side.jpg","title":"Narmer Palette","desc":"The side of the Narmer Palette with the two serpopards. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narmer_Palette_serpopard_side.jpg","alt":"A shield-shaped stone palette carved in bands: at the top a king in the red crown walks with attendants and standard-bearers towards rows of bound bodies whose heads lie between their legs; in the middle two long-necked leopards whose necks loop around a shallow round hollow; at the foot a bull breaking down a walled town."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the palette called a real palette rather than a plaque?","a":"Because it keeps the working hollow that such slabs were made for, a shallow circle framed by the entwined necks of two long-necked leopards, made monumental rather than abandoned."},{"q":"Why is the reading of the king's name conventional rather than certain?","a":"Because the catfish is secure while the chisel beside it has never had a convincing explanation."},{"q":"Why does calling it a record of unification need qualifying?","a":"Because Egypt's own antiquities ministry states that unification was in reality gradual, and the king's place — first of the First Dynasty or of a Dynasty 0 — is still argued."}]},
{"id":"wh-205","num":205,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Later Egyptians credited the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a first king called Menes, while radiocarbon modelling now reads it as a period of several centuries rather than a single event.","questions":["Three inscriptions naming a king known chiefly from Abydos have been found in the eastern Delta, evidence that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had begun before the 1st Dynasty.","Radiocarbon modelling places the accession of Aha between 3111 and 3045 BCE, narrowing historical estimates for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had ranged from 3400 to 2900 BCE."],"answer":"unification of Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Accession of Aha</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3111 – 3045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Dynastic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 2686 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>unification of Egypt</b> is the process by which the Nile valley and the Delta came under one king, conventionally placed at the opening of the 1st Dynasty about 3100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later Egyptians credited it to Menes, legendary first king of the Greek sources, whose name derives from the Meni heading New Kingdom lists, though his identification with any contemporary king is highly uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their own annals reached further back: the Palermo Stone, a fragment of a 5th-Dynasty slab, begins with predynastic kings of the period before the union of North and South.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its top row preserves nine names of predynastic kings of Lower Egypt, and had the line been complete it might once have held some 120.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below it each reign opens with one formula, an appearance as King of Upper Egypt, an appearance as King of Lower Egypt, then the Union of the Two Lands, a union performed afresh at every accession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Archaeology gives no such moment, and the Naqada culture may have been a single political unit throughout or a set of areas each under its own ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The eastern Delta, long thought marginal, now looks far more important, and at Tell el-Farkha the phase from Naqada IIIA to the middle of IIIB reads as one of competition between the most influential Naqadan proto-kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three inscriptions there name Iry-Hor, a king known chiefly from Abydos, taken as proof that he had some connection with the Delta and more probably ruled the whole of Egypt before the 1st Dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Radiocarbon modelling places the accession of Aha, first king of that dynasty, between 3111 and 3045 BCE, where historical estimates had ranged from 3400 to 2900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It reads the stretch from Naqada IID/IIIA to that accession as the period in which the country moved from cultural to political unification, a Predynastic period of six or seven centuries against four to five millennia for the same change in southwest Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"unification of Egypt","difficulty":2,"sources":["\"Menes,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/menes.html [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 1 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), 51-52, 57-58, sections 78, 93-94, 140. https://archive.org/details/ancientrecordsof01brea [Open access]","\"The Political Unification of Egypt in the Naqada Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/naqadan/inegypt.html [Open access]","Krzysztof Marek Ciałowicz, \"New Discoveries at Tell el-Farkha and the Beginnings of the Egyptian State,\" <i>Etudes et Travaux</i> 30 (2017): 231-50. https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.30.011 [Open access]","Michael Dee et al., \"An absolute chronology for early Egypt using radiocarbon dating and Bayesian statistical modelling,\" <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society A</i> 469, no. 2159 (2013): 20130395. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3780825/ [Open access]","\"Early Dynastic Period,\" Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt. https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/early-dynastic-period [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/240911_CvZ-Palermo-stone.jpg/1920px-240911_CvZ-Palermo-stone.jpg","title":"The Palermo Stone","desc":"The Palermo Stone, the largest surviving fragment of the Royal Annals of the Old Kingdom, in the Museo archeologico regionale Antonino Salinas, Palermo. CvZ, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:240911_CvZ-Palermo-stone.jpg","alt":"A dark stone fragment carved with horizontal registers of hieroglyphs, the topmost row divided into small rectangular compartments each holding a royal name."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Egyptians perform the Union of the Two Lands at every accession?","a":"Because the annals give each reign one formula — an appearance as King of Upper Egypt, an appearance as King of Lower Egypt, then the Union — so it was a rite renewed rather than a single past event."},{"q":"Why does archaeology give no moment of unification?","a":"Because the Naqada culture may have been a single political unit throughout or a set of areas each under its own ruler, and nothing in the record settles which."},{"q":"Why do the Tell el-Farkha inscriptions of Iry-Hor matter?","a":"Because he is a king known chiefly from Abydos, so his name in the eastern Delta is taken as proof of a connection there and probably of rule over the whole country before the 1st Dynasty."}]},
{"id":"wh-206","num":206,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"In the pictorial script called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most signs stand for consonants alone, and a determinative was added after a word to show which class of thing was meant.","questions":["Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were kept for monuments and temples while everyday writing used cursive hands, the surviving record leans heavily towards eternity rather than the business of ordinary life.","Standardised already in the first dynasties, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remained the anchor of Egyptian writing from the late 4th millennium BCE until the Roman Period, some three thousand years."],"answer":"Egyptian hieroglyphs","answerText":"Egyptian hieroglyphs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First writing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Standardised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the 3rd Dynasty</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Egyptian hieroglyphs</b> are the pictorial script of ancient Egypt, in which every sign is a recognisable image — a reed, an owl, a jackal — and in which two basic kinds of sign share the work, some denoting sounds and some denoting ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sound signs stand for consonants alone, singly or in groups of two or three, so the vowels a speaker supplied were never written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That leaves a great deal of ambiguity, since about, but and bite would share one spelling, and the remedy was a determinative placed after most words to show the class of thing meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second habit, the phonetic complement, repeats a consonant already carried by a two- or three-letter sign as a reminder of how to read it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those reminders served the eye as well, because inscriptions were composed as sequences of square blocks and an extra sign often completed one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Writing appears in the Nile valley in the late 4th millennium BCE, emerging among other systems of signs as a way of sharpening the ideological claims of the powers that preceded the pharaonic state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Standardisation came quickly, and already in the first dynasties there were settled norms for the direction of writing, the shapes of individual signs and the spelling of single words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The script then held for some three thousand years, the anchor of writing in Egypt from the earliest inscriptions until the Roman Period, yet it was never the everyday hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Simplified forms were in use within the first few hundred years of writing, and Greek writers later named the two standard cursives hieratic, meaning priestly, and demotic, meaning popular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hieroglyphs stayed with monument and temple, set beside formal art in what one account calls a horizon of eternity, so the surviving record leans towards the eternal rather than the everyday, and the signs themselves run from the purely pictorial to the strictly phonetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","egypt","writing","language"],"sources":["“The System of Hieroglyphic Writing, a Short Introduction,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/system1.html [Open access]","“The Sound (Phonetic) Signs in Egyptian Writing,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/phoneticsigns.html [Open access]","“The Development of Writing,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/development.html [Open access]","“The Hieratic Script,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/hieratic.html [Open access]","“Scope of Writing in Ancient Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/writing/scope.html [Open access]","Pascal Vernus, “La naissance de l’écriture dans l’Égypte pharaonique : une problématique revisitée,” <i>Archéo-Nil</i> 26 (2016): 105–34. https://doi.org/10.3406/arnil.2016.1105 [Open access]","Stéphane Polis, “The Functions and Toposyntax of Ancient Egyptian Hieroglyphs. Exploring the Iconicity and Spatiality of Pictorial Graphemes,” <i>Signata</i> 9 (2018): 291–363. https://doi.org/10.4000/signata.1920 [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Wall_inscriptions_at_Karnak_Temple_Complex_in_Luxor%2C_Egypt_-_IMG_7842_%2833825908220%29.jpg/1920px-Wall_inscriptions_at_Karnak_Temple_Complex_in_Luxor%2C_Egypt_-_IMG_7842_%2833825908220%29.jpg","title":"Hieroglyphs on a temple wall at Karnak","desc":"Columns of carved hieroglyphs, divided by ruled lines, on a wall of the Karnak temple complex at Luxor. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by je_blanco, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wall_inscriptions_at_Karnak_Temple_Complex_in_Luxor,_Egypt_-_IMG_7842_(33825908220).jpg","alt":"A sandstone wall carved with vertical columns of hieroglyphic signs, among them owls, vipers, reeds, hands and seated figures, separated by ruled lines."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the script need determinatives?","a":"Because its sound signs stand for consonants alone and vowels were never written, so words like about, but and bite would share one spelling until a sign showing the class of thing meant was added."},{"q":"Why did phonetic complements serve the eye as well as the ear?","a":"Because inscriptions were composed as sequences of square blocks, and an extra sign repeating a consonant often completed one."},{"q":"Why does the surviving record lean towards the eternal?","a":"Because hieroglyphs stayed with monument and temple, set beside formal art, while everyday writing used the cursive hands later named hieratic and demotic."}]},
{"id":"wh-207","num":207,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Because Greek was still read while the Egyptian scripts were not, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered a way back into hieroglyphs by carrying one priestly decree written out three times over.","questions":["The decree cut on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> orders that copies be set up in every temple of the first, second and third rank, so the broken slab is one survivor of many.","Praise for a young king’s remitted debts and his capture of a rebel city fills much of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose priests met at Memphis in 196 BCE to vote him honours."],"answer":"Rosetta Stone","answerText":"Rosetta Stone","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decree</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 196 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regnal year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th of Ptolemy V</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Rosetta Stone</b> is a broken slab of dark stone carrying one priestly decree written out three times, in hieroglyphs, in the everyday Egyptian script called demotic, and in Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Priests assembled at Memphis passed it on 27 March 196 BCE, the ninth year of Ptolemy V, to mark the first commemoration of his coronation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fourteen lines of the hieroglyphic text survive against 32 of the demotic and 54 of the Greek, the two upper corners and the lower right having broken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it lists the young king's benefactions, from debts of grain and money remitted to disaffected soldiers allowed back onto their land, set beside the storming of a rebel city the Greek calls Lycopolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In return the priests voted him statues, shrines and festivals, and the closing clause orders the decree cut in all three writings and set up in every temple of the first, second and third rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That instruction is why the slab is one copy among many, and broken duplicates have since been recovered elsewhere in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the time anyone tried to read it the scripts had long been silent, hieroglyphs still being carved in 394 CE and demotic still written at Philae into the 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> French soldiers turned the slab up near Rashid in the middle of July 1799, and the 19 July often given is arbitrary, since news of the find reached the Institut d’Égypte only on 29 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It passed to Britain under the capitulation of 1801 and reached London the next year, though ink impressions and engraved plates had already gone out across Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The way in was the cartouche, since the Greek named Ptolemy and the ring of signs repeated five times had to spell him, while an obelisk from Philae naming Cleopatra gave shared signs their sound values, enough for an alphabet of names though Coptic was needed to read connected Egyptian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Rosetta Stone</i> (London: British Museum, 1913), https://archive.org/details/rosettastone00budguoft. [Open access]","E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Decrees of Memphis and Canopus</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1904), https://archive.org/details/decreesofmemphis01budg. [Open access]","E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Decrees of Memphis and Canopus</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1904), 168, https://archive.org/details/decreesofmemphis01budg. [Open access]","Jean Leclant, “Champollion, la pierre de Rosette et le déchiffrement des hiéroglyphes,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 116, no. 3 (1972): 557–65, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1972.12797. [Open access]","Jean Leclant, “Le Lieutenant Bouchard, l’Institut d’Égypte et la pierre de Rosette,” <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 146 (1999): 6–24, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.1999.2411. [Open access]","Michel Dewachter, “Du texte au signe, la pierre de Rosette et les premières collections d’antiquités égyptiennes,” <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 146 (1999): 25–58, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.1999.2412. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","egypt","writing","language"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/The_Rosetta_Stone.jpg/1920px-The_Rosetta_Stone.jpg","title":"The Rosetta Stone","desc":"The Rosetta Stone photographed for the British Museum's own sixpenny guide to it of 1922, showing the hieroglyphic, demotic and Greek texts one above another. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by Donald Macbeth, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Rosetta_Stone.jpg","alt":"A broken slab of dark stone densely covered in three bands of writing, hieroglyphs at the top, a cursive script across the middle and Greek capitals below, with both upper corners and the lower right corner broken away."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the stone one copy among many?","a":"Because the decree's closing clause orders it cut in all three writings and set up in every temple of the first, second and third rank, and broken duplicates have since been recovered."},{"q":"Why were the scripts unreadable by the time the slab was found?","a":"Because they had long fallen silent — hieroglyphs still carved in 394 CE, demotic still written at Philae into the 5th century — and nobody had read either since."},{"q":"Why was the cartouche the way in?","a":"Because the Greek named Ptolemy and the ring of signs repeated five times had to spell him, while an obelisk from Philae naming Cleopatra gave shared signs their sound values."}]},
{"id":"wh-208","num":208,"category":"World History","question":"Egypt wrote on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, made by slicing the pith of a tall marsh sedge into strips, laying them in two crossed layers, and pressing them into a sheet.","questions":["Pliny lists nine grades of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from the thirteen-finger sheets once reserved for sacred books down to the coarse wrapping stuff sold to merchants, never more than twenty sheets to a roll.","One of Egypt's main exports was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, still being written on in the early Islamic period, though its production fell away as parchment and cloth-based paper took over."],"answer":"papyrus","answerText":"papyrus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest roll</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st Dynasty, c. 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Latest dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1087 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","egypt","writing","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Papyrus</b> is a writing material made from the pith of <i>Cyperus papyrus</i>, a giant sedge reaching about 4 m (13 feet) along the banks of the Nile and in its marshes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Theophrastus, writing in the 4th century BCE, says it stands in water some two cubits deep, that its stalk is three-cornered, and that Egyptians built boats from it and wove sails, mats and ropes from the rind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To make a sheet the green rind was peeled away and the white pith cut lengthwise into thin strips, which were laid side by side with their edges overlapping and a second layer set across them at right angles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pressing or beating fused the two layers, the plant's own sap doing the work of an adhesive, and the dried sheet was burnished smooth with a stone or shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pliny describes the same crossed layers but credits the muddy Nile water the table was moistened with, and he adds that a roll never held more than twenty sheets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He sorts the product into nine grades, the best thirteen fingers across and once kept for sacred books, the coarsest fit only for wrapping goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sheet heights varied with the period, commonly 38 to 42 cm (15 to 17 inches) in the Middle Kingdom and 16 to 20 cm (6 to 8 inches) in the New Kingdom, the tallest known 53.5 cm (21 inches) and from Gurob.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest surviving roll comes from the First Dynasty tomb of Hemaka at Saqqara, so the material long predates the story Pliny passes on, dating its discovery to Alexander's founding of Alexandria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Under Roman rule the codex arrived, leaves bound as a book rather than pasted into a roll, and in the late empire such books largely replaced rolls, parchment and later paper displacing papyrus itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It was one of Egypt's main exports and still written on in the early Islamic period, the latest datable example an Arabic document of 1087 CE, though production had fallen away in the 7th and 8th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ayman S. Taha, Mohamed Z. M. Salem, Wael A. A. Abo Elgat, Hayssam M. Ali, Ashraf A. Hatamleh, and Eslam M. Abdel-Salam, “Assessment of the Impact of Different Treatments on the Technological and Antifungal Properties of Papyrus (<i>Cyperus Papyrus</i> L.) Sheets,” <i>Materials</i> 12, no. 4 (2019): 620. https://doi.org/10.3390/ma12040620 [Open access]","Theophrastus, <i>Enquiry into Plants</i> IV.8.3–4, trans. Arthur Hort, Loeb Classical Library (London: Heinemann, 1916), 1:347–49. https://archive.org/details/enquiryintoplan01theo [Open access]","“Papyrus Making,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/papyrusmaking.html [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> XIII.23–24, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Bohn, 1855), 3:187–89. https://archive.org/details/naturalhistoryof31855plin [Open access]","“Papyrus: Size and Format,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/papyrussize.html [Open access]","“Papyrus as Writing Material,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/papyrus.html [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> XIII.21, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Bohn, 1855), 3:185–86. https://archive.org/details/naturalhistoryof31855plin [Open access]","“Writing Material: Codex,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/craft/codex.html [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Making_papyrus.jpg/1920px-Making_papyrus.jpg","title":"Strips laid in crossed layers","desc":"A modern demonstration of the method: wet strips of papyrus pith laid side by side on a board, with a second layer set across them, to be pressed and dried into a sheet. FrogUnderALilyPad, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Making_papyrus.jpg","alt":"Hands laying pale strips of papyrus pith side by side on a board, a second layer crossing them at right angles, with a rolling pin and more strips beside it."},"why":[{"q":"Why does a papyrus sheet need no adhesive?","a":"Because the strips are laid in two layers at right angles and pressing or beating fuses them, the plant's own sap doing the work — though Pliny credits the muddy Nile water instead."},{"q":"Why is Pliny's account of its discovery wrong?","a":"Because he dates it to Alexander's founding of Alexandria, while the earliest surviving roll comes from the First Dynasty tomb of Hemaka at Saqqara."},{"q":"Why did papyrus give way as a writing material?","a":"Because under Roman rule the codex arrived, leaves bound as a book rather than pasted into a roll, and parchment and later paper displaced the material itself."}]},
{"id":"wh-209","num":209,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"The Egyptians called their king nswt or ity, and the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that we use instead comes from per-aa, 'great house', which first named the palace rather than the man.","questions":["By the Middle Kingdom every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried five names, among them a Horus name and a birth name preceded by 'son of Ra' and written inside a cartouche.","A religious text sets out what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was for: to judge between men, to content the gods, to make what is Right happen and to annihilate what is Wrong."],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"pharaoh","answerText":"pharaoh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Five names by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2025 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sobekneferu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 BCE</span></div>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["title","history","religion","egypt","monarchy","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Pharaoh</b> is the English name for the king of ancient Egypt, taken from the Egyptian <i>per-aa</i>, 'great house', a phrase that named the palace and its government before it came to mean the monarch in person, and which reached English through Hebrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Egyptians used other words for their ruler: <i>nswt</i> and <i>ity</i> were the commonest, and their term for kingship itself was <i>nsyt</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kingship comes into view with the unification of Egypt around 3000 BCE and keeps its distinctive character until the Hellenistic and Roman periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From the First Dynasty the king was identified with Horus, the god of order, and from the Fourth Dynasty he bore the title 'son of Ra' as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was held to be divine in nature yet housed in a mortal body, and that double character is the heart of Egyptian kingship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By the Middle Kingdom he carried five names: a Horus name, a name of the Two Ladies, a Gold Horus name, a throne name taken at accession, and the birth name introduced as 'son of Ra'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The throne name and the birth name were each enclosed in an oval the Egyptians called <i>shen</i>, 'ring', which early Egyptologists renamed the cartouche.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A granite sphinx of Ramesses II carries the full five-fold titulary along its base, and his successor Merenptah added cartouches of his own to the shoulders after his father's death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One religious text states the office plainly: Ra placed the king on earth to judge between men, to content the gods, to make what is Right happen and to annihilate what is Wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was worshipped during his lifetime as well as after it, and although the office was normally male a few women held it, among them Sobekneferu, whose birth name is introduced as 'daughter of Ra'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1905), 73, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","“What is a king in Ancient Egypt?,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/king/what.html. [Open access]","“The five names of a king,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/kingname/index.html. [Open access]","Jennifer Houser Wegner, “The Granite Sphinx of Ramesses II,” <i>Expedition</i> 54, no. 3 (2012), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-granite-sphinx-of-ramesses-ii/. [Open access]","“What does a king have to do in ancient Egypt?,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/king/kingrepertory.html. [Open access]","“The cult of the reigning King in ancient Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/king/cult.html. [Open access]","“Nomen,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ideology/kingname/nomen.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Head_of_a_King_with_a_Nemes_Headdress_MET_LC-2021_41_96_EGDP031852.jpg/1920px-Head_of_a_King_with_a_Nemes_Headdress_MET_LC-2021_41_96_EGDP031852.jpg","title":"A king in the nemes headcloth","desc":"Head of a king wearing the nemes headdress, limestone, Egypt, ca. 332-30 BCE. Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_a_King_with_a_Nemes_Headdress_MET_LC-2021_41_96_EGDP031852.jpg","alt":"A broken limestone head on a black museum mount, wearing a striped headcloth that falls in lappets beside the face, with a small rearing cobra carved at the brow."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the word pharaoh not mean king in Egyptian?","a":"Because per-aa means 'great house' and named the palace and its government before coming to mean the monarch in person; the Egyptians' own commonest words for their ruler were nswt and ity."},{"q":"Why is the king's double character the heart of Egyptian kingship?","a":"Because he was held to be divine in nature yet housed in a mortal body, identified with Horus from the First Dynasty and titled son of Ra from the Fourth."},{"q":"Why were only two of the five royal names put in cartouches?","a":"Because the throne name taken at accession and the birth name introduced as 'son of Ra' were each enclosed in the ring the Egyptians called shen."}]},
{"id":"wh-210","num":210,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"The Step Pyramid at Saqqara, Sneferu’s first true pyramid at Meydum and Khufu’s great pyramid at Giza were all raised during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the age of Dynasties 3 to 6.","questions":["An official whose title is conventionally translated vizier organised the pyramid work forces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when the whole administration existed chiefly to supply the palace and its cult.","Cedar came from Byblos and an expedition reached Punt under Sahure, but by the long reign of Pepy II the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had lost its hold on parts of Upper Egypt."],"answer":"Old Kingdom","answerText":"Old Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2181 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty 4</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 2450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty 6</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2300 – 2181 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Old Kingdom</b> is the modern name for the Third to Sixth Dynasties of Egypt, about 2686 to 2181 BCE, one of the three main classical periods of Egyptian culture<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. Nearly all its royal building stands in one strip of desert between Meydum and Abu Rowash, the cemetery country of Memphis; everything else counts as provincial<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Netjerikhet, later called Djoser, was buried at Saqqara in the Step Pyramid, the earliest pyramidal structure surviving in Egypt and the first architectural complex made entirely of dressed stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Sneferu opened the Fourth Dynasty with the first true pyramid, at Meydum, then built two more at Dahshur for unknown reasons; his successor Khufu raised the great pyramid at Giza<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. No pyramid stood alone: each was the centre of a complex with a temple at its foot, a valley temple joined by a causeway and a satellite pyramid, where priests maintained the king’s cult<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. <br><br> The administration centred on the palace, and its principal aim was to supply the king’s household and keep the royal cult going in pyramids, sun temples and local temples<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. From the Fourth Dynasty the highest office carried the title conventionally translated vizier, who organised the pyramid work forces and to whom other palace officials answered; he also served as chief justice and often as chief of all the king’s works<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>. How many men a pyramid took can only be guessed at: Herodotus reports a hundred thousand at Khufu’s, where recent research calculates about 20,000 to 30,000<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>. Abroad the kings imported cedar from Byblos, left inscribed objects as far east as Ebla in Syria, and recorded an expedition to Punt under Sahure on the Palermo Stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>. From the end of the Fifth Dynasty an overseer of Upper Egypt shows the centre tightening its grip, but in the long reign of Pepy II it lost control of parts of Upper Egypt, and with that the Old Kingdom ends<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>.","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","egypt","architecture","monarchy","bronze age"],"sources":["“Egypt in the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ok/general.html. [Open access]","“Egyptian Kings of the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/oldkingdomkings.html. [Open access]","“Saqqara,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/saqqara. [Open access]","“Giza Plateau,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/giza-plateau. [Open access]","“Administration in Ancient Egypt: Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/administration/ok.html. [Open access]","“Administration in the Old Kingdom: the Provinces,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/administration/okprovince.html. [Open access]","“Pyramids in Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/index.html. [Open access]","“The workmen at a pyramid,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/workmen.html. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 82–83, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","“Foreign relations of the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foreignrelations/oldkingdom.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Bent_Pyramid_of_Snofru.jpg/1920px-Bent_Pyramid_of_Snofru.jpg","title":"The Bent Pyramid at Dahshur","desc":"The Bent Pyramid, built for Sneferu at Dahshur in the Fourth Dynasty, photographed in March 2023. Darer101, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Darer101, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bent_Pyramid_of_Snofru.jpg","alt":"A large limestone pyramid in desert sand whose sides change angle part way up, rising more steeply below and more shallowly above, with small figures of visitors at its foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why does nearly all the period's royal building stand in one strip of desert?","a":"Because that strip between Meydum and Abu Rowash is the cemetery country of Memphis, and everything outside it counts as provincial."},{"q":"Why did no pyramid stand alone?","a":"Because each was the centre of a complex — a temple at its foot, a valley temple joined by a causeway, a satellite pyramid — where priests maintained the king's cult."},{"q":"Why can the size of a pyramid workforce only be guessed at?","a":"Because the figures disagree wildly: Herodotus reports a hundred thousand men at Khufu's, where recent research calculates about 20,000 to 30,000."}]},
{"id":"wh-211","num":211,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Netjerikhet, first king of the Third Dynasty, was buried at Saqqara under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the earliest pyramidal structure surviving in Egypt and the first complex built entirely of dressed stone.","questions":["A stone mastaba enlarged on the ground and then raised by ever smaller rectangular blocks became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the transitional form between the flat tomb and the smooth-sided pyramid.","Late Period sources make Imhotep, who had been a principal minister, the architect of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and turned him into a god of medicine the Greeks equated with Asklepios."],"answer":"Step Pyramid of Djoser","answerText":"Step Pyramid of Djoser","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2667 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Third Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2686 – 2600 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Step Pyramid of Djoser</b> at Saqqara was raised for Netjerikhet, first king of the Third Dynasty; it is the earliest pyramidal structure surviving in Egypt and the first complex built entirely of dressed stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. Contemporary sources give only his Horus name, Netjerikhet; the name Djoser by which he is now known is attested nowhere until much later texts<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. It began as a stone mastaba, the flat-topped rectangular tomb earlier kings were buried under, then was widened on the ground and raised by further blocks, each smaller than the one below<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. The result was a terraced building, the transitional form between the mastaba and the smooth-sided pyramids that followed, and the first large structure of stone known in history<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. It stands at the centre of a funerary complex enclosed by a niched wall 277 by 544 metres (909 by 1,785 feet), the ancestor of the walled complexes of later reigns, which added a valley temple and a causeway<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>. <br><br> Egyptian memory gave the work to Imhotep, Netjerikhet's principal minister; his standing as architect of the complex, and as a deified son of the Memphite god Ptah, appears only in Late Period sources<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. Manetho preserved a tradition that building in stone was introduced under this king, whom he calls Tosorthros; earlier stone structures are now known, but the reign does mark the beginning of extensive building in stone<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. Imhotep's reputation outlasted the dynasty by millennia: scribes poured him a libation before work, and he became a god of medicine whom the Greeks called Imouthes and took for Asklepios<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. Every king of the Third Dynasty laid out a complex on the same large scale, and none of them was finished, the reigns being short<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. The first true pyramid came a dynasty later at Meydum, where Sneferu's builders began a step pyramid and changed the plan as it went up; Saqqara served Memphis as a cemetery for two thousand years more<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>.","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["building","history","egypt","architecture","religion","bronze age"],"sources":["“Egypt in the Old Kingdom,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/ok/general.html. [Open access]","“King Djoser,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/kingdjoser.html. [Open access]","“Evolution of the Pyramids,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/shape.html. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 112–14, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","“Saqqara,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/saqqara. [Open access]","“Pyramids in Egypt,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Saqqara%2C_Pyramid_of_Djoser%2C_Ancient_Egypt.jpg/1920px-Saqqara%2C_Pyramid_of_Djoser%2C_Ancient_Egypt.jpg","title":"The Step Pyramid of Djoser at Saqqara","desc":"The Step Pyramid at Saqqara, seen across the desert with visitors on the path at the right for scale. Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saqqara,_Pyramid_of_Djoser,_Ancient_Egypt.jpg","alt":"A terraced stone pyramid of weathered pale blocks rising in receding stages from flat desert sand under a clear blue sky, with a line of visitors on a walkway at the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the king better called Netjerikhet than Djoser?","a":"Because contemporary sources give only his Horus name, Netjerikhet, and the name Djoser is attested nowhere until much later texts."},{"q":"Why is the monument a transitional form?","a":"Because it began as a stone mastaba, the flat-topped tomb earlier kings were buried under, and was then widened and raised by successively smaller blocks into a terraced building."},{"q":"Why is Imhotep's authorship a late tradition?","a":"Because his standing as architect of the complex, and as a deified son of Ptah, appears only in Late Period sources rather than in contemporary ones."}]},
{"id":"wh-212","num":212,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Raised for Khufu on the plateau opposite modern Cairo, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood about 147 metres (481 feet) high, and no building anywhere exceeded that height for another 3,800 years.","questions":["Herodotus was told that a hundred thousand men had laboured for twenty years on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where modern estimates of the workforce run nearer twenty to thirty thousand.","Cosmic-ray imaging in 2017 revealed a void at least thirty metres long above the great inclined gallery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first large internal feature found there since the 19th century."],"answer":"Great Pyramid of Giza","answerText":"Great Pyramid of Giza","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2589 – 2566 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fourth Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2613 – 2494 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Great Pyramid of Giza</b> is the tomb raised for Khufu, founder of Egypt’s Fourth Dynasty, on the desert plateau opposite modern Cairo<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. It is the oldest and largest of the three pyramids there, the others belonging to his son Khafre and grandson Menkaure, and nothing built anywhere exceeded its height for 3,800 years<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. It stood about 147 metres (481 feet) high on a square base some 230 metres (755 feet) a side, laid out so accurately that the average error in their equality, squareness and level is under a ten-thousandth<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. It is limestone almost throughout, by one estimate some 2,300,000 blocks averaging two and a half tons, and the finely dressed casing that once sheathed it was quarried away in later centuries<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. The pyramid stood at the centre of a complex rather than alone: a funerary temple at its foot, a causeway to a valley temple, boat burials, and three smaller pyramids for queens<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. <br><br> How the Egyptians raised it is still argued over: almost nothing survives to describe the work, and the ramps, the setting of the angle and the lifting of the blocks are reconstructed rather than recorded<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Herodotus was told that a hundred thousand men had laboured on it for twenty years, a tradition some early Egyptologists accepted, where recent estimates of the workforce run nearer twenty to thirty thousand<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. Its interior is unusually elaborate, entered in the eighteenth course of the north face and running through a great inclined gallery to a granite burial chamber with hollow chambers stacked above it<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. The passages were sealed behind granite plugs and portcullises, the masonry above is visibly hastier than that below, and at least two changes of plan can be read in the building itself<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. In 2017 a team imaging it with cosmic-ray muons reported a void at least thirty metres long above that gallery, found by three kinds of detector and the first large internal feature since the 19th century<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>.","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","archaeology","architecture","religion","egypt"],"sources":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “Giza Plateau,” Egypt’s Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, accessed 21 August 2026, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/giza-plateau/. [Open access]","Digital Egypt for Universities, “The workmen at a pyramid,” University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/workmen.html. [Open access]","Digital Egypt for Universities, “How a pyramid was built,” University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/how.html. [Open access]","Digital Egypt for Universities, “Table of all known royal pyramids in Egypt,” University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/pyramids/owners.html. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 117–19, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","Kunihiro Morishima et al., “Discovery of a Big Void in Khufu’s Pyramid by Observation of Cosmic-Ray Muons,” <i>Nature</i> 552 (2017): 386–90, https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.01576. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Giza_Great_Pyramid_of_Khufu_%289793898043%29.jpg/1920px-Giza_Great_Pyramid_of_Khufu_%289793898043%29.jpg","title":"Great Pyramid of Giza","desc":"A corner of Khufu's pyramid at Giza, its stripped core stepping up from the desert floor, photographed in 2002. Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giza_Great_Pyramid_of_Khufu_(9793898043).jpg","alt":"A corner of the Great Pyramid rising steeply against a blue sky, its rough limestone blocks stepping upwards, with people standing at its base for scale."},"why":[{"q":"Why is how the pyramid was built still argued over?","a":"Because almost nothing survives to describe the work, so the ramps, the setting of the angle and the lifting of the blocks are reconstructed rather than recorded."},{"q":"Why can changes of plan be read in the building itself?","a":"Because the masonry above the burial chamber is visibly hastier than that below, and at least two such changes can be seen in the structure."},{"q":"Why did muon imaging matter in 2017?","a":"Because it reported a void at least thirty metres long above the great gallery, found by three kinds of detector and the first large internal feature discovered since the 19th century."}]},
{"id":"wh-213","num":213,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Carved directly out of the limestone bedrock beside Khafre's valley temple, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives a Fourth Dynasty king the body of a lion, the animal standing for his power.","questions":["A red granite slab set between the forepaws of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> records a dream in which the sun god, complaining that the desert sand had reached him, promised Thutmose IV the kingdom.","Worshipped in the Eighteenth Dynasty as Horemakhet, or Horus in the Horizon, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now threatened by groundwater rising beneath the Giza plateau from the canals and suburbs of Cairo."],"answer":"Great Sphinx of Giza","answerText":"Great Sphinx of Giza","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Khafre's reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2558 – 2532 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fourth Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2613 – 2494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dream Stela</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1390 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","egypt","religion","monarchy"],"abstract":"The <b>Great Sphinx of Giza</b> is a colossal figure with a lion's body and a king's head, carved directly out of the limestone bedrock during the Fourth Dynasty, about 2613 to 2494 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Egypt a sphinx was a recurring portrait of the reigning king, the lion's body standing for the Pharaoh's power, and this one is the oldest known and by far the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The evidence points to Khafre, builder of the second Giza pyramid, whose valley temple analysis has shown was finished before work began on the Sphinx and the temple in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The face has also been found to resemble Khafre's statues closely, though a standard history of 1905 could still say only that the attribution was not yet determined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure sits low on the plateau beside that temple, its rock base about 22 metres (72 feet) above sea level against roughly 68 metres (223 feet) at the pyramid above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By the Eighteenth Dynasty it was worshipped as Horemakhet, 'Horus in the Horizon', a form of the sun god, and Amenhotep II built a temple beside it for that god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His son Thutmose IV set a red granite slab between the forepaws, cut from an architrave of a neighbouring building, on which the god appears to the sleeping prince, complains that the desert sand has reached him, and promises the kingdom in return for clearing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Egyptologists read that surviving slab as a much later copy rather than the original record, and its lower third has flaked away, so more than half the text is lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The body was buried again through most of the centuries that followed, and when the front was dug clear in 1818 the paws proved to stretch some 15 metres (50 feet) forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The figure now faces a hazard its builders never met, in groundwater rising beneath the plateau from the irrigation canals and spreading suburbs of Cairo, which stands within a few metres of the surface behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “The Great Sphinx,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/monuments/the-great-sphinx/. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i> (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1905), 120, 327, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00brearich. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Eighteenth Dynasty</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), 320–23, https://archive.org/details/ancientrecordsof02brea. [Open access]","Howard Vyse, <i>Appendix to Operations Carried on at the Pyramids of Gizeh in 1837</i>, vol. 3 (London: James Fraser, 1842), 108–11, https://archive.org/details/operationscarri03vysegoog. [Open access]","Sayed Hemeda and Alghreeb Sonbol, “Sustainability Problems of the Giza Pyramids,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 8, no. 8 (2020), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-020-0356-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Sphinx_partially_excavated.jpg/1920px-Sphinx_partially_excavated.jpg","title":"The Great Sphinx after clearance","alt":"Sepia photograph of the Great Sphinx with its head and chest clear of the sand and its flanks still buried, two pyramids on the skyline behind.","desc":"The Sphinx of Giza, partially excavated, with two pyramids in the background. Photograph by Maison Bonfils, Beirut, between 1867 and 1899. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sphinx_partially_excavated.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the figure attributed to Khafre?","a":"Because analysis showed his valley temple was finished before work began on the Sphinx and the temple in front of it, and the face closely resembles his statues."},{"q":"Why is the Dream Stele read as a copy?","a":"Because Egyptologists take the surviving slab, cut from a neighbouring building's architrave, as a much later copy rather than the original record, and its lower third has flaked away."},{"q":"Why does the monument face a hazard its builders never met?","a":"Because groundwater is rising beneath the plateau from the irrigation canals and spreading suburbs of Cairo, standing within a few metres of the surface behind it."}]},
{"id":"wh-214","num":214,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"A body prepared by Egyptian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent about forty days drying in natron, a salt fetched from Wadi el-Natrun, within a procedure that ran to some seventy days in all.","questions":["Herodotus reports that Egyptian embalmers showed a bereaved family painted wooden models and offered three grades of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at three prices, the costliest drawing the brain out through the nostrils.","Labelled vessels from a Twenty-sixth Dynasty workshop at Saqqara have matched the substances used in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to their Egyptian names, showing conifer mixtures where myrrh had long been assumed."],"answer":"mummification","answerText":"mummification","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proto-embalming</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4300 – 3100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Whole process</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 70 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Saqqara workshop</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 664 – 525 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","egypt","religion","science"],"abstract":"<b>Mummification</b> is the deliberate preservation of a dead body, and in Egypt a requirement rather than a courtesy: the corpse had to go on serving as the dwelling of the ka and the ba, the aspects of a person that outlived it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The practice reaches far back, an early treatment of the dead beginning around 4300 BCE, before Egypt had kings to bury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In its developed form the embalmers drew the organs out through an incision about ten centimetres (four inches) long in the left side of the body and placed them in canopic jars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four jars were used, their stoppers carved as the heads of the four sons of Horus, each set of viscera under the care of a goddess named on the jar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The body was then dried in natron, a salt fetched from Wadi el-Natrun, for about forty days, and the whole procedure with its rituals ran to some seventy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Then came the wrapping, in bandages of linen, with protective amulets laid among them at particular points on the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, who saw the trade at work, reports that the embalmers showed a family painted wooden models and offered three grades at three prices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his account of the costliest the brain is drawn out through the nostrils with an iron hook and the flank opened with a stone knife, the cheapest being no more than a purge of the belly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ceramic vessels from a Twenty-sixth Dynasty embalming workshop at Saqqara are labelled with their contents and their use, and the residues in them include resins carried from tropical forests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two substances long translated as myrrh and sacred oil proved to be conifer mixtures, while scans of child mummies show the treatment varied with age at death, with status and with the place of burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, “Mummification Museum,” Discover Egypt's Monuments, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/museums/mummification-museum/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i>, trans. A. D. Godley, 2.86–88, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=86. [Open access]","E. A. Wallis Budge, <i>The Mummy: Chapters on Egyptian Funereal Archaeology</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1893), 160–62, https://archive.org/details/mummyhandbookofe00budguoft. [Open access]","Maxime Rageot et al., “Biomolecular analyses enable new insights into ancient Egyptian embalming,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7947 (2023): 287–93, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05663-4. [Open access]","Stephanie Zesch et al., “The multifaceted nature of Egyptian mummification: Paleoradiological insights into child mummies,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 12 (2024): e0316018, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0316018. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/80/Egyptian_-_A_Complete_Set_of_Canopic_Jars_-_Walters_41171%2C_41172%2C_41173%2C_41174_-_Group.jpg","title":"A complete set of canopic jars","desc":"Four Egyptian canopic jars, their stoppers carved as the heads of the four sons of Horus, who guarded the organs drawn from the body during embalming. Walters Art Museum, Baltimore, acquired by Henry Walters. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Walters Art Museum (41171, 41172, 41173, 41174), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_-_A_Complete_Set_of_Canopic_Jars_-_Walters_41171,_41172,_41173,_41174_-_Group.jpg","alt":"Four squat stone jars in a row, each with a carved stopper: a human head, a baboon, a jackal and a falcon."},"why":[{"q":"Why was preserving the body a requirement rather than a courtesy?","a":"Because the corpse had to go on serving as the dwelling of the ka and the ba, the aspects of a person that outlived it."},{"q":"Why did the trade offer three grades at three prices?","a":"Because it was a business: Herodotus reports embalmers showing families painted wooden models, the costliest drawing the brain out through the nostrils and the cheapest no more than a purge of the belly."},{"q":"Why has analysis changed what we thought the embalmers used?","a":"Because residues in labelled vessels from a Saqqara workshop include resins carried from tropical forests, and two substances long translated as myrrh and sacred oil proved to be conifer mixtures."}]},
{"id":"wh-215","num":215,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Each ancient Egyptian took from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whatever selection of its more than 190 spells could be afforded, and had them copied onto papyrus, linen, coffins and the walls of the tomb.","questions":["A Ramesside ostracon prices a decorated papyrus of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at one deben of silver, about half a year's wage for a workman or the price of three donkeys.","The best known scene in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to spell 125, where the heart of a dead person is weighed in a balance against the feather of Maat before Osiris."],"answer":"Book of the Dead","answerText":"Book of the Dead","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest spells</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1773 – 1650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1580 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st – 2nd century CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Book of the Dead</b> is not a book at all but a loose corpus of more than 190 separate spells<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, from which each ancient Egyptian took whatever selection was wanted for the journey after death. The title is a modern one, borrowed from the German <i>Totenbuch</i> that nineteenth-century scholars used, perhaps shaped by an Arabic phrase villagers used for papyri found in tombs<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. The Egyptians themselves called it the Spells for Going Forth by Day, and going forth by day meant the <i>ba</i>, the soul, leaving the tomb to travel with the sun god across the sky<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. Its spells did not appear from nowhere: many were revised out of the Pyramid Texts carved inside late Old Kingdom pyramids and the Coffin Texts written inside Middle Kingdom coffins<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>. The earliest recognisable spells are not on papyrus but on heart scarabs and coffins of the Thirteenth Dynasty, and the corpus only came into its own at the close of the Seventeenth Dynasty and through the Eighteenth<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>. <br><br> No two manuscripts are alike, because the choice, length and quality of a copy followed what its owner wanted and could pay for<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>. A Ramesside ostracon prices a decorated papyrus at one deben of silver, which was about half a year's wage for a workman, or three donkeys<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>. Spells were accordingly written on every surface a burial could offer: leather, linen bandages, cartonnage cases, coffins, funerary figures, amulets and tomb walls<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. The most reproduced picture in the whole corpus belongs to spell 125, in which the heart of the dead person is weighed against the feather of Maat in the hall of Osiris<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>. Even Tutankhamun was buried without a papyrus of it, his spells being on the tomb walls, his golden mask and his funerary figures, and the last attested spells of all were written in Demotic in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>.","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["text","history","religion","egypt","literature","writing"],"sources":["Foy Scalf, “What Is the Book of the Dead?” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 21–28, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Peter F. Dorman, “The Origins and Early Development of the Book of the Dead,” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 29–40, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Holger Kockelmann, “How a Book of the Dead Manuscript Was Produced,” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 67–74, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Foy Scalf, “The Death of the Book of the Dead,” in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 139–48, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Marcus Müller, “Das Totenbuch,” Totenbuch-Projekt, Universität Bonn, February 2012, https://totenbuch.awk.nrw.de/projekt/das-totenbuch. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://images.metmuseum.org/CRDImages/eg/original/DT11633.jpg","title":"The weighing of the heart, from the Book of the Dead of Nauny","desc":"A detail of the papyrus made for Nauny, a chantress of Amun. In the lower register jackal-headed Anubis kneels at a great balance, steadying the pan that holds her heart while a baboon sits on the beam beneath a sun disc; Nauny and a goddess stand at the left and Osiris presides enthroned at the right, wearing the atef crown and holding the crook and flail. Third Intermediate Period, Dynasty 21, about 1050 BCE; papyrus and paint. Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, Rogers Fund, 1930 (30.3.31).","credit":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, accession 30.3.31, public domain (CC0), https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/548344","alt":"A painted papyrus in two registers. Below, two women in long white robes face a tall balance; a kneeling figure with a jackal's head holds its plumb line, a small baboon perches on the crossbeam under a sun disc, and a seated god in a tall crown faces them from the right. Columns of hieroglyphs fill the spaces between the figures."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Book of the Dead not a book?","a":"Because it is a loose corpus of more than 190 separate spells from which each person took whatever selection was wanted, so no two manuscripts are alike."},{"q":"Why does the copy's quality vary so much?","a":"Because the choice, length and quality followed what its owner wanted and could pay for — a decorated papyrus is priced on one ostracon at about half a year's wage for a workman."},{"q":"Why is the English title misleading about what Egyptians called it?","a":"Because they called it the Spells for Going Forth by Day, meaning the soul leaving the tomb to travel with the sun god, where the modern title comes from a nineteenth-century German coinage."}]},
{"id":"wh-216","num":216,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had no founder and no scripture, its gods could merge freely, so the sun god joined to Amun became Amun-Re, the effective state god of the New Kingdom.","questions":["Ordinary worshippers in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rarely passed the outer courts, so devotion gathered at gateways and outer walls, where gods bore epithets promising a hearing to anyone who called.","The governing idea of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was maat, the right order of cosmos and society at once, personified as a goddess and set against isfet, its exact opposite."],"answer":"ancient Egyptian religion","answerText":"ancient Egyptian religion","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["practice","religion","history","egypt","mythology","bronze age"],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Amun's bark</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2025 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amun-Re</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Djême</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th – 8th century CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Ancient Egyptian religion</b> was polytheistic, a large and shifting company of gods worshipped along the Nile for more than three thousand years, and it had no founder, no creed and no scripture. Its governing idea was <i>maat</i>, the right order of the cosmos and of society at once, personified as a goddess and called the daughter of Re; its exact opposite was <i>isfet</i>, disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Maat present among people was held to guarantee the balance of the heavens as much as harmony on earth, and keeping her there was the king's charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gods were not exclusive and names merged freely: the sun god joined to Amun became Amun-Re, the effective state god of the New Kingdom,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> while Ptah, Sokar and Osiris fused into one mummiform god of resurrection. Re was god of the living and Osiris god of the dead, and each night the sun descended into the netherworld to unite with Osiris and be reborn at dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside a temple the daily ritual centred on a cult statue permanently installed in the rear of the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At festivals the god came out instead as a portable image carried in a bark, a rite attested at Thebes as early as the Eleventh Dynasty, when Amun began crossing the river to the west bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most Egyptians never went in, since admittance was strictly controlled and ordinary worshippers reached the interior only on festival days and only as far as the outer courts and hypostyle halls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Devotion therefore collected outside, at gateways, at shrines built against the outer walls, and around gods given epithets that promised a hearing, among them Amun-Who-Hears-the-Cry-of-Woe and Amun of Opet-Who-Answers-the-Poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Households kept their own dead close, with busts of ancestors set near the door and stelae naming a relative an 'excellent spirit of Re',<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and when Christianity spread the old sanctuaries were resettled rather than abandoned, a town growing inside the enclosure of the temple of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Bernadette Menu, “Maât, ordre social et inégalités dans l'Égypte ancienne,” <i>Droit et cultures</i> 69, no. 1 (2015): 51–73, https://doi.org/10.4000/droitcultures.3510. [Open access]","Foy Scalf, catalog entries, in <i>Book of the Dead: Becoming God in Ancient Egypt</i>, ed. Foy Scalf, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 39 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2017), 198, 348, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp39.pdf. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Egyptian Chronology,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2000), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/index.html. [Open access]","Martina Ullmann, “Thebes: Origins of a Ritual Landscape,” in <i>Sacred Space and Sacred Function in Ancient Thebes</i>, ed. Peter F. Dorman and Betsy M. Bryan, Studies in Ancient Oriental Civilization 61 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2007), 3–25, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/saoc61.pdf. [Open access]","Peter J. Brand, “Veils, Votives, and Marginalia: The Use of Sacred Space at Karnak and Luxor,” in <i>Sacred Space and Sacred Function in Ancient Thebes</i>, ed. Peter F. Dorman and Betsy M. Bryan, Studies in Ancient Oriental Civilization 61 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2007), 51–83, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/saoc61.pdf. [Open access]","Wolfram Grajetzki and Stephen Quirke, “Ancestor Busts,” <i>Digital Egypt for Universities</i> (London: Petrie Museum of Egyptian Archaeology, University College London, 2002), https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/religion/ancestor.html. [Open access]","Heike Behlmer, “Christian Use of Pharaonic Sacred Space in Western Thebes: The Case of TT 85 and 87,” in <i>Sacred Space and Sacred Function in Ancient Thebes</i>, ed. Peter F. Dorman and Betsy M. Bryan, Studies in Ancient Oriental Civilization 61 (Chicago: The Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2007), 163–75, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/saoc61.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Ear_Stela_of_Ramessumerysutekh_MET_LC-59_99_1_EGDP030127.jpg/1920px-Ear_Stela_of_Ramessumerysutekh_MET_LC-59_99_1_EGDP030127.jpg","title":"Ear stela of Ramessumerysutekh","desc":"A limestone votive stela carved with rows of human ears, dedicated by Ramessumerysutekh, ca. 1295–1186 BCE. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, accession 59.99.1. Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ear_Stela_of_Ramessumerysutekh_MET_LC-59_99_1_EGDP030127.jpg","alt":"A rectangular limestone slab carved in low relief with several rows of human ears above a line of hieroglyphs."},"why":[{"q":"Why did gods' names merge so freely?","a":"Because the gods were not exclusive: the sun god joined to Amun became Amun-Re, and Ptah, Sokar and Osiris fused into one mummiform god of resurrection."},{"q":"Why did most Egyptians never enter a temple?","a":"Because admittance was strictly controlled, and ordinary worshippers reached the interior only on festival days and only as far as the outer courts and hypostyle halls."},{"q":"Why did devotion collect outside the temple walls?","a":"Because that is where people could reach the god — at gateways and shrines built against the outer walls, around epithets like Amun-Who-Hears-the-Cry-of-Woe."}]},
{"id":"wh-217","num":217,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt spent roughly a century and a half after the Old Kingdom divided between rival houses at Herakleopolis and Thebes, a stretch called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ending with Mentuhotep II.","questions":["Middle Kingdom texts remember the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as famine and chaos, yet its pottery, provincial art and well-stocked cemeteries suggest that local life carried on much as before.","The dynasties later numbered seven to ten belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the seventy kings ruling seventy days that Manetho assigned them have no contemporary support."],"answer":"First Intermediate Period","answerText":"First Intermediate Period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2181 – 2055 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 to 11</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2055 BCE, under Mentuhotep II</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","egypt","archaeology","monarchy","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>First Intermediate Period</b> is the name Egyptologists give to the century and a half separating the Old Kingdom from the Middle Kingdom, dated on one common scheme to about 2181 to 2055 BCE and on another ending about 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its opening was a real break, since the weakness of the last Sixth Dynasty rulers left provincial governors free of Memphis, and those governors raised forces of their own and fought one another for territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rulers of the age are numbered in later kinglists as the seventh to eleventh dynasties, and much of that neatness is a fact about the lists rather than about Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Manetho's seventh dynasty is seventy kings ruling seventy days, for which no evidence exists at all and which may be a later literary metaphor for chaos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is likewise no contemporary support for dividing the ninth dynasty from the tenth, a split that may record nothing more than a list written across two columns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two rival houses did emerge: a Herakleopolitan line in the north, whose cemetery there stayed in use into the Middle Kingdom, and a Theban eleventh dynasty ruling only southern Egypt from about 2160 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Middle Kingdom writers remember the age as famine, poverty and collapsed order, and the Lament of Ipuwer, long read as a report of it, is now dated by its language to that later period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The material record disagrees: regional styles flourished in pottery, coffins and relief carving, provincial economies appear to have improved once free of central demands, and local rulers boasted of feeding the hungry and giving water to the thirsty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Coffin Texts appear now, funerary spells written inside private coffins that opened to ordinary Egyptians an afterlife the Pyramid Texts had reserved for kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether a drier climate and weaker Nile floods helped bring the Old Kingdom down is argued three ways, from no crisis to catastrophe, and the period ends when Mentuhotep II of Thebes reunited the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["“First Intermediate Period,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/first-intermediate-period. [Open access]","“Egypt in the First Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/1inter/index.html. [Open access]","“First Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/1stintermediateperkings.html. [Open access]","“Mentuhotep,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/mentuhotepII.html. [Open access]","Jean-Daniel Stanley and Sarah E. Wedl, “Significant Depositional Changes Offshore the Nile Delta in Late Third Millennium BCE: Relevance for Egyptology,” <i>E&amp;G Quaternary Science Journal</i> 70, no. 1 (2021): 83–92, https://doi.org/10.5194/egqsj-70-83-2021. [Open access]","“Egyptian Literary Compositions of the Middle Kingdom and Second Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/literature/midegsummaries.html. [Open access]","Carmen Pérez Die, “La nécropole de la Première Période Intermédiaire - début du Moyen Empire à Héracléopolis Magna,” in <i>Des Néferkarê aux Montouhotep</i>, TMO 40 (Lyon: Maison de l’Orient, 2005), 239–54, https://www.persee.fr/doc/mom_1955-4982_2005_act_40_1_2403. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Stela_of_the_mayor_Ituerneheh%2C_Egypt%2C_possibly_from_Naga-ed-Deir%2C_First_Intermediate_Period%2C_c._210-2040_BC%2C_painted_limestone_-_Harvard_Semitic_Museum_-_Cambridge%2C_MA_-_DSC06206.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Funerary stela of the mayor Ituerneheh","desc":"A painted limestone stela of the First Intermediate Period, probably from Naga ed-Deir, carved with the mayor Ituerneheh holding a staff, a smaller female figure beside him, and columns of hieroglyphs. Harvard Semitic Museum. Photograph by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_of_the_mayor_Ituerneheh,_Egypt,_possibly_from_Naga-ed-Deir,_First_Intermediate_Period,_c._210-2040_BC,_painted_limestone_-_Harvard_Semitic_Museum_-_Cambridge,_MA_-_DSC06206.jpg","alt":"A painted limestone slab carved in low relief with a standing man in a kilt holding a staff, a smaller woman at his side, and columns of hieroglyphs above and around them."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the dynastic numbering of this period a fact about the lists?","a":"Because Manetho's seventh dynasty is seventy kings ruling seventy days, for which no evidence exists, and the split between the ninth and tenth may record nothing more than a list written across two columns."},{"q":"Why does the material record disagree with how Egyptians remembered the age?","a":"Because regional styles flourished in pottery, coffins and relief carving, provincial economies appear to have improved once free of central demands, and local rulers boasted of feeding the hungry."},{"q":"Why can the Lament of Ipuwer no longer be read as a report of it?","a":"Because its language dates it to the Middle Kingdom, so it is a later writer's picture of collapse rather than a witness to this one."}]},
{"id":"wh-218","num":218,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Mentuhotep II of Thebes reunited Egypt to open the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose twelfth-dynasty kings founded Itjtawy, fortified Lower Nubia and left the literature that fixed classical Egyptian.","questions":["Fifteen fortresses rose along the Nile in Lower Nubia during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, built in about 32 years by kings from Senusret I to Senusret III to hold a new frontier.","Thebes and its god Amun rose from obscurity in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when a Theban house reunified Egypt and the oldest evidence for the temple at Karnak was laid down."],"answer":"Middle Kingdom","answerText":"Middle Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2055 BCE, under Mentuhotep II</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty 12</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1985 – 1795 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Senusret III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1874 – 1855 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th, 12th and 13th</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>Middle Kingdom</b> is the phase of ancient Egyptian history that opens with the reunification of the country under Mentuhotep II of Thebes and runs from the later eleventh dynasty through the twelfth into the thirteenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Egyptian ministry dates the reunification to about 2055 BCE and the twelfth dynasty to about 1985 to 1795 BCE, where University College London puts Mentuhotep II at 2046 to 1995 BCE and closes the dynasty with Sobekneferu about 1794 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king's Theban origins carried his city and its god Amun out of obscurity, and the oldest evidence for Karnak, later the wealthiest religious establishment in Egypt, belongs to this period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was buried across the river at Deir al-Bahari under a terraced monument cut partly into the cliff, which served as a partial model for the temple of Hatshepsut beside it five centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The age is a cultural high point above all for its writing, and the grammar of these compositions, called Middle Egyptian, remained the language of carved official inscriptions into the Roman period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Amenemhat I, who may have served Mentuhotep IV as vizier, founded a new royal residence called Itjtawy near modern Lisht, and its exact site has still not been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is named in three of the compositions the period is remembered for, the Tale of Sinuhe, the Teaching of Amenemhat and the Lament of Neferty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The twelfth dynasty drove the southern frontier into Lower Nubia, a region long worked for ebony, ivory, incense, gold and copper and read as a periphery held for its resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fifteen fortifications went up along the Nile there in about 32 years, from Senusret I to Senusret III, guarding the border, regulating the traders who passed and housing mining and military expeditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Senusret III fixed that frontier at the Second Cataract around Semna, and under him the provinces were brought down, the great rock-cut tomb chapels ceasing while the Fayum and Lisht cemeteries grew in scale and wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Middle Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/middle-kingdom. [Open access]","\"Kings of the Middle Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/middlekingdom.html. [Open access]","\"Amenemhat (I) Sehetepibre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/amenemhatI.html. [Open access]","Eduardo Ferreira, \"The Lower Nubian Egyptian Fortresses in the Middle Kingdom: A Strategic Point of View,\" <i>Athens Journal of History</i> 5, no. 1 (2019): 31–52, https://doi.org/10.30958/ajhis.5-1-2. [Open access]","\"Senusret (III) Khakaure,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/senusretIII.html. [Open access]","Roxana Flammini, \"Ancient Core-Periphery Interactions: Lower Nubia During Middle Kingdom Egypt (ca. 2050–1640 B.C.),\" <i>Journal of World-Systems Research</i> 14, no. 1 (2008): 50–74, https://doi.org/10.5195/jwsr.2008.348. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Mentuhotep_II_Deir_el-Bahari.JPG/1920px-Mentuhotep_II_Deir_el-Bahari.JPG","title":"The mortuary temple of Mentuhotep II at Deir el-Bahari","desc":"The terraced mortuary temple of Mentuhotep II at Deir el-Bahari, in the Theban necropolis, built partly against the cliff behind it. Rémih, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mentuhotep_II_Deir_el-Bahari.JPG","alt":"Ruins of a stone temple terrace on the desert floor beneath a high limestone cliff, with a line of column stumps standing on the platform and a colonnade running along its left side."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Thebes and its god rise with this dynasty?","a":"Because the king's Theban origins carried the city and Amun out of obscurity, and the oldest evidence for Karnak, later the wealthiest religious establishment in Egypt, belongs to this period."},{"q":"Why did the twelfth dynasty fortify Lower Nubia so heavily?","a":"Because the region was long worked for ebony, ivory, incense, gold and copper, and fifteen fortifications went up in about 32 years to guard the border, regulate traders and house mining and military expeditions."},{"q":"Why does the period's writing outlast the period?","a":"Because the grammar of its compositions, called Middle Egyptian, remained the language of carved official inscriptions into the Roman period."}]},
{"id":"wh-219","num":219,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Levantine settlers in the eastern Delta gave Egypt its fifteenth dynasty, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Greek name renders an Egyptian title meaning ruler of foreign lands.","questions":["Teeth from the Avaris cemeteries carry more incomers before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took power than during their rule, which fits a Delta-born elite rather than an invading army.","Ahmose sacked Avaris and drove the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out to open the eighteenth dynasty, and the chariot and the composite bow entered Egypt during their century in the north."],"answer":"Hyksos","answerText":"Hyksos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1638 – 1530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15th, ruling from Avaris</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expelled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Ahmose, c. 1550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Second Intermediate, c. 1700 – 1550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Hyksos</b> is the Greek form of the Egyptian title <i>heqa khasut</i>, ruler of foreign lands, and it names the fifteenth dynasty, which held northern Egypt from about 1638 to 1530 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Levantine migrants had been settling the north-eastern Delta since the twelfth dynasty, and a contemporary Egyptian text gives one of their leaders that title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How they came to power is not known; the weakening thirteenth dynasty could not prevent it and moved its capital from Itjtawy to Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From Avaris, the modern Tell al-Dab'a, they held the country as far south as al-Qusiya in Middle Egypt, Memphis included, while Nubian Kerma rose in the south and Egyptian kings at Thebes held the stretch between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their material culture is a variant of the Levantine Middle Bronze Age, seen at Avaris in pottery, weaponry, house plans and tombs placed inside living areas, and the composite bow and the horse and chariot entered Egypt in these years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The invasion story comes from Manetho, a priest writing twelve centuries afterwards whose account survives only in quotations by later historians such as Josephus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Reference works still carry it: University College London's Digital Egypt says they invaded at some unknown point, while noting that Egyptology agrees neither on the length of the Second Intermediate Period nor on the number and sequence of its kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strontium isotope ratios in 75 tooth samples from the Avaris cemeteries found more incomers before their rule than during it, which fits a ruling class of Near Eastern descent born and raised in the Delta rather than an army from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same study found far more non-local women than men, suggesting men stayed where they were born and wives came from elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Theban kings of the seventeenth dynasty fought back and Ahmose sacked Avaris to open the eighteenth dynasty, though not even the year of reunification is known; Seqenenre Taa seems to have died campaigning, but the Levantine axes read from his wounds were types Egyptian forces also carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["“Second Intermediate Period,” Discover Egypt’s Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/second-intermediate-period. [Open access]","“Egypt in the Second Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/2inter/index.html. [Open access]","“Kings of the Second Intermediate Period,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/2interkings/index.html. [Open access]","“Ahmose,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ahmose.html. [Open access]","Chris Stantis et al., “Who Were the Hyksos? Challenging Traditional Narratives Using Strontium Isotope (87Sr/86Sr) Analysis of Human Remains from Ancient Egypt,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 7 (2020): e0235414, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0235414. [Open access]","Uroš Matić, “Ethnicity of the Hyksos Forces and the Death of Pharaoh Seqenenre Tao,” <i>Etnoantropološki problemi</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): 821–49, https://doi.org/10.21301/eap.v19i3.5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/Scarab_with_the_Name_of_the_Hyksos_King_Khayan_MET_D_457_bottom.jpg","title":"Seal ring of the Hyksos king Khyan","desc":"The engraved underside of a glazed scarab naming the Hyksos king Khyan, mounted as a swivel ring in a gold bezel, in the Metropolitan Museum of Art. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scarab_with_the_Name_of_the_Hyksos_King_Khayan_MET_D_457_bottom.jpg","alt":"The oval underside of a pale green scarab set in a gold ring bezel, cut with a royal cartouche between scroll patterns, with an ankh sign below"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the invasion story a weak basis for the dynasty's origins?","a":"Because it comes from Manetho, a priest writing twelve centuries afterwards, whose account survives only in quotations by later historians such as Josephus."},{"q":"Why do the Avaris teeth argue against an army from outside?","a":"Because strontium ratios in 75 samples found more incomers before their rule than during it, which fits a ruling class of Near Eastern descent born and raised in the Delta."},{"q":"Why can the wounds of Seqenenre Taa not settle who killed him?","a":"Because the Levantine axes read from them were types Egyptian forces also carried."}]},
{"id":"wh-220","num":220,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt reached the Fourth Cataract and Syria during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the period of the eighteenth to twentieth dynasties whose kings abandoned the pyramid for rock-cut tombs at Thebes.","questions":["Two viziers served at once, at Thebes and Memphis, during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when a viceroy of Kush governed Nubia and was among the most important officials in the kingdom.","Akhenaten's turn to the Aten and the long reign of Ramesses II both fall within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began about 1550 BCE with the expulsion of the Hyksos and ended about 1069 BCE."],"answer":"New Kingdom","answerText":"New Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th, 19th and 20th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ahmose</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1525 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramesses II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1279 – 1213 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>New Kingdom</b> is the phase of ancient Egyptian history running from about 1550 to 1069 BCE, covering the eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth dynasties, when Egypt was the leading power of the ancient Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It opens with Ahmose, whose reign University College London dates to about 1550 to 1525 BCE, and ends with a country that had weakened steadily until it split in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its kings carried the frontier south to the Fourth Cataract in Nubia and north into what is now Syria, and the wealth that came back paid for building across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thebes was never the administrative capital, but from this period it ranked first in religious terms, and the temple of its god Amun at Karnak became one of the most important in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its kings were buried there as well, giving up the pyramid for rock-cut tombs in the Valley of the Kings on the west bank of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Government kept broadly the shape it had held in the Middle Kingdom, headed by the vizier, though from the early eighteenth dynasty two are attested, one residing at Thebes and one at Memphis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nubia was administered separately by the viceroy of Kush, an office created at the start of that dynasty and among the most important in the kingdom because the gold mines lay in his charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Akhenaten broke the pattern by proclaiming a single god in the sun-disk Aten and remaking royal ideology, art, architecture and language with it, and the Levantine territories were lost in his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sety I recovered much of that ground, Ramesses II fought the Hittites and then made peace with them, and Merenptah beat off a large attack by the raiders Egyptian sources call the Sea Peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The record that survives is overwhelmingly monumental, so a 2024 lead-isotope study of kohl from Nubian graves, some of New Kingdom date, adds evidence of another kind by tracing part of it to the Egyptian mining site of Gebel el-Zeit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["\"New Kingdom (about 1550-1069 BC),\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nk/index.html. [Open access]","\"Kings of the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/newkingdomkings.html. [Open access]","\"Thebes,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/thebes/index.html. [Open access]","\"Central Administration in the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/administration/nkcentral.html. [Open access]","\"The viceroy of Kush,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/viceroy.html. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","Rennan Lemos et al., \"Bronze Age Supply Chains between Ancient Egypt and Nubia Revealed by Lead Isotope Analysis of Kohl Samples,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 27633, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-79162-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Templo_de_Karnak%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_142.jpg/1920px-Templo_de_Karnak%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_142.jpg","title":"The temple of Amun at Karnak","desc":"Standing columns and a ruined pylon in the temple of Amun at Karnak, Luxor, the Theban sanctuary the kings of the New Kingdom built up above all others. Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Templo_de_Karnak,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_142.jpg","alt":"A wide paved court with two tall standing stone columns, a ruined pylon of carved blocks behind them, palm trees at the sides and fallen masonry on the ground"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the viceroy of Kush among the most important offices?","a":"Because the gold mines lay in his charge, and Nubia was administered separately from the rest of the country."},{"q":"Why did the kings give up the pyramid?","a":"Because they were buried at Thebes instead, in rock-cut tombs in the Valley of the Kings on the west bank of the river."},{"q":"Why does a study of kohl add evidence of another kind?","a":"Because the surviving record is overwhelmingly monumental, and lead isotopes traced part of the kohl in Nubian graves to the Egyptian mining site of Gebel el-Zeit."}]},
{"id":"wh-221","num":221,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Taking the throne name Maatkare and a full royal titulary, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled Egypt as king beside her young stepson Thutmose III for 21 years in the 15th century BCE.","questions":["The terraced mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari carries reliefs in which the god Amun fathers <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an argument in stone that made her kingship a matter of descent.","After her death the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut from monument after monument and left out of the king lists, surviving in Manetho's later summary only as a queen called Amenses."],"answer":"Hatshepsut","answerText":"Hatshepsut","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1479 – 1458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Co-rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 years with Thutmose III</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thutmose III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to c. 1425 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","archaeology","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Hatshepsut</b> was a queen of the eighteenth dynasty who took the full titles of a king and ruled Egypt in her own right for about twenty years in the 15th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was a daughter of Thutmose I and a wife of Thutmose II, his son by another marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Thutmose II died his heir, Thutmose III, was still a young boy by another wife, and Hatshepsut seems to have taken over the business of government on his behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within a few years she had assumed a royal titulary of her own, taking the throne name Maatkare, and the two ruled as kings together for 21 years until her death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her reign shaped how the office itself was understood, working out an ideology of kingship bound to the god Amun that defined the role for the rest of the New Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Her claim to the throne was argued in stone rather than simply asserted, and the argument survives on the walls of her mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari, the terraced building called Djeser-djeseru, the Holy of Holies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One set of reliefs has her father Thutmose I naming her his heir; another makes the god Amun her true father, a divine-birth sequence turning her kingship into a matter of descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other walls record a seaborne expedition to Punt, somewhere towards the southern end of the Red Sea, whose people, houses, trees and animals are drawn in unusual detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the rock-cut chapel of Speos Artemidos in Middle Egypt she went further, presenting herself as the ruler who had set Egypt in order after the disorder of the Hyksos, who had in fact been expelled a century before her reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After her death her name and her image were cut from monument after monument and left out of the later king lists, so that she reaches Manetho's summary twelve centuries on only as a queen called Amenses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["\"Hatshepsut,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/hatshepsut.html. [Open access]","\"Thutmose III,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/thutmosisiii.html. [Open access]","\"Speos Artemidos,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/temple/speos.html. [Open access]","\"Hatshepsut Temple,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/hatshepsut-temple. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Templo_funerario_de_Hatshepsut%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_13.jpg/1920px-Templo_funerario_de_Hatshepsut%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_13.jpg","title":"The mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari","desc":"Aerial view at sunrise of the mortuary temple of Hatshepsut at Deir el-Bahari, on the west bank at Luxor. Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Templo_funerario_de_Hatshepsut,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_13.jpg","alt":"An aerial view at sunrise of a three-terraced stone temple with long rows of square columns, set against a high desert cliff."},"why":[{"q":"Why was her claim to the throne argued in stone?","a":"Because it had to be made rather than assumed: reliefs at Deir el-Bahari have Thutmose I name her his heir, and a divine-birth sequence makes Amun her true father."},{"q":"Why is her Speos Artemidos claim historically false?","a":"Because she presents herself as the ruler who set Egypt in order after the Hyksos, who had in fact been expelled a century before her reign."},{"q":"Why does she reach Manetho only as a queen called Amenses?","a":"Because after her death her name and image were cut from monument after monument and left out of the later king lists."}]},
{"id":"wh-222","num":222,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Taking the throne name Menkheperre and leading at least 17 campaigns into Syria and Palestine, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pushed Egypt's border to the Euphrates and had the record cut into Karnak.","questions":["The Annals cut into the Amun temple at Karnak count the prisoners and tribute brought back from the campaigns of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who ruled beside Hatshepsut for 21 years before ruling alone.","Buried in tomb 34 in the Valley of the Kings, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built the festival hall at Karnak and a temple of millions of years called Henket-ankh on the Theban west bank."],"answer":"Thutmose III","answerText":"Thutmose III","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1479 – 1425 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sole rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaigns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 17, in years 23 to 42</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","warfare","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Thutmose III</b> was a king of the eighteenth dynasty who reigned from about 1479 to 1425 BCE, one of the longest reigns in Egyptian history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne as a young boy, so his aunt Hatshepsut took a royal titulary of her own and ruled beside him for 21 years; his sole rule began at her death and ran for more than thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In those years he led at least 17 campaigns into Syria and Palestine, reached the Euphrates, and carried Egypt's border to its furthest extent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The kings of his dynasty were pressing into a region of small city-states, none with the resources to resist them, though farther north the Mitanni and Hittite empires blocked any advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He built or rebuilt temples the length of the Nile valley and into Nubia, left as deep a mark on the Amun temple at Karnak as Hatshepsut had, and was buried in the Valley of the Kings in tomb 34.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The record of the campaigns was cut into the walls at Karnak as the Annals, and it is a ledger rather than a narrative, an enumeration built around the capture and quantity of prisoners and tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Counted up, it records at least 7,942 foreigners brought into Egypt across the twelve campaign years it covers, from year 23 to year 42.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About two thirds are servants received as tribute or as the contribution owed by Nubia rather than prisoners taken in battle, and the famous first campaign, the siege of Megiddo, yields 340 captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the fighting won was not a province either: the city-states remained loose vassals who paid occasional tribute and were given no Egyptian administration of the kind installed in Nubia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the west bank at Thebes he raised a temple of millions of years of his own, the Henket-ankh, 'the temple which endows Menkheperre with life', built on three levels where the cultivated land met the desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Thutmose III,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/thutmosisiii.html. [Open access]","\"Asia and Egypt,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foreignrelations/asiank.html. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","H&eacute;ctor Horacio Gerv&aacute;n, \"Introducci&oacute;n e integraci&oacute;n de extranjeros en el Egipto del Reino Nuevo: una aproximaci&oacute;n cuantitativa a los Anales de Tuthmosis III,\" <i>Anuario de la Escuela de Historia Virtual</i> 5, no. 5 (2014): 1&ndash;22, https://doi.org/10.31049/1853.7049.v0.n5.9364. [Open access]","Myriam Seco &Aacute;lvarez and Javier Mart&iacute;nez Bab&oacute;n, \"The Temple of Millions of Years of the Pharaoh Thutmose III (Luxor): An Update on the Research,\" <i>&Eacute;tudes et Travaux</i> 33 (2021): 179&ndash;220, https://doi.org/10.12775/EtudTrav.33.011. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Egypt-3B-023_-_Festival_Hall_%282217355364%29.jpg/1920px-Egypt-3B-023_-_Festival_Hall_%282217355364%29.jpg","title":"The festival hall of Thutmose III at Karnak","desc":"Akh-Menu of Tuthmosis III, the festival hall, Karnak Temple, Luxor, Egypt. Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt-3B-023_-_Festival_Hall_(2217355364).jpg","alt":"A long pillared hall of pale sandstone open to the sky, a row of square clerestory windows above its columns and fallen blocks on the gravel in front of it."},"why":[{"q":"Why are the Annals at Karnak a ledger rather than a narrative?","a":"Because they are built as an enumeration around the capture and quantity of prisoners and tribute, recording at least 7,942 foreigners brought into Egypt across twelve campaign years."},{"q":"Why did the campaigns not produce a province?","a":"Because the Syrian and Palestinian city-states remained loose vassals paying occasional tribute, with no Egyptian administration of the kind installed in Nubia."},{"q":"Why could Egypt press so far into that region at all?","a":"Because it was a country of small city-states, none with the resources to resist — while farther north the Mitanni and Hittite empires blocked any advance."}]},
{"id":"rm-047","num":"47","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The roads from the north and east came down to the Tiber crossing through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where they met the road running under the Capitol out to the Porta Trigemina.","questions":["Livy says the one foreign rite Romulus took over was the worship of Hercules at an altar in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, kept by two families until it passed to public slaves.","In the panic after Cannae a Gaulish and a Greek couple were buried alive beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in a stone vault Livy says had held human victims once before."],"answer":"Forum Boarium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First temples</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">213 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">212 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Forum Boarium</b> was the cattle market of ancient Rome, the low ground between the Palatine, the Capitoline and the Aventine, reaching from the Velabrum down to the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a crossing before it was a market: the routes from the north and east came down the Vicus Iugarius and the Vicus Tuscus to the Tiber at the Pons Sublicius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There they met the road running from the Campus Martius between the Capitol and the river to the Porta Trigemina, and the road down the Circus Maximus valley from the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A bronze bull said to have been brought from Aegina stood in it, and some ancient authorities derived the name from that rather than from the trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The space was public property and hard to keep clear: buildings crowded in until the name covered a whole district, and boundary stones under Tiberius and Claudius fixed its eastern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It held the Ara Maxima, the earliest centre of the cult of Hercules at Rome, which tradition placed where he killed the robber Cacus for stealing part of Geryon's herd and dragging the beasts backwards by the tail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy says this was the only foreign rite Romulus adopted, performed by Greek custom as Evander had instituted it and kept by the Potitii and Pinarii until it passed to public slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius writes that the Romans call it the Greatest Altar, hold none in greater veneration, and swear oaths and pay tithes on it to make a bargain unalterable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The twin temples of Fortuna and Mater Matuta stood at the northern edge, credited by tradition to Servius Tullius, burnt in 213 BCE and rebuilt the year after, on ground where monumental temple building had begun between about 650 and 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The market was also a place of dread: in the panic after Cannae a Gaulish and a Greek couple were buried alive beneath it, in a stone vault Livy says had held human victims once before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Forum Boarium","tags":["place","geography","history","italy","religion","iron age"],"sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Forum Boarium,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 224–25, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Forum_Boarium.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Herculis Invicti Ara Maxima,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 253–54, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Ara_Maxima_Herculis.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.39–40, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1B*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.56–57, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0144%3Abook%3D22%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","\"Fortuna et Mater Matuta, Aedes,\" in <i>Digital Augustan Rome</i>, ed. David Gilman Romano and Nicholas L. Stapp (Tucson: University of Arizona, 2016), https://www.digitalaugustanrome.org/records/fortuna-et-mater-matuta-aedes/. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Franz_Alt_Rom_Forum_Boarium_1859.jpg/1920px-Franz_Alt_Rom_Forum_Boarium_1859.jpg","title":"Forum Boarium","desc":"Franz Alt, Rom, Forum Boarium, 1859, with cattle and an ox-cart in the square before the round temple. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Alt_Rom_Forum_Boarium_1859.jpg","alt":"A watercolour of a Roman square with a round colonnaded temple on the right, a church and tall bell tower behind, and an ox-cart, cattle and townspeople in the foreground"},"locator":{"name":"Forum Boarium","at":[12.4811,41.8889],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was this ground a market at all?","a":"Because it was a crossing first: the routes from north and east came down to the Tiber there, meeting the road from the Campus Martius and the one down the Circus Maximus valley."},{"q":"Why did the name come to cover a whole district?","a":"Because the space was public property and hard to keep clear, buildings crowding in until boundary stones under Tiberius and Claudius had to fix its eastern edge."},{"q":"Why did Rome keep a Greek rite here when it kept no other?","a":"Because Livy says the cult of Hercules at the Ara Maxima was the only foreign rite Romulus adopted, performed by Greek custom as Evander had instituted it."}]},
{"id":"rm-051","num":"51","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Dionysius says the Romans repaired the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whenever storm or age damaged it, adding nothing to make it grander, so that it stayed the hut it had been.","questions":["Vitruvius points to the thatched roof of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as plain evidence of how simply the ancients had lived, its straw kept up beside the marble of the empire.","Crows dropped burning meat from an altar onto the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 12 BCE, and the fire was counted among the portents attending the death of Agrippa."],"answer":"casa Romuli","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hilltop huts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>casa Romuli</b> was a hut of straw with a thatched roof standing at the south-west corner of the Palatine, near the top of the Scalae Caci, which the Romans kept as the house of Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius says the twins were reared as herdsmen and lived on the hills in huts they built themselves, roofs and all, out of sticks and reeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of these, he writes, still stood in his own day on the flank of the Palatine that faces the Circus, and was held sacred by those who had charge of such things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they did with it is the point: nothing was added to make it grander, and whenever storm or age damaged it they repaired it back to what it had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vitruvius makes the same use of it, citing a thatched roof on a house of Romulus as plain evidence of how simply the ancients had lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A second casa Romuli, probably a copy, stood on the Capitoline, and it is that one Vitruvius names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hut burned at least twice and was rebuilt each time, once when a rite the pontifices were performing inside it got out of hand,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and again in 12 BCE when crows dropped burning meat on it from an altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Neither fire was treated as an accident: the first was counted among the portents that sent the Romans to the Sibylline books, the second among those that attended the death of Agrippa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The hut was still on record in the 4th century CE, more than a thousand years after the settlement it stood for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the memory lies something real, since the Palatine did carry huts whose traces survive, and the hilltop settlements of Rome grew together into one place across the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"casa Romuli","tags":["building","history","religion","italy","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Casa Romuli,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 101–2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Casa_Romuli.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.79, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>The Architecture of Marcus Vitruvius Pollio</i> 2.1.5, trans. Joseph Gwilt (London: Priestley and Weale, 1826), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Vitruvius/2*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 48.43, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/48*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 54.29, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/54*.html. [Open access]","Dominik Maschek, review of <i>The Genesis of Roman Architecture</i>, by John North Hopkins, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2016.10.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2016/2016.10.30/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Germalus_Huts_Model_%2849351033787%29.jpg/1920px-Germalus_Huts_Model_%2849351033787%29.jpg","title":"casa Romuli","desc":"A reconstruction model of the huts of the Germalus, one of the Palatine's two summits, made by Alberto Davico in 1950 and labelled as a model in the display. Jamie Heath, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germalus_Huts_Model_%2849351033787%29.jpg","alt":"A museum model of a hillside village of small oval thatched huts, with the outlines of their floors cut into the rock around them"},"locator":{"name":"casa Romuli","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the hut never improved?","a":"Because whenever storm or age damaged it those in charge repaired it back to what it had been, nothing being added to make it grander."},{"q":"Why were its fires not treated as accidents?","a":"Because the first was counted among the portents that sent the Romans to the Sibylline books, and the second among those attending the death of Agrippa."},{"q":"Why is there something real behind the memory?","a":"Because the Palatine did carry huts whose traces survive, and the hilltop settlements of Rome grew together into one place across the 8th and 7th centuries BCE."}]},
{"id":"rm-052","num":"52","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Plutarch says men were fetched from Tuscany to prescribe every detail of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began with a trench of first-fruits and a furrow ploughed round the boundary.","questions":["Timaeus put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 813 BCE, Cincius at 728, Fabius Pictor at 747 and Cato at 751, so the Romans never agreed on the year.","The day chosen for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was 21 April, kept as the country's birthday, though a shepherds' festival called the Parilia had been held on it long before."],"answer":"founding of Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parilia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City forms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>founding of Rome</b> is the act the Romans put at the head of their own history, fixed to a single day and carried out, they said, by a rite learned from Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has Romulus send to Tuscany for men who prescribed every detail of it in accordance with sacred writings, and taught it to him as a religious rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A circular trench was dug and the first-fruits of everything good and necessary were laid in it, and every man threw in a handful of soil from the country he had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They called the trench <i>mundus</i>, the word they also used for the heavens, and took it as the centre from which the city was marked out in a circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The founder shod a plough with a bronze share, yoked a bull and a cow, and drove a deep furrow round the boundary with every clod turned inwards; where a gate was to stand the share was lifted out and a gap left, so the wall was sacred and the gates were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The day was 21 April, kept as the birthday of the country, though a pastoral festival called the Parilia had been held on it before there was a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Velleius puts the founding on the Palatine on that festival in the sixth Olympiad, which is 753 BCE by the reckoning of Varro that became standard and 751 by Cato's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The year was never settled among the Romans themselves: Timaeus gave 813 BCE, Cincius 728, Fabius Pictor 747, Cato 751 and Polybius 750.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy's account of the day is a quarrel rather than a ceremony, with the brothers taking auguries on separate hills, disputing which sign counted, and Remus killed for leaping the new wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modern work on early Rome reaches the opposite conclusion from the tradition, finding people living on the site before the 8th century BCE and no single state there until the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"founding of Rome","tags":["event","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 11–12, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.8, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.74, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Gary Forsythe, review of <i>The Beginnings of Rome: Italy and Rome from the Bronze Age to the Punic Wars (c. 1000–264 BC)</i>, by Tim Cornell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.03.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.03.26/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Fregio_architettonico_col_solco_primario_della_colonia_aquileiese%2C_I_sec._dc.jpg/1920px-Fregio_architettonico_col_solco_primario_della_colonia_aquileiese%2C_I_sec._dc.jpg","title":"founding of Rome","desc":"A Roman relief of the first furrow being ploughed at the founding of the colony of Aquileia, 1st century CE, the veiled official guiding a plough drawn by a bull and a cow. Sailko, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fregio_architettonico_col_solco_primario_della_colonia_aquileiese,_I_sec._dc.jpg","alt":"A marble relief in which a yoked bull and cow draw a plough guided by a man with his toga drawn over his head, with a group of togate men following behind"},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why were the gates of the new city not sacred?","a":"Because the share was lifted out of the furrow where a gate was to stand, so the wall was consecrated by the plough and the gaps were not."},{"q":"Why was the year of the founding never settled among the Romans?","a":"Because Timaeus gave 813 BCE, Cincius 728, Fabius Pictor 747, Cato 751 and Polybius 750, with Varro's 753 becoming standard only later."},{"q":"Why does modern work reach the opposite conclusion from the tradition?","a":"Because it finds people living on the site before the 8th century BCE and no single state there until the 7th, where the tradition fixes one act to one day."}]},
{"id":"rm-053","num":"53","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy reports that some writers thought <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were suckled not by a wolf but by Larentia, whom the shepherds nicknamed 'she-wolf' for her unchaste life.","questions":["Being twins with no claim to precedence from seniority, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the naming of their city to augury, one watching from the Palatine and the other from the Aventine.","The curule aediles Cnaeus and Quintus Ogulnius set a group of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as infants suckled by the she-wolf beside the Ficus Ruminalis, long before Livy wrote."],"answer":"Romulus and Remus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wolf group</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the 290s BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Romulus and Remus</b> are the twin brothers Roman tradition made the founders of the city, born to a Vestal at Alba Longa who named Mars as their father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the king order them thrown into the Tiber, but the river was over its banks, so the men left the cradle in the standing water, and the flood as it fell set it down on dry ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A thirsty she-wolf came down from the hills to the crying and gave the babies her teats, and the king's flock-master Faustulus found her licking them and carried them home to his wife Larentia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy passes on the deflating version in the same breath: some writers held that Larentia was nicknamed 'she-wolf' among the shepherds for her unchaste life, and that this was where the marvel came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition was never one story: Plutarch opens his life of Romulus with rival accounts of who founded Rome and whose son Romulus was,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Dionysius reports a version in which Remus was seized during the Lupercalia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Grown, the brothers put their grandfather Numitor back on the throne at Alba and then set out to build a city where they had been exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Being twins, with no claim to precedence from seniority, they put it to the gods of the place by augury, Romulus watching from the Palatine and Remus from the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The signs were disputed, the quarrel turned violent, and Remus was killed for leaping the new wall, which left Rome with a fratricide at the head of its own foundation story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story was public and official long before Livy wrote it down: the curule aediles Cnaeus and Quintus Ogulnius set a group of the founders as infants suckled by the wolf beside the Ficus Ruminalis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius could still see a bronze wolf and two infants of ancient workmanship beside the Lupercal,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and modern historians weigh the cycle element by element as a tradition rather than a record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Romulus and Remus","tags":["person","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.4, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.6–7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.79–80, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 1–2, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Gary Forsythe, review of <i>The Beginnings of Rome: Italy and Rome from the Bronze Age to the Punic Wars (c. 1000–264 BC)</i>, by Tim Cornell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 1997.03.26, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/1997/1997.03.26/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Campana_relief_with_a_scene_depicting_the_moment_when_the_shepherd_Faustulus_finds_the_she-wolf_with_the_twins_Romulus_and_Remus%2C_2nd_century_AD%2C_Pergamon_Museum%2C_Berlin_%2849808961057%29.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Romulus and Remus","desc":"A Roman terracotta relief of the shepherd Faustulus finding the she-wolf with the twins, 2nd century CE, Pergamon Museum, Berlin. Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campana_relief_with_a_scene_depicting_the_moment_when_the_shepherd_Faustulus_finds_the_she-wolf_with_the_twins_Romulus_and_Remus,_2nd_century_AD,_Pergamon_Museum,_Berlin_%2849808961057%29.jpg","alt":"A terracotta relief in which a she-wolf stands beneath a rocky overhang suckling two infants, while a shepherd in a broad hat raises his hand in surprise at the right"},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy report a deflating version beside the marvel?","a":"Because some writers held that Larentia was nicknamed 'she-wolf' among the shepherds for her unchaste life, and that this was where the wonder came from."},{"q":"Why did the brothers have to consult the gods about the site?","a":"Because being twins they had no claim to precedence from seniority, so the question was put to the gods of the place by augury."},{"q":"Why does the story leave the city with an awkward inheritance?","a":"Because the signs were disputed, the quarrel turned violent, and Remus was killed for leaping the new wall, so a fratricide stands at the head of the foundation."}]},
{"id":"rm-054","num":"54","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The twins beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not part of the same work, having been added in the Renaissance and traditionally given to Antonio del Pollaiolo.","questions":["Restoration showed that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been cast in a single piece, a technique typical of the Middle Ages, and radiocarbon analysis in 2007 gave a medieval date.","A medieval date for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> severs it from the ancient she-wolves that Cicero, Sallust and Livy describe, though its traditional attribution was Etruscan."],"answer":"Capitoline Wolf","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Capitoline Wolf</b> is the bronze she-wolf in the Palazzo dei Conservatori in Rome, about 75 centimetres (30 inches) high, her head turned aside and her dugs heavy with milk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The twins beneath her are not part of the same work: they were added in the Renaissance and are traditionally given to the sculptor Antonio del Pollaiolo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient Rome certainly had statues of the subject, since the curule aediles Cnaeus and Quintus Ogulnius set a group of the founders as infants suckled by the wolf beside the Ficus Ruminalis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius describes another beside the Lupercal on the Palatine, a she-wolf suckling two infants, the figures in bronze and of ancient workmanship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The traditional attribution put the Conservatori bronze in an Etruscan workshop of the 5th century BCE, which made it one of those ancient statues rather than a copy of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That attribution was overturned from the workshop rather than the library: the restoration of the statue showed it had been cast in a single piece, a technique typical of the Middle Ages,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and radiocarbon analysis in 2007 gave a medieval date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the redating costs is the link to the ancient texts, since a medieval bronze cannot be any of the she-wolves that Cicero, Sallust and Livy describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is not a settled question: the statue was never unanimously accepted as an early or middle Republican masterpiece, and dating proposals are still being made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The uncertainty is about the object rather than the image, whose earliest forms are on coins showing the twins at the wolf, and which the imperial age set beside the meeting of Mars and Rhea Silvia as an emblem of the foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The motif is not only Roman: an infant abandoned to nature and saved by an animal recurs in other ancient traditions and is tied to the idea of kingship, and the wolf and twins are still a symbol of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Capitoline Wolf","tags":["object","art","history","italy","archaeology"],"sources":["“Capitoline She-wolf,” Museo Tattile Statale Omero, Ancona, https://museoomero.it/en/opere/capitoline-she-wolf/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.79, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Genevieve Gessert, review of <i>She-Wolf: The Story of a Roman Icon</i>, by Cristina Mazzoni, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2011.02.09, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2011/2011.02.09. [Open access]","Eric M. Moormann, review of <i>Pseudoantike Skulptur II: Klassizistische Statuen aus antiker und nachantiker Zeit</i>, by Sascha Kansteiner, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2017.08.19, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2017/2017.08.19/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Capitoline_she-wolf_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome%2C_Italy_-_DSC05797.jpg/1920px-Capitoline_she-wolf_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome%2C_Italy_-_DSC05797.jpg","title":"Capitoline Wolf","desc":"The Capitoline Wolf in the Palazzo dei Conservatori, Rome, with the Renaissance twins beneath her. Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capitoline_she-wolf_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome%2C_Italy_-_DSC05797.jpg","alt":"A bronze she-wolf standing with her head turned to the viewer's left, mouth open, with two bronze infant boys kneeling beneath her to suckle"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the bronze no longer read as an ancient statue?","a":"Because restoration showed it had been cast in a single piece, a technique typical of the Middle Ages, and radiocarbon analysis in 2007 gave a medieval date."},{"q":"Why does that redating cost more than a date?","a":"Because a medieval bronze cannot be any of the she-wolves that Cicero, Sallust and Livy describe, so the link to the ancient texts goes with it."},{"q":"Why is the uncertainty about the object rather than the image?","a":"Because the motif is attested on coins showing the twins at the wolf and was set beside the meeting of Mars and Rhea Silvia as an emblem of the foundation."}]},
{"id":"rm-055","num":"55","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Caesar told the crowd at his aunt's funeral that his family descended from Venus through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, giving his stock the sanctity of kings and the reverence owed to gods.","questions":["Livy gives two versions of the meeting between Latinus and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one a battle followed by peace, the other a parley in which the king simply gave his hand on it.","Alba Longa was built to bridge the four centuries between the fall of Troy, which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have escaped, and the founding of Rome in 753 BCE."],"answer":"Aeneas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Troy falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1183 BCE, on one reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alba founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 years after Lavinium</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Aeneas</b> is the Trojan whom Roman tradition made the ancestor of the Romans, spared at the sack of Troy along with Antenor because both had argued for peace and the surrender of Helen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy takes him through Macedonia and Sicily to the Laurentian coast, where the Trojans landed with nothing left but their arms and their ships and began to plunder the neighbourhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition forks there: in one version Latinus was beaten in battle and then made peace, in the other he stepped out in front of his lines, asked the strangers who they were, and gave his hand on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Either way he gave Aeneas his daughter Lavinia, and the town Aeneas built took her name; war with Turnus of the Rutulians and with Mezentius of Caere followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aeneas called Trojans and Aborigines alike by the single name Latins, won his last battle, and was buried by the river Numicius, where he was addressed as Jupiter Indiges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legend's real work was to bridge four centuries, Troy having fallen in 1183 BCE on the reckoning that became standard, and Rome having been founded in 753.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Alba Longa filled the gap: Livy has Ascanius leave Lavinium to his mother and build Alba thirty years later, and the Silvian kings run from him down to Numitor and Amulius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It anchored a cult as well, since Dionysius reports that the Penates brought from Troy kept returning to Lavinium when they were moved to Alba, so six hundred men were sent back to keep their rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It anchored a family too: Livy notes that the Julian house claimed Iulus as the founder of their name, and Dionysius says his descendants held a priestly prerogative down to his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Julius Caesar used the claim in public, telling the crowd at his aunt Julia's funeral that the Julii went back to Venus, so that his stock had at once the sanctity of kings and the reverence owed to gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Aeneas","tags":["person","history","religion","italy","bronze age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.45–70, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1C*.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 6, in <i>The Lives of the Caesars</i>, trans. J. C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.8, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Ara_Pacis_Aeneas-Numa_%2814727962756%29.jpg/1920px-Ara_Pacis_Aeneas-Numa_%2814727962756%29.jpg","title":"Aeneas","desc":"A relief panel of the Ara Pacis in Rome, a veiled figure sacrificing beside a small shrine of the Penates; scholars are divided over whether the sacrificer is Aeneas or Numa. Amphipolis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ara_Pacis_Aeneas-Numa_%2814727962756%29.jpg","alt":"A weathered marble relief of a bearded man with his head veiled standing at a rustic altar, attendants and a sow at the left and a small temple above them"},"locator":{"name":"Lavinium","at":[12.4784,41.6616],"zoom":7},"why":[{"q":"Why did the legend need a line of Alban kings?","a":"Because Troy fell in 1183 BCE on the standard reckoning and Rome was founded in 753, so four centuries had to be bridged between the two."},{"q":"Why did six hundred men have to be sent back to Lavinium?","a":"Because the Penates brought from Troy kept returning there when they were moved to Alba, so the rites had to be kept where the gods stayed."},{"q":"Why was the story useful to one Roman family in particular?","a":"Because the Julii claimed Iulus as the founder of their name, and Caesar told the crowd at his aunt's funeral that his stock had at once the sanctity of kings and the reverence owed to gods."}]},
{"id":"rm-056","num":"56","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says the Tullii, Servilii, Quinctii, Geganii, Curiatii and Cloelii entered the Roman senate from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose people were moved to Rome when their city fell.","questions":["Rome's war with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was for Livy a civil war between parent and child, since Lavinium was an offshoot of Troy and that city an offshoot of Lavinium.","One hour undid the four centuries <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had stood, and every building but the temples was levelled while its people walked out towards Rome."],"answer":"Alba Longa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 years after Lavinium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 753 BCE, under Rome's 3rd king</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Alba Longa</b> is the city Roman tradition placed on the Alban Hills as the mother of Rome, built by Ascanius thirty years after Lavinium and named for the long ridge it was laid out along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its kings, all bearing the name Silvius, fill the centuries between the fall of Troy and the founding of Rome, running down to Numitor and his brother Amulius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius makes it the mother of the towns of Latium as well, the Trojans moving up from Lavinium to build a larger walled city and sending colonists out from it to found the cities of the Prisci Latini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy calls the war that finished it a civil war between parent and child, both peoples being of Trojan descent, Lavinium an offshoot of Troy and Alba of Lavinium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was settled without a regular engagement, and the outcome was that one of the two cities was destroyed while the two nations were blended into one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy describes the demolition as a thing done in silence, the Albans wandering through houses they were seeing for the last time until the cavalry shouted them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They left their hearths and household gods behind, the Romans levelled every public and private building, and one hour undid the work of the four centuries Alba had stood; only the temples were spared, by the king's order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What followed is the point the story exists to make: the Roman citizen body was doubled, the Caelian hill was taken into the city, and Alban nobles were put into the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy names them — the Tullii, Servilii, Quinctii, Geganii, Curiatii and Cloelii — and says the enlarged senate needed a house of its own, the Curia Hostilia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the legend accounts for patrician families at Rome who were not Roman, and the yearly festival of the Latins went on being held on the mountain above Alban ground, with a common sacrifice to Jupiter Latiaris and Rome in charge of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Alba Longa","tags":["place","city","history","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.45, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1C*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.49, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg/1920px-Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","title":"Alba Longa","desc":"The Reduction of Alba Longa by Tullus Hostilius, a Florentine chest panel by Lo Scheggia of about 1430–35. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","alt":"A long painted panel showing a walled pink city on a ridge with armies of mounted and armoured men crossing the open ground before it"},"locator":{"name":"Alba Longa","at":[12.6503,41.7469],"zoom":7},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy call the war that ended the city a civil war?","a":"Because both peoples were of Trojan descent, Lavinium an offshoot of Troy and this city an offshoot of Lavinium, so parent and child were fighting."},{"q":"Why were the temples left standing when everything else came down?","a":"Because the king ordered it, so the levelling of every public and private building stopped at the sacred ones."},{"q":"Why does the story exist at all?","a":"Because it accounts for patrician families at Rome who were not Roman — the Tullii, Servilii, Quinctii, Geganii, Curiatii and Cloelii put into the senate when the citizen body was doubled."}]},
{"id":"rm-057","num":"57","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Claudius held Secular Games in 47 CE as Rome's eight hundredth year, counting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the era whose epoch Varro fixed at 753 BCE.","questions":["Romans dated a document by naming the year's two consuls rather than by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which belonged to scholars and historians rather than to clerks.","Philip and his son were consuls when Rome kept its thousandth year in April 248, an anniversary counted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a foundation placed in 753 BCE."],"answer":"ab urbe condita","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Epoch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 BCE, Varro's reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome's 800th</span><span class=\"dt-v\">47 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome's 1000th</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 248 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ab urbe condita</b> is Latin for 'from the founding of the city', and names the practice of counting years from the beginning of Rome itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The epoch is 753 BCE on the reckoning that Varro settled and that became standard, where Cato had given 751 and earlier writers ranged from 813 down to 728.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was never how a Roman dated a document, though: the ordinary practice was to name the year's two consuls, and Dionysius quotes a censor's record that gives the consuls and then counts the years since the kings were expelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An era running from the founding therefore sat beside other ways of counting and belonged to scholars and historians rather than to clerks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy opens his history by measuring his subject that way, saying it goes back beyond 700 years, from small and humble beginnings to a greatness that begins to be overburdened by itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy also treats the founding as the edge of what a historian can vouch for, since what happened before the city or while it was being built suits the poets better than authentic records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The count did surface in public, but only when a round number came up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tacitus reports Secular Games held under the consulship of Claudius and Vitellius, eight hundred years from the foundation of Rome, which is 47 CE and which Claudius treated as the city's eighth centenary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Historia Augusta</i> describes the same thing at the millennium, gladiatorial shows and races in the Circus for the thousandth anniversary of the founding, held in April 248 while Philip and his son were consuls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even there the year is identified by its consuls, which is the whole point of the era: the count from the founding was how Rome thought about its own age rather than how it wrote a date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"ab urbe condita","tags":["concept","history","italy","chronology"],"sources":["Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.8, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.74, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i>, preface, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3Dpr. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 11.11, trans. John Jackson, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/11A*.html. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>The Three Gordians</i> 33, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Gordiani_Tres*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg/1920px-Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg","title":"ab urbe condita","desc":"A detail of the Roman consular and triumphal lists in the Capitoline Museums, each entry naming a magistrate, his office and the people he triumphed over, with a year number beside it. Jose Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg","alt":"Close-up of a marble slab cut with lines of Latin capitals in red-filled letters, each line ending in a Roman numeral"},"why":[{"q":"Why was this not how a Roman actually dated a document?","a":"Because the ordinary practice was to name the year's two consuls, so the era belonged to scholars and historians rather than to clerks."},{"q":"Why does it surface in public only occasionally?","a":"Because it was used when a round number came up, as at the eighth centenary Claudius kept in 47 CE and the millennium celebrated in April 248."},{"q":"Why does Livy treat the founding as an edge rather than a beginning?","a":"Because what happened before the city, or while it was being built, suits the poets better than authentic records."}]},
{"id":"rm-058","num":"58","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has envoys asking Rome's neighbours for intermarriage, and their refusal is what the tradition makes the cause of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at games held in honour of Consus.","questions":["Dionysius counts the women taken in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 683 and says Romulus married them to as many Roman men by the rites of each woman's own country.","The fighting that followed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended when the women ran between the armies, appealing to fathers on one side and husbands on the other."],"answer":"rape of the Sabine women","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Placed at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 BCE or a few years after</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Women seized</span><span class=\"dt-v\">683, on one count</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>rape of the Sabine women</b> is the mass seizure of women from neighbouring peoples that Roman tradition placed at the very beginning of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy gives the motive: Rome was a match for its neighbours in war but had no women and no right of intermarriage, so its greatness threatened to last one generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Envoys who asked the surrounding nations for alliance and intermarriage were rebuffed everywhere, and asked in return whether Rome had opened an asylum for women as well as men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romulus then announced games in honour of Consus and had them cried in the neighbouring towns, so that Caenina, Antemnae and Crustumerium came, and the whole Sabine people with their families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a signal the young men seized the women who had come to watch; Dionysius puts their number at 683 and says Romulus married them to as many Roman men by each woman's own rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Both the date and the motive were argued over: Dionysius reports some placing it in Romulus's first year and Gellius in his fourth, with the motive given as a shortage of women, a pretext for war, or a design for alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch gives the Roman defence — neither wantonness nor a wish to do mischief but a purpose of binding two peoples together — beside the rival claim that only thirty were taken because Romulus wanted war rather than marriages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The episode left its mark on Roman weddings, since Talasius, the name shouted as one woman was carried past, became the cry at a Roman marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> War followed, Caenina attacking first and the Sabines under Titus Tatius last, and ended when the women ran between the armies, appealing to fathers on one side and husbands on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What was made was not a peace but a union: one state, the kingship shared, Rome the seat of government, the Romans taking the name Quirites and the thirty curiae named after Sabine women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"rape of the Sabine women","tags":["event","history","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.30–31, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2B*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 14–15, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Fregio_della_basilica_emilia%2C_ratto_delle_sabine.JPG/1920px-Fregio_della_basilica_emilia%2C_ratto_delle_sabine.JPG","title":"The seizure of the Sabine women on the Basilica Aemilia frieze","desc":"Fragments of the marble frieze from the Basilica Aemilia in the Roman Forum, 1st century BCE, carved with scenes of Rome's earliest legends.","credit":"Photo by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fregio_della_basilica_emilia%2C_ratto_delle_sabine.JPG","alt":"A long fragmentary marble relief in a lit case, its broken slabs set in dark plaster, showing striding draped men carrying off women who twist away."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome's greatness threaten to last one generation?","a":"Because it was a match for its neighbours in war but had no women and no right of intermarriage, and every embassy asking for both was rebuffed."},{"q":"Why were the games in honour of Consus announced abroad?","a":"Because they were cried in the neighbouring towns so that Caenina, Antemnae, Crustumerium and the whole Sabine people came with their families."},{"q":"Why is the number taken disputed among the sources?","a":"Because Dionysius puts it at 683 while a rival account says only thirty were seized, on the argument that Romulus wanted war rather than marriages."}]},
{"id":"rm-059","num":"59","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy says the injured Sabine parents flocked to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because his was the most influential name in those parts, and the war that followed ended in a shared kingship.","questions":["One of Rome's three early centuries of knights, the Titienses, was named after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Sabine king who ruled at Rome beside Romulus until he was killed at a sacrifice.","Bribing the commander's daughter to let his troops into the Roman citadel is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seized it, and after the peace he lived on that hill while Romulus stayed on the Palatine."],"answer":"Titus Tatius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Joint reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 6th year, at Lavinium</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Titus Tatius</b> is the king of the Sabines whom Roman tradition makes Romulus's colleague on the throne, the one man said to have ruled Rome beside its founder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He enters the story after the seizure of the Sabine women, when the injured parents flocked to him from all sides because his was the most influential name in those parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Caenina, Antemnae and Crustumerium thought him too slow and attacked on their own account, so his war came last of the four and was much the most serious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took the Roman citadel by bribing the daughter of its commander to let his men in, and they crushed her under their shields once they were inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting that followed ran through the valley that later held the Forum, and stopped only when the Sabine women threw themselves between the lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the treaty made was not a peace between two states but one state out of both: the kingship was shared, Rome was the seat of government, and the Romans took the name Quirites from Cures, his own town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His name stayed in the institutions, since one of the three early centuries of knights was the Titienses, and a hundred Sabines were added to the patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dionysius credits him with altars to Sun and Moon, Saturn, Vesta, Vulcan and Diana, and with tables to Juno Quiritis in every curia; Plutarch puts his house on the citadel hill and Romulus's on the Palatine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two ruled five years in harmony, their one joint war against Cameria, until kinsmen of Tatius set upon envoys who had come to seek redress and killed them, and he shielded the offenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The dead men's friends killed him at the altar as he sacrificed at Lavinium, and Romulus, who buried him on the Aventine, pursued nobody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Titus Tatius","tags":["ruler","history","mythology","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.50–52, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2B*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 19–23, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/L._Titurius_L._f._Sabinus_Cr344-1c.jpg/1920px-L._Titurius_L._f._Sabinus_Cr344-1c.jpg","title":"Titus Tatius on a Roman denarius of 89 BCE","desc":"Silver denarius of the moneyer L. Titurius Sabinus, Rome, 89 BCE: the bearded head of King Tatius labelled SABIN, and two soldiers carrying off Sabine women.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L._Titurius_L._f._Sabinus_Cr344-1c.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin side by side: a bearded head in profile with letters around it, and two striding soldiers each lifting a woman off the ground."},"locator":{"name":"Cures","at":[12.6866,42.1825],"zoom":7},"why":[{"q":"Why did his war come last and hit hardest?","a":"Because the injured parents flocked to him as the most influential name in those parts, and Caenina, Antemnae and Crustumerium thought him too slow and attacked on their own account."},{"q":"Why was the settlement a union rather than a peace?","a":"Because the kingship was shared, Rome was made the seat of government, and the Romans took the name Quirites from his own town of Cures."},{"q":"Why was he killed by the friends of murdered envoys?","a":"Because kinsmen of his had set upon men who came seeking redress and killed them, and he shielded the offenders."}]},
{"id":"rm-060","num":"60","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The asylum between two groves below the Capitol, the hundred senators called Patres and the bodyguard of three hundred Celeres all belong in Livy to the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome's first king.","questions":["Dionysius says the patricians killed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the senate-house and carried the pieces of his body out under their robes, one of several ancient accounts of how Rome's first king ended.","A storm at the Goat's Marsh is where Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disappear from the sight of his army, after which the Romans hailed him as a god and the son of a god."],"answer":"Romulus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">37 years, from 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vanished or died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">aged 54 or 55</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Romulus</b> is the first king of Rome in the Roman account of themselves, credited not only with founding the city but with giving it its institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has him take twelve lictors, and prefers the explanation that the number came from the Etruscans to the one that made it the twelve birds of his augury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To fill a city built larger than its people, he opened a refuge between two groves below the Capitol, and free men and slaves came in from the states round about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made a hundred senators, called Patres for their rank, whose descendants were the patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He kept three hundred men about him in peace as well as war, the Celeres, and was dearer to the people than to the patricians and dearest of all to his soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His wars were with the towns nearest Rome, and from the first he carried the arms of a king he had killed himself up to the Capitol and marked out there the temple of Jupiter Feretrius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The last was against Veii, which gave up land by the Tiber and the salt works at its mouth, sent fifty hostages and took a truce of a hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His end is told two ways: a storm at the <i>Caprae Palus</i> in the Campus Martius hid him from his army, after which the Romans hailed him as a god and the son of a god, and Proculus Julius swore he had met him coming down from heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Livy passes on a dim tradition that the senators tore him limb from limb, and Dionysius the charge that they killed him in the senate-house and carried the pieces out under their robes, because he had grown harsh and arbitrary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was said to have reigned 37 years and to have been in his mid-fifties, and Rome kept his name in the god Quirinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Romulus","tags":["ruler","history","mythology","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.15, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.55–56, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2B*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 27–29, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Jean-auguste-dominique_ingres%2C_romolo%2C_vincitore_di_acron%2C_porta_il_bottino_nel_tempio_di_giano%2C_1812%2C_00.jpg/1920px-Jean-auguste-dominique_ingres%2C_romolo%2C_vincitore_di_acron%2C_porta_il_bottino_nel_tempio_di_giano%2C_1812%2C_00.jpg","title":"Ingres, Romulus, Victor over Acron","desc":"Painted by Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres in 1812: Romulus leads away the arms stripped from Acron, king of Caenina, whose body lies on the right.","credit":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-auguste-dominique_ingres%2C_romolo%2C_vincitore_di_acron%2C_porta_il_bottino_nel_tempio_di_giano%2C_1812%2C_00.jpg","alt":"A wide painting: a laurel-crowned man in a red cloak strides forward with one arm raised, a trophy of gilded armour carried behind him and a dead man lying at the right."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the new city need a refuge below the Capitol?","a":"Because it had been built larger than its people, so free men and slaves were let in from the states round about to fill it."},{"q":"Why does Livy prefer one explanation of the twelve lictors?","a":"Because he takes the number from the Etruscans rather than from the twelve birds of the augury, the Etruscan practice being an institution rather than a portent."},{"q":"Why is his end told two ways?","a":"Because a storm hid him from his army and the Romans hailed him as a god, while a dim tradition has the senators tear him apart because he had grown harsh and arbitrary."}]},
{"id":"rm-061","num":"61","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy rejects the story that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> studied under Pythagoras, pointing out that the philosopher taught in southern Italy more than a century after this Sabine king was called to Rome.","questions":["The flamens of Jupiter, Mars and Quirinus, the Vestals, the twelve Salii with their shields and the first Pontifex Maximus are all credited by Livy to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome's second king.","The temple of Janus, open in war and shut in peace, is said to have stayed shut through the whole reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and to have been shut only twice after him."],"answer":"Numa Pompilius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">43 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">aged over 80</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Numa Pompilius</b> is the second king of Rome in the tradition, a Sabine from Cures whom the senators chose for his justice and piety though it tipped the balance of power towards the Sabines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy will not have the old story that he learned from Pythagoras, since the philosopher taught in southern Italy more than a century later, and puts his character down to the austere Sabine discipline instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was made king by augury, seated on a stone on the citadel facing south while an augur laid a hand on his head and asked Jupiter for signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He set out to refound by law and custom the city that Romulus had founded by arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He built the temple of Janus as an index of war and peace and shut it, and Plutarch says Rome kept it shut for the whole of his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> To hold a people with no war left to fear he taught them to fear the gods instead, and gave out that he was advised at night by the nymph Egeria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He appointed a flamen for Jupiter so that the king's rites would go on when kings took the field, and flamens for Mars and Quirinus beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He chose the Vestal virgins, gave them a public stipend and made their persons inviolable, and set twelve Salii to carry the shields called <i>ancilia</i> through the city in a dancing procession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first Pontifex Maximus was handed the whole of it written out and sealed, with which victims on which days at which temples and how the cost was met, and funerals, the dead and prodigies added to his charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy gives him 43 years of peace, and Plutarch has him die past eighty and be buried under the Janiculum in two stone coffins, one for his body and one for the books he had written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Numa Pompilius","tags":["ruler","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.18, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.19, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Numa</i> 20–22, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Numa*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/R%C3%B6m._Republik-_L._Pomponius_Molo_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5485585.jpg/1920px-R%C3%B6m._Republik-_L._Pomponius_Molo_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5485585.jpg","title":"Numa sacrificing, on a Roman denarius of 97 BCE","desc":"Denarius of L. Pomponius Molo: the head of Apollo, and on the reverse Numa Pompilius with the lituus at a lighted altar as a youth brings up a goat, labelled NVMA POMPIL.","credit":"Münzkabinett, Berlin (2358342), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B6m._Republik-_L._Pomponius_Molo_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5485585.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin with notched edges: a laurelled head in profile, and two standing figures at a small altar with flames, a goat between them."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy reject the story that he studied with Pythagoras?","a":"Because the philosopher taught in southern Italy more than a century later, so Livy puts the king's character down to the austere Sabine discipline instead."},{"q":"Why was a flamen appointed for Jupiter?","a":"Because the king's own rites would otherwise stop whenever kings took the field, so a priest was set to carry them on."},{"q":"Why did he give out that a nymph advised him at night?","a":"Because he had to hold a people with no war left to fear, so he taught them to fear the gods instead."}]},
{"id":"rm-062","num":"62","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Censorinus gives 355 days and twelve months for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one more day than the moon's twelve months fill, and says an odd number was thought fuller and luckier.","questions":["January and February were added and the year's start moved away from March under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why December still carries the name of a tenth month.","An intercalary month of 22 days, slipped into February every other year, is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to be held to the sun, a task later left to the pontiffs."],"answer":"calendar of Numa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Numa, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Year of Numa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 months, 355 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>calendar of Numa</b> is the twelve-month year that Roman writers credited to their second king, put in place of an older year of ten months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that older year had been they did not agree: Plutarch gives 360 days in months running from under 20 to 35, and Censorinus 304 days in ten named months of 30 or 31.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Censorinus is candid that the change itself was disputed, some historians holding that Rome had kept twelve months from the beginning, and others crediting the reform to Tarquinius rather than to Numa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The new year ran to 355 days, one more than twelve lunar months fill, which he puts down to a belief that an odd number was fuller and the luckier for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> January and February were made out of 51 added days together with one taken from each of the six hollow months, which gave January 29 days and February 28.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The order moved with them, since March had opened the year and became the third month while January, named from Janus, came first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The old order is still legible in the names, December being the tenth month and Quintilis and Sextilis the fifth and sixth counted from March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> February, left alone with an even number of days and thought the unluckier for it, was the month of purification and of offerings to the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because a lunar year drifts from the sun, an intercalary month of 22 or 23 days was put into February every other year, between the Terminalia and the Regifugium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy has that adjustment arranged so the days came round to the same point of the sun every twentieth year, and the pontiffs who later held the power to intercalate used it so freely that the civil year ran far from the natural one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"calendar of Numa","tags":["concept","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.19, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Numa</i> 18, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Numa*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Numa</i> 19, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Numa*.html. [Open access]","Censorinus, <i>De die natali</i> 20, ed. Friedrich Hultsch (Leipzig: Teubner, 1867), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Censorinus/text*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Calendarium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Calendarium.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Fasti_Antiates_maiores_reconstruction.png/1920px-Fasti_Antiates_maiores_reconstruction.png","title":"The Fasti Antiates Maiores, reconstructed","desc":"A painted calendar from Antium of the 1st century BCE, its surviving fragments shown in blue: twelve month columns with their day totals, and a thirteenth headed INTER for the intercalary month.","credit":"Reconstruction drawing by Levaring, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fasti_Antiates_maiores_reconstruction.png","alt":"A wide grid of thirteen columns of Latin abbreviations in red and black, with irregular blue patches marking the fragments that survive and Roman numerals along the foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the year run to 355 days rather than 354?","a":"Because a belief that an odd number was fuller and luckier added one to the twelve lunar months the year was built from."},{"q":"Why do the month names no longer match their positions?","a":"Because March had opened the year and became the third month when January was put first, leaving December as the tenth and Quintilis and Sextilis the fifth and sixth counted from March."},{"q":"Why did the civil year drift far from the natural one?","a":"Because a lunar year needs an intercalary month, and the pontiffs who held the power to insert it into February used that power freely."}]},
{"id":"rm-063","num":"63","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> entertain the Alban envoys at dinner while his own demanded redress at Alba, so that the refusal and the guilt of the war would be Alba's.","questions":["The Caelian hill was taken into the city and the senate-house called the Curia Hostilia was built for the enlarged senate under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the third king of Rome.","Mettius Fufetius was torn apart between two four-horse chariots on the order of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a punishment Livy says the Romans never used again."],"answer":"Tullus Hostilius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">32 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">died in a fire with his household</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tullus Hostilius</b> is the third king of Rome in the tradition, chosen after the death of Numa and remembered as his opposite, a man Livy calls more warlike even than Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Believing the state was growing soft for want of a war, he went looking for a pretext and found one in the cattle-raiding that went both ways across the Alban border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He kept the Alban envoys at a sumptuous dinner while his own were demanding redress at Alba, so that the refusal, and with it the guilt of the war, should be Alba's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first Alban war was settled by a combat of triplets, and in the next campaign, against Fidenae and Veii, the Alban commander Mettius Fufetius drew his men off to the hills mid-battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tullus had him tied between two four-horse chariots driven in opposite directions, which Livy calls the first and last punishment of its kind among the Romans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Alba Longa itself was pulled down and its people ordered to Rome, and what Livy describes is not a sack but a silence, families wandering their own houses for the last time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The number of citizens was doubled, the Caelian hill was taken into the city and made the king's own residence, and Alban nobles were enrolled among the senators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the enlarged senate he built a senate-house that was still called the Curia Hostilia in the time of Livy's fathers, and he raised ten troops of knights from the Albans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Sabine war followed, and then prodigies: a shower of stones on the Alban Mount, a voice from the grove above it, and a plague that the king would not allow to interrupt the campaigning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He died in a fire that took his whole household with him, which Livy puts down to Jupiter's anger at rites from Numa's books performed wrongly, while Dionysius reports that most held it arson by his successor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tullus Hostilius","tags":["ruler","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.22, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.28, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.31, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D31. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.35, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3B*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg/1920px-Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","title":"The Reduction of Alba Longa by Tullus Hostilius","desc":"Tempera, gold and silver on panel by Lo Scheggia, about 1430-35, from the front of a Florentine marriage chest: the fall of Alba imagined as a Renaissance siege.","credit":"Lo Scheggia, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lo_Scheggia%2C_Reduction_of_Alba_Longa_by_Tullus_Hostilius%2C_Circa_1430_-_35%2C_Sotheby%27s.jpg","alt":"A long narrow painted panel: a pink walled city on a hill with armies of armoured horsemen and foot soldiers spread across the plain before it."},"locator":{"name":"Alba Longa","at":[12.6503,41.7469],"zoom":7},"why":[{"q":"Why did he entertain the Alban envoys so lavishly?","a":"Because his own were demanding redress at Alba at the same time, so that the refusal, and with it the guilt of the war, should be Alba's."},{"q":"Why was the Alban commander's punishment unique in Roman memory?","a":"Because he was tied between two four-horse chariots driven in opposite directions, which Livy calls the first and last punishment of its kind among the Romans."},{"q":"Why do the accounts of his death disagree?","a":"Because Livy puts the fire down to Jupiter's anger at rites from Numa's books performed wrongly, while Dionysius reports that most held it arson by his successor."}]},
{"id":"rm-064","num":"64","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Which side each set of triplets fought for is disputed in the sources for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Livy follows the majority in making the Horatii the Romans.","questions":["The one man left standing among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> feigned flight so that his three wounded opponents would follow at intervals, and killed them one after another.","Dionysius says the six who fought as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were cousins, the sons of twin sisters married on the same day to a Roman and an Alban."],"answer":"Horatii and Curiatii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Tullus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left standing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of the 6</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Horatii and Curiatii</b> are the two sets of triplets who, in Roman tradition, settled the war between Rome and Alba Longa by fighting three against three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls the story one of the most widely celebrated in antiquity and admits that the accounts still disagree over which set was Roman, following the majority in giving the Horatii to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius makes the six of them cousins: an Alban named Sicinius married his twin daughters on one day to a Roman and an Alban, and each bore three sons at a birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In that version Tullus objects that it would be impious to arm cousins against one another, and the Alban commander answers that he had already sounded his own three in private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before the fight the two peoples were bound by treaty, which Livy uses to set down the fetial forms: the pure grass fetched from the citadel, the <i>pater patratus</i>, and the flint that killed the swine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two of the Romans fell at once and all three Albans were wounded, which left one Horatius untouched and facing three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He ran, judging that wounded men would follow at unequal speeds, and killed them one at a time as they came up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His sister met him at the Capene gate and knew on his shoulder the cloak she had made for her betrothed among the Curiatii, and he killed her for weeping over an enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was condemned for treason by two appointed judges, appealed to the people at the king's own prompting, and was acquitted for his courage rather than for the justice of his cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An expiation was owed all the same, so his father made him pass under a beam laid across the street with his head covered, and Livy says it still stood in his day, kept in repair at public cost and called the Sister's Beam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Horatii and Curiatii","tags":["event","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.24, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D24. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.25, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.26, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3A*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.14, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3A*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Cavalier_d%27Arpino_-_Combat_of_the_Horatii_and_the_Curiatii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Cavalier_d%27Arpino_-_Combat_of_the_Horatii_and_the_Curiatii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Combat of the Horatii and the Curiatii","desc":"A fresco by Giuseppe Cesari on the wall of the room named after the story in the Palazzo dei Conservatori, Rome: four brothers already down and the last two closing.","credit":"Giuseppe Cesari, Capitoline Museums (MC 9006), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cavalier_d%27Arpino_-_Combat_of_the_Horatii_and_the_Curiatii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A wall painting in a decorated frame: two armies drawn up in ranks behind a clearing where four armoured men lie dead and two others fight."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy admit uncertainty in so famous a story?","a":"Because the accounts still disagreed over which set of triplets was Roman, and he follows the majority in giving the Horatii to Rome."},{"q":"Why did the survivor run from three wounded men?","a":"Because he judged that wounded men would follow at unequal speeds, so he could kill them one at a time as they came up."},{"q":"Why was an expiation owed after an acquittal?","a":"Because he was acquitted for his courage rather than for the justice of his cause, so his father made him pass under a beam with his head covered."}]},
{"id":"rm-065","num":"65","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"The first bridge over the Tiber, the Pons Sublicius, and the port city of Ostia at its mouth are both credited to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the fourth king of Rome.","questions":["Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> write down the fetial law for declaring war, under which a herald recited his demand at the frontier and waited 33 days before war was proclaimed.","Captured Latins from Politorium, Tellenae and Ficana were settled on the Aventine by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a grandson of Numa who had the old king's religious commentaries set up in public."],"answer":"Ancus Marcius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">founded Ostia and bridged the Tiber</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ancus Marcius</b> is the fourth king of Rome in the tradition, a grandson of Numa through his daughter, and Livy makes him a mixture of that grandfather and of Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began by having the pontiff copy Numa's religious commentaries out of the old king's own papers and set them up in a public place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Latins took that for weakness and raided Roman land, reckoning that this king would spend his reign among chapels and altars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His answer was to write down the fetial law for declaring war, taken, Livy says, from the Aequicoli: a herald with his head bound in wool recites the demand at the frontier, at the gate and in the forum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Satisfaction was then allowed 33 days before war was proclaimed, a form Livy says the fetials still followed in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The war with the Latins took Politorium, Tellenae and Ficana, and the captured populations were carried off to Rome and settled on the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Politorium was razed once the Latins had reoccupied it, and the fighting ended in a pitched battle at Medullia that brought many thousands more into citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Janiculum across the river was walled and garrisoned, not for want of room but to keep a strong position out of enemy hands, and joined to the city by the first bridge over the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That bridge was the Pons Sublicius, named from the piles it stood on and built of wood without metal, which the pontiffs kept in repair with sacrifices as a sacred thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also dug the Fossa Quiritium, built a prison overlooking the Forum, and carried Roman power to the sea, walling Ostia at the Tiber mouth and setting salt pits on both banks, in a reign Livy gives as 24 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ancus Marcius","tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.44–45, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3C*.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Pons Sublicius,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 401–2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP%2A/Pons_Sublicius.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg/1920px-C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","title":"Numa and Ancus Marcius on a Roman denarius","desc":"Denarius of the moneyer C. Marcius Censorinus, whose family claimed descent from both kings: their bearded heads are shown one behind the other on the obverse.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C._Marcius_Censorinus_Cr346-1b.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin: two overlapping bearded heads in profile facing right, and a rider standing across the backs of two galloping horses."},"locator":{"name":"Ostia","at":[12.2917,41.7558],"zoom":8},"why":[{"q":"Why did copying Numa's commentaries invite an attack?","a":"Because the Latins took it for weakness and raided Roman land, reckoning that this king would spend his reign among chapels and altars."},{"q":"Why did the fetial law have to be written down?","a":"Because it fixed the forms for declaring war — a herald with his head bound in wool reciting the demand at the frontier, at the gate and in the forum, with 33 days allowed for satisfaction."},{"q":"Why was the first bridge built of wood without metal?","a":"Because the pontiffs kept it in repair with sacrifices as a sacred thing, so the Pons Sublicius was named from its piles and held no iron."}]},
{"id":"rm-066","num":"66","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"An eagle lifted the cap from the head of the Etruscan newcomer who became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his wife Tanaquil read it as a sign that the crown was coming to him.","questions":["Told to cut a whetstone with a razor to test his art, the augur Attus Navius did it, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up changing the centuries of knights.","The hundred new senators called the Lesser Houses, the first marking out of the Circus Maximus and the shops around the Forum are all credited by Livy to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome's fifth king."],"answer":"Tarquinius Priscus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">38 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">murdered by hired shepherds</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tarquinius Priscus</b> is the fifth king of Rome in the tradition and the first to come from outside Latium, a rich man of Tarquinii whose father was a Corinthian exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He moved to Rome at his wife Tanaquil's urging, since at Tarquinii he was still the son of a foreigner, while at Rome nobility was recent and won by merit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the Janiculum an eagle took the cap off his head, circled the carriage and put it back, which Tanaquil, expert like most Etruscans in such signs, read as the crown coming to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He entered the city as Lucius Tarquinius Priscus, made himself known by hospitality and kindness, and was trusted by Ancus with the guardianship of the king's sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says he was the first man to canvass for the crown and make a set speech for it, sending those boys out hunting on the day of the election.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A hundred new senators were added, called the Lesser Houses, and the plunder of Apiolae paid for games on a new scale, with a space first marked out for what became the Circus Maximus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wooden stands were allotted to patricians and knights to watch from, and the ground round the Forum was divided into building sites for arcades and shops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When he tried to change the centuries of knights, the augur Attus Navius told him nothing could be altered without the birds, and cut a whetstone through with a razor to prove his art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The king kept the names and numbers and only doubled the men in each, to 1,800, and went on to beat the Sabines and take town after town from the Latins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The works of peace were a stone wall, conduits draining the valleys to the Tiber and a platform levelled on the Capitol for a temple of Jupiter, until in his 38th year two shepherds sent by the sons of Ancus drove an axe into his head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tarquinius Priscus","tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","etruscan"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D40. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Sebastiano_Ricci_-_Tarquin_the_Elder_Consulting_Attius_Navius_-_72.PA.15_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg/1920px-Sebastiano_Ricci_-_Tarquin_the_Elder_Consulting_Attius_Navius_-_72.PA.15_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg","title":"Tarquin the Elder consulting Attus Navius","desc":"Oil by Sebastiano Ricci, J. Paul Getty Museum: the king in the red cloak, and the augur kneeling with his razor at the whetstone, one finger raised to the birds.","credit":"Sebastiano Ricci, J. Paul Getty Museum (72.PA.15), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebastiano_Ricci_-_Tarquin_the_Elder_Consulting_Attius_Navius_-_72.PA.15_-_J._Paul_Getty_Museum.jpg","alt":"A Baroque painting: a laurel-crowned man in a red cloak stands at the right, hand to his mouth, watching an old bearded man kneel over a block with a blade."},"locator":{"name":"Tarquinii","at":[11.7561,42.2492],"zoom":7,"within":"Tarquinia"},"why":[{"q":"Why did he leave Tarquinii for Rome?","a":"Because at Tarquinii he was still the son of a foreigner, while at Rome nobility was recent and won by merit."},{"q":"Why was the eagle read as a promise of the crown?","a":"Because it took the cap off his head, circled the carriage and put it back, and Tanaquil was expert like most Etruscans in such signs."},{"q":"Why did he only double the centuries of knights rather than rename them?","a":"Because the augur told him nothing could be altered without the birds and cut a whetstone through with a razor to prove it, so the names and numbers stood."}]},
{"id":"rm-067","num":"67","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Livy has Tarquinius Superbus set the plebs to dig <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a tunnel to take all the city's sewage, while Dionysius says the sewers were begun by the earlier Tarquin.","questions":["Strabo says wagons loaded with hay could pass through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Rome's other sewers in places, so closely were they vaulted with fitted stone.","The winding course of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the Argiletum through the Forum to the Tiber shows that it began as a stream in a marshy valley rather than as a dug tunnel."],"answer":"Cloaca Maxima","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Tarquins, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">from the Argiletum through the Forum to the Tiber</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Cloaca Maxima</b> is the great drain of Rome, which carried the water off the Forum and the valleys between the hills and emptied it into the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy gives the work to Tarquinius Superbus, who set the plebs to it once the temple was finished, and calls it a tunnel underground to receive all the sewage of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius gives the sewers to the earlier Tarquin, who began the digging of them, and Livy himself has that king drain the low ground round the Forum into the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To Dionysius the aqueducts, the paved roads and the sewers were the three works in which the greatness of Rome could best be seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds, on the authority of Gaius Acilius, that the censors once let out the cleaning and repair of the neglected sewers for a thousand talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo describes them vaulted with close-fitting stone and wide enough in places for a wagon loaded with hay to pass through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The channel measured in one surviving stretch is 4.2 m (13.8 feet) high and 3.2 m (10.5 feet) wide, built of peperino, vaulted and paved with blocks of lava.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its winding course, from the Argiletum where it gathers the water of the Esquiline, Viminal and Quirinal, through the Forum and the Velabrum to the river, is the course of a stream in a marshy valley rather than a line laid out by engineers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Writers under the empire describe it as underground from the first, but Plautus calls it a <i>canalis</i>, an open channel, which has suggested that it was not roofed until later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Little if anything of the first drain survives: some branch channels near the temple of Saturn may be of the 5th century BCE at latest, and the rest is of the 3rd century and after, much of it from Agrippa's restorations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cloaca Maxima","tags":["building","history","technology","italy","iron age"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.67, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3D*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.8, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Cloaca Maxima,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 126–27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP%2A/Cloaca_Maxima.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Cloaca_Maxima_-_%283070_%3B_A1%29.jpg/1920px-Cloaca_Maxima_-_%283070_%3B_A1%29.jpg","title":"The Cloaca Maxima on the Tiber","desc":"An albumen photograph taken between 1860 and 1888, catalogued as a view of the Cloaca Maxima, with the round temple of the Forum Boarium and the campanile of Santa Maria in Cosmedin above.","credit":"Photo by Edmond Behles, University of Strasbourg, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cloaca_Maxima_-_%283070_%3B_A1%29.jpg","alt":"A sepia photograph of a river running past a long stone embankment wall, with a small arch at the waterline on the left and a round colonnaded temple and a bell tower on the bank above."},"locator":{"name":"Cloaca Maxima","at":[12.4803,41.8889],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the channel wind rather than run straight?","a":"Because its course from the Argiletum through the Forum and the Velabrum to the river is that of a stream in a marshy valley rather than a line laid out by engineers."},{"q":"Why is it doubted that the drain was always covered?","a":"Because writers under the empire describe it as underground from the first, while Plautus calls it a canalis, an open channel."},{"q":"Why can so little of the original be seen?","a":"Because some branch channels near the temple of Saturn may be of the 5th century BCE at latest, and the rest is of the 3rd century and after, much of it from Agrippa's restorations."}]},
{"id":"rm-068","num":"68","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Dionysius says the seats of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were divided among the thirty curiae so that every spectator sat in his own section, and that it held 150,000 in his day.","questions":["Livy dates the first starting gates in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to 329 BCE, which shows that nothing permanent stood in the valley before then, whatever the kings were credited with.","The valley between the Palatine and the Aventine where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was laid out had been used for horse races in honour of Consus long before any building stood there."],"answer":"Circus Maximus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Tarquins, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Starting gates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first attested 329 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Circus Maximus</b> is the oldest and largest of Rome's racecourses, laid out in the valley between the Palatine and the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The valley suited it, running about 600 m (660 yards) long and 150 m (160 yards) wide, with the slopes on either side for a crowd to stand on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Races in honour of Consus were held there in the tradition long before any building stood in it, and it was at those games that the Sabine women were seized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy has Tarquinius Priscus first mark out a space there after the plunder of Apiolae, with places allotted to patricians and knights to build wooden stands on props.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius gives that king covered seats raised on scaffolding, where the spectators had stood before, and the places divided among the thirty curiae so that each had its own section.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy also has the last Tarquin set the plebs to the stands, so that the tradition divides between the two kings and modern topography treats both as tradition rather than record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first firm date is Livy's notice that the starting gates were put up in the circus in 329 BCE, which makes it plain nothing permanent had stood there before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those gates were probably of wood, since a century later a poet quoted by Cicero has the chariots sent out from painted jaws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By Dionysius's own day the course ran three and a half stades, ringed on three sides by a canal ten feet deep and wide and by porticoes of three storeys, the lowest of stone and the two above of wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He gives the circuit as eight stades and the crowd as 150,000, with vaulted starting-places all opened by a single rope and an outer portico of shops through which the crowd came and went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Circus Maximus","tags":["building","history","italy","iron age","sport"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.68, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3D*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Circus Maximus,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 114–20, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP%2A/Circus_Maximus.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Topographical_diagram_of_the_Circus_Maximus_by_Samuel_Ball_Platner.jpg/1920px-Topographical_diagram_of_the_Circus_Maximus_by_Samuel_Ball_Platner.jpg","title":"Plan of the Circus Maximus","desc":"A printed plan by Samuel Ball Platner: the starting gates at the left, the long spina down the middle with the two obelisks, the curved end at the right, and the Palatine above.","credit":"Samuel Ball Platner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Topographical_diagram_of_the_Circus_Maximus_by_Samuel_Ball_Platner.jpg","alt":"An engraved ground plan: a long narrow arena with tiers of seating hatched along both sides, a straight barrier down the centre and a rounded end."},"locator":{"name":"Circus Maximus","at":[12.4857,41.8859],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was this valley used for racing?","a":"Because it ran about 600 metres long and 150 wide with slopes on either side for a crowd to stand on, so races were held there long before any building stood in it."},{"q":"Why does the tradition credit two different kings with the seating?","a":"Because Livy has the elder Tarquin allot places for wooden stands and the last Tarquin set the plebs to build them, so modern topography treats both as tradition rather than record."},{"q":"Why is 329 BCE the first firm date here?","a":"Because Livy's notice that the starting gates were put up in that year makes it plain nothing permanent had stood in the valley before."}]},
{"id":"rm-069","num":"69","category":"Ancient Rome · History","question":"Flames were seen to play about the head of the sleeping boy who became <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Tanaquil read the omen and had him brought up as a son of the house.","questions":["Tanaquil told the crowd from a palace window that the wounded king would recover and had ordered them to obey <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who judged cases for days as though the power were lent.","The temple of Diana on the Aventine was built jointly by Rome and the Latin peoples under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Livy reads as an admission of Roman predominance."],"answer":"Servius Tullius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">44 years, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">killed at his daughter's urging</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Servius Tullius</b> is the sixth king of Rome in the tradition, and the one the Romans credited with their oldest institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy tells of flames playing about his head as he slept, seen by many, which Tanaquil read as a promise; the boy was then brought up as a child of the house and married to the king's daughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether he was born a slave Livy doubts, preferring the account in which his father was the leading man of Corniculum, killed when the town fell, and his mother spared as a captive of rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Tarquin was struck down, Tanaquil closed the palace, dressed the wound and told the crowd from an upper window that the king lived and had ordered them to obey Servius meanwhile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He sat in the royal chair, judged some cases and adjourned others as though consulting the king, and by the time the mourning was heard the throne was his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He preferred statecraft to arms, and his chief work abroad was the temple of Diana on the Aventine, built jointly by Rome and the Latins, which Livy reads as their admission of Roman predominance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius adds that the cities paid for it together and that a bronze pillar stood in the temple, engraved with the council's decrees and the names of the cities that had joined, still there in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Sabine heifer belongs to that temple: a prodigy whose sacrifice was to give empire to the sacrificer's city, until a Roman priest sent the Sabine to wash in the Tiber and offered it himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His end came out of his own household, and the street kept the memory of it, for his daughter Tullia drove her carriage over his body in what was afterwards called the Vicus Sceleratus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy gives him 44 years and says that with him all lawful kingship at Rome perished, adding that some held he had meant to lay the power down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Servius Tullius","tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","religion"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.39, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D39. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.45, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.48, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D48. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.26, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4B*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Servius_Tullius_When_a_Child%2C_Asleep_in_the_Apartment_of_King_Tarquinius_Priscus%2C_Angelica_Kauffmann%2C_1785.webp/1920px-Servius_Tullius_When_a_Child%2C_Asleep_in_the_Apartment_of_King_Tarquinius_Priscus%2C_Angelica_Kauffmann%2C_1785.webp","title":"Servius Tullius as a child, asleep in the palace of Tarquinius Priscus","desc":"Angelica Kauffmann, 1785, painted in grisaille. The scene is the prodigy Livy reports at 1.39: flames playing about the sleeping boy's head, a servant fetching water to douse them, and the queen Tanaquil holding him back so that the sign should not be disturbed.","credit":"Angelica Kauffmann, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Servius_Tullius_When_a_Child%2C_Asleep_in_the_Apartment_of_King_Tarquinius_Priscus%2C_Angelica_Kauffmann%2C_1785.webp","alt":"Painting in shades of grey: a boy sleeps on a couch with flames about his head while a woman restrains a servant carrying a vessel of water."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the queen conceal the old king's death?","a":"Because from an upper window she could tell the crowd the king lived and had ordered them to obey Servius meanwhile, which let him take the chair before the mourning was heard."},{"q":"Why does Livy read the temple on the Aventine as a political fact?","a":"Because it was built jointly by Rome and the Latins, which he takes as their admission of Roman predominance, and a bronze pillar in it carried the names of the cities that joined."},{"q":"Why does Livy doubt that the king was born a slave?","a":"Because he prefers the account in which the father was the leading man of Corniculum, killed when the town fell, and the mother spared as a captive of rank."}]},
{"id":"rm-070","num":70,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a citizen's place in the army and on the tax lists followed the rating of his property rather than his birth, and each class was divided into centuries.","questions":["Livy ranks Servius beside Numa as a founder of institutions for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which sorted the citizens into classes by wealth and turned four regions of the city into tribes.","The richest men carried the heaviest levy and the heaviest tax under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while everyone below the lowest rating made up one century that owed neither service nor money."],"answer":"Servian constitution","answerText":"Servian constitution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 44-year reign of Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centuries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">193, of which 98 in the first class</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City tribes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, replacing the earlier 3</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Servian constitution</b> is the arrangement that Roman tradition credits to Servius Tullius, under which the duties of peace and war were laid on each man in proportion to what he owned rather than on all alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls it by far the greatest of his works in peacetime and ranks Servius with Numa as a founder, the one of religious law, the other of the grades of dignity and fortune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rating sorted the citizens into classes, and each class into centuries of juniors, who took the field, and seniors, who stayed to hold the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy's first class is those whose property reached 100,000 in the old copper reckoning, formed into 80 centuries and armed in bronze with helmet, round shield, greaves and coat of mail; each class beneath lost a piece of that equipment as the rating fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below the last rating came a single century, excused the levy, and Dionysius adds that it paid no tax of any kind either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius counts 193 centuries altogether, 98 of them in the first class once the 18 centuries of cavalry are reckoned with its 80.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cavalry followed the same principle: Livy has 10,000 from the treasury for each horse and certain widows assessed 2,000 a year towards its keep, so the burden was shifted from the poor on to the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Servius also cut the city into four regions named after the hills and made tribes of them, so that levy and tax rested on where a man lived, not on the three tribes of descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The countryside was divided in the same way, though the number was already disputed when Dionysius wrote: he reports 26 parts from Fabius and 31 from Vennonius, and says Cato gave no figure at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the people were afterwards summoned to vote by these same centuries, the reform settled who decided as well as who paid, the wealthiest centuries being called first and the poorest hardly ever reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.14, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#14. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.15, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#15. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.18, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#18. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#20. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg/1920px-Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg","title":"The census frieze: a citizen gives his declaration","desc":"Left panel of the so-called census frieze from the Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, marble, late 2nd century BCE, from the Campus Martius, now in the Louvre (Ma 975). A clerk writes on a tablet while a citizen declares his property and a soldier is armed at the right. The relief is several centuries later than the reform Roman writers ascribed to Servius Tullius, and shows the procedure as the Republic knew it.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg","alt":"Marble relief of a seated clerk writing on a tablet, a standing man in a toga declaring, a second seated official, and a soldier putting on a plumed helmet."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the burden of war shifted onto the rich?","a":"Because duties were laid in proportion to property, so the first class served in full bronze armour and paid for cavalry horses while the lowest rating was excused the levy and the tax."},{"q":"Why did the reform settle who decided as well as who paid?","a":"Because the people were afterwards summoned to vote by these same centuries, the wealthiest called first and the poorest hardly ever reached."},{"q":"Why were the new tribes based on where a man lived?","a":"Because the city was cut into four regions named after the hills, so levy and tax rested on residence rather than on the three tribes of descent."}]},
{"id":"rm-071","num":71,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Roman citizens voting in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called up by centuries in order of wealth, so that the richest were asked first and the poorest were seldom reached at all.","questions":["Because an army could not lawfully be commanded inside the pomerium, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met out on the Campus Martius with a guard posted while the people gave their votes.","Livy has the first two consuls elected by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the prefect of the city, on the regulations that Roman writers ascribed to Servius Tullius."],"answer":"comitia centuriata","answerText":"comitia centuriata","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Instituted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the reign of Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centuries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">193, called in order of wealth</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">elected by it, after 244 years of kings</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>comitia centuriata</b> is the Roman assembly in which citizens voted not as individuals but in the centuries their property rating had placed them in, each century casting a single vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman writers derive it from the rating ascribed to Servius Tullius, and Livy says the older order had given every man the same weight, which Servius replaced with a graduation that left nobody openly disfranchised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The knights were summoned first, then the 80 centuries of the first class, and if these agreed the question was settled before the classes beneath them were reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius sets out the same order and says the calling seldom went past the fourth class, so that the fifth and the century of the poor were as good as superfluous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He reads the arrangement as a deception rather than a franchise, since every man was asked his opinion, yet a century of few counted for as much as a century of many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Aulus Gellius preserves the jurist Laelius Felix on how the three assemblies differ: the curiate votes by birth, the centuriate by rating and age, the tribal by district and place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same passage gives the reason they met on the Campus Martius: holding them within the pomerium was against religion, since an army may be commanded outside the city and not within it, and a guard stood watch while the people voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What it decided was the heaviest business of the state: Livy has the first two consuls elected by the centuries under the prefect of the city, on the regulations of Servius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It went on to choose consuls, praetors, censors and decemvirs, to make law of what the senate laid before it, to decide on war, and to hear a citizen's appeal against a capital sentence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That the arrangement was altered later is plain from both Livy and Dionysius, but no ancient writer says when the change came or what it consisted of, and it is reconstructed from passing allusions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#20. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.21, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#21. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 15.27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/15*.html#27. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Comitia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Comitia.html. [Open access]"],"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg/1920px-P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg","title":"A Roman voting scene on a denarius of P. Licinius Nerva","desc":"Silver denarius, Crawford 292/1. Roma helmeted on the obverse; on the reverse two citizens on the raised gangway, one taking a tablet from an attendant below and one dropping his into the basket. The type shows Roman voting without naming which assembly it is.","credit":"Photograph by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin: a helmeted head on the left; on the right, small figures on a railed gangway, one bending to take a tablet, another placing one in a basket."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the calling of the centuries seldom reach the poorest?","a":"Because the knights and the 80 centuries of the first class voted first, and if they agreed the question was settled before the classes beneath them were reached."},{"q":"Why does Dionysius call the arrangement a deception?","a":"Because every man was asked his opinion and yet a century of few counted for as much as a century of many."},{"q":"Why did this assembly have to meet outside the city?","a":"Because holding it within the pomerium was against religion, an army being commandable outside the city and not within it, so a guard stood watch on the Campus Martius while the people voted."}]},
{"id":"rm-072","num":72,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took in the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline, and Dionysius says the king it is named after was the last to enlarge the circuit of the city.","questions":["Most of what still stands of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is of Grotta Oscura tufa, quarried in the territory of Veii and laid after the Gauls had shown that Rome lay open.","Where no cliff protected the city, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran as an earth rampart with a broad ditch before it, across the tablelands of the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline."],"answer":"Servian Wall","answerText":"Servian Wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the reign of Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in squared stone, from 377 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Agger</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 stadia long, 50 Roman feet thick</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Servian Wall</b> is the circuit of wall, rampart and ditch that Roman tradition ascribes to Servius Tullius, and whose remains still stand at points across modern Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy says the king surrounded the city with a mound, moats and a wall, and that in doing so he extended the pomerium, the consecrated strip that no one might build on or cultivate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The work followed the census: with 80,000 counted, Livy writes, the city plainly had to be enlarged, so the Quirinal and Viminal were taken in, and then the Esquiline, where the king went to live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius gives the same two hills and calls Servius the last king to enlarge the circuit, which he did only after consulting the auspices as the law directed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that by his own day the wall was hard to make out among the buildings crowded against it, though it kept traces of its old structure in several places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest stretches that survive are of grey cappellaccio tufa, and are usually assigned to a wall of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of what stands is later: after the Gauls had shown that Rome lay open, the circuit was reinforced on largely the old line in blocks of Grotta Oscura tufa, quarried in the territory of Veii soon after that town fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has the censors let a contract for a wall of squared stone, paid for by a tax that drove debtors into fresh borrowing, and a later year given over to repairing walls and towers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the ground offered no cliff, across the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline, the defence was an agger, an earth rampart of seven stadia with a ditch before it thirty Roman feet deep and a hundred wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its line ran from the Tiber to the Capitol, north-east along the cliffs of that hill and the Quirinal, south over the Esquiline and round the Caelian and Palatine, and back to the river past the Forum Boarium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#13. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Murus Servii Tullii,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 350-55, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Murus_Servii_Tullii.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Esquilino_-_mura_serviane_all%27auditorium_1120875.JPG/1920px-Esquilino_-_mura_serviane_all%27auditorium_1120875.JPG","title":"Blocks of the wall built into a later building at Largo Leopardi","desc":"A stretch on the Esquiline, at Largo Leopardi in Rome, where the great tufa blocks stand incorporated in the brickwork of the building known as the Auditorium of Maecenas. Dionysius wrote in his own day that the wall was hard to make out among the buildings crowded against it.","credit":"Photograph by Lalupa, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Esquilino_-_mura_serviane_all%27auditorium_1120875.JPG","alt":"Large squared blocks of pale tufa standing at the corner of a brick building on a modern street, with an iron railing and an earth bank to the left."},"locator":{"name":"Servian Wall","at":[12.5017,41.9017],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the census make an enlargement necessary?","a":"Because with 80,000 counted the city plainly had to grow, so the Quirinal and Viminal were taken in, and then the Esquiline, where the king went to live."},{"q":"Why is most of the standing wall later than the king it is named for?","a":"Because after the Gauls had shown Rome lay open the circuit was reinforced on largely the old line in Grotta Oscura tufa, quarried in the territory of Veii soon after that town fell."},{"q":"Why was an earth rampart used on three of the hills?","a":"Because across the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline the ground offered no cliff, so the defence was an agger of seven stadia with a ditch thirty Roman feet deep before it."}]},
{"id":"rm-073","num":73,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A man gave his name, his age, his wife and children and the worth of his goods on oath at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and whoever stayed away from it risked imprisonment or death.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closed with the whole people drawn up in arms while a boar, a ram and a bull were led three times round them and sacrificed to Mars.","Livy makes the first count 80,000 and Dionysius 84,700 at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Republic afterwards entrusted to two magistrates elected for that business alone."],"answer":"Roman census","answerText":"Roman census","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Instituted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Servius Tullius, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">every fifth year, by censors from 443 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First count</span><span class=\"dt-v\">80,000 in Livy, 84,700 in Dionysius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","practice"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman census</b> is the sworn registration of the citizens and their property, instituted in Roman tradition by Servius Tullius so that the duties of peace and war might fall on each man in proportion to what he owned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says the work was hurried along by a law that threatened imprisonment and even death against anyone who evaded assessment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A man declared on oath his own name and his father's, or his patron's if he was a freedman, and stated his age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was then asked whether he had a wife, and gave her name with the number, names and ages of his children, while widows and orphans were entered by their guardians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius adds that Servius made freedmen declare with everyone else and put them in the four city tribes, where their descendants still stood in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The register closed with a muster, the whole people under arms in the field before the city and drawn up by centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A boar, a ram and a bull were led three times round the army and sacrificed to Mars, whose field it was, and that purification gave the ceremony its name, the lustrum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy puts the count at 80,000 and says Fabius Pictor, the oldest of the Roman historians, took it for the number who could bear arms, while Dionysius gives 84,700 from the censors' own records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Republic the consuls took the register until 443 BCE, when it passed to two censors of its own, an office that carried with it the <i>regimen morum</i>, the oversight of how citizens lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lustrum was meant to fall in every fifth year and gave its name to that span, though between 443 and 294 BCE only 21 were performed where 30 would have been regular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.22, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#22. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Censor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 260-66, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Censor.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Lustrum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 719-20, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Lustrum.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n3.jpg/1920px-Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n3.jpg","title":"The victims led round the army: ram, boar and bull","desc":"Right panel of the census frieze from the Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, marble, late 2nd century BCE, from the Campus Martius, now in the Louvre. Attendants lead a ram and a boar past soldiers with oval shields, the bull's hindquarters at the left edge. This is the triple offering Livy and Dionysius put at the close of the count.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n3.jpg","alt":"Marble relief of two half-dressed attendants leading a fleecy ram and a bristled boar, with helmeted soldiers holding tall oval shields behind them."},"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the registration end with the whole people under arms?","a":"Because a boar, a ram and a bull were led three times round the army drawn up by centuries and sacrificed to Mars, and that purification gave the ceremony its name."},{"q":"Why did a man declare his patron's name if he had one?","a":"Because a freedman's standing had to be recorded with him, and freedmen were made to declare with everyone else and put into the four city tribes."},{"q":"Why is the five-year interval a nominal one?","a":"Because between 443 and 294 BCE only 21 lustra were performed where 30 would have been regular."}]},
{"id":"rm-074","num":74,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Roman writers make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the seventh and last king, who took the throne by violence, kept a bodyguard about him, and let the senate dwindle by filling no vacancy in it.","questions":["Failing to storm Gabii, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent his son Sextus there as a pretended refugee, and answered his request for orders by striking the heads off the tallest poppies in his garden.","The spoils of Suessa Pometia gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the forty talents of silver he set aside for the temple of Jupiter, and the Volscian war he began ran on for two centuries."],"answer":"Tarquinius Superbus","answerText":"Tarquinius Superbus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 years, the last of the seven, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 244 years of kings, by Livy's count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Volscian war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened by him, over 200 years long</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","history","italy","iron age","warfare"],"abstract":"<b>Tarquinius Superbus</b> is the seventh and last king of Rome in Roman tradition, who took the throne by violence after the murder of Servius Tullius and reigned without being elected by the people or confirmed by the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says he earned the name by refusing his father-in-law burial, on the plea that Romulus had none, and by killing the leading men he suspected of favouring Servius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius draws the same surname from his bearing: he gave audience only to those he had sent for, did his business at home before a few intimates, and decided disputes by his mood rather than by law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both make the bodyguard the mark of the reign, a company of armed men, Roman and foreign, camped about the palace at night and attending him by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He tried capital cases without assessors, which let him kill, banish or fine anyone he suspected or disliked, or merely hoped to squeeze money out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He let the senate dwindle by filling no vacancy, so that its numbers and its standing fell together, and was the first king to make war, peace, treaties and alliances without asking it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius adds that he had the tables of Servius's laws taken out of the Forum and destroyed, abolished the tax based on the rating, and made the poorest citizen pay as much as the richest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was no mean soldier for all that: he opened the long war with the Volsci, took Suessa Pometia, and set aside the forty talents of silver from its spoils for a temple to Jupiter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gabii he took by fraud instead, sending his son Sextus there as a pretended refugee from his cruelty until the town made him its commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked for orders, the king walked in his garden and struck the heads off the tallest poppies with his stick, and after 25 years he was shut out of the city and the kingship was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.49, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D49. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.53, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.54, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.41, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#41. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.43, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#43. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Lawrence_Alma-Tadema_11.jpeg","title":"Tarquinius Superbus striking the poppy heads","desc":"Lawrence Alma-Tadema, 1867, oil on panel, private collection. The king walks in his garden with his stick while his son's messenger waits for an answer, the scene Livy gives at 1.54, in which the silent gesture told Sextus what to do with the leading men of Gabii.","credit":"Lawrence Alma-Tadema, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lawrence_Alma-Tadema_11.jpeg","alt":"Painting of a man in dark red robes holding a staff among tall red poppies, two figures in white watching him from the left, one carrying a tray."},"locator":{"name":"Gabii","at":[12.7158,41.8869]},"why":[{"q":"Why is his reign called unlawful rather than merely harsh?","a":"Because he took the throne by violence after a murder and reigned without being elected by the people or confirmed by the senate."},{"q":"Why did the senate's standing fall with its numbers?","a":"Because he filled no vacancy and was the first king to make war, peace, treaties and alliances without asking it."},{"q":"Why did he answer his son's request for orders with a stick?","a":"Because walking in his garden he struck the heads off the tallest poppies, which told Sextus at Gabii to remove the leading men without a word being written down."}]},
{"id":"rm-075","num":75,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Digging the foundations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned up a human head with the face perfect, which the soothsayers read as a sign that the place would be the head of all the world.","questions":["Vowed by one Tarquin and built by another, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held three shrines side by side under one pediment, Juno on one hand, Minerva on the other and Jupiter in the middle.","Terminus alone refused to be moved from the ground cleared for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Romans read the omen to mean that the bounds of their empire would stand firm for ever."],"answer":"Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus","answerText":"Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vowed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tarquinius Priscus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 3rd consulship, by the Republic</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 shrines under one pediment, sides near 200 feet</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus</b> was the great Capitoline temple of Rome, vowed by Tarquinius Priscus and built by Tarquinius Superbus on the height Livy calls the Tarpeian Mount.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dionysius says the elder Tarquin vowed temples to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva in a Sabine war and squared off the peak with retaining walls and banks of earth, but did not live to raise them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To free the ground for one deity the younger king deconsecrated the shrines already standing on it, some of them vowed by Titus Tatius in his battle with Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The omens allowed every removal but one: Terminus would not be called out from his borders, which was read to mean that the bounds of the empire would stand firm for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Digging the foundations then turned up a human head with the face still perfect, and the soothsayers of the city and of Etruria alike gave out that the place would be the head of all the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius has the Capitol take its name from that head, the Romans calling heads <i>capita</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it cost is already in dispute in Livy, who prefers Fabius's forty talents from the spoils of Pometia to Piso's forty thousand pounds of silver, and says the money barely paid for the foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Workmen were fetched from all Etruria, the treasury met the bills, and the plebs were compelled to labour on it besides serving in the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king was driven out before the work was done, and Dionysius says the Roman people finished it in the third consulship of the Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He makes it a high base some 800 feet in circuit with its sides close to 200, holding three shrines side by side under one pediment, Juno on one hand, Minerva on the other and Jupiter between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.59, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#59. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.61, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#61. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.53, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg/1920px-Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg","title":"Sacrifice before the Capitoline temple","desc":"Marble panel from a lost arch of Marcus Aurelius, later 2nd century CE, now in the Capitoline Museums. The emperor pours the offering while the bull is brought up, and behind them stands the temple front, with figures in the pediment. The building shown is a later rebuilding on the same foundations, not the temple the Tarquins raised.","credit":"Photograph by MatthiasKabel, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg","alt":"Marble relief of a veiled man pouring an offering, attendants and a bull beside him, with a columned temple front and a sculpted pediment behind."},"locator":{"name":"Temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did older shrines have to be deconsecrated first?","a":"Because the ground was to be freed for one deity, and some of the shrines standing on it had been vowed by Titus Tatius in his battle with Romulus."},{"q":"Why was one god's refusal to move read as a good sign?","a":"Because Terminus would not be called out from his borders, which was taken to mean that the bounds of the empire would stand firm for ever."},{"q":"Why is the cost of the building already disputed in Livy?","a":"Because he prefers Fabius's forty talents from the spoils of Pometia to Piso's forty thousand pounds of silver, and says the money barely paid for the foundations."}]},
{"id":"rm-076","num":76,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A stranger offered nine of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Tarquin, burned three when he refused her price, burned three more when he laughed, and got that price for the last three.","questions":["Kept in a stone chest under the Capitoline temple, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were opened only at the senate's order, when a prodigy or a disaster called for the gods to be appeased.","It was on the instruction of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Rome held its first lectisternium during a plague and later sent to Epidaurus to fetch Aesculapius."],"answer":"Sibylline Books","answerText":"Sibylline Books","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Acquired</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under the Tarquins, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Books</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 offered, 6 burned, 3 bought</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Keepers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 men, later 10, then 15</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Sibylline Books</b> were a collection of oracles kept by the Roman state and opened, at the senate's order, when a prodigy or a disaster called for the gods to be appeased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aulus Gellius tells from the old annals how a stranger, an old woman unknown at Rome, brought nine books to Tarquin and asked a price he thought absurd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She burned three and asked the same sum for the six, and when he laughed the harder she burned three more and asked it again for the last three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius has the king call in the augurs, who told him he had thrown away a gift of the gods, so he paid in full for what was left; the woman handed the books over, bade him guard them, and was never seen again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Which Tarquin bought them is not agreed, some accounts naming Priscus and others Superbus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> They were kept underground in the temple of Jupiter on the Capitol, in a stone chest, and nobody but their keepers might look at them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those keepers grew with the state, from two men under the kings to ten and then fifteen, with public slaves to help them and no inspection allowed in the slaves' absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy shows what consulting them meant: a pestilence brought the first lectisternium at Rome, six gods propitiated for eight days on three couches spread with the finest coverlets to be had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another plague, and the books sent an embassy to Epidaurus to fetch Aesculapius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether they foretold anything or only prescribed what should be done is hard to say, since the originals burned with the temple and their replacements were scraped together from Italy, Greece and Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.62, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html#62. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 1.19, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/1*.html#19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.47, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Sibyllini Libri,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1043-44, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Sibyllini_Libri.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Nicolai_Abildgaard%2C_Tarquinius_og_sibyllen%2C_1809%2C_KMS3724%2C_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg","title":"Tarquin and the Sibyl","desc":"Nicolai Abildgaard, 1809, Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen. The woman stands with the surviving rolls under her arm while the wreathed king leans forward from his couch to take them, the moment at which he pays for three what he would not pay for nine.","credit":"Nicolai Abildgaard, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolai_Abildgaard,_Tarquinius_og_sibyllen,_1809,_KMS3724,_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg","alt":"Painting in muted tones of an old woman in a dark cloak holding rolled scrolls under one arm, a wreathed man leaning from a couch to grasp her arm."},"locator":{"name":"Capitoline Hill","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the king end up paying full price for a third of the books?","a":"Because he called in the augurs after two lots had been burned, and they told him he had thrown away a gift of the gods."},{"q":"Why were the keepers watched as closely as the books?","a":"Because nobody but them might look at the text, they had public slaves to help them, and no inspection was allowed in the slaves' absence."},{"q":"Why is it hard to say whether the books foretold anything?","a":"Because the originals burned with the temple and their replacements were scraped together from Italy, Greece and Asia Minor."}]},
{"id":"rm-077","num":77,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy sets the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the siege of Ardea, where princes praising their wives over wine rode to Rome to see how each was spending the evening.","questions":["Told that the mind sins and not the body, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that she acquitted herself of the fault but not of the penalty, and then took her own life.","Dionysius has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drive to Rome in black with a dagger hidden under her robes, where Livy instead has her send for her father and her husband."],"answer":"Lucretia","answerText":"Lucretia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Story set</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at the siege of Ardea, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consequence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the end of 244 years of kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accounts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, differing on where she died</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","italy","iron age","event"],"abstract":"<b>Lucretia</b> is the Roman noblewoman whose rape by the king's son and death by her own hand are, in Roman tradition, what brought the monarchy down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy sets the beginning of it at the siege of Ardea, where the princes fell to praising their own wives over wine and rode to Rome to see how each was passing the evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They found the king's daughters-in-law at their feasting and Lucretia at her wool with her maids about her, late at night in the hall at Collatia, and gave her the prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius tells it without any wager, having Sextus lodge at Collatia in the house of his kinsman while on his father's military business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both accounts agree on what followed: he was received as a guest, and went armed to her room while the household slept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In Livy, when the threat of death would not move her, he threatened to kill her and lay a slave's body beside hers so that it might be called adultery, and by that threat he had his way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She sent for her father and her husband, each with one friend he could trust, and told them plainly what had been done and by whom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They urged that it is the mind that sins and not the body, and that where there has been no consent there is no guilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She answered that she acquitted herself of the fault but not of the penalty, so that no unchaste woman should live by her example, and stabbed herself with a knife she had hidden in her dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius has her drive to Rome instead, in black with a dagger under her robes, tell her story to the leading men in her father's house and die there in his arms, at which those present cried that they would sooner die a thousand deaths than bear such things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.57, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.58, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.64, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#64. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.66, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#66. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.67, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#67. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Sandro_Botticelli_078.jpg/1920px-Sandro_Botticelli_078.jpg","title":"Lucretia's body in the square","desc":"Sandro Botticelli, from his panel telling the story of Lucretia. Her body has been carried out and laid on a bier under a triumphal arch; a man stands over it on the plinth with a drawn sword while the armed crowd gathers, which is where the Roman accounts turn from her death to the overthrow of the kings.","credit":"Sandro Botticelli, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandro_Botticelli_078.jpg","alt":"Painting of a woman's body laid out on a low bier in a public square, a man with a raised sword standing above her on a column base, armed men crowding in on both sides."},"locator":{"name":"Collatia","at":[12.6666,41.9251]},"why":[{"q":"Why did a threat of disgrace succeed where a threat of death failed?","a":"Because he threatened to kill her and lay a slave's body beside hers so that it might be called adultery, and by that threat he had his way."},{"q":"Why did she reject the argument her own family pressed on her?","a":"Because although they urged that where there has been no consent there is no guilt, she acquitted herself of the fault but not of the penalty, so that no unchaste woman should live by her example."},{"q":"Why do the two accounts of the visit differ at the start?","a":"Because Livy sets it up as a wager among the princes over their wives, while Dionysius has Sextus lodge in his kinsman's house on his father's military business."}]},
{"id":"rm-078","num":78,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy dates the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from an oath sworn over the knife drawn from Lucretia's wound, that those swearing would suffer nobody to reign at Rome again.","questions":["Dionysius has the king ride for the city during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only to find the gates shut and the battlements full of armed men, and turn back to a camp already lost.","The two magistrates first chosen at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were named to exercise the power of the kings, under a title Dionysius glosses for his Greek readers as counsellors."],"answer":"expulsion of the kings","answerText":"expulsion of the kings","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">244 years of kings, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tarquinius Superbus, 25 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, chosen by the centuries</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>expulsion of the kings</b> is the rising that Roman tradition places at the end of Tarquinius Superbus's reign and treats as the beginning of the Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy has the men standing over Lucretia's body swear, on the knife drawn from her wound, that they would drive out the king and his house and suffer nobody else to reign at Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They carried her body down into the forum at Collatia, set a guard on the gates so that no word should reach the king, and marched under arms to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There Brutus, who happened to hold the office of tribune of the celeres, summoned the people and spoke of Sextus, of citizens set to clearing out ditches and sewers, and of the murder of Servius Tullius and the chariot driven over his body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crowd voted to strip the king of his sovereignty and to banish him with his wife and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius gives the same programme in constitutional form: a vote of banishment against Tarquin and his posterity for ever from the city and from Roman territory, then an interrex to nominate magistrates and the centuriate assembly to accept or refuse them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Spurius Lucretius, the dead woman's father, was made interrex, and Brutus and Collatinus were the men he named to exercise the power the kings had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their office was to be called <i>consules</i>, a word Dionysius explains to his Greek readers as meaning counsellors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tarquin, hearing what was happening, rode for the city and found the gates shut and the walls full of armed men, so turned back to a camp the consuls' letters had reached before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy closes the reign at 25 years and the whole age of kings at 244, and has the first two consuls elected in the assembly of centuries by the prefect of the city, on the regulations of Servius Tullius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.59, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.75, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#75. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.76, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#76. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.85, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#85. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Gavin_Hamilton_-_The_Death_of_Lucretia_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Gavin_Hamilton_-_The_Death_of_Lucretia_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The oath over Lucretia's body","desc":"Gavin Hamilton, oil on canvas. Brutus holds up the knife drawn from the wound while the other three lay hands on it and swear, and Lucretia is supported at the left. Livy makes this oath the beginning of the rising.","credit":"Gavin Hamilton, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gavin_Hamilton_-_The_Death_of_Lucretia_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"Painting of four men in Roman dress reaching towards a raised dagger held by a bearded man in armour, a dying woman supported at the left of the group."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was a guard set on the gates at Collatia?","a":"Because no word of what had happened was to reach the king before the armed party had marched on Rome."},{"q":"Why could Brutus summon the people at all?","a":"Because he happened to hold the office of tribune of the celeres, which gave him the standing to call an assembly."},{"q":"Why does the tradition give the new office a name meaning counsellors?","a":"Because the power the kings had held was now to be exercised by two men together, and Dionysius explains consules to his Greek readers in that sense."}]},
{"id":"rm-079","num":79,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keep up the appearance of an idiot under his uncle's rule, and not even object to the nickname that the pretence earned him.","questions":["Sent to Delphi with the king's sons, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Apollo a rod of gold hidden inside a hollow staff of cornel wood, as an emblem of what he was.","Knowing him at a distance by his escort of lictors, Arruns Tarquinius rode at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and each drove his spear through the other's shield so that both fell dying."],"answer":"Lucius Junius Brutus","answerText":"Lucius Junius Brutus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">one of the first 2, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">annual, where the 7 kings ruled for life</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 1st war against the exiled king</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"<b>Lucius Junius Brutus</b> is the man Roman tradition credits with leading the rising against Tarquinius Superbus and with holding, alongside Collatinus, the first of the annual magistracies that replaced the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy makes him the son of the king's sister, says his brother was killed on his uncle's orders, and has him take refuge in the appearance of an idiot rather than trust laws that gave him no protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius has the king put both his father and his elder brother to death for the family's inherited wealth, and derives the surname itself from the folly he put on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sent to Delphi in the company of the king's sons, more as their butt than their companion, he carried a rod of gold inside a hollow staff of cornel wood and gave it to Apollo as an emblem of himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle promised supreme power at Rome to whichever of them first kissed his mother, and Brutus, taking the words another way, pretended to stumble on landing and kissed the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He held the office of tribune of the celeres when Lucretia died, and it was he who drew the knife from the wound and put the oath to the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy dates the beginning of liberty from this time less because the consuls' power was smaller than a king's than because it was limited to a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As consul he had to preside over the punishment of his own sons for conspiring to bring the Tarquins back, and Livy notes that the office laid on a father the duty of seeing the sentence carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Arruns Tarquinius knew him at a distance by his escort of lictors and rode at him crying that here went the man who had driven them out, proud in their own insignia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each drove his spear through the other's shield, and both fell dying from their horses, so that Brutus did not outlive the first war of the Republic he had made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.59, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.5, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.68, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#68. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Capitoline_Brutus_Musei_Capitolini_MC1183.jpg/1920px-Capitoline_Brutus_Musei_Capitolini_MC1183.jpg","title":"The Capitoline Brutus","desc":"Bronze head with inlaid eyes, in the Capitoline Museums in Rome, set on a bust added long afterwards. It has been called Brutus since the 16th century, but nothing on it or in any ancient writer names the man, and the identification is a modern convention rather than evidence.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Capitoline_Brutus_Musei_Capitolini_MC1183.jpg","alt":"Dark bronze portrait head of a bearded man with inlaid eyes, mounted on a draped bust, against a patterned yellow wall."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did he take refuge in the appearance of an idiot?","a":"Because his brother had been killed on his uncle's orders and he could not trust laws that gave him no protection."},{"q":"Why did the oracle's answer go to him rather than the princes?","a":"Because it promised supreme power to whichever first kissed his mother, and he took the words another way, pretending to stumble on landing and kissing the earth."},{"q":"Why does Livy date liberty from this magistracy?","a":"Because the consuls' power was not smaller than a king's so much as limited to a year."}]},
{"id":"rm-080","num":80,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Because certain sacrifices had been the king's to perform in person, the Republic made a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to carry them out, under the chief pontiff so that he should be king in name only.","questions":["Dionysius puts in Brutus's mouth the proposal for a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the royal name might be kept for form's sake by a man holding it for life and doing nothing else.","Exempt from every military and civil duty and barred from any other office, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was inaugurated like a flamen rather than elected like a magistrate."],"answer":"rex sacrorum","answerText":"rex sacrorum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at the end of the kings, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">to keep the royal name for form's sake</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 only, the public sacrifices</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["title","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>rex sacrorum</b> was the Roman priest who took over the sacrifices the kings had made in person, created when the monarchy ended so that the gods should lose nothing by the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says a king for sacrifices was appointed to supply the place of the kings in those duties, and that his office was made subordinate to the pontifex maximus lest he should be king in anything more than name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy adds his own view that the Romans went to unreasonable lengths in devising safeguards for their liberty, down to the smallest points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius puts the proposal in Brutus's mouth, arguing that the name of the kingly power, which had entered the state under favourable auguries, should be preserved for form's sake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man was to hold the honour for life, exempt from all military and civil duties, and, like the king at Athens, to exercise this single function and no other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was not elected as a magistrate was but inaugurated, as the flamens were, in the assembly of the curiae that the pontiffs called for the purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aulus Gellius preserves the rule in the old jurists' words, that the calate assembly was summoned for the inaugurating of a king or of flamens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was called to the meetings of the college of pontiffs on occasion, and his residence stood on the Sacred Way beside the chief pontiff's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So the one thing of the monarchy that Rome kept was the title, fenced about on every side: no command, no other office, no part in affairs, and a superior in the college.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy's word for him is <i>rex sacrificulus</i> and Dionysius calls him the king of sacred rites, and <i>rex sacrorum</i> is the title that became usual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.74, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4D*.html#74. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Pontifex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 939-42, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Pontifex.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Rex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 991-94, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Rex.html. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 15.27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/15*.html#27. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Bucchero_rex_sacrorum_2.jpg/1920px-Bucchero_rex_sacrorum_2.jpg","title":"A bucchero cup scratched REX","desc":"Fragment of a bucchero vessel found at the Regia in the Roman Forum, end of the 6th century BCE, Museo Nazionale Romano at the Baths of Diocletian. The graffito names the owner as the rex, which the museum reads as either one of the last kings or the priest who took the king's rites over.","credit":"Photograph by Juliana Bastos Marques, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucchero_rex_sacrorum_2.jpg","alt":"Dark grey pottery fragment on a clear stand, with faint scratched letters running across its surface."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4864,41.8919],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the office created at all?","a":"Because the kings had made certain sacrifices in person, and a priest was needed to supply their place so that the gods should lose nothing by the change."},{"q":"Why was it placed under the chief pontiff?","a":"Because he was not to be king in anything more than name, so the title was kept and every power taken away from it."},{"q":"Why was he inaugurated rather than elected?","a":"Because he was a priest and not a magistrate, so the pontiffs called an assembly of the curiae for the purpose, as they did for the flamens."}]},
{"id":"rm-081","num":81,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Each of the thirty divisions of the Roman people carried one vote in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, settled by a majority of its own members, so that sixteen of them carried the whole.","questions":["Gellius keeps the old jurists' rule that where the vote is taken by kinds of men the assembly is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, summoned by a lictor where the centuries were summoned by a horn.","By the Punic Wars the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had become a form, with thirty lictors performing the ceremony in place of the thirty divisions that no longer came."],"answer":"comitia curiata","answerText":"comitia curiata","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Divisions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 curiae, 16 of them a majority</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Superseded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the centuries, under the 6th king</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"The <b>comitia curiata</b> was the oldest of the Roman assemblies, in which citizens voted not as individuals but in the thirty curiae into which the people were divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has Romulus make that division after the peace with the Sabines and fix the women's names to the curiae, though tradition did not remember whether the thirty were picked for age, for distinction, or by lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gellius keeps the old jurists' definition, that where the vote is taken by kinds of men the assembly is curiate, and the detail that a lictor called it where a horn-blower called the centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each curia carried one collective vote, settled by a majority of its own members, so that sixteen curiae carried the assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was done when fifteen stood against fifteen is not recorded anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius says that in this assembly the citizen of smallest means had an equal vote with the greatest, and that the poor, being far more numerous, carried what they pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy makes the same point about the old order, which Servius replaced with a graduation that left nobody openly disfranchised while putting the decision with the wealthiest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The curiae kept business of their own: admission to a curia, the wills made before the assembly when it was called for sacred business, and the adrogation of a man into another family under the pontiffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They also passed the <i>lex curiata de imperio</i>, a second meeting at which a magistrate already chosen was formally invested and given the right to hold assemblies himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the Punic Wars the thing was a form: the curiae no longer came, and thirty lictors performed the ceremony in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Noctes Atticae</i> 15.27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Gellius/15*.html#27. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Comitia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 329-42, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Comitia.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg/1920px-Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg","title":"Three lictors with their fasces","desc":"Stone relief from Iulia Concordia, National Museum of Concordia at Portogruaro. Lictors attended the magistrates, and it was a lictor who called the curiate assembly together, where a horn-blower called the centuries; by the late Republic thirty lictors were performing the assembly's business in place of the thirty curiae.","credit":"Photograph by Following Hadrian, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors,_National_Museum_of_Concordia,_Portogruaro,_Italy.jpg","alt":"Carved stone panel showing three men in togas walking in step, each carrying a bundled rod over his shoulder."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.485,41.8928],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why could sixteen units carry the whole assembly?","a":"Because each curia cast one collective vote settled by a majority of its own members, so a majority of the thirty decided the question."},{"q":"Why did the poor prevail in this assembly and not in the later one?","a":"Because here the citizen of smallest means had an equal vote with the greatest and the poor were far more numerous, where the centuriate order graded the vote by property."},{"q":"Why did thirty lictors come to stand in for the people?","a":"Because by the Punic Wars the curiae no longer attended, so the ceremony investing a magistrate was performed by lictors in their place."}]},
{"id":"rm-082","num":82,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Romulus created a hundred of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Livy says, either because the number was adequate or because there were only a hundred heads of houses to be found.","questions":["When Romulus died the hundred <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled alone, dividing into ten decuries and passing the insignia between them five days at a time for a year.","Brutus filled the thinned order with leading equestrians called conscripti, where the older members kept the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the house was afterwards addressed by both words together."],"answer":"patres","answerText":"patres","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 by Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grew to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200, then 300 under the 5th king</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refilled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 300 by Brutus, with conscripti</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","title"],"abstract":"The <b>patres</b> were the hundred senators Roman tradition has Romulus create, the fathers whose descendants were called patricians and whose council grew into the Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy is not sure why a hundred: either the number was adequate, he says, or there were only a hundred heads of houses who could be made senators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the kings the body was the king's own council, its members appointed by him and offering advice that he could follow or reject as he pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew with the city, to two hundred when the Sabine tribe was joined to the Ramnes and to three hundred when the third tribe came in, which Livy and Dionysius both place under Tarquinius Priscus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the death of Romulus it governed alone: the hundred divided into ten decuries, one man from each holding power five days at a time, with the insignia and the lictors passing between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That year without a king is where the interregnum got its name, and the name long outlasted the arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tarquinius Superbus thinned the order by killing senators and filling no vacancy, so one of Brutus's first acts as consul was to bring it back to three hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He took the new men from the leading citizens of equestrian rank, and they were known as <i>conscripti</i> where the older members kept the name <i>patres</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From that came the way of addressing the whole house, <i>patres conscripti</i>, which is <i>patres et conscripti</i>, the fathers and the enrolled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy says the measure did wonders for harmony in the state and brought patricians and plebeians together, and the figure of three hundred then stood for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.17, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Senatus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1013-22, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Senatus.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Rex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 991-94, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Rex.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Togato_Barberini.jpg/1280px-Togato_Barberini.jpg","title":"A Roman carrying his ancestors","desc":"The Togatus Barberini, a marble statue of a man in a toga holding a portrait head of an ancestor in each hand. Carrying the likenesses of one's forebears was how a Roman family displayed its standing, and it is the same word for father that gave the senators their name.","credit":"Photograph by Carlo Dell'Orto, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Togato_Barberini.jpg","alt":"Marble statue of a standing man in a heavy toga, holding a carved portrait head at waist height in each hand."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4854,41.8929],"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why is Livy unsure about the number a hundred?","a":"Because either the number was adequate, he says, or there were only a hundred heads of houses who could be made senators."},{"q":"Why does a year without a king give a word to Roman politics?","a":"Because the hundred divided into ten decuries and held power five days at a time, and the name interregnum long outlasted the arrangement."},{"q":"Why is the house addressed as fathers and enrolled?","a":"Because Brutus brought the order back to three hundred with men of equestrian rank, who were called conscripti while the older members kept the name patres."}]},
{"id":"rm-083","num":83,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Livy says Romulus made a hundred senators called <i>patres</i>, and that their descendants were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the hereditary order that held Rome's priesthoods and auspices.","questions":["When tribunes demanded plebeian consuls, the consuls answered that only <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could take the auspices, since Rome's dealings with the gods belonged to that one order.","So rarely was an outsider admitted by a curiate law that fewer than fifty families of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived by the end of the Republic, and Caesar had to make more."],"answer":"patricians","answerText":"patricians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traced to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Houses added</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Tullus Hostilius, c. 673 – 642 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Families left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">fewer than 50 by the end of the Republic</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>patricians</b> were Rome's hereditary aristocracy, the families descended from the <i>patres</i>, and they alone could take the auspices by which the state asked the gods whether to act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dionysius says Romulus separated the men eminent for birth, approved for virtue and wealthy for those times from the obscure, the lowly and the poor, calling the first fathers and the second plebeians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers disagreed about the name: some took <i>patres</i> from their being fathers of families, some from their age, some from their sharing out land with the poorer citizens, and most from the protection they gave the whole state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A hostile version held that only these men could name their fathers, and pointed to the heralds who summoned patricians by their own names and their fathers' while ox horns called the plebeians together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius rejects it, calling the herald a mark of honour and the horn one of speed, since a crowd cannot be named man by man in a short time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The order was built from the clans of three tribes, the Ramnes, the Tities and the Luceres, the last of them long unequal to the others and long called the fathers of the lesser clans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A king could enrol new houses: when Alba Longa fell, Tullus Hostilius brought the Tullii, the Servilii, the Quinctii, the Geganii, the Curiatii and the Cloelii into the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius has Romulus give this order the priesthoods, the magistracies and the courts while the plebeians farmed, herded and traded, and let every plebeian choose a patrician to protect him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Birth alone made a patrician and poverty could not unmake one; the only way out was adoption into a plebeian house, which meant leaving one's clan and its rites behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A curiate law could raise an outsider but seldom did, so fewer than fifty such families were left by the end of the Republic, and Caesar and then Augustus made new ones to fill the old priesthoods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.8, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#8. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.9, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#9. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Patricii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 875-77, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Patricii.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Fragment_from_the_Ara_Pacis_%28Altar_of_Peace%29%2C_between_13_and_9_BC%2C_discovered_on_the_Campus_Martius_in_Rome_in_1568%2C_RomeMoi%2C_Auguste%2C_Empereur_de_Rome_exhibition%2C_Grand_Palais%2C_Paris_-_14648840744.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Leading families in procession on the Ara Pacis","desc":"A fragment of the processional frieze of the Ara Pacis, carved between 13 and 9 BCE and found on the Campus Martius in 1568. The line is made up of members of the ruling house with priests, magistrates and senators, the children among them in the toga.","credit":"Photo by Following Hadrian, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A weathered marble relief of several adults in heavy draped togas walking in profile, with two children at knee height among them."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why could poverty not unmake one of these men?","a":"Because birth alone made a patrician, and the only way out was adoption into a plebeian house, which meant leaving one's clan and its rites behind."},{"q":"Why does Dionysius reject the hostile account of the name?","a":"Because the herald who summoned patricians by name was a mark of honour and the horn that called the plebeians one of speed, a crowd not being nameable man by man in a short time."},{"q":"Why did emperors have to create new patrician families?","a":"Because fewer than fifty were left by the end of the Republic, and the old priesthoods could be held by nobody else."}]},
{"id":"rm-084","num":84,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Shut out of the curiae, the old clans and every office, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were Rome's commons, citizens who fought in the army but could not marry a patrician.","questions":["When Alba fell its leading houses were made patrician and the rest were settled on the Caelian, which is where Rome's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first appear as an order of their own.","Ancus Marcius moved thousands of conquered Latins to Rome and gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Aventine to live on, after taking Politorium, Tellenae, Ficana and Medullia."],"answer":"plebeians","answerText":"plebeians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Distinct order</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from Tullus Hostilius, c. 673 – 642 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aventine given</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Ancus Marcius, c. 642 – 617 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Organised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 tribes under Servius Tullius, c. 578 – 535 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["people","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>plebeians</b> were the commons of Rome, citizens but not citizens in full right, and they stood outside the curiae, the old clans, the Senate and every civil and priestly office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their name carries the root of <i>impleo</i> and <i>compleo</i>, the filling of a space, and their citizenship was so thin that they are sometimes called <i>peregrini</i>, foreigners tolerated on condition of service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy and Dionysius put them at Rome from the beginning, among the crowd of free men and slaves who came to the asylum Romulus opened between the two groves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They appear as an order of their own under Tullus Hostilius: when Alba Longa was destroyed its leading houses were enrolled among the patricians and the rest were settled on the Caelian in submission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancus Marcius then took Politorium, Tellenae, Ficana and Medullia and moved many thousands of Latins to Rome, assigning them the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They were Latins, of the same stock as the Ramnes, and they far outnumbered the patricians, yet they belonged to none of the three tribes, none of the thirty curiae and none of the patrician clans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They fought in the army all the same, and had no right of marriage with a patrician, no appeal against a sentence, and no share of the public land beyond pasturing cattle on it for a fee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they did keep was their own rites, regulated by the patrician pontiffs, their own clans, and a father's power over his children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tarquinius Priscus meant to make new tribes of their leading families and name them after himself, but the augur Attus Navius held that nothing could be changed without the birds' consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Servius Tullius gave them an order of their own instead, four city districts and 26 in the country, each under a tribune with its own rites and assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Plebes or Plebs, Plebeii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.9, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/View_of_the_Caelian_Hill%2C_Rome%2C_with_the_church_of_Santi_Giovanni_e_Paolo%2C_seen_from_the_Aventine%2C_Rome_MET_DP873705.jpg/1920px-View_of_the_Caelian_Hill%2C_Rome%2C_with_the_church_of_Santi_Giovanni_e_Paolo%2C_seen_from_the_Aventine%2C_Rome_MET_DP873705.jpg","title":"The Caelian seen from the Aventine","desc":"A wash drawing of about 1750 by Joseph Vernet, looking across to the Caelian hill from the Aventine. Tradition settled the Albans on the first of those hills and the Latins Ancus Marcius brought to Rome on the second.","credit":"Joseph Vernet, c. 1750, Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"An ink and grey-wash drawing of low tiled roofs in the foreground and a further hill beyond, crowned by a bell tower and a long arcaded church, with open country in the distance."},"locator":{"name":"Aventine Hill","at":[12.4833,41.8833],"zoom":9,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why is their citizenship called thin?","a":"Because they stood outside the curiae, the old clans, the Senate and every civil and priestly office, with no right of marriage with a patrician and no appeal against a sentence."},{"q":"Why were people of the same stock kept outside the old order?","a":"Because they had come in as captured or transplanted populations settled on the Caelian and the Aventine, so they belonged to none of the three tribes, none of the thirty curiae and none of the clans."},{"q":"Why did an augur block their promotion under Tarquinius Priscus?","a":"Because the king meant to make new tribes of their leading families and name them after himself, and Attus Navius held that nothing could be changed without the birds' consent."}]},
{"id":"rm-085","num":85,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Every Roman <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had its own rites, a shrine to keep them in, and a name its members shared, ending in <i>-ius</i> for a man and <i>-ia</i> for a woman.","questions":["By the Twelve Tables the property of a man who died intestate with no agnates passed to his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the clan of families that bore his name and kept its rites together.","Adrogation took a man out of his <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and freed him of its rites, a renunciation made before the curiate assembly and known as the forswearing of the rites."],"answer":"gens","answerText":"gens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traced to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Romulus, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Divisions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 tribes, 30 curiae, 300 clans</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alban houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 taken in under Tullus Hostilius, c. 673 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"A <b>gens</b> was a Roman clan, a body of households that shared one name, one set of rites and a claim on one another's property, and the word holds the same element as the verb <i>gigno</i>, 'to beget', and means kin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scaevola the pontiff defined its members as those who bore the same name, were born free, had no slave among their forebears and had suffered no loss of civil standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Festus says a gens is made up of many households and Cincius that his own are the men called by his name; the shared name generally ended in <i>-ius</i> for a man and <i>-ia</i> for a woman, as in Julia and Cornelia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each had rites of its own, performed at set times at its own small shrine, and the pontiffs saw to it that they were not allowed to lapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They bound every member, so a man adopted, adrogated or set free from his father's power lost his clan and its rites together, the adrogated renouncing his before the curiate assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By the Twelve Tables the household of a man who died intestate and without agnates passed to his clansmen, as did the care of a madman left with no guardian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius has Romulus divide the people into three tribes, each tribe into ten curiae, and each curia into ten parts again, with the land split into thirty shares to match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> If those smallest parts are the clans, the arrangement gives a hundred to a tribe and three hundred in all, which is the figure the sources use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> To have one was long to be a patrician, and the kings took whole clans in: Tullus Hostilius enrolled six Alban houses, and the Claudii came from Sabine country under Attius Clausus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the law allowing the two orders to marry was put forward, the patricians complained that it would throw the rights of the houses into confusion, and some clans did come to hold plebeian families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Gens,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 567-70, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Gens.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 2.7, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/2A*.html#7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Patricii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 875-77, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Patricii.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Sarcophagus_of_Lucius_Cornelius_Scipio_Barbatus%2C_280_BC%2C_General_of_Third_Samnite_War_%28Consul_in_298_BC%29_%2848465463042%29.jpg/1920px-Sarcophagus_of_Lucius_Cornelius_Scipio_Barbatus%2C_280_BC%2C_General_of_Third_Samnite_War_%28Consul_in_298_BC%29_%2848465463042%29.jpg","title":"The sarcophagus of Scipio Barbatus","desc":"The stone coffin of Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbatus, consul in 298 BCE, now in the Vatican Museums, with two further inscribed slabs from the same tomb hung above it. The painted inscription opens with his name, and it is the clan name that comes first.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A large grey stone chest with a scrolled lid and a band of carved rosettes, its lower face cut with lines of Latin lettering picked out in red, standing against a dark red museum wall below two inscribed stone panels."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did adoption cost a man his clan?","a":"Because its rites bound every member, so a man adopted, adrogated or freed from his father's power lost the clan and its rites together, renouncing them before the curiate assembly."},{"q":"Why did the pontiffs concern themselves with private rites?","a":"Because each clan's rites were performed at set times at its own shrine, and the pontiffs saw to it that they were not allowed to lapse."},{"q":"Why did patricians object to marriage between the orders?","a":"Because they complained it would throw the rights of the houses into confusion, and some clans did come to hold plebeian families."}]},
{"id":"rm-086","num":86,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"The third of a Roman's names, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marked his household within the clan and came from a peculiarity of body or mind, or from something an ancestor had done.","questions":["Naso, Caecus, Cato, Capito, Brutus and Cicero are each a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the hereditary third name that told one household of a clan from another.","A Roman was known by a given name, a clan name and a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a fourth might be added for a victory, as Africanus was to Scipio."],"answer":"cognomen","answerText":"cognomen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Order</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 names, with a 4th for an honour</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named on</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th day for a boy, 8th for a girl</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lucumo renamed under Ancus, c. 642 – 617 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"A <b>cognomen</b> was the third of a Roman's names, marking the household he belonged to inside his clan, where the first name was his own and the second his clan's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That first name was one of only about thirty Varro counted in use, so it did little to tell one man from another and the two names after it did the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In early times a boy was given his when he came of age, an age Gellius reports the Romans counted as running to seventeen, and later custom moved the naming to the ninth day after birth, the eighth for a girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The clan name generally ended in <i>-ius</i>, and a woman bore only its feminine form, so every daughter of the Cornelii was Cornelia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two names were enough to designate a man and were all that many plebeians had, among them Gaius Marius, Quintus Sertorius and Gnaeus Pompeius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The third name came from a peculiarity of body or mind, or from some deed of the man reckoned the household's founder, and Naso, Caecus, Capito, Cato, Brutus, Scipio and Cicero are all of that kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most were hereditary and ran on to the last members of a household, though a few ended with the man they had been given to for a particular reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fourth name was an honour for something done — Africanus, Asiaticus, Macedonicus — voted sometimes by the people in assembly and sometimes simply taken by the man himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order was fixed at given name, clan name, household name and honour, and to take a clan or household name that was not yours was a forgery at law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An outsider took the whole apparatus on: the Etruscan Lucumo entered Rome and gave his name as Lucius Tarquinius Priscus, and the Sabine Attius Clausus was known there as Appius Claudius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Nomen,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 800-803, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Nomen.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Gens,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 567-70, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Gens.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 10.28, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/10*.html#28. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Funerary_inscription%2C_Epitaph_of_Quintus_Iulius_Quinti_%28CIL_08%2C_03151%29%2C_soldier_of_the_Third_Legion_Augusta%2C_lived_28_years._Inscription_from_Lambaesis_%28Numidia%29%2C_Lambaesis_Museum%2C_Algeria.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"A Roman name cut in full on a gravestone","desc":"The tombstone of a soldier of the Third Legion Augusta, from Lambaesis in Numidia. The second line reads Q · IVLIVS · Q · F · POL and the third begins FORTVNATVS: the given name, the clan name, his father's name, his voting tribe and last the household name.","credit":"Photo by Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall sandstone slab standing in a garden, carved at the top with the bust of a bearded man in profile and below it seven lines of Latin capitals."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the first name do so little work?","a":"Because Varro counted only about thirty in use, so the clan name and the household name after it had to distinguish one man from another."},{"q":"Why did some of these names die with the man who bore them?","a":"Because most were hereditary and ran on through a household, while a few had been given for a particular reason and ended there."},{"q":"Why was taking another family's name a matter for the courts?","a":"Because the order of given name, clan name, household name and honour was fixed, and to take a clan or household name not yours was a forgery at law."}]},
{"id":"rm-087","num":87,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"The augurs defined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a strip running the whole circuit of the city, marked by boundary stones, inside which alone the auspices of the city could be taken.","questions":["Ploughed with a bull and a cow and lifted where the gates were to stand, the furrow of a new town left the line called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, within which the auspices were taken.","Only a man who had taken land from an enemy might enlarge the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and even so the Aventine was left outside it until Claudius brought it in."],"answer":"pomerium","answerText":"pomerium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First line</span><span class=\"dt-v\">round the Palatine, after 753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Extended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Servius Tullius, c. 578 – 535 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aventine in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Claudius, 49 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","religion","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>pomerium</b> was Rome's sacred boundary, a line marked by stones set at intervals that fixed the limit within which the auspices of the city could be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The augurs' own books on the auspices define it as the space designated along the whole circuit of the city, outside the walls and marked off by fixed bounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name joins <i>post</i> and <i>murus</i>, behind the wall, though Livy would rather call it a going-round of the wall, since the strip lay on both sides and neither could be said to be behind the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A town was founded by yoking a bull and a cow to a plough and drawing a furrow around the site with the clods all falling inward, the little mound they made standing for the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At three points the plough was carefully lifted and carried a few feet, and those breaks marked where the gates would stand, three being what Etruscan ritual required of any new town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The strip itself might be neither built on nor ploughed, and as the city grew the boundary stones were carried forward to keep pace with the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gellius says the oldest line ran round the foot of the Palatine, while Tacitus describes a wider circuit from the Forum Boarium round the hill and up to the shrine of the Lares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Servius Tullius took in the Quirinal, the Viminal and the Esquiline, having first consulted the auspices as the law required, and Dionysius calls him the last king to widen the city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The right to move the line belonged only to a man who had enlarged Roman territory at an enemy's expense, and that was the ground Sulla claimed when he asked for the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Aventine stayed outside it for centuries, Messala holding that Remus had taken his auspices there and found the birds against him, until Claudius at last brought the hill within.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 13.14, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/13*.html#14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Pomoerium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 936-37, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Pomoerium.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Pomerium,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 392-96, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Pomerium.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4A*.html#13. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Cippus_of_the_pomerium_Claudius_Musei_Vaticani_inv.9268.jpg/1920px-Cippus_of_the_pomerium_Claudius_Musei_Vaticani_inv.9268.jpg","title":"A boundary stone of the sacred line","desc":"A marker of the pomerium set up under Claudius, now in the Vatican Museums. The last lines record that, the boundaries of the Roman people having been enlarged, he widened the line and marked it out afresh.","credit":"Photo by Marie-Lan Nguyen, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A thick, pitted limestone slab with a shouldered top, cut with nine lines of worn Latin capitals, standing against a grey wall."},"locator":{"name":"pomerium","at":[12.4869,41.8883],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy dispute the obvious derivation of the name?","a":"Because the strip lay on both sides of the wall and neither could be said to be behind the other, so he would rather call it a going-round of the wall."},{"q":"Why were the gates not part of the consecrated line?","a":"Because the plough was lifted and carried a few feet at three points, and those breaks marked where the gates would stand."},{"q":"Why could only certain men move the boundary?","a":"Because the right belonged to a man who had enlarged Roman territory at an enemy's expense, which is the ground Sulla claimed when he asked for the honour."}]},
{"id":"rm-088","num":88,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Rome's civic centre, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as a marshy valley between the Palatine and the Capitol, with a burial ground on its edge, and had to be drained before it could be built on.","questions":["Tarquinius Priscus drained the low valleys into the Tiber and divided the ground round the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into building plots, where arcades and rows of shops went up.","Games, funerals, courts and assemblies all took place in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a levelled oblong of about seven <i>iugera</i> ringed by shops, temples and porticoes."],"answer":"Forum Romanum","answerText":"Forum Romanum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Valley</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a marsh below the Palatine, before c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drained</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Tarquinius Priscus, c. 616 – 579 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Extent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 iugera, on Varro's figure</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","history","italy","iron age","building"],"abstract":"The <b>Forum Romanum</b> was Rome's market place and afterwards its civic centre, the low ground between the Palatine and the Capitol where the city came to do its business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The valley began as a marsh crossed by a stream, which served as one of the defences of the Palatine settlement and kept it apart from the Capitol and the Quirinal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Geology bears the swamp out, and Ovid remembered it, writing that wet marshes held the ground where the fora now stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the edge of the valley lay a burial ground whose latest graves come down to the end of the 7th century or the middle of the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the Sabines drive the Romans in flight across the whole of the ground the Forum would later occupy, which is the oldest thing his account says of the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Tarquinius Priscus drained the low parts of the city round it by conduits emptying into the Tiber, and the graves went out of use at about the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same king divided the ground about the Forum into building plots, and arcades and rows of shops were put up along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One row, the old shops, stood on the south side facing away from the sun, and a newer row was later set opposite them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finished thing was a levelled oblong of about seven <i>iugera</i>, ringed by houses, temples, basilicas and porticoes, and it served both as a market and as a place of judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Games were held there at festivals and funerals from the earliest times and justice was administered there, so that it became the place people came for business, then for politics, and at last for idleness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Forum Romanum,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 230-36, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Forum_Romanum.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Forum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 546-48, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Forum.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Foro_Romano_visto_dai_Musei_Capitolini_Roma.jpg/1920px-Foro_Romano_visto_dai_Musei_Capitolini_Roma.jpg","title":"The Forum valley from the Capitoline","desc":"The low ground between the Capitol and the Palatine, seen from above. Almost everything standing here is Republican or later; what the card describes is the valley itself, which was a marsh before it was drained.","credit":"Photo by Wolfgang Moroder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A wide sunlit view down into a long open archaeological site: a triple arch at the left, rows of broken columns and paving beyond, and a tall group of columns at the right, with churches and modern buildings around the edges."},"locator":{"name":"Forum Romanum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was this valley not built on earlier?","a":"Because it began as a marsh crossed by a stream, which served as one of the defences of the Palatine settlement and kept it apart from the Capitol and the Quirinal."},{"q":"Why did the graves on its edge go out of use when they did?","a":"Because Tarquinius Priscus drained the low parts of the city round it by conduits emptying into the Tiber at about the same time."},{"q":"Why did one row of shops face away from the sun?","a":"Because the old shops stood on the south side of the levelled ground, with a newer row later set opposite them."}]},
{"id":"rm-089","num":89,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Under the black paving called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands a broken pillar whose inscription, the oldest Latin from Rome, seems to lay down rites for the king in the Comitium.","questions":["Festus says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marks a place of ill omen, meant for the death of Romulus though he was never buried there, while others name Faustulus or Hostilius.","Two pedestals beneath the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would suit a pair of recumbent lions, and between them sits a block on which the black stone itself may once have stood."],"answer":"Lapis Niger","answerText":"Lapis Niger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscription</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE, on the pillar beneath</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tufa column</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shrine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd half of the 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","history","italy","iron age","building"],"abstract":"The <b>Lapis Niger</b> is a paving of black marble in the Comitium, the open inaugurated ground before the senate house where Rome came together for its curiate assemblies and its lawsuits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Festus explains the black stone in the Comitium as marking a place of ill omen, set apart for the death of Romulus, though he says it never came to be used for his burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other accounts put in the grave instead Faustulus, who reared him, or Hostilius, the grandfather of King Tullus Hostilius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius knew of a stone lion in the chief part of the Forum beside the platform of the orators, said to have been set over the body of Faustulus, who was killed trying to part the quarrelling brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elsewhere he says Hostilius was buried by the kings in that same chief part of the Forum and honoured with a monument and an inscription to his courage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The pavement is Taenarian marble, about 4 by 3 metres (13 by 10 feet), laid level with the Comitium's own floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath it stands a group of far older monuments: a four-sided inscribed pillar with its top broken away, a conical column of tufa, and a small shrine resting on two long bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inscription is the oldest Latin writing known from Rome, and what survives of it appears to be a law about rites that the king, or those attending him, were to perform in the Comitium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much of the pillar is missing is not known, so nothing fuller can be argued from it, and the lettering is placed at about 500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two pedestals of the shrine would suit a pair of recumbent lions, and the block set between them may be where the black stone itself once stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Sepulchrum Romuli,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 482-84, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Sepulcrum_Romuli.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.87, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html#87. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 3.1, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/3A*.html#1. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Comitium,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 134-38, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Comitium.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Rostra,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 450-52, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Rostra.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Lapis_Niger_in_the_Roman_Forum_%28149201570%29.jpg","title":"The monuments under the black paving","desc":"An engraved view of the group uncovered below the Lapis Niger: the two long bases with their pedestals, the block set between them, the broken inscribed pillar, and the conical column of tufa at the right.","credit":"Christian Hülsen, public domain, via the National Library of Poland and Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A fine line engraving of a low platform of stone blocks seen from one corner, carrying two shaped pedestals with a block between them, a broken squared pillar, and a round tapering column standing on a separate base."},"locator":{"name":"Lapis Niger","at":[12.4847,41.8925],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the black paving read as a place of ill omen?","a":"Because Festus says it was set apart for the death of Romulus, though he adds that it never came to be used for his burial."},{"q":"Why can so little be argued from the inscription?","a":"Because how much of the pillar is missing is not known, so nothing fuller can be built on what survives, though the lettering is placed at about 500 BCE."},{"q":"Why do the ancient accounts of who lies there disagree?","a":"Because some put Faustulus in the grave and some Hostilius, the grandfather of King Tullus Hostilius, beside the tradition of Romulus."}]},
{"id":"rm-090","num":90,"category":"Rome under the kings","question":"Year by year the pontifex maximus set down what had happened, and that record, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ran from the beginning of the state until it was closed in 133 BCE.","questions":["Livy blames the obscurity of the earliest times on records few and scanty, and on the burning of the city, which destroyed the pontifical commentaries where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were kept.","Livy will not judge the truth of what is told of the city's beginnings, so for the kings we fall back on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and on writers five centuries later."],"answer":"annales maximi","answerText":"annales maximi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kept</span><span class=\"dt-v\">yearly by the pontifex maximus, to 133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 4th century BCE, with the archives</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First historians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Fabius and Cincius, 3rd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","italy","iron age","concept"],"abstract":"The <b>annales maximi</b> were the record of each year's events kept by the pontifex maximus, and they are the nearest thing Rome had to a contemporary account of its own early history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They ran from the beginning of the state down to the pontificate of Publius Mucius Scaevola in 133 BCE, and then the keeping of them stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nothing else was set down at the time, and Livy says that written records are the only trustworthy memorials of events and that in those days they were few and scanty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What did exist, in the pontifical commentaries and in the public and private archives, nearly all perished when the city was burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy therefore treats what is told of the age before the city was built as fitter to adorn a poet's work than a historian's, and refuses to argue that it is either true or false.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first Romans to write their own history were Quintus Fabius and Lucius Cincius, who lived through the Punic Wars, five centuries after the last king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both wrote in Greek, and Dionysius says each set out with great exactness the events he had been present at while touching only summarily on the early ones that followed the founding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius names the Roman authorities he himself built on — Cato, Fabius Maximus, Valerius Antias, Licinius Macer, the Aelii, the Gellii and the Calpurnii — and calls their works like the Greek annals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He came to Italy in the year Augustus ended the civil war, and spent 22 years learning the language and reading those writers before he began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the whole account of the kings reaches a reader through men writing long afterwards, out of a record that was thin to begin with and burnt besides.","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Pontifex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 939-42, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Pontifex.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i>, preface, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3Dpr. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.6, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1A*.html#6. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.7, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1A*.html#7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Livius_-_Ab_urbe_condita%2C_nel_anno_MCCCCLXXXXIII_adi_XI_del_mese_di_febraio_-_2399185_S.jpg/1920px-Livius_-_Ab_urbe_condita%2C_nel_anno_MCCCCLXXXXIII_adi_XI_del_mese_di_febraio_-_2399185_S.jpg","title":"Livy's first decade, printed at Venice in 1493","desc":"The end of Livy's preface at the left and the opening of his first book at the right, in an Italian translation printed at Venice in February 1493. The woodcut shows Aeneas received by King Latinus.","credit":"BEIC digital library, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A printed folio page in two columns of dense early Italian type, with decorated initial letters and a woodcut at the top right showing a group of robed figures meeting under arches."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4864,41.8919],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why is so little of Rome's early record contemporary?","a":"Because almost nothing else was set down at the time, and what did exist in the pontifical commentaries and the archives nearly all perished when the city was burned."},{"q":"Why does Livy refuse to argue about the age before the city?","a":"Because he thinks what is told of it fitter to adorn a poet's work than a historian's, and will not say that it is either true or false."},{"q":"Why is the first Roman history five centuries late?","a":"Because Fabius and Cincius lived through the Punic Wars, and each set out exactly the events he had been present at while touching only summarily on the early ones."}]},
{"id":"rm-091","num":91,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Rome drove out its last king and put in his place two magistrates elected each year, and so began the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lasted until the rule of the emperors.","questions":["Livy dates the liberty of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the moment the consul's power was limited to a single year, rather than from any weakening of what the kings had held.","Polybius could not say whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a monarchy, an aristocracy or a democracy, since the consuls, the senate and the people each made it look like one of the three."],"answer":"Roman Republic","answerText":"Roman Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 BCE, when Octavian became Augustus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 244 years, on Livy's count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 a year, each for one year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["era","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman Republic</b> was the government Rome set up when it drove out its last king and put annually appointed magistrates where he had been, the king having until then been the only magistrate and held all the power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy counts the whole of the regal government, from the founding of the city to its liberation, as 244 years, of which the last king reigned 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two consuls were then chosen in the assembly of centuries, on the arrangement Servius Tullius had made, and the first of them were Lucius Junius Brutus and Lucius Tarquinius Collatinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy refers the origin of liberty to this moment less because the power of the kings was weakened than because the consul held it for one year only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first consuls kept all the old jurisdiction and the old insignia, but one of them alone carried the rods, so that the sight of two sets of them should not terrify the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Brutus had the people swear that they would suffer no man to reign at Rome, and he called an assembly to rehearse that oath when his colleague's name began to be spoken of with suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the expulsion onwards two consuls were appointed every year, and other magistracies were added as time went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They came to be divided into the greater and the lesser: the consuls, the praetors and the censors were elected in the assembly of centuries and held the greater auspices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius, writing when the state was at its height, said it was impossible even for a Roman to pronounce with certainty whether the whole system was monarchical, aristocratic or democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fix the eye on the power of the consuls and it looked like a monarchy, on the senate and it looked aristocratic, on the power of the mass of citizens and it was plainly a democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#11. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Magistratus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 723-25, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Magistratus.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Anonymous_Rome_Cr198-1.jpg/1920px-Anonymous_Rome_Cr198-1.jpg","title":"A Republican denarius with the city's name on it","desc":"An anonymous silver denarius struck at Rome: the helmeted head of Roma with the mark of value at the left, and on the other side the Dioscuri riding with ROMA cut into a tablet below them. It carries no ruler's name, only the state's.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin against black: at the left a head in a winged helmet facing right, at the right two horsemen with spears galloping above a panel lettered ROMA."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Republic","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"kind":"region","label":[12.48,41.89],"area":[[-9.6,43.9],[-8,43.85],[-5,43.75],[-3.5,43.55],[-1.8,43.45],[-1.5,44.5],[-1.3,46.2],[-2.5,47.3],[-4.9,48.4],[-3,48.9],[-1.5,49.7],[0,49.65],[1.5,50.9],[2.5,51.25],[4.3,51.95],[5.9,51.8],[6.1,51],[6.6,50.4],[7.3,49.8],[7.9,49],[8.2,48.4],[7.6,47.6],[8.2,47.4],[9.4,47],[9.6,46.6],[10.4,46.4],[11.5,46.2],[12.4,46.4],[13.5,46.5],[14.2,46.1],[14.6,45.6],[15.8,45],[16.6,44.2],[17.7,43.4],[18.8,42.6],[19.5,41.9],[20.2,41.5],[20.7,41.8],[21.7,42],[22.6,42.1],[23.5,41.6],[24.6,41.5],[25.5,41.1],[26.2,40.6],[26.5,40.1],[27.5,40.8],[29,41.3],[31,41.4],[32.5,41.9],[34,42],[35.5,41.9],[36.5,41.3],[35.8,40.6],[34.5,40.6],[33,40.4],[32,40.3],[31.2,39.6],[30.6,38.8],[30.3,38],[30.8,37.2],[31.5,36.9],[33,36.3],[34.5,36.5],[35.6,36.7],[36.3,37.2],[37,37.2],[38,36.6],[38.6,35.8],[38,34.5],[36.5,33],[35.6,32.4],[35.2,32.5],[34.9,32],[34.3,31.2],[33.5,30],[33,28],[33.5,25],[33,23.9],[31.5,24],[29,26],[26.5,29],[25,31],[23.5,31.5],[22,31.2],[20.5,31.5],[19.5,30.8],[17.5,30.7],[15.5,31.5],[13.5,32.3],[11.8,32.6],[10.5,33.4],[9.5,34.3],[8.5,35.2],[7.5,35.6],[6.8,36.2],[6,36.9],[5.8,37.1],[3,37.6],[0,37.2],[-3,36.6],[-5.4,36.05],[-6.5,36.7],[-9.5,37]]},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy date liberty from the consulship rather than the expulsion?","a":"Because the power of the kings was not weakened so much as limited: the consul held it for one year only."},{"q":"Why did only one consul carry the rods?","a":"Because the sight of two sets of them was not to terrify the people, though the first consuls kept all the old jurisdiction and insignia."},{"q":"Why could Polybius not name the kind of constitution it was?","a":"Because the consuls made it look monarchical, the senate aristocratic and the power of the mass of citizens plainly democratic."}]},
{"id":"rm-092","num":92,"category":"The early Republic","question":"All the king's powers passed to the two <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the fall of the monarchy, except the priestly ones, which were kept apart and given to a rex sacrorum.","questions":["Twelve lictors carrying the fasces walked in single file before a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whenever he appeared in public, though inside the city the axes were taken out of the bundles.","If one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died in office the other had to call the assembly of centuries to elect a replacement, who held powers in no way inferior to his own."],"answer":"consul","answerText":"consul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Instituted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the fall of the kings, 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 men, for one year each</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 305 BCE; before that, praetores</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Year began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January, from 153 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["title","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"A <b>consul</b> was the highest magistrate of the Roman Republic, one of two who held the office together and laid it down again after a single year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is probably <i>con</i> with the root of <i>salio</i>, so that consuls are those who go together; in the earliest times the title was <i>praetores</i> instead, and <i>consules</i> came in only in 305 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the fall of the monarchy every power the king had held passed to them, except the one that had made him the state's high priest, which was kept apart and given to the rex sacrorum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were elected in the assembly of centuries, presided over by a consul or a dictator, or by an interrex where neither was to be had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The day on which they entered office moved again and again before it settled on the first of January in 153 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Within the city the whole internal machinery of the state was under their charge: they brought embassies before the senate, consulted it on urgent matters and carried out its decrees in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They summoned the assemblies of the people and presided over them, introduced measures and saw that what the people had decreed was done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twelve lictors with the fasces went before a consul in a line one behind another, and he never appeared in public without them, the insignia of the office being those the kings had used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Inside the city and a thousand paces around it the axes were taken out of the bundles, a change credited to Valerius Publicola, who carried the law giving a citizen the right of appeal from a magistrate to the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If a consul died or laid down office, his colleague had to call the assembly and have a successor chosen, and such a man held powers in no way inferior to those of the consul elected at the year's beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 352-57, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#12. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/M._Iunius_Brutus_Cr433-1.jpg/1920px-M._Iunius_Brutus_Cr433-1.jpg","title":"A consul walking between his lictors","desc":"A silver denarius struck at Rome in 54 BCE by the moneyer Marcus Junius Brutus. The head is Libertas; on the other face his ancestor, the first consul, walks between two lictors with an attendant going before them, and BRVTVS is cut below.","credit":"Photo by Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin against black: a woman's head in profile at the left, and at the right four small figures in procession above the letters BRVTVS."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the office not inherit every royal power?","a":"Because the one that had made the king the state's high priest was kept apart and given to the rex sacrorum."},{"q":"Why were the axes taken out of the fasces inside the city?","a":"Because Valerius Publicola carried the law giving a citizen the right of appeal from a magistrate to the people, and the change ran to a thousand paces beyond the walls."},{"q":"Why did a consul who died have to be replaced rather than left out?","a":"Because his colleague had to call the assembly and have a successor chosen, and such a man held powers in no way inferior to the consul elected at the year's beginning."}]},
{"id":"rm-093","num":93,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Rome dated a year by naming its two consuls, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the official list of them, running from the expulsion of the kings to the death of Augustus.","questions":["Cut in four double panels on the west and south walls of the Regia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named the dictators and censors as well, with the triumphs on the pilasters beside them.","Livy warned that funeral speeches and lying inscriptions on family busts had falsified the record, so even a list like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may credit a house with offices it never held."],"answer":"Fasti Consulares","answerText":"Fasti Consulares","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 BCE to the death of Augustus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the Regia, in 4 double panels</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Each year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 consuls, and any suffect</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>Fasti Consulares</b> were the official register of the consuls of each year, kept among the public records, and they are the frame on which the chronology of the Republic hangs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <i>Fas</i> is divine law, and the days on which legal business could be done without impiety were the lawful days, so the books that marked them came to be called fasti themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word then widened, and in prose writers it is the technical term for the registers of consuls, dictators, censors and the other magistrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For nearly four and a half centuries that calendar was priestly knowledge, and a man who wanted to go to law had to ask the few who held it on what day he might bring his suit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then Gnaeus Flavius, a freedman's son and clerk to Appius Claudius, put up a calendar on whitened boards in the Forum with the lawful days marked, and made public the legal forms that had been shut in the pontiffs' closets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The great surviving list was cut into the Regia, in four double panels on its west and south sides, with the register of triumphs on the pilasters of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It ran from the expulsion of the kings to the death of Augustus and gave the consuls, the dictators with their cavalry commanders, the censors with the purifications they closed, and the triumphs and ovations in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fragments of the marble came to light in the Forum in 1547 and were set up on the Capitol, from which they are usually called the Fasti Capitolini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A list of this kind fixes a year by naming its two consuls, and where one of them died in office the man chosen in his place was entered with powers no less than his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy warns that the record was falsified all the same by funeral speeches and lying inscriptions on family busts, each house claiming for itself commands and offices it never held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Fasti,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 521-23, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Fasti.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Regia,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 440-43, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Regia.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.46, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D46. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 352-57, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D40. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Fasti_Consularii_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg/1920px-Fasti_Consularii_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg","title":"What is left of the consular list","desc":"The surviving fragments set into a wall of the Palazzo dei Conservatori in Rome. The small plaque at the foot says that it is not clear in what order the pieces should be put back.","credit":"Photo by José Luiz Bernardes Ribeiro, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall marble wall into which many irregular inscribed fragments have been set at intervals, most of the surface blank between them, with a dedicatory inscription above and a small lettered block on a pedestal below."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4864,41.8919],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did a word for divine law come to name a list of consuls?","a":"Because the days on which legal business could be done without impiety were the lawful days, so the books marking them were called fasti and the term widened to the magistrate registers."},{"q":"Why did publishing a calendar change who could go to law?","a":"Because for nearly four and a half centuries the lawful days were priestly knowledge, until Gnaeus Flavius put them up on whitened boards in the Forum with the legal forms beside them."},{"q":"Why does Livy warn against trusting the record?","a":"Because he says it was falsified by funeral speeches and lying inscriptions on family busts, each house claiming commands and offices it never held."}]},
{"id":"rm-094","num":94,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Cicero defines <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the power without which no war can be waged and no army held together, and a tribune of the plebs never received it.","questions":["Consuls were elected in the assembly of centuries, but only the curiate assembly could confer <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the command without which they could not touch military affairs.","A man holding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was called imperator, and under the Republic the title followed the name, so a proconsul styled himself Marcus Tullius Cicero Imperator."],"answer":"imperium","answerText":"imperium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conferred by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a curiate law of the 30 curiae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 consuls a year, and by praetors</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 509 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"<b>Imperium</b> was the power the Roman state gave a man to command its armies, and Cicero defines it as that without which military affairs cannot be administered, an army held together, or a war waged at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands against <i>potestas</i>: one may properly speak of a consul's potestas or of a consul's imperium, but a tribune of the plebs had potestas alone and never received the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Consuls were elected in the assembly of centuries, yet only the assembly of curies could confer the command, which Livy has Camillus call the assembly that confers the supreme command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The practice reached back to the kings, for Numa, Tullus Hostilius and Ancus Marcius are each said to have carried a curiate law about their own imperium once they had been chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without such a law a consul could not touch military affairs, and the senate once refused to recognise a Roman as a commander because he had not received it in due form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Republic the pure imperium could not be held or exercised inside the city, though it was sometimes granted for the single day of a triumph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belonged to no office of itself, so that a man might hold military command with no title but that of imperator, which is what receiving it made of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The title then came after the name, and a proconsul in Cilicia could properly style himself Marcus Tullius Cicero Imperator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the field the powers it carried were almost unchecked: a commander could make what demands he chose on the allies, levy and pick his soldiers, punish any man under him and spend whatever sum he decided from public funds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the plebs pressed for one of the consulships, arguing that no just administration was possible while the executive power stayed in the other order's hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Imperium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 628-30, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Imperium.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.52, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D52. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#12. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 352-57, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.37, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D37. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/L._Cornelius_Sulla%2C_AR_denarius%2C_84-83_BC%2C_RRC_359-2.jpg/1920px-L._Cornelius_Sulla%2C_AR_denarius%2C_84-83_BC%2C_RRC_359-2.jpg","title":"A commander's own coin, naming the title","desc":"A silver denarius struck at a military mint travelling with Sulla's army in 84 or 83 BCE. The head is Venus with Cupid; the other face sets a jug and a lituus between two trophies, with IMPER above and ITERVM below — imperator for the second time.","credit":"Photo by Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin: a woman's head in profile with a small winged figure beside her, and on the other side a jug and a curved staff between two armour trophies, with Latin lettering above and below."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did election not by itself give a consul his command?","a":"Because consuls were chosen in the assembly of centuries while only the assembly of curies could confer the command, by a law about his own imperium."},{"q":"Why did the power belong to no office of itself?","a":"Because a man might hold military command with no title but that of imperator, which is what receiving it made of him — so Cicero as proconsul could style himself so after his name."},{"q":"Why did the plebs press so hard for one of the consulships?","a":"Because in the field the powers were almost unchecked — levies, punishment, demands on allies, public money — and no just administration was possible while that stayed in the other order's hands."}]},
{"id":"rm-095","num":95,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Rods bound into a bundle with an axe in the middle, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were carried before the higher magistrates by lictors walking one behind another.","questions":["Publicola had the axes taken out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inside Rome and lowered them before the assembly, so that the people saw their dignity was greater than a consul's.","When the second board of decemvirs each took twelve lictors, the 120 of them filled the Forum carrying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the axes tied in, and Livy says they looked like ten kings."],"answer":"fasces","answerText":"fasces","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 lictors before a consul</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dictator</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 lictors; his second in command, 6</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Praetor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 lictors in the city, 6 outside</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Axes out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">inside Rome, from 509 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"The <b>fasces</b> were rods bound into a bundle with an axe set in the middle, the iron of it standing out from them, and they were carried by lictors before the higher magistrates of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome took them, and the office of lictor with them, from the Etruscans, and Livy traces the number twelve to the twelve cities of Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lictors went before the magistrate one behind another, and the one nearest him, the <i>proximus lictor</i>, was the man he gave his orders to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was they who carried out the punishment of condemned citizens, and they who made passers-by give way, dismount and uncover the head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Twelve went before each of the kings, and at the fall of the monarchy each consul had the twelve axes just as a king had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> To stop the people supposing that two kings had replaced one, the consuls settled that only one of them should be preceded by the axes and the other by rods alone, taking them in turn a month at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Valerius Publicola, who gave the people the right of appeal, then had the axes taken out altogether in Rome and left only one consul preceded by lictors, the other attended by a single servant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coming into the assembly he had the bundles lowered before it, and the crowd took the point at once: the dignity of the people stood higher than the consul's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside the city and at the head of an army each consul kept his axes and his own lictors; a dictator had them in Rome as well, with 24 lictors, and a praetor had two in the city and six beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second board of decemvirs appeared with twelve lictors apiece, the 120 of them filling the Forum with the axes tied in, which Livy says gave them the look of ten kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Fasces,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 520-21, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Fasces.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Lictor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 703-4, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Lictor.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.2, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5A*.html#2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Osimo%2C_municipio%2C_rilievo_romano_con_corteo_di_fasci_littori%2C_I_secolo_ac-I_dc%2C_dal_foro_di_auximum.jpg/1920px-Osimo%2C_municipio%2C_rilievo_romano_con_corteo_di_fasci_littori%2C_I_secolo_ac-I_dc%2C_dal_foro_di_auximum.jpg","title":"A file of lictors with their bundles","desc":"A relief from the forum of Auximum, now in the town hall at Osimo, carved between the 1st century BCE and the 1st century CE. The togate figures walk one behind another with the bundles slanted across their shoulders.","credit":"Photo by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A broken marble relief set on a round base: several men in heavy togas walk in single file, two of them carrying long bound bundles slanting up across the shoulder."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did only one consul at a time appear with the axes?","a":"Because the people were not to suppose that two kings had replaced one, so the consuls took them in turn a month at a time."},{"q":"Why did lowering the bundles before the assembly matter?","a":"Because the crowd took the point at once, that the dignity of the people stood higher than the consul's."},{"q":"Why did the second board of decemvirs look like ten kings?","a":"Because they appeared with twelve lictors apiece, the 120 of them filling the Forum with the axes still tied in."}]},
{"id":"rm-096","num":96,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Two or more men of equal power held every ordinary Roman office under the rule of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each able to stop whatever his fellow was doing, and the dictator alone was exempt.","questions":["The consuls took the running of the office in alternate months because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave them identical rights, and neither could carry anything through against the other's refusal.","Latin called each consul the other's <i>collega</i> and their shared tenure a <i>collegium</i>, and from those words comes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the rule that boards of tribunes and quaestors followed too."],"answer":"collegiality","answerText":"collegiality","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 BCE, with the first consuls</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consuls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 a year, equal in power</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Term</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 year, then answerable to the people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dictator</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 man, 6 months, no colleague</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","italy","iron age","institution"],"abstract":"<b>Collegiality</b> was the republican rule that a magistracy belonged to two or more men at once, each of them invested with the same powers as his fellows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its partner was annuality: a magistrate held office for a single year and then went back to being a private citizen, answerable for what he had done in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy dates the beginning of Roman liberty to the limiting of consular authority to one year rather than to any weakening of the powers the kings had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each of two consuls was the other's <i>collega</i> and their joint tenure a <i>collegium</i>, though the word properly described an association of at least three men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because their rights were equal neither could act over the other's objection, so the two could do nothing unless they agreed, and one consul might hear an appeal against his colleague's sentence or veto outright what he was doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> To keep the pair out of each other's way the business of the office was in practice carried by one of them at a time, the two changing over month by month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same shape ran down the ladder of office, and Roman writers speak of a college of tribunes of the plebs and a college of quaestors, so that a magistrate almost never stood alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the arrangement cost was unity of command, and Polybius, describing a consul who has taken the field, found his power over war and money so nearly unchecked that on that evidence alone the constitution might be called a monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dictator was the deliberate exception, a single magistrate whom no colleague could obstruct, and the price of that freedom was a term of six months instead of twelve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Annuality proved the easier principle to bend, for Polybius knew a senate that could supersede a commander whose year had run out or keep him in the field, while a consul laying down office still answered to the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Consul,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 354, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Consul.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Collegium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 310-11, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Collegium.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Dictator,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 406, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Dictator.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#12. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.15, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Relief_curule_chair_Massimo.jpg/1920px-Relief_curule_chair_Massimo.jpg","title":"Funerary relief with a curule chair","desc":"Marble funerary relief from the Torre Gaia on the Via Casilina at Rome, 50 BCE to 50 CE, now in the Palazzo Massimo alle Terme. The folding curule chair in the centre was the seat of the higher magistrates, and a figure in the toga stands on either side of it.","credit":"Museo Nazionale Romano, Palazzo Massimo alle Terme; photograph via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_curule_chair_Massimo.jpg","alt":"A worn marble relief showing a folding X-framed chair in the centre with a draped figure standing on each side of it."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why could two consuls do nothing unless they agreed?","a":"Because their rights were equal, so neither could act over the other's objection, and one might hear an appeal against his colleague's sentence or veto what he was doing."},{"q":"Why did they divide the year month by month?","a":"Because the business of the office had in practice to be carried by one of them at a time, to keep the pair out of each other's way."},{"q":"Why did the dictator's freedom cost him half a year?","a":"Because he was the deliberate exception, a single magistrate whom no colleague could obstruct, and the price of that was a term of six months instead of twelve."}]},
{"id":"rm-097","num":97,"category":"The early Republic","question":"While his countrymen hacked down the Pons Sublicius behind him, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held its far end alone against the Etruscans and then went into the Tiber in full armour.","questions":["Two ancient accounts of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disagree about the ending, Livy having him swim the Tiber to safety and Polybius having him give up his life in it deliberately.","Rome answered by setting up a statue and giving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as much public land as he could drive a plough around in one day, though lameness ended his career."],"answer":"Horatius Cocles","answerText":"Horatius Cocles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508 BCE, in Porsena's war</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 bridge, with 2 companions</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rewarded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a statue, and land ploughed in 1 day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Endings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2: Livy has him swim, Polybius drown</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"<b>Horatius Cocles</b> was the Roman who held the wooden bridge over the Tiber against an Etruscan army while his countrymen cut it down behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The story belongs to the war that followed the expulsion of the kings, when Lars Porsena of Clusium marched on the city and the Janiculum was taken by a sudden assault.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His surname was explained by a lost eye, struck out in an earlier battle, though Plutarch also passes on a story that his flattened nose made the crowd think of a Cyclops and get the word wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two men stood with him, Spurius Larcius and Titus Herminius, and he sent them back when only a little of the bridge was left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bridge was the Pons Sublicius, made of wood and fastened without metal of any kind, and it has been suggested that the iron was left out so that it could be pulled down when danger came from the Etruscan bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Livy has him pray to Father Tiberinus, leap into the river fully armed and swim across through the missiles, and calls the deed more famous than believable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius adds a spear driven through the buttock above the hip and a current split into eddies by the piles, and brings him ashore without losing his arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius tells it the other way, and has him plunge in once the bridge was cut and deliberately give up his life, counting his country's safety and his future fame worth more than the years he had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rewards are much the same in every version, a statue set up in the Comitium or the middle of the Forum and as much public land as he could plough round in a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius says that every man and woman gave him a day's food in a famine, and that his lameness afterwards kept him from the consulship and from any command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.23, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5B*.html#23. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.24, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5B*.html#24. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 5.25, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/5B*.html#25. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.55, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#55. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Publicola</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Publicola*.html#16. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Pons Sublicius,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 401-2, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Pons_Sublicius.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Le_Brun%2C_Charles_-_Horatius_Cocles_defending_the_Bridge_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Le_Brun%2C_Charles_-_Horatius_Cocles_defending_the_Bridge_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Horatius Cocles Defending the Bridge","desc":"Charles Le Brun painted this about 1642. It is an imagining of the story rather than evidence for it: the fighting is at the bridge head on the left, workmen break the timbers away on the right, a winged Victory carries a wreath, and the river god of the Tiber pours from an urn in the foreground.","credit":"Painting by Charles Le Brun, about 1642; via the Google Art Project and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Brun,_Charles_-_Horatius_Cocles_defending_the_Bridge_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of a battle at the head of a wooden bridge, with soldiers fighting on the left, workmen levering the bridge apart on the right, a winged figure above holding a laurel wreath, and a reclining river god pouring water from a jar below."},"locator":{"name":"Pons Sublicius","at":[12.4767,41.8844],"zoom":11,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy call the deed more famous than believable?","a":"Because it has a wounded man leap fully armed into the Tiber and swim across through the missiles, which is the part the accounts most differ over."},{"q":"Why does Polybius tell the ending the other way?","a":"Because his man plunges in once the bridge is cut and deliberately gives up his life, counting his country's safety and his future fame worth more than the years he had left."},{"q":"Why was the bridge fastened without metal?","a":"Because it has been suggested the iron was left out so that it could be pulled down when danger came from the Etruscan bank."}]},
{"id":"rm-098","num":98,"category":"The early Republic","question":"The exiled Tarquins fled to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Clusium, whose Etruscan army shut Rome in until famine and a run of Roman heroics are said to have moved him to terms.","questions":["Tacitus writes of a temple that survived when the city was surrendered, which is one of two traces suggesting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually took Rome rather than withdrawing from it impressed.","Gaius Mucius held his right hand in an altar fire in front of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the hostage Cloelia swam the Tiber out of his camp, in the stories Rome told about the siege."],"answer":"Lars Porsena","answerText":"Lars Porsena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War on Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 508 BCE, for the exiled Tarquins</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">hostages given, Veientine land returned</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tacitus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3.72, on a city that was surrendered</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pliny</span><span class=\"dt-v\">34.39, on a treaty banning iron arms</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"<b>Lars Porsena</b> was the Etruscan king of Clusium who made war on Rome soon after the kings were driven out, and whose war closes that story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exiled Tarquins came as suppliants, warning him not to let a new fashion for expelling kings go unpunished, and Livy says he judged an Etruscan on the Roman throne an honour to his nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The blockade brought corn to famine prices, and the senate bought the loyalty of the plebs by taking the salt trade into public hands and lifting the harbour dues and the war tax from the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome's answer in the tradition is a run of single deeds: Gaius Mucius crossed the river to kill him, struck down the secretary sitting beside him by mistake, and was seized.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Threatened with burning, he put his right hand into the altar fire and left it there, told the king that three hundred more had sworn the same oath, and took from the ruined hand the name Scaevola.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The terms that followed are not a victory: Rome gave hostages and handed territory back to Veii, and only then did the king take his men off the Janiculum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The hostage Cloelia swam the Tiber at the head of the other girls, was sent back to keep faith with the treaty, and was returned by the king with half the hostages of her choosing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy ends it handsomely, with the Tarquins abandoned, the hostages sent home and the Veientine land restored, and Plutarch leaves the camp stocked with provisions and an equestrian statue for Cloelia on the Sacred Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Two survivals cut across that, the first being Tacitus, who calls the temple of Jupiter one that neither Porsenna when the city was surrendered nor the Gauls when they captured it could violate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second is Pliny, who quotes the treaty the king granted the Roman people after the expulsion of the kings, in which iron was to be used only for tilling the fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.15, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>The Histories</i> 3.72, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (London: Macmillan, 1873), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0080%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 34.39, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D34%3Achapter%3D39. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Publicola</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Publicola*.html#19. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Mucius_Scaevola_vor_Porsenna_Rubens_van_Dyck.jpg/960px-Mucius_Scaevola_vor_Porsenna_Rubens_van_Dyck.jpg","title":"Mucius Scaevola before Porsenna","desc":"Painted in the workshop of Peter Paul Rubens about 1626, and attributed in part to Anthony van Dyck. The king sits enthroned at the left, Mucius holds his hand over the altar fire, and the secretary he killed by mistake lies in the foreground with the sword still in him. A 17th-century imagining of the scene, not a Roman one.","credit":"After Peter Paul Rubens, possibly with Anthony van Dyck, about 1626 to 1628; via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mucius_Scaevola_vor_Porsenna_Rubens_van_Dyck.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of a bearded king seated on a throne at the left, gesturing towards a young soldier who holds his right hand in the flames on a small altar, with a dead man in the foreground and armed onlookers behind."},"locator":{"name":"Clusium","at":[11.95,43.0167],"zoom":8},"why":[{"q":"Why did the senate take the salt trade into public hands?","a":"Because the blockade had brought corn to famine prices, so the loyalty of the plebs was bought by that and by lifting the harbour dues and the war tax from the poor."},{"q":"Why is the settlement not a Roman victory?","a":"Because Rome gave hostages and handed territory back to Veii, and only then did the king take his men off the Janiculum."},{"q":"Why do two later writers cut across the heroic version?","a":"Because Tacitus speaks of the city being surrendered to him, and Pliny quotes a treaty he granted in which iron was to be used only for tilling the fields."}]},
{"id":"rm-099","num":99,"category":"The early Republic","question":"The Latin army trying to put the Tarquins back was broken at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in Tusculan country, by a dictator who vowed a temple to Castor while the fighting was still going on.","questions":["Two horsemen of more than human size were said to have watered their horses in the Forum with news of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why the temple of Castor stands where it does.","Livy gives one consulship for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and reports that other authors give another, before admitting that the confusion of the lists makes any year impossible to fix."],"answer":"Battle of Lake Regillus","answerText":"Battle of Lake Regillus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 BCE, or 496 in some sources</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Lake Regillus, in Tusculan territory</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple vowed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to Castor, dedicated 484 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anniversary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 July, with a ride of the knights</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>battle of Lake Regillus</b> was the fight in which Rome broke the army of the Latins trying to put the Tarquins back, somewhere in Tusculan territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the dictator Aulus Postumius take the field with Titus Aebutius as his second in command, and says the Romans forced a battle on hearing the Tarquins were with the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was fought as a run of duels between commanders, and Livy says hardly a leader on either side left unwounded except the dictator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aged Tarquinius Superbus rode at Postumius and was carried off with a wound in the side, while Octavius Mamilius of Tusculum and Aebutius crippled each other in one shock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titus Herminius, who had stood with Horatius on the bridge, ran Mamilius through and was killed by a javelin while stripping the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Postumius turned the day by ordering his bodyguard to treat any fleeing Roman as an enemy and making the cavalry dismount to fight in front of the standards, and vowed a temple to Castor as it went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius adds the part Rome remembered: two horsemen of more than human size fought at the head of the Roman line, appeared that afternoon in the Forum, watered their horses at the spring by Vesta’s temple, told the city it had won, and were gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The temple went up where they were seen, at the south-east corner of the Forum beside that spring, dedicated in 484 BCE by the dictator’s son and called Castor's temple although raised for both brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The victory was kept on the Ides of Quintilis with sacrifices and a ride of the men who held the public horse, crowned with olive, and the Postumii still put the spring and the twins on their coins four centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When it happened is not recoverable: Livy reports that some authors give the battle to a different consulship, and says outright that the confusion of the lists makes any year impossible to fix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.19, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.13, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6A*.html#13. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Aedes Castoris,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 102-5, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Aedes_Castoris.html. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Lacus Iuturnae,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 311-13, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Lacus_Juturnae.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Tommaso_Laureti_-_Battle_of_Lake_Regillus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Tommaso_Laureti_-_Battle_of_Lake_Regillus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The Battle of Lake Regillus","desc":"Tommaso Laureti frescoed this between 1587 and 1594, and the photograph takes in the inscriptions and statues of the room below it. A late 16th-century imagining of the battle rather than any Roman record of it: the fight is all cavalry, with a winged Victory overhead.","credit":"Fresco by Tommaso Laureti, 1587 to 1594; via the Google Art Project and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tommaso_Laureti_-_Battle_of_Lake_Regillus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"A wall fresco of a cavalry battle, horses rearing and falling across the whole width of it, with a winged figure carrying a shield in the sky above and carved plaques and statues set into the wall beneath."},"locator":{"name":"Lake Regillus","at":[12.6931,41.8403],"kind":"battle","zoom":9},"why":[{"q":"Why did the dictator make his cavalry dismount?","a":"Because he turned the day by ordering his bodyguard to treat any fleeing Roman as an enemy and putting the horsemen on foot in front of the standards."},{"q":"Why was the temple built where it was?","a":"Because the two horsemen of more than human size were seen that afternoon watering their horses at the spring by Vesta's temple, and the building went up on that spot."},{"q":"Why can the year of the battle not be fixed?","a":"Because Livy reports that some authors give it to a different consulship and says outright that the confusion of the lists makes any year impossible to settle."}]},
{"id":"rm-100","num":100,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Mutual help in war and an equal share of the spoils were the terms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sworn between Rome and the Latin cities in the consulship of Spurius Cassius.","questions":["Cicero recalled a bronze column behind the Rostra, standing until shortly before his own day, on which the text of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been cut four centuries earlier.","The consul who gave his name to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was condemned for aiming at a throne a few years later, and Livy weighs two accounts of how he died."],"answer":"Foedus Cassianum","answerText":"Foedus Cassianum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sworn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">493 BCE, by Spurius Cassius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">with all the Latin cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Terms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">equal spoils; private suits in 10 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still readable</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on a bronze column, 4 centuries later</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["event","history","politics","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Foedus Cassianum</b> was the treaty that bound Rome and the cities of Latium into a defensive alliance, and it carries the name of Spurius Cassius, consul in the year it was sworn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cicero asks who does not know that a treaty was made with all the Latins in the consulship of Spurius Cassius and Postumus Cominius, the year conventionally counted as 493 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy sets it in the middle of the first secession of the plebs, and says one consul stayed in Rome to make it while the other went against the Volscians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius gives the reason for it: the Latins had not stirred during the sedition, had welcomed the return of the people, and had helped Rome against those who did revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also preserves the terms, which open by declaring peace between the Romans and all the cities of the Latins for as long as the heavens and the earth stay where they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Neither side was to make war on the other, bring in a foreign enemy, or grant a safe passage to anyone marching against the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each was to help the other with all its forces when attacked, and each was to take an equal share of the spoils of their common wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A private suit was to be settled within ten days and in the country where the contract had been made, and nothing could be added or taken away without the consent of Rome and of all the Latins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cicero remembered the text still cut on a bronze column behind the Rostra until shortly before his own day, four centuries after the oath was sworn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cassius was condemned for aiming at a throne after proposing to share conquered land with Latins and Hernici, and Livy weighs two accounts of his death, a private trial by his own father or a public one that ended with his house pulled down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.95, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6D*.html#95. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Balbo</i> 53, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0020%3Atext%3DBalb.%3Asection%3D53. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Balbo</i> 54, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0020%3Atext%3DBalb.%3Asection%3D54. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Ligue-latine-carte.png","title":"Latium and the cities of the Latin League","desc":"A modern map of Latium drawn by a Wikimedia contributor, with the Latin cities marked in orange and the neighbouring peoples named around them. Lake Regillus is shown east of the Alban Lake; the treaty itself, as Dionysius quotes it, names no cities at all.","credit":"Map by Cassius Ahenobarbus, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ligue-latine-carte.png","alt":"A relief map of the coast and hills south-east of the Tiber, with Roma and the other Latin towns marked as orange dots and the Etrusci, Sabini, Aequi, Hernici, Volsci and Aurunci lettered across the surrounding country."},"locator":{"name":"Latium","at":[12.7,41.73],"kind":"region","area":[[12.23,41.78],[12.45,41.92],[12.66,42.06],[12.82,41.98],[12.98,41.86],[13.12,41.68],[13.28,41.36],[13.1,41.23],[12.85,41.35],[12.63,41.44],[12.42,41.6],[12.28,41.72]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Latins get an alliance rather than a subjection?","a":"Because they had not stirred during the sedition, had welcomed the return of the people, and had helped Rome against those who did revolt."},{"q":"Why does the treaty fix where a private suit must be heard?","a":"Because a case was to be settled within ten days and in the country where the contract had been made, which kept ordinary dealings between the two peoples workable."},{"q":"Why is the text known so precisely?","a":"Because Dionysius preserves the terms and Cicero remembered it still cut on a bronze column behind the Rostra until shortly before his own day."}]},
{"id":"wh-223","num":223,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Founding a new capital at Akhetaten in his sixth year and worshipping the sun as a rayed disc, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke with the older gods and had Amun's name erased.","questions":["The Great Hymn to the Aten, cut into a courtier's tomb at Amarna, is the principal source for the beliefs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the king who came to the throne as Amenhotep IV.","Boundary stelae cut into the cliffs of Middle Egypt, written in Late Egyptian rather than the older formal language, record the new city that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dedicated to the sun-disc."],"answer":"Akhenaten","answerText":"Akhenaten","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1351 – 1334 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Highest year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Akhetaten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1347 – 1332 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","religion","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Akhenaten</b> was a king of ancient Egypt's eighteenth dynasty, a son of Amenhotep III who came to the throne as Amenhotep IV and whose highest attested regnal year is 17.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In his sixth year he took the name Akhenaten, 'one who is akh for the Aten', and founded a new royal residence in Middle Egypt, midway between Memphis and Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called it Akhetaten, 'the horizon of the Aten', after the god he worshipped: the sun in its most visible form, a disc whose rays end in hands offering life to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The names and images of Amun and his consort Mut were erased on accessible monuments throughout Egypt and Nubia, and royal ideology, art, architecture and the written language changed with the religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The city held perhaps 30,000 people and lasted about 15 years: Tutankhamun had left it for Memphis some two years after Akhenaten's death, and its temples and palaces were then demolished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The foundation is recorded on boundary stelae cut into the surrounding cliffs and dated to year 6, among the oldest statements surviving from the founding of any city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are also the first royal monuments written mainly in Late Egyptian, far closer to New Kingdom speech than the formal Middle Egyptian of earlier inscriptions, so their wording itself read as new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their text says the king is never to pass the city's limits, which earlier studies took for a vow to stay inside for life, though he is known to have travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chief source for the new belief is the Great Hymn to the Aten, cut in the tomb of the courtier Ay, which praises the sun as creator but leaves every other god out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly 400 cuneiform tablets from the site carry the diplomatic correspondence of the period, passing between the great kings of Egypt, Mitanni, Hatti, Alashiya and Babylonia and the mayors of the Syro-Palestinian towns, and they are a key source for the 14th-century Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["\"Akhenaten (Amenhotep IV),\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/akhnaton.html. [Open access]","\"The Boundary Stelae of Akhenaten,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/boundary.html. [Open access]","\"Belief in One God in Ancient Egypt,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/belief.html. [Open access]","\"Amarna: The Cuneiform Tablets,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/cuneiform.html. [Open access]","\"About Amarna,\" The Amarna Project, https://www.amarnaproject.com/about-amarna/. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","Cl&eacute;ment Dutrey, \"Distribution de l'information et strat&eacute;gies relationnelles dans le corpus de correspondances amarniennes : approche par l'analyse de r&eacute;seaux,\" <i>Journal of Historical Network Research</i> 6, no. 1 (2021), https://doi.org/10.25517/jhnr.v6i1.85. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Akhenaten%2C_Nefertiti_and_their_children.jpg/1920px-Akhenaten%2C_Nefertiti_and_their_children.jpg","title":"Akhenaten and Nefertiti beneath the Aten","desc":"House altar showing Akhenaten and Nefertiti with three of their daughters beneath the rayed disc of the Aten, 18th dynasty, about 1340 BCE. Gerbil, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Gerbil, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akhenaten,_Nefertiti_and_their_children.jpg","alt":"A limestone relief of a royal couple seated facing each other with small daughters on their laps and knees, the sun-disc above sending down long rays that end in tiny hands, some of them holding ankh signs."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the wording of the boundary stelae itself read as new?","a":"Because they are the first royal monuments written mainly in Late Egyptian, far closer to New Kingdom speech than the formal Middle Egyptian of earlier inscriptions."},{"q":"Why is the Great Hymn to the Aten the chief source for the new belief?","a":"Because it praises the sun as creator and leaves every other god out, cut in the tomb of the courtier Ay."},{"q":"Why do the Amarna tablets matter beyond Egypt?","a":"Because nearly 400 of them carry diplomatic correspondence between the great kings of Egypt, Mitanni, Hatti, Alashiya and Babylonia and the Syro-Palestinian mayors — a key source for the 14th-century Near East."}]},
{"id":"wh-224","num":224,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Coming to the throne as a boy and reigning roughly a decade, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put the gods of Egypt back after the Amarna period and set up restoration stelae at Karnak.","questions":["Tomb 62 in the Valley of the Kings is modest in size and decoration, but the roughly 5,000 objects packed into it belonged to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who died still in his teens.","Two years into the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the court left Akhetaten for Memphis, the cult of the Aten was abandoned, and the gods of Egypt were reinstalled."],"answer":"Tutankhamun","answerText":"Tutankhamun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1336 – 1327 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 9 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">KV62, Valley of the Kings</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","religion","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Tutankhamun</b> was a king of ancient Egypt's eighteenth dynasty who came to the throne as a boy under the throne name Nebkheperure and reigned roughly a decade, from about 1336 to 1327 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For Egyptian history his importance is the reinstallation of the gods after the Amarna period, recorded on restoration stelae set up at Karnak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court left Akhetaten for Memphis perhaps two years into the reign, and the cult of the Aten was abandoned utterly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under Tutankhamun the city Akhenaten had founded ceased to be the royal power-base, its monuments were dismantled by later rulers, and he was in time struck from the record himself for the connection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died still in his teens of a cause that is not known, and was buried across the river from Thebes in the Valley of the Kings, where more than sixty New Kingdom tombs are cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> His tomb, number 62 in that valley, is modest beside the others in both size and decoration, most likely because he came to the throne so young and ruled so briefly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only the burial chamber's walls carry any decoration, and where other royal tombs are covered in funerary texts such as the Amduat, his shows a single scene from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest of the painting gives the funeral or the king in the company of gods, and the small chamber meant the roughly 5,000 objects put in were stacked very tightly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those objects came from the life of the palace: clothes, jewellery, cosmetics, incense, furniture, toys, vessels of many materials, chariots and weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tomb's size has been argued over, since the larger one in which his successor Ay was buried may have been begun for him, and it has been suggested that KV62 already existed as a private tomb or a store and was enlarged to take the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Tutankhamun,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/tutankhamun.html. [Open access]","\"The Boundary Stelae of Akhenaten,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/boundary.html. [Open access]","\"Tomb of Tutankhamun,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/tomb-of-tutankhamun. [Open access]","\"Valley of the Kings,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/valley-of-the-kings. [Open access]","\"About Amarna,\" The Amarna Project, https://www.amarnaproject.com/about-amarna/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Gold_Death_Mask_of_Tutankhamun.jpg/1920px-Gold_Death_Mask_of_Tutankhamun.jpg","title":"The gold mask of Tutankhamun","desc":"The gold death mask of Tutankhamun, from his burial in the Valley of the Kings. Thomas Clouet, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Thomas Clouet, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_Death_Mask_of_Tutankhamun.jpg","alt":"A gold funerary mask of a young king in a striped headcloth of gold and blue glass, a vulture and a cobra above the brow, a plaited false beard below the chin and a broad inlaid collar across the chest."},"why":[{"q":"Why is his tomb modest beside the other royal tombs?","a":"Because he came to the throne so young and ruled so briefly, so only the burial chamber carries decoration and about 5,000 objects had to be stacked very tightly."},{"q":"Why was he struck from the record despite restoring the gods?","a":"Because of his connection to the Amarna period, whose city ceased to be the royal power-base under him and whose monuments later rulers dismantled."},{"q":"Why is the tomb's origin argued over?","a":"Because the larger tomb in which his successor Ay was buried may have been begun for him, and KV62 may already have existed as a private tomb or store, enlarged to take a king."}]},
{"id":"wh-225","num":225,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Fighting the Hittites at Qadesh and then making peace with them in his twenty-first year, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a bigger mark on Egypt's monuments than any other king.","questions":["The Great Temple at Abu Simbel was cut into the rock of Nubia for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose four seated colossi dominate its façade and whose own statue sits among the gods inside.","Moving the capital to Piramesse in the eastern Delta and adding a courtyard and a pylon at Luxor, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left his name at almost every site in Egypt."],"answer":"Ramesses II","answerText":"Ramesses II","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1279 – 1213 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hittite treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">regnal year 21</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">KV7, Valley of the Kings</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","history","egypt","warfare","building","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Ramesses II</b> was a king of ancient Egypt's nineteenth dynasty, the son of Sety I, who reigned from about 1279 to 1213 BCE under the throne name Usermaatre-setepenre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the number and scale of his monuments and the length of his reign he is one of the most important rulers Egypt had, and he left a larger mark on its monumental landscape than any other king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His name has been found at almost every Egyptian site, he moved the capital to Piramesse, modern Qantir in the eastern Delta, and he was buried in tomb 7 in the Valley of the Kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His war against the Hittites produced the battle of Qadesh, which was recorded in a pictorial and written composition unparalleled in extent among Egyptian sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his twenty-first year he made a peace treaty with the Hittite king Hattusili III, and both the Egyptian and the Hittite versions of it survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Karnak the bulk of the Great Hypostyle Hall and all of its decoration belong to him and his father, a forest of 134 columns 15 metres (49 feet) high around a central twelve that stand 21 metres (69 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Luxor he added a peristyle courtyard and a massive pylon fronted by colossal statues and a pair of obelisks 25 metres (82 feet) high, one of which has stood in the Place de la Concorde in Paris since 1836.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around 1264 BCE he had the Great Temple of Abu Simbel cut into the living rock in Nubia, its façade dominated by four seated colossi, one of which an ancient earthquake brought down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four gods are seated in its sanctuary, Amun-Ra, Ra-Horakhty, Ptah and a deified Ramesses II, and on two days a year, 22 February and 22 October, the sun reaches them down the length of the hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second rock-cut temple beside it belongs to Hathor and to his great royal wife Nefertari, whose colossi on the façade are the same size as his own, which is very rare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Ramesses II Usermaatre-setepenre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ramsesii.html. [Open access]","\"Abu Simbel,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/abu-simbel. [Open access]","\"Luxor Temple,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/luxor-temple. [Open access]","\"Karnak,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/karnak. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Templo_de_Rams%C3%A9s_II%2C_Abu_Simbel%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-02%2C_DD_03.jpg/1920px-Templo_de_Rams%C3%A9s_II%2C_Abu_Simbel%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-02%2C_DD_03.jpg","title":"The Great Temple of Abu Simbel","desc":"The rock-cut façade of the Great Temple at Abu Simbel, with the four seated colossi of Ramesses II. Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Templo_de_Rams%C3%A9s_II,_Abu_Simbel,_Egipto,_2022-04-02,_DD_03.jpg","alt":"A temple front cut straight into a sandstone cliff, four huge seated figures of a king flanking a low doorway, the second from the left broken off at the waist with its head and shoulders lying on the ground in front."},"why":[{"q":"Why is Qadesh unusual among Egyptian battle records?","a":"Because it was recorded in a pictorial and written composition unparalleled in extent among Egyptian sources."},{"q":"Why is the treaty with Hattusili III unusually well attested?","a":"Because both the Egyptian and the Hittite versions of it survive, so the agreement can be read from each side."},{"q":"Why is Nefertari's façade at Abu Simbel remarkable?","a":"Because her colossi on the smaller temple are the same size as the king's own, which is very rare."}]},
{"id":"wh-226","num":226,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Ramesses II's four divisions were strung out over 55 kilometres when the Hittite chariotry struck at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a fight both sides afterwards claimed to have won.","questions":["The Poem and the Bulletin, cut on temple walls across Egypt, record <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where an Egyptian army met the Hittites of Muwatalli II on the Orontes in 1274 BCE.","Egypt and Hatti met in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the fifth year of Ramesses II, and their war ran on for another sixteen years before the two kings made peace."],"answer":"Battle of Kadesh","answerText":"Battle of Kadesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1274 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regnal year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, of Ramesses II</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Divisions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, named for gods</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">regnal year 21</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","history","egypt","warfare","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Kadesh</b> was fought in 1274 BCE between Ramesses II of Egypt and the Hittite king Muwatalli II at the city of Kadesh on the Orontes, in the fifth year of the Egyptian reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egypt had been pressing into Syria and Palestine since the beginning of the eighteenth dynasty, a region of small city-states none of which could resist it, while farther north the Hittite empire blocked any advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Egyptian army marched in four divisions named for the gods Amun, Ra, Ptah and Seth, with the king at the head of the first of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Hittite chariotry struck, those divisions were strung out over 55 kilometres (34 miles), elements of the Amun and Ra divisions were scattered, and at Kadesh itself the numerical advantage lay with the Hittites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ramesses came close to defeat there, though he was not beaten, and the war with the Hittites ran on until his twenty-first year, when he made a peace treaty with their king Hattusili III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> With Megiddo it is one of only two battles of the late Bronze Age that are relatively well known, and it is the best documented of them from the Egyptian side, Hittite sources for it being scarce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ramesses had it recorded in a pictorial and written composition unparalleled in extent among Egyptian sources, cut on the walls of his temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The written account survives in two forms usually called the Poem and the Bulletin, which date the army's setting out to year 5, second month of the Shemu season, day 9.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hieratic Papyrus of Pentaur carries an account of the battle in a manuscript written under Merenptah, a generation after it was fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both sides claimed the victory, and there is no impartial source against which either claim can be tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Ramesses II Usermaatre-setepenre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ramsesii.html. [Open access]","\"Asia: Foreign Relations with Egypt in the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2003, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/foreignrelations/asiank.html. [Open access]","Sheikha Obaid Al-Harbi, \"Military Arrangements for the Battle of Kadesh in 1274 BC During the Era of the New Kingdom,\" <i>Journal of the College of Education for Women</i> 33, no. 4 (2022): 12&ndash;28, https://doi.org/10.36231/coedw.v33i4.1627. [Open access]","Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric Servajean, <i>Quatre &eacute;tudes sur la bataille de Qadech</i>, CENiM 6 (Montpellier, 2012), http://www.enim-egyptologie.fr/cahiers/6/Servajean_CENIM6.pdf. [Open access]","Maxim V. Panov, \"The Battle of Kadesh: Paleographic Notes on the Papyrus of Pentauret,\" <i>Sovremennye Issledovaniya Sotsialnykh Problem</i> 11, no. 3 (2019): 65&ndash;84, http://journal-s.org/index.php/mrsp/article/view/12264. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Battle_scene_from_the_Great_Kadesh_reliefs_of_Ramses_II_on_the_Walls_of_the_Ramesseum_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Battle_scene_from_the_Great_Kadesh_reliefs_of_Ramses_II_on_the_Walls_of_the_Ramesseum_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"The Kadesh reliefs of the Ramesseum","desc":"A line drawing of the battle scene from the Great Kadesh reliefs on the walls of the Ramesseum, published in James Henry Breasted's History of Egypt, 1927. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"James Henry Breasted, History of Egypt (1927), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_scene_from_the_Great_Kadesh_reliefs_of_Ramses_II_on_the_Walls_of_the_Ramesseum_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A line drawing of a carved battle scene: a king towering in a chariot at the left drawing a bow, ranks of chariots along the top and bottom, a mass of overturned chariots and fallen men in the middle, and at the right a walled city ringed by water with rows of figures inside it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Egyptian army come close to defeat?","a":"Because when the Hittite chariotry struck, its four divisions were strung out over 55 kilometres, elements of two were scattered, and at Kadesh itself the numerical advantage lay with the Hittites."},{"q":"Why can neither side's claim of victory be tested?","a":"Because both claimed it and there is no impartial source, the record being overwhelmingly Egyptian and Hittite sources for the battle scarce."},{"q":"Why is this battle so much better documented than others of its age?","a":"Because Ramesses had it recorded in a pictorial and written composition unparalleled in extent among Egyptian sources, cut on his temple walls."}]},
{"id":"wh-227","num":227,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Cut into the rock of a desert valley under a pyramid-shaped peak, the tombs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the kings of the New Kingdom and were nearly all robbed in antiquity.","questions":["Egyptians buried their dead on the west bank because the sun died there, and it is why the kings chose the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opposite Thebes for their own tombs.","Thutmose I is the first king confirmed to lie in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where royal tomb walls carry funerary texts such as the Amduat and the Book of Gates."],"answer":"Valley of the Kings","answerText":"Valley of the Kings","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1069 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18th, 19th and 20th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 60, plus 20 unfinished</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Thutmose I, from c. 1504 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","history","egypt","religion","bronze age"],"abstract":"The <b>Valley of the Kings</b> is a desert valley on the west bank of the Nile opposite Thebes, modern Luxor, where the rulers of Egypt's eighteenth, nineteenth and twentieth dynasties were buried, along with some relatives and a few high-ranking commoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thebes was never the administrative capital, but from the New Kingdom onward it ranked first in religious terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The west bank was chosen because the sun god set, and so died, in the west in order to be reborn in the east, which is why Egyptian cemeteries generally lay on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kings were laid under a pyramid-shaped peak rising from the cliffs, the pyramid being a symbol of rebirth and a natural one a sign of the divine, and the peak was sacred to Hathor as Mistress of the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isolation was the other reason: having seen what became of the Old and Middle Kingdom pyramids, and knowing robbery happened even in their own day, they chose hidden underground tombs in a secluded valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first New Kingdom ruler confirmed to lie there is Thutmose I, the third king of the eighteenth dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ineni, the official in charge of cutting that tomb, recorded that he oversaw the excavation of the cliff-tomb of his king 'in privacy; none seeing, none hearing'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burial chambers and the corridors approaching them were carved out of the rock, and the walls of most royal tombs carry funerary texts such as the Amduat and the Book of Gates, which helped the dead king reach the afterlife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The valley divides into an eastern and a western branch, the western holding only a handful of tombs, and there are over sixty in all with about twenty unfinished ones little more than pits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The workmen of the necropolis lived in a village at Deir el-Medina on the same west bank, where their own tombs include an architect's and a sculptor's, and most of the royal tombs were emptied in remote antiquity, Tutankhamun's excepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Valley of the Kings,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/valley-of-the-kings. [Open access]","\"Thebes, Finds from the Valley of the Kings,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/thebes/tombs/kings.html. [Open access]","\"Tomb of Tutankhamun,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/tomb-of-tutankhamun. [Open access]","\"Thebes,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/thebes/index.html. [Open access]","C&eacute;dric Larcher et al., \"Deir el-M&eacute;dina (2022),\" <i>Bulletin arch&eacute;ologique des &Eacute;coles fran&ccedil;aises &agrave; l'&eacute;tranger</i> (2023), https://doi.org/10.4000/baefe.8324. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Kir%C3%A1lyok_v%C3%B6lgye_%28keleti_v%C3%B6lgy_k%C3%B6z%C3%A9ps%C5%91_r%C3%A9sz%C3%A9nek_s%C3%ADrjai%29.jpg/1920px-Kir%C3%A1lyok_v%C3%B6lgye_%28keleti_v%C3%B6lgy_k%C3%B6z%C3%A9ps%C5%91_r%C3%A9sz%C3%A9nek_s%C3%ADrjai%29.jpg","title":"Tomb entrances in the East Valley","desc":"The central part of the East Valley, its tomb entrances labelled with their KV numbers by the photographer. Kingtut, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Kingtut at Hungarian Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kir%C3%A1lyok_v%C3%B6lgye_(keleti_v%C3%B6lgy_k%C3%B6z%C3%A9ps%C5%91_r%C3%A9sz%C3%A9nek_s%C3%ADrjai).jpg","alt":"A bare pale-limestone valley seen from above, with a dozen walled ramps and doorways cut into its slopes and floor, small groups of visitors on the paths between them, and yellow arrows and KV numbers added to the photograph to label each tomb."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the tombs cut on the west bank?","a":"Because the sun god set, and so died, in the west in order to be reborn in the east, which is why Egyptian cemeteries generally lay on that side."},{"q":"Why did the kings abandon visible monuments for hidden tombs?","a":"Because they had seen what became of the Old and Middle Kingdom pyramids and knew robbery happened in their own day, so they chose isolation in a secluded valley."},{"q":"Why does the natural peak above the valley matter?","a":"Because a pyramid was a symbol of rebirth and a natural one a sign of the divine, and the peak was sacred to Hathor as Mistress of the West."}]},
{"id":"wh-228","num":228,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Called Ipet-sut, 'The Most Select of Places', the temples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> housed the cult of Amun and a priesthood whose wealth gave it great political power.","questions":["A forest of 134 columns stands in the Great Hypostyle Hall at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where nearly every king of the New Kingdom left some mark on the temple of Amun.","A processional way lined with sphinxes joined Luxor Temple to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and in the Opet Festival the gods travelled along it to visit Amenemopet."],"answer":"Karnak","answerText":"Karnak","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2112 BCE – 306 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Precincts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3: Amun, Mut and Montu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hypostyle Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">134 columns, up to 21 metres (69 feet)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Obelisk</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hatshepsut's, almost 30 metres (98 feet)</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","history","egypt","religion","building","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Karnak</b> is a complex of temples on the east bank at Thebes, modern Luxor, in Upper Egypt, called Ipet-sut, 'The Most Select of Places', by the Egyptians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cult of Amun of Thebes was conducted in its Great Temple of Amun, which was extremely wealthy and whose priesthood held great political power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into several precincts, the largest Amun's, with his wife Mut's to the south, Montu's to the north, and Akhenaten's temple to the Aten on the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line of kings who reunified Egypt after the First Intermediate Period came from Thebes, the city became one of the most important in the country, and Amun's importance rose with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the earliest evidence for his temple, in the reign of Intef II, to the Ptolemaic period it was lavished with royal attention, additions were still made under Rome, and nearly every New Kingdom king left his mark here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Like most Egyptian temples the Great Temple of Amun is set on an east-west axis, temples being models of the cosmos and that layout mirroring the sun god's path across the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Exceptionally it has a north-south axis as well, turning it towards Luxor Temple, home of a version of Amun particular to south Luxor, and a sphinx-lined processional way joined the two for the Opet Festival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Great Hypostyle Hall is a forest of 134 columns 15 metres (49 feet) high around a central twelve of 21 metres (69 feet), begun perhaps by Amenhotep III and carried through by Sety I and his son Ramesses II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That hall was conceived as a temple of millions of years in its own right, and in it Sety I became a divine being by associating himself with Amun, Ra and Atum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hatshepsut raised an obelisk of almost 30 metres (98 feet) nearby, Thutmose III added a hall at the eastern end for various gods and his own cult, and the sacred lake where priests purified themselves is still there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["\"Karnak,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/archaeological-sites/karnak. [Open access]","\"Luxor Temple,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/monuments/luxor-temple. [Open access]","Hanane Gaber, \"Formes et m&eacute;canisme de divinisation de S&eacute;thi Ier dans la grande salle hypostyle du temple d'Amon &agrave; Karnak,\" <i>&Eacute;gypte Nilotique et M&eacute;diterran&eacute;enne</i> 19 (2026): 131&ndash;175, http://www.enim-egyptologie.fr/revue/2026/7/Gaber_ENiM19_p131-175.pdf. [Open access]","\"Thutmose III,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/thutmosisiii.html. [Open access]","\"Ramesses II Usermaatre-setepenre,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2001, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/ramsesii.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Luxor_Karnak-Tempel_2016-03-21_Gro%C3%9Fe_S%C3%A4ulenhalle_04.jpg/1920px-Luxor_Karnak-Tempel_2016-03-21_Gro%C3%9Fe_S%C3%A4ulenhalle_04.jpg","title":"The Great Hypostyle Hall at Karnak","desc":"Columns of the Great Hypostyle Hall in the Precinct of Amun-Re at Karnak. Djehouty, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Djehouty, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor_Karnak-Tempel_2016-03-21_Gro%C3%9Fe_S%C3%A4ulenhalle_04.jpg","alt":"A view looking up between huge sandstone columns with spreading capitals, carved all over with hieroglyphs and still holding traces of paint, carrying stone beams against a pale sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the temple set on an east-west axis?","a":"Because temples were models of the cosmos, and that layout mirrors the sun god's path across the sky."},{"q":"Why does it exceptionally have a second axis?","a":"Because a north-south one turns it towards Luxor Temple, home of a version of Amun particular to south Luxor, joined by a sphinx-lined processional way for the Opet Festival."},{"q":"Why did Amun's temple grow so rich?","a":"Because the kings who reunified Egypt after the First Intermediate Period came from Thebes, and Amun's importance rose with the city's, drawing royal attention from Intef II to the Ptolemies and beyond."}]},
{"id":"wh-229","num":229,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt went upstream past the First Cataract into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for its gold and copper, and in the New Kingdom governed it through a viceroy of Kush.","questions":["Senusret III built forts at Semna to hold a border in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Kerma culture later ruled the lower stretch of it after Egypt lost control.","Thutmose I pushed the conquest of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as far as the Fourth Cataract, destroying the Kerman state and making the region an Egyptian province."],"answer":"Nubia","answerText":"Nubia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Contact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2600 BCE, under Snefru</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lower Nubia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st to 2nd Cataract</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Conquered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to the 4th Cataract, under Thutmose I</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Viceroy of Kush</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 18th dynasty</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","history","egypt","geography","bronze age"],"abstract":"<b>Nubia</b> is the stretch of the Nile valley upstream of Egypt, its lower part running from the First to the Second Cataract and its upper part beyond, in modern southern Egypt and Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What drew Egypt to it was raw materials, gold and copper above all, with hard wood and ivory traded through the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Egyptians are attested there as early as the Old Kingdom at Buhen, officials' biographies of the sixth dynasty describe expeditions south, and a campaign is recorded under Snefru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the early Middle Kingdom fighting led to the conquest of Lower Nubia, Senusret I fortified Buhen, and Senusret III built more forts to hold a slightly more southerly border at Semna, leading several campaigns himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Upstream, south of the Second Cataract, lay the Kerma culture, which by its classical phase had formed a state centred on Kerma with large tombs and a massive building called the Deffufa, and which developed no writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Egypt lost control during the Second Intermediate Period, when Lower Nubia was ruled by the kings of Kerma and Nubians are well attested living in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Thutmose I the conquest reached the Fourth Cataract and the Kerman state was destroyed, and the whole province was placed under a 'viceroy of Kush' with his seat at Miam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That office was created at the start of the eighteenth dynasty, and its holder was among the most important officials of the New Kingdom because the gold mines were in his charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Local government was left to local leaders, who were brought to the Egyptian court while young to be given an Egyptian education, and Middle Kingdom forts such as Buhen were rebuilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ramesses II raised many temples in Lower Nubia and the material culture of the province is fully Egyptian, but traffic ran both ways, and lead isotopes show some of the galena in kohl from Nubian graves was mined at Gebel el-Zeit on Egypt's Red Sea coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["\"Nubia in the Old Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/ok.html. [Open access]","\"Foreign Relations: Nubia in the Middle Kingdom and Second Intermediate Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/mk.html. [Open access]","\"Nubia in the New Kingdom,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/nk.html. [Open access]","\"Nubia: Kerma,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/kerma.html. [Open access]","\"The Viceroy of Kush,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/viceroy.html. [Open access]","\"New Kingdom,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/new-kingdom. [Open access]","Rennan Lemos et al., \"Bronze Age Supply Chains between Ancient Egypt and Nubia Revealed by Lead Isotope Analysis of Kohl Samples,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 27633, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-79162-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg/1920px-Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg","title":"The Western Deffufa at Kerma","desc":"The Western Deffufa, a large mud-brick temple in the ancient city of Kerma in Sudan. Walter Callens, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Walter Callens, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg","alt":"A huge weathered block of mud brick standing several storeys high in flat desert, its sides eroded into rounded towers, with the low walls of a ruined town spread around its foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Egypt want the region at all?","a":"Because of raw materials — gold and copper above all, with hard wood and ivory traded through it — which is also why the viceroy of Kush was among the most important New Kingdom officials."},{"q":"Why did Egypt educate Nubian leaders' sons at court?","a":"Because local government was left to local leaders, who were brought to the Egyptian court while young to be given an Egyptian education."},{"q":"Why does the kohl in Nubian graves complicate a one-way picture?","a":"Because lead isotopes show some of its galena was mined at Gebel el-Zeit on Egypt's Red Sea coast, so material moved in both directions."}]},
{"id":"wh-230","num":230,"category":"Ancient Egypt","question":"Egypt spent the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided, with kings at Tanis in the Delta and Upper Egypt governed in practice by the high priest of Amun at Thebes.","questions":["Libyan dynasties from Bubastis and Sais and then Kushite kings from Napata all ruled during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended with an Assyrian conquest.","Between the New Kingdom and the Late Period lies the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when for much of the time Egypt was divided into two or more separate parts."],"answer":"Third Intermediate Period","answerText":"Third Intermediate Period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1069 – 664 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21st to 25th</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kushite rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from about 725 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Assyrian conquest, 664 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","egypt","monarchy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Third Intermediate Period</b> runs from about 1069 to 664 BCE, between the New Kingdom and the Late Period, and for much though not all of that time Egypt was divided into two or more parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ramesses XI, the last king of the New Kingdom, no longer ruled the whole country in effect by the end of his reign, and after his death a line of kings ruled from Tanis in the north-eastern Delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their authority was acknowledged everywhere, but Upper Egypt in practice governed itself under the high priest of Amun at Thebes, who held the highest military office as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Historians call the arrangement a theocracy, since the highest authority of all was the god Amun himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around 945 BCE a military man of Libyan origin from Bubastis took direct control of the country as Sheshonq I, founding the twenty-second dynasty, campaigning in the Levant and building on a large scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From the middle of that dynasty the country fell apart again in civil strife: after Osorkon II it was no longer united, and by the twenty-third many local rulers were independent, wearing royal regalia and taking royal titles of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egyptologists use the name 'twenty-third dynasty' for different groups of kings, and it expresses the disintegration of the state rather than a single line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Meanwhile the Kushite kingdom centred on Napata, near the Fourth Cataract in what is now Sudan, was rising, and from about 725 BCE its kings invaded Egypt and ruled it as the twenty-fifth dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Piye is known from a stela at Napata reporting a campaign into Egypt, though whether that campaign annexed the country at once is uncertain, and the exact duration and nature of Kushite rule is still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Neo-Assyrian empire had by then reached Palestine, and though the Kushite kings held Egypt for a time the Assyrians prevailed, sacking Thebes under Assurbanipal and, when Tanutamani withdrew, installing the kings of Sais to rule Egypt for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Third Intermediate Period,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/third-intermediate-period. [Open access]","\"Egypt in the Third Intermediate Period (1069-664 BC),\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/3inter/index.html. [Open access]","\"Nubia: The Napatan Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/nubia/napatan.html. [Open access]","\"Kings of the Third Intermediate Period,\" Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2000, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/chronology/3rdintermediateperkings.html. [Open access]","\"Late Period,\" Discover Egypt's Monuments, Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, Arab Republic of Egypt, https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/historical-periods/late-period. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Psusennes.jpg/1280px-Psusennes.jpg","title":"The silver coffin of Psusennes I","desc":"The silver anthropoid coffin of Psusennes I, a king of the twenty-first dynasty who ruled from Tanis, in the Egyptian Museum, Cairo. Jerzy Strzelecki, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Jerzy Strzelecki, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psusennes.jpg","alt":"The head and shoulders of a silver coffin lid shaped as a king, a gold band across the brow with a cobra at its edge, inlaid eyes, and a crook and flail laid across the chest."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the arrangement in Upper Egypt called a theocracy?","a":"Because the high priest of Amun at Thebes held the highest military office as well and governed in practice, while the highest authority of all was the god Amun himself."},{"q":"Why does the twenty-third dynasty not name a single line of kings?","a":"Because Egyptologists use it for different groups of kings, and it expresses the disintegration of the state, with many local rulers independent and wearing royal regalia."},{"q":"Why did Kushite rule over Egypt not last?","a":"Because the Neo-Assyrian empire had reached Palestine and prevailed, sacking Thebes under Assurbanipal and installing the kings of Sais to rule Egypt for them once Tanutamani withdrew."}]},
{"id":"wh-231","num":231,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Spread from the Himalaya to the Arabian Sea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built cities of wide gridded streets and covered drains, yet left no palaces, royal tombs or monuments to any ruler.","questions":["Standard weights, bricks in fixed proportions and chert blades carried 500 kilometres (300 miles) into Gujarat show how far the conventions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached during its urban phase.","Divided into Early, Mature and Late phases, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held its cities for some seven hundred years before aridity and a weakening monsoon coincided with their contraction after 1900 BCE."],"answer":"Indus Valley Civilisation","answerText":"Indus Valley Civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3: Early, Mature, Late</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, cores over 50 hectares (120 acres)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Contraction</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Indus Valley Civilisation</b>, also called the Harappan civilisation, was the Bronze Age urban society of the north-western Indian subcontinent, and its mature phase ran from about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its remains lie across what is now Pakistan and north-western India, over a range that eclipsed those of contemporary Mesopotamia and Egypt, reaching from the Himalaya to the Arabian Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologists divide it into three phases, Early or Pre-Harappan, Mature or Urban, and Late or Post-Harappan, of which only the middle one is a civilisation of cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five settlements are usually counted as cities, among them Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Rakhigarhi and Dholavira, with urban cores often far larger than 50 hectares (120 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wide streets running north to south, crossed by narrower lanes running east to west, and drains laid beneath them are the signature of their planning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the excavations have not produced is as telling as what they have, for there are no palaces, no temples that shut anyone out, no rich tombs and no monuments raised to glorify an individual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shared standards of measurement and of recording appear instead, and long-distance exchange worked on the same scale: blades of Rohri chert were carried some 500 kilometres (300 miles) from Sindh into Gujarat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its craftspeople commanded a highly specialised pyrotechnology, and stretched scarce wood with dung and the leftovers of crop processing to keep their kilns and hearths burning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Households in Gujarat ate widely, and the fish bones from five sites there run to 24 species across fourteen families, cut, charred and chewed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Growing aridity and a weakening monsoon from about 2100 BCE, and a sharp hydroclimatic shift in the Himalaya around 4,200 years ago, coincide with the contraction of the cities after seven centuries of urban life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Charusmita Surendra Gadekar, Rajesh Sasidharan Vasantha and Abhayan Girija Sasidharan, “Early Harappan interaction between Sindh and Gujarat, as evidenced by lithic tools,” <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 1 (2021), https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.5161. [Open access]","Carla Lancelotti, Juan José García-Granero, Charusmita S. Gadekar, Marco Madella and P. Ajithprasad, “‘Not all that burns is wood’. A social perspective on fuel exploitation and use during the Indus urban period (2600-1900 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0192364, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192364. [Open access]","G. S. Abhayan, Pramod P. Joglekar, P. Ajithprasad and S. V. Rajesh, “Ichthyoarchaeological perspectives on roles of fish and red meat during Indus Civilization in Gujarat, India,” <i>Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology</i> 4 (2025): 1517351, https://doi.org/10.3389/fearc.2025.1517351. [Open access]","E. A. Niederman, D. F. Porinchu and B. S. Kotlia, “Hydroclimate change in the Garhwal Himalaya, India at 4200 yr BP coincident with the contraction of the Indus civilization,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 23082, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-02496-5. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","bronze age","india","pakistan"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Mohenjo-daro-2010.jpg/1280px-Mohenjo-daro-2010.jpg","title":"Excavated streets and house walls at Mohenjo-daro","desc":"Baked-brick walls, lanes and doorways of one of the largest Indus cities, in the Sindh province of Pakistan.","credit":"Photograph by Comrogues, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0","alt":"Ruins of a city of low baked-brick walls forming rooms and narrow straight lanes, under a pale sky, with green fields on the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why is only the middle phase counted a civilisation of cities?","a":"Because the Early and Late phases are not urban: the mature phase of about 2600 to 1900 BCE holds the five settlements usually counted as cities, with cores often far larger than 50 hectares."},{"q":"Why is what the excavations have NOT found so telling?","a":"Because there are no palaces, no temples that shut anyone out, no rich tombs and no monuments raised to glorify an individual, which is unlike its contemporaries."},{"q":"Why did its craftspeople stretch their fuel?","a":"Because wood was scarce, so they burnt dung and the leftovers of crop processing with it to keep their kilns and hearths going."}]},
{"id":"wh-232","num":232,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Standing on an old channel of the Ravi in Pakistani Punjab, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first Indus site to be found, and the civilisation is named after it.","questions":["Gated walls around every neighbourhood, weights beside one of the gateways and a great open clearing in the north-east are the marks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an Indus city of many mounds rather than one.","Teeth from the urban-phase cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry isotopes suggesting that almost everyone buried there had come to the city as a child from somewhere else."],"answer":"Harappa","answerText":"Harappa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3700 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ravi phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3700 – 2800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kot Dijian phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2800 – 2600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Harappa</b> is a city of the Indus Valley Civilisation, standing on the left bank of a former channel of the Ravi in the Sahiwal district of Punjab, in Pakistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The civilisation is named after it, this having been the first of its sites to be found, beside a modern village of the same name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of five major urban centres so far identified, with Mohenjo-daro, Ganweriwala, Rakhigarhi and Dholavira, each of them set in a wide landscape of villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first settlers chose one of the oldest soils in the region, close to fertile alluvium and raised enough to offer some protection from flooding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Different parts of the settlement were lived in at different times, so what survives is not one tell but a group of mounds, from which colonial brick-mining later took much of the architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Occupation runs from about 3700 to 1300 BCE, through a Ravi phase, a Kot Dijian phase from about 2800, and the urban Harappan phase of about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Massive gated walls enclosed each of the city's neighbourhoods, weights have been found beside one of those gateways, and a large clearing fills the north-eastern quadrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its households burnt dung as well as wood, and lived on barley and wheat grown in two seasons, the summer crops on the monsoon rains and the winter crops on what those rains left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotopes in teeth from its urban-phase cemetery suggest that those buried there were almost all first-generation immigrants, so the graves record who came to the city rather than who was born in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After 1900 BCE the plan grew disordered and sanitation declined, and the skeletons of the post-urban burial ground carry more infection than those laid down before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Carla Lancelotti, Juan José García-Granero, Charusmita S. Gadekar, Marco Madella and P. Ajithprasad, “‘Not all that burns is wood’. A social perspective on fuel exploitation and use during the Indus urban period (2600-1900 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0192364, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192364. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Nathaniel James, Alexia Decaix, Isabell Villasana, J. Mark Kenoyer, Richard H. Meadow and Jade d’Alpoim Guedes, “Taphonomy and labour at the Indus Valley site of Harappa (3700–1300 BC),” <i>Antiquity</i> 99, no. 403 (2025): 83–100, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.196. [Open access]","Benjamin Valentine, George D. Kamenov, Jonathan Mark Kenoyer, Vasant Shinde, Veena Mushrif-Tripathy, Erik Otarola-Castillo and John Krigbaum, “Evidence for Patterns of Selective Urban Migration in the Greater Indus Valley (2600-1900 BC): A Lead and Strontium Isotope Mortuary Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 4 (2015): e0123103, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0123103. [Open access]","Gwen Robbins Schug, K. Elaine Blevins, Brett Cox and Kelsey Gray, “Infection, Disease, and Biosocial Processes at the End of the Indus Civilization,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e84814, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084814. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","pakistan"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Harappa_Ruins_-_VI_-_Mounds.jpg/1280px-Harappa_Ruins_-_VI_-_Mounds.jpg","title":"Excavated brickwork on one of the mounds at Harappa","desc":"House foundations cut into a mound of the site, in the Sahiwal district of Punjab, Pakistan.","credit":"Photograph by Hassan Nasir, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0","alt":"A shallow excavation of straight brick foundation walls laid out in blocks, cut into a sandy mound, with flat green plain and trees on the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the site survive as a group of mounds?","a":"Because different parts of the settlement were lived in at different times, so it is not one tell — and colonial brick-mining later took much of the architecture."},{"q":"Why did the first settlers choose this ground?","a":"Because it is one of the oldest soils in the region, close to fertile alluvium and raised enough to offer some protection from flooding."},{"q":"Why do the cemetery teeth record who came rather than who lived there?","a":"Because isotopes suggest those buried in the urban-phase cemetery were almost all first-generation immigrants."}]},
{"id":"wh-233","num":233,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Raised on brick platforms beside the Indus on Pakistan's Larkana Plain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drained its houses through pipes and soak pits and washed in a Great Bath on its western mound.","questions":["No palace, no exclusionary temple and no lavish burial has been found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Pillared Hall could hold hundreds of people and had no benches to seat any of them.","Measured as a spread of house sizes, the gap between rich and poor at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> narrowed over time, even as the Indus city grew more prosperous and its street plan took shape."],"answer":"Mohenjo-daro","answerText":"Mohenjo-daro","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Mohenjo-daro</b> is one of the largest cities of the Indus Valley Civilisation, standing near the Indus River on Pakistan's Larkana Plain and built up through the urban phase of about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of five major urban centres of that civilisation so far identified, and what survives of it is a cluster of mounds rather than a single one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its neighbourhoods were raised on massive platforms, and the drainage and public buildings that served them took the labour of thousands of people to put up and to keep working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water moved through wells, pipes, gutters and soak pits that carried waste away from bathing platforms inside individual houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the western mound stand its largest structures, among them the Great Bath and the building early excavators misnamed the Stupa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Hundreds of houses have been dug, many of them with bathing platforms, private wells and second storeys, and the brickwork was fine enough that much of the city still stands to be measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Pillared Hall ran more than 30 metres (100 feet) to a side on at least 20 brick pillars, and, having no benches at all, offered a bright enclosed space that could hold hundreds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nothing found in the city marks out a ruler, there being no palace, no temple that shuts anyone out, no lavish burial and no art that glorifies an individual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ritual imagery speaks of cosmic order rather than of any king, and the city is counted among the more collectively governed of early urban centres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether its largest houses belonged to an elite is still argued, and measured as a spread of house sizes the gap between rich and poor narrowed over the city's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, Iqtedar Alam and Cameron Petrie, “Inequality declined in the Bronze Age city of Mohenjo-daro,” <i>Antiquity</i> (2026): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2026.10359. [Open access]","Amy E. Thompson, Jessica Munson, Scott G. Ortman, Andrés G. Mejía Ramón, Gary M. Feinman, Gabriela Cervantes Quequezana, Pablo Cruz, Adam S. Green, Dan Lawrence and Paul Roscoe, “Assessing neighborhoods, wealth differentials, and perceived inequality in preindustrial societies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400699121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036974/. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Gary M. Feinman, David Stasavage, David M. Carballo, Sarah B. Barber, Adam Green, Jacob Holland-Lulewicz, Dan Lawrence et al., “The distribution of power and inclusiveness across deep time,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 12 (2026): eaec1426, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12998527/. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","pakistan"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Great_Bath_of_Mohenjo-daro.jpg/1280px-Great_Bath_of_Mohenjo-daro.jpg","title":"The Great Bath on the western mound at Mohenjo-daro","desc":"The brick-lined tank, with steps down into it, among the standing architecture of the western mound.","credit":"Photograph by Soban, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0","alt":"A large rectangular brick-paved tank sunk below ground level, with wide brick steps leading down into it, surrounded by standing brick walls and a mound of further ruins beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the city counted among the more collectively governed?","a":"Because nothing in it marks out a ruler — no palace, no temple that shuts anyone out, no lavish burial and no art glorifying an individual — and its ritual imagery speaks of cosmic order."},{"q":"Why did its infrastructure need thousands of people?","a":"Because the neighbourhoods were raised on massive platforms, and the drainage and public buildings that served them took that much labour to put up and to keep working."},{"q":"Why is the Pillared Hall notable for what it lacks?","a":"Because it ran more than 30 metres to a side on at least 20 brick pillars and had no benches at all, offering a bright enclosed space that could hold hundreds."}]},
{"id":"wh-234","num":234,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Surviving mostly on small stone stamp seals, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs to no more than fourteen signs in a line and has resisted eighty years of claimed decipherments.","questions":["Because no bilingual text has been found and the language behind it is unknown, readings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still range from Indo-Aryan through Dravidian to a purely numerical system.","A seal carrying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read from left to right, and the sealing stamped from it is read the other way, from right to left."],"answer":"Indus script","answerText":"Indus script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Indus script</b> is the undeciphered sign system of the Indus Valley Civilisation, and one of the shared marks, along with the weights, the pottery styles and the seals themselves, that make a site recognisably Indus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It survives mainly on stamp seals, and on pottery and other objects of stone, terracotta and copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counts of the surviving texts run from about 3,000 to about 3,800, and a single line carries no more than 14 signs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around 400 distinct signs have been identified, though one tally reaches 676.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A seal is read from left to right, and the sealing stamped from it the other way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The seals are small stone stamps, mostly about 6 to 13 square centimetres (1 to 2 square inches), cut in intaglio with a line of signs above an animal, and the same standard forms recur seal after seal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The animals engraved on them include the unicorn, the buffalo, the rhinoceros, the elephant and the short-horned bull.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eighty years of claimed decipherments have failed, the texts being short, no bilingual having been found and the underlying language unknown, and readings still range from an Indo-Aryan language through a Dravidian one to a purely numerical system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Statistical models place the script's conditional entropy nearer to that of natural languages than to non-linguistic sign systems, and the signs that open a text differ from those that close it, which is internal evidence of syntax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has since been argued that no such method can settle whether the script is writing at all, and the same models fit texts found in Mesopotamia and West Asia far worse than ones from the Indus valley itself, which may mean the signs were put to a different use abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Nisha Yadav, Hrishikesh Joglekar, Rajesh P. N. Rao, Mayank N. Vahia, Ronojoy Adhikari and Iravatham Mahadevan, “Statistical Analysis of the Indus Script Using n-Grams,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 5, no. 3 (2010): e9506, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0009506. [Open access]","Rajesh P. N. Rao, Nisha Yadav, Mayank N. Vahia, Hrishikesh Joglekar, R. Adhikari and Iravatham Mahadevan, “A Markov model of the Indus script,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 33 (2009): 13685–90, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2721819/. [Open access]","Fabio Tamburini, “On automatic decipherment of lost ancient scripts relying on combinatorial optimisation and coupled simulated annealing,” <i>Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence</i> 8 (2025): 1581129, https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2025.1581129. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","writing","language","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Clevelandart_1973.160.jpg/1280px-Clevelandart_1973.160.jpg","title":"Steatite seal with a two-horned bull and an inscription","desc":"A square Indus stamp seal, 3.2 centimetres (1.3 inches) to a side, with a line of signs cut above the animal.","credit":"Cleveland Museum of Art, 1973.160, purchase from the J. H. Wade Fund; public domain","alt":"A small square grey stone seal photographed against a plain background, carved with a horned bull standing below a horizontal line of angular signs."},"why":[{"q":"Why have eighty years of claimed decipherments failed?","a":"Because the texts are short, no bilingual has been found and the underlying language is unknown, so readings still range from Indo-Aryan to Dravidian to a purely numerical system."},{"q":"Why do statistical models suggest the signs carry syntax?","a":"Because the script's conditional entropy is nearer that of natural languages than of non-linguistic sign systems, and the signs opening a text differ from those closing it."},{"q":"Why may the signs have been used differently abroad?","a":"Because the same models fit texts found in Mesopotamia and West Asia far worse than ones from the Indus valley itself."}]},
{"id":"wh-235","num":235,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Wide north-south streets, narrower east-west lanes and household drains running beneath them are the signature of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, repeated from city to city across a very large area.","questions":["Bricks cut two widths to the length and stone weights running from a gram to more than ten kilograms show how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried a single set of standards.","For all the drains, platforms and standard bricks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most people in the civilisation are thought never to have lived in one of its cities at all."],"answer":"Indus urban planning","answerText":"Indus urban planning","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Indus urban planning</b> is the set of shared building conventions behind the cities of the Indus Valley Civilisation: well-organised streets, sanitation and water management, and a standardised system of measurement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wide streets ran north to south, crossed by narrower lanes running east to west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neighbourhoods stood on massive platforms, and the drainage and public buildings that served them took the labour of thousands of people to raise and to keep working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Households ran a pipe from a bathing platform indoors to the drains laid under the lanes, and drew water from brick wells built to be watertight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bricks themselves were made to a fixed proportion, two widths to the length, which allowed header-and-stretcher bonding and durable joints, and the quality of that masonry is why so much of it is still standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That proportion holds across the cities, and production on such a scale needed coordination among many brickmakers; comparable brickmaking is not found in Mesopotamia until nearly a thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stone weights run from about 1 gram to well over 10 kilograms (0.04 to 22 pounds), cut from hard stones carried down from the highlands and standardised closely enough to work as one system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They carry no mark of whose system they belong to, unlike the competing marked weights of contemporary Mesopotamia, so a user never had to say which was meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They turn up at rural sites as well as in the cities, and weights reported beside the gateway into a Harappa neighbourhood may point to taxation, though what was taxed and by whom is unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over the life of a city its houses grew more uniform and sat more squarely on its street plan, though most people in the civilisation are thought never to have lived in a city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Gwen Robbins Schug, K. Elaine Blevins, Brett Cox and Kelsey Gray, “Infection, Disease, and Biosocial Processes at the End of the Indus Civilization,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e84814, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084814. [Open access]","Amy E. Thompson, Jessica Munson, Scott G. Ortman, Andrés G. Mejía Ramón, Gary M. Feinman, Gabriela Cervantes Quequezana, Pablo Cruz, Adam S. Green, Dan Lawrence and Paul Roscoe, “Assessing neighborhoods, wealth differentials, and perceived inequality in preindustrial societies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 16 (2025): e2400699121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12036974/. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, Iqtedar Alam and Cameron Petrie, “Inequality declined in the Bronze Age city of Mohenjo-daro,” <i>Antiquity</i> (2026): 1–16, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2026.10359. [Open access]","Danika Parikh and Cameron A. Petrie, “‘We are inheritors of a rural civilisation’: rural complexity and the ceramic economy in the Indus Civilisation in northwest India,” <i>World Archaeology</i> 51, no. 2 (2019): 252–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643715/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","bronze age","technology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Water_canal_for_the_great_bath_-_Mohenjo-daro.jpg/1280px-Water_canal_for_the_great_bath_-_Mohenjo-daro.jpg","title":"A brick-lined water channel beside the Great Bath at Mohenjo-daro","desc":"The channel runs deep between two brick walls and passes under a corbelled brick roof.","credit":"Photograph by Quratulain, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0","alt":"A narrow, deep channel between two high walls of small flat bricks, roofed over at the far end by bricks stepped inward to meet."},"why":[{"q":"Why is so much Indus brickwork still standing?","a":"Because the bricks were made to a fixed proportion, two widths to the length, which allowed header-and-stretcher bonding and durable joints."},{"q":"Why did brick production on that scale require coordination?","a":"Because the same proportion holds across the cities, so many brickmakers were working to one standard — something not found in Mesopotamia until nearly a thousand years later."},{"q":"Why do the weights carry no maker's mark?","a":"Because they were standardised closely enough to work as one system, so unlike the competing marked weights of Mesopotamia a user never had to say which was meant."}]},
{"id":"wh-236","num":236,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Set among the salt flats and rocky islands of the Rann of Kachchh, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut a linked series of reservoirs deep into the bedrock around its walls and gateways.","questions":["One of only four Indus cities dug at length, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood for some fifteen centuries before an earthquake damaged it about four thousand years ago.","Cubic stone weights of the standard Indus kind, an open clearing inside the walls and reservoirs in the bedrock are what excavation has found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in Gujarat."],"answer":"Dholavira","answerText":"Dholavira","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4950 – 3450 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earthquake</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4450 – 4150 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Dholavira</b> is one of the five major urban centres so far identified in the Indus Valley Civilisation, lying in the island belt of the Rann of Kachchh, in Gujarat in western India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also one of only four Indus cities to have been excavated at length, alongside Harappa, Mohenjo-daro and Rakhigarhi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The settlement stood for about 1,500 years, from roughly 4,950 to 3,450 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its excavated plans show city walls and gateways, and a series of reservoirs cut deep into the bedrock around the town and joined to one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water storage on that scale is why the site is reached for whenever the water supply of Indus settlements is discussed, though how those settlements were watered is still poorly understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> An extensive open clearing lies enclosed within the walls, of the kind found at other Indus cities and often read as room for people to gather, though open ground can also be an accident of how a site forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cubic stone weights of the standard Indus kind have been recovered there, part of a family of Bronze Age weight systems across Western Eurasia that one study finds consistent with descent from a single original unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country around it is one of saline waste, rocky islands and active faults, and an earthquake damaged the town between about 4,450 and 4,150 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Khirsara, another Indus site in the same part of Gujarat, farmers turned from wheat and barley to drought-resistant millets at about 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That shift marks an abrupt arid event now recognised across many dated records, and it falls inside the same few centuries as the earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]","Javed N. Malik, Eshaan Srivastava, Mahendrasinh S. Gadhavi, Franz Livio, Nayan Sharma, Shreya Arora, Nicolò Parrino, Pierfrancesco Burrato and Attilio Sulli, “Holocene surface-rupturing paleo-earthquakes along the Kachchh Mainland Fault: shaping the seismic landscape of Kachchh, western India,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 11612, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-62086-z. [Open access]","Adam S. Green and Cameron A. Petrie, “Landscapes of Urbanization and De-Urbanization: A Large-Scale Approach to Investigating the Indus Civilization’s Settlement Distributions in Northwest India,” <i>Journal of Field Archaeology</i> 43, no. 4 (2018): 284–99, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7665814/. [Open access]","Nicola Ialongo, Raphael Hermann and Lorenz Rahmstorf, “Bronze Age weight systems as a measure of market integration in Western Eurasia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 118, no. 27 (2021): e2105873118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8271817/. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Yong Jun Kim, Eun Jin Woo, Nilesh Jadhav, Pranjali Waghmare, Yogesh Yadav, Avradeep Munshi et al., “Archaeological and anthropological studies on the Harappan cemetery of Rakhigarhi, India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 2 (2018): e0192299, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192299. [Open access]","Anil K. Pokharia, Rajesh Agnihotri, Shalini Sharma, Sunil Bajpai, Jitendra Nath, R. N. Kumaran and Bipin Chandra Negi, “Altered cropping pattern and cultural continuation with declined prosperity following abrupt and extreme arid event at ~4,200 yrs BP: Evidence from an Indus archaeological site Khirsara, Gujarat, western India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 10 (2017): e0185684, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0185684. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Water_storage_tanks_at_Dholavira_Citadel.jpg/1280px-Water_storage_tanks_at_Dholavira_Citadel.jpg","title":"A stone-lined water tank at Dholavira","desc":"The tank is cut below ground and faced with courses of flat stone, with a stepped ledge running down one side.","credit":"Photograph by Prof Ranga Sai, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A rectangular pit faced with courses of flat stone slabs, a stepped ledge running down one side, open to the sky and edged with dry grass."},"why":[{"q":"Why is Dholavira reached for whenever Indus water supply is discussed?","a":"Because it holds a series of reservoirs cut deep into the bedrock around the town and joined to one another, and how Indus settlements were watered is otherwise still poorly understood."},{"q":"Why is its open clearing not conclusive evidence of a gathering place?","a":"Because although such ground is often read as room for people to gather, open ground can also be an accident of how a site forms."},{"q":"Why do the earthquake and the Khirsara crop change belong together?","a":"Because both fall in the same few centuries: the town was damaged between about 4,450 and 4,150 years ago, and at Khirsara farmers turned to drought-resistant millets at about 4,200, an abrupt arid event now recognised widely."}]},
{"id":"wh-237","num":237,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Built over a tidal salt marsh at the head of the Gulf of Cambay, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was later left high and dry as the sea level dropped away from it.","questions":["A circular steatite seal found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is neither wholly Indian nor Sumerian in workmanship, and closely resembles the seals of the Persian Gulf islands.","Kilns with vitrified inner surfaces and high-grade copper ingots have been dug at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a smaller Indus site on the Gujarat coast rather than one of the great cities."],"answer":"Lothal","answerText":"Lothal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Harappan phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Lothal</b> is a settlement of the Indus Valley Civilisation on the Gujarat coast of western India, of the urban phase that ran from about 2600 to 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its excavator described it as an important harbour town at the head of the Gulf of Cambay, and the name has stuck: later work calls it the major port of the civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the excavations turned up to support that reading was a circular steatite seal, neither wholly Indian nor Sumerian in workmanship, closely resembling the seals found on the islands of the Persian Gulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seals of that kind are thought to have been made at the entrepôts of a cosmopolitan Gulf trade, Bahrain among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground the town stood on has since been reconstructed from satellite imagery and from the microscopic fossils in its sediments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A broad tidal channel once ran along the western side of the town, and an estuary lay to the east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lothal grew over a tidal salt marsh, and was left high and dry as the sea level dropped away from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the smaller Indus sites rather than one of the five cities, and among those, with Rupar and Kalibangan, that have produced human remains and burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kilns with vitrified inner surfaces and high-grade copper ingots have been dug there, which points to skilled copper smelting on the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming at Lothal has been argued to have differed from that of the great cities because the rainfall and the country differ, but systematically collected plant remains have not yet borne the idea out, so how the smaller settlements fed themselves is still an open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["S. R. Rao, “A ‘Persian Gulf’ Seal from Lothal,” <i>Antiquity</i> 37, no. 146 (1963): 96–99, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00037194. [Paywalled]","A. S. Khadkikar, C. Rajshekhar and K. P. N. Kumaran, “Palaeogeography around the Harappan port of Lothal, Gujarat, western India,” <i>Antiquity</i> 78, no. 302 (2004): 896–903, https://doi.org/10.1017/S0003598X00113535. [Paywalled]","Aditya Prakash Kanth, Manager Rajdeo Singh and Buddha Rashmi Mani, “Archaeometallurgical characterisation of ancient copper slags from pre-Harappan site, Kunal, India,” <i>Analytical Science Advances</i> 3, no. 7–8 (2022), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10989616/. [Open access]","C. A. Petrie, E. Lightfoot, P. J. Jones, J. R. Walker et al., “Animal movement on the hoof and on the cart and its implications for understanding exchange within the Indus Civilisation,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 158, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-50249-3. [Open access]","C. A. Petrie and J. Bates, “‘Multi-cropping’, Intercropping and Adaptation to Variable Environments in Indus South Asia,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 30, no. 2 (2017), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6991972/. [Open access]","Adam S. Green, “Of Revenue Without Rulers: Public Goods in the Egalitarian Cities of the Indus Civilization,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 823071, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.823071. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Lothal_Harappan_Civilization.jpg/1280px-Lothal_Harappan_Civilization.jpg","title":"The Lothal archaeological site, Gujarat","desc":"Brick embankment walls and steps enclosing a wide flat expanse, on the coastal plain of Gujarat.","credit":"Photograph by Ranjith Kumar Inbasekaran, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A wide flat expanse of bare earth enclosed by low brick walls, with brick steps at one side and a line of dark structures on a bank beyond, under a blue sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the site read as a port?","a":"Because a circular steatite seal found there, neither wholly Indian nor Sumerian in workmanship, closely resembles seals made at the entrepôts of the Gulf trade, Bahrain among them."},{"q":"Why is the town now far from the sea?","a":"Because it grew over a tidal salt marsh with a broad tidal channel to its west and an estuary to its east, and was left high and dry as the sea level dropped."},{"q":"Why is how the smaller settlements fed themselves still open?","a":"Because farming here has been argued to have differed from the great cities' given the rainfall and country, and systematically collected plant remains have not yet borne the idea out."}]},
{"id":"wh-238","num":238,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"After about 1900 BCE the interaction sphere retracted and village life took over, a change called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for which no single cause has ever been agreed.","questions":["Drought episodes rising from about 4,200 years ago are the leading explanation for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though disease, warfare, shifting rivers and broken trade routes have all been proposed as well.","The valley was already as arid as it is today by 2800 BCE, which is the awkward fact for any account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that rests on drying alone."],"answer":"decline of the Indus civilisation","answerText":"decline of the Indus civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Harappan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drought episodes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 4200 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>decline of the Indus civilisation</b> is the contraction of its cities and its wider exchange network after about 1900 BCE, a change variously called a collapse and a metamorphosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the Late Harappan phase, from about 1900 to 1300 BCE, the interaction sphere retracted, the urban centres lost population, and agrarian village life took over for most of the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlement shifted from many small sites around a few large centres to a de-nucleated pattern, and in parts of north-western India the density of sites may actually have risen after 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Since most people in the civilisation are thought never to have lived in a city, a turn towards villages is a change in where complexity lay rather than a simple emptying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> No single cause is agreed: hydroclimate anomalies, disease, warfare, rivers moved by tectonics, political instability, changes of crop and the breaking of trade routes have all been proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The climate case is the one most often made, and it rests on an increase in drought episodes from about 4,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lake core from the Garhwal Himalaya records a drawdown beginning at that date and a dry anomaly through the following century and a half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Khirsara in Gujarat the farmers answered by turning from wheat and barley to drought-resistant millets, and the site carried on with its prosperity reduced rather than ending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Charcoal and crop remains elsewhere show rising aridity and a weakening monsoon from about 2100 BCE, worked around for generations before the cities gave way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The awkward fact for any simple drying story is that the valley was already about as arid as it is now by 2800 BCE, and what changed after 2000 BCE was the steadiness of the rainfall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["E. A. Niederman, D. F. Porinchu and B. S. Kotlia, “Hydroclimate change in the Garhwal Himalaya, India at 4200 yr BP coincident with the contraction of the Indus civilization,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 23082, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-02496-5. [Open access]","Gwen Robbins Schug, K. Elaine Blevins, Brett Cox and Kelsey Gray, “Infection, Disease, and Biosocial Processes at the End of the Indus Civilization,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 12 (2013): e84814, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0084814. [Open access]","Adam S. Green and Cameron A. Petrie, “Landscapes of Urbanization and De-Urbanization: A Large-Scale Approach to Investigating the Indus Civilization’s Settlement Distributions in Northwest India,” <i>Journal of Field Archaeology</i> 43, no. 4 (2018): 284–99, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7665814/. [Open access]","Anil K. Pokharia, Rajesh Agnihotri, Shalini Sharma, Sunil Bajpai, Jitendra Nath, R. N. Kumaran and Bipin Chandra Negi, “Altered cropping pattern and cultural continuation with declined prosperity following abrupt and extreme arid event at ~4,200 yrs BP: Evidence from an Indus archaeological site Khirsara, Gujarat, western India,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 10 (2017): e0185684, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0185684. [Open access]","Carla Lancelotti, Juan José García-Granero, Charusmita S. Gadekar, Marco Madella and P. Ajithprasad, “‘Not all that burns is wood’. A social perspective on fuel exploitation and use during the Indus urban period (2600-1900 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0192364, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0192364. [Open access]","Danika Parikh and Cameron A. Petrie, “‘We are inheritors of a rural civilisation’: rural complexity and the ceramic economy in the Indus Civilisation in northwest India,” <i>World Archaeology</i> 51, no. 2 (2019): 252–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643715/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","archaeology","bronze age","climate"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Archaeological_artifacts_recovered_from_Khirsara%2C_Indus_Valley_Civilization.jpg/1280px-Archaeological_artifacts_recovered_from_Khirsara%2C_Indus_Valley_Civilization.jpg","title":"Objects excavated at Khirsara, an Indus site in Gujarat","desc":"A plate from the study that dated the site's turn to drought-resistant millets to about 4,200 years ago.","credit":"Pokharia et al., PLOS ONE 12, no. 10 (2017): e0185684, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0","alt":"A labelled plate of excavated finds: painted potsherds, a small jar, a shell bangle, copper implements, agate and carnelian beads, and two inscribed seals."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the change called a metamorphosis as well as a collapse?","a":"Because settlement shifted from many small sites around a few large centres to a de-nucleated pattern, and in parts of north-western India the density of sites may actually have risen after 1900 BCE."},{"q":"Why is a simple drying story awkward?","a":"Because the valley was already about as arid as it is now by 2800 BCE, and what changed after 2000 BCE was the steadiness of the rainfall rather than its amount."},{"q":"Why does Khirsara show adaptation rather than failure?","a":"Because its farmers turned from wheat and barley to drought-resistant millets and the site carried on, its prosperity reduced rather than ending."}]},
{"id":"wh-239","num":239,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Ancient DNA puts steppe pastoralist ancestry in the Swat valley in the first half of the 2nd millennium BCE, the strongest evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the subcontinent.","questions":["A genome from the Indus civilisation lacks the steppe ancestry now universal in the northwest, which places the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after that civilisation rather than during it.","Paternal lineages arriving from Central Asia in the Bronze Age, beside far older maternal ones, show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were strongly male-driven."],"answer":"Indo-Aryan migrations","answerText":"Indo-Aryan migrations","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Central Asia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2100 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reaching South Asia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Indo-Aryan migrations</b> are the movements that carried Indo-European speech into the Indian subcontinent, a late event of the middle to late Bronze Age, and the case for them now rests largely on ancient DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Upstream lies the Yamnaya complex of the steppes north of the Black and Caspian Seas, which appeared about 3300 BCE and whose descendants reached the Indo-Iranians through the Corded Ware, Fatyanovo and Sintashta cultures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from 523 ancient people show no steppe pastoralist ancestry at the Bactria-Margiana sites of Central Asia before 2100 BCE, and communities there mixing with people who carried it by about 2100 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Samples from the Swat valley put that ancestry further south in the first half of the 2nd millennium BCE, where it now makes up as much as 30 per cent of the ancestry of some groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its profile matches that of Bronze Age eastern Europe, which ties the two dispersals to one movement of people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An individual of the Indus civilisation, sequenced separately, carries little if any of that ancestry, so what is near-universal in the northwest today was not there then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across the subcontinent it is commonest in northern populations and thins towards the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The influx was strongly male-driven: the paternal lineages arrive from Central Asia in the Bronze Age, while the maternal lineages of the subcontinent are far older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Groups of traditionally priestly status, among them the custodians of the liturgy in Sanskrit, carry more steppe ancestry than a simple mixture model predicts, which is a second and independent line of argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The weak point is material culture, different enough that some archaeologists see no connection at all, and how much immigration took place remains extremely controversial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos, Swapan Mallick, Iosif Lazaridis et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]","Vasant Shinde, Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nadin Rohland, Swapan Mallick, Matthew Mah, Mark Lipson, Nathan Nakatsuka et al., “An Ancient Harappan Genome Lacks Ancestry from Steppe Pastoralists or Iranian Farmers,” <i>Cell</i> 179, no. 3 (2019): 729–35, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6800651/. [Open access]","Marina Silva, Marisa Oliveira, Daniel Vieira, Andreia Brandão, Teresa Rito, Joana B. Pereira, Ross M. Fraser et al., “A genetic chronology for the Indian Subcontinent points to heavily sex-biased dispersals,” <i>BMC Evolutionary Biology</i> 17 (2017): 88, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12862-017-0936-9. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639 (2025): 132–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11922553/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","genetics","migration","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Yamnaya-related_migrations.jpg/1280px-Yamnaya-related_migrations.jpg","title":"Yamnaya-related migrations, drawn after Anthony and after Narasimhan et al.","desc":"A map made for Wikipedia from those studies: the homeland north of the Black Sea at 3300 BCE, arrows west into Europe and east across the steppe, and one reaching the subcontinent about 1500 BCE.","credit":"Map by Joshua Jonathan, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A physical map of Eurasia with the Yamnaya homeland marked north of the Black Sea and black arrows running west into Europe, east across the steppe and south-east into the Indian subcontinent, each labelled with a date BCE."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the case now rest largely on ancient DNA?","a":"Because the material culture is different enough that some archaeologists see no connection at all, while the genomes tie the steppe profile of Bronze Age eastern Europe to the same movement of people."},{"q":"Why is the influx described as strongly male-driven?","a":"Because the paternal lineages arrive from Central Asia in the Bronze Age while the maternal lineages of the subcontinent are far older."},{"q":"Why is priestly ancestry a second and independent argument?","a":"Because groups of traditionally priestly status, among them the custodians of the Sanskrit liturgy, carry more steppe ancestry than a simple mixture model predicts."}]},
{"id":"wh-240","num":240,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Named for a body of Sanskrit texts rather than for any state, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follows the Indus civilisation in northern South Asia and is known chiefly from those texts.","questions":["Literature made first in the Punjab and later in the valley of the Ganges lets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> be traced by where its texts were composed.","Ten kings in coalition met Sudas of the Tritsus on the banks of the Parushni during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in a battle one of the hymns describes."],"answer":"Vedic period","answerText":"Vedic period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rigveda composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Language arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Vedic period</b> is the age of the Sanskrit texts called the Vedas, following the Indus civilisation in northern South Asia and named for a body of literature rather than for any state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its language came in with the spread of Indo-European speech into the subcontinent, an event of the middle to late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vedic Sanskrit is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan, from which the modern languages of northern India and Pakistan descend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three literary strata are distinguished inside the period: the four Vedas, then the Brahmanas, then the Sutras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The four are collections of hymns and prayers, made chiefly to accompany the pressing of soma and the offering of melted butter to the gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three of them were together called the threefold knowledge, and the Atharvaveda joined them only after a long struggle, being later in its language and much of its matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hymns ask the gods for wealth in cattle, for offspring, for long life and for victory, and they are not folk poetry but the skilled work of a priestly class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also record the quarrels of their own world: ten kings in coalition met Sudas of the Tritsus on the banks of the Parushni and were driven back as they tried to cross it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the earlier half of the period the centre lay in the Punjab; in the later half the writing turned speculative and prosaic and the centre had moved to the valley of the Ganges, until settlement reached from the mouths of the Indus to those of the Ganges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Groups of traditionally priestly status, the custodians of that liturgy, still carry more steppe ancestry than a simple mixture model predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","quote":{"book":"rigveda","n":"534","cite":"Hymn 7.18","text":"<p>Together came the Pakthas, the Bhalanas, the Alinas, the Sivas, the Visanins. Yet to the Trtsus came the Arya's Comrade, through love of spoil and heroes' war, to lead them.</p><p>Fools, in their folly fain to waste her waters, they parted inexhaustible Parusni. Lord of the Earth, he with his might repressed them: still lay the herd and the affrighted herdsman.</p>"},"sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos, Swapan Mallick, Iosif Lazaridis et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]","Niels Schoubben, “Animacy, postpositions, and the spatial cases in Niya Prakrit,” <i>Journal of South Asian Languages and Linguistics</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12458993/. [Open access]","Marina Silva, Marisa Oliveira, Daniel Vieira, Andreia Brandão, Teresa Rito, Joana B. Pereira, Ross M. Fraser et al., “A genetic chronology for the Indian Subcontinent points to heavily sex-biased dispersals,” <i>BMC Evolutionary Biology</i> 17 (2017): 88, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12862-017-0936-9. [Open access]"],"tags":["era","history","literature","language","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/1672_CE_manuscript_copy%2C_10th_century_BCE_Samaveda_Kauthuma_Samhita_Veyagana%2C_Schoyen_Collection_Norway.jpg/1280px-1672_CE_manuscript_copy%2C_10th_century_BCE_Samaveda_Kauthuma_Samhita_Veyagana%2C_Schoyen_Collection_Norway.jpg","title":"A folio of the Samaveda, copied in 1672","desc":"One of the four Vedas, in a manuscript made long after the hymns were composed; the small figures above the line belong to its notation for chanting.","credit":"Photograph by Ms Sarah Welch of a folio in the Schoyen Collection, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0","alt":"A wide palm-leaf-shaped manuscript folio written in Devanagari, eight close-set lines of ink on aged paper, with small numerals written above the letters."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the period named for a literature rather than a state?","a":"Because what defines it is the body of Sanskrit texts called the Vedas, not any political formation that can be traced in the ground."},{"q":"Why did the Atharvaveda join the other three only after a struggle?","a":"Because it is later in its language and much of its matter, where the other three were together called the threefold knowledge."},{"q":"Why are the hymns not folk poetry?","a":"Because they are the skilled work of a priestly class, made chiefly to accompany the pressing of soma and the offering of melted butter to the gods."}]},
{"id":"wh-241","num":241,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Falling into ten cycles, six of them the work of single families of seers, the 1,028 hymns of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> make it the oldest surviving book of the subcontinent.","questions":["About 250 hymns celebrate Indra and at least 200 Agni across the ten cycles of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose ninth book is given over entirely to Soma.","So close is its language to Avestan that whole stanzas of one can be turned word for word into the other, which places the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> near the root of Indo-Iranian."],"answer":"Rigveda","answerText":"Rigveda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Rigveda</b> is the oldest of the four Vedas and the oldest surviving book of the Indian subcontinent, a collection of hymns in Vedic Sanskrit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That language is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan, and it reached the subcontinent with the spread of Indo-European speech in the middle to late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stands so close to Avestan that whole stanzas of the one can be turned word for word into the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surviving Shakala recension holds 1,017 hymns, or 1,028 counting the eleven Valakhilya hymns inserted in the middle of the eighth book, where another school counted 1,025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are grouped in ten books called mandalas, or cycles, and the tradition counts 10,580 verses, 153,826 words and 432,000 syllables in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Books 2 to 7 are the family books, each handed down within the family of seers that made it and each arranged on the same plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Books 1, 8 and 10 are not one family's work but sets of groups gathered by authorship, and the ninth is unified instead by its subject, every hymn in it addressed to Soma, a collection put together only after the first eight books had been combined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 250 hymns celebrate Indra, nearly a quarter of the whole, and at least 200 celebrate Agni, the fire of the sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hymns ask for wealth in cattle, for offspring, for long life and for victory, and in the later ones purely abstract powers such as Wrath and Faith are deified for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of the latest turns on the origin of things and closes by doubting whether even the highest god knows how the world began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","quote":{"book":"rigveda","n":"966","cite":"Hymn 10.129","text":"<p>Who verily knows and who can here declare it, whence it was born and whence comes this creation? The Gods are later than this world's production. Who knows then whence it first came into being?</p><p>He, the first origin of this creation, whether he formed it all or did not form it, Whose eye controls this world in highest heaven, he verily knows it, or perhaps he knows not.</p>"},"sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology</i>, Grundriss der Indo-Arischen Philologie und Altertumskunde III.1.A (Strassburg: Karl J. Trübner, 1897), https://archive.org/stream/vedicmythology00macd_0/vedicmythology00macd_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Niels Schoubben, “Animacy, postpositions, and the spatial cases in Niya Prakrit,” <i>Journal of South Asian Languages and Linguistics</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12458993/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]","Ralph T. H. Griffith, trans., <i>The Hymns of the Rigveda</i>, vol. 1 (Benares: E. J. Lazarus and Co., 1889), https://archive.org/stream/hymnsrigveda02grifgoog/hymnsrigveda02grifgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","history","literature","language","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Rigveda_MS2097.jpg","title":"Two folios of a Rigveda manuscript, India, early 19th century","desc":"The text opens with the invocation and the first hymn to Agni; the red marks above and below the line are the pitch accents.","credit":"Manuscript MS 2097, Schoyen Collection, via Wikimedia Commons; public domain","alt":"Two paper folios written in Devanagari, ruled in red at the margins, with small red strokes marked above and below the letters."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the Rigveda stand so close to Avestan?","a":"Because whole stanzas of the one can be turned word for word into the other, the two languages being that near in their oldest recorded forms."},{"q":"Why are books 2 to 7 called the family books?","a":"Because each was handed down within the family of seers that made it and each is arranged on the same plan, unlike books 1, 8 and 10, which gather groups by authorship."},{"q":"Why is the ninth book unlike the rest?","a":"Because it is unified by subject rather than authorship, every hymn addressed to Soma, and was put together only after the first eight books had been combined."}]},
{"id":"wh-242","num":242,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Meaning put together, and so refined, the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is set against the Prakrits, the popular dialects it was distinguished from.","questions":["About four thousand rules written like algebraic formulae, in which brevity is sought at the cost of clarity, fixed the grammar of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 4th century BCE.","Official inscriptions ran in Middle Indian for centuries after Ashoka, yet verses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inside those same inscriptions show that it was still a living literary language."],"answer":"Sanskrit","answerText":"Sanskrit","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vedic Sanskrit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Panini's grammar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Sanskrit</b> is the classical language of the Indian subcontinent, and its name, samskrita, means literally 'put together', so 'refined' or 'elaborate'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is set against the Prakrits, the popular dialects, and the word is absent from the older grammarians but turns up in the earliest epic, the Ramayana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vedic Sanskrit is the oldest recorded stage of Indo-Aryan, from which Middle Indo-Aryan and then the modern languages of the north descend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The family reached the subcontinent with the spread of Indo-European speech in the middle to late Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its alphabet, worked out on phonetic principles, was already settled before the 4th century BCE and has not been altered since, the simple vowels first, then the diphthongs, then the consonants in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Panini's grammar, of about the 4th century BCE, casts the language into some four thousand rules written like algebraic formulae, in which brevity alone is sought, at the cost of being clear or unambiguous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It superseded every predecessor, came to be treated as an infallible authority, and the whole later literature of India was pressed into the mould it made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not a language of books alone: a later grammarian speaks of it as used in the world and calls its words current in the world, and Panini gives rules for the accent of calling from a distance, of salutation, and of question and answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the time of Ashoka, official decrees were composed in Middle Indian dialects for centuries, yet Sanskrit verses set inside those inscriptions show it living on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its liturgy is still in the keeping of groups of traditionally priestly status, who carry more steppe ancestry than a simple mixture model predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","William Dwight Whitney, <i>Sanskrit Grammar, Including Both the Classical Language and the Older Dialects of Veda and Brahmana</i> (Leipzig: Breitkopf and Härtel, 1879), https://archive.org/stream/sanskritgrammari00whit_0/sanskritgrammari00whit_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Niels Schoubben, “Animacy, postpositions, and the spatial cases in Niya Prakrit,” <i>Journal of South Asian Languages and Linguistics</i> 12, no. 2 (2025): 87, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12458993/. [Open access]","Rishabh Jain, Sachin M. Rathod, Pankaj Jha et al., “Layers in the sand: The genetic imprint of migration, culture, and Indus craft in the Thar Desert,” <i>Human Genetics and Genomics Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 100623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13213775/. [Open access]","Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos, Swapan Mallick, Iosif Lazaridis et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","language","literature","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Junagadh_inscription_of_Rudradaman_%28portion%29.jpg","title":"Part of the Junagadh rock inscription of Rudradaman","desc":"A rubbing of a Sanskrit prose inscription at Girnar in Gujarat, of the 2nd century CE, with the plate's line numbers down the margin.","credit":"Plate by J. F. Fleet, via Wikimedia Commons; public domain","alt":"A black-and-white rubbing of an inscription cut into rough rock, lines of angular letters showing white against the dark stone, numbered down the right margin."},"why":[{"q":"Why is Panini's grammar written so tersely?","a":"Because its some four thousand rules are cast like algebraic formulae in which brevity alone is sought, at the cost of being clear or unambiguous."},{"q":"Why is it wrong to think of Sanskrit as a language of books alone?","a":"Because a later grammarian calls its words current in the world, and Panini gives rules for the accent of calling from a distance, of salutation, and of question and answer."},{"q":"Why does the name itself carry a contrast?","a":"Because samskrita means 'put together', so refined or elaborate, and it is set against the Prakrits, the popular dialects."}]},
{"id":"wh-243","num":243,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Dug since 1959 at Yanshi in Henan, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> centred a four-level settlement hierarchy on one great site in the Yiluo river basin.","questions":["Palaces of unprecedented scale and craft under elite supervision belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose towns were nonetheless shrinking, unfortified, and without segregated elite burials.","Archaeologists went looking for the Xia in the ground where the records place them, and what they found instead was named the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is disputed to this day."],"answer":"Erlitou culture","answerText":"Erlitou culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Yiluo river basin, Henan</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭文化","hanzi":"二里头文化","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu wénhuà","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Èrlǐtóu wénhuà</span></div>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Erlitou culture</b> is the Bronze Age culture of the central plains of northern China, of about 1900 to 1500 BCE, named after its type site at Yanshi in Henan province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the earliest bronze-casting culture of central China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Archaeologists found the site in 1959 while looking for remains of the Xia people in the country where the ancient records place them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it belongs to the Xia or to the Shang has been argued ever since, several hundred articles deep by the middle of the 1990s and still unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some scholars connect its rise to the climatic deterioration around 2000 BCE that is thought to have brought down the Late Neolithic settlements of the Longshan period before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its setting is the Yiluo river basin, a rich alluvial pocket between the modern provinces of Shanxi and Henan, which through the 3rd millennium BCE grew from nine roughly equal villages of no more than 150 people into a chiefdom of two and then three tiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What follows is a stratified and bureaucratically governed state on four settlement levels, 148 large villages, 43 supravillages, 10 small centres and Erlitou itself above them, holding a population estimated at between about 28,000 and 82,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its palaces are of unprecedented scale and its crafts were made under elite supervision, and yet the towns were shrinking, no fortifications have been found, and the cemeteries hold no separated elite burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its drinking and pouring vessels travelled far: 24 of them were buried in thirteen elaborate tombs at Dadianzi, away to the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And its bronzes carry no highly radiogenic lead, the metal that arrives only with the Erligang phase that follows it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Xuelian Zhang, Shihua Qiu, Lianzhen Cai, Hong Xu, Haitao Zhao and Guoliang Chen, “14C Dating of the Erlitou Site,” <i>Radiocarbon</i> 64, no. 4 (2022): 819–32, https://doi.org/10.1017/RDC.2021.72. [Paywalled]","David S. Sandeford, “Organizational complexity and demographic scale in primary states,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 5, no. 5 (2018): 171137, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.171137. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi, Lingling Dai, Marie Balasse, Chunqing Zhao, Jiangtao Gao et al., “Social Complexification and Pig (<i>Sus scrofa</i>) Husbandry in Ancient China: A Combined Geometric Morphometric and Isotopic Approach,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 7 (2016): e0158523, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0158523. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Xiaojia Tang, Shui Shen, Xin Su et al., “From rammed earth to stone wall: Chronological insight into the settlement change of the Lower Xiajiadian culture,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0273161, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0273161. [Open access]"],"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/20260520_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg/1280px-20260520_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg","title":"Bronze jue from the Erlitou site, Yanshi","desc":"A three-legged bronze pouring vessel of the shape called a jue, excavated at Erlitou in Yanshi, Henan, and now in the Henan Museum. Vessels like this one are among the earliest cast bronzes known from the central plains; the metal has weathered to a green and brown crust.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A small dark bronze vessel on three thin splayed legs, with a long narrow trough spout rising at one side and a pointed tail at the other, its surface pitted and streaked with green corrosion, lit against a dark museum background."},"locator":{"name":"Erlitou culture","at":[112.689,34.693],"kind":"region","area":[[110.2,34.2],[110.5,35.6],[111.8,36.2],[113.2,35.8],[114.3,34.9],[114,33.9],[112.8,33.4],[111.2,33.6]]},"why":[{"q":"Why has the culture's dynastic identity been argued for decades?","a":"Because the site was found while looking for remains of the Xia in the country the ancient records place them, and whether it belongs to the Xia or the Shang was several hundred articles deep by the mid-1990s and is still unsettled."},{"q":"Why is its social picture an awkward one?","a":"Because its palaces are of unprecedented scale and its crafts were made under elite supervision, and yet the towns were shrinking, no fortifications have been found, and the cemeteries hold no separated elite burials."},{"q":"Why do its bronzes distinguish it from what follows?","a":"Because they carry no highly radiogenic lead, the metal that arrives only with the Erligang phase after it."}]},
{"id":"wh-244","num":244,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The transmitted Chinese records make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first of the dynasties, founded by Yu the Great, who earned the throne by mastering a catastrophic flood.","questions":["Only three chapters about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived into the <i>Book of Documents</i>, which is nearly all the Chinese canon preserves of the first of its dynasties.","Tang the Successful dethroned and banished the tyrant Jie to end the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then feared that later ages would quote his rebellion as an excuse for their own."],"answer":"Xia dynasty","answerText":"Xia dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2205 – 1767 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also given as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21st – 16th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"夏朝","hanzi":"夏朝","pinyin":"Xià cháo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Xià cháo</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Xia dynasty</b> is the first of the dynasties in the transmitted Chinese records, placed before the Shang and dated in Chinese scholarship to the 21st to 16th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It takes its name from Xia, a small principality in Henan of which Yu the Great was made earl before the throne came to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yu and his descendants are said to have held that throne for 439 years, and the traditional reckoning runs from about 2205 to 1767 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Very little of them reaches the Chinese canon: only three documents of Xia went into the <i>Book of Documents</i>, either because the recorder's duty was hardly exercised so early or because what he wrote was almost wholly lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The last of the line was Jie, a tyrant in the records, whom Tang the Successful dethroned and kept in banishment; Tang was then ashamed, afraid that later ages would quote the deed as an apology for rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Chinese scholarship has generally identified the dynasty with the Bronze Age remains at Erlitou in Yanshi, and it was that identification which carried the beginning of Chinese civilisation back beyond the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Whether those remains are Xia or early Shang is not settled, and the search that turned them up was itself a search for the Xia in the country the ancient documents give them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others place a Xia capital at Xinzhai in Henan instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The flood story has been pressed into service as well: an outburst flood dated to about 1920 BCE has been offered as the historical Great Flood, fixing the dynasty's start near 1900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> But the settlement that case rests on has been read as destroyed by an earthquake rather than by water, and the Great Flood's part in the making of the early dynasties is not confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Xuelian Zhang, Shihua Qiu, Lianzhen Cai, Hong Xu, Haitao Zhao and Guoliang Chen, “14C Dating of the Erlitou Site,” <i>Radiocarbon</i> 64, no. 4 (2022): 819–32, https://doi.org/10.1017/RDC.2021.72. [Paywalled]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Confucianism, Part I</i>, 2nd ed., Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1899), 63–64, 84–86, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofeas0003jame. [Open access]","Thomas Cucchi, Lingling Dai, Marie Balasse, Chunqing Zhao, Jiangtao Gao et al., “Social Complexification and Pig (<i>Sus scrofa</i>) Husbandry in Ancient China: A Combined Geometric Morphometric and Isotopic Approach,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 7 (2016): e0158523, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0158523. [Open access]","Peijun Shi, Fenggui Liu, Xingmin Meng, Qiang Zhou, Deyong Yu et al., “Recent Jishishan earthquake ripple hazard provides a new explanation for the destruction of the prehistoric Lajia Settlement 4000a B.P.,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 11630, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-60433-8. [Open access]","Qinglong Wu, Zhijun Zhao, Li Liu, Darryl E. Granger, Hui Wang et al., “Outburst flood at 1920 BCE supports historicity of China’s Great Flood and the Xia dynasty,” <i>Science</i> 353, no. 6299 (2016): 579–82, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aaf0842. [Paywalled]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d’évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Yu_ji_tu._LOC_2002626770.jpg/1280px-Yu_ji_tu._LOC_2002626770.jpg","title":"Yu ji tu, the Map of the Tracks of Yu","desc":"A rubbing of the Yu ji tu, engraved on stone in 1136 CE and now in the Forest of Stone Steles Museum in Xi'an. It is named for the flood-taming journeys the records attribute to the founder of the Xia, and lays the Yellow River and the Yangtze in heavy line over a grid whose squares each stand for about a hundred li. The sheet reproduced here was published in 1903 and carries a later cataloguer's pencilled notes and a library stamp.","credit":"Library of Congress, Geography and Map Division (https://www.loc.gov/item/2002626770/), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A map of China drawn in black ink over a fine grid of squares, the Yellow River and the Yangtze picked out as heavy winding lines and hundreds of small Chinese place names filling the land, with a title cartouche in the upper left and pencilled notes and a library stamp along the bottom edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why does so little of the dynasty reach the Chinese canon?","a":"Because only three documents of Xia went into the Book of Documents, either because the recorder's duty was hardly exercised so early or because what he wrote was almost wholly lost."},{"q":"Why was Tang ashamed of deposing Jie?","a":"Because he feared later ages would quote the deed as an apology for rebellion, whatever the records say of the tyrant he replaced."},{"q":"Why is the flood argument for dating the dynasty not settled?","a":"Because the settlement the case rests on has been read as destroyed by an earthquake rather than by water, and the Great Flood's part in the early dynasties is not confirmed."}]},
{"id":"wh-245","num":245,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Ruling along the Yellow River from about 1600 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the Bronze Age dynasty of the central plains and the earliest dynasty archaeology can vouch for.","questions":["A ditch in the palace area at Zhengzhou held nearly a hundred human skulls, most likely prisoners taken while the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was conquering the country around Anyang.","King Wu of Zhou overthrew the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in about 1046 BCE, and the people of one cemetery in Henan are thought to be its survivors, moved off by the victors."],"answer":"Shang dynasty","answerText":"Shang dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商朝","hanzi":"商朝","pinyin":"Shāng cháo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Shāng cháo</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Shang dynasty</b> ruled along the Yellow River from about 1600 to 1046 BCE, and it is the earliest dynasty of Chinese history that archaeological evidence supports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the Bronze Age dynasty of the central plains, and the copper objects it left carry highly radiogenic lead whose source is one of the enduring puzzles of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its writing is the oracle bone inscriptions, tens of thousands of them, the earliest known Chinese characters and the source of most of what is known of Shang life, politics and religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seat of the dynasty moved more than once: Zhengzhou and Anyang were both capitals of it, and frequent relocations are among the changes visible in the central plains over these centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chinese excavations at Yinxu in Henan in 1928 put the beginning of the ancient civilisation of China at the end of the dynasty, a starting point later work has pushed a great deal further back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Zhengzhou Shang City, dated to about 1509 to 1315 BCE, was the capital of the early dynasty, and what has been found there is chiefly of the Erligang period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1974 a ditch 15 metres (50 feet) long, holding nearly a hundred human skulls, was excavated in its palace area, and strontium, carbon and nitrogen isotopes point to prisoners of war taken as Shang forces conquered the country around Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The late dynasty left a record of its own weather: about 55,000 pieces of inscribed bone carry written observations spanning roughly two centuries, enough to index rainfall and flooding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its burials divide the living sharply, yet eleven genomes from a late Shang cemetery at Xingyang show no genetic difference between the high-grade tombs and the low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the transmitted account King Wu of Zhou conquered the dynasty in about 1046 BCE, and the people of that cemetery are taken for Shang who were moved away from the capital by the victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Jiaxin Tang, Rui Wang, Qingli Wei, Haifeng He, Chen Deng et al., “Social Stratification Without Genetic Differentiation at the Xisima Site in the Late Shang Dynasty,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 43, no. 1 (2026): msaf316, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12770819/. [Open access]","Fang Fang, Jingwen Liao, Xiaomin Zeng and Juzhong Zhang, “The Truth of Unusual Deaths under Military Expansion: Evidence from the Stable Isotopes of a Human Skull Ditch in the Capital City of the Early Shang Dynasty, China,” <i>Genes</i> 13, no. 11 (2022): 2077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9690788/. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d’évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Shang_Dynasty_City_Wall%2C_Zhengzhou_09.jpg/1280px-Shang_Dynasty_City_Wall%2C_Zhengzhou_09.jpg","title":"The Shang city wall at Zhengzhou","desc":"A stretch of the rammed-earth wall of Zhengzhou Shang City, the capital of the early dynasty, still standing as a low bank through the modern city in Henan. The site is dated to about 1509 to 1315 BCE; a footpath now runs along the top of it.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A broad bank of bare packed earth planted with young trees, running away from the viewer as a low ridge with a worn footpath along its crest, apartment blocks and a building under construction standing in the haze beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this the earliest dynasty archaeology supports?","a":"Because it left the oracle bone inscriptions, tens of thousands of them, the earliest known Chinese characters and the source of most of what is known of Shang life, politics and religion."},{"q":"Why does the Xingyang cemetery complicate the picture of Shang rank?","a":"Because its burials divide the living sharply, and yet eleven genomes from it show no genetic difference between the high-grade tombs and the low."},{"q":"Why can the late dynasty's own weather be studied?","a":"Because about 55,000 pieces of inscribed bone carry written observations spanning roughly two centuries, enough to index rainfall and flooding."}]},
{"id":"wh-246","num":246,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Carved on turtle plastrons and ox shoulder blades that had been drilled and cracked with heat, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest form of systematic Chinese writing.","questions":["More than 160,000 inscribed pieces carry the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and of the four thousand-odd distinct signs it uses only about a thousand have been read with confidence.","In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a sign first drawn as a kind of wheat came to write the verb ‘to come’, the two words having sounded the same."],"answer":"oracle bone script","answerText":"oracle bone script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the late Shang, at Anyang</span></div>","traditional":"甲骨文","hanzi":"甲骨文","pinyin":"jiǎgǔwén","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">jiǎgǔwén</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Oracle bone script</b>, jiaguwen in Chinese, is the earliest form of systematic Chinese writing, carved on turtle plastrons and the shoulder blades of cattle in the late Shang, from about 1250 to 1045 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bones were prepared by drilling pits into them and cracking them with heat, and what is written on them are divination records made for the royalty and nobility, most of them found at Anyang in the central plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 160,000 inscribed pieces are known, carrying some four thousand distinct signs, and by another count more than 4,600.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only about a thousand of those have been deciphered, their meanings and their corresponding modern Chinese characters confirmed; the rest are known as shapes rather than as words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was already a fully developed writing system when it first appears, and the bones excavated at Huayuanzhuang East in Yinxu belong to the reign of King Wu Ding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Many of the signs began as pictures of what they name, and one of the main characters for disaster is a pattern resembling water waves, which is taken to mean flooding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The script also borrowed sound: a sign first drawn as a kind of wheat came to write the verb ‘to come’, the two words having been pronounced alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That device is called jiajie in Chinese scholarship and the rebus principle elsewhere, and it is what lets a picture stand for a word no picture could show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Semantic association dominated the early formation of the signs, and a shift towards writing sound is visible afterwards, in the bronze inscriptions of the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading a bone is not a matter of running down the lines, since the order moves about the surface, and one sign may be written in strikingly different ways from one piece to the next, so a rubbing, a hand-drawn facsimile and a transcription are consulted together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","writing","language","china"],"sources":["Ludovica Ottaviano and Silvia Ferrara, “Mechanisms at the core of the Chinese script invention,” <i>Cognitive Linguistics</i> 37, no. 1 (2026): 39–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949599/. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu et al., “An open dataset for oracle bone character recognition and decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03807-x. [Open access]","Bang Li, Jing Yang, Yujie Liang, Xiaobin Hu, Zengmao Ding et al., “OBIMD: A Multi-modal Dataset for Contextual Interpretation of Oracle Bone Inscriptions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 13 (2026): 681, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-026-06967-0. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Mei Wang and Weihong Deng, “A dataset of oracle characters for benchmarking machine learning algorithms,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 87, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-02933-w. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_%28for_divination%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_%28for_divination%29.jpg","title":"Inscribed ox scapula from the reign of Wu Ding","desc":"An oracle bone of about 1200 BCE, carrying the work of the Bin group of diviners, who belong to the first period of the late Shang and so to the reign of King Wu Ding. The incised characters have been filled with red pigment to make them legible; the bone has broken along its lower edge.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A broad pale plate of bone, roughly triangular and cracked across the bottom, its whole surface covered in columns of small angular incised characters picked out in dark red."},"why":[{"q":"Why can only about a thousand of the signs be read?","a":"Because of the four thousand or more distinct signs, only that many have had their meanings and corresponding modern characters confirmed; the rest are known as shapes rather than as words."},{"q":"Why does the rebus principle matter for the script's development?","a":"Because it lets a picture stand for a word no picture could show — a sign first drawn as a kind of wheat came to write the verb 'to come', the two words being pronounced alike."},{"q":"Why is reading a bone not a matter of running down the lines?","a":"Because the order moves about the surface, and one sign may be written in strikingly different ways from piece to piece, so a rubbing, a facsimile and a transcription are consulted together."}]},
{"id":"wh-247","num":247,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Last capital of the Shang and one of the largest metal consumers in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew its metal from more than 500 km (300 miles) away.","questions":["All the kingly tombs of the Shang lie in one cemetery at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where thousands of lesser graves were dug over four phases and most of the rich ones were robbed in antiquity.","An extinct water buffalo was first named from bones found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Shang capital whose oracle bones carry most of what is known of the dynasty's divinations."],"answer":"Yinxu","answerText":"Yinxu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb M1004</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1243 – 1157 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"殷墟","hanzi":"殷墟","pinyin":"Yīnxū","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Yīnxū</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Yinxu</b>, the ruins of Yin, is the site of the last capital of dynastic Shang China, at Anyang in northern Henan, and it was occupied from about 1250 to 1045 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists divide it into four chronological phases, Yinxu I to IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of the largest metal consumers in Eurasia during the 2nd millennium BCE, and the metal was brought in from regions more than 500 km (300 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what is known of Shang divination comes from this ground: the oracle bones were mostly found at Anyang, and are the divination records of the royalty and nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bones excavated at Huayuanzhuang East belong to the reign of King Wu Ding and are kept at the Anyang workstation of the Institute of Archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The dead are the site's other archive: all the kingly tombs lie at Xibeigang, and thousands more graves fill the Western Area, taken for cluster burials of lineage members across all four phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rank is legible in them, in the quality and quantity of the metal, in the inscriptions on the bronzes, in the numbers of human and animal sacrifices, and in the size, position and orientation of the burial chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the rich tombs were looted in antiquity, though M1004, a kingly tomb dated to the site's second phase between 1243 and 1157 BCE, still gave up 941 weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An extinct water buffalo, <i>Bubalus mephistopheles</i>, was first discovered and named from bones found here, and is thought to have died out before the Eastern Zhou, around three thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chinese excavation began at the site in 1928, and what came out of it was what first carried the ancient civilisation of China back to the end of the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d’évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu et al., “An open dataset for oracle bone character recognition and decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03807-x. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Xue-Yuan Liang, Xin-Meng Liu, Xiao-Le Lei, Qingbo Hu, Xiaoning Guo et al., “Ancient mitogenomes reveal the genetic relationship among extinct and extant water buffaloes,” <i>iScience</i> 29 (2026): 116354, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13276306/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Oracle_bones_pit.JPG/1280px-Oracle_bones_pit.JPG","title":"Pit YH127 of oracle bones at Yinxu","desc":"A round shaft sunk into the earth at Yinxu and packed with inscribed bone and turtle plastron, shown here as the pit is presented on the site. A human skeleton lies against the far wall. Pits like this one held the divination records of the late Shang court in their thousands.","credit":"Photograph by Xuan Che, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Looking down into a round pit cut in pale earth, its floor heaped with hundreds of overlapping flat pieces of bone and turtle shell, a human skeleton lying along one side, the whole lit from above against dark surrounding ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the site an archive twice over?","a":"Because most of what is known of Shang divination comes from oracle bones found here, and the burials are a second record: all the kingly tombs at Xibeigang and thousands of graves in the Western Area."},{"q":"Why is rank legible in the graves?","a":"Because it shows in the quality and quantity of the metal, the inscriptions on the bronzes, the numbers of human and animal sacrifices, and the size, position and orientation of the burial chamber."},{"q":"Why does M1004 matter despite the looting?","a":"Because most of the rich tombs were robbed in antiquity, and this kingly tomb still gave up 941 weapons."}]},
{"id":"wh-248","num":248,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Cast for banqueting and for offering food and drink to the ancestors, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have no real parallel elsewhere in Bronze Age Eurasia, where the metal went mostly into weapons and tools.","questions":["Ceramic moulds and cores fired at surprisingly low temperatures made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible, and casting them was so closely controlled that the workshops cluster around the Shang capital.","About 21,000 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry inscriptions, from clan emblems of a few characters under the early Shang to Western Zhou texts running to 500 characters."],"answer":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerText":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under the Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1045 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"青銅禮器","hanzi":"青铜礼器","pinyin":"qīngtóng lǐqì","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">qīngtóng lǐqì</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Chinese ritual bronzes</b> are the vessels cast in enormous numbers under the Shang and Zhou dynasties, between about 1600 and 221 BCE, and used in banqueting, in offering food and drink for ancestor worship, and in signalling rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making them was the most prominent handicraft industry of Bronze Age China, and it was bound into the ritual and political systems of both dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The preference for putting bronze into vessels is peculiar to China, since elsewhere in Bronze Age Eurasia the typical products were weapons, tools and personal ornaments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Great quantities of copper, tin and lead went into them, and they were then buried in the tombs of the elite, taken out of circulation for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Casting carried symbolic weight and was tightly controlled: the workshops cluster around the Shang capital at Yinxu, and a vessel mould found far from it is a rarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They were cast in ceramic moulds and cores, and it is those moulds that made the complex casting methods used in early China possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The moulds of the early Shang were fired remarkably cool, in many cases at only 200 to 300 °C (390 to 570 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 21,000 of the bronzes carry inscriptions, from the clan emblems of a few signs that appear in the early Shang to Western Zhou texts running to 500 characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The metal itself divides the ranks: the highest elite had vessels alloyed to a controlled recipe from very pure copper, while the lower elites got metal that had been mixed and recycled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One question about them is still open, since where the highly radiogenic lead in the copper objects of the Shang came from is among the standing enigmas of Bronze Age China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]","Yunbing Zong, Shengkun Yao, Jianfeng Lang, Xuexiang Chen, Jiadong Fan et al., “Structural and compositional analysis of a casting mold sherd from ancient China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 3 (2017): e0174057, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0174057. [Open access]","Bichen Yan, Siran Liu, Matthew L. Chastain, Shugang Yang and Jianli Chen, “A new FTIR method for estimating the firing temperature of ceramic bronze-casting moulds from early China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 3316, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-82806-z. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Ludovica Ottaviano and Silvia Ferrara, “Mechanisms at the core of the Chinese script invention,” <i>Cognitive Linguistics</i> 37, no. 1 (2026): 39–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949599/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Shang_Bronze_Niu_%28Ox%29_Zun%2C_M54_Tomb.jpg/1280px-Shang_Bronze_Niu_%28Ox%29_Zun%2C_M54_Tomb.jpg","title":"Bronze ox zun from tomb M54 at Yinxu","desc":"A wine vessel of the type called a zun, cast in the form of a standing ox with a lidded opening in its back, from tomb M54 at Yinxu in Anyang. Every surface, the horns and hooves included, carries fine relief ornament of spirals and animal masks; the metal has weathered green and reddish brown.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A small bronze vessel shaped as a stocky standing ox with curved horns and a raised knob on the hinged lid set into its back, its whole body covered in dense low-relief scrollwork, lit against a dark museum background."},"why":[{"q":"Why is putting bronze into vessels peculiar to China?","a":"Because elsewhere in Bronze Age Eurasia the typical products were weapons, tools and personal ornaments, where here great quantities of copper, tin and lead went into ritual vessels and then into tombs."},{"q":"Why was casting tightly controlled?","a":"Because it carried symbolic weight: the workshops cluster around the Shang capital at Yinxu, and a vessel mould found far from it is a rarity."},{"q":"Why does the metal itself divide the ranks?","a":"Because the highest elite had vessels alloyed to a controlled recipe from very pure copper, while the lower elites got metal that had been mixed and recycled."}]},
{"id":"wh-249","num":249,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Only one top royal burial at Anyang has been found unrobbed, and it is the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a queen and one of the consorts of King Wu Ding.","questions":["About 1.6 tonnes (1.8 tons) of metal came out of the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and her vessels were alloyed from very pure copper where lesser people got recycled stock.","A jade parrot from the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first scientific evidence that the Shang burnt jade in the offering the records call the Liao sacrifice."],"answer":"Fu Hao","answerText":"Fu Hao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1045 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Yinxu, Anyang</span></div>","traditional":"婦好","hanzi":"妇好","pinyin":"Fù Hǎo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Fù Hǎo</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Fu Hao</b> was a queen of the Shang and one of the royal consorts of King Wu Ding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She is known from inscriptions cast into high-quality bronze ritual vessels, whose names have been matched against the same names in the records on the oracle bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those records are the divinations of the Shang royalty and nobility, and they were found for the most part at Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bones excavated at Huayuanzhuang East belong to Wu Ding's own reign, and of the more than 4,600 signs the corpus uses only about a thousand have been deciphered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her tomb is the only intact top royal burial so far excavated at Anyang, and something like 1.6 tonnes (1.8 tons) of metal came out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is one of only a small handful of tombs at the site with more than a hundred bronzes that were found unrobbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The metal grades its owner: her ritual vessels were alloyed to a controlled recipe from very pure copper, where people of lower rank in the western cemetery had bronze that had been mixed and recycled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vessels were cast in ceramic moulds and cores, the mould-making that made the complex casting methods of early China possible and was the most prominent handicraft industry of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A jade parrot from her tomb has given the first scientific evidence that the Shang burnt jade in the offering called the Liao sacrifice, confirming what the oracle bone inscriptions and later records say of the rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For all that, she was not the greatest of the Shang dead: the kingly tombs at Xibeigang run to as much as seven times the size of hers, though every one of them was looted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]","Rong Wang, Chang-sui Wang and Ji-gen Tang, “A jade parrot from the tomb of Fu Hao at Yinxu and Liao sacrifices of the Shang Dynasty,” <i>Antiquity</i> 92, no. 362 (2018): 368–82, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.220. [Paywalled]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu et al., “An open dataset for oracle bone character recognition and decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03807-x. [Open access]","Ke Ding, Siyang Li, Aijun Ding, Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang et al., “Archeological data with AI- and physics-based modeling explain typhoon-induced disasters in inland China around 3000 yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 10 (2026): eaeb1598, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12959396/. [Open access]","Bichen Yan, Siran Liu, Matthew L. Chastain, Shugang Yang and Jianli Chen, “A new FTIR method for estimating the firing temperature of ceramic bronze-casting moulds from early China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 3316, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-82806-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/20260408_Bronze_Rectangular_Ding_with_Kui-Dragon_ShapedFeet_and_Inscription_of_Fu_Hao.jpg/1280px-20260408_Bronze_Rectangular_Ding_with_Kui-Dragon_ShapedFeet_and_Inscription_of_Fu_Hao.jpg","title":"Rectangular ding from the tomb of Fu Hao","desc":"A four-legged bronze cauldron from Fu Hao's tomb at Yinxu, now in the Henan Museum, its feet cast in the form of kui dragons and its sides carrying bands of animal ornament. The museum records it as bearing her name, which is the kind of inscription by which the tomb's occupant was identified.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A deep rectangular bronze vessel, thickly encrusted with green corrosion, standing on four legs shaped as curling dragons, with two upright loop handles rising from its rim and relief ornament across each side, lit in a dark museum case."},"why":[{"q":"Why is she known at all?","a":"Because inscriptions cast into high-quality bronze ritual vessels carry names that have been matched against the same names in the oracle bone records."},{"q":"Why is her tomb so important to the study of the site?","a":"Because it is the only intact top royal burial so far excavated at Anyang, one of a small handful with more than a hundred bronzes found unrobbed."},{"q":"Why was she not the greatest of the Shang dead?","a":"Because the kingly tombs at Xibeigang run to as much as seven times the size of hers — though every one of them was looted."}]},
{"id":"wh-250","num":250,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The sacrificial pits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Sichuan were packed with ivory laid over bronzes, gold and jade, the leading site of the ancient Shu culture of the Chengdu Plain.","questions":["Carnelian beads from the pits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were cut from stone quarried more than 1,000 km (600 miles) to the north, the earliest such evidence in Bronze Age China.","Residues found in the ash layers at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show that silk was used there as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth."],"answer":"Sanxingdui","answerText":"Sanxingdui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pit K4</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3,148 – 2,966 BP</span></div>","traditional":"三星堆","hanzi":"三星堆","pinyin":"Sānxīngduī","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sānxīngduī</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Sanxingdui</b> is a Bronze Age site in Sichuan, in the western suburbs of Guanghan on the south bank of the Yazi river, and it is the leading representative of the ancient Shu culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That culture runs from the late Neolithic to the early Western Zhou, and the pits for which the site is known belong to about 1200 to 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The founding population of the Shu came chiefly from the millet and rice farming communities of East Asia, with only limited gene flow from the pastoralists of the western steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sacrificial pits lie in the core of the site and hold ivory, seashells, bronze, pottery, jade, stone, gold, bone and horn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of them were found in 1986 and six more by 2019, excavated the year after; radiocarbon puts the fourth at 3,148 to 2,966 years before the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They were filled in a deliberate order, the ivory laid over the bronzes, the gold and the jade beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Layers of ash lie above the artefacts, and silk residues found in them are evidence that silk was used here as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bronzes tie the place to the rest of Bronze Age China: over half of those found at Sanxingdui carry the same highly radiogenic lead as the vessels of the Central Plains, and it appears in both at virtually the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eleven carnelian beads from the pits were cut from stone that does not come from southern China at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their raw material lies more than 1,000 km (600 miles) to the north, which is the earliest direct geochemical evidence for long-distance carnelian exchange in Bronze Age China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Ruru Chen, Zhenbin Xie, Qing Xiao, Chong Wang and Rui Wen, “Insight into the microbial diversity and community in the sacrificial pits of Sanxingdui site (Sichuan, China),” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 15 (2024): 1489025, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2024.1489025. [Open access]","Hailing Zheng, Ruling Yang, Jianbo Guo, Zhenbin Xie, Jingxuan Liu et al., “Evidence of the use of silk by bronze age civilization for sacrificial purposes in the Yangtze River basin of China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29175, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-78687-7. [Open access]","Meiting Yan, Jiancheng Liu, Chunlei Qin, Xuemei He, Xiaoguang Li et al., “Sourcing the origins of carnelian in early Chinese civilizations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 7 (2026): e2524563123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913004/. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Lisiteng Luo, Yanling Liu, Yalan Liu, Yunhui Liu, Jie Zhong et al., “Paternal genomic history and Y-chromosome phylogeny from the Chengdu Plain reveal multiple Indigenous East Asian farmer contributions to ancient Shu,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 24, no. 1 (2026): 54, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-026-02527-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_2.jpg/1280px-Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_2.jpg","title":"Bronze mask from Sanxingdui","desc":"One of the cast bronze faces from the sacrificial pits at Sanxingdui, now in the museum on the site. The eyes are enormous almonds with the pupils standing proud of them, the nose is a blade, and the ears are broad wings pierced for fixing; the metal has weathered to grey and green.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A large bronze face seen from one side on a museum turntable, with a wide flat brow, a huge almond eye whose pupil projects from the socket, a sharply ridged nose, a long straight mouth and an outsized pointed ear."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the pits filled in a deliberate order?","a":"Because the ivory was laid over the bronzes with the gold and jade beneath it, and layers of ash lie above the artefacts — a sequence rather than a dump."},{"q":"Why do the bronzes tie the site to the rest of Bronze Age China?","a":"Because over half of those found here carry the same highly radiogenic lead as the vessels of the Central Plains, and it appears in both at virtually the same time."},{"q":"Why do eleven carnelian beads matter?","a":"Because their raw material comes from more than 1,000 kilometres to the north, which is the earliest direct geochemical evidence for long-distance carnelian exchange in Bronze Age China."}]},
{"id":"ps-001","num":1,"category":"Psychology","question":"Both a natural and a social science, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> studies mind and behaviour by experiment and measurement, reaching mental processes only through what can be observed or reported.","questions":["Questions about mind and behaviour belonged to philosophy for centuries before Wilhelm Wundt opened the world's first institute for experimental <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Leipzig in 1879.","Much of what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knows rests on Western and largely American samples, and concern about reproducibility has pushed the field towards preregistration, larger samples and open data."],"answer":"psychology","answerText":"psychology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">As a science</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the late 1800s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First institute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879, Leipzig</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Psychology</b> is the scientific study of mind and behaviour, and it puts the methods of science to work on questions people have always asked about why they think, feel and act as they do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behaviour can be watched directly, while mental processes cannot, so they are reached at one remove, through observable behaviour, biological indicators and what people report about themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That double subject matter puts the discipline in two places at once: behaviour is at root biological, which makes it a natural science, and it is shaped by dealings with other people, which makes it a social one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such questions belonged to philosophy until the late 1800s, when psychology came to be accepted as an academic discipline of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conventional marker is Leipzig, where Wilhelm Wundt founded the world's first institute for experimental psychology in 1879, having begun lecturing there four years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The field is many enterprises rather than one: the British Psychological Society recognises nine applied specialisms, among them clinical, forensic, educational, health, occupational and sport psychology, with work running from the living brain to the behaviour of large organisations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What holds them together is method rather than subject matter, since a psychological claim is expected to be put in terms that measurement can bear on and that evidence can count against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Turned on the field's own literature, that standard has proved uncomfortable: nine in ten researchers answering a recent survey in <i>Nature</i> agreed there is a reproducibility crisis, and studies of statistical power persistently find it below, and sometimes well below, 50%.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The remedies now spreading are preregistration of study protocols, registered reports reviewed before the results are known, open data and materials, and funding for replication studies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A second limit is who gets studied, since the literature remains overwhelmingly Western and its data largely American, so for many findings the reach beyond the people they were found in is simply unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","history"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Wilhelm Wundt Institute for Psychology, “History of Experimental Psychology in Leipzig,” Leipzig University, https://www.lw.uni-leipzig.de/en/wilhelm-wundt-institute-for-psychology/working-groups-1/cognitive-and-biological-psychology/history-of-experimental-psychology-in-leipzig. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","Michael Muthukrishna et al., “Beyond Western, Educated, Industrial, Rich, and Democratic (WEIRD) Psychology: Measuring and Mapping Scales of Cultural and Psychological Distance,” <i>Psychological Science</i> 31, no. 6 (2020): 678–701, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7357184/. [Open access]","Marcus R. Munafò et al., “A Manifesto for Reproducible Science,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 1 (2017): 0021, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7610724/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Psychologisch_Laboratorium_Heymans.jpg/1920px-Psychologisch_Laboratorium_Heymans.jpg","title":"psychology","desc":"A room in Gerard Heymans's psychological laboratory at Groningen, photographed in 1923. Collection of the University Museum of Groningen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psychologisch_Laboratorium_Heymans.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a laboratory room: wooden benches carrying a large black acoustic horn and other instruments, a wall panel of switches, dials and gauges, and wires strung across the ceiling."},"why":[{"q":"Why does psychology count as a natural and a social science at once?","a":"Because its subject is behaviour, which is at root biological and so belongs to natural science, while also being shaped by dealings with other people, which puts it among the social sciences."},{"q":"Why does method rather than subject matter hold the field together?","a":"Because its work runs from the living brain to the behaviour of large organisations, and what is common is that a claim must be put in terms measurement can bear on and evidence can count against."},{"q":"Why is the reach of many findings simply unknown?","a":"Because the literature remains overwhelmingly Western and its data largely American, so whether a result holds beyond the people it was found in has not been established."}]},
{"id":"ps-002","num":2,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology states its own scope as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, naming both what an organism does and the perceiving, remembering and deciding that can only be inferred from it.","questions":["Watson's manifesto of 1913 would have cut the phrase in half, and the cognitive revolution put the missing part back, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a settlement rather than an obvious pairing.","Only the first of the two things named by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be watched, so the evidence for the second is always somebody doing or saying something observable."],"answer":"behaviour and mental processes","answerText":"behaviour and mental processes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Behaviour alone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1913, on Watson's programme</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Both halves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">again from the 1950s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Behaviour and mental processes</b> is the standard statement of what psychology studies, and the working definitions the discipline gives of itself name both halves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Behaviour is what an organism does that somebody else could watch or record — a key pressed, a sentence spoken, a rat turning left, a pulse quickening — and it is observable in the same way by anyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mental processes are the perceiving, remembering, reasoning, deciding and feeling that behaviour is taken to express, and none of them can be watched at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are reached at one remove, through observable behaviour, biological indicators and what people report about themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That asymmetry is the discipline's standing practical problem: both halves are claimed, and the evidence for the second is always of the first kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The pairing is a settlement rather than an obvious truth, and psychology has redrawn it more than once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Watson's manifesto of 1913 made behaviour the whole of it, opening with the claim that psychology as the behaviourist views it is “a purely objective experimental branch of natural science” whose theoretical goal is the prediction and control of behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Introspection, he wrote, formed no essential part of its methods, and on the same view there was no dividing line between man and other animals, so a law of behaviour could be sought in whatever species showed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behaviourism then dominated experimental psychology for several decades, until by the 1950s work in linguistics, neuroscience and computer science had revived the mind as a subject of scientific inquiry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The break is drawn more sharply in textbooks than it was in the laboratory, since behaviourists had themselves worked on memory, perception, problem solving and learning, and what moved was the emphasis rather than the whole subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","behaviour"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","John B. Watson, “Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 20 (1913): 158–77, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Watson/views.htm. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.2 History of Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]","Charles I. Abramson, “Problems of Teaching the Behaviorist Perspective in the Cognitive Revolution,” <i>Behavioral Sciences</i> 3, no. 1 (2013): 55–71, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4217616/. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the two-part subject a standing practical problem?","a":"Because both halves are claimed but mental processes cannot be watched, so the evidence for them is always evidence of the first kind — behaviour, biological indicators and self-report."},{"q":"Why did behaviourism restrict psychology to behaviour alone?","a":"Because Watson’s 1913 manifesto made it a purely objective branch of natural science aiming at the prediction and control of behaviour, with introspection no essential part of its methods."},{"q":"Why is the cognitive break drawn too sharply in textbooks?","a":"Because behaviourists had themselves worked on memory, perception, problem solving and learning, so what moved was the emphasis rather than the whole subject."}]},
{"id":"ps-003","num":3,"category":"Psychology","question":"A survey of 174 behavioural biologists found them contradicting one another about what counts as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a plant bending its leaves towards the light among the cases that divided them.","questions":["Defined as the internally coordinated responses of a whole organism to stimuli, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> excludes what is better understood as growth, so a rabbit's thicker winter coat does not count.","Psychology treats a key pressed, a sentence spoken and a quickened pulse alike as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the observable side of its subject and the evidence its other claims finally rest on."],"answer":"behaviour","answerText":"behaviour","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Behaviour</b> is what a whole living organism does: its internally coordinated responses, actions and inactions alike, to stimuli inside or outside itself, excluding responses better understood as developmental change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That definition had to be proposed because none was agreed on, and the survey behind it found 52% of 174 members of three behaviour-focused societies contradicting themselves within one questionnaire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disagreements are about the edges: whether a plant bending its leaves towards the light behaves, whether unicellular algae swimming up a nutrient gradient do, whether sweating or a heart rate quickened by a nightmare counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Growth is generally excluded, a rabbit's thicker winter coat being development rather than conduct, while a person deciding to do nothing tomorrow if it rains divides opinion because nothing has yet been done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Definitions of this kind are not housekeeping: without one, teaching, research design and questions such as whether plants behave have nothing to settle them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Psychology uses the word for a narrower job, as the publicly observable side of what it studies and the evidence on which its claims about mental processes finally rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anything a person or an animal does that can be watched or recorded will serve, from a key pressed and a sentence spoken to a biological indicator such as a quickened heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before any of it can be counted it has to be pinned down, which is why researchers are told to fix clear criteria for what will be recorded and how it will be classified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Observers are then checked against one another, since anyone closely involved in a project may unconsciously skew what they see towards what they expect to find.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word is not confined to our own species either, and comparative psychology takes the same problems into others, each of which brings its own natural history and behavioural tendencies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","biology"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["Daniel A. Levitis, William Z. Lidicker Jr., and Glenn Freund, “Behavioural Biologists Don't Agree on What Constitutes Behaviour,” <i>Animal Behaviour</i> 78, no. 1 (2009): 103–10, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2760923/. [Open access]","John B. Watson, “Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 20 (1913): 158–77, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Watson/views.htm. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.2 Approaches to Research,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-2-approaches-to-research. [Open access]","Charles I. Abramson, “Why the Study of Comparative Psychology Is Important to Neuroscientists,” <i>Frontiers in Behavioral Neuroscience</i> 16 (2022): 1095033, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9872648/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Phototropism.jpg/1920px-Phototropism.jpg","title":"one of the cases that divides the definition","desc":"A potted orchid leaning towards a window: whether a plant turning to the light is behaving is one of the borderline cases on which behavioural biologists disagree. Tangopaso, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phototropism.jpg","alt":"A potted orchid on a windowsill, its leaves and flower spike leaning out towards the window while its pale aerial roots grow away from it into the room."},"why":[{"q":"Why did anyone need to propose a definition of behaviour?","a":"Because none was agreed on, and the survey behind the proposal found 52 per cent of 174 members of three behaviour-focused societies contradicting themselves within one questionnaire."},{"q":"Why is a definition of this kind not mere housekeeping?","a":"Because without one, teaching, research design and questions such as whether plants behave have nothing to settle them."},{"q":"Why are observers checked against one another?","a":"Because anyone closely involved in a project may unconsciously skew what they see towards what they expect to find, so criteria are fixed in advance and agreement measured."}]},
{"id":"ps-004","num":4,"category":"Psychology","question":"Perceiving, remembering and deciding are each a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and none of them can be watched, which is why psychology gets at them through behaviour, brain measures and what people say.","questions":["Donders subtracted one reaction time from another in 1868 to put a duration on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the oldest way of measuring something nobody can see.","Reading a fast answer as one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a slow one as another is a reverse inference, and controlling for strength of preference made the famous timing difference disappear."],"answer":"mental process","answerText":"mental process","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>mental process</b> is something a mind does — perceiving, attending, remembering, reasoning, judging, deciding, feeling — and it is the half of psychology's subject that nobody can watch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cognition is the usual umbrella for the thinking ones, taking in perception, knowledge, problem solving, judgment, language and memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because none of them is open to view, they are reached at one remove, through observable behaviour, biological indicators and what people report about themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Report does not reach all of them either, since not all aspects of cognition are consciously experienced and much of the organising work the brain does goes on without any awareness of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A mental process is therefore an inferred thing rather than an observed one, proposed to account for a pattern in what can actually be measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest instrument for the job is a clock: as far back as 1868 Franciscus Donders subtracted the time taken to respond to one unvarying stimulus from the time taken to respond to one of two events, and read the difference as the time needed to tell them apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mental chronometry has measured the time course of mental operations that way ever since, latterly alongside recordings of brain activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reasoning runs backwards, though, from a measurement to the process presumed to have produced it, and that is the step where it goes wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A much-cited line of work read fast choices as intuitive and slow ones as deliberative: controlling for how strongly people preferred one option removed the timing difference altogether, and altering a game's parameters could replicate, remove or reverse it at will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same caution applies to processes said to run unconsciously, where the absence of awareness is often assumed rather than tested, and where what counts as a proper test differs from one field to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","cognition"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “7.1 What Is Cognition?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/7-1-what-is-cognition. [Open access]","Michael I. Posner, “Timing the Brain: Mental Chronometry as a Tool in Neuroscience,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 3, no. 2 (2005): e51, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC548951/. [Open access]","Ian Krajbich et al., “Rethinking Fast and Slow Based on a Critique of Reaction-Time Reverse Inference,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 6 (2015): 7455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4500827/. [Open access]","Guido Hesselmann and Pieter Moors, “Definitely Maybe: Can Unconscious Processes Perform the Same Functions as Conscious Processes?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 6 (2015): 584, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4421938/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Hipp_Chronoscope%2C_c._1890%2C_view_1_-_Museum_of_Science_and_Industry_%28Chicago%29_-_DSC06506.JPG/1920px-Hipp_Chronoscope%2C_c._1890%2C_view_1_-_Museum_of_Science_and_Industry_%28Chicago%29_-_DSC06506.JPG","title":"a Hipp chronoscope, about 1890","desc":"A Hipp chronoscope of about 1890, the kind of clock with which 19th-century laboratories timed a response. Museum of Science and Industry, Chicago. Daderot, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hipp_Chronoscope,_c._1890,_view_1_-_Museum_of_Science_and_Industry_(Chicago)_-_DSC06506.JPG","alt":"A brass clock movement with two dials under a glass dome, standing on four black and gilt columns above a wooden base, with a driving weight hanging on a chain between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why is a mental process called inferred rather than observed?","a":"Because none of them is open to view, so each is proposed to account for a pattern in what can actually be measured — behaviour, biological indicators and report."},{"q":"Why does asking people not reach all of them?","a":"Because not all aspects of cognition are consciously experienced, and much of the organising work the brain does goes on without any awareness of it."},{"q":"Why is reading a process off a timing a fragile step?","a":"Because controlling for how strongly people preferred one option removed the fast-intuitive slow-deliberative difference altogether, and changing a game’s parameters could replicate, remove or reverse it at will."}]},
{"id":"ps-005","num":5,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology's claim to be a science rests on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which turns an idea into a falsifiable prediction and then lets evidence rather than authority settle it.","questions":["Studies of statistical power keep finding it below 50%, which is one reason a single result produced by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is weaker evidence than a headline suggests.","Preregistration, registered reports and open data were added to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that a prediction can be told apart from an explanation invented once the results were in."],"answer":"scientific method","answerText":"scientific method","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>scientific method</b> is the procedure by which a claim about the world is made answerable to evidence, and it is what psychology applies to behaviour and mental processes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Knowledge reached that way is empirical, grounded in objective and tangible evidence that can be observed time and again regardless of who is doing the observing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A theory is a well-developed set of ideas proposing an explanation for observed phenomena, and a hypothesis is a testable prediction about how the world will behave if that idea is right, usually put as an if-then statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has to be falsifiable, which means capable of being shown to be incorrect: a claim that no possible observation could count against is not being tested by any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ideas are then carried into the world and tested, and observations in the world suggest new ideas, the two directions running as deduction and induction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Applied to people the procedure is harder than the diagram suggests, and one study is weaker evidence than it looks: surveys of statistical power persistently find it below, and sometimes well below, 50%.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Low power raises the chance of a false positive as well as a false negative, and it does so most where the effect looked for is small and the field is crowded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sharpest form of that argument holds that most published research findings are false, and it turns on how likely a hypothesis was to be true before the study rather than on the figure reported after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The response has been to make transparency part of the method rather than an optional extra, through preregistered protocols, reports reviewed before the results are known, open data and materials, and funded replication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that buys is being measured rather than assumed: preregistered psychology studies have larger samples and far more often justify them by a power analysis, but no robust evidence has yet been found that preregistering prevents selective analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","John P. A. Ioannidis, “Why Most Published Research Findings Are False,” <i>PLoS Medicine</i> 2, no. 8 (2005): e124, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1182327/. [Open access]","Marcus R. Munafò et al., “A Manifesto for Reproducible Science,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 1 (2017): 0021, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7610724/. [Open access]","Olmo R. van den Akker et al., “Preregistration in Practice: A Comparison of Preregistered and Non-preregistered Studies in Psychology,” <i>Behavior Research Methods</i> 56, no. 6 (2024): 5424–33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11335781/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must a hypothesis be capable of being shown wrong?","a":"Because a claim that no possible observation could count against is not being tested by any of them, so falsifiability is what makes a prediction answerable to evidence at all."},{"q":"Why does low statistical power raise the chance of a false positive?","a":"Because it does so most where the effect looked for is small and the field is crowded, which is the ground of the argument that most published research findings are false."},{"q":"Why is preregistration’s benefit stated cautiously?","a":"Because preregistered psychology studies do have larger samples and far more often justify them by a power analysis, but no robust evidence has yet been found that preregistering prevents selective analysis."}]},
{"id":"ps-006","num":6,"category":"Psychology","question":"The computational, algorithmic and implementational tiers, and the four questions about mechanism, development, history and fitness, are both schemes of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and neither ranks one answer above another.","questions":["Because the same function can be carried by more than one neural substrate, a description at one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not a placeholder for one further down.","Asking what a trait is for and asking how its machinery works are questions at different <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so an answer to one leaves the other untouched."],"answer":"levels of analysis","answerText":"levels of analysis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Levels of analysis</b> are the different grains at which one and the same behaviour can be described and explained, and psychology works at several of them at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That span is why the discipline is counted a natural science and a social one together, its subject being biological at root and shaped throughout by dealings with other people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One much-used scheme divides a single explanation into three: a computational level asking what problem is being solved, an algorithmic level asking how it is solved, and an implementational level asking how that is realised in neural hardware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second scheme cuts differently, putting four questions to any trait — its mechanism, its development in the individual, its evolutionary history and its effect on fitness — of which two are proximate and two evolutionary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither scheme ranks its levels, and seeking answers to all four questions expands an explanation rather than replacing any part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Confusing one level with another is a standing source of bad argument, because a question about what a trait is for is not answered by describing the machinery that carries it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pull is usually downwards, towards the smallest parts available, and the objection to that is practical rather than sentimental: describing a system's parts in isolation is not enough to understand behaviour in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Psychology's own span runs from genetic interaction networks through neural networks to socio-cultural ones, and a mechanism has to be found at each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The levels divide within psychology too, separating broad cognitive domains such as language from task-specific functions such as naming, and both from the elementary processing units beneath them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is a technical reason they will not collapse together: the same function can be mediated by more than one neural substrate, so surface similarity in what people do may hide different mechanisms underneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Randolph M. Nesse, “Tinbergen's Four Questions: Two Proximate, Two Evolutionary,” <i>Evolution, Medicine, and Public Health</i> 2019, no. 1 (2019): 2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6343817/. [Open access]","Tobias U. Hauser et al., “Computational Psychiatry of ADHD: Neural Gain Impairments across Marrian Levels of Analysis,” <i>Trends in Neurosciences</i> 39, no. 2 (2016): 63–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4746317/. [Open access]","Francisco J. Parada and Alejandra Rossi, “If Neuroscience Needs Behavior, What Does Psychology Need?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 433, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5883085/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Morten Overgaard and Jesper Mogensen, “A Framework for the Study of Multiple Realizations: The Importance of Levels of Analysis,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 2 (2011): 79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3131792/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does confusing two levels produce bad argument?","a":"Because a question about what a trait is for is not answered by describing the machinery that carries it, and the two questions belong to different levels of one explanation."},{"q":"Why does answering all four questions not replace any of them?","a":"Because neither scheme ranks its levels, so seeking mechanism, development, evolutionary history and effect on fitness expands an explanation rather than reducing it to one part."},{"q":"Why will the levels not collapse into the smallest one?","a":"Because the same function can be mediated by more than one neural substrate, so surface similarity in what people do may hide different mechanisms underneath."}]},
{"id":"ps-007","num":7,"category":"Psychology","question":"Clinical, forensic, educational, health and occupational work are all <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is defined by aiming at a practical problem rather than at knowledge for its own sake.","questions":["A standard set for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asks that the best available research be integrated with clinical expertise, in the context of a patient's characteristics, culture and preferences.","Passport control and criminal investigation pushed face identification into the laboratory, which shows how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and basic work feed each other rather than dividing the discipline in two."],"answer":"applied psychology","answerText":"applied psychology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Applied psychology</b> is the part of the discipline aimed at a practical problem rather than at knowledge for its own sake, and the distinction it draws is about the purpose of a piece of work rather than its subject or its methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard definitions put it plainly: basic research aims at the advancement of knowledge regardless of any particular use, applied research has a problem-solving rationale oriented towards practical objectives, and experimental development turns research knowledge into new or improved products and services.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line between them is not a wall, since many academics pursue both at once and more of them say they prefer applied work than basic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the two sits use-inspired basic research, where a problem in the world shapes the hypotheses taken into the laboratory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Britain the applied side is organised into nine recognised specialisms, among them clinical, forensic, educational, health, occupational, and sport and exercise psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where psychologists actually work reflects that balance: about a quarter in universities and a quarter in hospitals and health services, the rest spread across government, business, schools, medical schools and independent practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The traffic runs in both directions, and work on individual differences in face identification was pushed into the laboratory by the demands of passport control and criminal investigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where research meets practice there is a formal standard: evidence-based practice in psychology is defined as the integration of the best available research with clinical expertise, in the context of the patient's characteristics, culture and preferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether it works that way is argued over, one critique holding the model to be scientocentric rather than genuinely tripartite, since the other two parts are legitimated by appeal to research evidence rather than on ground of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The categories mark what a piece of work is for rather than how good it is, which is why so many researchers sit across them rather than at one end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["J. M. Santos, H. Horta, and H. Luna, “The Relationship between Academics' Strategic Research Agendas and Their Preferences for Basic Research, Applied Research, or Experimental Development,” <i>Scientometrics</i> 127, no. 7 (2022): 4191–4225, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9285191/. [Open access]","Karen Lander, Vicki Bruce, and Markus Bindemann, “Use-Inspired Basic Research on Individual Differences in Face Identification: Implications for Criminal Investigation and Security,” <i>Cognitive Research: Principles and Implications</i> 3 (2018): 26, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6021459/. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.4 Careers in Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-4-careers-in-psychology. [Open access]","Henrik Berg, “Evidence-Based Practice in Psychology Fails to Be Tripartite: A Conceptual Critique of the Scientocentrism in Evidence-Based Practice in Psychology,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2253, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6794362/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the distinction about purpose rather than method?","a":"Because basic research aims at knowledge regardless of any use and applied research has a problem-solving rationale, while the subject and the methods may be identical."},{"q":"Why is the line between them not a wall?","a":"Because many academics pursue both at once, more say they prefer applied work than basic, and use-inspired basic research lets a problem in the world shape laboratory hypotheses."},{"q":"Why is the evidence-based practice model contested?","a":"Because one critique holds it scientocentric rather than genuinely tripartite, since clinical expertise and patient characteristics are legitimated by appeal to research evidence rather than on ground of their own."}]},
{"id":"ps-008","num":8,"category":"Psychology","question":"With 54 divisions in the American Psychological Association alone, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are named partly for what they study and partly for the settings where their problems arise.","questions":["Cognitive, developmental, social and biological work sit beside clinical, forensic and occupational practice among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which share a method more than they share a subject.","Critics describe the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a colourful picture of different subject matters whose interconnection is tenuous, with no agreement on what the core concepts even mean."],"answer":"subfields of psychology","answerText":"subfields of psychology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>subfields of psychology</b> are the areas the discipline divides into, some named for what they study and some for the settings their problems come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the study side sit biopsychology and evolutionary psychology, sensation and perception, cognitive psychology, developmental psychology, personality psychology and social psychology, each marked out by the question it asks rather than by any method of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the applied side sit clinical and counselling psychology, forensic psychology, health psychology, industrial-organisational psychology, and sport and exercise psychology, each marked out by where its problems arise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lists are national as well as intellectual, and the specialisms recognised by the British professional body number nine, a shorter and more institutional set than the American one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For scale, the American Psychological Association has 54 divisions representing a wide variety of specialties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where psychologists actually work follows the applied side more than the academic one, with about a quarter in universities, a quarter in hospitals and health services, and the rest spread across government, business, schools, medical schools and independent practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the divisions do not mark is a set of separate methods, since the common factor across the branches is a shared method and methodology rather than a shared subject matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One account puts that sharply, calling psychology a very colourful picture of different subject matters whose interconnectedness is often rather tenuous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The complaint is an old one and has not gone away: fragmentation and isolation within the discipline persist, and there is no consensus about the content and definition of its core concepts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not everyone holds that a single foundation is the remedy, and one argument is that psychology needs foundations held reflectively and continually renewed rather than fixed ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.3 Contemporary Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-3-contemporary-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.4 Careers in Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-4-careers-in-psychology. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","Mariann Märtsin, “Psychology: A Discipline in Need of Reflective Foundations,” <i>Integrative Psychological and Behavioral Science</i> 54, no. 3 (2020): 694–700, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7263803/. [Open access]","Roland Mayrhofer and Fabian Hutmacher, “The Principle of Inversion: Why the Quantitative-Empirical Paradigm Cannot Serve as a Unifying Basis for Psychology as an Academic Discipline,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 596425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7750523/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the subfields named on two different principles?","a":"Because some are marked out by the question they ask, like perception or development, and others by where their problems arise, like forensic or occupational psychology."},{"q":"Why do the divisions not mark separate methods?","a":"Because the common factor across the branches is a shared method and methodology rather than a shared subject matter."},{"q":"Why is fragmentation a standing complaint rather than an old one settled?","a":"Because isolation within the discipline persists and there is no consensus about the content and definition of its core concepts, though not everyone holds fixed foundations to be the remedy."}]},
{"id":"ps-009","num":9,"category":"Psychology","question":"Comte ranked disciplines by the complexity of their subject in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with physics at the top, the social sciences at the bottom and biology in between.","questions":["A test of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted positive results in 2,434 papers and found the odds about 2.3 times higher in the social sciences than in the physical.","Bibliometric work puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> down to how far a field can reach consensus as its subject grows complex, rather than to how carefully its people work."],"answer":"hierarchy of the sciences","answerText":"hierarchy of the sciences","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>hierarchy of the sciences</b> is an old proposal, due to Auguste Comte, that disciplines can be ordered by the complexity of their subject and the precision of the knowledge they reach, with the physical sciences at the top, the social sciences at the bottom and the biological sciences in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology is awkward to place on it, being counted a natural science and a social science at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ordering has been tested rather than merely asserted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One study read 2,434 papers that explicitly tested a hypothesis and counted how often the result came out supporting it, finding the odds of a positive result about 2.3 times higher in the social sciences than in the physical ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology and psychiatry did not sit where the ranking predicts but at its extreme, with roughly five times the odds of a positive result of space science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second study measured nearly 29,000 papers on properties such as the number of authors, the age of the references, the proportion of monographs cited and the use of first-person pronouns, and found the biological sciences taking intermediate values between the physical and the social.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the pattern reflects, on that account, is how far a field can reach consensus and accumulate knowledge, which falls as its subject grows more complex and less controllable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is worth holding on to, because the popular form of the ranking sorts fields into hard and soft ones and is usually meant as a verdict on how carefully their people work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Psychology's best-known trouble is not its own either, since studies of statistical power persistently find it below, and sometimes well below, 50%, across time and across the different disciplines examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its neighbours pull it both ways at once, its span running from genetic interaction networks through neural networks to socio-cultural ones, with the parts described in isolation never enough to account for behaviour in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"sources":["Daniele Fanelli, “‘Positive' Results Increase Down the Hierarchy of the Sciences,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 5, no. 4 (2010): e10068, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2850928/. [Open access]","Daniele Fanelli and Wolfgang Glänzel, “Bibliometric Evidence for a Hierarchy of the Sciences,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 6 (2013): e66938, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3694152/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Francisco J. Parada and Alejandra Rossi, “If Neuroscience Needs Behavior, What Does Psychology Need?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 433, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5883085/. [Open access]","Marcus R. Munafò et al., “A Manifesto for Reproducible Science,” <i>Nature Human Behaviour</i> 1 (2017): 0021, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7610724/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the ordering read as a fact about consensus rather than about care?","a":"Because what the pattern reflects is how far a field can reach consensus and accumulate knowledge, which falls as its subject grows more complex and less controllable."},{"q":"Why did psychology not sit where the ranking predicted?","a":"Because it came out at the extreme rather than in place: psychology and psychiatry showed roughly five times the odds of a positive result of space science across 2,434 hypothesis-testing papers."},{"q":"Why is low statistical power not psychology’s own failing?","a":"Because surveys find it below, and sometimes well below, 50 per cent across time and across the different disciplines examined, not in psychology alone."}]},
{"id":"ps-010","num":10,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology's results look obvious to a reader who has just met them, an effect of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lifts the judged likelihood of an outcome as soon as it is known.","questions":["After the 2014 Taiwan mayoral elections, 88% of participants recalled forecasts closer to the result than the ones they had actually made, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measured in the field.","Radiologists who had seen a clear mammogram spotted the same abnormality in a degraded copy better than colleagues who had not, showing that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches perception too."],"answer":"hindsight bias","answerText":"hindsight bias","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hindsight bias</b> is the tendency to treat an outcome as having been likely once it is known, so that judgements of probability made in hindsight exceed the ones made in foresight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is measured in two ways: in a hypothetical design one group answers in ignorance while another answers knowing the outcome and instructed to set that knowledge aside, and in a memory design people give a judgement, learn what happened, and then try to recall what they first said.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recalled judgement moves towards the outcome, and the standard models agree that knowing it alters the memory itself rather than merely the report of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The size of the shift is not fixed, and one experiment found it larger the more information had been encoded beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The alternative to testing such judgements is to trust them, which leaves intuition, other people's authority and blind luck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Outside the laboratory the effect is easy to find: asked after the 2014 mayoral elections in Taiwan to recall their own forecasts, 88% of participants remembered predicting something closer to the result than they actually had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reaches seeing as well as remembering, since radiologists shown a clear mammogram first were afterwards more accurate at finding the same abnormality in a heavily degraded copy than colleagues who had not seen it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That bears directly on any judgement made after the event, because something hard to make out at the time looks plain to anyone who already knows it is there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For a discipline whose findings are about people the consequence is sharp, since the same reader who could not have predicted a result will judge it obvious once told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This is an argument for the method rather than against common sense: a claim earns its place by surviving a prediction made in advance, not by sounding right afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","cognition"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Karolin Salmen, Florian K. G. Ermark, and Klaus Fiedler, “Pragmatic, Constructive, and Reconstructive Memory Influences on the Hindsight Bias,” <i>Psychonomic Bulletin and Review</i> 30, no. 1 (2023): 331–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9971109/. [Open access]","K. Kaida and N. Kaida, “Memory Load of Information Encoded Amplifies the Magnitude of Hindsight Bias,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 18, no. 4 (2023): e0283969, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10085031/. [Open access]","Hayden M. Schill, Samantha M. Gray, and Timothy F. Brady, “Visual Hindsight Bias for Abnormal Mammograms in Radiologists,” <i>Journal of Medical Imaging</i> 10, suppl. 1 (2023): S11910, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10190961/. [Open access]","Y. H. Chen et al., “Can Knowledge of Election Results Change Recall of Our Predictions? Neural Correlates of Political Hindsight Bias,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 14, no. 10 (2019): e0220690, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6786518/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is hindsight bias a change in memory rather than in reporting?","a":"Because in the memory design the recalled judgement itself moves towards the outcome, and the standard models agree that knowing the outcome alters the memory rather than merely the report."},{"q":"Why does the radiology result reach beyond remembering?","a":"Because radiologists shown a clear mammogram first were afterwards more accurate at finding the same abnormality in a heavily degraded copy, so something hard to make out looks plain to anyone who knows it is there."},{"q":"Why is this an argument for method rather than against common sense?","a":"Because the same reader who could not have predicted a result will judge it obvious once told, so a claim earns its place by surviving a prediction made in advance."}]},
{"id":"ps-011","num":11,"category":"Psychology","question":"The question of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now treated as badly put, since inheritance sets boundaries and experience decides where inside them a person falls.","questions":["Parents supply both the DNA and the rearing home, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are so hard to tell apart, and why 22% of the variance in household chaos proved heritable.","Body mass is strongly heritable within a population while the rise in obesity is put down to a changed environment, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refusing to separate."],"answer":"nature and nurture","answerText":"nature and nurture","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nature and nurture</b> names the question of how far differences between people come from what they inherit and how far from what happens to them, and the modern answer is that the question was badly put.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genes do not exist in a vacuum, and each person is a unique interaction between a genetic makeup and an environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two ideas do the work the dichotomy could not: a range of reaction, in which inheritance sets boundaries and experience decides where inside them somebody falls, and genetic-environmental correlation, in which genes influence the environment a person meets while that environment influences which genes are expressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Epigenetics adds that one genotype can be expressed in different ways and lead to very different outcomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Separating the two is hard for a plain reason: parents are the source of both the DNA and the rearing environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That makes an apparently environmental finding hard to trust, since the influence of a parent's behaviour on a child cannot be known without controlling for the DNA the two share.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measures of the environment turn out to be partly heritable themselves, and in one study 22% of the variance in household chaos was attributable to genetic factors, with 37% of its association with school achievement down to shared genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The traffic does not all run that way, and an adoption design that removes the shared-genes explanation still found hostile parenting carrying the effect of conflict between parents onto children's behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Family-based quasi-experimental methods combined with genomic data now allow genetic and environmental confounds to be controlled without being measured directly, though no one design removes them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Obesity shows why the old question misleads, body mass being strongly heritable within a population while the rise in obesity over recent decades is put down to a changed environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","genetics"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “3.1 Human Genetics,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/3-1-human-genetics. [Open access]","Sara A. Hart, Callie Little, and Elsje van Bergen, “Nurture Might Be Nature: Cautionary Tales and Proposed Solutions,” <i>npj Science of Learning</i> 6 (2021): 2, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7794571/. [Open access]","Gordon T. Harold et al., “The Nature of Nurture: Disentangling Passive Genotype-Environment Correlation from Family Relationship Influences on Children's Externalizing Problems,” <i>Journal of Family Psychology</i> 27, no. 1 (2013): 12–21, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3576129/. [Open access]","Tom A. McAdams, Rosa Cheesman, and Yasmin I. Ahmadzadeh, “Annual Research Review: Towards a Deeper Understanding of Nature and Nurture: Combining Family-Based Quasi-experimental Methods with Genomic Data,” <i>Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry</i> 64, no. 4 (2023): 693–707, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10952916/. [Open access]","Sarah E. Jackson, Clare H. Llewellyn, and Lee Smith, “The Obesity Epidemic — Nature via Nurture: A Narrative Review of High-Income Countries,” <i>SAGE Open Medicine</i> 8 (2020): 2050312120918265, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7222649/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the original question badly put?","a":"Because genes do not exist in a vacuum and each person is a unique interaction between a genetic makeup and an environment, so the two cannot be apportioned as separate shares."},{"q":"Why is an apparently environmental finding hard to trust?","a":"Because parents are the source of both the DNA and the rearing environment, so the influence of a parent’s behaviour cannot be known without controlling for the DNA the two share."},{"q":"Why does obesity show the old question misleading?","a":"Because body mass is strongly heritable within a population while the rise in obesity over recent decades is put down to a changed environment, and both statements are true at once."}]},
{"id":"ps-012","num":12,"category":"Psychology","question":"Psychology looks for the causes of behaviour, which commits it to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the claim that a decision is produced by what went before it.","questions":["Reading the readiness potential as the brain deciding before its owner knows made it the standard laboratory evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until the potential was reinterpreted.","Across 5,268 people in 20 countries, blame was assigned whatever the scenario said about causation, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not by itself remove responsibility in most participants' judgement."],"answer":"determinism","answerText":"determinism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Determinism</b> is the claim that decisions are causally determined by what went before them, or determined for all practical purposes, and it is the position a science that looks for the causes of behaviour starts from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The free will set against it is not the feeling of choosing but a stronger claim: that some decisions are both undetermined and genuinely the causes of what follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sharper worry is not determinism at all but epiphenomenalism, the possibility that decisions are not the causes of actions in the first place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Psychology's contribution has been a measurement rather than an argument, and it is the readiness potential, a slow negative electrical potential recorded from the scalp that builds in the run-up to a voluntary movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Timed against the moment people report having decided, that report falls only about 200 milliseconds before the movement and hundreds of milliseconds after the potential has begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The result was read as showing the brain committing to a movement before its owner knows of it, which is how a laboratory measurement entered a philosophical argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reinterpretation is that the potential reflects ongoing stochastic fluctuations in neural activity rather than a decision being taken, and that its apparent onset does not correspond to a physiologically real event at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What people themselves believe has also been measured, and belief in free will turns out to be predicted better by belief in a non-physical mind than by any view about causation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether determinism is felt to excuse anything varies by place: across 5,268 people in 20 countries responsibility was ascribed regardless, though Western participants separated the deterministic case from a comparison one where most Asian participants did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The practical fear that telling people their choices are caused will make them behave worse has not held up well either, one study finding fewer shocks administered after such a message rather than more, on a sample of only 40.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","philosophy"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Aaron Schurger et al., “What Is the Readiness Potential?,” <i>Trends in Cognitive Sciences</i> 25, no. 7 (2021): 558–70, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8192467/. [Open access]","Mark Balaguer, “Free Will, Determinism, and Epiphenomenalism,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 2623, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6333639/. [Open access]","Emilie A. Caspar et al., “The Influence of (Dis)belief in Free Will on Immoral Behavior,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5239816/. [Open access]","David Wisniewski, Robert Deutschländer, and John-Dylan Haynes, “Free Will Beliefs Are Better Predicted by Dualism than Determinism Beliefs across Different Cultures,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 14, no. 9 (2019): e0221617, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6738589/. [Open access]","Ivar R. Hannikainen et al., “For Whom Does Determinism Undermine Moral Responsibility? Surveying the Conditions for Free Will across Cultures,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2428, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6848273/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is epiphenomenalism the sharper worry?","a":"Because it is the possibility that decisions are not the causes of actions in the first place, which threatens more than the claim that they are themselves caused."},{"q":"Why has the readiness potential result been reinterpreted?","a":"Because the potential is now read as reflecting ongoing stochastic fluctuations in neural activity, its apparent onset not corresponding to a physiologically real event at all."},{"q":"Why has the fear about telling people their choices are caused not held up?","a":"Because one study found fewer shocks administered after such a message rather than more, though on a sample of only 40."}]},
{"id":"ps-013","num":13,"category":"Psychology","question":"Explaining a whole by taking it apart and accounting for it through its components is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the strategy behind much of modern medicine.","questions":["A simulated 6502 chip running Donkey Kong was analysed by the standard methods of neuroscience, and the failure to recover how it works is the sharpest case against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yet made.","Emergence is the objection to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since novel properties arise in self-organising systems and the whole becomes not merely more but very different from its parts."],"answer":"reductionism","answerText":"reductionism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Reductionism</b> is the strategy of explaining something by taking it apart and accounting for it through its components, and that divide-and-conquer approach has been responsible for tremendous successes in modern medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it promises is that the properties of a whole follow from the properties of its parts, so that knowing the parts amounts to knowing the thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Emergence is the standing objection: novel and coherent structures, patterns and properties arise during self-organisation in complex systems, and the whole becomes not merely more but very different from the sum of its parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second obstacle is formal rather than philosophical, since the same function can be realised by more than one underlying substrate, so an account at one level does not translate cleanly into one below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of this cancels the record: reduction identified specific disease agents and produced treatments aimed at them, so the argument is about limits rather than about worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its sharpest demonstration came from treating a microprocessor as though it were a brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard methods of neuroscience were applied to a fully simulated MOS 6502 running Donkey Kong, Space Invaders and Pitfall, and the structure they recovered looked impressive while getting nowhere near an understanding of how the processor really works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lesioning single transistors picked out game-specific effects, although transistors implement simple functions such as adders rather than anything game-specific, and some transistors showed strong tuning to screen brightness despite having no direct relation to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The general moral is that where parts interact in a nonlinear way, information about each of them separately is insufficient to explain the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The response has not been to abandon the parts but to study how they combine, since which social behaviour an animal produces depends jointly on the identity of the other animal and on states such as hunger, which no circuit examined alone would reveal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","philosophy"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Eric Jonas and Konrad Paul Kording, “Could a Neuroscientist Understand a Microprocessor?,” <i>PLoS Computational Biology</i> 13, no. 1 (2017): e1005268, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5230747/. [Open access]","Michael P. W. Grocott, “Integrative Physiology and Systems Biology: Reductionism, Emergence and Causality,” <i>Extreme Physiology and Medicine</i> 2, no. 1 (2013): 9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3710203/. [Open access]","Andrew C. Ahn et al., “The Limits of Reductionism in Medicine: Could Systems Biology Offer an Alternative?,” <i>PLoS Medicine</i> 3, no. 6 (2006): e208, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1459480/. [Open access]","Morten Overgaard and Jesper Mogensen, “A Framework for the Study of Multiple Realizations: The Importance of Levels of Analysis,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 2 (2011): 79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3131792/. [Open access]","Sarah Y. Dickinson et al., “From Reductionism toward Integration: Understanding How Social Behavior Emerges from Integrated Circuits,” <i>Frontiers in Integrative Neuroscience</i> 16 (2022): 862437, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9010670/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is taking a system apart not always enough to explain it?","a":"Because where parts interact in a nonlinear way, information about each of them separately is insufficient, and novel structures and properties arise in the whole that the parts do not carry."},{"q":"Why is the microprocessor study so damaging a demonstration?","a":"Because applying neuroscience’s standard methods to a simulated MOS 6502 recovered impressive-looking structure while getting nowhere near how the processor works — lesioned transistors showing game-specific effects they cannot have."},{"q":"Why is the argument about limits rather than about worth?","a":"Because reduction identified specific disease agents and produced treatments aimed at them, a record the objection does not cancel."}]},
{"id":"ps-014","num":14,"category":"Psychology","question":"How mental states relate to physical ones is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and psychology meets it as a working difficulty rather than as an abstraction.","questions":["Dualism, monism and emergentism are the three answers usually given to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and surveys find most people taking the middle one.","Psychiatry meets the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a dilemma, since claiming an independent mental sphere leads to substance dualism while reducing it to the physical loses the word mental."],"answer":"mind-body problem","answerText":"mind-body problem","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>mind-body problem</b> is the question of how mental states stand to physical ones, and psychology meets it as a working difficulty rather than as an abstraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three answers are usually distinguished: dualism, on which minds are in principle independent of the bodies they are only temporarily attached to; monism, on which the mind is the same thing as the brain; and emergentism, on which the mind is based on the brain and is more than just its activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked which they hold, people mostly take the middle position, and in a survey of 850 volunteers emergentism scored highest and dualism lowest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A smaller study using a different instrument found the same shape, with 53 of 94 participants classed as non-reductive materialists against 32 reductive materialists and 9 dualists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both samples were European, so that distribution is a fact about those participants rather than about people at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The scientific form of the problem is the explanatory gap, and explaining how neuronal activity gives rise to cognition is arguably the outstanding challenge of cognitive neuroscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One recent proposal holds a physicalist line by treating cognitive states as causally consistent abstractions of neuronal states rather than as a second set of things acting on them, on the model of temperature as an abstraction of molecular motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The question bites hardest in psychiatry, which faces a dilemma: save the notion of mental disorder by claiming an independent sphere of the mental and end in substance dualism, or reduce it to the physical and lose the word mental altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One way out is to treat the mental and the somatic as phenomenologically different but complementary aspects of one substance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stakes are practical rather than academic, since dualistic thinking can limit the therapeutic interventions offered to patients with major psychiatric disorders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","philosophy"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Tapani Riekki, Marjaana Lindeman, and Jari Lipsanen, “Conceptions about the Mind-Body Problem and Their Relations to Afterlife Beliefs, Paranormal Beliefs, Religiosity, and Ontological Confusions,” <i>Advances in Cognitive Psychology</i> 9, no. 3 (2013): 112–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4158462/. [Open access]","Marek Havlík et al., “Do Personality Features Influence Our Intuitions of the Mind-Body Problem? A Pilot Study,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 1219, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6062648/. [Open access]","Moritz Grosse-Wentrup et al., “Neuro-cognitive Multilevel Causal Modeling: A Framework That Bridges the Explanatory Gap between Neuronal Activity and Cognition,” <i>PLoS Computational Biology</i> 20, no. 12 (2024): e1012674, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11717354/. [Open access]","Michael Jungert, “Mental Realities: The Concept of Mental Disorder and the Mind-Body Problem,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 4 (2013): 809, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3817358/. [Open access]","Walter Glannon, “Mind-Brain Dualism in Psychiatry: Ethical Implications,” <i>Frontiers in Psychiatry</i> 11 (2020): 85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7063027/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the problem bite hardest in psychiatry?","a":"Because it faces a dilemma: claim an independent sphere of the mental and end in substance dualism, or reduce disorder to the physical and lose the word mental altogether."},{"q":"Why are the survey findings a fact about those participants?","a":"Because both samples were European, so the distribution across emergentism, materialism and dualism describes them rather than people at large."},{"q":"Why are the stakes practical rather than academic?","a":"Because dualistic thinking can limit the therapeutic interventions offered to patients with major psychiatric disorders."}]},
{"id":"ps-015","num":15,"category":"Psychology","question":"Examining one's own conscious experience as objectively as possible is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the founding method of experimental psychology and the first one it discarded.","questions":["Training did not save <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which stayed highly subjective with very little agreement between individuals, and psychology turned to what could be watched instead.","A beeper worn through an ordinary day, cueing a report of inner experience at that exact moment, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rebuilt to avoid the errors of remembering."],"answer":"introspection","answerText":"introspection","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Introspection</b> is the examination of one's own conscious experience as objectively as possible, and it was the founding method of experimental psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wundt used it experimentally, with instruments timing responses to stimuli, and Titchener built structuralism on the contents it reported, attending to what mental processes contain rather than what they are for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It failed on a practical point rather than a philosophical one: despite efforts to train people in the process, it remained highly subjective and there was very little agreement between individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It never left entirely, since what people report about themselves is still one of the routes by which psychology reaches a mental process, beside observable behaviour and biological indicators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The case for taking it seriously again is that first-person and third-person enquiry are complementary rather than competing, and that phenomena of a primarily first-person nature cannot be explored while bypassing what first-person enquiry uncovers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Descriptive experience sampling is the most developed of the disciplined versions: a randomised beeper worn through an ordinary day cues the wearer to attend to their inner experience at the precise moment it sounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About six samples are collected over some three hours and gone through in an interview within a day, and the cycle is repeated several times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What that design is trying to avoid is retrospection, which mixes experience together with beliefs about the self, beliefs about what experience ought to be like, and plain misremembering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Accuracy can also be measured directly, and participants asked to estimate their own reaction times reproduced both a task-switching cost and a memory-search cost in their estimates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those estimates were compressed rather than mistaken, running at 45 milliseconds against 76 measured for the switch and 40 against 81 for the larger memory set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Ulrich W. Weger, Johannes Wagemann, and Christian Tewes, “Editorial: The Challenges and Opportunities of Introspection in Psychology: Theory and Method,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2196, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6794359/. [Open access]","Leiszle R. Lapping-Carr and Christopher L. Heavey, “Pristine Inner Experience and Descriptive Experience Sampling: Implications for Psychology,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 2170, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5733077/. [Open access]","Daniel Bratzke and Donna Bryce, “Timing of Internal Processes: Investigating Introspection about the Costs of Task Switching and Memory Search,” <i>Attention, Perception, and Psychophysics</i> 84, no. 5 (2022): 1501–8, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9232406/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.2 History of Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did introspection fail as the founding method?","a":"Because despite efforts to train people in it, it remained highly subjective and there was very little agreement between individuals reporting on the same thing."},{"q":"Why does experience sampling use a beeper rather than an interview alone?","a":"Because it is trying to avoid retrospection, which mixes experience with beliefs about the self, beliefs about what experience ought to be like, and plain misremembering."},{"q":"Why are the reaction-time estimates called compressed rather than wrong?","a":"Because participants reproduced both the task-switching and the memory-search costs, at 45 milliseconds against 76 measured and 40 against 81, so the pattern was right and the scale short."}]},
{"id":"ps-016","num":16,"category":"Psychology","question":"The demand that a finding not depend on who produced it is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and in psychology it is enforced by procedures rather than by good intentions.","questions":["One brain-imaging dataset run through 6,912 pipelines gave maps in which no voxel was significant throughout, which is a hard limit on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in analysis.","Fixing criteria before recording and comparing what different observers saw are the everyday guards on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the second of them measured as inter-rater reliability."],"answer":"objectivity","answerText":"objectivity","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Objectivity</b> in science is the demand that a finding should not depend on who produced it, and scientific knowledge is called empirical because it is grounded in evidence that can be observed time and again regardless of who is observing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology's difficulty is that most of its evidence passes through a person before it becomes data, whether a participant reporting or a researcher deciding what to write down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The named risk is observer bias, since people closely involved in a research project may unconsciously skew their observations to fit their goals or expectations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The everyday guards against it are procedural: fix clear criteria in advance for the behaviours to be recorded and how they will be classified, then compare observations of the same event made by different people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That comparison has a name and a number, inter-rater reliability being the measure of consistency between observers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those guards discipline the person watching, and a larger discretion sits further downstream, in the analysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One brain-imaging dataset was put through 6,912 analysis pipelines and five thresholding methods, giving 34,560 significance maps, and no voxel came out significant across all of them while 90.3% were significant in at least some.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The conclusion drawn was that with enough flexibility a researcher determined to find activation in practically any brain region will very likely succeed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blinding is the obvious remedy, though its value is harder to demonstrate than expected, since across 142 meta-analyses and 1,153 trials no evidence was found of an average difference in estimated treatment effect between blinded and unblinded ones, on intervals wide enough to admit a considerable difference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Objectivity is better understood as a set of procedures than as a state of mind, and it does not require excluding first-person evidence, since first-person and third-person enquiry are complementary rather than competing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.2 Approaches to Research,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-2-approaches-to-research. [Open access]","Joshua Carp, “On the Plurality of (Methodological) Worlds: Estimating the Analytic Flexibility of fMRI Experiments,” <i>Frontiers in Neuroscience</i> 6 (2012): 149, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3468892/. [Open access]","Helene Moustgaard et al., “Impact of Blinding on Estimated Treatment Effects in Randomised Clinical Trials: Meta-epidemiological Study,” <i>BMJ</i> 368 (2020): l6802, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7190062/. [Open access]","Ulrich W. Weger, Johannes Wagemann, and Christian Tewes, “Editorial: The Challenges and Opportunities of Introspection in Psychology: Theory and Method,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 10 (2019): 2196, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6794359/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is objectivity harder in psychology than the definition suggests?","a":"Because most of its evidence passes through a person before it becomes data, whether a participant reporting or a researcher deciding what to write down."},{"q":"Why is the analysis stage a larger discretion than the observation?","a":"Because one imaging dataset put through 34,560 significance maps had no voxel significant across all of them while 90.3 per cent were significant in some, so enough flexibility will find activation almost anywhere."},{"q":"Why is blinding’s value harder to demonstrate than expected?","a":"Because across 142 meta-analyses and 1,153 trials no average difference in estimated treatment effect was found between blinded and unblinded ones, on intervals wide enough to admit a considerable difference."}]},
{"id":"ps-017","num":17,"category":"Psychology","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a well-developed set of ideas explaining observed phenomena, and the hypothesis drawn from it must be capable of being shown incorrect.","questions":["Psychology's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crisis is that they come and go with little cumulative progress, being neither decisively refuted nor accepted as established knowledge.","Ego depletion looked well supported and then measured near zero across many laboratories, which is the shortage of robust phenomena that starves <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of anything to explain."],"answer":"theory","answerText":"theory","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>theory</b> in science is a well-developed set of ideas proposing an explanation for observed phenomena, and a hypothesis is drawn from it as a testable prediction of how the world will behave if the idea is right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prediction has to be falsifiable, capable of being shown to be incorrect, or there is nothing for the evidence to bear on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology has no shortage of theories, being influenced by all of its historical perspectives at once, and on one account its branches are held together by a shared method and methodology rather than by anything they jointly say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it is short of is theories that accumulate, and the complaint now has a name: theories come and go with little cumulative progress, neither decisively refuted nor accepted as established knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is the theory crisis, and it is diagnosed as three problems rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first is a shortage of robust phenomena, the stable and reproducible effects that constrain theorising in more settled sciences, with ego depletion as the example: well supported at first, then found near zero in multi-laboratory work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second is construct validity, since most psychological constructs have never been properly validated and reliability tends to be measured in place of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third is causation, because interventions on psychological variables are fat-handed and change several things at once, which leaves what caused what hard to establish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The remedy recommended is phenomenon-driven work and patient construct validation rather than mathematical formalisation on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stakes reach past psychology, and biologists have begun citing its crisis as a warning, since testing a precise hypothesis drawn from a misapplied or misunderstood theory is a form of research waste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Laura F. Bringmann, “The Theory Crisis in Psychology: How to Move Forward,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): 779–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8273366/. [Open access]","Shinichi Nakagawa et al., “Poor Hypotheses and Research Waste in Biology: Learning from a Theory Crisis in Psychology,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11792729/. [Open access]","Roland Mayrhofer and Fabian Hutmacher, “The Principle of Inversion: Why the Quantitative-Empirical Paradigm Cannot Serve as a Unifying Basis for Psychology as an Academic Discipline,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 596425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7750523/. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.3 Contemporary Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-3-contemporary-psychology. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a shortage of robust phenomena a theory problem?","a":"Because stable reproducible effects are what constrain theorising in more settled sciences — ego depletion was well supported at first and then found near zero in multi-laboratory work."},{"q":"Why does fat-handed intervention make causation hard here?","a":"Because interventions on psychological variables change several things at once, which leaves what caused what hard to establish however clean the statistics are."},{"q":"Why does psychology’s theory crisis matter outside psychology?","a":"Because biologists have begun citing it as a warning: testing a precise hypothesis drawn from a misapplied or misunderstood theory is a form of research waste."}]},
{"id":"ps-018","num":18,"category":"Psychology","question":"A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a testable prediction of how the world will behave if an idea is right, often put as an if-then statement.","questions":["Exploratory work generates a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and confirmatory work tests one, the first using small flexible experiments and the second rigid pre-specified designs.","When experts scored ten dimensions of quality in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, none of the ten reached even moderate agreement at first, and only half did after refinement."],"answer":"hypothesis","answerText":"hypothesis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>hypothesis</b> is a testable prediction about how the world will behave if an idea is right, and it is often worded as an if-then statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is drawn from a theory, a well-developed set of ideas proposing an explanation for observed phenomena, and it has to be falsifiable, which means capable of being shown to be incorrect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ideas are carried into the world and tested while observations in the world suggest new ideas, so a hypothesis is both what deduction tests and what induction produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not every study is testing one, and the working distinction is between exploratory and confirmatory research.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Exploratory studies are small and flexible, with hypotheses loosely articulated or evolving across experiments, where confirmatory studies use rigid pre-specified designs and hypotheses stated in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Treating the first as though it were the second is the common error, since exploratory work buys efficiency with smaller samples and less fastidious designs and so favours catching possibilities over ruling out false ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What makes one hypothesis better than another has been broken into ten dimensions, among them validity, significance, novelty, feasibility, testability and clarity, with a short form of validity, significance and feasibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Judging them turns out to be harder than stating them, and in a validation of those metrics none of the ten reached even moderate agreement between expert raters at first, with half doing so after the raters were calibrated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A prediction is also only as good as what it came from, and testing a precise hypothesis drawn from a misapplied or misunderstood theory is a form of research waste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In psychology that bites harder than elsewhere, because most of its constructs have never been properly validated, so the terms a hypothesis is stated in may not pick out anything stable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","Jonathan Kimmelman, Jeffrey S. Mogil, and Ulrich Dirnagl, “Distinguishing between Exploratory and Confirmatory Preclinical Research Will Improve Translation,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 12, no. 5 (2014): e1001863, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028181/. [Open access]","Xia Jing et al., “Development, Validation, and Usage of Metrics to Evaluate the Quality of Clinical Research Hypotheses,” <i>BMC Medical Research Methodology</i> 25 (2025): 11, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11737058/. [Open access]","Shinichi Nakagawa et al., “Poor Hypotheses and Research Waste in Biology: Learning from a Theory Crisis in Psychology,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11792729/. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Laura F. Bringmann, “The Theory Crisis in Psychology: How to Move Forward,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): 779–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8273366/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must a prediction be capable of being shown incorrect?","a":"Because otherwise there is nothing for the evidence to bear on, and a hypothesis that no observation could contradict is not being tested by any of them."},{"q":"Why is treating exploratory work as confirmatory the common error?","a":"Because exploratory studies buy efficiency with smaller samples and less fastidious designs, so they favour catching possibilities over ruling out false ones."},{"q":"Why is judging a hypothesis harder than stating one?","a":"Because in a validation of ten quality dimensions none reached even moderate agreement between expert raters at first, with only half doing so after calibration."}]},
{"id":"ps-019","num":19,"category":"Psychology","question":"A precise description of a variable, saying exactly how and what is measured, is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and without one nobody else can repeat the experiment.","questions":["Calling two different phenomena by one name, or one phenomenon by two, was blamed a century ago on a vague link between theory and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a study actually uses.","Running 84 specifications of one question about smartphones and parenting turned the association positive in some of them, which shows how much rests on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chosen."],"answer":"operational definition","answerText":"operational definition","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"An <b>operational definition</b> is a precise description of a variable, stating exactly how and what a researcher measures in a particular experiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is what allows other people to interpret the data and to repeat the experiment if they choose to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In observational work it takes the form of criteria fixed before anything is recorded, saying which behaviours count and how they are to be classified, so that what different observers write down can be compared at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The choice is rarely forced, and one question can usually be operationalised in a good many defensible ways, none of them obviously the right one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which one is taken is not a detail of wording, because two studies using the same word may not be measuring the same thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That failure has two names, both over a century old: the jingle fallacy, in which two or more distinct phenomena are labelled with the same term, and the jangle fallacy, in which different terms are used for one phenomenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their originators attributed both to a vague connection between psychological theory and its operationalisation in empirical studies, and they are still treated as a significant threat to the validity of research.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The modern way of showing what such choices are worth is to make all of them, and one study of smartphone use and parenting ran 84 analytic specifications of the same question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The associations came out weak and mixed, and in some specifications more smartphone use went with better parenting rather than worse, which is the point of running the whole set, since a result that depends on one very specific combination of choices is fragile rather than established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Underneath both difficulties lies the same gap, since most psychological constructs have never been properly validated and most articles reviewed reported no validity evidence whatsoever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","science","psychology","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.3 Analyzing Findings,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-3-analyzing-findings. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.2 Approaches to Research,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-2-approaches-to-research. [Open access]","Barbara Hanfstingl et al., “Detecting Jingle and Jangle Fallacies by Identifying Consistencies and Variabilities in Study Specifications: A Call for Research,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 15 (2024): 1404060, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11393684/. [Open access]","Kathryn L. Modecki et al., “Tuning into the Real Effect of Smartphone Use on Parenting: A Multiverse Analysis,” <i>Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry</i> 61, no. 8 (2020): 855–65, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7496662/. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Laura F. Bringmann, “The Theory Crisis in Psychology: How to Move Forward,” <i>Perspectives on Psychological Science</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): 779–88, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8273366/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the choice of operationalisation not a detail of wording?","a":"Because one question can be operationalised in many defensible ways, so two studies using the same word may not be measuring the same thing at all."},{"q":"Why do the jingle and jangle fallacies threaten validity?","a":"Because one names two distinct phenomena with the same term and the other names one phenomenon with different terms, both arising from a vague connection between theory and its operationalisation."},{"q":"Why run 84 specifications of one question?","a":"Because a result that depends on one very specific combination of choices is fragile: in some of them more smartphone use went with better parenting rather than worse."}]},
{"id":"ps-020","num":20,"category":"Psychology","question":"Nine job titles are protected in British law while the bare word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not, so the label can be used by someone the regulator has never heard of.","questions":["A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a psychiatrist may both conduct therapy, but only the second holds a medical degree and may prescribe medication.","Most European countries regulate the practice of clinical psychology but not neuropsychology, so what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is entitled to do depends on which country is asking."],"answer":"psychologist","answerText":"psychologist","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"Who counts as a <b>psychologist</b> is a legal question as much as an academic one, and the answer depends on the country and on what the person actually does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the United Kingdom the health regulator protects nine titles under the heading of practitioner psychologists: practitioner psychologist and registered psychologist, together with the clinical, forensic, counselling, health, educational, occupational, and sport and exercise varieties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bare word is not among them, so somebody may lawfully call themselves a psychologist while being unable to call themselves a clinical one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of the nine are approved as part of the regulatory order but are not titles a registrant can register under on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside the regulated professions the professional body recognises a wider psychological workforce in practitioner and applied roles that are not regulated by law, and calls the growth of that workforce integral to the future of the health service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the United States the gate is licensure, and only those who pass the examination may call themselves licensed clinical or counselling psychologists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is also what separates them from psychiatrists, who hold a doctor of medicine: both may conduct therapy and counselling, psychiatrists may prescribe medication, and licensed clinical psychologists may administer and interpret psychological tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Regulation is uneven across Europe too, since most countries regulate the practice of clinical psychology but not of neuropsychology, and the definition of a clinical neuropsychologist varies from country to country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European certificate intended to standardise such specialisms exists so far only for psychotherapy and for work and organisational psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this reaches most of the discipline, since about a quarter of psychologists work in universities and much of the field — biopsychology, cognition, development, personality, social psychology — is research rather than practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","institution"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["Health and Care Professions Council, “The Professions We Regulate: Practitioner Psychologists,” https://www.hcpc-uk.org/about-us/who-we-regulate/the-professions/. [Open access]","British Psychological Society, “What Is Psychology?,” https://www.bps.org.uk/what-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.4 Careers in Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-4-careers-in-psychology. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.3 Contemporary Psychology,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-3-contemporary-psychology. [Open access]","Laura Hokkanen et al., “Clinical Neuropsychology as a Specialist Profession in European Health Care: Developing a Benchmark for Training Standards and Competencies Using the Europsy Model?,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 559134, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7573555/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can someone be a psychologist and not a clinical one?","a":"Because the UK regulator protects nine specific titles and the bare word is not among them, so the unqualified term may lawfully be used where the specific ones may not."},{"q":"Why are psychologists and psychiatrists distinguished by more than therapy?","a":"Because psychiatrists hold a doctor of medicine and may prescribe medication, while licensed clinical psychologists may administer and interpret psychological tests."},{"q":"Why does none of this reach most of the discipline?","a":"Because about a quarter of psychologists work in universities, and much of the field — biopsychology, cognition, development, personality, social psychology — is research rather than practice."}]},
{"id":"ps-021","num":21,"category":"Psychology","question":"Hume held that the mind's whole creative power is compounding and transposing what the senses afford, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its boldest form, the thesis that experience is our only source of ideas.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> knowledge in a given domain can be gained, if at all, only by experience, which is divided into the outward senses and the inward awareness of our own mental operations.","Asked where basic perceptual abilities come from, 1,188 people including professional scientists appealed to learning rather than innateness, which suggests an intuitive <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about the origins of human knowledge."],"answer":"empiricism","answerText":"empiricism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Empiricism</b> is the thesis that, for a given subject, we have no source of knowledge or of the concepts we use other than experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The experience meant is of two kinds: sense experience, through the outward senses, which yields knowledge of external objects, and reflective experience, the conscious awareness of our own mental operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The thesis is always relative to a subject area, so one may be an empiricist about the physical sciences and a rationalist about mathematics, and the two conflict only when formulated to cover the same ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It promises no knowledge, saying only that knowledge can be gained, if at all, by experience; Hume concluded on those terms that causation is never actually known, but presupposed by a habit of the mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hume put the positive claim starkly: all the materials of thinking are derived from our outward or inward sentiment, and the mind's whole creative power amounts to compounding, transposing, augmenting or diminishing what the senses afford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In psychology the word now travels chiefly in a methodological sense: scientific knowledge is empirical in that it rests on objective, tangible evidence that can be observed repeatedly whoever is doing the observing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that understanding facts are established only by evidence collected through research, and theories and hypotheses earn their standing by being tested against observation, which suggests further ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Quantitative-empirical methods dominate the sciences and psychology alike, and the founders of the academic discipline in the 19th century were already predominantly experimental in orientation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That dominance is argued about, one critique holding that the quantitative-empirical method cannot serve as a unifying basis for the discipline because it misses important dimensions of human nature, while granting that it has revealed a great deal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The older doctrine has an experimental afterlife too, since 1,188 people asked where basic perceptual and cognitive abilities come from, professional psychologists and neuroscientists among them, appealed to learning over innateness and dated those abilities far later than they in fact emerge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Peter Markie and M. Folescu, “Rationalism vs. Empiricism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2023/entries/rationalism-empiricism/. [Open access]","David Hume, <i>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</i>, ed. L. A. Selby-Bigge, sec. 2, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662.txt. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “2.1 Why Is Research Important?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/2-1-why-is-research-important. [Open access]","Roland Mayrhofer and Fabian Hutmacher, “The Principle of Inversion: Why the Quantitative-Empirical Paradigm Cannot Serve as a Unifying Basis for Psychology as an Academic Discipline,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 596425, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7750523/. [Open access]","Jinjing Jenny Wang and Lisa Feigenson, “Is Empiricism Innate? Preference for Nurture Over Nature in People's Beliefs About the Origins of Human Knowledge,” <i>Open Mind</i> 3 (2019): 89–100, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8412204/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is empiricism always relative to a subject area?","a":"Because one may be an empiricist about the physical sciences and a rationalist about mathematics, and the two positions conflict only when formulated to cover the same ground."},{"q":"Why does the thesis promise no knowledge?","a":"Because it says only that knowledge can be gained, if at all, by experience — which is how Hume could conclude that causation is never actually known but presupposed by a habit of the mind."},{"q":"Why is the survey of 1,188 people a comment on the doctrine?","a":"Because professional psychologists and neuroscientists among them appealed to learning over innateness and dated basic perceptual and cognitive abilities far later than they in fact emerge."}]},
{"id":"ps-022","num":22,"category":"Psychology","question":"The three theses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are that some propositions are knowable by intuition alone, that we have innate knowledge of certain truths, and that some concepts belong to our rational nature.","questions":["Holding that experience may trigger our awareness of a truth without ever supplying it is the mark of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which treats reason as a source that the senses cannot match.","Inquiry looks impossible if we either know the answer already or would not recognise it, a paradox answered by the doctrine of recollection, an early form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Greek thought."],"answer":"rationalism","answerText":"rationalism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Rationalism</b> is the view that, in some subject area, reason supplies knowledge or concepts that sense experience could not have provided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three theses are essential to it: that some propositions are knowable by intuition alone and others by deduction from them, intuition being a direct insight in which we simply see a proposition to be true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second, the innate knowledge thesis, holds that we know some truths as part of our own nature; experience may trigger awareness of that knowledge but does not provide it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The third, the innate concept thesis, makes some of the concepts we use part of that same nature, so experience neither supplies them nor settles what they contain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To count as a rationalist is to adopt at least one innate thesis; most add that the senses could not have supplied what reason gives and that what it gives is superior, though neither addition is required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An early version is the doctrine of recollection in Plato's <i>Meno</i>, answering the paradox that a man can inquire neither into what he knows, having no need, nor into what he does not, not knowing what to seek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Descartes reduced the method to four precepts: accept nothing not clearly and distinctly known, divide each difficulty into as many parts as needed, ascend from the simplest to the most complex, and enumerate so completely that nothing is omitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Superiority was argued two ways: Descartes held that what is intuited is certain beyond doubt while sense experience is always somewhat uncertain, and Plato located the advantage in the objects known, which are unchanging and eternal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology defined itself against that inheritance, becoming its own discipline in the late 1800s by adopting a method of observation and experiment rather than one resting on logical argument or previous authorities alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it left behind is a question rather than an answer — how much structure the mind brings to experience — which still drives much research in psychology and neuroscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","philosophy","science","psychology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Peter Markie and M. Folescu, “Rationalism vs. Empiricism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2023/entries/rationalism-empiricism/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Meno</i>, trans. Benjamin Jowett, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1643/pg1643.txt. [Open access]","René Descartes, <i>Discourse on the Method of Rightly Conducting One's Reason and of Seeking Truth in the Sciences</i>, trans. John Veitch, pt. 2, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/59/pg59.txt. [Open access]","<i>Psychology 2e</i>, “1.1 What Is Psychology?,” OpenStax, Rice University, 2020, https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-psychology. [Open access]","Jinjing Jenny Wang and Lisa Feigenson, “Is Empiricism Innate? Preference for Nurture Over Nature in People's Beliefs About the Origins of Human Knowledge,” <i>Open Mind</i> 3 (2019): 89–100, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8412204/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is one innate thesis enough to make someone a rationalist?","a":"Because the further claims — that the senses could not have supplied what reason gives, and that what it gives is superior — are usually added but are not required by the position."},{"q":"Why was the doctrine of recollection proposed in the Meno?","a":"Because it answers the paradox that a man can inquire neither into what he knows, having no need, nor into what he does not, not knowing what to seek."},{"q":"Why did psychology define itself against this inheritance?","a":"Because it became a discipline of its own by adopting observation and experiment rather than a method resting on logical argument or previous authorities alone."}]},
{"id":"ps-023","num":23,"category":"Psychology","question":"The claim that pairs of thoughts become linked by an organism's past experience, so that calling one to mind brings the other with it, is the core of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in all its forms.","questions":["Resemblance, contiguity in time or place, and cause or effect were counted as the three principles of connection among ideas by an early statement of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 18th century.","The gradually rising learning curve that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> predicts turns out to be an artefact of averaging, since individual animals show an abrupt, step-like rise from untrained to trained responding."],"answer":"associationism","answerText":"associationism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Associationism</b> connects learning to thought through an organism's causal history: pairs of thoughts become associated by past experience, so that bringing one to mind brings the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a single theory but a constellation of separable theses, serving as accounts of learning, of thinking, of mental structure, and of how thought is implemented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What ties them together is a commitment to a certain arationality of thought: states are associated because of the history that caused them, not because of any rational relation between what they mean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Locke is usually credited with the doctrine taking hold, though what he wrote was an account of unreasonableness — a connection of ideas owing to chance or custom rather than nature, which he called a degree of madness found in most men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hume gave the first serious treatment, counting three principles of connection among ideas — resemblance, contiguity in time or place, and cause or effect — while admitting the list could not easily be proved complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> James recast the business as physiology, insisting that association holds between objects thought of rather than between ideas, and that there is no elementary causal law of association but the law of neural habit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On his statement of it, when two elementary brain processes have been active together or in succession, one on recurring tends to propagate its excitement into the other, so contiguity, similarity and contrast are derivatives of one deeper fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The doctrine has been the engine behind empiricism for centuries, running from the British empiricists through the behaviourists to today's connectionists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its account of learning is challenged where it is most exposed: the gradually rising learning curve is an artefact of averaging across subjects, individual animals showing an abrupt, often step-like rise from untrained to trained responding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has proved durable all the same, since reinforcement learning formalises the same problem: how an agent comes to select beneficial actions by associating stimuli and responses with their consequences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Eric Mandelbaum and Raphaël Millière, “Associationist Theories of Thought,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2025 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2025/entries/associationist-thought/. [Open access]","John Locke, <i>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</i>, bk. 2, chap. 33, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/10615/10615-h/10615-h.htm. [Open access]","David Hume, <i>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding</i>, ed. L. A. Selby-Bigge, sec. 3, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662.txt. [Open access]","William James, <i>The Principles of Psychology</i>, vol. 1, chap. 14, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57628/pg57628.txt. [Open access]","C. R. Gallistel, Stephen Fairhurst, and Peter Balsam, “The Learning Curve: Implications of a Quantitative Analysis,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 101, no. 36 (2004): 13124–31, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC516535/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is arationality the thread through so many separate theses?","a":"Because states are held to be associated by the history that caused them rather than by any rational relation between what they mean, whether the thesis is about learning, thinking or structure."},{"q":"Why did James recast association as a law of neural habit?","a":"Because he held that association is between objects thought of rather than between ideas, so contiguity, similarity and contrast are derivatives of one deeper physiological fact."},{"q":"Why is the gradual learning curve challenged?","a":"Because it is an artefact of averaging across subjects, individual animals showing an abrupt, often step-like rise from untrained to trained responding."}]},
{"id":"ps-024","num":24,"category":"Psychology","question":"Locke asked his readers to suppose the mind to be white paper, void of all characters, which is the figure now remembered as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in psychology and philosophy alike.","questions":["Leibniz granted that nothing is in the intellect that was not first in the senses, then excepted the intellect itself, which is the standard reply to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a theory of mind.","The doctrine that the mind brings no ideas into the world and is furnished entirely by experience is known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though its author still allowed the mind faculties of its own."],"answer":"blank slate","answerText":"blank slate","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>blank slate</b> is the doctrine that the mind brings no ideas or principles into the world with it and is furnished entirely by experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Locke's own figure is not a slate but white paper: he asks us to suppose the mind void of all characters and without any ideas, then answers in one word how it comes to be furnished, from experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Experience has two fountains for him, sensation and the mind's reflection on its own operations, and the Latin tag now attached to his name appears nowhere in the Essay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His first book attacks innate principles directly: universal consent would prove nothing even if it existed, since agreement can be reached another way, and children cannot make sense of the maxims they are supposed to have been born knowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dilemma he presses is that innateness is either a real alternative to his account and plainly false, or trivially true and no threat to it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The polemic is nearly a sideshow, since the prize is won only by a positive account of knowledge built from experience, and that account gives the mind faculties to work on its ideas, so the slate is blank of ideas rather than of powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leibniz replied with an exception to the axiom: nothing is in the intellect that was not first in the senses, except the intellect itself, which supplies being, substance, unity, identity and cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He added that the exception partly agreed with the Essay itself, which already made the mind's reflection on its own nature a source of ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Locke put the doctrine to work on children, holding that nine parts in ten of the men we meet are what they are by their education, and that the almost insensible impressions of infancy have lasting consequences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The intuition reaches well beyond philosophy, since American adults, adults from a culture believing in reincarnation, children and professional scientists alike explain basic perceptual and cognitive abilities by learning rather than innateness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"sources":["John Locke, <i>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</i>, bks. 1–2, https://www.gutenberg.org/files/10615/10615-h/10615-h.htm. [Open access]","Jerry Samet, “The Historical Controversies Surrounding Innateness,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2024/entries/innateness-history/. [Open access]","Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, <i>New Essays Concerning Human Understanding</i>, trans. Alfred Gideon Langley, bk. 2, chap. 1, https://archive.org/stream/newessaysconcern00leibuoft/newessaysconcern00leibuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","John Locke, <i>Some Thoughts Concerning Education</i>, sec. 1, https://archive.org/stream/somethoughtsconc00lockuoft/somethoughtsconc00lockuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Jinjing Jenny Wang and Lisa Feigenson, “Is Empiricism Innate? Preference for Nurture Over Nature in People's Beliefs About the Origins of Human Knowledge,” <i>Open Mind</i> 3 (2019): 89–100, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8412204/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the slate blank of ideas rather than of powers?","a":"Because Locke’s positive account gives the mind faculties to work on its ideas, and reflection on its own operations is itself one of his two fountains of experience."},{"q":"Why did Locke think universal consent would prove nothing?","a":"Because agreement can be reached another way, and children cannot make sense of the maxims they are supposed to have been born knowing."},{"q":"Why did Leibniz reply with an exception rather than a denial?","a":"Because he granted that nothing is in the intellect that was not first in the senses except the intellect itself, which supplies being, substance, unity, identity and cause."}]},
{"id":"ps-025","num":25,"category":"Psychology","question":"Minds and bodies being fundamentally distinct kinds of substance, one characterised by thought and the other by extension, is the position known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the philosophy of mind.","questions":["Elisabeth of Bohemia objected that existing accounts tied causal power to extension, which left <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unable to say how an unextended mind could move a body at all.","A doctrine claiming that the mind would continue to be all that it is even if the body were not is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it made interaction the hardest question of all."],"answer":"substance dualism","answerText":"substance dualism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Substance dualism</b> is the view that minds and bodies are fundamentally distinct kinds of substance, the mind characterised by thought and the body by extension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A substance in that sense stands in need of nothing beyond itself in order to exist, and each is known through a single principal attribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Descartes reached it by doubt: he could suppose he had no body and no world, but not that he did not exist, and so was a substance whose whole essence consists only in thinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reasoning the mind is wholly distinct from the body, more easily known than it, and would continue to be all that it is even if the body were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two shifts came with it: dualism was joined to a new mechanistic conception of nature, and the argument for the mind's immateriality moved from the intellect to consciousness, taken to include sensory awareness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is in that mechanistic setting that interaction became the leading objection, since two radically different kinds of thing seem to lack what they would need in common to act on one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed the point first, in a correspondence she began in 1643, and she framed it exactly: existing accounts tied causal power to extension, so how could an unextended mind move a body?<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She asked about the mind acting on the body rather than the body on the mind, and rejected the reply appealing to the Scholastic notion of heaviness as unintelligible and inconsistent with the mechanist account of nature he had himself argued for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The doctrine held the field regardless: in 1949 it could still be called the official doctrine to which most philosophers, psychologists and religious teachers subscribed with minor reservation, and only then did the consensus evaporate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survives as an entry point to the subject in undergraduate teaching, where the thinking thing and the extended thing are introduced and then set against simple bodily illusions demonstrating a contrary account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","philosophy","psychology","science"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Howard Robinson and Ralph Weir, “Dualism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/. [Open access]","René Descartes, <i>Discourse on the Method of Rightly Conducting One's Reason and of Seeking Truth in the Sciences</i>, trans. John Veitch, pt. 4, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/59/pg59.txt. [Open access]","René Descartes, <i>Selections from the Principles of Philosophy</i>, trans. John Veitch, pt. 1, secs. 51–53, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4391/pg4391.txt. [Open access]","Lisa Shapiro, “Elisabeth, Princess of Bohemia,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2021 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2021/entries/elisabeth-bohemia/. [Open access]","Scott Hamilton and Trevor J. Hamilton, “Pedagogical Tools to Explore Cartesian Mind-Body Dualism in the Classroom: Philosophical Arguments and Neuroscience Illusions,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 6 (2015): 1155, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4530257/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is interaction the leading objection to the doctrine?","a":"Because two radically different kinds of thing seem to lack what they would need in common to act on one another, a point Elisabeth of Bohemia pressed in 1643."},{"q":"Why did she reject Descartes’ reply about heaviness?","a":"Because appealing to the Scholastic notion was unintelligible and inconsistent with the mechanist account of nature he had himself argued for."},{"q":"Why was the position still called the official doctrine in 1949?","a":"Because most philosophers, psychologists and religious teachers subscribed to it with minor reservation, and only then did that consensus evaporate."}]},
{"id":"ps-026","num":26,"category":"Psychology","question":"The thesis that everything is physical, including whatever at first glance looks biological or moral or mathematical, goes under the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the philosophy of mind.","questions":["Sensible qualities being nothing in the object but so many motions of matter pressing on the sense organs is a 17th-century statement of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> applied to perception.","Every event with a cause having a physical cause, together with mental events causing physical ones, is the argument from causal closure for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a thesis about mind."],"answer":"materialism","answerText":"materialism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Materialism</b> is the thesis that everything is physical, a claim about the nature of the world rather than about how it should be described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not deny that the world holds items seeming otherwise — biological, psychological, moral, mathematical — but insists these are in the end physical, or bear an important relation to the physical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word is old, entering English late in the 17th century; physicalism was coined in the 1930s by the Vienna Circle and meant at first a thesis about language, every statement being synonymous with a physical one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two are now often used interchangeably, though the second is the safer word, since one may be a materialist about mind and still hold that physics refers to entities not happily called material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ancestry is traced to Democritus and the claim that all is atoms and the void; the standard 19th-century history opens by saying materialism is as old as philosophy but not older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hobbes gave it a psychology: the qualities we call sensible are, in the object causing them, so many motions of matter pressing on the organs, and sense is nothing but the appearance motion produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A century later a French physician concluded boldly that man is a machine, that the universe holds a single substance differently modified, and that the soul is an empty word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The 20th-century version is the identity theory, on which experiences just are brain processes rather than merely correlated with them; it dates from the 1950s and was named, apparently first, by a psychologist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument most often given runs from causal closure: every event with a cause has a physical cause, mental events do cause physical ones, and one effect may not have two independent causes, so mental events must be physical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The honest caveat comes from within: an overwhelmingly materialist culture sets a lower standard of argument for the thesis than against it, and dualism is still the second most popular answer among professional philosophers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","philosophy","psychology","science"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Daniel Stoljar, “Physicalism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2024/entries/physicalism/. [Open access]","J. J. C. Smart, “The Mind/Brain Identity Theory,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2022 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2022/entries/mind-identity/. [Open access]","Thomas Hobbes, <i>Leviathan</i>, pt. 1, chap. 1, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3207/pg3207.txt. [Open access]","Julien Offray de La Mettrie, <i>Man a Machine</i>, trans. Gertrude Carman Bussey, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/52090/pg52090.txt. [Open access]","Howard Robinson and Ralph Weir, “Dualism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dualism/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is physicalism the safer word of the two?","a":"Because one may be a materialist about mind and still hold that physics refers to entities not happily called material, so the older word promises more than the thesis needs."},{"q":"Why does the causal closure argument reach its conclusion?","a":"Because every event with a cause has a physical cause, mental events do cause physical ones, and one effect may not have two independent causes."},{"q":"Why is a caveat offered from inside the position?","a":"Because an overwhelmingly materialist culture sets a lower standard of argument for the thesis than against it, and dualism is still the second most popular answer among professional philosophers."}]},
{"id":"ps-027","num":27,"category":"Psychology","question":"Holding that a body of concepts or knowledge is part of an individual's initial state, needing no input from the environment, is the commitment that defines <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a position.","questions":["One catalogue found at least six meanings bundled into the key word of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them present at birth, adapted over evolution, and shared by all members of a species.","The claim that instincts arise by the slow accumulation of slight profitable variations gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an evolutionary footing while leaving its central term as vague as it had been."],"answer":"nativism","answerText":"nativism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nativism</b> holds that some body of concepts or knowledge is part of an individual's initial state, or develops from that state without needing input from the environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In scientific psychology innate and instinctive have named the same cluster of properties, instinct being a now antiquated word for an innate psychological trait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Darwin set the modern terms: no complex instinct could be produced except by the slow accumulation of numerous slight profitable variations, and instincts are not always perfect and are liable to mistakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The trouble is that the key word carries six meanings at once: present at birth, a behavioural difference caused by a genetic one, adapted over evolution, unchanging through development, shared by all of a species, and not learned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The objection is not the ambiguity but the equivocation it invites, since a trait called innate for being present at birth is then treated as innate in the sense of insensitive to the environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Psychologists have long warned of it, Morgan observing in 1896 that terms in his field are used in different senses by different authors; one count puts the properties bundled into innateness at 26.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A second complaint is that innateness claims draw attention from the many subtle ways the environment contributes to development, an objection raised by biologists, psychologists and philosophers alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A psychological explanation has been offered for its staying power: innateness is a cornerstone of folk biology, and people across cultures assume each species has an essence determining its appearance and behaviour, holding an essence placeholder even when they can say nothing about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tendency is measurable rather than merely deliberative, since participants shown genetic concepts categorise faster when genes and fate are paired than when the two are kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Attempts to rescue a usable notion define it as genetic specification, invariance across developmental environments, or canalisation against disturbance, while eliminativists would drop the distinction; what is undisputed is that the everyday concept is out of step with recent biology and psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","philosophy","science"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Mark Cain, “Innateness and Contemporary Theories of Cognition,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/innateness-cognition/. [Open access]","Paul Griffiths and Stefan Linquist, “The Distinction Between Innate and Acquired Characteristics,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2025 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2025/entries/innate-acquired/. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 6th ed., chap. 8, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2009/pg2009.txt. [Open access]","C. Lloyd Morgan, <i>Habit and Instinct</i>, https://archive.org/stream/habitandinstinc00morggoog/habitandinstinc00morggoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Wren A. Gould and Steven J. Heine, “Implicit Essentialism: Genetic Concepts Are Implicitly Associated with Fate Concepts,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 7, no. 6 (2012): e38176, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3369887/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the ambiguity of innate not itself the objection?","a":"Because the objection is the equivocation it invites: a trait called innate for being present at birth is then treated as innate in the sense of insensitive to the environment."},{"q":"Why does the notion survive despite those warnings?","a":"Because innateness is a cornerstone of folk biology, people across cultures assuming each species has an essence determining its appearance and behaviour even when they can say nothing about it."},{"q":"Why is that tendency called measurable rather than deliberative?","a":"Because participants shown genetic concepts categorise faster when genes and fate are paired than when the two are kept apart."}]},
{"id":"ps-028","num":28,"category":"Psychology","question":"Insisting that inner observation alters what it observes and that time has only one dimension, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> denied that empirical psychology could ever be a natural science proper.","questions":["Only cognition whose certainty is apodictic counted as science for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which left chemistry a systematic art and psychology a natural description of the soul.","The doctrine that space and time are forms of sensibility, taken from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and given a naturalistic reading, sent 19th-century physiologists to study how spatial representation arises."],"answer":"Immanuel Kant","answerText":"Immanuel Kant","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 April 1724</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Königsberg, East Prussia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 February 1804</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Immanuel Kant</b> is the central figure in modern philosophy, a synthesiser of early modern rationalism and empiricism who set the terms for much of the two centuries that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fundamental idea of his critical philosophy is human autonomy, and his central claim about nature is that the human understanding is itself the source of the general laws that structure all our experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That claim bears directly on psychology, because he also held a demanding view of what a science is: only cognition whose certainty is apodictic deserves the name, and everything resting on merely empirical grounds is called science by courtesy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that measure chemistry was a systematic art rather than a science, since no law of the approach and retreat of the parts of matter could be presented in advance of experience and made mathematical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Empirical psychology he placed further still from that rank, and gave three reasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The first is that mathematics cannot reach the phenomena of inner sense, whose pure intuition is time, and time has only one dimension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second is that the manifold of internal observation can be separated only in thought and cannot be kept apart and recombined at will, so the discipline cannot even manage chemistry’s experimental trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third is the sharpest: another thinking subject is not amenable to investigation of this kind, and the observation itself alters and distorts the state of the object observed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What was left, on this verdict, was a natural description of the soul rather than a science of it, and later readers have split over whether the obstacle was the impossibility of mathematising inner sense or the absence of genuine laws that prediction and experiment could confirm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The irony is that the doctrine which did shape the new discipline was the other one, since treating space and time as forms of sensibility that need not be innate sent physiologists and psychologists to investigate empirically how spatial representation arises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["person","philosophy","psychology","science"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Michael Rohlf, “Immanuel Kant,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2024/entries/kant/. [Open access]","Immanuel Kant, <i>Metaphysical Foundations of Natural Science</i>, preface, trans. Ernest Belfort Bax, https://archive.org/stream/prolegomenametap00kantuoft/prolegomenametap00kantuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Eric Watkins and Marius Stan, “Kant’s Philosophy of Science,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Fall 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/kant-science/. [Open access]","Andrew Brook and Julian Wuerth, “Kant’s View of the Mind and Consciousness of Self,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2023/entries/kant-mind/. [Open access]","Immanuel Kant, <i>The Critique of Pure Reason</i>, trans. J. M. D. Meiklejohn, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Immanuel_Kant_by_Johann_Christoph_Frisch.jpg","title":"Immanuel Kant","desc":"Painting by Johann Christoph Frisch, a copy after the portrait by Johann Gottlieb Becker. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Immanuel_Kant_by_Johann_Christoph_Frisch.jpg","alt":"A half-length painted portrait of a slight, elderly man in a powdered wig and dark coat, turned slightly to one side against a plain dark ground"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Kant deny empirical psychology the rank of a science?","a":"Because mathematics cannot reach the phenomena of inner sense, whose pure intuition is time, and time has only one dimension."},{"q":"Why could it not even manage chemistry’s experimental trade?","a":"Because the manifold of internal observation can be separated only in thought and cannot be kept apart and recombined at will."},{"q":"Why is his sharpest reason the one about another subject?","a":"Because another thinking subject is not amenable to investigation of this kind, the observation itself altering and distorting the state of the object observed."}]},
{"id":"ps-029","num":29,"category":"Psychology","question":"Reading character from the shape of the head, on the theory that the brain is a set of organs whose size shows in the skull, was the business of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 19th century.","questions":["An MRI study of 5,724 people found that scalp curvature predicted no lifestyle measure at all, which is the most rigorous test <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has ever been given.","The founder counted 27 organs of the brain and his collaborator 35, a disagreement that did little to slow <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on its way across Europe."],"answer":"phrenology","answerText":"phrenology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First presented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1796, on the Continent</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1814</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Phrenology</b> professed to be a philosophy of the human mind, taking the brain as the organ of mind, the brain as a set of distinct organs, and the size of each organ as something legible in the shape of the head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was first presented to public consideration on the Continent in 1796 and in Britain in 1814.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gall counted 27 functional organs and Spurzheim later declared 35, and the two lectured across Europe to theatres filled to the brim, one explaining the findings while the other dissected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They differed about what the bumps meant, Gall holding that a certain bulge in the cranium made a man a murderer or a thief, and his collaborator insisting that God could never create anything innately bad and that people made themselves into murderers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The claims were specific enough to be embarrassing: those of a mirthful disposition were to show two prominent bumps on the forehead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The system carried the prejudices of its century in its own apparatus, the standard British textbook indexing skulls by nation and race alongside those of named murderers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Derivatives outlived it, a Belgian priest founding psychognomy from phrenology together with craniofacial measurement, physiognomy and graphology, and a Brazilian institute devoted to it staying active until the early 1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The central claim was eventually tested rather than dismissed, using structural MRI from 5,724 people to measure local scalp curvature and matching 23 lifestyle measures to phrenological faculties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result was no evidence for it: brain folding explained very little of the variance in scalp curvature, and the head-shape regressions produced no statistically significant or meaningful effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives of the enterprise is not the reading of bumps but the anatomy done alongside it, and the bare idea underneath the system that mental functions belong to particular parts of the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["theory","psychology","science","history"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["O. Parker Jones, F. Alfaro-Almagro, and S. Jbabdi, “An Empirical, 21st Century Evaluation of Phrenology,” <i>Cortex</i> 106 (2018): 26–35, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6143440/. [Open access]","Mohammad Bilal, Bryan Edwards, Marios Loukas, Rod J. Oskouian, and R. Shane Tubbs, “Johann Gaspar Spurzheim: A Life Dedicated to Phrenology,” <i>Cureus</i> 9, no. 5 (2017): e1295, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5493471/. [Open access]","J. G. Spurzheim, <i>The Physiognomical System of Drs. Gall and Spurzheim</i>, https://archive.org/stream/b24925020/b24925020_djvu.txt. [Open access]","George Combe, <i>A System of Phrenology</i>, https://archive.org/stream/systemofphrenolo00comb/systemofphrenolo00comb_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles André, “Paul Bouts and the Short Life of Psychognomy in Brazil,” <i>Arquivos de Neuro-Psiquiatria</i> 80, no. 8 (2022): 862–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9703887/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Numbered_phrenological_head%2C_1825_Wellcome_L0008290.jpg","title":"Numbered phrenological head, 1825","desc":"Front, back and side views of a head divided into numbered organs, from a work associated with Johann Gaspar Spurzheim. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numbered_phrenological_head,_1825_Wellcome_L0008290.jpg","alt":"Three engraved views of a bald human head, front, back and in profile, its surface divided into small numbered compartments covering the whole skull"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the central claim testable rather than merely dismissible?","a":"Because it says head shape reads brain organ size, which was checked with structural MRI from 5,724 people: folding explained very little of the variance in scalp curvature and the regressions gave no meaningful effects."},{"q":"Why did its two founders disagree about what a bump meant?","a":"Because Gall held that a bulge made a man a murderer or a thief, while Spurzheim insisted God could never create anything innately bad and that people made themselves so."},{"q":"Why does anything of the enterprise survive?","a":"Because what lasted is not the reading of bumps but the anatomy done alongside it, and the bare idea that mental functions belong to particular parts of the brain."}]},
{"id":"ps-030","num":30,"category":"Psychology","question":"The claim that a particular mental function depends on a particular part of the brain is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the argument over it is older than agreement that the mind is in the head.","questions":["Removing a portion of the cerebral hemispheres without abolishing intelligence, and finding that losing one sensation lost them all, was 19th-century evidence against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its strict form.","Because one behavioural outcome can be reached by different neurocognitive systems, degeneracy leaves <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without any area that is both necessary and sufficient for a function."],"answer":"localisation of function","answerText":"localisation of function","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Localisation of function</b> is the claim that a particular mental function depends on a particular part of the brain, and the argument is older than agreement that the mind is in the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Egyptians placed the higher functions in the heart and Greek scholars stayed undecided for centuries; once the head won, a second argument opened over whether they lay in the ventricles or in the nervous tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tissue prevailed, and the 19th century made what remained an experimental question by removing parts of animal brains and watching what was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Flourens reported that a fairly extensive portion of the cerebral hemispheres could be cut away, front, back, above or side, without intelligence being lost, so a restricted portion sufficed for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As removal went further intelligence weakened and died out, and once one sensation was lost all were, from which he concluded that the faculties have no separate seats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ninety years later Lashley reached a comparable verdict from mazes and lesions, naming equipotentiality: the capacity of any intact part of an area to do what destruction of the whole removes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He hedged it carefully: it is not absolute, probably applies only to the association areas and to functions more complex than simple sensitivity, and is bounded by a law of mass action under which performance falls with the extent of injury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lesion method was long the benchmark for showing a region necessary to a function and activation studies for showing one sufficient, so together they were expected to settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That hope was given up in the face of degeneracy, since one behavioural outcome can be reached by different neurocognitive systems, which leaves it doubtful that any area is both necessary and sufficient for a function.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What replaced the search for seats is the mapping of large-scale distributed networks: a language network answers to meaningful sentences and not to non-words, yet spreads across frontal, temporal and cingulate cortex beside other networks of the same shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Eliasz Engelhardt, “Cerebral Localization of the Mind and Higher Functions: The Beginnings,” <i>Dementia &amp; Neuropsychologia</i> 12, no. 3 (2018): 321–25, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6200162/. [Open access]","Pierre Flourens, <i>Examen de la phrénologie</i>, https://archive.org/stream/examendelaphrno00flougoog/examendelaphrno00flougoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Karl S. Lashley, <i>Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence</i>, https://archive.org/stream/brainmechanismsi00lash/brainmechanismsi00lash_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Sarah Genon, Andrew Reid, Robert Langner, Katrin Amunts, and Simon B. Eickhoff, “How to Characterize the Function of a Brain Region,” <i>Trends in Cognitive Sciences</i> 22, no. 4 (2018): 350–64, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7978486/. [Open access]","Rodrigo M. Braga, Lauren M. DiNicola, Hannah C. Becker, and Randy L. Buckner, “Situating the Left-Lateralized Language Network in the Broader Organization of Multiple Specialized Large-Scale Distributed Networks,” <i>Journal of Neurophysiology</i> 124, no. 5 (2020): 1415–48, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8356783/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Campbell_AW.Histological_studies_on_the_localisation_of_cerebral_function_Plate_1.jpg","title":"A cortical map of 1905","desc":"Plate 1 of Alfred Walter Campbell, Histological Studies on the Localisation of Cerebral Function, Cambridge University Press, 1905. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campbell_AW.Histological_studies_on_the_localisation_of_cerebral_function_Plate_1.jpg","alt":"Two line drawings of a human brain, one from the side and one cut down the middle, with the cortex divided into areas marked by different hatchings and stipplings and labelled prefrontal, frontal, precentral, postcentral, parietal, temporal, limbic, olfactory, visuo-sensory, visuo-psychic, audito-sensory and audito-psychic"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Flourens conclude the faculties have no separate seats?","a":"Because extensive portions of the hemispheres could be cut away without losing intelligence, and as removal went further all sensations were lost together rather than one at a time."},{"q":"Why did Lashley hedge equipotentiality so carefully?","a":"Because he held it not absolute, probably confined to the association areas and to functions more complex than simple sensitivity, and bounded by a law of mass action."},{"q":"Why was the hope of settling this by lesions and imaging given up?","a":"Because of degeneracy: one behavioural outcome can be reached by different neurocognitive systems, which leaves it doubtful that any area is both necessary and sufficient for a function."}]},
{"id":"ps-031","num":31,"category":"Psychology","question":"The posterior part of the third frontal convolution of the left hemisphere, argued in the 1860s to be the seat of articulate language, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it gave localisation its first solid case.","questions":["A patient with no paralysis of the articulatory muscles, who understood speech and could write but could not coordinate speech, was the evidence that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most famous region in the brain.","Imaging of the preserved brain of that first patient showed damage reaching well beyond <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, into connections projecting to areas far from the lesion itself."],"answer":"Broca's area","answerText":"Broca's area","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Broca's area</b> is the posterior part of the third frontal convolution of the left hemisphere, named for the surgeon who argued in the 1860s that it is the seat of articulate language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The symptom he defined was aphémie, the loss or alteration of the faculty of articulate language in a person with no paralysis of the muscles of articulation, who understands what is said and can make himself understood by gesture or in writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a patient has ideas and knows how to express them, having lost only the coordinating faculty of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At autopsy of such cases a very evident lesion was found almost constantly in the posterior half of the third frontal convolution, sometimes confined to it, and about nineteen times in twenty on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He drew the general moral himself: it is enough for one intellectual faculty to stand in special relation to a circumscribed part of the brain for the localisation of the others to become very probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The priority was not his alone, since Dax had connected speech to the left hemisphere decades earlier, and the long period between Willis and 1861 was full of contributory work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He expected exceptions too, writing that the rule would probably suffer a few rare ones in time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The brain of his first patient was preserved rather than sectioned, and imaging much later showed the damage extending well beyond the convolution that carries his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tractography applied to that lesion found disruption reaching connections which project to areas distant from it, so the classic case was never the clean local injury the textbooks made of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The view of language circuitry has since shifted from a few cortical centres to a distributed and dynamically interconnected system, and the classical modular model, still useful as a first approach to the aphasias, is no longer thought to describe the networks language actually uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["place","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Paul Broca, <i>Exposé des titres et travaux scientifiques</i>, https://archive.org/stream/BIUSante_110133x002x20/BIUSante_110133x002x20_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Eliasz Engelhardt, “Cerebral Localization of Higher Functions: The Period between Thomas Willis and Paul Broca,” <i>Dementia &amp; Neuropsychologia</i> 13, no. 2 (2019): 238–43, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6601299/. [Open access]","Michel Thiebaut de Schotten et al., “From Phineas Gage and Monsieur Leborgne to H.M.: Revisiting Disconnection Syndromes,” <i>Cerebral Cortex</i> 25, no. 12 (2015): 4812–27, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4635921/. [Open access]","Grigorios Nasios, Efthymios Dardiotis, and Lambros Messinis, “From Broca and Wernicke to the Neuromodulation Era: Insights of Brain Language Networks for Neurorehabilitation,” <i>Behavioural Neurology</i> 2019 (2019): 9894571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6679886/. [Open access]","Carmen Barba et al., “Beyond Broca and Wernicke: Epilepsy Surgery in the Language Areas,” <i>Epileptic Disorders</i> 28, no. 3 (2026): 581–601, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13276694/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cerveau_de_Louis_Victor_Leborgne_dit_Tantan_1.jpg/1280px-Cerveau_de_Louis_Victor_Leborgne_dit_Tantan_1.jpg","title":"The brain of Louis Victor Leborgne","desc":"The preserved brain of Broca's first aphasic patient, deposited by Broca at the Musee Dupuytren in 1861. Photograph by Selbymay, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerveau_de_Louis_Victor_Leborgne_dit_Tantan_1.jpg","alt":"A human brain preserved in fluid in a stoppered glass jar, its convolutions pale and slumped, standing behind a handwritten museum label and a black and white ten centimetre scale bar"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the symptom defined so narrowly?","a":"Because it names a loss of articulate language in someone with no paralysis of the articulating muscles, who understands speech and can answer by gesture or in writing."},{"q":"Why did Broca draw a general moral from one localisation?","a":"Because it is enough for one intellectual faculty to stand in special relation to a circumscribed part of the brain for the localisation of the others to become very probable."},{"q":"Why was the founding case never the clean injury textbooks made of it?","a":"Because imaging of the preserved brain showed damage extending well beyond that convolution, and tractography found disruption reaching connections projecting to distant areas."}]},
{"id":"ps-032","num":32,"category":"Psychology","question":"The posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere, associated with understanding language rather than producing it, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the classical account of the aphasias.","questions":["Fluent speech without sense, together with a failure to follow what is said, is the syndrome that follows damage to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than to the frontal speech region.","Interrupting the arcuate fasciculus was predicted in 1874 to give a third aphasia, distinct from damage to the frontal speech region and from damage to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself."],"answer":"Wernicke's area","answerText":"Wernicke's area","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wernicke's area</b> lies in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere and is associated with understanding language rather than producing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was identified in 1874, when a second kind of aphasia demanded an account: patients who spoke fluently but without sense and did not follow what was said to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What he offered was not a second centre bolted onto the first but a model, in which the sensory and the motor images of speech sit in different places and are joined by fibres running through the white matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third form of aphasia followed from it as a prediction, Leitungsaphasie or conduction aphasia, produced not by destroying either centre but by interrupting the tracts that join the frontal lobe to the occipito-temporal lobe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He named the tract that would have to be cut, the arcuate bundle of Burdach, and worked out what a deaf-mute with each of the three forms would look like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The programme was connectionist from the beginning, and he went on to sponsor the first white-matter atlas of the occipital lobe and to write its prologue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tract has held up: in a cohort of people with chronic aphasia after left-hemisphere stroke, the integrity of the arcuate fasciculus bore on spontaneous speech, word retrieval, repetition and comprehension alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The map has not held up as well, since four decades of neuroimaging have complemented and often challenged the classical views, and the notion of a motor speech centre in particular has been entirely transformed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Comprehension is now described as the integration of auditory and visual input with semantic and syntactic information, drawing on connectivity that reaches well past a single gyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What lasted is the shape of the argument rather than the chart: that a symptom can be predicted from the anatomy of connections, and that damage between centres matters as much as damage to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["place","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"sources":["Carl Wernicke, <i>Der aphasische Symptomencomplex</i>, https://archive.org/stream/b24763445/b24763445_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Pascale Tremblay and Simona M. Brambati, “A Historical Perspective on the Neurobiology of Speech and Language: From the 19th Century to the Present,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 15 (2024): 1420133, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11445075/. [Open access]","Pinaki D. Wani, “From Sound to Meaning: Navigating Wernicke’s Area in Language Processing,” <i>Cureus</i> 16, no. 9 (2024): e69833, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11491986/. [Open access]","Maria V. Ivanova, Allison Zhong, A. Turken, Juliana V. Baldo, and Nina F. Dronkers, “Functional Contributions of the Arcuate Fasciculus to Language Processing,” <i>Frontiers in Human Neuroscience</i> 15 (2021): 672665, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8267805/. [Open access]","Stephanie J. Forkel, Sajedha Mahmood, Francesco Vergani, and Marco Catani, “The White Matter of the Human Cerebrum: Part I. The Occipital Lobe by Heinrich Sachs,” <i>Cortex</i> 62 (2015): 182–202, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4298656/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a second aphasia not answered with a second centre?","a":"Because Wernicke offered a model instead, with sensory and motor images of speech in different places joined by fibres running through the white matter."},{"q":"Why does the predicted third aphasia matter so much?","a":"Because conduction aphasia follows from interrupting the tracts rather than either centre, so a symptom was predicted from the anatomy of connections before it was found."},{"q":"Why has the map held up less well than the tract?","a":"Because four decades of neuroimaging have challenged the classical views and comprehension is now described as drawing on connectivity reaching well past a single gyrus."}]},
{"id":"ps-033","num":33,"category":"Psychology","question":"A nerve carrying not the quality of what stimulates it but a quality of its own is the doctrine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, argued from electricity producing a different sensation in each sense.","questions":["Pressure on the eye raising a spot of light, and on the ear a humming, was the evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the older view that a nerve is a passive conductor.","Labelled-line coding in sensory neuroscience is the surviving descendant of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the 19th-century claim that we feel the state of our nerves rather than the world."],"answer":"specific nerve energies","answerText":"specific nerve energies","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The doctrine of <b>specific nerve energies</b> holds that a sensory nerve carries not the quality of what stimulates it but a quality of its own, so that we sense the state of the nerve rather than the state of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument was empirical: electricity applied to any organ of sense produces a sensation, but a different one in each — light in one nerve, sound in another, taste in a third, pain in a fourth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mechanical pressure does the same, raising a luminous spectrum in one nerve, a humming in another and pain in a third, which told against the older view of a nerve as a passive conductor tuned to certain qualities of bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The conclusion was that each nerve of sense has powers of its own which the exciting cause merely renders manifest: we do not feel the knife that hurts us but the painful state of our nerves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A consequence was drawn at once: blind people cannot recognise colours with their fingers, and the doctrine explains why they cannot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its author was the leading physiologist of his generation, teaching physiology as a comprehensive theory of life and opposing the speculative natural philosophy of his day with an empirical idealism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The doctrine survives in sensory neuroscience as labelled-line coding, the idea that a distinct population of fibres carries a distinct quality, still argued modality by modality rather than settled in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where it has given ground is on the tissue itself, since several visual cortical areas develop without visual experience and handle their content whatever sense delivers it, a property called supramodality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His consequence about blindness has given ground too: congenitally blind adults using a device that turns images into sound have passed the World Health Organization blindness threshold on a standard acuity chart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What remains is the half that never depended on the nerve: what a sensation is like is fixed by the machinery it runs through, not by the stimulus that started it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["theory","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"sources":["Johannes Müller, <i>Elements of Physiology</i>, trans. William Baly, bk. 3, sec. 4, https://archive.org/stream/Muller1838na12J-a/Muller1838na12J-a_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Ulrich Zeller and Ingmar Werneburg, “A Life Dedicated to Research and Ideal: Johannes Müller between Empirical Universality and Idealistic Vitalism Mirrored in Lecture Notes from 1851,” <i>Theory in Biosciences</i> 143, no. 3 (2024): 161–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11347478/. [Open access]","Omar Páez, Pedro Segura-Chama, Angélica Almanza, Francisco Pellicer, and Francisco Mercado, “Properties and Differential Expression of H+ Receptors in Dorsal Root Ganglia: Is a Labeled-Line Coding for Acid Nociception Possible?,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 12 (2021): 733267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8576393/. [Open access]","Luca Cecchetti, Ron Kupers, Maurice Ptito, Pietro Pietrini, and Emiliano Ricciardi, “Are Supramodality and Cross-Modal Plasticity the Yin and Yang of Brain Development? From Blindness to Rehabilitation,” <i>Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience</i> 10 (2016): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5099160/. [Open access]","Ella Striem-Amit, Miriam Guendelman, and Amir Amedi, “‘Visual’ Acuity of the Congenitally Blind Using Visual-to-Auditory Sensory Substitution,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 7, no. 3 (2012): e33136, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3306374/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did one stimulus applied to different nerves settle the argument?","a":"Because electricity produces a different sensation in each — light in one nerve, sound in another, taste in a third, pain in a fourth — which a passive conductor tuned to qualities of bodies could not do."},{"q":"Why has the doctrine given ground on the tissue itself?","a":"Because several visual cortical areas develop without visual experience and handle their content whatever sense delivers it, a property called supramodality."},{"q":"Why has its consequence about blindness given ground too?","a":"Because congenitally blind adults using a device that turns images into sound have passed the World Health Organization blindness threshold on a standard acuity chart."}]},
{"id":"ps-034","num":34,"category":"Psychology","question":"Trained as an army surgeon and later professor of physics at Berlin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> invented the ophthalmoscope and argued that seeing is an unconscious judgement about what would ordinarily have made such an image.","questions":["Having stated the conservation of force in an 1847 pamphlet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned to the senses, measured the curvature of the focusing lens, and made perception a judgement rather than a picture on the retina.","Treating the cochlea as a bank of resonators and the retina as three kinds of colour fibre, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reduced differences of pitch and of hue to differences between the nerves that carry them."],"answer":"Hermann von Helmholtz","answerText":"Hermann von Helmholtz","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1894</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hermann von Helmholtz</b> was a Prussian army physician who became professor of physiology at Königsberg and Heidelberg and professor of physics at Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name was made in 1847 by a pamphlet read to the Physical Society of Berlin, <i>Die Erhaltung der Kraft</i>, which stated the conservation of force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He invented the ophthalmoscope in 1851, bringing the retina of a living eye into view, and the ophthalmometer in 1855, with which he confirmed that the lens changes curvature as the eye focuses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His <i>Handbuch der physiologischen Optik</i> of 1867 came in three parts, on the optics of the eye, the sensations of vision and the theory of visual perception: the physics, the physiology and then the psychology of seeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <i>On the Sensations of Tone</i> did as much for hearing, treating the arches of the cochlea as resonators, so that a difference of pitch became a difference between nerve fibres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Both rest on the doctrine that a sensation follows the nerve rather than the stimulus: nerves, he wrote, are telegraph wires, carrying one current whatever apparatus is fixed to their ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same reasoning let him adopt Thomas Young's proposal that three kinds of retinal fibre suffice for every colour, which looked confirmed when only three cone pigments were found in the primate retina in 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A sign is not a picture, so perception must be interpretation: we believe we see whatever object would, under ordinary conditions, have produced the image we are conscious of, which made him an empiricist against Ewald Hering, for whom spatial vision was given rather than learned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He named these acts unconscious judgements and expected resistance, a judgement being taken for the summit of conscious thought; single-cell recording has since found support for both men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is still usually named the chief exponent of unconscious inference, though the term was Wilhelm Wundt's in 1862, and one recent account of colour vision calls the phrase a grander word for ignorance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","germany"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Hermann von Helmholtz, <i>Popular Lectures on Scientific Subjects</i>, trans. E. Atkinson, with an introduction by John Tyndall (London: Longmans, Green, 1873), https://archive.org/stream/popularlectures00helmgoog/popularlectures00helmgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann L. F. Helmholtz, <i>On the Sensations of Tone as a Physiological Basis for the Theory of Music</i>, trans. Alexander J. Ellis, 3rd ed., chap. 6 (London: Longmans, Green, 1895), https://archive.org/stream/onsensationsofto00helmrich/onsensationsofto00helmrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Wade, “Helmholtz at 200,” <i>i-Perception</i> 12, no. 4 (2021): 20416695211022374, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8255614/. [Open access]","Claudia Cristalli, “Unconscious Inferences in Perception in Early Experimental Psychology: From Wundt to Peirce,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 58, no. 4 (2022): 432–48, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9796652/. [Open access]","Semir Zeki, “The Paton Prize Lecture 2021: A Colourful Experience Leading to a Reassessment of Colour Vision and Its Theories,” <i>Experimental Physiology</i> 107, no. 11 (2022): 1189–1208, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11514330/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/Hermann_von_Helmholtz_Litho.jpg","title":"Hermann von Helmholtz in 1857","desc":"Lithograph by Rudolf Hoffmann after a photograph by Schallenberg of Bonn, printed by J. Haller and published by George Andre Lenoir. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_von_Helmholtz_Litho.jpg","alt":"A half-length lithograph portrait of a man in his thirties with dark hair combed back and a moustache, wearing a dark coat, waistcoat and bow tie, above a facsimile of his signature"},"why":[{"q":"Why must perception be interpretation on his account?","a":"Because a nerve carries a sign rather than a picture, so we believe we see whatever object would, under ordinary conditions, have produced the image we are conscious of."},{"q":"Why did the telegraph metaphor let him adopt Young’s colour theory?","a":"Because if a nerve carries one current whatever is fixed to its end, three kinds of retinal fibre suffice for every colour — which looked confirmed when three cone pigments were found in 1964."},{"q":"Why did he expect resistance to unconscious judgements?","a":"Because a judgement was taken for the summit of conscious thought, so calling perceptual acts judgements made without awareness ran against what the word meant."}]},
{"id":"ps-035","num":35,"category":"Psychology","question":"Stimulating a frog's nerve at two measured distances from its muscle and timing the twitch gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as roughly 27 metres a second, far slower than electricity in a wire.","questions":["Myelin insulates an axon and lets the impulse leap between the nodes of Ranvier, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is very much higher in a sheathed fibre than in a bare one.","Axon diameter, the thickness of the myelin sheath and the spacing of the nodes of Ranvier together set <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in a bird's retina does not always favour the sheathed fibres."],"answer":"nerve conduction velocity","answerText":"nerve conduction velocity","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nerve conduction velocity</b> is the speed at which an impulse travels along a nerve fibre, and for most of the history of physiology it was held to be beyond measuring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Johannes Müller's textbook lists the guesses then in circulation, Haller at 9,000 feet a minute, Sauvages at 32,400 and another physiologist at 57,600 million feet a second, and observes that none of them rests on sound experimental principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own verdict was that we shall probably never attain the power of measuring the velocity of nervous action, there being no immense space across which to compare its propagation as there is with light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That pessimism was answered in the winter of 1849 to 1850, by stimulating a frog's nerve at measured distances from its muscle and reading the delay before contraction off a galvanometer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The impulse turned out to travel at roughly 27 metres a second, which settled a live argument, since a nerve carrying a purely electrical signal ought to have conducted like a metal wire, orders of magnitude faster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He was convinced by the result, and a nervous system that works at a readable speed is one whose workings can be timed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The modern figure depends entirely on the fibre, the fastest human ones, which carry the sense of where the limbs are, running at something like 80 to 120 metres a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What makes the difference is myelin: the sheath insulates the axon and is broken at the nodes of Ranvier, so that the impulse leaps from node to node instead of creeping along the membrane, gaining speed while spending less energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Axon diameter, the thickness of the sheath and the spacing of the nodes all enter, which makes the speed a property of the whole arrangement rather than of the nerve as such.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor does a sheath guarantee haste, since recordings in the bird retina find some myelinated axons slower than unmyelinated ones, and velocities across species that track their way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","biology"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"sources":["Johannes Müller, <i>Elements of Physiology</i>, trans. William Baly, bk. 3, sec. 3, https://archive.org/stream/Muller1838na12J-a/Muller1838na12J-a_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Maria Şerban, “Measurement under Uncertainty: Theory-Measurement Relations in Early Electrophysiological Research,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 47, no. 4 (2025): 55, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12669311/. [Open access]","Ning Zhang, Rulan Yi, Fuwang Zhong, Yali Lu, Wenjia Chen, Zhidan Ke, Yi Zhang, Liang Zhou, Pengyu Wang, and Wei Li, “Oligodendrocytes and Myelination: Pioneering New Frontiers in Cognitive Neuroscience,” <i>Frontiers in Neuroscience</i> 19 (2025): 1618468, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12319009/. [Open access]","Gerrit E. Gmel, Paul F. Vollebregt, Marjolein E. G. Thijssen, Rosana Santos Escapa, Eleanor McAlees, Dave Mugan, John L. Parker, and Charles H. Knowles, “Electrophysiological Responses in the Human S3 Nerve during Sacral Neuromodulation for Fecal Incontinence,” <i>Frontiers in Neuroscience</i> 15 (2021): 712168, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8545143/. [Open access]","Christoph T. Block, Malte T. Ahlers, Christian Puller, Max Manackin, Dipti R. Pradhan, and Martin Greschner, “Saltatory Axonal Conduction in the Avian Retina,” <i>Journal of Physiology</i> 603, no. 21 (2025): 6533–47, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12576306/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Helmholtz_pendulum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany%2C_1895-1910_Wellcome_L0057770.jpg","title":"Helmholtz pendulum","desc":"A pendulum myograph made by the Berliner Physikalische Werkstatten in Berlin between 1895 and 1910. The swing stimulates a nerve at a chosen distance from its muscle and records both the stimulus and the contraction on smoked glass. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmholtz_pendulum%2C_Berlin%2C_Germany%2C_1895-1910_Wellcome_L0057770.jpg","alt":"A dark cast-iron laboratory instrument on a heavy base, a rectangular frame carrying a curved metal arc through which a pivoted arm swings past small brass contact blocks, with cloth-covered wires at the top and a knurled brass weight at one corner"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the speed thought to be unmeasurable?","a":"Because there is no immense space across which to compare its propagation as there is with light, which is why Müller expected the power of measuring it never to be attained."},{"q":"Why did 27 metres a second settle a live argument?","a":"Because a nerve carrying a purely electrical signal ought to have conducted like a metal wire, orders of magnitude faster than that."},{"q":"Why is the speed a property of an arrangement rather than of a nerve?","a":"Because axon diameter, the thickness of the myelin sheath and the spacing of the nodes all enter — and some myelinated axons in the bird retina are slower than unmyelinated ones."}]},
{"id":"ps-036","num":36,"category":"Psychology","question":"Grinding the legs of a compass flat so that they would press rather than prick, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measured how far apart two touches must lie before the skin feels them as two.","questions":["Comparing lifted weights and drawn lines, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found that the smallest difference a person notices stands in a ratio to the standard rather than being a fixed amount.","The first clear case of a nerve whose action is to inhibit came from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his brother Eduard, who found in 1846 that stimulating the vagus slows the heart."],"answer":"Ernst Heinrich Weber","answerText":"Ernst Heinrich Weber","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Touch measured</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1829</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1846</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ernst Heinrich Weber</b> was an anatomist and physiologist at Leipzig, one of three scientific brothers credited by a younger contemporary with founding the mechanics of the circulation, the muscles, the joints and the ear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His own subject was the skin, the first of the senses to be put to methodical and measuring investigation, and his instrument was a compass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He ground its legs flat to a third of a Paris line across so that the ends would press rather than prick, pain being never as local as a firm touch, and closed it by degrees until two impressions began to be felt as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure was written down, confirmed on another day and the survey carried over the body part by part, the observer rested before fatigue could spoil it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even past that limit the patch still felt elongated, so someone reporting a single point could often still say which way the line joining the ends had run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He measured lifted weights and drawn lines the same way, hunting the smallest difference still noticed, and found that what gets noticed is a ratio rather than an amount.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> We take in the ratios of tones, he wrote, without knowing their vibration numbers, and the proportions of a building without measuring it in inches, and we compare weights the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fechner later built a logarithmic law on those measurements, and Ewald Hering, editing the work in 1905, held that Weber had been wrongly made co-responsible for it, his own view implying sensation directly proportional to magnitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With his brother Eduard he showed in 1846 that stimulating the vagus nerve slows the heart, the first clear case of a nerve whose office is to inhibit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The compass survives in the clinic and is a poor measure of tactile acuity, performance staying above chance even at zero separation, so an orientation judgement is recommended instead; the real limit is set by afferent density and the skin's own elasticity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","germany"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Ernst Heinrich Weber, <i>Tastsinn und Gemeingefühl</i>, ed. Ewald Hering, Ostwalds Klassiker der exakten Wissenschaften 149 (Leipzig: Wilhelm Engelmann, 1905), https://archive.org/stream/tastsinnundgemei00webe/tastsinnundgemei00webe_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann von Helmholtz, “On Thought in Medicine,” in <i>Popular Lectures on Scientific Subjects</i>, 2nd ser., trans. E. Atkinson (London: Longmans, Green, 1881), 199–236, https://archive.org/stream/lecturespopularo00helmrich/lecturespopularo00helmrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Beatrice Paradiso, Dainius H. Pauza, Clara Limback, Giulia Ottaviani, and Gaetano Thiene, “From Psychostasis to the Discovery of Cardiac Nerves: The Origins of the Modern Cardiac Neuromodulation Concept,” <i>Biology</i> 13, no. 4 (2024): 266, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11047897/. [Open access]","Jonathan Tong, Oliver Mao, and Daniel Goldreich, “Two-Point Orientation Discrimination versus the Traditional Two-Point Test for Tactile Spatial Acuity Assessment,” <i>Frontiers in Human Neuroscience</i> 7 (2013): 579, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3772339/. [Open access]","Davide Deflorio, Massimiliano Di Luca, and Alan M. Wing, “Skin Properties and Afferent Density in the Deterioration of Tactile Spatial Acuity with Age,” <i>Journal of Physiology</i> 601, no. 3 (2023): 517–33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10107475/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Ernst_Heinrich_Weber_Litho.jpg","title":"Ernst Heinrich Weber in 1856","desc":"Lithograph by Rudolf Hoffmann after a photograph by Schenk of Jena, from the Gallerie ausgezeichneter Naturforscher published in Vienna. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernst_Heinrich_Weber_Litho.jpg","alt":"A half-length lithograph portrait of an elderly man with long side-whiskers and swept-back hair, in a dark double-breasted coat over a high white stock, above a facsimile of his signature"},"why":[{"q":"Why were the compass legs ground flat?","a":"Because the ends had to press rather than prick, pain never being as local as a firm touch, so a sharp point would have measured something other than tactile resolution."},{"q":"Why is a ratio rather than an amount what gets noticed?","a":"Because we take in the ratios of tones without knowing their vibration numbers and the proportions of a building without measuring it in inches, and weights are compared the same way."},{"q":"Why is the two-point compass a poor clinical measure?","a":"Because performance stays above chance even at zero separation, so an orientation judgement is recommended instead, the real limit being afferent density and the skin’s elasticity."}]},
{"id":"ps-037","num":37,"category":"Psychology","question":"Setting out to find a functional relation between body and soul while writing a book on the hereafter, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded psychophysics and made sensation grow with the logarithm of the stimulus.","questions":["Writing satire under the pen name Dr Mises and arguing in 1848 that plants have souls, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nonetheless gave psychology its first workable way of putting a number to a sensation.","Asking people which shapes they preferred rather than deducing beauty from principles, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded an aesthetics from below and held that liking follows association more than form."],"answer":"Gustav Theodor Fechner","answerText":"Gustav Theodor Fechner","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1887</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Gustav Theodor Fechner</b>, born on 19 April 1801, was a professor at Leipzig who wrote satires and verse under the pen name Dr Mises before the work that made his name in psychology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He held throughout that body and soul are two modes of appearance of one and the same underlying being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1848 he gave that view its boldest form in <i>Nanna</i>, arguing that plants have a soul-life of their own, a book long set aside as mysticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The three volumes of <i>Zend-Avesta</i>, in 1851, carried it to the heavens and the hereafter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Psychophysics grew out of writing that book: what he wanted was a functional relation between the two modes of appearance, as physics had for the colour and intensity of light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He defined the new subject as an exact doctrine of the relations of dependence between body and soul, or more generally between the physical and the psychical world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It expressly stops at appearances and declines to reach behind them for the essence of body or soul in the metaphysician's sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The route was long — he compared himself to a man on a circle's rim walking the whole way round to reach something one step behind him — and ended in a formula: the magnitude of a sensation goes not with the stimulus but with its logarithm, the threshold taken as the unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He sent the idea to the physicist Wilhelm Weber in 1850 hoping it might be 'a happy one', and was told it was correct and acute but not yet happy, a happy idea being one that meets new facts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also founded an aesthetics from below, settling questions of taste by asking people rather than by deducing from principles, and held that liking is governed less by an object's form than by what a life has associated with it — the formal half of which has fared worse, since people rating triangles do not peak at the golden ratio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","philosophy","germany"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Gustav Theodor Fechner, <i>Elemente der Psychophysik</i>, vol. 1 (Leipzig: Breitkopf und Härtel, 1860), https://archive.org/stream/elementederpsych001fech/elementederpsych001fech_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Gustav Theodor Fechner, <i>Elemente der Psychophysik</i>, vol. 2 (Leipzig: Breitkopf und Härtel, 1860), https://archive.org/stream/elementederpsych02fech/elementederpsych02fech_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Gustav Theodor Fechner, <i>On Life after Death</i>, trans. Hugo Wernekke (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle and Rivington, 1882), https://archive.org/stream/onlifeafterdeath00fechuoft/onlifeafterdeath00fechuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Giulia Parovel, “G. T. Fechner (1848): Plants as Sentient Living Beings,” <i>Plant Signaling and Behavior</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): 2632571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12928611/. [Open access]","Stefan A. Ortlieb, Werner A. Kügel, and Claus-Christian Carbon, “Fechner (1866): The Aesthetic Association Principle — A Commented Translation,” <i>i-Perception</i> 11, no. 3 (2020): 2041669520920309, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7264472/. [Open access]","Jay Friedenberg, “Aesthetic Judgment of Triangular Shape: Compactness and Not the Golden Ratio Determines Perceived Attractiveness,” <i>i-Perception</i> 3, no. 3 (2012): 163–75, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3485848/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Title_page_%22Vergleichende_Anatomie_der_Engel%22%2C_Fechner_1825_Wellcome_L0015342.jpg","title":"Comparative Anatomy of Angels, 1825","desc":"The title page of Vergleichende Anatomie der Engel, a sketch published at Leipzig under Fechner's pen name Dr Mises. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Title_page_%22Vergleichende_Anatomie_der_Engel%22%2C_Fechner_1825_Wellcome_L0015342.jpg","alt":"A foxed letterpress title page in blackletter reading Vergleichende Anatomie der Engel, eine Skizze von Dr. Mises, with a former owner's inked signature down the right margin and the imprint Leipzig 1825 at the foot"},"why":[{"q":"Why did psychophysics grow out of a book about the heavens?","a":"Because he held body and soul to be two modes of appearance of one being, and wanted a functional relation between them as physics had for the colour and intensity of light."},{"q":"Why does the new subject stop at appearances?","a":"Because it is an exact doctrine of the relations of dependence between the physical and the psychical, and expressly declines to reach behind them for the essence of body or soul."},{"q":"Why was Weber’s verdict that the idea was not yet happy?","a":"Because a happy idea is one that meets new facts, and he judged it correct and acute without yet having done that."}]},
{"id":"ps-038","num":38,"category":"Psychology","question":"Setting out to show that no fundamental difference divides the mental faculties of man and the higher mammals, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the difference in mind one of degree and not of kind.","questions":["Circulating printed queries to observers abroad in 1867 and asking for observation rather than memory, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tried to settle whether the same face means the same feeling everywhere.","Predicting in 1859 that psychology would rest on the gradual acquirement of every mental power, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent his last two decades on books about mind, emotion and expression."],"answer":"Charles Darwin","answerText":"Charles Darwin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Origin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descent of Man</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expression</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1872</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Charles Darwin</b> ended the <i>Origin of Species</i> in 1859 by predicting that psychology would rest on a new foundation, the acquirement of each mental power by gradation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made the case twelve years later in the <i>Descent of Man</i>, one chapter of which had, he said, no object but to show that no fundamental difference divides the mental faculties of man and the higher mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conclusion is the sentence the book is remembered by: the difference in mind between man and the higher animals, great as it is, is one of degree and not of kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The evidence was gathered instance by instance — a chimpanzee cracking a fruit with a stone, a monkey taught to open palm-nuts that later used stones unprompted — with curiosity, memory, imitation and reason traced through animals in incipient form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next year he gave expression a book of its own, written in four months between the proofs of the <i>Descent</i> and the sixth edition of the <i>Origin</i>, and illustrated with photographs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> To find whether expressions are inherited he circulated printed queries in 1867 among observers abroad, asking that observation and not memory be trusted, and had 36 replies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His reasoning was that a movement expressing one emotion across distinct human groups is likely innate, since a gesture acquired in early life would differ from group to group as languages do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same continuity argument was also used to arrange human groups on a scale, the book speaking of gradations between what it calls 'the highest men of the highest races' and 'the lowest savages'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The method did not survive: a case built from anecdotes drew the answering rule that no action may be read as the work of a higher mental faculty if a lower one will explain it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The question he set did survive, and a recent review finds emotional expressions largely universal in their production, with cultural nuances differing between spontaneous and posed ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","evolution"],"difficulty":1,"sources":["Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i> (London: John Murray, 1859), 488, https://archive.org/stream/onoriginofspecie1859darw/onoriginofspecie1859darw_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex</i>, vol. 1, chaps. 2–3 (New York: D. Appleton, 1871), https://archive.org/stream/descentofmansel01darw/descentofmansel01darw_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals</i>, chap. 1 (London: John Murray, 1872), https://archive.org/stream/bub_gb_NWAJAAAAQAAJ/bub_gb_NWAJAAAAQAAJ_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Louise Barrett, “Introduction to the Special Collection: The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 4 (2022): e57, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10426026/. [Open access]","Domenica Bruni, Pietro Perconti, and Alessio Plebe, “Anti-Anthropomorphism and Its Limits,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 2205, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6249301/. [Open access]","Adèle Gallant, Marie-Pier Mazerolle, Annalie Pelot, and Annie Roy-Charland, “Cultural Differences in the Production of Emotional Facial Expressions: A Review,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 17 (2026): 1699374, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12907163/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Expression_of_the_Emotions_Plate_I.png","title":"Plate I from The Expression of the Emotions","desc":"Photographs of crying and screaming children, reproduced in the chapter on suffering and weeping. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expression_of_the_Emotions_Plate_I.png","alt":"Six numbered oval photographs arranged in three rows, each showing a crying or screaming infant or small child, one of them held in the arms of an older child"},"why":[{"q":"Why did he think a shared expression pointed to inheritance?","a":"Because a gesture acquired in early life would differ from group to group as languages do, so one found across distinct human groups is likely innate."},{"q":"Why did the method not survive?","a":"Because the case was built from anecdotes, which drew the answering rule that no action may be read as the work of a higher mental faculty if a lower one will explain it."},{"q":"Why is the continuity argument not innocent?","a":"Because the same reasoning was used to arrange human groups on a scale, the book speaking of gradations between the highest men of the highest races and the lowest savages."}]},
{"id":"ps-039","num":39,"category":"Psychology","question":"Defining life as the continuous adjustment of internal relations to external relations four years before the <i>Origin</i> appeared, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the mind a product of evolution.","questions":["Because experiences were thought to accumulate across a race rather than a lifetime, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could treat an innate structure as inherited experience and hand each side of the nativist quarrel half the victory.","Darwin borrowed the phrase 'survival of the fittest' from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and later editions of the <i>Origin</i> say psychology will rest on a foundation he had already laid."],"answer":"Herbert Spencer","answerText":"Herbert Spencer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Principles</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1855</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Herbert Spencer</b> brought out a <i>Principles of Psychology</i> in 1855, four years before the <i>Origin of Species</i>, and it set out an account of mind as a product of evolution before Darwin had published one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its governing formula is a definition of life as the continuous adjustment of internal relations to external relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mind is the highest reach of that adjustment, so a mental process is to be understood by asking what it fits its owner for rather than what it is made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instinct is handled as compound reflex action, one rung on a continuous ladder running from the simplest nervous response up to reasoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mechanism was inheritance: experiences accumulate not within a single life but across a race, since individuals transmit the constitutions they receive along with whatever their own habits have altered in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Innate structure therefore became inherited experience, which settled the quarrel between empiricism and nativism by awarding each side half of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darwin took the phrase 'survival of the fittest' from him and called it more accurate than his own natural selection, and where the <i>Origin</i> had first said psychology would rest on a new foundation, its later editions said the foundation was already well laid by him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The programme reached further than psychology proper, since recapitulation and a psychological Lamarckism carried it into psychoanalysis, where inherited unconscious memories rested on the same principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All of it depended on the inheritance of acquired characteristics, which biological work between the wars disproved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What outlived the mechanism was the question it taught psychology to ask, which is what a mental process is for, while the habit-and-heredity thread is now cautiously compared with epigenetic inheritance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","philosophy","science","evolution"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Herbert Spencer, <i>The Principles of Psychology</i> (London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, 1855), https://archive.org/stream/b21965377/b21965377_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i>, 6th ed. (London: John Murray, 1876), https://archive.org/stream/onoriginspecies00dallgoog/onoriginspecies00dallgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Charles Darwin, <i>On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection</i> (London: John Murray, 1859), 488, https://archive.org/stream/onoriginofspecie1859darw/onoriginofspecie1859darw_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Geoffrey Marcaggi and Fabian Guénolé, “Freudarwin: Evolutionary Thinking as a Root of Psychoanalysis,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 9 (2018): 892, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6018481/. [Open access]","Mariagrazia Portera and Mauro Mandrioli, “Who’s Afraid of Epigenetics? Habits, Instincts, and Charles Darwin’s Evolutionary Theory,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 43, no. 1 (2021): 20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7875938/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Herbert_Spencer_at_46.jpg","title":"Herbert Spencer at 46","desc":"A vignetted photographic portrait of 1866, reproduced in the Life and Letters of Herbert Spencer. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herbert_Spencer_at_46.jpg","alt":"A vignetted profile portrait of a balding man with long side-whiskers and curling hair at the back, in a dark coat and bow tie, facing right, captioned in script beneath"},"why":[{"q":"Why does his definition of life decide how a mental process is studied?","a":"Because mind is the highest reach of the continuous adjustment of internal to external relations, so a process is understood by asking what it fits its owner for rather than what it is made of."},{"q":"Why did his mechanism settle the empiricism-nativism quarrel?","a":"Because innate structure became inherited experience: experiences accumulate across a race rather than within a life, awarding each side half of the argument."},{"q":"Why did the programme collapse and the question survive?","a":"Because all of it depended on the inheritance of acquired characteristics, disproved between the wars, while the question of what a mental process is for outlived the mechanism."}]},
{"id":"ps-040","num":40,"category":"Psychology","question":"Counting eminent relatives and taking reputation for a fair test of ability, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that a highly gifted race of men could be bred by judicious marriages over several generations.","questions":["Looking for a way to weigh nature and nurture in just scales, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned to the life-history of twins and gave psychology a method it has used ever since.","Setting parents against children to see how ability descends, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived at regression and at the correlation that grew out of it, both now standard everywhere."],"answer":"Francis Galton","answerText":"Francis Galton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eugenics coined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1883</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regression</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Francis Galton</b> was a London polymath and one of the first researchers into human heredity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <i>Hereditary Genius</i>, in 1869, opens by proposing to show that a man's natural abilities are derived by inheritance under exactly the same limitations as physical features, so that a highly gifted race of men could be produced by judicious marriages over several generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence was a count of eminent relatives, reputation being taken as a fair test of high ability, beginning with the judges of England from 1660.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He saw the objection that social advantage might explain the pattern, and tried to meet it by setting the sons of eminent men against the adopted nephews of popes, who had the advantages without the descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wanting to weigh nature and nurture in just scales, he turned to the life-history of twins, the first use of twins as a method for that question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He coined the word eugenics in 1883 for the science of improving stock, defining it to take in every influence tending to give the more suitable races or strains of blood a better chance of prevailing speedily over the less suitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The statistics came out of the same work, since setting parents against children gave him regression, the finding that the stature of adult offspring is on the whole more mediocre than that of their parents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Correlation grew from that, and both are now everywhere, regression in particular as a routine trap in medicine, where an extreme measurement moves back towards the middle on being repeated whether or not anything was done to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The programme he named outlived him as an organised movement, whose statistical wing was known as the biometricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reached the statute book: an Act of 1913 in Britain allowed those deemed feeble-minded to be institutionalised indefinitely, and a Supreme Court ruling of 1927 in the United States upheld enforced eugenic sterilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","research methods","evolution"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Francis Galton, <i>Hereditary Genius: An Inquiry into Its Laws and Consequences</i> (London: Macmillan, 1869), https://archive.org/stream/hereditarygenius1869galt/hereditarygenius1869galt_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Francis Galton, <i>Inquiries into Human Faculty and Its Development</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1908), https://archive.org/stream/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Francis Galton, <i>Natural Inheritance</i> (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://archive.org/stream/naturalinherita00galt/naturalinherita00galt_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Alex Aylward, Daniel J. Fairbanks, Maria Kiladi, and Gregory Radick, “Eugenics and the Genetics Society,” <i>Heredity</i> 135, no. 5 (2026): 301–12, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13219441/. [Open access]","Kaj S. Emanuel, Jari Dahmen, Inger N. Sierevelt, Mats Brittberg, and Gino M. M. J. Kerkhoffs, “Regression to the Mean: Statistical Bias Can Mislead Interpretation in Cartilage and Osteoarthritis Clinics and Research,” <i>Cartilage</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 405–8, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11558649/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Francis_Galton_1850s.jpg","title":"Francis Galton in middle life","desc":"A photographic portrait scanned from Karl Pearson's Life, Letters and Labours of Francis Galton, where it is dated to his middle life. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francis_Galton_1850s.jpg","alt":"A seated three-quarter-length photographic portrait of a balding man with side-whiskers in a dark frock coat over a patterned waistcoat, hands folded over a paper in his lap, in a carved chair"},"why":[{"q":"Why did he set popes’ adopted nephews against eminent men’s sons?","a":"Because he saw that social advantage might explain his pattern, and the nephews had the advantages without the descent."},{"q":"Why did the statistics come out of the heredity work?","a":"Because setting parents against children gave him regression — adult offspring being on the whole more mediocre in stature than their parents — and correlation grew from that."},{"q":"Why is regression a routine trap in medicine?","a":"Because an extreme measurement moves back towards the middle on being repeated whether or not anything was done to it."}]},
{"id":"ps-041","num":41,"category":"Psychology","question":"Squeezing a dynamometer, naming colours against the clock and bisecting a 50 cm line were among the ten tasks proposed in 1890 as the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of experimental psychology.","questions":["Galton had asked that any such measure be checked against an independent estimate of a person's powers, and eleven years later class marks at Columbia showed the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> predicting nothing.","Because acuity and speed seemed easier to measure than judgement, the earliest form of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded reaction times and skin sensitivity rather than what a person could reason out."],"answer":"mental test","answerText":"mental test","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1901</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>mental test</b> is a short standardised task whose result is recorded and set beside other people's, and the phrase was coined by James McKeen Cattell in a paper of 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Psychology, he opened, cannot attain the certainty and exactness of the physical sciences unless it rests on a foundation of experiment and measurement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten were proposed, among them pressure on a dynamometer, the two-point threshold, the least noticeable difference in weight, reaction time for sound, time for naming colours, bisecting a 50 cm line, judging ten seconds, and letters remembered on one hearing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind it lay the anthropometric laboratory Francis Galton had called for, where a man might get himself and his children weighed, measured and their bodily faculties tested by the best methods known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He appended remarks to the paper, and the first of them named the step the programme never took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The measures, he wrote, should be compared with an independent estimate of the man's powers, so that we might learn which of them are the most instructive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eleven years passed before anyone did it, and then Clark Wissler, a doctoral student at Columbia, set the laboratory tests against the students' own class marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He found that the tests showed little intercorrelation, that the physical measures correlated among themselves but hardly with them, and that class marks correlated well with each other and not with the tests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His verdict was carefully scoped: the tests seemed to have some value with children in the lower schools but told us nothing about the general worth of college students or adults, and he refused to conclude that testing was therefore a waste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ten tests have not come back, but a positive association between mental speed and reasoning is consistently reported, and in one recent study it came out of medium size, larger for the more complex speed tasks than the simplest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["J. McKeen Cattell, “Mental Tests and Measurements,” <i>Mind</i> 15, no. 59 (1890): 373–81, https://archive.org/stream/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Francis Galton, “Remarks,” appended to J. McKeen Cattell, “Mental Tests and Measurements,” <i>Mind</i> 15, no. 59 (1890): 380–81, https://archive.org/stream/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59/sim_mind_1890-07_15_59_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Clark Wissler, <i>The Correlation of Mental and Physical Tests</i> (New York: Columbia University, 1901), https://archive.org/stream/correlationment00wissgoog/correlationment00wissgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Natalie Borter, Katja Schlegel, and Stefan J. Troche, “How Speededness of a Reasoning Test and the Complexity of Mental Speed Tasks Influence the Relation between Mental Speed and Reasoning Ability,” <i>Journal of Intelligence</i> 11, no. 5 (2023): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10219451/. [Open access]","Francis Galton, <i>Inquiries into Human Faculty and Its Development</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1908), https://archive.org/stream/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft/inquiriesintohum00galtuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/K._Pearson%2C_The_Life%2C_Letters_and_Labours_Wellcome_L0032610.jpg","title":"Handbill for Galton's Anthropometric Laboratory","desc":"The laboratory's own printed notice, reproduced in Karl Pearson's Life, Letters and Labours of Francis Galton. Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K._Pearson%2C_The_Life%2C_Letters_and_Labours_Wellcome_L0032610.jpg","alt":"A printed handbill on orange paper headed ANTHROPOMETRIC LABORATORY, for the measurement in various ways of Human Form and Faculty, listing four numbered purposes and charging threepence or fourpence for the principal measurements"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the step Cattell named the one his programme never took?","a":"Because he wrote that the measures should be compared with an independent estimate of a man’s powers so we might learn which are instructive, and eleven years passed before anyone did it."},{"q":"Why did Wissler’s comparison undermine the tests?","a":"Because the tests showed little intercorrelation and class marks correlated well with each other and hardly at all with them, so the laboratory measures were not tracking what the marks tracked."},{"q":"Why did he refuse the broader conclusion?","a":"Because his verdict was scoped: the tests seemed to have some value with children in the lower schools, and he would not conclude that testing generally was a waste."}]},
{"id":"ps-042","num":42,"category":"Psychology","question":"Family studies of inherited feeble-mindedness and army testing on a scale of nearly two million men gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the numbers it wanted from the new science of psychology.","questions":["Indiana in 1907 and California in 1909 passed compulsory sterilisation laws, and the psychological test soon gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a way of deciding who belonged on the list.","When the author of the most influential immigrant testing study withdrew it in 1930 as without foundation, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost the evidence that psychology had appeared to give it."],"answer":"eugenics","answerText":"eugenics","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First US law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1907, Indiana</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Army testing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1917–19</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrawn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Eugenics</b> is the attempt to improve a population by controlling who has children, and in the decades after 1900 psychology supplied it with the instrument it had lacked, a test returning a number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Henry Goddard's <i>Kallikak Family</i> of 1912 traced two lines of descent from one man and called the result a natural experiment in which nobody could doubt that true heredity was at work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own text concedes that we do not know feeble-mindedness to be a unit character and that there are many reasons for thinking it cannot be, and assumes it anyway to simplify the illustration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The book sold widely and was taken seriously, and the legislation had begun before it: Indiana passed the first compulsory sterilisation law in 1907 and California followed in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over the 20th century the health authorities of California alone recommended sterilisation for more than 20,000 people held in state institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The instrument arrived at scale during the First World War, when 1,726,966 men were given a psychological examination before the end of January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reading those results, Carl Brigham concluded in 1923 that the average intelligence of immigrants was declining, and that the newer arrivals were intellectually inferior to the older ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own figures showed the score rising with years of residence in the United States, and he redrew that curve as a fall in the quality of successive arrivals rather than as a sign that the test measured familiarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seven years later he published a review holding that comparative studies of national and racial groups may not be made with existing tests, and that one of the most pretentious of them, his own, was without foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His reason was the one the data had shown all along, that a test in a language may be given only to people with equal opportunity to have acquired that language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","history","science","research methods"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Henry Herbert Goddard, <i>The Kallikak Family: A Study in the Heredity of Feeble-Mindedness</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1912), https://archive.org/stream/kallikakfamilya00goddgoog/kallikakfamilya00goddgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Robert M. Yerkes, ed., <i>Psychological Examining in the United States Army</i>, Memoirs of the National Academy of Sciences 15 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1921), https://archive.org/stream/psychologicalexa00yerkuoft/psychologicalexa00yerkuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Carl C. Brigham, <i>A Study of American Intelligence</i> (Princeton, NJ: Princeton University Press, 1923), https://archive.org/stream/studyofamericani00briguoft/studyofamericani00briguoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Carl C. Brigham, “Intelligence Tests of Immigrant Groups,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 37, no. 2 (1930): 158–65, https://archive.org/stream/sim_psychological-review_1930-03_37_2/sim_psychological-review_1930-03_37_2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Ingrid Grenon and Joav Merrick, “Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities: Eugenics,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 2 (2014): 201, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4202623/. [Open access]","Marie Kaniecki, Nicole L. Novak, Sarah Gao, Natalie Lira, Toni Ann Treviño, Kate O’Connor, and Alexandra Minna Stern, “Racialization and Reproduction: Asian Immigrants and California’s Twentieth-Century Eugenic Sterilization Program,” <i>Social Forces</i> 102, no. 2 (2023): 706–29, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10569381/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did psychology matter to the movement?","a":"Because it supplied the instrument the movement had lacked, a test returning a number, given at scale to 1,726,966 men before the end of January 1919."},{"q":"Why was Brigham’s reading of the army data wrong on its own figures?","a":"Because the score rose with years of residence in the United States, and he redrew that curve as a fall in the quality of successive arrivals rather than as familiarity with the language."},{"q":"Why did he later retract it?","a":"Because a test in a language may be given only to people with equal opportunity to have acquired that language, which is what his own data had shown all along."}]},
{"id":"ps-043","num":43,"category":"Psychology","question":"The German physician <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened his 1845 textbook by asking which organ must be diseased wherever there is madness, and answered that it could only be the brain.","questions":["Against the psychicists, who read insanity as godless disobedience, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> led the German somaticists in treating mental disorder as a bodily condition open to ordinary medical study.","Though remembered for the doctrine that mental illness is brain disease, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also named anger, fright and sorrow among the commonest immediate causes of insanity."],"answer":"Wilhelm Griesinger","answerText":"Wilhelm Griesinger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1817</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Textbook</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1845, 2nd edition 1861</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wilhelm Griesinger</b> was a German physician whose 1845 textbook <i>Die Pathologie und Therapie der psychischen Krankheiten</i> made the somatic view of madness, the doctrine that mental illness is bodily illness, the working assumption of German psychiatry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its opening section asks which organ must necessarily and invariably be diseased where there is madness, and answers that physiological and pathological facts show this organ can only be the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The claim was polemical, since German psychiatry had for decades been led by the psychicists, who read insanity in moral terms as the fruit of disobedient and godless behaviour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against them the somaticists held that mental disorder arises from bodily conditions, and is therefore open to the ordinary anatomical and clinical study medicine gives any other disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was no pure materialist, naming anger, fright and sorrow among the commonest immediate causes of insanity, and the doctrine had a practical edge in his advocacy of humane asylum care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> General paralysis of the insane, a fatal dementia with slurred speech and grandiose delusions, seemed to settle the argument, accounting for up to 20% of male asylum admissions around 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1913 Hideyo Noguchi and J. W. Moore demonstrated the syphilis spirochaete <i>Treponema pallidum</i> in the brains of paretic patients at a New York state hospital, giving a psychiatric syndrome a named cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A treatment followed, and the malaria fever therapy introduced in 1917 took the 1927 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for the therapeutic value of inoculating such patients with malaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Both halves have since been qualified, penicillin having displaced fever therapy from the 1940s, while a recent study of the asylum records found outcomes far poorer than the published claims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The larger promise remains outstanding, since psychiatric diagnosis still rests on clinical symptoms rather than on any bodily sign, and biological markers are as yet little integrated into care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","biology","history","germany"],"difficulty":4,"sources":["Lukas Bergner, Hubertus Himmerich, Kenneth C. Kirkby, and Holger Steinberg, “Descriptions of Disordered Eating in German Psychiatric Textbooks, 1803–2017,” <i>Frontiers in Psychiatry</i> 11 (2020): 504157, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.504157. [Open access]","Robert C. Bransfield, Charlotte Mao, and Rosalie Greenberg, “Microbes and Mental Illness: Past, Present, and Future,” <i>Healthcare</i> 12, no. 1 (2023): 83, https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare12010083. [Open access]","Wilhelm Griesinger, <i>Mental Pathology and Therapeutics</i>, trans. C. Lockhart Robertson and James Rutherford (London: New Sydenham Society, 1867), bk. 1, ch. 1, §1, https://archive.org/details/mentalpathologyt00grieuoft. [Open access]","Alison Clayton, “Malaria Therapy for Neurosyphilis at Mont Park Hospital for the Insane in Australia, 1927–1928,” <i>History of Psychiatry</i> 37, no. 2 (2026): 135–58, https://doi.org/10.1177/0957154x251403921. [Open access]","Hideyo Noguchi and J. W. Moore, “A Demonstration of Treponema Pallidum in the Brain in Cases of General Paralysis,” <i>Journal of Experimental Medicine</i> 17, no. 2 (1913): 232–38, https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.17.2.232. [Open access]","“Julius Wagner-Jauregg – Facts,” NobelPrize.org, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1927/wagner-jauregg/facts/. [Open access]","Itziar María Béjar-Botello, Sara Jiménez-Fernández, Gloria Pérez-Guerrero, Blanca Iglesias-Rosado, Luis Gutiérrez-Rojas, Jesús Herrera-Imbroda, and Inmaculada Romera, “Prognostic Biomarkers and Precision Psychiatry: A Review of the Available Evidence,” <i>Biomedicines</i> 14, no. 3 (2026): 558, https://doi.org/10.3390/biomedicines14030558. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Wilhelm_Griesinger%2C_001.jpg","title":"Wilhelm Griesinger","desc":"A lithograph portrait by M. Wiese, reproduced in Beschreibung des Bürger-Hospitals zu Stuttgart, published at Stuttgart in 1859. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelm_Griesinger%2C_001.jpg","alt":"A three-quarter-length lithograph portrait of a bearded man in his thirties, his hair combed back, wearing a dark coat and light waistcoat with a watch chain and a knotted black cravat, one hand resting at his hip"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the somatic claim polemical rather than obvious?","a":"Because German psychiatry had for decades been led by the psychicists, who read insanity in moral terms as the fruit of disobedient and godless behaviour."},{"q":"Why did general paralysis of the insane seem to settle the argument?","a":"Because it accounted for up to 20 per cent of male asylum admissions around 1900 and was then given a named cause, the syphilis spirochaete demonstrated in paretic brains in 1913."},{"q":"Why is the larger promise still outstanding?","a":"Because psychiatric diagnosis still rests on clinical symptoms rather than on any bodily sign, with biological markers as yet little integrated into care."}]},
{"id":"ps-044","num":44,"category":"Psychology","question":"The universal fluid of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was supposed to flow freely again once a patient, seated in a ring round an oak tub and holding an iron rod, had been brought to a crisis.","questions":["A blindfolded boy in a Paris orchard fainted while embracing an ordinary tree, which the royal commissioners of 1784 took as proof that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the work of imagination.","Convulsions came from imagination without the fluid and nothing came from the fluid without imagination, said the 1784 report that killed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as physics without killing its effects."],"answer":"animal magnetism","answerText":"animal magnetism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mesmer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1734–1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Paris practice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1778</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal commission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1784</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Animal magnetism</b> was the doctrine, advanced from the 1770s by the Viennese physician Franz Anton Mesmer, that a subtle invisible fluid fills the universe and that illness follows when its flow through the body is blocked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His 1766 Vienna dissertation had argued that the planets act on the human frame as the moon acts on the tides, and the fluid was that idea brought indoors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He began with magnets, then found them dispensable and treated by passes of the hands, bringing on a crisis of convulsions, laughter or sleep that was taken for the cure at work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Driven from Vienna in 1778 after his treatment of the blind pianist Maria Theresia von Paradis collapsed, he set up in Paris, where patients sat in a ring round an oak tub called the baquet, each holding an iron rod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vogue was enormous, and in March 1784 the king named a commission of four Paris physicians and five academicians, Benjamin Franklin and Lavoisier among them, to test whether the fluid existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The commissioners saw that the question was not whether patients had crises but whether the fluid produced them, and so arranged for people not to know when they were being magnetised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A woman known to be sensitive was blindfolded and magnetised, and her sensations no longer answered to the parts at which the operator worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In an orchard at Passy a boy of twelve, chosen by Mesmer's pupil Deslon, was blindfolded and led to embrace four ordinary trees while a magnetised fifth stood at a distance, and at the fourth he fainted into a crisis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The report concluded that imagination without magnetism produces convulsions and magnetism without imagination produces nothing, and that the existence of the fluid is absolutely destitute of proof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fluid is gone and the effects were not, so that explanation survives in the study of suggestion and expectancy, while the practice ran on through artificial somnambulism into hypnosis, a name dating from Braid's <i>Neurypnology</i> of 1843.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","history","research methods"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Benjamin Franklin et al., <i>Report of Dr. Benjamin Franklin, and Other Commissioners, Charged by the King of France, with the Examination of the Animal Magnetism, as Now Practised at Paris</i> (London: J. Johnson, 1785), 19, 23–24, 56–57, 67–68, 105–6, https://archive.org/details/b22393857. [Open access]","Gavriel Knafo and Joel Weinberger, “Exploring the Role of Conscious and Unconscious Processes in Hypnosis: A Theoretical Review,” <i>Brain Sciences</i> 14, no. 4 (2024): 374, https://doi.org/10.3390/brainsci14040374. [Open access]","Curtis E. Margo and Lynn E. Harman, “The Intriguing Blindness of Maria Theresia von Paradis and the Instructive Potential of Medical Biography,” <i>Cureus</i> 17, no. 2 (2025): e78948, https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.78948. [Open access]","James Kennaway, “Musical Hypnosis: Sound and Selfhood from Mesmerism to Brainwashing,” <i>Social History of Medicine</i> 25, no. 2 (2011): 271–89, https://doi.org/10.1093/shm/hkr143. [Open access]","Burkhard Peter, “Hypnosis in Psychotherapy, Psychosomatics and Medicine: A Brief Overview,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 15 (2024): 1377900, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1377900. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Animal_magnetism%2C_1814.jpg","title":"The operator putting his patient into a crisis","desc":"An engraving from E. Sibly, A Key to Physic and the Occult Sciences, published in 1814, with the fluid drawn as dotted arcs streaming from the operator's hands. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Animal_magnetism,_1814.jpg","alt":"A stipple engraving of a man in an 18th-century coat and breeches standing over a seated woman in a long gown, one hand raised and one held out towards her, with three dotted arcs curving through the air from his hands to her face and body"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the commissioners not test whether patients had crises?","a":"Because that was not the question: what was at issue was whether the fluid produced them, so they arranged for people not to know when they were being magnetised."},{"q":"Why is the orchard trial the decisive one?","a":"Because a blindfolded boy embraced four ordinary trees while the magnetised fifth stood at a distance, and he fainted into a crisis at the fourth."},{"q":"Why did the effects outlive the doctrine?","a":"Because imagination without magnetism produced convulsions and magnetism without imagination produced nothing, so what was real passed into the study of suggestion and expectancy."}]},
{"id":"ps-045","num":45,"category":"Psychology","question":"Chains and purges gave way at the York Retreat to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a regime of routine, occupation and kindly authority that worked on whatever self-command a patient had left.","questions":["Because the desire of esteem was thought to govern the insane more powerfully than fear, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> required attendants to address patients as far as possible as rational beings.","The state asylums built across America from the 1840s promised cure through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and filled instead with poor patients whom admission law kept inside long after treatment had given way to containment."],"answer":"moral treatment","answerText":"moral treatment","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">York Retreat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tuke's account</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1813</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Asylum era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 1800s – 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Moral treatment</b> was the regime, adopted at the end of the 18th century, that set out to cure madness by kindness, routine, occupation and whatever self-control the patient had left, in place of chains, purges and terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Retreat, a Quaker house near York that took its first patients in 1796, is its clearest statement, and Samuel Tuke's account of 1813 says that neither chains nor corporal punishment were tolerated there on any pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its psychology is the interesting part: most of the insane were held to keep a considerable degree of self-command, strengthened by treating them as far as possible as rational beings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fear was allowed a place and a small one, since the desire of esteem was thought to work more powerfully, and patients were seen to restrain themselves before strangers merely passing through the house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philippe Pinel developed the <i>traitement moral</i> in Paris from 1793, and the regime crossed to America with Benjamin Rush, Dorothea Dix and Thomas Story Kirkbride, who built recreation, schooling and work into the asylum day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its promise was curability, and the state asylums raised on it from the 1840s, among them the New York State Lunatic Asylum at Utica in 1843, were meant to be places of rest, refuge and cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Demand outran them at once, since Utica admitted 276 patients in its first year and had to be enlarged, while admission and discharge law kept poor patients inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Small therapeutic establishments became large overcrowded ones that all but abandoned treatment for containment, the wealthy going to quieter private asylums and the poor to custodial care at the lowest possible cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The founding story has itself been qualified, since the striking of the chains at Bicêtre is a myth attached to Pinel, who acted hesitantly where his colleague Jean-Baptiste Pussin drove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Historians of policy count it the first of four American cycles of reform, each promising that early treatment would prevent lasting disability and none of the first three delivering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["practice","psychology","science","history","institution"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["Samuel Tuke, <i>Description of the Retreat, an Institution near York, for Insane Persons of the Society of Friends</i> (York: W. Alexander, 1813), ch. 5, esp. 141, 157–58, https://archive.org/details/b33520240. [Open access]","Yiran Vanessa Zhang, “Utica State Hospital: Psychiatric Reform, Institutionalization, and Patient Justice in 19th Century America and Today,” <i>Yale Journal of Biology and Medicine</i> 98, no. 3 (2025): 349–55, https://doi.org/10.59249/bias7665. [Open access]","Preethy George, Nev Jones, Howard Goldman, and Abram Rosenblatt, “Cycles of Reform in the History of Psychosis Treatment in the United States,” <i>SSM – Mental Health</i> 3 (2023): 100205, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssmmh.2023.100205. [Open access]","Heinz-Peter Schmiedebach, “Historische Anmerkungen zur Ethik in der Psychiatrie: Herausforderungen im 19. Jahrhundert,” <i>Der Nervenarzt</i> 95, no. 7 (2024): 641–45, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00115-024-01657-x. [Open access]","Christopher Holligan, “Educative Psychological Treatment at Edinburgh's Royal Asylum: Unfolding <i>The Morningside Mirror</i>, 1845–1882,” <i>Social History of Medicine</i> 39, no. 1 (2026): 1–23, https://doi.org/10.1093/shm/hkae022. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Engraving_of_the_original_Retreat_near_York_instituted_1792.jpg","title":"The original building of the Retreat, York","desc":"A wood engraving after a painting by Cave, from Daniel Hack Tuke's Reform in the Treatment of the Insane, the Retreat's centenary volume. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Engraving_of_the_original_Retreat_near_York_instituted_1792.jpg","alt":"A wood engraving of a plain three-storey house with lower wings, standing among trees above a stream, with a woman in a bonnet, a laden donkey and two other figures on the path in front of it, captioned Original Building of the Retreat, York, Instituted 1792"},"why":[{"q":"Why did treating patients as rational beings count as treatment?","a":"Because most of the insane were held to keep a considerable degree of self-command, which was thought to be strengthened by being treated as far as possible as rational."},{"q":"Why was the desire of esteem preferred to fear?","a":"Because it was thought to work more powerfully, patients being seen to restrain themselves before strangers merely passing through the house."},{"q":"Why did the asylums built on the regime abandon it?","a":"Because demand outran them at once — Utica admitted 276 patients in its first year — and admission and discharge law kept poor patients inside, so treatment gave way to containment."}]},
{"id":"ps-046","num":46,"category":"Psychology","question":"A handbook of 1873 and 1874 announced a borderline science between physiology and psychology, and its author <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened rooms for a laboratory at Leipzig five years later.","questions":["A long study of language, myth and custom stands beside the experimental handbook, because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that the higher mental processes lay beyond the reach of the laboratory altogether.","Because only one volume of his handbook was ever translated, English-speaking psychology inherited the experimentalism of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and almost none of the philosophy that went with it."],"answer":"Wilhelm Wundt","answerText":"Wilhelm Wundt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Handbook</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1873–74, 5th edition 1902–3</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leipzig rooms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wilhelm Wundt</b> was the German physiologist and philosopher whose handbook of 1873 and 1874, the <i>Grundzüge der physiologischen Psychologie</i>, is usually taken to mark the birth of experimental psychology as a discipline of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The book announced a borderline science standing between physiology and psychology, one taking the points of contact between inner and outer life as its subject, which neither older discipline could sidestep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its method was experiment, since a fleeting conscious process cannot be held fast by attention while the observer is also its object, and pure self-observation can under no circumstances claim accuracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His readers took him for the man who gathered what Weber, Fechner, Helmholtz and Lotze had done separately into one centre, and persuaded both scientists and philosophers to accept the new science.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rooms for a laboratory were set apart at Leipzig in 1879 and his institute drew students from around the world, which turned a programme on paper into a discipline with a workforce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The programme was narrower than its reputation, since he held the experimental method to reach the sensory side of mental life and to fail beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The higher processes went to <i>Völkerpsychologie</i>, the study of language, myth and custom as products of the common mental life, which he called the chief source of knowledge about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only one volume of the handbook was ever translated into English, so the part carrying his philosophy never reached the readers who took his name for their founder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What crossed the Atlantic was the experimentalism without the rest, passed on through his student Titchener and fixed by Edwin Boring's history, which opens on the line that before him there had been psychology enough but no psychologists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Historians treat that portrait as a construction rather than a record, so the textbook founder and the man who put the higher processes outside the laboratory are not the same person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","history","germany"],"difficulty":2,"sources":["Wilhelm Wundt, <i>Principles of Physiological Psychology</i>, trans. Edward Bradford Titchener (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1904), introduction, §§1–2, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Wundt/Physio/intro.htm. [Open access]","Robert H. Wozniak, “Introduction to <i>Grundzüge der physiologischen Psychologie</i>, Wilhelm Wundt (1874),” in <i>Classics in Psychology, 1855–1914: Historical Essays</i> (Bristol: Thoemmes Press, 1999), https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Wundt/Physio/wozniak.htm. [Open access]","James McKeen Cattell, “The Psychological Laboratory at Leipsic,” <i>Mind</i> 13 (1888): 37–51, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Cattell/leipsic.htm. [Open access]","Jeremy Trevelyan Burman, “Meaning-Change Through the Mistaken Mirror: On the Indeterminacy of ‘Wundt’ and ‘Piaget’ in Translation,” <i>Review of General Psychology</i> 26, no. 1 (2022): 22–48, https://doi.org/10.1177/10892680211017521. [Open access]","Claudia Cristalli, “Unconscious Inferences in Perception in Early Experimental Psychology: From Wundt to Peirce,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 58, no. 4 (2022): 432–48, https://doi.org/10.1002/jhbs.22211. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Erstausgaben_f%C3%BCr_Wikipedia_IV_035.jpg","title":"Völkerpsychologie, volume 1, 1900","desc":"The title page of the first volume of Wundt's Völkerpsychologie, subtitled an investigation of the developmental laws of language, myth and custom, published at Leipzig by Wilhelm Engelmann in 1900. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erstausgaben_f%C3%BCr_Wikipedia_IV_035.jpg","alt":"A letterpress title page reading Völkerpsychologie, Eine Untersuchung der Entwicklungsgesetze von Sprache, Mythus und Sitte, von Wilhelm Wundt, Erster Band, Die Sprache, Erster Theil, with the imprint Leipzig, Verlag von Wilhelm Engelmann, 1900 at the foot"},"why":[{"q":"Why did his new science have to be experimental?","a":"Because a fleeting conscious process cannot be held fast by attention while the observer is also its object, so pure self-observation can under no circumstances claim accuracy."},{"q":"Why was his programme narrower than his reputation?","a":"Because he held the experimental method to reach the sensory side of mental life and fail beyond it, sending the higher processes to Völkerpsychologie instead."},{"q":"Why did English-speaking readers get a different founder?","a":"Because only one volume of the handbook was ever translated, so the philosophy never reached them and what crossed was the experimentalism, fixed by Boring’s history."}]},
{"id":"ps-047","num":47,"category":"Psychology","question":"Nearly half the research done at the Leipzig laboratory was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the measurement of how long a mental operation takes, and its students carried the method home to laboratories of their own.","questions":["Subtracting the time to answer one stimulus from the time to choose between two was the founding trick of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its founding difficulty, since inserting a stage may change the others.","Roughly 3,800 papers a year still turn on simple and choice reaction times, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remains a cornerstone binding psychology to the techniques of neuroscience."],"answer":"mental chronometry","answerText":"mental chronometry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Donders</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leipzig rooms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1879</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Journal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Philosophische Studien, from 1882</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mental chronometry</b> is the measurement of how long mental operations take, and it was the characteristic work of the psychological laboratory Wilhelm Wundt opened at Leipzig in 1879.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rooms were set apart in the university's Convict building with a yearly grant for apparatus, and by 1888 the laboratory filled four of them with two more about to be added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nineteen students were carrying on original research in a single semester, from everywhere but England, and the example was already being copied at Berlin, Bonn, Göttingen, Johns Hopkins, Harvard, Pennsylvania, Princeton, Cambridge and Copenhagen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wundt founded a journal to carry the results, the <i>Philosophische Studien</i>, which had issued fifteen parts since 1882, and the work in it fell under four heads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those were the measurement of sensation, the duration of mental processes, the time-sense, and attention, memory and the association of ideas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nearly half of everything done there belonged to the second head, since only experiment can hold a fleeting process still enough to be observed, and a process that takes time can be timed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The method came from Donders, who in 1868 subtracted the time to answer a single unvarying stimulus from the time to answer one of two, and called the difference the time to discriminate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He saw the trouble himself, writing that the course taken was not irreproachable, since a man who enters a room by two different doors is unlikely to leave by a third in the same interval both times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The assumption that inserting a stage leaves the others untouched has been argued over ever since, and cognitive neuroscience has had to ask whether brain activity is separable in the way the subtraction requires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method outlived the doubts, since roughly 3,800 papers a year still turn on simple and choice reaction times, and the technique remains a cornerstone binding psychology to neuroscience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","research methods","history"],"difficulty":4,"sources":["James McKeen Cattell, “The Psychological Laboratory at Leipsic,” <i>Mind</i> 13 (1888): 37–51, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Cattell/leipsic.htm. [Open access]","Michael I. Posner, “Timing the Brain: Mental Chronometry as a Tool in Neuroscience,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 3, no. 2 (2005): e51, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0030051. [Open access]","David M. Alexander, Chris Trengove, and Cees van Leeuwen, “Donders Is Dead: Cortical Traveling Waves and the Limits of Mental Chronometry in Cognitive Neuroscience,” <i>Cognitive Processing</i> 16, no. 4 (2015): 365–75, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10339-015-0662-4. [Open access]","José M. Medina, Willy Wong, José A. Díaz, and Hans Colonius, “Advances in Modern Mental Chronometry,” <i>Frontiers in Human Neuroscience</i> 9 (2015): 256, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2015.00256. [Open access]","Wilhelm Wundt, <i>Principles of Physiological Psychology</i>, trans. Edward Bradford Titchener (London: Swan Sonnenschein, 1904), introduction, §2, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Wundt/Physio/intro.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Binet_-_Introduction_%C3%A0_la_psychologie_exp%C3%A9rimentale%2C_Fig_9.png","title":"The Hipp chronoscope","desc":"A diagram of the Hipp chronoscope, the clock used to time reactions, from Alfred Binet's Introduction à la psychologie expérimentale of 1894. Public domain, via the Bibliothèque nationale de France and Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Binet_-_Introduction_%C3%A0_la_psychologie_exp%C3%A9rimentale%2C_Fig_9.png","alt":"A line diagram of a clockwork instrument with two dials on a rack of gearing at the right, wired through a commutator, a rheostat, two batteries and a pair of contact keys at the left, each part lettered"},"why":[{"q":"Why did nearly half the Leipzig work go to timing processes?","a":"Because only experiment can hold a fleeting process still enough to be observed, and a process that takes time can be timed."},{"q":"Why did Donders doubt his own subtraction?","a":"Because the course taken was not irreproachable: a man who enters a room by two different doors is unlikely to leave by a third in the same interval both times."},{"q":"Why has the method outlived the doubt?","a":"Because roughly 3,800 papers a year still turn on simple and choice reaction times, and the technique remains a cornerstone binding psychology to neuroscience."}]},
{"id":"ps-048","num":48,"category":"Psychology","question":"Biology has a morphology, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the school that claimed the same office for psychology, asking what is there in consciousness and in what quantity rather than what it is there for.","questions":["Sensation and affection were the only elements <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would admit, each nothing at all apart from attributes like quality, intensity and duration, any one of which at zero abolishes it.","Because trained observers never came to agree about what they found in their own consciousness, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not outlive the man who named it, though the laboratory it worked in did."],"answer":"structuralism","answerText":"structuralism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Titchener</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867–1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manifesto</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reply to Watson</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1914</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Structuralism</b> was the school that set experimental psychology the task of analysing consciousness into its elements, and it took its programme from a comparison with biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As biology divides into morphology, physiology and ontogeny, so Edward Titchener held that experimental psychology was the morphology of mind, ravelling the elemental processes out of the tangle of consciousness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its worker, he wrote, tries to discover first of all what is there and in what quantity, not what it is there for, his task being a vivisection that yields structural rather than functional results.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The elements came out at two, sensation and affection, with the idea a sub-species of the first, and after twenty years of minute analysis he could report no trace of a mental krypton or argon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An element is nothing apart from its constitutive attributes, quality and intensity and duration among them, and if any one of those takes the value zero the sensation ceases to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The method was trained introspection, and the distinction he drew is exact: from the structural standpoint introspection observes an Is, from the functional standpoint an Is-for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Unschooled introspection slides irresistibly into the second, reporting what a thing is for thought or for conduct rather than the mental material itself, which is why observers had to be schooled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rival camp was functionalism, and which of the two mattered, the parts or the working whole, was the dominant question as psychology was separating itself from philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then behaviourism denied there was anything there to observe, and Titchener answered in 1914 that the methods of science are in the last resort observational and its problems in the last resort analytical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The school did not outlive him, because the training never produced agreement between observers, and psychology kept the laboratory while giving up the mental anatomy it had been built to do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["theory","psychology","science","history"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["E. B. Titchener, “The Postulates of a Structural Psychology,” <i>Philosophical Review</i> 7 (1898): 449–65, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/structuralism.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “Structural and Functional Psychology,” <i>Philosophical Review</i> 8 (1899): 290–99, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/strucfunc.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “On ‘Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,’” <i>Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society</i> 53 (1914): 1–17, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/watson.htm. [Open access]","Lisa Kampen, “Pragmatism as Idealism? The Case of Mary Whiton Calkins,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 61, no. 3 (2025): e70028, https://doi.org/10.1002/jhbs.70028. [Open access]","OpenStax, “1.2 History of Psychology,” in <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: Rice University, 2020), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the school model itself on morphology?","a":"Because it took its task to be finding what is there and in what quantity rather than what it is there for, a vivisection yielding structural rather than functional results."},{"q":"Why did observers have to be schooled in introspection?","a":"Because unschooled introspection slides irresistibly into reporting what a thing is for thought or conduct — an Is-for — rather than the mental material itself."},{"q":"Why did the school not outlive its founder?","a":"Because the training never produced agreement between observers, so psychology kept the laboratory while giving up the mental anatomy it had been built to do."}]},
{"id":"ps-049","num":49,"category":"Psychology","question":"The Cornell laboratory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had rooms named optics, acoustics, haptics and reaction, one for each avenue by which a sensation might enter and be taken apart.","questions":["Wundt reached America chiefly through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who carried over the experimental half of the programme and left the philosophy that had gone with it behind.","Told by the behaviourists that there was nothing there to observe, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered in 1914 that the methods of science are in the last resort observational."],"answer":"Edward Titchener","answerText":"Edward Titchener","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cornell laboratory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 1891</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Edward Titchener</b> was an Englishman who studied under Wundt at Leipzig and then spent his career at Cornell, where he led the American school of structuralism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He held that the method of every science can be summed up in a single word as observation, and that the sciences differ not in their subject matter, which is human experience, but in the point of view they take on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His laboratory at Cornell opened in 1891 with a suite of six rooms and by 1898 had ten, named for the work done in them: optics, acoustics, haptics, reaction, taste and smell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The floor plan is the doctrine in architecture, a room for each avenue by which a sensation might enter and be taken apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American laboratory, he wrote, must serve instruction as well as research, so every student from the third undergraduate year was handled in the laboratory rather than in a lecture room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That is why so much of his working life went into manuals of laboratory practice and into training observers, since the method could produce nothing until there were people schooled to use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He worked at the method itself as well, holding in 1912 that the word introspection had grown equivocal and reserving it for procedures that were scientifically available and had actually been employed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the behaviourists said there was nothing there to observe, he answered in 1914 that the methods of science are in the last resort observational and its problems in the last resort analytical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was the point through which Wundt reached America, and what he carried over was the experimental half of the programme rather than the philosophy that had gone with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He died in 1927 and the school did not outlast him, leaving behind the laboratory teaching he had built and very little of the mental anatomy it had been built to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["person","psychology","science","history"],"difficulty":3,"sources":["E. Bradford Titchener, “A Psychological Laboratory,” <i>Mind</i> 7 (1898): 311–31, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/lab.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “The Postulates of a Structural Psychology,” <i>Philosophical Review</i> 7 (1898): 449–65, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/structuralism.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “The Schema of Introspection,” <i>American Journal of Psychology</i> 23 (1912): 485–508, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/introspection.htm. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “On ‘Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It,’” <i>Proceedings of the American Philosophical Society</i> 53 (1914): 1–17, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/watson.htm. [Open access]","Lisa Kampen, “Pragmatism as Idealism? The Case of Mary Whiton Calkins,” <i>Journal of the History of the Behavioral Sciences</i> 61, no. 3 (2025): e70028, https://doi.org/10.1002/jhbs.70028. [Open access]","OpenStax, “1.2 History of Psychology,” in <i>Psychology 2e</i> (Houston: Rice University, 2020), https://openstax.org/books/psychology-2e/pages/1-2-history-of-psychology. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Edward_Bradford_Titchener.jpg","title":"Edward Bradford Titchener","desc":"A studio portrait photograph dated to about 1890, photographer unknown. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Bradford_Titchener.jpg","alt":"A head-and-shoulders studio photograph of a young man in pince-nez spectacles, with a full moustache and pointed beard and his hair combed back, wearing a checked jacket over a waistcoat and a broad pale cravat"},"why":[{"q":"Why did his laboratory have a room for each sense?","a":"Because his doctrine was that consciousness is analysed into its elements, so the floor plan gave a room to each avenue by which a sensation might enter and be taken apart."},{"q":"Why did so much of his working life go into manuals and training?","a":"Because the method could produce nothing until there were people schooled to use it, and every student from the third undergraduate year was handled in the laboratory rather than a lecture room."},{"q":"Why did the school not outlast him?","a":"Because what he carried over from Wundt was the experimental half of the programme without the philosophy, and psychology kept the laboratory teaching while dropping the mental anatomy."}]},
{"id":"ps-050","num":50,"category":"Psychology","question":"Würzburg's observers reported states of mind with no picture, sound or feeling in them, and whether such <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exists was the question two laboratories could not settle between them.","questions":["Titchener answered the Würzburg claim by reporting his own mind as a picture gallery, since on his account <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a thing that could not exist at all.","Two laboratories with trained observers and controlled tasks disagreed about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and neither had any procedure by which it could convict the other of error."],"answer":"imageless thought","answerText":"imageless thought","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Würzburg work</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1901</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Titchener's reply</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1909</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Washburn's address</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1921</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Imageless thought</b> is thinking that carries no sensory content at all, and whether any such thing exists was the question that broke the first psychology of consciousness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The claim came from Oswald Külpe's laboratory at Würzburg, where observers set simple tasks of judgment reported states they could not describe as any image, sound or feeling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marbe, Watt, Ach and Messer named these states <i>Bewusstseinslagen</i>, conscious attitudes, and Binet's observer said of one that it was something vague, too difficult to describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cornell answered that the reports were incomplete, and that a properly trained observer would find the imagery the Würzburg observers had overlooked or failed to catch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titchener would not hedge: he did not at all believe the thought-element existed, and reported his own mind as a fairly complete picture gallery, not of finished paintings but of impressionist notes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both laboratories used trained observers, controlled tasks and the same method, and reached opposite answers, with no procedure that either could use to convict the other of error.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He set about repairing the method instead, holding by 1912 that the word introspection had grown equivocal and reserving it for procedures that were scientifically available and had actually been used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where a report describes a purely inner process with no external stimulus behind it, Margaret Floy Washburn told the American Psychological Association in 1921, there is no guarantee of any common factor at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is the realm, she said, where introspection is most vulnerable, and the failure to agree left the method open to attack from behaviourists and psychoanalysts alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The episode is still cited as the standard case against first-person method, though the argument now turns less on whether it failed than on what would count as doing it properly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["concept","psychology","science","history","research methods"],"difficulty":4,"sources":["Edward Bradford Titchener, <i>Lectures on the Experimental Psychology of the Thought-Processes</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), lectures 3–5, https://archive.org/details/lecturesonexperi00titc. [Open access]","E. B. Titchener, “The Schema of Introspection,” <i>American Journal of Psychology</i> 23 (1912): 485–508, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Titchener/introspection.htm. [Open access]","Margaret Floy Washburn, “Introspection as an Objective Method,” <i>Psychological Review</i> 29 (1922): 89–112, https://psychclassics.yorku.ca/Washburn/. [Open access]","Russell T. Hurlburt, Ben Alderson-Day, Charles Fernyhough, and Simone Kühn, “Can Inner Experience Be Apprehended in High Fidelity? Examining Brain Activation and Experience from Multiple Perspectives,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 8 (2017): 43, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2017.00043. [Open access]","Jaakko Belt, “Phenomenological Skepticism Reconsidered: A Husserlian Answer to Dennett's Challenge,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 11 (2020): 2058, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.02058. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://archive.org/download/lecturesonexperi00titc/page/n6_large.jpg","title":"Lectures on the Experimental Psychology of the Thought-Processes, 1909","desc":"The title page of the book in which Titchener answered the Würzburg claim, published by Macmillan in 1909. Public domain, scanned by the Internet Archive.","credit":"https://archive.org/details/lecturesonexperi00titc","alt":"A letterpress title page on browned paper reading Lectures on the Experimental Psychology of the Thought-Processes, by Edward Bradford Titchener, with the imprint New York, The Macmillan Company, 1909 at the foot"},"why":[{"q":"Why could the two laboratories not settle the dispute?","a":"Because both used trained observers, controlled tasks and the same method and reached opposite answers, with no procedure either could use to convict the other of error."},{"q":"Why is a purely inner report the most vulnerable case?","a":"Because where a report describes an inner process with no external stimulus behind it, as Washburn told the APA in 1921, there is no guarantee of any common factor at all."},{"q":"Why did the episode damage more than the two laboratories?","a":"Because the failure to agree left introspection open to attack from behaviourists and psychoanalysts alike, and it is still cited as the standard case against first-person method."}]},
{"id":"gw-001","num":1,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"India","answerText":"India","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1947</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"India"},"facts":[["Capital","New Delhi"],["Population","1.46B (2025)"],["Largest city","Mumbai"],["Area","3,287,263 km² (1,269,219 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Flag_of_India.svg","credit":"Government of India, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_India.svg)","alt":"The flag of India: three horizontal bands of saffron, white and green, with a navy-blue wheel of 24 spokes at the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","india"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>India</b> occupies the largest peninsula in the world, reaching south from the wall of the Himalayas into the ocean between the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 7,600 km (4,700 miles) of coast face south, the Arabian Sea on one side of the peninsula and the Bay of Bengal on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land falls into three parts: peninsular India south of the Vindhya and Satpura ranges, the alluvial plains of the Indus and the Ganges, and the mountains themselves, with the Lakshadweep, Andaman and Nicobar islands offshore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate is the monsoon in classic form, the surface winds reversing completely between January and July so that the warm, wet south-westerly of June to September brings 70 to 95 per cent of the year's rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rainfall is therefore wildly uneven, from under 150 mm (6 inches) a year in the Rajasthan desert to about 2,500 mm (98 inches) on the west coast and more than 10,000 mm (390 inches) on the Khasi hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> British rule over the subcontinent was argued over for a generation and then wound up in a matter of months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Congress Party under M. K. Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru demanded a free and united country, while the Muslim League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah mobilised Muslim voters behind the demand for a separate one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gandhi's Quit India movement, the cost of the Second World War and the naval mutiny of 1946 convinced Britain that the country could no longer be ruled, and a report of 16 May 1946 proposed an interim government to draft a constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That interim government was allowed to deal with other states before sovereignty passed, which is why the country took its United Nations seat on 30 October 1945, nearly two years early.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The India Independence Act of 18 July 1947 then divided British India on religious lines, and though the new republic declared itself secular, some 35 million Muslims remained in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — India,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0394en/CA0394EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “India,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/india. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: India,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/india. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Indian Independence: What Led to Partition in 1947?,” Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/indian-independence/. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “India,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/in.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg/1920px-Taj_Mahal_%28Edited%29.jpeg","title":"Taj Mahal, India","desc":"Taj Mahal.","credit":"Yann ; edited by Jim Carter, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taj_Mahal_(Edited).jpeg","alt":"A view of Taj Mahal."}},
{"id":"gw-002","num":2,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"China","answerText":"China","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1911</span><span class=\"dt-k\">PRC founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"China"},"facts":[["Capital","Beijing"],["Population","1.42B (2025)"],["Largest city","Shanghai"],["Area","9,600,000 km² (3,706,600 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fa/Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg","credit":"Design by Zeng Liansong, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_People%27s_Republic_of_China.svg)","alt":"The flag of China: a red field with one large gold star in the upper hoist corner and four smaller gold stars curving beside it."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>China</b> occupies the south-east of the Eurasian landmass, with the Pacific along its eastern side, the Himalaya rising to its south and the dry interior of Asia stretching away to its west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country tilts from one side to the other: average altitude falls from over 4,000 m (13,100 feet) in the west to less than 100 m (330 feet) along the eastern seaboard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third of it is mountain and a quarter plateau, and only about an eighth is plain, which is where the farmland and the great cities are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The East Asian monsoon waters the east and the south while mountains keep it out of the north-west, so a mean annual rainfall of 645 mm (25 inches) hides extremes above 2,000 mm (79 inches) on the south-east coast and under 25 mm (1 inch) in the Tarim basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 50,000 rivers drain basins of over 100 km² (39 sq mi); those reaching the sea, the Yangtze and the Yellow among them, cover about 65 per cent of the territory and the rest never reaches an ocean at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For sixty years foreign merchants were confined to the single port of Guangzhou under a system that kept them at arm's length from the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Through the 19th century the Qing dynasty met one incursion after another: two Opium Wars cost it Hong Kong, forced open the treaty ports and gave foreign powers extraterritorial rule in the major cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Defeat by Japan in 1894 and 1895 cost more territory still, including Taiwan and part of Manchuria, and ended Chinese suzerainty over Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Revolutionaries in the south overthrew the Qing in 1911, ending an imperial system more than two thousand years old, and the Communist Party, founded in Shanghai in 1921, took power after the civil war that followed the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which government held the country's seat at the United Nations was settled only in 1971, when the General Assembly voted to restore the lawful rights of the People's Republic there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — China,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/CA0221EN/ca0221en.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: China,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Chinese Revolution of 1911,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/chinese-rev. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Chinese Revolution of 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/chinese-rev. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Restoration of the Lawful Rights of the People's Republic of China in the United Nations,” resolution 2758, 26th session, 25 October 1971, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192054. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg/1920px-The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg","title":"Great Wall of China, China","desc":"Great Wall of China.","credit":"Severin.stalder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Wall_of_China_at_Jinshanling-edit.jpg","alt":"A view of Great Wall of China."}},
{"id":"gw-003","num":3,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"United States","answerText":"United States","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 4 July 1776</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1783, Treaty of Paris</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United States of America"},"facts":[["Capital","Washington, D.C."],["Population","347M (2025)"],["Largest city","New York City"],["Area","9,833,517 km² (3,796,742 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a4/Flag_of_the_United_States.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States.svg)","alt":"The flag of the United States: thirteen alternating red and white stripes, with fifty small white stars on a blue rectangle in the upper hoist corner."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","north america","united states"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>United States</b> began as thirteen British colonies whose Continental Congress appointed a committee of five on 10 June 1776 to draft a statement of independence for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It included John Adams, Benjamin Franklin, Robert R. Livingston and Roger Sherman, but the writing was delegated to Thomas Jefferson, who drafted the text between 11 and 28 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress spent the whole of 3 July and most of the following day revising it before adopting it, and delegates began signing the engrossed parchment on 2 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Declaring independence and securing it were different things: in 1782 Franklin rejected British overtures for a settlement that would have left the thirteen states with some autonomy inside the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Formal negotiations opened on 27 September 1782 and two months of hard bargaining produced preliminary articles in which Britain accepted American independence and boundaries and agreed to evacuate its forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Those articles also settled fishing rights on the Newfoundland banks and prewar debts owed to British creditors, and promised restitution of property lost by Americans loyal to the British cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Franklin had refused throughout to make a separate peace apart from France, which had been the American side's staunch ally through the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The new country then grew westward by purchase and by war: in 1803 it bought 530 million acres of North America from France for 15 million dollars, territory Spain had held since 1762 which made up all or part of fifteen modern states between the Mississippi and the Rocky Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under President James K. Polk the annexation of Texas in 1845, the Oregon Treaty with Britain in 1846 and the Mexican-American War, closed by the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo in 1848, delivered the future states of Texas, California, Nevada, New Mexico, Arizona, Utah, Washington and Oregon, with parts of five more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations dates the country's membership from 24 October 1945, the day the organisation itself came into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United States of America,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/us.html. [Open access]","National Archives and Records Administration, “Declaration of Independence (1776),” Milestone Documents, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/declaration-of-independence. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Treaty of Paris, 1783,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1776-1783/treaty. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Louisiana Purchase, 1803,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/louisiana-purchase. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Annexation of Texas, the Mexican-American War, and the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo, 1845–1848,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/texas-annexation. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Statue_of_Liberty%2C_New_York_Harbor%2C_USA-13Nov2011.jpg/1920px-Statue_of_Liberty%2C_New_York_Harbor%2C_USA-13Nov2011.jpg","title":"the Statue of Liberty, the United States","desc":"The Statue of Liberty in New York Harbor.","credit":"Mike Durkin from Forest Hills, NY, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Liberty,_New_York_Harbor,_USA-13Nov2011.jpg","alt":"A green copper statue holding a torch on an island in a harbour."}},
{"id":"gw-004","num":4,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Indonesia","answerText":"Indonesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Indonesia"},"facts":[["Capital","Jakarta"],["Population","286M (2025)"],["Largest city","Jakarta"],["Area","1,910,931 km² (737,815 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Flag_of_Indonesia.svg","credit":"Drawn by Gabbe, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Indonesia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Indonesia: two equal horizontal bands, red above white."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","indonesia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Indonesia</b> is a tropical archipelago of some 17,500 islands strung along the equator between the Indian and Pacific oceans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its coastline runs to more than 54,000 km (33,600 miles), longer than the circumference of the earth and second in the world only to Canada's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sumatra, Java, Nusa Tenggara, Kalimantan, Sulawesi, Maluku and Papua are the main islands, and most carry a volcanic range along their whole length, some of it still active and rising above 5,000 m (16,400 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is wet and tropical, with a dry season from March to August under Australian air masses and a wet one from September to March under Asian and Pacific air that has crossed the ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages about 2,700 mm (106 inches) a year, from 1,300 mm (51 inches) in East Nusa Tenggara to 4,300 mm (169 inches) in parts of Papua, and the temperature stays between 21 and 33 °C (70 and 91 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The archipelago was ruled from the Netherlands for centuries before it was a country of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dutch sovereignty ended in 1949, after a war of independence and the work of a United Nations commission, and Sukarno led the new republic, which took its own seat at the United Nations on 28 September 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That independence belonged to a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence from European rulers between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In April 1955 Indonesia hosted the meeting that gave the movement a voice, when 29 Asian and African governments gathered at Bandung to discuss decolonisation, economic development and the place of the Third World in the Cold War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It co-sponsored the conference with Burma, India, Pakistan and Sri Lanka, and the principles declared there were self-determination, mutual respect for sovereignty, non-aggression, non-interference and equality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Indonesia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0393en/CA0393EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Indonesia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/indonesia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Bandung Conference (Asian-African Conference), 1955,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/bandung-conf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Indonesia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/id.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Pradaksina.jpg/1920px-Pradaksina.jpg","title":"Borobudur, Indonesia","desc":"Borobudur.","credit":"Heri nugroho, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pradaksina.jpg","alt":"A view of Borobudur."}},
{"id":"gw-005","num":5,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Pakistan","answerText":"Pakistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1947</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Pakistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Islamabad"],["Population","255M (2025)"],["Largest city","Karachi"],["Area","796,095 km² (307,374 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/Flag_of_Pakistan.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:Zscout370, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pakistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Pakistan: a dark green field bearing a white crescent and five-pointed star, with a vertical white band at the hoist."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","pakistan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pakistan</b> reaches from the Arabian Sea in the south to the Karakoram in the north, standing where the north-western corner of the Indian subcontinent meets the mountain systems of Central Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its four provinces — Punjab, Sindh, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa and Balochistan — straddle two tectonic plates, the first two sitting on the north-western corner of the Indian plate and the others on the Eurasian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the mountains and the sea lie plains, deserts, hills and plateaux, with the plain of the Indus running down the middle and lakes across the fertile east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the country is semi-arid or arid: June is the hottest month on the plains, at over 38 °C (100 °F), against a mean minimum near 4 °C (39 °F) in midwinter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 494 mm (19 inches) a year, from under 100 mm (4 inches) in parts of Balochistan and Sindh to over 1,500 mm (59 inches) in the northern foothills, and above 5,000 m (16,400 feet) snowfall is the largest single store of water in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> British India was partitioned on religious lines in 1947, Pakistan for Muslims and India for Hindus, after the Muslim League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah mobilised Muslim voters behind the demand for a state of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Cripps Mission of 1942 had already aired the idea that regions might secede from the union, which lent the demand weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sir Cyril Radcliffe was given six weeks to draw the border, and because Muslims were concentrated at both ends of British India the new state came into being in two halves a thousand miles apart, with the rest of the subcontinent between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jinnah became governor general of the dominion on 15 August 1947, and it took its seat at the United Nations six weeks later, on 30 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The eastern half seceded in 1971 to become Bangladesh, leaving the country in the shape it has now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Pakistan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0403en/CA0403EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Pakistan,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/pakistan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Pakistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/pakistan. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Indian Independence: What Led to Partition in 1947?,” Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/indian-independence/. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Pakistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pk.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Badshahi_Mosque_front_picture.jpg/1920px-Badshahi_Mosque_front_picture.jpg","title":"Badshahi Mosque, Pakistan","desc":"Badshahi Mosque.","credit":"Romero Maia, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badshahi_Mosque_front_picture.jpg","alt":"A view of Badshahi Mosque."}},
{"id":"gw-006","num":6,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Nigeria","answerText":"Nigeria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nigeria"},"facts":[["Capital","Abuja"],["Population","238M (2025)"],["Largest city","Lagos"],["Area","923,768 km² (356,669 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Flag_of_Nigeria.svg","credit":"Design by Michael Taiwo Akinkunmi, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nigeria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Nigeria: three equal vertical bands, green, white and green."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","nigeria"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nigeria</b> lies in the tropical zone of West Africa, its southern edge on the Gulf of Guinea and its northern reaching up towards the Sahel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its coast is low-lying, a belt of lagoons and sandy beaches behind which stand thick mangrove and dense rainforest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inland the cover thins through the Guinea savannah of the middle belt to the Sudan savannah of the north and to near-desert in the north-eastern corner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A plateau of extinct volcanoes rises in the centre of the country and a mountainous zone runs the length of its eastern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rain averages about 1,150 mm (45 inches) a year, but the wet season shortens from nine months or more in the south to two or three in the north, where the total can fall to 400 mm (16 inches), and the mean air temperature is 26.6 °C (80 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The territory was under British sovereignty before it was Nigerian, and the federation became independent on 1 October 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first prime minister was Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, and the country took its seat at the United Nations six days later, on 7 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That year belonged to a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence from European rulers between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was no single pattern to it: some transfers were peaceful and orderly and others followed a protracted revolution, and the arrival of so many new members changed the composition of the United Nations itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nigeria is now a federal republic of 36 states and a capital territory, and it is by far the most populous country in Africa, holding more than a seventh of the continent's people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Nigeria,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9807en/I9807EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Nigeria,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/nigeria. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nigeria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nigeria. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Nigeria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ng.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Zuma_Rock.jpg","title":"Zuma Rock, Nigeria","desc":"Zuma Rock.","credit":"Jeff Attaway, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuma_Rock.jpg","alt":"A view of Zuma Rock."}},
{"id":"gw-503","num":503,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Washington, D.C.","answerText":"Washington, D.C.","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Residence Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Government moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1800</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United States of America","dot":"Washington, D.C."},"facts":[["Country","United States"],["Population","5.30M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","22nd largest"],["Population growth","+14.6% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","united states"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Washington, D.C.</b> was planned as a capital before it existed as a city: Congress passed a Residence Act, and President George Washington chose the ground for it from land ceded by Maryland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He appointed three commissioners to survey the site and oversee the design of the capital and its buildings, and they hired the French engineer Pierre Charles L'Enfant to plan the new city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> L'Enfant put the Capitol at the elevated east end of the Mall, on a rise then called Jenkins' Hill, which he described as a pedestal waiting for a monument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was dismissed the following year, having refused to produce any drawings for the building itself — he claimed to carry the design in his head — and having declined the commissioners' authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A competition suggested by Thomas Jefferson drew seventeen unsatisfactory plans before a late entry from William Thornton was accepted, and Washington selected the design in 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The government arrived before its buildings were ready: Congress, the Supreme Court and the Library of Congress moved into a Capitol whose north wing alone had been made habitable, some rooms still unfinished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Department of State came the same year from Philadelphia, first to the Treasury building and then to one of the Six Buildings, moving four times in the city's first sixteen months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The President's House was begun a year after the site was chosen, to a design by the Irish-born architect James Hoban, and John Adams and his wife Abigail moved into it still unfinished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> British troops set fire to the Capitol on 24 August 1814 and only a sudden rainstorm prevented its complete destruction; Hoban was appointed to rebuild the President's House, and James Monroe moved back in three years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Capitol still stands on its plateau 27 metres (88 feet) above the Potomac at the eastern end of the National Mall, grown with the country to more than 140,000 square metres (1.5 million square feet) and over 600 rooms, and the city has been the country's capital throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United States of America,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/us.html. [Open access]","Architect of the Capitol, “History of the U.S. Capitol Building,” https://www.aoc.gov/explore-capitol-campus/buildings-grounds/capitol-building/history. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Buildings of the Department of State,” Department History, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/buildings. [Open access]","The White House, “The White House,” About the White House, https://www.whitehouse.gov/about-the-white-house/the-white-house/. [Open access]","Architect of the Capitol, “U.S. Capitol Building,” Explore the Capitol Campus, https://www.aoc.gov/explore-capitol-campus/buildings-grounds/capitol-building. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Washington%2C_D.C._-_2007_aerial_view.jpg/1920px-Washington%2C_D.C._-_2007_aerial_view.jpg","title":"Washington, D.C.","desc":"Washington, D.C., seen from a distance.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington,_D.C._-_2007_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Washington, D.C.."}},
{"id":"gw-007","num":7,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Brazil","answerText":"Brazil","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 March 1824</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Brazil"},"facts":[["Capital","Brasília"],["Population","213M (2025)"],["Largest city","São Paulo"],["Area","8,515,767 km² (3,287,956 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Brazil.svg","credit":"Design by Raimundo Teixeira Mendes and others, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brazil.svg)","alt":"The flag of Brazil: a green field with a large yellow diamond, inside it a blue globe carrying white stars and a curved band reading ORDEM E PROGRESSO."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","brazil"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Brazil</b> takes up much of South America and is the fifth largest country in the world, divided into 26 states and a federal district across five geographical regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The North is almost all Amazon basin, the largest expanse of hot, humid forest on earth, and it occupies close to half the national territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Northeast holds the semi-arid lands, where rainfall is irregular and can fall below 250 mm (10 inches) a year, while the South lies in the temperate zone with cool, dry winters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain over the country as a whole averages 1,761 mm (69 inches) a year, from about 500 mm (20 inches) in that dry Northeast to more than 3,000 mm (118 inches) in the Amazon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Climate models project the basin warming by about 3.3 °C (5.9 °F) by 2100, with rainfall rising overall but falling around 10 per cent at the end of the dry season in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country was a Portuguese colony before it was a state of its own, its Atlantic ports at Recife, Salvador and Rio de Janeiro trading widely well before independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It emerged not as a republic but as a monarchy: the first constitution, granted by the Emperor Pedro I on 25 March 1824, opens by declaring the empire a free and independent nation that will accept no bond of union with any other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same document made the government hereditary, constitutional and representative, and established the Roman Catholic religion while tolerating others in private worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It divided the territory into provinces that could be subdivided as the state required, and those provinces are the ancestors of the states the federation is made of today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brazil was a founding member of the United Nations on 24 October 1945 and has since become a regional power and an active leader in international affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Brazil,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/CA0213EN/ca0213en.pdf. [Open access]","Matthieu Guimberteau et al., “Impacts of Future Deforestation and Climate Change on the Hydrology of the Amazon Basin: A Multi-Model Analysis with a New Set of Land-Cover Change Scenarios,” <i>Hydrology and Earth System Sciences</i> 21, no. 3 (2017): 1455–75, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-1455-2017. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Brazil,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/brazil. [Open access]","Brazil, <i>Constituição Politica do Imperio do Brazil</i>, 25 March 1824, Presidência da República, Casa Civil, https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/constituicao/constituicao24.htm. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Brazil,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/br.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Christ_the_Redeemer_-_Cristo_Redentor.jpg/1920px-Christ_the_Redeemer_-_Cristo_Redentor.jpg","title":"Christ the Redeemer (statue), Brazil","desc":"Christ the Redeemer (statue).","credit":"Arne Müseler, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_the_Redeemer_-_Cristo_Redentor.jpg","alt":"A view of Christ the Redeemer (statue)."}},
{"id":"gw-008","num":8,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Bangladesh","answerText":"Bangladesh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1971</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bangladesh"},"facts":[["Capital","Dhaka"],["Population","176M (2025)"],["Largest city","Dhaka"],["Area","148,460 km² (57,320 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Flag_of_Bangladesh.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bangladesh.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bangladesh: a bottle-green field with a red disc set slightly towards the hoist."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","bangladesh"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Bangladesh</b> is a low-lying riverine country at the head of the Bay of Bengal, formed as the largest deltaic plain in the world where the Ganges, the Brahmaputra and the Meghna meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hills rise along its western, northern and eastern edges, and a great swamp forest spreads across the southern coast where the delta meets the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 230 rivers cross it, most of them tributaries or distributaries of those three, and together they run to about 24,000 km (14,900 miles) and carry among the highest discharges of any river system on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only about 7 per cent of the catchment they drain lies inside the country, so most of the water arriving is rain that fell somewhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is the tropical monsoon: roughly 80 per cent of the rain falls between June and September, the annual average is 2,320 mm (91 inches), and cyclones come off the bay in the seasons on either side of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The land was the eastern part of Bengal, and it became the eastern wing of Pakistan when British India was partitioned on religious lines in 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because Muslims were concentrated at both ends of the subcontinent, that new state was split into two regions a thousand miles apart, with the rest of the subcontinent between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The eastern wing seceded in 1971 and became Bangladesh under Sheikh Mujibur Rahman, its first prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The new country joined the United Nations three years later, on 17 September 1974, and it is a member of the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the most densely populated countries in the world, and the delta that makes it fertile also leaves it exposed in turn to flood, drought and cyclone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Bangladesh,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2014, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0375en/CA0375EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Bangladesh,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/bangladesh. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bangladesh,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bangladesh. [Open access]","The National Archives, “Indian Independence: What Led to Partition in 1947?,” Classroom Resources, https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/resources/indian-independence/. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Bangladesh,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bd.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Save_the_sundarbans_20.jpg/1920px-Save_the_sundarbans_20.jpg","title":"the Sundarbans, Bangladesh","desc":"A tidal creek in the Sundarbans, the mangrove forest of the Ganges delta.","credit":"Shihabur Rahman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Save_the_sundarbans_20.jpg","alt":"A narrow channel of still water winding between dense mangroves, their breathing roots standing in the mud along both banks."}},
{"id":"gw-009","num":9,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Russia","answerText":"Russia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Russia"},"facts":[["Capital","Moscow"],["Population","144M (2025)"],["Largest city","Moscow"],["Area","17,098,246 km² (6,601,668 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Russia.svg","credit":"Design attributed to Peter the Great, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Russia: three equal horizontal bands, white above blue above red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","russia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Russia</b> is by far the largest country in the world, covering eastern Europe and northern Asia and reaching the Arctic, the Pacific, the Baltic, the Black Sea and the Caspian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has land frontiers with fourteen states, running from the Baltic and the Arctic in the west round to the Pacific rim in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three vast low plains make up much of it — the east European and the west Siberian, divided by the Urals, and the Caspian plain in the south-west — with fertile black soils to their south and medium mountains through southern Siberia and the far east, rising to Elbrus at 5,642 m (18,510 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Precipitation averages 460 mm (18 inches) a year, from under 200 mm (8 inches) at the Volga mouth to about 1,000 mm (39 inches) in the far-eastern mountains, and much of the country's fresh water is locked in permafrost and of no practical use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 120,000 rivers longer than 10 km (6 miles) drain it, 71 per cent of the territory northwards into the Arctic Ocean, and Lake Baikal, the deepest freshwater lake on earth and the largest by volume, chills the air above it enough to cut its own summer rainfall by about 15 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Soviet Union came to an end in late 1991 and the Russian Federation emerged as one of fifteen newly independent republics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An unsuccessful coup against Mikhail Gorbachev in August 1991 sealed its fate, weakening him and pushing Boris Yeltsin and the democratic opposition to the front of politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ukraine and Belarus declared independence within days, the Baltic states sought recognition of theirs, and in early December Yeltsin met their leaders at Brest to form the Commonwealth of Independent States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 25 December 1991 the Soviet flag came down over the Kremlin for the last time and Gorbachev resigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state it replaced had been a founding member of the United Nations on 24 October 1945 and held one of the five permanent Security Council seats that article 23 of the Charter names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Russian Federation,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0330en/CA0330EN.pdf. [Open access]","Hyunho Lee et al., “Effects of Lake Baikal on Summertime Precipitation Climatology over the Lake Surface,” <i>Geophysical Research Letters</i> 50, no. 10 (2023), https://doi.org/10.1029/2023GL103426. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]","United Nations, <i>Charter of the United Nations</i>, 26 June 1945, article 23, https://www.un.org/en/about-us/un-charter/full-text. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Russian Federation,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ru.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_in_Moscow.jpg/1920px-Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_in_Moscow.jpg","title":"Saint Basil's Cathedral, Russia","desc":"Saint Basil's Cathedral.","credit":"Tsy1980, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_in_Moscow.jpg","alt":"A view of Saint Basil's Cathedral."}},
{"id":"gw-505","num":505,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Islamabad","answerText":"Islamabad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Pakistan","dot":"Islamabad"},"facts":[["Country","Pakistan"],["Population","1.10M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","10th largest"],["Population growth","+9.9% (2017–2023)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","pakistan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Islamabad</b> is a capital that was decided on rather than grown into: the administrative centre of Pakistan was deliberately shifted from Karachi to a new city on the Potohar Plateau in the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The move set the government apart from the country's commercial life while keeping it accessible, and the new city was laid out beside the existing town of Rawalpindi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Capital Development Authority was established in 1960 to develop and maintain the new capital and the land around it, and it still administers the city and the master plan drawn for its wider territory in 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Karachi, the seat it replaced, had held the government from the beginning: the American Embassy opened there on the day Pakistan became independent, with Charles W. Lewis Jr. as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was in Karachi that the first Constituent Assembly of Pakistan met, in the Sindh Assembly Building on 10 August 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Muhammad Ali Jinnah was elected its president unanimously the next day, and the assembly formally approved the national flag at the same sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territories that made up the new country were meanwhile to be governed under the Government of India Act of 1935, the constitutional order inherited from British rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decision to build a capital of its own came under General Muhammad Ayub Khan, who took power in 1958 and set about remaking the country's institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of his first steps was to appoint a Constitution Commission in February 1960, charged with proposing how democracy might be strengthened and shaped to the country's own circumstances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city that came out of that programme is the capital the United Nations records for Pakistan, and the authority that built it still describes it as a place set apart from commerce and marked by its quiet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Pakistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pk.html. [Open access]","Capital Development Authority, “About Islamabad,” Government of Pakistan, https://www.cda.gov.pk. [Open access]","National Assembly of Pakistan, “History,” https://na.gov.pk/en/content.php?id=75. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Pakistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/pakistan. [Open access]","Capital Development Authority, “CDA History,” Government of Pakistan, https://www.cda.gov.pk/cdaHistory. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Skyline_of_Islamabad_from_Pakistan_Monument_%28Landscape%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Islamabad_from_Pakistan_Monument_%28Landscape%29.jpg","title":"Islamabad","desc":"Islamabad, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bilalnaqeeb, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Islamabad_from_Pakistan_Monument_(Landscape).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Islamabad."}},
{"id":"gw-010","num":10,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Ethiopia","answerText":"Ethiopia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Italy invades</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1895 and 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eritrea leaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ethiopia"},"facts":[["Capital","Addis Ababa"],["Population","135M (2025)"],["Largest city","Addis Ababa"],["Area","1,104,300 km² (426,373 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Flag_of_Ethiopia.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:SKopp, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ethiopia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ethiopia: three equal horizontal bands of green, yellow and red, with a blue disc at the centre bearing a yellow five-pointed star and rays."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","ethiopia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ethiopia</b> is a landlocked country in the north-eastern Horn of Africa, its high interior falling away on every side to lowlands and, beyond them, to the Red Sea and the Indian Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great East African Rift Valley cuts across it, dividing high mountains and flat-topped plateaux that are scored by deep gorges and ringed by lowlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground runs from 110 m (360 feet) below sea level at the Denakil Depression in the north-east to over 4,600 m (15,100 feet) in the Simien Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Height rather than latitude sets the climate: the mean annual temperature is 7 to 12 °C (45 to 54 °F) on the cool high plateaux and above 25 °C (77 °F) in the hot lowlands below 1,500 m (4,900 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 848 mm (33 inches) a year but is erratic and unevenly spread, from about 2,000 mm (79 inches) in the south-west to under 100 mm (4 inches) in the Afar lowlands, so dry spells and drought are a standing risk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A third of the country drains into the Nile, and the highlands supply roughly 85 per cent of the water that reaches Lake Nasser in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lake Tana, the source region of the Blue Nile, is one of the two headwater catchments whose future flows climate models disagree about most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Long isolated, the emperors began to consolidate the country in the middle of the 19th century, and Italian invasions in 1895 and 1935 forced it into wider world politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A revolution later brought the Derg to power and tied the country to international communism until the Mengistu regime fell in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ethiopia was among the earliest members of the United Nations, joining on 13 November 1945, within weeks of the organisation coming into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Ethiopia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9732en/I9732EN.pdf. [Open access]","M. T. Taye et al., “Assessment of Climate Change Impact on Hydrological Extremes in Two Source Regions of the Nile River Basin,” <i>Hydrology and Earth System Sciences</i> 15, no. 1 (2011): 209–22, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-15-209-2011. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ethiopia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ethiopia. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Ethiopia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/et.html. [Open access]","FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Egypt,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9729en/I9729EN.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Lalibela%2C_san_giorgio%2C_esterno_24.jpg/1920px-Lalibela%2C_san_giorgio%2C_esterno_24.jpg","title":"Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela, Ethiopia","desc":"Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela.","credit":"Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lalibela,_san_giorgio,_esterno_24.jpg","alt":"A view of Rock-Hewn Churches, Lalibela."}},
{"id":"gw-011","num":11,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Mexico","answerText":"Mexico","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 February 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mexico"},"facts":[["Capital","Mexico City"],["Population","132M (2025)"],["Largest city","Mexico City"],["Area","1,964,375 km² (758,449 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/Flag_of_Mexico.svg","credit":"Drawn by Alex Covarrubias, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mexico.svg)","alt":"The flag of Mexico: three equal vertical bands of green, white and red, with the national coat of arms — an eagle on a cactus holding a snake — at the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","north america","mexico"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mexico</b> lies between North and Central America, with the Gulf of Mexico and the Caribbean along its eastern side and the Pacific along its western.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is unusually varied for one country: the south lies in the tropics and the north in the temperate zone, but altitude keeps temperatures lower than the latitude alone would suggest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dry climates cover about 28 per cent of the territory, warm sub-humid ones 23 per cent, very dry ones 21 per cent and temperate sub-humid ones another 21 per cent, with mean annual temperatures mostly between 10 and 26 °C (50 and 79 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain is scarce in the north and heavier in the south-east and on the Gulf and Pacific slopes below the Tropic of Cancer, and most of it falls between June and October, often torrentially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tropical cyclones arrive between May and November on the Pacific, Gulf and Caribbean coasts alike, most often over Yucatán and Quintana Roo and over the Baja California peninsula and Sinaloa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Teotihuacan, in the valley north-east of the modern capital, was one of the largest cities of the pre-Columbian Americas, and its street and land alignments still shape the ground around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under Spanish sovereignty until Napoleon Bonaparte's invasion of Spain in 1808 gave Latin America its opening to fight for independence from colonial rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The constitution now in force declares the country a representative, democratic, secular and federal republic, made up of states that are free and sovereign in their internal affairs together with the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national territory it defines takes in the islands, reefs and cays of the adjacent seas, the Guadalupe and Revillagigedo islands out in the Pacific, the continental shelf and the territorial waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mexico was among the founding members of the United Nations, joining on 7 November 1945, and the organisation files it within Central America rather than North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — México,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2013, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0444es/CA0444ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mexico,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mexico. [Open access]","Mexico, <i>Constitución Política de los Estados Unidos Mexicanos</i>, articles 40 and 42, Cámara de Diputados, https://www.diputados.gob.mx/LeyesBiblio/pdf/CPEUM.pdf. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Mexico,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mx.html. [Open access]","Nawa Sugiyama et al., “Humans as Geomorphic Agents: Lidar Detection of the Past, Present and Future of the Teotihuacan Valley, Mexico,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 9 (2021): e0257550, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257550. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Chichen_Itza_3.jpg/1920px-Chichen_Itza_3.jpg","title":"Chichen Itza, Mexico","desc":"Chichen Itza.","credit":"Daniel Schwen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chichen_Itza_3.jpg","alt":"A view of Chichen Itza."}},
{"id":"gw-012","num":12,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Japan","answerText":"Japan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Expulsion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1639</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kanagawa treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Japan"},"facts":[["Capital","Tokyo"],["Population","123M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tokyo"],["Area","377,930 km² (145,920 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Flag_of_Japan.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Japan: a plain white field with a large crimson disc at its centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","japan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Japan</b> is an archipelago curving 2,600 km (1,600 miles) from north-east to south-west through the North Pacific, separated from the Asian mainland by the Sea of Japan and the East China Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Honshu, Hokkaido, Kyushu, Shikoku and Okinawa hold about 96 per cent of the land, and more than 3,300 smaller islands make up the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 70 and 80 per cent of the country is mountainous, the ranges of central Honshu rising above 3,000 m (9,800 feet) and Mount Fuji reaching 3,776 m (12,390 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That mountainous backbone splits the seasonal winds into two climates: the Pacific side is warm and humid in summer and dry and windy in winter, while the side facing the Sea of Japan takes heavy snow from December to February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,728 mm (68 inches) a year, from 800 mm (31 inches) in northern Hokkaido to 3,600 mm (142 inches) in the far south, with a rainy season from June to mid-July and typhoons in late summer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Portuguese, Spanish and Dutch traders reached the islands in the 16th and 17th centuries, but persistent attempts to convert the Japanese to Catholicism and a tendency to trade unfairly led Japan to expel most foreigners in 1639.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the two centuries that followed, trade was limited to Dutch and Chinese ships holding special charters, and the shoguns kept the borders otherwise closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That policy ended with the Treaty of Peace and Amity signed at Kanagawa on 31 March 1854, after four ships had sailed into the harbour of the shoguns' capital at Edo the previous July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 18 December 1956, eleven years after the organisation was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its winters have been changing since: a reconstruction of the years from 1959 to 2020 at five-kilometre resolution shows rapid warming from about 1980 and falling maximum snow depths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Japan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 1997, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0396en/CA0396EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Japan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/japan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The United States and the Opening to Japan, 1853,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/opening-to-japan. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Japan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jp.html. [Open access]","Hiroaki Kawase et al., “Historical Regional Climate Changes in Japan in Winter as Assessed by a 5-km Regional Climate Model with a Land Surface Process,” <i>Progress in Earth and Planetary Science</i> 10, no. 1 (2023), https://doi.org/10.1186/s40645-023-00536-4. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/View_of_Mount_Fuji_from_%C5%8Cwakudani_20211202.jpg/1920px-View_of_Mount_Fuji_from_%C5%8Cwakudani_20211202.jpg","title":"Mount Fuji, Japan","desc":"Mount Fuji.","credit":"Suicasmo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Mount_Fuji_from_%C5%8Cwakudani_20211202.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Fuji."}},
{"id":"gw-507","num":507,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Brasília","answerText":"Brasília","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Congress moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Brazil","dot":"Brasília"},"facts":[["Country","Brazil"],["Population","4.60M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","3rd largest"],["Population growth","-3.1% (2021–2025)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","brazil"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Brasília</b> is the last of a series of seats, and the series is the clearest way to see how Brazil's government moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was a Portuguese colony until 1815 and then part of a united kingdom with Portugal until 1822, when the empire of Brazil began under Dom Pedro I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rio de Janeiro had been the centre of that world since the Napoleonic wars, when the King of Portugal fled across the Atlantic and the American legation followed him there, staying from 1810 until it closed in July 1821.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American consulates had appeared at Rio and at Pernambuco in the 1810s, and Rio remained the seat of government through the empire and the republic that followed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Chamber of Deputies was created by the first Brazilian constitution, imposed by the emperor in 1824, and opened its first legislature on 6 May 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It sat first in the Cadeia Velha, the old gaol where Tiradentes had been held for three years before his execution, and it was there that the law abolishing slavery was passed in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The building was demolished in 1922 as unsafe and the Palácio Tiradentes went up in its place — the first put up specifically to house the Chamber — and was inaugurated on 6 May 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chamber sat there for 34 years, and its own list of seats then ends with a single entry that has run ever since: the Palácio do Congresso Nacional, in the new capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The move took the government a long way inland from the coastal cities where it had been carried on since the colony, and the whole Congress went with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the capital the United Nations records for Brazil, and the government the city was built to house has sat there ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Brazil,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/br.html. [Open access]","Câmara dos Deputados, “Conheça a Câmara,” Congresso Nacional, Brasil, https://www2.camara.leg.br/a-camara/conheca. [Open access]","Câmara dos Deputados, “Cronologia histórica e legislativa do Brasil,” Congresso Nacional, Brasil, https://www2.camara.leg.br/a-camara/conheca/historia/cronoindice.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Portugal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/portugal. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Brazil,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/brazil. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Brasilia_aerea_eixo_monumental.jpg/1920px-Brasilia_aerea_eixo_monumental.jpg","title":"Brasília","desc":"Brasília from the air, along the Eixo Monumental towards Lake Paranoá.","credit":"Portal da Copa / Governo do Brasil, CC BY 3.0 BR, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brasilia_aerea_eixo_monumental.jpg","alt":"A wide aerial view of a planned city: a television tower, low modernist blocks in green superblocks, broad avenues and a lake on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-013","num":13,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Egypt","answerText":"Egypt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 26 April 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suez Canal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nationalised 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Egypt"},"facts":[["Capital","Cairo"],["Population","118M (2025)"],["Largest city","Cairo"],["Area","1,002,000 km² (386,874 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Flag_of_Egypt.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Egypt.svg)","alt":"The flag of Egypt: three equal horizontal bands of red, white and black, with a gold eagle of Saladin at the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","egypt"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Egypt</b> occupies the north-eastern corner of Africa, with the Mediterranean to the north and the Red Sea to the east, the Sahara stretching away west of it and the Nile running the length of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of it is desert plateau, interrupted by the valley and delta of the Nile, which together make up about 4 per cent of the area and hold nearly all the farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land rises to about 1,000 m (3,300 feet) east of the valley and 800 m (2,600 feet) west of it, reaching 2,629 m (8,625 feet) at Mount Catherine in Sinai and falling 133 m (436 feet) below sea level in the Qattara Depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Summers are hot and dry from May to October, at 38 to 43 °C (100 to 109 °F) and up to 49 °C (120 °F) in the southern and western deserts, and winters mild from November to April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain is negligible — an average of 51 mm (2 inches) a year, about 200 mm (8 inches) on the northern coast and almost none in the south — so the Nile supplies nearly all the country's water, some 85 per cent of it rising in the Ethiopian highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Egypt was part of the Ottoman Empire and its foreign affairs were conducted from Istanbul, until Britain established a protectorate over it in 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence came in 1922 under King Ahmed Fuad, and from 1958 to 1961 the country was joined with Syria as a single state, the United Arab Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 26 July 1956 President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal Company, the joint British and French enterprise that had owned and run the canal since it was built in 1869.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The accords signed by President Anwar Sadat in September 1978 then framed the peace treaty concluded between Egypt and Israel in March 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Egypt had been a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, and the organisation files it within northern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Egypt,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9729en/I9729EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Egypt,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/egypt. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Suez Crisis, 1956,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/suez. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Egypt,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/eg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Camp David Accords and the Arab-Israeli Peace Process,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1977-1980/camp-david. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg/1920px-Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg","title":"Giza pyramid complex, Egypt","desc":"Giza pyramid complex.","credit":"KennyOMG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyramids_of_the_Giza_Necropolis.jpg","alt":"A view of Giza pyramid complex."}},
{"id":"gw-014","num":14,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Philippines","answerText":"Philippines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Philippines"},"facts":[["Capital","Manila"],["Population","117M (2025)"],["Largest city","Quezon City"],["Area","300,000 km² (115,831 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Flag_of_the_Philippines.svg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons contributors, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Philippines.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Philippines: a white triangle at the hoist bearing a golden sun and three stars, beside horizontal bands of blue above red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","philippines"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Philippines</b> is an archipelago of some 7,100 islands in South-East Asia, bounded by the Bashi Channel to the north, the Philippine Sea to the east, the Sulu and Celebes seas to the south and the South China Sea to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its northernmost islands lie about 240 km (150 miles) south of Taiwan and its southernmost 24 km (15 miles) off the coast of Borneo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the larger islands carry high mountain ranges running north to south, across the path of the prevailing air streams, so windward slopes are drenched and the ground in their lee falls into rain shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical and monsoonal, the temperature holding near 27 °C (81 °F) all year, with the north-east monsoon from late October to March, the south-west monsoon from May to October and Pacific trade winds between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages about 2,348 mm (92 inches) a year but ranges from some 960 mm (38 inches) in south-east Mindanao to more than 4,050 mm (159 inches) in central Luzon, where 9,006 mm (355 inches) fell in the single year of 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Spain ruled the islands for more than three centuries and gave up sovereignty over them in 1898, in the treaty that closed its colonial empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Filipino nationalists under Emilio Aguinaldo had wanted independence rather than a change of colonial ruler, and the war that broke out in February 1899 lasted three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 20,000 Filipino combatants were killed in it, and as many as 200,000 civilians died of violence, famine and disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country was nonetheless a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, before it was sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Full independence followed on 4 July 1946, 48 years after Spanish rule ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Philippines,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0405en/CA0405EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Philippines,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/philippines. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Philippine-American War, 1899–1902,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/war. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Philippines,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ph.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Banaue-terrace.JPG/1920px-Banaue-terrace.JPG","title":"Banaue Rice Terraces, the Philippines","desc":"Banaue Rice Terraces.","credit":"User: (WT-shared) Roundtheworld at wts wikivoyage, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banaue-terrace.JPG","alt":"A view of Banaue Rice Terraces."}},
{"id":"gw-015","num":15,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Democratic Republic of the Congo","answerText":"Democratic Republic of the Congo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dem. Rep. Congo"},"facts":[["Capital","Kinshasa"],["Population","113M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kinshasa"],["Area","2,345,410 km² (905,568 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Flag_of_the_Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:Nightstallion, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Democratic Republic of the Congo: a sky-blue field crossed diagonally by a red stripe edged in yellow, with a yellow star in the upper hoist corner."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","congo"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Democratic Republic of the Congo</b> sits astride the equator in central Africa, all but landlocked, with less than 100 km (60 miles) of Atlantic coast at the mouth of its own great river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine countries surround it, from the Central African Republic in the north through Uganda, Rwanda, Burundi and Tanzania in the east to Angola and Zambia in the south and the Republic of the Congo in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The equatorial half of the country is forest — some 125 million hectares of it — and the half nearer the tropics is savannah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is equatorial and hot, humid in the centre and more markedly tropical towards the north and south, and rain is regular and abundant: an average of 1,545 mm (61 inches) a year within a range of 800 to 1,800 mm (31 to 71 inches), falling over about eight months in twelve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some thirty large rivers feed the Congo, which runs 4,670 km (2,900 miles) and puts more water into the sea at its mouth than any river but the Amazon; five distinct sources of atmospheric moisture have been traced feeding its basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The territory was the Belgian Congo, and it was proclaimed independent of Belgian sovereignty on 30 June 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the months before that the Congolese had elected a president, Joseph Kasavubu, a prime minister, Patrice Lumumba, a senate and an assembly, and similar bodies in each of the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within a week the soldiers of the Force Publique mutinied against their Belgian commanders at the Thysville base over pay and advancement, the mutiny spread to other bases, and violence broke out across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thousands of Europeans left, and the new state fell into a crisis that ran on for five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It took its seat at the United Nations on 20 September 1960, less than three months after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — République démocratique du Congo,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9917fr/I9917FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Democratic Republic of the Congo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-democratic-republic. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Congo, Decolonization, and the Cold War, 1960–1965,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/congo-decolonization. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic Republic of the Congo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cd.html. [Open access]","Rogert Sorí et al., “A Lagrangian Perspective of the Hydrological Cycle in the Congo River Basin,” <i>Earth System Dynamics</i> 8, no. 3 (2017): 653–75, https://doi.org/10.5194/esd-8-653-2017. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Virunga_National_Park-107997.jpg/1920px-Virunga_National_Park-107997.jpg","title":"Virunga National Park, the Democratic Republic of the Congo","desc":"Virunga National Park.","credit":"Yvette Kaboza, CC BY-SA 3.0 igo, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virunga_National_Park-107997.jpg","alt":"A view of Virunga National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-510","num":510,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Addis Ababa","answerText":"Addis Ababa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 May 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">OAU founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ethiopia","dot":"Addis Ababa"},"facts":[["Country","Ethiopia"],["Population","4.60M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+87.6% (2012–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ethiopia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Addis Ababa</b> is the capital of a country that was never colonised, and its 20th century can be read from the comings and goings of the foreign missions in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American Legation there was established on 6 July 1909, when Hoffman Philip presented his credentials as minister resident and consul general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That followed the first official dealing between the two states, a treaty of commerce signed by Menelik II and Robert P. Skinner in December 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italian forces invaded and occupied the city, and although the United States never publicly recognised Italian authority in Ethiopia it withdrew its representation: the minister left on 4 March 1937 and the consulate closed at the end of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation reopened on 31 August 1943, once the occupation was over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two decades later the city became the meeting place of a continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The heads of 32 independent African states gathered there to sign the charter creating the Organisation of African Unity, Africa's first post-independence continental institution and the expression of a pan-African vision of an Africa united, free and in control of its own destiny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There were 32 of them to gather because European rule had been unravelling for a generation: some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The organisation lasted until 1999 and the African Union was launched as its successor in 2002, taking in all 55 states of the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its Commission, the secretariat that carries out the union's daily work, is based in the city, which is why an Ethiopian capital is where African business is done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ethiopia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/et.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ethiopia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ethiopia. [Open access]","African Union, “About the African Union,” https://au.int/en/overview. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","African Union, “The AU Commission,” https://au.int/en/commission. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Addis_Ababa_skyline.jpg/1920px-Addis_Ababa_skyline.jpg","title":"Addis Ababa","desc":"Addis Ababa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Simfan34, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Addis_Ababa_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Addis Ababa."}},
{"id":"gw-016","num":16,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Vietnam","answerText":"Vietnam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1954, at Geneva</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1977</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vietnam"},"facts":[["Capital","Hanoi"],["Population","102M (2025)"],["Largest city","Ho Chi Minh City"],["Area","331,317 km² (127,922 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Flag_of_Vietnam.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vietnam.svg)","alt":"The flag of Vietnam: a plain red field with a single large five-pointed yellow star in the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","vietnam"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Vietnam</b> occupies the eastern edge of the Indochina peninsula, a long narrow country with the South China Sea along its east and south, the Gulf of Thailand to the south-west and mountains along its western rim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is long and narrow, and its provinces are grouped into eight regions running from the North West and the Red river delta down the central coast and the highlands to the Mekong delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The north has four seasons and a subtropical climate; the south has two, wet and dry, and a tropical one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages about 1,820 mm (72 inches) a year, from 650 mm (26 inches) at Phan Rang on the south-central coast to 4,760 mm (187 inches) at Bac Quang in the north-east, and falls mostly between May and October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperatures run from around 15 °C (59 °F) in winter to 25 °C (77 °F) in summer, and the central coast is struck regularly by storms and heavy seas out of the South China Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country was a French protectorate within Indochina, and nationalist risings against that rule mounted through the late 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became an independent state within the French Union in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 7 May 1954 the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu fell after a four-month siege led by Ho Chi Minh, and the French pulled out of the region altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Geneva Conference that closed the war divided the territory along the 17th parallel, with separate governments in the north and the south, and a second and longer war followed until the negotiations that opened in Paris in 1969 produced a settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A reunited Vietnam took its seat at the United Nations on 20 September 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Viet Nam,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0412en/CA0412EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Vietnam,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/vietnam. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Dien Bien Phu and the Fall of French Indochina, 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/dien-bien-phu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Ending the Vietnam War, 1969–1973,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/ending-vietnam. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Viet Nam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vn.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Ha_Long_Bay_in_2019.jpg/1920px-Ha_Long_Bay_in_2019.jpg","title":"Ha Long Bay, Vietnam","desc":"Ha Long Bay.","credit":"Taewangkorea, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ha_Long_Bay_in_2019.jpg","alt":"A view of Ha Long Bay."}},
{"id":"gw-017","num":17,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Iran","answerText":"Iran","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Islamic Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">referendum 29–30 March 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iran"},"facts":[["Capital","Tehran"],["Population","92.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tehran"],["Area","1,630,848 km² (629,674 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/Flag_of_Iran.svg","credit":"Design by Hamid Nadimi, construction by ISIRI, SVG by SiBr4; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iran.svg)","alt":"The flag of Iran: three horizontal bands of green, white and red, with a red emblem in the centre and a band of repeated white script along the inner edges of the green and red stripes."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","iran"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Iran</b> lies in western Asia with the Caspian Sea along its northern edge and the Gulf of Oman and the Persian Gulf along its southern, and dry upland fills nearly all the ground between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 52 per cent of it is mountain and desert, and some 16 per cent stands more than 2,000 m (6,600 feet) above sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Zagros is the largest massif, running from the north-west down to the shores of the Persian Gulf and then east; other ranges follow the southern edge of the Caspian, and between them all lies the Central Plateau, more than half the country, part salt swamp and part loose sand and stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is one of extremes: summer temperatures in the interior have been recorded above 55 °C (131 °F), while the altitude and the continental position bring −30 °C (−22 °F) to the north-west in winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 228 mm (9 inches) a year, from under 50 mm (2 inches) in the deserts to 2,275 mm (90 inches) at Rasht by the Caspian, and roughly 90 per cent of the country is arid or semi-arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Lake Urmia, the salt lake of the north-west, was once among the largest in the region and has been shrinking towards barren desert as groundwater is drawn off for towns and farms around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state is old: it dealt with the wider world as the Kingdom of Persia, and negotiated its first treaty of friendship and commerce with a western republic in 1850 through its chargé d'affaires at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The monarchy fell in 1979, and the constitution then adopted declares the form of government to be an Islamic Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That constitution rests its claim on a referendum held on 29 and 30 March 1979, in which it records the affirmative vote of 98.2 per cent of eligible voters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Iran was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, and the organisation files it within southern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Iran (Islamic Republic of),” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0339en/CA0339EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iran,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iran. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Islamic Republic of Iran</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Iran_1989. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Iran (Islamic Republic of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ir.html. [Open access]","K. Moghtased-Azar et al., “Investigation of Correlation of the Variations in Land Subsidence (Detected by Continuous GPS Measurements) with the Level of Lake Urmia in the Northwest of Iran,” <i>Nonlinear Processes in Geophysics</i> 19, no. 6 (2012): 675–83, https://doi.org/10.5194/npg-19-675-2012. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_%28Throne_Hall%29_in_Persepolis.jpg/1920px-Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_%28Throne_Hall%29_in_Persepolis.jpg","title":"Persepolis, Iran","desc":"Persepolis.","credit":"Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hall_of_Hundred_Columns_(Throne_Hall)_in_Persepolis.jpg","alt":"A view of Persepolis."}},
{"id":"gw-018","num":18,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Turkey","answerText":"Turkey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turkey"},"facts":[["Capital","Ankara"],["Population","88.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Istanbul"],["Area","783,562 km² (302,535 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Flag_of_Turkey.svg","credit":"Drawn by David Benbennick, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Turkey.svg)","alt":"The flag of Turkey: a red field bearing a white crescent moon with a small white five-pointed star to its right."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","turkey"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Turkey</b> forms the bridge between Europe and Asia, with about 3 per cent of its land in Thrace on the European side and the rest in Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is ringed by the Black Sea, the Aegean and the Mediterranean, and its coastline runs over 10,000 km (6,200 miles) against a land frontier of about 2,950 km (1,830 miles), so more than three quarters of its perimeter is shore rather than boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Maritime and continental weather meet over highly varied ground, so the climates differ sharply: the Black Sea coast has rain all year and mild seasons, the Mediterranean coast hot dry summers and mild wet winters, and central Anatolia is a high plateau averaging 1,132 m (3,714 feet) with a semi-arid continental climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages about 643 mm (25 inches) a year but ranges from 1,260 to 2,500 mm (50 to 98 inches) on the Black Sea coast down to 200 to 600 mm (8 to 24 inches) in central Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The North Anatolian Fault runs the length of the country, and satellite measurements show strain building along it at a steady rate between the great earthquakes that release it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Turkey is what the Ottoman Empire became: the empire dissolved after the First World War and the modern state arose in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its constitution opens by declaring the state a republic, and makes that article unamendable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second article calls it a democratic, secular and social state governed by the rule of law, respecting human rights and loyal to the nationalism of Atatürk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The republic was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About a tenth of its territory is inland water, and the Black Sea, Aegean and Mediterranean coasts give it three quite different seaboards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Turkey,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0351en/CA0351EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Turkey,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/turkey. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Turkey</i>, articles 1 and 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Turkey_2017. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Türkiye,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tr.html. [Open access]","Ekbal Hussain et al., “Constant Strain Accumulation Rate between Major Earthquakes on the North Anatolian Fault,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 9, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-03739-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Hagia_Sophia_%28228968325%29.jpeg/1920px-Hagia_Sophia_%28228968325%29.jpeg","title":"Hagia Sophia, Turkey","desc":"Hagia Sophia.","credit":"Adli Wahid, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Sophia_(228968325).jpeg","alt":"A view of Hagia Sophia."}},
{"id":"gw-513","num":513,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Cairo","answerText":"Cairo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Egypt","dot":"Cairo"},"facts":[["Country","Egypt"],["Population","20.5M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+5.5% (2018–2023)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","egypt"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cairo</b> was the working seat of Egyptian government at a time when Egypt’s foreign relations were not conducted from it at all, but through the Ottoman Sultan’s government at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain established a protectorate in 1882, taking effective control of those relations while the country remained nominally Ottoman, so the American presence in the city stayed a quasi-diplomatic one under an Agent and Consul General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was that officer, J. Morton Howell, who delivered President Warren G. Harding’s letter recognising Egyptian independence to King Ahmed Fuad, and the American post in the city changed character with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Howell became the diplomatic representative, and on 24 June 1922 his position was raised to envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary, which is how the American legation in Egypt came to be established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomacy there was broken by war: between 5 and 10 June 1967 Israel defeated Egypt, Jordan and Syria and occupied the Sinai, Gaza, the West Bank, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, and Egypt accused Washington of helping it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The day after relations were cut an American interests section opened inside the Spanish embassy here, an arrangement that kept a channel open for nearly seven years without any formal tie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What ended it was Henry Kissinger’s shuttle diplomacy after the next war, when he travelled between Jerusalem and this city and put together an Egyptian-Israeli disengagement in eight days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two governments agreed to resume relations, and the American embassy in the city reopened the same day with Ambassador-designate Hermann F. Eilts in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four years later President Anwar Sadat signed the Camp David framework with President Carter and Menachem Begin, and a peace treaty between Israel and Egypt followed in March 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American post here has been in turn a consulate under Ottoman suzerainty, a legation, an interests section inside somebody else’s embassy and an embassy of its own, and through all of it the city remained the capital of a founding member of the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Egypt,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/eg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Egypt,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/egypt. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The 1967 Arab-Israeli War,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/arab-israeli-war-1967. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Kissinger's Shuttle Diplomacy, 1974,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/shuttle-diplomacy. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Camp David Accords and the Arab-Israeli Peace Process,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1977-1980/camp-david. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Cairo_skyline%2C_Nile_River%2C_Egypt.jpg/1920px-Cairo_skyline%2C_Nile_River%2C_Egypt.jpg","title":"Cairo","desc":"Cairo, seen from a distance.","credit":"Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo_skyline,_Nile_River,_Egypt.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Cairo."}},
{"id":"gw-019","num":19,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Germany","answerText":"Germany","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">German Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 January 1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Germany"},"facts":[["Capital","Berlin"],["Population","84.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Berlin"],["Area","357,581 km² (138,063 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Flag_of_Germany.svg","credit":"Drawn by User:SKopp, User:Madden and others, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany.svg)","alt":"The flag of Germany: three equal horizontal bands of black, red and gold."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","germany"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Germany</b> was a conglomeration of more than 300 kingdoms, duchies, principalities and free cities in the 18th century, and took form across the 19th under the helmsmanship of the largest of those monarchies, Prussia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prussia was the first of them to recognise the United States, signing a treaty of amity and commerce with it in 1785, and John Quincy Adams was sent as minister twelve years later to renew it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His letters of credence were addressed to a king who died before he could be received, so the new king recognised him at a private audience as the minister accredited to his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington had said in 1849 that it would recognise any unified German government that appeared capable of maintaining its power, so when the empire was proclaimed it re-accredited its minister to Prussia as minister to the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were rocky in the first half of the 20th century, the two states fighting on opposite sides in both world wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After the second of them American, British and Soviet forces divided and occupied the country, and its capital, lying far inside the Soviet-controlled east, was divided with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first crisis of the Cold War in Europe began on 24 June 1948, when Soviet forces blockaded the rail, road and water access to the Allied sectors and the western powers answered by flying in food and fuel until it was lifted eleven months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second opened with Khrushchev’s demand of November 1958 that the western powers pull their forces out of West Berlin within six months, and ended three years later with the building of the Berlin Wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thousands of Germans brought that wall down on 9 November 1989, two years after Ronald Reagan had stood beside it and called on Mikhail Gorbachev to tear it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The communist governments of eastern Europe collapsed one after another, and within a year the country was whole again, holding the United Nations seat it had entered in the 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Germany,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/de.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Germany,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/germany. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Airlift, 1948–1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Crisis, 1958–1961,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/berlin-crises. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Fall of Communism in Eastern Europe, 1989,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/fall-of-communism. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg/1920px-Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg","title":"Neuschwanstein Castle, Germany","desc":"Neuschwanstein Castle.","credit":"Thomas Wolf , www.foto-tw.de, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg","alt":"A view of Neuschwanstein Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-020","num":20,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Thailand","answerText":"Thailand","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Thailand"},"facts":[["Capital","Bangkok"],["Population","72.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bangkok"],["Area","513,140 km² (198,124 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Flag_of_Thailand.svg","credit":"Drawn by Zscout370, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Thailand.svg)","alt":"The flag of Thailand: five horizontal bands of red, white, blue, white and red, the blue band in the middle twice the width of the others."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","thailand"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Thailand</b> lies in the south-east of the Asian mainland, a broad northern body narrowing into the long isthmus that runs down the Malay peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Mekong runs along much of its eastern edge, the Gulf of Thailand lies to the south-east, and the Andaman Sea and the Indian Ocean to the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate turns on the alternation of two monsoons: the south-westerly brings heavy rain from May to October and the north-easterly is comparatively dry and cool from October to February, with heavy thunderstorms in the March and April between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages about 1,622 mm (64 inches) a year, from 1,100 mm (43 inches) on the central plain and in the north-east to 4,000 mm (157 inches) on the southern peninsula near the Andaman Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chao Phraya gathers the northern rivers and crosses the low central plain to the gulf, and in dry years salt water pushes so far up its lower course that the intrusion is a national concern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Thailand was one of the very few countries in South and South-East Asia to avoid colonisation in the 19th century, and it is a kingdom still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its constitution opens by declaring the country one and indivisible, and a kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It adopts a democratic form of government with the king as head of state, and vests sovereign power in the Thai people, to be exercised through the National Assembly, the Council of Ministers and the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 16 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is divided into more than seventy provinces, grouped into four regions with the metropolitan area of the capital, and the greater part of its people live along the rivers of the central plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Thailand,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0408en/CA0408EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Thailand,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/thailand. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Kingdom of Thailand</i>, sections 1, 2 and 3, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Thailand_2017. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Thailand,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/th.html. [Open access]","Saifhon Tomkratoke et al., “Drought and Salinity Intrusion in the Lower Chao Phraya River: Variability Analysis and Modeling Mitigation Approaches,” <i>Hydrology and Earth System Sciences</i> 29, no. 15 (2025): 3771–93, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-29-3771-2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg/1920px-%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg","title":"Wat Arun, Thailand","desc":"Wat Arun.","credit":"Mastertongapollo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%88%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A2%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%9B%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B9%8C%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%AD%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%932.jpg","alt":"A view of Wat Arun."}},
{"id":"gw-021","num":21,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"United Kingdom","answerText":"United Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of Paris</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ireland leaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United Kingdom"},"facts":[["Capital","London"],["Population","70.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","London"],["Area","243,610 km² (94,058 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_%283-5%29.svg","credit":"Vector drawing by Yaddah after the Acts of Union 1800, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg)","alt":"The flag of the United Kingdom: a blue field with a broad white-edged red cross upright and a red saltire edged in white running corner to corner."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","united kingdom"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>United Kingdom</b> was the state the thirteen American colonies declared their independence from, and the war that followed ran on for five years after the declaration, until General Cornwallis surrendered to George Washington on 19 October 1781.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came two years later at Paris, where David Hartley, a Member of Parliament representing King George III, signed the treaty alongside John Adams, Benjamin Franklin and John Jay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states have been on generally cordial terms ever since, the exceptions being a break in relations over the War of 1812 and the strain caused by the possibility that Britain might recognise the Confederacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American consulates spread across the islands in the decades that followed: the first in Scotland at Leith in 1798, one at Belfast two years earlier still, and others at Liverpool, Bristol, Falmouth, Cardiff and a dozen smaller ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most were closed again as trade moved on: Aberdeen in 1922, Dundee in 1940, Glasgow in 1965 and Liverpool not until 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The union itself changed shape after the First World War, when the Anglo-Irish Treaty gave independence to 26 Irish counties, constituted as the Irish Free State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Six counties of northeast Ulster chose to remain part of the kingdom with limited self-government, and it is that territory whose area the World Bank measures today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised the Irish Free State as a state with autonomous control over its own foreign relations in June 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Britain signed the Brussels Treaty of March 1948 with France, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg, each bound to help defend any of the others attacked, and that idea became the North Atlantic alliance the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same months British and American aircraft supplied western Berlin through the Soviet blockade, flying in food and fuel from airbases in western Germany, and both governments did so as founding members of the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gb.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The United Kingdom,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/united-kingdom. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Airlift, 1948–1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): United Kingdom,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GBR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2022. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ireland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ireland. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Stonehenge2007_07_30.jpg/1920px-Stonehenge2007_07_30.jpg","title":"Stonehenge, the United Kingdom","desc":"Stonehenge.","credit":"garethwiscombe, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stonehenge2007_07_30.jpg","alt":"A view of Stonehenge."}},
{"id":"gw-514","num":514,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Manila","answerText":"Manila","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">SEATO formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1954</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Philippines","dot":"Manila"},"facts":[["Country","Philippines"],["Population","13.7M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+3.0% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","philippines"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Manila</b> was the capital of a longstanding Spanish colony until an American squadron sailed into its bay in the spring of 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first battle of the war with Spain was fought there on 1 May, when Commodore George Dewey’s Asiatic Squadron defeated the Spanish naval force defending the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war ended with a treaty signed at Paris under which Spain agreed to sell the archipelago to the United States for twenty million dollars, and the Senate ratified it by a margin of one vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Filipinos who had been fighting for independence from Spain found the transfer no more than a change of colonial ruler, and Emilio Aguinaldo’s forces took control of most of Luzon and proclaimed an independent Philippine Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war that followed lasted more than three years, and a civil government was established under the future President William Howard Taft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The islands convened their first elected assembly in 1907, and American opposition at home to colonial rule never went away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independence came on the American national holiday, when President Truman proclaimed the Republic of the Philippines an independent state and the two governments signed a treaty by which the United States renounced all its claims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American ambassador, Paul V. McNutt, presented his credentials on the same day, which is the date diplomatic relations between the two states are counted from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eight years later the republic joined the United States, France, Britain, New Zealand, Australia, Thailand and Pakistan in forming the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, partly out of its close ties with Washington and partly from concern at a communist insurgency threatening its own government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The new organisation put its headquarters in Bangkok rather than here, and this city has been throughout the seat of a state that has belonged to the United Nations since the organisation was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Philippines,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ph.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Philippines,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/philippines. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Philippine-American War, 1899–1902,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO), 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/seato. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Manila_skyline_day.jpg/1920px-Manila_skyline_day.jpg","title":"Manila","desc":"Manila, seen from a distance.","credit":"Mike Gonzalez ( TheCoffee ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manila_skyline_day.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Manila."}},
{"id":"gw-022","num":22,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tanzania","answerText":"Tanzania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tanzania"},"facts":[["Capital","Dodoma"],["Population","71.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Dar es Salaam"],["Area","947,303 km² (365,756 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Flag_of_Tanzania.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tanzania.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tanzania: a green upper triangle and a blue lower one divided by a black diagonal band edged in yellow."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","tanzania"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tanzania</b> is the mainland together with Zanzibar, which is made up of the islands of Unguja and Pemba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Indian Ocean lies along its eastern side and three of the great African lakes — Victoria, Tanganyika and Nyasa — lie against its northern, western and south-western edges, so much of its outline is water rather than land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plains run along the coast, a plateau fills the centre and highlands rise to the north and south; Mount Meru and Mount Kilimanjaro dominate the north-east and the Southern Highlands divide the eastern plateau from the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Woodland, grassland and bushland cover about 80 per cent of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain runs from 500 to 1,000 mm (20 to 39 inches) over most of it and up to 3,000 mm (118 inches) north-east of Lake Tanganyika and in the Southern Highlands, falling in one season inland and in two — the short Vuli rains and the long Masika — along the coast and around Lake Victoria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The mainland was Tanganyika, held under British administration as a United Nations Trust Territory, and it became independent on 9 December 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Julius Nyerere was its first prime minister, and it took its seat at the United Nations five days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Zanzibar joined it afterwards, and the united republic takes its name from the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That independence belonged to a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence from European rulers between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was no single pattern to it: some transfers were peaceful and orderly and others followed a protracted revolution, and so many new members changed the composition of the United Nations itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — United Republic of Tanzania,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9836en/I9836EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Tanzania,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/united-republic-tanzania. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tanzania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tanzania. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “United Republic of Tanzania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tz.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Kilimanjaro_from_Amboseli.jpg/1920px-Kilimanjaro_from_Amboseli.jpg","title":"Mount Kilimanjaro, Tanzania","desc":"Mount Kilimanjaro.","credit":"Sergey Pesterev, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kilimanjaro_from_Amboseli.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Kilimanjaro."}},
{"id":"gw-023","num":23,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"France","answerText":"France","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 February 1778</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 July 1798</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"France"},"facts":[["Capital","Paris"],["Population","67.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Paris"],["Area","551,500 km² (212,935 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Flag_of_France.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg)","alt":"The flag of France: three equal vertical bands of blue, white and red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","france"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>France</b> is among the oldest allies of the United States: its monarchy recognised American independence when Conrad Alexandre Gérard, secretary of the king’s council of state, signed two treaties with Benjamin Franklin, Silas Deane and Arthur Lee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> French military and economic help during the American War of Independence was crucial to the victory, and the first American consular post in the country opened at Bordeaux the following month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began that August when Gérard presented his credentials to Congress, and Franklin was appointed the first American minister a month later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The revolution of 1789 overthrew the government of Louis XVI, and Thomas Jefferson’s rule that the United States should acknowledge as rightful any government formed by the will of a nation has been policy ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So relations survived every new French government constituted after 1789, most of them in the 19th century; each time the resident American representative simply submitted fresh credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The one real rupture came over the XYZ affair, when Congress abrogated the treaties of 1778 and the consular convention with them; a treaty signed at Mortefontaine in 1800 ended the Quasi-War that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A century later it was one of the Big Four, with Britain, the United States and Italy, that dominated the Paris Peace Conference and produced the Treaty of Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany invaded on 15 May 1940 and the government left Paris for Bordeaux; parliament met at Vichy that July and granted Marshal Pétain full and extraordinary powers, and the American embassy moved near the new regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the war it signed the Brussels Treaty of March 1948 with Britain, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg, each bound to defend any of the others attacked, and the North Atlantic alliance was built on that idea the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1956 it landed troops beside Britain’s over the Suez Canal and drew a public censure from Washington that soured relations for a time; it has held a United Nations seat since the organisation was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “France,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: France,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/france. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Suez Crisis, 1956,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/suez. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Tour_Eiffel_Wikimedia_Commons_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Tour_Eiffel_Wikimedia_Commons_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Eiffel Tower, France","desc":"Eiffel Tower.","credit":"Benh LIEU SONG, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_Eiffel_Wikimedia_Commons_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Eiffel Tower."}},
{"id":"gw-024","num":24,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Africa","answerText":"South Africa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1910</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Westminster</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa"},"facts":[["Capital","Pretoria (administrative)"],["Population","65.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Johannesburg"],["Area","1,221,037 km² (471,445 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Flag_of_South_Africa.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Africa.svg)","alt":"The flag of South Africa: a green horizontal Y shape edged in white and gold, separating red above from blue below, with a black triangle at the hoist."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","south africa"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>South Africa</b> is the southernmost country on the African continent, and its coastline of over 3,000 km (1,900 miles) links the east and west coasts of Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It holds the southern tip of the continent, with the Atlantic along its western coast and the Indian Ocean along its eastern and southern, and a mountain kingdom enclosed entirely within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A plateau dominates the topography, falling from 2,400 m (7,900 feet) in the east to 600 m (2,000 feet) in the sandy Kalahari, and it is cut off from the lower ground around it by the Great Escarpment, which reaches nearly 3,300 m (10,800 feet) and is known in its parts as the Drakensberg, the Stormberg and the Roggeveld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country is semi-arid: rain averages 495 mm (19 inches) a year, from under 100 mm (4 inches) in the western deserts to about 1,200 mm (47 inches) in the east, and 65 per cent of the land gets too little for rainfed crops and is grazed instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Summer runs from October to March at 15 to 30 °C (59 to 86 °F) and winter from April to September, when frost is common on the great plateau; only the Western Cape has a Mediterranean climate with winter rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Four colonies were joined on 31 May 1910 to create the Union of South Africa, a self-governing dominion whose external relations were still run from London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 recognised the dominions as autonomous and in no way subordinate, and the Statute of Westminster codified that in December 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 31 May 1961, 51 years to the day after the union, the government declared the country a republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its present constitution founds one sovereign democratic state on human dignity, equality, non-racialism and non-sexism, and on universal adult suffrage with a national common voters roll.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> South Africa was a founding member of the United Nations from 7 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — South Africa,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9821en/I9821EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of South Africa</i>, sections 1 and 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/South_Africa_2012. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Table_Mountain_DanieVDM.jpg/1920px-Table_Mountain_DanieVDM.jpg","title":"Table Mountain, South Africa","desc":"Table Mountain.","credit":"Danie van der Merwe from Cape Town, South Africa, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Table_Mountain_DanieVDM.jpg","alt":"A view of Table Mountain."}},
{"id":"gw-515","num":515,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Kinshasa","answerText":"Kinshasa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 July 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dem. Rep. Congo","dot":"Kinshasa"},"facts":[["Country","Democratic Republic of the Congo"],["Population","13.7M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+25.3% (2012–2016)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","congo"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kinshasa</b> was called Léopoldville when the country around it became independent of Belgium, and the American embassy opened there the same day with John D. Tomlinson as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the months before that the Congolese had elected a president, Joseph Kasavubu, a prime minister, Patrice Lumumba, a senate and an assembly, and similar bodies in each of the country’s many provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Eisenhower administration had high hopes of a stable, pro-Western central government, and those hopes vanished in a matter of days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soldiers of the Force Publique mutinied against their white Belgian commanders at the Thysville base on 5 July, seeking better pay and greater authority; the mutiny spread and violence broke out across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Belgium sent troops to restore order without asking either Kasavubu or Lumumba, and the government appealed to the United Nations for troops of its own and demanded that the Belgians withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council answered on 13 July, authorising an intervention force and calling on Belgium to withdraw — and the UN’s own record of that resolution identifies the country by this city, under its old name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Katanga province had declared its independence two days earlier and South Kasai followed in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lawrence Devlin, the CIA chief of station here, described the situation as a classic communist takeover, and his reports helped convince Washington that Lumumba had to be removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kasavubu dismissed Lumumba in September, Lumumba dismissed Kasavubu, and the first Cold War proxy confrontation over the newly independent, non-aligned states of Africa was under way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There were dozens of those states — some three dozen countries in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence between 1945 and 1960 — and this city has been the capital of one of them at the United Nations ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic Republic of the Congo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Democratic Republic of the Congo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-democratic-republic. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Congo, Decolonization, and the Cold War, 1960–1965,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/congo-decolonization. [Open access]","UN Security Council, resolution 143 (1960), adopted at the 873rd meeting, 13 July 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/619961. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Boulevard_du_30_juin%2C_Kinshasa.jpg/1920px-Boulevard_du_30_juin%2C_Kinshasa.jpg","title":"Kinshasa","desc":"Kinshasa, seen from a distance.","credit":"MONUSCO/Myriam Asmani, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulevard_du_30_juin,_Kinshasa.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kinshasa."}},
{"id":"gw-025","num":25,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Italy","answerText":"Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Unification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 April 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Italy"},"facts":[["Capital","Rome"],["Population","59.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Rome"],["Area","302,068 km² (116,629 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/Flag_of_Italy.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy.svg)","alt":"The flag of Italy: three equal vertical bands of green, white and red."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","italy"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Italy</b> did not exist as a state when the United States first dealt with the peninsula: Washington had relations with the Kingdom of Sardinia, the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies and the Papal States, and legations at Naples from 1831, Turin from 1840 and Rome from 1848.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It kept consulates at virtually every seaport as well, because trade, shipping and the protection of seamen were the main American business there, and 41 of them are listed for the ground the country now covers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The modern state began in 1861 with the unification of most of the peninsula under the House of Savoy, and it took in Venetia and the former Papal States, Rome among them, by 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation to Sardinia simply became the legation to the new kingdom, its minister presenting fresh credentials on the day the United States recognised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations stayed warm for eighty years and broke only once, when Benito Mussolini’s government declared war on the United States and stayed at war with it for two years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The kingdom gave way to a republic in 1946, and relations resumed with the successor state as they had been with its predecessor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had been one of the Big Four — with Britain, France and the United States — that dominated the Paris Peace Conference and produced the Treaty of Versailles a generation earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The republic joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in May 1950 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is a founding member of what became the European Union, having been in it from the first day of 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations admitted it only in 1955, a decade after the organisation was founded and nine years after the republic replaced the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Italy,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/it.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Italy,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/italy. [Open access]","European Union, “Italy – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/italy_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Italy and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/italy_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Colosseo_2020.jpg/1920px-Colosseo_2020.jpg","title":"Colosseum, Italy","desc":"Colosseum.","credit":"FeaturedPics, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colosseo_2020.jpg","alt":"A view of Colosseum."}},
{"id":"gw-026","num":26,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Kenya","answerText":"Kenya","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1895</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kenya"},"facts":[["Capital","Nairobi"],["Population","58.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Nairobi"],["Area","591,958 km² (228,556 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Flag_of_Kenya.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kenya.svg)","alt":"The flag of Kenya: horizontal bands of black, red and green separated by thin white stripes, with a red and white Maasai shield and two crossed spears over the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","kenya"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kenya</b> sits on the equator and on the East African coast, with the Indian Ocean along its south-eastern side, Lake Victoria on its western and the highlands of the Rift between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Rift Valley cuts through the Central Highlands and is the country's dominant feature; the ground climbs from sea level to the 5,199 m (17,057 feet) peak of Mount Kenya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between those extremes lie deserts such as the Chalbi, glaciated mountains, and inland waters that include Lake Turkana, the largest desert lake on earth, and the Kenyan share of Lake Victoria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rain averages 630 mm (25 inches) a year, from under 200 mm (8 inches) in the north to over 1,800 mm (71 inches) on the slopes of Mount Kenya, and falls in two seasons — long rains from March to May and short rains from October to December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 80 per cent of the land is arid or semi-arid and is grazed rather than farmed, while most of the population lives on the fifth of the country that gets enough rain to crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From 1895 until 1963 the territory was under British colonial rule as part of British East Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became independent on 12 December 1963 under Jomo Kenyatta as prime minister, and took its seat at the United Nations four days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That independence belonged to a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence from European rulers between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was no single pattern to it: some transfers were peaceful and orderly and others followed a protracted revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost 47,000 km² (18,000 sq mi) of the country is set aside in national parks, reserves and sanctuaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Kenya,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/i9762en/I9762EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Kenya,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kenya. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kenya,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kenya. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Kenya,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ke.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Masai_Mara_at_Sunset.jpg/1920px-Masai_Mara_at_Sunset.jpg","title":"Maasai Mara, Kenya","desc":"Maasai Mara.","credit":"Danijel Mihajlovic, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masai_Mara_at_Sunset.jpg","alt":"A view of Maasai Mara."}},
{"id":"gw-027","num":27,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Myanmar","answerText":"Myanmar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1948</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Myanmar"},"facts":[["Capital","Naypyidaw"],["Population","55.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Yangon"],["Area","676,577 km² (261,228 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Flag_of_Myanmar.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Myanmar.svg)","alt":"The flag of Myanmar: three horizontal bands of yellow, green and red with a large white five-pointed star across the centre."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","myanmar"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Myanmar</b> faces the Andaman Sea along its southern coast and the Bay of Bengal to the south-west, and mountain ranges close it in on its western, northern and eastern sides alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its seven states cover mainly the hill regions and its seven divisions the plains, a split that follows the mountain ranges running north to south down the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those ranges also set the courses of the rivers: the Ayeyarwady and its Chindwin branch drain 58 per cent of the territory, the Thanlwin a further 18 per cent off the Shan plateau in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is the tropical monsoon, with rain concentrated in the hot, humid months of the south-westerly from May to October and a cool, almost entirely dry north-easterly from December to March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages about 2,341 mm (92 inches) a year, from 4,000 to 6,000 mm (157 to 236 inches) on the Rakhine and Tanintharyi coasts down to 500 to 1,000 mm (20 to 39 inches) in the central dry zone, and 90 per cent of the rivers' discharge comes between May and October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country's own constitution dates the loss of its sovereignty to colonial intrusion in 1885.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Political leaders reached agreement with the British government in January 1947 on the process by which independence would be achieved, and during that year a constituent assembly was chosen and a constitution devised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The preamble records that the nation became an independent sovereign state again on 4 January 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations three months later, on 19 April 1948, and its independence belonged to a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa left European rule between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was no single pattern to that: some transfers were peaceful and orderly and others followed a protracted revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Myanmar,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0401en/CA0401EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burma,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burma. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of the Union of Myanmar</i>, preamble, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Myanmar_2008. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Myanmar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mm.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Bagan%2C_Burma.jpg","title":"Bagan, Myanmar","desc":"Bagan.","credit":"Corto Maltese 1999, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagan,_Burma.jpg","alt":"A view of Bagan."}},
{"id":"gw-516","num":516,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hanoi","answerText":"Hanoi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liaison office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 January 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vietnam","dot":"Hanoi"},"facts":[["Country","Vietnam"],["Population","4.50M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+8.4% (2016–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","vietnam"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hanoi</b> got its first American consulate while the country around it was still a French colony and general war was already under way in the north between Ho Chi Minh’s Viet Minh and French troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Washington recognised the State of Viet Nam three years later it dealt with Saigon instead, raising the consulate general there to a legation and then, in 1952, to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fall of the French garrison at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954 ended the First Indochina War, France pulled out of the region, and the powers at Geneva divided the country along the 17th parallel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States was not a signatory to the Geneva accords and did not recognise the government in this city, conducting relations solely with the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It backed the southern leader Ngo Dinh Diem when he refused the elections meant to reunify the country, which the communists seemed likely to win.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> By 1959 southern communist guerrillas had begun a large-scale insurgency, and after two American destroyers reported being fired on in the Gulf of Tonkin, Congress gave President Johnson authority to take whatever measures he thought necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nixon inherited the war in 1969 believing he could end it honourably within a year, and instead ordered secret bombing of North Vietnamese base camps in Cambodia while beginning troop withdrawals that never stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Henry Kissinger held his first private session with the northern leadership in August of that year and met the politburo member Le Duc Tho intermittently for months with no apparent progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American embassy in Saigon was evacuated in April 1975, and for twenty years the United States had no mission anywhere in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A liaison office opened here on 28 January 1995, relations were resumed that August, and the embassy here opened the day after the documents were signed, eighteen years after the reunified country had taken its seat at the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Viet Nam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Vietnam,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/vietnam. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Dien Bien Phu and the Fall of French Indochina, 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/dien-bien-phu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “U.S. Involvement in the Vietnam War: the Gulf of Tonkin and Escalation, 1964,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/gulf-of-tonkin. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Ending the Vietnam War, 1969–1973,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/ending-vietnam. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Purple_haze_of_Hanoi_Skyline_%2822507027147%29.jpg/1920px-Purple_haze_of_Hanoi_Skyline_%2822507027147%29.jpg","title":"Hanoi","desc":"Hanoi, seen from a distance.","credit":"cloud.shepherd, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purple_haze_of_Hanoi_Skyline_(22507027147).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hanoi."}},
{"id":"gw-028","num":28,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Colombia","answerText":"Colombia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 1819</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Panama leaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Colombia"},"facts":[["Capital","Bogotá"],["Population","53.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bogotá"],["Area","1,141,748 km² (440,831 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Flag_of_Colombia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colombia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Colombia: a broad yellow band across the top half, with blue and red bands of equal width below it."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","colombia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Colombia</b> occupies the north-west corner of South America and is the only country on that continent with a coast on both the Pacific and the Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archipelago of San Andrés, Providencia and Santa Catalina lies far out in the Caribbean and the island of Malpelo out in the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is set by the equator, by the Andes and by the intertropical convergence zone, and it is altitude rather than latitude that varies it, in bands the country calls thermal floors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The warm floor below 800 m (2,600 feet) averages over 24 °C (75 °F) and covers more than four fifths of the territory; the temperate floor to 1,800 m (5,900 feet) about a tenth; the cold floor to 2,800 m (9,200 feet) some 7 per cent; and the very cold and snow floors above that under 2 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 3,240 mm (128 inches) a year, of which 58 per cent runs off, which puts the country among those with the greatest natural water supply on earth; it drains five ways, 35 per cent to the Amazon, 28 to the Orinoco, 14 each to the Magdalena-Cauca and the Pacific and 8 to the Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Colombia had effectively won independence from Spain by 1819, and the federation of that time held the territory of four modern republics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ecuador and Venezuela left it in 1830, after which the country was known as New Granada and then, from 1856, as the Grenadine Confederation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It changed its name again in 1863 and settled on the Republic of Colombia in 1886.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Panama, then a province, separated in 1903; the French had begun cutting a canal across it in 1880 under Ferdinand de Lesseps and abandoned the attempt after nine years and some 20,000 deaths from malaria and yellow fever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The constitution now in force calls the country a decentralised unitary republic whose territorial units have autonomy, and Colombia has been a member of the United Nations since 5 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Colombia,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0572es/CA0572ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Colombia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/colombia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Colombia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Colombia_2015. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Building the Panama Canal, 1903–1914,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/panama-canal. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Colombia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/co.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Ca%C3%B1o_Cristales_01.jpg/1920px-Ca%C3%B1o_Cristales_01.jpg","title":"Caño Cristales, Colombia","desc":"Caño Cristales.","credit":"Astromario, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ca%C3%B1o_Cristales_01.jpg","alt":"A view of Caño Cristales."}},
{"id":"gw-029","num":29,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Korea","answerText":"South Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Korean War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1950–53</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Korea"},"facts":[["Capital","Seoul"],["Population","52.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Seoul"],["Area","100,401 km² (38,765 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Flag_of_South_Korea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Korea.svg)","alt":"The flag of South Korea: a white field with a red and blue circular taegeuk in the centre and four groups of black bars at the corners."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","korea"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>South Korea</b> occupies the southern part of a peninsula reaching off the east coast of the Asian landmass, with the Yellow Sea along its western side and the Sea of Japan along its eastern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 65 per cent of it is mountainous, most of all along the east coast: the Taebaek range crosses the country from north to south, the Sobaek runs from the south-west to the north-east, the highest ground is Halla-san at 1,950 m (6,400 feet), and the plains lie along the west and south coasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its four seasons are sharply drawn — winter bitterly cold under fronts out of Siberia, summer hot and humid under the maritime Pacific high, spring and autumn sunny and generally dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,274 mm (50 inches) a year and is evenly spread across the country, but about 70 per cent of it falls in the four summer months from June to September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Typhoons in summer and early autumn damage crops, as do the droughts that come before the summer monsoon breaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The peninsula was divided at the 38th parallel at the end of the Second World War, and neither of the powers overseeing that division would let its own Korea be absorbed by the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The southern republic was in being by the beginning of 1949, and its constitution calls it a democratic republic in which sovereignty resides in the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It defines the territory of the state as the whole Korean peninsula and its adjacent islands, and commits the state to seek unification by peaceful means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The northern army attacked in June 1950 and very nearly took the whole peninsula; the fighting ended in 1953 in an uneasy truce that fixed the division instead of settling it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two Korean states joined the United Nations together on 17 September 1991, more than four decades after that division and nearly forty years after the fighting stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Republic of Korea,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0406en/CA0406EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Republic of Korea (South Korea),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/korea-south. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Korea</i>, articles 1, 3 and 4, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Republic_of_Korea_1987. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Korean War and Japan's Recovery,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Front_view_of_the_Imperial_Throne_Hall_Geunjeongjeon_at_Gyeongbokgung_Palace_with_blue_sky_in_Seoul.jpg/1920px-Front_view_of_the_Imperial_Throne_Hall_Geunjeongjeon_at_Gyeongbokgung_Palace_with_blue_sky_in_Seoul.jpg","title":"Gyeongbokgung, South Korea","desc":"Gyeongbokgung.","credit":"Basile Morin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Front_view_of_the_Imperial_Throne_Hall_Geunjeongjeon_at_Gyeongbokgung_Palace_with_blue_sky_in_Seoul.jpg","alt":"A view of Gyeongbokgung."}},
{"id":"gw-030","num":30,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Sudan","answerText":"Sudan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sudan"},"facts":[["Capital","Khartoum"],["Population","52.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Khartoum"],["Area","1,878,000 km² (725,100 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Flag_of_Sudan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sudan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sudan: horizontal bands of red, white and black with a green triangle at the hoist."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","sudan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sudan</b> joins the Arab world of northern Africa to the Africa south of the Sahara, meeting the Red Sea in the north-east and reaching deep into the desert everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Highlands rise along its eastern edge, savannah opens to the south, and the Libyan and Nubian deserts run away west and north of the river that crosses it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country is generally flat, broken only by the Jebel Marra, the Red Sea Hills and the Nuba Mountains, and it falls into three ecological belts running north to south: desert, semi-desert and low-rainfall savannah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its soils follow the same pattern — clay in the centre and east, stabilised sand dunes in the west and north, red ironstone in the south, and alluvium along the Nile and the other rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 250 mm (10 inches) a year and ranges from 25 mm (1 inch) in the far north to 700 mm (28 inches) in the south, and the rainfall of the upper Blue Nile that feeds the country's rivers rises and falls with El Niño in the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The territory was governed under an Anglo-Egyptian condominium before it was a state of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Republic of Sudan came into being on 1 January 1956 and joined the United Nations on 12 November that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That independence belonged to a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence from European rulers between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There was no single pattern to it: some transfers were peaceful and orderly and others followed a protracted revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sudan was the largest country in Africa until South Sudan became independent in 2011, and is now the third largest, after Algeria and the Democratic Republic of the Congo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Sudan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/i9808en/I9808EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sudan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sudan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","M. A. H. Zaroug et al., “Simulating the Connections of ENSO and the Rainfall Regime of East Africa and the Upper Blue Nile Region Using a Regional Nested Model,” <i>Hydrology and Earth System Sciences</i> 18, no. 11 (2014): 4311–23, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-18-4311-2014. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Sudan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sd.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Mer%C3%B6e_Pyramids_with_camles_%2835119048185%29.jpg/1920px-Mer%C3%B6e_Pyramids_with_camles_%2835119048185%29.jpg","title":"Meroë, Sudan","desc":"The pyramids of Meroë.","credit":"Hans Birger Nilsen, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mer%C3%B6e_Pyramids_with_camles_(35119048185).jpg","alt":"Steep-sided pyramids standing in desert sand."}},
{"id":"gw-517","num":517,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Tehran","answerText":"Tehran","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1883</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Allied conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1943</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy seized</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 November 1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iran","dot":"Tehran"},"facts":[["Country","Iran"],["Population","9.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+6.0% (2011–2016)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","iran"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tehran</b> got its first resident American mission in June 1883, when the chargé d’affaires S. G. W. Benjamin presented his credentials to the Shah of Persia and the American legation in the city was established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states had first dealt with each other formally a generation earlier, and the treaty of commerce they negotiated then never came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city was the setting for the first meeting of the three Allied leaders of the Second World War, Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin spending four days there at the end of November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They used it to settle the timing of the invasion of northern France, to agree that the Soviet Union would declare war on Japan once Germany was beaten, and to discuss the fate of Poland and Germany after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eight years later the government nationalised the assets of the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company, and the dispute that followed drew the United States in as a mediator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> American documents released only decades afterwards record what the official account left out — the part played by the Central Intelligence Agency, and the covert action that led to the overthrow of Prime Minister Mohammad Mosadeq on 19 August 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The volume published in 1989 had carried none of it, and the criticism that followed changed United States law to oblige the government to publish a thorough and reliable record of its foreign policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A quarter of a century after the coup the monarchy fell in what the State Department’s own historians describe as one of the 20th century’s most significant revolutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Student militants seized the American embassy here and its staff on 4 November 1979, and the failure to secure their release led Washington to break off relations the following spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hostages were freed on 20 January 1981, relations were never restored, and Switzerland has acted ever since as the protecting power for American interests in the country whose capital this is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iran (Islamic Republic of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ir.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iran,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iran. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Tehran Conference, 1943,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/tehran-conf. [Open access]","James C. Van Hook, ed., “Preface,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1952–1954, Iran, 1951–1954</i> (Washington: U.S. Government Publishing Office, 2017), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1951-54Iran/preface. [Open access]","Linda Qaimmaqami, ed., “Preface,” in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1977–1980, Volume XI, Part 1, Iran: Hostage Crisis, November 1979–September 1980</i> (Washington: U.S. Government Publishing Office, 2020), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1977-80v11p1/preface. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Skyline_of_Tehran_on_a_hot_summer_day_in_2015_-_04.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Tehran_on_a_hot_summer_day_in_2015_-_04.jpg","title":"Tehran","desc":"Tehran, seen from a distance.","credit":"GolhaMedia, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Tehran_on_a_hot_summer_day_in_2015_-_04.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Tehran."}},
{"id":"gw-031","num":31,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Uganda","answerText":"Uganda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uganda"},"facts":[["Capital","Kampala"],["Population","51.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kampala"],["Area","241,550 km² (93,263 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4e/Flag_of_Uganda.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uganda.svg)","alt":"The flag of Uganda: six horizontal bands of black, yellow and red repeated twice, with a white disc in the centre carrying a grey crested crane."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","uganda"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Uganda</b> is a landlocked country on the equator in east-central Africa, a high plateau standing between the two arms of the Great Rift Valley and ringed by mountains at its edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it belongs to the interior plateau of the continent, averaging about 1,200 m (3,900 feet), with open plains in the north and flat-topped hills through the centre, west and east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plateau rises at both edges into mountains: the Rwenzori block and the Mufumbira volcanoes in the west, where Mount Stanley reaches 5,109 m (16,762 feet), and Mount Elgon at 4,321 m (14,177 feet) in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is equatorial with little seasonal swing, and rain averages 1,180 mm (46 inches) a year, from 750 mm (30 inches) in the north-eastern pastoral country to 1,500 mm (59 inches) around Lake Victoria and on the high ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The south has two rainfall peaks, in March to May and August to November, with no pronounced dry season between them, while the north has a marked dry one from November to March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Nile basin covers about 98 per cent of the country, and the chain of equatorial lakes that forms the upper White Nile lies largely inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lake Victoria drains at Jinja into the Victoria Nile, which runs through Lake Kyoga into Lake Albert and then north as the Albert Nile towards South Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory was under British sovereignty, and it became independent on 9 October 1962 under Milton Obote as prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It took its seat at the United Nations sixteen days later, on 25 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That independence came at the end of a wider unravelling in which some three dozen new states in Asia and Africa gained autonomy or outright independence from European rulers between 1945 and 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Uganda,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2014, https://www.fao.org/3/i9827en/I9827EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Uganda,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/uganda. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uganda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uganda. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Decolonization of Asia and Africa, 1945–1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/asia-and-africa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Uganda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ug.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/Murchison_Twin_Falls%2C_Uganda_%2816093084916%29.jpg","title":"Murchison Falls, Uganda","desc":"Murchison Falls.","credit":"Rod Waddington, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Murchison_Twin_Falls,_Uganda_(16093084916).jpg","alt":"A view of Murchison Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-032","num":32,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Spain","answerText":"Spain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 February 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Spain"},"facts":[["Capital","Madrid"],["Population","48.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Madrid"],["Area","506,008 km² (195,371 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Flag_of_Spain.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain.svg)","alt":"The flag of Spain: a red band, a broader yellow band and another red band, with the national coat of arms toward the hoist side of the yellow."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","spain"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The Continental Congress sent John Jay to <b>Spain</b> in 1779 to persuade the court to recognise the new American republic, and he spent two years there without success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Madrid was unwilling to risk its relations with the Congress in Philadelphia until it became clear that Britain and the United States were actually going to sign a treaty ending the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came when the American chargé d’affaires William Carmichael was officially received at court, though he had already been in the country for nine months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A consulate opened at Barcelona at the end of 1797, and a treaty of friendship, limits and navigation had been signed two years before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the two and a half centuries since, the two states have broken relations exactly once — in 1898, when the minister closed the legation in the capital on the day relations were severed and Congress declared war four days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The war that followed cost the country the last of its overseas empire: the treaty guaranteed the independence of Cuba, forced Spain to cede Guam and Puerto Rico to the United States, and had it sell the Philippines for twenty million dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relations resumed the next year, and the American legation was raised to embassy rank in 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the civil war of 1936 to 1939 the American embassy moved first to the consulate at Barcelona and then out of the country altogether, to St Jean de Luz in France, before being re-established at Burgos in April 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in August 1963 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of what is now the European Union at the start of 1986, and has held a seat at the United Nations since 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Spain,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/es.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Spain,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/spain. [Open access]","European Union, “Spain – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/spain_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Spain and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/spain_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/SF_maig_2_cropped.jpg","title":"Sagrada Família, Spain","desc":"Sagrada Família.","credit":"Canaan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SF_maig_2_cropped.jpg","alt":"A view of Sagrada Família."}},
{"id":"gw-033","num":33,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Algeria","answerText":"Algeria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Uprising begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1954</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 October 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Algeria"},"facts":[["Capital","Algiers"],["Population","47.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Algiers"],["Area","2,381,741 km² (919,595 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Flag_of_Algeria.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Algeria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Algeria: a green half and a white half, with a red crescent and five-pointed star across the join."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","algeria"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Algeria</b> stands at the north-west of Africa on the Mediterranean coast, and it is the largest country on the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a country of mountains at an average altitude of about 800 m (2,600 feet), with the Tell and Saharan Atlas ranges in the north and the Hoggar massif in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sahara covers more than 2 million km² (770,000 sq mi), some 87 per cent of the territory, and the cultivated ground within it comes to about 100,000 hectares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three climates divide the country: a Mediterranean coast and mountains on 4 per cent of the land, where winter rain can reach 1,600 mm (63 inches) and summer is nearly rainless; semi-arid high plateaux on 9 per cent at 100 to 400 mm (4 to 16 inches); and the arid desert below 100 mm (4 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Averaged over the whole territory the rainfall is 89 mm (3.5 inches) a year, and potential evapotranspiration runs from 800 mm (31 inches) in the north-east to more than 2,200 mm (87 inches) in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The coast was one of the Barbary states, owing a loose allegiance to the Ottoman Empire while conducting its own foreign relations and practising state-supported piracy on Mediterranean shipping to exact tribute from the trading powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Self-rule ended with the French conquest of 1830, and in 1848 France formally incorporated the settled northern territory as departments of metropolitan France rather than as a colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The constitution's preamble treats 1 November 1954 as the turning point in the country's future, the opening of a ferocious resistance to colonial rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independence came in 1962, and the constitution's first article declares Algeria a People's Democratic Republic, one and indivisible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 8 October 1962, three months after that independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Algérie,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/i9861fr/I9861FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Algeria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/algeria. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Barbary Wars, 1801–1805 and 1815–1816,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/barbary-wars. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the People's Democratic Republic of Algeria</i>, preamble and article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Algeria_2020. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Algeria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dz.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Tassili_n%27Ajjer-Tamghit_02.jpg/1920px-Tassili_n%27Ajjer-Tamghit_02.jpg","title":"Tassili n'Ajjer, Algeria","desc":"Tassili n'Ajjer.","credit":"IssamBarhoumi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tassili_n%27Ajjer-Tamghit_02.jpg","alt":"Weathered sandstone towers on a desert plateau."}},
{"id":"gw-518","num":518,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Ankara","answerText":"Ankara","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 April 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Notes exchanged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 February 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1952</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turkey","dot":"Ankara"},"facts":[["Country","Turkey"],["Population","5.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+6.2% (2017–2022)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","turkey"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ankara</b> was called Angora in English when the United States and the republic that had replaced the Ottoman Empire exchanged the notes there that restored relations between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For almost a century before that the American mission had sat at Constantinople, where David Porter presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires in September 1831 and where the legation was raised to embassy rank in 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That mission ended when the Ottoman government severed relations in April 1917, 16 days after the United States declared war on Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The list of American chiefs of mission shows the gap exactly: Abram Elkus left in April 1917 and nobody succeeded him until Joseph Clark Grew presented credentials in October 1927, a decade later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Grew served into 1932, and every ambassador since has been accredited to the republic rather than to an empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city mattered to Washington in a new way after the Second World War, when Britain announced that from 31 March 1947 it would stop paying for the Greek government’s war against the Greek communists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> President Truman went before a joint session of Congress that March to ask for aid to Greece and for aid to its neighbour as well, which had depended on the same British money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The principle he set out — that the United States would help any democratic nation threatened from outside or within — turned American policy away from staying out of distant quarrels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Greek civil war and the tensions beside it were among the reasons western Europe and the United States built the North Atlantic alliance, and in 1952 its members agreed to admit both countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city has been the seat of that member ever since, and the United Nations lists it as the capital under the modern spelling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Türkiye,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Turkey,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/turkey. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Chiefs of Mission for Turkey,” Department History, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/chiefsofmission/turkey. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Truman Doctrine, 1947,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/truman-doctrine. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO), 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Ankara_Ankara_Panorama_1-2_%2842487385781%29.jpg/1920px-Ankara_Ankara_Panorama_1-2_%2842487385781%29.jpg","title":"Ankara","desc":"Ankara, seen from a distance.","credit":"Murray Foubister, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ankara_Ankara_Panorama_1-2_(42487385781).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Ankara."}},
{"id":"gw-034","num":34,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Iraq","answerText":"Iraq","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mandate ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 December 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Iran war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980–88</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iraq"},"facts":[["Capital","Baghdad"],["Population","47.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Baghdad"],["Area","435,052 km² (167,975 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Flag_of_Iraq.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iraq.svg)","alt":"The flag of Iraq: three horizontal bands of red, white and black, with green Arabic script across the centre of the white band."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","iraq"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Iraq</b> is shaped like a basin: the great Mesopotamian alluvial plain of the Tigris and the Euphrates, ringed by mountains in the north and east and by desert in the south and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains close it in to the north and east, the Persian Gulf lies to its south-east, and desert runs away from the rivers to its south and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mountains reach 3,550 m (11,650 feet) and the deserts take up more than 40 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is continental and subtropical semi-arid, Mediterranean only in the northern and north-eastern mountains: winters are cool to cold at about 16 °C (61 °F) by day and 2 °C (36 °F) at night, and summers dry and hot, over 43 °C (109 °F) in July and August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 216 mm (9 inches) a year, from 1,200 mm (47 inches) in the north-east to under 100 mm (4 inches) across the 60 per cent of the country lying south, and both great rivers rise in Turkey, running about 1,000 and 1,300 km (620 and 810 miles) inside Iraq before they meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The land was Ottoman, and after that empire dissolved at the end of the First World War it was administered by Britain as a mandate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iraq was acknowledged an independent state in 1930 and the mandate ended two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 21 December 1945, within weeks of the organisation coming into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The war with Iran from 1980 to 1988 left the state intact and its national pride reinforced but owing some 37 billion dollars to Gulf creditors, and when they would not cancel the debt Saddam Hussein turned his threats on Kuwait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The constitution now in force calls the country a single federal, independent and fully sovereign state whose system of government is republican, representative, parliamentary and democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Iraq,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0340en/CA0340EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iraq,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iraq. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Gulf War, 1991,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/gulf-war. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Iraq</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Iraq_2005. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Iraq,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/iq.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Ziggarat_of_Ur_001.jpg/1920px-Ziggarat_of_Ur_001.jpg","title":"Ziggurat of Ur, Iraq","desc":"Ziggurat of Ur.","credit":"Tla2006 at English Wikipedia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziggarat_of_Ur_001.jpg","alt":"A view of Ziggurat of Ur."}},
{"id":"gw-035","num":35,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Argentina","answerText":"Argentina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 1816</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Argentina"},"facts":[["Capital","Buenos Aires"],["Population","46.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Buenos Aires"],["Area","2,780,400 km² (1,073,500 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_Argentina.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Argentina.svg)","alt":"The flag of Argentina: three horizontal bands of light blue, white and light blue, with a golden sun face in the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","argentina"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Argentina</b> runs 3,700 km (2,300 miles) down the southern end of South America, with the Atlantic along its eastern side and the Andes standing the whole length of its western.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Andes wall its western edge and two oceans moderate it, so the climate runs from humid subtropical in the north through temperate over most of the country to cold in the south and polar on the highest ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain ranges from 7,000 mm (276 inches) in the Andean forests of Patagonia to 50 mm (2 inches) in parts of San Juan and La Rioja, and averages 591 mm (23 inches) over the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three regions are separated by the 800 and 500 mm isohyets — a humid quarter in the north-east and the pampas, a semi-arid 15 per cent and an arid 61 per cent — and about 82 per cent of all the water that falls evaporates before it reaches a river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its native forests are under pressure from farming: in the Chaco, the Atlantic Forest and the Yungas an estimated million hectares were cleared in five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Argentina gained its independence from Spain in 1816, having declared it on 9 July of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tension between Centralist and Federalist forces then paralysed the new nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1854 the Federalist provinces ratified a constitution establishing the Argentine Confederation, which the Centralists repudiated while declaring themselves independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Confederation collapsed in 1861, opening the way to national rule under the Argentine Republic that same year; the constitution still in force adopts a federal, republican and representative form of government, with the federal authorities seated in a city that Congress declares the capital by special law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Argentina was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Argentina,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0438es/CA0438ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Argentina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/argentina. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Argentina — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ar. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Argentine Nation</i>, articles 1 and 3, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Argentina_1994. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Argentina,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ar.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Aerial_Foz_de_Igua%C3%A7u_26_Nov_2005.jpg","title":"Iguazu Falls, Argentina","desc":"Iguazu Falls.","credit":"Mariordo (Mario Roberto Durán Ortiz), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_Foz_de_Igua%C3%A7u_26_Nov_2005.jpg","alt":"A view of Iguazu Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-036","num":36,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Afghanistan","answerText":"Afghanistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 July 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Soviet invasion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 November 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Afghanistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Kabul"],["Population","44.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kabul"],["Area","652,864 km² (252,072 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","afghanistan"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"British authorities notified the American State Department in August 1919 that <b>Afghanistan</b> was officially free and independent in its affairs, and President Harding recognised it two years later by receiving an Afghan mission at the White House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed only in 1935, when William H. Hornibrook presented his credentials — he was accredited to Persia at the same time and lived at Tehran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation of its own opened at Kabul in June 1942 and was raised to embassy rank six years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country, together with Iceland and Sweden, at the 47th plenary meeting of its first session.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In February 1979 the American ambassador at Kabul, Adolph Dubs, was assassinated at his post, and every head of the mission after him was a chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the end of that year the Soviet Union sent thousands of troops in and took complete military and political control of the capital and much of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the only time the Soviet Union invaded a country outside the Eastern Bloc, and the historians who wrote that milestone add that ‘intervention’ describes it better than ‘invasion’, since Soviet domination had been growing since 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The embassy closed in January 1989 over doubts that the government left behind could protect diplomats once Soviet forces had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reopened as a liaison office in December 2001, seven weeks after a coalition campaign began against the Taliban and the sanctuary they had given Osama bin Laden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the World Trade Organization in July 2016, seven decades after taking the United Nations seat it has held since 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Afghanistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/af.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Afghanistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/afghanistan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Soviet Invasion of Afghanistan and the U.S. Response, 1978–1980,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1977-1980/soviet-invasion-afghanistan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Afghanistan, Iceland and Sweden to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 34 (I), 9 November 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209856. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Afghanistan and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/afghanistan_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Afghanistan%27s_Grand_Canyon.jpg","title":"Band-e Amir National Park, Afghanistan","desc":"Band-e Amir National Park.","credit":"USAID, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afghanistan%27s_Grand_Canyon.jpg","alt":"A view of Band-e Amir National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-519","num":519,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Berlin","answerText":"Berlin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Airlift</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1948–49</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Germany","dot":"Berlin"},"facts":[["Country","Germany"],["Population","3.90M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-1.5% (2022–2025)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","germany"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Berlin</b> was where the United States signed a consular convention with the new German Empire in December 1871, and where its legation was raised to embassy rank in 1893.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the Second World War American, British and Soviet forces divided and occupied the country, and the city — itself split into occupation zones — lay far inside the Soviet-controlled east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States, the United Kingdom and France held its western portions while Soviet troops held the eastern sector, and the question of who would keep the western zones produced the first crisis of the Cold War in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began on 24 June 1948, when Soviet forces blockaded the rail, road and water access to the Allied-controlled areas, and the western powers answered by flying in food and fuel from airbases in the west of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Soviets lifted the land blockade on 12 May 1949, and the division of the city among the four victors was then frozen in place by the Cold War, despite the postwar agreements to unify the zones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 10 November 1958 Khrushchev demanded that the three western powers pull their forces out of the western half within six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That ultimatum opened a three-year crisis over the city’s future, and the western half was by then too populous and too well established for another airlift to save it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thousands of Germans brought the wall down on 9 November 1989, two years after Ronald Reagan had stood beside it and called on Mikhail Gorbachev to tear it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The communist governments of eastern Europe collapsed one after another, and within a year the country was whole again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the people the city counted at the end of 2025, 976,419 were foreign nationals, and it is the seat of a state that has held its United Nations seat since the 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Germany,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/de.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Germany,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/germany. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Airlift, 1948–1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Berlin Crisis, 1958–1961,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/berlin-crises. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Fall of Communism in Eastern Europe, 1989,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/fall-of-communism. [Open access]","Amt für Statistik Berlin-Brandenburg, “Einwohnerbestand Berlin – Grunddaten,” https://www.statistik-berlin-brandenburg.de/a-i-5-hj. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Berlin_Downtown_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Berlin_Downtown_Skyline.jpg","title":"Berlin","desc":"Berlin, seen from a distance.","credit":"Nicolas Völcker, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin_Downtown_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Berlin."}},
{"id":"gw-037","num":37,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Canada","answerText":"Canada","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Balfour Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1927</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Canada"},"facts":[["Capital","Ottawa"],["Population","41.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Toronto"],["Area","9,984,670 km² (3,855,103 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Flag_of_Canada.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Canada.svg)","alt":"The flag of Canada: red bands at each end and a white square in the middle carrying a red maple leaf."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","north america","canada"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The Dominion of <b>Canada</b> was formed in 1867, but as an integral part of the British Empire its foreign relations stayed under the control of London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the next six decades it gradually won greater control of its external affairs, spurred in part by the demands of managing its relationship with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officials of the two governments dealt with each other increasingly through joint commissions and military cooperation, and the two even negotiated a bilateral fisheries treaty in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only in 1926 did the United Kingdom acknowledge that the dominion was fully autonomous in its foreign affairs, in the Balfour Declaration issued by that year’s Imperial Conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it as an independent state the following February, when Vincent Massey presented his credentials in Washington; an American legation opened in the capital that June and became an embassy in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some of the boundary between the two states had been settled long before any of that: the Rush-Bagot pact of 1817 had the two sides eliminate their fleets from the Great Lakes apart from small patrol vessels, and the Webster-Ashburton treaty settled the disputed boundary with Maine in 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its size is worth stating carefully, because the two sources this deck usually leans on disagree about it and one of them is plainly wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s series reports more than 15 million square kilometres, half as much again as the country actually holds, so the area here is taken from the United Nations and the population from the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two disagree the other way about people: the United Nations counts about 40 million and the World Bank, following the national statistical service, more than 41.6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has held a United Nations seat since November 1945 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Canada,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ca.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Canada,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/canada. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Canada and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/canada_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Canada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Rush-Bagot Pact, 1817 and Convention of 1818,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/rush-bagot. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Webster-Ashburton Treaty, 1842,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/webster-treaty. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Moraine_Lake_17092005.jpg/1920px-Moraine_Lake_17092005.jpg","title":"Moraine Lake, Canada","desc":"Moraine Lake.","credit":"Gorgo, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moraine_Lake_17092005.jpg","alt":"A view of Moraine Lake."}},
{"id":"gw-038","num":38,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Yemen","answerText":"Yemen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Aden taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">British exit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unification</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Yemen"},"facts":[["Capital","Sana'a"],["Population","42.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Sana'a"],["Area","527,968 km² (203,850 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/Flag_of_Yemen.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Yemen.svg)","alt":"The flag of Yemen: three equal horizontal bands of red, white and black."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","yemen"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Yemen</b> lies on the south-western edge of the Arabian Peninsula, with the Arabian Sea and the Gulf of Aden along its southern coast, the Red Sea along its western and desert inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It holds many islands, the largest of them Socotra, which lies far out in the Arabian Sea and carries plants found nowhere else, and Kamaran in the Red Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a mountainous country: the Yemen Mountain Massif is high and deeply dissected, rising to 3,760 m (12,336 feet), the western coastal plains are flat, and the eastern plateau falls away from 1,800 m (5,900 feet) towards the desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The climate is semi-arid to arid, with rain in spring and summer governed by the Red Sea Convergence Zone from March to May and by the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone from July to September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The coastal plains take under 200 mm (8 inches) a year and the eastern plateau under 100 mm (4 inches), while the western and southern mountain slopes get 300 to 500 mm (12 to 20 inches) and in places more than 1,000 mm (39 inches); desert accounts for 57 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Since the 19th century the country has been divided along a north-south line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The north belonged to the Ottoman Empire and became an independent republic after that empire collapsed in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The south fell under British influence from 1839, when Britain took the port of Aden, and the British withdrew in November 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two were formally unified as the Republic of Yemen in 1990, and its constitution calls it an Arab, Islamic and independent sovereign state whose integrity is inviolable and no part of which may be ceded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yemen has held a seat at the United Nations since 30 September 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Yemen,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0352en/CA0352EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Yemen,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/yemen. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Yemen — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ye. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Yemen</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Yemen_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Yemen,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ye.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AD%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA.jpg/1920px-%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AD%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA.jpg","title":"Shibam, Yemen","desc":"Shibam.","credit":"Bkar6190, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86%D8%A9_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D8%A7%D9%85_%D8%AD%D8%B6%D8%B1%D9%85%D9%88%D8%AA.jpg","alt":"A view of Shibam."}},
{"id":"gw-039","num":39,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Morocco","answerText":"Morocco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1786</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1912–56</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Morocco"},"facts":[["Capital","Rabat"],["Population","38.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Casablanca"],["Area","446,550 km² (172,414 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Flag_of_Morocco.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Morocco.svg)","alt":"The flag of Morocco: a red field with a green five-pointed star outlined in the centre."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","morocco"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Morocco</b> stands at the far north-west of Africa, facing both the Atlantic Ocean and the Mediterranean Sea, with the Strait of Gibraltar between it and the European mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains define it: the Rif in the north and the Atlas — Middle, High and Anti — which reaches 4,165 m (13,665 feet) at Toubkal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plains run the length of the coast, narrow along the Mediterranean and broad along the Atlantic, while south-east of the Atlas the ground turns to desert and semi-desert plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is temperate in the north and tropical in the south but semi-arid to arid over most of the country, with a hot dry summer and a cold wet winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 346 mm (14 inches) a year, from 700 mm (28 inches) in the far north-west to 25 mm (1 inch) in the south-east and up to 2,000 mm (79 inches) on the high ground, and sunshine runs from 2,400 hours a year in the north to over 3,400 in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its living variety is unusual for the Mediterranean: more than 24,000 animal and 7,000 plant species, with a fifth of the vascular plants found nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The kingdom is old enough to have signed a treaty of peace and friendship at Marrakech in 1786, under the Sultan Sidi Muhammad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became a French protectorate in 1912, with parts of the country under Spanish control, including Tangier, which was later administered as an international zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was restored in 1956, and Morocco took its seat at the United Nations on 12 November of the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its constitution calls the country a constitutional, democratic, parliamentary and social monarchy, founded on the separation, balance and collaboration of the powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Maroc,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0203fr/CA0203FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Morocco,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/morocco. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Morocco — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ma. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Kingdom of Morocco</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Morocco_2011. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Morocco,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ma.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Ksar_A%C3%AFt_Benhaddou%2C_Marocco_%28%D8%A3%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8%2C_%E2%B4%B0%E2%B5%A2%E2%B5%9C_%E2%B5%83%E2%B4%B0%E2%B4%B7%E2%B4%B7%E2%B5%93%29.jpg/1920px-Ksar_A%C3%AFt_Benhaddou%2C_Marocco_%28%D8%A3%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8%2C_%E2%B4%B0%E2%B5%A2%E2%B5%9C_%E2%B5%83%E2%B4%B0%E2%B4%B7%E2%B4%B7%E2%B5%93%29.jpg","title":"Aït Benhaddou, Morocco","desc":"Aït Benhaddou.","credit":"Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ksar_A%C3%AFt_Benhaddou,_Marocco_(%D8%A3%D9%8A%D8%AA_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%AD%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%8C_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%BA%D8%B1%D8%A8,_%E2%B4%B0%E2%B5%A2%E2%B5%9C_%E2%B5%83%E2%B4%B0%E2%B4%B7%E2%B4%B7%E2%B5%93).jpg","alt":"A view of Aït Benhaddou."}},
{"id":"gw-520","num":520,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Bangkok","answerText":"Bangkok","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratifications</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 April 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 October 1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">SEATO seat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1954</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Thailand","dot":"Bangkok"},"facts":[["Country","Thailand"],["Population","10.4M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-0.3% (2015–2018)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","thailand"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Bangkok</b> is where the United States and Siam exchanged ratifications of a treaty of amity and commerce in April 1836, three years after signing it — the first treaty the Americans made with any Asian country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had been negotiated by Edmund Roberts, a special agent sent to the Far East under instructions to make treaties with Cochinchina, Siam and the powers of Arabia on the Red Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consular presence followed in 1859, when John Hassett Chandler became acting vice consul in charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American legation in the country opened in October 1882, when John A. Halderman presented his credentials as minister resident and consul general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was raised to an embassy only after the Second World War, Edwin F. Stanton becoming the first American ambassador in April 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The city became the headquarters of the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, formed in September 1954 by the United States, France, Britain, New Zealand, Australia, the Philippines, Pakistan and the kingdom itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organisation kept no military forces of its own but hosted joint exercises for its members every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As the threat it was built against shifted from invasion to internal subversion, it worked instead at strengthening the economies and living standards of the southeast Asian states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It sponsored meetings and exhibitions on cultural, religious and historical subjects, and its non-Asian members paid for fellowships for southeast Asian scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state whose capital this is entered the United Nations under the name Siam in the organisation’s first full year, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in November 1982, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Thailand,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/th.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Thailand,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/thailand. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Southeast Asia Treaty Organization (SEATO), 1954,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/seato. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Thailand and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/thailand_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Siam to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 101 (I), 15 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209878. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Bangkok_skyline_view_from_Mahanakhon_Tower.jpg/1920px-Bangkok_skyline_view_from_Mahanakhon_Tower.jpg","title":"Bangkok","desc":"Bangkok, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bigcitydata, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangkok_skyline_view_from_Mahanakhon_Tower.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bangkok."}},
{"id":"gw-040","num":40,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","answer":"Angola","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Portuguese ships</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1482</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lisbon coup</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1976</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Angola</b> lies in southern Africa on the Atlantic, its coast running some 1,600 km (1,000 miles) with a high interior plateau standing behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The province of Cabinda is separated from the rest of the country, sitting between the two Congos north of the mouth of the Congo river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers about 23 million hectares, some 18 per cent of the territory, and two fifths of the country carries woodland; savannah makes up most of the rest, with a small desert on the southern coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three agro-ecological zones divide it: a humid tropical north above 1,500 mm (59 inches) of rain and 22 °C (72 °F); a central plateau between 1,000 and 2,500 m (3,300 and 8,200 feet), at 1,250 to 1,500 mm (49 to 59 inches) and 18 to 20 °C (64 to 68 °F); and a dry south at about 200 mm (8 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,010 mm (40 inches) over the country, and the north and centre have two wet seasons, September to December and February to April, where the south has a single one from October to March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its biological diversity is among the richest in Africa, and the escarpment zone in particular is full of species found nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Portuguese ships reached the north in 1482 and met the Kingdom of the Congo, which then stretched from what is now Gabon down to the Kwanza river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Portugal took the coast by a series of treaties and wars through the 16th century and turned to the slave trade; many scholars hold that by the 19th century Angola was the largest source of slaves for the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Portugal secured the interior early in the 20th century and went on treating its African colonies as overseas provinces, so three independence movements emerged, and a military coup in Lisbon in 1974 led to the Alvor Accords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was granted on 11 November 1975 and the country joined the United Nations on 1 December 1976; its constitution calls it a democratic state based on the rule of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Angola","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Angola"},"facts":[["Capital","Luanda"],["Population","39.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Luanda"],["Area","1,246,700 km² (481,350 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","angola"],"sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Angola,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9862fr/I9862FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Angola,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/angola. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Angola — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ao. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Angola</i>, article 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Angola_2010. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Angola,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ao.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Flag_of_Angola.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Angola.svg)","alt":"The flag of Angola: red over black, with a yellow machete, cogwheel and star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Kalandula_waterfalls_of_the_Lucala-River_in_Malange%2C_Angola.JPG/1920px-Kalandula_waterfalls_of_the_Lucala-River_in_Malange%2C_Angola.JPG","title":"Kalandula Falls, Angola","desc":"Kalandula Falls.","credit":"Paulo César Santos, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalandula_waterfalls_of_the_Lucala-River_in_Malange,_Angola.JPG","alt":"A view of Kalandula Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-041","num":41,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ukraine","answerText":"Ukraine","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">USSR dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ukraine"},"facts":[["Capital","Kyiv"],["Population","39.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kyiv"],["Area","603,550 km² (233,030 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","ukraine"],"abstract":"<b>Ukraine</b> lies in eastern Europe with the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov along its southern coast, and it is the largest country lying wholly within the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lowland predominates, interrupted by modest elevations — the Volyn-Podolsk plateau in the west, the Dnipro ridge in the centre and the Donets ridge in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only two parts are mountainous: the Carpathians in the south-west, rising to Hoverla at 2,061 m (6,762 feet), and the Crimean mountains at 1,545 m (5,069 feet) along the southern coast of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four agro-climatic zones run across it, from a humid north-west averaging 600 mm (24 inches) of rain, through a warm forested steppe at 500 mm (20 inches) and a semi-arid steppe at 450 mm (18 inches), to an arid south at about 360 mm (14 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Precipitation averages 565 mm (22 inches) a year including snow, which matters in the west; January means run from −6 °C (21 °F) inland to 0 °C (32 °F) in the south, and July means from 17 to 23 °C (63 to 73 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Ukraine was a constituent republic of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared its independence within days of the failed coup against Mikhail Gorbachev in August 1991, as Belarus did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In early December its leaders met those of Russia and Belarus at Brest to form the Commonwealth of Independent States, and the Soviet flag came down over the Kremlin for the last time on 25 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its constitution declares Ukraine a sovereign and independent, democratic, social and law-based unitary state whose territory within its present border is indivisible and inviolable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has held a seat at the United Nations in its own right since 24 October 1945, more than four decades before that independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Ukraine,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0331en/CA0331EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ukraine,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ukraine. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Ukraine</i>, articles 1 and 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Ukraine_2019. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Ukraine,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ua.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Flag_of_Ukraine.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ukraine.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ukraine: two equal horizontal bands, blue above yellow."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/80-391-0151_Kyiv_St.Sophia%27s_Cathedral_RB_18_2_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-80-391-0151_Kyiv_St.Sophia%27s_Cathedral_RB_18_2_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv, Ukraine","desc":"Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv.","credit":"Rbrechko, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:80-391-0151_Kyiv_St.Sophia%27s_Cathedral_RB_18_2_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Saint Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv."}},
{"id":"gw-042","num":42,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Poland","answerText":"Poland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Partitions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1772, 1793, 1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 January 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Poland"},"facts":[["Capital","Warsaw"],["Population","37.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Warsaw"],["Area","312,696 km² (120,733 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","poland"],"abstract":"<b>Poland</b> at the height of its power was a commonwealth that included Lithuania, Belarus and much of Ukraine, whose nobility elected the king and in which a single dissenting vote — the liberum veto — stopped any legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That arrangement invited foreign intervention and civil war, and left the country open to stronger neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Austria, Russia and Prussia took a first partition of it in 1772; the reforms behind the constitution of 1791 brought a second in 1793, and the suppression of a revolt a third in 1795.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It then vanished from the map of Europe until 1918, and the Grand Duchy of Warsaw that Napoleon made out of Prussian Poland in 1807 did not outlive his defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A republic was proclaimed on 3 November 1918, Józef Piłsudski became head of state eleven days later, and the pianist Ignacy Jan Paderewski formed a cabinet as prime minister that January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The thirteenth of Woodrow Wilson's Fourteen Points had called for an independent Poland with access to the sea, and the United States recognised the provisional government on 22 January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Versailles fixed the western border and cut a corridor to the Baltic along the Vistula, leaving Danzig, which had a German majority, a free city supervised by the League of Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The League proposed an eastern ethnic frontier known as the Curzon Line, but the country went to war with the Soviet Union in 1920 over its boundaries of 1772, and the Treaty of Riga left the border well east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Germany invaded on 1 September 1939 the American ambassador followed the government to France and then to London, and an embassy reopened in Warsaw only in July 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has held a United Nations seat since 1945, entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1967, the World Trade Organization in 1995 and the European Union in 2004, and its recorded population has fallen from 38.0 million in 2015 to 36.4 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Poland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pl.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Poland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/poland. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Poland and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/poland_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Poland – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/poland_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Poland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/POL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Flag_of_Poland.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Poland.svg)","alt":"The flag of Poland: two equal horizontal bands, white above red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Wawel_%284%29.jpg/1920px-Wawel_%284%29.jpg","title":"Wawel Castle, Poland","desc":"Wawel Castle.","credit":"Monika Towiańska, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wawel_(4).jpg","alt":"A view of Wawel Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-521","num":521,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"London","answerText":"London","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1 June 1785</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 June 1893</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First UN Assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United Kingdom","dot":"London"},"facts":[["Country","United Kingdom"],["Population","9.20M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+22.7% (2001–2021)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","united kingdom"],"abstract":"<b>London</b> is where the United States first kept a mission in Europe: diplomatic relations and the American Legation were established on 1 June 1785, when John Adams presented his credentials as minister plenipotentiary to George III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Adams found the reception so cool that he closed the legation in 1788, and the post then stood vacant for four years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate American consulate opened in the city in 1790 and stayed open, through everything that followed, until 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations broke when the United States declared war on 18 June 1812, though the chargé d'affaires Jonathan Russell did not close the legation until the end of July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Quincy Adams, son of the first minister, presented his own credentials in the city on 8 August 1815, and the mission has run without a break since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It became an embassy on 22 June 1893, when Thomas Bayard presented his credentials to the Court of St James.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1930 the city hosted the third of five conferences held to limit the fleets of the largest naval powers — a series that began at Washington in 1921 and closed with a second London meeting in 1935 — at which Britain, the United States, Japan, France and Italy tried to revise and extend the Five Power Treaty of 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1940 it held the exiled governments of Poland, Belgium, Czechoslovakia, Greece, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Norway and Yugoslavia, and one American ambassador was accredited to all of them at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first session of the United Nations General Assembly opened here on 10 January 1946 and adopted its first resolution, establishing a commission on the problems raised by the discovery of atomic energy, at a plenary meeting a fortnight later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That session resumed at Flushing Meadow in New York the same year; the country whose capital this is has held a United Nations seat since 1945 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gb.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: United Kingdom,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/united-kingdom. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The London Naval Conference, 1930,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/london-naval-conf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Poland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/poland. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Establishment of a Commission to Deal with the Problems Raised by the Discovery of Atomic Energy,” resolution 1 (I), 24 January 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209570. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The United Kingdom and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/united_kingdom_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/The_Thames_and_London_Skyline_from_Waterloo_Bridge_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4537341.jpg/1920px-The_Thames_and_London_Skyline_from_Waterloo_Bridge_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4537341.jpg","title":"London","desc":"London, seen from a distance.","credit":"Anthony Parkes, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Thames_and_London_Skyline_from_Waterloo_Bridge_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4537341.jpg","alt":"A wide view of London."}},
{"id":"gw-043","num":43,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Uzbekistan","answerText":"Uzbekistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">USSR dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uzbekistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Tashkent"],["Population","37.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tashkent"],["Area","448,969 km² (173,348 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","uzbekistan"],"abstract":"<b>Uzbekistan</b> is a landlocked country in Central Asia, with the remnant of the Aral Sea in its north-west, mountains along its eastern rim and the Kyzylkum desert filling much of the ground between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three zones divide it: desert, steppe and semi-arid country over 60 per cent of the land, mainly in the centre and west; the fertile valleys skirting the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, the Fergana among them; and mountains in the east reaching about 4,500 m (14,800 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is continental and dry, rain averaging 264 mm (10 inches) a year, from under 97 mm (4 inches) in the north-west to 425 mm (17 inches) in the mountains of the centre and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls in winter, mostly between October and April; July brings 42 to 47 °C (108 to 117 °F) on the plains, and January −11 °C (12 °F) to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Frost between late September and April holds most of the country to one crop a year, and only about 18 per cent of the cultivable land is actually cultivated because there is not enough water for more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Aral Sea at its northern edge has been shrinking for decades as the two rivers that fed it were drawn off for irrigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measurements of its southern basin found the surface had fallen 3.2 m (10 feet) in the seven years between 2006 and 2013 alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country was a constituent republic of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, and gained its independence in August 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The unsuccessful coup against Mikhail Gorbachev that month sealed the union's fate, and it was formally dissolved before the year was out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Uzbekistan joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Uzbekistan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2012, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0372en/CA0372EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uzbekistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uzbekistan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]","Elena Roget et al., “The Internal Seiche Field in the Changing South Aral Sea (2006–2013),” <i>Hydrology and Earth System Sciences</i> 21, no. 2 (2017): 1093–1105, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-21-1093-2017. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Uzbekistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/uz.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Flag_of_Uzbekistan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uzbekistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Uzbekistan: blue, white and green bands separated by thin red fimbriations, with a white crescent and twelve stars in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg","title":"Registan, Uzbekistan","desc":"Registan.","credit":"Registan_-_Gusjer.jpg : Gustavo Jeronimo from Aranjuez, Spain derivative work: MrPanyGoff, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RegistanSquare_Samarkand.jpg","alt":"A view of Registan."}},
{"id":"gw-044","num":44,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malaysia","answerText":"Malaysia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1957</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malaysia"},"facts":[["Capital","Kuala Lumpur"],["Population","36.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kuala Lumpur"],["Area","330,621 km² (127,653 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","malaysia"],"abstract":"<b>Malaysia</b> is two regions divided by the South China Sea: the peninsula in the west, lying between Thailand and Singapore, and the states of Sabah and Sarawak on the island of Borneo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those two states occupy the northern third of Borneo, and on the peninsula a mountainous spine, the Banjaran Titiwangsa, runs the whole length of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than 60 per cent of the country is rainforest, and the rest is mountain, mangrove swamp, low plain and sandy beach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It lies entirely in the equatorial zone, and two monsoons govern the climate: the north-easterly from October to March, which brings heavy rain to the peninsula's east coast and the wettest season to Sabah and Sarawak, and the drier south-westerly from May to September, with heavy rainfall in the April between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temperature holds at about 26 °C (79 °F) all year and rain averages 2,875 mm (113 inches), varying mainly with altitude — the Cameron Highlands sit at 18 °C (64 °F) with over 2,500 mm (98 inches) — while Sabah and Sarawak are wetter than the peninsula and the humidity holds near 80 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Federation of Malaya, previously part of the British Empire, became independent on 31 August 1957 under its prime minister Tunku Abdul Rahman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It took its seat at the United Nations seventeen days later, on 17 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The constitution names the federation Malaysia in both Malay and English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It lists thirteen states: Johore, Kedah, Kelantan, Malacca, Negeri Sembilan, Pahang, Penang, Perak, Perlis, Sabah, Sarawak, Selangor and Terengganu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The states on Borneo entered later than those on the peninsula, and the constitution still dates their territories from the day Malaysia itself was formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Malaysia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0216en/CA0216EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Malaysia,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/malaysia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malaysia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malaysia. [Open access]","<i>Federal Constitution of Malaysia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Malaysia_2007. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Malaysia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/my.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Flag_of_Malaysia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malaysia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Malaysia: fourteen red and white stripes with a blue canton bearing a yellow crescent and fourteen-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Petronas_Tower_spire.JPG/1920px-Petronas_Tower_spire.JPG","title":"Petronas Towers, Malaysia","desc":"Petronas Towers.","credit":"Descon7, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petronas_Tower_spire.JPG","alt":"A view of Petronas Towers."}},
{"id":"gw-045","num":45,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saudi Arabia","answerText":"Saudi Arabia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oil embargo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saudi Arabia"},"facts":[["Capital","Riyadh"],["Population","35.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Riyadh"],["Area","about 2,150,000 km² (830,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","saudi arabia"],"abstract":"<b>Saudi Arabia</b> is by far the largest country on the Arabian Peninsula, holding most of its interior, with the Red Sea along its western coast and the Persian Gulf along its eastern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Gulf coast runs 480 km (300 miles) and its Red Sea coast 1,750 km (1,090 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physiographic units make it up: the Western Mountains, called the Arabian Shield, rising to 2,000 m (6,600 feet) and cut by deep valleys; the Central Hills at 900 to 1,800 m (3,000 to 5,900 feet); the desert regions east of them at 200 to 900 m (700 to 3,000 feet), full of sand dunes; and the coastal strips, of which the Tahama Plain in the south is the important part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies in the tropical and subtropical desert belt and almost all of it is arid; cloudless skies make the temperature extreme, over 50 °C (122 °F) in the central summer and near freezing on winter nights, with weeks of snow in the mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain runs between 100 and 200 mm (4 and 8 inches) in the north and below 100 mm (4 inches) further south, though the higher west and south can take 500 mm (20 inches); over the whole country it averages 114 mm (4.5 inches) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state was known as the Kingdom of Hejaz and Nejd and its Dependencies until a decree of 18 September 1932 changed its name to the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Basic Law calls it a sovereign Arab Islamic state whose constitution is the Qur'an and the Prophet's Sunnah and whose language is Arabic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saudi Arabia was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As an Arab member of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries it took part in the embargo of 1973, which banned petroleum exports to several countries and cut oil production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Several years of bargaining between the oil-producing states and the oil companies had already unsettled a pricing system decades old, which made the embargo bite harder than it otherwise would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Saudi Arabia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0220en/CA0220EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saudi Arabia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saudi-arabia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Oil Embargo, 1973–1974,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/oil-embargo. [Open access]","<i>Basic Law of Governance of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Saudi_Arabia_2013. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Saudi Arabia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sa.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Flag_of_Saudi_Arabia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saudi_Arabia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Saudi Arabia: a green field bearing the shahada in white Thuluth script above a white sword."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Mada%27in_Saleh_2017.jpg/1920px-Mada%27in_Saleh_2017.jpg","title":"Hegra (Mada'in Salih), Saudi Arabia","desc":"Hegra (Mada'in Salih).","credit":"Ahmad AlHasanat, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mada%27in_Saleh_2017.jpg","alt":"A view of Hegra (Mada'in Salih)."}},
{"id":"gw-522","num":522,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the official capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dodoma","answerText":"Dodoma","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Union formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1964</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tanzania","dot":"Dodoma"},"facts":[["Country","Tanzania"],["Population","262k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","3rd largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tanzania"],"abstract":"<b>Dodoma</b> is the official capital of a country whose government has never entirely moved there, and the two international bodies that keep country profiles record the split in different ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat lists the capital as two cities at once, one marked acting and this one marked official; the United Nations Statistics Division simply gives this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits inland on the central plateau, several hundred kilometres from the Indian Ocean port where the state's foreign missions and much of its business remain, which is what the word acting in the Commonwealth's entry is doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the United Nations count it holds about a quarter of a million people, which makes it one of the smallest capitals of any country of comparable size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The designation has not pulled the embassies after it: the United States established diplomatic relations at the coastal city in December 1961, by raising its consulate general there to embassy rank, and has kept its mission on the coast ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country it serves was Tanganyika when it became independent from British administration as a United Nations trust territory, and President John F. Kennedy sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Julius Nyerere on 9 December 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted Tanganyika at its 1078th plenary meeting five days after that message, so the state took its United Nations seat within a week of ceasing to be a trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mainland state joined with the newly independent Zanzibar in 1964 to make the united republic that exists now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 9 December 1961, the day of independence itself, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in the same year, the Secretariat's own entry dating the accession to 1961 and attaching the reason, independence from Britain, to the date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Republic of Tanzania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tz.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “United Republic of Tanzania,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/united-republic-tanzania. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tanzania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tanzania. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Tanganyika to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1667 (XVI), 14 December 1961, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205649. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tanzania and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tanzania_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Central_Dodoma.JPG/1920px-Central_Dodoma.JPG","title":"Dodoma","desc":"Dodoma, seen from a distance.","credit":"Victor Mikheev, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Dodoma.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Dodoma."}},
{"id":"gw-751","num":751,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the acting capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dar es Salaam","answerText":"Dar es Salaam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy bombed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council response</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tanzania","dot":"Dar es Salaam"},"facts":[["Country","Tanzania"],["Population","4.71M (2016)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+8.0% (2012–2016)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tanzania"],"abstract":"<b>Dar es Salaam</b> is the seat from which its country is actually governed and where its foreign missions sit, although another city inland holds the official designation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat records the arrangement plainly, listing the capital as two cities, this one marked acting and the other marked official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate general stood here before independence, and on 9 December 1961 it was raised to embassy rank, with William R. Duggan left in charge as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was the same day President John F. Kennedy sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Julius Nyerere recognising Tanganyika, until then under British administration as a United Nations trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The embassy has stayed in the city through the union with Zanzibar in 1964 and through the designation of the inland capital, so the working seat and the official one have been apart for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 7 August 1998 that embassy and the American embassy at Nairobi were bombed within minutes of each other, in the attacks that put both cities into the Security Council's record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council met six days later and adopted a resolution strongly condemning the attacks in Kenya and Tanzania, calling on all states to help bring those responsible to justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The State Department's own account of the relationship says that relations between the two countries, cordial since independence, improved further after the bombings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on the day of its independence and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened in 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country's people are counted at just over 70 million, a figure that has risen by more than a third in a decade, and the United Nations files it under Eastern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United Republic of Tanzania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tz.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “United Republic of Tanzania,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/united-republic-tanzania. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tanzania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tanzania. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1189 (1998),” adopted at its 3915th meeting, 13 August 1998, S/RES/1189(1998), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/258404. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tanzania and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tanzania_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Dar_Es_Salaam_Skyline_%2834011488084%29.jpg","title":"Dar es Salaam","desc":"Dar es Salaam, seen from a distance.","credit":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dar_Es_Salaam_Skyline_(34011488084).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Dar es Salaam."}},
{"id":"gw-046","num":46,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mozambique","answerText":"Mozambique","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mozambique"},"facts":[["Capital","Maputo"],["Population","36.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Maputo"],["Area","799,380 km² (308,642 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mozambique"],"abstract":"<b>Mozambique</b> lies on the east coast of southern Africa, facing the Indian Ocean across the Mozambique Channel from Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its land borders run 4,445 km (2,762 miles) and its coastline 2,515 km (1,563 miles), with beaches, coral reefs and islands along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three divisions make up the country: a coastal belt over about 44 per cent of it, a middle plateau between 200 and 1,000 m (700 and 3,300 feet) over 29 per cent, and highlands around 1,000 m (3,300 feet) north of the Zambezi over 27 per cent, rising to Monte Binga at 2,436 m (7,992 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate runs from tropical and subtropical in the north and centre to semi-arid steppe and arid desert in the south, which is also the coolest part, between 19 and 30 °C (66 and 86 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,032 mm (41 inches) a year and falls from October to April, from 800 to 1,000 mm (31 to 39 inches) along the coast and over 2,000 mm (79 inches) in the north, down to 400 mm (16 inches) in the far south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Five phytogeographical regions meet in the country — miombo, mopane, undifferentiated woodland and coastal mosaic among them — and three recognised biodiversity hotspots lie within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Gorongosa mountains, the inselbergs of Quirimbas and the Chimanimani massif are the places that matter most for that variety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The territory was under Portuguese sovereignty for the better part of five centuries before it was a state of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The People's Republic of Mozambique came into being in 1975 under its first president Samora Machel, and joined the United Nations on 16 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in 1995, following its first democratic elections, although it had never been ruled by Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Mozambique,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9805en/I9805EN.pdf. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Mozambique,” Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/mozambique. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mozambique,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mozambique. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Mozambique — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=mz. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Mozambique,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mz.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Flag_of_Mozambique.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mozambique.svg)","alt":"The flag of Mozambique: green, black and yellow bands with a red hoist triangle bearing a star, a book, a hoe and a rifle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/ISS059-E-92404_-_View_of_Mozambique.jpg/1920px-ISS059-E-92404_-_View_of_Mozambique.jpg","title":"Bazaruto Archipelago, Mozambique","desc":"Bazaruto Archipelago.","credit":"Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, NASA Johnson Space Center, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS059-E-92404_-_View_of_Mozambique.jpg","alt":"A view of Bazaruto Archipelago."}},
{"id":"gw-047","num":47,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ghana","answerText":"Ghana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 March 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 March 1957</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ghana"},"facts":[["Capital","Accra"],["Population","35.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Accra"],["Area","238,537 km² (92,100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","ghana"],"abstract":"<b>Ghana</b> lies on the west coast of Africa, some 670 km (420 miles) from north to south and 560 km (350 miles) from east to west, with the Gulf of Guinea and the Atlantic along its southern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land is undulating and of low relief, most of its slopes under 1 per cent, and its highest point is Mount Afadjato in the Akwapim-Togo Ranges at 880 m (2,890 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five regions divide it: the low plains of the south, the Ashanti Uplands, the folded Akwapim-Togo Ranges, the Volta Basin over about 45 per cent of the ground on poor sandstone soils, and the dissected high plains of the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is warm and humid, rain averaging 1,187 mm (47 inches) a year and mean temperatures running from 26.1 °C (79 °F) near the sea to 28.9 °C (84 °F) in the far north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three river systems drain it, the Volta over 70 per cent of the country and the south-western Bia, Tano, Ankobra and Pra over 22 per cent, and about seven tenths of the land is subject to moderate or severe sheet and gully erosion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tropical high forest and savannah between them hold some 3,600 plant species, among them the West African cycad, the only gymnosperm known to grow wild here, along with 728 birds and 225 mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was known as the Gold Coast and belonged to the British Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was proclaimed independent on 6 March 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations two days after that, on 8 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution makes the sovereign state a unitary republic, made up of the regions that existed before the constitution came into force together with the territorial sea and the air space above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Ghana,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9749en/I9749EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ghana,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ghana. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Ghana</i>, article 4, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Ghana_1996. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Ghana — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gh.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Ghana — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=gh. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Flag_of_Ghana.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ghana.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ghana: red, gold and green horizontal bands with a black five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Cape_Coast_Castle%2C_Cape_Coast%2C_Ghana.JPG","title":"Cape Coast Castle, Ghana","desc":"Cape Coast Castle.","credit":"Rjruiziii, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Coast_Castle,_Cape_Coast,_Ghana.JPG","alt":"A view of Cape Coast Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-048","num":48,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Peru","answerText":"Peru","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Peru"},"facts":[["Capital","Lima"],["Population","35.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Lima"],["Area","1,285,216 km² (496,225 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","peru"],"abstract":"<b>Peru</b> occupies the centre-west of South America, with the Pacific along its whole western side and the Amazon basin opening out beyond the mountains to its east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three regions run the length of it: an extremely dry coastal Costa, the Andean Sierra, and the Selva, the forested Amazonian east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the coast the rainfall is under 40 mm (1.6 inches) a year in the south and centre and between 50 and 200 mm (2 and 8 inches) in the far north, and the winter fogs known as garúa are enough to keep parts of that desert green.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Sierra the daily swing of temperature is far larger than the seasonal one, as much as 22 °C (40 °F) in a single day, with a clear dry season from April to October and a wet one from November to March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,738 mm (68 inches) over the country and three watersheds share it: the Atlantic slope, 74 per cent of the territory and 97.2 per cent of the water, the Pacific slope at 22 and 2.2 per cent, and the closed basin of Lake Titicaca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its wild flora and fauna have been counted at 20,585 and 5,585 species, with the number of threatened ones rising alongside that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under Spanish sovereignty until independence was proclaimed in July 1821 under General San Martín.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its record since has included territorial disputes with its neighbours, periods of military rule and coups that overthrew civilian constitutional government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has held a seat at the United Nations since 31 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The constitution now in force opens by making the defence of the human person and respect for his dignity the supreme purpose of society and of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Perú,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0447es/CA0447ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Peru,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/peru. [Open access]","<i>Political Constitution of Peru</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Peru_2021. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Peru — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pe.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Peru — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=pe. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Flag_of_Peru.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Peru.svg)","alt":"The flag of Peru: three vertical bands, red, white and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Machu_Picchu%2C_2023_%28012%29.jpg/1920px-Machu_Picchu%2C_2023_%28012%29.jpg","title":"Machu Picchu, Peru","desc":"Machu Picchu.","credit":"Draceane, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machu_Picchu,_2023_(012).jpg","alt":"A view of Machu Picchu."}},
{"id":"gw-523","num":523,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Paris","answerText":"Paris","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pact signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 August 1928</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Government left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Summit collapsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"France","dot":"Paris"},"facts":[["Country","France"],["Population","11.0M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-2.0% (2020–2023)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","france"],"abstract":"<b>Paris</b> has given its name to more than one agreement, and the plainest case is the Kellogg-Briand Pact, an attempt to outlaw war altogether that fifteen nations signed there on 27 August 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The State Department's own account calls it the Pact of Paris for the city in which it was signed, and says it did little to stop the militarism of the 1930s or to prevent the war that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its origin was a proposal by the French foreign minister Aristide Briand for a bilateral agreement with the United States, published as an open letter in April 1927.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decade earlier the city had hosted the conference that produced the Treaty of Versailles, at which four powers dominated the settlement of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both episodes made the city a byword for a certain kind of diplomacy, conducted among great powers and named after the place rather than the parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It has also been abandoned: Germany invaded on 15 May 1940 and the French government withdrew from the city to Bordeaux.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Parliament met at Vichy that July and granted Marshal Pétain full and extraordinary powers, and the American embassy moved to be near the new regime rather than stay in the occupied capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A summit was to open here in May 1960 to discuss divided Germany, arms control and a general relaxation of tension between the Soviet Union and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American U-2 was shot down over Soviet territory on 1 May, and when President Eisenhower refused to promise an end to the overflights the Soviet delegation walked out just as the meeting was about to begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 11 million people in the city and its surroundings, a sixth of the country's whole population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “France,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: France,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/france. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Kellogg-Briand Pact, 1928,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/kellogg. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “U-2 Overflights and the Capture of Francis Gary Powers, 1960,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/u2-incident. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Paris_skyline_from_the_observation_deck_of_the_Montparnasse_tower%2C_July_2015.jpg/1920px-Paris_skyline_from_the_observation_deck_of_the_Montparnasse_tower%2C_July_2015.jpg","title":"Paris","desc":"Paris, seen from a distance.","credit":"Joe deSousa, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_skyline_from_the_observation_deck_of_the_Montparnasse_tower,_July_2015.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Paris."}},
{"id":"gw-049","num":49,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Madagascar","answerText":"Madagascar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Madagascar"},"facts":[["Capital","Antananarivo"],["Population","33.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Antananarivo"],["Area","587,041 km² (226,658 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","madagascar"],"abstract":"<b>Madagascar</b> is an island in the Indian Ocean whose broken relief sets its central highlands, rising to 2,876 m (9,436 feet), against its coasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two escarpments running north to south, the Angavo in the west and the Betsimisaraka in the east, drop the eastern side abruptly to the sea, where a narrow coastal strip is fringed with lagoons behind a coral barrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The west is two broad sedimentary basins, the Boeny in the north and the Menabe in the south, tilted gently towards the Mozambique Channel, and every river on the island rises in those central highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,513 mm (60 inches) a year and is very unevenly spread: the humid tropical east takes more than 1,500 mm (59 inches) and the north-east can pass 3,000 mm (118 inches), while the dry west has under 800 mm (31 inches) and the semi-arid far south under 400 mm (16 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trade winds and the monsoon divide the year into a hot wet season from October to March and a cooler drier one from April to September, and tropical cyclones strike most years, usually between December and March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is a megadiverse island: some 12,000 vascular plants of which 96 per cent grow nowhere else, 389 reptiles at 90 per cent endemic, 278 amphibians at 100 per cent, and 104 species and subspecies of lemur found only here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> France assumed sovereignty over the island as a colony in 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence returned in 1960, when the state was known as the Malagasy Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution declares the Malagasy people a nation organised as a sovereign, unitary, republican and secular state bearing the name Republic of Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Madagascar,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0199fr/CA0199FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Madagascar,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/madagascar. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Madagascar</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Madagascar_2010. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Madagascar — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mg.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Madagascar — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=mg. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Flag_of_Madagascar.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Madagascar.svg)","alt":"The flag of Madagascar: a white vertical band at the hoist beside horizontal red and green bands."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Adansonia_grandidieri_Pat_Hooper.jpg","title":"Avenue of the Baobabs, Madagascar","desc":"Avenue of the Baobabs.","credit":"Pat Hooper from Chicago, IL, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adansonia_grandidieri_Pat_Hooper.jpg","alt":"A view of Avenue of the Baobabs."}},
{"id":"gw-050","num":50,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Côte d'Ivoire","answerText":"Côte d'Ivoire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Côte d'Ivoire"},"facts":[["Capital","Yamoussoukro"],["Population","33.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Abidjan"],["Area","322,462 km² (124,503 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","cote divoire"],"abstract":"<b>Côte d'Ivoire</b> sits in the humid intertropical belt of West Africa, with 550 km (340 miles) of coast on the Gulf of Guinea and a wedge of territory reaching north into the savannah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its relief is gentle, mostly plateau between 100 and 400 m (330 and 1,300 feet), broken only in the west by a few summits above 1,000 m (3,300 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A coastal sedimentary basin makes up about 3 per cent of the ground and crystalline basement the other 97, which is why its soils are mostly ferrallitic and ferruginous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three agro-ecological zones divide it: a Guinean south over half the country, wetter than 1,500 mm (59 inches) a year and holding almost all the forest, where coffee and cocoa take two thirds of the cultivated ground; a Sudano-Guinean belt of 1,200 to 1,500 mm (47 to 59 inches); and a Sudanian savannah in the north of 900 to 1,200 mm (35 to 47 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four rivers drain it — the Cavally in the west, the Sassandra, which rises in Guinea, the Bandama, 1,050 km (650 miles) long over a basin of 97,000 km² (37,000 sq mi), and the Comoé in the east, which rises in Burkina Faso.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A third of the forest cover has gone since the 1960s, first to timber production and now chiefly to farming, with traditional shifting cultivation and charcoal among the activities behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under French sovereignty until it became independent on 7 August 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations six weeks later, on 20 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The state was called the Ivory Coast until 1986, when it took its French name instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its constitution opens by recognising the rights, freedoms and duties it sets out, and by undertaking to take every measure needed to make them effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Côte d'Ivoire,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9921fr/I9921FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cote d'Ivoire,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cote-divoire. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Côte d'Ivoire</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Cote_DIvoire_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Côte d'Ivoire — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ci.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Côte d'Ivoire — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ci. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg)","alt":"The flag of Côte d'Ivoire: three vertical bands, orange, white and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Basilique_notre_Dame_de_la_Paix_de_Yamoussoukro_5.jpg/1920px-Basilique_notre_Dame_de_la_Paix_de_Yamoussoukro_5.jpg","title":"the Basilica of Our Lady of Peace, Côte d'Ivoire","desc":"The Basilica of Our Lady of Peace at Yamoussoukro.","credit":"Didierwiki, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilique_notre_Dame_de_la_Paix_de_Yamoussoukro_5.jpg","alt":"A domed basilica with a colonnade above open lawns."}},
{"id":"gw-524","num":524,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the administrative capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Pretoria","answerText":"Pretoria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Credentials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 September 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa","dot":"Pretoria"},"facts":[["Country","South Africa"],["Population","2.50M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","4th largest"],["Population growth","+13.8% (2018–2022)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south africa"],"abstract":"<b>Pretoria</b> is where the President and the Cabinet of South Africa meet, which is what makes it the administrative capital of a country with three of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations footnotes its own country profile to record the division, naming this city the administrative capital, Cape Town the legislative one and Bloemfontein the judiciary one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not the first seat of the American mission: the United States opened a legation at Cape Town on 19 December 1929, appointing the consul general Ralph J. Totten as minister resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Totten presented those credentials in February 1930, and the legation was moved here six months later, on 20 June, with Totten promoted to envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He presented his new credentials on 8 September 1930, and the American mission has been seated in this city ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Department of State announced in December 1948 that the two countries' legations would be raised to embassies, and North Winship presented his credentials as the first American ambassador on 23 March 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country around it was created on 31 May 1910 by joining four colonies into a self-governing dominion whose foreign relations London still controlled, and it declared itself a republic on the same date in 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat records that the country joined in 1931, left in 1961 and rejoined in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> While the government seated here enforced apartheid, the General Assembly adopted at its 2185th plenary meeting, on 30 November 1973, a convention declaring that system a crime against humanity, annexed to the resolution itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city holds about 2.5 million people by the United Nations count, and the country has held a seat there since November 1945 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “South Africa and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/south_africa_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “International Convention on the Suppression and Punishment of the Crime of Apartheid,” resolution 3068 (XXVIII), 30 November 1973, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/191217. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/View_of_Pretoria%2C_Tshwane%2C_Gauteng%2C_South_Africa_-_20308763589.jpg/1920px-View_of_Pretoria%2C_Tshwane%2C_Gauteng%2C_South_Africa_-_20308763589.jpg","title":"Pretoria","desc":"Pretoria, seen from a distance.","credit":"South African Tourism, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Pretoria,_Tshwane,_Gauteng,_South_Africa_-_20308763589.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Pretoria."}},
{"id":"gw-752","num":752,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the legislative capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cape Town","answerText":"Cape Town","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Credentials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1930</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa","dot":"Cape Town"},"facts":[["Country","South Africa"],["Population","3.78M (2021)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+6.1% (2022–2025)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south africa"],"abstract":"<b>Cape Town</b> is where the South African Parliament sits, which is what makes it the legislative capital of a country whose executive and judicial seats are elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat states the division in that form, giving each city the institution that earns it its title rather than a bare label.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations records the same three-way split in a footnote to its country profile, though it names only the administrative capital in the field itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This was the first seat of American diplomacy in the country: a legation was established here on 19 December 1929, when the consul general Ralph J. Totten was appointed minister resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Totten presented his credentials to the Union government on 18 February 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Four months later the legation was moved inland to Pretoria and Totten was promoted, so the American mission's stay in this city lasted half a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country had been made on 31 May 1910 by joining four colonies into a self-governing dominion of the British Empire, sovereign at home while London kept control of its foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 called the United Kingdom and the dominions autonomous communities equal in status and in no way subordinate to one another, and the Statute of Westminster gave that formula the force of law in December 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States had already recognised the union on 5 November 1929, when President Herbert Hoover accepted the credentials of Eric Hendrik Louw as its first minister in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in June 1948 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since it opened; in between, the General Assembly adopted at its 2185th plenary meeting on 30 November 1973 a convention declaring apartheid a crime against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “South Africa and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/south_africa_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “International Convention on the Suppression and Punishment of the Crime of Apartheid,” resolution 3068 (XXVIII), 30 November 1973, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/191217. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Cape_Town_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Cape_Town_Skyline.jpg","title":"Cape Town","desc":"Cape Town, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bernard303, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Town_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Cape Town."}},
{"id":"gw-753","num":753,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the judicial capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bloemfontein","answerText":"Bloemfontein","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1910</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rejoined Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Africa","dot":"Bloemfontein"},"facts":[["Country","South Africa"],["Population","256k (2011)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south africa"],"abstract":"<b>Bloemfontein</b> is the seat of the Supreme Court of Appeal of South Africa, and that court is the whole reason it counts as one of the country's three capitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat sets the division out institution by institution: parliament at Cape Town makes that the legislative capital, the President and Cabinet at Pretoria make that the administrative one, and this court makes this the judicial one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations records the same three-way arrangement in a footnote to its country profile, calling this city the judiciary capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the least visible of the three from outside the country, since the American record of its own missions names the other two cities and never this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation went to Cape Town in 1929 and to Pretoria in 1930, and the embassy that grew out of it has stayed in the second of those cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country the court serves was assembled on 31 May 1910 out of four colonies, as a dominion sovereign at home whose external relations London kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Exactly 51 years later, on 31 May 1961, it declared itself a republic, and the American ambassador was reaccredited to the new state the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Secretariat records that the country joined the Commonwealth in 1931 on independence from Britain, left it in that same republican year of 1961, and rejoined in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between those dates the General Assembly adopted, at its 2185th plenary meeting on 30 November 1973, a convention declaring apartheid a crime against humanity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country has held a United Nations seat since 7 November 1945, belonged to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from June 1948, and has been in the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “South Africa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/za.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “South Africa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/south-africa. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Africa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-africa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “South Africa and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/south_africa_e.htm. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “International Convention on the Suppression and Punishment of the Crime of Apartheid,” resolution 3068 (XXVIII), 30 November 1973, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/191217. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Bloemfontein_skyline.jpg/1920px-Bloemfontein_skyline.jpg","title":"Bloemfontein","desc":"Bloemfontein, seen from a distance.","credit":"ShupingM, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloemfontein_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bloemfontein."}},
{"id":"gw-051","num":51,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nepal","answerText":"Nepal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nepal"},"facts":[["Capital","Kathmandu"],["Population","30.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kathmandu"],["Area","147,181 km² (56,827 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","nepal"],"abstract":"<b>Nepal</b> lies entirely within the Ganges basin, a landlocked country on the southern flank of the Himalaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With fifteen peaks above 7,000 m (23,000 feet), Everest among them at 8,848 m (29,029 feet), it is one of the highest countries on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three bands cross it within a width of only 145 to 241 km (90 to 150 miles): the high Himalaya over about a quarter of the land, the hills and mountain slopes over 56 per cent, and the plain called the terai, below 300 m (1,000 feet), over a fifth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That relief packs tropical, meso-thermal, micro-thermal, taiga and tundra climates into a very short horizontal span.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rain averages 1,500 mm (59 inches) a year, three quarters of it brought by the summer monsoon between June and September, and the extremes recorded run from 5,581 mm (220 inches) at Lumle in 1990 to 116 mm (4.6 inches) at Jomsom in 1988.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> More than 6,000 rivers drain the country, every one of them into the Ganges, through five basins that run from the Mahakali in the west to the Kosi in the east, which alone carries about 47 km³ (11 cubic miles) of water a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers about 29 per cent of the land and shrubland and degraded forest another tenth, over five physiographic zones running from the tropical to the nival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kingdom of Nepal was ruled by a monarchy into the 21st century, and a ten-year insurgency was fought against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The monarchy was abolished, a representative government created and that Maoist insurgency brought to an end in 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its constitution now calls the country an independent, indivisible, sovereign, secular, inclusive, democratic, socialism-oriented federal democratic republic, and it has held a seat at the United Nations since 14 December 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Nepal,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0402en/CA0402EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nepal,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nepal. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Nepal</i>, article 4, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Nepal_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Nepal — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/np.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Nepal — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=np. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/Flag_of_Nepal.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nepal.svg)","alt":"The flag of Nepal: two stacked crimson pennants with a blue border, bearing a white moon and sun."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/The_Annapurna_range_from_Pokhara.jpg/1920px-The_Annapurna_range_from_Pokhara.jpg","title":"the Annapurna range, Nepal","desc":"The Annapurna range from Pokhara.","credit":"Jmhullot, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Annapurna_range_from_Pokhara.jpg","alt":"Snow peaks rising beyond a lake and green hills."}},
{"id":"gw-052","num":52,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cameroon","answerText":"Cameroon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 February 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cameroon"},"facts":[["Capital","Yaoundé"],["Population","30.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Douala"],["Area","475,442 km² (183,569 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","cameroon"],"abstract":"<b>Cameroon</b> lies in Central Africa between Lake Chad in the north and the Atlantic in the south, running from 2° to 13° of northern latitude and from 8° to 13° of eastern longitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its relief is strongly contrasted: the Mandara mountains in the north, the Adamawa plateau across the centre, and western high plateaux carrying on south-west to the sea in a chain topped by Mount Cameroon at 4,070 m (13,350 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plains are the coastal belt in the south-west, the plain of Chad and the Diamaré in the far north, and the basins of the Benue in between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three climate zones follow that shape: a Sudano-Sahelian north above 10° with a dry season of seven to nine months and rainfall falling from 900 to 300 mm (35 to 12 inches) as one goes north; a Sudanian belt at about 1,000 mm (39 inches) and 22 °C (72 °F); and an equatorial south of some 2,000 mm (79 inches) at about 25 °C (77 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Averaged over the country the rain is about 1,600 mm (63 inches), and the vegetation changes with it from equatorial forest, which covers two fifths of the land, through savannah to steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Agriculture, forestry, fisheries, livestock and tourism are the sectors that rest on that variety, and farming alone employs more than half the workforce while contributing about a fifth of what the country produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was administered by France under a United Nations trusteeship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That trusteeship was ended on 1 January 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution records that the United Republic of Cameroon became the Republic of Cameroon by a law of 4 February 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Cameroun,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9877fr/I9877FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cameroon,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cameroon. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Cameroon</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Cameroon_2008. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cameroon — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cm.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Cameroon — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=cm. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Flag_of_Cameroon.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cameroon.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cameroon: three vertical bands, green, red and yellow, with a yellow star on the red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Mount_Cameroon_2.jpg","title":"Mount Cameroon, Cameroon","desc":"Mount Cameroon.","credit":"FORCHA101, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Cameroon_2.jpg","alt":"A long forested volcano behind palms and houses."}},
{"id":"gw-053","num":53,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Venezuela","answerText":"Venezuela","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 1819</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left Colombia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Border protest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1841</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Venezuela"},"facts":[["Capital","Caracas"],["Population","29.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Caracas"],["Area","916,445 km² (353,841 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","venezuela"],"abstract":"<b>Venezuela</b> occupies the northernmost part of South America, between about 0°38' and 12°12' north, with the Caribbean along its northern coast and the Atlantic to the north-east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Andes enter from Colombia and split into two branches, the Sierra de Perijá along its western rim and the Cordillera de Mérida, where Pico Bolívar rises to 5,007 m (16,427 feet), with the plain of Lake Maracaibo between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A coastal range and an interior range run parallel to the sea with a coastal plain between them, and south of the Orinoco the ground rises into the Guiana massif.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical and layered by altitude, from lowlands above 28 °C (82 °F) to high ground between 10 and 15 °C (50 and 59 °F), averaging 26 °C (79 °F), and the swing from year to year is no more than 4 °C (7 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 2,044 mm (80 inches) and falls mostly between April and November, when the intertropical convergence zone lies over the country; the Orinoco, which rises in the Guiana massif, drains 70.2 per cent of the territory through 436 tributary rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Independence from Spain was effectively achieved by 1819, as part of the Republic of Colombia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Venezuela separated from that federation in 1830.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1841 it protested at the British delineation of the boundary with British Guiana, claiming a frontier as far east as the Essequibo river on the strength of the limits it had held at independence from Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That claim covered two thirds of the colony's territory, and the discovery of gold in the disputed ground hardened both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has held a seat at the United Nations since 15 November 1945, and its constitution declares the Bolivarian Republic irrevocably free and independent, resting its moral property and its values of freedom, equality, justice and international peace on the doctrine of Simón Bolívar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Venezuela (República Bolivariana de),” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0441es/CA0441ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Venezuela,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/venezuela. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Venezuela_2009. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Venezuela — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ve.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Venezuela Boundary Dispute, 1895–1899,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/venezuela. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Flag_of_Venezuela.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Venezuela.svg)","alt":"The flag of Venezuela: yellow, blue and red horizontal bands with an arc of eight white stars on the blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Salto_Chur%C3%BAn_Mer%C3%BA.JPG/1920px-Salto_Chur%C3%BAn_Mer%C3%BA.JPG","title":"Angel Falls, Venezuela","desc":"Angel Falls.","credit":"Heribert Dezeo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salto_Chur%C3%BAn_Mer%C3%BA.JPG","alt":"A view of Angel Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-525","num":525,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Rome","answerText":"Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1797–1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 December 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Italy","dot":"Rome"},"facts":[["Country","Italy"],["Population","4.20M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-4.1% (2015–2023)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","italy"],"abstract":"<b>Rome</b> became the capital of a united Italy only in 1871, after the kingdom formed in 1861 absorbed Venetia and the former Papal States in the wake of the Franco-Prussian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American mission followed the capital rather than leading it: George P. Marsh oversaw its move from Turin to Florence in 1865 and from Florence to this city in 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate American legation to the Papal States had been established here in 1848, and an American consulate had been open in the city since 1797, closing only in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to an embassy in 1893, though the first appointee declined, and Wayne MacVeagh presented his credentials here as the first American ambassador in March 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy was closed on 11 December 1941, when Italy declared war on the United States, and relations were not re-established until October 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ambassador Alexander C. Kirk reopened it on 8 January 1945, presenting his credentials to a country still at war within its own borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has since given its name to the treaty that founded the International Criminal Court, adopted by a United Nations conference of plenipotentiaries that sat here from 15 June to 17 July 1998.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country it governs entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in May 1950 and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It took its United Nations seat on 14 December 1955, admitted in the same resolution as Nepal after a decade in which the Cold War had kept new members out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 4.2 million people in the city, roughly one Italian in fourteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Italy,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/it.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Italy,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/italy. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Italy and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/italy_e.htm. [Open access]","United Nations Diplomatic Conference of Plenipotentiaries on the Establishment of an International Criminal Court, “Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court,” A/CONF.183/9, 17 July 1998, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/260261. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Rome_skyline_panorama.jpg/1920px-Rome_skyline_panorama.jpg","title":"Rome","desc":"Rome, seen from a distance.","credit":"Nicholas Hartmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rome_skyline_panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Rome."}},
{"id":"gw-054","num":54,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Australia","answerText":"Australia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1901</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Australia"},"facts":[["Capital","Canberra"],["Population","27.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Sydney"],["Area","about 7.7 million km² (3.0 million sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","australia"],"abstract":"<b>Australia</b> was made on the first day of the 20th century, when six colonies were joined into a self-governing dominion of the British Empire, sovereign at home while London kept control of its dealings with everyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Control of that external policy came gradually over the next four decades rather than at any single moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 called the United Kingdom and the dominions autonomous communities equal in status and in no way subordinate to one another, and the Statute of Westminster gave that formula legal force in December 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statute did not apply here until the country adopted it, which its parliament did only on 9 October 1942, backdating the effect to the day war began with Germany in September 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country on 8 January 1940, when the two governments jointly announced that they were establishing diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Richard Gardiner Casey presented credentials as the first Australian minister in Washington that March, and Clarence E. Gauss reached the reverse post in July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two legations were raised to embassies in 1946, the White House announcing it in July and the ambassadors presenting credentials that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 1 November 1945, which makes it an original member, and it has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has grown from 23.8 million in 2015 to between 27.0 and 27.6 million, the United Nations giving the lower figure and the World Bank the higher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two also differ over the country's area by about 49,000 km², a spread larger than several of the countries in this collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Australia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/au.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Australia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/australia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Australia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/australia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Australia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Australia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Australia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Australia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Australia: a blue field with the Union Jack in the canton, the Commonwealth Star below it and the Southern Cross at the fly."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/ULURU.jpg/1920px-ULURU.jpg","title":"Uluru, Australia","desc":"Uluru.","credit":"Ek2030372672, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ULURU.jpg","alt":"A view of Uluru."}},
{"id":"gw-055","num":55,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Niger","answerText":"Niger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Niger"},"facts":[["Capital","Niamey"],["Population","28.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Niamey"],["Area","1,267,000 km² (489,190 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","niger"],"abstract":"<b>Niger</b> is a landlocked country of West Africa, reaching far up into the Sahara, with its people and its farmland gathered along the southern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three quarters of it lies in the desert zone of the north and the rest in the Sahelo-Sudanian belt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its relief has little contrast and its soils are sandy or sandy-clay, poor in nutrients and in organic matter, four fifths of the cultivable ground being dune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four climate zones follow the rainfall southwards: a Saharan zone over about 65 per cent of the territory taking under 100 mm (4 inches) a year; a Sahelo-Saharan zone over an eighth of it; a Sahelo-Sudanian zone over 21.9 per cent at 300 to 600 mm (12 to 24 inches); and a Sudanian fringe under 1 per cent in size that takes more than 600 mm (24 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evaporation runs between 1,700 and 2,100 mm (67 and 83 inches) a year, and two winds divide the seasons: the dry harmattan out of the Sahara from October to February and the moist monsoon from the south-west from March to September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Niger river and its tributaries drain the west of the country and the Komadougou Yobé and Lake Chad the east, and more than a thousand ponds lie between them, some 175 of them permanent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Drought and human pressure have degraded that living variety sharply over three decades, and about 84 per cent of the people depend on agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under French sovereignty until independence in 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution makes the state a sovereign republic and treats any attack on the republican form of the state or on its democratic institutions as high treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Niger,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0205fr/CA0205FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Niger,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/niger. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Niger</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Niger_2017. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Niger — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ne.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Niger — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ne. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Flag_of_Niger.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Niger.svg)","alt":"The flag of Niger: orange, white and green horizontal bands with an orange disc in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/1997_277-9A_Agadez_mosque.jpg","title":"the Great Mosque of Agadez, Niger","desc":"The Great Mosque of Agadez.","credit":"Dan Lundberg, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1997_277-9A_Agadez_mosque.jpg","alt":"A tall mud-brick minaret studded with projecting timbers."}},
{"id":"gw-056","num":56,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"North Korea","answerText":"North Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1945, at the 38th parallel</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Truce</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"North Korea"},"facts":[["Capital","Pyongyang"],["Population","27.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Pyongyang"],["Area","120,538 km² (46,540 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","north korea"],"abstract":"<b>North Korea</b> occupies the northern part of the Korean peninsula, with the Sea of Japan along its eastern coast and the Yellow Sea and the Korea Bay along its western.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mountains and uplands cover about 80 per cent of it, the north-eastern highlands standing on average 1,000 m (3,300 feet) above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four zones divide it: high mountain forest in the north-east over a fifth of the land, hill country over two fifths, a submountainous east coast over another fifth, and the western plains, where most of the rice grows, over the remaining sixth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is continental with four sharp seasons: long winters of −20 to −40 °C (−4 to −40 °F) under north-westerly winds off Siberia, with 37 days of snow in an average year, and a short hot humid summer of about 25 °C (77 °F) brought by monsoon winds off the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain and snow average 1,054 mm (41 inches) a year, ranging from 810 to 1,520 mm (32 to 60 inches), and some 60 per cent of it falls between June and September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the rivers run west to the Korea Bay; the Yalu runs south-west out of the Changbai range and the Tumen east from it to the Sea of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperate forest covers about 73 per cent of the territory and rivers and water another 6, the coast runs 3,070 km (1,910 miles) and 340 islands and islets lie off it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korea was temporarily divided at the 38th parallel at the end of the Second World War, to oversee the removal of Japanese forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kim Il Sung led the state that emerged in the north, and only in 1953 did the two sides reach an uneasy truce, crystallising a division that has lasted since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its constitution calls the country an independent socialist state representing the interests of all the Korean people; it took a seat at the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Democratic People's Republic of Korea,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0392en/CA0392EN.pdf. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Democratic People's Republic of Korea — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=kp. [Open access]","<i>Socialist Constitution of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea</i>, articles 1 and 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Peoples_Republic_of_Korea_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic People's Republic of Korea — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kp.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Korean War and Japan's Recovery,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Flag_of_North_Korea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Korea.svg)","alt":"The flag of North Korea: a wide red band between blue bands, with a red star on a white disc near the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Laika_ac_Mt._Paekdu_%287998657081%29.jpg/1920px-Laika_ac_Mt._Paekdu_%287998657081%29.jpg","title":"Paektu Mountain, North Korea","desc":"Paektu Mountain.","credit":"Laika ac from USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laika_ac_Mt._Paekdu_(7998657081).jpg","alt":"A view of Paektu Mountain."}},
{"id":"gw-526","num":526,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nairobi","answerText":"Nairobi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy bombed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council response</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kenya","dot":"Nairobi"},"facts":[["Country","Kenya"],["Population","4.60M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+40.1% (2009–2019)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","kenya"],"abstract":"<b>Nairobi</b> became the capital of an independent country on 12 December 1963, after nearly seventy years in which the territory around it was governed as part of British East Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Kenya on that day, two days after the Secretary of the Interior Stewart Udall had delivered a message of congratulation from President Lyndon Johnson to Prime Minister Jomo Kenyatta as special ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American embassy in the city both began on 2 March 1964, when Ambassador William Attwood presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 16 December 1963, four days after independence and before that embassy existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 4.6 million people in the city, roughly one Kenyan in twelve, and it is the only city in the country that the organisation's profile names at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 7 August 1998 the American embassy here was bombed, and the embassy at Dar es Salaam was bombed within minutes of it, the two attacks putting both capitals into the Security Council's record on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department's own account records the toll in this city: 12 American citizens killed, 32 locally engaged staff and 247 Kenyan citizens, with about 5,000 Kenyans injured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council met six days later and adopted a resolution strongly condemning the attacks in Kenya and Tanzania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 5 February 1964, less than two months after independence and before the American ambassador had presented his credentials, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since that body opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its people are counted at about 57.5 million, a figure that has risen by more than a fifth in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kenya,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ke.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kenya,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kenya. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1189 (1998),” adopted at its 3915th meeting, 13 August 1998, S/RES/1189(1998), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/258404. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Kenya and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kenya_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kenya,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KEN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Nairobi_skyline_from_Gem_Hotel.jpg/1920px-Nairobi_skyline_from_Gem_Hotel.jpg","title":"Nairobi","desc":"Nairobi, seen from a distance.","credit":"Daniel Case, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nairobi_skyline_from_Gem_Hotel.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nairobi."}},
{"id":"gw-057","num":57,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Syria","answerText":"Syria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French mandate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Syria"},"facts":[["Capital","Damascus"],["Population","26.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Damascus"],["Area","185,180 km² (71,498 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","syria"],"abstract":"<b>Syria</b> lies in south-western Asia east of the Mediterranean, a short coast in the west giving on to mountains and, beyond them, to plains and desert reaching far inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physiographic regions make it up: a coastal strip between the mountains and the sea; mountains and highlands running north to south parallel to that coast; the interior plains east of them, among which are those of Homs, Hama, Aleppo and Hassakeh; and the Badiah and the desert plains of the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is Mediterranean with a continental influence, cool rainy winters and warm dry summers with short springs and autumns between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of the country swings widely in a single day, by as much as 32 °C (58 °F) inland against about 13 °C (23 °F) on the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 252 mm (10 inches) a year, from 800 to 1,000 mm (31 to 39 inches) along the coast and up to 1,400 mm (55 inches) in the mountains, 300 to 600 mm (12 to 24 inches) on the western plains and foothills, and 150 to 200 mm (6 to 8 inches) across the centre and east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A national study of 1998 counted 3,100 flowering plants, ten gymnosperms of which eight were threatened, 754 algae and 641 fungi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country was part of the Ottoman Empire, and its foreign relations were conducted through the Sultan's government at Istanbul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After that empire collapsed the League of Nations approved a French mandate over Syria and Lebanon in 1922, which gave France exclusive control of their foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Effective independence came in 1944, and the state has held a seat at the United Nations since 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls the republic a democratic state with full sovereignty, indivisible, unable to give up any part of its territory, and part of the Arab homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Syrian Arab Republic,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0350en/CA0350EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Syria,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/syria. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Syrian Arab Republic</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Syria_2012. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Syria — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sy.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Syria — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=sy. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Flag_of_Syria_%282025-%29.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Syria_%282025-%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Syria: green, white and black horizontal bands with three red stars on the white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Palmyra_03.jpg/1920px-Palmyra_03.jpg","title":"Palmyra, Syria","desc":"Palmyra.","credit":"Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palmyra_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Palmyra."}},
{"id":"gw-058","num":58,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mali","answerText":"Mali","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">independent 20 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mali"},"facts":[["Capital","Bamako"],["Population","25.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bamako"],["Area","1,240,192 km² (478,841 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mali"],"abstract":"<b>Mali</b> is a landlocked country of West Africa, just over half of it desert, reaching from the Sahara in the north down into the wooded savannah of the far south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plateaux hold the south and west, among them the Mandingue plateau at 300 to 400 m (1,000 to 1,300 feet), with sanded plains beyond them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The alluvial plain of the inner delta of the Niger lies across the centre, and the Adrar des Ifoghas rises to 890 m (2,920 feet) in the north-east, an outlier of the crystalline massifs of the central Sahara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is Sudano-Sahelian, with very high mean temperatures and a wet season from June to September under the monsoon off the Gulf of Guinea, against a dry season of five to nine months under the harmattan off the Sahara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 280 mm (11 inches) a year and falls away northwards through four zones: a subhumid south above 1,200 mm (47 inches) over 6 per cent of the land, a Sudanian belt of 600 to 1,200 mm (24 to 47 inches) over 17 per cent, a Sahelian belt of 200 to 600 mm (8 to 24 inches) over 26 per cent, and desert steppe below 200 mm (8 inches) over the remaining half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The isohyets have shifted some 200 km (120 miles) south over recent decades, which has pushed those zones with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rich pastures of that inner delta carry the herds, and cattle, sheep and goats have all grown markedly in number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Federation of Mali, made up of the Republic of Senegal and the Soudanese Republic, gained independence from French sovereignty on 20 June 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The federation broke up within months and the Republic of Mali stood alone from September, joining the United Nations on the 28th of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution opens by declaring that all Malians are born and remain free and equal in rights and duties, and forbids discrimination on grounds of origin, region, language, race, sex, religion or political opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Mali,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0200fr/CA0200FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mali,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mali. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Mali</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Mali_2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Mali — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ml.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Mali — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ml. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_Mali.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mali.svg)","alt":"The flag of Mali: three vertical bands, green, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Djenne_great_mud_mosque.jpg/1920px-Djenne_great_mud_mosque.jpg","title":"Great Mosque of Djenné, Mali","desc":"Great Mosque of Djenné.","credit":"Ruud Zwart, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djenne_great_mud_mosque.jpg","alt":"A view of Great Mosque of Djenné."}},
{"id":"gw-059","num":59,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Burkina Faso","answerText":"Burkina Faso","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Burkina Faso"},"facts":[["Capital","Ouagadougou"],["Population","24.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Ouagadougou"],["Area","274,200 km² (105,869 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","burkina faso"],"abstract":"<b>Burkina Faso</b> is a landlocked Sahelian country of West Africa, lying between the Sahara and the forests of the Gulf of Guinea coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it sits on a single central plateau, so the relief is flat and the mean altitude only about 400 m (1,300 feet), running from 125 m (410 feet) in the south-east to 749 m (2,457 feet) at the Ténakourou peak in the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its soils are shallow and vulnerable to erosion by both water and wind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical and mostly Sahelian, with a rainy season and a dry one, and two winds to match: the moist monsoon that brings the rain and the hot dusty harmattan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rainfall grows southwards through three zones — a Sahelian north of 300 to 600 mm (12 to 24 inches) with a dry season of seven to nine months, a north-Sudanian centre, and a south-Sudanian belt above 900 mm (35 inches) — and mean temperatures run between 27 and 30 °C (81 and 86 °F) in the south and between 22 and 33 °C (72 and 91 °F) in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Five river basins drain the country into three international ones: the Volta over 63 per cent of the land, carrying its water into Ghana; the Niger over 30 per cent in the east and north; and the Comoé over 7 per cent, running into Côte d'Ivoire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Outside the south-west almost every one of those rivers is seasonal, and about 82 per cent of the territory rests on crystalline basement, which holds no thick aquifer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ecosystems are classed as forest, farmland, pasture, wetland, urban ground, mountain and conservation areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under French sovereignty and became independent, as Upper Volta, in 1960; it joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The state took the name Burkina Faso in 1984, and its constitution opens by declaring that all Burkinabè are born free and equal in rights, with an equal claim on the rights and freedoms it guarantees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Burkina Faso,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/I9864FR/i9864fr.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burkina Faso,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burkina-faso. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Burkina Faso</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Burkina_Faso_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Burkina Faso — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bf.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Burkina Faso — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=bf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Flag_of_Burkina_Faso.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burkina_Faso.svg)","alt":"The flag of Burkina Faso: red over green with a yellow five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/2016.05-441-131ap_wall_Lorop%C3%A9ni_Ruins_nr.Lorop%C3%A9ni%28Poni_Prv.%29%2CBF_sun15may2016-1106h.jpg/1920px-2016.05-441-131ap_wall_Lorop%C3%A9ni_Ruins_nr.Lorop%C3%A9ni%28Poni_Prv.%29%2CBF_sun15may2016-1106h.jpg","title":"Ruins of Loropéni, Burkina Faso","desc":"Ruins of Loropéni.","credit":"Rik Schuiling / TropCrop-TCS, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016.05-441-131ap_wall_Lorop%C3%A9ni_Ruins_nr.Lorop%C3%A9ni(Poni_Prv.),BF_sun15may2016-1106h.jpg","alt":"A view of Ruins of Loropéni."}},
{"id":"gw-527","num":527,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Naypyidaw","answerText":"Naypyidaw","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations begin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1948</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Myanmar","dot":"Naypyidaw"},"facts":[["Country","Myanmar"],["Population","547k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","3rd largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","myanmar"],"abstract":"<b>Naypyidaw</b> is the capital of Myanmar and the smallest capital of any large country in this collection: the United Nations counts about 547,000 people in it, against nearly 55 million in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same profile spells the name Nay Pyi Taw, in three words, which is the form the Burmese government itself uses and the reason the city appears under several spellings in English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not where the American mission sits, and it is not where that mission has ever sat: relations began at Rangoon, the former capital on the delta, and the record of them names no other city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They began there in an unusual order, before independence rather than after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Burmese leaders had agreed with the British in January 1947 on a staged process, and the provisional government created under it was allowed to conduct relations with other states before full sovereignty arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> So on 18 September 1947 the two governments agreed to exchange representatives with the rank of ambassador, and the American consulate general at Rangoon was raised to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition of the Union of Burma as an independent sovereign state followed on 4 January 1948, in a congratulatory message from President Harry Truman to President Sao Shwe Thaike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country at the 131st plenary meeting of its second special session, on 19 April 1948, fifteen weeks after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in July 1948, three months after that, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since the day it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 51.1 million in 2015 to 54.9 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure and on the country's area to within thirteen square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Myanmar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burma,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burma. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Myanmar and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/myanmar_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Myanmar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MMR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Union of Burma to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 188 (S-2), 19 April 1948, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/210015. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Hluttaw_Complex%2C_Naypyidaw.jpg/1920px-Hluttaw_Complex%2C_Naypyidaw.jpg","title":"Naypyidaw","desc":"Naypyidaw, seen from a distance.","credit":"Peerapat Wimolrungkarat , Thai Government photographer, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hluttaw_Complex,_Naypyidaw.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Naypyidaw."}},
{"id":"gw-060","num":60,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Taiwan","answerText":"Taiwan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US embassy arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN seat changes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US embassy closes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Taiwan"},"facts":[["Capital","Taipei (seat of government)"],["Population","about 23.0M"],["Largest city","New Taipei City"],["Area","36,197 km² (13,976 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","taiwan"],"abstract":"<b>Taiwan</b> is invisible to most of the institutions that describe countries, because they are organised around membership of the United Nations and it holds no seat there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> American consular posts existed on the island while it was a colony of the Empire of Japan, at Danshui from 1898 and at Taipei, then called Taihoku, from 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Chinese Communists established the People's Republic on the mainland in October 1949, the government of the Republic of China withdrew across the strait, and the American embassy followed it to Taipei on 19 December that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington went on recognising that government as the government of all China for another three decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Twice in that period the strait itself became a crisis, in 1954 and 1955 and again in 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly settled the question of the seat on 25 October 1971, adopting at its 1976th plenary meeting a resolution restoring what it called the lawful rights of the People's Republic in the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 1 January 1979 the United States recognised that republic as the sole legitimate government of China and withdrew recognition from the one in Taipei on the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The embassy there closed on 28 February and a liaison office in Beijing became an embassy the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One international body does list the island in its own right: the World Trade Organization admitted it on 1 January 2002 as the Separate Customs Territory of Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu, shortened to Chinese Taipei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That body admits customs territories rather than states, which is why a place with no United Nations seat can hold a seat in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: China,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Chinese Revolution of 1949,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/chinese-rev. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Taiwan Strait Crises: 1954–55 and 1958,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/taiwan-strait-crises. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Restoration of the Lawful Rights of the People's Republic of China in the United Nations,” resolution 2758 (XXVI), 25 October 1971, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192054. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Separate Customs Territory of Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu (Chinese Taipei) and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/chinese_taipei_e.htm. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Republic of China: a red field with a blue canton bearing a white twelve-pointed sun."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Taipei_Taiwan_Taipei-101-Tower-01.jpg/1920px-Taipei_Taiwan_Taipei-101-Tower-01.jpg","title":"Taipei 101, Taiwan","desc":"Taipei 101.","credit":"CEphoto, Uwe Aranas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taipei_Taiwan_Taipei-101-Tower-01.jpg","alt":"A tall tiered tower rising above a city."}},
{"id":"gw-061","num":61,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sri Lanka","answerText":"Sri Lanka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 February 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sri Lanka"},"facts":[["Capital","Colombo"],["Population","22.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Colombo"],["Area","65,610 km² (25,332 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","sri lanka"],"abstract":"<b>Sri Lanka</b> is a tropical island in the Indian Ocean, lying close to the south-eastern tip of the Indian subcontinent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three quarters of it is a broad first peneplain standing about 75 m (250 feet) above the sea; a second rises to 500 m (1,600 feet), and towards the south a third climbs steeply into a mountain massif reaching 2,500 m (8,200 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two monsoons bring most of the rain, and the climatologists divide the year into five seasons rather than two: a convectional period in March and early April, a pre-monsoon transition, the south-west monsoon from late May to late September, a cyclonic period into November, and the north-east monsoon from November to February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 2,000 mm (79 inches) a year and varies enormously around that: several stations on the western slopes of the central highlands record more than 5,000 mm (197 inches), while the northern and eastern lowlands depend on the weaker of the two monsoons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean temperature is about 27 °C (81 °F) in the lowlands and 15 °C (59 °F) in the central highlands, and a radial network of some 103 river basins runs off those highlands to every coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That range of soil, climate and relief makes it the richest country in Asia for the concentration of species it holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The island became an independent dominion within the British Commonwealth on 4 February 1948, as Ceylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution now names the state the Democratic Socialist Republic of Sri Lanka and calls it a unitary state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sovereignty, that constitution says, is in the people and is inalienable, and it includes the powers of government along with fundamental rights and the franchise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Sri Lanka,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0407en/CA0407EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sri Lanka,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sri-lanka. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Democratic Socialist Republic of Sri Lanka</i>, articles 2 and 3, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Sri_Lanka_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Sri Lanka — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lk.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Sri Lanka — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=lk. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Flag_of_Sri_Lanka.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sri_Lanka.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sri Lanka: a golden lion holding a sword on maroon, beside green and orange stripes, within a gold border."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Sigiriya_%28141688197%29.jpeg/1920px-Sigiriya_%28141688197%29.jpeg","title":"Sigiriya, Sri Lanka","desc":"Sigiriya.","credit":"Wrobell, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigiriya_(141688197).jpeg","alt":"A view of Sigiriya."}},
{"id":"gw-062","num":62,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malawi","answerText":"Malawi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 July 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malawi"},"facts":[["Capital","Lilongwe"],["Population","22.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Lilongwe"],["Area","118,480 km² (45,745 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","malawi"],"abstract":"<b>Malawi</b> is a landlocked country in southern Africa, lying between latitudes 9° and 17° south, about 900 km (560 miles) long and never more than 250 km (160 miles) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physiographic zones make it up: the highlands of Mulanje, Zomba and Dedza in the south, the plateau of the centre and north, the escarpment of the rift valley, and the rift plains along the lakeshore and down the Upper and Lower Shire valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is tropical continental and is governed by the huge mass of water in Lake Malawi, which fills nearly two thirds of the country's eastern side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are two seasons, the rains from November to April and a dry season from May to October that divides into a cool spell to July and a hot one to October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,180 mm (46 inches) a year and ranges from 700 to 2,400 mm (28 to 94 inches), heaviest on the Mulanje, Zomba, Dedza, Viphya and Nyika high ground and lightest in the Lower Shire valley and the rain shadows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> About 91 per cent of the country lies in the Zambezi basin, and the Shire is the lake's only outlet, running at an average of 400 cubic metres a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over 6,000 flowering plants grow there, 122 of them found nowhere else, and the greatest variety survives in 97 protected areas of which nine tenths are forest reserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under British sovereignty as Nyasaland, and it became independent on 6 July 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 1 December of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution declares the republic a sovereign state with rights and obligations under the law of nations, and gives it a flag, a coat of arms and an anthem of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Malawi,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2006, https://www.fao.org/3/i9753en/I9753EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malawi,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malawi. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Malawi</i>, section 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Malawi_2017. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Malawi — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mw.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Malawi — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=mw. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/Flag_of_Malawi.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malawi.svg)","alt":"The flag of Malawi: black, red and green horizontal bands with a rising red sun on the black."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Lake_Malawi%2C_shore_island.jpg/1920px-Lake_Malawi%2C_shore_island.jpg","title":"Lake Malawi, Malawi","desc":"Lake Malawi.","credit":"i_pinz, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Malawi,_shore_island.jpg","alt":"A sandy shore and open water with an island beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-528","num":528,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bogotá","answerText":"Bogotá","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 June 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1939</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Colombia","dot":"Bogotá"},"facts":[["Country","Colombia"],["Population","10.8M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+3.8% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","colombia"],"abstract":"<b>Bogotá</b> has been the seat of a government the United States recognised since 1822, but of four differently named states in that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 19 June that year, when President James Monroe received Manuel Torres as Colombian chargé d'affaires, and the federation Torres represented then took in the territory of today's Colombia, Ecuador, Venezuela and Panama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Richard Anderson of Kentucky presented his credentials here on 16 December 1823, opening the American mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations with the government in this city were not interrupted when Ecuador and Venezuela left the federation in 1830, though the state's name changed four times: New Granada from 1830, the Grenadine Confederation from 1856, the United States of Colombia from 1863 and the Republic of Colombia from 1886.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Panama separated in 1903, and the mission stayed where it was through that too, so one address in one city has served every one of those states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A treaty of friendship and commerce concluded with New Granada in 1846 and proclaimed in 1848 is still in force, which makes it one of the oldest American treaties anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to an embassy in 1939, with Spruille Braden as the first ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 10.8 million people in the city, footnoting that the figure covers the built-up area running out into Soacha, Chía and Funza rather than the city alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country around it entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only in October 1981, more than thirty years after that agreement began, and has belonged to the World Trade Organization since April 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 47.0 million in 2015 to 53.4 million, and the two international counts agree on that figure and put the country's area within 1,129 km² of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Colombia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/co.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Colombia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/colombia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Colombia and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/colombia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Colombia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Colombia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Bogot%C3%A1_Colombia_panorama.jpg/1920px-Bogot%C3%A1_Colombia_panorama.jpg","title":"Bogotá","desc":"Bogotá, seen from a distance.","credit":"Dori, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bogot%C3%A1_Colombia_panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bogotá."}},
{"id":"gw-063","num":63,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Zambia","answerText":"Zambia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Multi-party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Zambia"},"facts":[["Capital","Lusaka"],["Population","22.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Lusaka"],["Area","752,612 km² (290,585 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","zambia"],"abstract":"<b>Zambia</b> is a landlocked country lying between 8° and 18° south of the equator and between 22° and 34° east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Although it sits within the tropics its high ground gives it a subtropical climate, divided into a dry season from May to October and a wet one from November to April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dry season splits in two: a cool midwinter spell to July averaging about 16 °C (61 °F) and a hot one after it at about 24 °C (75 °F), with daily maxima of 30 to 40 °C (86 to 104 °F) common in the low-lying Zambezi, Gwembe and Luangwa valleys, where frost can still be recorded in the cool months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,020 mm (40 inches) a year, from 750 mm (30 inches) in the south through 900 to 1,200 mm (35 to 47 inches) in the centre to about 1,400 mm (55 inches) in the north, and it falls in a single season governed by the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole country drains into just two basins, the Zambezi and the Congo, and the Kafue and the Luangwa are the great tributaries within it, covering 152,000 and 165,000 km² (59,000 and 64,000 sq mi) respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Forests, farmed land and wetlands are the ecosystems the rural economy and the national one rest on most heavily, and conservation has so far been uneven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under British sovereignty until independence on 24 October 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 1 December of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It made the change from one-party rule to multi-party democracy in 1991, and has given shelter to refugees from across the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its constitution declares itself the supreme law of the republic, so that any written law, customary law or customary practice inconsistent with it is void.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Zambia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9761en/I9761EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Zambia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/zambia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Zambia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Zambia_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Zambia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/zm.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Zambia — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=zm. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Flag_of_Zambia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zambia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Zambia: a green field with orange, black and red stripes at the fly beneath an orange eagle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Cataratas_Victoria%2C_Zambia-Zimbabue%2C_2018-07-27%2C_DD_04.jpg/1920px-Cataratas_Victoria%2C_Zambia-Zimbabue%2C_2018-07-27%2C_DD_04.jpg","title":"Victoria Falls, Zambia","desc":"Victoria Falls.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cataratas_Victoria,_Zambia-Zimbabue,_2018-07-27,_DD_04.jpg","alt":"A view of Victoria Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-064","num":64,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kazakhstan","answerText":"Kazakhstan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kazakhstan"},"facts":[["Capital","Astana"],["Population","21.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Almaty"],["Area","2,724,900 km² (1,052,100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","kazakhstan"],"abstract":"<b>Kazakhstan</b> is the ninth largest country in the world and the largest of the fifteen states that came out of the Soviet Union after Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is sharply continental, with cold dry winters and hot dry summers: in the south the average runs from −3 °C (27 °F) in January to 30 °C (86 °F) in July, and in the north from −18 °C (0 °F) to 19 °C (66 °F), with −45 °C (−49 °F) on record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain and snow average only 250 mm (10 inches) a year, from under 100 mm (4 inches) in the Balkhash-Alakol depression and around the Aral Sea to as much as 1,600 mm (63 inches) in the eastern and south-eastern mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 70 and 85 per cent of that falls in the colder half of the year, snow usually beginning in November, and almost the whole territory is swept by strong winds that can gust past 40 metres a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four hydrological regions divide it — the Ob basin draining to the Arctic, the Caspian basin, the Aral basin, and interior lakes, depressions and deserts — and of some 39,000 rivers and streams, 7,000 run more than 10 km (6 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its surface water is very unevenly spread: the centre of the country holds 3 per cent of it while the Balkhash-Alakol and Irtysh basins in the east hold nearly three quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A territory almost the size of Western Europe, with high mountain zones at one end of its range, gives it a great variety of ecological systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was a constituent republic of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics and declared its independence in December 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution proclaims the republic a democratic, secular, legal and social state whose highest values are the individual, his life, his rights and his freedoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Kazakhstan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2012, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0366en/CA0366EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kazakhstan,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kazakhstan. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Kazakhstan</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Kazakhstan_2017. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Kazakhstan — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kz.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Kazakhstan — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=kz. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Flag_of_Kazakhstan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kazakhstan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Kazakhstan: a sky-blue field with a golden sun and steppe eagle, and a national ornament down the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Charyn_Canyon%2C_Kazakhstan_03.jpg/1920px-Charyn_Canyon%2C_Kazakhstan_03.jpg","title":"Charyn Canyon, Kazakhstan","desc":"Charyn Canyon.","credit":"Bgag, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charyn_Canyon,_Kazakhstan_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Charyn Canyon."}},
{"id":"gw-065","num":65,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Chad","answerText":"Chad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Chad"},"facts":[["Capital","N'Djamena"],["Population","21.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","N'Djamena"],["Area","1,284,000 km² (495,755 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","chad"],"abstract":"<b>Chad</b> is a landlocked country at the heart of Africa, reaching from the Sahara in the north down into wooded savannah in the far south, with about 19 million hectares of it cultivable and under a fifth of that cultivated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mean rainfall of 322 mm (13 inches) a year says almost nothing about it, because the figure hides an enormous spread across the country and from one year to the next; drought is frequent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three agroclimatic zones run north to south: a Saharan zone under 300 mm (12 inches) covering nearly half the territory and holding 2 per cent of the people; a Sahelian belt of 300 to 600 mm (12 to 24 inches) over 28 per cent of the ground carrying half the population; and a Sudanian zone above 600 mm (24 inches), reaching 1,200 mm (47 inches) at the southern tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rains come from June to September and the dry season from October to May, with the heaviest falls in July and August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Evapotranspiration reaches 3,000 mm (118 inches) in places, and daytime temperatures run between 28 and 42 °C (82 and 108 °F) through the year, falling as low as 14 °C (57 °F) at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Forest covers about 32.4 million hectares, a quarter of the country, and its inland waters hold roughly 500 billion cubic metres, fed by the Chari and the Logone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was part of French Equatorial Africa, the federation that governed France's central African possessions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its independence was proclaimed on 11 August 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls Chad a sovereign, independent, secular and social republic, one and indivisible, founded on the principles of democracy, the rule of law and justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Tchad,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/I9886FR/i9886fr.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Chad,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/chad. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Chad</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Chad_2018. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Chad — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/td.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Chad — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=td. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Flag_of_Chad.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Chad.svg)","alt":"The flag of Chad: three vertical bands, blue, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Ounianga_Serir.jpg","title":"Lakes of Ounianga, Chad","desc":"Lakes of Ounianga.","credit":"Jacques Taberlet, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ounianga_Serir.jpg","alt":"A view of Lakes of Ounianga."}},
{"id":"gw-529","num":529,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Seoul","answerText":"Seoul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations begin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 March 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"South Korea","dot":"Seoul"},"facts":[["Country","South Korea"],["Population","10.0M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-2.1% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south korea"],"abstract":"<b>Seoul</b> is named in the document that recognised the country around it: a White House statement of 1 January 1949 recognised the Republic of Korea, with its capital at this city, as an independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 25 March that year, when Ambassador John Myun Chang presented his credentials to President Truman in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state had been created out of the southern half of a peninsula the United States and the Soviet Union agreed during the Second World War to divide temporarily at the 38th parallel, so as to oversee the removal of Japanese forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither power would then let its own Korean ally be threatened by unification, and American forces withdrew from the south in the late 1940s without treating it as central to their defence of East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The northern army attacked in June 1950 and came close to conquering the entire peninsula before an American-led United Nations force pushed it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Only in 1953 did the two sides reach an uneasy truce, and the United States and this country signed a mutual security treaty that year designed to protect it from its neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither Korean state held a United Nations seat for another 38 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Security Council recommended both for membership in a single resolution on 8 August 1991, and the General Assembly admitted them together at the very first plenary meeting of its forty-sixth session, on 17 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations counts just under 10 million people in the city itself, counted for the city proper rather than for the wider built-up area that spreads well beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country around it holds some 51.7 million, so roughly one citizen in five lives inside the capital's own boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Republic of Korea (South Korea),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/korea-south. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Korean War, 1950–1953,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 702 (1991),” adopted at its 3001st meeting, 8 August 1991, S/RES/702(1991), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/125255. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea and the Republic of Korea to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/1, 17 September 1991, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/133631. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Seoul_from_Namsan_3.jpg/1920px-Seoul_from_Namsan_3.jpg","title":"Seoul","desc":"Seoul, seen from a distance.","credit":"kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seoul_from_Namsan_3.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Seoul."}},
{"id":"gw-066","num":66,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Chile","answerText":"Chile","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Chile"},"facts":[["Capital","Santiago"],["Population","20.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Santiago"],["Area","756,102 km² (291,932 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","chile"],"abstract":"<b>Chile</b> is three territories at once: a continental strip in the south-west of South America, a claimed Antarctic sector, and scattered islands in the South Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The continental part runs some 4,329 km (2,690 miles) from 17°30' to 56°30' south and is never wider than 445 km (277 miles) nor narrower than 90 km (56 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its islands run far out into the ocean: the Juan Fernández archipelago 667 km (414 miles) offshore, San Félix and San Ambrosio 926 km (575 miles), Salas y Gómez 3,463 km (2,152 miles) and Easter Island 3,700 km (2,300 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pacific, the Humboldt Current, the South Pacific anticyclone and the Andes between them give it an enormous range of climates: desert in the north, Mediterranean and temperate through the centre, cold and wet in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,522 mm (60 inches) over the whole territory but almost none falls in the Atacama, while the far south records around 3,000 mm (118 inches), and 101 river basins carry most of it quickly down to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That length, and the wall of mountains standing the whole way along it, make its landscapes among the most varied on earth, running from 17° to 56° south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country was a Spanish colony before it was a state of its own, and the mining and farming of its long central valley shaped it afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its distance from the northern hemisphere long limited its dealings with the wider world, since ships had to round Cape Horn at the southern tip of the continent to reach its main port at Valparaíso.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution declares the state unitary, with an administration decentralised or deconcentrated by function and by territory as the law provides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Chile,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0440es/CA0440ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Chile,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/chile. [Open access]","<i>Political Constitution of the Republic of Chile</i>, chapter I, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Chile_2021. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Chile — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cl.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Chile — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=cl. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/Flag_of_Chile.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Chile.svg)","alt":"The flag of Chile: white over red, with a blue square bearing a white star in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Cuernos_del_Paine_in_Torres_del_Paine_National_Park.jpg/1920px-Cuernos_del_Paine_in_Torres_del_Paine_National_Park.jpg","title":"Torres del Paine National Park, Chile","desc":"Torres del Paine.","credit":"Pedro Szekely from Los Angeles, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuernos_del_Paine_in_Torres_del_Paine_National_Park.jpg","alt":"Jagged peaks lit red at sunrise above a lake."}},
{"id":"gw-067","num":67,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Romania","answerText":"Romania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union of principalities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1878</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1881</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Romania"},"facts":[["Capital","Bucharest"],["Population","19.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bucharest"],["Area","238,398 km² (92,046 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","romania"],"abstract":"<b>Romania</b> came into being as a single state in 1862, when the Ottoman sultan allowed the principalities of Moldavia and Wallachia to unite under that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charles of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen became prince in 1866, the country sided with Russia in the war against the Ottomans of 1877 and 1878, and the treaties of San Stefano and Berlin recognised its independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charles became king in 1881, and the United States recognised the new kingdom on 7 April that year, when the American chargé d'affaires Eugene Schuyler had an audience with him and said so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American relations with the country then swung between warm and cold across the 20th century: it fought on the Allied side in the First World War and declared war on the United States in 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations began improving in the 1960s as the government distanced its foreign policy from the Soviet Union's, particularly after the invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The 1970s brought cultural, scientific and educational exchanges, and the 1980s brought criticism of the country's human rights record that strained the relationship again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Since the revolution of December 1989 unseated the communist government the relationship has been a strengthened one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took a United Nations seat on 14 December 1955, in the single batch of admissions that ended a decade of Cold War deadlock, and joined the European Union on 1 January 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It entered the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in November 1971, unusually early for a state in the Soviet bloc.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population is falling rather than rising, from 19.8 million in 2015 to 19.0 million, one of the few countries carded so far to show that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Romania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ro.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Romania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/romania. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Romania and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/romania_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Romania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ROU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/Flag_of_Romania.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania.svg)","alt":"The flag of Romania: three vertical bands, blue, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Castelul_Bran2.jpg/1920px-Castelul_Bran2.jpg","title":"Bran Castle, Romania","desc":"Bran Castle.","credit":"Dobre Cezar, CC BY-SA 3.0 ro, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castelul_Bran2.jpg","alt":"A view of Bran Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-068","num":68,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Somalia","answerText":"Somalia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Somalia"},"facts":[["Capital","Mogadishu"],["Population","20.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Mogadishu"],["Area","637,657 km² (246,201 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","somalia"],"abstract":"<b>Somalia</b> sits at the tip of north-eastern Africa and has the longest coastline on the continent, meeting the Gulf of Aden in the north and the Indian Ocean in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five physiographic zones divide it: the northern coastal plains, the Golis range behind them, the central coastal plains, a broad limestone and sandstone plateau across the centre and south, and the flood plains of the Juba and the Shabelle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those two rivers give the country its best farmland; almost everywhere else the climate is arid to semi-arid, at a daytime average of 27 °C (81 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 282 mm (11 inches) a year, as little as 50 mm (2 inches) along the northern coast, and it falls in two seasons — the Gu from April to June and the lesser Deyr in October and November — with the Jilal and the Hagaa dry between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rainfall varies more from year to year than that of any other mainland African state, evapotranspiration runs from 1,500 mm (59 inches) on the southern coast to 2,900 mm (114 inches) on the northern, and drought arrives moderately every three or four years and severely every seven to nine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is one of the richest countries of the Horn for the species it holds, and a high proportion of them are found nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of its people were nomadic pastoralists moving with their herds as pasture and water allowed, with settled farmers in villages where the water was reliable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Somali Republic was formed on 1 July 1960 by the union of a territory that had been under Italian administration with one that had been under British.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its provisional constitution calls the state a federal, sovereign and democratic republic founded on inclusive representation, a multiparty system and social justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Somalia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2014, https://www.fao.org/3/i9759en/I9759EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Somalia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/somalia. [Open access]","<i>Provisional Constitution of the Federal Republic of Somalia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Somalia_2012. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Somalia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/so.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Somalia — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=so. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Flag_of_Somalia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Somalia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Somalia: a light blue field with a single white five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Laas_Geel_rock_paintings.JPG/1920px-Laas_Geel_rock_paintings.JPG","title":"Laas Geel, Somalia","desc":"The rock paintings at Laas Geel.","credit":"Theodor Hoffsten, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laas_Geel_rock_paintings.JPG","alt":"Red and white painted cattle on a rock shelter wall."}},
{"id":"gw-530","num":530,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Khartoum","answerText":"Khartoum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition delivered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 February 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Operations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 May 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sudan","dot":"Khartoum"},"facts":[["Country","Sudan"],["Population","5.70M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","sudan"],"abstract":"<b>Khartoum</b> is where the United States recognised Sudan, and the recognition was carried across the city by hand: on 1 January 1956 the American liaison officer there, Arthur Beach, delivered a letter to the president of the Supreme Commission of Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country had been under an Anglo-Egyptian condominium until that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 15 February, when the American liaison office in the city was raised to embassy rank with Beach himself as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy closed on 6 June 1967 after demonstrations and stone-throwing outside it when the Arab-Israeli war of that month broke out, and Sudan severed relations the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American interests section opened inside the Netherlands embassy here on 14 August that year, so the United States kept a presence in the city without having a mission of its own in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were restored on 25 July 1972 when the embassy reopened under George C. Moore, but the following March Ambassador Cleo A. Noel Jr. was assassinated at post in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy suspended operations again on 7 February 1996, and full operations resumed only on 23 May 2002, with the ambassador working from Nairobi in the interval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city stands where the Blue Nile and the White Nile meet, the United Nations counts about 5.7 million people in it, and the country around it took a seat there on 12 November 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That profile records no surface area for the country at all, an omission rare enough to be worth noticing, so its figure of 1,878,000 km² rests on the World Bank alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its recorded population has risen from 40.0 million in 2015 to 51.7 million, and that is the count for what remains after the southern third of the country seceded in 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sudan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sudan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sudan. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SDN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SDN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The 1967 Arab-Israeli War,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1961-1968/arab-israeli-war-1967. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Khartoum_panorama-1_-_by_ScubaBeer.jpg","title":"Khartoum","desc":"Khartoum, seen from a distance.","credit":"Chris from Falmouth, UK, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khartoum_panorama-1_-_by_ScubaBeer.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Khartoum."}},
{"id":"gw-069","num":69,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Senegal","answerText":"Senegal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Senegal"},"facts":[["Capital","Dakar"],["Population","19.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Dakar"],["Area","196,712 km² (75,951 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","senegal"],"abstract":"<b>Senegal</b> stands at the westernmost point of the African continent, on the Atlantic, about 14 degrees north of the equator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its climate is Sahelian, with a rainy season that shortens as one goes north — June to October in the south, July to September in the north — and a dry season from November to June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperatures fall a little below 16 °C (61 °F) in the wet months and often pass 40 °C (104 °F) in summer, under the maritime trade winds in one season and the harmattan in the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 687 mm (27 inches) a year over the territory, but that mean hides a range from 1,500 mm (59 inches) in the south to 200 mm (8 inches) in the north, and evapotranspiration approaches 2,000 mm (79 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 1960 and 1980 the rainfall lines shifted 120 km (75 miles) southwards, taking the annual mean on the coast from 600 mm (24 inches) to 400 mm (16 inches), and although the totals later recovered the 2002 rains failed badly at 488 mm (19 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three climatic belts and six ecogeographical zones give it four main ecosystems, and its main rivers are the Senegal over 37 per cent of the territory, the Gambia over 30 and the Casamance over 11.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 80 per cent of its people live along a coastal strip less than 200 km (125 miles) wide, and the east of the country is almost empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became independent from France in 1960, with the dissolution of the Mali Federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 28 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution declares the republic secular, democratic and social, guaranteeing equality before the law without distinction of origin, race, sex or religion, and respecting every belief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Sénégal,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0209fr/CA0209FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Senegal,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/senegal. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Senegal</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Senegal_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Senegal — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sn.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Senegal — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=sn. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Flag_of_Senegal.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Senegal.svg)","alt":"The flag of Senegal: three vertical bands of green, yellow and red with a green five-pointed star in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/RetbaLakeShore.jpg/1920px-RetbaLakeShore.jpg","title":"Lake Retba, Senegal","desc":"Lake Retba.","credit":"Jjm2311, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RetbaLakeShore.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Retba."}},
{"id":"gw-070","num":70,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guatemala","answerText":"Guatemala","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guatemala"},"facts":[["Capital","Guatemala City"],["Population","19.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Guatemala City"],["Area","108,889 km² (42,042 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","guatemala"],"abstract":"<b>Guatemala</b> is the northernmost country of the Central American isthmus, lying between 13°44' and 17°48' north and reaching the Pacific in the south and the Caribbean in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is tropical and warm by latitude and is then cut about by the height of its mountains and the distance from either sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The year divides into a dry season from November to April and a wet one from May to October, broken in July and August by a spell of reduced rain the country calls the canícula, with about 119 rainy days in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages anything from 700 mm (28 inches) in the dry eastern zone to 5,000 mm (197 inches) in the north-west, and the Caribbean coast, the north-western transversal strip and the Pacific coastal region are the wettest of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean temperatures run from 23 to 33 °C (73 to 91 °F) on both coasts to about 20 °C (68 °F) in the middle mountain belt between 500 and 1,500 m (1,600 and 4,900 feet) and below that higher up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three watersheds carry its rivers away: the Pacific over 24 per cent of the territory, the Caribbean over 34 and the Gulf of Mexico over 42, the last of them the largest in flow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has the highest rate of endemism in Central America, at about 13 per cent of its species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was a member of the Federation of Central American States before it stood alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has held a seat at the United Nations since 21 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution says the state is organised to protect the person and the family, and that its supreme purpose is the realisation of the common good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Guatemala,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0418es/CA0418ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guatemala,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guatemala. [Open access]","<i>Political Constitution of the Republic of Guatemala</i>, articles 1 and 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Guatemala_1993. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Guatemala — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gt.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Guatemala — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=gt. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Flag_of_Guatemala.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guatemala.svg)","alt":"The flag of Guatemala: three vertical bands of sky blue, white and sky blue, with the national coat of arms on the white band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Tikal_3.jpg/1920px-Tikal_3.jpg","title":"Tikal, Guatemala","desc":"Tikal.","credit":"Mundo Maya, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tikal_3.jpg","alt":"A view of Tikal."}},
{"id":"gw-071","num":71,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ecuador","answerText":"Ecuador","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left Colombia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised alone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 December 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ecuador"},"facts":[["Capital","Quito"],["Population","18.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Guayaquil"],["Area","257,217 km² (99,312 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","ecuador"],"abstract":"<b>Ecuador</b> lies in the north-west of South America, facing the Pacific, with the equator that gives the country its name running straight across the north of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mainland sits between 1°28' north and 5°01' south, and the Galápagos archipelago lies about 1,000 km (620 miles) out in the ocean west of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Except in the Sierra, the Andean belt, the climate is humid tropical, and it is governed by the ocean: the cold Humboldt current, the equatorial undercurrent and the warm waters of El Niño between them set the temperature and the rain, above all on the coast and in the Galápagos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 2,274 mm (90 inches) a year and is very unevenly spread in both time and place, which is why the coast floods so severely and so regularly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the southern coast the annual figure runs from 100 mm (4 inches) at Salinas on the Santa Elena peninsula to about 1,000 mm (39 inches) further inland, while the wettest parts of the northern coast take 3,000 to 4,000 mm (118 to 157 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is one of the seventeen megadiverse countries on earth, which it owes to standing in the neotropics with the Andes running the length of it and two great ocean currents meeting along its shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was part of Colombia when that federation won its independence from Spain, recognised in 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ecuador withdrew from that federation in 1830 and was recognised as a separate state two years afterwards, in 1832.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has held a seat at the United Nations since 21 December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls the country a constitutional state of rights and justice — social, democratic, sovereign, independent, unitary, intercultural, multinational and secular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Ecuador,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0573es/CA0573ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ecuador,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ecuador. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Ecuador</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Ecuador_2021. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Ecuador — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ec.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Ecuador — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ec. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Flag_of_Ecuador.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ecuador.svg)","alt":"The flag of Ecuador: horizontal bands of yellow, blue and red, the yellow band double width, with the national coat of arms in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Lobo_marino_%28Zalophus_californianus_wollebaeki%29%2C_Punta_Pitt%2C_isla_de_San_Crist%C3%B3bal%2C_islas_Gal%C3%A1pagos%2C_Ecuador%2C_2015-07-24%2C_DD_11.JPG/1920px-Lobo_marino_%28Zalophus_californianus_wollebaeki%29%2C_Punta_Pitt%2C_isla_de_San_Crist%C3%B3bal%2C_islas_Gal%C3%A1pagos%2C_Ecuador%2C_2015-07-24%2C_DD_11.JPG","title":"Galápagos Islands, Ecuador","desc":"Galápagos Islands.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobo_marino_(Zalophus_californianus_wollebaeki),_Punta_Pitt,_isla_de_San_Crist%C3%B3bal,_islas_Gal%C3%A1pagos,_Ecuador,_2015-07-24,_DD_11.JPG","alt":"A view of Galápagos Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-531","num":531,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kampala","answerText":"Kampala","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 November 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy re-established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 June 1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uganda","dot":"Kampala"},"facts":[["Country","Uganda"],["Population","3.10M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+11.5% (2014–2019)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","uganda"],"abstract":"<b>Kampala</b> is where relations between Uganda and the United States were opened, broken off in practice and opened again, all within seventeen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country on 9 October 1962, in a congratulatory message from President John F. Kennedy to Prime Minister A. Milton Obote, Uganda having previously been under British sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began the same day, when the American consulate general in the city was raised to embassy status with Olcott H. Deming as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted Uganda sixteen days later, by resolution 1758 (XVII), adopted at its 1158th plenary meeting on 25 October 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country had joined the Commonwealth that year on independence from Britain, and had become a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 23 October, two days before it took its United Nations seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy here closed on 10 November 1973, when all American diplomatic personnel were withdrawn from the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department later named four reasons: persistent internal security problems, operating difficulties for American programmes and personnel, repeated public threats against embassy officials and other Americans by high Ugandan officials, and finally the abrupt expulsion of the Marine security guard that protected the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations themselves were not interrupted, Uganda kept its own embassy in Washington, and American interests here were represented in the meantime by the Federal Republic of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy was re-established on 18 June 1979 with David Halstead as chargé d'affaires ad interim, more than five years after the withdrawal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has grown with the country, whose recorded population rose by more than a third in the decade to 2025, from 37.5 million to 51.4 million, while the capital's own urban area is now put at 3.14 million, about one Ugandan in sixteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Uganda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ug.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uganda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uganda. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Uganda to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1758 (XVII), adopted at the 1158th plenary meeting, 25 October 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204481. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Uganda and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/uganda_e.htm. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Uganda,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/uganda. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Uganda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/UGA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Skyline_of_Kampala.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Kampala.jpg","title":"Kampala","desc":"Kampala, seen from a distance.","credit":"Flöschen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Kampala.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kampala."}},
{"id":"gw-072","num":72,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Netherlands","answerText":"Netherlands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1782</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1814</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 May 1942</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Netherlands"},"facts":[["Capital","Amsterdam"],["Population","18.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Amsterdam"],["Area","41,543 km² (16,040 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","netherlands"],"abstract":"The <b>Netherlands</b> was the second country in the world to recognise the United States, and it did so while both were at war with the same enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1779 the Continental Congress commissioned Henry Laurens as minister to negotiate a treaty and a loan, and the Royal Navy captured him on the way to Europe carrying a draft of it, after which Britain declared war on the Dutch republic in 1780.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 19 April 1782, when The Hague accepted the credentials of Minister John Adams, and diplomatic relations and the American legation there began the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The relationship then broke for thirteen years: the legation closed in May 1801, ostensibly to reduce expenditure but in truth because Dutch foreign policy was by then run from Paris, and the country was annexed outright to the French Empire in 1810.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Full sovereignty returned with Napoleon's defeat, and the two exchanged ministers again in 1814 and 1815.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The second interruption was the German invasion, after which the legation closed on 15 July 1940 and reopened in London a month later, beside the Dutch government in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was there, on 8 May 1942, that the mission was raised to embassy rank, Anthony J. Drexel Biddle Jr. presenting his new credentials as ambassador to a government sitting in another country's capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has been a member of the United Nations since 10 December 1945 and was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948, becoming a founding member of the World Trade Organization when that replaced the agreement on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two international counts of its total area agree to within three square kilometres, at 41,543 and 41,540.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The European Union publishes a third and smaller figure, 37,391 square kilometres, which is the land alone: a tenth of the country as the other two count it is water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Netherlands (Kingdom of the),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nl.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Netherlands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/netherlands. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Netherlands and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/netherlands_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Netherlands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NLD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Netherlands – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/netherlands_en?etrans=en. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Netherlands: three horizontal bands of red, white and blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/KinderdijkMolens02.jpg","title":"Kinderdijk, the Netherlands","desc":"Kinderdijk.","credit":"Lucas Hirschegger, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KinderdijkMolens02.jpg","alt":"A view of Kinderdijk."}},
{"id":"gw-073","num":73,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cambodia","answerText":"Cambodia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 7 February 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cambodia"},"facts":[["Capital","Phnom Penh"],["Population","18.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Phnom Penh"],["Area","181,035 km² (69,898 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","cambodia"],"abstract":"<b>Cambodia</b> lies in South-East Asia on the coast of the Gulf of Thailand, and it shares the basin of the Mekong with five other countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its climate is the tropical monsoon, set by the Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone: a dry season from November to April under the north-easterly, with February the driest month, and a wet one from May to October under the south-westerly drawn in off the Indian Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,400 mm (55 inches) a year and ranges from about 1,000 mm (39 inches) in the west to nearly 4,700 mm (185 inches) at Bokor in the south, with peaks in June and again in September and October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> April is the warmest month at a maximum of 36 °C (97 °F) and January the coolest, and from late July there may be ten days or more without meaningful rain, a break farmers call the short dry season and plant around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Mekong and Lake Tonle Sap are joined by the Tonle Sap river, 120 km (75 miles) long, which reverses its direction twice a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From July to the end of October the Mekong is high and pushes water up into the lake, swelling it to about 10,500 km² (4,100 sq mi); in early November the river turns and drains it away again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its plants have been counted at more than 3,000 species and estimated as high as 8,260, perhaps a tenth of them growing nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was part of French Indochina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Kingdom of Cambodia regained its independence after the Second World War, and was recognised as independent on 7 February 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, and its constitution makes it a kingdom in which the King rules according to the constitution and the principles of liberal multi-party democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Cambodia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0391en/CA0391EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cambodia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cambodia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Cambodia_2008. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cambodia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kh.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Cambodia — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=kh. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Flag_of_Cambodia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cambodia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cambodia: horizontal bands of blue, red and blue with a white outline of Angkor Wat in the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Angkor_Wat.jpg/1920px-Angkor_Wat.jpg","title":"Angkor Wat, Cambodia","desc":"Angkor Wat.","credit":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angkor_Wat.jpg","alt":"A view of Angkor Wat."}},
{"id":"gw-074","num":74,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Zimbabwe","answerText":"Zimbabwe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1980</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Zimbabwe"},"facts":[["Capital","Harare"],["Population","17.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Harare"],["Area","390,757 km² (150,872 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","zimbabwe"],"abstract":"<b>Zimbabwe</b> is a landlocked country in southern Africa, and its ground is usually sorted by height into four regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are the lowveld below 600 m (2,000 feet), the middleveld between 600 and 1,200 m (2,000 and 3,900 feet), the highveld between 1,200 and 2,000 m (3,900 and 6,600 feet) and the Eastern Highlands above that, where Mount Nyangani reaches 2,592 m (8,504 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its soils come mostly from granite, so about 70 per cent of them are sandy and light and limit what can be grown, though heavier clay soils of excellent quality are found in every region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is largely subtropical, with one rainy season from November to March, a cool winter from April to August and the hottest driest weeks from September to mid-November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 657 mm (26 inches) a year, over 1,000 mm (39 inches) in the Eastern Highlands and 300 to 450 mm (12 to 18 inches) in the southern lowveld, and it grows less reliable towards the south and the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Evaporation runs from about 1,400 mm (55 inches) in those highlands to 2,200 mm (87 inches) in the lowveld and generally exceeds the rain, so erratic rainfall constrains cropping across at least three fifths of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Drought arrives with El Niño and hit five farming seasons in the first dozen years of this century alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 4,440 vascular plants grow there, 214 of them nowhere else, along with 672 birds of which 450 breed in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Republic of Zimbabwe came into being in 1980 and joined the United Nations on 25 August of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution declares Zimbabwe a unitary, democratic and sovereign republic, and makes itself the supreme law, so that any law, practice, custom or conduct inconsistent with it is invalid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Zimbabwe,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9842en/I9842EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Zimbabwe,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/zimbabwe. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Zimbabwe</i>, section 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Zimbabwe_2017. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Zimbabwe — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/zw.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Zimbabwe — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=zw. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Flag_of_Zimbabwe.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zimbabwe.svg)","alt":"The flag of Zimbabwe: seven horizontal stripes of green, gold, red and black, with a white triangle at the hoist bearing a red star and the Zimbabwe Bird."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_%2833736918448%29.jpg/1920px-Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_%2833736918448%29.jpg","title":"Great Zimbabwe, Zimbabwe","desc":"Great Zimbabwe.","credit":"Andrew Moore from Johannesburg, South Africa, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_(33736918448).jpg","alt":"A view of Great Zimbabwe."}},
{"id":"gw-532","num":532,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Madrid","answerText":"Madrid","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 February 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 June 1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1913</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Spain","dot":"Madrid"},"facts":[["Country","Spain"],["Population","6.60M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+6.9% (2022–2025)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","spain"],"abstract":"<b>Madrid</b> spent four years declining to receive an American envoy before it recognised the country he came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Continental Congress appointed John Jay minister plenipotentiary on 29 September 1779 and sent him to the Spanish court, where he spent two years and was never formally received, Spain being unwilling to risk relations with Britain while the war's outcome was open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came only once it was clear Britain and the United States would sign a treaty ending that war, and it took the form of a reception: on 20 February 1783 the city received William Carmichael as chargé d'affaires, though he had been in Spain since May 1782.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American record dates the recognition, the establishment of diplomatic relations and the founding of the legation here all to that one act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain sent its own chargé d'affaires, Diego Gardoqui, to the United States in June 1785, and the two signed a Treaty of Friendship, Limits, and Navigation on 27 October 1795.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations have been broken exactly once since, and for a year: Spain severed them on 21 April 1898 and the American minister Stewart Woodford closed the legation that day, four days before Congress approved a declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bellamy Storer was appointed minister on 12 April 1899 and presented credentials that June, and the legation was raised to embassy rank when Joseph Willard presented his in October 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The civil war moved the mission out of the capital altogether, first to the American consulate at Barcelona, open since 1797, then across the border to Saint-Jean-de-Luz in France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It returned to Spain on 13 April 1939, not here but at Burgos, where H. Freeman Matthews was received as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955 under one resolution headed <i>Admission of New Members</i> that took in several states at once, has been a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade since 1963, and has belonged to the European Union since 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Spain,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/es.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Spain,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/spain. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Spain and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/spain_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Spain – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/spain_en?etrans=en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Madrid-skyline-251210-1.jpg/1920px-Madrid-skyline-251210-1.jpg","title":"Madrid","desc":"Madrid, seen from a distance.","credit":"FDV, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madrid-skyline-251210-1.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Madrid."}},
{"id":"gw-075","num":75,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guinea","answerText":"Guinea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1958</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guinea"},"facts":[["Capital","Conakry"],["Population","15.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Conakry"],["Area","245,857 km² (94,926 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Guinea</b> stands on the Atlantic coast of West Africa and is known as the water tower of the region, because so many of the rivers of that part of Africa rise inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its geology is unusually varied, and four geomorphological units run west to east: the recent deltaic coastal plains and lowlands; the crystalline massifs and high plateaux of the Fouta Djallon; the peneplains of Upper Guinea; and the granite and gneiss massifs of the forest region, cut by broad valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical, with a dry season of four to seven months and a wet one of five to eight depending on the region, mean temperatures between 21 and 27 °C (70 and 81 °F), and humidity above 60 per cent all year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,651 mm (65 inches) a year and ranges from 1,200 mm (47 inches) in Upper Guinea to 4,200 mm (165 inches) on the coast, brought by the monsoon off the Atlantic and dried out by the harmattan off the Sahara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The maritime strip in the west is about 300 km (185 miles) long and 100 to 150 km (60 to 95 miles) wide over a seventh of the country, and its 360,000 hectares of salt marsh include 260,000 of mangrove, the largest stand in West Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some 1,161 watercourses have been counted, in 23 river basins of which 14 are shared, and the Niger basin alone takes up 39 per cent of the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Guinean forests of West Africa are reckoned one of the 25 biodiversity hotspots of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under French sovereignty until it became independent in 1958 under Sékou Touré.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 12 December of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls Guinea a unitary republic, indivisible, secular, democratic and social.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Guinée,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0193fr/CA0193FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guinea,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guinea. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Guinea</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Guinea_2010. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Guinea — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gn.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Guinea — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=gn. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Flag_of_Guinea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Guinea.svg)","alt":"The flag of Guinea: three vertical bands of red, yellow and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Mount_Nimba_Strict_Nature_Reserve-108445.jpg/1920px-Mount_Nimba_Strict_Nature_Reserve-108445.jpg","title":"Mount Nimba, Guinea","desc":"Mount Nimba.","credit":"Guy Debonnet, CC BY-SA 3.0 igo, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Nimba_Strict_Nature_Reserve-108445.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Nimba."}},
{"id":"gw-076","num":76,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Benin","answerText":"Benin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975 and 1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Benin"},"facts":[["Capital","Porto-Novo"],["Population","15.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Cotonou"],["Area","114,763 km² (44,310 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","benin"],"abstract":"<b>Benin</b> is a narrow West African country on the northern shore of the Gulf of Guinea, running from 6°10' to 12°25' north and lying between 0°45' and 3°55' east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 7 million hectares of it are potentially cultivable, something like 63 per cent of the whole, though only a fraction of that is worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leached tropical ferruginous and ferrallitic soils cover roughly four fifths of the ground, with vertic soils over another twentieth, and the vegetation is heavily degraded into patches of forest and savannah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two clear climate zones divide it with a transition between them: a subequatorial south with two rainy seasons a year, a continental tropical north with one, and a subsudanian belt across the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain runs from 700 to 1,300 mm (28 to 51 inches) a year over 70 to 110 days, varying so much in time and place that rainfed cropping is a gamble, and mean maxima run from 28 to 33.5 °C (82 to 92 °F) against minima of 24.5 to 27.5 °C (76 to 82 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> More than half the people live in the south on less than an eighth of the territory, at densities between 220 and 442 to the square kilometre against 24 to 28 in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Farming is the country's main economic activity, and the cotton it grows in the north is the crop the export trade rests on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under French sovereignty and became independent, as the Republic of Dahomey, on 1 August 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year, became the People's Republic of Bénin in 1975 and took its present name in 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its constitution declares the state an independent sovereign republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Benin,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9863fr/I9863FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Benin,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/benin. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Benin</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Benin_1990. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Benin — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bj.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Benin — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=bj. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/Flag_of_Benin.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Benin.svg)","alt":"The flag of Benin: a green vertical band at the hoist beside horizontal bands of yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Ganvi%C3%A9_fishing_village_on_stilts_in_Benin_%2810282059623%29_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Ganvi%C3%A9_fishing_village_on_stilts_in_Benin_%2810282059623%29_%282%29.jpg","title":"Ganvie, Benin","desc":"Ganvie.","credit":"jbdodane, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ganvi%C3%A9_fishing_village_on_stilts_in_Benin_(10282059623)_(2).jpg","alt":"A view of Ganvie."}},
{"id":"gw-077","num":77,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Rwanda","answerText":"Rwanda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Rwanda"},"facts":[["Capital","Kigali"],["Population","15.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kigali"],["Area","26,338 km² (10,169 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","rwanda"],"abstract":"<b>Rwanda</b> is a landlocked country in the great lakes region of central Africa, standing at an average altitude of 1,250 m (4,100 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land tilts from west to east, rising from the south-eastern plateaux to a mountain chain called the Congo-Nile Crest, between 2,200 and 3,000 m (7,200 and 9,800 feet), and to a line of volcanoes reaching 4,507 m (14,787 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is made of a multitude of hills and steep mountains and is badly exposed to erosion; half the country suffers moderate to severe loss of soil, and two thirds of the cultivated ground is acid and exhausted because there is no land to spare for fallow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Height rather than latitude gives it a temperate tropical climate despite the equator, with a remarkably steady temperature through the year and three agro-climatic regions: high altitude at 1,300 to 2,000 mm (51 to 79 inches) of rain and 16 to 17 °C (61 to 63 °F); a central plateau at 1,200 to 1,400 mm (47 to 55 inches) and 18 to 21 °C (64 to 70 °F); and eastern plateau and western lowlands at 700 to 1,400 mm (28 to 55 inches) and 20 to 24 °C (68 to 75 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The year runs in four seasons — a short rains from mid-September to December carrying 30 per cent of the annual fall, a short dry spell, a long rains from March to May carrying 48 per cent, and a long dry season to mid-September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Congo-Nile Crest is the watershed: three quarters of the country drains to the upper Nile through the Nyabarongo and the Akagera, and the remaining quarter to the Congo, while water covers about 8 per cent of the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its forests have been logged heavily and a thousand hectares of the Karama savannah forest have been turned over to farmland and grazing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became independent on 1 July 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 18 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls the Rwandan state an independent, sovereign, democratic, social and secular republic, founded on government of Rwandans, by Rwandans and for Rwandans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Rwanda,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0206fr/CA0206FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Rwanda,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/rwanda. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Rwanda</i>, article 4, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Rwanda_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Rwanda — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/rw.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Rwanda — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=rw. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Flag_of_Rwanda.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rwanda.svg)","alt":"The flag of Rwanda: horizontal bands of sky blue, yellow and green, with a golden sun in the upper right."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Volcanoes_National_Park_Banner_Image.gif","title":"Volcanoes National Park, Rwanda","desc":"Volcanoes National Park.","credit":"Volcanoes National Park, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volcanoes_National_Park_Banner_Image.gif","alt":"A view of Volcanoes National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-533","num":533,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Algiers","answerText":"Algiers","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of peace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 March 1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1967</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Algeria","dot":"Algiers"},"facts":[["Country","Algeria"],["Population","2.70M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+13.3% (1998–2008)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","algeria"],"abstract":"<b>Algiers</b> recognised the United States 167 years before the United States recognised the country it is now the capital of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At American independence it was a nominal vassal of the Ottoman Empire with the power to conduct its own foreign relations, and in 1785 its dey declared war on the new republic and captured several of its ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain had been quick to tell the Barbary states that American vessels no longer had its navy's protection, and the Confederation government could raise neither a fleet nor the tribute that would buy peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recognition came by treaty: the Treaty of Peace and Amity was signed here on 5 September 1795, its original in Turkish, its first article promising peace between the citizens of the United States and Hassan Bashaw, dey of Algiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It freed 83 American captives and committed the United States to paying tribute, and the Senate consented to it the following March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> American consular representation began on 4 March 1796 when Joel Barlow presented credentials to the dey, John Paul Jones having been appointed in 1792 and died before reaching the post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That presence was interrupted from July 1812 to July 1815 by the second Barbary war, and ended when France conquered the city in 1830, an appointee choosing not to take up his post at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> France incorporated the coast into itself in 1848 as the departments of Algiers, Oran and Constantine, and self-rule returned only when Algerians voted for independence on 1 July 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country two days later, and the consulate general here was raised to embassy rank on 29 September 1962 with William J. Porter as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were severed again on 6 June 1967 after that month's Arab-Israeli war, American interests passing to an interests section in the Swiss embassy, and both embassies reopened on 12 November 1974, twelve years after the country took the United Nations seat granted it by resolution 1754 (XVII).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Algeria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Algeria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/algeria. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Barbary Wars, 1801–1805 and 1815–1816,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/barbary-wars. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace and Amity, Signed at Algiers September 5, 1795,” The Barbary Treaties 1786–1816, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/bar1795t.asp. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Democratic and Popular Republic of Algeria to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1754 (XVII), adopted at the 1146th plenary meeting, 8 October 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/204478. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/The_Bay_of_Algiers.jpg/1920px-The_Bay_of_Algiers.jpg","title":"Algiers","desc":"Algiers, seen from a distance.","credit":"Manini row, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bay_of_Algiers.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Algiers."}},
{"id":"gw-078","num":78,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Burundi","answerText":"Burundi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Burundi"},"facts":[["Capital","Gitega"],["Population","14.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bujumbura"],["Area","27,834 km² (10,747 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","burundi"],"abstract":"<b>Burundi</b> is a wholly landlocked country lying between 2°45' and 4°28' south and between 28°50' and 30°50' east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ground carries most of the relief forms of East Africa, from the Imbo, a subsided plain filled with sediment at the north end of Lake Tanganyika, up to 2,670 m (8,760 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That range lets eleven natural regions be told apart and gathered into five climate zones: the Tanganyika shore at 774 to 1,000 m (2,540 to 3,280 feet), tropical and hot at about 23 °C (73 °F) with 800 to 1,000 mm (31 to 39 inches) of rain and a dry season of five or six months, over 7 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Above it the western escarpments run from 1,000 to 2,000 m (3,300 to 6,600 feet) at 1,100 to 1,800 mm (43 to 71 inches), the central plateaux at 1,500 to 2,000 m (4,900 to 6,600 feet) take 1,150 to 1,500 mm (45 to 59 inches) at a cool 16 to 18 °C (61 to 64 °F) and cover 44 per cent of the country, and the Congo-Nile crest above 2,000 m (6,600 feet) takes 1,500 to 2,000 mm (59 to 79 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rains fall from September to April, heaviest in April, thinning or stopping for some weeks between mid-December and mid-February, and drying up in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its three great lakes all lie on its frontiers, among them Tanganyika, one of the deepest in the world at 1,453 m (4,767 feet), and the shallow northern lakes Cohoha and Rweru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine tenths of the people work in agriculture, which produces half of what the country makes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kingdom of Burundi became formally independent on 1 July 1962, when the United Nations ended the trusteeship over Ruanda-Urundi that Belgium had administered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 18 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls Burundi an independent, sovereign, secular, democratic and unitary republic that respects its ethnic and religious diversity, and declares the national territory inalienable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Burundi,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9865fr/I9865FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burundi,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burundi. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Burundi</i>, articles 1 and 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Burundi_2018. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Burundi — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bi.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Burundi — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=bi. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Flag_of_Burundi.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Burundi.svg)","alt":"The flag of Burundi: a white saltire dividing red and green fields, with a white disc at the centre bearing three red stars."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Lake_Tanganyika.jpg/1920px-Lake_Tanganyika.jpg","title":"Lake Tanganyika, Burundi","desc":"Lake Tanganyika.","credit":"Orrling, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Tanganyika.jpg","alt":"A long lake with hills along the far shore."}},
{"id":"gw-079","num":79,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bolivia","answerText":"Bolivia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bolivia"},"facts":[["Capital","Sucre"],["Population","12.6M (2025)"],["Largest city","Santa Cruz"],["Area","1,098,581 km² (424,164 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","bolivia"],"abstract":"<b>Bolivia</b> is a landlocked country in west-central South America, lying between 10°23' and 22°53' south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its temperature runs the whole way from equatorial lowland to arctic cold, and in the Andes both heat and rain are set by altitude, with cold winds sweeping the Altiplano and little to tell summer from winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that high plateau mean temperatures run between 7 and 11 °C (45 and 52 °F) and rain is scarce, mostly from summer storms in December and January, falling from 550 mm (22 inches) in the north of it to 300 mm (12 inches) in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Yungas valleys below are filled all year with humid cloud off the Amazon at 16 to 19 °C (61 to 66 °F) and about 1,350 mm (53 inches) of rain, while the eastern lowlands are warm at 23 to 27 °C (73 to 81 °F) and take 1,000 to 1,750 mm (39 to 69 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cold winds called surazos blow up from the south there, loaded with sand and dust, dropping the temperature suddenly; the driest corner of the country is the south-west, under 100 mm (4 inches) a year, and the wettest the north, where Pando records 2,200 mm (87 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rain averages 1,146 mm (45 inches) over the whole territory, and three watersheds share it: the Amazon over 66 per cent of the country, the Plata over 20, and the closed basin of the Altiplano with Lake Titicaca and the salt flats of Uyuni and Coipasa over the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has both an administrative capital and a constitutional one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence from Spain was declared on 6 August 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has held a seat at the United Nations since 14 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 2009, constitutes the country a unitary social state of plurinational communitarian law, free, independent, sovereign and democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Bolivia (Estado Plurinacional de),” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0439es/CA0439ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bolivia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bolivia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Plurinational State of Bolivia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Bolivia_2009. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Bolivia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bo.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Bolivia — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=bo. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Flag_of_Bolivia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bolivia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bolivia: three horizontal bands of red, yellow and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Vista_del_Salar_de_Uyuni.JPG/1920px-Vista_del_Salar_de_Uyuni.JPG","title":"Salar de Uyuni, Bolivia","desc":"The Salar de Uyuni.","credit":"Ximena Medina Sancho, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_del_Salar_de_Uyuni.JPG","alt":"A white salt flat running to the horizon under a deep blue sky."}},
{"id":"gw-080","num":80,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tunisia","answerText":"Tunisia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1797</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tunisia"},"facts":[["Capital","Tunis"],["Population","12.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tunis"],["Area","163,610 km² (63,170 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","tunisia"],"abstract":"<b>Tunisia</b> stands at the north of Africa with 1,300 km (810 miles) of coast on the Mediterranean, and about 11,160 km² (4,310 sq mi) of its ground is lake and salt flat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physiographic regions make it up: mountains in the north-west, east of the El-Talli and Saharan Atlas ranges that run in from the west and reach 1,500 m (4,900 feet) here, and are crossed by the only permanent rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then mountains in the south tilting east to the coastal plains and west to desert plains under dunes; the coastal plains themselves, broad and running the length of the sea; and the desert plains that are the northern limit of the Sahara, holding several salt flats of which the largest, the Chott El Jerid, covers 5,000 km² (1,900 sq mi) and lies 15 m (49 feet) below sea level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is essentially Mediterranean, and rain averages only 207 mm (8 inches) a year, a figure that can fall to a third of that or rise to three times it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls very unevenly: 594 mm (23 inches) in the north, 296 mm (12 inches) in the centre, 156 mm (6 inches) in the south and under 100 mm (4 inches) in the far south-west, with four fifths of it between October and March and potential evapotranspiration running from 1,200 to 1,800 mm (47 to 71 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Standing on the southern rim of the Mediterranean basin at a crossroads of ancient civilisations, it holds a wide variety of habitats and ecosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country was a state under nominal Ottoman dependence that conducted its own foreign policy and concluded treaties in its own name, and it signed a treaty of peace and friendship in 1797.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was restored in 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 12 November of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls Tunisia a free, independent and sovereign state whose system is republican.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Tunisie,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0212fr/CA0212FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tunisia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tunisia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Tunisia</i>, articles 1 and 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Tunisia_2022. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Tunisia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tn.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Tunisia — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=tn. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Flag_of_Tunisia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tunisia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tunisia: a red field with a white disc at the centre bearing a red crescent and five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/The_Amphitheatre_of_El_Jem%2C_built_around_AD_238_in_Thysdrus_in_Africa_Proconsularis%2C_the_estimated_capacity_is_35%2C000%2C_Tunisia_-_52717762494.jpg/1920px-The_Amphitheatre_of_El_Jem%2C_built_around_AD_238_in_Thysdrus_in_Africa_Proconsularis%2C_the_estimated_capacity_is_35%2C000%2C_Tunisia_-_52717762494.jpg","title":"Amphitheatre of El Jem, Tunisia","desc":"Amphitheatre of El Jem.","credit":"Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Amphitheatre_of_El_Jem,_built_around_AD_238_in_Thysdrus_in_Africa_Proconsularis,_the_estimated_capacity_is_35,000,_Tunisia_-_52717762494.jpg","alt":"A view of Amphitheatre of El Jem."}},
{"id":"gw-534","num":534,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Baghdad","answerText":"Baghdad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 March 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iraq","dot":"Baghdad"},"facts":[["Country","Iraq"],["Population","7.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","iraq"],"abstract":"<b>Baghdad</b> had an American consul before the country around it existed: John Henry Haynes was appointed on 10 August 1888, when the city was Ottoman, and presented his credentials here on 8 January 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Iraq on 9 January 1930, when its ambassador in London signed the Anglo-American-Iraqi Convention, whose preamble states that the United States recognises Iraq as an independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same treaty acknowledged the special relations between Britain and Iraq, then a mandate, and Iraq ended that status in 1932, taking a founding United Nations seat in December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation here opened on 30 March 1931 under Alexander K. Sloan, who was already the consul, and was raised to embassy rank on 28 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One curiosity of the record: when Iraq and Jordan formed an Arab Union in May 1958 the United States recognised it but established no relations with it, relations with each kingdom continuing unbroken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The union was dissolved after a coup here on 14 July 1958, and Iraq severed relations with the United States on 7 June 1967 in the wake of that month's Arab-Israeli war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American interests section opened in the Belgian embassy here on 1 October 1972 and was raised to an embassy again on 26 November 1984, by agreement between President Ronald Reagan and Foreign Minister Tariq Aziz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It closed on 12 January 1991 after the invasion of Kuwait that the Security Council had condemned at its 2932nd meeting on 2 August 1990, and Iraq severed relations again that February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Coalition forces invaded on 20 March 2003 and seized the city on 9 April, the Coalition Provisional Authority followed on 12 May, and it transferred sovereignty to an interim government on 28 June 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy reopened inside the Green Zone that same day, twenty days after the Security Council endorsed the incoming government by resolution 1546 (2004), in a country now counted at 47 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iraq,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/iq.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iraq,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iraq. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1546 (2004),” adopted at its 4987th meeting, 8 June 2004, S/RES/1546(2004), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/523025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 660 (1990),” adopted at its 2932nd meeting, 2 August 1990, S/RES/660(1990), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/94220. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Iraq,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRQ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Tigris_River_in_Baghdad_%282016%29.jpg/1920px-Tigris_River_in_Baghdad_%282016%29.jpg","title":"Baghdad","desc":"Baghdad, seen from a distance.","credit":"Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff from Washington D.C, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tigris_River_in_Baghdad_(2016).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Baghdad."}},
{"id":"gw-081","num":81,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"South Sudan","answerText":"South Sudan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 2011</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"S. Sudan"},"facts":[["Capital","Juba"],["Population","12.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Juba"],["Area","646,883 km² (249,762 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","south sudan"],"abstract":"<b>South Sudan</b> is the newest country in Africa, a landlocked state made from about 30 per cent of what was Sudan before 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its dominant feature is the White Nile, running over clay plains that rise gradually southwards into mountains reaching 3,000 m (9,800 feet) in the Imatong Hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sudd, the inland delta of that river, is one of the largest swamps on earth, a mesh of lakes, marshes and floodplains, parts of it infested with insects dangerous to people and to livestock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the country has a sub-humid climate: rain averages about 900 mm (35 inches) a year, from 700 mm (28 inches) in the eastern lowlands to 2,200 mm (87 inches) in Western Equatoria and the eastern highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The west and centre take two wet seasons and a growing year of 280 to 300 days, while the rest have one from May to October and 130 to 150 days; temperatures are typically above 25 °C (77 °F) and can pass 35 °C (95 °F) in the January to April dry season, which sets the pastoralists moving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Forest covers roughly a third of the territory and shrubs another 39 per cent, and the mountain vegetation of the Imatong makes it one of the richest concentrations of species in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost the whole country lies in the Nile basin, and a flood wave takes months to travel down from Lake Victoria into the Sudd, which stores much of it and loses much of the rest to evaporation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory won independence from British and Egyptian rule in 1956 as part of Sudan and then went through recurring civil war, mostly between north and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A peace agreement in 2005 ended that conflict and set a referendum on self-determination for January 2011, in which voters chose independence overwhelmingly; the republic declared it on 9 July 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations five days later, on 14 July, and its constitution calls it a sovereign and independent republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — South Sudan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/i9816en/I9816EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Sudan,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-sudan. [Open access]","<i>Transitional Constitution of the Republic of South Sudan</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/South_Sudan_2013. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “South Sudan — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ss.html. [Open access]","Douglas Mulangwa, Evet Naturinda, Charles Koboji, Benon T. Zaake, Emily Black, Hannah Cloke and Elisabeth M. Stephens, “Lake Victoria to the Sudd Wetland: flood wave timing, connectivity and wetland buffering across the White Nile,” <i>Hydrology and Earth System Sciences</i> 30, no. 16 (2026): 5297–5325, https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-30-5297-2026. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/Flag_of_South_Sudan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Sudan.svg)","alt":"The flag of South Sudan: horizontal bands of black, red and green separated by white stripes, with a blue triangle at the hoist bearing a gold star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Fishing_in_Sudd_wetland_-_by_CPWF_Basin_Focal_Project.jpg/1920px-Fishing_in_Sudd_wetland_-_by_CPWF_Basin_Focal_Project.jpg","title":"the Sudd, South Sudan","desc":"Fishing in the Sudd.","credit":"Water, food and livelihoods in River Basins, Photographer: Karen Conniff, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fishing_in_Sudd_wetland_-_by_CPWF_Basin_Focal_Project.jpg","alt":"A fisherman poling a dugout canoe through a wetland."}},
{"id":"gw-082","num":82,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belgium","answerText":"Belgium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 January 1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the Dutch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 October 1919</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belgium"},"facts":[["Capital","Brussels"],["Population","11.8M (2025)"],["Largest city","Brussels"],["Area","30,689 km² (11,849 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","belgium"],"abstract":"<b>Belgium</b> was recognised by three different sets of people on three different dates, and the last of them was the country it had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared independence from the Kingdom of the Netherlands on 4 October 1830, and most of the European powers recognised that independence in fact on 20 December of the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 6 January 1832, when the Department of State issued an exequatur to the Belgian consul at New York, which is a recognition delivered through a commercial appointment rather than a diplomatic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations followed on 18 June 1832, when Baron D. Behr sent his full powers to Secretary of State Edward Livingston, and the American legation at Brussels opened on 25 September when Hugh Legaré presented credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Netherlands itself held out until the Treaty of London of 19 April 1839, nine years after the declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation became an embassy on 3 October 1919, and the embassy closed on 15 July 1940 after the German invasion, a chargé d'affaires being accredited that November to the Belgian government established in England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reopened at Brussels on 14 September 1944 under Ernest de W. Mayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was a founding member of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948 and of the World Trade Organization from 1 January 1995, and joined the United Nations on 27 December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has been a member of the European Union since 1 January 1958, one of the six that founded it, and a party to Schengen since 26 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three institutions give its area three different figures, 30,528, 30,667 and 30,689 square kilometres, a spread of half a per cent between the smallest and the largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Belgium,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/be.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belgium,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belgium. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Belgium and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/belgium_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Belgium – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/belgium_en?etrans=en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Belgium,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_Belgium_%28civil%29.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belgium_(civil).svg)","alt":"The flag of Belgium: three vertical bands of black, yellow and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Grand-Place%2C_Brussels_-_panorama%2C_June_2018.jpg/1920px-Grand-Place%2C_Brussels_-_panorama%2C_June_2018.jpg","title":"Grand-Place, Belgium","desc":"Grand-Place.","credit":"Celuici, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand-Place,_Brussels_-_panorama,_June_2018.jpg","alt":"A view of Grand-Place."}},
{"id":"gw-083","num":83,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Haiti","answerText":"Haiti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1804</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Haiti"},"facts":[["Capital","Port-au-Prince"],["Population","12.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Port-au-Prince"],["Area","27,750 km² (10,710 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","haiti"],"abstract":"<b>Haiti</b> occupies a third of the island of Hispaniola, between the Atlantic Ocean and the Caribbean Sea, and is the third largest country of the Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is made of a main landmass and satellite islands — Gonâve, Tortue, the Cayemites and Île-à-Vache — and the terrain of all of them is steep, with abrupt mountain chains rising to 2,680 m (8,790 feet) at Pic la Selle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covered three fifths of the country in 1960 and under 100,000 hectares by 2011, about 3.5 per cent of it; with roughly half the ground on slopes steeper than 40 per cent, that clearance for firewood has caused serious erosion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heavy and irregular rain marks its humid tropical climate, a consequence of those mountains, averaging 1,440 mm (57 inches) a year but ranging from 800 to 3,200 mm (31 to 126 inches) with the relief and the winds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seasons differ by region: two rainy seasons in the south, one from April to October in the centre, and a long one in the north from September to June peaking in November and December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Mean temperature is 25 °C (77 °F) and varies more between day and night, by 10 to 12 °C (18 to 22 °F), than between the warmest and coolest months; between June and November tropical depressions can build into storms and hurricanes, and on average a devastating one arrives every five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For all its environmental damage the country still has, with its neighbour on the island, the second most varied flora in the Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is the second oldest republic in the western hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It won its independence from France in 1804.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, and its constitution makes French and Creole the languages that unite all Haitians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Profil de Pays AQUASTAT — Haïti,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0446fr/CA0446FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Haiti,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/haiti. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Haiti</i>, article 8, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Haiti_2012. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Haiti — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ht.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Haiti — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ht. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Flag_of_Haiti.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Haiti.svg)","alt":"The flag of Haiti: horizontal bands of blue and red with the national coat of arms on a white panel at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Citadelle_Laferri%C3%A8re.jpg/1920px-Citadelle_Laferri%C3%A8re.jpg","title":"Citadelle Laferrière, Haiti","desc":"Citadelle Laferrière.","credit":"Rémi Kaupp, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citadelle_Laferri%C3%A8re.jpg","alt":"A view of Citadelle Laferrière."}},
{"id":"gw-535","num":535,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Buenos Aires","answerText":"Buenos Aires","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 1816</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised by the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation moved away</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation returned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 February 1862</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Argentina","dot":"Buenos Aires"},"facts":[["Country","Argentina"],["Population","15.1M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+8.0% (2010–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","argentina"],"abstract":"<b>Buenos Aires</b> was itself the thing the United States recognised: the entry in the American guide is headed with the city's name, not the country's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Government of Buenos Ayres, which the guide calls the predecessor of Argentina, had declared independence from Spain on 9 July 1816 and was recognised on 27 January 1823, when Caesar Rodney was appointed minister plenipotentiary to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rodney presented his credentials on 27 December that year, which is the date relations began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mission then left the city for five years, because the country it served had split in two: Centralists here repudiated the constitution the Federalist provinces ratified in 1854 and declared themselves independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> James A. Peden held letters of credence to both sides, resided here until about 1 May 1857, then closed the legation and moved it to Paraná.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He brought it back on 25 February 1862, after the Argentine Confederation collapsed and the country was reunified as the Argentine Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation was raised to embassy rank on 1 October 1914, and Frederic Jessup Stimson presented his credentials as the first ambassador on 8 January 1915.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were then interrupted without being severed: after a coup, the United States judged the Farrell government of 1944 not in sympathy with the declared Argentine policy of joining the defence of the hemisphere, and told its ambassador not to enter into official relations with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ambassador Norman Armour was recalled to Washington on 27 June 1944 and a chargé d'affaires ran the embassy here until relations resumed on 19 April 1945, a decision taken at an inter-American conference in Mexico City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city now holds about 15.1 million people in its urban area against a national 45.9 million, a third of the whole country, which itself came to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade only in 1967 and whose land the two international counts measure 16,027 square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Argentina,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ar.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Argentina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/argentina. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Argentina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Argentina and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/argentina_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Argentina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Buenos_Aires_skyline_day.jpg/1920px-Buenos_Aires_skyline_day.jpg","title":"Buenos Aires","desc":"Buenos Aires, seen from a distance.","credit":"Luis Argerich from Buenos Aires, Argentina, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buenos_Aires_skyline_day.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Buenos Aires."}},
{"id":"gw-084","num":84,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Jordan","answerText":"Jordan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Jordan"},"facts":[["Capital","Amman"],["Population","11.5M (2025)"],["Largest city","Amman"],["Area","89,318 km² (34,486 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","jordan"],"abstract":"<b>Jordan</b> lies east of the Jordan River and is divided into twelve governorates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physiographic regions make it up, the first being the Jordan Rift Valley along its western side, about 5,000 km² (1,900 sq mi), which begins at Lake Tiberias 212 m (696 feet) below sea level and runs south through the Jordan Valley to the Dead Sea at 417 m (1,368 feet) below it, and on through the Wadi Araba to the Gulf of Aqaba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> East of that rift stand the Highlands, another 5,000 km² (1,900 sq mi) of mountain ranges and plains between 600 and 1,600 m (2,000 and 5,200 feet), cut by side wadis sloping back down into the valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then come the plains, about 10,000 km² (3,900 sq mi) running north to south, and beyond them the Al-Badiah desert region in the east, some 69,000 km² (26,600 sq mi) and an extension of the Arabian Desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is semitropical in the rift, Mediterranean in the highlands and continental in the eastern desert and plains, so that winter is warm in the valley, cool in the highlands and extremely cold and dry in the desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rain usually falls between October and May and runs from 50 mm (2 inches) in the eastern and southern deserts to 650 mm (26 inches) in the northern highlands; more than 91 per cent of the country gets under 200 mm (8 inches), and the long-run average is 94 mm (3.7 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four major ecosystems — desert, scarp and highland, sub-tropical and Mediterranean — give it a variety its size would not suggest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kingdom of Transjordan was a mandated territory placed under British protection after the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has held a seat at the United Nations since 14 December 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls the Hashemite Kingdom an independent sovereign Arab state, indivisible and unable to cede any part of itself, whose people are part of the Arab nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Jordan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/CA0219EN/ca0219en.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Jordan,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/jordan. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Jordan_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Jordan — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jo.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Jordan — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=jo. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c0/Flag_of_Jordan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jordan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Jordan: horizontal bands of black, white and green with a red triangle at the hoist bearing a white seven-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Al_khazneh.jpg/1920px-Al_khazneh.jpg","title":"Petra, Jordan","desc":"Petra.","credit":"Susanahajer, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_khazneh.jpg","alt":"A view of Petra."}},
{"id":"gw-085","num":85,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dominican Republic","answerText":"Dominican Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1844</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spanish rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861–65</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dominican Rep."},"facts":[["Capital","Santo Domingo"],["Population","11.5M (2025)"],["Largest city","Santo Domingo"],["Area","48,671 km² (18,792 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","dominican republic"],"abstract":"The <b>Dominican Republic</b> holds the eastern part of Hispaniola, the second largest island of the Greater Antilles, measuring 390 km (240 miles) at its widest from east to west and 265 km (165 miles) from north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four main mountain ranges cross it: the Cordillera Septentrional, the Cordillera Central through the middle of the country, where Pico Duarte reaches 3,175 m (10,417 feet), and the smaller Sierra de Neiba and Sierra de Bahoruco in the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three great valleys lie between them — the Cibao, the San Juan and the Enriquillo basin — each one held between two of those ranges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is markedly humid tropical, but being an island and having such broken relief it runs locally from arid to very wet, with lowland temperatures between 23 and 33 °C (73 and 91 °F) all year and frosts common on the high peaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,410 mm (56 inches) a year and varies from 350 mm (14 inches) in the Enriquillo basin to 2,743 mm (108 inches) in the Cordillera Oriental, with a dry season from December to March and a wet one from May to November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The north-eastern trade winds blow across it for more than half the year and soak the northern flank of the Cordillera Septentrional, and hurricanes are frequent in the wet months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies in the Caribbean Islands biodiversity hotspot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory was under French and then Spanish rule from as early as the 16th century, and it declared itself an independent nation in 1844.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reverted to Spanish rule in 1861 and won its independence again in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, and its constitution says the Dominican people constitute a nation organised as a free and independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — República Dominicana,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0414es/CA0414ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Dominican Republic,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/dominican-republic. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Dominican Republic</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Dominican_Republic_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Dominican Republic — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/do.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Dominican Republic — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=do. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Flag_of_the_Dominican_Republic.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dominican_Republic.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Dominican Republic: a white cross dividing blue and red quarters, with the national coat of arms at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Dominikanische_Republik_Santo_Domingo_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Col%C3%B3n_01.jpg/1920px-Dominikanische_Republik_Santo_Domingo_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Col%C3%B3n_01.jpg","title":"the Alcázar de Colón, the Dominican Republic","desc":"The Alcázar de Colón in Santo Domingo.","credit":"Georg Karl Ell, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominikanische_Republik_Santo_Domingo_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Col%C3%B3n_01.jpg","alt":"A stone Renaissance palace with two tiers of arches."}},
{"id":"gw-086","num":86,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"United Arab Emirates","answerText":"United Arab Emirates","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1971</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United Arab Emirates"},"facts":[["Capital","Abu Dhabi"],["Population","11.5M (2025)"],["Largest city","Dubai"],["Area","83,600 km² (32,300 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","united arab emirates"],"abstract":"The <b>United Arab Emirates</b> is a federation of seven emirates in the eastern corner of the Arabian Peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six of them lie along the Persian Gulf and the seventh, Fujayrah, on the eastern coast of the peninsula with direct access to the Gulf of Oman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of its territory, 77,700 km² (30,000 sq mi) is mainland and the rest is a shallow marine shelf carrying many islands and coral reefs; the islands, generally uninhabited, come to about 5,900 km² (2,300 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three ecological areas divide it — the north-eastern mountains, the sand and desert of the interior, and the marine coastal zone — and the largest of the seven emirates holds almost 87 per cent of the mainland by itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is arid with very high summer temperatures: the interior desert reaches about 50 °C (122 °F) and cools to around 4 °C (39 °F) in winter, while the coast where most people live reaches 46 °C (115 °F) at full humidity and has mild winters between 14 and 23 °C (57 and 73 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rain averages 78 mm (3 inches) a year, from under 40 mm (1.6 inches) around Liwa in the southern desert to 160 mm (6 inches) in the north-eastern mountains, falling on between nine and nineteen days, more than four fifths of it between December and March, and there is not a single perennial stream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That heat and dryness sharply limit the variety of life it can hold, which is grouped into three main kinds of ecosystem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The emirates were under British protection as the Trucial Sheikdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They formed a federation and gained independence in 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 9 December of that year, and its constitution calls the federation an independent and sovereign state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — United Arab Emirates,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0335en/CA0335EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The United Arab Emirates,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/united-arab-emirates. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the United Arab Emirates</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/United_Arab_Emirates_2009. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “United Arab Emirates — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ae.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “United Arab Emirates — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ae. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Flag_of_the_United_Arab_Emirates.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Arab_Emirates.svg)","alt":"The flag of the United Arab Emirates: horizontal bands of green, white and black with a red vertical band at the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Burj_Khalifa_%28worlds_tallest_building%29_and_the_Dubai_skyline_%2825781049892%29.jpg/1920px-Burj_Khalifa_%28worlds_tallest_building%29_and_the_Dubai_skyline_%2825781049892%29.jpg","title":"Burj Khalifa, the United Arab Emirates","desc":"Burj Khalifa.","credit":"imran shahabuddin, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burj_Khalifa_(worlds_tallest_building)_and_the_Dubai_skyline_(25781049892).jpg","alt":"A view of Burj Khalifa."}},
{"id":"gw-536","num":536,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kabul","answerText":"Kabul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 June 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 January 1989</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Operations suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Afghanistan","dot":"Kabul"},"facts":[["Country","Afghanistan"],["Population","4.10M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+24.8% (2020–2025)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","afghanistan"],"abstract":"<b>Kabul</b> has held an American mission that arrived 21 years after the recognition it belonged to, was raised, was left without an ambassador, closed, reopened and finally moved to another country altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Afghanistan on 26 July 1921, when President Warren Harding received an Afghan mission at the White House, British authorities having notified the State Department in August 1919 that the country was officially free and independent in its affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations followed 14 years later, on 4 May 1935, and even then the minister who presented credentials was accredited to Persia as well and resided at Tehran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation in the city itself opened only on 6 June 1942 under Charles W. Thayer, 21 years after the recognition, and became an embassy on 5 June 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 14 February 1979 the American ambassador here, Adolph Dubs, was assassinated at post, and from his death the mission was headed by a chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy closed on 30 January 1989, over concerns that the incoming government would not be able to protect diplomats once Soviet forces had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It returned as a liaison office on 17 December 2001, five weeks after the city fell to a coalition campaign that had begun on 7 October, and the United States recognised the Interim Authority on 22 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The office was re-designated an embassy on 17 January 2002, and on 31 August 2021 that embassy suspended operations and transferred them to Doha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country took its United Nations seat on 19 November 1946, the Security Council having recommended it at the 57th meeting on 29 August that year, in one resolution covering Afghanistan, Iceland and Sweden together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city holds about 4.11 million people, roughly one Afghan in eleven of the 43.8 million the two international counts agree on, and the country reached the World Trade Organization only on 29 July 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Afghanistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/af.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Afghanistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/afghanistan. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 8 (1946),” adopted at its 57th meeting, 29 August 1946, S/RES/8(1946), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/111971. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Afghanistan and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/afghanistan_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Afghanistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AFG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Kabul%2C_Afghanistan_-_panoramio_%281%29.jpg","title":"Kabul","desc":"Kabul, seen from a distance.","credit":"seair21, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kabul,_Afghanistan_-_panoramio_(1).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kabul."}},
{"id":"gw-087","num":87,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cuba","answerText":"Cuba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spain withdraws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Own government</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1902</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cuba"},"facts":[["Capital","Havana"],["Population","10.9M (2025)"],["Largest city","Havana"],["Area","109,884 km² (42,426 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","cuba"],"abstract":"<b>Cuba</b> is an archipelago of one large island, the Isla de la Juventud and some 1,600 islets, standing in the Caribbean at the mouth of the Gulf of Mexico as the westernmost of the Greater Antilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies between 19°49' and 23°16' north, the main island accounting for 104,550 km² (40,370 sq mi) of the territory, the Isla de la Juventud 2,204 km² (851 sq mi) and the offshore cays the remainder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its relief is complex but about 70 per cent of the ground is flat, with four main upland cores: the Guaniguanico range in the west, the Guamuhaya mountains in the south-centre, the Nipe-Sagua-Baracoa mountains in the east and the Sierra Maestra, where Pico Real del Turquino rises to 1,974 m (6,476 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is predominantly tropical, governed by the North Atlantic high and by winds out of the north-east in winter and the east-north-east in summer, with the warm Gulf Stream moderating it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,335 mm (53 inches) a year, four fifths of it between May and October, thinning from 1,437 mm (57 inches) in the west to 1,279 mm (50 inches) in the east, though Baracoa in the east records 2,518 mm (99 inches) because of its altitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Mean temperature is 24.5 °C (76 °F) with very little swing, 26 °C (79 °F) in January against 28 °C (82 °F) in August, and between June and November the island is frequently exposed to hurricanes and tropical storms that have done heavy damage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries 42 kinds of ecosystem and 23 kinds of landscape, from arid and semi-arid ground to humid tropical forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Spain relinquished sovereignty over the island after its defeat in the war of 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Cuban government took full control of the state's affairs in 1902.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a founding member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, and its constitution calls Cuba a democratic, independent and sovereign socialist state of law and social justice, an indivisible and unitary republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “Perfil de País AQUASTAT — Cuba,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0413es/CA0413ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cuba,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cuba. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Cuba</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Cuba_2019. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cuba — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cu.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Cuba — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=cu. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Flag_of_Cuba.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cuba.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cuba: five blue and white stripes with a red triangle at the hoist bearing a white five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Vi%C3%B1ales_Valley.jpg/1920px-Vi%C3%B1ales_Valley.jpg","title":"Viñales Valley, Cuba","desc":"Viñales Valley.","credit":"Severin.stalder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vi%C3%B1ales_Valley.jpg","alt":"A view of Viñales Valley."}},
{"id":"gw-088","num":88,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Czechia","answerText":"Czechia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federal republic ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 December 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council recommendation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 January 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Czechia"},"facts":[["Capital","Prague"],["Population","10.9M (2025)"],["Largest city","Prague"],["Area","78,871 km² (30,452 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","czechia"],"abstract":"<b>Czechia</b> came into being at midnight and was recognised the same day: the Czech and Slovak Federal Republic ceased to exist on 31 December 1992, and the United States formally recognised the new state on 1 January 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on that date as well, so recognition, relations and independence all share a single day, and the White House press secretary marked it by saying the United States looked forward to full and mutually productive relations with the new Czech state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The break is known as the Velvet Divorce, and the American guide uses that name for it in its own text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No new embassy had to be opened: Adrian A. Basora, ambassador to the federal republic since June 1992, simply served as ambassador to the new country from 1 January 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Security Council recommended admission to the United Nations at its 3158th meeting on 8 January, and the country took its seat on 19 January 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 15 April 1993 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has belonged to the European Union since 1 May 2004 and to the Schengen area since 21 December 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is a case where the United Nations count is the outlier: that source gives 10.61 million for 2025 where Eurostat gives 10.91 million and the World Bank 10.89 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The figure used here follows the two that agree, which is the practice wherever a country runs its own statistical service and the World Bank reports what that service publishes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On area there is no such argument: all three sources give 78,871 square kilometres, and about one Czech in eight lives in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Czechia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Czechia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/czech-republic. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 801 (1993),” adopted at its 3158th meeting, 8 January 1993, S/RES/801(1993), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/156781. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Czech Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/czech_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Czechia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/czechia_en?etrans=en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Czechia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CZE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Flag_of_the_Czech_Republic.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Czech_Republic.svg)","alt":"The flag of Czechia: horizontal white and red bands with a blue triangle at the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Charles_Bridge_%28Karl%C5%AFv_most%29%2C_Vltava_River%2C_Prague%2C_2015.jpg/1920px-Charles_Bridge_%28Karl%C5%AFv_most%29%2C_Vltava_River%2C_Prague%2C_2015.jpg","title":"Charles Bridge, Czechia","desc":"Charles Bridge.","credit":"Peter K Burian, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Bridge_(Karl%C5%AFv_most),_Vltava_River,_Prague,_2015.jpg","alt":"A view of Charles Bridge."}},
{"id":"gw-089","num":89,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Honduras","answerText":"Honduras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1823 – 1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 December 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1982</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Honduras"},"facts":[["Capital","Tegucigalpa"],["Population","11.0M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tegucigalpa"],["Area","112,492 km² (43,433 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","honduras"],"abstract":"<b>Honduras</b> lies in Central America between 12° and 16° north, with 880 km (550 miles) of Caribbean shore along its northern edge and a further 153 km (95 miles) on the Pacific through the Gulf of Fonseca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two mountain systems, western and eastern, are divided by the Comayagua depression, and more than three quarters of the ground slopes more steeply than one in four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four fifths of the surface stands between 600 and 2,850 m (2,000 and 9,350 feet) and a further seventh between 150 and 600 m (490 and 2,000 feet), the rest being the low valleys of the northern shore and the dry plains of the southern one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is hot and tropical in the lowlands and grades to temperate in the high country, falling into five agro-ecological zones whose mean rainfall runs from 2,400 mm (94 inches) a year on the Atlantic coast to between 400 and 1,200 mm (16 and 47 inches) in the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain comes in two peaks, in May and September, separated by a dry spell in July and August known locally as the canícula, while the Atlantic side is struck by tropical storms and hurricanes every four or five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the country stands where tropical and subtropical ecosystems converge, endemic species gather in relic pockets, above all in the cloud forests that rise beyond 1,000 m (3,300 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its water divides between two slopes: fourteen basins on the Atlantic side carry 86 per cent of the surface run-off, and five larger ones on the Pacific side the remaining 14.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence from Spain came in 1821, and two years later the country entered the Federation of Central American States, which broke up between 1838 and 1840.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 17 December 1945, in the organisation's founding year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1982, describes it as a state of law, sovereign and constituted as a free, democratic and independent republic to secure to its inhabitants justice, liberty, culture and social and economic well-being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Perfil de País — Honduras,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0420es/CA0420ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Honduras,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/honduras. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Honduras</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Honduras_2013. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Honduras — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hn.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Honduras — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=hn. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Flag_of_Honduras_%281949%E2%80%932022%2C_2026%E2%80%93present%29.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Honduras.svg)","alt":"The flag of Honduras: three horizontal bands of blue, white and blue, with five blue five-pointed stars on the white band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Copan_sculpture.jpg","title":"Copán, Honduras","desc":"Copán.","credit":"Adalberto Hernandez Vega from Copan Ruinas, Honduras, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copan_sculpture.jpg","alt":"A view of Copán."}},
{"id":"gw-537","num":537,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ottawa","answerText":"Ottawa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominion formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomy acknowledged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 November 1943</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Canada","dot":"Ottawa"},"facts":[["Country","Canada"],["Population","1.40M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","4th largest"],["Population growth","+11.8% (1986–1991)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","canada"],"abstract":"<b>Ottawa</b> received an American legation 60 years after the country it governs was formed, because for most of that time the country did not conduct its own foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Dominion of Canada was formed in 1867 but remained an integral part of the British Empire, its external affairs controlled from London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officials of the two governments dealt with each other through joint commissions and military cooperation all the same, and the two even negotiated a bilateral fisheries treaty in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was only in 1926 that the United Kingdom acknowledged full Canadian autonomy in foreign affairs, at the Imperial Conference that produced the Balfour Declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That declaration held the United Kingdom and the Dominions to be autonomous communities within the British Empire, equal in status and in no way subordinate one to another in any aspect of their domestic or external affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Recognition followed on 18 February 1927, when Vincent Massey presented credentials in Washington as Canadian envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary, and relations date from the same act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American legation here opened on 1 June 1927 under William Phillips, and was raised to embassy rank on 19 November 1943 when Ray Atherton presented credentials as ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 9 November 1945, in its founding year, and was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city itself is modest against the country: its urban area holds about 1.38 million people, roughly one Canadian in 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two international counts of the country's population differ by more than a million — 40.13 against 41.65 — and its area cannot be taken from the second at all, whose series gives 15.63 million square kilometres against the 9,984,670 the United Nations records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Canada,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ca.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Canada,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/canada. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Canada and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/canada_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Canada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Canada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ottawa_skyline_2018.jpg/1920px-Ottawa_skyline_2018.jpg","title":"Ottawa","desc":"Ottawa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Dennis G. Jarvis, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ottawa_skyline_2018.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Ottawa."}},
{"id":"gw-090","num":90,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Portugal","answerText":"Portugal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 May 1791</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 June 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation at Rio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1810 – 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Portugal"},"facts":[["Capital","Lisbon"],["Population","10.5M (2025)"],["Largest city","Lisbon"],["Area","92,230 km² (35,610 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","portugal"],"abstract":"<b>Portugal</b> was the first neutral country to open diplomatic relations with the United States, and the guide says so in its opening line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lisbon suggested an exchange of permanent missions in early 1791, which amounted to a recognition in fact, and made it formal on 13 May 1791 by accepting the credentials of the American minister David Humphreys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations and the American legation date from that same act, so this is one of the older continuous relationships in the whole record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consular presence is older still: George Washington appointed a consul for Madeira on 17 June 1790, the second consular appointment made under the American constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consulate opened in the Azores in 1795, at Ponta Delgada, is described by the State Department as the oldest continuously operating American consulate anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation left the country for eleven years without relations breaking: the king fled to Brazil during the Napoleonic wars, the mission followed him to Rio de Janeiro from 1810, and it returned to Lisbon in 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, under the single resolution that admitted several states at once, and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 6 May 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has belonged to the European Union since 1 January 1986 and to the Schengen area since 26 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is counted three ways for 2025: the United Nations gives 10.41 million, Eurostat 10.75 million and the World Bank 10.80 million, so the figure here sits inside a spread of nearly 400,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On area the three are almost identical — 92,226, 92,226 and 92,230 square kilometres — and about 28 in every hundred Portuguese live in the capital's urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Portugal,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pt.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Portugal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/portugal. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Portugal and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/portugal_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Portugal – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/portugal_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Portugal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Portugal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Flag_of_Portugal.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal.svg)","alt":"The flag of Portugal: green and red vertical bands with the national coat of arms over an armillary sphere on the division."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Bel%C3%A9m_Tower_in_Lisbon%2C_Portugal.jpg/1920px-Bel%C3%A9m_Tower_in_Lisbon%2C_Portugal.jpg","title":"Belém Tower, Portugal","desc":"Belém Tower.","credit":"Lisbon Photoshoots, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bel%C3%A9m_Tower_in_Lisbon,_Portugal.jpg","alt":"A view of Belém Tower."}},
{"id":"gw-091","num":91,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tajikistan","answerText":"Tajikistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tajikistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Dushanbe"],["Population","10.8M (2025)"],["Largest city","Dushanbe"],["Area","141,400 km² (54,590 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","tajikistan"],"abstract":"<b>Tajikistan</b> is a landlocked country of southeastern Central Asia whose mountains cover 93 per cent of the ground, and most of it stands above 3,000 m (9,800 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Pamir ranges in the east belong to the Himalayan chain and are among the highest and least accessible mountains in the world, rising to 7,495 m (24,590 feet) at the Ismoili Somoni peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Fergana valley, its main agricultural area, covers part of the north, while high ranges cut the northern and eastern provinces off from the centre and south through the winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three quarters of the flow of the Amu Darya is generated here: its largest tributary, the Panj, rises in the Pamirs and runs along almost the whole of the southern frontier, and the Vakhsh, the largest river within the country, crosses it from northeast to southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is continental and varies sharply with relief, rain and snow averaging 691 mm (27 inches) a year but running from under 100 mm (4 inches) in the southeast to 2,400 mm (94 inches) on the Fedchenko glacier, with recorded temperatures reaching 48°C (118°F) in July and falling to minus 49°C (minus 56°F) in January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The land rises through four belts of life, from desert flora on grey soils through mountain woodland and alpine cold desert to a nival zone above 4,500 m (14,800 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nearly seven tenths of the territory has been little touched by economic activity, and a quarter of it has lain in specially protected natural areas since 1983.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Formerly a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, the country became independent in September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1994, calls the republic a sovereign, democratic, law-governed, secular and unitary state, binds it to secure its own territorial integrity and forbids propaganda aimed at dividing that unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Tajikistan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2012, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0369en/CA0369EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tajikistan,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tajikistan. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Tajikistan</i>, articles 1 and 8, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Tajikistan_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Tajikistan — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tj.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Tajikistan — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=tj. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Flag_of_Tajikistan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tajikistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tajikistan: horizontal bands of red, white and green, with a gold crown and seven stars on the white band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Iskander-kul%2C_Tajikistan.JPG","title":"Iskanderkul, Tajikistan","desc":"Iskanderkul.","credit":"Rjruiziii, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iskander-kul,_Tajikistan.JPG","alt":"A view of Iskanderkul."}},
{"id":"gw-092","num":92,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Papua New Guinea","answerText":"Papua New Guinea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Papua New Guinea"},"facts":[["Capital","Port Moresby"],["Population","10.8M (2025)"],["Largest city","Port Moresby"],["Area","462,840 km² (178,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","papua new guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Papua New Guinea</b> occupies the eastern half of the island of New Guinea, just south of the equator, together with a scatter of islands, atolls and coral reefs around its coastline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Highly dissected mountain ranges are the dominant feature of the mainland, the Bismarck Archipelago and the North Solomons, reaching 4,509 m (14,790 feet), while the western half of the mainland holds the lowland plains and swamps of the Sepik-Ramu and Fly rivers, lying north and south of the main ranges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sepik has the largest catchment, 78,000 km² (30,100 sq mi), followed by the Fly at 61,000 km² (23,600 sq mi); most of the rest are under 5,000 km² (1,900 sq mi) and very steep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is humid and rainy, with a daily mean of 27°C (81°F) over most of the country and two prevailing winds, southeasterly from May to October and northwesterly from December to March, driving the rain against the mountain barriers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean annual rainfall runs from under 2,000 mm (79 inches) along parts of the coast to more than 8,000 mm (315 inches) in some mountain areas, with the driest ground receiving under 1,000 mm (39 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The forests of New Guinea are the third largest expanse of tropical rainforest on earth, after those of the Amazon and the Congo basin, and the country's waters lie within the Coral Triangle, where marine diversity is the highest known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its 33 mangrove species are the richest such assemblage in the world, and scientists reckon more than half its plants and animals have yet to be named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state that became independent on 16 September 1975 was made up of New Guinea, Papua — until then a trust territory administered by Australia — and the nearby islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 10 October of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, in force since independence, declares it a sovereign, independent state under the name of the Independent State of Papua New Guinea, adopted by authority of its peoples' inherent right as ancient, free and independent peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Papua New Guinea,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0404en/CA0404EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Papua New Guinea,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/papua-new-guinea. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Independent State of Papua New Guinea</i>, preamble and section 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Papua_New_Guinea_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Papua New Guinea — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pg.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Papua New Guinea — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=pg. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Flag_of_Papua_New_Guinea.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Papua_New_Guinea.svg)","alt":"The flag of Papua New Guinea: a diagonal division, red above with a golden bird of paradise and black below with the Southern Cross in white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Mount_Wilhelm.jpg","title":"Mount Wilhelm, Papua New Guinea","desc":"Mount Wilhelm.","credit":"Nomadtales ( talk · contribs ), CC BY-SA 2.1 au, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Wilhelm.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Wilhelm."}},
{"id":"gw-538","num":538,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sana'a","answerText":"Sana'a","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 March 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy moved here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1972</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Yemen","dot":"Sana'a"},"facts":[["Country","Yemen"],["Population","2.90M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+94.6% (2005–2023)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","yemen"],"abstract":"<b>Sana'a</b> became an American embassy city because the capital moved to it, not because the mission chose it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of the Yemen on 4 March 1946, in a letter from President Harry Truman to the Imam Yahya, and relations were agreed the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American minister presented credentials on 30 September 1946 while accredited to Saudi Arabia as well and resident at Jidda, and the legation that finally opened in 1959 was at Taiz, the old capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That legation was raised to embassy rank on 28 January 1963, though from 1962 to 1967 no minister or ambassador was accredited at all and a chargé d'affaires ran it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1966 the embassy moved from Taiz to this city, which the guide describes as the new capital of the Yemen Arab Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were severed on 7 June 1967 in the wake of the Arab-Israeli conflict, and resumed only after a visit by Secretary of State William Rogers in July 1972, when the embassy here reopened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Meanwhile the United States kept a separate embassy at Aden for the south, opened on 7 December 1967 and closed on 26 October 1969 when that state severed relations; it never reopened, even after the two Yemens announced their union on 22 May 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country's United Nations seat dates from 30 September 1947, which is the northern state's admission carried through the merger, and it joined the World Trade Organization only on 26 June 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council has since acted on the war here, adopting resolution 2216 at its 7426th meeting on 14 April 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city holds about 2.87 million people, roughly one Yemeni in fifteen of the 41.8 million the two international counts agree on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Yemen,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ye.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Yemen,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/yemen. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Yemen and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/yemen_e.htm. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 2216 (2015),” adopted at its 7426th meeting, 14 April 2015, S/RES/2216(2015), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/791250. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Yemen,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/YEM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Sana%27a_skyline.jpg/1920px-Sana%27a_skyline.jpg","title":"Sana'a","desc":"Sana'a, seen from a distance.","credit":"Rod Waddington, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sana%27a_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Sana'a."}},
{"id":"gw-093","num":93,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sweden","answerText":"Sweden","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised the US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 April 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 April 1818</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 December 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sweden"},"facts":[["Capital","Stockholm"],["Population","10.6M (2025)"],["Largest city","Stockholm"],["Area","447,424 km² (172,750 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","sweden"],"abstract":"<b>Sweden</b> offered its friendship to the United States before it was asked, and the guide quotes the man who said so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Swedish minister in Paris approached Benjamin Franklin in 1782 suggesting a treaty, remarking that he hoped it would be remembered that Sweden was the first power in Europe which had voluntarily and without solicitation offered its friendship to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Franklin had been appointed minister plenipotentiary to Sweden on 28 September 1782 but never went there, staying in Paris where he was already minister to France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition therefore took the form of a signature in a third country: the Treaty of Amity and Commerce was signed at Paris on 3 April 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sweden appointed its first consul to the United States that September, and the United States named its first consul to Stockholm only 35 years later, in May 1818.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations began on 29 April 1818, when Jonathan Russell presented credentials as minister to the court of Sweden and Norway, which were not then separate countries, and they have been unbroken since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy in 1947, when H. Freeman Matthews was appointed the first American ambassador and presented credentials on 5 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 19 November 1946, the Security Council having recommended it at the 57th meeting on 29 August in one resolution covering Afghanistan, Iceland and Sweden together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 30 April 1950, a founding member of the World Trade Organization, and has belonged to the European Union since 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its area is given three ways and one of the three has broken: the United Nations says 438,574 square kilometres, the European Union 447,424, and the World Bank's series jumped from 447,420 to about 528,500 in 2013 and has stayed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sweden,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/se.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sweden,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sweden. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 8 (1946),” adopted at its 57th meeting, 29 August 1946, S/RES/8(1946), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/111971. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Sweden and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/sweden_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Sweden – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/sweden_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Sweden,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2010:2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Flag_of_Sweden.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sweden.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sweden: a yellow Nordic cross on a blue field."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/20130202_Stockholm_Sk%C3%A4rg%C3%A5rd_%2816%29.jpg/1920px-20130202_Stockholm_Sk%C3%A4rg%C3%A5rd_%2816%29.jpg","title":"the Stockholm archipelago, Sweden","desc":"The Stockholm archipelago.","credit":"Belola80, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20130202_Stockholm_Sk%C3%A4rg%C3%A5rd_(16).jpg","alt":"Wooded islands and frozen channels seen from the air."}},
{"id":"gw-094","num":94,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Greece","answerText":"Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Otho acknowledged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 April 1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 November 1837</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 January 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1981</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Greece"},"facts":[["Capital","Athens"],["Population","10.4M (2025)"],["Largest city","Athens"],["Area","131,957 km² (50,949 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","greece"],"abstract":"<b>Greece</b> was recognised by a decision to negotiate rather than by any declaration, and the guide dates it to 7 November 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that day the American government empowered its minister at London, Andrew Stevenson, to enter upon the negotiation of a treaty of commerce and navigation with the King of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stevenson had been approached in April of that year by the Greek minister at London, who wanted relations of navigation between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An earlier chance had been declined in a very careful sentence: invited by Britain, France and Russia in April 1833 to acknowledge Prince Otho of Bavaria as king, Secretary of State Edward Livingston replied that the invariable practice of the United States was to recognise whichever government actually exercised political power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then said the assurances of the three mediating powers would enable him to acknowledge Otho, which is a willingness to recognise expressed without recognising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A consulate came first in practice: Gregory A. Perdicaris was appointed in June 1837 and took up his post at Athens on 7 January 1838.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was among those that joined the United Nations at its founding, on 25 October 1945, the day the Charter came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 March 1950 and has belonged to the European Union since 1 January 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population is a case where the United Nations is the outlier: that source gives 9.94 million for 2025 where Eurostat gives 10.41 million and the World Bank 10.41 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its area is agreed to within 300 square kilometres by all three, and about 32 in every hundred Greeks live in the capital's urban area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Greece,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Greece,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/greece. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Greece and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/greece_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Greece – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/greece_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Greece,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Greece,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Flag_of_Greece.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Greece.svg)","alt":"The flag of Greece: nine blue and white stripes with a white cross on a blue square at the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Oia_-_Santorini_2019.jpg/1920px-Oia_-_Santorini_2019.jpg","title":"Santorini, Greece","desc":"Santorini.","credit":"User:Anna.Tsolidou, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oia_-_Santorini_2019.jpg","alt":"Whitewashed terraces above a flooded volcanic caldera."}},
{"id":"gw-095","num":95,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Azerbaijan","answerText":"Azerbaijan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1918 – 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Azerbaijan"},"facts":[["Capital","Baku"],["Population","10.4M (2025)"],["Largest city","Baku"],["Area","86,600 km² (33,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","azerbaijan"],"abstract":"<b>Azerbaijan</b> lies on the southeastern slopes of the Caucasus with the Caspian Sea along its whole eastern edge, and about 43 per cent of it stands more than 1,000 m (3,300 feet) above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five physiographic regions make it up: the Greater Caucasus range in the north, the Lesser Caucasus south of it, the lowlands about the Kura and Araks rivers, the Talish mountains with the adjoining Lankaran lowland in the southeast, and the Nakhchivan autonomous republic in the southwest, which intervening territory cuts off from the rest of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is continental, arid in the lowlands with summer means above 22°C (72°F), while mountain temperatures fall below freezing in winter and severe frost may strike Nakhchivan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Humid subtropical weather holds along the Caspian shore, chiefly in the Lankaran lowlands, and mean rainfall over the country is about 447 mm (18 inches) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only 8.12 km³ (1.95 cubic miles) of renewable water is generated within its own ground each year against an incoming surface flow of 25.38 km³ (6.09 cubic miles), so most of its water arrives already in the Kura and the Araks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Standing where several biogeographical areas meet, it holds species of European, Central Asian and Mediterranean origin and forms part of the Caucasus ecoregion; its mountain forests carry the wild ancestors of apples, walnuts, chestnuts and pistachios.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fought over for centuries by Russian, Persian and Ottoman forces, the country lay at the crossroads of the trading routes between Europe and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the collapse of the Russian Empire in 1917 it was briefly an independent state, recognised in fact by the Allies in January 1920, until the arrival of the Red Army that April ended that independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence returned with the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991, and it was admitted to the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1995 calls the state a democratic, law-governed, secular, unitary republic, and its preamble claims the traditions of many centuries of statehood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Azerbaijan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0218en/CA0218EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Azerbaijan,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/azerbaijan. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Azerbaijan</i>, preamble and article 7, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Azerbaijan_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Azerbaijan — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/az.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Azerbaijan — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=az. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Flag_of_Azerbaijan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Azerbaijan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Azerbaijan: horizontal bands of blue, red and green, with a white crescent and eight-pointed star on the red band."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Qobustan_giri%C5%9F_qayas%C4%B1_2026_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Qobustan_giri%C5%9F_qayas%C4%B1_2026_%281%29.jpg","title":"Gobustan National Park, Azerbaijan","desc":"Gobustan National Park.","credit":"Rəcəb Yaxşı, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qobustan_giri%C5%9F_qayas%C4%B1_2026_(1).jpg","alt":"A view of Gobustan National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-539","num":539,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Rabat","answerText":"Rabat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 June 1881</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 March 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Morocco","dot":"Rabat"},"facts":[["Country","Morocco"],["Population","1.90M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","7th largest"],["Population growth","-7.8% (2004–2014)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","morocco"],"abstract":"<b>Rabat</b> held an American consulate for 75 years before it held an embassy, and the embassy arrived the week the country became independent again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The relationship it belongs to is the oldest the United States has: Morocco recognised the new republic on 23 June 1786, when a treaty of peace and friendship was signed at Marrakech by the American minister Thomas Barclay and Sidi Muhammad, sultan of Morocco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate followed at Tangier in 1797, and in 1821 Sultan Mawlay Suleiman gave the United States a building there — the first property abroad the country owned, and now an institute for Moroccan studies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consulate in this city opened on 25 June 1881, one of eight the guide lists across the country, most of which had closed by the end of the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Permanent diplomatic relations began elsewhere and late: the consulate at Tangier was raised to a legation on 8 March 1905, 119 years after the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The United States refused at first to recognise the French and Spanish protectorate established in 1912, and acknowledged it only on 20 October 1917, on entering the First World War, whereupon its minister at Tangier was downgraded to a diplomatic agent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was recognised on 7 March 1956, in a statement congratulating Morocco on the Franco-Moroccan declaration of 2 March by which France had recognised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy was established here on 11 June 1956, and the legation at Tangier became a consulate general on the same day, the mission following the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 12 November 1956 by resolution 1111 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, the sitting that admitted Tunisia in the resolution immediately after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city holds about 1.86 million people, roughly one Moroccan in 21 of the 38.4 million the two international counts agree on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Morocco,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ma.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Morocco,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/morocco. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Morocco to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1111 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207993. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Tunisia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1112 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207994. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Morocco,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Kasbah_of_the_Oudayas%2C_Rabat%2C_Morocco.jpg/1920px-Kasbah_of_the_Oudayas%2C_Rabat%2C_Morocco.jpg","title":"Rabat","desc":"Rabat, seen from a distance.","credit":"Bikerhiker75, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasbah_of_the_Oudayas,_Rabat,_Morocco.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Rabat."}},
{"id":"gw-096","num":96,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Israel","answerText":"Israel","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 May 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 May 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First Basic Law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1958</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Israel"},"facts":[["Capital","Jerusalem (seat of government)"],["Population","10.1M (2025)"],["Largest city","Jerusalem"],["Area","22,072 km² (8,522 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","israel"],"abstract":"<b>Israel</b> is a narrow country on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean, with 194 km (121 miles) of coast there and a further 12 km (7 miles) on the Gulf of Eilat in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four regions divide it: the Mediterranean coastal plain, about 40 km (25 miles) wide in the south and narrowing to some 5 km (3 miles) in the north; the central highlands of Galilee, Samaria and Judea; the Jordan Rift Valley; and the Negev.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The highlands average 610 m (2,000 feet) and reach 1,208 m (3,963 feet) at Mount Meron, and several valleys cut across them from east to west, the largest being the Jezreel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rift is a small part of the 6,500 km (4,040 miles) Syrian–East African system and holds the River Jordan, Lake Tiberias and the Dead Sea, while the Negev covers about 12,000 km² (4,630 sq mi), more than half the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is Mediterranean, with long hot dry summers and short cool wet winters: January runs from 5 to 10°C (41 to 50°F) and August from 18 to 38°C (64 to 100°F), and about 70 per cent of the rain falls between November and March, dropping from more than 1,100 mm (43 inches) in the north to under 100 mm (4 inches) in the far south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Lake Tiberias, the lowest freshwater lake in the world, has traditionally supplied about a third of the country's water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Although its ground holds only some 3.5 per cent of the world's known species, the assembly is unusually varied for so small a landscape, and 17 per cent of its wild plants and nearly a third of its vertebrates are reckoned endangered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state proclaimed its independence on 14 May 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 11 May 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has no single written constitution but a series of Basic Laws, of which the earliest is the law on the parliament of 1958 and the Basic Law on human dignity and liberty of 1992 declares its purpose to be setting down the values of the state as a Jewish and democratic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Israel,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0341en/CA0341EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Israel,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/israel. [Open access]","<i>Basic Law: The Knesset</i> (1958) and <i>Basic Law: Human Dignity and Liberty</i> (1992), section 1A, English texts at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Israel_2013. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Israel — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/il.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Israel — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=il. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Flag_of_Israel.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Israel.svg)","alt":"The flag of Israel: a white field with two horizontal blue stripes and a blue Star of David at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Israel-2013-Aerial_21-Masada.jpg/1920px-Israel-2013-Aerial_21-Masada.jpg","title":"Masada, Israel","desc":"Masada.","credit":"Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Israel-2013-Aerial_21-Masada.jpg","alt":"A view of Masada."}},
{"id":"gw-097","num":97,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Hungary","answerText":"Hungary","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Hungary"},"facts":[["Capital","Budapest"],["Population","9.50M (2025)"],["Largest city","Budapest"],["Area","93,030 km² (35,920 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","hungary"],"abstract":"<b>Hungary</b> had American consuls for half a century before it had an American minister, because until 1918 it was not a state the United States could send one to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A consular agent was appointed at Pesth in 1869 and a consul in 1874; the post became a consulate general in 1904 and closed in 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As the Central Powers faced defeat the Hungarian parliament declared independence from Austria on 17 October 1918, an independent government was formed on 1 November, and a republic was proclaimed on 16 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Treaty of Saint-Germain of 10 September 1919 recognised that independence and the Treaty of Trianon of 4 June 1920 fixed the new borders, which cost the country three-quarters of its prewar territory and two-thirds of its prewar population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States ratified neither treaty, so recognition had to wait for a Joint Resolution of Congress ending the state of war, passed on 2 July 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br>  The National Assembly accepted the terms of that resolution on 12 August and authorised its government to negotiate, and a treaty establishing friendly relations was signed on 29 August 1921 and entered into force on 17 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at the capital on 26 December 1921 under Ulysses Grant-Smith, who had been sent two years earlier as commissioner to report on political developments and promote commerce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were severed on 11 December 1941 and resumed after the war, and on 28 November 1966 the legation was raised to an embassy, one of the last two American missions anywhere to be upgraded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955 under resolution 995 (X), which admitted sixteen states at once, and acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 9 September 1973, while still a communist state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population is falling slowly, from about 9.59 million in 2023 to about 9.51 million in 2025, and the two international measures of its area differ by seven square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Hungary,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hu.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Hungary,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/hungary. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Hungary and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/hungary_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Hungary,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HUN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Hungary,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HUN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Flag_of_Hungary.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hungary.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hungary: horizontal bands of red, white and green."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hungarian_Parliament_Building_from_across_the_Danube%2C_2025-01-11.jpg/1920px-Hungarian_Parliament_Building_from_across_the_Danube%2C_2025-01-11.jpg","title":"Hungarian Parliament Building, Hungary","desc":"Hungarian Parliament Building.","credit":"Kilyann Le Hen, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_Parliament_Building_from_across_the_Danube,_2025-01-11.jpg","alt":"A view of Hungarian Parliament Building."}},
{"id":"gw-098","num":98,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Austria","answerText":"Austria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 November 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 August 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 May 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Austria"},"facts":[["Capital","Vienna"],["Population","9.20M (2025)"],["Largest city","Vienna"],["Area","83,879 km² (32,386 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","austria"],"abstract":"<b>Austria</b> was recognised twice in the 20th century, and the second time it had to be declared to have existed all along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Emperor Charles I abdicated on 12 November 1918 and a republic was proclaimed the next day, out of the empire that had concluded an armistice with the Allies nine days earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States did not ratify the Treaty of Saint-Germain, which formally dissolved the Dual Monarchy, so a Joint Resolution of Congress ended the state of war on 2 July 1921 instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition followed on 24 August 1921 with the signing in the capital of a treaty establishing friendly relations, proclaimed in force on 8 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Arthur Hugh Frazier presented credentials as chargé d'affaires on 26 November 1921, and Albert Henry Washburn arrived as the first American minister the following June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br>  After Germany annexed the country in March 1938 the legation closed on 30 April and became a consulate general, which itself closed on 9 July 1941 with every other American consulate in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the end of the Moscow Conference on 1 November 1943 the American, British and Soviet foreign ministers agreed that the annexation was null and void and that they favoured re-establishing a free and independent state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A provisional government was formed on 25 April 1945, a republic was proclaimed on 14 May, and on 8 August the country and its capital were divided into four occupation zones under an Allied Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Four Powers recognised the republic within its 1937 boundaries on 7 January 1946; accession to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade came on 19 October 1951, and the occupation ended with the Austrian State Treaty of 15 May 1955, which declared the state free, independent and neutral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> United Nations membership followed seven months after that treaty, under resolution 995 (X), and the population has risen from about 9.13 million in 2023 to about 9.21 million in 2025, the two international measures of the area differing by one square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Austria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/at.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Austria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/austria. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Austria and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/austria_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Austria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Austria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2023. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/41/Flag_of_Austria.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Austria: horizontal bands of red, white and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Hallstatt_-_Zentrum_.JPG/1920px-Hallstatt_-_Zentrum_.JPG","title":"Hallstatt, Austria","desc":"Hallstatt.","credit":"C.Stadler/Bwag, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hallstatt_-_Zentrum_.JPG","alt":"A view of Hallstatt."}},
{"id":"gw-540","num":540,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Luanda","answerText":"Luanda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liaison office opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Angola","dot":"Luanda"},"facts":[["Country","Angola"],["Population","8.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+14.8% (2014–2018)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","angola"],"abstract":"<b>Luanda</b> was the seat of a government the United Nations admitted more than sixteen years before the United States would recognise it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Portugal granted independence on 11 November 1975 while rival nationalist movements were fighting for control, and the American guide records that the Portuguese abandoned the city that year and the Popular Movement for the Liberation of Angola took power there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new state applied to join the United Nations at once: the Secretary-General circulated the president's letter of 22 April 1976 at the end of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Security Council referred the application to its Committee on the Admission of New Members at its 1931st meeting on 22 June, and the committee met the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There the American representative urged that a decision be deferred, and the United Kingdom, France, Italy and Japan supported the deferral while stating that they fully supported the application and wished no delay of even a single day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>  <br><br>  Consideration was renewed on 19 November at the request of three members of the Council, a draft recommending admission was introduced with nine sponsors, and the Assembly admitted the country at its 84th plenary meeting on 1 December 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American position did not move until 1993, recognition being withheld over the presence of Cuban combat troops and their part in regional conflicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A liaison office opened in the city on 10 January 1992, the year of the first multiparty elections, with Jeffrey Millington as its director.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Bill Clinton formally recognised the government on 19 May 1993, using the announcement to urge the opposition movement UNITA to accept a negotiated settlement, and relations were established on 14 July 1994 when Ambassador Edmund T. De Jarnette presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city holds about 8.04 million of the country's 39.04 million people by the United Nations count, roughly one Angolan in five, although the World Bank measure of the largest city, drawn on a wider boundary, is higher still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Angola,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ao.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Angola,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/angola. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the People’s Republic of Angola to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 31/44, adopted at the 84th plenary meeting, 1 December 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/199650. [Open access]","UN Secretary-General, “Application of the People’s Republic of Angola to Membership in the United Nations: Note by the Secretary-General,” A/31/85–S/12064, 30 April 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/671486. [Open access]","UN Security Council Committee on the Admission of New Members, “Report Concerning the Application of the People’s Republic of Angola for Membership in the United Nations,” S/12109, 23 June 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/225044. [Open access]","UN Security Council Committee on the Admission of New Members, “Report Concerning the Application of the People’s Republic of Angola for Membership in the United Nations,” S/12234, 22 November 1976, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/225171. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Angola,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AGO/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2024. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Luanda_Skyline_-_Angola_2015_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Luanda","desc":"Luanda, seen from a distance.","credit":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luanda_Skyline_-_Angola_2015_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Luanda."}},
{"id":"gw-099","num":99,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belarus","answerText":"Belarus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 March 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belarus"},"facts":[["Capital","Minsk"],["Population","9.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Minsk"],["Area","207,600 km² (80,150 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","belarus"],"abstract":"<b>Belarus</b> is a landlocked country of eastern Europe lying on the east European lowland, covered with young glacial deposits of gravel and sand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A moraine rampart crosses it from southwest to northeast and several of its larger rivers rise on it; in the south spreads the vast marshland of Polesye, and the highest hill reaches only 345 m (1,132 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 20,800 rivers run 90,600 km (56,300 miles) in all, seven of them longer than 500 km (310 miles): the Western Dvina, Neman, Vilia, Dnieper, Berezina, Sozh and Pripyat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is moderately continental, transitional in the west and fully continental in the centre and east, with a midday mean of 6°C (43°F) over the year, January between minus 4.5 and minus 8°C (24 and 18°F) and July between 17 and 18.5°C (63 and 65°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain and snow average 600 to 700 mm (24 to 28 inches) a year, from 550 mm (22 inches) in the southeast to 800 mm (31 inches) on the central heights, seven tenths of it between April and October, and snow lies for up to 120 days in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Natural ecosystems still occupy two thirds of the ground, which belongs to both the Eurasian taiga and the European broad-leaved zones; forest cover rose from 32.4 per cent in 1966 to 38 per cent in 2008, even as boreal ground gave way to steppe under a warming climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Held by the Russian empire from the end of the 18th century, the country declared a short-lived National Republic on 25 March 1918 before being absorbed by the Bolsheviks into what became the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Occupied during the Second World War and retaken in 1944, it stayed under Soviet control until declaring sovereignty on 27 July 1990 and independence on 25 August 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has nonetheless been a member of the United Nations since 24 October 1945, having held a seat in its own right while still a Soviet republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1994, calls the republic a unitary, democratic, social state based on the rule of law, founding itself on the centuries-long development of Belarusian statehood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Belarus,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0325en/CA0325EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belarus,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belarus. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Belarus</i>, preamble and article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Belarus_2004. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Belarus — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/by.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Belarus — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=by. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/85/Flag_of_Belarus.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belarus.svg)","alt":"The flag of Belarus: a broad red band above a green band, with a red-on-white ornamental pattern down the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Bialowieza_National_Park_in_Poland0029.JPG/1920px-Bialowieza_National_Park_in_Poland0029.JPG","title":"Belovezhskaya Pushcha, Belarus","desc":"Belovezhskaya Pushcha.","credit":"Jacek Karczmarz, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bialowieza_National_Park_in_Poland0029.JPG","alt":"A view of Belovezhskaya Pushcha."}},
{"id":"gw-100","num":100,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Switzerland","answerText":"Switzerland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1829</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 September 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Switzerland"},"facts":[["Capital","Bern"],["Population","9.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Zurich"],["Area","41,291 km² (15,943 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","switzerland"],"abstract":"<b>Switzerland</b> was recognised by the United States 173 years before it joined the United Nations, and it applied for that membership only in 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Congress of Vienna re-established its independence and neutrality in 1815, and its government was sending honorary consuls to Washington and New York as early as 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first official record of American recognition is the appointment of John Godfrey Boeker as consul general at Basel on 30 November 1829; he took up the post in October 1830.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A convention on the disposal of property was concluded on 18 May 1847, and diplomatic relations and an American legation both began on 29 June 1853, when Theodore S. Fay presented his credentials as minister resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy exactly a century later, Frances E. Willis being appointed the first American ambassador on 20 July 1953 and presenting her credentials on 9 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>  <br><br>  The application to the United Nations was made by letter of 20 June 2002 from the president and the chancellor of the Swiss Confederation, written on behalf of the Federal Council and circulated by the Secretary-General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the country on 10 September 2002 by resolution 57/1, more than half a century after the Charter entered into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Membership of the trading system had come far earlier, with accession to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 August 1966 and to the World Trade Organization on 1 July 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two international counts of its population sit between about 8.97 and 9.09 million, close enough that neither reading is a correction of the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its largest city is not its capital, and the gap is wide: the World Bank's measure of the largest city gives about 1.46 million against the roughly 426,000 the United Nations records for the seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Switzerland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ch.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Switzerland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/switzerland. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Swiss Confederation to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 57/1, 10 September 2002, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/473420. [Open access]","UN Secretary-General, “Application of the Swiss Confederation for Admission to Membership in the United Nations: Note by the Secretary-General,” A/56/1009–S/2002/801, 24 July 2002, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/469401. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Switzerland and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/switzerland_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Switzerland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Switzerland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Switzerland.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Switzerland.svg)","alt":"The flag of Switzerland: a square red field bearing a bold white cross."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Matterhorn_from_Domh%C3%BCtte_-_2.jpg","title":"Matterhorn, Switzerland","desc":"Matterhorn.","credit":"Photo: chil, on Camptocamp.org Derivative work: Zacharie Grossen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matterhorn_from_Domh%C3%BCtte_-_2.jpg","alt":"A view of Matterhorn."}},
{"id":"gw-541","num":541,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kyiv","answerText":"Kyiv","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 January 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Memorandum signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 December 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 2022</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ukraine","dot":"Kyiv"},"facts":[["Country","Ukraine"],["Population","3.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+0.1% (2019–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ukraine"],"abstract":"<b>Kyiv</b> is spelled two ways in the American recognition guide, whose heading for the mission there still reads \"Establishment of the American Embassy in Kiev (Kyiv), 1992\", while the United Nations statistical tables now write only the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy was established in the city on 23 January 1992, with Jon Gundersen as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition and diplomatic relations had come a month earlier, on 25 December 1991, announced in the address President George H. W. Bush made on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state governed from here had sat in the United Nations since 24 October 1945, taking its seat as a Soviet republic, so its membership preceded its recognition as an independent country by 46 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three years after the embassy opened, the government in this city gave up the nuclear weapons left on its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>  <br><br>  On 5 December 1994 the presidents of Ukraine, the Russian Federation and the United States and the prime minister of the United Kingdom signed a memorandum on security assurances in connection with Ukraine's accession to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons, circulated to the United Nations two days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In its first paragraph the other three reaffirmed their commitment to respect the independence and sovereignty and the existing borders of Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In its second they reaffirmed their obligation to refrain from the threat or use of force against its territorial integrity or political independence, and that none of their weapons would ever be used against it except in self-defence or otherwise in accordance with the Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly returned to the question at an emergency special session in resolution ES-11/1 of 2 March 2022, headed \"Aggression against Ukraine\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest city puts it at about 3.02 million in 2025, while the United Nations table prints 2,973,300 under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, which is the same series six years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ukraine,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ua.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ukraine,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ukraine. [Open access]","Permanent Representatives of the Russian Federation, Ukraine, the United Kingdom and the United States, “Memorandum on Security Assurances in Connection with Ukraine’s Accession to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons,” letter dated 7 December 1994, A/49/765–S/1994/1399, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/169471. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Aggression against Ukraine,” resolution ES-11/1 of 2 March 2022, A/RES/ES-11/1, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3959039. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Ukraine,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/UKR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Aerial_view_kyiv.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_kyiv.jpg","title":"Kyiv","desc":"Kyiv, seen from a distance.","credit":"Arne Müseler, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_kyiv.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kyiv."}},
{"id":"cnh-041","num":41,"category":"China · History","difficulty":2,"question":"Potters were at work in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> thousands of years before the first farmers were, at about sixteen sites whose vessels are the oldest known anywhere in the world.","questions":["A line along the Qinling mountains and the Huai River divides <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a northern half that farmed the millets and a southern half that grew rice.","Towards the end of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a city of some 300 hectares (740 acres) stood on the Yangtze delta behind dams raised about 5,100 years ago."],"answer":"Neolithic China","answerText":"Neolithic China","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9000 – 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Neolithic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7000 – 5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet cultures</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"中國新石器時代","hanzi":"中国新石器时代","pinyin":"Zhōngguó Xīnshíqì Shídài","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Neolithic China</b> is the long stretch of the East Asian past in which people came to live in settled villages on cultivated crops and domestic animals, and finished their stone tools by grinding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pottery there is far older than farming: about sixteen sites have yielded vessels made between roughly 20,000 and 10,000 years ago, which is the earliest record of the craft anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settled communities had formed by about 11,000 years ago in the Yellow River valley of the north and the Yangtze valley of the south, and those two regions divide the period still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The line between them runs east and west along the Qinling mountains and the Huai River; north of it the staple crops were the millets, and south of it rice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Early Neolithic proper, about 7000 to 5000 BCE, is dated by the first clear evidence of those domesticated grains and by pots varied enough to have been made for particular jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Storage pits at Cishan in Hebei held broomcorn millet between about 10,300 and 8,700 years ago, and the site is reported to have yielded more than 50,000 kilograms (110,000 pounds) of grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the south the lower Yangtze is an independent centre of rice domestication, where rice was being harvested by about 10,000 years ago in a process that ran on for millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pigs were being managed by roughly 9,000 years ago, perhaps in several places at once, and in the north they came to be fed on millet, as isotopes show the dogs were too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Liangzhu, on the Yangtze delta, held a city of some 300 hectares (740 acres) behind dams raised 5,100 years ago, and buried finely carved jades with its richest dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Social complexity and urban growth spread through the late Neolithic Longshan centuries in north and south alike, and it was then that wheat, cattle, sheep and goats reached northern China from western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","agriculture","china"],"sources":["Zhang Chi, “The Neolithic Stone Implements of China: A Review,” <i>Internet Archaeology</i> 26 (2009), sec. 1, https://doi.org/10.11141/ia.26.32. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Origins of Specialized Pottery and Diverse Alcohol Fermentation Techniques in Early Neolithic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 26 (2019): 12767–74, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6600912/. [Open access]","Hitomi Hongo, Hiroki Kikuchi, and Hiroo Nasu, “Beginning of Pig Management in Neolithic China: Comparison of Domestication Processes between Northern and Southern Regions,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 30–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214443/. [Open access]","Houyuan Lu et al., “Earliest Domestication of Common Millet (<i>Panicum miliaceum</i>) in East Asia Extended to 10,000 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 18 (2009): 7367–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2678631/. [Open access]","Jiajing Wang et al., “New Evidence for Rice Harvesting in the Early Neolithic Lower Yangtze River, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 12 (2022): e0278200, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0278200. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/20241110_Painted_Pottery_Basin%2C_Dahecun_Site%2C_Yangshao_Culture.jpg/1280px-20241110_Painted_Pottery_Basin%2C_Dahecun_Site%2C_Yangshao_Culture.jpg","title":"Painted pottery basin from the Dahecun site, Yangshao culture","desc":"A painted earthenware basin excavated at the Dahecun site in Zhengzhou, Henan, and held in the site museum there. The record names the site and the Yangshao culture and gives no date for the vessel itself. Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20241110_Painted_Pottery_Basin,_Dahecun_Site,_Yangshao_Culture.jpg","alt":"A wide shallow earthenware basin lit against a dark background, mended from sherds with the cracks and fills showing. Its flaring rim and outer wall carry a broad painted band of black and red: rows of dark ovals with pale centres, hatched leaf shapes and fine parallel lines. The inside is plain buff clay."},"why":[{"q":"Why does pottery here come before farming?","a":"Because about sixteen sites have yielded vessels made between roughly 20,000 and 10,000 years ago, which is the earliest record of the craft anywhere in the world."},{"q":"Why do two regions divide the period?","a":"Because the line along the Qinling mountains and the Huai River separates a north whose staple crops were the millets from a south whose staple was rice."},{"q":"Why do the pigs and dogs show what people were growing?","a":"Because in the north isotopes show both were fed on millet, so the animals carry the signature of the crop the villages lived on."}]},
{"id":"cnh-043","num":43,"category":"China · History","difficulty":2,"question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the northern crop was sown on loess and needed no water works, while the southern one stood in fields that had to be built and drained first.","questions":["At Tanghu on the Middle Yellow River both crops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were being grown together by 7,800 years ago, the rice a newcomer carried up from the Yangtze.","The bones of Yangtze delta farmers carry more marks of stress than northern ones, which is one measure of what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost the people who lived by it."],"answer":"millet and rice farming","answerText":"millet and rice farming","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 5800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First rice fields</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bunded paddies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 2000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"粟作與稻作","hanzi":"粟作与稻作","pinyin":"sùzuò yǔ dàozuò","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Millet and rice farming</b> are the two systems early China ran on: dry-sown grain on the loess of the north, and a crop grown standing in water in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because millet yields less from the same ground than wet rice, the northern millet cultures of 5000 to 3000 BCE covered far more country than their rice-growing neighbours on the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest rice fields, from about 6,000 years ago, were little more than pits dug in freshwater wetland with simple water controls, none of them larger than 10 square metres (110 square feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the next two thousand years they became bunded rectangular paddies engineered for irrigation and drainage, which is to say fields that had to be built before they could be sown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Millet needed none of that, and spread from a crescent around the Bohai Sea, where it was first cultivated about 5800 BCE, across eastern Asia in fits and starts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That spread quickened in the 4th millennium BCE, and in northern China millet farming carried a population that grew almost exponentially from 6000 to 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice went the other way, following the coast and the rivers south, crossing the Taiwan Strait about 5,000 years ago and reaching mainland Southeast Asia in the millennium after that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two met in the middle: at Tanghu on the Middle Yellow River, broomcorn millet and rice were being grown together by 7,800 years ago, the rice a newcomer from the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the two did to the people who grew them differed: Neolithic rice farmers of the Yangtze delta carry more marks of physical stress on their bones than the millet farmers of the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The marks are heaviest in the late Neolithic, when the scale of paddy cultivation was growing and the diet came to turn on rice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","tags":["practice","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["Christian Leipe et al., “Discontinuous Spread of Millet Agriculture in Eastern Asia and Prehistoric Population Dynamics,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 9 (2019): eaax6225, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6760930/. [Open access]","Ting Ma et al., “Holocene Coastal Evolution Preceded the Expansion of Paddy Field Rice Farming,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 39 (2020): 24138–43, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7533829/. [Open access]","Jianping Zhang et al., “Early Mixed Farming of Millet and Rice 7800 Years Ago in the Middle Yellow River Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 12 (2012): e52146, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0052146. [Open access]","Kenji Okazaki et al., “Paleo-Health of Neolithic Wet-Rice Farmers in the Yangtze River Delta: A Comparison with Early Millet Farmers in Northern China,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 132, no. 2 (2024): 65–77, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13134063/. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens et al., “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Panicum_miliaceum_subsp._miliaceum_sl18.jpg/1280px-Panicum_miliaceum_subsp._miliaceum_sl18.jpg","title":"A panicle of broomcorn millet","desc":"A ripening head of broomcorn millet, Panicum miliaceum, one of the two millets of early northern China. The photograph is a modern one of the species growing beside a cemetery in Vienna rather than of any Chinese crop. Stefan.lefnaer, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panicum_miliaceum_subsp._miliaceum_sl18.jpg","alt":"A single arching head of broomcorn millet against a clear blue sky, its fine branching stalks hung with hundreds of pale green grains, rising from a broad green leaf and stem at the left of the frame."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the millet cultures cover far more country than the rice ones?","a":"Because millet yields less from the same ground than wet rice, so the same population had to be spread over much more land."},{"q":"Why is a paddy a field that has to be built before it is sown?","a":"Because the earliest rice grounds were pits dug in wetland with simple controls, and over two thousand years they became bunded rectangular fields engineered for irrigation and drainage."},{"q":"Why do the bones of the two kinds of farmer differ?","a":"Because Neolithic rice farmers of the Yangtze delta carry more marks of physical stress than the millet farmers of the north, heaviest when paddy cultivation was growing."}]},
{"id":"cnh-044","num":44,"category":"China · History","difficulty":4,"question":"About thirty flutes cut from the wing bones of red-crowned cranes come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them drilled with seven holes and still playable.","questions":["Residues in sixteen vessels from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are of a drink of rice, honey and fruit that was being fermented as early as the 7th millennium BCE.","Silk protein has been found in three tombs at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that are 8,500 years old, alongside bone needles and rough tools for weaving."],"answer":"Jiahu","answerText":"Jiahu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7000 – 5500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fermented drink</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 7000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silk in tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"賈湖","hanzi":"贾湖","pinyin":"Jiǎhú","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jiahu</b> was a village in central Henan, on the upper reaches of the Huai, lived in from about 9,000 to 7,500 years ago in three long spells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its fields grew the earliest domesticated rice known in northern China, a crop of the Yangtze whose presence so far north is usually read as an introduction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sixteen of its pots have been tested for what they held, and the answer was a fermented drink of rice, honey and fruit — hawthorn, grape, or both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was being made as early as the 7th millennium BCE, and the vessels are shaped for the work: perforated basins and narrow-mouthed jars with high flaring necks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three of its graves have yielded the protein of silk, 8,500 years old and the earliest direct evidence of the material, and with it bone needles and rough tools for weaving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some thirty flutes have come out of the site, each cut from the ulna of a red-crowned crane with the joints sawn off and finger holes drilled down the shaft.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The holes number two, five, six, seven or eight, and most of the flutes have seven; marks left on the bone show that the spacing was set out before any hole was cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The best preserved of them was laid in a grave and still plays, giving something close to a seven-note scale — the oldest playable instrument known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site has also been credited with possibly the earliest Chinese pictographic writing, a claim that the works reporting it state as a question rather than a finding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The climate around it was warm and damp, which is why mulberry grew there for the silkworm, and why the village could settle and farm at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","music"],"sources":["Yuxuan Gong et al., “Biomolecular Evidence of Silk from 8,500 Years Ago,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 12 (2016): e0168042, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0168042. [Open access]","Patrick E. McGovern et al., “Fermented Beverages of Pre- and Proto-historic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 101, no. 51 (2004): 17593–98, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC539767/. [Open access]","Henan Museum 河南博物院, “贾湖骨笛 [The Jiahu Bone Flute],” accessed 27 August 2026, https://www.chnmus.net/ch/collection/treasure/details.html?id=508164979145447651. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Origins of Specialized Pottery and Diverse Alcohol Fermentation Techniques in Early Neolithic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 26 (2019): 12767–74, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6600912/. [Open access]","Jianping Zhang et al., “Early Mixed Farming of Millet and Rice 7800 Years Ago in the Middle Yellow River Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 12 (2012): e52146, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0052146. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/20260520_Bone_flute_from_Jiahu.jpg","title":"A bone flute from Jiahu","desc":"A bone flute from the Jiahu site, in the collection of the Henan Museum. Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20260520_Bone_flute_from_Jiahu.jpg","alt":"A slender tube of pale bone lying horizontally on two small clear supports against a dark background, gently curved along its length and pierced by a row of seven round finger holes, with a smaller eighth hole beside the last of them."},"locator":{"name":"Jiahu","at":[113.667,33.613]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the rice found here read as an introduction?","a":"Because it is a crop of the Yangtze and this village lies far to the north of it, on the upper reaches of the Huai."},{"q":"Why are the flutes evidence of planning rather than trial?","a":"Because marks left on the bone show that the spacing of the finger holes was set out before any hole was cut."},{"q":"Why could this village settle and farm where it did?","a":"Because the climate around it was warm and damp, which is also why mulberry grew there for the silkworm."}]},
{"id":"cnh-045","num":45,"category":"China · History","difficulty":3,"question":"More than three thousand sites of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are known in Henan alone, and its influence reached seven regions beyond the middle Yellow River.","questions":["Pig bones from the Xipo cemetery carry the signature of a millet diet, which is one measure of how closely the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> managed the animals that fed it.","Migrants from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the highlands of northwest Sichuan, where they brewed with a mould-and-rice starter as they had done on the Yellow River."],"answer":"Yangshao culture","answerText":"Yangshao culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5000 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foxtail leads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Westward move</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 – 3000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"仰韶文化","hanzi":"仰韶文化","pinyin":"Yǎngsháo wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Yangshao culture</b> is the Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River, dating from about 7,000 to 5,000 years ago and centred on what are now Henan, Shaanxi and Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than three thousand of its sites are known in Henan alone, and its influence reached beyond the middle Yellow River into seven regions around it, from Hebei and Ningxia to Qinghai, Hubei and Sichuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its villages were permanent, its pots painted, its pigs and dogs domestic, and its fields dry-farmed on the two millets with a little rice besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Broomcorn millet led at first and foxtail overtook it, a change that had happened on the western Loess Plateau by about 5,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes from the culture’s core and from its edges are alike enough that its spread is read as a movement of people rather than as neighbours copying a fashion in pots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pigs were kept in earnest: at Xipo their bones carry the signature of a diet of millet, and the foddering grew heavier through the period as pork became the main meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Set against the bones of the people buried in the same cemetery, the pig data show animals fed by hand rather than left to forage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Middle Neolithic genomes from the Yellow and the Yangtze basins are markedly different from each other, and yet genes moved both ways between them, so the millet north and the rice south were in touch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some of these people went west and south between about 6,000 and 5,000 years ago, as far as the highlands of northwest Sichuan, where they seem to have joined local foragers and taken up wild foods beside their millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They carried their rituals with them, brewing with a starter of mould and rice for what look like communal drinking festivals danced as well as drunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","agriculture"],"sources":["Lei Sun et al., “The Demic Expansion of Yangshao Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 186, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12218936/. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “Archaeological Evidence for Initial Migration of Neolithic Proto Sino-Tibetan Speakers from Yellow River Valley to Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 51 (2022): e2212006119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9907151/. [Open access]","Quan Zhang et al., “Stable Isotopes Reveal Intensive Pig Husbandry Practices in the Middle Yellow River Region by the Yangshao Period (7000–5000 BP),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257524, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257524. [Open access]","Yishi Yang et al., “Shift in Subsistence Crop Dominance from Broomcorn Millet to Foxtail Millet around 5500 BP in the Western Loess Plateau,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 939340, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.939340. [Open access]","Jianxue Xiong et al., “The Genomic History of East Asian Middle Neolithic Millet- and Rice-Agricultural Populations,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 5, no. 10 (2025): 100976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12791001/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Jiangzhai_settlement_model%2C_Yangshao_culture%2C_Lintong%2C_Shaanxi.jpg","title":"Model of the Yangshao village at Jiangzhai","desc":"A museum model of the Yangshao village at Jiangzhai in Lintong, Shaanxi, shown in the National Museum of China. It is a modern reconstruction rather than the excavated remains, and the small fenced enclosures in it are labelled as animal pens. Prof. Gary Lee Todd, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiangzhai_settlement_model,_Yangshao_culture,_Lintong,_Shaanxi.jpg","alt":"A large model village seen from above: scores of small round huts with domed thatched roofs, and a few square ones, set around an open central space of bare earth. Paths run between them, a watercourse curves round the outside, and two small fenced enclosures carry printed labels."},"locator":{"name":"Yangshao culture","at":[109.1,36.3],"kind":"region","area":[[105,35.6],[107,37.85],[110.5,38.5],[113.5,38],[115.5,36],[115,33.8],[112,32.8],[108.5,33.4],[105.5,34.2]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the culture's spread read as a movement of people?","a":"Because genomes from its core and from its edges are alike enough to rule out neighbours simply copying a fashion in pots."},{"q":"Why do the pig bones show hand-feeding rather than foraging?","a":"Because their millet signature grows heavier through the period, and set against the bones of the people buried in the same cemetery the animals were plainly fed by hand."},{"q":"Why is contact between the millet north and the rice south demonstrable?","a":"Because Middle Neolithic genomes from the two basins are markedly different from each other and yet genes moved both ways between them."}]},
{"id":"cnh-046","num":46,"category":"China · History","question":"Earthen structures read as animal pens stood in the middle of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Yangshao village at Xi'an whose house floors were fired to a hard red crust.","questions":["Archaeologists have given the opening stage of the Yangshao culture the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Shaanxi village lived in between roughly 4800 and 4300 BCE.","Bone chemistry from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives a mixed carbon signature rather than a pure millet one, so wild plants still fed its farmers on the Guanzhong Plain."],"answer":"Banpo","answerText":"Banpo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4800 – 4300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Xi'an, Shaanxi</span></div>","traditional":"半坡","hanzi":"半坡","pinyin":"Bànpō","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","agriculture"],"abstract":"<b>Banpo</b> is a Neolithic village on the Guanzhong Plain at Xi'an in Shaanxi, and among the most fully uncovered settlements of the Yangshao culture that farmed the middle Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Carbon and nitrogen measured on human bone from the site put its occupation between roughly 4800 and 4300 BCE, early in the long Yangshao sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name has since been borrowed twice over, for the Banpo phase that opens the Yangshao culture<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and for the Banpo culture whose cemeteries and pottery turn up across the Guanzhong region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Villages of that phase were laid out to a pattern, the houses standing inside a ring ditch with the burial ground and the pottery kilns set apart from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their pots are recognisable at a glance: bottles with pointed bottoms, round-bottomed basins and bowls, narrow-mouthed jars with swelling bellies, and covers made for burial urns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> In the middle of the settlement, as in neighbouring Jiangzhai, excavators found earthen structures they read as pens, which would mean the village kept its pigs together rather than household by household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Broad tracts of red burnt earth survive in the floors of its houses, showing that a floor could already be fired deliberately to harden and warm it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> What the villagers actually ate is less tidy than a field of millet suggests, since the carbon in their bones averages about −14.8 per mil, a mixed signal that leaves wild plants a considerable share of the diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The single Banpo individual whose protein and energy sources have been plotted separately falls between the C3 and C4 lines on both counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The phase did not last, and pottery of the Dongzhuang type marks the passage from Banpo to the Miaodigou culture that took its place;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> a Y-chromosome lineage recovered from a Banpo skeleton is now read as a marker of Yangshao millet farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Xinyi Liu and Rachel E. B. Reid, “The Prehistoric Roots of Chinese Cuisines: Mapping Staple Food Systems of China, 6000 BC–220 AD,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0240930, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0240930. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens, Yijie Zhuang, and Dorian Q. Fuller, “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Quan Zhang et al., “Stable Isotopes Reveal Intensive Pig Husbandry Practices in the Middle Yellow River Region by the Yangshao Period (7000–5000 BP),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0257524, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257524. [Open access]","Xin Wang et al., “Millet Manuring as a Driving Force for the Late Neolithic Agricultural Expansion of North China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 5552, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5882897/. [Open access]","Minglei Lv et al., “Ancient Genomes from the Tang Dynasty Capital Reveal the Genetic Legacy of Trans-Eurasian Communication at the Eastern End of Silk Road,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 22 (2024): 267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11577736/. [Open access]","Yishi Yang et al., “Shift in Subsistence Crop Dominance from Broomcorn Millet to Foxtail Millet around 5500 BP in the Western Loess Plateau,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 939340, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.939340. [Open access]","Wenqing Liu, “The Evolution of Cold Adaptation Technology within Ancient Buildings in Amur River Basin Viewed from Archaeology,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 21 (2022): 14470, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9656652/. [Open access]","Mengge Wang et al., “Multiple Human Population Movements and Cultural Dispersal Events Shaped the Landscape of Chinese Paternal Heritage,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 41, no. 7 (2024): msae122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11232699/. [Open access]","Jianxue Xiong et al., “The Genomic History of East Asian Middle Neolithic Millet- and Rice-Agricultural Populations,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 5, no. 10 (2025): 100976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12791001/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Banpo_utgr%C3%A4vning.jpg/1920px-Banpo_utgr%C3%A4vning.jpg","title":"The excavated village at Banpo","desc":"House floors, storage pits and pottery vessels left in place under the museum hall built over part of the settlement. Photograph by Bairuilong, 2014, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Bairuilong, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banpo_utgr%C3%A4vning.jpg","alt":"A wide excavation floor of pale earth under artificial light, showing the sunken outlines of round and rectangular house floors, several pits, and a group of large pottery jars standing where they were found."},"locator":{"name":"Banpo","at":[109.0512,34.2732]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the diet at this village less tidy than its fields suggest?","a":"Because the carbon in the villagers' bones averages about −14.8 per mil, a mixed signal that leaves wild plants a considerable share of what they ate."},{"q":"Why is the village read as keeping its pigs in common?","a":"Because excavators found earthen structures in the middle of the settlement, as at neighbouring Jiangzhai, which they read as pens rather than household enclosures."},{"q":"Why is a burnt floor evidence rather than damage?","a":"Because broad tracts of red burnt earth survive in the house floors, showing a floor could already be fired deliberately to harden and warm it."}]},
{"id":"cnh-047","num":47,"category":"China · History","question":"Low tiered platforms holding jade burials, and hardly any walls or moats, mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which flourished in the Liao valleys of northeastern China from about 4500 BCE.","questions":["A wattle-and-daub building at Niuheliang held a life-size female statue, the Goddess Temple of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose burial offerings are jade and almost never anything useful.","Ritual at the great centres of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stopped around 3350 BCE, yet its villages carried on for centuries, so its end was no sudden collapse."],"answer":"Hongshan culture","answerText":"Hongshan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4500 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ritual peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3850 – 3350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"紅山文化","hanzi":"红山文化","pinyin":"Hóngshān wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","religion"],"abstract":"The <b>Hongshan culture</b> is a Neolithic culture of the Liao and Daling river valleys in northeastern China, dated between about 4500 and 3000 BCE and counted among the earliest complex societies in East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its remains cover more than 200,000 square kilometres (77,000 square miles) of the region,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and it draws attention because it rose well before dynastic civilisation appeared on the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genomes tie its westernmost site in Hebei closely to the Liaoning core, so the culture is read as having spread with people rather than as a fashion copied by neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What sets it apart from its contemporaries on the Yellow River is what it lacks: no massive walls, no moats, no grand houses, and public building instead as low platforms raised in circular or square tiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hundreds of painted pottery cylinders were sometimes built into these platforms, which held burial crypts and slab graves whose offerings are almost all jade and hardly ever anything useful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That absence of everyday goods is read as marking ritual specialists rather than the merely rich, and a wattle-and-daub building at Niuheliang with a life-size female statue has been named the Goddess Temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ordinary life was plainer than the monuments suggest, the houses round or square, half sunk into the ground and about 20 square metres (215 square feet), timber-framed with daubed walls and a hearth in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Villages farmed millet, gathered nuts, kept pigs and sheep, hunted deer and worked leather, stone and clay; a survey near Chifeng found some twenty clusters of them, each perhaps 500 people, none ruling the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Niuheliang scatters its ceremonial architecture and jade burials across more than sixteen localities in poor hill country, and looks less like a town than a pilgrimage centre for a whole region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its end is not the total collapse it is often called: ritual at the great centres peaked between roughly 3850 and 3350 BCE and then stopped, while people went on living in small settlements for centuries after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Christian E. Peterson et al., “Hongshan Chiefly Communities in Neolithic Northeastern China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 13 (2010): 5756–61, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851873/. [Open access]","Rui Wang et al., “Genetic Formation of Neolithic Hongshan People and Demic Expansion of Hongshan Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 42, no. 6 (2025): msaf139, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12204185/. [Open access]","Chao Ning et al., “Ancient Genomes from Northern China Suggest Links between Subsistence Changes and Human Migration,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 2700, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7264253/. [Open access]","Deke Xu et al., “Synchronous 500-Year Oscillations of Monsoon Climate and Human Activity in Northeast Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 4105, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6739325/. [Open access]","C. Adam Berrey, Robert D. Drennan, and Christian E. Peterson, “Local Economies and Household Spacing in Early Chiefdom Communities,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 5 (2021): e0252532, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0252532. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Hongshan_Culture_Jade_Pig_Dragon_01.jpg/1920px-Hongshan_Culture_Jade_Pig_Dragon_01.jpg","title":"Hongshan jade pig-dragon","desc":"A coiled jade carving with a snouted head, a large central perforation and a small hole for suspension — the form by which Hongshan jades are recognised. Shanghai Museum. Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, CC0.","credit":"Gary Lee Todd, Wikimedia Commons, CC0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hongshan_Culture_Jade_Pig_Dragon_01.jpg","alt":"A pale green jade ornament shaped like a curled-up animal, its snouted head with pricked ears meeting its tail, pierced through the middle by a wide round hole and near the head by a small one, standing on a clear mount against a dark blue ground."},"locator":{"name":"Hongshan culture","at":[118.8825,42.255],"kind":"region","area":[[117.2,41.6],[117.35,42.65],[118.85,43.6],[120.55,43.35],[122.35,42.55],[122.6,41.55],[121.45,40.75],[119.75,40.6],[118.3,40.9]]},"why":[{"q":"Why do its jade graves point to ritual specialists rather than the rich?","a":"Because the offerings are almost all jade and hardly ever anything useful, and that absence of everyday goods is what marks the burials out."},{"q":"Why does its greatest site look like a pilgrimage centre rather than a town?","a":"Because Niuheliang scatters ceremonial architecture and jade burials across more than sixteen localities in poor hill country."},{"q":"Why is its end not the collapse it is often called?","a":"Because ritual at the great centres peaked between roughly 3850 and 3350 BCE and then stopped, while people went on living in small settlements for centuries after."}]},
{"id":"cnh-048","num":48,"category":"China · History","question":"Earth walls enclosing 300 hectares, dams holding the water off the low ground and 30 kilometres of canals make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the builder of China's earliest waterworks.","questions":["Human bones worked into tools went into the canals of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for over two centuries, a practice found in no earlier Neolithic culture of China.","An unusually wet spell around 2400 BCE ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose capital on the Yangtze delta was abandoned and then buried under a metre of clay."],"answer":"Liangzhu culture","answerText":"Liangzhu culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dams built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3250 – 2850 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City abandoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2400 – 2370 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"良渚文化","hanzi":"良渚文化","pinyin":"Liángzhǔ wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","technology"],"abstract":"The <b>Liangzhu culture</b> is the late Neolithic culture of the Yangtze delta, dated between about 3300 and 2300 BCE, and it left one of the earliest cities in East Asia and the earliest formal waterworks known in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its capital, near what is now Hangzhou, was enclosed by earth walls around some 300 hectares (740 acres), roughly 1.9 by 1.7 kilometres (1.2 by 1.1 miles), with the palace platform of Mojiaoshan raised in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beyond the walls stood dams and levees built to hold water off the low ground, the Tangshan levees alone running at least 5 kilometres (3 miles) and standing 2 to 7 metres (7 to 23 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The builders bundled clay into grass-wrapped packages like sandbags, and the knots in that grass survive well enough to be radiocarbon dated, which puts the high dams at about 3250 to 2850 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building them has been estimated at 3,000 people working nearly eight years to shift 2.88 million cubic metres (3.8 million cubic yards) of earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some 30 kilometres (19 miles) of moats, ditches and canals ran inside and around the city, controlled at eight water gates, and the delta's rice fields were drained and manured behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That scale has made Liangzhu a test case for the old argument that water control is what builds despotic states, though its own record puts ritual at least as far forward as engineering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Human bones worked into tools were dropped into the canals for more than two centuries, a practice unknown in earlier Neolithic China and perhaps a mark of what city life did to the standing of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside the core the pull was weaker: at Jiangzhuang the villagers hunted deer, kept a mixed herd of pigs and wild boar and stayed out of intensive farming altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city was abandoned in an unusually wet spell dated to roughly 2400 to 2370 BCE and buried under a metre (3 feet) of clay, and the delta's cultures then failed together in the aridity that set in around 2050 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]","Haiwei Zhang et al., “Collapse of the Liangzhu and Other Neolithic Cultures in the Lower Yangtze Region in Response to Climate Change,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 48 (2021): eabi9275, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8626068/. [Open access]","Junmei Sawada et al., “Worked Human Bones and the Rise of Urban Society in the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture, East Asia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 31441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15673-7. [Open access]","Yifei Liu et al., “Herding or Hunting? Animal Exploitation at Jiangzhuang (Jiangsu, China) During the Liangzhu Period (3300–2300 BC),” <i>Animals</i> 14, no. 23 (2024): 3461, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11640202/. [Open access]","Vernon L. Scarborough, “The Hydraulic Lift of Early States Societies,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13600–601, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748232/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Liangzhu_Archaeological_Site%2C_2019-07-20_05.jpg/1920px-Liangzhu_Archaeological_Site%2C_2019-07-20_05.jpg","title":"A section cut through the Liangzhu city wall","desc":"The south wall of the city, opened and roofed for display, showing the layer of loose rock the earth rampart was built on. Photograph by Siyuwj, 2019, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Siyuwj, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liangzhu_Archaeological_Site,_2019-07-20_05.jpg","alt":"A wide trench cut down through banked earth under a timber shelter, its floor paved with a long spread of rough pale stones, with a flight of steps running up the near side and visitors looking down from a walkway above."},"locator":{"name":"Liangzhu culture","at":[119.6667,30.15],"kind":"region","area":[[119,30.15],[119.35,30.95],[119.9,31.6],[120.75,32],[121.6,31.8],[122,31.15],[121.85,30.45],[121.35,29.95],[120.55,29.8],[119.65,29.85]]},"why":[{"q":"Why can the dams be dated so precisely?","a":"Because the builders bundled clay into grass-wrapped packages, and the knots in that grass survive well enough to be radiocarbon dated."},{"q":"Why is this city a test case for an old argument about the state?","a":"Because its scale of water control invites the claim that such works build despotic states, though its own record puts ritual at least as far forward as engineering."},{"q":"Why was the pull of the city weak a short way outside it?","a":"Because at Jiangzhuang the villagers hunted deer, kept a mixed herd of pigs and wild boar and stayed out of intensive farming altogether."}]},
{"id":"cnh-049","num":49,"category":"China · History","question":"A tube square outside and round within, called a cong, and a pierced disc called a bi are the two commonest shapes taken by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the ritual stone of a delta culture.","questions":["Bamboo drills turned with wet quartz sand, and cords dragged under a paste of emery, are how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was worn into shape, since nephrite is far too tough to carve.","Grave M3 at Sidun held 24 discs, 33 tubes and 14 tools of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around a man of about twenty, several of them burned or deliberately broken."],"answer":"Liangzhu jade","answerText":"Liangzhu jade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sidun M3</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"良渚玉器","hanzi":"良渚玉器","pinyin":"Liángzhǔ yùqì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","prehistory","archaeology","art","china","religion"],"abstract":"<b>Liangzhu jade</b> is the worked nephrite buried with the dead of the Liangzhu culture of the Yangtze delta between about 3300 and 2300 BCE, and it is what that culture is chiefly known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two shapes carry most of it: the <i>cong</i>, a tube square on the outside and round and hollow within, and the <i>bi</i>, a wide flat disc pierced by a small central hole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cong originates with Liangzhu, and a cong and a bi together are the essential ritual furniture of a high-ranking grave, the largest discs laid on the chest of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many cong carry a face in relief around the corners of the tube, a figure set over a beast mask, and tools of flint and shark teeth found beside the jades may be what cut those lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither shape has a use, and the pair has been called the earliest Chinese objects given forms wholly divorced from any function, ornamental or utilitarian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Nephrite is too tough to carve, so it was worn away instead: bamboo drills turned with water and quartz sand, and cords dragged through the stone under a paste of quartz and emery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A long hole was bored from both ends at once, and where the two bores met they left a ridge part way down and elliptical grooves along the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Workshops lie among the hills and rivers west of Lake Tai, within reach of the stone, the water, the sand and the grinding slabs, and unfinished pieces and waste turn up at several of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Tomb M12 at Fanshan the masks on several jades are cut so nearly alike that one pair of hands may have made them all, perhaps those of the man buried there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grave M3 at Sidun, dated to about 2320 BCE, held 24 bi, 33 cong, 14 jade tools and 9 ornaments around a man of about twenty, five cong broken in half and many of the jades burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Jean M. Green, “Unraveling the Enigma of the Bi: The Spindle Whorl as the Model of the Ritual Disk,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 32, no. 1 (1993): 105–24, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17022. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Late Neolithic and Early Bronze Age China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Cleveland Museum of Art, “Ceremonial Disc (Bi) 玉璧, 3300–2200 BCE,” accession 1917.974, accessed 28 August 2026, https://clevelandart.org/art/1917.974. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Jade_Cong_King%2C_2018-06-09_01.jpg/1920px-Jade_Cong_King%2C_2018-06-09_01.jpg","title":"The King of Cong, from Fanshan tomb M12","desc":"The largest and most finely worked Liangzhu cong known, square outside and bored round through the middle, with stacked faces carved at each corner. Zhejiang Provincial Museum. Photograph by Siyuwj, 2018, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Siyuwj, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jade_Cong_King,_2018-06-09_01.jpg","alt":"A squat block of pale cream jade, square in plan with rounded corners and a wide round hole bored down through it, each corner cut into stacked bands of a face with barred eyes and a nose, the flat surfaces covered in fine incised spirals."},"locator":{"name":"Liangzhu culture","at":[119.6667,30.15],"kind":"region","area":[[119,30.15],[119.35,30.95],[119.9,31.6],[120.75,32],[121.6,31.8],[122,31.15],[121.85,30.45],[121.35,29.95],[120.55,29.8],[119.65,29.85]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is this stone worn away rather than carved?","a":"Because nephrite is too tough to cut, so bamboo drills turned with water and quartz sand, and cords dragged under a paste of quartz and emery, did the work."},{"q":"Why can a boring be read off the finished object?","a":"Because the hole was drilled from both ends at once, and where the two bores met they left a ridge part way down and elliptical grooves along the walls."},{"q":"Why are these two shapes called the first objects divorced from function?","a":"Because neither the square tube nor the pierced disc has a use, ornamental or utilitarian, and they are the essential furniture of a high-ranking grave."}]},
{"id":"cnh-050","num":50,"category":"China · History","question":"Ten or so walled towns across Henan and Shandong, standing in clusters and ringed by moats, are the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the late Neolithic Yellow River.","questions":["A ditch cut into the wall base at Taosi held 31 people of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some of their skulls broken by blows, and a woman with an ox horn in her pelvis.","Rather than one people, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a set of regional groups — Hougang II, Wangwan III, Taosi, Kexingzhuang II — named after a town in Shandong."],"answer":"Longshan culture","answerText":"Longshan culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early–Middle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 2500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2500 – 1900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erlitou follows</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"龍山文化","hanzi":"龙山文化","pinyin":"Lóngshān wénhuà","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Longshan culture</b> is the late Neolithic of the Yellow River plains, about 3000 to 1900 BCE, and is less one culture than a set of regional ones sharing a way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are told apart by region: Hougang II in northern Henan, Wangwan III near Luoyang, Taosi in Shanxi, Kexingzhuang II in Shaanxi, and the Shandong Longshan that named the whole horizon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That name comes from Chengziyai near Longshan town in Shandong, 17.6 hectares (43 acres) of houses and kilns inside a wall that survives 2 to 3 metres (7 to 10 feet) high and once stood about 6.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten or so walled sites are known across the Henan and Shandong heartland and others as far off as Hubei and Sichuan, standing in regional clusters; unwalled settlements of the same date are smaller and plainer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The walled ones appear to grow larger through the period, read as a hierarchy forming, with major centres coming to hold power over minor ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Walls were of rammed earth in the south and east and of stone further north, mostly rectangular, moated and pierced by one to three gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Raising one took most of the people who lived there, and was done with ceremony: at Bianxianwang pots stood upright in the wall base beside a pig of thirteen to eighteen months and a dog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later the offerings are human — two children in ceramic coffins beneath Guchengzhai's southern wall, a child at Wangchenggang whose skull had been parted from the neck — and those killed are thought to have been war captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A ditch cut into the wall base at Taosi held 31 people, some of the skulls broken by blows, along with a woman of about 35 with an ox horn driven into her pelvis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metal was only just beginning: one slice of it from Zhoujiazhuang is the sole Longshan metallurgical find near the Zhongtiao mountains, and when Taosi fell its basin emptied, while the centres of the Central Plain went on moving for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Paola Dematté, “Longshan-Era Urbanism: The Role of Cities in Predynastic China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 38, no. 2 (1999): 119–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17124. [Open access]","Qian Yang, “Conflict and Identity: The Ritual of Wall Construction in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76786. [Open access]","Anne P. Underhill, “Variation in Settlements during the Longshan Period of Northern China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 197–228, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17047. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Taosi_plan.png","title":"Plan of the walled town at Taosi","desc":"The early-period enclosure, the far larger middle-period city that replaced it, a small walled section and the cemetery, set between the Nan River and Song Village Creek. Drawn after Huang Kejia et al.; CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"पाटलिपुत्र, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taosi_plan.png","alt":"A line plan on white, with pale blue watercourses running through it and two red outlines: a smaller rounded enclosure labelled early period city site, and a much larger one around it labelled middle period city site, with a cemetery marked at its south-east corner and a one-kilometre scale bar below."},"locator":{"name":"Longshan culture","at":[117.3542,36.7358],"kind":"region","area":[[108.5,34.5],[109.5,37.5],[112,38.5],[115,38],[118,37.8],[121,37.3],[122.3,36.4],[121,35.5],[118,34.5],[115,33.5],[112,33.2],[110,33.5]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is this called a set of cultures rather than one?","a":"Because the regional groups are told apart site by site — Hougang II, Wangwan III, Taosi, Kexingzhuang II and the Shandong Longshan that named the horizon — and share a way of life rather than an identity."},{"q":"Why are the walls read as evidence of a hierarchy forming?","a":"Because the walled sites grow larger through the period and stand in regional clusters, with major centres coming to hold power over minor ones while unwalled settlements stay smaller and plainer."},{"q":"Why do the offerings under the walls change over time?","a":"Because the earlier ones are pots with a pig and a dog, and the later ones are people — children in coffins, a beheaded child, 31 bodies in a ditch — thought to have been war captives."}]},
{"id":"cnh-051","num":51,"category":"China · History","question":"Walls up to 3.6 centimetres thick, pinched by hand and fired below 700 °C, are how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins, in the hands of foragers rather than farmers.","questions":["Kilns of wood and mud worked at about 620 °C, and clay dug from the soils nearest each village, lie behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> through its long sequence of wares.","The smear of a hand smoothing wet clay, and no pattern at all, is the only mark on the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which foragers were making twelve thousand years ago."],"answer":"Chinese Neolithic pottery","answerText":"Chinese Neolithic pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early wares</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,000 – 10,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Specialised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 7000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheel-made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 – 1900 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"新石器時代陶器","hanzi":"新石器时代陶器","pinyin":"Xīnshíqì shídài táoqì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","prehistory","archaeology","technology","china","art"],"abstract":"<b>Chinese Neolithic pottery</b> is the fired clay of China's long Neolithic, a craft older there than farming and the chief means by which archaeologists still tell one culture of the period from another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest pieces are thick and crumbly, their walls 2.9 to 3.6 centimetres (1.1 to 1.4 inches), tempered with unsieved crushed calcite or quartz and cracked across the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were pinched into shape by hand and fired at roughly 600 to 700 °C (1,100 to 1,300 °F), and the earliest of all, from Zengpiyan, probably below 250 °C (480 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their makers were foragers rather than farmers, comfortable but without ranks, and the freshwater shellfish they had begun to eat is part of why the pots appear when they do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slab building and then coiling came after the pinching, and the marks on the earliest surfaces are the smear of a hand smoothing the clay rather than any decoration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The oldest pots of all come from north of the Nanling mountains, and the Yangtze cultures that follow appear to descend from that northern tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the early Neolithic the shapes have become specialised, made for particular jobs rather than one vessel doing everything, and some of them were built for fermenting drink.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later the wheel arrives, and Longshan jars from Henan carry its marks inside the belly where the potter's hand could not reach, beside grey wares and black ones polished plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clay was dug close by: three Longshan sites in one district of Henan differ enough in composition to show each choosing its own soils, and one of them, surveyed over 16,800 square metres (4.2 acres), has yielded no kiln at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kilns were built of wood and mud, and one in Fujian was worked at about 620 °C (1,150 °F), which is what Neolithic kilns reach across Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Tracey L-D Lu, “Early Pottery in South China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 49, no. 1 (2010), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29097. [Open access]","Guishan Chen et al., “The Firing Temperatures of Burnt Clay from the Chinese Neolithic Cultural Relics and Its Paleoenvironmental Implications,” <i>Heliyon</i> 9, no. 10 (2023): e20628, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10569999/. [Open access]","Linyu Xia et al., “Compositional Analysis of Longshan Period Pottery and Ceramic Raw Materials in the Yongcheng Region, Henan Province,” <i>Materials</i> 18, no. 12 (2025): 2681, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12194330/. [Open access]","Li Liu et al., “The Origins of Specialized Pottery and Diverse Alcohol Fermentation Techniques in Early Neolithic China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 26 (2019): 12767–74, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6600912/. [Open access]","Chi Zhang and Hsiao-chun Hung, “The Neolithic of Southern China—Origin, Development, and Dispersal,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 47, no. 2 (2008): 299–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17291. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/The_Pot_of_Yuchanyan._Hunan_Museum.jpg/1920px-The_Pot_of_Yuchanyan._Hunan_Museum.jpg","title":"The pot from Yuchanyan cave","desc":"Among the oldest pottery vessels known anywhere, rebuilt from its sherds: the dark original fragments are set into a white restoration. Hunan Museum. Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Gary Lee Todd, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pot_of_Yuchanyan._Hunan_Museum.jpg","alt":"A wide conical vessel standing on a clear ring, most of it filled in with smooth white plaster, with a broad band of the original dark grey-brown potsherds running down one side, their surfaces roughly textured and cracked."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the earliest pots appear when they do?","a":"Because their makers were foragers who had begun to eat freshwater shellfish, and that food is part of why the craft arrives before farming does."},{"q":"Why are the marks on the oldest surfaces not decoration?","a":"Because they are the smear of a hand smoothing the clay, the vessels having been pinched into shape before slab building and coiling came in."},{"q":"Why can a wheel be detected on a finished jar?","a":"Because Longshan jars from Henan carry its marks inside the belly, where the potter's hand could not reach."}]},
{"id":"cnh-052","num":52,"category":"China · History","question":"Chinese tradition puts the first dynasty on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Zhongyuan of the middle Yellow River, and has complexity spreading outward from there by conquest and imitation.","questions":["Erlitou, a site of some 400 hectares near Yanshi, is the earliest unbroken link on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between its Neolithic villages and the Shang layers above them.","Walled towns in Shandong, Hubei and Sichuan, and a city behind dams on the Yangtze delta, show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not the only place where complexity rose."],"answer":"Central Plain","answerText":"Central Plain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2100 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erlitou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1900 – 1600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"中原","hanzi":"中原","pinyin":"Zhōngyuán","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","geography"],"abstract":"The <b>Central Plain</b>, the Zhongyuan, is the country of the middle and lower Yellow River around Henan, and it is where Chinese tradition puts the first dynasty and so the beginning of Chinese civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In that account the Xia ruled here from the 21st to the 17th century BCE, and social complexity spread outward from the plain over the next two thousand years by conquest and by imitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology went looking for it, and a survey in 1959, prompted by texts placing the Xia homeland in western Henan and southern Shanxi, found Erlitou near Yanshi, a site of some 400 hectares (990 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own deposit lies sandwiched between a Neolithic layer below and an early Shang one above, four building phases thick, and the assemblage in it was named the Erlitou culture after the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plain's sequence runs on unbroken from Neolithic villages through Erlitou into the Shang, which is not true everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was not alone: walled towns of the same centuries stand in Shandong, Hubei, Hunan and Sichuan, gathered in regional clusters rather than spreading out from any one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the northeast, tiered earthen platforms held burials furnished almost wholly with carved jade, in districts of perhaps five hundred people each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the Yangtze delta a walled city of some 300 hectares (740 acres) stood behind dams and levees a thousand years before Erlitou was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Set beside the contemporary chiefdoms of the Chifeng country to its north, the Erlitou polity looks larger and perhaps more centralised, but its social and political system was not different in kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The single-line story has accordingly given way to one in which several polities made Chinese civilisation between them, and the plain's distinction is not that it began first but that its line never broke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Paola Dematté, “Longshan-Era Urbanism: The Role of Cities in Predynastic China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 38, no. 2 (1999): 119–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17124. [Open access]","Christian E. Peterson et al., “Hongshan Chiefly Communities in Neolithic Northeastern China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 13 (2010): 5756–61, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851873/. [Open access]","Bin Liu et al., “Earliest Hydraulic Enterprise in China, 5,100 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 52 (2017): 13637–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5748178/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Late_Neolithic_cultures_in_China.jpg/1920px-Late_Neolithic_cultures_in_China.jpg","title":"Late Neolithic cultures of China","desc":"Fifteen culture areas of the late Neolithic ringed on a relief map, with Longshan on the Central Plain as one among them. Drawn after Li Liu and Xingcan Chen, The Archaeology of China (Cambridge, 2012), p. 170; the base map’s country and sea names are in German. Map by Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0.","credit":"Ismoon, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Neolithic_cultures_in_China.jpg","alt":"A coloured relief map of China and its neighbours with fifteen thin black ellipses drawn across it, each numbered and keyed at the right to a late Neolithic culture, spread from the Liao river in the north-east to Yunnan and Taiwan in the south."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Erlitou found where it was?","a":"Because a survey in 1959 was prompted by texts placing the Xia homeland in western Henan and southern Shanxi, and went looking there."},{"q":"Why has the single-line story of Chinese civilisation given way?","a":"Because walled towns of the same centuries stand in Shandong, Hubei, Hunan and Sichuan in regional clusters, and a delta city of 300 hectares stood a thousand years before Erlitou."},{"q":"Why is this region distinguished all the same?","a":"Because its sequence runs on unbroken from Neolithic villages through Erlitou into the Shang, which is not true everywhere else."}]},
{"id":"cnh-053","num":53,"category":"Ancient","question":"The walled Bronze Age city of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood on the Chengdu Plain in the Sichuan Basin, and is generally taken for a capital of the ancient state of Shu.","questions":["Eight pits at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held bronze, gold, jade and ivory deliberately broken or burnt before burial, among them a bronze figure over two and a half metres (eight feet) tall.","Bronze casting at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew on the same alloys and moulds as the Shang, but the masks, figures and trees it made resemble nothing found in central China."],"answer":"Sanxingdui","answerText":"Sanxingdui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1150 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pits filled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"三星堆","hanzi":"三星堆","pinyin":"Sānxīngduī","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Sanxingdui</b> is a Bronze Age walled city on the Chengdu Plain, in the Sichuan Basin of south-west China, occupied from roughly 1700 to 1150 BCE and generally taken for a capital of the ancient state of Shu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its walls enclose about 500 hectares (1,200 acres), and the settlement reaches some 1,200 hectares (3,000 acres) if the neighbourhoods around it are counted; inner earth walls divide the interior into what look like palace and workshop quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nine walled towns of the earlier Baodun culture had stood across the plain, and Sanxingdui was the one centre left when they declined, with every contemporary site near it far smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was contemporary with the Erligang and Late Shang traditions of eastern China, and one of its bronze trees was cast by the alloy recipes and piece-mould work of that same technical tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What it made with them resembles nothing found in central China, and the place of Shu in early Chinese civilisation is argued over still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Eight pits have been found in the southern part of the site, two of them in 1986 and six more from 2019, each holding high-value objects deliberately broken or burnt before they were buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between about 1200 and 1000 BCE they received bronze, gold, jade, ivory, cowrie shells and carnelian beads, laid down in ordered layers rather than thrown in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among them are a bronze figure 2.62 metres (8 feet 7 inches) tall, a mask 1.38 metres (4 feet 6 inches) wide, a bronze tree 3.95 metres (13 feet) high and a gold-sheathed staff 1.42 metres (4 feet 8 inches) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trace-element work on the carnelian points to raw material carried from far to the north, and silk has been detected in the ash lying over the objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Why the pits were filled is unsettled: a sacrifice, or a hurried destruction as power passed to Jinsha, in what is now Chengdu, where the same images go on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","bronze age","china","art"],"sources":["Rowan Flad, Timothy J. Horsley, Jade D’Alpoim Guedes, He Kunyu, Gwen Bennett, Pochan Chen, Li Shuicheng, and Jiang Zhanghua, “Survey, Excavation, and Geophysics at Songjiaheba — A Small Bronze Age Site in the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 52, no. 1 (2013): 119–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/38721. [Open access]","Kuei-chen Lin, “On Craft Production and the Settlement Pattern of the Jinsha Site Cluster on the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 367–400, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76789. [Open access]","Weijie Zhao, “New Archeological Marvels of Ancient Shu Civilization,” <i>National Science Review</i> 8, no. 7 (2021): nwab071, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8310767/. [Open access]","Hailing Zheng et al., “Evidence of the Use of Silk by Bronze Age Civilization for Sacrificial Purposes in the Yangtze River Basin of China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29175, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11589763/. [Open access]","Meiting Yan et al., “Sourcing the Origins of Carnelian in Early Chinese Civilizations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 7 (2026): e2524563123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913004/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_1.jpg/1920px-Ancient_Bronze_Mask_from_Sanxingdui_1.jpg","title":"Bronze mask from Sanxingdui","desc":"One of the large bronze masks recovered from the sacrificial pits, with wide almond eyes, a hooked nose and outspread ears. Sanxingdui Museum, Guanghan, Sichuan.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A green-patinated bronze mask of a face with wide almond-shaped eyes under heavy brows, a broad flat nose, a straight closed mouth and large ears spread out at each side, standing on a white plinth in a museum case."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this city's technical debt to central China measurable?","a":"Because one of its bronze trees was cast by the alloy recipes and piece-mould work of the Erligang and Late Shang tradition, while what it made with them resembles nothing found there."},{"q":"Why is the filling of the pits read as deliberate?","a":"Because the objects were broken or burnt before burial and laid down in ordered layers rather than thrown in."},{"q":"Why is the reason for that filling still unsettled?","a":"Because it may be a sacrifice or a hurried destruction as power passed to Jinsha, in what is now Chengdu, where the same images go on."}]},
{"id":"cnh-054","num":54,"category":"Ancient","question":"Chinese tradition makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first of the Three Dynasties, seventeen kings in the Central Plain before the Shang, though the received chronologies cannot agree on when they reigned.","questions":["The dynastic cycle begins with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last king Jie is said to have forfeited Heaven’s favour to Tang of the Shang through his own misrule.","No writing survives from the centuries assigned to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Taosi and Erlitou are the Central Plain sites most often matched to the capitals its texts describe."],"answer":"Xia dynasty","answerText":"Xia dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17, in the received lists</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rival reckonings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opens 2205 BCE, or 1989 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"夏朝","hanzi":"夏朝","pinyin":"Xià cháo","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xia dynasty</b> is the name traditional Chinese historiography gives to the earliest state in the country, placed in the Central Plain of the middle Yellow River and standing first in the sequence of Three Dynasties that runs Xia, Shang, Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian counted seventeen kings, but the received chronologies cannot agree when they reigned: one opens the dynasty in 2205 BCE and runs it 439 years, another opens it in 1989 and runs it 432.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A state-sponsored chronology published in 2000 gave a third answer, about 2070 to 1600 BCE, and that is the span most archaeological writing now works with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its founder is Yu, the oldest of the culture heroes the Western Zhou knew, remembered for draining the flood that had covered the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its last king, Jie, is said to have ruled so badly that the lords went over to Tang of the Shang, who drove him into exile and took the title of Son of Heaven in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That story is the dynastic cycle in its first turn: a house holds the favour of Heaven, forfeits it through the misconduct of a final ruler, and gives way to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scheme may itself be a Zhou invention, a linear succession of three hereditary houses composed to support a theory of dynastic cycles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing written survives from the centuries the Xia is assigned to, and what is told of it comes from texts set down long afterwards, some of it plainly legend, as when ten suns are said to have risen together in one reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition also grew backwards: the Western Zhou knew Yu alone, Confucius knew two sage rulers before him, and the Warring States knew older kings still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeologically, Taosi and Erlitou, the two great Central Plain centres between about 2300 and 1500 BCE, are the sites most often matched to the capitals the texts describe, and neither match is settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxc–cxciii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 167–71, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Xia_Dynasty_Jade_Yue_Axe.jpg/1920px-Xia_Dynasty_Jade_Yue_Axe.jpg","title":"A jade yue axe, labelled Xia","desc":"A ceremonial jade blade of the kind Chinese museums assign to the Xia period. National Museum of China, Beijing.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A flat wedge-shaped blade of pale grey-green jade with a single round drilled hole near its centre, mounted against a cream museum wall."},"why":[{"q":"Why has the sequence of three dynasties been read as a Zhou invention?","a":"Because a linear succession of three hereditary houses is exactly what a theory of dynastic cycles requires, a house forfeiting Heaven's favour through the misconduct of a final ruler."},{"q":"Why does the tradition about it grow backwards?","a":"Because the Western Zhou knew Yu alone, Confucius knew two sage rulers before him, and the Warring States knew older kings still."},{"q":"Why is no archaeological match for it settled?","a":"Because nothing written survives from the centuries assigned to it, so Taosi and Erlitou are matched to the capitals the texts describe without either identification being secure."}]},
{"id":"cnh-055","num":55,"category":"Ancient","question":"Born to a queen who escaped through a hole in the wall, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have won back the Xia from ten li square and five hundred men.","questions":["The <i>Shiji</i> passes straight from Xiang to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with nothing between, and only the Zuo Commentary tells how the Xia was lost to usurpers and taken back.","After hiding as a shepherd and then as a cook, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathered the remnant of the Xia people, destroyed the usurper’s two sons and sacrificed again to Yu."],"answer":"Shaokang","answerText":"Shaokang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Interregnum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 to 40 years, say the commentators</span><span class=\"dt-k\">He began with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 li square and 500 men</span></div>","traditional":"少康","hanzi":"少康","pinyin":"Shàokāng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shaokang</b> is the king who, in the traditional account of the Xia, won the dynasty back a generation after it had been taken by usurpers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The received king list runs from Yu to his son Qi, then to Taikang, who went hunting beyond the Lo for a hundred days and lost the kingdom, then to Zhongkang and to Xiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sima Qian passes over what came next in silence: in his annals Xiang dies and his son Shaokang takes power, with nothing between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The story of those years survives instead in the Zuo Commentary, where two speakers set it out centuries later to press arguments about wars of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the traditional account of the Xia in miniature, kept not as a record but as a lesson, and reaching us through the uses later writers found for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Ao, son of the usurper Zhuo, killed the lords of Zhenguan and Zhenxun and destroyed Xiang; Xiang’s queen was pregnant, escaped through a hole in the wall and reached her own state of Reng, where Shaokang was born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He grew up as master of the herdsmen of Reng, and when Ao sent a man to look for him he fled again and became cook to the ruler of Yu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That ruler gave him his two daughters in marriage and the town of Lun, ten <i>li</i> square, with five hundred men, and this was the whole of what he began with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gathered what was left of the people of Xia and revived its offices, set one man to watch Ao and another to mislead Ao’s brother, and destroyed them both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Restored, he sacrificed to the Xia kings with Yu as the correlate of Heaven; the commentary draws the moral that the usurpers fell because they had lost the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Ai, year 1, 794, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Hsiang, year 4, 424–25, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 165–68, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 78–79, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/%E5%B0%91%E5%BA%B7%EF%BC%88%E5%A4%8F%E5%95%86%E5%90%88%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg/1920px-%E5%B0%91%E5%BA%B7%EF%BC%88%E5%A4%8F%E5%95%86%E5%90%88%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg","title":"Shaokang in a Qing woodblock","desc":"A line drawing from an illustrated Qing edition of the Combined Biographies of Xia and Shang — an artist's idea, some three thousand years later, of a king known only from texts.","credit":"Unknown artist, 1814. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A woodblock line drawing of a standing figure in a tall crown and long trailing robes with hands folded at the waist, inside a heavy printed border."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the earliest history pass over these years in silence?","a":"Because in Sima Qian's annals Xiang dies and his son takes power with nothing between them, and the interregnum survives only in the Zuo Commentary."},{"q":"Why does that commentary preserve the story at all?","a":"Because two speakers set it out centuries later to press arguments about wars of their own, so it is kept as a lesson rather than as a record."},{"q":"Why does the restoration begin from so little?","a":"Because he was given the town of Lun, ten li square, with five hundred men, and gathered what was left of the people of Xia and revived its offices from that."}]},
{"id":"cnh-056","num":56,"category":"Ancient","question":"Nine provinces are set out in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each with its limits, its soil, the works done in it and the tribute it owes to the capital.","questions":["Sima Qian copied the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whole into his annals of the Xia, where the survey of the land stands as the founder’s first act of government.","Yu is named only twice in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, at its opening and its close, and without those two sentences it reads as the record of a spreading settlement."],"answer":"Tribute of Yu","answerText":"Tribute of Yu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9, each graded for soil and tribute</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Domains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, each 500 li wide</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yu is named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 times, at the opening and the close</span></div>","traditional":"禹貢","hanzi":"禹贡","pinyin":"Yǔ gòng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b><i>Tribute of Yu</i></b> is a chapter of the <i>Book of Documents</i> describing how the land was set in order after the flood, and it is the oldest geographical treatise in the Chinese tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It divides the country into nine provinces and works through them one at a time in a fixed order: the province’s limits, the works carried out in it, the quality of its soil, the tribute it owes and the roads it travels by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The soils are described as found — whitish and mellow in one province, red and clayey in another, miry in a third — and the fields and revenue of each are ranked by class and grade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second part runs through the nine mountain ranges and the nine great watercourses, and closes with a scheme of concentric squares five hundred <i>li</i> wide, from the sovereign’s own domain out through the nobles to the wild lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian copied the whole chapter into his annals of the Xia, where it stands at the head of the founder’s reign as the act that made the realm governable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The chapter is placed under Yu, but it names him only twice, in its opening sentence and its closing one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Take those two sentences away and what is left reads as the record of a settlement spreading slowly over drained ground, driving the older inhabitants before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The works it describes are far beyond one man in any case, so tradition credited Yu alone with the continuous labour of several generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No state of that age governed a country of the size it maps: the Three Dynasties era held dozens of polities at once, and the nine provinces are a scheme rather than a survey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whoever composed it was setting out a theory of how the country might have been brought to order by the founder of the Xia, and glorifying him by giving him the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","geography","china","literature"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 64–76, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 16–19, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 102–3, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 100–102, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Yuji_tu_-_enhanced_contrast.png/1280px-Yuji_tu_-_enhanced_contrast.png","title":"The Map of the Tracks of Yu","desc":"A rubbing of the stone map carved in 1136 and now in the Stele Forest at Xi'an, drawn on a ruled grid and naming the mountains and rivers of the Tribute of Yu.","credit":"Rubbing in the Library of Congress, contrast enhanced. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A square map in black line on white, covered edge to edge by a fine ruled grid, with river courses drawn as heavy winding lines and hundreds of small Chinese place names between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the chapter read differently without its first and last sentences?","a":"Because it names its supposed author only there, and what lies between reads as the record of a settlement spreading slowly over drained ground."},{"q":"Why are the nine provinces a scheme rather than a survey?","a":"Because no state of that age governed a country of the size it maps, the Three Dynasties era holding dozens of polities at once."},{"q":"Why was the whole of it credited to one man?","a":"Because the works described are far beyond any one person, so tradition gave him the continuous labour of several generations in order to glorify the founder."}]},
{"id":"cnh-057","num":57,"category":"Ancient","question":"Before the battle at Kan, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> charged the lord of Hu with abandoning the three commencements of the year and called it a punishment appointed by Heaven.","questions":["The lords passed over Yu’s chosen minister and went to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because he was their sovereign’s son, and with that inheritance the Xia became a dynasty rather than a succession.","Archers on the left, spearmen on the right and drivers between them were told by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that failing in their work would count as disregarding his own orders."],"answer":"Qi of Xia","answerText":"Qi of Xia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hosts at Kan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6, under six nobles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In a chariot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 men: driver, archer, spearman</span></div>","traditional":"夏啟","hanzi":"夏启","pinyin":"Xià Qǐ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Qi of Xia</b> is the second king of the traditional list and the one with whom the dynasty properly begins: he took his father Yu’s place by inheritance, which is what turns a house into a dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yu had named his minister Yi to follow him, as the rulers before him had done, but when Yi withdrew at the end of the mourning the lords went to Qi instead, saying he was their sovereign’s son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether Yu had thereby fallen short of the sages before him was argued in the Warring States, and the answer given was that Yi had served too briefly to be known, that Qi was able, and that the change was Heaven’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reign is remembered for one event, a battle at Kan against the lord of Hu, whose lands lay west across the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Book of Documents</i> preserves the speech he is said to have made before it, and it is the earliest piece in that collection to have been claimed as contemporary with what it describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He called together the six nobles who led his hosts and charged the lord of Hu with wasting the five elements and abandoning the three commencements of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The charge is put obscurely, but the calendar is the point: each house fixed the month its year began in, and to begin it elsewhere was to refuse the new dynasty’s authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is executing, he says, a punishment appointed by Heaven — the language the tradition would later use for the fall of dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then he sets out what each man must do, the archer on the chariot’s left and the spearman on its right with the driver between, and warns that to fail in it is to disregard him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Obedience will be rewarded before his ancestors and disobedience punished before the altar of the land, children included; a king on campaign carried both spirits with him, the ancestral tablet in its own chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 76–78, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 163–66, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 18 and 26, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 358–59, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/%E5%90%AF_%E6%B8%85%E4%BA%BA%E7%BB%98.jpg/1280px-%E5%90%AF_%E6%B8%85%E4%BA%BA%E7%BB%98.jpg","title":"Qi of Xia, painted under the Qing","desc":"A portrait on silk of the Xia's second king by an unknown Qing artist, made some three thousand years after the reign it shows.","credit":"Unknown artist. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A painted portrait on silk of a bearded man in a green scholar's cap and pale green robe, shown from the chest up against a plain ochre ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the dynasty properly begin with the second king?","a":"Because he took his father's place by inheritance, where the rulers before had named a minister to follow them, and inheritance is what turns a house into a dynasty."},{"q":"Why is a charge about the calendar a political one?","a":"Because each house fixed the month its year began in, so to begin it elsewhere was to refuse the new dynasty's authority."},{"q":"Why does his battle speech invoke two altars?","a":"Because obedience was to be rewarded before his ancestors and disobedience punished before the altar of the land, a king on campaign carrying both spirits with him."}]},
{"id":"cnh-058","num":58,"category":"Ancient","question":"Told that his cruelties were dangerous, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed to the sun and said he sat as firm on his throne as it sat in the sky.","questions":["Tang imprisoned by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the Terrace of Xia and then released, went on to raise an army, drive him to Mingtiao and take the throne in his place.","The people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are quoted asking when the sun would expire and saying they would all perish with it, in the speech Tang made before attacking him."],"answer":"Jie of Xia","answerText":"Jie of Xia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 Xia kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Defections from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Kong Jia, 4 reigns earlier</span></div>","traditional":"夏桀","hanzi":"夏桀","pinyin":"Xià Jié","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Jie of Xia</b> is the last king of the Xia in the traditional account, and the first of the bad last rulers on whom the Chinese dynastic cycle turns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lords had been falling away since the reign of Kong Jia, four kings before him, and under Jie they revolted in growing numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is said to have cared nothing for virtue and to have terrorised the hundred families until they would not bear him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He summoned Tang, the lord who was to supplant him, imprisoned him at the Terrace of Xia and then let him go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tang practised virtue, the lords took refuge with him and he led an army against Jie, who fled to Mingtiao and died in exile, saying he regretted not killing Tang when he had him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Book of Documents</i> keeps the speech Tang made before that campaign, and its most telling feature is that he had to argue his own people into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They asked what the crimes of the Xia were to them and why they were being called away from their fields; he answered that Heaven had laid the charge on him, and ended by threatening to put their children to death with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their own curse on Jie is quoted in it: he had once pointed to the sun and said he sat as firm on his throne as it sat in the sky, and they asked in reply when that sun would expire, saying they would all perish with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later writers made him and the last Shang king a pair who lost the throne by losing the people, driving them to their rivals as an otter drives fish into deep water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pairing runs deeper than the moral: the tyrant gaoling the founder who will replace him is told of the Shang’s last king too, and Jie’s personal name ends in a cyclical character, which is a Shang habit and no other Xia king’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 169–71, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 169–70, notes, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 84–86, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 300, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E6%94%BE%E6%A1%80%E5%8D%97%E5%B7%A2%E5%9C%96.jpg/1280px-%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E6%94%BE%E6%A1%80%E5%8D%97%E5%B7%A2%E5%9C%96.jpg","title":"The banishment of Jie","desc":"A plate from the imperially commissioned illustrated Shujing of 1905. Jie rides away from the walled place named Nanchao, to which the tradition has him banished.","credit":"Sun Jianai, 1905. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A Chinese line drawing of a bearded rider on a horse among bare trees, with a walled gateway on a bluff above him and a labelled hill in the foreground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the man who replaced him have to argue his own people into the war?","a":"Because they asked what the crimes of the Xia were to them and why they were being called away from their fields, so he answered that Heaven had laid the charge on him and threatened their children."},{"q":"Why is the sun in the people's curse an answer to the king?","a":"Because he had pointed to it and said he sat as firm on his throne as it sat in the sky, so they asked when that sun would expire and said they would perish with it."},{"q":"Why is his pairing with the last Shang king more than a moral?","a":"Because the tyrant gaoling the founder who will replace him is told of both, and his personal name ends in a cyclical character, which is a Shang habit and no other Xia king's."}]},
{"id":"cnh-059","num":59,"category":"Ancient","question":"In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, five brothers waiting north of the Lo sing that the people are the root of a country and that when the root is firm the country is tranquil.","questions":["Taikang hunted beyond the Lo for a hundred days and was barred from returning, and his five brothers turned Yu’s Cautions into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the <i>Book of Documents</i>.","Belonging to the chapters made out from tablets found in a wall and presented in 97 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been called a later fabrication since the Song."],"answer":"Songs of the Five Sons","answerText":"Songs of the Five Sons","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2070 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taikang's hunt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 days beyond the Lo</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Songs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, one from each brother</span></div>","traditional":"五子之歌","hanzi":"五子之歌","pinyin":"Wǔ zǐ zhī gē","translations":"","abstract":"<b><i>Songs of the Five Sons</i></b> is a chapter of the <i>Book of Documents</i> in which five brothers lament the throne their eldest has lost, and it is the most quoted text assigned to the Xia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taikang, Yu’s grandson, is said to have held the throne like a personator of the dead — the stand-in set in a dead man’s place at a sacrifice — and to have idled his virtue away until the people wavered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He hunted beyond the Lo for a hundred days without returning, and the lord of Qiong barred him from recrossing the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His five brothers, who had followed their mother after him, were waiting on the north bank; hearing of it, they put the Cautions of Yu into songs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first opens on the line most often taken from the collection: the people are the root of a country, and when the root is firm the country is tranquil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same song has him say that in dealing with millions he should feel as anxious as a man driving six horses with rotten reins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second lists what has never been anything but a prelude to ruin: a palace that is a wild of lust, a country a wild for hunting, a liking for spirits and music, lofty roofs, carved walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last three mourn the throne lost, the ancestor’s canons thrown into confusion, the temple overturned and the sacrifices put out, and close with the brothers unable to overtake the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its route to us is the point of it: it is not among the 29 documents a scholar hid in a wall before the burning of the books, but among the further ones deciphered from tablets found in another wall and presented to the throne in 97 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Critics from the Song onwards judged those additional chapters by their style and called them a later fabrication; the argument has run both ways since, and they are printed with the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 78–81, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 7–9, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), Introduction, 10–11, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 165–66, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E4%BB%B2%E5%BA%B7%E8%82%87%E4%BD%8D%E5%9C%96.jpg/1280px-%E6%AC%BD%E5%AE%9A%E6%9B%B8%E7%B6%93%E5%9C%96%E8%AA%AA_%E4%BB%B2%E5%BA%B7%E8%82%87%E4%BD%8D%E5%9C%96.jpg","title":"Zhongkang begins his reign","desc":"The plate for the chapter that follows the Songs in the same imperially commissioned illustrated Shujing of 1905: Taikang's younger brother takes the throne he had lost.","credit":"Sun Jianai, 1905. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A Chinese line drawing of a robed ruler enthroned in a tiled hall under a curved roof, with two attendants beside him and a dozen officials standing on the paving below."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the chapter open on a line about roots?","a":"Because the people are the root of a country, and when the root is firm the country is tranquil, which is the lesson the brothers draw from the throne being lost."},{"q":"Why is the route this text reached us by the point of it?","a":"Because it is not among the 29 documents hidden in a wall before the burning of the books but among the further ones presented to the throne in 97 BCE."},{"q":"Why has its authenticity been argued both ways?","a":"Because critics from the Song onwards judged those additional chapters by their style and called them a later fabrication, and they are printed with the rest all the same."}]},
{"id":"cnh-060","num":60,"category":"Ancient","question":"Robbers opening a Wei king’s grave in 279 CE found the tablets of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a chronicle running from the Yellow Emperor down to 298 BCE.","questions":["Where the received chronology opens the Xia in 2205 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opens it in 1989 and gives the dynasty 432 years, a disagreement its compiler’s date makes worth hearing.","Fifteen works came out of the tomb, and only a <i>Book of Changes</i> and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were thought worth keeping once the tablets had been transcribed."],"answer":"Bamboo Annals","answerText":"Bamboo Annals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 295 BCE, with King Xiang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">279 CE, in a tomb at Ji</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Huangdi to 298 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xia opens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1989 BCE, not 2205</span></div>","traditional":"竹書紀年","hanzi":"竹书纪年","pinyin":"Zhúshū jìnián","translations":"","abstract":"<b><i>Bamboo Annals</i></b> is a chronicle of early China found in a tomb in 279 CE, running from the Yellow Emperor down to 298 BCE, and it reaches a reader by a different route from every other account of that age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Robbers opened the grave of King Xiang of Wei, dead since 295 BCE, and found bamboo tablets written in small seal script carrying more than 100,000 words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were damaged in the handling before reaching the imperial library, and the emperor set his best scholars to transcribe them into current characters and put them in order, which took two years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifteen works could be made out; most were legend and were soon neglected, but two were not — a copy of the <i>Book of Changes</i> agreeing with the received text, and the annals themselves, in twelve or thirteen chapters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scholar who saw them in the library the next year records both the damage and those two, which is what makes the find hard to dismiss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their chronology is not the received one: they open the Xia in 1989 BCE rather than 2205, give it 432 years, and have Yu publish the seasons of Xia in his first year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where they disagree they have a claim to be heard, since their compiler belonged to Wei and lived under the last princes he describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> But tablets were lost and their order confused, passages seem to have drifted in from the eleven books of fragmentary sayings the same tomb held, and the notes printed with the entries have Yu born of a mother who swallowed a pearl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The annals proper carry showers of gold, monstrous animals and changes of sex, which have been likened to the marvels in Livy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chinese scholars have called the whole thing a forgery imposed on the world by one of the men who transcribed it, while modern work has gone the other way, screening the Xia entries and arguing that many are historical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 105–7, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 176–77, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxc–cxciii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 117–18, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Eastern_Zhou_Bamboo_Slips_from_Chu_Grave.jpg/1920px-Eastern_Zhou_Bamboo_Slips_from_Chu_Grave.jpg","title":"Eastern Zhou bamboo slips","desc":"Not the annals, which survive only as a text: these are inscribed slips from a Chu grave, sealed in preservative, of the kind and period found in King Xiang's tomb. Henan Provincial Museum, Zhengzhou.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Ten narrow strips of bamboo written over in black brush characters, each sealed upright in a glass tube of clear liquid and laid in a row beside a long bronze blade on a white museum tray."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this find hard to dismiss as an invention?","a":"Because a scholar who saw the tablets in the imperial library the year after the tomb was opened records both the damage and the two works that could be made out."},{"q":"Why do its entries have a claim to be heard where they disagree?","a":"Because its compiler belonged to Wei and lived under the last princes he describes, so the chronicle is close to its own end."},{"q":"Why is the text as printed still unreliable?","a":"Because tablets were lost and their order confused, passages seem to have drifted in from other books in the same tomb, and the annals carry showers of gold and changes of sex."}]},
{"id":"cnh-061","num":61,"category":"Ancient","question":"Noticing that the later a Chinese source is the longer the legendary age it describes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked in the 1920s whether the Xia had existed.","questions":["Seven volumes of <i>Debating Ancient History</i> came out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held that early Chinese history was a tale retold with new material added at the front.","The 400-hectare site of Erlitou, found in 1959 where the texts put the Xia, is the archaeological answer to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its case against the early dynasties."],"answer":"Doubting Antiquity School","answerText":"Doubting Antiquity School","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1920s, in China</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 volumes of Debating Ancient History</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Erlitou found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1959, in western Henan</span></div>","traditional":"疑古派","hanzi":"疑古派","pinyin":"Yígǔ pài","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Doubting Antiquity School</b> is the name given to a movement in Chinese historical scholarship of the 1920s that put the early dynasties, and the Xia above all, on trial as evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founder noticed a pattern in the records: the later a source, the longer the legendary age it describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Western Zhou knew only Yu; Confucius, in the Spring and Autumn period, knew Yao and Shun before him; people of the Warring States knew older kings still, Huangdi and Shennong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that he argued that early Chinese history is a tale told and retold, with new material added at the front rather than the back, and he questioned openly whether the Xia had existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The school edited seven volumes of <i>Debating Ancient History</i>, weeding out entries it judged unauthentic, and it noted how much plain legend the classical Xia records carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The doubt was not new: a Chinese scholar of the 18th century had called a chief source for the Xia a fabrication imposed on the world, and a French translator of the Chinese annals wrote in 1895 that the Xia belonged to prehistory rather than to history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What answered the school was not argument but digging: in 1959 a survey went looking for the Xia where the texts put it, in western Henan, and found the 400-hectare site of Erlitou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese scholarship has grown steadily more confident since, screening the old records’ Xia entries against other classical writing and bronze inscriptions and finding many defensible; the identification of Erlitou itself still rests on place and date rather than on writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At a seminar on Xia culture held in 1990 the Western participants were about evenly split on whether the dynasty existed, while the Chinese participants were unanimous that it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sceptics’ methodological worry has not gone away either: a programme that sets archaeology to find the capitals named in texts lets written history decide what archaeology looks for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["school of thought","history","archaeology","china","literature"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxv–clxvi, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 176–77, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Gu_Jiegang_1920s.png","title":"The school's founder in the 1920s","desc":"Gu Jiegang, who set out the argument that the legendary age of Chinese history grew longer the later the source describing it.","credit":"Photographer unknown, 1920s. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"An oval black-and-white studio portrait of a young man in round wire spectacles and a plain high-collared gown, looking straight at the camera."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the age of a source matter to what it describes?","a":"Because the later the source the longer the legendary age it gives, so early Chinese history reads as a tale retold with new material added at the front rather than the back."},{"q":"Why did excavation rather than argument answer the school?","a":"Because a survey went looking for the dynasty where the texts put it and found the 400-hectare site of Erlitou in western Henan."},{"q":"Why has the sceptics' methodological worry survived that find?","a":"Because a programme that sets archaeology to find the capitals named in texts lets written history decide what archaeology looks for, and the identification still rests on place and date rather than writing."}]},
{"id":"cnh-062","num":62,"category":"Ancient","question":"Between the Longshan cultures and the Shang, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the central Yellow River for some four hundred years, in four phases of roughly a century each.","questions":["The white and grey three-legged ritual vessels of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were made in the Yiluo basin and grow scarcer with every kilometre away from it.","Rammed-earth foundations and bronze vessels appear only in the last two phases of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so most of what makes it look like a state is late."],"answer":"Erlitou culture","answerText":"Erlitou culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, of about 100 years each</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 400 hectares</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭文化","hanzi":"二里头文化","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu wénhuà","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Erlitou culture</b> is the archaeological culture of the central Yellow River between about the 20th and the 17th centuries BCE, named for a site in the Luoyang basin and standing between the Longshan cultures and the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates spread it over some four hundred years, and it is divided into four phases of roughly a century each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its strata overlie the local Longshan culture and are probably continuous with it, and it was replaced without a gap by the Erligang phase of the Shang, though how far that succession was political as well as cultural is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what makes it look like a state belongs to its last two phases: the great rammed-earth foundations and the bronze vessels appear only in the third and fourth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ritual pottery is the culture’s signature — white and fine grey vessels standing on three legs, a form taken over from Shandong — made in the Yiluo basin and growing scarcer with distance from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the third phase the culture reached furthest, and the same vessels turn up at sites in western Henan and southern Shanxi, so alike in shape that they were more likely carried from the centre than copied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That pattern reads as prestige goods passed down to lesser elites in the periphery, and as a shared set of rituals rather than a shared population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The expansion went towards resources: Dongxiafeng in the Jinnan basin, at 25 hectares one of the largest sites of the period there, sits five kilometres (three miles) from the copper and salt of the Zhongtiao mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The centre itself, at about 400 hectares, was the only settlement of that size in the Central Plain, and people appear to have moved into it rather than a town having grown where it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> White pottery goes out of use under the Erligang, its place in ceremony taken by bronze vessels doing the same work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Pottery_he_%28wine_vessel%29%2C_Erlitou_Culture%2C_Erlitou_site%2C_Yanshi%2C_Henan.jpg","title":"A pottery he from Erlitou","desc":"A pale grey three-legged wine vessel from the type site at Yanshi in Henan, of the form the culture's ritual pottery takes.","credit":"Uncredited photograph, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale grey pottery jug standing on three hollow pointed legs, with a domed top, a short spout, a strap handle and a band of small impressed dots round its shoulder."},"locator":{"name":"Erlitou culture","at":[112.689,34.693],"kind":"region","area":[[110.2,34.2],[110.5,35.6],[111.8,36.2],[113.2,35.8],[114.3,34.9],[114,33.9],[112.8,33.4],[111.2,33.6]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the distant pottery read as carried rather than copied?","a":"Because the vessels at sites in western Henan and southern Shanxi are so alike in shape to those made in the Yiluo basin that they were more likely brought from the centre."},{"q":"Why does the expansion follow the resources?","a":"Because Dongxiafeng, one of the largest sites of the period in the Jinnan basin, sits five kilometres from the copper and salt of the Zhongtiao mountains."},{"q":"Why does its signature pottery disappear under the Shang?","a":"Because bronze vessels took over the same work in ceremony, so the white ware went out of use."}]},
{"id":"cnh-063","num":63,"category":"Ancient","question":"A palace and temple district of 10.8 hectares, boxed in by four intersecting roads, stood on the highest ground of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in western Henan.","questions":["No wall ever ran round the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the whole volume of its public building was seven times smaller than at the older centre it followed.","Rulers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> walled a bronze and turquoise workshop before they walled their own palace, controlling the making of power’s symbols first."],"answer":"Erlitou site","answerText":"Erlitou site","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 100 hectares</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 300 hectares</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At its height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 400 hectares</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭遺址","hanzi":"二里头遗址","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu yízhǐ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Erlitou site</b> is a Bronze Age settlement in the Yiluo basin of western Henan, the largest of its time in the Central Plain and the place its whole archaeological culture is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was found in 1959 by a survey looking for the Xia where the texts put it, and is divided into four phases from its layers and the sequence of its pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first, from about 1750 BCE, it covered more than 100 hectares and was the most densely peopled place in the region, though no house or public building of that phase has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the second it tripled to more than 300 hectares and began to be planned, with a palace and temple district of 10.8 hectares on the highest ground, boxed in by four intersecting roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A workshop walled in rammed earth went up south of it, given over to bronze and turquoise and drawing metal from southern Shanxi, so the rulers controlled the making of power’s symbols before they walled the palace itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Burials from late in that phase fall into four or five grades by the labour spent on the pit and the goods put in it, and the richest, the ones with bronze, are found at no other site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inside the palace district only the top two grades were buried, along with a small number of corpse dumps that include people buried alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere the dead lie close to the living, artisans near where they had worked, and rich and poor to some extent mixed across the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the place has not got is a wall around it, where the older centre at Taosi had one, and the whole volume of its public building was seven times smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached about 400 hectares at its height, the only settlement of that size in the Central Plain, and its political weight fell away in the last phase, as the Erligang centres rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Pottery_drain_pipe%2C_Erlitou_culture%2C_Erlitou_site%2C_Yanshi%2C_Henan.jpg/1920px-Pottery_drain_pipe%2C_Erlitou_culture%2C_Erlitou_site%2C_Yanshi%2C_Henan.jpg","title":"A drain pipe from Erlitou","desc":"A cord-marked ceramic drain pipe from the site at Yanshi in Henan.","credit":"Uncredited photograph, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A thick cylindrical pipe of pale grey fired clay, its outer surface covered in fine cord impressions, lying on its side in a museum case."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the workshop get its wall before the palace did?","a":"Because the rulers controlled the making of power's symbols first: a rammed-earth enclosure for bronze and turquoise went up south of the palace district in the same phase."},{"q":"Why is the burial evidence uneven?","a":"Because inside the palace district only the top two grades were buried, while elsewhere the dead lie close to the living with rich and poor to some extent mixed."},{"q":"Why is the absence of a wall around it notable?","a":"Because the older centre at Taosi had one, and the whole volume of this site's public building was seven times smaller."}]},
{"id":"cnh-064","num":64,"category":"Ancient","question":"Cast in moulds built of several pieces, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave China its first ritual vessels in metal and the emblem its rulers would use for the rest of the Bronze Age.","questions":["One walled workshop of about a hectare produced all the known <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and what survives in it is slag, crucible fragments and clay moulds rather than vessels.","Moulds for rims 36 centimetres across survive among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with no matching vessel found, because only medium and small tombs at the site have been dug."],"answer":"Erlitou bronze","answerText":"Erlitou bronze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Foundry from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1800 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vessels from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foundry area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1 hectare</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest moulds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">36 cm rim diameter</span></div>","traditional":"二里頭青銅器","hanzi":"二里头青铜器","pinyin":"Èrlǐtóu qīngtóngqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Erlitou bronze</b> is the metalwork of the Erlitou site in Henan, and it includes the earliest cast bronze ritual vessels known in China, which appear from about 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Metal had been worked in the country for centuries before that — ornaments, small tools and weapons, hammered or cast in simple stone moulds — and it carried no particular rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Taosi, where burials are sharply graded, the jade and the fine pottery went into the great tombs while a copper bell turned up in a small one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What changed at Erlitou was the technique: a vessel was cast in a mould built of several pieces, allowing shapes and surfaces no single stone mould can give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method appears to have been devised for ritual vessels in particular, and those vessels became the chief emblem of political and religious power for the rest of the Chinese Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The foundry took up about a hectare in the south of the site, walled in rammed earth together with the turquoise workshop, and it is the only place such vessels are known to have been cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives there is the waste of the work: thick deposits of slag, the remains of crucibles, and clay moulds for tools, weapons and ritual vessels alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of those moulds were cut for vessels up to 36 centimetres (14 inches) across the rim, and no matching vessel has turned up, since only medium and small tombs have been excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The copper behind it came from no state monopoly, for mining and smelting in the mountains that supplied Erlitou were small, local and sporadic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor was it all vessels: the same enclosure turned out bronze plaques whose faces were set with neatly cut turquoise, fitted together like a jigsaw into animal masks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]","Qin Xiaoli, “Turquoise Ornaments and Inlay Technology in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 55, no. 2 (2016): 208–39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/59199. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/20260408_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg/1280px-20260408_Bronze_Jue_from_Erlitou.jpg","title":"A bronze jue from Erlitou","desc":"A thin-walled three-legged wine cup unearthed at the Erlitou site in Yanshi, Henan Museum.","credit":"Photo by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A small green-corroded bronze cup on three splayed legs, with a long trough-shaped pouring spout at one end and a pointed lip at the other, lit against a dark museum case."},"why":[{"q":"Why does metal only now become an emblem of rank?","a":"Because it had been worked for centuries without carrying one — at Taosi a copper bell turned up in a small tomb while jade and fine pottery went into the great ones."},{"q":"Why was the new casting method the change that mattered?","a":"Because a mould built of several pieces allows shapes and surfaces no single stone mould can give, and it appears to have been devised for ritual vessels in particular."},{"q":"Why is the largest vessel cast there missing?","a":"Because some moulds were cut for rims up to 36 centimetres across and only medium and small tombs have been excavated."}]},
{"id":"cnh-065","num":65,"category":"Xia","question":"Commissioned by the Chinese government in 1996, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the Xia, Shang and Western Zhou absolute dates where firm chronology had begun only in 841 BCE.","questions":["Some 200 specialists in history, astronomy, archaeology and radiocarbon dating spent four and a half years on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose table was announced in Beijing in November 2000.","Wiggle-matched radiocarbon dates, retro-calculated astronomical events and tested textual entries were combined by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which put the Xia at 2070 to 1600 BCE."],"answer":"Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commissioned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1996, by the Chinese government</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 November 2000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Specialists</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 200, in four fields</span></div>","traditional":"夏商周斷代工程","hanzi":"夏商周断代工程","pinyin":"Xià Shāng Zhōu Duàndài Gōngchéng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project</b> is a Chinese state research programme that ran from 1996 to 2000 and set out to fix absolute dates for the three dynasties before the imperial age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Firm Chinese chronology began only in 841 BCE, and the Grand Historian said plainly why he stopped there: the Three Dynasties are ancient, and their chronology cannot be made precise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two millennia of argument since had produced at least 44 dates for the Zhou conquest of the Shang, the earliest 1130 BCE and the latest 1018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The programme came out of a senior official’s embarrassment on a 1995 visit to Egypt, whose precise chronology reached back to 2750 BCE, and its government backing drew criticism worldwide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 200 specialists in history, astronomy, archaeology and radiocarbon dating worked for four and a half years, in teams that ran independently and compared their results only at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For the conquest, a series of samples from a stratified pit at Fengxi was wiggle-matched against the calibration curve, cutting the range of possible dates from 112 years to 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A day-by-day account of the campaign preserved in the <i>Hanshu</i>, and a record in the <i>Guoyu</i> putting Jupiter in Quail Fire, then left one date in that span: 20 January 1046 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The report printed two rivals beside it, 1044 and 1027 BCE, the first resting on seven obscure graphs of a bronze inscription that has eight published readings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The table published on 9 November 2000 gave reign years for the late Shang and Western Zhou kings, and for the Xia only a framework of 2070 to 1600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nothing in it pushes the dates back — the received Xia span was already the 21st to the 17th century — and what it certainly left behind is a Chinese archaeology built on radiocarbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project","difficulty":5,"tags":["institution","history","archaeology","science","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Menghan Qiu, Linyao Du, Richard Staff, Ziyun Yan, Tingting Yan, Huihui Cao, Chris Gosden, Ruiliang Liu, and Guanghui Dong, “Chronology of Early China: A Radiocarbon Databank for Chinese Archaeology,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12 (2025): 1665, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12540757/. [Open access]","Xianglong Chen, “Radiocarbon Dating and Its Applications in Chinese Archeology: An Overview,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1064717, https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/feart.2023.1064717/full. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, clxxxvii–clxxxviii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “Social Complexity in North China during the Early Bronze Age: A Comparative Study of the Erlitou and Lower Xiajiadian Cultures,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 261–92, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17049. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/Li_gui_inscription.jpg","title":"A rubbing of the Li gui inscription","desc":"The inscription cast inside an early Western Zhou bronze vessel, whose reading is one of the disputed points in dating the Zhou conquest.","credit":"Rubbing uploaded by 陇右甘南, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A rubbing of an ancient Chinese bronze inscription, the angular seal-script characters showing white on a black ground in five vertical columns."},"why":[{"q":"Why did two millennia of scholarship leave the conquest undated?","a":"Because the argument had produced at least 44 dates for it, the earliest 1130 BCE and the latest 1018, with firm Chinese chronology beginning only in 841."},{"q":"Why did wiggle-matching a stratified pit help?","a":"Because matching a series of samples from Fengxi against the calibration curve cut the range of possible dates from 112 years to 30."},{"q":"Why did the report print rivals beside its own answer?","a":"Because 1044 and 1027 BCE remained defensible, the first resting on seven obscure graphs of a bronze inscription that has eight published readings."}]},
{"id":"cnh-066","num":66,"category":"Shang","question":"Ruling the Central Plain from about 1600 to 1046 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest Chinese dynasty attested in records of its own time, its diviners’ oracle bones.","questions":["Horse-drawn chariots, the royal hunt and writing all appear under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Anyang, its last seat, and at none of the centres that came before it.","Shared pottery and shared elite goods mark the reach of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though they show a culture rather than proof that one state ruled it all."],"answer":"Shang dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Middle Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1050 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商朝","hanzi":"商朝","pinyin":"Shāng cháo","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang dynasty</b> is the second of the three dynasties that ruled China before the imperial age, holding the Central Plain from about 1600 to 1046 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeology now divides its five and a half centuries in three: an early phase to about 1400 BCE, a middle phase to about 1250, and a late phase down to the Zhou conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early phase is known from Zhengzhou and its neighbours as the Erligang horizon, which is defined mostly by its pottery rather than by anything written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The centre moved more than once, and at Anyang two of its seats lie side by side: the walled town of Huanbei, which lasted less than a century from the 14th, and Yinxu after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anyang has three things no earlier centre can show — horse-drawn chariots, the royal hunt and writing — together with a bronze industry unmatched in scale and organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That writing makes the Shang the earliest Chinese dynasty attested in records of its own time, the inscriptions its court diviners left on oracle bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How far its rule reached is far from clear, since the sites sharing its pottery and its elite goods share a culture, which is not the same as belonging to one state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither Erlitou before it nor Anyang itself was walled, and the inscriptions name settlements rather than capitals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At least 10,000 late Shang burials have been dug outside the royal cemetery, and more than four in five hold a single coffin, a few pots and perhaps a cowry shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor was the countryside simple: Guandimiao, a village 200 kilometres (125 miles) from Anyang and the only one of its date nearly fully excavated, made pottery for a whole region and used bone tools from the capital’s own workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang dynasty","difficulty":2,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yanfeng Hou, Roderick Campbell, Zhipeng Li, Yan Zhang, Suting Li, and Yuling He, “The Guandimiao Bone Assemblage (and What It Says about the Shang Economy),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 281–310, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72094. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 183–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Ceremonial_Axe%2C_China%2C_Shang_dynasty%2C_14th-11th_century_BC%2C_cast_bronze_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00776.jpg/1280px-Ceremonial_Axe%2C_China%2C_Shang_dynasty%2C_14th-11th_century_BC%2C_cast_bronze_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00776.jpg","title":"A Shang ceremonial axe","desc":"A cast bronze axe of the 14th to 11th century BCE, its upper band worked with the animal masks characteristic of Shang bronzework.","credit":"Photo by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A broad bronze axe head, green and rust with corrosion, its flaring crescent blade below a band of stylised animal masks and a row of downward-pointing triangles."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this the earliest Chinese dynasty attested in its own records?","a":"Because the court diviners left inscriptions on oracle bones, so the state's writing survives from its own time rather than only in later texts."},{"q":"Why is the extent of its rule far from clear?","a":"Because the sites sharing its pottery and its elite goods share a culture, which is not the same as belonging to one state, and the inscriptions name settlements rather than capitals."},{"q":"Why is the countryside not the simple thing the capital suggests?","a":"Because Guandimiao, a village 200 kilometres away and the only one of its date nearly fully excavated, made pottery for a whole region and used bone tools from the capital's own workshops."}]},
{"id":"cnh-067","num":67,"category":"Shang","question":"The fullest connected account of the Shang in any transmitted text is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which names every king from the ancestor Xie down to the dynasty’s last.","questions":["Closing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its historian names his own sources: the sacrificial odes for the ancestor Xie, and the Documents and the Odes from Tang the Victorious onward.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives the Yin thirty generations and the Xia seventeen, against only fifteen for the Zhou ancestors over the same stretch, which is arithmetic that will not reconcile."],"answer":"Basic Annals of Yin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE, under the Han</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chapter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, of 130</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 kings, to Di Xin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fixed dates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">none before 841 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"殷本紀","hanzi":"殷本纪","pinyin":"Yīn běnjì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Basic Annals of Yin</b> is the chapter of Sima Qian’s <i>Shiji</i> that carries the Shang, and it is the fullest connected account of the dynasty the transmitted texts give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It opens with the ancestor Xie, born after his mother swallowed an egg let fall by a dark bird, and runs through every king to the last, closing with a Shang prince set over Song to continue the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dynasty carries two names in it: Shang, from the land granted to Xie, and Yin, taken when Pan Geng moved his people to a place of that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The historian names his own sources at the close of the chapter — the sacrificial odes for Xie, and from Tang the Victorious onward the Documents and the Odes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a short shelf: of the 29 chapters of the received Documents only nine treat anything before the Zhou, and five poems of the Odes concern the Yin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The story’s shape is a moral one, in which Tang takes the mandate of Heaven from the last Xia king and the Shang keep it until Di Xin, the 31st, throws it away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A cycle of that kind may be a Zhou idea laid over the past rather than a record of it, and the chapter was written more than a thousand years after the events it opens with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its own arithmetic strains: seventeen generations are given to the Xia and thirty to the Yin, against fifteen for the Zhou ancestors across the same stretch of time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor does it carry dates, since no year in the transmitted record is fixed before 841 BCE, so the annals hand down an order of kings with no calendar behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is no longer the only witness, because the Shang left writing of their own at Anyang, against which the chapter can now be read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Basic Annals of Yin","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxxxix–cxl, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 173–75, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 207–8, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/%E3%80%8E%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98%E3%80%8F%E5%94%90%E5%86%99%E6%9C%AC.jpg/1280px-%E3%80%8E%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98%E3%80%8F%E5%94%90%E5%86%99%E6%9C%AC.jpg","title":"A Tang manuscript copy","desc":"The close of chapter 29 of Sima Qian's history in a Tang-dynasty copy, the small characters between the columns being a commentator's notes.","credit":"ColBase, National Institutes for Cultural Heritage, Japan (Tokyo National Museum TB-1573), CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale manuscript scroll written in vertical columns of Chinese brush characters, with smaller commentary characters interleaved between them and a red seal near the left edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the historian's own list of sources tell against the chapter's fullness?","a":"Because it is a short shelf: of the 29 chapters of the received Documents only nine treat anything before the Zhou, and five poems of the Odes concern the Yin."},{"q":"Why does the chapter's own arithmetic strain?","a":"Because seventeen generations are given to the Xia and thirty to the Yin, against fifteen for the Zhou ancestors across the same stretch of time."},{"q":"Why can it now be tested rather than only believed?","a":"Because the Shang left writing of their own at Anyang, against which the chapter can be read."}]},
{"id":"cnh-068","num":68,"category":"Shang","question":"Remembered as the lord of Bo who took the mandate from the last Xia king, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bears a posthumous title glossed as the one who puts away cruelty and suppresses the tyrant.","questions":["Finding a hunter’s nets drawn on all four sides of a plain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took three of them away, and the lords said his virtue reached the birds and the beasts.","Yi Yin reached <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> either by talking his way from savoury flavours to government or after five summonses, and Mencius rejected the first of those accounts outright."],"answer":"Tang of Shang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE, in tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 Shang kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14th from Xie</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 before Bo</span></div>","traditional":"商湯","hanzi":"商汤","pinyin":"Shāng Tāng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tang of Shang</b> is the founder of the Shang in the traditional account, the lord who overthrew the last Xia king and took the mandate of Heaven from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tang is a posthumous title, glossed as the one who puts away cruelty and suppresses the tyrant; the name the annals give him is Tian Yi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the ancestor Xie down to him the line had shifted its seat eight times, and he settled at Bo, taking over the residence of an older king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name repays a second look, since from an ancestor six generations before him every Shang ruler carries one of the ten cyclical characters, said to mark the day he was born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began, the annals say, with the count of Ge, who had left off making the sacrifices, and it was by punishing him that Tang started.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One saying of his is quoted plain: men look into water to see their faces, and one looks at the people to know whether a government is good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another is the net, which he came upon drawn across all four sides of a plain by a hunter praying that everything under heaven should walk into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tang took away three of the sides and prayed instead that only what had tired of living should enter, and the lords said his virtue reached the birds and the beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His minister Yi Yin comes down in two versions: a man who could reach him only by entering a bride’s household and talking his way from savoury flavours to the art of government, or a private man sent for five times before he would come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mencius rejected the first, saying he had heard Yi Yin approached Tang by the doctrines of Yao and Shun and not by his knowledge of cookery, and the historian who gives both seems not to have pressed it either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tang of Shang","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","literature","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 175–76, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 177, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 178–79, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 180, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 364, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Tang_of_the_Shang_Dynasty.jpg/1280px-Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Emperor_Tang_of_the_Shang_Dynasty.jpg","title":"Tang of Shang imagined","desc":"An album leaf of the 19th or early 20th century, from a set of portraits of famous men, painted some three and a half thousand years after the reign it shows.","credit":"Philadelphia Museum of Art, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A painted album leaf showing a bearded ruler in a flat-topped crown hung with strings of white beads, wearing a dark robe patterned with pale discs."},"locator":{"name":"Shang dynasty","at":[113.6903,34.7522],"kind":"region","area":[[110,34],[110.5,36.5],[112.5,37.8],[115,37.5],[117.5,36.6],[118.5,35.3],[117.5,34],[115.5,33],[113,32.5],[111,33]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is the name he is remembered by not the name he bore?","a":"Because it is a posthumous title, glossed as the one who puts away cruelty and suppresses the tyrant, where the annals give his name as Tian Yi."},{"q":"Why does the story of the net stand for his rule?","a":"Because he took away three sides of a hunter's trap and prayed that only what had tired of living should enter, at which the lords said his virtue reached the birds and the beasts."},{"q":"Why did Mencius reject one account of his minister?","a":"Because he had heard Yi Yin approached him by the doctrines of Yao and Shun and not by his knowledge of cookery."}]},
{"id":"cnh-069","num":69,"category":"Shang","question":"North-west of modern Anyang, on the Yellow River’s alluvial plain, lie the ruins of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last Shang capital, which the oracle bones call Great Settlement Shang.","questions":["No inscription from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> names Pan Geng or the two kings after him, so the site’s own written record opens with Wu Ding, ninth from the dynasty’s end.","One foundry at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has given up more than 10,000 mould fragments, and the settlement round it was among the largest consumers of metal anywhere in Bronze Age Eurasia."],"answer":"Yinxu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, Yinxu I – IV</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wu Ding, 9th king from the end</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2006</span></div>","traditional":"殷墟","hanzi":"殷墟","pinyin":"Yīnxū","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Yinxu</b> is the last capital of the Shang, a spread of ruins on both banks of the Huan river north-west of modern Anyang in Henan, listed as a World Heritage site in 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle-bone inscriptions found there call the place Great Settlement Shang, which is literally a big urban centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Occupation ran for rather more than two centuries, from about 1250 to 1046 BCE, and archaeologists divide it into four phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tradition puts the move here earlier, under Pan Geng about 1300, but no inscription names him or the two kings after him, so the site’s own written record opens with Wu Ding, ninth from the end of the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It sat on the alluvial plain of the Yellow River with mountains to the west, on fertile floodplain soil among forests and abundant wildlife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Holocene climatic optimum was drawing to a close as the settlement grew, and the region turned cooler and drier around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its farmers widened what they sowed, to millet, rice, sorghum, barley and wheat together, which may have been insurance against a failed harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Society there was ordered by lineage, the king’s own line above the princely and other royal ones, and the artisans grouped by the trade they owed the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its appetite for metal was extraordinary — the settlement became one of the largest consumers of it anywhere in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE — and one foundry alone has given up more than 10,000 mould fragments, most for ritual vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The clay was chosen as carefully as the metal: those moulds were made of untempered loess, worked to concentrate its silt and so raise the porosity that keeps flaws out of a casting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Yinxu","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 11, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 183–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Oracle_bones_pit.JPG/1280px-Oracle_bones_pit.JPG","title":"Pit YH127 at Yinxu","desc":"The oracle-bone pit as it was found, packed with inscribed turtle plastrons and cattle shoulder blades; the settlement's own written record came out of ground like this.","credit":"Photo by Xuan Che, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oracle_bones_pit.JPG","alt":"A round pit cut into pale earth, its floor and sides densely packed with hundreds of flat cream-coloured pieces of bone and shell lying on top of one another."},"locator":{"name":"Yinxu","at":[114.3186,36.1225],"zoom":9,"within":"Anyang"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the site's written record open later than the tradition does?","a":"Because no inscription names the king said to have moved the capital here or the two after him, so the inscriptions begin with Wu Ding, ninth from the end of the dynasty."},{"q":"Why did its farmers sow so many crops at once?","a":"Because millet, rice, sorghum, barley and wheat together may have been insurance against a failed harvest as the region turned cooler and drier."},{"q":"Why was the clay for the moulds chosen as carefully as the metal?","a":"Because they were made of untempered loess worked to concentrate its silt, which raises the porosity that keeps flaws out of a casting."}]},
{"id":"cnh-071","num":71,"category":"Shang","question":"Cut into turtle plastrons and cattle shoulder blades, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest Chinese writing that survives, and about half of its signs are still unread.","questions":["Only about 5% of the signs in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are basic shapes, against 48% in early cuneiform and 90% in Egyptian hieroglyphs, the rest being built by combination.","One character may be written in markedly different ways in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the chief obstacle to reading it and to teaching a machine to recognise it."],"answer":"oracle bone script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899, in a prescription</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Distinct signs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 4,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Read so far</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,000 signs</span></div>","traditional":"甲骨文","hanzi":"甲骨文","pinyin":"jiǎgǔwén","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Oracle bone script</b> is the writing of the late Shang, cut into turtle plastrons and cattle shoulder blades between about 1250 and 1046 BCE, and the earliest Chinese writing that survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is above all the script of royal divination: the bone was drilled and heated until it cracked, and the charge put to the powers, sometimes with its outcome, was cut beside the crack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it records is not only divination, since administrative notes and even a scribe’s writing practice are among the things cut into the bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It went unread for three thousand years and was recognised only in 1899, when a noted antiquarian dosing himself with ground bone under a prescription saw characters on the pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The pieces were traced to Anyang, where the last Shang capital came to light, and about 160,000 inscribed fragments have since come out of the ground there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It is a script fully able to represent the language, and it leans heavily on pictures: about 60% of its shapes depict something, against 70% in the earliest cuneiform and 95% in Egyptian hieroglyphs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roughly 170 basic shapes can be made out, each standing for what it draws, for something beside it, or for a word that merely sounds the same; almost everything else is built by modifying or combining them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corpus runs to some 4,000 distinct signs, though nobody can give an exact figure, and at least half of them are still unread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One character can be written in markedly different ways, the parts or the strokes arranged differently, and that fluidity is the chief obstacle to reading it: it defeats machines as well as people, whose recognition rates fall away sharply on the variant forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The inscriptions date themselves by the day alone, in the repeating cycle of sixty names, though five lunar eclipses recorded by a single group of diviners have been calculated to a thirty-year window between 1201 and 1181 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"oracle bone script","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","language","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Bang Li, Jing Yang, Yujie Liang, Xiaobin Hu, Zengmao Ding, Xu Peng, Shengwei Han, Peichao Qin, Donghao Luo, Taisong Jin, Feng Gao, Yongge Liu, and Rongrong Ji, “OBIMD: A Multi-modal Dataset for Contextual Interpretation of Oracle Bone Inscriptions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 13 (2026): 681, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13128845/. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang, Kaile Zhang, Xinyu Wang, Shengwei Han, Yongge Liu, Jinpeng Wan, Haisu Guan, Zhebin Kuang, Lianwen Jin, Xiang Bai, and Yuliang Liu, “An Open Dataset for Oracle Bone Character Recognition and Decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11379903/. [Open access]","Ludovica Ottaviano, Kathryn Kelley, Mattia Cartolano, and Silvia Ferrara, “Metaphors and the Invention of Writing,” <i>Topics in Cognitive Science</i> 18, no. 1 (2026): 245–63, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12831613/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Shang_dynasty_inscribed_scapula_Ink_rubbing.jpg/1280px-Shang_dynasty_inscribed_scapula_Ink_rubbing.jpg","title":"An ink rubbing of an inscribed bone","desc":"A rubbing of a large inscribed ox bone in the National Museum of China; the script is normally studied from rubbings like this rather than from the fragile originals.","credit":"Rubbing of unknown authorship, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"An ink rubbing in which a broken slab of bone appears as a black shape, with columns of small angular Chinese characters standing out in white and the breaks running white across it."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this script called fully developed despite its pictures?","a":"Because it is able to represent the language, and only about 5% of its signs are basic, everything else built by modifying or combining them."},{"q":"Why is variability the chief obstacle to reading it?","a":"Because one character can be written in markedly different ways, the parts arranged differently or the strokes made differently, which defeats machines as well as people."},{"q":"Why is the script studied through copies rather than originals?","a":"Because the bones were drilled and heat-cracked before use and come out of the ground fragile and broken, so rubbings, facsimiles and transcriptions carry the work."}]},
{"id":"cnh-072","num":72,"category":"Shang","question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a cattle shoulder blade or turtle plastron was hollowed behind and burned until the face split, and the record was cut beside the crack.","questions":["One record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reads that on the gui-mao day it was cracked, Ke tested, and ten bovines should be offered to Shang Jia.","Nearly 22,000 divination sentences survive from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against barely 400 records of an outcome, and the inscribed bones were a minority of those used."],"answer":"Shang divination","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sentences read</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly 22,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Outcomes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">barely 400 recorded</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Diviner group</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 25 men</span></div>","traditional":"商代占卜","hanzi":"商代占卜","pinyin":"Shāngdài zhānbǔ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang divination</b> is the practice by which the late Shang court put questions to its ancestors and to the powers, by burning bone until it cracked and reading the crack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The material was cattle shoulder blades and turtle plastrons, prepared beforehand by drilling hollows into the back so that heat applied there would split the face in a controlled way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was cut beside the crack follows a form that repeats: the day, the diviner who tested it, and the proposition put.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of them reads that on the gui-mao day it was cracked and Ke tested, and that on the next jia-chen day ten bovines should be offered to Shang Jia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The day named is not arbitrary, since an ancestor received his offering on the day whose name he carried, and Shang Jia takes his on a jia day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The kings divined continually to settle that offerings were right in kind, number, colour and combination, and killed by the correct ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They acted as though divination could find the particular contract that would satisfy a given ancestor and lift a curse, since the dead were held to keep power over the living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diviners worked in groups, known by their names appearing on the same bones, by what they asked about and by the pits their bones came from, one such group counting about 25 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over the two centuries at Anyang the practice grew systematic, ending in a fixed cycle of five rites to the dead kings and queens run through the ten-day weeks, and diviners other than the king became markedly fewer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What survives is a sample and not a record: a large annotated corpus holds nearly 22,000 divination sentences and barely 400 notes of an outcome, and inscribed bones were only a minority of those used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang divination","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","religion","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Bang Li, Jing Yang, Yujie Liang, Xiaobin Hu, Zengmao Ding, Xu Peng, Shengwei Han, Peichao Qin, Donghao Luo, Taisong Jin, Feng Gao, Yongge Liu, and Rongrong Ji, “OBIMD: A Multi-modal Dataset for Contextual Interpretation of Oracle Bone Inscriptions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 13 (2026): 681, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13128845/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Shang_Inscribed_Tortoise_Plastron_%28for_divination%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Inscribed_Tortoise_Plastron_%28for_divination%29.jpg","title":"An inscribed tortoise plastron","desc":"A plastron used for divination in the reign of Wu Ding, its surface crossed by cracks with characters cut in groups beside them.","credit":"Photo by Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale oval tortoise shell plate mounted on a fabric board, crossed by branching dark cracks, with small angular Chinese characters incised in groups between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the bones drilled before they were burnt?","a":"Because hollows cut into the back meant that heat applied there would split the face in a controlled way."},{"q":"Why does the day named in an inscription matter?","a":"Because an ancestor received his offering on the day whose name he carried, so Shang Jia takes his on a jia day."},{"q":"Why is what survives a sample rather than a record?","a":"Because a large annotated corpus holds nearly 22,000 divination sentences and barely 400 notes of an outcome, and inscribed bones were only a minority of those used."}]},
{"id":"cnh-073","num":73,"category":"Shang","question":"Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the 22nd Shang king, the dynasty’s own written record begins, no inscription at Anyang naming any of the three kings before him.","questions":["For three years <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not speak, leaving government to his chief minister, before dreaming of the sage he then had searched for across the country.","Five lunar eclipses in the records of the diviners working for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been calculated to a window between 1201 and 1181 BCE."],"answer":"Wu Ding","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1192 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22nd Shang king</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eclipses dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1201 – 1181 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silent for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 years, in tradition</span></div>","traditional":"武丁","hanzi":"武丁","pinyin":"Wǔ Dīng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Wu Ding</b> is the Shang king with whom the dynasty’s own written record begins, the 22nd of its line and the first at Anyang whose reign the inscriptions reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronology project puts him at 1250 to 1192 BCE, and the late Shang is usually reckoned from him rather than from the move to Anyang three reigns earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No inscription found there names any of those three predecessors, so the site’s writing and his reign begin together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A group of about 25 diviners was at work in his later years, and five lunar eclipses recorded among them have been calculated to a window between 1201 and 1181 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those eclipses are among the few fixed points from which his regnal years can be worked out at all, and the later Shang kings divined far more themselves and used far fewer diviners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The transmitted account gives him a different kind of greatness: he came to the throne meaning to make the Shang flourish again, and had found nobody able to help him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For three years he did not speak, leaving every decision to his chief minister while he watched how the country behaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Then he dreamed of a sage, compared the face against all his ministers and officers without finding it, had it drawn, and had the country searched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man was found among convicts raising earthworks at the difficult passes of Fu; Wu Ding said that was he, talked with him, made him counsellor, and gave him the name of the place, so that he is called Fu Yue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When a pheasant settled on a sacrificial tripod and cried it was read as a warning that he favoured his own father’s rites above the other ancestors’, and the record says that once he reformed his government the dynasty prospered again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Wu Ding","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 195–96, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 196–97, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 11, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/%E9%99%88%E4%B9%A6%E7%94%BB%E5%8E%86%E4%BB%A3%E5%B8%9D%E7%8E%8B%E9%81%93%E7%BB%9F%E5%9B%BE%E9%92%B1%E9%99%88%E7%BE%A4%E4%B9%A6%E8%B5%9E%E5%90%88%E5%86%8C8.png/1280px-%E9%99%88%E4%B9%A6%E7%94%BB%E5%8E%86%E4%BB%A3%E5%B8%9D%E7%8E%8B%E9%81%93%E7%BB%9F%E5%9B%BE%E9%92%B1%E9%99%88%E7%BE%A4%E4%B9%A6%E8%B5%9E%E5%90%88%E5%86%8C8.png","title":"Wu Ding dreams of a helper","desc":"A painted album leaf whose title reads that Gaozong of Yin dreamed he was granted a good helper; Gaozong is Wu Ding's temple name.","credit":"Palace Museum, Beijing, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A painted album leaf: at the right a palace roof with two standing figures on the terrace above and guards asleep below it, at the left a page of Chinese calligraphy."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the dynasty's written record begin with this reign?","a":"Because no inscription found at the site names any of the three kings before him, so the writing and his reign begin together."},{"q":"Why can his regnal years be worked out at all?","a":"Because five lunar eclipses recorded among his diviners have been calculated to a window between 1201 and 1181 BCE, which are among the few fixed points available."},{"q":"Why did he say nothing for three years?","a":"Because he had come to the throne meaning to make the Shang flourish and found nobody able to help him, so he left every decision to his chief minister while he watched how the country behaved."}]},
{"id":"cnh-074","num":74,"category":"Shang","question":"Known first from the oracle bones and then from her own grave at Anyang, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a consort of Wu Ding who oversaw a domain of her own and led troops.","questions":["The only intact top royal burial yet dug at Anyang is that of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why more is known of her than of people who outranked her at court.","Ritual bronzes made for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were cast from very pure copper by controlled alloying, where those of lesser elites were remelted and carry more impurities."],"answer":"Fu Hao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consort of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wu Ding, r. c. 1250 – 1192 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Metal in tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1.6 tonnes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">up to 7 times larger</span></div>","traditional":"婦好","hanzi":"妇好","pinyin":"Fù Hǎo","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Fu Hao</b> is a consort of the Shang king Wu Ding, known first from the oracle bones and afterwards from her own grave at Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That order is what makes her remarkable: an individual named in a record of the 2nd millennium BCE and then met in the ground is a rare thing in Chinese archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fu is a term for a high-status woman or a royal spouse, and under a strictly patrimonial system such consorts held privileges and kinds of authority that were theirs alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The inscriptions show women of that rank overseeing territorial domains of their own, presenting tribute and gifts, leading troops into battle and going hunting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She was one of several of Wu Ding’s consorts, Fu Jing being another, and by no means the most important person buried at the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her grave is the only intact top royal burial yet excavated at Anyang, which is why more is known of her than of people who outranked her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It held something like 1.6 tonnes of metal, and the kingly tombs at Xibeigang, looted over and over, were up to seven times its size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Her ritual bronzes were cast from very pure copper by carefully controlled alloying, where the bronzes of lesser elites were made by remelting and carry more impurities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A woman who had borne male heirs was revered after death alongside the principal male ancestors, so the dead of her rank kept a place in the ritual round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the Shang sources give is description rather than praise: they record what she did, and the moral archetypes of what a woman ought to be are a Zhou habit rather than a Shang one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Fu Hao","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Sheri A. Lullo, review of <i>Women in Ancient China</i>, by Bret Hinsch, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 413–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76794. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 11, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg/1280px-Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg","title":"An owl-shaped zun made for Fu Hao","desc":"A bronze wine vessel in the form of a standing owl, cast with an inscription naming her.","credit":"Photo by Cangminzho, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A green and grey bronze vessel shaped as a standing owl on two thick legs and a tail, its surface covered in raised spiral and animal-mask ornament, with a crested lid."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the order of her discovery what makes her remarkable?","a":"Because she was named first in a record of the 2nd millennium BCE and afterwards met in the ground, which is a rare thing in Chinese archaeology."},{"q":"Why is more known of her than of people who outranked her?","a":"Because her grave is the only intact top royal burial yet excavated at the site, where the kingly tombs at Xibeigang were looted over and over."},{"q":"Why do the Shang sources describe her rather than praise her?","a":"Because they record what she did, the moral archetypes of what a woman ought to be being a Zhou habit rather than a Shang one."}]},
{"id":"cnh-075","num":75,"category":"Shang","question":"Burial M5 at Xiaotun in Anyang, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is one of the very few Shang graves found entirely intact and one of only two whose occupant can be named.","questions":["Jades already centuries old when they were buried, made by Neolithic cultures, were among the roughly 1,500 objects in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Anyang.","Inscriptions on ritual vessels from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, matched against names in the oracle bones, are what let its occupant be identified as an individual."],"answer":"tomb of Fu Hao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wu Ding, c. 1250 – 1192 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Objects</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,500</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronzes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">468 or 210, by count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Two ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">128 and 117.5 kilograms (282 and 259 pounds)</span></div>","traditional":"婦好墓","hanzi":"妇好墓","pinyin":"Fù Hǎo mù","translations":"","abstract":"<b>tomb of Fu Hao</b> is burial M5 at Xiaotun in Anyang, the grave of a consort of the Shang king Wu Ding and one of the very few Shang tombs found entirely intact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It lies away from the royal cemetery at Xibeigang, and beside the graves in that cemetery it is a small burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What was in it made its reputation: about 1,500 objects, among them weapons, bells, blades, mirrors and bronze sculptures of tigers, together with a great quantity of jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of its bronze ding stand 80 centimetres (31 inches) high and weigh 128 and 117.5 kilograms (282 and 259 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of the jades were already ancient when they were buried, pieces made by Neolithic cultures, which suggests they were valued for their age as much as for their look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Jade figures in formal poses and ceremonial dress went into the grave with her, probably meant as servants who would go on presenting offerings to the ancestors for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of only about five rich-bronze burials at Anyang found unlooted, and one of only two anywhere on the site whose occupant can be named at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The naming is done by matching inscriptions on the ritual vessels against names in the oracle bones, which is why an individual of the 2nd millennium BCE can be met here at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the name is unsettled, since several scholars read the graph as Zi rather than Hao, so the woman everyone calls Fu Hao may in fact be Fu Zi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So is the count of what it held: one study gives 468 bronzes in the tomb and another 210, and the two are almost certainly counting different things rather than contradicting each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"tomb of Fu Hao","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Sheri A. Lullo, review of <i>Women in Ancient China</i>, by Bret Hinsch, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 413–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76794. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_1.jpg/1280px-Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_1.jpg","title":"The tomb of Fu Hao, reconstructed at Yinxu","desc":"A full-size reconstruction of the burial chamber at the site museum in Anyang, laid out as the excavators recorded it: bronze vessels, bells and stone objects across the floor, with the skeletons of sacrificed attendants in the ledges along the walls. The objects shown are replicas, the originals being held in the National Museum of China in Beijing and the Henan Museum in Zhengzhou.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A rectangular pit with earth walls, its floor crowded with bronze vessels, two large bells and stone blades, and human skeletons laid out in narrow ledges along both side walls."},"why":[{"q":"Why could the occupant of this grave be named at all?","a":"Because inscriptions on its ritual vessels can be matched against names in the oracle bones, which is possible for only one other burial on the whole site."},{"q":"Why were some of its jades already old when they went in?","a":"Because pieces made by Neolithic cultures were buried with her, which suggests they were valued for their age as much as for their look."},{"q":"Why do published counts of its contents disagree so widely?","a":"Because one study gives 468 bronzes and another 210, and the two are almost certainly counting different things rather than contradicting each other."}]},
{"id":"cnh-076","num":76,"category":"Shang","question":"Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the ruler was the head of the apex lineage rather than an officer over subjects, and power passed to a king's brothers or to his sons.","questions":["A monopoly of approach to the royal ancestors, who were held to carry requests to the high god, is what made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> more than the leadership of one family among many.","Emblems on bronzes once read as lineage names may instead be the names of some 70 government offices, which would make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a good deal more bureaucratic than it looks."],"answer":"Shang kingship","answerText":"Shang kingship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In place</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31, in the traditional list</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Offices named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 70</span></div>","traditional":"商代王權","hanzi":"商代王权","pinyin":"Shāngdài wángquán","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang kingship</b> is the authority the Shang king held over the ruling house, the armies and the rites owed to the royal dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The traditional list of those kings runs to 31, Wu Ding standing 22nd in it and Di Xin last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Power passed dynastically inside the house, in hereditary succession from a king to his brothers or to his sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That house sat at the head of a society ordered by kinship, and on the fullest recent account the king was the leader of its apex lineage rather than an officer set over subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A much-quoted line in the <i>Zuozhuan</i> calls sacrifice and war the great affairs of the state, which for the Shang may be better put as the great affairs of the lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both grew more regular over the two centuries at Anyang, turning from occasional undertakings into systematic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What lifted the king above every other head of a family was his monopoly of approach to the royal ancestors, who were held to carry human business up to the high god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rank was physical as well as ritual, since the casting and circulation of metal followed the hierarchy closely: the highest elite were given vessels of carefully controlled alloy made from very pure copper, while those below them had secondary alloys with more impurities in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beneath the king his delegates and messengers were mostly <i>zi</i> princes, ranked by whether they held high military rank, marked <i>ya</i>, or high official rank, marked <i>bo</i>, and the heir was styled <i>Xiao Wang</i>, 'the Small King'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The emblems on bronzes, long read as the names of lineages, have lately been re-read as the names of some 70 government offices covering the army, finance, construction, communication and the running of the palace, each passing to the senior man of a lineage and then to one of his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","china","bronze age","politics"],"sources":["Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Vessel_for_wine_%28Fangyi%29._2_pictographs._Shang_Dynasty%2C_1300-1050_BCE._Bronze._From_China._Victoria_and_Albert_Museum%2C_London.jpg/1280px-Vessel_for_wine_%28Fangyi%29._2_pictographs._Shang_Dynasty%2C_1300-1050_BCE._Bronze._From_China._Victoria_and_Albert_Museum%2C_London.jpg","title":"A Shang bronze vessel with its owner's mark","desc":"A fangyi, a lidded wine container of the Shang period, in the Victoria and Albert Museum in London. The vessel and its lid carry an inscription of two pictographs, the kind of sign long read as the emblem of the owner's lineage and lately re-read as the name of an office.","credit":"Photograph by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A tall rectangular bronze vessel with a hipped, roof-like lid topped by a small knob, its sides covered in dense angular animal-mask ornament."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the king stand above every other head of a family?","a":"Because he held a monopoly of approach to the royal ancestors, who were held to carry human business up to the high god."},{"q":"Why is metal a measure of rank rather than of wealth?","a":"Because the highest elite were given vessels of carefully controlled alloy made from very pure copper, while those below had secondary alloys with more impurities."},{"q":"Why have the emblems on the bronzes been re-read?","a":"Because what were long taken for lineage names now look like the names of some 70 government offices, each passing to the senior man of a lineage and then to a descendant."}]},
{"id":"cnh-077","num":77,"category":"Shang","question":"Rain for the crops and floods held short of devastation were both in the gift of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was sovereign over the natural world and over the ancestral spirits themselves.","questions":["No one could approach <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> directly, so a request was laid before the ancestors instead, for them to carry up to a power that stood far from the living.","The king alone could receive the spirits of the royal dead and of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ruling the four quarters as the surrogate of a god whose grant his throne was."],"answer":"Shangdi","answerText":"Shangdi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"上帝","hanzi":"上帝","pinyin":"Shàngdì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shangdi</b> is the high god of the Shang, a name meaning 'High Sovereign', and the earliest references to him are the oracle-bone inscriptions themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was sovereign over the whole natural world and over the ancestral spirits, who settled the destiny of humans in his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The forces of nature were his: the rain a crop needed came from him, and so did a river flood held short of devastation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Command was his defining power, and the inscriptions carry the phrase <i>Di ming</i>, 'the decree of Di', a deity above all others being the one who could issue one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Yet he stood so far from the realm of the living that there was no direct way to approach him, so a request had to be laid before the ancestors to be carried up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Those ancestors were themselves graded, the older the more powerful, and all of them ranked below the powers of the pantheon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The one man who could cross that distance was the king, who alone could <i>bin</i>, 'receive the spirits', of Di and of the royal dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He ruled the four quarters as Di’s surrogate, and the right to rule was itself held to be Di’s grant, so that no line separated his religious authority from his political one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether he was one god at all is unsettled, since he has been identified with the first ancestor of the Shang and read instead as a general word for divinity, the graph being used of heads of lineages in the plural and as an honorific for an individual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Zhou afterwards spoke of <i>Tian</i>, 'Heaven', and Shangdi has been thought the same power under another name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["deity","religion","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 16, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Lisa Raphals, “Languages of Fate: Semantic Fields in Chinese and Greek,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 70–106, at 74, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Oracle_Bone_Script_Di_Variants.jpg/960px-Oracle_Bone_Script_Di_Variants.jpg","title":"The graph for Di in oracle-bone script","desc":"Sixteen variant forms of the graph di as it is written in the oracle-bone inscriptions, cut from rubbings and gathered into one plate. The name Shangdi is known first from inscriptions of this kind, and how the graph should be read is part of what is still argued over.","credit":"Compilation by the Wikimedia Commons user Quinlan718, CC BY-SA 4.0.","alt":"A grid of sixteen small black-and-white cut-outs, each showing a different angular form of the same incised character: a horizontal bar or triangle above a forked, crossed stem."},"why":[{"q":"Why could nobody approach this god directly?","a":"Because he stood so far from the realm of the living that a request had to be laid before the ancestors to be carried up to him."},{"q":"Why was there no line between the king's religious and political authority?","a":"Because he ruled the four quarters as the god's surrogate and the right to rule was itself held to be that god's grant."},{"q":"Why is it unsettled whether he was one god at all?","a":"Because he has been identified with the first ancestor of the Shang and read instead as a general word for divinity, the graph being used of heads of lineages in the plural."}]},
{"id":"cnh-078","num":78,"category":"Shang","question":"Making the dead into proper ancestors, who would then guide the nature powers and the high god, was the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a religion that belonged to every rank.","questions":["Far fewer cattle lie in the sacrificial pits than the inscriptions record being offered under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the likeliest answer being that the living ate them at the feast.","Burials, sacrificial pits, architecture, writing and feasting are all forms taken by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so its traces sit all over a Shang site rather than in one building."],"answer":"Shang ancestor worship","answerText":"Shang ancestor worship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One ox</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 200 kilograms (440 pounds) of meat</span></div>","traditional":"商代祖先崇拜","hanzi":"商代祖先崇拜","pinyin":"Shāngdài zǔxiān chóngbài","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang ancestor worship</b> is the cult the Shang paid to their own dead, a religion held to have belonged to all Shang people and one of the basic components of their society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its work was not simply to remember the dead but to make them into proper ancestors, who would then guide the nature powers and the high god on the living’s behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took several forms at once — burials, sacrificial pits, architecture, writing and feasting — so its traces lie all over a Shang site rather than in one building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rites ran on a calendar, repeated through the year and addressed to the high ancestors, the dead rulers and the consort queens alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Meat was cooked and presented in the great tripods, fermented drink was offered in three-legged beakers, and under the Shang such vessels and the jades beside them were tied to communicating with the ancestral spirits and to little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Spirits were held to be beyond control unless the living acted, which made the whole relationship a dangerous one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Yet far fewer cattle lie in the sacrificial pits than the inscriptions record being offered, and the likeliest answer is that the living ate them, at feasts that went with the sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One head of cattle yields over 200 kilograms (440 pounds) of meat, and by the late phases at Anyang cattle had passed pigs in dietary importance even in ordinary quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the same beasts fed the living and the dead, and by the end of the period cattle had overtaken human captives in royal sacrificial importance, while wild animals were almost never offered at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spending on burial fell across the centuries, read not as impoverishment but as a deliberate move from showing rank in material to showing it in symbol, and the cult itself is credited with driving the bronze industry that served it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","religion","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 15, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Clevelandart_1960.42.jpg/1280px-Clevelandart_1960.42.jpg","title":"A jue, the Shang cup for a libation","desc":"A bronze jue of the Shang period in the Cleveland Museum of Art. Vessels of this shape poured the fermented drink offered to the ancestral spirits, and a clan sign is cast inside the bowl.","credit":"Cleveland Museum of Art, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A slender bronze vessel on three long blade-like legs, with a deep bowl drawn out into a pouring lip at one end and a pointed tail at the other, two capped posts rising from its rim, and dense spiral ornament round its body."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the cult's work to make ancestors rather than to remember the dead?","a":"Because a proper ancestor would then guide the nature powers and the high god on the living's behalf, where an unattended spirit was held to be beyond control."},{"q":"Why are fewer cattle in the pits than the inscriptions record?","a":"Because the likeliest answer is that the living ate them at feasts that went with the sacrifice, one head yielding over 200 kilograms of meat."},{"q":"Why is falling expenditure on burial not read as impoverishment?","a":"Because it looks like a deliberate move from showing rank in material to showing it in symbol."}]},
{"id":"cnh-079","num":79,"category":"Shang","question":"The excavated pits at Anyang run about ten people to every animal, where the inscriptions record <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and animal offerings in something like parity.","questions":["Marking a royal funeral, answering a disaster, consecrating a building or securing fertility are among the occasions for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a practice known across many societies.","More than 80 skulls under the East Gate at Shimao show <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in China is older than the Shang by half a millennium, and its victims can now be sexed by their DNA."],"answer":"human sacrifice","answerText":"human sacrifice","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang record</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Shimao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 80 skulls at 1 gate</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pit ratio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 10 people to 1 animal</span></div>","traditional":"人祭","hanzi":"人祭","pinyin":"rénjì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>human sacrifice</b> is the killing of a person as an offering, known across many societies and undertaken to mark a royal funeral, to answer a disaster, to atone for a wrong, to consecrate a building or a place, or to secure fertility and health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Victims were also killed to serve a high-ranking person as retainers in the next world, so a single offering may be commemorative, expiatory, propitiatory and dedicatory at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In China the practice is older than the Shang: at Shimao, a walled city on the Loess Plateau, more than 80 human skulls lie buried beneath the East Gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two forms were used there, mass pits at the gates and at the palace, and victims entombed with the owner of a high-status grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient DNA has since corrected the record: 9 of the 10 East Gate victims were men, three of them read as women from the bones alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Shang the inscriptions show animals and people offered in something like parity, while the excavated pits run about ten people to every animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One divination proposes an exorcism to three high ancestors with a hundred jars of millet ale, a hundred Qiang captives and three hundred specially reared sheep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Qiang were a neighbouring people whose prisoners of war were offered like other flesh, and another inscription asks simply whether Qiang should be used in the offering to an ancestress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What royal practice divided was not the human from the animal but the wild from the domesticated, and a person could be brought into the second class through enslavement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor did any of it begin at Anyang: at Xiaoshuangqiao near Zhengzhou, a middle Shang site, the final report counts 33 pits holding human remains, one of them with at least 32 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","religion","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["María Constanza Ceruti, “Frozen Mummies from Andean Mountaintop Shrines: Bioarchaeology and Ethnohistory of Inca Human Sacrifice,” <i>BioMed Research International</i> 2015 (2015): 439428, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4543117/. [Open access]","Zehui Chen et al., “Ancient DNA from Shimao City Records Kinship Practices in Neolithic China,” <i>Nature</i> 648, no. 8094 (2025): 659–67, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12711557/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Shang_Sacrificial_Pit.jpg/1280px-Shang_Sacrificial_Pit.jpg","title":"A sacrificial pit at the Yin Ruins","desc":"An excavated pit kept and displayed where it was dug, at the on-site museum of the Yin Ruins in Anyang. The human remains lie as the excavators recorded them, under glass.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A shallow rectangular pit of pale earth seen from above through glass, with several human skeletons lying in it at different angles, some incomplete."},"why":[{"q":"Why can one offering be several kinds of act at once?","a":"Because victims might mark a royal funeral, answer a disaster, atone for a wrong, consecrate a building or serve a high-ranking person in the next world, so a killing may be commemorative, expiatory, propitiatory and dedicatory together."},{"q":"Why did ancient DNA change the record at Shimao?","a":"Because 9 of the 10 East Gate victims proved to be men, three of them having been read as women from the bones alone."},{"q":"Why does the royal practice divide the wild from the domesticated?","a":"Because that was the line it drew rather than the one between human and animal, and a person could be brought into the second class through enslavement."}]},
{"id":"cnh-080","num":80,"category":"Shang","question":"Shaft tombs cut with four ramps running out to the cardinal points are what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recognisable, and their number identifies nine kings who ruled at Yinxu.","questions":["The cruciform tomb M1004 and the ramped M260 both lie in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held the tallest and heaviest bronze the Shang ever cast before the robbers reached it.","Elsewhere at Anyang the dead lie among the houses, so the separateness of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, north-west of the settlement, is itself a statement about who was buried there."],"answer":"Xibeigang","answerText":"Xibeigang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings identified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramps</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, to the cardinal points</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tallest bronze</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 centimetres (52 inches)</span></div>","traditional":"西北岡","hanzi":"西北冈","pinyin":"Xīběigāng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Xibeigang</b> is the royal cemetery of the late Shang, the group of largest burials at Anyang, lying north-west of the settlement and set apart from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their number identifies nine kings as having ruled at Yinxu during the late Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are shaft tombs, but cut with four ramps running out to the cardinal directions, a layout that belonged to the ritual rather than to the convenience of digging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below the body many Shang graves have a small waist pit, and it often holds a sacrificed animal, most often a dog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Elsewhere at Anyang the dead lie among and beside the houses, so the separateness of this ground is itself a statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> M1004 is the cruciform tomb long known to be royal, and at the very bottom of it lay two monumental four-legged ding, one carrying wild buffalo heads in relief and the other the heads of Sika deer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That pair is thought to have belonged to Fu Ji, the first heir of Wu Ding, who died before taking the throne, since an heir apparent had the right to a royal tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> M260, east of the cemetery near Wuguancun, is a large tomb with one long narrow southern ramp, and the tallest and heaviest bronze the Shang ever cast, 133 centimetres (52 inches) high, came out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the thousands of tombs at Anyang were looted in antiquity and the royal ones emptied over and over, so what remains in them is what the robbers left; nor did tombs on this scale begin here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Size alone does not measure the effort a Shang burial took: outside this cemetery graves of very different sizes carry comparable goods, and what the ritual turned on was the interment rather than the hole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Yinxu_-_%E6%AE%B7%E5%A2%9F_%285067245178%29.jpg/1280px-Yinxu_-_%E6%AE%B7%E5%A2%9F_%285067245178%29.jpg","title":"The plan of royal grave M1400 at Xibeigang","desc":"The outline of one of the royal tombs in the East Zone at Xibeigang, marked out in clipped hedges above the filled-in shaft at the Yinxu site park. What is traced on the ground is the rectangular shaft with a ramp running away from it.","credit":"Photograph by tak.wing, CC BY-SA 2.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A wide lawn on a hazy day, with the plan of a large rectangular pit and a long approach ramp picked out in low clipped hedges, sprinklers playing beyond it."},"why":[{"q":"Why do these tombs have four ramps?","a":"Because the cruciform layout belonged to the ritual rather than to the convenience of digging, and the cemetery's separateness from the settlement is itself a statement."},{"q":"Why is one royal tomb assigned to a man who never reigned?","a":"Because an heir apparent had the right to a royal tomb, and M1004 is thought to have belonged to Fu Ji, the first heir of Wu Ding, who died before taking the throne."},{"q":"Why does size not measure the effort a burial took?","a":"Because outside this cemetery graves of very different sizes carry comparable goods, the ritual turning on the interment rather than on the hole."}]},
{"id":"cnh-081","num":81,"category":"Shang","question":"A king might use nine ding under the Zhou rules, the second rank seven and the fourth one, so a grave's set of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be read as a statement of standing.","questions":["The names and uses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> come from the received textual tradition rather than from residue analysis, which is why a steamer known for grain can turn up holding skulls.","Wine vessels grew fewer in graves and food vessels more common after the Zhou conquest, one sign that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had stopped being only a way of speaking to the ancestors."],"answer":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerText":"Chinese ritual bronzes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang and Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King's ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9, then 7, 5, 3 or 1 below</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Basic set</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 pieces, a gu and a jue</span></div>","traditional":"青銅禮器","hanzi":"青铜礼器","pinyin":"qīngtóng lǐqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chinese ritual bronzes</b> are cast vessels made for the offerings owed to the dead, used in banqueting, in presenting food and drink to the ancestors, and in signalling the standing of whoever owned them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each shape carries a name of its own: the ding a cooking tripod, the gui a grain server, the hu a liquor container, the jian a water container, the yan a steamer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those functions come from the received textual tradition rather than from residue or wear analysis of the vessels themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mostly the two agree, but not always, since grain is the likely thing steamed in a yan and human skulls have been found in yan in three burials at Yinxu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smallest set of all is two pieces, a gu beaker and a jue pitcher, and a grave holding even that was not a commoner’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Shang society can be ranked from its bronze assemblages as readily as from the size and shape of its graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Zhou the rule was written down: a king was entitled to nine ding, the second rank seven, the third five and the fourth three or one, with grain vessels, liquor and water containers and bells specified by number to match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Burial finds bear it out, one tomb in Gansu of the 11th or 10th century BCE holding 7 ding, 1 yan, 2 zun, 1 zhi, 1 jiao, 1 jia, 1 he, 2 you and 3 gui.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The conquest changed what they were for as well as who might have them, wine vessels growing fewer in graves and food vessels of the xu, gou and gui types more common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stopped being uniquely a means of speaking to the ancestors and became a way of recording an event or an office, many carrying an inscription that ties the vessel to one person or lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/20260520_Ding_Set_and_Gui_Set_from_Guo_State_Tombs.jpg/1280px-20260520_Ding_Set_and_Gui_Set_from_Guo_State_Tombs.jpg","title":"Five ding and four gui from the Guo state tombs","desc":"A graded set of five tripod cauldrons above four lidded grain vessels, Western Zhou, from the Guo state tombs and held by the Pingdingshan Museum. A set of this size is one grade of the ranked allowance, and the diagram on the wall behind sets out the ranks it belongs to.","credit":"Photograph by the Wikimedia Commons user Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0.","alt":"A museum case with five bronze tripod cauldrons on an upper shelf, each a little smaller than the one before, and four lidded round bronze bowls with loop handles on the shelf below."},"why":[{"q":"Why are the vessels' functions not established from the vessels?","a":"Because they come from the received textual tradition rather than from residue or wear analysis, and the two do not always agree — human skulls have been found in steamers at Yinxu."},{"q":"Why can Shang society be ranked from its bronzes?","a":"Because the assemblages grade as clearly as the size and shape of the graves, and even the smallest set, a beaker and a pitcher, was not a commoner's."},{"q":"Why did the conquest change what the vessels were for?","a":"Because they stopped being uniquely a means of speaking to the ancestors and became a way of recording an event or an office, many carrying an inscription tying them to one person or lineage."}]},
{"id":"cnh-082","num":82,"category":"Shang","question":"One large four-legged ding from Erligang took 19 mould pieces and 5 cores and six separate pours, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the scale the Shang worked it.","questions":["A clay model within, clay moulds without, and the molten alloy poured into the space between the two is the whole of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the method China's ritual bronzes were made by.","Hollow-legged pots shaped over inner moulds may be the ancestor of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the potter's technique standing behind the one that made the bronzes."],"answer":"piece-mould casting","answerText":"piece-mould casting","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Erligang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 mould pieces, 5 cores</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pours</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6, in 3 stages</span></div>","traditional":"塊範法","hanzi":"块范法","pinyin":"kuàifàn fǎ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Piece-mould casting</b> is how China’s ritual bronzes were made, casting being the shaping of metal while it is molten by pouring it into a mould, where forging beats it into shape cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Here a clay model sits within and clay moulds without, and the molten alloy of copper with tin and lead is poured into the space between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ancestor is a potter’s trick: hollow-legged vessels were shaped over inner moulds, the three legs made singly and joined, or the clay coiled around three leg moulds at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bronze form of it, inner model parts inside outer piece moulds, was first established at Erligang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Metal spacers held model and mould apart while the bronze went in, and their common use in the south suggests that particular trick was worked out there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A single large four-legged ding from Erligang took 19 mould pieces and 5 cores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those were a two-piece bottom mould round a cross-shaped core, five moulds and one core for the four walls, and twelve moulds and four cores for the legs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The metal went in six times over, in three stages: the bottom first, then the walls and handles joined to it, then the feet cast onto the base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far the moulds were reused is not agreed, one account having decorated mould sections used again and again across many vessel types, another that each vessel had its own mould and never the same one twice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the second reading no two are exactly alike in size or weight, and either way the shapes followed what the method allowed, with lids, handles and ornament cast on to a vessel already made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["practice","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Shang_Copper_Ingot%2C_Bronze_Casting_Implements%2C_%26_Clay_Mould_Fragments_%2845148962364%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Copper_Ingot%2C_Bronze_Casting_Implements%2C_%26_Clay_Mould_Fragments_%2845148962364%29.jpg","title":"Clay moulds, an ingot and the tools for cutting them","desc":"Fragments of clay mould, one with its ornament cut into the inner face and another shaped as an animal head, beside a copper ingot and the bronze knife and fine needles used to carve the moulds. On-site museum of the Yin Ruins at Anyang; the ingot came from Xiaotun South.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A museum case holding several grey clay mould fragments, one carved with a scrolled design on its inner face and another shaped as an animal head, beside a lumpy green-crusted copper ingot, a long bronze knife and four fine bronze needles."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the method begin with a potter's trick?","a":"Because hollow-legged vessels were already shaped over inner moulds, the legs made singly and joined or the clay coiled around three leg moulds at once."},{"q":"Why did one large cauldron need 19 mould pieces and 5 cores?","a":"Because it was cast in three stages, the bottom first, then the walls and handles joined to it, then the feet cast onto the base, with the metal poured six times over."},{"q":"Why does the reuse of moulds matter to how the vessels are read?","a":"Because one account has decorated sections used again across many vessel types, and on the other each vessel had its own mould, so that no two are exactly alike in size or weight."}]},
{"id":"cnh-083","num":83,"category":"Shang","question":"In the oracle bones the graph for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> works as a verb of sacrifice, the king its subject, a dead king or queen its indirect object and the offering its direct one.","questions":["Among Shang bronzes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one vessel used specifically to prepare and offer meat to the dead, and burnt animal bone has been found inside examples taken from burials.","The <i>Mozi</i> has a Xia king cast the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Kunwu, and its oracle promises that the nine will pass from Xia to Yin and from Yin to Zhou."],"answer":"ding","answerText":"ding","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Small fangding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22.9 centimetres (9 inches)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nine ding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">of the myth, passing 3 kingdoms</span></div>","traditional":"鼎","hanzi":"鼎","pinyin":"dǐng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>ding</b> is the Chinese ritual cauldron, standing on three legs or four, and among Shang bronzes the one vessel used specifically to prepare and offer meat to the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What most vessel names mean is taken from later texts rather than from residue analysis, but the ding is one place the two meet, burnt animal bone having been found inside examples taken from burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even so, bones in a ding are not proof of a feast, and one study of two Anyang cemeteries will not read the food and drink vessels that way without more work, since they may be provisions for the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the oracle-bone inscriptions the graph works as a verb of sacrifice, the king usually its subject, a dead king or queen its indirect object and the offering itself its direct one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One divination asks whether meat cut by the yue axe should be offered in the ding to Tang, the dynasty’s founder, and whether danger will follow; another whether thirty oxen prepared in a set way should go to Father Ding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They range enormously in size, from a four-legged example under 23 centimetres (9 inches) high to the largest bronze the Shang ever cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four-legged ding were far rarer than three-legged ones and belonged strictly to the ruling class and its heirs, though one might be bequeathed to a queen mother after her death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That scarcity is the stratification of Shang bronze users cast in metal, and the metal followed rank as well, the finest work using very pure copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Out of all this grew the myth that made the ding the token of a dynasty’s right to rule, the <i>Mozi</i> having a Xia king send a man to dig ore in the mountains and rivers and cast the vessels at Kunwu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its oracle promised ding that would boil without kindling and move without being carried, and said the nine would pass through three kingdoms, Xia losing them to the Yin and the Yin to the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 54, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/HouMuWuDingFullView.jpg/1280px-HouMuWuDingFullView.jpg","title":"The Hou Mu Wu ding","desc":"The largest surviving Shang bronze, a four-legged ding 133 centimetres high, cast in the late Shang and recovered from the royal cemetery at Anyang. A mobile phone left on the plinth gives the scale.","credit":"Photograph by the Wikimedia Commons user Mlogic, CC BY-SA 3.0.","alt":"A massive rectangular bronze cauldron on four thick cylindrical legs, with two upright loop handles on its rim, its sides covered in bands of dense scroll ornament and animal masks, standing on a low museum plinth."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this one of the few vessels whose use can be tested?","a":"Because burnt animal bone has been found inside examples taken from burials, where most vessel names rest on later texts rather than on residue."},{"q":"Why does bone inside one not prove a feast?","a":"Because the contents may be provisions for the dead, and one study of two Anyang cemeteries will not read the food and drink vessels as feasting gear without more work."},{"q":"Why were four-legged ones so much rarer than three-legged?","a":"Because they belonged strictly to the ruling class and its heirs, which is the stratification of Shang bronze users cast in metal."}]},
{"id":"cnh-084","num":84,"category":"Shang","question":"A face seen from the front resolving into an animal in profile on either side is the split image characteristic of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose commonest motif is the taotie.","questions":["The old reading of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as pictures of the animals given in sacrifice fails on the list, since buffalo, tigers, owls and elephants were almost never offered.","Cut into the mould rather than worked on the finished metal, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has an ancestry in Neolithic jades already shaped like birds, turtles, cicadas and silkworms."],"answer":"Shang bronze decoration","answerText":"Shang bronze decoration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cast from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Split image</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 face, 2 profiles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named beasts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6, besides dragons</span></div>","traditional":"商代青銅器紋飾","hanzi":"商代青铜器纹饰","pinyin":"Shāngdài qīngtóngqì wénshì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang bronze decoration</b> is the dense animal ornament cast into the surface of ritual vessels, wild and uncanny creatures covering the feasting bronzes of the Anyang elite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its most prominent motif is the taotie, a face of beasts with open fanged mouths whose horns nevertheless mark them as plant-eaters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it is set as a split image, so that a face seen from the front resolves into an animal in profile on either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That doubling has been explained as a way of showing a solid creature on a flat surface, as a reflection of dualism in Shang thought, and as decoration whose illusion says how important its owner was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ancestry is older than bronze, since Neolithic jades were already shaped like or decorated with birds, turtles, cicadas and silkworms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The ornament was cut into the mould rather than worked on the finished metal, and on the pair of monumental four-legged cauldrons from one royal tomb the heads of wild deer and wild buffalo stand out in relief with whole animals engraved inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not everything cast on a vessel is ornament: the emblems long read as the names of lineages, and lately as the names of offices, are writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor was the work generic, since the founders knew whom each vessel was being made for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the creatures mean is disputed, and the old answer that they show the animals given in sacrifice does not survive the list, because almost none of them was ever offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What buffalo, tigers, horned owls, elephants, deer and big-horned sheep have in common is wildness, and one vessel makes the point exactly, a split buffalo named by its backward horns and by the graph for buffalo cast inside, whose fanged mouth belongs to no buffalo at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["art","object","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/%E5%95%86%E4%BB%A3%E6%99%9A%E6%9C%9F%E9%A5%95%E9%A4%AE%E7%BA%B9%E6%96%9D2.jpg/1280px-%E5%95%86%E4%BB%A3%E6%99%9A%E6%9C%9F%E9%A5%95%E9%A4%AE%E7%BA%B9%E6%96%9D2.jpg","title":"A late Shang jia and its taotie bands","desc":"A bronze jia of the late Shang in the National Museum of China. Two registers of ornament run round the body, each mask divided down the middle by a vertical flange, which is the split composition the taotie is built on.","credit":"Photograph by the Wikimedia Commons user 三猎, CC BY-SA 4.0.","alt":"A bronze vessel on three long blade-like legs with a wide mouth, a loop handle and two capped posts rising from the rim, its body wrapped in two bands of dense scrolled ornament divided by raised vertical ribs."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the old explanation of the animals fail?","a":"Because the answer that they show the beasts given in sacrifice does not survive the list, almost none of them having ever been offered."},{"q":"Why is the split image read three different ways?","a":"Because it has been explained as a way of showing a solid creature on a flat surface, as a reflection of dualism in Shang thought, and as an illusion saying how important the owner was."},{"q":"Why is not everything cast on a vessel decoration?","a":"Because the emblems long read as lineage names, and lately as the names of offices, are writing rather than ornament."}]},
{"id":"cnh-085","num":85,"category":"Shang","question":"Blades called zhang, taken to be sceptres, and ge and yue axes are the commonest forms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and nothing shows any of the weapons was ever used.","questions":["Xiaotun lies near no source of the stone, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was cut from material carried in from Xinjiang, southern Henan or Liaoning, and some arrived as tribute.","Very little <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the graves of ordinary people at Anyang, though more than 2,000 pieces are known from the capital, most of them weapons and sceptres."],"answer":"Shang jade","answerText":"Shang jade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Worked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yinxu jades</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 2,000 known</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One workshop</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600+ unfinished, 260 grinders</span></div>","traditional":"商代玉器","hanzi":"商代玉器","pinyin":"Shāngdài yùqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang jade</b> is the worked stone of the Shang, made into weapons and sceptres far more than into vessels, and some of it inscribed with clan insignia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Blades called zhang, taken to be sceptres, along with ge and yue axes, are the commonest forms, and the same shapes turn up later at Sanxingdui far to the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing shows the weapons were ever used, and what marks them as ceremonial is the decoration: birds, figures of men with animals, and most often the mask with its pair of bulging eyes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ring-shaped jades and ring dragons carried on from the Neolithic, now cut flat with fine incisions rather than modelled in the round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than 2,000 jade objects had been dug at Yinxu by the mid-1980s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The work was done on the spot, two houses inside the palace and temple complex holding over 600 unfinished stone objects, many of them zhang blades, with 260 grinding stones beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xiaotun lies near no source of the stone, so it came in from a distance, Hetian in Xinjiang, Nanyang in southern Henan and Xiuyan in Liaoning all being proposed; five jade ge daggers reached the court as tribute from one polity alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who did the cutting is unsettled, the older view making the Shang jade workers slaves, while the workshop’s place inside the palace precinct suggests elite hands at the finishing and artisans elsewhere in the ancient world often held rank of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Very little of it reached ordinary graves, since in the Anyang cemeteries away from the royal ground the proportion of jade in a burial is extremely low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Workshops of this kind are commoner than the literature suggests, being dug and published far less often than elite burials, and by the late Shang jade had in any case lost its place as the foremost prestige material to bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 76, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Shang_Jade_Ge_Dagger-Axe%2C_Yinxu.jpg/1280px-Shang_Jade_Ge_Dagger-Axe%2C_Yinxu.jpg","title":"A jade ge from the Yin Ruins","desc":"A dagger-axe blade of pale jade from Anyang, one of the commonest Shang jade forms. The drilled hole is for lashing the blade to a shaft, and an excavation number has been inked on beside it.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A long flat blade of creamy, faintly veined jade lying on a red mount, tapering to a rounded point at one end and squared off at the other, with a small drilled hole near the squared end."},"why":[{"q":"Why are these weapons called ceremonial?","a":"Because nothing shows they were ever used, and what marks them out is the decoration — birds, figures of men with animals, and most often the mask with its bulging eyes."},{"q":"Why must the stone have come from a distance?","a":"Because Xiaotun lies near no source of it, and Hetian in Xinjiang, Nanyang in southern Henan and Xiuyan in Liaoning have all been proposed."},{"q":"Why is who did the cutting unsettled?","a":"Because the older view made the workers slaves, while the workshop's place inside the palace precinct suggests elite hands at the finishing."}]},
{"id":"cnh-086","num":86,"category":"Shang","question":"Dogs, nets, fire and beaters made the Shang hunt a large-scale event closely paralleling war, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what the elite rode in it.","questions":["Large tombs at Anyang are frequently accompanied by pits holding the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where more than four in five graves at the site are small pits with a coffin and a few pots.","That the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is new at Anyang rests on present evidence, and Zhengzhou can hardly be dug at all with nearly ten million people living on top of it."],"answer":"Shang chariot","answerText":"Shang chariot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One Zhou pit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 chariots, 56+ horses</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At the conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 war chariots</span></div>","traditional":"商代戰車","hanzi":"商代战车","pinyin":"Shāngdài zhànchē","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang chariot</b> is the light horse-drawn vehicle that appears at Anyang and at no earlier Chinese centre on present evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it came from is argued, a western origin having long been proposed and defended by Chinese scholars as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of three things the last capital can show that Erlitou and Zhengzhou cannot, the others being the royal hunt and writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> But that rests on present evidence, and the earlier centres are barely dug, since systematic excavation at Zhengzhou is out of the question with nearly ten million people living there now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the elite are found riding is the hunt, a large-scale event closely paralleling warfare and run with dogs, nets, fire and beaters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The bones from the palace and temple area bear that out, being full of wild and exotic animals matching the game the oracle bones record the king killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vehicle went into the ground with its owner, and the large tombs at Anyang are frequently accompanied by chariot pits with ramps and rich furnishings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing of the kind attends the ordinary dead, more than four in five burials at the site being small pits with a coffin, a few pots and perhaps a cowry shell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The custom outlasted the dynasty and grew: two pits beside one Western Zhou marquis held over 56 horses and 19 chariots, and another in the north-west at least 29 horses and three wooden vehicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The animals chosen there were young adults of four to twelve years, of which 21 of the 22 that could be sexed were male, and by the conquest the chariot counted in the field too, the Zhou king being remembered as leading three hundred of them against the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Zexian Huang, Qiang Ma, Chengrui Zhang, Ruoxin Cheng, Furen Hou, Yi Wu, Feng Luo, and Yue Li, “Early Evidence of Post-Mortem Fetal Extrusion in Equids: A Case from the Western Zhou Period (1045–771 BCE) Site of Yaoheyuan in Northwestern China,” <i>Animals</i> 14, no. 14 (2024): 2106, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11273478/. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Shang_Chariot_Sacrificial_Pit_05.jpg/1280px-Shang_Chariot_Sacrificial_Pit_05.jpg","title":"A chariot pit at the Yin Ruins","desc":"One of the chariot burials at Anyang, the two spoked wheels standing where they settled in the earth with the axle, hub and draught pole between them. The vehicle is the excavated one; the skeletons laid in the pit are replacements.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"An excavated rectangular pit with earth walls, a large spoked wheel standing at either side with its spokes preserved as ridges in the soil, and the pole and axle of a chariot lying between them among scattered bones."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the absence of chariots at earlier centres a weak argument?","a":"Because it rests on present evidence and those centres are barely dug, systematic excavation at Zhengzhou being out of the question with nearly ten million people living there."},{"q":"Why does the hunt read as a rehearsal for war?","a":"Because it was a large-scale event closely paralleling warfare, run with dogs, nets, fire and beaters, and the palace bones are full of the game the inscriptions record the king killing."},{"q":"Why is a chariot pit a mark of rank?","a":"Because the large tombs are frequently accompanied by one with ramps and rich furnishings, while more than four in five burials hold a coffin, a few pots and perhaps a cowry shell."}]},
{"id":"cnh-087","num":87,"category":"Shang","question":"The oracle bones call an enemy polity fang and drop the word when it turns ally, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is fought against a label that shifts rather than a fixed set of peoples.","questions":["Most campaigns of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put 3,000 to 5,000 men in the field, but one against the Qiang, whose country lay close by, deployed 13,000.","Noblemen sent out to levy troops from friendly cities, and waystations built along the routes they travelled, are how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was supplied with men."],"answer":"Shang warfare","answerText":"Shang warfare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaigns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vs the Qiang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,000 men in one campaign</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Other fang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3,000 – 5,000 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One royal tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">941 weapons</span></div>","traditional":"商代戰爭","hanzi":"商代战争","pinyin":"Shāngdài zhànzhēng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang warfare</b> is the campaigning of the Shang king and his allies against the polities the inscriptions call fang, the word for a people beyond Shang control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a relational label rather than an ethnic one, since a group counted as fang while it was an enemy and lost the word once it became an ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What those polities were like is not recoverable from the inscriptions, which say nothing of their social or political organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wars were fought for hegemony and for control of natural resources, and the ability to beat Qiang armies and offer Qiang captives was itself a demonstration of the king’s power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Force was raised rather than kept, noblemen being sent out to inspect and levy troops from friendly yi cities across the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A king and his officials were constantly on the road, to hunt, to raise troops, to drill them, to inspect the crops, to collect tribute, to attack a neighbour, to trade or to settle new ground, camping at waystations built along the routes they used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scale varied with the enemy: against most fang the Shang put 3,000 to 5,000 men in the field, but against the Qiang, whose country lay close by, one campaign deployed 13,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On one reading of the court’s office names the military branch was divided into minute duties, which is as near as the record comes to an order of battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the fighting was equipped with shows in one royal tomb alone, which held 941 weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It ended with the Zhou, who came against the last Shang king with three hundred war chariots and three thousand picked warriors besides their allies’ troops, and whose alliance included the Qiang on whom the Shang had spent so much force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","warfare","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Gideon Shelach, “The Qiang and the Question of Human Sacrifice in the Late Shang Period,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 35, no. 1 (1996), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17070. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Shang_Bronze_Ge_Dagger-Axes%2C_Yinxu.jpg/1280px-Shang_Bronze_Ge_Dagger-Axes%2C_Yinxu.jpg","title":"Bronze dagger-axes from the Yin Ruins","desc":"Ge dagger-axe blades from Anyang, mounted for display at the on-site museum of the Yin Ruins, with three more weapons laid on the shelf beneath them. Several keep the squared tang and the slots by which the blade was lashed to its shaft.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A lit museum case with six green-patinated bronze blades mounted on a pale panel, each a long leaf shape with a squared tang at one end, and three more weapons lying on the shelf below."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the word for these enemies not an ethnic label?","a":"Because a group counted as fang while it was an enemy and lost the word once it became an ally, so the term is relational."},{"q":"Why did one enemy draw far larger armies than the rest?","a":"Because the Qiang country lay close by, so where most campaigns put 3,000 to 5,000 men in the field, one against them deployed 13,000."},{"q":"Why is the dynasty's end an irony of its own warfare?","a":"Because the Zhou alliance that overthrew it included the Qiang, on whom the Shang had spent so much force."}]},
{"id":"cnh-088","num":88,"category":"Shang","question":"Which excavated site answers to which name in the transmitted record is the standing problem of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with Zhengzhou argued to be both the first seat and the second.","questions":["One account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has the Three Dynasties chasing new sources of metal, another has population expansion and fortified outposts planted in the periphery.","The layers at Yinxu for Pan Geng and the two kings after him are meagre and low in status, which is why one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is doubted as his."],"answer":"Shang capitals","answerText":"Shang capitals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Seats</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yanshi and Zhengzhou, 2 walled cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last seat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Yinxu, c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商代都城","hanzi":"商代都城","pinyin":"Shāngdài dūchéng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang capitals</b> are the successive seats of the Shang court, semipermanent cities that served as centres of political, military and religious power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court moved more than once and the sites are known: Yanshi and Zhengzhou in the early period, a walled town at Huanbei in the middle, and Yinxu at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is not known is which of them answers to which name in the transmitted record, since the later texts give the seats names the excavators cannot attach with confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhengzhou has been argued to be both the first Shang capital and the second, called Bo and Ao in later literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor is it clear that Yinxu is the Yin to which Pan Geng is said to have moved, since the layers there for his reign and the two after it are meagre, small in scale and low in status, no parallel to a royal capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Why the court moved at all is argued, and one popular answer is that the Three Dynasties were chasing new sources of metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The copper was certainly far off, in the Zhongtiao Mountains and along the middle and lower reaches of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That reading has been set aside for another, in which the strategy was population expansion and the planting of fortified outposts in the periphery, practised from Erlitou to the end of Erligang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third account makes moving the capital an instrument of policy in itself, first plainly visible under Pan Geng, with the earlier moves the experiments that taught it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What all of it rests on is the divination records, so the Shang political map has to be reconstructed from oracle-bone inscriptions rather than read off the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Liye Xie, Zahid Daudjee, Chun Fu Liu, and Pauline Sebillaud, “Settlement Relocation, Urban Construction, and Social Transformation in China’s Central Plain, 2300–1500 B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 2 (2020): 292–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104160. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Shang_Dynastic_Capitals_as_Recorded_in_Historical_Documents_%2810197655116%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Dynastic_Capitals_as_Recorded_in_Historical_Documents_%2810197655116%29.jpg","title":"The capital moves, as three texts give them","desc":"A panel at the Yin Ruins museum setting three early Chinese records side by side against six Shang kings. The three do not agree: where one puts Zu Yi's seat at pi, the others have geng and xing, and Pan Geng's is yin in two of them and bo in the third.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A museum wall panel headed “The Relocation of Shang Dynastic Capitals as Recorded in Historical Documents”, carrying a table of three rows, one for each text, and six columns, one for each king, filled with short romanised place names."},"why":[{"q":"Why can the excavated seats not be matched to the recorded names?","a":"Because the later texts give the capitals names the excavators cannot attach with confidence, and Zhengzhou has been argued to be both the first and the second."},{"q":"Why is it doubted that Yinxu is the city one king is said to have moved to?","a":"Because its layers for his reign and the two after it are meagre, small in scale and low in status, no parallel to a royal capital."},{"q":"Why do the explanations of the moves differ so widely?","a":"Because one makes the court chase new sources of metal, another makes it plant fortified outposts in the periphery, and a third makes moving the capital an instrument of policy in itself."}]},
{"id":"cnh-089","num":89,"category":"Shang","question":"Two rings of rammed earth enclose the 24 square kilometres of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose inner city held a few dozen foundations taken for palaces and temples.","questions":["The only hard evidence for casting ritual vessels by the piece-mould method in its period comes from a foundry in the outer city of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the periphery cast only tools.","Monumental four-legged ding were found in three caches at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, laid side by side in adapted wells and storage pits with their mouths up and their feet down."],"answer":"Zhengzhou Shang City","answerText":"Zhengzhou Shang City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walled area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 24 square kilometres</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foundations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 – 2,000 square metres</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caches</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, of monumental ding</span></div>","traditional":"鄭州商城","hanzi":"郑州商城","pinyin":"Zhèngzhōu Shāngchéng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zhengzhou Shang City</b> is the early Shang capital in central Henan, identified with one of the two seats the later texts call Bo and Ao, and one of the largest places built anywhere in Bronze Age China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It covered about 24 square kilometres (9 square miles), ringed by two circuits of rammed earth that made an inner city and an outer one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The royal elite lived inside the inner ring, where a few dozen rammed-earth foundations taken for palaces and temples run from 100 to 2,000 square metres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two large bronze foundries lie in the outer city, one of them at Nanguanwai about 700 metres south of the inner wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across some 0.8 hectares it has given up crucibles, slag, clay moulds, casting waste, copper ore and the remains of smelting furnaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its moulds were cut for tools, weapons and ritual vessels alike, and the shapes and ornament of early Shang bronzes found across the region match them, which makes this the place the vessels were made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only site anywhere with hard evidence for casting ritual vessels by the piece-mould method in its period, while the metallurgy sites out in the periphery cast only weapons and tools from stone moulds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Casting was held at the centre, then, but mining was not, since there is no evidence of direct control over the copper regions and the strategy looks instead like securing the networks along which metal moved, with outposts planted near the ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three caches at the site held monumental four-legged ding, buried in adapted wells, storage pits and refuse pits, the vessels set side by side with their mouths up and their feet down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They come in pairs or graduated sets rather than singly, every one larger than anything else found there, and how much more lies below is not knowable, since nearly ten million people live on top of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/20240817_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site_02.jpg/1280px-20240817_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site_02.jpg","title":"The inner wall, still standing in the middle of a city","desc":"A surviving stretch of the rammed-earth circuit that enclosed the inner city, its horizontal building courses still visible in the eroded face. Tower blocks stand a few hundred metres behind it: the modern city sits directly on top of the Shang one, which is why so much of the site can only be dug in the gaps between buildings.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A long earthen bank perhaps ten metres high, grassed along its top and bare tan soil on its steep face, running behind a wide paved plaza; a black stone marker stands in the foreground and white apartment towers rise beyond the bank."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this the place the early ritual vessels were made?","a":"Because its foundry moulds were cut for vessels whose shapes and ornament match early Shang bronzes found across the region, and it is the only site with hard evidence for piece-mould casting of them in its period."},{"q":"Why was casting central when mining was not?","a":"Because there is no evidence of direct control over the copper regions, and the strategy looks instead like securing the networks along which metal moved, with outposts planted near the ore."},{"q":"Why can the size of the deposit never be known?","a":"Because nearly ten million people live on top of the site today, so how much more lies below the modern city is not knowable."}]},
{"id":"cnh-090","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"A wall of only 7.5 hectares at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Hubei encloses the clearest case of the early Shang reaching far beyond the Central Plain.","questions":["Bronze vessels resembling those of Zhengzhou were buried in the Lijiazui tombs at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Hubei outpost that cast weapons and tools but no ritual vessels of its own.","The Tonglüshan copper mines lie close to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose bronzes carry the same highly radiogenic lead found at Zhengzhou, Xin’gan and Sanxingdui."],"answer":"Panlongcheng","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Erligang period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walled area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7.5 hectares (19 acres)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Whole site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">64 – 110 hectares, disputed</span></div>","traditional":"盤龍城","hanzi":"盘龙城","pinyin":"Pánlóngchéng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Pánlóngchéng</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Panlongcheng</b> is a walled Bronze Age settlement in Hubei, in the middle Yangtze valley, and the clearest case of the early Shang reaching far beyond the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands close to abundant copper deposits, and has produced evidence of bronze making from the Erlitou period, before that expansion reached it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wall encloses only 7.5 hectares (19 acres) and the settlement around it is many times that, though by how much is argued: the excavation report gives a near-square of 1,000 by 1,100 metres and so 110 hectares, but that takes in ravine and lake, and 64 hectares is the better figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Erlitou material had already spread here, and in Hubei it sat alongside indigenous cultural components rather than coming to dominate the assemblage as it did in southern Shanxi and southern Shaanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mine remains of Erlitou and Erligang date are known from several sites in the Yangxin region nearby, and the Tonglüshan mines at Daye lie close by as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Elite burials of Erligang date at Lijiazui have yielded bronze vessels that resemble those found at Zhengzhou, which suggests the vessels were made at the primary centre and sent out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Panlongcheng itself shows no sign of casting ritual vessels: like the other metallurgy sites of the periphery it worked bronze, but its products were weapons and tools cast from stone moulds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two Lijiazui tombs are ranked with the aristocrats and lesser military leaders, and with retainers and related elite, rather than at the royal grades known from Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mining and metallurgy look instead to have been organised locally, and outposts like this one are read as a strategy of resource absorption: securing the networks along which metal moved rather than controlling the digging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its bronzes carry highly radiogenic lead, a rare signature that turns up at Zhengzhou, Xin’gan and Sanxingdui at virtually the same time, and after thirty years of argument its source may be several sources rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Panlongcheng","sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson, and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond Ritual Bronzes: Identifying Multiple Sources of Highly Radiogenic Lead across Chinese History,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/20231126_Palace_in_Panlongcheng_%28114140%29.jpg/1280px-20231126_Palace_in_Panlongcheng_%28114140%29.jpg","title":"Inside the wall, a palace foundation","desc":"One of the excavated foundations within the walled area, laid out for visitors in the archaeological park. The low banks follow the lines of the wall trenches and the timber stumps stand where the posts did; the earth platform beneath them is the ancient part.","credit":"Photograph by Yumeto, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A rectangular earth platform in mown parkland, its rooms marked out by low walls of packed earth about knee height, with rows of short timber posts set at intervals; a rope fence and a boardwalk run across the foreground."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this outpost read as securing networks rather than mines?","a":"Because it shows no sign of casting ritual vessels, working only weapons and tools from stone moulds, while mining and metallurgy look to have been organised locally."},{"q":"Why are its finest bronzes thought to have been made elsewhere?","a":"Because the vessels from its elite burials resemble those found at Zhengzhou, which suggests they were cast at the primary centre and sent out."},{"q":"Why is the size of the settlement argued over?","a":"Because the excavation report's near-square of 1,000 by 1,100 metres takes in ravine and lake, so 64 hectares is the better figure than 110."}]},
{"id":"cnh-091","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Sixty grains of wheat or more turn up at most Shang sites, but the millet of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went on feeding almost everyone until the Zhou.","questions":["Isotopes from Anyang put dogs, pigs, rats and sacrificial victims on the same mostly millet diet that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced, poor in meat beside the people who were buried rather than offered.","Cooler, drier weather late in the dynasty drove the deforestation and multicropping of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, marked by a sharp rise in the number of stone knives and sickles."],"answer":"Shang agriculture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 millets, dry-farmed on loess</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60+ grains at most Shang sites</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enamel defects</span><span class=\"dt-v\">80.9% of Yinxu commoners</span></div>","traditional":"商代農業","hanzi":"商代农业","pinyin":"Shāngdài nóngyè","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang agriculture</b> was the dry farming of millet on the loess plains of northern China, the base on which everything else about the dynasty rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two millets had been the region’s crops since the Neolithic, and the divide between the millet-eating north and the rice-and-broad-spectrum south shows in the carbon isotopes of human bone from 6000 BCE and grew sharper after 5000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wheat and barley reached China from south-west Asia after 2000 BCE, and Shang sites are the first in central China to yield them in quantity, sixty grains or more from most of those examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They still fed almost nobody: wheat’s part in subsistence stayed minimal until the Zhou, after about 1000 BCE, when it began to climb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One explanation for that slowness is the kitchen, since wheat and barley fold into a tradition of grinding and baking more readily than into the steaming and boiling of the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Millet was drunk as well as eaten, and the ale brewed from it went to the ancestors by the hundred jars in a single royal offering, so the crop fed the dead as well as the living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotopes from Anyang put dogs, pigs, rats and human sacrificial victims on much the same mostly millet diet, poor in meat beside the people who were buried rather than offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cattle had arrived from the west in the 3rd millennium BCE and by the late Shang were being supplied to the capital in enormous numbers, foddered in part on millet by-products, having displaced pigs as the commonest sacrificial animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By then the climate had turned cooler and drier, and the answer to it was deforestation and multicropping, marked archaeologically by a sharp rise in the number of stone knives and sickles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people who did that work paid for it: among the commoners of Yinxu 80.9 per cent carry the enamel defects of childhood stress, 30.3 per cent cribra orbitalia, and 40.0 per cent of adults the bone marks of infection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang agriculture","sources":["Hua Zhang et al., “Osteoarchaeological Studies of Human Systemic Stress of Early Urbanization in Late Shang at Anyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0151854, “Archaeological Context” and “Results,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0151854. [Open access]","Xinyi Liu and Rachel E. B. Reid, “The Prehistoric Roots of Chinese Cuisines: Mapping Staple Food Systems of China, 6000 BC–220 AD,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0240930, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0240930. [Open access]","Zhenhua Deng et al., “Assessing the Occurrence and Status of Wheat in Late Neolithic Central China: The Importance of Direct AMS Radiocarbon Dates from Xiazhai,” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 29, no. 1 (2020): 61–73, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00334-019-00732-7. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe and Roderick Campbell, “To Eat or Not to Eat? Animals and Categorical Fluidity in Shang Society,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 1 (2021): 158–77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108215. [Open access]","Lu Peng, Katherine Brunson, Yuan Jing, and Li Zhipeng, “Zooarchaeological and Genetic Evidence for the Origins of Domestic Cattle in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 56, no. 1 (2017): 92–120, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/64744. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","agriculture","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/20240817_Stone_Sickles%2C_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site%2C_Shang.jpg/1280px-20240817_Stone_Sickles%2C_Zhengzhou_Shang_City_Site%2C_Shang.jpg","title":"Sickle blades from Zhengzhou Shang City","desc":"Eight stone sickle blades, the commonest farming tool the period leaves behind and the one whose numbers rise late in the dynasty. The blade at the right is set on a clear modern rod to show how it was hafted; the handle is the museum's, not the Shang's.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Eight curved blades of dark grey and grey-green stone, each roughly the length of a hand and worked to a long cutting edge, mounted in two columns on a pale museum wall; a ninth is fixed at an angle to a clear rod that stands in for a wooden handle."},"why":[{"q":"Why did wheat feed almost nobody for a thousand years after it arrived?","a":"Because its part in subsistence stayed minimal until the Zhou, one explanation being that wheat and barley fold into grinding and baking more readily than into the steaming and boiling of the north."},{"q":"Why did the crop feed the dead as well as the living?","a":"Because millet was drunk as well as eaten, and the ale brewed from it went to the ancestors by the hundred jars in a single royal offering."},{"q":"Why is the cost of that farming visible in the bones?","a":"Because among the commoners of Yinxu 80.9 per cent carry the enamel defects of childhood stress and 40.0 per cent of adults the bone marks of infection."}]},
{"id":"cnh-092","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Guandimiao’s 23 kilns and 22 houses are almost all that is known of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the villages and lesser centres that lay outside the Shang capitals.","questions":["A village of fewer than a hundred people made pottery for a whole region and oracle bones for itself, which is most of what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has left to study.","The hairpins and arrowheads of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> match the mass-produced work of a bone workshop 200 kilometres away, though its pottery went nowhere near the capital."],"answer":"Shang countryside","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Guandimiao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22, semi-subterranean</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kilns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23, updraft</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">269, under 100 people</span></div>","traditional":"商代農村","hanzi":"商代农村","pinyin":"Shāngdài nóngcūn","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang countryside</b> is everything outside the capitals — the villages and lesser centres that fed and supplied the court — and next to none of it has been dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Anyang itself has been excavated on a great scale, with over 10,000 burials besides the royal cemetery, and residential quarters, refuse and storage pits, drains, roads and workshops with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the early Shang the pattern outside the capital was of medium-sized regional centres placed at the main water-transport nodes near metal sources, whose remains resemble Zhengzhou’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the late Shang the picture rests on one site: Guandimiao in Xingyang, Henan, found in 2006 during salvage work ahead of the south-to-north water diversion, and the only village of its time in China to be nearly completely excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was small, with 22 semi-subterranean houses and 269 tombs over perhaps 150 years, and a population that probably never passed a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It also held 23 updraft kilns, near enough one to a household, and made pottery on a scale far beyond anything a hundred people could use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those pots did not go to Anyang 200 kilometres (125 miles) away, being unlike Anyang’s own, so the likely market was the country around, which makes the rural economy far more specialised than anyone had supposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Anyang the grey ware everybody used was made locally, and thin-section work picks out only a handful of vessels as probably foreign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What did move was bone: the village’s finest hairpins and arrowheads are identical in every respect to the mass-produced work of the Tiesanlu workshop at Anyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guandimiao made oracle bones of its own as well, chiselled by a local specialist, and the polity such places composed reads less as a territory than as a network of lineages with the king at the head of the senior one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang countryside","sources":["Hou Yanfeng et al., “The Guandimiao Bone Assemblage (and What it Says about the Shang Economy),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 281–310, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72094. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George (Rip) Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 182–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]","Hua Zhang et al., “Osteoarchaeological Studies of Human Systemic Stress of Early Urbanization in Late Shang at Anyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0151854, “Archaeological Context,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0151854. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Guandimiao_Site_in_1973_by_HEXAGON_satellite.jpg/1280px-Guandimiao_Site_in_1973_by_HEXAGON_satellite.jpg","title":"The country around Guandimiao, from orbit in 1973","desc":"Field strips, gullies and clustered village houses in Xingyang, photographed by an American reconnaissance satellite in November 1973 and declassified since. Nothing Shang is visible: the village was found in 2006 only because a canal was being cut through it, which is the whole difficulty of the subject.","credit":"KH-9 HEXAGON satellite photograph by the CIA, NRO and USGS; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A grey monochrome aerial photograph of flat farmland divided into hundreds of narrow field strips, cut by branching pale gullies, with three or four dense clusters of small village buildings and a straight railway line across the top."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the picture of the late Shang countryside rest on one village?","a":"Because Guandimiao is the only settlement of its time in China to be nearly completely excavated, found during salvage work ahead of a water diversion."},{"q":"Why is its pottery evidence of a specialised rural economy?","a":"Because 23 kilns, near one to a household, made pots on a scale far beyond what a hundred people could use, and those pots are unlike Anyang's own."},{"q":"Why is bone the thing that plainly travelled?","a":"Because the village's finest hairpins and arrowheads are identical in every respect to the mass-produced work of the Tiesanlu workshop at Anyang."}]},
{"id":"cnh-093","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Ten heavenly stems paired in turn with twelve earthly branches give the sixty day-names of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had no way of numbering a year at all.","questions":["Five lunar eclipses recorded by one group of diviners are what fixes part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to between 1201 and 1181 BCE, there being no era count to read.","A ten-day week called the xun is the unit the Shang court planned in, and it is ten days long because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had ten heavenly stems."],"answer":"Shang calendar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Day cycle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60 days, 10 stems, 12 branches</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Week</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 days, the xun</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lunar eclipses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, dated 1201 – 1181 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商代曆法","hanzi":"商代历法","pinyin":"Shāngdài lìfǎ","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang calendar</b> is the reckoning of time behind the oracle-bone inscriptions, and it is a calendar of days above all, since those inscriptions carry day notations and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A day was named by pairing ten signs called the heavenly stems with twelve called the earthly branches, each taken in turn, so that the pairs run out at sixty and start again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sixty are called jiazi after the first of them, and there is no year anywhere in the count: it was not applied to numbering years until the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That the shells use the signs for days rather than years was worked out early, from the plain fact that the combinations on them exceed the sixty a year cycle allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ten stems make a ten-day week, the xun, and that is the unit the court planned in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The round of five sacrificial rites the late kings ran through those weeks took about a Julian year to complete, which is the nearest thing the Shang had to a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A calendar built this way puts its meaning in the cycle rather than in the sequence, so what a day is fit or unfit for comes round again every ten, twelve or at most sixty days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because there is no era count, a Shang date cannot simply be read off the bone and has to be recovered from astronomy instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eleven possible solar and lunar eclipses survive in the corpus, and five lunar ones from a single group of diviners are generally taken to be real observations, which calculation places between 1201 and 1181 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The day-count outlived everything around it: Chinese records of the 17th and 18th centuries CE are still dated by stems and branches, and a modern database of them has to convert every date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang calendar","sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Frank H. Chalfant, “Early Chinese Writing,” <i>Memoirs of the Carnegie Museum</i> 4, no. 1 (1906): 21, 35, https://archive.org/details/earlychinesewrit01chal. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual: A Handbook of Biographical, Historical, Mythological, and General Literary Reference</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), part 2, s.v. “The Ten Stems (Cyclical Signs),” https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Mu-chou Poo, “How to Steer through Life: Negotiating Fate in the Daybook,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 107–28, at 113–14, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Pao K. Wang et al., “Construction of the REACHES Climate Database Based on Historical Documents of China,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 5 (2018): 180288, https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2018.288. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","science","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Heji_37986_Ganzhi_table.jpg/1280px-Heji_37986_Ganzhi_table.jpg","title":"The sixty days, cut into an ox shoulder blade","desc":"A complete table of the cycle on a scapula catalogued as Heji 37986, in the hand of the Huang diviner group and so from the reigns of the last two Shang kings. Each entry is a stem above a branch, and the columns run down through all sixty before the count starts again.","credit":"Photograph in the Yinxu Museum, rotated and cropped by Kanguole; released CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A pale curved slab of ox shoulder blade lit against a grey wall, its face covered with columns of small incised two-character groups in an early angular script, each character made of straight scored strokes."},"why":[{"q":"Why can a date not be read off a bone?","a":"Because there is no era count at all, only a repeating cycle of sixty day-names, so an absolute date has to be recovered from astronomy instead."},{"q":"Why was it worked out early that the signs mark days?","a":"Because the combinations on the shells exceed the sixty that a cycle of years would allow."},{"q":"Why does such a calendar put its meaning in the cycle?","a":"Because what a day is fit or unfit for comes round again every ten, twelve or at most sixty days, rather than standing at a point in a sequence."}]},
{"id":"cnh-094","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Chinese is the world’s only primary script still in continuous use, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the ancestor of every character written today.","questions":["Bronze inscriptions written into the soft clay of a casting mould run beside the incised oracle bones, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in cast metal as well as in shell and bone.","Half a million images spanning 3,000 years show that modern characters are more complex than their ancestors in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which upends the popular story of steady simplification."],"answer":"Shang writing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE, on bone</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, oracle bone to regular</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Modern shapes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">clerical script, c. 200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Korea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mokkan, 538 – 660 CE</span></div>","traditional":"商代文字","hanzi":"商代文字","pinyin":"Shāngdài wénzì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang writing</b> is the earliest Chinese writing there is, and what makes it matter beyond its own dynasty is that the characters written today descend from it, Chinese being the world’s only primary script still in continuous use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two Shang scripts run together: the oracle-bone script incised into shell and bone, and the bronze inscriptions, which were often written into the soft clay of the mould a vessel was cast in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bronze inscriptions begin in the Shang and are coeval with the bones, then become characteristic of the Western Zhou, so the line out of the Shang runs through cast metal as much as through divination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That the bone forms belong to that line was shown almost as soon as the bones surfaced, by setting their date-marks beside the cyclical characters already known from later writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Reading the ones still undeciphered works the same way, by comparison across the stages the script passed through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those stages are conventionally counted as five — oracle bone, bronze, seal, clerical and regular — and everything before the small seal script is grouped together as ancient characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shapes a reader would recognise today appear only with the clerical script of the early Han, a thousand years after the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The script left China as well: wooden tablets from the Korean kingdom of Paekche, of the 6th and 7th centuries CE, carry both practice at individual characters and attempts at composition in Sinitic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The popular account of what happened over those three millennia is one of steady simplification, and it does not survive being measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Half a million images spanning more than 3,000 years show complexity climbing to the seal script and falling after it, with modern characters still more complex than their oracle-bone ancestors, and 400 people who could not read any of them judged the pairs the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang writing","sources":["Simon J. Han, Piers Kelly, James Winters, and Charles Kemp, “Simplification Is Not Dominant in the Evolution of Chinese Characters,” <i>Open Mind</i> 6 (2022): 264–79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9987343/. [Open access]","Mengru Wang et al., “Study on the Evolution of Chinese Characters Based on Few-Shot Learning: From Oracle Bone Inscriptions to Regular Script,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272974, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272974. [Open access]","Marjorie Burge, “Wooden Inscriptions and the Culture of Writing in Sabi Paekche,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 47–73, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76771. [Open access]","Ming Yan, Jinger Pan, and Reinhold Kliegl, “The Beijing Sentence Corpus II: A Cross-Script Comparison between Traditional and Simplified Chinese Sentence Reading,” <i>Behavior Research Methods</i> 57 (2025): 60, https://doi.org/10.3758/s13428-024-02523-z. [Open access]","Frank H. Chalfant, “Early Chinese Writing,” <i>Memoirs of the Carnegie Museum</i> 4, no. 1 (1906): 35 and plate L, https://archive.org/details/earlychinesewrit01chal. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","writing","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png/1280px-%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png","title":"One character, from the Shang to now","desc":"A family tree of the character for horse, beginning with Shang bronze and oracle-bone forms in which the mane and legs are still drawn. Six Warring States branches are crossed out as dead ends, so the descent is a surviving line rather than the only one; the diagram marks the split between ancient and modern script at the Han.","credit":"Diagram by the Wikimedia contributor Panates, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A branching chart running top to bottom. At the top are angular pictorial forms of a horse with a mane and four legs; lines lead down through progressively squarer forms, six branches ending in red crosses, to the modern printed characters at the foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the bone script tied to later Chinese writing so quickly?","a":"Because its date-marks could be set beside the cyclical characters already known from later writing, and reading the undeciphered signs still works by comparison across the script's stages."},{"q":"Why does the story of steady simplification not survive measurement?","a":"Because half a million images spanning 3,000 years show complexity climbing to the seal script and falling after it, with modern characters still more complex than their oracle-bone ancestors."},{"q":"Why does the line out of the Shang run through metal as well as bone?","a":"Because bronze inscriptions begin in the Shang alongside the oracle bones, often written into the soft clay of the mould, and then become characteristic of the Western Zhou."}]},
{"id":"cnh-095","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Most of what is known of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from a rare radiogenic lead that reached Sanxingdui, Panlongcheng and Xin’gan at the same moment as the Central Plain.","questions":["Carnelian carried more than a thousand kilometres south to Sanxingdui puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one exchange sphere reaching from Sichuan to the Mongolian Plateau.","That Anyang’s copper came from the Yangzi is unreferenced and its jade from Xinjiang unsupported, so claims about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outrun the sourcing studies."],"answer":"Shang neighbours","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Exchange sphere</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1500 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiogenic lead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 50% of the lead used</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carnelian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1,000 km to the north</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ore fields</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, Tongling and Tonglüshan</span></div>","traditional":"商代方國","hanzi":"商代方国","pinyin":"Shāngdài fāngguó","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang neighbours</b> are the peoples and polities around the Shang state, and what can be said about them comes mostly from things that moved rather than from anything the inscriptions say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clearest of those things is a rare, highly radiogenic lead that supplied over half the lead used across an area running from the Yellow River to the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It appears at almost the same moment in the bronzes of the Central Plain and in those of Sanxingdui in Sichuan, Panlongcheng in Hubei and Xin’gan in Jiangxi, and then vanishes under the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where it came from has been argued over for thirty years — south-west China, the middle Yangtze, the Qinling and Zhongtiao mountains, even Africa — and the likeliest answer now is that there was no single source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mining itself spread across the south at the end of the Erlitou period, with the ore fields at Tongling in Jiangxi and Tonglüshan in Hubei both worked by the early Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Carnelian beads buried at Sanxingdui between about 1200 and 1000 BCE were cut from stone that came from more than 1,000 kilometres (600 miles) to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beads of the same period from Gansu, Shaanxi and Beijing carry the same northern signature, which puts the Sichuan Basin, the Central Plain, the Loess Plateau and the southern Mongolian Plateau in one exchange sphere between 1500 and 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that, the long-distance claims made most often about the Shang are the ones with least behind them: that Anyang’s copper ore came from the Yangtze is unsubstantiated and unreferenced, and no sourcing study supports the jade of Anyang and Xin’gan coming from Xinjiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The one such claim with provenance work behind it is that the north’s proto-porcelain was southern, and even there the work is contested and the studies disagree about which part of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the record bears is that the Shang sat inside a very wide network whose direction of travel is mostly unproven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang neighbours","sources":["Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson, and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond Ritual Bronzes: Identifying Multiple Sources of Highly Radiogenic Lead across Chinese History,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Meiting Yan et al., “Sourcing the Origins of Carnelian in Early Chinese Civilizations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 7 (2026): e2524563123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913004/. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Haichao Li, “Hierarchical, Ethnic, and Provenance Features of Western Zhou Period Proto-Porcelain in Northern China: New Evidence of a Redistribution System during the Western Zhou Dynasty,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 417–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108233. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Tongl%C3%BCshan_Shang%26Zhou_No.2_Mining_Site.jpg/1280px-Tongl%C3%BCshan_Shang%26Zhou_No.2_Mining_Site.jpg","title":"Where some of the copper came out","desc":"The No. 2 mining site of the Shang and Zhou periods at Tonglüshan in Hubei, an irregular oval mound about 40 metres across with a low adit at its foot and the ore body breaking through the grass at the right. The metal moved a long way; the workings did not, and there is nothing at the surface to say who the miners answered to.","credit":"Photograph by Huanokinhejo, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A low grassed hillock under a pale sky, thick with summer weeds and small trees, with a dark rectangular opening at its base beside a small blue sign, and weathered reddish rock outcropping on its right flank."},"why":[{"q":"Why is a rare kind of lead the clearest evidence about these contacts?","a":"Because it supplied over half the lead used from the Yellow River to the Yangtze, appearing at almost the same moment in the Central Plain, Sanxingdui, Panlongcheng and Xin'gan, and vanishing under the Zhou."},{"q":"Why are the most repeated long-distance claims the weakest?","a":"Because Anyang's copper coming from the Yangtze is unsubstantiated and unreferenced, and no sourcing study supports the jade of Anyang and Xin'gan coming from Xinjiang."},{"q":"Why do carnelian beads put four regions in one exchange sphere?","a":"Because beads at Sanxingdui were cut from stone carried more than 1,000 kilometres from the north, and beads of the same period in Gansu, Shaanxi and Beijing carry the same signature."}]},
{"id":"cnh-096","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Fu Hao’s tomb alone held 1.6 tonnes of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and she was one royal consort among several rather than the greatest person buried at Anyang.","questions":["Very pure copper with tin in controlled amounts went into the high elite’s vessels, while everyone below them got remelted <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carrying the impurities of mixed metal.","Lead was added to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 2 to 5 per cent to lower its melting point, but never as carefully measured out as the tin was."],"answer":"Shang bronze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiogenic lead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alloy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">copper, tin and 2 – 5% lead</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fu Hao’s tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.6 tonnes of metal</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Metal drawn from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 500 km away</span></div>","traditional":"商代青銅器","hanzi":"商代青铜器","pinyin":"Shāngdài qīngtóngqì","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang bronze</b> is an alloy of copper with tin and lead, and the difficulty of it lies as much in getting the three metals as in casting them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The appetite was enormous: Anyang became one of the largest consumers of metal anywhere in Eurasia in the 2nd millennium BCE, and one tomb alone, Fu Hao’s, held 1.6 tonnes of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was one of a king’s consorts and by no means the most important person buried there, so her grave is the tip of the supply rather than a measure of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Feeding that took a system able to draw metal from more than 500 kilometres (300 miles) away and to turn out a wide range of objects for people of very different rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the copper came from the southern ore fields, Tongling in Jiangxi and Tonglüshan in Hubei both being worked by the early Shang, and the mining there appears to have been organised locally rather than run from the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What the chemistry shows is that the metal was not shared out evenly, and the difference lies in how it was alloyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ritual vessels of the high elite were made by primary alloying, from very pure copper with tin added in controlled amounts, and that practice is found nowhere else in Bronze Age central China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything lower down the order was secondary metal, mixed and remelted, carrying the impurities that come with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lead was put in deliberately at about 2 to 5 per cent, which lowers the melting point and makes the alloy run better into a mould, though never as carefully measured as the tin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That lead is the part still unexplained: a strangely radiogenic kind appears on the Yellow River plain quite suddenly around 1400 BCE, the obvious candidate deposit in central China has been ruled out as its only supplier, and the least awkward reading now is that several sources fed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang bronze","sources":["Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury, Philly Howarth, Peter Bray, Limin Huan, Bohao Yao, Yuting Fu, and Jigen Tang, “Social Hierarchy and the Choice of Metal Recycling at Anyang, the Last Capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7606620/. [Open access]","Wei-dong Sun et al., “Origin of the Mysterious Yin-Shang Bronzes in China Indicated by Lead Isotopes,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 23304, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep23304. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson, and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond Ritual Bronzes: Identifying Multiple Sources of Highly Radiogenic Lead across Chinese History,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Tao Shi, “Copper Mining and Metallurgy in the Zhongtiao Mountains and Yangzi River Valleys in Early China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 60, no. 2 (2021): 382–416, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/108232. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg/1280px-Fuhao_Owl_Zun_front.jpg","title":"The metal the high elite got","desc":"One of a pair of owl-shaped zun from Fu Hao's tomb, inscribed with her name. A vessel of this class is what the chemistry calls primary alloying: very pure copper with tin added in measured quantity, a mixture nobody below the highest rank received. A fine vertical line runs down the front, of the kind a piece-mould join leaves.","credit":"Photograph by Cangminzho, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"A dark green-grey bronze vessel in the shape of a standing owl, about knee height, with a crested cap, swept-back plumes above the head, wings folded in raised spirals against its sides, and a body covered in dense low-relief pattern."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the chemistry show the metal was not shared out evenly?","a":"Because the high elite's ritual vessels were made by primary alloying from very pure copper with tin added in controlled amounts, while everything lower down was mixed and remelted secondary metal."},{"q":"Why was lead put into the alloy at all?","a":"Because at about 2 to 5 per cent it lowers the melting point and makes the metal run better into a mould, though it was never measured as carefully as the tin."},{"q":"Why is one tomb's 1.6 tonnes not a measure of the supply?","a":"Because its occupant was one of a king's consorts and by no means the most important person buried there, so the grave is the tip of it."}]},
{"id":"cnh-097","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"The family that produces a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lives across the tropical Indo-Pacific, so every one found on the north China plain had been carried a very long way.","questions":["A study costing Anyang grave goods by the labour behind them rates a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at two and a half times a freshwater one, which is a judgement about distance.","The <i>Book of Documents</i> has the Shang king Pan Geng rebuking his ministers for hoarding gems and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the oldest picture of them as treasure."],"answer":"cowrie shell","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Marine shell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">rated 250, freshwater 100</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cemeteries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 studied closely at Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chu supply noted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">852 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"海貝","hanzi":"海贝","pinyin":"hǎibèi","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>cowrie shell</b> is the shell of a marine snail of the family Cypraeidae, smooth and oval, with a mantle the animal can draw over the outside of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The family belongs to the tropical Indo-Pacific, ranging from the east coast of Africa to Polynesia, so a cowrie on the north China plain had already been carried a very long way before anyone buried it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Shell of one kind or another is among the commonest classes of object in Shang graves, ranking with bronze and pottery at the two Anyang cemeteries that have been studied closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At one of the two, though, nearly all of it lies in two exceptionally rich burials, so how common shell looks depends on which ground is counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where grave goods are costed by the labour they stand for, a marine shell is rated at two and a half times a freshwater one, which is a judgement about distance rather than about beauty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The traffic ran both ways: the late Shang court drew turtle shell from the south, where it was plentiful, along with metal, in a long-distance exchange of prestige goods with the polities of the periphery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Far inland to the north-west, seashells and other non-local materials in the graves of bronze-using cultures are read as evidence of trade with the Central Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The received text of the <i>Book of Documents</i> has the Shang king Pan Geng rebuking his ministers for hoarding cowries and gems, which is the oldest picture of them as treasure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The entry that has the southern state of Chu supplying tortoise shells and cowries for currency, and notes that both had grown scarce, belongs to the 9th century BCE, nearly two centuries after the dynasty ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bronze imitations came later still, small cast pieces nicknamed metallic cowries and made when the shells would no longer stretch to what was wanted, so calling the Shang cowrie money is reading a later system backwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"cowrie shell","sources":["Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 29–30 and 54, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “The Qiang and the Question of Human Sacrifice in the Late Shang Period,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 35, no. 1 (1996), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17070. [Open access]","Terrien de Lacouperie, <i>Catalogue of Chinese Coins from the VIIth Cent. B.C. to A.D. 621</i> (London: British Museum, 1892), introduction, vii–xii, https://archive.org/details/catalogueofchine00terr. [Open access]","Teresa Stephanie Tay, Hsien Rong Samuel Lee, and Mei Lin Neo, “Spawning and Larval Development of Two Tropical Cowries (Gastropoda: Cypraeidae), <i>Cypraea tigris</i> and <i>Mauritia arabica</i>, under Laboratory Conditions,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 10, no. 4 (2023): 221332, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10090884/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Shang_Shell_Money%2C_Yinxu.jpg/1280px-Shang_Shell_Money%2C_Yinxu.jpg","title":"Shell from the Yin Ruins","desc":"Two groups of cowries, each shell opened at the back as one must be to go on a string, shown beside the other shell the site yields: two bivalves, a long slender shell and a cluster of small spirals. It is a museum case, so the grouping is the curator's rather than the Shang's.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"About twenty small pale oval shells laid in two loose clusters on a white museum plinth, most with a ragged hole broken through the rounded upper surface, with three larger shells and a group of tiny spiral shells arranged in a row above them."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a shell in north China already a travelled object?","a":"Because the family belongs to the tropical Indo-Pacific, ranging from the east coast of Africa to Polynesia, so it had been carried a very long way before anyone buried it."},{"q":"Why does how common shell looks depend on which ground is counted?","a":"Because at one of the two closely studied Anyang cemeteries nearly all of it lies in two exceptionally rich burials."},{"q":"Why is calling these shells Shang money reading a later system backwards?","a":"Because the entry about Chu supplying them as currency belongs to the 9th century BCE, nearly two centuries after the dynasty ended, and the bronze imitations are later still."}]},
{"id":"cnh-098","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"The bronze bell called nao is the chief surviving instrument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and two Anyang burials each held a graded set of three of them.","questions":["Instruments are absent from the earliest bronzes at Dasikong and then run at 5 to 7 per cent of them, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is something a grave might hold rather than usually did.","Bells were counted among the ritual vessels rather than apart from them, which is the clearest thing the inventories say about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and what it was for."],"answer":"Shang music","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nao bells</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in sets of 3</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Share of bronzes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 – 7% at Dasikong</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 ding for a king</span></div>","traditional":"商代音樂","hanzi":"商代音乐","pinyin":"Shāngdài yīnyuè","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang music</b> is known from the instruments buried with the dead, chief among them the bronze bell called nao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those bells came in graded sets of three: one late Shang tomb at Anyang lists two such sets in its inventory and another lists one, counted among the ritual vessels rather than set apart from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fu Hao’s tomb, the only intact top royal burial dug at Anyang, held bells among its bronzes, beside weapons, blades, mirrors and sculptures of tigers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Instruments are all the same a small part of what a Shang grave holds: at the Dasikong cemetery they are absent from the bronzes of the earliest period and then run between about 5 and 7 per cent of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That share stays low through all four periods, which puts music among the things a burial might contain rather than among the things it usually did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The bronzes at Dasikong multiply sharply in the last period, when the ground seems to have become a burial place for groups reaching materials once kept for the greatest Shang elite, and the share that is instruments does not move with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the first of those Anyang tombs the six bells stand inside a total of 85 vessels, which is about the room music took in a great burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Zhou the numbers a person might own were fixed by rank, nine ding for a king, seven for the second rank and five for the third, and chime bells were specified in the same way as the vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ritual and music together, liyue, became the means by which the Zhou court carried its ethical order, and the essence of that system was the division of classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The classical Chinese account of instruments, later still, sorts them by the eight materials of nature, metal, stone, silk, bamboo, gourd, clay, skin and wood, a scheme taken from the eight trigrams rather than from how anything is played.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang music","sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 56–58, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","J. A. van Aalst, <i>Chinese Music</i> (Shanghai: Statistical Department of the Inspectorate General of Customs, 1884), 47–49, https://archive.org/details/chinesemusic00vana. [Open access]","Xin Yu et al., “Sacrificial Animals as Evidence for the Disintegration of the Ritual and Music System: A Case Study of the Zhuolu Site (790–410 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0318926, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0318926. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","music","archaeology","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bronze_Nao_Bells%2C_Late_Shang%2C_13th-11th_C._BC.jpg/1280px-Bronze_Nao_Bells%2C_Late_Shang%2C_13th-11th_C._BC.jpg","title":"Two nao of a set","desc":"Late Shang nao in the Palace Museum, Taipei. A nao stands mouth upward on a hollow shank rather than hanging, and there is no clapper inside it; the ornament is the same vocabulary of bosses and beast faces the vessels carry.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Two bronze bells heavily patinated bright green, each an open-mouthed cup widening upward on a short hollow stem, mounted on clear perspex rods; the nearer one is decorated with raised scrolls and round bosses in panels."},"why":[{"q":"Why is music counted among the things a burial might hold rather than usually did?","a":"Because at the Dasikong cemetery instruments are absent from the earliest bronzes and then run between about 5 and 7 per cent of them, a share that stays low through all four periods."},{"q":"Why does the room music took in a great burial matter?","a":"Because in one Anyang tomb the six bells stand inside a total of 85 vessels, which sets the scale of it against everything else buried."},{"q":"Why does the classical scheme of instruments not describe playing?","a":"Because it sorts them by the eight materials of nature — metal, stone, silk, bamboo, gourd, clay, skin and wood — a scheme taken from the eight trigrams."}]},
{"id":"cnh-099","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Casting tools in the graves at Xiaomintun tie the people buried there to the foundry beside them, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are identified at all.","questions":["Osteoarthritis in 167 Yinxu skeletons is worse in the upper body and worse at one site than the other, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did to the people who worked in them.","Worked bone at one excavated village runs from tools anybody could make to pieces identical to the output of Anyang’s largest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a continuum rather than a divide."],"answer":"Shang craft workshops","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Skeletons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">167, from 2 sites</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bone working</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 groups, by skill</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thin sections</span><span class=\"dt-v\">61, of Anyang pottery</span></div>","traditional":"商代手工業作坊","hanzi":"商代手工业作坊","pinyin":"Shāngdài shǒugōngyè zuōfang","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang craft workshops</b> are the production quarters of the late Shang capital, which grew as social stratification intensified and the division of labour increased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commoners of Yinxu appear to have been sorted into occupational groups doing specialised craft production, living in neighbourhoods segregated by trade that archaeologists identify from the workshops found beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Xiaomintun the ground holds bronze foundries, houses and graves together, and casting tools in many of the burials tie the people there to the foundry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Xin’anzhuang, next to pottery and bone workshops and a foundry of its own, nothing in the graves links the dead to a particular trade, so they are taken for non-specialist commoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A study of 167 skeletons from the two examined osteoarthritis at eight joints and three indicators in the spine, and found high frequencies at both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Men carry significantly more of it in the upper body than women, which is read as repetitive lifting and carrying, and Xiaomintun carries more than Xin’anzhuang in both sexes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The women of Xiaomintun are affected too, raising the possibility that casting was a family business and that this is how an occupational neighbourhood came about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those workshops were fed on a large scale: cattle bone was the main material for the mass-produced hairpins and other objects turned out at the capitals, with meat and bone moving through complex systems of supply and redistribution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Skill ran as a continuum rather than a divide, and at one excavated village the worked bone falls into four groups, from expedient tools anybody could make, through a local specialist’s oracle bones, to pieces identical in every respect to the mass-produced work of Anyang’s largest bone workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery ran the other way: thin sections of 61 vessels and raw materials from Anyang found that the grey ware everybody used was made locally, with only a handful probably foreign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang craft workshops","sources":["Hua Zhang, Deborah C. Merrett, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, Yuling He, Hongbin Yue, Zhanwei Yue, and Dongya Y. Yang, “Osteoarthritis, Labour Division, and Occupational Specialization of the Late Shang China — Insights from Yinxu (ca. 1250–1046 B.C.),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 5 (2017): e0176329, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5413014/. [Open access]","Hou Yanfeng et al., “The Guandimiao Bone Assemblage (and What it Says about the Shang Economy),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 281–310, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72094. [Open access]","Hua Zhang et al., “Osteoarchaeological Studies of Human Systemic Stress of Early Urbanization in Late Shang at Anyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0151854, “Archaeological Context,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0151854. [Open access]","James B. Stoltman, Zhichun Jing, Jigen Tang, and George (Rip) Rapp, “Ceramic Production in Shang Societies of Anyang,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 48, no. 1 (2009): 182–203, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29082. [Open access]","Lu Peng, Katherine Brunson, Yuan Jing, and Li Zhipeng, “Zooarchaeological and Genetic Evidence for the Origins of Domestic Cattle in Ancient China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 56, no. 1 (2017): 92–120, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/64744. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","archaeology","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Shang_Bone_Hairpins_%2844955835175%29.jpg/1280px-Shang_Bone_Hairpins_%2844955835175%29.jpg","title":"What the bone workshops made","desc":"Bone hairpins in the Yin Ruins museum, five of the commonest thing the capital's workshops turned out. They are plain, made to a pattern and mostly from cattle bone, and it is their sheer number rather than any one of them that shows the scale of the industry.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Five slender pins of pale bone laid in a row on a frosted glass shelf beneath a small red museum label reading “bone hairpin”; each tapers to a point, four are near-straight and the fifth is a flattened leaf shape."},"why":[{"q":"Why are two neighbourhoods read so differently?","a":"Because casting tools in many Xiaomintun burials tie the dead to the foundry, while nothing in the Xin'anzhuang graves links them to a trade, so those are taken for non-specialist commoners."},{"q":"Why do the women's bones suggest a family business?","a":"Because the women of Xiaomintun carry osteoarthritis too, which raises the possibility that casting was worked at by a household and that this is how an occupational neighbourhood came about."},{"q":"Why is skill described as a continuum rather than a divide?","a":"Because at one village the worked bone falls into four groups, from expedient tools anybody could make to pieces identical to the output of Anyang's largest bone workshop."}]},
{"id":"cnh-100","num":0,"category":"Shang","question":"Jade lay nearest the body, ritual vessels between the two coffins and the human victims outside the outer chamber, which is the order <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imposed on a rich grave.","questions":["Only about 200 of the more than 20,000 graves dug at Anyang are unlooted and still hold bronze vessels, which is the remainder from which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to be reconstructed.","A hollow averaging 0.08 cubic metres (3 cubic feet) cost almost nothing to dig, and yet in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it lies under the larger graves alone and marks most of a cemetery’s goods."],"answer":"Shang burial practice","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Graves dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000, at Anyang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unlooted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 200, holding bronzes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Waist pits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">135 of 351, at Dasikong</span></div>","traditional":"商代葬俗","hanzi":"商代葬俗","pinyin":"Shāngdài zàngsú","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang burial practice</b> is the way the Shang buried their dead, and it worked to one formula, the richest graves following a standard ideal and the poorer ones simplifying it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dead were laid on their backs, extended, in a rectangular shaft oriented north–south, usually inside a timber chamber holding one or two coffins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building a great one went in stages: the shaft, a small pit cut in the middle of its floor, the chamber raised over that, and the gap outside it rammed and sometimes packed with attendants, animals and pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The inner coffin went down last, vessels, weapons and instruments were set between the two, planks and mats covered them, more attendants went on top, and the shaft was refilled layer by layer with offerings as it rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That left a set of zones with their own contents, jade and personal things nearest the body, vessels and weapons above the coffin and on the ledges, and the human victims outside the chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Rank showed in the shaft: royal tombs carried up to four ramps, rich graves outside the cemetery none at all, and the lineage cemeteries below them far less of everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Non-Shang peoples used the same range, the graves of the Siwa culture running from large ones with a secondary ledge and a wooden coffin down to small pits with neither.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 20,000 graves have now been dug across the site at Anyang and about 4,600 published, but only some 200 are unlooted and hold bronze vessels, so the formula is read from a small remainder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pit under the coffin is the cheapest mark of all, averaging about 0.08 cubic metres (3 cubic feet), and yet at Dasikong the 135 graves that have one hold 76 per cent of the goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of the dead were laid face down instead, often east–west rather than north–south, at up to 30 per cent of some Anyang cemeteries and rarely before or after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang burial practice","sources":["Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China’s Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 135–68, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 40–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]","Zhengyuan Wang, “Ritual Re-representation and the Making of Sociality: Rethinking Elite Burials in the Late Shang Yinxu,” <i>Archaeology International</i> 28, no. 1 (2025): 63–67, https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10220948/1/ai-3683-wang.pdf. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Gideon Shelach, “The Qiang and the Question of Human Sacrifice in the Late Shang Period,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 35, no. 1 (1996), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17070. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","archaeology","religion","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_%2810198751423%29.jpg/1280px-Fu_Hao_Tomb%2C_c._1200_BC%2C_Reign_of_King_Wu_Ding%2C_Shang_Dynasty_%2810198751423%29.jpg","title":"Attendants on the ledge","desc":"A step cut into the wall of the shaft carries the dead who went down with the tomb’s owner, and the rammed layers of the refill run below it; from the reconstructed tomb at the Yin Ruins, Anyang.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons.","alt":"Human bones lying on a shelf of pale earth cut into the side of a pit, lit from above, with banded layers of packed reddish soil below."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the formula read from so small a remainder?","a":"Because more than 20,000 graves have been dug at the site and about 4,600 published, but only some 200 are unlooted and hold bronze vessels."},{"q":"Why does the order of building a great tomb matter?","a":"Because it left a set of zones with their own contents — jade nearest the body, vessels and weapons above the coffin and on the ledges, human victims outside the chamber."},{"q":"Why is the small pit under the coffin more than a cheap gesture?","a":"Because it averages about 0.08 cubic metres and yet at Dasikong the 135 graves that have one hold 76 per cent of the goods."}]},
{"id":"geo-005","num":5,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Alaska","answerText":"Alaska","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 January 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">49th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alaska"},"facts":[["Capital","Juneau"],["Population","740k (2024)"],["Largest city","Anchorage"],["Area","1,723,337 km² (665,384 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Flag_of_Alaska.svg","credit":"Benny Benson, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alaska.svg)","alt":"The flag of Alaska: eight gold stars on a dark blue field, seven of them forming the Big Dipper and a larger one standing alone in the upper corner."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Alaska</b> is by a wide margin the largest state of the United States, filling the north-western corner of the continent and cut off from the rest of the country by the width of western Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its total area is 1,723,337 square kilometres (665,384 square miles), nearly two and a half times that of Texas, though a seventh of that is water rather than land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fewer than 750,000 people live in all that space, so the largest state is also one of the least populous and much the most thinly settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land runs from the rainforest and fjords of the southern panhandle through interior taiga to alpine tundra, and the highest mountain in North America, 6,190 metres (20,310 feet) high, rises in Denali National Park and Preserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anchorage is much the largest city, with about 290,000 residents, while the seat of government sits at Juneau on the panhandle, a town of some 32,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> People have lived here for more than ten thousand years, Alaska being the American end of the Pleistocene crossroad that joined the continent to Siberia whenever the sea fell far enough to bare the floor of the Bering Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Russia claimed the coast after Vitus Bering was sent to explore it in 1725, but never supported more than about four hundred settlers, and its interest faded after defeat in the Crimean War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1867 Secretary of State William Seward agreed to buy the territory for $7.2 million, roughly two cents an acre, and it was formally transferred on 18 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Critics called the purchase Seward's Folly, and he was vindicated only when gold was found in the Yukon in 1896 and Alaska became the gateway to the Klondike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was governed as a territory for nearly a century, at times by the military and at times hardly at all, before entering the Union as the 49th state on 3 January 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jason G. Gauthier, \"History and the Census: Alaska and Its People,\" U.S. Census Bureau History, 1 January 2024, https://www.census.gov/about/history/stories/monthly/2024/january-2024.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Alaska,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ak/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Purchase of Alaska, 1867,\" Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/alaska-purchase. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Wonder_Lake_and_Denali.jpg/1920px-Wonder_Lake_and_Denali.jpg","title":"Denali, Alaska","desc":"Denali.","credit":"Denali National Park and Preserve, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wonder_Lake_and_Denali.jpg","alt":"A view of Denali."}},
{"id":"geo-501","num":501,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Sacramento","answerText":"Sacramento","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1854</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"California","dot":"Sacramento"},"facts":[["State","California"],["Population","536k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","35th largest"],["Population growth","+2.1% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/Flag_of_Sacramento%2C_California.svg","credit":"Henry W. Schmitt and DevinCook, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sacramento,_California.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sacramento: broad curving bands of blue, white and dark navy suggesting two rivers meeting, with a green shape at the upper hoist and a gold one at the fly, and CITY OF SACRAMENTO in white capitals along the foot."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sacramento</b> is the capital of California<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, standing in the Central Valley<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> where the American River joins the Sacramento.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 536,000 people live in the city, which makes it the sixth largest in the state, far behind Los Angeles and San Diego but the seat of government for both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground it occupies has been the homeland of the Nisenan people since time immemorial, and Europeans first settled at Sutter's Fort, built in 1839 by a Swiss immigrant on a Mexican land grant he called New Helvetia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the ten years that followed, the fort was the economic centre of the first permanent colonial foothold in the valley, and its industries rested on the exploited labour of the Native people who had helped build it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The discovery of gold at Sutter's Mill in Coloma in 1848 began the rush that undermined John Sutter's own businesses and brought a far greater exploitation of Native people across California.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city itself was founded in December 1848 by his son, John A. Sutter Jr., as an outgrowth of the fort, and it grew as migrants poured into a territory that became the thirty-first state in 1850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That new government had nowhere settled to sit: between 1849 and 1854 the capital moved between four towns, most of them less than five years old, which earned it the nickname of the floating capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sacramento held the legislature for about four months in 1852 before a severe flood drove it away, and after a year at Benicia the seat of government returned here for good in 1854.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its position on the river made it the natural entrepot of the gold country, a distribution point and a commercial centre for the whole northern interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became the terminus of the wagon trains, the stagecoach and riverboat lines, the telegraph, the pony express and the first transcontinental railroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["California Office of Historic Preservation, \"Old Sacramento,\" California Historical Landmark no. 812, California State Parks, https://ohp.parks.ca.gov/ListedResources/Detail/812. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","California State Parks, \"Sutter's Fort State Historic Park,\" https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=485. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, \"California: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/california-state-guide. [Open access]","California State Capitol Museum, \"Planning,\" Capitol History, California State Parks, https://capitolmuseum.ca.gov/about-the-capitol/history-of-the-capitol/planning/. [Open access]","California State Parks, \"California Indian Heritage Center Park Property,\" https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=22628. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Sacramento%2C_California_skyline.jpg/1920px-Sacramento%2C_California_skyline.jpg","title":"Sacramento","desc":"Sacramento, California, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacramento,_California_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Sacramento, California."}},
{"id":"geo-006","num":6,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hawaii","answerText":"Hawaii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 August 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50th state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Hawaii","zoom":14},"facts":[["Capital","Honolulu"],["Population","1.45M (2024)"],["Largest city","Honolulu"],["Area","28,313 km² (10,932 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Flag_of_Hawaii.svg","credit":"Dbenbenn, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hawaii.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hawaii: the British Union Flag in the upper hoist corner, over eight horizontal stripes of white, red and blue."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hawaii</b> is the only state of the United States outside North America and the only one made up entirely of islands, a volcanic chain in the central Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 28,313 square kilometres (10,932 square miles) hold more sea than land: only 16,635 square kilometres (6,423 square miles) of that is dry ground, the rest being the water between and around the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 1.45 million people live there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, and Honolulu is both the capital and much the largest place, with some 345,000 residents in the urban core the census counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Two of the world's most active volcanoes, Kīlauea and Mauna Loa, stand within Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park, which climbs from the sea to 4,170 metres (13,680 feet) and is a World Heritage Site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The islands were settled long before any written record, and their people divided the land into ahupuaʻa running from the mountains to the shore and lived under a body of sacred law, the kapu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> James Cook reached the archipelago in 1778 and named it the Sandwich Islands, and by 1810 Kamehameha I had brought the whole chain under a single crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised Hawaiian independence by treaty in 1826, Britain and France jointly guaranteed it in 1843, and the two governments exchanged diplomatic representatives from 1853.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In January 1893 a revolutionary party gathered at ʻIolani Palace forced Queen Liliʻuokalani, the last sovereign of the kingdom, to give up control of her government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Congress then passed a joint resolution of annexation, which President McKinley signed in July 1898, and the islands were annexed that August and organised as a United States territory two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pearl Harbor was attacked on 7 December 1941, bringing the United States into the Second World War<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>, and Hawaii entered the Union as the 50th state on 21 August 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Lavonda Kay Broadnax, \"Hawaii: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/hawaii-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Hawaii,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/hi/index.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Hawaii,\" A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/hawaii. [Open access]","\"Annexation of Hawaii: Topics in Chronicling America,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 12 June 2025, https://guides.loc.gov/chronicling-america-annexation-hawaii. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Episode_4_of_Kilauea%27s_December_2024_eruption_2025-01-16.jpg/1920px-Episode_4_of_Kilauea%27s_December_2024_eruption_2025-01-16.jpg","title":"Kīlauea, Hawaii","desc":"Kīlauea.","credit":"USGS/K. Lynn, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Episode_4_of_Kilauea%27s_December_2024_eruption_2025-01-16.jpg","alt":"A view of Kīlauea."}},
{"id":"geo-502","num":502,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Austin","answerText":"Austin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1846</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Texas","dot":"Austin"},"facts":[["State","Texas"],["Population","994k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","13th largest"],["Population growth","+3.3% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Flag_of_Austin%2C_Texas.svg","credit":"Alex Microbe, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austin,_Texas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Austin: on white, a gold-edged shield in red, white and blue bearing a lamp of learning, under a winged crest with a cross and sword, above CITY OF AUSTIN in blue capitals."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Austin</b> is the capital of Texas and the seat of Travis County, standing on the Colorado River at the eastern edge of the Hill Country, where the Edwards Plateau breaks towards the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Just under a million people live in the city, which makes it the fifth largest in Texas, after Houston, San Antonio, Dallas and Fort Worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was built to order: in 1839 the three-year-old Republic of Texas chose the site for a permanent capital and named the town for Stephen F. Austin, who first brought Anglo-American settlers there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A commission sent out by the Texas Congress bought 7,735 acres along the river, taking in a hamlet called Waterloo, at the urging of President Mirabeau B. Lamar, who wanted the government pushed west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Edwin Waller laid out a grid fourteen blocks square either side of Congress Avenue, running north from the water to Capitol Square, and Congress first met that November in a log cabin behind a stockade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The choice was fought over at once: in 1842, after Mexican troops took San Antonio, Sam Houston ordered the national archives moved away for safety, and the townspeople formed a vigilance committee and refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When rangers came for the papers that December, Angelina Eberly fired a cannon at them, and the committee overtook the wagons and brought the archives back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Austin's population fell below 200 before a convention meeting there in 1845 approved annexation to the United States, and authority passed from the republic's last president to the first state governor on 19 February 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A statewide vote settled the question for good in 1872, and the granite Capitol, paid for with three million acres of Panhandle land and crowned with a zinc Goddess of Liberty, was dedicated in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city won another statewide election in 1881 to become the site of the University of Texas, whose research from the 1960s drew the electronics industry to a town that had lived on government and learning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["David C. Humphrey, \"Austin, TX (Travis County),\" Handbook of Texas Online, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 27 August 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/austin-tx-travis-county. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Clinton Drake, \"Texas: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 28 November 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/texas-state-guide. [Open access]","Texas State Preservation Board, \"Capitol History,\" https://tspb.texas.gov/prop/tc/tc-history/history/index.html. [Open access]","Claudia Hazlewood, \"Archives War,\" Handbook of Texas Online, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 27 August 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/archives-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Downtown_Austin_Skyline_-_Lady_Bird_Lake_%2854987239041%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Austin_Skyline_-_Lady_Bird_Lake_%2854987239041%29.jpg","title":"Austin","desc":"Austin, Texas, seen from a distance.","credit":"ajay_suresh, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Austin_Skyline_-_Lady_Bird_Lake_(54987239041).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Austin, Texas."}},
{"id":"geo-007","num":7,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Michigan","answerText":"Michigan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 January 1837</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">as a free state</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Michigan"},"facts":[["Capital","Lansing"],["Population","10.1M (2024)"],["Largest city","Detroit"],["Area","250,487 km² (96,714 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Flag_of_Michigan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Michigan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Michigan: on dark blue, an eagle above a shield showing a man on a lakeshore at sunrise, flanked by an elk and a moose over Latin mottoes."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Michigan</b> is bordered by four of the five Great Lakes<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, and that is what shapes it: of its 250,487 square kilometres (96,714 square miles) almost 99,000 are Great Lakes water rather than land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 10.1 million people live there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, more than 645,000 of them in Detroit, the largest city, while Lansing, the capital, has about 114,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The north is older ground and emptier: Isle Royale lies far out in Lake Superior, and copper has been worked on the Keweenaw Peninsula for more than 7,000 years, first by Indigenous people who made it into tools and trade goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pictured Rocks raises sandstone cliffs straight out of Lake Superior, and at Sleeping Bear Dunes the bluffs stand 137 metres (450 feet) above Lake Michigan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lakes are also why the country was fought over: at the River Raisin in January 1813 the War of 1812 produced the greatest victory for Tecumseh's Confederation and the British, and the heaviest American defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The aftermath of that battle led to the federal laws that forced the removal, relocation and assimilation of native nations, and its consequences continue today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Michigan was admitted to the Union as a free state on 26 January 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of the 19th century the south-east was on its way to putting the world on wheels: maritime trade on the Detroit River had left a workforce skilled in metal and machine trades, and coachbuilders were already there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> R. E. Olds, Henry Ford, David Buick, Walter Chrysler and Henry Leland worked out between them how to make cars in quantity, and the modern assembly line of the early 1900s made them affordable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That built a middle class and a labour movement with it, and in the Second World War the region turned from cars to tanks, jeeps and aircraft as what Roosevelt called the Arsenal of Democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Michigan: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/michigan-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Michigan,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/mi/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"MotorCities National Heritage Area,\" U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/places/motorcities-national-heritage-area.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Pictured_Rocks_Bridalveil_Falls.jpg/1920px-Pictured_Rocks_Bridalveil_Falls.jpg","title":"Pictured Rocks, Michigan","desc":"Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore.","credit":"National Park Service, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pictured_Rocks_Bridalveil_Falls.jpg","alt":"A view of Pictured Rocks National Lakeshore."}},
{"id":"geo-503","num":503,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tallahassee","answerText":"Tallahassee","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1824</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spanish mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1656 – 1704</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Florida","dot":"Tallahassee"},"facts":[["State","Florida"],["Population","205k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","120th largest"],["Population growth","+4.5% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Flag_of_Tallahassee%2C_Florida_%282020%E2%80%93present%29.svg","credit":"City of Tallahassee, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tallahassee,_Florida_%282020%E2%80%93present%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Tallahassee: on white, a blue disc bearing a white star swept about by a curving line, above CITY OF TALLAHASSEE in dark capitals."},"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tallahassee</b> is the capital of Florida, in the north-western panhandle<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and with about 205,000 residents it is only the ninth largest city in a state of 23 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its site has been a seat of power for a very long time: Anhaica Apalache, the chief town of the Apalachee, stood here, and Hernando de Soto's expedition wintered in it in 1539 and 1540.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Spanish missionaries reached the Apalachee province in 1633, and in 1656 the crown chose the village of San Luis as its western capital and moved it to the ridge where the mission stands today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 1,400 people lived at San Luis, among them a Spanish deputy governor and one of the most powerful Apalachee chiefs, around a plaza with a council house on one side and a Franciscan church on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1704 raids out of Carolina destroyed most of the Apalachee missions and San Luis was burned and abandoned, its people killed, enslaved or driven into exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States took Florida by treaty in 1821 and inherited two colonial capitals at opposite ends of it, Pensacola in the west and St Augustine on the Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Territorial citizens asked Congress for a road between them, and the two years of construction that began in 1824 opened an interior nobody could previously cross without a guide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That road brought the establishment of Tallahassee, and it brought homesteaders onto land held by American Indians, whose displacement ran into the Second Seminole War of 1835 to 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Florida became the twenty-seventh state on 3 March 1845 with its government already fixed here, and here it has stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peninsula that now holds most of the state's people was barely settled then, which is why the most populous state of the south-east is governed from one of its smaller cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, \"Florida: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/florida-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Andrew Jackson Trail,\" U.S. Department of the Interior, https://home.nps.gov/articles/andrew-jackson-trail.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"San Luis de Talimali,\" National Historic Landmark Nomination, updated documentation, 2004, https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/67d92cf2-58d6-4530-b79d-4b182a00beae. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/TallahasseeCityscape.png","title":"Tallahassee","desc":"Tallahassee, Florida, seen from a distance.","credit":"Urbantallahassee, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TallahasseeCityscape.png","alt":"A wide view of Tallahassee, Florida."}},
{"id":"geo-008","num":8,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Louisiana","answerText":"Louisiana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1812, as the 18th state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Purchased</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1803, from France</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Louisiana"},"facts":[["Capital","Baton Rouge"],["Population","4.60M (2024)"],["Largest city","New Orleans"],["Area","135,659 km² (52,378 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Flag_of_Louisiana.svg","credit":"Curtis Vann, Jr., public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Louisiana.svg)","alt":"The flag of Louisiana: on dark blue, a white pelican feeding three chicks in its nest, above a ribbon reading UNION JUSTICE CONFIDENCE."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Louisiana</b> sits at the mouth of the Mississippi, and of its 135,659 square kilometres (52,378 square miles) nearly 24,000 are water rather than land<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> — the delta, the bayous and the marsh behind the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 4.6 million people live there<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>, New Orleans being the largest city with some 363,000 residents and Baton Rouge, the capital, about 221,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its deep past stands at Poverty Point in the north-east, where earthworks raised 3,000 years ago took five million hours of labour and sat at the centre of a trading network reaching hundreds of miles across the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name is French: Robert de La Salle, an early explorer of the river, called the country Louisiana after Louis XIV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spain had held it since 1762, France took it back in 1800 and took possession in 1802, and Americans who floated their produce down the river to the sea were alarmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Jefferson wrote that there was on the globe one single spot whose possessor was the natural and habitual enemy of the United States, and that it was New Orleans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He sent James Monroe to join Robert Livingston in Paris to buy that city; Napoleon, his army in St Domingue destroyed by yellow fever and war with England looming, offered the whole territory instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They took it, 530 million acres for $15 million, land that is now all or part of fifteen states, and Louisiana itself joined the Union in 1812 as the eighteenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What grew there resembles nowhere else in the country: a Cajun culture, and a Creole culture of mixed French, African American and Native American heritage, ethnically and linguistically distinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Cane River plantations preserve the second of those, and in the French Quarter the National Park Service keeps the roots of jazz, the music New Orleans gave the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Angela McMillian, \"Louisiana: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/louisiana-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Louisiana,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/la/index.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Louisiana Purchase, 1803,\" Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/louisiana-purchase. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/Atchafalaya_Basin.jpg","title":"the Atchafalaya Basin, Louisiana","desc":"Atchafalaya Basin.","credit":"U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, photographer not specified or unknown, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atchafalaya_Basin.jpg","alt":"A view of Atchafalaya Basin."}},
{"id":"geo-505","num":505,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Juneau","answerText":"Juneau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880, as a gold camp</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1906</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alaska","dot":"Juneau"},"facts":[["State","Alaska"],["Population","31.6k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","1295th largest"],["Population growth","-2.1% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Flag_of_Juneau%2C_Alaska.svg","credit":"Svgalbertian, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Juneau,_Alaska.svg)","alt":"The flag of Juneau: on pale blue, a green mountain and a band of water inside a rounded outline, beside JUNEAU and a gold star over ALASKA'S CAPITAL CITY."},"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Juneau</b> is the capital of Alaska, bordered by the ocean and nestled at the foot of Mount Roberts, on the shore of the Gastineau Channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 31,600 people live in the city and borough, which makes the seat of government only the third largest place in the state, some 160 behind Fairbanks and a tenth the size of Anchorage<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, in a state of about 740,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is one of just two state capitals in the country that cannot be reached by car, so a visitor arrives by aeroplane, by ferry or by cruise ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town began as a gold camp: in 1880 Joe Juneau and Richard Harris found gold on the creek they named Gold Creek, which still runs through the city into the channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The placers were soon worked out, but under them lay vast deposits of quartz lode, and those grew into two of the most famous hard-rock mines in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The town they paid for became a regional shipping and distribution point, with docks and warehouses, fisheries, hydro-electric power, banking and lumber behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1900 the territorial capital was moved from Sitka, Juneau became one of three district court headquarters — one of four by 1909 — and the city incorporated at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That move was slow and took almost the whole decade, which is why the city dates its capital status from 1906 rather than from the year Congress decided it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mining shaped the place for another half-century, and the Perseverance Trail still climbs from the middle of town to the ruins of the old workings, past waterfalls and mountain goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Mendenhall Glacier reaches down almost to the edge of the city, and the trails beside it show how life returns to ground the ice has just left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Juneau, Alaska,\" Alaska Public Lands, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 2 February 2024, https://home.nps.gov/anch/planyourvisit/juneau-alaska.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"The Valentine Building, Juneau, Alaska,\" National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/355a4365-0cd2-4aac-9e30-89f7038ff78d. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Alaskan Hotel, Juneau, Alaska,\" National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78000526_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Downtown_Juneau%2C_Alaska_from_the_Goldbelt_Tram.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Juneau%2C_Alaska_from_the_Goldbelt_Tram.jpg","title":"Juneau","desc":"Juneau, Alaska, seen from a distance.","credit":"Cullen328, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Juneau,_Alaska_from_the_Goldbelt_Tram.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Juneau, Alaska."}},
{"id":"geo-009","num":9,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Maine","answerText":"Maine","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 March 1820</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">under the Missouri Compromise</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maine"},"facts":[["Capital","Augusta"],["Population","1.41M (2024)"],["Largest city","Portland"],["Area","91,633 km² (35,380 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Flag_of_Maine.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Maine.svg)","alt":"The flag of Maine: on dark blue, a shield showing a pine tree and a resting moose, flanked by a farmer and a sailor, under a star and the motto DIRIGO."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Maine</b> is the largest of the New England states and the north-eastern corner of the country<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, with 91,633 square kilometres (35,380 square miles) of territory<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and only about 1.4 million people on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Acadia National Park protects the highest rocky headlands on the Atlantic coastline of the United States, and takes four million visits a year doing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Portland is the largest city with fewer than 70,000 residents, and Augusta, the capital, is only the tenth largest with about 19,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Inland the country runs to forest and mountain: Katahdin Woods and Waters looks out at Katahdin, and the Appalachian Trail, 2,190 miles of footpath conceived in 1921 and finished in 1937, runs from here to Georgia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the far north the St John valley is Acadian country, where families share a religion, languages and a history that hold them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Europeans wintered here before they settled anywhere else in the north: in 1604 Pierre Dugua's French expedition stopped on Saint Croix Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The winter was cruel — iced in and cut off from fresh water and game, 35 of the 79 men died before spring, when native people traded game for bread and the rest began to recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The expedition moved on that summer, but the French presence in North America had begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Maine became a state on 15 March 1820 under the Missouri Compromise<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, a bargain to hold the balance in Congress that admitted Missouri with slavery and Maine free, and barred slavery elsewhere in the Louisiana Territory north of latitude 36° 30′.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Frances Perkins National Monument here honours the first woman to hold a presidential cabinet post, the Secretary of Labor behind Social Security and the shorter working week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Jennifer Dickinson, \"Maine: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/maine-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Maine,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/me/index.htm. [Open access]","Ken Drexler, \"Missouri Compromise: Primary Documents in American History,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, 7 March 2019, https://guides.loc.gov/missouri-compromise. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Acadia_National_Park_02.JPG/1920px-Acadia_National_Park_02.JPG","title":"Acadia National Park, Maine","desc":"Acadia National Park.","credit":"Plh1234us, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acadia_National_Park_02.JPG","alt":"A view of Acadia National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-506","num":506,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Honolulu","answerText":"Honolulu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʻIolani Palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun 1879</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Hawaii","dot":"Honolulu","zoom":14},"facts":[["State","Hawaii"],["Population","345k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","56th largest"],["Population growth","-1.7% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Honolulu%2C_Hawaii.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Honolulu,_Hawaii.svg)","alt":"The flag of Honolulu: on yellow, a round seal reading CITY AND COUNTY OF HONOLULU and STATE OF HAWAII around a shield with a mountain, a ship and a sunburst."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Honolulu</b> is the capital of Hawaii and its largest city, on the south coast of Oʻahu, and a city in an unusual sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The census counts about 345,000 people in the urban core<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, while the city and county, which is the whole island, holds nearly a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been the seat of government since 1845, when Kamehameha III moved the royal residence from Lahaina on Maui and took over a coral-block palace on this ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American consuls had been at the port since 1820, and Kamehameha I had brought the whole chain under one rule some ten years before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kalākaua came to the throne in 1874, found the coral building falling down, pulled it down and commissioned a palace modelled on the ones he had seen in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> ʻIolani Palace, begun in 1879, is the only royal palace in the United States, built in a style called American Florentine that exists in no other building, and fitted with indoor plumbing, electric light and an early telephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was both residence and capitol for the last two monarchs, Kalākaua and his sister Queen Liliʻuokalani, and after the kingdom was overthrown in 1893 it served as the capitol of every government that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> West of the city lies Pearl Harbor, attacked on 7 December 1941 in the act that brought the United States into the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The USS Arizona, sunk at her mooring there, is the resting place of many of the 1,177 of her crew who died that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The memorial built over her draws about 1.4 million visitors a year to a state that has been in the Union since 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Counties: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" CO-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/counties/totals/co-est2024-alldata.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"ʻIolani Palace, Honolulu, Hawaiʻi,\" Places, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/places/iolanipalace.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Pearl Harbor National Memorial,\" U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/perl/index.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Hawaii,\" A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/hawaii. [Open access]","Lavonda Kay Broadnax, \"Hawaii: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/hawaii-state-guide. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Downtown_Honolulu_Panorama_%284670974859%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Honolulu_Panorama_%284670974859%29.jpg","title":"Honolulu","desc":"Honolulu, seen from a distance.","credit":"Daniel Ramirez from Honolulu, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Honolulu_Panorama_(4670974859).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Honolulu."}},
{"id":"geo-010","num":10,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Oklahoma","answerText":"Oklahoma","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1907, as the 46th state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reserved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1828, for Native Americans</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oklahoma"},"facts":[["Capital","Oklahoma City"],["Population","4.10M (2024)"],["Largest city","Oklahoma City"],["Area","181,037 km² (69,899 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/Flag_of_Oklahoma.svg","credit":"Xrmap flag collection, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Oklahoma.svg)","alt":"The flag of Oklahoma: on blue, a buff rawhide shield hung with seven feathers and crossed by a pipe and an olive branch, above OKLAHOMA in white."},"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Oklahoma</b> is a Choctaw word meaning red people<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, and the state it names covers 181,037 square kilometres (69,899 square miles) of the southern plains<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 4.1 million residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oklahoma City is both the capital and much the largest city, with some 713,000 people, and Tulsa in the north-east is the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The land came to the United States in 1803 with the Louisiana Purchase, and in 1828 Congress reserved it for Native Americans and required everyone else to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reservation is why the Cherokee walked here: forced from Georgia, Alabama and Tennessee in 1838 and 1839, they travelled by foot, horse, wagon and steamboat into Indian Territory, which is now Oklahoma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were not the only nation moved, and the territory was policed from outside it, Judge Isaac Parker's court at Fort Smith holding jurisdiction over Indian Territory until 1896.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The plains beyond were fought over as the army pushed in: on 27 November 1868 Custer's 7th Cavalry made a surprise dawn attack on the Cheyenne village of the peace chief Black Kettle, on the Washita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Older routes crossed the same ground — the Santa Fe Trail's freight wagons between western Missouri and New Mexico, and from 1857 John Butterfield's overland mail on its arcing sweep to the Far West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Act by act, tribal land was taken into United States territory, and Oklahoma did not become a state until 1907, as the 46th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The drought of the 1930s made a Dust Bowl of its rural counties and drove thousands of farmers into long migrations west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 19 April 1995 the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building in Oklahoma City was destroyed, and the ground where it stood is now a national memorial to those killed, those who survived and those who came to help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Karen B. Walfall, \"Oklahoma: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/oklahoma-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,\" Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Oklahoma,\" Find a Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/state/ok/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Wichita_Mountains%2C_Oklahoma%2C_USA.jpg/1920px-Wichita_Mountains%2C_Oklahoma%2C_USA.jpg","title":"the Wichita Mountains, Oklahoma","desc":"The Wichita Mountains.","credit":"OKJaguar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wichita_Mountains,_Oklahoma,_USA.jpg","alt":"Low granite mountains under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"geo-507","num":507,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Lansing","answerText":"Lansing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1872 – 1879</span></div>","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Michigan","dot":"Lansing"},"facts":[["State","Michigan"],["Population","114k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","273rd largest"],["Population growth","+1.5% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Lansing</b> is the capital of Michigan, a city of about 114,000 in the middle of the state and only the sixth largest of its cities<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>, in a state of 10.1 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the capital because of an argument: Michigan became a state in 1837 and pressed a territorial courthouse in Detroit into service as its first capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Detroit was the largest city and expected to keep the seat of government<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, but the state constitution of 1835 required the legislature to fix a permanent one by 1847.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lobbying between rival towns was intense and acrimonious, no candidate could carry a majority, and the legislature settled instead on an almost completely undeveloped area in the middle of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That surprising choice became the city of Lansing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The capitol built there between 1872 and 1879, to a design by Elijah E. Myers, was one of the first statehouses modelled on the national capitol in Washington: Renaissance Revival, cruciform, under a tall dome of cast and sheet iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the third capitol in the state's history and the only one to survive, and its cornerstone was laid on 2 October 1873 before a crowd of tens of thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its main front looks east down Michigan Avenue towards East Lansing, where Michigan Agricultural College had been founded in 1855 and is now Michigan State University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town also built cars: Ransom Eli Olds went into his father's machine shop here in 1885, built his first petrol car in 1896, and moved the works to Detroit in 1899 for want of capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He came back, founding the Reo Motor Car Company plant in Lansing in 1904 on the success of the curved-dash Oldsmobile of 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, \"Michigan: State Resource Guide,\" Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/michigan-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Michigan State Capitol,\" National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000396_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, \"Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,\" NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Reo Motor Car Company Plant,\" National Historic Landmarks, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/nationalhistoriclandmarks/reo-motor-car-company-plant.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Lansing_Skyline_2022.jpg/1920px-Lansing_Skyline_2022.jpg","title":"Lansing","desc":"Lansing, Michigan, seen from a distance.","credit":"WeaponizingArchitecture, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lansing_Skyline_2022.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Lansing, Michigan."}},
{"id":"geo-011","num":11,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"New York","answerText":"New York","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 9 July 1776</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">adopted 20 April 1777</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>New York</b> is a state of the north-eastern United States, covering 141,297 square kilometres (54,555 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and holding about 19.9 million people, with Albany as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It runs across a region of contrast, from the Atlantic shores of Long Island and the skyscrapers of Manhattan through the rivers, mountains and lakes of the interior to the plains of the Great Lakes region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dutch traders were there first: the West India Company had established the town of New Amsterdam at the southern end of Manhattan by 1626, when it sent Peter Minuit to oversee its business on the Hudson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> New York declared its independence on 9 July 1776, becoming one of the original thirteen states of the federal Union, and adopted its first constitution on 20 April 1777.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the autumn of that year American forces met a British army at Saratoga and forced it to surrender, a victory that renewed hopes of independence and won the war the foreign recognition it needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What made the state rich, though, was water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its canal system has run without a break since 1825, longer than any other constructed transport system in North America, and the corridor it threads crosses 843 kilometres (524 miles) of the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The canals built a network that made New York the Empire State and the nation’s foremost commercial and financial centre, and they opened the interior of the continent to settlers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Populations along the route at Syracuse, Rochester and Buffalo quadrupled between 1830 and 1850, and from 1892 to 1924 Ellis Island in the harbour processed over 12 million arriving immigrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> New York City, with about 8.5 million residents, remains by a wide margin the largest city in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New York"},"facts":[["Capital","Albany"],["Population","19.9M (2024)"],["Largest city","New York City"],["Area","141,297 km² (54,555 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_New_York.svg","credit":"State of New York, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_York.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state arms: two robed women flanking a shield of a sunrise over a river with ships, an eagle on a globe above, and a scroll below reading Excelsior."},"sources":["Angela McMillian, “New York: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/new-york-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, “Today in History,” New York: State Resource Guide, Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-york-state-guide/today-in-history. [Open access]","National Park Service, “A Crucial American Victory,” Saratoga National Historical Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 20 April 2024, https://www.nps.gov/sara/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Erie Canalway National Heritage Corridor,” U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/erie/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Historic Immigration Station,” Ellis Island, Statue of Liberty National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 3 February 2025, https://www.nps.gov/elis/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/3Falls_Niagara.jpg/1920px-3Falls_Niagara.jpg","title":"Niagara Falls, New York","desc":"Niagara Falls.","credit":"Saffron Blaze, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3Falls_Niagara.jpg","alt":"A view of Niagara Falls."}},
{"id":"geo-508","num":508,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Baton Rouge","answerText":"Baton Rouge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1849, and again from 1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dedicated 1932</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Baton Rouge</b> stands on the east bank of the Mississippi as the capital of Louisiana, a city of about 221,000 people and, after New Orleans, the second largest in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Louisiana, whose 4.6 million residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> make it a middling state by population, had entered the Union in 1812 as the eighteenth, named by the French explorer Robert de La Salle for Louis XIV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before the 1840s its government sat at New Orleans, and the move upriver was made good by a capitol built at Baton Rouge between 1847 and 1849.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its architect, James Dakin, called the design ‘castellated Gothic’, and as a Gothic Revival public building in America it is a unique example of a rare type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mark Twain, passing 35 years later, blamed Sir Walter Scott for it, doubting that this ‘little sham castle’ would ever have been built without his medieval romances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The arrangement did not hold: the interiors were destroyed in December 1862 while the building served as a garrison, and the state was governed once more from New Orleans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was rebuilt between 1880 and 1882 and housed the government again until 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 1930 Huey Long, elected governor two years earlier, wanted the old building turned over to some collector of antiques, having already commissioned Weiss, Dreyfous and Seiferth of New Orleans to design a replacement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What they produced was a sculptured skyscraper of 34 storeys rising 137 metres (450 feet), the tallest building in the South when it was built, and it was dedicated in May 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 8 September 1935 a Baton Rouge physician shot Long in a corridor on its first floor, ending the career of one of the most controversial politicians of the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Louisiana","dot":"Baton Rouge"},"facts":[["State","Louisiana"],["Population","221k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","108th largest"],["Population growth","-2.7% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Flag_of_Baton_Rouge%2C_Louisiana.svg","credit":"Niagara, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Baton_Rouge,_Louisiana.svg)","alt":"A red flag with the words Baton Rouge in white script, and at the hoist a navy shield bearing a fleur-de-lis, a castle and a Union Jack."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, “Louisiana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/louisiana-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Louisiana State Capitol, Baton Rouge, Louisiana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73000862_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Louisiana State Capitol, Baton Rouge, Louisiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/78001421_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Downtown_Baton_Rouge.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Baton_Rouge.jpg","title":"Baton Rouge","desc":"Baton Rouge, Louisiana, seen from a distance.","credit":"getmahesh, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Baton_Rouge.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Baton Rouge, Louisiana."}},
{"id":"geo-012","num":12,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Idaho","answerText":"Idaho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 July 1890, as the 43rd state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Women voting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th state to allow it</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Idaho</b> is a state of the American north-west covering 216,443 square kilometres (83,569 square miles), fourteenth largest of the fifty,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about two million residents and Boise as both its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name is thought to come from a Shoshone phrase meaning ‘gem of the mountains’, though the derivation has never been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shoshonean peoples have camped along the Snake River, which they call Bia Ogwaide, since time immemorial, fishing it for salmon, steelhead and sturgeon, and were probably the first to see fossils weathering out of its bluffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Nez Perce say the same of their own country to the north, in the words of their tribal executive committee: ‘We did not travel here; we are of this land.’<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the middle of the 19th century the Oregon Trail was carrying thousands of newcomers west from the eastern states, and life for the Shoshoneans was turned upside down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The territory was admitted as the forty-third state on 3 July 1890, and Idaho became the fourth state in the country to give women the vote, decades ahead of national suffrage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its landscape carries an unusual amount of the continent’s deep past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Craters of the Moon, a national monument since 1924, preserves the lava fields of the Snake River Plain,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> while the Hagerman valley, arid sagebrush scrub today, was Pliocene wetland, forest and grassland teeming with animals now long extinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A harder chapter is kept at Minidoka: after the attack on Pearl Harbor, an executive order of February 1942 moved nearly 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry into ten isolated relocation centres across seven states, one of them here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Most were American citizens, and they were held behind barbed wire and guard towers in barracks covered with tar paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Idaho"},"facts":[["Capital","Boise"],["Population","2.00M (2024)"],["Largest city","Boise"],["Area","216,443 km² (83,569 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a4/Flag_of_Idaho.svg","credit":"Paul B. Evans, after the Adjutant General of Idaho and Emma Edwards Green, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Idaho.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state seal — a woman and a miner flanking a shield of a river and mountains, an elk's head above — over a red and gold scroll reading State of Idaho."},"sources":["Lavonda Kay Broadnax, “Idaho: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 21 February 2024, https://guides.loc.gov/idaho-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Before the Fossils: Shoshoneans, the Oregon Trail, and Settlement,” Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/hafo/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Nez Perce National Historical Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 29 December 2022, https://www.nps.gov/nepe/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Craters of the Moon National Monument and Preserve, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 22 January 2024, https://www.nps.gov/crmo/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Minidoka National Historic Site, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/miin/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Shoshone_Falls%2C_Idaho.jpg/1920px-Shoshone_Falls%2C_Idaho.jpg","title":"Shoshone Falls, Idaho","desc":"Shoshone Falls.","credit":"Frank Schulenburg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shoshone_Falls,_Idaho.jpg","alt":"A view of Shoshone Falls."}},
{"id":"geo-509","num":509,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Augusta","answerText":"Augusta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Western</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1754</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">chosen 1827</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Augusta</b> is the capital of Maine, a city of about 19,000 people on the Kennebec River — only the tenth largest in a state of 1.4 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its beginnings were commercial rather than political: Fort Western was raised here in 1754 under Gershom Flagg, a master carpenter from Boston, for the Proprietors of the Kennebec Purchase, as a fortified fur trading post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its main building is 30 metres (100 feet) long and largely original, the two log blockhouses and the stockade around it reconstructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In September 1775 the fort was the starting point and main supply base of Benedict Arnold’s expedition against Quebec, which gives it a place in the War of Independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Maine became a state in 1820 its border with New Brunswick was undefined, and thousands of acres of valuable timber were at stake as agents from both sides claimed the harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That quarrel is what settled the town’s future.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Congress passed a bill on 16 January 1827 to put an arsenal at Augusta, whose central position on the Kennebec made it the logical site, and a month later the town was chosen for the state capital for the same reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kennebec Arsenal that followed, eight granite buildings on forty acres of the east bank raised between 1828 and 1838, is the most intact early 19th-century munitions depot in the United States, and its size answered a boundary dispute that ran until 1842 and nearly brought a third war with Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A house completed here in 1833 became for more than thirty years the home of James G. Blaine — congressman, senator, Secretary of State and presidential candidate — whose work to improve relations with Latin America began the Pan-American movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His family gave it to the state in 1919, and it has been the governor’s residence since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maine","dot":"Augusta"},"facts":[["State","Maine"],["Population","19.2k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","1996th largest"],["Population growth","+1.4% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Western, Augusta, Maine,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000009_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Kennebec Arsenal, Augusta, Maine,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000046_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “James G. Blaine House, Augusta, Maine,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000024_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Augusta%2C_Maine_downtown.jpg/1920px-Augusta%2C_Maine_downtown.jpg","title":"Augusta","desc":"Augusta, Maine, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augusta,_Maine_downtown.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Augusta, Maine."}},
{"id":"geo-013","num":13,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"West Virginia","answerText":"West Virginia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1863</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Loyal Virginia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">capitol at Wheeling, 1861 – 1863</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>West Virginia</b> is an Appalachian state covering 62,756 square kilometres (24,230 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.77 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Charleston, a place of some 46,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is bordered by Pennsylvania to the north, Maryland and Virginia to the east, Kentucky to the south-west and Ohio to the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Adena raised Grave Creek Mound above the Ohio here long before any of that: 19 metres (62 feet) high and 73 metres (240 feet) across, it is the largest conical earth mound in the New World and one of the largest mortuary mounds anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state itself exists because Virginia seceded in 1861 and its western counties would not go with it, so delegates from 27 of them met at Wheeling from 13 to 15 May that year in what became the First Wheeling Convention, 436 members strong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The custom house at Wheeling then served as the capitol of loyal Virginia from June 1861 until June 1863, and housed the convention that framed the new state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Abraham Lincoln signed the proclamation admitting West Virginia on 20 June 1863, and the capital then refused to settle: Wheeling first, then Charleston, then Wheeling again, and Charleston ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was at Charles Town, in the eastern panhandle, that John Brown had been convicted of treason, conspiracy and murder after his raid on Harpers Ferry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coal made the state and set it fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Matewan on 19 May 1920 the police chief Sid Hatfield stood in the way of coal-company detectives evicting striking miners from company housing, and the shooting that followed left seven detectives, two miners and the town’s mayor dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Hatfield was assassinated for it the next year, which touched off the Battle of Blair Mountain of 1921 — the largest and most violent labour uprising in American history, and the beginning of the end of company rule in the coalfields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"West Virginia"},"facts":[["Capital","Charleston"],["Population","1.77M (2024)"],["Largest city","Charleston"],["Area","62,756 km² (24,230 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Flag_of_West_Virginia.svg","credit":"Militaryace and Denelson83, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_West_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A white flag with a blue border, bearing the state arms — a farmer and a miner flanking a rock dated 20 June 1863, with two crossed rifles and a liberty cap below — wreathed in rhododendron."},"sources":["Angela McMillian, “West Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/west-virginia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Grave Creek Mound, Moundsville, West Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000751_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “West Virginia Independence Hall, Wheeling, West Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000660_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Matewan Historic District, Matewan, West Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/93000303_text. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/New_River_Gorge_Bridge.jpg/1920px-New_River_Gorge_Bridge.jpg","title":"the New River Gorge, West Virginia","desc":"New River Gorge Bridge.","credit":"JaGa, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_River_Gorge_Bridge.jpg","alt":"A view of New River Gorge Bridge."}},
{"id":"geo-510","num":510,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Oklahoma City","answerText":"Oklahoma City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 April 1889, in the land run</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by vote, 11 June 1910</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Oklahoma City</b> is the capital of Oklahoma and much the largest city in it, with about 713,000 residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> in a state of 4.1 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was made in a single afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> President Benjamin Harrison set noon on 22 April 1889 for the opening of nearly two million acres to settlers, and somewhere between 50,000 and 100,000 people took part in what became known as ‘Harrison’s Horse Race’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They raced for their claims on foot, on horseback, and by train, wagon and bicycle, and many were after town lots rather than farmland — for merchants and professionals the future town was the real prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Guthrie, 40 kilometres (25 miles) to the north, was the town the government favoured, and it became the capital of Oklahoma Territory in 1890 and of the state when Oklahoma entered the Union in 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Numbers settled the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Guthrie held steady at roughly 10,000 people between 1900 and 1910 while Oklahoma City rose from about the same figure to nearly 60,000, and Governor Charles Haskell called a statewide vote between Guthrie, Oklahoma City and Shawnee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was held on Saturday 11 June 1910 and Oklahoma City won overwhelmingly; that night Haskell had the state seal brought to the Lee-Huckins Hotel, wrote the proclamation on hotel stationery and set up temporary offices there, and the Supreme Court upheld the transfer in 1911.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oil arrived next: the Oklahoma City Number One discovery well was completed on 4 December 1928, and by 1935 the field had produced 409 million barrels of crude and 95 companies were employing twelve thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Capitol itself stands above an oil pool, and in 1941 a well was brought in by directional drilling on the south plaza of its main entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oklahoma","dot":"Oklahoma City"},"facts":[["State","Oklahoma"],["Population","713k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","20th largest"],["Population growth","+4.7% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Flag_of_Oklahoma_City%2C_Oklahoma.png","credit":"City Council of Oklahoma City, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Oklahoma_City,_Oklahoma.png)","alt":"A white flag with a thin red border, bearing the round blue and red city seal in the middle, quartered with a covered wagon, an oil derrick and an atom, and lettered Seal of the City of Oklahoma City."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Guthrie Historic District, Guthrie, Oklahoma,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001664_text. [Open access]","Karen B. Walfall, “Oklahoma: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/oklahoma-state-guide. [Open access]","Linda D. Wilson, “Oklahoma City,” The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture, Oklahoma Historical Society, last updated 8 June 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=OK025. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Downtown_Oklahoma_City_skyline_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Oklahoma_City_skyline_%282%29.jpg","title":"Oklahoma City","desc":"Oklahoma City, seen from a distance.","credit":"Greater Oklahoma City Chamber and Oklahoma City Convention and Visitors Bureau (CVB) (uploaded by Chamber employee Lillie-Beth Brinkman lbrinkman@okcchamber.com), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Oklahoma_City_skyline_(2).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Oklahoma City."}},
{"id":"geo-014","num":14,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Maryland","answerText":"Maryland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 April 1788, as the 7th state</span><span class=\"dt-k\">National capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Annapolis, 1783 – 1784</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Maryland</b> covers 32,131 square kilometres (12,406 square miles) on the mid-Atlantic coast<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.26 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Annapolis and its largest city at Baltimore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nearly a fifth of that area is water rather than land, the Chesapeake Bay reaching so far up into the state that it very nearly cuts it in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the Union as the seventh state on 28 April 1788, and in 1791 gave up land of its own for the new national capital at Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For nine months before that, Annapolis had been the national capital itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Maryland State House, begun in 1772 and finished about 1779, is the oldest building in the country still in active use as a seat of state government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Congress sat in it from 26 November 1783 until 13 August 1784, and two things happened there that shaped the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> George Washington appeared before Congress to resign his post as commander in chief, establishing the precedent of civilian leadership, and on 14 January 1784 the Treaty of Paris was ratified in the same room, giving formal end to the Revolutionary War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thirty years later the British came for Baltimore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They gathered in September 1814, bombarded Fort McHenry for 25 hours, failed to break the city’s defences and withdrew — and Francis Scott Key, watching and seeing a huge flag of 9 by 13 metres (30 by 42 feet) still over the garrison, wrote four stanzas to a popular tune that became the national anthem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A national monument in the state now commemorates Harriet Tubman, the Underground Railroad’s most famous conductor, who led people out of slavery to freedom in the North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maryland"},"facts":[["Capital","Annapolis"],["Population","6.26M (2024)"],["Largest city","Baltimore"],["Area","32,131 km² (12,406 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Flag_of_Maryland.svg","credit":"Michael Wheeler, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Maryland.svg)","alt":"A flag quartered with the black and gold check of the Calvert arms and the red and white botonny cross of the Crossland arms, the quarters alternating diagonally."},"sources":["Ken Drexler, “Maryland: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/maryland-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Maryland State House, Annapolis, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000385_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Star-Spangled Banner,” Fort McHenry National Monument and Historic Shrine, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 8 September 2020, https://www.nps.gov/fomc/learn/historyculture/the-star-spangled-banner.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Harriet Tubman Underground Railroad National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/hatu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Chesapeake_Bay_Bridge_July_2017-6.jpg/1920px-Chesapeake_Bay_Bridge_July_2017-6.jpg","title":"Chesapeake Bay, Maryland","desc":"The Chesapeake Bay Bridge crossing the bay.","credit":"JefferyGoldman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chesapeake_Bay_Bridge_July_2017-6.jpg","alt":"A long steel bridge striding across open water."}},
{"id":"geo-511","num":511,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Albany","answerText":"Albany","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1867 – 1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Schuyler Mansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hamilton married there, 1780</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Albany</b> is the capital of New York, a city of about 101,000 on the Hudson<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and only the sixth largest in a state of 19.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Dutch were on this stretch of the river first: the upper Hudson lay within the patroonship of Rensselaerswyck, and the museum of the colonial Dutch across the water shows finds from the Fort Orange excavations in Albany itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city’s grandest house belonged to Philip Schuyler, one of the wealthiest men of the Revolution, who directed the invasion of Canada in 1776 and planned the defences against the three-pronged British invasion of New York the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was also an architect of the destructive campaigns of 1779 against the Haudenosaunee and their allies, supplying George Washington with intelligence and invasion routes and afterwards working to claim Haudenosaunee land for the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His Albany home and his war work alike depended on enslaved as well as paid labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That house saw the war at close quarters: General John Burgoyne was held there as a prisoner after his surrender at Saratoga in 1777, Elizabeth Schuyler married Alexander Hamilton in it in 1780, and Loyalist rangers tried to carry the general off from it in July 1781.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city’s other monument took a generation to build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thomas Fuller began the New York State Capitol in 1867, and by 1875 the walls had reached the top of the second storey and the money had run out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A board of Frederick Law Olmsted, Leopold Eidlitz and Henry Hobson Richardson was appointed to review the design, the style was altered in 1876 from Second Empire to French Renaissance, and Isaac Perry saw it finished in 1899.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eidlitz’s Assembly Chamber carries the largest stone vault in the world, and Richardson’s western stair, modelled on the one at the Paris Opera, was carved so lavishly that it is still called the million dollar staircase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New York","dot":"Albany"},"facts":[["State","New York"],["Population","101k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","335th largest"],["Population growth","+2.2% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Flag_of_Albany%2C_New_York.svg","credit":"Albany Common Council, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Albany,_New_York.svg)","alt":"A flag of three horizontal bands, orange, white and blue, with the city arms on the white band: two figures flanking a shield of wheat sheaves and a beaver, under a sailing ship, over a scroll reading Assiduity."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","New York State Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation, “Schuyler Mansion State Historic Site,” https://parks.ny.gov/historic-sites/33/details.aspx. [Open access]","National Park Service, “New York State Capitol, Albany, New York,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000519_text. [Open access]","New York State Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation, “Crailo State Historic Site,” https://parks.ny.gov/historic-sites/30/details.aspx. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Albany_Downtown_Skyline_Keybank_and_SUNY_Building.jpg/1920px-Albany_Downtown_Skyline_Keybank_and_SUNY_Building.jpg","title":"Albany","desc":"Albany, New York, seen from a distance.","credit":"This image or media was taken or created by Matt H. Wade . To see his entire portfolio, click here . @ thatmattwade This image is protected by copyright ! If you would like to use it, please read this first., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albany_Downtown_Skyline_Keybank_and_SUNY_Building.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Albany, New York."}},
{"id":"geo-015","num":15,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Nevada","answerText":"Nevada","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Comstock Lode</span><span class=\"dt-v\">silver rush from 1859</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nevada</b> is the seventh largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> 286,380 square kilometres (110,572 square miles) of mostly grassland and barren country<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> holding about 3.27 million people, with Carson City as its capital and Las Vegas much its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Silver made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Virginia City, on the Comstock Lode, was the first silver rush town in the country, and the first place in the West where the methods of large-scale industrial and corporate enterprise were intensely applied and developed — the mines forced deep shafts down after the veins and the problems that came with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By August 1860 the town had 42 stores, 42 saloons, two stamp mills, five lumber yards and 868 dwellings for a population of 2,345, while the neighbouring Gold Hill held 638 — and the whole of Nevada then counted 6,857 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Statehood came four years later, on 31 October 1864, with Carson City chosen as the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Las Vegas, now much the biggest place in the state, was not founded until 15 May 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What changed the scale of Nevada was water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hoover Dam was raised in the Black Canyon of the Colorado on the Arizona line, where summer temperatures in the gorge reach 52 °C (125 °F) and cloudbursts and sudden floods were among the difficulties; the first concrete was placed on 6 June 1933 and the last on 29 May 1935, and the dam was dedicated that September, two years ahead of schedule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At 221 metres (726 feet) from the lowest foundation rock to the crest it was the highest dam in the world when it was built, and it remains the highest concrete dam in the western hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its reservoir was made deep enough to hold the normal flow of the river, average floods included, for two years, and the work has been compared as engineering to the Panama Canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nevada"},"facts":[["Capital","Carson City"],["Population","3.27M (2024)"],["Largest city","Las Vegas"],["Area","286,380 km² (110,572 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Flag_of_Nevada.svg","credit":"Caleb Moore, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nevada.svg)","alt":"A cobalt blue flag with a device in the upper hoist quarter: a silver star above the words Battle Born on a gold scroll, between two sprays of sagebrush, with the state name below."},"sources":["Angela McMillian, “Nevada: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/nevada-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Virginia City Historic District, Storey and Lyon Counties, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000458_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hoover Dam, Clark County, Nevada, and Mohave County, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/81000382_text. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/2017_Aerial_view_Hoover_Dam_4774.jpg/1920px-2017_Aerial_view_Hoover_Dam_4774.jpg","title":"Hoover Dam, Nevada","desc":"Hoover Dam.","credit":"Mariordo (Mario Roberto Durán Ortiz), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2017_Aerial_view_Hoover_Dam_4774.jpg","alt":"A view of Hoover Dam."}},
{"id":"geo-512","num":512,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Boise","answerText":"Boise","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Boise Basin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">gold rush, 1863</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assay Office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first assay 2 March 1872</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Boise</b> is the capital of Idaho and much the largest city in it, with about 238,000 residents<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> in a state of two million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gold brought it into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Elias Davidson Pierce found gold on the Clearwater River in 1860, which drew a rush of miners into northern Idaho the next year, and further placer strikes followed on the Salmon River in 1862 and in the Boise Basin in 1863.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With some 20,656 miners on the ground, Congress made Idaho a territory on 4 March 1863, and between 1861 and 1866 its gold output came to somewhere between 42 and 52 million dollars — second only to Nevada in those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Getting the metal to market was another matter: the mines lay so far from anywhere, behind so many mountains, that from 1864 there was a strong demand for a federal mint or assay office in Idaho.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Congress appropriated 75,000 dollars for one at Boise by an act of 19 February 1869.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Drawn up under Alfred B. Mullett, Supervising Architect of the Treasury, and built of local Boise sandstone, the Assay Office was substantially finished by June 1871, though machinery arrived so late that no assay was made in it until 2 March 1872.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By then the rich surface placers were largely worked out, and its dignity and stone stood in sharp contrast to the log and wooden buildings of the young territorial capital around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Idaho became a state in 1890,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the statehouse in Boise was completed in 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Outside it stands a bronze Lincoln on a base of sandstone quarried at nearby Table Rock, given to the state in 1915 and declaring on its east face that Idaho is related to Abraham Lincoln more than any other state — he having signed the act that created the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Idaho","dot":"Boise"},"facts":[["State","Idaho"],["Population","238k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","95th largest"],["Population growth","+1.0% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Boise%2C_Idaho%27s_official_flag.png","credit":"“Man got cards”, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boise,_Idaho%27s_official_flag.png)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the city seal in the middle: two men flanking a quartered shield under a scroll reading Peril, Energy, Success, ringed by the words Seal of Boise City."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Assay Office, Boise, Idaho,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000305_text. [Open access]","Lavonda Kay Broadnax, “Idaho: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 21 February 2024, https://guides.loc.gov/idaho-state-guide. [Open access]","Idaho State Historical Society, “Idaho State Capitol,” https://history.idaho.gov/capitol/. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Boise_Downtown_Panoramic.jpg/1920px-Boise_Downtown_Panoramic.jpg","title":"Boise","desc":"Boise, Idaho, seen from a distance.","credit":"Patrick Rodriguez, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boise_Downtown_Panoramic.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Boise, Idaho."}},
{"id":"geo-016","num":16,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Utah","answerText":"Utah","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1896</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mormon trek</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into the valley, 1847</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Utah</b> is the eleventh largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 219,882 square kilometres (84,897 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.5 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Salt Lake City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is nicknamed the Beehive State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brigham Young led the Mormon trek west from the Missouri Valley through the rugged Wasatch Mountains in 1847, and tradition holds that, threading his way down Emigration Canyon, he first saw the valley unobstructed from the knoll where a stone monument now stands and is said to have declared ‘this is the place’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The route he pioneered became known as the Mormon Trail, and carried the migrations into the Great Salt Lake Valley until 1850, when Parley Pratt opened the Golden Pass Trail to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the city they laid out, the temple on Temple Square took forty years to build, going up between 1853 and 1893 under Truman O. Angell and Joseph Young.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The Tabernacle organ beside it was first used in 1867 with 700 pipes working; it has run on electricity since 1915 and now has nearly 11,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Meanwhile the railroads were closing on one another across the desert, and the two lines met at Promontory on 10 May 1869 to complete the first transcontinental railroad, an occasion marked with four spikes of precious metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Utah itself did not become a state until 4 January 1896.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bingham Canyon had flourished on gold and silver from 1863 to 1893, and in 1887 Colonel Enos A. Wall filed claim to the vast body of low-grade copper ore lying under it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The open pit that followed became the most productive of all the low-grade copper mines that soon appeared, and it still yields about 14 percent of American copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Utah"},"facts":[["Capital","Salt Lake City"],["Population","3.50M (2024)"],["Largest city","Salt Lake City"],["Area","219,882 km² (84,897 sq mi)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Flag_of_Utah.svg","credit":"The State of Utah, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Utah.svg)","alt":"A flag of navy, white and red bands cut as snowy peaks, with a gold beehive inside a navy and gold hexagon over a white star at the centre."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Utah: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/utah-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Emigration Canyon, Salt Lake County, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000737_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Temple Square, Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000738_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Golden Spike National Historical Park, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 20 June 2025, https://www.nps.gov/gosp/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Bingham Canyon Open Pit Copper Mine, Salt Lake County, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000736_text. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Delicate_arch_sunset.jpg/1920px-Delicate_arch_sunset.jpg","title":"Delicate Arch, Utah","desc":"Delicate Arch.","credit":"Palacemusic, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delicate_arch_sunset.jpg","alt":"A view of Delicate Arch."}},
{"id":"geo-513","num":513,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Charleston","answerText":"Charleston","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Clendenin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by vote in 1877, settled 1885</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Charleston</b> is the capital of West Virginia and its largest city, a place of about 46,000 people<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> in a state of 1.77 million,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> standing on the flat floodplain where the Elk River joins the Great Kanawha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Settlers had reached the neighbourhood before the Revolution and abandoned it in 1779, though most were back by 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1788 George Clendenin and his brothers became the first settlers on the site of the present city, building Fort Clendenin near the river; it was renamed Fort Lee in 1792, and Clendenin was instrumental in the forming of Kanawha County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Salt made the valley’s early money, and the fortunes of the Kanawha rested in their turn on saltmaking, agriculture, land speculation, coal, timber and processing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The floodplain is narrow, which is why the town built upward rather than outward: a disproportionate number of its early buildings ran to several storeys, and the Kanawha Valley Bank went up twenty of them in 1928 on the ground the old state capitol had stood on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where the capital should sit was argued over for fifteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came here from Wheeling in 1870 and went back to Wheeling again, until the citizens of West Virginia voted in 1877 to make Charleston the permanent seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first state capitol building in the city opened in 1885, and after that the town’s growth was assured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The railways arrived with it — the Kanawha and Michigan reached Charleston in 1884, the Charleston, Clendenin and Sutton opened north into the interior in 1890, and branch lines were soon snaking out into most of the outlying country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A town of 3,162 people in 1870 held 6,742 by 1890; the Victorian capitol burned down in 1921, and the building raised to replace it was designed by Cass Gilbert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"West Virginia","dot":"Charleston"},"facts":[["State","West Virginia"],["Population","46.5k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","880th largest"],["Population growth","-4.8% (2020–2024)"]],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Flag_of_Charleston%2C_West_Virginia.svg","credit":"City of Charleston, West Virginia; vectorised by TheVexillologistofKingwood, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Charleston,_West_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A gold flag with a blue border, bearing the city arms in the middle — a quartered shield with a pick, a riverboat and crossed tools, under a red scroll reading Charleston and the date MDCCXCIV."},"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","West Virginia State Historic Preservation Office, “Downtown Charleston Historic District,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://wvculture.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/Downtown-charleston-historic-district.pdf. [Open access]","West Virginia State Historic Preservation Office, “Downtown Charleston Historic District: Additional Documentation,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, 2024, https://wvculture.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/WV_KanawhaCo_DowntownCharlestonHistoricDistrictAD_form.pdf. [Open access]","Angela McMillian, “West Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 5 October 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/west-virginia-state-guide. [Open access]"],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Charleston%2C_West_Virginia_-_Aerial_%2838749393954%29.jpg","title":"Charleston","desc":"Charleston, West Virginia, seen from a distance.","credit":"formulanone from Huntsville, United States, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charleston,_West_Virginia_-_Aerial_(38749393954).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Charleston, West Virginia."}},
{"id":"geo-017","num":17,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Minnesota","answerText":"Minnesota","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 May 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mesabi opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mountain Iron Mine, 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Minnesota</b> is the twelfth largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 225,163 square kilometres (86,936 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.8 million residents, its capital at St. Paul and its largest city at Minneapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the North Star State, it entered the Union on 11 May 1858.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that a portage trail of 13.7 kilometres (8.5 miles) — Gichi Onigaming, the Great Carrying Place — let travellers past the falls and gorges of the Pigeon River, part of an ancient trade route between the Great Lakes and the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 1731 and 1804 thousands of men shuttled furs and supplies over that Grand Portage to the North West Company depot, its headquarters and its rendezvous with the canoes out of Montreal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elsewhere the pipestone quarries have been worked for over 3,000 years, and 23 tribal nations are affiliated with ground where Indigenous quarriers still work them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The pine forests came next: the St. Croix Boom, opened in 1856, was where the great log drives ended and the timber was sorted, scaled and rafted for the sawmills downstream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At the peak the stored logs could cover 14 kilometres (nine miles) of river; the last went through on 12 June 1914, by which time the forests had been almost entirely cut over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Minneapolis meanwhile learned to make fine flour from hard spring wheat: a French purifier installed for Cadwallader Washburn in 1871 took out the bran and left the middlings, and after an explosion levelled his A Mill in 1878 he rebuilt it with automatic all-roller gradual-reduction machinery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> In 1890 a prospector employed by the Merritt brothers uncovered the Mesabi Range, the largest iron ore deposit in the world, whose beds lay flat and near the surface and so could be taken by open pit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> From the first shipment out of the Mountain Iron Mine in 1892 until 1961 the range supplied over two billion gross tons, more than half the ore mined in the United States in those years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Minnesota"},"facts":[["Capital","St. Paul"],["Population","5.79M (2024)"],["Largest city","Minneapolis"],["Area","225,163 km² (86,936 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Minnesota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/minnesota-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Grand Portage National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.nps.gov/grpo/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Pipestone National Monument, U.S. Department of the Interior, last updated 4 November 2020, https://www.nps.gov/pipe/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “St. Croix Boom Site, Washington County, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000407_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Washburn A Mill Complex, Minneapolis, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/83004388_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mountain Iron Mine, St. Louis County, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/68000052_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Minnesota.svg","credit":"Minnesota State Emblems Redesign Commission, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Minnesota.svg)","alt":"A flag whose hoist is a dark blue kite shape bearing a white eight-pointed star, with a plain light blue field on the fly."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Spit_Rock_Lighthouse_2012.jpg/1920px-Spit_Rock_Lighthouse_2012.jpg","title":"Split Rock Lighthouse, Minnesota","desc":"Split Rock Lighthouse.","credit":"JMLAX101, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spit_Rock_Lighthouse_2012.jpg","alt":"A view of Split Rock Lighthouse."}},
{"id":"geo-514","num":514,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Annapolis","answerText":"Annapolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from St. Mary's, 1695</span><span class=\"dt-k\">National capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 Nov 1783 – 13 Aug 1784</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naval Academy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1845</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Annapolis</b> is the capital of Maryland, a city of about 41,000 on a peninsula between Spa and College Creeks at the mouth of the Severn River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Maryland's capital had been at St. Mary's until 1695, when the royal governor Sir Francis Nicholson moved it to this more central spot, then a sparsely settled place called Anne Arundel Town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The new town of about 40 hectares (100 acres), soon enlarged to over 57 hectares (140 acres), was renamed for Princess Anne, the future queen, and incorporated in 1696.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its plan is a modified baroque one, of the kind used in the gardens at Versailles and proposed for London after the fire of 1666, reserving the highest ground for the state house and the church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A circle 161 metres (528 feet) across carries the State House and a smaller one of 104 metres (340 feet) the Anglican church, with radial streets running out from both — though those meeting the State House are set in a pinwheel, so none is on axis with its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Maryland State House, begun in 1772, is the oldest building in the country still in use as a seat of state government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between 26 November 1783 and 13 August 1784 it served as the nation's capital, the Continental Congress having come from Princeton because the building offered a national legislature the space and prestige it wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There Washington resigned his post as commander in chief before Congress, setting the precedent of civilian leadership of the military, and there the Congress ratified the Treaty of Paris on 14 January 1784, formally ending the Revolutionary War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In September 1786 the Annapolis Convention met here with representatives from six states and entertained the resolution out of which the Constitutional Convention grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States Naval Academy was established in 1845 on the site of Fort Severn, a four-hectare (10-acre) army reservation of fourteen buildings, and has trained many of the officers who have commanded the navy since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Maryland","dot":"Annapolis"},"facts":[["State","Maryland"],["Population","40.7k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","1011th largest"],["Population growth","-0.3% (2020–2024)"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Flag_of_Annapolis%2C_Maryland.svg","credit":"RCraig09, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Annapolis,_Maryland.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing a royal crown above a thistle and a Tudor rose on one stem, over a gold scroll reading VIXI LIBER ET MORIAR."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Maryland: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/maryland-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Colonial Annapolis Historic District, Anne Arundel County, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000383_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Maryland State House, Annapolis, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000385_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “United States Naval Academy, Annapolis, Maryland,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000386_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Aerial_view_of_Annapolis%2C_Maryland_LCCN2011631342.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-Aerial_view_of_Annapolis%2C_Maryland_LCCN2011631342.tif.jpg","title":"Annapolis","desc":"Annapolis, Maryland, seen from a distance.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Annapolis,_Maryland_LCCN2011631342.tif","alt":"A wide view of Annapolis, Maryland."}},
{"id":"geo-018","num":18,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"New Jersey","answerText":"New Jersey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1787</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Trenton, from 1790</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>New Jersey</b> is the 47th largest American state, covering only 22,591 square kilometres (8,723 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> but holding about 9.5 million people, its capital at Trenton and its largest city Newark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Garden State, it was admitted to the Union on 18 December 1787, and Trenton became the official capital in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Princeton's Nassau Hall, built in 1754–56 and named for King William III of Nassau, was the first important collegiate building of the middle colonies and for nearly fifty years held the whole college.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It served as barracks and hospital during the Revolution and saw the British last stand at the Battle of Princeton; from June to November 1783 the Continental Congress sat in it, thanked Washington for the war and received news that the peace treaty was signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1791 Alexander Hamilton and his associates chartered the Society for Establishing Useful Manufactures, capital $500,000, to prove American manufacturing profitable and so free the country of British goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The 20-metre (65-foot) head of the Great Falls of the Passaic at Paterson was to drive it; a first scheme for a canal to tidewater was abandoned as too costly in 1792, and Hamilton had Pierre Charles L'Enfant examine the raceway plan that replaced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was the first attempt in the United States to harness the whole power of a major river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In October 1858 William Parker Foulke dug bones from a marl pit at Haddonfield that proved to be the first relatively complete dinosaur skeleton found anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Joseph Leidy, who described it as <i>Hadrosaurus foulkii</i>, found the thigh bone 40 percent longer than the upper arm and concluded that it reared onto its hind legs, against Richard Owen, who coined 'Dinosauria' in 1841 and thought such creatures lumbering quadrupeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A century later, at Holmdel in 1965, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson stumbled with the Horn Antenna on the microwave background radiation filling the universe, confirming the Big Bang and winning the 1978 Nobel Prize for Physics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Jersey"},"facts":[["Capital","Trenton"],["Population","9.50M (2024)"],["Largest city","Newark"],["Area","22,591 km² (8,723 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “New Jersey: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-jersey-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nassau Hall, Princeton, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000465_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Great Falls of the Passaic/Society for Establishing Useful Manufactures Historic District, Paterson, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000391_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hadrosaurus foulkii Leidy Site, Haddonfield, New Jersey,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/94001648_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Horn Antenna, Holmdel, New Jersey,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/89002457_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_New_Jersey.svg","credit":"Author of xrmap, with modifications by Denelson83 and ChrisRuvolo, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Jersey.svg)","alt":"A buff flag bearing the state arms — a blue shield with three ploughs, a horse's head above it, and two robed women holding a liberty staff and a cornucopia, over a scroll reading LIBERTY AND PROSPERITY and the date 1776."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Delaware_Water_Gap_aerial_view.jpg/1920px-Delaware_Water_Gap_aerial_view.jpg","title":"the Delaware Water Gap, New Jersey","desc":"The Delaware Water Gap from the air.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delaware_Water_Gap_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"A river cutting a notch through a forested ridge."}},
{"id":"geo-515","num":515,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Carson City","answerText":"Carson City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Eagle Valley townsite, 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">act of 1869, built 1871</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Carson City</b> is the capital of Nevada, a city of about 58,000 in Eagle Valley and the state's sixth largest place, far behind Las Vegas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four men bought the Eagle Ranch from John Mankin in 1858, and Abraham Curry, credited with making it a town, had a townsite surveyed that September, the lots sold at first for $50 or given to anyone who would build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nevada obtained statehood on 31 October 1864, and Carson City was chosen as its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An act for a state capitol was signed on 23 February 1869, and Joseph Gosling's design was built by Peter Cavanaugh and Son in stone quarried at the state prison: a two-storey Grecian cross under a cupola 9 metres (30 feet) across and 37 metres (120 feet) above the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What paid for it was the Comstock Lode, where Virginia City became the first silver-rush town and where large-scale industrial mining was worked out between 1860 and 1864.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> As the lode poured out silver, the cost and risk of hauling bullion to San Francisco became a problem, and the answer was a branch of the United States Mint in the small town itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was designed by Alfred B. Mullett, supervising architect of the Treasury from 1866 to 1874, whose other western work was the Portland custom house and the old San Francisco Mint, Curry superintended the building and became its first superintendent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1887 the Nevada legislature set up a commission to found a school for Native Americans in Ormsby County, and the Stewart Indian School opened in December 1890, named for Senator William M. Stewart, who pressed the idea in Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was one of about 25 off-reservation Bureau of Indian Affairs boarding schools then operating, the only one in Nevada's history and the state's only secondary school for Native people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The Comstock mines paid $125,335,925 in dividends by 1882 and dominated western mining through the 1870s, the decade in which Carson City got its capitol and its mint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nevada","dot":"Carson City"},"facts":[["State","Nevada"],["Population","58.1k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","702nd largest"],["Population growth","-0.8% (2020–2024)"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Flag_of_Carson_City%2C_Nevada.gif","credit":"City of Carson City, Nevada, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Carson_City,_Nevada.gif)","alt":"A white flag crossed by a blue band, with the city seal at the hoist — an eagle over the capitol dome and a horse-drawn wagon, ringed by the words Carson City Nevada and the date 1858."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Nevada: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/nevada-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Abraham Curry House, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/87000501_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Virginia City Historic District, Storey County, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000458_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nevada State Capitol, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75002126_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “U.S. Mint, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75002127_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Stewart Indian School, Carson City, Nevada,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/85002432_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Skyline_of_Carson_City%2C_NV.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Carson_City%2C_NV.jpg","title":"Carson City","desc":"Carson City, Nevada, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Carson_City,_NV.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Carson City, Nevada."}},
{"id":"geo-019","num":19,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Massachusetts","answerText":"Massachusetts","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 February 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Harvard</span><span class=\"dt-v\">established 1636</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lowell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">canals from 1822</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Massachusetts</b> is the seventh smallest of the American states, covering 27,336 square kilometres (10,554 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.1 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Boston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is officially a Commonwealth rather than a state, and it was admitted to the Union on 6 February 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Harvard College was established in 1636, the first colonial institution of higher learning, and its liberal arts course was patterned on those of Oxford and Cambridge; Massachusetts Hall, put up between 1718 and 1720, is its oldest surviving building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The college was meant chiefly to supply clergy for the colonies, though its graduates went into every walk of colonial life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Springfield an ordnance works that had supplied the whole north-eastern theatre in the Revolution was made a federal arsenal by act of Congress in 1794, seven years after Daniel Shays and his farmers tried and failed to raid it on 25 January 1787.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> That rising did as much as anything to build support for a stronger national government, and so bore indirectly on the making of the Constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The works then helped settle the principle of interchangeable parts: in 1822 a workman there, Thomas Blanchard, built a machine for turning gun stocks that proved the forerunner of the machinery for making standardised parts of every other kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nathan Appleton and Patrick Tracy Jackson began harnessing the Pawtucket Falls of the Merrimack in the same year, and by 1826 the first of their canals were finished and the city of Lowell established, named for the textile manufacturer Francis Cabot Lowell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Within two decades Lowell was the cotton-textile centre of the country, and it is reckoned the first American city developed primarily to be industrial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A quieter experiment ran alongside: Henry David Thoreau lived in a one-room cabin at Walden Pond from 4 July 1845 to 6 September 1847, finishing one book there and gathering the material for <i>Walden, or Life in the Woods</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Massachusetts"},"facts":[["Capital","Boston"],["Population","7.14M (2024)"],["Largest city","Boston"],["Area","27,336 km² (10,554 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Massachusetts: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/massachusetts-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Massachusetts Hall, Harvard University, Cambridge, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000769_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Springfield Armory, Springfield, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000898_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lowell Locks and Canals Historic District, Lowell, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001972_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Walden Pond, Concord, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000790_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Flag_of_Massachusetts.svg","credit":"State of Massachusetts, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Massachusetts.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing a blue shield on which a Native man stands holding a bow and arrow beside a white star, ringed by a blue scroll with the Latin motto and topped by an arm holding a sword."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/White_Crest_Beach_Wellfleet.jpg/1920px-White_Crest_Beach_Wellfleet.jpg","title":"Cape Cod, Massachusetts","desc":"Cape Cod.","credit":"This image or media was taken or created by Matt H. Wade . To see his entire portfolio, click here . @ thatmattwade This image is protected by copyright ! If you would like to use it, please read this first., CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Crest_Beach_Wellfleet.jpg","alt":"A view of Cape Cod."}},
{"id":"geo-516","num":516,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Salt Lake City","answerText":"Salt Lake City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mormon emigration of 1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Douglas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1864 – 1866</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Salt Lake City</b> is the capital of Utah and much its largest city, with about 218,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Brigham Young led the Mormon emigration of 1847 west from the Missouri Valley through the Wasatch Mountains, and tradition holds that he first saw the valley unobstructed from a knoll at the foot of Emigration Canyon, where a stone monument now stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Beehive House was built for Young in his two capacities, as second president of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and as first governor of Deseret, later Utah Territory, and was an official residence as much as a family home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ulysses S. Grant, William Tecumseh Sherman, Ralph Waldo Emerson, Mark Twain, Horace Greeley and Dom Pedro of Brazil were all entertained there in his lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1893 the church bought the house as the official residence of its presidents, and Lorenzo Snow and Joseph F. Smith were the only two who lived in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The territory's dealings with Washington were the most turbulent in the history of the American territorial system: it rejected public schools, federal land policy, the two-party system, parts of the common law and the primacy of the civil courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> President James Buchanan sent a military expedition in 1857 to compel the Mormon population to cooperate with federal officials, and between 1862 and 1887 Congress banned polygamy, reformed the courts, dissolved the Nauvoo Legion and put a commission over the territory's elections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only in the defeated South after the Civil War had the government interfered so far in a region's own affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Colonel Patrick Connor and the California Volunteers founded Fort Douglas in the foothills above the city in September 1862, nominally to guard the transcontinental telegraph and the mail routes, and in practice giving the government a permanent base close to the Mormon capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Council Hall, put up between 1864 and 1866 for the city's own offices, doubled as the territorial capitol until 1894.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Utah","dot":"Salt Lake City"},"facts":[["State","Utah"],["Population","218k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","111th largest"],["Population growth","+9.0% (2020–2024)"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/31/Flag_of_Salt_Lake_City_%282020%29.svg","credit":"Arianna Meinking and Elio Kennedy-Yoon for Salt Lake City Corporation, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salt_Lake_City_(2020).svg)","alt":"A flag with a blue upper half and a white lower half, a white sego lily with a gold centre set in the blue near the hoist."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Utah: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/utah-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Emigration Canyon, Salt Lake County, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000737_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Brigham Young Complex, Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000626_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old City Hall (Council Hall), Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000846_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Douglas, Salt Lake City, Utah,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000628_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Salt_Lake_City_panorama.jpg","title":"Salt Lake City","desc":"Salt Lake City, seen from a distance.","credit":"Original uploader was user:Skyguy414 at en.wikipedia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salt_Lake_City_panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Salt Lake City."}},
{"id":"geo-020","num":20,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Ohio","answerText":"Ohio","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 March 1803</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canal opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ohio and Erie, 1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Flyer III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1905</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ohio</b> is the 34th largest of the American states, covering 116,098 square kilometres (44,826 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11.9 million residents and its capital and largest city alike at Columbus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Buckeye State, it became the 17th state on 1 March 1803.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that the Hopewell, whose presence in southern Ohio runs from about 300 BCE to 250 CE, raised geometric earthworks across what was the focal area of that culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Newark group was among the largest and most complicated of them, covering something like 5 square kilometres (2 square miles) of circles, rectangles and octagons joined by parallel walls, with mounds and smaller rings inside and outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of it went under the growing town, and three portions survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Ohio and Erie Canal, joining Cleveland to Portsmouth, was finished in 1832, and with the Miami and Erie it formed the trunk of a network of some 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) that tied Lake Erie to the Ohio River and reached the canals of New York, Pennsylvania and Indiana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Cut between 1820 and 1845, it was among the most extensive and successful antebellum canal systems in the country, and it brought population, towns and trade in a rush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Ripley, on a hill above the Ohio River and northern Kentucky, the Presbyterian minister John Rankin built a house in 1828 that became one of the first stations on the Underground Railroad, and from it he is thought to have helped as many as 2,000 escaping slaves northward into Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His <i>Letters on American Slavery</i>, published as a book in 1826, were among the first clearly argued antislavery writings printed west of the Appalachians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In 1905 Wilbur and Orville Wright built the Flyer III, the first aeroplane capable of sustained and controlled flight and fit for practical use, their object being a machine of practical utility rather than “a useless and extravagant toy.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Ohio"},"facts":[["Capital","Columbus"],["Population","11.9M (2024)"],["Largest city","Columbus"],["Area","116,098 km² (44,826 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Ohio: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/ohio-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Newark Earthworks, Licking County, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000614_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ohio and Erie Canal, Cuyahoga County, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000607_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “John Rankin House, Ripley, Ohio,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000485_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wright Flyer III, Dayton, Ohio,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/90001747_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Flag_of_Ohio.svg","credit":"John Eisenmann, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ohio.svg)","alt":"A swallow-tailed flag of red and white stripes with a blue triangle at the hoist, in which seventeen white stars surround a red disc within a white ring."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Cuyahoga_Valley_National_Park_20.jpg/1920px-Cuyahoga_Valley_National_Park_20.jpg","title":"Cuyahoga Valley, Ohio","desc":"Cuyahoga Valley National Park.","credit":"Niagara66, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuyahoga_Valley_National_Park_20.jpg","alt":"A view of Cuyahoga Valley National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-517","num":517,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"St. Paul","answerText":"St. Paul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Snelling</span><span class=\"dt-v\">site bought 1805</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Northern</span><span class=\"dt-v\">merged 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kellogg-Briand</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Pact of 1928</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>St. Paul</b> is the capital of Minnesota and its second largest city, with about 307,000 people, standing on the Mississippi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lieutenant Zebulon Pike bought the site of the future Fort Snelling from the Sioux in 1805 while looking for the river's source, paying Chief Little Crow and his people a few baubles, some rum and the promise of $2,000 or its worth in trade goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fort, on the bluff commanding the meeting of the Minnesota and the Mississippi, was the first American fort in what is now Minnesota, and it mattered to the security of the north-west and to the army's own change from a frontier force into a modern one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> James J. Hill, born in Ontario in 1838 and in St. Paul by 1856, drove his lines to the Pacific: he bought the St. Paul and Pacific Railroad in 1878 and built from it a network across the north-west, merged in 1890 into the Great Northern Railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took the Northern Pacific in 1893 and the Chicago, Burlington and Quincy soon after, but his attempt to gather them into the Northern Securities Company was defeated in 1901 under the new Sherman Anti-Trust Law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Hill's mansion was the showplace of the city until his death in 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Frank Billings Kellogg, a lawyer of the same city, was Calvin Coolidge's secretary of state from 1925 to 1929, and under him the department moved away from the interventionism of the administrations before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He negotiated the Kellogg-Briand Pact of 1928, in which 62 nations eventually agreed to renounce war as an instrument of national policy, and it brought him the Nobel Peace Prize for 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Fitzgeralds moved to 593 Summit Avenue about 1914 and later to number 599, their permanent house in the city, and Scott returned there in July 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By the 1920s, with <i>The Great Gatsby</i>, he had come to embody the disillusioned literary outlook of the post-war years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Minnesota","dot":"St. Paul"},"facts":[["State","Minnesota"],["Population","307k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","68th largest"],["Population growth","-1.3% (2020–2024)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Minnesota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, last updated 15 September 2023, https://guides.loc.gov/minnesota-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Snelling, Hennepin County, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000401_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “James J. Hill House, Saint Paul, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000405_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Frank B. Kellogg House, Saint Paul, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001035_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “F. Scott Fitzgerald House, Saint Paul, Minnesota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000440_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Flag_of_Saint_Paul%2C_Minnesota.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saint_Paul,_Minnesota.svg)","alt":"A yellow flag crossed by a broad blue band, bearing a red shield charged with a log cabin, a capitol dome and a winged wheel, under a blue star and over a red scroll reading SAINT PAUL."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Saint_Paul_skyline%2C_West_Side_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Saint Paul","desc":"Saint Paul, Minnesota, seen from a distance.","credit":"Cliff from I now live in Arlington, VA (Outside Washington DC), USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Paul_skyline,_West_Side_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Saint Paul, Minnesota."}},
{"id":"geo-021","num":21,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Illinois","answerText":"Illinois","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 December 1818</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Springfield, from 1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pullman built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880 – 1884</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Illinois</b> is the 25th largest of the American states, covering 149,995 square kilometres (57,914 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 12.7 million residents, its capital at Springfield and its largest city Chicago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Prairie State and the Land of Lincoln, it became a state on 3 December 1818, and Springfield took the capital in 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cahokia, east of St. Louis, was the largest Mississippian community in eastern North America, covering more than 1,600 hectares (4,000 acres) and lived in from about 800 to 1500 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than a hundred mounds stand there: flat-topped platforms carrying temples and officials' quarters, and burial mounds holding the graves of important people, often with retainers beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Monks Mound, the largest, covers about 6 hectares (15 acres) and climbs in four terraces to roughly 30 metres (100 feet) above the valley floor, and the town around it was laid out on north-south and east-west axes, with a palisade about its inner precinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> George Pullman had a model industrial town built south of Chicago between 1880 and 1884, planned throughout by an architect and a landscape engineer working to one scheme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was the first serious American attempt to plan a workers' town whole, industry, housing, churches and recreation coordinated under one scheme against the crowded and unsanitary districts of other industrial cities, and the Pullman strike of 1894 made it a landmark of the labour movement as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1889 Jane Addams and Ellen Gates Starr moved into a decayed mansion on Halsted Street, by then in an immigrant slum, and made of it Hull-House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was not the first of its kind in the country, but it answered the needs of those who came to it as nothing else then did, and its closeness to them earned a reputation well beyond America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Addams had come to the work after Rockford Seminary and a broken start in medicine, and her father, a miller, a bank president and a state senator, had been a friend of Lincoln.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Illinois"},"facts":[["Capital","Springfield"],["Population","12.7M (2024)"],["Largest city","Chicago"],["Area","149,995 km² (57,914 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Illinois: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/illinois-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cahokia Mounds, Madison and St. Clair Counties, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000899_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pullman Historic District, Chicago, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000054_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jane Addams' Hull-House, Chicago, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000315_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Flag_of_Illinois.svg","credit":"Denelson83, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Illinois.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing the state seal in outline — an eagle on a boulder holding a shield and a banner reading State Sovereignty National Union, with a rising sun behind — over the state's name in blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Starved_Rock%2C_Starved_Rock%2C_Illinois%2C_April_2009.jpg/1920px-Starved_Rock%2C_Starved_Rock%2C_Illinois%2C_April_2009.jpg","title":"Starved Rock, Illinois","desc":"Starved Rock above the Illinois River.","credit":"HeatherMarieKosur, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Starved_Rock,_Starved_Rock,_Illinois,_April_2009.jpg","alt":"A wooded sandstone butte standing over a wide river."}},
{"id":"geo-518","num":518,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Trenton","answerText":"Trenton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trent's Town</span><span class=\"dt-v\">laid out from 1719</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 December 1776</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1790</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Trenton</b> is the capital of New Jersey, a city of about 91,000 people on the Delaware and the fifth largest place in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> William Trent, a Scottish merchant who had settled in Philadelphia about 1682 and traded in partnership with William Penn and James Logan, bought some 320 hectares (800 acres) across the Assunpink Creek at the Falls of the Delaware in 1714, and enlarged the tract over the years that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He built a summer house on the river bank in 1719, moved into it for good in 1721, and had a township laid out that he called Trent's Town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The barracks that still stand were begun on 31 May 1758 and finished in March 1759, put up by the New Jersey General Assembly to winter 300 British troops during the French and Indian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were one of five raised at Trenton, Burlington, Elizabethtown, Perth Amboy and New Brunswick so that soldiers need not be billeted by force on private householders, and the other four are gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On Christmas night 1776 Washington crossed the Delaware, and the next morning surprised and took the thousand-man German garrison of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The barracks, of undressed native stone and built in a U with a balcony the length of its inner side, is the only major building still standing that belongs to that battle, the rest of the field lying under the modern town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The crossing has taken on an almost legendary character in American memory, but it was a realistic and carefully planned stroke meant to rescue a failing cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By it Washington carried the war to the enemy and gave the new nation and his often-beaten army a taste of victory at the lowest ebb of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Trent's own house, where a Loyalist doctor tended American and Hessian wounded alike during the battle, later held three governors of New Jersey among its tenants,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the town became the state's official capital in 1790.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Jersey","dot":"Trenton"},"facts":[["State","New Jersey"],["Population","91.2k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","385th largest"],["Population growth","+0.4% (2020–2024)"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Flag_of_Trenton%2C_New_Jersey.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Trenton,_New_Jersey.svg)","alt":"A flag divided blue at the hoist and yellow at the fly, with the round city seal in the middle — a horse's head above a blue shield bearing three gold wheatsheaves, over the date 1792."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “New Jersey: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-jersey-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “William Trent House, Trenton, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000388_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Barracks, Trenton, New Jersey,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000506_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Washington's Crossing, Mercer County, New Jersey and Bucks County, Pennsylvania,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000650_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Aerial_view_of_Trenton_bridges%2C_December_2022.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_of_Trenton_bridges%2C_December_2022.jpg","title":"Trenton","desc":"Trenton, New Jersey, seen from a distance.","credit":"James Loesch from Trenton, New Jersey, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Trenton_bridges,_December_2022.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Trenton, New Jersey."}},
{"id":"geo-022","num":22,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Virginia","answerText":"Virginia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jamestown</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1607</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Africans landed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1619</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 June 1788</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Virginia</b> is the 35th largest of the American states, covering 110,787 square kilometres (42,775 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 8.8 million residents, its capital at Richmond and its largest city Virginia Beach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Old Dominion, it entered the Union as the 10th state on 25 June 1788, and it is the birthplace of more American presidents than any other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> England's first permanent colony was planted at Jamestown in 1607 by a joint venture called the Virginia Company, twenty years after the lost colony at Roanoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1619 an English privateer put ashore ‘20 and odd’ Africans, traded to the colonists for victuals, and Native American, English and African met at one small outpost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Williamsburg began in 1633 as a palisaded barrier called Middle Plantation, became the colony's capital in 1699 and was renamed for William III, and for eighty years it was a political and cultural centre of American life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Washington, Patrick Henry, George Wythe, Thomas Jefferson and George Mason all studied, taught or held office there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> George Washington inherited Mount Vernon in 1752 on the death of his half-brother Lawrence, and for the twenty years before the Revolution farming it was his main occupation; he retired there in 1797 and was buried on the grounds two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His first military errand came in October 1753, carrying a British ultimatum to the French in the Ohio valley, whose refusal began the French and Indian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Thomas Jefferson designed the whole of Monticello, lived there through the most productive years of his life and died there in 1826.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> His plan of government for the Northwest Territory produced the ordinance of 1787, the first ban Congress passed on the spread of slavery;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> as a young politician he had argued for prohibiting slavery in new American territories, yet he never freed his own slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Virginia"},"facts":[["Capital","Richmond"],["Population","8.81M (2024)"],["Largest city","Virginia Beach"],["Area","110,787 km² (42,775 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/virginia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History & Culture,” Historic Jamestowne, Colonial National Historical Park, last updated 1 November 2021, https://www.nps.gov/jame/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Williamsburg Historic District, Williamsburg, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000925_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mount Vernon, Fairfax County, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000833_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Monticello, Albemarle County, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000826_text. [Open access]","Peter Onuf, “Thomas Jefferson: Life in Brief,” Miller Center of Public Affairs, University of Virginia, https://millercenter.org/president/jefferson/life-in-brief. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Flag_of_Virginia.svg","credit":"Commonwealth of Virginia; vectorised by LadyofHats and others, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A deep blue flag bearing the state seal in a white circle — a woman in armour standing over a fallen figure, with the motto Sic Semper Tyrannis beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Skyline_Drive_in_the_Fall_%2821852619608%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_Drive_in_the_Fall_%2821852619608%29.jpg","title":"Shenandoah National Park, Virginia","desc":"Shenandoah National Park.","credit":"Shenandoah National Park from Virginia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_Drive_in_the_Fall_(21852619608).jpg","alt":"A view of Shenandoah National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-519","num":519,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Boston","answerText":"Boston","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1712 – 1713</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tea Party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1773</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Subway opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 September 1897</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Boston</b> is the capital of Massachusetts, its largest city and its oldest, with about 673,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Old State House, put up in 1712 and 1713 as the town's second town house, is the oldest Georgian public building left in the country, and it held the offices of the Province of Massachusetts Bay and of Suffolk County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1776 until 1798 it was the seat of the Commonwealth's government, taking its present name when the new house on Beacon Hill was finished; fire had gutted it in 1747 and it was rebuilt the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Old South Meeting House, built in 1729 and 1730, seated so many that town meetings moved there when Faneuil Hall proved too small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The most consequential was the heated session of 16 December 1773 that ended in the Boston Tea Party; under the occupation the building was stripped of its fittings and used as a riding school for the King's cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Before 1805 the black inhabitants of the city worshipped in the churches of whites, and on 8 August that year the Reverend Thomas Paul and twenty others founded the First African Baptist Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Black labourers and craftsmen built its plain Federal meeting house on Beacon Hill, the oldest surviving African American church building in the country, and as the only sizeable hall in the city owned by black people it became the centre of their community as well as their worship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cut off from political life and with little schooling to be had, such congregations became a training ground for leadership, which is why the church came to hold so large a place in black American life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tremont Street Subway, opened on 1 September 1897, was the first underground public transport system in the country and only the fifth in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 50 million rides were taken on it in its first full year, and its success led at once to a similar system in New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Massachusetts","dot":"Boston"},"facts":[["State","Massachusetts"],["Population","673k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","25th largest"],["Population growth","-0.8% (2020–2024)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Massachusetts: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/massachusetts-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State House, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000779_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old South Meeting House, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000778_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “African Meeting House, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000087_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tremont Street Subway, Boston, Massachusetts,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000788_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Flag_of_Boston.svg","credit":"City of Boston; vectorised by Svgalbertian, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Boston.svg)","alt":"A light blue flag bearing the round city seal — a view of the harbour town ringed by a gold band with a Latin motto, over the city’s Latin foundation inscription and the date 1630."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Boston_skyline_from_East_Boston_November_2016_panorama_5.jpg/1920px-Boston_skyline_from_East_Boston_November_2016_panorama_5.jpg","title":"Boston","desc":"Boston, seen from a distance.","credit":"King of Hearts, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boston_skyline_from_East_Boston_November_2016_panorama_5.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Boston."}},
{"id":"geo-023","num":23,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Washington","answerText":"Washington","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Columbia River</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reached in 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">San Juan crisis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1889</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Washington</b> is the 18th largest of the American states, covering 184,661 square kilometres (71,298 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 8 million residents and its largest city at Seattle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its archaeological record documents Interior and Coast Salish habitation stretching back thousands of years, and it was granted statehood on 11 November 1889 after several decades as a territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1775 the Spanish navigator Bruno Heceta sailed into a great bay he named Assumption and noted in his log that its currents made him think it the mouth of some great river, but he was swept back out into the Pacific before he could look closer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American captain and fur trader Robert Gray found the Columbia in 1792, the climax of a long search for the legendary Great River of the West, and it gave the United States a claim to the Pacific Northwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That led on to the Lewis and Clark expedition of 1804 to 1806 and to Fort Astoria in 1811, and provoked British exploration and fur posts in answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The quarrel over who owned the San Juan archipelago came to a head in 1859, by which time 25 Americans had settled there and the Hudson's Bay Company had some 5,000 sheep and 20 employees on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> General Winfield Scott, then 74, proposed a joint military and naval occupation until the title could be settled, each side keeping a hundred men on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two nations held armed camps there for thirteen years, and the sovereignty was resolved only by the Treaty of Washington of 1871 and the German Emperor's arbitration that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pioneer Square is the heart of old Seattle and holds some of the handsomest Richardsonian Romanesque fronts in the north-west, the best preserved being the Pioneer Building by Elmer H. Fisher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Good stone being scarcer there than brick, the style was worked locally in brick with terracotta detail, what sandstone there was coming from Chuckanut, Wilkeson, Tenino and the San Juans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Washington"},"facts":[["Capital","Olympia"],["Population","7.96M (2024)"],["Largest city","Seattle"],["Area","184,661 km² (71,298 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Washington: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/washington-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Chinook Point, Pacific County, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000747_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “American and English Camps, San Juan Island, Washington,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000369_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pioneer Building, Pergola, and Totem Pole, Seattle, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/77001340_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Flag_of_Washington.svg","credit":"Washington Secretary of State, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Washington.svg)","alt":"A dark green flag bearing the state seal in the middle — a portrait bust of George Washington in a gold ring lettered with the state's name and the date 1889."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Rainier20200906.jpg/1920px-Rainier20200906.jpg","title":"Mount Rainier, Washington","desc":"Mount Rainier.","credit":"Caleb Riston, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainier20200906.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Rainier."}},
{"id":"geo-520","num":520,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Columbus","answerText":"Columbus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehouse bill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 January 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ace of Aces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 aircraft, 1918</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Columbus</b> is the capital of Ohio and its largest city, with about 933,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town's fear of losing the capital ended on 26 January 1838, when the new statehouse bill passed and the shops shut for a day of drinking and dancing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The competition the commissioners then staged drew more than sixty entries, one of them Gothic, though the panic of 1837 had not subsided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Work stopped for years, the foundations were covered over with earth and neighbours grazed their cows on the Capitol mound, and in 1843 a resolution to move the capital away passed the Senate and was stopped only with great effort in the House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city secured it at last in the winter of 1846 with the Act to Provide for the Erection of a New State House, in which the legislature appropriated no money but turned the whole workforce of the state penitentiary over to the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Capitol was not occupied until 1857, and it is reckoned one of the outstanding statements of Greek Revival architecture in America, its scale and daring simplicity among the most distinguished in native American design.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Ohio Theatre was the work of Thomas White Lamb, who began in 1909 designing for William Fox in New York and built more than 300 theatres in styles from Adam and French Baroque to Hindu and Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is one of the few picture palaces still refurbished and in use, a gilded extravaganza offering escape from the realities of the Depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eddie Rickenbacker grew up in a plain frame house in the city, formed his interest in motor cars and aeroplanes there, and came home to it from the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One of the country's leading racing drivers before the war, he flew in the first combat mission ordered by an American commander of an American squadron, shot down 26 German aircraft in under six months to become the American Ace of Aces, and afterwards bought Eastern Air Lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Ohio","dot":"Columbus"},"facts":[["State","Ohio"],["Population","933k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","15th largest"],["Population growth","+3.0% (2020–2024)"]],"questions":[],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Flag_of_Columbus%2C_Ohio.svg","credit":"City of Columbus, Ohio; vectorised by Alex43223, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Columbus,_Ohio.svg)","alt":"A vertical tricolour of gold, white and crimson, the white band bearing the round city seal — an eagle over a shield with a sailing ship, ringed by stars and a laurel wreath."},"sources":["Library of Congress, “Ohio: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/ohio-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ohio Statehouse, Columbus, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72001011_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ohio Theatre, Columbus, Ohio,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001437_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Captain Edward V. Rickenbacker House, Columbus, Ohio,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001426_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Columbus_Downtown_skyline_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Columbus_Downtown_skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Columbus","desc":"Columbus, Ohio, seen from a distance.","credit":"Trëxo, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columbus_Downtown_skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Columbus, Ohio."}},
{"id":"geo-024","num":24,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Oregon","answerText":"Oregon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Migration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1843</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1846</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Admitted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Timberline Lodge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1935 – 1938</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Oregon</b> is the 9th largest of the American states, covering 254,799 square kilometres (98,379 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 4.3 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Salem and its largest city Portland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first wagons went west from Missouri in 1836, when a missionary party under Marcus and Narcissa Whitman set out for the Willamette valley and had to abandon them 320 kilometres (200 miles) short of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the spring of 1843 a wagon train of nearly a thousand people gathered at Independence, Missouri, and the Great Migration reached Oregon Country safely against a chorus of doubters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its success turned on South Pass, a valley 19 kilometres (12 miles) wide that was virtually the only place between the plains and Oregon where wagons could cross the Rocky Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1846 so many Americans had settled the region that Britain ceded Oregon Country to the United States,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and Congress passed the act admitting Oregon to the Union in 1859.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Placer gold was found at Rich Gulch on Jackson Creek in the Rogue River valley late in December 1851 or early in January 1852, by two prospectors from California.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Jacksonville sprang up that summer as a tent city of 500, and by 8 January 1853, when it became the seat of Jackson County, it held 900 people and up to 200 frame buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The mines drew Chinese labourers east of the Cascades, and at John Day the Kam Wah Chung Company building, a trading post of about 1866 on The Dalles Military Road, became the commercial, social and spiritual centre of the settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Ing Hay, a trained herbal doctor and pulsologist who reached the region in 1885, went into partnership there in early 1887 with Lung On, and the rooms and papers left behind are perhaps the best collection of their kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On the south slope of Mount Hood at about 1,800 metres (6,000 feet), Timberline Lodge was built between 1935 and 1938 by artists and craftsmen of the Works Progress Administration, and Franklin D. Roosevelt dedicated it in 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oregon"},"facts":[["Capital","Salem"],["Population","4.27M (2024)"],["Largest city","Portland"],["Area","254,799 km² (98,379 sq mi)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture,” Oregon National Historic Trail, https://www.nps.gov/oreg/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Congress, “An Act for the Admission of Oregon into the Union,” 35th Cong., 2nd sess., 11 Stat. 383, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-11/pdf/STATUTE-11-Pg383.pdf. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jacksonville Historic District, Jackson County, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000950_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Kam Wah Chung Company Building, John Day, Oregon,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001575_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Timberline Lodge, Government Camp, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001572_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Flag_of_Oregon.svg","credit":"User:Denelson83, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Oregon.svg)","alt":"A navy blue flag lettered STATE OF OREGON in gold above a gold shield ringed with stars, showing a covered wagon, oxen, a sunset over the sea and ships, with the year 1859 below it."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Crater_Lake_winter_pano2.jpg/1920px-Crater_Lake_winter_pano2.jpg","title":"Crater Lake, Oregon","desc":"Crater Lake.","credit":"WolfmanSF, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crater_Lake_winter_pano2.jpg","alt":"A view of Crater Lake."}},
{"id":"geo-521","num":521,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Springfield","answerText":"Springfield","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839 – 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">House Divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 June 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1874</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dana House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1902</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Springfield</b> is the capital of Illinois and its 7th largest city, with about 113,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Old State Capitol, designed by John F. Rague, served as the state's fifth capitol from 1839 until 1876, and Abraham Lincoln sat in the legislature there from December 1840.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He pleaded more than 200 cases in the building's Supreme Court room, and on 16 June 1858, accepting the Republican nomination for the Senate, he made the House Divided speech from the floor of that hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Through the presidential campaign of 1860 he worked from the governor's office in the same building, which stayed his headquarters until he left for Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stephen A. Douglas, three times sent to the Senate by the legislature meeting there and first at 34, spoke in the hall on 25 April 1861 at his old rival's request, rallying southern Illinois for the Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The plain frame house at Eighth and Jackson streets is the only one Lincoln ever owned, bought in January 1844 from the Reverend Charles Dresser, who had married him to Mary Todd two years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was home for sixteen years apart from his term in Congress, grew from a one-storey cottage into a full two storeys of twelve rooms, and none of the family lived in it again after the grand levee of 6 February 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lincoln lay in state in the capitol the night before his burial on 3 May 1865,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the citizens of Springfield organised the National Lincoln Monument Association at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Work on the tomb began in 1869 and it was dedicated in 1874, an attempt to kidnap the body for ransom was foiled in 1876, and in 1901 the casket was moved into a cement vault 3 metres (10 feet) below the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Frank Lloyd Wright designed the Dana House in 1902 and built it around the family's old home, pulling that down as he went but keeping one room intact, as a condition of the owner's inheritance required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Illinois","dot":"Springfield"},"facts":[["State","Illinois"],["Population","113k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","279th largest"],["Population growth","-1.4% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Illinois: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/illinois-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State Capitol, Springfield, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000331_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Lincoln Home, Springfield, Illinois,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000076_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lincoln Tomb, Springfield, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000330_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Susan Lawrence Dana House, Springfield, Illinois,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000774_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Flag_of_Springfield%2C_Illinois.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Springfield,_Illinois.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a large red star outlined in white within a ring of small white stars, the city and state named in gold letters along the bottom."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Downtown_Springfield_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Springfield","desc":"Springfield, Illinois, seen from a distance.","credit":"spfldnet, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Springfield_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Springfield, Illinois."}},
{"id":"geo-025","num":25,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Arizona","answerText":"Arizona","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pueblo Grande</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 – 1450 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mission founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1700</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silver strike</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1877</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 February 1912</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Arizona</b> is the 6th largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 295,234 square kilometres (113,990 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.6 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Phoenix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reached statehood on 14 February 1912, the last of the 48 conterminous states to be admitted, and women won the vote there that same year, eight years before the country as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hohokam culture of the southern desert began about 100 BCE, kept to the desert valleys and lived by intensive irrigation farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Pueblo Grande, now inside Phoenix, a village of those farmers raised a platform building 6 metres (20 feet) high covering more than a hectare (3 acres), with a ball court and large canals heading water out of the Salt River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The site was lived in from about 1 to 1450 CE, which is what makes it a record of the whole development of the culture rather than of one moment in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Mission San Xavier del Bac was founded in 1700 by the Jesuit Eusebio Francisco Kino, at a place then described as beyond the rim of Christendom and the furthest northern reach of Spain's empire in America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The present church, the third or perhaps the fourth on the site, was begun in 1783 and finished in 1797, and has stood under the flags of Spain, Mexico and the United States in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Silver was found in the San Pedro valley in 1877 by Ed Schieffelin, who had been told by troopers at Fort Huachuca that the only thing he would find out there was his tombstone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The boom town that grew on the strike took that name and became, briefly, the biggest and most notorious town in Arizona Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It is remembered less for its mines than for sixty seconds on 26 October 1881, when four men walked down Fremont Street to meet five others in a horse yard and fought the gun battle known as the OK Corral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arizona"},"facts":[["Capital","Phoenix"],["Population","7.58M (2024)"],["Largest city","Phoenix"],["Area","295,234 km² (113,990 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Arizona: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/arizona-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pueblo Grande Ruin and Irrigation Sites, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000185_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “San Xavier del Bac Mission, Tucson, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000191_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tombstone Historic District, Tombstone, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000171_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Flag_of_Arizona.svg","credit":"Madden, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Arizona.svg)","alt":"A flag whose upper half is a fan of thirteen red and gold rays and whose lower half is dark blue, with a large copper five-pointed star across the middle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Canyon_River_Tree_%28165872763%29.jpeg/1920px-Canyon_River_Tree_%28165872763%29.jpeg","title":"the Grand Canyon, Arizona","desc":"Grand Canyon.","credit":"Lennart Sikkema, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canyon_River_Tree_(165872763).jpeg","alt":"A view of Grand Canyon."}},
{"id":"geo-522","num":522,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Richmond","answerText":"Richmond","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Liberty or Death</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1775</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1785</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tredegar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd in the US, 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Penny Savings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Richmond</b> is the capital of Virginia and its 3rd largest city, with about 234,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In St John's Church on 23 March 1775 Patrick Henry made the Liberty or Death speech, which swept the Virginia Convention meeting there from a tone of conciliation to resolutions putting the colony into a state of defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thomas Jefferson designed the state Capitol while he was in France, helped by the architect Charles-Louis Clérisseau and taking as his model the Maison Carrée, a Roman temple at Nîmes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Begun in 1785 and complete in its temple form by 1798, it introduced neo-classicism to American public building and was the first use of the temple form for a public building since Roman times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Houdon's statue of George Washington in Continental uniform was set in the rotunda in 1796, and the building houses the oldest legislature in the country, grown out of the colonial House of Burgesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The same building was the meeting place of the Confederate Congress through the years the city was the Confederate capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What made it worth holding was as much iron as politics: under Joseph R. Anderson, who had run it since 1841, the Tredegar Iron Works ranked third among American ironworks in 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Short of iron and coal, Anderson bought mines, furnaces and ore boats of his own with Confederate money, building one of the earliest far-reaching vertical combinations in the American iron industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Maggie Lena Walker, born in the city in 1867 to a mother who had been enslaved, became executive secretary-treasurer of the Independent Order of Saint Luke in 1899 and in 25 years took it from 57 chapters to 1,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1903 she led the order into banking with the Saint Luke Penny Savings Bank, the first American woman known to found and head a bank, and she ran it as president and then as chairman until her death in 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Virginia","dot":"Richmond"},"facts":[["State","Virginia"],["Population","234k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","98th largest"],["Population growth","+3.1% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Virginia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/virginia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “St. John’s Episcopal Church, Richmond, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000920_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Virginia State Capitol, Richmond, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000911_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tredegar Iron Works, Richmond, Virginia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71001048_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Maggie Lena Walker House, Richmond, Virginia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75002100_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Flag_of_Richmond%2C_Virginia.svg","credit":"HoosierMan1816, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Richmond,_Virginia.svg)","alt":"A flag with a dark blue field above white and red bars, carrying the white silhouette of a boatman poling a flat boat through waves, ringed by ten white stars."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Richmond%2C_Virginia_skyline_01.jpg/1920px-Richmond%2C_Virginia_skyline_01.jpg","title":"Richmond","desc":"Richmond, Virginia, seen from a distance.","credit":"Ɱ, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richmond,_Virginia_skyline_01.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Richmond, Virginia."}},
{"id":"geo-026","num":26,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"New Mexico","answerText":"New Mexico","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Taos Pueblo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 900 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">San Estevan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1629 – 1642</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 January 1912</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trinity test</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 July 1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>New Mexico</b> is the 5th largest of the American states,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> covering 314,917 square kilometres (121,590 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2.1 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Santa Fe and its largest city Albuquerque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Santa Fe has been the capital since 1610, and the territory was admitted to the Union on 6 January 1912.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taos Pueblo is the last site of a Puebloan occupation of the Taos valley reaching back to at least 900 CE, and stands as evidence of how far that people adapted to centuries of change around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest phase identified in the valley, the Valdez, is dated to about 900 to 1200 CE by cross-dating a mineral-painted ware known as Taos Black-on-white.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its sites are pithouses and pithouse villages with work areas and rooms built of jacal and adobe beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The great church of San Estevan del Rey at Acoma was built between 1629 and 1642 and repaired in 1799 and 1800, and is the finest of the Spanish colonial mission churches in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Mission architecture here is neither as baroque as the churches of Texas nor as polished as those of California: the plan and general form are Spanish, while the construction and much of the ornament are Puebloan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Los Alamos was set up in 1943 to design and build a nuclear weapon, and by the end of that year the implosion method, in which high explosive squeezes a subcritical mass of plutonium to supercriticality, had reached a point where a test was unavoidable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Testing it meant spending the country's entire supply of plutonium, so the first task was to devise a way of recovering the metal should the device fail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The world's first nuclear device was fired on 16 July 1945 at what is now the White Sands Missile Range, on the ground called the Trinity Site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Mexico"},"facts":[["Capital","Santa Fe"],["Population","2.13M (2024)"],["Largest city","Albuquerque"],["Area","314,917 km² (121,590 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “New Mexico: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-mexico-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Taos Pueblo, Taos County, New Mexico,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000496_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “San Estevan del Rey Mission Church, Acoma, New Mexico,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000417_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Trinity Site, Socorro County, New Mexico,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000493_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Flag_of_New_Mexico.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Mexico.svg)","alt":"A plain yellow flag bearing a red sun symbol in the centre, a circle with four groups of four rays reaching out from it."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/2026_White_Sands_National_Park_Dunes_Clouds_and_Sun.jpg/1920px-2026_White_Sands_National_Park_Dunes_Clouds_and_Sun.jpg","title":"White Sands, New Mexico","desc":"The gypsum dunes of White Sands.","credit":"Pablorgalindo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2026_White_Sands_National_Park_Dunes_Clouds_and_Sun.jpg","alt":"White dunes stretching to the horizon under a broken sky."}},
{"id":"geo-523","num":523,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Olympia","answerText":"Olympia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First legislature</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 February 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bigelow House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pioneer Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in use from 1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">bought 1901</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Olympia</b> is the capital of Washington and the 24th largest city in the state, with about 56,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Territorial Governor Isaac I. Stevens divided the territory into legislative and judicial districts, called an election for 20 January 1854 and fixed the first legislature to meet a month later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That session met in a hired room above a restaurant, ratified the choice of Olympia as the seat of government and drew up a code of laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second met in the Masonic Hall in December 1854, and Edmond Sylvester, who had founded the town, gave the territory 4.9 hectares (12 acres) on Capitol Hill to build on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pioneer Capitol that followed measured 12 by 24 metres (40 by 80 feet), a two-storey wooden building like a New England colonial church with a small tower and no steeple, the House meeting above and the Council below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It was still in use at statehood and at the constitutional convention of 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Foundations for a new capitol were laid in 1893 but the depression stopped the work, and in 1901 the legislature bought the Thurston County Courthouse for $350,000 instead, making it the state's second permanent Capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ernest Flagg's design for the campus had got no further than its foundation, and when he was called back he proposed a group of buildings rather than one, so that work could go on as space was needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The commission took the idea and not the architect: a national competition drew 37 designs, and Wilder and White of New York won it with a plan built round a large domed building meant to read from a distance as one broad base under the dome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Among the oldest buildings left in the town is the Bigelow House of 1854, Gothic Revival, home of Daniel R. Bigelow, a pioneer lawyer who sat in that first territorial legislature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Washington","dot":"Olympia"},"facts":[["State","Washington"],["Population","56.3k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","730th largest"],["Population growth","+1.6% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Washington: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/washington-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol Building, Olympia, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001877_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Washington State Capitol Historic District, Olympia, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79002564_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Daniel R. Bigelow House, Olympia, Washington,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79002562_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Olympia%2C_WA_Aerial_Scene.jpg/1920px-Olympia%2C_WA_Aerial_Scene.jpg","title":"Olympia","desc":"Olympia, Washington, seen from a distance.","credit":"Libbyflyguy, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olympia,_WA_Aerial_Scene.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Olympia, Washington."}},
{"id":"geo-027","num":27,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Georgia","answerText":"Georgia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Colony founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1732</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Savannah settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1733</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Echota</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1825 – 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nobel Prize</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1964</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Georgia</b> is the 24th largest of the American states, covering 153,910 square kilometres (59,425 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Atlanta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The colony was established in 1732 and settled at Savannah the year after, the last of the thirteen to be founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was also the only one founded and ruled by a board of trustees sitting in London, so that for its first two decades there was no governor or governing body in the colony itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that, Etowah stood above its river in the north of the state, a Mississippian centre of three large platform mounds and a village inside an encircling ditch that a palisade once ran beside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It served the smaller villages around it, and is one of three sites in the country to give extensive evidence of the Southeastern Ceremonial Complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> New Echota, called New Town until 1825, was the capital of the Cherokee Nation for the thirteen years to 1838, its government patterned on that of the United States and its territory reaching across the north of the state into three neighbouring ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Cherokee Phoenix, edited by Elias Boudinot, was the first newspaper printed in a Native American language, set in English and in the syllabary Sequoyah had devised in 1821, and it appeared from 21 February 1828 until 1834.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those years ended in the signing of a treaty that led to the removal of the Cherokee to the west and to the near-total disappearance of the town itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Martin Luther King Jr. was born in his grandfather's house at 501 Auburn Avenue in Atlanta and was raised in that community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He was pastor of the Dexter Avenue and Ebenezer Baptist churches, stood at the front of the civil rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s, organised the largest marches the country had seen and was given the Nobel Peace Prize in 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Georgia"},"facts":[["Capital","Atlanta"],["Population","11.2M (2024)"],["Largest city","Atlanta"],["Area","153,910 km² (59,425 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Georgia: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/georgia-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Etowah Mounds, Bartow County, Georgia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000272_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “New Echota, Gordon County, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000869_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Martin Luther King Jr. Historic District, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000677_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Flag_of_the_State_of_Georgia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_State_of_Georgia.svg)","alt":"A flag of three broad bars, red above white above red, with a blue canton carrying the state arms in gold within a ring of thirteen white stars."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Aerial_view_of_wetlands_in_Okefenokee.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_of_wetlands_in_Okefenokee.jpg","title":"the Okefenokee Swamp, Georgia","desc":"Okefenokee Swamp.","credit":"Ryan Hagerty, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_wetlands_in_Okefenokee.jpg","alt":"A view of Okefenokee Swamp."}},
{"id":"geo-524","num":524,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Salem","answerText":"Salem","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Methodist mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1834</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Willamette</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First mill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kay mill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1896</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Salem</b> is the capital of Oregon and its 2nd largest city, with about 180,000 people, some 80 kilometres (50 miles) south of Portland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Oregon was the domain of the fur trade until 1834, when the Reverend Jason Lee and a small band of Methodist missionaries set up a station on the Willamette about 16 kilometres (10 miles) north of the present city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was the first mission established anywhere in the American north-west, and it ran satellite stations at The Dalles, Clatsop Plains, Oregon City and south of Puget Sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The mission expanded to Salem in 1841, where a house for Lee and a parsonage for the preacher in charge were put up, and those white-painted frame buildings are the only structures left from the mission period anywhere in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The preacher in charge also directed the Indian Manual Labor Training School, the forerunner of Willamette University.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Willamette was founded by those missionaries and settlers in 1842, which makes it the oldest institution of higher learning west of the Missouri River, and the territorial legislature gave it that name in 1853.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Waller Hall, its oldest surviving building, was a considerable undertaking for the 1860s and was begun only after four years of collecting the money for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mechanised woollen industry of the Pacific coast dates from 1857, when the first full textile factory on that coast opened here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thomas Kay, Squire Farrar and C. P. Bishop founded their own woollen mill in 1889, and when fire destroyed its frame building in 1895 the present mill was finished the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For a time it was the largest plant of its kind in the state, and it stayed under the family's ownership and management until 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Oregon","dot":"Salem"},"facts":[["State","Oregon"],["Population","180k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","148th largest"],["Population growth","+2.8% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["National Park Service, “Salem Downtown State Street–Commercial Street Historic District, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/01001067_text. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jason Lee House, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001580_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Waller Hall, Willamette University, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001593_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Thomas Kay Woolen Mill, Salem, Oregon,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001579_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Flag_of_Salem%2C_Oregon_2022.svg","credit":"JKeaglePhD, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Salem,_Oregon_2022.svg)","alt":"The flag of Salem: a blue vertical band at the hoist beside a white field bearing a large pink cherry blossom, with a white seven-pointed star cut out of its centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Salem_Oregon_aerial.jpg/1920px-Salem_Oregon_aerial.jpg","title":"Salem","desc":"Salem, Oregon, seen from a distance.","credit":"Oregon Department of Transportation, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salem_Oregon_aerial.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Salem, Oregon."}},
{"id":"geo-028","num":28,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Carolina","answerText":"South Carolina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Charlesfort</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1562 – 1563</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Santa Elena</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1566 – 1587</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stono rising</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 September 1739</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 May 1788</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>South Carolina</b> is the 40th largest of the American states, covering 82,933 square kilometres (32,020 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.5 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Columbia and its largest city Charleston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the thirteen original colonies, it was named Carolina after King Charles I, divided from North Carolina in 1710, and ratified the Constitution on 23 May 1788 as the eighth state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two centuries before that its coast was contested ground: the French under Jean Ribault built Charlesfort in 1562 and gave it up three years before the Spanish arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pedro Menéndez de Avilés founded Santa Elena on the same ground in 1566 as an outpost against French intrusion into Spanish Florida, and the town was that province's capital until 1587.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At least one French fort and five Spanish ones were built in the vicinity of the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> By the Revolution it was reckoned one of the richest colonies in America, its merchants and planters forming a strong governing class, and more battles and skirmishes of that war were fought here than in any other state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wealth rested on slavery, and on 9 and 10 September 1739, some 32 kilometres (20 miles) south-west of Charleston, the Stono rising became one of the most serious insurrections of the colonial period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Witnesses said the rebels marched like a disciplined company, and the conditions behind it were plain enough: many of the enslaved had been brought from Africa recently and kept much of their own culture, they far outnumbered the white population, and the Spanish in Florida were a standing military threat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When Union forces took St Helena Island in 1861 the planters fled and left the Gullah people, who had kept their own language and customs, and northern missionaries arrived the next year to teach them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Robert Smalls, born into slavery at Beaufort in 1839, made himself nationally famous by carrying off the steamer <i>Planter</i>, then sat in the state assembly from 1868 and in the senate from 1870 before going to Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Carolina"},"facts":[["Capital","Columbia"],["Population","5.48M (2024)"],["Largest city","Charleston"],["Area","82,933 km² (32,020 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “South Carolina: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/south-carolina-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Charlesfort–Santa Elena Site, Beaufort County, South Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001822_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Stono River Slave Rebellion Site, Charleston County, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001840_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Penn School Historic District, St. Helena Island, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001824_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Robert Smalls House, Beaufort, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001823_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Flag_of_South_Carolina.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Carolina.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a white crescent in the upper hoist corner and a white palmetto tree in the middle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Baldcypress_%28Taxodium_distichum%29_on_Congaree_National_Park_Low_Boardwalk_trail.jpg/1920px-Baldcypress_%28Taxodium_distichum%29_on_Congaree_National_Park_Low_Boardwalk_trail.jpg","title":"Congaree National Park, South Carolina","desc":"Congaree National Park.","credit":"Miguel.v, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baldcypress_(Taxodium_distichum)_on_Congaree_National_Park_Low_Boardwalk_trail.jpg","alt":"A view of Congaree National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-525","num":525,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Phoenix","answerText":"Phoenix","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Canal dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Townsite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1870</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indian School</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1891</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Phoenix</b> is the capital of Arizona and its largest city, with about 1.7 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under the modern city lies Pueblo Grande, where Hohokam farmers raised a platform building 6 metres (20 feet) high with a ball court beside it and dug large canals to lead water out of the Salt River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No settlement other than theirs was made in the area through the Spanish and Mexican periods, and the land passed to the United States after the war with Mexico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anglo settlers arrived only in the 1860s: farmers moved into the valley in 1867 and had dug a canal and harvested a crop by the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1870 the settlement held 240 people and was moved 3 kilometres (two miles) west, where the first official townsite was laid out, and in 1871 it became the seat of Maricopa County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The territorial capital wandered for 25 years before it settled here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It began at Fort Whipple in the Chino valley, moved to Prescott in April 1864 because of Confederate sympathies around Tucson, went to Tucson itself in 1867 when the southern counties had the larger population, and returned to Prescott in 1877.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Phoenix took it for good in 1889, the legislature and territorial offices sitting at first in the municipal buildings until the business of the territory outgrew them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Phoenix Indian School, founded in 1891, was the only federal boarding school in Arizona away from a reservation and by 1900 the second largest in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It belonged to the policy of assimilation that governed federal Indian education from the late 1880s to the 1930s, and its outing system placed pupils as day labourers in the growing city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arizona","dot":"Phoenix"},"facts":[["State","Arizona"],["Population","1.67M (2024)"],["Rank in the country","5th largest"],["Population growth","+4.0% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pueblo Grande Ruin and Irrigation Sites, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000185_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Phoenix Townsite, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78000550_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Arizona State Capitol Building, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000455_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Phoenix Indian School Historic District, Phoenix, Arizona,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/01000521_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Flag_of_Phoenix%2C_Arizona.svg/1280px-Flag_of_Phoenix%2C_Arizona.svg.png","credit":"City of Phoenix, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Phoenix,_Arizona.svg)","alt":"A deep maroon flag bearing a large white disc in which a stylised bird rises with its wings curving round to close the circle."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Downtown_Phoenix_Skyline_%286974043971%29.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Phoenix_Skyline_%286974043971%29.jpg","title":"Phoenix","desc":"Phoenix, Arizona, seen from a distance.","credit":"Alan Stark from Goodyear, AZ, United States, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Phoenix_Skyline_(6974043971).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Phoenix, Arizona."}},
{"id":"geo-029","num":29,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"North Carolina","answerText":"North Carolina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1799</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mint gold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">through 1828</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NC Mutual</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 October 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First flight</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>North Carolina</b> is the 28th largest of the American states, covering 139,391 square kilometres (53,819 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 11 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Raleigh and its largest city Charlotte.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the twelfth state after the Revolution and lies on the Atlantic coast midway between New York and Florida, bounded by Virginia, South Carolina, Georgia and Tennessee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold was found on John Reed's farm along Little Meadow Creek in 1799, which opened the earliest documented gold mining within the present United States and led to the country's first gold rush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Through 1828 all the native gold coined by the United States Mint came from North Carolina, and largely from Cabarrus and the counties around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A strike near Dahlonega in 1829 spread the fever across the southern Appalachians, whose fields produced somewhere between $50 million and $65 million before 1860.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Eclipsed by California and largely worked out by 1860, those fields had still made an industry and trained the miners who went on to open the West.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Seven black men met at Durham on 20 October 1898 to organise the North Carolina Mutual Life Insurance Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It grew out of the mutual benefit societies and fraternal orders that had offered life insurance since the 18th century and had become, after the church, the most important social institution in African American life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only two of the seven lasted beyond the first hard year, and most of the credit for organising the firm belongs to John Merrick, a former slave and a prosperous barber in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1903 the Wright brothers flew the first aeroplane at Kitty Hawk, a flight of 37 metres (120 feet) in 12 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Carolina"},"facts":[["Capital","Raleigh"],["Population","11.0M (2024)"],["Largest city","Charlotte"],["Area","139,391 km² (53,819 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “North Carolina: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/north-carolina-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Reed Gold Mine, Cabarrus County, North Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000587_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “North Carolina Mutual Life Insurance Company Building, Durham, North Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75001258_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/Flag_of_North_Carolina.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Carolina.svg)","alt":"A flag with a blue vertical band at the hoist bearing a white star between the letters N and C above two dated scrolls, beside a red bar above a white one."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Cape_Hatteras_Lighthouse_December_2020.jpg/1920px-Cape_Hatteras_Lighthouse_December_2020.jpg","title":"Cape Hatteras, North Carolina","desc":"Cape Hatteras Lighthouse.","credit":"Jschildk, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Hatteras_Lighthouse_December_2020.jpg","alt":"A tall lighthouse painted in a black and white spiral."}},
{"id":"geo-526","num":526,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Santa Fe","answerText":"Santa Fe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">winter 1609 – 10</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Barrio de Analco</span><span class=\"dt-v\">settled 1620</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pueblo Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1680 – 1693</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Santa Fe</b> is the capital of New Mexico and its 4th largest city, with about 91,000 people,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and has been the capital since 1610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Don Pedro de Peralta, third governor of the province of Nuevo México, established the settlement in the winter of 1609 and 1610 with a palacio for a fortress, a plaza laid out before it and a wall around the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The wall did not survive; the palace did, and houses for the Spanish officers and officials went up on the other three sides of the plaza.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That building, the Palace of the Governors, is one of the oldest public buildings of European origin in America and has been in constant use since the early 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For nearly three hundred years it was the seat of government in what its nomination calls the oldest continuously occupied capital in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the Pueblo Revolt the Spanish were driven out, and for the thirteen years from 1680 to 1693 Pueblo people from Galisteo, Cienega and San Marcos lived in the casa real itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A fragment of adobe wall dug out beneath one room in 1962 is thought to date from that occupation rather than from any earlier pueblo, and storage pits found in the 1970s are believed to be theirs as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Barrio de Analco, settled in 1620 and rebuilt in the 18th century, is one of the oldest neighbourhoods of European inception in what is now the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its name means 'the other side of the water', the far bank of the Santa Fe River from the quarter where officials and prominent citizens lived and had their own chapel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To the traders who came up the Santa Fe Trail the plaza was the goal of the whole trek, the place where they parked their wagons, unloaded them and laid out their goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Mexico","dot":"Santa Fe"},"facts":[["State","New Mexico"],["Population","90.6k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","390th largest"],["Population growth","+3.5% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Palace of the Governors, Santa Fe, New Mexico,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000489_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Santa Fe Plaza, Santa Fe, New Mexico,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000491_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Barrio De Analco Historic District, Santa Fe, New Mexico,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/68000032_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “New Mexico: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-mexico-state-guide. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Flag_of_Santa_Fe%2C_New_Mexico.png","credit":"City of Santa Fe, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico.png)","alt":"The flag of Santa Fe: on gold, a cream disc lettered LA VILLA REAL DE LA SANTA FE DE SAN FRANCISCO DE ASIS in blue around a shield of castle, lion and eagle above stars and stripes, with ribbons reading 1610, 1821 and 1846."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Panoramic_view_of_downtown_Santa_Fe%2C_New_Mexico.jpg/1920px-Panoramic_view_of_downtown_Santa_Fe%2C_New_Mexico.jpg","title":"Santa Fe","desc":"Santa Fe, New Mexico, seen from a distance.","credit":"Road Travel America, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramic_view_of_downtown_Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Santa Fe, New Mexico."}},
{"id":"geo-030","num":30,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Pennsylvania","answerText":"Pennsylvania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Meadowcroft</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 16,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Colony founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1681</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Valley Forge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1777 – 1778</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drake's well</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 August 1859</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pennsylvania</b> is the 33rd largest of the American states, covering 119,280 square kilometres (46,054 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 13.1 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Harrisburg and its largest city Philadelphia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the original thirteen colonies, it was established in 1681 by William Penn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philadelphia was both the colony's capital and the first capital of the new nation during the Revolution, but it held neither honour for long: the state moved its business to Lancaster in 1799, and Harrisburg became its enduring capital in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meadowcroft Rockshelter, on the north bank of Cross Creek about 48 kilometres (30 miles) south-west of Pittsburgh, was used and reused as a short-term hunting and gathering camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Material from its lower levels has been radiocarbon dated to 16,000 BP and pre-Clovis times, and it is those remains and the well-stratified sequence above them that make the shelter exceptional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The bitter winter of 1777 and 1778 at Valley Forge was endured by the ragged and hungry troops who staggered into camp on 19 December 1777.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The training and discipline imposed there made a force that could meet a professional enemy on equal terms, and news of the French alliance was announced on the Grand Parade on 6 May 1778.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Pennsylvania Rock Oil Company sent Edwin L. Drake to look at its property near Titusville in December 1857, and he concluded that only drilling would produce oil in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He hired a driller who had worked on salt wells and raised a derrick housing a steam engine that drove a white oak battering ram into the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Drake struck oil on 27 August 1859, the world's first oil well, and the petroleum boom that followed has only grown since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Pennsylvania"},"facts":[["Capital","Harrisburg"],["Population","13.1M (2024)"],["Largest city","Philadelphia"],["Area","119,280 km² (46,054 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Pennsylvania: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/pennsylvania-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Meadowcroft Rockshelter, Washington County, Pennsylvania,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/78002480_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Valley Forge, Chester and Montgomery Counties, Pennsylvania,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000657_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Drake Oil Well, Titusville, Pennsylvania,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000695_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Flag_of_Pennsylvania.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pennsylvania.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state arms in the middle: a shield with a ship, a plough and three sheaves, held between two rearing black horses under an eagle, above a red scroll."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Independence_Hall.jpg/1920px-Independence_Hall.jpg","title":"Independence Hall, Pennsylvania","desc":"Independence Hall.","credit":"Rdsmith4, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Independence_Hall.jpg","alt":"A view of Independence Hall."}},
{"id":"geo-527","num":527,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Atlanta","answerText":"Atlanta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appropriation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">$1 million, 1883</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">finished 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Herndon Home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">planned 1906</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fox Theatre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1929</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Atlanta</b> is the capital of Georgia and its largest city, with about 520,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has had five authorised capitals and four capitol buildings, three of them built by the state itself: one at Louisville in the 18th century, one at Milledgeville in 1807 and the present one here, finished in 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city gave the state the five-acre City Hall Lot in the middle of town to build it on, and in 1883 the legislature appropriated a million dollars for the work under a board of five commissioners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Wesley Dobbs coined the name Sweet Auburn for Auburn Avenue, which was called 'the richest Negro street in the world'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its success was bound up with the residential pattern forced on black Atlantans by the rise of Jim Crow, so that the district stands both as a result of segregation and as a case for the merits of black enterprise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Alonzo F. Herndon was the wealthiest black man in the city: he ran its largest barber shop, was president of an insurance company, and owned and let some fifty houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From about 1906 his first wife, Adrienne Herndon, planned the family a house beside Atlanta University, overlooking the city from the west, with a terraced roof garden and rooms meant for entertaining.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> W. E. B. Du Bois eulogised him in <i>The Crisis</i> in 1927 as a man whose money had bought him power and whose friendships had kept it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Fox Theatre, a movie palace the size of a city block, opened on Christmas Day 1929 at the height of that building type's golden age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It had been planned as a mosque for some 5,000 members of a shriners' order, and was enlarged at the suggestion of the film magnate William Fox into an auditorium ringed with shops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Georgia","dot":"Atlanta"},"facts":[["State","Georgia"],["Population","520k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","36th largest"],["Population growth","+4.3% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Georgia State Capitol, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71001099_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sweet Auburn Historic District, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76000631_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Herndon Home, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/00000261_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fox Theatre, Atlanta, Georgia,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74002230_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/Flag_of_Atlanta%2C_Georgia.svg","credit":"Devinkelly, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Atlanta,_Georgia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Atlanta: on dark blue, a gold circular seal showing a phoenix rising from flames with wings spread, RESURGENS above and ATLANTA, GA. below, and the dates 1847 and 1865 at either side."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Aerial_of_Downtown_Atlanta%2C_GA.jpg/1920px-Aerial_of_Downtown_Atlanta%2C_GA.jpg","title":"Atlanta","desc":"Atlanta, seen from a distance.","credit":"Ron Reiring, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_of_Downtown_Atlanta,_GA.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Atlanta."}},
{"id":"geo-031","num":31,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Wisconsin","answerText":"Wisconsin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Effigy mounds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 1000 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cornish miners</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 May 1848</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wisconsin</b> is the 23rd largest of the American states, covering 169,635 square kilometres (65,496 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.0 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Madison and its largest city Milwaukee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nearly a fifth of that area is water, and most of it is not the state's own: 24,195 square kilometres (9,342 square miles) is its share of the Great Lakes, against 5,172 square kilometres (1,997 square miles) of inland lakes and rivers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the Union on 29 May 1848 and is nicknamed the Badger State and America's Dairyland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between about 700 and 1000 CE people of the Effigy Mound Culture raised earth mounds across the region in the shapes of animals and in linear and conical forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Man Mound near Baraboo is a rare human figure among them, and the only monumental representation of the human form surviving from prehistoric times in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When Congress created Wisconsin Territory in 1836 the old lead region of the south-west was its most populous corner, and Governor Henry Dodge chose Belmont as the territorial capital on that ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The legislature sat there for one session of 46 days, from 25 October to 9 December 1836, and used it to pick Madison instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Unemployed tin miners had begun coming to Mineral Point from Cornwall in 1830, and they built stone cottages along Shake Rag Street below the ridge where they dug their lead pits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Dairying was already becoming the leading agricultural pursuit in the state when William Henry took the university's chair of agriculture in 1880 and began testing the butterfat content of Jersey and Holstein milk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Stephen Babcock's butterfat test of 1890 revolutionised the dairy industry worldwide, and the cold-curing process worked out beside it made Wisconsin a leading producer of high-quality cheddar cheese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wisconsin"},"facts":[["Capital","Madison"],["Population","5.96M (2024)"],["Largest city","Milwaukee"],["Area","169,635 km² (65,496 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Wisconsin: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/wisconsin-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Man Mound, Sauk County, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78000138_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Capitol, Belmont, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000036_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pendarvis, Mineral Point, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000038_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “University of Wisconsin Dairy Barn, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/02000600_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Flag_of_Wisconsin.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wisconsin.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag with the state name above and the year 1848 below, and between them the state arms: a sailor and a miner flanking a quartered gold shield, beneath a badger and a scroll reading Forward."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Apostle_Island_Sea_Cave_in_Winter.jpg/1920px-Apostle_Island_Sea_Cave_in_Winter.jpg","title":"the Apostle Islands, Wisconsin","desc":"Apostle Islands.","credit":"Jeff the quiet, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apostle_Island_Sea_Cave_in_Winter.jpg","alt":"A view of Apostle Islands."}},
{"id":"geo-528","num":528,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Columbia","answerText":"Columbia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at Columbia, 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">College</span><span class=\"dt-v\">chartered 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Secession vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 December 1860</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">February 1865</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Columbia</b> is the capital of South Carolina and its second largest city, with 144,788 residents against Charleston's 157,665.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The colony had built its first State House at Charleston in 1753; a second went up here in 1790, and the legislature held its first meeting in the new town that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> South Carolina College was chartered in 1801, an antebellum college of the classics on a campus where enslaved people worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Robert Mills, a native South Carolinian who studied under Hoban and Latrobe and became the first American-trained federal architect, designed a classical brick mansion here for the merchant Ainsley Hall, built in 1823 at the centre of a four-acre block.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hall died before it was finished and nobody ever lived in it, so the house is remembered for its architect, the designer of the Washington Monument, who served under seven presidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On Monday 17 December 1860 the South Carolina Convention of the People assembled in the First Baptist Church and declared unanimously that the state should leave the Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smallpox was raging in the city, so the delegates voted to reassemble at Charleston, where the Ordinance of Secession was adopted and signed three days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In February 1865 Sherman's army occupied Columbia, seized the contents of the plant that had been printing Confederate currency, and burned the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sherman ordered a number of public buildings put to the torch, the old State House among them, but for reasons nobody has explained he spared the unfinished new capitol that John Niernsee had been raising since 1851 out of granite from the Congaree River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The college closed during the war and reopened under Reconstruction as the University of South Carolina, a model for multiracial education, before being segregated again until 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Carolina","dot":"Columbia"},"facts":[["State","South Carolina"],["Population","145k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","194th largest"],["Population growth","+5.8% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","South Carolina General Assembly, “State House History,” South Carolina Student Connection, https://www.studentconnection.scstatehouse.gov/history. [Open access]","University of South Carolina, “Our History,” https://www.sc.edu/about/our_history/index.php. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ainsley Hall House, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000595_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Baptist Church, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000800_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Confederate Printing Plant, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79002393_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “South Carolina Statehouse, Columbia, South Carolina,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000598_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Flag_of_Columbia%2C_South_Carolina.svg","credit":"Nate Puza, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Columbia,_South_Carolina.svg)","alt":"The flag of Columbia: on dark navy, a sweeping band of royal blue, pale blue and white curving from the lower hoist to the upper fly, with a white seven-pointed star in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Fall_skyline_of_Columbia_SC_from_Arsenal_Hill.jpg","title":"Columbia","desc":"Columbia, South Carolina, seen from a distance.","credit":"Akhenaton06, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fall_skyline_of_Columbia_SC_from_Arsenal_Hill.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Columbia, South Carolina."}},
{"id":"geo-032","num":32,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Indiana","answerText":"Indiana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grouseland</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1804 – 1812</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tippecanoe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1811</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 December 1816</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Railway Union</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1893 – 1897</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Indiana</b> is the 38th largest of the American states, covering 94,326 square kilometres (36,420 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.9 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Indianapolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Hoosier State, it became the 19th state on 11 December 1816, with its first capital at Corydon and the seat of government moved to Indianapolis in 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> William Henry Harrison lived at Grouseland, his mansion at Vincennes, from about 1804 until 1812, the years in which he was territorial governor of Indiana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tecumseh, a Shawnee who had lived among white settlers and saw what further land sales would cost, came there to press a confederation of nations bound to sell no more, and warned that he would meet settlers with force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From Grouseland in 1811 Harrison mounted the offensive that ended at Tippecanoe, where Tecumseh's brother the Prophet was defeated and the hope of a confederation with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Eugene Debs founded the American Railway Union at Terre Haute in June 1893, wanting one organisation for every railway worker rather than one for the firemen and another for the engineers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It struck the Great Northern over a wage cut in April 1894, James J. Hill gave in after eighteen days, and by that June the union counted 150,000 members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Pullman strike followed and collapsed against the employers, a federal injunction and the arrival of soldiers, and Debs was jailed for six months for defying the injunctions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> More than 100,000 people met him at Chicago in November 1895, but the union never recovered and disbanded in June 1897, half a year after he declared for socialism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sarah Breedlove, a laundress in St Louis, went into business in 1905 with $1.50, married Charles J. Walker the next year, and by 1910 had moved her whole operation to Indianapolis, where the Walker Building housed a company employing some 3,000 women along with a theatre and a ballroom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Indiana"},"facts":[["Capital","Indianapolis"],["Population","6.92M (2024)"],["Largest city","Indianapolis"],["Area","94,326 km² (36,420 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Indiana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/indiana-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “William Henry Harrison Home (Grouseland), Vincennes, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000018_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Eugene V. Debs House, Terre Haute, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000008_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Madame C. J. Walker Building, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/80000062_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Flag_of_Indiana.svg","credit":"Paul Hadley, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Indiana.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a gold torch with rays streaming from it, ringed by thirteen gold stars with five more inside them and one larger star above the flame under the word INDIANA."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Indiana_Dunes_National_Lakeshore%2C_Michigan_City%2C_Indiana%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2012-10-20%2C_DD_03.jpg/1920px-Indiana_Dunes_National_Lakeshore%2C_Michigan_City%2C_Indiana%2C_Estados_Unidos%2C_2012-10-20%2C_DD_03.jpg","title":"the Indiana Dunes","desc":"Indiana Dunes National Park.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indiana_Dunes_National_Lakeshore,_Michigan_City,_Indiana,_Estados_Unidos,_2012-10-20,_DD_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Indiana Dunes National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-529","num":529,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Raleigh","answerText":"Raleigh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Site chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">burned June 1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1833 – 1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Daniels</span><span class=\"dt-v\">editor, 1894 – 1948</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Raleigh</b> is the capital of North Carolina and its second largest city, with 499,825 residents against Charlotte's 943,476.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitutional convention of 1787 resolved that the capital must lie within 16 kilometres (10 miles) of Isaac Hunter's tavern in Wake County, and nothing came of it for four years while opponents called the county a wilderness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In early 1792 the commissioners met at Hunter's, adjourned to the house of Senator Joel Lane, and ten days later voted to buy 400 hectares (1,000 acres) of his plantation for £1,378.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The surveyor William Christmas centred his plan on Union Square, kept for the State House, with four public parks at its corners, and the rest sold off lot by lot at auction to pay for the building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Governor Alexander Martin proposed the name, for Sir Walter Raleigh, who had sent the first English colonists to North Carolina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The state house burned in June 1831, and the next year the General Assembly appropriated $50,000 for a new Capitol of stone from a quarry in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ithiel Town and Alexander Jackson Davis were engaged as architects, and Governor David Lowry Swain laid the cornerstone on 4 July 1833.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> David Paton, a young architect from Edinburgh, took charge of the work, writing to William Strickland of Philadelphia about the design as it rose, and the building was finished in the spring of 1840.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Christ Church, begun in 1846 to Richard Upjohn's design, was among the first Gothic Revival churches in the southern states and set the style there for a long time after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Josephus Daniels edited the Raleigh <i>News and Observer</i> from 1894 until his death in 1948, a leading voice of reform by his biographer's account and one of the chief spokesmen for Black disfranchisement in the white supremacy campaign of 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Carolina","dot":"Raleigh"},"facts":[["State","North Carolina"],["Population","500k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","39th largest"],["Population growth","+6.8% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Kelly Agan, Christy E. Allen and Rebecca Hyman, “Raleigh: The History of North Carolina's Capital, Past and Present,” NCpedia, Government &amp; Heritage Library, State Library of North Carolina, revised January 2025, https://www.ncpedia.org/geography/raleigh. [Open access]","National Park Service, “North Carolina State Capitol, Raleigh, North Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000476_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Christ Episcopal Church, Raleigh, North Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/87002597_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Josephus Daniels House (Wakestone), Raleigh, North Carolina,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001342_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/25/Flag_of_Raleigh%2C_North_Carolina.svg","credit":"Svgalbertian Original version by Miss Kate Denson, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Raleigh,_North_Carolina.svg)","alt":"The flag of Raleigh: three vertical stripes of red, white and red, the white bearing the city seal, a green oak tree ringed by CITY OF RALEIGH and NORTH CAROLINA inside a green oak wreath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Raleigh_downtown_skyline_-_panoramio_crop.png/1920px-Raleigh_downtown_skyline_-_panoramio_crop.png","title":"Raleigh","desc":"Raleigh, North Carolina, seen from a distance.","credit":"TheTexasNationalist99, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raleigh_downtown_skyline_-_panoramio_crop.png","alt":"A wide view of Raleigh, North Carolina."}},
{"id":"geo-033","num":33,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Kentucky","answerText":"Kentucky","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 June 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Big Bone Lick</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dug 1803 – 1807</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ovariotomy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1809</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nursing Service</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1925</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kentucky</b> is the 37th largest of the American states, covering 104,656 square kilometres (40,408 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 4.6 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Frankfort and its largest city Louisville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted on 1 June 1792 as the 15th state and the first west of the Appalachian Mountains, and its government was organised three days later in a Lexington tavern under Isaac Shelby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A committee chose Frankfort as the permanent seat, and the General Assembly met there for the first time on 1 November 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Big Bone Lick, a salt source among sulfur springs south-west of Cincinnati, drew mammals including mammoths and mastodons, which became trapped in its soft marshy bog.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thomas Jefferson, who had read of the fossils, sent Meriwether Lewis there in October 1803, and when those specimens went down with a boat near Natchez he sent William Clark back in 1807 for a three-week dig that yielded nearly 300 bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Enslaved African Americans worked from the 18th century in the depths of Mammoth Cave making saltpetre, the main ingredient of the black gunpowder that mattered so much in the War of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After that war the cave became a tourist destination, and the guides who found and opened its routes from 1838 were enslaved young men: Stephen Bishop, Mat and Nick Bransford and Alfred Croghan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Danville on Christmas Day 1809, well before practical anaesthesia, Ephraim McDowell performed the first successful ovariotomy on Jane Todd Crawford, who had ridden some 97 kilometres (60 miles) to reach him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mary Breckinridge, having watched nurse-midwives at work in France, Britain and a remote corner of Scotland, founded the Frontier Nursing Service at Wendover in 1925, the first attempt to professionalise midwifery in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its outposts each covered about 180 square kilometres (70 square miles) under a registered nurse-midwife, in a county where 43 midwives attended 265 births against one physician's 40.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kentucky"},"facts":[["Capital","Frankfort"],["Population","4.59M (2024)"],["Largest city","Louisville"],["Area","104,656 km² (40,408 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Kentucky: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kentucky-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Big Bone Lick,” Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/big-bone-lick.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “African American History,” Mammoth Cave National Park, https://www.nps.gov/maca/learn/historyculture/african-american-history.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Dr. Ephraim McDowell House, Danville, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000355_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wendover, Leslie County, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75000792_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Flag_of_Kentucky.svg","credit":"Commonwealth of Kentucky, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kentucky.svg)","alt":"A navy flag bearing the state seal: two men, one in buckskin and one in a frock coat, clasping hands within a ring reading UNITED WE STAND, DIVIDED WE FALL, below the words COMMONWEALTH OF KENTUCKY and above a spray of goldenrod."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Historic_Entrance_%28Mammoth_Cave%2C_Kentucky%2C_USA%29_2_%2837773583192%29.jpg/1920px-Historic_Entrance_%28Mammoth_Cave%2C_Kentucky%2C_USA%29_2_%2837773583192%29.jpg","title":"Mammoth Cave, Kentucky","desc":"The historic entrance to Mammoth Cave.","credit":"James St. John, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historic_Entrance_(Mammoth_Cave,_Kentucky,_USA)_2_(37773583192).jpg","alt":"A dark cave mouth in a mossy limestone bluff."}},
{"id":"geo-530","num":530,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Harrisburg","answerText":"Harrisburg","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1812</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1816 – 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 February 1897</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Present capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dedicated 1906</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Harrisburg</b> is the capital of Pennsylvania and only its ninth largest city, with 50,649 residents against Philadelphia's 1,573,916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philadelphia was the colony's capital and the first capital of the new nation, the state moved its business to Lancaster in 1799, and Harrisburg became its enduring capital in 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Act approved in February 1810 accepted four hectares (ten acres) from William Maclay beside a tract John Harris had already given the state, and ordered two fireproof office buildings for the Land Office, the Treasury, the Surveyor-General and the Auditor-General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commissioners invited architects to submit plans and elevations, offering $400 for the design adopted and $200 for the next best, and retained Stephen Hills as architect and builder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hills, born at Ashford in Kent in 1771 and apprenticed seven years to a house-wright there, had reached Boston by 1797 and Harrisburg before 1810, and his two office buildings were ready for the government's arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The main building, begun about 1816 and finished in 1822 at a cost of $148,000 without furnishings, had its cornerstone laid in 1819 by Governor Findlay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It burned on Tuesday 2 February 1897, after 75 years in which its halls had heard Thaddeus Stevens, Simon and Donald Cameron, Matthew Quay and Andrew Curtin, and had received Lafayette in 1825, Charles Dickens in 1843 and the Prince of Wales in 1860.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The present Capitol was begun in 1898 and dedicated on 4 October 1906.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Middletown, 16 kilometres (10 miles) to the south-east,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> the Three Mile Island Unit 2 reactor partially melted down on 28 March 1979, the most serious accident in the history of American commercial nuclear power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A relief valve stuck open while the control room's instruments showed it shut, so the staff did not realise coolant was pouring out; the releases were small and the commission records no detectable health effects on workers or the public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Pennsylvania","dot":"Harrisburg"},"facts":[["State","Pennsylvania"],["Population","50.6k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","807th largest"],["Population growth","+1.1% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Pennsylvania: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/pennsylvania-state-guide. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “The First Capitol Buildings, Harrisburg, Pennsylvania,” HABS No. PA-394, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/pa/pa0300/pa0394/data/pa0394data.pdf. [Open access]","U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission, “Christopher M. Crane Clean Energy Center,” Operating Nuclear Power Reactors, https://www.nrc.gov/info-finder/reactors/tmi1.html. [Open access]","U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission, “Backgrounder on the Three Mile Island Accident,” NRC Library Fact Sheets, https://www.nrc.gov/reading-rm/doc-collections/fact-sheets/3mile-isle.html. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Flag_of_Harrisburg%2C_Pennsylvania.svg","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Harrisburg,_Pennsylvania.svg)","alt":"The flag of Harrisburg: a gold border round a blue field bearing a red-edged white keystone with the Capitol dome in blue, a sun and a fleur-de-lis, and HARRISBURG, PA curved in gold beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Harrisburg%2C_PA_Skyline_2021.jpg/1920px-Harrisburg%2C_PA_Skyline_2021.jpg","title":"Harrisburg","desc":"Harrisburg, Pennsylvania, seen from a distance.","credit":"Jeffrey Hayes, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harrisburg,_PA_Skyline_2021.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Harrisburg, Pennsylvania."}},
{"id":"geo-034","num":34,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tennessee","answerText":"Tennessee","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jubilee Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1873 – 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">X-10 reactor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 November 1943</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sun studio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened January 1950</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tennessee</b> is the 36th largest of the American states, covering 109,153 square kilometres (42,144 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 7.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Nashville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined as the 16th state in 1796, is home to the most visited national park in the country, the Great Smoky Mountains, and has in Nashville what is called the country music capital of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> William Blount governed the Southwest Territory from the mansion he built at Knoxville in 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He presided over the convention that met in January 1796 and proclaimed the territory a state, and the first legislature sent him to the United States Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fisk University was one of many schools the American Missionary Association and the Western Freedmen's Aid Commission founded for the freedmen, and took its name from the general then running the Freedmen's Bureau in Tennessee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> To raise money its officials formed a choir to tour the North singing spirituals; the first trip was no financial success, but it gave the group a name, the Jubilee Singers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They went north again in 1872, sang at the World Peace Jubilee in Boston and toured for years at home and abroad, and the money saved the university and raised Jubilee Hall between 1873 and 1876.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Land near Clinton was bought late in 1942 under the pretence of a demolition range, and the X-10 pilot reactor went into operation at Oak Ridge on 4 November 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It was the world's first full-scale nuclear reactor, the first to produce significant heat and measurable plutonium, and in 1946 the first to make radioactive isotopes for medical therapy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Memphis, in a studio opened in January 1950, Sam Phillips recorded B.B. King, Howlin' Wolf, Ike Turner, Elvis Presley, Johnny Cash, Jerry Lee Lewis, Carl Perkins and Roy Orbison inside eight years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Tennessee"},"facts":[["Capital","Nashville"],["Population","7.23M (2024)"],["Largest city","Nashville"],["Area","109,153 km² (42,144 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Tennessee: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/tennessee-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Blount Mansion, Knoxville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000726_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Jubilee Hall, Fisk University, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000817_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “X-10 Reactor, Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Oak Ridge, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000720_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sun Record Company, Memphis Recording Service, Memphis, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/03001031_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/Flag_of_Tennessee.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tennessee.svg)","alt":"A red flag with a narrow blue stripe down the fly edge and, in the middle, three white stars set in a ring inside a blue disc bordered in white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/View_atop_Cliff_Tops_on_Mount_LeConte%2C_GSMNP%2C_TN.jpg/1920px-View_atop_Cliff_Tops_on_Mount_LeConte%2C_GSMNP%2C_TN.jpg","title":"the Great Smoky Mountains, Tennessee","desc":"Great Smoky Mountains National Park.","credit":"AppalachianCentrist, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_atop_Cliff_Tops_on_Mount_LeConte,_GSMNP,_TN.jpg","alt":"A view of Great Smoky Mountains National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-531","num":531,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Madison","answerText":"Madison","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chosen capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">North Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Armory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened May 1894</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jacobs House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Madison</b> is the capital of Wisconsin and its second largest city, with 285,300 residents against Milwaukee's 563,531.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territorial legislature sat at Belmont for a single session of 46 days in the autumn of 1836, and used it to pick Madison as the capital over some twenty other places that wanted the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Building began the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The university opened its first building, North Hall, on 17 September 1851, and began extension work in 1885 with farmers' institutes, short courses in farming and summer schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the first decade of the 20th century it established the ideal of a state university's service to its community so effectively that the arrangement was imitated nationally and abroad under the name of the Wisconsin Idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The drill hall of the Armory, opened in May 1894, was for years the largest room in the city and the place its political meetings were held; William McKinley spoke at a rally there that October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Republican state conventions of 1902 and 1904 renominated Robert La Follette for governor in it, and the second of them is of national importance in the history of the Progressive movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> La Follette opened that campaign with a new tactic, the roll call: he went into each Stalwart legislator's district, spoke on the questions the legislature had lately voted on, and then read out how the local member had voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 17 May the state central committee met in the Armory, awarded a majority of the disputed seats to the Progressives by unanimous vote, and the Stalwarts walked out to the Fuller Opera House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herbert Jacobs and his wife came to Taliesin in the autumn of 1936 with what they called a challenge, and the house Frank Lloyd Wright built them here was the first Usonian house in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wisconsin","dot":"Madison"},"facts":[["State","Wisconsin"],["Population","285k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","77th largest"],["Population growth","+3.9% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Capitol, Belmont, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000036_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “North Hall, University of Wisconsin, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000021_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “University of Wisconsin Armory and Gymnasium, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/93001618_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Herbert A. Jacobs House, Madison, Wisconsin,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000073_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Flag_of_Madison%2C_Wisconsin_%282018%E2%80%93present%29.svg","credit":"Original: Alan De Smet at English Wikipedia Updated edit: User:HarJIT, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Madison,_Wisconsin_%282018–present%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Madison: a broad white diagonal band across a light blue field, carrying a black cross-shaped device around a gold disc."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Downtown_Madison_Skyline_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Madison_Skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Madison","desc":"Madison, Wisconsin, seen from a distance.","credit":"Corey Coyle, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Madison_Skyline_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Madison, Wisconsin."}},
{"id":"geo-035","num":35,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Missouri","answerText":"Missouri","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1812</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pony Express</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 3 April 1860</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shelley case</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 May 1948</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Missouri</b> is the 21st largest of the American states, covering 180,540 square kilometres (69,707 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 6.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Jefferson City and its largest city Kansas City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was organised as a territory in 1812 and, after Louisiana, was the second state of the Louisiana Purchase to be admitted, on 10 August 1821.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ste. Genevieve, the old French town on the Mississippi, keeps the house of Louis Bolduc, a lead miner, merchant and planter of its early years, and an academy of 1807 that served as a school until the Civil War closed it for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At St. Joseph the Patee House was the headquarters of Russell, Majors and Waddell, who owned the Pony Express, and the cannon that opened the service was fired in front of it on 3 April 1860.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Eads Bridge, three ribbed steel arches on granite-faced limestone piers, carried a lower and an upper deck across the Mississippi at St. Louis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> It was the first bridge James Eads designed and the only one he built, and steel was used on it for the first time as the main metal of a major structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A two-family flat of 1906 in the same city gave its name to Shelley v. Kraemer, a case about whether private agreements restricting property to white owners could be enforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The state supreme court had ordered the covenant enforced in December 1946, the state arguing that because it stood ready to enforce such covenants against white owners it could enforce them against Black ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 3 May 1948 the Supreme Court held, six to nothing with three justices not sitting, that no racial covenant of any kind could be enforced by a state court, since to do so would be state action forbidden by the Fourteenth Amendment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The decision reinstated the force of that amendment and left the doctrine of separate but equal open to challenge on every front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Missouri"},"facts":[["Capital","Jefferson City"],["Population","6.25M (2024)"],["Largest city","Kansas City"],["Area","180,540 km² (69,707 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Missouri: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/missouri-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ste. Genevieve Historic District, Ste. Genevieve, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000892_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Patee House, St. Joseph, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000414_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Eads Bridge, St. Louis, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000946_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Shelley House, St. Louis, Missouri,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/88000437_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Flag_of_Missouri.svg","credit":"Marie Watkins Oliver, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Missouri.svg)","alt":"A flag of three horizontal bands, red above white above blue, with the state seal in the middle inside a blue ring of white stars, two bears holding a shield under the motto SALUS POPULI SUPREMA LEX ESTO."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/St_Louis_night_expblend_cropped.jpg","title":"the Gateway Arch, Missouri","desc":"Gateway Arch.","credit":"St_Louis_night_expblend.jpg : Daniel Schwen derivative work: ← fetch comms, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Louis_night_expblend_cropped.jpg","alt":"A view of Gateway Arch."}},
{"id":"geo-532","num":532,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Indianapolis","answerText":"Indianapolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nomination</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Harrison, 1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenaeum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1893 – 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fieldhouse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927 – 1928</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Indianapolis</b> is the capital of Indiana and much the largest city in it, with 891,484 residents against Fort Wayne's 273,203.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state's first capital was Corydon, and the seat of government was moved here in 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenaeum, built as Das Deutsche Haus for the Socialer Turnverein in two campaigns, a German Romanesque wing in 1893 and 1894 and a Renaissance block in 1897 and 1898, is a fine example of Germanic architectural influence in the Midwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the house at 1204 North Delaware Street, Benjamin Harrison accepted the Republican nomination for the presidency in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The front porch was not there at the time: he added it himself after coming home from Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Joseph Jarrett Cole began at 19 as a salesman for a carriage company here in 1888, opened a carriage works of his own in 1904, and turned it over to making luxury motor cars in 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first Cole car reached the market in 1909; he pioneered eight-cylinder engines and aluminium gears and wheels, and assembled his cars from parts bought in from other makers rather than building the whole thing himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Butler Fieldhouse, put up in 1927 and 1928, is the earliest of the great college fieldhouses, which with rule changes that quickened the game transformed college basketball over the following decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was the largest indoor sports place in the state until the late 1960s, and the finals of a high school tournament famous enough to be called Hoosier Hysteria are played in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> James Whitcomb Riley, the Hoosier Poet, spent the last 23 years of his life as a paying guest at 528 Lockerbie Street and died in the house on 22 July 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Indiana","dot":"Indianapolis"},"facts":[["State","Indiana"],["Population","891k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","16th largest"],["Population growth","+0.4% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Indiana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/indiana-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Athenaeum (Das Deutsche Haus), Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000032_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Benjamin Harrison House, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000010_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cole Motor Car Company, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/83000128_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Butler Fieldhouse, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/83003573_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “James Whitcomb Riley House, Indianapolis, Indiana,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000799_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Indianapolis.svg","credit":"Roger E. Gohl, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Indianapolis.svg)","alt":"The flag of Indianapolis: a navy field quartered by a white cross, with a red disc at the crossing bearing a white five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Panorama_of_downtown_Indianapolis_skyline%2C_looking_northeast.jpg/1920px-Panorama_of_downtown_Indianapolis_skyline%2C_looking_northeast.jpg","title":"Indianapolis","desc":"Indianapolis, seen from a distance.","credit":"Momoneymoproblemz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_downtown_Indianapolis_skyline,_looking_northeast.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Indianapolis."}},
{"id":"geo-036","num":36,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Arkansas","answerText":"Arkansas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arkansas Post</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1686</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Survey base</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 November 1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 June 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Central High</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1957</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Arkansas</b> is the 29th largest of the American states, covering 137,732 square kilometres (53,179 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.1 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its capital and largest city alike at Little Rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the 25th state on 15 June 1836, long after the peoples who had built a thriving culture along the Mississippi by about 500 CE and the Spanish and French who came exploring in the 16th and 17th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The migration routes to the West Coast went round it, so for the first 150 years of its life as territory and state it stayed rural, thinly settled and without a large city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arkansas Post, the trading post Henri de Tonti founded in 1686, was the first European foothold in the lower Mississippi valley, and Montgomery's Tavern there held the territory's first General Assembly in February 1820.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In a swamp in the east, where three counties meet, a stone marks the base established on 10 November 1815 from which United States engineers surveyed the lands of the Louisiana Purchase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The first survey run from that point was made to satisfy the land bounties promised to the soldiers of the War of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Rohwer the people held in a Japanese American relocation camp designed and built the funerary monuments of its cemetery, the largest and most elaborate of the three such cemeteries that survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The house of Daisy and L. C. Bates was the command post of the Little Rock school crisis, a haven for the nine Black students and the place they planned from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 24 September 1957 President Eisenhower signed an executive order, and nine hours and 49 minutes later a thousand soldiers of the 101st Airborne deployed in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> They collected the nine teenagers at the Bates house, and Central High School desegregated while the nation and much of the world watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arkansas"},"facts":[["Capital","Little Rock"],["Population","3.09M (2024)"],["Largest city","Little Rock"],["Area","137,732 km² (53,179 sq mi)"]],"sources":["Library of Congress, “Arkansas: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/arkansas-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Arkansas Post National Memorial, Arkansas County, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000198_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Louisiana Purchase Survey Marker, Blackton, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000206_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Rohwer Relocation Center Memorial Cemetery, Desha County, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/92001882_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Daisy Bates House, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/01000072_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Little Rock Central High School, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/77000268_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Flag_of_Arkansas.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Arkansas.svg)","alt":"A red flag bearing a large white diamond edged in blue, the state name across it in blue with one star above and three below, and 25 white stars set in the blue border."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Fordyce_Bathhouse_Hot_Springs_NP_2023.jpg/1920px-Fordyce_Bathhouse_Hot_Springs_NP_2023.jpg","title":"Bathhouse Row, Hot Springs, Arkansas","desc":"The Fordyce Bathhouse on Bathhouse Row.","credit":"Larry D. Moore, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fordyce_Bathhouse_Hot_Springs_NP_2023.jpg","alt":"The front of a large early twentieth-century bathhouse."}},
{"id":"geo-533","num":533,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Frankfort","answerText":"Frankfort","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly met</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1793</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liberty Hall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1796 – 1800</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1827 – 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dedicated 1910</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Frankfort</b> is the capital of Kentucky and only its fourteenth largest city, with 28,610 residents against Louisville's 640,796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A committee chose it as the permanent seat of government, and the General Assembly met there for the first time on 1 November 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Brown built Liberty Hall in the new capital between 1796 and 1800, on the pattern of the finest Federal architecture he had seen in the East, and above all in Philadelphia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its achievement is not that it was built on the frontier but that, remote as that frontier was, it is one of the most perfect examples of Federal domestic building ever put up in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More than two years after the second state house burned, the legislature approved a rebuilding in 1827, and the commissioners took the plans of Gideon Shryock, who was 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What he raised between 1827 and 1830 caught the essence of the American contribution to the Greek Revival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A special session of the General Assembly in February 1905 chose a 14-hectare (34-acre) site in the south of the town and voted $40,000 to buy it, and ground was broken that May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The new Capitol was dedicated on 1 June 1910, having cost $1,820,000 with the land, the building and the furnishings together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Greenhill Cemetery a limestone obelisk was raised in July 1924 by the Women's Relief Corps of the Grand Army of the Republic, inscribed to the Black soldiers of Franklin County who fought in the Civil War, with the 140 names of the men on its other three faces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is the only monument in the state to any of the nearly 30,000 Kentucky African Americans who served in the Union army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kentucky","dot":"Frankfort"},"facts":[["State","Kentucky"],["Population","28.6k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","1401st largest"],["Population growth","0.0% (2020–2024)"]],"sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Kentucky: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kentucky-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Liberty Hall, Frankfort, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000344_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Statehouse, Frankfort, Kentucky,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000346_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Kentucky State Capitol, Frankfort, Kentucky,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000804_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Colored Soldiers Monument in Frankfort, Franklin County, Kentucky,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/97000701_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Flag_of_Frankfort%2C_Kentucky.svg","credit":"City of Frankfort, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Frankfort,_Kentucky.svg)","alt":"The flag of Frankfort: on dark blue, a pale blue river-like band edged in white curving across the lower hoist, and a large gold five-pointed star at the fly with a blue star at its heart."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Downtown_-_Frankfort%2C_Kentucky_-_DSC09295.JPG/1920px-Downtown_-_Frankfort%2C_Kentucky_-_DSC09295.JPG","title":"Frankfort","desc":"Downtown Frankfort, Kentucky.","credit":"Daderot, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_-_Frankfort,_Kentucky_-_DSC09295.JPG","alt":"A street of nineteenth-century brick storefronts."}},
{"id":"geo-037","num":37,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Alabama","answerText":"Alabama","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1819</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sloss furnaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">blown 12 April 1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Redstone stand</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Church bombing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 September 1963</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alabama"},"facts":[["Capital","Montgomery"],["Population","5.16M (2024)"],["Largest city","Huntsville"],["Area","135,767 km² (52,420 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Alabama</b> is the 30th largest of the American states, covering 135,767 square kilometres (52,420 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Montgomery and its largest city Huntsville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Set off as a territory in 1817, it entered the Union on 14 December 1819 as the 22nd state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its government sat at St. Stephens, then Huntsville, Cahaba and Tuscaloosa, before the legislature settled on Montgomery in 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Delegates from six seceded states met in the new capitol in February 1861, adopted a provisional constitution for the Confederate States of America, and sat there until the capital moved to Richmond in May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Booker T. Washington was chosen to run a normal school the legislature had chartered at Tuskegee, and at his death in 1915 it taught 38 trades and professions to 1,537 students.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> James Withers Sloss left his partners in 1881 to build his own blast furnaces at Birmingham, and the first was blown on 12 April 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They made nothing but pig iron until they were last tapped in 1970, and by 1898 Birmingham was said to be the third largest point of its export in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It never caught Pittsburgh at steel, its ores carrying too much phosphorus for the ordinary Bessemer furnace and too little for the basic one, and the loading, tapping and casting were done largely by Black workers, almost 40 per cent of all southern iron and steel workers by 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Huntsville a stand built from salvaged steel in 1953 was the first in the country able to hold a whole launch vehicle rather than an engine, and rockets proved on it put the first American satellite in orbit on 31 January 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Some 50 unsolved dynamite bombings between 1947 and 1965 earned the city the name Bombingham, and the blast that killed four schoolgirls at the Sixteenth Street Baptist Church on 15 September 1963 did much to carry the Civil Rights Act of 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Alabama: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/alabama-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Alabama State Capitol, Montgomery, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000152_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tuskegee Institute, Tuskegee, Alabama,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000151_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sloss Blast Furnaces, Birmingham, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000162_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Redstone Test Stand, Huntsville, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76000341_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sixteenth Street Baptist Church, Birmingham, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/80000696_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Flag_of_Alabama.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alabama.svg)","alt":"A white flag bearing a broad crimson saltire, a diagonal cross running from corner to corner."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Edmund_Pettus_Bridge_03.jpg/1920px-Edmund_Pettus_Bridge_03.jpg","title":"the Edmund Pettus Bridge, Alabama","desc":"Edmund Pettus Bridge.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edmund_Pettus_Bridge_03.jpg","alt":"A view of Edmund Pettus Bridge."}},
{"id":"geo-534","num":534,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Nashville","answerText":"Nashville","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Nashborough</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1780</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1845 – 1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parthenon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1897</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand Ole Opry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1943 – 1974</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Tennessee","dot":"Nashville"},"facts":[["State","Tennessee"],["Population","705k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","21st largest"],["Population growth","+2.2% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Nashville</b> is the capital of Tennessee and its largest city, the balance of its consolidated metropolitan government holding 704,963 residents against Memphis's 610,919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> James Robertson and his band of settlers built a fort above the Cumberland River in about 1780; the log Fort Nashborough that later stood in Riverfront Park was a replica of 1930, and it was pulled down in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town has been known as the Athens of the South since the 1820s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> William Strickland's capitol, its cornerstone laid on 4 July 1845 and its last terrace stone not set until 1859, is a prime example of the Greek Revival, with 28 Ionic columns 10 metres (33 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strickland died in 1854 and was laid, at his own request, in a vault in the north-east wall; his son Francis finished the building, whose fossil-bearing limestone was cut in large part by convicts from the state penitentiary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The slim tower over its roof copies the Choragic Monument of Lysicrates, raised in Athens in 335 BCE to hold a tripod won by a stage chorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the state's centennial exposition the city put up a Parthenon of wood and plaster in 1897 at a cost of $30,000, and liked it well enough to keep it and lay a park around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By 1920 it was past repair, and the concrete replica that took its place consumed eleven years of research and building, its concrete mixed to look like weathered marble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Union Station was dedicated in 1900 to the design of Richard Montfort, an engineer of the Louisville and Nashville Railroad who had no architectural training and took the Allegheny County courthouse for his model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> WSM was the first station to broadcast country music nationally, in 1925, and from 1943 to 1974 its Grand Ole Opry played at the Ryman Auditorium, a Gothic Revival tabernacle finished in 1892, carrying a rural southern style to the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fort Nashborough, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/11000454_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Parthenon, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001236_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Tennessee State Capitol, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000894_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nashville Union Station and Trainshed, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/69000178_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ryman Auditorium, Nashville, Tennessee,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000819_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_Nashville%2C_Tennessee.png","credit":"City of Nashville, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nashville,_Tennessee.png)","alt":"The flag of Nashville: on navy with a yellow stripe at the fly, a white disc in a ring of yellow points bearing a mounted figure, a fleur-de-lis above, and METROPOLITAN GOVERNMENT OF NASHVILLE AND DAVIDSON COUNTY around it in blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Nashville%2C_TN_skyline.jpg/1920px-Nashville%2C_TN_skyline.jpg","title":"Nashville","desc":"Nashville, Tennessee, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nashville,_TN_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nashville, Tennessee."}},
{"id":"geo-038","num":38,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Mississippi","answerText":"Mississippi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand Village</span><span class=\"dt-v\">abandoned 1730</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 1817</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836 – 1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vicksburg falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1863</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Mississippi"},"facts":[["Capital","Jackson"],["Population","2.94M (2024)"],["Largest city","Jackson"],["Area","125,438 km² (48,432 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Mississippi</b> is the 32nd largest of the American states, covering 125,438 square kilometres (48,432 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2.9 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital and largest city alike at Jackson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted to the Union on 10 December 1817 as the 20th state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Natchez the Grand Village of the Natchez, three flat-topped mounds along St Catherine Creek, was the governmental and religious seat of that people in the late 17th and early 18th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> French explorers first reached it in 1682, and it was abandoned in 1730 after the Natchez were beaten by a combined force of French, Choctaw and Tunica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> William Nichols built the Old Capitol at Jackson between 1836 and 1840, the largest and most impressive Greek Revival building raised in the state before the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The Married Women's Property Act passed there in 1839 was the first law by any state to protect the property of married women, and it set a precedent others followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> So was the Black Code of 1865, whose restrictions on the freedmen helped bring on the first federal civil rights law, in 1866, and the Fourteenth Amendment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vicksburg commanded the Mississippi River from a bluff, and after a siege of 47 days John C. Pemberton called his chief officers together on 3 July 1863 and surrendered the city to Ulysses S. Grant the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Isaiah Thornton Montgomery and his cousin Benjamin Green, who had been enslaved on the Davis plantation at Davis Bend, founded the Black town of Mound Bayou on railroad land in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> On 30 September 1962 segregationist rioters fought federal troops at the Lyceum in Oxford over the court-ordered admission of James Meredith to the University of Mississippi, a turning point in the enforcement of Brown v. Board of Education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Mississippi: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/mississippi-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Grand Village of the Natchez Site, Natchez, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000408_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000087_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Pemberton's Headquarters, Vicksburg, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000319_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Montgomery, I. T., House, Mound Bayou, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/76001092_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lyceum–The Circle Historic District, University, Mississippi,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/08001092_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/Flag_of_Mississippi.svg","credit":"Rocky Vaughn, Sue Anna Joe and Dominique Pugh, copyrighted free use, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mississippi.svg)","alt":"A dark blue panel between gold and red vertical bands, carrying a white magnolia blossom ringed by twenty white stars and one gold star, above the words IN GOD WE TRUST."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Illinois_Monument.jpg/1920px-Illinois_Monument.jpg","title":"Vicksburg, Mississippi","desc":"Vicksburg National Military Park.","credit":"KaitlinRatliff, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illinois_Monument.jpg","alt":"A view of Vicksburg National Military Park."}},
{"id":"geo-535","num":535,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jefferson City","answerText":"Jefferson City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cole County jail</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lohman's Landing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1834</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1914 – 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 October 1924</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Missouri","dot":"Jefferson City"},"facts":[["State","Missouri"],["Population","42.6k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","970th largest"],["Population growth","-1.6% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Jefferson City</b> is the capital of Missouri and only its sixteenth largest city, with 42,564 residents against Kansas City's 516,032.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Cole County jail of about 1825 had the sheriff's quarters on its ground floor and iron-sheathed cells above, and its thick limestone walls and tiny barred windows let no prisoner out in 111 years of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lohman's Landing on the riverfront, whose first stone section went up in 1834, served in turn as warehouse, inn, store and wharf-master's office before Charles F. Lohman bought it in 1852 and gave it his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state's second capitol, designed by Stephen Hills and in use by 1840, burned in February 1911, and the legislature voted for a new one soon afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A State Capitol Commission toured other capitols for ideas and ran a national competition, won in 1912 by Egerton Swartwout and Evarts Tracy, both former associates of McKim, Mead and White.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their building went up between 1914 and 1917 and was dedicated on 6 October 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is faced in pale grey Missouri limestone, carries 134 columns of the Corinthian order and stands on a high bluff above the Missouri River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It copies no other capitol and keeps the standard form all the same, a domed centre between two wings for the legislative houses, and its murals and statues make much of the state's frontier past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three streets away the governor's mansion of 1871 is the work of George Ingham Barnett, born at Nottingham and trained in London, who came to St. Louis in 1839 and practised there for 50 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The legislature voted $50,000 for it and it cost $74,960 in the end, and the red granite columns of its portico were quarried on the land of B. Gratz Brown, the first governor to live in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Cole County Jail, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1334, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo0500/mo0565/data/mo0565data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Jefferson Landing Building, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1194, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo0100/mo0163/data/mo0163data.pdf. [Open access]","Holly K. Chamberlain, “Missouri State Capitol, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1840, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo1000/mo1092/data/mo1092data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Governor's Mansion, 100 Madison Street, Jefferson City, Cole County, Missouri,” HABS No. MO-1841, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mo/mo1000/mo1093/data/mo1093data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Flag_of_Jefferson_City%2C_Missouri.svg","credit":"Isaiah Jimmerson (vectorized by Deltaspace42), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jefferson_City,_Missouri.svg)","alt":"The flag of Jefferson City: on dark red, the white outline of the Capitol dome at the hoist, a white five-pointed star at the fly, and a white and slate-blue bridge over waves along the foot."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Jefferson_City_Bridge_2025_Cropped.jpg/1920px-Jefferson_City_Bridge_2025_Cropped.jpg","title":"Jefferson City","desc":"Jefferson City, Missouri, across the Missouri River.","credit":"Antony-22, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jefferson_City_Bridge_2025_Cropped.jpg","alt":"A river bridge with a town on the far bank."}},
{"id":"geo-039","num":39,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Delaware","answerText":"Delaware","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Holy Trinity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1698 – 1699</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 December 1787</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1787 – 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Powder mills</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1802</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Delaware"},"facts":[["Capital","Dover"],["Population","1.05M (2024)"],["Largest city","Wilmington"],["Area","6,446 km² (2,489 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Delaware</b> is the 49th largest of the American states, covering 6,446 square kilometres (2,489 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.05 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Dover and its largest city Wilmington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is nicknamed the First State because it was the first to ratify the Constitution, on 7 December 1787, and it takes its name from Thomas West, Lord De La Warr, for whom the explorer Samuel Argall named the bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Swedish Lutherans laid the cornerstone of Holy Trinity at Wilmington on 28 May 1698 and dedicated the church on Trinity Sunday the year after, on ground that had been used for burial for sixty years already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was built of Brandywine granite with brick around the openings, and when its side walls began to spread, small porticos were raised against them in 1749 and a larger one in 1762.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state house at Dover went up between 1787 and 1792 on foundations of brick taken from the building of 1722 it replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> That house served as the seat of the state until 1932 and as the courthouse of Kent County until 1873.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Éleuthère Irénée du Pont de Nemours founded his powder company on the Brandywine in 1802, and the mills he put up were patterned on the French ones in which he had learned the trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> About twenty of them survive, generally in pairs, the oldest of them built in 1822 under his own supervision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Their stone walls stand about a metre (three feet) thick and are heavily buttressed, while the roof and the wall facing the creek were framed as lightly as they could be made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An explosion would then blow out across the water, the effect being almost that of firing a colossal mortar where there was the least chance of doing harm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Delaware: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/delaware-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Holy Trinity Church (Old Swedes), Seventh & Church Street, Wilmington, New Castle County, Delaware,” HABS No. DE-7, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/de/de0100/de0161/data/de0161data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Delaware State House, East side of The Green, Dover, Kent County, Delaware,” HABS No. DE-199, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/de/de0200/de0223/data/de0223data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “DuPont Powder Mill, Hagley, New Castle County, Delaware,” HABS No. DE-2, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/de/de0100/de0154/data/de0154data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Flag_of_Delaware.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Delaware.svg)","alt":"A pale blue-green flag with a buff diamond at its centre bearing the state arms — a farmer and a rifleman flanking a shield of wheat, maize and an ox — above the date December 7, 1787."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Cape_Henlopen_State_Park_beach_looking_north_July_2022.jpeg/1920px-Cape_Henlopen_State_Park_beach_looking_north_July_2022.jpeg","title":"Cape Henlopen, Delaware","desc":"Cape Henlopen State Park.","credit":"Dough4872, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Henlopen_State_Park_beach_looking_north_July_2022.jpeg","alt":"A view of Cape Henlopen State Park."}},
{"id":"geo-536","num":536,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Little Rock","answerText":"Little Rock","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French Rock</span><span class=\"dt-v\">named 1722</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun 1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arsenal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">surrendered 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun 1899</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Arkansas","dot":"Little Rock"},"facts":[["State","Arkansas"],["Population","205k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","121st largest"],["Population growth","+1.1% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Little Rock</b> is the capital of Arkansas and its largest city, with 204,774 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bénard de la Harpe came up the river in April 1722 and noted a steep outcrop of clean limestone on the right bank, which his party called the French Rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A letter to the Secretary of War in 1805 reported that the stony shore had caused the place to be named the Little Rocks, and from 1818 the rock served as a landmark and the starting point for most land surveys south of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gideon Shryock of Kentucky drew the state house begun there in 1833, but his plans cost more than the state would pay, and his assistant George Weigart cut them down under Governor John Pope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was built is a stuccoed brick block in the Greek Revival manner, its central portico carried on four Doric columns, and it may be the first professionally designed public building in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The secession conventions of 1861 met in it, and it saw the Reconstruction violence of 1874, but its most notable years were 1912 to 1916, when the state Board of Health worked from it with the University of Arkansas Medical School against hookworm, malaria and the fevers that came almost every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The War Department voted $14,000 for an arsenal in the town after statehood, and building began in 1838 on the ground that is now MacArthur Park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 8 February 1861 Captain Totten gave it up intact, with 10,000 stand of arms, to Governor Henry M. Rector.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Douglas MacArthur was born in the building on 26 January 1880, and in 1892 the government traded it to the city on condition that the grounds be kept as a park.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The present capitol, begun in 1899 on a hill west of the centre, resembles the Capitol at Washington and is a fine piece of classical design in the Ionic order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Little Rock, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000124_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State House, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000037_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “U.S. Arsenal Building, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000127_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Arkansas State Capitol, Little Rock, Arkansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory–Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000494_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/Flag_of_Little_Rock%2C_Arkansas.svg","credit":"Dyfsunctional, copyrighted free use, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Little_Rock,_Arkansas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Little Rock: a white field crossed by a blue and gold horizontal band and a green and gold vertical one, with a gold-rimmed seal at the centre showing a rocky outcrop beside water under the state outline, lettered CITY OF LITTLE ROCK and ARKANSAS."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Skyline_of_Little_Rock%2C_Arkansas_-_20050319.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Little_Rock%2C_Arkansas_-_20050319.jpg","title":"Little Rock","desc":"Little Rock, Arkansas, seen from a distance.","credit":"Broooooooce (Bruce W. Stracener) ., Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Little_Rock,_Arkansas_-_20050319.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Little Rock, Arkansas."}},
{"id":"geo-040","num":40,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Connecticut","answerText":"Connecticut","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 January 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Morgan built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1841</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Twain house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1874 – 1891</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nautilus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 January 1955</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Connecticut"},"facts":[["Capital","Hartford"],["Population","3.68M (2024)"],["Largest city","Bridgeport"],["Area","14,357 km² (5,543 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Connecticut</b> is the 48th largest of the American states, covering 14,357 square kilometres (5,543 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 3.7 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Hartford and its largest city Bridgeport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of the six New England states and one of the original thirteen colonies, it became a state on 9 January 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Charles W. Morgan</i>, built in 1841 at New Bedford in Massachusetts for $48,849.85, is the only wooden whaleship left from the American fleet of the 19th century, and she lies at Mystic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Her frame and planking are live oak and her upper decks yellow pine, and she was the usual round-bowed, square-rigged whaler of her day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Hartford, Edward Tuckerman Potter designed for Samuel Clemens a house of a kind the town had not seen, asymmetrical and patterned in black and vermilion brick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Clemens oversaw the building himself, adding a patch of tin to the roof so that he could hear the rain on it and a window above the dining-room fireplace so that he could watch the flames meet the snowflakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He is remembered for Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn and so for the Midwest, but he did not write in Missouri: the great books came in the 17 years he lived in this house, from its completion in 1874 until money troubles took the family to Europe in 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The state house that went up at Hartford between 1872 and 1880 is white marble from the quarries at East Canaan with granite from Westerly, in what its architect called modern secular Gothic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> On 17 January 1955 Commander Eugene P. Wilkinson cast off from the submarine base on the Thames and signalled to the crowds ashore, “Underway on Nuclear Power”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It was the first time the power of the atom had moved a ship; the idea went back to 1939 and a physicist at the Naval Research Laboratory, and the boat had taken from 1949 to 1955 to build.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Connecticut: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/connecticut-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Charles W. Morgan, Mystic, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000804_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mark Twain House, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000884_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Connecticut State Capitol, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000834_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “USS Nautilus, Groton, Connecticut,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/79002653_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Flag_of_Connecticut.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Connecticut.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a white shield with three grapevines, each carrying bunches of purple grapes, above a white ribbon reading QUI TRANSTULIT SUSTINET."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/GilletteCastleCT%284%29_AM_1504_Vibrant.jpg/1920px-GilletteCastleCT%284%29_AM_1504_Vibrant.jpg","title":"Gillette Castle, Connecticut","desc":"Gillette Castle State Park.","credit":"Philip McQuade, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GilletteCastleCT(4)_AM_1504_Vibrant.jpg","alt":"A view of Gillette Castle State Park."}},
{"id":"geo-537","num":537,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Montgomery","answerText":"Montgomery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1847, rebuilt 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inauguration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bus boycott</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1955 – 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Selma march</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1965</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Alabama","dot":"Montgomery"},"facts":[["State","Alabama"],["Population","196k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","133rd largest"],["Population growth","-2.4% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Montgomery</b> is the capital of Alabama and its fourth largest city, with 195,818 residents against Huntsville's 230,402.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capitol was finished on 26 October 1847 and first occupied by the legislature that December, standing on a rise at the head of Dexter Avenue, the town's main business street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two years later, on 14 December 1849 and with the legislature in session, fire took almost the whole building and left only the masonry foundations and part of the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was rebuilt on Stephen Decatur Button's plans, dome and portico and all, and the legislature met in it again on 1 October 1851, the clock going into the tower four months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jefferson Davis was sworn in on its west portico on 18 February 1861, and the permanent Confederate constitution was adopted in the building on 11 March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Martin Luther King was called to the pastorate of Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, at the foot of that same street, in 1954, to a congregation of about 600 that held most of the city's Black intellectuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In December 1955 Rosa Parks refused to give up her seat on a city bus and was arrested, and the anger that followed produced the Montgomery Improvement Association to fight bus segregation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decision handed down in 1956 by Judge Frank M. Johnson at the federal courthouse struck down segregated seating on those buses and carried the principle of <i>Brown v. Board of Education</i> out of the schools and into the rest of public life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The boycott ran for 381 days and ended on 21 December 1956, when the Supreme Court held that bus segregation was illegal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Freedom Rides came through in 1961 and the march from Selma ended here in 1965, and between them these actions desegregated schools and public places across the South.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Alabama State Capitol, Montgomery, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000152_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, Montgomery, Alabama,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000431_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “United States Post Office and Courthouse (Frank M. Johnson, Jr. Federal Building), Montgomery, Alabama,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/98000611_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Alabama: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/alabama-state-guide. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Skyline_views_of_downtown_area%2C_Montgomery%2C_Alabama_LCCN2010637559.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-Skyline_views_of_downtown_area%2C_Montgomery%2C_Alabama_LCCN2010637559.tif.jpg","title":"Montgomery","desc":"Montgomery, Alabama, seen from a distance.","credit":"Carol M. Highsmith, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_views_of_downtown_area,_Montgomery,_Alabama_LCCN2010637559.tif","alt":"A wide view of Montgomery, Alabama."}},
{"id":"geo-041","num":41,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"New Hampshire","answerText":"New Hampshire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 June 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The Elms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1800</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chase born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 January 1808</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amoskeag</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17,000 workers at its peak</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Hampshire"},"facts":[["Capital","Concord"],["Population","1.41M (2024)"],["Largest city","Manchester"],["Area","24,214 km² (9,349 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>New Hampshire</b> is the 46th largest of the American states, covering 24,214 square kilometres (9,349 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.4 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Concord and its largest city Manchester.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Granite State and lying in the north-eastern corner of the country, it became a state on 21 June 1788.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Manchester was planned, built and dominated for more than a hundred years by the group of Boston men who created and ran the Amoskeag Manufacturing Company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At its height early in the 20th century it was the largest textile plant in the world, employing as many as 17,000 people in some thirty mills over 740,000 square metres (8 million square feet) of floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The mills were directed from an office in Boston, so it fell to the agent on the spot to plan production and to handle everything to do with labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Ebenezer Webster moved his family into a farmhouse called The Elms in 1800, near the tavern at Salisbury he had kept since 1784 and lived in for the fifteen years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His son Daniel was away at Dartmouth then, and bought the place in 1829 when his brother Ezekiel died, keeping it as an experimental farm and as the burial ground of his parents and four of his brothers and sisters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Once the railway was finished in 1847 he could come up from Boston in three hours, and he spent his visits walking the farm, hunting and fishing and seeing old friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Salmon Portland Chase was born on 13 January 1808 at Cornish, in a frame house of about 1790 that is thought to be the only building still standing that had anything to do with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He went on to serve in all three branches of the federal government, as an antislavery senator, as Secretary of the Treasury through most of the Civil War, and, on Abraham Lincoln's nomination of 6 December 1864, as Chief Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “New Hampshire: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/new-hampshire-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Straw, William Parker, House, Manchester, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/87002068_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Daniel Webster Family Home, West Franklin, New Hampshire,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74000196_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Salmon P. Chase Birthplace, Cornish Flat, New Hampshire,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/75000133_text. [Open access]","Federal Judicial Center, “Chase, Salmon Portland,” Biographical Directory of Article III Federal Judges, https://www.fjc.gov/history/judges/chase-salmon-portland. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/28/Flag_of_New_Hampshire.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Hampshire.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state seal: a frigate on the stocks before a rising sun, ringed by laurel and nine gold stars, with the date 1776 beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/White_Mountains_12_30_09_81.jpg/1920px-White_Mountains_12_30_09_81.jpg","title":"Mount Washington, New Hampshire","desc":"Mount Washington.","credit":"Harvey Barrison, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_Mountains_12_30_09_81.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Washington."}},
{"id":"geo-538","num":538,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jackson","answerText":"Jackson","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mansion built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839 – 1841</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Evers house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Evers killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1963</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Mississippi","dot":"Jackson"},"facts":[["State","Mississippi"],["Population","141k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","198th largest"],["Population growth","-8.0% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Jackson</b> is the capital of Mississippi and its largest city,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with 141,449 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The English-born William Nichols was state architect when the governor's mansion went up between 1839 and 1841, and he was supervising the building of the capitol at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tilghman M. Tucker was the first governor to occupy it, in 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only seven governors' mansions raised in the 19th century are still lived in by governors, four of them built before the Civil War, and of those four the Mississippi house is the most distinguished piece of architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eudora Welty grew up in a Tudor Revival house on Pinehurst Street beside the campus of Belhaven College, in a garden her mother Chestina planted and which has been restored to how it stood between 1925 and 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Medgar Evers applied to the law school of the University of Mississippi in 1954, the year the Supreme Court held school segregation unconstitutional, and was refused on the ground that his letters of recommendation would not serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He became the first field secretary the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People had in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Selling insurance in the Delta before that, he had found he could not press policies on people who had not enough to eat, and used the visits instead to sign them into the local chapter or to help them start one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A house was built for his family in 1956 on what is now Margaret Walker Alexander Drive, a low ranch house of the sort that went up everywhere after the war, with a carport at one end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was shot dead in its driveway in 1963, and the house is listed for the seven years he lived in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Mississippi: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/mississippi-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Mississippi Governor's Mansion, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000085_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Welty, Eudora, House, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/02001388_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Evers, Medgar, House, Jackson, Mississippi,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/00001459_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/Flag_of_Jackson%2C_Mississippi.svg","credit":"Nameneko at English Wikipedia, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Jackson,_Mississippi.svg)","alt":"The flag of Jackson: a green field quartered by a white cross, with a blue disc ringed in gold at the centre bearing a large gold five-pointed star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/JacksonMS_Downtown_Panorama.jpg/1920px-JacksonMS_Downtown_Panorama.jpg","title":"Jackson","desc":"Jackson, Mississippi, seen from a distance.","credit":"chmeredith from Jackson, MS, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JacksonMS_Downtown_Panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Jackson, Mississippi."}},
{"id":"geo-042","num":42,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Vermont","answerText":"Vermont","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 4 June 1777</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">adopted 8 July 1777</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 March 1791</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rockingham</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1787–1800</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Vermont"},"facts":[["Capital","Montpelier"],["Population","648k (2024)"],["Largest city","Burlington"],["Area","24,906 km² (9,616 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Vermont</b> is the 45th largest of the American states, covering 24,906 square kilometres (9,616 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 648,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Montpelier and its largest city Burlington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It entered the Union as the 14th state on 4 March 1791, and for the fourteen years before that it had governed itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement began in a tavern at Windsor, where on 4 June 1777 72 delegates from a territory then calling itself New Connecticut met to frame a constitution for a proposed independent republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> While they sat, a letter was read out from Thomas Young of Philadelphia, a close friend of Ethan Allen, advising them on the steps to statehood and recommending the name Vermont instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The delegates gathered again on 2 July in the tavern kept by Elijah West, and on the 8th they adopted the constitution the building is now named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That document was the first in the country to prohibit slavery and to establish universal manhood suffrage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A legislature chosen in March 1778 met at Windsor on the 12th of that month, and by setting a government going under the 1777 constitution Vermont became a republic independent of Great Britain and of every other state in North America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rockingham began a meeting house on 9 June 1787 and finished it in 1800, a plain two-storey building put up by the town under a master builder known as General Fuller and used both as a house of worship and as a town house for public business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Services stopped there in 1839 and voters met in it until about 1870, after which relic hunters badly damaged the empty building; subscriptions and a town appropriation restored it in 1906, and it was rededicated on 15 August 1907.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Barre grew around what one survey calls the vital granite industry in Vermont, where firms such as Grearson and Lane, founded in 1897, cut and polished stone in timber-framed sheds fitted with lathes, grindstones, saws and boring machines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Vermont: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/vermont-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Old Constitution House, Windsor, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000075_text. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Rockingham Meeting House, Rockingham, Windham County, Vermont,” HABS No. VT-22, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/vt/vt0000/vt0004/data/vt0004data.pdf. [Open access]","Holly K. Chamberlain, “Grearson & Lane Company, Granite Turning Shed, Barre, Washington County, Vermont,” HAER No. VT-9, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/vt/vt0000/vt0099/data/vt0099data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Flag_of_Vermont.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Vermont.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing the state arms — a lone pine before green hills, with a cow and three sheaves of wheat — under a red deer's head, over a scroll reading FREEDOM AND UNITY."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/South_from_Mount_Mansfield.jpg/1920px-South_from_Mount_Mansfield.jpg","title":"the Green Mountains, Vermont","desc":"Green Mountains.","credit":"Mike9827, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_from_Mount_Mansfield.jpg","alt":"A view of Green Mountains."}},
{"id":"geo-539","num":539,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Dover","answerText":"Dover","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Penn warrant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">signed at Dover, 1683</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Seat of state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 1777</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Woodburn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built c. 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State College</span><span class=\"dt-v\">established 1891</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Delaware","dot":"Dover"},"facts":[["State","Delaware"],["Population","40.2k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","1026th largest"],["Population growth","+1.8% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Dover</b> is the capital of Delaware and, with about 40,000 residents, the second largest place in the state after Wilmington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its centre is The Green, a small 18th-century square whose east side is taken up by the Old State House and the Supreme Court building, with the Kent County court house and private homes round the rest, many of them 18th-century and some wearing Victorian fronts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The square has been the seat of Delaware's government since 1777, and almost every decision the state has taken since was made on it or near it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Declaration of Independence was read out in front of the state house in July 1776 and King George's portrait ceremonially burned there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On or very near the same site Delaware became the first state to ratify the federal constitution, on 7 December 1787.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside the state house the General Assembly of what the nomination calls the northernmost slave state recorded unqualified disapproval of secession on 3 January 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town plan of 1717 set aside two public squares for houses of worship, and Christ Church, begun in 1704 as a mission of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel, moved onto one of them in 1734.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dover is older still: the founder of the Hillyard family took out a warrant for 1,200 hectares (3,000 acres) in 1683, signed there, the only surviving evidence that the Proprietor ever visited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His descendant Charles Hillyard built Woodburn around 1790 as a country seat north of the town, which had grown around it by the middle of the next century; local tradition, resting on its position and its owner's abolitionist leanings, makes it a station on the underground railroad, and it houses the governor now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the head of the St Jones River stands Loockerman Hall, a brick Georgian house of 1730 or earlier, one of the finest pre-Revolutionary houses left in the state, which served seventy years as classroom and dormitory for the Delaware State College founded in 1891.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old State House, The Green, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000219_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Governor's House (Woodburn), Kings Highway, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72000279_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Loockerman Hall, Delaware State College Campus, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/71000218_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Christ Church, Water and South State Streets, Dover, Delaware,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001500_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Dover_Delaware.jpg/1920px-Dover_Delaware.jpg","title":"Dover","desc":"Downtown Dover, Delaware.","credit":"Tim Kiser ( w:User:Malepheasant ), CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dover_Delaware.jpg","alt":"A main street of low brick shopfronts."}},
{"id":"geo-043","num":43,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Montana","answerText":"Montana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">created 26 May 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 November 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silver at Butte</span><span class=\"dt-v\">found 1874</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copper at Butte</span><span class=\"dt-v\">found 1883</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Montana"},"facts":[["Capital","Helena"],["Population","1.14M (2024)"],["Largest city","Billings"],["Area","380,831 km² (147,040 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Montana</b> is the 4th largest of the American states, covering 380,831 square kilometres (147,040 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 1.14 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Helena and its largest city Billings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Abraham Lincoln signed the act creating the Territory of Montana on 26 May 1864, and 25 years later, on 8 November 1889, it became the 41st state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fort Benton on the Missouri had been a fur trading post before the territory existed, and in the gold years of the 1860s it was the head of steamboat navigation and so the commercial centre of the whole region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Supplies came up the river to the town and went overland from there to Helena, Virginia City and the smaller camps, until placer mining fell away in the 1870s and the railways reached the territory in the 1880s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Butte prospered from gold as early as 1864 as well, but its first residents put up as few buildings as they could and nothing from that era survives in the centre of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Its fortunes turned in 1874 when silver was found, and again in 1883 with copper, which quickly became the chief product of the mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A run of lawsuits, take-overs and mergers between 1884 and 1906 gathered the workings under the Anaconda Company, and the methods changed with the ore body from overhead stoping to block caving to open-pit mining.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> For more than 80 years Butte Hill was one of the largest and most productive underground mining districts in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Cornish miners left the failing tin workings at home for Centerville and Walkerville, and Chinese and Irish neighbourhoods in the town were joined by Italians at Meaderville and Finns on East Broadway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The city's first skyscraper, an eight-storey steel-framed block, went up in 1901; by 1931 copper had fallen to five cents a pound, and a general strike against Anaconda in 1935 lengthened the bread lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Montana: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/montana-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Engineering Record, “Fort Benton Bridge, Spanning the Missouri River, Fort Benton, Chouteau County, Montana,” HAER No. MT-9, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0075/data/mt0075data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Engineering Record, “Butte Historic District, Butte, Silver Bow County, Montana,” HAER No. MT-35, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0040/data/mt0040data.pdf. [Open access]","Monica E. Hawley, “Butte Mineyards, Butte, Silver Bow County, Montana,” HAER No. MT-36, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0041/data/mt0041data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Flag_of_Montana.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montana.svg)","alt":"A blue flag with MONTANA in gold across the top, above the state seal: mountains and a waterfall behind a plough, a pick and a shovel, on a scroll reading ORO Y PLATA."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Lake_McDonald%2C_Glacier_National_Park%2C_Montana_%28crop%29.jpg/1920px-Lake_McDonald%2C_Glacier_National_Park%2C_Montana_%28crop%29.jpg","title":"Glacier National Park, Montana","desc":"Lake McDonald in Glacier National Park.","credit":"TLJones007, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_McDonald,_Glacier_National_Park,_Montana_(crop).jpg","alt":"A mountain reflected in a still lake."}},
{"id":"geo-540","num":540,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hartford","answerText":"Hartford","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hooker arrived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1633</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named Hartford</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1637</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burying ground</span><span class=\"dt-v\">set apart 1640</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Atheneum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened July 1844</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Connecticut","dot":"Hartford"},"facts":[["State","Connecticut"],["Population","122k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","243rd largest"],["Population growth","+0.4% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Hartford</b> is the capital of Connecticut and its fourth largest city, with 122,129 residents, after Bridgeport, Stamford and New Haven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The congregation that founded it gathered at Newtown, now Cambridge in Massachusetts, in 1632, and Thomas Hooker arrived from England the next year and was recognised as its minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1636 the church moved as a body to the west bank of the Connecticut River, and in 1637 the settlers named the place after Hertford in England, the birthplace of Samuel Stone, the teacher who had come with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They raised four meeting houses on or near the same ground: a plain hipped-roof box in 1636, a framed one with glass windows and a small steeple in 1640, a third with a tall spire in 1739 and the present church in 1807.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ancient Burying Ground beside it was set apart by the town in 1640 and took every burial in Hartford until 1803, and Samuel Stone's marker of 1663 is the oldest still standing there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Wadsworth Atheneum on Main Street was begun in April 1842 and finished in July 1844 to a castellated Gothic design by Ithiel Town, in cream granite from Glastonbury, at a cost of nearly $35,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its nomination calls it the first free art museum in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Henry Hobson Richardson was commissioned in September 1875 to design the commercial block on Main Street that still carries the name of the Cheney silk family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city calls itself the insurance capital of the world, and one of the firms behind the claim began in 1851 as the American Temperance Life Insurance Company, which would write policies only for people who pledged to abstain from alcohol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Renamed Phoenix Mutual Life in 1861, it stayed downtown when others left for the suburbs, and its elliptical glass tower of 1963 by Max Abramovitz anchored Constitution Plaza, the first urban renewal project in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “First Church of Christ and the Ancient Burying Ground, 60 Gold Street, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001324_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wadsworth Atheneum, 25 Atheneum Square North, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000709_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cheney Building, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78002852_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Phoenix Life Insurance Company Building, One American Row, Hartford, Connecticut,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/04001462_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Flag_of_Hartford%2C_Connecticut.svg","credit":"City of Hartford, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hartford,_Connecticut.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hartford: on navy, the city seal in white and gold, an eagle above a shield showing a stag in a landscape, the motto POST NUBILA PHOEBUS on a scroll, with CITY OF HARTFORD above and CONNECTICUT below."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Hartford%2C_CT_skyline_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Hartford%2C_CT_skyline_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Hartford","desc":"Hartford, Connecticut, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hartford,_CT_skyline_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hartford, Connecticut."}},
{"id":"geo-044","num":44,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Wyoming","answerText":"Wyoming","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Old Bedlam</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suffrage bill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">passed 1869</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1886</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 July 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wyoming"},"facts":[["Capital","Cheyenne"],["Population","588k (2024)"],["Largest city","Cheyenne"],["Area","253,335 km² (97,813 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Wyoming</b> is the 10th largest of the American states, covering 253,335 square kilometres (97,813 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the least populous of the fifty, with about 588,000 residents;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cheyenne is both its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became the 44th state on 10 July 1890 and is nicknamed the Equality State, having been the first to enfranchise women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Fort Laramie the army's Engineer Department raised the officers' quarters known as Old Bedlam in 1849,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and the sutler built his store in three goes: adobe about 1850, stone in 1865 and lime concrete about 1885.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The capitol at Cheyenne was begun in 1886 and carries a gold-leafed dome and pinnacle visible for miles in any direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first victory for women's suffrage in the United States had come in the Territory of Wyoming, where a bill passed the council by 6 votes to 2 and the lower house by 6 to 4 in 1869.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Esther Morris, who had heard Susan B. Anthony lecture in Illinois before moving west, was a force behind it and lobbied members of the territorial council herself, with the help of legislators' wives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Warnings of disaster were answered by the elections of 1870, which passed off smoothly, and when Wyoming applied for admission in 1889 Democrats opposed it on the ground that a full-suffrage state would encourage others to extend the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Joseph M. Carey, the territorial delegate in Washington, telegraphed home that suffrage might have to be given up to gain statehood, and the legislature answered, “We will remain out of the Union a hundred years rather than come in without the women.”<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Congress voted for statehood in 1890, the House by 139 to 127, and Utah and Colorado soon followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between 1870 and 1910 there were 480 separate initiatives in 33 states to amend their constitutions and give women the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Wyoming: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/wyoming-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wyoming State Capitol and Grounds, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001935_text. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Old Bedlam, Fort Laramie, Goshen County, Wyoming,” HABS No. WYO-8, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/wy/wy0000/wy0002/data/wy0002data.pdf. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Sutler’s Store, Fort Laramie, Goshen County, Wyoming,” Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/wy/wy0000/wy0003/data/wy0003data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Flag_of_Wyoming.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wyoming.svg)","alt":"A red flag with a white and blue border, bearing the white silhouette of a bison with the state seal set on its flank."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Yellowstone_National_Park_%28WY%2C_USA%29%2C_Old_Faithful_Geyser_--_2022_--_2599.jpg/1920px-Yellowstone_National_Park_%28WY%2C_USA%29%2C_Old_Faithful_Geyser_--_2022_--_2599.jpg","title":"Old Faithful, Wyoming","desc":"Old Faithful.","credit":"Dietmar Rabich, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellowstone_National_Park_(WY,_USA),_Old_Faithful_Geyser_--_2022_--_2599.jpg","alt":"A view of Old Faithful."}},
{"id":"geo-541","num":541,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Concord","answerText":"Concord","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legislature</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first sat 13 March 1782</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Concord wagon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first built 1813</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Concord coach</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first built 1826</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pierce died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 October 1869</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"New Hampshire","dot":"Concord"},"facts":[["State","New Hampshire"],["Population","44.7k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","921st largest"],["Population growth","+1.5% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Concord</b> is the capital of New Hampshire and its third largest city, with 44,674 residents, after Manchester and Nashua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legislature first sat here on 13 March 1782, in the North Meeting House, and found it so cold that it adjourned to Judge Timothy Walker's store on North Main Street, the council meeting in the minister's south parlour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lewis Downing reached the town in 1812 and built the first Concord wagon the year after, enlarging his wagon-building trade for the next thirteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1826, with J. Stephens Abbot, he made New Hampshire's first stage coach, the Concord coach, which grew famous quickly, each one built by hand and ornamented individually.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The partnership was dissolved in 1847 and both men went on making coaches; Downing's son had joined at seventeen and was still president of Abbot, Downing & Company when he died in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Franklin Pierce came back to a house at 52 South Main Street after leaving the White House in 1857, boarding with Willard Williams, a mechanic in one of the town's coach and wagon works.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nathaniel Hawthorne, his college roommate and lifelong friend, came to stay for ten days in the spring of 1864 in failing health, and the two then travelled north together to Plymouth, where Hawthorne died in a hotel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the front porch of the same house in 1865 Pierce spoke to a crowd gathered to protest at the assassination of Lincoln, and reassured them of his own patriotism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His wife had died there in 1863 and he died there himself on 8 October 1869, in the south-east front bedroom, a funeral service was held in the house, and the body then lay in state at the capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The gasholder house raised on South Main Street in 1888 held 3,400 cubic metres (120,000 cubic feet) until a natural gas pipeline reached the town in 1952, and is the last of its kind in the country still holding its original gasholder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Concord Historic District, Concord, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75000234_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Downing, Lewis, Jr., House, 33 Pleasant Street, Concord, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/87001425_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Franklin Pierce House, 52 South Main Street, Concord, New Hampshire,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79000318_text. [Open access]","William L. Taylor, “Concord Gasholder House, South Main Street, Concord, Merrimack County, New Hampshire,” HAER No. NH-7, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/nh/nh0100/nh0131/data/nh0131data.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Downtown_Concord_HD.JPG/1920px-Downtown_Concord_HD.JPG","title":"Concord","desc":"Downtown Concord, New Hampshire.","credit":"Teemu08 at en.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Concord_HD.JPG","alt":"A main street of tall brick blocks."}},
{"id":"geo-045","num":45,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Colorado","answerText":"Colorado","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cliff dwellings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun about 1200 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Balcony House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">enlarged in the 1240s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 August 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cripple Creek</span><span class=\"dt-v\">gold struck 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Colorado"},"facts":[["Capital","Denver"],["Population","5.96M (2024)"],["Largest city","Denver"],["Area","269,601 km² (104,094 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Colorado</b> is the 8th largest of the American states, covering 269,601 square kilometres (104,094 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 5.96 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Denver is both its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Centennial State and the Highest State, it became the 38th state on 1 August 1876.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the south-western corner of the state, from about 1200 CE, the people the survey calls the Anasazi began raising dwellings in the alcoves high above Soda Canyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Balcony House, named for the balconies still standing across two pairs of its rooms, holds 38 rooms and two ceremonial chambers called kivas, set around two plazas inside a single alcove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Building began at the north side of the north plaza, a second campaign in the 1240s put up the central core, and the generation after that doubled the number of rooms and finished the place as it now stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That last generation sealed the cross wall between the two plazas and dug a tunnel near the entrance, both of which suggest a growing concern with who could get in and where they could go; the people left around 1300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They had reached the dwelling from above, by ropes and by holds in the rock face, and the ladder that climbs about 9 metres (30 feet) to it from below is modern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Six centuries later the prospector Robert Womack, who had searched these mountains for about fifteen years, sank a shaft 15 metres (48 feet) deep and struck a rich gold vein in 1890.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Others made discoveries near his claim and companies bought them up, and by 1893 Cripple Creek was the focus of a rush that made it one of the most productive gold districts in American history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Womack's own mine, soon eclipsed by its neighbours, worked almost without a break from 1890 to 1928 and again from 1936 until 1954, spending its last four years as a tourist attraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Colorado: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/colorado-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Balcony House, Mesa Verde National Park, Cortez vicinity, Montezuma County, Colorado,” HABS No. CO-178, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/co/co0300/co0380/data/co0380data.pdf. [Open access]","Kurt Schweigert, “El Paso Lode (Gold King Mine), Cripple Creek vicinity, Teller County, Colorado,” HAER No. CO-92, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/co/co0900/co0925/data/co0925data.pdf. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/46/Flag_of_Colorado.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Colorado.svg)","alt":"Three horizontal bands, blue, white and blue, with a large red letter C at the hoist enclosing a gold disc."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Maroon_Bells_%2811553%29a.jpg/1920px-Maroon_Bells_%2811553%29a.jpg","title":"the Maroon Bells, Colorado","desc":"Maroon Bells.","credit":"Rhododendrites, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maroon_Bells_(11553)a.jpg","alt":"A view of Maroon Bells."}},
{"id":"geo-542","num":542,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Montpelier","answerText":"Montpelier","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chartered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 August 1781</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1833 – 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857 – 1859, after fire</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Clothespins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">factory built 1918</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Vermont","dot":"Montpelier"},"facts":[["State","Vermont"],["Population","7.92k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","3756th largest"],["Population growth","-2.1% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Montpelier</b> is the capital of Vermont and the smallest state capital in the United States, with 7,915 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The township was granted in 1780 and chartered on 14 August 1781 by Timothy Bigelow and 59 associates, to hold 9,300 hectares (23,040 acres), north-east of the geographic centre of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grist, saw and wool-carding mills grew up along the Winooski and its North Branch, and later in the 19th century steam replaced water power and the railway came in beside the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The National Clothespin Company raised its building on Granite Street in 1918, in that same strip between water and rails, and as demand fell and cheap imports arrived it became the last works of its kind in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> College Hall, put up between 1868 and 1872, is an outstanding French Second Empire building and one of the town's architectural landmarks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The legislature resolved in 1831 to build a new state house, and a committee of four went to look at the capitols of Connecticut, Massachusetts and New Hampshire before accepting a design from Ammi B. Young, a 32-year-old architect of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was his first large commission; he took the exterior from the Grecian Doric of the Theseion at Athens and the interior from the Ionic; the building was begun in 1833, occupied in 1836 and finished two years later at a cost of $132,007.23.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The timber interior burned in January 1857 and Thomas William Silloway rebuilt to the old design, adding a bay at either end and two at the back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He replaced Young's low saucer dome with a taller timber-framed one about 17 metres (57 feet) high, sheathed in copper and covered with gold leaf, above walls of Barre granite from the Wheaton Quarry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thomas Waterman Wood, a native of the town who became president of the National Academy, built himself a Rural Gothic summer cottage above the gorge in 1850 and painted the townspeople, from every walk of life, as his subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Elizabeth Andre et al., “National Clothespin Factory, One Granite Street, Montpelier, Washington County, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/05001422_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Vermont State House, Montpelier, Vermont,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000739_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Athenwood and Thomas W. Wood Studio, 41–39 Northfield Street, Montpelier, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/74000259_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “College Hall, Montpelier, Vermont,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75000146_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/35/Flag_of_Montpelier%2C_Vermont.svg","credit":"Original: Chet Larrow Vector: Jack Ryan Morris, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Montpelier,_Vermont.svg)","alt":"The flag of Montpelier: green hills across the foot of a dark navy field, with a ring of gold stars in the sky above them."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Montpelier%2C_Vermont.jpg/1920px-Montpelier%2C_Vermont.jpg","title":"Montpelier","desc":"Downtown Montpelier, Vermont.","credit":"Doug Kerr, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montpelier,_Vermont.jpg","alt":"A street of painted brick shopfronts."}},
{"id":"geo-046","num":46,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Kansas","answerText":"Kansas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Territory opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the act of 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lecompton</span><span class=\"dt-v\">convention met 1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 January 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brown v. Board</span><span class=\"dt-v\">decided 17 May 1954</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kansas"},"facts":[["Capital","Topeka"],["Population","2.97M (2024)"],["Largest city","Wichita"],["Area","213,100 km² (82,278 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Kansas</b> is the 15th largest of the American states, covering 213,100 square kilometres (82,278 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2.97 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Topeka and its largest city Wichita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was admitted to the Union as a free state on 29 January 1861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854 let the territory decide for itself whether it would be free or slave, and outsiders of opposing sympathies poured in to take it by weight of numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rival constitutions were approved in mass assemblies at Topeka, Lecompton, Lawrence and Wyandotte, and the plain white frame hall at Lecompton is where the proslavery draft was written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lecompton was the territorial capital from August 1855 until January 1861 and the headquarters of the proslavery side, and the second territorial legislature sat in the hall from 12 January to 20 February 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A special election late that year chose delegates for a constitutional convention; free-state men refused to take part, holding that it had been rigged, and all 60 delegates returned were proslavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The constitution they framed was supported by President Buchanan and rejected by Congress, and it inflamed the quarrel between the sections that broke out shortly afterwards in civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Almost a century later the Monroe Elementary School at Topeka, built in 1926 by Thomas W. Williamson, was one of four schools in the city serving the Black community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sumner Elementary, the neighbourhood school, refused to enrol Linda Brown because she was black, and Monroe was the segregated school she attended instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The case that followed, Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka, ended on 17 May 1954 with the Supreme Court holding that “separate educational facilities are inherently unequal”, which took away the legal basis for segregation in the 21 states that had segregated schoolrooms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Kansas: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kansas-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Lecompton Constitution Hall, Lecompton, Kansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/71000312_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Sumner Elementary School and Monroe Elementary School, Topeka, Kansas,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/87001283_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Flag_of_Kansas.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kansas.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag with a sunflower on a twisted bar above the state seal — a river steamer, ox wagons, a farmer ploughing and a herd of bison — and KANSAS in gold beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Monument_Rocks.jpg/1920px-Monument_Rocks.jpg","title":"Monument Rocks, Kansas","desc":"Monument Rocks (Kansas).","credit":"Wistar92, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_Rocks.jpg","alt":"A view of Monument Rocks (Kansas)."}},
{"id":"geo-543","num":543,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Helena","answerText":"Helena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">summer 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Masonic charter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1865</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mansions built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1885</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cathedral</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1908 – 1924</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Montana","dot":"Helena"},"facts":[["State","Montana"],["Population","34.7k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","1182nd largest"],["Population growth","+8.1% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Helena</b> is the capital of Montana and its sixth largest city, with 34,729 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold was found in Last Chance Gulch in the summer of 1864 and the news brought a stampede of hopeful miners, and within months a citizens' committee had laid out streets, elected commissioners and given the place its present name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Masons first gathered in March 1865 for the funeral of a man who had asked for their rites, took out a charter that November as Helena City Lodge No. 10, and had outgrown two halls by 1884.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> T. F. Mathias and Fred Heinlein designed the second Masonic Temple that followed, three storeys of brick with stone trim in the Renaissance manner, hailed as a mark of local craftsmanship and materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> William A. Chessman, born in Massachusetts in 1830, came in 1865 from the placer diggings of California and Virginia City and rose to head the water company that became the city's own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The house he built at 6th and Ewing in 1885 was bought by the State of Montana in 1913 as the first official governor's mansion, and it served nine governors over 46 years before a new one replaced it in 1959.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Samuel T. Hauser raised a mansion of his own the same year, in a city that already had a good many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hauser had been one of the party whose 1870 expedition produced the idea of setting Yellowstone aside, and in July 1885 President Cleveland made him governor of the Territory of Montana, the first resident of the territory to hold the office rather than a man sent from the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Cathedral of Saint Helena went up between 1908 and 1924 in tawny Bedford limestone under a steeply pitched red-tile roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is Gothic Revival, loosely patterned after the cathedral at Cologne and a near replica of the Votivkirche in Vienna, cruciform in plan, with a long nave and single-storey aisles running to twin transepts and a semicircular apse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Historic American Buildings Survey, “Second Masonic Temple, Broadway and Jackson Streets, Helena, Lewis and Clark County, Montana,” HABS No. MT-34, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, National Park Service, https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/pnp/habshaer/mt/mt0000/mt0018/data/mt0018data.pdf. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Former Montana Executive Mansion, Helena, Montana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000357_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hauser Mansion, Helena, Montana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/79001404_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cathedral of Saint Helena, Helena, Montana,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80004270_text. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Helena_Montana_2006.jpg/1920px-Helena_Montana_2006.jpg","title":"Helena","desc":"Helena, Montana, seen from a distance.","credit":"RTC at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helena_Montana_2006.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Helena, Montana."}},
{"id":"geo-047","num":47,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Nebraska","answerText":"Nebraska","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 March 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 July 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kennard House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1869</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fairview</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Bryan's home 1902 – 1921</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nebraska"},"facts":[["Capital","Lincoln"],["Population","2.01M (2024)"],["Largest city","Omaha"],["Area","200,330 km² (77,348 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Nebraska</b> is the 16th largest of the American states, covering 200,330 square kilometres (77,348 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 2 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Lincoln and its largest city Omaha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Cornhusker State, it became a state on 1 March 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of the first questions the new state faced was where to put its capital, and the legislature, sitting at Omaha, which had been the territorial capital since 1854, named three of its own officers to decide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Governor David Butler, Secretary of State Thomas Perkins Kennard and Auditor John Gillespie chose the site of Lincoln on 29 July 1867, largely at Kennard's urging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether the place would survive at all, let alone remain the capital, was uncertain until the depression years of the 1870s had passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1869 the Chicago architect John Keys Winchell designed three similar masonry houses in the town, one for each commissioner, and building them did much to persuade Nebraskans that Lincoln had a future; only Kennard's still stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> William Jennings Bryan practised law in Illinois before moving to Lincoln, and in 1891 he became the first Democrat Nebraska sent to Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He took the Democratic convention at Chicago in 1896 with a speech for bimetallism that ended “you shall not crucify mankind upon a cross of gold”, was nominated by the Democrats and the Populists together, travelled 29,000 kilometres (18,000 miles) and made 600 speeches in 27 states, and lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Willa Cather lived at Red Cloud from the age of eleven until she left for the state university at seventeen, and that house is minutely described in <i>The Song of the Lark</i> and in two later stories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> She held that a writer gathers his thematic material before he is fifteen, and the people and the prairies she remembered from those years are the raw material of <i>O Pioneers!</i> and <i>My Ántonia</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “Nebraska: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/nebraska-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Thomas P. Kennard House, Lincoln, Nebraska,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/69000134_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Fairview, Lincoln, Nebraska,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000947_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Willa Cather House, Red Cloud, Nebraska,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/69000139_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Flag_of_Nebraska.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nebraska.svg)","alt":"A blue flag bearing the state seal in gold and grey: a blacksmith at his anvil, a steamboat, a train and mountains, ringed by GREAT SEAL OF THE STATE OF NEBRASKA and the date MARCH 1ST 1867."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chimney_Rock_NE.jpg/1920px-Chimney_Rock_NE.jpg","title":"Chimney Rock, Nebraska","desc":"Chimney Rock National Historic Site.","credit":"Mike Tigas from Columbia, MO, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chimney_Rock_NE.jpg","alt":"A view of Chimney Rock National Historic Site."}},
{"id":"geo-544","num":544,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Cheyenne","answerText":"Cheyenne","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Site chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Town surveyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Depot built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1886 – 1887</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Wyoming","dot":"Cheyenne"},"facts":[["State","Wyoming"],["Population","65.7k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","602nd largest"],["Population growth","+0.8% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Cheyenne</b> is the capital of Wyoming and its largest city, with 65,704 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 4 July 1867, with the Union Pacific construction crew still at Julesburg in Colorado, its chief engineer Grenville Dodge met General C. C. Augur where the line would cross Crow Creek, to fix a place for an army post and supply depot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The town was surveyed within a week and the lots sold by the railway's own land company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first houses and businesses were tents, shanties, adobes and prefabricated buildings put up, taken down and put up again as the tracks moved west, and lots that first sold for $150 reached $2,000 in the speculation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the time the construction crew arrived they found a town of some 4,000 people with two daily newspapers, more than 200 businesses and a working town government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It called itself the Magic City of the Plains, and its own newspaper wrote that Cheyenne was a creation of the Union Pacific and by that corporation's acts would stand or fall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The depot that stands there now was designed by the Boston firm of Van Brunt & Howe and built in 1886 and 1887, Richardsonian Romanesque on massive stone walls, on the site of the wooden station of 1867.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It faces the capitol ten blocks north along Capitol Avenue, begun in 1886 under David W. Gibbs and William Dubois, which became the state capitol on Wyoming's admission in 1890 and was finished in stages down to 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Erasmus Nagle's mansion, finished in 1888 at a cost of $50,000, was built of rough grey sandstone blocks rejected for the capitol, and the contractor who rejected them was proved right when they began to flake in the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among those the cattle boom drew was Moreton Frewen, a younger son of English country gentry, whose house survives as a token of the tie between the decline of British agriculture and the first stage of ranching on the high plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Union Pacific Railroad Depot, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/73001934_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wyoming State Capitol and Grounds, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73001935_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Moreton Frewen House, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001904_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nagle-Warren Mansion, Cheyenne, Wyoming,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/76001955_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Cheyenne%2C_Wyoming.svg","credit":"Jack Ryan Morris, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cheyenne,_Wyoming.svg)","alt":"The flag of Cheyenne: a brown border round a white field bearing the city seal in brown and gold, a bison ringed by CITY OF CHEYENNE and THE EQUALITY STATE, with 1867 and WYOMING beneath it."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Lincolnway_-_Cheyenne_-_Wyoming_%2851837641009%29.jpg/1920px-Lincolnway_-_Cheyenne_-_Wyoming_%2851837641009%29.jpg","title":"Cheyenne","desc":"Downtown Cheyenne, Wyoming.","credit":"Tony Webster, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lincolnway_-_Cheyenne_-_Wyoming_(51837641009).jpg","alt":"A block of old commercial buildings on a wide street."}},
{"id":"geo-048","num":48,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"South Dakota","answerText":"South Dakota","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ingalls arrive</span><span class=\"dt-v\">De Smet, 1879</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pa's house</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built 1887</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 November 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wounded Knee</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 December 1890</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Dakota"},"facts":[["Capital","Pierre"],["Population","925k (2024)"],["Largest city","Sioux Falls"],["Area","199,729 km² (77,116 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>South Dakota</b> is the 17th largest of the American states, covering 199,729 square kilometres (77,116 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with about 925,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> its capital at Pierre and its largest city Sioux Falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nicknamed the Mount Rushmore State, it became the 40th state on 2 November 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ingalls family reached De Smet in 1879, after homesteading had failed for them in Wisconsin and Kansas, and Charles Ingalls kept time for the railway then being built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their first year there is the subject of <i>By the Shores of Silver Lake</i>, and <i>The Long Winter</i> and <i>Little Town on the Prairie</i> follow the family to 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He gave up farming for carpentry and put up a storey-and-a-half frame house in the town in 1887, the last home of his wife and of his daughter Mary, whose blindness gave her a part in the books, since much of what Laura set down she had first described aloud to her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> On 29 December 1890, on the Pine Ridge reservation in the south-west of the state, the United States Army's Seventh Cavalry and the followers of the Miniconjou chief Big Foot met at Wounded Knee Creek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The site's nomination records that the event is called the Wounded Knee Massacre or the Battle of Wounded Knee according to the perspective of the writer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> New laws and a run of commissions had lately taken great tracts of land from the Lakota, and they and other tribes confined to reservations had turned to the Ghost Dance to hold on to their own way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The army's guns stood on the hill above the camp; many of the Miniconjou died in the dry ravine below it and others got away along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A party from the Pine Ridge agency found eleven wounded survivors on 1 January 1891, a burial party laid 146 men, women and children in a mass grave on the hill, and the survivors dedicated a granite monument over it on 28 May 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Library of Congress, “South Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/south-dakota-state-guide. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ingalls House, De Smet, South Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75001717_text. [Open access]","Richard E. Jensen and Eli Paul, “Wounded Knee Battlefield, Pine Ridge Indian Reservation, South Dakota,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, National Park Service, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000719_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Flag_of_South_Dakota.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Dakota.svg)","alt":"A mid-blue flag with the state seal at its centre inside a gold sunburst, ringed by the words SOUTH DAKOTA and THE MOUNT RUSHMORE STATE."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mount_Rushmore_detail_view_%28100MP%29.jpg/1920px-Mount_Rushmore_detail_view_%28100MP%29.jpg","title":"Mount Rushmore, South Dakota","desc":"Mount Rushmore.","credit":"Thomas Wolf , www.foto-tw.de, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Rushmore_detail_view_(100MP).jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Rushmore."}},
{"id":"geo-545","num":545,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Denver","answerText":"Denver","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Denver City</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded spring 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Auraria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">laid out October 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One city</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the act of 1864</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mint building</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1897 – 1904</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Colorado","dot":"Denver"},"facts":[["State","Colorado"],["Population","729k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","19th largest"],["Population growth","+1.9% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Denver</b> is the capital of Colorado and its largest city, with 729,019 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the spring of 1858 General William E. Larimer Jr. founded Denver City on a jumped claim, with four cabins, one at each corner of Larimer and Fifteenth Streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fifteenth Street, called F Street until late in the century, had been the military trail from Santa Fe and Fort Union up to the trading posts north of the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across Cherry Creek, an Indian trader named John T. Smith gave up his claim at the mouth of the creek for a nominal fee on 29 October 1858, and the town of Auraria was laid out with eight cabins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A territorial act of 1864 deeded the ground to the town fathers and made one city of Auraria, Denver and Highland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Congress had created the Territory of Colorado in February 1861, and on 21 April 1862 it provided for a branch mint at Denver for the coinage of gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Treasury bought a private mint at Sixteenth and Market Streets from Clark, Gruber & Company and opened it in September 1863, but it struck no coin at all: bullion was melted, refined, assayed, stamped and handed back to whoever had brought it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From 1869 it did less still, only melting, assaying and returning, and not until 1895 did an act provide for a United States Mint at Denver for the coinage of gold and silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A site at the corner of Colfax Avenue and Delaware Street was bought on 22 April 1896, and the new building went up intermittently under James Knox Taylor from 1897 to 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The House of Lions on Pennsylvania Street, usually called the Molly Brown House, is what its nomination calls an emotional landmark of Coloradans as much as a historical one, standing for the rags-to-riches turn in the state's early years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Larimer Square, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000468_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Auraria 9th Street Historic District, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000466_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Denver Mint, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72000270_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Molly Brown House, Denver, Colorado,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72000269_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Flag_of_Denver%2C_Colorado.svg","credit":"Margaret Overbeck, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Denver,_Colorado.svg)","alt":"The flag of Denver: on navy, a white and red zigzag of mountain peaks across the lower half with a gold disc for the sun above."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Denver%2C_Colorado_skyline_%28cropped_3x5%29.jpg/1920px-Denver%2C_Colorado_skyline_%28cropped_3x5%29.jpg","title":"Denver","desc":"Denver, seen from a distance.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denver,_Colorado_skyline_(cropped_3x5).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Denver."}},
{"id":"geo-049","num":49,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"North Dakota","answerText":"North Dakota","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dakota Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">organised in 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 November 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">League in power</span><span class=\"dt-v\">elections of 1916</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mill opens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 October 1922</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Dakota"},"facts":[["Capital","Bismarck"],["Population","797k (2024)"],["Largest city","Fargo"],["Area","183,108 km² (70,698 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>North Dakota</b> is the 19th largest of the American states, covering 183,108 square kilometres (70,698 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 797,000 residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> its capital at Bismarck and its largest city Fargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It and South Dakota were both part of Dakota Territory, organised in 1861, and each became a state on 2 November 1889.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Long before that the Hidatsa lived in earthlodge villages along the Knife River, and the one called Big Hidatsa was occupied until about 1850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It covers about 6 hectares (15 acres), is crossed by ditches thrown up as parts of it were abandoned or enlarged, and is thought to hold the best defined lodge depressions of any large village on the Great Plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It stood among the villages near Fort Mandan, where Lewis and Clark passed the winter of 1804 to 1805, and Charbonneau and Sacagawea came from another Hidatsa village about 2.4 kilometres (a mile and a half) to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Farmers here had complained since the Farmers Alliance of the 1890s that they were at the mercy of the milling and railway companies of <b>Minnesota</b>, which docked them for wet or impure grain and charged heavily to carry it away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Bills to buy the state a grain elevator passed in 1893, 1909 and 1911 and came to nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Nonpartisan League, founded in 1915, made a state-owned mill one of the main planks of its platform, won the elections of 1916, and carried its programme through the legislature of 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That programme also produced the Bank of North Dakota, a state hail insurance scheme and a homebuilding plan, and its first mill, at Drake, ground flour from the middle of August 1919 and closed in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Building at Grand Forks began in 1920 and stopped that autumn, and Governor R. A. Nestos pressed a button on 30 October 1922 to start the mill that is still the only one owned by an American state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “North Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/north-dakota. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Big Hidatsa Village Site, Mercer County, North Dakota,” National Survey of Historic Sites and Buildings, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000600_text. [Open access]","Steven R. Hoffbeck, “North Dakota Mill and Elevator, Grand Forks, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, National Park Service, 1991, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/92000433_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Flag_of_North_Dakota.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Dakota.svg)","alt":"A dark blue flag bearing a bald eagle with spread wings holding an olive branch and arrows, a fan of gold rays and thirteen stars above it, and NORTH DAKOTA on a red scroll below."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/View_of_Theodore_Roosevelt_National_Park.jpg/1920px-View_of_Theodore_Roosevelt_National_Park.jpg","title":"Theodore Roosevelt National Park, North Dakota","desc":"Theodore Roosevelt National Park.","credit":"NPS/Mark Hoffman, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Theodore_Roosevelt_National_Park.jpg","alt":"A view of Theodore Roosevelt National Park."}},
{"id":"geo-546","num":546,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Topeka","answerText":"Topeka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 October – 11 November 1855</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1855</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Curtis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">vice president, 1929 – 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Kansas","dot":"Topeka"},"facts":[["State","Kansas"],["Population","125k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","232nd largest"],["Population growth","-0.9% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Topeka</b> is the capital of Kansas and the seat of Shawnee County, the fifth largest city in the state with 125,467 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was settled in December 1854,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> three months after Anthony Ward brought his family to a farm about 1.6 kilometres (a mile) to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Constitution Hall, begun as a shop in 1855, was the town's first stone building, and the Free State convention was called to order in its unplastered upper room on 23 October and sat until 11 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The constitution framed there drew on those of New York and Ohio, prohibited slavery and adopted homestead protection, and it passed by 1,731 votes to 46 in a popular election on 15 December 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress rejected it and President <b>Franklin Pierce</b> condemned it, and on 4 July 1856, with John Brown and his partisans looking on, federal troops dispersed the Free State legislature as it sat in the hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Kansas entered the Union as a free state on 29 January 1861<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> under the Wyandotte Constitution, for which the Topeka document set the course.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charles Curtis was born in North Topeka on 25 January 1860, and after his mother died he was brought up partly by his father's parents in the town and partly on the Kaw reservation in Morris County.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His grandmother there, Julie Conville Pappan, was a granddaughter of the Kaw chief White Plume; Curtis reported for the North Topeka Times from 1877, read law in a Topeka office, and was elected county attorney for Shawnee County in 1884.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He sat in the House of Representatives from 1893 to 1907 and in the Senate from 1907 to 1929, apart from two years from 1913, and was influential in Indian and farm legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1929 he became the first vice president from west of the Mississippi, the first from Kansas and the first of Native American ancestry, and he retired from office after the ticket of <b>Herbert Hoover</b> and Curtis was beaten in 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Constitution Hall—Topeka, Topeka, Kansas,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/08000669_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Ward-Meade House, Topeka, Kansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/75000727_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Kansas: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/kansas-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Charles Curtis House, Topeka, Kansas,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/73000778_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Flag_of_Topeka%2C_Kansas.svg","credit":"The Greater Topeka Partnership, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Topeka,_Kansas.svg)","alt":"The flag of Topeka: a white band at the hoist against a blue field, overlaid by a large gold sunflower with a blue centre and one green petal, and a white five-pointed star towards the fly."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/View_from_Kansas_State_Capitol_dome%2C_looking_east.jpg/1920px-View_from_Kansas_State_Capitol_dome%2C_looking_east.jpg","title":"Topeka","desc":"Topeka, Kansas, seen from a distance.","credit":"Mike Linksvayer, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Kansas_State_Capitol_dome,_looking_east.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Topeka, Kansas."}},
{"id":"geo-050","num":50,"category":"Geography","question":"The state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Iowa","answerText":"Iowa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cornerstone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Old Capitol, 4 July 1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 December 1846</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amana founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1855</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to Des Moines, 1857</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Iowa"},"facts":[["Capital","Des Moines"],["Population","3.24M (2024)"],["Largest city","Des Moines"],["Area","145,746 km² (56,273 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","united states","state"],"abstract":"<b>Iowa</b> is the 26th largest of the American states, covering 145,746 square kilometres (56,273 square miles)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> with about 3.24 million residents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with Des Moines its capital and its largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It takes its name from the Iowa people who lived there, and it became a state on 28 December 1846.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The building on the campus at Iowa City called the Old Capitol was raised in the <b>Greek Revival</b> manner as the third territorial capitol, and was the first permanent state capitol from 1846 to 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its architect, John Francis Rague, had won the $200 prize for the first <b>Illinois</b> capitol at Springfield in 1837 and sent in plans for this one in 1839; the cornerstone was laid on 4 July 1840 and he resigned nine days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Iowa's first governor was inaugurated there, its first six General Assemblies met there and the state constitution was drafted there, and when the legislature left for Des Moines in 1857 the University of Iowa took it over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 1714 a group in <b>Germany</b> withdrew from the Lutheran Church and called itself the Community of True Inspiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Persecuted at home, 800 of them followed Christian Metz to the United States between 1843 and 1846, founded six villages near Buffalo in New York and two in <b>Canada</b>, and agreed to hold all property in common except their clothes and household goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By 1854 the spread of Buffalo was pressing on them, and they began moving to Iowa, where Amana, a name meaning 'remaining true', was founded in 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Middle, High, West, South and East Amana followed over the next seven years, and they bought the town of Homestead outright to give themselves a railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Incorporated under Iowa law in 1859 as the Amana Society and governed by thirteen elected elders, it was the most successful and longest lived of the utopian communities founded in 19th-century America, and it exists still in altered form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “State Area Measurements and Internal Point Coordinates,” Geography Program reference file, https://www.census.gov/geographies/reference-files/2010/geo/state-area.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for the United States, Regions, States, District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” NST-EST2024-ALLDATA, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/state/totals/NST-EST2024-ALLDATA.csv. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Iowa: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/iowa-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol, Iowa City, Iowa,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000475_text. [Open access]","Blanche H. Schroer and Ray H. Mattison, “Amana Colonies, Iowa,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, National Park Service, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/66000336_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Flag_of_Iowa.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Iowa.svg)","alt":"Three vertical bands, blue, white and red, the white bearing an eagle carrying a blue streamer that reads OUR LIBERTIES WE PRIZE AND OUR RIGHTS WE WILL MAINTAIN, with IOWA in red beneath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Loess_Hills_0633.jpg/1920px-Loess_Hills_0633.jpg","title":"the Loess Hills, Iowa","desc":"The Loess Hills.","credit":"Chris Light, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loess_Hills_0633.jpg","alt":"Steep grass-covered hills rolling away under a wide sky."}},
{"id":"geo-547","num":547,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Lincoln","answerText":"Lincoln","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitol begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Goodhue dies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tower finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unicameral</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first sat in 1937</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Nebraska","dot":"Lincoln"},"facts":[["State","Nebraska"],["Population","301k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","72nd largest"],["Population growth","+3.2% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Lincoln</b> is the capital of Nebraska<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and, with 300,619 residents, the second largest city in the state after Omaha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capitol, begun in 1922 and finished in 1932, was designed by Bertram Grosvenor Goodhue, who had spent twenty years building in the Gothic manner with Ralph Adams Cram before breaking away in 1914.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His first study was wholly classical, and by 1919, having seen Eliel Saarinen's work, he had arrived at a tall tower over low wings, a classical skyscraper meant as a new American style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wings were built round the old statehouse of 1888, which came down in 1925; the tower, 122 metres (400 feet) high, was finished in 1930, and Lee Lawrie's Sower, 5.8 metres (19 feet) tall, was hoisted on to the top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hartley Burr Alexander, professor of philosophy at the state university, wrote the sculptural programme and chose its inscriptions, which run from Moses on Sinai to Nebraska's admission to the Union, with every county's name carved round the base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The body that meets in it is unlike any other state legislature in the country, and George Norris made it so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Norris began practising law in Nebraska in 1885, sat in the House from 1902 and in the Senate from 1912 to 1943, a Republican who called himself an independent and said he would rather be right than regular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He wore out two sets of tyres driving about the state to campaign for a single chamber, arguing that two houses merely copied a Parliament of Commons and Lords in a country that recognises one class.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The amendment carried in 1934 by 286,086 votes to 193,152 and made the legislature nonpartisan as well, so that a candidate's party is not printed on the ballot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the new body first sat in 1937 its membership had fallen from 133 to 43 and its committees from 61 to 18, and its session ran 98 days against the last two-chamber one's 110.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Nebraska State Capitol, Lincoln, Nebraska,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/70000372_text. [Open access]","Nebraska Legislature, “History of the Nebraska Unicameral,” Nebraska Legislature, https://nebraskalegislature.gov/about/history_unicameral.php. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Senator George W. Norris House, McCook, Nebraska,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/67000006_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Nebraska: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/nebraska-state-guide. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Flag_of_Lincoln%2C_Nebraska_%282022%29.svg","credit":"Ed Mejia, American Marketing Association Lincoln, Lincoln Young Professionals Group, City of Lincoln, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lincoln,_Nebraska_%282022%29.svg)","alt":"The flag of Lincoln: a teal upper half over a dark green lower half, with tan rays fanning out from a central round emblem of quartered arcs and upright lines around a small white star."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Nighttime_skyline_of_downtown_Lincoln%2C_Nebraska%2C_USA_%282015%2C_from_Arnold_Heights_Park%29.jpg/1920px-Nighttime_skyline_of_downtown_Lincoln%2C_Nebraska%2C_USA_%282015%2C_from_Arnold_Heights_Park%29.jpg","title":"Lincoln","desc":"Lincoln, Nebraska, seen from a distance.","credit":"Hanyou23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nighttime_skyline_of_downtown_Lincoln,_Nebraska,_USA_(2015,_from_Arnold_Heights_Park).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Lincoln, Nebraska."}},
{"id":"geo-548","num":548,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Pierre","answerText":"Pierre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plate buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 March 1743</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oahe Mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1873</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plate found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1913</span><span class=\"dt-k\">St. Charles</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 1 January 1911</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"South Dakota","dot":"Pierre"},"facts":[["State","South Dakota"],["Population","13.8k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","2548th largest"],["Population growth","-2.2% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Pierre</b> is the capital of South Dakota<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and, with 13,788 residents, the second smallest state capital in the country and the eleventh largest city in its own state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On a hill at Fort Pierre, across the Missouri, François and Louis-Joseph de La Vérendrye buried a lead plate on 30 March 1743.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It bore the names of Louis XV of <b>France</b>, the Marquis de Beauharnois, viceroy of French <b>Canada</b>, their father Pierre and themselves, and it was meant to found a French claim to the upper Missouri and draw <b>Louisiana</b> as the whole Mississippi drainage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were among the first Europeans to reach the middle of the continent, 61 years before Lewis and Clark, and their journeys were written off at the time for finding no passage to the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plate lay there until 1913, when children playing on the hill turned it up, and it has been with the South Dakota State Historical Society ever since; a granite monument marks the hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> North of the town the Oahe Mission was founded in 1873 as the headquarters of missionary work among the Sioux, and in 1877 the Reverend S. R. Riggs built its chapel from lumber brought up the river from Yankton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It opened a boarding school for Indian children in 1883 and another for girls in the 1890s, and though the mission closed in 1914 services were held there in the Sioux language until 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pierre's own prospects were pressed hardest by Charles L. Hyde, who arrived in 1887, traded land and put up nine brick business blocks expecting the town to become a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He opened the St. Charles Hotel on 1 January 1911 at a reception given by the governor, and until the governor's mansion was finished in the 1940s it housed several of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That same year Hyde was indicted for fraudulent advertising through the mails, and the case went as far as the White House before President <b>William Howard Taft</b> pardoned him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Verendrye Site, Fort Pierre, South Dakota,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/74001899_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Oahe Chapel, Pierre, South Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80003725_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “St. Charles Hotel, Pierre, South Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/80003724_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “South Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/south-dakota-state-guide. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d2/Flag_of_Pierre%2C_South_Dakota.png","credit":"Unknown author , colorized by John G. Moisan, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pierre,_South_Dakota.png)","alt":"The flag of Pierre: on white, a round seal in green and gold showing a sunrise over hills and a steamboat on a river, lettered CITY OF PIERRE above and INCORPORATED 1883 below."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Downtown_Pierre%2C_South_Dakota.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Pierre%2C_South_Dakota.jpg","title":"Pierre","desc":"Downtown Pierre, South Dakota.","credit":"WeaponizingArchitecture, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Pierre,_South_Dakota.jpg","alt":"A wide main street of low storefronts."}},
{"id":"geo-549","num":549,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Bismarck","answerText":"Bismarck","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Camp Hancock</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 April 1872</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribune</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first issue, 11 July 1873</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Custer news</span><span class=\"dt-v\">telegraphed 5 July 1876</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statehood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889, with Bismarck the capital</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"North Dakota","dot":"Bismarck"},"facts":[["State","North Dakota"],["Population","77.8k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","486th largest"],["Population growth","+5.6% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Bismarck</b> is the capital of North Dakota<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and, with 77,772 residents, the second largest city in the state after Fargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army set up Camp Hancock on the line of the Northern Pacific Railway on 16 April 1872, though no troops arrived until 8 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The post guarded the building of the railway and was held by companies D and H of the 17th Infantry until 1877, after which it served as a supply depot for Fort Abraham Lincoln and the posts further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its military life ended on 16 April 1894, when the War Department handed the ground to the Department of Agriculture and the weather station moved in; the two-storey supply building of 1872 still stands and appears to be the oldest in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The railway reached the Missouri a month after Colonel Clement A. Lounsberry arrived in May 1873 to open a three-man newspaper office, having settled long before on publishing where the Northern Pacific would cross the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first Bismarck Tribune came off a hand press on 11 July 1873, promising its best efforts towards building up the town, and it is still the oldest newspaper in the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lounsberry's correspondent Mark Kellogg rode with George Custer to cover the campaign of 1876 and was one of the few civilians killed at the Little Bighorn on 25 June, where Custer and 261 men died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The steamer Far West brought the news down the Yellowstone, and on 5 July Lounsberry telegraphed it to the New York Herald and brought out a special edition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Marshall H. Jewell, who bought the paper in 1884, campaigned for statehood for nearly twelve years and got it in 1889, with Bismarck as the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Northern Pacific's own depot, put up after the fire of 8 August 1898 had levelled much of the centre, was designed by Charles Reed and Allen Stem of St. Paul in a Spanish mission manner rare on the northern plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Camp Hancock Site, Bismarck, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/72001004_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Bismarck Tribune Building, Bismarck, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/82001309_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Northern Pacific Railway Depot, Bismarck, North Dakota,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/77001022_text. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “North Dakota: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/north-dakota. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Flag_of_Bismarck%2C_North_Dakota.svg","credit":"original by Mark Kenneweg; vectorization by Flagvisioner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bismarck,_North_Dakota.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bismarck: a gold border round a field of white over red, with BISMARCK in blue capitals whose A is a gold star, NORTH DAKOTA in blue on a gold ribbon, and a blue disc at the hoist bearing a white star in a gold wreath."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Aerial_Photo_of_the_city_of_Bismarck._Looking_Northeast.jpg","title":"Bismarck","desc":"Bismarck, North Dakota, seen from a distance.","credit":"North Dakota Department of Transportation, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_Photo_of_the_city_of_Bismarck._Looking_Northeast.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Bismarck, North Dakota."}},
{"id":"geo-550","num":550,"category":"Geography","question":"The capital of the state shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Des Moines","answerText":"Des Moines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital moves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to Des Moines, 1857</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Uncle Henry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1836 – 1916</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wallaces' Farmer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1895</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Historical Building</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898 – 1910</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","map":{"layer":"us-states","key":"Iowa","dot":"Des Moines"},"facts":[["State","Iowa"],["Population","213k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","113th largest"],["Population growth","-0.4% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","united states","city","capital"],"abstract":"<b>Des Moines</b> is the capital of <b>Iowa</b><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and its largest city, with 213,096 residents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legislature moved there from the Old Capitol at Iowa City in 1857.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The capitol complex stands above the meeting of the Des Moines and Raccoon rivers, and across Grand Avenue from the capitol is the State Historical Building, put up in two campaigns between 1898 and 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Oliver O. Smith designed it in a Beaux-Arts classicism, low and spread out where the capitol is compact and vertical, so that it answers the older building without competing with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The firm of Proudfoot, Bird and Rawson dominated architecture in the state for thirty years from 1900, putting up thirty buildings at Ames, eighteen at the University of Iowa and five at Northern Iowa, as well as the Hotel Fort Des Moines, once the largest in Iowa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Henry Wallace, born on a farm near West Newton in <b>Pennsylvania</b> on 19 March 1836, edited the Iowa Homestead from 1885 and in 1895 helped found Wallaces' Farmer, which he edited until his death in 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He argued for scientific farming, did much to make Iowa State a serious agricultural research institution, and pressed to get its findings out to farmers through extension work, institutes and trains fitted up for teaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1908 <b>Theodore Roosevelt</b> put him on the Country Life Commission, beside Gifford Pinchot and Liberty Hyde Bailey, to examine the whole farm problem from soil waste to spiritual waste; it held thirty hearings and reported in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 1911 he presided over the third National Conservation Congress at Kansas City and gave its keynote address.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Consulted by President <b>William Howard Taft</b> about whether to keep his Secretary of Agriculture, he wired his own and other Midwestern papers to raise a campaign for the man, and Taft named an otherwise new cabinet and kept him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["U.S. Census Bureau, “Annual Estimates of the Resident Population for Incorporated Places in the United States: April 1, 2020 to July 1, 2024,” SUB-EST2024, Population Division, https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest/datasets/2020-2024/cities/totals/sub-est2024.csv. [Open access]","Library of Congress, “Iowa: State Resource Guide,” Library of Congress Research Guides, https://guides.loc.gov/iowa-state-guide. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Old Capitol, Iowa City, Iowa,” National Historic Landmark Nomination, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NHLS/72000475_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Iowa State Historical Building, Des Moines, Iowa,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/78001251_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hotel Fort Des Moines, Des Moines, Iowa,” National Register of Historic Places Inventory — Nomination Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/82002636_text. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Wallace, Henry, House, Des Moines, Iowa,” National Register of Historic Places Registration Form, https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/93000412_text. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/Flag_of_Des_Moines%2C_Iowa.svg","credit":"Mysid, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Des_Moines,_Iowa.svg)","alt":"The flag of Des Moines: a red wedge at the hoist, and across the navy field three white bands each carrying a row of navy semicircular arches."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Morning_Skyline_-_Des_Moines%2C_Iowa_-_Winter_on_the_Des_Moines_River_%2824805016620%29.jpg/1920px-Morning_Skyline_-_Des_Moines%2C_Iowa_-_Winter_on_the_Des_Moines_River_%2824805016620%29.jpg","title":"Des Moines","desc":"Des Moines, Iowa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Tony Webster from Minneapolis, Minnesota, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morning_Skyline_-_Des_Moines,_Iowa_-_Winter_on_the_Des_Moines_River_(24805016620).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Des Moines, Iowa."}},
{"id":"gw-101","num":101,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sierra Leone","answerText":"Sierra Leone","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 September 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sierra Leone"},"facts":[["Capital","Freetown"],["Population","8.80M (2025)"],["Largest city","Freetown"],["Area","71,740 km² (27,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","sierra leone"],"abstract":"<b>Sierra Leone</b> lies in West Africa between about 7° and 10° north, extending some 340 km (210 miles) from north to south and at most about 300 km (190 miles) from east to west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Atlantic coastline runs about 400 km (250 miles) along the south and west, and its highest point is Loma Mansa, at 1,948 m (6,391 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physical regions divide it: coastal plains over 14 per cent of the ground, interior plains over 43 per cent, plateau over 22 and hills and mountains over 21.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands at the northern limit of the equatorial rainforest and is one of the most humid countries in Africa, with mean rainfall of 2,526 mm (99 inches) a year ranging from 1,900 to more than 4,000 mm (75 to 157 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A pronounced dry season runs from December to March and takes only 6 or 7 per cent of the year's rain; relative humidity reaches 95 to 100 per cent in the wet season and can fall to 20 per cent under the harmattan, with daily mean temperatures between 25 and 28°C (77 and 82°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Twelve river basins drain the country, of which the most important, from west to east, are the Kolente, Kaba, Rokel, Pampana, Sewa, Moa and Mano; only a ninth to a sixth of the year's discharge comes between December and April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seven vegetation types are recognised — moist rainforest, semi-deciduous, montane, mangrove, savannah, farm bush and swamp forest — and its fish stocks are the most diverse along the west coast of Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Previously under British sovereignty, the country became independent on 27 April 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 27 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1991, reinstated in 1996, declares it a sovereign republic whose boundaries are set out in the first schedule of that text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Sierra Leone,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/I9758EN/i9758en.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sierra Leone,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sierra-leone. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Sierra Leone</i>, chapter 1, section 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Sierra_Leone_2013. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Sierra Leone — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sl.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Sierra Leone — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=sl. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Flag_of_Sierra_Leone.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sierra_Leone.svg)","alt":"The flag of Sierra Leone: three horizontal bands of green, white and blue."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Tiwa_Island_-_flickr.jpg/1920px-Tiwa_Island_-_flickr.jpg","title":"Tiwai Island, Sierra Leone","desc":"Tiwai Island.","credit":"Dorothy Voorhees, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiwa_Island_-_flickr.jpg","alt":"A broad river running past dense rainforest."}},
{"id":"gw-102","num":102,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Togo","answerText":"Togo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Togo"},"facts":[["Capital","Lomé"],["Population","8.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Lomé"],["Area","56,785 km² (21,925 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","togo"],"abstract":"<b>Togo</b> is a narrow West African country fronting the Gulf of Guinea, and its relief runs through a coastal strip, plains, plateaus, mountains and valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two sedimentary basins frame it: one of clay and sandstone under the savannah region in the north, and a coastal basin of sand, limestone and clay in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between them lies a crystalline shield of schists, micaschists, quartzites and gneiss carrying the Kara, central and plateau regions, and its vegetation is made up of savannah and forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two climates divide the country: a subtropical one in the south with two rainy seasons alternating with dry ones, and a Sudanian one in the north with a single rainy season, mean rainfall over the whole standing at 1,168 mm (46 inches) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean maximum temperatures rise northward from 30°C (86°F) on the coast to 34°C (93°F) at Mango, while mean minima fall the other way, from 23°C (73°F) to 13°C (55°F), and potential evapotranspiration runs from 1,500 mm (59 inches) in the southwestern hills to nearly 2,000 mm (79 inches) in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three basins drain it: the Volta basin over 26,700 km² (10,300 sq mi), carrying the Oti, Kara and Mô to the northwest; the Mono basin over 21,300 km² (8,200 sq mi), carrying the Mono, Anié and Amou to the southeast; and the Lake Togo basin over 8,000 km² (3,100 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of the smallest countries in West Africa, it nonetheless holds aquatic ecosystems, mangroves and several forest types, with three amphibians and one plant found nowhere else and about a tenth of its territory protected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Togoland had been administered by France as a United Nations trust territory, and the republic became independent in 1960 under Prime Minister Sylvanus Olympio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1992 calls the Togolese Republic a secular, democratic and social state of law, one and indivisible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Profil de Pays — Togo,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0211fr/CA0211FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Togo,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/togo. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Togolese Republic</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Togo_2007. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Togo — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tg.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Togo — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=tg. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Flag_of_Togo.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Togo.svg)","alt":"The flag of Togo: five green and yellow stripes with a white star on a red canton."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Togo_Taberma_house_02.jpg/1920px-Togo_Taberma_house_02.jpg","title":"Koutammakou, Togo","desc":"Koutammakou.","credit":"Erik Kristensen, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Togo_Taberma_house_02.jpg","alt":"A view of Koutammakou."}},
{"id":"gw-103","num":103,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Laos","answerText":"Laos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lao PDR</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 December 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Laos"},"facts":[["Capital","Vientiane"],["Population","7.90M (2025)"],["Largest city","Vientiane"],["Area","236,800 km² (91,430 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","laos"],"abstract":"<b>Laos</b> is a landlocked country in mainland Southeast Asia, stretching more than 1,700 km (1,060 miles) along a north–south axis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some four fifths of it is hill and mountain, rising to 2,820 m (9,250 feet) at Phu Bia, and about a third of the ground has a slope steeper than 30 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Mekong is its main river: nine tenths of the country lies within that basin, a quarter of the basin lies within the country, and it yields 35 per cent of the river's total flow through some 39 main tributaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical, with a rainy season from mid-April to mid-October under the humid southwest monsoon, which brings about three quarters of the year's rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean rainfall is 1,834 mm (72 inches) a year but runs from 1,300 mm (51 inches) in the northern valleys to over 3,700 mm (146 inches) at high elevations in the south, and the Mekong's level may swing by 20 m (66 feet) between the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Five ecoregions divide it, forest covers more than 40 per cent of the land, and the Mekong and its tributaries alone hold some 500 native fish species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its constitution dates the founding of the unified Lane Xang country to the middle of the 14th century, under Chao Fa Ngum, and records that the land was repeatedly invaded from the 18th century onward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A protectorate of France, it became an independent state within the French Union in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 14 December 1955, and the Lao People's Democratic Republic was proclaimed on 2 December 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first constitution, promulgated on 15 August 1991, calls the republic an independent country with sovereignty over its territory, unified and indivisible and belonging to all its multi-ethnic people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Lao People's Democratic Republic,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0397en/CA0397EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Laos,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/laos. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Lao People's Democratic Republic</i>, preamble and article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Laos_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Lao People's Democratic Republic — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/la.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Lao People's Democratic Republic — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=la. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Flag_of_Laos.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Laos.svg)","alt":"The flag of Laos: red bands above and below a broad blue band bearing a white disc."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/The_river_of_Kuang_si_waterfalls.jpg","title":"Kuang Si Falls, Laos","desc":"Kuang Si Falls.","credit":"NruasPaoYPP, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_river_of_Kuang_si_waterfalls.jpg","alt":"A view of Kuang Si Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-104","num":104,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Hong Kong","answerText":"Hong Kong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Joint Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Transfer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Hong Kong"},"facts":[["Capital","Tamar (government seat)"],["Population","7.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","?"],["Area","1,107 km² (427 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","asia","hong kong"],"abstract":"<b>Hong Kong</b> was a British colony, and the United States had a consular post in it by 1843, more than a century and a half before it was handed to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its future was settled by treaty rather than by war: the joint declaration of the two governments on the question of Hong Kong was signed at Peking on 19 December 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Parliament gave effect to it in the Hong Kong Act 1985, whose first section provides that from 1 July 1997 the Crown should no longer have sovereignty or jurisdiction over any part of the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That section was to come into force on the exchange of instruments of ratification of the declaration, so an Act of one parliament was made to wait upon a treaty with another state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Basic Law that now governs it declares the region an inalienable part of China and authorises a high degree of autonomy, with executive, legislative and independent judicial power including final adjudication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It also provides that the socialist system shall not be practised there and that the previous capitalist system and way of life shall remain unchanged for fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The territory had already entered the world trading system in its own right, acceding to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 23 April 1986 while still a British dependency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995, two and a half years before the transfer, and sits there as Hong Kong, China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its own census counted 7,413,070 people in 2021 against 7,336,585 five years earlier, a rise of about one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The international counts differ by roughly a hundred thousand, and the World Bank's series has fallen in each of the last two years rather than risen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “China, Hong Kong SAR,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: China,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","Hong Kong Act 1985, c. 15, s. 1, The National Archives, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1985/15/section/1/enacted. [Open access]","Constitutional and Mainland Affairs Bureau, “The Basic Law of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the People’s Republic of China, Chapter I: General Principles,” Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, https://www.basiclaw.gov.hk/en/basiclaw/chapter1.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Hong Kong, China and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/hong_kong_china_e.htm. [Open access]","Census and Statistics Department, “2021 Population Census,” Government of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, https://www.censtatd.gov.hk/en/scode600.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Hong Kong SAR, China,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HKG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Flag_of_Hong_Kong.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hong_Kong.svg)","alt":"The flag of Hong Kong: a white five-petalled bauhinia flower on a red field."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Hong_Kong_island_and_Victoria_Harbour.jpg/1920px-Hong_Kong_island_and_Victoria_Harbour.jpg","title":"Victoria Harbour, Hong Kong","desc":"Victoria Harbour.","credit":"PrzemekJaczewski, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hong_Kong_island_and_Victoria_Harbour.jpg","alt":"A dense harbour city of towers seen from above."}},
{"id":"gw-105","num":105,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Turkmenistan","answerText":"Turkmenistan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2008</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turkmenistan"},"facts":[["Capital","Ashgabat"],["Population","7.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Ashgabat"],["Area","491,210 km² (189,660 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","turkmenistan"],"abstract":"<b>Turkmenistan</b> is a Central Asian country with the Caspian Sea along its western edge, and the Kara Kum desert covers four fifths of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Kopetdag chain runs along the southwest, where the Shakhshakh peak reaches 2,912 m (9,554 feet), and the highest ground is Airybaba, at 3,137 m (10,292 feet) in the Kougitantau range in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 12 per cent of the surface is water, talus, rock and precipice rather than soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is sharply continental and arid: mean rainfall is about 191 mm (8 inches) a year, from under 80 mm (3 inches) in the northeast to 300 mm (12 inches) in the Kopetdag, and it falls between October and April, summers sometimes passing with none at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean annual temperature runs from 11 to 18°C (52 to 64°F), January averages about minus 4°C (25°F) over most of the country, July exceeds 30°C (86°F) everywhere, and evaporation from open water reaches 2,000 to 2,300 mm (79 to 91 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Almost all its water arrives in the Amu Darya, which rises in snow-covered mountains far upstream, and the Kara Kum canal, built in the 1950s and at 1,400 km (870 miles) the longest in the world, draws 10 to 12 km³ (2.4 to 2.9 cubic miles) from that river each year to irrigate more than 1.2 million hectares (3.0 million acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is counted one of the world's centres of genetic diversity, and the Kopetdag, Koytendag and Badhyz mountains, though under 5 per cent of the territory, are isolated refuges for its most ancient biological relics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Formerly a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, it formally declared independence in October 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution declares it a democratic, legal and secular state with a presidential form of government, and its preamble names the strengthening of its internationally recognised status of permanent neutrality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Turkmenistan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2012, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0371en/CA0371EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Turkmenistan,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/turkmenistan. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Turkmenistan</i>, preamble and article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Turkmenistan_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Turkmenistan — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tm.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Turkmenistan — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=tm. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Flag_of_Turkmenistan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Turkmenistan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Turkmenistan: a green field with a white crescent and five stars beside a vertical carpet-pattern stripe."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Darvasa_gas_crater_panorama.jpg/1920px-Darvasa_gas_crater_panorama.jpg","title":"Darvaza gas crater, Turkmenistan","desc":"Darvaza gas crater.","credit":"Tormod Sandtorv, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darvasa_gas_crater_panorama.jpg","alt":"A view of Darvaza gas crater."}},
{"id":"gw-106","num":106,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Libya","answerText":"Libya","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ottoman rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1835</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded to Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 October 1912</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 December 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Libya"},"facts":[["Capital","Tripoli"],["Population","7.50M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tripoli"],["Area","1,759,540 km² (679,362 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","libya"],"abstract":"<b>Libya</b> stands on the southern shore of the Mediterranean, and about 95 per cent of it is desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physiographic regions make it up: the coastal plains, of varying width; the northern mountains close behind them, the Jabal Nafusah in the west and the Jabal al Akhdar in the east; the internal depressions with their oases across the centre; and the southern and western mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The weather changes abruptly between the sea to the north and the Sahara to the south: the coastal strip has dry summers and relatively wet winters, the two highland belts a steppe climate with more rain, more humidity and snow on the hills, and the interior a pre-desert and desert regime of torrid heat and wide daily swings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain is extremely scarce, 93 per cent of the surface receiving under 100 mm (4 inches) a year and the country as a whole averaging 56 mm (2 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only the two northern regions behind the coast exceed the 250 to 300 mm (10 to 12 inches) reckoned necessary for rainfed farming, and temperatures range from above 40°C (104°F) in summer to below freezing in winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Before 1835 the country's northwest was one of the Barbary states of North Africa, owing a loose allegiance to the Ottoman Empire rather than standing wholly apart from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its authority to conduct its own international relations ended in that year, when the Ottoman Empire reasserted its formerly nominal suzerainty over its Libyan provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ottoman Empire ceded the territory to Italy by the Treaty of Ouchy on 18 October 1912, and it remained an Italian colony until 1947, having been under joint Franco-British occupation from 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence was declared on 24 December 1951, the country becoming a kingdom under Idris I, and it was admitted to the United Nations on 14 December 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The interim constitutional declaration issued after the revolution of 17 February 2011 calls it an independent democratic state in which the people are the source of all powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Libya,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/i9803en/I9803EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Libya,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/libya. [Open access]","<i>Interim Constitutional Declaration of Libya</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Libya_2012. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Libya — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ly.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Barbary Wars, 1801–1805 and 1815–1816,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1801-1829/barbary-wars. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/Flag_of_Libya.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Libya.svg)","alt":"The flag of Libya: red, black and green bands with a white crescent and star at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Leptis_Magna_%2829%29_%288288918733%29.jpg/1920px-Leptis_Magna_%2829%29_%288288918733%29.jpg","title":"Leptis Magna, Libya","desc":"Leptis Magna.","credit":"joepyrek from Richmond, Va, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leptis_Magna_(29)_(8288918733).jpg","alt":"A view of Leptis Magna."}},
{"id":"gw-107","num":107,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kyrgyzstan","answerText":"Kyrgyzstan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kyrgyzstan"},"facts":[["Capital","Bishkek"],["Population","7.30M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bishkek"],["Area","199,949 km² (77,201 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","kyrgyzstan"],"abstract":"<b>Kyrgyzstan</b> is a landlocked country of Central Asia dominated by the western reaches of the Tian Shan in the northeast and the Pamir-Alay in the southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 94 per cent of it rises above 1,000 m (3,300 feet) and 40 per cent above 3,000 m (9,800 feet); the highest mountain, Victory Peak, reaches 7,439 m (24,406 feet) and stands in the Mustag massif, among the world's largest glaciers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Fergana range separates the eastern and central mountains from the Fergana valley in the west and southwest, and the other lowlands are the Chu and Talas valleys in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Issyk-Kul, in the northeast, covers 6,236 km² (2,408 sq mi) and is the world's second largest high-mountain lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is continental, with hot summers and frost throughout the country in winter: valley means run from minus 18°C (0°F) in January to 28°C (82°F) in July, and rain and snow average 533 mm (21 inches) a year, from 150 mm (6 inches) on the Fergana plains to over 1,000 mm (39 inches) in the mountains, falling mainly between October and April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> No river flows into the country: 87 per cent of it is ground on which flow is generated, most rivers fed by glacier or snowmelt and peaking between April and July, and the Syr Darya basin alone covers 55 per cent of the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its mountain pastures carry the main agricultural sector, providing between 60 and 89 per cent of livestock feed and more than 200 kinds of medicinal herb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Formerly a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, the country became independent in August 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was admitted to the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 2021 calls the Kyrgyz Republic an independent, sovereign, democratic, unitary, secular and social state governed by the rule of law, and its preamble invokes the precepts of Manas the Magnificent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Kyrgyzstan,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2012, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0367en/CA0367EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kyrgyzstan,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kyrgyzstan. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Kyrgyz Republic</i>, preamble and article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Kyrgyz_Republic_2021. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Kyrgyzstan — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kg.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Kyrgyzstan — Biodiversity Facts,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=kg. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Flag_of_Kyrgyzstan.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kyrgyzstan.svg)","alt":"The flag of Kyrgyzstan: a red field bearing a yellow sun crossed by the roof-poles of a yurt."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Issyk-Kul%2C_Kyrgyzstan_%2843713946455%29.jpg/1920px-Issyk-Kul%2C_Kyrgyzstan_%2843713946455%29.jpg","title":"Issyk-Kul, Kyrgyzstan","desc":"Issyk-Kul.","credit":"Ninara from Helsinki, Finland, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Issyk-Kul,_Kyrgyzstan_(43713946455).jpg","alt":"A wide mountain lake with a sandy beach in front of bare hills."}},
{"id":"gw-108","num":108,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Paraguay","answerText":"Paraguay","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 May 1811</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Paraguay"},"facts":[["Capital","Asunción"],["Population","7.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Asunción"],["Area","406,752 km² (157,048 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","paraguay"],"abstract":"<b>Paraguay</b> stands in the centre of South America between 19°18' and 27°31' south, with the Tropic of Capricorn running almost through the middle of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Paraguay river divides it into two very different halves: the Chaco in the west, which is 61 per cent of the ground, and the eastern region between the Paraguay and the Paraná, which is the other 39 per cent and has a rolling topography over the Guaraní aquifer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its uplands are the Amambay, Mbaracayú and Caaguazú ranges in the east, none of which passes 850 m (2,790 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical to subtropical with a mean of 22°C (72°F), and rainfall varies fourfold across the country, from 400 mm (16 inches) a year in the northwest of the Chaco to more than 1,700 mm (67 inches) in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain is least in July and August and heaviest between October and March, usually as storms; the eastern region is humid with moderate swings in temperature and no part of it wholly free of frost, while the Chaco alternates floods with very dry spells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country lies across two geological formations, the Brazilian shield and the Andean depression, and holds warm humid forests and warm dry ones, with about 13,000 plant species of which more than two thirds are regionally endemic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the 1,130 mm (44 inches) of rain that falls on it each year, three quarters is lost again to evaporation and transpiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It declared its independence from Spain on 15 May 1811, and much of the century and a half that followed was marked by civil war and military coups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been a member of the United Nations since 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1992, declares the republic forever free and independent and constitutes it as a social state of law, unitary, indivisible and decentralised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Perfil de País — Paraguay,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0443es/CA0443ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Paraguay,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/paraguay. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Paraguay</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Paraguay_2011. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Paraguay — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/py.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Paraguay — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=py. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Flag_of_Paraguay.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Paraguay.svg)","alt":"The flag of Paraguay: red, white and blue bands with an emblem at the centre of the white."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/La_Sant%C3%ADsima_Trinidad_de_Paran%C3%A1_-_Church_ruins.jpg/1920px-La_Sant%C3%ADsima_Trinidad_de_Paran%C3%A1_-_Church_ruins.jpg","title":"the Jesuit missions of the Guaranis, Paraguay","desc":"The church ruins at La Santísima Trinidad de Paraná.","credit":"P. Hughes, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Sant%C3%ADsima_Trinidad_de_Paran%C3%A1_-_Church_ruins.jpg","alt":"Red stone church ruins standing on cropped grass."}},
{"id":"gw-109","num":109,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nicaragua","answerText":"Nicaragua","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1823 – 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1987</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nicaragua"},"facts":[["Capital","Managua"],["Population","7.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Managua"],["Area","130,374 km² (50,338 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","central america","nicaragua"],"abstract":"<b>Nicaragua</b> lies at the centre of the Central American isthmus between 10° and 14° north, with the Caribbean along its eastern side and the Pacific along its western.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three regions divide it: the Pacific region, 15.2 per cent of the territory, with the best agricultural soils and the densest settlement; the Central region, 29.6 per cent, mountainous with small intermontane valleys and soils of middling fertility; and the Caribbean region, 55.2 per cent, flat and forested with poor soils and little-exploited natural resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four climates are recognised under Köppen's scheme: a tropical savannah on the Pacific side and the western slope of the Central cordillera, up to 1,000 m (3,300 feet), with a marked dry season of four to six months and 500 to 2,000 mm (20 to 79 inches) of rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then a dry subtropical climate over the Central massif; a monsoon forest climate over the middle and northern Caribbean plain, three fifths of the country, with 2,400 to 5,000 mm (94 to 197 inches) of rain and a steady 27°C (81°F); and a rainforest climate in the southeast taking over 4,000 mm (157 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean rainfall over the whole territory is 2,280 mm (90 inches) a year, most of it between May and November, and the Caribbean side is periodically struck by tropical storms and hurricanes between July and November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It holds the largest and best preserved tracts of tropical rainforest and lowland ecosystem in Central America, with 463 km (288 miles) of Caribbean and 372 km (231 miles) of Pacific coast, and its Caribbean forests still carry tapir, harpy eagle and jaguar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those forests are a vital link in the chain of humid forest running from Mexico to Colombia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> After independence from Spain and from the Mexican Empire the country joined the Federation of Central American States in 1823.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It withdrew from that federation on 5 November 1838, and has been a member of the United Nations since 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1987, calls it an independent, free, sovereign, unitary and indivisible state, organised as a democratic republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Perfil de País — Nicaragua,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0421es/CA0421ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nicaragua,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nicaragua. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Nicaragua</i>, articles 6 and 7, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Nicaragua_2014. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Nicaragua — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ni.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Nicaragua — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ni. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/19/Flag_of_Nicaragua.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nicaragua.svg)","alt":"The flag of Nicaragua: blue, white and blue bands with the national coat of arms at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Ometepe_Island_with_Concepcion_Volcano_-_From_Lake_Nicaragua_-_Nicaragua_-_01_%2831377352020%29.jpg/1920px-Ometepe_Island_with_Concepcion_Volcano_-_From_Lake_Nicaragua_-_Nicaragua_-_01_%2831377352020%29.jpg","title":"Ometepe, Nicaragua","desc":"Ometepe and the Concepción volcano.","credit":"Adam Jones from Kelowna, BC, Canada, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ometepe_Island_with_Concepcion_Volcano_-_From_Lake_Nicaragua_-_Nicaragua_-_01_(31377352020).jpg","alt":"A cloud-capped volcano rising from a lake."}},
{"id":"gw-110","num":110,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Serbia","answerText":"Serbia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">FRY declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 2000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Union ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 June 2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Serbia"},"facts":[["Capital","Belgrade"],["Population","6.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Belgrade"],["Area","88,444 km² (34,148 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","serbia"],"abstract":"<b>Serbia</b> was one of the two republics that declared a new Federal Republic of Yugoslavia in April 1992, after Slovenia, Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina and Macedonia had seceded from the old federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 21 May 1992 the United States announced that it would not recognise that new state as the successor to the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, which is what the two republics claimed it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American ambassador was recalled from the capital while the mission stayed open under a chargé d'affaires, and relations were severed on 23 March 1999, one day before the NATO bombing began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly then admitted the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia as a new member on 1 November 2000, without a vote at its 48th meeting, so the state took a seat rather than inheriting one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eleven days later an exchange of letters between Presidents Clinton and Kostunica established full diplomatic relations, and the embassy at the capital reopened in May 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The federation became Serbia and Montenegro in 2003, Montenegro invoked its right to secede and declared independence on 3 June 2006, and two days later Serbia declared itself the successor to the union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kosovo's own declaration of independence, adopted on 17 February 2008, was referred by the General Assembly to the International Court of Justice in October of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Serbia filed a written statement, written comments and replies to the judges' questions in those proceedings, and in July 2010 the Court held that the declaration had not violated international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Court described Security Council resolution 1244 of 10 June 1999 as having established a temporary and exceptional legal régime that superseded the Serbian legal order on an interim basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations counts its people expressly without Kosovo but marks its area with no such note, and its population is falling: the World Bank's series is down more than half a million since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Serbia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/rs.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Serbia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/serbia. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 55/12, adopted without vote at the 48th meeting, 1 November 2000, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/426129. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Accordance with International Law of the Unilateral Declaration of Independence in Respect of Kosovo,” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/141. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Serbia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SRB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Flag_of_Serbia.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia.svg)","alt":"The flag of Serbia: red, blue and white bands with the national coat of arms toward the hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/P037152-661920_-_Belgrade_fortress_stand_at_the_downtown_of_Belgrade.jpg/1920px-P037152-661920_-_Belgrade_fortress_stand_at_the_downtown_of_Belgrade.jpg","title":"Belgrade Fortress, Serbia","desc":"Belgrade Fortress.","credit":"Oliver Bunic, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P037152-661920_-_Belgrade_fortress_stand_at_the_downtown_of_Belgrade.jpg","alt":"A view of Belgrade Fortress."}},
{"id":"gw-111","num":111,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bulgaria","answerText":"Bulgaria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 October 1908</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 May 1909</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bulgaria"},"facts":[["Capital","Sofia"],["Population","6.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Sofia"],["Area","110,996 km² (42,856 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","bulgaria"],"abstract":"<b>Bulgaria</b> had diplomatic relations with the United States for six years before it was recognised by it, which is the reverse of the usual order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John B. Jackson, the American minister to Greece, Romania and Serbia, presented his credentials at the capital on 19 September 1903, and that date marks the establishment of relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition waited on independence, which was declared on 5 October 1908; on 3 May 1909 Secretary of State Knox instructed a chargé to convey President Taft's congratulations on the country's admission to the community of sovereign and independent states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An earlier revolt against Ottoman rule in 1876 had produced two treaties in quick succession, San Stefano establishing a large Bulgarian state and Berlin in 1878 replacing it with a smaller principality north of the Balkan Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A consular agency opened at the capital on 12 January 1912 reporting to Bucharest, became a consulate general in 1915, and an American legation followed on 18 March 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Relations were severed when the country declared war on the United States on 13 December 1941, though the United States did not declare war in return until 5 June 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Roosevelt believed it would not have declared war without pressure from Nazi Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, one of the sixteen states the General Assembly took in together at its 555th plenary meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Accession to the World Trade Organization came on 1 December 1996 and to the European Union on 1 January 2007, and the euro area followed on 1 January 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its population is falling fast, the World Bank's series down about 550,000 since 2015, and the United Nations count sits some 280,000 above the two European ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bulgaria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bulgaria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bulgaria. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Bulgaria and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/bulgaria_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Bulgaria,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/bulgaria_en. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “555th Plenary Meeting, Wednesday, 14 December 1955,” Official Records of the Tenth Session, A/PV.555, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/646501. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bulgaria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BGR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Flag_of_Bulgaria.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bulgaria.svg)","alt":"The flag of Bulgaria: horizontal bands of white, green and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Rila_Monastery%2C_August_2013.jpg/1920px-Rila_Monastery%2C_August_2013.jpg","title":"Rila Monastery, Bulgaria","desc":"Rila Monastery.","credit":"Raggatt2000, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rila_Monastery,_August_2013.jpg","alt":"A view of Rila Monastery."}},
{"id":"gw-112","num":112,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"El Salvador","answerText":"El Salvador","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">United Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1823 – 1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"El Salvador"},"facts":[["Capital","San Salvador"],["Population","6.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","San Salvador"],["Area","21,041 km² (8,124 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","central america","el salvador"],"abstract":"<b>El Salvador</b> lies on the Pacific coast of Central America between 13°09' and 13°27' north, the smallest of the Central American republics and the only one with no Caribbean shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three relief regions rise between sea level and 2,700 m (8,860 feet), the first the coastal plain along the Pacific, at most 32 km (20 miles) wide and 15 per cent of the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then the central plateau of hills, valleys and tablelands, averaging 600 m (2,000 feet) and holding a quarter of the ground and most of the people; and beyond it the mountainous zone of ranges, narrow valleys and ravines over the remaining 60 per cent, where most of the coffee is grown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its weather is set by humid air carried from the Caribbean on the trade winds, by the intertropical convergence zone, which keeps temperatures steady through the year, and by northerly winds bringing cooler air at the year's end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean rainfall is 1,784 mm (70 inches) a year, from 1,400 mm (55 inches) in the northwest to 2,400 mm (94 inches) around the Santa Ana and Izalco volcanoes, falling in two peaks in June and September with a dry season from November to April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Just over a quarter of the country is forested, counting shade-grown coffee, and between 2000 and 2010 it lost 6.6 per cent of that cover to subsistence farming, sugarcane and pasture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Temperature follows the relief, from 22 to 28°C (72 to 82°F) on the coastal plains down to between 10 and 16°C (50 and 61°F) above 1,800 m (5,900 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1821 it declared independence from Spain along with the other Central American provinces, and in 1823 the United Provinces of Central America was formed of five under General Manuel José Arce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It declared itself an independent republic in 1839, though several decades of frequent revolutions followed, and it has been a member of the United Nations since 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1983, calls it a sovereign state in which sovereignty lies with the people, and declares the territory over which it exercises jurisdiction irreducible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Perfil de País — El Salvador,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0419es/CA0419ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: El Salvador,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/el-salvador. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of El Salvador</i>, articles 83 and 84, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/El_Salvador_2014. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “El Salvador — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sv.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “El Salvador — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=sv. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Flag_of_El_Salvador.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_El_Salvador.svg)","alt":"The flag of El Salvador: blue, white and blue bands with the national coat of arms at the centre."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/36/Santa_Ana_Volcano.USAF.C-130.1.jpg","title":"Santa Ana Volcano, El Salvador","desc":"Santa Ana Volcano.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Ana_Volcano.USAF.C-130.1.jpg","alt":"A view of Santa Ana Volcano."}},
{"id":"gw-113","num":113,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Republic of the Congo","answerText":"Republic of the Congo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Multi-party rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Congo"},"facts":[["Capital","Brazzaville"],["Population","6.50M (2025)"],["Largest city","Brazzaville"],["Area","342,000 km² (132,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","republic of the congo"],"abstract":"The <b>Republic of the Congo</b> straddles the equator in central Africa, between 3°30' north and 5° south, with 120 km (75 miles) of Atlantic frontage in the southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its natural regions are the coastal plain, the Mayombe chain, the Niari-Nyanga plain, the Chaillu massif, the Batéké plateaux, the Congo basin depression and the plateaux of the northwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers about 58 per cent of the territory and savannah about 42, over acid soils falling into two main classes, ferralitic and hydromorphic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three climates meet here: an equatorial one in the north, very humid, with more than 1,700 mm (67 inches) of rain and means between 24 and 26°C (75 and 79°F); a humid tropical one in the southwest with 1,200 to 1,700 mm (47 to 67 inches) and monthly means between 21 and 27°C (70 and 81°F); and a subequatorial one over the plateaux and the depression with about 1,600 mm (63 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole territory is well watered, averaging 1,650 mm (65 inches) a year against a potential evapotranspiration of about 1,300 mm (51 inches), and its drainage runs into two basins, the Congo over 72 per cent of the country and the Kouilou-Niari over 16.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its landscape carries the second largest rainforest in the world: the Mayombe, Chaillu and North-Congo forests cover 22.4 million hectares (55 million acres) between them, and forest of some type is 65 per cent of the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before independence the country was part of French Equatorial Africa, whose administration sided with the Free French, and its chief town served as the symbolic capital of Free France between 1940 and 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Independence from French Equatorial Africa was proclaimed on 15 August 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the last two decades of the Cold War it allied chiefly with the Soviet Union and the Eastern bloc, and it adopted multi-party democracy in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September 1960, and its constitution calls the republic a state of law, sovereign, unitary and indivisible, decentralised, secular and democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Profil de Pays — Congo,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9919fr/I9919FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Republic of the Congo,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-republic. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of the Congo</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Congo_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Congo — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cg.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Congo — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=cg. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_the_Congo.svg)","alt":"The flag of the Republic of the Congo: a yellow diagonal band between green and red."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Loxodonta_cyclotis_29048923.jpg/1920px-Loxodonta_cyclotis_29048923.jpg","title":"Nouabalé-Ndoki National Park, the Republic of the Congo","desc":"Nouabalé-Ndoki National Park.","credit":"Matt Muir, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loxodonta_cyclotis_29048923.jpg","alt":"A view of Nouabalé-Ndoki National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-114","num":114,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Singapore","answerText":"Singapore","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Singapore"},"facts":[["Capital","Singapore"],["Population","6.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Singapore"],["Area","728 km² (281 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","singapore"],"abstract":"<b>Singapore</b> is a city-state whose capital and country are the same place, and the American consulate there is older than the state by well over a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joseph Balestier was appointed consul on 4 July 1836, at a port American merchants in the China trade had been calling at on their way to and from Canton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island had been under British sovereignty since the early 19th century, became independent of the British Empire in 1963, joined the Federation of Malaysia, and broke away in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the new state on 11 August 1965 in a letter from Secretary of State Dean Rusk to the Singaporean foreign minister, S. Rajaratnam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth the same year on leaving that federation, and the United Nations admitted it on 21 September 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An American embassy followed on 4 April 1966 under Richard H. Donald as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It belonged to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 20 August 1973 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country is getting physically larger: the World Bank's surface-area series records 719 square kilometres for 2015 and 728 for 2023, so the reclamation of land from the sea shows up as arithmetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its three published areas differ, the Commonwealth giving 720 where the United Nations and the World Bank agree with each other, so any figure for it carries a date as well as a number.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two international counts of its people differ by about 240,000, and the United Nations puts all but a few thousand of them inside the capital city itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Singapore,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Singapore,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/singapore. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Singapore,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/singapore. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Singapore and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/singapore_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Singapore,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SGP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Singapore,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SGP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Flag_of_Singapore.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Singapore.svg)","alt":"The flag of Singapore: a red band above a white one, with a crescent and five stars in the upper hoist."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Supertree_Grove%2C_Gardens_by_the_Bay%2C_Singapore_-_20120712-02.jpg/1920px-Supertree_Grove%2C_Gardens_by_the_Bay%2C_Singapore_-_20120712-02.jpg","title":"Gardens by the Bay, Singapore","desc":"Gardens by the Bay.","credit":"Shiny Things ., CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Supertree_Grove,_Gardens_by_the_Bay,_Singapore_-_20120712-02.jpg","alt":"A view of Gardens by the Bay."}},
{"id":"gw-115","num":115,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Denmark","answerText":"Denmark","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 June 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 October 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1827</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Denmark"},"facts":[["Capital","Copenhagen"],["Population","6.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Copenhagen"],["Area","42,938 km² (16,579 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","denmark"],"abstract":"<b>Denmark</b> recognised the United States rather than the other way about, the American consul at the capital receiving his exequatur from the Danish government on or about 9 June 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That same act established consular relations, and diplomatic relations followed on 12 October 1801, when the Danish minister resident presented his credentials in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at the capital on 20 September 1827 under Henry Wheaton as chargé d'affaires, and the two governments have not severed relations since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation did close for the war: German forces occupied the city on 9 April 1940, the American minister left on 5 June, and the legation itself shut on 20 December 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He took up residence at Ottawa as minister to a Danish government that remained in Danish hands until German authorities seized complete control in August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation reopened on 16 June 1945 after the country's liberation, and the relationship since has been close enough for the United States to keep an early-warning radar facility at Thule in Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1917 Denmark had sold the Danish West Indies to the United States, and those islands are now the United States Virgin Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was among the original members of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 May 1950, and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the European Union on 1 January 1973 and negotiated an opt-out from the euro, so it keeps the krone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its published area and population expressly exclude the Faroe Islands and Greenland, which the United Nations shows separately, and the three sources for its size differ by only eighteen square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Denmark,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Denmark,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/denmark. [Open access]","European Union, “Denmark,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/denmark_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Denmark and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/denmark_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Denmark,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DNK/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/Flag_of_Denmark.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Denmark.svg)","alt":"The flag of Denmark: a white cross offset toward the hoist on a red field."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/The_Nyhavn_Canal_3.jpg/1920px-The_Nyhavn_Canal_3.jpg","title":"Nyhavn, Denmark","desc":"Nyhavn.","credit":"European Commission, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Nyhavn_Canal_3.jpg","alt":"A view of Nyhavn."}},
{"id":"gw-116","num":116,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lebanon","answerText":"Lebanon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">French mandate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lebanon"},"facts":[["Capital","Beirut"],["Population","5.80M (2025)"],["Largest city","Beirut"],["Area","10,452 km² (4,036 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","lebanon"],"abstract":"<b>Lebanon</b> is a mountainous country on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean, about 60 km (37 miles) wide and about 225 km (140 miles) long from north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four bands run in parallel from north to south: a flat narrow coastal strip; the Lebanon mountains, whose mid-range summits reach 1,000 m (3,300 feet) and whose highest point, Qurnat as Sawda, is 3,087 m (10,128 feet); the fertile Bekaa valley at about 900 m (2,950 feet); and the Anti-Lebanon chain, rising to 2,800 m (9,190 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 70 per cent of the land is carbonate rock laid down between the Middle Jurassic and the Eocene, and its soils are typically Mediterranean and generally calcareous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is Mediterranean, with heavy rain from November to May and dry conditions through the other seven months, and the sea, the relief and the desert beyond make many microclimates: the coast averages 20°C (68°F) over the year, the Bekaa 16°C (61°F) and the mountain zones below 10°C (50°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean rainfall is 823 mm (32 inches) a year, from 700 to 1,000 mm (28 to 39 inches) on the coast and 1,500 to 2,000 mm (59 to 79 inches) on the high mountains, but only 400 mm (16 inches) in the east and under 200 mm (8 inches) in the northeast; above 2,000 m (6,600 feet) it comes as snow, sustaining some 2,000 springs through the dry months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It holds one of the highest densities of plant diversity in the Mediterranean basin, with about 2,600 terrestrial plants, 3.5 per cent of them found nowhere else, and its western slopes carry the southernmost stands of the cedar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under the Ottoman Empire its foreign relations were conducted through the Sultan's government at Constantinople.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After that empire collapsed the League of Nations approved a French mandate over it and Syria in 1922, giving France exclusive control of their foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It achieved effective independence in 1944 and joined the United Nations on 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1926 calls it a sovereign, free and independent homeland, unitary in land, people and institutions, and a democratic parliamentary republic founded on public freedoms and social justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Lebanon,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0344en/CA0344EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lebanon,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lebanon. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Lebanese Republic</i>, preamble, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Lebanon_2004. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Lebanon — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lb.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Lebanon — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=lb. [Open access]"],"answerFlag":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Flag_of_Lebanon.svg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons (https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lebanon.svg)","alt":"The flag of Lebanon: red bands above and below a white one bearing a green cedar."},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Temple_of_Bacchus%2C_Baalbek%2C_Lebanon_%2849890013476%29.jpg/1920px-Temple_of_Bacchus%2C_Baalbek%2C_Lebanon_%2849890013476%29.jpg","title":"Baalbek, Lebanon","desc":"Baalbek.","credit":"Lodo from Moscow, Russia, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Bacchus,_Baalbek,_Lebanon_(49890013476).jpg","alt":"A view of Baalbek."}},
{"id":"gw-117","num":117,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Finland","answerText":"Finland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 May 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Finland"},"facts":[["Capital","Helsinki"],["Population","5.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Helsinki"],["Area","338,363 km² (130,643 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","finland"],"abstract":"<b>Finland</b> declared its independence from the Russian Empire on 6 December 1917, shortly after the Bolshevik revolution, and a civil war followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 7 May 1919 in a letter from Secretary of State Robert Lansing to the Finnish foreign minister Rudolf Holsti, having held back over concerns about the country's political instability and its ties with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 27 May 1919, when the acting secretary of state accepted Armas Herman Saastamoinen as provisional envoy of the de facto government, President Wilson being away at the Paris peace conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at Helsingfors on 19 March 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were severed on 30 June 1944 and the legation shut the same day, the State Department pointing to the Finnish government's admission of a military partnership with Germany against the Allies. <br><br> A special mission opened at Helsinki on 16 January 1945 after the armistice, full relations resumed on 1 September 1945, and the legation became an embassy on 15 November 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations only on 14 December 1955, ten years after that organisation was founded, and the European Union on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has been in the euro area since 1999 and the Schengen area since 2001, and it had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 25 May 1950 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Military non-alignment ended after Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine: it applied on 18 May 2022 and deposited its instrument of accession on 4 April 2023, becoming the alliance's 31st member on the anniversary of the day the treaty was signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its own figures have to be read twice, because the United Nations profile counts the autonomous Åland Islands in the population it prints and excludes them from the surface area printed beneath it, where Eurostat gives 338,363 square kilometres and 5,635,971 people for the whole country and the World Bank 5,646,436 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Finland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fi.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Finland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/finland. [Open access]","European Union, “Finland – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/finland_en. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO member countries,” https://www.nato.int/en/about-us/organization/nato-member-countries. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Finland and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/finland_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Finland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FIN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Suomenlinna_aerial.JPG","title":"Suomenlinna, Finland","desc":"Suomenlinna.","credit":"Migro, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suomenlinna_aerial.JPG","alt":"A view of Suomenlinna."}},
{"id":"gw-118","num":118,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Liberia","answerText":"Liberia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 November 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Liberia"},"facts":[["Capital","Monrovia"],["Population","5.70M (2025)"],["Largest city","Monrovia"],["Area","111,369 km² (43,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","liberia"],"abstract":"<b>Liberia</b> lies in West Africa, extending about 465 km (289 miles) from north to south, with an Atlantic coastline of about 520 km (323 miles) along its south and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its terrain is mostly flat to rolling coastal plain, rising to rolling plateau and low mountains in the northeast, and its shore is a line of lagoons, mangrove swamps and river-deposited sandbars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four physical regions lie parallel to that coast: the coastal plains up to 100 m (330 feet), hills from 100 to 300 m (330 to 980 feet), plateaus from 300 to 600 m (980 to 1,970 feet) and mountainous ground above 600 m.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical, hot and humid: winters are dry with hot days and cool to cold nights, summers wet and cloudy with frequent heavy showers, and the rainy season runs from April to November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mean rainfall is 2,391 mm (94 inches) a year, varying from 2,000 to 5,000 mm (79 to 197 inches), and an acute water deficit is nonetheless felt for three to five months, particularly in the uplands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some 97 per cent of the country drains from northeast to southwest, and six major rivers — the Mano, Lofa, Saint Paul, Saint John, Cestos and Cavalla — carry two thirds of it between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands in the Upper Guinean forest, one of the two most significant forest blocks in Africa, is counted among fourteen world centres of plant endemism, and holds more than 2,900 vascular plants, 600 birds and 150 mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state began in 1822 as a settlement founded for freedmen and recaptured slaves, and in 1847 that settlement constituted itself as a republic, recognised soon after by several European states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 2 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1986, calls it a unitary sovereign state divided into counties for administrative purposes, with a republican form of government in three separate and coordinate branches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Liberia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9751en/I9751EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Liberia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/liberia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Liberia</i>, article 1 of chapter 3, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Liberia_1986. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Liberia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lr.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Liberia — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=lr. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Loxodontacyclotis.jpg/1920px-Loxodontacyclotis.jpg","title":"Sapo National Park, Liberia","desc":"Sapo National Park.","credit":"Thomas Breuer, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loxodontacyclotis.jpg","alt":"A view of Sapo National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-119","num":119,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Norway","answerText":"Norway","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 October 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO founder</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy to Oslo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Norway"},"facts":[["Capital","Oslo"],["Population","5.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Oslo"],["Area","323,772 km² (125,009 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","norway"],"abstract":"<b>Norway</b> was transferred from Danish to Swedish rule by the Treaty of Kiel in 1814, and accepted a union with Sweden under a common monarch while keeping its own constitution and national assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American consulates had opened at Christiania, which took the name Oslo in 1925, and at Christiansand in 1809, nearly a century before independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It demanded a flag and a consular service of its own to promote its maritime commerce, and when Sweden would not concede, the assembly declared the union at an end on 7 June 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sweden accepted, a treaty of separation was signed on 26 October, and Secretary of State Elihu Root told the Norwegian foreign minister on 30 October that the United States recognised the separation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same message established relations by stating that Christian Hauge would be received as chargé d'affaires, and the first treaty between the two countries had been signed in 1893, while the union still stood. <br><br> Germany invaded on 9 April 1940, the government left Oslo for England, and the American legation closed on 15 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A legation near the Norwegian government opened in London on 2 August 1940 and became an embassy in May 1942 under Anthony J. Drexel Biddle Jr., who was accredited also to seven other exiled governments, and it moved back to Oslo on 31 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 27 November 1945, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 10 July 1948 and the World Trade Organization at its founding on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was one of the twelve countries whose foreign ministers signed the North Atlantic Treaty in Washington on 4 April 1949, and its own, Halvard M. Lange, later helped draft the alliance's report on non-military cooperation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How large it is depends on what is counted: the United Nations gives 323,772 square kilometres and footnotes its population as including Svalbard and Jan Mayen, where the World Bank gives 624,500, nearly twice as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Norway,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/no.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Norway,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/norway. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Norway,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NOR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO member countries,” https://www.nato.int/en/about-us/organization/nato-member-countries. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Norway and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/norway_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Geirangerfjord_.jpg/1920px-Geirangerfjord_.jpg","title":"Geirangerfjord, Norway","desc":"Geirangerfjord.","credit":"Andreas Trepte, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geirangerfjord_.jpg","alt":"A view of Geirangerfjord."}},
{"id":"gw-120","num":120,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Slovakia","answerText":"Slovakia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Czechoslovakia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 October 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 December 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Slovakia"},"facts":[["Capital","Bratislava"],["Population","5.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bratislava"],["Area","49,035 km² (18,933 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","slovakia"],"abstract":"<b>Slovakia</b> was part of the Kingdom of Hungary under the Dual Monarchy of 1867 to 1918, while Bohemia and Moravia belonged to its Austrian half; the two were joined in one state only in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Czecho-Slovak National Council in Paris as a de facto government at war with the German and Austro-Hungarian empires on 3 September 1918, and it declared independence on 18 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What that war made was not recognised in the next: when German forces occupied Prague in March 1939 the United States refused to recognise either the protectorate over Bohemia and Moravia or the establishment of a Slovak state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate general opened at Bratislava in 1947 and shut in 1950, after the communist government alleged that American diplomatic personnel were engaged in espionage and demanded their numbers be cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The federation ended by negotiation: Slovakia declared itself sovereign in July 1992, talks with the Czech Republic followed, and on 31 December 1992 the Czech and Slovak Federal Republic ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 1 January 1993 the two became separate states and the United States recognised both that day, saying an ambassador would be appointed shortly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American embassy at Bratislava followed three days later under Paul Hacker as chargé d’affaires, and the first ambassador, Theodore E. Russell, presented his credentials that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 19 January 1993, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade that 15 April, and the World Trade Organization at its founding on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined NATO on 29 March 2004 with six other countries, the largest wave of enlargement in the alliance’s history, and the European Union that 1 May, entering the Schengen area in 2007 and the euro area in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations and Eurostat give exactly the same area, 49,035 square kilometres, and different populations: 5,475 thousand, which the United Nations footnotes as a projection, against Eurostat’s 5,419,451 and the World Bank’s 5,413,813.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Slovakia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Czechoslovakia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/czechoslovakia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Slovakia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/slovakia. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO member countries,” https://www.nato.int/en/about-us/organization/nato-member-countries. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Slovak Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/slovak_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","European Union, “Slovakia – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/slovakia_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Slovak Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVK/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Spissky_Hrad-LV-2.jpg/1920px-Spissky_Hrad-LV-2.jpg","title":"Spiš Castle, Slovakia","desc":"Spiš Castle.","credit":"Pierre Bona, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spissky_Hrad-LV-2.jpg","alt":"A view of Spiš Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-121","num":121,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ireland","answerText":"Ireland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 July 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ireland"},"facts":[["Capital","Dublin"],["Population","5.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Dublin"],["Area","69,825 km² (26,960 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","ireland"],"abstract":"<b>Ireland</b> gained independence from the United Kingdom on 6 December 1921, when representatives of the two states signed the Anglo-Irish Treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That agreement gave independence to 26 counties, constituted as the Irish Free State, while six counties of northeast Ulster chose to remain part of the United Kingdom with limited self-government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American recognition took two and a half years more and passed through the former sovereign: on 24 June 1924 the British ambassador in Washington asked the United States to approve his government’s plan for an Irish minister to handle matters relating exclusively to the Free State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Secretary of State Charles E. Hughes replied on 28 June that the President would be pleased to receive a duly accredited minister plenipotentiary of the Free State, which recognised it as a state with autonomous control over its foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 7 October 1924, when Timothy A. Smiddy presented his credentials in Washington, and the American legation at Dublin opened on 27 July 1927 under Frederick A. Sterling. <br><br> The connections between the two countries date to the mid-19th century, when a large number of Irish immigrants came to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations at the 555th plenary meeting of the General Assembly on 14 December 1955, in the same admission as Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 22 December 1967 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined what is now the European Union on 1 January 1973 and the euro area on 1 January 1999, and it has negotiated an opt-out from the Schengen area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its published sizes disagree by 455 square kilometres, at 69,825 for the United Nations, 69,947 for Eurostat and 70,280 for the World Bank, and so do its populations, which run from a projected 5,308 thousand through 5,439,898 to 5,484,367.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ireland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ie.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ireland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ireland. [Open access]","European Union, “Ireland – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/ireland_en. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “555th Plenary Meeting, Wednesday, 14 December 1955,” Official Records of the Tenth Session, A/PV.555, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/646501. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Ireland and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/ireland_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Ireland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Ireland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Cliffs-Of-Moher-OBriens-From-South.JPG/1920px-Cliffs-Of-Moher-OBriens-From-South.JPG","title":"Cliffs of Moher, Ireland","desc":"Cliffs of Moher.","credit":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cliffs-Of-Moher-OBriens-From-South.JPG","alt":"A view of Cliffs of Moher."}},
{"id":"gw-122","num":122,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Central African Republic","answerText":"Central African Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2016</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Central African Rep."},"facts":[["Capital","Bangui"],["Population","5.50M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bangui"],["Area","622,984 km² (240,535 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","central african republic"],"abstract":"The <b>Central African Republic</b> lies at the heart of the African continent and is landlocked, with no access to the sea at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its relief is an Oubanguian ridge, a kind of peneplain between 400 and 800 m (1,300 and 2,600 feet), which divides the country's drainage between the basin of Lake Chad and that of the Congo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Fertit massif in the east and the Yadé in the west bound that peneplain and rise above 1,000 m (3,300 feet), while the soils are mainly ferralitic over sandstone, turning ferruginous tropical northward, with vertisols particular to the Chad basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vegetation runs from dense humid forest in the south to wooded and grassy savannah in the north, and the climate is humid tropical in the south and dry in the north, with a dry season from November to April and rains from May to October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four agro-ecological zones follow that gradient: an equatorial forest zone in the southwest with 1,500 to 1,800 mm (59 to 71 inches) of rain, a Sudano-Guinean zone with 1,100 to 1,500 mm (43 to 59 inches), a Sudano-Sahelian zone with 800 to 1,100 mm (31 to 43 inches) and a Sahelian zone of unstable rain and frequent drought, mean temperature standing between 25 and 26°C (77 and 79°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its two river basins are water towers for central Africa: the Chari drains north toward Lake Chad and the Oubangui south toward the sea, which is why the ridge divides the country so sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covered about 15 per cent of the territory in 1986 and was falling by 2.5 per cent a year between 1985 and 1996, with slash-and-burn farming and the use of wood for fuel by almost the whole population the main causes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Independence from French Equatorial Africa was proclaimed on 13 August 1960, under Prime Minister David Dacko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls it a state of law, unitary, sovereign, indivisible, secular and democratic, and makes the seizure of sovereignty by coup, rebellion or mutiny a crime that cannot lapse with time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Profil de Pays — République Centrafricaine,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9885fr/I9885FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Central African Republic,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/central-african-republic. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Central African Republic</i>, articles 1 and 24, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Central_African_Republic_2016. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Central African Republic — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cf.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Central African Republic — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=cf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Dzanga_Bai_forest_clearing_with_African_forest_elephants%2C_Central_African_Republic.jpg/1920px-Dzanga_Bai_forest_clearing_with_African_forest_elephants%2C_Central_African_Republic.jpg","title":"Dzanga-Sangha, the Central African Republic","desc":"Dzanga Bai in the Dzanga-Sangha reserve.","credit":"JosepMGracia, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dzanga_Bai_forest_clearing_with_African_forest_elephants,_Central_African_Republic.jpg","alt":"Forest elephants gathered in a clearing at the edge of rainforest."}},
{"id":"gw-123","num":123,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"New Zealand","answerText":"New Zealand","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 September 1907</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 February 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1947</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"New Zealand"},"facts":[["Capital","Wellington"],["Population","5.30M (2025)"],["Largest city","Auckland"],["Area","268,107 km² (103,517 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","new zealand"],"abstract":"<b>New Zealand</b> was raised from a colony to a self-governing Dominion of the British Empire on 26 September 1907, sovereign in its domestic affairs while the United Kingdom kept control of its relations with the wider world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 recognised the United Kingdom and the Dominions as autonomous communities within the empire, equal in status and in no way subordinate one to another in any aspect of their domestic or external affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Kingdom put that into law in the Statute of Westminster of December 1931, whose terms would not take effect in a Dominion until that Dominion’s own government adopted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the country as independent, with autonomous control of its foreign relations, on 16 February 1942, when the New Zealand envoy Walter Nash presented his credentials to President Roosevelt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statute was not adopted at home until the Statute of Westminster Adoption Act became law on 25 November 1947, five years after the recognition it would have justified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An American legation opened at Wellington on 1 April 1942 and was raised to an embassy in December 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat dates its membership to 1931 and the Statute of Westminster, so three bodies give three dates for one transition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polynesian settlers reached both main islands about 1280, dated by radiocarbon on the bones and gnawed seeds of the Pacific rat they brought with them, a millennium later than earlier chronologies proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Treaty of Waitangi, signed in 1840, is the nation’s founding agreement, celebrated and argued over ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was an original member of the United Nations from 24 October 1945, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 30 July 1948 and was a founding member of the World Trade Organization; its United Nations population expressly excludes the Cook Islands, Niue and Tokelau, and stands at 5,252,000 where the World Bank counts 5,324,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “New Zealand,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: New Zealand,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/new-zealand. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “New Zealand,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/new-zealand. [Open access]","Museum of New Zealand Te Papa Tongarewa, “The Treaty of Waitangi,” https://www.tepapa.govt.nz/discover-collections/read-watch-play/maori/treaty-waitangi. [Open access]","Janet M. Wilmshurst, Atholl J. Anderson, Thomas F. G. Higham, and Trevor H. Worthy, “Dating the Late Prehistoric Dispersal of Polynesians to New Zealand Using the Commensal Pacific Rat,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 22 (2008): 7676–80, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0801507105. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “New Zealand and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/new_zealand_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: New Zealand,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NZL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Milford_Sound_%28New_Zealand%29.JPG/1920px-Milford_Sound_%28New_Zealand%29.JPG","title":"Milford Sound, New Zealand","desc":"Milford Sound.","credit":"Maros M r a z ( Maros ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milford_Sound_(New_Zealand).JPG","alt":"A view of Milford Sound."}},
{"id":"gw-124","num":124,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Palestine","answerText":"Palestine","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Basic Law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2003</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Advisory opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 2004</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Observer status</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 November 2012</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Palestine"},"facts":[["Capital","Jerusalem (claimed); Ramallah is the seat"],["Population","5.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Gaza"],["Area","6,025 km² (2,326 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","palestine"],"abstract":"<b>Palestine</b> is two separated territories: the West Bank, landlocked west of the Jordan River and covering 5,655 km² (2,183 sq mi), and a narrow coastal strip on the Mediterranean of 365 km² (141 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five zones run east to west, the first the Jordan Valley, from 90 to 375 m (295 to 1,230 feet) above sea level, taking only 100 to 200 mm (4 to 8 inches) of rain a year and troubled by salinity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then the eastern slopes, between Mediterranean and desert, with 150 to 300 mm (6 to 12 inches); the central highlands at 400 to 1,000 m (1,300 to 3,300 feet), from 300 mm (12 inches) in the south to 600 mm (24 inches) in the north; and the semi-coastal region with 400 to 700 mm (16 to 28 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The coastal plain takes 200 to 400 mm (8 to 16 inches) and has fertile soils farmed on groundwater, though over-pumping has drawn the sea into its aquifer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is of the eastern Mediterranean type, with cool rainy winters and hot dry summers, and the limestone hills act as a porous sponge, absorbing most of the rain and returning much of it as springs along the margins of the highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under the arrangements as they stood in 2008 the territory was not recognised as a fully sovereign state and had full control only of Area A; in Area B, about 100 rural areas mapped by the Oslo Accords, security lay in Israeli hands, and Area C, 59 per cent of the West Bank, was under full Israeli control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the General Assembly's request the International Court of Justice gave an advisory opinion on 9 July 2004 on the legal consequences of the wall built in the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An application for full United Nations membership was submitted on 23 September 2011, and on 29 November 2012 the General Assembly accorded Palestine non-member observer State status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Forest covers 1.7 per cent of the land and 63 of its species are counted as threatened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its Basic Law, adopted in 2003 and amended in 2005, calls itself a temporary law for a transitional period and provides for a democratic parliamentary system founded on party pluralism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Occupied Palestinian Territory,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/CA0348EN/ca0348en.pdf. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “State of Palestine — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ps.html. [Open access]","<i>Palestinian Basic Law</i>, preamble and article 5, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Palestine_2005. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Status of Palestine in the United Nations,” resolution 67/19, adopted 29 November 2012, https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/n12/479/74/pdf/n1247974.pdf. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Legal Consequences of the Construction of a Wall in the Occupied Palestinian Territory,” advisory opinion of 9 July 2004, case 131, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/131. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Church_of_the_Nativity_%287703592746%29.jpg/1920px-Church_of_the_Nativity_%287703592746%29.jpg","title":"Church of the Nativity, Palestine","desc":"Church of the Nativity.","credit":"Neil Ward, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Nativity_(7703592746).jpg","alt":"A view of Church of the Nativity."}},
{"id":"gw-125","num":125,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Oman","answerText":"Oman","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 October 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Basic Law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1996</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Oman"},"facts":[["Capital","Muscat"],["Population","5.50M (2025)"],["Largest city","Muscat"],["Area","309,980 km² (119,684 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","oman"],"abstract":"<b>Oman</b> occupies the southeastern corner of the Arabian Peninsula, with a coastline of almost 3,165 km (1,967 miles) running southwest from the Strait of Hormuz and overlooking three seas: the Persian Gulf, the Gulf of Oman and the Arabian Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A detached part of the country lies at the tip of the Musandam Peninsula, on the southern shore of that strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three physiographic regions make it up, the first the coastal plain, whose chief parts are the Batinah in the north and the Salalah in the south, rising from the sea to 500 m (1,640 feet) inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then the mountain ranges, a seventh of the territory, running from Musandam to Ras Al-Hadd and reaching 3,000 m (9,800 feet) at the Jebel Al Akhdar; and the internal regions between plain and mountain, below 500 m and covering 82 per cent of the country in desert, sand and gravel plains, including part of the Rub' al Khali, the Empty Quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is arid to semi-arid and differs sharply by region: hot and humid in summer on the coast, hot and dry inland, moderate all year in the higher land and in Dhofar, where a summer monsoon blows from June to September while the north takes its rain between November and April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The long-term weighted mean rainfall is 62 mm (2 inches) a year, potential evaporation runs from 1,660 mm (65 inches) on the Salalah plain to 2,200 mm (87 inches) inland, and in nearly all its wadis surface run-off lasts only hours after a storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For all that aridity it carries a relatively high diversity, with more than 1,208 plants identified, 78 of them endemic, and a fauna including the Arabian oryx, the Nubian ibex, the Arabian tahr and the Arabian leopard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sultanate concluded a bilateral treaty of amity and commerce in 1833, signed by His Majesty Seyed Syeed bin Sultan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 7 October 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its Basic Law, promulgated in 1996, calls the Sultanate an Arab, Islamic, independent state with full sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Oman,” revised version, Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2021, https://www.fao.org/3/cb4413en/cb4413en.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Oman,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/oman. [Open access]","<i>Basic Law of the Sultanate of Oman</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Oman_2011. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Oman — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/om.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Oman — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=om. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sultan_Qaboos_Grand_Mosque_%281%29.jpg/1920px-Sultan_Qaboos_Grand_Mosque_%281%29.jpg","title":"the Sultan Qaboos Grand Mosque, Oman","desc":"The Sultan Qaboos Grand Mosque at Muscat.","credit":"Mostafameraji, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Qaboos_Grand_Mosque_(1).jpg","alt":"A domed mosque with minarets behind flowering gardens."}},
{"id":"gw-126","num":126,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mauritania","answerText":"Mauritania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mauritania"},"facts":[["Capital","Nouakchott"],["Population","5.30M (2025)"],["Largest city","Nouakchott"],["Area","1,030,700 km² (397,956 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mauritania"],"abstract":"<b>Mauritania</b> lies in northwest Africa between the 15th and 27th parallels north, with 600 km (373 miles) of Atlantic coast along its western side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than half of it, the whole north, is desert and thinly peopled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Sahelian belt about 200 km (124 miles) wide runs from west to east across the south, and in the centre and north the relief rises into massifs such as the Adrar and the Tagant, cresting between 400 and 800 m (1,300 and 2,600 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Apart from the alluvial plain of the Senegal river in the south, called the Chemama, most of the rest is lines of dunes that green over with pasture when it rains and carry what rainfed crops there are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is Saharan in the north and Sahelian in the south, generally hot and dry: maxima pass 44°C (111°F) in May and June, minima can fall to 10°C (50°F) in January and February, and prevailing northeasterly winds drive the sand forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rains fall over about four months from June to September and are very irregular, running on a north-south gradient from a few millimetres to 450 mm (18 inches) a year in the Guidimakha, with most of the country under 300 mm (12 inches); two great droughts struck in 1984-85 and 1991-92.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Renewable surface water is put at 11.1 km³ (2.66 cubic miles) a year and almost all of it is the Senegal river and its tributaries, only 0.1 km³ (0.02 cubic miles) being generated inside the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone carries a rich fishery, with more than 400 demersal species counted and about a hundred of commercial value, and fishing supplies a tenth of the economy and between a third and a half of exports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Previously under French sovereignty, the country became independent in 1960 and joined the United Nations on 27 October 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution, adopted in 1991, calls Mauritania an Islamic, indivisible, democratic and social republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Profil de Pays — Mauritanie,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0201fr/CA0201FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mauritania,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mauritania. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Islamic Republic of Mauritania</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Mauritania_2012. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Mauritania — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mr.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Mauritania — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=mr. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Richat_Structure_ISS030-E-12516.jpg/1920px-Richat_Structure_ISS030-E-12516.jpg","title":"Richat Structure, Mauritania","desc":"Richat Structure.","credit":"NASA, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richat_Structure_ISS030-E-12516.jpg","alt":"A view of Richat Structure."}},
{"id":"gw-127","num":127,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Costa Rica","answerText":"Costa Rica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 November 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Costa Rica"},"facts":[["Capital","San José"],["Population","5.20M (2025)"],["Largest city","San José"],["Area","51,100 km² (19,730 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","costa rica"],"abstract":"<b>Costa Rica</b> lies between 8° and 11° north, with 212 km (132 miles) of Caribbean shore on the east and the Pacific to the south and west, and Cocos Island, 24 km² (9 sq mi) out in the Pacific, forms part of its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Guanacaste-Tilarán cordillera crosses from the far northwest to the southeast as far as the middle of the country, where it forks: one branch runs east as the Central cordillera, carrying the Irazú and Poás volcanoes, the other southeast as the cordillera of Talamanca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the two lies the Central Valley, which holds the principal cities; humid forested plains stretch along the Atlantic coast in the northeast, and the Nicoya and Osa peninsulas close the far northwest and southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relief between sea level and 3,431 m (11,257 feet) sets the climate: humid tropical to 600 m (2,000 feet), humid subtropical to 1,600 m (5,250 feet) and cold above that, under Caribbean trade winds and Pacific monsoon winds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperatures run between 18 and 27°C (64 and 81°F) all year and rainfall between 1,300 and 7,500 mm (51 and 295 inches), the Pacific slope having a clear dry season and a wet one from May to October while the Caribbean slope's dry season is barely marked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The driest region, the northern Pacific, takes between 1,400 and 2,500 mm (55 and 98 inches) a year and can go seven months without rain, and its rainfall is bimodal, peaking in June and September with a lull between them known as the veranillo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forests, wetlands, páramos and mangroves cover about 28,419 km² (10,973 sq mi), and forest cover has held at 52 per cent since 2010, though the páramos and the mangroves have both been shrinking since the 1990s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country was originally part of the United Provinces of Central America, which had separated from Mexico in 1823.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those provinces had broken into five states by 1839, but it did not formally declare its own independence until 30 August 1848.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 2 November 1945, and its constitution of 1949 calls it a democratic, free, independent, multiethnic and multicultural republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Perfil de País — Costa Rica,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0416es/CA0416ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Costa Rica,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/costa-rica. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Costa Rica</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Costa_Rica_2020. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Costa Rica — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cr.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Costa Rica — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=cr. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Arenal_volcano_%2870785p%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Arenal_volcano_%2870785p%29_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Arenal Volcano, Costa Rica","desc":"Arenal Volcano.","credit":"Rhododendrites, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arenal_volcano_(70785p)_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Arenal Volcano."}},
{"id":"gw-128","num":128,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kuwait","answerText":"Kuwait","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 May 1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kuwait"},"facts":[["Capital","Kuwait City"],["Population","5.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kuwait City"],["Area","17,818 km² (6,880 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","kuwait"],"abstract":"<b>Kuwait</b> lies at the head of the Persian Gulf, which it overlooks from the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land is generally flat, with slightly undulating desert plains sloping gently toward the northeast and reaching about 300 m (980 feet) above sea level, and most of it is desert with a few oases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its arable soil is 80 to 90 per cent sand: it drains and airs well but holds very little water, is poor in organic matter and is crossed at various depths by hard pans known locally as gutch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is a desert one, with a long dry hot summer in which temperatures pass 45°C (113°F) and sandstorms are frequent, and a cooler winter in which they sometimes fall below 4°C (39°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rainy season runs from October to May; over about 100 km² (39 sq mi) the year's rain is under 100 mm (4 inches) and over the rest between 100 and 300 mm (4 and 12 inches), the long-term mean for the country being about 121 mm (5 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its coastline runs about 660 km (410 miles) and takes in nine offshore islands, and its most important feature is the bay at its head, a nursery ground for fish and shrimp larvae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nine geomorphological forms and four desert ecosystems are recognised — sand dunes, swamps and salt depressions, flat desert and desert plateau — and the Wadi Al Batin valley runs along the western edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country was under British protection from 1899 and became fully independent in June 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 14 May 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was invaded by Iraq on 2 August 1990, and its constitution, dated 1962 and reinstated in 1992, calls Kuwait an Arab, independent, fully sovereign state whose sovereignty may not be surrendered and no part of whose territory may be ceded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Kuwait,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0343en/CA0343EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kuwait,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kuwait. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the State of Kuwait</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Kuwait_1992. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Kuwait — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kw.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Kuwait — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=kw. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Kuwait_City_Kuwait_Towers_Panoramic_View_1.jpg/1920px-Kuwait_City_Kuwait_Towers_Panoramic_View_1.jpg","title":"the Kuwait Towers, Kuwait","desc":"The Kuwait Towers.","credit":"Zairon, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuwait_City_Kuwait_Towers_Panoramic_View_1.jpg","alt":"Three slender towers carrying blue-tiled spheres above a bay."}},
{"id":"gw-129","num":129,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Panama","answerText":"Panama","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 November 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1972</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Panama"},"facts":[["Capital","Panama City"],["Population","4.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Panama City"],["Area","75,320 km² (29,081 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","panama"],"abstract":"<b>Panama</b> lies between 7° and 9° north and is the narrowest country of the Central American isthmus, with 1,700 km (1,060 miles) of Pacific coast and 1,288 km (800 miles) on the Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A mountain corridor crosses it from west to east — the Chiriquí cordillera, the Tabasará sierra, the Central cordillera, the San Blas cordillera and the Darién range — dividing its waters between the Pacific slope, which takes 70 per cent of the territory, and the Caribbean, which takes 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covered 45 per cent of the country in 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is generally humid tropical, worked on by both oceans and by the movements of the intertropical convergence zone, with temperatures steady at about 27°C (81°F) through the year, a mean relative humidity between 76 and 92 per cent and two clear seasons, dry from December to April and wet from May to November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rainfall is the heaviest on the isthmus, though the Arco Seco of Coclé and the Azuero peninsula is tropical dry country taking under 1,500 mm (59 inches) a year, the least anywhere in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some 52 river basins drain it, mostly short streams running straight to the coast; the largest on the Pacific side is the Bayano at 4,984 km² (1,924 sq mi), and on the Caribbean side the Chagres, whose 3,338 km² (1,289 sq mi) and the artificial lakes of Alajuela and Gatún regulate the flow on which the interoceanic canal depends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country stands in the world's most biodiverse region, on the isthmus joining two continents, and its forests make an important corridor for migrating species; protected areas cover 38.66 per cent of the national territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was part of Colombia until 3 November 1903, when Panamanians revolted against the Colombian government, declared an independent republic and set up a provisional governing junta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 13 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1972 says the Panamanian nation is organised as a sovereign and independent state whose government is unitary, republican, democratic and representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Perfil de País — Panamá,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0422es/CA0422ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Panama,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/panama. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Panama</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Panama_2004. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Panama — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pa.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Panama — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=pa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Panama_Canal_Miraflores_Locks.jpg","title":"the Panama Canal, Panama","desc":"The Miraflores Locks on the Panama Canal.","credit":"User:Stan Shebs, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panama_Canal_Miraflores_Locks.jpg","alt":"A cargo ship in a canal lock chamber."}},
{"id":"gw-130","num":130,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Croatia","answerText":"Croatia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Croatia"},"facts":[["Capital","Zagreb"],["Population","3.90M (2025)"],["Largest city","Zagreb"],["Area","56,594 km² (21,851 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","croatia"],"abstract":"<b>Croatia</b> had been a constituent part of Yugoslavia, and the United States recognised it as an independent state on 7 April 1992, announcing the decision in a White House statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 6 August 1992, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in remarks to the press, and the American embassy at Zagreb opened on 25 August under a chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations without a vote at its 86th plenary meeting on 22 May 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It later brought Serbia before the International Court of Justice under the Genocide Convention, and on 3 February 2015 the Court dismissed its claim in its entirety, holding it unnecessary to rule on other questions such as succession to responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 30 November 2000 and the European Union on 1 July 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It adopted the euro and entered the Schengen area on the same day, 1 January 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations and Eurostat give exactly the same area, 56,594 square kilometres, where the World Bank’s surface-area series gives 88,070, half as much again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is falling steeply: the World Bank’s series runs from 4,151,725 in 2015 to 3,876,200 in 2025, a loss of about 7 per cent in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Court had ruled on the preliminary objections in that case on 18 November 2008, and heard witnesses and witness-experts in hearings running from 3 March to 1 April 2014 before giving its judgment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its capital holds 685,000 people, about a sixth of the country, and the United Nations counts 3,848,000 people in all against Eurostat’s 3,874,350, a difference of some 26,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Croatia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Croatia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/croatia. [Open access]","European Union, “Croatia – EU country profile,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/croatia_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Croatia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/croatia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Croatia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HRV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Croatia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HRV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Croatia to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/238, adopted at the 86th plenary meeting, 22 May 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/148233. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Application of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide (Croatia v. Serbia),” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/118. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/View_in_Plitvice_Lakes_National_Park.jpg/1920px-View_in_Plitvice_Lakes_National_Park.jpg","title":"Plitvice Lakes National Park, Croatia","desc":"Plitvice Lakes National Park.","credit":"Zysko serhii, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_in_Plitvice_Lakes_National_Park.jpg","alt":"A view of Plitvice Lakes National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-131","num":131,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Georgia","answerText":"Georgia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 July 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Georgia"},"facts":[["Capital","Tbilisi"],["Population","3.80M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tbilisi"],["Area","69,700 km² (26,911 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","georgia"],"abstract":"<b>Georgia</b> lies in the Caucasus, with 309 km (192 miles) of Black Sea coast along its western side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three physiographic regions divide it: mountains over about 54 per cent of the ground, highlands over 33 per cent and valleys over 13.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Caucasus range makes its northern edge and its highest peak stands some 5,000 m (16,400 feet) above the sea, while about 70 per cent of the country lies below 1,700 m (5,580 feet); crops will grow up to 2,000 m (6,600 feet) and above that there is only pasture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 1,065 mm (42 inches) a year and the country falls into two climatic halves: the west is humid subtropical with mild winters and summers that are not very hot, taking between 1,100 and 1,700 mm (43 and 67 inches), where draining the excess is the chief difficulty for farmers, and means run from 5°C (41°F) in January to 22°C (72°F) in July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The east is dry subtropical, with fairly cold winters and arid hot summers and between 500 and 1,100 mm (20 and 43 inches) of rain, four fifths of it between March and October, its longest dry spell running 50 to 60 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Forest covers 41 per cent of the territory, a figure steady over the past decade, and about 65 per cent of the species of the Caucasus — a recognised biodiversity hotspot — depend on it; 18 freshwater habitats are counted critical and only a quarter of their area is protected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Erosion by water and wind, degrading farming practice and other processes have degraded almost 35 per cent of the farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union and declared its independence from it in April 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 31 July 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls it an independent, unified and indivisible state, as confirmed by the referendum of 31 March 1991 held across the whole territory, and a democratic republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Georgia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/CA0338EN/ca0338en.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Georgia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/georgia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Georgia</i>, articles 1 and 3, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Georgia_2018. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Georgia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ge.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Georgia — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ge. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Gergeti_Trinity_Church_09.23.jpg/1920px-Gergeti_Trinity_Church_09.23.jpg","title":"Gergeti Trinity Church, Georgia","desc":"Gergeti Trinity Church.","credit":"This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote . Feel free to use any of my images, but please mention me as the author and may send me a message. (สามารถใช้ภาพได้อิสระ แต่กรุณาใส่เครดิตผู้ถ่ายและอาจส่ง ข้อความบอกกล่าวด้วย ) Please do not upload an updated image here without consultation with the Author. The author would like to make corrections only at his own source. This ensures that the changes are preserved. Please if you think that any changes should be required, please inform the author. Otherwise you can upload a new image with a new name. Please use one of the templates derivative or extract ., CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gergeti_Trinity_Church_09.23.jpg","alt":"A view of Gergeti Trinity Church."}},
{"id":"gw-132","num":132,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Eritrea","answerText":"Eritrea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 May 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Eritrea"},"facts":[["Capital","Asmara"],["Population","3.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Asmara"],["Area","121,144 km² (46,774 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","eritrea"],"abstract":"<b>Eritrea</b> stands in the Horn of Africa with more than 1,000 km (620 miles) of coast on the Red Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of it is savannah, steppe and desert, particularly in the southwestern lowlands and in the east near that sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its highlands, where altitudes run between 1,500 and 2,000 m (4,900 and 6,600 feet), are among the oldest inhabited parts of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six agro-ecological zones are distinguished: the semi-desert is the largest at 38.7 per cent of the ground and the arid lowland next at 33.3, while the moist highland where most of the cropping is done covers 8.7 and the sub-humid escarpment under 1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies in the Sahelian rainfall belt and takes its rain from the southwestern monsoon: the mean is about 380 mm (15 inches) a year but runs from under 50 mm (2 inches) to over 1,000 mm (39 inches), with more than nine tenths of the territory below 450 mm (18 inches) and only 1 per cent above 650 mm (26 inches), and the climate goes from hot and arid by the sea to temperate sub-humid in the eastern highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its habitats include juniper forests, riverine forests, coral reefs and traditional farming systems, and 126 mammals, 557 birds, 90 reptiles, 19 amphibians and 700 plants have been recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its marine zone lies in the southern Red Sea and holds 500 fish species, five marine turtles, eight or more cetaceans and the dugong, and the country is recognised as a centre of origin and of diversity for sorghum, wheat and barley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It had previously been under Ethiopian sovereignty, and declared its independence on 27 April 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 28 May of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1997 calls Eritrea a sovereign and independent state founded on the principles of democracy, social justice and the rule of law, and a unitary state divided into units of local government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Eritrea,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9736en/I9736EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Eritrea,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/eritrea. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Eritrea</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Eritrea_1997. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Eritrea — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/er.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Eritrea — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=er. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Asmara_Eritrea_Fiat_Tagliero_Building_%2830476150040%29.jpg/1920px-Asmara_Eritrea_Fiat_Tagliero_Building_%2830476150040%29.jpg","title":"Asmara, Eritrea","desc":"The Fiat Tagliero building in Asmara.","credit":"Clay Gilliland from Chandler, U.S.A., CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asmara_Eritrea_Fiat_Tagliero_Building_(30476150040).jpg","alt":"A modernist service station with long cantilevered wings."}},
{"id":"gw-133","num":133,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mongolia","answerText":"Mongolia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plebiscite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mongolia"},"facts":[["Capital","Ulaanbaatar"],["Population","3.50M (2025)"],["Largest city","Ulaanbaatar"],["Area","1,564,116 km² (603,909 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","mongolia"],"abstract":"<b>Mongolia</b> is a landlocked country in the north of the central Asian plateau, made up principally of inter-mountain plateaux, with about 80 per cent of the territory standing above 1,000 m (3,300 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its main ranges are the Mongolian Altai in the west and the Khangai and Khentii in the north and centre, with the large depression of the Great Lakes between them and elevated plains to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four regions divide it — the Khangai forest region, the eastern steppe, the Gobi desert and the semi-desert — and some four fifths of the land can be used for pasture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is severe, with long cold winters in which the mean monthly temperature stays below freezing everywhere from November to March, and late spring and early autumn frosts cut the growing season to 80 or 100 days in the north and 120 to 140 in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain averages 241 mm (9 inches) a year, from 400 mm (16 inches) in the north to under 100 mm (4 inches) in the southern Gobi, and four fifths or more of it falls between June and August, with low soil moisture in spring and strong spring winds driving evaporation and erosion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It occupies an ecological transition zone where the Siberian taiga, the Central Asian steppe, the Altai mountains and the Gobi meet, and its ecosystems have been less disturbed than those around them: hundreds of thousands of migratory gazelles still cross the eastern steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over 5,682 plant species are recorded, forest occupies 12.1 per cent of the land, and more than 13,000 insect species are known, with new ones described every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For roughly 200 years before 1911 the country lay within the borders of the Chinese empire, and after that empire collapsed a group of nationalists established a new republic in 1924 with Soviet assistance, its independence affirmed by a plebiscite in October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 27 October 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1992 calls Mongolia an independent and sovereign republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Mongolia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2011, https://www.fao.org/3/CA0400EN/ca0400en.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mongolia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mongolia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Mongolia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Mongolia_2001. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Mongolia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mn.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Mongolia — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=mn. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Gobi_Desert.jpg","title":"Gobi Desert, Mongolia","desc":"Gobi Desert.","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gobi_Desert.jpg","alt":"A view of Gobi Desert."}},
{"id":"gw-134","num":134,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Uruguay","answerText":"Uruguay","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1811</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1828</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uruguay"},"facts":[["Capital","Montevideo"],["Population","3.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Montevideo"],["Area","173,626 km² (67,037 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","uruguay"],"abstract":"<b>Uruguay</b> lies in the southeast of the American continent, between the left bank of the Río de la Plata and the left bank of the Uruguay river, with that estuary to its south and the Atlantic to its east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 4 million hectares (9.9 million acres) are reckoned potentially fit for cropping, and a rotation of crops and pasture is needed on them to keep the soil from degrading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is temperate, without great swings through the year and with abundant if irregular rain: mean annual temperature runs from 16°C (61°F) in the southeast to 20°C (68°F) in the northeast, mean maxima between 28 and 33°C (82 and 91°F) and mean minima between 6 and 9°C (43 and 48°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Frosts are frequent from May to September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no dry season, but the rain is extremely irregular from year to year in total, frequency and intensity alike, running from 1,100 mm (43 inches) in the south to 1,600 mm (63 inches) in the north, and strong continuous winds — cool in winter, warm in summer — blow for most of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country stands in a biogeographic transition zone where several regions meet, giving natural grassland interspersed with wetlands, several kinds of native forest and important bodies of water including coastal lagoons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its economy rests chiefly on agricultural exports, livestock historically the leading sector though soya has gained ground, and its long-term energy policy and investment in renewable sources place it second in South America for environmental sustainability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became independent of Spain in 1811 and was annexed by Brazil until 1825, and after a three-year federation with Argentina it became an independent nation in 1828.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 18 December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls the Oriental Republic of Uruguay the political association of all the inhabitants within its territory, sovereignty residing fundamentally in the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Perfil de País — Uruguay,” Organización de las Naciones Unidas para la Alimentación y la Agricultura, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0442es/CA0442ES.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uruguay,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uruguay. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Oriental Republic of Uruguay</i>, articles 1 and 4, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Uruguay_2004. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Uruguay — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/uy.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Uruguay — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=uy. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Basilica_del_Sanct%C3%ADsimo_Sacramento.jpg","title":"Colonia del Sacramento, Uruguay","desc":"Colonia del Sacramento.","credit":"JGHowes , photographer ( Canon AE-1 camera)., Attribution, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_del_Sanct%C3%ADsimo_Sacramento.jpg","alt":"A view of Colonia del Sacramento."}},
{"id":"gw-135","num":135,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Puerto Rico","answerText":"Puerto Rico","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 November 1953</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Puerto Rico"},"facts":[["Capital","San Juan"],["Population","3.20M (2025)"],["Largest city","San Juan"],["Area","8,868 km² (3,424 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","puerto rico"],"abstract":"<b>Puerto Rico</b> is a Caribbean island of 8,868 square kilometres, and the United Nations gives it a country profile of its own without a membership date, because it is not a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spain ceded sovereignty over it, with Guam and the Philippines, in the peace treaty that ended the war of 1898 and closed the Spanish colonial empire in the western hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has belonged to the United States ever since, and the United Nations profile for the United States expressly excludes its data.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 27 November 1953, at its 459th plenary meeting, the General Assembly resolved that information about it need no longer be transmitted under Article 73e of the Charter, the article that binds a state administering a territory whose people have not yet attained full self-government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That decision took it off the list of places the United States reported on to the United Nations, and no later resolution has put it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its population is falling steeply: the World Bank’s series runs from 3,535,167 in 2015 to 3,184,835 in 2025, a loss of about a tenth in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations puts the figure slightly higher, at 3,235,000, and the two published areas differ by two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three-quarters of its people live in and around the capital, whose metropolitan area the United Nations counts at 2,451,400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people are densely settled, at 372 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations records 89 men there for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It uses the United States dollar, and the profile footnotes that its national accounts are compiled in accordance with the System of National Accounts of 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Puerto Rico,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Cessation of the Transmission of Information under Article 73e of the Charter in Respect of Puerto Rico,” resolution 748 (VIII), adopted at the 459th plenary meeting, 27 November 1953, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/211641. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Puerto Rico,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Puerto Rico,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_%2816869927311%29.jpg/1920px-Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_%2816869927311%29.jpg","title":"Castillo San Felipe del Morro, Puerto Rico","desc":"Castillo San Felipe del Morro.","credit":"Chad Sparkes from Kissimmee,Florida, United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_San_Felipe_Del_Morro_(16869927311).jpg","alt":"A view of Castillo San Felipe del Morro."}},
{"id":"gw-136","num":136,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","answerText":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dayton signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ICJ judgment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 February 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bosnia and Herz."},"facts":[["Capital","Sarajevo"],["Population","3.10M (2025)"],["Largest city","Sarajevo"],["Area","51,209 km² (19,772 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","bosnia and herzegovina"],"abstract":"<b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b> had been a constituent part of Yugoslavia, and the United States recognised it as an independent state on 7 April 1992, announcing the decision in a White House statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted it to the United Nations without a vote at its 86th plenary meeting on 22 May 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 6 August 1992, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in remarks to the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Victor Jackovich presented his credentials as American ambassador on 23 June 1993, but there was no embassy for him to work in: one was established on 10 November 1993 on the premises of the American embassy in Vienna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An embassy in Sarajevo itself followed only on 4 July 1994, with Jackovich as ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The war was ended by the General Framework Agreement for Peace, reached at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base near Dayton, Ohio, in November 1995 and formally signed at Paris on 14 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country’s constitution is Annex 4 of that agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It brought Serbia and Montenegro before the International Court of Justice under the Genocide Convention, and on 26 February 2007 the Court found that Serbia had violated its obligation to prevent the genocide at Srebrenica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Court also found that other acts before it had not been accompanied by the specific intent that defines genocide — the intent to destroy a protected group in whole or in part — and that financial compensation was not the appropriate reparation, since it had not been shown that the genocide would have been averted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is falling steeply: the World Bank’s series runs from 3,518,541 in 2015 to 3,140,095 in 2025, and the two published areas differ by a single square kilometre, at 51,209 and 51,210.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bosnia and Herzegovina,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ba.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bosnia-herzegovina. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/237, adopted at the 86th plenary meeting, 22 May 1992, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/148234. [Open access]","Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, “Dayton Peace Agreement,” OSCE Mission to Bosnia and Herzegovina, https://www.osce.org/bih/126173. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Application of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide (Bosnia and Herzegovina v. Serbia and Montenegro),” Overview of the Case, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/91. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BIH/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Bosnia and Herzegovina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BIH/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Mostar_Old_Town_Panorama_2007.jpg/1920px-Mostar_Old_Town_Panorama_2007.jpg","title":"Stari Most, Bosnia and Herzegovina","desc":"Stari Most.","credit":"Ramirez, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mostar_Old_Town_Panorama_2007.jpg","alt":"A view of Stari Most."}},
{"id":"gw-137","num":137,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Armenia","answerText":"Armenia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1918 – 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Soviet republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Armenia"},"facts":[["Capital","Yerevan"],["Population","3.00M (2025)"],["Largest city","Yerevan"],["Area","29,743 km² (11,484 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","armenia"],"abstract":"<b>Armenia</b> is a landlocked country in the Caucasus, mountainous, with 77 per cent of its territory between 1,000 and 2,500 m (3,300 and 8,200 feet) above the sea and a mean altitude of 1,850 m (6,070 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its highest point is Mount Aragats at 4,095 m (13,435 feet), and 42 per cent of the area is unusable for habitation, its uplands, plateaus, river valleys and depressions carrying high seismicity over most of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rocky high ranges separating narrow fertile valleys make the centre and north; toward the south lie the broad, flat and fertile Ararat valleys along the left bank of the Araks; and around Lake Sevan in the east the ground is generally rolling with rocky outcrops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two river basins divide it, the Araks in the southwest and the Kura in the northeast, which converge further downstream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is highland continental, with hot summers and cold winters and six climate zones from dry subtropical to rigorous high mountainous: the mean annual temperature is 5.5°C (42°F), July runs around 16 to 17°C (61 to 63°F) but 24 to 26°C (75 to 79°F) in the Ararat valley, winter averages almost minus 7°C (19°F), and rain totals 592 mm (23 inches), from 200 mm (8 inches) in the driest districts to over 1,000 mm (39 inches) high up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its landscape diversity has shaped an unusual flora: about 3 per cent of it, some 123 species, is found nowhere else, and it holds more than 200 edible plants and over 1,000 fodder plants, with a tenth reckoned to have medicinal properties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between the 4th and the 19th centuries the country was conquered and ruled by, among others, Persians, Arabs, Byzantines, Mongols and Turks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was an independent republic briefly from 1918 to 1920, until local communists came to power after an invasion by the Red Army, and it became part of the Trans-Caucasian Soviet Socialist Republic in 1922 and the Armenian Soviet Socialist Republic in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 1995 calls the Republic of Armenia a sovereign, democratic, social and rule-of-law state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Armenia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/CA0217EN/ca0217en.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Armenia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/armenia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Armenia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Armenia_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Armenia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/am.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Armenia — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=am. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Monasterio_Khor_Virap%2C_Armenia%2C_2016-10-01%2C_DD_25.jpg/1920px-Monasterio_Khor_Virap%2C_Armenia%2C_2016-10-01%2C_DD_25.jpg","title":"Khor Virap, Armenia","desc":"Khor Virap.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monasterio_Khor_Virap,_Armenia,_2016-10-01,_DD_25.jpg","alt":"A view of Khor Virap."}},
{"id":"gw-138","num":138,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Namibia","answerText":"Namibia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 March 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Namibia"},"facts":[["Capital","Windhoek"],["Population","3.10M (2025)"],["Largest city","Windhoek"],["Area","825,229 km² (318,623 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","namibia"],"abstract":"<b>Namibia</b> lies along the southwestern coast of Africa, running about 1,300 km (808 miles) from north to south and between about 450 and 900 km (280 and 560 miles) from east to west, not counting the Caprivi Strip panhandle in the northeast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its highest point is the Konigstein, at 2,606 m (8,550 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three topographical regions make it up: the coastal desert region, which is the Namib and follows the whole length of the shore in mobile dunes, gravel and sandy plains; the inland plateau, which covers more than half the country and holds the Tsaris, Anas and Erongo massifs and the Great Western Escarpment; and the dune- and grass-covered Kalahari beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Savannah covers about 64 per cent of the surface, desert vegetation 16 and dry woodland 20.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is hot and dry with sparse, erratic rain — second in aridity within Africa only to the Sahara, with 92 per cent of the land hyper-arid, arid or semi-arid — and of the 285 mm (11 inches) that falls in a mean year, 83 per cent evaporates, 14 is taken up by plants and only 2 becomes run-off; along the coastal belt more water arrives as fog than as rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For all that dryness the country holds a remarkable range of habitats, from deserts taking under 10 mm (0.4 inches) of rain a year to subtropical wetlands and savannas taking over 600 mm (24 inches), and its coastline of 1,500 km (930 miles) is washed by nutrient-rich water supporting some of the greatest concentrations of marine life anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wildlife numbers have been rising across much of it over the past thirty years, black rhino and elephant among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory had previously been under South African sovereignty, and attained its independence on 21 March 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 23 April of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution establishes the Republic of Namibia as a sovereign, secular, democratic and unitary state founded upon the principles of democracy, the rule of law and justice for all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Namibia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9756en/I9756EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Namibia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/namibia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Namibia</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Namibia_2014. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Namibia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/na.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Namibia — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=na. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sossusvlei_south_view.jpg/1920px-Sossusvlei_south_view.jpg","title":"Sossusvlei, Namibia","desc":"Sossusvlei.","credit":"Hans Hillewaert, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sossusvlei_south_view.jpg","alt":"A view of Sossusvlei."}},
{"id":"gw-139","num":139,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lithuania","answerText":"Lithuania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lithuania"},"facts":[["Capital","Vilnius"],["Population","2.90M (2025)"],["Largest city","Vilnius"],["Area","65,286 km² (25,207 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","lithuania"],"abstract":"<b>Lithuania</b> is one of the three Baltic states, with the Baltic Sea along its western side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It forms part of the east European plain, and within it lowland plains alternate with hilly uplands: from west to east there are three plains — the Pajuris, the middle lowland and the eastern lowland — and three uplands, the Zemaiciai or Baltic, the Aukstaiciai and the eastern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The highest hill reaches only 293 m (961 feet) above the sea, and forest covers around 30 per cent of the ground, the central and western parts being the best for crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country is semi-humid and its climate transitional between maritime and continental: maritime in a coastal zone 12 to 15 km (7 to 9 miles) wide, continental in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rain and snow average 656 mm (26 inches) a year, from under 550 mm (22 inches) in the north to more than 846 mm (33 inches) in the Zemaiciai hills, with over two thirds of it between April and October; the mean annual temperature is between 6.5 and 7.9°C (44 and 46°F), July averaging about 19.7°C (67°F) and January about minus 2.9°C (27°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Forest, bog, wetland and meadow make its natural ecosystems, and wetlands cover 7.9 per cent of the country, though 70 per cent of them were lost to drainage and peat extraction between 1960 and 1980, after which moss and grass communities gave way to trees and shrubs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Agricultural land is 54 per cent of the territory, roughly seven tenths of it arable and the rest meadow and pasture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The interwar republic was invaded and annexed by the Soviet Union in 1940, along with the other Baltic states, and the country declared its independence again in March 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution says the state shall be an independent democratic republic, a provision alterable only by a referendum carried by three quarters of the citizens entitled to vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Lithuania,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2016, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0328en/CA0328EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lithuania,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lithuania. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Lithuania</i>, articles 1 and 148, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Lithuania_2019. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Lithuania — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lt.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Lithuania — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=lt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Kry%C5%BEi%C5%B3_kalnas_%28G%C3%B3ra_Krzy%C5%BCy%29.JPG/1920px-Kry%C5%BEi%C5%B3_kalnas_%28G%C3%B3ra_Krzy%C5%BCy%29.JPG","title":"Hill of Crosses, Lithuania","desc":"Hill of Crosses.","credit":"Pudelek (Marcin Szala), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kry%C5%BEi%C5%B3_kalnas_(G%C3%B3ra_Krzy%C5%BCy).JPG","alt":"A view of Hill of Crosses."}},
{"id":"gw-140","num":140,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Qatar","answerText":"Qatar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2003</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Qatar"},"facts":[["Capital","Doha"],["Population","3.10M (2025)"],["Largest city","Doha"],["Area","11,627 km² (4,489 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","qatar"],"abstract":"<b>Qatar</b> is a small peninsula in the Persian Gulf, at most about 180 km (112 miles) along its north–south axis and 85 km (53 miles) across at its widest, with a number of small offshore islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water surrounds it on every side but the south, where it joins the Arabian mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land falls from 100 m (330 feet) above the sea in the south to under 50 m (164 feet) in the north, and is rocky desert with scattered oases formed by 850 separate depressions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In those depressions colluvial soils of calcareous loam, sandy loam and sandy clay loam have gathered to depths of 30 to 150 cm (12 to 59 inches) over limestone debris and bedrock; known locally as rodat, they are the country's main agricultural soils, while highly saline depressions called sabkha lie along the coasts of Umm Said and Dukhan and the southern boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is that of the northern desert belt: rain averages about 80 mm (3 inches) a year over the period from 1972 to 2005 and is extremely erratic in time and place, summer temperatures pass 40°C (104°F), annual evaporation averages 2,200 mm (87 inches), and evapotranspiration runs from under 2 mm a day in December to 10 mm a day in June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its summers are hot from June to August and its weather is pleasant between November and February, average maximum and minimum temperatures standing at 31°C (88°F) and 22°C (72°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because the rain is so light and so variable it is not counted a reliable supplement to irrigation, yet it remains the main source of irrigation water through its recharge of the groundwater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country had been a protectorate under Great Britain and declared its independence on 3 September 1971, following the termination date of its treaties with that power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 21 September 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution of 2003 calls Qatar an Arab state, sovereign and independent, whose religion is Islam, whose system is democratic and whose people are part of the Arab nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Qatar,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2008, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0349en/CA0349EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Qatar,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/qatar. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the State of Qatar</i>, article 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Qatar_2003. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Qatar — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/qa.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Qatar — Country Profile Overview,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=qa. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Doha_skyline_%26_museum.jpg/1920px-Doha_skyline_%26_museum.jpg","title":"Museum of Islamic Art, Doha, Qatar","desc":"Museum of Islamic Art, Doha.","credit":"Haakon S. Krohn, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doha_skyline_%26_museum.jpg","alt":"A view of Museum of Islamic Art, Doha."}},
{"id":"gw-141","num":141,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Jamaica","answerText":"Jamaica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Jamaica"},"facts":[["Capital","Kingston"],["Population","2.80M (2025)"],["Largest city","Kingston"],["Area","10,990 km² (4,243 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","jamaica"],"abstract":"<b>Jamaica</b> lies south of Cuba at 18°15' north and 77°30' west and is the largest island of the English-speaking Caribbean, part of the Greater Antilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three landform regions make it up: the eastern mountains, the central valleys and plateaus, and the coastal plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A series of ranges runs along the island's west-northwest to east-southeast axis, exceeding 1,000 m (3,300 feet) in the eastern third and rising to 2,256 m (7,402 feet) at Blue Mountain Peak, with the John Crow Mountains lying north of them; major alluvial lowlands occupy the southern half, often beside coastal swamps, and a narrow discontinuous coastal plain runs round the edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Karst formations dominate the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is tropical maritime, hot and humid with a temperate interior: daily means run from 26°C (79°F) in February to 30°C (86°F) in August in the lowlands and from 15 to 22°C (59 to 72°F) higher up, and rain averages 2,051 mm (81 inches), from 3,000 to 5,000 mm (118 to 197 inches) on the northeastern slopes of the Blue Mountains to under 1,500 mm (59 inches) on the southern coastal plains, which stand in their rain shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rainfall peaks in October and again in May, the main dry season runs from December to April, and tropical storms and hurricanes come between July and November with flood-producing rain of high intensity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island ranks fifth in the world for plant endemism and is unusual for animals too: 98.2 per cent of its 514 native land snails and every one of its 22 native amphibians are found nowhere else, and ten hydrological basins carry over a hundred streams and rivers besides subterranean waterways, springs and blue holes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country had been a British colony and became independent on 6 August 1962, remaining a member of the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 18 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution takes that day as the appointed day from which its citizenship runs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — Jamaica,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2015, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0415en/CA0415EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Jamaica,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/jamaica. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of Jamaica</i>, chapter 1, section 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Jamaica_2015. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Jamaica — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jm.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Jamaica — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=jm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Dunns_River_Falls_climb.JPG/1920px-Dunns_River_Falls_climb.JPG","title":"Dunn's River Falls, Jamaica","desc":"Dunn's River Falls.","credit":"Breakyunit at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunns_River_Falls_climb.JPG","alt":"A view of Dunn's River Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-142","num":142,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Gambia","answerText":"Gambia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1996</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gambia"},"facts":[["Capital","Banjul"],["Population","2.80M (2025)"],["Largest city","Serekunda"],["Area","11,295 km² (4,361 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","gambia"],"abstract":"The <b>Gambia</b> sits in the Sahelian zone on the west coast of Africa and is the smallest country on the African mainland, about a fifth of it wetland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The River Gambia runs from east to west through the middle of it, dividing the land into two strips 25 to 50 km (16 to 31 miles) wide and about 300 km (186 miles) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two geomorphological units are distinguished: the upland plateaus, whose weathered tropical soils have low fertility and hold little water but drain well, cut by watercourses that have carved narrow fluvio-colluvial valleys called wulumbangos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And the lowlands, which take in the river's floodplain in the Upper Valley and the tidal plains, called bantafaros, of the Central and Lower Valleys, where the soils are flat, fine-textured and poorly drained and can turn acid sulphate unless waterlogging is prevented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vegetation is savannah with shrub and grass understoreys, mangroves lining the floodplain in the western half, and the climate has two seasons — rains from June or July to October and a long dry period from November to May, when a hot dry wind blows down off the Sahara and can bring sandstorms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Rain averages 836 mm (33 inches) a year, over 1,000 mm (39 inches) in the south and under 800 mm (31 inches) in the north, where it is more reliable, and it has been declining steadily since 1875, driving salinity up in the lowlands and aridity up on the plateaus; the daily mean is 30°C (86°F) in the dry season and 27°C (81°F) in the wet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 3,339 species from 17 taxonomic groups have been recorded, among them 1,005 flowering plants, 627 fishes, 566 birds, 126 mammals and 77 reptiles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country had previously been under British sovereignty, and the ceremonies marking its independence were held on 18 February 1965 at the capital of the day, Bathurst.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 21 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution declares The Gambia a sovereign republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Country Profile — The Gambia,” Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/i9740en/I9740EN.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Gambia,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/gambia. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of The Gambia</i>, section 1, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Gambia_2018. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Gambia — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gm.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “The Gambia — Status and Trends of Biodiversity,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=gm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/JamesIsland.jpg/1920px-JamesIsland.jpg","title":"Kunta Kinteh Island, the Gambia","desc":"Kunta Kinteh Island.","credit":"Niels Elgaard Larsen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesIsland.jpg","alt":"A view of Kunta Kinteh Island."}},
{"id":"gw-143","num":143,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Gabon","answerText":"Gabon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN membership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gabon"},"facts":[["Capital","Libreville"],["Population","2.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Libreville"],["Area","267,668 km² (103,347 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","gabon"],"abstract":"<b>Gabon</b> stands in central Africa and is crossed by the equator, with about 800 km (497 miles) of shore giving it access to the Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its relief is varied but consists chiefly of plateaus, which cover the greater part of the surface, and of hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mountains are low and steeply pitched, and their rim forms an escarpment standing several hundred metres above the plain below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers about 22 million hectares (54 million acres), some 82 per cent of the territory, and savannah and natural pasture make up the other forms of vegetation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is equatorial with two rainy seasons: rain averages 1,831 mm (72 inches) a year and varies across the country between 1,400 and 3,800 mm (55 and 150 inches), temperatures run from 21 to 28°C (70 to 82°F), evapotranspiration is 1,400 mm (55 inches) a year, and three climatic types are told apart by the distribution and rhythm of the rain — a pure equatorial climate, a transitional one in the central zone, and the climate of the southwest and the central Atlantic littoral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Forest is put at 85 per cent of the country by another reckoning, with savannahs, mangroves and wetlands making up the rest, and its trees include an estimated 8,000 plant species of which a fifth grow nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Monts de Cristal are its richest region for plants and probably one of the richest in tropical Africa; 600 birds, 150 mammals and 100 amphibians are recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its independence was proclaimed on 17 August 1960, under Prime Minister Léon M'ba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its constitution calls Gabon an indivisible, secular, democratic and social republic, affirming the separation of state and religion and recognising all religious beliefs so far as public order allows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["FAO, “AQUASTAT Profil de Pays — Gabon,” Organisation des Nations Unies pour l'alimentation et l'agriculture, 2005, https://www.fao.org/3/ca0187fr/CA0187FR.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Gabon,” United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/gabon. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Gabonese Republic</i>, article 2, English text at the Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Gabon_2011. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Gabon — Country Profile,” UNdata, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ga.html. [Open access]","Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity, “Gabon — Country Profile Overview,” Country Profiles, https://www.cbd.int/countries/profile?country=ga. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Gabon_Loango_National_Park_View_of_Lodge_from_water.jpeg/1920px-Gabon_Loango_National_Park_View_of_Lodge_from_water.jpeg","title":"Loango National Park, Gabon","desc":"Loango National Park.","credit":"Kurt Dundy at English Wikipedia, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gabon_Loango_National_Park_View_of_Lodge_from_water.jpeg","alt":"A view of Loango National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-144","num":144,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Botswana","answerText":"Botswana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Botswana"},"facts":[["Capital","Gaborone"],["Population","2.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Gaborone"],["Area","581,730 km² (224,610 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","botswana"],"abstract":"<b>Botswana</b> is landlocked in the centre of southern Africa, bordered by Namibia, Zambia, Zimbabwe and South Africa, and most of it is covered by deep Kalahari sand, with rocky hills in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A large inland river delta in the north-west, where the Okavango spreads out and dies in the desert, holds a rich variety of wildlife and is a World Heritage site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives the surface area as 582,000 square kilometres and the population as 2,562,000 in 2025, at 4.5 people to the square kilometre, with that population growing by 1.6 per cent a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat and the World Bank both put the area more exactly at 581,730 square kilometres, and the Commonwealth gave the population as 2.352 million in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The capital, Gaborone, held 269,300 people in 2018 and is also the headquarters of the Southern African Development Community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The country had been under British sovereignty as Bechuanaland, and became independent on 30 September 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day, when an American embassy was established at Gaberones — the older spelling of the capital — with Charles H. Pletcher as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in 1966 following independence from Britain, and the United Nations on 17 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was also a founding member of the Southern African Development Co-ordination Conference, formed with eight other countries at Lusaka in Zambia in 1980, which grew into the present community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diamonds, copper, nickel, cattle and wildlife are its listed natural resources; income per head reached 7,820 US dollars in 2023; and it joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 August 1987 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Botswana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bw.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Botswana,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/botswana. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Botswana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/botswana. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Botswana,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/botswana. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Botswana and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/botswana_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Botswana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BWA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Okavango_Delta%2C_Botswana1.jpg/1920px-Okavango_Delta%2C_Botswana1.jpg","title":"Okavango Delta, Botswana","desc":"Okavango Delta.","credit":"Joachim Huber, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okavango_Delta,_Botswana1.jpg","alt":"A view of Okavango Delta."}},
{"id":"gw-145","num":145,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Moldova","answerText":"Moldova","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 August 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Moldova"},"facts":[["Capital","Chișinău"],["Population","2.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Chișinău"],["Area","33,846 km² (13,068 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","moldova"],"abstract":"<b>Moldova</b> is a landlocked country of eastern Europe whose surface area the United Nations gives as 33,847 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two published populations do not agree, and the reason is printed in a footnote: the United Nations gives 2,996,000 for 2025 including the Transnistria region, while the World Bank series, which follows the national statistical service, gives 2,360,527.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That second series has fallen every year since 2015, when it stood at 2,835,978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital, Chișinău, held 504,300 people in 2019, and the country counts 85.1 men for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers 11.8 per cent of the land, agriculture still employs 51.1 per cent of those in work, and recorded unemployment is 1.3 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of that state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were agreed at a White House meeting between Bush and President Mircea Snegur on 18 February 1992, and an American embassy at Chișinău followed on 13 March that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992 and the World Trade Organization on 26 July 2001, and its present constitution came into force on 27 August 1994, repealing the one of 15 April 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe established a mission there on 4 February 1993 to help settle the Transdniestrian conflict, and its 57 participating states support a settlement based on the country’s independence, sovereignty and territorial integrity within its internationally recognised borders, with a special status for Transdniestria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Moldova applied to join the European Union on 3 March 2022, was given a European perspective on 23 June that year by unanimous agreement of the leaders of all 27 member states, and the European Council decided on 14 December 2023 to open accession negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Moldova,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/md.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Moldova,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Moldova,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/moldova. [Open access]","Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, “OSCE Mission to Moldova,” https://moldova.osce.org/. [Open access]","Constitute Project, “Moldova’s Constitution of 1994 with Amendments through 2016,” https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Moldova_2016. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Republic of Moldova and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/moldova_e.htm. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Moldova,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/moldova_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Stanca_deasupra_Rautului_Butuceni.jpg","title":"Orheiul Vechi, Moldova","desc":"Orheiul Vechi.","credit":"Alex Prodan md, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanca_deasupra_Rautului_Butuceni.jpg","alt":"A view of Orheiul Vechi."}},
{"id":"gw-146","num":146,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Albania","answerText":"Albania","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 December 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 June 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 March 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Albania"},"facts":[["Capital","Tirana"],["Population","2.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tirana"],["Area","28,748 km² (11,100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","albania"],"abstract":"<b>Albania</b> lies on the western side of the Balkan peninsula in southern Europe, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 28,748 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two published populations are far apart: the United Nations projects 2,772,000 for 2025, while the World Bank series gives 2,349,580 and has fallen in every one of the past ten years, from 2,731,293 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital, Tirana, held 484,600 people in 2019, forest covers 28.8 per cent of the land, and agriculture still employs 34.9 per cent of everyone in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mutual recognition with the United States came on 28 July 1922, when the American Commissioner Maxwell Blake was authorised by Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes to notify the Albanian foreign minister of de jure recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 4 December 1922, when Ulysses Grant-Smith presented his credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary at Tirana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Italian forces invaded in 1939 and occupied Tirana on 8 April; relations ended on 5 June that year, when the Albanian foreign minister told the American minister that Italy had taken control of Albania’s foreign affairs, and the legation closed on 16 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An informal American mission sent in 1945 to study reopening them was withdrawn in November 1946, and the two states resumed relations only on 15 March 1991, a gap of nearly 52 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Albania had joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, in the middle of that gap, and joined the World Trade Organization on 8 September 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It had taken part in NATO’s Membership Action Plan since the plan began in 1999, signed an accession protocol in July 2008 and became a member of the alliance on 1 April 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European Union identified it as a potential candidate at Thessaloniki in June 2003, granted it candidate status in June 2014, opened accession negotiations at a first intergovernmental conference in July 2022, and in December 2025 it opened the last of all 33 chapters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Albania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/al.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Albania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ALB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Albania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/albania. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Albania and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/albania_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Albania,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/albania_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Amphitheatre_of_Butrint_2009.jpg/1920px-Amphitheatre_of_Butrint_2009.jpg","title":"Butrint, Albania","desc":"Butrint.","credit":"Geoff Wong, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphitheatre_of_Butrint_2009.jpg","alt":"A view of Butrint."}},
{"id":"gw-147","num":147,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lesotho","answerText":"Lesotho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lesotho"},"facts":[["Capital","Maseru"],["Population","2.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Maseru"],["Area","30,355 km² (11,720 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","lesotho"],"abstract":"<b>Lesotho</b> is a landlocked kingdom in southern Africa, entirely surrounded by South Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 30,355 square kilometres and its population as 2,363,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Southern African Development Community calls it the only country in the world with all of its territory more than 1,000 metres above sea level, and says more than 75 per cent of it is mountainous, with only a quarter counted as lowland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its natural resources are listed as diamonds, wildlife, mohair, wool and water, and forest covers just 1.1 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only about a quarter of its people live in towns, most of them on the lower land to the west, and the capital, Maseru, held 201,900 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head has fallen rather than risen, from 1,121 US dollars in 2015 to 916 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country had been under British sovereignty as Basutoland, and became independent on 4 October 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of Lesotho that same day, when an American embassy was established at Maseru with Richard St. F. Post as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 17 October 1966 and the Commonwealth the same year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lesotho was a founding member of the Southern African Development Co-ordination Conference, formed at Lusaka in Zambia in April 1980, which became the Southern African Development Community in 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under a treaty signed in 1986 the Lesotho Highlands Water Project harnesses the Senqu, or Orange, river to send water to the Gauteng region of South Africa and to generate hydro-electricity for Lesotho; the Commonwealth gives the area as 30,350 square kilometres and the population as 2.14 million in 2022; and the country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 8 January 1988 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lesotho,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ls.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Lesotho,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/lesotho. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Kingdom of Lesotho,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/lesotho. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lesotho,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lesotho. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Lesotho and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/lesotho_e.htm. [Open access]","Lesotho Highlands Development Authority, “Project Description,” https://www.lhda.org.ls/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Maletsunyanefalls.JPG/1920px-Maletsunyanefalls.JPG","title":"Maletsunyane Falls, Lesotho","desc":"Maletsunyane Falls.","credit":"BagelBelt, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maletsunyanefalls.JPG","alt":"A view of Maletsunyane Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-148","num":148,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guinea-Bissau","answerText":"Guinea-Bissau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 September 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 June 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guinea-Bissau"},"facts":[["Capital","Bissau"],["Population","2.20M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bissau"],["Area","36,125 km² (13,948 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","guinea-bissau"],"abstract":"<b>Guinea-Bissau</b> lies on the Atlantic coast of western Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 36,125 square kilometres and its population as 2,250,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by more than a quarter in ten years, from 1,786,457 in 2015 to 2,249,515 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 69.8 per cent of the land, 59.5 per cent of the sites that matter most for land-based biodiversity are protected, and agriculture employs half of everyone in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Bissau, held 578,800 people in 2019, and income per head was 855 US dollars in 2023 against 587 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country had been under Portuguese sovereignty, and the Security Council recommended it unanimously for membership of the United Nations at its 1791st meeting on 12 August 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Republic of Guinea-Bissau nearly a month later, on 10 September 1974, when President Gerald R. Ford sent a letter to that effect to the President of the Council of State, Luís de Almeida Cabral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the republic at its 2233rd plenary meeting on 17 September 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 3 September 1975, when the Guinea-Bissau ambassador Gil Vicente Vaz Fernandes presented his credentials to Ford, and an American embassy at Bissau followed on 30 June 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That embassy suspended operations on 14 June 1998, at the start of a civil war, and has not reopened: since 13 February 2003 the American ambassador to Senegal has been accredited to Guinea-Bissau as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 17 March 1994 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995, and the World Bank measures the surface area at 36,130 square kilometres, within five of the United Nations figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guinea-Bissau,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guinea-Bissau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guinea-Bissau,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guinea-bissau. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 356 (1974), adopted at its 1791st meeting, of 12 August 1974,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/93484. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Guinea-Bissau to membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/3205 (XXIX), United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/189827. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guinea-Bissau and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guinea_bissau_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Guinea-Bissau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Bubaque_port.jpg/1920px-Bubaque_port.jpg","title":"Bubaque, Guinea-Bissau","desc":"Bubaque.","credit":"Nammarci, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bubaque_port.jpg","alt":"A view of Bubaque."}},
{"id":"gw-149","num":149,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Slovenia","answerText":"Slovenia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Slovenia"},"facts":[["Capital","Ljubljana"],["Population","2.10M (2025)"],["Largest city","Ljubljana"],["Area","20,273 km² (7,827 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","slovenia"],"abstract":"<b>Slovenia</b> lies in southern Europe where the Alps, the Pannonian plain and the Dinaric uplands meet above a short Adriatic coast, and both the United Nations and the European Union give its area as 20,273 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank series puts it at 20,480 square kilometres, about one per cent higher, and is the outlier of the three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 2,117,000 by the United Nations count and 2,130,850 by Eurostat, at about 105 people to the square kilometre, and the capital, Ljubljana, held 286,500 in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Slovenia had been a constituent part of Yugoslavia and won its independence from it in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised it as an independent state on 7 April 1992, when the administration of George H. W. Bush announced the decision in a White House statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It joined the United Nations on 22 May 1992, diplomatic relations with the United States were established on 6 August that year, and an American embassy opened at Ljubljana on 25 August under a chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 30 October 1994, and became a member of the World Trade Organization on 30 July 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of NATO on 29 March 2004, alongside six other states, in the largest wave of enlargement in the alliance’s history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the European Union on 1 May 2004, adopted the euro on 1 January 2007 and entered the Schengen area on 21 December that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a parliamentary democratic republic whose head of state is a directly elected president, and it has no regions but is divided into 212 municipalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Slovenia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/si.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Slovenia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/slovenia_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Slovenia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/slovenia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Slovenia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/slovenia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Slovenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lake_Bled_from_the_Mountain.jpg/1920px-Lake_Bled_from_the_Mountain.jpg","title":"Lake Bled, Slovenia","desc":"Lake Bled.","credit":"Canadianhockey91, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Bled_from_the_Mountain.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Bled."}},
{"id":"gw-150","num":150,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Equatorial Guinea","answerText":"Equatorial Guinea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 October 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 October 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ties suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 March 1976</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Eq. Guinea"},"facts":[["Capital","Malabo"],["Population","1.90M (2025)"],["Largest city","Bata"],["Area","28,051 km² (10,831 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","equatorial guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Equatorial Guinea</b> is made up of a mainland region in Middle Africa and islands in the Gulf of Guinea, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 28,052 square kilometres and its population as 1,938,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by a third in ten years, from 1,453,923 in 2015, and 111.4 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Malabo, held 296,800 people in 2019, and income per head was 6,558 US dollars in 2023, down from 9,069 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country had been under Spanish colonial rule for most of the modern era and gained independence from Spain on 12 October 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the time of independence, the American guide to recognition records, it had one of the highest incomes per head and the highest literacy rates in Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised that independence on 28 October 1968, when President Lyndon Johnson appointed Albert W. Sherer, the ambassador to Togo, to act as ambassador to Equatorial Guinea as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 21 November 1968, with Sherer resident at Lomé, and an American embassy at Santa Isabel, now Malabo, followed on 1 August 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council had recommended admission unanimously at its 1458th meeting on 6 November 1968, and the General Assembly admitted the republic at its 1714th plenary meeting on 12 November, a month after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States suspended relations on 14 March 1976, after its ambassador and consul were declared personae non gratae in what the State Department called an unwarranted affront, and resumed them on 19 December 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The embassy at Malabo was closed on 31 October 1995 and its work moved to Yaoundé, and it reopened in 2006, when Donald J. Johnson presented his credentials on 23 November as the first resident ambassador in more than ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Equatorial Guinea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gq.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Equatorial Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNQ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Equatorial Guinea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/equatorial-guinea. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 260 (1968), adopted at its 1458th meeting, of 6 November 1968,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/90762. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Equatorial Guinea to membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/2384 (XXIII), United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/202552. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Pico_Basil%C3%A9.jpg","title":"Pico Basile, Equatorial Guinea","desc":"Pico Basile.","credit":"Serge Moons, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pico_Basil%C3%A9.jpg","alt":"A view of Pico Basile."}},
{"id":"gw-151","num":151,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Latvia","answerText":"Latvia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restoration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Latvia"},"facts":[["Capital","Riga"],["Population","1.90M (2025)"],["Largest city","Riga"],["Area","64,594 km² (24,940 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","latvia"],"abstract":"<b>Latvia</b> lies on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea in northern Europe, and both the United Nations and the European Union give its area as 64,594 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its population is 1,854,000 by the United Nations count and 1,856,932 by Eurostat, and it has fallen in almost every year since 2015, when the World Bank series stood at 1,977,527.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The country counts 86.6 men for every 100 women, and its capital, Riga, held 633,800 people in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised Latvia on 28 July 1922, when the American Commissioner at Riga, Evan Young, informed the Latvian foreign office; Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes had instructed him by telegram on 25 July to advise Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania of the decision on the 28th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established the same day, and a legation at Riga opened on 13 November 1922 under Frederick W. B. Coleman, who was accredited to all three Baltic states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The legation closed on 5 September 1940, after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the three states that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States never recognised that incorporation, allowed Latvian representatives accredited by the last independent government to remain with diplomatic status, and held that relations had continued uninterrupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It recognised the restoration of Latvian independence on 2 September 1991, resumed normal relations on 5 September and opened an embassy at Riga on 2 October, and it regards the present government as a legal continuation of the interwar republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Latvia joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991 and the World Trade Organization on 10 February 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of NATO on 29 March 2004 and of the European Union on 1 May that year, entered the Schengen area on 21 December 2007 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Latvia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lv.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Latvia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/latvia_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Latvia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/latvia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Latvia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/latvia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Latvia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LVA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/House_of_Blackheads_at_Dusk_3%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia_-_Diliff.jpg/1920px-House_of_Blackheads_at_Dusk_3%2C_Riga%2C_Latvia_-_Diliff.jpg","title":"House of the Blackheads (Riga), Latvia","desc":"House of the Blackheads (Riga).","credit":"Diliff, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_of_Blackheads_at_Dusk_3,_Riga,_Latvia_-_Diliff.jpg","alt":"A view of House of the Blackheads (Riga)."}},
{"id":"gw-152","num":152,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"North Macedonia","answerText":"North Macedonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 February 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 September 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"North Macedonia"},"facts":[["Capital","Skopje"],["Population","1.80M (2025)"],["Largest city","Skopje"],["Area","25,713 km² (9,928 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","north macedonia"],"abstract":"<b>North Macedonia</b> is landlocked in the southern Balkans, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 25,713 square kilometres and its population as 1,814,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has fallen in every year since 2015, when the World Bank series stood at 1,912,430, and 94.8 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Skopje, held 589,800 people in 2019, forest covers 39.4 per cent of the land, and income per head was 8,606 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country achieved independence in 1991 with the peaceful breakup of Yugoslavia, but recognition was delayed by Greece’s objection to its use of the name Macedonia, and it was first recognised under the provisional designation ‘the former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations under that designation on 8 April 1993, and the World Trade Organization on 4 April 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States opened a liaison office at Skopje on 3 December 1993, recognised the former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia on 9 February 1994, and confirmed on 13 September 1995 that relations existed, President Clinton announcing that he had received a positive response from President Kiro Gligorov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Christopher R. Hill presented his credentials on 29 July 1996 as the first American ambassador, and Washington began using the constitutional name Republic of Macedonia in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Prespa Accord signed with Greece in June 2018 settled the decades-long dispute over the name, and in February 2019 the country became the Republic of North Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became NATO’s 30th member on 27 March 2020, having joined the Partnership for Peace in 1995 and the Membership Action Plan in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European Union identified it as a potential candidate at Thessaloniki in 2003, its stabilisation and association agreement was the first in the region and has been in force since 2004, it applied in March 2004 and was granted candidate status in December 2005, and accession negotiations opened at an intergovernmental conference in July 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “North Macedonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mk.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Macedonia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/macedonia. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “North Macedonia,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/north-macedonia_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “North Macedonia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/macedonia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: North Macedonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MKD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Ohrid_from_Samuel%27s_Fortress_2.jpg/1920px-Ohrid_from_Samuel%27s_Fortress_2.jpg","title":"Lake Ohrid, North Macedonia","desc":"Lake Ohrid.","credit":"kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ohrid_from_Samuel%27s_Fortress_2.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Ohrid."}},
{"id":"gw-153","num":153,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kosovo","answerText":"Kosovo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomy ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1989</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resolution 1244</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 June 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 February 2008</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ICJ opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 July 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kosovo"},"facts":[["Capital","Pristina"],["Population","1.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Pristina"],["Area","10,887 km² (4,203 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","kosovo"],"abstract":"<b>Kosovo</b> is a landlocked territory of the western Balkans whose population the World Bank puts at 1,576,876 in 2025, down from 1,788,274 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ottomans ruled it for more than four centuries, until Serbia acquired the territory during the First Balkan War of 1912 and 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was partitioned in 1913 between Serbia and Montenegro, incorporated after the First World War into the Kingdom of the Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, and made an autonomous province of Serbia within socialist Yugoslavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yugoslav constitution of 1974 gave it the standing of a socialist autonomous province with nearly the same rights as the six constituent republics, and in 1989 Slobodan Milošević ended that autonomy and imposed direct rule from Belgrade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Peaceful resistance led by Ibrahim Rugova gave way to an armed one in 1997, and a NATO air campaign ran for 78 days from March to June 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council adopted resolution 1244 at its 4011th meeting on 10 June 1999, by 14 votes to none with one abstention, suspending Belgrade’s governance and placing the territory under a United Nations interim administration with a NATO peacekeeping force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That force, KFOR, deployed on 12 June 1999 and is still there, its headquarters at Camp Film City in Pristina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kosovo declared independence on 17 February 2008, and the United States recognised it the next day, when Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice announced President George W. Bush’s decisions in a press statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly asked the International Court of Justice whether the declaration accorded with international law, and on 22 July 2010 the Court held that it did not violate international law, resolution 1244 having been silent on the territory’s final status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The European Union acknowledged the declaration in February 2008 while calling Kosovo a case sui generis, its stabilisation and association agreement came into force on 1 April 2016, and the Union lists its membership status as potential candidate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["World Bank, “Population, total: Kosovo,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/XKX/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kosovo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kosovo. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1244 (1999), adopted at its 4011th meeting, on 10 June 1999,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/274488. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “NATO’s role in Kosovo,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natolive/topics_48818.htm. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Accordance with International Law of the Unilateral Declaration of Independence in Respect of Kosovo,” Advisory Opinion of 22 July 2010, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/141. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Kosovo,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/kosovo_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Gra%C4%8Danica_Monastery%2C_2010.jpg/1920px-Gra%C4%8Danica_Monastery%2C_2010.jpg","title":"Gračanica Monastery, Kosovo","desc":"Gračanica Monastery.","credit":"Quinn Dombrowski, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gra%C4%8Danica_Monastery,_2010.jpg","alt":"A stone Byzantine church with clustered domes."}},
{"id":"gw-154","num":154,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bahrain","answerText":"Bahrain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resident envoy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 June 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bahrain"},"facts":[["Capital","Manama"],["Population","1.60M (2025)"],["Largest city","Manama"],["Area","778 km² (300 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","bahrain"],"abstract":"<b>Bahrain</b> is an island state in the Persian Gulf, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 778 square kilometres and its population as 1,643,000 in 2025, or about 2,099 people to the square kilometre; the World Bank series gives 1,600,366 for the same year, up from 1,370,322 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank’s area series both disagrees and moves, from 785 square kilometres in 2020 to 790 and then 800 by 2023, where the United Nations figure stands still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly nine in ten of its people live in towns, forest covers 0.8 per cent of the land, and income per head was 28,453 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Manama, held 600,400 people in 2019, and 163.4 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1968 the British government announced its decision, reaffirmed in March 1971, to end its treaty relationships with the Persian Gulf sheikdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Bahrain first joined the other eight states then under British protection — Qatar and the seven Trucial Sheikhdoms, now the United Arab Emirates — in an effort to form a union of Arab emirates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The nine had still not agreed terms of union by 1971, which prompted Bahrain to declare itself fully independent on 15 August that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the State of Bahrain the same day, in a State Department press release, and opened an embassy at Manama on 21 September 1971 under a chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bahrain joined the United Nations on that same date, and the ambassador first accredited to it, who presented credentials on 17 February 1972, lived at Kuwait for the whole of his posting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Joseph W. Twinam presented his credentials on 10 June 1974 and became the first American ambassador resident at Manama, and the country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 13 December 1993 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bahrain,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bh.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bahrain,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bahrain. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Bahrain, Kingdom of and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/bahrain_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bahrain,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Bahrain,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Tree_of_Life%2C_Bahrain_-_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B5%80%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%86_%E0%B4%AE%E0%B4%B0%E0%B4%82%2C_%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%B9%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%88%E0%B5%BB_01.JPG/1920px-Tree_of_Life%2C_Bahrain_-_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B5%80%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%86_%E0%B4%AE%E0%B4%B0%E0%B4%82%2C_%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%B9%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%88%E0%B5%BB_01.JPG","title":"Tree of Life (Bahrain), Bahrain","desc":"Tree of Life (Bahrain).","credit":"കാക്കര, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tree_of_Life,_Bahrain_-_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B5%80%E0%B4%B5%E0%B4%A8%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%86_%E0%B4%AE%E0%B4%B0%E0%B4%82,_%E0%B4%AC%E0%B4%B9%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%88%E0%B5%BB_01.JPG","alt":"A view of Tree of Life (Bahrain)."}},
{"id":"gw-155","num":155,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Timor-Leste","answerText":"Timor-Leste","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 May 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 May 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 September 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 2024</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Timor-Leste"},"facts":[["Capital","Dili"],["Population","1.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Dili"],["Area","14,919 km² (5,760 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","timor-leste"],"abstract":"<b>Timor-Leste</b> occupies the eastern half of the island of Timor in south-eastern Asia, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 14,919 square kilometres and its population as 1,418,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by nearly a fifth in ten years, from 1,205,131 in 2015, and only about three in ten of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Dili, held 281,100 people in 2019, income per head was 1,503 US dollars in 2023, the country uses the United States dollar as its own currency, and there are 95.4 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory had been a Portuguese colony until 1975 and was under Indonesian sovereignty from 1976 to 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a popular referendum held under United Nations auspices in 1999, the International Force for East Timor kept the peace until formal independence; before that the United States had had limited relations of any sort with the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council had placed the territory under a United Nations transitional administration by resolution 1272, adopted unanimously at its 4057th meeting on 25 October 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Independence came on 20 May 2002, and the United States recognised the country the same day, opening an embassy at Dili under Chargé d’Affaires Shari Villarossa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council recommended admission without a vote at its 4542nd meeting on 23 May 2002, three days after independence, and the General Assembly admitted the Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste on 27 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grover Rees 3rd became the first American ambassador when he presented his credentials on 10 December 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the World Trade Organization on 30 August 2024, and the World Bank measures its surface area at 14,870 square kilometres against the United Nations figure of 14,919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Timor-Leste,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tl.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Timor-Leste,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TLS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Timor-Leste,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/timor-leste. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1272 (1999), adopted at its 4057th meeting, on 25 October 1999,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/291410. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1414 (2002), adopted at its 4542nd meeting, on 23 May 2002,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/465317. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Timor-Leste and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/timor_leste_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Timor-Leste,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TLS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Christ_Dili.jpg/1920px-Christ_Dili.jpg","title":"Cristo Rei of Dili, Timor-Leste","desc":"Cristo Rei of Dili.","credit":"Kok Leng, Yeo from Singapore, Singapore, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_Dili.jpg","alt":"A view of Cristo Rei of Dili."}},
{"id":"gw-156","num":156,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Estonia","answerText":"Estonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation Tallinn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restoration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Estonia"},"facts":[["Capital","Tallinn"],["Population","1.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Tallinn"],["Area","45,339 km² (17,505 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","estonia"],"abstract":"<b>Estonia</b> lies on the eastern shore of the Baltic Sea in northern Europe, and its three published areas do not agree: 45,261 square kilometres at the United Nations, 45,336 at Eurostat and 45,340 at the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 1,344,000 by the United Nations count and 1,369,995 by Eurostat, and the capital, Tallinn, held 441,300 people in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head reached 30,201 US dollars in 2023, against 17,733 in 2015, and the country is divided into 15 counties and 79 municipalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Estonia on 28 July 1922, when the American Commissioner at Riga, Evan Young, informed the Estonian foreign office; Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes had instructed him by telegram on 25 July to advise Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania of the decision on the 28th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Young continued as the American representative to all three Baltic states, with the new rank of minister, and no separate legation was opened at Tallinn until 30 June 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That legation closed on 5 September 1940, after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the three states in August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States never recognised the incorporation, permitted Estonian representatives accredited by the last independent government to remain with diplomatic status, and regards the present government as a legal continuation of the interwar republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It recognised the restoration of Estonian independence on 2 September 1991, and normal relations resumed two days later, when a memorandum of understanding was signed by Deputy Assistant Secretary Curtis Kamman and the Estonian foreign minister, Lennart Meri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Estonia joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991 and the World Trade Organization on 13 November 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It became a member of NATO on 29 March 2004 and of the European Union on 1 May that year, entered the Schengen area on 21 December 2007 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Estonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ee.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Estonia – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/estonia_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Estonia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/estonia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Estonia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/estonia_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Estonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/EST/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Old_Town_of_Tallinn%2C_Tallinn%2C_Estonia_-_panoramio_%2858%29.jpg/1920px-Old_Town_of_Tallinn%2C_Tallinn%2C_Estonia_-_panoramio_%2858%29.jpg","title":"Tallinn Old Town, Estonia","desc":"Tallinn Old Town.","credit":"Ben Bender, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Town_of_Tallinn,_Tallinn,_Estonia_-_panoramio_(58).jpg","alt":"A view of Tallinn Old Town."}},
{"id":"gw-157","num":157,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Trinidad and Tobago","answerText":"Trinidad and Tobago","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 October 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Trinidad and Tobago"},"facts":[["Capital","Port of Spain"],["Population","1.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Chaguanas"],["Area","5,130 km² (1,980 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","caribbean","trinidad and tobago"],"abstract":"<b>Trinidad and Tobago</b> is a two-island state at the southern end of the Caribbean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 5,127 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its published populations differ by a tenth: the United Nations gives 1,511,000 for 2025 where the World Bank gives 1,367,764 and the Commonwealth Secretariat 1.4 million for 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth counts the islands separately, at about 4,800 square kilometres for Trinidad and 300 for Tobago, and describes three mountain ranges and high waterfalls on the larger island and a single range, beaches and coral reefs on the smaller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Just over half the population lives in towns, the urban agglomeration of the capital, Port of Spain, held 544,300 people in 2019, and income per head was 16,960 US dollars in 2023, down from 18,781 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Spanish and then British colonial rule, the two islands achieved independence on 31 August 1962 and joined the Commonwealth that year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the new state the same day by establishing an embassy at Port-of-Spain, with William H. Christensen as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> William Henry Hastie, a judge of the United States Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit, led the American delegation at the independence ceremonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Robert G. Miner presented his credentials as the first American ambassador on 1 December 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 18 September 1962 and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 23 October that year, less than two months after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became a member of the World Trade Organization on 1 March 1995, and the American guide to recognition calls it one of the most industrialised countries in the English-speaking Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Trinidad and Tobago,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tt.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Trinidad and Tobago,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TTO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Trinidad and Tobago,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/trinidad-and-tobago. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Trinidad and Tobago,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/trinidad-and-tobago. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Trinidad and Tobago and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/trinidad_tobago_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Digging_asphalt%2C_Pitch_Lake%2C_Trinidad%2C_W.I.%2C_by_Detroit_Publishing_Company%2C_1890s%2C_from_the_Library_of_Congress_-_det.4a05483.jpg/1920px-Digging_asphalt%2C_Pitch_Lake%2C_Trinidad%2C_W.I.%2C_by_Detroit_Publishing_Company%2C_1890s%2C_from_the_Library_of_Congress_-_det.4a05483.jpg","title":"Pitch Lake, Trinidad and Tobago","desc":"Pitch Lake.","credit":"Detroit Publishing Company, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Digging_asphalt,_Pitch_Lake,_Trinidad,_W.I.,_by_Detroit_Publishing_Company,_1890s,_from_the_Library_of_Congress_-_det.4a05483.jpg","alt":"A view of Pitch Lake."}},
{"id":"gw-158","num":158,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cyprus","answerText":"Cyprus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":["Cyprus","N. Cyprus","Cyprus U.N. Buffer Zone"]},"facts":[["Capital","Nicosia"],["Population","1.40M (2025)"],["Largest city","Nicosia"],["Area","9,251 km² (3,572 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","cyprus"],"abstract":"<b>Cyprus</b> is an island in the eastern Mediterranean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 9,251 square kilometres against Eurostat’s 9,253.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its published populations count different territories: the United Nations gives 1,371,000 for 2025 and marks the figure as referring to the whole country, the World Bank agrees at 1,370,754, and Eurostat gives 979,865, which is the area the government controls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital, Nicosia, held 269,500 people in 2018, forest covers 18.7 per cent of the land, and income per head was 37,077 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island had been a British Crown Colony since 1925 and became independent in 1960, and the United States recognised it on 16 August that year in a State Department press release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American embassy at Nicosia began the same day, when the consulate general was elevated, and Ambassador Fraser Wilkins presented his credentials on 19 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Cyprus joined the United Nations on 20 September 1960 and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 15 July 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1983 a Turkish-controlled area in the north of the island declared its independence, calling itself the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Turkey has been the only country in the world to recognise it, and the United States, which never has, has maintained diplomatic relations with Cyprus without interruption since 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became a member of the World Trade Organization on 30 July 1995 and of the European Union on 1 May 2004, and adopted the euro on 1 January 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a presidential republic whose president is both head of state and head of government, and the European Union records it as still in the process of joining the Schengen area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cyprus,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cy.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Cyprus – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/cyprus_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cyprus,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cyprus. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cyprus and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cyprus_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cyprus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Roca_de_Afrodita%2C_Chipre%2C_2021-12-10%2C_DD_65.jpg/1920px-Roca_de_Afrodita%2C_Chipre%2C_2021-12-10%2C_DD_65.jpg","title":"Petra tou Romiou, Cyprus","desc":"Petra tou Romiou.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roca_de_Afrodita,_Chipre,_2021-12-10,_DD_65.jpg","alt":"A view of Petra tou Romiou."}},
{"id":"gw-159","num":159,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mauritius","answerText":"Mauritius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 March 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 March 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 April 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">SADC member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 August 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mauritius"},"facts":[["Capital","Port Louis"],["Population","1.30M (2025)"],["Largest city","Port Louis"],["Area","2,040 km² (788 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","mauritius"],"abstract":"<b>Mauritius</b> is an island state in the south-west Indian Ocean, about 2,400 kilometres from the south-east coast of Africa, whose land area the Southern African Development Community gives as 2,040 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main island is 67 kilometres long and 46 at its widest, and owes its origin to submarine volcanic activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The published areas differ because they count different islands: the United Nations gives 1,979 square kilometres and says in a footnote that this excludes Saint Brandon and Agalega, while the Commonwealth gives 1,864 for Mauritius itself, 104 for Rodrigues and 2,050 including the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is 1,268,000 by the United Nations count, which does include those islands, and 1,243,741 by the World Bank; forest covers 19.5 per cent of the land and income per head was 11,304 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> English is the official language, French and Creole are widely spoken, and the country is a parliamentary democracy in which coalition government is the norm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Mauritius had been under British sovereignty and became independent on 12 March 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it that same day, when an American embassy was established at Port Louis with William B. Hussey as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 24 April 1968 and the Commonwealth in 1968, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the Southern African Development Community in 1995, attending the bloc’s 15th summit at Midrand in South Africa on 28 August as its 12th member state and its first island nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 2 September 1970, and became a member of the World Trade Organization when that body opened on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mauritius,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mu.html. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Mauritius,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/mauritius. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Mauritius,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/mauritius. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mauritius,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MUS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mauritius,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mauritius. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mauritius and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mauritius_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Le_Morne_Peninsula_in_Mauritius_%2853697779236%29.jpg/1920px-Le_Morne_Peninsula_in_Mauritius_%2853697779236%29.jpg","title":"Le Morne Brabant, Mauritius","desc":"Le Morne Brabant.","credit":"\"dronepicr\", CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Morne_Peninsula_in_Mauritius_(53697779236).jpg","alt":"A view of Le Morne Brabant."}},
{"id":"gw-160","num":160,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Eswatini","answerText":"Eswatini","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"eSwatini"},"facts":[["Capital","Mbabane and Lobamba"],["Population","1.30M (2025)"],["Largest city","Mbabane"],["Area","17,364 km² (6,704 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","eswatini"],"abstract":"<b>Eswatini</b> is a landlocked kingdom in southern Africa, bordered by Mozambique to the east and by South Africa on every other side, whose area the Southern African Development Community gives as 17,364 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations puts the population at 1,256,000 in 2025 and the Commonwealth Secretariat at 1.16 million in 2022, and only about a quarter of it lives in towns, at 72.3 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has two capitals, and the United Nations records the division in a footnote of its own: Mbabane is the administrative capital and Lobamba the legislative one, which is also the seat of the royal court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mbabane held only 68,000 people in 2018, income per head was 3,717 US dollars in 2023, and the Commonwealth gives the area a little higher, at 17,400 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its listed natural resources are sugar, food products, wood pulp and wildlife, and the community calls it the only absolute monarchy in southern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The territory had been under British sovereignty and became independent on 6 September 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Swaziland that same day, when an American embassy was established at Mbabane with Chris C. Pappas, Jr., as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 24 September 1968, and the Commonwealth in 1968 following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a founding member of the Southern African Development Co-ordination Conference, formed at Lusaka in Zambia in April 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 2018 the country was renamed Eswatini, having joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 8 February 1993 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995 under its former name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Eswatini,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sz.html. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Kingdom of Eswatini,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/kingdom-eswatini. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Eswatini,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kingdom-eswatini. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Swaziland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/swaziland. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Eswatini and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/swaziland_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Granite_monolith.jpg","title":"Sibebe, Eswatini","desc":"Sibebe.","credit":"theswazigirl, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Granite_monolith.jpg","alt":"A view of Sibebe."}},
{"id":"gw-161","num":161,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Djibouti","answerText":"Djibouti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 June 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 June 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Djibouti"},"facts":[["Capital","Djibouti"],["Population","1.20M (2025)"],["Largest city","Djibouti"],["Area","23,200 km² (8,960 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","djibouti"],"abstract":"<b>Djibouti</b> is a small country of the Horn of Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 23,200 square kilometres and its population as 1,184,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by about a sixth in ten years, from 1,020,313 in 2015, and the World Bank measures the same area at 23,200 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly four in five of its people live in towns, forest covers 0.2 per cent of the land, and income per head was 3,469 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, which shares the country’s name, held 568,800 people in 2019 — close to half the country — though the United Nations notes that the figure is for the surrounding cercle rather than the city alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory that became Djibouti had been under French sovereignty, and the American post there was a consulate general to the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Republic of Djibouti on 27 June 1977, in a congratulatory message from President Jimmy Carter to President Hassan Gouled Aptidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established the same day, and rather than an embassy being opened that consulate general became the American embassy at Djibouti, under Walter S. Clarke as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 20 September 1977, less than three months later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 16 December 1994, five months before the World Trade Organization admitted it on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Services employ 92.9 per cent of everyone in work, and recorded exports rose from 251 million US dollars in 2015 to 4,466 million in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Djibouti,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dj.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Djibouti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DJI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Djibouti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DJI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Djibouti,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/djibouti. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Djibouti and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/djibouti_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Abbe-8.jpg/1920px-Abbe-8.jpg","title":"Lake Abbe, Djibouti","desc":"Lake Abbe.","credit":"Jbcombes, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbe-8.jpg","alt":"A view of Lake Abbe."}},
{"id":"gw-162","num":162,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Fiji","answerText":"Fiji","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 August 1844</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1874</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 July 1971</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Fiji"},"facts":[["Capital","Suva"],["Population","930k (2025)"],["Largest city","Suva"],["Area","18,272 km² (7,055 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","fiji"],"abstract":"<b>Fiji</b> is an island country of Melanesia, and the United Nations and the Commonwealth Secretariat both give its surface area as 18,272 square kilometres, with a population of 933,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That population has barely moved in a decade, from 918,578 in 2015 to 933,154 in 2025, at 51.1 people to the square kilometre; forest covers 60.6 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Suva, held 178,300 people in 2018, and income per head was 5,889 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States kept a consular presence here long before Britain took the islands: Commercial Agent John B. Williams was appointed the first consul to the Fiji Islands on 19 August 1844, resident at Auckland in New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island chain became a Crown colony within the British Empire thirty years later, in 1874.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Fiji gained independence from British rule on 10 October 1970, and the United States recognised it that day in a statement by Charles W. Yost, its representative in the United Nations Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations three days later, on 13 October 1970.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 22 July 1971, when Fiji’s ambassador Semesa K. Sikivou presented his credentials to President Richard M. Nixon, and an American embassy at Suva followed on 1 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No American ambassador lived there until John P. Condon presented his credentials on 11 April 1978, his predecessors having been resident at Wellington; a military coup in 1987 began what the guide to American recognition calls the coup cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Commonwealth suspended Fiji’s membership three times — from October 1987 to October 1997, from June 2000 to December 2001 and from December 2006 to September 2014 — and the country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 16 November 1993 and the World Trade Organization on 14 January 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Fiji,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fj.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Fiji,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/fiji. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Fiji,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FJI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Fiji,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/fiji. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Fiji and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/fiji_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Nanuya.jpg/1920px-Nanuya.jpg","title":"Yasawa Islands, Fiji","desc":"Yasawa Islands.","credit":"Schnop79, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanuya.jpg","alt":"A view of Yasawa Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-163","num":163,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Comoros","answerText":"Comoros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy Moroni</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 August 1985</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Comoros"},"facts":[["Capital","Moroni"],["Population","880k (2025)"],["Largest city","Moroni"],["Area","1,861 km² (719 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","comoros"],"abstract":"<b>Comoros</b> is an archipelago in the Mozambique Channel, and its two published areas count different islands: the United Nations gives 2,235 square kilometres and the World Bank 1,861, a difference of nearly 400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difference is Mayotte, which France governs and which the General Assembly has treated as Comorian since resolution 31/4 of 21 October 1976; the question was still on its agenda in September 2025, 49 years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 883,000 by the United Nations count and 882,847 by the World Bank, at 474 people to the square kilometre, and it joined the United Nations on 12 November 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 18.9 per cent of the land, the capital, Moroni, held 62,400 people in 2018, and income per head was 1,703 US dollars in 2023, against 1,329 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the Republic of the Comoros on 15 August 1977, and recognition and diplomatic relations came in one act: a joint communiqué issued by the two governments announcing them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The first American chargé d’affaires ad interim, Robert S. Barrett IV, presented his credentials the next day and was resident at Antananarivo in Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American embassy at Moroni was established on 26 August 1985.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It closed on 30 September 1993, after eight years, and later ambassadors were accredited from Port Louis in Mauritius instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 6 March 2006 the responsibility moved again, from the embassy at Port Louis to the one at Antananarivo — the third capital to hold it, and the one it started from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Comoros became a member of the World Trade Organization on 21 August 2024, 49 years after joining the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Comoros,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/km.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Comoros,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Comoros,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Comoros,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/comoros. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Question of the Comorian Island of Mayotte,” A/RES/31/4, adopted at the 39th plenary meeting, 21 October 1976, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/188920. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Question of the Comorian island of Mayotte,” A/DEC/79/576, 5 September 2025, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4087867. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Comoros and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/comoros_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Mount_Karthala_%2811000398163%29.jpg","title":"Mount Karthala, the Comoros","desc":"Mount Karthala.","credit":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Karthala_(11000398163).jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Karthala."}},
{"id":"gw-164","num":164,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guyana","answerText":"Guyana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 May 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 July 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guyana"},"facts":[["Capital","Georgetown"],["Population","840k (2025)"],["Largest city","Georgetown"],["Area","214,969 km² (83,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","guyana"],"abstract":"<b>Guyana</b> lies on the northern coast of South America, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 214,969 square kilometres and its population as 836,000 in 2025 — 4.2 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forest covers 93.8 per cent of the land, the capital, Georgetown, held 109,900 people in 2018, and 94.8 men are counted for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head has more than tripled in three years, from 6,779 US dollars in 2020 to 20,189 in 2023, having been 5,640 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch colonies of Essequibo, Demerara and Berbice, which make up the modern country, were ceded to Great Britain at the Congress of Vienna and established as a British colony in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Guyanese independence on 26 May 1966 by establishing an American embassy at Georgetown, with Delmar R. Carlson as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 5 July 1966, six weeks after independence, and became a member of the World Trade Organization when that body opened on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 20 September 1966 and the Commonwealth that year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Commonwealth gives the population as 786,560 in 2022 and rounds the area to 215,000 square kilometres, while the World Bank’s series gives 835,986 for 2025, a figure that has risen by a tenth since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of that territory is claimed by Venezuela, and Guyana has taken the dispute to the International Court of Justice as <i>Arbitral Award of 3 October 1899</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Court held public hearings on the merits at the Peace Palace from 4 to 11 May 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guyana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gy.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guyana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guyana. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guyana and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guyana_e.htm. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Guyana,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/guyana. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guyana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUY/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Arbitral Award of 3 October 1899, Guyana v. Venezuela,” https://www.icj-cij.org/case/171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e2/GuyanaKaieteurFalls2004.jpg","title":"Kaieteur Falls, Guyana","desc":"Kaieteur Falls.","credit":"Sorenriise at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GuyanaKaieteurFalls2004.jpg","alt":"A view of Kaieteur Falls."}},
{"id":"gw-165","num":165,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Solomon Islands","answerText":"Solomon Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 July 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 July 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Solomon Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Honiara"],["Population","840k (2025)"],["Largest city","Honiara"],["Area","28,896 km² (11,157 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","solomon islands"],"abstract":"<b>Solomon Islands</b> is an archipelago of Melanesia, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 28,896 square kilometres and its population as 839,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by nearly a third in ten years, from 639,415 in 2015, and fewer than a quarter of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three published areas do not agree: 28,896 at the United Nations, 28,900 at the World Bank and 30,407 at the Commonwealth Secretariat, which also gives the population as 686,900 for 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The capital, Honiara, held 81,800 people in 2018, and income per head has barely moved in a decade, from 2,045 US dollars in 2015 to 2,042 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American relations with the islands began in the late 19th century, when United States vessels started stopping at them, and in the Second World War they became the site of intense fighting, particularly on Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The islands had been under British sovereignty, and the United States recognised them on 7 July 1978, when Senator John H. Glenn, as the President’s personal representative, delivered a letter to that effect from President Jimmy Carter to Prime Minister Peter Kenilorea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They joined the United Nations on 19 September 1978, and diplomatic relations were established on 9 October, when Mary S. Olmsted presented her credentials as ambassador — accredited to Papua New Guinea as well, and resident at Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American consulate at Honiara was upgraded to an embassy on 4 July 1988.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That embassy closed on 30 July 1993 and reopened on 27 January 2023, after nearly thirty years, during which the ambassador again lived at Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in 1978 following independence from Britain, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 28 December 1994 and the World Trade Organization on 26 July 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Solomon Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sb.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Solomon Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Solomon Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2023. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Solomon Islands,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/solomon-islands. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Solomon Islands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/solomon-islands. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Solomon Islands and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/solomon_islands_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Marovo_Lagoon_%2816_August_2008%29.jpg/1920px-Marovo_Lagoon_%2816_August_2008%29.jpg","title":"Marovo Lagoon, the Solomon Islands","desc":"Marovo Lagoon.","credit":"Xplore Dive from Mooloolaba, Australia, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marovo_Lagoon_(16_August_2008).jpg","alt":"A shallow turquoise lagoon fringed with palms."}},
{"id":"gw-166","num":166,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bhutan","answerText":"Bhutan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recommended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 October 1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bhutan"},"facts":[["Capital","Thimphu"],["Population","800k (2025)"],["Largest city","Thimphu"],["Area","38,394 km² (14,824 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","bhutan"],"abstract":"<b>Bhutan</b> is a landlocked country of the eastern Himalaya, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 38,394 square kilometres and its population as 797,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank agrees closely, at 796,682 people and 38,390 square kilometres, and that population has grown by about a twelfth in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only two in five of its people live in towns, at 20.8 to the square kilometre across the country as a whole, and the capital, Thimphu, held 203,300 of them in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 3,839 US dollars in 2023 against 2,954 in 2015, recorded exports were 812 million dollars against 782 million, and forest covers 71.2 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Security Council recommended the country for membership of the United Nations unanimously, at its 1566th meeting, on 10 February 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The General Assembly admitted it at its 1934th plenary meeting on 21 September 1971, more than seven months after that recommendation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bhutan is not a member of the World Trade Organization: its working party on accession was established on 6 October 1999 and the accession has not been completed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Agriculture employed 58 per cent of everyone in work in 2015 and 43.8 per cent by 2023, while services rose from 32.4 to 40.9 per cent and industry from 9.7 to 15.3.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Use of the internet rose from 39.8 people in every hundred in 2015 to 88.4 by 2023, and the fertility rate fell from 1.8 births per woman to 1.4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 75.6 years for women and 71.8 for men, under-five mortality has fallen from 30.6 deaths per thousand live births to 20.5, and the country counts 114.6 men for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bhutan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bt.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bhutan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BTN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Bhutan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BTN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 292 (1971), adopted at its 1566th meeting, of 10 February 1971,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/90800. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of Bhutan to membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/2751 (XXVI), United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192050. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Bhutan,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_bhoutan_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Paro_Taktsang%2C_Bhutan_%28edited%29.jpg/1920px-Paro_Taktsang%2C_Bhutan_%28edited%29.jpg","title":"Paro Taktsang, Bhutan","desc":"Paro Taktsang.","credit":"Original: Nina R from Africa Derivative work: UnpetitproleX, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paro_Taktsang,_Bhutan_(edited).jpg","alt":"A view of Paro Taktsang."}},
{"id":"gw-167","num":167,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Macau","answerText":"Macau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 January 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sea area since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2015</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Macao"},"facts":[["Capital","?"],["Population","720k (2025)"],["Largest city","?"],["Area","33 km² (13 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","asia","macau"],"abstract":"<b>Macau</b> lies on the southeastern coast of China, in the Pearl River Delta, and consists of a peninsula and islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land has grown by reclamation, from around 29.5 square kilometres in 2010 to 32.9 at the end of 2018, and since 2015 it has also exercised jurisdiction over a maritime area of 85 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s own area series records the same growth, holding at 32.9 square kilometres through 2020 and 33 from 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the area as 33 square kilometres and the population as 722,000 in 2025, which is 22,563 people to the square kilometre; the World Bank counts 685,900, having recorded 642,900 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Agriculture employs 0.5 per cent of those in work, and income per head fell from 72,404 US dollars in 2015 to 37,103 in 2020 before recovering to 64,158 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At the by-census of 2016, 88.7 per cent of the population was of Chinese ethnicity, down 3.7 percentage points on 2011, and 1.8 per cent of Portuguese ethnicity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among residents aged three and over, 80.1 per cent spoke mostly Cantonese at home, 5.5 per cent Mandarin, 3.0 per cent Tagalog, 2.8 per cent English and 0.6 per cent Portuguese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the population was born in mainland China, 43.6 per cent against the 40.7 per cent born in the territory itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Chief Executive is selected by election or through consultation held locally, serves five years and may not serve more than two consecutive terms; an amendment in 2012 raised the election committee from 300 members to 400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 11 January 1991, four years before the World Trade Organization opened, and was a founding member of that body on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations, “Common Core Document Forming Part of the Reports of States Parties: Macao, China,” HRI/CORE/CHN-MAC/2019, 5 August 2020, United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3884318. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Macao SAR, China,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “China, Macao SAR,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mo.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Macao, China and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/macao_china_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Macao SAR, China,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/%E5%A4%A7%E4%B8%89%E5%B7%B4%E7%89%8C%E5%9D%8A.jpg/1920px-%E5%A4%A7%E4%B8%89%E5%B7%B4%E7%89%8C%E5%9D%8A.jpg","title":"Ruins of St. Paul's, Macau","desc":"Ruins of St. Paul's.","credit":"GG001213, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A7%E4%B8%89%E5%B7%B4%E7%89%8C%E5%9D%8A.jpg","alt":"A view of Ruins of St. Paul's."}},
{"id":"gw-168","num":168,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Luxembourg","answerText":"Luxembourg","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1878</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 July 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 July 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Luxembourg"},"facts":[["Capital","Luxembourg"],["Population","680k (2025)"],["Largest city","Luxembourg"],["Area","2,586 km² (998 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","luxembourg"],"abstract":"<b>Luxembourg</b> is landlocked in western Europe, and its three published areas differ a little: 2,586 square kilometres at the United Nations, 2,590 at the World Bank and 2,595 at Eurostat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is 680,000 by the United Nations count, 681,973 by Eurostat and 686,970 by the World Bank, having grown by a fifth since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Income per head was 128,936 US dollars in 2023 against 105,475 in 2015, and more than nine in ten of its people live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a grand duchy and a parliamentary constitutional monarchy, whose Chamber of Deputies has 60 members elected every five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country achieved full independence in 1867, and the United States recognised it on 31 May 1878, when President Rutherford B. Hayes issued an exequatur to François Berger, the consul general ad interim of Luxembourg at New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations began on 17 July 1903, when the American ambassador to the Netherlands presented his credentials to Luxembourg and stayed resident at The Hague; in 1923 the accreditation passed to the ambassador at Brussels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany occupied the country on 10 May 1940 and the American legation closed on 15 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States then accredited its ambassador to Canada to the Luxembourg government in exile at Ottawa, and when that government moved to the United Kingdom on 14 October 1943 credentials were presented to it in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The legation at Luxembourg reopened on 23 September 1944, and the representation was raised to ambassador on 21 September 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Luxembourg was a founding member of the United Nations on 24 October 1945 and of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 January 1948, joined the European Communities at their start on 1 January 1958, entered the Schengen area on 26 March 1995 and adopted the euro on 1 January 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Luxembourg,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lu.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Luxembourg,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LUX/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Luxembourg – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/luxembourg_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Luxembourg,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LUX/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Luxembourg,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/luxembourg. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Luxembourg and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/luxembourg_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Luxembourg_City_pano_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg/1920px-Luxembourg_City_pano_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg","title":"Luxembourg City, Luxembourg","desc":"Luxembourg City above the Alzette valley.","credit":"Benh LIEU SONG, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxembourg_City_pano_Wikimedia_Commons.jpg","alt":"An old town on a rock spur above a green river valley."}},
{"id":"gw-169","num":169,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Suriname","answerText":"Suriname","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 December 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GATT member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 March 1978</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Suriname"},"facts":[["Capital","Paramaribo"],["Population","640k (2025)"],["Largest city","Paramaribo"],["Area","163,820 km² (63,251 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","south america","suriname"],"abstract":"<b>Suriname</b> is on the northeastern coast of South America, and the United Nations and the World Bank agree exactly on its surface area, 163,820 square kilometres, with a population of 640,000 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 4.1 people to the square kilometre it is thinly settled, two in three of them in towns, and the capital, Paramaribo, held 239,500 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by a tenth since 2015, when the World Bank counted 581,627, forest covers 95 per cent of the land, and agriculture employs only 7.4 per cent of those in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head has fallen rather than risen, from 8,814 US dollars in 2015 to 6,792 in 2020 and 5,976 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory had been a Dutch colony dating from 1667, was accorded increasing degrees of autonomy from 1951, and was granted independence by the Netherlands in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A ceremony to proclaim that independence was held at Paramaribo on 25 November 1975, and the United States was represented at it by the Secretary of the Navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That same day President Gerald Ford wrote to President Johan Henri Eliza Ferrier extending American recognition and saying that he hoped for the prompt establishment of diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Relations and an American embassy at Paramaribo were established on that date too, with Robert L. Flanagin as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Suriname joined the United Nations on 4 December 1975, nine days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1980 the elected parliamentary government was overthrown and a military regime installed in its place; a democratic government was re-established in 1991, and the country acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 22 March 1978 and to the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Suriname,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area (sq. km): Suriname,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SUR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Suriname,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/suriname. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Suriname and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/suriname_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Suriname,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SUR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Houses_at_Waterkant%2C_Paramaribo.JPG/1920px-Houses_at_Waterkant%2C_Paramaribo.JPG","title":"Paramaribo, Suriname","desc":"The Waterkant at Paramaribo.","credit":"Mark Ahsmann, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houses_at_Waterkant,_Paramaribo.JPG","alt":"Tall white-painted wooden colonial houses along a street."}},
{"id":"gw-170","num":170,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Montenegro","answerText":"Montenegro","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 March 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrawn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 December 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 June 2006</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 June 2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Montenegro"},"facts":[["Capital","Podgorica"],["Population","620k (2025)"],["Largest city","Podgorica"],["Area","13,812 km² (5,333 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","montenegro"],"abstract":"<b>Montenegro</b> is in the western Balkans, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 13,812 square kilometres and its population as 633,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 623,129.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head was 11,885 US dollars in 2023 against 6,455 in 2015, two in three of its people live in towns, and the capital, Podgorica, held 177,200 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Great Powers of Europe recognised an independent and sovereign Principality of Montenegro at the Congress of Berlin in 1878.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 3 March 1905, when President Theodore Roosevelt approved the Diplomatic and Consular Act, which assigned the American mission in Greece responsibility for representation in Montenegro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the First World War the kingdom was subsumed into the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, and President Woodrow Wilson decided on 30 December 1920 to withdraw recognition of an independent Montenegro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On 21 January 1921 the acting Secretary of State told Montenegro’s honorary consul-general that, in view of the present status of Montenegro, the government no longer considered it necessary to accord recognition to her diplomatic and consular officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Republic of Montenegro declared independence from the State Union of Serbia and Montenegro on 3 June 2006, and the United States recognised it again on 13 June, 85 years after withdrawing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 15 August 2006, and the country had joined the United Nations on 28 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It applied for European Union membership in 2008, was granted candidate status in 2010, and accession negotiations opened on 29 June 2012; all 33 screened chapters have been opened and 16 provisionally closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 29 April 2012 and became a member of NATO on 5 June 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Montenegro,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/me.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Montenegro,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Montenegro,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/montenegro. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Montenegro,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/montenegro_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Montenegro and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/montenegro_e.htm. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Kotor_aerial_1.jpg/1920px-Kotor_aerial_1.jpg","title":"Bay of Kotor, Montenegro","desc":"Bay of Kotor.","credit":"kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kotor_aerial_1.jpg","alt":"A view of Bay of Kotor."}},
{"id":"gw-171","num":171,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Western Sahara","answerText":"Western Sahara","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">ICJ opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 October 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">MINURSO set up</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 April 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"W. Sahara"},"facts":[["Capital","El Aaiún"],["Population","601k (2025)"],["Largest city","El Aaiún"],["Area","266,000 km² (103,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","africa","western sahara"],"abstract":"<b>Western Sahara</b> is a territory in northwestern Africa of 266,000 square kilometres, with 601,000 people in 2025 and its largest settlement, El Aaiún, holding 232,400 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations notes that this area comprises the Northern Region, the former Saguia el Hamra, and the Southern Region, the former Rio de Oro, the two parts of the territory Spain colonised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is desert of 2.3 people to the square kilometre, 2.5 per cent of it forested, and the Moroccan dirham is the currency in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In December 1974 the General Assembly asked the International Court of Justice whether the territory had belonged to no one when Spain colonised it, and what legal ties it had with Morocco and the Mauritanian entity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Court answered on 16 October 1975 that it had not been unowned, and that ties of allegiance existed between the Sultan of Morocco and some of its tribes, and rights over land between the Mauritanian entity and the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It found no tie of territorial sovereignty with either, and so nothing to affect self-determination or the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples of 14 December 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 29 April 1991 the Security Council unanimously established the United Nations Mission for the Referendum in Western Sahara, to organise and supervise a vote on self-determination with the Organization of African Unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That vote has not been held, and the Secretariat’s 2025 working paper for the Special Committee on decolonization sets out both parties’ positions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Morocco holds that a solution should rest on its autonomy initiative and on its territorial integrity, while the Frente Popular para la Liberación de Saguía el-Hamra y Rio de Oro holds that this is the last colony in Africa, whose people have an inalienable right to self-determination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Secretary-General reported in 2024 that the situation was marked by low-intensity hostilities, with intermittent firing across the berm, and that the lack of a full ceasefire remained a major setback.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Western Sahara,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/eh.html. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Western Sahara: Overview of the Case,” Advisory Opinion of 16 October 1975, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/61. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples,” A/RES/1514, 14 December 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206145. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 690, adopted at its 2984th meeting, on 29 April 1991,” S/RES/690, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/112199. [Open access]","UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Western Sahara: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/17, 8 January 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4073767. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Dakhla%2C_Western_Sahara_%2811%29.jpg/1920px-Dakhla%2C_Western_Sahara_%2811%29.jpg","title":"Dakhla, Western Sahara, Western Sahara","desc":"Dakhla, Western Sahara.","credit":"YoTuT from United States, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dakhla,_Western_Sahara_(11).jpg","alt":"A view of Dakhla, Western Sahara."}},
{"id":"gw-172","num":172,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malta","answerText":"Malta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Euro area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2008</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malta"},"facts":[["Capital","Valletta"],["Population","570k (2025)"],["Largest city","Valletta"],["Area","316 km² (122 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","malta"],"abstract":"<b>Malta</b> is an archipelago in the central Mediterranean, and the European Union gives its area as 316 square kilometres and its population as 574,250, where the United Nations gives 545,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 568,847 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is among the most crowded countries on earth, at more than 1,700 people to the square kilometre, with 94.7 per cent of them living in towns and 1.4 per cent of the land forested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes islands that are mainly low, rocky and flat, with many coastal cliffs, the three largest inhabited, and Sicily to the north with Libya and Tunisia to the south and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Malta was a Crown Colony of the United Kingdom from 1814 until 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 18 September 1964, when President Lyndon B. Johnson sent a letter to Prime Minister Giorgio Borg Olivier, and the embassy at Valletta was established three days later under Harrison Lewis as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The first American ambassador, George J. Feldman, presented his credentials on 5 October 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Malta joined the Commonwealth in 1964 following independence from Britain, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 17 November and joined the United Nations on 1 December, both within eleven weeks of that recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995 and of the European Union on 1 May 2004, joined the Schengen area on 21 December 2007 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a single-chamber parliamentary republic with six seats in the European Parliament, and its income per head of 40,900 euros in 2023 ranked ninth in the Union against an average of 38,100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tourism is central, with 2,287,000 arrivals in 2022 against 659,000 in 2020, and the United Nations counts the population of the capital as that of the Northern Harbour and Southern Harbour districts rather than of Valletta itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Malta,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mt.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Malta – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/malta_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Malta,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Malta,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/malta. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malta,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malta. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Malta and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/malta_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Facade_Hagar_Qim.jpg","title":"Ħaġar Qim, Malta","desc":"Ħaġar Qim.","credit":"Hamelin de Guettelet, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facade_Hagar_Qim.jpg","alt":"A view of Ħaġar Qim."}},
{"id":"gw-173","num":173,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Maldives","answerText":"Maldives","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1982, left 2016, rejoined 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Maldives"},"facts":[["Capital","Malé"],["Population","530k (2025)"],["Largest city","Malé"],["Area","300 km² (116 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","maldives"],"abstract":"The <b>Maldives</b> is a chain of coral atolls in the Indian Ocean, and the United Nations gives its land area as 300 square kilometres and its population as 530,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 527,799 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes around 1,200 small coral islands and sandbanks sheltered by barrier reefs, about 200 of them inhabited, with India and Sri Lanka the nearest neighbours some 600 and 645 kilometres (370 and 400 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> UNESCO records that Maldivian coral reefs hold the richest diversity in the region and are the seventh largest in the world, accounting for about 5 per cent of the world’s reef area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only two people in five live in a town, 2.7 per cent of the land is forested, and 1,675,000 visitors arrived by air in 2022 against 555,000 in 2020, the year the economy contracted by 32.9 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 12,530 US dollars in 2023, against 9,645 in 2015 and 7,394 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Maldives became independent from Britain in 1965, and the United States recognised it on 10 August that year, when President Lyndon B. Johnson sent a message to Prime Minister Ibrahim Nasir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 21 September 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 9 April 1966, when the American ambassador to Ceylon, Cecil B. Lyon, presented his credentials while remaining resident at Colombo, and no American embassy has ever opened at Malé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country became a republic in 1968, and the ambassador at Colombo was reaccredited to it on 13 January 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 19 April 1983 and joined the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995; it joined the Commonwealth in 1982, withdrew on 13 October 2016 and rejoined on 1 February 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Maldives,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mv.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Maldives,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Maldives,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/maldives. [Open access]","UNESCO, “Baa Atoll Biosphere Reserve,” Man and the Biosphere Programme, https://www.unesco.org/en/mab/baa-atoll. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Maldives,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/maldives. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Maldives and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/maldives_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Maldives_soft_coral.jpg/1920px-Maldives_soft_coral.jpg","title":"Maldives, the Maldives","desc":"Maldives.","credit":"Tchami, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maldives_soft_coral.jpg","alt":"A view of Maldives."}},
{"id":"gw-174","num":174,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cabo Verde","answerText":"Cabo Verde","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 July 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 July 2008</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cabo Verde"},"facts":[["Capital","Praia"],["Population","527k (2025)"],["Largest city","Praia"],["Area","4,033 km² (1,557 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","cape verde"],"abstract":"<b>Cabo Verde</b> is an archipelago in the Atlantic off West Africa, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 4,033 square kilometres and its population as 527,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 524,877 for 2024 and an area of 4,030.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its capital, Praia, held 167,500 in 2018, two people in three live in towns, and 11 per cent of the land is forested, while life expectancy at birth is 79.5 years for women and 73.3 for men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 4,961 US dollars in 2023 against 3,415 in 2015, and services carry the economy, with agriculture at 5.2 per cent of value added and industry at 12.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is larger than the country: 785,000 non-resident tourists stayed in its hotels in 2022, against 180,000 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands were under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Republic of Cape Verde on 5 July 1975, when President Gerald R. Ford sent a letter to that effect to its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 19 July, when the Cape Verdean ambassador presented his credentials to Ford, and the country joined the United Nations on 16 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first American ambassador presented her credentials on 18 November 1976 while accredited to Guinea-Bissau as well and resident at Bissau, and the embassy at Praia opened only on 28 January 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the World Trade Organization on 23 July 2008, and its money is the Cabo Verde escudo, exchanged at 105.7 to the US dollar in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2013 it was renamed Cabo Verde, the form the United Nations and the World Trade Organization both use, while the American guide still files it under Cape Verde.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cabo Verde,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cv.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cabo Verde,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CPV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cape Verde,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cape-verde. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cabo Verde and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cape_verde_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Cabo Verde,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CPV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2019:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Cape_Verde_Pico_do_Fogo_b.jpg/1920px-Cape_Verde_Pico_do_Fogo_b.jpg","title":"Pico do Fogo, Cabo Verde","desc":"Pico do Fogo.","credit":"Cayambe, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Verde_Pico_do_Fogo_b.jpg","alt":"A view of Pico do Fogo."}},
{"id":"gw-175","num":175,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Brunei","answerText":"Brunei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 June 1850</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Brunei"},"facts":[["Capital","Bandar Seri Begawan"],["Population","466k (2025)"],["Largest city","Bandar Seri Begawan"],["Area","5,765 km² (2,226 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","asia","brunei"],"abstract":"<b>Brunei</b> is a small state on the northern coast of Borneo, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 5,765 square kilometres and its population as 466,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 462,721 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a coastal plain rising to mountains in the east, with the South China Sea to the north and the Malaysian state of Sarawak on every other side, dividing the country in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 72.1 per cent of the land, 77.9 per cent of the people live in towns, and the capital, Bandar Seri Begawan, held only 40,800 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Industry is 60.7 per cent of value added against agriculture’s 1.2, and income per head was 32,963 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is slight and was all but extinguished: 218,000 visitors arrived in 2015, 62,000 in 2020, and 4,000 by air in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The United States signed a Treaty of Peace, Friendship, Commerce and Navigation with Brunei on 23 June 1850, which entered into force on 11 July 1853 and is still in effect today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A consulate followed from 1865 to 1868, the post called Brunei, Borneo, and Consul Captain C. Lees Moses presented his credentials there on 18 July 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Brunei became a British protectorate in 1888, at which point the United Kingdom took over its foreign affairs, and a constitution of 1959 declared it self-governing while leaving foreign affairs, security and defence to Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised its full independence on 1 January 1984, and Ambassador Barrington King presented his credentials at Bandar Seri Begawan on 28 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Brunei joined the United Nations on 21 September 1984 and the Commonwealth the same year following independence, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 9 December 1993 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Brunei Darussalam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bn.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Brunei Darussalam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Brunei Darussalam,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/brunei-darussalam. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Brunei Darussalam,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/brunei. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Brunei Darussalam and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/brunei_darussalam_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_golden_domed_Sultan_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_Mosque_%2818404149198%29.jpg/1920px-The_golden_domed_Sultan_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_Mosque_%2818404149198%29.jpg","title":"Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque, Brunei","desc":"Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque.","credit":"Jorge Láscar from Melbourne, Australia, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_golden_domed_Sultan_Omar_Ali_Saifuddin_Mosque_(18404149198).jpg","alt":"A view of Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddien Mosque."}},
{"id":"gw-176","num":176,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belize","answerText":"Belize","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 October 1981</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belize"},"facts":[["Capital","Belmopan"],["Population","423k (2025)"],["Largest city","Belize City"],["Area","22,965 km² (8,867 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","belize"],"abstract":"<b>Belize</b> is on the Caribbean coast of Central America, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 22,965 square kilometres and its population as 423,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 417,072 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a long east coast, mainly flat and swampy, with low mountains in the south, Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula to the north and Guatemala to the west and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tropical forest covers 58.4 per cent of the land, fewer than half its people live in towns, and 373,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 144,000 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Income per head was 7,489 US dollars in 2023, agriculture is 10.5 per cent of value added, and the capital, Belmopan, held only 23,000 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States opened a consulate in British Honduras on 3 March 1847, fifteen years before the territory became a British colony in 1862.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The colony took the name Belize in June 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 1960s the United States sought and failed to mediate the dispute between Belize and Guatemala over contested Belizean territory, which dates back to a treaty of 1859.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Belize became independent from the United Kingdom on 21 September 1981, and the United States recognised it five weeks later, on 29 October, by raising its consulate general to an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 25 September 1981 and the Commonwealth that year following independence, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 7 October 1983 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Guatemala and Belize put the territorial claim to the International Court of Justice by a special agreement of 8 December 2008 with a protocol of 25 May 2015, and the Court was seised of the dispute on 12 June 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Belize,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bz.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Belize,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLZ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Belize,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/belize. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belize,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belize. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Belize and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/belize_e.htm. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Guatemala’s Territorial, Insular and Maritime Claim: Guatemala/Belize,” https://www.icj-cij.org/case/177. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Belize_Blue_Hole_%28TMP%29_%2816912331906%29.jpg/1920px-Belize_Blue_Hole_%28TMP%29_%2816912331906%29.jpg","title":"the Great Blue Hole, Belize","desc":"The Great Blue Hole.","credit":"The TerraMar Project, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belize_Blue_Hole_(TMP)_(16912331906).jpg","alt":"A deep circular sinkhole in a pale turquoise reef."}},
{"id":"gw-177","num":177,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bahamas","answerText":"Bahamas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 July 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accession talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 18 July 2001</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bahamas"},"facts":[["Capital","Nassau"],["Population","403k (2025)"],["Largest city","Nassau"],["Area","13,940 km² (5,382 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","bahamas"],"abstract":"The <b>Bahamas</b> is an archipelago in the western Atlantic, and the three institutions that measure it disagree: the United Nations gives its surface area as 13,940 square kilometres, the Commonwealth 14,000 and the World Bank 13,880.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 403,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count and 401,283 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, with 279,700 of them in the capital, Nassau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes nearly 700 coral islands, around 30 of them inhabited, generally flat and low-lying, 100 kilometres (60 miles) southeast of Florida and 80 kilometres (50 miles) northeast of Cuba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 50.9 per cent of the land, 83.1 per cent of the people live in towns, and the country holds 75.2 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 35,896 US dollars in 2023, and 1,452,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 441,000 in 2020, more than three times the country’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Bahamas became fully independent from the United Kingdom in 1973 and joined the Commonwealth that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 10 July 1973, and the act of recognition was the opening of an embassy: relations and the embassy at Nassau began that same day, with Moncrieff J. Spear as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 18 September 1973, ten weeks after that recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not yet a member of the World Trade Organization: its accession working party was established on 18 July 2001, and more than two decades later the accession is still in progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Bahamian dollar was exchanged at one to the US dollar in 2024, and services carry the economy, agriculture supplying 0.5 per cent of value added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bahamas,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bs.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “The Bahamas,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/bahamas. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bahamas,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Bahamas,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bahamas. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Bahamas,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_bahamas_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Bahamas,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2019:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Bahamas_1989_%28535%29_Little_Exuma_Williamstown_%2824569757813%29.jpg/1920px-Bahamas_1989_%28535%29_Little_Exuma_Williamstown_%2824569757813%29.jpg","title":"Exuma, the Bahamas","desc":"Exuma.","credit":"Rüdiger Stehn from Kiel, Deutschland, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bahamas_1989_(535)_Little_Exuma_Williamstown_(24569757813).jpg","alt":"A view of Exuma."}},
{"id":"gw-178","num":178,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Iceland","answerText":"Iceland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Foreign affairs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 April 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 June 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">NATO founder</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 1949</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iceland"},"facts":[["Capital","Reykjavík"],["Population","398k (2025)"],["Largest city","Reykjavík"],["Area","103,000 km² (39,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","iceland"],"abstract":"<b>Iceland</b> is an island in the North Atlantic, and the United Nations and the World Bank both give its surface area as 103,000 square kilometres, with a population of 398,000 in 2025 by the first count and 386,506 in 2024 by the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It holds four people to the square kilometre, 93.9 per cent of them in towns and 216,400 in the capital, Reykjavík.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only 0.5 per cent of the land is forested, and income per head was 80,827 US dollars in 2023 against 52,930 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has grown by about a sixth in a decade, from 330,815 in 2015 to 386,506 in 2024, while visitors numbered 1,289,000 in 2015, 486,000 in 2020 and 698,000 in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany occupied Denmark on 9 April 1940, and Iceland took control of its own foreign relations the next day, without yet declaring itself independent of the Danish crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In July and December 1940 Icelandic officials asked the United States to place the island under its protection under the Monroe Doctrine, to forestall a feared German invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States was unenthusiastic at first, but concluded that occupying Iceland was a matter of its own national defence, and did so on 7 July 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American legation at Reykjavík followed on 30 September 1941, when Lincoln MacVeagh presented his credentials, three years before there was an independent Iceland to have relations with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After a plebiscite the country formally became an independent republic on 17 June 1944, and the United States recognised it that same day in public statements of congratulation by President Franklin D. Roosevelt and Secretary of State Cordell Hull; the legation was raised to an embassy on 3 November 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Iceland was one of the twelve countries that created NATO on 4 April 1949, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 21 April 1968 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iceland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/is.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Iceland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ISL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iceland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iceland. [Open access]","North Atlantic Treaty Organization, “Member countries,” https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/topics_52044.htm. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Iceland and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/iceland_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Gullfoss_from_the_Air_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Gullfoss, Iceland","desc":"Gullfoss.","credit":"Nickspix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gullfoss_from_the_Air_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A view of Gullfoss."}},
{"id":"gw-179","num":179,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Vanuatu","answerText":"Vanuatu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 July 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 September 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 July 2024</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vanuatu"},"facts":[["Capital","Port Vila"],["Population","335k (2025)"],["Largest city","Port Vila"],["Area","12,189 km² (4,706 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","vanuatu"],"abstract":"<b>Vanuatu</b> is an island country in the southwestern Pacific, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 12,189 square kilometres and its population as 335,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 327,777 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a double chain of 13 principal islands and many smaller ones, volcanic and coral in origin and around 65 of them inhabited, about 800 kilometres (500 miles) west of Fiji and nearly 1,800 kilometres (1,100 miles) east of Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 36.3 per cent of the land, only a quarter of the people live in towns, and the capital, Port Vila, held 52,700 in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture is 24.4 per cent of value added, income per head was 3,492 US dollars in 2023, and 30,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 90,000 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain and France held the islands together, under the joint sovereignty of a condominium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Vanuatu gained its independence on 30 July 1980, and the United States recognised the new republic soon afterwards, when President Jimmy Carter sent a message to that effect to its government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in 1980, whose own record gives the reason as the ending of the Anglo-French condominium rather than independence from Britain, and the United Nations on 15 September 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were not established until 30 September 1986, when representatives of both states signed an agreement to that effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ambassadorial relations began on 10 April 1987, when the American ambassador to Papua New Guinea presented his credentials, no mission having been opened at Port Vila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States opened its embassy at Port Vila only on 19 July 2024, 44 years after recognising the country, which had joined the World Trade Organization on 24 August 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Vanuatu,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vu.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Vanuatu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VUT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Vanuatu,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/vanuatu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Vanuatu,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/vanuatu. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Vanuatu and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/vanuatu_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Mount_Yasur_eruption_2006%2C_Tanna_Island%2C_Vanuatu%2C_VAN_0516.jpg/1920px-Mount_Yasur_eruption_2006%2C_Tanna_Island%2C_Vanuatu%2C_VAN_0516.jpg","title":"Mount Yasur, Vanuatu","desc":"Mount Yasur.","credit":"Rolf Cosar, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Yasur_eruption_2006,_Tanna_Island,_Vanuatu,_VAN_0516.jpg","alt":"A view of Mount Yasur."}},
{"id":"gw-180","num":180,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"New Caledonia","answerText":"New Caledonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Matignon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 June 1988</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nouméa Accord</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 May 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendums</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018, 2020 and 2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"New Caledonia"},"facts":[["Capital","Nouméa"],["Population","295k (2025)"],["Largest city","Nouméa"],["Area","19,100 km² (7,375 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","new caledonia"],"abstract":"<b>New Caledonia</b> is a group of Pacific islands about 1,500 kilometres (930 miles) east of Australia, administered by France as a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 19,100 square kilometres and its population as 295,000 in 2025, where the Secretariat’s own working paper gives a land area of 18,575 square kilometres and 268,500 people at the start of 2023, and the World Bank 292,639 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 1,422,543 square kilometres, and the main island, Grande Terre, accounts for 16,750 of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is Nouméa, 71.1 per cent of the people live in towns, forest covers 45.8 per cent of the land, and income per head was 34,981 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 41 per cent of the population is Melanesian, mainly Kanak, and 24 per cent of European origin; French is official and about 27 Kanak languages are spoken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> James Cook named Grande Terre New Caledonia in 1774, and France annexed the territory on 24 September 1853, making it an overseas territory with limited autonomy in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Kanak independence movement launched in the 1970s led to the violence of the 1980s known as the <i>événements</i>, in which about 80 people died between 1984 and 1988.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Matignon Agreements of 26 June 1988, between the Front de libération nationale kanak et socialiste, the anti-independence Rassemblement pour la Calédonie dans la République and France, established three provinces to restore the balance of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nouméa Accord, signed on 5 May 1998, transferred non-sovereign powers and institutions to New Caledonia between 1998 and 2018 and provided for up to three referendums on accession to full sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three were held, on 4 November 2018, 4 October 2020 and 12 December 2021, and the General Assembly had declared in 1986 that France must transmit information on the territory under Chapter XI of the Charter, affirming its people’s inalienable right to self-determination under resolution 1514.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “New Caledonia: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/11, 18 March 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4080540. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “New Caledonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nc.html. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Implementation of the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples,” A/RES/41/41 A, 2 December 1986, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192415. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples,” A/RES/1514, 14 December 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206145. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: New Caledonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NCL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Isle_of_Pines.jpg/1920px-Isle_of_Pines.jpg","title":"Isle of Pines (New Caledonia), New Caledonia","desc":"Isle of Pines (New Caledonia).","credit":"Speddie23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isle_of_Pines.jpg","alt":"A view of Isle of Pines (New Caledonia)."}},
{"id":"gw-181","num":181,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Barbados","answerText":"Barbados","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Barbados"},"facts":[["Capital","Bridgetown"],["Population","283k (2025)"],["Largest city","Bridgetown"],["Area","431 km² (166 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","barbados"],"abstract":"<b>Barbados</b> is an island in the eastern Caribbean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 431 square kilometres and its population as 283,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 430 square kilometres and 287,370 for 2022 and the World Bank 282,467 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is 657 people to the square kilometre, and yet only 31.2 per cent of them live in what the United Nations counts as towns, the capital, Bridgetown, holding 89,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a fairly flat island rising through terraced tablelands to Mount Hillaby at 336 metres (1,102 feet) and surrounded by coral reefs, with Saint Lucia to the north, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines to the west and Trinidad and Tobago to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 14.7 per cent of the land, and income per head was 23,804 US dollars in 2023 against 18,954 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States kept a consular presence on the island from 11 June 1823, when Barbados was still a British colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> After a constitutional conference with Great Britain in June 1966 at which Barbados negotiated its independence, it became an independent state within the British Commonwealth on 30 November 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it that same day by opening its embassy at Bridgetown, with George Dolgin as chargé d’affaires ad interim, so that the recognition and the mission were one act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Barbados joined the Commonwealth in 1966 following independence, and the United Nations on 9 December, nine days after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 15 February 1967 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bridgetown has since served the United States as a base for its diplomatic relations with the wider Caribbean region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Barbados,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bb.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Barbados,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/barbados. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Barbados,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Barbados,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/barbados. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Barbados and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/barbados_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Bathsheba%2C_Barbados_08.jpg/1920px-Bathsheba%2C_Barbados_08.jpg","title":"Bathsheba, Barbados, Barbados","desc":"Bathsheba, Barbados.","credit":"Postdlf, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bathsheba,_Barbados_08.jpg","alt":"A view of Bathsheba, Barbados."}},
{"id":"gw-182","num":182,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"French Polynesia","answerText":"French Polynesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sovereignty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Re-inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Fr. Polynesia"},"facts":[["Capital","Papeete"],["Population","282k (2025)"],["Largest city","Faʼaʼā"],["Area","3,687 km² (1,424 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","french polynesia"],"abstract":"<b>French Polynesia</b> is a group of 118 islands in five archipelagos scattered across 2.5 million square kilometres of the South Pacific, administered by France as a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 3,687 square kilometres and its population as 282,000 in 2025, where the Secretariat’s working paper gives a land area of about 3,500 square kilometres and 279,000 for 2022 and the World Bank 281,807 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 5.5 million square kilometres, the capital is Papeete with 136,000 people, and 61.9 per cent of the population lives in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 43.1 per cent of the land, income per head was 22,759 US dollars in 2023, and the population is about 65 per cent Polynesian, 16 per cent of mixed descent, 12 per cent white and 5 per cent of Chinese origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people arrived in successive waves of migration between the 4th century and the end of the 14th; Magellan reached the islands in 1521 and European settlement began after Captain Wallis arrived in 1767.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From the early 19th century the Pomare dynasty extended its influence over Tahiti and the Tuamotu and Leeward Islands, and signed a protectorate treaty with France in 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1880 King Pomare V granted France sovereignty over the islands that were dependencies of the Tahitian crown, creating the French Establishments in Oceania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These became an overseas territory when the French Union was established in 1946, have been called French Polynesia since 1957, and were reclassified as an overseas collectivity by a constitutional amendment in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people reaffirmed their wish to remain a territory of France in a referendum held in 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly put the territory back on the list of non-self-governing territories by resolution 67/265, adopted without a vote at its 82nd plenary meeting on 17 May 2013, on a draft moved by Nauru, Solomon Islands and Tuvalu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “French Polynesia: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/7, 8 January 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4073636. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “French Polynesia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pf.html. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Self-Determination of French Polynesia,” A/RES/67/265, 17 May 2013, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/755481. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Self-Determination of French Polynesia: Revised Draft Resolution,” A/67/L.56/Rev.1, 1 March 2013, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/744942. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: French Polynesia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PYF/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Bora_Bora_-_Mt_Otemanu.jpg/1920px-Bora_Bora_-_Mt_Otemanu.jpg","title":"Bora Bora, French Polynesia","desc":"Bora Bora and Mount Otemanu.","credit":"rachel_thecat, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bora_Bora_-_Mt_Otemanu.jpg","alt":"A green volcanic peak rising from a turquoise lagoon."}},
{"id":"gw-183","num":183,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"São Tomé and Príncipe","answerText":"São Tomé and Príncipe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accession talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 26 May 2005</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"São Tomé and Principe"},"facts":[["Capital","São Tomé"],["Population","240k (2025)"],["Largest city","São Tomé"],["Area","964 km² (372 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","sao tome and principe"],"abstract":"<b>São Tomé and Príncipe</b> is a two-island state in the Gulf of Guinea, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 964 square kilometres and its population as 240,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 960 square kilometres and 235,536 people for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is 250 people to the square kilometre, 73.6 per cent of them living in towns and 80,100 in the capital, São Tomé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its forest is shrinking, from 57.3 per cent of the land in 2015 to 54.1 in 2020 and 52.8 by 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture is 13.4 per cent of value added and industry 13.7, leaving services at 72.9, income per head was 2,963 US dollars in 2023 against 1,589 in 2015, and the currency is the dobra, exchanged at 22 to the US dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is very small, with 15,000 visitors in 2021 against 26,000 in 2015, and the country exported goods worth an estimated 3 million US dollars in 2024, while its population grows at 2 per cent a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised the Democratic Republic of São Tomé and Príncipe on 12 July 1975, when President Gerald R. Ford sent a letter to that effect to its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 16 September 1975, two months after that recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 10 October 1975 by an exchange of diplomatic notes, and the American guide records no embassy, no consulate and no resident ambassador at any point since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is not a member of the World Trade Organization: its accession working party was established on 26 May 2005 and the accession is still in progress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sao Tome and Principe,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/st.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sao Tome and Principe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/STP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sao Tome and Principe,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sao-tome-and-principe. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accession status: Sao Tome and Principe,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_sao_tome_principe_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Sao Tome and Principe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/STP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Pico_C%C3%A3o_Grande.jpg/1920px-Pico_C%C3%A3o_Grande.jpg","title":"Pico Cão Grande, São Tomé and Príncipe","desc":"Pico Cão Grande.","credit":"Philippe Bourachot, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pico_C%C3%A3o_Grande.jpg","alt":"A view of Pico Cão Grande."}},
{"id":"gw-184","num":184,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Samoa","answerText":"Samoa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Samoa"},"facts":[["Capital","Apia"],["Population","219k (2025)"],["Largest city","Apia"],["Area","2,842 km² (1,097 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","samoa"],"abstract":"<b>Samoa</b> is an archipelago in the central South Pacific about halfway between New Zealand and Hawaii, of nine islands, four of them inhabited, the largest being Savai’i and Upolu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 2,842 square kilometres and its population as 219,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 2,800 and 198,400 for 2022 and the World Bank 218,019 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only 18.1 per cent of its people live in towns, 36,100 of them in the capital, Apia, and forest covers 57.8 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Agriculture is 12.5 per cent of value added and income per head was 4,765 US dollars in 2023, while visitors fell from 128,000 in 2015 to 23,000 in 2020 and had recovered only to 49,000 by 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American merchants called at the islands in the first half of the 19th century, and a commercial agent was posted at Apia as early as 1844.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The first American consul, Jonathan S. Jenkins, took up the post at Apia on 17 May 1856, and that office closed on 23 September 1927.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Western Samoa had been a trust territory administered by New Zealand, and the United States recognised its independence on 1 January 1962, when Senator Oren Long delivered a congratulatory message from President John F. Kennedy to its two heads of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 14 July 1971, when Ambassador Kenneth Franzheim II presented his credentials at Apia; every American ambassador since has been accredited to New Zealand as well and resident at Wellington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An embassy was established at Apia on 15 November 1988 and has been led by a chargé d’affaires ever since, the ambassador having never moved there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country changed its official name to the Independent State of Samoa in 1997, joined the Commonwealth in 1970 after the New Zealand administration ended, hosted its heads of government meeting in 2024, and joined the World Trade Organization on 10 May 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Samoa,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/samoa. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Samoa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ws.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/WSM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Samoa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/samoa. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Samoa and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/samoa_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lalomanu_Beach_-_Samoa.jpg/1920px-Lalomanu_Beach_-_Samoa.jpg","title":"Lalomanu, Samoa","desc":"Lalomanu.","credit":"Neil, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lalomanu_Beach_-_Samoa.jpg","alt":"A view of Lalomanu."}},
{"id":"gw-185","num":185,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Lucia","answerText":"Saint Lucia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Associated state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 February 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saint Lucia"},"facts":[["Capital","Castries"],["Population","180k (2025)"],["Largest city","Castries"],["Area","616 km² (238 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","saint lucia"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Lucia</b> is a volcanic island in the eastern Caribbean between Martinique to the north and Saint Vincent to the south-west, and the United Nations and the Commonwealth both give its surface area as 616 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its population is 180,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 183,630 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s and 179,744 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, at 292 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes a central ridge of mountains, many rivers, boiling sulphur springs and sandy beaches; forest covers 34 per cent of the land and only 18.8 per cent of the people live in towns, 22,300 of them in the capital, Castries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 13,555 US dollars in 2023, agriculture is 1.3 per cent of value added, and 356,000 visitors arrived in 2022, twice the island’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British colonial rule was established in 1815, and the island took part in the West Indies Federation from 1958 until its collapse in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> With the other Windward Islands it became a state associated with the United Kingdom in 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A United States special representative was appointed to it and four neighbouring territories on 1 September 1977, concurrently with his posts as ambassador to Barbados and to Grenada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saint Lucia achieved full independence on 22 February 1979, and the United States, which had informally recognised it as an associated state, did not recognise its independence formally until it appointed Sally Angela Shelton as ambassador on 17 May 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She presented her credentials on 11 June 1979, and every American ambassador since has been resident in Barbados, the United States keeping no official presence on the island itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saint Lucia joined the Commonwealth in 1979 following independence and the United Nations on 18 September that year, acceded to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 13 April 1993 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Lucia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lc.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Saint Lucia,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/saint-lucia. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Lucia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LCA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Lucia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-lucia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Saint Lucia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/saint_lucia_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Pitons-aerial.jpg","title":"Gros Piton, Saint Lucia","desc":"Gros Piton.","credit":"Caribbeandy, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pitons-aerial.jpg","alt":"A view of Gros Piton."}},
{"id":"gw-186","num":186,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guam","answerText":"Guam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Naval rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899 – 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Organic Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1950</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guam"},"facts":[["Capital","Hagåtña"],["Population","169k (2025)"],["Largest city","Dededo"],["Area","541 km² (209 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","guam"],"abstract":"<b>Guam</b> is the southernmost and largest of the Mariana Islands, about 2,200 kilometres (1,370 miles) south of Tokyo, and a non-self-governing territory administered by the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 541 square kilometres and its population as 169,000 in 2025, where the working paper gives a land area of 544 and the 2020 census figure of 153,836, and the World Bank 540 and 167,777 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island is two geological halves of about equal size, a coralline limestone plateau in the north and mountains in the south, with Apra Harbor among the largest deep-water ports in the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its exclusive economic zone covers 214,059 square kilometres, the capital is Hagåtña, English and CHamoru are its languages, and income per head was 35,904 US dollars in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 32.8 per cent CHamoru, 29.1 per cent Filipino and 13.2 per cent other Pacific islanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The island was inhabited by the CHamoru, a people of Malayo-Polynesian descent, when missionaries from Spain arrived in the late 17th century, and the United States Navy administered it from 1899 to 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1950 Congress passed the Organic Act of Guam, which established institutions of local government and contains a bill of rights, and since then the territory has been under the general supervision of the Secretary of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guam is an unincorporated territory, so not all provisions of the Constitution apply to it, and because only states choose presidential electors its residents cannot vote for the President while living there, although people born on Guam are United States citizens and may vote once resident in a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has a 15-member legislature, a governor and a delegate to Congress, and its principal sources of revenue are tourism and the United States military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly first addressed the transmission of information on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946, and the United Nations still counts Guam separately from the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Guam: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/9, 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4080736. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Guam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gu.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Guam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Two_Lovers_Point_from_Tumon_Bay.JPG/1920px-Two_Lovers_Point_from_Tumon_Bay.JPG","title":"Two Lovers Point, Guam","desc":"Two Lovers Point.","credit":"Abasaa, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_Lovers_Point_from_Tumon_Bay.JPG","alt":"A view of Two Lovers Point."}},
{"id":"gw-187","num":187,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Curaçao","answerText":"Curaçao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomous</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Curaçao"},"facts":[["Capital","Willemstad"],["Population","186k (2025)"],["Largest city","Willemstad"],["Area","444 km² (171 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","curacao"],"abstract":"<b>Curaçao</b> is an island in the southern Caribbean and one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, and the United Nations and the World Bank both give its surface area as 444 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their population figures diverge sharply, the United Nations giving 186,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 155,967 for 2024, a difference of about a fifth that neither source explains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The capital is Willemstad, the currency is the Netherlands Antillean guilder, exchanged at 1.8 to the US dollar in 2024, and the island holds 437 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts Curaçao separately from the Netherlands for statistical purposes, and notes that its figure for the capital’s population is really the island’s total excluding some neighbourhoods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly considered a communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam at its 557th plenary meeting on 15 December 1955, adopting resolution 945 by 21 votes to 10 with 33 abstentions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 10 October 2010 the Netherlands Antilles were dissolved: Curaçao and Sint Maarten became autonomous countries, while Bonaire, Sint Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Curaçao is responsible for matters such as good governance, education and its own judicial system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kingdom as a whole has overarching responsibility for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, and agreements made on those matters apply to the entire Kingdom and bind all four countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In other areas, among them healthcare, tourism and employment, each of the four countries determines its own policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Dutch ministries of foreign affairs and defence, and the Dutch embassies and consulates around the world, serve all four countries, and the Netherlands must take account of the Caribbean part’s interests when it makes foreign policy for the Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Curaçao,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Curacao,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUW/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Curacao,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam,” A/RES/945, 15 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/208173. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg/1920px-Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg","title":"Willemstad, Curaçao","desc":"Willemstad.","credit":"Coolcaesar, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Handelskade_in_Willemstad.jpg","alt":"A view of Willemstad."}},
{"id":"gw-188","num":188,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kiribati","answerText":"Kiribati","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US agent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 May 1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 September 1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kiribati"},"facts":[["Capital","Tarawa"],["Population","136k (2025)"],["Largest city","Tarawa"],["Area","811 km² (313 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","kiribati"],"abstract":"<b>Kiribati</b> is a country of 33 islands in the central Pacific, only 20 of them inhabited, most of them very low-lying atolls, and it holds the South Pacific’s largest marine reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth and the World Bank give its land area as 811 and 810 square kilometres, where the United Nations gives 726 and says in a footnote that this is land area only, excluding 84 square kilometres of uninhabited islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That footnote is the whole of the difference, since 726 and 84 make 810.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its population is 136,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 134,518 in 2024 by the World Bank’s and 119,400 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s, and it is governed from Tarawa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States appointed a commercial agent, Adolph Rick, to Butaritari in the Gilbert Islands on 25 May 1888, to protect American trade in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> With the Ellice Islands the Gilberts became a British protectorate in 1892 and the whole group a British colony in 1916; Japan occupied several of the islands during the Second World War, after which they reverted to their former colonial status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Britain expanded self-government during the 1960s, the Gilbert and Ellice Islands separated in 1975, and Kiribati became independent on 12 July 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised the republic on 20 September 1979, when the two countries signed a Treaty of Friendship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They agreed to establish diplomatic relations on 12 August 1980, and William Bodde Jr. was appointed Envoy Extraordinary and Minister Plenipotentiary on 26 September 1980, stationed at Suva in Fiji because there were no diplomatic facilities on Kiribati.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in 1979 following independence but did not join the United Nations until 14 September 1999, twenty years after becoming independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Kiribati,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kiribati. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Kiribati,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KIR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Kiribati,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ki.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kiribati,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kiribati. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kiribati,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KIR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Line5304_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/1920px-Line5304_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg","title":"Tarawa, Kiribati","desc":"Tarawa.","credit":"LCDR Eric Johnson, NOAA Corps., Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Line5304_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg","alt":"A view of Tarawa."}},
{"id":"gw-189","num":189,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Seychelles","answerText":"Seychelles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 1996</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Seychelles"},"facts":[["Capital","Victoria"],["Population","133k (2025)"],["Largest city","Victoria"],["Area","457 km² (176 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","africa","seychelles"],"abstract":"<b>Seychelles</b> is an island country in the western Indian Ocean, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 457 square kilometres, where the Commonwealth gives 452 and the World Bank 460.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its population is 133,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count and 121,354 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, whose series jumps from 99,258 in 2021 to 119,878 in 2022, a break rather than a year’s growth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth describes over 100 islands in two groups, an inner group of about 40 that are rocky and mountainous and an outer group that is low-lying and coralline, with Kenya about 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles) west and Madagascar about 1,100 kilometres (700 miles) south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 73.3 per cent of the land, 57.1 per cent of the people live in towns, and 28,100 of them in the capital, Victoria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 16,735 US dollars in 2023, and 332,000 visitors arrived in 2022, two and a half times the country’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Seychelles attained independence from British sovereignty on 29 June 1976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it that same day, when representatives of the two states signed an agreement at Victoria relating to the establishment, operation and maintenance of a tracking and telemetry facility on the island of Mahé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 1 July 1976, when the American consulate at Victoria was raised to embassy status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That embassy closed on 30 August 1996 and relations were conducted through the American embassy in Mauritius for 27 years, until it re-opened on 1 July 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 21 September 1976 and the Commonwealth in 1976 following independence, and became a member of the World Trade Organization on 26 April 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Seychelles,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sc.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Seychelles,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/seychelles. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Seychelles,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Seychelles,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/seychelles. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Seychelles and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/seychelles_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Seychelles,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/Valle%C3%A9_de_mai_at_Praslin.jpg","title":"the Vallée de Mai, the Seychelles","desc":"The Vallée de Mai on Praslin.","credit":"Brocken Inaglory, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valle%C3%A9_de_mai_at_Praslin.jpg","alt":"A dense forest of coco de mer palms."}},
{"id":"gw-190","num":190,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Grenada","answerText":"Grenada","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 November 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 February 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Grenada"},"facts":[["Capital","Saint George’s"],["Population","117k (2025)"],["Largest city","Saint George’s"],["Area","345 km² (133 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","grenada"],"abstract":"<b>Grenada</b> is a volcanic island country in the eastern Caribbean about 160 kilometres (100 miles) north of Venezuela, with a ridge of mountains running north to south, steep valleys and large areas of tropical rainforest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 345 square kilometres and its population as 117,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 344 and 112,519 for 2022 and the World Bank 117,207 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 52.1 per cent of the land, only 36.4 per cent of the people live in towns, and income per head was 11,246 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of the southern Grenadines are a dependency of Grenada, and 149,000 visitors arrived in 2022 against 54,000 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the capital’s population as 39,300, but its own footnote says the figure refers to Saint George Parish rather than to the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Grenada gained independence from the United Kingdom on 7 February 1974, and the United States recognised it on the occasion of that independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 29 November 1974, when the Grenadian ambassador, Marie J. McIntyre, presented her credentials to President Gerald R. Ford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An American diplomatic mission followed on 25 February 1975, when Ambassador Theodore R. Britton presented his credentials at Saint George’s; he was also ambassador to Barbados and resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States maintained relations through the joint American and Organization of Eastern Caribbean States military invasion of the island in 1983, which followed the assassination of Prime Minister Maurice Bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American embassy at Saint George’s was established on 2 February 1984 and is staffed by a chargé d’affaires who reports to the ambassador resident in Bridgetown, and Grenada joined the United Nations on 17 September 1974 and the World Trade Organization on 22 February 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Grenada,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/grenada. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Grenada,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gd.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Grenada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Grenada,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/grenada. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Grenada and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/grenada_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/St_Georges_Grenada_Fort_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"St. George's, Grenada, Grenada","desc":"St. George's, Grenada.","credit":"cwi.aida, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Georges_Grenada_Fort_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A view of St. George's, Grenada."}},
{"id":"gw-191","num":191,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Micronesia","answerText":"Micronesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1989</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 December 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Micronesia"},"facts":[["Capital","Palikir"],["Population","114k (2025)"],["Largest city","Weno"],["Area","702 km² (271 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","micronesia"],"abstract":"<b>Micronesia</b>, formally the Federated States of Micronesia, is a country in the western Pacific, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 702 square kilometres and its population as 114,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 700 and 113,160 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 92.1 per cent of its land, only 22.8 per cent of its people live in towns, and the capital, Palikir, holds about 7,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 4,084 US dollars in 2023 against 2,911 in 2015, and the United States dollar is its currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Visitors numbered 31,000 in 2015 and 18,000 in 2019, counted as arrivals in the states of Kosrae, Chuuk, Pohnpei and Yap and excluding the country’s own citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the Second World War the islands formed part of the United Nations Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, administered by the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised Micronesian independence on 3 November 1986, when President Ronald Reagan noted in Proclamation 5564 that it had fulfilled its obligations under the terms of the trusteeship agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From that day the Federated States of Micronesia were no longer under American trusteeship, and a Compact of Free Association agreed between the two countries entered into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An Office of the United States Representative opened the same day, and Michael G. Wygant presented his credentials on 2 October 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That office was upgraded to embassy status on 20 September 1989, and the first American ambassador, Aurelia Erskine Brazeal, presented her credentials on 18 September 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Security Council did not determine that the trusteeship had terminated until 22 December 1990, when it found by 14 votes to 1 that the objectives of the agreement had been fully attained for Micronesia, the Marshall Islands and the Northern Mariana Islands, whose peoples had approved their new status in plebiscites; the country joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Micronesia (Federated States of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Micronesia, Fed. Sts.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FSM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Micronesia, Fed. Sts.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FSM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Micronesia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/micronesia. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 683, adopted at its 2972nd meeting, on 22 December 1990,” S/RES/683, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/103696. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Nan_madol.jpg","title":"Nan Madol, Micronesia","desc":"Nan Madol.","credit":"NOAA, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nan_madol.jpg","alt":"A view of Nan Madol."}},
{"id":"gw-192","num":192,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Aruba","answerText":"Aruba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Aruba"},"facts":[["Capital","Oranjestad"],["Population","108k (2025)"],["Largest city","Oranjestad"],["Area","180 km² (69 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","aruba"],"abstract":"<b>Aruba</b> is an island in the southern Caribbean and one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, and the United Nations and the World Bank agree on its size: 180 square kilometres and about 108,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is 601 people to the square kilometre, its capital is Oranjestad with 29,900, and its currency is the Aruban florin, exchanged at 1.8 to the US dollar in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations counts Aruba separately from the Netherlands for statistical purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten each have their own government, headed by a Governor who represents the King, where in the Netherlands the head of government is the King himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Governor is appointed by the King for six years and may be reappointed once, so may serve at most 12 years; he has no ministerial responsibility and takes no part in day-to-day government, and he appoints an Advisory Council of five members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Each of the three also has a Minister Plenipotentiary in The Hague, representing its government in the Netherlands and in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Together those ministers and the ministers of the Dutch government make up the Council of Ministers for the Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Netherlands maintains a single Representation across the three countries, with its headquarters at Willemstad in Curaçao and an office at Oranjestad, which advises the Dutch Minister of the Interior and Kingdom Relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Kingdom as a whole answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, while each country decides its own policy on matters such as healthcare, tourism and employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The General Assembly considered a communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam at its 557th plenary meeting on 15 December 1955, adopting resolution 945 by 21 votes to 10 with 33 abstentions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Aruba,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/aw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Aruba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ABW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Aruba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ABW/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Governance of Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/governance-of-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Representation of the Netherlands in Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/representation-of-the-netherlands-in-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Communication from the Government of the Netherlands concerning the Netherlands Antilles and Surinam,” A/RES/945, 15 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/208173. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Arikok_National_Park.jpg/1920px-Arikok_National_Park.jpg","title":"Arikok National Park, Aruba","desc":"Arikok National Park.","credit":"Misty Johnson, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arikok_National_Park.jpg","alt":"A view of Arikok National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-193","num":193,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"United States Virgin Islands","answerText":"United States Virgin Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1754 – 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Transferred</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1917</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"U.S. Virgin Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Charlotte Amalie"],["Population","105k (2023)"],["Largest city","Charlotte Amalie"],["Area","352 km² (136 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","virgin islands"],"abstract":"The <b>United States Virgin Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter and an unincorporated, organized territory of the United States, administered by the Office of Insular Affairs of the Department of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lie in the eastern Caribbean, 1,730 kilometres (1,075 miles) southeast of the southeastern tip of the contiguous United States and 97 kilometres (60 miles) east of Puerto Rico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are four main islands, Saint Croix, Saint John, Saint Thomas and Water Island, and the capital, Charlotte Amalie, is on Saint Thomas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population figures diverge widely: the Secretariat’s working paper gives 104,917 for 2023 and the World Bank 104,377 for 2024, where the United Nations statistics division gives 84,000 for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land area is 352 square kilometres by the working paper, 347 by the statistics division and 350 by the World Bank, and the exclusive economic zone covers 33,744 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> English or English creole is spoken by 74.7 per cent of the people, Spanish or Spanish creole by 16.78 per cent and French or French creole by 6.57 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tourism is the primary economic activity, accounting for 80 per cent of output and of employment, and the manufacturing sector is petroleum refining, rum distilling, textiles, electronics, pharmaceuticals and watch assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 36,983 US dollars in 2022 and unemployment 3.7 per cent in October 2024, and the territory has a unicameral legislature of 15 senators, a governor and a delegate to the United States Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands belonged to Denmark from 1754 to 1917, as the Danish West Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 31 March 1917 the Government of Denmark transferred ownership to the United States, and they have been an organized, unincorporated territory ever since; the General Assembly first addressed the transmission of information on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “United States Virgin Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/16, 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4080346. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Virgin Islands, U.S.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VIR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “United States Virgin Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vi.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Virgin Islands, U.S.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VIR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/StJohnTrunkBay.jpg","title":"Trunk Bay, the United States Virgin Islands","desc":"Trunk Bay.","credit":"Ben Whitney, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StJohnTrunkBay.jpg","alt":"A view of Trunk Bay."}},
{"id":"gw-194","num":194,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tonga","answerText":"Tonga","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">United</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1845</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1886</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 June 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1972</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tonga"},"facts":[["Capital","Nuku’alofa"],["Population","104k (2025)"],["Largest city","Nuku’alofa"],["Area","747 km² (288 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","tonga"],"abstract":"<b>Tonga</b> is two roughly parallel chains of around 170 islands in the southwestern Pacific, only 36 of them inhabited, the western ones volcanic and well above sea level and the eastern ones low coral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 747 square kilometres and its population as 104,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 748 and 105,690 for 2022 and the World Bank 104,175 for 2024, down from 106,095 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forest covers 12.4 per cent of the land, only 23.1 per cent of the people live in towns, and 22,900 of them are in the capital, Nuku’alofa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head was 4,859 US dollars in 2023 against 3,805 in 2015, and the currency is the pa’anga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands were united in 1845 under King George Tupou I and became a constitutional monarchy in 1875.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The United States and the King of Tonga signed a Treaty of Amity, Commerce and Navigation at Nuku’alofa on 2 October 1886, which is the act that recognised the kingdom, and it entered into force in 1888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A consul-general was appointed to Nuku’alofa in 1897 but was resident at Apia in Samoa, and the post closed in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Tonga became a protectorate of Great Britain in 1900 the British Foreign Office took control of all its external affairs, the United States ceased to appoint consuls-general, and on 28 July 1920 the Foreign Office terminated the 1886 treaty on the King’s behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tonga became an independent and sovereign nation within the British Commonwealth on 4 June 1970, joining the Commonwealth that year on withdrawing from the protectorate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised it a second time on 4 October 1972 by appointing Kenneth Franzheim II as ambassador; since 1979 its ambassador to Fiji has also been accredited to Tonga and resident at Suva, an embassy at Nuku’alofa opened in 2023, and the country joined the World Trade Organization on 27 July 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Tonga,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/tonga. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Tonga,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/to.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tonga,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TON/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tonga,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tonga. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tonga and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tonga_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Royal_Palace%2C_Nuku%27alofa%2C_Nov_18.jpg/1920px-Royal_Palace%2C_Nuku%27alofa%2C_Nov_18.jpg","title":"Nukuʻalofa, Tonga","desc":"Nukuʻalofa.","credit":"Uhooep, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Palace,_Nuku%27alofa,_Nov_18.jpg","alt":"A view of Nukuʻalofa."}},
{"id":"gw-196","num":196,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","answerText":"Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded to Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1763</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 December 1981</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Vin. and Gren."},"facts":[["Capital","Kingstown"],["Population","100k (2025)"],["Largest city","Kingstown"],["Area","389 km² (150 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","saint vincent"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</b> lies in the eastern Caribbean and consists of the island of Saint Vincent and the northern Grenadines, a group of 32 smaller islands and cays to the south; the southern Grenadines belong to Grenada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives its surface area as 389 square kilometres and its population as 100,000 in 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 390 and 110,900 for 2022 and the World Bank 100,616 for 2024, down from 106,960 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country is volcanic and mountainous with one volcano still active, and 73.2 per cent of its land is forested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Just over half its people live in towns, 26,600 of them in the capital, Kingstown, and income per head was 10,767 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Saint Vincent was ceded to Great Britain in 1763, was briefly under French rule from 1779 until 1796, and then returned to British rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It passed through a variety of stages as a colony and Commonwealth state and, after a referendum in 1979, became the last of the Windward Islands to reach independence, on 27 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A United States special representative had been appointed to it and four neighbouring territories on 1 September 1977, concurrently with his posts as ambassador to Barbados and to Grenada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States, which had informally recognised Saint Vincent as a federated state in the British Commonwealth, did not recognise its independence formally until it appointed Milan D. Bish as ambassador on 23 November 1981, more than two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Relations were established on 8 December 1981, when the Vincentian ambassador presented his credentials and Bish presented his at Bridgetown the same day, being concurrently ambassador to Antigua and Barbuda, Barbados, Dominica and Saint Lucia and special representative to Saint Christopher and Nevis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every American ambassador to the country has been resident at Bridgetown, and it joined the United Nations on 16 September 1980, the Commonwealth in 1979 following independence, and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “St Vincent and the Grenadines,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/st-vincent-and-grenadines. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Vincent and the Grenadines,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vc.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Vincent and the Grenadines,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VCT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Vincent and the Grenadines,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-vincent-grenadines. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Saint Vincent and the Grenadines and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/saint_vincent_grenadines_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Soufriere.jpg","title":"La Soufrière (volcano), Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","desc":"La Soufrière (volcano).","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soufriere.jpg","alt":"A view of La Soufrière (volcano)."}},
{"id":"gw-197","num":197,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Antigua and Barbuda","answerText":"Antigua and Barbuda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Antigua and Barb."},"facts":[["Capital","Saint John’s"],["Population","94.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Saint John’s"],["Area","442 km² (171 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","antigua and barbuda"],"abstract":"<b>Antigua and Barbuda</b> is a dry tropical country of three islands in the eastern Caribbean: Antigua, with secluded bays, sandy beaches, coral reefs and volcanic rocks rising to about 400 metres (1,300 feet) at Mount Obama; flat, coral Barbuda with its large lagoon; and tiny, rocky, uninhabited Redonda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth gives their areas as 280, 161 and 1.6 square kilometres, where the United Nations gives 442 for the whole and the World Bank 440.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is 94,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 97,928 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s and 93,772 in 2024 by the World Bank’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only a quarter of its people live in towns, 20,800 of them in the capital, Saint John’s, forest covers 19.2 per cent of the land, and income per head was 21,787 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Visitors numbered 265,000 in 2022 against 125,000 in 2020, nearly three times the country’s own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Antigua and Barbuda became an independent state within the Commonwealth of Nations on 1 November 1981, with Queen Elizabeth as its first Queen and Vere Cornwall Bird as its first prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised it as an independent state the same day, by raising its consulate general at Saint John’s to embassy status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on that date, with Paul Byrnes as principal officer, and Ambassador Milan D. Bish presented his credentials on 8 January 1982 while resident at Bridgetown in Barbados.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American embassy at Saint John’s closed on 30 June 1994, and every ambassador since has remained resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 11 November 1981 and the Commonwealth in 1981 following independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Antigua and Barbuda,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/antigua-and-barbuda. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Antigua and Barbuda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ag.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Antigua and Barbuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ATG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Antigua and Barbuda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/antigua-barbuda. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Antigua and Barbuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ATG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Antigua_Shirley%27s_Heights_English_Harbour.jpg/1920px-Antigua_Shirley%27s_Heights_English_Harbour.jpg","title":"English Harbour, Antigua and Barbuda","desc":"English Harbour.","credit":"Dr. Thomas Liptak, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antigua_Shirley%27s_Heights_English_Harbour.jpg","alt":"A view of English Harbour."}},
{"id":"gw-198","num":198,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Isle of Man","answerText":"Isle of Man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2015</span><span class=\"dt-v\">83,593 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2024</span><span class=\"dt-v\">84,160 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN estimate 2025</span><span class=\"dt-v\">84,000 people</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Isle of Man"},"facts":[["Capital","Douglas"],["Population","84.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Douglas"],["Area","572 km² (221 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","isle of man"],"abstract":"The <b>Isle of Man</b> is one of the three Crown Dependencies of the United Kingdom, with Jersey and Guernsey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Crown Dependencies are not part of the United Kingdom but are self-governing dependencies of the Crown, which means they have their own directly elected legislative assemblies, their own administrative, financial and legal systems and their own courts of law, though most rules and regulations are the same as the United Kingdom’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Kingdom’s constitutional relationship with them is managed by its Ministry of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the island’s surface area as 572 square kilometres and its population as 84,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 570 square kilometres and 84,160 people for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The capital is Douglas, holding 27,200; 52.7 per cent of the people live in towns, forest covers 6.1 per cent of the land, and the currency is the pound sterling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That population has barely moved in a decade, the World Bank giving 83,593 for 2015 against 84,160 for 2024, a rise of about half a per cent in nine years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations counts the island separately from the United Kingdom for statistical purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The islands are collectively referred to as “the islands” in the Immigration Act 1971 and the British Nationality Act 1981, and the Channel Islands among them comprise the Bailiwick of Jersey and the Bailiwick of Guernsey, which includes Herm, Sark and Alderney.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Common Travel Area is an administrative arrangement between the United Kingdom, Ireland and the three Crown Dependencies, implemented in United Kingdom domestic law by statute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Someone living on the island who applies there holds a Crown Dependency variant British passport, while one who applies directly to His Majesty’s Passport Office may hold a non-variant one, and dual nationality is recognised on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["His Majesty’s Passport Office, “Isle of Man: Knowledge Base Profile,” Home Office, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/isle-of-man-knowledge-base-profile/isle-of-man-knowledge-base-profile. [Open access]","Ministry of Justice, “Crown Dependencies: Jersey, Guernsey and the Isle of Man,” https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/crown-dependencies-jersey-guernsey-and-the-isle-of-man. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Isle of Man,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/im.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Isle of Man,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IMN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Home Office, “Common Travel Area,” https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/common-travel-area/common-travel-area-accessible. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Isle of Man,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IMN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Laxey_Wheel_-_panoramio_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Laxey_Wheel_-_panoramio_%282%29.jpg","title":"Laxey Wheel, the Isle of Man","desc":"Laxey Wheel.","credit":"Lobster1, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laxey_Wheel_-_panoramio_(2).jpg","alt":"A view of Laxey Wheel."}},
{"id":"gw-199","num":199,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Andorra","answerText":"Andorra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 March 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Andorra"},"facts":[["Capital","Andorra la Vella"],["Population","83.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Andorra la Vella"],["Area","468 km² (181 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","andorra"],"abstract":"<b>Andorra</b> is a landlocked principality in the eastern Pyrenees, and the United Nations gives its surface area as 468 square kilometres and its population as 83,000 in 2025, where the World Bank gives 470 and 81,938 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That population has grown by about a seventh in a decade, from 72,174 in 2015; 88 per cent of it lives in towns, 22,600 of them in the capital, Andorra la Vella, and 34 per cent of the land is forested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 46,812 US dollars in 2023 against 38,655 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a co-principality, with the Bishop of Urgell in Spain as one co-prince and the President of France as the other, an arrangement that dates back centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That, together with its position, is why Andorra was traditionally isolated from European and international affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The American guide records no act of recognition at all: Andorra has considered itself independent since medieval times and so was already independent when the United States declared its own independence in 1776.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Unlike many other independent states of the late 18th century, Andorra never signed a bilateral treaty with the United States, which would have legally constituted recognition of the new republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ratified a modern constitution on 14 March 1993, providing for its independence and sovereignty as a representative democracy, and the General Assembly admitted it to membership without a vote at its 108th meeting on 28 July that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 21 February 1995 by an exchange of letters, the Andorran government asking for full relations and Secretary of State Warren Christopher reciprocating by appointing the American consul general in Barcelona to represent the United States to Andorra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every American ambassador to Andorra has also been accredited to Spain and resident in Madrid, while officials of the consulate general in Barcelona conduct day-to-day relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Andorra,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ad.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Andorra,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AND/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Andorra,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AND/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Andorra,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/andorra. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Principality of Andorra to Membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/47/232, 28 July 1993, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/174476. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Andorra_la_Vella_-_view2.jpg/1920px-Andorra_la_Vella_-_view2.jpg","title":"Andorra la Vella, Andorra","desc":"Andorra la Vella.","credit":"Tiia Monto, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andorra_la_Vella_-_view2.jpg","alt":"A view of Andorra la Vella."}},
{"id":"gw-200","num":200,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cayman Islands","answerText":"Cayman Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1661 – 1671</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Jamaica</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1863</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 November 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cayman Is."},"facts":[["Capital","George Town"],["Population","76.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","George Town"],["Area","264 km² (102 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","cayman islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Cayman Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and comprise three islands: Grand Cayman, Cayman Brac and the adjacent Little Cayman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They lie some 290 kilometres (180 miles) west of Jamaica and about the same distance south of Cuba, with a land area of 264 square kilometres and an exclusive economic zone of 119,137 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The population figures differ: the Secretariat’s working paper gives 84,738 for 2023, of whom 46 per cent are Caymanian and 54 per cent are not, where the United Nations statistics division gives 76,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 74,457 for 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The statistics division counts the whole population as urban, forest covers 53 per cent of the land, income per head was 97,583 US dollars in 2023 against 76,379 in 2015, and life expectancy at birth is 83.1 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The islands were reportedly sighted by Christopher Columbus in the late 15th century and were first settled by the British between 1661 and 1671.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A tradition of self-government began to grow from 1734, and a legislative assembly known as the Assembly of Justices and Vestry was established in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slavery was abolished in 1834.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1863 the Cayman Islands became a dependency of Jamaica, and when Jamaica became independent 99 years later the islands remained under the British Crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The current constitution entered into force on 6 November 2009 and was amended in November 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under it the Governor, appointed by the British Crown, is responsible for external affairs, defence and internal security, and the General Assembly first addressed the transmission of information on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Cayman Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/5, 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4079488. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cayman Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ky.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cayman Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Cayman Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Seven_Mile_Beach_cayman_sun.jpg","title":"Seven Mile Beach, Grand Cayman, the Cayman Islands","desc":"Seven Mile Beach, Grand Cayman.","credit":"User2369, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seven_Mile_Beach_cayman_sun.jpg","alt":"A view of Seven Mile Beach, Grand Cayman."}},
{"id":"gw-202","num":202,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dominica","answerText":"Dominica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1978</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dominica"},"facts":[["Capital","Roseau"],["Population","66.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Roseau"],["Area","750 km² (290 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","dominica"],"abstract":"<b>Dominica</b> is a small, fertile, volcanic island in the eastern Caribbean between the French islands of Guadeloupe and Marie-Galante to the north and Martinique to the south, with a range of high forested mountains and several active volcanoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations, the Commonwealth and the World Bank all give its surface area as 750 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is 66,000 in 2025 by the United Nations count, 72,000 in 2022 by the Commonwealth’s and 66,205 in 2024 by the World Bank’s, down from 70,410 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Forest covers 63.8 per cent of the land, 70.8 per cent of the people live in towns, and 14,900 of them are in the capital, Roseau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Income per head was 9,786 US dollars in 2023 against 7,680 in 2015, agriculture supplies 18.4 per cent of value added against 16.5 in 2015, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar, exchanged at 2.7 to the US dollar in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Great Britain granted independence to the Commonwealth of Dominica on 3 November 1978, the island having been a British colony before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day, with Frank V. Ortiz Jr., its ambassador to Barbados, attending the independence celebrations as the personal representative of President Jimmy Carter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first American ambassador to Dominica, Sally Angela Shelton, presented her credentials at Bridgetown in Barbados, where she remained resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States maintains no official presence on the island, its ambassador and embassy staff being resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dominica joined the Commonwealth in 1978 following independence and the United Nations on 18 December that year, and the United States maintained relations through the political discontent, arising from long-term economic problems, that led to an interim government in 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Commonwealth Secretariat, “Dominica,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/dominica. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Dominica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Dominica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DMA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Dominica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DMA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Dominica,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/dominica. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Dominica_Boiling_Lake.jpg/1920px-Dominica_Boiling_Lake.jpg","title":"Boiling Lake, Dominica","desc":"Boiling Lake.","credit":"Bayukjdr, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominica_Boiling_Lake.jpg","alt":"A view of Boiling Lake."}},
{"id":"gw-203","num":203,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bermuda","answerText":"Bermuda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sighted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1505</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1609</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1684</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bermuda"},"facts":[["Capital","Hamilton"],["Population","65.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","St. George's"],["Area","53 km² (21 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","bermuda"],"abstract":"<b>Bermuda</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and consists of 8 major and 130 smaller islands in the western Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies about 917 kilometres (570 miles) east of the North Carolina coast, and its land area of 53.35 square kilometres sits inside an exclusive economic zone of 450,370 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population figures differ: the Secretariat’s working paper gives 63,905 for 2024, the World Bank 64,636 for the same year, and the United Nations statistics division 65,000 for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That division counts the whole population as urban and files the territory under the code 060 in the region it calls Northern America, and life expectancy at birth was 82.4 years in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2016 census recorded a population about 52 per cent black, 31 per cent white, 9 per cent of mixed race and 8 per cent other races, and the language is English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were sighted in 1505 by the Spanish explorer Juan de Bermúdez and were called ‘La Bermuda’ by 1510, but stayed uninhabited until 1609, when British settlers bound for Virginia were wrecked on a reef.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> King James I extended the charter of the Virginia Company to cover Bermuda in 1612, and government passed to the British Crown after that charter was annulled in 1684.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bermuda was among the territories on which the United Kingdom transmitted information under Article 73 e of the Charter, as the General Assembly noted on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The constitution of 1968, last revised in 2003, leaves the Governor responsible for defence, external affairs, internal security and the police, with a premier heading a cabinet drawn from a 36-member House of Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At a referendum in 1995, 73.6 per cent of those voting rejected independence on a turnout of 58.8 per cent, and income per head reached 125,842 US dollars in 2023 against 104,855 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Bermuda: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/3, 5 March 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4079355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Bermuda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bermuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BMU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Horseshoebay.Bermuda.JPG/1920px-Horseshoebay.Bermuda.JPG","title":"Horseshoe Bay, Bermuda, Bermuda","desc":"Horseshoe Bay, Bermuda.","credit":"Ekem, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horseshoebay.Bermuda.JPG","alt":"A view of Horseshoe Bay, Bermuda."}},
{"id":"gw-204","num":204,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Greenland","answerText":"Greenland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1721</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish county</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Home rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Self-government</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 June 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Greenland"},"facts":[["Capital","Nuuk"],["Population","57.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Nuuk"],["Area","2,166,086 km² (836,330 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","greenland"],"abstract":"<b>Greenland</b> is the world’s largest island and a self-governing region within the Kingdom of Denmark, with a surface area of 2,166,086 square kilometres (836,330 square miles), about 81 per cent of it under ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division puts its population density at 0.1 people per square kilometre; counting only the ice-free ground, Statistics Greenland makes it 0.3, the lowest in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is about 57,000, the World Bank giving 56,831 for 2025 where the statistics division rounds to 56,000, and 87 per cent of it is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every town and settlement stands on the coast, most of them on the south-west, and no roads run between them, so travel is by sea or by air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital, Nuuk, held 19,880 people on 1 January 2024; the island runs 2,670 kilometres (1,660 miles) north to south and has 44,087 kilometres (27,400 miles) of coastline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Greenland became a Danish colony in 1721 and a Danish county in 1953.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly ended the transmission of information on the island under Article 73 e of the Charter at its 499th plenary meeting, on 22 November 1954.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Home rule was granted in 1979 and self-government established on 21 June 2009, and the island has its own flag and issues its own stamps but has no currency of its own, the Danish krone being legal tender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered the European Economic Community with Denmark in 1973 and left in 1985, after a referendum held in 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture, with forestry and fishing, made up 18.3 per cent of gross value added in 2023, income per head was 54,374 US dollars that year, and the statistics division files the island under the code 304 in the region it calls Northern America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Statistics Greenland, <i>Greenland in Figures 2024</i>, 21st ed. (Nuuk: Statistics Greenland, 2024), 4, https://stat.gl/publ/en/GF/2024/pdf/Greenland%20in%20Figures%202024.pdf. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Greenland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gl.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Greenland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Cessation of the Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter in Respect of Greenland,” A/RES/849(IX), 22 November 1954, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/211980. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Greenland_Ilulissat-25.jpg","title":"Ilulissat Icefjord, Greenland","desc":"Ilulissat Icefjord.","credit":"Michael Haferkamp, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greenland_Ilulissat-25.jpg","alt":"A view of Ilulissat Icefjord."}},
{"id":"gw-205","num":205,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Faroe Islands","answerText":"Faroe Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2015</span><span class=\"dt-v\">48,898 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2025</span><span class=\"dt-v\">54,900 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Islands</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Faeroe Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Tórshavn"],["Population","55.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Tórshavn"],["Area","1,393 km² (538 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","faroe islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Faroe Islands</b> are an archipelago in the North Atlantic which the United Nations files under Northern Europe with the code 234 and a surface area of 1,393 square kilometres (538 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Statistics Faroe Islands lists 18 islands and measures each of them separately, from Streymoy at 374.89 square kilometres down to Lítla Dímun at 0.83, the last of them the only one with no residents at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The highest point is Slættaratindur on Eysturoy at 880 metres (2,890 feet), and the largest lake is Leitisvatn on Vágar at 3.57 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two closest counts of the population agree: Statistics Faroe Islands recorded about 55,000 through 2025 and the World Bank gives 54,900 for that year, where the statistics division rounds to 56,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown in every year the World Bank records, from 48,898 in 2015, and 14.6 per cent of it is counted as foreign-born, a figure that includes people born in Denmark and Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The capital is Tórshavn, with 20,800 people in 2018, and only 42.2 per cent of the population was counted as urban in 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Danish krone, and for statistical purposes the United Nations excludes the islands from its figures for Denmark, treating them as a separate area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> European Union law does not reach them: the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union provides at Article 355 that the Treaties ‘shall not apply to the Faeroe Islands’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth was 82.8 years for women and 78.6 for men in 2025, the fertility rate 2.2 children per woman, and forest covers 0.1 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Exports were worth 2,364 million US dollars in 2024 against imports of 1,963 million, a surplus of 401 million, and the agricultural production index, set at 100 for 2014 to 2016, stood at 108 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Faroe Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fo.html. [Open access]","Statistics Faroe Islands, “Islands, Mountains, Islets and Lakes,” https://hagstova.fo/en/environment/geography/islands-mountains-islets-og-lakes. [Open access]","Statistics Faroe Islands, “Population,” https://hagstova.fo/en/population/population/population. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Faroe Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FRO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, <i>Consolidated Version of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union</i>, art. 355, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:12016E355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/G%C3%A1sadalur_%2851256491640%29.jpg/1920px-G%C3%A1sadalur_%2851256491640%29.jpg","title":"Gásadalur, the Faroe Islands","desc":"Gásadalur.","credit":"Mike Maguire, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A1sadalur_(51256491640).jpg","alt":"A view of Gásadalur."}},
{"id":"gw-206","num":206,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Kitts and Nevis","answerText":"Saint Kitts and Nevis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1623</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Associated state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1983</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 September 1983</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Kitts and Nevis"},"facts":[["Capital","Basseterre"],["Population","47.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Basseterre"],["Area","261 km² (101 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","americas","saint kitts and nevis"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Kitts and Nevis</b> is a two-island federation in the Caribbean with its capital at Basseterre, its area given as 261 square kilometres (101 square miles) by the United Nations statistics division and 260 by the World Bank, and the Commonwealth measuring Saint Kitts at 176 and Nevis at 93.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The population is about 47,000, the statistics division and the World Bank agreeing to within a hundred people, though the Commonwealth Secretariat gives 53,000 for 2022, a figure the World Bank’s series never passes through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only 30.8 per cent of the population is counted as urban, income per head was 22,625 US dollars in 2023, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Industry made up 23 per cent of gross value added in 2023 against 1.6 per cent for agriculture and 75.4 for services, and women held 31.2 per cent of the seats in parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 76.3 years for women and 68.8 for men, and the fertility rate is 1.5 children per woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were the site of Great Britain’s first Caribbean settlement, in 1623, and passed through centuries of British and French colonial rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together with Anguilla they became a self-governing state in association with Great Britain in 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saint Christopher and Nevis declared independence on 19 September 1983 and the United States recognised it the same day, Ambassador Milan D. Bish heading the American delegation at the ceremonies, and the country joined the United Nations four days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been called Saint Kitts and Nevis since 3 August 1988, and it joined the Commonwealth in 1983 on independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States keeps no official presence in the federation, a consular agent resident in nearby Antigua assisting its citizens there, and every American ambassador to it has been resident at Bridgetown in Barbados.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Kitts and Nevis,” https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-kitts-nevis. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Kitts and Nevis,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kn.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “St Kitts and Nevis,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/st-kitts-and-nevis. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Kitts and Nevis,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KNA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: St. Kitts and Nevis,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KNA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/BrimstoneHill01.jpg/1920px-BrimstoneHill01.jpg","title":"Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park, Saint Kitts and Nevis","desc":"Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park.","credit":"Ukexpat, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrimstoneHill01.jpg","alt":"A view of Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park."}},
{"id":"gw-207","num":207,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"American Samoa","answerText":"American Samoa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of Berlin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratification Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 February 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To the Interior</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elected governor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1977</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"American Samoa"},"facts":[["Capital","Pago Pago"],["Population","46.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Tafuna"],["Area","199 km² (77 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","american samoa"],"abstract":"<b>American Samoa</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter and an unincorporated, unorganized territory of the United States, administered by the Interior Department’s Office of Insular Affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies in the South Pacific about 3,700 kilometres (2,300 miles) south-west of Hawaii, and its seven islands are Tutuila, Aunuu, the Manu’a Islands of Ofu, Olosega and Ta’u, and the atolls of Rose Island and Swains Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land area is 200 square kilometres by the working paper and the World Bank and 199 by the statistics division, and its exclusive economic zone 404,391.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population is falling: the World Bank’s series runs from 52,878 in 2015 to 46,029 in 2025, and the statistics division gives −1.6 per cent a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forest covers 85.4 per cent of it, the languages are English and Samoan, and the United Nations files the territory under Polynesia with code 016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Not all provisions of the Constitution or the laws of the United States apply there, and a person born in the territory neither of whose parents is a citizen is a United States national rather than a citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such nationals may live and work anywhere in the United States but may not vote in its federal elections, while everyone in the territory over 18 votes locally and for its delegate to the House of Representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archipelago is thought to have been settled some 3,000 years ago from South-East Asia, the Dutch reached it in 1722, and the Treaty of Berlin of 1899 apportioned its eastern islands to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deeds ceding those islands were accepted under the Ratification Act of 20 February 1929, which made American Samoans United States nationals, and Executive Order 10264 of 29 June 1951 moved the territory from the Navy to the Interior Department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The governor has been elected by popular vote since 1977, the legislature is the bicameral Fono, and tuna canning is the largest private employer, at a minimum wage of 6.36 dollars an hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “American Samoa: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/1, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4109505. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “American Samoa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/as.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: American Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ASM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: American Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ASM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Pola_Islands_Tutuila_NPS.jpg/1920px-Pola_Islands_Tutuila_NPS.jpg","title":"National Park of American Samoa, American Samoa","desc":"National Park of American Samoa.","credit":"Tavita Togia, National Park Service, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pola_Islands_Tutuila_NPS.jpg","alt":"A view of National Park of American Samoa."}},
{"id":"gw-208","num":208,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Turks and Caicos Islands","answerText":"Turks and Caicos Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1799, by Great Britain</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Separate colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Own governor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in force 15 October 2012</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turks and Caicos Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Cockburn Town"],["Population","47.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Providenciales"],["Area","948 km² (366 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","turks and caicos islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Turks and Caicos Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and comprise 40 islands and cays 145 kilometres (90 miles) north of Haiti and the Dominican Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six are permanently inhabited: Grand Turk, where the capital Cockburn Town stands, Providenciales, which holds the great majority of the population, North Caicos, Middle Caicos, South Caicos and Salt Cay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land area is 948.2 square kilometres, a figure the statistics division measures to the shoreline at low water, and the exclusive economic zone runs to 154,068 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population figures differ: the working paper gives 50,828 for 2024 where the statistics division gives 47,000 for 2025 and the World Bank 46,855, a quarter more than the 36,605 the World Bank recorded in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Income per head reached 30,349 US dollars in 2023, tourism and financial services carry the economy, and wetlands cover nearly half the surface of the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first inhabitants were the Arawak-speaking Taino, and Great Britain annexed the islands in 1799 as part of the Bahamas, attaching them later to Jamaica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They became a separate colony in 1962, when Jamaica became independent, but kept close constitutional ties with the Bahamas until a governor replaced their administrator after Bahamian independence in 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Turks and Caicos Islands Constitution Order 2011, in force on 15 October 2012, is the fifth constitution since 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under it the legislature consists of the Crown and a House of Assembly of a Speaker, fifteen elected members, four appointed members and the Attorney General, who may not vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Governor keeps responsibility for defence, external affairs, internal security including the police, public appointments and the regulation of international financial services, and the United Nations files the territory under the Caribbean with code 796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Turks and Caicos Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/15, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111022. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Turks and Caicos Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tc.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Turks and Caicos Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TCA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","The Turks and Caicos Islands Constitution Order 2011, SI 2011/1681, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2011/1681/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Turtle_Cove_Providenciales_Beach.jpg/1920px-Turtle_Cove_Providenciales_Beach.jpg","title":"Providenciales, the Turks and Caicos Islands","desc":"Providenciales.","credit":"Tim Sackton, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turtle_Cove_Providenciales_Beach.jpg","alt":"A view of Providenciales."}},
{"id":"gw-209","num":209,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Northern Mariana Islands","answerText":"Northern Mariana Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Covenant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN trusteeship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ended 22 December 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US immigration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2008</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"N. Mariana Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Garapan"],["Population","44.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Saipan"],["Area","457 km² (176 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","northern mariana islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Northern Mariana Islands</b> are a commonwealth in political union with the United States, lying in Micronesia, with a land area of 457 square kilometres (176 square miles) by the statistics division and 460 by the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their government sits at Capital Hill on Saipan, though the statistics division names Garapan as the capital, and 91.7 per cent of the population is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has fallen in every year the World Bank records, from 51,514 in 2015 to 43,541 in 2025, and the statistics division puts the annual rate at −1.6 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Half the people living there were born abroad, imports ran to 7,225 million US dollars in 2022 against exports of 2,072 million, and forest covers 53 per cent of the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 81.0 years for women and 77.4 for men, and the currency is the United States dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, which the United States administered on behalf of the United Nations from 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Covenant making them a United States territory passed into federal law in 1975, a constitution was adopted in 1977, and the first constitutional government took office in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 22 December 1990, at its 2972nd meeting and by fourteen votes to one, the Security Council determined that the Trusteeship Agreement had ceased to apply to them, to the Federated States of Micronesia and to the Marshall Islands, whose peoples had approved new status agreements in plebiscites the Trusteeship Council observed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The statistics division gave the islands a code of their own, 580, in 1991, having counted them until then within the Pacific Islands Trust Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Federal minimum wage rules reached the commonwealth in 2007 and federal immigration law in 2008, and the Department of Homeland Security took over its immigration and border controls in June 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Northern Mariana Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mp.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Northern Mariana Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Northern Mariana Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Office of Insular Affairs, US Department of the Interior, “Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands,” https://www.doi.gov/oia/islands/cnmi. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 683 (1990), Adopted by the Security Council at Its 2972nd Meeting, on 22 December 1990,” S/RES/683(1990), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/103696. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Managaha_SW_2014.JPG/1920px-Managaha_SW_2014.JPG","title":"Managaha, the Northern Mariana Islands","desc":"Managaha.","credit":"Marcus Hsu, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Managaha_SW_2014.JPG","alt":"A view of Managaha."}},
{"id":"gw-210","num":210,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sint Maarten","answerText":"Sint Maarten","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Own country</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 2010</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hurricane Irma</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 2017</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sint Maarten"},"facts":[["Capital","Philipsburg"],["Population","44.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Lower Prince's Quarter"],["Area","34 km² (13 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","sint maarten"],"abstract":"<b>Sint Maarten</b> is a country of the Kingdom of the Netherlands in the Caribbean of 34 square kilometres (13 square miles), which the United Nations files as Sint Maarten (Dutch part) under the code 534, with about 44,000 people and its capital at Philipsburg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population density is 1,291.9 people to the square kilometre, the whole of it is counted as urban, and 69.5 per cent of the people living there were born abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has grown in every year the World Bank records, from 37,471 in 2015 to 43,923 in 2025, and income per head was 39,237 US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Services make up 93.3 per cent of gross value added against 6.7 for industry, and 373,000 tourists arrived in 2022 against 505,000 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Netherlands Antillean guilder, and the statistics division excludes the island from its figures for the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 10 October 2010 Curaçao and Sint Maarten became autonomous countries, while Bonaire, Sint Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The statistics division gave Sint Maarten a code of its own that year, having counted it until then within the Netherlands Antilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Kingdom as a whole answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, while each of its four countries decides its own policy on matters such as healthcare, tourism and employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Governor appointed by the King for six years, renewable once, heads the government without ministerial responsibility, and a Minister Plenipotentiary in The Hague sits with the Dutch ministers in the Council of Ministers for the Kingdom, though Sint Maarten, unlike Aruba and Curaçao, has none in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hurricane Irma struck on 6 September 2017 and destroyed many homes and much infrastructure, and the Netherlands set aside 550 million euros for reconstruction on two conditions, an Integrity Chamber and improved border controls, with up to 470 million of it administered by the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sx.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SXM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Responsibilities of the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/responsibilities-of-the-netherlands-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Governance of Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/governance-of-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Reconstruction of St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/reconstruction-of-st-maarten. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Maho_Beach.jpg/1920px-Maho_Beach.jpg","title":"Maho Beach, Sint Maarten","desc":"Maho Beach.","credit":"Patrick Hawks, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maho_Beach.jpg","alt":"An airliner passing low over sunbathers on a beach."}},
{"id":"gw-211","num":211,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Liechtenstein","answerText":"Liechtenstein","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sovereignty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1806</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Confederation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">again in 1866</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Liechtenstein"},"facts":[["Capital","Vaduz"],["Population","40.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Schaan"],["Area","160 km² (62 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","liechtenstein"],"abstract":"<b>Liechtenstein</b> is a country of 160 square kilometres (62 square miles) — the figure the statistics division and the World Bank both give — in the region the United Nations calls Western Europe, with about 40,000 people and its capital at Vaduz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only 14.4 per cent of its population is counted as urban, and 69.4 per cent of the people living there were born abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 201,150 US dollars in 2023, industry making up 42.1 per cent of gross value added against 57.7 for services and 0.2 for agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 85.5 years for women and 82.2 for men, forest covers 41.9 per cent of the land, and 98.5 per cent of its important sites for terrestrial biodiversity are protected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Swiss franc, and the World Bank’s series has the population rising in every year from 37,494 in 2015 to 41,024 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Formerly part of the Holy Roman Empire, Liechtenstein achieved sovereignty in 1806, joined the German Confederation in 1815 and became independent again in 1866.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It yielded the conduct of its foreign affairs to Switzerland for much of the twentieth century, so its direct diplomatic connections with other states are of relatively recent origin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States executed its first treaty with Liechtenstein in 1926, when Switzerland still represented it abroad, an exchange of notes between the Swiss and American governments extending the rights of a United States–Swiss agreement to Liechtenstein’s citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began pursuing membership of international organisations in its own right at the end of the twentieth century, and joined the United Nations on 18 September 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations with the United States were established on 10 February 1997, when Madeleine M. Kunin, the American ambassador to Switzerland, became the first accredited to Liechtenstein, resident at Bern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Liechtenstein,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/li.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Liechtenstein,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LIE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Liechtenstein,” https://history.state.gov/countries/liechtenstein. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Liechtenstein,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LIE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Castillo%2C_Vaduz%2C_Liechtenstein%2C_2022-10-23%2C_DD_65.jpg/1920px-Castillo%2C_Vaduz%2C_Liechtenstein%2C_2022-10-23%2C_DD_65.jpg","title":"Vaduz Castle, Liechtenstein","desc":"Vaduz Castle.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo,_Vaduz,_Liechtenstein,_2022-10-23,_DD_65.jpg","alt":"A view of Vaduz Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-212","num":212,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"British Virgin Islands","answerText":"British Virgin Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dutch settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1648</span><span class=\"dt-k\">British control</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1666</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ministerial rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"British Virgin Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Road Town"],["Population","40.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Road Town"],["Area","151 km² (58 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","british virgin islands"],"abstract":"The <b>British Virgin Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and comprise some 60 islands, islets and cays, twenty of them inhabited and filed by the United Nations under the Caribbean with code 092.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They lie about 100 kilometres (60 miles) east of Puerto Rico and 25 kilometres (16 miles) from the United States Virgin Islands, with which they form one archipelago, the major islands being Tortola, Virgin Gorda, Anegada and Jost Van Dyke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land area is 153 square kilometres by the working paper, 151 by the statistics division and 150 by the World Bank, against an exclusive economic zone of 80,117 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The population is about 40,000 — 39,471 for 2024 in the working paper and 39,732 for 2025 at the World Bank — and income per head was 40,478 US dollars in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Road Town, the capital, stands on Tortola, the largest island; the language is English, the currency the United States dollar, and financial services and tourism carry the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest known inhabitants were the Arawaks and the Caribs, and the Dutch made the first permanent European settlement in 1648.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> British planters took control in 1666 and the islands became a British colony, and ministerial government arrived with the constitution of 1967 under a new office of Chief Minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Virgin Islands Constitution Order 2007 the British Crown appoints a governor answering for defence, internal security, external affairs, the terms of service of public servants and the administration of the courts, with some external-affairs matters delegated to the territorial government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Commission of Inquiry into the territory’s governance was established in January 2021, and its report was made public on 29 April 2022 with 49 recommendations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It found unlawful a 20-year residence requirement the Cabinet had applied to belonger status since 2004, contrary to the 1977 Immigration and Passport Act, and a review published in September 2023 made 15 further recommendations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “British Virgin Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/4, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111018. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “British Virgin Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vg.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: British Virgin Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VGB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: British Virgin Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VGB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Virgin_Gorda_North_Sound_2010.jpg/1920px-Virgin_Gorda_North_Sound_2010.jpg","title":"Virgin Gorda, the British Virgin Islands","desc":"Virgin Gorda.","credit":"Gruepig, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgin_Gorda_North_Sound_2010.jpg","alt":"A view of Virgin Gorda."}},
{"id":"gw-213","num":213,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Gibraltar","answerText":"Gibraltar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded to Britain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1713, Treaty of Utrecht</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN appeal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">resolution 2070, 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brussels talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 November 1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gibraltar"},"facts":[["Capital","Gibraltar"],["Population","40.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Gibraltar"],["Area","6 km² (2.3 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","gibraltar"],"abstract":"<b>Gibraltar</b> is a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom: a narrow peninsula running south from the south-western coast of Spain, joined to it by an isthmus about 1.6 kilometres (1 mile) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Algeciras lies 8 kilometres (5 miles) across the bay and Africa 32 kilometres (20 miles) across the strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is itself contested: the administering Power gives 5.8 square kilometres and Spain, which claims sovereignty, 4.8, while the statistics division gives 6 and the World Bank rounds to 10.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The population was 37,936 on 2022 data and 40,126 for 2025 at the World Bank, at 4,012.6 to the square kilometre; the currency is the Gibraltar pound, at par with sterling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There is no known natural resource and no farmland, so the economy rests on tourism and on financial services — banking, insurance, shipping and online gaming — with output of £3.1 billion in 2024/25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Spain ceded sovereignty to the United Kingdom under the Treaty of Utrecht in 1713; the United Kingdom holds that its territorial waters follow from sovereignty over the land, and Spain that article 10 ceded only the city, castle, port and fortifications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly first appealed to both governments to hold talks in resolution 2070 (XX) of 1965, and in 2025 urged them to reach a definitive solution in the spirit of the Brussels Declaration of 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Gibraltar Constitution Order 2006 the Governor answers for external affairs, defence, internal security and certain public appointments, and the elected government for everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parliament may make laws for the peace, order and good government of Gibraltar and the British Crown retains the same power, unused since 2006; appeals run from the supreme court through a court of appeal to the Privy Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An agreement on taxation between Spain and the United Kingdom regarding Gibraltar took effect on 4 March 2021, after Britain left the European Union, and the United Nations files the territory under Southern Europe with code 292.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Gibraltar: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/8, 23 February 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4105789. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Gibraltar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gi.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Gibraltar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GIB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Gibraltar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GIB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/View_of_the_Rock_of_Gibraltar_from_the_Strait.jpg/1920px-View_of_the_Rock_of_Gibraltar_from_the_Strait.jpg","title":"the Rock of Gibraltar, Gibraltar","desc":"The Rock of Gibraltar.","credit":"rob Stoeltje, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Rock_of_Gibraltar_from_the_Strait.jpg","alt":"A steep limestone headland rising from the sea."}},
{"id":"gw-214","num":214,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Monaco","answerText":"Monaco","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1793, by France</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">regained in 1814</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty renewed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2005</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 May 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Monaco"},"facts":[["Capital","Monaco"],["Population","38.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Monaco"],["Area","2 km² (0.77 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","monaco"],"abstract":"<b>Monaco</b> is a sovereign principality of 2 square kilometres (0.77 square miles) on the Mediterranean coast, with about 38,000 people at 25,732.2 to the square kilometre, all of them counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole state is one city, also called Monaco, the currency is the euro, and the United Nations files the principality under Western Europe with code 492.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its area is a case for checking a source before quoting it: the World Bank’s surface-area series gives 74.9 square kilometres, some 37 times the statistics division’s figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Income per head was 256,581 US dollars in 2023, services making up 88.5 per cent of gross value added, and 70.2 per cent of the people living there were born abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 88.7 years for women and 84.7 for men, and the World Bank’s series has the population peaking at 38,956 in 2023 and easing to 38,341 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When the United States declared independence in 1776 Monaco was already a sovereign country, under the military protection of Louis XVI of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was annexed to the First French Republic in 1793, regained its independence and sovereignty in 1814, and had both reaffirmed by the Treaty of Vienna the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1815 to 1861 it stood under the protectorship of the Kingdom of Piedmont-Sardinia, and it has been under the military safeguard of France since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Monaco and the United States exchanged their first consular agents after the American Civil War, and the marriage of Grace Kelly to Prince Rainier III in 1956 strengthened the tie further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The principality joined the United Nations on 28 May 1993 and the Council of Europe in October 2004, and when France and Monaco renegotiated their 1918 treaty in 2005 the new provisions reaffirmed Monaco’s independence and sovereignty, after which it began to receive foreign ambassadors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Monaco,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mc.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Monaco,” https://history.state.gov/countries/monaco. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Monaco,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MCO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Monaco,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MCO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/The_Casino%2C_Monte_Carlo_-_panoramio_cropped.jpg/1920px-The_Casino%2C_Monte_Carlo_-_panoramio_cropped.jpg","title":"Monte Carlo Casino, Monaco","desc":"Monte Carlo Casino.","credit":"Steve Hedin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Casino,_Monte_Carlo_-_panoramio_cropped.jpg","alt":"A view of Monte Carlo Casino."}},
{"id":"gw-215","num":215,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Marshall Islands","answerText":"Marshall Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Self-governing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 October 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ended 22 December 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Marshall Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Majuro"],["Population","36.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Majuro"],["Area","181 km² (70 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","marshall islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Marshall Islands</b> are a republic in Micronesia of 181 square kilometres (70 square miles), with about 36,000 people at 201.6 to the square kilometre and their capital at Majuro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has fallen in every year the World Bank records, from 48,800 in 2015 to 36,282 in 2025, a quarter of it gone in a decade, and the statistics division puts the annual rate at −3.5 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the United States dollar, income per head was 6,952 dollars in 2023, and about 77 per cent of the population is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agriculture makes up 19.7 per cent of gross value added against 12.4 for industry and 67.9 for services, and imports ran to 18,375 million US dollars in 2022 against exports of 1,277 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gave the islands a code of their own, 584, in 1991, having counted them until then within the Pacific Islands Trust Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The republic became self-governing in 1979, and on 25 June 1983 it began negotiating a Compact of Free Association with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Compact was agreed and entered into force on 21 October 1986, ending the trusteeship agreement and giving the islands the right to exercise self-determination, while the United States kept responsibility for their defence and the continued use of Kwajalein Atoll for military purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the independence of the Marshall Islands that day, and an Office of the United States Representative opened at Majuro under Samuel B. Thomsen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 22 December 1990 the Security Council determined, by fourteen votes to one at its 2972nd meeting, that the Trusteeship Agreement had ceased to apply to the islands, to the Federated States of Micronesia and to the Northern Mariana Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 26 July 1989, the representative’s office was raised to an embassy that September, William Bodde Jr. was appointed ambassador on 27 June 1990, and the republic joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Marshall Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mh.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Marshall Islands,” https://history.state.gov/countries/marshall-islands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Marshall Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MHL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 683 (1990), Adopted by the Security Council at Its 2972nd Meeting, on 22 December 1990,” S/RES/683(1990), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/103696. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/IJN_Nagato.jpg/1920px-IJN_Nagato.jpg","title":"Bikini Atoll, the Marshall Islands","desc":"Bikini Atoll.","credit":"AshS1987, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IJN_Nagato.jpg","alt":"A view of Bikini Atoll."}},
{"id":"gw-216","num":216,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"San Marino","answerText":"San Marino","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 May 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Extradition treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1906</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consular relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 April 1925</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"San Marino"},"facts":[["Capital","San Marino"],["Population","34.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Dogana"],["Area","61 km² (24 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","san marino"],"abstract":"<b>San Marino</b> is a republic of 61 square kilometres (24 square miles) enclosed by Italy, with about 34,000 people at 550.4 to the square kilometre and its capital, of the same name, holding 4,500 of them in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank rounds the area to 60 square kilometres and gives 34,109 people for 2025, a figure the statistics division rounds to 34,000, and the United Nations files the republic under Southern Europe with code 674.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The currency is the euro, income per head was 58,917 US dollars in 2023, and 97.4 per cent of the population is counted as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Industry makes up 40.1 per cent of gross value added against 59.9 for services and almost nothing for agriculture, which employs 0.3 per cent of those in work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life expectancy at birth is 87.3 years for women and 84.4 for men, and the fertility rate is 1.2 children per woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Regent Captains of San Marino wrote to President Abraham Lincoln on 29 March 1861 offering him honorary citizenship, and his reply of 7 May is taken as the moment the United States recognised the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lincoln wrote that “although your dominion is small, your State is nevertheless one of the most honored, in all history”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two states signed a treaty of extradition at Rome on 10 January 1906 and established consular relations on 9 April 1925, the consul at Florence generally serving as the American representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No ambassador was accredited to the republic until 2006, and Ronald Spogli, who was also ambassador to Italy, presented his credentials on 8 March 2007 from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> San Marino joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992, and the World Bank’s series has its population rising from 32,897 in 2015 to 34,109 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “San Marino,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: San Marino,” https://history.state.gov/countries/san-marino. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: San Marino,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SMR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: San Marino,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SMR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Guaita_Fortress_-_San_Marino_-_2024_02_13_-_GT_02_%28details%29.jpg/1920px-Guaita_Fortress_-_San_Marino_-_2024_02_13_-_GT_02_%28details%29.jpg","title":"Guaita, San Marino","desc":"Guaita.","credit":"Terragio67, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guaita_Fortress_-_San_Marino_-_2024_02_13_-_GT_02_(details).jpg","alt":"A view of Guaita."}},
{"id":"gw-217","num":217,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Åland","answerText":"Åland","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">To Russia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1809</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Demilitarised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1921, by ten states</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomy Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Åland"},"facts":[["Capital","Mariehamn"],["Population","30.8k (2025)"],["Largest city","Mariehamn"],["Area","1,586 km² (612 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","europe","åland"],"abstract":"<b>Åland</b> is an autonomous, demilitarised, Swedish-speaking region of Finland, a group of islands of 1,586 square kilometres (612 square miles) in the Baltic between Finland and Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There are 6,757 islands of at least a quarter of a hectare and some 20,000 smaller ones and skerries, and 30,836 residents at the end of 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The main island holds 70 per cent of the land and 90 per cent of the people; Mariehamn, the only town, was founded in 1861 and holds 11,957, with 1,990 in the outer archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 40 per cent of the inhabitants were born outside Åland, most in Finland or Sweden but some from about 110 countries in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has 16 municipalities, the smallest of them, Sottunga, being the smallest in all Finland, and the United Nations gave the islands a code of their own in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Åland passed to the Russian Empire in 1809, and after British and French troops took the fortress at Bomarsund in the Crimean War it was demilitarised by a one-sided Russian commitment at Paris in 1856.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the League of Nations settled the islands’ constitutional affiliation in 1921, an international convention signed by ten states confirmed that demilitarisation and added neutralisation, so no military may be present, the islands may not be fortified, and they must be kept outside any theatre of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia is not a party to the 1921 convention, but the Moscow Treaty of 1940 and the Paris Peace Treaty of 1947 both carry demilitarisation provisions, though neither mentions neutralisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Autonomy rests on an Act on the Autonomy of Åland passed by the Parliament of Finland, the third of which took effect on 1 January 1993; amending it needs a constitutional majority in Finland and the consent of the 30-member Lagting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Åland acceded to the European Union with Finland in 1995 after two referendums and on a protocol of its own, which treats it as a third territory for indirect taxation; the treaties apply only as that protocol provides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Government of Åland, “Facts about Åland,” https://www.aland.ax/en/facts-about-aland. [Open access]","Statistics and Research Åland, “Facts about Åland,” https://www.asub.ax/en/facts-about-aland. [Open access]","Nordic Statistics Database, “AREA02: Land Use, Square Kilometres by Reporting Country, Unit and Time,” https://pxweb.nordicstatistics.org/pxweb/en/Nordic%20Statistics/Nordic%20Statistics__Geography%20and%20climate__Land%20use/AREA02.px/. [Open access]","European Union, <i>Consolidated Version of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union</i>, art. 355, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:12016E355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/KasterholmsCastel.jpg/1920px-KasterholmsCastel.jpg","title":"Kastelholm Castle, Åland","desc":"Kastelholm Castle.","credit":"OmJagKundeDrömma, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KasterholmsCastel.jpg","alt":"A view of Kastelholm Castle."}},
{"id":"gw-219","num":219,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Anguilla","answerText":"Anguilla","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Colonised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1650</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UK dependency</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in force 1982</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last amended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Anguilla"},"facts":[["Capital","The Valley"],["Population","16.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","The Valley"],["Area","96 km² (37 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","anguilla"],"abstract":"<b>Anguilla</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, lying 240 kilometres (150 miles) east of Puerto Rico, 113 kilometres (70 miles) north-west of Saint Kitts and Nevis and 8 kilometres (5 miles) north of Sint Maarten and Saint Martin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is relatively flat, its few rolling hills rising to 65 metres (213 feet), and its main island runs 26 kilometres (16 miles) at its longest and 5 kilometres (3 miles) at its widest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land area is 96 square kilometres by the working paper and 91 by the United Nations statistics division, against an exclusive economic zone of 92,178 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population is 16,010 on the working paper’s 2025 estimate where the statistics division gives 15,000 for the same year, all of it counted as urban, and the capital is The Valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> English is spoken by 97 per cent of the people, with Spanish and Chinese also spoken, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar, pegged to the United States dollar at 2.6882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Anguilla was originally inhabited by the Arawaks and was colonised by British and Irish settlers in 1650, after which it was periodically attached to Saint Kitts and Nevis and to various regional structures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a dependency of the United Kingdom in 1980, and the Anguilla Constitution Order came into force in 1982 and has been amended in 1990, 2019 and 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Under it the government consists of the Governor, the Executive Council and the House of Assembly, the Governor answering for defence, external affairs, internal security including the police, international financial services and their regulation, and public service appointments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tourism and financial services carry the economy, income per head was 28,850 US dollars on the 2025 estimate, and services made up 80.4 per cent of gross value added in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations files the territory under the Caribbean with code 660, and the General Assembly first took note of the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Anguilla: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/2, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4104532. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Anguilla,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ai.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","The Anguilla Constitution Order 1982, SI 1982/334, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1982/334/made. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Anguilla_Shoal_Bay_is_the_BEST_beach_in_the_Caribbean._-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Anguilla_Shoal_Bay_is_the_BEST_beach_in_the_Caribbean._-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Shoal Bay, Anguilla","desc":"Shoal Bay in Anguilla.","credit":"onj, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anguilla_Shoal_Bay_is_the_BEST_beach_in_the_Caribbean._-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A white sand beach curving along clear turquoise water."}},
{"id":"gw-220","num":220,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Palau","answerText":"Palau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ended 10 November 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Palau"},"facts":[["Capital","Melekeok"],["Population","18.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Koror"],["Area","459 km² (177 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","palau"],"abstract":"<b>Palau</b> is a republic in the western Pacific of 459 square kilometres (177 square miles), with about 18,000 people at 38.4 to the square kilometre and the United States dollar as its currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations names Melekeok as its capital, though the figure it gives for the capital’s population, 11,400 in 2018, refers by its own footnote to Koror, and the American embassy stands at Koror too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population is almost flat: the World Bank’s series runs from 17,770 in 2015 to 17,663 in 2025, moving by less than one per cent across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Income per head was 15,899 US dollars in 2023, gross domestic product 282 million, and agriculture made up 3.3 per cent of gross value added.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations files the republic under Micronesia with code 585, having counted it until 1994 within the Pacific Islands Trust Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Palau formed part of the United Nations Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, administered by the United States after the Second World War, and began the process towards independence in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people approved a new status agreement in a plebiscite observed by a visiting mission of the Trusteeship Council, and its legislature adopted a resolution approving the same agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> President Bill Clinton issued proclamation 6726 on 27 September 1994, recording that the United States had fulfilled its obligations under the Trusteeship Agreement, and the Compact of Free Association took effect on 1 October, which is the date of American recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 10 November 1994 the Security Council, meeting for the 3455th time, determined unanimously that the objectives of the Trusteeship Agreement had been fully attained and that it had ceased to apply to Palau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Palau joined the United Nations on 15 December 1994, an American ambassador resident at Manila was accredited to it on 6 December 1996, and an embassy of its own opened at Koror on 10 October 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Palau,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pw.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Palau,” https://history.state.gov/countries/palau. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Palau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PLW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 956 (1994), Adopted by the Security Council at Its 3455th Meeting, on 10 November 1994,” S/RES/956(1994), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/163867. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Ngerukewid-2016-aerial-view-Luka-Peternel.jpg/1920px-Ngerukewid-2016-aerial-view-Luka-Peternel.jpg","title":"Rock Islands, Palau","desc":"Rock Islands.","credit":"Luka Peternel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngerukewid-2016-aerial-view-Luka-Peternel.jpg","alt":"A view of Rock Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-221","num":221,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cook Islands","answerText":"Cook Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Free association</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2001, reaffirmed 2015</span><span class=\"dt-k\">High income</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2019</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 September 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cook Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Avarua"],["Population","13.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Avarua"],["Area","236 km² (91 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","cook islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Cook Islands</b> are a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand, lying in Polynesia between French Polynesia and American Samoa, with about 13,000 people and their capital at Avarua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives the islands 236 square kilometres (91 square miles), a figure its own footnote says excludes Niue, which it calls part of the Cook Islands but administered separately because of remoteness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its figure for the capital’s population, 13,100 in 2018, refers by another footnote to the whole island of Rarotonga, so it is not a figure for Avarua at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the New Zealand dollar, the whole population is counted against a density of 55.3 people to the square kilometre, and the statistics office took its most recent census of population and dwellings in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations files the islands under Polynesia with code 184, and for statistical purposes its data for New Zealand exclude them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The free-association arrangement was established in 1965, and the islands are part of the Realm of New Zealand, so they share a head of state, King Charles III.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They conduct their own affairs, hold an international legal personality of their own and conduct their own international relations, while New Zealand carries a constitutional responsibility to answer requests for help with foreign affairs, disasters and defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cook Islanders hold New Zealand citizenship, and a large Cook Islands community lives in New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The principles of the partnership were set out in a Joint Centenary Declaration signed in 2001 and reaffirmed in a joint statement in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands graduated in 2019 to the category of high-income country, ending their eligibility for official development assistance and making them the first Pacific island country or territory to do so since 2000, and the United States recognised them as a sovereign and independent state on 25 September 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cook Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ck.html. [Open access]","New Zealand Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade, “Cook Islands,” https://www.mfat.govt.nz/en/countries-and-regions/pacific/cook-islands. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cook Islands,” https://history.state.gov/countries/cook-islands. [Open access]","Cook Islands Statistics Office, https://www.stats.gov.ck/. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/Aitutaki_from_top_2006.jpg","title":"Aitutaki, the Cook Islands","desc":"Aitutaki.","credit":"Mr Bullitt, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aitutaki_from_top_2006.jpg","alt":"A palm-fringed shore above a pale blue lagoon."}},
{"id":"gw-222","num":222,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nauru","answerText":"Nauru","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">full member 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 September 1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nauru"},"facts":[["Capital","None; offices at Yaren"],["Population","12.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Denigomodu"],["Area","21 km² (8 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","nauru"],"abstract":"<b>Nauru</b> is a republic in Micronesia of 21 square kilometres (8 square miles) — a single raised coral island — with about 12,000 people at 601.3 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has no official capital: government offices stand in the district of Yaren, and the figure the United Nations gives for that capital, 11,300 in 2018, refers by its own footnote to the whole of Nauru.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank, which files the republic under its Nauruan name Naoero, has the population rising in every year from 10,954 in 2015 to 12,025 in 2025, and gives the land area as 20 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The currency is the Australian dollar, income per head was 14,812 US dollars in 2023, and industry made up 36.5 per cent of gross value added against 61 for services and 2.4 for agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unemployment stood at 23 per cent at the last measurement the statistics division carries, and gross domestic product was 176 million US dollars in 2023 against 104 million in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nauru passed through several colonial and military occupations before becoming a United Nations trust territory after the Second World War, and the Commonwealth records that it joined on attaining full independence as a former trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It achieved independence in 1968 and joined the Commonwealth the same year as an associate member, becoming a full member in 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Nauru on 24 October 1976, when its ambassador to Australia, Marshall Green, presented credentials while remaining resident at Canberra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That accreditation moved from Canberra to Suva on 24 April 1996, when the American ambassador to Fiji, Don L. Gevirtz, presented his credentials, and there has never been an American embassy in Yaren.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The republic joined the United Nations on 14 September 1999, in the same year it took up full Commonwealth membership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nauru,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nr.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Nauru,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/nauru. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nauru,” https://history.state.gov/countries/nauru. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Naoero,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NRU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Naoero,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NRU/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Buada_Lagoon%2C_Nauru_2007.jpg/1920px-Buada_Lagoon%2C_Nauru_2007.jpg","title":"Buada Lagoon, Nauru","desc":"Buada Lagoon.","credit":"Lorrie Graham/AusAID, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buada_Lagoon,_Nauru_2007.jpg","alt":"A view of Buada Lagoon."}},
{"id":"gw-225","num":225,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tuvalu","answerText":"Tuvalu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 May 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 2000</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tuvalu"},"facts":[["Capital","Funafuti"],["Population","10.0k (2025)"],["Largest city","Funafuti"],["Area","26 km² (10 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","tuvalu"],"abstract":"<b>Tuvalu</b> is a Polynesian country of 26 square kilometres (10 square miles) by the United Nations statistics division and 25.6 by the Commonwealth Secretariat, with about 10,000 people at 316.4 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth gives its capital as Vaiaku, where the government offices stand, on the islet of Fongafale in Funafuti atoll, while the statistics division names Funafuti and counts 7,000 people there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is falling: the World Bank’s series runs from 10,963 in 2015 to 9,492 in 2025, where the Commonwealth recorded 11,790 in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The currency is the Australian dollar, income per head was 6,880 US dollars in 2023, and gross domestic product was 68 million against 36 million in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under Polynesia with code 798 and marks it both a least developed country and a small island developing State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were called the Ellice Islands under British sovereignty, as part of the Gilbert and Ellice Islands Colony, and took the name Tuvalu on becoming an independent country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised Tuvalu on 1 October 1978, when Elizabeth G. Stevens, acting as the President’s personal representative, delivered a letter to that effect from President Jimmy Carter to Governor General Penitala Teo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 10 May 1979, when Ambassador Inoke Faletau presented his credentials to President Carter, and Tuvalu joined the Commonwealth in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John P. Condon became the first American ambassador to Tuvalu on 24 January 1980, accredited also to Fiji and Tonga and resident at Suva, and every ambassador since has stayed at Suva with no mission established at Funafuti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tuvalu joined the United Nations on 5 September 2000, 22 years after the United States recognised it, and its people had had only very occasional contact with Americans before the late 1970s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Tuvalu,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tv.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Tuvalu,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/tuvalu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tuvalu,” https://history.state.gov/countries/tuvalu. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tuvalu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TUV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Funafuti_airport_-_Fiji_Airways.jpg","title":"Funafuti, Tuvalu","desc":"Funafuti.","credit":"Michael Coghlan, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funafuti_airport_-_Fiji_Airways.jpg","alt":"A view of Funafuti."}},
{"id":"gw-226","num":226,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Pierre and Miquelon","answerText":"Saint Pierre and Miquelon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Organic law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 2007</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Territorial Council</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 members, 5 years</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Pierre and Miquelon"},"facts":[["Capital","Saint-Pierre"],["Population","6.00k (2025)"],["Largest city","Saint-Pierre"],["Area","242 km² (93 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","saint pierre and miquelon"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Pierre and Miquelon</b> is a French overseas collectivity of 242 square kilometres (93 square miles) in the North Atlantic, east of the Canadian coast, made up of several islands and islets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 6,000 people live there at 24.2 to the square kilometre, the capital is Saint-Pierre, the official language is French and the currency is the euro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The highest point is the Morne de la Grande Montagne on Miquelon, at 240 metres (790 feet), and the archipelago runs north-east to south-west along the line of the Appalachians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its climate is cold and humid and dominated by the sea, averaging 5.4 degrees Celsius over the year, 15.5 in August and −3.3 in February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 600 species of vascular plants grow there, some 500 of them native, alongside 177 species of moss, and more than 300 species of bird are recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It became an overseas collectivity under the organic law of 21 February 2007, the same law that reorganised Saint-Barthélemy and Saint-Martin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Local power is exercised by a Territorial Council of 19 members elected for five years by universal suffrage, and by its president, while the French State is represented by a prefect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taxation and customs fall to the territory, along with the powers conferred elsewhere on French regions and departments, and the law of the French Republic applies directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archipelago is one of the European Union’s overseas countries and territories, listed in Annex II to the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, to which the special arrangements for association in Part Four of that treaty apply rather than the treaties in general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fishing, processing and aquaculture remain the principal route of economic diversification, construction depends heavily on public procurement, and the United Nations files the archipelago under Northern America with code 666.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Pierre and Miquelon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pm.html. [Open access]","Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “Saint-Pierre-and-Miquelon,” https://www.overseas-association.eu/saint-pierre-and-miquelon. [Open access]","Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “OCTs,” https://www.overseas-association.eu/octs. [Open access]","European Union, <i>Consolidated Version of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union</i>, art. 355, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:12016E355. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Sainte-Pierre_aerial.jpg/1920px-Sainte-Pierre_aerial.jpg","title":"Saint-Pierre, Saint Pierre and Miquelon","desc":"The island of Saint-Pierre from the air.","credit":"Doc Searls from Santa Barbara, USA, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sainte-Pierre_aerial.jpg","alt":"A low bare island with a town on its northern shore."}},
{"id":"gw-227","num":227,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Helena","answerText":"Saint Helena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1502</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1659</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saint Helena"},"facts":[["Capital","Jamestown"],["Population","5.00k (2025)"],["Largest city","Half Tree Hollow"],["Area","309 km² (119 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","africa","saint helena"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Helena</b> is a volcanic island in the South Atlantic, 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles) from Angola, and the seat of a non-self-governing territory called Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ascension lies 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) to the north-west and Tristan da Cunha 2,400 kilometres (1,500 miles) to the south, the three holding 309 square kilometres (119 square miles) of land in an exclusive economic zone of 1,641,294.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Secretariat counts 4,122 people on Saint Helena in June 2025, about 800 on Ascension and 221 on Tristan da Cunha, each island having its own seat: Jamestown, Georgetown and Edinburgh of the Seven Seas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Saint Helena pound and income per head was £9,210 in 2023/24; Ascension has no permanent population, so no such figure exists for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Legislative Council has 15 seats of which 12 are elected, six are held by women, including the Chief Minister and the Speaker, and there are no political parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The island was reportedly found in 1502 by a Spanish navigator in Portuguese service and was a port of call for ships bound to the East Indies by the end of that century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Netherlands claimed it in 1633 without settling it, and the British East India Company took possession and settled it in 1659, two years after Oliver Cromwell granted a charter to govern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a Crown colony in 1833, a British dependent territory in 1981 and a British overseas territory in 2002, and the General Assembly first noted the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The constitution that came into force in September 2009 changed the territory’s name from Saint Helena and its Dependencies, giving the three island groups equal standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The administering Power calls Tristan da Cunha the most remote inhabited island in the world: no airport, nine scheduled sailings a year, and a voyage from Cape Town of at least seven days; the United Nations files the territory under Western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Saint Helena: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/13, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111021. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Helena,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sh.html. [Open access]","The St Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha Constitution Order 2009, SI 2009/1751, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/1751/contents/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Overlooking_Jamestown_from_the_south.jpg/1920px-Overlooking_Jamestown_from_the_south.jpg","title":"Jamestown, Saint Helena, Saint Helena","desc":"Jamestown, Saint Helena.","credit":"Kevstan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Overlooking_Jamestown_from_the_south.jpg","alt":"A view of Jamestown, Saint Helena."}},
{"id":"gw-228","num":228,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Montserrat","answerText":"Montserrat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1632</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plymouth abandoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in force 2011</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Montserrat"},"facts":[["Capital","Brades; Plymouth abandoned 1997"],["Population","4.40k (2025)"],["Largest city","Brades"],["Area","103 km² (40 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","montserrat"],"abstract":"<b>Montserrat</b> is a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, in the Leeward Islands of the eastern Caribbean, 43 kilometres (27 miles) south-west of Antigua and 64 kilometres (40 miles) north-west of Guadeloupe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole island is volcanic, and of its 103 square kilometres (40 square miles) only 40.1 are habitable, inside an exclusive economic zone of 7,582 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its capital, Plymouth, was abandoned in 1997 after the volcanic eruption, and most government offices now stand at Brades, which the statistics division records as Brades Estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 4,400 people live there, income per head was 19,261 US dollars in 2024, the public sector is the largest employer, and the currency is the East Caribbean dollar at 2.70 to the United States dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Volcanic activity keeps the most fertile land, pasture and fishing grounds restricted or unreachable, so the island depends largely on imports and its food security is low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Christopher Columbus named the island after a monastery near Barcelona; its Indigenous name is Alliouagana, the Land of the Prickly Bush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first European settlers, mostly Irish, arrived in 1632, and the island later took in indentured Irish servants transported to the British West Indies, before a major slave uprising on Saint Patrick’s Day in 1768.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> France occupied it intermittently during the eighteenth century until it was confirmed as British in 1783, and it became a Crown colony in 1871.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Federation of the West Indies broke up in 1962 Montserrat chose Crown colony status over associated statehood, and the General Assembly had first noted the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under the Montserrat Constitution Order 2010, in force from September 2011, the Governor answers for internal security, external affairs, defence, the public service and the regulation of international financial services, and the United Nations files the island under the Caribbean with code 500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Montserrat: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/10, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111020. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Montserrat,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ms.html. [Open access]","The Montserrat Constitution Order 2010, SI 2010/2474, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2010/2474/contents/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Soufriere_Hills.jpg/1920px-Soufriere_Hills.jpg","title":"Soufrière Hills, Montserrat","desc":"Soufrière Hills.","credit":"CommonismNow, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soufriere_Hills.jpg","alt":"A view of Soufrière Hills."}},
{"id":"gw-229","num":229,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Falkland Islands","answerText":"Falkland Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">On the UN list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reservations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GA resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2065 (XX)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Falkland Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Stanley"],["Population","3.66k (2021 census)"],["Largest city","Stanley"],["Area","12,173 km² (4,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","territory","americas","falkland islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Falkland Islands</b>, which the United Nations calls the Falkland Islands (Malvinas), are a non-self-governing territory administered by the United Kingdom: East Falkland, West Falkland and hundreds of smaller islands, about 12,173 square kilometres (4,700 square miles) in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They lie in the South Atlantic 770 kilometres (480 miles) north-east of Cape Horn and 480 kilometres (300 miles) east of the mainland, at 0.3 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The 2021 census, which every household was required to answer except British Forces personnel and their families, counted 3,662 residents: 2,974 in Stanley, 354 in Camp and 334 civilians at the Mount Pleasant Complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The currency is the Falkland Islands pound, and a British defence force based at Mount Pleasant numbered about 830 personnel on 28 November 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South Georgia lies 1,300 kilometres (800 miles) to the south-east and the South Sandwich Islands 750 kilometres (470 miles) beyond it, and the Governor is Commissioner for both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands have been on the United Nations list of non-self-governing territories since 1946, after the United Kingdom transmitted information under Article 73 e of the Charter in accordance with General Assembly resolution 66 (I).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At the Fourth Committee’s twenty-fifth meeting, on 6 December 1946, Argentina entered a reservation that it did not recognise British sovereignty over the islands, and the United Kingdom a parallel reservation that it did not recognise Argentine sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dispute between the two governments over sovereignty is recognised by the General Assembly in its resolution 2065 (XX) and in the later resolutions on the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitution approved in 2008 came into force on 1 January 2009 and has been protested by Argentina; under it the legislature elects its own speaker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Policy is considered by an Executive Council of three members of the Legislative Assembly elected annually from among themselves, and the United Nations files the territory under South America, its Spanish register naming it Islas Malvinas (Falkland).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Falkland Islands (Malvinas): Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/6, 31 March 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111019. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Falkland Islands (Malvinas),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fk.html. [Open access]","The Falkland Islands Constitution Order 2008, SI 2008/2846, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2008/2846/contents/made. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Aerial_photo_Port_Stanley.jpg","title":"Stanley, Falkland Islands, the Falkland Islands","desc":"Stanley, Falkland Islands.","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_photo_Port_Stanley.jpg","alt":"A view of Stanley, Falkland Islands."}},
{"id":"gw-231","num":231,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Niue","answerText":"Niue","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Free association</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Partnership</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 July 2022</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 September 2023</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 November 2025</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Niue"},"facts":[["Capital","Alofi"],["Population","2.00k (2025)"],["Largest city","Alofi"],["Area","260 km² (100 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","country","oceania","niue"],"abstract":"<b>Niue</b> is a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand, a single raised coral island of 260 square kilometres (100 square miles) in the South Pacific with its capital at Alofi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 2,000 people live there, at seven to the square kilometre, and the currency is the New Zealand dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is part of the Realm of New Zealand, so the two share a head of state, the King in Right of New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Niue Constitution Act 1974 New Zealand provides the economic and administrative assistance Niue needs, and help with foreign affairs, defence and security if the Niue government asks for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In return the two consult regularly on matters of mutual concern, and Niue may not enter defence arrangements that would engage New Zealand’s sovereign competencies or act in ways that might undermine the Realm’s vital security interests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The free-association arrangement dates from 1974, and all Niueans are New Zealand citizens under New Zealand law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division files Niue under Polynesia and excludes it from its data for New Zealand, though its Cook Islands profile still describes the island as part of the Cook Islands, administered separately because of remoteness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> New Zealand and Niue signed a Statement of Partnership on 21 July 2022, and at their first Leaders’ Meeting on 13 November 2025 the two prime ministers signed a Political Declaration affirming the free-association relationship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Niue as a sovereign and independent state on 25 September 2023, when Secretary of State Antony Blinken and Premier Dalton Tagelagi signed a joint statement establishing diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small-scale agriculture and the processing of organic produce for export — honey, vanilla and noni juice — make up the primary sector, tourism is a large contributor, almost everything else is imported, and the United Nations gives the island the code 570.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Niue,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nu.html. [Open access]","New Zealand Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade, “Niue,” https://www.mfat.govt.nz/en/countries-and-regions/pacific/niue. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Niue,” https://history.state.gov/countries/niue. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cook Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ck.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Avatele_Beach%2C_looking_southwest.jpg/1920px-Avatele_Beach%2C_looking_southwest.jpg","title":"Niue, Niue","desc":"Niue.","credit":"Pseudopanax at English Wikipedia, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Avatele_Beach,_looking_southwest.jpg","alt":"A view of Niue."}},
{"id":"gw-232","num":232,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Vatican City","answerText":"Vatican City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lateran Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">signed 11 February 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vatican"},"facts":[["Capital","Vatican City"],["Population","882residents (2024)"],["Largest city","Vatican City"],["Area","0.44 km² (0.17 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","geography","country","europe","vatican city"],"abstract":"<b>Vatican City</b> is a sovereign state of 0.44 square kilometres (0.17 square miles), or 44 hectares, on a hill by the right bank of the Tiber inside Rome, and the United Nations files it as the Holy See under Southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its territory is partly walled and takes in Saint Peter’s Square as far as the marble strip that joins the ground to the outer parts of the Colonnade, a boundary anyone may cross freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five entrances are guarded by the Pontifical Swiss Guard and the Gendarmerie Corps, and the currency is the euro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 31 December 2024 the state had 673 citizens, 458 of them living inside the walls including 120 Swiss Guards, and 882 residents in all, citizens and non-citizens together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 30 per cent of its citizens live outside the state, most of them because their work is diplomatic, and 66 cardinals hold its citizenship, ten of whom live inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The hill held a private circus built by the emperor Caligula, and north of it, in a necropolis along a secondary road, lies the burial place of Saint Peter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantine raised a great basilica over that grave between 324 and 326, and the present Saint Peter’s replaced it in the 16th and 17th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state itself was created by the Lateran Treaty between the Holy See and Italy, signed on 11 February 1929 and ratified on 7 June that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its personality as a sovereign entity under international law, separate from the Holy See, is universally recognised, and its structures are deliberately reduced to the minimum its functions require.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the territory is so small, many of the Holy See’s offices stand in buildings elsewhere in Rome which the Lateran Treaty protects with the immunity foreign embassies enjoy, and the United States recognised the Holy See on 10 January 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Governorate of Vatican City State, “Geography,” https://www.vaticanstate.va/en/state-and-government/general-informations/geography.html. [Open access]","Governorate of Vatican City State, “Population,” https://www.vaticanstate.va/en/state-and-government/general-informations/population.html. [Open access]","Governorate of Vatican City State, “Origins and Characteristic,” https://www.vaticanstate.va/en/state-and-government/general-informations/origins-and-characteristic.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Holy See,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/va.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Holy See,” https://history.state.gov/countries/holy-see. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Basilica_di_San_Pietro_in_Vaticano_September_2015-1a.jpg/1920px-Basilica_di_San_Pietro_in_Vaticano_September_2015-1a.jpg","title":"St. Peter's Basilica, Vatican City","desc":"St. Peter's Basilica.","credit":"Alvesgaspar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_di_San_Pietro_in_Vaticano_September_2015-1a.jpg","alt":"A view of St. Peter's Basilica."}},
{"id":"gw-233","num":233,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Pitcairn Islands","answerText":"Pitcairn Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sighted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1767, by Robert Pitcairn</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1790, from HMS Bounty</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Order</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Pitcairn Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Adamstown"],["Population","35 (2023)"],["Largest city","Adamstown"],["Area","35.5 km² (13.7 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","territory","oceania","pitcairn islands"],"abstract":"The <b>Pitcairn Islands</b> are a non-self-governing territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the United Kingdom, and lie midway between New Zealand and South America at 25 degrees south and 130 degrees west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They comprise four islands — Pitcairn, Henderson, Ducie and Oeno — of which only Pitcairn is inhabited, with 35.5 square kilometres (13.7 square miles) of land in all and 4.35 on Pitcairn itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their exclusive economic zone of 842,000 square kilometres is now a marine protected area, and the United Nations files the territory under Polynesia with code 612, having previously listed it as Pitcairn Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Secretariat counted 35 people in 2023 where the territory’s own government says around fifty; the capital and only settlement is Adamstown, above Bounty Bay, the currency is the New Zealand dollar and there are no political parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Incomes rest largely on tourism, the sale of stamps and coins, handicrafts and honey, and the territory receives budgetary aid from the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands are named after Robert Pitcairn, a British midshipman who sighted them in 1767, and Pitcairn was uninhabited when castaways from HMS <i>Bounty</i> — nine mutineers and eighteen Polynesians — arrived in 1790; Pitcairn Island itself runs about 3.2 kilometres (2 miles) by 1.6 kilometres (1 mile).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most islanders today trace their descent from those mutineers and their Tahitian companions, and the official languages are English and Pitkern, a mixture of eighteenth-century English and Tahitian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A new constitution took effect in March 2010, replacing the Pitcairn Order 1970 and the Royal Instructions of the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Governor is appointed by the British Crown, and in practice the United Kingdom’s High Commissioner to New Zealand holds that office concurrently, so responsibility for administering the island rests with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Island Council and the Deputy Mayor are elected every two years in November and the Mayor every three, and the General Assembly first noted the information transmitted on such territories under Article 73 e of the Charter on 14 December 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Pitcairn: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/12, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4102971. [Open access]","The Pitcairn Constitution Order 2010, SI 2010/244, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2010/244/contents/made. [Open access]","The Pitcairn Order 1970, SI 1970/1434, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1970/1434/contents/made. [Open access]","Government of the Pitcairn Islands, https://www.government.pn/. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Transmission of Information under Article 73 e of the Charter,” A/RES/66, 14 December 1946, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209781. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Standard Country or Area Codes for Statistical Use (M49),” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/methodology/m49/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Adamstown1.jpg","title":"Adamstown, Pitcairn Islands, the Pitcairn Islands","desc":"Adamstown, Pitcairn Islands.","credit":"Makemake at de.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adamstown1.jpg","alt":"A view of Adamstown, Pitcairn Islands."}},
{"id":"gr-351","num":351,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Greek verse written in couplets of a hexameter followed by a shorter pentameter, and carried by the reed pipe rather than the lyre, is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whatever its subject.","questions":["Callinus of Ephesus called his countrymen to arms and Mimnermus wrote of love, yet both worked in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a kind of verse defined by its metre and not by its subject.","The couplet Simonides used to commemorate the dead became the verse of the Greek epigram in general, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> long outlived the reed pipe that had once accompanied it."],"answer":"elegy","answerText":"elegy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Callinus and Archilochus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Metre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2-line couplet: hexameter, then pentameter</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Elegy</b> in Greek is a matter of metre rather than of mood, since any poem built from couplets that pair a dactylic hexameter with a shorter pentameter counts as one, whatever its subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two words lie behind the name and the Greeks kept them apart, <i>elegos</i> for a song of mourning barely distinguishable from the dirge, and <i>elegeion</i> for the couplet as a form of verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Where the word itself came from is not known and it may not be Greek at all, one suggestion making it a Carian borrowing and another a repeated cry of grief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The couplet enters literature early in the 7th century BCE with Callinus of Ephesus and Archilochus, and it is a variation on the heroic hexameter rather than a form invented apart from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its second line falls into two equal halves that each break off a syllable short, and it was this twice-repeated dying fall that ancient critics heard as fitted to the expression of grief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Roman poets inherited the doctrine that the unequal couplet had carried lament first and fulfilled desire afterwards, along with the admission that nobody could say who had published the first of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archaic poems that survive are in fact of every variety, and the melancholy the modern word carries is not what marked the ancient genre out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Callinus and Tyrtaeus used the metre to call men to arms, Solon to argue politics and defend his own legislation, and Mimnermus to write of love and the shortness of youth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was carried by the <i>aulos</i>, the reed pipe, rather than by the lyre, so the Greeks never counted it as melic verse, and it soon became a measure recited as readily as sung.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its ordinary home was the symposium, though a papyrus of Simonides on the dead of Plataea shows it at public occasions too, and from commemorating the dead it became the verse of the epitaph and then of the epigram at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 49–51, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 75–78, trans. C. Smart, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0065:card=73. [Open access]","Krystyna Bartol, “Elegijne nastroje: wczesna elegia grecka i nie tylko,” <i>Poznańskie Studia Polonistyczne. Seria Literacka</i> 18 (2011): 11–22, https://doi.org/10.14746/pspsl:2011.18.2. [Open access]","Herbert Kynaston, <i>Extracts from the Greek Elegiac Poets, from Callinus to Callimachus</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1880), v–viii, https://archive.org/details/extractsfromgre01kynagoog. [Open access]","Cecilia Nobili, “Threnodic Elegy in Sparta,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51, no. 1 (2011): 26–48, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/1631. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 76–81, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Livio Sbardella, “Aulodes and Rhapsodes: Performance and Forms of Greek Elegy from Mimnermus to Hermesianax,” <i>Aitia</i> 8, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.4000/aitia.2247. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), cxxiv–cxxv, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Aulos_player_Met_24.97.28.jpg/1280px-Aulos_player_Met_24.97.28.jpg","title":"A player of the aulos","desc":"Attic red-figure lekythos attributed to the Brygos Painter, about 480 BCE, showing a girl playing the double reed pipe that accompanied elegy. Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York, 24.97.28.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aulos_player_Met_24.97.28.jpg","alt":"An orange figure on a black Greek oil flask: a woman in a long pleated robe stands playing a double reed pipe with both hands, a spotted pipe-case hanging behind her and a couch at her side."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the form defined by metre rather than by mood?","a":"Because any poem built from couplets pairing a hexameter with a shorter pentameter counts as one, and the surviving archaic poems call men to arms, argue politics and write of love."},{"q":"Why did ancient critics hear the couplet as fitted to grief?","a":"Because its second line falls into two equal halves that each break off a syllable short, and it was that twice-repeated dying fall they read as lament."},{"q":"Why was it never counted among the melic kinds?","a":"Because it was carried by the aulos, the reed pipe, rather than by the lyre, and it soon became a measure recited as readily as sung."}]},
{"id":"gr-352","num":352,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Blame was the business of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the archaic Greek genre that took its name from the metre in which, Aristotle says, its poets lampooned one another.","questions":["Archilochus rounded on Lycambes and Hipponax on the artist Bupalus, each of them naming his enemy outright, which is what the archaic Greeks expected of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a form.","A servant called Iambe made the grieving Demeter laugh with jokes at once obscene and aggressive, and Greek tradition made her the inventor of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which kept that laughter."],"answer":"iambus","answerText":"iambus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Archilochus of Paros</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 poets, Archilochus to Hipponax</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Staple line</span><span class=\"dt-v\">iambic trimeter, 6 feet in 3 pairs</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Iambus</b> is the third of the great forms of archaic Greek verse beside epic and melic song, and what defines it is not its metre but its business, which is blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its poets proclaim <i>psogos</i> against people who break the rules a community lives by, so the genre acts as a censor inside a society that thinks in terms of praise and blame.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word itself is not Greek and its origin is unknown, though it belongs with <i>dithyrambos</i> and <i>thriambos</i>, names of songs tied to the worship of Dionysus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek tradition instead derived it from Iambe, the servant at Eleusis who in the <i>Homeric Hymn to Demeter</i> alone broke the grieving goddess out of her silence with jokes both obscene and aggressive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle gave a different account, holding that the metre kept the name because it was the one in which the early poets had lampooned each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Iambic verse has almost no early history and appears suddenly in the middle of the 7th century BCE, already finished and exact, in the hands of Archilochus of Paros.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is Ionian, it had a place in the popular festivals of Demeter and Iakchos, and the same ritual roots produced the performances that became tragedy and comedy at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexandrian scholars fixed a canon of three, Archilochus, Semonides of Amorgos and Hipponax of Ephesus, and each is remembered with a named enemy, Lycambes for the first and the artist Bupalus for the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The staple line is the trimeter, six iambic feet counted in three pairs, which Aristotle called the most conversational of all metres because ordinary talk keeps falling into it, and Hipponax slowed it by lengthening the last foot but one into the limping <i>scazon</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Horace has rage arm Archilochus with the iambus, and comedy and tragedy then took the measure over for dialogue, so one poet's weapon of abuse became the working line of the Athenian stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Antonio Aloni, “La performance giambica nella Grecia arcaica,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 1, no. 1 (2006): 83–107, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/79. [Open access]","William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 68–70, 98–99, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1448b, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1448b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1449a, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1449a. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Ars Poetica</i> 79–82, trans. C. Smart, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0065:card=73. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 85–88, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Revellers_BM_VaseE508.jpg/1280px-Revellers_BM_VaseE508.jpg","title":"Revellers with a barbitos","desc":"Detail from an Attic red-figure bell-krater of 450 to 440 BCE, said to be from Aegina, showing three revellers on a komos, one of them carrying a barbitos. The drinking party and the revel are the setting most iambic verse was performed in. British Museum, London, 1893,0712.13.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Revellers_BM_VaseE508.jpg","alt":"Three orange figures on a black Greek mixing bowl stride along in headbands and loose cloaks, the middle one carrying a tall lyre with two long curving arms."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this genre defined by its business rather than its metre?","a":"Because its poets proclaim psogos against people who break the rules a community lives by, so it acts as a censor inside a society that thinks in terms of praise and blame."},{"q":"Why do the ancient explanations of the name disagree?","a":"Because Greek tradition derived it from Iambe, whose obscene jokes broke Demeter's silence, while Aristotle held the metre kept the name because early poets had lampooned each other in it."},{"q":"Why did the trimeter become the line of the Athenian stage?","a":"Because Aristotle called it the most conversational of all metres, ordinary talk keeping falling into it, so comedy and tragedy took it over for dialogue."}]},
{"id":"gr-353","num":353,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"One famous fragment shrugs off a shield abandoned in a bush to a Thracian Saian, a piece of soldierly candour that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the byword for the anti-heroic.","questions":["Paros kept an open-air shrine with altars where a poet was honoured beside the gods, founded with Delphi's blessing, and the poet worshipped there was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, dead some centuries before.","Lycambes broke off his daughter's betrothal and was pursued in verse for the rest of the poet's life, which is the best-known story told about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Paros."],"answer":"Archilochus","answerText":"Archilochus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Paros, then Thasos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of the 3 classical iambic poets</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Archilochus</b> of Paros is the first Greek poet who speaks in his own voice about his own life, and antiquity ranked him beside Homer and Hesiod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belongs to the middle of the 7th century BCE, and iambic verse has almost no history before him, appearing suddenly in his hands already finished and exact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The life the ancients told is a soldier's: a son of Telesicles who left Paros for the colony on Thasos, served as a mercenary and was killed in a battle against Naxos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His most quoted lines shrug at a shield he left in a bush for a Thracian Saian to boast over, since he had saved himself and could get another no worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That refusal of the heroic pose is why he was read as Homer's opposite, though a fragment on Telephus, where flight is forced by a god, suggests the theme is more ambivalent than plain defiance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The quarrel that made his name was with Lycambes, who promised him his daughter Neobule and then broke off the match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A papyrus at Cologne, published in 1974, gave back his first nearly complete poem, an epode of 35 lines in which he and a younger sister of Neobule talk their way to a seduction, though whether that is love poetry or revenge on the family is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later tradition said his verses drove Lycambes and his daughters to hang themselves, but much of that life is drawn from the poems, where the speaking voice may be a mask rather than the man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Paros went on to make him a hero: a citizen named Mnesiepes, with Delphi's approval, laid out an open-air precinct called the Archilocheion, with altars at which the gods and Archilochus were sacrificed to together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its inscription tells how the boy, driving a cow to market by moonlight, traded jokes with a band of women, then found the cow gone and a lyre at his feet, and understood they were the Muses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Rafael de Carvalho Matiello Brunhara and Thiago Koslowsky da Rosa, “O fragmento de Télefo e a fuga em Arquíloco,” <i>Cadernos de Letras da UFF</i> 28, no. 56 (2018): 49–63, https://doi.org/10.22409/cadletrasuff.2018n56a544. [Open access]","William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 69, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 86–88, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Miroslav Marcovich, “A New Poem of Archilochus: <i>P.Colon.</i> inv. 7511,” <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 16, no. 1 (1975): 5–14, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/8631. [Open access]","Matteo Rivoli, “L'iscrizione di Mnesiepes dall'Archilocheion di Paro,” <i>Axon</i> 4, no. 1 (2020): 141–64, https://doi.org/10.30687/Axon/2532-6848/2020/01/008. [Open access]","Antonio Aloni, “La performance giambica nella Grecia arcaica,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 1, no. 1 (2006): 83–107, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/79. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Archilochos_fragment_PK7511r.jpg","title":"The Cologne papyrus of Archilochus","desc":"Papyrus Cologne inv. 7511, recto, of the 1st or 2nd century CE, carrying the epode that gave back the first nearly complete poem of Archilochus. Papyrussammlung Köln.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archilochos_fragment_PK7511r.jpg","alt":"A tall strip of golden-brown papyrus, ragged at top and bottom, closely written with about forty lines of Greek in unspaced capital letters."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the life told of this poet an unreliable guide to him?","a":"Because much of it is drawn from the poems themselves, where the speaking voice may be a mask rather than the man, including the tale that his verses drove Lycambes and his daughters to hang themselves."},{"q":"Why is the abandoned shield read as more than plain defiance?","a":"Because a fragment on Telephus, where flight is forced by a god, suggests the theme is more ambivalent than the shrug that made him Homer's opposite."},{"q":"Why did Paros sacrifice to him beside the gods?","a":"Because a citizen named Mnesiepes laid out an open-air precinct with Delphi's approval, the Archilocheion, with altars at which the gods and the poet were honoured together."}]},
{"id":"gr-354","num":354,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Antiquity called her simply the Poetess, as Homer was the Poet, and an epigram ascribed to Plato made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a tenth Muse beside the nine.","questions":["Of the nine books Alexandria edited, almost nothing survives whole, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read in quotations and torn papyri, with a single complete hymn to Aphrodite among them.","Mytilene struck her face on its coins, and Herodotus knew poems in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> attacked her brother Charaxus over his affair with a courtesan in Egypt."],"answer":"Sappho","answerText":"Sappho","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Eresos and Mytilene, Lesbos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Sappho</b> of Lesbos is the most famous woman of the ancient Greek world and was reckoned by universal consent in antiquity its greatest poet of love.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was born at Eresos to a noble family and lived at Mytilene, on an island the Greeks reckoned the most musical they knew, whose lyric flowered at the very moment its politics tore it apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Her dialect is the Aeolic actually spoken on Lesbos rather than a literary one, and her verse technique is of extraordinary variety, using perhaps more metres than any other Greek poet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of them, the four-line stanza built on the hendecasyllable that Alcaeus used as well, has carried her name in European verse ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexandria edited her into nine books arranged probably by metre, and she was called simply the Poetess, as Homer was the Poet, while an epigram ascribed to Plato made her a tenth Muse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Almost none of it survives whole, and she is read in quotations by later critics and in torn papyri, with a single complete poem, a hymn asking Aphrodite for help in love.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The songs are addressed to a circle of women, and what that circle was, a school, a cult group or a company of friends, is not settled by anything the fragments themselves say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus already knew poems in which she attacked her brother Charaxus over his affair with a courtesan in Egypt, and a papyrus published this century has given back a poem that names him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her own city struck her face on its coins, Horace set her in the underworld beside Alcaeus with the shades listening in silence, and a line of women poets followed her across Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later antiquity could not place a woman who was neither a courtesan nor silent, so comedy made her one, and the tale of her leap from the Leucadian cliff for love of the ferryman Phaon belongs to that invention rather than to her life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 226–31, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Anni Arukask, “The Metrical Structure of the Sapphic Hendecasyllable and Sappho's Aiolikon in Lesbian Poetry,” <i>Studia Metrica et Poetica</i> 7, no. 1 (2020): 97–113, https://doi.org/10.12697/smp.2020.7.1.04. [Open access]","Giulia Donelli, “Herodotus, the Old Sappho and the Newest Sappho,” <i>Lexis</i> 39 (2021): 13–34, https://doi.org/10.30687/Lexis/2724-1564/2021/01/001. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Odes</i> 2.13, trans. John Conington, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0025:book=2:poem=13. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 90–93, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Athina Dimopoulou, “La violence politique à Lesbos à l'époque d'Alcée et Pittacos,” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 45, no. 1 (2019): 23–36, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2019_num_45_1_4695. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Sappho_coin_British_Museum.jpg/960px-Sappho_coin_British_Museum.jpg","title":"Sappho on a coin of Mytilene","desc":"Copper-alloy coin of Mytilene on Lesbos, 2nd century CE, carrying a head of Sappho and the local form of her name, PSAPPHO. British Museum, London, BNK,G.510.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sappho_coin_British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A small, heavily worn bronze coin held in a museum mount, showing a woman’s head in profile facing right with her hair bound up, and Greek letters around the edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why is her verse read in fragments rather than whole?","a":"Because almost nothing survives entire and she is known from quotations by later critics and torn papyri, with a single complete poem, a hymn asking Aphrodite for help in love."},{"q":"Why is the nature of her circle of women unsettled?","a":"Because the songs are addressed to it and nothing the fragments themselves say decides whether it was a school, a cult group or a company of friends."},{"q":"Why is the leap from the Leucadian cliff not part of her life?","a":"Because later antiquity could not place a woman who was neither a courtesan nor silent, so comedy made her one, and the tale of Phaon belongs to that invention."}]},
{"id":"gr-355","num":355,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"The aristocratic songs of faction that Mytilene's symposia sang, against Melanchros and Myrsilos and finally against Pittacus himself, are the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, our one witness from inside.","questions":["Like Archilochus before him, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost his shield to the enemy at Sigeum and sang about it, telling the herald that the Athenians had hung it up in Athena's temple.","Horace heard him in the underworld chanting of shipwreck, exile and war, and the shades pressed closer when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sang of battle and tyrants driven out."],"answer":"Alcaeus","answerText":"Alcaeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Mytilene, Lesbos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Exile</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 years, under Pittacus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Alcaeus</b> of Mytilene is the poet of a city at war with itself, and the one direct witness to the faction fighting that convulsed Lesbos around 600 BCE, since he was among the men doing the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belongs with Sappho to the Aeolic tradition of the island, and the two are named together constantly, working in the same dialect and sharing the hendecasyllabic line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His verse was sung at the symposia of the <i>hetairiai</i>, the political clubs of aristocrats bound by blood and by common interest, which is where his songs of faction found their audience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For eight years he and the other nobles fought the tyrant Melanchros, and when Melanchros was killed they got Myrsilos in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Lesbians at last handed power to Pittacus, later counted among the Seven Sages, to end the fighting; Alcaeus called him a tyrant and spent 15 years in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He wandered in Thrace and sailed as far as Egypt, while his brother Antimenidas took service with the Babylonians, and in the end Pittacus pardoned him and he came home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Like Archilochus he lost his shield, at Sigeum against the Athenians, and sang about it himself, telling the herald that the enemy had hung it up in the temple of Athena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alexandria edited him into ten books, all of it occasional verse: hymns, drinking songs, love songs, and above all the <i>stasiotika</i>, the songs of faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Horace, who made the stanza named after him his own, heard him in the underworld chanting of shipwreck, exile and war, with the silent dead pressing closer for the songs of battle and tyrants driven out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The paradox of the island is that its violence peaked exactly when its lyric did, on what the Greeks reckoned the most musical ground they knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Athina Dimopoulou, “La violence politique à Lesbos à l'époque d'Alcée et Pittacos,” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 45, no. 1 (2019): 23–36, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2019_num_45_1_4695. [Open access]","Anni Arukask, “The Metrical Structure of the Sapphic Hendecasyllable and Sappho's Aiolikon in Lesbian Poetry,” <i>Studia Metrica et Poetica</i> 7, no. 1 (2020): 97–113, https://doi.org/10.12697/smp.2020.7.1.04. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 210–11, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 91–92, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Horace, <i>Odes</i> 2.13, trans. John Conington, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0025:book=2:poem=13. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Alkaios_Sappho_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2416_full.jpg/1280px-Alkaios_Sappho_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2416_full.jpg","title":"Alcaeus and Sappho","desc":"Side A of an Attic red-figure kalathos attributed to the Brygos Painter, about 470 BCE, found at Akragas in Sicily: Alcaeus with a barbitos on the left and Sappho on the right, both named in the painted inscriptions. Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich, 2416.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alkaios_Sappho_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2416_full.jpg","alt":"A tall black Greek vase in a museum case, painted in orange with a bearded man in a long robe holding a lyre with two long curving arms and, facing him, a woman in a pleated robe carrying another."},"why":[{"q":"Why is he the one direct witness to the fighting on Lesbos?","a":"Because he was among the men doing it, fighting the tyrant Melanchros for eight years with the other nobles and then spending fifteen years in exile under Pittacus."},{"q":"Why were his songs of faction sung at drinking parties?","a":"Because they were performed at the symposia of the hetairiai, the political clubs of aristocrats bound by blood and common interest, which is where such songs found their audience."},{"q":"Why does his lost shield read differently from Archilochus's?","a":"Because he sang about it himself, telling the herald that the enemy had hung it up in the temple of Athena at Sigeum."}]},
{"id":"gr-356","num":356,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus counted five Aeolian cities dividing one island, a sixth having been enslaved by Methymna, and that island, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, was reckoned the most musical ground in Greece.","questions":["Terpander of Antissa, Arion of Methymna, Sappho of Eresos and Alcaeus of Mytilene were all born on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, within a century or so of one another.","The stanza forms named after Sappho and Alcaeus are called Aeolic after the dialect spoken on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where both of them were made."],"answer":"Lesbos","answerText":"Lesbos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Aeolic song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Terpander, Arion, Sappho, Alcaeus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, once 6 before Arisba fell</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"abstract":"<b>Lesbos</b> is the large island off the coast of Asia Minor that produced more of the earliest Greek song than anywhere else, and the Greeks reckoned it the most musical ground they knew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus counted five Aeolian cities dividing it between them, a sixth, Arisba, having been enslaved by its own kinsmen at Methymna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sappho and Alcaeus stand for the Aeolic tradition of Greek poetry, which is the island's own, and the metres they used are still classified as Aeolic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first name in that tradition is Terpander of Antissa, credited with giving the lyre seven strings instead of four and with the first musical victory at the Spartan Carneia, a distinction early Greek tradition hands out freely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He shaped the kitharodic nome, the solo sung to the lyre, into a piece of seven marked parts, and his supremacy in that form was said to have lasted two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Arion of Methymna followed him, a lyre-player second to none in his age, whom Herodotus names as the first man known to compose a dithyramb, give it that name and teach it at Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story that a dolphin carried him ashore at Taenarus after pirates put him overboard was told by Corinthians and Lesbians alike, and it is the scene in which he puts on his singing robes to die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Then came Sappho of Eresos and Alcaeus of Mytilene, whose work is monody rather than choral song, sung by one voice and built in short stanzas that follow one another unbroken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their shared hendecasyllabic line and the four-line stanzas named after each of them carried Aeolic verse into Latin and, through Horace, into modern European poetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The paradox of the place is that all this came at the moment its politics tore it apart, and the generation that made the songs also fought the faction wars Alcaeus sang about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.151, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=151. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.23, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Athina Dimopoulou, “La violence politique à Lesbos à l'époque d'Alcée et Pittacos,” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 45, no. 1 (2019): 23–36, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2019_num_45_1_4695. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 77–78, 90–92, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), lx–lxii, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Anni Arukask, “The Metrical Structure of the Sapphic Hendecasyllable and Sappho's Aiolikon in Lesbian Poetry,” <i>Studia Metrica et Poetica</i> 7, no. 1 (2020): 97–113, https://doi.org/10.12697/smp.2020.7.1.04. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","geography","greece","archaic greece","literature","poetry"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Lesbos_1211x897.jpg/1280px-Lesbos_1211x897.jpg","title":"Lesbos from orbit","desc":"Landsat image of Lesbos, 2000, in false colour: the Gulf of Kalloni cuts deep into the middle of the island from the south and the Gulf of Gera into its south-eastern corner, with the coast of Asia Minor at the top right.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lesbos_1211x897.jpg","alt":"A satellite view of a green and pink island in a near-black sea, with one long inlet biting deep into its centre from the south and a smaller one into its south-eastern corner, and a mainland coast at the top right."},"locator":{"name":"Lesbos","at":[26.28,39.21],"zoom":6},"why":[{"q":"Why are Greek metres still classified as Aeolic?","a":"Because the tradition Sappho and Alcaeus stand for is the island's own, and its dialect and its lines carried into Latin and, through Horace, into modern European poetry."},{"q":"Why is the credit given to Terpander hard to press?","a":"Because he is said to have given the lyre seven strings and won the first musical victory at the Spartan Carneia, a distinction early Greek tradition hands out freely."},{"q":"Why is the island's flowering called a paradox?","a":"Because it came at the moment its politics tore it apart, the generation that made the songs being the one that fought the faction wars Alcaeus sang about."}]},
{"id":"gr-357","num":357,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Ionian elegy turned from war to the shortness of youth in the hands of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose long poem was named after Nanno, a player of the reed pipe.","questions":["Propertius rated the love poetry of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> above Homer’s, and the Alexandrians took from him the plan of a book of elegies named for a beloved.","Men are like the leaves that the season brings out, says a fragment of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> borrowed straight from the Iliad, and youth is enjoyed for a cubit's length of time."],"answer":"Mimnermus","answerText":"Mimnermus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Colophon or Smyrna, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nanno</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 long elegy, or a book of them</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Mimnermus</b> is the elegist who turned the metre away from the battlefield towards pleasure and its shortness, and he gave elegy the pensive erotic tone it kept afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He flourished in the second half of the 7th century BCE, and the ancient notices make him a man of Colophon or of Smyrna, the city Colophon had settled and Lydia by then ruled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His best-known work is the <i>Nanno</i>, a long poem or a gathering of poems named after a woman who played the reed pipe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was remembered as a singer rather than a reciter, nicknamed the clear-toned one, and his elegy was performed to that pipe rather than spoken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The note struck again and again is that youth is short and old age hateful, and Horace summed the creed up as the view that nothing is pleasant without love and jesting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One fragment takes Homer's image of men as the leaves a spring brings out, and adds that we enjoy the flower of youth for a cubit's length of time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another follows the Sun through his endless labour, sleeping in a hollow winged bed of gold that carries him back over the sea from west to east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war poems are there as well, richer than those of Tyrtaeus or Callinus, one of them telling how Smyrna was taken, and he brought the settlers of both his cities straight from Pylos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The plan of the <i>Nanno</i>, a book of elegies gathered under a beloved's name, became a formative idea for the elegists of Alexandria and for their Roman imitators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Propertius wrote that his verse was worth more in love than Homer's, which is close to the highest thing a Roman elegist could say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Kynaston, <i>Extracts from the Greek Elegiac Poets, from Callinus to Callimachus</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1880), viii, 8–9, 61–62, https://archive.org/details/extractsfromgre01kynagoog. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 81, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Livio Sbardella, “Aulodes and Rhapsodes: Performance and Forms of Greek Elegy from Mimnermus to Hermesianax,” <i>Aitia</i> 8, no. 1 (2018), https://doi.org/10.4000/aitia.2247. [Open access]","G. L. Huxley, “Mimnermus and Pylos,” <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 2, no. 2 (1959): 109–10, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/12441. [Open access]","William Ross Hardie, <i>Res Metrica: An Introduction to the Study of Greek and Roman Versification</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1920), 50, https://archive.org/details/resmetrica0000unse. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"why":[{"q":"Why does his elegy sound different from that of the war poets?","a":"Because he turned the metre away from the battlefield towards pleasure and its shortness, striking again and again the note that youth is short and old age hateful."},{"q":"Why was he remembered as a singer rather than a reciter?","a":"Because he was nicknamed the clear-toned one and his elegy was performed to the reed pipe rather than spoken, the pipe carrying the metre in the archaic period."},{"q":"Why did the Nanno matter to poets centuries later?","a":"Because a book of elegies gathered under a beloved's name became a formative idea for the elegists of Alexandria and for their Roman imitators."}]},
{"id":"gr-358","num":358,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Nearly 1,400 lines survive under one name because the collection was used as a schoolbook, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by far the best preserved of the Greek elegists.","questions":["Cyrnus is named again and again through the poems as a seal set on them, so that a later reader could tell what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote from what anyone else did.","Megara’s old families lost to men the poet called base, and one of the best-known couplets of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says the city is still a city but its people are different."],"answer":"Theognis","answerText":"Theognis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 520 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Megara</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Corpus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,400 elegiac lines</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Theognis</b> of Megara is by far the best preserved of the Greek elegists, and the roughly 1,400 lines that come down under his name are the largest body of archaic elegy there is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They survive because the collection was put together as a schoolbook in the 3rd century BCE, and they show the interpolation a schoolbook attracts, with passages of Solon, Mimnermus, Tyrtaeus and Euenus grown into the text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Editors therefore use the name for the poems the manuscripts attribute to him rather than as a claim that one Megarian poet wrote them all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost all of it is addressed to Cyrnus, son of Polypaus, a younger man whom the speaker instructs in how a noble ought to live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The repetition of that name is deliberate: it is called a seal set on the verses, so that a theft will be noticed and nobody can pass them off as his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The politics are those of a beaten aristocrat in a city famous for its factions, who calls the men now in charge base and himself and his friends noble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cyrnus, this city is still a city, but the citizens are different, runs one elegy, which has those citizens wearing goatskins and grazing outside the walls a generation before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Good and bad in these poems are political words before they are moral ones, marking the old families off from the new men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was driven into exile and poverty, and some of the poems are the exile waiting, telling himself to drink when his enemies drink and let nobody count his wounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon and Plato both quote his couplet on learning good from the good, and it is as a store of such maxims that the collection was assembled at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 83–84, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Sara De Martin, “Criticising Change: From Theognis to Plato,” <i>Lexis</i> 40 (2022): 387–414, https://doi.org/10.30687/Lexis/2724-1564/2022/02/006. [Open access]","Herbert Kynaston, <i>Extracts from the Greek Elegiac Poets, from Callinus to Callimachus</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1880), 68–69, https://archive.org/details/extractsfromgre01kynagoog. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Meno</i> 95d, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0178:text=Meno:section=95d. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Memorabilia</i> 1.2.20, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0208:book=1:chapter=2:section=20. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/POxy.v0023.n2380.a.01_Theognis.jpg","title":"A papyrus of Theognis","desc":"Oxyrhynchus Papyrus 2380, one of only two papyrus fragments of Theognis, carrying lines 254 to 278 and copied in the 2nd or 3rd century CE. Sackler Library, Oxford.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POxy.v0023.n2380.a.01_Theognis.jpg","alt":"Two ragged brown fragments of papyrus, photographed beside a centimetre scale, carrying lines of Greek written in capital letters with holes and losses across them."},"why":[{"q":"Why does so much more of this poet survive than of the other elegists?","a":"Because the collection was put together as a schoolbook in the 3rd century BCE, and it was as a store of maxims that the roughly 1,400 lines were assembled at all."},{"q":"Why is the name used for the poems rather than claimed for one author?","a":"Because a schoolbook attracts interpolation, and passages of Solon, Mimnermus, Tyrtaeus and Euenus have grown into the text."},{"q":"Why is one name repeated all through the verses?","a":"Because the address to Cyrnus is called a seal set on the poems, so that a theft will be noticed and nobody can pass them off as his own."}]},
{"id":"gr-359","num":359,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Driven out of Teos when the Persians came, and later the ornament of Polycrates' court and then of Athens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sang of wine and desire at one court after another.","questions":["Herodotus has Polycrates lying in the men's quarters with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Teos beside him, and refusing to turn or answer a herald from the Persian governor.","A book of later imitations printed in 1554 gave Europe the image of a genial old toper, which has all but replaced the real <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a court poet with a sharp tongue."],"answer":"Anacreon","answerText":"Anacreon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 545 – 510 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Teos, Abdera, Samos, Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Anacreon</b> of Teos is the Greek poet of wine, desire and the drinking party, and he spent his working life at the courts of tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He left Teos with his fellow citizens in 545 BCE, when the Persian general Harpagus threatened Ionia, and helped to found the colony of Abdera in Thrace, where he seems to have lost his shield in battle as Archilochus and Alcaeus had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He appears next on Samos, at the court Polycrates had made famous for its magnificence, and Herodotus puts him beside the tyrant in the men's quarters when a Persian herald came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After Polycrates was murdered he took an invitation from Hipparchus to Athens, where he knew Xanthippus the father of Pericles, and when Hipparchus in turn was assassinated he seems to have gone on to the princes of Thessaly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alexandria divided him into five books of songs, iambics, elegies and epigrams, and Strabo said his poetry was full of Polycrates, though the tyrant's name is in none of the surviving fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Asked why his songs were not addressed to the gods, he is said to have answered that the boys of the court were his gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The popular picture of a tipsy and amorous old man leaves out his talent for satire, and his attack on Artemon is as effective a weapon as anything Archilochus threw at Lycambes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Teos put his face on its coins, and Pausanias saw his statue on the Athenian Acropolis standing beside that of Xanthippus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Anacreontea</i>, a body of imitations written across many centuries and first printed in 1554, took such hold of European taste that the graceful old toper has largely displaced the poet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His dates come from ancient chronographers rather than from records, and the synchronisms that fix him, Ibycus and Polycrates together were built long afterwards out of unequal grids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 281–82, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 283–85, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 280–81, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 94, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “I Policrati ibicei. Ibico, Anacreonte, Policrate e la cronografia dei poeti della ‘corte' di Samo,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 3, no. 1 (2008): 1–72, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/143. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.121, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=121. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Anacreon_monte_calvo.jpg/1280px-Anacreon_monte_calvo.jpg","title":"The Monte Calvo Anacreon","desc":"Marble statue of a bearded singer from a Roman villa at Monte Calvo near Rieti, carved in the 2nd century CE after a Greek original of the 5th century BCE and identified by modern scholarship as Anacreon. Ny Carlsberg Glyptotek, Copenhagen, IN 491.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anacreon_monte_calvo.jpg","alt":"A life-size white marble statue of a bearded man standing naked in a museum gallery, a cloak slung over both shoulders and both arms broken away, with a tree-trunk support at his side."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this poet move from court to court?","a":"Because he left Teos when Harpagus threatened Ionia, went to Polycrates on Samos, took Hipparchus's invitation to Athens when Polycrates was murdered, and moved on when Hipparchus was assassinated."},{"q":"Why does the popular picture of him mislead?","a":"Because the tipsy and amorous old man leaves out his talent for satire, his attack on Artemon being as effective a weapon as anything Archilochus threw at Lycambes."},{"q":"Why has the imitator largely displaced the poet?","a":"Because the Anacreontea, written across many centuries and first printed in 1554, took such hold of European taste that the graceful old toper stands for him."}]},
{"id":"gr-360","num":360,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Born at Rhegion where Ionians and Dorians had settled together, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went east to Samos and became, with Anacreon, one of the first poets to work for a tyrant.","questions":["A flock of cranes was said to have witnessed the murder and betrayed the killers, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is remembered by people who have never read a line of him.","The early poems follow Stesichorus into heroic legend and the later ones turn to roses, spring and desire, which is the two-part career of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Rhegion."],"answer":"Ibycus","answerText":"Ibycus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 560 – 525 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Rhegion, then Samos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of the 9 lyric poets</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Ibycus</b> was a Greek lyric poet of the 6th century BCE, born at Rhegion in southern Italy and counted among the nine whom Alexandria fixed as the canon of melic verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rhegion had been founded by Chalcidians from Ionian Euboea together with Dorian fugitives from Messene, so both dialects were spoken there and it is not known which stock he came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He led a wandering life like the rhapsodes until he settled on Samos, at the court of Polycrates, where Anacreon was with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two of them are the first poets known to have put their muse at a tyrant's disposal, and how much independence either kept cannot now be recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His work falls into two periods: before Samos he followed Stesichorus into heroic legend, telling of the Argonauts, of Herakles and of the men who fought at Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Samos the manner changes, and what survives is full of roses, spring, strange birds and the bright sleepless dawn that wakes the nightingales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato has Parmenides quote him on old age: an old race-horse trembling as he is led out to run again, which is how the poet says desire takes him against his will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His songs for boys' choruses were especially praised, and he was said to have brought an Aeolo-Ionic spirit into verse whose language and music were Dorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is best known, though, for a story: that robbers killed him on a lonely shore and he called on a passing flock of cranes to avenge him, which they did when one of the murderers pointed them out in a crowd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That tale is first told more than four centuries after his death and no earlier poet mentions it, and even his dates are chronographers' work, built long afterwards by synchronising him with Polycrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 268–71, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 105, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “I Policrati ibicei. Ibico, Anacreonte, Policrate e la cronografia dei poeti della ‘corte' di Samo,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 3, no. 1 (2008): 1–72, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/143. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xx, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Parmenides</i> 137a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Parm.:section=137a. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Heinrich_Schwemminger_-_Die_Kraniche_des_Ibykus_-_3739_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg/1280px-Heinrich_Schwemminger_-_Die_Kraniche_des_Ibykus_-_3739_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg","title":"The cranes of Ibycus","desc":"Heinrich Schwemminger, The Cranes of Ibycus, a 19th-century painting of Schiller’s ballad on the legend: the poet dying on the shore with his lyre, calling to the birds overhead while his killers stand over him.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Schwemminger_-_Die_Kraniche_des_Ibykus_-_3739_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum.jpg","alt":"A dark 19th-century painting: a fair-haired man in a red robe lies on the ground clutching a lyre and raising one arm towards birds in the sky, while two half-naked men with knives stand over him."},"why":[{"q":"Why can his dialect not settle where he came from?","a":"Because Rhegion was founded by Chalcidians from Ionian Euboea together with Dorian fugitives from Messene, so both were spoken there and it is not known which stock he came from."},{"q":"Why does his poetry change after he settles on Samos?","a":"Because before it he followed Stesichorus into heroic legend, and at the tyrant's court what survives is full of roses, spring, strange birds and the sleepless dawn."},{"q":"Why is the story of the cranes not evidence about him?","a":"Because it is first told more than four centuries after his death and no earlier poet mentions it, as even his dates are chronographers' work built by synchronising him with Polycrates."}]},
{"id":"gr-361","num":361,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Struck blind for slandering Helen, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote a recantation saying she never sailed and never saw Troy, and the story goes that his sight came back.","questions":["Ancient critics called him the lyric Homer and complained that he was long, since the Oresteia of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone ran to two books and his whole work to 26.","Not to know three lines of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a Greek way of calling someone illiterate, which says how thoroughly the choral poet of Himera had been read."],"answer":"Stesichorus","answerText":"Stesichorus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Matauros, Himera, Catana</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 books at Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Stesichorus</b> is the great figure of early Greek choral poetry, and the name is a title rather than a name, since it means chorus-setter and the man himself was called Tisias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came from Matauros in the west of Italy and became a citizen of Himera in Sicily during the long struggle against the tyrant Phalaris, retiring to Catana when Phalaris won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he did for Greek literature was threefold: he brought the epic saga west, he invented the stately narrative manner of lyric, and he made the heroes rather than the gods the chief theme of choral song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Antiquity called him the lyric Homer and said he bore the weight of the epic on his lyre, and the metres named after him are built out of halves of the epic hexameter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale was epic as well: the <i>Oresteia</i> alone filled two books and his collected work at Alexandria filled 26, which is why the critics who praised him also complained that he was long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The one personal story told of him is the Palinode, which Plato reports: struck blind for what he had said of Helen, he wrote that the tale was untrue, that she never boarded the ships nor came to the towers of Troy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The recantation is an argument as much as an act of piety: it was hard to believe that Troy had let itself be destroyed rather than give Helen up, and easier to conclude that the Trojans never had her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His reach can be measured by a proverb: not to know three lines of him was a Greek way of saying that a man could not read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nothing survives whole, partly because he was too plain to need a grammarian's notes, but a papyrus found at Lille gave back a long stretch of his Theban story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even his dates are unsteady, the ancient chronographers having fixed them by tying him to other poets rather than to any record of his own life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 101–3, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedrus</i> 243a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Phaedrus:section=243a. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “Un poeta senza età. Note di cronografia stesicorea e alcmanico-saffico-simonidea,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): 1–46, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/1071. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xxx–xxxi, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Antonietta Gostoli, “Some Aspects of the Theban Myth in the Lille Stesichorus,” <i>Greek, Roman and Byzantine Studies</i> 19, no. 1 (1978): 23–27, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/7551. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/POxy_XXXII_2617.jpg/1280px-POxy_XXXII_2617.jpg","title":"P.Oxy. XXXII 2617, a papyrus of the Geryoneis","desc":"Fragments of a papyrus roll from Oxyrhynchus in Egypt, mounted together, carrying part of the <i>Geryoneis</i> — the poem in which Heracles kills the three-bodied herdsman Geryon and takes his cattle.","credit":"Oxyrhynchus Papyri database, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POxy_XXXII_2617.jpg","alt":"Dozens of small brown papyrus fragments mounted in rows on a pale board, each carrying a few lines of Greek capital letters, with a measuring scale along the bottom edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this poet's name not his name?","a":"Because it means chorus-setter and is a title of his office; the man himself was called Tisias, and it is his work in choral poetry that the name records."},{"q":"Why was he called the lyric Homer?","a":"Because he brought the epic saga west, made the heroes rather than the gods the theme of choral song, and the metres named after him are built out of halves of the epic hexameter."},{"q":"Why is the Palinode an argument as well as a recantation?","a":"Because it was hard to believe Troy had let itself be destroyed rather than give Helen up, and easier to conclude that the Trojans never had her."}]},
{"id":"gr-362","num":362,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus says the epitaph of the seer Megistias at Thermopylae was paid for by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Ceos, and tradition gives him all three of the inscriptions there.","questions":["Charged with avarice because he wrote for a fixed price rather than for vague patronage, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came nearer than any earlier Greek poet to selling his work on the open market.","Greece turned to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than to Pindar to write the record of its Persian War dead, and at 80 he could still record a 56th victory with a chorus."],"answer":"Simonides","answerText":"Simonides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 556 – 468 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Iulis on Ceos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at Syracuse, aged 89</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Simonides</b> of Ceos was the first Greek to make a profession of poetry, and the first whom Greece asked to write the record of a war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born at Iulis on Ceos about 556 BCE, was called to Athens by Hipparchus, and after the Peisistratids fell he lived with the Scopadae at Crannon and the Aleuadae at Larissa in Thessaly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When those princes went over to Persia he returned to Athens, and there beat Aeschylus in a competition for an elegy on Marathon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For years afterwards he commemorated the men and the cities that had fought at Artemisium, Salamis and Plataea, and Herodotus names him as the author of an epitaph at Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The best known of those lines is the one for the Spartan dead: stranger, go tell the Lacedaemonians that we lie here obedient to their commands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He was charged with avarice, but what he had actually done was to write for a fixed price rather than for unspecified patronage, which was the nearest thing then possible to selling work on the open market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is credited with introducing the epinikion, the artistic ode for a victory at the games, and he worked in nearly every other kind as well, from dithyrambs and dirges to epigrams.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A tradition also made him the inventor of a system of memory and the man who brought four Ionic letters into the Athenian alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 80 he could record a 56th victory won with a chorus, and he spent his last years at the court of Hiero in Syracuse, dying there at 89.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A papyrus published in the 20th century added a long elegy on the dead of Plataea to what survived of him, which had been little more than quoted epigrams and the fragment on Danae adrift in her chest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 105–8, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), 302–3, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Cecilia Nobili, “Threnodic Elegy in Sparta,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 51, no. 1 (2011): 26–48, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/1631. [Open access]","Massimiliano Ornaghi, “Un poeta senza età. Note di cronografia stesicorea e alcmanico-saffico-simonidea,” <i>Annali Online dell'Università di Ferrara. Sezione Lettere</i> 9, no. 2 (2014): 1–46, https://doi.org/10.15160/1826-803X/1071. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.228, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=228. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/InscriptieTherm.jpg","title":"The epitaph for the Spartan dead at Thermopylae","desc":"A modern slab at Thermopylae cut with the couplet ancient tradition gives to Simonides: stranger, announce to the Lacedaemonians that here we lie, obedient to their commands.","credit":"Photograph by Napoleon Vier and Sofie Debognies, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:InscriptieTherm.jpg","alt":"A reddish stone slab set flat in a cobbled pavement, cut with four lines of widely spaced Greek capital letters."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the charge of avarice made against him?","a":"Because he wrote for a fixed price rather than for unspecified patronage, which was the nearest thing then possible to selling work on the open market."},{"q":"Why did Greece turn to him to record its war?","a":"Because he was the first to make a profession of poetry, and for years he commemorated the men and cities that fought at Artemisium, Salamis and Plataea, Herodotus naming him for an epitaph at Thermopylae."},{"q":"Why did the 20th century change what could be read of him?","a":"Because a published papyrus added a long elegy on the dead of Plataea to what had been little more than quoted epigrams and the fragment on Danae adrift in her chest."}]},
{"id":"gr-363","num":363,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"After the tables were cleared and a libation poured, the men of the house reclined for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, at which wine was mixed with water and the songs went round the couches.","questions":["Athenian mixing bowls, jugs, drinking cups and wine-coolers all take their shapes from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the scenes painted on them are largely of the parties they were used at.","Both Plato and Xenophon wrote a book named after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Greeks took from the East in the 7th century BCE when the couch replaced the chair at dinner."],"answer":"symposium","answerText":"symposium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 650 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">from the East, in place of the chair</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Libations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, then the paean</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>symposium</b> was the drinking party that followed dinner in a Greek house, a formal occasion with its own order of business rather than a night of casual drinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not the banquet Homer describes, which is eaten sitting on chairs: the couched version was innovated in the East and reached the Greeks in the later 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guests were as many as there were couches, so the party stayed small, and one city could hold several at once, each admitting one group under strict rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon gives the opening: the tables were carried out, the company poured a libation and sang a hymn, and only then did the drinking begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three libations were poured, to Zeus and Hera, to the Heroes and to Zeus the Saviour, and the paean sung after the third introduced everything that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Wine was drunk mixed with water and the proportion was argued over: one of water to two of wine was a toper's mixture, Anacreon urged ten to five, and half and half was thought to bring on madness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How hard a company drank was its own decision, and in Plato's <i>Symposium</i> the guests agree that each will drink as he pleases, then send the flute-girl away so they can talk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later that evening Alcibiades appoints himself president of the drinking, demands the largest cup in the house and calls for the wine-cooler, which is the other way an evening was governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of what followed went round the couches in order, a branch of myrtle passing from singer to singer in place of the lyre, and the games included kottabos, in which players flicked their wine lees at a target.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Athenian fine pottery takes its shapes from this room, and the comic poet Epicharmus set out where an evening could lead: a feast, a drinking-party, a revel, a swinery, a summons, a condemnation and a fine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Federica Cordano, “La percezione antica dell'Orientalizzante,” <i>Aristonothos. Rivista di Studi sul Mediterraneo Antico</i> 3 (2008): 1–22, https://doi.org/10.6092/2037-4488/368. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Symposium</i> 2.1, trans. O. J. Todd, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0212:text=Sym.:chapter=2:section=1. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Symposium</i> 176a–e, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Sym.:section=176a. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Symposium</i> 213e, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Sym.:section=213e. [Open access]","Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xcix–ci, 222, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Jorge Bravo, “Boys, Herms, and the Objectification of Desire on Athenian Sympotic Vases,” <i>Eugesta</i> 7 (2017), https://doi.org/10.54563/eugesta.499. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 275–76, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","history","greece","archaic greece","food","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Fresco_painting_from_lateral_walls_of_the_Tomb_of_the_Diver_depicting_a_symposium_scene%2C_5th_century_BC%2C_Paestum_Archaeological_Museum.jpg/1280px-Fresco_painting_from_lateral_walls_of_the_Tomb_of_the_Diver_depicting_a_symposium_scene%2C_5th_century_BC%2C_Paestum_Archaeological_Museum.jpg","title":"A symposium painted in the Tomb of the Diver","desc":"The long wall of a 5th-century BCE tomb at Paestum in southern Italy: three couches with low tables beneath them, one man playing the lyre, another the double pipes, and cups in several hands.","credit":"Photograph by Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fresco_painting_from_lateral_walls_of_the_Tomb_of_the_Diver_depicting_a_symposium_scene,_5th_century_BC,_Paestum_Archaeological_Museum.jpg","alt":"A wide painted wall showing three couches side by side, each holding one or two reclining men, with small tables underneath and musical instruments and drinking cups in their hands."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this not the banquet Homer describes?","a":"Because Homer's is eaten sitting on chairs, while the couched version was innovated in the East and reached the Greeks in the later 7th century BCE."},{"q":"Why did the number of couches decide the size of the party?","a":"Because guests were as many as there were couches, so the party stayed small, and one city could hold several at once, each admitting one group under strict rules."},{"q":"Why was the strength of the wine a matter for argument?","a":"Because it was drunk mixed with water and the proportion varied: one of water to two of wine was a toper's mixture, Anacreon urged ten to five, and half and half was thought to bring on madness."}]},
{"id":"gr-364","num":364,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"A guest handed the myrtle branch had to take up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> his neighbour had begun, and the name of these drinking songs means 'crooked' for reasons antiquity could never settle.","questions":["Athenaeus preserved a whole Athenian songbook of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which the most famous promises to carry a sword in a branch of myrtle as Harmodius and Aristogeiton did.","Plato quotes a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the <i>Gorgias</i> as a catch everyone had heard over their cups, ranking health first among the good things of life, then beauty, then honest wealth."],"answer":"skolion","answerText":"skolion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Harmodius song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">attributed to Callistratus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Attic collection</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before 450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"abstract":"A <b>skolion</b> was a song sung over the wine at a Greek drinking party, and its name means 'crooked', which the ancients themselves could not agree how to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some said the songs went round the room in a zigzag order, others that the melody was winding, others that the drinkers were too tipsy to manage what was easy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dicaearchus distinguished three kinds of banquet song: the paean the company sang after the libations, the songs each guest took up in turn as a myrtle branch went round in place of the lyre, and the harder ones left to the skilled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristophanes shows the drill taught in the <i>Wasps</i>, where the tables are spread, the libations poured and the piper finishes his prelude before the pupil must show how he takes up a song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The metre is nearly always logaoedic, and Terpander was called the inventor, though what he did was give an old rhythm a full accompaniment rather than make it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Attic skolia Athenaeus preserved were a songbook put together in Athens by aristocrats shortly before 450 BCE, improvised in manner, twisting a famous poem's thought or sketching a scene from a story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato quotes one in the <i>Gorgias</i> as a catch everyone had heard over their cups: health is best, then beauty, and third is wealth got without guile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The most celebrated is the Harmodius song, attributed by Hesychius to Callistratus and datable to about 500 BCE, which begins by promising to carry a sword in a branch of myrtle as Harmodius and Aristogeiton did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It repeats the popular Athenian belief that the Hipparchus those two killed in 514 BCE was the tyrant, which is the story Thucydides broke off his narrative to correct, insisting that the elder son Hippias held the tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Another mourns the exiles who held the rock of Leipsydrion against Peisistratus, and beside these popular songs skolia were written as art poetry by Alcaeus, Pindar, Timocreon and Aristotle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Weir Smyth, <i>Greek Melic Poets</i> (London: Macmillan and Co., 1900), xcv–ciii, 478–79, https://archive.org/details/greekmelicpoets00smytuoft. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 90, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 1216–22, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0044:card=1219. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=54. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Gorgias</i> 451e, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0178:text=Gorg.:section=451e. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","greece","archaic greece","poetry","music"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the ancients not explain the song's name?","a":"Because some said the songs went round the room in a zigzag order, others that the melody was winding, and others that the drinkers were too tipsy to manage what was easy."},{"q":"Why did the songs a company sang fall into kinds?","a":"Because Dicaearchus distinguished the paean sung after the libations, the songs each guest took up as a myrtle branch went round, and the harder ones left to the skilled."},{"q":"Why is the Harmodius song evidence Thucydides argued against?","a":"Because it repeats the popular Athenian belief that the Hipparchus killed in 514 BCE was the tyrant, where he insisted the elder son Hippias held the tyranny."}]},
{"id":"gr-365","num":365,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a story-writer and a fellow-slave of the courtesan Rhodopis under Iadmon the Samian, and records that the Delphians who killed him later had to pay for it.","questions":["Socrates spent his last days in prison turning the fables of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into verse, which Plato reports as very nearly the only poetry the philosopher ever wrote.","The beast fable is older than <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since Hesiod already has a hawk tell a captive nightingale that the strong take whom they please and only a fool resists."],"answer":"Aesop","answerText":"Aesop","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Floruit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 572 – 569 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the 52nd Olympiad</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">564 BCE, at Delphi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Life of Aesop</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st c. BCE – 2nd c. CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Aesop</b> is the name attached to the Greek beast fable, and what the ancient evidence suggests is a story-figure invented to provide the fables with an author rather than a writer whose works survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus, the earliest witness, calls him a story-writer and a slave of Iadmon the Samian, a fellow-slave of the courtesan Rhodopis, and says the Delphians killed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The killing left a trace that looks administrative: obeying an oracle, Delphi offered compensation to anyone who would take it, and only the grandson of Iadmon came forward to claim it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient chronographers gave him a floruit in the 52nd Olympiad and put his death at Delphi in 564 BCE, but everything else in the life comes from stories rather than from record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He is in any case not a poet, since the fable was a kind of story that anyone might put into verse or collect in prose, and tradition simply made him its author.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The form is older than the name, and Hesiod already tells one, in which a hawk seizes a nightingale and informs her that the strong take whom they please and only a fool resists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 422 BCE Aristophanes could raise a laugh from the Delphi story on stage, where an old man begins it and is cut off before he reaches the point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato has Socrates spend his last days in prison turning Aesop's fables into verse, which is the clearest measure of how thoroughly the stories were known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A fictional biography, the <i>Life of Aesop</i>, was put together between the 1st century BCE and the 2nd century CE, and its fullest version makes him a grotesquely ugly slave who is given the power of speech by a goddess and then outwits his philosopher master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The fables are anonymous popular matter rather than one man's work, and about a hundred of them touch on religion, with Zeus overshadowing the other gods and Hermes made the butt of the jokes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.134, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=134. [Open access]","Gilbert Murray, <i>A History of Ancient Greek Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1897), 89, https://archive.org/details/historyofancient00murr. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 1446–48, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0044:card=1446. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedo</i> 60c–d, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0170:text=Phaedo:section=60d. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Works and Days</i> 202–12, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0132:card=202. [Open access]","Ioannis M. Konstantakos, “Life of Aesop and Adventures of Criticism: A Review-Article on Manolis Papathomopoulos' Recent Edition of the Vita Aesopi, Version G,” <i>Myrtia</i> 28 (2013): 355–92, https://revistas.um.es/myrtia/article/view/188071. [Open access]","Michał Wojciechowski, “Gods, Religion and Its Criticism in the Greek Aesopic Fables,” <i>Studia Warmińskie</i> 60 (2023): 357–81, https://doi.org/10.31648/sw.8722. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","greece","archaic greece","storytelling"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/Aesop_and_fox--red-figure_cup--Greece--c_450_BC.jpg","title":"A man and a fox on a red-figure cup","desc":"The tondo of a Greek cup of about 450 BCE in the Vatican Museums: a bald, big-headed man wrapped in his mantle listens to a fox that seems to be talking to him. The man has been taken for Aesop since 1843, but nothing on the cup names him.","credit":"Unknown Greek artist, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aesop_and_fox--red-figure_cup--Greece--c_450_BC.jpg","alt":"The circular interior of a pottery cup, bordered with a meander pattern, showing a seated bearded man in a mantle facing a small fox that raises one paw towards him."},"why":[{"q":"Why is he read as an invented author rather than a writer?","a":"Because the fable was a kind of story anyone might put into verse or collect in prose, and the form is older than the name, Hesiod already telling one about the hawk and the nightingale."},{"q":"Why does the Delphic killing look like a matter of record?","a":"Because obeying an oracle Delphi offered compensation to anyone who would take it, and only the grandson of Iadmon came forward to claim it."},{"q":"Why is Socrates in prison good evidence about the fables?","a":"Because Plato has him spend his last days turning them into verse, which is the clearest measure of how thoroughly the stories were known."}]},
{"id":"gr-366","num":366,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Where Hesiod had generated the world by births and unions among gods, the thinkers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set out instead to explain it from principles inherent in the world itself.","questions":["Not one complete work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives, so the whole subject is built from quoted fragments and from later writers' reports of what each thinker is supposed to have held.","The questions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are what Socrates says he burned to answer as a young man: why each thing comes to be, and whether we think with blood or air or fire."],"answer":"Presocratic philosophy","answerText":"Presocratic philosophy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inquiry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Greek cities of Ionia and Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cited as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">DK, as in DK22B35</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Presocratic philosophy</b> is the modern name for the inquiry begun in the Greek cities during the 6th and 5th centuries BCE by thinkers whom antiquity recognised as the first philosophers and scientists of its tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What marks the work off is a commitment to explain the world naturalistically, out of principles inherent in the world itself, rather than by the doings of persons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hesiod is the contrast: his <i>Theogony</i> begins with Chaos, then wide-bosomed Earth, then Eros, and generates everything else by births and unions until the gods have divided their honours among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle, looking back, said that most of the earliest philosophers recognised only material principles, meaning that of which all things consist, from which they come and into which they are ultimately resolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato lets Socrates describe the inquiry from inside, as a youthful eagerness to know why each thing comes to be and perishes, and whether we think with blood or air or fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> These men belonged to a wider company of sages, and what set their wisdom apart was that its first literary form moved away from the poetry and music that had carried Greek thought until then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Not one complete work survives, so the subject is built from fragments, meaning passages that later writers quote, and from testimonia, meaning their reports of what a thinker held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standard collection numbers each thinker and sorts the evidence into testimonies and fragments, which is why a passage is cited in a form like DK22B35 rather than by any page.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Material does still turn up, and the Derveni Papyrus, found in 1962 and probably written late in the 4th century BCE, proved to be a commentary by someone who knew the philosophical theories of his day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The label is itself an 18th-century coinage made current in the 19th, and read strictly as chronology it fails, since the last of these thinkers were contemporaries of Socrates and even of Plato.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]","Hesiod, <i>Theogony</i> 116–38, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0130:card=116. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedo</i> 96a–b, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0170:text=Phaedo:section=96a. [Open access]","Livio Rossetti, “Introduction. La circulation du savoir à l'époque de Thalès (et peu après),” <i>Dialogues d'histoire ancienne</i> 48, no. 2 (2022): 15–23, https://www.persee.fr/doc/dha_0755-7256_2022_num_48_2_5249. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Derveni_Papyrus_-_Fragments_on_display_in_the_Archaelogical_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg/1280px-Derveni_Papyrus_-_Fragments_on_display_in_the_Archaelogical_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg","title":"The Derveni Papyrus on display","desc":"The charred fragments of the Derveni Papyrus, laid out in eight lit windows of a case in the Archaeological Museum of Thessaloniki. Found in 1962, it is the oldest surviving Greek manuscript and its author knew the philosophical theories of his own day.","credit":"Photograph by Gts-tg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Derveni_Papyrus_-_Fragments_on_display_in_the_Archaelogical_Museum_of_Thessaloniki.jpg","alt":"A dark museum display table with eight lit rectangular windows, each holding rows of small blackened papyrus scraps against a white backing."},"why":[{"q":"Why is Hesiod set against these thinkers rather than counted among them?","a":"Because his Theogony generates the world by births and unions among persons, where they undertook to explain it out of principles inherent in the world itself."},{"q":"Why is a passage of theirs cited by a code rather than a page?","a":"Because no complete work survives, so the standard collection numbers each thinker and sorts the evidence into testimonies and fragments, which is what a citation like DK22B35 names."},{"q":"Why does the label fail if read as chronology?","a":"Because the last of these thinkers were contemporaries of Socrates and even of Plato, and the term is an 18th-century coinage made current in the 19th."}]},
{"id":"gr-367","num":367,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Herodotus says a battle between Lydians and Medes broke off when day turned to night, a loss of daylight <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had foretold to the Ionians within the year.","questions":["Aristotle reports that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held the permanent entity to be water and the earth to float on it, though the reasons he gives for that view are his own guesses.","Plato tells how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell into a pit while watching the stars and was laughed at by a servant girl for missing what lay at his own feet."],"answer":"Thales","answerText":"Thales","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 580 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eclipse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dated 585 BCE by tradition</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Thales</b> of Miletus is the man Aristotle named as the first to inquire into nature in this way, and he seems to have lived around the beginning of the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle gives the doctrine plainly: the permanent entity is water, which is why Thales also held that the earth floats on water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reasons are Aristotle's own guesses rather than a report, and he offers that Thales had noticed that the nourishment of everything is moist and that seeds have a moist nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guesswork matters because the doxographers say Thales wrote no book, so there is nothing of his own against which Aristotle can be checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read closely, the water doctrine turns out to be two arguments rather than one: a biological claim that water is the source of life, and a physical claim that water holds the earth up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Greek <i>arche</i>, meaning 'first principle', is Aristotle's word and not Thales', who never said that things which perish turn back into water, a question the later Ionians were the first to raise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The famous stories are about his standing rather than his doctrine, and the oldest is in Herodotus: Thales foretold a loss of daylight to the Ionians, and when day turned to night in the middle of a battle the Lydians and Medes stopped fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tradition dates that eclipse to 585 BCE, but a prediction of the kind described was almost certainly beyond anything he could have done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus also credits him with a political proposal, that the Ionians set up a single council at Teos and count their own cities as no more than demes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plato has him fall into a pit while watching the stars, to a servant girl's laughter, and Aristotle has him corner the olive presses to show that a philosopher could be rich if he cared to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]","Ryszard Legutko, “Thales on Water,” <i>Peitho. Examina Antiqua</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): 81–90, https://doi.org/10.14746/pea.2017.1.4. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.74, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=74. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.170, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=170. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Theaetetus</i> 174a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Theaet.:section=174a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1259a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=1:section=1259a. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science","astronomy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg/1280px-NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg","title":"The Seven Sages, in a mosaic from Baalbek","desc":"Seven portrait roundels of the Sages of Greece, each labelled in Greek with a name and a maxim, set around the Muse Calliope. The mosaic dates to the 3rd century CE, and Thales was counted among the Seven in the ancient lists.","credit":"Photograph by Clemens Schmillen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg","alt":"A square mosaic floor panel with seven circular portraits of bearded men ranged around a larger central roundel of a young woman in a yellow robe, Greek lettering beside each face."},"why":[{"q":"Why cannot Aristotle's account of him be checked?","a":"Because the doxographers say he wrote no book, so there is nothing of his own to set against Aristotle's report, and the reasons offered are Aristotle's own guesses."},{"q":"Why is the doctrine of water two claims rather than one?","a":"Because it holds both a biological claim, that water is the source of life, and a physical one, that water holds the earth up."},{"q":"Why is the eclipse story doubted as a prediction?","a":"Because a prediction of the kind Herodotus describes was almost certainly beyond anything he could have done, whatever happened in the middle of that battle."}]},
{"id":"gr-368","num":368,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"The one surviving sentence of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> calls the first principle neither water nor any other familiar element but the indefinite, from which come all the heavens and the worlds in them.","questions":["Asked what holds the earth up, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that it stays put for want of any reason to move, sitting at the centre and related alike to every point of the edge.","Aristotle judged the earth-at-rest argument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ingenious but not true, while antiquity credited him with a sundial gnomon, a globe and the first map of land and sea."],"answer":"Anaximander","answerText":"Anaximander","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 545 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, after Thales</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aged 64</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 58th Olympiad</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Anaximander</b> of Miletus, son of Praxiades, was remembered as the successor of Thales, and he is the first Greek thinker of whom a sentence survives in something close to his own words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Simplicius, commenting on Aristotle more than a thousand years later, quotes him saying that the first principle is neither water nor any other of the familiar elements but the indefinite, <i>to apeiron</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From it come all the heavens and the worlds in them, and the things out of which things come to be are also those into which they pass away, as must be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then the clause everyone remembers: they give penalty and recompense to one another for their injustice, in accordance with the ordering of time, which even Simplicius called rather poetical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The picture is of a system that keeps itself in balance, in sharp contrast with the quarrelsome personified gods who interfere in the heavens and in human affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The balance shows in the measurements as well as the language, since the intervals between earth, fixed stars, moon and sun are equal and the sun is reckoned the same size as the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He asked what holds the earth up, and answered that it stays where it is for want of any reason to move, sitting at the centre and related alike to every point of the edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle, who preserves that argument, calls it ingenious but not true, and elsewhere counts Anaximander among those who must have had some conception of matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He is credited with the gnomon of a sundial, set up at Sparta to mark the solstices, with a globe, and with the first map to draw the outline of land and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herodotus is the earliest witness to what such maps looked like, and he laughs at them: drawn round as if turned by compasses, ringed by Ocean, with Asia and Europe made the same size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>De Caelo</i> 295b, trans. J. L. Stocks (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), https://archive.org/details/decaelo00aris. [Open access]","Maria Antonietta Salamone, “Equality and Justice in Early Greek Cosmologies: The Paradigm of the 'Line of the Horizon',” <i>Philosophy and Cosmology</i> 18 (2017): 22–31, http://ispcjournal.org/journals/2017/Salamone_Philosophy_and_Cosmology_vol_18.pdf. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.36, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=36. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 1069b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=12:section=1069b. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 2.1–2, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science","astronomy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/Anaximander_Mosaic.jpg","title":"A Roman mosaic identified as Anaximander","desc":"A floor mosaic of the early 3rd century CE from Johannisstrasse in Trier, showing a seated bearded philosopher with a sundial in his hands. It is identified as Anaximander, to whom antiquity credited the sundial gnomon.","credit":"Roman mosaic, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anaximander_Mosaic.jpg","alt":"A square mosaic panel with braided and chevron borders framing a round central picture of an elderly bearded man seated on a stool, holding a fan-shaped sundial."},"why":[{"q":"Why does his answer about the earth need no support beneath it?","a":"Because it stays where it is for want of any reason to move, sitting at the centre and related alike to every point of the edge."},{"q":"Why is his surviving sentence contrasted with the gods?","a":"Because it describes a system that keeps itself in balance, things giving penalty and recompense to one another in accordance with the ordering of time, rather than quarrelsome persons interfering."},{"q":"Why did Herodotus laugh at the maps of this kind?","a":"Because they were drawn round as if turned by compasses, ringed by Ocean, with Asia and Europe made the same size."}]},
{"id":"gr-369","num":369,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Where his predecessors had named a stuff and stopped, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> supplied a mechanism: air condensing into wind, cloud, water, earth and stone, and rarefying into fire.","questions":["Plutarch illustrates the theory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with breath, which comes out cool through pursed lips and warm through an open mouth, tying condensation to cooling.","Aristotle set <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside Diogenes as holding air to be prior to water and, of all the bodily elements, most truly the first principle of things."],"answer":"Anaximenes","answerText":"Anaximenes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 546 – 526 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, after Anaximander</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the 63rd Olympiad</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Anaximenes</b> of Miletus, son of Eurystratus and remembered as the pupil of Anaximander, was the third and last of the Milesian thinkers, and Apollodorus made him a contemporary of the fall of Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He put air where Anaximander had put the indefinite, which looks like a step back towards the water of Thales until you see what it bought him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Air is neutral enough to serve as a principle and yet has properties of its own, among them colour, temperature, humidity, motion, taste and smell, where the indefinite had almost none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what made possible the thing his predecessors never supplied, a stated mechanism by which one thing becomes another, and the mechanism is condensation and rarefaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rarefied, air becomes fire; condensed further and further it becomes wind, then cloud, then water, then earth, and at last stones, and the rest come to be from these.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch ties the two processes to cooling and heating with an example anyone can try, since breath through pursed lips comes out cool and through an open mouth comes out warm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle set him beside Diogenes of Apollonia as holding air to be prior to water and, of all the bodily elements, most truly the first principle of things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That framing is Aristotle's own, and it carries his assumption that the stuff persists underneath as a substratum, which the Milesians may never have held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read the other way about, the originating stuff is not inert matter but an active source of life, which is what the older Greek word for nature carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of the man himself little survives: he held that the stars go round the earth and never under it, and he is said to have written simply and unaffectedly in the Ionic dialect, which is prose rather than the verse the poets still used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 984a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=984a. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 2.3, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]","Nestor-Luis Cordero, “The Dynamic Conception of Being in the First Philosophers and the Notion of φύσις,” <i>Journal of Ancient Philosophy</i> 16, no. 2 (2022): 1–23, https://www.revistas.usp.br/filosofiaantiga/article/view/203154. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"why":[{"q":"Why is choosing air not simply a step backwards?","a":"Because air is neutral enough to serve as a principle and yet has properties of its own — colour, temperature, humidity, motion, taste and smell — where the indefinite had almost none."},{"q":"Why does that choice let him supply what his predecessors had not?","a":"Because a stuff with properties can be worked on, so he could state a mechanism by which one thing becomes another: condensation and rarefaction through fire, wind, cloud, water, earth and stone."},{"q":"Why is Aristotle's framing of him treated with caution?","a":"Because it carries his assumption that the stuff persists underneath as a substratum, which the Milesians may never have held; read otherwise the originating stuff is an active source of life."}]},
{"id":"gr-370","num":370,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes together make up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the doxographers who arranged them as a master-and-pupil succession may have been tidying rather than reporting.","questions":["The thinkers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew no line between the questions we would call scientific and those we would call philosophical, and pursued both with the same methods.","The city that housed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell to the Persians in 494 BCE, and Athens later fined Phrynichus a thousand drachmas for putting that loss on the stage."],"answer":"Milesian school","answerText":"Milesian school","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 526 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Thales, Anaximander, Anaximenes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Miletus fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Milesian school</b> is the name given to three thinkers of a single Ionian city, Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes, with whom Greek philosophy is conventionally begun, all three of them active during the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doxographers made them a succession, Anaximander the pupil of Thales and Anaximenes the pupil of Anaximander, and ran the same line on through Anaxagoras as far as Socrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That master-and-pupil chain may not be true, but their views resemble one another closely enough to justify treating the three of them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What each did was name an originating stuff, water and then the indefinite and then air, and account for the world out of it, which is the pattern Aristotle later described as recognising only material principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The framing is his, and reading them as committed to one stuff persisting underneath as a substratum presumes a theory of change that had not yet been formulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What they plainly did share was a refusal to call in help from outside the natural world, so that the account of the cosmos is complete in its own terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They drew no line between the questions we would call scientific and those we would call philosophical, and saw no need for distinct methods to answer them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their appetite for measuring and explaining what happens in the sky and on the ground was as strong as their interest in causes and principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miletus itself was both the setting and part of the reason, a city at the height of its wealth that Herodotus calls the glory of Ionia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persians took it in 494 BCE, mining the walls and enslaving the people, and when Phrynichus put the fall on the Athenian stage the whole theatre wept and the city fined him a thousand drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.28, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=28. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.18, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=18. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=21. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 1.13, 2.1–3, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 983b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=983b. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Milet_2013-03-25q.jpg/1280px-Milet_2013-03-25q.jpg","title":"The site of Miletus","desc":"The city Herodotus calls the glory of Ionia now lies inland among standing water and scattered ruins. Everything left above ground is later than the three thinkers, the archaic city having been destroyed in 494 BCE.","credit":"Photograph by Rabe!, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milet_2013-03-25q.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a flat green site scattered with low stone ruins and shallow pools of standing water, with a cultivated plain and hazy blue hills beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why are the three treated together despite the doubtful succession?","a":"Because although the master-and-pupil chain the doxographers drew may not be true, their views resemble one another closely enough to justify it."},{"q":"Why did they draw no line between science and philosophy?","a":"Because they saw no need for distinct methods, their appetite for measuring and explaining what happens in the sky and on the ground being as strong as their interest in causes."},{"q":"Why does the city itself belong in the account?","a":"Because Miletus was at the height of its wealth and Herodotus calls it the glory of Ionia, until the Persians took it in 494 BCE, mining the walls and enslaving the people."}]},
{"id":"gr-371","num":371,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Born on Samos about 570 BCE and settled at about forty in Croton in southern Italy, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded a strict way of life that his followers were still keeping generations later.","questions":["The early evidence makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> famous as an expert on the soul's fate, an authority on ritual and a wonder-worker, and not at all as the mathematician of the modern picture.","Xenophanes, a contemporary, has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stop the beating of a puppy because he recognised in its yelping the soul of a friend of his."],"answer":"Pythagoras","answerText":"Pythagoras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Samos, then Croton</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emigrated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at about 40</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Pythagoras</b> is among the most famous of Greek philosophers and among the least securely known, since he wrote nothing and no contemporary left a detailed account of his thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He grew up on Samos and moved at about forty to Croton in southern Italy, where most of his activity took place, and he lived from about 570 to about 490 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later antiquity turned him into a semi-divine figure who had originated everything true in Greek philosophy, and treatises were forged in his name to prove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Getting behind that glorification is what scholars call the Pythagorean question, and the way through it is to prefer the earliest evidence to the later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that evidence his fame rested on none of what the modern picture gives him: he was an expert on the fate of the soul, an authority on ritual, a wonder-worker with a golden thigh, and the founder of a strict way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest surviving word about him is his contemporary Xenophanes', who has him stop the beating of a puppy because he recognised in its yelping the soul of a friend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells how the Thracian Salmoxis was said to have been his slave on Samos and to have taught his countrymen that they would never die, and calls Pythagoras one of the greatest Greek teachers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus also reports a rule about burial, that nothing woollen may go into the grave, which he says is called Orphic and Bacchic but is in fact Egyptian and Pythagorean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato says he was honoured above all for a way of living, and that his successors were still known in Plato's own day for keeping to the Pythagorean life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As for the theorem, there is evidence that he valued relationships between numbers of that kind, but it is not likely that he proved it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Carl Huffman, “Pythagoras,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2024 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2024/entries/pythagoras/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.6, 8.36, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.95, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=95. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=81. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 600b, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0168:book=10:section=600b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Pythagoras_in_the_Roman_Forum%2C_Colosseum.jpg/1280px-Pythagoras_in_the_Roman_Forum%2C_Colosseum.jpg","title":"A marble head identified as Pythagoras","desc":"A Roman copy of a Greek original of the 2nd or 1st century BCE, conventionally called Pythagoras. No likeness made in his lifetime survives, and a portrait of that date belongs to the tradition that had already remade him.","credit":"Photograph by Szilas, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pythagoras_in_the_Roman_Forum,_Colosseum.jpg","alt":"A white marble bust of a bearded man wearing a thick cloth turban wound round his head, seen against a plain ochre wall."},"why":[{"q":"Why is what he thought so hard to recover?","a":"Because he wrote nothing and no contemporary left a detailed account, while later antiquity made him semi-divine and forged treatises in his name to prove it."},{"q":"Why does the earliest evidence give a different man from the modern picture?","a":"Because on it his fame rested on the fate of the soul, on ritual authority, on wonder-working and on founding a strict way of life, rather than on mathematics."},{"q":"Why is the theorem not credited to him on this evidence?","a":"Because although there is evidence that he valued relationships between numbers of that kind, it is not likely that he proved it."}]},
{"id":"gr-372","num":372,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Since ten was the complete number, the astronomers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needed ten revolving bodies, and with only nine visible they supplied a counter-earth for the tenth.","questions":["Aristotle's phrase 'the so-called Pythagoreans' both records what the adherents of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called and quietly doubts their connection with the man himself.","The followers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split into those who kept the rules handed down as things heard and those who pursued mathematics, each denying the other was the genuine article."],"answer":"Pythagoreanism","answerText":"Pythagoreanism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 350 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Greek cities of Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Opposites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 pairs, per Aristotle</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pythagoreanism</b> covers several different things, and keeping them apart is most of the difficulty: the way of life Pythagoras taught, the philosophy of a group active in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE, and a later habit of calling almost any earlier thinker a Pythagorean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle writes of 'the so-called Pythagoreans', which records what that group was called and at the same time questions their connection with the man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He names none of them, but the philosophy he assigns them is close to what survives of Philolaus of Croton, who made limiters and unlimiteds his first principles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their claim, as Aristotle reports it, is that the principles of mathematics are the principles of everything and that numbers are the ultimate things in the physical universe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They found analogues in number for justice, for soul or mind and for opportunity, and they saw that the ratios of the musical scales are numbers as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The method shows plainest in their astronomy: ten being the complete number, the revolving bodies had to be ten, and with only nine visible they supplied a counter-earth for the tenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One group among them set out ten pairs of principles, among them limit and unlimited, odd and even, right and left, male and female, light and darkness, and good and evil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato names them just once, for holding that harmony and astronomy are kindred sciences, the ears being framed for the movements of harmony as the eyes are for the heavens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The communities themselves split in two, the acusmatici keeping rules handed down as things heard, among them not to stir a fire with a knife and to abstain from beans, and the mathematici pursuing mathematics and the study of nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Each side denied the other was truly Pythagorean, and the communities died out around the middle of the 4th century BCE, having given women an unusually large part for an ancient school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Carl Huffman, “Pythagoreanism,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Summer 2024 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2024/entries/pythagoreanism/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 985b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=985b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986a. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 530d, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0168:book=7:section=530d. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.17–19, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]"],"tags":["school of thought","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Gaffurio_Pythagoras.png/1280px-Gaffurio_Pythagoras.png","title":"The harmonic ratios, in a woodcut of 1492","desc":"Four panels from Franchino Gaffurio’s <i>Theorica musicae</i>, with numbered bells, glasses, monochord strings and pipes, and Philolaus named in the last. It is a late witness to the doctrine rather than an ancient one, printed nearly two thousand years after the school it depicts.","credit":"Woodcut from Gaffurio, Theorica musicae, 1492, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaffurio_Pythagoras.png","alt":"A black and white woodcut in four panels showing robed men striking bells, glasses, strings and pipes, each object marked with a number such as 16, 12, 9, 8, 6 or 4."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Aristotle write of the 'so-called' Pythagoreans?","a":"Because the phrase records what the group was called while at the same time questioning their connection with the man himself."},{"q":"Why did their astronomy require a body nobody could see?","a":"Because ten being the complete number the revolving bodies had to be ten, and with only nine visible they supplied a counter-earth for the tenth."},{"q":"Why did the communities split in two?","a":"Because the acusmatici kept rules handed down as things heard while the mathematici pursued mathematics and the study of nature, and each side denied the other was truly Pythagorean."}]},
{"id":"gr-373","num":373,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"It was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who noticed that Ethiopians make their gods snub-nosed and black while Thracians make theirs red-haired, and that horses with hands would draw horse-shaped gods.","questions":["Aristotle preserves the argument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that calling the gods born is as impious as calling them mortal, since either way there was a time when they did not exist.","By his own reckoning <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent 67 years tossing his cares up and down the land of Greece, having set out at 25, which makes him about 92 when he wrote it."],"answer":"Xenophanes","answerText":"Xenophanes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Floruit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">540 – 537 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the 60th Olympiad</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wandered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">67 years, from age 25</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Xenophanes</b> of Colophon was a travelling poet of the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE who turned his verse on the gods, and who says he spent 67 years tossing his cares up and down the land of Greece after setting out at 25.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He is remembered above all for a criticism of anthropomorphism in religion: mortals suppose that gods are born, wear their own clothes, and have a voice and a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ethiopians make their gods snub-nosed and black and Thracians make theirs blue-eyed and red-haired, and if horses and oxen had hands to draw with, their gods would come out looking like horses and oxen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The complaint against the poets is moral as well: Homer and Hesiod had given the gods everything that counts as a reproach among men, theft and adultery and mutual deceit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle preserves a sharper argument, that calling the gods born is as impious as calling them mortal, since either way there was a time when they did not exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In place of all that he set one god greatest among gods and men, not at all like mortals in body or in thought, which reads as monotheism until you notice the plural in the same line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius reports him holding that the substance of god is spherical and in no way resembles a man, is all eye and all ear but does not breathe, and is the whole of mind and thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was an observer too, and fossil sea creatures found inland at Syracuse, Paros and Malta gave him the ground for a cycle in which the earth is drowned and dried out again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About knowledge he was as careful as about the gods, holding that no mortal will ever know anything about them with certainty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later readers made him the founder of Eleatic philosophy on the strength of brief remarks by Plato and Aristotle, though Aristotle also says he gave no definite teaching at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Lesher, “Xenophanes,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Summer 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2023/entries/xenophanes/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.18–20, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Sophist</i> 242d, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Soph.:section=242d. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Rhetoric</i> 2.23, trans. J. H. Freese, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0060:book=2:chapter=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","poetry","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Xenophanes_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg","title":"An imagined Xenophanes, engraved in 1655","desc":"A plate from Thomas Stanley’s <i>History of Philosophy</i>, showing a bearded man in a turban leaning on a staff. No ancient portrait of Xenophanes is known, and this belongs to the long habit of illustrating philosophers nobody had seen.","credit":"Engraving from Thomas Stanley, The History of Philosophy, 1655, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xenophanes_in_Thomas_Stanley_History_of_Philosophy.jpg","alt":"A finely hatched engraving of an elderly bearded man in a turban and heavy draped robes, leaning on a long staff in an open landscape, with his name lettered beneath."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the horses and oxen make the argument rather than illustrate it?","a":"Because they show the gods a people describes to be a projection: Ethiopians make theirs snub-nosed and black and Thracians blue-eyed and red-haired, so hands to draw with would give oxen gods like oxen."},{"q":"Why is calling a god born as impious as calling one mortal?","a":"Because either way there was a time when the god did not exist, which is the sharper argument Aristotle preserves against the poets' theogonies."},{"q":"Why is reading him as a monotheist not straightforward?","a":"Because the line setting one god not at all like mortals calls it greatest among gods and men, so the plural stands in the same sentence."}]},
{"id":"gr-374","num":374,"category":"Archaic art, verse and thought","question":"Writing at Ephesus about 500 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the unity of opposites his central idea and wrote the first surviving Greek text to use kosmos for the world.","questions":["Sea is the purest water and the most polluted, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, being drinkable and healthy for fish but undrinkable and harmful for men.","The one book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was deposited in the temple of Artemis and made deliberately obscure, so that none but adepts should approach it."],"answer":"Heraclitus","answerText":"Heraclitus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Ephesus, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1, in 3 discourses</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Heraclitus</b> of Ephesus was active around 500 BCE and wrote a single book, which he is said to have deposited in the temple of Artemis and to have made deliberately obscure so that none but adepts should approach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius says it was divided into three discourses, on the universe, on politics and on theology, and Theophrastus, who had read it, thought parts of it half-finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The most famous thing he is supposed to have said is that you cannot step twice into the same river, but that is Plato's paraphrase rather than his own words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The one river fragment carrying the density of his style puts the emphasis differently: on those stepping into the same rivers, other and other waters flow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a claim about how a thing stays the same, since a body of water is a river precisely because its water changes, and stop the flow and you have a lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Flux is a special case of a wider idea, the unity of opposites, which he illustrates plainly: sea is the purest water and the most polluted, healthy for fish and harmful for men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His cosmology fits in a sentence, and it is the first surviving Greek text to use <i>kosmos</i> for the world: this world-order, the same for all, no god or man made, but ever was and is and will be, everliving fire kindled in measures and quenched in measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle set him beside Hippasus as making fire the first principle, and elsewhere beside Empedocles among those who held that destruction alternates without end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That second report sits badly with the fragment itself, which has the world always existing while only portions of it are transformed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belonged to no school and implies he taught himself, and he has been called a material monist and a process philosopher, an empiricist and a mystic, the first real philosopher and an obscurantist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Daniel W. Graham, “Heraclitus,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Winter 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2023/entries/heraclitus/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Cratylus</i> 402a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Crat.:section=402a. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.5–6, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>De Caelo</i> 279b, trans. J. L. Stocks (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), https://archive.org/details/decaelo00aris. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 984a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=984a. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Filosofo_detto_eraclito%2C_da_villa_dei_papiri%2C_peristilio_quadrato.JPG/1280px-Filosofo_detto_eraclito%2C_da_villa_dei_papiri%2C_peristilio_quadrato.JPG","title":"A bronze bust long called Heraclitus","desc":"Found in the Villa of the Papyri at Herculaneum and now in Naples, inv. 5623. The museum says the identification remains problematic: the piece was once taken for Heraclitus and is now more often thought to be Empedocles.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Filosofo_detto_eraclito,_da_villa_dei_papiri,_peristilio_quadrato.JPG","alt":"A dark bronze bust of an elderly bearded man with a deeply lined brow and hollow eye sockets, wearing a wreath and a draped cloak, on a pale stone plinth."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the most famous saying attributed to him not his words?","a":"Because 'you cannot step twice into the same river' is Plato's paraphrase; his own fragment says that on those stepping into the same rivers, other and other waters flow."},{"q":"Why is the river an argument about sameness rather than change?","a":"Because a body of water is a river precisely because its water changes: stop the flow and what remains is a lake."},{"q":"Why does Aristotle's report of an alternating destruction sit badly with the fragment?","a":"Because the fragment has the world always existing, no god or man having made it, while only portions of it are transformed in measures."}]},
{"id":"gr-375","num":375,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"Almost everything we have of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives because Simplicius, writing a thousand years later, copied 52 verses into a commentary and explained that the original had become scarce.","questions":["A goddess promises <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two things in his poem: the unshaken heart of well-rounded reality, and the notions of mortals, in which there is no genuine trustworthiness.","Plato says that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> protested all his life, in prose and in verse, that one must never let the thought prevail that not-being is."],"answer":"Parmenides","answerText":"Parmenides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 515 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Elea, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Poem</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 verses, 160 surviving</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Parmenides</b> was a philosopher of Elea, a Greek colony on the Tyrrhenian coast of southern Italy, born about 515 BCE by an inference from Plato, who has him at about 65 meeting a Socrates of about twenty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius calls him the son of Pyres and a pupil of Xenophanes who followed him in nothing, and says it was a Pythagorean named Ameinias who drew him to the life of study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Speusippus, Plato’s successor at the Academy, reported that he drew up the laws for his own city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A portrait head found at Elea in the 1960s carries an inscription naming him Parmeneides, son of Pyres, Ouliadēs, Natural Philosopher, and tying him to a cult of Apollo the Healer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote one work, a poem in the hexameters of epic and perhaps eight hundred verses long, of which about 160 survive, from a single word to the 62 lines of fragment 8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It opens on a chariot ride to a goddess who promises two things: the unshaken heart of well-rounded reality, and the notions of mortals, in which there is no genuine trustworthiness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first is an argument that What Is is ungenerated and deathless, whole and uniform, still and perfect, complete from every side like the bulk of a well-rounded globe, a unity Aristotle read as one in definition and therefore finite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The other road is refused outright, and Plato quotes the refusal: never let this thought prevail, that not-being is, but keep your mind from this way of investigation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The longer second half was an ordinary cosmology of heavens, sun, moon, stars, earth and living creatures, and it is the part we know least about, since later readers quoted the metaphysics and let the rest go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That the metaphysics survives whole is owed to Simplicius, who transcribed 52 of its verses in the 6th century CE and gave his reason: the scarcity of Parmenides’ treatise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["John Palmer, “Parmenides,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2025 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2025/entries/parmenides/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Parmenides</i> 127b, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Parm.:section=127b. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.21–23, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Sophist</i> 237a, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Soph.:section=237a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Busto_di_Parmenide.jpg/1280px-Busto_di_Parmenide.jpg","title":"The inscribed herm from Elea","desc":"A marble head on a pillar cut with his name and titles, found at Elea in the 1960s and shown at the archaeological park of Velia. The carving is Roman work of the 1st century CE, five centuries after him, so it is an idea of Parmenides rather than a likeness.","credit":"Photograph by Sergio Spolti, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busto_di_Parmenide.jpg","alt":"A weathered marble portrait head of a bearded man mounted on a tall rectangular pillar, against a rough stone wall, with lines of Greek capitals carved on the pillar."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the second half of his poem the part least known?","a":"Because it was an ordinary cosmology of heavens, sun, moon, stars, earth and living creatures, and later readers quoted the metaphysics and let the rest go."},{"q":"Why does the metaphysics survive whole at all?","a":"Because Simplicius transcribed 52 of its verses in the 6th century CE and gave his reason: the scarcity of the treatise."},{"q":"Why does the goddess offer two things rather than one?","a":"Because she promises the unshaken heart of well-rounded reality and also the notions of mortals, in which there is no genuine trustworthiness."}]},
{"id":"gr-376","num":376,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"The book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is usually called a defence of Parmenidean monism, but Plato has him deny that and call it a young man’s polemic, stolen before he could publish it.","questions":["Aristotle preserves four arguments by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against motion, among them the flying arrow that is motionless and the quickest runner who never overtakes the slowest.","Plato calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Eleatic Palamedes, whose art can make the same things appear to his hearers alike and unlike, one and many, at rest and in motion."],"answer":"Zeno of Elea","answerText":"Zeno of Elea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Elea, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Floruit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">464 – 460 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Zeno of Elea</b> was born about 490 BCE, a date got from Plato’s dialogue, which brings him to Athens at nearly 40 in the company of Parmenides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius calls him the son of Teleutagoras and the adopted son of Parmenides, says he preferred his own small city to Athens, and reports that Aristotle called him the inventor of dialectic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His life story is mostly a plot against a local tyrant, told several ways, in which he bit off the man’s ear, or his nose, and held on until he was stabbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote one book of arguments, and the account of it as a defence of Parmenidean monism is what the young Socrates guesses in Plato’s dialogue and what Plato’s Zeno immediately denies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a support for Parmenides against people jeering at him, Zeno says, paying them back with interest, written in a spirit of controversy when he was young and stolen before he could decide whether to publish it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Every argument in it aims to show that things are not many, and the pattern is an antinomy: if existences are many, they must be both like and unlike, which is impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plato elsewhere calls him the Eleatic Palamedes, whose art can make the same things appear to his hearers alike and unlike, one and many, at rest and in motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The four arguments against motion survive only in Aristotle’s summary: the half-way point must be reached first, the quickest runner never overtakes the slowest, the flying arrow is motionless, and two rows pass each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle names no tortoise, only the quickest runner and the slowest, though a cup painted in Italy about a century earlier shows a runner outstripping a large one and is taken for the earliest response to the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls the reasoning fallacious, the arrow assuming that time is made of indivisible moments and the half-way argument assuming that an unlimited number of points cannot be passed in a limited time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["John Palmer, “Zeno of Elea,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Summer 2025 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2025/entries/zeno-elea/. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Parmenides</i> 127e–128e, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Parm.:section=127e. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.25–29, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Physics</i> 6.9, 239b, trans. R. P. Hardie and R. K. Gaye, in <i>The Works of Aristotle Translated into English</i>, vol. 2, ed. W. D. Ross (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1930), https://archive.org/details/works0002aris. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedrus</i> 261d, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0174:text=Phaedrus:section=261d. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Zeno_of_Elea_Tibaldi_or_Carducci_Escorial.jpg/1280px-Zeno_of_Elea_Tibaldi_or_Carducci_Escorial.jpg","title":"Zeno at the doors of Truth and Falsity","desc":"A fresco of the 1590s in the library of the Escorial, lettered ZENON HELEATES, in which he points a crowd of young men at two doorways marked <i>Falsitas</i> and <i>Veritas</i>. No ancient portrait of him is known, and the scene is an emblem of dialectic rather than anything he is recorded as doing.","credit":"Fresco by Pellegrino Tibaldi, El Escorial, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeno_of_Elea_Tibaldi_or_Carducci_Escorial.jpg","alt":"A wide painted frieze in which an elderly bald man in an orange robe stretches out both arms toward two dark doorways lettered Falsitas and Veritas, while a press of half-draped young men surges after him."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the standard account of his book's purpose uncertain?","a":"Because a defence of Parmenidean monism is what the young Socrates guesses in Plato's dialogue and what Plato's Zeno immediately denies, calling it a payment with interest to men jeering at Parmenides."},{"q":"Why does every argument take the form of an antinomy?","a":"Because each aims to show that things are not many, arguing that if existences are many they must be both like and unlike, which is impossible."},{"q":"Why does Aristotle call the arrow argument fallacious?","a":"Because it assumes that time is made of indivisible moments, as the half-way argument assumes that an unlimited number of points cannot be passed in a limited time."}]},
{"id":"gr-377","num":377,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"Parmenides, Zeno and Melissus are counted together as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, although the last of the three was a Samian, and not an Eleate at all.","questions":["Plato is the first to speak of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a group, calling it a sect in his own region and starting it from Xenophanes and even earlier.","Aristotle dismisses the two chief members of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as contentious reasoners whose premisses are false and whose conclusions do not follow."],"answer":"Eleatic school","answerText":"Eleatic school","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 540 – 440 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Elea, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Members</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, of whom 2 were Eleates</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Eleatic school</b> is the name given to a group of thinkers linked to Elea, a Greek colony on the coast of southern Italy, whose common thesis is that what there is, is one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plato is the earliest to treat them as a group, calling them the Eleatic sect in our region and starting them from Xenophanes and even earlier, and elsewhere bringing on a stranger from Elea as one of the followers of Parmenides and Zeno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The membership antiquity settled on is Parmenides, Zeno and Melissus, with Xenophanes attached at the head of the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two of those four were not Eleates at all: Xenophanes was a wandering poet from Colophon in Ionia, and Melissus was a Samian whom his countrymen elected admiral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The succession is thinner than it looks, since Aristotle reports only that Parmenides is said to have been Xenophanes’ disciple, and Diogenes Laertius says Parmenides was instructed by him but was no follower of his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The one doctrine they are credited with covers real disagreements, and Aristotle separates the versions: for Parmenides what is one is one in definition and therefore finite, for Melissus it is materially one and therefore unlimited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diogenes gives Melissus his own summary, a universe unlimited, unchangeable, immovable, one and uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle handles the pair roughly, calling both of them contentious reasoners whose premisses are false and whose conclusions do not follow, and Melissus’ argument gross and palpable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The label travelled further than the doctrine, and Diogenes has even the atomist Leucippus born at Elea, though some say Abdera and others Miletus, and made a pupil of Zeno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the group really did was set the problem the next generation had to solve, since after it any account of a changing world of many things had to say first how change and plurality were possible at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plato, <i>Sophist</i> 216a, 242d, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0172:text=Soph.:section=216a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Physics</i> 1.2–3, 185a–186a, trans. R. P. Hardie and R. K. Gaye, in <i>The Works of Aristotle Translated into English</i>, vol. 2, ed. W. D. Ross (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1930), https://archive.org/details/works0002aris. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.21–30, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Patricia Curd, “Presocratic Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2023 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2023/entries/presocratics/. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","philosophy","greece","archaic greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Velia_Porta_Rosa_Esterno_P1170089.JPG/1280px-Velia_Porta_Rosa_Esterno_P1170089.JPG","title":"The Porta Rosa at Elea","desc":"A stone arch over the road that crossed the city now called Velia, in southern Italy, which gives the school its name. Two of the four thinkers grouped under that name were not Eleates at all: Xenophanes came from Colophon in Ionia and Melissus from Samos.","credit":"Photograph by Crompippo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Velia_Porta_Rosa_Esterno_P1170089.JPG","alt":"A round-headed stone arch standing in a gap between two overgrown earth banks, with a grassy track leading up to it and bright sky beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the name of the group a poor guide to its membership?","a":"Because two of the four were not Eleates at all: Xenophanes was a wandering poet from Colophon in Ionia, and Melissus was a Samian whom his countrymen elected admiral."},{"q":"Why does the one doctrine they share cover a disagreement?","a":"Because Aristotle separates the versions: for Parmenides what is one is one in definition and therefore finite, for Melissus materially one and therefore unlimited."},{"q":"Why did the group matter to the philosophers who came after?","a":"Because it set the problem they had to solve, since after it any account of a changing world of many things had to say first how change and plurality were possible at all."}]},
{"id":"gr-378","num":378,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"The story that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leapt into the craters of Etna to prove his own divinity is the one part of his life we can fairly confidently rule out.","questions":["Everything is made of four roots moved by Love and Strife, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who named the roots after gods rather than after fire, earth, air and water.","Homer and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have nothing in common but the metre, Aristotle wrote, so that one should be called a poet and the other a scientist."],"answer":"Empedocles","answerText":"Empedocles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 484 – 424 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Acragas, in Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Poems</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, or perhaps 1</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Empedocles</b> was a Sicilian of Acragas, born early in the 5th century BCE to an aristocratic family whose grandfather had kept horses and won at Olympia, and Aristotle says he died at 60.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wrote in the hexameters of epic, and antiquity knew two poems under his name, <i>On Nature</i> and the <i>Purifications</i>, though whether that is two works or one is now argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A papyrus at Strasbourg published in 1999 carries lines of <i>On Nature</i> that editors had long assigned to the <i>Purifications</i>, making the physics and the religion harder to pull apart than they looked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle could not settle what to call him, and in the <i>Poetics</i> says that Homer and Empedocles have nothing in common but the metre, so that one should be called a poet and the other a scientist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was remembered as a doctor and an orator besides, and Plato has Socrates explain colour by the effluences and passages that Empedocles held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The physics makes everything out of four roots, named as gods rather than as fire, earth, air and water, moved by two forces, Love drawing them into one and Strife pulling them apart, in a cycle without end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle credits him with being the first to make the material elements four, and the first to make the source of motion two contrary forces instead of one, while complaining that he uses neither consistently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own verses greet his fellow citizens as an immortal god, no longer mortal, crowned with ribbons and garlands and followed by thousands who want prophecies or a cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of that grew the story everybody knows, that he threw himself into the craters of Etna to make good the claim, and was found out when the mountain threw up one of his bronze sandals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius gives it as one report among several, on the authority of Hippobotus, and the leap is the one part of his life that can be ruled out with any confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["K. Scarlett Kingsley and Richard Parry, “Empedocles,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Winter 2024 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2024/entries/empedocles/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.62, 8.69, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 985a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=985a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1447b, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1447b. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Meno</i> 76c, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0178:text=Meno:section=76c. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","philosophy","greece","science","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Agrigento%2C_Valley_of_the_Temples_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Agrigento%2C_Valley_of_the_Temples_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The temple ridge at Acragas","desc":"The Greek city of Acragas in Sicily, now Agrigento, with the Temple of Concordia standing on the ridge. Empedocles opens by greeting the people who dwell in the great city beside the yellow Acragas, on the lofty citadel.","credit":"Photograph by Maarten Sepp, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrigento,_Valley_of_the_Temples_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A long dry ridge covered in olive trees and scattered blocks of fallen stone, with a small honey-coloured Doric temple standing at its far end and a modern town on the hills beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the division of his work into two poems now argued over?","a":"Because a papyrus published in 1999 carries lines of On Nature that editors had long assigned to the Purifications, making the physics and the religion harder to pull apart than they looked."},{"q":"Why could Aristotle not decide what to call him?","a":"Because he wrote in the hexameters of epic while doing natural philosophy, so the Poetics says he and Homer have nothing in common but the metre and one should be called a poet, the other a scientist."},{"q":"Why did the story of the leap into Etna grow up around him?","a":"Because his own verses greet his fellow citizens as an immortal god crowned with ribbons, and the tale makes him prove the claim; it is the one part of his life that can be ruled out with confidence."}]},
{"id":"gr-379","num":379,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"I write these things as they seem to me to be true, wrote <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for the tales told by the Greeks are, as it appears to me, many and absurd.","questions":["At Thebes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> traced his descent to a god in the sixteenth generation, and the priests walked him past 345 wooden statues, father to son, with no god at the end.","Alone in Aristagoras’ council <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opposed the Ionian revolt, listing all the nations subject to Darius, and then advised seizing the temple treasure to buy command of the sea."],"answer":"Hecataeus of Miletus","answerText":"Hecataeus of Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Miletus, in Ionia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, both lost</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Hecataeus of Miletus</b> was a Greek writer of the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE, the son of Hegesander, working in prose at a time when nearly everything worth saying was said in verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Demetrius quotes his opening, and it is a remarkable way to begin a book: Hecataeus of Miletus thus relates — I write these things as they seem to me to be true, for the tales told by the Greeks are, as it appears to me, many and absurd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Antiquity knew a prose history under his name and a description of the earth, and nothing of either survives except in later quotation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo, following Eratosthenes, puts him with Anaximander as the two who came after Homer in geography, saying that Anaximander first published a chart and that Hecataeus left a work identifiable from his other writings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Heraclitus, a contemporary, put him in a short list of men whose reading had taught them nothing: much learning does not teach understanding, else it would have taught Hesiod and Pythagoras, and again Xenophanes and Hecataeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus names him four times and never twice in the same relation, which is a fair measure of how one writer used another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Twice he is the adviser at Miletus, alone in opposing the Ionian revolt after listing the nations Darius ruled, and afterwards telling Aristagoras to fortify Leros and wait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once he is corrected: at Thebes he traced his own descent to a god in the sixteenth generation, and the priests walked him past 345 wooden statues, each the son of the one before, with no god at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Once he is an authority, cited for the claim that Athens drove out the Pelasgians unjustly, after which Herodotus gives the Athenians’ own account instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> And in the middle of all this Herodotus laughs at the mapmakers who draw the world as round as if made with compasses, with the Ocean river running about it, and names nobody at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.36, 5.125, 6.137, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=36. [Open access]","Demetrius, <i>On Style</i> 12, ed. and trans. W. Rhys Roberts (Cambridge: at the University Press, 1902), https://archive.org/details/demetriusonstyle01deme. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 1.1.11, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=1:chapter=1:section=11. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 9.1, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.143, 4.36, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=143. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","geography","greece","archaic greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg/1280px-Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg","title":"The world according to Hecataeus","desc":"A reconstruction engraved for Edward Bunbury’s <i>A History of Ancient Geography</i> in 1879, drawn from the fragments. No map of his survives, and the shape here — a disc with the Ocean running round it — is the one Herodotus laughed at.","credit":"Engraving from Edward Bunbury, A History of Ancient Geography, London: John Murray, 1879, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bunbury_Vol_1_Map_02_Hecataeus_p_148.jpg","alt":"An engraved circular world map lettered THE WORLD ACCORDING TO HECATAEUS, with a hatched band of Oceanus round the rim and the land divided into Europa, Asia and Libya."},"why":[{"q":"Why is his opening sentence remarkable for its date?","a":"Because it is prose at a time when nearly everything worth saying was said in verse, and it names its author and says the tales told by the Greeks are many and absurd."},{"q":"Why is the story of the Theban statues a lesson about his method?","a":"Because he traced his descent to a god in the sixteenth generation, and the priests walked him past 345 wooden statues, each the son of the one before, with no god at the end."},{"q":"Why is Herodotus's use of him hard to summarise?","a":"Because he names him four times and never twice in the same relation — twice the adviser at Miletus, once corrected at Thebes, once an authority whose claim is then answered."}]},
{"id":"gr-380","num":380,"category":"Archaic Greek culture","question":"The reputation of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the father of anatomy rests on the excision of one eyeball, and on discoveries made by a writer two centuries after him.","questions":["Health is the equality of the powers in the body and disease the sole rule of one of them, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, borrowing the language of politics for medicine.","The earliest Greek on record to put understanding in the brain, and to separate understanding from perceiving, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who wrote chiefly on medicine."],"answer":"Alcmaeon of Croton","answerText":"Alcmaeon of Croton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">at Croton, in southern Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Book</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1, in fragments</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Alcmaeon of Croton</b> was a Greek writer of the early 5th century BCE from a city in southern Italy whose doctors Herodotus calls the best in Greece, though whether he practised medicine is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius says he wrote chiefly on medicine and preserves his opening, addressed to three men who may have been Pythagoreans: of things invisible, as of mortal things, only the gods have certain knowledge; to us, as men, only inference is possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He probably wrote his book between 500 and 450 BCE, and nearly every other question about him is argued over, his exact dates included, and what he owed to Pythagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle wrote a book in reply to him, and in the <i>Metaphysics</i> compares him with a group of Pythagoreans for making opposites the principles of things, leaving open who took the idea from whom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is left of him is a handful of quotations and reports, most of them about the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The claim that made his name is that the brain is the seat of understanding, and that understanding is a different thing from perceiving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Theophrastus reports him saying that man alone understands while other creatures perceive without understanding, and that all the senses are connected in some way with the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato has Socrates recall the question unsettling him in his youth: whether it is the blood, or air, or fire by which we think, or whether the brain furnishes the sensations of hearing and sight and smell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His account of health is borrowed from politics, since the equality of the powers in the body — wet and dry, hot and cold, bitter and sweet — keeps a person well, and the sole rule of any one of them makes them ill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is often called the father of anatomy, and the evidence will not carry it: it goes no further than the excision of one eyeball, and the detailed work on the eye credited to him is a later writer’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Carl Huffman, “Alcmaeon,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Fall 2025 Edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2025/entries/alcmaeon/. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 8.83, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph02diog. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 986a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0052:book=1:section=986a. [Open access]","Theophrastus, <i>De sensibus</i> 25–26, trans. George M. Stratton, in <i>Theophrastus and the Greek Physiological Psychology before Aristotle</i> (London: George Allen and Unwin, 1917), https://archive.org/details/theophrastusgree00stra. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedo</i> 96b, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0170:text=Phaedo:section=96b. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","science","philosophy","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Crotone%2C_parco_archeologico_di_capo_colonna%2C_tempio_di_hera_lacinia%2C_01.jpg/1280px-Crotone%2C_parco_archeologico_di_capo_colonna%2C_tempio_di_hera_lacinia%2C_01.jpg","title":"The last column at Croton","desc":"One Doric column left standing of the temple of Hera Lacinia at Capo Colonna, the great sanctuary of Croton, the city he came from. Of his own book only a few sentences survive, quoted by later writers.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crotone,_parco_archeologico_di_capo_colonna,_tempio_di_hera_lacinia,_01.jpg","alt":"A single fluted Doric column standing on a low stepped platform on a grassy headland above a flat blue sea, with scattered foundation blocks around it."},"why":[{"q":"Why does his opening sentence limit what he will claim?","a":"Because it says that of things invisible, as of mortal things, only the gods have certain knowledge, and to us, as men, only inference is possible."},{"q":"Why is his account of health borrowed from politics?","a":"Because the equality of the powers in the body — wet and dry, hot and cold, bitter and sweet — keeps a person well, while the sole rule of any one of them makes them ill."},{"q":"Why will the title father of anatomy not stand?","a":"Because the evidence goes no further than the excision of one eyeball, and the detailed work on the eye credited to him is a later writer's."}]},
{"id":"gr-381","num":381,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The fullest account <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives of itself is a rock face in western Iran, and what it says there is a king's version of his own seizure of power.","questions":["Herodotus says that Darius divided <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into twenty provinces, which they call satrapies, and fixed for each of them a yearly tribute in silver or in gold.","Over 2,100 Elamite tablets from Persepolis record the food moved through one region of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with everyone in the state's employ drawing a fixed ration."],"answer":"Achaemenid Empire","answerText":"Achaemenid Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">from Ionia to Gandara</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lands claimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23, at Behistun</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> was the Persian state that ran from Ionia in the west to Gandara in the east, and the fullest account it gives of itself is cut into a rock face in western Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius put his own words there in three languages: I am Darius, the great king, the king of kings, the king of Persia, the king of the provinces, the son of Hystaspes, the grandson of Arsames, the Achaemenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists 23 lands that came to him — Persia, Susiana, Babylonia, Assyria, Arabia, Egypt, the islands of the sea, Sparda, Ionia, Media and the rest — and says that by the grace of Auramazda they became subject and brought him tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crisis that made him king is described there in moral rather than political terms: when Cambyses had gone into Egypt the people became hostile, and the Lie multiplied in the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a king’s account of his own seizure of power, and the empire’s working papers read nothing like it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Over 2,100 clay tablets from Persepolis, written in Elamite, record the movement of food through one region in the 15 years down to 493 BCE, with everyone in the state’s employ drawing a fixed ration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus turns the same empire into a tax table, saying that Darius divided it into 20 provinces, which they call satrapies, and fixed what each should pay yearly by the talent in silver or gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that the Persians called Darius the merchant, Cambyses the master and Cyrus the father, because Darius made petty profit out of everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon, writing later still, has Cyrus send out satraps while keeping the garrison commanders answerable to nobody but himself, so that a satrap who grew insolent would meet opposition inside his own province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And Aeschylus put the empire on an Athenian stage as a council of elders left behind at the royal abode, waiting for news of a king who does not come back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.89, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=89. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i>, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=1. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 8.6.1, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=8:chapter=6:section=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["state","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Behistun_inscription_reliefs.jpg/1280px-Behistun_inscription_reliefs.jpg","title":"Darius on the rock at Behistun","desc":"The relief in the cliff face in western Iran: Darius with one foot on a prostrate rival, a line of roped captives before him, and above them the winged figure the excavators name as the god Auramazda. The panels around them carry the same text in Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian.","credit":"Photograph by Hara1603, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Behistun_inscription_reliefs.jpg","alt":"A weathered rock relief of a robed king standing with a raised hand and one foot on a fallen man, faced by a row of bound figures roped at the neck, with a winged disc above and dense panels of cuneiform around them."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the empire's own inscription describe a crisis in moral terms?","a":"Because it is a king's account of his own seizure of power, so Darius writes that when Cambyses had gone into Egypt the people became hostile and the Lie multiplied in the provinces."},{"q":"Why do the Persepolis tablets read so differently from that rock face?","a":"Because they are working papers: over 2,100 of them record the movement of food through one region in fifteen years, with everyone in the state's employ drawing a fixed ration."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus turn the empire into a table of tribute?","a":"Because he describes Darius dividing it into twenty satrapies and fixing what each should pay yearly in silver or gold, which is why the Persians called him the merchant."}]},
{"id":"gr-382","num":382,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Reigning from about 559 to 530 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded the Persian empire, ending Median rule over Asia beyond the Halys after leading the Persians against his own grandfather Astyages.","questions":["Even the words on the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reach us in two versions, one reported by Aristobulus and a quite different one by Onesicritus.","The Massagetae queen Tomyris filled a skin with human blood and pushed into it the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, telling the dead man she was giving him his fill of blood."],"answer":"Cyrus the Great","answerText":"Cyrus the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 559 – 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">30 years less one, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Median rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">128 years, less the Scythians</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Cyrus the Great</b> founded the Persian empire in the middle of the 6th century BCE, and Herodotus opens by saying he could give three other versions of him, and follows the Persians who wish not to magnify the story but to tell the truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The version he chose is a folk tale: Astyages, king of the Medes, dreams that a vine grows from his daughter and covers the whole of Asia, and orders her newborn son killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The child is not killed, is brought up among herdsmen, and in the end leads the Persians against his own grandfather, ending Median rule over Asia beyond the Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Astyages, deposed after 35 years, was kept in Cyrus’ own house until he died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says the same of the end: many stories are told of Cyrus’ death, and the one he gives is the one he finds most credible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In it he is killed fighting the Massagetae, one year short of thirty on the throne, and their queen Tomyris fills a skin with human blood and pushes his head into it, giving him, as she had promised, his fill of blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek accounts admire him even so, which is not how they usually treat a Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aeschylus has the ghost of Darius call him blessed in good fortune, a king who established peace for all his people and won the favour of the gods by his right-mindedness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato says that under Cyrus the Persians kept the due measure between slavery and freedom, and so became free and then masters of many others; Xenophon made him the subject of a book on how a ruler comes to be obeyed willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the words on his tomb reach us twice over: Aristobulus read there, O man, I am Cyrus, I established the Persian empire and was king of Asia, grudge me not therefore this monument, while Onesicritus read, I, Cyrus, king of kings, lie here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.95, 1.107–130, 1.214, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=95. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 1.1.1, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=1:chapter=1:section=1. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.7, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=7. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 765–772, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 694a, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=694a. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Pasargadae%2C_Ir%C3%A1n%2C_2016-09-24%2C_DD_01.jpg/1280px-Pasargadae%2C_Ir%C3%A1n%2C_2016-09-24%2C_DD_01.jpg","title":"The tomb at Pasargadae","desc":"A gabled stone chamber on six receding steps, on the plain of Pasargadae in southern Iran. Strabo reports that the chamber had been robbed by Alexander’s day, and gives two different readings of the inscription on it; the stone now carries none.","credit":"Photograph by Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pasargadae,_Ir%C3%A1n,_2016-09-24,_DD_01.jpg","alt":"A plain limestone burial chamber with a pitched roof standing on a broad plinth of six receding steps, alone on a dry plain under a deep blue sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus say which version of the man he is choosing?","a":"Because he could give three others, and follows the Persians who wish not to magnify the story but to tell the truth — and says the same of the death he reports."},{"q":"Why do Greek writers admire a Persian king?","a":"Because Aeschylus has Darius's ghost call him blessed and right-minded, Plato says the Persians under him kept the due measure between slavery and freedom, and Xenophon made him a study in willing obedience."},{"q":"Why are even the words on his tomb uncertain?","a":"Because they reach us twice over, Aristobulus reading a request not to grudge him the monument and Onesicritus reading only that Cyrus, king of kings, lies here."}]},
{"id":"gr-383","num":383,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"What Cyrus did with Croesus after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in three incompatible written versions and in one painted vase older than any of them.","questions":["Told that if he marched he would destroy a great empire, Croesus began <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and destroyed his own, having ruled 14 years and stood a siege of 14 days.","Sardis fell during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a Mardian who climbed the one stretch of the acropolis so sheer that nobody had thought to set a guard on it."],"answer":"Persian conquest of Lydia","answerText":"Persian conquest of Lydia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardis fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 546 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">after a siege of 14 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Croesus reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Persian conquest of Lydia</b> ended the kingdom of Croesus and brought Persia to the Aegean, and Aeschylus sums it up in a line: the Lydians and Phrygians Cyrus won to his rule, and the whole of Ionia he subdued by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Croesus had sent to Delphi to ask whether he should march against the Persians, and was told that if he did he would destroy a great empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sardis fell after a siege of 14 days, taken by a Mardian who climbed the one stretch of the acropolis where no guard had been set, the stretch round which an earlier king had not carried the lion his concubine bore him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Croesus had ruled 14 years and been besieged 14 days, and Herodotus observes that in fulfilling the oracle he had destroyed his own great empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Afterwards, on Croesus’ own advice, Cyrus is said to have forbidden the Lydians weapons of war and set their sons to lyre-playing and shop-keeping, so that they should never revolt again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What Cyrus then did with Croesus survives in three incompatible versions and one picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Herodotus he is set on a pyre with twice seven Lydian boys, and when it is lit he calls on Apollo, and a storm breaks out of a clear and windless sky and puts the fire out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Bacchylides, writing a generation earlier, Croesus builds the pyre himself rather than be enslaved, Zeus quenches it, and Apollo carries him and his family off to live among the Hyperboreans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Athenian vase painted about the same time shows him enthroned on the burning pyre in royal robes and a laurel crown, a sceptre in one hand and a libation pouring from the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> And in Xenophon there is no pyre at all: Croesus is brought before Cyrus and greets him as his sovereign lord, since fortune grants that he should bear the title henceforth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.53, 1.84, 1.86–87, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=86. [Open access]","Bacchylides, <i>Ode 3</i>, ed. and trans. Richard C. Jebb, <i>Bacchylides: The Poems and Fragments</i> (Cambridge: at the University Press, 1905), https://archive.org/details/cu31924052998659. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 7.2.9, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=7:chapter=2:section=9. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 765–772, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.155, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=155. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the oracle's answer come true against the man who asked it?","a":"Because he was told that if he marched he would destroy a great empire, and Herodotus observes that in fulfilling it he destroyed his own."},{"q":"Why did the acropolis fall where it did?","a":"Because a Mardian climbed the one stretch where no guard had been set, being the stretch round which an earlier king had not carried the lion his concubine bore him."},{"q":"Why is what became of Croesus impossible to settle?","a":"Because three incompatible versions survive with a picture beside them — a storm quenching the pyre, Apollo carrying him to the Hyperboreans, and Xenophon's account with no pyre at all."}]},
{"id":"gr-384","num":384,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Cyrus answered the offer that opened <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a fable of a flute-player who netted the fish that would not dance to his piping, and told them to stop dancing now.","questions":["Only two cities answered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by leaving, the Phocaeans loading families, goods and even their temple statues aboard and sailing, and the Teians doing the same.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Bias of Priene told the Ionians to sail together to Sardinia and found one city for all of them, and they did not."],"answer":"Persian conquest of Ionia","answerText":"Persian conquest of Ionia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionia fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 545 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">to Harpagus, city by city</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities that left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, Phocaea and Teos</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Persian conquest of Ionia</b> followed straight on the fall of Lydia, and it opened with a refusal that Cyrus answered with a fable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ionians and Aeolians offered to become his subjects on the terms they had held under Croesus, having refused him before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He told them of a flute-player who piped to the fish, and when they would not come out netted them and told them, as they leapt, to stop dancing now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The work was given to Harpagus, a Mede, who took the cities one at a time by driving the people inside their walls and raising earthworks against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeschylus gives the result in half a line, the whole of Ionia subdued by force, and Herodotus’ summary is drier: thus Ionia was enslaved for the second time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Phocaea was the first city he attacked, and its people were the earliest Greeks to make long voyages, finding the Adriatic, Tyrrhenia, Iberia and Tartessus in fifty-oared ships and founding Marseilles, where Strabo describes temples of Ephesian Artemis and Delphian Apollo in the citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides has them beating a Carthaginian fleet while that foundation was going on, and the king of Tartessus, hearing that Median power was growing, gave them the money to wall their city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wall did not save it: they asked Harpagus for a day to deliberate, and while his army stood off they launched their ships and put families, goods and the statues from their temples aboard; the Teians did the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These were the only Ionians who left; the rest fought well, lost their cities and did as they were told, and Miletus had made its own treaty with Cyrus and struck no blow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two ways out had been offered and neither taken: Bias of Priene, put by one account at the head of the Seven Sages, told them to sail together to Sardinia and found one city for all Ionians, and Thales had earlier urged a single council at Teos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.141, 1.162–170, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=141. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=13. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 765–772, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 4.1.4, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=4:chapter=1:section=4. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 1.82–88, trans. R. D. Hicks (London: Heinemann, 1925), https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Cyrus answer the Ionian offer with a fable?","a":"Because they had refused him before the war and offered terms only after Lydia fell, so he told of a flute-player who netted the fish that would not come out to his piping."},{"q":"Why did the wall the Phocaeans built not save them?","a":"Because they asked Harpagus for a day to deliberate, and while his army stood off they launched their ships and left with their families, goods and the statues from their temples."},{"q":"Why is the conquest read as a failure of Ionian union?","a":"Because two ways out had been offered and neither taken — Bias urging them to sail together and found one city in Sardinia, and Thales a single council at Teos."}]},
{"id":"gr-385","num":385,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Aeschylus gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one flat line in the roll of Persian kings, fourth in succession, the son of Cyrus, and passes no verdict on him at all.","questions":["Herodotus has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stab the Apis calf in the thigh, and kills him at the end with his own sword falling into his thigh in the same place.","Darius cut into the rock at Behistun that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a brother Smerdis and afterwards slew him, and that the people did not know he was slain."],"answer":"Cambyses II","answerText":"Cambyses II","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 522 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">7 years and 5 months, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">conquered 525 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cambyses II</b> was Cyrus’ son and successor and the king who took Egypt in 525 BCE,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and almost all that is told of him comes from Herodotus, who thought him mad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came back from a failed march to find Memphis keeping festival for a new Apis, took the rejoicing for mockery of his losses, and had the magistrates killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then drew his dagger on the calf, aimed at the belly and struck the thigh, and asked the priests whether their god was flesh and blood, able to feel iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that act, the Egyptians said, the madness dated: he killed his brother Smerdis on the strength of a dream, and Herodotus concludes he was insane, since only a madman derides religion and custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end is shaped to match: springing onto his horse, he lost the cap from his scabbard and the naked blade went into his thigh, in the place where he had wounded Apis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus is no neutral hearer of Egyptian stories about him: when they claimed him as one of their own, the son of a daughter of Apries, he calls it false, saying they falsify the story to claim kinship with Cyrus’ house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persian royal record confirms one thing only: Darius cut into the rock at Behistûn that Cambyses had a brother Smerdis, of the same mother and father, that he slew him, and that the people did not know it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aeschylus gives him a single flat line in the roll of kings — fourth in succession, the son of Cyrus ruled the host — and no verdict at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plato explains him by his upbringing: Cyrus’ sons were reared over-pampered and undisciplined, so that the one killed the other and then, mad with drink, lost his throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> And Xenophon ends his own Cyrus with a deathbed speech dividing the empire between the brothers, the throne to Cambyses and a satrapy to the younger, and stops before any of it happens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.2, 3.27–38, 3.64–66, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=29. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 773, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=759. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 695b, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=695b. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 8.7.11, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=8:chapter=7:section=11. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>A History of Egypt from the Earliest Times to the Persian Conquest</i>, 2nd ed. (New York: Charles Scribner’s Sons, 1912), 21, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptfr00breauoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/Cambyses_apis.JPG","title":"Apis stela of Cambyses II","desc":"A round-topped stela from the Serapeum at Saqqara, dated to the sixth year of Cambyses, showing the king kneeling at the left as an Egyptian pharaoh before the Apis bull at the right, above a block of hieroglyphic text.","credit":"Photograph by Jona Lendering, livius.org, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain)","alt":"A weathered round-topped stone stela carved in low relief: at the upper left a kneeling man with arms raised faces a standing bull at the upper right, with cartouches above them and rows of hieroglyphs filling the lower half."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus conclude the king was insane?","a":"Because in his account only a madman derides religion and custom, and this king stabbed the Apis calf, killed the magistrates and killed his own brother on the strength of a dream."},{"q":"Why does he reject the Egyptian claim that the king was Egyptian?","a":"Because they made him the son of a daughter of Apries, and he says they falsify the story to claim kinship with the house of Cyrus."},{"q":"Why is the Persian record so much thinner than the Greek?","a":"Because it confirms one thing only, Darius cutting into the rock that the king had a brother of the same mother and father, slew him, and the people did not know it."}]},
{"id":"gr-386","num":386,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The rock at Behistun carries <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>'s own account of his accession, and it ends by asking whoever reads it to believe what is written and not to hold it for lies.","questions":["Getting six fellow conspirators past the palace guards in Herodotus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tells them that when it is necessary to lie one should lie, since liars and truth-tellers are equally after advantage.","Told that Sardis had been burnt, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shot an arrow into the sky and set a servant to say to him at every dinner, master, remember the Athenians."],"answer":"Darius I","answerText":"Darius I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 522 – 486 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">36 years, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Behistun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">carved by 510 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Darius I</b> took the Persian throne on the death of Cambyses, and is the one Achaemenid king who left his own account of how he did it, cut into a cliff at Behistun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It opens: he is Darius, the great king, the king of kings, the king of Persia, the king of the provinces, the son of Hystaspes, the Achaemenian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cambyses, it says, killed his brother Smerdis in secret, after which a Magian called Gaumata rebelled and lied to the people, calling himself Smerdis, the son of Cyrus, and no Persian or Mede dared take the kingdom back until he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine more rebel kings follow, and of every one it says the same thing, that he lied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The end argues with the reader: let what he has done be believed and not held for lies, let a later king beware of lies and destroy the liar, for he was not a liar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The carving is too high to be read from the road, so copies were sent into all lands, and fragments of an Aramaic one survive among the papyri of the Jewish garrison at Elephantine in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus gives the same skeleton other flesh: seven conspirators kill the Magian and then debate how to be governed, Darius arguing that nothing is better than the rule of the one best man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He knows the debate is doubted and insists on it: sentiments were uttered there which some Greeks find incredible, but there is no doubt they were spoken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His Darius is untroubled by the Lie: to get the seven past the guards he says that when it is necessary to lie one should lie, liars and truth-tellers being equally after advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The throne comes to him by a groom's trick, a mare tethered overnight so that his horse would neigh first at sunrise, while Plato says he was no king's son and an Athenian chorus in Aeschylus remembers a god-like king who did no wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.72, 3.80–87, 5.105, 7.4, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=72. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 852–856, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=852. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 695c–d, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=695c. [Open access]","A. Cowley, <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), 248–49, https://archive.org/details/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Behistun_relief.jpg/1280px-Behistun_relief.jpg","title":"The relief at Behistun","desc":"The rock-cut relief above the inscription: Darius stands at the left with one foot on the prostrate Gaumata and a hand raised to the winged figure of Ahuramazda, while the defeated rebel kings are led before him roped together at the neck, with panels of cuneiform around and below.","credit":"Photograph by Patrick C (dynamomosquito on Flickr), via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0)","alt":"A weathered rock-cut relief: at the left a bearded king in a long robe raises one hand towards a winged disc carrying a human figure and rests a foot on a man lying beneath him, while a line of bound men in varied headgear stands before him roped together at the neck, with blocks of cuneiform writing filling the spaces around and beneath."},"why":[{"q":"Why does his inscription argue with its reader?","a":"Because it asks that what he has done be believed and not held for lies, and warns a later king to beware of lies and destroy the liar, since he was not a liar."},{"q":"Why were copies of it made if the original stands on a cliff?","a":"Because the carving is too high to be read from the road, so copies were sent into all lands, and fragments of an Aramaic one survive among the papyri at Elephantine."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus's king sit oddly beside the inscription's?","a":"Because the rock condemns the Lie in every rebel, while Herodotus has him say that when it is necessary to lie one should lie, liars and truth-tellers being equally after advantage."}]},
{"id":"gr-387","num":387,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Xenophon says the governor of a Persian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to keep up horsemen and archers, feed the garrisons, and answer at a yearly review for the state of the land itself.","questions":["Oroetes governed the Phrygian, Lydian and Ionian <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Sardis with a thousand spearmen of his own, and Darius thought it unsafe to send an army openly against him.","Cyrus the Younger held Lydia as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> along with command of everyone who mustered in the plain of Castolus, so that the office joined a whole province to an army."],"answer":"Satrapy","answerText":"Satrapy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"A <b>satrapy</b> was a province of the Achaemenid Empire and its governor a satrap, a word Darius uses in Old Persian at Behistun of Dadarshish, whom he calls his servant and satrap in Bactria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon sets out what it required: a governor kept up a fixed number of horsemen, archers, slingers and light infantry, and fed the garrisons in the citadels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Once a year the troops came to the place of muster, the king reviewing those near his residence and sending trusted agents to the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land was judged the same way, so a governor whose province was populous and well cultivated gained more territory, presents and a seat of honour, while one whose lay untilled was punished and replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In principle the two duties went to separate officers, one over the residents and the tribute and one over the troops, but Xenophon adds that a satrap attends to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That made a satrapy a province with an army inside it, and Cyrus the Younger held his with command of everyone who mustered in the plain of Castolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What held such a thing together was paper: travellers drew rations at stations a day apart against a sealed document, and the Persepolis tablets record Artaphernes, satrap of Sardis, authorising two journeys at the empire's far end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It could also fail, as Herodotus' Oroetes shows: governing the Phrygian, Lydian and Ionian province from Sardis with a guard of a thousand spearmen, he killed a fellow governor and ambushed a messenger Darius had sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Darius judged it unsafe to send an army openly against a satrap while still new to the throne, and asked which Persian would deal with him by cunning rather than force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thirty volunteered; the lot fell to Bagaeus, who came to Sardis with sealed letters that a royal scribe read out one by one, until the guards lowered their spears at one forbidding them to guard Oroetes, and killed him at the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Oeconomicus</i> 4.5–11, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0212:text=Ec.:chapter=4:section=5. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.126–128, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=127. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 6, https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 1.1.2, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=1:chapter=1:section=2. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","geography","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Istanbul_Archaeological_Museum_Satrap_Sarcophagus_Long_side_Satrap_about_to_kill_a_panther_022.jpg/1280px-Istanbul_Archaeological_Museum_Satrap_Sarcophagus_Long_side_Satrap_about_to_kill_a_panther_022.jpg","title":"The Satrap Sarcophagus","desc":"A long side of the marble sarcophagus from the royal necropolis at Sidon, now in Istanbul, which is named from the figure carved on it rather than from any inscription: he rides down a panther among other horsemen, between borders of egg-and-dart and palmette.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A carved marble frieze in low relief: a rider with his arm raised behind him drives a rearing horse to the left, a panther runs between the horses at the centre, and a second rider approaches from the right, with bands of egg-and-dart and palmette ornament above and below."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a governor judged on his farmland as well as his troops?","a":"Because one whose province was populous and well cultivated gained territory, presents and a seat of honour, while one whose lay untilled was punished and replaced."},{"q":"Why did the office become dangerous to the king?","a":"Because although the civil and military duties were in principle separate, a satrap attended to both, so a province came with an army inside it — as Oroetes showed, killing a fellow governor and ambushing a royal messenger."},{"q":"Why did Darius deal with Oroetes by cunning rather than force?","a":"Because he judged it unsafe to send an army openly against a satrap while still new to the throne, so Bagaeus went to Sardis with sealed letters read out one by one."}]},
{"id":"gr-388","num":388,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus counted 111 posting stations along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between Sardis and Susa, and made it 450 parasangs, or 90 days of walking for a man on foot.","questions":["Asked by Cleomenes how far it was to the king along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Aristagoras of Miletus answered truthfully that it was three months inland, and was told to leave Sparta before sunset.","A man and a horse waited at every day's interval along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that a letter passed from hand to hand, stopped by neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness."],"answer":"Royal Road","answerText":"Royal Road","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">111, Sardis to Susa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On foot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">90 days, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Royal Road</b> ran from Sardis to Susa, and Herodotus counted it out: 111 posting stations, 450 parasangs, 13,500 stades, and 90 days of walking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It kept to inhabited and guarded country, with a defile and a fortress at the Halys, two more on the Cilician frontier, and ferries over four navigable rivers, three of them called the Tigris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Adding the stretch from Ephesus to Sardis made 14,040 stades and three days more, and Herodotus works that sum in order to check a story he has already told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it Aristagoras of Miletus, asking Sparta to march inland against the king, was asked by Cleomenes how far it was and made the mistake of telling the truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three months, he said, and Cleomenes told him to be out of Sparta before sunset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Along the same stations ran the courier post, the angareion, with a man and a horse standing at each day's interval, and Herodotus says nothing mortal travels faster, stopped by neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon credits Cyrus with the invention, saying he measured how far a horse could be ridden hard in one day, set his stations at that distance, and put an officer at each to take in spent riders and send on fresh ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system's own paperwork survives, since the Persepolis tablets record travellers drawing a day's rations at each station, and express horses kept permanently at the posts for the fast messengers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Read against those, Herodotus' single rider for a whole day's journey looks slow, and the horses were more likely changed at every station, some 30 km (20 miles) apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Armies used the road as well as letters, since Xenophon's Cyrus set out from Sardis and made the Maeander in three stages and 22 parasangs, and Darius' inscription has him send a messenger down it to his satrap in Bactria with orders to march.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.50, 5.52–54, 8.98, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=52. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 8.6.17–18, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=8:chapter=6:section=17. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 6, https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 1.2.5, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=1:chapter=2:section=5. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","geography","technology","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/PTF_1208-101.jpg/1280px-PTF_1208-101.jpg","title":"An Achaemenid administrative tablet","desc":"A clay tablet of the Achaemenid administration, its face covered with lines of cuneiform. Thousands of such tablets survive from Persepolis, and among them are the day-by-day records of the rations issued to travellers along the empire's roads.","credit":"Photograph by اردشیر خسروی, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A palm-sized slab of reddish baked clay, rounded on one side and broken on the other, its whole face ruled into about a dozen lines of wedge-shaped cuneiform signs."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus work out the distance so carefully?","a":"Because he is checking a story he has already told, in which Aristagoras admitted to Cleomenes that the march inland was three months and was told to be out of Sparta before sunset."},{"q":"Why do the Persepolis tablets make his courier look slow?","a":"Because they record travellers drawing a day's rations at each station and express horses kept permanently at the posts, so the horses were more likely changed at every station some 30 km apart."},{"q":"Why did the road have to keep to inhabited country?","a":"Because it had to be guarded and supplied along its whole length, with a defile and fortress at the Halys, two more on the Cilician frontier and ferries over four navigable rivers."}]},
{"id":"gr-389","num":389,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus counted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by penning 10,000 men behind a low wall and filling it again until all had passed through, then doubted his own figure of 1,700,000.","questions":["Asking after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the chorus of Aeschylus wonders whether the drawing of the bow has triumphed or the might of the sharp spear-head has prevailed.","At Plataea the men of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> threw away their bows, fought at a fence of shields and broke the Spartan spears short, and Herodotus says they lacked armour rather than courage."],"answer":"Persian army","answerText":"Persian army","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Land force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,700,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Counted by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">pens of 10,000 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">beaten in 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"abstract":"The <b>Persian army</b> that invaded Greece was a coalition, and Herodotus dresses its Persian contingent in soft caps called tiaras and tunics scaled with iron like a fish, carrying wicker shields, short spears, long bows and reed arrows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gives the land force as 1,700,000, counted by packing 10,000 men together, drawing a line round them, building a low wall on it, and filling the pen again until all had passed through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then doubts it, reckoning that a choenix of wheat a day for each man would take more than a million bushels, and wondering how they were fed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon, who met such an army at Cunaxa, has it marching in national divisions, each a solid square, with white-cuirassed horsemen, wicker shields, Egyptians behind wooden ones reaching to their feet, and scythed chariots in front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo says its men were made from the age of five, taught to ride, to shoot and to speak the truth, run in companies of fifty, and hardened to heat, cold and rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Greeks thought of that army as archers, and Aeschylus asked an Athenian audience outright: has the drawing of the bow triumphed, or the might of the sharp spear-head?<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same play Atossa asks whether the bow suits Greek hands, and is told far from it: they have lances for close fight and shields that serve for armour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Plataea the test came: the Persians threw away their bows to meet the charge, fought at a fence of shields and then hand to hand, laying hold of the Spartan spears and breaking them short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus' verdict is not that they lacked courage: they were neither less valorous nor weaker, he says, but had no armour and fought as though naked against men fully armed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius counts differently, his inscription saying only that after he became king he fought nineteen battles and took nine kings, so that the empire's own war record numbers battles rather than soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.60–61, 7.187, 9.62–63, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=60. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 147–149, 239–240, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=140. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 1.8.9–10, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=1:chapter=8:section=9. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.18, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=18. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Persepolis_The_Persian_Soldiers.jpg/1280px-Persepolis_The_Persian_Soldiers.jpg","title":"Guardsmen on the Apadana stairs","desc":"A relief from the Apadana at Persepolis showing files of guardsmen in Persian and Median dress, the Medes in rounded caps and boots, each with a long spear and some with a round shield, beneath a winged disc flanked by sphinxes.","credit":"Photograph by Arad, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A carved stone relief of six bearded men standing in file, each holding an upright spear taller than himself; three wear tall fluted headdresses and long pleated robes and the others rounded caps and trousers, two of them carrying large round shields, with a winged disc and sphinxes on the band above."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus doubt the figure he has just given?","a":"Because he reckons that a choenix of wheat a day for each man would take more than a million bushels, and wonders how such a host was fed."},{"q":"Why did the Persians fare badly at close quarters at Plataea?","a":"Because they had no armour and fought as though naked against men fully armed, which Herodotus is careful to distinguish from any lack of courage."},{"q":"Why does the empire's own record give no army size?","a":"Because Darius's inscription says only that after he became king he fought nineteen battles and took nine kings, so it numbers battles rather than soldiers."}]},
{"id":"gr-390","num":390,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says the corps called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was held at exactly ten thousand men, another being chosen whenever one of them fell out through death or sickness.","questions":["Sent in at Thermopylae once the Medes had been roughly handled, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fared no better, their spears being shorter than the Greek ones in a pass too narrow for numbers.","Mardonius kept the whole corps of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for his army in Greece after Salamis, all but their general Hydarnes, who would not quit the king's person."],"answer":"Immortals","answerText":"Immortals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Strength</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thermopylae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">sent in, 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">After Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">kept in Greece, 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Immortals</b> are known from Herodotus, who says ten thousand picked Persians served under Hydarnes son of Hydarnes and explains the name himself: whenever one fell out through death or sickness another was chosen, so they were never more nor fewer than ten thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were the best men in the army and the most richly adorned, standing out by the gold they wore, and their food came on their own camels, apart from the rest of the host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the march out of Sardis they framed the king, a thousand of them carrying golden pomegranates on their spear-shafts instead of a spike and surrounding the nine thousand who carried silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xenophon knows a body of the same size without the name, saying Cyrus took ten thousand spearmen who guarded the palace day and night and went drawn up on either side of him when he travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo describes what such a man carried: a rhomb-shaped shield, a quiver, a battle-axe and a short sword, a cap rising like a tower, and a breastplate of iron scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> At Thermopylae Xerxes sent them in when the Medes had been roughly handled, and it was thought they would easily accomplish the task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They fared neither better nor worse than the Medes, since their spears were shorter than the Greek ones and their numbers no use in a narrow space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What opened the pass was not the corps but a path, and Hydarnes led them out of camp at lamp-lighting time to take it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Salamis Mardonius chose the whole corps for the army he kept in Greece, all but Hydarnes himself, who said that he would not quit the king's person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two other records stand beside that Greek name: Darius' inscription has a Hydarnes leading an army for him a generation earlier, and Aeschylus, never using the name, has the king's choicest troops, foremost in loyalty, killed to the last man on an islet off Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.41, 7.83, 7.211, 7.215, 8.113, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=83. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 7.5.68, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=7:chapter=5:section=68. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 441–464, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=441. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.19, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=19. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Archers_frieze_Louvre_AOD488.jpg/1280px-Archers_frieze_Louvre_AOD488.jpg","title":"The archers frieze from Susa","desc":"Glazed brick spearmen from the palace of Darius at Susa, now in the Louvre, each with a bow and quiver slung at his back. Friezes of this kind are often labelled as the Immortals, though nothing on them carries that name, which is Greek and comes from Herodotus.","credit":"Photograph by Jastrow, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain)","alt":"A wall of turquoise glazed bricks beneath a palmette border, moulded in low relief with four bearded men in file wearing long patterned robes, each holding an upright spear in both hands and carrying a bow and a quiver slung over the shoulder."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus say the corps was never more nor fewer than ten thousand?","a":"Because whenever one fell out through death or sickness another was chosen, which is the explanation he gives for the name itself."},{"q":"Why did the best troops in the army fail at Thermopylae?","a":"Because their spears were shorter than the Greek ones and their numbers were no use in a narrow space, so they fared neither better nor worse than the Medes."},{"q":"Why did Hydarnes not stay in Greece with the corps he commanded?","a":"Because although Mardonius chose the whole body for the army he kept there, Hydarnes said that he would not quit the king's person."}]},
{"id":"gr-391","num":391,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Darius' <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened with a bridge over the Bosphorus by Mandrocles of Samos and two marble pillars lettered in Assyrian and in Greek with the nations of his army.","questions":["During Darius' <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Scythian kings sent him a bird, a mouse, a frog and five arrows, which he read as a surrender and Gobryas read as a threat.","Left to guard the Danube bridge during Darius' <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Ionian tyrants were told by Histiaeus that each of them held his own city by the king's power alone."],"answer":"Scythian campaign","answerText":"Scythian campaign","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 513 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Megabazus, 80,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"Darius' <b>Scythian campaign</b> took a Persian army across the Bosphorus on a bridge by Mandrocles of Samos, and Herodotus says the king set two marble pillars beside it, lettered in Assyrian and in Greek with the names of every nation in his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His census gave 700,000 men besides 600 ships, a figure Justin repeats, and the Byzantines later carried the pillars off to build an altar of Artemis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the Danube he tied sixty knots in a thong and left it with the Ionian tyrants guarding the bridge, to untie one a day and sail home if the knots ran out before he returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Scythians would not fight but withdrew before him, and Strabo says he found himself in danger of being cut off in a waterless plain, and turned back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their kings sent him a bird, a mouse, a frog and five arrows, which Darius read as a surrender of earth and water and Gobryas as a warning that unless the Persians turned into birds or mice or frogs they would not get home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The knots ran out, and the Scythians came to the bridge and told the Ionians to break it and be free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Miltiades the Athenian was for doing it, but Histiaeus of Miletus answered that each of them held his city by Darius' power and would lose it if that power fell, and the rest came round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin gives the war a different cause altogether, a Scythian king's daughter refused in marriage, and says the losses out of so vast a number were scarcely accounted a disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Darius' own monument records a different Scythian war, one he won, crossing the water, killing many, taking their leader Skunkha prisoner and setting another over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it left was Megabazus in Europe with 80,000 men, and Aeschylus later set on an Athenian stage the ghost of the man who bridged the Bosphorus, condemning his son for shackling a strait like a slave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.87–89, 4.98, 4.131–132, 4.137, 4.143, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=87. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.14, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=7:chapter=3:section=14. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 745–752, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=739. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","iran","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Behistun_relief_Skunkha.jpg/500px-Behistun_relief_Skunkha.jpg","title":"Skunkha at the end of the line","desc":"The last of the bound captives on the Behistun relief, in the tall pointed cap of the Saka, with a cuneiform label cut above his head. His figure was added to the rock after the rest, for a Scythian war that Darius did commemorate.","credit":"Photograph by Leen van Dorp, livius.org, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0)","alt":"A tall narrow detail of a weathered rock relief: a bearded man in a very high pointed cap stands with his hands bound behind him and a rope at his neck, with lines of cuneiform cut in the rock above him."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the king leave a knotted thong at the Danube?","a":"Because the Ionian tyrants guarding the bridge were to untie one knot a day and sail home if the sixty ran out before he returned."},{"q":"Why did the Ionians keep the bridge when the knots ran out?","a":"Because Histiaeus answered that each of them held his city by Darius' power and would lose it if that power fell, and the rest came round to him."},{"q":"Why does the king's own monument not record this campaign?","a":"Because it records a different Scythian war, one he won, crossing the water, killing many and taking their leader Skunkha prisoner."}]},
{"id":"gr-392","num":392,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Carrying a bronze tablet engraved with a map of all the earth, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed along it as far as Susa and asked Sparta to free the Ionians from the Persian king.","questions":["Athens voted twenty ships after hearing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Herodotus remarks that it is easier to deceive many than one, since Cleomenes alone had sent him away.","Having raised Ionia against Darius, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed Miletus to another man, sailed for Thrace and was killed besieging a town whose people were ready to leave under treaty."],"answer":"Aristagoras","answerText":"Aristagoras","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">raised in 499 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">offered 50 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 497 BCE, in Thrace</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Aristagoras</b> was deputy tyrant of Miletus, a city Strabo describes with four harbours, one of them able to take a fleet, and with colonies peopling the whole Black Sea and the Propontis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He promised the Persians Naxos, quarrelled with the commander of the fleet sent to take it, failed, and then raised Ionia in revolt against the king who had made him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He went to Sparta carrying a bronze tablet engraved with a map of all the earth, the sea and the rivers, and pointed along it through Lydia, Phrygia, Cappadocia, Cilicia and Armenia to Susa, where the great king kept his storehouses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The map came out of his own city, Anaximander of Miletus being remembered as the first to draw the outline of land and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His argument was that the Persians carried bows and short spears and went to battle in trousers, so that Sparta might have all Asia instead of fighting Argives who had no gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Cleomenes asked how far it was to the king, and on being told three months sent him away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came back in a suppliant's garb and offered ten talents, then more, then fifty, until Cleomenes' daughter Gorgo, eight or nine years old, said the stranger would corrupt her father unless he left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens heard the same speech and voted twenty ships, which Herodotus calls the beginning of troubles for Greeks and foreigners alike, remarking that it is easier to deceive many than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides notes that the islands had been left for Darius and the Phoenician fleet, and Justin runs the sequence to its end: the Ionians beaten at sea, and Darius turning his whole fury on Athens for having helped them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristagoras did not see it out, for Herodotus says he showed little courage, handed Miletus to another man, sailed for Thrace and was killed there besieging a town whose people were ready to leave under treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.30–38, 5.49–51, 5.97, 5.124–126, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=49. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 2.1–2, trans. R. D. Hicks, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), 1:131, https://archive.org/details/livesofeminentph01diog. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=6. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.16, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=16. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Miletus_-_Ancient_Greek_theatre_02.jpg/1280px-Miletus_-_Ancient_Greek_theatre_02.jpg","title":"The theatre at Miletus","desc":"The standing theatre on the site of Miletus in Turkey. Strabo describes the city with four harbours, one of which would take a fleet; the site now lies among fields, with no sea in sight.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A large ruined theatre of pale stone on a green plain: a high wall with an arched tunnel entrance at the left, tiers of broken seating curving away to the right, and fallen blocks scattered over the grass in front."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he carry a bronze map to Sparta?","a":"Because he meant to point along it through Lydia, Phrygia, Cappadocia, Cilicia and Armenia to Susa and the king's storehouses; the map came out of his own city, where Anaximander first drew land and sea."},{"q":"Why did the appeal to Sparta fail and the one to Athens succeed?","a":"Because Cleomenes asked how far it was to the king and sent him away on being told three months, where Athens heard the same speech and voted twenty ships."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus call those twenty ships the beginning of troubles?","a":"Because Darius turned his whole fury on Athens for having helped the Ionians, so the aid given to the revolt drew the war onto Greece."}]},
{"id":"gr-393","num":393,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"To reach Aristagoras while the roads were watched, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shaved the head of a trusted slave, marked it, and waited for the hair to grow before sending him to Miletus.","questions":["Darius wrote to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the most precious of possessions is a wise and loyal friend, and invited him to leave Miletus and share the royal table and counsels at Susa.","Artaphernes told <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Sardis that he had stitched the shoe which Aristagoras then put on, and later sent his embalmed head to the king at Susa."],"answer":"Histiaeus","answerText":"Histiaeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Message sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 499 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At the bridge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Danube, c. 513 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 493 BCE, impaled</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Histiaeus</b> was tyrant of Miletus, a city Strabo gives four harbours and colonies throughout the Black Sea, and he had saved Darius' bridge over the Danube by arguing that every Ionian tyrant held his city by the king's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius rewarded him with Myrcinus in Thrace, then, warned that a clever Greek in a country of timber and silver was a danger, wrote him a letter of great warmth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was nothing he longed for so much as to see him, the king said, the most precious of possessions being a wise and loyal friend, and he invited him to leave Miletus and share his table and counsels at Susa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was an honour that could not be refused and could not be left, and he stayed disliking his detention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So he shaved the head of his most trusted slave, marked it, waited for the hair to grow, and sent the man to Aristagoras with no message except that he should shave the man and look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus says he did it because the roads were guarded, and Aeneas Tacticus, in a manual of siegecraft of the next century, gives the same reason, no letter-carrier being able to pass unnoticed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aulus Gellius adds what the Greek accounts do not, that the slave had long had bad eyes, so the shaving could pass for a cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the marks said was revolt, and he reckoned that if Ionia rose he would be sent down to the coast to settle it, while if Miletus stayed quiet he would never see it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was sent, and Artaphernes met him at Sardis with the whole thing understood: you stitched this shoe, he said, and Aristagoras put it on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin ends the revolt at sea, and Histiaeus in flight, taken by Harpagus and impaled by Artaphernes, who sent his head embalmed to Susa, where Darius rebuked them for not bringing him alive and had it washed and buried with honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.137, 5.11, 5.23–24, 5.35, 6.1, 6.30, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=35. [Open access]","Aeneas Tacticus, <i>On the Defence of Fortified Positions</i> 31.28–29, trans. members of the Illinois Greek Club, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1923), 171, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.43525. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=6. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>The Attic Nights</i> 17.9, trans. William Beloe (London: J. Johnson, 1795), 3:287, https://archive.org/details/atticnights03gelluoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Temple_of_Artemis_Sardis_Turkey.jpg/1280px-Temple_of_Artemis_Sardis_Turkey.jpg","title":"Sardis","desc":"The ruins of the temple of Artemis on the site of Sardis in Turkey. Sardis was the seat of Artaphernes, who told Histiaeus that he had stitched the shoe Aristagoras put on, and who had him impaled there and his head sent to Susa.","credit":"Photograph by Simon Jenkins, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0)","alt":"Two tall Ionic columns still standing among fallen column drums and block foundations on a grassy archaeological site, with eroded pale hillsides and green ridges rising behind under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the king's warm letter a way of removing him?","a":"Because Darius had been warned that a clever Greek in a country of timber and silver was a danger, and an invitation to share the royal table and counsels could not be refused and could not be left."},{"q":"Why was a message tattooed on a slave's head rather than written?","a":"Because the roads were guarded and no letter-carrier could pass unnoticed, a reason Herodotus gives and a manual of siegecraft repeats in the next century."},{"q":"Why did he want Ionia to revolt at all?","a":"Because he reckoned that if it rose he would be sent down to the coast to settle it, while if Miletus stayed quiet he would never see it again."}]},
{"id":"gr-394","num":394,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began when Aristagoras made a pretence of giving up his own tyranny at Miletus and then put down the tyrants the Persians had installed elsewhere.","questions":["To finish the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Persians netted the islands, their men linking hands in a line from one sea to the other and walking each island through to hunt the people down.","Nepos says Darius gave as his reason for invading Greece that during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Ionians had stormed Sardis with Athenian help and killed his garrison there."],"answer":"Ionian Revolt","answerText":"Ionian Revolt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 493 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 ships, Eretria 5</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">tribute fixed, 493 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Ionian Revolt</b> was the rising of the Greek cities of Asia against Darius, and it began with Aristagoras making a pretence of giving up his own tyranny at Miletus and granting the city equality of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He did the same through the rest of Ionia, banishing some of the tyrants the Persians had installed and handing others over to the cities they had governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens sent twenty ships and Eretria five, the allies marched inland and burnt Sardis, and the Athenians then went home and would not come back however he begged them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It spread far wider than Ionia, to Cyprus, to the Hellespont and to Caria, where an ambush laid on the road at Pedasus destroyed a Persian army by night with three of its generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides sets out what was at stake, the coastal cities having been reduced by Cyrus and the islands left for Darius and the Phoenician fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Persians took the cities back one at a time, and once Miletus had fallen they netted the islands, the men linking hands in a line from the northern sea to the southern and walking the whole island through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artaphernes then measured the land by parasangs, fixed each city's tribute at about what it had paid before, and made the Ionians agree to settle their quarrels by law instead of by robbing one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mardonius went further the next spring, deposing every Ionian tyrant and setting up democracies in their cities, which Herodotus records for the wonder of Greeks who doubt that a Persian ever argued for democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artaphernes is also one of the few men in this story to appear in a Persian document, authorising travellers' rations twice in the tablets from Persepolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Justin runs the war to its end at sea, and Nepos says Darius gave as his reason for invading Greece that the Ionians had stormed Sardis with Athenian help and killed his garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.37, 5.99–103, 5.121, 6.31, 6.42–43, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=37. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.5, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 23, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.16, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=16. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 6, https://isac.uchicago.edu/research/publications/oip/persepolis-fortification-tablets. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Reliefs_in_Persepolis_%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%87_%D9%87%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D8%AA%D8%AE%D8%AA_%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%AF_09.jpg/1280px-Reliefs_in_Persepolis_%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%87_%D9%87%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D8%AA%D8%AE%D8%AA_%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%AF_09.jpg","title":"A delegation on the Apadana stairs","desc":"One of the files of subject peoples carved on the great stairway of the Apadana at Persepolis: five men in long robes and soft caps carry bowls, folded cloth and stacked vessels towards the king, walking past a stylised tree between borders of rosettes.","credit":"Photograph by Mostafa Meraji, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A grey stone relief of five bearded men in long robes and soft round caps walking in file to the left, carrying shallow bowls, folded cloth and a stack of vessels, past a tall stylised tree, with bands of carved rosettes above and below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the revolt open with a tyrant giving up his own power?","a":"Because Aristagoras made a pretence of it and granted Miletus equality of government, then did the same through Ionia, banishing some Persian-installed tyrants and handing others to their cities."},{"q":"Why is Mardonius's settlement recorded as a wonder?","a":"Because he deposed every Ionian tyrant and set up democracies in their cities, which Herodotus notes for Greeks who doubt that a Persian ever argued for democracy."},{"q":"Why did the rising decide what came next for Athens?","a":"Because Darius gave as his reason for invading Greece that the Ionians had stormed Sardis with Athenian help and killed his garrison."}]},
{"id":"gr-395","num":395,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says one soldier's fire caught the reed roofs and spread house to house, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed a city the Ionians had entered unopposed.","questions":["The Persians later made the loss of Cybebe's temple in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> their pretext for burning the temples of Greece, and Athens' twenty ships were long remembered for it.","Charon of Lampsacus said only that the Athenians took everything but the king's wall, a far quieter account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than the one Herodotus gives."],"answer":"burning of Sardis","answerText":"burning of Sardis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">498 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 493 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 ships, Eretria 5</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>burning of Sardis</b> in 498 BCE was the most famous stroke of the Ionian Revolt, and in the long run it did the Greeks more harm than good. Sardis had been the capital of the Lydian kings and was now the seat of Persian power in western Anatolia, the far end of the Royal Road and the residence of Artaphernes, satrap of Lydia and brother of Darius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo describes a large city with a strong citadel below a mountain whose stream once carried down the gold dust behind the proverbial wealth of Croesus, and Aeschylus a generation later calls the place 'rich in gold'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Athens sent twenty ships and Eretria five, the Eretrians repaying an old debt to Miletus rather than doing Athens a favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristagoras stayed at Miletus and gave the command to his brother Charopinus and a citizen named Hermophantus, and the force left its ships near Ephesus and marched inland over Tmolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> They took the lower city without a fight, but Artaphernes held the citadel with a large garrison, though a Roman account has the Ionians storming Sardis and putting the garrison to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plunder was impossible because most houses were built of reeds and even the brick ones were roofed with them, so when a soldier set one alight the fire ran from roof to roof across the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lydians and Persians driven from the burning streets massed in the marketplace by the Pactolus and turned to fight, and the Ionians withdrew to Tmolus and reached their ships after dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Persian troops caught the retreating army at Ephesus and routed it, Athens quit the war for good, and a temple of the goddess Cybebe that had burned became the stated Persian pretext for burning Greek temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charon of Lampsacus, an earlier writer, recorded only that the Athenians took everything but the king's wall and went back to Miletus, which is a smaller and quieter event than the one Herodotus describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.25, 5.52, 5.99–103, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=101. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 13.4.5, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=13:chapter=4:section=5. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Of Herodotus's Malice</i> 24, in <i>Plutarch's Morals</i>, ed. William W. Goodwin, vol. 4 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1878), 344–45, https://archive.org/details/plutarchsmorals04plut. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 45–52, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=41. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran","anatolia"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Sardis_citadel.jpg/1280px-Sardis_citadel.jpg","title":"The citadel of Sardis","desc":"A stretch of fortification wall still standing on the citadel ridge at Sardis, above the plain of the Hermus. Herodotus says the Ionians took the town below but never this height, which Artaphernes held with a large garrison while the houses burned.","credit":"Photograph by Ken Mayer, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)","alt":"A broken stretch of stone wall standing on a scrub-covered ridge, its courses of masonry ending raggedly at both sides, with a wide hazy valley of fields and low hills falling away behind under a pale sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the city burn when nobody meant to burn it?","a":"Because plunder was impossible, most houses being built of reeds and even the brick ones roofed with them, so when a soldier set one alight the fire ran from roof to roof."},{"q":"Why did the stroke do the Greeks more harm than good?","a":"Because the retreating army was caught and routed at Ephesus, Athens quit the war for good, and a burnt temple of Cybebe became the stated Persian pretext for burning Greek temples."},{"q":"Why is the scale of the event uncertain?","a":"Because Charon of Lampsacus, an earlier writer, recorded only that the Athenians took everything but the king's wall and went back to Miletus, which is smaller and quieter than Herodotus's account."}]},
{"id":"gr-396","num":396,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus explains the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the ruin of a fleet that mutinied after seven days of drill under the sun and sat in the shade rather than row.","questions":["Of the 353 Ionian triremes drawn up for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Samians sailed away but for eleven, and the Chians broke the enemy line and lost most of their ships.","Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Persians offered the crews safety for deserting and enslavement for fighting, and Herodotus says afterwards that the Ionians all blamed each other."],"answer":"Battle of Lade","answerText":"Battle of Lade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ionian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">353 triremes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 ships</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Lade</b> in 494 BCE ended the Ionian Revolt at sea, and Herodotus tells it as a story about an alliance that came apart before the fighting began. Meeting at the Panionium, the Ionians raised no land army but left Miletus to defend its own walls, manning every ship in haste and gathering at Lade, a small island off the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus lists the contingents city by city, from the Milesians' 80 ships on the eastern wing to the Samians' 60 on the western, 353 triremes in all against a Persian fleet of 600.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides had already recorded that Ionia was a real sea power under Cyrus and Cambyses, and that the Phocaeans beat a Carthaginian fleet while they were founding Marseilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persian commanders, doubting they could win, sent the exiled Ionian tyrants among the crews with an offer and a threat: desert and nothing would be burnt, fight and lose and be enslaved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dionysius of Phocaea, who had brought three ships, was given command and drilled the fleet in rowing through the enemy line, keeping the crews at work all day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They obeyed for seven days, then, unused to the labour and worn by the sun, refused to go on, pitched tents on the island and sat in the shade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Seeing the disorder, the Samians took an offer from their own exiled tyrant and sailed off when the Phoenicians came on, all but 11 ships whose captains stayed and were named on a pillar at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lesbians followed and most of the rest with them, and the Chians, 100 ships with 40 picked marines apiece, broke the enemy line and took many ships before losing most of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus refuses to say who was brave and who was not, since the Ionians all blamed each other, and the sea they fought over is now a plain, the Maeander having filled the gulf between Priene and Miletus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Lade_1.jpg/1280px-Lade_1.jpg","title":"The island of Lade today","desc":"The hill that was once the island of Lade, standing above cotton fields near Miletus. The gulf the two fleets fought in has been filled by the Maeander, a process Pausanias already describes as under way in his own day.","credit":"Photograph by O. Mustafin, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0)","alt":"A long low wooded hill with scattered white houses and a slender minaret rising from a wide flat field of green crops under a clear blue sky."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.7–6.17, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=14. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=13. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 10.25, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=10:chapter=25. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.7, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.24.11, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=8:chapter=24:section=11. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"locator":{"name":"Lade","at":[27.1,37.55],"kind":"battle"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Persian commanders negotiate before fighting?","a":"Because they doubted they could win, so they sent the exiled Ionian tyrants among the crews with an offer and a threat: desert and nothing would be burnt, fight and lose and be enslaved."},{"q":"Why did the training that might have won the battle fail?","a":"Because the crews obeyed Dionysius for seven days and then, unused to the labour and worn by the sun, refused to go on and sat in the shade on the island."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus refuse to say who fought bravely?","a":"Because the Ionians all blamed each other afterwards, so he declines to apportion the courage and the blame between them."}]},
{"id":"gr-397","num":397,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says the Persians mined the walls and took the city in the sixth year of the revolt, and after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the survivors were settled by Darius on the Tigris.","questions":["An oracle had promised that the city would become a banquet and glorious gifts for many, which Herodotus reckoned fulfilled by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the burning of Didyma.","Greek writers blamed luxury and faction rather than siegecraft for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a proverb ran that Milesians had once been brave."],"answer":"sack of Miletus","answerText":"sack of Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th year of the revolt</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 493 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>sack of Miletus</b> in 494 BCE ended the Ionian Revolt on land, and the Greeks remembered it less as a defeat than as a city being taken off the map. Miletus had been the greatest of the Ionian cities, and Ephorus credited its people with beating the Scythians and planting colonies all round the Hellespont and the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek writers explained its fall by luxury and faction rather than by siegecraft, and Heraclides of Pontus tells how the people trampled their enemies' children under oxen and the rich later burnt their enemies alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A proverb ran that Milesians had once been brave, and Aristotle is quoted for the judgement that pleasure had dissolved everything valiant in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Lade the Persians besieged the city by sea and by land, mined its walls and used every device against it, taking it in the sixth year from Aristagoras' revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the men were killed, the women and children were made slaves, and Herodotus says the calamity matched an oracle that had promised Miletus would become a banquet and glorious gifts for many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The captives were taken to Susa and settled by Darius at Ampe, near the mouth of the Tigris, while the Persians kept the plain and gave the hill country to Carians from Pedasa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus writes that Miletus was left empty of Milesians, and those who escaped sailed with the Samians to Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oracle of Apollo at Didyma was plundered and burnt, though Strabo puts that at the order of Xerxes and says the priestly Branchidae gave up the god's treasure and fled with the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias makes it Xerxes as well, who took the bronze Apollo that Seleucus later sent back, while a charcoal layer dug on the Taxiarchis hill at Didyma fits either date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Miletus_Delphinion_in_2015_3355.jpg/1280px-Miletus_Delphinion_in_2015_3355.jpg","title":"The Delphinion at Miletus","desc":"The sanctuary of Apollo Delphinios in the centre of Miletus, now an empty rectangle of scattered marble in the grass. Herodotus ends his account of the siege by writing that Miletus was left empty of Milesians.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A wide flat archaeological enclosure of grass and low scrub, edged and crossed by scattered blocks and slabs of white marble, with dry hills and a hazy mountain range beyond."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.18–6.22, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=19. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.5, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.16.3, 8.46.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=8:chapter=46:section=3. [Open access]","Athenaeus, <i>Deipnosophists</i> 12.26, trans. C. D. Yonge, attalus.org, https://www.attalus.org/old/athenaeus12a.html. [Open access]","Udo Schlotzhauer, review of <i>Der archaische Heiligtumsbefund vom Taxiarchis-Hügel in Didyma</i>, by Helga Bumke, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2025.02.04, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2025/2025.02.04/. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran","anatolia"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Greek writers explain the fall by luxury rather than siegecraft?","a":"Because a proverb ran that Milesians had once been brave, and Aristotle is quoted for the judgement that pleasure had dissolved everything valiant in the city."},{"q":"Why is the city said to have been emptied rather than merely taken?","a":"Because most of the men were killed, the women and children enslaved, the captives settled at Ampe near the mouth of the Tigris, and the hill country given to Carians from Pedasa."},{"q":"Why is the burning of the oracle at Didyma hard to date?","a":"Because Strabo and Pausanias put it under Xerxes rather than in this sack, and a charcoal layer dug on the Taxiarchis hill fits either date."}]},
{"id":"gr-398","num":398,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Athens fined <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a thousand drachmas and forbade his play for ever, because a tragedy on the fall of Miletus had set the whole theatre weeping.","questions":["Herodotus and Strabo give different titles to the lost tragedy by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which nothing else survives, and Callisthenes is Strabo's authority for the fine.","In Aristophanes' <i>Wasps</i> old jurymen hum the sweet songs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose tragic victory of 476 BCE Themistocles paid for as choregos."],"answer":"Phrynichus","answerText":"Phrynichus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Miletus fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 drachmas</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later victory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">476 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Phrynichus</b> was an Athenian tragic poet of the early 5th century BCE, and he is remembered chiefly for one lost play that cost him a fine and was forbidden for ever. Soon after Miletus fell to the Persians in 494 BCE he put a tragedy on that disaster before an Athenian audience, and Herodotus says the whole theatre fell to weeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They fined him a thousand drachmas for reminding them of a calamity that touched them so closely, and forbade anyone ever to stage the play again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing of it survives but the title, which Herodotus gives as <i>The Fall of Miletus</i> and Strabo, citing Callisthenes, as <i>The Taking of Miletus by Darius</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus sets that grief against the Sybarites, who made no such return although every Milesian, young and old, had shaved his head in mourning when Sybaris was taken by Croton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus is precise about the ground of the fine: not that the play was bad, but that it recalled a calamity the Athenians felt as their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither the fine nor the ban ended his career, and he won the tragic prize in 476 BCE, with Themistocles paying as his choregos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch quotes the tablet Themistocles set up to record it, which names the choregos, the poet and the archon of the year and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aeschylus later staged the <i>Persians</i>, a tragedy on the same war set in Susa among Persian elders waiting for news, so that the grief on stage was the enemy's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens went on singing Phrynichus long afterwards: in Aristophanes' <i>Wasps</i> the old jurymen come through the streets before dawn with lanterns, humming the sweet old songs of his <i>Sidonian Women</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Athen_Theatre_of_Dionysus_BW_2017-10-09_14-29-49.jpg/1280px-Athen_Theatre_of_Dionysus_BW_2017-10-09_14-29-49.jpg","title":"The theatre of Dionysus","desc":"The theatre of Dionysus below the Acropolis at Athens, where the tragedies were staged. What stands is later work in stone; the theatre that wept at Phrynichus' play was largely wood.","credit":"Photograph by Berthold Werner, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A ruined open-air theatre seen from above: a paved semicircular orchestra at the centre, curved tiers of broken stone seating rising around it, and a scatter of fallen blocks and dark trees behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=21. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.7, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=14:chapter=1:section=7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 5.4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=5. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 1–25, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=1. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 219–220, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0044:card=220. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","people","history","literature","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the poet fined for a play the audience wept at?","a":"Because Herodotus is precise about the ground: not that the play was bad, but that it recalled a calamity the Athenians felt as their own."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus set the Sybarites against the Athenians here?","a":"Because every Milesian, young and old, had shaved his head in mourning when Sybaris was taken by Croton, and the Sybarites made no such return."},{"q":"Why is the ban not evidence that his career was ended?","a":"Because he won the tragic prize in 476 BCE with Themistocles as his choregos, and Athens was still singing his Sidonian Women in Aristophanes's day."}]},
{"id":"gr-399","num":399,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The fleet was wrecked rounding Athos and the Brygi wounded its general by night, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned back without reaching Athens or Eretria.","questions":["Herodotus records that at the start of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Ionian tyrants were deposed and democracies set up, which he tells for the wonder of Greeks who doubt a Persian argued for democracy.","About 300 ships and more than 20,000 men were lost on Athos during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some drowned, some frozen and some, Herodotus says, taken by wild beasts."],"answer":"Mardonius' campaign","answerText":"Mardonius' campaign","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">492 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 300</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Men lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Mardonius' campaign</b> of 492 BCE was the first Persian expedition sent against Athens and Eretria, and it turned back within the year without reaching either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius replaced his other generals and gave it to Mardonius son of Gobryas, a young man lately married to the king's daughter Artozostre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sailing along Ionia he did something Herodotus records for the wonder of Greeks who doubt a Persian ever argued for democracy: he deposed the Ionian tyrants and set up democracies in their cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army crossed the Hellespont and marched through Europe, the fleet took Thasos without a blow, and Macedonia was added to the subject peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin has Persia already bound to the Macedonian house a generation earlier, when Megabazus demanded hostages of Amyntas and Bubares married Amyntas' daughter instead of fighting him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> From Acanthus, which Strabo places on the isthmus of Athos and Pliny on the far side of it, the fleet put out to round the promontory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Acte runs a long way into the Aegean and ends in a high mountain looking towards it, and Thucydides describes the small towns along it, peopled by mixed races speaking two languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A north wind, great and irresistible, caught the ships as they passed and drove many of them onto Athos, and Herodotus reports about 300 ships lost and more than 20,000 men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some were dashed on the rocks, some drowned, some died of cold and some, he says, were carried off by the wild beasts of that coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Brygi of Thrace then attacked the camp in Macedonia by night and wounded Mardonius, and though he subdued them he led the army home, an expedition Herodotus calls inglorious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Mount_Athos%2C_from_sea_level_to_top.jpg/1280px-Mount_Athos%2C_from_sea_level_to_top.jpg","title":"The flank of Athos","desc":"The wooded slopes and grey crags of Mount Athos falling to the sea. Herodotus says a north wind drove the Persian fleet onto this coast, and that some of the men who reached the shore were taken by wild beasts.","credit":"Photograph by Marooned, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)","alt":"A steep wooded mountainside of dark green trees broken by pale grey crags, descending from left to right under a deep blue sky, with a small cluster of white buildings low on the slope and the sea just visible at the far right."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.43–6.45, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=44. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7, fragment 31, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=7:chapter=fragments. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 4.109, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=4:chapter=109. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>Natural History</i> 4.17, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=4:chapter=17. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 7.3–4, trans. John Selby Watson, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 70–72, https://archive.org/details/justincornelius00eutrgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why is this general's Ionian settlement recorded for the wonder of Greeks?","a":"Because he deposed the Ionian tyrants and set up democracies in their cities, which Herodotus notes for those who doubt a Persian ever argued for democracy."},{"q":"Why did the fleet come to grief at Athos?","a":"Because Acte runs a long way into the Aegean and ends in a high mountain, and a great north wind caught the ships as they rounded it, losing about 300 and more than 20,000 men."},{"q":"Why did the army turn back without reaching its targets?","a":"Because after the wreck the Brygi of Thrace attacked the camp in Macedonia by night and wounded the general, and though he subdued them he led the army home."}]},
{"id":"gr-400","num":400,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The heralds who asked Athens and Sparta for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were thrown into a pit and a well, and told to fetch the king's tokens from down there.","questions":["Aegina gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Darius, and Athens gladly took that as a pretext to go to Sparta and accuse the islanders of betraying Greece.","Darius asked the Scythians and the Macedonians for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> long before he asked the Greeks, and his own inscription instead counts 23 lands that brought him tribute."],"answer":"earth and water","answerText":"earth and water","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Darius' heralds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 491 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xerxes' heralds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 481 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Earth and water</b> were what a Persian king's heralds asked for as a token of submission, and a city that gave them had answered the question of war or surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says Darius sent heralds through Greece to learn whether the Greeks meant to fight him or give themselves up, and set his coastal cities building warships and horse transports at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The demand was older than that: Darius had sent it to the Scythian king Idanthyrsus in the field, and Persian envoys had asked it of Amyntas of Macedonia, who gave it readily and feasted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many mainland Greeks gave earth and water, and so did every island the heralds reached, Aegina among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens took that as a move against itself, and went to Sparta to accuse the Aeginetans of betraying Greece, gladly using the gift as a pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Athens and Sparta answered differently: the heralds were thrown into the pit at Athens and into a well at Sparta, and told to fetch the king's earth and water from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus will not say what befell Athens for it, and doubts the usual explanation, but he records that the anger of Agamemnon's herald Talthybius settled on Sparta until two men went to Xerxes to die for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias answers the question Herodotus leaves open, saying the wrath fell at Athens not on the city but on the house of Miltiades, who was responsible for the heralds' deaths there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch adds that Themistocles had the King's interpreter put to death by decree for prostituting the speech of Greece to barbarian terms, and Diodorus records a later vote to tithe the Greeks who went over willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Darius' own inscription at Behistun counts submission differently, listing 23 lands that became subject by the grace of Auramazda and brought him tribute, which is submission in the language of provinces and taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Persepolis_-_Royal_audience_low-relief.jpg","title":"A royal audience","desc":"An Achaemenid relief of a royal audience, the king enthroned with the crown prince behind him while an official bows before him with his hand raised to his mouth. It is the gesture of submission that the demand for earth and water asked for at a distance.","credit":"Photograph by Philippe Chavin, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)","alt":"A dark stone relief in a museum behind a rope barrier: a bearded king in a long robe sits on a high chair holding a staff, a second figure stands behind him, and to the right a man bows slightly with one hand raised to his lips, with two incense burners between them."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.126, 5.18, 6.48–6.49, 7.133–7.134, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=48. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 6.2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=6. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.12.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=12:section=7. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), 4–5, https://archive.org/details/sculpturesinscri00brituoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["practice","concept","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a king's heralds ask for earth and water?","a":"Because giving them answered the question of war or surrender, so Darius sent heralds through Greece to learn whether the Greeks meant to fight him or give themselves up."},{"q":"Why did Athens treat Aegina's submission as an attack on itself?","a":"Because it went to Sparta to accuse the Aeginetans of betraying Greece, gladly using the gift as a pretext in a quarrel it already had."},{"q":"Why does the empire's own record describe submission differently?","a":"Because Darius's inscription lists 23 lands that became subject by the grace of Auramazda and brought him tribute, which is submission in the language of provinces and taxes."}]},
{"id":"wh-251","num":251,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Land granted to the king's own kin after the fall of the Shang seeded the regional states of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and those states outlived it as independent powers.","questions":["Firm Chinese chronology begins only in 841 BCE, so the conquest that opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a date reconstructed from astronomy and inscriptions rather than one anybody recorded.","A capital of some 8.62 square kilometres (3.33 square miles) on the Feng river inside modern Xi'an served the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose westernmost vassal seat lay at Yaoheyuan in Ningxia."],"answer":"Western Zhou","answerText":"Western Zhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1045 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">877 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Firm dates from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">841 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"西周","hanzi":"西周","pinyin":"Xī Zhōu","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Xī Zhōu</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Western Zhou</b> is the first phase of Zhou rule in China, commonly dated from 1045 to 771 BCE and followed by the Eastern Zhou from 770 to 221 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its founding act was the conquest of the Shang, which the Xia–Shang–Zhou Chronology Project dated to 20 January 1046 BCE by retrocalculating the celestial positions recorded for King Wu's attack, though its own report carries rival proposals of 1044 and 1027.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Firm Chinese chronology begins only in 841 BCE, the first year of the Gonghe reign, from which Sima Qian could compile an absolute year-by-year table of events in twelve states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The royal domain lay in the Guanzhong basin of what is now central Shaanxi, and a capital called Fengyi stood on the western bank of the Feng river inside modern Xi'an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That site covers about 8.62 square kilometres (3.33 square miles) and has given up artificial river channels, rammed-earth palace foundations, housing, cemeteries, buried hoards of bronze and whole quarters of workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One workshop fired coarse sand-tempered cooking pots between 877 and 771 BCE in kilns stoked with grasses, millet, rice and wheat residues and the wood of oak, elm and sweetgum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To hold the conquered territory the court granted land to members of the royal Ji clan and to allied lineages, whose regional states served as political alliances and military outposts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bronze inscriptions distinguish the rulers of those satellite states who belonged to the royal lineage from the heads of merely affiliated ones, and many of the domains grew into the independent states of the Eastern Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yaoheyuan in Ningxia shows how far that reached, a walled town of about 96 hectares (240 acres) with a palace foundation, a bronze foundry and inscribed oracle bones, probably the westernmost vassal capital of the early Western Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The highly radiogenic lead that is the chemical signature of Shang bronze appears to disappear under the Zhou, though the data on either side of that short window are thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","bronze age","china"],"sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Zhikun Ma, Shu Liu, Xuan Yi, Liya Tang, Zhongyang Fu et al., “Fuel types and use in late Western Zhou (877 – 771 BCE) industrial contexts in Northwest China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 20166, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11364631/. [Open access]","Zexian Huang, Qiang Ma, Chengrui Zhang, Ruoxin Cheng, Furen Hou et al., “Early Evidence of Post-Mortem Fetal Extrusion in Equids: A Case from the Western Zhou Period (1045–771 BC) Site of Yaoheyuan in Northwestern China,” <i>Animals</i> 14, no. 14 (2024): 2106, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani14142106. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, Jessica Rawson and A. Mark Pollard, “Beyond ritual bronzes: identifying multiple sources of highly radiogenic lead across Chinese history,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 11770, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30275-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Li_Gui%2C_2016-09-09_01.jpg/1280px-Li_Gui%2C_2016-09-09_01.jpg","title":"The Li gui, a Western Zhou food vessel","desc":"A bronze gui in the National Museum of China, cast in the earliest years of Zhou rule. Its inscription records the day of the conquest of the Shang, and the seven graphs at the heart of that passage have been read eight different ways, which is why the vessel sits at the centre of the argument over when the conquest happened.","credit":"Photograph by Siyuwj, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A round bronze bowl with two large loop handles, standing on a square pedestal base, its surface weathered grey-green and cast in bands of coiled relief ornament, lit inside a museum case."},"why":[{"q":"Why does firm Chinese chronology begin only in 841 BCE?","a":"Because that is the first year of the Gonghe reign, from which Sima Qian could compile an absolute year-by-year table of events in twelve states."},{"q":"Why is the date of the conquest itself uncertain?","a":"Because it was reached by retrocalculating the celestial positions recorded for King Wu's attack, and the project's own report carries rival proposals of 1044 and 1027 beside its 1046 BCE."},{"q":"Why did the court grant land to clan members and allied lineages?","a":"Because that was how it held the conquered territory: the regional states served as political alliances and military outposts, and many grew into the independent states of the Eastern Zhou."}]},
{"id":"wh-252","num":252,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The Zhou justified their conquest by arguing that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had already passed once before, from the Xia to the Shang, and so could pass again.","questions":["Mencius made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a theory of history, holding that benevolence won the Three Dynasties their thrones and the want of it lost them again.","Floods, earthquakes and civil war were read as signs that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was being withdrawn, a doctrine Xunzi rejected by insisting Heaven's course is constant."],"answer":"Mandate of Heaven","answerText":"Mandate of Heaven","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duke of Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1042 – 1035 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Western Han</span><span class=\"dt-v\">206 BCE – 8 CE</span></div>","traditional":"天命","hanzi":"天命","pinyin":"Tiānmìng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Tiānmìng</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>, <i>tianming</i>, is the doctrine that Heaven grants the right to rule and takes it back from a house that governs badly, so power is held on terms, not by right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Zhou proclaimed it to justify their conquest of the Shang, and the Duke of Zhou argued from an earlier transfer, Xia to Shang, conceding that the defeated dynasty had genuinely held it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Book of Documents</i> states the condition in its own words, that the Supreme Ruler is not invariable in his decree, which its commentators gloss as the decree leaving one family for another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Addressing the conquered, the Zhou tell the people of the Shang that the kings of Zhou now hold the celestial decree, and reproach them for triflingly rejecting it themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Shang had their own version of the idea in Shangdi and the divine command, and the graph for Heaven appears in their oracle bones only in the form meaning 'great'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Early Zhou documents show not the swagger of a victorious people but what has been called a concerned consciousness, and beside it the words for virtue and reverence take on moral weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mencius made the idea a theory of history: only a moral man receives the Mandate, and benevolence was how the Three Dynasties gained the throne and the want of it how they lost it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He set a limit on it too: a sovereign may present a man to Heaven, but cannot make Heaven give that man the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xunzi rejected the doctrine outright, insisting that Heaven's course is constant, unchanged by the sage Yao and by the tyrant Jie alike, so order and disorder are not Heaven's doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its afterlife is long: under the Western Han the evolving concept shaped how disasters were answered, and the same two characters, read as the nature Heaven confers, were taken by Song scholars for human nature and by Ming Jesuits for the soul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","religion","philosophy","china"],"sources":["W. H. Medhurst, trans., <i>Ancient China. The Shoo King, or the Historical Classic</i> (Shanghae: Mission Press, 1846), “The Instructions of E” and “The Numerous Regions,” https://archive.org/details/ancientchinashoo00confuoft. [Open access]","Maja Maria Kosec, “Humanization of Chinese Religion: From Heaven (tian 天) to Ritual (li 礼) in Xu Fuguan and Li Zehou,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 11, no. 3 (2023): 203–27, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2023.11.3.203-227. [Open access]","Dawid Rogacz, “In the Shadow of the Decay. The Philosophy of History of Mencius and Xunzi,” <i>Asian Studies</i> 5, no. 1 (2017): 147–71, https://doi.org/10.4312/as.2017.5.1.147-171. [Open access]","Zhen Yang and Francis Ludlow, “Climatic and societal impacts of volcanic eruptions in the Western Han Dynasty (206 BCE–8 CE): a comparative study,” <i>Climate of the Past</i> 21, no. 11 (2025): 2061–81, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-21-2061-2025. [Open access]","Qinghe Xiao, “The Christian interpretations on the nature of heaven's mandate in late Ming and early Qing dynasties,” <i>HTS Teologiese Studies/Theological Studies</i> 80, no. 3 (2024): a9994, https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v80i3.9994. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Da_Yu_ding_inscription.jpg/1280px-Da_Yu_ding_inscription.jpg","title":"The inscription of the Da Yu ding","desc":"A rubbing of the long inscription cast inside the Da Yu ding, a bronze cauldron of the early Western Zhou. It opens by recalling that King Wen received Heaven's great charge, and it is one of the earliest surviving statements of the claim in the Zhou's own words rather than in a text handed down.","credit":"Public domain rubbing uploaded by User:Mountain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A rubbing in two panels, white archaic Chinese characters on black, set in tight vertical columns; the graphs are angular and pictorial, quite unlike modern script."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Zhou concede that the Shang had genuinely held the mandate?","a":"Because the Duke of Zhou argued from an earlier transfer, Xia to Shang, so the doctrine needed the defeated dynasty to have held it legitimately before losing it."},{"q":"Why does Mencius set a limit on the doctrine?","a":"Because on his account a sovereign may present a man to Heaven but cannot make Heaven give that man the throne."},{"q":"Why did Xunzi reject it outright?","a":"Because he insisted Heaven's course is constant, unchanged by the sage Yao and by the tyrant Jie alike, so order and disorder are not Heaven's doing."}]},
{"id":"wh-253","num":253,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Half a million images spanning three millennia show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not steadily simplify, and that the modern forms are in fact more complex than the earliest.","questions":["Because the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were built from visual metaphor rather than sound, specialists count the system among the world's few independent inventions of writing.","The five recognised stages of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run from the oracle bones through bronze, seal and official forms to the regular script still written today."],"answer":"Chinese characters","answerText":"Chinese characters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First attested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze script</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1045 – 771 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"漢字","hanzi":"汉字","pinyin":"Hànzì","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Hànzì</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Chinese characters</b> are the units of the oldest writing system still in daily use, and one of only three scripts known to have been invented from nothing rather than adapted from a neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest large body of them is cut into oracle bones and cast into bronzes, from the late Shang between 1250 and 1045 BCE and the Western Zhou between 1045 and 771 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their shapes were built by visual metaphor and metonymy, a part standing for the whole or a picture extended to a related idea, rather than by writing down the sounds of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sound entered the system only as a secondary refinement, so a written form maps straight onto a meaning instead of reaching it through a pronunciation, which is what makes the older sound-first accounts of how writing evolves look too narrow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same metaphorical machinery is at work in the two other pristine inventions, the Mesopotamian and the Egyptian scripts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Five stages of the script are conventionally distinguished: oracle bone, bronze, seal, official and the regular script written today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything older than the small seal script, which appeared once the first Qin emperor had unified the country, is grouped together as the ancient characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The familiar belief that the characters have grown steadily simpler turns out to be a claim that can be measured, and half a million images spanning more than 3,000 years do not bear it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There is no consistent simplification through time, and modern characters are, contrary to the popular belief, visually more complex than their earliest known counterparts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Simplicity appears to trade against distinctiveness, and across writing systems generally the complexity of a character depends chiefly on how large a linguistic unit it stands for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","language","writing","china"],"sources":["Ludovica Ottaviano and Silvia Ferrara, “Mechanisms at the core of the Chinese script invention,” <i>Cognitive Linguistics</i> 37, no. 1 (2026): 39–62, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949599/. [Open access]","Mengru Wang, Yu Cai, Li Gao, Ruichen Feng, Qingju Jiao et al., “Study on the evolution of Chinese characters based on few-shot learning: From oracle bone inscriptions to regular script,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272974, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272974. [Open access]","Simon J. Han, Piers Kelly, James Winters and Charles Kemp, “Simplification Is Not Dominant in the Evolution of Chinese Characters,” <i>Open Mind</i> 6 (2022): 264–79, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9987343/. [Open access]","Ludovica Ottaviano, Kathryn Kelley, Mattia Cartolano and Silvia Ferrara, “Metaphors and the Invention of Writing,” <i>Topics in Cognitive Science</i> 18, no. 1 (2026): 245–63, https://doi.org/10.1111/tops.12768. [Open access]","Helena Miton and Olivier Morin, “Graphic complexity in writing systems,” <i>Cognition</i> 214 (2021): 104771, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8346946/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png/1280px-%E9%A6%AC-Chinese_characters_evolution.png.jpg","title":"The character for horse, traced through the scripts","desc":"A modern map of the descent of one character, 馬 for horse, from the Shang oracle bones and bronzes down through Western and Eastern Zhou, the Qin small seal and the Han clerical hands to the regular script and its simplified form. The red crosses mark the regional Warring States hands that died out, and the chart is careful to say that nothing above its top line is clear evidence.","credit":"Diagram by Panates, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A branching chart of brush and incised forms of one Chinese character, spiky and pictorial at the top under labels for the Shang and Zhou, growing squarer and more regular downwards through Qin and Han, with several side branches struck through by red crosses and two settled modern forms at the foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the older sound-first accounts of writing look too narrow here?","a":"Because these shapes were built by visual metaphor and metonymy, with sound entering only as a secondary refinement, so a written form maps straight onto a meaning rather than through a pronunciation."},{"q":"Why does the belief that characters grew simpler not hold?","a":"Because half a million images spanning more than 3,000 years show no consistent simplification, and modern characters are visually more complex than their earliest known counterparts."},{"q":"Why might simplicity not be an unmixed good in a script?","a":"Because it appears to trade against distinctiveness, and across writing systems a character's complexity depends chiefly on how large a linguistic unit it stands for."}]},
{"id":"wh-254","num":254,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"Proteins recovered from soil in three tombs at Jiahu put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in China 8,500 years ago, long before any surviving cloth of it.","questions":["Residues in the ash above the Sanxingdui pits show <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> being used as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth.","Before the mulberry moth was domesticated, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was drawn from several wild species, and the Palmyra finds turn out to be Indian tasar rather than Chinese."],"answer":"silk","answerText":"silk","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest silk</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanxingdui pits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"絲","hanzi":"丝","pinyin":"Sī","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sī</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Silk</b> is a protein fibre spun by moth caterpillars into a cocoon, and the protein that makes up most of the thread is fibroin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has been a luxury throughout recorded history, and the raising of silkworms mattered to ancient trade as much as it did to technology and to culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oldest direct evidence comes from three tombs at the Neolithic site of Jiahu, where mass spectrometry found the key peptides of silk fibroin in soil 8,500 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rough weaving tools and bone needles were excavated with them, so the people buried there could probably weave and sew, which no cloth of that age survives to show directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where and when sericulture began is nevertheless still called an unsolved question in archaeology, because textiles rarely survive and the species that made a given fibre is hard to establish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Two methods now separate them: antibodies raised against the two silks show no cross-reaction with each other, and proteomics supplies biomarkers for the domestic mulberry moth and the Chinese oak moth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before the mulberry silkworm was domesticated, silks were taken from a range of moth species with regional specificity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Applied to archaeological material from Palmyra, the same kind of analysis identified wild silk from the Indian tasar moth rather than anything Chinese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Bronze Age China the fibre also carried meaning, and residues in the ash layers over the sacrificial pits at Sanxingdui show silk used as a material carrier for communication between Heaven and Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tree that feeds the domestic moth travelled as well: white mulberry comes from East Asia and particularly China, and reached Central Asia through exchanges that ran along the Silk Road in both directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","technology","china"],"sources":["Yuxuan Gong, Li Li, Decai Gong, Hao Yin and Juzhong Zhang, “Biomolecular Evidence of Silk from 8,500 Years Ago,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 12 (2016): e0168042, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0168042. [Open access]","Hailing Zheng, Ruling Yang, Jianbo Guo, Zhenbin Xie, Jingxuan Liu et al., “Evidence of the use of silk by bronze age civilization for sacrificial purposes in the Yangtze River basin of China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 29175, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-78687-7. [Open access]","Boyoung Lee, Elisabete Pires, A. Mark Pollard and James S. O. McCullagh, “Species identification of silks by protein mass spectrometry reveals evidence of wild silk use in antiquity,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 4579, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-08167-3. [Open access]","Jincui Gu, Qingqing Li, Boyi Chen, Chengfeng Xu, Hailing Zheng et al., “Species identification of Bombyx mori and Antheraea pernyi silk via immunology and proteomics,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 9381, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-45698-8. [Open access]","Caihui Wang, Yutian Yang, Lu Yang, Xue Ling, Zhikun Ma et al., “The chloroplast genome elucidates the origin of mulberry in Central Asia,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1592308, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1592308. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Life_cycle_of_a_silkworm_from_egg_to_moth_-_DPLA_-_2f77d2d5eff44d5b14a55e47dec2687d.jpg/1280px-Life_cycle_of_a_silkworm_from_egg_to_moth_-_DPLA_-_2f77d2d5eff44d5b14a55e47dec2687d.jpg","title":"The silkworm from egg to moth","desc":"An engraved plate of 1882 from an American instruction book on silk culture, laying the whole life cycle out on a mulberry branch and keying it below: the moth laying eggs, the worm at three days and at ten, its moults, the full-grown worm about to spin, the cocoon in its floss, the cocoon opened to show the chrysalis, and the moth emerging.","credit":"Plate from the Women's Silk Culture Association, 1882, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A pale engraved plate showing a mulberry branch with caterpillars of several sizes feeding on the leaves, moths with folded wings, two loops of raw silk thread at the lower left, and a cocoon cut open to show the chrysalis inside, with a numbered key printed underneath."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the beginning of sericulture still called unsolved?","a":"Because textiles rarely survive and the species that made a given fibre is hard to establish, so direct evidence is scarce and hard to attribute."},{"q":"Why does the Jiahu evidence rest on chemistry rather than cloth?","a":"Because no cloth of that age survives: mass spectrometry found the key peptides of silk fibroin in 8,500-year-old soil, with weaving tools and bone needles beside it."},{"q":"Why does identifying the moth species matter?","a":"Because before the mulberry silkworm was domesticated silks came from a range of moths, and analysis at Palmyra identified wild silk from the Indian tasar moth rather than anything Chinese."}]},
{"id":"wh-255","num":255,"category":"The Indus and early China","question":"The earliest working of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in China belongs to the Xinglongwa culture of the Liao River, and the craft reached its height at Niuheliang two millennia later.","questions":["From the middle of the 4th millennium BCE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shifted from personal ornament to ritual object, appearing in rich burials just as elite groups were emerging.","No text before the Eastern Zhou explains the cong tube or the bi disc, so the meaning of the abstract <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shapes is genuinely lost."],"answer":"jade","answerText":"jade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6200 – 5400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hongshan peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3200 – 2000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"玉","hanzi":"玉","pinyin":"Yù","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Yù</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jade</b> in China is chiefly nephrite, a stone made of two amphibole minerals, tremolite and actinolite, which are told apart by the ratio of magnesium to iron in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prehistoric jades are found in large numbers across the mainland and throughout the Neolithic, and their distribution is divided into seven regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest is the Liao River, where jade use begins with the Xinglongwa culture of about 6200 to 5400 BCE and craftsmanship reaches its height in the late Hongshan, about 3500 to 3000 BCE, at Niuheliang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the lower Yangzi around Lake Tai jade first appears in the Majiabang and Songze cultures and becomes prevalent in the Liangzhu, about 3200 to 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Early jades are mostly personal ornaments with little relation to the wealth of a grave, but from the middle of the 4th millennium BCE ritual items come to dominate, alongside the first elite groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three forms recur across the regions: the cong, a tube square outside and cylindrical within, often carved with face masks; the bi, a flat disc with a small central hole; and the yazhang tablet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Liangzhu bi in Cleveland, dated between 3300 and 2200 BCE, is 32 centimetres (12.5 inches) across with an opening of only 5.4 centimetres (2.1 inches).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No text earlier than the Eastern Zhou says what those abstract shapes meant, and the familiar reading of a round heaven and a square earth comes from much later writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One proposal derives the bi from the discoidal spindle whorl, a flat perforated disc that was an ordinary Neolithic tool, linking the two on formal, historical and contextual grounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other jades are shaped like or decorated with birds, turtles, cicadas and silkworms, and Shang diviners later used the bones of animals the Neolithic had carved in jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","art","china"],"sources":["Li Liu, “‘The Products of Minds as Well as of Hands’: Production of Prestige Goods in the Neolithic and Early State Periods of China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 42, no. 1 (2003): 1–40, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17179. [Open access]","Jean M. Green, “Unraveling the Enigma of the Bi: The Spindle Whorl as the Model of the Ritual Disk,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 32, no. 1 (1993): 105–24, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17022. [Open access]","Cleveland Museum of Art, “Ceremonial Disc (Bi) 玉璧, 3300–2200 BCE,” accession 1917.974, accessed 29 August 2026, https://clevelandart.org/art/1917.974. [Open access]","Rui Oliveira Lopes, “Securing the Harmony between the High and the Low: Power Animals and Symbols of Political Authority in Ancient Chinese Jades and Bronzes,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 53, no. 2 (2014): 195–225, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/42779. [Open access]","Yi Bao, Chaohong Zhao, Yuesheng Li and Xuemei Yun, “A method of determining heated ancient nephrite jades in China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 8 (2018): 13523, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-018-30564-w. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Ritual_object_(cong)%2C_China%2C_probably_Jiangsu_or_Zhejiang_province%2C_Neolithic_period%2C_Liangzhu_culture%2C_3300-2000_BC%2C_nephrite_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00793.jpg/1280px-Ritual_object_(cong)%2C_China%2C_probably_Jiangsu_or_Zhejiang_province%2C_Neolithic_period%2C_Liangzhu_culture%2C_3300-2000_BC%2C_nephrite_-_Arthur_M._Sackler_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC00793.jpg","title":"A Liangzhu cong","desc":"A nephrite cong of the Liangzhu culture, probably from Jiangsu or Zhejiang, in the Arthur M. Sackler Museum at Harvard. The tube is square on the outside and cylindrical within, and each corner is cut into tiers carrying the reduced eyes and bars of the face motif.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall stone tube of dark grey-brown nephrite standing on a white plinth, square in plan with a round bore through it, its four corners stepped into horizontal tiers incised with pairs of small circles and straight bars."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the familiar meaning of the cong and bi doubtful?","a":"Because no text earlier than the Eastern Zhou says what those abstract shapes meant, and the reading of a round heaven and a square earth comes from much later writing."},{"q":"Why is the shift from ornament to ritual object significant?","a":"Because early jades are mostly personal ornaments with little relation to a grave's wealth, and from the middle of the 4th millennium BCE ritual items come to dominate alongside the first elite groups."},{"q":"Why has the bi been derived from a spindle whorl?","a":"Because a discoidal whorl is a flat perforated disc that was an ordinary Neolithic tool, and the two have been linked on formal, historical and contextual grounds."}]},
{"id":"wh-256","num":256,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Because it is named for a technology rather than fixed to a date, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins at different times in different places and is barely used of the Americas at all.","questions":["Lead in tin ingots recovered off Israel gives their parent ores an age of about 291 million years, putting the reach of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tin trade as far as Europe.","Copper and tin seldom occur together, so almost every culture of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to join exchange networks running over very long distances to get both."],"answer":"Bronze Age","answerText":"Bronze Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Near East</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eastern collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"青銅時代","hanzi":"青铜时代","pinyin":"Qīngtóng Shídài","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qīngtóng Shídài</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Bronze Age</b> is the middle period of the three-age system, the age in which, on the definition that named it, weapons and cutting tools were made of copper or bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is named for a technology rather than pinned to a date, so it opens at different times in different places, and the phrase has meant different things in different disciplines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Egyptologists barely need it, since the Old Kingdom is the Levantine Early Bronze Age and the Middle Kingdom the Levantine Middle Bronze Age, and Egypt periodises its own history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The term is largely absent from the archaeology of Oceania, the Americas and Africa south of the Sahara, and remarkably resilient everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making the metal took a long chain of specialists, from mining and smelting to casting, annealing and recasting, which presumes a community able to feed people who grow no food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Bronze therefore travels with cities, complex states and writing, which appears in Mesopotamia and Egypt late in the 4th millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Copper and tin seldom lie together, so almost every Bronze Age culture joined exchange networks over vast distances, and the period looks less like a stage than a web.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far that web reached can now be measured: lead in tin ingots recovered off the coast of Israel gives their parent ores a model age of about 291 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That excludes Anatolian, central Asian and Egyptian deposits, which formed either much earlier or much later, and points instead to European tin of the Variscan orogeny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the eastern Mediterranean the linked Aegean, Egyptian, Syro-Palestinian and Hittite world collapsed about 3,200 years ago, which palaeoclimate data from Cyprus tie to abrupt climate change, famine and the invasions recorded as the Sea Peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["era","history","archaeology","technology","bronze age"],"sources":["Julien Cooper and Yegor Grebnev, “Introduction: Bronze Age Civilizations,” <i>Old World: Journal of Ancient Africa and Eurasia</i> 3, no. 1 (2023): 2–4, https://doi.org/10.1163/26670755-20230009. [Open access]","Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, “Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,” in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 65, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “Visible Language: The Earliest Writing Systems,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 15–17, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Daniel Berger, Jeffrey S. Soles, Alessandra R. Giumlia-Mair, Gerhard Brügmann, Ehud Galili et al., “Isotope systematics and chemical composition of tin ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and other Late Bronze Age sites in the eastern Mediterranean Sea: An ultimate key to tin provenance?” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Uluburun_copper_ingot_oxhide_shaped.jpg/1280px-Uluburun_copper_ingot_oxhide_shaped.jpg","title":"A copper ingot from the Uluburun wreck","desc":"An oxhide-shaped copper ingot of about 1300 BCE, raised from the Uluburun shipwreck off the Turkish coast and now in the Bodrum Museum of Underwater Archaeology. The four projecting handles are a standard shipping form, and the wreck also carried the tin ingots whose lead has since been used to argue for a European source.","credit":"Photograph by Zunkir, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A thick rectangular slab of corroded metal, brown and streaked with green, drawn out into a pointed handle at each of its four corners, standing upright on a museum mount in a dark case."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the Bronze Age open at different times in different places?","a":"Because it is named for a technology rather than pinned to a date, and the phrase has meant different things in different disciplines."},{"q":"Why does bronze travel with cities and writing?","a":"Because making it took a long chain of specialists, from mining and smelting to casting, annealing and recasting, which presumes a community able to feed people who grow no food."},{"q":"Why does the period look less like a stage than a web?","a":"Because copper and tin seldom lie together, so almost every Bronze Age culture joined exchange networks over vast distances — tin ingots off Israel point to European ores."}]},
{"id":"wh-257","num":257,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"A tin content above one per cent is usually taken to show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made on purpose rather than smelted by accident from a tin-bearing copper ore.","questions":["At Anyang the purest metal went to the ritual vessels of the highest elite, while lesser burials got <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mixed and recycled from older objects.","A peat bog in Serbia records lead pollution from about 3600 BCE, the earliest such trace in Europe and the cost of making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at scale."],"answer":"bronze","answerText":"bronze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tin bronze known</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 4th millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Balkan lead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 3600 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"青銅","hanzi":"青铜","pinyin":"Qīngtóng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qīngtóng</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Bronze</b> is copper alloyed with tin, and in archaeometallurgy it counts as the real copper alloy of antiquity, made deliberately to improve on what copper alone can do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tin makes the melt more fluid and easier to cast, lowers copper's melting point, and at about a tenth of the mixture stiffens the metal more than arsenic or zinc will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tin content above one per cent is usually read as deliberate alloying rather than an accident of the ore, though some workers set the bar at five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There were four routes to it: adding metallic tin to molten copper, reducing cassiterite in the melt under charcoal at about 1200 °C (2200 °F), smelting a natural copper-tin ore, or co-smelting a copper ore with cassiterite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first bronze was probably an accident of the third kind, recognised afterwards by its golden colour, its hardness and how much better it cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tin was not the only choice, and Anatolian smiths of the Early Bronze Age also alloyed with antimony, nickel and lead before that variety narrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The metal was reused as well as made, and at Anyang what went back into the crucible followed rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ritual vessels for the highest elite were cast from very pure copper by carefully controlled alloying, while lower elites had bronzes made by secondary alloying from metal carrying more impurities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Working out where any of it came from is harder than it looks, because the lead isotope signatures of different ore regions overlap, as a statistical treatment of the candidate sources makes plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The industry left a mark of its own: a peat bog in western Serbia carries anthropogenic lead from about 3600 BCE, the earliest such pollution documented in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","science","bronze age"],"sources":["Omid Oudbashi, S. Mohammadamin Emami and Parviz Davami, “Bronze in Archaeology: A Review of the Archaeometallurgy of Bronze in Ancient Iran,” in <i>Copper Alloys: Early Applications and Current Performance — Enhancing Processes</i>, ed. Luca Collini (Rijeka: InTech, 2012), under “3.3 Tin bronze,” https://cdn.intechopen.com/pdfs/30478/InTech-Bronze_in_archaeology_a_review_of_the_archaeometallurgy_of_bronze_in_ancient_iran.pdf. [Open access]","Gonca Dardeniz, “Why did the use of antimony-bearing alloys in Bronze Age Anatolia fall dormant after the Early Bronze Age? A case study from Resuloğlu (Çorum, Turkey),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 6 (2020): e0234563, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0234563. [Open access]","Jack Longman, Daniel Veres, Walter Finsinger and Vasile Ersek, “Exceptionally high levels of lead pollution in the Balkans from the Early Bronze Age to the Industrial Revolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 25 (2018): E5661–68, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1721546115. [Open access]","Sarah De Ceuster, Jurian Hoogewerff and Patrick Degryse, “Provenancing ancient materials with lead isotopes: overlap uncovered,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 4628, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-88909-1. [Open access]","Ruiliang Liu, A. Mark Pollard, Qin Cao, Cheng Liu, Victoria Sainsbury et al., “Social hierarchy and the choice of metal recycling at Anyang, the last capital of Bronze Age Shang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 18794, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-75920-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Bronze_Age_hoard_(FindID_177968).jpg/1280px-Bronze_Age_hoard_(FindID_177968).jpg","title":"A founder's hoard from Suffolk","desc":"Five pieces of a Late Bronze Age hoard found in Suffolk, each shown front and back against a ruler: two fragments of socketed axes, the end of an unidentified implement, and two lumps of run copper alloy. Broken tools and casting waste collected like this are what a founder's hoard is, though why such deposits were buried is still argued over.","credit":"Photograph from the Portable Antiquities Scheme and the Trustees of the British Museum, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Ten corroded green-brown lumps of metal laid out in a row on a white ground above two rulers and two handwritten finds labels; some are curved fragments of thin cast walls, others are shapeless blobs of solidified metal."},"why":[{"q":"Why is a tin content above one per cent read as deliberate?","a":"Because below that it can be an accident of the ore, so the threshold is what distinguishes intentional alloying — though some workers set the bar at five."},{"q":"Why was the first bronze probably an accident?","a":"Because smelting a natural copper-tin ore would produce it unbidden, and it was recognised afterwards by its golden colour, its hardness and how much better it cast."},{"q":"Why is provenancing bronze harder than it looks?","a":"Because the lead isotope signatures of different ore regions overlap, as a statistical treatment of the candidate sources makes plain."}]},
{"id":"wh-258","num":258,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Ancient DNA from 435 people has traced the ancestry of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back to three genetic clines rather than to any single earlier population.","questions":["Burial mounds dominate the landscape the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left behind, and isotopes from the bones in them show a herding economy of sheep, goat and cattle.","Spreading from Hungary to Kazakhstan by 3000 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rode horses of the line from which all modern domestic stock descends."],"answer":"Yamnaya culture","answerText":"Yamnaya culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Complex</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 2600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ancestors formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Maximal extent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Yamnaya culture</b> is the archaeological complex that appeared across the steppes north of the Black and Caspian seas about 3300 BCE and, by 3000 BCE, reached from Hungary in the west to Kazakhstan in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known above all from burial mounds, which dominate the archaeological landscape of the region and hold most of what survives of its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient DNA from 435 individuals has now placed its origins among the Eneolithic peoples who came before it, and finds three genetic clines rather than one ancestral group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Caucasus-lower Volga cline ran between a Neolithic southern end and a northern end on the lower Volga, and people of that cline contributed about four-fifths of Yamnaya ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moving west along the Dnipro and Don they mixed with local hunter-gatherers to form the Serednii Stih groups, from whom Yamnaya ancestors took shape around 4000 BCE and then grew quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Stable carbon and nitrogen isotopes from 105 people and 50 animals across these mounds show a herding economy built on sheep, goat and cattle on the grasslands north of the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The genetic boundary between the Caucasus groups and the steppe groups beside them is sharp, and it follows the eco-geographic line between mountain and open grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yamnaya groups of about 3200 to 2600 BCE also rode horses of the DOM2 line, the direct ancestors of modern domestic stock, and took them into their diet and their rituals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That mobility is what makes the culture matter beyond its own steppe, since its descendants carried both people and languages far into Europe and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The region stayed a crossroads long afterwards: genomes spanning 7000 BCE to 1800 CE from what is now Ukraine show ancestry there fluctuating from period to period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639 (2025): 132–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11922553/. [Open access]","Corina Knipper, Sabine Reinhold, Julia Gresky, Nataliya Berezina, Claudia Gerling et al., “Diet and subsistence in Bronze Age pastoral communities from the southern Russian steppes and the North Caucasus,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 10 (2020): e0239861, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0239861. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann and Volker Heyd, “Horse genetics, archaeology, and the beginning of riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ady7336. [Open access]","Chuan-Chao Wang, Sabine Reinhold, Alexey Kalmykov, Antje Wissgott, Guido Brandt et al., “Ancient human genome-wide data from a 3000-year interval in the Caucasus corresponds with eco-geographic regions,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 10 (2019): 590, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-08220-8. [Open access]","Lehti Saag, Olga Utevska, Stanislav Zadnikov, Iryna Shramko, Kyrylo Gorbenko et al., “North Pontic crossroads: Mobility in Ukraine from the Bronze Age to the early modern period,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11 (2025): eadr0695, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adr0695. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Kurgans_in_southern_Ukraine.png/1280px-Kurgans_in_southern_Ukraine.png.jpg","title":"Kurgans in southern Ukraine","desc":"Three burial mounds of the steppe standing in worked fields, from a study of the plants that survive on them. Mounds like these were raised from the local loess, most of them between about 5,000 and 700 years ago, and they run from 10 to 110 metres (33 to 360 feet) across and 1 to 15 metres (3 to 49 feet) high.","credit":"Photographs by Iwona Dembicz and colleagues, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Three photographs of low grassy mounds rising out of flat farmland: one seen across a ripe wheat field, one with a van parked at its foot for scale, and a larger one with three people standing on its crest above a green crop."},"locator":{"name":"Yamnaya culture","at":[35.1667,47.8333],"kind":"region","area":[[27.5,46.5],[28.5,49],[33,51],[39,52],[46,52],[52,51.5],[57,50.5],[58,48.5],[54,46.5],[48,45.5],[42,45],[37,45],[32,45.2],[29,45.5]]},"why":[{"q":"Why does the culture matter beyond its own steppe?","a":"Because of its mobility: its descendants carried both people and languages far into Europe and Asia, which is what makes a herding society on the grasslands a subject of wider history."},{"q":"Why do ancient genomes find clines rather than one ancestral group?","a":"Because DNA from 435 individuals places its origins among earlier Eneolithic peoples along three clines, of which the Caucasus-lower Volga one contributed about four-fifths of Yamnaya ancestry."},{"q":"Why is the genetic boundary with the Caucasus so sharp?","a":"Because it follows the eco-geographic line between mountain and open grassland, so the herding economy and the population edge coincide."}]},
{"id":"wh-259","num":259,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"An open dataset traces inherited words across 160 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sorting 25,731 lexemes into 4,981 cognate sets and marking every word known to have been borrowed sideways.","questions":["Anatolian, Tocharian, Greek, Armenian, Baltic, Slavic, Germanic, Italic and Celtic are all branches of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose common ancestor survives only as a reconstruction.","Ancient DNA ties the spread of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to herders of the steppes north of the Black and Caspian seas between about 3300 and 1500 BCE."],"answer":"Indo-European languages","answerText":"Indo-European languages","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dispersal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Languages coded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">160, in 13 clades</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Indo-European languages</b> are a family whose branches include Anatolian, Tocharian, Greek, Armenian, Albanian, Indic, Iranian, Baltic, Slavic, Germanic, Italic and Celtic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are held to descend from one reconstructed ancestor, Proto-Indo-European, recovered from the inherited words the daughter languages still share.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An open dataset now tracks those shared words across 160 languages, sorting 25,731 lexemes into 4,981 cognate sets against 170 reference meanings in the core vocabulary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built by 89 linguists from 355 cited sources and marks every known case of a word borrowed sideways rather than inherited, since borrowing is what most often misleads a family tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reconstruction can be read for content as well as for shape: only one or perhaps two cereal terms can be recovered for the basal stage of the family, while the core stage acquired a fuller set.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That documents a shift from a largely non-agricultural economy to a mixed one of herding and farming between the two stages of the protolanguage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also means the eastern Yamnaya of the Don-Volga steppe, where evidence of farming is lacking, is not a perfect match for the core Indo-European speech community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ancient DNA nonetheless ties the family’s spread to the Yamnaya complex north of the Black and Caspian seas, and puts its dispersal roughly between 3300 and 1500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes from South and Central Asia show steppe ancestry reaching the subcontinent in the 2nd millennium BCE, which is where the Indic branch enters the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Folktales told in these societies track the language tree rather than mere distance, and one of them, <i>The Smith and the Devil</i>, has been traced back to the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","language","genetics","migration"],"sources":["Cormac Anderson, Matthew Scarborough, Lechosław Jocz, Martin Joachim Kümmel, Thomas Jügel et al., “The Indo-European Cognate Relationships dataset,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12 (2025): 1541, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12405575/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639 (2025): 132–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11922553/. [Open access]","Guus Kroonen, Anthony Jakob, Axel I. Palmér, Paulus van Sluis and Andrew Wigman, “Indo-European cereal terminology suggests a Northwest Pontic homeland for the core Indo-European languages,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0275744, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0275744. [Open access]","Sara Graça da Silva and Jamshid J. Tehrani, “Comparative phylogenetic analyses uncover the ancient roots of Indo-European folktales,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 3, no. 1 (2016): 150645, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.150645. [Open access]","Vagheesh M. Narasimhan, Nick Patterson, Priya Moorjani, Nadin Rohland, Rebecca Bernardos et al., “The formation of human populations in South and Central Asia,” <i>Science</i> 365, no. 6457 (2019): eaat7487, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6822619/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Indo-European_languages_distribution.png/1280px-Indo-European_languages_distribution.png.jpg","title":"Where Indo-European languages are spoken today","desc":"The countries and regions in which an Indo-European language is the majority tongue. The band running from Ireland and Iberia across Europe to Iran and northern India is the old extent; the reach into the Americas, Australia and southern Africa dates from European settlement after 1500 CE rather than from the ancient dispersal.","credit":"Map by Industrius and Radosław Botev, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A world map with Europe, Iran, northern India, most of the Americas, Australia, New Zealand and small parts of southern Africa shaded red, and the rest of Asia and Africa left grey."},"why":[{"q":"Why does a lexical dataset mark every borrowed word?","a":"Because borrowing is what most often misleads a family tree, so a word taken sideways has to be separated from one inherited from the ancestor."},{"q":"Why can reconstruction say something about economy?","a":"Because only one or perhaps two cereal terms can be recovered for the basal stage while the core stage acquired a fuller set, which documents a shift from a largely non-agricultural economy to a mixed one."},{"q":"Why is the eastern Yamnaya not a perfect match for the core speech community?","a":"Because evidence of farming is lacking on the Don-Volga steppe, where the reconstructed core vocabulary implies herding and farming together."}]},
{"id":"wh-260","num":260,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Every horse alive descends from a steppe population of the lower Volga-Don, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left the Botai herders of Kazakhstan with no surviving line at all.","questions":["Setting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 2200 BCE, where the modern clade’s favourable mutations first appear, is disputed by those who point to animals milked and ridden centuries earlier.","Sampling from 8000 to 1000 BCE shows that domestic horses reach the southern Caucasus and Anatolia only in the Bronze Age, well after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the steppes."],"answer":"domestication of the horse","answerText":"domestication of the horse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Botai keeping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">DOM2 mutations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2200 – 2100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mobility spreads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>domestication of the horse</b> turned a hunted grassland animal, among the most heavily taken large game of Ice Age Europe, into the one that carried people and goods across Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mapped from 273 ancient genomes, the horses alive today descend from a population of the western Eurasian steppes, above all the lower Volga-Don region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people of Botai in Central Asia bridled, milked and corralled horses about 3500 BCE, but that lineage left no descendants among modern stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The modern line replaced almost every local population as it spread rapidly across Eurasia, moving in step with equestrian material culture including the spoke-wheeled chariots of Sintashta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A further 475 genomes put reproductive control of that lineage at about 2200 BCE, achieved by close-kin mating and shortened generation times after a bottleneck beginning no earlier than about 2700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Riding left marks in the genome too, with strong selection at two genes, GSDMC and ZFPM1, tied to locomotion and temperament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading the 2200 BCE date refutes the familiar picture of great horse herds accompanying the steppe migrations into Europe around 3000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against it, a genetic shift alone is argued not to define domestication at all: horses of several backgrounds were managed, milked and ridden long before, and Yamnaya groups of about 3200 to 2600 BCE already rode animals of the modern line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Selection for endurance and temperament began centuries earlier still, which makes the process protracted and regionally varied rather than a single breakthrough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From outside the steppe it looks like an arrival: in the southern Caucasus and Anatolia, sampled from about 8000 to 1000 BCE, domestic horses appear only in the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["Pablo Librado, Naveed Khan, Antoine Fages, Mariya A. Kusliy, Tomasz Suchan et al., “The origins and spread of domestic horses from the Western Eurasian steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8550961/. [Open access]","Pablo Librado, Gaetan Tressières, Lorelei Chauvey, Antoine Fages, Naveed Khan et al., “Widespread horse-based mobility arose around 2200 BCE in Eurasia,” <i>Nature</i> 631, no. 8022 (2024): 819–25, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11269178/. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann and Volker Heyd, “Horse genetics, archaeology, and the beginning of riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ady7336. [Open access]","Silvia Guimaraes, Benjamin S. Arbuckle, Joris Peters, Sarah E. Adcock, Hijlke Buitenhuis et al., “Ancient DNA shows domestic horses were introduced in the southern Caucasus and Anatolia during the Bronze Age,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 38 (2020): eabb0030, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abb0030. [Open access]","Elaine Turner, “Horse, Reindeer and Bison Hunters at Solutré: An Archaeozoological Analysis of a Magdalenian Bone Assemblage,” <i>Bonner zoologische Beiträge</i> 50 (1999): 143–82, https://archive.org/details/biostor-136046. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/Horse_geographic_and_genetic_affinities.webp","title":"How the modern horse line took over Eurasia","desc":"Ancestry in ancient horse genomes, mapped at three moments. Before 3000 BCE and through the 3rd millennium the sampled sites carry a patchwork of lineages; after about 2000 BCE almost every site across Europe and the steppe carries the same one, the ancestor of the domestic horse of today. Figure 1 from the study of 273 ancient genomes on which the card rests.","credit":"Figure by Pablo Librado and colleagues, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Three stacked maps of Europe and northern Asia, each dotted with pie charts showing the ancestry of horses sampled at that site. In the first two the pies are many colours; in the third, for after 2000 BCE, almost all of them are a single orange."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the Botai evidence not make those horses ancestral?","a":"Because although its people bridled, milked and corralled horses about 3500 BCE, that lineage left no descendants among modern stock."},{"q":"Why is a genetic date argued not to define domestication?","a":"Because horses of several backgrounds were managed, milked and ridden long before, and Yamnaya groups of about 3200 to 2600 BCE already rode animals of the modern line."},{"q":"Why does the process look protracted rather than a breakthrough?","a":"Because selection for endurance and temperament began centuries before reproductive control was achieved around 2200 BCE, and the practice varied by region."}]},
{"id":"wh-261","num":261,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"The spoke-wheeled vehicle built by the Sintashta culture of the steppes is the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it spread across Eurasia with the horses that drew it.","questions":["Scenes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are carved on the slabs above the Shaft Graves at Mycenae, though whether they show war, hunting or funeral games has never been settled.","Because no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> existed in northern or central Europe at the time, a group at Mycenae using one could only have come from the Near East."],"answer":"chariot","answerText":"chariot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sintashta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mycenae stelae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>chariot</b> is a light two-wheeled vehicle drawn by horses, and the spoke-wheeled version built by the Sintashta culture of the steppes is the earliest of the kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The modern domestic horse line spread rapidly across Eurasia in step with equestrian material culture, the Sintashta chariots among it, replacing almost every local horse population as it went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chariots were not the beginning of the relationship, since horses of several backgrounds had been managed, milked and ridden for centuries before any of this.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How far back riding itself goes is contested, and the skeletal changes to hip, femur and lower back often read as a rider’s marks are a weaker guide than they look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those features have been used to argue for mounted riding among the Yamnaya in the 4th and 3rd millennia BCE, a claim the method itself cannot carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the Aegean the earliest images of the vehicle are carved on the stone slabs that stood over the Shaft Graves at Mycenae, the first large-scale relief sculpture of the mainland Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether those scenes show warfare, hunting or races at funeral games has never been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their presence has been used to argue about where the buried came from, since chariots did not exist in northern or central Europe at the time and a chariot-using group could only have come from the Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Keeping such teams took knowledge as well as animals, and from the 4th millennium before the present written sources describe training systems and husbandry in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many of the practices they set down are still in use, which makes the chariot age the beginning of a continuous tradition rather than a closed episode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","history","archaeology","technology","bronze age"],"sources":["Pablo Librado, Naveed Khan, Antoine Fages, Mariya A. Kusliy, Tomasz Suchan et al., “The origins and spread of domestic horses from the Western Eurasian steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8550961/. [Open access]","David Anthony, Martin Trautmann and Volker Heyd, “Horse genetics, archaeology, and the beginning of riding,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 20 (2026): eady7336, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ady7336. [Open access]","Lauren Hosek, Robin J. James and William T. T. Taylor, “Tracing horseback riding and transport in the human skeleton,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10 (2024): eado9774, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ado9774. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 16: The Shaft Graves,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-16-narrative/. [Open access]","Weronika Klecel and Elżbieta Martyniuk, “From the Eurasian Steppes to the Roman Circuses: A Review of Early Development of Horse Breeding and Management,” <i>Animals</i> 11, no. 7 (2021): 1859, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8300240/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Dispersion_of_double-horse_burials_%28complete_skeletons_only%29_2000-1300_BC.jpg/1280px-Dispersion_of_double-horse_burials_%28complete_skeletons_only%29_2000-1300_BC.jpg","title":"Bronze Age double-horse burials","desc":"The 42 sites across Poland, Ukraine, the middle Volga, the Urals, northern Kazakhstan and Greece where a matched pair of horses was buried whole between about 2000 and 1300 BCE. A pair is the signature of a chariot team, so the spread of these graves tracks the spread of the vehicle; the red marker is Husiatyn in western Ukraine.","credit":"Map by Przemysław Makarowicz and colleagues, drawn by J. Niebieszczański, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A relief map reaching from the Baltic to Kazakhstan, with numbered horse symbols clustered in southern Poland and western Ukraine, strung along the middle Volga and the Urals, spread across northern Kazakhstan, and marked at three points in Greece."},"why":[{"q":"Why are the skeletal marks of riding a weak guide?","a":"Because the changes to hip, femur and lower back often read as a rider's are less diagnostic than they look, and cannot carry the claim of mounted riding among the Yamnaya."},{"q":"Why have the Mycenae grave slabs been used to argue about origins?","a":"Because chariots did not exist in northern or central Europe at the time, so a chariot-using group could only have come from the Near East."},{"q":"Why is the chariot age the start of a continuous tradition?","a":"Because written sources from the 4th millennium before the present describe training systems and husbandry in detail, and many of those practices are still in use."}]},
{"id":"wh-262","num":262,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Vases from Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy at its harbours, and colonies on Kythera and Rhodes, place the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the centre of a Bronze Age sea network.","questions":["Crete had no tin of its own, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imported the scarcer half of bronze by sea, along with the copper that Knossos seems to have controlled.","Genomes from Bronze Age Crete are mostly those of local farming stock, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew out of the island's own Neolithic population rather than from an incoming people."],"answer":"Minoan civilisation","answerText":"Minoan civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3100 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1470 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Knossos burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1375 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan civilisation</b> of Bronze Age Crete sat at the centre of a sea network that reached Egypt, the Levant, Cyprus and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vases from all four turn up at the southern harbour of Kommos, whose shipshed and purpose-built transport amphorae show produce moving out as steadily as goods came in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Northwards the influence ran up a chain of islands that specialists call the Western String, the route by which Crete drew silver and lead from the mines of Attica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two places look like actual colonies rather than influenced neighbours, Kastri on Kythera and probably Trianda on Rhodes, while Cretan pottery reaches Miletus, Iasos and Knidos on the coast of Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Knossos seems to have held the copper trade with Cyprus or south-eastern Turkey, since bronzework across the southern Aegean fell into permanent decline once the palace burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether any of this amounted to an empire has never been settled, and Thucydides' report that Minos ruled the sea has been argued over ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade and the hiring of Cretan craftsmen abroad account for everything actually found, without needing to credit the island with a fleet or a warrior caste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tin was not local either, and the ingots recovered at Mochlos on the north coast show Crete importing the scarcer half of bronze from a source still in dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor were the Cretans themselves newcomers, since Early Bronze Age Aegean genomes are homogeneous and descend mainly from the region's Neolithic farmers, with only small-scale gene flow from east of the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Isotopes say the islanders largely stayed put, while mainland ancestry entered gradually between the 17th and the 12th centuries BCE and marriage between close kin was practised at a rate unmatched in the ancient DNA record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","geography","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger, Jeffrey S. Soles, Alessandra R. Giumlia-Mair, Gerhard Brügmann, Ehud Galili et al., “Isotope systematics and chemical composition of tin ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and other Late Bronze Age sites in the eastern Mediterranean Sea: An ultimate key to tin provenance?” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]","Florian Clemente, Martina Unterländer, Olga Dolgova, Carlos Eduardo G. Amorim, Francisco Coroado-Santos et al., “The genomic history of the Aegean palatial civilizations,” <i>Cell</i> 184, no. 10 (2021): 2565–86, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2021.03.039. [Open access]","Michael Richards, Colin Smith, Olaf Nehlich, Vaughan Grimes, Darlene Weston et al., “Finding Mycenaeans in Minoan Crete? Isotope and DNA analysis of human mobility in Bronze Age Crete,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272144, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272144. [Open access]","Eirini Skourtanioti, Harald Ringbauer, Guido Alberto Gnecchi Ruscone, Raffaela Angelina Bianco, Marta Burri et al., “Ancient DNA reveals admixture history and endogamy in the prehistoric Aegean,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 7, no. 2 (2023): 290–303, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-022-01952-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Ship_procession_fresco%2C_part_2%2C_Akrotiri%2C_Greece.jpg/1280px-Ship_procession_fresco%2C_part_2%2C_Akrotiri%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Ships of the Bronze Age Aegean","desc":"Part of a painted frieze from a house at Akrotiri on Thera, a town deep in the Minoan orbit and buried by the eruption of its own volcano. Oared ships with awnings and deck cabins pass between leaping dolphins, and it is shipping of this kind on which Crete's reach across the eastern Mediterranean rested.","credit":"Wall painting from Akrotiri, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A cracked plaster wall painting of long oared ships seen from the side, each with a canopy over the deck and small figures aboard, with leaping dolphins in the pale space between them."},"locator":{"name":"Minoan civilisation","at":[25.1631,35.2981],"kind":"region","area":[[23.35,35.75],[24.5,35.95],[25.3,36.6],[25.6,36.6],[25.75,36.15],[26.55,35.45],[26.45,34.8],[25.5,34.75],[24.3,34.75],[23.35,35.05]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is an empire not needed to explain the finds?","a":"Because trade and the hiring of Cretan craftsmen abroad account for everything actually found, without crediting the island with a fleet or a warrior caste."},{"q":"Why does the fall of Knossos show up in bronzework across the Aegean?","a":"Because Knossos seems to have held the copper trade with Cyprus or south-eastern Turkey, and bronzework across the southern Aegean fell into permanent decline once the palace burned."},{"q":"Why does the Western String matter?","a":"Because that chain of islands is the route by which Crete drew silver and lead from the mines of Attica."}]},
{"id":"wh-263","num":263,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Oxhide ingots, Baltic amber and a shipwreck full of a merchant's tackle place the palaces of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the western end of a trading world reaching to Egypt.","questions":["The burning of the palaces around 1200 BCE ended <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Boeotia, the Argolid, Messenia and Laconia lost most of their people in the century that followed.","Mainland ancestry appears on Crete between the 17th and the 12th centuries BCE, marking the centuries in which the weight of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over the island steadily grew."],"answer":"Mycenaean Greece","answerText":"Mycenaean Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1050 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Palaces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mycenaean Greece</b> is the Late Bronze Age culture of the Greek mainland, and its palaces stood at the western end of a trading world reaching from the Baltic to Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper moved along it as four-handled ingots of about 20 kilograms (45 pounds), the so-called oxhide type, found from Syria and Cyprus to Crete, Sicily and Sardinia and painted in Egyptian tombs from the reign of Thutmose III onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ship wrecked off Cape Gelidonya carried a ton of such metal with scrap bronze, weights, scarabs and a cylinder seal, the working kit of a merchant captain rather than a royal cargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all the amber in Mycenaean graves is Baltic, and one proposal has it arriving along a chain of royal gift-exchanges across Europe of which these kings were the southernmost link.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crete came within that reach as well, mainland ancestry appearing on the island gradually between the 17th and the 12th centuries BCE as the influence of the mainland over it intensified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the Cretan cemetery of Armenoi, one of the few places off the palaces to yield the newly adopted Linear B script, four of the buried grew up somewhere other than Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Around 1200 BCE the mainland palaces burned, and Boeotia, western Attica, the Corinthia, the Argolid, Messenia and Laconia lost most of their people over the century that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Egyptian sources name raiders who had wrecked the Levant, Cyprus and Anatolia, yet there is no sound evidence that they reached the Aegean at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every single-cause explanation offered has oversimplified, whether a change in the nature of warfare, the severing of trade routes or drought, for a collapse spread across a century and half a continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cores from Cyprus do record an abrupt dry episode and famine running through those decades, so climate belongs in the account even though it cannot carry the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","warfare","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Eirini Skourtanioti, Harald Ringbauer, Guido Alberto Gnecchi Ruscone, Raffaela Angelina Bianco, Marta Burri et al., “Ancient DNA reveals admixture history and endogamy in the prehistoric Aegean,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 7, no. 2 (2023): 290–303, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-022-01952-3. [Open access]","Michael Richards, Colin Smith, Olaf Nehlich, Vaughan Grimes, Darlene Weston et al., “Finding Mycenaeans in Minoan Crete? Isotope and DNA analysis of human mobility in Bronze Age Crete,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272144, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272144. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 28: The Collapse of Mycenaean Palatial Civilization and the Coming of the Dorians,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-28-narrative/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Mycenaean_%27stirrup_jar%27%2C_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_7137_p01.jpg/1280px-Mycenaean_%27stirrup_jar%27%2C_clay_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_S_7137_p01.jpg","title":"A Mycenaean jar in the Egyptian Museum","desc":"A small clay stirrup jar, the closed shape Mycenaean potters made for oil and poured through the narrow spout at the shoulder. The Museo Egizio in Turin dates it to the Egyptian New Kingdom, between about 1539 and 1292 BCE, which puts it at the far end of the trade described here.","credit":"Photograph by the Museo Egizio, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A squat wheel-made pot of buff clay banded in reddish brown, with a short narrow spout rising from the shoulder and the top broken away, standing on a clear museum mount."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Gelidonya wreck read as a merchant's cargo?","a":"Because it carried a ton of copper with scrap bronze, weights, scarabs and a cylinder seal — the working kit of a merchant captain rather than a royal shipment."},{"q":"Why can the Sea Peoples not explain the Aegean collapse?","a":"Because although Egyptian sources name raiders who wrecked the Levant, Cyprus and Anatolia, there is no sound evidence that they reached the Aegean at all."},{"q":"Why has every single-cause explanation of the collapse oversimplified?","a":"Because it was spread across a century and half a continent, so neither a change in warfare, nor severed trade routes, nor drought alone can carry it."}]},
{"id":"wh-264","num":264,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Dated exactly, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would tie the calendars of the Aegean, Egypt and the Near East to the ice cores and the lake beds, which is why it is fought over.","questions":["Radiocarbon puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the late 17th or early 16th century BCE and the pottery sequences the better part of a century later, a gap nobody has closed.","A carbonised olive shrub on Therasia and a tree-ring series spanning 1700 to 1500 BCE both shift the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> towards the 16th century, narrowing an old argument without ending it."],"answer":"Minoan eruption","answerText":"Minoan eruption","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiocarbon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1627 – 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archaeology</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1550 – 1480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Therasia shrub</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1560 – 1540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Minoan eruption</b> blew out the middle of the Aegean island of Thera and was one of the largest volcanic events of the past 4,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ash fell across the eastern Mediterranean and left a single geological marker that, dated precisely, could anchor the ice cores, the cave deposits and the lake sediments alongside the archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It would then tie the separate calendars of the Aegean, Egypt and the Near East to one another and to that environmental record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This is why the date has been fought over for decades, and why the fight matters a long way beyond one island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two bodies of evidence disagree: radiocarbon has pointed to the late 17th or early 16th century BCE, while pottery sequences and Egyptian links point to the mid-16th or early 15th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Part of the gap turns out to lie in the measuring, since the international calibration curve treats a whole hemisphere as one and Mediterranean and Anatolian wood is offset from it by up to 22 radiocarbon years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Growing seasons of different length and timing are one cause and the move from older counting methods to accelerator mass spectrometry another, and correcting for both also settles a long quarrel over Mesopotamian chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An annual-resolution radiocarbon series built from calendar-dated tree rings between 1700 and 1500 BCE shifts the answer for Thera towards the 16th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carbonised olive shrub buried by the eruption on neighbouring Therasia dates slightly younger than an earlier branch, raising the odds on the middle of that century and overlapping sulfate markers already found in the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon on Egyptian objects of the 17th and early 18th Dynasties puts the eruption before the reign of Ahmose, and a chemical shift in dated tree rings around 1560 BCE may be the event itself, on a reading its authors say needs further substantiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","geology","science","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["Charlotte L. Pearson, Peter W. Brewer, David Brown, Timothy J. Heaton, Gregory W. L. Hodgins et al., “Annual radiocarbon record indicates 16th century BCE date for the Thera eruption,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 8 (2018): eaar8241, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aar8241. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Lukas Wacker, Ulf Büntgen, Christopher Bronk Ramsey, Michael W. Dee et al., “Radiocarbon offsets and old world chronology as relevant to Mesopotamia, Egypt, Anatolia and Thera (Santorini),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 10 (2020): 13785, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-69287-2. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Kostas Sbonias, Iris Tzachili and Timothy J. Heaton, “Olive shrub buried on Therasia supports a mid-16th century BCE date for the Thera eruption,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 6994, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-33696-w. [Open access]","Hendrik J. Bruins and Johannes van der Plicht, “The Minoan Thera eruption predates Pharaoh Ahmose: Radiocarbon dating of Egyptian 17th to early 18th Dynasty museum objects,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0330702, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0330702. [Open access]","Charlotte Pearson, Matthew Salzer, Lukas Wacker, Peter Brewer, Adam Sookdeo and Peter Kuniholm, “Securing timelines in the ancient Mediterranean using multiproxy annual tree-ring data,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 15 (2020): 8410–15, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1917445117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Santorini%E2%80%99s_Hidden_Worlds_%28154523%29.jpg","title":"What the eruption left of Thera","desc":"Thera from Landsat 9. The crescent is what remains of a round island: Therasia lies across the water to the west, Aspronisi to the south, and the dark lava islands at the centre have grown inside the flooded caldera since. The olive shrub that gives the eruption one of its dates was buried on Therasia.","credit":"NASA Earth Observatory image by Wanmei Liang, using Landsat data from the U.S. Geological Survey, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A satellite view of pale islands around a dark blue bay: a long crescent on the east and south, a smaller island on the west, a sliver to the south-west, and two small dark islands at the centre of the water."},"why":[{"q":"Why does one island's eruption date matter so widely?","a":"Because its ash left a single geological marker that, dated precisely, would anchor ice cores, cave deposits and lake sediments and tie the calendars of the Aegean, Egypt and the Near East together."},{"q":"Why do radiocarbon and pottery sequences disagree?","a":"Because radiocarbon has pointed to the late 17th or early 16th century BCE while pottery sequences and Egyptian links point to the mid-16th or early 15th."},{"q":"Why did part of the gap lie in the measuring?","a":"Because the international calibration curve treats a whole hemisphere as one, and Mediterranean and Anatolian wood is offset from it by up to 22 radiocarbon years."}]},
{"id":"wh-265","num":265,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Built from the signs of an older Cretan script for a language it fits badly, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded palace accounts in Greek and vanished when those palaces burned.","questions":["A syllabary with no way at all to write a closed syllable, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was pressed into service for Greek, a language thick with consonant clusters and final consonants.","Greece could write in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 1200 BCE and then could not write at all for four centuries, until an alphabet of Semitic origin reached it."],"answer":"Linear B","answerText":"Linear B","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Writing returns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Linear B</b> is the writing of the Mycenaean palaces, a mainly syllabic script of some 89 signs standing for bare vowels and for open consonant-and-vowel syllables, with more than a hundred ideograms beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not built for Greek: 45 of its signs have close equivalents in Linear A, the older and still unread writing of Crete, from which it was derived in order to write a different language altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where, when, why, by whom and in what circumstances that adaptation was made are five questions with proposals attached and no answers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On one reading the two are a single repertoire of signs governed by two different sets of language-specific rules, and the first hands to write Linear B were scribes trained in the older one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fit is poor all the same, since a syllabary with no sign for a closed syllable was set to work on a language thick with consonant clusters and final consonants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It survives in only two forms, clay tablets legible because the fires that destroyed the palaces baked them, and one to three words painted on the shoulder of a coarse stirrup jar before firing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tablets come from palaces alone, while some 140 of the painted jars are known, clustered in Boeotia, the Argolid and Crete and largely made in western Crete, which is how Greek is known to have been spoken there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing here was an instrument of palace administration and had no life outside it, though these tablets are also the earliest Greek anyone can read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So when the palaces burned around 1200 BCE, writing disappeared with them, along with the economies they ran and the great building programmes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek was written again only some four centuries later, in an alphabet of Semitic origin whose date and circumstances of adoption are both still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","writing","language","bronze age"],"sources":["Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 25: The Linear B Tablets and Mycenaean Social, Political, and Economic Organization,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-25-narrative/. [Open access]","Theodore Nash, review of <i>Aegean Linear Script(s): Rethinking the Relationship between Linear A and Linear B</i>, by Ester Salgarella, Cambridge Classical Studies, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2021.04.30, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2021/2021.04.30/. [Open access]","“Palace of Nestor: Description,” Odysseus, Ministry of Culture and Sports, Hellenic Republic, http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh352.jsp?obj_id=2562. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Clay_tablets_inscribed_with_Linear_B.jpg/1280px-Clay_tablets_inscribed_with_Linear_B.jpg","title":"Linear B tablets from Pylos","desc":"A case in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens holding tablets from the palace of Pylos, which the museum dates to the 13th century BCE. Both of the shapes the script was written on are here: the long thin palm-leaf tablets carrying a line or two, and the rectangular page tablets that hold more. None of it was meant to last, and it survives only because the fire that destroyed the palace baked the clay.","credit":"Photograph by HerrGutmannsWiki, released as CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A tall lit museum case holding about thirty small clay tablets mounted on a pale board, some long and narrow, some rectangular, each carrying faint incised lines of writing, above a lower shelf of seal stones and small fragments."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the script fit Greek badly?","a":"Because it was derived from Linear A to write a different language, and a syllabary with no sign for a closed syllable was set to work on a language thick with consonant clusters and final consonants."},{"q":"Why does it survive in only two forms?","a":"Because the clay tablets are legible only because the fires that destroyed the palaces baked them, and the other form is a few words painted on a stirrup jar before firing."},{"q":"Why did writing disappear when the palaces burned?","a":"Because it was an instrument of palace administration with no life outside it, so Greek was written again only some four centuries later, in an alphabet of Semitic origin."}]},
{"id":"wh-266","num":266,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Eight languages are attested in the archives of their capital, and their own was the earliest Indo-European ever written down, which makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a state that dealt with everybody.","questions":["Named after a people of the Hebrew Bible who were not them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran a Bronze Age state in central Anatolia and were forgotten so completely that later Greek writers never mention them.","Ancient genomes barely find in Anatolia the steppe ancestry that marks every other Indo-European region, which makes the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the hardest of the family to place."],"answer":"Hittites","answerText":"Hittites","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hattusa, from c. 1650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Hittites</b> were the people of a Bronze Age state in central Anatolia, one of the powers whose entanglement with Egypt, the Aegean and the Levant made up the Late Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is not their own: it was fixed on them from the Hatti-land of Assyrian records and the Hittites of the Hebrew Bible, who were in fact the Iron Age states of northern Syria and southeastern Turkey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Land and capital alike came from the Hattians who were there before them, the site already appearing as Hattush in Old Assyrian merchants' records when the Hittites took it over around 1650 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their own language belongs to the Anatolian branch of Indo-European and is the earliest of that family committed to writing, set down in cuneiform borrowed from Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eight separate languages have been identified in the archives of their capital, which is what the paperwork of a state dealing with everybody looks like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Where that language came from is a live question, because ancient genomes show Anatolia reshaped by movement within West Asia and barely touched by the steppe migrations that carried Indo-European everywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The answer now proposed is a population of the Caucasus and the lower Volga which supplied about four-fifths of the ancestry of the Yamnaya and at least a tenth of that of the Bronze Age central Anatolians who spoke Hittite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On that reading the parent tongue of both branches was last spoken as one between about 4400 and 4000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the horses that drew their chariots arrived rather than being bred at home, appearing suddenly and in quantity at the end of the 3rd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state itself was gone by about 1200 BCE, its capital emptied and burnt and then left alone for centuries and its memory so thoroughly lost that Herodotus and Pausanias, both of them from Asia Minor, say nothing of it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["people","state","history","archaeology","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “The Hittites and the Aegean World,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 2 (1974): 2–10, https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-hittites-and-the-aegean-world/. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Songül Alpaslan-Roodenberg, Ayşe Acar, Ayşen Açıkkol, Anagnostis Agelarakis et al., “The genetic history of the Southern Arc: A bridge between West Asia and Europe,” <i>Science</i> 377, no. 6609 (2022): eabm4247, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abm4247. [Open access]","Iosif Lazaridis, Nick Patterson, David Anthony, Leonid Vyazov, Romain Fournier et al., “The genetic origin of the Indo-Europeans,” <i>Nature</i> 639, no. 8053 (2025): 132–42, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08531-5. [Open access]","Silvia Guimaraes, Benjamin S. Arbuckle, Joris Peters, Sarah E. Adcock, Hijlke Buitenhuis et al., “Ancient DNA shows domestic horses were introduced in the southern Caucasus and Anatolia during the Bronze Age,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 38 (2020): eabb0030, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abb0030. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Kadesh_Treaty%2C_written_in_Akkadian._1269_BCE._From_Bo%C4%9Fazk%C3%B6y%2C_Turkey._Clay_tablet._Museum_of_the_Ancient_Orient%2C_Istanbul%2C_Turkey.jpg/1280px-Kadesh_Treaty%2C_written_in_Akkadian._1269_BCE._From_Bo%C4%9Fazk%C3%B6y%2C_Turkey._Clay_tablet._Museum_of_the_Ancient_Orient%2C_Istanbul%2C_Turkey.jpg","title":"The treaty with Egypt","desc":"Two fragments of the peace treaty between Hattusili and Ramesses II, found at Boğazköy, the Hittite capital, and now in the Museum of the Ancient Orient in Istanbul, which dates them to 1269 BCE. It is written in neither Hittite nor Egyptian but in Akkadian, and in the Mesopotamian script the Hittites had borrowed.","credit":"Photograph by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Two fragments of a thick clay tablet mounted on a clear perspex stand, the larger covered edge to edge in fine wedge-shaped cuneiform and cracked into pieces that have been fitted back together."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the name Hittites not their own?","a":"Because it was fixed on them from the Hatti-land of Assyrian records and the Hittites of the Hebrew Bible, who were in fact the Iron Age states of northern Syria and southeastern Turkey."},{"q":"Why is the origin of their language a live question?","a":"Because ancient genomes show Anatolia reshaped by movement within West Asia and barely touched by the steppe migrations that carried Indo-European everywhere else."},{"q":"Why were eight languages found in their archives?","a":"Because that is what the paperwork of a state dealing with everybody looks like, its own tongue set down in cuneiform borrowed from Mesopotamia."}]},
{"id":"wh-267","num":267,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"The Lions' Gate, a rock sanctuary of carved gods and an archive on its citadel all belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Hittite capital high on the Anatolian plateau.","questions":["Long read as a city sacked at the end of the Bronze Age, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now looks to have been emptied by its own administration first and burnt only afterwards.","Juniper rings record a severe run of dry years around 1198 BCE, just as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the Hittite state that ruled from it were given up for good."],"answer":"Hattusa","answerText":"Hattusa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1650 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yazılıkaya</span><span class=\"dt-v\">cult site from c. 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Drought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1198 – 1196 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hattusa</b> in central Anatolia was the capital of the Hittite kingdom and empire, the political and religious centre of one of the major powers of the ancient Near East between about 1650 and 1200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From it the Hittites held central, southern and southeastern Anatolia together with the northern Levant and northern Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site is on the World Heritage list for its urban organisation and for what survives of its temples, royal residences and fortifications, above all the sculpture of the Lions', the Sphinxes' and the Royal Gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its defences had to be carried across a gorge, and that stretch survives as cuttings and traces worked into the rock rather than as standing wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The citadel of Büyükkale held the archives, and the first season of digging there brought out some 2,500 tablets and fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> About 3 kilometres (2 miles) to the north lies Yazılıkaya, a labyrinth of limestone slabs that had been a place of worship since the 16th century BCE and was made over by Hattusili III and his son Tudhaliya IV into a setting for the kingdom's pantheon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two processions of gods are carved along its main chamber, one female and one male, meeting on the back wall in the goddess Hebat and the storm god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Feeding the place took a vast storage silo in the early Hittite period and later a scatter of smaller ones, alongside heavy clearance of the landscape and a farming strategy that leaned on water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Juniper rings from central Anatolia record an unusually severe run of dry years from about 1198 to 1196 BCE, at the point the Hittite state gave way along with the rest of the Late Bronze Age world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city was long read as the victim of a sack, but excavation now indicates that it was emptied by the royal administration first and burnt only afterwards, and why it was given up is still not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["Sturt W. Manning, Cindy Kocik, Brita Lorentzen and Jed P. Sparks, “Severe multi-year drought coincident with Hittite collapse around 1198–1196 BC,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05693-y. [Open access]","L. Repola, M. Marazzi and S. Tilia, “Constructing and Representing: A New Project for 3D Surveying of Yazılıkaya – Hattuša,” <i>International Archives of the Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing and Spatial Information Sciences</i> XLII-5/W1 (2017): 395–402, https://doi.org/10.5194/isprs-archives-XLII-5-W1-395-2017. [Open access]","Leopoldo Repola, “Extraction of Constructive Geometries from Numerical Models: The City Walls in the Hattusa Gorge,” <i>Disegnare con</i> 16, no. 30 (2023): 10.1–10.13, https://disegnarecon.univaq.it/ojs/index.php/disegnarecon/article/view/1103. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, “The Hittites and the Aegean World,” <i>Expedition</i> 16, no. 2 (1974): 2–10, https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-hittites-and-the-aegean-world/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Lion_Gate%2C_Hattusa_01.jpg/1280px-Lion_Gate%2C_Hattusa_01.jpg","title":"The Lion Gate","desc":"One of the three carved gateways the site is best known for, with the Sphinxes' and the Royal Gates. The lions face outward from the jamb stones, and past the gap the wall runs on over bare upland; the same circuit had to be carried across a gorge, where it survives only as cuttings worked into the rock.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A gap in a wall of huge rough-cut blocks, flanked by two lions carved in relief out of the massive jamb stones, mouths open and facing outward; beyond the gate the wall runs on across dry open upland under a blue sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why does part of the city's defences survive only as rock cuttings?","a":"Because they had to be carried across a gorge, so that stretch was worked into the rock rather than built as standing wall."},{"q":"Why has the reading of the city's end changed?","a":"Because it was long taken for the victim of a sack, and excavation now indicates it was emptied by the royal administration first and burnt only afterwards."},{"q":"Why do juniper rings bear on the state's collapse?","a":"Because they record an unusually severe run of dry years from about 1198 to 1196 BCE, at the point the Hittite state gave way with the rest of the Late Bronze Age world."}]},
{"id":"wh-268","num":268,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"A port whose wealth came from opening the Mediterranean onto the Syrian interior, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> traded with the Aegean, Cyprus, Egypt and western Asia until it was destroyed.","questions":["Tablets recording frantic preparations against the Sea Peoples, and a burnt layer with arrowheads scattered through it, date the fall of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to about 1190 BCE.","The alphabetic cuneiform of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to the Northwest Semitic tradition from which the Greek alphabet later came, and its texts already mark where words end."],"answer":"Ugarit","answerText":"Ugarit","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zenith</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1192 – 1190 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ramesses III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1188 BCE or 1177 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ugarit</b> was a Bronze Age port kingdom on the north Syrian coast, and its wealth came from a position that opened the Mediterranean directly onto the Syrian heartland, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its harbour towns sat inside a network of long-distance trade and exchange running between the Aegean, Cyprus, the Levant, Egypt and western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper reached that coast as the four-handled oxhide ingots found from Cyprus to Sardinia, and Ras Shamra, the modern name of the site, is among the places they turn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kingdom belonged to the linkage of Aegean, Egyptian, Syro-Palestinian and Hittite civilisations that made up the Late Bronze Age world of the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Writing there was alphabetic cuneiform, a set of wedge-shaped signs used as letters rather than as syllables, and one of the Northwest Semitic systems from whose tradition the Greek alphabet later came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Ugaritic texts already mark where one word ends and the next begins, most of them along the lines of the spoken phrase and a minority, found chiefly in everyday documents, along the grammar instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tablets give a glimpse of the frantic preparations the city and its neighbours made, in vain, against the invasions of the Sea Peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Gibala on the kingdom's southern edge a destruction layer holds bronze arrowheads scattered through the town, walls thrown down, houses burnt and the ash of the fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon on that layer, anchored to Hittite, Levantine and Egyptian kings and to astronomical observation, dates the terminal destructions of the northern Levant to 1192 to 1190 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ramesses III afterwards claimed that no land had been able to stand before them, and listed Hatti, Carchemish, Arzawa and Alashiya as cut off at one time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","bronze age"],"sources":["David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Karel Van Lerberghe, Tom Boiy, Klaas Vansteenhuyse et al., “The Sea Peoples, from Cuneiform Tablets to Carbon Dating,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e20232, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0020232. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski, Elise Van Campo, Joël Guiot, Sabine Le Burel, Thierry Otto and Cecile Baeteman, “Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Contexts of and Relations between Early Writing Systems, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Cindy Kocik, Brita Lorentzen and Jed P. Sparks, “Severe multi-year drought coincident with Hittite collapse around 1198–1196 BC,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05693-y. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Syrien%2C_historische_Stadt_Ugarit.1.JPG/1280px-Syrien%2C_historische_Stadt_Ugarit.1.JPG","title":"Ras Shamra, the site of Ugarit","desc":"The excavated city on the Syrian coast, its wall footings standing a course or two high among the spring flowers. The cuneiform tablets that made the place famous came out of this ground; the destruction that ended the kingdom is dated at its southern harbour town to about 1190 BCE.","credit":"Photograph by Feldstein, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"Low stone wall footings of many small rooms laid out across a green field of grass and wildflowers, standing only a course or two high, with trees and a modern town along the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the city so rich?","a":"Because its position opened the Mediterranean directly onto the Syrian heartland, Anatolia and Mesopotamia, putting its harbours inside a network running from the Aegean to Egypt."},{"q":"Why is its writing significant beyond the kingdom?","a":"Because it is alphabetic cuneiform, wedge-shaped signs used as letters rather than syllables, one of the Northwest Semitic systems from whose tradition the Greek alphabet later came."},{"q":"Why can the region's destruction be dated so tightly?","a":"Because radiocarbon on the Gibala destruction layer, anchored to Hittite, Levantine and Egyptian kings and to astronomical observation, dates the terminal destructions to 1192 to 1190 BCE."}]},
{"id":"wh-269","num":269,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Cut on rock faces at an Egyptian mining site in the Sinai, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest alphabetic writing anyone has found, and it is only partly readable.","questions":["Borrowing the shapes of hieroglyphs without the system behind them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was devised among Semitic speakers working inside Egypt as miners, builders and mercenaries.","Latin, Cyrillic, Arabic and Devanagari all descend from the tradition the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened, which needs signs in tens where older systems needed them in hundreds."],"answer":"Proto-Sinaitic script","answerText":"Proto-Sinaitic script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wadi el-Hol</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1850 – 1700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or as late as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Proto-Sinaitic script</b> is the earliest known alphabetic writing, linear pictographic signs cut on statuettes, stone panels and rock faces at Serabit el-Khadem, an Egyptian mining site in the Sinai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A closely similar script turned up in the 1990s on two single-line rock inscriptions at Wadi el-Hol, in the desert near Thebes in Upper Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither group has a stratified archaeological context, so the dating is argued over, some placing them at the opening of the 2nd millennium BCE and others as late as the 14th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The signs plainly take their shapes from hieroglyphs and are just as plainly not used as Egyptian used them, so the alphabet was invented among Semitic speakers inside the Egyptian realm, borrowing the pictures without the system behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Asiatics worked in Egypt through the Middle Kingdom as builders, miners and mercenaries, which is the setting such an invention needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Each sign is held to stand for the first sound of the Semitic word its picture names, and it is on that assumption that the Sinai texts have been partially read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they give up are phrases like 'for the Lady' and 'chief of the miners'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assumption is disputed, on the ground that most letters of the consonantal script carry no association with any particular object at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is not disputed is the economy of the thing: an alphabet needs signs in tens where a logographic or syllabic system needs them in hundreds, and this earliest West Semitic version wrote consonants alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Latin, Cyrillic, Arabic, Devanagari and Bengali all descend from that tradition, and Greek with them, though a script easier to learn does not by itself make a population literate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","technology","archaeology","writing","language","bronze age"],"sources":["Joseph Lam, “The Invention and Development of the Alphabet,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 189–92, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “Visible Language: The Earliest Writing Systems,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 21, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","L. R. Siddall, review of <i>Writing: Theory and History of the Technology of Civilization</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.04.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.04.24/. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Contexts of and Relations between Early Writing Systems, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Oxford Studies in Ancient Documents, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Proto-Sinaitic_inscription_number_345_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Sinai 345, drawn in 1916","desc":"Alan Gardiner's drawing of one of the inscriptions from Serabit el-Khadem. Its signs are recorded from four places on a small carved animal, the shoulder, the two sides and the space between the paws, and it was on drawings of this kind that the script was first partly read.","credit":"Drawing by Alan H. Gardiner, published 1916, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A line drawing of four short rows of simple pictographic signs copied from an inscribed object, among them a hand, an eye, a fish and a wavy line, each row labelled for where on the object it was cut, with the number 345 beneath."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the script's date argued over?","a":"Because neither the Sinai group nor the Wadi el-Hol inscriptions has a stratified archaeological context, so proposals range from the opening of the 2nd millennium BCE to the 14th century."},{"q":"Why is it thought to have been invented by Semitic speakers inside Egypt?","a":"Because the signs plainly take their shapes from hieroglyphs and are just as plainly not used as Egyptian used them, and Asiatics worked in Egypt through the Middle Kingdom as builders, miners and mercenaries."},{"q":"Why is the acrophonic assumption disputed?","a":"Because most letters of the consonantal script carry no association with any particular object at all, even though the Sinai texts have been read on the assumption that each sign stands for its picture's first sound."}]},
{"id":"wh-270","num":270,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Kings addressing each other as brothers and mayors begging for troops share one box of 382 tablets, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the city Akhenaten built as his capital.","questions":["Written mostly in Akkadian whatever the sender spoke at home, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the diplomatic correspondence of an Egyptian court in the 14th century BCE.","Counted as a network, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split in two, the great kings organising around trade and the Syrian mayors sitting in a web structured by war."],"answer":"Amarna letters","answerText":"Amarna letters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1365 – 1330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1352 – 1338 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Amarna letters</b> are the diplomatic correspondence of the Egyptian court in the 14th century BCE, 382 clay tablets from the city Akhenaten built as his capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A woman of Amarna came upon nearly 400 of them in 1887, and Petrie afterwards located the building they had come from and found more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most are addressed to Amenhotep III and then to his successor Akhenaten, and the whole archive covers a window of only fifteen to thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are written mostly in Akkadian, a dialect of Babylonian and the language diplomacy used across the whole region, whatever the sender spoke at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some forty are to or from the rulers Egypt treated as great kings, those of Babylonia, Mitanni and Hatti, whose own empire ran from central Anatolia into the northern Levant, with Assyria joining once Mitanni fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Between those kings the language is brotherhood, and the business is precious goods and the marriages of royal women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest of the archive is different in kind, the mayors of the Syrian and Palestinian towns writing about raids and rivals and asking for troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counted as a network the split is sharp: the great kings organise their exchanges around trade, while the mayors sit in a web structured by war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything at the site is closely dated, the city having been lived in for little more than a decade, so the quantities of Mycenaean pottery there against almost no Minoan are read as a sign of who by then dominated Aegean trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is one palace's in-tray rather than a whole world, and it leans heavily on Rib-Hadda of Byblos, whose letters take up so much of the dossier that his persistence bends the picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","history","archaeology","writing","bronze age"],"sources":["Clément Dutrey, “Distribution de l’information et stratégies relationnelles dans le corpus de correspondances amarniennes : approche par l’analyse de réseaux,” <i>Journal of Historical Network Research</i> 6, no. 1 (2021): 1–40, https://doi.org/10.25517/jhnr.v6i1.85. [Open access]","“Amarna: The Cuneiform Tablets,” Digital Egypt for Universities, University College London, 2002, https://www.ucl.ac.uk/museums-static/digitalegypt/amarna/cuneiform.html. [Open access]","Alex Aissaoui, “The Amarna Diplomacy in IR Perspective: A System of States in the Making,” <i>Estudos Internacionais</i> 6, no. 2 (2018): 9–29, https://periodicos.pucminas.br/index.php/estudosinternacionais/article/view/14069. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Cindy Kocik, Brita Lorentzen and Jed P. Sparks, “Severe multi-year drought coincident with Hittite collapse around 1198–1196 BC,” <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05693-y. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, “Lesson 18: The Nature and Extent of Neopalatial Minoan Influence in the Aegean and Eastern Mediterranean Worlds,” <i>Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology</i> (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-18-narrative/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/EA_256_amarna_letter.jpg","title":"One letter, EA 256","desc":"Knudtzon's 1915 copy of a single tablet from the archive, its cuneiform set out obverse, reverse and edges with the lines numbered down the sides and the names of sender and recipient at the head. His edition is still how the corpus is referred to, every letter in it carrying an EA number.","credit":"Hand copy by Jørgen Alexander Knudtzon, Die El-Amarna-Tafeln, 1915, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","alt":"A printed line drawing of a clay tablet's inscription, rows of wedge-shaped cuneiform signs arranged in ruled blocks labelled obverse and reverse, with line numbers down both sides and the heading EA 256 above."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the letters divide into two kinds of correspondence?","a":"Because the great kings of Babylonia, Mitanni and Hatti write in the language of brotherhood about precious goods and royal marriages, while the Syrian and Palestinian mayors write about raids, rivals and troops."},{"q":"Why does the archive bend the picture it gives?","a":"Because it is one palace's in-tray rather than a whole world, and it leans heavily on Rib-Hadda of Byblos, whose letters take up so much of the dossier."},{"q":"Why is the pottery at the site so useful for dating trade?","a":"Because the city was lived in for little more than a decade, so its quantities of Mycenaean against almost no Minoan ware are a tightly dated sample."}]},
{"id":"wh-271","num":271,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Goods from at least seven cultures lay in the single hull of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Baltic amber and African ebony to Cypriot copper and a Kassite cylinder seal.","questions":["Unlike a palace store filled over decades, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds one voyage's goods together, everything that was aboard on the day the ship went down.","More than a hundred cobalt blue glass ingots came up from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, raw Egyptian glass of the sort Mycenaean beadmakers imported and worked into jewellery."],"answer":"Uluburun shipwreck","answerText":"Uluburun shipwreck","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1320 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Uluburun shipwreck</b> is a merchant ship that went down off south-western Turkey about 1320 BCE, the densest single record of Late Bronze Age exchange yet found, with goods from at least seven cultures in one hull.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its bulk cargo was raw metal, roughly 360 copper ingots and 160 tin ingots weighing some 12 tons, the two in about the ten-to-one ratio that makes bronze, though where the tin was mined is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of the copper travelled as oxhide ingots, four-handled slabs of about 23 kilograms (51 pounds) cast from Cypriot ore, a shape found from Syria and Crete as far west as Sicily and Sardinia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around the metal lay materials from far beyond the sea the ship was crossing: African ebony logs, elephant tusks, hippopotamus teeth, ostrich eggshells, Baltic amber beads and a stone mace of northern Balkan type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There were also more than a hundred cobalt blue glass ingots, raw glass made in Egypt of the kind Mycenaean beadmakers imported, which the Amarna letters call mekku-stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What makes a wreck worth more than a storeroom is that it is a moment rather than an accumulation, a miniature of the whole eastern Mediterranean trade caught on one day of one voyage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A palace store holds goods that came in over decades and a tomb what mourners chose to bury, so neither shows what one ship was carrying at once. Read that way the cargo argues for a circuit rather than a line: the Levantine coast to Cyprus, on to the Aegean, sometimes west to Sardinia, then home past North Africa and Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also separates the traffic, raw materials moving in bulk while small finished luxuries of ivory, gold and carved stone travelled among them as the gifts kings sent one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whose ship it was is still argued: the crew's weapons, galley wares and lutes look Syro-Canaanite while other belongings point to passengers from the Greek mainland, and such vessels may have had no nationality at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","object","archaeology","history","bronze age","turkey","cyprus"],"sources":["\"Uluburun Late Bronze Age Shipwreck Excavation,\" Institute of Nautical Archaeology, https://nauticalarch.org/projects/uluburun-late-bronze-age-shipwreck-excavation/. [Open access]","N. Fawcett and J. C. Zietsman, \"Uluburun – The Discovery and Excavation of the World's Oldest Known Shipwreck,\" <i>Akroterion</i> 46 (2001): 5–20, https://doi.org/10.7445/46-0-116. [Open access]","James D. Muhly, \"Archaeometry and Shipwrecks: A Review Article,\" <i>Expedition</i> 53, no. 1 (2011), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/archaeometry-and-shipwrecks/. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 22: Aspects of Mycenaean Trade,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-22-narrative/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Why Central Asia's Mushiston Is Not a Source for the Late Bronze Age Tin Ingots from the Uluburun Shipwreck,\" <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1211478, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2023.1211478. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Bodrum_Museum_Uluburun_shipwreck_reconstruction_in_2015_3707.jpg","title":"The Uluburun ship reconstructed, Bodrum Museum of Underwater Archaeology","desc":"A full-size reconstruction showing how the cargo was stowed in the hull, with the copper oxhide ingots stacked low along the keel and the jars and smaller goods packed around them.","credit":"Photo by Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodrum_Museum_Uluburun_shipwreck_reconstruction_in_2015_3707.jpg","alt":"A reconstructed wooden ship's hull seen from above in a museum, its floor filled with rows of dark four-handled metal ingots and rounded storage jars."},"why":[{"q":"Why is a wreck worth more than a storeroom?","a":"Because it is a moment rather than an accumulation: a palace store holds goods that came in over decades and a tomb what mourners chose to bury, where a hull shows what one ship carried at once."},{"q":"Why does the cargo argue for a circuit rather than a line?","a":"Because its goods come from at least seven cultures, which fits a route from the Levantine coast to Cyprus, on to the Aegean, sometimes west, then home past North Africa and Egypt."},{"q":"Why is the ship's nationality still argued?","a":"Because the crew's weapons, galley wares and lutes look Syro-Canaanite while other belongings point to passengers from the Greek mainland, and such vessels may have had no nationality at all."}]},
{"id":"wh-272","num":272,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Copper lies all round the Near East, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that hardens it into bronze lies in no verified deposit there, so every bronze workshop had to import it.","questions":["Donkey caravans from Assur crossed more than 1,000 kilometres of Anatolia carrying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and textiles, and the merchants' letters at Kanesh count it out by the talent.","Panned from stream beds like alluvial gold, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaves no trace once a deposit is worked out, which is why its ancient sources are still being argued over."],"answer":"tin","answerText":"tin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named in texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the mid-3rd millennium BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anatolian wars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1895 – 1835 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tin</b> is the metal that turns copper into bronze, and hardly any of it occurs anywhere near the societies that used the most of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was won not from hard rock but from cassiterite, an oxide weathered out of granite and washed down into stream beds, where it was panned much as prospectors later panned for gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is part of why the ancient workings are so hard to find, since a tin stream panned out in antiquity leaves nothing behind, though the granite that fed it remains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No verified deposits worth the name lie in Mesopotamia, Egypt, the Levant or the Aegean, which is why tin has been called the mystery metal of the ancient Near East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sumerian <i>AN.NA</i> and Akkadian <i>annaku</i> both mean 'tin', the earliest such texts date from the middle of the 3rd millennium BCE, and Gudea of Lagash said in the 22nd century BCE that his came from Meluhha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the metal had to travel, the tin trade is the earliest long-distance commerce in a bulk commodity that survives in its own traders' writing. From the start of the 2nd millennium BCE merchants from Assur drove donkey caravans more than 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) into central Anatolia, where no single power ruled and each city hosted a merchant colony called a <i>karum</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their letters from Kanesh count tin by the talent beside the textiles it travelled with and the silver it came home as, and record about seven wars among the Anatolian kingdoms, between 1895 and 1835 BCE, that shut the roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Mari itinerary a century later carries tin on westward through Aleppo, Qatna, Ebla and Hazor to a man at Ugarit whose title may mean Cretan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where any of it was mined is still argued, with the Taurus, Central Asia and Cornwall all read out of the isotopes, so a trade documented in thousands of letters has no agreed source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","technology","archaeology","history","bronze age","geology"],"sources":["Robert Maddin, Tamara Stech Wheeler, and James D. Muhly, \"Tin in the Ancient Near East: Old Questions and New Finds,\" <i>Expedition</i> 19, no. 2 (1977), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/tin-in-the-ancient-near-east/. [Open access]","İrfan Albayrak and Melike Zoroğlu, \"Eski Asurca Belgeler Işığında Eski Anadolu'da Yol ve Ticaret Güvenliği,\" <i>Ankara Üniversitesi Dil ve Tarih-Coğrafya Fakültesi Dergisi</i> 63, no. 2 (2023): 1358–83, https://doi.org/10.33171/dtcfjournal.2023.63.2.19. [Open access]","Wayne Powell et al., \"Tin from Uluburun Shipwreck Shows Small-Scale Commodity Exchange Fueled Continental Tin Supply across Late Bronze Age Eurasia,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 48 (2022): eabq3766, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9710885/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Isotope Systematics and Chemical Composition of Tin Ingots from Mochlos (Crete) and Other Late Bronze Age Sites in the Eastern Mediterranean Sea: An Ultimate Key to Tin Provenance?,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 6 (2019): e0218326, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0218326. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"Why Central Asia's Mushiston Is Not a Source for the Late Bronze Age Tin Ingots from the Uluburun Shipwreck,\" <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1211478, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2023.1211478. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Letter_sent_by_two_Assyrian_merchants_to_their_partner_discussing_various_aspects_of_their_business_%2C1900-1800_BCE_Kultepe%2C_Turkey_Fitzwilliam_Museum%2C_Cambridge.jpg","title":"A letter between Assyrian merchants at Kanesh, 1900–1800 BCE","desc":"Two merchants write to their partner about their business. Thousands of such letters survive from the Assyrian colony at Kanesh, and they are the fullest record anywhere of how the tin trade was actually run. Fitzwilliam Museum, Cambridge.","credit":"Photo by SpeakingArch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Letter_sent_by_two_Assyrian_merchants_to_their_partner_discussing_various_aspects_of_their_business_%2C1900-1800_BCE_Kultepe%2C_Turkey_Fitzwilliam_Museum%2C_Cambridge.jpg","alt":"A small rectangular clay tablet standing upright in a museum case, its surface covered in closely packed rows of wedge-shaped cuneiform signs."},"why":[{"q":"Why are ancient tin workings so hard to find?","a":"Because the metal was won from cassiterite panned out of stream beds rather than cut from hard rock, and a stream panned out in antiquity leaves nothing behind."},{"q":"Why is the tin trade the earliest documented long-distance bulk commerce?","a":"Because the metal had to travel, and the Assur merchants' own letters from Kanesh count it by the talent beside the textiles it moved with and the silver it came home as."},{"q":"Why does a trade this well documented still have no agreed source?","a":"Because the Taurus, Central Asia and Cornwall have all been read out of the isotopes, so thousands of letters record the traffic without settling where it was mined."}]},
{"id":"wh-273","num":273,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Copper daggers, flint arrowheads, stone wristguards and an inverted-bell drinking pot travel together as the package of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Iberia to Britain after 2750 BCE.","questions":["Ancient DNA gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two different answers at once, since its pots reached Iberia without migrants while in Britain they came with a near-total change of population.","Named for a vessel shape rather than a place or a people, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had crossed western Europe and northwest Africa by 2500 BCE and vanished before 1800 BCE."],"answer":"Bell Beaker culture","answerText":"Bell Beaker culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spread from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2750 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Disappeared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2200 – 1800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Bell Beaker culture</b> is the assemblage of grave goods that spread across western and central Europe from about 2750 BCE and had gone again between 2200 and 1800 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is named for its pot, a drinking vessel with an inverted-bell profile, and it travels as a package with a copper dagger, barbed flint arrowheads, a stone wristguard and V-perforated buttons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest radiocarbon dates for Beaker pottery are around 2750 BCE in Atlantic Iberia, which is one reason that coast is proposed as its birthplace, though the Lower Rhine and several separate origins are argued as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 2500 BCE the complex reached across western Europe and into northwest Africa, France, Italy and central Europe, and within another century it was in Britain and Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lipid residues in a small set of Beaker vessels from central Germany point to dairy products, one of the few direct signs of what the pots actually held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Whether that spread carried people or only objects was argued over for a century, and ancient DNA has now answered it differently in each region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genome-wide data from 400 prehistoric Europeans, 226 of them buried with Beakers, found only slight affinity between Iberia and central Europe, and a separate transect of Iberian genomes likewise finds little outside influence there between the Neolithic and the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Britain the same package arrived with steppe-related ancestry, and about 90 per cent of the island's gene pool was replaced within a few hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Bohemia 271 genomes show the Beaker groups losing their Y-chromosome diversity outright about 2400 BCE, while their overall ancestry shifted back towards the Neolithic farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The wristguards make a smaller version of the same point: they are so unworn that they read as prestige objects, yet the bones of the Bohemian men buried with them suggest they had drawn bows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","bronze age","europe","genetics"],"sources":["Iñigo Olalde et al., \"The Beaker Phenomenon and the Genomic Transformation of Northwest Europe,\" <i>Nature</i> 555, no. 7695 (2018): 190–96, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5973796/. [Open access]","Jessica Ryan, Jocelyn Desideri, and Marie Besse, \"Bell Beaker Archers: Warriors or an Ideology?,\" <i>Journal of Neolithic Archaeology</i> (2018): 97–122, https://doi.org/10.12766/jna.2018S.6. [Open access]","Luka Papac et al., \"Dynamic Changes in Genomic and Social Structures in Third Millennium BCE Central Europe,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 35 (2021): eabi6941, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8386934/. [Open access]","Cristina Valdiosera et al., \"Four Millennia of Iberian Biomolecular Prehistory Illustrate the Impact of Prehistoric Migrations at the Far End of Eurasia,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 13 (2018): 3428–33, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5879675/. [Open access]","Adrià Breu et al., \"Pottery Spilled the Beans: Patterns in the Processing and Consumption of Dietary Lipids in Central Germany from the Early Neolithic to the Late Bronze Age,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301278, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301278. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/-2350_Hockergrab_%28Glockenbecherkultur%2C_2500-2200_v._Chr.%29_Nationalmuseum_Prag_anagoria.jpg","title":"A Bell Beaker burial from Tisice, Bohemia, 2500–2200 BCE","desc":"The body laid on its side with the knees drawn up, the single-grave rite of the eastern half of the Beaker world. It is the Bohemian cemeteries of burials like this one that carry the ancient DNA and the skeletal evidence discussed here. National Museum, Prague.","credit":"Photo by anagoria, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:-2350_Hockergrab_%28Glockenbecherkultur%2C_2500-2200_v._Chr.%29_Nationalmuseum_Prag_anagoria.jpg","alt":"A human skeleton in a museum display, lying on its side with the legs sharply bent up towards the chest, laid in a shallow bed of earth."},"locator":{"name":"Bell Beaker culture","at":[-3.6183,40.1592],"kind":"region","area":[[-9.5,38.5],[-9,42.5],[-8.5,43.5],[-5,43.8],[-1.5,43.4],[-1,46.5],[-4.5,48.5],[-6,52],[-9.5,54],[-6,57.5],[-2,57.5],[0.5,53.5],[4.5,53],[9,54.5],[13,54.3],[17,52.5],[19,49.5],[16,47],[12,45.5],[8,44],[3,42.5],[-0.5,39],[-5,36.3],[-8.5,36.9]]},"why":[{"q":"Why did ancient DNA not give one answer about the spread?","a":"Because it answered differently by region: in Britain the package arrived with steppe-related ancestry and about 90 per cent of the gene pool was replaced, where Iberia shows little outside influence."},{"q":"Why is the culture named for a pot?","a":"Because it is an assemblage of grave goods rather than a people, travelling as a package of the bell-profiled drinking vessel with a copper dagger, flint arrowheads, a wristguard and V-perforated buttons."},{"q":"Why do the wristguards complicate their own reading?","a":"Because they are so unworn that they read as prestige objects, and yet the bones of the Bohemian men buried with them suggest they had drawn bows."}]},
{"id":"wh-274","num":274,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Flat graves for almost everybody and a few great mounds heaped with gold mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, central Europe's first society to divide its dead by rank.","questions":["Ancient DNA from cemeteries of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows men staying where they were born and women marrying in, so sons inherited the farmstead and daughters left.","Named after a village near Prague, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran from about 2200 to 1550 BCE and left princely tombs at Leubingen, Helmsdorf and Łęki Małe."],"answer":"Únětice culture","answerText":"Únětice culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2200 – 1550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leubingen tomb</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1942 BCE, give or take ten years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Łęki Małe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2130 – 1800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Únětice culture</b> is the Early Bronze Age culture of central Europe, current from about 2200 to 1550 BCE across Bohemia, Moravia, central Germany and western Poland, and named after a village near Prague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the first society in temperate Europe whose graves divide people sharply by rank: most of its dead lie in flat inhumation graves, while a few were buried under great mounds with gold and bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The richest of those, at Leubingen in central Germany, was raised over a timber chamber whose oak has been tree-ring dated to 1942 BCE, give or take ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Łęki Małe in Greater Poland a chain of such barrows went up between about 2130 and 1800 BCE beside the fortified settlement of Bruszczewo, a differentiated society that held together for perhaps fourteen generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man in the princely tomb at Helmsdorf did not die in bed: three of his bones carry injuries from a bladed weapon made around the moment of death, placed as though someone meant to kill him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In Bohemia, at the culture's core, 271 genomes spanning the 3rd millennium record a population turnover of at least 40 per cent as this new social order emerged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ancient DNA shows how such a society was held together: 46 people buried at Leubingen resolve into five family pedigrees, a community organised patrilineally in which men stayed and women came in from elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Far to the south, in Lower Austria, 129 genomes from Únětice and neighbouring cemeteries give the same picture, and carry stretches of shared DNA linking communities a long way apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sons inherited the farmstead and daughters married out, which is how a landholding elite reproduces itself, and here it can be seen centuries before any written law says so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The grave goods follow the same logic: at Leubingen how much a person was buried with varied with sex, age and where they had come from, while what kinds of object they were given did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","bronze age","europe","genetics"],"sources":["Sandra Penske et al., \"Kinship Practices at the Early Bronze Age Site of Leubingen in Central Germany,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 3871, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10873355/. [Open access]","Janusz Czebreszuk et al., \"Chronology and Social Significance of the 'Princely' Barrow Cemetery in Łęki Małe and the Central European Early Bronze Age,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0300591, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0300591. [Open access]","Nicole Nicklisch et al., \"Bioarchaeological Investigations of the Princely Grave at Helmsdorf Attesting to the Violent Death of an Early Bronze Age Ruler,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 16139, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9515160/. [Open access]","Anja Furtwängler et al., \"Tracing Social Mechanisms and Interregional Connections in Early Bronze Age Societies in Lower Austria,\" <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2025): 131, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12775072/. [Open access]","Luka Papac et al., \"Dynamic Changes in Genomic and Social Structures in Third Millennium BCE Central Europe,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 35 (2021): eabi6941, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8386934/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/MUFT_-_Leubingen_F%C3%BCrstengrab_1_Rekonstruktion.jpg","title":"The Leubingen princely grave, reconstructed","desc":"A museum reconstruction of the timber chamber under the Leubingen mound and the way its occupant was laid out with his metalwork. Museum for Prehistory in Thuringia, Weimar.","credit":"Photo by Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUFT_-_Leubingen_F%C3%BCrstengrab_1_Rekonstruktion.jpg","alt":"A museum display of a tent-shaped wooden chamber built of split logs, with a figure laid out inside it on the floor among small metal objects."},"locator":{"name":"Únětice culture","at":[14.3542,50.15],"kind":"region","area":[[11,50],[11.3,51.6],[13,52],[15.5,51.8],[17.8,51.2],[19,49.8],[18.6,48.4],[17,47.8],[15,48],[13.2,48.6],[11.8,49.2]]},"why":[{"q":"Why is this the first temperate European society whose graves divide by rank?","a":"Because most of its dead lie in flat inhumation graves while a few were buried under great mounds with gold and bronze, a split earlier cultures there do not show."},{"q":"Why does ancient DNA show how such a society held together?","a":"Because 46 people at Leubingen resolve into five family pedigrees, a patrilineal community in which men stayed and women came in from elsewhere — sons inheriting the farmstead and daughters marrying out."},{"q":"Why is that pattern significant so early?","a":"Because it is how a landholding elite reproduces itself, and here it can be seen centuries before any written law says so."}]},
{"id":"wh-275","num":275,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Gold on bronze marks a sun, a crescent and a scatter of stars on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, among them a cluster of seven usually read as the Pleiades.","questions":["Copper from a mine in Salzburg and gold from a Cornish river went into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so a picture of the sky is also a map of Bronze Age trade.","Rather than being cast in one piece, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was hammered out of a flat blank over about ten heatings, spiralling outwards from its centre."],"answer":"Nebra sky disc","answerText":"Nebra sky disc","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hoard buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Disc made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 to 200 years earlier</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carnon pick</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1620 – 1497 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Nebra sky disc</b> is a bronze disc about 32 centimetres (13 inches) across, inlaid with gold showing a sun or full moon, a crescent and a scatter of stars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A cluster of seven of those stars is widely read as the Pleiades, and on that reading the disc is a gauge for when to add a leap month and keep the lunar and solar years together, though not everyone accepts it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was buried on the Mittelberg hill in Saxony-Anhalt with two swords, two axes, two spiral arm-rings and a chisel, and those bronzes date the hoard to about 1600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disc itself is probably a century or two older than the burial, and it belongs to the Únětice culture of Early Bronze Age central Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not a casting: the metal was cast flat and then hot-forged, heated and hammered about ten times, working outwards in a spiral from the centre, in an alloy of copper with about 2.6 per cent tin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What lifts it above being a beautiful object is that its materials come from opposite ends of Europe, and that the picture on it was edited. The copper matches the ores of the Mitterberg mine in Salzburg so closely that other European deposits have been ruled out, while the gold matches natural gold from the Carnon River in Cornwall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An antler pick from the alluvial workings of the Carnon valley has been radiocarbon dated to 1620 – 1497 BCE, showing that those streams were being worked in the same era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The disc was altered over generations rather than made at one moment, and the arcs along its edges and the curved shape at its foot were added long after the first image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the reading of the team that published it, that first version encodes real astronomical knowledge, which later hands replaced with a plainer concern for where the sun rises and sets, and at last with myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","archaeology","history","bronze age","europe","astronomy"],"sources":["Sebastian Dieck et al., \"Archaeometallurgical Investigation of the Nebra Sky Disc,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 28868, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11582812/. [Open access]","Ernst Pernicka et al., \"Why the Nebra Sky Disc Dates to the Early Bronze Age: An Overview of the Interdisciplinary Results,\" <i>Archaeologia Austriaca</i> 104 (2020): 89–122, https://doi.org/10.1553/archaeologia104s89. [Open access]","State Museum of Prehistory, Halle, \"Nebra Sky Disc,\" https://www.landesmuseum-vorgeschichte.de/en/nebra-sky-disc. [Open access]","\"Nebra Sky Disc,\" Memory of the World Register, UNESCO, https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/nebra-sky-disc. [Open access]","Sandra Penske et al., \"Kinship Practices at the Early Bronze Age Site of Leubingen in Central Germany,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 3871, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10873355/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/Himmelsscheibe_von_Nebra_-_Landesmuseum_f%C3%BCr_Vorgeschichte_in_Halle_-_HD.jpg","title":"The Nebra sky disc, State Museum of Prehistory, Halle","desc":"The original disc, where it is kept. The gold circle, crescent and scattered dots belong to the first version of the image; the arc along the right edge and the curved shape at the foot were added later.","credit":"Photo by Shonagon, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himmelsscheibe_von_Nebra_-_Landesmuseum_f%C3%BCr_Vorgeschichte_in_Halle_-_HD.jpg","alt":"A round blue-green bronze disc inlaid with gold shapes: a full circle, a crescent, scattered dots including a tight cluster of seven, an arc along one edge and a curved boat-like shape at the bottom."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the disc's materials matter as much as its image?","a":"Because the copper matches the Mitterberg ores in Salzburg closely enough to rule other deposits out, while the gold matches natural gold from the Carnon River in Cornwall."},{"q":"Why is it not a single moment's work?","a":"Because the picture was edited over generations: the arcs along its edges and the curved shape at its foot were added long after the first image."},{"q":"Why is the astronomical reading not universally accepted?","a":"Because it depends on identifying one cluster of seven stars as the Pleiades and taking the disc as a gauge for adding a leap month, which not everyone accepts."}]},
{"id":"wh-276","num":276,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"No copper and no tin lay in the ground of southern Scandinavia, so every bronze of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was carried in from the Alps, Slovakia or Britain.","questions":["Hollowed oak coffins under mounds have kept the wool, hair and skin of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so we know what its people wore and not only what they were buried with.","Thousands of ships cut into rock outcrops are the commonest image left by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose crossings to Norway ran out of sight of land for tens of kilometres."],"answer":"Nordic Bronze Age","answerText":"Nordic Bronze Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Bronze Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oak coffins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Nordic Bronze Age</b> is the Bronze Age of southern Scandinavia, from about 1700 to 500 BCE, and it is the richest metal-using culture in Europe to have had no metal of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Analysis of 550 Scandinavian bronzes puts the start about 2100 BCE through the Únětice hubs of central Germany, carrying high-impurity metal from the Austrian Inn valley and Slovakia and high-tin metal from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Únětice world fell about 1600 BCE the supply faltered and old stock was melted down again, but the break opened a direct route to the Carpathian basin and the eastern Alps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That same eastern route carried Baltic amber south, and by 1600 BCE it was reaching Mediterranean cities in quantity, which is the likeliest thing the north was paying with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the region put at the centre of its imagery was not the land but the sea, and thousands of boat images are cut into outcrops across southern Scandinavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Simulating the crossing between Jutland and south-west Norway suggests the direct open-water route of about 110 kilometres (68 miles), out of sight of land for up to 50, was preferred to 700 kilometres of coast-hugging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The culture is also the best preserved in Bronze Age Europe, since its elite were laid in hollowed oak coffins under mounds between about 1500 and 1100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The waterlogged and slightly acid conditions inside dissolved the bones but kept the wool, hair and skin, so a young woman buried at Egtved 3,400 years ago is known by her corded skirt and blouse rather than by her skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strontium in her teeth, hair and wool was read in 2015 as showing that she was born abroad and had travelled far in her final months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A 2019 study found that agricultural lime has shifted the strontium of modern Danish water, and that lime-free values put her origin close to her grave instead, a question now reopened against a baseline built from 628 ancient people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","culture","archaeology","bronze age","europe","history"],"sources":["Heide W. Nørgaard, Ernst Pernicka, and Helle Vandkilde, \"Shifting Networks and Mixing Metals: Changing Metal Trade Routes to Scandinavia Correlate with Neolithic and Bronze Age Transformations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252376, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8208583/. [Open access]","Marta Díaz-Guardamino et al., \"Boats on the Rocks: Late Prehistoric Nautical Iconography and Landscape, from Northwest Iberia to Scandinavia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 6 (2026): e0349417, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13249362/. [Open access]","Boel Bengtsson et al., \"Seafaring and Navigation in the Nordic Bronze Age: The Application of an Ocean Voyaging Tool,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 4 (2025): e0320791, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11964266/. [Open access]","Karin Margarita Frei et al., \"Tracing the Dynamic Life Story of a Bronze Age Female,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 5 (2015): 10431, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4440039/. [Open access]","Erik Thomsen and Rasmus Andreasen, \"Agricultural Lime Disturbs Natural Strontium Isotope Variations: Implications for Provenance and Migration Studies,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 3 (2019): eaav8083, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6415960/. [Open access]","Karin Margarita Frei et al., \"Bronze Age Non-Elite Mobility in Denmark Examined through a New Human-Based Strontium Isotope Baseline,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0341434, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12880727/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/DO-4368-Egtvedpigens_dragt.jpg","title":"The Egtved girl's clothing, as it lay in the coffin","desc":"The short corded skirt, the blouse and the bronze belt disc of a young woman buried in an oak coffin in Jutland about 3,400 years ago. The bones dissolved in the acid water inside; the wool did not. National Museum of Denmark.","credit":"Photo by Roberto Fortuna and Kira Ursem, Nationalmuseet, Denmark, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DO-4368-Egtvedpigens_dragt.jpg","alt":"Brown woollen clothing laid out as found in a grave: a short skirt of hanging cords below a round bronze plate, with a short-sleeved top above it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Scandinavian smiths melt down old stock about 1600 BCE?","a":"Because the fall of the Únětice world faltered the supply that had carried metal through central Germany — though the break opened a direct route to the Carpathian basin and the eastern Alps."},{"q":"Why are these burials the best preserved in Bronze Age Europe?","a":"Because the elite were laid in hollowed oak coffins under mounds, and the waterlogged, slightly acid conditions dissolved the bones while keeping the wool, hair and skin."},{"q":"Why has the Egtved girl's origin been reopened?","a":"Because agricultural lime has shifted the strontium of modern Danish water, and lime-free values put her origin close to her grave rather than abroad."}]},
{"id":"wh-277","num":277,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Walled mud-brick towns in the desert oases of Turkmenistan and Uzbekistan belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which traded with the Indus, Mesopotamia and the Syro-Anatolian world.","questions":["Copper from Iran and tin from a Tajik mine went into the bronze of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose farmers were also growing grapes in four cultivated varieties.","Genetically the people of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> descend from the region's own Chalcolithic farmers, and today's Tajiks and Yagnobi descend in turn from them."],"answer":"Oxus civilisation","answerText":"Oxus civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Main phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2300 – 1950 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gonur Depe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2500 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-2nd millennium BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Oxus civilisation</b>, also called the Bactria-Margiana Archaeological Complex, is the Bronze Age culture of southern Central Asia, in the oases of what are now Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan and northern Afghanistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It built the first proto-urban centres of the region, walled compounds of mud brick holding thousands of people and watered by irrigation, one of which, Ulug-depe, covers 13 hectares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its farmers grew grapes as well as grain, and pips from Sapalli and Djarkutan in southern Uzbekistan fall into four shapes under cultivation, those from a temple larger and closer to domesticated forms than those from ordinary houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its bronze was made from copper out of polymetallic deposits in Iran and from the copper-tin mine at Mushiston in Tajikistan, the first tin deposit anywhere tied directly to finished bronzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later it came to lean on metal from the territory of the Andronovo herders to the north, whose own copper came from the Tian Shan mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the civilisation matter is where it sat, in a network of exchange called the Middle Asian Interaction Sphere that ran to the Indus, to Mesopotamia and to the Syro-Anatolian world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Gonur Depe, the administrative and religious centre of ancient Margiana, sacred rooms held miniature stone columns shaped like a chess rook, cut from onyx, marble, gypsum and limestone, and nobody knows what they were for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its people were not incomers: genetically they descend from the local Chalcolithic population of the region, with only occasional individuals of steppe or South Asian ancestry among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Indo-Iranian speakers of Central Asia today, the Tajiks and the Yagnobi, draw their ancestry from those same people, with steppe gene flow added later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The civilisation ended in the middle of the 2nd millennium BCE, its great sites giving way to small villages spread over a wider area, at a pace that differed from valley to valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","history","bronze age","asia"],"sources":["Perle Guarino-Vignon et al., \"Genetic Analysis of a Bronze Age Individual from Ulug-Depe (Turkmenistan),\" <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 13 (2022): 884612, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9441711/. [Open access]","Daniel Berger et al., \"The Rise of Bronze in Central Asia: New Evidence for the Origin of Bronze Age Tin and Copper from Multi-Analytical Research,\" <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1224873, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2023.1224873. [Open access]","Guanhan Chen et al., \"Morphotype Broadening of the Grapevine (<i>Vitis vinifera</i> L.) from Oxus Civilization 4000 Years Ago,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 16331, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9522827/. [Open access]","Élise Luneau, \"Effondrement ou évolution de la civilisation de l'Oxus ? Une révision de la transition de l'âge du Bronze à l'âge du Fer en Asie centrale méridionale,\" <i>Les Nouvelles de l'Archéologie</i> 163 (2021): 36–43, https://doi.org/10.4000/nda.11987. [Open access]","Natalia Nikolaevna Skakun and Vera Vladimirovna Terekhina, \"Manufacturing Technology of Stone Miniature Columns from the Bronze Age Site Gonur Depe, Southern Turkmenistan,\" <i>Journal of Lithic Studies</i> 8, no. 2 (2021), https://doi.org/10.2218/jls.4490. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/BMAC_bronze_tools_%28cropped%29_Gonur_Depe.jpg","title":"Bronze tools from Gonur Depe","desc":"Excavated bronzes of the Oxus civilisation, from the study that traced its copper to Iranian deposits and its tin to the mine at Mushiston in Tajikistan.","credit":"Figure by Berger, Kaniuth, Boroffka, Brugmann, Kraus, Lutz, Teufer, Wittke and Pernicka, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BMAC_bronze_tools_%28cropped%29_Gonur_Depe.jpg","alt":"Several corroded green-brown bronze objects photographed against a plain ground: flat blades, a shaft-hole implement and long pins."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the civilisation's position matter so much?","a":"Because it sat in the Middle Asian Interaction Sphere, a network of exchange running to the Indus, to Mesopotamia and to the Syro-Anatolian world."},{"q":"Why is the Mushiston mine notable?","a":"Because it is the first tin deposit anywhere tied directly to finished bronzes, alongside copper from polymetallic deposits in Iran."},{"q":"Why can its people not be read as incomers?","a":"Because genetically they descend from the local Chalcolithic population of the region, with only occasional individuals of steppe or South Asian ancestry among them."}]},
{"id":"wh-278","num":278,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Ten genomes from Ashkelon show European-related ancestry arriving with the Iron Age, the first hard test of whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were migrants or only a fashion.","questions":["Almost everything written about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from the victory inscriptions of the pharaohs who say they beat them, which is a poor kind of witness.","Terracotta coffins in the shape of a body, long attributed to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, turn out to copy an Egyptian burial custom rather than an Aegean one."],"answer":"Sea Peoples","answerText":"Sea Peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1400 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destructions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1192 – 1190 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ashkelon burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1379 – 1126 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sea Peoples</b> is the modern label for groups named in Egyptian, Hittite and Ugaritic records of the 14th to 11th centuries BCE and blamed by the pharaohs for wrecking the eastern Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nearly everything said about them comes from royal inscriptions, above all the temple of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu, which is a victory monument before it is a chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Egypt's account of its own wars cannot be checked against itself, so whether people really moved has to be asked where they are said to have settled rather than where they are said to have been beaten. In the 12th century BCE, Ashkelon, Ashdod and Ekron, three of the five cities the Hebrew Bible calls Philistine, changed sharply in their building and their pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether that was migration or the borrowing of Aegean fashions was argued over for decades with no way of deciding between them, since pots can travel without people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Genomes from ten people buried at Ashkelon settle part of it, since the early Iron Age population carries European-related ancestry that the Bronze Age population before it does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four of those ten are infants buried beneath houses of the late 12th century BCE, and their bones are carbon-dated between 1379 and 1126 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In individuals of the later Iron Age that ancestry has gone, diluted within no more than two centuries, so a migration did happen and left no lasting mark on the gene pool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objects say something similar about labels, since the terracotta anthropoid coffins long called Philistine copy an Egyptian burial tradition rather than an Aegean one, taken up by people wealthy enough to want it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The firmest date for the whole episode comes from radiocarbon rather than a pharaoh's regnal year, since destruction layers in the northern Levant fall between 1192 and 1190 BCE, and the label still does not say who these people were or whether they ever acted as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","event","archaeology","history","migration","genetics","bronze age"],"sources":["Michal Feldman et al., \"Ancient DNA Sheds Light on the Genetic Origins of Early Iron Age Philistines,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 7 (2019): eaax0061, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6609216/. [Open access]","Marco Rossi, \"Anthropoid 'Philistine' Sarcophagi: Concluding Remarks?,\" <i>Asia Anteriore Antica</i> 6 (2024): 63–80, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-2467. [Open access]","The Epigraphic Survey, <i>Medinet Habu</i>, vol. 1, <i>Earlier Historical Records of Ramses III</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 8 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1930), https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip8.pdf. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"The Sea Peoples, from Cuneiform Tablets to Carbon Dating,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e20232, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0020232. [Open access]","James Henry Breasted, <i>Ancient Records of Egypt</i>, vol. 4, <i>The Twentieth to the Twenty-sixth Dynasties</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1906), §§59–64, https://archive.org/details/ancient-records-of-egypt-vol-4_202012. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/92/Prisoners_from_the_Battle_of_the_Nile_Delta.jpg","title":"Bound prisoners on the walls of Medinet Habu","desc":"A 19th-century plate of the reliefs of Ramesses III, showing captives taken in the sea battle. The monument is the fullest source for the Sea Peoples and it was built to say that Egypt won.","credit":"Drawing by Carl Richard Lepsius, from Denkmaler aus Aegypten und Aethiopien (1859), sheet 211, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prisoners_from_the_Battle_of_the_Nile_Delta.jpg","alt":"A line engraving of rows of bound captives in profile, hands tied behind them, each row of figures wearing a different headdress and dress."},"why":[{"q":"Why can the Egyptian record not be tested against itself?","a":"Because nearly everything said about them comes from royal inscriptions, above all Medinet Habu, which is a victory monument before it is a chronicle."},{"q":"Why did pottery alone fail to settle whether people moved?","a":"Because pots can travel without people, so the sharp change in building and ware at Ashkelon, Ashdod and Ekron was argued over for decades with no way of deciding."},{"q":"Why is the migration described as leaving no lasting mark?","a":"Because the European-related ancestry that appears in the early Iron Age population at Ashkelon has gone in later Iron Age individuals, diluted within no more than two centuries."}]},
{"id":"wh-279","num":279,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Three consecutive dry years around 1198 BCE in Anatolian tree rings mark the sharpest moment yet dated within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a shock is not the same as a cause.","questions":["Writing, palaces and monumental building all stop in the Aegean around 1200 BCE in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which a potter painted marks that only look like Linear B.","Palace states from Greece to the edge of Mesopotamia failed between the mid-13th and the late 12th century BCE in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, unevenly and for no one reason."],"answer":"Late Bronze Age collapse","answerText":"Late Bronze Age collapse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destructions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dry years</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1198 – 1196 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Levant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1192 – 1190 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Late Bronze Age collapse</b> is the run of destructions, abandonments and political failures that ended the palace states of the eastern Mediterranean between the middle of the 13th century BCE and the end of the 12th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A slow shift to drier, cooler conditions around 1200 BCE has long been part of the explanation, but societies adapt to slow change, and adapting is what the Hittites had done for five centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What juniper rings and stable isotopes from central Anatolia record instead is a short shock: three consecutive and unusually severe dry years, about 1198 to 1196 BCE, at the moment the Hittite state failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the northern Levant, stratified radiocarbon anchored in king lists and astronomical observations puts the terminal destructions between 1192 and 1190 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pollen and isotopes from Cyprus and the Syrian coast read a sequence out of the same years: famine first, then seaborne raiding, then political and economic failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Precision of that order is rare for any ancient catastrophe, and it still does not prove cause, since the chronology itself is contested and radiocarbon from northern Greece runs decades earlier than the conventional dates of the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the collapse took away most completely was administration, and with it the writing that had existed to serve it and almost nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around 1200 BCE writing, the palace buildings and their economies, and monumental building of every kind ceased in the Aegean, and the emblems of the palatial world stopped being drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On a jar from the Argolid a potter painted three or four marks along the belly that look vaguely like Linear B signs without being any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether that is a joke at the expense of a vanished bureaucracy or the memory of a script nobody could read any more, it is what the end of a writing system looks like from underneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","era","history","archaeology","climate","anatolia","bronze age"],"sources":["Sturt W. Manning et al., \"Severe Multi-Year Drought Coincident with Hittite Collapse around 1198–1196 BC,\" <i>Nature</i> 614, no. 7949 (2023): 719–24, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946833/. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"The Sea Peoples, from Cuneiform Tablets to Carbon Dating,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 6, no. 6 (2011): e20232, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0020232. [Open access]","David Kaniewski et al., \"Environmental Roots of the Late Bronze Age Crisis,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 8, no. 8 (2013): e71004, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0071004. [Open access]","Jeremy B. Rutter, \"Lesson 29: Post-Palatial Twilight: The Aegean in the Twelfth Century B.C.,\" Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology (Hanover, NH: Dartmouth College), https://sites.dartmouth.edu/aegean-prehistory/lessons/lesson-29-narrative/. [Open access]","Kenneth Wardle, Thomas Higham, and Bernd Kromer, \"Dating the End of the Greek Bronze Age: A Robust Radiocarbon-Based Chronology from Assiros Toumba,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 9 (2014): e106672, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0106672. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","title":"The hearth of the throne room at Pylos","desc":"The great round hearth of the megaron in the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, which burned either late in Late Helladic IIIB or early in IIIC — one of the palace destructions that gave the collapse its name.","credit":"Photo by YukioSanjo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hearth_of_the_megaron_of_Pylos.jpg","alt":"A large low circular hearth of plastered clay set in the floor of a ruined hall, ringed by the stone bases of four columns, under a modern shelter."},"why":[{"q":"Why is slow climate change an insufficient explanation?","a":"Because societies adapt to slow change, and adapting is what the Hittites had done for five centuries — what the tree rings record instead is a short shock of three severe dry years."},{"q":"Why does unusually precise dating still not prove cause?","a":"Because the chronology itself is contested, and radiocarbon from northern Greece runs decades earlier than the conventional dates of the south."},{"q":"Why did writing disappear so completely?","a":"Because what the collapse took away most completely was administration, and the writing had existed to serve it and almost nothing else."}]},
{"id":"wh-280","num":280,"category":"The Bronze Age world","question":"Ore lies almost everywhere while tin lies almost nowhere, which is the strongest reason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced bronze rather than any advantage in the metal itself.","questions":["A furnace too cool to melt the metal left <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a spongy bloom to be hammered clean of slag, so nothing could be cast the way bronze was.","Much of sub-Saharan Africa took up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without any age of bronze before it, and at Bassar in Togo smelting begins in the 5th century BCE."],"answer":"ironworking","answerText":"ironworking","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 3,000 years of it</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bassar smelting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ironworking</b> is the winning of iron from its ore and the shaping of what comes out, and unlike bronze it could not be poured, since early furnaces gave a spongy bloom that had to be hammered clean of slag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What iron offered at first was not a better metal but a commoner one, since its ores lie almost everywhere while copper is uncommon and tin is rare. Iron objects are older than iron smelting, and some of the earliest were worked from meteorites, which supply metal that needs no furnace to reduce it from ore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That story has been overstated, though, because a high nickel content is not a firm test of meteoritic origin, metallography is needed to decide, and many objects long called meteoritic turn out to be ordinary bloomery iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Working meteorites appears to have been irregular and sporadic rather than a stage every society passed through on its way to smelting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Bronze Age had run on metal carried thousands of kilometres along routes that shifted repeatedly, failed outright at least once, and left whole regions melting down old stock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Iron needed no such network, which is the strongest reason it became the ordinary metal for tools rather than any advantage in the metal itself. Nothing in that is a ladder of improvement: the neat sequence of stone, bronze and iron is a scheme proposed in the 1830s for arranging a museum collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of sub-Saharan Africa makes the point by having no age of bronze at all, and iron has been produced there for more than 3,000 years, with technical choices multiplied to a degree unequalled on other continents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Bassar in northern Togo smelting begins in the 5th century BCE, and about 50,000 tonnes of iron were made there between the 13th and the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["technology","concept","archaeology","history","africa","iron age","bronze age"],"sources":["Vladimir Zavyalov and Nataliya Terekhova, \"Meteoritic Iron Artefacts Redux,\" <i>Archeologické Rozhledy</i> 71, no. 2 (2019): 155–67, https://doi.org/10.35686/AR.2019.8. [Open access]","Caroline Robion-Brunner et al., \"Origines et étapes de la diversité des techniques sidérurgiques en Afrique de l'Ouest : le cas de la production du fer dans le Bassar (Togo),\" <i>Afriques</i> 13 (2022), https://doi.org/10.4000/afriques.3408. [Open access]","Nikos Nerantzis et al., \"Iron Working in Aegean Thrace during Antiquity: New Evidence for Smelting Activity of the 4th Century BC at the City on the Molyvoti Peninsula,\" <i>Scientific Culture</i> 8, no. 3 (2022): 105–23, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6640260. [Open access]","Heide W. Nørgaard, Ernst Pernicka, and Helle Vandkilde, \"Shifting Networks and Mixing Metals: Changing Metal Trade Routes to Scandinavia Correlate with Neolithic and Bronze Age Transformations,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): e0252376, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8208583/. [Open access]","Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, \"Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,\" in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 67, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Bloomery_slag_%28FindID_511447%29.jpg","title":"A fragment of bloomery slag","desc":"The waste of the process, and the commonest thing it leaves behind: a heavy, bubbled lump still rich enough in iron to pull a magnet. Recorded by the Portable Antiquities Scheme.","credit":"Photo by Tom Redmayne for the Portable Antiquities Scheme, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloomery_slag_%28FindID_511447%29.jpg","alt":"A dense grey-brown lump of slag on a plain background, its broken surface pitted with round air bubbles and streaked with rust."},"why":[{"q":"Why did iron become the ordinary metal for tools?","a":"Because its ores lie almost everywhere while copper is uncommon and tin rare, so it needed none of the long, fragile networks bronze ran on — not because the metal itself was better."},{"q":"Why has the meteoritic-iron story been overstated?","a":"Because a high nickel content is not a firm test of meteoritic origin, metallography is needed to decide, and many objects long called meteoritic turn out to be ordinary bloomery iron."},{"q":"Why is the stone-bronze-iron sequence not a ladder of improvement?","a":"Because it is a scheme proposed in the 1830s for arranging a museum collection, and much of sub-Saharan Africa has no age of bronze at all."}]},
{"id":"wh-281","num":281,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Defined by a technology rather than a date, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opens near the Bronze Age collapse in the Near East and centuries later elsewhere.","questions":["Radiocarbon from Sindos in northern Greece puts the local <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> earlier than the pottery-based scheme allows, which its authors say requires the Greek periodisation to be revised.","In much of sub-Saharan Africa the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first metal age there was, because no age of bronze came before it."],"answer":"Iron Age","answerText":"Iron Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 BCE in the Near East</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aegean steel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 5th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Iron Age</b> is the last stage of the three-age system, and it is defined by use rather than by a date: in Thomsen's words, the period in which iron was used for the articles to which it is eminently suited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because that definition is technological, the period begins at a different time in every region, close to the Bronze Age collapse in the Near East and centuries later further north and west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In much of sub-Saharan Africa it is the first metal age there was, since no age of bronze preceded it, and iron has been produced there for over 3,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Steel takes longer to master than iron, and the earliest systematic use of ultra-high carbon steel in the Aegean belongs to the 7th and early 5th centuries BCE, generations after iron had become the ordinary metal there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The label therefore marks a threshold in technique rather than a moment in time. <br><br> Its dates are a scheme rather than a measurement, and where the scheme has been tested against physics it has moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greek Early Iron Age chronology was built from pottery styles tied to historical dates in the eastern Mediterranean, and had never been checked by a long series of radiocarbon dates from the Aegean itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon on 21 stratified animal bones from Sindos in northern Greece puts the period earlier than the conventional scheme allows, enough that its authors call for the Greek periodisation to be revised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The far north shows the other half of the problem, since pottery of the Finnish Iron Age is dated to between about 2,000 and 1,450 years before the present, long after Greece had passed into written history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the term names, then, is a technology and a state of the evidence, and the two do not arrive together anywhere.","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["era","concept","archaeology","history","iron age","technology"],"sources":["Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, \"Of the Different Periods to Which the Heathen Antiquities May Be Referred,\" in <i>Guide to Northern Archaeology</i>, ed. the Earl of Ellesmere (London: James Bain, 1848), 67, https://archive.org/details/guidetonorthern00ellegoog. [Open access]","Ümit Güder et al., \"Earliest Evidence for Systematic Use of Ultrahigh Carbon Steel in the Ancient Aegean in the Archaic Milesia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 3 (2025): e0312244, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0312244. [Open access]","Stefanos Gimatzidis and Bernhard Weninger, \"Radiocarbon Dating the Greek Protogeometric and Geometric Periods: The Evidence of Sindos,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 5 (2020): e0232906, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0232906. [Open access]","Petro Pesonen et al., \"Pottery, Language and Iron: Reassessing Luukonsaari and Sirnihta Wares and Their Cultural Context,\" <i>Documenta Praehistorica</i> 52 (2025): 186–211, https://journals.uni-lj.si/DocumentaPraehistorica/article/view/20680. [Open access]","Caroline Robion-Brunner et al., \"Origines et étapes de la diversité des techniques sidérurgiques en Afrique de l'Ouest : le cas de la production du fer dans le Bassar (Togo),\" <i>Afriques</i> 13 (2022), https://doi.org/10.4000/afriques.3408. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/Early_Iron_Age_hoard_%28FindID_512333%29.jpg","title":"A lump the record cannot assign to either age","desc":"Casting waste from a hoard of socketed axes in Lancashire, recorded by the Portable Antiquities Scheme as probably of the Late Bronze Age or Early Iron Age. Where the boundary falls is a question about the evidence rather than about the object.","credit":"Photo by Dot Boughton for the Portable Antiquities Scheme, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Iron_Age_hoard_%28FindID_512333%29.jpg","alt":"A rough lump of green-brown corroded copper alloy on a plain background beside a scale, its surface irregular where the metal overflowed a mould."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the period begin at a different time in every region?","a":"Because it is defined by use rather than by a date — the period in which iron was used for the articles to which it is eminently suited — so it follows the technology and not the calendar."},{"q":"Why did radiocarbon at Sindos unsettle Greek chronology?","a":"Because that chronology was built from pottery styles tied to eastern Mediterranean dates and had never been checked by a long series of Aegean radiocarbon dates, and 21 stratified bones put the period earlier."},{"q":"Why does steel not arrive with iron?","a":"Because it takes longer to master: the earliest systematic use of ultra-high carbon steel in the Aegean belongs to the 7th and early 5th centuries BCE, generations after iron became ordinary there."}]},
{"id":"wh-282","num":282,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Conquered kingdoms paid tribute as clients and were then annexed as provinces under royal governors by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which by the 7th century BCE reached from Anatolia to Egypt.","questions":["Nearly everything the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says about itself survives in palace inscriptions whose later editions inflate their own figures, one booty of 1,235 sheep swelling to 100,225.","Nineveh fell to the armies of Babylon and the Medes in 612 BCE, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last court held out at Harran with troops sent by Egypt."],"answer":"Neo-Assyrian Empire","answerText":"Neo-Assyrian Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">912 – 609 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 7th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nineveh falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">612 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> was the state based on the Tigris in northern Mesopotamia that grew out of the earlier Middle Assyrian kingdom and dominated the Near East from about 912 to 609 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its height early in the 7th century BCE its authority ran from central Anatolia to the Mediterranean coast and Egypt, and eastward to the Persian Gulf and western Iran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What set it apart was that conquest usually ended in annexation: a beaten kingdom paid tribute as a client for a time, then became a province run by a governor answerable to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roads and irrigation works tied those provinces to the centre, while people deported from conquered lands were resettled in the capitals, which grew larger than any city of the previous two millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Assyrian dating is unusually firm for so early a period, because a running list of year-names is pinned to a solar eclipse of 15 June 763 BCE, fixing many events to the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tušḫan on the upper Tigris shows a province in practice: opened in 879 BCE, it had a governor's palace, a garrison, a city wall and archives of grain, cloth and timber floated downriver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Annexation did not always sweep local rulers away, since the first known governor of neighbouring Naʾiri, in office in 849 BCE, was probably an Aramean dynast handed an Assyrian title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Control of production was patchy too, as scribes tracked metal and cloth closely but barely followed pottery, and the elite Palace Ware used at Tušḫan was most likely made by potters on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost everything the empire says about itself comes from inscriptions written for the palace, and successive editions of the same annals inflate their own figures, one booty of 1,235 sheep swelling to 100,225.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end came fast, with a Babylonian chronicle putting Nineveh's fall to the armies of Babylon and the Medes in 612 BCE and the last court holding out at Harran, with Egyptian help, until 610.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","archaeology","politics","iron age","asia","iraq"],"sources":["Ashish Sinha, Gayatri Kathayat, Harvey Weiss, Hanying Li, Hai Cheng, Justin Reuter, Adam W. Schneider, Max Berkelhammer, Selim F. Adalı, Lowell D. Stott, and R. Lawrence Edwards, “Role of Climate in the Rise and Fall of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): eaax6656, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax6656. [Open access]","Alexander Johannes Edmonds, “Just a Series of Misunderstandings? Assyria and Bīt-Zamāni, Ḫadi-/Iḫtadi-libbušu, and Aramaic in the Early Neo-Assyrian State,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica: Journal of Ancient Near Eastern Cultures</i> 3 (2021): 73–91, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-1188. [Open access]","Britt E. Hartenberger, James A. Davenport, and Timothy Matney, “Tracing Fineware Production in the Neo-Assyrian Empire: Neutron Activation Analysis of Common and Palace Ware in the Upper Tigris River Valley, Turkey,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0315378, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0315378. [Open access]","A. T. Olmstead, <i>Assyrian Historiography: A Source Study</i> (Columbia: University of Missouri, 1916), 6–8, https://archive.org/stream/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 1–23, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4b/Taylor_Prism-2.jpg","title":"The empire in its own words","desc":"The Taylor Prism, carrying Sennacherib's account of his campaign in Judah. It claims 200,150 prisoners taken in a war whose one great objective, Jerusalem, was never captured.","credit":"Photo by David Castor, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taylor_Prism-2.jpg","alt":"A tall six-sided baked clay prism standing on end in a museum case, each narrow face ruled into columns of small cuneiform signs."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Assyrian conquest different from mere tribute-taking?","a":"Because it usually ended in annexation: a beaten kingdom paid tribute as a client for a time, then became a province run by a governor answerable to the king."},{"q":"Why is Assyrian dating unusually firm for so early a period?","a":"Because a running list of year-names is pinned to a solar eclipse of 15 June 763 BCE, fixing many events to the year."},{"q":"Why must the empire's own figures be read with caution?","a":"Because almost everything it says about itself comes from palace inscriptions, and successive editions of the same annals inflate their figures — one booty of 1,235 sheep swelling to 100,225."}]},
{"id":"wh-283","num":283,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Sennacherib enlarged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a circuit of 21,815 cubits behind double walls and 15 gates, and fed it by canal from a mountain on the Urartian border.","questions":["Eighteen canals dug into the Khosr and a two-million-stone aqueduct at Jerwan were built to water <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last Assyrian capital, on the Tigris.","Armies of Babylon and the Medes destroyed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 612 BCE, the Tigris city whose builder had named its new palace the one without a rival."],"answer":"Nineveh","answerText":"Nineveh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 704 – 612 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Circuit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21,815 great cubits</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">612 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nineveh</b> was the last capital of the Neo-Assyrian Empire, an old city on the Tigris at what is now Mosul in northern Iraq, rebuilt on a new scale by Sennacherib after 704 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His inscriptions say the earlier circuit measured 9,300 cubits, that he added 12,515 cubits of the surrounding plain, and that the whole came to 21,815 great cubits behind double walls with 15 gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old palace, which he calls too small, came down for one he named the Palace without a Rival, with a park beside it planted with mountain and Chaldean trees and with trees that bore wool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The streets were widened and straightened onto a royal boulevard, and a building rule had any citizen whose rebuilt house encroached on the royal road impaled over his own doorway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The labour was imported, with Chaldeans, Aramaeans, Mannaeans and the people of Que and Hilakku set to carry the basket and mould the bricks for a capital larger than any city of the previous two millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A city that size could not live on the Khosr, the small Tigris tributary running through it, which had carried its name in cuneiform since Middle Assyrian times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So most of the king's civic work was water: an inscription of 690 BCE has him digging eighteen canals into the Khosr and cutting a channel from Kisiri into the middle of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bulk of the supply came from much further off, from Mount Tas on the Urartian border, and the canal carrying it had to be taken across a valley at Jerwan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The aqueduct built there runs more than 280 metres (920 feet) long and 22 metres (72 feet) wide, of well over two million dressed stones, and its excavators knew no structure of the kind surviving from before Roman times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Babylonian chronicle puts the city's fall to the armies of Babylon and the Medes in 612 BCE, after which what was left of the Assyrian kingdom held out at Harran further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["city","place","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 94–103, 152–54, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]","Thorkild Jacobsen and Seton Lloyd, <i>Sennacherib's Aqueduct at Jerwan</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 24 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1935), v, 8–10, 36–38, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip24.pdf. [Open access]","Abdul Rahman Younis Abdul Rahman, “Al-Khawser River in Cuneiform Sources,” <i>Athar Alrafedain</i> 1, no. 1 (2012): 158–76, https://doi.org/10.33899/athar.2012.69780. [Open access]","Ashish Sinha, Gayatri Kathayat, Harvey Weiss, Hanying Li, Hai Cheng, Justin Reuter, Adam W. Schneider, Max Berkelhammer, Selim F. Adalı, Lowell D. Stott, and R. Lawrence Edwards, “Role of Climate in the Rise and Fall of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): eaax6656, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax6656. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 1–23, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Jerwan_archaeological_site%2C_part_of_Neo-Assyrian_king_Sennacherib%27s_canal_system_06.jpg","title":"The canal crossing a valley","desc":"The aqueduct at Jerwan, which carried a canal to Nineveh across a valley on more than 280 metres (920 feet) of dressed stone. Its excavators knew no other structure of the kind surviving from before Roman times.","credit":"Photo by Levi Clancy, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerwan_archaeological_site%2C_part_of_Neo-Assyrian_king_Sennacherib%27s_canal_system_06.jpg","alt":"A broad band of dressed limestone blocks in several courses running across a dry grassy valley, a modern dirt track cutting through it, with bare hills beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why was most of Sennacherib's civic work about water?","a":"Because a city of that size could not live on the Khosr, the small Tigris tributary running through it, so canals and a channel from Kisiri were cut to feed it."},{"q":"Why was an aqueduct needed at Jerwan?","a":"Because the bulk of the supply came from Mount Tas on the Urartian border, and the canal carrying it had to be taken across a valley."},{"q":"Why did the rebuilding need imported labour?","a":"Because the capital was to be larger than any city of the previous two millennia, so Chaldeans, Aramaeans, Mannaeans and the people of Que and Hilakku were set to carry the basket and mould the bricks."}]},
{"id":"wh-284","num":284,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Nineveh yielded about 30,000 tablets and fragments collected under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a king whose own inscriptions claim he could read the script of Sumer and obscure Akkadian.","questions":["Esarhaddon left Assyria to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Babylonia to his brother Shamash-shum-ukin, an arrangement that broke into civil war and ended with the brother overthrown in 648 BCE.","The annals of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were re-edited so thoroughly that a governor’s small raid became the fourth royal campaign in one version and disappeared from the next."],"answer":"Ashurbanipal","answerText":"Ashurbanipal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">668 BCE – c. 631 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brother deposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">648 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ashurbanipal</b> ruled Assyria from Nineveh from 668 to about 631 BCE, at the height of an empire that had become the largest the world had yet seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His father divided the succession, leaving him Assyria and making his brother Shamash-shum-ukin king of Babylonia, an arrangement set down on the same tablet that describes his schooling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unusually for a king, his inscriptions claim learning: he says he took the scribal art of Adapa, worked out hard problems of division and multiplication, and read the script of Sumer and the obscure Akkadian that is difficult to master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement broke into civil war, and after Shamash-shum-ukin was overthrown in 648 BCE his household, his concubines, officers, chariots and palace furniture, was paraded before his brother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His annals were rewritten to suit: a small raid led by a governor in his accession year becomes the king's fourth campaign in one edition, and has vanished altogether from the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What is not in dispute is the collection of tablets at Nineveh, most of it gathered in the second half of the reign, once that war had been won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 30,000 tablets and fragments were recovered from his own palace, from Sennacherib's palace and from the temple of Nabu, and are now in the British Museum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are royal correspondence, medicine, astronomy and astrology, omens, incantations and literature, so the collection is the working library of a court as much as a library of letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Named scholars built it, among them the chief scribe Ištar-šumu-ēreš, who appears in some 80 letters and reports, most of them on astrology, and signed tablets from two stages of his career.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tablets are written in a hand so controlled it has been called a 'typewriter' script, and the collection, shattered in antiquity, is still being reassembled from its fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","person","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 290, 378–79, 394, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Eva Miller, “Drawing Distinctions: Assyrians and Others in the Art of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 82–107, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.87846. [Open access]","Ali Yassin Aljbori, “Ashurbanipal Library: Past and Present,” <i>Athar Alrafedain</i> 3, no. 1 (2018): 2–19, https://doi.org/10.33899/athar.2020.164532. [Open access]","Babette Schnitzlein and Jon Taylor, “Typological Aspects of Scholarly Tablets in the Library of Ashurbanipal,” <i>KASKAL</i>, n.s., 1 (2024): 205–40, https://doi.org/10.30687/KASKAL/5235-1939/2024/01/015. [Open access]","Sophie Cohen, “The Scribe's Imprint: Ištar-šumu-ēreš and the Formation of Ashurbanipal's Library,” <i>KASKAL</i>, n.s., 1 (2024): 265–82, https://doi.org/10.30687/KASKAL/5235-1939/2024/01/017. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/British_Museum_Cuneiform_planisphere_K8538.jpg","title":"A round tablet of the constellations","desc":"A circular tablet of constellations from the library at Nineveh, restored from fragments. Part of it was accidentally turned to glass by the fire that destroyed the place where it lay.","credit":"Photo by BabelStone, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Cuneiform_planisphere_K8538.jpg","alt":"A round clay disc the colour of dark olive, chipped along one edge, its face divided into eight wedge-shaped sectors by ruled lines and each sector filled with small cuneiform signs."},"why":[{"q":"Why are his claims of learning unusual for a king?","a":"Because his inscriptions say he took the scribal art of Adapa, worked hard problems of division and multiplication, and read Sumerian and the obscure Akkadian that is difficult to master."},{"q":"Why must his annals be read as edited documents?","a":"Because a small raid led by a governor in his accession year becomes the king's fourth campaign in one edition and has vanished altogether from the last."},{"q":"Why is the Nineveh collection a working library rather than a library of letters?","a":"Because its 30,000 tablets are royal correspondence, medicine, astronomy and astrology, omens, incantations and literature — what a court used rather than what it displayed."}]},
{"id":"wh-285","num":285,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Assyria broke resistance by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, uprooting whole communities and settling them elsewhere in the empire, a method used routinely from the middle of the 9th century BCE.","questions":["Sargon II is said to have moved 27,280 people out of the kingdom of Israel, one instance of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by which Assyria governed the lands its army had taken.","Sennacherib’s claim of 200,150 people carried off from Judah with their livestock is a record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the policy that filled the Assyrian capitals."],"answer":"mass deportation","answerText":"mass deportation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Regular policy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the mid-9th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samaria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 721 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mass deportation</b> was one of the two instruments by which the Neo-Assyrian Empire held the Near East, the other being an army that took the field almost every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moving conquered people was an old idea, but it became a regular political method only from the middle of the 9th century BCE, used to break resistance and to make revolt harder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figures given are large: Sargon II is said to have taken 27,280 people out of the kingdom of Israel about 721 BCE, and Tiglath-pileser III 55,000 from a single district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sieges that produced them are described in the same voice, Sennacherib claiming 46 walled cities of Judah taken by battering-rams, siege engines, mines, tunnels and assault on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people are then counted as property, 200,150 of them, great and small, male and female, carried off with horses, mules, asses, camels, cattle and sheep as spoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those numbers are the state's own and they grow between editions, so the round 200,000 has been read as a scribe's flourish over a real 150.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The violence is real but graded rather than indiscriminate: at Ekron the officials who had led the revolt were killed and their bodies hung on stakes round the city while the rest were pardoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Burning captives, the only violence towards children the inscriptions describe, is rare, and falls on peoples the Assyrians placed furthest from themselves, poor towns in the mountains without treasure to take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The palace reliefs put the same material on the walls, so that foreign bodies and foreign landscapes being reshaped by Assyrian hands became the decoration of the rooms where the empire was run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deportation was in the end a labour policy: those moved were resettled and absorbed, and the capitals they filled grew larger than any city of the previous two millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","concept","history","warfare","politics","iron age","migration"],"sources":["Vladimir Sazonov, “Küüditamispoliitika kui muistne tava Uus-Assüüria impeeriumis 9.–7. sajandini eKr,” <i>Mäetagused</i> 44 (2010): 153–74, http://www.folklore.ee/tagused/nr44/sazonov.pdf. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 12, 32–33, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]","A. T. Olmstead, <i>Assyrian Historiography: A Source Study</i> (Columbia: University of Missouri, 1916), 7–8, https://archive.org/stream/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich/assyrianhistorio00olmsrich_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Ben Dewar, “The Burning of Captives in the Assyrian Royal Inscriptions, and Early Neo-Assyrian Conceptions of the Other,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 67–81, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.88852. [Open access]","Eva Miller, “Drawing Distinctions: Assyrians and Others in the Art of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 9, no. 2 (2021): 82–107, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.87846. [Open access]","Ashish Sinha, Gayatri Kathayat, Harvey Weiss, Hanying Li, Hai Cheng, Justin Reuter, Adam W. Schneider, Max Berkelhammer, Selim F. Adalı, Lowell D. Stott, and R. Lawrence Edwards, “Role of Climate in the Rise and Fall of the Neo-Assyrian Empire,” <i>Science Advances</i> 5, no. 11 (2019): eaax6656, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aax6656. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Detail_of_a_gypsum_wall_relief_depicting_the_deportation_of_the_inhabitants_of_the_city_of_Lachish_by_the_Assyrian_army._Reign_of_Sennacherib%2C_700-692_BCE%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_Iraq%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg/1920px-Detail_of_a_gypsum_wall_relief_depicting_the_deportation_of_the_inhabitants_of_the_city_of_Lachish_by_the_Assyrian_army._Reign_of_Sennacherib%2C_700-692_BCE%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_Iraq%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg","title":"Leaving with what they could carry","desc":"A detail of the gypsum wall reliefs from Nineveh showing the people of Lachish being deported by the Assyrian army, with a laden pack animal, under escort. Reign of Sennacherib, 700 to 692 BCE.","credit":"Photo by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_a_gypsum_wall_relief_depicting_the_deportation_of_the_inhabitants_of_the_city_of_Lachish_by_the_Assyrian_army._Reign_of_Sennacherib%2C_700-692_BCE%2C_from_Nineveh%2C_Iraq%2C_currently_housed_in_the_British_Museum.jpg","alt":"A carved stone relief in shallow relief showing a heavily laden camel walking to the right, led by a man in a fringed tunic, over a background of overlapping scale-like shapes standing for hilly ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did deportation become a regular method rather than an occasional act?","a":"Because from the middle of the 9th century BCE it was used systematically to break resistance and make revolt harder, alongside an army that took the field almost every year."},{"q":"Why are the recorded numbers suspect?","a":"Because they are the state's own and grow between editions, so the round 200,150 has been read as a scribe's flourish over a real 150."},{"q":"Why is the violence described as graded rather than indiscriminate?","a":"Because at Ekron the officials who led the revolt were killed and the rest pardoned, and burning captives is rare and falls on poor mountain towns with no treasure to take."}]},
{"id":"wh-286","num":286,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Nabopolassar founded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from inside Assyria, and his allies the Medes and Scythians helped him take Nineveh in 612 BCE and Harran two years later.","questions":["The glazed lions, dragons and bulls of the Ishtar Gate belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose kings called themselves providers of Esagila and Ezida rather than conquerors.","Cyrus II took Babylonia in 539 BCE, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though tablets from Nippur suggest the change of officials there was sharper than usually assumed."],"answer":"Neo-Babylonian Empire","answerText":"Neo-Babylonian Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War with Assyria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">616 – 609 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nebuchadnezzar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">604 – 562 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Neo-Babylonian Empire</b>, also called the Chaldaean monarchy, was the state ruled from Babylon between the end of the Assyrian empire and the Persian conquest, and its founder was Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a revolt inside Assyria rather than an invasion from outside, since Nabopolassar was already in his tenth year as king of Babylon in 616 BCE, when the chronicle of the war opens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That war set Babylon, the Medes under Kyaxares and the Scythians against Assyria and its ally Egypt, and it took Nineveh in 612 BCE and the last Assyrian court at Harran two years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His son Nebuchadnezzar II ruled from 604 to 562 BCE, and on his foundation bricks calls himself king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, first-born son of Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The title counts temples supplied and a father named rather than campaigns won, which is a different way for a Mesopotamian king to say who he is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the empire left most visibly is glazed brick, and a lion from Nebuchadnezzar's throne room stands about 1.2 metres (4 feet) high and 1.8 metres (6 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is white on a blue-green ground with a yellow mane, and lions like it ran as a dado round the foot of a wall of glazed columns, lotus buds and palmettes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Outside, along the processional street and on the Ishtar Gate, they were joined by dragons and bulls, the animals of the gods Marduk and Adad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The empire ended in 539 BCE, when the Persian king Cyrus II took Babylonia in a transfer of power generally described as having gone smoothly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tablets from a mound at Nippur called Tablet Hill suggest otherwise, showing changes among the higher officials at that moment, and they are the kind of family archive the city has produced rather than a royal annal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","archaeology","politics","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 1–23, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Brique de fondation de Nabuchodonosor II,” inv. AO 32450, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010156663. [Open access]","Robert H. Dyson, Jr., “A Babylonian Lion in Toronto,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 2 (1963), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-babylonian-lion-in-toronto/. [Open access]","Bernhard Schneider, “Tracing Regime Change during the Transition from the Neo-Babylonian to the Achaemenid Empire at Nippur: Reconstruction of Archives Excavated in 1889,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica: Journal of Ancient Near Eastern Cultures</i> 4 (2022): 115–34, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-1576. [Open access]","Steve Tinney, “The Uses of Writing: Inscribed Objects and Texts in the Middle East Galleries,” <i>Expedition</i> 60, no. 1 (2018), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/the-uses-of-writing/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Lion_Ishtar_gate_IAM_inv88.144.jpg/1920px-Lion_Ishtar_gate_IAM_inv88.144.jpg","title":"A lion in glazed brick","desc":"A striding lion from the Ishtar Gate at Babylon, of the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II. Istanbul Archaeology Museums, inv. 88.144.","credit":"Photo by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lion_Ishtar_gate_IAM_inv88.144.jpg","alt":"A panel of glazed bricks showing a lion striding to the left in white relief, its mane picked out in ochre, against a ground of blue and green tiles."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the empire's founding a revolt rather than an invasion?","a":"Because Nabopolassar was already in his tenth year as king of Babylon in 616 BCE when the chronicle of the war opens, so the state grew out of Assyria from within."},{"q":"Why does Nebuchadnezzar's brick title say what it does?","a":"Because it counts temples supplied and a father named — provider of Esagila and Ezida, first-born son of Nabopolassar — rather than campaigns won, which is a different way to say who a king is."},{"q":"Why is the smooth Persian takeover of 539 BCE questioned?","a":"Because tablets from Tablet Hill at Nippur show changes among the higher officials at that moment, and they are a family archive rather than a royal annal."}]},
{"id":"wh-287","num":287,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Necho II of Egypt lost to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Carchemish in 605 BCE, the year he took the Babylonian throne and began 43 years of building.","questions":["The magnetism in the bricks of the Ishtar Gate dates it to about 569 BCE, inside the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name is stamped on its earliest phase.","Foundation bricks from Babylon call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, and first-born son of Nabopolassar, a title of temples rather than of conquests."],"answer":"Nebuchadnezzar II","answerText":"Nebuchadnezzar II","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">605 – 562 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carchemish</span><span class=\"dt-v\">605 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ishtar Gate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 569 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Nebuchadnezzar II</b> ruled Babylon from 605 to 562 BCE, the longest reign of the Chaldaean dynasty and the years in which the Neo-Babylonian Empire stood at its height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to the throne in the year of Carchemish, the battle on the Euphrates at which he beat Necho II of Egypt and took the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That fight was not a scramble over Assyria's corpse but the last blow of a long war in which Egypt had been trying to keep Assyria alive against Babylon and its northern allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What survives of him is mostly building rather than campaigning, and his foundation bricks call him king of Babylon, provider of Esagila and Ezida, first-born son of Nabopolassar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bricks themselves were fired below 400 °C (750 °F), a low heat, and one a laboratory can still recover today from the state of the iron in the clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Ishtar Gate, the entrance complex to the city and the most famous thing Babylon has left, went up in three phases, the earliest of them in bricks inscribed with his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The magnetism locked into that clay as it was fired shows all three phases were built inside his reign, most probably one immediately after another, rather than added later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Matched against the Levantine reference curve for the strength of the ancient magnetic field, the intensity recorded in the gate's bricks fits best at about 569 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That falls after the Babylonian campaign against Judah and Jerusalem in 586 BCE, though not immediately after, so whether the gate was raised to mark that victory is left open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the gate and along the processional street beside it his workmen set lions, dragons and bulls in glazed brick, the animals of the gods Marduk and Adad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Anita Di Chiara, Lisa Tauxe, Helen Gries, Barbara Helwing, Matthew D. Howland, and E. Ben-Yosef, “An Archaeomagnetic Study of the Ishtar Gate, Babylon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 1 (2024): e0293014, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0293014. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 15–21, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Musée du Louvre, “Brique de fondation de Nabuchodonosor II,” inv. AO 32450, Département des Antiquités orientales, https://collections.louvre.fr/en/ark:/53355/cl010156663. [Open access]","Robert H. Dyson, Jr., “A Babylonian Lion in Toronto,” <i>Expedition</i> 5, no. 2 (1963), https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/a-babylonian-lion-in-toronto/. [Open access]","Rodrigo P. da Silva, Diego R. G. Tudela, Casimiro S. Munita, Roberto Hazenfratz, Sonia H. Tatumi, Marcio Yee, and Juan C. R. Mittani, “Firing Temperature Determination and Thermoluminescence Dating of a Brick with Cuneiform Characters Found in the Ruins of Ancient Babylon,” <i>Revista do Museu de Arqueologia e Etnologia</i> 28 (2017): 58–69, https://doi.org/10.11606/issn.2448-1750.revmae.2017.133911. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Barillet_de_temple_de_shamash_a_sippar.jpg/1920px-Barillet_de_temple_de_shamash_a_sippar.jpg","title":"A building inscription, not an annal","desc":"A barrel cylinder from the temple of Shamash at Sippar, of the reign of Nebuchadnezzar II. Royal Museums of Art and History, Brussels, MRAH O 4984.","credit":"Photo by Applejuice, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barillet_de_temple_de_shamash_a_sippar.jpg","alt":"A barrel-shaped cylinder of pale clay lying on its side in a museum case, its whole surface ruled into bands and filled with lines of cuneiform."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Carchemish not merely a scramble over Assyria's corpse?","a":"Because it was the last blow of a long war in which Egypt had been trying to keep Assyria alive against Babylon and its northern allies."},{"q":"Why can the Ishtar Gate's phases be placed inside one reign?","a":"Because the magnetism locked into the clay as it was fired shows all three phases were built within his reign, most probably one immediately after another."},{"q":"Why is it left open whether the gate marked the fall of Jerusalem?","a":"Because the intensity recorded in its bricks fits best at about 569 BCE, after the 586 BCE campaign but not immediately after it."}]},
{"id":"wh-288","num":288,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Greek writers place the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on terraces four plethra square, watered by engines from the river, but no Babylonian text mentions them at all.","questions":["One argument moves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Nineveh, where Sennacherib describes a park like Mount Amanus beside his palace, fed by a canal he cut from Kisiri.","Diodorus credits the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> not to Semiramis but to a later king wanting to please a woman who missed the mountain meadows of her own country."],"answer":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon","answerText":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reign of Nebuchadnezzar II, 605 – 562 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hanging Gardens of Babylon</b> are known only from Greek and Roman writers, and the fullest surviving account does not name the king who built them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus describes a garden four plethra on each side, rising tier on tier so that the whole looked like a theatre, its topmost gallery level with the battlements of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath the terraces ran galleries carrying the weight, roofed with stone beams and layered with reeds and bitumen, then baked brick, then lead against the damp, then earth deep enough for the largest trees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He credits the work not to Semiramis but to a later king who wanted to please a woman longing for the mountain meadows of the country she came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo agrees on the square of four plethra and the vaults, and adds engines beside the stairs at which men were kept continually raising water from the Euphrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Nothing found on the ground at Babylon has settled the matter, and there have been no Babylonian texts to fix the gardens' location or describe them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What does survive is a garden of the right kind in another city, since Sennacherib describes a great park like Mount Amanus planted beside his palace at Nineveh, with mountain and Chaldean trees and trees that bore wool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had the water for it, having cut a canal from Kisiri to bring the Khosr to his orchards and run it through irrigation ditches inside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Later writers confused Nineveh with Babylon often enough that one argument moves the gardens there altogether and makes Sennacherib, not Nebuchadnezzar, their builder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That reading is disputed, since a garden and lake beside the palace would block the way into it, and the relief said to show them may be a summary of a reign rather than a picture of a place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","place","history","archaeology","iron age","iraq"],"sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 2.10, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (1933), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/2A*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 16.1.5, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D1%3Asection%3D5. [Open access]","Robert Koldewey, <i>The Excavations at Babylon</i>, trans. Agnes S. Johns (London: Macmillan, 1914), 91–100, https://archive.org/stream/excavationsatbab0000robe/excavationsatbab0000robe_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Antanas Stančius and Petras Grecevičius, “Influence of Ancient Mesopotamian Aesthetics of Gardens/Parks and Water Installations on the Development of Landscape Architecture,” <i>Athens Journal of Architecture</i> 8, no. 1 (2022): 9–34, https://doi.org/10.30958/aja.8-1-1. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 96–99, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]","Thorkild Jacobsen and Seton Lloyd, <i>Sennacherib's Aqueduct at Jerwan</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 24 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1935), 31–33, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip24.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Athanasius_Kircher_-_Turris_Babel_-_Hanging_gardens_of_Babylon.jpg/1280px-Athanasius_Kircher_-_Turris_Babel_-_Hanging_gardens_of_Babylon.jpg","title":"Somebody's guess, 1679","desc":"The gardens as engraved for Athanasius Kircher's Turris Babel. The colonnades, obelisks and galleons come from the engraver's own century rather than from the ancient descriptions.","credit":"Athanasius Kircher, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athanasius_Kircher_-_Turris_Babel_-_Hanging_gardens_of_Babylon.jpg","alt":"A dense engraving of an enormous rectangular palace of tiered classical colonnades with planted terraces on its roofs, obelisks at its corners, sailing ships on the river beside it and a hilly city behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the identity of the gardens' builder unresolved?","a":"Because they are known only from Greek and Roman writers, the fullest account does not name the king, and no Babylonian text fixes their location or describes them."},{"q":"Why has the garden been moved to Nineveh by some scholars?","a":"Because Sennacherib describes a great park like Mount Amanus beside his palace there, with the canal water to feed it, and later writers confused the two cities often enough."},{"q":"Why is that relocation disputed?","a":"Because a garden and lake beside the palace would block the way into it, and the relief said to show them may be a summary of a reign rather than a picture of a place."}]},
{"id":"wh-289","num":289,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Judaeans deported to Babylonia in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> settled at Al-Yahudu between Nippur and Uruk, where tablets record them farming and naming children after their god.","questions":["Jerusalem fell in 586 BCE and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed, though the sources disagree about its scale, 2 Kings counting 10,000 deported and Jeremiah 3,023.","Persia took Babylonia in 539 BCE, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that had removed the court, the priests and the craftsmen of Judah but left its peasants on the land."],"answer":"Babylonian captivity","answerText":"Babylonian captivity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deportations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">597 – 582 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jerusalem falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">586 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Babylonian captivity</b> was the deportation of Judaeans to Babylonia in a series of removals between 597 and 582 BCE, and it lasted until Persia took Babylonia in 539.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Zedekiah's rebellion in 588 BCE brought the Babylonian army back to Judah, and Jerusalem fell two years later after a siege that ended the kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The burnt mud brick of that destruction can be dated by the magnetic direction locked into it as it cooled, which fixes the campaign that ended the kingdom of Judah at 586 BCE without reference to any text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the same campaign against which the building of Babylon's own Ishtar Gate is dated, the gate going up a little after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The removals took the court, the officials, the priests and the craftsmen, an elite rather than a population, and the land was not left empty: peasants stayed, some fled to Egypt, and a governor was set over what remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How many went is not agreed even inside the sources, as 2 Kings gives 10,000 in one verse and 7,000 with 1,000 smiths and craftsmen in another, while Jeremiah gives 3,023 for the same deportation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two later removals are counted at about 832 men after the city fell and 745 more after the governor left in charge was murdered in 582 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Babylonia the deported can be read about directly, in cuneiform tablets from Al-Yahudu, a country town somewhere between Nippur and Uruk where Judaean families settled and farmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tablets show them dealing with Babylonian neighbours and taking on some of their ways, while going on giving their children Judaean names built round the name of their god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those names say what the exiles thought was happening to them: 'God exiled', 'God will redeem us', 'God will heal us', 'God will comfort us'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","archaeology","iron age","migration","iraq"],"sources":["Phaswane S. Makuwa, “The Emptiness of Exilic and Early Persian Judah: A Historical Study,” <i>In die Skriflig</i> 48, no. 1 (2014), https://doi.org/10.4102/ids.v48i1.724. [Open access]","Itzhak Amar, “The Genealogy of Jeconiah (1 Chronicles 3:17-24) in Light of the Tablets from Àl-Yâhudu,” <i>Old Testament Essays</i> 37, no. 3 (2024): 1–23, https://doi.org/10.17159/2312-3621/2024/v37n3a1. [Open access]","Yoav Vaknin, Ron Shaar, Oded Lipschits, et al., “Reconstructing Biblical Military Campaigns Using Geomagnetic Field Data,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 44 (2022): e2209117119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2209117119. [Open access]","Bernhard Schneider, “Tracing Regime Change during the Transition from the Neo-Babylonian to the Achaemenid Empire at Nippur: Reconstruction of Archives Excavated in 1889,” <i>Asia Anteriore Antica: Journal of Ancient Near Eastern Cultures</i> 4 (2022): 115–34, https://doi.org/10.36253/asiana-1576. [Open access]","Anita Di Chiara, Lisa Tauxe, Helen Gries, Barbara Helwing, Matthew D. Howland, and E. Ben-Yosef, “An Archaeomagnetic Study of the Ishtar Gate, Babylon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 1 (2024): e0293014, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0293014. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Al-Yahudu_Tablets3.jpg/1280px-Al-Yahudu_Tablets3.jpg","title":"The exiles' own paperwork","desc":"Cuneiform tablets from the Judaean settlements in Babylonia, displayed under the names of the towns they record, Al-Yahudu among them. Bible Lands Museum, Jerusalem.","credit":"Photo by עמית אבידן, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Yahudu_Tablets3.jpg","alt":"Small pillow-shaped clay tablets covered in cuneiform, mounted on pins in a dark museum case beside white-on-black labels reading Bit-Nashar, Al-Yahudu and URU sha Tub-Yama."},"why":[{"q":"Why can the fall of Jerusalem be dated without a text?","a":"Because the burnt mud brick of that destruction carries the magnetic direction locked into it as it cooled, which fixes the campaign to 586 BCE."},{"q":"Why do the sources disagree about how many were taken?","a":"Because 2 Kings gives 10,000 in one verse and 7,000 with 1,000 smiths and craftsmen in another, while Jeremiah gives 3,023 for the same deportation."},{"q":"Why do the Al-Yahudu tablets matter so much?","a":"Because they let the deported be read about directly: dealing with Babylonian neighbours, taking on some of their ways, and still giving their children Judaean names built round the name of their god."}]},
{"id":"wh-290","num":290,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Assyrian scribes named the northern of the two kingdoms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after a dynasty that had already ended, calling it 'the house of Omri' for well over a century afterwards.","questions":["Personal names recorded in the northern kingdom are more varied than those of the southern one, one measurable difference between <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the paired Hebrew states of the Iron Age.","Samaria fell to Assyria in the 720s BCE and became a province, ending the northern half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while the southern kingdom survived as a tributary for more than a century."],"answer":"Israel and Judah","answerText":"Israel and Judah","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron Age II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 950 – 586 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Samaria taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">720s BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jerusalem falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">586 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Israel and Judah</b> were two neighbouring kingdoms in the southern Levant during the Iron Age II, roughly 950 to 586 BCE, the northern of them ruled from Samaria in the hills and the southern from Jerusalem in the highlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are usually spoken of together, but they were separate states, and the clearest evidence of that is the working records each kept. Assyrian scribes called the northern kingdom <i>Bit Humri</i>, 'the house of Omri', after a dynasty that had ended long before, and kept the name for well over a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Samaria's own paperwork survives as ink-written potsherds listing shipments of wine and oil to the palace, and handwriting analysis of 31 of them concluded that two scribes most likely wrote them all, at Samaria itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Judah's administration shows instead in storage-jar handles stamped 'belonging to the king', which are absent from the northern kingdom's destruction layers, a gap read as the rivalry between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Personal names are a second measure: of 920 recorded from the two kingdoms the northern sample is much the smaller and yet the more varied, read as a more open society astride the trade routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jerusalem itself grew earlier than used to be thought, as 103 radiocarbon dates from five excavation areas put its westward spread in the 9th century BCE, with heavy fortification following a mid-8th-century earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Assyria took the northern kingdom's outlying districts in 733 and 732 BCE and Samaria itself in the 720s, after which it became a province whose governor appears in the Assyrian year-lists like any other official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Judah went on as a tributary, and when Sennacherib campaigned in 701 BCE his annals claim 46 walled cities taken and Hezekiah shut up 'like a caged bird' in Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They never claim the city itself, which is as near as a royal inscription comes to recording a failure; Judah lasted until Babylon destroyed Jerusalem in 586 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","archaeology","iron age","israel"],"sources":["Ariel Vishne, Mitka R. Golub, Eli Piasetzky, Israel Finkelstein, and Barak Sober, “Diversity Statistics of Onomastic Data Reveal Social Patterns in Hebrew Kingdoms of the Iron Age,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 20 (2025): e2503850122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2503850122. [Open access]","A. T. Olmstead, <i>History of Assyria</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1923), 131, 139, https://archive.org/stream/historyofassyria00olms/historyofassyria00olms_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 2–4, 26, 40, 341, 438–39, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Shira Faigenbaum-Golovin, Arie Shaus, Barak Sober, Eli Turkel, Eli Piasetzky, and Israel Finkelstein, “Algorithmic Handwriting Analysis of the Samaria Inscriptions Illuminates Bureaucratic Apparatus in Biblical Israel,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 1 (2020): e0227452, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0227452. [Open access]","Yoav Vaknin, Ron Shaar, Oded Lipschits, et al., “Reconstructing Biblical Military Campaigns Using Geomagnetic Field Data,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 44 (2022): e2209117119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2209117119. [Open access]","Johanna Regev, Yuval Gadot, Joe Uziel, et al., “Radiocarbon Chronology of Iron Age Jerusalem Reveals Calibration Offsets and Architectural Developments,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 19 (2024): e2321024121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2321024121. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>The Annals of Sennacherib</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 2 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1924), 11–12, 32–34, https://oi.uchicago.edu/sites/oi.uchicago.edu/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip2.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/LMLK_Seals_on_Jar_Handles.jpg/1280px-LMLK_Seals_on_Jar_Handles.jpg","title":"Belonging to the king","desc":"Storage-jar handles from Judah, each stamped with a royal seal. Herodian Quarter Museum, Old City of Jerusalem.","credit":"Photo by Tom Bahar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LMLK_Seals_on_Jar_Handles.jpg","alt":"Four broken pottery jar handles mounted in a museum case, each carrying a small oval stamp impression showing a winged emblem above a line of Hebrew letters."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the two kingdoms' records show they were separate states?","a":"Because each kept its own administration: Samaria's survives as ink-written potsherds listing wine and oil shipments, while Judah's shows in storage-jar handles stamped 'belonging to the king', absent from the north."},{"q":"Why is the omission in Sennacherib's annals telling?","a":"Because they claim 46 walled cities taken and Hezekiah shut up like a caged bird but never claim Jerusalem itself, which is as near as a royal inscription comes to recording a failure."},{"q":"Why are personal names used as a measure of the two societies?","a":"Because of 920 recorded from the two kingdoms the northern sample is much the smaller and yet the more varied, read as a more open society astride the trade routes."}]},
{"id":"wh-291","num":291,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"A charred lump from a synagogue ark at En-Gedi was unwrapped by computer into Leviticus, a book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and proved identical in its consonants to the medieval text.","questions":["The prologue to Sirach shows that by 132 BCE the three parts of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were already available in Greek, so both its shape and its first translation are that old.","Radiocarbon dates on 24 scroll samples trained a handwriting model that redated 135 more, pushing the manuscript record of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> older than palaeography had put it."],"answer":"Hebrew Bible","answerText":"Hebrew Bible","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest layers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Greek by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">132 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">En-Gedi scroll</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd – 4th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hebrew Bible</b> is the collection of Jewish scriptures written mostly in Hebrew and built up over centuries out of texts composed, copied and revised by many hands rather than by single authors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its oldest layers are dated by language and content: the core of Deuteronomy to the late 7th century BCE, the early Deuteronomistic material to that period or the exile, the Priestly writings later still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Word-frequency analysis across 50 chapters of the first nine books separates those three bodies of writing, supporting divisions drawn long ago on other grounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The collection was already described in three parts by the 2nd century BCE, since the prologue to Sirach, written in Egypt in 132 BCE, speaks of 'the Law, the Prophets, and the rest of the books' as already turned into Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A few passages are not in Hebrew at all but in Aramaic, a change one ancient Greek translator marked by switching dialect where the book of Daniel changes language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Until the last century the oldest complete copies were medieval, the version fixed by the Masoretes and still followed by most printed editions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scrolls from the caves of the Judaean Desert then took the manuscript evidence back a thousand years, and radiocarbon dates on 24 of them trained a handwriting model that dated 135 more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both run older than the estimates by eye they replace, and one sample of Daniel falls between 230 and 160 BCE, overlapping the years the last part of that book is thought to have been written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study of the Great Isaiah Scroll found a break in its columns at about column 27, evidence of two scribes whose hands are too alike for the eye to separate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A charred lump from a synagogue ark at En-Gedi, too fragile to unroll, was unwrapped by computer into the opening of Leviticus, dated to the 3rd or 4th century CE and identical in its consonants to the medieval text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["text","history","religion","literature","iron age","israel"],"sources":["Shira Faigenbaum-Golovin, Alon Kipnis, Axel Bühler, Eli Piasetzky, Thomas Römer, and Israel Finkelstein, “Critical Biblical Studies via Word Frequency Analysis: Unveiling Text Authorship,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0322905, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0322905. [Open access]","Henry Barclay Swete, <i>An Introduction to the Old Testament in Greek</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1900), 24, 58, https://archive.org/stream/introductiontool00swet/introductiontool00swet_djvu.txt. [Open access]","William Brent Seales, Clifford Seth Parker, Michael Segal, Emanuel Tov, Pnina Shor, and Yosef Porath, “From Damage to Discovery via Virtual Unwrapping: Reading the Scroll from En-Gedi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 9 (2016): e1601247, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601247. [Open access]","Mladen Popović, Maruf A. Dhali, Lambert Schomaker, et al., “Dating Ancient Manuscripts Using Radiocarbon and AI-Based Writing Style Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0323185, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0323185. [Open access]","Mladen Popović, Maruf A. Dhali, and Lambert Schomaker, “Artificial Intelligence Based Writer Identification Generates New Evidence for the Unknown Scribes of the Dead Sea Scrolls Exemplified by the Great Isaiah Scroll,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): e0249769, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0249769. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Great_Isaiah_Scroll.jpg/1920px-Great_Isaiah_Scroll.jpg","title":"The Great Isaiah Scroll","desc":"The best preserved of the biblical scrolls from Qumran, photographed unrolled; the change of scribe that handwriting analysis reports falls about half way along. Israel Museum, Jerusalem.","credit":"Photograph by Ardon Bar Hama, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Isaiah_Scroll.jpg","alt":"A long parchment scroll photographed unrolled and laid out in three horizontal strips, each ruled into many narrow columns of small Hebrew script, with worn and torn edges."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the collection dated by language and content?","a":"Because it was built up over centuries by many hands, so its layers are placed by their vocabulary and subject — the core of Deuteronomy to the late 7th century BCE, the Priestly writings later still."},{"q":"Why did the Judaean Desert scrolls change the manuscript picture?","a":"Because until then the oldest complete copies were medieval, and the scrolls took the evidence back a thousand years — with radiocarbon on 24 of them training a model that dated 135 more."},{"q":"Why could a scribal change be found that the eye had missed?","a":"Because a study of the Great Isaiah Scroll found a break at about column 27, evidence of two scribes whose hands are too alike to separate by looking."}]},
{"id":"wh-292","num":292,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Papyri from a Judaean garrison at Elephantine record a temple of their god in Egypt, evidence that the early practice of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not confined to the temple at Jerusalem.","questions":["A Judaean historian of the 1st century CE counts three schools within <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Pharisees, the Sadducees and the Essenes, which is a picture of a plural rather than a single practice.","After Rome destroyed the temple in 70 CE the centre of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lay in reading and applying the law in local assemblies, one of which kept its Leviticus scroll at En-Gedi."],"answer":"Judaism","answerText":"Judaism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yeb petition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">408 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">En-Gedi burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Judaism</b> is the religion of the Jewish people, built around one God and around the Torah, and it takes its name from Judah, the southern of the two Iron Age Hebrew kingdoms. Its formative centuries are the ones after that kingdom ended, when the temple in Jerusalem was rebuilt under Persian rule and a written law became what the community organised itself around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That law was authoritative without being fixed: through the Second Temple period it was interpreted, updated and in places rewritten, and works such as the Temple Scroll give versions that never entered the canon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jerusalem was not the only place of sacrifice either: papyri from a Judaean garrison at Elephantine in Egypt describe a temple of the god Ya'u standing there before Persian rule and pulled down by local enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 408 BCE the priests there wrote to the governor of Judaea for leave to rebuild it, saying that since the loss they had worn sackcloth, drunk no wine and offered neither meal-offering nor sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Away from Judaea the scriptures were read in other languages, an interpreter turning the lesson into Aramaic where that was spoken, while in Egypt a written Greek translation was made instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the 1st century CE it was plural rather than single, and a Judaean historian counts three schools among his own people, the Pharisees, the Sadducees and the Essenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The scrolls hidden in the caves of the Judaean Desert belong to the same world, and radiocarbon dating has pushed the copying of literary manuscripts there back before the scribal revival once credited with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome destroyed the temple in 70 CE, and the fullest account of the burning is by that same historian, writing for a Roman readership and putting the fire down to the defenders themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What outlasted the temple was the reading of the law in local assemblies, and the synagogue at En-Gedi kept a scroll of Leviticus in its ark until the town burnt about 600 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["religion","practice","history","iron age","israel"],"sources":["Simon Paganini, “Deuteronomy in the Second Temple Period: Law and Its Developing Interpretation,” <i>Verbum et Ecclesia</i> 34, no. 2 (2013), https://doi.org/10.4102/ve.v34i2.800. [Open access]","A. Cowley, ed., <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), 108–22, https://archive.org/stream/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Henry Barclay Swete, <i>An Introduction to the Old Testament in Greek</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1900), 3–4, 24, https://archive.org/stream/introductiontool00swet/introductiontool00swet_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Josephus, <i>The Wars of the Jews</i> 2.119, trans. William Whiston, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0148%3Abook%3D2%3Awhiston+chapter%3D8%3Awhiston+section%3D2. [Open access]","Mladen Popović, Maruf A. Dhali, Lambert Schomaker, et al., “Dating Ancient Manuscripts Using Radiocarbon and AI-Based Writing Style Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0323185, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0323185. [Open access]","Josephus, <i>The Wars of the Jews</i> 6.249–53, trans. William Whiston, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0148%3Abook%3D6%3Awhiston+chapter%3D4%3Awhiston+section%3D5. [Open access]","William Brent Seales, Clifford Seth Parker, Michael Segal, Emanuel Tov, Pnina Shor, and Yosef Porath, “From Damage to Discovery via Virtual Unwrapping: Reading the Scroll from En-Gedi,” <i>Science Advances</i> 2, no. 9 (2016): e1601247, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1601247. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif/1280px-Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif","title":"The petition from Yeb","desc":"The letter of 408 BCE from Yedoniah and the priests of the Judaean garrison at Elephantine to the governor of Judaea, asking leave to rebuild their temple.","credit":"Plate from Sachau 1907, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif","alt":"A sheet of papyrus photographed flat, carrying a dozen long lines of cursive Aramaic script, cracked and darkened down the left-hand side."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the written law authoritative without being fixed?","a":"Because through the Second Temple period it was interpreted, updated and in places rewritten, and works such as the Temple Scroll give versions that never entered the canon."},{"q":"Why does Elephantine complicate the picture of a single sanctuary?","a":"Because papyri describe a temple of the god Ya'u standing at that Judaean garrison in Egypt before Persian rule, whose priests wrote to Judaea in 408 BCE for leave to rebuild it."},{"q":"Why must the fullest account of the temple's burning be read carefully?","a":"Because it is by a Judaean historian writing for a Roman readership, who puts the fire down to the defenders themselves."}]},
{"id":"wh-293","num":293,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Genome-wide data from 14 western sites finds almost no Levantine ancestry, so what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exported across the Mediterranean was a language, a script and a way of trading rather than a population.","questions":["A Roman-period geographer says the dye-works of Tyre made the city unpleasant to live in, which is also where the wealth of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from along that coast.","Byblos, Sidon, Tyre and Arwad were separate city-states rather than one kingdom, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is better described as a coast and a network than as a country."],"answer":"Phoenicia","answerText":"Phoenicia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Purple works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1100 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tell el-Burak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">725 – 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phoenicia</b> was not a state but a string of independent city-states along the Levantine coast, and a Roman-period geographer reports that its own people still argued over whether Tyre or Sidon was its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cities faced the sea because the plain behind them is narrow, and their living came from shipping, timber, metals and above all from a dye. The same geographer says the Tyrians surpassed all nations in navigation, that their purple stood in the highest estimation, and that the sheer number of dye-works made the city unpleasant to live in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dye is far harder to find in the ground than in the texts: it is assumed to have been made all round the Mediterranean, yet only one excavated site anywhere shows sustained large-scale production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That site is Tel Shiqmona on the Carmel coast, in the border country between the Israelite kingdom and Phoenician Tyre, where workshops ran in sequence for about five centuries from roughly 1100 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Behind the harbours lay a working countryside: the estate at Tell el-Burak in Lebanon, in use from 725 to 350 BCE, has given up the first Iron Age wine press found in that country and a lime plaster stiffened with crushed pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reach of the trade shows in the ground at Sidon, whose long stratigraphy mixes Greek, Cypriot and Egyptian pottery with local wares and now carries a series of radiocarbon dates on short-lived samples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Phoenician settlements appear across the central and western Mediterranean during the 1st millennium BCE, and that spread has long been read as people moving out of the homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Genome-wide data for 210 individuals, 196 of them from 14 Phoenician and Punic sites, finds almost no Levantine ancestry in the western settlements, whose people instead resemble those of Sicily and the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What travelled was a language, a script, a religion and a way of trading rather than a population, a different sort of expansion from the one the word colony suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","state","history","archaeology","genetics","iron age"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 16.2.22, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D2%3Asection%3D22. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 16.2.23, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D2%3Asection%3D23. [Open access]","Golan Shalvi, Naama Sukenik, Paula Waiman-Barak, et al., “Tel Shiqmona during the Iron Age: A First Glimpse into an Ancient Mediterranean Purple Dye ‘Factory’,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 4 (2025): e0321082, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0321082. [Open access]","Silvia Amicone, Adriano Orsingher, Emma Cantisani, et al., “Innovation through Recycling in Iron Age Plaster Technology at Tell el-Burak, Lebanon,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 24284, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-05844-x. [Open access]","Claude Doumet-Serhal, Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bernhard Weninger, et al., “An Interdisciplinary Approach to Iron Age Mediterranean Chronology through Combined Archaeological and 14C-Radiometric Evidence from Sidon, Lebanon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 3 (2023): e0274979, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0274979. [Open access]","Harald Ringbauer, Ayelet Salman-Minkov, Dalit Regev, et al., “Punic People Were Genetically Diverse with Almost No Levantine Ancestors,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8070 (2025): 139–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08913-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Byblos_ancient_ruins,_old_Phoenician_city_of_Byblos,_Lebanon.jpg/1280px-Byblos_ancient_ruins,_old_Phoenician_city_of_Byblos,_Lebanon.jpg","title":"A city on a narrow coast","desc":"The excavated foundations of Byblos, one of the Phoenician city-states, with the sea on one side and the hills close behind it.","credit":"Photo © Vyacheslav Argenberg / vascoplanet.com, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos_ancient_ruins,_old_Phoenician_city_of_Byblos,_Lebanon.jpg","alt":"Low stone wall foundations of an ancient town on a headland, with cypress trees among them, a bay beyond and a built-up hillside in the distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the cities face the sea?","a":"Because the plain behind them is narrow, so their living came from shipping, timber, metals and above all from the purple dye."},{"q":"Why is the dye industry so hard to find archaeologically?","a":"Because although it is assumed to have been made all round the Mediterranean, only one excavated site anywhere — Tel Shiqmona — shows sustained large-scale production."},{"q":"Why is the western expansion not what the word colony suggests?","a":"Because genome-wide data for 196 people from 14 Phoenician and Punic sites finds almost no Levantine ancestry there, so what travelled was a language, a script, a religion and a way of trading."}]},
{"id":"wh-294","num":294,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Herodotus says the Ionians learned their letters from the Phoenicians and went on calling them Phoenician, which is one ancient account of how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Greece.","questions":["Hebrew and Aramaic speakers took over the 22 signs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without adding letters for sounds their own languages had and Phoenician did not.","The epitaph of Ahiram, cut on a sarcophagus lid at Byblos around 1000 BCE, is the earliest long text in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a consonantal script of 22 signs read right to left."],"answer":"Phoenician alphabet","answerText":"Phoenician alphabet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ahiram epitaph</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek adoption</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th – 8th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Phoenician alphabet</b> was a consonantal script of 22 signs, written from right to left, used by the Phoenician cities through the 1st millennium BCE and ancestral to most of the alphabets in use today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It writes consonants only and leaves the vowels to the reader, and its letters are no longer pictures, the shapes having drifted away from the objects their ancestors were drawn from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fixed order is the form the older alphabetic tradition settled into, and that order is already recognisable in the longer sign list of Bronze Age Ugarit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest long example is the epitaph of Ahiram, cut about 1000 BCE on the lid of a stone sarcophagus at Byblos and judged, when it was published, some four centuries older than the Moabite stele.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phoenician texts often mark their word divisions, and the marks seem to follow how a passage was to be spoken rather than where its grammar divides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When Hebrew and Aramaic speakers took the script over for their own languages they added no signs for the sounds Phoenician lacked, but made the 22 available letters do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shapes drifted apart all the same, so that distinct Hebrew and Aramaic scripts grew out of the same 22 signs across the 1st millennium BCE while the direction of writing stayed put.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the Phoenicians who came with Cadmus brought letters to a Greece that had not known them, that the Ionians were taught and changed a few shapes, and that the Greeks went on calling the characters Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How the transfer really happened is argued over, from a chance meeting of traders in some Mediterranean harbour to a formal and deliberate act, and the earliest Greek specimens are themselves written right to left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dividing speech into consonants and vowels, which the Greek version made explicit, is an analysis rather than something a language offers up, speech being a continuous wave of sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","technology","archaeology","writing","language","iron age"],"sources":["Joseph Lam, “The Invention and Development of the Alphabet,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 191–92, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","René Dussaud, “Les inscriptions phéniciennes du tombeau d’Ahiram, roi de Byblos,” <i>Syria</i> 5, no. 2 (1924): 135–57, https://doi.org/10.3406/syria.1924.3038. [Open access]","Joseph Lam, review of <i>The Semantics of Word Division in Northwest Semitic Writing Systems: Ugaritic, Phoenician, Hebrew, Moabite and Greek</i>, by Robert S. D. Crellin, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2024.06.22, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2024/2024.06.22/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 5.58, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","A. Sebastian Anderson, review of <i>The Early Greek Alphabets: Origin, Diffusion, Uses</i>, ed. Robert Parker and Philippa M. Steele, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.10.31, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.10.31/. [Open access]","L. R. Siddall, review of <i>Writing: Theory and History of the Technology of Civilization</i>, by Barry B. Powell, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2010.04.24, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2010/2010.04.24/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/National_Museum_of_Beirut_%E2%80%93_Ahiram_sarcophagus_inscription_1.jpg/1280px-National_Museum_of_Beirut_%E2%80%93_Ahiram_sarcophagus_inscription_1.jpg","title":"The epitaph of Ahiram","desc":"The inscription running along the lid of Ahiram's sarcophagus at Byblos, cut in Phoenician letters about 1000 BCE. National Museum of Beirut.","credit":"Photo by Onceinawhile, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Beirut_%E2%80%93_Ahiram_sarcophagus_inscription_1.jpg","alt":"The edge of a heavy limestone sarcophagus lid carrying a single line of angular incised letters, above a chest carved with a procession of figures in low relief."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Hebrew and Aramaic speakers add no letters when they took the script?","a":"Because they made the 22 available signs do for the sounds Phoenician lacked, and the shapes drifted apart into distinct scripts instead."},{"q":"Why is Herodotus's account of the transfer to Greece not the end of the matter?","a":"Because how it really happened is argued over, from a chance meeting of traders in a Mediterranean harbour to a formal and deliberate act — though the earliest Greek specimens are themselves written right to left."},{"q":"Why is separating consonants from vowels an analysis rather than an observation?","a":"Because speech is a continuous wave of sound and offers no such division up, so the Greek version's explicit vowels record a way of hearing a language apart."}]},
{"id":"wh-295","num":295,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"The oldest surviving reckoning of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a Tyrian king-list quoted by Josephus, in which the sister of Pygmalion flees him in his seventh year and builds a city in Libya.","questions":["Elissa is said to have bought as much ground as an ox-hide would cover and cut it into strips, which is the legend attached to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a Latin epitome.","Convention dates the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to about 814 BCE, though what can be dated on the ground there is later and rests on no Carthaginian record at all."],"answer":"founding of Carthage","answerText":"founding of Carthage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 814 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>founding of Carthage</b> is dated by convention to about 814 BCE, a date owed to Greek and Roman writers rather than to anything the city itself left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest chain of reasoning survives in Josephus, who quotes a historian working from Tyrian records: the kings of Tyre are listed in turn, and in the seventh year of Pygmalion 'his sister fled away from him, and built the city Carthage in Libya'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That source gives the sister no name and no story, only an interval: 155 years and 8 months from the reign of Hiram to the building of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story arrives later, in an epitome of a Latin history: Elissa flees a brother who murdered her husband for his gold, bargains for as much African ground as an ox-hide will cover, has the hide cut into the thinnest strips, and takes the hill afterwards called Byrsa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same account has Tyrians founding Utica before Carthage and the settlers paying their neighbours an annual rent for the ground, a trading post's arrangement rather than a conqueror's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What can be dated on the ground at Carthage runs later than that, and radiocarbon results from the site were among the evidence that reopened the conventional Iron Age chronology of the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The traditional date rests on Timaeus and on the epitome of Trogus, neither a Carthaginian record, so when Phoenicians reached the site and when ancient writers thought they did are two questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Utica, Gadir and Carthage are the foundations the westward expansion is surveyed through, and it runs on through the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city became something no foundation story predicts: genome-wide data finds the people of Carthage itself taking most of their ancestry from a profile resembling that of Sicily and the Aegean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> North African ancestry is a minority contributor at every site sampled, Carthage included, so a tale about one party out of Tyre is a poor guide to who actually lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","archaeology","iron age","migration"],"sources":["Russell Clark, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of the Phoenician and Punic Mediterranean</i>, ed. Carolina López-Ruiz and Brian R. Doak, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2022.08.13, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2022/2022.08.13/. [Open access]","Josephus, <i>Against Apion</i> 1.18, trans. William Whiston, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0216%3Abook%3D1%3Awhiston+section%3D18. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 18.4–5, trans. John Selby Watson, Attalus, https://www.attalus.org/translate/justin2.html. [Open access]","Claude Doumet-Serhal, Stefanos Gimatzidis, Bernhard Weninger, et al., “An Interdisciplinary Approach to Iron Age Mediterranean Chronology through Combined Archaeological and 14C-Radiometric Evidence from Sidon, Lebanon,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 3 (2023): e0274979, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0274979. [Open access]","Harald Ringbauer, Ayelet Salman-Minkov, Dalit Regev, et al., “Punic People Were Genetically Diverse with Almost No Levantine Ancestors,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8070 (2025): 139–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08913-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Byrsa_panorama.jpg/1280px-Byrsa_panorama.jpg","title":"The hill called Byrsa","desc":"The excavated summit of Byrsa at Carthage, looking out over the Gulf of Tunis. The Latin story derives the hill's name from the ox-hide Elissa is said to have cut into strips.","credit":"Photo by Asma Ben Gharbi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byrsa_panorama.jpg","alt":"Low ruined walls and column stumps on a hilltop among pines and scrub, with a bay, a modern white city and blue hills in the distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the conventional date rest on nothing Carthaginian?","a":"Because it comes from Timaeus and from the epitome of Trogus, and the oldest chain of reasoning survives in Josephus quoting a historian working from Tyrian king lists."},{"q":"Why does the earliest source give no story?","a":"Because it supplies only an interval — 155 years and 8 months from Hiram's reign — and an unnamed sister who fled and built the city; Elissa and the ox-hide arrive in a much later Latin epitome."},{"q":"Why is a foundation tale a poor guide to who lived there?","a":"Because genome-wide data finds the people of Carthage taking most of their ancestry from a profile resembling Sicily and the Aegean, with North African ancestry a minority contributor."}]},
{"id":"wh-296","num":296,"category":"Iron Age Near East","question":"Sargon's account of his eighth campaign praises the canals and orchards of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the same breath as it describes burning the crops and opening the granaries.","questions":["Assyrian records use the name Uruadri from the 13th century BCE, long before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> existed as the mountain kingdom around Lake Van that Assyria later fought.","The kings of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote their own language in Mesopotamian cuneiform, and their fortresses survive as mudbrick walls that can be told from later brick by the grain in them."],"answer":"Urartu","answerText":"Urartu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Name in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 13th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Urartu</b> was an Iron Age kingdom in the mountains around Lake Van, in what is now eastern Turkey and Armenia, at its height between about 800 and 600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is older than the kingdom: Assyrian scribes write Uruadri from the 13th century BCE, when it stood not for a state but for a country that rose in revolt at a king's accession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rulers set down a language of their own in the cuneiform script Mesopotamia had already adapted to Akkadian, Hittite, Hurrian and half a dozen others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly everything Assyria says about it was written to record a victory over it, so the picture has to be read against the grain of the men who wrote it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is a picture even so: Sargon's account of his eighth campaign has the Urartian king showing his people where the waters gushed out, cutting a channel that 'brought plenty, like the Euphrates', leading countless smaller channels off it, and making fruit and grapes as plentiful as rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The same text then burns those crops, opens the granaries and lets the Assyrian army eat the grain, which is the only reason the orchards are described at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That campaign ended at Musasir, the shrine of the god Haldi, out of which Sargon claims 6,170 people, 34 talents and 18 minas of gold, and 160 talents of silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the ground the kingdom survives as fortresses, and at Artaxata in Armenia a massive mudbrick wall is taken for part of an Urartian fort of about 2.5 hectares (6 acres).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The brick itself can be told apart: the size, sorting and shape of the grains in Urartian mudbrick separate it statistically from the Hellenistic brick later raised on the same hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Assyria outlasted it, though not by long, and Urartu goes out of the record during the 6th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","place","history","archaeology","iron age"],"sources":["Marta Lorenzon, Benjamín Cutillas-Victoria, Elisabeth Holmqvist, et al., “Exploring Mudbrick Architecture and Its Re-use in Artaxata, Armenia, during the 1st Millennium BC: A Multidisciplinary Study of Earthen Architecture in the Armenian Highlands,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 10 (2023): e0292361, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0292361. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 1, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from the Earliest Times to Sargon</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1926), 37–38, 292, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria1/ancient_records_assyria1_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Christopher Woods, “The Earliest Mesopotamian Writing,” in <i>Visible Language: Inventions of Writing in the Ancient Middle East and Beyond</i>, ed. Christopher Woods, Oriental Institute Museum Publications 32 (Chicago: Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago, 2010), 33, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oimp32.pdf. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 8–11, 86–87, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Sofia Kouki, Marta Lorenzon, Benjamín Cutillas-Victoria, et al., “Against the Grain: Leveraging Machine Learning to Analyze Mudbrick Structures,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 5 (2026): e0349295, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0349295. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Van_Fortress_From_Northwest.JPG/1280px-Van_Fortress_From_Northwest.JPG","title":"The rock of Van","desc":"The citadel ridge above Van in eastern Turkey, the seat of the Urartian kings. Its rock face is cut into terraces and its crest carries fortification walls.","credit":"Photo by Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Fortress_From_Northwest.JPG","alt":"A long rocky ridge rising from a green plain, its face cut into terraces and its crest lined with ruined stone walls and a tower, under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why must the Assyrian record of the kingdom be read against the grain?","a":"Because nearly everything Assyria says about it was written to record a victory over it."},{"q":"Why are the Urartian orchards described in Sargon's account at all?","a":"Because the text goes on to burn those crops and open the granaries for the Assyrian army, so the irrigation and the fruit are there to make the destruction impressive."},{"q":"Why can Urartian mudbrick be identified as such?","a":"Because the size, sorting and shape of the grains in it separate it statistically from the Hellenistic brick later raised on the same hill."}]},
{"id":"wh-297","num":297,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"A wall some 20 metres (65 feet) thick, burnt mudbrick over smashed pottery and a gate faced in ashlar mark the ruined defences of Sardis, capital of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taken by Cyrus in 547 BCE.","questions":["Herodotus derives the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Lydus and says the land was once Maeonia, while Assyrian annals know its king Gyges as Guggu, ruler of a place across the sea.","Scores of burial mounds at Bin Tepe, a massive rubble-and-mudbrick wall at Sardis, and ashlar cut midway through Alyattes's reign are the physical remains of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in western Anatolia."],"answer":"Lydia","answerText":"Lydia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 680 – 547 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardis taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">547 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Lydia</b> was a kingdom of western Anatolia with its capital at Sardis, beneath Mount Tmolus on the Hermus plain, whose kings came to rule every people west of the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its people spoke Lydian, an Indo-European language of the Anatolian family, written in an alphabet whose letters look Greek and which is certainly not descended straight from the Phoenician script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The script was read with the help of a bilingual funerary stele from the Sardis necropolis, its Aramaic half dated by the regnal year of a Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus derives the country's name from Lydus and says it was once called Maeonia, and that the Mermnad house of Croesus took Sardis from an older line claiming descent from Heracles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest Mermnad attested outside Greek writing is Gyges, who enters Assyrian records as Guggu of Luddi, a place across the sea \"whose name, the kings, my fathers, had not heard\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ashurbanipal's annals have him beat off the Cimmerians and send two of their chiefs in fetters, then desert to Egypt and be overrun in his turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sardis was defended on a matching scale, by a wall about 20 metres (65 feet) thick of coursed rubble and mudbrick laid down in the last quarter of the 7th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cut ashlar masonry becomes an important part of Lydian building only midway through the reign of Alyattes, perhaps after that king came home from his war with the Medes about 585 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tombs are the largest class of monument the kingdom left, and scores of mounds cluster at Bin Tepe, 10 kilometres (6 miles) north of the city, the greatest called the tomb of Alyattes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wall fell with the city about 547 BCE under burnt mudbrick and smashed pottery, but Lydian stonework outlasted it: Darius's charter at Susa says the stone was wrought by \"Ionians and Sardians\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","place","history","archaeology","iron age","anatolia"],"sources":["Christopher Ratté, <i>Lydian Architecture: Ashlar Masonry Structures at Sardis</i>, Archaeological Exploration of Sardis Report 5 (Cambridge, MA: Archaeological Exploration of Sardis, 2011), xv, 3, 11, 63, 108, https://www.sardisexpedition.org/en/publications/r5. [Open access]","Enno Littmann, <i>Lydian Inscriptions, Part I</i>, Publications of the American Society for the Excavation of Sardis 6 (Leyden: E. J. Brill, 1916), 19, 23, https://archive.org/stream/lydianinscriptio00littuoft/lydianinscriptio00littuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 297–98, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","John Griffiths Pedley, <i>Ancient Literary Sources on Sardis</i>, Archaeological Exploration of Sardis Monograph 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1972), 1–2, https://www.sardisexpedition.org/en/publications/m2. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.6–7, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Tomb_of_Alyattes%2C_August_2024.jpg/1280px-Tomb_of_Alyattes%2C_August_2024.jpg","title":"The tomb of Alyattes at Bin Tepe","desc":"The greatest of the Lydian burial mounds, standing over the Hermus plain north of Sardis, with a smaller tumulus beside it.","credit":"Photograph by Chillenbro, 2024. CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Alyattes%2C_August_2024.jpg","alt":"A broad, bare, dome-shaped earthen mound rising above olive groves and ploughed fields under a clear sky, a second smaller mound at its right."},"why":[{"q":"Why could the Lydian script be read?","a":"Because a bilingual funerary stele from the Sardis necropolis carries an Aramaic half dated by the regnal year of a Persian king."},{"q":"Why does Gyges matter for dating the dynasty?","a":"Because he is the earliest Mermnad attested outside Greek writing, entering Assyrian records as Guggu of Luddi, a place across the sea whose name earlier kings had not heard."},{"q":"Why did Lydian stonework outlast the kingdom?","a":"Because Darius's charter at Susa says the stone there was wrought by Ionians and Sardians, decades after Sardis fell about 547 BCE."}]},
{"id":"wh-298","num":298,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Clay moulds at Guanzhuang, punches on a karshapana and struck electrum in Anatolia are three separate ways of making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose stamp lets metal be counted instead of weighed.","questions":["Cast in clay moulds in Henan and struck with five small punches on the Ganges plain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears in traditions that share an idea but not a technique.","A Samarian hoard holding both cut silver sheets and stamped pieces shows how slowly <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> displaced bullion that had to be weighed and tested at every payment."],"answer":"coinage","answerText":"coinage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Anatolia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Guanzhuang mint</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 640 – 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punch-marked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Coinage</b> is metal issued in standardised pieces under an authority's stamp, so that a payment can be counted out rather than weighed and tested at every exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It appears in three separate traditions within roughly the same century, in western Anatolia, on the Ganges plain and in the Central Plains of China, and the three do not share a technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Around the Aegean and on the Ganges the piece is struck, driven by a hammer against dies or punches, while in China it was cast in clay moulds like any other bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation of a bronze foundry at Guanzhuang in Henan has produced clay moulds for casting spade coins, and their technical character shows the site working as a mint for standardised pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon dates put well-organised minting there at about 640 to 550 BCE, which makes it the oldest securely dated minting site known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> India's earliest coinage is the punch-marked coin, of silver or a silver alloy, thought to have circulated from the 7th century BCE and struck not from one die but with several small punches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Local issues carry one to four symbols, while the imperial karshapana bears five on its face, often overlapping or half off the flan, at a standard of about 3.4 grams (0.12 ounces).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its silver falls away sharply after the death of Ashoka in 232 BCE, as weak successors and lost territory brought on a rapid debasement of the coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stamped metal did not sweep weighed metal away, and a hoard from the region of Samaria holds 4th-century BCE coins together with cut silver sheets of the century before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lead and silver isotopes now trace where Mediterranean coin metal came from, and they set a Phoenician world treating silver as a plain commodity against a Greek one monetising its economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","technology","iron age","china","india"],"sources":["Hao Zhao, Xiangping Gao, Yuchao Jiang, Yi Lin, Jin Zhu, Sicong Ding, Lijun Deng, and Ji Zhang, “Radiocarbon-Dating an Early Minting Site: The Emergence of Standardised Coinage in China,” <i>Antiquity</i> 95, no. 383 (2021): 1161–78, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2021.94. [Open access]","Amit Kumar Upadhyay, Neeraj Kumar Giri, Rajiv Prakash, and Hirdyesh Mishra, “Surface Elemental and Structural Analysis of Ancient Indian Punch-Marked Coins (600 to 200 BCE): Insights into Metallurgy and Economic Practices,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 30190, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-76356-3. [Open access]","F. Albarède, J. Blichert-Toft, F. de Callataÿ, G. Davis, P. Debernardi, L. Gentelli, H. Gitler, F. Kemmers, S. Klein, C. Malod-Dognin, J. Milot, P. Télouk, M. Vaxevanopoulos, and K. Westner, “From Commodity to Money: The Rise of Silver Coinage around the Ancient Mediterranean (Sixth–First Centuries BCE),” <i>Archaeometry</i> 63, no. 1 (2021): 142–55, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7821003/. [Open access]","Dana Ashkenazi, Maayan Cohen, Haim Gitler, Mati Johananoff, and Oren Tal, “A Material Study of Persian-Period Silver Coins and Hacksilber from Samaria,” <i>Materials</i> 18, no. 7 (2025): 1678, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11990297/. [Open access]","Andrew Meadows, review of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Greek and Roman Coinage</i>, ed. William E. Metcalf, Bryn Mawr Classical Review 2013.05.11, https://bmcr.brynmawr.edu/2013/2013-05-11.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Spade_Currency%2C_Zhou_Royal_Court%2C_Spring_%26_Autumn_Era_05.jpg/1280px-Spade_Currency%2C_Zhou_Royal_Court%2C_Spring_%26_Autumn_Era_05.jpg","title":"Hollow-handled spade coins of the Zhou royal house","desc":"Cast bronze in the shape of the tool it replaced, each blade carrying cast characters. Spring and Autumn period, Shanghai Museum.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, Shanghai Museum. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spade_Currency%2C_Zhou_Royal_Court%2C_Spring_%26_Autumn_Era_05.jpg","alt":"Three corroded green-brown bronze objects shaped like small spades, each with a narrow hollow handle above a flared blade cast with characters, mounted on a museum board."},"why":[{"q":"Why does coinage save the work of weighing?","a":"Because a standardised piece issued under an authority's stamp can be counted out, rather than weighed and tested for purity at every exchange."},{"q":"Why are the three traditions taken to be independent?","a":"Because they do not share a technique: around the Aegean and on the Ganges the piece is struck against dies or punches, while in China it was cast in clay moulds like any other bronze."},{"q":"Why did stamped metal not sweep weighed metal away?","a":"Because a hoard from the region of Samaria holds 4th-century BCE coins together with cut silver sheets of the century before."}]},
{"id":"wh-299","num":299,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Assyrian kings took tribute from 28 mayors and many chieftains rather than from any king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, to whom Herodotus gives a capital of seven walls and a line of four kings.","questions":["After Ashur fell the Babylonian king met the leader of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over its ruins and made a formal alliance, and Nineveh went the same way two years later.","Astyages was the last king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, deposed by Cyrus, though Darius still faced a rebel in that country claiming descent from the house of Cyaxares."],"answer":"Medes","answerText":"Medes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Assyrian records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th – 7th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nineveh falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">612 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Astyages deposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Medes</b> were an Iranian-speaking people of the Zagros mountains and the western plateau, and Herodotus makes them the masters of everything in Asia beyond the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They enter the record in Assyrian annals of the 9th century BCE, when Shalmaneser III came down to \"the lands of Messi and of the Medes\" and Shamshi-Adad V marched against \"the land of the Matai\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those annals describe a country of many small rulers rather than a kingdom: Tiglath-pileser III takes tribute from \"the chieftains of the Medes\" as far as Mount Bikni, and Sargon II from 28 mayors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Assyria carried them off in tens of thousands, 65,000 in a single campaign, and Sennacherib could still call them the distant Medes \"whose name no one among the kings, my fathers, had ever heard\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus tells another story altogether, of a people who chose Deioces as king, built him Ecbatana inside seven rings of wall, and were ruled by four kings in succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The two accounts meet at the fall of Assyria, where the Babylonian chronicle brings the Medes in under Cyaxares, and after Ashur fell the Mede and the Babylonian met over its ruins and made a formal alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nineveh went the same way two years later, and with it the empire that had been taking Median tribute for the better part of two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Median rule was itself ended by Cyrus, who deposed Astyages after what Herodotus reckons a reign of 35 years, and the Persians took Asia from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name outlived the power, for Darius records at Behistun a rebel who rose in Media claiming the family of Cyaxares, and Herodotus knew of that revolt as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whatever the Medes built is described in Assyrian, Babylonian, Old Persian and Greek, and never once in Median.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","state","history","politics","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 1, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from the Earliest Times to Sargon</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1926), 206–7, 257, 281, 286, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria1/ancient_records_assyria1_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 6, 118, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","C. J. Gadd, <i>The Fall of Nineveh: The Newly Discovered Babylonian Chronicle, No. 21,901, in the British Museum</i> (London: British Museum, 1923), 9–11, https://archive.org/stream/fallofninevehbab0000cjga/fallofninevehbab0000cjga_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>The Behistan Inscription of King Darius: Translation and Critical Notes to the Persian Text</i> (Nashville: Vanderbilt University, 1908), 16–17, https://archive.org/stream/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.95–130, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Medes_and_Persians_at_eastern_stairs_of_the_Apadana%2C_Persepolis.JPG/1280px-Medes_and_Persians_at_eastern_stairs_of_the_Apadana%2C_Persepolis.JPG","title":"Medes and Persians on the Apadana stairs at Persepolis","desc":"Two kinds of dress alternate along the eastern stairway, rounded caps and short belted coats beside fluted tiaras and long robes, identified as Medes and Persians.","credit":"Photograph by Bontenbal, 2011. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medes_and_Persians_at_eastern_stairs_of_the_Apadana%2C_Persepolis.JPG","alt":"A carved stone frieze of bearded men walking in profile, some in tall fluted headdresses and long robes and others in rounded caps and short belted coats, each holding the hand of the man ahead."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the Assyrian annals and Herodotus describe different Medes?","a":"Because the annals describe many small rulers — tribute from chieftains and from 28 mayors — where Herodotus tells of a people who chose Deioces as king and were ruled by four kings in succession."},{"q":"Why did the name outlive the power?","a":"Because Darius records at Behistun a rebel who rose in Media claiming the family of Cyaxares, a revolt Herodotus knew of as well."},{"q":"Why is everything about them at second hand?","a":"Because whatever the Medes built is described in Assyrian, Babylonian, Old Persian and Greek, and never once in Median."}]},
{"id":"wh-300","num":300,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"A clay cylinder in Akkadian has Marduk seek a righteous prince and call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by name, then bring him into Babylon without battle to restore gods to their cities.","questions":["Herodotus says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Babylon by drawing the Euphrates into a marsh and walking his army up the emptied channel, and died years later fighting the Massagetae.","Styling himself king of Anshan through Cambyses and Teispes, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Babylon's old titles for his own and founded the empire Darius later claimed kinship with."],"answer":"Cyrus the Great","answerText":"Cyrus the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 559 – 530 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Babylon taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cyrus the Great</b> founded the Persian empire, and the fullest statement of how he wished his conquest of Babylon understood is a clay cylinder written in Akkadian in his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a Babylonian text of an entirely conventional kind, denouncing Nabonidus for stopping the daily offerings and grinding his people down until Marduk sought out \"a righteous prince, after his own heart\" and called Cyrus by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On it he takes the old titles, king of Babylon, king of Sumer and Akkad, king of the four quarters of the world, and traces himself through Cambyses and an earlier Cyrus to Teispes, all styled kings of Anshan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That city was no invention of his: Assyrian annals list Anzan and Parsuash among the allies the king of Elam brought against Sennacherib at Halule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cylinder has him enter Babylon \"without battle and conflict\", the people kissing his feet, and returning gods to their cities with their people gathered back to their dwellings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those restorations are Mesopotamian, naming Ashur, Susa, Agade and the cities beyond the Tigris, and the surviving text says nothing of Judah, ending in a prayer that the gods ask Marduk for long life for Cyrus and his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greek writers tell the same conquest quite differently, Herodotus having him take Babylon by drawing the Euphrates off into a marsh and walking his men up the emptied channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus gives him his end as well, killed among the Massagetae after a reign one year short of thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xenophon made him something else again, the model ruler of a long didactic romance on how it is that men come to obey willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> And Darius, who was no son of his, tied his own line to the same royalty at Behistun, naming Achaemenes as its founder and calling Cambyses son of Cyrus a man of his family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","ruler","history","politics","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Robert William Rogers, <i>Cuneiform Parallels to the Old Testament</i> (New York: Eaton and Mains, 1912), 380–84, https://archive.org/stream/cuneiformparalle00rogeuoft/cuneiformparalle00rogeuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), 155–56, https://archive.org/stream/ancient_records_assyria2/ancient_records_assyria2_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.188–214, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=191. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 1.1, trans. Walter Miller (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>The Behistan Inscription of King Darius: Translation and Critical Notes to the Persian Text</i> (Nashville: Vanderbilt University, 1908), col. I, sections 2–3 and 10, https://archive.org/stream/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog/behistaninscrip00tolmgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg/1280px-Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg","title":"The Cyrus Cylinder","desc":"A barrel-shaped clay cylinder written in Akkadian in the name of Cyrus, from Babylon and now in the British Museum.","credit":"Photograph by Prioryman, 2012. CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_Cylinder_front.jpg","alt":"A barrel-shaped clay cylinder, pale ochre and broken across the middle, its curved surface covered in close horizontal lines of cuneiform, resting on a clear museum mount."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the Cyrus Cylinder a conventional Babylonian text?","a":"Because it denounces Nabonidus for stopping the daily offerings and has Marduk seek out a righteous prince and call Cyrus by name, which is how such a document had long been written."},{"q":"Why is it a poor source for the return of the Judaeans?","a":"Because its restorations are Mesopotamian, naming Ashur, Susa, Agade and the cities beyond the Tigris, and the surviving text says nothing of Judah."},{"q":"Why do Greek accounts of the conquest differ so much?","a":"Because Herodotus has him take Babylon by drawing the Euphrates off into a marsh and walking his men up the channel, where the cylinder has him enter without battle and conflict."}]},
{"id":"wh-301","num":301,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Beside the tomb of Darius at Naqsh-i Rustam, 29 lands are listed as belonging to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the doubtful reader is told to count the figures carrying the king's throne.","questions":["Clay tablets from Persepolis show the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeping its accounts in Elamite, glossing them in Aramaic, and feeding Ionian mothers who had just given birth.","Cyrus took Babylon's old titles and Darius listed 29 subject lands, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended when a satrap of Bactria murdered its last king in flight from Alexander."],"answer":"Achaemenid Empire","answerText":"Achaemenid Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Babylon taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">539 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Darius III, killed 330 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> was the Persian state ruled by the house of Achaemenes, and it took over conquered places rather than remaking them: at Babylon Cyrus assumed the city's own titles, king of Sumer and Akkad and of the four quarters of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clay cylinder written there in his name goes further, having Marduk seek out a righteous prince, call Cyrus by name and bring him into the city without battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The empire's widest claim is the inscription beside the tomb of Darius I at Naqsh-i Rustam, listing 29 lands from Ionia, Egypt and Babylon to Gandara, India and the Scythians beyond the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To a reader who thought that list short the text points at the carving above it, telling him to count the figures bearing the king's throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There Darius is styled king of kings and king of lands containing all kinds of men, made king by Ahura Mazda, one king of many and one lord of many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> How the state actually worked is clearest in the Persepolis archive, whose tablets are written in Elamite, glossed in Aramaic, and joined by one in Greek and one in Babylonian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They record food rather than money, the movement and issue of grain, wine and livestock between 509 and 494 BCE, down to Ionian mothers drawing grain for a newborn, twice as much for a boy as a girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Parties on the roads drew rations at stations about a day's journey apart, on a sealed document from the king or a named official, the journeys logged running to Sardis, Egypt, Kandahar and India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus adds the shape of the tax: Persia itself paid nothing, while its subjects sent Arabian frankincense, Ethiopian gold dust and tusks, and boys and maids from the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dynasty ended in 330 BCE, when Darius III, retreating toward Bactra ahead of Alexander, was seized and murdered by Bessus, his own satrap of Bactria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","politics","warfare","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Robert William Rogers, <i>Cuneiform Parallels to the Old Testament</i> (New York: Eaton and Mains, 1912), 380–84, https://archive.org/stream/cuneiformparalle00rogeuoft/cuneiformparalle00rogeuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions Transliterated and Translated with Special Reference to Their Recent Re-examination</i>, Vanderbilt Oriental Series (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 44–45, https://archive.org/stream/ancientpersianle00tolm_0/ancientpersianle00tolm_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.97, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=97. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 1–2, 40–41, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip92.pdf. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 17.73, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=17:chapter=73. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Tomb_of_Darius_I_front.jpg/1280px-Tomb_of_Darius_I_front.jpg","title":"The tomb of Darius I at Naqsh-i Rustam","desc":"The cruciform tomb front cut into the cliff in southern Iran. Above the columned false doorway the king stands before a fire altar on a platform carried by two rows of small figures, each labelled in the accompanying inscriptions as one of the lands of the empire.","credit":"Photograph by Blondinrikard Fröberg, 2015. CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Darius_I_front.jpg","alt":"A cross-shaped tomb front carved high in a pale limestone cliff, with a columned false doorway below and, above it, a small robed figure standing before an altar on a platform borne up by two rows of tiny carved men."}},
{"id":"wh-302","num":302,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Clay tablets kept on the terrace of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> call the place Parsa, and its audience-hall stairways carry tribute-bearers from 23 nations of the empire.","questions":["Darius founded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and asked Ahura Mazda on its terrace wall to keep Persia from an evil host, from famine and from Deceit.","Alexander is said to have taken 120,000 talents from the treasury at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and then burned its palaces at the end of a feast in 330 BCE."],"answer":"Persepolis","answerText":"Persepolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Darius I, before c. 513 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Archives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 – 458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">330 BCE, by Alexander</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Persepolis</b> was the palace terrace of the Achaemenid kings in southern Iran, and the clay tablets kept there call the place Parsa, the name the royal inscriptions give to Persia itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was founded by Darius I, and beneath his audience hall lay two pairs of gold and silver tablets naming his kingdom as reaching from the Scythians beyond Sogdiana to Ethiopia, and from India to Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The terrace is artificial, roughly 455 by 300 metres (1,490 by 980 feet), shaped out of a rocky slope by cutting the high ground away and filling the hollows with the rubble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On its southern retaining wall Darius asks Ahura Mazda to protect this country, beautiful and possessing good horses and good men, from an evil host, from famine and from Deceit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes cut his own text four times over the winged bulls of his gatehouse, saying that by the grace of Ahura Mazda he made this gateway of all lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The stairways of the audience hall carry tribute-bearing delegations from 23 nations, each led by a Persian or Median usher, and no woman appears in any sculpture at the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was a working centre and not only a stage: thousands of clay tablets sat in the fortification wall at the terrace's northeastern corner, the food accounts of the surrounding region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second archive, from the treasury, records silver paid out between 492 and 458 BCE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> to Egyptian woodworkers and inscription-cutters, Ionian and Syrian wage earners, and Carian goldsmiths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus calls it the capital of the Persian kingdom and the richest city under the sun, and has Alexander find 120,000 talents there, reckoned in silver and gathered since Cyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same account has the palaces burned in 330 BCE at the end of a drunken feast, urged on by an Athenian woman named Thais, in requital for Xerxes' burning of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions Transliterated and Translated with Special Reference to Their Recent Re-examination</i>, Vanderbilt Oriental Series (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 36–41, https://archive.org/stream/ancientpersianle00tolm_0/ancientpersianle00tolm_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 17.62, 70–72, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=17:chapter=71. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 1–2, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip92.pdf. [Open access]","George G. Cameron, <i>Persepolis Treasury Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 65 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 9–11, https://archive.org/stream/oip65/oip65_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Erich F. Schmidt, <i>Persepolis I: Structures, Reliefs, Inscriptions</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 68 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 40, 61, 70, 84–85, https://archive.org/stream/oip68/oip68_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Apadana_and_Tachara_of_Persepolis.jpg/1280px-Apadana_and_Tachara_of_Persepolis.jpg","title":"The terrace at Persepolis","desc":"Standing columns of the audience hall, with the doorways of Darius's palace behind them and the retaining wall of the terrace in front, below the hillside the site is cut into.","credit":"Photograph by Carole Raddato, 2019. CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apadana_and_Tachara_of_Persepolis.jpg","alt":"Seven tall fluted stone columns standing on a raised stone platform under a wide sky, with the square doorframes of a ruined palace behind them and a dry hillside to the left."}},
{"id":"wh-303","num":303,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Beside a canal cut from the Nile to the sea, a stele has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> say he is a Persian who seized Egypt, and that ships then passed through as he wished.","questions":["Wanting a memorial no king had had, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> struck coins from gold refined to an extreme purity, and killed the governor of Egypt for copying him in silver.","Babylonians moulded the bricks and Medes and Egyptians worked the gold for the Susa palace of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who also founded Persepolis and sent ships down the Indus."],"answer":"Darius the Great","answerText":"Darius the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 522 – 486 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">36 years, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Darius the Great</b> left his account of himself on stone in several places, and the plainest of them is a stele he set up beside a canal he had cut across Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On it he says he is a Persian, that from Persia he seized Egypt, that he commanded this canal dug from the Nile to the sea that goes from Persia, and that ships then passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus tells the canal differently, giving it to the Egyptian king Necho, who lost 120,000 men digging and stopped when a prophecy said he toiled in advance for a foreigner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same book has Darius send Scylax of Caryanda down the Indus to find where it reaches the sea, a voyage of thirty months, after which he subjugated the Indians and used that sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had gold refined to an extreme purity and struck into coin, wanting a memorial no king had had, and killed his governor of Egypt for copying it in silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The palace he raised at Susa was built by the whole empire: Babylonians moulded the bricks, Assyrians brought the cedar to Babylon, Ionians and Sardians cut the stone, Medes and Egyptians worked the gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He founded Persepolis as well, and beneath its audience hall his builders laid gold and silver tablets naming the kingdom he held, from the Scythians beyond Sogdiana to Ethiopia and from India to Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Coined silver begins to appear beside payment in kind in the Persepolis accounts late in his reign, a change tentatively placed at 493 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the far south of the empire it looks different again: a contract of 495 BCE from the garrison at Elephantine has two sisters exchange property granted them by the king's judges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus gives him 36 years in all, and one plain failure among the works, a campaign beyond the Danube from which the Persians got away while the Scythians missed them again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","technology","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions Transliterated and Translated with Special Reference to Their Recent Re-examination</i>, Vanderbilt Oriental Series (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 50–51, https://archive.org/stream/ancientpersianle00tolm_0/ancientpersianle00tolm_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.158, 4.44, 4.142, 4.166, 7.4, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=44. [Open access]","George G. Cameron, <i>Persepolis Treasury Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 65 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 2–3, 11, https://archive.org/stream/oip65/oip65_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Erich F. Schmidt, <i>Persepolis I: Structures, Reliefs, Inscriptions</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 68 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 70, https://archive.org/stream/oip68/oip68_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. Cowley, <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), 1–2, https://archive.org/details/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Frieze_of_Archers_AOD_488,_Louvre_2008.jpg/1280px-Frieze_of_Archers_AOD_488,_Louvre_2008.jpg","title":"Guards from the palace at Susa","desc":"Two figures from the glazed-brick friezes of Darius's palace at Susa, now in the Louvre, each carrying a spear, a bow and a quiver, their robes patterned in squares and rosettes.","credit":"Photograph by dynamosquito, 2008. CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frieze_of_Archers_AOD_488,_Louvre_2008.jpg","alt":"Two bearded men in long patterned robes walking in profile, moulded in relief from glazed bricks in turquoise, ochre and white, each holding a tall spear upright with a bow and quiver on his shoulder."}},
{"id":"wh-304","num":304,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"About three hundred ration tablets from Persepolis record a day's flour issued to travellers on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is how the spacing of its supply stations is known.","questions":["Guarded as it was, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not carry a secret, so a man at Susa sent word to Miletus under the grown-back hair of a shaved and marked slave.","Parties arriving at Persepolis along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were more than two and a half times the size of those leaving, and most of the arrivals were going there to stay."],"answer":"Royal Road","answerText":"Royal Road","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Travel texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 – 494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Between capitals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,200 stadia, per Strabo</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Royal Road</b> is what Herodotus called the highway he measured from Sardis to Susa, at 111 posting stations and 450 parasangs, ninety days of walking for a man on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He notes in passing that the roads were guarded, which is why a man wanting to send word from Susa to Miletus unseen shaved a slave's head, marked it, and waited for the hair to grow back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon credits the posting system to Cyrus, who is said to have measured how far a horse could be ridden hard in a day and set a station at that distance, with an officer at each to send on fresh riders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The system's own paperwork survives at Persepolis, where about three hundred tablets issue travellers a day's flour, most for a single day, which is how the spacing of the supply stations is known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its commonest ends are Persepolis and Susa rather than Sardis, so the line Herodotus measured turns out to be one branch of something wider.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The traffic is seasonal: six of those texts record a journey to Susa in the five months from June to October, against 42 in the other seven, the court being elsewhere in the heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carried people as much as letters, and parties arriving at Persepolis average more than two and a half times the size of those leaving, most of the arrivals going there to stay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo, writing later, reckons the interior from Susa to Persepolis at 4,200 stadia, the stretch between the two places those tablets most often name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For letters there was the angareion, a man and a horse standing at each day's interval, of which Herodotus says nothing mortal travels faster, stopped by neither snow nor rain nor heat nor darkness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The roads served an invader as well: Alexander sent to Babylon, Mesopotamia and Susa for pack animals and three thousand camels to carry the treasure of Persepolis away along them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","history","geography","technology","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 40–41, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip92.pdf. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.35, 5.52–53, 8.98, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=53. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Cyropaedia</i> 8.6.17–18, trans. Walter Miller, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0204:book=8:chapter=6:section=17. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 17.71, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=17:chapter=71. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.1, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/Persepolis_clay_tablet.jpg","title":"A clay tablet from Persepolis","desc":"An inscribed tablet of the kind the Persepolis archives are made of, in the museum on the site. Documents like this one issued rations to named parties travelling between the empire's cities.","credit":"Photograph by Pentocelo, 2007. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persepolis_clay_tablet.jpg","alt":"A small rounded slab of pale brown clay lying on a plain grey surface, its upper face covered in rows of small wedge-shaped impressions, one corner broken away."}},
{"id":"wh-305","num":305,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Built from the Old Persian words for kingdom and for protecting, the title of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is used by Darius at Behistun of the men he set over Bactria and Arachosia.","questions":["A Nile boat needing repair in 412 BCE was reported up a chain of officers to Arsames, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Egypt, whose order came back down in Aramaic.","Holding both the revenue and the troops of a province, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could shade into a dynast, and in 362 BCE the western ones rebelled together with Egypt and Sparta."],"answer":"Satrap","answerText":"Satrap","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Satraps' revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">362 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arsames in Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">order of 412 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>satrap</b> was the governor of a province of the Achaemenid Empire, and the Old Persian word behind the Greek one, <i>xšaθrapāvan</i>, is built from the words for 'kingdom' and 'to protect'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Darius uses the title at Behistun of men he set at the far ends of his empire, calling Dadarshish his servant and satrap in Bactria and Vivana his satrap in Arachosia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus counts twenty such provinces, each set a yearly tribute, and notes that Persis is not among the tributaries because the country of the Persians is altogether exempt from tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That exemption turns out to be structural: the Persian homeland is left out of the satrapal lists altogether, so the system governed the empire everywhere except its own core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Satraps themselves appear in the Persepolis ration texts in the plainest way, receiving wine at one place and grain at another out of the same accounts as everyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> What the office did day to day survives best in Egypt, where a papyrus of 412 BCE has boatmen report a Nile boat needing repair up a chain of officers to Arsames, the satrap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His order comes back down in Aramaic to an Egyptian official, treasury men inspect the hull, the ship's carpenters agree, the work is recited item by item, and the satrap orders it done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because one man held a province's revenue and its troops, the office could shade into a dynasty, and Mausolus of Caria held many strongholds and cities with Halicarnassus as his capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 362 BCE the satraps of the western coast rebelled together, with the Egyptian king and the Spartans alongside them, so that the king had to fight all of it at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came apart by the self-interest that made it, since Orontes, chosen as the rebels' general, arrested the men carrying their money and sold the cities back to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","title","history","politics","iron age","iran"],"sources":["Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions Transliterated and Translated with Special Reference to Their Recent Re-examination</i>, Vanderbilt Oriental Series (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 19, 21, 83, https://archive.org/stream/ancientpersianle00tolm_0/ancientpersianle00tolm_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.89, 3.97, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=97. [Open access]","George G. Cameron, <i>Persepolis Treasury Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 65 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 12, https://archive.org/stream/oip65/oip65_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. Cowley, <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), 88–89, https://archive.org/details/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 15.90–91, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=15:chapter=90. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), texts PF 679–681, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip92.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Persicher_Satrap_-_Tissaphernes%2C_cropped.jpg/1280px-Persicher_Satrap_-_Tissaphernes%2C_cropped.jpg","title":"A satrap on his own coinage","desc":"A silver coin in the Berlin cabinet, catalogued as carrying the portrait of the Persian satrap Tissaphernes and dated 397 to 396 BCE. Governors in western Asia Minor struck coin under their own names.","credit":"Photograph by Reinhard Saczewski, Münzkabinett Berlin. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persicher_Satrap_-_Tissaphernes,_cropped.jpg","alt":"A worn silver coin filling the frame, struck with the head of a bearded man in profile facing right, wearing a soft close-fitting cap with a flap down the side of his face."}},
{"id":"wh-306","num":306,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"No sacrifice could be made without a Magus in the Persian religion known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose worshippers built no statues or temples and offered on the highest peaks.","questions":["The fire of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fed with dry wood stripped of its bark and fanned rather than blown, since a man who breathed on the flame could be put to death.","Setting a supreme god against an evil principle and promising the resurrection of the dead, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived the Arab conquest among the Parsis of India."],"answer":"Zoroastrianism","answerText":"Zoroastrianism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ahura Mazda</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in royal inscriptions from c. 520 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek accounts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parsi landing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 716 CE, by tradition</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zoroastrianism</b> is the religion of the Avesta, whose teaching is ascribed to Zarathustra and whose supreme god is Ahura Mazda, called Ormazd in the later tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central tenets are two principles, a good and an evil one, all beings divided between them, a world of limited duration, the defeat of the evil at the end of it, and the resurrection of the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Achaemenid kings begin their inscriptions on the same god: a great god is Ahura Mazda, who created this earth, who created yonder heaven, who created man, who created welfare for man, and who made the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether those kings held the dualism of the Avesta is not settled, since the evil spirit is never named in what they cut, though a royal inscription was not meant as a creed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus describes practice rather than belief: the Persians raise no statues, temples or altars, and sacrifice on the highest peaks to the whole circuit of heaven, and to sun, moon, earth, fire and water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> No sacrifice could be offered without a Magus, who chanted over the meat what Herodotus calls the song of the birth of the gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds two things about the Magi: they leave the dead to be mangled by birds or dogs, and they kill every creature they can except dogs and men, taking great pride in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo describes the fire, fed with dry wood stripped of its bark and fanned rather than blown, since a man who breathed on the flame or fouled it was put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Magi chanted holding bundles of slender myrtle rods, and in Cappadocia a body of them called Pyraethi kept many temples of the Persian gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the Arab conquest of Persia part of the community left by sea, and the Parsis of India put their landing at Sanjan, by their own tradition, at about 716 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["religion","practice","history","iron age","iran"],"sources":["James Darmesteter, trans., <i>The Zend-Avesta, Part I: The Vendîdâd</i>, Sacred Books of the East 4 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1880), xliii–xliv, https://archive.org/stream/zendavesta01darm/zendavesta01darm_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.131–132, 1.140, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=131. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 15.3.13–15, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=15:chapter=3:section=14. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions Transliterated and Translated with Special Reference to Their Recent Re-examination</i>, Vanderbilt Oriental Series (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 39, 44–45, https://archive.org/stream/ancientpersianle00tolm_0/ancientpersianle00tolm_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Dosabhai Framji Karaka, <i>History of the Parsis</i>, vol. 1 (London: Macmillan, 1884), 30, https://archive.org/stream/historyofparsisi01karauoft/historyofparsisi01karauoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Zoroastrian_Fire_Temple%2C_Yazd_01.jpg/1280px-Zoroastrian_Fire_Temple%2C_Yazd_01.jpg","title":"The fire temple at Yazd","desc":"A working Zoroastrian fire temple in central Iran, its portico carrying the winged figure of the faravahar above the entrance and a circular pool in the court before it.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, 2016. CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zoroastrian_Fire_Temple,_Yazd_01.jpg","alt":"A low pale stone building with a columned porch and a winged emblem set in tiles above it, standing behind a large circular pool of green water, with cypress trees to either side."}},
{"id":"wh-307","num":307,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Nine roped prisoners face a king with one foot on a fallen rival in the relief above <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a tenth in a pointed cap was added to them later.","questions":["Diodorus took the sculptures over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the work of Semiramis, calling the writing Syriac and the roped rebels a hundred of the queen's lance-bearers.","Beside each bound figure on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a label saying that man lied, one of them claiming to be Bardiya the son of Cyrus."],"answer":"Behistun Inscription","answerText":"Behistun Inscription","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 520 BCE, under Darius I</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Above the plain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 150 m (500 ft)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Versions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 languages, 9 columns</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Behistun Inscription</b> is cut into a cliff in western Iran about 150 metres (500 feet) above the plain, over the caravan road that passes between the rock and the springs below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relief has Darius with his left foot on Gaumata the Magian, who lies on his back with his hands raised, a bow in the king's left hand and his right lifted to Auramazda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine rebel leaders face him, roped together by the neck with their hands tied behind them, and a tenth in a tall pointed cap, Skunkha the Scythian, was cut into the group later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below the panel stand five columns of Old Persian, with three of Elamite to the left and one of Babylonian above them, over about 18 metres (58 feet) of rock face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A short label is cut beside each bound figure and they all say one thing: this Gaumata lied, saying I am Bardiya the son of Cyrus; this Nidintu-Bel lied, saying I am Nebuchadrezzar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Darius set up other monuments making different claims: Herodotus describes a carved horseman inscribed that he won the kingdom by the excellence of his horse and of Oebares his groom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Behistun text was published as well as carved, an Aramaic version of it being sent abroad and turning up among the papyri of the Jewish garrison in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of that helped anyone read the rock: Diodorus took the sculptures for the work of Semiramis on her march from Babylon to Ecbatana, the king for the queen, and the roped prisoners for a hundred lance-bearers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He called the writing Syriac and had her climb on the piled packs of her baggage animals, while later travellers made Auramazda a heraldic device, or a cross with the Twelve Apostles below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came down by ladder and rope in the end, and the man who published the Persian columns warned that one copy could not be trusted like the rest, the ledge beneath his ladder falling away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","object","history","archaeology","iron age","iran"],"sources":["L. W. King and R. C. Thompson, <i>The Sculptures and Inscription of Darius the Great on the Rock of Behistûn in Persia</i> (London: British Museum, 1907), xii–xiv, https://archive.org/stream/sculpturesinscri00brituoft/sculpturesinscri00brituoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions Transliterated and Translated with Special Reference to Their Recent Re-examination</i>, Vanderbilt Oriental Series (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 34–35, https://archive.org/stream/ancientpersianle00tolm_0/ancientpersianle00tolm_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Henry Creswicke Rawlinson, <i>The Persian Cuneiform Inscription at Behistun, Decyphered and Translated</i> (London: John W. Parker, 1846), xx, https://archive.org/stream/10250456bsb/10250456bsb_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 3.88, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=3:chapter=88. [Open access]","A. Cowley, <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), xv, https://archive.org/details/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Behistun_Inscription_in_Persia_ca._520_BC-_UNESCO_World_Heritage_Site.jpg/1280px-Behistun_Inscription_in_Persia_ca._520_BC-_UNESCO_World_Heritage_Site.jpg","title":"The relief at Behistun","desc":"The carved panel seen from below and to one side, with the roped line of captives running towards the king, the winged figure above them, and columns of cuneiform cut into the rock around and beneath.","credit":"Photograph by PersianDutchNetwork, 2015. CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Behistun_Inscription_in_Persia_ca._520_BC-_UNESCO_World_Heritage_Site.jpg","alt":"A weathered rock panel high on a cliff, carved with a row of small standing figures roped together at the neck facing a larger robed man, a winged disc above them, and dense blocks of wedge-shaped writing filling the stone around."}},
{"id":"wh-308","num":308,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Persian inscriptions at Persepolis and at Van in Armenia name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the king who completed his father Darius's buildings and added a great gate of his own.","questions":["Herodotus says that a disputed succession gave the Persian throne to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who then reconquered Egypt and led the failed invasion of Greece in 480 and 479 BCE.","Diodorus records that the captain of the royal bodyguard was let into the bedchamber at night and murdered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 465 BCE, after which Artaxerxes I took the Persian throne."],"answer":"Xerxes I","answerText":"Xerxes I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">486 – 465 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Invaded Greece</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 – 479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">465 BCE, by the captain of the bodyguard</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","warfare","iron age","iran"],"abstract":"<b>Xerxes I</b> ruled the Achaemenid Empire from 486 to 465 BCE. Herodotus reports that he was the son of Darius the Great by Atossa, a daughter of Cyrus, and won a disputed succession against an elder half-brother, Artobazanes, by arguing that he alone had been born after their father became king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His own account mentions no dispute, saying only that Darius made him the greatest after himself and that he became king when his father went from the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His inscriptions are cut in Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian, credit every act to Ahura Mazda, and repeat his father's titles almost word for word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One stands at Van in Armenia, in a rock niche that Xerxes says his father had ordered cut and then left without any inscription.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Egypt had revolted at the end of Darius's reign, and Xerxes subdued it in the year after his father's death, handing the country to his brother Achaemenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His invasion of Greece in 480 and 479 BCE failed, and nearly everything told of that campaign, and of his carrying off a golden statue from the great temple at Babylon, comes from Greek writers rather than from any Persian or Babylonian record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Persepolis he completed his father's buildings, then raised the terrace gate whose inscription calls it a colonnade for all countries, a palace of his own, and the quarters excavators named the Harem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also began the Throne Hall that his son finished, and the Treasury tablets there record few workmen through the years of the Greek war but large gangs again near the end of his reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 465 BCE Artabanus, the captain of the royal bodyguard, was let into his bedchamber at night and killed him, and after a struggle among his sons the throne passed to Artaxerxes I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions Transliterated and Translated with Special Reference to Their Recent Re-examination</i>, Vanderbilt Oriental Series (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 39–42, 53–54, https://archive.org/stream/ancientpersianle00tolm_0/ancientpersianle00tolm_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Erich F. Schmidt, <i>Persepolis I: Structures, Reliefs, Inscriptions</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 68 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 41–42, 65–68, 238–44, 255–64, https://archive.org/stream/oip68/oip68_djvu.txt. [Open access]","George G. Cameron, <i>Persepolis Treasury Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 65 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 13–16, https://archive.org/stream/oip65/oip65_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.183, 7.2–3, 7.7, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=2. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.69, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=69. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Gate_of_All_Nations_01.jpg/1280px-Gate_of_All_Nations_01.jpg","title":"The Gate of All Nations, Persepolis","desc":"The western doorway of the terrace gate built by Xerxes I at Persepolis, flanked by colossal human-headed bulls, with one column of the hall still standing behind them.","credit":"Majid Taghipour, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_of_All_Nations_01.jpg","alt":"Two massive weathered stone piers carved with colossal human-headed bulls flank a gateway on a bare plain, with a tall fluted column standing between them."}},
{"id":"wh-309","num":309,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Twelve bronze lion-weights from Nineveh carry a label in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside the Assyrian one, under the names of Shalmaneser, Sargon and Sennacherib.","questions":["About 203 of the 269 green chert mortars and plates dug from the Persepolis Treasury are inscribed in ink in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the empire's written language.","Because papyrus survived only in Egypt, the fullest run of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to come down to us is the archive of the Jewish garrison at Elephantine on the Nile."],"answer":"Aramaic","answerText":"Aramaic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before 727 BCE, at Zenjirli</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Egyptian papyri</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Displaced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Greek in Egypt, by about 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","text","history","politics","technology","iron age","iran"],"abstract":"<b>Aramaic</b> is a Semitic language whose oldest long texts are the royal inscriptions of the small kingdoms of northern Syria, written in an alphabet borrowed from Phoenician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It spread far beyond them: while the eastern empires ruled it was the language of commerce and diplomacy, and it gave way to Greek only in Ptolemaic times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Assyria used it early, and twelve of the bronze lion-weights from Nineveh carry an Aramaic label beside the Assyrian one, under the names of Shalmaneser, Sargon and Sennacherib.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aramaic dockets were also written in ink along the edge of cuneiform tablets, and Assyrian and Babylonian sculpture shows scribes writing on scrolls, which cuneiform would not be put on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ashurbanipal kept Aramaean scribes, and a Persian satrap two centuries later sent his orders to an Egyptian boat-builder in the same language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Achaemenids it was the empire's written language: the Persepolis clay tablets are in Elamite, but the orders behind them are thought to have been put first into Aramaic, in ink on parchment rather than cuneiform on clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of those tablets carry an Aramaic gloss in ink, and others record hides delivered to treasuries as writing material, so how much of the administration was written on perishable stuff cannot be known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 203 of the 269 green chert mortars, pestles and plates dug out of the Persepolis Treasury are inscribed in ink in Aramaic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only in Egypt could papyrus survive, which is why the fullest surviving run of it is the archive of a Jewish garrison at Elephantine, in much the same Aramaic as parts of the book of Ezra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The alphabet outlived the language's official standing: the Jews took it over with the language after the Exile, and the square letters of the Hebrew Bible, the Nabataean hand and the Palmyrene all descend from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["G. A. Cooke, <i>A Text-book of North-Semitic Inscriptions: Moabite, Hebrew, Phoenician, Aramaic, Nabataean, Palmyrene, Jewish</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1903), xix, xxi, 159–92, 214–320, https://archive.org/stream/textbookofnorths00cookuoft/textbookofnorths00cookuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. Cowley, <i>Aramaic Papyri of the Fifth Century B.C.</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1923), xiii, xv, https://archive.org/details/aramaicpapyrioff00ahikuoft. [Open access]","George G. Cameron, <i>Persepolis Treasury Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 65 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1948), 20–21, https://archive.org/stream/oip65/oip65_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Richard T. Hallock, <i>Persepolis Fortification Tablets</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 92 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1969), 4–5, 15, https://isac.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/uploads/shared/docs/oip92.pdf. [Open access]","Raymond A. Bowman, <i>Aramaic Ritual Texts from Persepolis</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 91 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1970), 1, https://archive.org/stream/oip91/oip91_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif/1280px-Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif","title":"An Aramaic letter from Elephantine","desc":"A papyrus letter written in Aramaic by the Jewish community at Elephantine in Egypt, asking the governor of Judah for permission to rebuild their temple.","credit":"Letter by Yedoniah, photographer unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephantine_Temple_reconstruction_request.gif","alt":"A wide sheet of papyrus covered in eleven long lines of dark cursive Semitic writing, with a torn hole near the left edge."}},
{"id":"wh-310","num":310,"category":"Iron Age Near East and Persia","question":"Excavators at Persepolis found the Apadana's mud brick baked red and its floor burned black, physical traces of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 330 BCE.","questions":["Arrian, Diodorus and Curtius give the Persian dead at Gaugamela as 300,000, 90,000 and 40,000, which is how loosely the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is recorded.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended when Bessus, satrap of Bactria, killed Darius III on the road and put on the upright tiara as Artaxerxes himself."],"answer":"Fall of the Achaemenid Empire","answerText":"Fall of the Achaemenid Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gaugamela</span><span class=\"dt-v\">331 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persepolis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">sacked and burned, 330 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last king killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">330 BCE, by Bessus of Bactria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","iron age","iran"],"abstract":"The <b>fall of the Achaemenid Empire</b> took place between Alexander's invasion of Asia and the murder of Darius III in 330 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decisive battle was fought near Gaugamela in 331 BCE, in the Athenian month Pyanepsion and soon after a lunar eclipse, and Darius got away from the field towards Media.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it cost is not agreed: Arrian gives about 300,000 Persian dead against 100 Macedonians, Diodorus more than 90,000 against 500, and Curtius 40,000 against fewer than 300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Babylon did not resist, its priests and rulers coming out in a body to hand over the city, its citadel and its money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alexander ordered the temples Xerxes was said to have destroyed rebuilt, and left the Persian Mazaeus as satrap there with a Macedonian general and a separate revenue officer beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Persepolis the excavators found the looting itself recorded in the ground, in objects dropped in the portico of the Throne Hall as the contents of its rooms were carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Apadana then burned in a fire hot enough to bake its grey-green mud brick red and scorch the floor black to a centimetre's depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Treasury the stone vessels had been smashed against a wall and the royal tableware shattered among them, which the excavators read as vandalism rather than as any rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Darius fled towards Bactra with the Bactrian cavalry and the king's kinsmen, and was seized and killed on the road by Bessus, his own satrap of Bactria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bessus then put on the upright tiara, called himself Artaxerxes and claimed to be king of Asia, and Plutarch has him torn apart between two bent trees when he was caught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 17.5, 17.61, 17.64, 17.73, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=17:chapter=73. [Open access]","Arrian, <i>The Anabasis of Alexander; or, The History of the Wars and Conquests of Alexander the Great</i>, trans. E. J. Chinnock (London: Hodder and Stoughton, 1884), 170–71, 191–92, https://archive.org/stream/cu31924026460752/cu31924026460752_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Erich F. Schmidt, <i>Persepolis I: Structures, Reliefs, Inscriptions</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 68 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1953), 19, 131, https://archive.org/stream/oip68/oip68_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Raymond A. Bowman, <i>Aramaic Ritual Texts from Persepolis</i>, Oriental Institute Publications 91 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1970), 1, https://archive.org/stream/oip91/oip91_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives</i>: Alexander 43, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=43. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Battle_of_Issus_mosaic_-_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_-_Naples_2013-05-16_16-25-06_BW.jpg/1280px-Battle_of_Issus_mosaic_-_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_-_Naples_2013-05-16_16-25-06_BW.jpg","title":"Alexander and Darius III on the Alexander Mosaic","desc":"A Roman floor mosaic from the House of the Faun at Pompeii, thought to copy a Hellenistic painting, showing Alexander charging from the left and Darius III turning away in his chariot at the battle of Issus.","credit":"Alexander Mosaic, House of the Faun, Pompeii; Museo Archeologico Nazionale, Naples. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Issus_mosaic_-_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_-_Naples_2013-05-16_16-25-06_BW.jpg","alt":"A wide, damaged floor mosaic of a cavalry battle: a bare-headed rider with a levelled lance at the left, a thicket of upright spears across the centre, and at the right a crowned figure in a chariot reaching back as his driver whips the horses away."}},
{"id":"wh-311","num":311,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Asked what held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> together, Herodotus's Athenians answer kinship in blood and speech, shared shrines and sacrifices, and a common way of life.","questions":["The political unit of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the polis, which Aristotle describes as several villages grown together until they were self-sufficient enough to live well.","Rome closed the history of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when Lucius Mummius razed Corinth in 146 BCE and the rest of the country was subjected under different generals."],"answer":"Ancient Greece","answerText":"Ancient Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 146 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chaeronea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Corinth razed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE, by Rome</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["culture","era","history","politics","greece","iron age"],"abstract":"<b>Ancient Greece</b> was not one state but a world of separate cities on the mainland, the Aegean islands and the coast of Asia Minor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus has the Athenians name what held them together: the kinship of all Greeks in blood and speech, shared shrines and sacrifices, and a common way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The unit was the polis, which Aristotle describes as several villages grown together until they were self-sufficient, existing for the sake of not merely living but living well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A citizen was one who shared in judgement and office, so resident foreigners and the enslaved lived in the city without being of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land is broken and the sea is never far, and Thucydides has Athens settling Ionia and most of the islands while the Peloponnesians took most of Italy and Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Persia invaded twice and was thrown back both times, and Athens turned the alliance that followed into an empire and into a democracy that Pericles praised for favouring the many instead of the few.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The war between Athens and Sparta ran 27 years, and ended with the Long Walls and Piraeus taken and the Athenian empire finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Philip of Macedon broke the Athenian and Boeotian line at Chaeronea in 338 BCE, his young son Alexander commanding one wing and being first through the enemy front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Alexander crossed the Hellespont four years later with about 32,000 foot and 4,500 cavalry, and threw his spear into the ground to claim Asia as a prize won by the spear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome ended it: Lucius Mummius razed Corinth in 146 BCE, and the rest of the country as far as Macedonia was subjected under different generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=144. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.12, 2.37, 5.26, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=5:chapter=26. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1252b–1253a, 1275a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=1:section=1252b. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.1.3, 8.6.23, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=8:chapter=6:section=23. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 16.84, 16.86, 17.17, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=16:chapter=86. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Agrigento_-_Valle_dei_Templi_-_Temple_of_Concordia_-_1.jpg/1280px-Agrigento_-_Valle_dei_Templi_-_Temple_of_Concordia_-_1.jpg","title":"A Greek temple in Sicily","desc":"The Temple of Concordia at Agrigento, built by the Greek colonists of Akragas and among the most complete Doric temples standing anywhere, a reminder that the Greek world reached far beyond the Aegean.","credit":"Benjamin Smith, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrigento_-_Valle_dei_Templi_-_Temple_of_Concordia_-_1.jpg","alt":"A honey-coloured Doric temple standing almost intact on a ridge against a blue sky, its columns and both pediments in place, with a line of visitors walking past its steps."}},
{"id":"wh-312","num":312,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Pausanias doubted whether Panopeus could be called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, having no offices, gymnasium, theatre, agora or piped water, though it still sent delegates to the Phocian assembly.","questions":["Aristotle capped the size of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the largest population that can be taken in at one view, since citizens had to know each other to judge lawsuits.","Telling a beaten army it was itself a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wherever it sat down, Nicias said that men make the city and not walls or ships empty of men."],"answer":"polis","answerText":"polis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still described</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Pausanias, about 150 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","politics","history","greece","iron age"],"abstract":"A <b>polis</b> was a Greek state made of a town, the farmland around it and the citizens who belonged to both. Pausanias, coming to Panopeus in Phocis, doubted whether the word could be used at all of people with no government offices, no gymnasium, no theatre, no agora and no piped water, living in huts on the edge of a ravine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Yet Panopeus had borders with its neighbours and sent delegates to the Phocian assembly, so it passed the political test while failing the urban one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristotle lists what a state cannot do without: an official over the market, another over the buildings and roads, another over the country outside the walls, receivers of the public money and recorders of contracts and verdicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small poleis combined these offices and large ones split them, which is much of what the difference in scale amounted to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The size was limited by the same logic: citizens had to know one another's characters to elect officials and judge lawsuits, so Aristotle set the ceiling at the largest population that can be taken in at one view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Athens a boy was entered on his deme's roll at eighteen, his demesmen voting on oath that he was of age, free and legitimate, and a rejected claimant could be sold by the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then spent two years on garrison duty and patrol before joining the general body of citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the citizens were the state, Nicias could tell a beaten army that they were themselves a city wherever they sat down, since men make the city and not walls or ships empty of men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of Greece lived otherwise, the Aetolians in unwalled villages scattered far apart, and when Thales proposed one council chamber for all Ionia with the other cities counted as demes, the advice was remembered rather than taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1321b–1322a, 1326b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1321b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>The Athenian Constitution</i> 42, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=42. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.4.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=4:section=1. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.94, 7.77, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=77. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.170, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=170. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Priene_Bouleuterion_1999_040.jpg/1280px-Priene_Bouleuterion_1999_040.jpg","title":"A council chamber above its own farmland","desc":"The stone benches of the council chamber at Priene in Ionia, built about 200 BCE to hold some 500 men, with the ruined town behind it and the plain the city farmed running to the horizon.","credit":"Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Priene_Bouleuterion_1999_040.jpg","alt":"Tiers of weathered stone benches in the foreground stepping down to a small open floor, with a scatter of fallen columns and blocks beyond and a wide cultivated plain stretching to a hazy horizon."}},
{"id":"wh-313","num":313,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Herodotus shows <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at work when the Theraeans, after seven rainless years, drew lots to take one of every pair of brothers and sailed to Libya.","questions":["Thucydides records that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Sicily began when Archias of Corinth drove the Sicels off the island where the inner city of Syracuse stands.","Aristotle blamed land for the factions that broke cities founded by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Achaeans of Sybaris expelling the Troezenians and the Sybarites at Thurii thrown out in turn."],"answer":"Greek colonisation","answerText":"Greek colonisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First in Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Naxos, about 734 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Massalia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 600 BCE, from Phocaea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naucratis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">granted by Amasis, 6th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","practice","history","greece","archaic"],"abstract":"<b>Greek colonisation</b> is the modern name for the cities Greeks planted overseas from the 8th century BCE, from the western Mediterranean to the Black Sea. Herodotus tells how one began: after seven rainless years Delphi told the Theraeans to send a colony to Libya, and they drew lots to take one of every pair of brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They put Battus at their head as leader and king and sailed in two fifty-oared ships, the whole founding party of the city that became Cyrene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides dates the Sicilian foundations one against another and names the man in charge of each: Thucles at Naxos, Archias of Corinth at Syracuse, Evarchus at Catana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also says plainly what founding meant for the people already there, since Archias began by driving the Sicels off the island where the inner city stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A colony took its mother city's gods with it: the Phocaeans setting out for Massalia were told to fetch a guide from Artemis of Ephesus, and Aristarcha came with a plan of the temple and its statues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Massalia's own colonies kept the image's shape and every rite observed in the mother city, so the cults ran on down a second generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not every Greek settlement was a colony: at Naucratis in Egypt nine cities shared a precinct and supplied the overseers of a trading port that Amasis had given the Greeks to live in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Land was the usual quarrel, and Aristotle lists cities that split over it, the Achaeans of Sybaris expelling the Troezenians and the Sybarites at Thurii claiming the larger share and being thrown out in their turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The habit outlasted the movement: camped on the Black Sea with the remains of a Greek army, Xenophon thought it a fine thing to gain territory and power for Greece by founding a city there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.178, 4.150–153, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=4:chapter=153. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.3–4, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=3. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 4.1.4, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=4:chapter=1:section=4. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1303a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=5:section=1303a. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 5.6.15–17, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=5:chapter=6:section=15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Cyrene%2C_Libya.jpg/1280px-Cyrene%2C_Libya.jpg","title":"The Temple of Zeus at Cyrene","desc":"The Doric temple of Zeus at Cyrene in Libya, the city the Theraeans founded after Delphi told them to, standing on the North African coast in the same architecture Greeks built with at home.","credit":"Travcoa Travel, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrene,_Libya.jpg","alt":"A long ruined colonnade of thick Doric columns in warm sandstone, several still carrying their architrave blocks, seen past the branches of a pine on bare open ground."}},
{"id":"wh-314","num":314,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Herodotus thought <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Hesiod had taught the Greeks the descent of the gods, given them their names and determined their spheres and outward forms.","questions":["To size the Trojan expedition Thucydides counted the twelve hundred ships that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lists, while allowing for the exaggeration a poet would feel licensed to employ.","Plato has Glaucon meet people who call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the educator of Hellas and say a man should order his whole life by that one poet."],"answer":"Homer","answerText":"Homer","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not much before 700 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cited by Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">481 BCE, over command of the fleet</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","literature","history","greece","iron age"],"abstract":"<b>Homer</b> is the name Greek tradition gave to the <i>Iliad</i> and the <i>Odyssey</i>, and for history what matters is less who he was than what the two poems did. Herodotus thought Homer and Hesiod had taught the Greeks the descent of the gods, given the gods their names, and determined their spheres, functions and outward forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato has Glaucon meet people who call Homer the educator of Hellas and say a man should order his whole life by him, which is the claim Plato is arguing against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The geographers made him the founder of their science, Strabo reckoning that he had reached the limits of the earth in imagination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At a council before the Persian invasion the Athenians pressed their claim to command the fleet by citing his line that their Menestheus was the best of all at marshalling armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Historians used the poems as evidence and knew the risk: Thucydides counted the twelve hundred ships of the Catalogue, and their crews, to size the Trojan expedition against his own war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He allowed for the exaggeration a poet would feel licensed to employ, and warned against judging a city's power by the size of its ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle put the difference sharply: the poet's object is not to tell what actually happened but what could and would happen, either inevitably or according to probability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He praised Homer for building the Odyssey round a single action rather than everything that ever befell Odysseus, and said the Iliad was made the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the Iliad opens not on the war or the city but on one man's anger, and the first word of the Greek is that wrath.","quote":{"book":"homer-iliad","n":"1","cite":"Book 1","text":"<p>The wrath sing, goddess, of Peleus' son, Achilles, that destructive wrath which brought countless woes upon the Achaeans, and sent forth to Hades many valiant souls of heroes, and made them themselves spoil for dogs and every bird; thus the plan of Zeus came to fulfillment, from the time when first they parted in strife Atreus' son, king of men, and brilliant Achilles.</p>"},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.53, 7.161, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=53. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.9–10, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=10. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 606e, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0168:book=10:section=606e. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 1.1.2, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=1:chapter=1:section=2. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1451a, trans. W. H. Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1451a. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Homer%2C_Iliad%2C_Papyrus_Hawara_24-28.jpg/1280px-Homer%2C_Iliad%2C_Papyrus_Hawara_24-28.jpg","title":"The Catalogue of Ships on papyrus","desc":"A papyrus of the Iliad carrying part of the Catalogue of Ships, the passage Thucydides counted to judge the size of the Trojan expedition, written in Greek capitals with no spaces between the words.","credit":"Oxford, Bodleian Library, Papyrus Hawara 24–28. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homer,_Iliad,_Papyrus_Hawara_24-28.jpg","alt":"A torn sheet of papyrus covered in about eighteen lines of large Greek capital letters running together without word breaks, the upper corner lost."}},
{"id":"wh-315","num":315,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Herodotus says the Phoenicians who came with Cadmus brought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a Greece without letters, and that the Ionians called them Phoenician ever after.","questions":["Pliny gathered versions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that do not agree: sixteen letters from Cadmus, four more from Palamedes at Troy, four from Simonides, eighteen from Aristotle.","Tacitus says the Latin letters keep the shapes of the oldest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which reached Italy from Demaratus of Corinth and from Evander the Arcadian."],"answer":"Greek alphabet","answerText":"Greek alphabet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE, from Phoenician</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Claudius added 3</span><span class=\"dt-v\">41 – 54 CE, and they lapsed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","technology","history","greece","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>Greek alphabet</b> is the Phoenician letters adapted to write Greek, and its one great change was to give the vowels signs of their own. Herodotus says the Phoenicians who came with Cadmus brought letters to a Greece that had not had them, and that the Ionians who learned them called them Phoenician ever after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that he had himself seen Cadmean writing on tripods in the temple of Ismenian Apollo at Thebes, one reading that Amphitryon dedicated it from the spoils of the Teleboae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias records the counter-argument that Cadmus was Egyptian rather than Phoenician, and answers it from the Phoenician name of the Athena worshipped on the spot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pliny gathered versions that do not agree: Cadmus brought sixteen letters, Palamedes added four at Troy and Simonides four more, while Aristotle made the original eighteen and gave two of them to Epicharmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the letters were for, Aeschylus put in Prometheus's mouth: the combining of them is the mother of the Muses' arts, and what holds all things in memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tacitus traced the story further back and further on: Egypt first, then the Phoenicians, whose seamanship carried the letters to Greece and who took the credit for what they had been taught.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From Greece they reached Italy twice over, the Etruscans learning them from Demaratus of Corinth and the Aborigines from Evander the Arcadian, which is why the Latin letters have the shapes of the oldest Greek ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus notes in passing that the Ionians long called sheets of papyrus 'skins', because before papyrus they had written on the skins of sheep and goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Changing an alphabet proved harder than adding to it: Claudius put three new letters into Latin, they were used while he reigned and dropped afterwards, and could still be read on the bronze tablets in the squares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.58–59, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=58. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 11.14, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0078:book=11:chapter=14. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>Natural History</i> 7.57, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=7:chapter=57. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 9.12.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=9:chapter=12:section=2. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Prometheus Bound</i> 459–461, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0010:card=442. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Boustrophedon_inscriptions_Gortys.jpg/1280px-Boustrophedon_inscriptions_Gortys.jpg","title":"Greek letters cut at Gortyn","desc":"Part of the great inscription at Gortyn in Crete, written boustrophedon, so that alternate lines run in opposite directions and their letters face the other way with them.","credit":"Jebulon, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boustrophedon_inscriptions_Gortys.jpg","alt":"A long band of pale limestone blocks built into a wall, cut with lines of large archaic Greek capitals, the letters of alternate lines reversed as if seen in a mirror."}},
{"id":"wh-316","num":316,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Herodotus says the people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had once been the worst-governed of nearly all the Greeks, and that they changed to good government under the lawgiver Lycurgus.","questions":["Two kings reigned at once in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Thucydides says its constitution had gone unchanged for more than four hundred years by the end of the Peloponnesian War.","In his battle order at Plataea, Herodotus gives the 5,000 citizens of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a guard of 35,000 light-armed helots, seven appointed to each man."],"answer":"Sparta","answerText":"Sparta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Good government</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE, per Thucydides</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leuctra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">371 BCE, 400 Spartiates fell</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["city","history","greece","archaic","warfare","politics"],"abstract":"<b>Sparta</b> was the chief city of Laconia in the southern Peloponnese, and its people, who called themselves Lacedaemonians, were strong enough to arrange the affairs of other Greek states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says they had once been the worst-governed of nearly all the Greeks, and changed to good government under Lycurgus, whom the Delphic oracle greeted as more god than man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To Lycurgus he credits the sworn divisions, the bands of thirty and the common meals, along with the ephors and the council of elders, adding that the Spartans said it came from Crete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides gives that constitution more than four hundred years down to the end of the Peloponnesian War, and says the Lacedaemonians led the allied Greeks against Xerxes by virtue of superior power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two kings reigned at once, with privileges Herodotus lists: two priesthoods of Zeus, and the right to make war on whatever land they wished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The exiled king Demaratus told Xerxes that the Lacedaemonians were free and yet not wholly free, since law was their master, whom they feared far more than his men feared him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All of it rested on the helots, and Herodotus's battle order at Plataea measures the dependence: 5,000 Spartiates stood with 35,000 light-armed helots, seven to each man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides says fear of the helots governed Spartan policy at all times, and that two thousand who claimed to have served best in war were garlanded, led round the temples as free men, then made to disappear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Aristotle thought the state ruined itself through its landholding, estates falling into few hands and nearly two-fifths of the country passing to women, so that a land able to support thirty thousand hoplites had fewer than a thousand citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It could not endure a single blow, he wrote, but perished for want of men, and Xenophon bears him out at Leuctra: of some seven hundred Spartiates there, about four hundred lay among the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.65, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=65. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.56, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=56. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.104, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=104. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.28, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=28. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.18, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=18. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 4.80, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=4:chapter=80. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1270a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1270a. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 6.4.15, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0206:book=6:chapter=4:section=15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg/1280px-Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg","title":"Marble hoplite from the Spartan acropolis","desc":"A marble warrior of about 480 to 470 BCE, found in the sanctuary of Athena Chalkioikos on the acropolis of Sparta and traditionally called Leonidas, though nothing identifies him.","alt":"The head and shoulders of a marble statue of a warrior wearing a crested Corinthian helmet pushed back from a bearded face.","credit":"Mary Harrsch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-317","num":317,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Every inhabitant of Attica counted as a citizen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Thucydides says, because Theseus had abolished the council chambers and magistrates of the townships and merged them into one.","questions":["Thucydides counted 600 talents of tribute, 6,000 talents of coined silver and 300 galleys fit for service in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War.","A hostile writer says the sea power of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had gathered every delicacy of Sicily, Italy, Cyprus, Egypt, Lydia and Pontus into one place."],"answer":"Classical Athens","answerText":"Classical Athens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Classical era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 480 – 323 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War's outbreak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 triremes, 600 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plague dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,400 hoplites, 300 cavalry</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["city","history","greece","classical","politics","warfare"],"abstract":"<b>Classical Athens</b> was the city and state of Attica between the Persian Wars and the age of Alexander, and the greatest naval power in the Greek world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides traces its shape to Theseus, who abolished the council chambers and magistrates of the Attic townships and merged them into the single council-chamber and town-hall of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everyone in Attica thereby counted as a citizen of one state, and the Athenians still paid in Thucydides' day for the Synoikia, the feast of that union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Themistocles held the harbour at Piraeus more valuable than the upper city, and kept advising the Athenians that if hard pressed by land they should go down into it and defy the world with their fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Peloponnesian War began Thucydides counted 600 talents of yearly tribute, 6,000 talents of coined silver on the Acropolis, 13,000 hoplites, 1,200 cavalry, 1,600 archers and 300 galleys fit for service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That fleet made the port a market for the whole Mediterranean, and a hostile Athenian writer complains that every delicacy of Sicily, Italy, Cyprus, Egypt, Lydia and Pontus was gathered into one place by sea power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hearing every kind of dialect the Athenians took something from each, he says, so that where other Greeks kept their own speech and dress they used a mixture from Greeks and foreigners alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The plague of the war's second year fell on the people of Piraeus first, which is why it was said the Peloponnesians had poisoned the reservoirs, and reached the upper city only afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came back a second time, and Thucydides thought nothing reduced Athenian power more: 4,400 hoplites in the ranks and 300 cavalry died of it, besides a number of the multitude never ascertained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the funeral oration over the war's first dead he has the city called the school of Hellas, and alone of its contemporaries found on testing to be greater than its reputation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.15, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=15. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=93. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Pseudo-Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 2.7–8, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=2. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.48, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=48. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.87, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=87. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.41, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=41. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg","title":"Rowers of an Athenian trireme","desc":"A marble fragment of about 410 to 400 BCE, found on the acropolis of Athens in 1852, showing nine oarsmen seated above the ranked oars of a warship.","alt":"A broken slab of marble carved in low relief with a row of seated oarsmen above three banks of oars.","credit":"Marsyas, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-318","num":318,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Aristotle's Politics gives the marks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as offices filled by lot, no property qualification, short terms and judicial duties open to every citizen.","questions":["The Constitution of the Athenians says that under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the people had made itself master of everything, administering it by decrees and by jury courts in which the people ruled.","A hostile pamphleteer grants the case for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since it is the steersmen and shipwrights rather than the hoplites who give the city its strength."],"answer":"Athenian democracy","answerText":"Athenian democracy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 508 – 322 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly pay</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 obol, rising to 3</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fed by the state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 20,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","politics","greece","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Athenian democracy</b> was the constitution under which the adult male citizens of Athens governed themselves directly, filling most offices by lot and settling public business in the assembly and the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Constitution of the Athenians says the people had made itself master of everything, administering it by decrees and by jury courts in which it ruled, and judges them right, since a few are more easily corrupted than the many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's Politics lists its marks: election by lot to any office not needing skill, no property qualification, no office held twice, short tenure, and judicial duties exercised by all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behind them lies liberty understood as governing and being governed in turn, equality reckoned by number rather than worth, so that the poor prevail by being more numerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides has it defined in the funeral oration: its administration favours the many instead of the few, advancement falls to reputation for capacity, and poverty bars nobody able to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> A hostile Athenian pamphleteer grants the case rather than denying it: the poor are right to have more than the highborn, because it is they who man the ships, the steersmen and shipwrights giving the city its strength far more than the hoplites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He adds that the people claimed no allotted share in the generalships or the cavalry commands, seeing more to be gained by leaving those to the ablest men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was expensive: the tributes and taxes fed more than 20,000 men, among them 6,000 jurymen, 500 councillors, 1,600 archers and 1,200 cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pay was what let a poor man serve, and it was resisted before Agyrrhius carried an obol for the assembly; later it stood at a drachma a meeting and half a drachma for a day in the courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The same work counts eleven constitutions since Ion, among them the Four Hundred, the Thirty, and the democracy restored when the exiles returned from Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=41. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 24, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=24. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.37, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=37. [Open access]","Pseudo-Xenophon, <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.2–3, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 62, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=62. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg/1280px-Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg","title":"An Athenian allotment machine","desc":"A kleroterion in the museum of the ancient agora at Athens, its columns of narrow slots holding the bronze identity tickets of the citizens among whom offices and jury places were drawn by lot.","alt":"A slab of marble carved with about a dozen columns of narrow horizontal slots, standing on a plinth in a museum case.","credit":"Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleroterion,_AGMA,_225287.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-319","num":319,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Thucydides says the greatest achievement of past times, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, nevertheless found a speedy decision in two actions by sea and two by land.","questions":["Herodotus makes the land army of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 1,700,000 men, counted by penning 10,000 at a time inside a wall of stones built to a man's navel.","The Greeks who resisted in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made an end of all their feuds, the greatest being that between Athens and Aegina, and sent spies into Asia."],"answer":"Greco-Persian Wars","answerText":"Greco-Persian Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">499 – 479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xerxes' army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,700,000, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Greco-Persian Wars</b> were fought between the Achaemenid Empire and the Greek cities in the early 5th century BCE, from the revolt of the Ionians to the failure of Xerxes' invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides calls the Median war the greatest achievement of past times and notes that it nevertheless found a speedy decision in two actions by sea and two by land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus makes the land army 1,700,000 strong, counted by packing 10,000 men together, drawing a line round them and building a wall of stones on it to the height of a man's navel, then filling the pen again until all were numbered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greeks who cared for the common welfare met and exchanged guarantees, resolving to make an end of all their feuds, the greatest then running being the war between Athens and Aegina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They sent men into Asia to spy out the king's doings and envoys to Argos, to Gelon in Sicily, to Corcyra and to Crete, in the hope that all of Greek blood might work together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Athens gave up its claim to command at sea when the allies objected, judging that if they quarrelled over the leadership Hellas must perish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first invasion was turned back at Marathon, and the second was met at Thermopylae and then at Salamis. The Persians were beaten at Plataea and at Mykale on the same day, a rumour of the victory in Boeotia reaching the Greeks at Mykale as they advanced, which Herodotus took for a sign of the divine ordering of things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the spoils a tithe was set apart for Delphi and made into the tripod resting on a bronze three-headed serpent, with a bronze Zeus for Olympia and a Poseidon for the Isthmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Forced to declare an opinion he says will displease most people, Herodotus judges that the Athenians were the saviours of Hellas, since no wall across the Isthmus could avail while the king was master of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.23, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.60, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=60. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.145, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=145. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.3, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=3. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.100, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=100. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=81. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.139, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=139. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_serpent_head_from_Delphi_about_478_BC_then_Hippodrome_Istanbul_4103.jpg/1280px-Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_serpent_head_from_Delphi_about_478_BC_then_Hippodrome_Istanbul_4103.jpg","title":"A serpent's head from the Delphi tripod","desc":"One of the bronze serpent heads from the column that carried the tripod dedicated at Delphi out of a tithe of the spoils, now in the Istanbul Archaeology Museum.","alt":"A weathered bronze serpent's head with a scaled jaw and an empty eye socket, mounted on a stand in a museum case.","credit":"Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istanbul_Archaeology_Museum_serpent_head_from_Delphi_about_478_BC_then_Hippodrome_Istanbul_4103.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-320","num":320,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Herodotus says the Persians thought the Athenians mad at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, seeing how few they were and that they came on at a run without cavalry or archers.","questions":["Hippias son of Peisistratus chose the ground for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, it being the part of Attica best suited to horses and the nearest to Eretria.","About 6,400 of the invaders and 192 Athenians fell at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Epizelus lost his sight without ever being wounded."],"answer":"Battle of Marathon","answerText":"Battle of Marathon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,400 Persians, 192 Athenians</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Charge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 stades at a run</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Marathon</b> was fought on the coastal plain of that name in Attica, where an Athenian force with Plataean help destroyed the expedition Darius had sent against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herodotus says Darius dismissed Mardonius after his failure and gave the command to Datis, a Mede, and to his own nephew Artaphrenes, ordering them to enslave Athens and Eretria and bring the slaves before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretria fell first, and the Persians then sailed to Attica and landed at Marathon, which Hippias son of Peisistratus chose for them as the ground best suited to horses and the nearest to Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Athens sent the runner Philippides to Sparta, and he was there the day after leaving the city, but the Spartans would not march before the moon was full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ten generals were evenly divided, so Miltiades went to the polemarch Callimachus, whose vote counted with theirs by ancient custom, and told him it lay in his hands to enslave Athens or make her free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Athenians charged at a run across the eight stades, some 1.5 kilometres (0.9 miles), that lay between the armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Persians thought them mad, seeing how few they were and that they came on so fast with neither cavalry nor archers, and Herodotus calls them the first Greeks known to charge at a run and the first to endure the sight of Median dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Persians and Sacae broke the Athenian centre and pursued inland, but both wings won, let the beaten men flee, and turned in together on those who had come through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 6,400 of the invaders fell and 192 Athenians, and Epizelus lost his sight without a wound, telling ever after of a tall armed man whose beard overshadowed his shield passing him by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Two thousand Lacedaemonians reached Attica on the third day out from Sparta once the moon was full, went to Marathon to look at the Medes, and departed praising the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.94, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=94. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.102, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=102. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.106, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=106. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.109, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=109. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.112, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=112. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.113, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=113. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.117, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=117. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.120, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=120. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg/1280px-Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg","title":"A helmet dedicated at Olympia by Miltiades","desc":"A bronze helmet of Corinthian type dedicated in the sanctuary at Olympia, with the name Miltiades incised along the cheek-piece, traditionally taken for the helmet of the general who commanded at Marathon.","alt":"A corroded green bronze helmet with a long nose-guard and cheek-piece, Greek letters incised along its lower edge, on a museum stand.","credit":"Oren Rozen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-321","num":321,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Demaratus told Xerxes that the Spartans combing their hair before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were following their custom when about to risk their lives.","questions":["A Malian named Epialtes hoped for a great reward from the king when he betrayed the mountain path that decided <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the Greeks holding it.","The Immortals under Hydarnes fared no better than the Medes at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, their spears being shorter and their numbers useless in the narrow ground."],"answer":"Battle of Thermopylae","answerText":"Battle of Thermopylae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Leonidas brought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 Spartans</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 days of assaults</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Thermopylae</b> was the Greek attempt to hold the coastal pass into central Greece against the army of Xerxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A survey satisfied the Greeks that the invaders could use neither their whole army nor their horsemen there, so they marched to the pass and sent the fleet to Artemisium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leonidas commanded, bringing the three hundred Spartans allotted him and choosing men who had living sons, and taking Thebans with him on purpose because they were suspected of medizing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A scout found the Spartans combing their hair, and Demaratus told Xerxes that this was their custom when they were about to risk their lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The assaults failed for two days, the Immortals under Hydarnes faring no better than the Medes, since their spears were shorter and their numbers useless in the narrows, while the Spartans feigned flight and turned on their pursuers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Epialtes son of Eurydemus, a man of Malis who expected a great reward, then told the king of a path across the mountain,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the Phocians set to guard it heard the enemy in the fallen oak leaves, withdrew uphill under a shower of arrows to meet their end, and were passed by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Leonidas sent the allies away and stayed, Herodotus judging that he saw them unwilling and meant to leave a great name behind him, and reporting an oracle that Sparta must lose her city or her king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Only the Thespians and the Thebans remained with him, the first gladly, saying they would not abandon him, and the second because he held them as hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> They fought over his body until they had driven the enemy off four times, then drew back to a hill and defended themselves with swords, hands and teeth until they were buried under missiles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Xerxes had the head struck off and impaled, which Herodotus takes for proof of an anger past the common, the Persians being beyond all men in honouring brave warriors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.177, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=177. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.205, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=205. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.209, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=209. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.211, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=211. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.213, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=213. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.218, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=218. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.220, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=220. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.222, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=222. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.225, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=225. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.238, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=238. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Thermopylae_panorama_1.jpg/1280px-Thermopylae_panorama_1.jpg","title":"The mountain wall above the pass","desc":"Mount Kallidromon rising over Thermopylae. The sea once came in close under these cliffs, leaving only a narrow road; the level ground in front of them is silt laid down since.","alt":"A steep wooded mountain with grey crags rising behind a wide flat strip of pale open ground.","credit":"Davide Mauro, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thermopylae_panorama_1.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-322","num":322,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Themistocles sent his servant Sicinnus to tell the Persian generals that the Greeks meant to flee, which brought on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the narrows.","questions":["Aeschylus, who fought in it, has the Greek line at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> come on shouting to free their native land, their children, their wives and the tombs of their ancestors.","Watching <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from beneath Mount Aegaleos, Xerxes said that his men had become women and his women men when Artemisia rammed a ship."],"answer":"Battle of Salamis","answerText":"Battle of Salamis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">378 triremes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Salamis</b> was the sea fight in the straits between Attica and the island of Salamis, where the Greek fleet of 378 triremes destroyed the far larger Persian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Delphi had told the Athenians that everything within the sacred border of Cecrops would be taken, and yet that Zeus would grant the Trito-born a wall of wood, a stronghold for them and their children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the Peloponnesians outvoting him and set on falling back to the Isthmus, Themistocles slipped out of the council and sent his servant Sicinnus by boat to the Persian generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sicinnus told them the Greeks were terrified and meant to flee, and that the finest deed of all lay in not letting them escape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Finding it credible, the Persians landed men on the islet of Psyttalea and stopped the whole passage in silence through the night, so that the Greeks could not get out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Aeschylus, who fought in the battle, has the same tale brought by a Greek, and Xerxes ordering the exits barred on pain of every captain's head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His messenger reports the Greek line coming on with a shout: on, you men of Hellas, free your native land, your children, your wives, the temples of your fathers' gods and the tombs of your ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Crowded into the narrows the Persian ships could not help one another, smashed their own banks of oars, and rolled over until the sea was hidden by wrecks and slaughtered men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Xerxes watched from beneath Mount Aegaleos, asking after every feat who had done it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and when Artemisia rammed a friendly ship to get clear and it was reported to him as an enemy's, he said that his men had become women and his women men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He then feared the Greeks would sail to the Hellespont and break the bridges, leaving him trapped in Europe, and resolved on flight while making a show of preparing another battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.48, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=48. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.141, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=141. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.75, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=75. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.76, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=76. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.90, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=90. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.88, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=88. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 353–383, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=384. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 395–430, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=402. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.97, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=97. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/The_trireme_Olympias_on_23_February_2019.jpg/1280px-The_trireme_Olympias_on_23_February_2019.jpg","title":"An Athenian trireme rebuilt","desc":"The Olympias, a full-scale modern reconstruction of an Athenian trireme, showing the three banks of oar-ports and the ram at the bow. Ships of this kind made up both fleets in the straits.","alt":"A long wooden warship moored at a quay, its hull pierced by three rows of oar-ports, with a high curved stern and a projecting ram at the bow.","credit":"George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_trireme_Olympias_on_23_February_2019.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-323","num":323,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Thucydides says the tribute of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first fixed at 460 talents, received by officers called the Treasurers for Hellas.","questions":["The allies of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had themselves to blame, Thucydides says, for arranging to pay money rather than serve, which left them without ships when they revolted.","Naxos was besieged and made to return to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first allied city subjugated and a precedent that the rest of them followed."],"answer":"Delian League","answerText":"Delian League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 478 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">460 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["institution","history","greece","warfare","politics","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Delian League</b> was the alliance of Greek states formed after the Persian invasion under the leadership of Athens, with its common treasury on the island of Delos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says the violence of the Spartan commander had begun to be disagreeable to the Greeks, so the Ionians and the newly freed cities went to the Athenians and asked them as kinsmen to lead instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens fixed which cities were to contribute money and which ships, the professed object being to pay themselves back for their sufferings by ravaging the king's country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The office of Treasurers for Hellas was instituted to receive what was contributed, the tribute was first fixed at 460 talents, and the congresses were held in the temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The allies were independent at the start and acted on the resolutions of a common congress, and Thucydides explains that he tells the story at all because it accounts for the growth of the Athenian empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Athenians took Eion on the Strymon and enslaved Scyros, and when Naxos left the confederacy she was besieged and made to return, the first allied city subjugated and a precedent the rest followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Arrears of tribute and failure of service were the chief causes of defection, the Athenians being severe and exacting with men neither used to nor disposed for continuous labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For this the allies had themselves to blame, since most of them arranged to pay money rather than serve, so that Athens built up her navy on their contributions while a revolt found them without ships or experience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Speaking at Olympia, the Mytileneans said they had become allies of the Greeks for liberation from the Mede and not allies of Athens for the subjugation of the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Once the Athenians relaxed their hostility to the king and turned to subduing the allies, they said, every one was enslaved but themselves and the Chians, who still sent their contingents as nominally free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.95, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=95. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.97, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=97. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.99, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=99. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.10, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=10. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG/1280px-EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG","title":"The allies' tribute in stone","desc":"Fragments of the first of the stelai on which Athens recorded, year by year from 454 BCE, the sixtieth part of each ally's tribute set aside as an offering to Athena, the sums written in Greek acrophonic numerals.","alt":"Broken pieces of an inscribed marble slab set into plaster, covered with columns of Greek capitals and numerals.","credit":"Marsyas, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG"}},
{"id":"wh-324","num":324,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Thucydides writes that what was nominally a democracy became in the hands of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a government by the first citizen, led rather than leading.","questions":["The Constitution of the Athenians credits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with taking functions from the Areopagus, urging the state towards naval power and first making jury service a paid office.","Athens was told by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to carry in its property, guard the city instead of fighting, and make ready the fleet in which its real strength lay."],"answer":"Pericles","answerText":"Pericles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">429 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived on</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 years and 6 months into the war</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","history","greece","politics","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Pericles</b> led Athens through the middle of the 5th century BCE and into the war with Sparta, and Thucydides calls him the first man of his time there, ablest alike in counsel and in action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Constitution of the Athenians says he made the constitution more democratic still, taking away some of the functions of the Areopagus and urging the state strongly towards naval power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was the first to make jury service a paid office, on the advice of Damonides that he should give the multitude what was their own, his private means being no match for the public generosity of Cimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On his proposal citizenship was confined to those of citizen birth on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the outbreak of the war he told the Athenians to carry in their property from the country, not to go out and fight but to guard the city and make ready the fleet, in which their real strength lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Knowing that Archidamus, who led the invasion, was a friend of his and might spare his estates, he announced beforehand that if they were passed over he gave them up to be public property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the invasion and the plague turned the city against him he was fined, and not long afterwards elected general again, the people judging him the best man of all for the public necessities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had told them to wait quietly, look to their navy, attempt no new conquests and hazard nothing during the war, and Thucydides says that what they did was the very contrary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His rank, ability and known integrity let him lead the multitude instead of being led by them, so that seeing them insolently elated he could reduce them to alarm, and in a panic restore their confidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What was nominally a democracy became in his hands government by the first citizen, and he outlived the beginning of the war by two years and six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.139, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=139. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=26. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.65, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=65. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Testa_di_pericle%2C_copia_del_Ii_secolo_da_orig._di_kresilas_del_450-400_ac_ca.jpg/1280px-Testa_di_pericle%2C_copia_del_Ii_secolo_da_orig._di_kresilas_del_450-400_ac_ca.jpg","title":"A Roman copy of the Kresilas portrait","desc":"A Roman copy, in the Museo Barracco at Rome, of the portrait type ascribed to the sculptor Kresilas, the helmet pushed back on the head to mark a general. The ancient head stands on a modern bust.","alt":"A marble head of a bearded man wearing a helmet pushed back on his crown, mounted on a plain modern bust.","credit":"Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Testa_di_pericle%2C_copia_del_Ii_secolo_da_orig._di_kresilas_del_450-400_ac_ca.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-325","num":325,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Plutarch has the enemies of Pericles cry that Greece was insulted when her war contributions went on temples like <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead of the fighting.","questions":["Callicrates and Ictinus were the architects of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose cella ran a hundred feet, with Pheidias holding the general oversight of the work.","Pausanias found the front pediment of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> given to the birth of Athena and the rear to her contest with Poseidon for the land."],"answer":"Parthenon","answerText":"Parthenon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 447 – 432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold on it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 talents, all removable</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","greece","art","religion","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Parthenon</b> is the temple of Athena that stands on the acropolis of Athens, built in the years after the Persian sack and paid for out of the tribute of the allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the building programme was what Pericles' enemies attacked hardest, crying in the assembly that Greece was insulted when her enforced war contributions went on gilding and bedizening the city like a wanton woman with costly statues and temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He answered that the city owed no account of the money so long as it kept the enemy off, since the allies furnished not a horse, not a ship, not a hoplite, but money only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The materials were stone, bronze, ivory, gold, ebony and cypress, and the work called on carpenters, moulders, bronze-smiths, stone-cutters, dyers, goldsmiths, painters and embroiderers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pheidias had the general oversight of everything, while Callicrates and Ictinus were the architects of the temple, whose cella ran a hundred feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch marvels at the speed, since works that men thought would each take many generations were finished within a single administration, and calls them created in a short time for all time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias found the front pediment given to the birth of Athena and the rear to the contest between Athena and Poseidon for the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The statue inside was of ivory and gold, standing upright in a tunic to the feet with the Gorgon's head worked in ivory on the breast, a Victory some four cubits high in one hand and a spear in the other, a shield at the feet and a serpent beside the spear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides reckoned forty talents of pure gold on it, all of it removable, which the city might use to save itself and would have to restore afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch says the gold had been cast about the figure so that it could be taken off and weighed, and that when Pheidias was accused of embezzling it Pericles told the accusers to do exactly that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.+Per.+12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.+Per.+13. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 31, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.+Per.+31. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.5–7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=24. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Facade_and_North_Colonnade_of_the_Parthenon_on_the_Acropolis%2C_Athens.jpg/1280px-Facade_and_North_Colonnade_of_the_Parthenon_on_the_Acropolis%2C_Athens.jpg","title":"The colonnade in 1842","desc":"A daguerreotype of the west front and north colonnade, taken by Joseph-Philibert Girault de Prangey and among the earliest photographs of the building, made long before any modern restoration.","alt":"An early photograph of a ruined marble temple, its fluted Doric columns and entablature standing above a floor of fallen blocks.","credit":"Joseph-Philibert Girault de Prangey, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facade_and_North_Colonnade_of_the_Parthenon_on_the_Acropolis%2C_Athens.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-326","num":326,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Aristotle traces the two kinds of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to improvisation, one from the prelude to the dithyramb and the other from the prelude to the phallic songs.","questions":["Aeschylus raised the actors of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from one to two and curtailed the chorus, while Sophocles added a third actor and scene-painting.","The chorus-leaders for the tragedies of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were appointed by the chief archon from among the wealthiest men in the city."],"answer":"Greek theatre","answerText":"Greek theatre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Actors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1, then 2, then 3</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["practice","art","history","greece","religion","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Greek theatre</b> means the plays performed in competition at the Athenian festivals of Dionysus, where the chief archon appointed the chorus-leaders for the tragedies from among the wealthiest men in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristotle says both tragedy and comedy began in improvisation, the one out of the prelude to the dithyramb and the other out of the prelude to the phallic songs still kept up in many cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He credits Aeschylus with first raising the number of actors from one to two, curtailing the chorus and giving the dialogue the leading part, and Sophocles with the third actor and with scene-painting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Being a development of the satyr play, tragedy was late in rising from short plots and comic diction to its full dignity, and it took the iambic metre because that is the most conversational.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The beginnings of comedy are obscure, he says, because nobody took it seriously: a chorus was granted to a comic poet only late, and before that its performers were volunteers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch tells how, when Sophocles entered the contest with his first plays, the archon saw the partisanship running high among the spectators and would not draw the judges by lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He kept the generals in the theatre after their libation and made them swear and sit instead, ten in all, one from each tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sophocles won, and Aeschylus is said to have taken it so badly that he left before long for Sicily, where he died and was buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aristotle defines tragedy as a representation of an action heroic, complete and of a certain magnitude, in language enriched with ornament, acted rather than narrated, effecting through pity and fear a relief to those emotions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its six parts are plot, character, diction, thought, spectacle and song, and the most important is the arrangement of the incidents, since a tragedy represents not men but a piece of action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1449a, trans. W. Hamilton Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1449a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1449b, trans. W. Hamilton Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1449b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Poetics</i> 1450a, trans. W. Hamilton Fyfe, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0056:section=1450a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 56, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=56. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.+Cim.+8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/View_of_the_Great_Theatre_of_Epidaurus_on_23_May_2019.jpg/1280px-View_of_the_Great_Theatre_of_Epidaurus_on_23_May_2019.jpg","title":"The theatre at Epidaurus","desc":"One of the best preserved of the Greek theatres, its stone seating banked round a circular orchestra with the foundations of the stage building beyond; a single figure stands at the centre for scale.","alt":"A great fan of stone seating cut into a hillside above a circular earth floor, with low ruined foundations behind it and wooded hills beyond.","credit":"George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Great_Theatre_of_Epidaurus_on_23_May_2019.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-327","num":327,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Chaerephon asked the oracle at Delphi whether anyone was wiser than <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Plato has the Pythia answer that no one was.","questions":["The indictment Xenophon records against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> charged rejecting the gods the state acknowledged, bringing in strange deities, and corrupting the youth.","Invited to name his own penalty, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposed meals in the prytaneum, as a man who made the Athenians happy in reality rather than in appearance."],"answer":"Socrates","answerText":"Socrates","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">399 BCE, by hemlock</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","philosophy","history","greece","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Socrates</b> was an Athenian philosopher who wrote nothing himself, and Aristotle says he disregarded the physical universe and confined his study to moral questions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In Plato's <i>Apology</i> he tells the court that his friend Chaerephon once asked the oracle at Delphi whether anyone was wiser than Socrates, and that the Pythia answered no one was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unable to make sense of it, since he was conscious of no wisdom in himself, he went to a man of reputation to prove the god wrong, found him thinking himself wise when he was not, and concluded that he was wiser in one small thing only: what he did not know he did not think he knew either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The poets he questioned turned out to compose not by wisdom but by nature and inspiration, like prophets who say many fine things and know none of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The craftsmen really did know what he did not, but each of them, because he practised his art well, supposed himself wise in the greatest matters too, and that folly obscured the wisdom he had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xenophon gives the indictment in its own terms: that Socrates rejected the gods the state acknowledged, brought in strange deities, and corrupted the youth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He compared himself to a gadfly fastened on the city by the god, the city being a large and well-bred horse that is sluggish from its size and needs stinging to rouse it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Convicted and invited to name his own penalty, he proposed meals in the prytaneum, as a man who made the Athenians happy in reality rather than in appearance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plato's <i>Phaedo</i> has him drink the hemlock and grow cold from the feet upward, his last words being that they owed a cock to Asclepius and must not neglect the debt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He sought the universal within that moral sphere, Aristotle adds, and was the first to concentrate upon definition, which is where Plato took up from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Plato, <i>Apology</i> 21, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Apol.+21. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Apology</i> 22, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Apol.+22. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Apology</i> 30, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Apol.+30. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Apology</i> 36, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Apol.+36. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Memorabilia</i> 1.1.1, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.+Mem.+1.1.1. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedo</i> 118, trans. Harold North Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Phaedo+118. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 1.987b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.+Met.+1.987b. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Socrates_BM_GR1973.03-27.16.jpg/1280px-Socrates_BM_GR1973.03-27.16.jpg","title":"A Roman copy of the portrait type","desc":"A marble head in the British Museum, a Roman copy after a Greek original of the late classical period, with the snub nose and broad bearded face that the ancient portraits of him agree on.","alt":"A weathered marble head of a bald, broad-faced man with a snub nose and a long curled beard, mounted on a stand.","credit":"Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_BM_GR1973.03-27.16.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-328","num":328,"category":"Greece and the Hellenistic world","question":"Aristotle says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that definition cannot concern sensible things, and so assumed entities of another kind that he called Forms.","questions":["Men fettered in a cave, seeing only shadows thrown on the rock before them, are the picture <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> uses in the Republic for our condition.","Diogenes Laertius says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was about to compete with a tragedy when he heard Socrates outside the theatre, and burned his poems instead."],"answer":"Plato","answerText":"Plato","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 429 – 347 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 tragedy, on hearing Socrates</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","philosophy","history","greece","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Plato</b> was an Athenian who studied under Socrates and wrote almost everything in the form of a dialogue, with Socrates usually doing the talking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diogenes Laertius says he was about to compete for a prize with a tragedy when he stopped to hear Socrates in front of the theatre of Dionysus, and then burned his poems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He lived afterwards in the Academy, a gymnasium outside the walls of Athens standing in a grove named after the hero Hecademus, whose shady walks the comic poet Eupolis had already put into a play.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aristotle says he took up from Socrates but held that the problem of definition cannot concern sensible things, since these are always changing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He therefore assumed entities of another kind, which he called Forms, sensible things bearing their names and existing by participation in them, a word Aristotle says he simply put in place of the Pythagoreans' imitation without either school explaining what it meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the <i>Republic</i> he pictures men fettered in a cave from childhood, able to look only forward, with a fire burning behind them and a road with a low wall along it between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Along that wall others carry implements and figures of men, so that the prisoners, who have never turned their heads, see nothing but the shadows thrown on the rock in front of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same work holds that there can be no rest from troubles for cities until philosophers become kings, or those called kings take seriously to philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the <i>Phaedrus</i> he has Socrates tell how the Egyptian Theuth offered writing to the king, who answered that it would breed forgetfulness in those who learned it, being an elixir not of memory but of reminding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Written words, that dialogue adds, are like the creatures of painting: they seem to speak, but question them and they go on saying one and the same thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 1.987b, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.+Met.+1.987b. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 514, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Rep.+514. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 473, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Rep.+473. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Phaedrus</i> 275, trans. Harold N. Fowler, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.+Phaedrus+275. [Open access]","Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 3.1, trans. R. D. Hicks, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0258:book=3:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg/1920px-Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg","title":"A Roman copy of the Academy portrait","desc":"A marble head in the Capitoline Museums in Rome, a Roman copy of the portrait Silanion is said to have made for the Academy in Athens in the philosopher's own lifetime.","alt":"A marble head of a bearded man with deep-set eyes and thick curled hair, against a plain blue background.","credit":"Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-329","num":329,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Because man alone has speech and can declare the just from the unjust, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> concluded in the <i>Politics</i> that the city is natural and man is by nature a political animal.","questions":["Diogenes Laertius says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chose a public walk in the Lyceum and paced up and down it discussing philosophy, which is how his school came to be called Peripatetic.","Moral virtue comes not from nature but from habit, in the view of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who wrote that men become builders by building and that virtue lies in hitting the mean."],"answer":"Aristotle","answerText":"Aristotle","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">384 – 322 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">With Plato</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 years, from the age of 17</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lyceum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 years of lecturing</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","philosophy","science","history","greece","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Aristotle</b> was born at Stagira, the son of Nicomachus, a physician who had lived at the Macedonian court as friend and doctor to King Amyntas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to Athens at seventeen, stayed with Plato twenty years and was called his most genuine disciple, but left the Academy while Plato still lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He went afterwards to the court of Philip and took the young Alexander as his pupil, later asking Alexander to restore Stagira, which Philip had destroyed, and drawing up laws for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back in Athens he chose a public walk in the Lyceum and paced up and down it discussing philosophy with his pupils until it was time to rub themselves with oil, from which the school took the name Peripatetic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Metaphysics</i> opens by saying that all men naturally desire knowledge, the proof being the esteem in which the senses are held apart from any use, sight above all, since it reveals the most distinctions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His way into a subject was to break a composite whole into its elements and to watch things develop from the beginning, which is how the <i>Politics</i> approaches the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every partnership, that work argues, is formed with a view to some good, so the one containing all the others must aim at the highest good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From this comes the claim it is best known for, that man is by nature a political animal, and that a man citiless by nature is either below humanity or above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Speech is the reason: other animals have voice enough to signal pain and pleasure, but man alone declares the advantageous and the harmful, and so the just and the unjust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In ethics he held that moral virtue is not planted by nature but formed by habit, men becoming builders by building and harpers by playing the harp, and that it lies in hitting the mean, feeling and acting at the right time, towards the right people and in the right way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Diogenes Laertius, <i>Lives of Eminent Philosophers</i> 5.1–10, trans. R. D. Hicks, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0258:book=5:chapter=1. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i> 1.980a, trans. Hugh Tredennick, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.+Met.+1.980a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1.1252a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.+Pol.+1.1252a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 1.1253a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.+Pol.+1.1253a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i> 1103a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.+Nic.+Eth.+1103a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i> 1106b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.+Nic.+Eth.+1106b. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg/1280px-Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg","title":"A Roman copy of the portrait type","desc":"A marble head in the Palazzo Altemps in Rome, a Roman copy after a Greek bronze attributed to Lysippos. The banded alabaster drapery below the neck is a modern addition and no part of the ancient portrait.","alt":"A white marble head of a balding, bearded man with a short curled beard, set on a bust of banded orange stone drapery.","credit":"Jastrow, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-330","num":330,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Thucydides began writing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the moment it broke out, expecting it to be greater and more worthy of relation than any war that had gone before.","questions":["Sparta voted the treaty broken in 431 BCE, less persuaded by its allies than afraid of Athenian power, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed lasted 27 years and cost Athens its empire.","It was during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Athenian envoys told the Melians that right is only in question between equals, while the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must."],"answer":"Peloponnesian War","answerText":"Peloponnesian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">431 – 404 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 years in Thucydides' count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-v\">415 – 413 BCE, the expedition lost</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","warfare","history","politics","greece","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Peloponnesian War</b> was fought between Athens with its maritime empire and Sparta with the Peloponnesian League, and Thucydides counts it one war of 27 years, ending when Sparta took the Long Walls and Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He began writing at the moment it broke out, believing it would be great and more worthy of relation than any before it, and calls it the greatest movement yet known in history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Spartans voted the treaty broken not so much persuaded by their allies' arguments as afraid of the growth of Athenian power, seeing most of Hellas already subject to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fighting opened without a declaration when some three hundred Thebans entered Plataea by night in the fifteenth year of the thirty years' truce, let in by citizens who wanted their enemies destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Faction then spread from city to city, the popular leaders calling in the Athenians and the oligarchs the Spartans, since in war an ally was always at hand for either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Words had to change their ordinary meaning, Thucydides writes: reckless audacity was counted the courage of a loyal ally, prudent hesitation specious cowardice, and moderation a cloak for unmanliness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He warns that the interval of the Peace of Nicias only looks like peace, since neither side gave back what it had agreed and the fighting never wholly stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Melos the Athenian envoys refused to argue about justice at all, saying that right is only in question between equals in power, while the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The expedition against Syracuse ended with fleet and army destroyed and at least 7,000 prisoners penned in the quarries, a defeat Thucydides calls at once the most glorious to the victors and the most calamitous to the conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Athens held out until its fleet was gone, then accepted terms: pull down the Long Walls and those of Piraeus, give up all but twelve ships, take back its exiles and follow Sparta wherever it led.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.1, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.88, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=88. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.2, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=2. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.82, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=82. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.26, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=5:chapter=26. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 5.89, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=5:chapter=89. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 7.87, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=87. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 2.2, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0206:book=2:chapter=2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Syracuse_-_Latomia_del_Paradiso_-_2.jpg/1280px-Syracuse_-_Latomia_del_Paradiso_-_2.jpg","title":"A stone quarry at Syracuse","desc":"The Latomia del Paradiso, one of the ancient quarries cut into the rock at Syracuse. Thucydides says the captured Athenians were penned in the quarries there for some seventy days; which of them held the prisoners is not known.","alt":"A deep quarry with sheer pale limestone walls and dark undercut galleries at the base, with grass and scrub on the floor.","credit":"Benjamin Smith, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syracuse_-_Latomia_del_Paradiso_-_2.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-331","num":331,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Setting down his inquiry so that great deeds should not lose their glory, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened the <i>Histories</i> by asking what cause had set Greeks and barbarians warring on each other.","questions":["Because his business was to set down what he was told and not to believe it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> filled the <i>Histories</i> with reports he passes on and openly declines to vouch for.","The Egyptians reverse the customs of other men, wrote <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since their women buy and sell in the market while the men stay at home and weave."],"answer":"Herodotus","answerText":"Herodotus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 484 – 425 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Histories</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 books</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">book 2</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","historian","literature","history","greece","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Herodotus</b> calls his work the display of an inquiry, set down so that what men have done should not be effaced by time and the cause of the Greek and barbarian war not be lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He will speak of small and great cities alike, he says, since many once great are now small and many great in his day were small before, prosperity never continuing in one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His business is to set down what he is told, he tells the reader, and to believe it is none of his business, a rule to hold for the whole history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He marks where his own testimony stops: to a point in the second book all is his own sight, judgement and inquiry, and after it he records what the priests told him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rival accounts stand side by side without a verdict, the Persian story of the carrying off of Io beside the Phoenician, and he will not say which is true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Egyptian book runs longest of the nine, because he says that land has more wonders than any other and everywhere presents works beyond description.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its customs he reports as contrary to those of the rest of mankind: the women buy and sell in the market while the men stay at home and weave, and men carry burdens on their heads where women carry them on their shoulders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where he argues he argues closely, calling the third of three Greek explanations of the Nile flood the most plausible and the most erroneous of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That explanation had the river rising from melting snow, and he answers that the country it flows through has neither rain nor frost, and that cranes come there every year to winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The longest of his set pieces ends in Solon's answer to Croesus, that man is entirely chance, that no day of a life brings anything like another, and that nobody can be called fortunate before he has ended it well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.1, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.5, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=5. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.32, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=32. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.22, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=22. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.35, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=35. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.99, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=99. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.152, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=152. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/03/POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg","title":"The Histories on papyrus","desc":"Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, a fragment of Book 8 written out in Egypt in the early 2nd century CE, more than five hundred years after the work itself was composed.","alt":"A ragged brown papyrus fragment written in even lines of Greek capital letters, with holes and torn edges.","credit":"Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-332","num":332,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Diodorus says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned a kingdom that had been a slave to the Illyrians into the greatest dominion in Europe, and was the first to organise the Macedonian phalanx.","questions":["Demosthenes warned the Athenians that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marched unchecked because he brought skirmishers, cavalry, archers and mercenaries with him, and kept no season of the year for inaction.","The Athenian and Boeotian line broke at Chaeronea in 338 BCE, giving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the mastery of Greece two years before he was killed at a festival in his own kingdom."],"answer":"Philip II of Macedon","answerText":"Philip II of Macedon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">359 – 336 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">As king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 years, in Diodorus' count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chaeronea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","warfare","politics","greece","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Philip II of Macedon</b> was king for 24 years, taking over a kingdom Diodorus calls a slave to the Illyrians and leaving it the greatest of the dominions in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus says he steadied the Macedonians after a heavy defeat by calling them together in assemblies, drilling them constantly under arms and equipping them anew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He devised the compact order and equipment of the phalanx, in imitation of the close-order fighting with overlapping shields at Troy, and was the first to organise the Macedonian phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also bought and promised where he could not fight, withdrawing from Amphipolis and declaring it autonomous while the Athenians were trying to put a rival on his throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Amphipolis later gave him a pretext he breached its wall with battering rams and took it, and its position towards Thrace, Diodorus says, contributed greatly to his aggrandisement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Demosthenes told the Athenians that Philip marched unchecked not because he led a phalanx of heavy infantry but because he brought skirmishers, cavalry, archers and mercenaries with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Against a city at variance with itself he brought up his artillery and laid siege, and the orator adds that he made no difference between summer and winter and kept no season for inaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> At Chaeronea he stationed his son on one wing with the most seasoned generals beside him, and took command of the other at the head of picked men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fighting was long and many fell on both sides, and Diodorus has the king advance well in front and concede the credit for the victory not even to Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At a festival in his own kingdom a man nursing a private grievance waited until the attendants had gone ahead into the theatre, saw the king left alone, and stabbed him through the ribs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 16.1, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=16:chapter=1. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 16.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=16:chapter=3. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 16.8, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=16:chapter=8. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 16.86, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=16:chapter=86. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 16.94, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=16:chapter=94. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Philippic</i> 3.49, trans. J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0070:speech=9:section=49. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Philippic</i> 3.50, trans. J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0070:speech=9:section=50. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Macedonia_-_king_Philippos_II_-_355-348_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_laureate_head_of_Zeus_-_Philippos_II_on_horseback_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg/1280px-Macedonia_-_king_Philippos_II_-_355-348_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_laureate_head_of_Zeus_-_Philippos_II_on_horseback_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg","title":"A coin struck in his own name","desc":"The reverse of a silver tetradrachm minted at Amphipolis in about 355–348 BCE, showing a horseman with the king's name, ΦΙΛΙΠΠΟΥ, running round the edge.","alt":"A worn silver coin showing a rider on a walking horse in relief, with Greek letters around the rim.","credit":"ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_-_king_Philippos_II_-_355-348_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_laureate_head_of_Zeus_-_Philippos_II_on_horseback_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-333","num":333,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Plutarch has Philip tell <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after he had mastered the horse Bucephalas, to seek out a kingdom equal to himself, since Macedonia had not room for him.","questions":["Told that elephants and 200,000 foot waited beyond the Ganges, the Macedonians refused to cross, and it was there that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned back from India.","Asked what he was leaving for himself after giving the crown property away to his companions, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that he was leaving his hopes, and sailed for Asia."],"answer":"Alexander the Great","answerText":"Alexander the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">356 – 323 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">336 – 323 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Babylon, aged 32</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","person","history","warfare","politics","greece","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Alexander the Great</b> was the son of Philip II, and Plutarch has Philip tell the boy, after he had mastered the horse Bucephalas, to seek out a kingdom equal to himself since Macedonia had not room for him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philip sent for Aristotle to teach him, and repeopled Aristotle's native Stageira, which he had himself destroyed, restoring its citizens from exile and slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He called the <i>Iliad</i> a viaticum of the military art and kept a recension of it with his dagger under his pillow, sending home for books when he could find none in the interior of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Accounts of the force he crossed with differ: the lowest give 30,000 foot and 4,000 horse, the highest 43,000 and 5,000, with provisions reckoned at 70 talents by one writer and thirty days by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before sailing he gave nearly all the crown property away to his companions, and when Perdiccas asked what he was leaving for himself he answered that he was leaving his hopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In Parthia he first put on eastern dress, not the full Median fashion but something devised midway between Persian and Median, which Plutarch says the Macedonians found offensive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch offers two readings of it, that he wished to adapt himself to native custom, or that he was accustoming his own men by degrees to the obeisance he wanted from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army stopped him in the Punjab: worn down by the fight with Porus, and told that 200,000 foot, 80,000 horse and 6,000 elephants waited beyond the Ganges, they refused to cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He died at Babylon, and the court Journals as Plutarch reports them give the fever day by day, the dice with Medius, the sacrifices carried out, the talk with Nearchus about the great sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Diodorus tells it otherwise, with a great beaker of unmixed wine and a shriek as if struck, and has the dying king leave the kingdom to the strongest; both writers record that the accounts did not agree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 6, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=6. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=8. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=15. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 45, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=45. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 62, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=62. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 76, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=76. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Alexander</i> 77, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0243:chapter=77. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 17.117, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=17:chapter=117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Alexander_the_Great_mosaic.jpg/1280px-Alexander_the_Great_mosaic.jpg","title":"A Roman floor mosaic of the king","desc":"A detail of the Alexander Mosaic from the House of the Faun at Pompeii, laid about two centuries after his death and thought to copy a lost Hellenistic painting. He wears a cuirass with a Gorgon's head at the breast.","alt":"A mosaic showing a young man with tousled hair in an ornamented cuirass, riding beside a brown horse's head, with patches of the surface lost.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_the_Great_mosaic.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-334","num":334,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"The three centuries between Alexander's death and the Roman settlement of the east are called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Polybius says it was then that history first became an organic whole.","questions":["Antigonus took the diadem after his son's victory in Cyprus and Ptolemy, Seleucus, Lysimachus and Cassander followed, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to be an age of rival kings.","Warned to watch the clouds looming in the west, the Greeks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended by having a Roman envoy draw a circle round a king and order him to answer inside it."],"answer":"Hellenistic period","answerText":"Hellenistic period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">323 – 30 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings first</span><span class=\"dt-v\">306 BCE, Antigonus and Ptolemy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome steps in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">168 BCE, at Eleusis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","greece","politics","warfare","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Hellenistic period</b> opens with the death of Alexander the Great in 323 BCE, when his generals met at Babylon and divided the satrapies of the empire among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Perdiccas found in the dead king's memoranda orders for a thousand warships against Carthage, a road along the African coast to the Pillars of Heracles, six temples at 1,500 talents apiece, and the transplanting of populations between Asia and Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Judging the outlay unprecedented, and unwilling to seem to be detracting from Alexander's glory, he laid the plans before the assembly of the Macedonians, which voted not to carry them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The generals went on fighting as officers of a nominal kingdom until, after Demetrius' victory in Cyprus, Antigonus assumed the diadem and used the style of king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ptolemy did the same, and in rivalry with them Seleucus, Lysimachus and Cassander called themselves kings too, so that one empire had become a set of competing dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius, writing in the next century, dates from the 140th Olympiad the moment at which history became an organic whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before it, he says, the doings of the world were dispersed, held together by no unity of initiative, results or locality; after it the affairs of Italy and Africa were interlinked with those of Greece and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He gives Agelaus of Naupactus a speech at that same conference, urging the Greeks to compose their quarrels and King Philip to turn his eyes to the war in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> If they waited for the clouds looming there to settle on Greece, Agelaus warned, they might lose even the power of deciding their own differences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The warning was made good when a Roman envoy handed Antiochus IV the senate's decree, drew a circle round him with a stick cut from a vine, and told him not to step out of it until he had answered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 18.3, trans. Russel M. Geer, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1947), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/18A*.html#3. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 18.4, trans. Russel M. Geer, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1947), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/18A*.html#4. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 20.53, trans. Russel M. Geer, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1954), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/20C*.html#53. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html#3. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.104, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html#104. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 29.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/29*.html#27. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Gigantomachy_frieze_of_the_Pergamon_Altar_Athena-0001.jpg/1280px-Gigantomachy_frieze_of_the_Pergamon_Altar_Athena-0001.jpg","title":"The frieze of the great altar at Pergamon","desc":"Athena dragging the giant Alkyoneus by the hair, from the Gigantomachy frieze of the great altar built at Pergamon in the 2nd century BCE, one of the kingdoms that came out of Alexander's empire. Now in Berlin.","alt":"A deeply carved marble frieze of a winged goddess seizing a struggling giant by the hair, with a woman rising from the ground and a winged figure flying in.","credit":"MatthiasKabel, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gigantomachy_frieze_of_the_Pergamon_Altar_Athena-0001.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-335","num":335,"category":"Ancient Greece","question":"Strabo describes the Museum at Alexandria, its walk, its hall and its priest, and never once names the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the Ptolemies are famous for having built.","questions":["Aulus Gellius puts nearly 700,000 volumes in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and says they burned by accident, at the hands of auxiliary soldiers, during the first Roman war at Alexandria.","Aristotle was the first collector of books Strabo knew of, and it was he who suggested the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the kings of Egypt, though we are never told whether they took the advice."],"answer":"Library of Alexandria","answerText":"Library of Alexandria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ptolemies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Egypt, 305 – 30 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Books</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly 700,000, says Gellius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">48 BCE, the Alexandrian war</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["institution","history","literature","greece","egypt","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Library of Alexandria</b> was the collection of books gathered by the Ptolemaic kings of Egypt, and Strabo, describing the city, sets out the Museum it belonged to without naming a library at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Museum, he says, was part of the palace quarter, with a public walk, a place furnished with seats, and a great hall in which the men of learning took their common meal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They held property in common and had a priest set over them, appointed by the kings and in Strabo's own day by Caesar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere Strabo says Aristotle was the first man he knew of to make a collection of books, and that it was he who suggested to the kings of Egypt the forming of a library.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage has the Attalid kings hunting for books to stock their own library at Pergamum, which is why the owners of Aristotle's books at Scepsis hid them in a cellar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the collection held is stated only in round figures: Aulus Gellius gives nearly 700,000 volumes acquired or written in Egypt under the Ptolemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All of them were burned, he says, during the sack of the city in the first war with Alexandria, not intentionally or by anyone's order but accidentally, by the auxiliary soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ammianus Marcellinus repeats the figure of 700,000 and credits it to the unremitting energy of the Ptolemaic kings, but puts the libraries in the Serapeum rather than the palace quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch says only that Caesar, driven to use fire against the enemy fleet, saw it spread from the dockyards and destroy the great library.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The three do not agree about where the books stood or how they came to be lost, and none of them says how the count of 700,000 was arrived at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 17.1.8, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=17:chapter=1:section=8. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 13.1.54, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=13:chapter=1:section=54. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 7.17, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/7*.html#17. [Open access]","Ammianus Marcellinus, <i>Res Gestae</i> 22.16.13, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Ammian/22*.html#16. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Lives: Caesar</i> 49, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0244:chapter=49. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/AlexSarapeionPompeysPillar.jpg/1280px-AlexSarapeionPompeysPillar.jpg","title":"The Serapeum precinct at Alexandria","desc":"The site of the temple of Serapis, where Ammianus Marcellinus places the libraries. The column standing over the stripped foundations was raised in the Roman period, long after the Ptolemies, and the sphinxes were brought there from elsewhere.","alt":"A tall smooth granite column on a plinth, flanked by two stone sphinxes, standing over bare pale foundations under a clear blue sky.","credit":"Roland Unger, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlexSarapeionPompeysPillar.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-336","num":336,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy set out to trace the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back above 700 years, from slender beginnings to a greatness he says is now burdened by its own magnitude.","questions":["Polybius asks by what means and under what system of polity <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> subjected nearly the whole inhabited world to its sole government in less than 53 years.","Kings first, then Brutus and the consulship, then dictators, decemvirs, Sulla and Caesar: Tacitus runs through the constitutional history of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a single opening paragraph."],"answer":"Ancient Rome","answerText":"Ancient Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 BCE – 476 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 – 27 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Principate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 27 BCE, under Augustus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["culture","era","history","politics","rome","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Ancient Rome</b> began as a city on the Tiber and grew into an empire around the Mediterranean, and Livy, setting out to write its history, says the story must be traced back above 700 years, from slender beginnings to a greatness now burdened by its own magnitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He tells the reader to attend above all to what life and morals were like, and through what men and by what policies the empire was established and enlarged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also claims that no state was ever greater or richer in good examples, and none where avarice and luxury came so late or thrift was so long held in honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius puts the question from outside, asking by what means and under what system of polity the Romans in less than 53 years subjected nearly the whole inhabited world to their sole government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Virgil gives the answer Rome liked to give: let others mould bronze better and plead better at the bar, for Rome's own arts are to rule mankind, to dispose peace and war, and to tame the proud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Tacitus compresses the constitutional history into a paragraph: Rome at the beginning was ruled by kings, and freedom and the consulship were established by Lucius Brutus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dictatorships were held for a temporary crisis, the power of the decemvirs did not last beyond two years, and the despotisms of Cinna and Sulla were brief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pompey and Crassus soon yielded before Caesar, and Lepidus and Antony before Augustus, who, when the world was wearied by civil strife, subjected it to empire under the title of Prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Augustus put on record his hope to be called the author of the best possible government, and could boast, Suetonius says, that he had found the city built of brick and left it in marble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tacitus adds what the new order cost the record: the histories of the emperors were falsified through terror while they lived, and written under a recent hatred once they were dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i>, preface, trans. B. O. Foster, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Liv.+1.pr.1. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html#1. [Open access]","Virgil, <i>Aeneid</i> 6.851–853, trans. John Dryden, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Verg.+A.+6.851. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 1.1, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0078:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Augustus</i> 28, in <i>The Lives of the Caesars</i>, trans. J. C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Roman_Forum_east_end_panorama.jpg/1920px-Roman_Forum_east_end_panorama.jpg","title":"The Forum at Rome","desc":"The civic centre of the city Livy and Tacitus wrote about, now a field of foundations, standing columns and arches below the modern city.","alt":"Ruins of a Roman forum seen from above: rows of standing columns, brick foundations, a triple arch and grassy ground, with a domed church and modern buildings behind.","credit":"Nhartmannphotos, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Forum_east_end_panorama.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-337","num":337,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Herodotus derives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a Lydian famine, but Dionysius answers that they share neither language nor gods nor institutions with the Lydians.","questions":["Lictors, an ivory stool and a purple-bordered toga came to Rome from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Diodorus says, along with the war trumpet and the peristyle house.","Twelve cantons voting together for a national priest is how Livy shows the politics of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, offended when Veii tired of yearly elections and chose a king."],"answer":"Etruscan civilisation","answerText":"Etruscan civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cantons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">twelve, by Livy's count</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Veii taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["culture","history","politics","italy","rome","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Etruscan civilisation</b> was that of the peoples of Tyrrhenian central Italy, whose origins the Greeks and Romans argued over without ever agreeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus explains them by a famine in Lydia, endured for eighteen years by playing games on alternate days so as not to think of food: at last the king divided his people by lot, and the half led away by his son Tyrrhenus sailed from Smyrna and settled among the Umbrians, taking the leader's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus rejects that outright, since the Tyrrhenians share neither language nor gods nor institutions with the Lydians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those nearest the truth, he thinks, say the nation migrated from nowhere but was native to the country, agreeing with no other people in speech or manner of living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The familiar names are all outsiders': the Greeks said Tyrrhenians, the Romans Etruscans from the country and Tusci from their skill in the ceremonies of worship, while their own name for themselves was Rasenna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Diodorus says they were masters of the sea long enough for the sea along Italy to be named after them, and credits them with the war trumpet and with the dignity surrounding rulers: lictors, an ivory stool and a purple-bordered toga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Romans adopted most of it, he says, and transferred it to their own political institutions, taking the peristyle house along with the insignia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They elaborated divination by thunder and lightning more than all other men, he adds, and were still employed as interpreters of those omens in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy shows their politics from the Roman side: twelve cantons voting together for a national priest, and taking offence when Veii tired of annual elections and chose a king, a man already disliked for his pride of wealth who had broken off the Games in revenge for losing that priesthood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How easily the names slipped is Dionysius' warning: Latins, Umbrians and Ausonians were all once called Tyrrhenians, and many historians took Rome itself for a Tyrrhenian city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.94, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=94. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.29, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1B*.html#29. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.30, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1B*.html#30. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 5.40, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/5B*.html#40. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0026:book=5:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Piacenza_Bronzeleber.jpg/1280px-Piacenza_Bronzeleber.jpg","title":"A bronze liver, marked out for reading","desc":"A model of a sheep's liver found near Piacenza, its surface divided into regions and inscribed with the names of gods. Diodorus says the Etruscans elaborated divination more than all other men, and that Romans still employed them to read the omens in his own day.","alt":"A dark green bronze object shaped like a liver, its upper surface divided into small compartments each carrying an inscription.","credit":"Lokilech, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piacenza_Bronzeleber.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-338","num":338,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Dionysius found the Greek historians naming quite different founders for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from a son of Aeneas to a son of Odysseus and Circe.","questions":["Six vultures to one brother and twelve to the other decided which of them would carry out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though some said the twelve were a lie.","Plutarch says the version of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the widest credence was first published among the Greeks by Diocles of Peparethus, whom Fabius Pictor followed."],"answer":"founding of Rome","answerText":"founding of Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vultures</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 to Remus, 12 to Romulus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Two foundings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 generations apart, in one account</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome","italy","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>founding of Rome</b> is the event the Romans placed at the head of their own history, and their writers could not agree on who had done it or when.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dionysius of Halicarnassus set the Greek accounts side by side: Cephalon of Gergis made the founder Romus, a son of Aeneas, in the second generation after the Trojan war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One writer had Aeneas reach Italy with Odysseus and name the city after a Trojan woman, Rome, who had burned the ships to end the wandering, and Aristotle told the same trick of storm-driven Achaeans whose captive women fired the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Callias married that same Rome to Latinus and gave her three sons, Romus, Romulus and Telegonus, while Xenagoras made Romus a son of Odysseus and Circe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius then observes that the Romans have not one ancient historian or chronicler of their own, and reports accounts giving two foundings of Rome fifteen generations apart, with a third older still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The version that prevailed, Plutarch says, had the widest credence and the greatest number of vouchers, and was first published among the Greeks by Diocles of Peparethus, whom Fabius Pictor follows on most points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even in that version there are variations, and its outline runs from the kings of Alba through Amulius, who took the kingdom from his brother and made his brother's daughter a priestess of Vesta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the brothers came to build, they quarrelled over the site and agreed to settle it by the flight of birds, six vultures appearing to Remus and twice that number to Romulus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some said, however, that Remus truly saw his six while Romulus lied about his twelve, and only saw them when Remus had come over to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Remus mocked the trench where the wall was to run and leaped across it, and was struck dead: by Romulus himself, according to some, and by his companion Celer according to others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.72, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html#72. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 1.73, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/1D*.html#73. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 3, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html#3. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 9, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html#9. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Romulus</i> 10, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html#10. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Sex._Pompeius%2C_AR_denarius%2C_137_BC%2C_RRC_235-1c.jpg/1280px-Sex._Pompeius%2C_AR_denarius%2C_137_BC%2C_RRC_235-1c.jpg","title":"The story on a coin of 137 BCE","desc":"A silver denarius struck at Rome by Sextus Pompeius Fostlus: the helmeted head of Roma on one side, and on the other the she-wolf suckling the twins, with the shepherd Faustulus looking on and birds in the fig tree above.","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin: a helmeted head in profile, and a wolf standing over two infants with a standing figure and a tree behind.","credit":"Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sex._Pompeius%2C_AR_denarius%2C_137_BC%2C_RRC_235-1c.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-339","num":339,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Florus calls the age of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the infancy of the Roman people, and notes how conveniently each of its seven kings suited the moment he was needed in.","questions":["An interrex called the vote that made Numa the second king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Numa refused the insignia until the augurs had watched for birds.","The fasces, the robes of state and the chairs of office reached the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Etruria, Florus says, brought over by Tarquinius Priscus."],"answer":"Roman Kingdom","answerText":"Roman Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditionally</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 – 509 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7, from Romulus to Superbus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Numa's calendar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 months</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","politics","rome","italy","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman Kingdom</b> is the age of seven kings that Roman tradition set between the founding of the city and the Republic, and which Florus calls the first age and the infancy of the Roman people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He says Romulus made the fathers, called Patres from their authority and the senate from their age, and was then carried out of human sight while holding an assembly by a lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some thought the senate had torn him to pieces for his harshness, but a storm and an eclipse made it look like a deification, and Julius Proculus reported that he had appeared to him as Quirinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The throne was filled by choice rather than descent: an interrex called for a vote and the citizens all chose Numa Pompilius, invited from Cures among the Sabines for his reputation for piety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Numa would not take the royal insignia until his authority had been ratified by heaven, and stood veiled on the Capitol while the chief augur watched for birds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Florus credits him with the pontiffs, the augurs, the Salii and the other priesthoods, with dividing the year into twelve months, and with handing the hearth of Vesta to her virgins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tullus Hostilius is remembered as the founder of military discipline and Ancus Marcius as the builder, who walled the city and bridged the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tarquinius Priscus enlarged the senate and, after the augur Attius Naevius proved his art by cutting a whetstone with a razor, brought over from Etruria the fasces, the robes of state and the chairs of office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Servius Tullius, whose mother had been a slave, entered the people on a census roll and arranged them in classes, so that a great state was run with the exactitude of a small household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Florus himself notes how conveniently the seven suited their moments, boldness in Romulus and piety in Numa, and adds that even the last king's tyranny served, since it drove the people to liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Florus, <i>Epitome of Roman History</i> 1.1, trans. E. S. Forster, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1929), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Florus/Epitome/1A*.html#1. [Open access]","Florus, <i>Epitome of Roman History</i> 1.2, trans. E. S. Forster, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1929), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Florus/Epitome/1A*.html#2. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Numa</i> 7, in <i>The Parallel Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Numa*.html#7. [Open access]","Florus, <i>Epitome of Roman History</i> 1.5, trans. E. S. Forster, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1929), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Florus/Epitome/1A*.html#5. [Open access]","Florus, <i>Epitome of Roman History</i> 1.8, trans. E. S. Forster, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1929), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Florus/Epitome/1A*.html#8. [Open access]","Florus, <i>Epitome of Roman History</i> 1.6, trans. E. S. Forster, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1929), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Florus/Epitome/1A*.html#6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg/1280px-Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg","title":"Lictors and their rods","desc":"A relief from Iulia Concordia showing three lictors carrying the fasces over their shoulders. Florus says the rods and the other insignia of office reached Rome from Etruria under Tarquinius Priscus; the carving itself is Roman, from long after the kings.","alt":"A stone relief of three young men in tunics walking in file, each carrying a bundle of rods over one shoulder.","credit":"Following Hadrian, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_from_Iulia_Concordia_depicting_three_lictors%2C_National_Museum_of_Concordia%2C_Portogruaro%2C_Italy.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-340","num":340,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The senate held the treasury and the consuls the armies, but in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only the people could confer honours and inflict punishment, which Polybius calls society's only bonds.","questions":["A single tribune interposing could stop the senate deciding anything at all, which is how Polybius explains the balance holding the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> together.","Freed from danger abroad and grown rich on its own success, Polybius warned, a state such as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> decays through extravagance and rivalry for office."],"answer":"Roman Republic","answerText":"Roman Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 – 27 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 — consuls, senate, people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribune's veto</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 was enough</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["institution","era","history","politics","rome","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman Republic</b> was the government between the expulsion of the kings and the rule of the emperors, and Polybius, having examined it, judged it impossible to find a better political system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The senate held the treasury, he says: no quaestor could disburse for any object without its decree, and even the censors' outlay on public works every five years came as a grant from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also took the grave crimes across the peninsula needing public investigation, treason, conspiracy, poisoning and assassination, and any community's claim for arbitration or protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The consuls meanwhile had uncontrolled authority over armaments and operations in the field, so that little seemed left for anybody else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What was left to the people was the right to confer honours and inflict punishment, which Polybius calls the only bonds holding kingdoms, states and human society together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> None of the three could act alone: the senate could not punish the gravest offences unless the people confirmed its decree, and a single tribune interposing stopped it deciding anything at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The people in turn were bound to the senate by the censors' contracts for public buildings, navigable rivers, harbours, mines and lands, in which almost everyone had an interest and over which the senate was supreme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because each part could hamper the others or work with them, their union was adequate to every emergency, and against a common danger nothing needful was neglected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Prosperity was the danger: freed from menace abroad, Polybius says, men are corrupted by flattery and idleness, and the only check is that no part can outgrow the others without being thwarted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He expected decay all the same, since long supremacy makes life extravagant and men fiercer in their rivalry for office, and thought a reader who followed his argument could foretell it unaided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#13. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.14, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#14. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.16, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#16. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#17. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.18, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#18. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.57, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#57. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Curia_Iulia_front.jpg/1280px-Curia_Iulia_front.jpg","title":"The senate house in the Roman Forum","desc":"The Curia Julia, the meeting hall of the senate in the Roman Forum, begun under Julius Caesar and rebuilt by Diocletian after a fire; its plain brick front and bronze doors mark the chamber Polybius treated as one of the three parts of the constitution.","alt":"A tall plain brick building with a low pediment, three high windows above a set of bronze doors, reached by a short flight of steps.","credit":"Jensens, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curia_Iulia_front.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-341","num":341,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A senator called on for his opinion in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could first speak on any other subject he liked, for as long as he liked, which is how Cato talked out a day.","questions":["Men who had held office but were not yet on the censors' roll could vote in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, yet their names came last and nobody ever asked them their opinion.","A resolution of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was void if made before sunrise or after sunset, and a tribune interposing could stop one outright, leaving it recorded merely as an auctoritas."],"answer":"Roman Senate","answerText":"Roman Senate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 – 27 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Meeting houses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hostilia, Julia and 3 more</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Decrees as law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 27 BCE, under Augustus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","rome","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman Senate</b> was the standing council of the Roman state, and its resolutions reached into religion, administration, provincial matters and all foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A man who had held office did not become a senator until the censors entered him on the roll, though he might still attend and vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such men were the <i>pedarii</i>, a name Gellius explains either from voting by stepping across to the man they agreed with, or from walking to the House while those of curule rank rode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The distinction survived in the summons itself, which the consuls issued in the traditional words 'senators and those who have the right to express their opinion in the senate'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The House met in a <i>curia</i>, a building credited with a religious character and built with a cornice half way up each wall so the voice was not lost in the height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A sitting began when a magistrate convoked the House and put his question, and nothing resolved before sunrise or after sunset was valid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Opinions were then asked in an order that had varied before a law fixed it: the man the censors had enrolled first, the consuls-elect, or whoever a consul chose to compliment, though never anyone below consular rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sallust shows the practice in the debate over Catiline's captured conspirators, where Silanus, as consul-elect, was called first and recommended death,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> although the decree that passed followed Cato's proposal instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A senator asked his opinion could first speak on any other subject, for as long as he liked, which is how Cato talked out a day against Caesar, who had him arrested until the House followed him to prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decree was named for the consul who consulted the House, which was said to resolve rather than command; once carried it was written on tablets and laid up in the treasury, and a single tribune interposing could stop one, what had been agreed being recorded as an <i>auctoritas</i> instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 3.18, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/3*.html#18. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Curia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 377, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Curia.html. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 4.10, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/4*.html#10. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The War with Catiline</i> 50, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Sallust/Bellum_Catilinae*.html#50. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The War with Catiline</i> 53, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Sallust/Bellum_Catilinae*.html#53. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Senatusconsultum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1022-28, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Senatusconsultum.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Inside_Restored_Senate_House%28Curia%29_FORUM_11Mar2006.JPG/1280px-Inside_Restored_Senate_House%28Curia%29_FORUM_11Mar2006.JPG","title":"Inside the senate house","desc":"The chamber of the Curia Julia in the Roman Forum, with its coloured marble floor and the low steps running along either side on which the senators' benches stood.","alt":"A tall brick-walled hall with a patterned marble floor, low steps along its sides, a headless porphyry statue against the far wall and a carved marble panel standing at the right.","credit":"Dneale52, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inside_Restored_Senate_House%28Curia%29_FORUM_11Mar2006.JPG"}},
{"id":"wh-342","num":342,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A board of ten with every other magistracy suspended drew up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ten tables in 451 BCE and two more the next year, engraved on metal in the comitium.","questions":["Gellius says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> punished theft neither as harshly as Draco, who made every theft capital, nor as lightly as Solon, who fined a thief twice the value.","Livy called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the source of all public and private law, even in an age when statutes were piled one upon another in a confused heap."],"answer":"Twelve Tables","answerText":"Twelve Tables","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decemvirs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">451 – 449 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tables</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10, then 2 more</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent to Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 commissioners</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["text","history","law","politics","rome","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Twelve Tables</b> were Rome's first written code of law, drawn up by a board of ten men who took office at the beginning of 451 BCE with every other magistracy suspended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A tribune had proposed a written body of law as early as 462 BCE, and the patricians resisted for nine years before agreeing to send three commissioners abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The tribunes had asked for legislators drawn equally from both orders, and the patricians answered that they agreed about the laws and differed only over who should make them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three returned with the laws of Athens, and the board was created, from whose decisions there was no appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ten tables were then exhibited, the people told to turn each item over, discuss it and bring forward whatever seemed superfluous or defective, before the assembly of centuries passed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two further tables were added by a second board the next year, making the twelve, and the whole was engraved on tables of metal and set up in the comitium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What they contained is known mostly from later quotation, and Gellius reports that they punished theft neither as harshly as Draco, who made every theft capital, nor as lightly as Solon, who fined twice the value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A thief caught in the act could be killed only if he stole by night or drew a weapon by day; otherwise a free man was scourged and handed over to the man he had robbed, a slave scourged and thrown from the rock, and a boy flogged at the praetor's discretion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even that had been softened by Gellius's own day, when a suit for manifest theft claimed four times the value instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy, writing four centuries later, called the tables the source of all public and private law even in an age when statutes were piled one upon another in a confused heap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.31, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D31. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.37, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D37. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 11.18, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/11*.html#18. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Decemviri,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 388-89, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Decemviri.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Xii_leges.jpg/1280px-Xii_leges.jpg","title":"Imagined in a law book of 1555","desc":"A woodcut from a law book printed in 1555, lettered ‘Leges XII. Tab.’, showing a bench of robed figures with tablets and books; no copy of the tables themselves survives, so every picture of them is a later imagining.","alt":"A finely hatched woodcut strip of about a dozen robed and bearded figures seated side by side on a curved bench, several holding open tablets or books, with a scroll beneath lettered LEGES XII. TAB.","credit":"Sebastian Sapia, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xii_leges.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-343","num":343,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy makes debt the spark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with an old man showing the Forum the scars of his campaigns and then his back scored by his creditor's lash.","questions":["The plebs walked out to the Sacred Mount during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Menenius Agrippa was sent to tell them the fable of the limbs that conspired to starve the belly.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in 286 BCE when the dictator Quintus Hortensius reconciled the orders and gave the resolutions of the plebs the force of law binding the whole people."],"answer":"Conflict of the Orders","answerText":"Conflict of the Orders","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First secession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">367 BCE, first held in 366</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last secession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">286 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","law","rome","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Conflict of the Orders</b> was the long struggle by which Rome's plebeians won a share in the offices and priesthoods the patricians kept to themselves, having been shut out of the assemblies, the senate and every office of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Livy makes debt the spark, with men complaining that while they fought abroad for liberty and empire they were oppressed at home, and safer among the enemy than among their own people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An old man came into the Forum in rags, showed the scars of his campaigns and then his back scored by the lash, and told how war-tax and usury had taken his farm and at last his person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 494 BCE the plebs walked out of the city to the Sacred Mount across the Anio, entrenched a camp and waited there, giving and taking no provocation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The senate sent Menenius Agrippa, an eloquent man of plebeian birth, who told them the fable of the limbs that conspired to starve the belly and starved themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The agreement that ended the secession gave the plebs magistrates of its own, inviolable in their persons, able to protect a man against the consuls, and closed to any patrician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decemvirs had forbidden marriage between the orders, and Canuleius asked what greater disgrace there could be than for part of a community to be held unworthy of intermarriage, an exile within the same walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The quaestorship opened in 421 BCE and with it the way into the senate, and in 367, re-elected for the tenth time, Sextius and Licinius carried the consulship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The patricians gave way in exchange for a praetor, and the rest followed: the dictatorship in 356 BCE, the censorship in 351, the praetorship in 336, and the pontificate and augurate under the Ogulnian law of 300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A last secession in 286 BCE ended when the dictator Quintus Hortensius reconciled the orders and gave the plebs' own resolutions the force of law binding the whole people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.4, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Plebs,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg/1280px-P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg","title":"Citizens at the ballot","desc":"A silver denarius struck at Rome in the late 2nd century BCE, long after the Conflict had ended: on the reverse, lettered NERVA, one citizen takes a ballot from an attendant below while another drops his into the basket, on the raised gangway used at Roman elections.","alt":"Two sides of a worn silver coin on black: at left a helmeted head facing right with lettering around it, at right three small figures on a railed platform, one bending to take a small tablet, one placing a tablet into a basket, with NERVA lettered above.","credit":"Johny SYSEL, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P._Licinius_Nerva_Cr292-1.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-344","num":344,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says a man owed sixteen years' service in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before he turned 46, and could hold no political office at all until he had served ten of them.","questions":["Two centurions commanded each maniple of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, chosen not for daring but for steadiness, as men who would hold their ground when hard pressed and die at their posts.","Discipline in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on the fustuarium, in which a tribune touched the condemned man with a cudgel and the whole camp then beat or stoned him to death."],"answer":"Roman legion","answerText":"Roman legion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Strength</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,200 foot, 300 cavalry</span><span class=\"dt-k\">So recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 346 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Property bar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ended 107 BCE, under Marius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["institution","history","warfare","rome","classical","army"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman legion</b> was the standing formation of the Republic's citizen army, 4,200 foot and 300 cavalry, raised to 5,000 in times of exceptional danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A man owed sixteen years in the infantry or ten in the cavalry before he turned 46, and nobody could hold a political office until he had served ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunes of the four legions chose in rotation from batches of four men of like age and build, each legion picking first in its turn so that all got men of the same standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One man was picked to swear that he would obey his officers and carry out their orders, and the rest each swore to do the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They were sorted by age and means, the youngest and poorest serving as velites, then the hastati, those in their prime as principes, and the oldest six hundred as triarii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Each carried a curved shield of two planks glued together and faced with canvas and calf-skin, iron-rimmed and iron-bossed, a sword that thrust and cut with both edges, two pila, a helmet and greaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The classes were divided into maniples under two centurions apiece, so that one was always there to command, and what was wanted was not daring but steadiness, men who would hold their ground and die at their posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Livy's account of the fighting order the hastati engaged first and, failing to break the enemy, fell back through the gaps in the line of the principes, while the triarii knelt under their standards, spears planted point upwards like a bristling palisade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Discipline rested on the fustuarium, in which a tribune touched the condemned man with a cudgel and the whole camp then beat or stoned him, and a man who escaped could not go home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Until 107 BCE nobody below the property qualification served in the regular ranks, and it was in his first consulship that Marius took in all classes, the lowest included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#19. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.20, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#20. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.21, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#21. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.23, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#23. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.24, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#24. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.37, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#37. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Exercitus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 486-510, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Exercitus.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg/1280px-Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg","title":"Registering at the census","desc":"Part of the marble frieze from the Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, of the late 2nd century BCE and found in the Campus Martius at Rome: a clerk writes on a hinged tablet while citizens in togas are registered, and a soldier in armour with a plumed helmet stands at the right.","alt":"A weathered marble relief in which a seated clerk writes on a hinged tablet at the left, three men in togas stand or sit beside him, and at the right a soldier in a plumed helmet and armour holds a tall shield.","credit":"Marie-Lan Nguyen, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-345","num":345,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius calls the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the longest and greatest war then known, run without a break for 24 years and costing Rome seven hundred warships.","questions":["Rome had never given a thought to the sea when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, and built a hundred quinqueremes although her shipwrights had never made one in their lives.","Before the last of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Romans had made up their minds already and were waiting only for a pretext that would look just to other nations."],"answer":"Punic Wars","answerText":"Punic Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 between Rome and Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 years, ending 241 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace terms</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 talents over 50 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","warfare","rome","carthage","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Punic Wars</b> were the three wars fought between Rome and Carthage, of which Polybius calls the first the longest, most unintermittent and greatest war then known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began over Messana, where the Mamertines appealed to Carthage and to Rome at once, and the Romans knew the help asked of them was hard to excuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They gave it because Carthage already held Africa, much of Iberia and the islands of the Sardinian and Tyrrhenian seas, and would threaten the whole peninsula if Sicily fell too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome had never given a thought to the sea, and now built a hundred quinqueremes and twenty triremes although her shipwrights had never made a quinquereme in their lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting ran 24 years without a break and cost Rome about seven hundred quinqueremes and Carthage about five hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Carthage gave up the islands lying between Sicily and the mainland and paid an indemnity that Rome's commissioners raised by a thousand talents, and in 241 BCE Sicily became the first Roman province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Polybius weighs the second war's causes both ways: Saguntum had put itself under Rome's protection years earlier, so if its destruction was the cause then Carthage had broken the treaty of Lutatius and the undertaking not to cross the Ebro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> But if the cause was rather the seizure of Sardinia and the tribute exacted with it, he says, Carthage had good reason to avenge herself on those who had injured her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The peace that closed that war left her cities and her own laws, but took every warship save ten triremes and all her elephants, forbade her to fight outside Africa or within it without Rome's leave, and set 10,000 talents over fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For the third war the Romans had long since made up their minds and waited only for a pretext that would look just to other nations, a war thought dishonourable making victory smaller and failure more perilous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.10, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html#10. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.20, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html#20. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.63, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html#63. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.30, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#30. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.18, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html#18. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html#2. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Provincia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 966-70, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Provincia.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Carthaginian_naval_ram_%282%29.jpg/1280px-Carthaginian_naval_ram_%282%29.jpg","title":"A warship's bronze ram","desc":"A bronze ram from a Carthaginian warship, dated to the battle off the Egadi Islands that decided the first of the wars in 241 BCE or to shortly before it, and raised from a depth of 80 metres (260 feet) in 2010.","alt":"A large corroded bronze ram from an ancient warship seen from the side and filling the frame: a curved upper blade above three long horizontal fins, pierced by rivet holes, its green and reddish surface pitted by the sea.","credit":"Sb2s3, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthaginian_naval_ram_%282%29.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-346","num":346,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says that of all that befell Rome and Carthage the cause was one man and one mind, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who directed Iberia through his brothers and Sicily through others.","questions":["At nine years old <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was led to the altar by his father, told to lay a hand on the victim, and made to swear the oath he later repeated to Antiochus.","Polybius took the count of the army <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought down into the plain of the Po from the general's own inscription on the column at Lacinium."],"answer":"Hannibal","answerText":"Hannibal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oath</span><span class=\"dt-v\">sworn at 9 years old</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alps</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 days, in 218 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrived with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000 foot, 6,000 cavalry</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","history","warfare","rome","carthage","classical"],"abstract":"<b>Hannibal</b> was the Carthaginian general who fought Rome in the second Punic War, and Polybius says that of all that befell both nations the cause was one man and one mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He ran the campaign in the peninsula himself, directed Iberia through his brothers and Sicily through others, and kept the Romans alarmed in Illyria and across the Adriatic through his understanding with Philip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was nine when his father, sacrificing before setting out, led him to the altar, asked whether he wished to come, and made him lay his hand on the victim and swear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He told that story himself many years afterwards, at the court of Antiochus, to a king grown suspicious of him after his final defeat had driven him from his own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His first stroke was the siege of Saguntum, which he took to shut the Romans out of Iberia, to frighten the tribes into order, and to leave no enemy behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The march from New Carthage, in the first year of the war, 218 BCE, took five months, of which fifteen days were spent crossing the Alps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He came down into the plain of the Po with 12,000 African and 8,000 Iberian foot and no more than 6,000 cavalry, a count Polybius took from Hannibal's own inscription at Lacinium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius is scathing about the writers who made that crossing miraculous, since a general ignorant of the road and the country would have been the most incompetent of leaders, and Celts had brought armies over the Alps before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Crossing the marshes he put the Africans and Iberians in front and the Celts behind under his brother's cavalry, and gave no thought to the pack animals, reckoning that if beaten he would need no provisions and victorious he would want none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some accused him of extreme cruelty and others of avarice, and Polybius grants that it is no easy thing to state the truth about a man engaged in public affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#11. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#17. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.48, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#48. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.56, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#56. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.79, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#79. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 9.22, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/9*.html#22. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Exercitus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 486-510, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Exercitus.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Monedas_hispano-cartaginesas_pertenecientes_al_Tesoro_de_La_Puebla_de_los_Infantes_-_M.A.N.jpg/1280px-Monedas_hispano-cartaginesas_pertenecientes_al_Tesoro_de_La_Puebla_de_los_Infantes_-_M.A.N.jpg","title":"A quarter-shekel with an elephant","desc":"Two Hispano-Carthaginian silver coins of the 3rd century BCE from a hoard found at La Puebla de los Infantes, now in the National Archaeological Museum in Madrid; the smaller, at the left, is a quarter-shekel with an elephant on the reverse.","alt":"Two small worn silver coins mounted on a plain pale wall: the smaller at the left shows an elephant walking in relief, the larger at the right is rubbed almost smooth.","credit":"Dorieo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monedas_hispano-cartaginesas_pertenecientes_al_Tesoro_de_La_Puebla_de_los_Infantes_-_M.A.N.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-347","num":347,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says the Romans put eight legions into the field at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a thing never done before, some 80,000 foot and rather more than 6,000 cavalry.","questions":["Hannibal's centre stood in a crescent at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and when the Celts in it gave way the legions crowded after them until the Africans stood on both their flanks.","Of the 6,000 Roman cavalry at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seventy escaped with the consul to Venusia, and about 70,000 men died on the field that day."],"answer":"Battle of Cannae","answerText":"Battle of Cannae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">216 BCE, in Apulia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Romans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 legions, some 80,000 foot</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 70,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","rome","carthage","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Cannae</b> was fought in 216 BCE in Apulia, where Hannibal had seized the citadel in which the Romans kept their corn so that they could not avoid a battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Senate resolved to fight, sent out both consuls, and put eight legions into the field, which Polybius says the Romans had never done before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each legion held about 5,000 men, and the whole army with its allies came to some 80,000 foot and rather more than 6,000 cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consuls commanded on alternate days, and on his day Terentius crossed the river and drew the army up facing south, the maniples closer together than usual and each many times deeper than it was wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hannibal set the Iberian and Celtic infantry in the centre with the Africans on either side of them, then walked the middle companies forward until his line stood in a crescent with its convex face towards the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The centres met first, the crescent being far in advance of the wings, and when the Celts gave way the legions crowded in from the flanks and pressed after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That carried the Romans so far forward that the heavy-armed Africans stood on both their flanks, whereupon each wing faced inwards and charged the crowd from its own side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the 6,000 cavalry, seventy escaped with Terentius to Venusia; about 10,000 foot were taken at the camp, perhaps 3,000 got away, and about 70,000 died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hannibal lost about 4,000 Celts, 1,500 Iberians and Africans and 200 cavalry, and Polybius draws the lesson that it is better to fight with half the enemy's infantry and an overwhelming force of cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tarentum surrendered, Campanian towns invited Hannibal in, and the Romans gave up hope of holding the peninsula, yet the Senate strengthened the city and deliberated with what Polybius calls manly coolness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.107, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#107. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.113, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#113. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.115, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#115. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.117, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#117. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.118, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#118. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Exercitus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 486-510, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Exercitus.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Area_archeologica_e_Antiquarium_di_Canne_della_Battaglia_-_MIBAC_2013-05-02_00-03-51.jpg/1280px-Area_archeologica_e_Antiquarium_di_Canne_della_Battaglia_-_MIBAC_2013-05-02_00-03-51.jpg","title":"The hill at Canne, and the plain beyond","desc":"The archaeological area at Canne della Battaglia, on the hill whose citadel and grain stores Hannibal seized in order to force a battle; the standing walls are of the later settlement, and the flat country beyond is where the armies fought.","alt":"Looking down into a ruined apsed building of pale stone with two broken column shafts standing on its floor, and beyond its walls a flat plain of vineyards and fields running to the horizon.","credit":"maxzocca, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Area_archeologica_e_Antiquarium_di_Canne_della_Battaglia_-_MIBAC_2013-05-02_00-03-51.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-348","num":348,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says the Romans had decided on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> long before and waited only for a pretext that would look just to other nations.","questions":["Three streets of six-storey houses climbed to the citadel, and in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Romans took them roof by roof, crossing the alleys on timbers laid as bridges.","Scipio wept at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and spoke the lines in which Troy and Priam perish, telling Polybius beside him that he feared for his own country."],"answer":"destruction of Carthage","answerText":"destruction of Carthage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE, to Scipio</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700 years old, says Appian</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 years of war</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","warfare","rome","carthage","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>destruction of Carthage</b> ended the last war between Rome and Carthage in 146 BCE, and Polybius says the Romans had made up their minds long before and waited only for a pretext that would look just abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Carthaginians gave up their weapons and accepted every condition, and their envoys reached Rome to find that war had already been voted and the generals had left with their armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When spring came Scipio took the wall around the war harbour and the market beside it, and his men stripped the gold shrine in the temple there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From that market three streets climbed to the citadel between houses six storeys high, and the Romans took them roof by roof, throwing timbers over the alleys and crossing on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the streets below men were stabbed or hurled down alive onto the spear points of those beneath, and when Scipio reached the citadel he fired all three streets together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Hasdrubal came out with an olive branch and sat at Scipio's feet, and his wife, reproaching him from the burning temple, killed her children, threw them into the fire and followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Appian says the city had stood 700 years and had held out for three years of continuous war without ships or arms and in the face of famine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Scipio wept openly for his enemies and spoke the lines in which Troy and Priam perish, telling Polybius, who stood beside him, that he feared for his own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Senate sent ten of its own number to settle Africa, ordered whatever was left of the city obliterated and nobody allowed to live there, and had a praetor sent out yearly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius reports the opinions current in his own country: some called it wise statesmanship, others a lust for domination like that of Athens and Sparta, others said Carthage had given no offence, and others that Rome had traded open dealing for something like treachery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html#2. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html#3. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 127-130, trans. Horace White, ed. Jona Lendering, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-26/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 131-135, trans. Horace White, ed. Jona Lendering, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-27/. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html#9. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.22, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html#22. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Byrsa_panorama.jpg/1280px-Byrsa_panorama.jpg","title":"Byrsa hill today","desc":"The excavated remains on Byrsa, the citadel hill that Scipio's men fought their way up street by street, with the Gulf of Tunis and the modern city beyond.","alt":"A hillside of low ruined stone walls and foundations among grass and shrubs, with a tall pine at the left, and beyond them a blue gulf, a line of hills and the white buildings of a modern city.","credit":"Asma BEN GHARBI, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byrsa_panorama.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-349","num":349,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"At the Isthmian games a herald proclaimed the cities free during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the crowd shouted so loudly that he had to come forward and read it again.","questions":["Polybius was present at Corinth during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and saw soldiers throw pictures on the ground and play draughts on them.","Philip gave up his garrisoned cities and all but five light warships and paid a thousand talents in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began as a declaration of freedom."],"answer":"Roman conquest of Greece","answerText":"Roman conquest of Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Corinth fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Philip paid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 talents over 10 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 — Macedonia and Achaia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","rome","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman conquest of Greece</b> began not with an annexation but with a proclamation of freedom, made after Rome had beaten Philip and his Macedonians in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten commissioners came out from Rome with a decree of the senate on the peace with Philip, by which all the Greeks of Europe and Asia were to be free and under their own laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Philip was to give up the cities he held with garrisons, surrender his warships except five light vessels and his flagship of sixteen banks of oars, and pay a thousand talents over ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the Isthmian games, with the most distinguished men of almost the whole world assembled, the herald proclaimed the Corinthians, Phocians, Locrians, Euboeans, Thessalians and others free, without garrisons and subject to no tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shout that went up was so great that many missed the words, and the herald had to come into the middle of the stadium and read them again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Achaean League at last went to war with Rome under leaders Polybius calls demented and perverse, the people swept along as by a torrent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Too dismayed by each day's news for any general reflection, he says, they could not see that they and their wives and children were on the road to ruin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some killed themselves in strange desperation, some fled the towns across country to no particular place, and some arrested their neighbours to surrender them as having opposed Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Corinth was taken, and Polybius, who was present, records the soldiers' contempt for works of art and votive offerings, and saw them throw pictures on the ground and play draughts on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mummius triumphed for Greece in the same year that Scipio triumphed for Carthage, 146 BCE, and Macedonia and Achaia took their places among the provinces Rome governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.46, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html#46. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.44, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html#44. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.16, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html#16. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 39.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/39*.html#2. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 131-135, trans. Horace White, ed. Jona Lendering, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-27/. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Provincia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 966-70, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Provincia.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/The_Temple_of_Apollo_in_Ancient_Corinth_on_June_6%2C_2018.jpg/1280px-The_Temple_of_Apollo_in_Ancient_Corinth_on_June_6%2C_2018.jpg","title":"The temple that outlived the sack","desc":"The seven standing Doric columns of the archaic temple of Apollo at Corinth, with the citadel hill of Acrocorinth behind; the temple was already old when the city was taken and its pictures and votive offerings carried off.","alt":"Seven weathered limestone Doric columns standing in a row on a low platform, two of them still carrying a length of architrave, with a bare rocky mountain rising behind and dry grass and scattered blocks in front.","credit":"George E. Koronaios, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Temple_of_Apollo_in_Ancient_Corinth_on_June_6,_2018.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-350","num":350,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The wild beasts of Italy have caves and lairs, said the elder of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the men who fight and die for Italy have nothing but air and light.","questions":["The law the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pressed let nobody hold more than 500 iugera of public land, the rest to be divided among the poor by three elected commissioners.","Scipio Nasica led the senators up to the Capitol with his toga wound about his head, and the elder of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was beaten down by the statues of the kings."],"answer":"Gracchi brothers","answerText":"Gracchi brothers","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tiberius</span><span class=\"dt-v\">tribune and killed, 133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gaius</span><span class=\"dt-v\">tribune 123, killed 121 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land limit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 iugera of public land</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","history","politics","law","rome","classical"],"abstract":"The <b>Gracchi brothers</b> were two tribunes, Tiberius in 133 BCE and Gaius from 123, who tried to give the Roman poor a share of the public land and were both killed for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Rome had taken land from the peoples it conquered and let anyone work the undistributed part for a share of the crop, meaning to multiply the free population that supplied its allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The opposite happened: the rich took the greater part, bought or forced out their poorer neighbours, and worked vast tracts with slaves rather than with free labourers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tiberius, as tribune, lamented that a people so valiant in war and so close in blood to the Romans was declining into pauperism and paucity of numbers with no hope of remedy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His law let nobody hold more than 500 iugera of the public land, with half as much again for each son, the rest to be divided among the poor by three elected commissioners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch says he drafted it with the pontifex maximus, the consul and jurist Scaevola and his own father-in-law, and that no law dealing with so great a wrong was ever framed in gentler terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The wild beasts of Italy have their caves and lairs, he told the crowd, but the men who fight and die for Italy have nothing but the air and the light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the senate met, Scipio Nasica led the senators up to the Capitol with the border of his toga wound about his head, calling on those who would save the country to follow him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tiberius was beaten down by the statues of the kings, and the bodies were thrown into the Tiber that night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gaius stood for the tribunate after his brother's death and took up the same cause, and died on the Aventine when the senate told him to lay down his arms and come and say what he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.7, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#7. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.9, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#9. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.16, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#16. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.21, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#21. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.26, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#26. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Tiberius Gracchus</i> 9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html#9. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Agrariae Leges,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 38-44, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Agrariae_Leges.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi.jpg/1920px-Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi.jpg","title":"The Gracchi, by Eugene Guillaume","desc":"A bronze double bust of Tiberius and Gaius Sempronius Gracchus, modelled by the French sculptor Eugene Guillaume in the 19th century. The plinth is inscribed to the two brothers as tribunes of the plebs who deserved well of the Roman people, and the scroll under Tiberius' hand carries the word for property. It is a modern imagining rather than an ancient likeness, no portrait of either brother having survived.","credit":"Eugene Guillaume, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi.jpg","alt":"A bronze bust of two young men in togas standing side by side, the left one resting his hand on an open scroll and the right one with a hand on his shoulder, above an inscribed plinth."}},
{"id":"wh-351","num":351,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"When the command against Mithridates was taken from one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and given to the other, the loser led six legions on Rome and said he came to deliver her from tyrants.","questions":["The first man ever elected consul seven times was one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and he died seventeen days after taking up that seventh term, at the age of seventy.","About 40 senators and 1,600 knights were posted on a list in the forum by the winner of the war between <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who paid informers and let anyone kill them."],"answer":"Marius and Sulla","answerText":"Marius and Sulla","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Marius</span><span class=\"dt-v\">consul seven times, 107 – 86 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sulla</span><span class=\"dt-v\">dictator from 82 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Proscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 40 senators, 1,600 knights</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Marius and Sulla</b> were the two Roman commanders whose quarrel over a single military command grew into the first war fought between Roman armies, directly after the Social War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaius Marius was the first man ever elected consul seven times, and he was seventy years old at his death, still lamenting that he was dying before he had finished what he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> When Mithridates of Pontus invaded, the command against him fell by lot to the consul Lucius Cornelius Sulla, and Marius induced the tribune Publius Sulpicius to have it transferred to himself instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sulla had gone to his army at Capua, and when the news reached him the soldiers told him to lead them to Rome, whereupon every senior officer he had deserted him but one quaestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Envoys met him on the road and asked why he was marching with armed forces against his own country, and he answered that he came to deliver her from tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He seized the Esquiline gate with one legion and fought his way in, in the first battle waged inside the city with bugle and standards rather than as a mere faction fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Marius fled, came back with Cinna, and the heads of the killed were hung before the rostra, that of the consul Octavius being the first head of a consul so exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took up a seventh consulship on the first day of 86 BCE and died seventeen days later, worn out by the thought of the war coming back across the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Sulla returned and proscribed about 40 senators and 1,600 knights, exhibiting the list in the forum, offering prizes to assassins and rewards to informers, and barring the sons and grandsons of the proscribed from office for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> He then had an interrex name him dictator, an office in abeyance 120 years and now held for no fixed term, and afterwards laid that power down of his own free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.55, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#55. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.57, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#57. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.58, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#58. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.71, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#71. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.95, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#95. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.99, 1.103, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#99. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caius Marius</i> 9, 45-46, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html#9. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Proscriptio,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 963-964, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Proscriptio.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Dictator,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 404-408, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Dictator.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/L._Cornelius_Sulla%2C_AR_denarius%2C_84-83_BC%2C_RRC_359-2.jpg/1920px-L._Cornelius_Sulla%2C_AR_denarius%2C_84-83_BC%2C_RRC_359-2.jpg","title":"Denarius of Sulla, 84 to 83 BCE","desc":"A silver denarius struck at the military mint travelling with Sulla's army as it moved back towards Rome. The obverse carries the head of Venus with Cupid holding a palm branch and the name L SVLLA below; the reverse a jug and an augur's staff between two trophies, with the legend IMPER ITERVM, imperator for the second time.","credit":"Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L._Cornelius_Sulla,_AR_denarius,_84-83_BC,_RRC_359-2.jpg","alt":"The two faces of a silver Roman coin: a woman's head in profile with a small winged figure beside it, and a jug and a curved staff standing between two trophies."}},
{"id":"wh-352","num":352,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Escaping gladiators camped on Vesuvius and climbed down the cliff on ladders of wild vine to open the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Rome at first treated as brigandage rather than as a war.","questions":["For three years two praetors and both consuls were beaten in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until Crassus revived decimation on his own men and forced the last battle in Lucania.","Six thousand survivors were crucified along the road from Capua to Rome after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Pompey, who cut down the fugitives, claimed he had extirpated the war."],"answer":"Third Servile War","answerText":"Third Servile War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">73 – 71 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Escaped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 to 78 gladiators</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crucified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 along the road to Rome</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Third Servile War</b> is the modern name for the rising of 73 to 71 BCE that Florus records as the war against Spartacus, opening his chapter by saying he cannot name it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It began in a gladiatorial school at Capua kept by Lentulus Batiatus, most of whose men were Gauls and Thracians and were shut up in close confinement to be reserved for the arena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 200 planned to escape and 78 got out, seizing cleavers and spits from a kitchen, though Appian counts about 70 men armed with clubs and daggers and Florus 30 or rather more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They plundered a waggon of gladiators' weapons on the road, elected Spartacus, Crixus and Oenomaus as their leaders, and occupied Vesuvius, where herdsmen and shepherds joined them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Besieged on the mountain by the praetor Clodius, they cut the wild vine growing over the summit into ladders, climbed down the cliff face, and took the Roman camp from behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Rome treated the rising at first as brigandage rather than war, and Spartacus beat two praetors and then both consuls, taking the lictors and the very mount of one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By Appian's count the army reached 70,000, and Spartacus sacrificed 300 Roman prisoners to the shade of Crixus before turning back from a march on Rome for want of arms and allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Licinius Crassus took the command with six new legions, revived the old punishment of decimation on the men who had run, and walled the rebels into the toe of the peninsula from sea to sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Spartacus broke out, was refused terms, and died on his feet in the last battle, wounded in the thigh and fighting on one knee behind his shield; his body was never found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Crassus crucified 6,000 of the survivors along the whole road from Capua to Rome, and Pompey, who cut down the fugitives, wrote to the senate that he had extirpated the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.116, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#116. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.117, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#117. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.118, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#118. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.120, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#120. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Crassus</i> 8-9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Crassus*.html#8. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Crassus</i> 10-11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Crassus*.html#10. [Open access]","Florus, <i>Epitome of Roman History</i> 2.8, trans. Edward Seymour Forster, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1929), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Florus/Epitome/2B*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Santa_Maria_Capua_Vetere_-_Anfiteatro_campano_-_202209101610.jpg/1920px-Santa_Maria_Capua_Vetere_-_Anfiteatro_campano_-_202209101610.jpg","title":"Under the arena at Capua","desc":"The passages beneath the amphitheatre at Santa Maria Capua Vetere, where fighters and animals waited to be lifted into the arena above. Capua was the centre of the gladiatorial trade and the town Spartacus escaped from, though the building standing today is a later one than the school of 73 BCE.","credit":"Viaggiatore73, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Maria_Capua_Vetere_-_Anfiteatro_campano_-_202209101610.jpg","alt":"A narrow brick-walled passage running under a Roman amphitheatre, its arched niches green with moss and a paved channel down the middle."}},
{"id":"wh-353","num":353,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Told that the young man he had spared would one day deal the death blow to the aristocracy, Sulla answered that in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there was more than one Marius.","questions":["Held for about 40 days by pirates near Pharmacussa, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> paid 50 talents, raised a fleet the moment he was ashore, and inflicted the punishment he had joked about in captivity.","The calendar had drifted so far under the pontiffs that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fixed the year at 365 days with one added every fourth year, and made that year fifteen months long."],"answer":"Julius Caesar","answerText":"Julius Caesar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 100 BCE, or perhaps 102</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pirates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 talents, about 40 days held</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Calendar reform</span><span class=\"dt-v\">46 BCE, a year of 365 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Julius Caesar</b> was a Roman general, politician and writer, born about 100 BCE, who rose through the Republic's offices to a dictatorship and was murdered on his way to the senate house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He lost his father in his sixteenth year, married Cornelia the daughter of Cinna, and refused Sulla's order to put her away, losing his priesthood, her dowry and his inheritances for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He went into hiding, changing covert almost nightly with a quartan fever and buying off Sulla's detectives, until the Vestal virgins and his kinsmen won him a pardon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sulla gave way saying that the man they were so eager to save would one day deal the death blow to the aristocracy, for in this Caesar there was more than one Marius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crossing to Rhodes to study oratory he was taken by pirates near Pharmacussa, held about 40 days, and after paying 50 talents raised a fleet and inflicted the punishment he had joked about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He served his first campaigns in the eastern provinces, won the civic crown at the storming of Mytilene, and was made military tribune by vote of the people on his return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Suetonius describes him tall and fair with keen black eyes, twice attacked by the falling sickness on campaign, and troubled enough by his baldness to comb his scanty hair forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was skilled in arms and horsemanship and of incredible endurance, marching bareheaded at the head of his army in sun and rain, and swimming the rivers that barred his path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As dictator he reformed the calendar the pontiffs had let drift, fixing the year at 365 days with one added every fourth year so that the harvest festivals fell in summer again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> To start the new reckoning clean he put two extra months before December, making that year fifteen months long; he also filled the senate, enlarged the magistracies and planned public libraries under Varro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 1, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#1. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 2-5, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#2. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 45, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#45. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 57, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#57. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 40, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#40. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 41, 44, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#41. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caesar</i> 59, 63, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html#59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Head_of_Julius_Caesar_from_Tusculum_MAntTorino_2089_n01.jpg/1920px-Head_of_Julius_Caesar_from_Tusculum_MAntTorino_2089_n01.jpg","title":"The Tusculum head, Museo di Antichita, Turin","desc":"A marble head found at Tusculum and generally taken to be a portrait made in Caesar's own lifetime rather than after his death. The thin lined face and the scanty hair combed forward from the crown match the description Suetonius gives, though no ancient portrait carries a name and every identification of one is an argument.","credit":"Marie-Lan Tay Pamart, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_Julius_Caesar_from_Tusculum_MAntTorino_2089_n01.jpg","alt":"A marble head of a middle-aged man with a long thin face, deep lines from nose to mouth, and short hair combed forward over a receding hairline."}},
{"id":"wh-354","num":354,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Caesar's own memoirs are almost the only account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they open by declaring that all Gaul is divided into three parts, held by three different peoples.","questions":["Caesar had to build a second line of works facing outwards at Alesia during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when the rest of Gaul came in arms to relieve the town he was besieging.","Asinius Pollio thought the memoirs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carelessly made and careless of the truth, saying their author gave a perverted account of his own actions."],"answer":"Gallic Wars","answerText":"Gallic Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaigns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 58 BCE, under ten years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alesia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">52 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plutarch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 cities, 300 nations taken</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Gallic Wars</b> is the name given to the campaigns Caesar fought in Gaul from 58 BCE, and nearly everything known about them comes from the memoirs he wrote about them himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That account opens by declaring that all Gaul is divided into three parts, held by the Belgae, the Aquitani, and the people who call themselves Celts and whom the Romans call Gauls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It explains that the Belgae are the bravest of them because they are furthest from the Province and merchants least often reach them with the goods that soften the mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sailed north to the Britons twice, saying they had sent help to his enemies in almost every Gallic war and that he wanted to see their harbours and landing places for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In 52 BCE Vercingetorix drew most of Gaul into one revolt, lost his cavalry in the open field, and fell back on Alesia, a hill town of the Mandubii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Caesar walled the town about with ditches, a rampart and towers set at intervals, and then had to build a second line facing outwards when the rest of Gaul came to relieve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plutarch puts 300,000 in the relieving army and not less than 170,000 inside, and says the men on the inner wall learned the outer battle was won only from the wailing in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Vercingetorix put on his finest armour, rode out through the gate, circled Caesar where he sat, dismounted, laid down his arms and sat at his feet until he was led off for the triumph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plutarch reckons that in under ten years Caesar stormed more than 800 cities, subdued 300 nations, and of three million enemies killed a million and enslaved as many again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those figures come from the winning side, and Asinius Pollio thought the memoirs carelessly made and careless of the truth, saying Caesar gave a perverted account of his own actions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Julius Caesar, <i>Gallic War</i> 1.1, trans. W. A. McDevitte and W. S. Bohn (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1869), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0001%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Julius Caesar, <i>Gallic War</i> 4.20, trans. W. A. McDevitte and W. S. Bohn (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1869), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0001%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Julius Caesar, <i>Gallic War</i> 7.68, trans. W. A. McDevitte and W. S. Bohn (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1869), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0001%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D68. [Open access]","Julius Caesar, <i>Gallic War</i> 7.72, trans. W. A. McDevitte and W. S. Bohn (New York: Harper &amp; Brothers, 1869), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0001%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 56, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#56. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caesar</i> 15-27, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html#15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Vercingetorix_stater_CdM.jpg","title":"Gold stater struck for Vercingetorix","desc":"A gold coin carrying the name VERCINGETORIXS around a curly-haired head, in the Cabinet des Medailles in Paris. Gallic staters copy the head on the gold of Philip of Macedon, so the face is a borrowed type rather than a likeness of the man; what the coin gives is his name, in his own lifetime, from the side that left no written history.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vercingetorix_stater_CdM.jpg","alt":"A worn gold coin showing a head in profile with thick curly hair, and letters running round the rim ending in the name Vercingetorixs."}},
{"id":"wh-355","num":355,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"At the river that bounded his province Caesar said the whole issue would be with the sword once they crossed the little bridge, and so opened <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 49 BCE.","questions":["Pompey was routed at Pharsalus in the second year of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and sat in his tent with one word about his quarters until the enemy were on his own rampart.","Appian gives figures for the dead at Pharsalus ranging from 6,000 to 25,000 in the battle that decided <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the lower count coming from an officer who fought in it himself."],"answer":"Caesar's civil war","answerText":"Caesar's civil war","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">49 – 45 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pharsalus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">48 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pollio's count</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 Pompeians fell</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Caesar's civil war</b> was fought from 49 to 45 BCE between Caesar and the commanders the senate backed under Pompey, and left Caesar without a rival in the Roman world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It opened when the tribunes' veto on his behalf was set aside and they fled to his camp; he sent cohorts ahead in secret and stayed at a banquet to hide it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the Rubicon, the boundary of his province, he paused and said that they could still draw back, but that once over the little bridge the whole issue would be with the sword.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian has him say that leaving the stream uncrossed would breed distress for him and crossing it distress for all mankind, before he went over uttering the phrase 'the die is cast'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pompey and the consuls fell back on Brundisium and sailed; Caesar took Rome, then beat Pompey's lieutenants in Hispania, saying he went to meet an army without a leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He crossed to Macedonia, was driven off at Dyrrachium, and routed Pompey at Pharsalus in 48 BCE, where the beaten man sat in his tent until the enemy were on his rampart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Walking among the dead Caesar said they would have it so, and that after such wars they would have condemned him in their courts if he had disbanded his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Appian gives 200 legionaries lost on Caesar's side, or 1,200 by other accounts, and says exaggerating writers put Pompey's dead at 25,000 where Pollio, who fought there, recorded 6,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pompey fled to Alexandria and was murdered there; Caesar fought a winter war in the city, left the country to Cleopatra and her younger brother, and beat Pharnaces in Pontus in five days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He finished the war against Scipio and Juba at Thapsus and against Pompey's sons at Munda in Hispania, then held five triumphs, showing in the Pontic one an inscription of three words: 'I came, I saw, I conquered'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 31-33, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#31. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 34-35, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#34. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 36-37, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#36. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 2.35, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/2*.html#35. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 2.82, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/2*.html#82. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caesar</i> 32-56, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html#32. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/R%C3%B6m._Republik-_C._Iulius_Caesar_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5503460.jpg/1920px-R%C3%B6m._Republik-_C._Iulius_Caesar_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5503460.jpg","title":"Silver denarius struck for Caesar","desc":"A denarius of the Roman Republic in the Muenzkabinett in Berlin. The obverse carries a diademed head of Venus; the reverse a trophy of arms with a captive seated on either side of it and the name CAESAR below.","credit":"Reinhard Saczewski, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B6m._Republik-_C._Iulius_Caesar_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5503460.jpg","alt":"The two faces of a silver Roman coin: a woman's head in profile wearing a diadem, and a trophy of armour with two seated captives beneath it above the letters CAESAR."}},
{"id":"wh-356","num":356,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Spurinna had warned that the danger would come not later than the Ides, and on the day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Caesar went in laughing at him as a false prophet.","questions":["More than 60 men joined the plot that ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, led by Gaius Cassius and by Marcus and Decimus Brutus at a meeting called in the Hall of Pompey.","The physician Antistius held that of the 23 wounds given in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only the second, in the breast, was mortal, and three slaves carried the body home."],"answer":"assassination of Julius Caesar","answerText":"assassination of Julius Caesar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 March 44 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Conspirators</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 60 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wounds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23, one of them mortal</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>assassination of Julius Caesar</b> took place on 15 March 44 BCE at a meeting of the senate held in the Hall of Pompey, which the conspirators chose for the time and the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 60 men joined the plot, led by Gaius Cassius and by Marcus and Decimus Brutus, after separate groups of two and three came together into one conspiracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They had considered killing him at the elections in the Campus Martius or on the Sacred Way, and gave up both when the senate meeting was called.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The soothsayer Spurinna had warned him to beware of a danger coming not later than the Ides, and he went in laughing at him as a false prophet, the day having brought no harm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Spurinna answered that the day had come but had not gone; on the way in a note revealing the plot was handed to Caesar, who kept it unread in his left hand with the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Tillius Cimber came up to plead for his exiled brother and caught the toga at both shoulders, and as Caesar cried that this was violence one of the Cascas stabbed him below the throat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He caught Casca's arm and ran it through with his stylus, then, seeing drawn daggers on every side, muffled his head in his robe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He drew the lap of the toga down to his feet so as to fall decently, and took 23 wounds without a word, though some wrote that he asked Marcus Brutus, 'You too, my child?'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The physician Antistius judged that of all those wounds only the second, in the breast, was mortal, and three common slaves carried the body home with one arm hanging down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conspirators had meant to throw him into the Tiber and revoke his acts, but held back for fear of the consul Antony and of Lepidus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome","classical","warfare"],"sources":["Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 80-81, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#80. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Julius</i> 82, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Julius*.html#82. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caesar</i> 63-66, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caesar*.html#63. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Brutus</i> 14, 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Brutus*.html#14. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 2.149, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: Macmillan, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/2*.html#149. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Eid_mar_aureus_BM.JPG","title":"The EID MAR coin, British Museum","desc":"A gold coin struck for Marcus Brutus in the years after the killing. Two daggers stand either side of the cap given to a freed slave, and the letters below read EID MAR, for the Ides of March. The hole at the top was made later, when someone wore it.","credit":"Geni, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eid_mar_aureus_BM.JPG","alt":"A worn gold coin showing two daggers with a rounded cap between them and the letters EID MAR beneath, with a small hole pierced through the top edge."}},
{"id":"wh-357","num":357,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Some wanted the first Roman emperor called Romulus as a second founder, but Plancus carried the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead, from the augural sense of a consecrated place.","questions":["The republic was transferred from his own control to the will of the senate and the Roman people, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote of his sixth and seventh consulships, in 28 and 27 BCE.","Asking his friends whether he had played the comedy of life fitly, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added the tag about being clapped from the stage, and died soon after kissing his wife."],"answer":"Augustus","answerText":"Augustus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Title Augustus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 January 27 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pater patriae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 February 2 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 CE, after 44 years alone</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Augustus</b> was the first Roman emperor, born Gaius Octavius, who took that name in 27 BCE and ruled alone for 44 years until his death in 14 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The civil wars of his rise were five, called Mutina, Philippi, Perusia, Sicily and Actium, and the first and the last of them were against Mark Antony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He ruled first with Antony and Lepidus together, then with Antony alone for nearly twelve years, and finally by himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his own account he says that at 19, on his own initiative and at his own expense, he raised an army and restored liberty to a republic oppressed by a faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He says he drove his father's killers into exile by due process of law, and twice defeated them in battle when they made war on the republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In his sixth and seventh consulships, of 28 and 27 BCE, he says he transferred the republic from his own control to the will of the senate and the Roman people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some had wanted him called Romulus, as a second founder of the city; Plancus carried the name Augustus instead, from the augural sense of a place consecrated by the rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The senate also hung laurels on his doorposts and set a golden shield in the Curia whose inscription named his valour, his clemency, his justice and his piety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then wrote the sentence that decides how all of it is read: after that time he took precedence of all in rank, but of power possessed no more than his colleagues in any magistracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On his last day he called for a mirror, had his hair combed, and asked his friends whether he had played the comedy of life fitly before adding the tag that dismissed him from the stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","ruler","history","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Suetonius, <i>Divus Augustus</i> 8-9, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html#8. [Open access]","Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 1-3, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/1*.html. [Open access]","Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 34, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/6*.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Augustus</i> 99-100, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html#99. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Augustus</i> 7, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html#7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Statue-Augustus.jpg","title":"The Augustus of Prima Porta","desc":"A marble statue found in Livia's villa at Prima Porta and now in the Vatican, showing Augustus bare-footed in a decorated cuirass with his arm raised to address troops. It is an official image rather than a record of how he looked, showing him young and unlined whatever his age at the time.","credit":"Till Niermann, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue-Augustus.jpg","alt":"A marble statue of a young man in a decorated breastplate and draped cloak, barefoot, his right arm raised and outstretched, with a small child figure beside his right leg."}},
{"id":"wh-358","num":358,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Appian wrote that the rulers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called imperatores rather than kings, out of respect for an old oath, and added that they were very kings in fact.","questions":["Augustus left a document in his own hand listing the arms, fleets, kingdoms, provinces and taxes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with the advice that it be kept within the bounds it then had.","The bounds ran from the western ocean to Mount Caucasus and the Euphrates, and up the Nile southward, which is how Appian opens his account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its extent."],"answer":"Roman Empire","answerText":"Roman Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First emperor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Augustus, from 27 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To master Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 years, half under kings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Appian reckons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">900 years of Roman power</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman Empire</b> was the state Rome became when the republic gave way to rule by one man, conventionally dated from the senate's grant of the name Augustus in 27 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Appian, writing from inside it, begins his history with the boundaries of the nations under Roman sway, from the islands of the north-western ocean round the whole Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He puts the bounds at the western ocean, Mount Caucasus and the Euphrates, up the Nile to the south and through Arabia to the eastern ocean, with every island of that sea inside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome had needed 500 years to master the peninsula, he says, half of them under kings, and 200 more to bring most of those nations under its rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rulers were not called kings, out of respect for the oath sworn when the kings were driven out, but imperatores, and Appian adds that they are very kings in fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the empire was made of is set out in a document Augustus wrote in his own hand and left to be read to the senate after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It listed the citizens and allies under arms, the fleets, the subject kingdoms, the provinces, the taxes direct and indirect, the necessary expenses and the customary bounties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> To it he added one piece of advice, that the empire should be kept within the bounds it then had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Appian says the emperors on the whole followed that course, aiming to preserve what they held by prudence rather than to extend their rule over poor and profitless tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He reckons the Romans had been at it for 900 years by his own day, losing 20,000 men in a single day, at another time 40,000 and once 50,000, and never giving up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["state","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["Appian, <i>Roman History</i>, Preface 1-3, trans. Horace White, with notes by Jona Lendering, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-preface-1/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>Roman History</i>, Preface 6-10, trans. Horace White, with notes by Jona Lendering, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-preface-2/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>Roman History</i>, Preface 11, trans. Horace White, with notes by Jona Lendering, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-preface-3/. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 1.11, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0078%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 34, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/6*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Roman_Empire_125_political_map.svg/1920px-Roman_Empire_125_political_map.svg.png","title":"The provinces in 125 CE","desc":"A modern map of the empire under Hadrian, showing the provinces and their capitals, the frontier line and its walls, the main roads, and the bases of the thirty legions with each legion named. It is a reconstruction rather than a Roman document, and the Romans made no map like it.","credit":"ArdadN and EraNavigator, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_125_political_map.svg","alt":"A detailed map of the Mediterranean world with the Roman provinces coloured and named in Latin, a dashed frontier line running round them, and numbered legionary bases marked along it."}},
{"id":"wh-359","num":359,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Augustus had the gate of Janus Quirinus shut three times, against twice in all the city's earlier history, as the emblem of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> his rule was said to have begun.","questions":["Robbers of the world, says a chief in Tacitus of the men who brought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who give the lying name of empire to robbery and make a solitude and call it peace.","Baths, banquets, the toga and a Roman education for the sons of chiefs were how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made to hold, and the conquered called it civilisation."],"answer":"Pax Romana","answerText":"Pax Romana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 BCE, under Augustus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Janus shut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 times in his principate</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earlier</span><span class=\"dt-v\">shut only 2 times in all</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pax Romana</b> is the name given to the long stretch of relative peace inside the Roman Empire, beginning under Augustus, who took that name in 27 BCE, and running for roughly two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its emblem was the gate of Janus Quirinus, which the Romans shut only when there was peace, secured by victory, throughout their whole domain by land and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Augustus records that before his birth it had been shut twice in all since the founding of the city, and that the senate ordered it shut three times while he was princeps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The definition sits inside that sentence: what closed the gate was peace won by victory rather than peace agreed between equals, and the two are not the same thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian, writing about two hundred years into it, says the city was greatly embellished, its revenue much increased, and everything moved toward a lasting prosperity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Tacitus put the other account in the mouth of a chief drawn up against a Roman army at the far end of the island his father-in-law was conquering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Robbers of the world, he has him say, who having exhausted the land by their universal plunder now rifle the deep, and who covet poverty and riches with equal eagerness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To robbery, slaughter and plunder they give the lying name of empire; they make a solitude and call it peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same book shows how such a peace was made to hold in a conquered province: aid for temples, courts and houses, a liberal education for the sons of the chiefs, then the toga, the baths and the elegant banquet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Step by step they were led to the things that dispose to vice, and called it civilisation, Tacitus writes, when it was but a part of their servitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 12-13, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/2*.html. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Agricola</i> 30, trans. Sara Bryant (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0081%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Appian, <i>Roman History</i>, Preface 6-10, trans. Horace White, with notes by Jona Lendering, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-preface-2/. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Agricola</i> 21, trans. Sara Bryant (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0081%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 34, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/6*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Ara_Pacis_Rom.jpg/1920px-Ara_Pacis_Rom.jpg","title":"The Ara Pacis Augustae, Rome","desc":"The Altar of Augustan Peace, voted by the senate for Augustus's return from Spain and Gaul and dedicated in 9 BCE, now rebuilt under a modern roof beside the Tiber. Its outer wall carries a procession of the imperial family above panels of acanthus scroll; the person in the doorway gives the scale.","credit":"Manfred Heyde, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ara_Pacis_Rom.jpg","alt":"A rectangular marble enclosure carved all over with figures in procession above panels of scrolling foliage, with steps leading up to an opening in one side."}},
{"id":"wh-360","num":360,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Four courses were laid on the firm ground under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: stones a hand could grasp, broken stone in lime, brick and pottery fragments, and polygonal blocks of hard silex.","questions":["Gaius Gracchus measured every one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by miles, planted stone pillars to mark the distances, and set smaller stones along both sides so a rider could mount without help.","The censorship of Appius Claudius in 312 BCE is remembered for an aqueduct and for the first of the great <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran from Rome to Capua."],"answer":"Roman roads","answerText":"Roman roads","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appian Way</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun 312 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Paving</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 courses over firm ground</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Marked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 stone pillar per Roman mile</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Roman roads</b> were the paved public highways of the Roman state, of which the first of the great ones was the Appian Way, begun in the censorship of Appius Claudius in 312 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy says that censorship is remembered chiefly for two public works, the road and the aqueduct that carry his name, and the road ran in the first instance from Rome to Capua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before that a road might be no more than a track worn by feet, hooves and wheels, or one whose surface had merely been hardened with gravel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A paved road began with two shallow trenches marking its width, the loose earth between them dug out until firm ground was reached, or piles driven where the ground was swampy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that footing came four courses: stones a hand could just grasp, broken stone rammed in lime, a bed of brick and pottery fragments, and polygonal blocks of hard silex fitted into one even surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The making of them belonged to the censors, and when no censors were in office to the consuls, the praetor, the aediles or whomever the senate thought fit to appoint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaius Gracchus carried his roads straight through the country, bridging every ravine and filling every hollow, and made both sides of equal height so the work looked even along its whole length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He measured every road by miles and planted stone pillars to mark the distances, and set smaller stones along both sides so that a rider could mount without help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Milestones found in Gaul give the distances not only in Roman miles but in Gallic leagues, a measure somewhat greater than the mile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Augustus rebuilt the Flaminian Road himself as far as Ariminum, and handed the other highways to men who had been given triumphs, asking them to spend their prize money on the paving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","history","technology","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Viae,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1188-1194, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Viae.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caius Gracchus</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html#7. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Milliare,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 767-768, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Milliare.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.29, trans. William Masfen Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Augustus</i> 30, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html#30. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Rome_Lazio_Via_Appia_Antica.jpg/1920px-Rome_Lazio_Via_Appia_Antica.jpg","title":"Paving of the Via Appia Antica","desc":"The surviving surface of the Appian Way outside Rome: polygonal blocks of hard volcanic stone, cut irregularly but fitted so closely that the road reads as one mass. Three more courses of stone, rubble and lime lie underneath it.","credit":"Nicholas Hartmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rome_Lazio_Via_Appia_Antica.jpg","alt":"A straight road paved with large irregular grey stone blocks fitted tightly together, running away between grass verges and trees."}},
{"id":"wh-361","num":361,"category":"Rome","question":"For 441 years the city drew its water from the Tiber, from wells and from springs, until the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was brought in by Appius Claudius as censor.","questions":["Compare with such an array of indispensable structures, wrote the man in charge of every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> running into Rome, the idle Pyramids or the useless though famous works of the Greeks.","Men called puncturers bored secret pipes from every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the pavements of Rome, and the theft was measured by the weight of lead taken up when they were removed."],"answer":"Roman aqueduct","answerText":"Roman aqueduct","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Appian, 312 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Before that</span><span class=\"dt-v\">441 years on wells and springs</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 aqueducts by Frontinus's day</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>Roman aqueduct</b> was a channel that carried water into a city by gravity alone, running for long distances at a slight and nearly uniform fall, and nine of them ran into Rome in Frontinus's day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For 441 years from the founding of the city, he writes, the Romans were satisfied with what they drew from the Tiber, from wells and from springs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first was the Appian, brought in by Appius Claudius as censor in 312 BCE, the same censorship that gave Rome its first paved highway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The channel itself was a trough of brick or stone lined with cement under an arched coping, cut through hills by tunnel and carried over valleys on substructures or on arches in two or three tiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The arcades everyone pictures are the exception: most of a long aqueduct ran underground, and only the stretch nearest the city stood up on arches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Augustus restored the channels of aqueducts that were falling into disrepair through age, and doubled what the Marcia carried by turning a new spring into its channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Frontinus, appointed water commissioner, had plans made showing where the valleys lay, where rivers were crossed, and which hillside conduits needed constant care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Compare, he says, with such an array of indispensable structures carrying so many waters, the idle Pyramids or the useless though famous works of the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He found secret pipes running under the pavements all over the city, bored by men called puncturers who sold water from them to the businesses along the route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So little was reaching the public basins that he measured how much had been stolen by the weight of lead taken up when those branch pipes were removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","technology","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Frontinus, <i>The Stratagems; and, The Aqueducts of Rome</i> 1, trans. Charles Edwin Bennett, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Frontinus/De_Aquis/Bennett/1*.html. [Open access]","Frontinus, <i>The Stratagems; and, The Aqueducts of Rome</i> 2, trans. Charles Edwin Bennett, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Frontinus/De_Aquis/Bennett/2*.html. [Open access]","Philip Smith, \"Aquaeductus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 108-115, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Aquaeductus.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.29, trans. William Masfen Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 20, trans. Frederick William Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/4*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/2_-_Parco_degli_Acquedotti.jpg/1920px-2_-_Parco_degli_Acquedotti.jpg","title":"Aqueduct arches in the Parco degli Acquedotti, Rome","desc":"An aqueduct arcade in the Parco degli Acquedotti outside Rome, its piers of squared stone carrying the water channel above the arches and running on towards the horizon. Only the stretch nearest the city stood up on arches like these; most of a long aqueduct's course ran underground.","credit":"DonZaucker, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2_-_Parco_degli_Acquedotti.jpg","alt":"A long line of tall stone arches on massive piers running away across dry grassland, with a dirt track beside them and umbrella pines visible through one of the near arches."}},
{"id":"wh-362","num":362,"category":"Rome","question":"Vespasian began the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the ground that had been the ornamental lake of Nero's Golden House, and his son Titus opened it in 80 CE with a hundred days of games.","questions":["Romans knew the building as the <i>Amphitheatrum Flavium</i>, and the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a later one, taken from the colossal bronze statue of Nero that stood beside it.","A late Roman catalogue credits the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with 87,000 <i>loca</i>, a measure of seating rather than a count of people, and the seats have been reckoned at no more than 45,000."],"answer":"Colosseum","answerText":"Colosseum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Vespasian, before 79 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enlarged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened by Titus in 80 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last games</span><span class=\"dt-v\">gladiators 404 CE, beasts 523 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Colosseum</b> is the great amphitheatre at Rome, begun by Vespasian in the hollow between the Velia, the Esquiline and the Caelian, on ground that had been the ornamental lake of Nero's Golden House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vespasian carried the work to the top of the second arcade and dedicated the building before his death in 79 CE, and his son Titus added the third and fourth storeys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Titus opened the enlarged amphitheatre in 80 with games that ran for a hundred days, given with the dedication of the baths hastily built beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Suetonius says five thousand wild beasts of every kind were shown in a single day; Dio puts the animals killed at nine thousand, and describes cranes fighting, four elephants, and the arena flooded for a sea-fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Romans called it the <i>Amphitheatrum Flavium</i>; Colosseum is a later name, taken from the colossal bronze statue of Nero that stood beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The building is elliptical, 188 by 156 metres (617 by 512 feet), and its outer wall of travertine rises 48.5 metres (159 feet) in four storeys, the lower three open arcades in Doric, Ionic and Corinthian order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lowest arcade has 80 arches, four of them unnumbered main entrances at the ends of the axes and 76 numbered on the façade, and sockets round the top held the masts of the awnings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arena measured 86 by 54 metres (282 by 177 feet) and had a wooden floor over substructures holding animal dens and lifts, with a fence and a raised podium between it and the spectators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Seating went by rank: marble chairs on the podium for senators and magistrates, tiers of stone above them, wooden seats for women under a colonnade, and standing room on the roof for the poorest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A late Roman catalogue credits the building with 87,000 <i>loca</i>, a measure of seating rather than a count of people, and the seats themselves have been reckoned at no more than 45,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","technology","art","rome","classical"],"sources":["Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Amphitheatrum Flavium,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 6-11, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Amphitheatrum_Flavium.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Vespasianus</i> 9, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Vespasian*.html#9. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Titus</i> 7, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Titus*.html#7. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 66.25, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/66*.html#25. [Open access]","Philip Smith, \"Amphitheatrum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 82-90, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Amphitheatrum.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Colosseum_in_Rome%2C_Italy_-_April_2007.jpg/1920px-Colosseum_in_Rome%2C_Italy_-_April_2007.jpg","title":"The Colosseum at dusk","desc":"The surviving stretch of the outer wall, standing to its full four storeys at the left: three tiers of open arcades with engaged half-columns between the arches, and above them the solid attic that carried the masts of the awnings. To the right the wall breaks off and only the inner structure remains.","credit":"Diliff, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colosseum_in_Rome,_Italy_-_April_2007.jpg","alt":"A vast elliptical stone amphitheatre lit gold against a deep blue evening sky, its outer wall rising in three rows of arches under a solid upper storey at one end and broken away at the other."}},
{"id":"wh-363","num":363,"category":"Rome","question":"Gaius lists the sources of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as statutes, plebiscites, senatusconsults, imperial constitutions, edicts of the magistrates who could issue them, and the opinions of jurists.","questions":["Each praetor published an edict on taking office, and because his successors adopted most of it the edict became a standing part of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Hadrian had Salvius Julianus arrange by subject.","Justinian set a commission of three to make an elementary book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and their <i>Institutes</i> appeared in 533 with the <i>Digest</i>, outside which the older jurists no longer had authority."],"answer":"Roman law","answerText":"Roman law","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sources listed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Gaius, 2nd century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edict fixed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Hadrian, 117-138 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Codified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Digest and Institutes, 533 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Roman law</b> is the law of the Roman state and the jurisprudence built on it, which Ulpian introduces by quoting Celsus: law is the art of knowing what is good and just.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ulpian divides it into public law, which has reference to the Roman state, and private law, which concerns the advantage of the individual citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gaius, writing in the 2nd century CE, lists its sources as statutes, plebiscites, senatusconsults, constitutions of the emperors, edicts of the magistrates authorised to issue them, and the opinions of jurists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justinian's <i>Institutes</i> repeats the same six four centuries later and adds the unwritten law, since ancient custom approved by the consent of those who follow it is like a statute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A statute was an enactment of the whole people and a plebiscite of the commonalty alone, until the <i>lex Hortensia</i> gave plebiscites the force of statutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Each praetor issued an edict on entering office setting out how he would exercise his jurisdiction, and it bound only for his year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> But magistrates adopted most of what their predecessors had published, so the edict grew into a standing body of law, and Hadrian had the jurist Salvius Julianus compile it and arrange it by subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jurists gave opinions on points of law, and where they were unanimous the judge was forbidden to depart from them, while Hadrian ruled that where they disagreed he might follow whichever he chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What a state lays down for its own members is its <i>ius civile</i>, and what natural reason establishes among all peoples is the <i>ius gentium</i>, and Roman law was reckoned to be partly each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Justinian set Tribonian, Theophilus and Dorotheus to make an elementary book of it, and their <i>Institutes</i> appeared in 533 with the <i>Digest</i>, outside which the older jurists no longer had authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","rome","classical"],"sources":["Gaius, <i>Institutes</i> 1.1-7, trans. Edward Poste, 4th ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1904), https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Anglica/gai1_Poste.htm. [Open access]","<i>The Digest or Pandects</i> 1.1, trans. Samuel Parsons Scott, <i>The Civil Law</i>, vol. 2 (Cincinnati: Central Trust Company, 1932), https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Anglica/D1_Scott.htm. [Open access]","Justinian, <i>Institutes</i> 1.1-2, trans. John Baron Moyle, 5th ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1913), https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Anglica/just1_Moyle.htm. [Open access]","George Long, \"Edictum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 444-446, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Edictum.html. [Open access]","George Long, \"Institutiones,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 639-640, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Institutiones.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Lex_de_auctoritate_Vespasiani.JPG/1920px-Lex_de_auctoritate_Vespasiani.JPG","title":"The Lex de imperio Vespasiani","desc":"A Roman statute cut into bronze, conferring powers on the emperor Vespasian, now in the Capitoline Museums. The clause headed SANCTIO near the foot sets out what follows if the law is broken, and the small panel above records the tablet's return to the Capitol in a later age.","credit":"Libens libenter, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lex_de_auctoritate_Vespasiani.JPG","alt":"A large dark bronze tablet set into a marble wall, covered in close lines of incised Latin capitals, with the word SANCTIO in larger letters near the bottom."}},
{"id":"wh-364","num":364,"category":"Rome","question":"Ulpian's complete <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried both the <i>commercium</i> and the <i>connubium</i>, where a <i>Latinus</i> had the first without the second and a <i>peregrinus</i> had neither of them.","questions":["The senate admitted the loyal Italians to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the Social War, then enrolled them in ten new tribes that voted last, so a majority was often reached first.","Caracalla made all the people in his empire share in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Dio says honoured them in name only, the real purpose being the taxes that aliens had not paid."],"answer":"Roman citizenship","answerText":"Roman citizenship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">To all Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the lex Julia, 90 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Latin right</span><span class=\"dt-v\">lex Pompeia, c. 89 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Roman citizenship</b> was the status carrying the full rights of a member of the Roman state, and Ulpian's <i>civis</i> is the man who possesses those rights complete.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its private side was the <i>commercium</i> and the <i>connubium</i>: the power to acquire property by Roman title, to make and take under a Roman will, and to contract a marriage the law recognised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Free persons within the Roman world fell into three classes, <i>cives</i>, <i>Latini</i> and <i>peregrini</i>; a <i>Latinus</i> had the commercium but not the connubium, and a <i>peregrinus</i> had neither.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaius divides men first into free and slave, the free into freeborn and freed, and freedmen again into Roman citizens, <i>Latini</i>, and men on the footing of enemies surrendered at discretion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Which class a freed slave entered depended on how he had been held and freed, the <i>lex Aelia Sentia</i> closing the best of the three to any who had been chained, branded or sent to the arena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>ius Latii</i> was the status intermediate between the other two and was given to whole towns and countries, by Vespasian to the whole of Hispania and by Hadrian to many cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A <i>lex Pompeia</i> gave it to the Transpadani about 89 BCE, with the further privilege of obtaining the Roman citizenship by having held a magistracy in their own city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Social War ended with a <i>lex Julia</i> of 90 BCE extending the citizenship to all Italy, and a <i>lex Plautia Papiria</i> the next year admitting those enrolled in allied towns who were living in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian says the senate admitted the loyal Italians but enrolled them in ten new tribes that voted last, so a majority was often reached before their vote counted, which they noticed only later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Caracalla at last made all the people in his empire citizens, which Dio says honoured them in name only, the real purpose being revenue, since aliens paid few of the taxes he had raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","rome","classical"],"sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Civitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 288-293, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Civitas.html. [Open access]","Gaius, <i>Institutes</i> 1.9-13, trans. Edward Poste, 4th ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1904), https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Anglica/gai1_Poste.htm. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.49, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html#49. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 78.9, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/78*.html#9. [Open access]","George Long, \"Latinitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 669-670, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Latinitas.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Bronze_military_diploma_MET_DP105604.jpg/1920px-Bronze_military_diploma_MET_DP105604.jpg","title":"A soldier's bronze diploma","desc":"One leaf of a discharge certificate of about 149 CE, issued to men who had served 25 years in four cavalry regiments and seven cohorts in Upper Pannonia. The text records that the emperor gave them the Roman citizenship, to those of them who did not have it, and the right of marriage with the wives they then had.","credit":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_military_diploma_MET_DP105604.jpg","alt":"A small rectangular bronze plate, green with corrosion and pierced by two holes, engraved with eleven lines of abbreviated Latin capitals."}},
{"id":"wh-365","num":365,"category":"Rome","question":"Strabo says the Oscans, then the Tyrrhenians and Pelasgians, then the Samnites held <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which stood on the Sarno and served as the port of Nola, Nuceria and Acerrae.","questions":["The cloud that rose while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was buried looked to Pliny like a pine tree, a tall trunk spreading at the top into branches and dark where it carried more earth.","Dio says the ash buried Herculaneum and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the second while its people sat in the theatre, and that at Rome it filled the air and darkened the sun."],"answer":"Pompeii","answerText":"Pompeii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 August 79 CE, per Pliny</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pompeii</b> was a town of Campania standing on the river Sarno below Vesuvius, buried with its neighbour Herculaneum when the mountain erupted in 79 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strabo says it had been held first by the Oscans, then by the Tyrrhenians and Pelasgians, then by the Samnites, and that every one of them was driven out in its turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sarno both carried cargoes inland and sent them out to sea, so the town served as the port of Nola, Nuceria and Acerrae, the inland cities behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo describes Vesuvius as ringed with dwellings and beautiful farmland, its flat summit barren and ash-coloured with rocks that looked eaten out by fire, and infers from them that the district had once been alight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Nero an earthquake demolished a large part of the town, which Tacitus calls populous, and Pliny says the ground had been trembling for days before the end without much alarming anyone in Campania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pliny the Younger, then 18 and at Misenum across the bay, dates the eruption to 24 August, when his mother pointed out a cloud of unusual size and shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He likens it to a pine tree, shooting up on a tall trunk and spreading at the top into branches, bright in places and elsewhere dark and spotted as it carried more or less earth and cinders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His uncle sailed towards the coast to take people off, dined and slept at Stabiae while pumice piled against the door, and died on the shore, his body found when light returned on the third day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Misenum the darkness came down like a shut room with the lights put out, and Pliny heard people crying that there were no gods left and that the last endless night had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dio says the ash buried both cities, Pompeii while its people sat in the theatre, and that at Rome it darkened the sun; Titus turned the estates of the heirless dead to rebuilding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["city","history","geography","archaeology","rome","classical"],"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.4.8, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5D*.html. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.22, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0078%3Abook%3D15%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Pliny the Younger, <i>Letters</i> 6.16 and 6.20, trans. William Melmoth, rev. F. C. T. Bosanquet (London: George Bell and Sons, 1900), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2811/pg2811.txt. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 66.21-23, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/66*.html#21. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Divus Titus</i> 8, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Titus*.html#8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Forum_in_Pompeii_3.jpg/1920px-Forum_in_Pompeii_3.jpg","title":"The forum, with Vesuvius behind","desc":"The colonnade along the forum, the town's public square, seen from its southeast corner with the mountain that buried it standing at the head of the view. Strabo, writing before the eruption, had noticed that the summit looked burnt and concluded the district must once have been on fire.","credit":"Wknight94, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forum_in_Pompeii_3.jpg","alt":"A row of tall stone columns carrying an architrave, running away into the distance beside an open grassy square, with a broad grey mountain rising on the horizon beyond."}},
{"id":"wh-366","num":366,"category":"Rome","question":"Tacitus writes that the great fire may have been accidental or contrived by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since authors have given both accounts, and that a rumour of his singing went out everywhere.","questions":["Suetonius opens his life of Galba by saying that the imperial line came to an end with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Golden House held a colossal statue of himself 120 Roman feet high.","To be rid of the rumour that he had burned Rome, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fastened the guilt on the Christians, and even Tacitus records the pity their punishment aroused."],"answer":"Nero","answerText":"Nero","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">54-68 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great fire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 days and 7 nights</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in his 32nd year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nero</b> was the last of the Julio-Claudian emperors, whose line Suetonius says came to an end with him at the head of his life of Galba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dio says Poppaea urged him to be rid of his mother Agrippina, that Seneca too is said to have pressed it, and that a stage ship which parted and closed again was copied for the purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He performed in public as a singer to the lyre, opening with \"My lords, of your kindness give me ear\" while soldiers stood by and every seat was filled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dio says the old families were made to sing, to drive teams, to kill wild beasts and to fight as gladiators, some willingly and some much against their will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His Golden House had a vestibule large enough for a colossal statue of himself 120 Roman feet high, and he said that in it he could at last begin to live like a human being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The great fire began beside the circus among shops of inflammable wares, and Tacitus writes that whether it was accidental or contrived by the emperor is uncertain, since authors have given both accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the city's 14 districts four came through untouched, three were levelled and seven were left with a few shattered, half-burnt relics of houses, while Dio puts the loss at nearly two-thirds of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tacitus reports as a rumour gone out everywhere that Nero sang of the destruction of Troy on a private stage while the city burned; Suetonius states it flatly, in stage costume, from the tower of Maecenas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To be rid of that rumour he fastened the guilt on the Christians, and Tacitus, no friend to them, records that pity arose because they were destroyed not for the public good but to glut one man's cruelty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Declared a public enemy by the senate, he had a grave dug in his presence and wept, saying again and again what an artist the world was losing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Suetonius, <i>Nero</i> 31, 38, 49, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Nero*.html. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.38-40, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0078%3Abook%3D15%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>Annals</i> 15.44, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (New York: Random House, 1942), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0078%3Abook%3D15%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 62.12-18, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/62*.html. [Open access]","Suetonius, <i>Galba</i> 1, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Galba*.html#1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Nero_Musei_Capitolini_MC2825.jpg/1280px-Nero_Musei_Capitolini_MC2825.jpg","title":"A bronze face of Nero","desc":"A fragment of a bronze portrait made in the first years of the reign, in the Capitoline collections at the Centrale Montemartini. The hair and the bust that belong with it are in Baltimore, so what stands here is the face on its own.","credit":"Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nero_Musei_Capitolini_MC2825.jpg","alt":"A broken bronze face, green and pitted with corrosion, its eye sockets hollow and the top of the head missing, mounted on a stand against a dark ground."}},
{"id":"wh-367","num":367,"category":"Rome","question":"Dio says Decebalus took fright at the approach of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, knowing that on the earlier occasion he had beaten not the Romans but Domitian, and that now he faced both.","questions":["Asked by Pliny what to do about the Christians of Bithynia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replied that no general rule could be laid down and that anonymous informations were foreign to the spirit of the age.","The <i>Digest</i> quotes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the rule that nobody should be convicted on suspicion, since it is better that a guilty man go unpunished than an innocent one be condemned."],"answer":"Trajan","answerText":"Trajan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">98-117 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Per Dio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 years, 6 months, 15 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Trajan</b> was the Roman emperor under whom the empire reached its furthest extent, conquering Dacia and pushing east beyond the Euphrates, ground his successor gave up almost at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dio says Decebalus was frightened at his approach, knowing well that on the earlier occasion it was not the Romans he had beaten but Domitian, and that now he faced both Romans and Trajan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dio admires above all the stone bridge he threw over the Danube, 20 piers of squared stone 150 Roman feet high above their foundations and 60 wide, standing 170 feet apart and joined by arches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each pier had to be set in a river so deep, so full of eddies and so muddy that the stream could not be turned aside while the work went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reaching the Persian Gulf, he said he would certainly have crossed over to the Indi had he still been young, and he counted Alexander a lucky man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Digest</i> quotes his rescripts for the rule that nobody absent should be convicted, and nobody on suspicion, since it is better that a guilty man go unpunished than an innocent one be condemned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Governing Bithynia, the younger Pliny wrote to ask what to do about the Christians, not knowing whether the profession itself was punishable or only the crimes supposed to go with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He answered that no general rule could be laid down, that Pliny should not go out of his way to look for them, and that anonymous informations were foreign to the spirit of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Asked for a fire brigade at Nicomedia he refused, saying such societies form factious assemblies whatever they are called, and that engines and the help of the populace would be safer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His death was hidden some days so Hadrian's adoption could be announced first, the letters to the senate signed by Plotina; the nations he had conquered revolted, and Hadrian let the eastern conquests go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 68.6-33, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/68*.html. [Open access]","Pliny the Younger, <i>Letters</i> 10.33-34 and 10.96-97, trans. William Melmoth, rev. F. C. T. Bosanquet (London: George Bell and Sons, 1900), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2811/pg2811.txt. [Open access]","<i>The Digest or Pandects</i> 48.19.5, trans. Samuel Parsons Scott, <i>The Civil Law</i>, vol. 11 (Cincinnati: Central Trust Company, 1932), https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Anglica/D48_Scott.htm. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>Hadrian</i> 5, 9, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Hadrian/1*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 69.1-2, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/69*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/011_Conrad_Cichorius%2C_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule%2C_Tafel_XI.jpg/1920px-011_Conrad_Cichorius%2C_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule%2C_Tafel_XI.jpg","title":"The Dacian war on Trajan's Column","desc":"A plate from Conrad Cichorius's 1896 photographic survey of the column's spiral relief, taken from casts. Legionaries in segmented armour raise a wall of squared stone while others carry baskets of earth and cut timber; a group of officers stands at the left. Much of the column shows the army working rather than fighting.","credit":"Conrad Cichorius, Die Reliefs der Traianssaule, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:011_Conrad_Cichorius%2C_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule%2C_Tafel_XI.jpg","alt":"A long carved stone relief in shallow brown tones showing Roman soldiers in armour building a wall of squared blocks, carrying baskets and handling timber, with officers standing apart at one end."}},
{"id":"wh-368","num":368,"category":"Rome","question":"A dedication found at Jarrow says the necessity of keeping the empire within its limits was laid on Hadrian by divine precept, and that he then added <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between either shore of the Ocean.","questions":["At Carvoran a stone records that the century of Silvanus built 112 Roman feet of the rampart of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Flavius Secundus, the prefect, which is how the whole work was parcelled out.","Germanic soldiers calling themselves tribesmen of Twenthe raised a pillar at Housesteads on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Mars Thincsus and the two Alaisiagae, alongside the Divinity of the Emperor."],"answer":"Hadrian's Wall","answerText":"Hadrian's Wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from about 122 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">80 Roman miles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One stint</span><span class=\"dt-v\">112 Roman feet of rampart</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hadrian's Wall</b> was the frontier work the Romans drew across the north of the province of Britannia, and both the stones and the literary record give its length as 80 Roman miles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A dedication found at Jarrow says that the necessity of keeping the empire within its limits had been laid upon Hadrian himself by divine precept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same stone goes on that, the barbarians having been dispersed and the province recovered, he added a frontier line running between either shore of the Ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ends by recording that the army of the province built this defence-work under the charge of Aulus Platorius Nepos, the emperor's propraetorian legate in the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The literary account is blunter and shorter, saying only that Hadrian was the first to build a wall, 80 miles in length, to separate the barbarians from the Romans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A slab from milecastle 38 names the Second Augustan Legion as the builders, again under Platorius Nepos, and is dated on the governor's term to about 122 to 126.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Smaller stones record far smaller stints, and one from Carvoran says that the century of Silvanus built 112 Roman feet of rampart under Flavius Secundus, the prefect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between them the two show how the work was parcelled out, a legion to a milecastle and a century to a hundred feet, and signed wherever it was finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The men who then stood on it had often come a long way: at Housesteads a pillar was raised to the god Mars Thincsus and the two Alaisiagae, Beda and Fimmilena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was set up by Germanic soldiers who call themselves tribesmen of Twenthe, and they honoured those gods and the Divinity of the Emperor in the same breath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","warfare","geography","rome","classical"],"sources":["RIB 1051, \"Imperial dedication to Hadrian,\" in <i>Roman Inscriptions of Britain</i>, ed. R. G. Collingwood and R. P. Wright, online ed., https://romaninscriptionsofbritain.org/inscriptions/1051. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>Hadrian</i> 11, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Hadrian/1*.html. [Open access]","RIB 1638, \"Dedication to Emperor Hadrian,\" in <i>Roman Inscriptions of Britain</i>, ed. R. G. Collingwood and R. P. Wright, online ed., https://romaninscriptionsofbritain.org/inscriptions/1638. [Open access]","RIB 1820, \"Centurial stone of Silvanus,\" in <i>Roman Inscriptions of Britain</i>, ed. R. G. Collingwood and R. P. Wright, online ed., https://romaninscriptionsofbritain.org/inscriptions/1820. [Open access]","RIB 1593, \"Altar dedicated to Mars Thincsus, the Alaisiagae, and the Divinity of the Emperor,\" in <i>Roman Inscriptions of Britain</i>, ed. R. G. Collingwood and R. P. Wright, online ed., https://romaninscriptionsofbritain.org/inscriptions/1593. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Hadrians_Wall_from_Housesteads1_crop.jpg/1280px-Hadrians_Wall_from_Housesteads1_crop.jpg","title":"The wall near Housesteads","desc":"A stretch of the wall seen from its own top, running down across open grazing and up again towards a wood. What survives is the core and the lower courses; the ground either side is empty, which is what the line looked like to anyone approaching it from the north.","credit":"Jamesflomonosoff, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadrians_Wall_from_Housesteads1_crop.jpg","alt":"A low stone wall a few courses high running away across green pasture, dipping into a hollow and rising again towards a line of trees under a grey sky."}},
{"id":"wh-369","num":369,"category":"Rome","question":"The <i>Augustan History</i> says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was devoted to philosophy as long as he lived and kept attending the Stoic Apollonius for instruction even after entering the imperial family.","questions":["The notebook <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept for himself was written not in Latin but in the language of the Greeks, under a title meaning simply 'to himself'.","Dio judges that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not meet the good fortune he deserved, being frail and beset by troubles, and admires him the more for bringing the empire through them."],"answer":"Marcus Aurelius","answerText":"Marcus Aurelius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">161-180 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Meditations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 books</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Marcus Aurelius</b> was a Roman emperor who is remembered less for what he did than for a notebook he kept for himself and never meant anyone else to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Augustan History</i> says he was devoted to philosophy for as long as he lived, and that even after entering the imperial family he went on attending the Stoic Apollonius for instruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He wrote the notebook not in Latin but in the language of the Greeks, and it comes down under a title that means simply 'to himself'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its second book opens by telling him what to say at daybreak: that he will meet the busy-body, the thankless, the bully, the treacherous, the envious and the unneighbourly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The answer he gives himself is that they act so from not knowing good from evil, that the wrong-doer is his kinsman in mind if not in blood, and that people are made to work together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Almost none of the reign was quiet, and the war on the Danube had been put off for years so that Rome could finish the war in the east first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A pestilence came home with the returning army, and the <i>Augustan History</i> says the dead were carried out in carts and waggons and that thousands died, many of them nobles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before leaving for the front he summoned priests from every side, performed foreign rites and purified the city, and was delayed by the ceremonies from setting out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His rulings are quoted in the <i>Digest</i>, among them a decree that a man who thinks he has a claim should sue for it, and that the other party keep possession meanwhile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dio judges that he did not meet the good fortune he deserved, being frail and beset by troubles nearly all his reign, and admires him the more for having brought the empire through them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","person","history","philosophy","politics","rome","classical"],"quote":{"book":"marcus-aurelius-meditations","n":"2","text":"Say to thyself at daybreak: I shall come across the busy-body, the thankless, the bully, the treacherous, the envious, the unneighbourly. All this has befallen them because they know not good from evil. … For we have come into being for co-operation, as have the feet, the hands, the eyelids, the rows of upper and lower teeth. Therefore to thwart one another is against Nature; and we do thwart one another by shewing resentment and aversion.","cite":"Meditations 2.1, trans. C. R. Haines"},"sources":["Marcus Aurelius, <i>The Communings with Himself</i> 2.1, trans. Charles Reginald Haines, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1916), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Marcus_Aurelius_%28Haines_1916%29. [Open access]","Marcus Aurelius, <i>Ta eis heauton</i>, ed. Johannes Leopold (Leipzig: Teubner, 1908), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A2008.01.0641. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>Marcus Antoninus</i> 2, 12-13, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Marcus_Aurelius/1*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 72.36, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/72*.html. [Open access]","<i>The Digest or Pandects</i> 48, trans. Samuel Parsons Scott, <i>The Civil Law</i>, vol. 11 (Cincinnati: Central Trust Company, 1932), https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Anglica/D48_Scott.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Rome_Capitoline_Marcus_Aurelius_statue_original.jpg/1920px-Rome_Capitoline_Marcus_Aurelius_statue_original.jpg","title":"The bronze equestrian statue","desc":"The original bronze, now indoors in the Capitoline Museums with a copy standing outside in its place. The emperor rides with one hand raised and no weapon, and patches of the gilding that once covered the whole group survive on the drapery and on the horse's harness.","credit":"Nicholas Hartmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rome_Capitoline_Marcus_Aurelius_statue_original.jpg","alt":"A larger-than-life bronze statue of a bearded man in a cloak on horseback, his right arm raised and open, with gold showing through the green weathering on his robe and the horse's bridle."}},
{"id":"wh-370","num":370,"category":"Rome","question":"The <i>Augustan History</i> says that during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the armies began to range about on all sides and the generals in every province to murmur, after Valerian was taken alive by Sapor.","questions":["A milestone in Britannia set up for Marcus Cassianius Latinus Postumus is hard evidence from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the western provinces had an emperor of their own for about a decade.","Aurelian brought the east out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by defeating Zenobia, then built walls round the city of Rome without extending the sacred boundary, which needed an emperor who had added territory."],"answer":"Crisis of the Third Century","answerText":"Crisis of the Third Century","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">235-284 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pretenders</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 named, 22 after the padding</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Postumus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">emperor in the west, 260-269</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Crisis of the Third Century</b> was the half-century in which the Roman empire came near to breaking apart, with emperors made and unmade by their own armies and whole regions running themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It turns on a defeat in the east, where the emperor Valerian was taken alive by Sapor, the king of Persia, and never came home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While his son Gallienus rejoiced at the news, the <i>Augustan History</i> says, the armies began to range about on all sides and the generals in every province to murmur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Greater than any of it, that account adds, was the grief of all men that a Roman emperor was being held as a slave in Persia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same collection prints letters supposedly sent to Sapor by other kings, warning him that he had not so much conquered as sown the seeds of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One of its lives is given to the men who claimed the purple, and its own arithmetic shows the strain: strike out two women and six youths who never held power and the thirty fall to 22.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of the claims were solid enough to be cut in stone, and a milestone in Britannia salutes Marcus Cassianius Latinus Postumus as emperor and as our lord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is dated by his offices to between 260 and 269, so for about a decade the western provinces had an emperor of their own and said so on the public roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The east came back first, when Aurelian defeated Zenobia, who was wielding the imperial power in the name of her sons, and reduced that half to its former state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also built the walls of the city of Rome, and did not extend the sacred boundary, since only an emperor who had added foreign territory to the empire might do that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>The Two Valerians</i>, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Valeriani_Duo*.html. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>The Two Gallieni</i>, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Gallieni_duo*.html. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>The Thirty Pretenders</i>, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Tyranni_XXX*.html. [Open access]","RIB 2232, \"Milestone of Postumus,\" in <i>Roman Inscriptions of Britain</i>, ed. R. G. Collingwood and R. P. Wright, online ed., https://romaninscriptionsofbritain.org/inscriptions/2232. [Open access]","<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>Aurelian</i> 21, 25-26, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Aurelian/2*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Aurelian_Walls_-_Porta_Asinaria.jpg/1920px-Aurelian_Walls_-_Porta_Asinaria.jpg","title":"The walls Aurelian built round Rome","desc":"A stretch of the circuit at the Porta Asinaria, brick-faced and battlemented, with the two round towers that flank the gate. The capital of an empire that had long been defended at its frontiers was given a wall of its own.","credit":"MrPanyGoff, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurelian_Walls_-_Porta_Asinaria.jpg","alt":"A long brick city wall with a battlemented top and blocked arches along it, meeting a gateway flanked by two tall round towers, under a clear blue sky."}},
{"id":"wh-371","num":371,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"After the army hailed him emperor in 284 CE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> split the rule of Rome between four men, redrew its provinces and then, uniquely among Roman emperors, resigned the throne while still alive.","questions":["Lactantius blamed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for an empire quartered between four courts, in which more men drew wages from the state than paid taxes into it.","In March 303 CE churches were levelled and scriptures burnt across the empire on the orders of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the emperor who had already broken the provinces into far smaller units."],"answer":"Diocletian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">284-305 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abdicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 305 CE, at Nicomedia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persecution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">began March 303 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Diocletian</b> was commanding the household troops when the army hailed him emperor in 284 CE, and his answer to the disorder of the preceding decades was to divide the government of the empire rather than concentrate it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Augustan History puts his acclamation after Numerian's death, and has him climb the tribunal and run the praetorian prefect Aper through with his own sword.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rather than rule alone he shared the work out, taking Maximian as a fellow emperor and adding Galerius and Constantius beneath the two of them, so that there were two <i>Augusti</i> and two <i>Caesares</i> at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The provinces were broken into much smaller units under many more governors, and the army and the administration both grew to match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lactantius, a Christian writer who loathed him, complained that there were now fewer men paying taxes than drawing wages, and that farms went out of cultivation as the burden rose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> When prices ran away he issued an edict fixing a maximum for goods and wages, which Lactantius says caused blood to be shed over trifles, frightened sellers out of the market and was abandoned as unworkable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He built on a scale to match, raising Nicomedia towards the magnificence of Rome and putting up baths in the capital whose dedication names all six of his rulers together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In March 303 CE he ordered the churches levelled everywhere and the scriptures burnt, and Lactantius and Eusebius, who wrote separately, describe the same edicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Then on 1 May 305 CE he did what no Roman emperor had done before, standing in tears before the troops outside Nicomedia to say he was worn out and wanted rest, and laying the throne down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Galerius, who had pressed him to go, had already named the flaw in the whole arrangement: harmony is easily kept between two equals, and never among four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Diocletian","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["<i>Historia Augusta</i>, <i>Carus, Carinus and Numerian</i> 13, 18, trans. David Magie, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Historia_Augusta/Carus_et_al*.html. [Open access]","Lactantius, <i>Of the Manner in Which the Persecutors Died</i> 7, trans. William Fletcher, in <i>Ante-Nicene Fathers</i>, vol. 7, ed. Alexander Roberts, James Donaldson and A. Cleveland Coxe (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1886), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0705.htm. [Open access]","Eusebius, <i>Church History</i> 8.2, trans. Arthur Cushman McGiffert, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1890), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/250108.htm. [Open access]","Lactantius, <i>Of the Manner in Which the Persecutors Died</i> 12, 18-19, trans. William Fletcher, in <i>Ante-Nicene Fathers</i>, vol. 7, ed. Alexander Roberts, James Donaldson and A. Cleveland Coxe (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1886), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0705.htm. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Thermae Diocletiani,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 527-30, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Thermae_Diocletiani.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Portrait_of_the_Four_Tetrarchs%2C_a_porphyry_sculpture_sacked_from_the_Byzantine_Philadelphion_palace_in_1204%2C_Treasury_of_St._Marks%2C_Venice_%2819896369591%29.jpg","title":"The four tetrarchs","desc":"A porphyry group of four rulers embracing in pairs, carved in the 4th century and set into a corner of St Mark's in Venice after being taken from Constantinople in 1204. It is conventionally read as Diocletian's college of two senior and two junior emperors.","alt":"Four stone figures in military dress, each with an arm round the shoulders of the one beside him, carved as two pairs against a wall.","credit":"Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_the_Four_Tetrarchs%2C_a_porphyry_sculpture_sacked_from_the_Byzantine_Philadelphion_palace_in_1204%2C_Treasury_of_St._Marks%2C_Venice_%2819896369591%29.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-372","num":372,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"In 312 CE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> won the battle at the Milvian Bridge, and both the Christian writers who describe it say a sign from heaven told him how to fight.","questions":["The letter from Milan that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> issued with Licinius gave Christians liberty to worship, and in the same breath gave that liberty to everyone else as well.","Eusebius says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reigned 32 years, put off his baptism until he was dying near Nicomedia, and gave his own name to the new capital he built."],"answer":"Constantine the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Caesar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">306-307 CE, on a milestone</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Milvian Bridge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">312 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arch in Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 315-316 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">32 years, per Eusebius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Constantine the Great</b> took power in 306 CE on his father's death, and a granite milestone from Cornwall cut within the year styles him <i>nobilissimo Caesari</i>, son of the deified Constantius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the junior imperial rank rather than the throne, though Lactantius says his father commended him to the soldiers and put the sovereign power into his hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six years later he defeated his rival Maxentius at the Milvian Bridge outside Rome, where the bridge behind the beaten army gave way and Maxentius was driven into the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lactantius says he was told in a dream the night before to mark his soldiers' shields with the monogram of Christ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eusebius tells it otherwise, with a cross of light above the midday sun bearing the words 'Conquer by this', the dream coming only after it, and says he had the story from the emperor himself on oath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The senate raised the arch that still stands beside the Colosseum for that victory, and it was finished about 315 or 316 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the letter he and Licinius issued from Milan, which Lactantius quotes entire, Christians were given liberty to follow their own worship undisturbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same document grants that liberty to everyone else in the same breath, so it opens the empire to all religions rather than making Christianity the religion of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He gave his own name to a new capital, filling it with churches, forbidding sacrifice there, and carrying the old gods' statues out of their temples to stand as ornaments in its hippodrome and palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Eusebius says he reigned 32 years all but a few months, was baptised only at the very end near Nicomedia after hoping for the river where Christ was baptised, and died at noon on Pentecost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Constantine the Great","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","religion","rome","classical"],"sources":["RIB 2233, \"Milestone of Constantine I,\" in <i>Roman Inscriptions of Britain</i>, ed. R. G. Collingwood and R. P. Wright, online ed., https://romaninscriptionsofbritain.org/inscriptions/2233. [Open access]","Lactantius, <i>Of the Manner in Which the Persecutors Died</i> 24, 44, trans. William Fletcher, in <i>Ante-Nicene Fathers</i>, vol. 7, ed. Alexander Roberts, James Donaldson and A. Cleveland Coxe (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1886), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0705.htm. [Open access]","Eusebius, <i>Life of Constantine</i> 1.28-31, trans. Ernest Cushing Richardson, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1890), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/25021.htm. [Open access]","Samuel Ball Platner and Thomas Ashby, \"Arcus Constantini,\" in <i>A Topographical Dictionary of Ancient Rome</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1929), 36-38, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Gazetteer/Places/Europe/Italy/Lazio/Roma/Rome/_Texts/PLATOP*/Arcus_Constantini.html. [Open access]","Lactantius, <i>Of the Manner in Which the Persecutors Died</i> 48, trans. William Fletcher, in <i>Ante-Nicene Fathers</i>, vol. 7, ed. Alexander Roberts, James Donaldson and A. Cleveland Coxe (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1886), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0705.htm. [Open access]","Eusebius, <i>Life of Constantine</i> 3.48, 54, trans. Ernest Cushing Richardson, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1890), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/25023.htm. [Open access]","Eusebius, <i>Life of Constantine</i> 4.53, 62-64, trans. Ernest Cushing Richardson, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1890), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/25024.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Arch_of_Constantine_%28Rome%29_-_South_side%2C_from_Via_triumphalis.jpg","title":"The Arch of Constantine","desc":"The arch the senate raised beside the Colosseum for the victory over Maxentius. The inscriptions over its side arches mention the tenth anniversary of the reign, which is what dates the finished monument to about 315 or 316 CE.","alt":"A three-arched Roman triumphal arch in marble, seen head on, with sculpted roundels and a long inscription across its attic.","credit":"Paris Orlando, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arch_of_Constantine_%28Rome%29_-_South_side%2C_from_Via_triumphalis.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-373","num":373,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Pliny wrote to Trajan for guidance because he had never tried a case of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and did not know whether the name itself was the crime, or whether repentance earned a pardon.","questions":["Trajan's rule that followers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not to be hunted out, but punished if brought and proved, was still being applied at Lyons two generations later.","An edict of 380 CE ordered every people under Roman rule to hold <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the form Peter was said to have given the Romans, and judged everyone else a heretic."],"answer":"Christianity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Lyons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17th year of Antoninus Verus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made official</span><span class=\"dt-v\">380 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Christianity</b> first enters the Roman record as a policing matter, Suetonius listing Nero's punishment of the Christians between a cap on spending and a ban on cooked food in taverns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls them a class of men given to a new and mischievous superstition, which is the whole of what a Roman biographer thought worth saying about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pliny, governing Bithynia two generations later, wrote to Trajan that he had never attended such a trial and did not know whether the name itself was the crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He put the question three times and punished those who held to it, judging that obstinacy alone deserved correction, and sent Roman citizens away for trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the accused confessed to was meeting before dawn on a fixed day, singing to Christ as to a god, swearing not to steal or cheat or break faith, and eating an ordinary meal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Trajan answered that no general rule could be laid down, that they were not to be hunted out, and that anonymous accusations had no place and were foreign to the spirit of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That rule held: the churches of Lyons and Vienne described a persecution in which the emperor ordered the steadfast killed and those who denied released, citizens beheaded and the rest sent to the beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pliny had already reported the temples almost deserted and the sacrificial victims going unsold, which is a Roman governor's own measure of how far it had spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tertullian, addressing provincial governors, complained that a flood or a famine brought the cry 'away with the Christians to the lion', and answered that the blood of Christians is seed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 380 CE an edict of Gratian, Valentinian and Theodosius ordered every people under their rule to hold the faith Peter gave the Romans, and judged everyone else a heretic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Christianity","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","religion","history","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Suetonius, <i>Nero</i> 16, trans. John Carew Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Nero*.html. [Open access]","Pliny the Younger, <i>Letters</i> 10.96-97, numbered XCVII-XCVIII in this edition, trans. William Melmoth, ed. F. C. T. Bosanquet, Project Gutenberg ebook 2811, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2811/pg2811.txt. [Open access]","Eusebius, <i>Church History</i> 5, introduction and 5.1, trans. Arthur Cushman McGiffert, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1890), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/250105.htm. [Open access]","Tertullian, <i>Apology</i> 37, 40, 50, trans. S. Thelwall, in <i>Ante-Nicene Fathers</i>, vol. 3, ed. Alexander Roberts, James Donaldson and A. Cleveland Coxe (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1885), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0301.htm. [Open access]","<i>Codex Theodosianus</i> 16.1.2, ed. Theodor Mommsen and Paul M. Meyer (Berlin: Weidmann, 1905), in <i>The Roman Law Library</i>, ed. Yves Lassard and Alexandr Koptev, https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Constitutiones/CTh16.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Roman_Britain_-_Image_of_Christ_from_Hinton_St_Mary_%28central_roundel_of_a_4th_century_AD_mosaic_floor%29_-_British_Museum.jpg","title":"A monogram in a villa floor","desc":"The central roundel of a mosaic floor from a Roman villa at Hinton St Mary, laid in the 4th century CE. The chi-rho monogram stands behind the head, and the bust is usually read as Christ.","alt":"A circular Roman floor mosaic showing a robed bust facing forward, a monogram of two overlapping letters set behind the head and pomegranates to either side.","credit":"Andres Rueda, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Britain_-_Image_of_Christ_from_Hinton_St_Mary_%28central_roundel_of_a_4th_century_AD_mosaic_floor%29_-_British_Museum.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-374","num":374,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"In 325 CE the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> settled that the Son was 'begotten, not made' and of one substance with the Father, and fixed when the eastern churches would keep Easter.","questions":["Constantine summoned bishops from every quarter of the empire to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, gave them the imperial post to travel by, and addressed them in Latin through an interpreter.","A church history of the next century says 318 bishops subscribed the creed of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that five refused because the word homoousios is nowhere in scripture."],"answer":"First Council of Nicaea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Met</span><span class=\"dt-v\">325 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bishops</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 250, per Eusebius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Subscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">318 bishops</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 passed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>First Council of Nicaea</b> met in 325 CE, called by Constantine to settle two questions that had divided the churches: what to say about Arius, and when to keep Easter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eusebius says the emperor summoned bishops from every quarter by letter, put the public conveyance at their disposal and supplied transport for the rest, and chose Nicaea in Bithynia for the meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They came from across the empire, with one bishop from Persia and one from Scythia among them, and Eusebius counts more than 250 of them in the hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Constantine came in person, addressed them in Latin through an interpreter, then sat and listened while the two sides accused one another and a violent controversy broke out at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dispute was over Arius, who taught that the Son was made, that he came from things that are not, and that there was a time when he was not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The council's own letter to Alexandria reports that his opinion was anathematised unanimously, and that the churches of the east which had followed the Jewish reckoning would henceforth keep Easter with everyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The creed it issued called the Son 'begotten, not made', of the substance of the Father, and <i>homoousios</i>, of one substance with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A church history written a century afterwards says 318 bishops subscribed, and that five would not, objecting to that word, which is nowhere in scripture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eusebius of Caesarea wrote home to his own church to explain that he had signed once the sense had been scrutinised, 'peace being the object which we set before us'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The council also passed twenty canons of ordinary church business, ending with a rule that on Sundays and through Pentecost prayer should be made standing rather than kneeling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"First Council of Nicaea","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","religion","politics","rome","classical"],"sources":["Eusebius, <i>Life of Constantine</i> 3.6-13, trans. Ernest Cushing Richardson, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1890), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/25023.htm. [Open access]","First Council of Nicaea, creed and canons, trans. Henry Percival, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 14, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1900), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3801.htm. [Open access]","First Council of Nicaea, synodal letter to the church of Alexandria, trans. Henry Percival, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 14, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1900), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3801.htm. [Open access]","Socrates Scholasticus, <i>Church History</i> 1.8, trans. A. C. Zenos, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 2, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1890), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/26011.htm. [Open access]","Eusebius of Caesarea, letter to his church, in Athanasius, <i>De Decretis</i>, trans. John Henry Newman and Archibald Robertson, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 4, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1892), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2804.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/The_first_Ecumenical_Council_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The council remembered","desc":"A fresco of the First Ecumenical Council painted about 1700 in St Sophia of Kyiv, with the emperor enthroned among ranks of bishops. Seven councils were painted there and this is the only one preserved.","alt":"A wall painting of an enthroned crowned figure holding an orb, flanked by rows of haloed bishops in vestments carrying books and scrolls.","credit":"Anonymous fresco of about 1700 in St Sophia of Kyiv; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_first_Ecumenical_Council_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-375","num":375,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"By convention the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is dated to 476 CE, when federate troops demanded a third of the land, killed Orestes and made his bodyguard Odoacer king in his place.","questions":["Zosimus shows the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> already under way when the Britons and Armorica expelled their Roman magistrates and set up governments of their own.","Procopius says the man who took Italy after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> never called himself emperor and was called king to the end, yet ruled as truly an emperor as any before him."],"answer":"Fall of the Western Roman Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Augustulus, 476 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Odoacer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ruled about 13 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Theoderic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">killed him in 493 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>fall of the Western Roman Empire</b> is dated to 476 CE, when the last emperor in Italy was pushed aside, but the west had been coming apart for two generations before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Zosimus says that when the barbarians crossed the Rhine the Britons and the whole of Armorica revolted from the empire, expelled the Roman magistrates and set up governments of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alaric's Goths took Rome in 410 CE, and Jerome wrote that his voice stuck in his throat as he dictated, and that the city which had taken the whole world was itself taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Augustine answered the charge that abandoning the old gods had brought the sack on the city, and observed that the plundering and burning were the ordinary custom of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was new, he said, was that the largest churches were set apart to be filled with people to whom quarter was given, and that none were dragged out of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The end in Italy came out of a quarrel about land: Procopius says the federate troops demanded a third of it, killed Orestes when he refused, and made his own bodyguard Odoacer their king in exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His son Romulus Augustulus, a boy who had held the title for months, was set aside, and the accounts differ: Procopius has him left to live as a private citizen, Jordanes exiled to a castle in Campania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jordanes wrote that the western empire of the Roman race perished with this Augustulus, and that from then on kings of the Goths held Rome and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Odoacer ruled for about thirteen years until Theoderic, sent west by the emperor Zeno, defeated him and killed him at a feast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Theoderic never took the name of emperor and was called king to the end, yet Procopius says he was in fact as truly an emperor as any who had held the office from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Fall of the Western Roman Empire","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","history","politics","warfare","rome","classical"],"sources":["Zosimus, <i>New History</i> 6.5, trans. anon. (London: Green and Chaplin, 1814), https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/zosimus06_book6.htm. [Open access]","Jerome, <i>Letter</i> 127.12, trans. W. H. Fremantle, G. Lewis and W. G. Martley, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd series, vol. 6, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1893), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3001127.htm. [Open access]","Augustine, <i>City of God</i> 1.1, 1.7, trans. Marcus Dods, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 1st series, vol. 2, ed. Philip Schaff (Buffalo, NY: Christian Literature Publishing, 1887), https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/120101.htm. [Open access]","Jordanes, <i>The Origin and Deeds of the Goths</i> 242-243, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow, Project Gutenberg ebook 14809, https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14809/pg14809.txt. [Open access]","Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 5.1, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Procopius/Wars/5A*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/Gold_Solidus_of_Romulus_Augustulus.jpg","title":"The last western emperor","desc":"A gold solidus of Romulus Augustulus, struck in 475 or 476 CE, the name and titles of the last emperor in the west running round a helmeted bust. It is in the American Numismatic Society's collection.","alt":"A worn gold coin showing a helmeted bust facing front and holding a spear, with Latin capitals running round the rim.","credit":"American Numismatic Society, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_Solidus_of_Romulus_Augustulus.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-376","num":376,"category":"Ancient India","question":"The Buddhist canon lists sixteen <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, great countries of northern India whose names are names of peoples rather than of places.","questions":["Some of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were not kingdoms at all but clan states, where business was done in a mote hall and the presiding chief was elected rather than born to it.","The list of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was already out of date when it was written down, since Kosala had taken Kasi and Magadha had absorbed Anga by the Buddha's lifetime."],"answer":"mahajanapadas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the canon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 great countries</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Clan states</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 listed with capitals</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600-500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>mahajanapadas</b> are the sixteen great countries of northern India named in a stock list that recurs through the Buddhist canon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The names in it are names of peoples rather than of countries, Angas and Magadhas and Kasis, so whoever drew the list up was thinking tribally and not geographically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The list is older than the books that carry it and may even be pre-Buddhist, because it does not fit the conditions of the Buddha's own lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By then Kosala had taken Kasi, Magadha had absorbed Anga and the Assakas probably belonged to Avanti, yet the list still calls all three independent and important.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They ran from Gandhara in the north-west of the Punjab to Avanti in Malwa, and were of two kinds, monarchies and tribal republics owning no allegiance to any paramount power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Not all of the sixteen were kingdoms, and in the clan states the administrative and judicial business was done in public assembly in a common mote hall, young and old alike present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A single chief was elected to preside over the sessions and over the state between them, and bore the title raja, which in that use must have meant something like a Roman consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The discourse on the Buddha's death opens with the king of Magadha declaring that he will annihilate the Vajjis, and sending his chief minister to ask the Buddha what he makes of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The answer is seven conditions — frequent and well-attended assemblies, proceeding by ancient constitutions rather than new decrees, honouring elders and shrines — under which their growth is to be expected and not their decline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three of the sixteen had lost their independence before the list was written down, and the power the texts actually describe is Kosala, the most important kingdom of the Buddha's lifetime, with Magadha beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"mahajanapadas","difficulty":4,"tags":["state","history","politics","india","iron age"],"sources":["T. W. Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1903), 19-20, https://archive.org/stream/buddhistindia00davi/buddhistindia00davi_djvu.txt. [Open access]","T. W. Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1903), 23-24, https://archive.org/stream/buddhistindia00davi/buddhistindia00davi_djvu.txt. [Open access]","T. W. Rhys Davids, \"The Early History of the Buddhists,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), chap. 7, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","<i>Maha-parinibbana Sutta</i> 1.1-5, trans. Sister Vajira and Francis Story (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1998), https://accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/dn/dn.16.1-6.vaji.html. [Open access]","Vincent A. Smith, <i>The Early History of India</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1904), 25-26, https://archive.org/stream/earlyhistoryofin00smit_2/earlyhistoryofin00smit_2_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Cyclopean_Wall_-_Rajgir_01.jpg","title":"The wall of Rajagriha","desc":"Part of the stone wall that ran round the old Magadhan capital of Rajagriha, now Rajgir in Bihar, carried across the gaps between the hills that ring the site.","alt":"A broad wall of undressed stone blocks running along a green ridge towards a gap in low hills, under a sky of cumulus.","credit":"Sumitsurai, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyclopean_Wall_-_Rajgir_01.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-377","num":377,"category":"Ancient India","question":"The ritual religion of the Vedas turns into enquiry about the self in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Sanskrit texts that stand at the end of the Brahmanas and are also called Vedanta.","questions":["The name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means sitting down beside, since they were imparted to selected pupils in sessions from which the wider circle was shut out.","'That art thou' and 'I am brahma' are the two ways the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> state their central doctrine, that the self in each person is the self of the universe."],"answer":"Upanishads","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Older ones</span><span class=\"dt-v\">no later than 550 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Principal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 counted</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tat tvam asi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Chandogya 6.8-16</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Upanishads</b> are the Sanskrit texts that stand at the end of the Brahmanas, and in which the ritual religion of the Vedas turns into enquiry about the self.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word means something like sitting down beside, first a confidential session and then a secret doctrine, because they were imparted to selected pupils in lectures the wider circle was shut out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Coming at the end of the Veda they are also called Vedanta, a term later read as its final goal rather than merely its end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are in effect a new religion set against the ritual side of the same books, since the aim is no longer happiness bought by sacrificing correctly but release through knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older ones are dated on the whole no later than 550 BCE, by reckoning back from the Buddha's death, since Buddhism is in part a development of what they teach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> <i>Atman</i> means no more than breath in the Rigveda and self in the Brahmanas, and here it becomes the self of the universe, identical with the self in each person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chandogya says so in a sentence repeated through nine sections: that which is the subtile essence, in it all that exists has its self, it is the True, it is the Self, and thou art it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Brihadaranyaka says the same the other way about, that whoever knows 'I am brahma' becomes the All, and that even the gods cannot prevent it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The teaching comes as conversation rather than as doctrine: Yajnavalkya, leaving for the forest, tells his wife that a lump of salt thrown into water cannot be got out again, though the water tastes salt everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thirteen are counted as the principal ones, among them the Katha, in which a boy asks Death himself whether a man exists afterwards and is told that even the gods have doubted about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Upanishads","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","history","philosophy","religion","india","iron age"],"sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), 204-205, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), 218-223, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. Max Muller, trans., <i>The Upanishads</i>, part 1, Sacred Books of the East 1 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 107, https://archive.org/stream/upanishads01ml/upanishads01ml_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Robert Ernest Hume, trans., <i>The Thirteen Principal Upanishads</i> (London: Oxford University Press, 1921), https://archive.org/stream/thirteenprincipa00hume/thirteenprincipa00hume_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. Berriedale Keith, \"The Period of the Later Samhitas, the Brahmanas, the Aranyakas, and the Upanishads,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), chap. 5, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Brihadaranyaka_upanishad_adhyaya_1_folio_3b%2C_pages_1r_1v_2r_2v%2C_Schoenberg_Center_manuscript%2C_Penn_Library.jpg","title":"A manuscript of the Brihadaranyaka","desc":"Four pages of a Sanskrit manuscript of the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad, in the collections of the Penn Libraries. The text is written in Devanagari, with section numbers and marks picked out in red.","alt":"Four pages of a manuscript in Devanagari script, closely written in black ink on cream paper with numbers and marks in red.","credit":"Ms Sarah Welch, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brihadaranyaka_upanishad_adhyaya_1_folio_3b,_pages_1r_1v_2r_2v,_Schoenberg_Center_manuscript,_Penn_Library.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-378","num":378,"category":"Ancient India","question":"The name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as a Persian word for the land beyond the Indus, so the religion is called after a river rather than after a founder or a creed.","questions":["In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Vedas count as revelation and the law-books as what is remembered, and the shared ideas include samsara, the round of rebirth, and karma.","More than 99% of the roughly billion adherents of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> live in Asia and the Pacific, and they are a majority in only Nepal, India and Mauritius."],"answer":"Hinduism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Hinduism</b> is the religion of about a billion people, some 15% of the world, and it takes its name from a river rather than from a founder or a creed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Avestan and Old Persian word Hindu meant the land beyond the Indus, the Sanskrit Sindhu, the name of the stream being carried over to the territory it watered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no one book: the Vedas are counted as revelation, <i>shruti</i>, while the law-books and the rest of the tradition are <i>smriti</i>, what is remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Out of that literature come the ideas most often named as shared, samsara, the round of rebirth, and karma, action, which decides what the next birth will be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both are developed in the oldest Upanishads and were firmly established by the time Buddhism arose, since the Buddha accepted rebirth without argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The classical law-books set out four orders of life for a man, the student, the householder, the hermit and the ascetic, all of which Manu says spring from the order of householders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same books assume a fourfold division of society whose members are imagined as born from the mouth, the arms, the thighs and the feet of the creator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What Hindus actually do varies enormously, and the traditions usually named as major are Vaishnavism, devoted to Vishnu, and Shaivism, organised around Shiva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Reliable figures for the size of either do not exist, because most countries collect no census or survey data on subgroups of Hindus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 99% of Hindus live in Asia and the Pacific and 94% of them in India, and they are a majority in only three countries, Nepal, India and Mauritius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Hinduism","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","religion","history","philosophy","india"],"sources":["A. V. Williams Jackson, \"The Persian Dominions in Northern India down to the Time of Alexander's Invasion,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 324, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), 223-224, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), 34-35, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Georg Buhler, trans., <i>The Laws of Manu</i>, Sacred Books of the East 25 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1886), 6.87, 10.45, https://archive.org/stream/lawsofmanu00bh/lawsofmanu00bh_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Pew Research Center, \"Hindus,\" in <i>The Global Religious Landscape</i> (Washington, DC: Pew Research Center, 2012), https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2012/12/18/global-religious-landscape-hindu/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Gopuram_of_the_Meenakshi_Temple_at_Madurai.jpg/1920px-Gopuram_of_the_Meenakshi_Temple_at_Madurai.jpg","title":"The east gopuram of the Meenakshi Amman Temple, Madurai","desc":"A gateway tower of the temple at Madurai in Tamil Nadu, its tiers covered in painted stucco figures of deities and attendants. Temples of this kind are among the most visible institutions of the living religion in southern India.","credit":"Photograph by Richard Mortel, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gopuram_of_the_Meenakshi_Temple_at_Madurai.jpg","alt":"A tall pyramidal temple gateway rising in tiers against a blue sky, every tier crowded with brightly painted sculpted figures."}},
{"id":"wh-379","num":379,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Manu's law-book divides society into four <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, gives each its own work, and insists there is no fifth, though the groups people actually belong to number in the hundreds.","questions":["The Sanskrit word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means 'colour', and it names the fourfold order of Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra that a late Rigvedic hymn derives from the body of a primeval man.","A classical law-book assigns teaching to the Brahmana, protection to the Kshatriya and service to the Shudra, but the four <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are a frame rather than the birth-groups people marry within."],"answer":"varna","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Varna</b> is the fourfold ordering of society set out in the classical Sanskrit texts, into Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra, and the Sanskrit word itself means 'colour'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its charter is a late hymn of the Rigveda, the Purusha-sukta, the one place in that collection where the four are named, from the primeval man's mouth, arms, thighs and feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Manu's law-book repeats the image and gives each its work: teaching to the Brahmana, protection to the Kshatriya, cattle, trade and land to the Vaishya, and to the Shudra \"one occupation only\", to serve the other three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first three are twice-born, initiated into study of the Veda; the Shudra \"has one birth only\", and Manu states flatly that \"there is no fifth\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The many other groups the law-books meet are accounted for as the offspring of marriages between the four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What people actually belong to is <i>jati</i>, the birth-group, and there are hundreds of them, each an endogamous group with a common name, a traditional occupation and a social standing of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two are not the same thing: nowhere in the Rigveda is there any sign of the castes into which the four orders were later subdivided, so the scheme is a frame rather than a description.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The texts also put people outside the order altogether: Manu sets the dwellings of the Chandalas outside the village, their dress \"the garments of the dead\", their food taken from broken dishes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Indian Constitution prohibits caste discrimination including untouchability, and reserved places in universities and government jobs go to Dalits, Scheduled Tribes and Other Backward Classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A national survey in 2019 and 2020 found 25% of Indian adults identifying as Scheduled Caste and 35% as Other Backward Class, with 64% saying it is very important to stop women marrying outside their caste.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"varna","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","practice","history","religion","india"],"sources":["Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), 152-153, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Arthur A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton and Company, 1900), 133-134, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Georg Buhler, trans., <i>The Laws of Manu</i>, Sacred Books of the East 25 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1886), 1.31, 1.87-91, 10.4, https://archive.org/stream/lawsofmanu00bh/lawsofmanu00bh_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Georg Buhler, trans., <i>The Laws of Manu</i>, Sacred Books of the East 25 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1886), 10.51-52, https://archive.org/stream/lawsofmanu00bh/lawsofmanu00bh_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"Peoples and Languages,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 53-55, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Neha Sahgal, Jonathan Evans, Ariana Monique Salazar, Kelsey Jo Starr and Manolo Corichi, <i>Religion in India: Tolerance and Segregation</i> (Washington, DC: Pew Research Center, 2021), https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2021/06/29/religion-in-india-tolerance-and-segregation/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/1407_CE%2C_Manava-nyaya-sastra-tika_Hindu_law_text_and_commentary%2C_palm_leaf_manuscript%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Bhujimol_script%2C_Malla_kingdom.jpg/1920px-1407_CE%2C_Manava-nyaya-sastra-tika_Hindu_law_text_and_commentary%2C_palm_leaf_manuscript%2C_Sanskrit%2C_Bhujimol_script%2C_Malla_kingdom.jpg","title":"A Sanskrit law text on palm leaf, copied in 1407 CE","desc":"Two folios of the Manava-nyaya-sastra-tika, a Hindu law text with its commentary, written in Bhujimol script in the Malla kingdom. Books of this kind are where the fourfold scheme and the duties attached to it are set out.","credit":"Photograph by Ms Sarah Welch, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1407_CE,_Manava-nyaya-sastra-tika_Hindu_law_text_and_commentary,_palm_leaf_manuscript,_Sanskrit,_Bhujimol_script,_Malla_kingdom.jpg","alt":"Two long narrow palm-leaf folios covered in small Sanskrit script, each pierced by a binding hole at its centre."}},
{"id":"wh-380","num":380,"category":"Ancient India","question":"The teacher called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried a title rather than a name, taught for 44 years after his awakening, and died at Kusinara in the country of the Mallas.","questions":["A Sakya of Kapilavatthu, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is made a prince by the later legend, though the oldest texts give his father as a wealthy landowner rather than a king.","The first discourse of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, given in a deer park at Varanasi to five ascetics, sets out a middle way between indulgence and self-affliction and the eightfold path."],"answer":"Gautama Buddha","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 563 – 483 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also given</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480, or 478 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">aged 80, at Kusinara</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Gautama Buddha</b> is the teacher the Buddhist tradition is built around, and Buddha is a title rather than a name, marking the night on which \"the ascetic Gotama had become the Buddha, the awakened\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He belonged to the Sakya people, whose town of Kapilavatthu lay on the northern edge of the Ganges plain, and Gotama was his clan name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The later legend makes him a prince and the oldest texts do not: his father Suddhodana appears there as one of the wealthy landowners of the Sakya, whom \"later legends first transformed into the great king\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He left home, studied under the teachers of the day, took up the austerities and gave them up, and is said to have reached his awakening under a tree by the Neranjara river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His first discourse, given at Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana to five ascetics, sets out a middle way between indulgence and self-affliction, the eightfold path, and the noble truth of suffering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He taught for the rest of his life, 44 of his eighty years by the tradition's reckoning, and died at Kusinara in the country of the Mallas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His dates are not independently recorded but reckoned back from Ashoka, whose consecration the Ceylon chronicles place 218 years after the death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The volume that prints 483 BCE calls it provisional in its Buddhism chapter, prefers 478 or 477 in its Jain one, and an older study put the death about 480.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The earliest evidence from outside the tradition is Ashoka's: about 250 BCE he visited the garden of Lumbini as the birthplace, set up a commemorative pillar and remitted the village's tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The discourses themselves are held to have been compiled before the council at Vesali, roughly a century after his death, which is as close to him as the record reaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Gautama Buddha","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","religion","history","philosophy","india"],"sources":["Hermann Oldenberg, <i>Buddha: His Life, His Doctrine, His Order</i>, trans. William Hoey (London: Williams and Norgate, 1882), 95-108, https://archive.org/stream/buddhahislifehis00olde/buddhahislifehis00olde_djvu.txt. [Open access]","\"Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta: Setting the Wheel of Dhamma in Motion\" (SN 56.11), trans. Thanissaro Bhikkhu, Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn56/sn56.011.than.html. [Open access]","Hermann Oldenberg, <i>Buddha: His Life, His Doctrine, His Order</i>, trans. William Hoey (London: Williams and Norgate, 1882), 197-201, https://archive.org/stream/buddhahislifehis00olde/buddhahislifehis00olde_djvu.txt. [Open access]","T. W. Rhys Davids, \"The Early History of the Buddhists,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 171, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Jarl Charpentier, \"The History of the Jains,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 157-158, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 497-499, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Ashoka_Pillar%2C_Lumbini%2C_Rupandehi%2C_Nepal.jpg/1920px-Ashoka_Pillar%2C_Lumbini%2C_Rupandehi%2C_Nepal.jpg","title":"Ashoka's pillar at Lumbini","desc":"The pillar Ashoka set up at Lumbini, standing today beside the Maya Devi temple and hung with prayer flags. Its inscription, cut into the shaft behind the railing, is the earliest evidence about the Buddha from outside the tradition.","credit":"Photograph by Shadow Ayush, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka_Pillar,_Lumbini,_Rupandehi,_Nepal.jpg","alt":"A weathered sandstone column standing inside a gold railing hung with coloured prayer flags, in front of a white temple building."}},
{"id":"wh-381","num":381,"category":"Ancient India","question":"A person enters <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by taking refuge three times over in the Buddha, the teaching and the order, and its monks recite their list of offences twice a month.","questions":["The four truths and the eightfold path stand at the centre of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which offers itself as a middle way between indulgence in pleasure and deliberate self-affliction.","Although <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> accepts rebirth and holds that karma decides it, it denies a permanent self, so what carries over between lives is a moral sequence rather than a soul."],"answer":"Buddhism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founder died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">483 BCE or c. 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canon collected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by about 250 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Buddhism</b> is the religion built on the Buddha's teaching, entered by a threefold formula: \"I take my refuge in the Buddha, I take my refuge in the Dhamma, I take my refuge in the Sangha\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its core is the four truths of the first discourse: suffering, its origin in craving, its cessation, and the eightfold path that leads there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That path is offered as a middle way between indulgence in sensual pleasure and self-affliction, both of which the first discourse calls base, ignoble and unprofitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The teaching denies a permanent self: form \"is not self\", and neither is feeling, perception, mental fabrication nor consciousness, since none of them can be commanded to be as one wishes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rebirth is accepted all the same and karma decides it, so what carries over from one life to the next is a moral sequence rather than a soul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The order of monks, the <i>sangha</i>, is entered by having the hair and beard cut off, putting on yellow robes and reciting those refuges three times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poverty ranks next to chastity among its duties, a monk owning his robes and his bowl and begging his food, and an order of nuns stands beside the order of monks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Twice a month the assembled monks recite the <i>Patimokkha</i>, the list of offences a monk may confess, which is the basis of the whole body of Buddhist church law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They travel for most of the year but must settle for the rains, a rule the texts trace to complaints that wandering monks were crushing the young crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lay believers support the order without entering it, and the discourses themselves were put together, on the evidence of their own contents, about half way between the Buddha's death and Ashoka.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Buddhism","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","religion","practice","philosophy","india"],"sources":["T. W. Rhys Davids and Hermann Oldenberg, trans., <i>Vinaya Texts</i>, Part I, Sacred Books of the East 13 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1881), Mahavagga I, 12, 3-4 (114-115), https://archive.org/stream/vinayatexts01davi/vinayatexts01davi_djvu.txt. [Open access]","\"Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta: Setting the Wheel of Dhamma in Motion\" (SN 56.11), trans. Thanissaro Bhikkhu, Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn56/sn56.011.than.html. [Open access]","\"Pancavaggi Sutta: Five Brethren\" (SN 22.59), trans. Thanissaro Bhikkhu, Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn22/sn22.059.than.html. [Open access]","Hermann Oldenberg, <i>Buddha: His Life, His Doctrine, His Order</i>, trans. William Hoey (London: Williams and Norgate, 1882), 242-261, https://archive.org/stream/buddhahislifehis00olde/buddhahislifehis00olde_djvu.txt. [Open access]","T. W. Rhys Davids and Hermann Oldenberg, trans., <i>Vinaya Texts</i>, Part I, Sacred Books of the East 13 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1881), Mahavagga II, 3, 1-3 (241-242) and III, 1, 3 - 2, 2 (298-299), https://archive.org/stream/vinayatexts01davi/vinayatexts01davi_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann Oldenberg, <i>Buddha: His Life, His Doctrine, His Order</i>, trans. William Hoey (London: Williams and Norgate, 1882), 354-366, 377-390, https://archive.org/stream/buddhahislifehis00olde/buddhahislifehis00olde_djvu.txt. [Open access]","T. W. Rhys Davids, \"The Early History of the Buddhists,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 195-196, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Hsun_laung%2C_Mandalay%2C_Myanmar.JPG","title":"Monks on the alms round, Mandalay","desc":"A line of monks with their bowls receiving food from a stallholder in Mandalay. The order lives by begging and the laity by giving, an arrangement the earliest rules already take for granted.","credit":"Photograph by Wagaung, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hsun_laung,_Mandalay,_Myanmar.JPG","alt":"A queue of barefoot monks in dark red robes holding covered bowls, being served by a woman at a street stall."}},
{"id":"wh-382","num":382,"category":"Ancient India","question":"The monks of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> take five great vows, the first renouncing all killing of living beings, and its followers count 24 teachers of one law in succession.","questions":["Non-injury to every living creature is the central discipline of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which divided into one sect whose monks wear white clothes and another whose ascetics wear none at all.","The last teacher of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a contemporary of the Buddha, and both his own followers and the hostile Buddhist texts name the same town as the place he died."],"answer":"Jainism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mahavira died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 468 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Church divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by about 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Jainism</b> is the religion of the Jina, the victor, whose followers count 24 <i>tirthankaras</i> teaching the same law in succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its central discipline is non-injury: \"all breathing, existing, living, sentient creatures should not be slain, nor treated with violence, nor abused, nor tormented, nor driven away\", which the Acaranga Sutra calls \"the pure, unchangeable, eternal law\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A monk takes five great vows, the first renouncing \"all killing of living beings, whether subtile or gross, whether movable or immovable\", and undertaking not to cause or consent to it either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The last of the 24 is Mahavira, a contemporary of the Buddha whom the Buddhist texts call Nigantha Nataputta, Niggantha being their name for the sect and meaning those freed from fetters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both sides describe him, his own followers as the victor and the Buddhists as a pretender, and both name Pava as the place he died, an agreement on a collateral fact that puts him on historical ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The 23rd, Parshva, is probably historical as well, and Mahavira's teaching is described as a renovated form of his creed, the two differing over the number of vows and whether to wear clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The traditional date for Mahavira's death, 528 BCE, is rejected, and reckoning back from a fixed point in Chandragupta's reign gives 468 BCE instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The church later divided into the Svetambaras, \"monks in white clothes\", and the Digambaras, \"sky-clad or naked ascetics\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two disagree about the scriptures themselves, the Digambaras holding that the real canon was hopelessly lost and that the Svetambara collection is late and unauthoritative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That collection was written down at the council of Valabhi in the 5th or the beginning of the 6th century CE, and the Jains, never numerous, have long been settled in western India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Jainism","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","religion","practice","philosophy","india"],"sources":["Hermann Jacobi, trans., <i>Gaina Sutras</i>, Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1884), Introduction, xxxiv-xxxv, https://archive.org/stream/jainasutrasparti029233mbp/jainasutrasparti029233mbp_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann Jacobi, trans., <i>Gaina Sutras</i>, Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1884), Akaranga Sutra I, 4, 1 (36), https://archive.org/stream/jainasutrasparti029233mbp/jainasutrasparti029233mbp_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann Jacobi, trans., <i>Gaina Sutras</i>, Part I, Sacred Books of the East 22 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1884), Akaranga Sutra II, 15 (202-203), https://archive.org/stream/jainasutrasparti029233mbp/jainasutrasparti029233mbp_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Hermann Oldenberg, <i>Buddha: His Life, His Doctrine, His Order</i>, trans. William Hoey (London: Williams and Norgate, 1882), 66-67, 72-73, https://archive.org/stream/buddhahislifehis00olde/buddhahislifehis00olde_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Jarl Charpentier, \"The History of the Jains,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 153-156, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Jarl Charpentier, \"The History of the Jains,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 165-166, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Jarl Charpentier, \"The History of the Jains,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 169-170, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/The_Gommateshwara_Statue%2C_Shravanabelagola_01.jpg/1920px-The_Gommateshwara_Statue%2C_Shravanabelagola_01.jpg","title":"The monolithic figure of Bahubali at Shravanabelagola","desc":"A colossal Jain figure cut from a single rock in Karnataka, standing naked with creepers grown up his legs and arms after a meditation held so long that the plants overtook him. It is a Digambara image, of the sect whose ascetics wear nothing.","credit":"Photograph by Rohit14400, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Gommateshwara_Statue,_Shravanabelagola_01.jpg","alt":"A huge grey stone figure of a standing naked man, arms at his sides, with carved vines twining up his legs and arms, on a temple terrace."}},
{"id":"wh-383","num":383,"category":"Ancient India","question":"The first state to hold most of the Indian subcontinent, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled from Pataliputra through viceroys at Takshasila, Ujjayini and Kalinga, and lasted 137 years by the Puranas.","questions":["A Greek ambassador at the capital of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> described a city walled in wood with 570 towers and 64 gates, and claimed that no Indian anywhere was a slave.","The edicts of Ashoka survey the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the Hindu Kush to Ceylon, but distinguish the king's own dominions from border peoples he counted within his influence."],"answer":"Maurya Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 321 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">137 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 184 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Maurya Empire</b> was the first state to hold most of the Indian subcontinent, founded by Chandragupta about 321 BCE on the kingdom of Magadha, with its capital at Pataliputra on the Ganges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inscriptions of Ashoka give, for the first time in history, a comprehensive survey of India from the Hindu Kush to Ceylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They also show that it was not one uniform territory: inside \"the king's dominions\" lay the central government at Pataliputra and the viceroyalties of Takshasila, Ujjayini and Kalinga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beyond those lay \"the border peoples\", Gandharas, Kambojas and Yavanas to the north-west and Rashtrikas, Bhojas and Pulindas to the south, whom the emperor counted within his influence rather than his rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was the head of a confederation united for imperial purposes whose members kept their own civil government and internal administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The capital is described by a Greek ambassador who lived there: a parallelogram 80 stadia long and 15 wide, girded with a wooden wall pierced with loopholes for arrows, 570 towers and 64 gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same account reports that \"all the Indians are free, and not one of them is a slave\", which is where an outsider's report shows its limits, since the Indian material enumerates seven kinds of slave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What is known of the empire otherwise comes from the Greek writers, the <i>Arthashastra</i>, the Pali canon and the code of Manu, from which large deductions have to be made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ashoka's own dates are anchored by the Hellenic kings his edicts name as his contemporaries, which is the firmest fixed point in early Indian chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Puranas disagree about how many kings followed him but agree that the dynasty lasted 137 years, ending about 184 BCE when the last Maurya, Brihadratha, was killed by his commander Pushyamitra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Maurya Empire","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","politics","india","iron age"],"sources":["F. W. Thomas, \"Chandragupta, the Founder of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 467-473, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"Indian Native States after the Period of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 514-516, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. W. McCrindle, trans., <i>Ancient India as Described by Megasthenes and Arrian</i> (Calcutta: Thacker, Spink, 1877), 66-69, https://archive.org/stream/ancientindiaasd02mccrgoog/ancientindiaasd02mccrgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Political and Social Organisation of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 474, 484-485, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 502, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 511-512, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Lion_capital_of_Ashoka%2C_Sarnath.jpg/1920px-Lion_capital_of_Ashoka%2C_Sarnath.jpg","title":"The lion capital from Ashoka's pillar at Sarnath","desc":"Four lions back to back on a polished sandstone capital, standing on an abacus carved with wheels and animals above a bell of lotus petals. It is the finest surviving object of the Maurya state, now in the museum at Sarnath.","credit":"Photograph by lisa bat, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lion_capital_of_Ashoka,_Sarnath.jpg","alt":"A polished stone capital of lions standing back to back above a round abacus carved with a wheel and a bull, on a fluted lotus base, in a museum gallery."}},
{"id":"wh-384","num":384,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Known to the Greeks as Sandrocottus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Pataliputra from the last Nanda king and later traded the Kabul valley to Seleucus for 500 elephants.","questions":["The Puranas and the Buddhist books both give <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a reign of 24 years, but they are not independent witnesses and they disagree about the year it began.","Seleucus crossed the Indus about 305 BCE, found <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> waiting with an immense army, and settled for a safe retirement, a marriage alliance and a gift of elephants."],"answer":"Chandragupta Maurya","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 321 – 297 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 years, disputed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Seleucid treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 305 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chandragupta Maurya</b> founded the Maurya Empire by overthrowing the Nanda king of Magadha and taking Pataliputra, and the Greeks knew him as Sandrocottus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their Agrammes or Xandrames, the king he displaced, is recognised without difficulty as the Dhana-Nanda of the Sanskrit books, whose dynasty the Puranas say ruled exactly a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A precise date for that overthrow is on present evidence impossible, though 321 BCE is not unlikely if the campaign had the help of Porus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tradition gives him a Brahman mentor, Chanakya, also called Kautilya and Vishnugupta, to whom the <i>Arthashastra</i> is ascribed, and the Pali account makes Chanakya a native of Takshasila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of the story of the founding survives only in a play, the <i>Mudrarakshasa</i>, written perhaps in the 7th century CE and preserving outlines of the events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> About 305 BCE Seleucus crossed the Indus and found him master of all Hindustan and waiting with an immense army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Either no battle followed or an indecisive one, and Seleucus took a safe retirement and a gift of 500 elephants in exchange for the Greek dominions as far as the Kabul valley, with a marriage alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That left the empire established behind the Hindu Kush and above Herat, and Seleucus sent Megasthenes as his envoy to \"Sandrakottos, the king of the Prasii, whose capital was Palibothra\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His reign is put at 24 years by the Puranas and the Buddhist books alike, but the two are not independent, and the starting year is 321 BCE in the Ceylon reckoning and 313 in the Jain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The contrast is the point: what is known of his country and its administration is trustworthy where the chronology of the man himself is defective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chandragupta Maurya","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","india"],"sources":["F. W. Thomas, \"Chandragupta, the Founder of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 469-471, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Chandragupta, the Founder of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 467, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Chandragupta, the Founder of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 472, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Chandragupta, the Founder of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 473, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. W. McCrindle, trans., <i>Ancient India as Described by Megasthenes and Arrian</i> (Calcutta: Thacker, Spink, 1877), Introduction, https://archive.org/stream/ancientindiaasd02mccrgoog/ancientindiaasd02mccrgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Jarl Charpentier, \"The History of the Jains,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 156-157, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Clevelandart_1967.242.jpg/1920px-Clevelandart_1967.242.jpg","title":"A punch-marked silver coin of the Maurya period","desc":"No likeness of him survives. Indian coinage of the period was cut to weight from sheets of silver or copper and stamped with punches rather than struck from dies, each coin carrying its own combination of marks. Cleveland Museum of Art.","credit":"Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clevelandart_1967.242.jpg","alt":"A small rectangular piece of silver stamped with several separate punch marks, among them a sun, a crescent on a staff and animal figures."}},
{"id":"wh-385","num":385,"category":"Ancient India","question":"A manual of statecraft in fifteen books, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> prescribes five standing institutes of spies and tests a minister by having one propose the king's murder to him.","questions":["Ascribed to the minister who put Chandragupta on the throne, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nowhere dates itself, and its authorship is a claim the book makes in its own closing verse.","Alongside its chapters on spies and poisons, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tells a king that in the happiness of his subjects lies his happiness and in their welfare his welfare."],"answer":"Arthashastra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditionally</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b><i>Arthashastra</i></b> is a Sanskrit manual of statecraft ascribed to Kautilya, also called Chanakya and Vishnu Gupta, and it defines its own subject plainly: \"the subsistence of mankind is termed artha\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs to fifteen books and 150 chapters and prescribes rather than narrates, taking in ministers, forts, revenue, law, war and, above all, information.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first book puts four sciences at the head of a king's education: philosophy, the triple Vedas, economics, and the science of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whole chapters go to espionage, with five standing institutes of spies to test the character of the king's servants and wandering spies recruited from orphans the state feeds and trains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ministers are proved by allurements: a spy \"under the guise of a fraudulent disciple\" proposes murdering the king to see who agrees, and those who refuse \"are pure\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Beside all that it states the king's obligation without hedging: \"In the happiness of his subjects lies his happiness; in their welfare his welfare\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The book claims its author at the close, as the man who \"quickly rescued the scriptures and the science of weapons and the earth which had passed to the Nanda king\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the book's own claim rather than an outside record: it prescribes, and nowhere dates itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was lost for centuries and recovered only in the 20th century, and the survey written soon afterwards called it \"perhaps the most precious work in the whole of Sanskrit literature\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That survey took its date to fall within or near the Maurya period, which is why the standard account of Maurya government is written largely out of it, beside the Greek writers and the edicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Arthashastra","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["text","concept","politics","history","india"],"sources":["Kautilya, <i>Arthashastra</i>, trans. R. Shamasastry (Bangalore: Government Press, 1915), Book XV, chap. 1 (515-520), https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Arthashastra/Book_XV. [Open access]","Kautilya, <i>Arthashastra</i>, trans. R. Shamasastry (Bangalore: Government Press, 1915), Book I, chaps. 1-2, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Arthashastra/Book_I. [Open access]","Kautilya, <i>Arthashastra</i>, trans. R. Shamasastry (Bangalore: Government Press, 1915), Book I, chaps. 10-12, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Arthashastra/Book_I. [Open access]","Kautilya, <i>Arthashastra</i>, trans. R. Shamasastry (Bangalore: Government Press, 1915), Book I, chap. 19, https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Arthashastra/Book_I. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Chandragupta, the Founder of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 467, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Political and Social Organisation of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 474, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7b/Rediscovered_circa_16th_century_Arthashastra_manuscript_in_Grantha_script_from_the_Oriental_Research_Institute_%28ORI%29_which_was_found_in_1905_05.jpg","title":"Palm leaves of the recovered manuscript","desc":"Folios in Grantha script, threaded on a cord, from the Oriental Research Institute at Mysore, where a manuscript of the treatise came to light early in the 20th century and the modern text was made from it.","credit":"Unknown scribe, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rediscovered_circa_16th_century_Arthashastra_manuscript_in_Grantha_script_from_the_Oriental_Research_Institute_%28ORI%29_which_was_found_in_1905_05.jpg","alt":"Three long palm-leaf folios threaded on a white cord, closely written in a rounded south Indian script."}},
{"id":"wh-386","num":386,"category":"Ancient India","question":"After conquering Kalinga <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded deep remorse for it, counted the dead and deported himself, and had the account cut into rock for his subjects to read.","questions":["The inscriptions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> call him Beloved-of-the-Gods rather than by the name history uses, and measure his reform by the animals no longer killed in his own kitchen.","Asking for growth in the essentials of all religions rather than for one of them, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appointed officers to work among every religion and to see to the treatment of prisoners."],"answer":"Ashoka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 274 – 236 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kalinga war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th year of his reign</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dhamma officers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from his 13th year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ashoka</b> was the third Maurya emperor and a grandson of Chandragupta, and in his own inscriptions he never uses that name: he is \"Beloved-of-the-Gods, King Piyadasi\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those titles were not his alone, being known from contemporary kings in Ceylon and from a descendant of his own, so they were probably normal royal usage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Eight years after his coronation he conquered Kalinga, and the count of it is his own: 150,000 deported, 100,000 killed and many more dead of other causes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He records \"deep remorse\" for the conquest, and says he is pained still more by what falls on Brahmans, ascetics and householders who behave properly and are loyal to their friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thirteen years after his coronation he appointed officers called Dhamma Mahamatras, who were to work among all religions and for the proper treatment of prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Rock Edict 1 measures the change in his own kitchen: hundreds of thousands of animals were killed there daily to make curry, and now three, \"and the deer not always\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He claims provision for the medical treatment of men and animals throughout his domain and beyond it, as far as the Cholas and Pandyas and where the Greek king Antiochos rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Along the roads he had banyans planted for shade, wells dug at intervals and rest-houses built, and adds that these are \"but minor achievements\", such as former kings had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On religion he asks restraint rather than uniformity, valuing \"growth in the essentials of all religions\", whose root is \"not praising one's own religion, or condemning the religion of others\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All of it is the king's own account of the king, published by him on rock and pillar, and that is at once what makes it evidence and what makes it a claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ashoka","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","india"],"sources":["Rock Edicts 1-2, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Rock Edict 5, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Rock Edict 12, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Rock Edict 13, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Pillar Edict 7, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 496-498, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 504-509, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Elephant-sculpture-dhauli.JPG/1920px-Elephant-sculpture-dhauli.JPG","title":"The rock-cut elephant at Dhauli","desc":"The forepart of an elephant cut out of the living rock at Dhauli in Odisha, above the face carrying two of Ashoka's edicts. The site lies near the ground where the war with Kalinga was fought.","credit":"Photograph by Kumar shakti, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephant-sculpture-dhauli.JPG","alt":"The head, trunk and forelegs of an elephant carved out of a mass of grey rock, the rest of the animal still uncut stone."}},
{"id":"wh-387","num":387,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Cut into rock faces and onto stone pillars at more than thirty places across India, Nepal, Pakistan and Afghanistan, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the earliest Indian writing that can still be read.","questions":["Nearly all in Prakrit and mostly in Brahmi, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are issued by a king who calls himself \"Beloved-of-the-Gods, King Piyadasi\" and only rarely gives his own name.","Ten of the polished stone pillars carrying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still stand, quarried at Chunar, hauled overland and each once capped with a carved animal above the text."],"answer":"Edicts of Ashoka","answerText":"Edicts of Ashoka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First edict</span><span class=\"dt-v\">259 BCE at latest</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rock edicts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">258 BCE at latest</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pillar edicts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">243 BCE at latest</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Edicts of Ashoka</b> are a body of inscriptions cut into rock faces and free-standing stone pillars in the 3rd century BCE, at more than thirty places across India, Nepal, Pakistan and Afghanistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are the earliest corpus of written documents from India that can be read, and one of them says why they last: it was \"written on stone so that it might endure long\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The texts fall into groups: fourteen major rock edicts repeated at five sites, a pair addressed to officials in Kalinga, minor rock edicts, seven pillar edicts and shorter pillar inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A rock edict stands wherever a suitable rock face was available, but the pillars were placed deliberately: some at a site in the Buddha's life, others near towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten inscribed pillars survive, 12 to 15 metres (40 to 50 feet) high, quarried at Chunar and dragged overland to where they were raised, each capped with an animal and polished to a shine the weather has not taken off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nearly all of them are in Prakrit dialects that vary considerably from one site to another, most written in Brahmi, the alphabet from which the later Indian scripts descend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The eastern versions use a form of Magadhi, probably the court language, the western ones a dialect closer to Sanskrit, and one edict in Afghanistan is bilingual, in Aramaic and Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The king who speaks in them calls himself \"Beloved-of-the-Gods, King Piyadasi\", and only a few copies give the name Ashoka, which is what identifies him with the king of the Buddhist chronicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the corpus is exhortation, but some of it records acts: the Lumbini pillar marks the king's visit to the Buddha's birthplace and cuts the village's tax to an eighth of its produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because they are dated by the king's own regnal years and stand where they were meant to be read, they tie Indian evidence to a place and a reign, in the government's own wording.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Introduction, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Note 1, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","A. Berriedale Keith, \"The Period of the Later Samhitas, the Brahmanas, the Aranyakas, and the Upanishads,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 146-147, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"Sources of History,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 60-62, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Minor Pillar Edict 1, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","Rock Edict 5, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["text","object","history","politics","writing","india","buddhism"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Ashokan_edict_in_Delhi.jpg/1920px-Ashokan_edict_in_Delhi.jpg","title":"The minor rock edict at Bahapur, Delhi","desc":"A dome of rock cut over its whole surface with the text of a minor rock edict, the letters set out between ruled lines. A rock edict was put wherever a suitable face could be found.","credit":"Photograph by Vidishaprakash, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashokan_edict_in_Delhi.jpg","alt":"A large smooth boulder filling the frame, its curved surface covered in close rows of angular early Indian letters."}},
{"id":"wh-388","num":388,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Under Ashoka the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became state business, with monks named one by one for each province and a mission to Ceylon led by a man said to be the emperor's own son.","questions":["Relics of the missionaries themselves came to light in the stupas at Sanchi, which is the outside check on the Pali chronicles' account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Mauryas.","From a circuit of the old kingdoms of Kasi-Kosala and Magadha, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the order to Kashmir, the Himalaya, Suvarnabhumi and Ceylon within two centuries."],"answer":"spread of Buddhism","answerText":"spread of Buddhism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Third Council</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19th year from the coronation</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceylon mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 246 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanchi stupas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd or 1st century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>spread of Buddhism</b> began from a small district: for the Buddha's own lifetime the order wandered the old kingdoms of Kasi-Kosala and Magadha, roughly modern Oudh and Bihar, and seldom went further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its year alternated between wandering and three months of rest in the rains, spent near towns where the charity of believers was sure, so a community could only stand where lay people would keep it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Ashoka the state took a hand: Rock Edict 13 claims that conquest by dhamma had been won six hundred yojanas away, where Antiochos rules, and beyond him Ptolemy, Antigonos, Magas and Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Mahavamsa places the Third Buddhist Council at Pataliputra in the nineteenth year from his coronation, where eighteen divisions of the confession were named and the decision went to the school of the Sthaviras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its close was the signal for organised missions, and the monk sent to each region is named: Madhyantika to Kashmir and Gandhara, Maharakshita to the Greek country, Majjhima to the Himalaya, Sona and Uttara to Suvarnabhumi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Those are not merely legendary names: relics of several of the missionaries came to light in the stupas at Sanchi, built in the 2nd or 1st century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ceylon was entrusted to Mahinda, said to be the emperor's own son, who arrived in the year of king Devanampiya Tissa's coronation, about 246 BCE, and converted him and his people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A park south of Anuradhapura was given to the new church, its buildings became the Great Vihara, and a stupa was raised there to hold the Buddha's collar-bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> All of that comes from the island's own Pali chronicles, and the northern Buddhist texts ignore the council altogether, which is what makes the Sanchi relics worth more than another chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The edicts themselves never order anyone to become a Buddhist: what the king proclaims is dhamma, and he says he honours ascetics and householders of all religions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Hermann Oldenberg, <i>Buddha: His Life, His Doctrine, His Order</i>, trans. William Hoey (London: Williams and Norgate, 1882), 141-143, https://archive.org/stream/buddhahislifehis00olde/buddhahislifehis00olde_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Rock Edict 13, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 498, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 499, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","L. D. Barnett, \"The Early History of Ceylon,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 608, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Rock Edict 12, in \"The Edicts of King Asoka,\" an English rendering by Ven. S. Dhammika, Wheel Publication 386/387 (Kandy: Buddhist Publication Society, 1993), Access to Insight (BCBS Edition), 30 November 2013, https://accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/dhammika/wheel386.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["event","concept","history","religion","buddhism","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Mihintale_Rock.jpg/1920px-Mihintale_Rock.jpg","title":"The rock at Mihintale, Sri Lanka","desc":"The granite outcrop above the forest and tanks near Anuradhapura where tradition puts Mahinda's arrival and his meeting with the king. A monastery was built on the spot; the stair and railing are modern.","credit":"Photograph by Dennis Sylvester Hurd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mihintale_Rock.jpg","alt":"A bare grey granite outcrop rising out of palm forest, a metal stair climbing to a railed platform on its summit, with lakes and flat green country behind."}},
{"id":"wh-389","num":389,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Solid and walked round rather than entered, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is raised over relics, and the oldest account has a share of the Buddha's going to each prince who built one.","questions":["At Sanchi the Great <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a hemispherical dome on a plinth, about 16 metres (54 feet) high, with a narrow ledge between the two that served as a processional path.","Carved on the gateways at Sanchi, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands for a Buddha himself, since the reliefs there never show him as a man but only by a horse, an umbrella or footprints."],"answer":"stupa","answerText":"stupa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanchi begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enlarged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gateways</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 50 – 1 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>stupa</b> is a solid monument raised over relics, walked round rather than entered, and it is the oldest building type Buddhism has of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest account puts its origin at the Buddha's death: his relics were divided out among princes and nobles, each of whom built a stupa and instituted a festival in its honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That cult belonged to the laity from the start, since the Buddha told Ananda not to trouble about honours to his body, and the old rules of the order say nothing at all about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ashoka is said to have built shrines in great numbers and to have redistributed among them the relics that had first been portioned between eight favoured cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The deposit was housed handsomely: relic caskets of beryl and rock crystal came out of the stupas at Bhattiprolu and Piprahwa, and the lapidary's was the one art in which Maurya craftsmen excelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Great Stupa at Sanchi stands about 16 metres (54 feet) high without its rail and umbrella, an almost hemispherical dome on a lofty plinth, the narrow ledge between them serving as a processional path.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its first form was Ashoka's, of brick, crowned by a stone umbrella and not more than half the present size, raised beside the lion-crowned pillar at the south gateway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was enlarged later by a casing of stone, with a bigger umbrella inside a square rail on the summit and a massive rail round the base, whose pillars and copings were gifts from many devotees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The four carved gateways came last of all, and can hardly be dated earlier than the final fifty years before the Christian era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On them the Buddha is never shown as a man: his departure from Kapilavastu is a riderless horse under an umbrella and his journey a line of footprints, and each earlier Buddha is a stupa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Hermann Oldenberg, <i>Buddha: His Life, His Doctrine, His Order</i>, trans. William Hoey (London: Williams and Norgate, 1882), 376-377, https://archive.org/stream/buddhahislifehis00olde/buddhahislifehis00olde_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. H. Marshall, \"The Monuments of Ancient India,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 627, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","F. W. Thomas, \"Asoka, the Imperial Patron of Buddhism,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 497-498, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. H. Marshall, \"The Monuments of Ancient India,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 623, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. H. Marshall, \"The Monuments of Ancient India,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 627-628, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. H. Marshall, \"The Monuments of Ancient India,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 630, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["building","object","religion","history","buddhism","india","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/General_View_of_Sanchi_Tope_from_North.jpg/1920px-General_View_of_Sanchi_Tope_from_North.jpg","title":"The Great Stupa at Sanchi in the 1880s","desc":"The hemispherical dome on its plinth, the ground rail running round the base and a carved gateway standing in front of it, photographed before the site was cleared and restored.","credit":"Photograph by Lala Deen Dayal, 1885-1887, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_View_of_Sanchi_Tope_from_North.jpg","alt":"A sepia photograph of a large grassed-over dome behind a low stone railing, a tall carved gateway standing in front of it and small figures on the scrubby slope below."}},
{"id":"wh-390","num":390,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Known almost entirely from the coin legends of about thirty kings and two queens, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled the Kabul valley and the Punjab after Greek Bactria crossed the Hindu Kush.","questions":["Strabo says the rulers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became masters of Ariana and India and that Menander conquered more nations than Alexander, a claim their scattered coins do not really support.","Menander, the one king of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remembered in Indian literature, appears as Milinda in a Pali dialogue where he puts knotty questions to the elder Nagasena and is converted."],"answer":"Indo-Greek kingdoms","answerText":"Indo-Greek kingdoms","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kabul valley</span><span class=\"dt-v\">soon after 206 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yavana kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd – 1st century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Coins at Broach</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 1st century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Indo-Greek kingdoms</b> were the states that the Greek rulers of Bactria set up south of the Hindu Kush, in the Kabul valley, on the north-west frontier and in the Punjab.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a century the mountains had been the boundary, but the expedition of Antiochus the Great in 206 BCE showed the way was open, and within a few years the house of Euthydemus held Kabul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo, quoting a lost history by Apollodorus of Artemita, reports that these Greeks became masters of Ariana and India and that their chiefs, Menander above all, conquered more nations than Alexander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The claim will not bear its full weight: their successors probably held no lasting sway over so wide a country, and most princes whose coins we have ruled small northern kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost everything known of them comes from the coin legends of about thirty kings and two queens, together with one Indian inscription: the column at Besnagar, set up by Heliodorus, an envoy from Antialcidas of Taxila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Their bilingual coins carry a Greek legend on one face and a translation into an Indian language and alphabet on the other, and it was these that gave scholars the clue to the Indian scripts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Menander is the only one of them celebrated in the ancient literature of India, identified with Milinda, the Yavana king of Sakala, the modern Sialkot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the <i>Milindapanha</i>, a Pali dialogue, the elders send for Nagasena because the king is \"in the habit of harassing the brethren by knotty questions\", and Nagasena answers them and converts him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At those meetings the king is attended by five hundred Yonakas, and it is as a disputant \"hard to equal, harder still to overcome\" rather than as a conqueror that he is remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their money outlasted their rule: coins of Apollodotus and Menander were still passing at Barygaza on the Gujarat coast in the last quarter of the 1st century CE, long after Greek power there had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["E. J. Rapson, \"The Successors of Alexander the Great,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 541-542, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"The Successors of Alexander the Great,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 542-544, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"Sources of History,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 61, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"The Successors of Alexander the Great,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 549, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","<i>Milindapanha</i>, in T. W. Rhys Davids, trans., <i>The Questions of King Milinda</i>, Part I, Sacred Books of the East 35 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1890), 28-30, https://archive.org/stream/questionsofkingm01davi/questionsofkingm01davi_djvu.txt. [Open access]","<i>Milindapanha</i>, in T. W. Rhys Davids, trans., <i>The Questions of King Milinda</i>, Part I, Sacred Books of the East 35 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1890), 6-8, https://archive.org/stream/questionsofkingm01davi/questionsofkingm01davi_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"The Successors of Alexander the Great,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 548, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"Indian Native States after the Period of the Maurya Empire,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 521, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["state","history","politics","greece","india","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Menander_I_Drachm%2C_Sear7604.jpg","title":"A silver drachm of Menander","desc":"The king hurling a spear, ringed by his name and titles in Greek; on the other face Athena, ringed by the same titles in Kharoshthi. Coins like this are nearly the whole of the evidence for these kingdoms.","credit":"Photograph by ancientcointraders.com, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menander_I_Drachm,_Sear7604.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin side by side: a man throwing a spear ringed by Greek capitals, and a standing armed figure ringed by angular Kharoshthi letters."}},
{"id":"wh-391","num":391,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Grown out of the Yueh-chi who had taken Bactria from the Sakas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed the Hindu Kush after 25 CE and took the Kabul valley from its Parthian overlord.","questions":["The kings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called themselves Great King, Supreme King of Kings and Son of the Gods, the last of those titles carried west from the borders of China.","Chinese princes held hostage at Kanishka's court summered in Kapisa and wintered in India, which shows how far across the passes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached at its height."],"answer":"Kushan Empire","answerText":"Kushan Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kushans in Kabul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 25 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vima Kadphises</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 64 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kanishka's era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 78 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kushan Empire</b> grew out of the Yueh-chi, a people driven from their country on the Chinese frontier by the Huns about 165 BCE and pushed westward until they took Bactria from the Sakas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Bactria they were five tribes under five chiefs, and more than a century after settling there the chief of the Kushanas won the supremacy and gave the united kingdom his tribe's name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bactria thus became for a second time the nursery of a power that would dominate north-west India, and like the Greeks before them the Kushans crossed the Hindu Kush into Kabul and Kandahar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those first conquests were made after 25 CE at the expense of the Parthian overlord by Kujula Kadphises, and his son Vima Kadphises carried the empire from the Kabul valley into north-west India before 64 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their coins and inscriptions style the king \"Great King, Supreme King of Kings, Son of the Gods, the Kushana\", and that last title the Yueh-chi had brought with them from the borders of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Kanishka is the best remembered of the line: the excavations at Taxila put his reign near the end of the 1st century CE, and he almost certainly founded the era beginning in 78 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dates in his reign are counted in that era, and an inscription at Sue Vihara far down the Indus falls in his eleventh year, 89 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reach of the empire shows in a Chinese account: the princes held as hostages at Kanishka's court spent the summer in Kapisa, the spring and autumn in Gandhara and the winter in India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Sakas and Parthians they had displaced did not disappear, but went on governing their own states as more or less nominal feudatories of the Kushans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of it comes from a history the Kushans wrote: the dynasty is reconstructed from coins, dated inscriptions and Chinese records, and its greatest king's reign rests on stratigraphy read out of the ground at Taxila.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["E. J. Rapson, \"The Scythian and Parthian Invaders,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 565-566, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"The Scythian and Parthian Invaders,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 583-584, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"The Scythian and Parthian Invaders,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 581-582, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"The Scythian and Parthian Invaders,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 585, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","E. J. Rapson, \"The Successors of Alexander the Great,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 556, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["state","history","politics","india","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Great_Emperor_Kanishka_-_Greatest_of_Kushan_Monarchs_-_Circa_1st_Century_CE_-_Mathura_-_Government_Museum_-_Mathura_2013-02-23_5836.JPG/1920px-Great_Emperor_Kanishka_-_Greatest_of_Kushan_Monarchs_-_Circa_1st_Century_CE_-_Mathura_-_Government_Museum_-_Mathura_2013-02-23_5836.JPG","title":"The headless statue of Kanishka at Mathura","desc":"The king in a stiff belted coat and heavy padded boots, a sword at his left hand and a mace under his right, with an inscription cut along the hem that names him. Government Museum, Mathura.","credit":"Photograph by Biswarup Ganguly, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Emperor_Kanishka_-_Greatest_of_Kushan_Monarchs_-_Circa_1st_Century_CE_-_Mathura_-_Government_Museum_-_Mathura_2013-02-23_5836.JPG","alt":"A red sandstone statue of a standing man with head and shoulders broken away, in a long straight coat over trousers and thick padded boots, a sword at his side, on a plinth in a museum gallery."}},
{"id":"wh-392","num":392,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Made in the north-west under Scythian, Parthian and then Kushan rule, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put Hellenistic form to Indian religious use and reached the Jumna by the time of Kanishka.","questions":["Not one of the many thousands of sculptures of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a date in any known era, so the rough rule is that the earlier they are the more Hellenistic they look.","At Bharhut and Sanchi the Buddha is missing even from his own life story, and it is in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that he first stands in the middle of the panel as a man."],"answer":"Greco-Buddhist art","answerText":"Greco-Buddhist art","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kushan period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 78 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buddha types</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st – 2nd century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated pieces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">0 of many thousands</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Greco-Buddhist art</b> is the Buddhist sculpture of the north-west, the school named after Gandhara, in which Hellenistic form was turned to Indian religious use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It first sprang into being under the Scythian and Parthian rulers there, but it was under the Kushan kings that it produced the majority of the sculptures that have made it famous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had taken shape well before them: the Buddha types peculiar to it were already fixed and standardised in the reign of Kanishka, and its influence had reached the banks of the Jumna by then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dating it is the difficulty, because among the many thousands of sculptures that represent it there is not one that bears a date in any known era, and style gives no reliable sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The working maxim is that the earlier they are, the more nearly they approach Hellenistic work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its most consequential product is the figure of the Buddha himself, and the argument for where that figure was first made rests on an absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the railings and gates of Bharhut and Sanchi the Buddha is missing even from the scenes of his own life, while on the reliefs of the Punjab he stands in the middle of the panel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The oldest statues of him known when the case was put had all come from the district of Peshawar, so it was there that the cradle of the image was placed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How large a part classical art played in India has been much disputed, some holding it almost a negligible factor and others that it underlay the whole fabric of Indian art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the north-west it long held complete supremacy, obscuring the indigenous traditions, and as Indian influence grew stronger it culminated in the Gandhara school, which left an indelible mark on Buddhist art across Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["J. H. Marshall, \"The Monuments of Ancient India,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 648, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. Foucher, \"The Greek Origin of the Image of Buddha,\" in <i>The Beginnings of Buddhist Art and Other Essays in Indian and Central-Asian Archaeology</i>, trans. L. A. Thomas and F. W. Thomas (Paris: Paul Geuthner; London: Humphrey Milford, 1917), 117, https://archive.org/stream/beginningsofbudd00fouc/beginningsofbudd00fouc_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. Foucher, \"The Greek Origin of the Image of Buddha,\" in <i>The Beginnings of Buddhist Art and Other Essays in Indian and Central-Asian Archaeology</i>, trans. L. A. Thomas and F. W. Thomas (Paris: Paul Geuthner; London: Humphrey Milford, 1917), 117-118, https://archive.org/stream/beginningsofbudd00fouc/beginningsofbudd00fouc_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. H. Marshall, \"The Monuments of Ancient India,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 649, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","J. H. Marshall, \"The Monuments of Ancient India,\" in <i>The Cambridge History of India</i>, vol. 1, ed. E. J. Rapson (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1922), 630, https://archive.org/stream/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft/cambridgehistory01rapsuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["art","object","history","religion","buddhism","india","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Standing_Buddha%2C_near_Peshawar%2C_Pakistan%2C_Kushan_dynasty%2C_100s-200s_AD%2C_schist_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC08671.jpg/1920px-Standing_Buddha%2C_near_Peshawar%2C_Pakistan%2C_Kushan_dynasty%2C_100s-200s_AD%2C_schist_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC08671.jpg","title":"A standing Buddha from near Peshawar","desc":"Schist, of the Kushan period. The wavy hair gathered in a topknot, the moustache and the heavy robe falling in deep folds are Hellenistic; the halo and the gesture are not.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standing_Buddha,_near_Peshawar,_Pakistan,_Kushan_dynasty,_100s-200s_AD,_schist_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo,_Japan_-_DSC08671.jpg","alt":"A grey stone statue of a standing man in a heavy draped robe, with wavy hair tied above the forehead and a plain round disc behind his head, on a carved plinth in a dark museum gallery."}},
{"id":"wh-393","num":393,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Reckoned from a coronation on 26 February 320 CE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran up the Ganges valley and had its conquests cut into one of Ashoka's own pillars.","questions":["A Chinese pilgrim spent six years in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without troubling to name its king, but did record a government that fined criminals rather than maiming them.","Merchants in the cities of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept houses where the poor and the diseased were examined by doctors and fed free, while outcastes struck wood to warn of their approach."],"answer":"Gupta Empire","answerText":"Gupta Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gupta era begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 February 320 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Allahabad pillar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 360 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fa-hien in India</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 5th century</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Gupta Empire</b> was the north Indian power whose era begins on 26 February 320 CE, the day taken for the coronation of Chandragupta I, and which was reckoned in for centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A marriage into the Lichchhavis raised him from local chief to \"Sovereign of Maharajas\": he struck coins in the joint names of himself, his queen and that people, and his son called himself their daughter's son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His territory ran up the Ganges valley as far as its junction with the Jumna, over Tirhut, South Bihar and Oudh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That son, Samudragupta, had his conquests cut into one of Ashoka's own pillars, in a Sanskrit panegyric by a court poet which survives practically complete and belongs to about 360 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His boasts of sanguinary war therefore stand on the same stone as the edicts of a king who called the conquest by dhamma the best conquest of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A Chinese pilgrim, Fa-hien, spent six years in the empire at the beginning of the 5th century and never troubled to name the monarch in whose territories he was travelling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What he did record was the government: the people had no households to register and no magistrates to attend, only those who worked the royal land paid a share of it, and anyone might go or stay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> \"The king governs without decapitation or (other) corporal punishments\", he wrote; criminals were fined according to the case, repeated rebellion cost a right hand, and the royal guards drew salaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the cities the heads of Vaishya families kept houses of charity where the poor, the orphaned, the maimed and the diseased were examined by doctors, given food and medicine, and left when they were better.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same traveller records the Chandalas living apart and striking a piece of wood as they entered a city so that others might avoid them, which is the other half of the picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Vincent A. Smith, <i>The Early History of India</i>, 3rd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1914), 280, https://archive.org/stream/earlyhistoryofin00smit/earlyhistoryofin00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Vincent A. Smith, <i>The Early History of India</i>, 3rd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1914), 283-284, https://archive.org/stream/earlyhistoryofin00smit/earlyhistoryofin00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Vincent A. Smith, <i>The Early History of India</i>, 3rd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1914), 294, https://archive.org/stream/earlyhistoryofin00smit/earlyhistoryofin00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Fa-hien, <i>A Record of Buddhistic Kingdoms</i>, trans. James Legge (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1886), 42-43, https://archive.org/stream/arecordbuddhist00legggoog/arecordbuddhist00legggoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Fa-hien, <i>A Record of Buddhistic Kingdoms</i>, trans. James Legge (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1886), 79, https://archive.org/stream/arecordbuddhist00legggoog/arecordbuddhist00legggoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","politics","india","buddhism"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/India%2C_Chandragupta_II%2C_Gupta_Period_-_Coin_with_Figure_of_an_Archer_-_1977.62_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.jpg/1920px-India%2C_Chandragupta_II%2C_Gupta_Period_-_Coin_with_Figure_of_an_Archer_-_1977.62_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.jpg","title":"A gold coin of Chandragupta II","desc":"Both faces: the king standing with his bow, his name in Brahmi down the field, and on the other side a goddess seated on a lotus. Cleveland Museum of Art.","credit":"Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India,_Chandragupta_II,_Gupta_Period_-_Coin_with_Figure_of_an_Archer_-_1977.62_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a gold coin side by side, one struck with a standing figure holding a long bow, the other with a seated figure on a lotus ringed by a border of dots."}},
{"id":"wh-394","num":394,"category":"Ancient India","question":"Ten signs and a position that fixes each one's value, with a zero to hold an empty place, make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose earliest certain zero in India is cut at Gwalior.","questions":["Brahmagupta treated zero as no Greek arithmetic had, and it is on that treatment as much as on inscriptions that the Indian claim to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rests.","Carried to Baghdad with Indian astronomy, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Europe through an arithmetic whose author's name, in Latin, became the word algorism."],"answer":"Hindu-Arabic numerals","answerText":"Hindu-Arabic numerals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Brahmagupta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 7th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gwalior zero</span><span class=\"dt-v\">876 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Algorism</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th century</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hindu-Arabic numerals</b> are the ten signs in use today together with the place-value system that goes with them, in which a digit's position fixes its value and a zero holds an empty place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Place value is the whole of the invention and it requires a zero: had the principle been worked out in Greece, we might be using Greek numerals now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest undoubted zero in India is in an inscription at Gwalior of 876 CE, where both 50 garlands and 270 are written with one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bakhshali manuscript is probably older and marks the zero with a dot, and inscriptions are late witnesses in any case, since a notation is written and used long before it is cut in stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The other evidence is the arithmetic itself: early in the 7th century Brahmagupta treats the properties of zero as no Greek arithmetic does, and Bhaskara later takes the unallowed liberty of dividing by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The perfecting of the system has been called the grandest of Hindu achievements, but its ingredients may not all be Indian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Babylonian tablets use place value in base sixty, and later Babylonian records carry a zero sign that was never used in calculation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An imperfect notation may therefore have reached India, been carried across from the sexagesimal scale to the decimal, and been brought to perfection there over centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Indian astronomy reached Baghdad in the 8th century, where al-Khwarizmi saw what the position system was worth and wrote an arithmetic on it; the Latin form of his name gave such books the name algorism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The words still carry the route: to Leonardo of Pisa they were the figures of the Indians, \"Novem figure indorum he sunt 987654321\", and the craft of using them was named after a man from Khwarezm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["David Eugene Smith and Louis Charles Karpinski, <i>The Hindu-Arabic Numerals</i> (Boston: Ginn and Company, 1911), 1-2, https://archive.org/stream/hinduarabicnumer00smit/hinduarabicnumer00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]","David Eugene Smith and Louis Charles Karpinski, <i>The Hindu-Arabic Numerals</i> (Boston: Ginn and Company, 1911), 51-52, https://archive.org/stream/hinduarabicnumer00smit/hinduarabicnumer00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]","David Eugene Smith and Louis Charles Karpinski, <i>The Hindu-Arabic Numerals</i> (Boston: Ginn and Company, 1911), 52-53, https://archive.org/stream/hinduarabicnumer00smit/hinduarabicnumer00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Florian Cajori, <i>A History of Mathematics</i>, 2nd ed., revised and enlarged (New York: Macmillan, 1919), 88-89, https://archive.org/stream/historyofmathema1894cajo/historyofmathema1894cajo_djvu.txt. [Open access]","David Eugene Smith and Louis Charles Karpinski, <i>The Hindu-Arabic Numerals</i> (Boston: Ginn and Company, 1911), 97, https://archive.org/stream/hinduarabicnumer00smit/hinduarabicnumer00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]","David Eugene Smith and Louis Charles Karpinski, <i>The Hindu-Arabic Numerals</i> (Boston: Ginn and Company, 1911), 10, https://archive.org/stream/hinduarabicnumer00smit/hinduarabicnumer00smit_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","history","science","technology","india"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/876_CE_monolithic_rock_Chaturbhuj_Hindu_Temple_Gwalior_Fort%2C_Zero_Inscription.jpg/1280px-876_CE_monolithic_rock_Chaturbhuj_Hindu_Temple_Gwalior_Fort%2C_Zero_Inscription.jpg","title":"The Chaturbhuj temple at Gwalior Fort","desc":"Cut out of the rock of the fort. The inscription on its wall, dated 876 CE, carries the earliest zero in India that nobody disputes; the picture shows the building, not the writing.","credit":"Photograph by Varun Shiv Kapur, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:876_CE_monolithic_rock_Chaturbhuj_Hindu_Temple_Gwalior_Fort,_Zero_Inscription.jpg","alt":"A small carved sandstone temple with a tall stepped spire and pillared porch, cut into the face of a red rock escarpment."}},
{"id":"wh-395","num":395,"category":"Ancient India","question":"In the plays of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the king speaks the learned language and the women and servants answer in the popular dialects, which is how the two stood in life as well.","questions":["Fixed by grammarians and used long after it ceased to be anyone's mother tongue, the language of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held a place much like that of Latin in medieval Europe.","The Panchatantra, five books of animal fables and the most travelled work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, went into Pahlavi, then Syriac and Arabic, under the names of two jackals in it."],"answer":"Classical Sanskrit literature","answerText":"Classical Sanskrit literature","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Court epic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">popular by the 4th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buddhacharita</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into Chinese, 414 – 421 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Panchatantra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into Pahlavi, 6th century</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Classical Sanskrit literature</b> is what was written in the polished literary language after the Vedic period: court epic, drama, lyric and fable, running from about 200 BCE to 1100 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sanskrit stood beside living vernaculars as a learned language, its position much like that of Hebrew among the Jews or of Latin in the Middle Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The drama shows the arrangement plainly: the king and men of rank speak Sanskrit, while the forms of the popular dialect are given to women and to men of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It shows too that those who did not speak it understood it, since Sanskrit is used on stage in conversation with speakers of Prakrit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How far the style reached is clear from a Buddhist monk's life of the Buddha, which calls itself a mahakavya and was translated into Chinese between 414 and 421 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Dating any of it is the difficulty: for the most famous classical poets there is no historical authority, and Kalidasa's date was argued over within a thousand years and is still uncertain by a century or two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The most travelled work of the whole literature is the Panchatantra, five books of animal fables in prose with aphoristic verse, and it existed by the first half of the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was made as a mirror for princes: a king looks for someone who can teach his three idle sons, and the book is what the Brahman he finds composes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its tales are set inside one another like a nest of boxes, a manner of telling that Persia and Arabia took from India, the Arabian Nights being the famous case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A Persian king had it put into Pahlavi before 579 CE, and it went west from there as Kalilag and Damnag in Syriac and Kalilah and Dimnah in Arabic, named after two jackals in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["A. A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1900), 10, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1900), 23, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1900), 24, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1900), 318, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1900), 319, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1900), 368-369, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","A. A. Macdonell, <i>A History of Sanskrit Literature</i> (London: William Heinemann, 1900), 370, https://archive.org/stream/historyofsanskri00macduoft/historyofsanskri00macduoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["text","literature","history","india","language"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Arjuna-celestials.jpg/1920px-Arjuna-celestials.jpg","title":"Arjuna and the celestials","desc":"A folio from a manuscript of the Kiratarjuniya, one of the classical court epics, painted in the Kangra hills more than a thousand years after the poem was written. Los Angeles County Museum of Art.","credit":"Los Angeles County Museum of Art, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arjuna-celestials.jpg","alt":"A brightly painted miniature: an archer standing on a rocky hillside among animals on the left, and a crowd of figures on horses, elephants and camels filling the sky on the right."}},
{"id":"wh-396","num":396,"category":"Ancient China","question":"After the western capital fell in 771 BCE the court moved east to Luoyang and began the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the king kept the sacrifices while his vassals kept the armies.","questions":["When the last king died in 256 BCE and Qin carried off the nine ritual tripods, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended after more than five centuries of shrinking royal authority.","The 36 solar eclipses entered in one vassal state's chronicle can still be recalculated today, which lets the middle of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> be checked year by year rather than merely accepted."],"answer":"Eastern Zhou","answerText":"Eastern Zhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">770 – 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Luoyang, from 770 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Nan, died 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexed by Qin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">249 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["dynasty","history","archaeology","politics","bronze age","china"],"abstract":"The <b>Eastern Zhou</b> is the second and much longer phase of the Zhou dynasty in China, usually dated from 770 to 256 BCE, though some run it on to the Qin unification of 221 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name records a move: with the western capital lost in 771 BCE the court settled at Lo, a city already known as the eastern capital and later called Luoyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> King Ping was enthroned, in Sima Qian's phrase, so that he might be charged with the sacrifices of the Zhou, and real power passed steadily to his vassals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The royal domain shrank to a stretch of land north and south of the Yellow River, and vassal houses moved east with the court, the Guo State going from Shaanxi to Sanmenxia in Henan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chronicle kept in the vassal state of Lu records 36 solar eclipses between 722 and 481 BCE, and calculation recovers 32 of them, so the period's dates can be checked rather than accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Order rested on a graded hierarchy of king, vassal lord, minister and gentleman, each rank with its own rites, its own music and its own set of ritual bronzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A king was due nine ritual tripods, a lord seven and a minister five, but at Zhuolu in Hebei one grave held three and another four, an even number the scheme forbids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 606 BCE a ruler of Chu asked a royal envoy the size and weight of the Zhou tripods, and was told that their weight might not yet be inquired about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tooth dentine from 23 people at two Central Plains cemeteries shows children weaned between two and a half and four years old, and boys eating more millet than girls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> King Nan died in 256 BCE, Qin took the nine tripods the year after, and in 249 BCE it annexed the last scrap of royal land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 177–79, 191–92, 221–22, 325–27, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, pt. 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: Lane, Crawford & Co., 1872), prolegomena, 85–89, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Xiyan Wu, Pengxiang Liu, Fei Yan, RongPeng Jin, Canshuo Zhong et al., “Ancient Genomes Reveal the Genetic Structure and Population Interaction in the Central Plains of China during the Eastern Zhou Period,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 16 (2025): 1602625, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2025.1602625. [Open access]","Xin Yu, Hailin Liu, Zhe Zhang, Huiqiu Shao, Chunxue Wang et al., “Sacrificial Animals as Evidence for the Disintegration of the Ritual and Music System: A Case Study of the Zhuolu Site (790–410 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0318926, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0318926. [Open access]","Melanie J. Miller, Yu Dong, Kate Pechenkina, Wenquan Fan and Siân E. Halcrow, “Raising Girls and Boys in Early China: Stable Isotope Data Reveal Sex Differences in Weaning and Childhood Diets during the Eastern Zhou Era,” <i>American Journal of Physical Anthropology</i> 172, no. 4 (2020): 567–85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7496748/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Eastern_Zhou_Stone_Chimes.jpg/1920px-Eastern_Zhou_Stone_Chimes.jpg","title":"Stone chimes from an Eastern Zhou tomb","desc":"A graded set of hanging stone chimes in the Eastern Zhou gallery of the Henan Provincial Museum in Zhengzhou. Music was ranked as strictly as ritual bronzes were, so a set like this marked a household's standing.","alt":"A row of curved pale stone slabs, each smaller than the last, hung from cords against a fabric-covered board in a museum case.","credit":"Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Zhou_Stone_Chimes.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-397","num":397,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Between 722 and 481 BCE the chronicle of the state of Lu gives its name to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the Zhou king reigned while one great state after another actually led.","questions":["No new state arose during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the number of them fell steadily as large neighbours swallowed small ones and a Zheng earl even wounded the king in battle.","Five presiding chiefs, beginning with Duke Huan of Qi, did the king's work in his name during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and which five they were has been argued over ever since."],"answer":"Spring and Autumn period","answerText":"Spring and Autumn period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chunqiu entries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">722 – 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Within</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Eastern Zhou, 770 – 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First hegemon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Huan of Qi, 683 – 642 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jin divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">confirmed 403 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","archaeology","politics","warfare","china"],"abstract":"The <b>Spring and Autumn period</b> is the earlier half of the Eastern Zhou, named after the <i>Chunqiu</i> or <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i>, the chronicle of the state of Lu, whose entries run from 722 to 481 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mencius wrote that the world had fallen into decay, that ministers were murdering their rulers and sons their fathers, and that Confucius was afraid and made the book.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Royal authority had become a form of words, and in 706 BCE the earl of Zheng gave battle to the king himself, who was wounded in the fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No new state arose in these centuries and their number fell steadily, the large absorbing the small; Jin's greatness, an officer of the time said, came of swallowing eight neighbours of its own royal surname.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To keep the wreck in check the states invented the presiding chief, one lord directing the others and doing the king's work while professing loyalty to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Five presiding chiefs are usually named, beginning with Huan of Qi and Wen of Jin, but the list is disputed and some drop Xiang of Song for Helü of Wu or Goujian of Yue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coin was already being cast to a standard, the workshop at Guanzhuang in Henan, a walled town that belonged to Zheng, minting between 640 and 550 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Epidemics enter the Chinese record in this period, the earliest of 5,338 outbreaks compiled from the historical sources falling in 674 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A sword from the Chu tombs that yielded the Sword of Goujian, dated between 496 and 464 BCE, carries a tin-rich skin more than twice as hard as the bronze beneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicle's own entries stop in 481 BCE and its commentaries run about a generation further, and Jin, the state that had led longest, was broken up by its own grandees soon afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 178, 199–223, 262–63, 335, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, pt. 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: Lane, Crawford & Co., 1872), prolegomena, 1–2, 112–16, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Xiyan Wu, Baoxu Ding, Linyi Nie, Canshuo Zhong, Pengxiang Liu et al., “Genomic Insights into the Complex Demographic History and Inbreeding Phenomena during Zhou Dynasty on the Central Plains of China,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 15 (2024): 1471740, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2024.1471740. [Open access]","Chi Xie, Chunlin Fu, Sishi Li, Lingmin Liao, Guantao Chen and Chunxu Pan, “A Special Ancient Bronze Sword and Its Possible Manufacturing Technique from Materials Science Analysis,” <i>Materials</i> 15, no. 7 (2022): 2491, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8999735/. [Open access]","Qiao Liu, Chenyuan Qin, Shimo Zhang and Jue Liu, “Trends of Infectious Diseases, Epidemic Patterns, and the Association with Meteorological Events: 2500 Years of Evidence from an Observational Study,” <i>Journal of Global Health</i> 15 (2025): 04254, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12412270/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Ch%27un-Ch%27iu_Period%2C_722-481_BC.jpg/1920px-Ch%27un-Ch%27iu_Period%2C_722-481_BC.jpg","title":"The states of the Spring and Autumn period","desc":"A 1935 atlas plate of China between 722 and 481 BCE, with Jin, Qi, Chu, Qin, Song, Zheng, Wu and Yue coloured and the shrunken royal domain outlined in yellow. The boundaries are as they were understood in the 1930s.","alt":"A coloured historical map of eastern China, its states shaded in pastel blocks with their names in older romanisation, and two inset maps.","credit":"Albert Herrmann and Georg Westermann, Historical and Commercial Atlas of China, map 8, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%27un-Ch%27iu_Period,_722-481_BC.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-398","num":398,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Between 475 and 221 BCE seven great states fought each other down to a single survivor, which is why the era is called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after a book of its own speeches.","questions":["When three families carved up Jin in 403 BCE and the Zhou king confirmed them as princes, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is usually said to have begun, and no tradition of vassalage survived it.","Rulers who were properly only marquises took the royal title one after another during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Wei before 336 BCE and the duke of Song in 318 BCE."],"answer":"Warring States period","answerText":"Warring States period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">475 – 221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jin partitioned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">confirmed 403 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wei by 336 BCE, Song 318 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qin unification, 221 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","archaeology","politics","warfare","china"],"abstract":"The <b>Warring States period</b> is the later half of the Eastern Zhou in China, usually dated from 475 to 221 BCE and ending with the Qin conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name comes from the <i>Zhanguo Ce</i>, the collection of speeches and stratagems that is the period's own record, and some authorities open the period at 403 BCE instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 403 BCE the Zhou king recognised three families that had carved up the state of Jin, confirming as princes of the empire men who had held only the rank of marquis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fourteen states remained, of which seven came to be called the Seven Heroes: Qin, Chu, Qi, Yan, Han, Zhao and Wei, with the border states free to grow at the expense of the tribes beyond them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One after another their rulers took the royal title that belonged to the Zhou alone, Wei before 336 BCE and the duke of Song in 318 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomacy became a trade: Su Qin, who died in 317 BCE, and Zhang Yi, who died in 310, studied persuasion under the same teacher and built the rival alliances the period turned on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> War changed with it, and riding astride, a habit taken from the northern tribes, did not become usual among Chinese armies until the 3rd century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three glass beads from a Qin tomb of these centuries are of the lead-barium silicate glass peculiar to ancient China, coloured by copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A man buried at Tuchengzi had a thigh bone broken in two places, and it was set and knitted, though badly out of true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The states walled their frontiers as well: 347 earthen watchtowers survive along the wall King Zhaoxiang of Qin ran across what is now Shaanxi, and it was Qin that ended the period in 221 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 193, 263–68, 285–86, 310–11, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 1–2, 93, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Wenhui Ha, Feng Sun and Congwen Zhai, “Sci-Tech Analysis and Related Research on Three Glass Beads Unearthed from M686 in the Qin Cemetery of Warring States Period in Hejia, Zhouling,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0331048, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0331048. [Open access]","Xingyi Li, Yangyang Tong, Xiaoning Ma, Jiachen Chang and Binyan He, “The Building Concept of Border Defence Facilities of Qin: Watchtowers along the King of Zhaoxiang Great Wall in Shaanxi Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329298, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0329298. [Open access]","Haiyang Xing, Ruiqi Zou, Xiongfeng Tang, Min Yi, Zhuoting Xie et al., “Segmental Femoral Fracture Malunion: Evidence and Prognostic Analysis of Medical Intervention in the Third Century BC,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 4930, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-55300-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/The_Contending_States--Boundaries_of_350_BC.jpg/1920px-The_Contending_States--Boundaries_of_350_BC.jpg","title":"The contending states in 350 BCE","desc":"A 1935 atlas plate of China at 350 BCE, with Qin, Zhao, Yan, Qi, Wei, Han and Chu coloured, the Zhou royal domain reduced to a yellow speck beside Luoyang, and each state's frontier wall marked with the date it was built. The boundaries are as they were reconstructed in the 1930s.","alt":"A tall coloured historical map of China, its states shaded in pastel blocks with older romanised names, hatched lines marking frontier walls, and the Xiongnu named across the north.","credit":"Albert Herrmann and Georg Westermann, Historical and Commercial Atlas of China, map 9, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Contending_States--Boundaries_of_350_BC.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-399","num":399,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Asked for one word to guide a whole life, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered reciprocity: do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself, a rule he admitted was hard.","questions":["Born in the state of Lu about 551 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rose to be its minister of Crime, and the <i>Analects</i> records his conversations as his followers remembered them.","Of the Five Classics only the <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i> is even roughly the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose personal name was Qiu and whose family still keeps his name at Qufu."],"answer":"Confucius","answerText":"Confucius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 551 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Zou, in the state of Lu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Descendants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">some 70 generations</span></div>","traditional":"孔夫子","hanzi":"孔夫子","pinyin":"Kǒng Fūzǐ","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Kǒng Fūzǐ</span></div>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","philosophy","history","literature","china"],"abstract":"<b>Confucius</b> is the Latinized form of Kong Fuzi, the Philosopher Kong, a teacher of the state of Lu whose personal name was Qiu and who was styled Zhongni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was born about 551 BCE, though his exact birthday and even his birth year are disputed, and his birthplace is claimed by two places near Qufu in Shandong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His father died when he was three, and the offices he first held in Lu asked only that he keep his accounts straight and see that the oxen and sheep on the public fields were fat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He began teaching at about twenty, later rose to be minister of Crime in Lu, and was never chief minister of the state, though it has often been said he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He described himself as a transmitter and not a maker, one who loved the ancients and sought knowledge in them rather than claiming to have been born with it, and he died in 479 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Asked for one word to serve as a rule of practice for a whole life, he answered reciprocity: what you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When a disciple said he was already practising it, the master answered that he had not attained to that, which suggests he knew how hard the rule is to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Analects</i> records his conversations rather than being a book he wrote, and it was probably put together not by his disciples but by theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the Five Classics only the <i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i> can, with an approximation to correctness, be described as of his own making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mencius called him a complete concert, and the Confucius Temple at Qufu was raised in his home town, where the Kong family traces some seventy generations from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), prolegomena, 1–2, 14–16, 48–49, 56–90, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 228–31, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 372, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, pt. 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: Lane, Crawford & Co., 1872), prolegomena, 16–22, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Kuiju Li, Kezhu Han, Gele Teri, Yuxiao Tian, Menglei Cui et al., “A Study on the Materials Used in Ancient Wooden Architectural Paintings at DaZhong Gate in Confucius Temple, Qufu, Shandong, China,” <i>Materials</i> 17, no. 9 (2024): 2170, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11085686/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Confucius_1727.jpg","title":"Confucius, from a stone tablet of 1727","desc":"An ink rubbing taken from a tablet at Xi'an in Shaanxi, engraved in 1727. No likeness of Confucius survives from his own time, so every portrait of him is a later imagining.","alt":"A rubbing in white line on black of a bearded man in a tall scholar's cap and wide-sleeved robe, hands folded, with an inscription down the right edge.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius_1727.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-400","num":400,"category":"Ancient China","question":"The ladder from a rectified heart through the cultivated person and the regulated family to the well-ordered state is the spine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held official ground in China for two thousand years.","questions":["Ren, the inner moral sentiment, and li, the outward rule of conduct, are the two central words of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and counting them across its classics shows li the commoner of the pair.","Mencius held that human nature is good and Xunzi that it is bad from the outset, an argument carried on inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than between it and its rivals."],"answer":"Confucianism","answerText":"Confucianism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Confucius, c. 551 – 479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Official under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Han, 206 BCE – 220 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ren peaks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sui and Tang, 581 – 907 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Li peaks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wei and Jin, 220 – 420 CE</span></div>","traditional":"儒家","hanzi":"儒家","pinyin":"Rújiā","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Rújiā</span></div>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["school of thought","philosophy","religion","history","china"],"abstract":"<b>Confucianism</b> is the body of teaching that grew from Confucius and his followers, and it dominated Chinese thought and government for more than two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its two central words are ren and li: ren is the inner moral sentiment, usually rendered benevolence, and li the outward rule of conduct, usually rendered ritual propriety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Great Learning</i> sets out the school's ladder: to order the state, first regulate the family; to regulate the family, cultivate the person; to cultivate the person, rectify the heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Government therefore rests on the ruler's own conduct rather than on force, the aim being to illustrate illustrious virtue throughout the kingdom by beginning with a rectified heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mencius, a century after Confucius, made the goodness of human nature the school's doctrine, testifying wherever he went that man's nature is good and that the sages had realised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Xunzi held the opposite, that man's nature is bad from the outset, and the argument between the two positions ran on inside the school.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Confucianism became the official system of thought and behaviour in ancient China, especially after Dong Zhongshu's work under the Han, which ran from 206 BCE to 220 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its canon settled as the Five Classics and, later, the Four Books, and the <i>Analects</i>, the <i>Mencius</i>, the <i>Great Learning</i> and the <i>Doctrine of the Mean</i> make up the second of those.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Counting the words across 132 classic Confucian works in the <i>Siku Quanshu</i> shows li used more often than ren throughout, most markedly before the Sui and Tang, with both at their lowest under the Yuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The junzi, the gentleman the school set out to make, is still used as a measure: three studies of 2,259 people in China found the trait associated with better adjustment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 1–2, 27–34, 357–59, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), prolegomena, 40–41, 57–58, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 326, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","Bo Hu, Fugui Xing, Miaorong Fan and Tingshao Zhu, “Research on the Evolution of ‘Ren’ and ‘Li’ in <i>SikuQuanshu</i> Confucian Classics,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 12 (2021): 603344, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.603344. [Open access]","Boqiang Zhao, Changlin Liu, Palizhati Muhetaer and Ping Hu, “Through the Cultural Prism: The Influence of a Distinctive Chinese Personality — Junzi Personality — on Psychosocial Adjustment in China,” <i>International Journal of Psychology</i> 61, no. 4 (2026): e70231, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13246274/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Han_dynasty_scholars_relief_%E8%AE%B2%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%BB_%E7%A0%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E9%9D%92%E6%9D%A0%E5%9D%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%87%8D%E5%BA%86%E5%B8%82%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A6%86%E8%97%8F.jpg/960px-Han_dynasty_scholars_relief_%E8%AE%B2%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%BB_%E7%A0%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E9%9D%92%E6%9D%A0%E5%9D%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%87%8D%E5%BA%86%E5%B8%82%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A6%86%E8%97%8F.jpg","title":"A lecture, on a Han pictorial brick","desc":"A rubbing from a moulded brick of the Han dynasty, found in Chengdu and now in the Three Gorges Museum in Chongqing. A teacher sits on a raised platform and his pupils kneel on mats before him.","alt":"A grey rubbing showing a robed figure seated on a low dais at the left and six others kneeling on mats around him, all in tall caps.","credit":"Chongqing China Three Gorges Museum, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Han_dynasty_scholars_relief_%E8%AE%B2%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%BB_%E7%A0%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E9%9D%92%E6%9D%A0%E5%9D%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%87%8D%E5%BA%86%E5%B8%82%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A6%86%E8%97%8F.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-401","num":401,"category":"Ancient China","question":"The Way that cannot be told gives its name to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose founding book opens by saying that the Tao which can be trodden is not the enduring and unchanging Tao.","questions":["Wu wei, doing nothing, is the central term of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which prizes gentleness, economy and shrinking from taking precedence of others, and calls arms instruments of evil omen.","The man who dreamt he was a butterfly and woke unsure which was dreaming belongs to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose founding book is ascribed to a keeper of the Zhou archives."],"answer":"Taoism","answerText":"Taoism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Laozi born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traditionally 604 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Visited by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Confucius, c. 551 – 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"道家","hanzi":"道家","pinyin":"Dàojiā","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Dàojiā</span></div>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["school of thought","philosophy","religion","history","china"],"abstract":"<b>Taoism</b> is the Chinese tradition built on the Tao, the Way, which its founding book opens by declaring cannot be told: the Tao that can be trodden is not the enduring and unchanging Tao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book is the <i>Tao Te Ching</i>, ascribed to Laozi, the Old Philosopher, whom Sima Qian's short account makes a native of Chu and keeper of the Zhou archives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The historical Laozi must be distinguished from the legend that grew from him: he is supposed to have been born in 604 BCE, and his name is explained by his looking old at birth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its central term is wu wei, rendered as doing nothing or inaction and called a grand characteristic of the Tao, and it runs through the <i>Tao Te Ching</i> and the <i>Zhuangzi</i> alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The book names three things it prizes: gentleness, economy, and shrinking from taking precedence of others, and it holds that with gentleness one can be bold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Arms it calls instruments of evil omen, hateful to all creatures, which they who have the Tao do not like to employ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Zhuangzi</i> is the tradition's other founding book, and it closes its second chapter with the story of the man who dreamt he was a butterfly and woke unsure which of the two was dreaming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confucius, then a junior, is said to have travelled to the imperial court to ask the aged philosopher about ceremonies and music.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A religion grew out of the philosophy, with scriptures of its own — a tractate on actions and their retributions, a Classic of Purity — and much taken from Buddhism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is today counted one of China's five major religions, alongside Buddhism, Catholicism, Christianity and Islam, whose public attention has been tracked in search data since 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism</i>, part 1, Sacred Books of the East 39 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), 1–33, 47, 73, 110, 130, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi01oxfo. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism</i>, part 2, Sacred Books of the East 40 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), 235–54, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi00laozuoft. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 231–32, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","Liam C. Butchart, “Taoism, Bioethics, and the COVID-19 Pandemic,” <i>Tzu Chi Medical Journal</i> 34, no. 1 (2022): 107–12, https://doi.org/10.4103/tcmj.tcmj_77_21. [Open access]","Xianhang Xu, Mohd Anuar Arshad, Hong Liu, Mengjiao Zhao, Jiejing Yang et al., “Spatiotemporal Analysis and Forecasting of Public Attention to China's Five Major Religions,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 29116, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15396-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Zhang_Lu-Laozi_Riding_an_Ox.jpg/960px-Zhang_Lu-Laozi_Riding_an_Ox.jpg","title":"Laozi riding an ox","desc":"Ink on paper by the Ming painter Zhang Lu. The old philosopher rides a water buffalo with a rolled book under his hand, and later collectors have stamped their seals down the margins.","alt":"An ink painting of a white-bearded old man in loose robes seated sideways on a plodding water buffalo, holding a rolled scroll, with red seal impressions around the edges.","credit":"Zhang Lu, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhang_Lu-Laozi_Riding_an_Ox.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-402","num":402,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Holding that a state should be run by published standards and measured rewards and punishments rather than by a ruler's moral example, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> armed Qin for its conquest of all China.","questions":["Shang Yang's twenty ranks of merit, won by decapitating enemy soldiers and closed to hereditary claim, were the sharpest instrument of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the tradition of statecraft the Qin took up.","The historian Sima Tan coined the name <i>fajia</i> for thinkers who never called themselves a school, and European writers later rendered it as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a label scholars now find misleading."],"answer":"Legalism","answerText":"Legalism","traditional":"法家","hanzi":"法家","pinyin":"Fǎjiā","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Fǎjiā</span></div>","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang Yang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in Qin, 359 – 338 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Han Fei</span><span class=\"dt-v\">d. 233 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named fajia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Sima Tan, d. 110 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Legalism</b> is the usual English name for the current of Chinese political thought which held that a state should be run by published standards and measured rewards and punishments rather than by a ruler's moral example.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It renders <i>fajia</i>, the school of <i>fa</i>, though <i>fa</i> means standard, model or method at least as often as it means law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Tan coined the term and Liu Xiang applied it to ten works in the Han imperial catalogue, so nobody in it had ever called himself a member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four writers form its core: Shang Yang, who died in 338 BCE, Shen Buhai, who died in 337, Shen Dao, and Han Fei, who died in 233.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Han Fei is said to have studied under the Confucian Xunzi,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> who taught that man's nature is evil and needs the civilising influence of teachers and laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Shang Yang held high office in Qin between 359 and 338 BCE, and rebuilt the state on two levers so thoroughly that his own identity law later turned him away from an inn as he fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rewards were unified, so that emolument, office and rank came exclusively from military attainment,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> in twenty ranks of merit won largely by decapitating enemy soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Punishments were unified likewise, knowing no degree or grade, so that from ministers of state down to ordinary folk whoever disobeyed was to be guilty of death and not pardoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Book of Lord Shang</i> holds that a sage able to strengthen the state does not model himself on antiquity,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and it derides benevolence and filial duty as parasites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Confucius had said the reverse, that people led by punishments avoid them without shame,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while Han Fei held the law to be a state's only doctrine; the Qin burned the books in 213 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["school of thought","philosophy","politics","history","china"],"sources":["J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 12–13, 29–30, 69, 75, 125–26, 275–79, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Yuri Pines, “Legalism in Chinese Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2023/entries/chinese-legalism/. [Open access]","Yuri Pines, “Alienating Rhetoric in the <i>Book of Lord Shang</i> and its Moderation,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 34 (2012): 79–110, https://doi.org/10.4000/extremeorient.254. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 146, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]","Homer H. Dubs, trans., <i>The Works of Hsüntze</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 301–2, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.103953. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hanfeizi_or_Han_Feizi%2C_Qing_dynasty%2C_Hunan_Museum.jpg/960px-Hanfeizi_or_Han_Feizi%2C_Qing_dynasty%2C_Hunan_Museum.jpg","title":"A Qing edition of the <i>Han Feizi</i>","desc":"An 1893 reprint by the Hongwen Book Company of a Song edition, open at its title leaf in the Hunan Museum. The label beneath quotes the classic summary of the school: neither distinguishing kin from stranger nor noble from base, but deciding everything by the standard.","alt":"A thread-bound Chinese book lying open in a display case, the right leaf carrying four columns of large brushed characters and the left leaf close-set columns of small print, with a museum caption card below it.","credit":"Huangdan2060, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanfeizi_or_Han_Feizi,_Qing_dynasty,_Hunan_Museum.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-403","num":403,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Thirteen chapters ascribed to a general of the state of Wu, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds that supreme excellence is not to fight and conquer but to break the enemy's resistance without fighting.","questions":["The most quoted line of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that all warfare is based on deception, and its armies are told to take their shape from the enemy as water does from the land.","A Jesuit in Peking sent the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Paris, where it appeared in French in 1772, the first of the Chinese military classics to be read in Europe."],"answer":"Art of War","answerText":"Art of War","traditional":"孫子兵法","hanzi":"孙子兵法","pinyin":"Sūnzǐ bīngfǎ","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sūnzǐ bīngfǎ</span></div>","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sun Wu, 6th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Helü of Wu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reigned 514 – 496 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Into French</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1772, by Joseph-Marie Amiot</span></div>","abstract":"The <b><i>Art of War</i></b> is a Chinese treatise on strategy in thirteen short chapters, ascribed to Sun Wu, a general said to have served King Helü of Wu,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and probably the oldest work of its kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It opens by calling war a matter of vital importance to the state, a matter of life and death and a road either to safety or to ruin, which can on no account be neglected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five constant factors are to be weighed before an army moves, and the chapter that sets them out turns at its end on a single sentence: all warfare is based on deception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Supreme excellence, it says, is not to fight and conquer in every battle but to break the enemy's resistance without fighting at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreknowledge is the means: if you know the enemy and know yourself, your victory will not stand in doubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its armies are told to behave like water, which avoids heights and runs downhill, taking their shape from the enemy as water takes it from the land, so a general who wins by adapting is called inscrutable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sima Qian tells how Sun Wu proved himself by drilling 180 palace women with halberds and beheading the two royal concubines he had put in command when the ranks only laughed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the man existed at all has been doubted since the Song, when Ye Shi pointed out that the Zuo Commentary never mentions him although it names far smaller figures of the same years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That silence tells, because from those narratives as full an account of China between 721 and about 460 BCE can be gathered as of any European nation in the Middle Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Giles answered in 1910 that the maxims read like a working soldier's rather than a recluse's;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the book had reached Europe in 1772, its 82 reported chapters long since reduced to thirteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["text","warfare","philosophy","history","china"],"sources":["Lionel Giles, trans., <i>Sun Tzŭ on the Art of War: The Oldest Military Treatise in the World</i> (London: Luzac, 1910), xi–xiii, xxi, xxv–xxvi, 1, 6, 17, 28, 109, https://archive.org/details/artofwaroldestmi00suntuoft. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 234, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","Albert Galvany, “Sly Mouths and Silver Tongues: the Dynamics of Psychological Persuasion in Ancient China,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 34 (2012): 15–40, https://doi.org/10.4000/extremeorient.250. [Open access]","Joseph-Marie Amiot, trans., <i>Art militaire des Chinois, ou Recueil d'anciens traités sur la guerre</i>, ed. Joseph de Guignes (Paris: Didot l'aîné, 1772), “Avis” and “Les treize articles sur l'art militaire,” https://archive.org/details/art-militaire-des-chinois-ou-recueil-danciens-traites-sur-la-guerre. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, pt. 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: Lane, Crawford & Co., 1872), preface, v–vi, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Eleven_Commentaries_to_The_Art_of_War_by_Sunzi_WDL11410.jpg","title":"The Eleven Commentaries edition, printed 1190 to 1193","desc":"The opening of the Song printing held by the National Library of China, headed Basic Biography of Sunzi. The column of text is Sima Qian's account of the drill: the king asks whether the thirteen chapters may be tried on women, and Sun Wu divides 180 of them into two companies under the two favourite concubines. Later owners have stamped their seals down the margin.","alt":"A page of a very old Chinese book, dense columns of woodblock characters in a ruled frame on browned paper, with a dozen square red seal impressions stamped down the right margin and a blank facing leaf.","credit":"National Library of China, via the World Digital Library and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleven_Commentaries_to_The_Art_of_War_by_Sunzi_WDL11410.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-404","num":404,"category":"Ancient China","question":"King of Qin at thirteen, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the last of the warring states in 221 BCE and coined a title joining the words for the three sovereigns and five emperors of legend.","questions":["The 36 commanderies under appointed officials, one width for every cart axle and one set of characters for all writing were the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who abolished posthumous names.","Fourteen prisoners in a mass grave, some still in iron leg shackles, were found at the tomb of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who died on tour at Shaqiu in 210 BCE aged 51."],"answer":"Qin Shi Huang","answerText":"Qin Shi Huang","traditional":"秦始皇","hanzi":"秦始皇","pinyin":"Qín Shǐhuáng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qín Shǐhuáng</span></div>","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">259 BCE, at Handan</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King of Qin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 247 BCE, aged thirteen</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First Emperor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">210 BCE, at Shaqiu</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Qin Shi Huang</b> was born Zheng at Handan in 259 BCE, became king of Qin at thirteen, and in 221 BCE took the last of the warring states to become the first ruler of a united China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He rejected the title his ministers proposed and took a grander one, huangdi, which joined the words for the three sovereigns and the five emperors of legend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Posthumous names were abolished, and he declared that his successors would be counted by number, second generation and third, down to ten thousand generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The empire was cut into 36 commanderies under officials he appointed, in place of the hereditary lords the Zhou dynasty had left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Laws, weights and measures were unified, cart axles were set to a single width, and one set of characters was imposed on all writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The programme was the one Legalist writers had argued for, and he is said to have read Han Fei with delight before Li Si became his chancellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 213 BCE Li Si had the histories of the other states burned and required anyone concealing the Odes, the Documents or the hundred schools to hand them in, exempting only medicine, divination and agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 219 BCE he sent an expedition of several thousand young men and women to sea to look for the immortals of Penglai and the drug that prevents death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His tomb at Mount Li was built in part by prisoners, 14 of them thrown into a mass grave and some in iron leg shackles, whose bones show a diet unlike the local workers' millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He died on tour at Shaqiu in 210 BCE, aged 51, and within four years the dynasty meant to last ten thousand generations had been destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["ruler","person","politics","history","china"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 100–101, 123–28, 135, 152, 172–73, 191–92, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chóu Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 97, 268, https://archive.org/details/ancienthistoryc00hirtgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 125–26, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Yuri Pines, “Legalism in Chinese Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2023/entries/chinese-legalism/. [Open access]","Ying Ma, Benjamin T. Fuller, Weigang Sun, Songmei Hu, Liang Chen et al., “Tracing the Locality of Prisoners and Workers at the Mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang: First Emperor of China (259–210 BC),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 26731, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890548/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Qin_Shi_Huang.jpg/960px-Qin_Shi_Huang.jpg","title":"A 19th-century portrait of the First Emperor","desc":"An album leaf in the British Library, painted some two thousand years after his death and so an imagining rather than a likeness. He wears the flat imperial crown with its curtains of beads, and the inscription beside him gives his surname and personal name and counts his reign at 37 years, 25 of them as king and 12 as emperor.","alt":"A hand-coloured portrait on aged paper of a stern bearded man in yellow robes, wearing a flat black board-shaped crown hung with strings of white beads, with columns of Chinese characters beside his head.","credit":"British Library, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_Shi_Huang.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-405","num":405,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Ruling a united China for only about 15 years, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced the hereditary fiefs of the Zhou with 36 commanderies under officials the throne appointed and could remove.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the eunuch Zhao Gao tested the court by presenting a deer and calling it a horse, then prosecuted those who had said it was a deer.","Xianyang was sacked in 206 BCE and its palaces are said to have burned for three months, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and passing its commanderies and laws to the Han."],"answer":"Qin dynasty","answerText":"Qin dynasty","traditional":"秦朝","hanzi":"秦朝","pinyin":"Qín cháo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qín cháo</span></div>","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 – 206 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emperors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, then Ziying for a month</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second Emperor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Huhai, 209 – 207 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolts began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">209 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Qin dynasty</b> ruled a united China from 221 to 206 BCE and was the shortest of the great dynasties, lasting about 15 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It abolished the hereditary fiefs of the Zhou and governed through 36 commanderies and their counties, each under an official the throne appointed and could remove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its law was written and applied by rule rather than by rank, on the principle that a state runs on published standards backed by rewards and punishments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its works were built by conscripts and convicts: roads and canals, a wall along the northern frontier, a palace at Xianyang and the emperor's own tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qin had been walling its border long before it was an empire, and 347 watchtowers survive along the wall King Zhaoxiang raised in what is now Shaanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In 213 BCE the chancellor Li Si had the histories of the other states burned and private copies of the Odes and the Documents surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The damage was less complete than it sounds, since the edict spared the book of divination and the copies held by the court scholars, and it was pressed for only about three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More may have been lost in the sack of Xianyang in 206 BCE, when the fires among the palaces are said to have burned for three months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Huhai reigned as the Second Emperor under the eunuch Zhao Gao, who tested the court by presenting a deer and calling it a horse, and quietly prosecuted those who said it was a deer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The provinces had begun to rise in 209 BCE, Zhao Gao killed the emperor in 207, Ziying held power little more than a month, and the Han that followed kept Qin's commanderies, laws and offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["dynasty","era","politics","history","china"],"sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 98–99, 135, 172–73, 191–92, 203–5, 211–12, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning, and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), prolegomena, 6–9, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 125–26, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Yuri Pines, “Legalism in Chinese Philosophy,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2023 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2023/entries/chinese-legalism/. [Open access]","Xingyi Li, Yangyang Tong, Xiaoning Ma, Jiachen Chang and Binyan He, “The Building Concept of Border Defence Facilities of Qin: Watchtowers along the King of Zhaoxiang Great Wall in Shaanxi Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12380348/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/026_S-83_Qin_Ban_Liang%2C_221-208_BC%2C_30mm.jpg/960px-026_S-83_Qin_Ban_Liang%2C_221-208_BC%2C_30mm.jpg","title":"A Qin half-tael coin, both faces","desc":"A bronze coin of about 30 mm, round with a square hole, from the years of the dynasty. The two seal characters either side of the hole read half tael, the weight the coin was meant to have, and the back is left blank.","alt":"Two views of a corroded bronze disc with a square hole punched through the middle, the left face carrying two angular characters either side of the hole and the right face plain, both green and grey with encrustation.","credit":"Gary Lee Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:026_S-83_Qin_Ban_Liang,_221-208_BC,_30mm.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-406","num":406,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Buried in three pits beside the First Emperor's mausoleum, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is about 7,000 life-sized ceramic warriors, officers and horses standing in battle formation with real weapons.","questions":["As many as 40,000 bronze arrowheads have come from Pit 1 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, usually in bundles of 100 that once filled a single warrior's quiver.","Chromium on the weapons of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was long read as an ancient anti-rust coating, until analysis showed it had seeped from the lacquer after burial."],"answer":"Terracotta Army","answerText":"Terracotta Army","traditional":"兵馬俑","hanzi":"兵马俑","pinyin":"Bīngmǎyǒng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Bīngmǎyǒng</span></div>","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Figures</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 7,000, in 3 pits</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrowheads</span><span class=\"dt-v\">as many as 40,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 210 BCE, near Lintong</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Terracotta Army</b> is the array of about 7,000 life-sized ceramic warriors, officers and horses buried in three pits beside the mausoleum of the First Emperor of China near Xi'an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stand in battle formation and were equipped not with models but with working weapons, of which the bronze parts survive while the wooden shafts and stocks have decayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From the partial excavation of Pit 1 alone have come over 260 crossbow triggers, more than 80 ferrules and as many as 40,000 arrowheads, usually in bundles of 100 that once filled one warrior's quiver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figures were made from clay processed centrally and then given out to local workshops, and their paste recipes tie them to the ground of the mausoleum itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bundles of arrowheads hold together chemically from the casting workshop to the pit, which points to weapons made in semi-autonomous cells rather than on a single production line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Some weapons carry marks, three of them painted in ink on triggers, and one on a lance scabbard names the Qin central workshop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bronze has survived remarkably well, many blades still sharp, and for forty years this was explained by an anti-rust chromate coating two millennia ahead of its time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Analysis published in 2019 found that the chromium tracks the kind of object rather than its condition, and comes from the chromium-rich lacquer on the warriors and fittings, migrating onto the metal after burial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The credit belongs instead to the burial soil, which is moderately alkaline and very fine grained, and to the composition of the bronze itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian describes the tomb a century later with its 700,000 workmen, its rivers of mercury and its crossbows set to shoot an intruder, and says nothing at all about an army of clay figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","art","archaeology","history","china"],"sources":["Marcos Martinón-Torres, Xiuzhen Li, Yin Xia, Agnese Benzonelli, Andrew Bevan et al., “Surface Chromium on Terracotta Army Bronze Weapons Is neither an Ancient Anti-Rust Treatment nor the Reason for Their Good Preservation,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9 (2019): 5289, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6449376/. [Open access]","Patrick Sean Quinn, Shangxin Zhang, Yin Xia and Xiuzhen Li, “Building the Terracotta Army: Ceramic Craft Technology and Organisation of Production at Qin Shihuang's Mausoleum Complex,” <i>Antiquity</i> 91, no. 358 (2017): 966–79, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2017.126. [Open access]","Marcos Martinón-Torres, Xiuzhen Janice Li, Andrew Bevan, Yin Xia, Zhao Kun and Thilo Rehren, “Making Weapons for the Terracotta Army,” <i>Archaeology International</i>, no. 13/14 (2009–2011): 65–75, https://doi.org/10.5334/ai.1316. [Open access]","Andrew Bevan, Xiuzhen Li, Zhen Zhao, Jianhua Huang, Stuart Laidlaw et al., “Ink Marks, Bronze Crossbows and Their Implications for the Qin Terracotta Army,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 6 (2018): 55, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-018-0239-5. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 193–95, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/51714-Terracota-Army.jpg/960px-51714-Terracota-Army.jpg","title":"Pit 1, under its hangar","desc":"The largest of the three pits, roofed over as a museum hall. The warriors stand in the excavated corridors between rammed-earth partition walls, with horses at the near end and visitors on the gallery at the back.","alt":"The inside of a vast hangar over an excavation, with long parallel earth walls and columns of grey clay soldiers standing in the trenches between them, horses in the foreground and a crowd of visitors along a rail at the far end.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:51714-Terracota-Army.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-407","num":407,"category":"Ancient China","question":"In 214 BCE the general Meng Tian led 300,000 men north and linked older rammed-earth barriers into the first imperial version of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Ming later rebuilt in brick.","questions":["A single Warring States section in Shaanxi survives as 426 kilometres (265 miles) of rammed earth carrying 347 watchtowers, a prototype for the much longer <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that later empires built.","Mosses and cyanobacteria coat about two-thirds of the surviving earthen stretches of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and rather than eating the packed soil away they make it markedly harder to erode."],"answer":"Great Wall of China","answerText":"Great Wall of China","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Qin state wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under King Zhaoxiang, 306 – 251 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Imperial wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 214 BCE, under Meng Tian</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brick rebuild</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ming, 14th – 17th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"長城","hanzi":"长城","pinyin":"Chángchéng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Chángchéng</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Great Wall of China</b> is not one wall but a long series of frontier barriers, built, abandoned and rebuilt across northern China over some two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest belonged to the rival states of the Warring States period, each walling its own northern and western edge centuries before any of them ruled the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One such barrier, raised under King Zhaoxiang of Qin, who reigned from 306 to 251 BCE, runs 426 kilometres (265 miles) through Shaanxi and still carries 347 watchtowers, 89 beacon towers, 14 passes and 8 forts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its towers were solid platforms of rammed earth between 2 and 12 metres (7 and 39 feet) high, not the hollow brick chambers a visitor pictures today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archaeologists treat this state wall as the prototype of everything that came after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Once Qin had unified the country, the general Meng Tian was sent north with 300,000 men, and in 214 BCE the ground he took beyond the Yellow River was made into 34 prefectures with a wall along the bank as a barrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sima Qian reports that the campaign followed a prophetic book saying that what would destroy Qin was Hu, read as the northern peoples though it named the emperor’s own son Huhai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Meng Tian joined and lengthened the walls the older states had left rather than starting from nothing, a point Chinese and Western scholars alike have long made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Out in the deserts of Gansu and Xinjiang the builders bound the earth with bundles of reeds, and dating those reeds has turned the wall into an archive of the oases that fringed the eastern Tarim Basin from Han times onward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The brick face and hollow towers most photographs show are Ming work, and much of the older earthen wall survives at all because mosses and cyanobacteria cover about 67 per cent of it, raising its strength and resistance to wear by 37 to 321 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Xingyi Li, Yangyang Tong, Xiaoning Ma, Jiachen Chang, and Binyan He, “The Building Concept of Border Defence Facilities of Qin: Watchtowers along the King of Zhaoxiang Great Wall in Shaanxi Province,” <i>PLOS One</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329298, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0329298. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 167–69, https://archive.org/stream/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Robert Patalano, Jing Hu, Qin Leng, Weiguo Liu, Huanye Wang, Patrick Roberts, Michael Storozum, Lin Yang, and Hong Yang, “Ancient Great Wall Building Materials Reveal Environmental Changes Associated with Oases in Northwestern China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 22517, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-27071-4. [Open access]","Yousong Cao, Matthew A. Bowker, Manuel Delgado-Baquerizo, and Bo Xiao, “Biocrusts Protect the Great Wall of China from Erosion,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 49 (2023): eadk5892, https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10708191. [Open access]","Friedrich Hirth, <i>The Ancient History of China to the End of the Chou Dynasty</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1908), 250, https://archive.org/stream/ancienthistoryof00hirt/ancienthistoryof00hirt_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["building","warfare","history","china","archaeology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/20090529_Great_Wall_Jinshanling_0903_8233.jpg/960px-20090529_Great_Wall_Jinshanling_0903_8233.jpg","title":"The Ming wall at Jinshanling","desc":"Brick facing and hollow watchtowers on the ridges north-east of Beijing — the Ming rebuilding that almost every photograph of the Great Wall shows.","alt":"A brick wall running along wooded ridges, with square towers standing at intervals along the crest.","credit":"Photograph by Jakub Hałun, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20090529_Great_Wall_Jinshanling_0903_8233.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-408","num":408,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Liu Bang took the imperial title in 202 BCE and founded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ruled four centuries, broke once under Wang Mang, and gave the Chinese people their ethnic name.","questions":["Doctoral chairs in the Five Classics were endowed at the Imperial Academy in 136 BCE by Emperor Wu of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose scholars also sorted earlier thinkers into schools.","Commanderies, garrisons and state-sponsored settlers carried cast iron from the central plains out to Sichuan and Xinjiang under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the first dynasty of a unified China to outlive its founder."],"answer":"Han dynasty","answerText":"Han dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Western Han</span><span class=\"dt-v\">206 BCE – 9 CE, capital Chang’an</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xin interregnum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 – 23 CE, under Wang Mang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eastern Han</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 – 220 CE</span></div>","traditional":"漢朝","hanzi":"汉朝","pinyin":"Hàncháo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Hàncháo</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Han dynasty</b> ruled China for roughly four centuries, from 206 BCE to 220 CE, and was the first dynasty of a unified China to outlive its founder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Liu Bang, who rose out of the wars that followed the collapse of Qin, took the title of emperor early in 202 BCE, and the first Han emperors held their capital at Chang’an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line was broken once, when Wang Mang set up a Xin dynasty of his own between 9 and 23 CE, and the house restored from 25 CE is known as the Later, or Eastern, Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It marked the country so deeply that the dynasty gave the Chinese the name of their own ethnicity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of the characteristics of the imperial Chinese state and of Chinese society were first settled in these four centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Han kept the Qin machinery of commanderies and counties, which was at once the backbone of its administration and the channel along which its material culture and technology travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cast iron from large furnaces went out with the garrisons and the settlers into Sichuan, the far south and, once the Western Regions Protectorate was set up, into what is now southern Xinjiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Emperor Wu, who reigned from 140 to 87 BCE, endowed doctoral chairs in the Five Classics at the Imperial Academy in 136 BCE, and Han rulers began drawing scholars into government by examination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Han scholars settled the classical texts into the shapes still read, and it was they who divided earlier thinkers into the schools — Confucian, Mohist, Legalist, Daoist — that the argument has used ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the frontier their historians were less detached: the Xiongnu, whose empire reached from Inner Mongolia to Lake Baikal, were the equals of the Han rather than their subjects, and the version of that relationship handed down is a myth the Han themselves built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 380–90, https://archive.org/stream/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Sophia-Karin Psarras, “Chinois et Xiongnu à l’époque han,” <i>Dialogues d’histoire ancienne</i> 16, no. 1 (1990): 161–69, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.1990.1461. [Open access]","Anne Cheng, “La « Maison des Han » : avènement et fin de l’histoire,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 29–43, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.932. [Open access]","Stephen C. Angle, “Social and Political Thought in Chinese Philosophy,” <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2022 edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2022/entries/chinese-social-political/. [Open access]","Wasima Talati, Mengyi Zhang, Ting Chen, Ling Chen, and Jian Ma, “Iron Production at the Zhuoerkut Site in Southern Xinjiang during the Han Dynasty,” <i>npj Heritage Science</i> 14 (2026): 339, https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-026-02604-6. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["dynasty","state","history","china","politics","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Eastern_han_husband_and_wife_banquet_mural_BM2_Zhucun_Luoyang_%E6%B4%9B%E9%98%B3%E6%9C%B1%E6%9D%91%E4%B8%9C%E6%B1%89%E5%A2%93BM2%E5%A4%AB%E5%A6%87%E5%AE%B4%E9%A5%AE%E5%9B%BE.jpg/960px-Eastern_han_husband_and_wife_banquet_mural_BM2_Zhucun_Luoyang_%E6%B4%9B%E9%98%B3%E6%9C%B1%E6%9D%91%E4%B8%9C%E6%B1%89%E5%A2%93BM2%E5%A4%AB%E5%A6%87%E5%AE%B4%E9%A5%AE%E5%9B%BE.jpg","title":"An Eastern Han banquet, painted in a tomb","desc":"A husband and wife dining under a canopy with their attendants, on the wall of a tomb at Zhucun near Luoyang, now in the Luoyang Ancient Tombs Museum.","alt":"A faded wall painting of two seated figures under a draped canopy, with low tables of dishes before them and attendants standing to either side.","credit":"Eastern Han tomb mural, Luoyang Ancient Tombs Museum; photograph by 马学曾, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_han_husband_and_wife_banquet_mural_BM2_Zhucun_Luoyang_%E6%B4%9B%E9%98%B3%E6%9C%B1%E6%9D%91%E4%B8%9C%E6%B1%89%E5%A2%93BM2%E5%A4%AB%E5%A6%87%E5%AE%B4%E9%A5%AE%E5%9B%BE.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-409","num":409,"category":"Ancient China","question":"The routes that carried silk, people and disease between China and the Mediterranean are known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a name a German geographer coined only in 1877.","questions":["Eggs of a marsh-dwelling liver fluke in a Han relay-station latrine, 1,500 kilometres (930 miles) from any marsh, show how far people travelled on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two thousand years ago.","In the mountains of inner Asia, 258 known sites of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sit along the very paths that centuries of nomadic herding between winter camps and summer pasture had already worn."],"answer":"Silk Road","answerText":"Silk Road","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Routes west</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 2nd century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xuanquanzhi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a relay station, 111 BCE – 109 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The name</span><span class=\"dt-v\">coined 1877, as die Seidenstrasse</span></div>","traditional":"絲綢之路","hanzi":"丝绸之路","pinyin":"Sīchóu zhī Lù","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sīchóu zhī Lù</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Silk Road</b> is the modern name for the shifting web of overland and sea routes that carried goods, people and ideas between China, Central Asia, India, Iran and the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was never a single road, and the geographer who coined <i>die Seidenstrasse</i> in 1877 knew as much: he used the word in the plural as well as the singular, generally preferred plainer terms such as trade routes, and warned that no one route was the only one or even the most important.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he did hold was that the trade in silk was what drove the growth of contact across inner Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shape of the network is clearest in the mountains, where 258 known high-altitude sites cluster along paths that centuries of seasonal herding had already worn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modelling the yearly movement of nomadic herders between winter camps and summer pasture reproduces the geography of those highland routes without using a single archaeological site as an input.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The lowland routes were opened westward by Han China from the 2nd century BCE, as it pushed into what is now Xinjiang to contain the nomads pressing on its northern frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The states of Central Asia largely escaped Han control even so, though a representative of the Han was installed there to administer them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> China’s advance in that direction also helped, indirectly, to bring down the Greek kingdom of Bactria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> People as well as goods moved along the routes, and the plainest proof of it is a latrine at the Han relay station of Xuanquanzhi, in use from 111 BCE to 109 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its personal hygiene sticks carry the eggs of the Chinese liver fluke, a parasite that needs wet marshland and lives nowhere within 1,500 kilometres (930 miles) of that desert margin, so somebody had brought it from the well-watered east or south of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Daniel C. Waugh, “Richthofen’s ‘Silk Roads’: Toward the Archaeology of a Concept,” <i>The Silk Road</i> 5, no. 1 (2007): 1–10, http://www.silkroadfoundation.org/newsletter/vol5num1/srjournal_v5n1.pdf. [Open access]","Michael D. Frachetti, C. Evan Smith, Cynthia M. Traub, and Tim Williams, “Nomadic Ecology Shaped the Highland Geography of Asia’s Silk Roads,” <i>Nature</i> 543, no. 7644 (2017): 193–98, https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/1544288/1/Frachetti%20et%20al%20Final%20Revision.pdf. [Open access]","Hui-Yuan Yeh, Ruilin Mao, Hui Wang, Wuyun Qi, and Piers D. Mitchell, “Early Evidence for Travel with Infectious Diseases along the Silk Road: Intestinal Parasites from 2000 Year-Old Personal Hygiene Sticks in a Latrine at Xuanquanzhi Relay Station in China,” <i>Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports</i> 9 (2016): 758–64, https://doi.org/10.17863/cam.10406. [Open access]","Paul Bernard, “Un Chinois, des nomades et la fin de la Bactriane grecque (145–128 av. J.-C.),” in <i>La Grèce dans les profondeurs de l’Asie</i>, Cahiers de la Villa Kérylos 27 (Paris: Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres, 2016), 101–20, https://www.persee.fr/doc/keryl_1275-6229_2016_act_27_1_1536. [Open access]","Sophia-Karin Psarras, “Chinois et Xiongnu à l’époque han,” <i>Dialogues d’histoire ancienne</i> 16, no. 1 (1990): 161–69, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.1990.1461. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","geography","china","asia","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Dunhuang.yumenguan.jpg/960px-Dunhuang.yumenguan.jpg","title":"The Jade Gate, west of Dunhuang","desc":"Yumenguan, a Han rammed-earth fort on the desert road west, of the same kind and district as the relay station at Xuanquanzhi.","alt":"A large square block of weathered rammed earth standing alone on flat desert gravel under a clear sky, with a low doorway in one wall.","credit":"Photograph by Popolon, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunhuang.yumenguan.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-410","num":410,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Ranked, classically schooled and salaried, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran the Chinese empire from a county office, buried in paperwork and expected to remonstrate with the emperor himself.","questions":["Doctoral chairs in the Five Classics at the Imperial Academy from 136 BCE were an early step towards recruiting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by examination, on a far smaller scale than later dynasties managed.","In 1057 the chief examiner failed every candidate who wrote in the ornate style then fashionable, a reminder of how much becoming a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned on prose."],"answer":"scholar-official","answerText":"scholar-official","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Han academy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">doctoral chairs from 136 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Song doctorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the jinshi concours of 1057</span></div>","traditional":"士大夫","hanzi":"士大夫","pinyin":"shì dàfū","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">shì dàfū</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>scholar-official</b> was one of the ranked and salaried men, schooled in the classics, through whom the Chinese empire was governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The frame they worked in was the Qin and Han system of commanderies and counties, at once the backbone of imperial administration and the channel along which the state’s own technology reached its frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Han emperors set up central schools and began bringing scholars into government by examination, though on a far smaller scale than later dynasties would.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Emperor Wu endowed doctoral chairs in the Five Classics at the Imperial Academy in 136 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was taught there mattered, since the state the Han built rested on ritual and the character of its rulers as much as on laws and standards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By the Song the <i>shi dafu</i> were at the centre of political life, and no account of the period can begin anywhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The doctoral examination of 1057 shows what hung on it: its chief examiner, Ouyang Xiu, imposed a plain archaic prose and failed everyone who wrote in the ornate manner then fashionable at the imperial university.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The post carried a duty as well as a career, since under every dynasty an official could send up a memorial criticising the emperor’s own decisions, a practice called remonstrance and quite distinct from the censure of subordinates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How the machine actually worked is far less clear than its rules, and the real question is how so small a body of officials kept order, and even prosperity, across so vast a country for so long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They managed it while buried under fussy instructions and paperwork, liable to censure at every step, and obliged to bend the regulations in order to work at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Wasima Talati, Mengyi Zhang, Ting Chen, Ling Chen, and Jian Ma, “Iron Production at the Zhuoerkut Site in Southern Xinjiang during the Han Dynasty,” <i>npj Heritage Science</i> 14 (2026): 339, https://doi.org/10.1038/s40494-026-02604-6. [Open access]","Stephen C. Angle, “Social and Political Thought in Chinese Philosophy,” <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2022 edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2022/entries/chinese-social-political/. [Open access]","Anne Cheng, “La « Maison des Han » : avènement et fin de l’histoire,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 29–43, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.932. [Open access]","Hengying Rong, “Du péril de l’écriture extravagante et des politiques exceptionnelles : Ouyang Xiu, examinateur du concours de doctorat en 1057,” <i>Études chinoises</i> 38, no. 1–2 (2019): 291–307, https://doi.org/10.3406/etchi.2019.1670. [Open access]","Léon Vandermeersch, “L’institution chinoise de remontrance,” <i>Études chinoises</i> 13, no. 1–2 (1994): 31–45, https://doi.org/10.3406/etchi.1994.1202. [Open access]","Pierre-Étienne Will, “Bureaucratie officielle et bureaucratie réelle : sur quelques dilemmes de l’administration impériale à l’époque des Qing,” <i>Études chinoises</i> 8, no. 1 (1989): 69–142, https://doi.org/10.3406/etchi.1989.1099. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["title","institution","politics","history","china","practice"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Han_dynasty_scholars_relief_%E8%AE%B2%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%BB_%E7%A0%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E9%9D%92%E6%9D%A0%E5%9D%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%87%8D%E5%BA%86%E5%B8%82%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A6%86%E8%97%8F.jpg/960px-Han_dynasty_scholars_relief_%E8%AE%B2%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%BB_%E7%A0%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E9%9D%92%E6%9D%A0%E5%9D%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%87%8D%E5%BA%86%E5%B8%82%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A6%86%E8%97%8F.jpg","title":"A lecture, on a Han pictorial brick","desc":"A teacher on a dais with pupils kneeling on mats before him, moulded on a brick from Qinggangpo near Chengdu and now in the Chongqing China Three Gorges Museum.","alt":"A dark rubbing of a moulded brick showing a robed figure seated on a low dais and six others kneeling on mats, facing him.","credit":"Han pictorial brick, Chongqing China Three Gorges Museum, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Han_dynasty_scholars_relief_%E8%AE%B2%E5%AD%A6%E7%94%BB_%E7%A0%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B7%9D%E6%88%90%E9%83%BD%E9%9D%92%E6%9D%A0%E5%9D%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%87%8D%E5%BA%86%E5%B8%82%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%A6%86%E8%97%8F.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-411","num":411,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Condemned to castration in 98 BCE for defending a general who had surrendered, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bore it rather than leave the 130 chapters of his history unfinished.","questions":["Twelve annals, ten tables, eight treatises, thirty hereditary houses and seventy biographies are the five parts into which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arranged a history every later dynasty copied.","Succeeding his father as Grand Scribe at the Han court, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wrote privately rather than on imperial commission and gave Empress Lü her own annals because she truly ruled."],"answer":"Sima Qian","answerText":"Sima Qian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 145 BCE – c. 86 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Castrated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">98 BCE, for defending Li Ling</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Letter to Ren An</span><span class=\"dt-v\">91 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"司馬遷","hanzi":"司马迁","pinyin":"Sīmǎ Qiān","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sīmǎ Qiān</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Sima Qian</b> succeeded his father Sima Tan as Grand Scribe at the Han court and wrote the work that founded Chinese historiography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both men saw themselves as heirs of the <i>shi</i>, the annalist-astrologers who kept the memory of human affairs and of the heavens and could draw on it to advise a ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had prepared early: at ten he knew the principal ancient texts by heart, and from twenty he travelled the regions of the empire gathering what was written and what was told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worked in a generation that was editing the older texts into the forms still read and quarrelling over versions reconstituted after the Qin burning of 213 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own book, though, was written privately rather than on imperial commission, as the dynastic histories after him would be, and it is often impossible to say which parts are his father’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In 98 BCE he spoke up for the general Li Ling, who had surrendered to the Xiongnu, and Emperor Wu had him tried for deceiving the throne and condemned to castration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The code allowed a sentence to be bought off, but his family had no money and his friends deserted him, so he suffered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Writing to Ren An seven years later he said he had made 130 chapters, that the blow fell before his draft was done, and that he bore the worst of punishments without resentment rather than leave the task unfinished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i> run from the legendary Xia to his own emperor in five parts — twelve annals, ten tables, eight treatises, thirty hereditary houses and seventy biographies — an arrangement every later official history kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The judgements in them are his own, and not neutral: he gave Empress Lü her own annals because she held the real power and put Confucius and a rebel leader among the hereditary houses, while the account the Han historians left of the Xiongnu is a myth of their own making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, xxvi–xliv and ccxxvi–ccxxxviii, https://archive.org/stream/lesmemoireshisto0001edou/lesmemoireshisto0001edou_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Béatrice L’Haridon, “Mémoires historiques (Shiji),” in <i>Encyclopédie des historiographies: Afriques, Amériques, Asies</i>, vol. 1, ed. Nathalie Kouamé, Éric P. Meyer and Anne Viguier (Paris: Presses de l’Inalco, 2020), 1188–91, https://books.openedition.org/pressesinalco/27189. [Open access]","Anne Cheng, “La « Maison des Han » : avènement et fin de l’histoire,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 29–43, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.932. [Open access]","Stephen C. Angle, “Social and Political Thought in Chinese Philosophy,” <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2022 edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2022/entries/chinese-social-political/. [Open access]","Sophia-Karin Psarras, “Chinois et Xiongnu à l’époque han,” <i>Dialogues d’histoire ancienne</i> 16, no. 1 (1990): 161–69, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.1990.1461. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","literature","china","han dynasty","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/%E5%8F%B8%E9%A9%AC%E8%BF%81%E5%A2%93_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-%E5%8F%B8%E9%A9%AC%E8%BF%81%E5%A2%93_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The way up to Sima Qian's tomb","desc":"The stone stair and gate at Hancheng in Shaanxi, where his grave and temple stand; the arch carries the line from the Odes he quoted at the end of his chapter on Confucius.","alt":"A weathered timber and stone gateway with an inscribed board, standing at the foot of a paved stair that climbs a wooded slope to temple roofs above.","credit":"Photograph by dayu490301, CC BY 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%B8%E9%A9%AC%E8%BF%81%E5%A2%93_-_panoramio.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-412","num":412,"category":"Ancient China","question":"Reported to the Han throne in 105 CE but practised at least a century earlier, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave China a writing surface cheaper than silk and far lighter than a roll of bamboo.","questions":["A hemp fragment found at Lop Nur among objects of 49 BCE is one of the finds that put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a century before the court official traditionally credited with inventing it.","Chinese prisoners taken in Turkestan in 751 carried <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Samarkand, from where it made its way across the Islamic world and eventually into Spain."],"answer":"papermaking","answerText":"papermaking","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lop Nur fragment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">hemp paper, with objects of 49 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cai Lun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reported to the throne, 105 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To Samarkand</span><span class=\"dt-v\">751, after a battle in Turkestan</span></div>","traditional":"造紙","hanzi":"造纸","pinyin":"zàozhǐ","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">zàozhǐ</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Papermaking</b> mats plant fibre into a thin sheet, and it gave China a writing surface cheaper than silk and lighter than wood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before it, books and documents were written on wooden tablets, on bamboo slips bound together into rolls, and on pieces of silk, lighter but costly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 200,000 such documents, written between the 5th century BCE and the 4th century CE, have been excavated, some 60,000 of them Han slips and tablets from Dunhuang and Juyan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chinese tradition credits the invention to Cai Lun, head of the imperial workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Hou Han shu</i>, compiled in the 5th century, says that since silk was expensive and bamboo heavy he thought of using tree bark, hemp, rags and fish nets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In 105 CE he reported the process to the emperor, and it is that report rather than any first sheet that the traditional date commemorates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether he was the inventor or only the official who became its patron is uncertain; he ended by drinking poison rather than face judges in a palace intrigue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A hemp fragment came to light at Lop Nur among objects of 49 BCE, another in a Shaanxi tomb of the reign of Emperor Wu, and two more at Juyan, all of the 1st century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Shaanxi piece proved to be cannabis fibre with a little ramie mixed in, putting the craft at least a century before the report, and what a sheet is made of stays legible enough afterwards to date a manuscript by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Paper began to replace the older materials from the early 2nd century CE, and in July 751 Chinese papermakers taken prisoner in Turkestan carried the craft to Samarkand, whence it travelled to Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jean-Pierre Drège, “Les débuts du papier en Chine,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 131, no. 4 (1987): 642–52, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.1987.14537. [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Drège, review of <i>Zhongguo zaozhi jishu shigao</i>, by Pan Jixing, <i>Bulletin de l’École française d’Extrême-Orient</i> 71 (1982): 263–66, https://www.persee.fr/doc/befeo_0336-1519_1982_num_71_1_1475. [Open access]","Thomas Francis Carter, <i>The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward</i> (New York: Columbia University Press, 1925), 3–8 and 93–98, https://archive.org/stream/inventionofprint00cart_0/inventionofprint00cart_0_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Olivier Venture, “Livres et documents exhumés de la Chine ancienne,” in <i>Encyclopédie des historiographies: Afriques, Amériques, Asies</i>, vol. 1, ed. Nathalie Kouamé, Éric P. Meyer and Anne Viguier (Paris: Presses de l’Inalco, 2020), https://books.openedition.org/pressesinalco/26622. [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Drège, “Notes codicologiques sur les manuscrits de Dunhuang et de Turfan,” <i>Bulletin de l’École française d’Extrême-Orient</i> 74 (1985): 485–504, https://doi.org/10.3406/befeo.1985.1680. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","technology","history","china","object","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Five_Steps_of_Papermaking_-_Step_4_-_Pressing_the_Paper_-_As_described_by_Cai_Lun_in_105_CE.jpg/960px-Five_Steps_of_Papermaking_-_Step_4_-_Pressing_the_Paper_-_As_described_by_Cai_Lun_in_105_CE.jpg","title":"Pressing the paper, in a Ming woodcut","desc":"A papermaker lifting the bamboo screen from a stack of new sheets, with a second stack under the press behind him; the caption reads ‘turning the mould and pressing the paper’.","alt":"A line-drawn interior in which a man lifts a slatted screen away from a thick stack of wet sheets on a table, with another stack under a weighted beam nearby.","credit":"Ming dynasty woodcut, unknown artist, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Steps_of_Papermaking_-_Step_4_-_Pressing_the_Paper_-_As_described_by_Cai_Lun_in_105_CE.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-413","num":413,"category":"Ancient China","question":"A rising of peasants in yellow headcloths in 184 CE opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which closed 36 years later when the last emperor abdicated to a warlord’s son.","questions":["Offices sold with the proceeds split between emperor and eunuchs, a massacre in the palace and a puppet on the throne all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 2nd century CE.","Cao Cao, grandson by adoption of a chief palace eunuch, held the last emperor for years, and his son completed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by taking the throne in 220 CE."],"answer":"fall of the Han dynasty","answerText":"fall of the Han dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yellow Turbans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">rose in 184 CE, under Zhang Jue</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dong Zhuo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">took the capital, 189 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abdication</span><span class=\"dt-v\">220 CE, in favour of Cao Pi</span></div>","traditional":"漢朝滅亡","hanzi":"汉朝灭亡","pinyin":"Hàncháo mièwáng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Hàncháo mièwáng</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>fall of the Han dynasty</b> ended four centuries of empire, the Later or Eastern Han having ruled since 25 CE, and it took 36 years, from a rising in 184 CE to an abdication in 220.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By then offices were being sold, with the proceeds divided between the emperor and the palace eunuchs, and officials who protested were condemned to death by the hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rising took its name from the yellow turbans its members wore, and its leader Zhang Jue was a healer who claimed a knowledge of magic medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People came to him in thousands, and when his agents in the capital were discovered and killed he raised the standard of revolt; before the year was out the rebellion had reached an alarming size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His followers were untrained and were dispersed almost as fast as they had gathered, but bands went on troubling the provinces long after the brothers who led them were dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In 189 the general who was to destroy the eunuchs was murdered by them first, and the palace was stormed and the eunuchs killed wholesale, men without moustaches being taken for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dong Zhuo then marched in, deposed the emperor, set a boy on the throne as Emperor Xian and withdrew the court to Chang’an as a coalition of provincial governors advanced on Luoyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xian reigned for three decades as a prisoner and a prize fought over by rival generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The general who ended by holding him was Cao Cao, whose father had been adopted as a son by the chief eunuch of the palace and who rose through a campaign against the insurgents until supreme power had passed into his hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cao Cao died in 220 and his son Cao Pi made Xian abdicate in his favour, a precedent repeated for the next two or three centuries, and a long spell of disunity followed the fall of the house that had given the Chinese the name of their own ethnicity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Li Ung Bing, <i>Outlines of Chinese History</i>, ed. Joseph Whiteside (Shanghai: Commercial Press, 1914), 92–102, https://archive.org/stream/outlinesofchines00liun/outlinesofchines00liun_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1901), 121–24, https://archive.org/stream/ahistorychinese01gilegoog/ahistorychinese01gilegoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Stephen C. Angle, “Social and Political Thought in Chinese Philosophy,” <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2022 edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2022/entries/chinese-social-political/. [Open access]","Sophia-Karin Psarras, “Chinois et Xiongnu à l’époque han,” <i>Dialogues d’histoire ancienne</i> 16, no. 1 (1990): 161–69, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.1990.1461. [Open access]","Anne Cheng, “La « Maison des Han » : avènement et fin de l’histoire,” <i>Extrême-Orient, Extrême-Occident</i> 9 (1987): 29–43, https://doi.org/10.3406/oroc.1987.932. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","china","warfare","han dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/CMOC_Treasures_of_Ancient_China_exhibit_-_Fragment_of_Xiping_stone_classics%2C_detail.jpg/960px-CMOC_Treasures_of_Ancient_China_exhibit_-_Fragment_of_Xiping_stone_classics%2C_detail.jpg","title":"A fragment of the Xiping stone classics","desc":"Part of the authorised text of the classics cut into stone at the imperial academy in the 170s CE, of which only broken pieces survive.","alt":"A broken slab of dark stone carved with rows of clerical-script Chinese characters, the fracture running diagonally across it.","credit":"Photograph by Editor at Large, CC BY-SA 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CMOC_Treasures_of_Ancient_China_exhibit_-_Fragment_of_Xiping_stone_classics%2C_detail.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-414","num":414,"category":"Ancient China","question":"After the Han fell, China broke into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: Cao Wei in the north, Shu Han in the west and Sun Wu in the south-east, none of which ever mastered the others.","questions":["Chen Shou, who had served Shu before the Jin employed him, wrote the 65-scroll history of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that every later account, and every novel, has rested on.","Luo Guanzhong’s novel about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would come first in any vote for China’s greatest, though it was written more than a thousand years after the wars it describes."],"answer":"Three Kingdoms","answerText":"Three Kingdoms","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cao Wei</span><span class=\"dt-v\">220 – 266 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shu Han</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 – 263 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sun Wu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">222 – 280 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reunified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">280 CE, by the Jin</span></div>","traditional":"三國","hanzi":"三国","pinyin":"Sān Guó","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Sān Guó</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Three Kingdoms</b> were the three states China broke into after the Han: Cao Wei in the north from 220 CE, Shu Han in the west from 221 and Sun Wu in the south-east from 222.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wei was made when Cao Pi took the throne the last Han emperor abdicated in 220; Liu Bei then declared himself emperor at Chengdu and Sun Quan followed five years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None ever mastered the others, and the period closed in 280 when the Jin, who had already displaced Wei, took the last of them; the disunity begun by the Han’s collapse ran on far longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost everything a reader is likely to know of it comes from a novel written more than a thousand years afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>San kuo chih yen i</i>, ascribed to Luo Guanzhong, is a book of stirring battles and cunning generals that would come first in any vote for China’s greatest novel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The history behind it is the <i>Records of the Three Kingdoms</i> by Chen Shou, who lived from 233 to 297 and had served Shu before the Jin employed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began after the Jin conquest, and although he was an official historian he wrote privately; the book was copied out at his house on the emperor’s order after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs to 65 scrolls, 30 for Wei and 20 for Wu but only 15 for Shu, which kept no official historian and had to be written from documents he had gathered and from memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As a subject of the Jin, who claimed the mandate through Wei, he gave the Wei rulers imperial annals and the title of emperor, while the rulers of Shu and Wu got biographies and the title of lord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was thought too brief, so in 429 Pei Songzhi was ordered to annotate it and his commentary has been part of the book ever since; with the <i>Shiji</i> and the two Han histories it is one of the four earliest standard histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Chen Jianmei, “The Records of the Three Kingdoms (<i>Sanguo zhi</i>),” in <i>Encyclopédie des historiographies: Afriques, Amériques, Asies</i>, vol. 1, ed. Nathalie Kouamé, Éric P. Meyer and Anne Viguier (Paris: Presses de l’Inalco, 2020), https://books.openedition.org/pressesinalco/28936. [Open access]","Li Ung Bing, <i>Outlines of Chinese History</i>, ed. Joseph Whiteside (Shanghai: Commercial Press, 1914), 96–104, https://archive.org/stream/outlinesofchines00liun/outlinesofchines00liun_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1901), 276–79, https://archive.org/stream/ahistorychinese01gilegoog/ahistorychinese01gilegoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Stephen C. Angle, “Social and Political Thought in Chinese Philosophy,” <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2022 edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2022/entries/chinese-social-political/. [Open access]","Sophia-Karin Psarras, “Chinois et Xiongnu à l’époque han,” <i>Dialogues d’histoire ancienne</i> 16, no. 1 (1990): 161–69, https://doi.org/10.3406/dha.1990.1461. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["era","state","history","politics","china","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG/960px-A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG","title":"A torn leaf of the Records","desc":"Part of the Biography of Bu Zhi, written out in clerical script under the Eastern Jin, within a century of the events it reports.","alt":"A ragged sheet of pale manuscript in a display case, carrying columns of brushed Chinese characters, torn away along the bottom right.","credit":"Photograph by 猫猫的日记本, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG"}},
{"id":"wh-415","num":415,"category":"Ancient China","question":"The traditional beginning of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an emperor’s dream of a foreign god, a mission of eighteen men sent to India, and a sutra translated at Luoyang about 67 CE.","questions":["The Northern Wei counted 30,000 temples and more than 200,000 monks and nuns in the 520s, by which time <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had grown far beyond the court that first noticed it.","One dynastic history files monks among diviners and sorcerers and another among barbarians, so most of what is known of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from its own canon instead."],"answer":"Chinese Buddhism","answerText":"Chinese Buddhism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Emperor Ming</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reigned 58 – 75 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Luoyang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first translations, about 67 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Northern Wei</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Luoyang monasteries, 494 – 534 CE</span></div>","traditional":"漢傳佛教","hanzi":"汉传佛教","pinyin":"Hànchuán Fójiào","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Hànchuán Fójiào</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Chinese Buddhism</b> grew out of translation, and the oldest Chinese account of its arrival is a dream: the <i>Hou Han shu</i> has the emperor Ming, who reigned from 58 to 75 CE, dreaming of a foreign god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In one telling the emperor sent a mission of eighteen men to India, which came back about 67 CE with two Indian Buddhists; they settled at Luoyang and translated the <i>Sutra of Forty-two Sections</i>, the first of a long line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another Indian translator, at Chang’an, turned the Lotus sutra into Chinese towards the close of the 2nd century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The religion grew fastest in the centuries of disunity that followed the Han, which is when it came of age in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the reign that ran from 516 to 527 the Northern Wei counted 415 sets of Buddhist books, 30,000 temples and more than 200,000 monks and nuns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Yang Xuanzhi’s memoir, written about 545, describes some forty great monasteries of Luoyang in its splendour between 494 and 534, and uses them to tell the story of the civil war that wrecked the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It carries a long excursus as well, the account of a journey two Buddhists made through Central Asia into northern India.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The official histories were slower to take the religion seriously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Jinshu</i> files monks’ lives beside those of diviners, magicians, seers and sorcerers, and the <i>Songshi</i> puts them among the barbarians; the <i>Weishu</i>, of 554 to 565, is the first official history to give Buddhism a chapter of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what is known comes instead from the Buddhist canon, a hundred works written between the 5th and the 14th century, and from the tens of thousands of manuscripts recovered at Dunhuang and Turfan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Sylvie Hureau, “Les sources de l’histoire du bouddhisme chinois,” in <i>Encyclopédie des historiographies: Afriques, Amériques, Asies</i>, vol. 1, ed. Nathalie Kouamé, Éric P. Meyer and Anne Viguier (Paris: Presses de l’Inalco, 2020), https://books.openedition.org/pressesinalco/29575. [Open access]","Damien Chaussende, “Mémoire sur les monastères bouddhiques de Luoyang (<i>Luoyang qielanji</i>),” in <i>Encyclopédie des historiographies: Afriques, Amériques, Asies</i>, vol. 1, ed. Nathalie Kouamé, Éric P. Meyer and Anne Viguier (Paris: Presses de l’Inalco, 2020), https://books.openedition.org/pressesinalco/27160. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A History of Chinese Literature</i> (New York: D. Appleton, 1901), 108–12, https://archive.org/stream/ahistorychinese01gilegoog/ahistorychinese01gilegoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Li Ung Bing, <i>Outlines of Chinese History</i>, ed. Joseph Whiteside (Shanghai: Commercial Press, 1914), 118–20, https://archive.org/stream/outlinesofchines00liun/outlinesofchines00liun_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Stephen C. Angle, “Social and Political Thought in Chinese Philosophy,” <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i> (Spring 2022 edition), ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2022/entries/chinese-social-political/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["religion","history","china","practice","literature","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/20230609_Cave_20_of_Yungang_caves.jpg/960px-20230609_Cave_20_of_Yungang_caves.jpg","title":"The seated Buddha of Cave 20","desc":"Cut for the Northern Wei court at Yungang near their capital at Pingcheng, and open to the weather since the front of the cave fell away.","alt":"A colossal seated Buddha carved from a pale sandstone cliff, hands in the lap, a standing attendant at his shoulder and rows of small carved Buddhas behind.","credit":"Photograph by Yumeto, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20230609_Cave_20_of_Yungang_caves.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-416","num":416,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"The kings and queens of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled the middle Nile from about 900 BCE to about 350 CE, and for a century they ruled Egypt too as its 25th dynasty.","questions":["Historians split the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a Napatan phase, with its capital below the holy mountain of Gebel Barkal, and a Meroitic one named for the city further upstream.","A stela of about 1925 BCE carries the earliest known use of the name behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the people of the middle Nile seem to have used of themselves."],"answer":"Kingdom of Kush","answerText":"Kingdom of Kush","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 BCE – c. 350 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Napatan phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">capital at Gebel Barkal, 4th cataract</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25th dynasty, c. 750 – 650 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kingdom of Kush</b> was the state of the middle Nile, in what is now Sudan, whose kings and queens ruled hundreds of kilometres of the upper Nile valley from about 900 BCE to about 350 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name is far older than the kingdom, and it seems to have been the people’s own for themselves: its earliest attestation is a stela of year 18 of Senusret I, about 1925 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rulers of Kush were descendants of local Nubian chiefs who had built an independent state in the 9th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Downstream at Kerma an earlier Nubian state had had its centre, and excavation there has found settlements and cemeteries running on through the Napatan, Meroitic and Christian periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scholars divide the kingdom’s history into two phases, Napatan and Meroitic, each named after the political and religious centre of its day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Napatan capital stood at the foot of the holy mountain of Gebel Barkal near the Fourth Cataract, and its culture was marked strongly by Egypt’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its kings then took Egypt itself, ruling the country for roughly a century as the pharaohs of the 25th dynasty, between about 750 and 650 BCE, before they were driven out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Expulsion was not an ending: they went on governing their own kingdom for more than seven centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Egypt’s counter-stroke was slighter than it sounds, since Psamtik II sent his expedition south without going himself and died at least four years later, at the start of his seventh year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kush kept a language of its own in Meroitic, the chief tongue of the middle Nile from south of Aswan to south of modern Khartoum,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Amun stayed its great god, with a temple to him at el-Hassa, south of the royal cemetery of Meroë, dug since 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Claude Rilly, “Où en est le déchiffrement du méroïtique, langue ancienne du Soudan ?,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 163, no. 1 (2019): 463–87, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2019.96830. [Open access]","Janice Yellin, “La transition entre le Napatéen tardif et l’Époque méroïtique, d’après les recherches sur la nécropole royale sud de Méroé,” trans. Marie-Claire Cuvillier, <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 174 (2009): 8–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.2009.2552. [Open access]","Charles Bonnet, Dominique Valbelle and Salah El Din M. Ahmed, “Les sanctuaires de Kerma du Nouvel Empire à l’époque méroïtique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 144, no. 3 (2000): 1099–1120, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2000.16192. [Open access]","Serge Sauneron and Jean Yoyotte, “La campagne nubienne de Psammétique II et sa signification historique,” <i>Bulletin de l’Institut français d’archéologie orientale</i> 50 (1952): 157–207, https://doi.org/10.3406/bifao.1952.2089. [Open access]","Vincent Rondot, “El-Hassa : un temple à Amon dans l’île de Méroé au Ier siècle de notre ère,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 156, no. 1 (2012): 167–82, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2012.93393. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","africa","sudan","egypt","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Amun_Tempel_Barkal_SW.jpg/960px-Amun_Tempel_Barkal_SW.jpg","title":"The temple of Amun below Gebel Barkal","desc":"Column drums of the great Amun temple at the foot of the holy mountain, beside the Napatan capital of Kush.","alt":"Weathered sandstone column drums and low walls standing in desert scrub, with the sheer cliff of a flat-topped mountain rising behind them in low sunlight.","credit":"Photograph by Bertramz, CC BY 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amun_Tempel_Barkal_SW.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-417","num":417,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Kings and queens of Kush were buried under pyramids beside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the royal city on the middle Nile that gave its name to the later and longer of the kingdom’s two phases.","questions":["A temple of Amun with its own processional way and a ceremonial palace stands 25 km (16 miles) south of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Kushite capital whose surrounding island is still giving up such buildings.","An adventurer dug into the great pyramids of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1834 and carried off a queen’s treasure, which was afterwards divided between museums in Berlin and Munich."],"answer":"Meroë","answerText":"Meroë","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Meroitic phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the later phase of Kush, to c. 350 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Amun at el-Hassa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a temple of about 100 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Qore Nebmaatre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Amanakhareqerema, late 1st century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Meroë</b> was the royal city of the kingdom of Kush on the middle Nile, in what is now Sudan, and it gave its name to the Meroitic period, the later and longer of the kingdom’s two phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The court had come upstream from Napata, whose centre lay below Gebel Barkal near the Fourth Cataract, and each phase is named after whichever place was then the political and religious centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kings and queens were buried in cemeteries beside the city, and it is in the southern royal necropolis that the change from Napatan to Meroitic can be followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Meroë stood at the middle of a landscape of temples: at el-Hassa, 25 km (16 miles) to the south, a sanctuary of a particular form of Amun has its own processional way and ceremonial palace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another Amun temple stood downstream at Dangeil, below where the Atbara joins the Nile, in a stretch of northern Sudan still thinly recorded and now being lost to new fields and roads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Kush had a language of its own in Meroitic, the chief tongue of the middle Nile in antiquity, used from south of Aswan to south of modern Khartoum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its rulers wrote themselves <i>qore</i> in it, and one of them, Nebmaatre Amanakhareqerema, is placed by the el-Hassa material at the end of the 1st century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Meroitic texts were scattered through monographs and journals around the world before they were gathered into a single repertory of the kingdom’s epigraphy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Europeans met the script long before they could read it: Gau published the first Meroitic inscription in 1819, and two English travellers who copied another at Soleb in 1821 could say only that its characters were unknown and most resembled Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Meroë itself was plundered before it was studied, an adventurer digging into its great pyramids in 1834 and taking the treasure of the queen Amanishakheto, whose jewels went to Berlin and Munich, by then the kingdom having been gone some fifteen centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Vincent Rondot and Giorgio Nogara, “El-Hassa 2018 : en 100 apr. J.-C., un nouveau temple à Amon dans l’île de Méroé. Les acquis et les questions,” <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 201 (2019): 79–103, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.2019.2777. [Open access]","Claude Rilly, “Où en est le déchiffrement du méroïtique, langue ancienne du Soudan ?,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 163, no. 1 (2019): 463–87, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2019.96830. [Open access]","Jean Leclant, “Les études méroïtiques, état des questions,” <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 50 (1967): 6–15, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.1967.1468. [Open access]","Janice Yellin, “La transition entre le Napatéen tardif et l’Époque méroïtique, d’après les recherches sur la nécropole royale sud de Méroé,” trans. Marie-Claire Cuvillier, <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 174 (2009): 8–28, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.2009.2552. [Open access]","Salah el Din Mohamed Ahmed and Julie Anderson, “Le temple d’Amon à Dangeil (Soudan),” <i>Bulletin de la Société française d’égyptologie</i> 162 (2005): 10–27, https://doi.org/10.3406/bsfe.2005.2660. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["city","place","history","africa","sudan","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/A_great_pyramid_at_Meroe_in_1821.jpg/960px-A_great_pyramid_at_Meroe_in_1821.jpg","title":"A great pyramid at Meroë in 1821","desc":"Drawn on Cailliaud's journey, with its capstone and its chapel doorway still standing, thirteen years before the pyramids were dug into for treasure.","alt":"A lithograph of a steep stone pyramid rising to an intact point, a small chapel with carved figures built against its base, and a travellers' camp with horses and tents on the sand beside it.","credit":"Plate from Frédéric Cailliaud, Voyage à Méroé, 1826, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_great_pyramid_at_Meroe_in_1821.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-418","num":418,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"The largest stelae on earth were carved and raised at the highland town that gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its name, one of them standing 33 m (108 feet) tall and weighing 520 tons.","questions":["Sabaeans from Ma’rib had settled in Tigray about the 8th century BCE, and the South Arabian script they wrote in lies behind the writing of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that later rose there.","The conversion of king Ezana in the middle of the 4th century made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Christian, and its coins carry the names of his successors down to the town’s decline."],"answer":"Kingdom of Aksum","answerText":"Kingdom of Aksum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Aksum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 150 BCE – c. 700 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Christian from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the conversion of Ezana, mid-4th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great stele</span><span class=\"dt-v\">33 m (108 feet) tall, 520 tons</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kingdom of Aksum</b> grew up around the town of Aksum, in what is now the Tigray region of northern Ethiopia, where a highland civilisation flourished for some 850 years between 150 BCE and 700 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sabaeans from Ma’rib and the region of Sana’a had settled in Tigray about the 8th century BCE, in the time of the ruler Karib’il Watar, and left nearly 200 inscriptions in the South Arabian script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Ethiopian script grew out of that hand, and recent finds suggest it descends from a cursive South Arabian writing rather than from the monumental form long taken to be its parent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For a long time the oldest known examples of it were the royal inscriptions from Aksum itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The kingdom’s stonework is what survives most visibly: its builders carved and raised the largest stelae on earth, the greatest of them 33 m (108 feet) tall and weighing 520 tons, hauled 5 km (3 miles) from the hills of Gobo Dura to the northwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Aksum struck its own coinage, and silver pieces survive from the kings who reigned before Christianity, in the 3rd and the beginning of the 4th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its gold has been analysed again and again, more than 200 specimens in four published studies, because the metal is one of the few ways of putting the Aksumite kings in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The conversion of king Ezana in the middle of the 4th century marks the beginning of the Christian era in Ethiopia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Building followed at once, and the sacred quarter was soon shut in by a bastioned wall, with crosses cut into pottery and modelled in terracotta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nearly four hundred coins carry the names of the kings from Ezana’s immediate successors down to the decline of the ancient town, about the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Tekle Hagos, “Current Management Challenges at the World Heritage site of Aksum, Ethiopia,” <i>Annales d’Éthiopie</i> 32 (2018): 293–315, https://doi.org/10.3406/ethio.2018.1658. [Open access]","Jean-François Breton, review of <i>Recueil des inscriptions de l’Éthiopie des périodes pré-axoumite et axoumite</i>, tome III, by Abraham J. Drewes, <i>Annales d’Éthiopie</i> 34 (2022): 387–89, https://www.persee.fr/doc/ethio_0066-2127_2022_num_34_1_1725. [Open access]","Abraham-Johannes Drewes and Roger Schneider, “Origine et développement de l’écriture éthiopienne jusqu’à l’époque des inscriptions royales d’Axoum,” <i>Annales d’Éthiopie</i> 10 (1976): 95–107, https://doi.org/10.3406/ethio.1976.1163. [Open access]","Henri de Contenson, “Aperçus sur les fouilles à Axoum et dans la région d’Axoum en 1958 et 1959,” <i>Annales d’Éthiopie</i> 3 (1959): 101–6, https://doi.org/10.3406/ethio.1959.1302. [Open access]","Jean-Noël Barrandon, Éric Godet and Cécile Morrisson, “Le monnayage d’or axoumite : une altération particulière,” <i>Revue numismatique</i> 6, no. 32 (1990): 186–211, https://doi.org/10.3406/numi.1990.2024. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","africa","ethiopia","religion","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Stelen_von_Aksum.JPG/960px-Stelen_von_Aksum.JPG","title":"Standing stelae at Aksum","desc":"Two of the carved monoliths of the northern stelae field, cut with storeys of false windows and a false door at the foot.","alt":"Two tall slender stone monoliths standing against an evening sky, carved with rows of rectangular panels, with a woman in a white shawl walking past them for scale.","credit":"Photograph by Fabian Lambeck, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stelen_von_Aksum.JPG"}},
{"id":"wh-419","num":419,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"The earliest large figurative sculpture in Africa outside Egypt was made by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of central Nigeria, whose terracotta figures begin about 900 BCE and whose sites run to about 1 BCE.","questions":["More than 11,000 plant remains from 50 sites show that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ate pearl millet above all, though the complete absence of chaff suggests the grain arrived already clean.","Acid soils have destroyed every bone at sites of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so nobody can say whether its people kept animals or lived by hunting alone."],"answer":"Nok culture","answerText":"Nok culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Nok culture</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First figurines</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about the mid-1st millennium BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Nok culture</b> of central Nigeria is among the best known of prehistoric West Africa, and its sites are dated from about 1500 to about 1 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its terracotta figures are the earliest large figurative sculpture made in Africa outside Egypt, and the first of them appear about 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nok also carries early evidence of iron working in West Africa, though the reinvestigated furnaces at Taruga put the technology only at about the middle of the 1st millennium BCE, later than the first figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A project begun at Frankfurt in 2005 recorded more than 300 sites west of the Jos Plateau and examined or excavated 80 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Before it almost nothing had been asked about the context of the figures, which had been admired for decades on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The people who made them farmed: more than 11,000 plant remains from 50 sites are dominated by pearl millet, with cowpea and the fruit of <i>Canarium schweinfurthii</i> behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chaff is entirely absent from that assemblage, and the absence is real rather than a failure of sieving, so the excavated places look like sites where clean grain was brought in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the stone-pot arrangements read as ritual places tied to burial, that points to feasting rather than to ordinary domestic life at most of the sites dug so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fat residues in 458 potsherds from twelve sites carry bee products in more than a third of the vessels that yielded any lipid at all, the earliest such evidence in West Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the ground cannot say is whether the Nok kept animals, since its acid soils have destroyed every bone, and illicit digging for figures has churned the pits that remain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Julie Dunne, Alexa Höhn, Gabriele Franke, Katharina Neumann, Peter Breunig, Toby Gillard, Caitlin Walton-Doyle and Richard P. Evershed, “Honey-collecting in prehistoric West Africa from 3500 years ago,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 12 (2021): 2227, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-021-22425-4. [Open access]","Louis Champion, Alexa Höhn, Katharina Neumann, Gabriele Franke and Peter Breunig, “A question of rite—pearl millet consumption at Nok culture sites, Nigeria (second/first millennium BC),” <i>Vegetation History and Archaeobotany</i> 32, no. 3 (2023): 263–83, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00334-022-00902-0. [Open access]","Peter Breunig and James Ameje, “The making of ‘Nok terracotta’,” <i>Afrique : Archéologie &amp; Arts</i> 4 (2006): 91–102, https://doi.org/10.4000/aaa.1398. [Open access]","Angela Fagg Rackham, Gabriele Franke, Henrik Junius, Tanja M. Männel and Christina Beck, “Early West African Iron Smelting: The Legacy of Taruga in Light of Recent Nok Research,” <i>African Archaeological Review</i> 34, no. 3 (2017): 321–43, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10437-017-9262-2. [Paywalled]","Alexa Höhn, Gabriele Franke and Annika Schmidt, “Pits at Pangwari: Charcoal Taphonomy at a Multi-phased Nok Site, Central Nigeria,” in <i>Plants and People in the African Past</i> (Cham: Springer, 2018), 271–99, https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-89839-1_14. [Paywalled]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","history","africa","nigeria","art","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Male_Head%2C_Nok_culture%2C_Kaduna%2C_Plateau%2C_or_Nassarawa_state%2C_Nigeria%2C_550-50_BC%2C_terracotta_-_Brooklyn_Museum_-_Brooklyn%2C_NY_-_DSC08511.JPG/960px-Male_Head%2C_Nok_culture%2C_Kaduna%2C_Plateau%2C_or_Nassarawa_state%2C_Nigeria%2C_550-50_BC%2C_terracotta_-_Brooklyn_Museum_-_Brooklyn%2C_NY_-_DSC08511.JPG","title":"A Nok head in terracotta","desc":"Dated by the museum to between 550 and 50 BCE, with the pierced pupils and nostrils and the heavy ridged brows that mark the style.","alt":"A life-sized head modelled in pale coarse clay, seen from the front: heavy ridged eyebrows above almond eyes whose pupils are pierced right through, a broad nose with drilled nostrils, and a curved moustache above parted lips.","credit":"Brooklyn Museum, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_Head%2C_Nok_culture%2C_Kaduna%2C_Plateau%2C_or_Nassarawa_state%2C_Nigeria%2C_550-50_BC%2C_terracotta_-_Brooklyn_Museum_-_Brooklyn%2C_NY_-_DSC08511.JPG"}},
{"id":"wh-420","num":420,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"More than 500 languages spoken today by some 240 million people descend from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began in western Africa between about 6,000 and 4,000 years ago.","questions":["Phylogeographic modelling puts the route of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> straight through the Congo rainforest about 4,420 years ago, well before the Sangha River Interval corridor had opened.","Genome-wide data show that speakers who moved during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took in gene flow from the local groups whose country they entered, rather than displacing them."],"answer":"Bantu expansion","answerText":"Bantu expansion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">western Africa, c. 4000 – 2000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Forest route</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2500 BCE on one model</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Languages today</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 500, about 240 million speakers</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Bantu expansion</b> is the spread of the Bantu languages and of the people who spoke them across most of sub-Saharan Africa, beginning in western Africa between about 6,000 and 4,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the largest demographic event of the late Holocene in Africa, and more than 500 Bantu languages are spoken today by some 240 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There is broad agreement across the disciplines that the first spread was demic, people moving and not words alone, and that the route ran through the Congo rainforest before reaching the savannas east and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is still argued over is the pathway taken and the manner of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One phylogeographic study weighed four proposals against each other: an early split north of the forest, a passage through the Sangha River Interval corridor about 2,500 years ago, a route along the coast about 4,000 years ago, and a crossing of the forest itself before that corridor opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its answer was the forest crossing, at about 4,420 years ago with a 95% interval running from 4,040 to 5,000, well before the corridor was open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genome-wide work from the southern edges of the expansion agrees on a late split between western and eastern speakers, after a joint passage through the rainforest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genetic diversity falls the further one goes from western Africa, which fits a serial-founder model, and admixture dates that rise with distance argue for a fairly steady rate of movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The newcomers did not simply displace the people already there: Bantu speakers took in substantial gene flow from local groups in the regions they moved into, and small forager ancestries remain measurable in living populations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One explanation the evidence does not bear out is an environmental one, since reconstructed climate and vegetation do not predict where or when the languages divided, where plain geographical distance does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Cesar A. Fortes-Lima et al., “The genetic legacy of the expansion of Bantu-speaking peoples in Africa,” <i>Nature</i> 625, no. 7995 (2024): 540–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06770-6. [Open access]","Ezequiel Koile, Simon J. Greenhill, Damián E. Blasi, Remco Bouckaert and Russell D. Gray, “Phylogeographic analysis of the Bantu language expansion supports a rainforest route,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 32 (2022): e2112853119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2112853119. [Open access]","Armando Semo et al., “Along the Indian Ocean Coast: Genomic Variation in Mozambique Provides New Insights into the Bantu Expansion,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 37, no. 2 (2020): 406–16, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msz224. [Open access]","Sam Tallman, Maria das Dores Sungo, Sílvio Saranga and Sandra Beleza, “Whole genomes from Angola and Mozambique inform about the origins and dispersals of major African migrations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 14 (2023): 7967, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-43717-x. [Open access]","Robert Beyer, Joy S. Singarayer, Jay T. Stock and Andrea Manica, “Environmental conditions do not predict diversification rates in the Bantu languages,” <i>Heliyon</i> 5, no. 10 (2019): e02630, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2019.e02630. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","africa","migration","language","prehistory"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Map_of_the_Bantu_languages.svg/960px-Map_of_the_Bantu_languages.svg.png","title":"Where the Bantu languages are spoken","desc":"The Bantu branch shaded within the wider Niger-Congo family; fewer than a fifth of the languages will fit on the map.","alt":"A map of Africa with the whole of the continent south of a line from Cameroon to Kenya shaded pink and labelled with dozens of language names, the rest of West Africa in greys.","credit":"Map by SUM1 after Sting, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Bantu_languages.svg"}},
{"id":"wh-421","num":421,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Foggaras dug between pairs of vertical shafts watered the oases of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose capital Garama can still be seen at Germa in the Fezzan of south-western Libya.","questions":["Herodotus placed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ten days west of Augila, calling them in one passage a feeble people ignorant of weapons and in another a warlike nation of raiders.","Cornelius Balbus led a punitive expedition into the Fezzan against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 19 BCE, and Pliny records that they filled their own wells with sand against such raids."],"answer":"Garamantes","answerText":"Garamantes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman raid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Balbus into the Fezzan, 19 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Defeated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Quirinius, about 20 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Garama</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Julius Maternus, about 100 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Garamantes</b> were the people of the Fezzan, in what is now south-western Libya, and the capital of their kingdom, Garama, can still be seen at Germa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their reach ran east to about the borders of modern Libya and a good deal further west and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest surviving Greek account of them is confused: Herodotus places them ten days west of Augila, then calls them in one passage a feeble people ignorant of weapons and in another a warlike nation of raiders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What political form they had is still an open question, whether a band, a tribe, a chiefdom or a Saharan state defined on its own terms, and it has been hard to answer for want of archaeological evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome came to them rather than the other way about, Cornelius Balbus leading a punitive expedition into the Fezzan against them in 19 BCE and Quirinius beating them near their own country about 20 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Pliny records how they fought back, filling their wells with sand so that the road into their country could not be kept open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Water was their real achievement, and they took it from foggaras, underground channels that tap an aquifer and carry it out to daylight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A foggara gallery is dug between pairs of vertical shafts sunk 4 to 50 m (13 to 160 feet) apart, running at a shallower gradient than the ground above until it meets the surface and the water comes out into a channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The technique is older than they are, going back to the Achaemenid or Assyrian empires, and it has watered desert settlement over a great part of the earth for more than two and a half thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its spread across North Africa has been tied to the growth of trade across the Sahara; what they did not have is the emerald of legend, since no ancient text about Libya names an emerald, or even a green stone, from their country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["David Mattingly, “Nouveaux aperçus sur les Garamantes : un État saharien ?,” <i>Antiquités africaines</i> 37 (2001): 45–61, https://doi.org/10.3406/antaf.2001.1331. [Open access]","René Rebuffat, “La Tripolitaine romaine et les peuples indigènes,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 162, no. 2 (2018): 517–49, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2018.96503. [Open access]","Andrew Wilson, “Foggaras in ancient North Africa : or how to marry a Berber princess,” <i>Publications de l’École française de Rome</i> 426 (2009): 19–39, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_2009_act_426_1_9828. [Paywalled]","Théodore Monod, “Le mythe de « l’Émeraude des Garamantes »,” <i>Antiquités africaines</i> 8 (1974): 51–66, https://doi.org/10.3406/antaf.1974.944. [Open access]","Oric Bates, <i>The Eastern Libyans: An Essay</i> (London: Macmillan, 1914), https://archive.org/stream/easternlibyanses00bate/easternlibyanses00bate_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["people","history","africa","libya","technology","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Germa_-_Garamantengr%C3%A4ber.jpg/960px-Germa_-_Garamantengr%C3%A4ber.jpg","title":"Garamantian tombs at Germa","desc":"A row of stepped tombs on the sand outside the old capital, with the escarpment of the wadi behind them.","alt":"Eight or nine low stepped tombs of pale mudbrick standing in a line on flat desert sand, with a dark rock escarpment along the horizon behind.","credit":"Photograph by Franzfoto, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germa_-_Garamantengr%C3%A4ber.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-422","num":422,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Egyptian ships fetched incense, gold and live baboons from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a country the pharaohs called the land of the god and whose position is still argued over.","questions":["Strontium and oxygen in 155 living baboons gave a chemical map that placed two mummified New Kingdom animals, and so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in the Horn of Africa.","Long placed in Somalia or southern Arabia, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now sought on the African shore of the Red Sea, perhaps at the port later known as Adulis."],"answer":"Land of Punt","answerText":"Land of Punt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sought from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Old Kingdom, c. 2800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hatshepsut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">her expedition, c. 1473 – 1458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Baboon mummy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Valley of the Monkeys, c. 800 – 540 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Land of Punt</b> was a country the Egyptians traded with for well over a thousand years, which they called the land of the god and knew for its incense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gold, turquoise, cedar and perfumes were wanted from the Old Kingdom onwards for temple ritual and for the cult of the dead, and pharaohs sent distant expeditions under high officials, with soldiers, to fetch them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where Punt actually lay has been argued over for as long as it has been studied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was long placed in Somalia or on the southern coast of Arabia, a guess that took little account of the distances a ship would have had to cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Egyptian end of the route is now visible on the ground, in a line of harbour sites along the Red Sea coast at Mersa Gawasis, Ayn Soukhna, El-Markha and Wadi el-Jarf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The far end has been approached instead through the baboons the Egyptians brought home, which they held sacred and mummified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oxygen and strontium in 155 living baboons from 77 places gave a chemical map of the region against which the mummies could be placed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of them, from the New Kingdom, came from a region taking in much of Ethiopia, Eritrea and Djibouti and parts of Somalia and Yemen, while five later ones had lived long enough in Egypt to have been bred there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A mummy of about 800 to 540 BCE from the Valley of the Monkeys was then found to carry a mitochondrial genome matching baboons living now in Eritrea, Ethiopia and eastern Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That points at the port of Adulis on the Eritrean coast, and has been taken to suggest that Punt and Adulis were the same trading place a thousand years apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Nathaniel J. Dominy, Salima Ikram, Gillian L. Moritz, Patrick V. Wheatley, John N. Christensen, Jonathan W. Chipman and Paul L. Koch, “Mummified baboons reveal the far reach of early Egyptian mariners,” <i>eLife</i> 9 (2020): e60860, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.60860. [Open access]","Franziska Grathwol et al., “Adulis and the transshipment of baboons during classical antiquity,” <i>eLife</i> 12 (2023): e87513, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.87513. [Open access]","Jean Leclant, “L’Exploration des côtes de la mer Rouge. A la quête de Pount et des secrets de la mer Erythrée,” <i>Annales d’Éthiopie</i> 11 (1978): 69–73, https://doi.org/10.3406/ethio.1978.905. [Open access]","Pierre Tallet, “Les Égyptiens et le littoral de la mer Rouge à l’époque pharaonique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 153, no. 2 (2009): 687–719, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2009.92529. [Open access]","Catherine Chadefaud, “À la recherche de l’or, de la turquoise, du cèdre et des parfums précieux. Les expéditions royales dans l’Égypte ancienne,” <i>Actes des congrès nationaux des sociétés historiques et scientifiques</i> 130 (2008): 11–23, https://www.persee.fr/doc/acths_1764-7355_2008_act_130_3_1450. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","history","egypt","africa","geography","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Baboon_mummy_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_P_1485_p016.jpg/960px-Baboon_mummy_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_P_1485_p016.jpg","title":"The hand of a mummified baboon","desc":"Still wrapped in linen at the wrist; it is animals such as this one whose chemistry has been used to look for Punt.","alt":"The dried hand and forearm of a baboon lying on a pale surface beside a centimetre scale, the fingers curled and the wrist bound in coarse linen.","credit":"Museo Egizio, Turin, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baboon_mummy_-_Museo_Egizio%2C_Turin_P_1485_p016.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-423","num":423,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"The earliest hard evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not a route on a map but glass, copper and cowries buried in Sahelian graves long before Arab merchants organised the crossing.","questions":["Rock carvings of horse-drawn chariots strung across the desert were long read as proof of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though nothing shows the vehicles ever carried freight from one side to the other.","The Garamantes of the Fezzan are routinely cast as the middlemen of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, yet no object of demonstrably West African origin has been found anywhere in their oases."],"answer":"trans-Saharan trade","answerText":"trans-Saharan trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sahel imports</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 – 700 CE, at Kissi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dromedary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on both margins, 3rd century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold northwards</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the mid-8th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sahelian states</span><span class=\"dt-v\">beads, 7th – 13th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Trans-Saharan trade</b> is the exchange of goods, people and ideas across the Sahara, between the Mediterranean world to its north and the Sahel and savanna belts to its south. Caravans carried gold and enslaved people north and salt, cloth and beads south, and by the 7th to 13th centuries CE Egyptian, Levantine and Middle Eastern glass was reaching rural sites in Mali and Senegal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even that traffic is argued over, since isotope work on Fatimid gold coins finds copper and iron signatures matching West African ores beside lead signatures that do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Before Arab armies reached North Africa the record is thinner, almost nothing tangible being reported of the lands beyond the desert’s southern fringe until the first Arabic sources appear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stories filled the gap, of Phoenician and Carthaginian gold and of routes said to have been kept deliberately secret, though no Roman coin has ever been excavated at a West African site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The desert’s own rock art looked like harder evidence, with hundreds of two-wheeled vehicles drawn by horses and sometimes oxen engraved and painted across the Tassili n’Ajjer and the Fezzan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The questions of their age, origin and function were posed early and perhaps answered too easily, and they read better as an aristocratic society’s self-image than as freight routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water carried the traffic, and the northern oases were fed by foggaras, galleries tunnelled between paired shafts to tap groundwater, whose spread across North Africa is argued to be bound up with the trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Garamantes who dug them are routinely cast as middlemen, yet nothing demonstrably West African has been found in their territory, and Sahelian-looking pottery from the Wadi Tanezzuft proved to be local clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The firm early evidence lies south instead, in Middle Eastern glass, Mediterranean copper alloys and Indian Ocean cowries buried at Kissi in Burkina Faso between the 2nd and 7th centuries CE, with dromedaries on both margins from the 3rd century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Sonja Magnavita, “Initial Encounters: Seeking traces of ancient trade connections between West Africa and the wider world,” <i>Afriques</i> 4 (2013), https://doi.org/10.4000/afriques.1145. [Open access]","Gabriel Camps, “Les chars sahariens. Images d’une société aristocratique,” <i>Antiquités africaines</i> 25 (1989): 11–40, https://doi.org/10.3406/antaf.1989.1152. [Paywalled]","Andrew Wilson, “Foggaras in ancient North Africa : or how to marry a Berber princess,” <i>Publications de l’École française de Rome</i> 426 (2009): 19–39, https://www.persee.fr/doc/efr_0223-5099_2009_act_426_1_9828. [Paywalled]","Miriam Truffa Giachet, Bernard Gratuze, Anne Mayor and Eric Huysecom, “Compositional and provenance study of glass beads from archaeological sites in Mali and Senegal at the time of the first Sahelian states,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 12 (2020): e0242027, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0242027. [Open access]","Louise de Palaminy, Sandrine Baron, Franck Poitrasson, Robert Kool, Maryse Blet-Lemarquand and François-Xavier Fauvelle, “New insights into the Trans-Saharan gold trade in the Islamic Middle Ages revealed by multi-isotopic (Pb–Fe–Cu) and elemental characterization of Fatimid gold coins,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0353759, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0353759. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","africa","iron age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Foggara_in_Tala.jpg/960px-Foggara_in_Tala.jpg","title":"A foggara delivering water at Tala","desc":"Groundwater led out of an underground gallery in the Algerian Sahara and split between channels; the spread of these works across North Africa has been argued to be bound up with the growth of the trade.","alt":"Sunlit sand and bare rock in which a shallow stone-lined basin holds brown water, fed from a tunnel mouth at one end and divided at the other by a low comb of slabs into several small channels.","credit":"Bernard Gagnon, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foggara_in_Tala.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-424","num":424,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Ten of the seventeen known colossal basalt heads stand at San Lorenzo, the first urban centre of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on the humid Gulf lowlands of southern Veracruz and Tabasco.","questions":["Serpentinite carried from Oaxaca and Puebla was buried by the ton at the centres of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose own lowland country yields almost no stone to carve or to build with.","Ceremonial complexes raised by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 1100 and 750 BCE are aligned on dates 13 and 20 days apart, the oldest trace of the 260-day calendar."],"answer":"Olmecs","answerText":"Olmecs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Olmec era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">San Lorenzo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">apogee c. 1400 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">La Venta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tres Zapotes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">four plazas, 400 BCE – 300 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Olmecs</b> are the earliest of Mesoamerica’s monument-building societies, known from the humid lowlands of southern Veracruz and Tabasco on the Gulf of Mexico between about 1500 and 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their country has almost no stone of its own and little else in it survives burial, so what is left of them is chiefly what they carved and what they hid in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three great centres followed one another, San Lorenzo, then La Venta, then Tres Zapotes, each raising earthen platforms and basalt monuments cut from blocks hauled up to 70 km (43 miles) from the Tuxtla Mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At San Lorenzo a natural plateau was levelled on a scale implying command of a great deal of labour, and recent work calls it the first urban centre in Mesoamerica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The colossal heads are the most famous survivals, seventeen of them known, from 1.47 to 3.40 m (4 ft 10 in to 11 ft 2 in) tall, and ten from San Lorenzo alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Most read the heads as portraits of individual rulers, though their naturalism has also been argued to mark them as the severed heads of defeated rivals rather than as honoured lords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With no draught animals the basalt was floated on rafts and dragged over logs, while the greenstone buried by the ton came from further still, serpentinite from Cuicatlán in Oaxaca and, at La Venta, Tehuitzingo in Puebla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ceremonial complexes raised along the Gulf Coast between 1100 and 750 BCE are aligned on sunrises and sunsets separated by multiples of 13 and 20 days, the earliest evidence anywhere for the 260-day calendar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> While those centres were building, people in the surrounding countryside laid out plazas up to a kilometre (0.6 miles) long that carry no sculpture of rulers at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far the Olmecs shaped the rest of Mesoamerica is contested: their pottery and figurines travelled widely, yet the earliest formal ballcourt known is at Etlatongo in the Oaxacan highlands, dated to 1374 BCE, and none is accepted at San Lorenzo or La Venta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Barbara L. Stark and Wesley D. Stoner, “Mixed Governance Principles in the Gulf Lowlands of Mesoamerica,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 814545, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.814545. [Open access]","Claude-François Baudez, “Beauty and ugliness in Olmec monumental sculpture,” <i>Journal de la Société des américanistes</i> 98, no. 2 (2012): 7–31, https://doi.org/10.4000/jsa.12294. [Open access]","Ivan Šprajc, Takeshi Inomata and Anthony F. Aveni, “Origins of Mesoamerican astronomy and calendar: Evidence from the Olmec and Maya regions,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 1 (2023): eabq7675, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq7675. [Open access]","Olaf Jaime-Riverón, Dolores Tenorio Castilleros, Thomas Calligaro and Juan Carlos Cruz Ocampo, “A Study of Olmec Serpentinite: exchange, production, distribution, and consumption during the Early and Middle Formative period in Mesoamerica,” <i>Internet Archaeology</i> 26 (2009), https://doi.org/10.11141/ia.26.21. [Open access]","Jeffrey P. Blomster and Víctor E. Salazar Chávez, “Origins of the Mesoamerican ballgame: Earliest ballcourt from the highlands found at Etlatongo, Oaxaca, Mexico,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 11 (2020): eaay6964, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aay6964. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","americas","mexico","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Olmec_Colossal_Head%2C_San_Lorenzo%2C_Veracruz%2C_1200-600_BC_-_front.jpg/960px-Olmec_Colossal_Head%2C_San_Lorenzo%2C_Veracruz%2C_1200-600_BC_-_front.jpg","title":"An Olmec colossal head from San Lorenzo","desc":"Carved from a basalt block brought down from the Tuxtla Mountains, wearing the close-fitting helmet that all seventeen known heads share, its brow carved with insignia.","alt":"A vast rounded stone head on a wooden plinth, the face broad and heavy-lidded with thick parted lips and a flattened nose, wearing a close-fitting helmet carved with a band of ornament across the brow.","credit":"Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmec_Colossal_Head%2C_San_Lorenzo%2C_Veracruz%2C_1200-600_BC_-_front.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-425","num":425,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Maize, the 260-day calendar and the rubber ballgame were shared across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a culture area running from central Mexico into Guatemala, Belize and El Salvador that was never one state.","questions":["The Preclassic, Classic and Postclassic periods divide the past of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose last stage closed with the Spanish conquest of 1519 and the colonial centuries that followed it.","A count by twenties was in use across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the Olmecs onwards, and its Long Count numbers are written positionally with a sign for zero."],"answer":"Mesoamerica","answerText":"Mesoamerica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Archaic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5000 – 2500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Preclassic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2500 BCE – 150 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Classic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">150 – 900 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Postclassic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">900 – 1519 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Mesoamerica</b> is a historically and culturally defined region taking in central and southern Mexico, Belize, Guatemala and El Salvador, with the border country of Honduras, western Nicaragua and north-western Costa Rica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was never one state but a culture area, home to many peoples and languages connected by farming and by commerce, and treated as a unit because of what its societies held in common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hunting and gathering gave way to farming in the Archaic, permanent settlement and pottery came with the Preclassic from 2500 BCE, large centres multiplied through the Classic, and the Postclassic ran from 900 to 1519 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crops arrived slowly, and at El Gigante in western Honduras, where 375 radiocarbon dates anchor nearly 9,800 plant remains, tree fruits and squash appear about 11,000 years ago, maize about 4,400 and beans about 2,200.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tree crops predominated there through most of the Holocene, and only after about 4,000 years ago did field crops take over, driven by a growing reliance on maize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Societies across the region kept a 260-day count in which 20 day signs meshed with the numbers 1 to 13, and ran it alongside a 365-day year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nothing corresponded to a leap day, so the two cycles drifted against the seasons and the sun had to be watched, which is why important buildings were aligned on the sunrises and sunsets of particular dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting went by twenties from the Olmecs onwards, and the Long Count is a positional notation carrying a sign for zero, arrived at without positions and zero having to be invented together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rubber ballgame was played from one end of the region to the other, and more than 2,300 probable courts have been identified across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Postclassic closed with the Spanish conquest of 1519 to 1521, and the colonial centuries that followed brought heavy mortality, the loss of languages and cultures, and large forced movements of people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Xavier Roca-Rada, Yassine Souilmi, João C. Teixeira and Bastien Llamas, “Ancient DNA Studies in Pre-Columbian Mesoamerica,” <i>Genes</i> 11, no. 11 (2020): 1346, https://doi.org/10.3390/genes11111346. [Open access]","Douglas J. Kennett et al., “Trans–Holocene Bayesian chronology for tree and field crop use from El Gigante rockshelter, Honduras,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 6 (2023): e0287195, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0287195. [Open access]","Ivan Šprajc, Takeshi Inomata and Anthony F. Aveni, “Origins of Mesoamerican astronomy and calendar: Evidence from the Olmec and Maya regions,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 1 (2023): eabq7675, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq7675. [Open access]","Jeffrey P. Blomster and Víctor E. Salazar Chávez, “Origins of the Mesoamerican ballgame: Earliest ballcourt from the highlands found at Etlatongo, Oaxaca, Mexico,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 11 (2020): eaay6964, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aay6964. [Open access]","Berenice Rojo-Garibaldi, Costanza Rangoni, Diego L. González and Julyan H. E. Cartwright, “Non-power positional number representation systems, bijective numeration, and the Mesoamerican discovery of zero,” <i>Heliyon</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): e06580, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2021.e06580. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["place","geography","history","archaeology","americas"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Codex_Borgia_page_5.jpg/960px-Codex_Borgia_page_5.jpg","title":"A page of the Codex Borgia","desc":"Red rules divide the sheet into cells holding the day signs by which the 260-day count was kept, with larger figures along the top and bottom.","alt":"A screenfold manuscript page divided by red rules into a grid of small cells, each holding a painted sign such as an animal head, a flint knife or a house, with larger seated figures in the top and bottom rows.","credit":"1898 facsimile edition, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Borgia_page_5.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-426","num":426,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"More than 2,300 probable courts survive for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, twenty of them at El Tajín, where the walls carry carved panels of figures and deities.","questions":["Nineteen rubber balls from a spring at El Manatí push the equipment of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back to 1600 BCE, well before the earliest court yet found in the highlands.","How many players took the field in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is put at anywhere between one and seven, a range resting on hypothesis rather than on anything demonstrable."],"answer":"Mesoamerican ballgame","answerText":"Mesoamerican ballgame","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rubber balls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">El Manatí, from 1600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest court</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Paso de la Amada, c. 1650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Highland court</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Etlatongo, 1374 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">El Tajín</span><span class=\"dt-v\">twenty courts, 600 – 900 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mesoamerican ballgame</b> was a contest played with a solid rubber ball on a purpose-built court, and it is one of the traits that define the region as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 2,300 probable courts have been identified across it, twenty at the Veracruz city of El Tajín alone, whose court walls carry carved panels of figures and deities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A court was ordinary civic furniture rather than a rarity, and in the standard plaza layout of southern Veracruz between 300 and 900 CE one is typically part of the plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The equipment goes back further than the architecture: nineteen rubber balls between 8 and 30 cm (3 to 12 inches) across came from an ancient spring at El Manatí, the oldest dating to 1600 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest known court is at Paso de la Amada in the Chiapas lowlands, built about 1650 BCE, and the oldest in the highlands is at Etlatongo in Oaxaca, dated to 1374 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Players are shown on small ceramic figurines, many of which are whistles, wearing thick padded belts or yokes with loincloths beneath and round pendants at the chest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The game was tied to death early on: a monument near San Lorenzo shows a figure in such a belt standing over a captive, and in the Maya creation story it is played against the lords of the underworld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court itself could be charged with meaning, and at Chactún and Calakmul carved stelae were cut up and set into the playing space at its corners and centre, transferring their standing to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of what is said about the game is hypothesis hardened into assertion, and the number of players a side is put anywhere between one and seven on evidence that cannot settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the buildings do show is a consistent relation between a court’s length and its width, implying real architectural rules, and three forms of the game are still played in western Mexico as ulama.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jeffrey P. Blomster and Víctor E. Salazar Chávez, “Origins of the Mesoamerican ballgame: Earliest ballcourt from the highlands found at Etlatongo, Oaxaca, Mexico,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 11 (2020): eaay6964, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aay6964. [Open access]","Eric Taladoire, “Cinco tesis discutibles relativas al juego de pelota,” <i>Arqueología</i> 50 (2015): 191–209, https://revistas.inah.gob.mx/index.php/arqueologia/article/view/10426. [Open access]","Lorena G. Valle-Chavarria, Carlos E. Berumen-Rodriguez and Edgardo J. Suarez-Dominguez, “A Study of the Formal Architectural-Sculptural Characteristics of El Tajin,” <i>Conservation Science in Cultural Heritage</i> 19, no. 1 (2019): 143–56, https://doi.org/10.6092/issn.1973-9494/9992. [Open access]","Octavio Quetzalcóatl Esparza Olguín and Rogelio Valencia Rivera, “La recolocación de monumentos dentro del juego de pelota como mecanismo de activación ritual durante el periodo Clásico maya,” <i>Journal de la Société des américanistes</i> 106, no. 1 (2020): 157–86, https://doi.org/10.4000/jsa.18002. [Open access]","Barbara L. Stark and Wesley D. Stoner, “Mixed Governance Principles in the Gulf Lowlands of Mesoamerica,” <i>Frontiers in Political Science</i> 4 (2022): 814545, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpos.2022.814545. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","history","archaeology","americas","mexico"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/SBCLookingEastTajin.JPG/960px-SBCLookingEastTajin.JPG","title":"The South Ballcourt at El Tajín","desc":"One of twenty courts at the site, a long alley between two walls whose stone panels carry the site's best-known carving; the visitors give the scale.","alt":"A long flat corridor of mown grass running between two low stone walls of large squared blocks, with wooded hills behind and a scattering of visitors standing along it.","credit":"AlejandroLinaresGarcia, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SBCLookingEastTajin.JPG"}},
{"id":"wh-427","num":427,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Founded about 500 BCE on a hilltop where the three arms of the Valley of Oaxaca meet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the capital of one of the earliest states in the Americas.","questions":["The carved figures called Danzantes, read as shamans or as war captives, were cut in the first period of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and what they meant to its own people is still unknown.","Governance at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> scales as relatively collective rather than autocratic, and it stayed that way for more than a thousand years, which is far from the usual picture of an early state."],"answer":"Monte Albán","answerText":"Monte Albán","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Monte Albán I, 500 – 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zapotec state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Formative, 300 – 100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Monte Albán II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 BCE – 200 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Monte Albán III</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 – 500 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Monte Albán</b> is a hilltop city in the Valley of Oaxaca in southern Mexico, and it was the capital of the Zapotec state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The valley is three arms meeting in a Y — the Etla to the north-west, the Tlacolula to the east, the Ocotlán-Zimatlán to the south — each of which had grown its own centre between 700 and 500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was founded where they meet, at the start of a phase running from 500 to 300 BCE, and became one of the earliest state-level societies in the Americas, and a multi-ethnic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was densely built across its ridge rather than laid out on a grid, which is the contrast usually drawn with the orthogonal, apartment-based plan of Teotihuacan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hill stands where great earthquakes are frequent, on the subduction zone off the Pacific coast and within the plate beneath the continent, so the site has been shaken many times and was damaged in 1999 and 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its best-known monuments are the Danzantes, human figures cut in stone during the city’s first period, whose bodily postures and apparent nakedness have had them read as shamans or as war captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What they meant to the people who carved them remains a mystery, and much of what has been argued about the city’s origins and the imagery of the Zapotec state rests on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On measures of how power was distributed the city comes out as relatively collective rather than autocratic, and it stayed that way for more than a thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Migration is central to every account of how the state came together, yet of the 75 people from the valley whose bones and teeth have been tested only 8 per cent read as incomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city’s rise and its long decline are bound up with the fortunes of Teotihuacan in the Basin of Mexico and of the great Maya cities to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Sofía I. Pacheco-Forés, Nicolas Gauthier, Lacey B. Carpenter, Gwyneth Gordon and Kelly J. Knudson, “Radiogenic strontium isotope variability in the Valley of Oaxaca: A predictive isoscape for Mesoamerican paleomobility studies,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 12 (2025): e0338628, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0338628. [Open access]","Diane Z. Chase et al., “Mesoamerican urbanism revisited: Environmental change, adaptation, resilience, persistence, and collapse,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 31 (2023): e2211558120, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2211558120. [Open access]","Gary M. Feinman et al., “The distribution of power and inclusiveness across deep time,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 12 (2026): eaec1426, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aec1426. [Open access]","Bernd Walter Fahmel Beyer, “Los Danzantes de Monte Albán y su relación con el jaguar,” <i>Anales de Antropología</i> 53, no. 1 (2019): 67–74, https://doi.org/10.22201/iia.24486221e.0.1.64002. [Open access]","Shri Krishna Singh, Arturo Iglesias Mendoza, Danny Arroyo, Mario Ordaz, Leonardo Alcántara and David Almora, “Ground Motion at the World Heritage Archaeological Site of Monte Albán, Oaxaca, Mexico during Recent Damaging Earthquakes,” <i>Geofísica Internacional</i> 65, no. 1 (2026): 1847–61, https://doi.org/10.22201/igeof.2954436xe.2026.65.1.1895. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","americas","mexico"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Monte-Alban-South-Platform.jpg/960px-Monte-Alban-South-Platform.jpg","title":"The stairway to the South Platform","desc":"The seated figure half way up is a person, put there by the photographer to give the scale of the steps.","alt":"A very wide flight of shallow stone steps climbing the whole width of a grassy platform under a clear sky, with one small seated figure part way up giving the scale.","credit":"Uspn, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte-Alban-South-Platform.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-428","num":428,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Airborne laser scanning has redrawn what is known about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, mapping dense settlement beneath forest canopy that no survey on the ground could ever have reached.","questions":["The Puuc country of Yucatán, one of the most crowded parts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, has no rivers or lakes at all, so its people kept their water in rock-cut cisterns and reservoirs.","Ancient individuals and people living in Yucatán today share maternal lineages across some 1,300 years, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost its cities without losing its descendants."],"answer":"Maya civilisation","answerText":"Maya civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700 BCE – 250 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Classic period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">250 – 1000 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Terminal Classic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 – 1000 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Postclassic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1000 – 1500 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Maya civilisation</b> is the long tradition of city-building in the lowlands of the Yucatán peninsula and the country around it, and it was never a single state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cities first emerged there in the late Middle and Late Preclassic, between 700 BCE and 250 CE, and many more followed through the Classic period from 250 to 1000 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Airborne laser scanning has changed what can be said about them, because it maps settlement under forest canopy that no ground survey could reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It shows the Puuc region of Yucatán to be among the most densely settled parts of the lowlands, with population raised and gathered into centres and no defensive works anywhere in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That country has no rivers or lakes at all, so its people stored what fell, in rock-cut cisterns and in reservoirs, and the same imagery picks out lime ovens and quarries that have eaten away whole hillsides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Why the cities grew is a question about several forces at once, and one model has climate downturns, conflict between groups and strong economies of scale working together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same pathways that drew people in also built patron-client relations and lasting inequality into the cities, and decided whether a given city held together or came apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The collapse that followed was neither sudden nor uniform: it ran for almost two hundred years, and Maya people went on founding cities in other places while it happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the wetlands of northern Belize a farming settlement carries straight through it, occupied from the Terminal Classic of 800 to 1000 CE into the Postclassic of 1000 to 1500 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor did the people end with the cities, since maternal lineages run unbroken for about 1,300 years between ancient Maya individuals and the Maya living at Tixcacaltuyub today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Weston C. McCool et al., “Modeling the rise and demise of Classic Maya cities: Climate, conflict, and economies of scale,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 42 (2025): e2512325122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2512325122. [Open access]","William M. Ringle, Tomás Gallareta Negrón, Rossana May Ciau, Kenneth E. Seligson, Juan C. Fernandez-Diaz and David Ortegón Zapata, “Lidar survey of ancient Maya settlement in the Puuc region of Yucatan, Mexico,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 4 (2021): e0249314, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0249314. [Open access]","Diane Z. Chase et al., “Mesoamerican urbanism revisited: Environmental change, adaptation, resilience, persistence, and collapse,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 31 (2023): e2211558120, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2211558120. [Open access]","Lara M. Sánchez-Morales et al., “Maya Postclassic persistence in the Birds of Paradise Wetland Fields, Belize,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 10 (2026): e2521892123, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2521892123. [Open access]","Gustavo Medina Tavares, Bastien Llamas and Rafael Bisso-Machado, “Continuity and discontinuity in Native American populations: Insights from ancient and modern mitochondrial DNA,” <i>Genetics and Molecular Biology</i> 49, suppl. 1 (2026): e20250248, https://doi.org/10.1590/1678-4685-GMB-2025-0248. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","americas","city"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Tikal-Plaza-And-North-Acropolis.jpg/960px-Tikal-Plaza-And-North-Acropolis.jpg","title":"The Great Plaza at Tikal","desc":"Temple I on the right, the North Acropolis on the left, and beyond them the forest under which laser scanning has found the rest of the settlement.","alt":"A wide grassy plaza between ruined stone terraces on the left and a tall steep-sided stepped pyramid on the right, dense green forest closing in behind, with crowds of people gathered across the grass.","credit":"Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tikal-Plaza-And-North-Acropolis.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-429","num":429,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Painted fragments from San Bartolo carry the earliest securely dated example of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and eleven of them together show several hands already at work by 300 BCE.","questions":["Signs for whole words and signs for syllables are combined in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose syllabic grid still has gaps that epigraphers are filling one reading at a time.","Royal births, enthronements, marriages and wars are what most texts in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> record, until the 9th century turns them towards the correct performance of ceremonies."],"answer":"Maya script","answerText":"Maya script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">San Bartolo, 300 – 200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Terminal Classic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">texts shift, 800 – 900 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Day count</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 signs against numbers 1 to 13</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Maya script</b> is a logosyllabic writing system, which is to say that some of its signs stand for whole words and others for syllables, and the two kinds are combined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its decipherment is still far from finished, with a considerable number of logograms and syllabic signs yet to be read, so gaps in the syllabic grid are being filled one proposal at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest securely dated example comes from painted mural fragments at San Bartolo in Guatemala, sealed in an early phase of a pyramid and assigned to between 300 and 200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eleven fragments were recovered together, and what they show is not a beginning but an established tradition, written in several different hands and set beside painted images.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of them records the day 7 Deer, a date in the 260-day divinatory count in which 20 named days run against the numbers 1 to 13.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> What the surviving monumental texts mostly record is dynastic: the births of royal children, accessions to the throne, marriages and wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Through the Terminal Classic, between 800 and 900 CE, that emphasis shifts away from the doings of kings and towards the correct performance of certain ceremonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those ceremonies look like planting and rain-beckoning rites, timed to the agricultural year, and the shift has been read against evidence for reduced rainfall and repeated drought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the conquest of Yucatán, scribes were long held to have commanded both scripts and to have carried matter out of the old books into new alphabetic ones, an argument since challenged in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The count itself never stopped: the 260-day calendar that the San Bartolo fragment dates to the 3rd century BCE is still kept in Maya communities today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["David Stuart, Heather Hurst, Boris Beltrán and William Saturno, “An early Maya calendar record from San Bartolo, Guatemala,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 15 (2022): eabl9290, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abl9290. [Open access]","Sergei Vepretskii and Albert Davletshin, “A Possible [tze] Syllable and its Associates in Maya Writing,” <i>Estudios de Cultura Maya</i> 59 (2022): 11–36, https://doi.org/10.19130/iifl.ecm.59.22x871. [Open access]","Eva Jobbová, Christophe Helmke and Andrew Bevan, “Ritual responses to drought: An examination of ritual expressions in Classic Maya written sources,” <i>Human Ecology</i> 46, no. 5 (2018): 759–81, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10745-018-0019-6. [Open access]","Paul Sullivan, “Scribal Syncretism in Colonial Yucatan, Reconsidered,” <i>Estudios de Cultura Maya</i> 56 (2020): 127–51, https://doi.org/10.19130/iifl.ecm.2020.56.2.0005. [Open access]","Ivan Šprajc, Takeshi Inomata and Anthony F. Aveni, “Origins of Mesoamerican astronomy and calendar: Evidence from the Olmec and Maya regions,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 1 (2023): eabq7675, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq7675. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","text","history","archaeology","americas"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Escritura_maya.jpg/960px-Escritura_maya.jpg","title":"Carved glyph blocks from Yaxchilán","desc":"Each block sets a large main sign among smaller ones attached above, below and to the side; now in the National Museum of Anthropology in Mexico City.","alt":"A close view of a carved stone panel filled with squarish blocks of relief carving, each holding a rounded central motif surrounded by smaller elements, arranged in rows and columns.","credit":"Juan Carlos Fonseca Mata, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Escritura_maya.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-430","num":430,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Bak’tuns, k’atuns, tuns, winals and k’ins are the five units of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose day zero has to be tied to a European calendar before any of its dates can be read.","questions":["The multipliers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run 1, 20 and 18 × 20 rather than by twenties throughout, the third place taking 18 so that its year unit comes near a solar one.","Radiocarbon dates from a carved lintel at Tikal support the constant of 584,283 days used to convert the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had fallen out of use before the Spanish arrived."],"answer":"Long Count","answerText":"Long Count","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Day zero</span><span class=\"dt-v\">0.0.0.0.0, on the day 4 Ahau</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Base date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 August 3114 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GMT constant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">584,283 days added</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tikal lintel</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9.13.3.7.18, 6 August 695 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Long Count</b> is a running tally of days from a fixed starting point, the device by which Mesoamerican inscriptions fix an event absolutely rather than within a repeating cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is written in five units — the bak’tun of 144,000 days, the k’atun of 7,200, the tun of 360, the winal of 20 and the k’in of one — so that 9.13.3.7.18 stands for 1,390,838 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not a plain count by twenties: the multipliers run 1, 20, 18 × 20 and upwards, the third place taking 18 so that the tun comes near a solar year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The digits 0 to 19 are bars for five and dots for one with a sign for zero, and the system was reached without positions and zero having to be invented at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Day zero, written 0.0.0.0.0, carries the day name 4 Ahau in the 260-day count, which meshes 20 day names with the numbers 1 to 13, and the two ran on together without reform through every later change of period, culture and language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest securely dated record of that day count is a 7 Deer sign painted at San Bartolo in Guatemala between 300 and 200 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tying day zero to a European calendar is a separate problem, because the count fell out of use before the Spanish came and the proposals differ by as much as a thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The accepted answer adds 584,283 days, which places day zero on 11 August 3114 BCE, while a second convention adds 584,284 and gives 12 August, a difference of one day that has yet to be satisfactorily explained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The strongest test of the accepted figure is a carved wooden lintel from Tikal, dated by accelerator radiocarbon and fitted to the calibration curve with a Bayesian model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that reading its date 9.13.3.7.18 falls on 6 August 695 CE, the day a king of Tikal defeated his long-standing rival from Calakmul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Douglas J. Kennett et al., “Correlating the Ancient Maya and Modern European Calendars with High-Precision AMS 14C Dating,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 3 (2013): 1597, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep01597. [Open access]","Arnold Lebeuf, “The origin of the Tzolkin and the Long Count: Hans Ludendorff was right,” <i>Przegląd Archeologiczny</i> 68 (2020): 5–16, https://doi.org/10.23858/PA68.2020.001. [Open access]","Berenice Rojo-Garibaldi, Costanza Rangoni, Diego L. González and Julyan H. E. Cartwright, “Non-power positional number representation systems, bijective numeration, and the Mesoamerican discovery of zero,” <i>Heliyon</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): e06580, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2021.e06580. [Open access]","Ivan Šprajc, Takeshi Inomata and Anthony F. Aveni, “Origins of Mesoamerican astronomy and calendar: Evidence from the Olmec and Maya regions,” <i>Science Advances</i> 9, no. 1 (2023): eabq7675, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abq7675. [Open access]","David Stuart, Heather Hurst, Boris Beltrán and William Saturno, “An early Maya calendar record from San Bartolo, Guatemala,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 15 (2022): eabl9290, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abl9290. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","americas","astronomy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Olmec_Stele_C_with_Numbers%2C_Tres_Zapotes%2C_Veracruz%2C_600-100_BC.jpg/960px-Olmec_Stele_C_with_Numbers%2C_Tres_Zapotes%2C_Veracruz%2C_600-100_BC.jpg","title":"Stela C from Tres Zapotes, Veracruz","desc":"The lower fragment of Stela C, carved between about 600 and 100 BCE, with a column of bar-and-dot numerals reading a Long Count date.","alt":"A weathered stone slab in a museum case, carved down its centre with a vertical column of horizontal bars and rows of dots.","credit":"Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmec_Stele_C_with_Numbers%2C_Tres_Zapotes%2C_Veracruz%2C_600-100_BC.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-431","num":431,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Laid out on a single grid 15.5° east of north and home to perhaps 100,000 people, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the largest city of ancient Mesoamerica, yet it has left no royal portrait.","questions":["Most of the people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived in walled apartment compounds, and its dedicatory caches at the Moon and Sun Pyramids held nearly 200 carnivores alongside human sacrificial victims.","Eruptions of Popocatépetl and Xitle sent refugees into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which grew into a multiethnic metropolis with quarters for people from Oaxaca, the Gulf Coast and Michoacán before collapsing around 550 CE."],"answer":"Teotihuacan","answerText":"Teotihuacan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rapid growth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from about 100 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Apartment blocks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from about 200 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Apogee</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 550 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">around 550 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Teotihuacan</b> was the largest city of Mesoamerica in the 1st millennium, a metropolis in the Valley of Mexico growing rapidly from about 100 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It covered roughly 20 km² (8 square miles), quartered by a north–south axis called the Street of the Dead and by east–west avenues, and by 150 CE held perhaps 80,000 to 100,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That axis is flanked by the Moon Pyramid, the Sun Pyramid and the walled Ciudadela enclosing the Feathered Serpent Pyramid, and buildings across the city stand 15.5° east of astronomical north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From about 200 CE more than 2,000 apartment compounds housed most of the population, of every rank, in roofed structures with open patios and drainage, each shared by several households.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was multiethnic, taking in people displaced by eruptions of Popocatépetl in the 1st century CE and Xitle around 320, with an Oaxaca barrio in the southwest, a Merchants’ Barrio of Gulf Coast people in the east and a Michoacán group in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> How it was governed is unsettled, because the site has yielded no ruler’s tomb, no royal portraiture and none of the dynastic stelae other Mesoamerican cities produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not one piece of its art shows a person subjugating another, and many archaeologists therefore argue for a collective government or co-rulership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ritual was staged on a great scale: the caches inside the Moon and Sun Pyramids held nearly 200 carnivores — eagles, wolves and felids — with human sacrificial victims, and injuries and maize-rich diets show some had been kept captive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its reach ran far beyond the valley: a spider monkey given by Maya dignitaries and sacrificed in the ceremonial core attests formal dealings by 250 to 300 CE, a century before the Entrada recorded in Maya inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That relationship later soured, Maya-style murals in the Plaza of the Columns being smashed between about 350 and 450 CE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>, and the city collapsed around 550, though its architecture and orientation went on being copied elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","history","archaeology","americas","mexico"],"sources":["Tom Froese, Carlos Gershenson and Linda R. Manzanilla, “Can Government Be Self-Organized? A Mathematical Model of the Collective Social Organization of Ancient Teotihuacan, Central Mexico,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 10 (2014): e109966, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0109966. [Open access]","Nawa Sugiyama et al., “Humans as geomorphic agents: Lidar detection of the past, present and future of the Teotihuacan Valley, Mexico,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 9 (2021): e0257550, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0257550. [Open access]","Linda R. Manzanilla, “Cooperation and tensions in multiethnic corporate societies using Teotihuacan, Central Mexico, as a case study,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 30 (2015): 9210–15, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4522775/. [Open access]","Nawa Sugiyama et al., “Earliest evidence of primate captivity and translocation supports gift diplomacy between Teotihuacan and the Maya,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 47 (2022): e2212431119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9704712/. [Open access]","Nawa Sugiyama, Andrew D. Somerville and Margaret J. Schoeninger, “Stable Isotopes and Zooarchaeology at Teotihuacan, Mexico Reveal Earliest Evidence of Wild Carnivore Management in Mesoamerica,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 9 (2015): e0135635, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0135635. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/La_avenida_de_los_muertos_en_las_Pir%C3%A1mides_de_Teotiuac%C3%A1n.jpg/960px-La_avenida_de_los_muertos_en_las_Pir%C3%A1mides_de_Teotiuac%C3%A1n.jpg","title":"The Avenue of the Dead from the air","desc":"Teotihuacan seen along its main axis, with the Moon Pyramid closing the far end and the terraces of the Sun Pyramid at the right.","alt":"An aerial view of a wide straight avenue running through a dry plain lined with low stone platforms, a stepped pyramid at its far end and a larger one to the right.","credit":"Ralibreros112, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_avenida_de_los_muertos_en_las_Pir%C3%A1mides_de_Teotiuac%C3%A1n.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-432","num":432,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"The great temple of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands at 3,150 m (10,300 feet) in the Peruvian highlands, its dark restricted galleries holding a fanged granite monolith and twenty shell trumpets.","questions":["Bone tubes from a sealed gallery gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its first direct evidence of vilca and tobacco snuff, confirming what its tenon heads with streaming noses had implied.","Cinnabar from Huancavelica and vilca from the eastern Andean flanks reached the temples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose exchange networks broke up in the centuries after 500 BCE."],"answer":"Chavín culture","answerText":"Chavín culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formative</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Middle to Late, 1200 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Site’s height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gallery 3</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 350 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temple ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">around 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Chavín culture</b> takes its name from Chavín de Huántar in the north-central highlands of Peru, a ceremonial and population centre of roughly 1000 to 500 BCE standing at 3,150 m (10,300 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It belongs to the Middle to Late Formative Period, about 1200 to 400 BCE, when marked social inequality, irrigation, terracing and craft production spread through the central Andes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site is built of open plazas, stone-faced platforms and religious sculpture, with a network of galleries running inside them: dark, enclosed chambers whose entrances were tightly restricted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One leads to the Lanzón, a granite monolith over 4 m (13 feet) high; nearly 700 ceramic vessels came out of the Ofrendas Gallery and 20 complete <i>Strombus</i> shell trumpets out of the Caracolas Gallery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tenon heads set into the outer walls show faces in the act of transformation, several of them with mucus running from the nostrils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Sealed deposits in Gallery 3, beside the circular plaza, have supplied the direct evidence long suspected: bone tubes of about 800 to 350 BCE carrying alkaloids and starches of vilca and wild tobacco.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vilca will not grow above 2,700 m (8,900 feet), so it had to be carried up from the eastern flanks of the Andes, one thread of the long-distance networks linking the ceremonial centres of coast, highland and upper Amazon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another is cinnabar: the red pigment used across much of Peru in these centuries came chiefly from Huancavelica, and the shift in its supply after 500 BCE tracks the breaking up of the Chavín phenomenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The temple stopped working as one around 500 BCE, though work on its main buildings had fallen off well before that, and the janabarriu pottery of the site’s height belongs to roughly 800 to 500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How the abandonment happened is still argued over, and the ruins were not left alone: Huarás, Mariash-Recuay and Callejón groups returned to rebuild and to bury their dead among the old walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","americas","religion","peru"],"sources":["John W. Rick et al., “Pre-Hispanic ritual use of psychoactive plants at Chavín de Huántar, Peru,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 19 (2025): e2425125122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12088378/. [Open access]","Jason Nesbitt, “Psychoactive plants and secret rituals in ancient Peru,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 24 (2025): e2509867122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12184634/. [Open access]","John W. Rick et al., “La cronología de Chavín de Huántar y sus implicancias para el Periodo Formativo,” <i>Boletín de Arqueología PUCP</i>, no. 13 (2009): 87–132, https://doi.org/10.18800/boletindearqueologiapucp.200901.003. [Open access]","Michelle Young et al., “Mass spectrometry measurements of mercury isotope ratios support geochemical sourcing of archaeological cinnabar in the Andean region,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 7 (2025): e0326414, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0326414. [Open access]","Lisseth Rojas-Pelayo and John W. Rick, “Cuerpos, arquitectura y memoria: prácticas funerarias como mediaciones entre ocupaciones post-Chavín en el corredor C-D de Chavín de Huántar,” <i>Bulletin de l’Institut français d’études andines</i> 54, no. 2 (2025): 231–59, https://doi.org/10.4000/15r3o. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Chavin_lanzon_stela2_cyark.jpg/960px-Chavin_lanzon_stela2_cyark.jpg","title":"The carved face of the Lanzón","desc":"A laser scan of the upper part of the Lanzón, the granite monolith standing in its gallery at Chavín de Huántar.","alt":"A close view of a stone shaft carved in low relief with a huge round eye, a broad grinning mouth and bands of curling ornament.","credit":"CyArk, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chavin_lanzon_stela2_cyark.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-433","num":433,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Scraped into a Peruvian desert tableland by clearing away its surface stones, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include trapezoids kilometres long and animal figures averaging 90 m (300 feet) across.","questions":["Six months of machine-guided survey added 303 new figures to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, nearly doubling the 430 figurative geoglyphs found in almost a century of searching.","Small figures among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sit within sight of walking trails and show people, while the giant ones lie beside the ceremonial network and mostly show wild animals."],"answer":"Nazca Lines","answerText":"Nazca Lines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">On the pampa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 100 BCE, Initial Nasca</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relief-type</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Initial Nasca, 100 BCE – 50 CE, or earlier</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Line-type</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Nasca period, 100 BCE – 650 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to the 15th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Nazca Lines</b> are geoglyphs cut into the Nazca Pampa, a desert tableland 50 km (30 miles) inland from the south coast of Peru and 500 m (1,600 feet) above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A geoglyph is made by clearing the stone pavement off the pale sediment beneath, so the figure stands out from what is left; the word joins the Greek <i>geo</i>, ‘earth’, to <i>glyph</i>, an engraved mark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They have lasted because the pampa floods rarely and is no use for farming; the ground they cover runs to 629 km² (243 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some are geometric — the lines themselves, several kilometres long, and the giant trapezoids — while others are figurative, showing people, animals, plants and tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figurative ones come in two styles: line-type, averaging 90 m (300 feet) and 64 per cent wild animals, and relief-type, averaging 9 m (30 feet) and mostly humans, llamas and severed heads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Aerial photography began surveying them in the 1940s, under the theory that they were an astronomical calendar; today they are mapped from satellite imagery against the threat of destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A survey guided by machine learning then added 303 new figures in six months, nearly doubling the 430 found in the previous century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such figures were long credited to the Nasca, but the tradition began around 750 BCE under the Paracas culture<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and in the Chincha valley Paracas geoglyphs are tied to sun-aligned mounds of the 4th to 2nd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the pampa they were in use by the 1st century BCE and went on into the 15th century CE, the line-type figures probably made between 100 BCE and 650 CE, while the neighbouring Palpa valleys’ sequence rests on more than 150 radiocarbon dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Five purposes have been proposed, from astronomy to artistic expression, and the distribution now argues for two: relief-type figures lie about 43 m (140 feet) from winding trails and were made for small groups, while line-type figures lie about 34 m (110 feet) from the ceremonial network and were community work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","art","history","archaeology","americas","peru"],"sources":["Masato Sakai et al., “AI-accelerated Nazca survey nearly doubles the number of known figurative geoglyphs and sheds light on their purpose,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 40 (2024): e2407652121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2407652121. [Open access]","Rubén García Soto, “Geoglifos paracas de la costa sur: Cerro Lechuza y Cerro Pico,” <i>Boletín de Arqueología PUCP</i>, no. 17 (2013): 151–68, https://doi.org/10.18800/boletindearqueologiapucp.201301.007. [Open access]","Henry Tantaleán et al., “Paracas en el valle de Chincha: nuevos datos y explicaciones,” <i>Boletín de Arqueología PUCP</i>, no. 17 (2013): 31–56, https://doi.org/10.18800/boletindearqueologiapucp.201301.002. [Open access]","K. Hanzalová and K. Pavelka, “Map of Nasca Geoglyphs,” <i>The International Archives of the Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing and Spatial Information Sciences</i> XL-5/W2 (2013): 309–11, https://doi.org/10.5194/isprsarchives-XL-5-W2-309-2013. [Open access]","Markus Reindel and Johny A. Isla, “Evidencias de culturas tempranas en los valles de Palpa, costa sur del Perú,” <i>Boletín de Arqueología PUCP</i>, no. 10 (2006): 237–83, https://doi.org/10.18800/boletindearqueologiapucp.200601.011. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/L%C3%ADneas_de_Nazca%2C_Nazca%2C_Per%C3%BA%2C_2015-07-29%2C_DD_52.JPG/960px-L%C3%ADneas_de_Nazca%2C_Nazca%2C_Per%C3%BA%2C_2015-07-29%2C_DD_52.JPG","title":"The Hummingbird","desc":"One of the line-type figurative geoglyphs of the Nazca Pampa, drawn as a single unbroken path with a long straight line running away from it.","alt":"An aerial view of pale desert ground on which a bird with a long beak and outstretched wings is drawn in one continuous narrow line.","credit":"Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%ADneas_de_Nazca%2C_Nazca%2C_Per%C3%BA%2C_2015-07-29%2C_DD_52.JPG"}},
{"id":"wh-434","num":434,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Nine river valleys on Peru’s north coast held the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose adobe huacas, irrigation canals, portrait pottery and gilded tumbaga survive without a line of writing to explain them.","questions":["Ancient DNA from the tomb group at Huaca Cao Viejo has shown that the elite of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were buried as families, one man with his own strangled son at his feet.","Potters of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> coiled and moulded their vessels while their Virú-Gallinazo neighbours hammered theirs, which is one reason the two are now thought to have coexisted rather than followed one another."],"answer":"Moche culture","answerText":"Moche culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 – 800 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Longer estimate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 – 950 CE, nine valleys</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Señora de Cao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">buried c. 500 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Moche culture</b> held nine river valleys on the north coast of Peru, flourishing between about 100 and 800 CE<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>, though the dates given for it differ, one recent study running it from 300 to 950 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It left no writing, so everything known about it is archaeological, and its politics within and between the valleys are still not settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people raised pyramid-like adobe temples, the huacas, and the irrigation networks that made an arid coast farmable<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and Huaca Cao Viejo in the Chicama valley still stands 30 m (100 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hierarchy was capped by a political and religious elite who waged war, impersonated deities in elaborate rituals and were buried in the huacas themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their metalwork used gold, copper and silver, and its most famous product is tumbaga, an alloy that looks like solid gold once its surface has been stripped of the baser metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Pottery is the other great craft: finely modelled and painted with deities, animals and combat scenes, the human figures given a striking likeness to real faces<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>, and built up by coiling and moulding where the neighbouring Virú-Gallinazo potters hammered theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That difference matters, because the two traditions were long thought to be successive and now look to have shared the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dark paints on their vessels are iron oxides, mostly hematite, with magnetite and minerals such as jacobsite in some of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their fine-line scenes carry ulluchu, a grooved, comma-shaped fruit that floats through sacrifices beside runners and priests, identified as species of <i>Guarea</i> and chemically suited to keeping a victim’s blood flowing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient DNA from six people buried at Huaca Cao Viejo around 500 CE, among them the Señora de Cao, shows one family across four generations, with a strangled boy at one man’s feet who proved to be his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","americas","peru","art"],"sources":["Jeffrey Quilter et al., “Family relations of Moche elite burials on the North Coast of Peru (~500 CE): Analyses of the Señora de Cao and associated individuals,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 1 (2025): e2416321121, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11725780/. [Open access]","Marta Porcaro et al., “Characterization of an Ancient Bimetallic Alloy from Moche Civilization (Peru),” <i>Materials</i> 16, no. 22 (2023): 7211, https://doi.org/10.3390/ma16227211. [Open access]","Alicia Espinosa, Gabriel Prieto and Walter Alva, “Tradiciones técnicas y producción cerámica virú-gallinazo y mochica: nuevas miradas sobre las relaciones entre dos grupos sociales del Período Intermedio Temprano en la Costa Norte del Perú,” <i>Boletín de Arqueología PUCP</i>, no. 26 (2019): 85–102, https://doi.org/10.18800/boletindearqueologiapucp.201901.005. [Open access]","Rainer W. Bussmann and Douglas Sharon, “Naming a phantom – the quest to find the identity of Ulluchu, an unidentified ceremonial plant of the Moche culture in Northern Peru,” <i>Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine</i> 5 (2009): 8, https://doi.org/10.1186/1746-4269-5-8. [Open access]","Nino Del-Solar-Velarde, Rémy Chapoulie and Luis Jaime Castillo, “Análisis químico y mineralógico de pinturas en cerámicas arqueológicas del valle de Jequetepeque y sus implicancias arqueológicas,” <i>Boletín de Arqueología PUCP</i>, no. 26 (2019): 33–47, https://doi.org/10.18800/boletindearqueologiapucp.201901.002. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Portrait_head_stirrup_spout_vessel%2C_Moche%2C_Peru%2C_c._100-500_AD%2C_ceramic%2C_polychrome_slip_-_Portland_Art_Museum_-_Portland%2C_Oregon_-_DSC09212.jpg/960px-Portrait_head_stirrup_spout_vessel%2C_Moche%2C_Peru%2C_c._100-500_AD%2C_ceramic%2C_polychrome_slip_-_Portland_Art_Museum_-_Portland%2C_Oregon_-_DSC09212.jpg","title":"A Moche portrait head vessel","desc":"A stirrup-spout bottle modelled as a human head and painted in slip, made on the north coast of Peru between about 100 and 500 CE.","alt":"A ceramic vessel shaped as a man's head with a headband, the face painted in red and dark brown, with a looped handle and a cylindrical spout rising from the crown.","credit":"Portland Art Museum, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_head_stirrup_spout_vessel%2C_Moche%2C_Peru%2C_c._100-500_AD%2C_ceramic%2C_polychrome_slip_-_Portland_Art_Museum_-_Portland%2C_Oregon_-_DSC09212.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-435","num":435,"category":"Africa and the Americas in antiquity","question":"Platform mounds and sunken circular plazas went up in the Supe, Pativilca and Fortaleza valleys from about 2600 BCE, the work of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the earliest complex society of the Andes.","questions":["Cotton grown for nets rather than for food, and fish traded inland for crops, underpinned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose valleys were settled before 3000 BCE and abandoned by about 1800 BCE.","How far maize fed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is disputed: coprolites and residues make it a primary food, while isotopes from teeth at Áspero and Caral make it marginal beside sweet potato and squash."],"answer":"Norte Chico civilisation","answerText":"Norte Chico civilisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Supe occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban sites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt to plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Archaic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3000 – 1800 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Norte Chico civilisation</b> is the earliest complex society of the Andes, named after the stretch of north-central Peruvian coast where its sites cluster in the Supe, Pativilca and Fortaleza valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its period, the Late Archaic of 3000 to 1800 BCE, is when large permanent communities settled, monumental architecture first appeared and a distinctive Andean religion shows in the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Supe valley was occupied before 3000 BCE and urban settlements grew there around 2900 BCE, but only from about 2600 BCE was the built space reordered into the pyramids and sunken circular plazas it is known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Caral the Greater Pyramidal Building has a circular plaza of its own and the walled Temple of the Amphitheatre the largest sunken plaza in the city, while at Caballete six platform mounds stand in a rough U around an open space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cotton was grown for industry rather than food, and nets of it at least 8 by 4 m (26 by 13 feet) were made on the shore, where fish and shellfish were traded inland for crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What that economy rested on is argued about, and the argument is about maize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One reading of coprolites, pollen and stone-tool residues, with 126 radiocarbon dates behind it, has maize widely grown, intensively processed and a primary part of the diet throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that, starch grains in teeth from Áspero and Caral put eight food plants on the table — sweet potato, squash, potato, chilli, algarrobo, manioc, bean and maize — while the isotopes make maize marginal and the C3 plants the foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A reanalysis of 572 skeletons from 39 Andean sites goes further: on the coast it was cultivation rather than fishing that fed the first complex societies, and maize became a staple only around 500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were not alone in building: a megalithic circular plaza was raised in the Cajamarca highlands about 2750 BCE, in the same Late Preceramic centuries that produced the first Andean monuments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","history","archaeology","americas","peru"],"sources":["Ruth Shady, “La civilización Caral: sistema social y manejo del territorio y sus recursos. Su trascendencia en el proceso cultural andino,” <i>Boletín de Arqueología PUCP</i>, no. 10 (2006): 59–89, https://doi.org/10.18800/boletindearqueologiapucp.200601.004. [Open access]","Jonathan Haas et al., “Evidence for maize (<i>Zea mays</i>) in the Late Archaic (3000–1800 B.C.) in the Norte Chico region of Peru,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 13 (2013): 4945–49, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3612639/. [Open access]","Marco Yseki et al., “Analysis of starch grains trapped in human dental calculus in Áspero, Peru during the Initial and Late Preceramic period,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 14143, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-41015-6. [Open access]","Luis Pezo-Lanfranco and André Carlo Colonese, “The role of farming and fishing in the rise of social complexity in the Central Andes: a stable isotope perspective,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 4582, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-55436-4. [Open access]","Jason L. Toohey et al., “A monumental stone plaza at 4750 B.P. in the Cajamarca Valley of Peru,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 7 (2024): eadl0572, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adl0572. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Pir%C3%A1mide_Mayor_-_Caral.jpg/960px-Pir%C3%A1mide_Mayor_-_Caral.jpg","title":"The Greater Pyramidal Building at Caral","desc":"The stepped terraces and central stair of Caral's largest mound, with the wall of its sunken circular plaza curving across the foreground.","alt":"A low stone-faced pyramid of several terraces rising from desert sand, with a stair up its middle and a curved retaining wall in front of it.","credit":"Soto Caldaa Cesar, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pir%C3%A1mide_Mayor_-_Caral.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-436","num":436,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Historians cut the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into an early period from 324 to 842 CE, a middle one to 1204, and a late one ending with the Ottoman conquest in 1430.","questions":["Enemies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried off 22,000 people from Thessaloniki in 904 and 17,000 from Reggio di Calabria three years earlier, among many such hauls after 600.","The plague that struck under Justinian was long held to have killed tens of millions across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a reading that a recent survey of the evidence rejects."],"answer":"Byzantine Empire","answerText":"Byzantine Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early Byzantine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">324 – 842 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Middle Byzantine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">842 – 1204 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Byzantine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1204 – 1430 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Justinian I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reigned 527 – 565 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Byzantine Empire</b> is what modern historians call the Roman state that carried on in the east, and its history is usually cut into an Early Byzantine period of 324 to 842 CE, a Middle one to 1204 and a Late one to 1430.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justinian I, who reigned from 527 to 565, presides over its first great phase, and his coins date the abandoned drains and latrines at Pompeiopolis in Anatolia to the middle of that century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That was part of a wider change: across Anatolia the Late Antique cities were transforming from the 6th century, and by the 7th many had largely lost their urban functions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There followed centuries so thin in archaeological evidence that they are called the Dark Ages, until the late 9th century brought the renewed prosperity of the Middle Byzantine period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not everything shrank: Thessaloniki, the second city, kept its political and economic standing, and the churches raised there as Christianity spread reshaped its streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The provinces could be productive in unlikely places: at Shivta in the Negev desert runoff agriculture watered vineyards, though modelling suggests a long drought cut wine production so far that recovery took more than a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> War carried people off in numbers: 5,000 from Isauria in 650, 17,000 from Reggio di Calabria in 901, 22,000 from Thessaloniki in 904 and 12,000 from Tiriolo in 929 or 930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plague that struck under Justinian, running from about 541 to 750 CE, has long been given tens of millions of deaths and credited with ending antiquity and starting the Middle Ages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reading the written sources, legislation, coinage, papyri, inscriptions, pollen, ancient DNA and burials against one another finds no support for that, and continuity rather than collapse across the plague period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The break came from outside: Constantinople fell to the crusaders in 1204 and the empire fragmented, and Thessaloniki, after a plague and a long siege, was taken by the Ottomans in 1430.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["state","history","archaeology","europe","religion"],"sources":["Asterios Aidonis et al., “Urban survival, population dynamics and historical transitions in Thessaloniki (316 BC–1600 AD),” <i>Archaeological and Anthropological Sciences</i> 18, no. 4 (2026): 74, https://doi.org/10.1007/s12520-026-02439-z. [Open access]","Lee Mordechai et al., “The Justinianic Plague: An inconsequential pandemic?,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 51 (2019): 25546–54, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6926030/. [Open access]","Grigori Simeonov, “In enemy hands: the Byzantine experience of captivity between the seventh and tenth centuries,” <i>Early Medieval Europe</i> 31, no. 3 (2023): 430–58, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10962249/. [Open access]","Melis Uzdurum et al., “Late Antique–Early Byzantine urban transformation in Mid-Northern Anatolia: A multiproxy approach from Pompeiopolis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 4 (2026): e0344987, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0344987. [Open access]","Barak Garty et al., “Wine economy in Byzantine Shivta (Negev, Israel): Exploring the role of runoff agriculture and droughts through Agent-Based Modeling,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 7 (2025): e0325204, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0325204. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Mosaic_of_Justinian_I_-_San_Vitale_-_Ravenna_2016.jpg/960px-Mosaic_of_Justinian_I_-_San_Vitale_-_Ravenna_2016.jpg","title":"Justinian I in mosaic at San Vitale","desc":"The emperor with clergy, courtiers and guards, in the mosaic set in the apse of San Vitale at Ravenna in the 540s.","alt":"A wall mosaic of a haloed emperor in purple holding a golden bowl, flanked by men in white robes, a bishop with a cross and soldiers with a shield, against a gold ground.","credit":"José Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_of_Justinian_I_-_San_Vitale_-_Ravenna_2016.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-437","num":437,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Inaugurated on 11 May 330 and soon given a senate of its own, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the eastern Roman capital and held that place for more than eleven centuries.","questions":["Grain from Alexandria was unloaded at the Theodosian Harbour of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose excavation at Yenikapı has since produced 37 wrecked ships of the 5th to 11th centuries.","In 1453 Orban's great bronze bombard threw 600-kilogram (1,300-pound) granite balls at the land walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which no besieger had ever carried before."],"answer":"Constantinople","answerText":"Constantinople","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Refounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 May 330 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Harbour</span><span class=\"dt-v\">begun c. 390 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Latin rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1204 – 1261 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ottoman conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 May 1453 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Constantinople</b>, on the promontory where the Golden Horn meets the Sea of Marmara, was refounded by Constantine the Great on the Greek colony of Byzantion and inaugurated on 11 May 330.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site commanded the shipping lane between the Mediterranean and the Black Sea, and its landward side was narrow enough to be closed by a single line of walls. The city Constantine left at his death in 337 was still mostly a building site, with the Great Palace, the hippodrome, his forum, the Capitol and his mausoleum standing but no major church finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another forty years passed before the new quarters filled with houses, a water line came into operation and the colonnaded streets reached the walls in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was Constantine's son Constantius who made it a Christian capital in earnest, granting it the rights of Rome, establishing a senate, appointing a city prefect and building churches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The capital was fed by the annona, the state allotment of food, and the ships that brought grain from Alexandria berthed in the Theodosian Harbour, begun about 390 on a natural bay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rescue excavation at Yenikapı for the Marmaray rail tunnel opened more than 58,000 square metres (14 acres) of that harbour and recovered at least 37 ships lost there between the 5th and early 11th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ceremonial centre was the circular forum of Constantine, opened with the city itself, where a porphyry column carried the emperor in gilded bronze beside a senate house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The defences outlasted the wealth that paid for them: by the 1420s the walls were in serious disrepair, and Murad II’s siege of 1422 exposed the weak stretch where the Lykos stream entered the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were broken in 1453 by Ottoman gunnery, Orban's bronze bombard firing from 500 metres (550 yards) and throwing granite balls of about 600 kilograms (1,300 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["city","history","archaeology","europe","politics"],"sources":["Albrecht Berger, “Constantine’s City: the Early Days of a Christian Capital,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 10 (2020): 11–29, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.10.01. [Open access]","Cemal Pulak, Rebecca Ingram, and Michael Jones, “Eight Byzantine Shipwrecks from the Theodosian Harbour Excavations at Yenikapı in Istanbul, Turkey: an introduction,” <i>International Journal of Nautical Archaeology</i> 44, no. 1 (2015): 39–73, https://doi.org/10.1111/1095-9270.12083. [Open access]","Anthony Kaldellis, “The Forum of Constantine in Constantinople: What do we know about its original architecture and adornment?,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 56, no. 4 (2016): 714–39, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/15755. [Open access]","Marios Philippides, “Venice, Genoa, and John VIII Palaeologus’ Renovation of the Fortifications of Constantinople,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 56, no. 2 (2016): 377–97, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/15641. [Open access]","Aristotle Kakaliagos and Nikolaos Ninis, “Damage and failure of Orban’s gun during the bombardment of Constantinople walls in 1453,” <i>Frattura ed Integrità Strutturale</i> 13, no. 50 (2019): 481–96, https://doi.org/10.3221/IGF-ESIS.50.40. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Theodosian_Walls_of_Constantinople%2C_Istanbul_%2824053561188%29.jpg/960px-Theodosian_Walls_of_Constantinople%2C_Istanbul_%2824053561188%29.jpg","title":"The Theodosian land walls of Constantinople","desc":"The fifth-century land walls that closed the city's landward side, photographed near the Belgratkapı in Istanbul.","credit":"Photograph by Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodosian_Walls_of_Constantinople%2C_Istanbul_%2824053561188%29.jpg","alt":"A ruined fortification of alternating brick and stone courses, with projecting towers, standing on open grass under a clear blue sky."}},
{"id":"wh-438","num":438,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Between 527 and 565 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reconquered Africa and Italy, rebuilt the Great Church of the capital, and had the whole of Roman law recompiled into a code, digest and textbook.","questions":["Procopius describes silkworm eggs reaching Constantinople in about 552 under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which gave the empire a silk industry of its own instead of buying through Persia.","Plague reached the capital in 541, and the wall that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> threw across the Isthmus of Corinth did not keep the Avars and Slavs out of Greece forty years later."],"answer":"Justinian I","answerText":"Justinian I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">527 – 565 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reconquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">533 – 554 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plague</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 541 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 November 565 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Justinian I</b> ruled the eastern Roman empire from 527 to 565 and set out to restore Roman rule, Roman law and Christian orthodoxy across the whole Mediterranean world. He came from a Latin-speaking family in the Balkans and was raised to the throne by his uncle Justin I, whose reign he had already dominated as an adviser. His armies took the Vandal kingdom in Africa in 533 and 534 and then fought the Ostrogoths for Italy in a war that ran from 535 to 554 and ruined the country it recovered. That expansion was the centrepiece of the official account of the reign: the proem to Procopius' <i>Buildings</i> praises Justinian's enlargement of the empire, his establishment of orthodoxy and his care for every part of his rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same work catalogues churches, monasteries, bridges, road repairs and hospices raised at his order, among them the Nea Ekklesia of the Virgin in Jerusalem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Fortification took up much of the effort: he rebuilt the wall across the Isthmus of Corinth so that, Procopius claimed, the Peloponnese would be safe even if the defences at Thermopylae were forced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did not hold, and the Avar and Slav raids that reached central Greece from about 580 left destruction at Isthmia, Corinth, Nemea and Kenchreai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the east the war with the Persian king Khusro I ran through the 540s, with a Roman invasion of Persarmenia and a Persian siege of Edessa, and ended in a truce agreed in the spring of 545.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Procopius also records silkworm eggs reaching the capital about 552, which freed the empire from buying raw silk through Persia, though the insects probably came from Sogdiana rather than from China itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reign met its great disaster in the plague that arrived in 541, and how far it thinned the population is now disputed, one reading of the written, archaeological and genetic evidence finding no sign of a demographic collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","history","europe","politics","religion"],"sources":["Martina Biamino, “Justinian, Pilgrimages and the Theotokos: Imperial Propaganda in <i>De Aedificiis</i> V,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 215–37, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.03. [Open access]","Robert L. Hohlfelder, “Trans-Isthmian Walls in the Age of Justinian,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 18, no. 2 (1977): 173–79, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/7821. [Open access]","Geoffrey Greatrex, “Roman Campaigns and Negotiations in the East, 542–545,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 11 (2021): 569–78, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.11.29. [Open access]","Katarzyna Maksymiuk, “‘Industrial espionage’ of Justinian I (527–565),” <i>Historia i Świat</i> 10 (2021): 445–53, https://doi.org/10.34739/his.2021.10.26. [Open access]","Lee Mordechai, Merle Eisenberg, Timothy P. Newfield, Adam Izdebski, Janet E. Kay, and Hendrik Poinar, “The Justinianic Plague: An inconsequential pandemic?,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 51 (2019): 25546–54, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1903797116. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Solidus_of_Justinian_I%2C_538-545.png/960px-Solidus_of_Justinian_I%2C_538-545.png","title":"Gold solidus of Justinian I","desc":"A solidus of 4.39 grams struck at Constantinople between 538 and 545, showing the emperor helmeted and facing, with the legend D N IVSTINIANVS PP AVG.","credit":"Photograph Dumbarton Oaks, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Justinian_I%2C_538-545.png","alt":"A gold coin with a facing bust of a helmeted emperor holding a globe surmounted by a cross, ringed by a Latin legend."}},
{"id":"wh-439","num":439,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Compiled between 528 and 534 under the quaestor Tribonian, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathered imperial law, juristic writing and a students’ manual into the collection that carried Roman law into modern Europe.","questions":["The compilers were ordered to cut and rewrite the classical jurists so the excerpts said what the emperor wanted, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is full of interpolations.","Published on 16 December 533, the Digest of the classical jurists formed the largest part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, beside a code of imperial law and a manual for first-year students."],"answer":"Corpus Juris Civilis","answerText":"Corpus Juris Civilis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Code</span><span class=\"dt-v\">529 CE, revised 534 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Institutes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 November 533 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Digest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 533 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent to Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">554 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Corpus Juris Civilis</b> is the name later given to the compilation of Roman law made under Justinian I between 528 and 534, together with the new laws he went on issuing afterwards. He announced a fresh code of imperial enactments in February 528 and published it in April 529, replacing the collections that had served the empire until then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end of 530 he ordered the <i>ius antiquum</i>, the classical juristic commentaries, to be sifted and collated into a Digest under his quaestor Tribonian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four senior law professors from Beirut and Constantinople worked on it beside eleven practising advocates, so working lawyers were thought as necessary to the job as teachers were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Digest appeared on 16 December 533 after three years, a month after the <i>Institutiones</i>, a manual for students, and the law-school syllabus was rebuilt around the pair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The compilers were told to settle the questions the jurists had disagreed about, and to alter the excerpts where necessary so that they carried the approved rule without saying so, a practice now called interpolation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second edition of the code followed in November 534, and the later laws, the <i>Novellae</i>, were issued mostly in Greek, though Justinian came from the Latin-speaking Balkans and called Latin the language of his fathers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The code keeps rescripts going back to the 3rd century, many of them about people caught between slavery and freedom, presented as a guide to the law of Justinian’s own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A pragmatic sanction extended it to Italy in 554, but the 7th and 8th centuries are silent in the history of Roman law in the west, and the manuscripts have to be traced rather than read off a continuous tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The revival of legal study in Italy about 1100 made it the foundation of the civil-law tradition, and a complete English version appeared only in 1932, translated single-handed and criticised ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","history","politics","europe","concept"],"sources":["Halcyon Weber, “A Hypothesis regarding Justinian’s <i>decisiones</i> and the Digest,” <i>Roman Legal Tradition</i> 11 (2015): 42–117, https://romanlegaltradition.org/contents/2015/RLT11-WEBER.PDF. [Open access]","Judith Evans Grubbs, “Between Slavery and Freedom: Disputes over Status and the <i>Codex Justinianus</i>,” <i>Roman Legal Tradition</i> 9 (2013): 31–93, https://romanlegaltradition.org/contents/2013/RLT9-EVANSGRUBBS.PDF. [Open access]","Luca Loschiavo, “Was Rome still a Centre of Legal Culture between the 6th and 8th Centuries? Chasing the Manuscripts,” <i>Rechtsgeschichte – Legal History</i> 23 (2015): 83–108, https://doi.org/10.12946/rg23/083-108. [Open access]","Timothy G. Kearley, “The Enigma of Samuel Parsons Scott,” <i>Roman Legal Tradition</i> 10 (2014): 1–37, https://romanlegaltradition.org/contents/2014/RLT10-KEARLEY.PDF. [Open access]","Vojin Nedeljković, “Justinian’s πάτριος φωνή,” <i>Balcanica</i> 47 (2016): 55–73, https://doi.org/10.2298/BALC1647055N. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Vatican%2C_Biblioteca_Apostolica_Vaticana_Ms_Vatican_Bibliotheca_Palatina_Latin_765_fol_1r_Miniature_of_Justinian_having_the_Corpus_Juris_Civilis_compiled.jpg/960px-Vatican%2C_Biblioteca_Apostolica_Vaticana_Ms_Vatican_Bibliotheca_Palatina_Latin_765_fol_1r_Miniature_of_Justinian_having_the_Corpus_Juris_Civilis_compiled.jpg","title":"Justinian and his jurists in a 14th-century manuscript","desc":"An opening miniature showing the emperor enthroned among lawyers, from a 14th-century copy of the Institutes and Codex now in the Vatican Library.","credit":"Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Pal. lat. 765, fol. 1r, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vatican%2C_Biblioteca_Apostolica_Vaticana_Ms_Vatican_Bibliotheca_Palatina_Latin_765_fol_1r_Miniature_of_Justinian_having_the_Corpus_Juris_Civilis_compiled.jpg","alt":"A gilded medieval miniature of a crowned emperor seated with an open book, surrounded by robed lawyers, under a winged angel."}},
{"id":"wh-440","num":440,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Built for Justinian and finished in 537, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried its dome on pendentives at a scale nobody had attempted before, and was the cathedral of Constantinople for nine centuries.","questions":["Byzantine writers took the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to mean not a saint but the Divine Wisdom, which they identified with Christ, the Word made flesh.","Paul the Silentiary wrote a poem on the lamps and windows of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for its re-consecration in 562, after the first dome had come down and been rebuilt higher."],"answer":"Hagia Sophia","answerText":"Hagia Sophia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">532 – 537 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dome fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">558 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rededicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">562 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Became a mosque</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1453 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hagia Sophia</b> is the great church of Constantinople, built for Justinian between 532 and 537 on the site of an earlier basilica that went back to the middle of the 4th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name means Holy Wisdom, and Byzantine writers from the 5th century to the 14th understood that Wisdom to be Christ, the Word made flesh, rather than a saint of that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The building carries a dome over a square by way of pendentives, the curved triangles of masonry that turn a square bay into a circular base, a solution that leaves the corners open instead of filling them with the squinch arches earlier builders used. Pendentives had been built before in smaller churches, so its architects had predecessors, but nobody had used them at this scale or composed them so boldly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dome seems to rest on light, because a ring of windows runs round its foot and the piers that carry it are masked behind screens of columns. <br><br> The 550s were a decade of intense seismic activity, and ten earthquakes affecting Roman and Byzantine territory can be identified from the written sources between 550 and 557.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first dome came down in 558, and the replacement, raised steeper and higher by the nephew of one of the original architects, is the ribbed and window-ringed dome that stands over the building today. For the re-consecration in 562 Justinian had Paul the Silentiary write a long poem on the church, and its best-known passages describe the lighting, natural and artificial together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The figural mosaics were destroyed under the iconoclast emperors and replaced afterwards: the apse Virgin carries an inscription saying that what the impostors had cast down here, pious emperors have set up again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It became a mosque after the Ottoman conquest of 1453, a museum in 1935, and a mosque once more in 2020.","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","religion","archaeology","europe"],"sources":["Zofia Brzozowska, “The Church of Divine Wisdom or of Christ – the Incarnate ‘Logos’? Dedication of Hagia Sophia in Constantinople in the Light of Byzantine Sources from 5th to 14th Century,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 2 (2012): 85–96, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.02.08. [Open access]","Ivica Marković, “The meaning, origin and use of masonry pendentives at the early Christian architecture,” <i>Arhitektura i urbanizam</i> 50 (2020): 36–48, https://doi.org/10.5937/a-u0-25841. [Open access]","Vasile Mărculeț, Cătălina Mărculeț, and Ioan Mărculeț, “Cutremurele de pământ care au afectat teritoriile Imperiului Romano-Bizantin în perioada 550–560,” <i>Revista Bistriţei</i> 39 (2025): 7–45, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.17569437. [Open access]","Tatiana L. Aleksandrova, “The Image of Light in the ‘Ekphrasis of the Hagia Sophia’ by Paul the Silentiary,” <i>Studia Litterarum</i> 8, no. 1 (2023): 82–99, https://doi.org/10.22455/2500-4247-2023-8-1-82-99. [Open access]","Natalia Teteriatnikov, “Hagia Sophia, Constantinople: Religious images and their functional context after iconoclasm,” <i>Zograf</i> 30 (2005): 9–19, https://doi.org/10.2298/ZOG0530009T. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Hagia_Sophia%27s_%E2%8C%8030.6m_main_dome%2C_semidomes%2C_and_medallions_%288395018647%29.jpg/960px-Hagia_Sophia%27s_%E2%8C%8030.6m_main_dome%2C_semidomes%2C_and_medallions_%288395018647%29.jpg","title":"The main dome of Hagia Sophia from below","desc":"The dome, about 30.6 metres (100 feet) across, resting on an arcade of forty windows above the pendentives and semidomes, with Ottoman calligraphic roundels on the piers.","credit":"Photograph by Jorge Láscar, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Sophia%27s_%E2%8C%8030.6m_main_dome%2C_semidomes%2C_and_medallions_%288395018647%29.jpg","alt":"Looking up inside a domed church at a ribbed dome ringed with windows, with a half-dome below it and large black calligraphic discs on the piers."}},
{"id":"wh-441","num":441,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Daughter of a bear keeper at the hippodrome and an open patron of Monophysite Christianity, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Justinian’s wife and empress from 527 until her death in 548.","questions":["Almost everything scandalous ever said about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from one invective by Procopius, a book that stayed unpublished until a manuscript was printed at Lyon in 1623.","A Syriac writer who admired <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and wrote independently of Procopius still says she came from the brothel, which is why that part of the story is hard to dismiss."],"answer":"Theodora","answerText":"Theodora","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Empress</span><span class=\"dt-v\">527 – 548 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">548 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Secret History</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First printed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1623 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Theodora</b> was the wife of Justinian I and empress of the eastern Roman empire from his accession in 527 until her death in 548, and the mosaic panel in San Vitale at Ravenna sets her and her court opposite his. Procopius says in his <i>Secret History</i> that she was the daughter of a bear keeper at the hippodrome and had worked as a prostitute before Justinian married her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not his word alone: John of Ephesus, who admired her and wrote independently of him, still admits that she came from the brothel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As empress she was an open supporter of Monophysite Christianity, and a Latin chronicler recording her death called her an enemy of the council of Chalcedon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She bore Justinian no children, and she died in 548, seventeen years before he did, leaving him to reign alone for longer than they had reigned together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nearly everything lurid ever said about her comes from that one book, an invective written in the late 540s by a man who had served the general Belisarius for at least thirteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its date is argued over, because Procopius says a state of affairs had lasted 32 years and does not make clear whether he counts from Justin I’s accession in 518 or Justinian’s in 527.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cross-references between his works suggest it was finished about 550, alongside the histories in which the same author praises the regime the book attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Theodora’s hold over Justinian is a theme the work is built around rather than a neutral report, and Procopius credits Justinian with his uncle’s deeds partly in order to lengthen it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The book itself then vanished for a thousand years: a manuscript was found and printed at Lyon in 1623 under the title the 10th-century <i>Suda</i> gives it, meaning unedited notes, and 17th-century readers took it for a look behind the curtain of Byzantine history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","history","europe","politics","religion"],"sources":["John Scarborough, “Theodora, Aetius of Amida, and Procopius: Some Possible Connections,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 53, no. 4 (2013): 742–62, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/14791. [Open access]","Brian Croke, “Procopius’ <i>Secret History</i>: Rethinking the Date,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 45, no. 4 (2005): 405–31, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/331. [Open access]","Anthony Kaldellis, “The Date and Structure of Prokopios’ <i>Secret History</i> and His Projected Work on Church History,” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 49, no. 4 (2009): 585–616, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/1261. [Open access]","David Alan Parnell, “The Rivalry of Procopius of Caesarea and Antonina the Patrician,” <i>Classica Cracoviensia</i> 27 (2024): 243–62, https://doi.org/10.12797/CC.27.2024.27.10. [Open access]","Andrey V. Golubkov, “Anekdota of Procopius of Caesarea between History and Literature: Basileus vs Demon; Basilissa vs Hetaera,” <i>Studia Litterarum</i> 3, no. 4 (2018): 106–15, https://doi.org/10.22455/2500-4247-2018-3-4-106-115. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Mosaic_of_Theodora_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_%28Ravenna%2C_Italy%29.jpg/960px-Mosaic_of_Theodora_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_%28Ravenna%2C_Italy%29.jpg","title":"Theodora and her court at San Vitale","desc":"The mosaic panel in the basilica of San Vitale at Ravenna, consecrated in 547, showing the empress carrying a chalice with her courtiers and ladies behind her.","credit":"Photograph by Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_of_Theodora_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_%28Ravenna%2C_Italy%29.jpg","alt":"A gold-ground mosaic of a crowned woman in purple holding a jewelled cup, flanked by courtiers and women in patterned silks."}},
{"id":"wh-442","num":442,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Beginning at Pelusium in 541 and reaching Constantinople the next year, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened a pandemic of <i>Yersinia pestis</i> that returned in waves until about 750.","questions":["Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed a quarter of the eastern empire or far fewer is disputed, because pollen cores, coinage and papyri show no sign of a collapse in population.","The bodies in a Bavarian graveyard and in a mass grave at Jerash have yielded <i>Yersinia pestis</i> DNA, which settles what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually was."],"answer":"Plague of Justinian","answerText":"Plague of Justinian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">541 CE, at Pelusium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constantinople</span><span class=\"dt-v\">542 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First Pandemic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">541 – 750 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">DNA confirmed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2013 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Plague of Justinian</b> is the name given to the outbreak that struck the eastern Roman empire in 541 and to the pandemic it opened, which came back in waves until about 750.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Procopius, who lived through it, says it began in Egypt among the people of Pelusium and then moved over the whole earth, halting in each country for a set time before going on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It reached Constantinople in 542 and had touched North Africa, Italy, Spain and the Frankish border by the winter of 543.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contemporaries described swellings in the groin and the armpit, and for centuries the disease was identified as bubonic plague on the strength of those descriptions alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That identification now rests on ancient DNA rather than on reading, because <i>Yersinia pestis</i> has been recovered from the bones of people buried in the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest early evidence came from Aschheim in Bavaria, a graveyard of 438 people with a striking number of double and multiple burials clustered in the second half of the 6th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2025 the bacterium was sequenced from five individuals in a mass grave at Jerash in Jordan, the first genomes from the eastern Mediterranean and the closest yet to where the outbreak was first recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How many it killed is a different question and an open one: on one account it took between a quarter and a half of the eastern empire’s people, fifteen to a hundred million, in a few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that, a survey of legislation, coinage, papyri, inscriptions, pollen and burial practice finds nothing that tracks a population collapse, and 505 papyri dated between 520 and 570 show no break at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The route is contested as well, since there is little evidence for enough rats in the pre-modern Mediterranean to carry a rat-and-flea pandemic, and bubonic plague can turn pneumonic and pass between people without any vector.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","science","genetics","europe"],"sources":["Michaela Harbeck, Lisa Seifert, Stephanie Hänsch, David M. Wagner, Dawn Birdsell, Katy L. Parise, Ingrid Wiechmann, et al., “Yersinia pestis DNA from Skeletal Remains from the 6th Century AD Reveals Insights into Justinianic Plague,” <i>PLoS Pathogens</i> 9, no. 5 (2013): e1003349, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.ppat.1003349. [Open access]","Lauren A. White and Lee Mordechai, “Modeling the Justinianic Plague: Comparing hypothesized transmission routes,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 4 (2020): e0231256, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0231256. [Open access]","Lee Mordechai, Merle Eisenberg, Timothy P. Newfield, Adam Izdebski, Janet E. Kay, and Hendrik Poinar, “The Justinianic Plague: An inconsequential pandemic?,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 51 (2019): 25546–54, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1903797116. [Open access]","Maria Grazia Moroni, “Peste, carestia e cause secondo Procopio di Cesarea,” <i>Picenum Seraphicum</i> 36 (2022): 73–100, https://doi.org/10.63277/2385-1341/3245. [Open access]","Swamy Adapa, Karen Hendrix, Aditya Upadhyay, Subhajeet Dutta, Andrea Vianello, Gregory O’Corry-Crowe, et al., “Genetic Evidence of <i>Yersinia pestis</i> from the First Pandemic,” <i>Genes</i> 16, no. 8 (2025): 926, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12385600/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg/960px-Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg","title":"Yersinia pestis under the electron microscope","desc":"The bacterium that causes plague, photographed in the foregut of a flea; ancient DNA has since recovered it from bodies buried in the 6th century.","credit":"NIAID, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yersinia_pestis_scanned_with_electron_micrograph.jpg","alt":"A scanning electron micrograph showing a dense mass of rod-shaped bacteria."}},
{"id":"wh-443","num":443,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Theophanes credits a man named Kallinikos with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the incendiary that the Byzantine navy sprayed from siphons and that the empire kept secret for centuries.","questions":["The recipe for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was guarded so well that historians can only guess at it, though most now think petroleum from the Black Sea coast was the main ingredient.","When an Arab fleet besieged Constantinople in 717, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was turned on it, and the siege was lifted on 15 August the following year."],"answer":"Greek fire","answerText":"Greek fire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Kallinikos, 7th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arab siege</span><span class=\"dt-v\">717 – 718 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege lifted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 718 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 12th century</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Greek fire</b> was the incendiary weapon of the Byzantine navy, a liquid that could be thrown or sprayed at enemy ships and went on burning where it landed. Incendiary devices in war were not a Byzantine invention, but the Byzantines were the first to use them on a large scale and to real effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Theophanes credits its creation to a man named Kallinikos, in a notice too short to say whether he devised the mixture, the means of firing it, or both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The simplest way to use such a mixture was to seal it in barrels or clay pots and throw them with a catapult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The elaborate way was a machine: a heated container, a connecting pipe and a nozzle, with a piston that pressurised the mixture and drove it out to be lit at the muzzle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What was in it is not known, because the empire kept the method of making it so successfully that nothing survives to settle the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Medieval writers name sulphur, saltpetre, tar, resin, colophony, petroleum and plant oils among the ingredients, and say the sort that caught fire on water had quicklime in it as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The modern argument has run through saltpetre, which is not attested in Europe or the Near East before the 13th century, and quicklime, which needs the water the sources say was not always there; petroleum now has most support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That fits the geography, since crude oil seeped to the surface on the north-east shore of the Black Sea, at Tmutarakan on the Taman peninsula across the Kerch strait from Crimea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Excavation has found the workings there and the high-necked jars that carried the oil to the capital, and Theophanes, who reports the weapon at the Arab siege lifted on 15 August 718, gives the victory to God rather than to chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["technology","history","warfare","europe","science"],"sources":["Maciej Kokoszko and Mirosław J. Leszka, “Naval Fire/Liquid Fire. Byzantine ‘Miracle’ Weapon and the Question of its Familiarity to the Bulgarians between the 7th and 11th Century,” <i>Fasciculi Archaeologiae Historicae</i> 25 (2012): 9–15, https://journals.iaepan.pl/fah/article/view/1448. [Open access]","Witold Świętosławski, “Greek Fire in the Military Activities of the Cumans,” <i>Fasciculi Archaeologiae Historicae</i> 25 (2012): 17–20, https://journals.iaepan.pl/fah/article/view/1449. [Open access]","Valeriy E. Naumenko, “About the Bosporos Oil of Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus and the Byzantine ‘Greek Fire’: Archaeological Evidence,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4</i> 24, no. 6 (2019): 81–89, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.6.6. [Open access]","Arkadiusz Siwko, “Between Rus’, Byzantium, and the Nomads: Tmutarakan’ in the Narrative of the Rus’ian Chronicles,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 75–96, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.40. [Open access]","Błażej Cecota and Konrad Figat, “Islam, the Arabs and Umayyad Rulers According to Theophanes the Confessor’s Chronography,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 2 (2012): 97–111, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.02.09. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Greekfire-madridskylitzes1.jpg/960px-Greekfire-madridskylitzes1.jpg","title":"Greek fire in the Madrid Skylitzes","desc":"A miniature from the 12th-century illustrated manuscript of John Skylitzes, showing the weapon sprayed from the prow of a ship against the fleet of the rebel Thomas the Slav.","credit":"Biblioteca Nacional de España, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greekfire-madridskylitzes1.jpg","alt":"A medieval manuscript painting of two ships, with a tube at the prow of one shooting a jet of flame at the other."}},
{"id":"wh-444","num":444,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Two spells of official hostility to religious images, from the 720s to 787 and from 815 to 843, make up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the winners wrote up as a heresy imposed from the palace.","questions":["A lost inscription of about 730 at a Lombard royal villa near Pavia says Caesar Leo had fallen into the pit of schism, the earliest sign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaching Italy.","Byzantine hymns name Leo III, Constantine V and Leo V as the villains of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but never name Theophilos, whose posthumous rehabilitation kept him out of them."],"answer":"Byzantine Iconoclasm","answerText":"Byzantine Iconoclasm","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 726 – 787 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nicaea II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">787 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">815 – 843 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Images restored</span><span class=\"dt-v\">843 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Byzantine Iconoclasm</b> was the imperial policy of removing religious images and forbidding their veneration, pursued in two spells: from the 720s to the council of 787, and again from 815 to 843. The date it began has moved with the evidence, and the 726 that older accounts give is no longer the accepted answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its earliest trace outside the empire is a lost inscription of about 730 from Corteolona, a royal residence of the Lombard king Liutprand near Pavia, saying that the emperor Leo had fallen from the summit of righteousness into the pit of schism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The patriarch Germanos had already written to a bishop, some time between 720 and 730, in fear that contesting sacred images would split the church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That letter survives only because it was read into the acts of the second council of Nicaea, the assembly that restored the images in 787.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The images came back for good in 843, and the mosaic of the Virgin then set in the apse of Hagia Sophia was given an inscription claiming that pious emperors had put back what impostors had thrown down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What that victory also produced was an account of the quarrel written by the side that won it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the 10th century ordinary monks knew iconoclasm as the work of three emperors, Leo III, Constantine V and Leo V, which is the shape 9th-century iconodules had given it: a heresy born in the palace rather than the church and imposed with little support from believers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The hymns written for the confessors name those emperors and their nicknames, and pass over the last iconoclast emperor, Theophilos, in silence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That silence was bought by his posthumous rehabilitation, which rests on a deathbed conversion the sources do not bear out, one 9th-century historian having him die an iconoclast while his widow resisted the restoration out of loyalty to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","religion","europe","art"],"sources":["Francesca Dell’Acqua and Clemens Gantner, “Resenting Byzantine Iconoclasm. Its Early Reception in Italy through an Inscription from Corteolona,” <i>Medieval Worlds</i> 9 (2019): 160–86, https://doi.org/10.1553/medievalworlds_no9_2019s160. [Open access]","Tatyana A. Senina, “Life of Saint Nikephoros of Sebaze as an Illustration of the Perception of the Iconoclastic Era in the 10th Century,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4</i> 24, no. 6 (2019): 284–96, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2019.6.22. [Open access]","Tatiana Senina, “Representation of the Iconoclast Emperors in the Byzantine Offices in Honour of the Confessors of Iconoduly,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4</i> 27, no. 6 (2022): 131–42, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2022.6.10. [Open access]","Martha Vinson, “The Terms ἐγκόλπιον and τενάντιον and the Conversion of Theophilus in the <i>Life of Theodora</i> (BHG 1731),” <i>Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies</i> 36, no. 1 (1995): 89–99, https://grbs.library.duke.edu/index.php/grbs/article/view/3151. [Open access]","Natalia Teteriatnikov, “Hagia Sophia, Constantinople: Religious images and their functional context after iconoclasm,” <i>Zograf</i> 30 (2005): 9–19, https://doi.org/10.2298/ZOG0530009T. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Crucifixion_with_iconoclasts%2C_Chludov_Psalter%2C_folio_67r.jpg/960px-Crucifixion_with_iconoclasts%2C_Chludov_Psalter%2C_folio_67r.jpg","title":"The Chludov Psalter, folio 67r","desc":"A page of a 9th-century Greek psalter: below the Crucifixion, where a soldier lifts a sponge of vinegar on a pole, two men efface a roundel icon of Christ with a sponge on a pole of their own.","credit":"Moscow State Historical Museum, MS D.129, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crucifixion_with_iconoclasts%2C_Chludov_Psalter%2C_folio_67r.jpg","alt":"A manuscript page of Greek text with the Crucifixion painted at the right and, below it, two figures raising a pole against a small round portrait of Christ."}},
{"id":"wh-445","num":445,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Historians now treat <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a quarrel that widened over centuries rather than a rupture achieved by the mutual anathemas exchanged at Constantinople in 1054.","questions":["The Greek charges that Latin priests shaved, used unleavened bread and sang no alleluia in Lent are the small change of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a division whose theological core was the Filioque.","Kyivan metropolitans denounced Latin heresies while their princes went on marrying westward, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks less like a single break than a slow estrangement."],"answer":"East–West Schism","answerText":"East–West Schism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Photian schism</span><span class=\"dt-v\">858 – 867 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Union synod</span><span class=\"dt-v\">879 – 880 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mutual anathemas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1054 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Error lists</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 – 1700 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>East–West Schism</b> is the division between the Greek church of Constantinople and the Latin church of Rome, dated by convention to 1054 but better understood as a quarrel opened in the 9th century. Its first crisis came when Photios was raised to the patriarchate in 858 over the deposed Ignatios, and peaked with his encyclical of 867, which broke communion with Pope Nicholas I.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three things were at issue: jurisdiction over the newly baptised Bulgarians, whether Constantinople could claim powers in the East matching the pope’s in the West, and whether churches north of the Alps could add <i>Filioque</i> to a creed saying only that the Spirit proceeds from the Father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That breach was mended by a synod at Constantinople in 879 and 880, which settled Photios’s standing and the Bulgarian question and reaffirmed the creed without unauthorised additions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It neither abolished the Roman primacy nor let it work apart from conciliar agreement, and is read now as evidence for a church understood as a communion of churches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Filioque</i> remained the one charge with real theological weight, argued over by Greek writers from Photios to the last generation before 1453.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their handling of it grew subtler rather than harder: Maximus the Confessor had distinguished the Latin <i>procedere</i> from the Greek word for origination, Maximos Planoudes translated Augustine on the Trinity late in the 13th century, and by the 14th some Byzantines read him as compatible with their own doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest ran toward triviality: wafers instead of leavened bread, imposed celibacy, no alleluia in Lent, even the tonsure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lists of Latin errors circulated from the later 12th century, adding that western priests shaved and ate unclean animals, and stayed popular in the Balkans and eastern Europe into the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yet the break was slower on the ground than on the page: the metropolitans of Kyiv condemned Latin heresies while their princes kept western ties and even the cults of saints such as Olaf of Norway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["event","history","religion","europe","politics"],"sources":["Filippo Carcione, “La recezione dell’autorità pontificia a Costantinopoli tra IX e XI secolo: alcune circostanze,” <i>Theologica Leoniana</i> 12 (2023): 81–96, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.13822894. [Open access]","Wojciech Słomski, “The Synod of Constantinople 879–880 as a witness to the ecclesiology of communion: The horos, canons and the problem of the boundaries of orthodoxy,” <i>HTS Teologiese Studies / Theological Studies</i> 82, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v82i1.11463. [Open access]","Petr Pashkov, “‘Augustine Agreed with Our Teachers’: Interpretations of Saint Augustine’s Triadology in Byzantine Anti-Latin Polemic,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4</i> 30, no. 6 (2025): 140–49, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2025.6.10. [Open access]","Ангел Николов and Камен Станев, “Обрядовые уклонения и дурные привычки латинских еретиков в византийско-славянской полемической литературе Средневековья,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 4 (2014): 125–39, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.04.08. [Open access]","Simon Malmenvall, “Reception of Latin Christendom in Kyivan Rus’ (10th–12th Centuries) between Doctrinal Identity and Practical Considerations,” <i>Ephata</i> 6, no. 2 (2024): 29–50, https://doi.org/10.34632/ephata.2024.17753. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/The_interrogation_of_Patriarch_Photios.jpg/960px-The_interrogation_of_Patriarch_Photios.jpg","title":"The interrogation of Patriarch Photios","desc":"A 13th-century miniature in the Madrid Skylitzes showing the patriarch Photios examined before a seated tribunal. His deposition and return divided Constantinople and Rome for a generation from 858.","credit":"Madrid Skylitzes, Biblioteca Nacional de España; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_interrogation_of_Patriarch_Photios.jpg","alt":"A manuscript painting: a bearded man in a plain robe stands gesturing before five seated figures under two red arches, with clergy and onlookers at either side."}},
{"id":"wh-446","num":446,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Councils rather than a single head hold together <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose churches govern themselves and whose worship aims at the deification of those who share in it.","questions":["The word <i>theosis</i> stands in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> roughly where justification stands in Protestant theology, and Gregory Palamas kept it from becoming pantheism by dividing God’s energies from his essence.","Hierarchies of heaven and earth, a priesthood ranked apart from the laity and a liturgy that acts on those attending it came into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a forged Neoplatonist corpus."],"answer":"Eastern Orthodox Church","answerText":"Eastern Orthodox Church","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dionysian corpus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Basil’s anaphora</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Essence-energies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council of Crete</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2016 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Eastern Orthodox Church</b> is the communion of self-governing churches that carry forward the Greek Christianity of the Roman East, and it is held together by councils rather than by a single head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Conciliarity is its basic organising institution, running from the parish to the level of the whole communion, and it has proved flexible enough to bend to very different needs and circumstances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is also slow: a Holy and Great Council of the whole body met only in Crete in 2016, after decades of preparatory conferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of its inherited picture of the world came from writings passed under the name of Dionysius the Areopagite, composed around 500 and shown to be pseudonymous by how closely they follow the Neoplatonist Proclus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From them the tradition took a layered universe of heavenly and earthly hierarchies, a ranked priesthood set apart from the laity, and a liturgy understood to act upon those who attend it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That liturgy is old, and it has been edited: the eucharistic prayer bearing the name of Basil is a revision of an older one from Caesarea, expanded with biblical and Christological matter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument for that was made in 1931 and can now be tested against a database holding the whole of surviving Greek literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the worship is for is caught in a word western Christians seldom use, <i>theosis</i> or deification: the real union of a human being with God as the last stage of salvation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It occupies the place that justification holds in Protestant theology, and the two traditions have been arguing over the pair for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What keeps deification from collapsing into pantheism is a distinction Gregory Palamas defended in the 14th century: creatures share in God’s energies and never in his essence, so the gulf between creator and creature stands while a real communion crosses it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","religion","europe","philosophy"],"sources":["Vladimir Kharlamov, “Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite and Eastern Orthodoxy: Acceptance of the Corpus Dionysiacum and Integration of Neoplatonism into Christian Theology,” <i>Theological Reflections: Euro-Asian Journal of Theology</i>, no. 16 (2016): 120–54, https://doi.org/10.29357/issn.2521-179X.2016.16.120. [Open access]","Ciprian I. Streza, “Modern theological research: The authorship of the Byzantine anaphora of Saint Basil under investigation with the Thesaurus Linguae Graecae database,” <i>HTS Teologiese Studies / Theological Studies</i> 77, no. 4 (2021), https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v77i4.6684. [Open access]","Barend J. Van Wyk, “Theosis in die Oosters-Ortodokse en Westers-Protestantse teologiese debat,” <i>HTS Teologiese Studies / Theological Studies</i> 75, no. 4 (2019), https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v75i4.5355. [Open access]","Christos Terezis and Elias Tempelis, “Aspects of the Revelation of the Divine in St. Gregory Palamas’ Treatise <i>De Operationibus Divinis</i>,” <i>Perichoresis</i> 17, no. 4 (2019): 3–21, https://doi.org/10.2478/perc-2019-0025. [Open access]","Dalibor Đukić and Rade Kisić, “The Holy and Great Council in the context of synodality of the church-canonical and ecumenical perspective,” <i>Zbornik radova Pravnog fakulteta, Novi Sad</i> 55, no. 4 (2021): 571–600, https://doi.org/10.5937/zrpfns55-30159. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80._%D0%A5%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%8C%D1%8F._26.06.2016.jpg/960px-%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80._%D0%A5%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%8C%D1%8F._26.06.2016.jpg","title":"Primates at the Holy and Great Council, Chania, 26 June 2016","desc":"Heads of several of the self-governing Orthodox churches on the closing day of the Holy and Great Council in Crete, the first assembly of the whole communion in modern times.","credit":"Photograph by the Hellenic Ministry of Foreign Affairs, CC BY-SA 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80._%D0%A5%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%8C%D1%8F._26.06.2016.jpg","alt":"Seven bearded churchmen in black and white robes, several holding staffs and wearing pectoral medallions, standing before a stone doorway with clergy behind them."}},
{"id":"wh-447","num":447,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Moravia asked Constantinople for teachers in 863, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered with a new alphabet and a liturgy in the Slavs’ own tongue, against clergy who wanted Latin.","questions":["A 10th-century Bulgarian monk credited the elder of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with making 38 letters for a people who until then had reckoned with strokes and notches.","Excavators at Blatnograd have found pottery scratched with what may be rounded Glagolitic, from the winter <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent there on the road from Moravia to Rome."],"answer":"Cyril and Methodius","answerText":"Cyril and Methodius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mission begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">863 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Blatnograd</span><span class=\"dt-v\">866 – 867 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cyril and Methodius</b> were brothers from Thessalonica sent out from Constantinople to Great Moravia after 863, at the invitation of its prince Rastislav.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rastislav had sworn loyalty to the Frankish emperor Louis but held the German and Latin mission in his lands at arm’s length, and only that initiative of his, taken without Frankish approval, let a Byzantine mission in at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had room to try it because the Franks were then fighting one another for mastery of a realm running from the Pyrenees to the Carpathians, in which Moravia counted as a small problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the work the elder brother, Constantine the Philosopher, who took the name Cyril at the end of his life, made an alphabet for Slavonic, the script now called Glagolitic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Bulgarian monk writing early in the 10th century recorded that the Slavs while still pagan had no books and reckoned with strokes and notches, and that God then sent them Constantine, who made 38 letters for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Where those letters came from is among the oldest unsettled questions in Slavic studies, and every account of it is a hypothesis rather than a finding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One much-followed suggestion builds most of them out of three sacred signs, the cross, the circle and the triangle, and the alphabet also seems to show that its inventor knew Semitic languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another derives a whole family of the characters from the Greek letter upsilon, turned to the left or the right to make several letters out of one shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Late in 866 the brothers broke their journey from Moravia to Rome at Blatnograd in Pannonia, the seat of prince Chezil, where excavation has turned up burnished pottery bottles scratched with what may be rounded Glagolitic letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The script spread from Moravia and Pannonia into Bohemia, briefly into Hungary, marginally into Rus’, and above all into Croatia, where the pope allowed its use and where it was still written long after it had died out elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","religion","europe","technology"],"sources":["Karel Mácha, “The philosopher Konstantin and his mission. A few notes on the edge of Cyril and Methodius Mission,” <i>Caritas et veritas</i> 3, no. 1 (2013): 11–28, https://doi.org/10.32725/cetv.2013.003. [Open access]","Valeri Yotov, “Ceramics from the Capital of the Blatna Principality – the Traces of the Mission of the Brothers Cyril and Methodius in Blatnograd,” <i>Povolzhskaya Arkheologiya</i> 2, no. 32 (2020): 200–210, https://doi.org/10.24852/pa2020.2.32.200.210. [Open access]","Boris Uspenskij, “Glagolitic Script as a Manifestation of Sacred Knowledge,” <i>Studi Slavistici</i> 10 (2013): 7–27, https://doi.org/10.13128/studi_slavis-14132. [Open access]","Анатолий Михайлович Кузнецов, “«Бывают странные сближенья…»: греческий юпсилон и глаголица,” <i>Slavistica Vilnensis</i> 57, no. 2 (2012): 7–14, https://doi.org/10.15388/SlavViln.2012.2.1207. [Open access]","Radoslav Večerka, “The Glagolitic Script in Medieval Europe Literature,” <i>Bohemica Olomucensia</i> 7, no. 3–4 (2015): 20–31, https://doi.org/10.5507/bo.2015.024. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/CodVatSlav3Glag158.jpg/960px-CodVatSlav3Glag158.jpg","title":"A page of the Codex Assemanianus","desc":"Folio 158 of an 11th-century Glagolitic gospel book, Codex Vaticanus Slavicus 3, written in the round Glagolitic the brothers' pupils carried out of Moravia.","credit":"Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Vat. Slav. 3; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CodVatSlav3Glag158.jpg","alt":"A manuscript page covered in rust-red rounded letters unlike any Latin or Greek script, with looped ornamental initials and a decorated headpiece."}},
{"id":"wh-448","num":448,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"The Primary Chronicle traces <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Varangians from beyond the sea, an origin story argued over for two centuries with more heat than the finds warrant.","questions":["Four treaties with Byzantium, a fleet that attacked Constantinople in 941 and Sviatoslav’s Khazar campaigns all belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the state ruled from Kyiv along the river roads.","St Sophia in Kyiv and the Caves monastery were raised in the 11th-century flowering of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose builders set Byzantine models beside local ones."],"answer":"Kievan Rus’","answerText":"Kievan Rus’","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rus’ attack</span><span class=\"dt-v\">941 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sviatoslav</span><span class=\"dt-v\">964 – 972 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tzimiskes’ war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">971 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1110s CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Kievan Rus’</b> was the state built along the river roads of eastern Europe by princes ruling from Kyiv, and its own account of its beginning is a story of foreigners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Primary Chronicle, compiled in the early 12th century, says that Varangians from beyond the sea founded the first realm of the Rus’, and archaeology does show heavy Scandinavian traffic through these lands in the Viking age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What that traffic means has been argued over for two centuries in a debate often steered by nationalist feeling and political aim rather than by the finds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle preserves four treaties made with Byzantium, and the dating of its first version to the 1110s is what sets a limit on when the grand princely title can have come into use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations with the empire were not only diplomatic: a Rus’ fleet attacked Constantinople in 941, and John Tzimiskes campaigned against the Rus’ in 971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Sviatoslav, whose short independent reign fell between 964 and 972, spent it on campaigns against his neighbours, above all the Khazar Khaganate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How many eastern campaigns he made, who went with him and how far Byzantine diplomacy pushed him are all still disputed, and the Dnipro-Buh estuary was the strategic hinge of the Rus’ route toward the Caspian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The 11th century brought a cultural rise that the chroniclers and later readers alike have called a golden age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> St Sophia was raised in Kyiv and the Caves monastery founded, and men such as Ilarion of Kyiv wrote the theology on which a Rus’ identity was built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The cathedral’s mosaics and painted ornament, made as the country was being Christianised, set Byzantine models beside local ones and are the plainest surviving measure of what the state had become.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","europe","politics","religion"],"sources":["Charlotta Hillerdal, “Vikings, Rus, Varangians: The ‘Varangian Problem’ in View of Ethnicity in Archaeology,” <i>Current Swedish Archaeology</i> 14, no. 1 (2006): 87–108, https://doi.org/10.37718/csa.2006.05. [Open access]","Mikhail Raev, “The emergence of the title Velikii Kniaz’ in Rus’ and the Povest’ Vremennykh let,” <i>Zbornik radova Vizantološkog instituta</i>, no. 51 (2014): 47–69, https://doi.org/10.2298/ZRVI1451047R. [Open access]","Volodymyr Kuzovkov, “Dnipro-Buh Estuary Coast in Context of Eastern Campaigns of Prince Sviatoslav,” <i>Eminak</i>, no. 3 (2021): 59–66, https://doi.org/10.33782/eminak2021.3%2835%29.541. [Open access]","Oleksandr Fylypchuk, “Saint Theodore Stratelates and the Rus’-Byzantine wars,” <i>Науковий вісник Чернівецького національного університету. Історія</i>, no. 53 (2021): 6–18, https://doi.org/10.31861/hj2021.53.6-18. [Open access]","Ростислав Завгородній, “Ornamental Decoration of Saint Sophia Cathedral in Kyiv of the 11th Century,” <i>Visual Culture of Ukraine</i>, no. 1 (2023): 12–22, https://doi.org/10.62479/vcu.2023.01.012. [Open access]","Justyna Kroczak, “The Role of the Bible in the Formation of Philosophical Thought in Kievan Rus’,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 6 (2016): 61–74, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.06.04. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Oranta-Kyiv.jpg/960px-Oranta-Kyiv.jpg","title":"The Orans mosaic, St Sophia, Kyiv","desc":"The apse mosaic of the Virgin at prayer in St Sophia in Kyiv, set in the 11th century in roughly two million tesserae and standing six metres high.","credit":"St Sophia Cathedral, Kyiv, via Google Arts & Culture; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oranta-Kyiv.jpg","alt":"A gold-ground mosaic of a standing woman in a blue robe and gold-brown mantle, both hands raised, filling a curved apse above a patterned band."}},
{"id":"wh-449","num":449,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Founded on the lower Danube in 681 and lost to Byzantium by 1018, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was for three centuries the empire’s most dangerous neighbour in Europe.","questions":["The <i>Zlatostruy</i>, the earliest Slavonic gathering of Chrysostom’s homilies, belongs to the 10th-century literary flowering of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Symeon and his son Peter.","Rather than the Byzantine themes, the administrative <i>komitats</i> of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resemble the Frankish marches in how they were set up and how their governors were appointed."],"answer":"First Bulgarian Empire","answerText":"First Bulgarian Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Asparuh</span><span class=\"dt-v\">681 CE – c. 700 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Boris baptised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">860s CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Symeon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">893 – 927 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1018 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>First Bulgarian Empire</b> was the state that the Bulgars under Asparuh founded on the lower Danube in 681, and it was born out of a war with Byzantium that ended in the empire recognising it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the opening of the 9th century its territory had almost doubled, its people were of several nations, and it faced Byzantium on one side and the Frankish realm on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That growth forced a fresh administrative division into <i>komitats</i>, whose real likeness is to the Frankish marches rather than to the Byzantine themes, in where they were put, how they were set up and how their governors were appointed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its ruler Boris accepted Christianity in the 860s, and the Byzantine account of the bargaining behind it is strange enough that one reading treats it as the wreck of a plan to marry him to an empress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His son Symeon, who ruled from 893 to 927, fought long and victorious wars against the empire and carried Bulgaria to the leading place in south-eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The 10th century was the country’s literary flowering, and in its first quarter the earliest Slavonic collection of John Chrysostom’s homilies was put together, the <i>Zlatostruy</i> or Golden Stream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Work of that kind shows how thoroughly Byzantine models were taken over and made to serve a new language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The politics were less steady: a son of Symeon named Michael rose against his brother Peter around 930, and the throne was contested more than once in the decades that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> War with Byzantium ran through the whole of the empire’s life, and the seals and chronicles show regiments from the eastern themes of Anatolia being brought west to fight in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the end tsar Samuel avoided pitched battle, fought light and held fortresses, and by 1018 the state had lost its independence for a century and a half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","europe","politics","warfare"],"sources":["Symeon Antonov, “The Byzantine East and Bulgaria – The Eastern Armies of Byzantium in the Wars Against the First Bulgarian Empire,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 14 (2024): 215–50, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140x.14.02. [Open access]","Nikolay Hrissimov, “On the Origins of Komitats in the First Bulgarian Empire,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 9 (2019): 429–53, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140x.09.23. [Open access]","Kostiantyn Bardola, “‘The Unbroken Fellowship’: What Did Khan Boris and Empress Theodora Agree upon?,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 14 (2024): 251–77, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140x.14.22. [Open access]","Yavor Miltenov, “A Note on the Adoption of the Byzantine Models in Medieval Bulgaria (9th–10th Centuries). The Case with the Zlatostruy Collection,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 7 (2017): 161–67, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140x.07.11. [Open access]","Mirosław J. Leszka, “Bunt Michała przeciw carowi Piotrowi (?930),” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i>, no. 58 (2017): 15–23, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2017.58.2. [Open access]","Ivelin Ivanov, “Tsar Samuel Against Emperor Basil II: Why Did Bulgaria Loose the Battle With the Byzantine Empire at the Beginning of the 11th Century?,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 1 (2011): 205–12, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140x.01.12. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Saint_Theodore_%28Ceramic_icon%29.jpg/960px-Saint_Theodore_%28Ceramic_icon%29.jpg","title":"The ceramic icon of Saint Theodore","desc":"An icon of Saint Theodore painted across glazed ceramic tiles at the Preslav literary school, among the finest surviving work of the Bulgarian court in its literary century.","credit":"Preslav literary school; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Theodore_%28Ceramic_icon%29.jpg","alt":"A bearded haloed face made up of many square ceramic tiles, several missing, the eyes and dark hair painted in brown and red over a pale ground."}},
{"id":"wh-450","num":450,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Romanos IV Diogenes marched east to destroy the Seljuk state and instead lost <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Alp Arslan on 26 August 1071, and was taken prisoner.","questions":["Turkmen who had tried and failed to settle Anatolia in the first half of the 11th century stayed there after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and principalities soon rose on Byzantine ground.","Modern Turkey made a founding story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, although the research finds it was largely passed over in the Republic’s public life until the 1950s."],"answer":"Battle of Manzikert","answerText":"Battle of Manzikert","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 August 1071</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alexios I</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1081 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Manzikert</b> was fought on 26 August 1071 in eastern Anatolia between the emperor Romanos IV Diogenes and the Seljuk sultan Alp Arslan, and ended in an unexpectedly heavy Byzantine defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romanos had taken the throne on the first day of 1068, out of the crisis that ran through the century, and marched east having mobilised everything the empire had in order to destroy the Great Seljuk state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of the writing since has dwelt on what the Seljuk army did well and less on what the Byzantines did badly, and one reading puts the chief cause in the emperor’s confidence in his numbers and in an inherited sense of superiority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground mattered too: the plain of Malazgirt is broken country, and its relief, water and approaches shaped where armies could stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Romanos was taken prisoner, and his exchange with the sultan is told most vividly in a Norman poem drawing on what knights who fought there remembered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Turkmen had been moving west out of Turkestan and Iran since the Seljuk state was founded in 1037, and their attempts to settle Anatolia in the first half of the 11th century had come to nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After 1071 they stayed, and within decades the Danishmendids, Saltuqids, Mengujekids and Artuqids, and the Seljuk sultanate of Anatolia itself, ruled land that had been Byzantine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that felt like from underneath can be read in the life of Christodoulos, born at Nicaea in 1020, driven in turn from Latros, Strobilos and Kos before an emperor gave him the island of Patmos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The empire did not fall over at once, and the claim that the Turks took Asia Minor within ten years is rejected; the deeper trouble was the feud of the Komnenos and Doukas families, settled only when Alexios I seized the throne in 1081.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In modern Turkey the battle became a founding story of national identity, though it was largely passed over in the Republic’s public life until the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","europe","politics"],"sources":["Murat Keçiş, “İhanetten İmparatorluğa: IV. Romanos Diogenes,” <i>Belleten</i> 86, no. 305 (2022): 89–117, https://doi.org/10.37879/belleten.2022.089. [Open access]","Mehmet Akif Ceylan, “Malazgirt Çevresinin Coğrafi Özellikleri ve Malazgirt Meydan Savaşına Etkileri,” <i>Dünya Coğrafyası ve Kalkınma Perspektifi</i>, no. 9 (2026): 29–50, https://doi.org/10.58606/jwgdp.1942913. [Open access]","Hazhar Hussein and Othman Omar, “Turkmen exodus to the Anatolia and establishing their pricipalities,” <i>Humanities Journal of University of Zakho</i> 11, no. 2 (2023): 454–68, https://doi.org/10.26436/hjuoz.2023.11.2.1068. [Open access]","Yavuz Selim Altınok, “Anadolu’da Türk Kent Kültürünü Oluşturan Mekânsal Yapılanmalar,” <i>Ahbar</i>, no. 3 (2025): 44–58, https://doi.org/10.62425/ahbar.1653405. [Open access]","Tevfik Orkun Develi, “Malazgirt Savaşı’nın popüler tarihçiliğe yansımaları: Cumhuriyet gazetesi örneği,” <i>Turkish History Education Journal</i> 12, no. 2 (2023): 157–77, https://doi.org/10.17497/tuhed.1342600. [Open access]","Cüneyt Güneş, “On Birinci Yüzyılda Bir Keşişin İzinden: Patmoslu Christodoulos ve Türkler,” <i>Ortaçağ Araştırmaları Dergisi</i> 6, no. 1 (2023): 427–46, https://doi.org/10.48120/oad.1283773. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/131_Bataille_de_Malazgirt.jpg/960px-131_Bataille_de_Malazgirt.jpg","title":"Manzikert in a 15th-century French manuscript","desc":"A miniature in a Paris copy of Boccaccio’s De casibus virorum illustrium, showing the fighting above and, below, the sultan using the captured emperor as a mounting block. The armour is that of the painter’s own century, not of 1071.","credit":"Rohan Master, Bibliothèque nationale de France, Français 226, fol. 265; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:131_Bataille_de_Malazgirt.jpg","alt":"A manuscript painting: mailed and plate-armoured soldiers fight on a hillside above, while below a crowned man in blue mounts a white horse, stepping on a man lying face down."}},
{"id":"wh-451","num":451,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"An army that had set out for Egypt instead carried out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in April 1204, installed a Latin emperor and shared the Greek provinces among its leaders.","questions":["Beside the two Old French eyewitness chronicles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands an Old Russian account written into the Novgorod First Chronicle within about fifty years.","The Grand Komnenoi of Trebizond called themselves the only lawful heirs of a Byzantium that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had, in their telling, merely broken apart for a time."],"answer":"Sack of Constantinople","answerText":"Sack of Constantinople","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">City stormed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1204 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Latin Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1204 – 1261 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trebizond</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1204 – 1461 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sack of Constantinople</b> was the storming and plundering of the Byzantine capital in April 1204 by an army that had set out to fight in Egypt and never reached it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crusaders had left Venice in the summer of 1202 and spent the next two years on a coast-hugging progress that turned as much on where an army could be fed as on where it meant to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The expedition carried its own churchmen of three distinct kinds, and the chronicler Robert of Clari, a poor knight from Picardy who was there, has more to say about them than about the strategy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two Old French accounts survive from participants, and beside them stands an eastern one: an Old Russian story of the taking of the city, written into the Novgorod First Chronicle by the middle of the 13th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its author appears to have had his information from people who saw it, and his account is sober enough that historians use it as evidence rather than as literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What followed was not a raid but a partition: a Latin emperor was installed in Constantinople and the empire’s lands were shared out among the victors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pope Innocent III, who had not wanted the diversion, then treated the survival of that Latin empire as vital, since he hoped to use it as the base for crusades to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Greek world did not disappear but broke into pieces, each claiming to be the real one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Trebizond on the Black Sea the Grand Komnenoi traced their line straight back to the Komnenian emperors and held themselves the only lawful heirs of a Byzantium they described as merely fallen apart for a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They put that claim on their coins, seals and chrysobulls and in portraits in the palace and the great churches, and their state outlasted the reconquest of Constantinople by two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","europe","religion"],"sources":["Zdzisław Pentek, “The Fourth Crusade and the Problem of Food Provision in The Accounts of Robert De Clari and Geoffroy De Villehardouin,” <i>Studia Historiae Oeconomicae</i> 32, no. 1 (2014): 109–14, https://doi.org/10.2478/sho-2014-0006. [Open access]","Zofia A. Brzozowska, “Opowieść o zdobyciu Konstantynopola przez uczestników czwartej wyprawy krzyżowej (6712/1204) w średniowiecznym piśmiennictwie ruskim,” <i>Slavia Antiqua</i>, no. 57 (2016): 109–52, https://doi.org/10.14746/sa.2016.57.6. [Open access]","Zdzisław Pentek, “The Clergy during the Fourth Crusade as Portrayed in Robert of Clari’s Chronicle,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 14 (2024): 563–71, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.14.04. [Open access]","Francesco Dall’Aglio, “Innocent III and South-eastern Europe: Orthodox, Heterodox, or Heretics?,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 9 (2019): 11–25, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.09.01. [Open access]","Tatyana Bardashova, “The Imperial Visual Propaganda in the Empire of Trebizond (1204-1461),” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4</i>, no. 3 (2015): 79–91, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2015.3.8. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"The crusaders at Venice","desc":"A 14th-century French miniature of the Fourth Crusade taking ship at Venice, the port the army left in the summer of 1202 on the road that ended at Constantinople.","credit":"Bibliothèque nationale de France, Français 2824, fol. 181; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fran%C3%A7ais_2824%2C_fol._181%2C_Arriv%C3%A9e_des_crois%C3%A9s_%C3%A0_Venise.jpeg","alt":"A manuscript painting of two crowded boats being rowed away from a many-towered white castle, in a blue and gold border.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Fran%C3%A7ais_2824%2C_fol._181%2C_Arriv%C3%A9e_des_crois%C3%A9s_%C3%A0_Venise.jpeg/960px-Fran%C3%A7ais_2824%2C_fol._181%2C_Arriv%C3%A9e_des_crois%C3%A9s_%C3%A0_Venise.jpeg"}},
{"id":"wh-452","num":452,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Mehmed II’s guns and an earlier failed siege lay behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1453, which ended eleven centuries of Roman rule from the city.","questions":["Doukas, Chalkokondyles, Sphrantzes and Kritoboulos all wrote at length about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and disagree so sharply that the familiar story is a composite of four.","Anonymous laments in everyday Greek mourned <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for two centuries afterwards, dwelling less on the fighting than on the beauty of the place itself."],"answer":"Fall of Constantinople","answerText":"Fall of Constantinople","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">City taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1453 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Scholarios</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1454 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Fall of Constantinople</b> was the conquest of the city by the Ottoman Turks under Sultan Mehmed II in 1453, and it is the event by which the Byzantine Empire is reckoned to have ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not the first Ottoman attempt: a siege in 1422 had failed, but it taught the besiegers a great deal about the walls, about what their guns could do and about how the defence behaved, and that knowledge was spent in 1453.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four Greek writers described the conquest at length, and the striking thing is how far they disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Doukas, Laonikos Chalkokondyles, George Sphrantzes and Kritoboulos of Imbros hold sharply different political views of the event and of the world before and after it, so the standard narrative is a composite of four incompatible ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of them reached for the classical historians to explain it, reading Herodotus and Thucydides in a way that put fortune at the centre of historical causation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Away from the historians the loss was worked out in verse, in anonymous laments composed in everyday Greek over the next two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They dwell less on the fighting than on the city itself, its beauty and its wonders, gone, as one of them says, like smoke or mist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The conquest did not abolish the Greek church, and Mehmed appointed Gennadios Scholarios patriarch of the city under Ottoman rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The familiar story that Scholarios kept his seat for a few months in the church of the Holy Apostles rests on sources written a century or more later, and the contemporary evidence, including his own writings, does not support it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What he did do there was officiate now and then and rescue some of the relics the church had held, which is a smaller claim and the one the sources will carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","warfare","europe","politics"],"sources":["Maja Nikolić, “The greatest misfortune in the Oikoumene: Byzantine historiography on the fall of Constantinople in 1453,” <i>Balcanica</i>, no. 47 (2016): 119–33, https://doi.org/10.2298/BALC1647119N. [Open access]","Tatiana Kushch, “The Ottoman Siege and Assault of Constantinople in 1422 AD: Its Military and Political Aspects,” <i>Vestnik Volgogradskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta. Seriya 4</i> 22, no. 5 (2017): 261–70, https://doi.org/10.15688/jvolsu4.2017.5.24. [Open access]","Mathieu de Bakker, “Explaining the End of an Empire: the Use of Ancient Greek Religious Views in Late Byzantine Historiography,” <i>Histos</i> 9 (2015): 127–71, https://doi.org/10.29173/histos89. [Open access]","Michał Bzinkowski, “Gone Like Smoke or Mist – the Beauty and Wonders of Constantinople in Vernacular Laments on its Fall,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 15 (2025): 11–30, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140x.15.26. [Open access]","Nicholas Melvani, “Gennadios Scholarios and the Church of the Holy Apostles,” <i>Zbornik radova Vizantološkog instituta</i>, no. 57 (2020): 117–42, https://doi.org/10.2298/ZRVI2057117M. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"The fall of Constantinople remembered","desc":"A 19th-century Greek painting of the siege and fall, made by Panagiotis Zografos under the direction of General Makriyannis, with captions in Greek beneath. It is memory rather than record.","credit":"Painting by Panagiotis Zografos; public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zografos-Makriyannis_02_The_fall_of_Konstantinople.jpg","alt":"A naive painting: a white walled city on a headland at the top right, a turbaned commander enthroned in the middle, soldiers and ships around him, and lines of Greek script below.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Zografos-Makriyannis_02_The_fall_of_Konstantinople.jpg/960px-Zografos-Makriyannis_02_The_fall_of_Konstantinople.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-453","num":453,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Caught between Rome and Persia with no neutral ground of its own, the Armenian kingdom under Tiridates III carried out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> early in the 4th century.","questions":["A cleric named Mashtots gave Armenian an alphabet a century after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that a church which had been borrowing its books could write its own.","The see at Tayk, founded during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, later lost its people to Chalcedonian belief and to the Georgian language, which shows how such a church could be undone."],"answer":"Christianisation of Armenia","answerText":"Christianisation of Armenia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace of Nisibis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">298 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Adoption</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 298 – 301 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alphabet made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 400s CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tayk diocese</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to the 900s CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Christianisation of Armenia</b> is the adoption of Christianity as the religion of the Armenian kingdom under Tiridates III, the first such adoption by any state and one whose date is argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One account of why it happened is political rather than devotional: Greater Armenia lay between two great powers, and its own religion, syncretic and then pressed by Zoroastrian reform, left it no neutral ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Christianity suited because it belonged to neither side and was rejected by both, so the king was buying a new kind of independence, and one recent argument dates his adoption of it to just after the peace of Nisibis in 298, or perhaps 299, rather than to the traditional 301.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The church that followed put bishops across the kingdom, and the see at Tayk, founded by Gregory himself, was still an Armenian Apostolic diocese in the 9th and 10th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tayk also shows how such a church could be lost: Chalcedonian belief spread there, part of the population drifted away from the Armenian church, and in time they were speaking Georgian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A church without a script borrows its books, and at the opening of the 5th century a cleric named Mashtots set out with his patriarch Sahak and his king Vramshapuh to give Armenian an alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They found that one already existed, held by a Syriac bishop called Daniel, and obtained it; some sources say it could not render Armenian sounds and others that it needed only small adjustments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is possible that all of the accounts describe the same process from different angles, weighting the Armenian team’s own contribution differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once Armenians had a literature they wrote about the wider Christian world, placing Ethiopia and its people within the biblical account of how the nations were divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their tales of other conversions invite comparison: at Aksum, Christianity now looks to have spread upwards through the population rather than down from a king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","religion","asia","politics"],"sources":["Artsruni Sahakyan, “Preconditions and Reasons for Christianity to Become a State Religion in Greater Armenia,” <i>Armenian Folia Anglistika</i> 20, no. 1 (2024): 161–74, https://doi.org/10.46991/AFA/2024.20.1.161. [Open access]","Аркади Акопов et al., “Распространение халкидонизма в Тайке и его влияние на формирование историко-культурной среды,” <i>Материалы по археологии и истории античного и средневекового Причерноморья</i>, no. 15 (2023): 736–44, https://doi.org/10.53737/2284.2023.77.54.028. [Open access]","Alessandro Orengo, “L’invenzione dell’alfabeto armeno: fatti e problemi,” <i>Rhesis. International Journal of Linguistics, Philology and Literature</i> 7, no. 1 (2016): 9–27, https://doi.org/10.13125/rhesis/5591. [Open access]","Zaroui Pogossian, “Armeno-Aethiopica in the Middle Ages: Geography, Tales of Christianization, Calendars, and Anti-Dyophysite Polemics,” <i>Aethiopica</i> 24 (2021): 104–40, https://doi.org/10.15460/aethiopica.24.0.1627. [Open access]","Rugare Rukuni, “Negus Ezana: Revisiting the Christianisation of Aksum,” <i>Verbum et Ecclesia</i> 42, no. 1 (2021), https://doi.org/10.4102/ve.v42i1.2083. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"Khor Virap, on the Ararat plain","desc":"The monastery of Khor Virap in the Ararat plain, built over the pit in which Armenian tradition says Gregory the Illuminator was imprisoned before the king's conversion.","credit":"Photograph by Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khor_Virap_Monastery%2C_Ararat_Plain%2C_Armenia.jpg","alt":"A walled monastery of pale stone with a red conical dome, standing on a rocky rise above flat green fields.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Khor_Virap_Monastery%2C_Ararat_Plain%2C_Armenia.jpg/960px-Khor_Virap_Monastery%2C_Ararat_Plain%2C_Armenia.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-454","num":454,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"United under David the Builder and dominant in its region by the 12th century, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was annexed piece by piece to Russia six hundred years later.","questions":["The painter Tevdore signed and dated murals in Svaneti in 1096, 1112 and 1130, which makes the art of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unusually easy to place in time.","David the Builder married his daughter to the Muslim ruler of Shirvan around 1111, one of the ways <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worked its power over its neighbours."],"answer":"Kingdom of Georgia","answerText":"Kingdom of Georgia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">David IV</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1089 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Court murals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1096 – 1130 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1801 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Kingdom of Georgia</b> was the Christian state that grew out of the Caucasian principalities and became the dominant power of its region in the 12th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> David IV the Builder came to the throne in 1089 at the age of sixteen, and is the king credited with uniting the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That power was worked partly by marriage: his daughter Tamar was married about 1111 to Manuchehr III, the Muslim ruler of Shirvan, and lived and reigned at his court for more than twenty years before returning home in the 1140s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She spent her last years as a nun at Tighva, the monastery she had built as her own retirement house and burial place, having cut her ties with her family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The kingdom had grown at the expense of its neighbours in belief as well as territory: to the south in Tayk, Chalcedonian Christianity spread among Armenians who drifted from their own church and in time were speaking Georgian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its art is unusually well dated, because the painters signed and dated their work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tevdore, a painter of David the Builder’s court, left murals in the highland churches of Svaneti with inscriptions giving his name, his standing and the year: Iprari in 1096, Lagurka in 1112, Nakipari in 1130.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They have usually been read as provincial work, though their iconography and style belong to the Byzantine and wider eastern Christian currents of their day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kingdom also produced a secular literature, and Shota Rustaveli, its great poet, has been read since as a figure of a Georgian renaissance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What remained of the eastern Georgian kingdom, Kartli-Kakheti, was annexed by Russia at the opening of the 19th century, which opened a new stage of Russian expansion into the Caucasus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","europe","asia","religion"],"sources":["Natia Natsvlishvili, “Female Monastic Patronage in Medieval Georgia: Queen Tamar and Her Monastery in Tighva,” <i>Studia Ceranea</i> 13 (2023): 599–617, https://doi.org/10.18778/2084-140X.13.38. [Open access]","Mzia Janjalia and Marina Bulia, “Rethinking the labels: Wall paintings by royal painter Tevdore in the Georgian highlands,” <i>Zograf</i>, no. 48 (2024): 27–52, https://doi.org/10.2298/ZOG2448027J. [Open access]","Аркади Акопов et al., “Распространение халкидонизма в Тайке и его влияние на формирование историко-культурной среды,” <i>Материалы по археологии и истории античного и средневекового Причерноморья</i>, no. 15 (2023): 736–44, https://doi.org/10.53737/2284.2023.77.54.028. [Open access]","V. Chelidze, “In the Footsteps of the Contexts of the Georgian Renaissance Poem: Shota Rustaveli in Jerusalem,” <i>Language and Text</i> 7, no. 4 (2020): 41–60, https://doi.org/10.17759/langt.2020070404. [Open access]","Valeriy Morkva, “Unlocking the Caucasus for Empire: Roots, causes and consequences of the Russian annexation of the East Georgian kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti, 1801,” <i>Cappadocia Journal of Area Studies</i> 2, no. 3 (2021): 152–72, https://doi.org/10.38154/cjas.15. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"Christ in the apse at Gelati","desc":"A 12th-century mosaic in the monastery of Gelati, the foundation of David IV the Builder, with the open book lettered in Georgian.","credit":"Photograph by Peter Ashton, CC BY-SA 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kutaisi%2C_Gelati_Monastery_Mosaic.jpg","alt":"A mosaic of a long-haired bearded figure in red and blue, one hand raised, holding an open book written in Georgian script, against a gold ground.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Kutaisi%2C_Gelati_Monastery_Mosaic.jpg/960px-Kutaisi%2C_Gelati_Monastery_Mosaic.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-455","num":455,"category":"Byzantium and the Christian East","question":"Read through Ezana’s own coins and inscriptions rather than through distant church historians, the founding of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looks less like a royal decree than a rise from below.","questions":["The <i>Kǝbrä nägäśt</i>, which grounds the Ethiopian monarchy in descent from Solomon, is now read as a 14th-century work made to serve <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a new dynasty.","Armenian writers of the 1st millennium placed Ethiopia within their accounts of the nations, one of many outside views of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its origins."],"answer":"Ethiopian Orthodox Church","answerText":"Ethiopian Orthodox Church","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proclamation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 333 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kǝbrä nägäśt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300s CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ethiopian Orthodox Church</b> is the Christian church of the Ethiopian highlands, which traces itself to the conversion of the Aksumite kingdom in the 4th century under the king Ezana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The royal proclamation accepting Christianity there has been put at about 333, though the Ethiopian calendar runs seven years behind and the same figure has been read as 340.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story usually told comes from church historians writing far away, above all Rufinus; reading Ezana through the coins and inscriptions he issued himself gives a different picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that evidence he used his new religion much as Constantine had a few decades before, presenting his faith one way to one audience and another way to another for political effect, and Christianity in the Horn now looks to have risen through the population rather than been pressed on it from the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ethiopian tradition reaches back further, to the official baptised on the road in the Acts of the Apostles, a 1st-century account that shapes how Ethiopians understand their own church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Aksumite world into which Christianity came was not blank: its religion carried Judaic elements, and how far these preceded the conversion or were read back into it is still argued about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That question runs through the <i>Kǝbrä nägäśt</i>, the book that grounds the Ethiopian monarchy in descent from Solomon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has been read as a work of the early 14th century, praising the nobility and orthodoxy of a new Solomonic elite and answering political prophecy then circulating in Syriac, rather than as a survival from the 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The church belonged to a wider eastern Christian world, and Armenian writers of the 1st millennium placed Ethiopia and the Ethiopians in their own accounts of how the nations were divided and converted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those texts drew on classical and Hellenistic notions of Ethiopia, each author bending the material to what his own composition needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","religion","africa","politics"],"sources":["Aaron Michael Butts, “Ezana of Aksum: The First Christian African King,” <i>Aethiopica</i> 28 (2025): 7–38, https://doi.org/10.15460/gfj9t143. [Open access]","Rugare Rukuni, “Negus Ezana: Revisiting the Christianisation of Aksum,” <i>Verbum et Ecclesia</i> 42, no. 1 (2021), https://doi.org/10.4102/ve.v42i1.2083. [Open access]","Rugare Rukuni, “Early Ethiopian Christianity: Retrospective enquiry from the perspective of Indigenous Christianity,” <i>HTS Teologiese Studies / Theological Studies</i> 76, no. 3 (2020), https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v76i3.5892. [Open access]","Pierluigi Piovanelli, “The Apocryphal Legitimation of a ‘Solomonic’ Dynasty in the Kǝbrä nägäśt – A Reappraisal,” <i>Aethiopica</i> 16 (2013): 7–44, https://doi.org/10.15460/aethiopica.16.1.698. [Open access]","Zaroui Pogossian, “Armeno-Aethiopica in the Middle Ages: Geography, Tales of Christianization, Calendars, and Anti-Dyophysite Polemics,” <i>Aethiopica</i> 24 (2021): 104–40, https://doi.org/10.15460/aethiopica.24.0.1627. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"The Ezana Stone at Aksum","desc":"The trilingual inscription of King Ezana at Aksum, in Greek, Ge'ez and Ge'ez written in the Sabaean alphabet, with the Greek face shown. Inscriptions like this are the evidence for reading his conversion apart from the church historians.","credit":"Photograph by Rajesh Singh, CC BY 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EzanaGreekTablet.jpg","alt":"A tall grey stone slab standing under a corrugated shelter, its face covered in closely cut lines of Greek capitals.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/EzanaGreekTablet.jpg/960px-EzanaGreekTablet.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-456","num":456,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Nearly 8,500 published inscriptions, the irrigated kingdoms of Yemen and the oracle arrows drawn before Hubal at Mecca all belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, later called the jahiliyya.","questions":["Historians studying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lean on rock graffiti and South Arabian inscriptions, because the fullest narrative accounts of it were written down under the Abbasids two centuries later.","Jewish and Christian communities, literate kingdoms in Yemen and caravan towns in the northern desert all sat within <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was far from the empty waste of later legend."],"answer":"pre-Islamic Arabia","answerText":"pre-Islamic Arabia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscriptions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Era ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">610 – 622 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pre-Islamic Arabia</b> is the name given to the peninsula and its peoples in the centuries before Muhammad began to preach, a world known from inscriptions, from poetry and from writings set down long afterwards. Its southern corner, the highlands and wadis of what is now Yemen, carried an urban civilisation that flourished from the early 1st millennium BCE until the coming of Islam, with its own languages, scripts and gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A curated online archive of that material now publishes nearly 8,500 inscriptions, most of them in the Ancient South Arabian languages, and a companion corpus of North Arabian texts holds about 3,500 more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farther north the land was mostly steppe and desert worked by herders and threaded by caravan routes, and its people scratched short prayers and names onto rock faces in related scripts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Religion varied from place to place: at Mecca an idol called Hubal stood beside a well inside the Kaʿba, oracle arrows were drawn in front of it, and animals were brought there to be killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Jewish and Christian communities in and around the peninsula are well documented too, so the age was never simply a pagan one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later Muslim writers nevertheless called the whole period the jahiliyya, the age of ignorance, and filled it with idolatry, feuding and the burying of infant daughters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the fullest accounts of that picture were composed under the Abbasids, two centuries later, historians now read it as much for what it says about early Muslim identity as for what it preserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same caution reaches Mecca itself, which is barely named in outside writing before the middle of the 8th century, so a minority of scholars doubts that it was already a trading and pilgrimage town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most still accept the traditional geography, and the surviving inscriptions and graffiti make it plain that Arabia before Islam was a connected and articulate place rather than an empty one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","religion","asia","archaeology"],"sources":["Ilkka Lindstedt, “The Portrayal of the Pre-Islamic Arabs as Murderers of Their Own Infants,” <i>Approaching Religion</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 42–55, https://doi.org/10.30664/ar.148924. [Open access]","Soner Aksoy, “The Impact of Religious Practices on Shaping Cultural Habits: The Case of Child Sacrifice among the Pre-Islāmic Arabs from the Qur’ānic Perspective,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 8 (2024): 1019, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15081019. [Open access]","Annamaria De Santis, Matteo Gallo, Irene Rossi, and Jérémie Schiettecatte, “The digital Gazetteer of Ancient Arabia: An example of reuse and exploitation of annotated textual corpora,” <i>Umanistica Digitale</i>, no. 11 (2021): 125–43, https://doi.org/10.6092/issn.2532-8816/13681. [Open access]","Manfred Sing, “Where Do the Multi-Religious Origins of Islam Lie? A Topological Approach to a Wicked Problem,” <i>Entangled Religions</i> 9 (2019): 165–210, https://doi.org/10.13154/er.v9.2019.165-210. [Open access]","Ilkka Lindstedt, “Early Islamic Inscriptions from Northeast Jordan,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): 35–66, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.136123. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Slab_with_an_inscription_about_the_political_activities_of_the_kings_of_Sheba._Ancient_South_Arabian_script_appears._From_Yemen%2C_2nd_century_CE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg/1280px-Slab_with_an_inscription_about_the_political_activities_of_the_kings_of_Sheba._Ancient_South_Arabian_script_appears._From_Yemen%2C_2nd_century_CE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg","title":"Sabaean inscription from Yemen, 2nd century CE","desc":"A limestone slab carrying a long text in the Ancient South Arabian script, recording the doings of the kings of Saba. Ancient Orient Museum, Istanbul.","alt":"A cracked limestone slab densely covered in about twenty lines of angular incised South Arabian letters.","credit":"Photograph by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slab_with_an_inscription_about_the_political_activities_of_the_kings_of_Sheba._Ancient_South_Arabian_script_appears._From_Yemen%2C_2nd_century_CE._Ancient_Orient_Museum%2C_Istanbul.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-457","num":457,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The first full biography of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was written by Ibn Isḥāq more than a century after his death, and survives mainly through Ibn Hishām's later edition.","questions":["Agreements attributed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised protection to Christian communities, and the town he ruled from 622 bound its clans and its Jewish groups into one body.","Arabic rock inscriptions of the early Islamic centuries name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and quote the Quran, evidence for his standing that owes nothing to the written biographies."],"answer":"Muhammad","answerText":"Muhammad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 570 – 632 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revelation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 610 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hijra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">622 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Muhammad</b> is the prophet of Islam, born at Mecca into the Quraysh about 570 CE and remembered as a merchant before he began to preach. Muslims hold that from about 610 he received the Quran from God, delivered through the angel Gabriel in instalments over the remaining years of his life. His preaching against the idols of his own city made him unwelcome in it, and in 622 he and his followers moved to the oasis town of Yathrib, afterwards called Medina. There he was arbiter and ruler as well as preacher, and the community he built was conceived as one body holding people of different faiths under a single agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Documents attributed to him extended protection to Christian groups, and those texts were copied and appealed to for centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He died at Medina in 632, and within a decade the armies of the community he left behind had taken Palestine, Syria, Mesopotamia and Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What is known of his life comes from the sīrah, and the earliest full work of the kind was Ibn Isḥāq's, composed more than a century later and surviving mainly through Ibn Hishām's edition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its raw material had been gathered earlier, from questions his companions put to him and from collectors such as al-Zuhrī, whose students wrote the first books of the genre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronology is less firm than it looks, because several calendars were in use in Arabia at the same time, and the dates given for his birth, his emigration and his death had to be reconciled between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Arabic inscriptions cut into rock in the deserts of Jordan name the prophet Muhammad and quote the Quran, evidence for his standing that owes nothing to the biographies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","history","religion","asia","politics"],"sources":["Suleyman Sertkaya, “A Critical and Historical Overview of the Sīrah Genre from the Classical to the Modern Period,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 3 (2022): 196, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13030196. [Open access]","Ibrahim Zein and Ahmed El-Wakil, “On the Origins of the Hijrī Calendar: A Multi-Faceted Perspective Based on the Covenants of the Prophet and Specific Date Verification,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 1 (2021): 42, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12010042. [Open access]","Ilkka Lindstedt, “Early Islamic Inscriptions from Northeast Jordan,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): 35–66, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.136123. [Open access]","Craig Considine, “Religious Pluralism and Civic Rights in a ‘Muslim Nation’: An Analysis of Prophet Muhammad’s Covenants with Christians,” <i>Religions</i> 7, no. 2 (2016): 15, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel7020015. [Open access]","Marco Demichelis, “Arab Christian Confederations and Muhammad’s Believers: On the Origins of Jihad,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 9 (2021): 710, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12090710. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Medium_shot_of_the_Green_Dome_at_The_Prophet%27s_Mosque_%28Al_Masjid-e-Nabawi%29.jpg/1280px-Medium_shot_of_the_Green_Dome_at_The_Prophet%27s_Mosque_%28Al_Masjid-e-Nabawi%29.jpg","title":"The Green Dome, Prophet's Mosque, Medina","desc":"The green dome over the chamber holding Muhammad's tomb, at the mosque in Medina raised on the site of the house he lived in after 622.","alt":"A green dome and a tall pale minaret rising above the roofline of a mosque against a clear sky.","credit":"Photograph by TheHadiRahim, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medium_shot_of_the_Green_Dome_at_The_Prophet%27s_Mosque_%28Al_Masjid-e-Nabawi%29.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-458","num":458,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The Muslim era counts its years from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the move of Muhammad and his followers from Mecca to the oasis of Yathrib in 622 CE.","questions":["After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the town Muhammad had come to held pagan, Muslim and Jewish clans at feud, and a set of agreements bound them into one community.","The calendar counting from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may have been fixed on arrival or only a generation later, and rival Arabian calendars ran on until 638 CE."],"answer":"Hijra","answerText":"Hijra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Emigration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">622 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Calendar fixed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 AH / 638 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Hijra</b> is the emigration of Muhammad and his followers from Mecca to the oasis town of Yathrib in 622 CE, the event from which Muslims count their years. It moved a harried preaching movement to a place where its leader could govern, and Yathrib, renamed Medina, became the first Muslim polity with Muhammad as its ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The town he entered was not a blank one: pagan Arab clans lived there beside Jewish and newly Muslim ones, all of them at feud, and a father might be a pagan while his son was a Muslim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A set of agreements known together as the Constitution of Medina bound these groups into a single community, and the fullest version of them survives in Ibn Isḥāq's biography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Few readers think the whole text was drafted at one sitting, since it reads as a bundle of separate treaties, one of which sets out the standing of the Jewish tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its terms grant that the Jews have their religion and the Muslims theirs, so the community it defines is a body of two peoples rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That toleration reached only the monotheists, and worshippers of the old gods appear to be shut out of the arrangement altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later documents attributed to Muhammad extended a similar protection to Christian groups, and they were copied and appealed to for centuries afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Muslim calendar takes the year of the move as its first, though whether it was fixed then or under a later caliph is argued about, and rival Arabian calendars were still running as late as 638 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Muslims have read the Medina years ever since as the model of good government, which makes the emigration a religious turning point as much as a political one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","religion","asia","politics"],"sources":["Ibrahim Zein and Ahmed El-Wakil, “On the Origins of the Hijrī Calendar: A Multi-Faceted Perspective Based on the Covenants of the Prophet and Specific Date Verification,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 1 (2021): 42, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12010042. [Open access]","Drago Đurić, “Religious tolerance in the Edict of Milan and in the Constitution of Medina,” <i>Filozofija i društvo</i> 24, no. 1 (2013): 277–92, https://doi.org/10.2298/FID1301277D. [Open access]","Craig Considine, “Religious Pluralism and Civic Rights in a ‘Muslim Nation’: An Analysis of Prophet Muhammad’s Covenants with Christians,” <i>Religions</i> 7, no. 2 (2016): 15, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel7020015. [Open access]","Ishraq Ali and Mohammad E. Abdollahi, “Divine traditions and the governance of the city-state of Medina,” <i>HTS Teologiese Studies/Theological Studies</i> 80, no. 3 (2024): a10094, https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v80i3.10094. [Open access]","Suleyman Sertkaya, “A Critical and Historical Overview of the Sīrah Genre from the Classical to the Modern Period,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 3 (2022): 196, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13030196. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Mezquita_de_Quba%2C_Medina%2C_Arabia_Saudita%2C_2025-05-22%2C_DD_16-18_HDR.jpg/1280px-Mezquita_de_Quba%2C_Medina%2C_Arabia_Saudita%2C_2025-05-22%2C_DD_16-18_HDR.jpg","title":"The Quba Mosque, Medina","desc":"The mosque at Quba on the southern edge of Medina, held by Muslim tradition to stand where Muhammad's party halted at the end of the emigration from Mecca. The present building is modern.","alt":"A white mosque with four slender minarets and low domes, floodlit against a night sky.","credit":"Photograph by Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mezquita_de_Quba%2C_Medina%2C_Arabia_Saudita%2C_2025-05-22%2C_DD_16-18_HDR.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-459","num":459,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Every chapter but the ninth of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opens with the basmalah, and the 114 of them are arranged from longest to shortest apart from the first.","questions":["Fear that reciters were dying in battle prompted the first collection of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under Abu Bakr, and copies were later sent out from Medina under ʿUthmān.","Muslims hold <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to be the word of God given to Muhammad, and treat it as the first source of belief and practice in Islam."],"answer":"Quran","answerText":"Quran","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revealed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 610 – 632 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First collected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Abu Bakr, d. 634 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Readings fixed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ibn Mujāhid, d. 936 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Quran</b> is the scripture of Islam, which Muslims hold to be the word of God revealed to Muhammad and the first source for deciding what Muslims believe and do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Classical scholarship divides it into 114 surahs, or chapters, of between 3 and 286 verses each, and arranges them from the longest to the shortest, the short opening chapter excepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every surah but the ninth begins with the basmalah, the phrase meaning ‘in the name of God, the Merciful, the Compassionate’, and a few scholars therefore counted the eighth and ninth as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word quran does not by itself mean a book: a physical copy is a mushaf, and the text was kept on scrolls and other objects as well as on paper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its language reworks the vocabulary of the poetry that came before it, lifting ordinary Arabic words out of their everyday setting and giving them a new weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The tradition dates the first gathering of the whole text to the caliphate of Abu Bakr, prompted by fear that the men who had memorised it were being killed in battle, and assigns the work to Zayd ibn Thabit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> ʿUthmān is then said to have had copies made and distributed in an orderly way, after which readings that did not match them stood outside the canon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among those was the reading transmitted from Ibn Masʿūd, who had taken seventy surahs from the Prophet's own lips and is reported to have come round to the majority view.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Muslim scholars themselves disagreed about how much of the arrangement was fixed by revelation and how much was settled by the Prophet's followers after his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern critics have questioned that whole account, and the early manuscripts found at Sanaa are among the evidence weighed against the most sceptical of their theories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["text","history","religion","asia","literature"],"sources":["Brannon Wheeler, “Quran as Scripture in Classical Muslim Scholarship,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 11 (2021): 1013, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12111013. [Open access]","Ismail Albayrak, “Revisiting the Meaning of the Divine Preservation of the Qur’an: With Special References to Verse 15:9,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 11 (2022): 1064, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13111064. [Open access]","Israa Mahmood Eıd Eıd, “Differences in the Qirāʾāt between the Mushaf of ʿAbdullāh Ibn Masʿūd and the Mushafs of ʿUthmān b. ʿAffān,” <i>İlahiyat Akademi</i>, no. 22 (2025): 441–69, https://doi.org/10.52886/ilak.1744558. [Open access]","Samed Yazar and İslam Batur, “Rhetorical Transformation in the Qurʾān and Pre-Islamic Poetry: A Comparative Analysis of Space, Animal, and Natural Figures,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 9 (2025): 1186, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16091186. [Open access]","Manfred Sing, “Where Do the Multi-Religious Origins of Islam Lie? A Topological Approach to a Wicked Problem,” <i>Entangled Religions</i> 9 (2019): 165–210, https://doi.org/10.13154/er.v9.2019.165-210. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Folios_from_a_Qur%27an_Manuscript_MET_sf26-161-1r.jpg/1280px-Folios_from_a_Qur%27an_Manuscript_MET_sf26-161-1r.jpg","title":"Two folios from a 10th-century Quran","desc":"Facing leaves of an early Quran in kufic script, the consonants in dark brown ink and the vowels marked in red, with a gilded chapter heading and arabesque leaves in the margin. Metropolitan Museum of Art.","alt":"Two parchment leaves side by side, each with about seven lines of angular brown Arabic script, red dots and a gold ornament.","credit":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Folios_from_a_Qur%27an_Manuscript_MET_sf26-161-1r.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-460","num":460,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Within ten years of Muhammad's death the armies of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held Palestine, Syria, Mesopotamia and Egypt, and the first wave ran on until 751 in the east.","questions":["Rock inscriptions cut in the Jordanian desert name the Prophet and quote the Quran, evidence of how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had reached into ordinary life within a century.","The Quran is the first source for deciding what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asks of a believer, and it is recited in daily worship and taught as the centre of learning."],"answer":"Islam","answerText":"Islam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Preaching</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 610 – 632 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Conquests</span><span class=\"dt-v\">632 – 751 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Islam</b> is the religion of submission to one God, whose followers hold Muhammad to be the last of his messengers and the Quran to be his word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book is treated as the first source for settling what Muslims believe and do, is recited in daily worship, and stands at the centre of Muslim learning and of the teaching of Arabic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The community that formed around Muhammad at Medina called itself the believers, and it grew up among Jews, Christians and worshippers of the old gods rather than apart from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within a decade of his death in 632 its armies held Palestine, Syria, Mesopotamia and Egypt, and the first phase of that expansion closed only at Poitiers in 732 and at Talas in 751.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inscriptions cut into rock by ordinary people in the Jordanian desert in the century afterwards name the Prophet and quote the Quran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Those same rocks carry Christian Arabic texts as well, a sign that religious identities in the early Islamic period were still overlapping and in flux.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The frontier the conquests crossed was not a blank one either, since the Arab Christian confederations in Byzantine service had already given warfare a sacred colouring of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How quickly Islam became a religion plainly distinct from Judaism and Christianity is among the hardest questions in the field, because the evidence for its first hundred years is thin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A revisionist school goes further, arguing that seventh-century writing from outside the tradition describes no recognisably Muslim empire and does not use the words Islam or Muslim at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most scholars reject that reading, but all of them work from the same difficulty, that the tradition's own account of its first decades was written down long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["religion","concept","history","practice","asia"],"sources":["Brannon Wheeler, “Quran as Scripture in Classical Muslim Scholarship,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 11 (2021): 1013, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12111013. [Open access]","Manfred Sing, “Where Do the Multi-Religious Origins of Islam Lie? A Topological Approach to a Wicked Problem,” <i>Entangled Religions</i> 9 (2019): 165–210, https://doi.org/10.13154/er.v9.2019.165-210. [Open access]","Marcin Grodzki, “‘Muslims’ and ‘Islam’ in Middle Eastern Literature of the Seventh and Eighth Centuries AD: An Alternative perspective of West European oriental scholarship,” <i>Studia Orientalia</i> 112 (2012): 1–16, https://journal.fi/store/article/view/9521. [Open access]","Marco Demichelis, “Arab Christian Confederations and Muhammad’s Believers: On the Origins of Jihad,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 9 (2021): 710, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12090710. [Open access]","Ilkka Lindstedt, “Early Islamic Inscriptions from Northeast Jordan,” <i>Studia Orientalia Electronica</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): 35–66, https://doi.org/10.23993/store.136123. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Exterior_of_the_Dome_of_the_Rock%2C_Jerusalem7.jpg/1280px-Exterior_of_the_Dome_of_the_Rock%2C_Jerusalem7.jpg","title":"The Dome of the Rock, Jerusalem","desc":"The shrine raised by the Umayyad caliphs over the rock on the Temple Mount, its outer wall carrying a long band of Arabic inscription under the gilded dome. The tilework is later than the building.","alt":"An octagonal building faced in blue and green tiles under a gold dome, with a band of Arabic script running along the top of the wall.","credit":"Photograph by Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exterior_of_the_Dome_of_the_Rock%2C_Jerusalem7.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-461","num":461,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Reciting that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is his messenger is the first of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the one rite by which a person enters Islam.","questions":["Prayer five times a day, a month of daylight fasting and a pilgrimage made once in a lifetime are three of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the acts every Muslim owes.","Liu Zhi explained <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Qing China in the vocabulary of Neo-Confucian self-cultivation, calling prostration the body's turning towards the Sovereign Lord."],"answer":"Five Pillars of Islam","answerText":"Five Pillars of Islam","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Five Pillars of Islam</b> are the five acts of worship a Muslim owes: the profession of faith, prayer, almsgiving, the fast of Ramadan and the pilgrimage to Mecca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first of them, the shahada, declares that there is no god but God and that Muhammad is the messenger of God, and reciting it is the one rite by which a person enters Islam.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prayer, salat, is performed five times a day at set intervals, joining spoken recitation to a fixed sequence of standing, bowing, prostration and sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almsgiving, zakat, is an obligation on wealth rather than a gift: what is handed over is owed to God and is redistributed to the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fast of Ramadan requires healthy adult Muslims to take neither food nor drink during the hours of daylight for a whole lunar month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The pilgrimage, the hajj, must be made once in a lifetime by any adult Muslim physically and financially able to make it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Quran holds the five together: it is recited in the daily prayers, and it is the first source a scholar turns to in settling what practice requires of a believer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their shape has stayed much the same across enormous distances of space and time, while the sense made of them has not. In Qing China, Liu Zhi rendered all five in the vocabulary of Neo-Confucian self-cultivation, calling prostration the body's turning towards the Sovereign Lord and the pilgrimage the return of body, mind and possessions to the Divine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hajj is still described by those who complete it as a change of life rather than a journey, and Moroccan pilgrims come home to a standing in their community that they did not have before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","religion","concept","history","asia"],"sources":["Bin You, Guangyu Su, and Timothy D. Knepper, “The Inculturation of Islamic Rituals Through Confucian-Islamic Synthesis: A Study of Liu Zhi’s <i>The Interpretation of the Five Pillars</i>,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 12 (2025): 1565, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16121565. [Open access]","Adeeb Obaid Alsuhaymi and Fouad Ahmed Atallah, “The Role of Ritual Prayer (Ṣalāh) in Self-Purification and Identity Formation: An Islamic Educational Perspective,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 11 (2025): 1347, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16111347. [Open access]","Kholoud Al-Ajarma, “After Hajj: Muslim Pilgrims Refashioning Themselves,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 1 (2021): 36, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12010036. [Open access]","Brannon Wheeler, “Quran as Scripture in Classical Muslim Scholarship,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 11 (2021): 1013, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12111013. [Open access]","Laura Jones-Ahmed, “Isolation, Community and Spirituality: British Muslim Experiences of Ramadan in Lockdown,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 1 (2022): 74, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13010074. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/The_Kaaba_during_Hajj.jpg/1280px-The_Kaaba_during_Hajj.jpg","title":"Pilgrims circling the Kaʿba during the hajj","desc":"The Kaʿba in the Masjid al-Haram at Mecca, ringed by pilgrims in the white cloth of ihram during the 2018 hajj season. The pilgrimage is the fifth of the pillars.","alt":"A black cube-shaped building draped in cloth with a gold band, surrounded by a dense crowd of people in white.","credit":"Photograph by Adli Wahid, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Kaaba_during_Hajj.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-462","num":462,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The four caliphs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled from Muhammad's death in 632 until ʿAlī's reign ended in 661, and later writers called them the rightly guided.","questions":["Tribes across Arabia revolted when Abu Bakr rather than ʿAlī was made the Prophet's heir, at the opening of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose third caliph had the Quran gathered.","Later caliphs in Spain and North Africa remembered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a golden age whose battles were to be emulated, which made its memory a political tool."],"answer":"Rashidun Caliphate","answerText":"Rashidun Caliphate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Caliphs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">632 – 661 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʿUthmān</span><span class=\"dt-v\">644 – 656 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʿAlī</span><span class=\"dt-v\">656 – 661 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Rashidun Caliphate</b> is the rule of the first four successors of Muhammad, running from his death in 632 to the end of ʿAlī's reign in 661.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rashidun means the rightly guided, and it is a name later Muslim writers gave the four rather than one those four claimed for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The succession was contested from the first day: when Abu Bakr rather than ʿAlī was made the Prophet's heir, tribes across Arabia revolted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Muslim traditions have never agreed about the four since: Sunnis accept them all, Twelver Shiʿis reject the first three, and the Zaydi schools divide between thinking those three merely mistaken and repudiating them outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the third caliph, ʿUthmān, who ruled from 644 to 656, a commission of scholars gathered the revelations into a single Quran and the rival recensions were destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That text, called the Vulgate of ʿUthmān, was accepted by most of the community and rejected by the Alides, who held it to be a falsified version of what had been revealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The armies of these caliphs took Syria and Egypt in the 630s and 640s, and seaborne expeditions reached Cyprus in ʿUthmān's own reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> ʿUthmān's death was followed by internal conflict, and after ʿAlī it was Muʿāwiya who took the headship of the state and kept it in his family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Afterwards the period hardened into a model, and caliphs as far away as Spain and North Africa presented their own wars as a return to the battles of the Prophet and the rightly guided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because so much of what is told about these thirty years was written down inside that model, the age is now read as much for the religious representation as for the record of fact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["state","history","religion","asia","politics"],"sources":["Mohammad Ali Amir-Moezzi, “The Shi’is and the Qur’an: Between Apocalypse, Civil Wars, and Empire,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 1 (2022): 1, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13010001. [Open access]","John Andrew Morrow, “The Covenants of the Prophet and the Subject of Succession,” <i>Religions</i> 10, no. 11 (2019): 593, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel10110593. [Open access]","Javier Albarrán, “The Jihād of the Caliphs and the First Battles of Islam: Memory, Legitimization and Holy War, from Cordoba to Tinmal,” <i>Al-ʿUṣūr al-Wusṭā</i> 26 (2018): 113–50, https://doi.org/10.7916/alusur.v26i1.6856. [Open access]","Barbara Zając, “The Standing Caliph Coins found in Marea/Philoxenite, Egypt,” <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilisation</i> 29 (2025): 161–74, https://doi.org/10.12797//SAAC.29.2025.29.08. [Open access]","Murat Öztürk, “Bizans İmparatorluğu ve İslâm Hilâfeti Arasında Paylaşılamayan Ada: Kıbrıs (28-353/648-965),” <i>Şarkiyat Mecmuası</i>, no. 41 (2022): 267–304, https://doi.org/10.26650/jos.1164172. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/PERF_558%2C_recto.jpg/1280px-PERF_558%2C_recto.jpg","title":"Bilingual receipt on papyrus, Egypt, 643 CE","desc":"A receipt written in Greek and Arabic together in newly conquered Egypt and dated April 643, in the reign of the second caliph. Austrian National Library, Vienna.","alt":"A torn sheet of brown papyrus with lines of cursive Greek above and lines of early Arabic below.","credit":"Austrian National Library, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PERF_558%2C_recto.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-463","num":463,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Syria, Egypt and Mesopotamia fell to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within a decade of Muhammad's death, and the first wave ran on to Poitiers in 732 and Talas in 751.","questions":["One reading makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a migration of peoples from beyond the Roman frontier; another points to the Arabic slogans and titles on the conquerors' own earliest documents.","The Ifriqiyan coast was taken only with difficulty during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and became a frontier of watchful ascetics facing the Byzantine enemy across the sea."],"answer":"early Muslim conquests","answerText":"early Muslim conquests","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Syria, Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 – 649 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Poitiers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">732 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Talas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">751 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>early Muslim conquests</b> are the campaigns that carried the community Muhammad had founded out of Arabia and across most of the Near East within a single lifetime. Syria and Egypt were taken in the 630s and 640s, so that within a decade of the Prophet’s death in 632 the whole eastern Mediterranean coast, along with Mesopotamia, had changed hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seaborne expeditions reached Cyprus in the reign of the third caliph, and the island ended under an administration shared with the Byzantines that lasted for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The advance did not stop at the Levant: the first phase of it closed at Poitiers in the west in 732 and at Talas in the east in 751.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ifriqiya, the eastern Maghreb, was conquered only with difficulty under the Umayyads, and its coast then filled with ribat settlements where devout men watched for the Byzantine enemy and prayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What drove the expansion is the most argued-over question in the field: one reading makes it the movement of a peripheral people from beyond the Roman frontier, much like the barbarian migrations in Europe, and offers no religious explanation at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against that stand the conquerors’ own earliest documents, which carry Arabic religious slogans and give their leader the title <i>amīr al-muʾminīn</i>, ‘commander of the believers’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both sides prefer 7th- and 8th-century evidence to the Arabic narratives written from the 9th century onwards, which read the whole expansion as the work of the new religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those later narratives turned the campaigns into an exemplary golden age, and caliphs from Córdoba to the Atlas afterwards justified their own wars by pointing back to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On the ground the change came more slowly: Byzantine coin went on circulating at Egyptian sites such as Marea until the monetary reform of 696 put a caliphal coinage in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","asia","politics"],"sources":["Fred M. Donner, “Robert Hoyland, <i>In God’s Path: The Arab Conquests and the Creation of an Islamic Empire</i>,” <i>Al-ʿUṣūr al-Wusṭā</i> 23 (2015): 134–40, https://doi.org/10.7916/alusur.v23i1.7042. [Open access]","Marco Demichelis, “Arab Christian Confederations and Muhammad’s Believers: On the Origins of Jihad,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 9 (2021): 710, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12090710. [Open access]","Murat Öztürk, “Bizans İmparatorluğu ve İslâm Hilâfeti Arasında Paylaşılamayan Ada: Kıbrıs (28-353/648-965),” <i>Şarkiyat Mecmuası</i>, no. 41 (2022): 267–304, https://doi.org/10.26650/jos.1164172. [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Van Staëvel, “Ribāṭ in Early Islamic Ifrîqiya: Another Islam from the Edge,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 8 (2023): 1051, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14081051. [Open access]","Barbara Zając, “The Standing Caliph Coins found in Marea/Philoxenite, Egypt,” <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilisation</i> 29 (2025): 161–74, https://doi.org/10.12797//SAAC.29.2025.29.08. [Open access]","Javier Albarrán, “The Jihād of the Caliphs and the First Battles of Islam: Memory, Legitimization and Holy War, from Cordoba to Tinmal,” <i>Al-ʿUṣūr al-Wusṭā</i> 26 (2018): 113–50, https://doi.org/10.7916/alusur.v26i1.6856. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Medina_of_Sousse%2C_Sousse_Governorate%2C_Tunisia%2C_January_2024_-_Ribat_Fortress.jpg/1280px-Medina_of_Sousse%2C_Sousse_Governorate%2C_Tunisia%2C_January_2024_-_Ribat_Fortress.jpg","title":"The ribat of Sousse, Tunisia","desc":"A ribat on the Ifriqiyan coast, one of the fortified retreats where devout men kept watch for the Byzantine enemy across the sea and performed their devotions.","alt":"A low square stone fort with round corner towers and a battlemented parapet under a dark sky.","credit":"Photograph by Sharon Hahn Darlin, CC BY 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medina_of_Sousse%2C_Sousse_Governorate%2C_Tunisia%2C_January_2024_-_Ribat_Fortress.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-464","num":464,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The killing of Husayn and 72 companions at Karbala in 680 hardened <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Shia Muslims have mourned it on the tenth of Muharram ever since.","questions":["At the root of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a question about succession: whether Muhammad appointed ʿAlī outright, or whether the choice rested with the elders of the community.","A compiler working under the Abbasids cut ʿAlī's harshest words about ʿĀisha from his collection, because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was turning violent in his own century."],"answer":"Sunni–Shia split","answerText":"Sunni–Shia split","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Succession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">632 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʿAlī</span><span class=\"dt-v\">656 – 661 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Karbala</span><span class=\"dt-v\">680 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sunni–Shia split</b> is the oldest and deepest division in Islam, and it begins in a disagreement about who should have led the community after Muhammad died in 632. Twelver Shiʿi scholars hold that the Prophet appointed ʿAlī, his cousin and son-in-law, as his spiritual and political successor outright, and on that ground reject the first three caliphs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sunni tradition holds instead that the decision rested with the elders of the community, and some Sunnis argue that Muhammad implicitly appointed Abu Bakr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The positions between are more various than the two names suggest: one Zaydi school held the first three caliphs merely mistaken rather than sinful, another repudiated them, and Sufis, keeping clear of the quarrel, recognised Abu Bakr as political successor while calling ʿAlī both political and spiritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> ʿAlī did rule at last, from 656 to 661, but the state passed after him to Muʿāwiya and stayed in that family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In 680 ʿAlī’s son Husayn, the Prophet’s grandson, was put to death with 72 of his male companions at Karbala on the tenth day of the month of Muharram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That day became Ashura, its mourning kept secretly under the Umayyads and grown since into the central ritual of Shiʿi devotion, with Husayn’s death as the pattern of sacrifice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The quarrel worked backwards into the texts as well: the Alides came to hold the caliphal Quran a falsified version of the revelation whose true sense had to be drawn out by interpretation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It could also turn violent, and Sunnis and Shiʿis clashed in the 3rd and 4th centuries of the Islamic era over such matters as the conduct of ʿĀisha, the Prophet’s widow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Shiʿi compiler serving as a judge under the Abbasids cut ʿAlī’s harshest sayings about her out of his collection, apparently to keep the peace between readers of both kinds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","religion","asia","politics"],"sources":["John Andrew Morrow, “The Covenants of the Prophet and the Subject of Succession,” <i>Religions</i> 10, no. 11 (2019): 593, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel10110593. [Open access]","Mohammad Ali Amir-Moezzi, “The Shi’is and the Qur’an: Between Apocalypse, Civil Wars, and Empire,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 1 (2022): 1, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13010001. [Open access]","Tareq Ayoub, “Heroism and Being-towards-Death: On Sacrificial Martyrdom in Contemporary Shiʿism,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 8 (2023): 971, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14080971. [Open access]","Murat Öztürk, “Bizans İmparatorluğu ve İslâm Hilâfeti Arasında Paylaşılamayan Ada: Kıbrıs (28-353/648-965),” <i>Şarkiyat Mecmuası</i>, no. 41 (2022): 267–304, https://doi.org/10.26650/jos.1164172. [Open access]","Mahboubeh Khazaei, Yahya Mirhoseini, Kamal Sahraei, and AliMohammad Mirjalili, “Conscious Selection in Ḥadith Compilation to Mitigate Sectarian Divisions: A Case Study of Narratives Concerning ʿĀisha in <i>Nahj al-Balāghah</i>,” <i>Religions</i> 17, no. 2 (2026): 193, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel17020193. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Kerbela_Hussein_Moschee.jpg/1280px-Kerbela_Hussein_Moschee.jpg","title":"The shrine of Husayn at Karbala","desc":"Pilgrims filling the streets around the shrine of Husayn in Karbala, Iraq, at the end of the forty days of mourning that follow Ashura.","alt":"A gold-domed shrine in a walled precinct at the centre of a dense city, with vast crowds filling the surrounding streets.","credit":"Photograph by Larry E. Johns, US Army, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerbela_Hussein_Moschee.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-465","num":465,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Muʿāwiya founded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 661 and ruled it from Damascus, and it held the Muslim world for the next ninety years.","questions":["In 696 ʿAbd al-Malik reformed the coinage of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its money began to carry the profession of faith around the rim.","The painted bathhouse at Qusayr Amra was raised for a caliph of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 743, and its frescoes adapt older imagery of the region to new ideas of beauty and rank."],"answer":"Umayyad Caliphate","answerText":"Umayyad Caliphate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Caliphate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">661 – 750 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʿAbd al-Malik</span><span class=\"dt-v\">685 – 705 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Coin reform</span><span class=\"dt-v\">696 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Umayyad Caliphate</b> was the first hereditary dynasty to rule the Muslim world, holding it from 661 to 750, and it governed from Syria rather than from Arabia or Iraq.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was founded by Muʿāwiya, who took the state after ʿAlī and passed it to his son Yazīd I, and its rulers bound themselves to the Arab tribes of Syria by marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under it the conquests went on: Ifriqiya in the eastern Maghreb was taken only with difficulty and became a frontier province, and Cyprus was governed jointly with the Byzantine emperor under a treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> ʿAbd al-Malik, who ruled from 685 to 705, was the caliph who made the empire look like one, and much of what he did was done in metal and in stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After his rival Ibn al-Zubayr was killed in 692 he set about consolidating his hold, and in 696 he reformed the coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The coins struck just before that reform show him standing with a sword and carry the shahada around the rim, so that the state’s money now declared its religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the public face, the dynasty’s desert palaces in the Syrian steppe were painted with figures and scenes drawn from older traditions of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bathhouse at Qusayr Amra in the Jordanian steppe, whose inscription names al-Walīd ibn Yazīd and dates the building to 743 or 744, is covered in frescoes whose female figures adapt pre-Islamic imagery to Umayyad ideas of beauty and rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What those paintings were for is argued over, and the old explanation from the patron's own reputation for pleasure no longer holds the field on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dynasty fell in 750, when the caliphate passed to the Abbasids, and with it the centre of the Muslim world moved from Syria towards Iraq.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","politics","asia","religion"],"sources":["Barbara Zając, “The Standing Caliph Coins found in Marea/Philoxenite, Egypt,” <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilisation</i> 29 (2025): 161–74, https://doi.org/10.12797//SAAC.29.2025.29.08. [Open access]","Murat Öztürk, “Bizans İmparatorluğu ve İslâm Hilâfeti Arasında Paylaşılamayan Ada: Kıbrıs (28-353/648-965),” <i>Şarkiyat Mecmuası</i>, no. 41 (2022): 267–304, https://doi.org/10.26650/jos.1164172. [Open access]","Inbal Kol, “Revisiting Qusayr Amra: A New Perspective on Female Imagery,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 9 (2025): 1168, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16091168. [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Van Staëvel, “Ribāṭ in Early Islamic Ifrîqiya: Another Islam from the Edge,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 8 (2023): 1051, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14081051. [Open access]","Marco Demichelis, “Arab Christian Confederations and Muhammad’s Believers: On the Origins of Jihad,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 9 (2021): 710, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12090710. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/First_Umayyad_gold_dinar%2C_Caliph_Abd_al-Malik%2C_695_CE.jpg/1280px-First_Umayyad_gold_dinar%2C_Caliph_Abd_al-Malik%2C_695_CE.jpg","title":"Standing Caliph dinar of ʿAbd al-Malik","desc":"A gold dinar struck for ʿAbd al-Malik in the mid-690s, showing the caliph standing with a sword and carrying an Arabic legend round the rim. British Museum.","alt":"A worn gold coin showing a standing bearded figure in a long robe, ringed by an Arabic inscription.","credit":"Photographed at the British Museum by PHGCOM, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Umayyad_gold_dinar%2C_Caliph_Abd_al-Malik%2C_695_CE.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-466","num":466,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Al-Walīd I built the Great Mosque of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 705 and 715 on the site of a Byzantine basilica, paying for it out of seven years of Syrian land tax.","questions":["For ninety years the caliphs ruled the Muslim world from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its mint struck the coins that showed the caliph standing with a sword.","When the Abbasids took the caliphate in 750 they left <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a new capital in Iraq, which turned the state away from the Mediterranean."],"answer":"Damascus","answerText":"Damascus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Umayyad capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">661 – 750 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Mosque</span><span class=\"dt-v\">705 – 715 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Damascus</b> is the chief city of Syria, and for the ninety years the Umayyad caliphs held the Muslim world it was the seat from which they ruled it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its mint struck the empire’s money, including the copper pieces that showed the caliph standing with a sword before the reform of 696 replaced his figure with script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its Great Mosque was built by the caliph al-Walīd I between 705 and 715 on the site of the Byzantine basilica of St John the Baptist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One report says he paid for the building and its decoration out of the revenue of seven years of the land tax of Syria, and it became the most magnificent point of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its walls, colonnades and courtyard galleries were faced inside and out with mosaic — buildings, towns, and trees heavy with ripe fruit — probably meant for the vision of Paradise the Quran describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One panel on the north colonnade carries a river with a ship on it, a subject common in the late antique Mediterranean and not looked for in an early Islamic mosque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The glazed mosaic technique behind all of it was a Roman Mediterranean one, and the domes over the mosque’s most important spaces follow the same convention as the Dome of the Rock and al-Aqsa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fires and earthquakes damaged the building again and again, and the Arabic historians, geographers and travellers who came through the city recorded the restorations that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The caliphs also built out into the Syrian steppe, leaving painted palaces and bathhouses whose imagery drew on older traditions of the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the Abbasids took the caliphate in 750 they governed from Iraq instead, and moving the capital east turned the state away from the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["city","history","geography","asia","religion"],"sources":["Zaraza Friedman, “The Ship Depicted on the North Colonnade of the Great Mosque at Damascus: A Nilotic Theme or the Representation of Paradise?,” <i>Journal of Mosaic Research</i> 8 (2015): 17–31, https://dergipark.org.tr/tr/download/article-file/294116. [Open access]","Murat Öztürk, “Bizans İmparatorluğu ve İslâm Hilâfeti Arasında Paylaşılamayan Ada: Kıbrıs (28-353/648-965),” <i>Şarkiyat Mecmuası</i>, no. 41 (2022): 267–304, https://doi.org/10.26650/jos.1164172. [Open access]","Barbara Zając, “The Standing Caliph Coins found in Marea/Philoxenite, Egypt,” <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilisation</i> 29 (2025): 161–74, https://doi.org/10.12797//SAAC.29.2025.29.08. [Open access]","Inbal Kol, “Revisiting Qusayr Amra: A New Perspective on Female Imagery,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 9 (2025): 1168, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16091168. [Open access]","Belén Cuenca-Abellán, “The Experimentation of the Sacred in al-Ḥakam II’s Maqṣūra: An Architecture Based on Emotions,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 2 (2024): 242, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15020242. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Umayyad_Mosque%2C_Damascus_%28%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%B4%D9%82%29%2C_Syria_-_Detail_of_mosaics_in_west_portico_of_courtyard_-_PHBZ024_2016_1371_-_Dumbarton_Oaks.jpg/1280px-Umayyad_Mosque%2C_Damascus_%28%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%B4%D9%82%29%2C_Syria_-_Detail_of_mosaics_in_west_portico_of_courtyard_-_PHBZ024_2016_1371_-_Dumbarton_Oaks.jpg","title":"Mosaic in the courtyard of the Umayyad Mosque","desc":"Part of the mosaic along the west portico of the Great Mosque of Damascus, showing pavilions and houses among tall trees along a river.","alt":"A long mosaic panel of gold ground with green trees, colonnaded buildings and a river running below them.","credit":"Photograph by Frank Kidner for Dumbarton Oaks, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Mosque%2C_Damascus_%28%D8%AF%D9%85%D8%B4%D9%82%29%2C_Syria_-_Detail_of_mosaics_in_west_portico_of_courtyard_-_PHBZ024_2016_1371_-_Dumbarton_Oaks.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-467","num":467,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Charles Martel met the wali ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ghāfiqī at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in about 732, one of many clashes between Frankish rulers and the governors of al-Andalus.","questions":["The place, the date and the course of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are all uncertain, and the town the French name it after is not where the fighting happened.","Latin chroniclers treated the army defeated at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as one more divine scourge, and only the Mozarabic Chronicle set Arabs against Europeans."],"answer":"Battle of Tours","answerText":"Battle of Tours","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 732 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raids end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Tours</b>, called the battle of Poitiers in French, was fought about 732 between the Frankish mayor of the palace Charles Martel and an army under ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ghāfiqī, the governor of al-Andalus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Muslim forces had taken Ifriqiya and the Maghreb between 645 and 709 and then crossed into Spain, where the Visigothic kingdom of Toledo collapsed and its elites came over quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their raids into southern Gaul met a Frankish aristocracy already divided against itself, and Duke Eudes of Aquitaine is now reckoned the chief loser of 732 whoever won the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charles won it, and the victory counts as one of the first steps in the Carolingian recovery of southern Gaul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It did not end the raids, nor the quarrel with the governors of al-Andalus, which ran on until a new balance settled early in the 9th century between the Carolingian empire and a deeply divided Muslim world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Umayyad advance in the west is all the same conventionally reckoned to have stopped here, and 732 is given as the closing date of the first phase of the conquests on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the documents say is thinner than the battle’s fame suggests: the place, the date and the course of the fighting are all uncertain, and the town of Poitiers is not where it was fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost every Latin chronicler took the Saracen army for a divine scourge like the other peoples they called pagan, and only the Mozarabic Chronicle set Arabs against Europeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charles Martel himself was remembered badly for centuries afterwards, condemned from Carolingian times for seizing church property and placed in hell by a bishop’s vision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its present fame is recent: a widely read book of 1996 ranged it beside the Crusades and the sieges of Vienna as a clash of civilisations, and French identitarian movements have since made the name their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","europe","politics"],"sources":["Adrien Bayard, “William Blanc et Christophe Naudin, <i>Charles Martel et la bataille de Poitiers : de l’histoire au mythe identitaire</i>,” <i>Médiévales</i> 71 (2016): 196–98, https://doi.org/10.4000/medievales.7966. [Open access]","Marco Demichelis, “Arab Christian Confederations and Muhammad’s Believers: On the Origins of Jihad,” <i>Religions</i> 12, no. 9 (2021): 710, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel12090710. [Open access]","Fred Hailon, “De quoi Poitiers est-il le discours ? 732, Charles Martel : hypertrophie d’un stéréotype extrémisant,” <i>DIversité REcherches et terrains</i>, no. 9 (2017), https://doi.org/10.25965/dire.852. [Open access]","Jean-Pierre Van Staëvel, “Ribāṭ in Early Islamic Ifrîqiya: Another Islam from the Edge,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 8 (2023): 1051, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14081051. [Open access]","Murat Öztürk, “Bizans İmparatorluğu ve İslâm Hilâfeti Arasında Paylaşılamayan Ada: Kıbrıs (28-353/648-965),” <i>Şarkiyat Mecmuası</i>, no. 41 (2022): 267–304, https://doi.org/10.26650/jos.1164172. [Open access]","Nicolas Cutaia, “Mouvements islamophobes et Charles Martel : quelle réappropriation ? Cas d’étude sur le Bloc identitaire et l’affaire de la mosquée de Poitiers,” <i>Cahiers Mémoire et Politique</i> (2013): 11–27, https://doi.org/10.25518/2295-0311.69. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Steuben_-_Bataille_de_Poitiers.png/1280px-Steuben_-_Bataille_de_Poitiers.png","title":"Charles de Steuben, the Battle of Poitiers, 1837","desc":"A painting made eleven centuries after the event, for the historical galleries at Versailles. It is a picture of how the 19th century wanted the battle remembered rather than a record of it.","alt":"A crowded battle painting: an armoured man on a white horse swings an axe beside a stone cross, over fallen figures in red and white.","credit":"Charles de Steuben, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Steuben_-_Bataille_de_Poitiers.png"}},
{"id":"wh-468","num":468,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The Iberian Peninsula was full of churches when Islam arrived in 711, and what the newcomers made there was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a frontier for eight centuries.","questions":["ʿAbd al-Raḥmān III declared himself caliph in 929, after which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the leading centre of art and learning in the western Mediterranean.","Builders in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set Roman and Visigothic columns into their new mosques, which was both cheap and a claim on the older past of the land."],"answer":"al-Andalus","answerText":"al-Andalus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Islam arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">711 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Umayyad emirs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 756 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caliphate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 929 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Al-Andalus</b> is the name for the part of the Iberian Peninsula under Muslim rule, which began with the arrival of Islam in 711 and made the country a frontier of the Islamic world for eight centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The peninsula the newcomers entered was peppered with Christian churches, shrines and monasteries, in the countryside as much as in the towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An Umayyad refugee, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān I, established himself there and ruled from 756 to 788, having fought his way in against the governor Yūsuf al-Fihrī.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His great mosque at Cordoba set Roman and Visigothic columns and capitals into its arcades from the founding phase onwards, and his successors kept the practice while adding new work from their own workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That reuse was more than thrift: it made a claim on the Roman and Visigothic past of the land, and so on the rightfulness of its Islamisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In 929 ʿAbd al-Raḥmān III declared himself caliph, and al-Andalus became the leading centre of art and learning in the western Mediterranean, in open competition with the Abbasids, the Fatimids and Byzantium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His son al-Ḥakam II enlarged the congregational mosque of the capital, taking the opinion of the city’s jurists and astronomers about the direction of prayer before he began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dynasty kept a relic of its origins, a copy of the Quran said to have been carried from Damascus by the first emir, and showed it in the caliph’s own enclosure at the mosque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The caliphate did not hold: rival claimants took the title, and one of them, ʿAlī ibn Ḥammūd, ruled as a Shiʿi caliph from 1016 to 1018 and struck coins recognised as far off as Oran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Underneath the politics the buildings kept changing hands, late antique churches becoming early Islamic mosques and other churches being rebuilt by Christians within al-Andalus itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","geography","europe","religion"],"sources":["Susana Calvo Capilla, “The Visual Construction of the Umayyad Caliphate in Al-Andalus through the Great Mosque of Cordoba,” <i>Arts</i> 7, no. 3 (2018): 36, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts7030036. [Open access]","Belén Cuenca-Abellán, “The Experimentation of the Sacred in al-Ḥakam II’s Maqṣūra: An Architecture Based on Emotions,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 2 (2024): 242, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15020242. [Open access]","Javier Albarrán, “The Jihād of the Caliphs and the First Battles of Islam: Memory, Legitimization and Holy War, from Cordoba to Tinmal,” <i>Al-ʿUṣūr al-Wusṭā</i> 26 (2018): 113–50, https://doi.org/10.7916/alusur.v26i1.6856. [Open access]","Almudena Ariza Armada, “The Ḥammūdid Caliphate: A New Look through the Lens of Numismatics,” <i>Al-ʿUṣūr al-Wusṭā</i> 26 (2018): 169–200, https://doi.org/10.7916/alusur.v26i1.6858. [Open access]","María de los Ángeles Utrero Agudo, “Stratigraphy Matters: Questioning the (Re)Sacralisation of Religious Spaces from Late Antiquity to the Early Middle Ages in the Iberian Peninsula,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 9 (2023): 1199, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14091199. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Madinat_al-Zahra_Salon_Rico_exterior_arcade_IMG_1062.jpg/1280px-Madinat_al-Zahra_Salon_Rico_exterior_arcade_IMG_1062.jpg","title":"Arcade of the Salón Rico, Madinat al-Zahra","desc":"Horseshoe arches with carved voussoirs on the reception hall of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān III's palace city outside Cordoba, photographed during restoration.","alt":"A row of horseshoe arches with red and white voussoirs and deeply carved stone panels above the springing.","credit":"Photograph by R Prazeres, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madinat_al-Zahra_Salon_Rico_exterior_arcade_IMG_1062.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-469","num":469,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The caliphate passed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 750, and it held the title for five centuries until 1258, ruling from Iraq rather than from Syria.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the translation of Greek, Syriac and Persian learning into Arabic reached its height, and medicine, astronomy and mathematics flourished with it.","Al-Manṣūr laid out a round city inside three rings of wall for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its outer wall looks to have been symbolic rather than defensive."],"answer":"Abbasid Caliphate","answerText":"Abbasid Caliphate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Caliphate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">750 – 1258 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">al-Maʾmūn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">d. 833 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">al-Mutawakkil</span><span class=\"dt-v\">847 – 861 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Abbasid Caliphate</b> took the caliphate from the Umayyads in 750 and held the title, at least in name, until 1258.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its rulers governed from Iraq rather than Syria, and moving the capital east turned the state away from the Mediterranean and shaped its armies into ground forces rather than fleets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second caliph, al-Manṣūr, built a new capital there in the 8th century and named it Madīnat al-Salām, 'the city of peace'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was laid out as a circle inside three rings of wall, and the outer ring looks to have been symbolic rather than defensive, since the inner one was higher, stronger and crenelated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plan was not a soldiers’ camp enlarged: it set out its founder’s political order in space and made a claim about paradise besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Abbasids the translation of Greek, Syriac, Persian and Indian learning into Arabic reached its height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That work had begun under the Umayyads, much of it done by Syriac scholars outside the court who had already put Aristotle’s logic into their own language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under al-Maʾmūn, who died in 833, translations multiplied and mathematics, medicine, astronomy and astrology flourished with them, and the effort went on under al-Mutawakkil from 847 to 861.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cities of the empire stayed mixed: the head of a Jewish academy in Baghdad sent a pupil to ask the Nestorian patriarch what a word in the Psalms meant in Syriac.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was also under the Abbasids that most of what is told about the first Muslim century was written down, which is why those accounts are read for the empire that produced them as well as for the events they report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","politics","asia","science"],"sources":["Murat Öztürk, “Bizans İmparatorluğu ve İslâm Hilâfeti Arasında Paylaşılamayan Ada: Kıbrıs (28-353/648-965),” <i>Şarkiyat Mecmuası</i>, no. 41 (2022): 267–304, https://doi.org/10.26650/jos.1164172. [Open access]","Samet Şenel, “Science Expanding Amid Political Challenges: Translation Activities During the al-Mutawakkil ‘Alā’llāh Period (232–247 H/847–861 CE),” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 430, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16040430. [Open access]","Kianoosh Rezania, “Eschatologia Iranica I: From Zoroastrian Cosmos to Abbasid Madīnat al-Salām: A Journey through Utopia and Heterotopia,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 10 (2024): 1170, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15101170. [Open access]","Mohammad Ali Amir-Moezzi, “The Shi’is and the Qur’an: Between Apocalypse, Civil Wars, and Empire,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 1 (2022): 1, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13010001. [Open access]","Yosaif M. Dubovick, “‘Oil, which shall not quit my head’: Jewish-Christian Interaction in Eleventh-century Baghdad,” <i>Entangled Religions</i> 6 (2018): 95–123, https://doi.org/10.13154/er.v6.2018.95-123. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Malwiya_03.jpg/1280px-Malwiya_03.jpg","title":"The Malwiya minaret at Samarra","desc":"The spiral minaret of the Great Mosque of Samarra, raised under the caliph al-Mutawakkil in the 9th century CE, when the Abbasid court had moved upriver from Baghdad. It stands about 52 metres (170 feet) high, climbed by a ramp winding anticlockwise round the outside.","alt":"A tall sand-coloured brick tower rising in a broad spiral, wider at the base and narrowing to a small pillared kiosk at the top, standing on a square platform under a hazy sky.","credit":"Photograph by Ekhlas Sami, 2010. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malwiya_03.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-470","num":470,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Founded on the Tigris in 762 as Madīnat al-Salām, 'the city of peace', <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew from a walled circle into the Abbasid capital and is today the capital of Iraq.","questions":["A caliph chose a hamlet among Nestorian monasteries for its cool nights and its freedom from mosquitoes, and the city he founded there, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, spread over both banks of the Tigris.","Sacked by the Mongols in 1258 and stripped of its caliph, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nonetheless remained the chief city of the Tigris and Euphrates lands and is the capital of Iraq today."],"answer":"Baghdad","answerText":"Baghdad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">762 CE, by al-Manṣūr</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abbasid capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">762 – 1258 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Population</span><span class=\"dt-v\">About 7 million, 2025</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["city","history","geography","asia","politics","iraq"],"abstract":"<b>Baghdad</b> is the capital of Iraq, a city of about seven million people standing on the Tigris in the middle of the Mesopotamian plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was founded in 762 by the second Abbasid caliph, al-Manṣūr, after years of searching for a site for a new dynasty's capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He settled on a hamlet on the western bank just above the mouth of the Sarat canal, where Nestorian monasteries stood and the monks told him the district was free of mosquitoes and cool at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The official name he gave it was Madīnat al-Salām, 'the city of peace', and that is the name struck on Abbasid coins, though ordinary speech kept the older Baghdad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he built was a walled burgh exactly circular in plan with four gates, and it was only the nucleus: the metropolis grew out from it over both banks. <br><br> His garden palace outside the walls was called al-Khuld, 'eternity', after the paradise of the Quran, one of several Abbasid buildings whose names made a claim about the world to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For 75 years the court sat in West Baghdad; the caliphs then left for Samarra upriver for 58 years, and on their return East Baghdad held the government for close on four centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city drew the empire's learning towards it: al-Manṣūr called the head physician of the Persian school at Jundishapur to treat him in 765, and the first Muslim medical school was founded here under Hārūn al-Rashīd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its scholarship was not confined to one community: about the year 1000 the rabbinic academy of Pumbedita and the seat of the Nestorian patriarch both stood in the city, and gatherings for interfaith debate were common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hulagu's Mongols besieged it in 1258 and put the last caliph to death, but the destruction did not unseat it, and it has been the chief city of the Tigris and Euphrates country ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Guy Le Strange, <i>Baghdad during the Abbasid Caliphate: From Contemporary Arabic and Persian Sources</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1900), 9–15, 168–69, 340–56, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_rdcoAAAAYAAJ. [Open access]","Kianoosh Rezania, “Eschatologia Iranica I: From Zoroastrian Cosmos to Abbasid Madīnat al-Salām: A Journey through Utopia and Heterotopia,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 10 (2024): 1170, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15101170. [Open access]","Mehmet Mahfuz Söylemez, “The Jundishapur School,” <i>American Journal of Islam and Society</i> 22, no. 2 (2005): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.35632/ajis.v22i2.455. [Open access]","Yosaif M. Dubovick, “‘Oil, which shall not quit my head’: Jewish-Christian Interaction in Eleventh-century Baghdad,” <i>Entangled Religions</i> 6 (2018): 95–123, https://doi.org/10.13154/er.v6.2018.95-123. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Iraq,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/iq.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%B1%D8%B3%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%86%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D8%A8%D8%BA%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AF_%283%29_cropped_edited.jpg/1280px-%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%B1%D8%B3%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%86%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D8%A8%D8%BA%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AF_%283%29_cropped_edited.jpg","title":"The Mustansiriya Madrasa, Baghdad","desc":"The courtyard of the Mustansiriya, a college founded in Baghdad by the caliph al-Mustansir in the 1230s and one of the few Abbasid buildings in the city still standing.","alt":"A rectangular courtyard of pale brick faced with tiers of pointed arches, a tall arched recess at the far end, a date palm and a tiled minaret behind, all reflected in a still pool in the foreground.","credit":"Photograph by Taisir Mahdi, 2014. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%B1%D8%B3%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%86%D8%B5%D8%B1%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D9%81%D9%8A_%D8%A8%D8%BA%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AF_%283%29_cropped_edited.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-471","num":471,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Baghdad's caliphs kept a library where Greek and Sanskrit works were turned into Arabic, and later writers made that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a research academy the sources never describe.","questions":["Manuscripts bought, conquered or exchanged were stored in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Baghdad and rendered into Arabic, sometimes straight from the Greek and sometimes through a Syriac version first.","Accounts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disagree about who founded it — a Barmakid vizier, a caliph, or physicians from Persia — and after 833 it drops out of the record entirely."],"answer":"House of Wisdom","answerText":"House of Wisdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Hārūn al-Rashīd, 786 – 809 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">al-Maʾmūn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">813 – 833 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last noticed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 833 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","science","asia","technology","iraq"],"abstract":"The <b>House of Wisdom</b>, in Arabic Bayt al-Ḥikma, was a library at the Abbasid court in Baghdad where manuscripts were collected and translated, and later retelling has made it a research academy that no early source describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Accounts of its founding disagree: one gives it to Yaḥyā ibn Khālid the Barmakid, vizier to Hārūn al-Rashīd, another has physicians from the Persian school at Jundishapur set it up near the Baghdad hospital on the model of their own house of learning, and a third has the caliph al-Maʾmūn install astronomers in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What is claimed for its equipment is modest: a well-stocked library, an observatory, and somewhere for translators to work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The work itself is not in doubt, since manuscripts were bought, conquered or exchanged and then turned into Arabic either straight from the Greek or through a Syriac version first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sanskrit texts were paid for as well as Greek astronomy and mathematics, so the reading was never confined to one tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the Barmakid family fell the place passed into the caliph's own hands, and after al-Maʾmūn's death in 833 it disappears from the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was never the whole of the translation movement, which had begun under the Umayyads and was largely carried by Syriac Christian scholars working away from any court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jundishapur shows the pattern: its teachers went on moving to Baghdad after the city was founded, and the Persian school declined and vanished as they left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The best known of the translators, Ḥunayn ibn Isḥāq, was a Christian from Hira whose master threw him out for asking too many questions, and who vanished for years to learn Greek before an old acquaintance recognised him in the street reciting Homer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The colleges Baghdad is remembered for are later and far better documented: the Niẓāmiyya went up on the east bank in the 11th century and al-Mustanṣir's in the 13th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Samet Şenel, “Science Expanding Amid Political Challenges: Translation Activities During the al-Mutawakkil ‘Alā’llāh Period (232–247 H/847–861 CE),” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 430, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16040430. [Open access]","Edward G. Browne, <i>Arabian Medicine: Being the FitzPatrick Lectures Delivered at the College of Physicians in November 1919 and November 1920</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1921), 5–6, 24–25, https://archive.org/details/arabianmedicineb00browiala. [Open access]","Mehmet Mahfuz Söylemez, “The Jundishapur School,” <i>American Journal of Islam and Society</i> 22, no. 2 (2005): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.35632/ajis.v22i2.455. [Open access]","Reynold A. Nicholson, <i>A Literary History of the Arabs</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 359, https://archive.org/details/literaryhistoryo00nichuoft. [Open access]","Guy Le Strange, <i>Baghdad during the Abbasid Caliphate: From Contemporary Arabic and Persian Sources</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1900), 88, 263–78, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_rdcoAAAAYAAJ. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2c/Maqamat_hariri.jpg","title":"A library, painted at Baghdad in 1237","desc":"Scholars talking under a wall of shelved books, from a manuscript of al-Ḥarīrī's Maqāmāt illustrated by Yaḥyā ibn Maḥmūd al-Wāsiṭī. It is a scene from a work of fiction rather than a picture of the House of Wisdom, and it is painted four centuries after the caliphs it is usually printed beside.","alt":"A medieval painting of nine bearded men in turbans and coloured robes seated in a row, gesturing in conversation, beneath four tiers of red-framed niches packed with stacks of books.","credit":"Bibliothèque nationale de France, MS Arabe 5847. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maqamat_hariri.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-472","num":472,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Historians call the centuries in which Greek and Indian learning was translated into Arabic and carried from Baghdad to Córdoba the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a label coined long afterwards.","questions":["The hospital, the observatory and the algebra textbook all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose usual close is the Mongol destruction of Baghdad in 1258.","A century ago a standard account gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a hundred years, from 750 to 850, where popular usage now stretches it across five centuries and several caliphates."],"answer":"Islamic Golden Age","answerText":"Islamic Golden Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Usually dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 1258 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fihrist compiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">987 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Baghdad sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1258 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","science","asia","technology","religion"],"abstract":"The <b>Islamic Golden Age</b> is a modern label for a period of Muslim learning and art usually placed between the 8th century and the 13th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its base was the movement that turned Greek, Persian, Syriac and Indian works into Arabic, begun under the Umayyads and carried furthest by the Abbasid caliphs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Manuscripts were gathered by purchase, conquest and exchange and translated by the best scholars a court could attract, working from the Greek directly or through a Syriac version.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Fihrist, an index of the sciences compiled at Baghdad in 987, shows both what had been assembled and how much has since been lost, since barely one book in a thousand that it lists survives even in fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Medicine grew early and fast, and the bimaristan, the hospital, spread across the Islamic world as a place that treated the poor without charge and trained physicians at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Those hospitals kept libraries, examined their doctors before appointing them and paid them a salary, and divided their wards by sex and by disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Astronomy, geometry, engineering and music were studied beside medicine, and the translators were often working scientists themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of it was centred on one court: after 929 al-Andalus was the leading centre of art and learning in the western Mediterranean, competing directly with Baghdad and Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The label's span has grown in the telling, since a standard account of a century ago gave the golden age a single hundred years, from 750 to 850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its usual close is the Mongol destruction of Baghdad in 1258, though that same account dates the decline earlier, to the victory of one school of theology over another and the rise of Turkish power at court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Edward G. Browne, <i>Arabian Medicine: Being the FitzPatrick Lectures Delivered at the College of Physicians in November 1919 and November 1920</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1921), 4–6, https://archive.org/details/arabianmedicineb00browiala. [Open access]","Samet Şenel, “Science Expanding Amid Political Challenges: Translation Activities During the al-Mutawakkil ‘Alā’llāh Period (232–247 H/847–861 CE),” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 430, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16040430. [Open access]","Fatma Çapan, “İslam Dünyası'nda Bimaristanlar ve Gelişme Süreçleri,” <i>Gaziantep University Journal of Social Sciences</i> 18, no. 3 (2019): 1205–19, https://doi.org/10.21547/jss.492008. [Open access]","Reynold A. Nicholson, <i>A Literary History of the Arabs</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 359, https://archive.org/details/literaryhistoryo00nichuoft. [Open access]","Susana Calvo Capilla, “The Visual Construction of the Umayyad Caliphate in Al-Andalus through the Great Mosque of Cordoba,” <i>Arts</i> 7, no. 3 (2018): 36, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts7030036. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/MIA_-_Planispheric_Astrolabe%2C_Iran%2C_984_AD_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"A planispheric astrolabe of 984 CE","desc":"Made in brass by Ḥāmid ibn al-Khiḍr al-Khujandī, at Rayy in Iran or at Baghdad, and dated 374 AH. The instrument models the sky on a flat disc, and is used to tell the time, find the direction of prayer and take the altitude of a star. Museum of Islamic Art, Doha.","alt":"A circular brass disc hung from a ring, its face cut into open curling arms of pierced metal over an engraved plate, with Arabic numerals and letters round the rim.","credit":"Photograph by Ciphers. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MIA_-_Planispheric_Astrolabe,_Iran,_984_AD_(cropped).jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-473","num":473,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The Latin form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>'s name gave English the word algorithm, and the first word of his book on completion and reduction gave it algebra.","questions":["Al-Maʾmūn set <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to revise Ptolemy's tables, observe at Baghdad and Damascus and measure a degree of the meridian, besides writing on calculation.","Sorting problems into roots, squares and simple numbers and then into six cases, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> justified each rule by cutting and rearranging areas on the page."],"answer":"al-Khwārizmī","answerText":"al-Khwarizmi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under al-Maʾmūn, 813 – 833 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Listed in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Fihrist, c. 987 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","science","asia","technology","iraq"],"abstract":"<b>Al-Khwārizmī</b> was a mathematician and astronomer at Baghdad in the reign of the caliph al-Maʾmūn, who ruled from 813 to 833.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost everything known about him comes from the Fihrist, the index of books compiled at Baghdad about 987, which lists his works and little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The caliph set him to make extracts from an Indian astronomical handbook, to revise Ptolemy's tables, to observe at Baghdad and Damascus, and to measure a degree of the earth's meridian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His best-known book is a short treatise on calculating by completion and reduction, and from the first of those two Arabic words the word algebra descends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says in its preface that the caliph encouraged him to write a popular work, which implies both that other treatises already existed and that he never claimed to have invented the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He wrote it for use rather than for elegance, naming inheritance, legacies, partition, lawsuits, trade, the measuring of land and the digging of canals as the cases men constantly meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method sorts every quantity into roots, squares and simple numbers, and every problem into six cases such as 'squares are equal to roots', each with its own rule and a worked example.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rules are justified by cutting and rearranging areas, an argument that runs back to Old Babylonian mathematics, and joining that older practice to a Greek idea of proof is part of what made algebra a discipline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second book carried the Indian place-value numerals into Arabic, and its Latin form carried his own name into Europe as Algoritmi, which is where algorithm comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He belonged to a generation whose translations and observations were paid for at court, so his astronomy and his arithmetic sat inside the same patronage as the translators' work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Frederic Rosen, ed. and trans., <i>The Algebra of Mohammed ben Musa</i> (London: Oriental Translation Fund, 1831), vi–viii, 3–7, https://archive.org/details/algebraofmohamme00khuwrich. [Open access]","Florian Cajori, <i>A History of Mathematics</i>, 2nd ed., revised and enlarged (New York: Macmillan, 1919), 102–3, https://archive.org/details/historyofmathema1894cajo. [Open access]","Jens Høyrup, “Al-Khwârizmî, Ibn Turk, and the Liber Mensurationum: On the Origins of Islamic Algebra,” <i>Erdem</i> 2, no. 5 (1986): 445–84, https://doi.org/10.32704/erdem.1986.5.445. [Open access]","Reynold A. Nicholson, <i>A Literary History of the Arabs</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 359, https://archive.org/details/literaryhistoryo00nichuoft. [Open access]","Samet Şenel, “Science Expanding Amid Political Challenges: Translation Activities During the al-Mutawakkil ‘Alā’llāh Period (232–247 H/847–861 CE),” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 4 (2025): 430, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16040430. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/25/Bodleian_MS._Huntington_214_roll332_frame36.jpg","title":"Two pages of the Algebra, with its diagrams","desc":"An opening from a manuscript of al-Khwārizmī's treatise on completion and reduction, showing geometrical solutions to two quadratic equations. The red rectangles at the foot of each page are the areas whose cutting and rearranging justify the rule. Bodleian Library, MS Huntington 214.","alt":"Two facing pages of an old manuscript in dense brown Arabic script, each with a small diagram of nested rectangles drawn in red ink at the bottom, lettered in Arabic.","credit":"Bodleian Libraries, University of Oxford. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodleian_MS._Huntington_214_roll332_frame36.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-474","num":474,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Summoned at eighteen to treat a Samanid king, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was rewarded with the run of the royal library and later wrote a medical encyclopedia of nearly a million words.","questions":["The five books of the Canon of Medicine, translated into Latin and printed at Venice in 1544, carried <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>'s system into European teaching for centuries.","Distinguishing a thing's essence from its being, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called God the Necessary Existent, whose existence is identical with himself and who falls under no genus or category."],"answer":"Avicenna","answerText":"Avicenna","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 980 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">near Bukhara</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1036 or 1037 CE, aged 58</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","science","asia","philosophy","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Avicenna</b>, in Arabic Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Sīnā, was a physician, philosopher, poet and minister of state, born about 980 to a father from Balkh and a mother from a village near Bukhara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He kept a record of his own life down to his twenty-first year, and his pupil Abū ʿUbayd of Jūzjān wrote the rest of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At ten he knew the Quran and the Arabic classics, spent six years on jurisprudence, philosophy, logic, Euclid and the Almagest, and came to medicine at sixteen, finding it not difficult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Metaphysics defeated him until a cheap manual by al-Fārābī solved his difficulties, and at barely eighteen he was called to treat the Samanid ruler Nūḥ ibn Manṣūr, who let him use the royal library.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That library later burned, and his detractors said he had fired it himself to keep a monopoly of what he had read there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He served and fled a succession of princes, became prime minister at Hamadan after curing an amir of the colic, was imprisoned by mutinous soldiers and recalled when the colic came back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He worked all day for the amir and lectured and dictated much of the night, and died worn out in 1037, aged 58.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His Canon of Medicine runs to nearly a million words in five books, on general principles, simple drugs, diseases organ by organ, diseases that spread, and compound medicines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its systematic plan displaced the earlier handbooks, and in Gerard of Cremona's Latin, printed at Venice in 1544, it long remained a standard authority in Europe as well as in the hospitals of his own world, which kept libraries and examined their physicians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In philosophy he separated a thing's essence from its being, called God the Necessary Existent whose existence is identical with himself, and held the highest happiness to be the comprehension of God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Edward G. Browne, <i>Arabian Medicine: Being the FitzPatrick Lectures Delivered at the College of Physicians in November 1919 and November 1920</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1921), 57–63, https://archive.org/details/arabianmedicineb00browiala. [Open access]","Nathaniel B. Taylor, “Is God a Substance? Avicenna on Essence, Being, and the Categories,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 12 (2023): 1469, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14121469. [Open access]","Reynold A. Nicholson, <i>A Literary History of the Arabs</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 266–67, https://archive.org/details/literaryhistoryo00nichuoft. [Open access]","Fatma Çapan, “İslam Dünyası'nda Bimaristanlar ve Gelişme Süreçleri,” <i>Gaziantep University Journal of Social Sciences</i> 18, no. 3 (2019): 1205–19, https://doi.org/10.21547/jss.492008. [Open access]","Ismail Lala and Reham Alwazzan, “Transcendental Happiness in the Thought of Ibn Sīnā and Ibn ʿArabī,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 6 (2023): 729, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14060729. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/The_Canon_of_Medicine.jpg/1280px-The_Canon_of_Medicine.jpg","title":"A manuscript of the Canon of Medicine","desc":"A Persian manuscript copy of Avicenna's Canon, open at a page of anatomical diagrams, on display at the Museum and Mausoleum of Avicenna at Hamadan in Iran, the city where he served as prime minister and where he is buried.","alt":"An old book lying open on a wooden table, its two pages covered in fine Arabic script with a small group of hand-drawn anatomical diagrams in the lower left.","credit":"Photograph by Coffeetalkh, 2010. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Canon_of_Medicine.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-475","num":475,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Hospitals that fed and clothed their patients, salaried and examined their doctors and kept libraries were the working institutions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which grew from Greek and Persian practice.","questions":["Al-Rāzī lectured in Baghdad's first hospital and Avicenna wrote the book that displaced his, and both worked in Arabic in the tradition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though both were Persians.","Galen went into Syriac and then Arabic to give <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a technical vocabulary, and the Latin versions made later from those Arabic texts often garbled the words."],"answer":"Islamic medicine","answerText":"Islamic medicine","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Translation era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 900 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʿAḍudī hospital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 974 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","history","science","asia","technology","religion"],"abstract":"<b>Islamic medicine</b> is the practice and literature that grew in the Islamic world from the 8th century out of Greek, Syriac, Persian and Indian material; it is also called Arabian medicine, though its best-known writers were Persians and many of its translators were Christians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its immediate parent was the school at Jundishapur in Persia, which held a hospital, a house of translation, a library, a pharmacology laboratory and an observatory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Jundishapur's teachers went on moving to Baghdad after that city was founded, and the Persian school declined and disappeared as they left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Translation made the vocabulary possible, since Galen and the rest had to go into Syriac and Arabic first, and the Latin versions made afterwards from the Arabic often mangled the very words they claimed to render.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Baghdad's older hospital is presumed to be where al-Rāzī lectured and so founded the city's medical school; he died in 932, and half a century later a Buyid prince built the great ʿAḍudī hospital on the Tigris bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That hospital was long famous as a school of medicine, and it was one of many across the Islamic world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bimaristan treated patients without charge and gave them a bed, food, clothing and money while they were recovering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wards were divided by sex and by complaint, mobile branches went where fixed ones could not, libraries were kept, and physicians drew a salary and had to pass an examination before appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four Persian writers dominate the surviving literature — ʿAlī ibn Rabban, al-Rāzī, al-Majūsī and Ibn Sīnā — and the last of them eventually displaced the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether these physicians dissected human bodies was an open question a century ago and has still not been settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Edward G. Browne, <i>Arabian Medicine: Being the FitzPatrick Lectures Delivered at the College of Physicians in November 1919 and November 1920</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1921), 33–37, 65–70, https://archive.org/details/arabianmedicineb00browiala. [Open access]","Fatma Çapan, “İslam Dünyası'nda Bimaristanlar ve Gelişme Süreçleri,” <i>Gaziantep University Journal of Social Sciences</i> 18, no. 3 (2019): 1205–19, https://doi.org/10.21547/jss.492008. [Open access]","Mehmet Mahfuz Söylemez, “The Jundishapur School,” <i>American Journal of Islam and Society</i> 22, no. 2 (2005): 1–27, https://doi.org/10.35632/ajis.v22i2.455. [Open access]","Guy Le Strange, <i>Baghdad during the Abbasid Caliphate: From Contemporary Arabic and Persian Sources</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1900), 62–63, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_rdcoAAAAYAAJ. [Open access]","Reynold A. Nicholson, <i>A Literary History of the Arabs</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 267, https://archive.org/details/literaryhistoryo00nichuoft. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Bimaristan_of_nur_al-din%2C_damascus%2C_syria%2C_easter_2004_%281524490934%29.jpg","title":"The bimaristan of Nūr al-Dīn at Damascus","desc":"The front of the hospital built at Damascus in 1154, its entrance recess carved in tiers of small niches under a fluted red dome. It stands in a working street of the old city, and later housed a museum of Arab medicine.","alt":"A pale stone facade at the end of a narrow street, with a deeply carved honeycombed recess above its doorway and a tall ribbed red dome rising behind it, framed by shopfronts and overhead cables.","credit":"Photograph by seier+seier, 2004. CC BY 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bimaristan_of_nur_al-din,_damascus,_syria,_easter_2004_(1524490934).jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-476","num":476,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Geometry, calligraphy and vegetal ornament do the work in a mosque that figures do in a church, which is one reason <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is so often said to forbid the human image.","questions":["An Umayyad bath house painted with hunters, bathers and enthroned kings is a reminder that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept the human figure everywhere except in its mosques.","Roman columns reused in Córdoba and Byzantine mosaic laid at Damascus show how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took over older material and older craft and set them to a new purpose."],"answer":"Islamic art","answerText":"Islamic art","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 690 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Qusayr Amra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 743 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Córdoba mosque</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 785 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","history","art","asia","religion","europe"],"abstract":"<b>Islamic art</b> is the art of the lands where Islam was the ruling faith, from Spain to India, and it is a matter of buildings, writing, ornament, ceramics, metal and cloth rather than of any single medium. It is often said to forbid the human figure, which is true of the mosque and untrue of almost everywhere else. Qusayr Amra, an Umayyad bath house in the Jordanian steppe finished about 743, is painted with hunters, bathers, musicians and enthroned kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its imagery is borrowed from the late antique world around it and turned to Umayyad ideas of beauty and rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the coinage carried a picture of the caliph, standing with a sword inside a ring of Arabic script, until ʿAbd al-Malik's reform of 696 replaced the image with writing alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside a mosque the living figure is what is missing, and its place is taken by script, geometry and plant forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Great Mosque at Damascus was faced with mosaic towns, palaces, rivers and trees in the Byzantine manner, an inhabited landscape with the inhabitants left out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Córdoba the builders set Roman and Visigothic columns into their arcades and ran a chosen programme of Quranic text round the walls, claiming the older past of the land for a new order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 10th century the architects there began to use geometry in a new way, letting the same figures govern ground plan, elevation and ornament together, and measuring the human view by them as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Taking over older material and older technique and setting them to say something new is the habit of the tradition rather than an exception in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Inbal Kol, “Revisiting Qusayr Amra: A New Perspective on Female Imagery,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 9 (2025): 1168, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16091168. [Open access]","Barbara Zając, “The Standing Caliph Coins found in Marea/Philoxenite, Egypt,” <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilisation</i> 29 (2025): 161–74, https://doi.org/10.12797//SAAC.29.2025.29.08. [Open access]","Zaraza Friedman, “The Ship Depicted on the North Colonnade of the Great Mosque at Damascus: A Nilotic Theme or the Representation of Paradise?,” <i>Journal of Mosaic Research</i> 8 (2015): 17–31, https://dergipark.org.tr/tr/download/article-file/294116. [Open access]","Susana Calvo Capilla, “The Visual Construction of the Umayyad Caliphate in Al-Andalus through the Great Mosque of Cordoba,” <i>Arts</i> 7, no. 3 (2018): 36, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts7030036. [Open access]","Felix Arnold, “Mathematics and the Islamic Architecture of Córdoba,” <i>Arts</i> 7, no. 3 (2018): 35, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts7030035. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Quseir_Amra_Zodiac.jpg/1280px-Quseir_Amra_Zodiac.jpg","title":"The zodiac dome at Qusayr ʿAmra","desc":"The vault over the hot room of an Umayyad bath house in the Jordanian desert, painted in the 8th century with the constellations of the northern sky as human and animal bodies. Nothing of the kind appears in a mosque of the same period.","credit":"Photograph by Bontenbal, 2007. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quseir_Amra_Zodiac.jpg","alt":"A shallow stone dome seen from below, its plaster painted in ochre and olive green with worn human and animal figures and faint curved guide lines, an arched window cut through the wall to the right."}},
{"id":"wh-477","num":477,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The caliph al-Ḥakam II enlarged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 962, adding a triple façade of mosaic that became the model for great mosques all across the western Islamic world.","questions":["A covered passage ran from the Umayyad palace straight into the ruler's enclosure inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the caliph could pray with his people without crossing the street.","Reused ancient columns carry the horseshoe arches of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a cathedral was built inside it three centuries after Castile took the city."],"answer":"Great Mosque of Córdoba","answerText":"Great Mosque of Cordoba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under ʿAbd al-Raḥmān I, 756 – 788 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enlarged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">962 – 976 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cathedral built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1523 – 1607 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["building","history","art","europe","religion","spain"],"abstract":"The <b>Great Mosque of Córdoba</b> was begun in the 8th century by ʿAbd al-Raḥmān I, and its plan of parallel naves carried on arcades made it easy to extend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> ʿAbd al-Raḥmān II and al-Ḥakam II pushed it southwards in turn, and al-Manṣūr added a whole eastern block in the 10th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient material was reused from the founding phase onwards, and in the 9th-century enlargement salvaged pieces were set beside new work from the Umayyad workshops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old story that it was raised over the Visigothic church of San Vicente is not supported by what has been dug: the reused sculpture came from a basilica at Mérida nearly 200 kilometres (125 miles) away, dismantled in the mid-9th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The mosque stood against the Alcázar, the dynasty's palace, and a covered passage called the sābāṭ ran between them so that the ruler could reach his enclosure without going into the street.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Al-Ḥakam II made enlarging it one of his first acts as caliph, and in 962 he consulted the city's jurists, astronomers and chief judge about the plan and the direction of the qibla.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he built is a triple front of mihrab, treasury and palace door, faced with glazed mosaic under domed bays, and it became the model for great mosques across the western Islamic world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prayer niche is 3.77 metres (12 feet) wide and 3.57 deep, a size matched only by the two great mosques of the Abbasids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its architects were applying geometry in a new way, letting one set of figures govern plan, elevation and ornament together, while horseshoe arches let short columns carry a high roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Castile took the city in 1236 and, where other Iberian mosques were pulled down, this one was used as a church; a cathedral was raised inside it between 1523 and 1607.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Susana Calvo Capilla, “The Visual Construction of the Umayyad Caliphate in Al-Andalus through the Great Mosque of Cordoba,” <i>Arts</i> 7, no. 3 (2018): 36, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts7030036. [Open access]","Antonio Gámiz-Gordo, Juan Cantizani-Oliva, and Juan Francisco Reinoso-Gordo, “The Mosque-Cathedral of Cordoba: Graphic Analysis of Interior Perspectives by Girault de Prangey around 1839,” <i>ISPRS International Journal of Geo-Information</i> 10, no. 3 (2021): 181, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijgi10030181. [Open access]","Felix Arnold, “Mathematics and the Islamic Architecture of Córdoba,” <i>Arts</i> 7, no. 3 (2018): 35, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts7030035. [Open access]","Alberto León-Muñoz, “The Alcázar of Córdoba: The Seat of Islamic Power in Al-Andalus,” <i>Arts</i> 12, no. 5 (2023): 202, https://doi.org/10.3390/arts12050202. [Open access]","María de los Ángeles Utrero Agudo, “Stratigraphy Matters: Questioning the (Re)Sacralisation of Religious Spaces from Late Antiquity to the Early Middle Ages in the Iberian Peninsula,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 9 (2023): 1199, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14091199. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Cordoba_Cathedral_2024_-_Maqsura.jpg/1280px-Cordoba_Cathedral_2024_-_Maqsura.jpg","title":"The prayer niche of al-Ḥakam II","desc":"The mihrab built for al-Ḥakam II after 962, framed by bands of gold and blue mosaic carrying Quranic text, under the ribbed dome of the enclosure reserved for the ruler. Not a living figure appears anywhere in it.","alt":"An arched recess in a mosque wall, its horseshoe arch and rectangular frame covered in gold and deep blue mosaic inscriptions and foliage, with carved stucco arcades above and a decorated vault overhead.","credit":"Photograph by Fernando, 2024. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cordoba_Cathedral_2024_-_Maqsura.jpg"}},
{"id":"wh-478","num":478,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Proclaimed in Ifrīqiya and named for Muhammad's daughter, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Egypt in 969, founded Cairo and ruled from it until Saladin ended the line in 1171.","questions":["The Abbasid caliph's name was struck from the Friday prayers in Egypt when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the country, and the call to prayer was altered to Shiʿi usage.","Ruling a country that was mostly not Ismaili, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaned on Sunni officials and on the loyalty of Christians and Jews rather than trying to convert them."],"answer":"Fatimid Caliphate","answerText":"Fatimid Caliphate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">909 – 910 CE, in Ifrīqiya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">969 – 1171 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","politics","africa","religion","egypt"],"abstract":"The <b>Fatimid Caliphate</b> was an Ismaili Shiʿi dynasty that rose in North Africa early in the 10th century and ruled Egypt from 969 until 1171.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its founder was proclaimed at Kairouan in January 910 as ʿUbaydallāh al-Mahdī, the awaited one, by a missionary who had won over the Berbers of Ifrīqiya to his cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dynasty claimed descent from Muhammad's daughter Fāṭima, and the claim was disputed at the time and is disputed still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From Ifrīqiya it took the rest of North Africa and Sicily, which it held from 909 to 1061, and in 969 its general Jawhar conquered Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He founded a new capital beside Fustat and called it al-Qāhira: the trenches had been opened at an unlucky hour, with the planet al-Qāhir in the ascendant, and the city took its name in the hope of turning the omen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Abbasid caliph's name was struck out of the Friday prayers, the black Abbasid robes were banned, the call to prayer was altered to Shiʿi usage and the new coins carried Fatimid formulas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the century in which Shiʿi dynasties held most of the Islamic world at once, the Buyids at Baghdad, the Hamdanids in Syria and the Qarmatians in Arabia beside the Fatimids in Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The caliphs governed a country that was mostly not Ismaili, and leaned on Sunni officials and on the loyalty of Christians and Jews rather than on conversion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Jewish community of Fustat flourished under them, and the papers its synagogue store-room kept are now a chief source for the social and economic history of the whole region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cairo grew into a walled palace city with gates, the mosque of al-Ḥākim and a hall of science, until Saladin ended the dynasty in 1171 and returned Egypt to Sunni rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>A History of Egypt in the Middle Ages</i> (London: Methuen, 1901), 92–105, 116, 158, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptin00lane. [Open access]","Nuha Alshaar, “Christians and Muslims of Sicily Under Aghlabid and Fāṭimid Rule: A Cultural and Historical Perspective,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 10 (2025): 1291, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16101291. [Open access]","Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>The Story of Cairo</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1902), 113–45, https://archive.org/details/storyofcairo00lane. [Open access]","Ahmed Mohamed Sheir, “Shared Memory and History: The Abrahamic Legacy in Medieval Judaeo-Arabic Poetry from the Cairo Genizah,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 12 (2024): 1431, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15121431. [Open access]","Mohammad Ali Amir-Moezzi, “The Shi’is and the Qur’an: Between Apocalypse, Civil Wars, and Empire,” <i>Religions</i> 13, no. 1 (2022): 1, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel13010001. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Cairo%2C_moschea_di_al-aqmar%2C_04.JPG/1280px-Cairo%2C_moschea_di_al-aqmar%2C_04.JPG","title":"The facade of the al-Aqmar mosque","desc":"The street front of a small mosque built in Cairo in 1125 for the Fatimid caliph al-Āmir, its entrance set under a fluted hood with a pierced medallion at the centre and bands of foliated Kufic script above and below.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, 2015. CC BY 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo%2C_moschea_di_al-aqmar%2C_04.JPG","alt":"A honey-coloured carved stone facade in low evening light, with a tall pointed recess whose fluted shell radiates from a round openwork medallion, flanked by smaller niches and horizontal bands of Arabic lettering."}},
{"id":"wh-479","num":479,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Founded at Cairo in 970 by a Shiʿi dynasty and given over to the learned in 988, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has taught students from West Africa to Malaya ever since.","questions":["Each nation of students at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had its own riwāq, or portico, and none of them was asked to pay a piastre in fees.","Theology, hadith, jurisprudence, grammar, prosody, logic, rhetoric and algebra made up the old curriculum at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose 239 professors taught nine thousand students in 1901."],"answer":"al-Azhar","answerText":"al-Azhar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 April 970 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 June 972 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made a school</span><span class=\"dt-v\">988 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","religion","africa","egypt","building"],"abstract":"<b>Al-Azhar</b> is a mosque and school of Islamic learning at Cairo, founded by the Fatimids within a year of their conquest of Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their general Jawhar laid its foundations on 3 April 970, once the walls of the new palace city were begun, and the building was finished on 24 June 972.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was made over to the use of the learned in 988, and has been one of the chief universities of Islam ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before it, Egypt had stood well behind Baghdad, Damascus and Cordova as a place of learning, and had produced no poet, historian or critic of the first rank in Arabic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was an Ismaili foundation in a country whose people were mostly not Ismaili, and whose rulers leaned on Sunni officials rather than trying to convert them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> After the Fatimids fell that balance was settled the other way: in 1265 the Mamluk sultan Baybars appointed a chief judge for each of the four Sunni schools of law in Egypt, which had not been done there before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Maghribī scholar passing through on pilgrimage around 1300 named it as one of the two great mosques of the city and wrote down its founder and its dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Students gathered from across the Muslim world, from West Africa to the Malay states, each nation to the riwāq or portico assigned to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were taught theology, exegesis, hadith, jurisprudence, grammar, prosody, logic, rhetoric and algebra, and not one of them paid a fee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine thousand students were attending the lectures of 239 professors in 1901, the teachers giving their knowledge without reward and living by private tuition and by copying manuscripts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>The Story of Cairo</i> (London: J. M. Dent, 1902), 123–24, https://archive.org/details/storyofcairo00lane. [Open access]","Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>A History of Egypt in the Middle Ages</i> (London: Methuen, 1901), 91, 104, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptin00lane. [Open access]","Nuha Alshaar, “Christians and Muslims of Sicily Under Aghlabid and Fāṭimid Rule: A Cultural and Historical Perspective,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 10 (2025): 1291, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16101291. [Open access]","Hatim Muhammad Mahamid, “Religious Policy of the Mamluk Sultan Baybars (1260–1277 AC),” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 11 (2023): 1384, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14111384. [Open access]","Muhammet Enes Midilli, “Blending Pilgrimage and Learning or the Literary Genres of Riḥla and Muʿjam: Al-Tujībī’s Travelogue to Egypt and Ḥijāz,” <i>Religions</i> 16, no. 3 (2025): 344, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel16030344. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Cairo_-_Islamic_district_-_Al-Azhar_Mosque_courtyard_with_minarets.JPG/1280px-Cairo_-_Islamic_district_-_Al-Azhar_Mosque_courtyard_with_minarets.JPG","title":"The courtyard of al-Azhar","desc":"The open court of the mosque founded in Cairo in 970, looking across its marble paving to the arcades and to three carved minarets, all of them added long after the Fatimid building.","credit":"Photograph by Daniel Mayer, 2008. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cairo_-_Islamic_district_-_Al-Azhar_Mosque_courtyard_with_minarets.JPG","alt":"A wide sunlit courtyard paved in pale stone, bounded by whitewashed arcades on slender columns under a stepped parapet, with three tall carved minarets and a ribbed dome rising behind against a blue sky."}},
{"id":"wh-480","num":480,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"The inward strand of Islam, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew out of an ascetic movement whose teachers were remembered for a turning of the whole life rather than for doctrine.","questions":["Organised from the 12th century into orders, each gathered around the teaching of a founding master, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spread across India through the Chishtī line.","A highwayman's repentance, a Mamluk sultan's endowments and a modern polemic all belong to the history of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which has been argued about for twelve centuries."],"answer":"Sufism","answerText":"Sufism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Early ascetics</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 9th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">al-Fuḍayl</span><span class=\"dt-v\">d. 803 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ibn ʿArabī</span><span class=\"dt-v\">d. 1240 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","history","religion","asia","philosophy","india"],"abstract":"<b>Sufism</b>, in Arabic taṣawwuf, is the inward and mystical strand of Islam, concerned with purifying the self and drawing near to God rather than with law and doctrine alone. It grew out of an ascetic movement of the 8th and 9th centuries whose figures are remembered above all for tawba, a repentance that turned a whole life round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Al-Fuḍayl ibn ʿIyāḍ, who died in 803, was said to have been a highwayman before he repented, and he taught what has been called an ethic of awe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His pupil Bishr al-Ḥāfī, who died in 841, recast that teaching into a public and visible way of living, and two distinct lines of asceticism ran on from the pair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later Sufi thought turned philosophical: Ibn ʿArabī, who died in 1240, agreed with Ibn Sīnā that the highest happiness is the comprehension of God, but denied that it lies in the annihilation of the self.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> From the 12th century the tradition organised itself into orders, each gathered around the teaching of a founding master.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Chishtī order, whose founder Muʿīn al-Dīn Chishtī died in 1236 and who settled it at Lahore and Ajmer, became the largest in India and carried Islam far into the subcontinent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That founder insisted that Sufism without the sharia is superstition, which is one answer from inside the tradition to a charge often made against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rulers paid for it as well: the Mamluk sultan Baybars endowed colleges in Cairo and Damascus and took a close interest in the shrines and the popular devotion around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is argued about still, and a recent study warns that a broad theological label, Sufi or Salafi, predicts nothing about whether those who wear it accept or reject violence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Jamal Ali Assadi, Mahmoud Naamneh, and Khaled Sindawi, “Repentance Made Manifest: From Highwayman to Ṣūfī in the Thought and Practice of al-Fuḍayl ibn ʿIyāḍ and Bishr al-Ḥāfī,” <i>Religions</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 54, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel17010054. [Open access]","Ismail Lala and Reham Alwazzan, “Transcendental Happiness in the Thought of Ibn Sīnā and Ibn ʿArabī,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 6 (2023): 729, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14060729. [Open access]","Hatim Muhammad Mahamid, “Religious Policy of the Mamluk Sultan Baybars (1260–1277 AC),” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 11 (2023): 1384, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14111384. [Open access]","Vahit Göktaş and Saeyd Rashed Hasan Chowdury, “An Evaluation of Mu'in al-Din Chishti's Sufi Influences in the Indian Subcontinent: The Case of Chishti Tariqa,” <i>Şırnak Üniversitesi İlahiyat Fakültesi Dergisi</i>, no. 31 (2023): 47–68, https://doi.org/10.35415/sirnakifd.1244284. [Open access]","Hazim Fouad, “Unveiling the Innovators—A Glimpse on Sufi-Salafi Polemics,” <i>Religions</i> 11, no. 3 (2020): 144, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel11030144. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Dargah_of_moinuddin_chishti.jpg/1280px-Dargah_of_moinuddin_chishti.jpg","title":"The shrine of Muʿīn al-Dīn Chishtī at Ajmer","desc":"The dome over the tomb of the founder of the Chishtī order, who died in 1236 at Ajmer in Rajasthan, lit for a festival. Shrines like this became the visible centre of Sufi devotion in the subcontinent.","credit":"Photograph by VrMUSLIM, 2014. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dargah_of_moinuddin_chishti.jpg","alt":"A white marble dome at dusk, ringed with strings of small electric lamps and crowned by a gilded finial, rising above a decorated parapet against a grey evening sky."}},
{"id":"wh-481","num":481,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Leaving Tangier for the pilgrimage in 1325 and not seeing home again for 24 years, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dictated a book of travels whose furthest reaches are still argued over.","questions":["A judge by training who served the sultan of Delhi for years before going home to Morocco, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the traveller whose thirty years on the road produced the Rihla.","Dictated at Fez in 1356 to a man of letters who had never been east, the Rihla of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> records a journey across most of the 14th-century Muslim world."],"answer":"Ibn Battuta","answerText":"Ibn Battuta","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1304 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Set out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1325 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rihla written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1356 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1368 or 1377 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Ibn Battuta</b> was a Moroccan jurist and traveller who left Tangier in 1325 to make the pilgrimage to Mecca and did not come home for 24 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was born in 1304 into a family of legal scholars, and the last part of his name, al-Ṭanjī, says only which town he came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trained as a judge and a memoriser of the Quran, he was given judgeships in several of the countries he passed through, helped by fluent Arabic and Persian and a plain, almost dervish, way of dressing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From North Africa and the Middle East he went north to the Golden Horde, where he described the women of Uzbek Khan’s court, and on across Central Asia to Delhi, reached in 1333.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sultan Muhammad ibn Tughluq kept him there in high office for some seven or eight years before he moved on to the Maldives and, in 1345, took ship from Bengal for China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He reached Morocco again in 1349, settled at Fez under the Marinid sultan Abū ʿInān, and in 1356 the journey was finally put into writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He did not write it: at the sultan’s order he dictated to Ibn Juzayy, a man of letters who had never been east, who cast it in ornate prose and filled in what the traveller could not remember.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because he kept no notes and spoke some thirty years after the events, the dates and the route contradict each other in places, and borrowings from earlier writers keep being identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether he saw everything he describes is therefore disputed, and one specialist has remarked that the same reasoning would prove he was both certainly in the Malay archipelago and never there at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The text is uncertain at the level of single words too, a rare term he used at Alexandria in 1326 for a Sufi posture surviving in two manuscript readings, and yet it joined the small set of works on which the geography of the medieval Islamic world rests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Beyza Aybike Deveci and Arzu Yıldız Aydın, “Ibn Battuta’s Observations on India from a Historical Religious Perspective: Examples of Beliefs and Cultural Practices,” <i>Şırnak Üniversitesi İlahiyat Fakültesi Dergisi</i>, no. 38 (2025): 459–80, https://doi.org/10.35415/sirnakifd.1734068. [Open access]","Aglaia Iankovskaia, “At the Edge of the World of Islam: Ibn Baṭṭūṭa in the Malay Archipelago,” <i>Entangled Religions</i> 11, no. 5 (2020), https://doi.org/10.46586/er.11.2020.8673. [Open access]","Giuseppe Cecere, “<i>Tarfīq</i> versus <i>Tazyīq</i>: On a Rare Sufi Term in Ibn Baṭṭūṭā and Jewish-Muslim Interactions in Medieval Egypt,” <i>Quaderni di Linguistica e Studi Orientali</i> 2 (2016): 265–90, https://doi.org/10.13128/QULSO-2421-7220-18756. [Open access]","Elmira G. Sayfetdinova, “The Image of the Golden Horde Womens in the Notes of an Arab Traveler Ibn Battuta,” <i>From History and Culture of Peoples of the Middle Volga Region</i> 13, no. 4 (2023): 10–17, https://doi.org/10.22378/2410-0765.2023-13-4.10-17. [Open access]","Guy Le Strange, <i>The Lands of the Eastern Caliphate</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1905), 16, 133, https://archive.org/details/landsofeasternca00lest. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","geography","islam","travel","morocco","14th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Historic_copy_of_selected_parts_of_the_Travel_Report_by_Ibn_Battuta%2C_1836_CE%2C_Cairo.jpg/1280px-Historic_copy_of_selected_parts_of_the_Travel_Report_by_Ibn_Battuta%2C_1836_CE%2C_Cairo.jpg","title":"A manuscript of the Rihla","desc":"A 19th-century copy of parts of Ibn Battuta’s travel account, made at Cairo in 1836 and shown here open at two pages of Arabic with rubrics in red. The text survives in copies of this kind rather than in anything the traveller wrote himself.","credit":"Photograph by Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin, 2019. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historic_copy_of_selected_parts_of_the_Travel_Report_by_Ibn_Battuta%2C_1836_CE%2C_Cairo.jpg","alt":"An old bound book lying open in a display case, both pages filled with small Arabic script in brown ink with occasional words picked out in red."}},
{"id":"wh-482","num":482,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Writing in a castle on the Algerian steppe, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced an introduction to universal history that asked what makes a report about the past worth believing.","questions":["The binding solidarity of a kin group, ʿaṣabiyya, is the term with which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explained how one dynasty takes power from another and then loses it again.","Born at Tunis in 1332 and buried at Cairo in 1406, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is called a founder of sociology for the science of social organisation his Muqaddima opened up."],"answer":"Ibn Khaldun","answerText":"Ibn Khaldun","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 May 1332 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Tunis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1352 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1406 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Ibn Khaldun</b> was a North African historian and official, born at Tunis in 1332 into a family of Yemeni Arab descent that had lived at Seville and crossed to Africa when that city fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took his first post in 1352 under the Marinid sultan Abū ʿInān at Fez, was imprisoned on political charges in 1356, and was let out two years later when the sultan died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After years of shifting service to rulers across the Maghreb and Muslim Spain he withdrew to a castle on the Algerian steppe and wrote there, in about three years, the book he is remembered for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That book is the <i>Muqaddima</i>, the long introduction to a universal history, in which he set out how to tell a sound report about the past from a false one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It treats civilisation as a movement from the nomadic to the settled and from the countryside to the town, and he named the subject it opened up ʿilm al-ʿumrān, the science of human social organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> His central term is ʿaṣabiyya, the binding solidarity of a kin group, which he treats as the force that lets one group take power from another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A dynasty founded on it passes, he argued, through birth, growth and decay, each stage lasting some forty years, so that the whole turn takes about a hundred and twenty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the third generation the hardness of desert life is a distant memory, luxury has taken its place and the solidarity that won the state has gone, leaving the ruler to hire soldiers instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Decline is therefore internal first, and a later reader of his work stresses that invasion from outside bites only to the degree that a society has already come apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He is often called a founder of sociology on the strength of this, though he ended as a working judge, grand qadi of the Maliki school at Cairo, losing and regaining the post repeatedly before he died there in 1406.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jelena Vukoičić, “Political Theory of Ibn Khaldun,” <i>Kom: časopis za religijske nauke</i> 4, no. 2 (2015): 111–34, https://doi.org/10.5937/kom1502111V. [Open access]","Mohammad Javid Fedayee, “Ibn Khaldun’s Contribution to the Study of the Social Dynamics of International Relations,” <i>İbn Haldun Çalışmaları Dergisi</i> 8, no. 2 (2023): 91–103, https://doi.org/10.36657/ihcd.2023.108. [Open access]","Fadila Grine and Ouassila Khezzar, “Critical Review on Ibn Khaldun’s Political Thought and Relevance: The Perspective of Abdallah Sharit,” <i>İbn Haldun Çalışmaları Dergisi</i> 2, no. 1 (2017): 69–92, https://doi.org/10.36657/ihcd.2017.21. [Open access]","Zaid Ahmad, Nobaya Ahmad, and Haslinda Abdullah, “Ibn Khaldun on Urban Planning: A Contemporary Reading of the Muqaddima,” <i>İbn Haldun Çalışmaları Dergisi</i> 1, no. 2 (2016): 315–30, https://doi.org/10.36657/ihcd.2016.15. [Open access]","Ejder Okumuş, “The Science of Umran, Conflict Resolution and Peace,” <i>İbn Haldun Çalışmaları Dergisi</i> 3, no. 1 (2018): 1–30, https://doi.org/10.36657/ihcd.2018.33. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","concept","islam","north africa","14th century","theory"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Ibn_Khald%C5%ABn_autograph%2C_al-Muqaddima%2C_MS_At%C4%B1f_Efendi_1936%2C_f._7a.png/1280px-Ibn_Khald%C5%ABn_autograph%2C_al-Muqaddima%2C_MS_At%C4%B1f_Efendi_1936%2C_f._7a.png","title":"The title page of the Muqaddima","desc":"The opening leaf of a Süleymaniye Library manuscript of the Muqaddima said to carry Ibn Khaldun’s own hand, crowded about with later owners’ notes, seals and library stamps.","credit":"Süleymaniye Kütüphanesi, Istanbul, MS Atıf Efendi 1936, fol. 7a. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibn_Khald%C5%ABn_autograph%2C_al-Muqaddima%2C_MS_At%C4%B1f_Efendi_1936%2C_f._7a.png","alt":"A worn manuscript page densely covered in Arabic script of several different hands and sizes, with a boxed note at the top left, two round seal impressions and a red-outlined inscription."}},
{"id":"wh-483","num":483,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Entering Baghdad in 1055 to end a century of Buyid control, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reunited Muslim Asia from Afghanistan to the Mediterranean under one Turkish sultan.","questions":["Alp Arslan routed the Byzantines at Manzikert in 1071 and took their emperor prisoner, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened Anatolia to Turkish settlement.","The vizier Nizam al-Mulk endowed a great college beside the Tigris for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose sultans governed Baghdad while the caliph kept his name in the prayers."],"answer":"Seljuk Empire","answerText":"Seljuk Empire","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Baghdad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1055 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manzikert</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1071 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanjar died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1157 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Seljuk Empire</b> was the state built by a family of Turkman chiefs from the steppes beyond the Oxus, descended from a leader named Seljuk, who overran Persia in the 11th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their supremacy at Baghdad began when Tughril Beg entered the city in 1055, closing the hundred and ten years in which the Persian Buyids had held the caliph and his capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The caliph stayed on as head of Sunni Islam while the sultan governed, and the newcomers took over the palace and government offices the Buyids had used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At their widest they held western Asia from the borders of Afghanistan to the Greek frontier and the edge of Egypt, under a single sovereign for the first time in generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Malik Shah is remembered as the best of them: he built bridges, canals and caravanserais, and his roads were safe enough that two travellers could ride unescorted from Merv to Damascus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In 1064 Alp Arslan took Ani, the Armenian capital, and in the spring of 1071 he routed a Byzantine army at Manzikert and took the emperor Romanus Diogenes prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anatolia was opened by that defeat, and Turkish groups moving in from Iran and Central Asia founded the Danishmendid, Saltuqid, Mengujekid and Artuqid principalities beside the Seljuk sultanate of Rum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Baghdad, Malik Shah built the sultan’s mosque and his vizier Nizam al-Mulk founded and endowed the Nizamiyya college on the riverbank below the caliph’s palaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A long-standing view credits their arrival with putting new force into Sunni Islam, pushing the Byzantines back and raising the generation of fighters against whom the crusaders repeatedly failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The empire itself did not hold: its supremacy lasted about a century, to the death of Sanjar in 1157, after which the line survived chiefly in Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Reynold A. Nicholson, <i>A Literary History of the Arabs</i> (London: T. Fisher Unwin, 1907), 265–66, https://archive.org/details/literaryhistoryo00nichuoft. [Open access]","Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>Saladin and the Fall of the Kingdom of Jerusalem</i> (London: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1898), 9–11, https://archive.org/details/saladinfallofkin00lane. [Open access]","Guy Le Strange, <i>The Lands of the Eastern Caliphate</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1905), 140–41, https://archive.org/details/landsofeasternca00lest. [Open access]","Guy Le Strange, <i>Baghdad during the Abbasid Caliphate: From Contemporary Arabic and Persian Sources</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1900), 326–27, https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_rdcoAAAAYAAJ. [Open access]","Yavuz Selim Altınok, “Anadolu’da Türk Kent Kültürünü Oluşturan Mekânsal Yapılanmalar,” <i>Ahbar</i>, no. 3 (2025): 44–58, https://doi.org/10.62425/ahbar.1653405. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","warfare","islam","turkey","iran","11th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Gran_Mezquita_de_Isfah%C3%A1n%2C_Isfahan%2C_Ir%C3%A1n%2C_2016-09-19%2C_DD_43-45_HDR_Alt.jpg/1280px-Gran_Mezquita_de_Isfah%C3%A1n%2C_Isfahan%2C_Ir%C3%A1n%2C_2016-09-19%2C_DD_43-45_HDR_Alt.jpg","title":"A brick dome at Isfahan","desc":"The inside of a domed chamber in the Great Mosque of Isfahan, its ribs, squinches and star patterns all worked in cut brick rather than tile or plaster, with a shaft of sunlight crossing it.","credit":"Photograph by Diego Delso, 2016. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gran_Mezquita_de_Isfah%C3%A1n%2C_Isfahan%2C_Ir%C3%A1n%2C_2016-09-19%2C_DD_43-45_HDR_Alt.jpg","alt":"Looking straight up into a large brick dome patterned with interlocking stars, ringed by smaller vaults and arches, with a beam of sunlight slanting across from a window."}},
{"id":"wh-484","num":484,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Born at Tikrit into a Kurdish family serving Turkish princes, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made himself vizier of Egypt and then took Jerusalem back from the crusaders.","questions":["After the army of the kingdom of Jerusalem was destroyed at Hattin on 4 July 1187, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the city in October and let its people ransom themselves.","Richard the Lionheart beat <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Arsuf in September 1191, but the Third Crusade ended without recovering the city the sultan had taken four years earlier."],"answer":"Saladin","answerText":"Saladin","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1137 or 1138 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hattin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1187 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jerusalem taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1187 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1193 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Saladin</b>, in Arabic Ṣalāḥ al-Dīn Yūsuf ibn Ayyūb, was born in the year 532 of the Muslim calendar, 1137 or 1138, in the citadel of Tikrit, where his father Ayyūb was governor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The family were Kurds of the Rawadiya clan from Dvin in Armenia who had come south into the service of the Turkish princes then ruling Mesopotamia and Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His uncle Shirkuh invaded Egypt three times on behalf of Nur al-Din of Damascus and was made vizier there, and when he died in 1169 the office passed to his nephew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Egypt was still governed by the Fatimid caliphs, a Shiʿi dynasty that recognised no authority at Baghdad, so the new vizier had to build his position without alarming either them or his master in Syria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He sent for his father and brothers to share it with him, and offered the vizierate to Ayyūb, who refused the honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> By 1180 he was arbitrating among the Muslim rulers between the Euphrates and the Nile, and that October he presided over a congress at which the princes of Mesopotamia, the sultan of Konya and the king of Armenia sealed a general peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On Saturday 4 July 1187 he destroyed the army of the kingdom of Jerusalem at Hattin, where the defenders were worn down by thirst and by the smoke of burning scrub.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city capitulated on Friday 2 October, ninety years after the crusaders had taken it, with forty days allowed for ransom and slavery for those left unransomed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation has since shown how completely the countryside emptied: the whole settlement system of Transjordan was abandoned after Hattin, and a new town was later founded at Shawbak on the site of the crusader lordship’s capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Richard the Lionheart beat him at Arsuf on 7 September 1191, denting a reputation for invincibility, but the Third Crusade ended without recovering the holy city, and Saladin died in 1193.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Bahā’ al-Dīn ibn Shaddād, <i>The Life of Saladin</i>, trans. C. R. Conder (London: Committee of the Palestine Exploration Fund, 1897), 1–4, https://archive.org/details/lifesaladin00condgoog. [Open access]","Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>Saladin and the Fall of the Kingdom of Jerusalem</i> (London: G. P. Putnam’s Sons, 1898), 4–8, 96–103, 160–61, 209–10, 229–30, https://archive.org/details/saladinfallofkin00lane. [Open access]","Gleb Zilberstein, Svetlana Zilberstein, and Pier Giorgio Righetti, “Richard the Lionheart and the Ferocious Saladin Face to Face in Arsuf: A Proteomic Study,” <i>Heritage</i> 4, no. 4 (2021): 3382–3401, https://doi.org/10.3390/heritage4040188. [Open access]","Guido Vannini, “Al-Jaya Palace and the New Shawbak Town: A Medieval Frontier and the Return of the Urbanism in the Southern Transjordan,” <i>Studies in Ancient Art and Civilization</i> 24 (2020): 83–98, https://doi.org/10.12797/SAAC.24.2020.24.04. [Open access]","Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>A History of Egypt in the Middle Ages</i> (London: Methuen, 1901), 190–211, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptin00lane. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["person","ruler","history","warfare","islam","egypt","syria","12th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_%28Saladin%29._AH_564-589_%281169-1193_CE%29_%C3%86_Dirham_%2830.1mm%2C_13.28_g%2C_6h%29._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_%28AD_1190-91%29._Sultan_sitting_facing%2C_cross-legged%2C_on_high-backed_throne_%28obverse%29.jpg/1280px-Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_%28Saladin%29._AH_564-589_%281169-1193_CE%29_%C3%86_Dirham_%2830.1mm%2C_13.28_g%2C_6h%29._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_%28AD_1190-91%29._Sultan_sitting_facing%2C_cross-legged%2C_on_high-backed_throne_%28obverse%29.jpg","title":"A dirham struck for Saladin","desc":"A bronze dirham of Saladin dated 586 of the Muslim calendar, 1190 or 1191, showing a ruler seated cross-legged on a high-backed throne and holding an orb, with an Arabic legend around the rim.","credit":"Classical Numismatic Group. CC BY-SA 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_%28Saladin%29._AH_564-589_%281169-1193_CE%29_%C3%86_Dirham_%2830.1mm%2C_13.28_g%2C_6h%29._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_%28AD_1190-91%29._Sultan_sitting_facing%2C_cross-legged%2C_on_high-backed_throne_%28obverse%29.jpg","alt":"A worn dark bronze coin showing a round-faced figure sitting cross-legged with a headdress, holding a small ball, framed by a throne back and a ring of Arabic lettering."}},
{"id":"wh-485","num":485,"category":"The Islamic world","question":"Ruled by soldiers who had entered the country as purchased slaves, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held Egypt and Syria from 1250 until the Ottomans took Cairo in 1517.","questions":["When the Mongols were checked at ʿAyn Jālūt in 1260, the victory belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose regiments had just taken Egypt from their Ayyubid masters.","Two dynasties, the Bahris on a Nile island and the Circassian Burjis in the citadel of Cairo, made up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across two and a half centuries."],"answer":"Mamluk Sultanate","answerText":"Mamluk Sultanate","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1250 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ʿAyn Jālūt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1260 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burjis from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1382 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ottoman conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1517 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Mamluk Sultanate</b> ruled Egypt and Syria from the middle of the 13th century until 1517, governed by men who had arrived in the country as purchased slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Turkish slaves had long been valued in Muslim households, and rulers had come to trust bought foreigners further than their own Arab or Persian subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A boy took the language and religion of his master, studied law and politics, was freed once he was judged fit for office, and then owned mamluks of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prisoners of war were only part of the supply: children of the steppe peoples were sent west for sale as well, sometimes at very high prices, to sultans and emirs alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1250 the slave regiments of the last effective Ayyubid killed his heir, and the throne passed first to his widow Shajar al-Durr and then, through a decade of coups, to the soldiers themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Mongols had taken Baghdad and killed the caliph in 1258, and in 1260 they overran Syria, whereupon the sultan Qutuz answered Hulagu’s demand for submission by executing his envoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 3 September 1260 the two armies met at ʿAyn Jālūt near Beisan, and the Mongol charge broke the Egyptian militia, but the pursuers lost formation and were cut down by the mamluks who had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Baybars, a Kipchak Turk who had fetched a low price in the slave market because of a cataract in one eye, murdered Qutuz on the way home and is called the real founder of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rule by former slaves needed justifying, and it was argued for in two directions at once, to the Mongol khans in diplomatic letters and to the people of Syria through shrines and public memory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was two dynasties rather than one, the Bahris quartered on a Nile island until 1382 and the largely Circassian Burjis in the citadel until 1517, and in its last century Arab and Berber families took over much of the provincial administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Muir, <i>The Mameluke or Slave Dynasty of Egypt, 1260–1517 A.D.</i> (London: Smith, Elder, 1896), 3–4, 15, https://archive.org/details/mameluke00muir. [Open access]","Stanley Lane-Poole, <i>A History of Egypt in the Middle Ages</i> (London: Methuen, 1901), 242–66, https://archive.org/details/historyofegyptin00lane. [Open access]","Anne F. Broadbridge, “Mamluk Legitimacy and the Mongols: The Reigns of Baybars and Qalāwūn,” <i>Mamlūk Studies Review</i> 5 (2001): 91–118, https://knowledge.uchicago.edu/records/e2m4q-8nv05. [Open access]","Anne Troadec, “Baybars and the Cultural Memory of Bilād al-Shām: The Construction of Legitimacy,” <i>Mamlūk Studies Review</i> 18 (2014–15), https://doi.org/10.6082/M1N58JhT. [Open access]","Yossef Rapoport, “The Rise of Provincial Arab Ruling Families in Mamluk Egypt, 1350–1517,” <i>Mamlūk Studies Review</i> 25 (2022), https://doi.org/10.6082/hepf-z386. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","warfare","islam","egypt","syria","13th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Horsemen_wheeling_around%2C_with_a_sword_in_each_one%27s_hand_on_the_horse%27s_back._Dated_773_%281371%29_Mamluk_Egypt_or_Syria.jpg/1280px-Horsemen_wheeling_around%2C_with_a_sword_in_each_one%27s_hand_on_the_horse%27s_back._Dated_773_%281371%29_Mamluk_Egypt_or_Syria.jpg","title":"Two mamluks at sword drill","desc":"Riders wheeling past one another with swords raised, from a manual of horsemanship copied in Mamluk Egypt or Syria in 1371. Manuals like this taught the drill the sultanate’s soldiers were bought and trained for.","credit":"British Library, Add. MS 18866. Photograph CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horsemen_wheeling_around%2C_with_a_sword_in_each_one%27s_hand_on_the_horse%27s_back._Dated_773_%281371%29_Mamluk_Egypt_or_Syria.jpg","alt":"A manuscript painting of two horsemen riding past each other in opposite directions, each with a curved sword lifted overhead, between stylised plants on a plain cream ground."}},
{"id":"wh-486","num":486,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"A label invented by scholars who thought themselves modern, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> names the thousand years European historians put between the ancient world and their own.","questions":["Nine centuries before anyone thought to use the term <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a bishop in 6th-century Gaul wrote a history that counted the years from the creation of the world.","Where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> starts and stops has never been settled: 395, 476 and the migrations all serve for its opening, and 1453, 1492 or the 1640s for its close."],"answer":"Middle Ages","answerText":"Middle Ages","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 1500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Term coined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Standardised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17th century CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Middle Ages</b> is the name European historians give to the thousand years or so between the ancient world and their own, and it was invented long after the period it covers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italian humanists of the 15th century first raised the question of dividing the past into ages, and in the 17th the German historian Christoph Cellarius fixed the three-part scheme of ancient, medieval and modern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word carries a judgement in it: a middle age is what lies between two better ones, and those who coined it took themselves to be ending it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it begins has never been settled — the division of the Roman Empire in 395, the deposing of the last western emperor in 476, or the movement of peoples across the frontier — and its end still less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even 476 is a weak cut, since two legitimate emperors were reigning at the time and only the junior one, in Italy, was set aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The people inside it counted time quite differently, and a bishop writing in 6th-century Gaul reckoned the years from the creation of the first man because his readers were losing hope of an approaching end of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Northumbria a century and a half later a monk wrote by the year of our Lord instead, dating the arrival of the Angles and Saxons in Britain to 449 and counting emperors from Augustus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the closing date 1453 and 1492 have both been used, and Soviet textbooks ran the age on to the English revolution of the 1640s, tying the whole of it to feudalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is now studied with evidence its namers could not have imagined: genomes taken from early medieval cemeteries have been used to measure how far migration lay behind the changes that followed Roman rule in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The label has lasted because it is useful rather than because it is accurate, and both of its ends are best read as conventions rather than as events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Yegane Çağlayan, “XX. Yüzyılda Rusya Tarih Yazımında Avrupa’nın Ortaçağ Tarihinin Dönemselleştirme Algısı,” <i>Ortaçağ Araştırmaları Dergisi</i> 7, no. 1 (2024): 125–44, https://doi.org/10.48120/oad.1380284. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>The Invasion of Europe by the Barbarians</i> (London: Macmillan, 1928), 202–3, https://archive.org/details/invasionofeurope0000jbbu. [Open access]","Gregory of Tours, <i>History of the Franks</i>, trans. Ernest Brehaut (New York: Columbia University Press, 1916), 3, 6–7, https://archive.org/details/historyoffranks01greg. [Open access]","Bede, <i>The Ecclesiastical History of the English Nation</i>, trans. L. C. Jane (London: J. M. Dent, 1903), 29–30, https://archive.org/details/ecclesiasticalhix00bede. [Open access]","Joscha Gretzinger et al., “The Anglo-Saxon Migration and the Formation of the Early English Gene Pool,” <i>Nature</i> 610, no. 7930 (2022): 112–19, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05247-2. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["era","history","concept","europe","historiography","theory"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_f%C3%A9vrier.jpg/1280px-Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_f%C3%A9vrier.jpg","title":"February in a book of hours","desc":"The February page of a calendar painted for a French duke between 1412 and 1416, with the month’s saints and zodiac in the arch above and, below, a snowbound farm where a woman and two companions warm themselves at the fire while others cut wood and drive a laden donkey.","credit":"Musée Condé, Chantilly, MS 65, fol. 2v. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_f%C3%A9vrier.jpg","alt":"A tall manuscript painting: at the top a blue half-arch of stars and calendar lettering, below it a snow-covered farmyard with sheep in a pen, beehives, a dovecote and an open-fronted house where three figures sit by a hearth."}},
{"id":"wh-487","num":487,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Between about 375 and 568 the peoples beyond the Rhine and Danube moved into the empire, a movement historians call the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and still argue about.","questions":["Genomes from a Lombard cemetery and an Alemannic graveyard have been used to test whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved whole peoples or only war bands and their households.","Beginning with the Gothic flight across the Danube and the disaster at Adrianople in 378, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended the Roman order in the west without ever intending to."],"answer":"Migration Period","answerText":"Migration Period","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Convention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 375 – 568 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Adrianople</span><span class=\"dt-v\">378 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rhine crossed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">406 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lombards</span><span class=\"dt-v\">568 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Migration Period</b> is the name given to the movement of peoples into and across the Roman west between roughly the late 4th century and the late 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened when the Huns broke the Gothic power on the Danube and the Visigoths were let over the river into the empire, a settlement that went wrong and ended with the destruction of a Roman army at Adrianople in 378.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Rhine frontier gave way in 406, and over the following century Vandals, Sueves, Burgundians, Franks and others took land inside the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of them were not trying to destroy the empire but to get into it, and their kings ruled as its officers or its heirs rather than against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Gothic history written in the 6th century traced the Goths to an island in the north, from which three ships were said to have carried them under a king called Berig, and gave their kingdom a span of 2,030 years that no record could support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the movements were actually made of is argued about, and ancient genomes have begun to be brought to bear on the question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The 63 burials of two cemeteries linked to the Lombards, who entered Italy from Pannonia in 568, showed each graveyard built around one large pedigree and containing at least two ancestry groups whose funerary customs differed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Niederstotzingen in Germany, the richest Alemannic burial known, five of the eight men tested were close relatives and three were not, two of those showing an affinity to southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In Britain the same methods find a large influx: people buried in eastern England drew up to 76 per cent of their ancestry from the continental North Sea zone, though the proportion varies widely between sites and inside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The picture that results is neither a whole people on the march nor a mere change of fashion, but kindreds and war bands settling in new country and then taking outsiders in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["J. B. Bury, <i>The Invasion of Europe by the Barbarians</i> (London: Macmillan, 1928), 42–60, 91–110, https://archive.org/details/invasionofeurope0000jbbu. [Open access]","Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i>, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 5, 57–58, 71, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","Carlos Eduardo G. Amorim et al., “Understanding 6th-Century Barbarian Social Organization and Migration through Paleogenomics,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 3547, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-018-06024-4. [Open access]","Niall O’Sullivan et al., “Ancient Genome-Wide Analyses Infer Kinship Structure in an Early Medieval Alemannic Graveyard,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 9 (2018): eaao1262, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aao1262. [Open access]","Joscha Gretzinger et al., “The Anglo-Saxon Migration and the Formation of the Early English Gene Pool,” <i>Nature</i> 610, no. 7930 (2022): 112–19, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05247-2. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","migration","europe","genetics","5th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Bracteates_Darum_Nationalmuseet.jpg/1280px-Bracteates_Darum_Nationalmuseet.jpg","title":"Gold pendants of the migration centuries","desc":"Stamped gold discs from a hoard found at Darum in Denmark, made in the 5th and 6th centuries. Ornaments like these travelled far beyond the workshops that made them, which is part of why archaeologists once read them as tracking whole peoples on the move.","credit":"Nationalmuseet, Copenhagen. Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, 2017. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bracteates_Darum_Nationalmuseet.jpg","alt":"Thirteen small round gold pendants and one rectangular gold mount arranged in rows against black, each disc stamped with a faint figure or animal and fitted with a loop for suspension."}},
{"id":"wh-488","num":488,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Sharing a name, a language and a Bible of their own, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended up ruling Italy and Spain as Homoian Christians over a Nicene population.","questions":["A bishop translated the Bible into their tongue in the 4th century, making the first literary text in any Germanic language for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and their kin.","Kept at separate work by Theoderic in Italy and broken at Vouillé in Gaul, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the two Gothic kingdoms carved out of the Roman west."],"answer":"Ostrogoths and Visigoths","answerText":"Ostrogoths and Visigoths","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adrianople</span><span class=\"dt-v\">378 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">410 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Theoderic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">489 – 526 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vouillé</span><span class=\"dt-v\">507 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Ostrogoths and Visigoths</b> were the two great Gothic peoples of late antiquity, described in a 6th-century Gothic history as two peoples of one tribe who had once obeyed a single king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Visigoths were pushed over the Danube by the Huns, destroyed a Roman army at Adrianople in 378, sacked Rome under Alaric in 410 and were then settled in Aquitaine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From Toulouse they took most of Spain and, under Euric, nearly all of Gaul south of the Loire, so that in the 480s a Gothic kingdom of the whole west looked probable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both peoples were Christians of the Homoian kind rather than the Nicene, a difference usually labelled Arian although the teaching of Arius never reached them directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their Bible was the work of the 4th-century bishop Wulfila, who put it into Gothic and in doing so made the first literary text in any Germanic language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Ostrogoths crossed the Julian Alps in August 489 under Theoderic, broke the last resistance of Odovacar three and a half years later, and held Italy until Theoderic died in 526.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His government kept the two nations at separate work, Romans at the arts of peace and Goths under arms, under one king of both and bound to what his official letters call civilitas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That court corresponded across the west, procuring a harper for the king of the Franks and writing to Alaric II and to Clovis I alike to dissuade them from war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They fought anyway, and at Vouillé in 507 the Franks broke Visigothic power in Gaul and left the kingdom to Spain, where it lasted another two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their religion has often been given as the reason neither kingdom absorbed its Roman subjects, and it is in Spain, in the churches and holy places its kings and bishops made, that the change of course after their conversion can best be watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i>, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 57–58, 79, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>The Invasion of Europe by the Barbarians</i> (London: Macmillan, 1928), 91–110, 159–60, 211–15, 250–52, https://archive.org/details/invasionofeurope0000jbbu. [Open access]","Carole Mary Cusack, “Wulfila, the Gothic Bible, and the Mission to the Goths,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 10 (2024): 1177, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15101177. [Open access]","Cassiodorus, <i>The Letters of Cassiodorus</i>, trans. Thomas Hodgkin (London: Henry Frowde, 1886), 24, 168, 193–98, https://archive.org/details/lettersofcassiod00cassuoft. [Open access]","Pablo Poveda Arias, “Making <i>loca sacra</i> in Visigothic Iberia: The Case of Churches,” <i>Religions</i> 14, no. 5 (2023): 664, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel14050664. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["people","history","warfare","europe","italy","spain","5th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Paris_-_Mus%C3%A9e_de_Cluny_-_les_temps_m%C3%A9rovingiens_-_Couronne_votive_-_002.jpg/1280px-Paris_-_Mus%C3%A9e_de_Cluny_-_les_temps_m%C3%A9rovingiens_-_Couronne_votive_-_002.jpg","title":"A Visigothic votive crown","desc":"A gold hoop hung on chains and set with sapphires, emeralds, amethysts, pearls and rock crystal, made in Visigothic Spain about the middle of the 7th century and given to a church rather than worn.","credit":"Musée de Cluny, Paris. Photograph by Thesupermat, 2016. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_-_Mus%C3%A9e_de_Cluny_-_les_temps_m%C3%A9rovingiens_-_Couronne_votive_-_002.jpg","alt":"A wide band of plain gold suspended from four fine chains, studded with alternating pale pearls and coloured stones, with pear-shaped violet pendants hanging from its lower edge."}},
{"id":"wh-489","num":489,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Defeating the Roman ruler of northern Gaul at Soissons in 486, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went on to found the Frankish kingdom and take the Catholic side of a divided Christendom.","questions":["Theoderic sent a harper and a letter urging peace, but in 507 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke the Visigothic kingdom in Gaul at Vouillé and took Aquitaine.","Baptised by bishop Remi after a battle against the Alamanni, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chose the Nicene creed of his Roman subjects rather than the Homoian faith of the Gothic kings."],"answer":"Clovis I","answerText":"Clovis I","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Soissons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">486 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alamanni</span><span class=\"dt-v\">496 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vouillé</span><span class=\"dt-v\">507 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">511 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Clovis I</b> was the Frankish king who turned a small Salian kingdom on the lower Rhine into a power holding most of Gaul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 486 he moved on Soissons and beat Syagrius, the last man to govern the Roman remnant of northern Gaul, who fled to the Visigothic court at Toulouse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A war against the Alamanni that broke out in 496 was the decisive one for the growth of Merovingian power, and he absorbed the other Frankish kingdoms as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He married Clotilda, a Burgundian princess, and was baptised by bishop Remi, whose greeting at the font — worship what you burned, burn what you worshipped — is the best-known sentence in the Frankish histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The choice mattered because the other new kingdoms of the west were Homoian rather than Nicene, so a Catholic Frankish king had the Roman clergy and population of Gaul with him and not against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Theoderic in Italy, who was married to his sister and had given his daughter to Alaric II, tried to hold the peace, procuring a first-rate harper for the Frankish court and writing to both kings to dissuade them from war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It failed, and in the twenty-fifth year of the reign the Franks met the Goths on the plain of Vouillé at the tenth milestone from Poitiers, where the Goths fled and the Franks won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Theoderic did not reach his son-in-law in time, the Burgundians having cut him off, though his generals rescued Arles in the following years and kept Narbonensis for the Visigoths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Gothic history of the next generation gives the same defeat from the other side, as the end of the Visigothic kingdom in Gaul rather than the making of a Frankish one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The emperor sent him the consulship, which his own historian embroiders into the title augustus that he never held, and he died in 511 with Gaul in Frankish hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Gregory of Tours, <i>History of the Franks</i>, trans. Ernest Brehaut (New York: Columbia University Press, 1916), 36–47, https://archive.org/details/historyoffranks01greg. [Open access]","J. B. Bury, <i>The Invasion of Europe by the Barbarians</i> (London: Macmillan, 1928), 214–15, 238–39, 250–53, https://archive.org/details/invasionofeurope0000jbbu. [Open access]","Carole Mary Cusack, “Wulfila, the Gothic Bible, and the Mission to the Goths,” <i>Religions</i> 15, no. 10 (2024): 1177, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel15101177. [Open access]","Cassiodorus, <i>The Letters of Cassiodorus</i>, trans. Thomas Hodgkin (London: Henry Frowde, 1886), 24, 193–98, https://archive.org/details/lettersofcassiod00cassuoft. [Open access]","Jordanes, <i>The Gothic History of Jordanes</i>, trans. Charles Christopher Mierow (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1915), 79, https://archive.org/details/gothichistoryofj00jorduoft. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["person","ruler","history","warfare","europe","france","5th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Bapt%C3%AAme_de_Clovis.jpg/1280px-Bapt%C3%AAme_de_Clovis.jpg","title":"The baptism of Clovis","desc":"The king in the font with a bishop’s hand on his head and a dove above carrying the phial of chrism, painted for a French royal chronicle about 1375, some nine centuries after the event it shows.","credit":"Bibliothèque nationale de France, Grandes Chroniques de France. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bapt%C3%AAme_de_Clovis.jpg","alt":"A manuscript painting inside a lobed frame: a crowned man stands naked in a round stone font while three clerics in mitres lean over him, and a white dove flies down from the corner with a small vessel in its beak."}},
{"id":"wh-490","num":490,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Grown out of the kingdoms that followed Roman Britain and ended at Hastings in 1066, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now studied from cemeteries as much as from chronicles.","questions":["A chronicler of the 8th century dated the arrival of Saxons, Angles and Jutes to 449, which is where the traditional account of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins.","Genomes from medieval cemeteries put the continental share of ancestry in the east at up to 76 per cent, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is measured now."],"answer":"Anglo-Saxon England","answerText":"Anglo-Saxon England","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditionally</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 449 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mission to Kent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">597 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Egbert</span><span class=\"dt-v\">827 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1066 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Anglo-Saxon England</b> is the name for the English kingdoms that grew up after Roman rule ended in Britain and lasted until the Norman conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story told in the 8th century is that a British king hired Saxon troops against raiders from the north, that they came in 449 in three long ships, and that they then turned on their hosts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same account divides the newcomers into Saxons, Angles and Jutes, giving the Jutes to Kent and the Isle of Wight, and names Hengist and Horsa as their first commanders, descended from the god Woden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much of that is history is now tested against burials, and genomes from a cemetery near Cambridge suggest the modern population of eastern England owes about 38 per cent of its ancestry to the incomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A wider study of 460 medieval north-west Europeans found people in eastern England drawing up to 76 per cent of their ancestry from the continental North Sea zone, with wide variation between cemeteries and inside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The same work found that women buried with grave goods were more often of immigrant ancestry than local, while men buried with weapons were as likely to be local as not, so grave goods do not simply mark descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Christianity returned from two directions, and a mission sent from Rome reached Kent in 597 and preached to its king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These were not gentle centuries: a child buried at Edix Hill in Cambridgeshire about 540 carried both plague and an invasive bacterial infection, and the first plague pandemic reached England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Politically the land held several kingdoms at once, and the chronicle keeps a list of the kings who held a wider overlordship, from Ælle of the South Saxons to Egbert of Wessex, who took Mercia and everything south of the Humber in 827.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The last of the line, Harold, was beaten in 1066 by a Norman duke who then waited at Hastings to see whether the country would submit, and it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Bede, <i>The Ecclesiastical History of the English Nation</i>, trans. L. C. Jane (London: J. M. Dent, 1903), 29–30, 45–46, https://archive.org/details/ecclesiasticalhix00bede. [Open access]","<i>The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</i>, trans. J. A. Giles (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1914), 62, 143–46, https://archive.org/details/anglosaxonchroni00gile. [Open access]","Joscha Gretzinger et al., “The Anglo-Saxon Migration and the Formation of the Early English Gene Pool,” <i>Nature</i> 610, no. 7930 (2022): 112–19, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-05247-2. [Open access]","Stephan Schiffels et al., “Iron Age and Anglo-Saxon Genomes from East England Reveal British Migration History,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 7 (2016): 10408, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms10408. [Open access]","Meriam Guellil et al., “An Invasive <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i> Serotype b Infection in an Anglo-Saxon Plague Victim,” <i>Genome Biology</i> 23 (2022): 22, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-021-02580-z. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","migration","europe","england","genetics","5th century"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Museo_Brit%C3%A1nico%2C_Londres%2C_Inglaterra%2C_2022-11-26%2C_DD_46.jpg/1280px-Museo_Brit%C3%A1nico%2C_Londres%2C_Inglaterra%2C_2022-11-26%2C_DD_46.jpg","title":"The Sutton Hoo helmet","desc":"The helmet from a ship burial in Suffolk of about 625, reassembled from hundreds of fragments, with its iron face mask, moustache and tinned bronze panels of warriors and interlace.","credit":"British Museum, London. Photograph by Diego Delso, 2022. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museo_Brit%C3%A1nico%2C_Londres%2C_Inglaterra%2C_2022-11-26%2C_DD_46.jpg","alt":"A rusted iron helmet with a full face mask, showing eye holes, a nose and moustache formed as a bird in flight, hinged cheek pieces and a domed cap patterned with panels, many parts filled in with plain reddish material."}},
{"id":"wh-491","num":491,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"A ship 27 metres (89 feet) long was hauled up from the river Deben and buried under a mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with a helmet, gold and Byzantine silver inside it.","questions":["Ten silver bowls made in the eastern Roman empire about 600 were stacked near the head of an empty body-space in the chamber at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in Suffolk.","A whetstone carved with faces, a purse of Frankish gold coins and shoulder clasps of garnet lay in the chamber under the largest mound at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in Suffolk."],"answer":"Sutton Hoo","answerText":"Sutton Hoo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ship burial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 625 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rædwald died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">624 or 625 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wuffing kings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th – 8th century</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sutton Hoo</b> is a cemetery of burial mounds above the river Deben in Suffolk, and the largest of them held a ship about 27 metres (89 feet) long with a chamber built amidships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The timbers had rotted entirely away, leaving a stain in the sand with the iron rivets still standing in the lines along which they had been driven. No body was found, and whether the acid soil dissolved one or none was ever laid there is still open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chamber held a helmet, a sword, a coat of mail, shoulder clasps of gold and garnet, a great buckle, a whetstone carved with faces and ten silver bowls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Snape, a few miles up the coast, and this mound are the two oldest proper ship graves known anywhere, and the nearest thing to them in English writing is the funeral that opens <i>Beowulf</i>, where treasure is heaped on a dead king and the boat given to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The goods say how far the man buried with them could reach: the bowls were made in the eastern provinces of the Byzantine empire about 600, the coins came from Merovingian mints and the textiles from the Levant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Black lumps in the chamber, catalogued for decades as pine tar off the ship, were shown by chemical analysis in 2016 to be bitumen from the Middle East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is the first material evidence for Middle Eastern bitumen reaching Britain, and it counts among the grave goods rather than among the ship's fittings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The burial is usually given to Rædwald of the East Angles, who died in 624 or 625 and whom Bede counted the fourth English ruler to hold an overlordship south of the Humber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bede says the same king was baptised in Kent and afterwards kept in one temple an altar for the Christian sacrifice and a smaller one for offerings to devils, which is the sort of half-turned court this grave seems to belong to, though nothing in it names him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Bede, <i>The Ecclesiastical History of the English Nation</i>, trans. L. C. Jane (London: J. M. Dent, 1903), 96–97, 125–26, https://archive.org/details/ecclesiasticalhix00bede. [Open access]","Michael Bintley, “The Byzantine Silver Bowls in the Sutton Hoo Ship Burial and Tree-Worship in Anglo-Saxon England,” <i>Papers from the Institute of Archaeology</i> 21, no. 1 (2011): 34–45, https://doi.org/10.5334/pia.378. [Open access]","Pauline Burger et al., “Identification, Geochemical Characterisation and Significance of Bitumen among the Grave Goods of the 7th Century Mound 1 Ship-Burial at Sutton Hoo, Suffolk,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 12 (2016): e0166276, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0166276. [Open access]","Niels Bonde and Frans-Arne Stylegar, “Between Sutton Hoo and Oseberg – Dendrochronology and the Origins of the Ship Burial Tradition,” <i>Danish Journal of Archaeology</i> 5, no. 1–2 (2016): 19–33, https://doi.org/10.1080/21662282.2016.1245885. [Open access]","<i>Beowulf</i>, trans. Francis Barton Gummere, in <i>The Oldest English Epic</i> (New York: Macmillan, 1909), lines 26–52, https://archive.org/details/TheOldestEnglishEpicGummere. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","history","archaeology","europe","england","object"],"quote":{"book":"beowulf","n":"0","cite":"Prelude, lines 26–52","text":"<p>Then they bore him over to ocean’s billow, loving clansmen, as late he charged them, while wielded words the winsome Scyld, the leader belovéd who long had ruled.</p><p>… In the roadstead rocked a ring-dight vessel, ice-flecked, outbound, atheling’s barge: there laid they down their darling lord on the breast of the boat, the breaker-of-rings, by the mast the mighty one. Many a treasure fetched from far was freighted with him.</p><p>… High o’er his head they hoist the standard, a gold-wove banner; let billows take him, gave him to ocean. Grave were their spirits, mournful their mood. No man is able to say in sooth, no son of the halls, no hero ’neath heaven,—who harbored that freight!</p>"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Anglo_Saxon_burial_mound_at_Sutton_Hoo_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5895216.jpg/1280px-Anglo_Saxon_burial_mound_at_Sutton_Hoo_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5895216.jpg","title":"A mound at Sutton Hoo","desc":"One of the grassed burial mounds on the ridge above the river Deben in Suffolk, with an interpretation board at its foot. The largest of the group covered a buried ship and the chamber that held the treasure.","credit":"Photograph by Neil Theasby, 2018, for Geograph Britain and Ireland. CC BY-SA 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anglo_Saxon_burial_mound_at_Sutton_Hoo_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5895216.jpg","alt":"A low rounded grassy mound in rough pasture under a grey sky, with a wooden bench and a small red sign standing in front of it and trees along the skyline behind."}},
{"id":"wh-492","num":492,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Leaving ordinary society for a life of prayer, poverty and obedience, alone in a cell or together under an abbot, is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has meant since the 3rd century.","questions":["The Egyptian desert filled with cells in the 4th century, and the Latin west borrowed the practice through Basil, Jerome and Cassian, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Europe.","Because a life of reading and manual work both needs books and makes them, houses of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the libraries and schools of the early medieval west almost by accident."],"answer":"Christian monasticism","answerText":"Christian monasticism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins in Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cenobitic rules</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bede finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">731 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Christian monasticism</b> is the practice of leaving ordinary society for a life ruled by prayer, poverty and obedience, kept either alone or in a community under a superior. It grew up in Egypt, and the life of Antony written soon after his death says that when he began there were not yet many monasteries and no monk at all knew the far desert.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ascetics then kept their discipline on the edge of their own villages; by the end of Antony’s life, the same book says, cells had risen even in the mountains and the desert had been colonised by monks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two shapes grew side by side, the hermit alone in his cell and the community under an abbot, and the Rule of Benedict opens by sorting monks into those two kinds and two others it despises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Latin west took the practice over through writers who translated or adapted the eastern material, among them Basil in Rufinus’ version, Augustine, Jerome and Cassian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Rules written for whole communities appear later than was long thought, in the 6th century rather than the 5th, and what marks them off is how much authority they hand the abbot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The daily round was divided between the divine office, set hours of reading and manual work, with seniors sent about the house to see that nobody was idle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cassiodorus wrote a manual for his own house in the same century that set the seven liberal arts inside that programme and gave instructions for the copying of texts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Monasteries in this period increasingly became schools in their own right, and literacy, which had belonged to the Roman elite, reached monks and nuns from every rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bede says of himself that he was born in the territory of the monastery of Wearmouth and Jarrow, was given to it at seven years old to be taught by Abbot Benedict, and spent the rest of his life there at the scriptures and the singing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Athanasius, <i>Life of Antony</i>, §§ 3, 14, trans. H. Ellershaw, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd ser., vol. 4, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace (New York: Christian Literature Company, 1892), https://archive.org/details/selectlibraryofn04schauoft. [Open access]","Marilyn Dunn, “Community, Authority, Rule: Re-Thinking Normative Texts in Early Western Monasticism,” <i>Religions</i> 17, no. 1 (2025): 11, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel17010011. [Open access]","<i>The Rule of Our Most Holy Father St. Benedict, Patriarch of Monks</i>, ed. one of the Benedictine Fathers of St Michael’s (London: R. Washbourne, 1875), chaps. 1, 48, https://archive.org/details/rulestbenedictf00benegoog. [Open access]","Bede, <i>The Ecclesiastical History of the English Nation</i>, trans. L. C. Jane (London: J. M. Dent, 1903), bk. 5, chap. 24, https://archive.org/details/ecclesiasticalhix00bede. [Open access]","Marcus C. C. Rynningsjö Carlsén, “The Purposes and Authority of Secular Education in the Monastic Rule of Cassiodorus,” <i>Religions</i> 17, no. 2 (2026): 229, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel17020229. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","history","religion","europe","concept"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Saint_Bishoy_Monk_at_gate.jpg/1280px-Saint_Bishoy_Monk_at_gate.jpg","title":"A monk at the gate","desc":"A monk unlocking a gate in the Monastery of Saint Bishoy at Wadi el Natrun in Egypt, one of the desert houses that grew out of the movement Antony began.","credit":"Photograph by Mark Fischer, 2016. CC BY-SA 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Bishoy_Monk_at_gate.jpg","alt":"A man in a long black habit and cap seen from behind, opening a tall latticed wooden door set in an arch in a plain yellow wall."}},
{"id":"wh-493","num":493,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"A prologue and 73 chapters calling the monastery a school of the service of God make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the shortest of the rules and the one the west settled on.","questions":["Stability, amendment of manners and obedience are the three things a monk promises in his own handwriting under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which then sets the hours of his day.","Neither wine nor meat is forbidden outright by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the quantity and the hour of every meal are fixed, and idleness is policed as the enemy of the soul."],"answer":"Rule of Saint Benedict","answerText":"Rule of Saint Benedict","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Benedict born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 480 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gregory writes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 593 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Rule of Saint Benedict</b> is a short manual for running a monastery, written in Italy in the 6th century and made up of a prologue and 73 chapters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It calls the house a school of the service of God and promises that nothing in it will be ordained too rigorous or burdensome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first chapter divides monks into four kinds — those living in community under a rule and an abbot, hermits who have served a long apprenticeship first, and two sorts it will not discuss — and then legislates for the first alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The abbot is said to hold the place of Christ in the monastery, and the Rule comes back again and again to what kind of man he must be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A monk entering promises stability, amendment of manners and obedience, writes the promise out in his own hand and lays it on the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The day is divided between the divine office, set hours of reading and manual work, with seniors sent about to see that nobody is idle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Food is measured rather than forbidden: no dish or drink is banned outright, not even wine or meat, but the quantity and the hour are fixed and have to be justified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Guests are to be received as Christ, and the tools and goods of the house are to be treated with the reverence given to the vessels of the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its last chapter says outright that the Rule is a beginning and not the height of perfection, and sends the reader on to the eastern fathers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gregory the Great, writing in 593, calls it a rule for monks excellent for its discretion, and modern argument has moved the first western rules for communities from the 5th century into the 6th, with the authority handed to the abbot as the thing that changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Rule of Our Most Holy Father St. Benedict, Patriarch of Monks</i>, ed. one of the Benedictine Fathers of St Michael’s (London: R. Washbourne, 1875), prologue and chaps. 1–2, 31, 48, 53, 58, 73, https://archive.org/details/rulestbenedictf00benegoog. [Open access]","Gregory the Great, <i>The Dialogues of Saint Gregory the Great</i>, bk. 2, chap. 36, trans. P. W., ed. Edmund G. Gardner (London: Philip Lee Warner, 1911), https://archive.org/details/dialoguesofsaint00greg. [Open access]","Marilyn Dunn, “Community, Authority, Rule: Re-Thinking Normative Texts in Early Western Monasticism,” <i>Religions</i> 17, no. 1 (2025): 11, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel17010011. [Open access]","Gabriele Archetti, “Nourishment and Asceticism: The Measure of Food,” <i>Crkva u Svijetu</i> 58, suppl. 1 (2023): 349–70, https://hrcak.srce.hr/310259. [Open access]","Grzegorz Chojnacki, “Care for the Work of Creation in the Rule of Saint Benedict and in the Documents of the Cistercian Order: A Historical-Systematic Overview,” <i>Religions</i> 17, no. 8 (2026): 988, https://doi.org/10.3390/rel17080988. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["text","history","religion","europe","italy","practice"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Codex_Sangallensis%2C_Regula_S_Benedicti%2C_exh._Benedictines_NG_Prague%2C_150730.jpg/1280px-Codex_Sangallensis%2C_Regula_S_Benedicti%2C_exh._Benedictines_NG_Prague%2C_150730.jpg","title":"An early copy of the Rule","desc":"A manuscript of the Rule of Saint Benedict made at Reichenau between 800 and 833, exhibited open at two pages of Latin with a large red initial and marginal notes added in the 16th century.","credit":"Stiftsbibliothek St Gallen, Cod. 914. Photograph by Zde, 2015. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Sangallensis%2C_Regula_S_Benedicti%2C_exh._Benedictines_NG_Prague%2C_150730.jpg","alt":"An old book lying open in a display case, its thick pages covered in small brown Latin script, with several lines picked out in red capitals and a large red letter beginning a paragraph."}},
{"id":"wh-494","num":494,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Claiming succession from the apostle Peter, the bishop of Rome turned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into an office that could crown an emperor, judge a king and be defied by both.","questions":["A forged grant said to come from Constantine gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its claim to the west, and land from Frankish kings gave it a state to match.","Writing that God set two lights in the sky as He set two dignities on earth, an 11th-century holder of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> claimed he could depose a king."],"answer":"papacy","answerText":"papacy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Bishops of Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 1st century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gregory elected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">590 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Charles crowned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gregory VII died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1085 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>papacy</b> is the office of the bishop of Rome, who claims to succeed the apostle Peter and who in the medieval west came to claim authority over the whole church and over kings besides. Its own record of itself is the Liber Pontificalis, a series of papal lives begun in the 6th or 7th century by a member of the papal court and carried on for each pope until the 15th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was taken through the Middle Ages as the oldest and most trustworthy history of the office, and it carries apocryphal councils and untrustworthy stories beside genuine documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gregory the Great sent a mission to the English in 596, its leader reached Kent the next year, and the pope answered his questions about running a new church with a set of written rulings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three years into the same pontificate he wrote four books of dialogues on Italian holy men, working as a teacher of monks as much as a governor of churches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The 8th century changed the office materially: a Frankish alliance gave the popes land in Italy, and it was probably then that the Donation of Constantine was forged, pretending that the first Christian emperor had granted the west to Pope Sylvester.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On Christmas Day 800 Leo III crowned Charles in Rome, and Charles is said to have disliked the title so much that he would not have entered the church had he foreseen the pope's design.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether that reluctance was real or a courtier's gloss, the act left emperor-making in papal hands, and the two powers argued for four centuries about what it had meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gregory VII pressed the claim furthest, writing to William the Conqueror that God had set the apostolic and royal dignities as He set the sun and moon, so the giver of a crown might take it away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He died at Salerno in 1085 with the quarrel unfinished, and the struggle over who invested bishops outlasted the men who began it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Book of the Popes (Liber Pontificalis)</i>, trans. Louise Ropes Loomis (New York: Columbia University Press, 1916), ix–xvii, https://archive.org/details/bookofpopesliber00loom. [Open access]","Bede, <i>The Ecclesiastical History of the English Nation</i>, trans. L. C. Jane (London: J. M. Dent, 1903), bk. 1, chaps. 23, 25, 27, https://archive.org/details/ecclesiasticalhix00bede. [Open access]","James Bryce, <i>The Holy Roman Empire</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 43–49, 155–60, https://archive.org/details/theholyromanemp00bryciala. [Open access]","Einhard, <i>The Life of the Emperor Charles</i>, chap. 28, in <i>Early Lives of Charlemagne</i>, trans. A. J. Grant (London: Chatto and Windus, 1922), 43, https://archive.org/details/earlylivesofchar00einhiala. [Open access]","Gregory the Great, <i>The Dialogues of Saint Gregory the Great</i>, trans. P. W., ed. Edmund G. Gardner (London: Philip Lee Warner, 1911), introduction, https://archive.org/details/dialoguesofsaint00greg. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["institution","history","religion","europe","italy","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Rome_-_Basilica_di_San_Giovanni_in_Laterano_-_facade.jpg/1280px-Rome_-_Basilica_di_San_Giovanni_in_Laterano_-_facade.jpg","title":"The pope's own cathedral","desc":"The front of the basilica of Saint John Lateran in Rome, the cathedral church of the bishop of Rome and the seat of the papacy through most of the Middle Ages, rebuilt with this facade in the 18th century.","credit":"Photograph by Patrik Kunec, 2019. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rome_-_Basilica_di_San_Giovanni_in_Laterano_-_facade.jpg","alt":"A wide pale stone church front of two storeys with tall columns and arched openings, crowned by a balustrade carrying fifteen large statues against a blue morning sky."}},
{"id":"wh-495","num":495,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Wars over 47 years almost doubled the Frankish kingdom under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who took Italy from the Lombards and forced the Saxons to give up their gods.","questions":["Tablets were kept under the pillow of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that he could practise forming letters in spare moments, but he had begun too late and never learned to write.","The pope set a crown on the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Rome on Christmas Day 800, an act that made emperors a papal gift and was argued over for four centuries."],"answer":"Charlemagne","answerText":"Charlemagne","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Frankish king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">768 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Takes Lombardy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">774 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crowned emperor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">814 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Charlemagne</b> was king of the Franks from 768 and emperor from 800, and at his death he ruled most of Latin Europe outside Britain, Muslim Spain and the far south of Italy. His family had climbed through the office of mayor of the palace, and he was crowned as a boy at Saint-Denis beside his brother when a pope anointed their father Pippin king of the Franks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Einhard, who served at his court, says he reigned 47 years and that his wars so enlarged an already great kingdom that the additions almost doubled it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took the Lombard kingdom in Italy, sent its king into exile, and handed the authority the Lombards had held back to the bishop of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Saxon war ran a generation and Einhard calls it the longest and cruellest the Franks ever fought; a rearguard cut to pieces in the Pyrenees on the way home from Spain was a small reverse that later became the <i>Song of Roland</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On Christmas Day 800 the pope crowned him in Rome, and he is said to have disliked the title so much that he would not have gone into the church that day had he known what was intended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Aachen he built a great church and gathered scholars about him, among them Peter of Pisa and Alcuin of York, whom Einhard calls the most learned man of his time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He learned Latin well enough to speak it like his own tongue and could follow Greek, worked at rhetoric, dialectic, astronomy and reckoning, and kept tablets under his pillow to practise forming letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He never managed to write, the attempt having been begun too late in life, and yet on campaign against the Saxons in 798 he was corresponding with Alcuin about the backwards motion of Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within seventy years of his death a monk of Saint Gall was writing him up out of stories rather than documents, which is how fast legend closed over him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Einhard, <i>The Life of the Emperor Charles</i>, chaps. 6–7, 15, 25–26, 28, in <i>Early Lives of Charlemagne</i>, trans. A. J. Grant (London: Chatto and Windus, 1922), 18–20, 33–34, 40–43, https://archive.org/details/earlylivesofchar00einhiala. [Open access]","A. J. Grant, introduction and notes to <i>Early Lives of Charlemagne</i> (London: Chatto and Windus, 1922), v–vii, 163, https://archive.org/details/earlylivesofchar00einhiala. [Open access]","Thomas Hodgkin, <i>The Life of Charlemagne</i> (New York: A. L. Burt, 1909), 92–93, 304–5, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcharlemagn00hodg. [Open access]","James Bryce, <i>The Holy Roman Empire</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 43–53, https://archive.org/details/theholyromanemp00bryciala. [Open access]","Tom McLeish and Mary Garrison, “Reversals in Wartime: Alcuin and Charlemagne Discuss Retrograde Motion,” <i>Interfaces: A Journal of Medieval European Literatures</i> 8 (2021): 14–53, https://doi.org/10.54103/interfaces-08-03. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["person","ruler","history","warfare","europe","france"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Aachen_Cathedral_Karlsthron_02-edit.jpg/1280px-Aachen_Cathedral_Karlsthron_02-edit.jpg","title":"Charlemagne's throne at Aachen","desc":"The stone seat in the upper gallery of the chapel Charles built at Aachen, made of plain marble slabs reached by six steps. Later kings of Germany were enthroned on it for six centuries.","credit":"Photograph by Arnoldius, 2008, edited by MenkinAlRire. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aachen_Cathedral_Karlsthron_02-edit.jpg","alt":"A plain marble chair with high side slabs standing on a stepped platform inside a vaulted chamber with black and white banded arches."}},
{"id":"wh-496","num":496,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"The clear minuscule hand behind the small letters we still write was one product of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the revival of learning run from Charlemagne's court at Aachen.","questions":["Most surviving manuscripts of the Latin classics were copied in the 9th century, which is the plainest debt the modern world owes to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its scriptoria.","Nicknames out of scripture and antiquity went round the court where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began: the king was called David, and the scholar fetched from York signed himself Flaccus."],"answer":"Carolingian Renaissance","answerText":"Carolingian Renaissance","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revival</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 8th – 9th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Letters on Mars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">798 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alcuin died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">804 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Carolingian Renaissance</b> is the name given to a revival of Latin learning in the Frankish empire from the late 8th century, driven from a royal court rather than from the cities. Charles gathered teachers at Aachen, among them Peter of Pisa for grammar and Alcuin of York, whom his biographer calls the most learned man of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The circle worked on easy terms and gave one another nicknames out of scripture and antiquity: the king was David, Alcuin signed himself Flaccus, and Einhard was Bezaleel after the craftsman of the Tabernacle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The king studied rhetoric, dialectic, astronomy and reckoning under them, and kept tablets under his pillow to practise letters he never mastered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of this was ornament: correct texts were wanted for worship and for law, and one of the royal capitularies puts the state of the clergy's writing before them as a matter of duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest result that survives is the hand itself, a clear minuscule that replaced the sprawling characters then in use and became the basis of the small letters of the modern alphabet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many of the best manuscripts of the classical Latin authors date from the 9th century, and they exist because of the copying this revival set going.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Alcuin's letters show both what the learning was for and what it was against: he wrote to kings about their conduct, to bishops about their silence, and to monks about their reading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The correspondence went on even at war, and in 798, while Charles was campaigning against the Saxons, the two men exchanged letters about the backwards motion of Mars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That exchange is the longest discussion of retrograde motion in the Latin west up to its date, which measures both the ambition of the revival and how far it still had to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Einhard, <i>The Life of the Emperor Charles</i>, chap. 25, in <i>Early Lives of Charlemagne</i>, trans. A. J. Grant (London: Chatto and Windus, 1922), 40–41, https://archive.org/details/earlylivesofchar00einhiala. [Open access]","A. J. Grant, introduction to <i>Early Lives of Charlemagne</i> (London: Chatto and Windus, 1922), v–vi, https://archive.org/details/earlylivesofchar00einhiala. [Open access]","G. F. Browne, <i>Alcuin of York</i> (London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1908), 23–24, 108–10, https://archive.org/details/alcuinofyorklect00browuoft. [Open access]","Thomas Hodgkin, <i>The Life of Charlemagne</i> (New York: A. L. Burt, 1909), 312–13, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcharlemagn00hodg. [Open access]","Tom McLeish and Mary Garrison, “Reversals in Wartime: Alcuin and Charlemagne Discuss Retrograde Motion,” <i>Interfaces: A Journal of Medieval European Literatures</i> 8 (2021): 14–53, https://doi.org/10.54103/interfaces-08-03. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","art","europe","france","text"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Minuscule_caroline.jpg/1280px-Minuscule_caroline.jpg","title":"A page in Carolingian minuscule","desc":"A leaf of a Carolingian gospel book carrying part of Luke's account of the Passion, written in the clear rounded hand that spread from the Frankish scriptoria and lies behind the small letters still in use.","credit":"British Library, Add. MS 11848, fol. 160v. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minuscule_caroline.jpg","alt":"A parchment page filled with evenly spaced brown script in short paragraphs, several lines written in red, with a narrow column of smaller notes down the left margin."}},
{"id":"wh-497","num":497,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"German princes elected him and the pope crowned him in Italy, so the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered to two constituencies that hardly ever wanted the same thing.","questions":["Seven princes were fixed as the electors by a bull of 1356, which settled how the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was chosen after generations of argument over the procedure.","Hundreds of principalities, free cities and bishoprics, each with rights of its own, made up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its later centuries, held together by a diet rather than a government."],"answer":"Holy Roman Empire","answerText":"Holy Roman Empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Charles crowned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Otto crowned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">962 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Golden Bull</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1356 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abdication</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1806 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Holy Roman Empire</b> was a political order in central Europe that claimed to carry on the Roman empire in the west, and it lasted from the 10th century until 1806. Where to start it is disputed: some date it from the crowning of Charles in Rome on Christmas Day 800, an act his own biographer says he resented, and others from the coronation of Otto the Great in 962, the Carolingian empire having fallen apart in 888.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The emperor was a German king first, chosen by German princes, and only afterwards crowned in Italy by the pope, so the office rested on two constituencies that rarely agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the crown lay in papal hands the two powers quarrelled for two centuries, most bitterly between Henry IV and Gregory VII from 1076.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The office also carried a duty of missionary war, and the German advance east into Slav lands was understood as part of what an emperor was for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Golden Bull of 1356 fixed the election in a college of seven princes and settled a procedure that had been argued over for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After that the empire was less a state than a frame: hundreds of principalities, cities and bishoprics, each with rights of its own, held together by a diet and a court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bryce judged that it might well have ended with the last of the Hohenstaufen in 1250, and that it lived on six hundred years more chiefly because the Germans had grown fond of the style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even late on it remained the fountain of European titles, and Russia had to negotiate for years before the empire would recognise its rulers as emperors in 1745 and 1746.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Francis II laid the title down in 1806 under pressure from Napoleon, and Voltaire's line that it was neither holy nor Roman nor an empire has outlasted most of the arguments made for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Bryce, <i>The Holy Roman Empire</i> (London: Macmillan, 1897), 49–53, 122–27, 153–60, 211–20, 230–33, 353–56, https://archive.org/details/theholyromanemp00bryciala. [Open access]","Einhard, <i>The Life of the Emperor Charles</i>, chap. 28, in <i>Early Lives of Charlemagne</i>, trans. A. J. Grant (London: Chatto and Windus, 1922), 43, https://archive.org/details/earlylivesofchar00einhiala. [Open access]","Thomas Hodgkin, <i>The Life of Charlemagne</i> (New York: A. L. Burt, 1909), 268–75, https://archive.org/details/lifeofcharlemagn00hodg. [Open access]","Herbert Fisher, <i>The Medieval Empire</i>, vol. 2 (London: Macmillan, 1898), 1–20, https://archive.org/details/medievalempire02fishuoft. [Open access]","M. A. Petrova, “Hermann Karl von Keyserlingk and the Recognition of the Russian Imperial Title by the Holy Roman Empire in 1745–1746,” <i>Vestnik MGIMO-Universiteta</i> 14, no. 6 (2021): 89–109, https://doi.org/10.24833/2071-8160-2021-6-81-89-109. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","politics","europe","germany","institution"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Weltliche_Schatzkammer_Wien_white_background.jpg/1280px-Weltliche_Schatzkammer_Wien_white_background.jpg","title":"The imperial crown","desc":"The crown used at the coronation of the German kings and emperors, an octagon of gold plates set with pearls and unpolished stones, with an arch and a jewelled cross added later. It is kept in Vienna.","credit":"Kaiserliche Schatzkammer, Vienna. Photograph by Gryffindor. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weltliche_Schatzkammer_Wien_white_background.jpg","alt":"A tall gold crown of eight hinged plates thick with pearls, emeralds, sapphires and enamel pictures, carrying a single arch across the top and a gemmed cross at the front, standing on red cloth."}},
{"id":"wh-498","num":498,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Raiding, trading and settling across Europe and the north Atlantic between about 750 and 1050 made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the most travelled people of early medieval Europe.","questions":["Everything written about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from the people they attacked or from Icelanders three centuries later, so the cruelty is inflated and the kings are dressed up.","Ancient genomes show ancestry moving into Scandinavia by about 800, so the age of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was traffic in both directions and not a simple outward explosion."],"answer":"Vikings","answerText":"Vikings","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Viking Age</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 750 – 1050 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lindisfarne</span><span class=\"dt-v\">793 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last invasion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1066 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Vikings</b> is the name English usage gives to the Scandinavians who raided, traded and settled across Europe and the north Atlantic roughly between 750 and 1050. The word names an activity rather than a nation — most Scandinavians farmed — and the English chronicle that records the attacks calls them heathen men and not Vikings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything written about them comes either from the people they attacked or from Icelanders three centuries later, so the chronicles inflate the cruelty and the sagas dress up long-dead kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first recorded raid on England fell on the church at Lindisfarne in 793, and a scholar writing from the Frankish court said nobody had supposed such an attack from the sea was possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within a lifetime the raids had become invasions: a great heathen army wintered in England, carved up Northumbria and drove kings out of East Anglia and Mercia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> They settled as well as raided, and the same armies that spent winters in England also fought in Frankland, on the Meuse and along the Seine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ancient genomes now show traffic into Scandinavia as well as out of it: by about 800 a large share of Viking Age individuals carried ancestry from central European groups absent from the earlier Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the age was not a simple outward explosion from a settled homeland but a period of movement in both directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their gods, their ships and their violence were all written down afterwards by Christians, which is why the picture has needed so much correcting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The age closes in the 11th century as the Scandinavian kingdoms became Christian monarchies, and the last serious northern invasion of England failed in 1066.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</i>, trans. J. A. Giles (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1914), 62–64, 143–46, https://archive.org/details/anglosaxonchroni00gile. [Open access]","Leo Speidel et al., “High-Resolution Genomic History of Early Medieval Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 637, no. 8044 (2025): 118–26, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08275-2. [Open access]","G. F. Browne, <i>Alcuin of York</i> (London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1908), 126–30, https://archive.org/details/alcuinofyorklect00browuoft. [Open access]","Beñat Elortza Larrea, “Archaic Martial Traditions in High Medieval Scandinavia: A Glimpse of Viking Age Warfare?,” <i>Viking</i> 84, no. 1 (2021): 105–26, https://doi.org/10.5617/viking.9048. [Open access]","Asser, <i>Asser’s Life of King Alfred</i>, trans. Albert S. Cook (Boston: Ginn, 1906), 25–31, https://archive.org/details/asserslifeofking00asseiala. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["people","history","warfare","migration","europe","genetics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/NO-vikingskiphuset-oseberg-01.jpg/1280px-NO-vikingskiphuset-oseberg-01.jpg","title":"The Oseberg ship","desc":"The oak ship from a burial mound at Oseberg in Norway, dug up in 1904 and reassembled almost whole. Ships like it carried raiders and traders across the North Sea and up the rivers of Europe.","credit":"Vikingskipshuset, Oslo. Photograph by Balou46, 2015. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NO-vikingskiphuset-oseberg-01.jpg","alt":"A long dark wooden ship with a high curving prow standing on supports in a white vaulted hall, its overlapping planks and single mast socket clearly visible."}},
{"id":"wh-499","num":499,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Whirlwinds, lightnings and fiery dragons in the air are what the chronicle puts before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the attack that opened the Viking age in Britain.","questions":["Cuthbert’s island house, founded in 635 and holding the most venerated relics in Britain, was destroyed by heathen men in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 793.","A letter from the Frankish court said no such horror had appeared in Britain in 350 years, and that no attack from the sea like the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been thought possible."],"answer":"Lindisfarne raid","answerText":"Lindisfarne raid","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Raid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">793 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wearmouth sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">794 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lindisfarne raid</b> is the attack on the island monastery off the Northumbrian coast that the English chronicle records under the year 793. The house had been founded in 635, when King Oswald asked the Scots for a bishop and gave Aidan a see on the island; it held the relics of Cuthbert and was the most venerable place in Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chronicle puts dire forewarnings first — whirlwinds, lightnings and fiery dragons seen flying in the air, then a famine — and only afterwards the ravaging of heathen men, who destroyed God’s church through rapine and slaughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It dates the attack to the sixth of the ides of January, which is 8 January, though 19th-century editors argued for 8 June; both surviving manuscripts that name a month say January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Alcuin, an Englishman then at the Frankish court, wrote to King Æthelred that in nearly 350 years no such horror had appeared in Britain, and that nobody had supposed such an attack from the sea was possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He wrote separately to the bishop and monks of Lindisfarne, and again the next year when Wearmouth and Jarrow were plundered in their turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the same letter he tells the king that the calamity is a punishment, reading it through Gildas as the Britons’ ruin had been read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That reading matters, because the churchmen who wrote the raid down had reasons of their own for making it a portent as well as an event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is treated as the opening of the Viking Age in Britain, though the age itself is usually dated from about 750 and had begun elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What made it new was not cruelty but reach, which is exactly Alcuin’s point: an attack from that quarter had not been thought possible at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</i>, trans. J. A. Giles (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1914), 62–63, https://archive.org/details/anglosaxonchroni00gile. [Open access]","Leo Speidel et al., “High-Resolution Genomic History of Early Medieval Europe,” <i>Nature</i> 637, no. 8044 (2025): 118–26, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08275-2. [Open access]","G. F. Browne, <i>Alcuin of York</i> (London: Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge, 1908), 122–33, https://archive.org/details/alcuinofyorklect00browuoft. [Open access]","Beñat Elortza Larrea, “Archaic Martial Traditions in High Medieval Scandinavia: A Glimpse of Viking Age Warfare?,” <i>Viking</i> 84, no. 1 (2021): 105–26, https://doi.org/10.5617/viking.9048. [Open access]","Bede, <i>The Ecclesiastical History of the English Nation</i>, trans. L. C. Jane (London: J. M. Dent, 1903), bk. 3, chap. 3, https://archive.org/details/ecclesiasticalhix00bede. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","religion","europe","england","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Holy_Island%2C_Lindisfarne_Priory_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8137762.jpg/1280px-Holy_Island%2C_Lindisfarne_Priory_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8137762.jpg","title":"Lindisfarne Priory","desc":"The ruins on Holy Island, standing over the site of the monastery Aidan founded in 635 and the heathen men destroyed in 793. The stonework above ground is from the Norman priory that replaced it.","credit":"Photograph by Michael Garlick, 2025, for Geograph Britain and Ireland. CC BY-SA 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holy_Island%2C_Lindisfarne_Priory_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8137762.jpg","alt":"Roofless red sandstone walls and a broken arch rising out of close-cropped grass, with a churchyard of leaning headstones in the foreground and the sea beyond."}},
{"id":"wh-500","num":500,"category":"Medieval Europe","question":"Driven into the Somerset marshes in 878 and back on the throne within months, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the only English king that later ages gave that byname.","questions":["Learning had decayed so far that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could remember nobody south of the Thames able to turn a Latin letter into English, so he began a programme of translation himself.","Fortified towns, a rebuilt fleet and a levy that kept half the army at home while half fought are the reforms credited to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who died in 899."],"answer":"Alfred the Great","answerText":"Alfred the Great","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">849 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King of Wessex</span><span class=\"dt-v\">871 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Edington</span><span class=\"dt-v\">878 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">899 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Alfred the Great</b> was king of Wessex from 871 to 899 and the only English king to be given that name. His life was written by Asser, a Welsh bishop at his court, who says he was born at Wantage in 849 and traces his descent back through the West Saxon kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Danish army drove him into the Somerset marshes at Athelney in 878, and the fighting turned at Edington, after which he made terms with their leader Guthrum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He built ships, garrisoned fortified towns and reorganised the levy so that half the army was in the field while half was at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asser also has him measuring the hours by six candles of equal weight burning before the relics he carried everywhere, and correcting judges who gave sentences they could not justify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The most striking evidence is Alfred’s own: in the preface to his translation of Gregory’s <i>Pastoral Care</i> he writes that learning had decayed so far that he could remember no one south of the Thames able to turn a letter from Latin into English.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He set about a programme of translation into English of the books he thought most needful for men to know, and sent copies out to his bishops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He drew scholars from outside Wessex, and a letter survives in which the archbishop of Rheims commends the man he is sending and urges the king to check barbaric rudeness by his own authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He did not rule all England — the Danelaw lay outside him — but he is styled king of the Anglo-Saxons rather than of the West Saxons alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chronicle kept in his reign is the spine of English history for the period, and the life Asser wrote is where the reputation begins: modern work on it asks as much about how the cult of Alfred was made as about what he did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Asser, <i>Asser’s Life of King Alfred</i>, trans. Albert S. Cook (Boston: Ginn, 1906), 1–2, 27–29, 61–63, https://archive.org/details/asserslifeofking00asseiala. [Open access]","“King Alfred’s Preface to His Translation of Gregory’s Pastoral Care,” in <i>Asser’s Life of King Alfred</i>, trans. Albert S. Cook (Boston: Ginn, 1906), 69–71, https://archive.org/details/asserslifeofking00asseiala. [Open access]","<i>The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</i>, trans. J. A. Giles (London: G. Bell and Sons, 1914), 70–80, https://archive.org/details/anglosaxonchroni00gile. [Open access]","“Letter from Fulco, Archbishop of Rheims, to Alfred,” in <i>Asser’s Life of King Alfred</i>, trans. Albert S. Cook (Boston: Ginn, 1906), 72–77, https://archive.org/details/asserslifeofking00asseiala. [Open access]","Sayime Durmaz and Gökmen Günay Gökbayır, “‘Vita Ælfredi’ Işığında Wessex Kralı Büyük Alfred (871-899),” <i>ODÜ Sosyal Bilimler Araştırmaları Dergisi</i> 13, no. 3 (2023): 3351–72, https://doi.org/10.48146/odusobiad.1288135. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["person","ruler","history","warfare","europe","england"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Alfred_Jewel_2025.JPG/1280px-Alfred_Jewel_2025.JPG","title":"The Alfred Jewel","desc":"A pear-shaped ornament of gold, enamel and rock crystal found in Somerset, its rim lettered in Old English with the words meaning Alfred ordered me to be made. It probably headed a pointer for following a text.","credit":"Ashmolean Museum, Oxford. Photograph by Geni, 2025. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_Jewel_2025.JPG","alt":"A gold teardrop-shaped object under a domed crystal, showing an enamelled figure in green and blue holding two rods, framed by a lettered gold border and ending in an animal head."}},
{"id":"gc-001","num":1,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Guangdong","answerText":"Guangdong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty ports</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zones opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangdong"},"facts":[["Capital","Guangzhou"],["Population","126M (2020)"],["Largest city","Guangzhou"],["Area","179,800 km² (69,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"廣東","hanzi":"广东","pinyin":"Guǎngdōng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guangdong</b> is a province on the southern coast of mainland China, and for most of its recorded history the sea rather than the land behind it has set its direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its great city, Guangzhou, stands at the head of the Pearl River estuary, and long contact with foreign traders gave the province an influence over its neighbours out of all proportion to its size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Tang collapsed in the 10th century the region was held at Canton by one of the generals who divided the south into seven separate houses while the north was fought over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Guangdong and neighbouring Fujian resisted the Manchu conquest longer than anywhere else, and Canton, where the last adherents of the Ming had entrenched themselves, was stormed on 26 November 1650.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Piracy followed the trade: a fleet of some six hundred junks worked the estuary for years and was broken only in 1810, when the provincial government turned a quarrel between its leaders to account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Qing then narrowed foreign trade to a single door, and in 1757 an imperial edict made Canton the sole staple and forbade foreign trade at every other port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreigners dealt through a guild of Chinese merchants first organised in 1720 and known to them as the Co-hong, under a system of security merchants instituted in 1754 that made one Chinese house answerable for each foreign ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement supported a whole industry of goods made for foreign buyers, of which the most distinctive was export painting, at its height under the Qing and fading in the early 20th century as photography arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also produced a war, and the Treaty of Nanking of 29 August 1842 ended the monopoly, opening four further ports and ceding Hong Kong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second opening began in July 1979, when the central government decided that Guangdong and Fujian should lead it, and the first special economic zones followed at Shenzhen, Zhuhai and Shantou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1910), 65–67, 296, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.69484. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 44, 49, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 203, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Jinghui Ao, Ziying Ye, Weicong Li, and Shanshan Ji, “Impressions of Guangzhou City in Qing Dynasty Export Paintings in the Context of Trade Economy: A Color Analysis of Paintings Based on K-means Clustering Algorithm,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): art. 77, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-024-01195-4. [Open access]","Douglas Zhihua Zeng, “How Do Special Economic Zones and Industrial Clusters Drive China's Rapid Development?,” Policy Research Working Paper 5583, World Bank, March 2011, https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/310891468018256346. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/39002-Danxiashan_%2848989060302%29.jpg/1920px-39002-Danxiashan_%2848989060302%29.jpg","title":"Mount Danxia, Guangdong","desc":"The red sandstone peaks of Mount Danxia.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:39002-Danxiashan_(48989060302).jpg","alt":"Steep red sandstone hills above forest."}},
{"id":"gc-501","num":501,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Guangzhou","answerText":"Guangzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second Opium War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1857–1858</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangdong","dot":"Guangzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Guangdong"],["Population","18.7M (2020)"],["Rank in China","5th largest"],["Population growth","+47.0% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","china"],"traditional":"廣州","hanzi":"广州","pinyin":"Guǎngzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guangzhou</b> is the capital of Guangdong, at the head of the Pearl River estuary, and the city Europeans long knew as Canton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Tang dynasty collapsed in the 10th century it was held through two or three reigns by one of the generals who divided the south among themselves while the north was fought over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Adherents of the fallen Ming entrenched themselves in the city against the Manchu conquest, and it was stormed on 26 November 1650 in a siege an eyewitness thought worth recording.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the regulation of foreign trade began in 1702, when an official broker known to the foreigners as the Emperor's Merchant was made the sole channel through which they could buy tea and silk or sell anything of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That monopoly satisfied nobody, and the Canton merchants formed a guild of their own in 1720, working from 1754 under a system that made one of them answerable for each foreign ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An imperial edict of 1757 then made the city the sole staple for foreign trade and closed every other port to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreigners were confined to a row of factories outside the wall, came up for the trading season and withdrew to Macao when it ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain replaced the East India Company's monopoly with a crown officer, and Lord Napier reached Macao on 15 July 1834; his attempt to address the governor directly rather than through the merchants ended in his withdrawal and death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The quarrel over opium that followed became a war, and the Treaty of Nanking of 29 August 1842 ended the city's monopoly by opening four other ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its workshops had meanwhile built an export trade in goods made for foreign taste, above all painting and porcelain, whose surviving pieces are now studied as maritime heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 1 (London: Longmans, Green, 1910), 63–67, 71–74, 296, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.69484. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 44, 112–22, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 203–7, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Jinghui Ao, Ziying Ye, Weicong Li, and Shanshan Ji, “Impressions of Guangzhou City in Qing Dynasty Export Paintings in the Context of Trade Economy: A Color Analysis of Paintings Based on K-means Clustering Algorithm,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 12, no. 1 (2024): art. 77, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-024-01195-4. [Open access]","Jinghui Ao, Weicong Li, Shanshan Ji, and Simin Chen, “Maritime Silk Road Heritage: Quantitative Typological Analysis of Qing Dynasty Export Porcelain Bowls from Guangzhou from the Perspective of Social Factors,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 11, no. 1 (2023): art. 263, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-023-01103-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Guangzhou_Skyline%2C_shot_in_Panyu_District%2C_Guangzhou.jpg/1920px-Guangzhou_Skyline%2C_shot_in_Panyu_District%2C_Guangzhou.jpg","title":"Guangzhou","desc":"Guangzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"YikyuenG, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guangzhou_Skyline,_shot_in_Panyu_District,_Guangzhou.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Guangzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-002","num":2,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shandong","answerText":"Shandong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">German lease</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To Japan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shandong"},"facts":[["Capital","Jinan"],["Population","102M (2020)"],["Largest city","Linyi"],["Area","157,900 km² (61,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"山東","hanzi":"山东","pinyin":"Shāndōng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shandong</b> is a coastal province of northern China, a peninsula reaching into the Yellow Sea with the plain of the lower Yellow River behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was once an island, and was joined to the mainland only as alluvium carried down by the rivers silted up the water between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its standing in Chinese culture rests on two things above all: the memory of Confucius, and Mount Tai, one of the country's five sacred mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Qufu, where he was born, is a place of pilgrimage still, and its historic quarters have lately been rebuilt under conservation schemes in which the residents' own voices are easily lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Grand Canal crossed the province and long gave its produce an easy outlet north and south, before the railways began to take that trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Yellow River has been the other constant: having silted its bed up above the surrounding country, it broke through the embankments, flooded the low-lying west for years together and sought a new outlet to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its delta has been settled and resettled ever since, villages following the channels, because the silt that ruins one district improves the soil of another and makes it worth farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Germany then claimed the province as its sphere of development, and by a convention signed on 6 March 1898 the enclave of Kiaochow was ceded to it on lease, provisionally for ninety-nine years, together with the right to build railways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Resentment of that pressure fed the Boxer society, organised here with the sympathy of officials, and after the murder of the missionary S. M. Brooks on the last day of 1899 the governor Yuxian was held responsible for its rise and was replaced by Yuan Shikai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany lost the enclave in the First World War, but China did not get it back: article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles renounced those rights in favour of Japan, the railway to the provincial capital included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 88–89, 174–92, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 78–82, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 382–86, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Yuting Liu, Yu Ye, Chang Jiang, Jinfeng Jiang, Fahao Wang, Jun Li, and Xiangping Zhang, “Dynamics of Rural Settlement Patterns in the Yellow River Delta in Response to River Diversion and Avulsion Since 1855,” <i>Rural Landscapes: Society, Environment, History</i> 11, no. 1 (2024): art. 2, https://doi.org/10.16993/rl.100. [Open access]","Yingchun Zhang and Zongjie Wu, “Heritage Conservation and Urban Landscaping of Ancient Pan Pool Neighborhood, Qufu: A Historical and Indigenous Perspective,” <i>Conservation Science in Cultural Heritage</i> 14 (2014): 175–89, https://doi.org/10.6092/issn.1973-9494/5451. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Germany,” 28 June 1919, part IV, section VIII, article 156, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiv.asp. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/50304-Taishan_%2849055660366%29.jpg/1920px-50304-Taishan_%2849055660366%29.jpg","title":"Mount Tai, Shandong","desc":"Mount Tai.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:50304-Taishan_(49055660366).jpg","alt":"A stone stairway climbing a wooded mountain."}},
{"id":"gc-003","num":3,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Henan","answerText":"Henan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1509 – 1315 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Northern Song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">960 – 1127 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Henan"},"facts":[["Capital","Zhengzhou"],["Population","99.4M (2020)"],["Largest city","Zhengzhou"],["Area","167,000 km² (64,500 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"河南","pinyin":"Hénán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Henan</b> lies at the heart of China's Central Plains, spanning the middle and lower reaches of the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its fertility earned it the name of the Central Flower, and the name Zhongguo, the Middle Kingdom, later given to the whole country, may go back to the same idea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The valley of the Luo, running parallel to the Yellow River, is famous in Chinese history, while the mountains closing the province on the west leave few passes towards the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Songshan region at its centre, which takes in both Zhengzhou and Luoyang, is the core area of early Chinese civilisation, and settlement there has been traced from the Neolithic through the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beneath the modern provincial capital lies Zhengzhou Shang City, the seat of the early Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Excavations at Yinxu, in this province, were read in 1928 as showing that Chinese civilisation began towards the end of the Shang, and the ruins uncovered at Erlitou in Yanshi in 1960 as carrying its beginnings back to the Xia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both readings have since been qualified: several regions, among them the Yellow River and Yangtze basins, the country north and south of the Yanshan mountains and the western Liao basin, are now argued to have crossed into civilisation by about 3300 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Song dynasty was founded from Kaifeng, where the army found their chosen general drunk and threw the yellow robe over him before he could reply; by the end of his seventeen-year reign most provinces had submitted, though the Liao and Xia kingdoms held out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kaifeng was besieged again when a Taiping column reached the provincial capital in June, was repulsed after three days and crossed the Yellow River westwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its recognised traditional villages cluster in the north around Anyang and Hebi, in the centre near Pingdingshan and in the south at Xinyang, while eastern Henan has few, partly because the Yellow River has flooded it so long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 57–59, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Lijie Yan, Xia Wang, Li Zhang, and Fei Teng, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Clustering Patterns of Settlements from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age, 9000–3000 BP, in the Songshan Mountain Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0351644, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0351644. [Open access]","Zhongpei Zhang, “Processus d'évolution de la civilisation chinoise antique,” <i>Comptes rendus des séances de l'Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i> 148, no. 2 (2004): 533–42, https://doi.org/10.3406/crai.2004.22728. [Open access]","Fang Fang, Jingwen Liao, Xiaomin Zeng, and Juzhong Zhang, “The Truth of Unusual Deaths under Military Expansion: Evidence from the Stable Isotopes of a Human Skull Ditch in the Capital City of the Early Shang Dynasty, China,” <i>Genes</i> 13, no. 11 (2022): 2077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9690788/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39–40, 245–46, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Man Zhang, Jiabin Wu, and Yu Liu, “Temporal and Spatial Patterns and Determinants of Traditional Villages in Henan Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 5 (2026): e0350290, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0350290. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/27427-Luoyang_%2849067744628%29.jpg/1920px-27427-Luoyang_%2849067744628%29.jpg","title":"the Longmen Grottoes, Henan","desc":"The Longmen Grottoes.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:27427-Luoyang_(49067744628).jpg","alt":"Buddha figures carved into a river cliff."}},
{"id":"gc-004","num":4,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jiangsu","answerText":"Jiangsu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ming capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1368 – 1403</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangsu"},"facts":[["Capital","Nanjing"],["Population","84.7M (2020)"],["Largest city","Suzhou"],["Area","107,200 km² (41,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"江蘇","hanzi":"江苏","pinyin":"Jiāngsū","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jiangsu</b> lies on the Yellow Sea in the lower reaches of the Yangtze and the Huai, flat almost throughout and threaded with canals and lakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name is a compound of two of its own cities, Jiangning — which is Nanjing — and Suzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Grand Canal runs the length of it, and Suzhou, Changzhou, Zhenjiang, Yangzhou and Huai'an all stand on its line, while in the south a dense web of canals and lakes carries traffic that heavier shipping cannot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhu Yuanzhang made Nanjing his capital when he expelled the Mongols and founded the Ming in 1368, and after a reign of thirty years left the throne to a grandson of sixteen, whom his own uncle overthrew five years later and who took the capital back to Peking in 1403.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the city was the residence of the viceroy of the Two Jiangs, and had been the capital of the empire before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Taiping took Nanjing, Zhenjiang and Yangzhou, garrisoned them and put them in a state of defence, and were confident enough afterwards to send a division north to try for Peking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Winning the province back took years: Suzhou surrendered to the foreign-officered Ever-Victorious Force under Charles Gordon, its rebel commanders were executed by the governor once it had, and Nanjing fell last of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jiangsu is one of the country's great grain regions, growing wheat, rice, maize, soybeans, rapeseed and peanuts, rice above all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That farmland is under pressure from the towns, and satellite mapping shows single-season rice giving way to built-up ground, the same conversion that has driven the province's stock of stored carbon down since 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lake Taihu shows the pressure in the water: across two decades of satellite images its cyanobacterial blooms have spread, beginning earlier in the year and ending later as the climate has warmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 153–56, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 43, 240–41, 261–69, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Kashif Ali Solangi, Canhua Yang, Farheen Solangi, Weirong Zhang, Jinling Zhang et al., “Estimating Rice Cropping Area and Analyzing Land Use and Land Cover Changes in Jiangsu Province Using Multispectral Satellite Imagery,” <i>Plants</i> 15, no. 5 (2026): 715, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12986608/. [Open access]","Zhenxing Xiong, Yihao Zhang, Maohong Liu, Yuan Gao, and Tianci Gu, “Assessing Land Urbanization and Ecological Planning Impact on Carbon Stock and Its Economic Value from a Multi-Scenario Perspective,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 30494, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-13896-2. [Open access]","Xinyi Li, Xin Hang, Shihua Zhu, Liangxiao Sun, and Yachun Li, “Response of Cyanobacterial Blooms to Climate Warming: Evidence from Satellite Observations and Long-Term Meteorological Data in Lake Taihu,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 38820, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12589429/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Suzhou_Zhuozheng_Yuan_2015.04.23_08-13-49.jpg/1920px-Suzhou_Zhuozheng_Yuan_2015.04.23_08-13-49.jpg","title":"the classical gardens of Suzhou, Jiangsu","desc":"The Humble Administrator's Garden at Suzhou.","credit":"Zhangzhugang, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suzhou_Zhuozheng_Yuan_2015.04.23_08-13-49.jpg","alt":"A pavilion and pond in a classical Chinese garden."}},
{"id":"gc-005","num":5,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Sichuan","answerText":"Sichuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanxingdui</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Minor Han</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 – 263 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Sichuan"},"facts":[["Capital","Chengdu"],["Population","83.7M (2020)"],["Largest city","Chengdu"],["Area","486,000 km² (188,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"四川","pinyin":"Sìchuān","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Sichuan</b> is a province of south-western China, and its name means \"four rivers\", for the Yalong, the Min, the Chong and the Jialing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tibet lies to its west and Shaanxi and Gansu to its north, and its capital stands at the centre of the basin where most of its people have always lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest power that basin is known to have held was the kingdom of Shu, whose centre at Sanxingdui gave up two pits of bronzes in 1986 — a standing figure over two and a half metres tall, a great mask, and trees of bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Charcoal from a further pit opened in 2021 dates to between 1199 and 1017 BCE, which is the late Shang, so those bronzes were being cast while the dynasty of the Central Plains ruled far to the north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shu afterwards moved its centre to Jinsha, which lies inside modern Chengdu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Under the Han the region was divided into five principalities, of which the one called Shu was seized by the Minor Han dynasty of 221 to 263 CE, whose capital stood where Chengdu stands now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its founder, Liu Bei, was a poor kinsman of the imperial house who had dealt in straw sandals in his youth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The last years of the Ming brought a rebellion that carried destruction across the whole province, and the Manchus who followed had to spread peace over it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eastern Sichuan was then peopled from elsewhere, and a century ago a traveller found it rare to meet anyone who called himself a native: a man named instead the province his immigrant ancestor had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That mixing is still legible in the people: work on the Y chromosome across the Chengdu plain finds Han, Tibetan, Yi, Qiang and Hui whose paternal lines descend chiefly from indigenous East Asian millet and rice farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 104–6, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Weijie Zhao, “New Archeological Marvels of Ancient Shu Civilization,” <i>National Science Review</i> 8, no. 7 (2021): nwab071, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8310767/. [Open access]","Kuei-chen Lin, “On Craft Production and the Settlement Pattern of the Jinsha Site Cluster on the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 367–400, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76789. [Open access]","Alexander Hosie, <i>Szechwan: Its Products, Industries and Resources</i> (Shanghai: Kelly and Walsh, 1922), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/szechwanitsprodu00hosi. [Open access]","Lisiteng Luo, Yanling Liu, Yalan Liu, Yunhui Liu, Jie Zhong et al., “Paternal Genomic History and Y-Chromosome Phylogeny from the Chengdu Plain Reveal Multiple Indigenous East Asian Farmer Contributions to Ancient Shu,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 24, no. 1 (2026): 54, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-026-02527-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/1_jiuzhaigou_valley_wu_hua_hai_2011b.jpg/1920px-1_jiuzhaigou_valley_wu_hua_hai_2011b.jpg","title":"Jiuzhaigou, Sichuan","desc":"The lakes of Jiuzhaigou.","credit":"Chensiyuan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_jiuzhaigou_valley_wu_hua_hai_2011b.jpg","alt":"Clear turquoise lakes below wooded mountains."}},
{"id":"gc-502","num":502,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Jinan","answerText":"Jinan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rail to Qingdao</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1904</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Springs again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 2003</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shandong","dot":"Jinan"},"facts":[["Province","Shandong"],["Population","9.20M (2020)"],["Rank in China","28th largest"],["Population growth","+13.4% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","china"],"traditional":"濟南","hanzi":"济南","pinyin":"Jǐnán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jinan</b> is the capital of Shandong, standing on the right bank of the Yellow River and to the north-west of Mount Tai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the province was governed from here, by a governor rather than by a viceroy of the kind that ruled the province next door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city is built over karst, and groundwater rising through the limestone feeds the springs it is famous for, of which Baotu is the chief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pumping for a growing city drew the water table down until the springs failed, and only when the wells the city had sunk for itself were closed did Baotu begin flowing again, on 6 September 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has flowed since, helped by artificial recharge and by an emergency plan that fixes the level at which warnings are issued, while the city has spread outwards until the belt where it meets the countryside runs north-east and east in an almost unbroken band.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its modern history turns on a railway, and on who was to be allowed to build it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Germany leased the enclave of Kiaochow in 1898 it took with it the right to build in Shandong, and an imperial charter of 1 June 1899 set up a company to run a line from the coast at Tsingtau up to Jinan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A Chinese scheme for a line from Tientsin through Jinan to Chinkiang had been authorised at the end of 1897, and the German envoy opposed it at once, claiming that his country alone might build railways in the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German line was finished in 1904, and the trade of Shandong moved along it from the old port of Chefoo to Tsingtau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles then handed Germany's rights in the enclave and in that railway to Japan rather than back to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Changsuo Li, Liting Xing, Yanan Dong, Yuming Peng, Xuerui Xing et al., “Numerical Simulation and Protection of the Dynamic Change of Jinan Karst Spring Based on Coupling of Seepage and Conduit Flow,” <i>Heliyon</i> 8, no. 9 (2022): e10428, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9437806/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 78–82, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 88–90, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]","“Treaty of Peace with Germany,” 28 June 1919, part IV, section VIII, article 156, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiv.asp. [Open access]","Dawei Mei, Ningning Liu, and Haijiao Yu, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Driving Factors of Urban–Rural Integration in the Jinan Metropolitan Area,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 17223, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13234028/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Panorama_of_Jinan_from_Mt_Qianfo.jpg/1920px-Panorama_of_Jinan_from_Mt_Qianfo.jpg","title":"Jinan","desc":"Jinan, seen from a distance.","credit":"Vikarna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_Jinan_from_Mt_Qianfo.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Jinan."}},
{"id":"gc-006","num":6,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hebei","answerText":"Hebei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet at Cishan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aquifer recovery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hebei"},"facts":[["Capital","Shijiazhuang"],["Population","74.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Baoding"],["Area","188,800 km² (72,900 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"河北","pinyin":"Héběi","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hebei</b> wraps around Beijing and Tianjin on the North China Plain, running from the Bohai Sea in the east to the Taihang mountains in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Qing it was Zhili, the metropolitan province, and its viceroy resided not where the government sits today but at Baoding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the mountains to the north lies Chengde, known also as Jehol, the warm river, and it is through that country that a traveller coming down from the Mongolian plateaux reaches the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plain has been farmed for a very long time: storage pits at Cishan, in Wu'an on the province's southern edge, held common millet dating back about 10,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That makes broomcorn millet the earliest dry-farming crop yet identified in East Asia, which its resistance to drought would go some way to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Foxtail and broomcorn millet were being domesticated across the north between roughly 8300 and 4300 BCE, and the yields they eventually reached fed a population that grew steadily from 5000 to 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Xinglong, in northern Hebei on the Inner Mongolian border, shows the other side of that: at 5700 to 5600 BCE its people were still gathering wild plants in quantities that often outweighed the millet they grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming a dry plain on that scale has since been done with groundwater, and the aquifer beneath it became one of the most severely depleted in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since 2020 the water levels have risen again, passing their 2005 depth by 2024, in a reversal driven by bringing surface water up from the humid south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The province's ecologically richest ground lies in the north, on the plateau around Chengde and Zhangjiakou, and its poorest is the plain where most of its people live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 66–68, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Houyuan Lu, Jianping Zhang, Kam-biu Liu, Naiqin Wu, Yumei Li et al., “Earliest Domestication of Common Millet (<i>Panicum miliaceum</i>) in East Asia Extended to 10,000 Years Ago,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 18 (2009): 7367–72, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2678631/. [Open access]","Chris J. Stevens, Yijie Zhuang, and Dorian Q. Fuller, “Millets, Dogs, Pigs and Permanent Settlement: Productivity Transitions in Neolithic Northern China,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 6 (2024): e44, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2024.31. [Open access]","Di Long, Yuancheng Xu, Yingjie Cui, Yanhong Cui, James J. Butler Jr. et al., “Unprecedented Large-Scale Aquifer Recovery through Human Intervention,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 7296, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-62719-5. [Open access]","Min-Na Gao, Yong-Xiang Hu, Guo-Fei Shang, and Wei Li, “Research on the Impact of Spatial and Temporal Changes in Ecological Product Value in Hebei Province Based on Land Use Changes,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 9571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11926369/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Chengde_Mountain_Resort_22944-Chengde_%2844830038471%29.jpg/1920px-Chengde_Mountain_Resort_22944-Chengde_%2844830038471%29.jpg","title":"the Chengde Mountain Resort, Hebei","desc":"The imperial summer resort at Chengde.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chengde_Mountain_Resort_22944-Chengde_(44830038471).jpg","alt":"Imperial garden buildings beside a lake."}},
{"id":"gc-503","num":503,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Zhengzhou","answerText":"Zhengzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1509 – 1315 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">7·20 rainstorm</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 July 2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Henan","dot":"Zhengzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Henan"],["Population","12.6M (2020)"],["Rank in China","9th largest"],["Population growth","+46.1% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","china"],"traditional":"鄭州","hanzi":"郑州","pinyin":"Zhèngzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Zhengzhou</b> is the capital of Henan, standing where the western hills of the province give way to the plain of the Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Songshan region it sits in is the core area of early Chinese civilisation, and its settlement clusters have been traced there from the Neolithic through the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beneath the modern streets lies Zhengzhou Shang City, the capital of the early Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Excavation since the 1950s has turned up inner and outer city walls, palaces, water supply and drainage, handicraft workshops, cellars of bronzes, sacrificial remains and tombs, most of them of the Erligang period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1974 a ditch was found in the palace area holding nearly a hundred human skulls, most of them sawn across just above the ear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Who those people were has been disputed ever since, and strontium, carbon and nitrogen isotopes measured on eleven of the skulls have been used to ask where they grew up and what they had eaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The answer proposed is that they were not slaves but prisoners taken from the Xiaomintun district of Anyang, which would open a bloody chapter between the two Shang capitals that nothing else records; the authors are careful to say that more work is needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The railway driven through Henan at the end of the Qing, joining the Yellow River basin to that of the Yangtze, was already drawing trade and activity towards the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> As the built-up area spread, the city's heat island spread with it from the centre outwards, worsening until about 2014 and easing since as tree cover was planted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its other modern difficulty is the ground itself: after the extreme rainstorm of 20 July 2021 radar from orbit found zones of subsidence and of uplift within the city, the sinking keeping step with falling groundwater and worst in the districts built up most densely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Fang Fang, Jingwen Liao, Xiaomin Zeng, and Juzhong Zhang, “The Truth of Unusual Deaths under Military Expansion: Evidence from the Stable Isotopes of a Human Skull Ditch in the Capital City of the Early Shang Dynasty, China,” <i>Genes</i> 13, no. 11 (2022): 2077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9690788/. [Open access]","Zheng Zhou, Jiyuan Hu, Jiayao Wang, Lijun Wang, Tianrong Qiao et al., “Identifying Spatiotemporal Pattern and Trend Prediction of Land Subsidence in Zhengzhou Combining MT-InSAR, XGBoost and Hydrogeological Analysis,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 3848, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-87789-9. [Open access]","Yarong Yang, Fan Song, Jie Ma, Zheng Wei, Lili Song, and Wei Cao, “Spatial and Temporal Variation of Heat Islands in the Main Urban Area of Zhengzhou under the Two-Way Influence of Urbanization and Urban Forestry,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 8 (2022): e0272626, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272626. [Open access]","Lijie Yan, Xia Wang, Li Zhang, and Fei Teng, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Clustering Patterns of Settlements from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age, 9000–3000 BP, in the Songshan Mountain Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0351644, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0351644. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 57–59, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Zhengzhou_CBD.JPG/1920px-Zhengzhou_CBD.JPG","title":"Zhengzhou","desc":"Zhengzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Planetfinder, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhengzhou_CBD.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Zhengzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-007","num":7,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hunan","answerText":"Hunan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Middle Neolithic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8000 – 5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1852</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hunan"},"facts":[["Capital","Changsha"],["Population","66.4M (2020)"],["Largest city","Changsha"],["Area","211,800 km² (81,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"湖南","pinyin":"Húnán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hunan</b> means \"south of the lake\", and nearly the whole province lies south of Dongting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital stands on the right bank of the Xiang, the river down which most of its traffic has always moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cultivation shows early in this basin: the middle Neolithic of the middle and lower Yangtze, from 8000 to 5000 BCE, is the first phase of farming before domestication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its key cultures are the Pengtoushan-Zaoshi of the Li basin around the lake and the Datang of the middle and lower Xiang, and most of their sites sit on river terraces rather than in caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Farming did not simply spread outward from them: the Gaomiao culture of the Yuan valley in the north of the province derived from Pengtoushan and yet appears to have given agriculture up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On Mount Heng an inscription of seventy-seven characters was long held to record the emperor Yu's work on the flood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was copied about 1210 from a stone then standing in a Taoist temple, published in the form now generally accepted about 1510 by a Hunan antiquary, and found broken on the summit in 1666 by a man who had reached it with ladders and hooks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Taiping crossed into the province in 1852 and their boats went up the Xiang past the provincial capital, taking one town after another for supplies without setting up a government in any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The armies raised against them left a legacy of their own: the Gelaohui, a Hunan society, grew out of a loyal club founded by Zeng Guofan during the rebellion and turned into an organisation for overthrowing the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dongting, the second largest freshwater lake in the country, still swells and shrinks between the dry season and the flood, while the province's forests, its chief carbon sinks, have been taking up less as transport and industrial land spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 131–32, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from \"The Middle Kingdom\"</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 15–16, 245–48, 433–34, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Chi Zhang and Hsiao-chun Hung, “The Neolithic of Southern China—Origin, Development, and Dispersal,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 47, no. 2 (2008): 299–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17291. [Open access]","Hongyong Xiang, Xiang Li, Jie Chen, Yuping Dai, Ting Yi et al., “The Dynamic Changes of Eco-Environmental Quality in Dongting Lake Wetland during 1990–2020, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 34526, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12494795/. [Open access]","Qikang Zhong, Yuxin Liu, Zhe Li, and Jiawei Zhu, “Impact of Land Use Change on Carbon Metabolism in Hunan Province: An Ecological Network Analysis Approach,” <i>iScience</i> 28, no. 9 (2025): 113209, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12392657/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/1_tianzishan_wulingyuan_zhangjiajie_2012.jpg/1920px-1_tianzishan_wulingyuan_zhangjiajie_2012.jpg","title":"Wulingyuan, Hunan","desc":"The sandstone pillars of Wulingyuan.","credit":"chensiyuan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_tianzishan_wulingyuan_zhangjiajie_2012.jpg","alt":"Tall narrow sandstone pillars rising out of forest."}},
{"id":"gc-504","num":504,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Nanjing","answerText":"Nanjing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ming capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1368 – 1403</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nanking treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 August 1842</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangsu","dot":"Nanjing"},"facts":[["Province","Jiangsu"],["Population","9.31M (2020)"],["Rank in China","27th largest"],["Population growth","+16.4% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"南京","pinyin":"Nánjīng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nanjing</b> is the capital of Jiangsu and stands on the lower Yangtze, where the two banks give it a composite landscape of mountains, hills and plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the capital of the empire long before Beijing was, and under the Qing it was the residence of the viceroy of the Two Jiangs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Four princes of the Qi reigned here in the 6th century, the last of them besieged in his own capital by a minister who turned on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Zhu Yuanzhang made it his seat when he expelled the Mongols and founded the Ming in 1368, calling it the Southern Capital, which is what its name means; his son took the capital back to Peking in 1403.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ming city walls still stand among modern ones, and the two carry quite different plants: the historic walls hold many more species, over half of them woody, where the modern walls are dominated by herbs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In the older romanisation the city was written Nanking, and the treaty that ended the First Opium War was signed here and carries that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was concluded on board Her Britannic Majesty's ship Cornwallis on 29 August 1842, and the ratifications were exchanged at Hong Kong the following 26 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its second article opened Canton, Amoy, Fuchau-fu, Ningpo and Shanghai to British residence and trade, and its third ceded Hong Kong in perpetuity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A decade later the Taiping made the city their capital, held it while their armies went north and west, and lost it last of all the places they had taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Building along its river frontage has been restricted since 2021 under the policy of conserving the Yangtze rather than developing it, and the modern city is now large enough to be modelled building by building, commercial premises turning out far more carbon-intensive than homes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Changran Sun, Gongxian Fang, Yangyang Lu, Zihan Zhu, Mingjiang Deng, and Guohua Fang, “Multiscale Spatiotemporal Evolution and Patterns of Riverine Ecological Security in Nanjing Yangtze River Basin,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 3 (2026): 115149, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115149. [Open access]","Yuxin Chen, Zhenyu Wang, Quan Wen, Jing Meng, Jingwen Huo et al., “A Building-Scale Modeling Framework for Urban Net-Zero Transitions in Nanjing,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 8954, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-64016-7. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, ed., <i>Treaties between the Empire of China and Foreign Powers</i>, 4th ed. (Shanghai: North-China Herald, 1902), 1–5, https://archive.org/details/treatiesbetween00mayegoog. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 32, 43, 240–41, 269, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Wenxin Yu, Kaidi Wang, Yunfeng Yang, Sha Li, and Yao Xiong, “Environmental Drivers and Seasonal Dynamics of Spontaneous Plant Communities on Urban Walls: A Case Study in Nanjing, China,” <i>Plants</i> 15, no. 4 (2026): 541, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12944048/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 153, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Skyline_of_Nanjing_%28View_from_Nanjing_Railway_Station%29%2C_Jul_4_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Nanjing_%28View_from_Nanjing_Railway_Station%29%2C_Jul_4_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Nanjing","desc":"Nanjing, seen from a distance.","credit":"A Chinese ID, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Nanjing_(View_from_Nanjing_Railway_Station),_Jul_4_2024_(cropped).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nanjing."}},
{"id":"gc-008","num":8,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Zhejiang","answerText":"Zhejiang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5300 – 4300 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Southern Song</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1127 – 1280 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Zhejiang"},"facts":[["Capital","Hangzhou"],["Population","64.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Hangzhou"],["Area","105,500 km² (40,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"浙江","pinyin":"Zhèjiāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Zhejiang</b> lies at the southern edge of the Yangtze delta, meeting Shanghai and Jiangsu along its northern border, and is a mountainous province rather than a deltaic one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its bay and plains were settled very early, and the lower Yangtze sequence runs from the Shangshan through the Kuahuqiao and Hemudu cultures to Liangzhu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pigs at Jingtoushan and at Kuahuqiao were eating cooked starchy plants and human waste by 8,000 years ago, which makes them the earliest managed pigs known in the lower Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Geese at the rice village of Tianluoshan about 7,000 years ago were bred locally over several generations and may have been fed cultivated paddy rice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Liangzhu, in what is now Hangzhou, walled a capital with dams, canals and a jade industry, and abandoned it in an exceptionally wet spell between 4,345 and 4,324 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Thousands of years later an Arab account records the sack of Canfu, a port near Hangzhou Bay, in which a hundred and twenty thousand Muslims, Jews, Christians and Parsees engaged in trade were destroyed, which says something about what that trade was worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Jin overran the north, the last emperor of the Northern Song saw his capital in ruins, fled to Nanjing and then to Hangzhou at the mouth of the Qiantang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nanjing was pillaged, but Hangzhou lay too far for the invaders, and it grew in size and strength into a famous capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That emperor, Gaozong, resigned in 1162 after a reign of thirty-six years and survived his abdication by twenty-four more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The province he left behind is still one of the country's leading ones for industry, and the great majority of its larger enterprises are privately owned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 139–41, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 40–41, 63, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Haiwei Zhang, Hai Cheng, Ashish Sinha, Christoph Spötl, Yanjun Cai et al., “Collapse of the Liangzhu and Other Neolithic Cultures in the Lower Yangtze Region in Response to Climate Change,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 48 (2021): eabi9275, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi9275. [Open access]","Jiajing Wang, Yiyi Tang, Yunfei Zheng, Leping Jiang, Xiaolin Ma et al., “Early Evidence for Pig Domestication (8,000 cal. BP) in the Lower Yangtze, South China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 24 (2025): e2507123122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2507123122. [Open access]","Masaki Eda, Yu Itahashi, Hiroki Kikuchi, Guoping Sun, Kai-hsuan Hsu et al., “Multiple Lines of Evidence of Early Goose Domestication in a 7,000-y-old Rice Cultivation Village in the Lower Yangtze River, China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 12 (2022): e2117064119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2117064119. [Open access]","Junmei Sawada, Kazuhiro Uzawa, Minoru Yoneda, Yasuo Hagihara, Yu Itahashi et al., “Worked Human Bones and the Rise of Urban Society in the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture, East Asia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 31441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15673-7. [Open access]","Fan Tu, Ruijia Ji, Jinyu Zhou, and Gabriel Hoh Teck Ling, “Shaping Industrial Spatial Density: How Floor Area Ratio Varies across Regions and Sectors in Zhejiang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 3 (2026): e0343089, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0343089. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/West_Lake%2C_Hangzhou_2025.jpg/1920px-West_Lake%2C_Hangzhou_2025.jpg","title":"West Lake, Zhejiang","desc":"West Lake at Hangzhou.","credit":"Takashishin, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Lake,_Hangzhou_2025.jpg","alt":"A causeway and pagoda across a broad lake."}},
{"id":"gc-505","num":505,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Chengdu","answerText":"Chengdu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plain settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shu capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 – 263 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Sichuan","dot":"Chengdu"},"facts":[["Province","Sichuan"],["Population","20.9M (2020)"],["Rank in China","4th largest"],["Population growth","+38.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"成都","pinyin":"Chéngdū","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Chengdu</b> is the capital of Sichuan and stands at the centre of the basin, on the plain that is the province's garden and its granary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plain's water supply never fails, because the Min is divided where it leaves the mountains at Guanxian — Dujiangyan today — into a network of streams and cross-channels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of that hydraulic engineering grew the linpan, the wooded farmstead clusters that are the plain's own landscape and have a history of more than 2,300 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> People were there long before the water was managed: the late Neolithic Baodun period on the plain runs from about 2400 to 1700 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jinsha, the largest known settlement of the Shi'erqiao culture that followed Sanxingdui, is not one site but a cluster of more than twenty published components in and around the modern city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At its heart lay a ceremonial precinct on the south bank of the Modi river, and once the people of Sanxingdui had dispersed Jinsha became the region's new focus of exchange between regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Han the surrounding country was one of five principalities, and the Minor Han dynasty of 221 to 263 CE ruled it from where the city now stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing is left of that dynasty's palace in the north-west of the city, and the walled enclosure shown to visitors as its imperial residence was really the seat of a Ming prince, a son of the dynasty's founder sent to govern the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The provincial annals call it the Shu Wang Fu, the residence of the Sichuan prince, and in 1665 it became the hall in which candidates sat the provincial examinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> West of the city the Chengdu section of the Giant Panda National Park spreads across Dujiangyan, Chongzhou, Dayi, Qionglai and Pengzhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Alexander Hosie, <i>Szechwan: Its Products, Industries and Resources</i> (Shanghai: Kelly and Walsh, 1922), 18–19, 26, https://archive.org/details/szechwanitsprodu00hosi. [Open access]","Kuei-chen Lin, “On Craft Production and the Settlement Pattern of the Jinsha Site Cluster on the Chengdu Plain,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 367–400, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76789. [Open access]","Kabilijiang Umel, Pingpu Zhu, Shuyu Zhang, Mikiko Ishikawa, and Liang Yu, “Perception Study of the Conservation and Utilization of Linpan Cultural Landscape Heritage: The Case of Dujiangyan City,” <i>Journal of Environmental Engineering and Landscape Management</i> 32, no. 1 (2024): 45–56, https://doi.org/10.3846/jeelm.2024.20773. [Open access]","Qianqian Wang, Chi Xu, Yubo Gou, Mingchun Zhang, Ke He et al., “Species Identification and Spatial Diversity Patterns of the Giant Panda National Park (GPNP) in Chengdu, Sichuan, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 3 (2026): e73180, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12949331/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 105, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Anshun_Bridge_Jin_River_Chengdu_night_2026_dllu.jpg/1920px-Anshun_Bridge_Jin_River_Chengdu_night_2026_dllu.jpg","title":"Chengdu","desc":"Chengdu, seen from a distance.","credit":"Daniel Lu ( User:dllu ), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anshun_Bridge_Jin_River_Chengdu_night_2026_dllu.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Chengdu."}},
{"id":"gc-009","num":9,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Anhui","answerText":"Anhui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Five Dynasties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">907 – 960 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1853</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Anhui"},"facts":[["Capital","Hefei"],["Population","61.0M (2020)"],["Largest city","Hefei"],["Area","141,000 km² (54,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"安徽","pinyin":"Ānhuī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Anhui</b> takes its name from two of its own towns, Anqing and Huizhou, and sits inland at the head of the Yangtze delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Qing it was governed not from its present capital but from Anqing, in the south-west of the province on the left bank of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between the Yangtze and the Huai the Huoshan range runs from south-west to north-east, affording beautiful sites and difficult passages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South of the river stands Huangshan, a confused chain whose valleys seldom exceed a few hundred yards in width and which is at its most tangled in the Huizhou country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chao Lake lies in the middle of the province, one of the largest bodies of fresh water in the country, and it is from there that the modern capital draws its drinking water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> During the Five Dynasties, while rival houses fought over the valley of the Yellow River, the south and west were portioned among seven ruling families, and this region was held by generals who had served the Tang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Taiping came through it on their northern march, taking Liuhe and Fengyang without meeting serious opposition before crossing into Henan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Huai is a boundary as well as a river: the Qinling-Huai line, which it marks across the province, is the conventional divide between northern and southern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ratoon rice, harvested twice from one sowing, grows almost entirely south of that line, and autumn temperatures north of it are what keep it from spreading further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Water now crosses the line the other way: the Yangtze-to-Huai diversion runs from the river to Chao Lake, along the Jianghuai Canal and northward from there, to relieve drought on the Huai and clean both the lake and the river, and building it turned farmland and forest into water and built ground faster than the province had changed in the decade before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 146–48, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 245–46, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Wenqing Ding, Guangzhi Shi, Hui Zha, Haojie Miao, Mengmin Lu, and Jing Jin, “Ecological Impacts and Supply Demand Evolution of the Yangtze to Huaihe Water Transfer Project in Anhui Section,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 20311, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11366761/. [Open access]","Wei Luo, Lixin Yuan, Shengmin Yan, Linlin Wang, Yongfu Yu et al., “Study on the Suitable Area of Ratoon Rice in China under Climate Change,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 12 (2025): e72724, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12710086/. [Open access]","Juan Huang, Jinyan Zhan, Haiming Yan, Feng Wu, and Xiangzheng Deng, “Evaluation of the Impacts of Land Use on Water Quality: A Case Study in the Chaohu Lake Basin,” <i>The Scientific World Journal</i> 2013 (2013): 329187, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3736410/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Huangshan_pic_4.jpg/1920px-Huangshan_pic_4.jpg","title":"Huangshan, Anhui","desc":"The granite peaks of Huangshan.","credit":"Chi King, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huangshan_pic_4.jpg","alt":"Granite peaks and pines above a sea of cloud."}},
{"id":"gc-506","num":506,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Shijiazhuang","answerText":"Shijiazhuang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rail concession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 May 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Building began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1903</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hebei","dot":"Shijiazhuang"},"facts":[["Province","Hebei"],["Population","11.2M (2020)"],["Rank in China","14th largest"],["Population growth","+10.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"石家莊","hanzi":"石家庄","pinyin":"Shíjiāzhuāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shijiazhuang</b> is the capital of Hebei and stands on the plain in the west of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is not the province's old seat of government: under the Qing the viceroy of Zhili lived at Baoding, and the ancient prefecture on this stretch of the plain was Zhengding, a few miles to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city owes its existence to two railways that meet here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first is the Peking–Hankow line, carried south from Baoding on its way to the Yellow River; the second runs west to Taiyuan, the capital of Shanxi, and leaves the first at a station a few miles south of Zhengding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The concession for that western line went to the Russo-Chinese Bank under an agreement of 21 May 1898, before the Boxer rising, and the cost was then put at 25 million francs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When the route was surveyed the country proved to offer so many obstacles that the line would cost a great deal more, and fresh terms had to be settled with the Chinese government afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Construction began under the Peking–Hankow line's own engineers in 1903, on the metre gauge, and went slowly against the engineering difficulties of the hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Growth in people and in industry has left the modern city with a standing air-pollution problem that bears directly on its residents' health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aircraft flown through a haze that settled over it in November 2020 found the pollution dominated by fine particulates, with black carbon layered through the air above the streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its road emissions peak twice a day, between seven and nine in the morning and five and seven in the evening, and gather on the trunk roads through Qiaoxi, the central district that is the municipality's political, economic and financial heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Bing-Chun Liu, Arihant Binaykia, Pei-Chann Chang, Manoj Kumar Tiwari, and Cheng-Chin Tsao, “Urban Air Quality Forecasting Based on Multi-Dimensional Collaborative Support Vector Regression (SVR): A Case Study of Beijing-Tianjin-Shijiazhuang,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0179763, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5510805/. [Open access]","Xuesi Zhou, Xiaoshen Sun, Yang Yang, Xiaorui Zhang, Zhaochu Huang et al., “Aircraft Observations on a Continuous Haze Pollution Event in Shijiazhuang Area,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 10 (2023): 1066610, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2022.1066610. [Open access]","Percy Horace Kent, <i>Railway Enterprise in China: An Account of Its Origin and Development</i> (London: Edward Arnold, 1907), 92, 102, 169–70, https://archive.org/details/railwayenterpri00kent. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 66–67, 76, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Min-Na Gao, Yong-Xiang Hu, Guo-Fei Shang, and Wei Li, “Research on the Impact of Spatial and Temporal Changes in Ecological Product Value in Hebei Province Based on Land Use Changes,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 9571, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11926369/. [Open access]","Lili Ren, Xuliang Guo, Jiangling Wu, and Amit Kumar Singh, “Data Mining and Spatio-Temporal Characteristics of Urban Road Traffic Emissions: A Case Study in Shijiazhuang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 12 (2023): e0295664, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10718443/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Skyline_of_Shijiazhuang_2012.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Shijiazhuang_2012.jpg","title":"Shijiazhuang","desc":"Shijiazhuang, seen from a distance.","credit":"ping lin, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Shijiazhuang_2012.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Shijiazhuang."}},
{"id":"gc-010","num":10,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hubei","answerText":"Hubei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Heritage listing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hanyang works</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1890s</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hubei"},"facts":[["Capital","Wuhan"],["Population","57.8M (2020)"],["Largest city","Wuhan"],["Area","185,900 km² (71,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"湖北","pinyin":"Húběi","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hubei</b> lies at the core of the middle reaches of the Yangtze, in central China, ringed by mountains on the east, west and north and low and flat in the middle where the basin opens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It borders Anhui to the east, Chongqing to the west, Shaanxi to the north-west, Jiangxi and Hunan to the south and Henan to the north, a position that earned it the name of the Nine Provinces' Thoroughfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Jing and Chu cultures grew up on that river network, and the province holds more immovable cultural heritage than all but six others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of the sites with provincial standing the largest class is historic buildings, among them the Golden Temple on Wudang Mountain and ancient Longzhong at Xiangyang, while the ancient sites include the ruins of Ji'nan City, the Chu capital, and of Qujialing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where all of it stands has been measured: it clusters most densely on Wuhan, while its geometric centre, dynasty by dynasty, falls around Jingshan and Jingmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the 1890s Zhang Zhidong, viceroy at Wuchang, argued against extending the railways along the coast and for great trunk lines through the interior, safely remote from the sea, beginning with one from Peking to Hankou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was transferred to Wuchang with orders to build it of Chinese material and under Chinese direction, found himself unable to do so, and established the Hanyang steel works to provide for the future instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The province is also where northern China gets much of its water: the central line of the South-to-North Water Diversion draws from the Danjiangkou Reservoir on the Han, the Yangtze's largest tributary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That reservoir supplies more than twenty cities, Beijing and Tianjin among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ecologically the province stands high in the east and the west and low in the middle, and it is on the Jianghan Plain between them that its network of corridors breaks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Xiaojie Chen, Longyang Huang, and Changcheng Zhang, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Trade-offs/Synergies of Ecosystem Services in Hubei Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 35697, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12518542/. [Open access]","Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 85–86, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]","Wenyuan Zhang, Lihong Ma, Jiayu Xu, and Yujie Peng, “Spatial-Temporal Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors of Immovable Cultural Heritage in Hubei, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 24570, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12238651/. [Open access]","Di Long, Wenting Yang, Bridget R. Scanlon, Jianshi Zhao, Dagen Liu et al., “South-to-North Water Diversion Stabilizing Beijing's Groundwater Levels,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11, no. 1 (2020): 3665, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7374106/. [Open access]","Junfeng Zeng and Shushen Li, “Evaluating and Optimizing Ecological Networks in the Hubei Yangtze River Economic Belt Using an Importance-Sensitivity and Circuit-Graph Approach,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 43220, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12680631/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Wudang_Mountain_%2854131425234%29.jpg/1920px-Wudang_Mountain_%2854131425234%29.jpg","title":"the Wudang Mountains, Hubei","desc":"The Wudang Mountains.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wudang_Mountain_(54131425234).jpg","alt":"Temple roofs on a forested mountain ridge."}},
{"id":"gc-507","num":507,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Changsha","answerText":"Changsha","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang kilns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">620 – 907 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1852</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hunan","dot":"Changsha"},"facts":[["Province","Hunan"],["Population","10.0M (2020)"],["Rank in China","18th largest"],["Population growth","+42.7% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"長沙","hanzi":"长沙","pinyin":"Chángshā","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Changsha</b> is the capital of Hunan and stands on the right bank of the Xiang, in the lower reaches of the Dongting Lake system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has long been called a city of mountain and water, for its abundant surface water and for the basin it sits in with hills all around the built-up ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Tang the kilns here were a major centre of celadon production, and their opaque glazes are still studied for how the colour was got into them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The answer is a high-calcium glaze in which copper and iron ions and the separation of the melt into phases together decide what the eye sees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Taiping crossed into the province in 1852 and their boats went up the Xiang past the city, taking one town after another for supplies without setting up a government in any of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The modern city has spread across the plain, its built-up ground growing nearly threefold between 2000 and 2023 and fastest between 2010 and 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Changsha is one of the ten hottest cities in the country, and its heat island is worst in August, the month with the highest temperatures and the least frequent rain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its parks and waters push back against that: green patches cool the ground around them, and open water cools it further still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measured across the county and the six urban districts, the city's ecological quality fell between 2003 and 2014 and then recovered part of the way by 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The worst of that loss was east of the Xiang, in the Xingma, Airport and Huangli groups, where the city has been growing outward fastest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Xinyi Qiu, Sung-Ho Kil, Hyun-Kil Jo, Chan Park, Wonkyong Song, and Yun Eui Choi, “Cooling Effect of Urban Blue and Green Spaces: A Case Study of Changsha, China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 3 (2023): 2613, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9916382/. [Open access]","Chan Lu, Lei Shi, Lihua Fu, Simian Liu, Jianqiao Li, and Zhenchun Mo, “Urban Ecological Environment Quality Evaluation and Territorial Spatial Planning Response: Application to Changsha, Central China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 4 (2023): 3753, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9961913/. [Open access]","Zuopeng Zhang, Zhe Li, and Zhirong Li, “Evaluating the Change and Trend of Construction Land in Changsha City Based GeoSOS-FLUS Model and Machine Learning Methods,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 9602, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11926109/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 131, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Lala Jia, Maolin Zhang, Yongqiang Yu, Zihan Li, and Yongbin Yu, “The Coloring Mechanism of Opaque Glazes from the Changsha Kiln of the Tang Dynasty,” <i>Materials</i> 17, no. 23 (2024): 5803, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11642496/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 245–48, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Night_view_of_Xiang_River_in_Changsha_2025032403.jpg/1920px-Night_view_of_Xiang_River_in_Changsha_2025032403.jpg","title":"Changsha","desc":"Changsha, seen from a distance.","credit":"Huangdan2060, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Night_view_of_Xiang_River_in_Changsha_2025032403.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Changsha."}},
{"id":"gc-011","num":11,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Guangxi","answerText":"Guangxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lingqu Canal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qin, over 2,200 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiping begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1844</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Nanning"],["Population","50.1M (2020)"],["Largest city","Nanning"],["Area","237,600 km² (91,700 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"廣西","hanzi":"广西","pinyin":"Guǎngxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guangxi</b> is an autonomous region rather than a province, named for the Zhuang, and its country is a basin of mountainous hills falling away from the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It holds the second largest concentration of ethnic minorities in the country, settled in the pattern of large mixed communities and small concentrated ones that long contact produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its nationally listed traditional villages gather around one main core at Guilin, with lesser ones at Hezhou, Liuzhou and Yulin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Nanling mountains along the northern border separate the Pearl River basin from the Yangtze, and under the Qin the Lingqu Canal was cut across them to join the headwaters of the Xiang to the Gui.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Water has run from one basin into the other ever since, and studies of freshwater fish treat the canal as a route by which populations have passed between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In May 1844 Hong Xiuquan, Feng Yunshan and two companions set out to visit a relative living near the Miao in Guangxi, keeping themselves on the road by peddling ink-stones and writing brushes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They preached and baptised as they went, and a kinsman who took a school in one of the districts stayed several years and baptised more than fifty converts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Out of that mission grew the Taiping rebellion, which within a decade held Nanjing and the towns along much of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No recruits ever came from Guangxi once the movement had left it, and when the last of its walled cities fell the Guangxi men among the prisoners were the only ones executed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the region's rural settlements sit today, and why they sit there, has been mapped across the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jiayi Yan, Jianyong Lin, Aihua Wang, Yadong Qie, Cong Hu et al., “Conserving Large Old Trees in Guangxi, South China: Diversity, Distribution and Conservation Strategies,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 2 (2026): e73043, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12900623/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 238–43, 262–63, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Jianmei Tan, Jiayan Liu, Yi Zou, and Xiaoshu Wei, “Spatial Influencing Factors and Protection Strategies of Traditional Villages in Ethnic Areas: A Case Study of Guangxi,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 4 (2026): e0336147, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13098987/. [Open access]","Yun Chen, Guangmin Deng, Ziyu Le, and Cuizhang Fu, “Mountains, Lakes, and Ancient Drainage Networks Sculpt the Phylogeographic Architecture of the Stream Headwater Fish <i>Acrossocheilus kreyenbergii</i> in China,” <i>Genes</i> 16, no. 12 (2025): 1393, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12732399/. [Open access]","Wenjun Zheng, Wentao Cao, Guifang Li, Sijia Zhu, and Xianyan Zhang, “Spatial Pattern Characteristics and Factors for the Present Status of Rural Settlements in the Lijiang River Basin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 5 (2023): 4124, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001701/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/87318-Li-River.jpg/1920px-87318-Li-River.jpg","title":"the Li River, Guangxi","desc":"The karst hills of the Li River.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:87318-Li-River.jpg","alt":"Steep limestone hills along a winding river."}},
{"id":"gc-508","num":508,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hangzhou","answerText":"Hangzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Liangzhu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5300 – 4500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Song capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1127 – 1280 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Zhejiang","dot":"Hangzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Zhejiang"],["Population","11.9M (2020)"],["Rank in China","12th largest"],["Population growth","+37.2% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"杭州","pinyin":"Hángzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hangzhou</b> is the capital of Zhejiang and stands at the mouth of the Qiantang, at the southern edge of the Yangtze delta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Liangzhu culture built its walled city in what is now the municipality, with enclosures, moats, dams and canals, and jade that carried religious meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rice cultivation was its economic base, and it is the late Neolithic of the whole delta seen at its most elaborate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Jin overran the north and the capital fell, the last emperor of the Northern Song fled first to Nanjing and then here; Nanjing was pillaged, but this city lay too far for the invaders and grew in size and strength into a famous capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That emperor, Gaozong, resigned in 1162 after a reign of thirty-six years and survived his abdication by twenty-four more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The city is known above all for West Lake, which sits higher than the river plain around it, so that apart from four small streams almost nothing feeds it naturally and its water has to be brought in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Keeping it clear has taken two thousand years of management and, lately, five things at once: preventing the lake silting into marsh, cutting the nutrients coming in, controlling those already in the sediment, bringing back the submerged plants and then holding the whole system steady.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Successful recoveries of this kind are rare anywhere, and rarer still in shallow subtropical lakes, where large ones such as Taihu, Chao and Dianchi have not managed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lake went onto the World Heritage List in 2011, the first Chinese entry in the lake category.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Beijing-Hangzhou Grand Canal ends here, and the greenway now laid along its banks is studied for how restorative the people walking it find it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Guoliang Bai, Yadong Tang, Yunli Liu, Zisen Liu, Lingwei Kong et al., “Sustainable Innovation for the Clear Waters of Hangzhou West Lake, China: Achievements and Insights,” <i>The Innovation</i> 7, no. 3 (2026): 101148, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12957564/. [Open access]","Yue Wang, Mengjie Yang, Yijun Lu, Qiaoyi He, Youli Zhang et al., “Bridging Landscape and Perceived Restorativeness: An Empirical Study of Greenways along the Grand Canal, Hangzhou,” <i>Frontiers in Psychology</i> 17 (2026): 1742799, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12916418/. [Open access]","Junmei Sawada, Kazuhiro Uzawa, Minoru Yoneda, Yasuo Hagihara, Yu Itahashi et al., “Worked Human Bones and the Rise of Urban Society in the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture, East Asia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 31441, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15673-7. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 40–41, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 139–41, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hangzhou_Skyline_on_West_Lake.jpg/1920px-Hangzhou_Skyline_on_West_Lake.jpg","title":"Hangzhou","desc":"Hangzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Yinweichen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hangzhou_Skyline_on_West_Lake.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hangzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-012","num":12,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Yunnan","answerText":"Yunnan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Baiyangcun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2650 – 1690 BCE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Yunnan"},"facts":[["Capital","Kunming"],["Population","47.2M (2020)"],["Largest city","Kunming"],["Area","394,100 km² (152,200 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","province","asia","china"],"traditional":"雲南","hanzi":"云南","pinyin":"Yúnnán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Yunnan</b> is a plateau province in the south-western corner of China, its ground declining in steps from the north-west and mountainous over the great majority of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly half of China's animal and plant species are found within it, many of them ancient in origin, which gives the province both a high rate of endemism and a fragile ecology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stands at the crossroads of temperate China, north-eastern India and tropical mainland South-East Asia, which is why its farming history matters well beyond its own borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rice and millet spread into it from central China in the 3rd millennium BCE, wheat and barley followed in the 2nd, and by the late 1st millennium the land was being worked in two seasons rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Baiyangcun, on the middle Jinsha, has given up rice, foxtail and broomcorn millet and wild soybean together, though whether its farmers carried rice southwards is doubted, early rice here having been grown differently from the way it was in mainland South-East Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Panthays, a Muslim people long settled in the west of the province, date their origin from the Tang and were generally left unmolested so long as they obeyed the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under Mongol rule their numbers grew until they began to take part in the government, and they had done so ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> During the Taiping rebellion the emperor's authority in Yunnan was practically in abeyance, and over the western half of the province it was superseded altogether by a Panthay revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Putting that rising down is one of the three things the regency of the 1860s and 1870s is credited with, beside quelling the Taiping and opening diplomatic relations with the treaty powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plateau cluster of cities around Kunming, Qujing and Yuxi still holds most of the province's people and makes most of its economy, and what its energy costs is now tracked city by city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Wenqi Tang, Jianqiang Li, Huiying Yan, Lianling Cha, Yuan Yang, and Linling Wang, “Multi-Scenario Simulation of Land Use Change Based on the Objectives of Ecological Protection and Food Security in Yunnan Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 37506, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12559345/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 51, 367–68, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Rita Dal Martello, “The Origins of Multi-Cropping Agriculture in Southwestern China: Archaeobotanical Insights from Third to First Millennium B.C. Yunnan,” <i>Asian Archaeology</i> 6, no. 1 (2022): 65–85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9373101/. [Open access]","Feiling Yang, Jinming Hu, and Ruidong Wu, “Combining Endangered Plants and Animals as Surrogates to Identify Priority Conservation Areas in Yunnan, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 30753, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4990906/. [Open access]","Ning You, Libo Han, Shaomei Li, Jingzhen Ma, and Qing Xu, “Spatio-Temporal Dynamics of Per Capita Energy Consumption Carbon Emissions in the Central Yunnan Urban Agglomeration,” <i>Carbon Balance and Management</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): 80, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13224723/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Terrace_field_yunnan_china_denoised.jpg/1920px-Terrace_field_yunnan_china_denoised.jpg","title":"the Hani rice terraces, Yunnan","desc":"The Hani rice terraces at Honghe.","credit":"Jialiang Gao , www.peace-on-earth.org, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terrace_field_yunnan_china_denoised.jpg","alt":"Flooded rice terraces stepping down a hillside."}},
{"id":"gc-509","num":509,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hefei","answerText":"Hefei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Urban growth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2000 – 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Anhui","dot":"Hefei"},"facts":[["Province","Anhui"],["Population","9.37M (2020)"],["Rank in China","26th largest"],["Population growth","+25.7% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"合肥","pinyin":"Héféi","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hefei</b> is the capital of Anhui and stands in the middle of the province, on hills and low mounds under a humid subtropical climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jianghuai watershed runs east to west across the whole of its territory, so the city sits astride the divide between the basin of the Yangtze and that of the Huai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has not always been the seat of anything so large: under the Qing this was the prefectural town of Luzhou, a little north of Chao Lake, while the province itself was governed from Anqing on the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chao Lake now lies inside the municipality, one of the largest bodies of fresh water in the country, fed by more than thirty rivers, and the city drinks from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its administrative area takes in four urban districts, the county-level city of Chaohu and four counties, and the four districts together are its economic and administrative core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The modern city has grown at a pace few others have matched, the share of its people living in towns rising steeply since 2000 and its built-up ground spreading out across the low hills to meet the lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is now reckoned one of the four principal cities of science and education in the country, a sub-central city of the Yangtze delta agglomeration, and one of the ten largest hubs on the high-speed rail network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Within its own metropolitan area of seven cities it has become the core of the industrial carbon-emission network, carrying far more influence over the rest than the average.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Industrial emissions across those seven rose unevenly between 2010 and 2022, and the correlations between them tightened as the cities grew into one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The largest emitter of them all is not Hefei but Huainan, on the Huai to the north, which stands far above the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Zhiqiang Yao, Shi Chen, and Yinlan Huang, “Exploring the Threshold of Human Activity Impact on Urban Ecosystem Service Value: A Case Study of Hefei,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 24118, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12230127/. [Open access]","Ran Yu, Hongsheng Cheng, Yun Ye, Qin Wang, Shuping Fan et al., “Optimization of the Territorial Spatial Patterns Based on MOP and PLUS Models: A Case Study from Hefei,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 3 (2023): 1804, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9914123/. [Open access]","Dawei Dai, Yuewen Liu, Shuhang Zhao, and Yue Chen, “Study on Spatial Correlation and Driving Factors of Industrial Carbon Emission in the Hefei Metropolitan Area,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 3445, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11772829/. [Open access]","Juan Huang, Jinyan Zhan, Haiming Yan, Feng Wu, and Xiangzheng Deng, “Evaluation of the Impacts of Land Use on Water Quality: A Case Study in the Chaohu Lake Basin,” <i>The Scientific World Journal</i> 2013 (2013): 329187, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3736410/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 146, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Skylines_of_Hefei_at_Tianehu.jpg/1920px-Skylines_of_Hefei_at_Tianehu.jpg","title":"Hefei","desc":"Hefei, seen from a distance.","credit":"钉钉, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skylines_of_Hefei_at_Tianehu.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hefei."}},
{"id":"gc-013","num":13,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jiangxi","answerText":"Jiangxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xianrendong</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 19,780 – 8,575 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassies passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1793 and 1816</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Nanchang"],["Population","45.2M (2020)"],["Largest city","Ganzhou"],["Area","166,900 km² (64,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"江西","pinyin":"Jiāngxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jiangxi</b> lies along the southern bank of the middle and lower Yangtze, mountainous and hilly over most of its ground, flanked by the Wuyi range to the east, the Nanling to the south and the Luoxiao and Jiuling to the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Poyang Lake, the largest body of fresh water in the country, sits in the north of the province and is joined to the Yangtze through a single outlet at Hukou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Five river systems feed it, the Gan, the Xin, the Fu, the Xiu and the Rao, and with their tributaries they drain almost the whole of Jiangxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two caves in the north of the province, Xianrendong and Diaotonghuan, have yielded some of the earliest pottery known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The stone toolkits found in them combine pebble tools with small flaked pieces of quartz, flint and crystal, and lie among quantities of land animal, fish and bird remains together with rice phytoliths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From that evidence the earliest occupants have been read as collectors of wild rice and their successors as cultivators of it, which would place the caves close to the beginning of rice farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> West of Poyang lie the vale and grotto of the White Deer, where Zhu Xi, the great commentator of Confucius, lived and taught, and which is a celebrated place of pilgrimage for Chinese scholars still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across the south of the province runs the Meiling pass, named for the plum trees around it and long known as the Ambassadors' Route, the way by which embassies travelled between Canton and Peking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lord Macartney returned to Canton over that pass in 1793 and Lord Amherst in 1816.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The kilns of Jingdezhen in the north-east became one of the most important centres of porcelain in Chinese history, and the glazes of their Yuan blue-and-white are analysed still for how the cobalt took its colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Xu Song, Xi Chen, Bofu Zheng, and Wei Wan, “Spatiotemporal Patterns and Driving Mechanisms of Terrestrial Carbon Storage in Jiangxi Province,” <i>Carbon Balance and Management</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): 60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13001212/. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 140–45, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]","Tracey L-D Lu, “Early Pottery in South China,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 49, no. 1 (2010), https://hdl.handle.net/10125/29097. [Open access]","Chi Zhang and Hsiao-chun Hung, “The Neolithic of Southern China—Origin, Development, and Dispersal,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 47, no. 2 (2008): 299–329, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17291. [Open access]","Jun Sun, Qijiang Li, Xiaoyan Xia, Min Tang, Yan Liang, and Linxin Ouyang, “Effect of Luanbai Glaze on the Coloration of Cobalt Pigment in Yuan Dynasty Jingdezhen Porcelains: An Experimental Study,” <i>Materials</i> 19, no. 11 (2026): 2254, https://doi.org/10.3390/ma19112254. [Open access]","Chun Fu, Libin Xiang, Yezhong Liu, and Tianming Zhou, “Response of Nitrogen Emissions to Land Use Changes and Driving Forces Analysis in Jiangxi Province under Multiple Scenarios,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 29599, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-12965-w. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/16137-Lushan_%2831834519847%29.jpg/1920px-16137-Lushan_%2831834519847%29.jpg","title":"Mount Lu, Jiangxi","desc":"Mount Lu.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16137-Lushan_(31834519847).jpg","alt":"Wooded mountain slopes above a valley."}},
{"id":"gc-510","num":510,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Wuhan","answerText":"Wuhan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hankou a port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Towns merged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hubei","dot":"Wuhan"},"facts":[["Province","Hubei"],["Population","12.4M (2020)"],["Rank in China","10th largest"],["Population growth","+27.2% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"武漢","hanzi":"武汉","pinyin":"Wǔhàn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wuhan</b> is the capital of Hubei and stands at the confluence of the Yangtze and the Han, the core city of the urban cluster of the middle Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began as three separate towns at that meeting of the rivers — Hankou, Wuchang and Hanyang — and takes its name and its shape from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hankou lived on Yangtze commerce, and after it was opened as a port in 1861 it grew into a trading centre whose main streets ran parallel to the water while the cross-streets ended at the wharves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wuchang, on the southern bank opposite the other two, is the seat of the provincial government, and the lakes ringing it to the north, east and south pushed its roads outward into a radial pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was from Wuchang in the 1890s that Zhang Zhidong pressed for trunk railways through the interior rather than along the coast, and, unable to build one of Chinese material as he had undertaken, set up the Hanyang steel works instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The three municipalities were merged in 1927 to form the modern city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first bridge ever thrown across the Yangtze opened here in 1957, and the city has gone on adding them since, across the Han as well as the greater river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is nicknamed the City of a Hundred Lakes, and its parks and waters work as cold islands against the summer heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old residential quarters of Wuchang are now assessed street by street for the quality of the public space in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its stretch of the river was long counted a gap in the range of the critically endangered Yangtze finless porpoise, but acoustic monitoring between 2020 and 2022 picked the animal up on nearly half the days listened to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Jufang Song, Yongxuan Qiao, and Yihan Liu, “Evaluation of the Cold Island Effect of the Urban Parks in the Main Urban Area of Wuhan from the Perspective of Supply and Demand,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 13 (2025): 1523210, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1523210. [Open access]","Ran Peng, Keyuan Ding, Haixu Guo, Xueliang Liu, Yehao Liu et al., “The Impact of Rivers and Lakes on Urban Transportation Expansion: A Case Study of the Century-Long Evolution of the Road Network in Wuhan, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0298678, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0298678. [Open access]","Zhi-Tao Wang, Peng-Xiang Duan, Tomonari Akamatsu, Ke-Xiong Wang, and Ding Wang, “Increased Yangtze Finless Porpoise Presence in Urban Wuhan Waters of the Yangtze River during Fishing Closures,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 4 (2024): e11247, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.11247. [Open access]","Zixuan Guo, Hong Xu, Qiushuang Lin, and Xuanhe Li, “Deep Learning Assessment of Street Spatial Quality in Old Residential Communities of Wuchang, Wuhan, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 45176, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-28936-0. [Open access]","Hosea Ballou Morse, <i>The International Relations of the Chinese Empire</i>, vol. 3 (London: Longmans, Green, 1918), 85–86, https://archive.org/details/cu31924017552310. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Skyline_of_Wuhan-2.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Wuhan-2.jpg","title":"Wuhan","desc":"Wuhan, seen from a distance.","credit":"Majorantarktis, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Wuhan-2.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Wuhan."}},
{"id":"gc-014","num":14,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Liaoning","answerText":"Liaoning","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hongshan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4500 – 3000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manchu manifesto</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1618</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Liaoning"},"facts":[["Capital","Shenyang"],["Population","42.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Shenyang"],["Area","148,600 km² (57,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"遼寧","hanzi":"辽宁","pinyin":"Liáoníng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Liaoning</b> is a province of north-eastern China facing the Bohai and the Yellow Sea, its ground descending southward from uplands in the east and west to the lowlands between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the one province of the north-east with both a seaboard and a land frontier, and its jurisdiction runs out over the water as well as the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Long before any of that the valleys of the Liao and the Daling held the Hongshan culture, whose core zone at Niuheliang, Dongshanzui and Hutougou holds the largest ceremonial platforms and nearly every known burial furnished with elaborate jade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Manchu state rose here: in 1618 the chieftain Tianming published a manifesto against the house of Ming, announcing to Heaven the seven things he was bound to revenge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were petty oppressions on people crossing the frontiers, help given to his enemies, the breaking of an oath and treaty of peace, and the killing of his envoys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He had already taken the title of emperor, and he overran the north-eastern provinces and dealt hard with the people of Liaodong before dying in 1627, leaving his revenge and his army to his son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mukden, which is Shenyang, was the capital of the Manchu country of Shengjing, and the dynasty kept its pride in the place long after it had taken Peking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the modern century the province became one of the anchors of the country's heavy industrial base, the state building the Anshan steelworks here as it built the First Automobile Works in Jilin and the Daqing oilfield in Heilongjiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its Bohai ports drew metallurgy, military manufacturing and trade infrastructure to it, and among the north-eastern provinces it is the one that came to specialise in metal mining and smelting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The restructuring of state enterprises after the reform era closed a great many works and left a distinct abandoned-factory heritage behind it, and the Tiexi district of Shenyang has since been remade as an industrial heritage corridor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Boqian Jiang, Dan Wang, Qiuju Ye, Jiayi Luo, Bingqian Jin, and Haibo Yin, “Construction of Eco-Quality Dual-Suitable Distribution Areas in Liaoning Province,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1591921, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12307492/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 44–45, 455, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Lina Ke, Shusheng Yin, Shuting Wang, and Quanming Wang, “Spatiotemporal Changes Caused by the Intensive Use of Sea Areas in the Liaoning Coastal Economic Zone of China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0242977, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0242977. [Open access]","Christian E. Peterson et al., “Hongshan Chiefly Communities in Neolithic Northeastern China,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 107, no. 13 (2010): 5756–61, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2851873/. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Mukden_Palace_drone_view_5_%28cropped_%26_rotated%29.jpg/1920px-Mukden_Palace_drone_view_5_%28cropped_%26_rotated%29.jpg","title":"Mukden Palace, Liaoning","desc":"The Mukden Palace at Shenyang.","credit":"Techyan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mukden_Palace_drone_view_5_(cropped_%26_rotated).jpg","alt":"The tiled roofs of a Manchu imperial palace."}},
{"id":"gc-511","num":511,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Nanning","answerText":"Nanning","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sponge City</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2013</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Built land grew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1990 – 2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guangxi","dot":"Nanning"},"facts":[["Region","Guangxi"],["Population","8.74M (2020)"],["Rank in China","36th largest"],["Population growth","+31.3% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"南寧","hanzi":"南宁","pinyin":"Nánníng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nanning</b> is the capital of the Guangxi Zhuang Autonomous Region and stands in the south-central part of it, in a basin of alluvial plain ringed by hills.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Yong, a major tributary of the Pearl River system, runs east to west across the city and spreads a branching drainage network that has shaped how the built-up area grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That river is why the place mattered before it was a capital: under the Qing, Nanning Fu stood at the head of practicable steam navigation up the Yu, with landing places above and below the rapid downstream that made the passage possible all year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Just above the town the Zuo comes in, formed by two rivers that rise in Tonkin and meet at Lungchow near the frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rain falls here in half the year rather than through it, which in a basin makes the city unusually liable to flooding, and it is one of the pilots of the national Sponge City programme begun in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The ground beneath it is a red clay that is half clay particles by weight, its minerals quartz, kaolinite, illite, calcite and hematite, the last of these the iron oxide that cements the soil and gives it its colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That clay swells and shrinks as it wets and dries, which is a standing problem for anyone building a road across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 1990 and 2020 the built-up land increased more than fivefold, spreading east and south-east onto the flat ground, and almost nine tenths of what it took was farmland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city's territory is divided into fifteen districts and counties, and it remains one of the major rice-growing areas of southern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its urban forests are studied for what they do to the carbon held in the soil beneath them, which is one of the few things a city can add to rather than spend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Yun-chuan Yang, Xiao-han Huang, Jiao-yin Wei, Li-ping Liao, Guo-qiang Feng et al., “Urban Expansion Drivers and Land Use Scenario Simulation in Nanning, China, Using the PLUS Model,” <i>Science Progress</i> 109, no. 1 (2026): 00368504261417161, https://doi.org/10.1177/00368504261417161. [Open access]","Wei Zhou, Zhao Wei, Ning Feng, Mi Luo, Bingpeng Qu, Qiren Luo, and Jianbing Zhang, “Urban Forest Plant Diversity Affects Soil Organic Carbon by Regulating Functional Genes in Nanning,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 3 (2026): 114856, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.114856. [Open access]","Sen Deng, Hongri Zhang, Jian Wei, Kaike Huang, Yanxin Song et al., “Hydrostructural and Dynamic Characteristics of Compacted Nanning Red Clay Considering Wetting-Drying Impacts,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 11483, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41777-9. [Open access]","Peng Li, Siyan Li, Jing Zhang, Liting Cen, Qi Li, and Hui Li, “Dietary Risk Analysis of Heavy Metal and Metalloid Pollution in Rice: Case Study of Nanning City in China,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 14 (2026): 1781196, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1781196. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 171–72, 196–98, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Nanning_skyline_2008.JPG/1920px-Nanning_skyline_2008.JPG","title":"Nanning","desc":"Nanning, seen from a distance.","credit":"Saigon punkid ( talk ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanning_skyline_2008.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Nanning."}},
{"id":"gc-015","num":15,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Fujian","answerText":"Fujian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Min kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">907 – 960 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wuyishan park</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Fujian"},"facts":[["Capital","Fuzhou"],["Population","41.5M (2020)"],["Largest city","Quanzhou"],["Area","124,000 km² (47,900 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"福建","pinyin":"Fújiàn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Fujian</b> is a province on the south-eastern coast of China, facing Taiwan across the strait, and mountainous and long-shored at once: eight parts mountain, one part water and one part field, as the old description of it has it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its forests cover a higher proportion of the land than those of any other province, and it carries several hundred nationally listed traditional villages, thickest in the hill country away from the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> During the Five Dynasties, when rival houses fought over the valley of the Yellow River, Fujian was held by the King of Min.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Song and the Yuan, Quanzhou was one of the greatest maritime trading ports of China, and it is a World Heritage city now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ibn Battuta landed there — at Zayton, as the port was then known — and his accounts of it and of Canton and Hangzhou are among the fullest that survive of the trade of the southern coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That trade brought religions in with the cargoes, and the local Minyue traditions took in Buddhism, Islam, Christianity and Taoism over the centuries that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inland the Hakka built the great earth houses that draw visitors today, and what those visitors take to be authentic about them has itself become a subject of study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Wuyi Mountains hold what is reckoned the largest intact stretch of primary mid-subtropical forest anywhere at that latitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Wuyishan became one of the first five national parks in China when the system was set up in 2021, and it guards the headwaters of the Min, the province's principal river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nearly everything the province earns is still earned within sight of the sea, its six coastal cities making the great bulk of what it makes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mengjie Song, Xiaoxi Huang, Xueqiong Wei, Xuwei Tang, Zhixiang Rao et al., “Spatial Patterns and the Associated Factors for Breast Cancer Incidence in Fujian Province,” <i>BMC Women's Health</i> 23, no. 1 (2023): 247, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10170828/. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 39, 70–71, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]","Congzhi Li, Ying Pan, Weilun Yin, and Siren Lan, “Spatial Distribution and Influencing Factors of Rural Cultural Ecosystem Services: A Case Study of Fujian, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 10510, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-95600-y. [Open access]","Wenjun Chen and Chaoxiang Wen, “Spatial Benefit Assessment and Marine Climate Response of Coastal Zone in Fujian Province under Cross-System Interaction,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 11 (2024): e0306988, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0306988. [Open access]","Yong Zhu, Guoqiang Han, Ji'an Zhuang, and Mo Wang, “Authenticity and Place Attachment as Dual Pathways for Sustainable Tourism in the Historic Quanzhou West Street,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 44980, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-28662-7. [Open access]","Mingxia Zhang, Naifeng Lin, Guangyong You, Yan Wang, Lei Wang et al., “Variations and Influencing Factors of Vegetation Net Primary Productivity over 31 Years in Wuyishan National Park,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 29002, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-71812-6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Three_round%2C_one_oval%2C_one_square_%2816831811084%29.jpg/1920px-Three_round%2C_one_oval%2C_one_square_%2816831811084%29.jpg","title":"the Fujian tulou","desc":"A Fujian tulou.","credit":"Scott Edmunds, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_round,_one_oval,_one_square_(16831811084).jpg","alt":"A large circular earthen communal dwelling."}},
{"id":"gc-512","num":512,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Kunming","answerText":"Kunming","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lake divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1996</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grade lifted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2016</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Yunnan","dot":"Kunming"},"facts":[["Province","Yunnan"],["Population","8.46M (2020)"],["Rank in China","37th largest"],["Population growth","+31.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"昆明","pinyin":"Kūnmíng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kunming</b> is the capital of Yunnan and stands in the north-central part of it on the plateau, with mountains on three sides and Dianchi Lake to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Qing it was Yunnan Fu, often called Yunnan Sheng, set among the table-lands towards the north-east of the province, with thirteen other prefectures ranged around it by distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its altitude at a low latitude gives it a subtropical highland climate fed by warm moist air off the Indian Ocean, and the smallest swing between its hottest and coldest months of any city in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dianchi is the largest body of fresh water in south-western China, and remarkably shallow for its size, so that what enters it is not quickly diluted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A causeway built in 1996 divides it into the small Caohai arm and the much larger outer lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because the lake sits at the lowest point of the city and has no other outlet for what the city discharges into it, its water fell to the worst national grade in 1986 and was not lifted out of that grade until 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cyanobacteria still bloom there from May to November, and the basin that drains into it takes in a wide stretch of central Yunnan across several administrative units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The microbial communities of the water are now studied for how they assemble and what drives them from one habitat within the lake to the next, which is a way of asking what the recovery has actually changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has grown very fast, its built-up ground spreading across the basin between 2012 and 2021 and running up against the lake it drains into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Migratory gulls have wintered in its parks and along that lake shore since the 1980s, and their gut microbiomes have been sequenced for what the birds carry with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ting Chen, Zhibin Ren, Yao Fu, and Chang Liu, “Correlation Analysis of Landscape Patterns and Urban Thermal Environment in Kunming Based on a Panel Data Model,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 27375, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-78118-7. [Open access]","Jun Chen, Zhizhong Zhang, Bowen Wang, Jiaojiao Yang, Guangxiu Cao et al., “Diversity, Assembly, and Habitat-Driven Dynamics of Microbial Communities in Eutrophic Dianchi Lake, Southwest China,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 14, no. 3 (2026): 554, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms14030554. [Open access]","Wenjuan Pei, Yilong Peng, Kai Fan, Jiansheng Zhang, Yunchun Chen et al., “The Impact of Land Use on Eco-Environment in the Dianchi Basin,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 11 (2024): e32085, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e32085. [Open access]","Jun Zhang, Xue Zhang, Xueping Tan, and Xiaodie Yuan, “A New Approach to Monitoring Urban Built-Up Areas in Kunming and Yuxi from 2012 to 2021,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 19 (2022): 12198, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph191912198. [Open access]","Feng Liao, Jing Qian, Ruian Yang, Wenpeng Gu, Rufang Li et al., “Metagenomics of Gut Microbiome for Migratory Seagulls in Kunming City Revealed the Potential Public Risk to Human Health,” <i>BMC Genomics</i> 24, no. 1 (2023): 269, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12864-023-09379-1. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 208–10, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Kunming.%E6%98%86%E6%98%8E.skyline.sunset.2013.jpg/1920px-Kunming.%E6%98%86%E6%98%8E.skyline.sunset.2013.jpg","title":"Kunming","desc":"Kunming, seen from a distance.","credit":"FRED, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunming.%E6%98%86%E6%98%8E.skyline.sunset.2013.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Kunming."}},
{"id":"gc-016","num":16,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shaanxi","answerText":"Shaanxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhaoxiang wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 272 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang Chang'an</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 907 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shaanxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Xi'an"],["Population","39.5M (2020)"],["Largest city","Xi'an"],["Area","205,600 km² (79,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"陝西","hanzi":"陕西","pinyin":"Shǎnxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shaanxi</b> lies along the middle Yellow River, and the Guanzhong plain at its centre carried Chang'an, capital of the Tang and the eastern end of the Silk Road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long before that the state of Qin rose on the same ground and walled its northern frontier, and 347 watchtowers of the wall built under King Zhaoxiang still stand, rammed out of the soil beneath them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They run through the north of the province between desert and loess gully, close enough to the Qin capital at Xianyang to be its first line of defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Going the other way meant crossing the Qinling, which the Qin–Shu road did by a family of routes — Baoxie, Ziwu, Chencang, Tangluo and Wuguan — at their height under the Sui and the Tang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nearly six hundred heritage sites are strung along it, thickest at Xi’an, Hanzhong and Ankang and thinning in the high country to a scatter of military passes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Genomes from Tang burials at the capital are mostly indistinguishable from the Neolithic farmers of the same valley, though several carry Western Eurasian ancestry, so the road shows in the graves as well as in the records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the twentieth century the province’s weight moved north to Yan’an, whose revolution sites at Yangjialing, Zaoyuan and Wangjiaping, with the seat of the Shaanxi–Gansu–Ningxia border region government, lead a route of memorials running its length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The north is loess country, and its villages live in yaodong, cave dwellings cut into hillsides or sunk as courtyards below the fields, which the firm soil allows and which the surrounding earth keeps at an even temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Across the middle the Qinling, taken with the Huai River, marks the line conventionally drawn between northern and southern China, and it works as a real barrier, plant populations on either side of it being genetically distinct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its forests hold the densest wild giant panda population in the country, nearly a fifth of all the wild pandas there are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Minglei Lv et al., “Ancient Genomes from the Tang Dynasty Capital Reveal the Genetic Legacy of Trans-Eurasian Communication at the Eastern End of Silk Road,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 22 (2024): 267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11577736/. [Open access]","Kankan Li, Ziqiang Huang, Yinghui Pang, Sijie Chen, Yijing Fan et al., “Cultural Heritage of the Qin-Shu Ancient Road in Shaanxi: Spatial Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0331676, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0331676. [Open access]","Xingyi Li, Yangyang Tong, Xiaoning Ma, Jiachen Chang, and Binyan He, “The Building Concept of Border Defence Facilities of Qin: Watchtowers along the King of Zhaoxiang Great Wall in Shaanxi Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329298, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0329298. [Open access]","Yuxin Feng, Yunxia Tian, and Xiaoyu Lv, “The Network Characteristics of Classic Red Tourist Attractions in Shaanxi Province, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0299286, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0299286. [Open access]","Runze Liu, Xiang Wang, Lin Yuan, Yunting Gao, Yan Zhang et al., “Analyzing the Ecologicality and Functionality of Kiln Architecture in China through KH Coder Data Mining Algorithm and Hierarchical Event Coding,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 16189, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97659-z. [Open access]","Chunxue Jiang, Tian Shi, Zhongmei Mo, and Cai Zhao, “Across a Phylogeographic Break in the Qinling Mountains-Huaihe River Line,” <i>BMC Ecology and Evolution</i> 24, no. 1 (2024): 107, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12862-024-02297-0. [Open access]","Chun Yang, Wanyu Wen, Yuhang Wang, Zhijiao Yang, Changcai Li et al., “Do Giant Pandas Prefer Steeper Habitats? A Case Study on Panda Spatial Utilization in the Qinling Mountains, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 3 (2025): e71074, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.71074. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/51714-Terracota-Army.jpg/1920px-51714-Terracota-Army.jpg","title":"the Terracotta Army, Shaanxi","desc":"The Terracotta Army near Xi'an.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:51714-Terracota-Army.jpg","alt":"Ranks of life-size pottery soldiers in an excavation pit."}},
{"id":"gc-513","num":513,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Nanchang","answerText":"Nanchang","answerDate":"","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jiangxi","dot":"Nanchang"},"facts":[["Province","Jiangxi"],["Population","6.26M (2020)"],["Rank in China","60th largest"],["Population growth","+24.0% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"南昌","pinyin":"Nánchāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Nanchang</b> is the capital of Jiangxi and stands in the north-centre of the province, where the Gan runs north towards Poyang Lake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Gan is the largest of the five river systems feeding that lake, and its basin covers almost the whole province, so the city sits near the gathering point of nearly all of Jiangxi's water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the flood season small steamers could once come up the Gan as far as this city, junks of middling tonnage as far as Ganzhou, and small craft as far as Nan'an in the far south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Above Ji'an the river runs through a set of rapids called the eighteen, which is much of what kept the larger boats below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Poyang itself is deep as well as broad, and rises nearly thirty feet above its ordinary level when the season turns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The city is the core of the urban cluster of the middle Yangtze and the political, economic, cultural and transport hub of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is made up of the Donghu, Xihu, Qingyunpu, Qingshan Lake and Honggutan districts together with Xinjian and the outlying counties of Nanchang, Anyi and Jinxian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its built-up area is read in rings, the First and Second Ring Roads marking off a core, an inner suburban belt and an outer one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How strong the monsoon is varies a great deal from one year to the next, so the city meets heat waves, drought and cold spells in turn rather than a steady season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its parks are now studied for how evenly their cooling reaches the people living around them, and how safe its streets feel has been mapped from several kinds of data at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Li Wang, Liang Lu, Zhibin Tu, Liping Qiu, Xiaoqin Tong et al., “Spatiotemporal Aggregation and Population Distribution Characteristics of HIV/AIDS in Nanchang City,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0342375, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0342375. [Open access]","Youqiang Zhao, Liu Pinyi, Gong Peng, and Zhang Jian Ping, “Optimizing Spatial Equity of Urban Park Cooling Services: Integrating Landscape Metrics with K-Means and PSO Algorithms,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 3 (2026): e0344026, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0344026. [Open access]","Chun Fu, Libin Xiang, Yezhong Liu, and Tianming Zhou, “Response of Nitrogen Emissions to Land Use Changes and Driving Forces Analysis in Jiangxi Province under Multiple Scenarios,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 29599, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-12965-w. [Open access]","Linduo Yuan, Yuze Dan, Yi Leng, and Jiaxin Zhang, “Influencing Factors of Urban Safety Perception Based on the Combination of Multi-Source Data and Machine Learning,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 3972, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-34110-3. [Open access]","Louis Richard, <i>Comprehensive Geography of the Chinese Empire and Dependencies</i>, trans. M. Kennelly (Shanghai: T'usewei Press, 1908), 142, https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegeo00rich_0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Nanchang_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Nanchang_Skyline.jpg","title":"Nanchang","desc":"Nanchang, seen from a distance.","credit":"Akira CA, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanchang_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Nanchang."}},
{"id":"gc-017","num":17,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Guizhou","answerText":"Guizhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Guizhou province</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1413</span><span class=\"dt-k\">FAST first light</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2016</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guizhou"},"facts":[["Capital","Guiyang"],["Population","38.6M (2020)"],["Largest city","Bijie"],["Area","176,200 km² (68,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"貴州","hanzi":"贵州","pinyin":"Guìzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guizhou</b> is an inland province of south-western China on the Yunnan–Guizhou Plateau, made up of plateau mountain, hill and basin, and the only province in the country with no plains at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the Ming its mountain peoples were only loosely joined to the Central Plains, held under Tusi chieftains whom the terrain and the poor roads left largely to themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yuan drove official post roads through the country, and around 1413 the Ming made Guizhou a province and began replacing those chieftains with officials the central government sent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlers followed from the Central Plains with their farming and their cattle, until the wars at the end of the dynasty emptied much of the new farmland again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Qing the Kangxi emperor froze the corvée tax and maize, potato and sweet potato arrived, so people and cropland grew together — until poppy took the best fields and cultivation climbed the slopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Fifty ethnic groups live in the province, forty-nine of them minorities, and the Miao, Bouyei, Dong and Shui are each more numerous here than in any other province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its 757 villages on the national register of traditional Chinese villages are second only to one other province’s, and they cluster in the south-east, at the heart of what was once called the Miaojiang Corridor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Congjiang County, in the Qiandongnan Miao and Dong Autonomous Prefecture, Zhuang villages still brew WeiJiu from glutinous rice, mountain spring water and an ancestral koji, in five stages ending in sealing and aging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Karst runs through most of the province, and working thin soil on limestone strips the ground back to bare rock, which makes rocky desertification the standing threat to its ecosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same rock has been an asset as well: a natural karst basin here, quiet at radio wavelengths, holds the largest and most sensitive filled-aperture radio telescope in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Qiuju Mao, Liang Xie, and Lai Fan, “Spatial Characteristics and Determinants of Traditional Village Distribution in Guizhou Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0324275, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0324275. [Open access]","Yetong Li, Qihua Ke, and Zhuodong Zhang, “Millennial Evolution of a Karst Socio-Ecological System: A Case Study of Guizhou Province, Southwest China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 22 (2022): 15151, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192215151. [Open access]","Lei Shao, Shucai Li, Long Yang, Shenbing Wei, Gang Shen et al., “Analysis of Microbial Structure and Function in Fermented Grains during the Fermentation Process of Congjiang WeiJiu Based on High-Throughput Sequencing,” <i>PeerJ</i> 14 (2026): e21180, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.21180. [Open access]","Chuncan Meng, Yingqing Cen, and Xu Xue, “Response of Vegetation Greenness to Extreme Droughts and Possible Mechanisms in Guizhou Province, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): e72945, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.72945. [Open access]","Beibei Zhang and Zhongfa Zhou, “The Spatiotemporal Evolution of Ecosystem Health and Its Driving Factors in Karst Rocky Desertification Areas: A Case Study of Guizhou Province, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 5 (2025): e71374, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.71374. [Open access]","Weijie Zhao, “China’s ‘Sky Eye’: Exploring the Universe by Measuring Faint Cosmic Radio Signals,” <i>National Science Review</i> 10, no. 7 (2023): nwad003, https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwad003. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/%E9%BB%84%E6%9E%9C%E6%A0%91%E7%80%91%E5%B8%83_2026-07-31_01.jpg/1920px-%E9%BB%84%E6%9E%9C%E6%A0%91%E7%80%91%E5%B8%83_2026-07-31_01.jpg","title":"Huangguoshu Waterfall, Guizhou","desc":"The Huangguoshu Waterfall.","credit":"Kcx36, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BB%84%E6%9E%9C%E6%A0%91%E7%80%91%E5%B8%83_2026-07-31_01.jpg","alt":"A wide waterfall falling into a green gorge."}},
{"id":"gc-514","num":514,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Shenyang","answerText":"Shenyang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Manchu rise</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1618 – 1627</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Industrial base</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1948</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Liaoning","dot":"Shenyang"},"facts":[["Province","Liaoning"],["Population","9.07M (2020)"],["Rank in China","30th largest"],["Population growth","+11.9% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"瀋陽","hanzi":"沈阳","pinyin":"Shěnyáng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shenyang</b> is the capital of Liaoning and stands in the middle of the province, in the transition between a branch of the Changbai mountains and the flood plain of the Liao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its land is alluvial plain in the west with low hills to the north-east and south-east, and it is the communication, commercial, scientific and cultural centre of north-eastern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the Manchus it was Mukden, the capital of their country of Shengjing, and the dynasty kept its pride in the city long after it had taken Peking and moved there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its winters are severe and dry and its summers hot; measured against Beijing they run several degrees colder on average, with the better part of a month of extra severely cold days in the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government designated it a key investment and industrial base in 1948, and before the 1980s it was the centre of heavy industrial development in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That history left a city built for industry, and with it decades of pollution the planning of the time did not account for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its urban area grew many times over across the century to 2010, and the rate of that growth rose sharply after 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The built-up area lay almost wholly north of the Hun until then, and the south bank has been the front of expansion ever since, spreading outward in rings that are now a four-ring road network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city has abundant wetlands, more of them than anywhere else in the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The labile carbon in those wetland soils and the microbes that work on it are now studied for what the city's growth has done to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Miao Liu, Yanyan Xu, Yuanman Hu, Chunlin Li, Fengyun Sun, and Tan Chen, “A Century of the Evolution of the Urban Area in Shenyang, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 6 (2014): e98847, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0098847. [Open access]","Hanlong Gu, Chongyang Huan, and Fengjiao Yang, “Spatiotemporal Dynamics of Ecological Vulnerability and Its Influencing Factors in Shenyang City of China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 2 (2023): 1525, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20021525. [Open access]","Lei Fan, Zixian Li, and Yan Zhou, “Seasonal Outdoor Thermal Comfort and Neutral PET Thresholds in a Severe Cold Dwa Climate City of Shenyang China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 15265, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-46003-0. [Open access]","Shiyun Wang, Jiaoyue Wang, Fengming Xi, Yan Yin, Na Zhao et al., “Variations in Wetland Soil Labile Organic Carbon Fractions and Microbial Characteristics along Urban-Rural Gradients,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 26243, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-12080-w. [Open access]","S. Wells Williams, <i>A History of China, Being the Historical Chapters from “The Middle Kingdom”</i> (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1897), 44–45, 455, https://archive.org/details/ahistorychinabe00willgoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Skyline_of_Hunnan_District%2C_Shenyang_drone_view_3.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Hunnan_District%2C_Shenyang_drone_view_3.jpg","title":"Shenyang","desc":"Shenyang, seen from a distance.","credit":"Techyan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Hunnan_District,_Shenyang_drone_view_3.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Shenyang."}},
{"id":"gc-018","num":18,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shanxi","answerText":"Shanxi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yungang caves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">471 – 494 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pingyao listed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shanxi"},"facts":[["Capital","Taiyuan"],["Population","34.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Taiyuan"],["Area","156,700 km² (60,500 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"山西","pinyin":"Shānxī","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Shanxi</b> is a province of northern China on the eastern side of the Loess Plateau, shaped as two mountain ranges flanking a trough: the Taihang east, the Lüliang west, and a chain of basins between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land falls from the north-east towards the south-west and is cut into fragments by dense gullies, which is what makes soil erosion the province’s abiding problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Buddhist inheritance is the greatest thing it holds: on the southern cliff of Wuzhou Mountain, west of Datong, stand the Yungang Grottoes, one of China’s four major grotto complexes and a World Heritage Site since 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cave 6 was cut under Emperor Xiaowen of the Northern Wei and finished before the court moved to Luoyang in 494, its central pillar, 37 scenes from the life of the Buddha and Sinicized statues marking the point at which Buddhist art in China became Chinese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pingyao, in the middle of the province, is one of the four best-preserved ancient cities in the country and went onto the World Heritage list in 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Inside its walls the lacquerware, the paper-cuts, the Liu He Tai pillows and the cloth shoes are still made, and tourism has become the town’s pillar industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Coal is the fact that shapes everything else here: more than two fifths of the province’s surface sits over coal mines, and it is one of the country’s critical energy supply bases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Twelve mining areas are planned across it, and long mining has left subsidence over a wide area, damaging more than a thousand villages, so the province is now a national pilot for putting that ground back in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its vegetation divides north from south — deciduous broadleaf and mixed forest below, temperate scrub and semi-arid grassland above — and the northern part lies inside the national sandstorm source control project.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All of it lies within the core conservation zone of the Yellow River Basin, which the Fen reaches by running the length of the trough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Guofeng Dang, Guibin Li, and Jinzhou Hu, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Driving Mechanisms of Multiple Scales Ecological Security in Shanxi Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 11626, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-44386-8. [Open access]","Lulu Chen, Huabin Chai, Weibing Du, Zengzeng Lian, Yu Wang et al., “Spatiotemporal Dynamics of Ecological Quality and Its Drivers in Shanxi Province and Its Planned Mining Areas,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 31023, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15550-3. [Open access]","Yan Lu, Yu Yan, Mengyao Wang, and Long Zhou, “Study on the Influencing Factors of Photovoltaic Siting in Coal Mining Subsidence Areas: Taking Shanxi Province as an Example,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 43603, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-26672-z. [Open access]","Jinfeng Wang, Ya Li, Sheng Wang, Qing Li, Lingfeng Li, and Xiaoling Liu, “Assessment of Multiple Ecosystem Services and Ecological Security Pattern in Shanxi Province, China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 6 (2023): 4819, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20064819. [Open access]","Shangxiao Qiao, Zeao Wang, Runping Zhang, Yu Wang, Cen Wang et al., “Microbial Community Analysis and Environmental Association in Cave 6 of the Yungang Grottoes,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 13, no. 12 (2025): 2788, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms13122788. [Open access]","Xing Zhang and Jasni Dolah, “Designing Strategies of Pingyao Lacquerware Tourist Souvenirs Based on Tourists’ Demand,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 7 (2024): e0305662, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0305662. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/61292-Yungang-Grottoes_%2828498548881%29.jpg/1920px-61292-Yungang-Grottoes_%2828498548881%29.jpg","title":"the Yungang Grottoes, Shanxi","desc":"The Yungang Grottoes.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:61292-Yungang-Grottoes_(28498548881).jpg","alt":"A colossal seated Buddha carved from a cliff."}},
{"id":"gc-515","num":515,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Fuzhou","answerText":"Fuzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1842</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Fujian","dot":"Fuzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Fujian"],["Population","8.29M (2020)"],["Rank in China","39th largest"],["Population growth","+16.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"福州","pinyin":"Fúzhōu","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Fuzhou</b> is the capital of Fujian and the political, economic and cultural centre of the province, standing on its eastern coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground is high in the north-west and low in the south-east, a pattern local description calls mountains backing the sea: extensions of the Daiyun range inland, hilly terraces through the middle, and river valley and coastal plain along the seaboard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That sequence of mountain, hill, plain and coast within the bounds of one city is unusually complete, and its irregular shore runs past a great many islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Min, the largest river in Fujian, crosses the whole territory from north-west to south-east and enters the East China Sea at Mawei, building an estuarine delta as it goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was one of the five ports opened to British residence and trade by the treaty signed at Nanking in 1842, alongside Canton, Amoy, Ningpo and Shanghai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Summers here are punishing, and Fuzhou is counted one of the four furnace cities of China, with a strong urban heat island standing over its built-up core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Who bears that heat is not evenly distributed across the city, and it has been assessed for the justice of the arrangement as well as for the temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The metropolitan area built around the city was the second in the country to be approved at national level, and it reaches well beyond the municipal boundary into the towns along the Min.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Land-use simulation and carbon assessment are now run together over that area to work out what it should become, rather than deciding the two questions one after the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its ecological and recreational functions are planned together rather than separately, on the reasoning that in a city hemmed between hills and water the same ground has to do both jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Junting Bai, Rui Sun, Yifan Liu, Jie Chen, and Xiaohe Li, “Integrating Ecological and Recreational Functions to Optimize Ecological Security Pattern in Fuzhou City,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 778, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-84660-1. [Open access]","Fengjuan Zhou, Juan Wang, Zhongxiang Li, Daoyuan Zhou, Tingting Hu, and Fuhe Wang, “Multi-Scenario Simulation of Land Use Change and Landscape Ecological Vulnerability Analysis in Fuzhou City,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 6331, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-35642-y. [Open access]","Fengxiao Cao, Yimeng Zhou, Yu Luo, Yuming Shang, Jinsu Yang, and Di Yang, “A VSD-Based Framework for Assessing Climate Justice in Urban Outdoor Cooling Spaces: A Case Study of Fuzhou,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 13 (2025): 1724719, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1724719. [Open access]","Qiuyi Zhang, Ronghui Huang, Changhua Zhu, Liyun Huang, and Di Yang, “Integrating Land Use Simulation and Carbon Assessment for Sustainable Urban Planning in Fuzhou Metropolitan Area,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 30382, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-13961-w. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, ed., <i>Treaties between the Empire of China and Foreign Powers</i>, 4th ed. (Shanghai: North-China Herald, 1902), 1–5, https://archive.org/details/treatiesbetween00mayegoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Fuzhou_Gulou_District_Panorama.jpg/1920px-Fuzhou_Gulou_District_Panorama.jpg","title":"Fuzhou","desc":"Fuzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"DannyFangles, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuzhou_Gulou_District_Panorama.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Fuzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-019","num":19,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Chongqing","answerText":"Chongqing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wartime industry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1938 – 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Core widened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2020</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Chongqing"},"facts":[["Capital","Chongqing"],["Population","32.1M (2020)"],["Largest city","Chongqing"],["Area","82,400 km² (31,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","city"],"traditional":"重慶","hanzi":"重庆","pinyin":"Chóngqìng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Chongqing</b> is one of the four municipalities of China, governed directly from Beijing rather than through a province, and divided into 38 districts and counties of which 26 are districts, eight counties and four autonomous counties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands at the confluence of the Jialing and the Yangtze, in the low hills at the eastern end of the Sichuan basin, and is the standing example in the country of a mountain city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1938 the National Resources Commission moved its defence industry into the five south-western provinces, with more of its projects here than anywhere else, and the city became the political and economic centre of the Chinese hinterland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a factory needed from its ground changed with the war: before it, rail and road and water together; during it, water alone — and above all concealment, which is why some works were dug into the mountainsides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commission had one power plant before the war and seven by the end of it, and by December 1945 it held 128 industrial and mining enterprises across the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The built-up core of the modern city is Yuzhong, a small district of steep ground carrying 21 parks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government set out a pattern in 2014 of one urban area and two town groups, in which the city proper leads the urban clusters of the Three Gorges Reservoir area and of the Wuling mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That city proper was widened from nine districts to 21 in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jialing, which joins the Yangtze in the middle of the built-up area, supplies the city with industrial, farm and domestic water and is a conservation area for the upper Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Together with Chengdu it forms an economic circle described as the fourth pole of growth in the country, and the light it throws at night has spread outwards from the two cities into the country between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yudan Zhang, Yuanqing Li, Yanan Chen, Shirao Liu, and Qingyuan Yang, “Spatiotemporal Heterogeneity of Urban Land Expansion and Urban Population Growth under New Urbanization,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 13 (2022): 7792, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19137792. [Open access]","Yangjie Wu, “Searching for the Heritage of the Second Sino-Japanese War: A Study on the Site Selection Strategy of the Defence Industrial Buildings of the National Resources Commission (1937–1945),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 11 (2024): e0311436, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0311436. [Open access]","Aiping Gou, Xuyuan Wang, Jiangbo Wang, Chenjie Wang, and Guanzheng Tan, “Spatial Pattern and Heterogeneity of Green View Index in Mountainous Cities: A Case Study of Yuzhong District,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 12576, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97946-9. [Open access]","Maolan Zhang, Guoming Zeng, Dong Liang, Yiran Xu, Yan Li et al., “An Analysis of the Colony Structure of Prokaryotes in the Jialing River Waters in Chongqing,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 9 (2022): 5525, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19095525. [Open access]","Wenli Liang, Rui Liu, and Pinglang Kou, “Nighttime Light Dynamics Reveal Peri-Urban Brightening and Population Decoupling in the Chengdu Chongqing Economic Circle,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 4601, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-34706-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Dazu_2007_685_full_view.jpg/1920px-Dazu_2007_685_full_view.jpg","title":"the Dazu Rock Carvings, Chongqing","desc":"The Dazu Rock Carvings.","credit":"G41rn8, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dazu_2007_685_full_view.jpg","alt":"Painted figures carved in relief along a rock face."}},
{"id":"gc-516","num":516,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Xi'an","answerText":"Xi'an","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Qin Shihuang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">259 – 210 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 907 CE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shaanxi","dot":"Xi'an"},"facts":[["Province","Shaanxi"],["Population","12.2M (2020)"],["Rank in China","11th largest"],["Population growth","+43.9% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"西安","pinyin":"Xī'ān","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Xi’an</b> is the capital of Shaanxi and stands where Chang’an stood, the capital of the Tang and the point from which the Silk Road ran west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tang Chang’an was laid out as a grid of walled wards around a Palace City and an Imperial City, with two markets in it, and its eastern and western halves were governed as separate counties, Wannian and Chang’an.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wards had characters of their own — Pingkang, where the courtesans lived, Xuanyang with its offices, Tongyi with its temple — and a Tang tale such as <i>The Tale of Li Wa</i> can be followed ward by ward across the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genomes recovered from burials of that period are mostly indistinguishable from the Neolithic farmers of the middle Yellow River, though several carry Western Eurasian ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Hui, the largest of Shaanxi’s ethnic minorities and Muslim in the main, trace their origins here to the same dynasty, Han families having joined with Arab, Persian, Mongol and Uyghur ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> A thousand years before any of that the First Emperor was buried east of the modern city, under a mound guarded by pits of life-sized ceramic soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their bronze weapons were long held to owe their condition to a chromium anti-rust coating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chromium turned out to have leached from nearby lacquer after burial, and the credit belongs to the soil the army was laid in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two cemeteries beside the mausoleum say something about how it was built: at Liyi lie local Qin workers who ate millet, and at Shanren prisoners thrown into a mass grave, some in iron leg shackles, who had eaten a poorer and more mixed diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How the complex was raised, and by whom, is still only partly known, and what those bones record of diet is among the few direct answers there are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Zhaoyi Ma, Jie He, and Shuaishuai Liu, “Representation of the Spatio-Temporal Narrative of The Tale of Li Wa,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 4 (2020): e0231529, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0231529. [Open access]","Minglei Lv et al., “Ancient Genomes from the Tang Dynasty Capital Reveal the Genetic Legacy of Trans-Eurasian Communication at the Eastern End of Silk Road,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 22 (2024): 267, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11577736/. [Open access]","Ying Ma, Benjamin T. Fuller, Weigang Sun, Songmei Hu, Liang Chen et al., “Tracing the Locality of Prisoners and Workers at the Mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang: First Emperor of China (259-210 BC),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6, no. 1 (2016): 26731, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep26731. [Open access]","Marcos Martinón-Torres, Xiuzhen Li, Yin Xia, Agnese Benzonelli, Andrew Bevan et al., “Surface Chromium on Terracotta Army Bronze Weapons Is Neither an Ancient Anti-Rust Treatment nor the Reason for Their Good Preservation,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 9, no. 1 (2019): 5289, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-40613-7. [Open access]","Li Zhang, Fei Zhan, Wenfang Zhang, Sanping Song, and Shisheng Zhu, “Forensic Characteristics of 38 Y-STR Loci in the Hui Population from Shaanxi Province, Northwest China,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 19 (2024): e38501, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e38501. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Skyline_of_Xi%27an_CBD.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Xi%27an_CBD.jpg","title":"Xi'an","desc":"Xi'an, seen from a distance.","credit":"Derxxxxx, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Xi%27an_CBD.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Xi'an."}},
{"id":"gc-020","num":20,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Heilongjiang","answerText":"Heilongjiang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Railway contract</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Blackland rules</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2021</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Heilongjiang"},"facts":[["Capital","Harbin"],["Population","31.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Harbin"],["Area","473,000 km² (183,000 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"黑龍江","hanzi":"黑龙江","pinyin":"Hēilóngjiāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Heilongjiang</b> is the northernmost and easternmost province of China, at the top of a north-east that meets Russia and Mongolia along its northern and western edges and North Korea in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cold that deep is what makes its soil: plant matter piles up faster than it can rot, and the residue is the black humus layer the region is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That black soil is rich in organic matter and holds a great deal of carbon, and it lies under cultivated land, forest, grassland, wetland and lake alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is now at high risk of degradation — worn by erosion on slopes, by wind, by tillage, by snowmelt runoff and by repeated freezing and thawing — and the province passed regulations on protecting and using its blackland in 2021.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Bei’an, where the ground has been farmed a long time, the organic matter and the nitrogen in the soil have both been falling year on year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What drew the modern province was a railway: after the Chinese Eastern Railway contract of 1898, Russia built railway workshops and locomotive repair shops at Harbin, Manzhouli and other places along the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan supplanted Russian influence in the north-east after the Russo-Japanese War of 1905 and founded the South Manchuria Railway Company to run railways, mining and steel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The coal and iron ore under the region laid the groundwork for the heavy industry that followed, and the north-east now carries hundreds of listed industrial heritage sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The province holds more forest than any other in China, though its state forest areas have long leaned on felling and processing timber rather than on anything of higher value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its cities are built for a cold season that runs more than three months, and Harbin, its capital, is the standing example in the country of a cold-region city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]","Chen Qu, Wen Li, Jia Xu, and Song Shi, “Blackland Conservation and Utilization, Carbon Storage and Ecological Risk in Green Space,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 4 (2023): 3154, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20043154. [Open access]","Jia Sui and Yingjie Dai, “Analysis of Conservation Practices for Black Soil Based on Organic Matter and Nitrogen Contents in the Black Soil Region of Northeast China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 23989, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-09426-9. [Open access]","Shuo Diao and Yude Geng, “Efficiency Evaluation and Nonlinear Multi-Objective Optimization of Forestry Industry Transformation,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13, no. 1 (2023): 21216, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-47953-5. [Open access]","Wente Pan, Shuqi Li, Yang Ye, Yuan Huang, Haocheng Liu et al., “Morphological Pattern of Building Clusters in Cold Regions: Evidence from Harbin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 24 (2022): 17083, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192417083. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Jingpo_Lake%2C_rowing_boat.jpg","title":"Jingpo Lake, Heilongjiang","desc":"Jingpo Lake.","credit":"drnan tu, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jingpo_Lake,_rowing_boat.jpg","alt":"A lake held behind a lava dam among wooded hills."}},
{"id":"gc-517","num":517,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Guiyang","answerText":"Guiyang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dasongshan tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">206 BCE – 1644 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Qianling park</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1957</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Guizhou","dot":"Guiyang"},"facts":[["Province","Guizhou"],["Population","5.99M (2020)"],["Rank in China","65th largest"],["Population growth","+38.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"貴陽","hanzi":"贵阳","pinyin":"Guìyáng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Guiyang</b> is the capital of Guizhou and stands among the hills at the middle of the province, on the watershed between the Yangtze and the Pearl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Very little of its ground is flat — mountain and hill take nearly all of it — so the city has had to be built by cutting into slopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tombs at Dasongshan, at Machang in the Gui’an New Area, were named among China’s ten leading archaeological finds of 2022, and they testify to the changes central Guizhou went through between the Han and the Ming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes from eight of the people buried there under the Song and the Ming are closest to farmers of the Yellow River Basin, with clear southern influences from Guangxi, coastal Fujian and Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of them are close instead to the Hmong-Mien peoples of the region, so more than one ancestry is represented in the same burial ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Migrants from the Central Plains and the Jiangnan brought ox-ploughing with them, which changed the swidden farming the Hmong-Mien speakers had practised in these mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rock beneath the city is Triassic dolomite and limestone, and the karst formed in it runs through the whole catchment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aha Reservoir in the south-west drains that karst country into a lake fed by five rivers, and the wetland park around it was named a national demonstration site in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three of the city’s parks were made by turning remnant mountain forest into public ground — Qianlingshan in 1957, Huaguoyuan in 2010, Yuelianghu in 2020 — and the old forest in them still carries more native species than the planted ground beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the city burns shows in its air: over the Huaxi district sulphate rather than nitrate dominates the water-soluble ions, where in a developed city nitrate would predominate instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yongfei Luan, Guohe Huang, Guanghui Zheng, and Yuee Wang, “Correlation between Spatio-Temporal Evolution of Habitat Quality and Human Activity Intensity in Typical Karst Basins,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 21 (2022): 14294, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192114294. [Open access]","Wen Wan, Huangzhen Huang, Changguo Hu, Xinglong Zhang, Qingting Zhou et al., “Ancient Genomes in Southwest China Revealed Genetic Interactions among Diverse Populations in the Historical Period,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23, no. 1 (2025): 174, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-025-02299-4. [Open access]","Yin Su, Bingze Ling, and Jintong Ren, “Seasonal and Spatial Variations in Eutrophication and Water Quality of Lake Aha, Guiyang City, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 5 (2025): e0321562, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0321562. [Open access]","Weize Wang, Xiaoyan Gao, Chunhua Cen, Mengping Jian, Zijin Wang, and Jingyi Yang, “Impact of Transforming Karst Mountainous Forests into Urban Parks on Plant Diversity Patterns,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 8 (2024): e70194, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70194. [Open access]","Yunwu Li, Xianqin Wang, Peng Xu, Jiaqun Gui, Xingqiang Guo et al., “Chemical Characterization and Source Identification of PM2.5 in the Huaxi Urban Area of Guiyang,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 30451, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-81048-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Guiyang_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Guiyang_Skyline.jpg","title":"Guiyang","desc":"Guiyang, seen from a distance.","credit":"Ryedamien, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guiyang_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Guiyang."}},
{"id":"gc-021","num":21,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Xinjiang","answerText":"Xinjiang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Water mapped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1900 – 2020s</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Xinjiang"},"facts":[["Capital","Ürümqi"],["Population","25.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Ürümqi"],["Area","1,664,900 km² (642,800 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"新疆","pinyin":"Xīnjiāng","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Xinjiang</b> is an autonomous region in the far north-west and by a long way the largest of China’s provincial-level divisions, about a sixth of the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its structure is three mountain ranges flanking two basins: the Altai along the north, the Tianshan across the middle and the Kunlun along the southern rim, with the Junggar and the Tarim lying between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what fills those basins is desert, gobi and thin scrub, so water decides where anyone can live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The corridors between those ranges have carried people for thousands of years, and the earliest inhabitants of the north-west were formed about 6,000 years ago out of Ancient North Eurasian and Altai hunter-gatherer ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the early Bronze Age ancestry arrived at once from southern Central Asia and from the steppe, and by the Iron Age eastern Eurasian ancestry out of the Baikal region had become prominent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The eastern Tianshan was a channel in both directions, East Asian millet and painted pottery moving along it one way and West Asian wheat, barley, bronze and livestock the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An indigenous Tarim ancestry ran through all of that and on into the historical period, while the Iron Age people of the eastern Tianshan were formed from millet farmers and steppe herders together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pattern of the region’s rivers and canals has been reconstructed over 120 years from old survey sheets: dispersed in 1900, it drew together after the middle of the 20th century and has held that shape since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Out on the oasis plains it is cropland, irrigation and water policy that have driven the change, and the region now grows the great bulk of China’s cotton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Along the northern slope of the Tianshan, where most of the towns are, built-up land and dry farmland are both growing, grassland is shrinking fastest, and the glaciers are under increasing human pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Nuo Li, Wenjie Yu, Kexin Liu, Yihang Zhou, Hanlu Zhang, and Haiwei Zhang, “Reveal the Deployable Solar Energy Potential and Emission Reduction Benefits in the Arid Areas of Xinjiang,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 10437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-40841-8. [Open access]","Xue Zhao, Daxuan Zhang, Bing Sun, Shasha Yang, Yimeng Qi et al., “Tracing Bronze to Iron Age Population Dynamics in Northwest Xinjiang Using Ancient Time-Series Genomic Data,” <i>Genome Biology</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): 33, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-026-03943-0. [Open access]","Xiaomin Yang, Xinyue Zou, Haijun Li, Xiaoyu Yang, Bo Wang et al., “Ancient Genomes Uncover Dynamic Cultural and Genetic Interplay in the Eastern Tianshan,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 43, no. 3 (2026): msag057, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msag057. [Open access]","Raorao Su and Zhen Zhao, “Long-Term Evolution of Hydrographic Network Density in Arid Xinjiang, China: Reconstruction from Historical Maps,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 18764, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-49759-7. [Open access]","Hongbo Wang, Fuchang Jiang, and Xingpeng Wang, “Evaluation of Cotton Production Sustainability and Water Footprints in the Oasis Area of Southern Xinjiang,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 17 (2026): 1789172, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1789172. [Open access]","Lina Tang, Alimujiang Kasimu, Haitao Ma, and Mamattursun Eziz, “Monitoring Multi-Scale Ecological Change and Its Potential Drivers in the Economic Zone of the Tianshan Mountains' North Slope,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 4 (2023): 2844, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20042844. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/The_Heavenly_Lake_%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0_%288064381762%29.jpg","title":"the Heavenly Lake of Tianshan, Xinjiang","desc":"The Heavenly Lake in the Tian Shan.","credit":"Caitriana Nicholson from 北京 ~ Beijing, 中国 ~ China, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Heavenly_Lake_%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0_(8064381762).jpg","alt":"An alpine lake below snow-covered mountains."}},
{"id":"gc-518","num":518,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Taiyuan","answerText":"Taiyuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jinyang founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">497 BCE</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shanxi","dot":"Taiyuan"},"facts":[["Province","Shanxi"],["Population","5.30M (2020)"],["Rank in China","80th largest"],["Population growth","+26.2% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"太原","pinyin":"Tàiyuán","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Taiyuan</b> is the capital of Shanxi and stands at the northern end of the Jinzhong Basin, on ground that runs from mountain through hill and plain to valley, a shape often likened to a dustpan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 497 BCE Zhao Yang, a noble of the state of Jin, founded Jinyang along the Fen here, and the city that grew from it was a political and military centre of China for over a thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives of Jinyang’s western wall runs for some 800 metres (2,600 feet) in Jinyuan district, its rammed layers still visible, part of it above ground and part still buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plants have taken the wall, and they both hold the earth together and break it apart, which is why conservators now argue about whether that green cap is a disease or a defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Jin Temple south-west of the city is among the earliest royal gardens in China and the best-preserved Tang- and Song-style temple garden still standing, with its painted sculptures, inscriptions and plaques.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Ninety-three heritage trees of fourteen species grow in its grounds, one cypress counted among the Three Wonders of the Jin Temple and a stand of old ginkgos remembered for Wang Qiong, a Ming official.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across the city the commonest heritage trees are jujube and pagoda tree, the jujube being one of the oldest cultivated fruits in the world, domesticated on the middle and lower Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A basin holds on to whatever a city puts into the air, and ozone runs above the national standard here through the summer, peaking in the middle of the afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The farmland around the city was long watered with sewage, and its soils were tested for seven heavy metals: the averages fell below the national risk values, except arsenic and cadmium at three points in Xiaodian district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Industry, fertiliser, pesticide and the irrigation water itself were all named as sources, in a city whose population is ageing quickly and whose summers are getting hotter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Yang Pan, Shijie Wang, Chunping Xie, and C. Y. Jim, “Species Diversity and Spatial Pattern of Heritage Trees in Taiyuan,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 17584, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-99731-0. [Open access]","Ruihua Shang, Chenyang Li, Xiaoju Yang, Pengju Han, and Weiwei Liu, “Research on MICP Restoration Technology for Earthen City Walls Damaged by Primary Vegetation Capping in China,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 13, no. 12 (2025): 2802, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms13122802. [Open access]","Yifei Chen, Lin Ma, Chaomin Ren, Xiaojuan Yang, Lifang Zhao et al., “Projected Cardiovascular Mortality Attributable to Extreme Temperatures under Climate Scenarios in Taiyuan,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 3 (2026): 114970, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.114970. [Open access]","Lili Guo, Tianyu Gao, Bingxi Wang, Yang Cui, Qiusheng He et al., “Spatial Characteristics, Sources of Volatile Organic Compounds and Effects on O3 Formation in Summer in Taiyuan,” <i>Toxics</i> 14, no. 3 (2026): 220, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics14030220. [Open access]","Ying Zhao, Han Yan, and Fei Wang, “Distribution, Source, and Ecological Risk of Heavy Metals in Sewage Irrigation of Taiyuan, Shanxi Province,” <i>Toxics</i> 12, no. 2 (2024): 120, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics12020120. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Skyline_of_Taiyuan%2C_Oct_1_2023.png/1920px-Skyline_of_Taiyuan%2C_Oct_1_2023.png","title":"Taiyuan","desc":"Taiyuan, seen from a distance.","credit":"A Chinese ID, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Taiyuan,_Oct_1_2023.png","alt":"A wide view of Taiyuan."}},
{"id":"gc-022","num":22,"category":"Geography","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Gansu","answerText":"Gansu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Millet farmed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,900 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wheat arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,200 BP</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Gansu"},"facts":[["Capital","Lanzhou"],["Population","25.0M (2020)"],["Largest city","Lanzhou"],["Area","425,800 km² (164,400 sq mi)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","asia","china","province"],"traditional":"甘肅","hanzi":"甘肃","pinyin":"Gānsù","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Gansu</b> is a province deep inland in north-western China, most of it standing on the second of the country’s great terraces, with plateau, mountain and desert interleaved across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Arid and semi-arid country covers three quarters of the province, and soil erosion and desertification are its constant problems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its north-western half is the Hexi Corridor, a trench running from the Wushaoling mountains to the province’s western edge, with three inland river systems rising in it and a string of oases along them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That corridor is the far end of the eastern Silk Road, whose route from Xi’an to Dunhuang crosses loess plateau, the north-eastern rim of the Tibetan Plateau and the corridor itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Millet farming came west along it early: charred grains directly dated at Dadiwan, in Qin’an, give about 7,900 years ago, the earliest on the whole route, while wheat and barley did not reach China until roughly 5,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Gansu is a contact zone in the fullest sense, its people carrying Yellow River, western Eurasian steppe and Tibetan Plateau ancestry, and places such as Linxia and Xiahe are where languages have long mixed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Buddhism that travelled the same road left the Maijishan and Mogao grottoes, both World Heritage Sites, whose wall paintings are now threatened by the microbes living on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oases have carried nomadic and farming ways of life together since early times, and their growth has been traced across two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rain falls sparingly at the corridor’s north-western end and much more freely at its south-eastern, against an evaporation that dwarfs either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What grows is sparse and patchy, drought-tolerant shrubs and herbs on gravel, sand and clay with almost no surface humus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Hui Yu, Bo Zhang, Qianqian He, and Hou Xiao, “Primary Determinants of Water Yield Services in Arid NW China: An Empirical Analysis of Gansu Province,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 8 (2025): e0329580, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0329580. [Open access]","Haiming Li, Yingyu Qian, Zeli Wang, Nathaniel James, Yifu Cui et al., “Multiple Waves of Westward Dry-Land Agriculture Expansions along the East Silk Road during the Neolithic Age,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 6, no. 3 (2026): 1321–31, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2025.12.013. [Open access]","Xia Tang, Linshan Yang, Hao Qu, and Qi Feng, “The Evolution of Artificial Oasis over Two Millennia in Hexi Corridor, China,” <i>Heliyon</i> 9, no. 12 (2023): e22690, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2023.e22690. [Open access]","Wenxia Ma, Qiqi Chen, Fasi Wu, Dongpeng He, Yulong Duan et al., “Difference and Environmental Drivers of Bacterial Communities on Wall Paintings of the Maijishan and Mogao Grottoes, China,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 16 (2025): 1657118, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2025.1657118. [Open access]","Haodong Chen, Huangzhen Huang, Boxuan Zhao, Yiling Jiang, Mengting Xu et al., “Genetic and Linguistic Landscapes of Gansu-Qinghai Populations,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 6, no. 3 (2026): 1332–42, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2026.04.001. [Open access]","Xinyi Zhou, Zhaoxiang Zhang, James F. White, Yingxiang Miao, and Shanjia Li, “Geographical Patterns and Drivers of Species and Phylogenetic Diversity of Desert Plant Communities in the Hexi Corridor,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 9 (2025): e72114, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.72114. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Crescent_Lake_from_the_Singing_Sand_Dunes_%2820230918101214%29.jpg/1920px-Crescent_Lake_from_the_Singing_Sand_Dunes_%2820230918101214%29.jpg","title":"Crescent Lake, Gansu","desc":"Crescent Lake at Dunhuang.","credit":"N509FZ, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crescent_Lake_from_the_Singing_Sand_Dunes_(20230918101214).jpg","alt":"A crescent-shaped spring among high sand dunes."}},
{"id":"gc-520","num":520,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Harbin","answerText":"Harbin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Railway from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pneumonic plague</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1910 – 1911</span></div>","map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Heilongjiang","dot":"Harbin"},"facts":[["Province","Heilongjiang"],["Population","10.0M (2020)"],["Rank in China","19th largest"],["Population growth","-5.9% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","city","asia","china"],"traditional":"哈爾濱","hanzi":"哈尔滨","pinyin":"Hā'ěrbīn","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Harbin</b> is the capital of Heilongjiang and stands on the north-eastern part of the Northeast China Plain, in the basin of the Songhua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a plain city rather than an old walled one, grown up since the beginning of the 20th century and largely by self-organisation, with little central control over how it spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made it was a railway: after the Chinese Eastern Railway contract of 1898 Russia built railway workshops and locomotive repair shops here, and Japan supplanted that influence after the Russo-Japanese War of 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the winter of 1910 and the spring of 1911 pneumonic plague spread across the north-east, killing on a great scale and throwing the region into turmoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wu Lien-teh brought it to an end within four months by compulsory quarantine, by isolating patients, by masks, by segmented martial law and by burning the dead, and China reached for the same measures against SARS in 2003.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese army ran a bacteriological laboratory on the edge of the city, Unit 731, and the ground it stood on has been examined since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A live strain of <i>Bacillus anthracis</i> was isolated from soil taken there and confirmed by its phenotype and its whole genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A wider resampling of the country around the site found none at all, which points away from the strain having come from anything local and natural.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city is the standing example in China of a cold-region city, its cold season running more than three months, and its buildings are clustered for that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is greener than that suggests: a survey of 44 of its parks counted 38 butterfly species, with more of them on the urban fringe than at the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Wente Pan, Shuqi Li, Yang Ye, Yuan Huang, Haocheng Liu et al., “Morphological Pattern of Building Clusters in Cold Regions: Evidence from Harbin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 24 (2022): 17083, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192417083. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]","He Liu, Mingli Jiao, Siqi Zhao, Kai Xing, Ye Li et al., “Controlling Ebola: What We Can Learn from China’s 1911 Battle against the Pneumonic Plague in Manchuria,” <i>International Journal of Infectious Diseases</i> 33 (2015): 222–26, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijid.2015.02.013. [Open access]","Yarong Wu, Yuan Yuan, Bing Yuan, Jiaxin Li, Jinglin Wang, and Yujun Cui, “Bacteriologic and Genomic Investigation of <i>Bacillus anthracis</i> Isolated from World War II Site, China,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 30, no. 12 (2024): 2687–89, https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3012.231520. [Open access]","Kai Wang, Yuxin Qi, Yuandong Hu, and Dan Han, “Distribution and Influences on Butterfly Diversity in Urban Park Green Spaces: A Case Study of Harbin, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 6 (2025): e71554, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.71554. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Jihong_Bridge_and_Skyline_of_Daoli%26Daowai_District%2CHarbin_20251021.jpg/1920px-Jihong_Bridge_and_Skyline_of_Daoli%26Daowai_District%2CHarbin_20251021.jpg","title":"Harbin","desc":"Harbin, seen from a distance.","credit":"JacobLyu, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jihong_Bridge_and_Skyline_of_Daoli%26Daowai_District,Harbin_20251021.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Harbin."}},
{"id":"gc-023","num":23,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Shanghai","answerText":"Shanghai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty port</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aurore medicine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1912 – 1952</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"上海","pinyin":"Shànghǎi","translations":"","difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Shanghai"},"facts":[["Capital","Shanghai"],["Population","24.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Shanghai"],["Area","6,341 km² (2,448 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Shanghai</b> is a municipality on the eastern coast of China, standing on estuarine deltaic alluvial plain, flat country that rises hardly at all above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yangtze Estuary in front of it is one of the largest alluvial estuaries in the world, divided by Chongming Island into two branches, the southern carrying the great bulk of the runoff and sediment that reaches the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tides are semidiurnal and its tidal flats run a kilometre or two wide, reaching a good deal further at their broadest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was one of five ports opened to British residence and trade by the treaty signed at Nanking in 1842, alongside Canton, Amoy, Foochow and Ningpo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The English and the Chinese texts of that treaty do not say quite the same thing, the translators on each side having shaped their version towards the power their own side was claiming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The foreign settlements that followed brought institutions of their own: <i>l’Aurore</i>, a Jesuit and French university, taught medicine here from 1912 to 1952 without belonging wholly to the mission or to French imperial policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of its hundreds of students were not Christians, though the Jesuit administration kept a record of the baptisms among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Games arrived the same way: badminton moved out of the expatriate enclaves into Shanghai society between 1912 and 1949, and the assumptions it carried about who should play were reworked as women took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The estuary’s flats have shrunk by more than a third in three decades as land was claimed from them, though less on the Shanghai bank than the Jiangsu one, its own being fed by abundant sediment and by artificial siltation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cultivated land is still the largest single land use here by area, and turning it into construction land is what has cut the municipality’s carbon stocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Ying Chen, Jinwei Dong, Xiangming Xiao, Min Zhang, Bo Tian, Yunxuan Zhou, Bo Li, and Zhijun Ma, “Land Claim and Loss of Tidal Flats in the Yangtze Estuary,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6, no. 1 (2016): 24018, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep24018. [Open access]","Yangyang Wu, Jinli Yang, Siliang Li, Yang Zhao, Chunzi Guo, Xiaodong Yang, Yue Xu, Fujun Yue, Zhonghua Zhang, Songyu Yang, Guanghong Zhou, Haobiao Wu, Panli Yuan, and Guangjie Luo, “Decoding Carbon Pathways of Shanghai Megacity through Historical Land Use Patterns and Urban Ecosystem Transformations,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 6326, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-90755-0. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, ed., <i>Treaties between the Empire of China and Foreign Powers</i>, 4th ed. (Shanghai: North-China Herald, 1902), 1–5, https://archive.org/details/treatiesbetween00mayegoog. [Open access]","Manli Li and Xujun Tian, “Psychological Dynamics among Chinese and British Negotiators and Translators: A Text-Analytical Study of Translation Manipulation in the English–Chinese Translation of the Treaty of Nanking,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 6 (2025): e0326356, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0326356. [Open access]","Steven Pieragastini and Martin Robert, “Imperial Mission: Jesuits, French Diplomacy, and Medical Education at <i>l’Aurore</i> University in Shanghai, 1912–1952,” <i>Medical History</i> 68, no. 2 (2024): 200–216, https://doi.org/10.1017/mdh.2024.17. [Open access]","Zhuomiao Zhao, Chang Liu, and Kohei Kawashima, “From Feminine Leisure to Urban Craze: The Popularization of Badminton in Republican Shanghai,” <i>Frontiers in Sports and Active Living</i> 8 (2026): 1717411, https://doi.org/10.3389/fspor.2026.1717411. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/The_Bund_2.jpg/1920px-The_Bund_2.jpg","title":"the Bund, Shanghai","desc":"The Bund and the Pudong skyline.","credit":"钉钉, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bund_2.jpg","alt":"A riverfront of old banking houses facing modern towers."}},
{"id":"gc-521","num":521,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Ürümqi","answerText":"Ürümqi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Glacier watched</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Glacier split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1993</span></div>","traditional":"烏魯木齊","hanzi":"乌鲁木齐","pinyin":"Wūlǔmùqí","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Xinjiang","dot":"Ürümqi"},"facts":[["Region","Xinjiang"],["Population","4.05M (2020)"],["Rank in China","125th largest"],["Population growth","+30.3% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Ürümqi</b> is the capital of Xinjiang and stands on the southern margin of the Junggar Basin, with mountains on three sides including the northern slopes of the Tianshan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It lies in the heart of the Eurasian landmass and is the inland city furthest from any sea in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits in the core zone of the Silk Road Economic Belt and is a hub of the Belt and Road Initiative, its confinement in a mountain-basin system making it a small model of the problems of arid Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Being ringed by mountains leaves it prone to winter temperature inversions, when a lid of warm air settles over colder air at the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Valley winds drain cold air downslope into the lower city at night, deepening the inversion, and the wind beneath it is too slow to carry off what the city emits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the head of the Ürümqi River sits Glacier No. 1, a reference glacier of the World Glacier Monitoring Service since 1959 and among the most closely watched in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It split into an eastern and a western branch in 1993 and has been losing both area and thickness ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The built-up area has spread northwards since 2000 because the mountains leave it nowhere else to go, taking farmland with it as it went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How a block is built changes what the air above it does, and the city’s form and its pollutants have been measured against one another for their effect on its heat island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The dry air that makes life hard here has also preserved a great deal, and Xinjiang holds the richest collection of leather relics in China, kept in museums that fight a running battle with salt-tolerant, spore-forming bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Wenya Zhang, Aliya Baidourela, Fuxiang Ma, Julati Xiaokelati, Gongxin Yang, Qian Sun, and Shiwei Wang, “Policy-Driven Carbon Sink Enhancement in Dryland Cities: A Case Study of Urumqi, a Core City on China's New Silk Road,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 11083, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-40905-9. [Open access]","Ren Cai, Xian Yang, and Keming Zhao, “Simulation Study of Low-Level Disturbances Affecting Near Surface Inversion Events in Urumqi,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 21568, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-05089-8. [Open access]","Hu Yuhan, Wang Feiteng, Zhang Chunxiang, Pan Ruiqi, Du Zhencai, and Ming Jing, “Climatic Controls on Surface Albedo and Mass Balance of Urumqi Glacier No. 1 in the Last Decade, 2014–2024,” <i>iScience</i> 28, no. 12 (2025): 113942, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2025.113942. [Open access]","Jiayu Fan, Xuegang Chen, Weihong Zhang, Mei Zhao, and Xinlu Yang, “Comparison of Mediating Effects of Air Pollutants on Urban Morphology and Urban Heat Island Intensity at Block Scale,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 18433, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-02665-w. [Open access]","Yuanyuan Wang, Yu Wang, Lilong Hou, Liwen Zhong, Hailiang Yang et al., “Assessment of Airborne and Surface Microbes on Leather Cultural Relics in Museums of Arid Regions Represented by Xinjiang, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 35107, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-19013-7. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Urumqi_Skyline_July_2019.jpg/1920px-Urumqi_Skyline_July_2019.jpg","title":"Ürümqi","desc":"Ürümqi, seen from a distance.","credit":"Albertaont, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urumqi_Skyline_July_2019.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Ürümqi."}},
{"id":"gc-024","num":24,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Jilin","answerText":"Jilin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Baekdu eruption</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 946 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"吉林","pinyin":"Jílín","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jilin"},"facts":[["Capital","Changchun"],["Population","24.1M (2020)"],["Largest city","Changchun"],["Area","187,400 km² (72,400 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Jilin</b> is a province in the middle of north-eastern China, its land tilting down from south-east to north-west, mountain and hill in the east giving way to plain across the centre and the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of that plain is black soil, a dark earth rich in organic matter that formed on loess-like clay, on river and flood deposits, on moraine and on windblown material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is fertile enough to have made the province one of the country’s principal grain bases, and the black soil belt of north-eastern China holds about an eighth of the world’s black soils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That soil is being worn away, and long intensive cultivation has thinned the plough layer, hardened the pan beneath it and cut the organic matter above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jilin passed the country’s first local regulations for protecting black land, and has taken conservation tillage across a large and still growing share of its fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the east the Changbai range carries some of the best-preserved temperate forest in Asia, its slopes banded by height from mixed conifer and broadleaf through birch to alpine grassland at the top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the head of that range stands a volcano the Chinese call Changbaishan and the Koreans Paektu, sitting astride the border between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its Millennium Eruption began late in 946 and was among the largest of the Common Era, laying ash across Russia and Japan and leaving its signal in Greenland ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its effect on climate was smaller than that suggests: the sulphur fell out quickly and in winter, so the aerosol veil it raised was a short-lived one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the modern era the province’s industry was framed around Changchun’s motor works, with power and chemicals beside it, and its film studio is now counted among the north-east’s industrial heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Yue Zhu, Jiafu Liu, and Baihao Zhang, “Evaluation and Trade-Offs/Synergies of Ecosystem Services in Jilin Province,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 24873, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-10338-x. [Open access]","Xu Sun, Rui Yan, Ling Zhao, and Yunxian Yan, “Determinants of Farmers' Decision-Making Behavior for Black Soil Protection in Northeast China — A Case of Jilin Province,” <i>PLOS One</i> 20, no. 12 (2025): e0339285, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0339285. [Open access]","Guzailinuer Aihemaitijiang, Lujuan Zhang, Mingtang Li, Yanan Chen, Jiquan Zhang, Feng Zhang, and Chunli Zhao, “PAH Contamination, Sources and Health Risks in Black Soil Region of Jilin Province, China,” <i>Toxics</i> 12, no. 12 (2024): 937, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics12120937. [Open access]","Zhuoma Wan, Yunga Wu, Peng Zhang, Zhijing Xie, Donghui Wu, and Stefan Scheu, “Drivers of Centipede and Spider Diversity and Biomass Along an Elevation Gradient on Changbai Mountain, China,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 15, no. 10 (2025): e72074, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.72074. [Open access]","Giyoon Lee, Andrea Burke, William Hutchison, Patrick Sugden, Celeste Smith et al., “Phasing and Climate Forcing Potential of the Millennium Eruption of Mt. Baekdu,” <i>Communications Earth &amp; Environment</i> 5, no. 1 (2024): 549, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-024-01713-z. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/%E4%BB%8E%E9%95%BF%E7%99%BD%E5%B1%B1%E8%A5%BF%E5%9D%A1%E7%9C%8B%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0-2017-08-24_1.jpg/1920px-%E4%BB%8E%E9%95%BF%E7%99%BD%E5%B1%B1%E8%A5%BF%E5%9D%A1%E7%9C%8B%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0-2017-08-24_1.jpg","title":"Heaven Lake, Jilin","desc":"Heaven Lake in the Changbai Mountains.","credit":"Antigng, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BB%8E%E9%95%BF%E7%99%BD%E5%B1%B1%E8%A5%BF%E5%9D%A1%E7%9C%8B%E5%A4%A9%E6%B1%A0-2017-08-24_1.jpg","alt":"A crater lake ringed by bare volcanic peaks."}},
{"id":"gc-522","num":522,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Lanzhou","answerText":"Lanzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built-up growth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2000 – 2020</span></div>","traditional":"蘭州","hanzi":"兰州","pinyin":"Lánzhōu","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Gansu","dot":"Lanzhou"},"facts":[["Province","Gansu"],["Population","4.36M (2020)"],["Rank in China","115th largest"],["Population growth","+20.6% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Lanzhou</b> is the capital of Gansu and sits at the geometric centre of China’s continental land map, with mountain ranges to its north and south and the Yellow River running between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A valley leaves a city nowhere to spread but along itself, so Lanzhou has grown as a belt, and its road network has taken the same strip shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The river enters at the confluence with the Datong and leaves at Dabaogou, crossing six counties and districts on its way through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city has given its name to one of the three most famous fast foods in China: Lanzhou beef noodles, eaten here for more than two hundred years and now listed as part of the country’s intangible cultural heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A bowl of them turns on the bouillon rather than the meat, and the city sells about a million of them a day across more than 1,500 restaurants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It stands on the upper Yellow River in the transition between the monsoon and non-monsoon climates, its natural vegetation grassland of herbs, with the Tengger and Badain Jaran deserts along its edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same valley that shapes the city traps its air: the atmosphere over it is stably stratified and calm days are frequent, which keeps industrial and heating emissions from dispersing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That once made this one of the most polluted places in the country, and it remains a political and industrial centre for the north-west with a high proportion of heavy industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its built-up area grew far faster in the first decade of this century than in the second, the valley having filled up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It anchors the Lanzhou–Xining agglomeration, nine prefecture-level units across two provinces, counted an ecological barrier for the upper Yellow River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Minan Yang, Yongsheng Qian, Xin Li, Yueqing Ou, and Junwei Zeng, “River Valley Urban Network and Morphology: A Study on the Urban Morphology Evolution of Lanzhou,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0302686, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0302686. [Open access]","Zheqi Zhang, Jiaolong Jiang, Mingwu Zang, Kaihua Zhang, Dan Li, and Xiaoman Li, “Flavor Profile Analysis of Instant and Traditional Lanzhou Beef Bouillons Using HS-SPME-GC/MS, Electronic Nose and Electronic Tongue,” <i>Bioengineering</i> 9, no. 10 (2022): 582, https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering9100582. [Open access]","Juanli Li, Chengyu Feng, Xuanlong Ma, and Duo Wu, “Impact of the Urban Atmospheric Environment on Otolaryngologic Disease Outpatient Visits in Lanzhou, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 21556, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-07352-4. [Open access]","Jin Ma, Xuan Yang, Xiaodan Li, Lide Zhang, Jiaojiao Lei, Lina Wang, and Xuelu Liu, “Research on Temporal-Spatial Distribution Differences and Formation Mechanisms of NPP in the Lanzhou Section of the Yellow River Mainstream,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 29132, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-15226-y. [Open access]","Tonghui Yu, Mengru Wang, Xinyu Li, Xuan Zhu, Mengting Yang, and Jiqiang Niu, “Land Spatial Optimization in Ecologically Fragile Regions of the Lanzhou Xining Urban Agglomeration Based on a Multidimensional Ecosystem Framework,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 8533, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-38134-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Lanzhou_Skyline_201906.jpg/1920px-Lanzhou_Skyline_201906.jpg","title":"Lanzhou","desc":"Lanzhou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Pieceofmetalwork, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lanzhou_Skyline_201906.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Lanzhou."}},
{"id":"gc-025","num":25,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Inner Mongolia","answerText":"Inner Mongolia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Erdaojingzi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3700 – 3330 BP</span></div>","traditional":"內蒙古","hanzi":"内蒙古","pinyin":"Nèi Měnggǔ","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Inner Mongolia"},"facts":[["Capital","Hohhot"],["Population","24.0M (2020)"],["Largest city","Chifeng"],["Area","1,183,000 km² (456,800 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Inner Mongolia</b> is an autonomous region stretched along China’s northern edge, mostly plateau, far from any ocean and rimmed with mountains, so its climate is markedly continental.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rainfall falls away westward against a far higher evaporation, which leaves the greater part of it arid or semi-arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Grassland, forest and desert are all extensive here, and of the grassland just over half is typical steppe, three tenths meadow steppe and a sixth desert steppe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> People have lived off this ground a very long time: the Hongshan culture, one of the earliest complex societies in Neolithic East Asia, spread over a great stretch of country reaching from what is now Inner Mongolia into Liaoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Erdaojingzi, in Chifeng, stands a Bronze Age settlement so well preserved that it is called the Pompeii of the East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Millet fed the people there and their pigs and dogs alike, while their cattle and sheep grazed more widely, and their ancestry was closest to the farming populations of the Yellow River rather than to the earlier Neolithic groups of the west Liao country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The grassland has been degrading since the 1960s, and satellite records from 1982 to 2020 find most of it worn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The division runs along the 115th meridian, the west far barer than the east, and restoration programmes begun in the early 2000s have slowed the decline without reversing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sand is the other pressure, and the Kubuqi, which lies along the southern edge of the middle Yellow River, is planted with shrub shelterbelts under the Three Norths forestation programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The region also holds the richest mineral reserves in the country, seventeen of them among the national leaders, and its open-pit coal mines bear hard on the dry ground they stand on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Qi Shi, Wenlan Wang, Xuerui Zhu, Chuan Ye, and Jintai Li, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Drivers of Grassland Ecosystem Service Value in Inner Mongolia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 15201, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-45150-8. [Open access]","Haojun Zheng, Yao Huang, Wen Zhang, Changqing Song, Qing Zhang, Wenjuan Sun, Yongqiang Yu, Lijun Yu, Haigang Li, Cunhou Zhang, Wenfang Jiang, Xinyue Yang, and Guocheng Wang, “The Implementation of Ecological Protection in Inner Mongolia Has Slowed Down Grassland Degradation,” <i>Fundamental Research</i> 5, no. 6 (2025): 2719–30, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fmre.2024.10.006. [Open access]","Xinyu Guo, Guang Yang, ChenGuang Wang, Yue Ren, Xueying Han, Ning Wang, Guangpu Jia, and Shi Qiao, “Improvement Effects of Different Shrub Sand Fixation Plantations on Vegetation and Soil in the Kubuqi Desert,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1688154, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1688154. [Open access]","Lijia Zhang, Zhenqi Hu, Dazhi Yang, Huanhuan Li, Bo Liu, He Gao, Congjie Cao, Yan Zhou, Junfang Li, and Shuchang Li, “Land Use Dynamic Evolution and Driving Factors of Typical Open-Pit Coal Mines in Inner Mongolia,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 15 (2022): 9723, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph19159723. [Open access]","Rui Wang, Lihuan Zhu, Hao Ma, Meiling Song, Guanju Ma et al., “Genetic Formation of Neolithic Hongshan People and Demic Expansion of Hongshan Culture Inferred from Ancient Human Genomes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 42, no. 6 (2025): msaf139, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msaf139. [Open access]","Xiaohong Lv, Yao Yu, Lin Ban, Panxin Du, Jianxue Xiong et al., “Population History and Subsistence of Farming Communities in an Agro-Pastoral Transition Zone of Northern China: Ancient DNA and Isotopic Evidence,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13870, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-42242-3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Hulun_Buir.jpg/1920px-Hulun_Buir.jpg","title":"the Hulunbuir grassland, Inner Mongolia","desc":"The Hulunbuir grassland.","credit":"Emmazzye, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hulun_Buir.jpg","alt":"Open green grassland rolling away under a wide cloudy sky."}},
{"id":"gc-524","num":524,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Changchun","answerText":"Changchun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Industry rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1946 – 1957</span></div>","traditional":"長春","hanzi":"长春","pinyin":"Chángchūn","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Jilin","dot":"Changchun"},"facts":[["Province","Jilin"],["Population","9.07M (2020)"],["Rank in China","31st largest"],["Population growth","+3.4% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Changchun</b> is the capital of Jilin and a sub-provincial city standing in the hinterland of the north-eastern plain, on open flat ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It borders Heilongjiang to the north, the Yanbian Korean Autonomous Prefecture to the east and Liaoning to the south-west, with the Changbai mountains beyond its southern edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its river is the Yitong, the second tributary of the Second Songhua, and through it the city belongs to a basin draining four provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wetlands are plentiful around it, of lake, swamp and river kinds alike, and a century of industry has left its mark in their mud, mercury accumulating in the artificial wetlands strung along the river through the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The built city has grown outwards in rings rather than along an axis, and roughly an eighth of its central area is road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It is one of the cities of the old industrial north-east, a region whose factories were first built along the railway corridors the Russians and then the Japanese drove through it, and what it was built to make still shapes it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Under the First Five-Year Plan the industrial framework of the whole province was set around Changchun’s automobile industry, with power and chemicals beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Changchun Film Studio came out of the same decades and is counted now among the region’s best-known light-industrial heritage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The architectural marks of the Manchukuo years — sawtooth roofs, arched portals — largely disappeared from the region’s factories in the decades that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its built-up area has expanded a great deal since the 1980s, and the road verges that came with it have been colonised by plants nobody planted, many of them from outside the region altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Ming Ma, Yichen Zhang, Jiquan Zhang, Mingda Li, Jiale Zhu, and Yang Wang, “Assessment of Urban Seismic Social Vulnerability Based on Game Theory Combination and TOPSIS Model: A Case Study of Changchun City,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 8189, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-92372-3. [Open access]","Jing Zong, Hongjie Zhang, Xuemei Li, Xinyu Bai, Yufei Hu, Dan Cui, Zhaojun Wang, and Gang Zhang, “Process of Mercury Accumulation in Urban Strip River Artificial Wetland Ecosystems: A Case Study of Changchun, a Typical Industrial City in Northeast China,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 15 (2024): 1392904, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2024.1392904. [Open access]","Qingxi Shen, Xue Tan, and Xipeng Li, “Relationship Between Spatial Pattern and Function of Urban Land Use in Changchun, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 9 (2023): e0291121, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0291121. [Open access]","Diyang Liu, Congcong Zhao, Yongfang Wang, and Yuandong Hu, “Distribution Characteristics and Adaptation Mechanisms of Exotic Spontaneous Plant Diversity in Urban Road Green Spaces of Changchun, China,” <i>Plants</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 107, https://doi.org/10.3390/plants15010107. [Open access]","Yu Ban, Jianfei Chen, Chang Liu, Jiaqi Wang, and Jingyi Liu, “Determination of the Temporal-Spatial Pattern Distribution and Evolution of Industrial Heritage in Northeast China and Its Influencing Factors,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 13206, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-41854-z. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Changchun_skyline_with_Ji_Tower_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Changchun_skyline_with_Ji_Tower_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Changchun","desc":"Changchun, seen from a distance.","credit":"wanghongliu, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Changchun_skyline_with_Ji_Tower_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Changchun."}},
{"id":"gc-026","num":26,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Beijing","answerText":"Beijing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peking Man</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Water diverted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"北京","pinyin":"Běijīng","translations":"","difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Beijing"},"facts":[["Capital","Beijing"],["Population","21.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Beijing"],["Area","16,400 km² (6,330 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Beijing</b> is a municipality at the northern edge of the North China Plain, its ground high in the north-west and low in the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Continuous mountains stand across the west and the north — the Taihang and the Yanshan, the ecological barriers of the whole region — and a wide plain fills the south-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That plain was laid down by five rivers, the Yongding, the Chaobai, the Ju, the Wenyu and the Juma, whose alluvial fans overlap and interlock into a stacked system of sand and clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People were here long before any of that mattered to anyone: at Zhoukoudian, in the limestone karst south-west of the city, an international party of Chinese, Swedish, Austrian, French and Canadian palaeontologists dug a rich Ice Age fauna out of the caves in the 1920s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among it were the specimens first named <i>Sinanthropus pekinensis</i>, Peking Man, and counted now as <i>Homo erectus pekinensis</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Enamel proteins have since been recovered from six of those Middle Pleistocene individuals and their kin at two other Chinese sites, from about four hundred thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They carry a variant of one enamel gene found in no other human lineage at all — not in the <i>Homo erectus</i> of Dmanisi, nor at Atapuerca, nor in Denisovans, Neanderthals or ourselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Water has been the modern city’s oldest difficulty, a drought setting in from 1999 as the population climbed by millions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two thirds of the demand was drawn from wells in the decade to 2013, and the ground sank as a result, parts of it dropping several centimetres a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The water table fell steadily until the middle route of the South-to-North Water Diversion opened in 2014, since when it has been rising again, and the municipality has planted new forest across its plains in two rounds since 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Guangyao Duan, Huili Gong, Beibei Chen, Xiaojuan Li, Xingyao Pan, Min Shi, and Hang Zhang, “Spatiotemporal Heterogeneity of Land Subsidence in Beijing,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12, no. 1 (2022): 15120, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-16674-6. [Open access]","Cuiyun Cheng, Shuping Zhang, Meichun Zhou, Yanchun Du, and Chazhong Ge, “Identifying Important Ecosystem Service Areas Based on Distributions of Ecosystem Services in the Beijing–Tianjin–Hebei Region, China,” <i>PeerJ</i> 10 (2022): e13881, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.13881. [Open access]","Peter Dodson, “China Shares Fossil Treasures with the World,” <i>The Anatomical Record</i> 308, no. 10 (2025): 2806–12, https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.25696. [Open access]","Qiaomei Fu, Zhongyou Wu, E. Andrew Bennett, Song Xing, Qiang Ji et al., “Enamel Proteins from Six <i>Homo erectus</i> Specimens across China,” <i>Nature</i> 655, no. 8121 (2026): 141–47, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10478-8. [Open access]","Yuan Li, Rui Wang, Hongbo Ma, and Jian-Min Zhang, “Rising Groundwater Table Due to Restoration Projects Amplifies Earthquake Induced Liquefaction Risk in Beijing,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 1466, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-56525-2. [Open access]","Hongyuan Wei, Adu Gong, Jinhong Wan, Jiaming Zhang, Zexin Fu, and Mengfei Jiang, “Differentiating the Thermal Effects of Land Use Change and Functional Intensity Dynamics in Beijing,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 10701, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-44866-x. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/The_Forbidden_City_-_View_from_Coal_Hill.jpg/1920px-The_Forbidden_City_-_View_from_Coal_Hill.jpg","title":"the Forbidden City, Beijing","desc":"The Forbidden City.","credit":"Pixelflake, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Forbidden_City_-_View_from_Coal_Hill.jpg","alt":"The great halls and courtyards of an imperial palace."}},
{"id":"gc-525","num":525,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Hohhot","answerText":"Hohhot","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"呼和浩特","pinyin":"Hūhéhàotè","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Inner Mongolia","dot":"Hohhot"},"facts":[["Region","Inner Mongolia"],["Population","3.45M (2020)"],["Rank in China","146th largest"],["Population growth","+20.2% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Hohhot</b> is the capital of the Inner Mongolia Autonomous Region and stands near China’s northern border, on ground that inclines gradually from north-east to south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Daqing and Manhan mountains close around the city, leaving a pocket of land whose one wide opening faces south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Summers there are short and hot and winters dry and cold, cold enough that the city burns coal for heating half the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the difficulty its setting creates, because a pocket ringed by hills disperses very little of what is put into the air above it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Temperature inversions are frequent through the winter and pollutants are held under them rather than carried away, so heavily polluted weather forms whenever the air goes still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The city is a centre of Mongolian medicine, a tradition built on the theory its practitioners call the Three Roots and Seven Essentials and on the harmony of people with the natural world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its pulse and urine diagnosis and its principles of cold and heat resemble those of Han Chinese medicine in form while resting on a different footing altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It works too with processed mineral and animal preparations that Han medicine does not use, which is part of why it needs a standard-setting system of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hohhot sits at one corner of the Hohhot, Baotou and Ordos triangle, the most important economic circle and urban belt in the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That belt carries a heavy share of the region’s industry, most of Inner Mongolia’s industrial sulphur dioxide and carbon dioxide coming from it, and the city’s own surface is watched by satellite radar for the subsidence that comes with rapid building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Kailin Ji, Bing Sun, Haijun Zhou, Wenjing Sun, Xiaotao Fu, Ying Sun, Huifang Ren, Yangchao Lv, Xi Chun, and Zhiqiang Wan, “Impacts of COVID-19 Lockdown on PM2.5-Bound Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons in Hohhot, Northern China: Characteristics, Sources, and Source-Specific Health Risks,” <i>Environmental Health</i> 24, no. 1 (2025): 5, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12940-025-01161-y. [Open access]","Xue Wang, Chenghua Gao, Yuan Xia, Xiaoqian Xu, Lehui Li, Yan Liu, Xing Yao, Ning Cao, Zichao Li, and Xin Fang, “Effect of Air Pollutants and Meteorological Factors on Daily Outpatient Visits of Allergic Rhinitis in Hohhot, China,” <i>Journal of Asthma and Allergy</i> 16 (2023): 1217–28, https://doi.org/10.2147/JAA.S430062. [Open access]","Yuejuan Chen, Siai Du, Pingping Huang, Huifang Ren, Bo Yin, Yaolong Qi, Cong Ding, and Wei Xu, “Analysis and Prediction of Urban Surface Transformation Based on Small Baseline Subset Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar and Sparrow Search Algorithm–Convolutional Neural Network–Long Short-Term Memory Model,” <i>Sensors</i> 24, no. 8 (2024): 2634, https://doi.org/10.3390/s24082634. [Open access]","Yazhou Zhao, Zhou Yin, Xin Zhang, Yue Kuang, Yishu Zhao, Jing Liu, Qingling Zhang, and Yanping Li, “Collaborative Control Path of Pollution and Carbon Reduction in Industrial Field in Hohhot–Baotou–Ordos Region of Inner Mongolia,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 23 (2024): e40695, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e40695. [Open access]","Shenzhen Liu, Zili Yang, Alun, and Min Li, “Policy Text Analysis on Inheritance and Innovation of Mongolian Medicine Culture in China,” <i>Frontiers in Public Health</i> 14 (2026): 1814485, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1814485. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Hohhot_Central_Square.jpg/1920px-Hohhot_Central_Square.jpg","title":"Hohhot","desc":"Hohhot, seen from a distance.","credit":"David Wilmot from Wimbledon, United Kingdom, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hohhot_Central_Square.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Hohhot."}},
{"id":"gc-027","num":27,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tianjin","answerText":"Tianjin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reserve made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Water diverted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 2014</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"天津","pinyin":"Tiānjīn","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Tianjin"},"facts":[["Capital","Tianjin"],["Population","13.9M (2020)"],["Largest city","Tianjin"],["Area","11,966 km² (4,620 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Tianjin</b> is a municipality on the western shore of Bohai Bay at the eastern edge of the North China Plain, low and flat, most of its ground standing only a few metres above the sea on a plain built of river and marine sediment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beneath it lie hundreds of metres of Neogene and Quaternary beds in which sand aquifers alternate with silt and clay, a confined system that compacts when it is pumped dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole Haihe river system drains to the sea here, gathering a great part of northern China into one outlet on the Tianjin coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The coast keeps a record of where it used to run: the Ancient Coast and Wetland reserve, approved in October 1992, holds shell dikes, oyster reefs and the Qilihai marsh together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the first and only state-level reserve in China where an ancient coast and a wetland lie in one place, and its oyster beaches have no equal elsewhere in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The shore around them is salt country, its shallow water table brackish and alkaline and the soils above it saline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city carried foreign concessions well into the twentieth century, and the Japanese one kept a directory of its own residents: a dentist named Otojiro Shirai opened a dental hospital there in 1936, on retiring from the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pumping the aquifer from the 1950s onwards sank the plain in five separate centres, the city itself dropping by metres between 1959 and 1983.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inland districts were still falling quickly into the 2000s, but the rates have eased since 2017 and the sinking is now concentrated in the reclaimed ground of the coastal Binhai New Area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The South-to-North Water Diversion has supplied the municipality since 2014, and with limits on pumping and deliberate recharge the water table has begun to recover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Haolin Zhao, Hongyue Zhou, Dashan Zhou, and Chaoying Zhao, “Two Decades of Land Subsidence in Tianjin, China, Measured with Multi-Temporal InSAR Observations,” <i>Sensors</i> 26, no. 4 (2026): 1203, https://doi.org/10.3390/s26041203. [Open access]","Huashan Xu, Yufen Ren, Hua Zheng, Zhiyun Ouyang, and Bo Jiang, “Analysis of Runoff Trends and Drivers in the Haihe River Basin, China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 17, no. 5 (2020): 1577, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17051577. [Open access]","Chen Chen, ChaoFeng Shao, and YanMin Shi, “Dynamic Evaluation of Ecological Service Function Value of Qilihai Wetland in Tianjin,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 17, no. 19 (2020): 7108, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17197108. [Open access]","Feng-Chou Cheng, Ling-Hsia Wang, and Chun-Pin Chiang, “The Overseas Expansion of Japanese Dentists in the Early 20th Century: The Case of a Japanese Immigrant Dentist in North China,” <i>Journal of Dental Sciences</i> 20, no. 3 (2025): 1925–27, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jds.2025.03.028. [Open access]","Haixia Zhao, Binjie Gu, Dechao Chen, Jiaojiao Tang, Xinliang Xu, Zhi Qiao, and Junqi Wang, “Physicochemical Properties and Salinization Characteristics of Soils in Coastal Land Reclamation Areas: A Case Study of China-Singapore Tianjin Eco-City,” <i>Heliyon</i> 8, no. 12 (2022): e12629, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2022.e12629. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/%E5%A4%A9%E6%B4%A5%E4%B9%8B%E7%9C%BC%E5%8C%971.jpg/1920px-%E5%A4%A9%E6%B4%A5%E4%B9%8B%E7%9C%BC%E5%8C%971.jpg","title":"the Tianjin Eye","desc":"The Tianjin Eye over the Hai River.","credit":"KangTyngrwey, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A4%A9%E6%B4%A5%E4%B9%8B%E7%9C%BC%E5%8C%971.jpg","alt":"A large ferris wheel built over a river bridge."}},
{"id":"gc-528","num":528,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Haikou","answerText":"Haikou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">A town from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,200 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"海口","pinyin":"Hǎikǒu","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hainan","dot":"Haikou"},"facts":[["Province","Hainan"],["Population","2.87M (2020)"],["Rank in China","174th largest"],["Population growth","+40.4% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Haikou</b> is the capital of Hainan and stands on the northern shore of the island, across the Qiongzhou Strait from the south coast of the Chinese mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the only tropical coastal provincial capital in the country, governed by the monsoon and by the sea around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is believed to have first become a town in the Han dynasty, some 2,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its air is among the cleanest of any Chinese city, sitting in the lowest pollution tier of the 168 that are closely monitored, and what pollution there is comes from traffic and building rather than from industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the rain that falls on it comes between May and October, and the year here is warm and wet enough that humidity rarely drops far, the sunshine long and the solar radiation fierce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The city drinks mainly from the Nandu, which is the largest river on the island and drains a good part of its northern half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three treatment works supply the main urban area, and that water has been tracked from the river to the household tap for the microplastics it carries along the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the city’s eastern side lies Dongzhaigang, the first mangrove reserve established in China, and Hainan holds the richest mangrove flora in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Inland from the coast the Li build their boat-shaped houses from the plants of the rainforest, a tradition now proposed for World Heritage listing as rural depopulation empties the villages that keep it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Haikou’s own green space has been mapped for what grows in it, and in a tropical city of this kind most of what grows has been planted rather than left standing, so the mature native trees that remain are the ones worth counting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Jichao Peng, Xinxin Wu, Jun Chen, Jin Qian, Nan Li, Yang Yi, Yue Huang, Juncai Lu, Wenxing Zhang, Zhengyu Li, Zhao Li, Min Li, and Xiaoran Liu, “Short-Term Effects of Ambient Air Pollutants and Meteorological Factors on Emergency Department Visits in Tropical Haikou, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 20849, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-03517-3. [Open access]","Xiangxiang Li, Yihan Yu, Mei Yang, Shaobai Wen, and Jun Zhang, “Tracking Microplastics Contamination in Drinking Water Supply Chain in Haikou, China: From Source to Household Taps,” <i>Toxics</i> 12, no. 11 (2024): 793, https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics12110793. [Open access]","Hai-Li Zhang, Lin-Yuan Guo, Mir Muhammad Nizamani, and Hua-Feng Wang, “Distribution Patterns and Drivers of Urban Green Space and Plant Diversity in Haikou, China,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 14 (2023): 1202115, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2023.1202115. [Open access]","Yufeng Lin, Zifeng Luo, Xuan Gu, Yijuan Deng, Pingping Guo, Guogui Chen, Wenqing Wang, and Mao Wang, “Optimizing Mangrove Afforestation: Mollusc Biodiversity Comparisons Reveal Optimal Mudflat–Mangrove Area Ratio,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 9 (2024): e70330, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70330. [Open access]","Guang-Hui Ma, Ming-Xun Ren, Ding-Hai Yang, and Xiao-Dong Mu, “Boat-Shaped Houses of the Indigenous Li People on Hainan Island, China: Plant Resources and Ecological Adaptations,” <i>Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine</i> 21, no. 1 (2025): 67, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13002-025-00818-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Haikou_skyline_in_2013_-_01.jpg/1920px-Haikou_skyline_in_2013_-_01.jpg","title":"Haikou","desc":"Haikou, seen from a distance.","credit":"Anna Frodesiak, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haikou_skyline_in_2013_-_01.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Haikou."}},
{"id":"gc-028","num":28,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Hainan","answerText":"Hainan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Li settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4,000 – 3,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rainforest park</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2020</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"海南","pinyin":"Hǎinán","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Hainan"},"facts":[["Capital","Haikou"],["Population","10.1M (2020)"],["Largest city","Haikou"],["Area","33,900 km² (13,100 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Hainan</b> is the southernmost province of China, and the island it is named for lies at the northern end of the South China Sea shelf, facing Guangdong across the Qiongzhou Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The province as administered also takes in island groups far out in the sea, which is why its land is reckoned larger than the island itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island is low around its rim and high in the middle, rising through terraces and hills to Wuzhi Mountain and Parrot Mountain, and its climate is tropical monsoon, long in summer and without a winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Li are its aboriginal people, and the ancestry that dominates them is the one carried by the Tai-Kadai speakers of southern China and northern Vietnam, the Bai-Yue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their divergence from those mainland populations began three to four thousand years ago, when the ancestors of the Li migrated and settled the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Isolation deepened the bottleneck that followed, and malaria has left its own mark on them, in variants of malaria-related genes carried nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They build boat-shaped houses out of the forest around them, four kinds of them from 26 plant species across 13 families, the frames bound with lianas and strips of bamboo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those houses stay cooler and drier inside than the brick ones now replacing them, which is what centuries of adaptation to a rainforest look like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than three fifths of the island stays forested the year round, and a seventh of it became a national park in 2020, where camera trapping at Jianfengling has found fifteen mammal species from six orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The coast carries the richest mangrove flora in the country, 26 true mangroves and 11 semi-mangroves of the 37 species China holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Zihan Jin, Changsheng Xiong, Qiaolin Luan, and Fang Wang, “Dynamic Evolutionary Analysis of Land Use/Cover and Ecosystem Service Values on Hainan Island,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 20, no. 1 (2022): 776, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph20010776. [Open access]","Jia Geng, Mingsheng Yuan, Shen Xu, Tingting Bai, Yang Xiao, Xiaopeng Li, and Dong Xu, “Urban Expansion Was the Main Driving Force for the Decline in Ecosystem Services in Hainan Island during 1980–2015,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 23 (2022): 15665, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192315665. [Open access]","Hao Chen, Rong Lin, Yan Lu, Rui Zhang, Yang Gao, Yungang He, and Shuhua Xu, “Tracing Bai-Yue Ancestry in Aboriginal Li People on Hainan Island,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 39, no. 10 (2022): msac210, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msac210. [Open access]","Guang-Hui Ma, Ming-Xun Ren, Ding-Hai Yang, and Xiao-Dong Mu, “Boat-Shaped Houses of the Indigenous Li People on Hainan Island, China: Plant Resources and Ecological Adaptations,” <i>Journal of Ethnobiology and Ethnomedicine</i> 21, no. 1 (2025): 67, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13002-025-00818-9. [Open access]","Yufeng Lin, Zifeng Luo, Xuan Gu, Yijuan Deng, Pingping Guo, Guogui Chen, Wenqing Wang, and Mao Wang, “Optimizing Mangrove Afforestation: Mollusc Biodiversity Comparisons Reveal Optimal Mudflat–Mangrove Area Ratio,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 9 (2024): e70330, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70330. [Open access]","Wenbo Yan, Xiangxiang Lu, Zhigao Zeng, Shaoliang Xue, Qi Wang, Shiqin Mo, and Chunshen Liang, “Unveiling the Mammalian Diversity and Conservation Significance of Jianfengling Region: A Camera-Trapping Survey of Mammals in Hainan Tropical Rainforest National Park,” <i>Animals</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 721, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani16050721. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Yalong_Bay_%E4%BA%9E%E9%BE%8D%E7%81%A3_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Yalong_Bay_%E4%BA%9E%E9%BE%8D%E7%81%A3_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Yalong Bay, Hainan","desc":"Yalong Bay at Sanya.","credit":"lienyuan lee, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yalong_Bay_%E4%BA%9E%E9%BE%8D%E7%81%A3_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A curving sandy beach with waves and a headland beyond."}},
{"id":"gc-529","num":529,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Yinchuan","answerText":"Yinchuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Resettling from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1983</span></div>","traditional":"銀川","hanzi":"银川","pinyin":"Yínchuān","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Ningxia","dot":"Yinchuan"},"facts":[["Region","Ningxia"],["Population","2.86M (2020)"],["Rank in China","177th largest"],["Population growth","+43.4% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Yinchuan</b> is the capital of the Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region and stands on the Ningxia Plain, with the Helan mountains along its western side and the Ordos Plateau away to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Yellow River runs through it from south-west to north-east, and the oasis around the city is the artesian irrigation district of that river’s alluvial plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ground under it is a subsidence basin opened by faulting in the Cainozoic, bounded by the East Helan fault zone to the west and filled deep with loose Quaternary deposit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its climate is arid with long cold winters, and what little rain falls comes almost entirely between July and September, against an evaporation many times greater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With so little water of its own, the farms and factories of the oasis draw on the Yellow River instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the Helan mountains along the city’s western edge, at Helankou, ancient pastoralists carved petroglyphs into the sandstone cliffs, and those carvings are the best record there is of how they lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rock there is weathering under freeze-thaw and wet-dry cycles, and the images are at risk of going with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The land steps down from the mountain front to the river in a series of plains, piedmont alluvial fans giving way to alluvial and lake deposits as the ground flattens eastward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city proper is built of three central districts, each with a character of its own: Xingqing the old commercial quarter, Jinfeng a newer area of business and housing, and Xixia the district of colleges and research institutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is the core city of its region, drawing people from Gansu, Shaanxi, Anhui and Zhejiang, and it has received many of those resettled within Ningxia since 1983, whose fitting into the city has itself been studied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Lina Mi, Juncang Tian, Jianning Si, Yuchun Chen, Yinghai Li, and Xinhe Wang, “Evolution of Groundwater in Yinchuan Oasis at the Upper Reaches of the Yellow River after Water-Saving Transformation and Its Driving Factors,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 17, no. 4 (2020): 1304, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17041304. [Open access]","Zhao Ru, Long Dongping, Li Jing, Yang Meiling, and Wen Xinyu, “Ecological Migrants' Socio-Spatial Integration in Yinchuan City, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0275853, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0275853. [Open access]","Wengang Qu, Hui Qian, Chao Yang, Panpan Xu, Yanyan Gao, Leiqiang Wei, and Qi Long, “A Comprehensive Multicriteria Decision Analysis Framework for Shallow Geothermal Energy Suitability Evaluation in Yinchuan Area, China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 6420, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-90559-2. [Open access]","Xiangchao Kong, Faguo He, Ran Lv, Lizhi Zhang, Xin Yang, and Tengfei Lu, “Experimental Study on the Degradation of Sandstone-Petroglyph Carriers in Helankou: Effects of Freeze-Thaw and Wet-Dry Cycles,” <i>iScience</i> 27, no. 8 (2024): 110576, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2024.110576. [Open access]","Guifen Lyu, Niwat Angkawisittpan, Xiaoli Fu, and Somchat Sonasang, “Investigating the Relationship between Built Environment and Urban Vitality Using Big Data,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15, no. 1 (2025): 579, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-84279-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Skyline_of_Jinfeng_District%2C_Yinchuan_%2853724463112%29.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Jinfeng_District%2C_Yinchuan_%2853724463112%29.jpg","title":"Yinchuan","desc":"Yinchuan, seen from a distance.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Jinfeng_District,_Yinchuan_(53724463112).jpg","alt":"A wide view of Yinchuan."}},
{"id":"gc-029","num":29,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Ningxia","answerText":"Ningxia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hui here from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tang, 618 CE</span></div>","traditional":"寧夏","hanzi":"宁夏","pinyin":"Níngxià","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Ningxia"},"facts":[["Capital","Yinchuan"],["Population","7.20M (2020)"],["Largest city","Yinchuan"],["Area","66,400 km² (25,600 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Ningxia</b> is an autonomous region in north-western China, and the only one of the mainland’s province-level divisions that lies entirely inside the Yellow River basin, so everything about it turns on that river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The river enters at Xiaheyan in Zhongwei, crosses north-eastward through Qingtongxia, Yinchuan and Shizuishan and leaves for Inner Mongolia, watering an irrigated basin as it goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That basin is alluvial plain, flat and cut through with ditches, the Yinchuan oasis lying in a fault-subsidence trough beneath the Helan mountains; the climate over it is continental and arid, and most of the little rain that falls comes between July and September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the farming rests on is the sunshine and the long frost-free season rather than the rain, which plainly cannot carry it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Helan mountains above the plain, at Helankou, ancient pastoralists cut petroglyphs into the sandstone, and those carvings are the best record there is of how they lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The rock is weathering under freeze-thaw and wet-dry cycles, and many of the images are now at risk of vanishing altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ningxia is the Hui Autonomous Region and holds about a third of all China’s Hui, a people formed of Han families joined with Arab, Persian, Mongol and Uyghur ones, and whose presence here is traced back to the Tang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> East of the river the land runs into the Mu Us sands and the Ordos, desert grassland carrying drought-hardy shrubs and herbs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Arable land there is scarce enough that the desert grassland is widely broken up for maize, fertilised heavily and watered by pumping the groundwater several times a season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The region is also the recognised home ground of the goji berry, long held to be the place where that plant is properly grown, and its berries carry metabolic signatures particular to where they were raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Qiangqiang Yang, Zeyu Wei, Xiaocong Qiu, and Zengfeng Zhao, “Beta Diversity Patterns and Drivers of Macroinvertebrate Communities in Major Rivers of Ningxia, China,” <i>Animals</i> 15, no. 14 (2025): 2034, https://doi.org/10.3390/ani15142034. [Open access]","Zhanping Wang, Juncang Tian, and Kepeng Feng, “Response of Runoff towards Land Use Changes in the Yellow River Basin in Ningxia, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0265931, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265931. [Open access]","Xiangchao Kong, Faguo He, Ran Lv, Lizhi Zhang, Xin Yang, and Tengfei Lu, “Experimental Study on the Degradation of Sandstone-Petroglyph Carriers in Helankou: Effects of Freeze-Thaw and Wet-Dry Cycles,” <i>iScience</i> 27, no. 8 (2024): 110576, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2024.110576. [Open access]","Li Zhang, Fei Zhan, Wenfang Zhang, Sanping Song, and Shisheng Zhu, “Forensic Characteristics of 38 Y-STR Loci in the Hui Population from Shaanxi Province, Northwest China,” <i>Heliyon</i> 10, no. 19 (2024): e38501, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e38501. [Open access]","Peng Kang, Jinpeng Hu, Yaqing Pan, Xuan Qu, Yichao Ran, Chenxi Yang, and Bingru Liu, “Response of Soil Fungal-Community Structure and Function to Land Conversion to Agriculture in Desert Grassland,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 15 (2024): 1413973, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2024.1413973. [Open access]","Yan Yan, Wei Ma, Yage Li, Chen Zhang, Fang Li, Tianqing Huang, Beibei Gao, Huihui Meng, Yunfei Hu, and Huan Wu, “Untargeted Metabolomics Reveals Region-Specific Metabolic Signatures and Discriminative Markers in Goji Berry (Lycium barbarum L.),” <i>Metabolites</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 326, https://doi.org/10.3390/metabo16050326. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Xi_Xia_Imperial_Tombs_%2854154641924%29.jpg/1920px-Xi_Xia_Imperial_Tombs_%2854154641924%29.jpg","title":"the Western Xia tombs, Ningxia","desc":"The Western Xia tombs.","credit":"xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xi_Xia_Imperial_Tombs_(54154641924).jpg","alt":"Eroded earthen tomb mounds on a desert plain."}},
{"id":"gc-530","num":530,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Xining","answerText":"Xining","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kumbum built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1379 – 1942</span></div>","traditional":"西寧","hanzi":"西宁","pinyin":"Xīníng","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Qinghai","dot":"Xining"},"facts":[["Province","Qinghai"],["Population","2.47M (2020)"],["Rank in China","203rd largest"],["Population growth","+11.7% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Xining</b> is the capital of Qinghai and stands in the valley of the Huangshui River, on the north-eastern margin of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, at an average elevation of about 2,261 metres (7,418 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ground is high in the north-west and low in the south-east, shallow hills and river terraces rather than open plain, and its districts are strung along the water because the mountains leave nowhere else for them to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That valley is the heart of the Hehuang, the low and relatively level corner of the province where farming is possible, and it has been administered by the central government since the Han dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Military-farming colonies planted there over many centuries made it the settled core of Qinghai, and the traditional villages around it are more thickly clustered than anywhere else on the Tibetan Plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of those villages were raised under the Ming and the Qing, though a few are older than the Yuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The city stood on the Qinghai route of the Silk Road, and the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road ran from it across the interior of the plateau to Lhasa, the only such route the records carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tangfan and Tea Horse roads carried the same traffic along the plateau’s edge, and the Tuyuhun migration route through Amdo crossed here too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Kumbum Monastery, in a narrow valley at Lushar in Huangzhong, was begun in 1379 to mark the birthplace of Tsongkhapa, who founded the Gelug school of Tibetan Buddhism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Building went on there for 563 years, until 1942, and it is one of the six great Gelug monasteries and the centre of Tibetan Buddhism in the province; under the Ming, when the court moved settlers into the Hehuang, its halls took on Han building practice beside Tibetan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Han communities are concentrated around the city while Tibetans hold the cold high country, Hui and Salar the Yellow River valley and Mongolians the dry grassland, six peoples that took their present shape under the Yuan, Ming and Qing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Zhenxu Ning, Shuzhen He, Xinghao Liao, Chunguang Ma, and Jing Wu, “Health Impacts of a Cold Wave and Its Economic Loss Assessment in China's High-Altitude City, Xining,” <i>Archives of Public Health</i> 82, no. 1 (2024): 52, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13690-024-01284-7. [Open access]","Shibin Ma, Haiquan Xu, Rongfang Xin, Li Huang, Yonghao Hou, Jia Wang, and Xuesong Yang, “Integrated Effects of Land Cover, Topography, Urban Morphology, and PM2.5 on Land Surface Temperature,” <i>PLOS One</i> 21, no. 5 (2026): e0344297, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0344297. [Open access]","Liu Jin, Zongqi Wang, and Xiaohong Chen, “Spatial Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors of Traditional Villages on the Tibetan Plateau in China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 20 (2022): 13170, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192013170. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Jing Zhang, Yunying Ren, and Xiaofan An, “Temporal and Spatial Features of the Palace Building Space of Qinghai’s Kumbum Monastery and Its Evolution,” <i>PLOS One</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0262155, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0262155. [Open access]","Xin Jia, Harry F. Lee, Mengchun Cui, Chao Liu, Lin Zeng, Ricci P. H. Yue, Yang Zhao, and Huayu Lu, “Habitat Variability and Ethnic Diversity in Northern Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7, no. 1 (2017): 918, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-01008-8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Xining_skyline.jpg/1920px-Xining_skyline.jpg","title":"Xining","desc":"Xining, seen from a distance.","credit":"NoGhost, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xining_skyline.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Xining."}},
{"id":"gc-030","num":30,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Qinghai","answerText":"Qinghai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8,500 – 7,300 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tibetan Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 842 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"青海","pinyin":"Qīnghǎi","translations":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Qinghai"},"facts":[["Capital","Xining"],["Population","5.92M (2020)"],["Largest city","Xining"],["Area","722,300 km² (278,900 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Qinghai</b> is a province of the north-eastern Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, and it takes its name from Qinghai Lake, the largest inland saltwater lake in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Humans first reached the plateau some 21,000 years ago but settled its northern part permanently only after about 8,000 years ago, moving up along the Yellow River as barley and sheep made the heights liveable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Shalongka, in the upper Yellow River valley in the province’s east, microlithic hunter-gatherers kept relatively fixed seasonal camps between 8,500 and 7,300 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Millet farmers of the Yangshao culture then came up from the Loess Plateau and founded the earliest settled villages on the plateau, at that same site between about 5,900 and 5,100 years ago, and the Qijia culture followed some 4,100 to 3,900 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Central government reached the Huangshui valley under the Han, and the military-farming colonies planted then made the Hehuang, the corner where that river meets the Yellow River, the settled core of the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> From 313 to 663 CE the region was held by the Tuyuhun, a polity founded by nomadic-pastoral Xianbei people who had come from the north-east, until the armies of the Tibetan Empire annexed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That empire, the Tubo, ran from 618 to 842 and moved people into the region in numbers to hold it, and out of that came the Amdo Tibetan identity and dialect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Tang-Tibet Ancient Road ran roughly 2,240 kilometres (1,390 miles) from Chang’an to Lhasa across the province, and is the only branch of the Silk Road recorded through the interior of the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cemeteries at Dulan show that the Qinghai route was a major hub of that traffic, and the Tangfan and Tea Horse roads later carried goods and people the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Under the Ming the court moved settlers into the Hehuang to repair the damage of war, and the villages of that valley are still the thickest cluster of traditional settlement on the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Jiesheng Sun, Yuanyuan Ding, Yong Wang, and Chongyi E, “Vegetation Dynamics and Its Driving Force in the Qinghai Lake Basin, China,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 16 (2025): 1691672, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1691672. [Open access]","Xin Jia, Harry F. Lee, Mengchun Cui, Chao Liu, Lin Zeng, Ricci P. H. Yue, Yang Zhao, and Huayu Lu, “Habitat Variability and Ethnic Diversity in Northern Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7, no. 1 (2017): 918, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-01008-8. [Open access]","Jingyi Gao, Guangliang Hou, Yongming Xiao, Chongyi E, Haicheng Wei, Yongjuan Sun, Manping Sun, Hongpan Xue, Zhuoma Wende, Sunmei Jin, and Xiaoliang Chen, “Vegetation History and Survival Patterns of the Earliest Village on the Qinghai–Tibetan Plateau,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 903192, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2022.903192. [Open access]","Kongyang Zhu, Panxin Du, Jiyuan Li, Jianlin Zhang, Xiaojun Hu, Hailiang Meng, Liang Chen, Boyan Zhou, Xiaomin Yang, Jianxue Xiong, Edward Allen, Xiaoying Ren, Yi Ding, Yiran Xu, Xin Chang, Yao Yu, Sheng Han, Guanghui Dong, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Shaoqing Wen, “Cultural and Demic Co-Diffusion of Tubo Empire on Tibetan Plateau,” <i>iScience</i> 25, no. 12 (2022): 105636, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2022.105636. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Liu Jin, Zongqi Wang, and Xiaohong Chen, “Spatial Distribution Characteristics and Influencing Factors of Traditional Villages on the Tibetan Plateau in China,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 20 (2022): 13170, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph192013170. [Open access]","Jing Zhang, Yunying Ren, and Xiaofan An, “Temporal and Spatial Features of the Palace Building Space of Qinghai’s Kumbum Monastery and Its Evolution,” <i>PLOS One</i> 17, no. 10 (2022): e0262155, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0262155. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/QinghaiHu1.jpg/1920px-QinghaiHu1.jpg","title":"Qinghai Lake","desc":"Qinghai Lake.","credit":"Andreas Gruschke (Freiburg), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QinghaiHu1.jpg","alt":"A deep blue lake with hills along the far shore."}},
{"id":"gc-531","num":531,"category":"The provincial capitals","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the province or region shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Lhasa","answerText":"Lhasa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tubo empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 842 CE</span></div>","traditional":"拉薩","hanzi":"拉萨","pinyin":"Lāsà","translations":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Tibet","dot":"Lhasa"},"facts":[["Region","Tibet"],["Population","868k (2020)"],["Rank in China","282nd largest"],["Population growth","+55.1% (2010–2020)"]],"abstract":"<b>Lhasa</b> is the capital of the Tibet Autonomous Region and stands in the south-central Qinghai-Tibet Plateau at about 3,650 metres (11,980 feet) above the sea, ringed by mountains with the Lhasa River running through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That river gives the city its name and its water, running 568 kilometres (353 miles), and its basin holds a great deal of water by the standards of the plateau, little of which is drawn on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The climate is cold semi-arid, with dry winters and springs, a short summer in which the rain arrives, and air thin enough to let solar radiation and ultraviolet through with little to stop them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The city is the political and economic centre of the region and the spiritual heartland of Tibetan Buddhism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Potala Palace, the Jokhang Temple and Sera Monastery all stand here, symbols of faith and places of rest at once, and the religion is worked so deeply into the streets and temple precincts that it can be studied in them directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The empire whose road ended here began in the middle of the 7th century, when more than a dozen Tibetan tribes were united under the Bod tribe of the Yarlung valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That empire, the Tubo, ran from 618 to 842, annexed the Tuyuhun in 663 and threw its weight across the whole of western China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Tang-Tibet Ancient Road ended here, running from Chang’an with post stations set about 22 kilometres (14 miles) apart, the distance the Tang and Tubo caravanserai system worked to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is the only branch of the Silk Road recorded through the interior of the plateau, and 512 archaeological sites of the Tubo period are known across Tibet and Qinghai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tea will not grow at this height, so from about the 8th century the Tea Horse Road carried it up from the Han-farmed lowlands in trade for horses, and brick tea, butter tea and tsampa, a food of cooked barley, are still much of what is eaten here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","city","asia"],"sources":["Zimeng Guo, Junyu Chen, Hanyue Yu, Qiangying Zhang, Bu Duo, and Xiaomei Cui, “Characteristics, Sources and Potential Ecological Risk of Atmospheric Microplastics in Lhasa City,” <i>Environmental Geochemistry and Health</i> 46, no. 9 (2024): 347, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10653-024-02125-w. [Open access]","Lingling Wu, Yufeng Ren, Peng Huang, Shiyu Yuan, Chun Zhou, Zhongshun Gu, Yinan Guo, and Li Zhou, “Evaluation of GSMaP and MSWEP Precipitation Products for Runoff Simulation in the Lhasa River Basin,” <i>PLOS One</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0342995, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0342995. [Open access]","Dongzhu Gadan and Zaisheng Zhang, “Spiritual Places: Spatial Recognition of Tibetan Buddhist Spiritual Perception,” <i>PLOS One</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301087, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301087. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Kongyang Zhu, Panxin Du, Jiyuan Li, Jianlin Zhang, Xiaojun Hu, Hailiang Meng, Liang Chen, Boyan Zhou, Xiaomin Yang, Jianxue Xiong, Edward Allen, Xiaoying Ren, Yi Ding, Yiran Xu, Xin Chang, Yao Yu, Sheng Han, Guanghui Dong, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Shaoqing Wen, “Cultural and Demic Co-Diffusion of Tubo Empire on Tibetan Plateau,” <i>iScience</i> 25, no. 12 (2022): 105636, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2022.105636. [Open access]","Cai Wen, Qing Zhang, Fei Xie, and Jixin Jiang, “Brick Tea Consumption and Its Relationship with Fluorosis in Tibetan Areas,” <i>Frontiers in Nutrition</i> 9 (2022): 1030344, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2022.1030344. [Open access]","Zhefei Zeng, Ziyi Liang, Yan Chen, Qi Shu, Junru Li, Norzin Tso, Mengyan Chen, Shutong Zhang, Xin Tan, La Qiong, and Junwei Wang, “Adaptation Analysis of Two Asteraceae Invasive Plants in Lhasa, Tibet,” <i>PeerJ</i> 13 (2025): e19667, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.19667. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Lhasa_from_the_Pabonka_Monastery.JPG/1920px-Lhasa_from_the_Pabonka_Monastery.JPG","title":"Lhasa","desc":"Lhasa, seen from a distance.","credit":"Qeqertaq, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_from_the_Pabonka_Monastery.JPG","alt":"A wide view of Lhasa."}},
{"id":"gc-031","num":31,"category":"The provinces and regions","question":"The province, autonomous region or municipality shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Tibet","answerText":"Tibet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">High plateau by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40,000 – 30,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tubo empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 842 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"西藏","pinyin":"Xīzàng","translations":"","difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"china-provinces","key":"Tibet"},"facts":[["Capital","Lhasa"],["Population","3.65M (2020)"],["Largest city","Lhasa"],["Area","1,228,400 km² (474,300 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Tibet</b> is an autonomous region holding the core of the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau in south-western China, its south-eastern canyons wet and sharply zoned by height, its north-western hinterland alpine and arid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaic hominins were on the plateau about 160,000 years ago, and modern humans had reached its high country, above 4,500 metres (14,800 feet), between roughly 40,000 and 30,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Handprints and footprints at Quesang in the south put people there at least 20,000 years ago, but that presence was intermittent, and permanent occupation dates only from the early Holocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Millet farmers from the Yellow River basin took up cold-tolerant barley and carried it onto the plateau between about 3,600 and 3,300 years ago, and rather than replacing the foragers already there they lived alongside them for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ancestry of Tibetans today traces mainly back to those Neolithic farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the middle of the 7th century more than a dozen Tibetan tribes were united under the Bod tribe of the Yarlung valley, and the Tubo empire that grew out of that ran from 618 to 842 and threw its weight across the whole of western China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its armies annexed the Tuyuhun in 663 and moved people north-east in numbers to hold the ground, and out of that came the Amdo Tibetan identity and dialect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Tang-Tibet Ancient Road belongs to the same period, running from Chang’an to Lhasa with post stations set about 22 kilometres (14 miles) apart, and it is the only branch of the Silk Road recorded through the interior of the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tea will not grow at these heights, so from about the 8th century the Tea Horse Road carried it up from the Han-farmed lowlands in trade for horses, and brick tea, butter tea and tsampa, a food of cooked barley, are still much of what is eaten here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tibetan Buddhism shapes the built landscape as much as it does belief, and its greatest houses stand at Lhasa: the Potala Palace, the Jokhang Temple and Sera Monastery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","tags":["place","geography","china","province","asia"],"sources":["Guanglin He, Mengge Wang, Xing Zou, Pengyu Chen, Zheng Wang, Yan Liu, Hongbin Yao, Lan-Hai Wei, Renkuan Tang, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Hui-Yuan Yeh, “Peopling History of the Tibetan Plateau and Multiple Waves of Admixture of Tibetans Inferred from Both Ancient and Modern Genome-Wide Data,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 12 (2021): 725243, https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2021.725243. [Open access]","Luo Guo, Xiaoyan Zhang, Zhan Lin, Zhuang Cui, Changping Li, and Su Wu, “Spatiotemporal Analysis of Plague Risk in Tibet: Multi-Source Data-Driven Ensemble Model Development and Validation,” <i>One Health</i> 22 (2026): 101354, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.onehlt.2026.101354. [Open access]","Kongyang Zhu, Panxin Du, Jiyuan Li, Jianlin Zhang, Xiaojun Hu, Hailiang Meng, Liang Chen, Boyan Zhou, Xiaomin Yang, Jianxue Xiong, Edward Allen, Xiaoying Ren, Yi Ding, Yiran Xu, Xin Chang, Yao Yu, Sheng Han, Guanghui Dong, Chuan-Chao Wang, and Shaoqing Wen, “Cultural and Demic Co-Diffusion of Tubo Empire on Tibetan Plateau,” <i>iScience</i> 25, no. 12 (2022): 105636, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2022.105636. [Open access]","Zhuoma Lancuo, Guangliang Hou, Changjun Xu, Yuying Liu, Yan Zhu, Wen Wang, and Yongkun Zhang, “Simulating the Route of the Tang-Tibet Ancient Road for One Branch of the Silk Road across the Qinghai-Tibet Plateau,” <i>PLOS One</i> 14, no. 12 (2019): e0226970, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0226970. [Open access]","Cai Wen, Qing Zhang, Fei Xie, and Jixin Jiang, “Brick Tea Consumption and Its Relationship with Fluorosis in Tibetan Areas,” <i>Frontiers in Nutrition</i> 9 (2022): 1030344, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2022.1030344. [Open access]","Dongzhu Gadan and Zaisheng Zhang, “Spiritual Places: Spatial Recognition of Tibetan Buddhist Spiritual Perception,” <i>PLOS One</i> 19, no. 5 (2024): e0301087, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0301087. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Mt._Everest_from_Gokyo_Ri_November_5%2C_2012.jpg/1920px-Mt._Everest_from_Gokyo_Ri_November_5%2C_2012.jpg","title":"Mount Everest, Tibet","desc":"The north face of Mount Everest.","credit":"Rdevany, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mt._Everest_from_Gokyo_Ri_November_5,_2012.jpg","alt":"A snow-covered peak rising above a glacier."}},
{"id":"rm-101","num":101,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Debt bondage, the auspices and the consulship were all at stake in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the two-century struggle in which Rome's plebeians won the rights its patricians held alone.","questions":["Livy's ragged centurion in the Forum, the withdrawal to the Sacred Mount and the first tribunes of the plebs are the opening episodes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as the annalists tell it.","When the lex Hortensia made the resolutions of the plebs binding on every citizen, it closed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, some two centuries after the commons first walked out of Rome."],"answer":"Conflict of the Orders","answerText":"Conflict of the Orders","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 494 – 287 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First secession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE, to the Sacred Mount</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plebeian consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">367 BCE, L. Sextius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Closed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">lex Hortensia, 287 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","institution","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"The <b>Conflict of the Orders</b> is the modern name for the struggle by which the plebeians of the early Republic won the rights the patricians had held alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Its story comes from writers of the late Republic and later, and Livy warned that records for those centuries were few and nearly all had perished when the city burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He opens the quarrel with debt: with a Volscian war threatening, the orders were bitterly hostile over the plight of debtors, who said they fought abroad for liberty and were enslaved at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An old centurion gave that grievance a face, appearing in the Forum emaciated, baring his battle scars and the marks of his creditor's lash, and telling how usury had cost him his farm and then his person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commons withdrew to the Sacred Mount beyond the Anio, and the settlement that brought them back gave the plebs its own magistrates, inviolable in person, empowered against the consuls and closed to patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Every later gain took that shape of pressure and concession, and the patrician argument was religious: asked why no plebeian might be consul, the consuls answered that no plebeian could take the auspices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 445 BCE the tribune Canuleius carried a law permitting the orders to marry, and rather than yield the consulship the Senate allowed military tribunes with consular power to be chosen from both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Licinius and Sextius, re-elected tribune ten times and blocking every curule election for five years, carried a law that one consul be plebeian; Sextius took the office in 367 BCE, the nobility exacting a patrician praetorship for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup> Debt bondage ended in 326 BCE, when the people enacted that a debtor's goods, not his person, should answer for a debt, and those already bound were released.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Ogulnian law of 300 BCE co-opted plebeians into the colleges of pontiffs and augurs, and the lex Hortensia, conventionally dated 287 BCE, made the plebs' own resolutions binding on the whole citizen body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"10\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"11\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.28, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Plebs,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA%2A/Plebs.html. [Open access]","Gaius, <i>The Institutes of Gaius</i> 1.3, trans. James Muirhead (Edinburgh: T. & T. Clark, 1880), 3, https://archive.org/stream/institutesgaius00unkngoog/institutesgaius00unkngoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Uprising_of_the_plebeyans%2C_c._494_%28First_secessio_plebis%29.jpg","title":"The first secession of the plebs, imagined in 1872","desc":"A wood engraving from Ludolph von Alvensleben's Allgemeine Weltgeschichte, printed at Vienna in 1872, showing ragged and half-clothed plebeians raising their fists at two men in togas who are hurrying away. It is a picture of the tradition rather than of the event: no ancient source describes the scene, and the print's Roman street is a 19th-century guess.","credit":"Illustration from Ludolph von Alvensleben, Allgemeine Weltgeschichte, vol. 1, Vienna 1872; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uprising_of_the_plebeyans%2C_c._494_%28First_secessio_plebis%29.jpg","alt":"A monochrome wood engraving of a crowd of barefoot, barely dressed men and women with raised fists and clenched hands, pressing forward past a bearded old man in rags towards two clean-shaven men in heavy togas who twist away from them."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the story of the struggle so hard to check?","a":"Because it comes from writers of the late Republic and later, and Livy warned that records for those centuries were few and nearly all had perished when the city burned."},{"q":"Why was the patrician case against plebeian consuls a religious one?","a":"Because asked why no plebeian might be consul, the consuls answered that no plebeian could take the auspices, which put the bar outside anything a vote could settle."},{"q":"Why did the Senate invent military tribunes with consular power?","a":"Because after the law of 445 BCE allowed the orders to marry it would rather open a substitute office to both than yield the consulship itself."}]},
{"id":"rm-102","num":102,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Menenius Agrippa's fable of the belly and the limbs was told to persuade the seceders of 494 BCE to come home, ending the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the promise of tribunes.","questions":["Livy calls the Sacred Mount beyond the Anio the likelier site of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though he knew an older account that put the withdrawal of 494 BCE on the Aventine instead.","Ordered out of Rome on the pretext of an Aequian war, the legions instead entrenched a camp on a hill and began the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which won the commons their first magistrates."],"answer":"first secession of the plebs","answerText":"first secession of the plebs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Sacred Mount, 3 Roman miles out</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribunes made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, per Dionysius; 2 or 5, per Livy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","italy","iron age"],"abstract":"The <b>first secession of the plebs</b> was the withdrawal of the Roman commons from the city in 494 BCE, and tradition makes it the act that won them magistrates of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy sets it after a campaign: the soldiers were still bound by the oath sworn to the consuls, so the Senate ordered the legions out of the city on the pretext of a renewed war with the Aequi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The men are said to have thought first of killing the consuls to be free of the oath, and to have given the idea up on hearing that no religious obligation could be dissolved by a crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the urging of Sicinius they ignored the consuls instead and withdrew to the Sacred Mount beyond the Anio, three Roman miles out, where they entrenched a camp and lived quietly, offering no provocation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy records that Piso put the withdrawal on the Aventine, and calls the Sacred Mount the more generally accepted account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Panic followed in the city, where neither order trusted the other and everyone feared what a foreign war would mean while the commons sat in their camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Senate sent Menenius Agrippa, an eloquent man of plebeian descent, who told the seceders how the limbs once conspired to starve the idle belly and wasted away with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The terms that brought them back gave the plebs magistrates whose persons were inviolable and who could protect a citizen against the consuls, and no patrician might hold the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy is unsure how many were made: two were elected and chose three colleagues, though some said only two, created on the Sacred Hill where the lex sacrata was passed, while Dionysius names five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dionysius has all Romans swear over the victims to keep that law for ever, an altar to Jupiter the Terrifier raised on the summit, and two assistants granted soon after who became the aediles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.45, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6B*.html#45. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.89, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6D*.html#89. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.90, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6D*.html#90. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Plebs,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/48/Aniene_a_Ponte_Nomentano_00238.JPG","title":"The Aniene at the Ponte Nomentano, northern Rome","desc":"The river Livy puts between the city and the Sacred Mount, seen from the bridge that carries the Via Nomentana over it. His three Roman miles are about 4.4 kilometres (2.8 miles), which is the measure of the secession: the commons camped within an afternoon's walk of the Forum, and stayed there.","credit":"Photograph by Lalupa, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aniene_a_Ponte_Nomentano_00238.JPG","alt":"A narrow green river running between steep banks of dense summer trees, photographed from a bridge looking upstream, with no buildings in sight."},"locator":{"name":"The Sacred Mount","at":[12.5325,41.9408],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the soldiers consider killing the consuls?","a":"Because they were still bound by the oath sworn to them, and gave the idea up on hearing that no religious obligation could be dissolved by a crime."},{"q":"Why was withdrawing from the city an effective weapon?","a":"Because the seceders entrenched a camp three miles out and lived quietly, leaving neither order trusting the other and everyone fearing what a foreign war would mean."},{"q":"Why is even the place of the secession uncertain?","a":"Because Livy records that Piso put the withdrawal on the Aventine while calling the Sacred Mount the more generally accepted account, and the sources disagree on the number of tribunes too."}]},
{"id":"rm-103","num":103,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Sacrosanct by oath, forbidden to leave Rome for a whole day and keeping his doors open all night, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> existed to shield one citizen from a magistrate.","questions":["Polybius reports that a single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could stop the Senate deciding anything at all, or even meeting, simply by interposing against it.","The office began on 10 December, could not be held in two successive years, and was closed to patricians; a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also lost his power a Roman mile beyond the gates."],"answer":"tribune of the plebs","answerText":"tribune of the plebs","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE, after the first secession</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 at first, 10 from 457 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Year began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Power ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 Roman mile beyond the gates</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["title","institution","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"The <b>tribune of the plebs</b> was the officer the Roman commons created in 494 BCE to shield a citizen from the magistrates, and it was the one office a patrician could not hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its purpose was auxilium, help against abuse by a patrician magistrate, and it came with no army, no province and no command of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made that help possible was sacrosanctity: the tribune's person was declared sacred, and whoever violated it was an outlaw whose goods were forfeit to the temple of Ceres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because anyone in need had to be able to reach him at any hour, the doors of a tribune's house stood open day and night, and he might not be away from the city for a whole day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His power stopped one Roman mile beyond the gates, and Livy has the consuls remark that a tribune who went out with the army came under their authority like any other citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The ancient writers agree that there were two at first, and Livy has the number raised to ten on terms that no man should hold the office in two successive years; the year began on 10 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Any one of them could stop the rest, a single colleague's intercessio making a resolution void, and Polybius reports that one tribune interposing left the Senate unable to decide anything or even to meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That veto grew until it could be laid on any act of any magistrate during his year of office, and on a decree of the Senate, without a reason being given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the decemvirate the consuls Valerius and Horatius renewed the inviolability with revived rites and a law, and Duillius carried a resolution that whoever left the plebs without tribunes should be scourged and beheaded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tribunes came in time to take part in the Senate's business, though seated on benches before its opened doors rather than among the senators themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Tribunus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1148-52, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Tribunus.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.16, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html#16. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.89, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6D*.html#89. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Tribuno_della_plebe_-_Vecellio_Cesare_-_1860.jpg","title":"A tribune of the plebs, as a Renaissance costume book imagined him","desc":"A plate and its facing page from Cesare Vecellio's Habiti antichi et moderni, in the Paris edition of 1859-60. The dress is a 16th-century guess, but the text beside it repeats what the ancient sources say of the office: the person inviolable, the single word veto enough to suspend the Senate and the consuls, the house open by day and night, and the number of tribunes first two and later ten.","credit":"Cesare Vecellio, Habiti antichi et moderni di tutto il Mondo (Paris: Firmin Didot Freres Fils, 1859-60); public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tribuno_della_plebe_-_Vecellio_Cesare_-_1860.jpg","alt":"An engraved book opening: on the left a standing man in a short tunic and cloak holding a short staff, framed in ornament; on the right a page of Italian and French text headed Tribuno della plebe and Tribun du peuple."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did a tribune’s house stand open day and night?","a":"Because his purpose was auxilium, help against a magistrate, so anyone in need had to be able to reach him at any hour — and he might not be away from the city for a whole day."},{"q":"Why did his power stop a mile outside the gates?","a":"Because the office protected a citizen against magistrates in the city, and Livy has the consuls remark that a tribune who went out with the army came under their authority like anyone else."},{"q":"Why could a single tribune stop the whole college?","a":"Because one colleague’s intercessio made a resolution void, and Polybius reports that one tribune interposing left the Senate unable to decide anything or even to meet."}]},
{"id":"rm-104","num":104,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Livy's jurists argued that the law of 449 BCE conferred no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on anyone, since what it did was make the offender sacer, which is not the same thing at all.","questions":["The oath sworn over the victims on the Sacred Mount gave the tribunes their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Dionysius reports the Romans still honouring it four centuries later.","Because an aedile could be seized and gaoled by a higher magistrate, Livy takes it as proved that the aedileship carried no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whatever the law of the consuls said."],"answer":"sacrosanctity","answerText":"sacrosanctity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sworn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE, on the Sacred Mount</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Penalty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">accursed; goods consecrated to Ceres</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renewed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">449 BCE, by Valerius and Horatius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","institution","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"<b>Sacrosanctity</b> was the protection the Roman plebeians threw around their own magistrates in 494 BCE: a curse on whoever laid hands on them, rather than a guard or a power of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The law drawn up on the Sacred Mount forbade anyone to compel a tribune against his will, to whip him or order him whipped, or to kill him or order him killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whoever did any of those things was to be accursed and his goods consecrated to Ceres, and anyone who killed such a man was to be guiltless of murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So that no later plebs could repeal it, all the Romans swore over the victims to keep it for all time, with a prayer that the gods above and below should turn on whoever broke it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius says the Roman custom of treating a tribune's person as sacrosanct arose from that oath and was still kept in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Livy has the settlement of 494 BCE give the plebs magistrates who were inviolable, and reports that some put the lex sacrata on the mount itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The protection therefore rested on a bargain between the orders and an oath sworn by the plebs alone, not on a law of the whole Roman people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the decemvirate, in 449 BCE, the consuls Valerius and Horatius revived the ancient rites and enacted that whoever offered violence to a tribune, an aedile or a decemviral judge should be devoted to Jupiter and his goods sold for the temple of Ceres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Jurists then read that law closely and answered that it made nobody sacrosanct at all: it made the offender sacer, and an aedile could still be seized and imprisoned by a higher magistrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Only the tribunes were sacrosanct, Livy concludes, and by the ancient oath rather than by statute; some read the law as covering the consuls too, which he rejects because a judge was then called praetor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.89, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6D*.html#89. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Tribunus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1148-52, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Tribunus.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Plebs,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]"],"locator":{"name":"The Sacred Mount","at":[12.5325,41.9408],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the protection a curse rather than a guard?","a":"Because the law made whoever laid hands on a tribune accursed with his goods consecrated to Ceres, and anyone who killed such a man guiltless of murder, so the deterrent was religious."},{"q":"Why did all the Romans swear over the victims?","a":"Because the oath was what stopped a later plebs repealing it, binding the law for all time with a prayer that the gods above and below turn on whoever broke it."},{"q":"Why did jurists say the law of 449 BCE made nobody sacrosanct?","a":"Because it made the offender sacer rather than the officer inviolable, and an aedile could still be seized and imprisoned by a higher magistrate."}]},
{"id":"rm-105","num":105,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Because a tribune could not summon the patricians, what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> voted was reckoned a scitum rather than a lex, and the meeting itself was not comitia.","questions":["A law of 471 BCE moved the election of the tribunes to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taking from the patricians the votes their clients had been casting in choosing them.","Voting by local tribes rather than by centuries, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the poorer citizens a weight that the property classes of the older assembly had denied them."],"answer":"concilium plebis","answerText":"concilium plebis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Summoned by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a tribune of the plebs, from 494 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elects</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 tribunes and the plebeian aediles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Binding on all</span><span class=\"dt-v\">339 BCE, and by the lex Hortensia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","concept","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"The <b>concilium plebis</b> was the assembly of the Roman plebeians alone, summoned by their own tribunes, in which the commons chose their magistrates and voted the resolutions called plebiscita.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its name marks a legal distinction: a man who could call together only a part of the people held a concilium, where one who could call the whole people held comitia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because a tribune could not summon the patricians or refer any matter to them, what such a meeting passed was a scitum and not a lex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plebeians voted in the local tribes rather than in the centuries, where the wealthiest classes could settle a question before the poorer ones were asked at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Those tribes had begun as a topographical arrangement of little importance to the state, and grew into the real representation of the people as the commons came to outnumber the patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In 471 BCE a law carried by Volero Publilius moved the election of the plebs' own magistrates to the assembly of the tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy makes the point of it plain: the measure looked harmless and took from the patricians the power of electing through their clients' votes the tribunes they wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fight over it ran the length of a year, the plebs re-electing Volero and the patricians answering with the consulship of Appius Claudius, who lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A law of 449 BCE laid down that the people should be bound by what the plebs voted in its tribes, and a Publilian law of 339 BCE said again that those decisions bound all the Quirites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gaius records that the patricians went on claiming they were not bound by resolutions passed without their authority, until the lex Hortensia made the plebs' votes binding on the whole people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Plebiscitum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 927-28, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebiscitum.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Comitia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 330-40, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Comitia.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.58, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Gaius, <i>The Institutes of Gaius</i> 1.3, trans. James Muirhead (Edinburgh: T. & T. Clark, 1880), 3, https://archive.org/stream/institutesgaius00unkngoog/institutesgaius00unkngoog_djvu.txt. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Plebs,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was what this assembly passed not called a law?","a":"Because a man who could summon only part of the people held a concilium, and a tribune could not summon the patricians or refer anything to them, so its votes were scita rather than leges."},{"q":"Why did voting in the tribes rather than the centuries matter?","a":"Because in the centuries the wealthiest classes could settle a question before the poorer ones were asked at all, where the tribes had begun as a topographical arrangement of little importance."},{"q":"Why did Volero’s harmless-looking law provoke a year of fighting?","a":"Because it took from the patricians the power of electing, through their clients’ votes, the tribunes they wanted."}]},
{"id":"rm-106","num":106,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Named from the care of Ceres' temple and made beside the tribunate in 494 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as an assistant's post and grew into a magistracy of buildings, markets and games.","questions":["When the plebeian holders of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused to add a fourth day to the Great Games, young patricians volunteered and the senate created two more from its own order.","The decrees of the senate, which consuls had suppressed or altered at will, were put into the keeping of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the temple of Ceres after the decemvirate."],"answer":"aedileship","answerText":"aedileship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">494 BCE, with the tribunate</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Keepers of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">senate decrees, from 449 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Curule pair</span><span class=\"dt-v\">367 BCE, with the praetorship</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, then 4 in all</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["title","institution","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"The <b>aedileship</b> was the office the Roman plebs created beside its tribunate in 494 BCE, and its name is said to come from the care of the temple, the aedes, of Ceres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Dionysius has the plebeians ask the senate for leave to appoint two of their own each year to assist the tribunes, to decide the causes referred to them, to oversee sacred and public places, and to see the market supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their duties at first were ministerial, and the aediles heard the smaller cases the tribunes handed down to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the decemvirate they were made keepers of the decrees of the senate, which the consuls had until then suppressed or altered as they pleased, and those decrees were lodged with them at the temple of Ceres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They kept the plebiscites as well, and took on the upkeep of buildings sacred and private, the state of the streets, and the corn distributed among the plebs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The law of 449 BCE that renewed the tribunes' inviolability covered an aedile too, devoting to Jupiter whoever offered him violence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether that made an aedile sacrosanct is the question Livy says the jurists answered no to, since an aedile could still be seized and gaoled by a higher magistrate, though Smith's dictionary reads the same chapter the other way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two more aediles were added from the patricians after the plebeian pair refused to superintend a fourth day added to the Great Games and the younger patricians offered to take the honour on themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy makes these curule aediles part of one bargain with the praetorship, the patricians' equivalent for conceding a consulship to the plebs in 367 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The senate then felt some shame at keeping the new office to its own order, agreed that it should go to the plebs in alternate years, and afterwards left it open to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Aediles,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 18-20, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Aediles.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 6.90, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1940), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/6D*.html#90. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Plebs,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6b/Rilievo_funerario_con_sella_curulis%2C_I_sec._a.C._-_I_sec._d.C._Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme_-FG.jpg","title":"A curule chair on a Roman funerary relief","desc":"Marble from a tomb on the Via Casilina outside Rome, of the later 1st century BCE or earlier 1st century CE, now in the Palazzo Massimo alle Terme. The folding seat carved at its centre is the sella curulis, the chair the curule aediles took their name from and one of the honours that set them apart from the plebeian pair; the relief commemorates some curule magistrate and need not be an aedile's.","credit":"Photograph by Fabrizio Garrisi, via Wikimedia Commons, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rilievo_funerario_con_sella_curulis,_I_sec._a.C._-_I_sec._d.C._Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme_-FG.jpg","alt":"A weathered limestone relief block: a folding backless chair carved in high relief at the centre under a draped cloth, flanked by two standing figures, with a narrow frieze of small figures along the top edge."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why were the decrees of the senate lodged with these officers?","a":"Because until then the consuls had suppressed or altered them as they pleased, so after the decemvirate the aediles were made their keepers at the temple of Ceres."},{"q":"Why is it disputed whether an aedile was sacrosanct?","a":"Because the law of 449 BCE devoted to Jupiter whoever offered him violence, and yet an aedile could still be seized and gaoled by a higher magistrate — which Livy reads one way and Smith’s dictionary the other."},{"q":"Why did the office end up open to both orders?","a":"Because the curule aedileship was created as part of the patricians’ price for conceding a consulship, and the senate then felt some shame at keeping it and agreed to alternate years."}]},
{"id":"rm-107","num":107,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Livy has the matrons send the mother and wife of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out to his camp, and a temple to Fortuna Muliebris marked the day he turned his Volscian army away from Rome.","questions":["Told that corn had come from Sicily during a famine, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that the plebs could have it at the old price only if the senate had its old powers back.","A dash through an open gate at a Volscian town, and fire thrown on the houses along its wall, won <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the surname he is remembered by."],"answer":"Coriolanus","answerText":"Coriolanus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Corioli taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 493 BCE, in Livy's account</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the assault that gave him his surname</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"abstract":"<b>Coriolanus</b> is the surname Roman tradition gives a patrician of the early Republic who took a Volscian town, was driven out of Rome, and came back against it at the head of a Volscian army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy calls him Cnaeus Marcius and Plutarch Caius Marcius, and both put him among the young soldiers at the siege of Corioli.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There he beat back a sortie, made a dash through the open gate, cut down the men nearest him and threw fire on the houses along the wall; the town fell, and gave him his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy sets the siege in the consulship of Postumus Cominius, the year of the treaty with the Latin cities, counted as 493 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> When corn came from Sicily in a famine, he told the senate that if the plebs wanted it at the old price they must give the senate back its old powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He asked whether a man who could not put up with a Tarquin as king should put up with a Sicinius, and told the commons to secede again and enjoy the scarcity they had made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribunes impeached him, he did not appear on the day of trial, and he was condemned in his absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He went into exile among the Volscians, was taken in by Attius Tullius, their leading man and a lifelong enemy of Rome, and the two planned the war together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has the matrons send his mother Veturia and his wife Volumnia out to the camp with his two small sons, where she asked whether she had come to a son or an enemy; he broke camp, and a temple was built to Fortuna Muliebris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch tells the same scene with the names moved, Volumnia the mother and Vergilia the wife, and has Volscian conspirators fall on him in a body, where Livy will not choose between the traditions of his death and cites Fabius, the oldest authority, for an old age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D40. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Coriolanus</i> 1, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Coriolanus*.html#1. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Coriolanus</i> 4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Coriolanus*.html#4. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Coriolanus</i> 33, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Coriolanus*.html#33. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Coriolanus</i> 39, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Coriolanus*.html#39. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Balbo</i> 53, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0020%3Atext%3DBalb.%3Asection%3D53. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/Gavin_Hamilton_-_Coriolanus_Act_V%2C_Scene_III.jpg","title":"The women come to the camp, engraved in 1803","desc":"James Caldwall's engraving after Gavin Hamilton, from Boydell's Shakespeare Gallery. Coriolanus stands in armour at the left while the women plead; the standing matron is named Volumnia here, because the picture follows Shakespeare, who follows Plutarch, and Livy calls the mother Veturia and gives that name to the wife instead.","credit":"Engraving by James Caldwall after Gavin Hamilton, published by J. and J. Boydell, London, 1803; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons, from the Library of Congress. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gavin_Hamilton_-_Coriolanus_Act_V,_Scene_III.jpg","alt":"A monochrome engraving: a young man in Roman armour with a sword stands at the left of a camp, turning towards a standing veiled matron who reaches out to him; kneeling women and a small child with raised arms fill the middle, with tents, spears and soldiers behind."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the corn from Sicily become a political weapon?","a":"Because he told the senate that if the plebs wanted it at the old price they must give the senate back its old powers, turning a famine into terms."},{"q":"Why was he condemned without a hearing?","a":"Because the tribunes impeached him and he did not appear on the day of trial, so the sentence was passed in his absence."},{"q":"Why does the tradition not settle how he died?","a":"Because Plutarch has Volscian conspirators fall on him in a body while Livy will not choose, citing Fabius, the oldest authority, for an old age instead."}]},
{"id":"rm-108","num":108,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Livy's deputation crossed the Tiber to four iugera of field and found <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at a ditch or a plough, and he sent for his toga before he would hear them.","questions":["A dictatorship granted for six months and laid down on the sixteenth day is the fact the tradition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rests on, though Livy says the plebs mistrusted him.","Made dictator a second time at eighty against Spurius Maelius, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called the killing lawful because the accused had not come when summoned."],"answer":"Cincinnatus","answerText":"Cincinnatus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dictator</span><span class=\"dt-v\">458 BCE, laid down on day 16 of 180</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dictator again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">439 BCE, in his 80th year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Farm</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 iugera across the Tiber</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"abstract":"<b>Cincinnatus</b> is Lucius Quinctius, a Roman patrician of the 5th century BCE whose two dictatorships became the Republic's standing example of a man who took supreme power and gave it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Livy has an army trapped by the Aequi and the senate's deputation crossing the Tiber to four iugera of field, about one hectare (2.5 acres), where they found him digging a ditch or ploughing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He called to his wife Racilia for his toga, wiped off the dust and the sweat, put it on, and was saluted dictator.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy introduces the scene as a lesson for anyone who thinks there is no room for honour or virtue without lavish wealth, so the story arrives already labelled as a moral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pliny repeats it a century later as what tradition says, and explains that the senate's messengers were called viatores from fetching men out of their fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> He relieved the army, triumphed, and laid down on the sixteenth day a dictatorship granted for six months, which is the fact the whole tradition rests on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same chapters carry what the pageant leaves out: the plebs were not glad to see him, holding the power excessive and the man more dangerous than it, and he kept the office long enough to force the trial of Marcus Volscius through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smith's dictionary of biography, which gathers the ancient references, calls him a violent opponent of the plebeians' claims, dates the two dictatorships to 458 and 439 BCE, and reports that the legend's inconsistencies have long been pointed out and that some have thought it altogether fabulous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In that second dictatorship, at eighty, he was set against Spurius Maelius, a rich corn dealer accused of aiming at monarchy, and he named Gaius Servilius Ahala master of the horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ahala ran Maelius down and killed him in the street, and the dictator told the assembly the killing was lawful even if Maelius were guiltless of treason, because he had not come when summoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.26, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.15, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 18.4, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D18%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Cincinnatus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology</i> (London: John Murray, 1873), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0104%3Aentry%3Dcincinnatus-bio-2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Alexandre_Cabanel_-_Cincinnatus_Receiving_Deputies_of_the_Senate.jpeg","title":"Cincinnatus receiving the envoys, painted in 1843","desc":"Alexandre Cabanel's picture in the Musee Fabre, Montpellier: a lean, sunburnt ploughman with his oxen faces the togate envoys and their scroll, with a walled Rome on the hill behind. The painter keeps him stripped to the work, which is Pliny's version of the tradition; in Livy he sends for his toga before he will hear the message at all.","credit":"Alexandre Cabanel, Cincinnatus recevant les ambassadeurs de Rome, 1843, Musee Fabre; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandre_Cabanel_-_Cincinnatus_Receiving_Deputies_of_the_Senate.jpeg","alt":"An oil painting: a bare-chested man in a short white kilt stands beside a yoked ox at the left of a dusty field, turning towards a group of men in white and orange togas, one holding a scroll, with hills and a walled city behind."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the whole tradition rest on one fact?","a":"Because he laid down on the sixteenth day a dictatorship granted for six months, which is what makes the story about giving power back rather than about taking it."},{"q":"Why should the scene at the plough be read carefully?","a":"Because Livy introduces it as a lesson for anyone who thinks there is no room for honour or virtue without lavish wealth, so the story arrives already labelled as a moral."},{"q":"Why does the pageant leave out how the plebs saw him?","a":"Because the same chapters say they were not glad to see him, holding the power excessive and the man more dangerous than it, and he kept the office long enough to force a trial through."}]},
{"id":"rm-109","num":109,"category":"The early Republic","question":"A consul proclaimed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone and without witnesses between midnight and morning, after taking the auspices, and no election was held for the office at all.","questions":["Polybius told his Greek readers that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had 24 lictors to a consul's twelve, and that every magistrate but the tribunes left office at his appointment.","Six months was the outside limit of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who could take no money the senate had not granted and could not leave Italy or ride a horse in Rome without leave."],"answer":"Roman dictator","answerText":"Roman dictator","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Limit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 months, often laid down sooner</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Appeal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">none at first; a law of 449 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last old-style</span><span class=\"dt-v\">216 BCE, after Cannae</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["title","institution","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman dictator</b> was a single magistrate given the whole authority of the state for a limited time, appointed when two consuls checking each other were thought too slow for the danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The office was first called magister populi, the master of the people, and the sacred books kept that name for it to the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was not elected: a consul proclaimed him, probably without witnesses, between midnight and morning, and with the auspices taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says it is not clearly ascertained in what year the office was created, who that year's consuls were, or who the first dictator was, though his oldest authorities gave Titus Lartius, with Spurius Cassius as master of the horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that when the axes were first carried before a dictator inside the city a great fear fell on the people, since there was no colleague to appeal to and no right of appeal at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius, explaining Rome to Greeks, says the consuls have twelve lictors and the dictator 24, and that he is a general with absolute power before whom every magistrate in Rome but the tribunes ceases to hold office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A dictator had to name a master of the horse, and if that man died another was named in his place, so the power was never quite held alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The limits were real: six months at the outside, no money but what the senate granted, no leaving Italy, and no riding a horse in Rome without the people's leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the decemvirate a law forbade the appointment of any magistrate from whom there was no appeal, and the dictator's freedom from it is thought to have ended there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lesser dictators were named to hold elections, fix holidays, preside at games or conduct trials; the last named to carry on the state's business came after Cannae in 216 BCE, and the dictatorships of Sulla and Caesar followed neither the old law nor the old precedent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Dictator,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 404-8, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Dictator.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.18, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.87, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html#87. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/06_Julius_Caesar%2C_Aemilia_Denarius._Caesar_CAESAR_DICT_PERPETVO%2C_Fasces_Caduceus_axe_globe_hands_L.AEMILIVS_BVCA._Winckless_collection.jpg","title":"A denarius reading CAESAR DICT PERPETVO","desc":"Silver, struck for Caesar by the moneyer Lucius Aemilius Buca. The reverse carries the fasces with its axe, crossed with a caduceus, beside a globe and clasped hands; the obverse legend names him dictator in perpetuity, which is the one thing the old rules of the office forbade.","credit":"Photograph by Andrew McCabe, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:06_Julius_Caesar,_Aemilia_Denarius._Caesar_CAESAR_DICT_PERPETVO,_Fasces_Caduceus_axe_globe_hands_L.AEMILIVS_BVCA._Winckless_collection.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin on black: at the left a laureate male head in profile facing right with a legend around the rim; at the right a bundle of rods with an axe head crossed with a herald's staff, a small globe above and two clasped hands at the left."},"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4828,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome need a single magistrate at all?","a":"Because two consuls checking each other were thought too slow for a danger, so the whole authority of the state was given to one man for a limited time."},{"q":"Why did a great fear fall on the people at the first appointment?","a":"Because the axes were carried before him inside the city, and there was no colleague to appeal to and no right of appeal at all."},{"q":"Why was the power never quite held alone?","a":"Because a dictator had to name a master of the horse, and if that man died another was named in his place."}]},
{"id":"rm-110","num":110,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Commissioners went to Athens to copy Solon's laws before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were drawn up, and a board of ten men from whom there was no appeal did the drafting.","questions":["Livy calls the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the source of all public and private jurisprudence, and says they still were in his own day, under the heap of later legislation.","No text of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives, only brief archaic fragments quoted by later writers, though they were cut on bronze and set up publicly for anyone to read."],"answer":"Twelve Tables","answerText":"Twelve Tables","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commissioners</span><span class=\"dt-v\">454 BCE, to Athens and the Greek cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The Ten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">451 BCE; a second board in 450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Twelve complete</span><span class=\"dt-v\">449 BCE, ratified by the centuries</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["text","institution","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"The <b>Twelve Tables</b> were Rome's first written law, drawn up by a commission that set the ordinary constitution aside and published on tablets in the Forum for anyone to read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Livy has the senate agree to send commissioners to Athens to copy the famous laws of Solon and to look into the institutions, customs and laws of the other Greek states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On their return a board of Ten was created, from whom there was no appeal, and every other magistracy was suspended while they worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ten tables were exhibited to an assembly and amended on what the public said of them before the centuries passed them, and two more were added under a second board the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls the result the source of all public and private jurisprudence, and says it still was in his own day, under the heap of later legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The text was cut on bronze and set up in a public place, one late authority says the first ten were on ivory, and Livy notes that some writers had the aediles do the setting up on the tribunes' orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is not the code but fragments quoted by later writers, brief and archaic, so what the tables said is known at second hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those fragments run from procedure to burial: a defendant summoned to court must go, a debtor adjudged had thirty days and could then be held in bonds on a pound of grits a day, and no body was to be buried or burned inside the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The eleventh table forbade marriage between plebeians and patricians, and a tribune carried the repeal of that provision in 445 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> After the Gauls burned the city an order went out to collect the old treaties and laws, which has been read both as meaning the tables were lost and as meaning they were looked for and found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Lex XII Tabularum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 688-90, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Lex_XII_Tabularum.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.31, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D31. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.57, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]","\"The Twelve Tables,\" in <i>The Avalon Project: Documents in Law, History and Diplomacy</i> (New Haven: Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School), https://avalon.law.yale.edu/ancient/twelve_tables.asp. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Plebs,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 923-27, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebs.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Xii_leges.jpg","title":"The Twelve Tables imagined in a 1555 law book","desc":"A woodcut from a 16th-century law book, lettered LEGES XII TAB, showing twelve robed and bearded jurists seated in a niche, each holding a tablet. No ancient tablet survives, so every picture of the Twelve Tables is somebody's invention; this one dresses the decemvirs as Renaissance lawyers.","credit":"Reproduction of a woodcut from a 16th-century law book, dated 1555 on Commons, uploaded by Sebastian Sapia; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xii_leges.jpg","alt":"A wide monochrome woodcut: a row of about twelve bearded men in long robes and caps seated side by side in a shallow arched niche, most holding a small tablet or book, above a scrolled cartouche lettered LEGES XII. TAB."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was publishing the law in the Forum the point of it?","a":"Because the tablets were set up in a public place for anyone to read, which made the law knowable rather than something the magistrates alone could state."},{"q":"Why is what the tables said known only at second hand?","a":"Because what survives is not the code but brief archaic fragments quoted by later writers, so the text is reconstructed from citations."},{"q":"Why is the fate of the tables after the Gallic sack disputed?","a":"Because the order to collect the old treaties and laws has been read both as meaning they were lost and as meaning they were looked for and found."}]},
{"id":"rm-111","num":111,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Every other magistrate abdicated, the tribunes included, when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the whole government of Rome in 451 BCE to get the law written down.","questions":["A Forum filled with 120 lictors bearing axes in their rods announced the second year of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Livy says the Ten looked like ten kings.","When the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would neither resign nor hold elections, a secession forced a decree that it stand down and that the Pontifex Maximus choose new tribunes."],"answer":"decemvirate","answerText":"decemvirate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Board of Ten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">451 BCE, all other offices suspended</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second board</span><span class=\"dt-v\">450 BCE, and refused to give it up</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lictors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">120, with axes in the fasces</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","event","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"The <b>decemvirate</b> was the board of ten men, the decemviri legibus scribundis, to whom Rome handed its whole government in 451 BCE so that its law could be written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every other magistrate had to abdicate, the tribunes of the plebs among them, and from the Ten there was no appeal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy has the first board administer justice in turn, the one presiding for the day attended by the twelve lictors while each of the others had a single usher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He says a singular harmony prevailed among them and the most perfect equity was shown to others, and their ten tables were passed by the assembly of the centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two tables were thought to be still wanting, so a second board was elected for another year, of whom Appius Claudius alone had sat on the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On its first day that second board dropped the rule that only one man held the fasces: all ten took them at once, and the Forum filled with 120 lictors carrying the axes tied in the rods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They explained that since they were invested with absolute power of life and death there was no reason for the axes to be removed, and Livy says they presented the appearance of ten kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The terror fell on everyone at first and then on the plebs alone, the patricians going unmolested, and when the year ran out the Ten would neither resign nor hold an election for successors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A secession of the soldiers and the plebs ended it, and the senate decreed that the decemvirs resign, that the Pontifex Maximus hold an election of tribunes, and that nobody suffer for the secession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The commons went back to the Aventine to choose those tribunes, the consulship returned, and the ten tables and the two together made the Twelve Tables, cut in brass and set up in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Decemviri,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 386-87, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Decemviri.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.34, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.54, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.57, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D57. [Open access]"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did every other magistracy have to be suspended?","a":"Because the board was given the whole government so the law could be written down, and from the Ten there was no appeal — the tribunes of the plebs abdicating with the rest."},{"q":"Why did all ten taking the fasces at once matter?","a":"Because it filled the Forum with 120 lictors carrying the axes tied in the rods, and Livy says they presented the appearance of ten kings."},{"q":"Why did the terror settle on the plebs alone?","a":"Because the patricians went unmolested, so what had begun as a fear falling on everyone became one order's grievance and ended in a secession."}]},
{"id":"rm-112","num":112,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Presiding over the election for the second board of Ten, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> returned his own name, which Livy calls the worst of precedents and a thing only tribunes had done.","questions":["The charge Verginius brought against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a single one, that contrary to law he had adjudged a free person to be a slave.","Told to name an umpire or go to prison, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> said that he appealed, though it was he who had taken the right of appeal away."],"answer":"Appius Claudius Crassus","answerText":"Appius Claudius Crassus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decemvir</span><span class=\"dt-v\">451 BCE, and again in 450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Charge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 only: adjudging a free person a slave</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before his trial, after 450 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","history","politics","italy"],"abstract":"<b>Appius Claudius Crassus</b> is the decemvir whom Roman tradition made the villain of the fall of the decemvirate, and the only man to sit on both boards of Ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has him take the presidency of the first board at the plebs' own wish, and says he had assumed such a new character that from a bitter enemy of the people he suddenly appeared as their advocate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the second board was to be chosen he presided over the election himself and returned his own name, a thing Livy says nobody but a tribune had ever done and calls the worst of precedents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In office the Ten dropped the rule that one man held the fasces, and 120 lictors filled the Forum with the axes tied in the rods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The story that brought him down is Verginia's: he set a client to claim a betrothed girl as his slave while her father was away with the army, and gave judgment for the claimant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Her father took her aside among the booths by the shrine of Venus Cloacina, stabbed her with a butcher's knife, and said that in the only way he could he vindicated her freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army and the plebs seceded, the decemvirs resigned, and Verginius prosecuted him on one charge only, that contrary to law he had adjudged a free person to be a slave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Told to name an umpire or go to prison, Appius said that he appealed, and Livy makes the whole assembly hear that the man who had taken the right of appeal away was now using it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He pleaded his ancestors, his resignation of the consulship to make equal laws for all, and the laws still in force whose author was being carried to gaol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In Livy's account he put an end to his life before the day of trial, as his colleague Oppius did after him; their property was confiscated and the rest of the Ten went into exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.48, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D48. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.58, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D58. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Decemviri,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 386-87, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Decemviri.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/40/La_mort_de_Virginie%2C_Gabriel-Fran%C3%A7ois_Doyen%2C_1756-8.jpg","title":"The death of Verginia, painted about 1757","desc":"Gabriel-Francois Doyen's history painting: the decemvir sits on his tribunal at the upper left, the father stands at the centre with the knife, and the girl falls back into the arms behind her. Livy puts the killing a few steps away, among the booths by the shrine of Venus Cloacina and only in sight of the tribunal; the painter brings it to the judge's feet.","credit":"Gabriel-Francois Doyen, La mort de Virginie, about 1757; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_mort_de_Virginie,_Gabriel-Fran%C3%A7ois_Doyen,_1756-8.jpg","alt":"A crowded oil painting on marble steps: a magistrate in red on a raised seat at the upper left, a bearded man at the centre with his arm raised holding a blade, a young woman in white collapsing into the arms of figures behind her, and soldiers and onlookers recoiling on both sides."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did presiding over his own election set the worst of precedents?","a":"Because he returned his own name, which Livy says nobody but a tribune had ever done, so the man conducting the election became one of its results."},{"q":"Why is Verginia’s death the turning point of the story?","a":"Because her father stabbed her among the booths by the shrine of Venus Cloacina, saying that in the only way he could he vindicated her freedom, and the army and plebs then seceded."},{"q":"Why did his appeal condemn him in the assembly’s eyes?","a":"Because the man who had taken the right of appeal away was now using it, which Livy makes the whole assembly hear."}]},
{"id":"rm-113","num":113,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Lucius Verginius asked leave to question the nurse, took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> aside to the booths by the shrine of Venus Cloacina, and stabbed her to keep her free.","questions":["Appius ruled that a girl in her father's power could be surrendered only to her father, and so handed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the client who claimed her as his slave.","Livy says the decemvirs fell as the kings had fallen, and sets the death of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside Lucretia's as the same cause working a second time."],"answer":"Verginia","answerText":"Verginia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">449 BCE, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">in the Forum, by the shrine of Venus Cloacina</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Verginia</b> is the plebeian girl whose killing by her own father brought down the decemvirate in 449 BCE, in the account Roman writers handed down. Livy opens the episode by calling it a repetition, saying the decemvirs ended as the kings had and fell for the same cause, with the death of Lucretia standing behind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was betrothed to Lucius Icilius, a former tribune, and her father was away with the army on Algidus when the decemvir Appius Claudius set a client to claim her as a slave born in his own house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The seizure came as she went to school in the Forum, where the grammar schools were held in booths, and her maid’s cries invoking the protection of the Quirites drew a crowd that stopped it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a lawsuit rather than an abduction because a claim on a person’s status went before a magistrate, and an assertor could lay hands on a reputed slave to maintain their freedom, a right the Twelve Tables protected by capping his security at 50 asses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Appius ruled that the protection held only where freedom itself was the thing claimed, and that a girl in her father’s power could be given up to nobody but her father, so the claimant kept her meanwhile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of the judgment Livy writes that earlier writers may have stated some ground for it, but that he can find none anywhere that would justify it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Verginius asked leave to question her nurse, took her aside to the booths by the shrine of Venus Cloacina, stabbed her, and said that in the only way he could he vindicated her freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The army and the plebs seceded, the Ten resigned, and the plebs went back to the Aventine to elect tribunes again under the Pontifex Maximus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tellings differ on the family: Livy makes Publius Numitorius her grandfather and her mother alive, where Dionysius makes him her maternal uncle and says she had lost her mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","law","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.45, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.47, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.48, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D48. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.54, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 11.28, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/11B*.html. [Open access]","George Long, \"Assertor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 143, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Assertor.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/Filippino_Lippi_-_Sc%C3%A8nes_de_l%27histoire_de_Virginie_Appius_Claudius_fait_arr%C3%AAter_Virginie%2C_le_d%C3%A9cemvir_condamne_Virginie_%C3%A0_l%27esclavage%2C_Virginie_est_tu%C3%A9e_par_Virginius%2C_MI_501.jpg","title":"Scenes from the story of Verginia, a 15th-century panel","desc":"Filippino Lippi tells the whole episode across one long panel, in the manner of Florentine furniture painting: the Louvre's title names three scenes, the arrest, the judgment condemning her to slavery, and the killing. The tribunal is the fixed point at the centre, under the dark arch, with the decemvir raised above the crowd; everything else moves, left and right, along a loggia that is Florentine architecture rather than Roman.","credit":"Filippino Lippi, Scenes from the story of Verginia, Musee du Louvre MI 501; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Filippino_Lippi_-_Sc%C3%A8nes_de_l%27histoire_de_Virginie_Appius_Claudius_fait_arr%C3%AAter_Virginie%2C_le_d%C3%A9cemvir_condamne_Virginie_%C3%A0_l%27esclavage%2C_Virginie_est_tu%C3%A9e_par_Virginius%2C_MI_501.jpg","alt":"A long, low painted panel about three times as wide as it is tall, showing a loggia of five arches with landscape behind them. Figures in bright tunics and cloaks hurry left and right across a pale pavement: at the left a woman is pulled away by the arm, at the centre a robed man sits enthroned on a raised dais under the dark middle arch with a crowd pressing round the steps, and at the right people flee towards a waiting red horse held by a groom."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the seizure a lawsuit rather than an abduction?","a":"Because a claim on a person’s status went before a magistrate, and an assertor could lay hands on a reputed slave to maintain their freedom, a right the Twelve Tables protected."},{"q":"Why does Livy refuse to defend the judgment?","a":"Because he writes that earlier writers may have stated some ground for it but that he can find none anywhere that would justify it."},{"q":"Why does Livy open the episode by calling it a repetition?","a":"Because the decemvirs ended as the kings had and fell for the same cause, with the death of Lucretia standing behind Verginia’s."}]},
{"id":"rm-114","num":114,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Two consuls carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 449 BCE, restoring the appeal the decemvirs had annulled and making anyone who appointed a magistrate without it lawful to kill.","questions":["By the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> what the plebs voted in their tribes was to bind the whole people, an enactment Livy records being carried again in 339 BCE.","Violence against a tribune, an aedile or a decemviral judge forfeited the offender to Jupiter under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his goods were sold for the temple of Ceres."],"answer":"Valerio-Horatian laws","answerText":"Valerio-Horatian laws","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">449 BCE, by the consuls</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Laws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, with 2 lesser measures</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Repeated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">339 BCE and 287 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Valerio-Horatian laws</b> are the three consular laws of 449 BCE, carried by the consuls Lucius Valerius Potitus and Marcus Horatius Barbatus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first carried in the Assembly of Centuries that what the plebs passed in their tribes should bind the whole people, a point Livy says had been doubtful until then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius says what was at stake: the patricians had treated tribal votes as the plebs’ private business, and the poor prevailed in the tribal assembly where the patricians prevailed in the centuriate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The second restored the appeal the decemvirs had annulled and went further, forbidding any magistrate from whom there was no appeal and making whoever appointed one lawful to kill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The third made anyone who offered violence to a tribune, an aedile or a decemviral judge forfeit to Jupiter, his goods sold for the temple of Ceres, Liber and Libera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Livy adds that the jurists denied this made anybody sacrosanct, since it made the offender <i>sacer</i> instead, and that only the tribunes were sacrosanct, by the old plebeian oath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was restored was the <i>provocatio</i>, the appeal to the people in a matter touching life, as against the <i>appellatio</i>, the call on a magistrate for help; Appius Claudius used both at his trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two lesser measures went with them: decrees of the senate, till then altered at a consul’s pleasure, were to be lodged with the aediles at the temple of Ceres, and Marcus Duillius carried a plebiscite that whoever left the plebs without tribunes be scourged and beheaded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first law will not stay put: Livy has the same enactment carried again in 339 BCE by the dictator Quintus Publilius Philo, that the decisions of the plebs bind all the Quirites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A third, the Lex Hortensia of 287 BCE, is the one later jurists treat as putting plebiscites finally on the same footing as laws; the surviving notices do not say whether 449 BCE was a first step or a date read back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","law","history","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 11.45, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/11C*.html. [Open access]","George Long, \"Plebiscitum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 927-28, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plebiscitum.html. [Open access]","George Long, \"Appellatio,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 107-08, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Appellatio.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.56, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D56. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/R%C3%B6m._Republik-_P._Porcius_Laeca_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5547989.jpg","title":"A denarius of P. Porcius Laeca, about 110 BCE","desc":"The reverse shows a citizen at the left raising his hand to an officer, a lictor with the rods at the right, and PROVOCO along the bottom: I appeal. The coin is three centuries younger than the law, and a moneyer advertising his own family's part in the legislation on appeal, so it is evidence of how Romans remembered the right rather than of what was carried in 449 BCE.","credit":"Reinhard Saczewski, Munzkabinett, Staatliche Museen zu Berlin; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B6m._Republik-_P._Porcius_Laeca_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5547989.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a silver Roman coin side by side. At the left, a helmeted head facing right with the letters P LAECA running down the edge and an X behind. At the right, three small standing figures: a robed man at the left with a raised hand, an armoured figure in the centre with one arm outstretched, and a smaller figure at the right holding a bundle of rods, with the word PROVOCO struck along the bottom."},"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did it matter which assembly a vote was taken in?","a":"Because the poor prevailed in the tribal assembly where the patricians prevailed in the centuriate, so the patricians had treated tribal votes as the plebs’ private business."},{"q":"Why did the appeal law forbid a whole class of magistracy?","a":"Because restoring provocatio was not enough while an office existed from which there was no appeal, so appointing one was made lawful to punish with death."},{"q":"Why is it unclear what the first of the three actually settled?","a":"Because the same enactment is carried again in 339 BCE and again in 287, and the surviving notices do not say whether 449 was a first step or a date read back."}]},
{"id":"rm-115","num":115,"category":"The early Republic","question":"The tribune Gaius Canuleius carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 445 BCE, opening marriage between patricians and plebeians after the decemvirs had banned it only a few years before.","questions":["What the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> granted was conubium, the set of conditions making a marriage lawful, whose chief effect was that children followed their father’s rank and came under his power.","Asked why no plebeian could be consul, the consuls said none could take the auspices, and they conceded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hoping the tribunes would drop the rest."],"answer":"Lex Canuleia","answerText":"Lex Canuleia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">445 BCE, by the tribune Gaius Canuleius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">repealing the decemvirs’ marriage ban</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Followed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">444 BCE, the first consular tribunes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lex Canuleia</b> is the law of 445 BCE, carried by the tribune Gaius Canuleius, that allowed marriage between patricians and plebeians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it granted was <i>conubium</i>, which in Roman law is not a licence to marry but the whole set of conditions making a marriage lawful, and whose chief effect is that the children follow their father’s condition and fall under his power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That is why the ban mattered: without <i>conubium</i> a union left the children’s standing and inheritance uncertain, so the question was descent rather than affection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The prohibition Canuleius attacked was not ancient, and he told the assembly so, saying the decemvirs had made it within the last few years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He argued that intermarriage is a right commonly granted to neighbours and foreigners, and that Rome had given a conquered enemy citizenship, which is more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy has the patricians answer that their blood would be contaminated and the rights of the houses thrown into confusion, and that a child of such a marriage would be half of each and not even in harmony with himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second and separate bill, brought by nine tribunes, would have let the people elect consuls from either order, and the consuls’ reason for refusing it gave the case away: no plebeian could take the auspices, and the decemvirs had banned intermarriage to keep the auspices from being vitiated by uncertain descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plebs heard in that answer the claim that they were hateful to the gods, and the patricians gave way on the marriage law alone, expecting the tribunes to drop the consulship or postpone it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They did neither, and the settlement, reached in private councils that Dionysius says Valerius and Horatius were pointedly not invited to, was to allow military tribunes with consular power to be elected instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> So the law that passed was the one about marriage, and the demand carried alongside it was answered by inventing a new office rather than by opening the old one.","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","law","history","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.4, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Matrimonium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 735-44, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Matrimonium.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 11.57, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/11C*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/11/Marble_sarcophagus_fragment-_marriage_scene_MET_DP276799.jpg","title":"A Roman marriage on a sarcophagus, early 3rd century CE","desc":"The man and the woman join right hands at the centre, the gesture that stands for a lawful Roman marriage, with a small Cupid below and a third figure behind them. It is some six centuries younger than the law, so it shows what conubium was worth to Romans who had long had it rather than the moment it was extended across the orders.","credit":"Marble sarcophagus fragment: marriage scene, Roman, early 3rd century CE; The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_sarcophagus_fragment-_marriage_scene_MET_DP276799.jpg","alt":"A worn marble relief panel, taller than it is wide. A man in a heavy toga stands at the left and a veiled woman at the right, their inner arms reaching towards each other so that their right hands meet at the centre, though the hands themselves are broken away and both faces are lost. A small winged child stands between them at knee height, and a fourth draped figure is half visible behind their joined arms."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did a ban on intermarriage matter so much?","a":"Because conubium is the set of conditions making a marriage lawful, chiefly that children follow their father’s condition, so without it their standing and inheritance were uncertain."},{"q":"Why did Canuleius insist the prohibition was recent?","a":"Because the decemvirs had made it within the last few years, so it could not be defended as ancestral custom — and Rome had granted intermarriage to neighbours and foreigners."},{"q":"Why did the patricians’ answer on the consulship give the case away?","a":"Because they said no plebeian could take the auspices and that the ban existed to keep the auspices from being vitiated by uncertain descent, which the plebs heard as being called hateful to the gods."}]},
{"id":"rm-116","num":116,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Rome elected <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead of consuls in most years from 444 to 367 BCE, an office open to plebeians from the start whose first college was chosen wholly from patricians.","questions":["The first three <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resigned after three months in 444 BCE, the augurs having ruled that the man who presided over their election took the auspices from the wrong place.","The censorship was created beside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the powers of the consulship being split between two new magistracies rather than shared with the plebeians."],"answer":"consular tribunes","answerText":"consular tribunes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First elected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">444 BCE, 3 patricians</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First plebeian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Publius Licinius Calvus, 1 of 6</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abolished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">367 BCE, by the Licinian law</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Consular tribunes</b>, properly military tribunes with consular power, were the magistrates Rome elected in place of consuls in most years between 444 and 367 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They were conceded after the tribunes of the plebs pressed for the consulship to be open to either order, and unlike the consulship this office was open to plebeians from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy records a second explanation out of his own sources, that some writers leave the plebeian question out altogether and say three commanders were needed because two consuls could not manage Veii, the Aequi and Volscians and the revolt of Ardea at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first three, in 444 BCE, were all patricians, and they resigned after three months on the augurs’ ruling that the man who presided over their election had not taken the auspices from the right place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thereafter the senate proposed and the people decided, year by year, whether to elect consuls or tribunes, so the two offices alternate through the lists for the rest of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Their number was three or four at first and was raised to six in 405 BCE, and where the censors are counted with them the sources sometimes name eight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The censorship was created in the same years and by the same manoeuvre, the powers of the consulship being divided between two new magistracies rather than shared with the plebs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is the measure of the concession: the office was open to plebeians from 444 BCE and none held it for the whole of the Veientine war, until the tribunes secured the election of Publius Licinius Calvus with five patrician colleagues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy’s own text shows how uncertain the record is, one chapter opening on whether that year had tribunes or consuls instead, a question he cannot settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The office was abolished in 367 BCE by the Licinian law, which restored the consulship, 77 years after the demand that had produced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["title","institution","history","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Leonhard Schmitz, \"Tribunus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 1148-52, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Tribunus.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016_%282%29.jpg","title":"The Fasti Consulares in the Capitoline Museums","desc":"Rome's official list of its annual magistrates, cut on marble and set up in the Forum under Augustus, is where the years of the consular tribunes are recorded. The stretch shown here is a later one, of consuls; what the picture is for is the state of the record itself, broken and pieced together four centuries after the office was abolished, which is why Livy can open a chapter unable to say whether a given year had tribunes or consuls.","credit":"Jose Luiz Bernardes Ribeiro, Fasti Consulares, Sala della Lupa, Palazzo dei Conservatori, Musei Capitolini, Rome; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fasti_Consularii_%28detail%29_-_Sala_della_Lupa_-_Palazzo_dei_Conservatori_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016_%282%29.jpg","alt":"A large marble slab densely inscribed with columns of Latin names in capital letters, the strokes picked out in faded red. The stone is cracked across in several places and the gaps between fragments are filled with plain plaster, so that whole lines break off and resume further along."},"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was a new office invented instead of the old one opened?","a":"Because the patricians would not concede the consulship, so a magistracy with consular power was created that plebeians could hold from the start."},{"q":"Why does Livy give a second explanation for the office?","a":"Because some of his sources leave the plebeian question out and say three commanders were needed for Veii, the Aequi and Volscians and the revolt of Ardea at once."},{"q":"Why is the concession smaller than it looks?","a":"Because no plebeian held it for the whole of the Veientine war, and the censorship was created in the same years by dividing the consulship’s powers rather than sharing them."}]},
{"id":"rm-117","num":117,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Two patrician magistrates took over the census in 443 BCE, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew from counting citizens into a control over conduct answerable to nobody else in the state.","questions":["A dictator cut the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from five years to eighteen months in 433 BCE, and its holders answered by striking him from his tribe and taking away his vote.","The <i>nota censoria</i> was the instrument of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, valid only if both holders agreed and liftable by their successors or by a law, so it was never a judgment."],"answer":"censorship","answerText":"censorship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">443 BCE, 2 magistrates</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Term cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">433 BCE, 5 years to 18 months</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">421 years, to 22 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>censorship</b> was created in 443 BCE, when two magistrates took over the census that the consuls had held since the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The reason was the concession of the year before: because consular tribunes might be plebeians, the patricians took the census off the consulship and gave it to two censors who had to be patrician.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy says the senate welcomed the plan partly because it added another patrician magistracy, and that he thinks they foresaw its holders enlarging it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their reach grew far past counting heads: the register of citizens and their property, the roll of the senate and the equestrian order, the letting of the state’s revenues, and the <i>regimen morum</i>, a control over conduct answerable to nobody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its instrument was the <i>nota censoria</i>, which could be set against a man already tried and punished, counted as <i>ignominia</i> rather than <i>infamia</i>, could be lifted by the next censors or by a law, and was invalid unless both agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The office was unique in its length, running a whole <i>lustrum</i> of five years, until the dictator Mamercus Aemilius cut it to eighteen months in 433 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy says he did it having been left no war to fight, telling the assembly that liberty is safest when great powers are held briefly, then laid down his own dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The censors answered by striking him out of his tribe, raising his assessment eightfold and taking away his vote, which shows the power he had curbed better than any definition of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its patrician monopoly outlasted the consulship’s: Gaius Marcius Rutilus stood in 351 BCE with both consuls declaring they would allow no vote for him, a Publilian law of 339 BCE then required one censor to be plebeian, and no plebeian closed a <i>lustrum</i> before 280 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The office ran for 421 years, from 443 to 22 BCE, though many a <i>lustrum</i> went by with no censor chosen at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["title","institution","history","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.24, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D24. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.22, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Censor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 260-66, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Censor.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg","title":"The census frieze, late 2nd century BCE","desc":"A clerk sits at the left with a hinged wooden tablet on his knee, writing down the citizen who stands before him; an official sits at the centre and a soldier pulls on his helmet at the right. The panel comes from the Campus Martius, where the census was taken, and belongs to the monument conventionally called the Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, though what the building actually was is not settled.","credit":"Jastrow, 2007. Census frieze from the so-called Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, Musee du Louvre; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_Louvre_n1.jpg","alt":"A carved marble relief panel. At the left a seated man in a tunic writes on a hinged wooden tablet resting on his knee; a togate man stands over him with one arm extended towards the tablet. In the middle a second official sits on a low seat, and a third togate figure stands behind him with a hand on his shoulder. At the right a soldier in a mail shirt reaches up to settle a crested helmet on his head."},"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the census taken off the consulship?","a":"Because consular tribunes might be plebeians, so the patricians moved the census to two censors who had to be patrician — which added another patrician magistracy at the same time."},{"q":"Why was the censors’ mark so powerful a weapon?","a":"Because the regimen morum was a control over conduct answerable to nobody, and the nota could be set against a man already tried and punished."},{"q":"Why does the quarrel with Mamercus Aemilius show what he had curbed?","a":"Because after he cut the office from five years to eighteen months the censors struck him out of his tribe, raised his assessment eightfold and took away his vote."}]},
{"id":"rm-118","num":118,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Opened to either order in 421 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried a seat in the senate with it, which is why the patricians fought so hard to keep it closed.","questions":["Varro derives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a verb meaning to seek out, one kind of holder seeking out public money and the other seeking out crimes.","No plebeian won the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until 409 BCE, when three of the four places went to plebeians who valued it for opening the path to consulships and triumphs."],"answer":"quaestorship","answerText":"quaestorship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Elected from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 BCE, per Tacitus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Opened to plebs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">421 BCE, 4 places</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First plebeians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">409 BCE, 3 of 4</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>quaestorship</b> was the lowest of Rome’s regular magistracies and the first of them the plebeians were allowed to hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name covered two offices at once, and Varro derives it from <i>quaero</i>, to seek out: quaestors who sought out public money and quaestors who sought out crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>quaestores parricidii</i> were two public accusers who brought capital charges before the assembly and then carried out the sentence, and they are the men who threw Spurius Cassius from the Tarpeian rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The treasury quaestors, who did not exist under the kings, made the payments out of the <i>aerarium</i> and took in the revenues, kept the accounts in the <i>tabulae publicae</i>, registered fines and debts, housed foreign envoys and paid for the state funerals the senate decreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They were the senate’s paymasters rather than its masters, and could not spend public money except as it directed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In 421 BCE the consuls proposed adding two quaestors to serve them in the field, and the tribunes of the plebs demanded half the places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The senate refused, then offered instead that the election be open to either order as the consular tribunate already was; when that bought nothing the proposal was dropped and the tribunes took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy and Tacitus disagree about which pair came first: Livy has two city quaestors doubled by two for the war, where Tacitus has the first elected pair attached to the war and two added afterwards for business at Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the patricians were defending is plain enough, since a man who had been quaestor had a seat in the senate unless the next censors struck him out, and at the first election under the new rule a tribune’s son and a tribune’s brother stood and lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No plebeian was elected until 409 BCE, when three of the four places went to plebeians, and Livy says they valued the office not for itself but for opening the path to consulships and triumphs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["title","institution","history","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.44, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D44. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.54, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D54. [Open access]","Leonhard Schmitz, \"Quaestor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 980-82, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Quaestor.html. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>The Annals</i> 11.22, trans. Alfred John Church and William Jackson Brodribb (London: Macmillan, 1876), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0078%3Abook%3D11%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/L._Calpurnius_Piso_Caesoninus_and_Q._Servilius_Caepio_Cr330-1b.jpg","title":"A denarius struck by two quaestors, about 100 BCE","desc":"The obverse carries the head of Saturn with the two moneyers' names and a Q for their office; on the reverse two quaestors sit on a bench between ears of grain, over the legend AD FRV EMV and EX S C, for buying grain and by decree of the senate. It is three centuries younger than the card's period, and it shows the office doing exactly what the sources describe: spending public money, and only on the senate's authority.","credit":"Johny SYSEL, denarius of L. Calpurnius Piso Caesoninus and Q. Servilius Caepio, Crawford 330/1b; CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L._Calpurnius_Piso_Caesoninus_and_Q._Servilius_Caepio_Cr330-1b.jpg","alt":"The two faces of a silver Roman coin side by side against black. At the left, a bearded head facing right with letters running round the rim and a single letter Q beneath the chin. At the right, two seated figures on a low bench, one holding out a small object, with an ear of grain standing upright on either side of them and two lines of capital letters struck below."},"locator":{"name":"Temple of Saturn","at":[12.4842,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did one name cover two unlike offices?","a":"Because the word comes from quaero, to seek out: quaestors who sought out public money and quaestors who sought out crimes, one paying the state’s bills and the other bringing capital charges."},{"q":"Why did the patricians fight over so junior a post?","a":"Because a man who had been quaestor had a seat in the senate unless the next censors struck him out, so the lowest magistracy was the door to the highest body."},{"q":"Why did the plebeians value it themselves?","a":"Because Livy says they valued the office not for itself but for opening the path to consulships and triumphs."}]},
{"id":"rm-119","num":119,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Servius graded the infantry by property and each man armed himself, but Livy’s other account has the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fight in maniples of hastati, principes and triarii once pay came in.","questions":["When the first two lines had failed, the third line of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rose from kneeling behind planted spears, which is why Romans said matters had come down to the triarii.","By Polybius’ day the hastati of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried no hasta and the principes stood second, so its vocabulary is a fossil of the phalanx that came before it."],"answer":"legion","answerText":"legion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legion size</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,000 foot in 492 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">4,200 foot and 300 horse in 346 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle lines</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3: hastati, principes, triarii</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The early Roman <b>legion</b> is known from two descriptions in Livy that do not describe the same army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first grades the infantry by property: the first class in helmet, round shield, greaves and mail with spear and sword, the second the same without the mail, the third without greaves, the fourth with spear and javelin only, and the fifth with slings and stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only men enrolled in those classes could serve and the poorest were exempt, because each man armed himself at his own expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is what the senate changed when it decreed pay out of the treasury, before coined money existed, so contributions went in as copper carried by weight in wagons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy’s second description, set in 340 BCE, says the Romans had used the round <i>clipeus</i> and took up the oblong <i>scutum</i> once the soldiers were paid, and gave up the Macedonian sort of phalanx for maniples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He sets out fifteen maniples of <i>hastati</i> in front, fifteen of <i>principes</i> behind them, and behind the standards fifteen more divided into <i>triarii</i>, <i>rorarii</i> and <i>accensi</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> If the hastati could not break the enemy they fell back through the gaps between the principes, and if those failed too they passed through to the triarii, who had knelt with shields over their shoulders and spears planted like a palisade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The triarii then closed ranks and went in as the army’s last hope, which is the origin of the saying that matters have come down to the triarii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The words had already come loose from the things: by Polybius’ day the hastati carried no <i>hasta</i>, the pilani carried hastae rather than <i>pila</i>, and the principes stood second rather than first, which suggests the vocabulary was fixed while Rome fought as a phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The size is unsettled too, since Livy gives 5,000 men to each of four legions in 340 BCE where 4,000 is reported for 492 BCE and 4,200 foot with 300 horse for 346 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"tags":["institution","warfare","history","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 1.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.25, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D25. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Exercitus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 489-511, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Exercitus.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.59, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Situla_della_certosa%2C_600-550_ac._ca%2C_da_tomba_68_necropoli_della_certosa_04.JPG","title":"Warriors in file on the Certosa situla, about 600 to 550 BCE","desc":"A column of spearmen in crested helmets, each behind a large round shield, worked in bronze on a bucket from an Etruscan grave at Bologna. It is neither Roman nor from the Republic, and that is why it is here: this is the kind of army Livy says Rome fought as before the maniple, and the shield is the clipeus he says the scutum replaced.","credit":"Sailko, Situla della Certosa, Museo Civico Archeologico di Bologna; CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Situla_della_certosa,_600-550_ac._ca,_da_tomba_68_necropoli_della_certosa_04.JPG","alt":"A close view of a bronze bucket, green with corrosion, carrying bands of hammered figures. The upper band shows men walking one behind another to the right, each in a tall crested helmet and carrying a long spear over the shoulder, with a large round shield covering the body from shoulder to knee. A lower band shows smaller figures on foot carrying objects."},"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why could only the propertied classes serve?","a":"Because each man armed himself at his own expense, which is exactly what changed when the senate decreed pay out of the treasury."},{"q":"Why does the manipular line fall back through itself?","a":"Because if the hastati could not break the enemy they retired through the gaps between the principes, and if those failed too they passed through to the triarii kneeling behind."},{"q":"Why do the names suggest the vocabulary outlived the formation?","a":"Because by Polybius’ day the hastati carried no hasta, the pilani carried hastae rather than pila, and the principes stood second rather than first."}]},
{"id":"rm-120","num":120,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Livy has the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run ten summers and winters and end through a mine driven into the citadel rather than by any assault on the walls.","questions":["Roman soldiers built huts to winter through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the tribunes said that was the real reason pay had been settled, a gift tainted with poison.","Camillus vowed a tenth of the spoils to Apollo and asked Juno to leave the city during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then sold the survivors he had spared into slavery."],"answer":"siege of Veii","answerText":"siege of Veii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 summers and winters</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Veii fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE, taken by a mine</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spoils</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a 10th vowed to Apollo</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>siege of Veii</b> is the war that Roman tradition made the turning point of the early Republic, and Livy has it last ten summers and winters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Etruscan cantons had refused the Veientines help while they kept a king, who had offended them by pulling his own slaves out of the games after losing a priesthood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Roman commanders trusted a blockade rather than an assault and built huts to winter in, which Livy calls a novelty to the Roman soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribunes answered that this was the real reason pay had been settled, a gift from their adversaries tainted with poison, since men kept in the field all year could not be at home to press for reform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marcus Furius Camillus was made dictator in the tenth year, executed the men who had fled the camp in a panic, and raised a fresh army nobody tried to avoid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> He vowed a tenth of the spoils to Pythian Apollo and called on Juno, who then dwelt in Veii, to follow the Romans to a city that would soon be hers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He then attacked on every side to draw attention from a mine driven under the walls, which put picked men inside the temple of Juno in the citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Both Livy and Plutarch report the tale that the Veientine soothsayer said victory would go to whoever completed the sacrifice, and that the men in the mine heard him and seized the entrails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Livy declines to vouch for it, writing that such things are fitter to adorn a stage than to inspire belief; the harder fact he records is that the unarmed were spared and sold as slaves next day, their price the only part of the takings reaching the treasury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the goddess the two part again: Plutarch has her image answer that she was willing, and says Livy gives that answer to bystanders, where Livy records only that she moved by appliances of little power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","religion","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.19, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.22, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Camillus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), 93-121, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Tempio_di_veio.JPG","title":"The Portonaccio sanctuary at Veii","desc":"Tufa foundations in the grass, with a modern steel and concrete frame standing over one corner to show where the roofline ran; nothing above the blocks is ancient. This is the sanctuary outside the walls whose roof carried the terracotta Apollo, not the temple of Juno in the citadel that Livy has the mine come up in, and it is most of what is left of the city Rome spent ten years taking.","credit":"Livioandronico2013, Portonaccio temple, Veii; CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tempio_di_veio.JPG","alt":"An archaeological site in bright sunshine. Rough blocks of weathered tufa lie in courses across the foreground, marking out a rectangular plan in short grass. Behind them a tall open framework of thin steel mesh rises to carry a modern pale concrete cornice and a row of rounded eaves tiles, sketching the line of a vanished roof against the sky, with wooded slopes beyond."},"locator":{"name":"Veii","at":[12.3897,42.0233],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did wintering in huts become a political grievance?","a":"Because the tribunes said it was the real reason pay had been settled, a gift tainted with poison, since men kept in the field all year could not be at home to press for reform."},{"q":"Why did Veii face Rome without Etruscan help?","a":"Because the cantons refused help while it kept a king, who had offended them by pulling his own slaves out of the games after losing a priesthood."},{"q":"Why does Livy decline to vouch for the soothsayer’s tale?","a":"Because he writes that such things are fitter to adorn a stage than to inspire belief, and records instead that the unarmed were sold next day."}]},
{"id":"rm-121","num":121,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Impeached over the spoils of Veii and fined in his absence, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went into exile praying that his ungrateful citizens would soon feel the need of him.","questions":["A schoolmaster walked his pupils out of Falerii and offered them as hostages, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had him whipped back through the gates by the boys themselves.","Breaking off the weighing of the Gallic ransom, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told his men to win their country back by steel and not by gold, and was hailed as the second founder."],"answer":"Marcus Furius Camillus","answerText":"Marcus Furius Camillus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Took Veii</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE, as dictator</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15,000 asses, in his absence</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hailed as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 titles, by his soldiers</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Marcus Furius Camillus</b> is the man Roman tradition made the second founder of the city, and Livy gives him that title in so many words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was dictator in the tenth year at Veii, took the place, vowed a tenth of the spoils to Apollo and carried Juno away to the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says his triumph was drawn by four white horses, which no commander had done before or afterwards since such a car was held sacred to the king of the gods, and that this began the citizens’ dislike of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Falerii a schoolmaster walked his pupils into the Roman camp and offered them as hostages, and Camillus answered that there are rights of war as of peace, and that Rome had learnt to fight with justice as well as courage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had the man stripped and bound and gave the boys rods to scourge him back through their own gates, at which the Faliscans surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The tithe he had vowed at Veii undid him, since a tribune impeached him over the spoils and his own tribesmen said they would pay whatever fine was set but could not acquit him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He went into exile praying that if he suffered wrongfully his ungrateful citizens would soon feel the need of him, and was condemned in his absence to a fine of 15,000 asses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy sets that prayer beside a plebeian’s report of a voice heard in the night announcing that the Gauls were coming, which nobody acted on because the man was of humble rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recalled as dictator, Camillus broke off the weighing of the ransom, told the Gauls that no compact made without him was valid, and ordered his men to win their country back by steel and not by gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His soldiers then called him a Romulus, the father of his country and the second founder of the city, and he followed it by talking the plebs out of abandoning Rome for Veii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.22, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.27, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D27. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.49, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D49. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Camillus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), 93-121, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Domenico_Corvi_-_Camillus_and_the_Schoolmaster_of_Falerii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Camillus and the schoolmaster of Falerii, painted about 1765","desc":"Domenico Corvi, working after a composition by Poussin, puts the schoolmaster stripped and bound at the left with his own pupils driving him off, and Camillus at the right in cuirass and cloak. The eagle over the tent is 18th-century furniture rather than anything a Roman army of this date carried, and the scene is the story Rome told about itself rather than a report of what happened at Falerii.","credit":"Domenico Corvi, after Nicolas Poussin, Camillus and the Schoolmaster of Falerii, about 1764-66; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domenico_Corvi_-_Camillus_and_the_Schoolmaster_of_Falerii_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","alt":"An oil painting in a plain wooden frame. At the left a bald bearded man, stripped to a cloth about his waist with his hands tied behind him, stumbles forward while five small boys crowd round striking at him with switches. At the right a bearded commander in a decorated cuirass and a red cloak stands with one arm out, beside a dark campaign tent topped by a gilded eagle and a pale horse."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did a triumph turn the citizens against him?","a":"Because it was drawn by four white horses, which no commander had done before or since, such a car being held sacred to the king of the gods."},{"q":"Why did he send the Faliscan schoolmaster back scourged?","a":"Because he held that there are rights of war as of peace and that Rome had learnt to fight with justice as well as courage, so the children were not spoils to accept."},{"q":"Why could he refuse to honour the ransom the Gauls had agreed?","a":"Because as dictator he told them no compact made without him was valid, and ordered his men to win their country back by steel and not by gold."}]},
{"id":"rm-122","num":122,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Because copper was weighed rather than counted before Rome coined silver, the soldiers’ <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes its name from the verb for weighing out.","questions":["Senators carried their own copper to the treasury in wagons to fund the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the tribunes asked where else the money could come from but a tax on the people.","Livy says nobody had asked for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though sixteen years earlier he has candidates promising soldiers’ pay out of a tax on the occupiers of public land."],"answer":"stipendium","answerText":"stipendium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pay decreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">405 BCE, at the taking of Anxur</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">each man had served at his own cost</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Knights paid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">403 BCE, for the first time</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>stipendium</b> is the pay the Roman state began giving its soldiers, and the moment Livy has the senate grant the plebs something before they had asked for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Until then every man served at his own expense, so a campaign cost a soldier his keep and whatever his farm lost while he was away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy puts the decree at the taking of Anxur and says the plebs crowded the senate house, took the senators by the hand and allowed that they were rightly called Fathers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The word records how the money moved, being from <i>stipem</i> and <i>pendo</i>, because before silver was coined at Rome copper was weighed rather than counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That is not a figure of speech: the senators paid first and carried their copper by weight to the treasury in wagons, where the city could watch them do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The tribunes of the plebs objected at once, asking where the money could come from except a tax on the people, and saying the senate was being generous at other men’s expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They added that veterans who had paid their own way would not let others serve on easier terms, and offered to protect anyone who refused the war tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The senate answered by arrangement rather than argument, since leading plebeians paid in after the senators, the crowd saw them applauded, and the tribunes’ protection was refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy’s own earlier narrative undercuts the claim that nobody had asked, for sixteen years before he has candidates for the consular tribuneship promising pay for the soldiers out of a tax on the occupiers of the public land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pay for the knights followed during the war with Veii, and once that war kept the army out through the winter the tribunes changed their objection, calling the pay a gift from their adversaries tainted with poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","institution","warfare","history","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.59, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D59. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 4.60, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D60. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Robert Whiston, \"Stipendium,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i>, ed. William Smith (London: John Murray, 1875), 1071-72, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Stipendium.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/MNR_medagliere_-_sala_A_monete_e_prezzi%2C_aes_rude%2C_aes_signatum_P1070853.jpg","title":"Aes rude and aes signatum, in the Palazzo Massimo","desc":"A steelyard balance hangs over a pan of rough bronze lumps, with cast bronze bars and a row of weights around it; the case's own label reads metal by weight. The bars are later than the decree the card is about, so this is not the money that paid the first stipendium, but it is the practice the word records, bronze reckoned on a balance rather than counted out.","credit":"Lalupa, aes rude and aes signatum, Museo Nazionale Romano, Palazzo Massimo alle Terme; CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MNR_medagliere_-_sala_A_monete_e_prezzi,_aes_rude,_aes_signatum_P1070853.jpg","alt":"A lit museum case. In the centre a bronze steelyard hangs from a hook, its chains supporting a shallow pan heaped with rough green lumps of metal. Around the pan lie flat cast bars, one showing a winged horse in relief and another an oval shield. Along a shelf behind stand rounded weights and two shaped like human heads, and a black label at the front reads AES RUDE, AES SIGNATUM."},"locator":{"name":"Temple of Saturn","at":[12.4842,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was serving at one’s own expense so heavy a burden?","a":"Because a campaign cost a soldier his keep and whatever his farm lost while he was away, so the poorest were exempt from the levy altogether."},{"q":"Why did the tribunes object to a grant in the plebs’ favour?","a":"Because they asked where the money could come from except a tax on the people, and said the senate was being generous at other men’s expense."},{"q":"Why does Livy’s own narrative undercut the story of a free gift?","a":"Because sixteen years earlier he has candidates for the consular tribuneship promising pay for the soldiers out of a tax on the occupiers of the public land."}]},
{"id":"rm-123","num":123,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Roman envoys who had joined a fight against the Gauls were made consular tribunes instead of surrendered, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed within the year.","questions":["No dictator was named and no camp was pitched before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Livy says none of the Romans were killed while actually fighting.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> more men crossed the Tiber to hostile Veii than took the road home, and nobody sent word of the defeat to Rome."],"answer":"battle of the Allia","answerText":"battle of the Allia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">390 BCE, by the usual dating</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 km from Rome, at the Tiber</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">where the Allia runs into it</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>battle of the Allia</b> is the defeat that let the Gauls into Rome, and both Livy and Plutarch make it a failure of Roman conduct before a failure of arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three Fabii sent as envoys to the Gauls at Clusium joined the fighting themselves, and one of them killed a Gallic chieftain and was recognised while stripping the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Gauls demanded their surrender; the senate thought the demand just but would not put that in a decree about men of such rank, and referred it to the people, who elected all three consular tribunes for the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No dictator was named, though one had been named for far smaller wars, and Plutarch says what most confounded the Romans was the number of their commanders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The armies met about 16 km (11 Roman miles) from Rome, where the Allia runs into the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The tribunes chose no ground for a camp, dug no entrenchments and formed up without waiting for favourable auspices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To avoid being outflanked they stretched the line until the centre could hardly hold, and set a reserve on a low hill at the right, which the Gaulish chieftain took for a trap and attacked first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest ran almost before they had seen the enemy, and Livy says none were killed while actually fighting, the dead being cut down from behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> More crossed the Tiber to Veii, a hostile city, than took the road home, many drowning under the weight of their armour, and nobody sent word to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The right wing reached the citadel without shutting the gates behind it, Plutarch says the day was afterwards counted among the unluckiest in the calendar, and Livy that the records which might have told the story better burned in the sack that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","event","warfare","history","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.37, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D37. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Camillus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), 93-121, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Evariste-Vital_Luminais_-_Gaulois_en_vue_de_Rome.jpg","title":"Gauls in sight of Rome, a 19th-century painting","desc":"Evariste-Vital Luminais paints the moment after the Allia, mounted warriors halting on a ridge while one points at the city on the plain. The horses and the heroic bearing are the painter's: Livy's Gauls come on quickly and on foot, and a French artist of this period is painting the ancestors his own country claimed as much as the army that beat Rome.","credit":"Evariste-Vital Luminais, Gaulois en vue de Rome, Musee des Beaux-Arts de Nancy; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evariste-Vital_Luminais_-_Gaulois_en_vue_de_Rome.jpg","alt":"A dark oil painting in a wide format. On the right, several bearded warriors on heavy horses crowd together on a green ridge under a heavy sky; one, bare-backed in a dark helmet, raises an arm to point away to the left, and the others carry long shields and spears. In the distance at the left, two more riders sit against a pale horizon with a low city spread along the plain beyond."},"locator":{"name":"Allia","at":[12.52,42.0175],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did an embassy start a war?","a":"Because the three Fabii sent as envoys joined the fighting at Clusium, one of them killing a Gallic chieftain and being recognised while stripping the body."},{"q":"Why did the people’s answer make the demand worse?","a":"Because the senate thought the surrender just, referred it to the people, and the people elected all three consular tribunes for the next year."},{"q":"Why is the defeat read as a failure of conduct?","a":"Because the tribunes chose no ground for a camp, dug no entrenchments and formed up without waiting for favourable auspices, and Livy says none were killed while actually fighting."}]},
{"id":"rm-124","num":124,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Old men in ivory chairs waited in the streets during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Livy and Plutarch cannot agree on whether the city was levelled or only partly burnt.","questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Vestals buried what they could not carry in jars, and a plebeian took his own family off his waggon to give it to them.","The Gauls entered through an open gate during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, met nobody, and hesitated more at the patricians’ open halls than at the barricaded plebeian houses."],"answer":"Gallic sack of Rome","answerText":"Gallic sack of Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">390 BCE, by the usual dating</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gauls entered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 days after the Allia, per Plutarch</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the Capitol alone was held</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Gallic sack of Rome</b> is the one occasion in the early Republic when the city itself was taken, and the two fullest accounts disagree about how much was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The men of fighting age held the Citadel and Capitol, while a crowd of plebeians for whom there was neither room nor food went out over the Janiculum and scattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Vestals buried what they could not carry in jars and took the rest away on foot, until a plebeian, Lucius Albinius, put his own family and goods off his waggon and gave it to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those who had held curule office would not leave, and sat in their ivory chairs in the robes they wore driving the chariots of the gods, having devoted themselves to death, which Plutarch states outright and Livy only on the authority of some writers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Gauls came in through the Colline gate, which stood open, met nobody, and hesitated more at the patricians’ open halls than at the barricaded houses of the plebs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They stood before the seated men as if before statues until one stroked Marcus Papirius’ beard and was struck with an ivory staff, after which the old men were killed in their chairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy then says the fires were far from as indiscriminate or as extensive as might be expected on the first day of a captured city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He offers three reasons and settles on none: that not all the Gauls wanted it destroyed, that a few fires were meant to frighten the Capitol into surrender, and that what stood was worth keeping as a pledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch allows no such qualification, having them plunder for many days and then burn the houses down and level them with the ground in anger at the Capitol’s defenders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch adds that no accurate chronology of the period survives because of the confusion this disaster caused, and Livy that the records nearly all perished in it, which is why he calls everything before it obscure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","warfare","history","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D40. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Camillus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), 93-121, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html. [Open access]"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the old magistrates stay in their chairs?","a":"Because those who had held curule office had devoted themselves to death, and sat in their ivory chairs in the robes they wore driving the chariots of the gods."},{"q":"Why do the two accounts disagree about the burning?","a":"Because Livy says the fires were far less extensive than might be expected and offers three reasons without settling on one, where Plutarch has the houses burnt and levelled in anger."},{"q":"Why is everything before this date called obscure?","a":"Because the records nearly all perished in the sack, and Plutarch adds that no accurate chronology of the period survives because of the confusion it caused."}]},
{"id":"rm-125","num":125,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Told that the weights were false, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put his sword on the scale and answered with two words Livy calls intolerable to Roman ears.","questions":["Quintus Sulpicius agreed with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a thousand Roman pounds of gold to end the siege, a ransom Livy says was never in fact paid over.","Polybius has no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, only Gauls called home by an attack on their own country who made a treaty and left with their spoil."],"answer":"Brennus","answerText":"Brennus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ransom agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 Roman pounds of gold</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 months, per Plutarch and Polybius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">390 BCE, by the usual dating</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Brennus</b> is the name Roman tradition gives the Gaulish chieftain who took Rome, and whether he ever bargained for it at all depends on which account is read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Livy famine decided the siege, and the Gauls in their camp on low burnt ground suffered worse, dying so fast that they burned their dead in heaps at a spot afterwards called the Busta Gallica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During a truce the besieged threw bread down among the Gaulish pickets to show they were not starving, and then could neither hide the hunger nor bear it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The consular tribune Quintus Sulpicius agreed with Brennus on a thousand Roman pounds of gold, the ransom of a people Livy says was destined before long to rule the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Gauls used false weights, and when Sulpicius protested Brennus put his sword on the scale, belt and all and with a mocking laugh in Plutarch, and gave the answer Livy calls intolerable to Roman ears: woe to the vanquished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Livy and Plutarch then have Camillus arrive, refuse the compact and drive the Gauls off, so that the gold is never paid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius, writing earlier and from outside the Roman tradition, has no Brennus, no false weights and no rescue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his account the Gauls took all of Rome except the Capitol three days after the battle, were called away by an invasion of their own country by the Veneti, made a treaty and went home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also reports what Gauls were saying about it a century and a half later, when their descendants were being urged to attack Rome again: that their ancestors had taken the city, held it seven months, given it up of their own free will as an act of grace, and gone home with their spoil unbroken and unscathed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch’s seventh month of the siege agrees with that figure, which leaves the ransom, the sword and the rescue resting on the Roman account alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.48, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D48. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.18, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.22, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.49, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D49. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Camillus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), 93-121, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Brennus_and_Sulpicius_%28illustration%29.jpg","title":"Brennus at the weighing, an engraving of 1886","desc":"Paul Lehugeur draws the moment the card turns on: the balance on its tripod, the gold in the pans, the Gaul laying his sword across the beam and the togate Romans facing him. Two things to notice. The horned helmet is the 19th century's idea of a Gaul rather than anything excavated, and the book it comes from is a history of France, so Brennus is being drawn as an ancestor; and this is a scene only the Roman sources have, since in Polybius the Gauls simply make a treaty and go home.","credit":"Paul Lehugeur, Histoire de France en cent tableaux, Paris, 1886; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brennus_and_Sulpicius_%28illustration%29.jpg","alt":"A detailed black and white wood engraving. At the centre a large balance hangs from a wooden tripod topped by a carved bearded head, with heaped gold in one pan and stacked weights in the other. A warrior in a horned helmet, mail and a neck ring stretches his sword out across the beam from the left, among spearmen and seated figures; on the right several men in togas watch, one gesturing. Behind them stand broken and burnt city walls."},"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4852,41.8922],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why does it matter that Polybius wrote from outside the Roman tradition?","a":"Because he has no Brennus, no false weights and no rescue, only Gauls called home by an invasion of their own country who made a treaty and went."},{"q":"Why did the besieged throw bread down among the pickets?","a":"Because it was meant to show they were not starving, and then they could neither hide the hunger nor bear it."},{"q":"Why do the Gauls’ own descendants matter to the argument?","a":"Because a century and a half later they were saying their ancestors held Rome seven months and gave it up as an act of grace, going home unbroken and unscathed."}]},
{"id":"rm-126","num":126,"category":"The early Republic","question":"Sentries slept and dogs stayed quiet as the Gauls climbed, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made noise enough to rouse Marcus Manlius and save the citadel.","questions":["Livy says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were spared in the famine because they were sacred to Juno, where Plutarch says they were neglected and it was hunger that kept them awake.","Long afterwards dogs were still crucified yearly on a gibbet of elder in Rome for having failed on the night the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not."],"answer":"geese of the Capitol","answerText":"geese of the Capitol","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">390 BCE, by the usual dating</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roused</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 man, Marcus Manlius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">later thrown from the same rock</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>geese of the Capitol</b> are the birds Roman tradition credits with saving the citadel from a night attack during the Gallic siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Gauls had found an easier way up the cliff by the temple of Carmentis, Livy thinks from the footprints of a messenger who had come from Veii.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They sent an unarmed man ahead to try the path, then handed their weapons up to one another and hauled each other over the difficult ground, so quietly that the sentries never woke and neither did the dogs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says the geese were sacred to Juno and had been left untouched in spite of the famine, and that their clamour and the beating of their wings roused Marcus Manlius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch tells it the other way about: the birds had been neglected and were in a bad way, and it was their hunger that kept them wakeful and restless enough to notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Manlius knocked the first Gaul off the summit with the boss of his shield so that he fell on the men behind him, and the rest were dislodged with stones and javelins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At daybreak every soldier carried him half a pound of meal and a quarter of a pint of wine, which Livy says sounds little but was an overwhelming proof of affection when each man was stinting himself to give it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sentries were called out next, and since the soldiers agreed in blaming one man he was flung from the cliff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Manlius was condemned himself some years afterwards, and the tribunes moved his trial to a grove from which the Capitol could not be seen, because while it was in sight the people would not convict him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They then threw him from the same rock, and the birds kept their credit long after: Pliny records that dogs were still crucified yearly on a gibbet of elder between two temples in Rome for having failed on that night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","animal","history","religion","italy"],"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.47, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.20, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Camillus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), 93-121, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 29.14, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D29%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.48, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D48. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/MotteGeese.jpg","title":"Juno's sacred geese, painted in 1889","desc":"Henri-Paul Motte paints the birds in their keeping rather than the night alarm: an attendant stooping to quiet a goose that has spread its wings, and the rest in marble pens behind. The green marble, the gilt mouldings and the attendant are the painter's; and his geese are plump and well kept, where Livy's had been left alive through a famine and Plutarch's were starving, which in his telling is why they were awake at all.","credit":"Henri-Paul Motte, 1889; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MotteGeese.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of a marble temple interior. A young woman in a pale green tunic and red laced shoes bends forward with her hands on her knee towards a white goose that has stopped in front of her, wings half spread and beak open. Behind her runs a low green marble enclosure with gilded mouldings and barred openings, more white geese resting on straw inside it, and at the right a gilt lion's head spouts water into a basin."},"locator":{"name":"Capitoline Hill","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the sentries and the dogs fail where the birds did not?","a":"Because the Gauls sent an unarmed man ahead, handed their weapons up to one another and hauled each other over the difficult ground so quietly that nothing woke."},{"q":"Why do the two accounts explain the birds’ wakefulness differently?","a":"Because Livy has them sacred to Juno and spared in spite of the famine, where Plutarch has them neglected and hungry, which is what kept them restless."},{"q":"Why was Manlius tried out of sight of the Capitol?","a":"Because while it was in sight the people would not convict him, so the tribunes moved his trial to a grove from which it could not be seen."}]},
{"id":"rm-127","num":127,"category":"Rome","question":"When the Gauls had gone the senate ordered a shrine in the Nova Via to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, expiating a nocturnal voice that had warned of their coming and been ignored.","questions":["Cicero's sceptic asks why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spoke before anybody knew his name and then fell silent once he had been given a seat, an altar and a name of his own.","Marcus Caedicius, a plebeian, reported a night voice that the magistrates ignored because of his rank, and after the sack the Romans raised a shrine to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in atonement."],"answer":"Aius Locutius","answerText":"Aius Locutius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Voice heard</span><span class=\"dt-v\">shortly before 390 BCE, in the Nova Via</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shrine ordered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after the sack of 390 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">an altar within a sacred enclosure</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Aius Locutius</b>, also written Aius Loquens, is a Roman god named for a voice heard in the city shortly before the Gauls sacked it, and given a shrine on the Nova Via afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Livy puts it in the year before the disaster: a plebeian, Marcus Caedicius, told the tribunes he had heard in the night, in the Nova Via above the temple of Vesta, a voice louder than any human one saying that the Gauls were coming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No notice was taken, partly because of the informant's humble rank and partly because the Gauls were a distant and therefore unknown nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cicero gives the warning differently, as a voice from Vesta's grove below the Palatine ordering the walls and gates repaired or the city would be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The name is built from two verbs of speaking, <i>aio</i> and <i>loquor</i>, so the god is called after the act rather than named beforehand and credited with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> When the Gauls had gone, Camillus put religious business first: the senate ordered the captured temples restored and purified, thanked Caere for sheltering Rome's priests and treasures, founded Capitoline Games for Jupiter's protection of the citadel, and set a shrine in the Nova Via to expiate the neglected voice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy calls that building a temple; Cicero, three centuries later, calls it an altar still to be seen enclosed by a hedge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cicero also has a speaker ask why the god spoke before anybody knew who he was, and fell silent once he had a seat, an altar and a name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city around it went up in haste, with roof tiles at public expense and leave to quarry stone and cut timber freely on security of finishing within the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nobody laid out straight streets, so the old sewers, built under public ground, run beneath private houses, and Rome looked to Livy like a place settled at random rather than planned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.50, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D50. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>De Divinatione</i> 1.101, trans. William Armistead Falconer, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 20 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cicero/de_Divinatione/1*.html. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>De Divinatione</i> 2.69, trans. William Armistead Falconer, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 20 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cicero/de_Divinatione/2*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Aius Locutius,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology</i> (London: John Murray, 1873), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0104%3Aentry%3Daius-locutius-bio-1. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"tags":["deity","religion","mythology","history","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4864,41.8917],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Via_Nova_Roma.jpg/1280px-Via_Nova_Roma.jpg","title":"The Nova Via, looking west towards the Capitol","desc":"Livy and Cicero both put the voice and its shrine on this road, which runs along the foot of the Palatine above the house of the Vestals. The paving and the brick ruins along it are imperial and centuries later than the shrine, and nothing of the altar itself survives above ground; what the photograph shows is the line of the street.","credit":"Photograph by Jensens, released into the public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Via_Nova_Roma.jpg","alt":"A paved ancient road of large flat grey stones running away from the camera between iron railings. On the left rises a bank of ruined brick walls and arches with cypresses above them; on the right lower brick ruins, and beyond them in the distance the buildings and church towers of the Capitoline hill under a blue sky with white cloud."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the warning ignored?","a":"Because of the informant’s humble rank, and because the Gauls were a distant and therefore unknown nation."},{"q":"Why is the god named after an act of speaking?","a":"Because the name is built from aio and loquor, so it was given after the voice was heard rather than belonging to a god known beforehand."},{"q":"Why do Rome’s sewers run under private houses?","a":"Because the city was rebuilt in haste with leave to quarry and cut timber on security of finishing within the year, and nobody laid out straight streets."}]},
{"id":"rm-128","num":128,"category":"Rome","question":"Ten years of vetoed elections ended when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were carried, one of which required that one consul each year be elected from the plebs.","questions":["One clause of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> forbade anyone to hold more than 500 iugera of public land, and Livy records that Licinius Stolo was later fined for breaking it.","Camillus settled the quarrel over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by a bargain: the patricians gave up their monopoly of the consulship and the plebs accepted a patrician praetor to judge in the city."],"answer":"Licinio-Sextian laws","answerText":"Licinio-Sextian laws","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">367 BCE, by the usual dating</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sextius consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">366 BCE, the first plebeian</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">after ten tribunates and five years without elections</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Licinio-Sextian laws</b> are the three bills by which, in Livy's account, the tribunes Gaius Licinius Stolo and Lucius Sextius Lateranus opened the consulship to the plebs and limited debt and the holding of public land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first deducted interest already paid from the principal and spread the rest over three yearly instalments; the second capped holdings of public land at 500 iugera; the third abolished consular tribunes and required one consul from each order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The patricians answered as before, persuading other tribunes to veto, so that for weeks the assembly met and did nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sextius turned the weapon round: if the veto had such power the plebs would use it too, and no curule elections were held for five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has the pair ask how patricians could each occupy more than 500 iugera while a plebeian was allotted two, one man thus holding the land of nearly three hundred citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Appius Claudius answered on religious ground, that the city had been founded under auspices which belonged to the patricians and that a plebeian consul would profane them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bills were carried at the tenth tribunate of the two men, a fourth measure divided the ten keepers of the Sibylline Books evenly between the orders, and Plutarch has Camillus vow a temple to Concord as the quarrel ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sextius was elected the first plebeian consul, the patricians refused to confirm him, and Camillus settled it by a bargain: they conceded the consul, the plebs a praetor, patrician and charged with justice in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That praetorship is dated to 366 BCE, was kept in patrician hands as compensation for the loss, and was not held by a plebeian until 337.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Varro credits the land bill to a Stolo and the family name to the suckers grubbed from a vine's roots; Livy records Stolo himself fined for holding a thousand iugera with a son emancipated to evade his own law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.35, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Camillus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 2, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), 93-121, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Camillus*.html. [Open access]","Varro, <i>On Agriculture</i> 1.2.9, trans. William Davis Hooper and Harrison Boyd Ash, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1934), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Varro/de_Re_Rustica/1*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Praetor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dpraetor-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","law","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Temple-concorde-vestiges.jpg/1280px-Temple-concorde-vestiges.jpg","title":"The site of the Temple of Concord in the Forum","desc":"Plutarch has Camillus vow this temple while the quarrel was at its height, and the vote to build it followed the concession of a plebeian consul. What is on the ground is not his building: the standing temple was Tiberius' rebuilding, and what survives of it is the concrete core of the podium against the Tabularium wall, with a fallen cornice block and a column base left in front of it.","alt":"A grassy plot in the Roman Forum behind low metal railings. Behind it runs a high wall of concrete and brickwork with a carved marble cornice block and a column base standing against it at the left, a tall square brick tower rising above, and a modern pale building with a rounded end and umbrella pines on the skyline at the right.","credit":"Photograph by Cassius Ahenobarbus, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple-concorde-vestiges.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Sextius stop every curule election for five years?","a":"Because the patricians had persuaded other tribunes to veto his bills, so he turned the weapon round: if the veto had such power the plebs would use it too."},{"q":"Why was the land cap put as a comparison rather than a figure?","a":"Because a patrician might occupy more than 500 iugera while a plebeian was allotted two, so one man held the land of nearly three hundred citizens."},{"q":"Why is the praetorship part of the same settlement?","a":"Because the patricians conceded the consul and took a praetor in exchange, patrician and charged with justice in the city, as compensation for the loss."}]},
{"id":"rm-129","num":129,"category":"Rome","question":"A mutiny in Campania was followed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Livy reports as banning interest and allowing both consuls of a year to be plebeian.","questions":["Quintus Fabius Maximus had a clause of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> read aloud to stop his own re-election, since it barred any man from holding the same office twice within ten years.","Livy introduces the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as what he finds in other authorities, and says the old writers agree on nothing beyond the fact of a mutiny and its suppression."],"answer":"Lex Genucia","answerText":"Lex Genucia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">342 BCE, by the usual dating</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Re-election barred</span><span class=\"dt-v\">for 10 years in the same office</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and both consuls might be plebeian</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lex Genucia</b> is a group of measures Livy reports as passed after a mutiny of the Roman army in Campania, and which finished the opening of the consulship to the plebs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mutiny had been settled by the dictator Marcus Valerius with an amnesty for all who had taken part and a military law forbidding a soldier's name to be struck off the roll without his consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy lists what he finds in other authorities: a bill of the tribune Lucius Genucius making lending at interest illegal, rules against holding the same office twice within ten years or two offices in one year, and a provision that both consuls might lawfully be plebeian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That last clause matters because the earlier settlement had required one plebeian consul rather than permitted two, leaving the second place patrician in practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy is careful about all of it, saying the old writers agree in nothing but that there was a mutiny and that it was put down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Tacitus sets the usury clause in a sequence: the Twelve Tables capped the monthly rate, a tribune's bill halved it, lending at interest was banned outright, and plebiscites chased evasions that kept coming back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One evasion was simple: a ban bound citizens only, so loans were made in the names of Latins and allies until a law of 193 BCE stopped it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ten-year rule can be watched failing too: Quintus Fabius Maximus, pressed to stand for the consulship again, had it read aloud to stop them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The clamour drowned it, the tribunes offered to exempt him by a vote, and Fabius asked what was the object of making laws if the men who made them broke them, and said they were ruling the laws instead of the laws ruling them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was elected anyway, so two of the four rules Livy reports can be seen evaded or set aside by the assembly that passed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Tacitus, <i>The Annals</i> 6.16, trans. John Jackson, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1937), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/6A*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Fenus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dfenus-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","law","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Livy%2C_Vaticanus_Palatinus_lat._875.jpg/1280px-Livy%2C_Vaticanus_Palatinus_lat._875.jpg","title":"The first page of Livy, copied in the 15th century","desc":"Everything on this card comes from Livy, and this is the shape in which he survives: a manuscript written about 1,400 years after him, opening on a note about his life and then on the first words of his preface. The red rubric above the decorated initial names Victorianus as having corrected the text for the Symmachi, a line copied down the centuries along with the work.","credit":"Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Vaticanus Palatinus lat. 875, fol. 1r; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livy%2C_Vaticanus_Palatinus_lat._875.jpg","alt":"A page of a handwritten Latin manuscript on cream parchment. A block of small brown script at the top is followed by two lines of red capitals, then the main text beside a large decorated initial letter filled with white interlacing vine ornament on gold and blue, with a small painted figure below it and a circular Vatican Library stamp in the right margin."},"why":[{"q":"Why did permitting two plebeian consuls change anything?","a":"Because the earlier settlement had required one plebeian consul rather than permitted two, which left the second place patrician in practice."},{"q":"Why did banning interest not stop lending at interest?","a":"Because a ban bound citizens only, so loans were made in the names of Latins and allies until a law of 193 BCE stopped it."},{"q":"Why does Fabius’ election show the ten-year rule failing?","a":"Because he had the law read aloud to stop them, the clamour drowned it, and he asked what was the object of making laws if the men who made them broke them."}]},
{"id":"rm-130","num":130,"category":"Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the families whose ancestors had held a curule magistracy, and their outward mark was the right to keep ancestral wax masks in the hall.","questions":["Sallust says the people still disposed of the lesser offices while the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed the consulship from hand to hand, thinking no commoner anything but a disgrace to it.","At the funeral of one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, relatives wore the wax masks of his ancestors and sat in a row on ivory chairs while the speaker recounted their deeds."],"answer":"nobiles","answerText":"nobiles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Emerged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after the consulship opened, 366 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First new man</span><span class=\"dt-v\">L. Sextius, consul 366 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the masks of office-holding ancestors marked the class</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>nobiles</b> were the Roman families whose ancestors had held a curule magistracy, a class formed once the consulship was opened to plebeians, and one that carried no legal privilege at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name means the known, against the ignobiles who were not, and what held them together was a common interest in keeping the high offices among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their outward mark was the ius imaginum, a right resting on custom and no enactment: the wax masks of ancestors who had held office, kept in the hall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pliny says they stood each in a niche ready for the family's funerals, the pedigree drawn in lines across the painted faces and a room of records beside them stating what each man had done in office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Spoils taken from an enemy were fixed outside such a house and a buyer might not take them down, so the house went on triumphing after it changed hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius says what they were for: at the funeral of a distinguished man they were worn by relatives chosen for build and bearing, in togas bordered with purple for a consul or praetor, wholly purple for a censor, gold-worked for a triumph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They rode in chariots behind the fasces and other insignia of the offices each man had held, then sat in a row on ivory chairs while the speaker worked back through their deeds from the oldest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius thought no sight better fitted to make a young man willing to endure anything for a name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The class was also a wall: Sallust says the people still disposed of the lesser offices while the nobility passed the consulship from hand to hand, no commoner however distinguished being thought anything but a disgrace to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He gives Marius the reply: he cannot boast the statues, triumphs or consulships of ancestors, but can show spears, a banner, horse trappings and the scars on his breast, and these are his statues and his nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Nobiles, Nobilitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dnobiles-cn. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.53-54, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 35.2, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D35%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 63, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper and Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0126%3Achapter%3D63. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 85, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper and Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0126%3Achapter%3D85. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["people","institution","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Togato_Barberini.jpg/1280px-Togato_Barberini.jpg","title":"A Roman holding two ancestral portraits","desc":"The statue known as the Togatus Barberini, carved around three centuries after the class described here formed, is the nearest thing in stone to what Polybius and Pliny describe: a man in a toga carrying the portrait heads of two forebears, one in each hand. The head on the statue's own shoulders does not belong to it and was joined to the body later.","credit":"Photograph by Carlo Dell'Orto, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Togato_Barberini.jpg","alt":"A white marble statue of a standing man in a toga, gaunt and bald, looking upward. In each lowered hand he supports a separate portrait bust of an older man cut off at the chest, the left one resting on a stump carved with leaves."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a class with no legal privilege still a barrier?","a":"Because what held it together was a common interest in keeping the high offices among themselves, the people disposing of the lesser ones while the consulship passed from hand to hand."},{"q":"Why were the ancestral masks worn at a funeral?","a":"Because relatives chosen for build and bearing wore them in the robes of the offices each man had held, so the dead paraded their careers behind the fasces."},{"q":"Why does Marius answer with his scars?","a":"Because he cannot boast the statues, triumphs or consulships of ancestors, so he shows spears, a banner, horse trappings and his wounds and calls these his nobility."}]},
{"id":"rm-131","num":131,"category":"Rome","question":"Polybius says ten years of military service came before any office on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the ranked ladder of magistracies a Roman of the Republic climbed.","questions":["The greater offices of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were filled by the people voting in centuries and the lesser by the people voting in tribes, which followed who held imperium.","From the First Punic War the aediles paid for the public games themselves, which put a price on the first step of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and kept poorer men off it."],"answer":"cursus honorum","answerText":"cursus honorum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Service first</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 years, per Polybius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Praetorship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">added 366 BCE, patrician until 337 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">a consul had 12 lictors, a praetor 6</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>cursus honorum</b> is the ladder of elected offices a Roman of the Republic climbed, ranked so that each rung carried more authority than the one below it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius says nobody was eligible for any political office at all until he had completed ten years of military service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The offices were divided into greater and lesser: the greater held imperium, the power to command and to compel, while the lesser had potestas only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Which assembly chose a man followed that division, the greater magistrates being elected by the people voting in centuries and the lesser by the people voting in tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The censorship counted among the greater by the weight of what it did rather than by holding imperium, and below all of them lay the minor posts of the vigintivirate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The praetor was called the consuls' colleague and was elected at the same assembly under the same auspices, the consuls chosen first and the praetors after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was a curule magistrate with imperium and six lictors, where a consul had twelve, and his work was to give judgment in the city while the consuls were away with the armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The aedileship was the first step towards the higher offices, and from the First Punic War the cost of the public games fell on the aediles instead of the treasury, which put a price on that rung and kept poorer men off it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ranking decided who could stop whom as well, since a magistrate's act could be blocked only by an equal or a greater power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the ladder never listed is the thing Sallust says Marius lacked: he had every qualification for the consulship except antiquity of family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Magistratus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dmagistratus-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Praetor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dpraetor-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Nobiles, Nobilitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dnobiles-cn. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 63, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper and Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0126%3Achapter%3D63. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","concept","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Scipio-tomb.jpg","title":"A career cut on a sarcophagus","desc":"The sarcophagus of Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbatus, whose epitaph names his offices as consol censor aidilis, consul, censor and aedile, which is the reverse of the order he would have climbed them in. An old photograph, taken before the surface was cleaned, of a stone that is not the ladder's rule but a Roman's own record of having climbed it.","credit":"From an old classical encyclopedia; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scipio-tomb.jpg","alt":"A monochrome photograph of a large carved stone sarcophagus. Its lid ends in two Ionic volutes with a name cut across the top, below which runs a band of dentils and then a row of carved rosettes between fluted panels, and beneath that four lines of Latin capitals cut into the plain lower block, with a crack running down through them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did which assembly elected a man follow the office’s rank?","a":"Because the greater magistrates held imperium and were elected by the people voting in centuries, where the lesser held potestas only and were elected in the tribes."},{"q":"Why did the aedileship keep poorer men off the ladder?","a":"Because from the First Punic War the cost of the public games fell on the aediles rather than the treasury, which put a price on that rung."},{"q":"Why does the ranking decide who can stop whom?","a":"Because a magistrate’s act could be blocked only by an equal or a greater power, so the ladder settles obstruction as well as precedence."}]},
{"id":"rm-132","num":132,"category":"Rome","question":"Livy says the patricians created the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the curule aedileship in their own interest, as the price of conceding one consulship to the plebs.","questions":["The holder of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> posted the rules he meant to follow at the start of his year, which Cicero calls an annual law because it lasted only as long as his imperium.","Each man elected to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took over his predecessor's edict with what changes he thought fit, so Roman law grew by inheritance rather than by legislation."],"answer":"praetorship","answerText":"praetorship","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">366 BCE, with the curule aedileship</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First plebeian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">337 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the edict was posted yearly and inherited</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>praetorship</b> was created with the curule aedileship as the patricians' price for giving up the consulship, and Livy says they made both offices in their own interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first praetor was Spurius Furius, a son of Camillus, and the tribunes protested that three patrician magistrates in curule chairs and the bordered toga had been set against one plebeian consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their sharpest objection was that the praetor gave judgment as though he were a colleague of the consuls, elected under the same auspices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was a curule magistrate holding imperium, less than the consuls' and owing them obedience, and the office stayed in patrician hands until 337 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Unlike the consulship its numbers could be added to as circumstances required: a second praetor was made for suits involving foreigners, two more when Sicily and Sardinia had to be governed, and two more for the Spanish provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What made the office matter was the edict, posted on a whitened board at the start of the year, setting out the rules he meant to follow, and which Cicero calls an annual law because it stood only as long as his imperium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because the men who issued such edicts held the honores, the law they made was called the ius honorarium and set beside the older ius civile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each praetor took over his predecessor's edict with whatever additions and deletions he thought fit, the inherited part being the edictum tralaticium, so the body of it grew year by year while a bad innovation could be dropped by the next man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He judged from a curule chair set on the comitium and was allowed out of the city for no more than ten days together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 149 BCE a praetor also presided over the first standing criminal court, the one for extortion; the annual edicts stopped only when what they had built was fixed in a settled form under the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.42, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D42. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Praetor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dpraetor-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Edictum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dedictum-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Magistratus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dmagistratus-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["title","institution","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Relief_sella_curulis_Massimo.jpg/1280px-Relief_sella_curulis_Massimo.jpg","title":"An empty curule chair on a funerary relief","desc":"The seat itself, carved on a tomb of the 1st century BCE or CE from the Via Casilina outside Rome, where the chair stands empty for the office the dead man had held. A praetor gave judgment from one of these, set up on the comitium.","credit":"Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_sella_curulis_Massimo.jpg","alt":"A worn marble relief. In the centre stands an empty folding chair carved in low relief, its legs crossing in an X below a broad flat seat, with the draped lower bodies of standing figures at either side."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the tribunes object to the new office at all?","a":"Because three patrician magistrates in curule chairs had been set against one plebeian consul, and the praetor gave judgment as though he were a colleague of the consuls."},{"q":"Why could praetors be added where consuls could not?","a":"Because the number was not fixed by the office: a second was made for suits involving foreigners, two more for Sicily and Sardinia and two more for the Spanish provinces."},{"q":"Why did an annual edict build a lasting body of law?","a":"Because each praetor took over his predecessor’s with what additions and deletions he thought fit, so the inherited part grew while a bad innovation could be dropped by the next man."}]},
{"id":"rm-133","num":133,"category":"Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved the choosing of senators from the consuls to the censors, and told them to enrol the best man of every rank.","questions":["Almost everything known about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from one sentence in a Roman lexicographer, and even its date is deduced rather than recorded anywhere.","Appius Claudius made the first censors' roll of the senate under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the next year's consuls ignored it and read out the list as it stood before him."],"answer":"Lex Ovinia","answerText":"Lex Ovinia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 339 BCE, before 312 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First roll</span><span class=\"dt-v\">312 BCE, by Appius Claudius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">known from one sentence of Festus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lex Ovinia</b> is the law that took the choosing of senators away from the consuls and gave it to the censors, with an instruction to enrol the best man of every rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The right had belonged first to the kings and then to the consuls, who by the surviving account picked the men closest to themselves, patricians first and afterwards plebeians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under that practice being left out carried no disgrace, the roll being a magistrate's list of the men he wanted about him rather than a register anyone had a claim to be on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nothing of the law itself survives: a single sentence in a Roman lexicographer, saying that a tribunician Ovinian law laid down that the censors should choose the best of every order into the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even its date is deduced rather than recorded, later than the last consular tribunes and earlier than the first revision by censors anyone reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The usual argument places it after the law of 339 BCE that required one censor always to be a plebeian, on the ground that a tribune would not have handed the choice of the senate to two patricians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The men it empowered held no imperium and no lictors and ranked below the consuls and praetors, and were now to decide who sat in the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first such revision Livy records is that of Appius Claudius in 312 BCE, which brought him so much odium that his colleague resigned in shame and he held the censorship alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The consuls of the next year told the assembly that the vacancies had been filled out of caprice and for popularity, that men far better than some of those chosen had been passed over, and that the whole order had been disgraced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They then ignored the new roll altogether and read out the list of senators as it had stood before Appius was made censor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Senatus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dsenatus-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Censor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcensor-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","law","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Curia_Iulia_front.jpg/1280px-Curia_Iulia_front.jpg","title":"The senate house in the Forum","desc":"The building whose roll this law decided who was on. What stands today is a late rebuilding, more than three centuries after the law, raised on the site of the older curia where the senate of the Republic met; the brick face was stripped of its marble facing long ago.","credit":"Photograph by Jensens, released into the public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curia_Iulia_front.jpg","alt":"A tall plain brick building in the Roman Forum, seen head on, with a shallow pediment above a dentilled cornice, three large grilled windows high in the wall, and a single arched doorway with bronze doors at the top of a flight of steps."},"why":[{"q":"Why did being left off the roll once carry no disgrace?","a":"Because it was a magistrate’s list of the men he wanted about him rather than a register anyone had a claim to be on, the consuls picking those closest to themselves."},{"q":"Why is even the law’s date deduced rather than recorded?","a":"Because almost nothing survives but a single sentence in a lexicographer, so it is placed later than the last consular tribunes and earlier than the first revision by censors anyone reports."},{"q":"Why did the consuls of 311 read out the old list?","a":"Because they told the assembly that Appius had filled the vacancies out of caprice and for popularity and disgraced the whole order, so they ignored his roll altogether."}]},
{"id":"rm-134","num":134,"category":"Rome","question":"Livy makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turn on one beaten debtor, and says it left a man's goods rather than his body answerable for what he owed.","questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a defaulting debtor could be kept sixty days in chains, cried at three market days, and then put to death or sold beyond the Tiber.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not excuse the debt itself, since the man could still be kept at work with the value of his labour set against what he owed."],"answer":"Lex Poetelia","answerText":"Lex Poetelia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">326 BCE, by Livy's dating</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Before it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60 days in chains, then death or sale</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">bonds forbidden, the debt still worked off</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lex Poetelia</b> is the Roman law that ended nexum, the bond by which a free man pledged his own labour and his own body against a debt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was made in the old form of bronze and balance before witnesses, which is why a Roman spoke of being freed from it by copper and scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An unpaid creditor laid hands on the debtor before the magistrate and, unless somebody stood surety, could take him home for sixty days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His name and the sum owed were cried on three market days, his chains might weigh no more than fifteen pounds, and the creditor owed him a pound of meal a day if he could not keep himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the end he could be put to death or sold beyond the Tiber, his goods going to the men he owed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Livy shows it working a lifetime earlier, when Manlius saw a decorated centurion led off as an adjudged debtor, paid the sum in the Forum and freed him by copper and scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome had tried the problem many ways by then, lending from the treasury, cutting the legal rate and even abolishing debts, none of which touched the bond itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy dates the change to 326 BCE and makes it turn on one case, a young man pledged for his father's debt whose creditor had him stripped and beaten when he refused him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A crowd carried him to the senate house and showed the senators his back, and the consuls were told to put it to the people that no man be held in irons or the stocks unless he had earned a punishment, and that a debt be answered by his goods and not his body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it did not do was excuse the debt, since the man could still be kept at work with his labour set against what he owed and restored once it was paid; what went were the fetters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.28, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 6.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Nexum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dnexum-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Manus Injectio,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dmanus-injectio-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Fenus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dfenus-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","law","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Aesrude.jpg/1280px-Aesrude.jpg","title":"Aes rude, bronze weighed out as money","desc":"A lump of unworked bronze, shown from two sides. Before Rome struck coin, money of this kind was weighed rather than counted, which is what the bronze and the balance in the nexum ceremony are: the metal was put on the scales and the words were spoken over it, and the same form undid the bond as had made it.","credit":"Photograph by Chuy1530, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aesrude.jpg","alt":"Two photographs side by side of the same rough lump of ancient bronze, pitted and irregular, its dark grey surface mottled with rust-red and yellow-green corrosion, against a plain pale background."},"why":[{"q":"Why had earlier remedies not touched the problem?","a":"Because lending from the treasury, cutting the legal rate and even abolishing debts all left the bond itself standing, by which a free man pledged his own body."},{"q":"Why did one case carry the law?","a":"Because a young man pledged for his father’s debt was stripped and beaten for refusing his creditor, and a crowd carried him to the senate house and showed the senators his back."},{"q":"Why did the law not excuse the debt itself?","a":"Because a man could still be kept at work with his labour set against what he owed and restored once it was paid; what went were the fetters."}]},
{"id":"rm-135","num":135,"category":"Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 287 BCE made a vote of the plebs bind the whole Roman people, level with a law passed in the assembly of centuries.","questions":["Livy records two earlier laws to the same effect as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the same rule appears to have been enacted three times over.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the tribunes could deal with the senate directly, though they still had no lictors, no fasces and no curule chair."],"answer":"Lex Hortensia","answerText":"Lex Hortensia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">287 BCE, by the dictator Hortensius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">plebiscites made binding on the whole people</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lex Hortensia</b> is the law of 287 BCE that made a vote of the plebs bind the whole Roman people, putting a plebiscite on the same footing as a law passed in the assembly of centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A plebiscite was what the plebs decreed in answer to a question put by one of its own magistrates, and because a tribune could neither summon the patricians nor lay anything before them it was a scitum rather than a lex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plebs had claimed from the beginning the power to bind its own members, above all by the sacred laws that made its officers inviolable and laid down that whoever killed a man so protected should not be held a murderer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the Hortensian law dispensed with, on one account of it, was the requirement that the senate sanction such a vote before it could stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Until then the two kinds of measure had tended to divide by subject, laws dealing mostly with administration and the constitution and plebiscites with private law, and afterwards the word law was used of both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The difficulty is that Livy records two earlier enactments to exactly the same effect, one carried by the consuls Valerius and Horatius and one by the dictator Publilius Philo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the first Livy says it had been a doubtful point of law whether the patricians were bound by what the plebs ordained, and that settling it put a very effective weapon into the tribunes' hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Why the same rule needed passing three times is explained by no ancient writer, and what is offered instead is conjecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the law changed for the tribunes is plainer: they became magistrates of the whole community, with positive powers and the right to transact business directly with the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They kept none of the marks of one, having no lictors, no fasces, no purple border and no curule chair, and no right to take the auspices on the Roman people's behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Plebiscitum,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dplebiscitum-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Lex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dlex-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Tribunus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dtribunus-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 3.55, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D3%3Achapter%3D55. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.12, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","law","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was a plebiscite not already a law?","a":"Because a tribune could neither summon the patricians nor lay anything before them, so what the plebs decreed was a scitum binding its own members."},{"q":"Why is it puzzling that this law was needed at all?","a":"Because Livy records two earlier enactments to exactly the same effect, and no ancient writer explains why the same rule needed passing three times."},{"q":"Why did the tribunes remain magistrates of an odd kind?","a":"Because they became magistrates of the whole community with no lictors, no fasces, no purple border, no curule chair and no right to take the auspices for the Roman people."}]},
{"id":"rm-136","num":136,"category":"Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 300 BCE opened the colleges of pontiffs and augurs to plebeians, the last places in the Roman state still shut to them.","questions":["Livy could not discover how the augurs had come to number four before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since their college was supposed to hold a multiple of three.","Decius argued for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by asking whether the gods had heeded his father's devotion less than they would a patrician's prayers."],"answer":"Lex Ogulnia","answerText":"Lex Ogulnia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 BCE, by the tribunes Ogulnius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Colleges raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 8 pontiffs and 9 augurs</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">no plebeian pontifex maximus until 254 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lex Ogulnia</b> is the law of 300 BCE that opened the two great priestly colleges to plebeians, the last places in the Roman state still shut to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunes Quintus and Gnaeus Ogulnius aimed it not at the poor but at the leading men of the plebs, who had been consuls and had triumphed, and to whose distinctions nothing was lacking but a priesthood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pontiffs were priests of no particular god but a college standing above all other priests, with the regulation of the calendar and the whole of the public worship in their charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The augurs read the gods' will in birds, and since their approval was wanted before public business could go forward the auspices were a political instrument in patrician hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The bill added four pontiffs and five augurs, every one of them to be taken from the plebs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Livy stops to admit a difficulty in his own account: the augurs then numbered four, though their college was supposed to hold a multiple of three, one for each of the old tribes, and he cannot discover how it had fallen to that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appius Claudius spoke against the bill and Publius Decius for it, and Livy says the arguments were much those used over the consulship a lifetime earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Decius asked whether the gods had listened less to his own father, who devoted himself for the legions standing on a spear, than they would to a patrician, and answered that the plebeians had a nobility of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The law passed, the four pontiffs and the five augurs were named, and the colleges stood at eight and nine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The chief priesthood was another matter, and no plebeian was pontifex maximus until 254 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Pontifex,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dpontifex-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Augur,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Daugur-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","law","history","religion","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg/1280px-Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg","title":"A Roman public sacrifice","desc":"The worship the pontiffs superintended, carved about five centuries after this law: the celebrant with his head veiled by a fold of his toga, pouring at a portable altar before a temple, a boy holding the box of incense, a piper playing so that no ill-omened sound should be heard, and at the right the bull with the attendant who will kill it, his axe held against his shoulder.","credit":"Photograph by MatthiasKabel, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas_relief_from_Arch_of_Marcus_Aurelius_showing_sacrifice.jpg","alt":"A marble relief panel crowded with figures. On the left a bearded man in a toga drawn up over his head reaches towards a small tripod altar; behind and beside him stand other togate men, a boy with a casket and a piper; at the right a large bull faces the altar with a bare-chested attendant beside it holding an axe, and behind them all rise the columns and pediment of a temple."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the priesthoods worth a political fight?","a":"Because the augurs’ approval was wanted before public business could go forward, so the auspices were a political instrument in patrician hands."},{"q":"Why was the bill aimed at the leading men of the plebs?","a":"Because they had been consuls and had triumphed, and to their distinctions nothing was lacking but a priesthood."},{"q":"Why does Livy stop to admit a difficulty in his own account?","a":"Because the augurs then numbered four though their college was supposed to hold a multiple of three, one for each of the old tribes, and he cannot discover how it had fallen to that."}]},
{"id":"rm-137","num":137,"category":"Rome","question":"Livy says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first to put the sons of freedmen in the senate, and that his colleague resigned rather than share the censorship with him.","questions":["The Aemilian law allowed a censor eighteen months, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stayed on past them, arguing that no censor appointed after that law could be bound by it.","Old and blind, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had himself carried to the senate house to stop a peace with Pyrrhus, and said he now regretted only that he was not deaf as well."],"answer":"Appius Claudius Caecus","answerText":"Appius Claudius Caecus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Censor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">312 BCE, alone after his colleague left</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 months, then refused to go</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">blind in old age, he spoke against peace with Pyrrhus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Appius Claudius Caecus</b> was a Roman censor and consul of the late 4th century BCE whose career is a list of things nobody had done before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As censor in 312 BCE he began the road and the aqueduct that carry his name, and finished both alone after his colleague resigned in disgust at the way he revised the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The consuls of the next year refused to use his roll and read out the list of senators as it had stood before him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy says he was the first to pollute the senate with the sons of freedmen and, when the house would not have them, spread the poorest citizens through all the tribes instead; that is the hostile version, and it is the only one Livy preserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Aemilian law allowed a censor eighteen months and he simply stayed on, arguing that each censor is appointed by a later order of the people, and the last order of the people is the law of the moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Gnaeus Flavius, a freedman's son whose following Livy traces to that censorship, became curule aedile and put on public view the forms of legal action kept until then in the closets of the pontiffs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also set up a calendar on whitened boards in the Forum marking the days on which business could lawfully be done, and dedicated a temple of Concord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When young nobles sitting in a sickroom agreed among themselves not to rise for him, Flavius had his curule chair carried in and sat looking at them from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Half a century later, old and blind, he had himself carried on a litter through the Forum to the senate house when it seemed peace would be made with Pyrrhus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His sons and sons-in-law lifted him through the door, and Plutarch has him say he had counted his blindness a misfortune until now, when he regretted only that he was not deaf as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.33, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D33. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.46, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D46. [Open access]","Plutarch, \"Pyrrhus,\" in <i>Lives</i>, vol. 9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","politics","law","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Roman Forum","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1f/Appio_Claudio_Cieco_in_Senato.jpg","title":"Appius led into the senate house, painted in the 19th century","desc":"Cesare Maccari's painting of the scene Plutarch describes, made more than two thousand years after it. The painter has him walking, held up by a younger man; Plutarch has him carried through the Forum on a litter and lifted through the door by his sons and sons-in-law.","credit":"Cesare Maccari, 19th century; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Appio_Claudio_Cieco_in_Senato.jpg","alt":"A painting of a crowded hall of men in white togas. In the middle a very old man with a long white beard and closed eyes is walking forward, one hand on a staff and the other on the shoulder of a younger man who supports him, while senators to either side turn to watch."},"why":[{"q":"Why could he stay in office past the legal eighteen months?","a":"Because he argued that each censor is appointed by a later order of the people, and the last order of the people is the law of the moment."},{"q":"Why did publishing the forms of legal action matter?","a":"Because they had been kept in the closets of the pontiffs, so putting them on view, with a calendar of the days business could be done, took the law out of priestly keeping."},{"q":"Why should Livy’s account of his censorship be read carefully?","a":"Because the charge that he polluted the senate with the sons of freedmen is the hostile version, and it is the only one Livy preserves."}]},
{"id":"rm-138","num":138,"category":"Rome","question":"Appius Claudius laid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> southward from Rome during his censorship, and it was the first Roman highway to be paved.","questions":["Procopius says the stone for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was quarried far off, cut polygonal and laid without concrete, the joints so close the blocks look grown together.","A traveller without baggage needed five days to go from Rome to Capua along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was wide enough for two waggons to pass."],"answer":"Via Appia","answerText":"Via Appia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">312 BCE, by Appius Claudius as censor</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome to Capua</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 days on foot, per Procopius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">polygonal lava blocks laid without mortar</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Via Appia</b> is the road Appius Claudius laid southward from Rome during his censorship in 312 BCE, the first Roman highway to be paved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roads were the censors' business, by an old enactment which Cicero quotes, putting the city's temples, roads, waters, treasury and revenues into their keeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A made road was built in four courses: a footing of stones the size of a hand, rubble and lime above it, a bedding of pounded potsherds and lime, and on top polygonal blocks of lava, the surface crowned to throw off water like the shell of a tortoise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Raised footways ran along each side, edged with kerb-stones set at intervals in the line of the edging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Such roads carried the names of the men who made them, which is why one is the Appian, another the Cassian and another the Flaminian; Isidore says the Romans took the practice of paving from the Carthaginians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Procopius saw it some nine centuries later and called it one of the noteworthy sights of the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He gives it as five days' journey for a traveller without baggage, from Rome to Capua, and wide enough for two waggons going opposite ways to pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stone, he says, was quarried far off because none of that kind is found near the road, then worked smooth, cut to a polygonal shape and laid without concrete or anything else between the blocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The joints closed so tightly that the stones look grown together rather than fitted, and after all that time and traffic none had parted, worn thin or lost its polish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The example took: within five years the next censors were building roads through the country districts out of public funds, one of them the same Junius Bubulcus who as consul had vowed a temple to Salus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Viae,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dviae-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Censor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcensor-cn. [Open access]","Procopius, <i>History of the Wars</i> 5.14, trans. H. B. Dewing, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Procopius/Wars/5C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["building","technology","history","geography","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Via Appia","at":[12.5138,41.8556],"zoom":7,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/RemainsViaAppia.jpg/1280px-RemainsViaAppia.jpg","title":"The paving of the Appian Way today","desc":"The polygonal blocks of dark lava that Procopius describes, still in place outside Rome, with the crown of the road running down the middle and the ground falling away at either side. The lamp post and the clipped hedge are modern; so is the asphalt further up, where the surviving paving stops.","credit":"Photograph by Paul Vlaar, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RemainsViaAppia.jpg","alt":"A stretch of ancient road running away from the camera, paved with close-fitted irregular polygonal blocks of dark grey stone, worn smooth and slightly domed along the middle, with a rough stone wall on the left and a clipped hedge on the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why were roads a censor’s business?","a":"Because an old enactment Cicero quotes put the city’s temples, roads, waters, treasury and revenues into their keeping."},{"q":"Why does a Roman road carry one man’s name?","a":"Because such roads were named for the men who made them, which is why one is the Appian, another the Cassian and another the Flaminian."},{"q":"Why did Procopius find the surface intact nine centuries later?","a":"Because the polygonal blocks were worked smooth and laid without concrete or anything between them, closing so tightly that the stones look grown together."}]},
{"id":"rm-139","num":139,"category":"Rome","question":"Of the 11,190 paces the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran, only sixty were above ground, the rest of Rome's first aqueduct being a buried channel.","questions":["Frontinus says Appius Claudius stretched his censorship by various subterfuges until the Appian Way and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were both finished.","For 441 years Rome drank from the Tiber, from wells and from springs, and then the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought water in from the country for the first time."],"answer":"Aqua Appia","answerText":"Aqua Appia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">312 BCE, in Appius Claudius' censorship</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11,190 paces, 11,130 of them underground</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">only 60 paces ran above ground</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Aqua Appia</b> is the first Roman aqueduct, brought into the city by Appius Claudius during the censorship of 312 BCE in which he also began the road that carries his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Frontinus says that for the first 441 years from the founding of the city the Romans made do with the Tiber, with wells and with springs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water was the censors' charge, by the same old enactment that put the city's temples, roads, treasury and revenues into their keeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Frontinus says his colleague Gaius Plautius, called the Hunter for having found the springs, laid the office down within eighteen months believing Appius would do the same; Livy says he resigned in shame at the way Appius had revised the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Appius did not resign, and Frontinus reports that he stretched his term by various subterfuges until both the road and the water were finished, which is why the aqueduct carries his name alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its channel ran 11,190 paces from the intake to the salt works by the Porta Trigemina, where the distribution began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of that distance 11,130 paces were underground, and only sixty were above ground, carried on substructures and, near the Porta Capena, on arches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The picture an aqueduct calls up is a line of arches striding over the Campagna, and the first of them was a tunnel for all but a few hundred feet of its length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pieces of the buried channel have been found under the city, notably where it runs through old tufa quarries on the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Forty years afterwards the Anio Vetus was built with the spoils taken from Pyrrhus, and by Frontinus' own day nine aqueducts ran into Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Frontinus, <i>The Aqueducts of Rome</i> 1.4-5, trans. Charles E. Bennett, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Frontinus/De_Aquis/Bennett/1*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Aquaeductus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Daquaeductus-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Censor,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcensor-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Viae,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dviae-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["building","technology","history","geography","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Porta Capena","at":[12.4894,41.8828],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome need no aqueduct for four centuries?","a":"Because for the first 441 years from the founding of the city the Romans made do with the Tiber, with wells and with springs."},{"q":"Why does the aqueduct carry one name when two censors began it?","a":"Because his colleague laid the office down within eighteen months while Appius stretched his term by various subterfuges until both the road and the water were finished."},{"q":"Why does the first aqueduct look nothing like the picture of one?","a":"Because of its 11,190 paces only sixty ran above ground, so it was a tunnel for all but a few hundred feet of its length."}]},
{"id":"rm-140","num":140,"category":"Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Rome's cast bronze coinage, whose pieces were worth their weight, the as being a full pound of copper at the start.","questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Romans paid in formless lumps of copper, and Pliny says the first figure stamped on the metal was a sheep.","Rome struck no silver until five years before the First Punic War, having reckoned until then in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and in copper by weight."],"answer":"aes grave","answerText":"aes grave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The as</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 pound of copper, falling to 10 ounces</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First silver</span><span class=\"dt-v\">269 BCE, the denarius at 10 asses</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">cut to 2 ounces in the First Punic War</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Aes grave</b> is the name given to Rome's earliest coinage, heavy pieces of cast bronze whose value was their weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Italy copper was the usual medium of exchange and the measure of value, where the Greeks reckoned in silver and the East in gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cattle came before metal, an ox counting as ten sheep, and after them the pound of copper took that place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before there were coins there was aes rude, formless lumps passed from hand to hand, found in quantity in votive wells such as the one at Vicarello.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lumps then took fixed shapes and marks: cubes stamped with an ox or a trident and weighing anywhere from an ounce to a pound, and plain elliptical pieces all of one weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Pliny says king Servius was the first to put a stamp on copper, that before him the raw metal was used, and that the first figure stamped was a sheep, from which he derives the Latin for 'money'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The as was a pound of copper, but few surviving pieces reach that weight and most come to about ten ounces, so the standard was already falling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its types were the prow of a warship on one face and a god's head on the other, Janus on the as, Jupiter on the half, Hercules on the quarter and Mercury on the sixth, each with its mark of value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome struck no silver until 485 years from the founding of the city, five years before the First Punic War, and during that war cut the as to two ounces to clear its debts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mint stood on the Capitol beside the temple of Juno Moneta, vowed by the dictator Lucius Furius Camillus and built where the house of Manlius Capitolinus had stood, and her name is where the word 'money' comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 33.13, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0137%3Abook%3D33%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"As,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Das-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Aes,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Daes-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Moneta,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dmoneta-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.28, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/R%C3%B6m._Republik-_Aes_Grave%2C_Proraserie_libral_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5472200.jpg/1280px-R%C3%B6m._Republik-_Aes_Grave%2C_Proraserie_libral_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5472200.jpg","title":"Cast bronze coin of the Roman prow series","desc":"A piece of the cast bronze coinage of the Roman Republic. The two pellets set beside each face are its mark of value, two of the twelve ounces that made up the as, so the coin is a sixth of one. The reverse carries the prow of a warship that gives the series its name.","credit":"Reinhard Saczewski, Munzkabinett, Staatliche Museen zu Berlin; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B6m._Republik-_Aes_Grave,_Proraserie_libral_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5472200.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a green-patinated cast bronze coin: a capped head in profile with two round pellets below it, and a ship prow with two pellets beneath."},"difficulty":4,"tags":["object","technology","history","art","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Capitoline Hill","at":[12.4831,41.8933],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"why":[{"q":"Why was Rome’s early money reckoned by weight?","a":"Because in Italy copper was the usual medium of exchange and the measure of value, so the pound of copper took the place cattle had held before it."},{"q":"Why do surviving pieces weigh less than the pound they stand for?","a":"Because most come to about ten ounces, so the standard was already falling before the war with Carthage cut the as to two ounces to clear the state’s debts."},{"q":"Why is the mint’s site the origin of a word still used?","a":"Because it stood on the Capitol beside the temple of Juno Moneta, and her name is where the word money comes from."}]},
{"id":"rm-141","num":141,"category":"Rome","question":"Polybius traced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the withdrawal of the Gauls in 387 BCE, after which one people after another — Latins, Etruscans, Celts, Samnites — fell into Roman hands.","questions":["Every stage of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in a treaty rather than annexation, leaving each defeated people bound to Rome alone and owing soldiers instead of tribute.","Colonies of settlers planted on usually a third of each conquered territory served as garrisons during <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, holding down country the legions had just crossed."],"answer":"Roman expansion in Italy","answerText":"Roman expansion in Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome’s rise</span><span class=\"dt-v\">traced by Polybius from 387 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pyrrhus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">invited by Tarentum in 280 BCE, driven out</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Italy subdued</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 264 BCE, all but the Celts</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Roman expansion in Italy</b> is the long sequence of wars and settlements by which one city in Latium came to control the peninsula, and Polybius dated its beginning to the withdrawal of the Gauls from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that point, he wrote, one nation after another in Italy fell into Roman hands: first the Latins, then the Etruscans, then the Celts, then the Samnites on the eastern and northern edges of Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two ways of dealing with a beaten community were open, a treaty of alliance or reduction to complete subjection by conquest or surrender, and Rome used both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Boii and the Etruscans were defeated at Lake Vadimon and beaten again the following year, after which they sent envoys to Rome and made a treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Tarentum insulted Roman ambassadors and called in Pyrrhus, Rome had already reduced the Etruscans and Samnites and won many battles against the Italian Celts, and it turned for the first time to the rest of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius adds that the Gallic defeats had been a training, so that the legions met Pyrrhus like practised gladiators, and that crushing the Gauls in time left Rome free to fight first for Italy and then for Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pyrrhus was driven out, those who had taken his part were subdued, and every people of Italy except the Celts was brought to obedience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What held the result together was alliance rather than annexation: the states taken into the Roman confederacy kept a degree of independence, and the bond among them was a perpetual military alliance with Rome at its head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The older confederacies were broken up, so that the Hernici, the Samnites and the Greek cities of the south each faced Rome alone, and definite military burdens were fixed on every state received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Colonies were planted as garrisons in conquered towns, the settlers taking usually a third of the land, and Cicero later called these old Italian foundations the bulwarks of empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.6, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.20, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Socii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dsocii-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Foederatae Civitates,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dfoederatae-civitates-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Colonia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcolonia-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":5,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Roman_conquest_of_Italy_en.svg/1280px-Roman_conquest_of_Italy_en.svg.png","title":"The stages of Rome's conquest of Italy","desc":"Territory under Roman control at seven dates, from the city and its immediate neighbours about 500 BCE to the whole peninsula by 264 BCE and the north and the islands by 218 BCE. The peoples Rome fought are named across the map: Aequi, Volsci and Marsi around Latium, the Etruscans and Umbri to the north, the Samnites in the central mountains, and the Lucani, Brutii and Messapi in the south.","credit":"Javierfv1212, Fulvio314 and Amphipolis; released into the public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_conquest_of_Italy_en.svg","alt":"A map of Italy shaded in seven colours, each a stage of Roman expansion, with a key giving the dates from about 500 BCE to 218 BCE and the names of the Italian peoples printed across the peninsula."},"why":[{"q":"Why did alliance rather than annexation hold the result together?","a":"Because the states taken into the confederacy kept a degree of independence, and the bond among them was a perpetual military alliance with Rome at its head."},{"q":"Why were the older confederacies broken up?","a":"Because it left the Hernici, the Samnites and the Greek cities of the south each facing Rome alone, with definite military burdens fixed on every state received."},{"q":"Why did Polybius treat the Gallic wars as a training?","a":"Because the legions met Pyrrhus like practised gladiators, and crushing the Gauls in time left Rome free to fight first for Italy and then for Sicily."}]},
{"id":"rm-142","num":142,"category":"Rome","question":"Livy opens <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a Latin praetor demanding in the Capitol that one consul be chosen from Rome and one from Latium, with the senate shared equally.","questions":["During <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the consul Decius Mus veiled his head, stood on a spear, devoted himself and the enemy to the gods below, and rode into the Latin ranks.","The Campanians, the Volsci and the Sidicini fought beside the Latin League in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended when Camillus and Maenius took Pedum and beat the Antiates at the Astura."],"answer":"Latin War","answerText":"Latin War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consuls chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">341 BCE, Manlius Torquatus and Decius Mus</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Latium subdued after Pedum and the Astura</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Latin War</b> set Rome against the Latin League, with the Campanians, the Volsci and the Sidicini on the Latin side, and Polybius makes the Latins the first people Rome mastered after the Gauls left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Livy says the Latins met in council under the pretext of a Samnite war, and that their two praetors, both Roman colonists, drew in the colonies of Signia and Velitrae and the Volsci.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The senate summoned the league’s ten chiefs, and in the Capitol the Latin praetor L. Annius of Setia offered peace on equal terms: one consul from Rome and one from Latium, the senate shared, one nation and one republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consul T. Manlius Torquatus answered that if the senate took such terms from a man of Setia he would come into the house with his sword drawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The year’s consuls were made to resign early so new ones could be elected sooner, and an interregnum returned Manlius Torquatus, for the third time, with P. Decius Mus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Livy puts the decisive battle at the foot of Vesuvius where the road led to Veseris, and there the haruspex read Decius’ death in the victims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the hastati on the left gave way, Decius had the pontifex maximus dictate the devotio, veiled his head, stood on a spear, gave the enemy’s legions and himself to the Divine Manes and to Earth, and rode in among the Latins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Manlius had already put his own son to death for taking up a Latin horseman’s challenge to single combat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A council at Vescia heard that Rome had won only the name of victory, one consul having killed his son and the other devoted himself, but the levy raised on that argument was destroyed at Trifanum and the Latins and Campanians surrendered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two more years of fighting followed, until the consuls L. Furius Camillus and C. Maenius beat the Tiburtines at Pedum and the Antiates and their neighbours at the Astura, and came home to a triumph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.5, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.7, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D9. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.6, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Simon_de_Vos_-_Death_of_Decius_Mus.jpg/1280px-Simon_de_Vos_-_Death_of_Decius_Mus.jpg","title":"The death of Decius Mus, painted in 1641","desc":"Simon de Vos gives the moment Livy describes: the consul, having devoted himself and the enemy to the gods below, rides into the Latin line and is brought down by their weapons, with a winged Victory carrying the wreath and palm above him. Oil on copper, in the Hermitage Museum.","credit":"Simon de Vos, The Death of Decius Mus, 1641, Hermitage Museum, Saint Petersburg; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simon_de_Vos_-_Death_of_Decius_Mus.jpg","alt":"A crowded battle painting: a fair-haired rider on a rearing white horse is speared by a helmeted horseman, dead and wounded men lie in the foreground, and a winged female figure with a palm branch flies overhead."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the Latin terms unacceptable to Rome?","a":"Because they asked for one consul from Rome and one from Latium and a shared senate, and Manlius answered that if the senate took such terms he would come into the house with his sword drawn."},{"q":"Why did the Latins think Rome had won only the name of victory?","a":"Because one consul had put his own son to death and the other had devoted himself, so the council at Vescia raised a fresh levy on that argument — and it was destroyed at Trifanum."},{"q":"Why did Decius stand on a spear with his head veiled?","a":"Because the haruspex had read his death in the victims, so he had the pontifex maximus dictate the devotio and gave the enemy’s legions and himself to the Divine Manes and to Earth."}]},
{"id":"rm-143","num":143,"category":"Rome","question":"Rome broke up the Latin League after the Latin War but kept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the yearly sacrifice to Jupiter Latiaris on the Alban Mount.","questions":["Dionysius says 47 cities shared in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, bringing lambs, cheeses or a measure of milk while one bull was sacrificed in common and the meat divided among them.","No consul could take the field until the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been held, and while he was on the Alban Mount a prefect appointed for that festival kept his place at Rome."],"answer":"Feriae Latinae","answerText":"Feriae Latinae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">League dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, and the festival kept on</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cities sharing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">47, each with an appointed share</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Feriae Latinae</b> was the yearly festival of Jupiter Latiaris on the Alban Mount, the one institution of the Latin confederacy Rome kept after breaking the league apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Dionysius credits its foundation to Tarquin, who set aside a temple for the joint use of the Romans, the Latins, the Hernici and two Volscian cities, so that they might meet each year to feast and sacrifice in common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A law fixed it on a high mountain commanding the city of the Albans, ordered everyone there to abstain from hostility while it lasted, and set the share each city contributed and the portion it received.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In all 47 cities took part, bringing lambs, cheeses or a measure of milk, with one bull sacrificed in common and the meat divided out, and Dionysius says the Romans superintended it in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The festival is older than that story allows, and before the union the Latins’ own chief magistrate presided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> After the Latin War Rome broke the confederacy up, and Livy says the Latin cities were left without intermarriage, free trade or common councils with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The councils were where the league’s business was done, and as long as the Latin republic lasted the gathering on the mount had itself deliberated on the confederacy’s affairs and settled its members’ disputes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The presidency Tarquin had taken from the Latin magistrate stayed with Rome’s chief magistrates once the Latin commonwealth was gone, and the Romans alone offered the white oxen on behalf of all who shared in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its date was fixed each year by the magistrates, no consul might take the field until it had been held, and one neglected ceremony meant the whole festival was repeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy shows the cost of getting it wrong, a flawed election leaving the sacrifice unproclaimed and the consular tribunes made to resign in expiation; while they were on the mount a prefect appointed for the festival alone kept their place at Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 4.49, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1939), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/4C*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Feriae,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dferiae-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 5.17, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D5%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Praefectus Urbi,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dpraefectus-urbi-cn. [Open access]","Rianne A. M. Hermans, \"Latin Cults through Roman Eyes: Myth, Memory and Cult Practice in the Alban Hills\" (PhD diss., University of Amsterdam, 2017), chap. 4, https://hdl.handle.net/11245.1/cd8d636c-def7-44a4-9043-231c0d428d28. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["practice","history","religion","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Alban Mount","at":[12.7053,41.7492],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Monte_Cavo_e_lago_Albano.jpg/1280px-Monte_Cavo_e_lago_Albano.jpg","title":"The Alban Mount seen across Lake Albano","desc":"Monte Cavo, the highest of the Alban Hills, photographed from the far shore of the crater lake below it. The temple of Jupiter Latiaris stood on the summit, and the cities of Latium came up to it each year for the common sacrifice.","credit":"Deblu68; released into the public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Cavo_e_lago_Albano.jpg","alt":"A wooded volcanic mountain with a rounded summit rising beyond the wooded rim of a lake, under a clear blue sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome keep this festival after breaking the league?","a":"Because the presidency Tarquin had taken from the Latin magistrate stayed with Rome’s chief magistrates once the Latin commonwealth was gone, and Rome alone offered the oxen for all."},{"q":"Why was the gathering more than a sacrifice?","a":"Because while the Latin republic lasted it deliberated on the confederacy’s affairs and settled its members’ disputes, which is what the councils were for."},{"q":"Why could one mistake cost the whole ceremony?","a":"Because a single neglected rite meant the festival was repeated, and no consul might take the field until it had been held — a flawed election once forced the consular tribunes to resign."}]},
{"id":"rm-144","num":144,"category":"Rome","question":"A town whose people were Roman citizens but who elected no magistrates of their own was a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, governed by a deputy the praetor sent out from Rome.","questions":["Capua was placed among the self-governing communities in 338 BCE and reduced after its revolt in 211 to the rank of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, governed from Rome.","Of the 22 towns Festus lists as holding the rank of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, 18 were communities with the citizenship but no vote and only four were colonies of Roman citizens."],"answer":"praefectura","answerText":"praefectura","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capua</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, keeping a commonwealth of its own</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">211 BCE, reduced to government from Rome</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lex Julia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">90 BCE, every Italian community raised</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>praefectura</b> was an Italian community whose people held Roman citizenship but elected no magistrates of their own, and was governed instead by a prefect sent from Rome, a deputy appointed by a magistrate rather than by the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Latin War ended, Camillus put the future settlement of Latium before the senate before the next elections were held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy shows Rome deciding each place separately: Lanuvium got the full citizenship with its cult, Tusculum kept the citizenship it had, Velitrae lost its walls and had its senate deported across the Tiber, and Antium got colonists and citizenship but lost its warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other Latin cities lost the rights of intermarriage, free trade and common councils with one another, while Capua, Fundi and Formiae were allowed the private rights of Roman citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Latium and Capua had already been stripped of territory, which was shared out among the Roman plebs at two jugera a head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Ancient writers used the word municipium two ways, for communities keeping a commonwealth of their own separate from Rome and for communities with no authorities of their own that were governed from Rome, both classes being citizens without the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Capua shows the difference: in 338 BCE it was placed in the first class, and in 211, after its revolt, it was reduced to the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Such towns fell under the jurisdiction of the praetor urbanus, who could not discharge it in person and so named deputies, praefecti iure dicundo, to administer Roman law there; Festus lists 22 praefecturae, 18 of them communities without the vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether every colony and municipium began as a praefectura, or only the communities that held the citizenship without the vote, is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A town that gained the full franchise gained with it the right to elect its own magistrates and ceased to be a praefectura, though even after the Lex Julia of 90 BCE some were still governed by prefects sent from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Colonia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcolonia-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Praefectus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dpraefectus-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Capua","at":[14.251,41.0847],"zoom":8,"within":"Capua"},"why":[{"q":"Why did citizens end up governed by an official they had not chosen?","a":"Because such towns fell under the praetor urbanus, who could not discharge that jurisdiction in person and named deputies to administer Roman law there."},{"q":"Why did Rome settle each Latin city on different terms?","a":"Because the point was to prevent the league re-forming: the cities lost intermarriage, free trade and common councils with one another while each was dealt with separately."},{"q":"Why does Capua appear in two different classes?","a":"Because in 338 BCE it was placed among the communities keeping a commonwealth of their own, and in 211, after its revolt, reduced to those governed from Rome."}]},
{"id":"rm-145","num":145,"category":"Rome","question":"The private half of Roman citizenship, commercium and conubium, was what a community held under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, without the vote or the right to stand for office at Rome.","questions":["A man from a town holding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could enter the Roman citizenship by discharging one of the higher magistracies at home, and under the emperors his family sometimes came with him.","After the Social War extended citizenship across Italy, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survived only as a favour granted abroad, to the Transpadane Gauls in 89 BCE and by Vespasian to the whole of Spain."],"answer":"Latin rights","answerText":"Latin rights","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">League dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, the reciprocal exchange ended</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Narrowed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">268 BCE, after the colony at Ariminum</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Granted abroad</span><span class=\"dt-v\">89 BCE, to the Transpadane Gauls</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Latin rights</b> were what a community could hold towards Rome without Roman citizenship: the private half of that citizenship, namely commercium, the capacity to act under the civil law, and conubium, that of making a lawful Roman marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they left out was the public half, the vote in the assemblies and the right to stand for Roman office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They began as reciprocal rights, and Dionysius has Spurius Cassius claim he made the Latins friends of Rome by granting them equal rights of citizenship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That exchange ended with the Latin War, when commercium and conubium ceased to pass between the league’s members and each town dealt with Rome directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The privileges then varied from place to place, some towns keeping conubium, commercium and the right to acquire citizenship by settling at Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Colonies founded after the dissolution under the name of Latin colonies were planted by Rome alone and lay outside Latium, their settlers mainly Latins but including poorer Romans who surrendered their citizenship for an allotment of land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a colony was largely independent, was not bound to take Roman law unless it asked, struck its own coinage, and its people counted as foreigners at Rome while still serving in its armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rights were then narrowed: after Ariminum in 268 BCE new Latin foundations tended to be confined to commercium, and the right to become a citizen by settling at Rome was withdrawn, Latins being expelled in 187 and 177 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What remained was a ladder: a Latin who held one of the higher magistracies at home came into the Roman citizenship, and under the emperors that carried his family with him in some towns and not others, the difference between the greater and the lesser Latin right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The laws ending the Social War extended citizenship across Italy, so the status vanished at home and was afterwards granted abroad as a favour, to the Transpadane Gauls in 89 BCE and by Vespasian to all Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Latinitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dlatinitas-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Civitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcivitas-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Colonia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcolonia-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 8.70, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1943), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/8C*.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Lex_Salpensana_de203620-2dc6-11e2-b417-000475f5bda5_1_2.jpg/1280px-Lex_Salpensana_de203620-2dc6-11e2-b417-000475f5bda5_1_2.jpg","title":"A facsimile of the charter of Salpensa","desc":"An 1858 engraved facsimile of the bronze municipal charter of Salpensa in Spain, granted under the Flavians to a town holding Latin status. Its chapters set out how magistrates of such a town, with their parents, wives and children, acquired Roman citizenship, and the rubrics for the duumvirs, the aediles and the quaestor stand out in the margins.","credit":"Facsimile of 1858, Biblioteca Virtual Miguel de Cervantes; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lex_Salpensana_de203620-2dc6-11e2-b417-000475f5bda5_1_2.jpg","alt":"A black and white plate of two dense columns of Roman capitals without word spaces, ruled off within a plain border, with short rubric headings breaking the text."},"why":[{"q":"Why do these rights cover marriage and contract but not voting?","a":"Because they are the private half of Roman citizenship — commercium and conubium — and leave out the public half, the vote in the assemblies and the right to stand for office."},{"q":"Why did the rights stop passing between Latin towns?","a":"Because they began as reciprocal rights among the league, and after the Latin War commercium and conubium ceased to pass between its members, each town dealing with Rome directly."},{"q":"Why did a Roman ever surrender citizenship to join a Latin colony?","a":"Because it bought an allotment of land, the colony being largely independent and striking its own coinage, though its people counted as foreigners at Rome."}]},
{"id":"rm-146","num":146,"category":"Rome","question":"Gellius says a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a free town whose people were Roman citizens using their own laws, bound by no Roman statute their own citizens had not ratified.","questions":["Where a colony was a miniature of Rome carrying all its laws, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept its own, which is why Hadrian was puzzled that Italica wanted to become a colony instead.","The word comes from taking up a duty, and a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shared that burden with the Roman people while remaining a town with laws and magistrates of its own."],"answer":"municipium","answerText":"municipium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest grants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, to Capua, Fundi and Formiae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lex Julia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">90 BCE, all Italy made municipia or colonies</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>municipium</b> was an Italian town whose people held Roman citizenship while keeping their own laws and magistrates, and Gellius says such townsmen were bound by no Roman law except what their own citizens had ratified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient writers derived the word from taking up a duty, though the preferred explanation now reads the same words as receiving a gift, so that it named a relation of hospitality before a kind of town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Festus defines it two ways: people who came to Rome and shared in everything but the vote and office, and those whose whole community had passed into the Roman state, the first keeping a commonwealth of their own, the second governed from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Capua shows the difference, in the first class in 338 BCE and reduced to the second in 211 after its revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy records the earliest of those grants: after the Latin War, Capua, Fundi and Formiae were allowed the private rights of Roman citizens, with Cumae and Suessula on the same footing, their lands having already been taken from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Whichever kind it was, the town had no share in the public law, so its people were citizens but not citizens of the fullest right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gellius adds that the people of Caere were the first to hold the citizenship without the vote, granted the honour and left free of service for sheltering Rome’s sacred objects in the Gallic war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A colony was the opposite, not a town coming into the citizenship from outside but one transplanted from the state, carrying all the laws of the Roman people and none of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gellius thought it strange that colonies were reckoned the better thing, seeming miniatures of Rome, while the rights of the municipal towns grew obscure and fell out of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He cites Hadrian wondering why Italica and Utica should want the standing of colonies, and Praeneste begging Tiberius to be turned back into a municipium, which he granted as a favour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Aulus Gellius, <i>The Attic Nights</i> 16.13, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A2007.01.0072%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Colonia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcolonia-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Civitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcivitas-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Tusculum","at":[12.7075,41.7967],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Tuscolo_2022_-_1.jpg/1280px-Tuscolo_2022_-_1.jpg","title":"The excavated town of Tusculum","desc":"Tusculum in the Alban Hills, whose people already held Roman citizenship before the Latin War and kept it in the settlement afterwards. The site was abandoned in the Middle Ages and never built over, so the street lines and wall footings of the ancient town lie open on the ridge.","credit":"Gigi er Gigliola, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuscolo_2022_-_1.jpg","alt":"A dry grassy hilltop with low excavated stone walls and cut foundations, oak trees along the ridge and open country falling away behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why could such a town’s people be citizens and still keep their own law?","a":"Because they held the private rights and no share in the public law, so Gellius says they were bound by no Roman law except what their own citizens had ratified."},{"q":"Why did the standing come to seem the lesser one?","a":"Because a colony carried all the laws of the Roman people and seemed a miniature of Rome, so the rights of the municipal towns grew obscure and fell out of use."},{"q":"Why did Praeneste ask to be turned back into one?","a":"Because the standing was a favour rather than a demotion in its own eyes, and Tiberius granted the request — as Hadrian wondered why Italica and Utica wanted to be colonies instead."}]},
{"id":"rm-147","num":147,"category":"Rome","question":"A man holding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had conubium and commercium but no share in the public law, so he could neither vote in the assemblies nor stand for Roman office.","questions":["Anagnia was punished in 306 BCE with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, losing its own government and the right of intermarriage, while three Hernican towns kept their independence by choice.","The registers of citizens holding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were called the tables of the Caerites, after the first town to be given the citizenship in that form."],"answer":"civitas sine suffragio","answerText":"civitas sine suffragio","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First grants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, to Capua, Fundi and Formiae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caere</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the status dated to about 353 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anagnia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">306 BCE, as a punishment</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Civitas sine suffragio</b> was Roman citizenship without the vote, carrying the private rights of conubium and commercium but no share in the public law, so that its holder could neither vote in the assemblies nor stand for office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy records the earliest grants after the Latin War, when Capua, Fundi and Formiae were allowed the private rights of Roman citizens and Cumae and Suessula were put on the same footing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gellius says the people of Caere were the first to hold the citizenship in this form, given the honour and left free of service and burdens for sheltering Rome’s sacred objects during the Gallic war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the register of Caere’s burghers was the first of its kind, the lists of those holding only this inferior franchise were called the tables of the Caerites, and to be entered on them came to mean being made an aerarius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The status itself is dated to about 353 BCE, a generation later than the Gallic war with which the tradition connects the grant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Holding it brought no exemption from Rome’s demands, since these citizens served in the legions or in a corps of their own, and they ceased to be aerarii only on coming into the full franchise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether the grant was a favour or a penalty depended on the case, and Livy’s account of the Hernican settlement shows both at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Three Hernican towns, Aletrium, Verulae and Ferentinum, had their own government restored to them because they preferred it to the Roman franchise, and were allowed to intermarry with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Anagnia and the towns that had taken up arms were given the citizenship without the vote instead, stripped of self-government and of intermarriage among themselves, their magistrates left with no function but religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The status ended when the laws passed at the close of the Social War made every community in Italy a municipium or a colony of Roman citizens holding the full franchise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aulus Gellius, <i>The Attic Nights</i> 16.13, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A2007.01.0072%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Aerarii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Daerarii-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Civitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcivitas-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D43. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Caere","at":[12.0993,41.9994],"zoom":8,"within":"Caere"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Cerveteri%2C_necropoli_della_banditaccia%2C_via_sepolcrale_principale%2C_02.jpg/1280px-Cerveteri%2C_necropoli_della_banditaccia%2C_via_sepolcrale_principale%2C_02.jpg","title":"The main funerary street at Caere","desc":"The Banditaccia cemetery of Caere, the Etruscan city whose people were the first to hold Roman citizenship without the vote. Its register of burghers was the first of its kind, and the lists of citizens holding that inferior franchise took their name from it.","credit":"Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerveteri,_necropoli_della_banditaccia,_via_sepolcrale_principale,_02.jpg","alt":"A dirt road running between grassy burial mounds cut into soft rock, with cypresses and umbrella pines on the slopes above."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Caere’s register remembered in the name of a penalty?","a":"Because it was the first list of its kind, so the rolls of those holding the inferior franchise were called the tables of the Caerites, and to be entered on them meant being made an aerarius."},{"q":"Why did the status bring no relief from Rome’s demands?","a":"Because these citizens still served in the legions or in a corps of their own, and ceased to be aerarii only on coming into the full franchise."},{"q":"Why can the same grant be a favour and a punishment?","a":"Because three Hernican towns had their own government restored because they preferred it, while Anagnia and the towns that had taken up arms were given this citizenship and stripped of self-government."}]},
{"id":"rm-148","num":148,"category":"Rome","question":"Gellius calls a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an offshoot of the citizen body, a small image and likeness of the Roman people, planted where Rome needed a place held.","questions":["The senate resolved that 2,500 names be enrolled for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Cales, and three commissioners were appointed to settle the men and allocate their holdings.","Settlers marched to the site of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under a standard and ploughed the line of its walls, and both appear on the coins some of them struck."],"answer":"colonia","answerText":"colonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ariminum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">268 BCE, a colony founded beyond Latium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Veterans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 201 BCE, colonies for discharged men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alba</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 colonists, and 4,000 to Sora</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>colonia</b> was a body of citizens sent out by a public act of the state to hold a place, and Gellius calls one an offshoot of the citizen body, a small image and likeness of the Roman people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The word shares its root with the verb to cultivate and with colonus, a tiller of the earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early foundations were garrisons planted in conquered towns, the settlers taking a share of the land, usually a third, while the old inhabitants kept the rest and lived among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were meant to hold down a beaten people and block incursions: Livy says the colony at Nequinum was an outpost against the Umbrians, and the place was renamed Narnia after the river Nar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cicero called them the bulwarks of empire; others were founded to increase Rome’s numbers, to carry off turbulent citizens, or from 201 BCE to settle veterans, and after Ariminum in 268 BCE they lay beyond Latium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> None was established without a law, generally a bill of the plebs resting on a decree of the senate, so a colony was never a body of adventurers but an organised act of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy’s account of the colony sent to Cales shows the machinery: the senate resolved that 2,500 names be enrolled, and three commissioners were appointed to settle the men and allocate the holdings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Numbers varied greatly, 300 heads of families in the earliest foundations against 4,000 sent to Sora and 6,000 to Alba in one year, and an unattractive colony could be filled by levy or by lot, with no leave to withdraw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Citizens gave in their names at Rome in the phrase used of enrolling soldiers, and went out to the site under a standard, both marking the military character of the business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line of the new town’s walls was marked out with a plough, standard and plough both appear on the coins of some colonies, and religious ceremonies attended the foundation, whose anniversary was kept after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Colonia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcolonia-cn. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>The Attic Nights</i> 16.13, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A2007.01.0072%3Abook%3D16%3Achapter%3D13. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Latinitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dlatinitas-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Alba Fucens","at":[13.4131,42.08],"zoom":8,"within":"Alba Fucens"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Mura_di_Cosa.jpg/1280px-Mura_di_Cosa.jpg","title":"The circuit wall of the colony at Cosa","desc":"Part of the polygonal wall thrown round Cosa on the Tuscan coast, built of large blocks fitted without mortar. A colony of this kind was planted to hold a stretch of country, and the wall is the first thing the settlers put up.","credit":"Albarubescens, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mura_di_Cosa.jpg","alt":"A long stretch of ancient wall built from large irregular limestone blocks, running away between grass and evergreen scrub."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a colony never a body of adventurers?","a":"Because none was established without a law, generally a bill of the plebs resting on a decree of the senate, with commissioners appointed to settle the men and allocate the holdings."},{"q":"Why were the early foundations planted where they were?","a":"Because they were garrisons meant to hold down a beaten people and block incursions — the colony at Nequinum an outpost against the Umbrians."},{"q":"Why did the settlers go out under a standard?","a":"Because citizens gave in their names at Rome in the phrase used of enrolling soldiers, both marking the military character of the business."}]},
{"id":"rm-149","num":149,"category":"Rome","question":"A Roman who joined a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave up his citizenship for a holding of land, and served afterwards in the allied cohorts rather than in the legions.","questions":["Where colonies of citizens were set on the coast, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was almost always planted inland, an independent community under no Roman magistrate and free to keep its own law.","Alba took 6,000 settlers and Sora 4,000 in a single year, the scale on which Rome planted a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> inland to hold the country it had just taken."],"answer":"Latin colony","answerText":"Latin colony","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">After the wars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE with the Latins, 306 with the Hernicans</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The twelve</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded after 268 BCE, from Ariminum</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>Latin colony</b> was led out by Roman commissioners after a vote of the people, exactly as a colony of citizens was, and differed from one entirely in law: it formed an independent community under no Roman magistrate, not bound to take Roman law except so far as it chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its settlers gave up the Roman franchise and counted as foreigners at Rome, serving not in the legions but in cohorts and squadrons like the other allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With the older allied states they made up the Latin name, and they held commercium with Rome and probably conubium as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Roman who joined one had to do so of his own will, since the loss of citizenship it carried could not be imposed on him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Colonies of this kind fall into three groups: five ascribed to the old Latin alliance of the regal period, seven to the renewed alliance that took in the Hernici, and 27 planted in conquered land outside Latium after the wars with the Latins and the Hernicans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where colonies of citizens in the same period were as a rule set on the sea coast, the Latin ones were placed almost invariably in the interior of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were substantial towns: Livy gives 2,500 names enrolled for Cales under three commissioners, and 6,000 settled at Alba with 4,000 at Sora in a single year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their position grew worse with time, and the line between them and holders of the full franchise sharper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cicero speaks of the legal standing of ‘the twelve colonies’, understood as the twelve founded after 268 BCE beginning with Ariminum, which had only a lesser Latin right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That lesser right allowed no coinage beyond copper in some cases, gave commercium without conubium, and made the full Roman franchise harder to reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Colonia,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dcolonia-cn. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Latinitas,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dlatinitas-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D16. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Socii,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dsocii-cn. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Norba","at":[12.9986,41.5872],"zoom":8,"within":"Norba"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Antica_Norba%2C_Porta_Maggiore_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Antica_Norba%2C_Porta_Maggiore_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The main gate of Norba","desc":"Porta Maggiore at Norba in the Volscian hills, where the road runs between the polygonal circuit wall and a free-standing bastion that covered the approach. Norba was one of the Latin colonies planted on the edge of the plain.","credit":"Pietro Scerrato, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antica_Norba,_Porta_Maggiore_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A track climbing between a high wall of large fitted limestone blocks on the left and a separate square block of the same masonry on the right, with pines behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a Roman have to join one of his own free will?","a":"Because joining cost him the Roman franchise, and that loss could not be imposed on him — the settlers counting as foreigners at Rome and serving in cohorts rather than the legions."},{"q":"Why were these colonies placed inland?","a":"Because colonies of citizens in the same period were as a rule set on the sea coast, while these were planted almost invariably in the interior of the peninsula."},{"q":"Why did the standing get worse over time?","a":"Because the twelve founded after 268 BCE had only a lesser Latin right, giving commercium without conubium and making the full Roman franchise harder to reach."}]},
{"id":"rm-150","num":150,"category":"Rome","question":"Rome left a beaten Italian community in being but took part of its land, often a third, into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the land the Roman people held in common.","questions":["Whoever would work the wasted parts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> might do so for a tenth of the grain and a fifth of the fruit, with a toll on his flocks.","Appian says the rich came to hold most of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, absorbed their poorer neighbours’ plots and worked vast tracts with slaves, so that Italy’s free population fell."],"answer":"ager publicus","answerText":"ager publicus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Latin War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, land shared at 2 jugera a head</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">occupiers paid a tenth of grain, a fifth of fruit</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>ager publicus</b> was the land the Roman people held in common, made out of conquest: a beaten Italian community was usually left standing but stripped of part of its territory, often a third.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Appian says the Romans took a share of the land of each people they subdued and built towns on it, or sent settlers into towns already there, using them as outposts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cultivated part was assigned to colonists, sold or leased at once, and land sold by the quaestors passed in full ownership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest sale recorded is Pometia, whose leading men Livy says were beheaded and its people sold, the town razed and the land put up for sale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Land given outright to citizens was surveyed, divided by baulks into centuries and allotted by lot, the oldest assignments being two jugera a man, which passed to his heirs after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Livy’s settlement after the Latin War shows it at work, Latium and Capua losing territory that was shared among the Roman plebs at two jugera a head, three in the Falernian district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It went on piecemeal as well, and Frusino was mulcted of a third of its land for having stirred up the Hernican revolt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of the conquered land, however, lay wasted by war with no time to distribute it, so Rome proclaimed that whoever would work it might do so for a tenth of the grain and a fifth of the fruit, with a toll on flocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who took it up were called possessores and their holdings possessiones, and though occupying state land gave no right against the state, the arrangement was recognised in law and could be undone only by legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Appian says the purpose was to multiply the Italian stock and with it the supply of allies, and that the opposite followed, the rich taking most of the undistributed land, absorbing their poorer neighbours’ plots and working vast tracts with slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.7, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, William Wayte and G. E. Marindin, eds., \"Agrariae Leges,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1890), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0063%3Aentry%3Dagrariae-leges-cn. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 2.17, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","politics","agriculture","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Museo_archeologico_%28Fano%29_136.jpg/1280px-Museo_archeologico_%28Fano%29_136.jpg","title":"A boundary stone of the land commissioners","desc":"A cippus from near Fano recording that Aulus Terentius Varro Lucullus, as propraetor, saw to the restoring of boundary markers by decree of the senate, and naming the three commissioners for granting and assigning land who set them up. Stones like this one marked the public land off from private ground.","credit":"Accurimbono, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museo_archeologico_(Fano)_136.jpg","alt":"A tapering limestone post standing against a museum wall, its upper half cut with nine lines of Roman capitals, a printed label beside it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome invite anyone to occupy the land it had taken?","a":"Because most of it lay wasted by war with no time to distribute it, so whoever would work it might do so for a tenth of the grain, a fifth of the fruit and a toll on flocks."},{"q":"Why could an occupier be secure without owning anything?","a":"Because occupying state land gave no right against the state, yet the arrangement was recognised in law and could be undone only by legislation."},{"q":"Why did the policy produce the opposite of its purpose?","a":"Because it was meant to multiply the Italian stock and the supply of allies, and instead the rich took most of the undistributed land, absorbed poorer neighbours’ plots and worked vast tracts with slaves."}]},
{"id":"rm-151","num":151,"category":"Rome","question":"Livy opens the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by saying his history now turns to wars greater than any before in forces, in length and in the ground they covered.","questions":["The first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began when the Campanians, beaten at the gates of Capua, asked Rome for help against a people Rome had lately made a treaty with.","Samnium fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a federal league under a war leader called Embratur, and in the last of them it drew in Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls."],"answer":"Samnite Wars","answerText":"Samnite Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">343 BCE, over Campania</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second</span><span class=\"dt-v\">326 BCE, over Palaepolis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Third</span><span class=\"dt-v\">298 BCE, ending in surrender in 290</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Samnite Wars</b> were three wars between Rome and the Samnite league of the central Apennines, and Livy opens his account by saying his history now turns to wars greater than any before in the forces engaged, the time they lasted and the ground they covered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that the cause came from outside rather than between the two peoples, then allies: the Samnites attacked the Sidicini, who fled to the Campanians, and the Campanians were beaten on Sidicine ground and again at the gates of Capua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shut inside their walls with no help near, the Campanians asked Rome, and the senate, hesitating over its treaty with Samnium, agreed; so the first war began in 343 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Samnium was a federal league rather than a state, with no record of a standing council, and in war the tribes chose a commander over the whole confederacy, called Embratur in their own tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy names one such, Gaius Pontius son of Herennius, as captain-general in the year of the capitulation at Caudium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The second broke out in 326 BCE over the Greek city of Palaepolis, into which Samnite and Nolan troops had been put, with a levy called through Samnium and Privernum, Fundi and Formiae sounded out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome sent envoys first and got an insolent answer, the Samnites denying the charges and saying they had no reason to distrust their own strength if it came to fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The third began in 298 BCE, when the Samnites attacked the Lucanians and the party against them called Rome in, and it was fought on a far larger scale, Samnium leaguing with Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They laid down their arms in 290 BCE, in a year when a consul could still earn a triumph for ending wars that had lasted more than fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius counted the Samnites among the peoples Rome mastered before turning to the rest of Italy, and reckoned the experience of those wars a training for what came after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Samnium,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dsamnium-geo. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.6, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Bovianum","at":[14.4714,41.4869],"zoom":8,"within":"Bovianum"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg/1280px-Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg","title":"Samnite warriors on a tomb frieze from Nola","desc":"A painted frieze from a tomb at Nola in Campania, made in the 4th century BCE while these wars were being fought. Two warriors on foot carry round shields and spears, one in a horned helmet and one in a plumed helmet over a decorated cuirass, and a horseman rides in from the right.","credit":"Anonymous 4th-century BCE painter; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samnite_soldiers_from_a_tomb_frieze_in_Nola_4th_century_BCE.jpg","alt":"A wall painting in red, yellow and white showing two armoured spearmen with round shields and crested helmets and, at the right, a rider on a dappled horse, framed by a meander band above and a wave pattern below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did two allies end up at war with each other?","a":"Because the cause came from outside: the Samnites attacked the Sidicini, the Campanians took their part and were beaten, and Rome agreed to help them despite its treaty with Samnium."},{"q":"Why is Samnium described as a league rather than a state?","a":"Because there is no record of a standing council, and in war the tribes chose a commander over the whole confederacy rather than having a permanent head."},{"q":"Why was the third war fought on a larger scale than the first two?","a":"Because Samnium leagued with Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls, so what had begun over the Sidicini became a war across much of Italy."}]},
{"id":"rm-152","num":152,"category":"Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened in 343 BCE, when the fetials had been sent to demand redress and the people voted the declaration the senate had moved.","questions":["After the first year of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Carthage sent a golden crown of 25 pounds for the Capitol and Falerii asked to make its truce permanent.","Rome ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by renewing the old alliance and telling Samnium it was free to make peace or war with the Sidicini as it chose."],"answer":"First Samnite War","answerText":"First Samnite War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">343 – 341 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 battles: Gaurus, Saticula, Suessula</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">341 BCE, the old alliance renewed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>First Samnite War</b> began in 343 BCE, after Rome took up the Campanians’ appeal, sent the fetials to demand redress and, on the senate’s motion, had the people vote a declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both consuls marched, Marcus Valerius Corvus into Campania to camp at Mount Gaurus and Aulus Cornelius Cossus into Samnium to camp at Saticula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Samnite legions came into Campania first, expecting the main theatre there and eager to punish the Campanians for helping others against them and then calling in help themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Valerius beat them below Gaurus and Cornelius near Saticula, and a third battle at Suessula, where the beaten army had been reinforced and attacked again, ended the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both consuls triumphed and the news travelled: Falerii asked to turn its forty years’ truce into a lasting treaty, the Latins sent against the Paeligni the army raised with Rome in mind, and Carthage sent a golden crown of 25 pounds for the Capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The next year the war was checked not by Samnium but by Rome’s own soldiers, the troops left to winter at Capua and Suessa being softened by the city and plotting to seize it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That mutiny, unrest at Rome and the cooling of the Latins together disposed the senate to make an end, and a treaty in 341 BCE renewed the old alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The account that makes Samnium humbled and suing for peace is hard to square with the terms, the answer given being that nothing hindered the Samnites from making peace or war with the Sidicini as they pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy says they marched on the Sidicini at once with the same troops, and that when those offered their surrender to Rome the senate refused it as made too late and out of necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They gave themselves to the Latins instead, the Campanians joined, and the army that invaded Samnium under Latin command opened the war Rome had to fight against its own allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.32, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Samnium,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dsamnium-geo. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 7.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Saticula","at":[14.4986,41.087],"zoom":8,"within":"Saticula"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Sant_Agata_de_Goti_the_village_on_the_rock.jpg/1280px-Sant_Agata_de_Goti_the_village_on_the_rock.jpg","title":"The rock of Saticula","desc":"Sant'Agata de' Goti stands on the tufa spur that ancient Saticula occupied, on the edge of Samnium above the Campanian plain. The consul Cornelius camped here in the first year of the war; the buildings on the cliff are medieval and later, but the position is the reason the place mattered.","credit":"Tery14, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sant_Agata_de_Goti_the_village_on_the_rock.jpg","alt":"A town of pale stone houses standing along the top of a low cliff, with fig trees and orchard rising towards it from below and cloud above."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Samnites expect the fighting to be in Campania?","a":"Because they meant to punish the Campanians for helping others against them and then calling in help themselves, so their legions came there first."},{"q":"Why did Rome make peace after winning three battles?","a":"Because the next year its own soldiers wintering at Capua and Suessa were softened by the city and plotted to seize it, and that mutiny with unrest at Rome disposed the senate to end it."},{"q":"Why do the terms not fit the story of a humbled Samnium?","a":"Because the answer given was that nothing hindered the Samnites from making peace or war with the Sidicini as they pleased, and they marched on them at once with the same troops."}]},
{"id":"rm-153","num":153,"category":"Rome","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke out in 326 BCE over Palaepolis and Neapolis, into which Samnite and Nolan troops had been put after Rome declared war on those Greek cities.","questions":["Rome lost an army in the pass at Caudium during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the news carried Satricum, Fregellae and Sora over to the enemy.","Livy ends the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by saying the old treaty was restored in 304 BCE, a claim hard to credit when Samnium had been beaten and its capital sacked twice."],"answer":"Second Samnite War","answerText":"Second Samnite War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">326 – 304 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Caudium</span><span class=\"dt-v\">321 BCE, an army taken in the pass</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bovianum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">sacked in 311 BCE and again in 305</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Second Samnite War</b> broke out in 326 BCE over the Greek cities of Palaepolis and Neapolis, into which the Samnites and the Nolans put a strong garrison after Rome had declared war on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome had sent envoys first and been answered insolently, the Samnites denying the charges and saying they had no reason to distrust their own strength if it came to fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Palaepolis fell to a plot from within, its Samnite garrison drawn down to the shore by a false errand while the Romans were let into the upper town; Neapolis escaped by taking Rome’s alliance and kept it ever after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome gained at the same time the alliance of the Lucanians and the Apulians, peoples with whom Livy says it had until then had no dealings, friendly or hostile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tarentum soon talked the Lucanians out of it, but the Apulians held, and the war was fought in two halves: on the Campanian frontier and up the Volturnus into Samnium, and in Apulia for those allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The early campaigns were small, and though the Samnites twice sued for peace they got only a year’s truce and did not keep it, so no real impression had been made on their strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 321 BCE they took a Roman army in the pass at Caudium under Gaius Pontius, a year Livy calls memorable for the disaster and the capitulation made there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The defeat shook Rome’s allies, the colonies of Satricum, Fregellae and Sora went over, and a second Samnite victory at Lautulae endangered Roman power in Campania for a moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome recovered, reduced Apulia, carried the war into Lucania and took Bovianum, the Pentrian capital, in 311 BCE and again in 305, while a fresh Etruscan war drew off part of its strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peace came in 304 BCE on terms nobody can now state: Livy says the old treaty was restored, which is hard to credit, and it is likelier that Samnium acknowledged Roman supremacy in some form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Samnium,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dsamnium-geo. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.26, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D26. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.45, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Neapolis","at":[14.2681,40.8518],"zoom":8,"within":"Neapolis"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Rothert%2C_Bd1Bl16K2_-_Zweiter_Samniterkrieg.jpg/1280px-Rothert%2C_Bd1Bl16K2_-_Zweiter_Samniterkrieg.jpg","title":"The second war mapped in a German atlas of 1906","desc":"Eduard Rothert's plate for the war, colouring Rome and its allies, Samnium and its allies, and Campania as the country in dispute, with campaign arrows dated from Caudium in 321 through Lautulae, Sutrium and Lake Vadimon to Bovianum in 305. The arrows and the German commentary are the compiler's reading, not an ancient record.","credit":"Eduard Rothert, Karten und Skizzen aus der Geschichte des Altertums (1906); public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rothert,_Bd1Bl16K2_-_Zweiter_Samniterkrieg.jpg","alt":"A coloured historical map of central Italy, Rome and its territory in orange, Samnium in green and Campania in yellow, with red campaign arrows carrying dates and two blocks of German text below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the war have two separate theatres?","a":"Because Rome had gained the Apulians as allies at the same time, so the fighting ran on the Campanian frontier and up the Volturnus into Samnium, and in Apulia for those allies."},{"q":"Why had the early campaigns settled nothing?","a":"Because the Samnites twice sued for peace and got only a year’s truce which they did not keep, so no real impression had been made on their strength."},{"q":"Why can nobody state the terms it ended on?","a":"Because Livy says the old treaty was restored, which is hard to credit after so long a war, so it is likelier that Samnium acknowledged Roman supremacy in some form."}]},
{"id":"rm-154","num":154,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"In 321 BCE a Roman consular army marching to relieve Luceria was trapped between two blocked defiles at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and made to pass under the yoke.","questions":["Gaius Pontius lured the consuls into the pass called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with ten men dressed as shepherds, all repeating that his army was away in Apulia besieging Luceria.","Asked what to do with the army caught at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Herennius advised his son either to free every Roman unharmed or to kill them all, and rejected any middle course."],"answer":"Caudine Forks","answerText":"Caudine Forks","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The disaster</span><span class=\"dt-v\">321 BCE, at a pass in Samnium</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the army disarmed and sent under the yoke</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Caudine Forks</b> were a pass in Samnium where a Roman consular army was surrounded and forced to capitulate in 321 BCE, during the Second Samnite War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy describes two deep and narrow defiles, wooded hills rising on either side, with a watered grassy plain lying between them and a road running its length.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Samnite commander Gaius Pontius sent ten soldiers disguised as shepherds to graze cattle near the Roman outposts, every one of them telling the same story about his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That story put the Samnites far away in Apulia besieging Luceria, and the consuls, choosing speed, took the short road through the passes rather than the longer coastal route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the far end they found the way blocked by felled trees and piled rock, and when they turned back the entrance behind them had been closed as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pontius, holding an army he had caught rather than beaten, wrote for counsel to his father Herennius, who was carried to the camp in a wagon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herennius advised first that the whole force be let go unharmed, then, asked again, that every man be killed, and of a middle course he said it neither wins friends nor removes enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His son took neither: the army was disarmed and sent under the yoke in a single garment apiece, and Rome was to quit Samnium and withdraw its colonists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has the consuls pass out first and the rest by rank, jeered at and some of them killed, and the Campanians afterwards send lictors, arms, horses and provisions to men who would not enter Capua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rome repudiated the capitulation and fought on, and Smith held that the circumstances and character of the disaster are greatly disguised in the surviving accounts, which report no lasting check to Roman arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Caudine Forks","at":[14.5931,41.0244],"zoom":8,"within":"Caudium"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Stretta_di_Arpaia_da_Forchia.jpg/1280px-Stretta_di_Arpaia_da_Forchia.jpg","title":"The Stretta di Arpaia, seen from Forchia","desc":"The narrow valley of Arpaia in the province of Benevento, commonly identified with the pass where the Roman army was trapped. Wooded slopes close in from both sides on a flat floor, which is the ground Livy describes.","credit":"Decan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stretta_di_Arpaia_da_Forchia.jpg","alt":"A green valley floor of scrub and small trees running between two hills, the bare grey slope of one on the left and a wooded ridge on the right, with a village in the gap between them under a blue sky."},"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D2. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.4, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D6. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Samnium,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dsamnium-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the consuls take the short road into the passes?","a":"Because ten Samnite soldiers disguised as shepherds all told the same story, putting their army far away in Apulia besieging Luceria."},{"q":"Why did Herennius reject his own middle course?","a":"Because letting the army go would win friends and killing it would remove enemies, where disgracing it neither wins friends nor removes enemies."},{"q":"Why is the disaster thought to be disguised in the accounts?","a":"Because the surviving narratives report no lasting check to Roman arms, which sits oddly with a consular army surrendered and sent under the yoke."}]},
{"id":"rm-155","num":155,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy calls each of the fifteen companies of hastati a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and gives the principes fifteen more of them with fifteen behind the standards for the triarii.","questions":["Polybius gives each line of the legion ten of these units, and every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had two centurions and two optiones so that it should never be left without a commander.","The word comes from manus and first meant a wisp of hay on a pole, the earliest Roman standard, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was simply the men who served under one ensign."],"answer":"maniple","answerText":"maniple","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Livy's date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">340 BCE, in the Latin War</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">when the phalanx gave way to maniples</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>maniple</b> was the tactical subdivision of the Roman legion in the centuries between the abandoning of the phalanx and the rise of the cohort as the standard unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word derives from <i>manus</i>, and Roman tradition held that a wisp of hay tied to a pole made the first standard, so a maniple was the men who served under one ensign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy sets the change in the war against the Latins, when the phalanx was given up and the infantry distributed into companies drawn up with short intervals between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his account fifteen maniples of <i>hastati</i> stood in front, fifteen of <i>principes</i> behind them, and fifteen more behind the standards held the <i>triarii</i>, <i>rorarii</i> and <i>accensi</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius, writing later, sorts the men by age and property into <i>velites</i>, <i>hastati</i>, <i>principes</i> and <i>triarii</i>, the last always 600 strong and the two middle grades 1,200 each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Each grade was divided into ten maniples with two centurions, two <i>optiones</i> and two standard-bearers apiece, so that a maniple should never be left without a commander.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius wanted centurions who were steady rather than showy, slow to engage without cause, and willing to die holding their ground against superior numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A man of the front grades carried the curved <i>scutum</i>, a Spanish sword, two <i>pila</i>, a helmet and greaves, with three upright feathers a cubit long to make him look twice his height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The intervals were the point of the arrangement: a line that failed fell back through the gaps in the one behind, and the <i>triarii</i> closed up as the last resort, which gave Rome a proverb for desperate straits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two accounts cannot be reconciled, and by Polybius' day the names had drifted from the weapons they once described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","warfare","history","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Campus Martius","at":[12.4772,41.8978],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Altar_of_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_%28detail%29.jpg/1280px-Altar_of_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_%28detail%29.jpg","title":"Roman soldiers on the Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus","desc":"A detail of the marble relief in the Louvre, carved in the 2nd century BCE while the legion still fought in maniples. Two soldiers stand in mail with plumed helmets, tall oval shields and swords at the right hip, close to the panoply Polybius describes.","credit":"Miketaylor30, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altar_of_Domitius_Ahenobarbus_%28detail%29.jpg","alt":"A weathered white marble relief showing two soldiers in mail shirts and crested helmets standing behind large oval shields, one adjusting his helmet, with draped civilian figures at either edge."},"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D8. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D21. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.23, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D23. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.24, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D24. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Exercitus,\" in <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Exercitus.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did each maniple have two of every officer?","a":"Because the doubling was deliberate, so that a maniple should never be left without a commander."},{"q":"Why were the intervals the point of the formation?","a":"Because a line that failed fell back through the gaps in the one behind, and the triarii closed up as the last resort — which gave Rome a proverb for desperate straits."},{"q":"Why did Polybius want unshowy centurions?","a":"Because he wanted men steady rather than spirited, slow to engage without cause, and willing to die holding their ground against superior numbers."}]},
{"id":"rm-156","num":156,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A Lucanian embassy complaining of Samnite pressure brought Rome into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran from 298 to 290 BCE and closed a struggle of more than forty years.","questions":["During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Samnites drew the Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls into a league, so that for several campaigns the Tiber valley mattered more than Samnium itself.","The levy at Aquilonia belongs to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Livy has an aged priest read an oath from a linen book while those who refused it were killed at the altar."],"answer":"Third Samnite War","answerText":"Third Samnite War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">298 – 290 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">ended by Manius Curius Dentatus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Third Samnite War</b>, fought from 298 to 290 BCE, was the last of the three and the one that ended more than forty years of intermittent fighting between Rome and the Samnites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy has it begin with a Lucanian embassy complaining that the Samnites had tried to draw them into an alliance and had attacked them when they refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome was already at war with the Etruscans and Umbrians, and Smith reads the move against Lucania as an attempt to open a second front while Roman attention lay elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Samnites then built a league with the Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls, so that for several campaigns the fighting in Samnium mattered less than the fighting in the valley of the Tiber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That confederacy was broken at Sentinum in 295, where the Gauls and Samnites were beaten together and Gellius Egnatius, who had assembled it, was killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Samnites fought on regardless, and in 293 concentrated 40,000 men at Aquilonia under a levy that made failure to appear a capital offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy describes an enclosure of linen cloth in the middle of the camp where an aged priest read an oath from an old linen book, and the men who refused it were killed beside the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That army was beaten all the same, and the Etruscans, acting on a secret understanding with Samnium, chose the moment when both consuls were away to reopen their own war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausing at this point, Livy writes that the wars had run for six-and-forty years, and that neither the defeats nor the toil had been able to break the spirit of that people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roman historians close the struggle in 290, when Manius Curius Dentatus triumphed; the Samnites appear again after 282, but as auxiliaries rather than as principals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Perusia","at":[12.3888,43.1122],"zoom":8,"within":"Perusia"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Rothert%2C_Bd1Bl16K3_-_Dritter_Samniterkrieg.jpg/1280px-Rothert%2C_Bd1Bl16K3_-_Dritter_Samniterkrieg.jpg","title":"The third war mapped in a German atlas of 1906","desc":"Roman territory is washed in pink and Samnium with its allies in green, so the plate shows at a glance how far the coalition reached, from Etruria and Umbria down through Samnium to Lucania. Red arrows mark the campaigns and the dated battles include Sentinum in 295 and Aquilonia in 293.","credit":"Eduard Rothert, Karten und Skizzen aus der Geschichte des Altertums (1906); public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rothert,_Bd1Bl16K3_-_Dritter_Samniterkrieg.jpg","alt":"A coloured historical map of Italy with a pink band along the west coast for Roman territory and a large green area covering Etruria, Umbria, Samnium and Lucania, crossed by red campaign arrows and labelled with Latin place names."},"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.31, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D31. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.38, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D38. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.45, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D45. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Samnium,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dsamnium-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fighting matter more in the Tiber valley than in Samnium?","a":"Because the Samnites built a league with the Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls, so for several campaigns the confederacy rather than Samnium itself was the war."},{"q":"Why did an oath have to be sworn under compulsion at Aquilonia?","a":"Because the levy made failure to appear a capital offence, and in an enclosure of linen an aged priest read the oath from an old linen book, those refusing killed beside the altar."},{"q":"Why does Livy pause to remark on the Samnites’ spirit?","a":"Because the wars had by then run six-and-forty years, and neither the defeats nor the toil had been able to break it."}]},
{"id":"rm-157","num":157,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome broke the coalition of Samnites, Gauls, Etruscans and Umbrians at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 295 BCE, after two of the four had been drawn away by raids near Clusium.","questions":["Publius Decius Mus devoted himself to death at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when Gaulish chariots panicked his cavalry, following the example his father had set a generation before.","Livy reports 25,000 enemy dead at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and complains that some writers had swollen the opposing army to a million foot and a thousand war chariots."],"answer":"Battle of Sentinum","answerText":"Battle of Sentinum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">295 BCE, in Umbria</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Decius Mus devoted himself and was killed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Sentinum</b>, fought in 295 BCE in Umbria, broke the coalition of Samnites, Gauls, Etruscans and Umbrians that had been assembled against Rome in the Third Samnite War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The four peoples had agreed not to camp together: the Gauls would take the brunt beside the Samnites while the Etruscans and Umbrians fell on the Roman camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three deserters brought the plan to Fabius, and orders sent to two other commanders to ravage the country by Clusium drew the Etruscans and Umbrians away before the fighting began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has a wolf and a hind run out between the lines, the hind speared by the Gauls and the wolf let through the Roman ranks, which a soldier in the front rank read as a sign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fabius held the right with the first and third legions against the Samnites, and Decius the left with the fifth and sixth against the Gauls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Fabius fought defensively on purpose, knowing that both enemies attacked hardest at the start and that their strength drained away as the day wore on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Decius attacked instead, and on a third cavalry charge his horsemen were broken by armed men on chariots and wagons whose noise threw the Roman horses into panic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Unable to halt the rout, he called on the name of his father, devoted himself to death for the army, and the left wing steadied behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fabius then took the Samnites in flank, drove them past the Gauls to their camp, where Gellius Egnatius fell, and sent Campanian horse round to strike the Gauls from behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy counts 25,000 enemy dead and 8,000 taken against 8,700 Romans, complains that some writers had swollen the enemy to a million foot, and where the field lay is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Sentinum","at":[12.8622,43.4267],"zoom":8,"within":"Sentinum"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Sassoferrato_Veduta_del_paese.jpg/1280px-Sassoferrato_Veduta_del_paese.jpg","title":"Sassoferrato, the town beside the site of Sentinum","desc":"A 19th-century engraving of the hill town in the Marche below which the Roman town of Sentinum stood, with the open valley floor in front of it. Livy places the battle only in the district of Sentinum, and no source fixes the field more closely than that.","credit":"Giuseppe Barberis; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sassoferrato_Veduta_del_paese.jpg","alt":"A black and white engraving of an Italian hill town, houses climbing a ridge to a ruined castle keep and a church at the summit, with a broad empty valley floor and a track in the foreground."},"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.27, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D27. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.28, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.29, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D29. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.30, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Sentinum,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dsentinum-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the coalition split its forces?","a":"Because the four peoples had agreed that the Gauls and Samnites would take the brunt while the Etruscans and Umbrians fell on the Roman camp."},{"q":"Why did Fabius fight defensively while Decius attacked?","a":"Because he knew both enemies attacked hardest at the start and that their strength drained away as the day wore on, so holding cost him nothing."},{"q":"Why does Livy complain about other writers’ figures?","a":"Because some had swollen the enemy to a million foot, where he counts 25,000 dead and 8,000 taken against 8,700 Roman losses."}]},
{"id":"rm-158","num":158,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome completed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Volsinii in the year before the First Punic War, having reduced the cities one at a time over more than a century since the fall of Veii.","questions":["During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a consul crossed the Ciminian forest in 310 BCE, a wood Livy calls more frightful and impassable than the German forests, through which no trader had ventured.","Perusia, Cortona and Arretium bought a thirty-year truce during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the cities that fought on were beaten at Lake Vadimon and afterwards at Sentinum."],"answer":"Roman conquest of Etruria","answerText":"Roman conquest of Etruria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Veii falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE, the first city taken</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ciminian forest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">crossed in 310 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Volsinii</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in arms in 265 BCE, the last to submit</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman conquest of Etruria</b> ran from the fall of Veii in 396 BCE to the submission of Volsinii on the eve of the First Punic War, and it took the cities piecemeal rather than at a stroke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rome pushed north to Sutrium and Nepete after the Gaulish sack and planted colonies in both, which became the barrier against Etruria on that side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tarquinii and Volsinii led the resistance in turn, but a forty-year truce agreed with the Tarquinians in 351 seems to have been kept, and no Etruscan war is heard of again until 311.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 310 a consul crossed the Ciminian forest, which Livy calls more frightful and impassable than the German forests and through which no trader had yet ventured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy has a kinsman of the consul, brought up at Caere and fluent in Etruscan, go ahead disguised as a shepherd and come back with an alliance with Camerinum in Umbria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He adds, hedging it, that there was authority for saying Roman boys were then taught Etruscan literature as they were taught Greek in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A night attack on the Etruscan camp followed, after which Perusia, Cortona and Arretium, the three leading cantons, asked for peace and were given a thirty-year truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest were beaten at Lake Vadimon, where Livy says the Etruscans fought with swords from the first exchange and seemed some new people rather than the one so often defeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those cities joined the Samnites and even the Senonian Gauls before being broken at Sentinum in 295 and at Vadimon again in 283, and Volsinii was the last to submit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rome razed Volsinii and moved its people, telling itself that slaves had seized the city and that it had freed them, and how the other cities were treated is almost wholly unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Sutrium","at":[12.2278,42.2439],"zoom":8,"within":"Sutrium"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Trachyte_boulders_inside_the_Ancient_Beech_Forest_of_the_Monte_Cimino%2C_Lazio%2C_Italy.jpg/1280px-Trachyte_boulders_inside_the_Ancient_Beech_Forest_of_the_Monte_Cimino%2C_Lazio%2C_Italy.jpg","title":"Inside the beech forest on Monte Cimino","desc":"Trachyte boulders under the canopy of the old beech wood on Monte Cimino, in the range Livy calls the Ciminian forest. It is the sort of ground that made the crossing of 310 BCE worth recording as a feat.","credit":"Albarubescens, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trachyte_boulders_inside_the_Ancient_Beech_Forest_of_the_Monte_Cimino,_Lazio,_Italy.jpg","alt":"A large grey boulder blotched with moss and lichen resting on smaller rocks under the green canopy of a beech wood, with slender pale trunks rising around it."},"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.37, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D37. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.39, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D39. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Etruria,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Detruria-geo. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Volsinii,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dvolsinii-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the conquest take a century and a half?","a":"Because it took the cities piecemeal rather than at a stroke: Tarquinii and Volsinii led resistance in turn, and a forty-year truce agreed in 351 seems to have been kept."},{"q":"Why was crossing the Ciminian forest so remarkable?","a":"Because Livy calls it more frightful and impassable than the German forests, through which no trader had yet ventured."},{"q":"Why is Rome’s account of taking Volsinii worth noticing?","a":"Because it razed the city and moved its people while telling itself that slaves had seized it and that it had freed them."}]},
{"id":"rm-159","num":159,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Fabius beat the Umbrians at Mevania in 308 BCE during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after they had boasted of marching on Rome while a consul stood behind them.","questions":["A treaty of 299 BCE opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome wanting a friend on the Adriatic flank while the Etruscans were trying to hire the Senonian Gauls.","Two consuls finished the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a single campaign in 268 BCE, once the Senonian Gauls were no longer dangerous enough to make Picenum worth courting."],"answer":"Roman conquest of Umbria and Picenum","answerText":"Roman conquest of Umbria and Picenum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mevania</span><span class=\"dt-v\">308 BCE, the Umbrians beaten</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Picentine treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">299 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Picenum reduced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">268 BCE, in one campaign</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman conquest of Umbria and Picenum</b> brought the country on the Adriatic side of the Apennines under Rome between the end of the 4th century and 268 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Umbrian people had no common government, its tribes taking different lines, so that some fought beside the Etruscans while others made their own terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fabius had already reached them by sending a scout through Etruria, and the Camertes promised support if a Roman army could get across the Ciminian forest to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 308 the Umbrians called out every fighting man and talked of leaving one consul behind them and marching on Rome, a threat taken seriously in a city that remembered the Gauls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fabius came by forced marches to Mevania and broke them there, and Livy says his men snatched the standards from the bearers rather than fighting them as armed enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That defeat brought in the rest of the tribes, Ocriculum on notably favourable terms, and after Sentinum in 295 the Umbrians never appear in arms as a people again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Picenum came into Rome's orbit by treaty in 299, when the Etruscans were trying to hire the Senonian Gauls and Rome wanted a friend on that flank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy says the Gauls refused, holding that their pay had bought only their forbearance, and asked for land instead, which the Etruscans would not give to men they thought savage neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The treaty held while the Senones were dangerous; once they were not, two consuls reduced Picenum in a single campaign in 268, and why the war began is not recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Colonies followed at Sena Gallica and Ariminum, and how the Umbrians were received is unknown beyond that the terms differed from city to city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Mevania","at":[12.6111,42.9333],"zoom":8,"within":"Mevania"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Panorama_dell%27Alta_valle_del_Tronto_046.jpg/1280px-Panorama_dell%27Alta_valle_del_Tronto_046.jpg","title":"The upper valley of the Tronto, in Picene country","desc":"The Tronto is the river of Asculum, which the Picentes seem always to have treated as their capital. The village is modern; what the photograph shows is the broken hill country of the region, which Rome courted as an ally for twenty years before reducing it in one campaign.","credit":"Infinitispazi, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_dell%27Alta_valle_del_Tronto_046.jpg","alt":"A stone village of tiled roofs and a clock tower standing on a wooded spur, with steep green Apennine ridges falling away on either side and a valley road winding into the distance."},"sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.41, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D41. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 10.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D10%3Achapter%3D10. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Umbria,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dumbria-geo. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Picenum,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dpicenum-geo. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.36, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D36. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Umbrian tribes take different sides?","a":"Because the people had no common government, so some fought beside the Etruscans while others made their own terms with Rome."},{"q":"Why was the threat of 308 taken so seriously?","a":"Because the Umbrians talked of leaving one consul behind them and marching on Rome, in a city that remembered the Gauls."},{"q":"Why did the Gauls refuse to fight for Etruscan pay?","a":"Because they held that their pay had bought only their forbearance, and asked for land instead, which the Etruscans would not give to men they thought savage neighbours."}]},
{"id":"rm-160","num":160,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome destroyed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 283 BCE after Roman envoys were murdered, driving the survivors off the Adriatic coast and planting Sena Gallica on the land they had held.","questions":["Polybius puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> last in the Gallic settlement of the Po plain, on the Adriatic shore below the Lingones, living in open villages with no permanent buildings.","A thirty-year treaty with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke in 299 BCE, when they paid off a Transalpine horde and turned it against Rome, marching through Etruria in its company."],"answer":"Senones","answerText":"Senones","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty broken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">299 BCE, after thirty years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arretium</span><span class=\"dt-v\">besieged in 283 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">283 BCE, and the tribe expelled</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Senones</b> were the Gallic people of the Adriatic coast south of the Po, the southernmost of the tribes that took the plain from the Etruscans, and Rome destroyed them in 283 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius places them last in that settlement, below the Lingones on the sea, and describes people living in open villages without permanent buildings, sleeping on straw and following only war and farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He has these Gauls beat Rome at the Allia and hold the city except the Capitol, then make terms and go home when the Veneti invaded their own territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A treaty followed and held thirty years, until in 299 they bought off a Transalpine horde with gifts and appeals to kinship and turned it against Rome instead, marching through Etruria in its company.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They fought Rome again beside the Samnites near Camerium and were broken with the rest of the coalition at Sentinum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 283 they besieged Arretium, an Etruscan town under Roman protection, and beat the army that came to relieve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sources then part company over the embassy: Polybius has envoys sent afterwards to ransom prisoners and murdered, where another account has them sent first to warn the Senones off and killed against the law of nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Either way Rome answered by destroying them, the consul entering their country, burning what lay in front of him, putting the men to the sword and carrying off the women and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The survivors were driven out, and Rome planted Sena Gallica on the coast, the first colony it ever founded in Gaul and named after the tribe it replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Boii rose next in fear of the same fate and were beaten at Lake Vadimon and again the year after, which Polybius thought left Rome free for Pyrrhus and then Carthage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["people","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Sena Gallica","at":[13.2167,43.7167],"zoom":8,"within":"Sena Gallica"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Torques_in_oro_da_Montefortino_di_Arcevia_1_-_Museo_archeologico_nazionale_delle_Marche.jpg/1280px-Torques_in_oro_da_Montefortino_di_Arcevia_1_-_Museo_archeologico_nazionale_delle_Marche.jpg","title":"A gold torque from the Montefortino necropolis","desc":"A twisted gold neck-ring with worked terminals, from the cemetery at Montefortino di Arcevia in the country the Senones held, now in the archaeological museum at Ancona. Bronze vessels and a second torque from the same burials lie in the case below it.","credit":"Accurimbono, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torques_in_oro_da_Montefortino_di_Arcevia_1_-_Museo_archeologico_nazionale_delle_Marche.jpg","alt":"A gold neck-ring of twisted rope-like form with two decorated end-pieces almost meeting, displayed upright on black velvet in a glass museum case with bronze bowls and other gold ornaments beneath."},"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.17, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D17. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.18, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D18. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.19, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D19. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.20, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Gallia Cisalpina,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dgallia-cisalpina-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the thirty-year treaty end?","a":"Because in 299 they bought off a Transalpine horde with gifts and appeals to kinship and turned it against Rome instead, marching through Etruria in its company."},{"q":"Why does it matter which embassy was killed?","a":"Because Polybius has envoys sent afterwards to ransom prisoners and murdered, where another account has them sent first to warn the Senones off — one makes Rome’s answer reprisal, the other outrage."},{"q":"Why did the Boii rise immediately afterwards?","a":"Because they feared the same fate, the men having been put to the sword, the women and children carried off and the survivors driven out."}]},
{"id":"rm-161","num":161,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Two consular armies trapped a Gallic host returning from a raid at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 225 BCE, one consul dying for a hill above the road they were marching on.","questions":["The Gaesatae stood naked in the front rank at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Polybius puts down to bravado, and their small shields left them open to the Roman pilum.","Forty thousand Gauls died at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and ten thousand were taken, one king captured and the other killing himself, and their gold neck-chains went up on the Capitol."],"answer":"Battle of Telamon","answerText":"Battle of Telamon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">225 BCE, on the Etruscan coast</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">one consul killed, one Gallic king taken</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Telamon</b> of 225 BCE, fought on the coast of Etruria where Polybius places it though Frontinus put it near Populonia, destroyed a Gallic army caught between two Roman consular armies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Insubres and Boii had raised that army by hiring the Gaesatae from beyond the Alps, whose name Polybius derives from a word meaning to serve for hire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their kings Concolitanus and Aneroestes were promised gold at once and the wealth of Rome afterwards, and reminded that Gauls had once held the city for seven months and left it unconquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome enrolled legions and pushed troops out to the frontier before the Gauls had stirred, and Polybius says the alarm let Carthage build up its power in Iberia unwatched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The consul Gaius Atilius, landing at Pisae from Sardinia, learnt from prisoners that the Gauls lay between his army and that of Lucius Aemilius behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Gauls drew up facing both ways, Gaesatae and Insubres to the rear and Taurisci and Boii to the front, with the waggons on the wings and the plunder heaped on a hill under guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Gaesatae stripped and stood in front naked, which Polybius puts down to vanity and bravado while giving their own reason, that brambles would catch in clothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Atilius was killed fighting for a hill above the road and his head was carried to the Gallic king, though his cavalry took the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pilum broke the Gaesatae, whose shields did not cover them, and Polybius judges the Roman sword the better weapon, the Gallic one able to cut but not thrust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Forty thousand were killed and ten thousand taken with Concolitanus among them, Aneroestes killed himself, and the captured gold neck-chains were hung on the Capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Telamon","at":[11.1339,42.5533],"zoom":8,"within":"Telamon"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Dying_Gaul.jpg/1280px-Dying_Gaul.jpg","title":"The Dying Gaul, in the Capitoline Museums","desc":"A Roman marble copy of a Hellenistic statue made for a different war, the victories of Pergamon over the Galatians, so it is not a picture of Telamon. What it does show is how the classical world pictured these enemies: naked, with matted hair and a moustache, and a twisted torque at the throat of the kind Aemilius hung on the Capitol.","credit":"BeBo86, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dying_Gaul.jpg","alt":"A white marble statue of a naked wounded man propped on one arm on a round base, his head bowed, a twisted metal ring at his neck, with a sword and a horn lying beside him against a black background."},"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.22, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D22. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.27, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D27. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.28, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D28. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.30, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D30. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.31, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D31. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Telamon,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dtelamon-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Gauls have to draw up facing two ways?","a":"Because one consular army had landed at Pisae behind them while the other lay in front, so they set the Gaesatae and Insubres to the rear and the Taurisci and Boii to the front."},{"q":"Why did the Gaesatae fight naked?","a":"Because their own reason was that brambles would catch in clothing, though Polybius puts it down to vanity and bravado — and the pilum then broke them, their shields not covering them."},{"q":"Why did the Roman sword prove the better weapon?","a":"Because the Gallic one could cut but not thrust, which is why Polybius judges the comparison at all."}]},
{"id":"rm-162","num":162,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The storming of Mediolanum in 222 BCE ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Insubres submitted without terms after the consuls had pressed that their offers of peace be refused.","questions":["Facing destruction during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Insubres took the golden standards called the immovables from the temple of Minerva and mustered fifty thousand men.","Rome planted Placentia and Cremona in 218 BCE to hold the ground won in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Hannibal crossed into Italy that year hoping to raise the same Gauls against it."],"answer":"Roman conquest of Cisalpine Gaul","answerText":"Roman conquest of Cisalpine Gaul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Insubres beaten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">223 BCE, in a great battle</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mediolanum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">stormed in 222 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Colonies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Placentia and Cremona, 218 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman conquest of Cisalpine Gaul</b> followed the victory at Telamon, which Polybius says gave Rome the hope of clearing the Celts out of the valley of the Po altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 223 the consuls Publius Furius and Gaius Flaminius crossed into Insubrian country by way of the Anamares and the Cenomani, both allies of Rome, and set about wasting the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seeing that nothing would turn the Romans from destroying them, the Insubrian chiefs took the golden standards called the immovables from the temple of Minerva and mustered fifty thousand men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were beaten, and the next year sent embassies offering unlimited submission, which the new consuls Marcus Claudius Marcellus and Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio pressed the senate to refuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Frustrated in that, the Insubres took active measures to hire thirty thousand more Gaesatae from the Rhone country, and held themselves ready for the attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The consuls besieged Acerrae, and the Insubres crossed the Po to lay siege to Clastidium as a diversion, which Marcellus relieved with his cavalry and light infantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Acerrae fell with its stores of corn, and Mediolanum, the chief place of the Insubres, was taken by storm, at which they submitted without terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Marcellus triumphed in 221 carrying the spolia opima, having killed with his own hand a Gallic prince named Virdomarus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 218 Rome planted two Latin colonies of six thousand men each, Placentia on the south side of the Po and Cremona a little downstream on the north bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It did not settle the country, since Hannibal crossed into Italy that same year hoping to raise these Gauls against Rome, and Polybius wrote his account so that later readers would not be dismayed by such invasions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Mediolanum","at":[9.19,45.4642],"zoom":8,"within":"Mediolanum"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Casteggio%2C_veduta_del_paese_nel_1865_%28xilografia%29.jpg/1280px-Casteggio%2C_veduta_del_paese_nel_1865_%28xilografia%29.jpg","title":"Casteggio, the Roman Clastidium, in an 1865 wood engraving","desc":"The town stands on a low ridge above open fields between the Po and the Apennine foothills. It was the place the Insubres besieged as a diversion in 222 BCE, and where Marcellus, coming up with cavalry and light troops, won the spolia opima.","credit":"Unknown engraver, 1865; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casteggio,_veduta_del_paese_nel_1865_%28xilografia%29.jpg","alt":"A black and white wood engraving of an Italian town of low tiled roofs strung along a ridge with a church spire at its crest, seen across bare fields with three pollarded trees in the foreground."},"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.31, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D31. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.32, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D32. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.34, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D34. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.35, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D35. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Gallia Cisalpina,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dgallia-cisalpina-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Insubres take out their sacred standards?","a":"Because they saw that nothing would turn the Romans from destroying them, so they took the golden immovables from the temple of Minerva and mustered fifty thousand men."},{"q":"Why did Rome refuse an unlimited submission?","a":"Because the new consuls pressed the senate to refuse it, and the Insubres, frustrated in that, hired thirty thousand more Gaesatae from the Rhone country instead."},{"q":"Why did the conquest not settle the country?","a":"Because Hannibal crossed into Italy in the same year the colonies were planted, hoping to raise these Gauls against Rome."}]},
{"id":"rm-163","num":163,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A Spartan colony on a peninsula closing its harbour, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the greatest Greek city of southern Italy and gave its name to the gulf it stood on.","questions":["The Partheniae, Spartans denied citizen rights after the First Messenian War, followed Phalanthus to found <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the gulf, having been sent to Delphi to consult about a colony.","Rome declared war on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 282 BCE after Roman ships were attacked in the gulf, Thurii was seized from its Roman garrison, and Roman envoys were treated with contumely."],"answer":"Tarentum","answerText":"Tarentum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome declares war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">282 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">after ships attacked and envoys insulted</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pyrrhus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3,000 men sent ahead under Milo</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tarentum</b> was the greatest Greek city of southern Italy, a Spartan colony on a peninsula closing the mouth of a long shallow harbour on the gulf that took its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antiochus, quoted by Strabo, says its founders were the Partheniae, Spartans born during the First Messenian War and denied citizen rights, who plotted against the free citizens and were found out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their leader Phalanthus was sent to Delphi instead of punished, and the god gave him Satyrium and the rich land of Taras, telling him to become a bane to the Iapygians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo describes a spacious market holding a bronze colossus of Zeus, the largest anywhere but Rhodes, with a very beautiful gymnasium and the acropolis above the harbour mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its policy towards Rome was to encourage others and send nothing, and Livy has it hold out vain hopes of help to Palaepolis and then blame the Palaepolitans for giving in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In 321 a Tarentine embassy ordered Rome and Samnium alike to stop fighting and threatened to join whichever side refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The consul Papirius kept them waiting, took the auspices and marched, saying that a people too torn by faction to manage their own affairs had no business prescribing peace to others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A treaty later barred Roman warships from passing the Lacinian cape, and when a Roman squadron entered the gulf the Tarentines attacked it, sank four ships and took one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They then seized Thurii, an ally Rome had just saved from the Lucanians, expelled its garrison and treated the envoys who came to complain with contumely, so Rome declared war in 282.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are usually pictured as too luxurious to defend themselves, which Smith calls much exaggerated, since they fielded cavalry and a phalanx of their own, the Leucaspids, named from their white shields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["city","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Tarentum","at":[17.24,40.4644],"zoom":8,"within":"Tarentum"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Colonne_Doriche_Tempio_di_Poseidone_Taranto.jpg/960px-Colonne_Doriche_Tempio_di_Poseidone_Taranto.jpg","title":"The Doric columns of the temple at Taranto","desc":"Two heavy archaic columns, lit at night and hemmed in by later buildings, are all that stands above ground of the Greek city. They are commonly assigned to a temple of Poseidon and are the oldest Greek architecture in southern Italy.","credit":"Eziobruno, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colonne_Doriche_Tempio_di_Poseidone_Taranto.jpg","alt":"A monochrome photograph of two squat fluted stone columns with wide flat capitals standing on a low platform, floodlit from below, with the walls and balconies of modern apartment buildings pressing close behind them."},"sources":["William Smith, ed., \"Tarentum,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dtarentum-geo. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>The Geography of Strabo</i> 6.3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 8.27, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D8%3Achapter%3D27. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 9.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0026%3Abook%3D9%3Achapter%3D14. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Thurii,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dthurii-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the consul refuse the Tarentine embassy a hearing?","a":"Because he said a people too torn by faction to manage their own affairs had no business prescribing peace to others, and marched after taking the auspices."},{"q":"Why did the attack on the Roman squadron amount to a war?","a":"Because a treaty barred Roman warships from passing the Lacinian cape, and the Tarentines then seized Thurii, an ally Rome had just saved, and treated the envoys with contumely."},{"q":"Why is the picture of a city too soft to fight exaggerated?","a":"Because it fielded cavalry and a phalanx of its own, the Leucaspids, named from their white shields."}]},
{"id":"rm-164","num":164,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Tarentum invited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into Italy after Rome declared war on the city, and the Hellenistic army he brought was the first Rome had ever faced.","questions":["Cineas asked <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> what he would do after conquering Rome, then after Sicily, then after Africa, and urged him to be content with what he already held.","The oracle at Dodona told <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that if he crossed into Italy Romans should conquer, and he read the words the way he wanted and sailed without waiting for spring."],"answer":"Pyrrhus of Epirus","answerText":"Pyrrhus of Epirus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossed to Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">280 BCE, invited by Tarentum</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">20 elephants and some 25,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Pyrrhus of Epirus</b> was the Greek king who brought a Hellenistic army into Italy at the invitation of Tarentum, the first such army Rome ever had to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dio says he was king of Epirus but had made much of the Greek world his own, courted by the Aetolians, by Philip of Macedon and by the chieftains of Illyricum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was the last in a line of foreign generals Tarentum hired, after Archidamus, Alexander the Molossian, Cleonymus and Agathocles, and Strabo says the city could never obey the men it called in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch has a reveller named Meton break into the assembly to warn the Tarentines they would have a different life and diet once Pyrrhus was in the city, and be thrown out for saying it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cineas the Thessalian asked him what he would do after conquering Rome, then after Sicily, then after Africa, and urged him to be content with what he already held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> He put the question to the oracle at Dodona, which answered that if he crossed into Italy Romans should conquer, and he read it the way he wished and would not wait for spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cineas went ahead with three thousand men and Pyrrhus followed with twenty elephants, three thousand horse, twenty thousand foot, two thousand archers and five hundred slingers, only for a north wind out of season to scatter the fleet in mid-crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius thought Rome came to the contest well prepared, its long Gallic wars having inured the legions to the worst and left them like trained and experienced gladiators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Tarentum he shut the gymnasia and the public walks, stopped the drinking-bouts and festivals and enrolled men for service, at which many left the city calling it servitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He won his battles and lost the campaign, and Dio judges that his appetite for war and for command overrode Cineas and sent him out of Italy and Sicily in disgrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","politics","greece","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Dodona","at":[20.7878,39.5464],"zoom":8,"within":"Dodona"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Pyrrhus_MAN_Napoli_Inv6150_n03.jpg/960px-Pyrrhus_MAN_Napoli_Inv6150_n03.jpg","title":"A helmeted head from the Villa of the Papyri, identified as Pyrrhus","desc":"A Roman marble found at Herculaneum and now in Naples, wearing a helmet wreathed with oak leaves. The identification as Pyrrhus is the conventional one and rests on comparison rather than on any inscription.","credit":"Marie-Lan Nguyen; public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyrrhus_MAN_Napoli_Inv6150_n03.jpg","alt":"A white marble bust of a clean-shaven man turned slightly to his right, wearing a close-fitting helmet with a turned-up brim and a band of carved oak leaves running over the crown."},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 13–16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>The Geography of Strabo</i> 6.3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Tarentum,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dtarentum-geo. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.20, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0234%3Abook%3D2%3Achapter%3D20. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i>, Fragments of Book IX, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/9*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Cineas press him about what came after victory?","a":"Because each conquest opened onto the next — Rome, then Sicily, then Africa — so he urged the king to be content with what he already held."},{"q":"Why did the oracle at Dodona settle nothing?","a":"Because it answered that if he crossed into Italy Romans should conquer, and he read it the way he wished and would not wait for spring."},{"q":"Why did Tarentum find its own champion oppressive?","a":"Because he shut the gymnasia and public walks, stopped the drinking-bouts and festivals and enrolled men for service, at which many left the city calling it servitude."}]},
{"id":"rm-165","num":165,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Laevinus lost the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Siris in 280 BCE, the first time a Roman army met a Hellenistic one, and elephants broke his cavalry before it could close.","questions":["Looking across at the Roman camp before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Pyrrhus told the friend beside him that the discipline of these barbarians was not barbarous.","Plutarch gives two counts of the dead at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Dionysius and from Hieronymus, which differ by eight thousand Romans and about nine thousand of his own men."],"answer":"Battle of Heraclea","answerText":"Battle of Heraclea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">280 BCE, on the river Siris</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Rome's first defeat by a Hellenistic army</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Heraclea</b>, fought in 280 BCE on the banks of the Siris in Lucania, was Rome's first engagement with a Hellenistic army and the first defeat it took from one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pyrrhus camped in the plain between Pandosia and Heraclea, a Tarentine foundation where the Greeks of Italy held their common assembly, with the river in front of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He sent a herald first to ask whether Rome would accept arbitration from the Italian Greeks, and Laevinus answered that the Romans neither chose him as a mediator nor feared him as a foe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Riding up to look at the Roman camp, Pyrrhus was amazed at its watches and its order, and told the friend beside him that the discipline of these barbarians was not barbarous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He meant to wait for his allies, but the Romans forced the river at a ford and at many points at once, and the guard on the bank gave way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch counts seven turns of fortune, and says the exchange of armour Pyrrhus made with Megacles nearly lost him the day, when Megacles was killed and the spoils carried to Laevinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had to ride along his own line bare-faced to be recognised, and the elephants, which Dio says no Italian had seen before, panicked the Roman horses before they could close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Thessalian cavalry then broke them, and Plutarch reports two counts of the dead: from Dionysius nearly fifteen thousand Romans and thirteen thousand of Pyrrhus' men, from Hieronymus seven thousand and under four thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whichever is right, the men Pyrrhus lost were his best, and with them went the friends and generals he most relied on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome did not depose Laevinus, and Fabricius said it was not the Epirotes who had beaten the Romans but Pyrrhus who had beaten Laevinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Heraclea","at":[16.6772,40.2119],"zoom":8,"within":"Heraclea"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Parco_Archeologico_Herakleia.jpg/1280px-Parco_Archeologico_Herakleia.jpg","title":"The excavated streets of Heraclea, above the plain of the Siritis","desc":"An aerial view of the archaeological park at Policoro, where the foundations of the Greek city stand on a low plateau. Beyond it the flat coastal plain runs away towards the gulf, which is the kind of ground the battle was fought on.","credit":"Donato Fusco, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parco_Archeologico_Herakleia.jpg","alt":"An aerial photograph looking across a dry plateau covered with the low stone foundations of a gridded ancient town, with a modern farmhouse at the left and cultivated fields and greenhouses stretching to the horizon beyond."},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 16–18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i>, Fragments of Book IX, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/9*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Siris,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dsiris-geo. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Pandosia,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dpandosia-geo. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>The Geography of Strabo</i> 6.3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1924), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0198%3Abook%3D6%3Achapter%3D3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the sight of the Roman camp change the king’s mind?","a":"Because he had expected barbarians and found watches and order, telling the friend beside him that the discipline of these barbarians was not barbarous."},{"q":"Why did an exchange of armour nearly lose him the battle?","a":"Because Megacles, wearing his, was killed and the spoils carried to Laevinus, so the king had to ride along his own line bare-faced to be recognised."},{"q":"Why was this victory already a bad bargain?","a":"Because the men he lost were his best, and with them went the friends and generals he most relied on, whichever of the two counts of the dead is right."}]},
{"id":"rm-166","num":166,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Pyrrhus won the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 279 BCE on the second day, having seized the broken ground first so the Romans had to meet his elephants on the level.","questions":["Daunian troops from Arpi stumbled on the royal camp during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and took the baggage, so the victors spent the night on a hill without food or tents.","Congratulated on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Pyrrhus answered that one more such victory over the Romans would ruin him, since his losses could not be replaced and theirs could."],"answer":"Battle of Asculum","answerText":"Battle of Asculum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">279 BCE, near Asculum in Apulia</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">fought over two days by one account</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Asculum</b> was fought near the Apulian town of that name in 279 BCE, the second time Pyrrhus of Epirus beat a Roman army in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rome had refused to discuss peace at all until he took his army out of Italy, so he had no way forward but another battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the first day the ground was against him, broken and cut by a swift river with wooded banks, so that neither his cavalry nor his elephants could be brought to bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting broke off at nightfall, and on the second he occupied the awkward ground first and forced the Romans to meet him front to front on the level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dionysius sets out the array in detail, the Tarentine phalanx with its white shields beside the Bruttians and Lucanians, the elephants held behind both wings, and two thousand picked horse with the king outside the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The legions fought their swords against the Macedonian spears and gave ground where Pyrrhus pressed, but it was the elephants that did the damage, and Plutarch says the Romans met them as men meet an earthquake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Meanwhile four thousand Daunian foot from Arpi came upon the royal camp by chance, and Pyrrhus lost his tents, his pack animals and all his baggage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His men passed that night on a height in the open without food or attendants, and Dionysius says wounded men died there who could have been saved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The counts differ: Hieronymus gives six thousand Romans dead and, from the king's own commentaries, 3,505 of his own, where Dionysius reports one all-day fight and over fifteen thousand fallen on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Told he had won, Pyrrhus said that one more such victory over the Romans would ruin him utterly, since his losses could not be replaced while theirs filled up as from a spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","politics","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Asculum","at":[15.5619,41.2183],"zoom":8,"within":"Asculum"},"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Trapezophoros_con_due_grifoni_che_sbranano_una_cerva.jpg/1280px-Trapezophoros_con_due_grifoni_che_sbranano_una_cerva.jpg","title":"A marble table support from a Daunian grave at Ascoli Satriano","desc":"Two griffins tearing a fallen doe, carved in polychrome marble in the 4th century BCE and recovered from the tomb of a wealthy Daunian at the town near which the battle was fought. It is not a picture of the battle, but it shows what the people who came down on Pyrrhus' baggage had made of Greek art and Greek money.","credit":"Maredentro, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trapezophoros_con_due_grifoni_che_sbranano_una_cerva.jpg","alt":"A carved marble group on a black ground showing two crouching griffins with tall fan-shaped wings facing each other, their beaks in the flank and neck of a deer lying between them, with traces of red and blue paint."},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 21, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/20*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Asculum,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dasculum-geo. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Apulia,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dapulia-geo. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., \"Tarentum,\" in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.04.0064%3Aentry%3Dtarentum-geo. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he have to fight a second battle at all?","a":"Because Rome had refused to discuss peace until he took his army out of Italy, so there was no way forward but another engagement."},{"q":"Why did the ground decide how the two days went?","a":"Because on the first it was broken and cut by a swift river so that neither cavalry nor elephants could be brought to bear, and on the second he took that ground first and forced a fight on the level."},{"q":"Why did a chance raid cost him as much as the fighting?","a":"Because four thousand Daunian foot came upon the royal camp and took his tents, pack animals and baggage, leaving his men a night in the open where wounded men died who could have been saved."}]},
{"id":"rm-167","num":167,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Pyrrhus gave his name to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when he told a man congratulating him after Asculum that one more such success against Rome would ruin him utterly.","questions":["Cineas described the shape of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before it happened, calling the Roman senate a council of many kings and the Roman people a hydra to fight.","A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what a commander wins when his own losses cannot be replaced and the beaten enemy fills its ranks again as from a spring."],"answer":"Pyrrhic victory","answerText":"Pyrrhic victory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The remark</span><span class=\"dt-v\">279 BCE, after Asculum</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">reported by Plutarch</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>Pyrrhic victory</b> is a success won at a cost so heavy that it does the winner as much harm as a defeat would have done. The phrase comes from Plutarch, who has Pyrrhus answer a man congratulating him after Asculum that one more such victory over the Romans would ruin him utterly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch gives the arithmetic behind it: Pyrrhus had lost a great part of the force he brought over and all but a few of his friends and generals, and could summon no more from home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His Italian allies were growing indifferent, while the Roman army filled up again as from a spring, and defeat did not cost the Romans their courage but sharpened it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cineas had already seen the shape of it, reporting that the senate looked to him like a council of many kings and the people like a Lernaean hydra to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He had counted twice as many soldiers under the consul as had faced Pyrrhus before, with many times that number of Romans still fit to bear arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome refused to talk terms while he remained in Italy, saying it would fight him with all its might though he routed ten thousand men like Laevinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cost was material as well as human: Dionysius has the royal camp plundered at Asculum, the army on a bare hill without food, and wounded men dying who might have lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pyrrhus left after six years holding nothing, and Plutarch judges that what he won by his exploits he lost by chasing what he did not have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antigonus likened him to a dice player who makes many fine throws and does not know how to use them, and Dio says his appetite for war sent him home in disgrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","history","warfare","greece","italy"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 21, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 26, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Dionysius of Halicarnassus, <i>Roman Antiquities</i> 20, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1950), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Dionysius_of_Halicarnassus/20*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i>, Fragments of Book IX, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/9*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could the same losses ruin one side and not the other?","a":"Because Pyrrhus could summon no more from home while the Roman army filled up again as from a spring, and defeat sharpened Roman courage rather than costing it."},{"q":"Why had Cineas already seen how the war would go?","a":"Because he reported the senate a council of many kings and the people a Lernaean hydra, having counted twice as many soldiers under the consul as had faced Pyrrhus before."},{"q":"Why is Antigonus’ comparison with a dice player apt?","a":"Because he made many fine throws and did not know how to use them: what he won by his exploits he lost by chasing what he did not have."}]},
{"id":"rm-168","num":168,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Manius Curius Dentatus caught Pyrrhus coming down out of the woods at daybreak and beat him in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the last battle the king fought in Italy.","questions":["Plutarch says a night march through thick woods went wrong and left Pyrrhus in full view at dawn, which opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside a town then called Maleventum.","Roman javelins turned the elephants back through their own ranks at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the captured camp taught Rome the walled marching camp it used afterwards."],"answer":"Battle of Beneventum","answerText":"Battle of Beneventum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">275 BCE, on the Arusian plains</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">beside Maleventum, in Samnium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">268 BCE, with the Latin colony</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Beneventum</b> in 275 BCE was the last battle Pyrrhus of Epirus fought in Italy, on the Arusian plains beside a Samnite town the Romans then called Maleventum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He had come back from Sicily, losing ships to the Carthaginians in the strait and men and two elephants to the Mamertines on the road north, and reached Tarentum with 20,000 foot and 3,000 horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the Samnites, broken by repeated defeats and resentful of the Sicilian venture, sent him few men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pyrrhus divided his army, sending half into Lucania to keep the second consul away, and led the rest against Manius Curius Dentatus, who was camped near the town and waiting for that help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Unfavourable sacrifices kept the consul where he was, so the king tried to force a decision before the other army could come up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He set out by night with his best men and his most warlike elephants, but the circuit through thick woods was long, the lights gave out and the soldiers lost their way and straggled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Daybreak showed him coming down from the heights in full view; the sacrifices had turned favourable, and Curius led his forces out, routed the foremost of the enemy and took some elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consul then gave battle in the open, where the elephants drove part of his line back on the camp until the guards on the parapets hurled their javelins and turned the beasts back through Pyrrhus’ own ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Frontinus adds that the Romans took the royal camp, and that from its plan, Pyrrhus having been the first to enclose a whole army within one entrenchment, they worked towards the walled marching camp of later centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pyrrhus left Italy for good; the booty paid for Rome’s second aqueduct, contracted by Curius as censor,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and in 268 BCE a Latin colony gave the ill-omened town its luckier name of Beneventum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","italy","greece"],"locator":{"name":"Beneventum","at":[14.7826,41.1297],"zoom":8,"within":"Beneventum"},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 24, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Beneventum,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=beneventum-geo. [Open access]","Frontinus, <i>Strategemata</i> 4.1.14, trans. Charles E. Bennett, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Frontinus/Strategemata/4*.html. [Open access]","Frontinus, <i>The Aqueducts of Rome</i> 1.6, trans. Charles E. Bennett, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Frontinus/De_Aquis/Bennett/1*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Piatto_con_elefanti_in_assetto_da_guerra_%28Museo_nazionale_etrusco_di_Villa_Giulia%2C_Roma%29.jpg/1280px-Piatto_con_elefanti_in_assetto_da_guerra_%28Museo_nazionale_etrusco_di_Villa_Giulia%2C_Roma%29.jpg","title":"A war elephant on a painted plate of the 3rd century BCE","desc":"The centre of a plate found in a tomb of the Macchie necropolis at Capena, painted with an elephant in a red caparison carrying a crenellated tower with armed figures in it, a driver at its neck and a calf walking behind. Commons dates the plate to the 3rd century BCE and records the museum's suggestion that it was made for Curius' triumph; either way it is what an Italian painter of about this time made of the animals Pyrrhus brought over. Photographed from the reproduction panel shown at the Villa Giulia while the plate itself was on loan.","credit":"Anonymous painter of the 3rd century BCE, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piatto_con_elefanti_in_assetto_da_guerra_%28Museo_nazionale_etrusco_di_Villa_Giulia%2C_Roma%29.jpg","alt":"A cracked pottery plate painted in orange and cream on black, showing an elephant in profile with a red cloth on its back, a square battlemented tower carrying two small figures with spears, a driver seated at its neck, and a small elephant calf following behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he attack before the second consul arrived?","a":"Because Curius was waiting for that army and unfavourable sacrifices kept him in camp, so Pyrrhus tried to force a decision first."},{"q":"Why did the night march fail?","a":"Because the circuit through thick woods was long, the lights gave out and the men lost their way, so daybreak showed him coming down from the heights in full view."},{"q":"Why did the elephants lose him the battle?","a":"Because the guards on the camp parapets hurled their javelins and turned the beasts back through his own ranks."}]},
{"id":"rm-169","num":169,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"With Pyrrhus dead in Argos, his officer Milo handed the citadel over to the consul Papirius and ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which the city gave up its arms and ships.","questions":["Rome pressed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by land while a Carthaginian fleet lay off the harbour, and the garrison commander asked only to march out unharmed with his men and his money.","Tarentum came out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 272 BCE with its own laws intact, its walls pulled down, its ships handed over and a tribute to pay."],"answer":"Siege of Tarentum","answerText":"Siege of Tarentum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">The surrender</span><span class=\"dt-v\">272 BCE, to the consul Papirius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the citadel first, then the city</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Siege of Tarentum</b> in 272 BCE closed the war the city had begun by calling Pyrrhus of Epirus into Italy, and put the greatest Greek city of the south under Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tarentum had sent for the king about 281 BCE, and he had shipped his general Milo ahead with 3,000 men to hold the citadel before crossing himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Pyrrhus at last gave up Italy, after six years of war there,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> he sailed home and left Milo behind with a garrison, letting it be understood that he would come back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was by then divided, and some Tarentines whom Milo had injured seized a stronghold in their own country under a leader named Nico and raided him from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome took the allies first, subduing the Samnites, the Lucanians and the Bruttians, and left the case of the Tarentines until last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What settled the matter was the news from Argos, where Plutarch says a woman watching the street fighting from a roof threw down a tile that broke Pyrrhus’ neck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One party in Tarentum then called in the Carthaginians, and the consul Lucius Papirius pressed the city by land while their fleet lay off the water front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Milo surrendered the citadel on terms, marching out unharmed with his followers and his money, and the Carthaginians, being at peace with Rome, sailed away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smith’s geographical dictionary reads the same chapter differently and has the fleet arrive too late to matter, Milo having already capitulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city gave up its arms and its ships, pulled down its walls and agreed to pay tribute, keeping its own laws under watch;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius says Rome went on to subdue everyone who had taken Pyrrhus’ side, and all Italy but the Celts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","italy","greece"],"locator":{"name":"Tarentum","at":[17.24,40.4644],"zoom":8,"within":"Tarentum"},"sources":["Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i>, Fragments of Book X, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/10*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Tarentum,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=tarentum-geo. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 34, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.6, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=6. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Pyrrhus</i> 26, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pyrrhus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Taras_-_281-272_BC_-_silver_didrachm_-_warrior_on_horseback_-_youth_riding_dolphin_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg/1280px-Taras_-_281-272_BC_-_silver_didrachm_-_warrior_on_horseback_-_youth_riding_dolphin_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","title":"A silver didrachm of Taras struck during the war, about 281 to 272 BCE","desc":"The obverse of a nomos of Taras in the Munich coin collection, dated by the museum to about 281 to 272 BCE and so to the years between the appeal to Pyrrhus and the surrender: a helmeted horseman with two spears and a round shield blazoned with a star. The reverse, not shown here, carries the youth on a dolphin the city had put on its money for two centuries.","credit":"ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taras_-_281-272_BC_-_silver_didrachm_-_warrior_on_horseback_-_youth_riding_dolphin_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","alt":"A worn silver coin photographed on grey cloth, showing in relief a horse walking to the left with a rider in a crested helmet holding two spears behind a large round shield decorated with an eight-pointed star, with Greek letters in the field."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the city not fall while the king was away?","a":"Because he left Milo behind with a garrison holding the citadel, letting it be understood that he would come back."},{"q":"Why did news from Argos settle the siege?","a":"Because Pyrrhus was killed there, a woman watching the street fighting from a roof throwing down a tile that broke his neck, so no return could be expected."},{"q":"Why did the Carthaginian fleet sail away without fighting?","a":"Because they were at peace with Rome, and Milo had surrendered the citadel on terms — though Smith’s dictionary reads the same chapter as having them arrive too late to matter."}]},
{"id":"rm-170","num":170,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome held Italy through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a set of separate treaties under which each allied community kept its own laws and owed soldiers rather than a standing tribute.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the senate voted the year’s numbers and the consuls told each allied city how many men to enrol, on what day, and at what place.","Polybius shows the reach of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 225 BCE, when the returns of men able to bear arms came to more than 700,000 foot and 70,000 horse."],"answer":"Roman confederation","answerText":"Roman confederation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Counted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">225 BCE, over 700,000 foot</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">and 70,000 horse, allies included</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman confederation</b> is the name given to the web of separate treaties by which Rome held Italy after the conquest, each community bound to Rome on terms of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smith’s dictionary of antiquities describes the allied communities as independent states, keeping their own laws and magistrates, under a general liability to furnish a contingent to the Roman army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a people owed depended on how it had come under Roman power, so terms ran from the equal alliances long held with Tibur, Praeneste and Naples down to the plain dependence of the defeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word covered only part of Italy: Roman colonies and any place that had received the Roman citizenship were outside it, and the Latins were counted separately again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The senate voted each year how many troops were wanted, and the consuls fixed each people’s share in proportion to its men of military age, from lists the allies themselves drew up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The consuls sent word to the magistrates of the allied cities naming the number, the day and the place, and each city enrolled its own men, swore them in and appointed a commander and a paymaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the muster twelve Roman officers, the prefects of the allies, picked a third of the cavalry and a fifth of the infantry as extraordinarii and divided the rest into a right and a left wing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius says the allied infantry generally equalled the citizen legions in number while the allied cavalry ran to three times as many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The allied states paid and clothed their own soldiers and Rome fed them, and Rome could call on the allies for money, corn and ships besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the arrangement was worth showed in 225 BCE, when the returns of men able to bear arms, Romans and allies together, came to more than 700,000 foot and 70,000 horse;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> the allies had no share in the citizenship, and in the end demanded it with arms in their hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["Leonhard Schmitz, “Socii,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1050–51, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Socii.html. [Open access]","George Long, “Foederatae Civitates,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 542–43, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Foederatae_Civitates.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.24, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=2:chapter=24. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=6:chapter=21. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.26, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=6:chapter=26. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/The_Growth_of_Roman_Power_in_Italy.jpg","title":"The growth of Roman power in Italy, mapped in 1911","desc":"William R. Shepherd's plate colours Roman territory and Roman colonies apart from the Latin colonies and from the Roman allies as they stood about 100 BCE, marks the extent of Roman land about 338 BCE with a broken line, and dates each colony where it was planted. The allied country is the pink that surrounds and separates the Roman green, which is what a confederation of separate treaties looked like on the ground.","credit":"William R. Shepherd, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Growth_of_Roman_Power_in_Italy.jpg","alt":"A coloured map of Italy and Sicily with a legend, green patches marking Roman territory and colonies, bright red marking Latin colonies and pale pink the Roman allies, with named towns and Roman roads across the peninsula."},"why":[{"q":"Why is it a web of treaties rather than a single system?","a":"Because what a people owed depended on how it had come under Roman power, running from equal alliances with Tibur, Praeneste and Naples down to the plain dependence of the defeated."},{"q":"Why did the allies enrol their own men?","a":"Because the consuls sent word naming the number, the day and the place, and each city then enrolled and swore in its own soldiers and appointed a commander and a paymaster."},{"q":"Why did the arrangement end in war?","a":"Because the allies had no share in the citizenship, and in the end demanded it with arms in their hands."}]},
{"id":"rm-171","num":171,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome’s Italian allies, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, kept their own laws and magistrates and owed the republic soldiers, but had no vote and no share in the citizenship.","questions":["A treaty made a community one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, liable for troops and, when Rome asked, for money, corn and ships, and forbidden to make war on its own account.","Polybius says Rome fed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> free of charge while stopping the price of a citizen soldier’s grain and clothing out of his pay."],"answer":"socii","answerText":"socii","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grievances</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 173 BCE, magistrates on tour</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the status ended by the lex Julia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>socii</b> were the Italian communities allied to Rome by treaty, keeping their own laws and magistrates and owing soldiers for Rome’s armies rather than a place in the Roman state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their standing varied with the way each had come under Roman power, and the equal alliances a few kept, as Camerinum and Heraclea did into late times, were exceptions to a general dependence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman colonies and any community that had received the citizenship fell outside the name, and the Latins were reckoned apart from the socii again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome could call on them for money, corn and ships as well as men; they might not take up arms on their own account, and quarrels among them were settled by the senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against that they often had the same share of booty and of conquered land as Romans, and were sometimes sent out beside them as colonists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the field their own states paid and clothed them while Rome fed them, and Polybius notes that the grain was a free gift to the allies where a citizen had its price stopped out of his pay by the quaestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The allied cavalry drew less than the Roman: one and a third medimni of wheat and five of barley a month, against two and seven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> As Rome grew the relation soured, and from 173 BCE magistrates travelling through Italy expected allied towns to lodge them and to furnish pack animals, with no redress against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaius Gracchus, quoted by Gellius, told of a consul’s wife at Teanum Sidicinum who wanted the men’s baths, and of the town’s chief magistrate stripped and flogged in the forum because they were cleared too slowly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Fulvius Flaccus proposed giving the Italians the citizenship, Appian says the senate was angry at the thought of making its subjects equal citizens and the attempt was dropped;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> the allies got it in the end only by war, and the lex Julia closed the status altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["Leonhard Schmitz, “Socii,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1050–51, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Socii.html. [Open access]","George Long, “Foederatae Civitates,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 542–43, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Foederatae_Civitates.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.39, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=6:chapter=39. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 10.3, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/10*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.21, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could allies not act on their own account?","a":"Because they might not take up arms by themselves and quarrels among them were settled by the senate, so their foreign policy was Rome’s."},{"q":"Why was the corn ration a real difference between allies and citizens?","a":"Because the grain was a free gift to the allies where a citizen had its price stopped out of his pay by the quaestor."},{"q":"Why does the story from Teanum Sidicinum survive?","a":"Because Gaius Gracchus told it as evidence of what the alliance had become — the town’s chief magistrate stripped and flogged in the forum because the baths were cleared too slowly for a consul’s wife."}]},
{"id":"rm-172","num":172,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome kept its Italian allies and the number of men each owed on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a register the consuls drew each community’s quota from year by year.","questions":["In 209 BCE eighteen Latin colonies told the consuls that the soldiers they owed under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were ready, and that more would follow if Rome asked for them.","Every community listed on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> armed and paid its own contingent, so the register worked as an indirect tax as much as a levy list."],"answer":"formula togatorum","answerText":"formula togatorum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Seen at work</span><span class=\"dt-v\">209 BCE, eighteen colonies ready</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">twelve refusers punished six years later</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>formula togatorum</b> was the register on which Rome kept its Italian allies and the number of men each of them owed, rendered by Mommsen as the roll of contingent-furnishing Italians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It covered the communities of Italy, whose men served in the legions and the allied wings, and not the foreign allies, whose troops served as auxiliaries outside the line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The senate voted the year’s total, and the consuls set each people’s share in proportion to its men of military age, from lists the communities themselves drew up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius shows the machinery working in 225 BCE, when the magistrates were ordered to hand in lists of all citizens of military age and the allies were called up as stores were gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mommsen took each community’s number to be fixed, with Rome free to ask for more at need, and read the roll as an indirect tax as well, since every community armed and paid its own men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What that meant can be read in Livy: in 209 BCE the delegates of eighteen Latin colonies answered that the soldiers they owed stood ready and that more would follow if Rome wanted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Twelve other colonies had refused, and almost six years later the senate ordered each of them to furnish twice the largest infantry contingent it had raised since the Carthaginians landed, with 120 cavalry besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three foot soldiers might stand for each horseman a colony lacked, the men came from its wealthiest citizens, and its delegates were held in Rome until the levy was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same decree laid an annual property tax on the twelve and had the Roman censors hand the colonies’ censors the schedule to assess by, their lists to be brought to Rome and sworn to before they left office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The allies came in time to furnish three times the citizens’ cavalry, while the older rule that their infantry should not outnumber the citizen army held for a long while.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":5,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["Theodor Mommsen, <i>The History of Rome</i>, trans. William Purdie Dickson, new ed., vol. 2 (London: Macmillan, 1913), 54, https://archive.org/stream/historyofrome02mommuoft/historyofrome02mommuoft_djvu.txt. [Open access]","Leonhard Schmitz, “Socii,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1050–51, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Socii.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.23, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=2:chapter=23. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 27.10, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=27:chapter=10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 29.15, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=29:chapter=15. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the roll a form of taxation as well as a levy?","a":"Because every community armed and paid its own men, so what the register set was a charge on the community as much as a number of soldiers."},{"q":"Why did Rome punish twelve colonies through the same register?","a":"Because it ordered each to furnish twice the largest infantry contingent it had raised since the Carthaginians landed, with 120 cavalry besides, drawn from its wealthiest citizens."},{"q":"Why did the censors hand over their own schedule?","a":"Because the colonies’ censors were to assess by it and bring the lists to Rome and swear to them before leaving office, so the assessment could not be quietly softened."}]},
{"id":"rm-173","num":173,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Gaius Flaminius built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as censor in 220 BCE, carrying the great north road from Rome to Ariminum and into the Gallic country he had beaten.","questions":["Augustus wrote that as consul for the seventh time he made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the city to Ariminum, together with every bridge on it but the Mulvian and the Minucian.","Fabius marched up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to meet the consul near Ocriculum after Trasimene, which showed within three years what the road had been built for."],"answer":"Via Flaminia","answerText":"Via Flaminia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">220 BCE, by the censor Flaminius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Rome to Ariminum, about 310 km</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebuilt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 BCE, by Augustus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Via Flaminia</b> was Rome’s great north road, running from the city to Ariminum on the Adriatic, and one of the first Roman highways whose date of building is known for certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was made by Gaius Flaminius during his censorship in 220 BCE, to open a free line of communication with the Gallic country he had subdued a few years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roads outside the city were the censors’ charge under the republic, and a great line like this one was laid out about 4 to 4.6 m (13 to 15 feet) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It left Rome by the Porta Flaminia and crossed the Tiber at the Milvian Bridge, its course studded with tombs on both sides for miles, as the Appian Way’s was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The itineraries make the whole distance about 310 km (210 Roman miles) by the direct line through Narnia and Mevania, and twelve miles more by the loop through Interamna and Spoletium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its military value showed within three years: Livy has Fabius, made dictator after Trasimene in 217 BCE, march up the Flaminian road to meet the consul near Ocriculum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cicero later named it one of three roads from Rome to Cisalpine Gaul, beside the Aurelian along the lower sea and the Cassian through the middle of Etruria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Augustus took its care on himself in 27 BCE, and records in his own account that as consul for the seventh time he made the road from the city to Ariminum, and all the bridges but the Mulvian and the Minucian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two arches were voted him for the work, one at Rome and one at Ariminum, where the marble arch by the Pesaro gate still stands with his bridge over the Ariminus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Vespasian afterwards cut a tunnel through the rock at Intercisa, the Furlo, which also survives, and in the civil war of 69 CE both sides understood that holding the road would come near to deciding it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","technology","history","geography","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Via Flaminia","at":[12.4767,41.9109],"zoom":7,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["William Smith, ed., “Via Flaminia,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=via-flaminia-geo. [Open access]","William Ramsay, “Viae,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1191–95, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Viae.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 22.11, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=22:chapter=11. [Open access]","Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 20, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/4*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Ariminum,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=ariminum-geo. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Arco_d%27Augusto%2C_Rimini_Italy.jpg/1280px-Arco_d%27Augusto%2C_Rimini_Italy.jpg","title":"The arch of Augustus at Rimini, where the road ended","desc":"The marble arch raised in honour of Augustus at Ariminum, the northern end of the Via Flaminia, standing where the gate towards Pesaro was. Smith's dictionary calls it Corinthian work in white marble, disfigured by later additions: the battlements along the top and the brick walling on either side are among them.","credit":"Il Malatestiano, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arco_d%27Augusto,_Rimini_Italy.jpg","alt":"A single white marble Roman arch with fluted columns, a pediment and round portrait medallions, set into ruined brick walls and topped by medieval battlements, with a modern street and passers-by visible through the opening."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the road built where it was?","a":"Because it opened a free line of communication with the Gallic country its builder had subdued a few years earlier, running from Rome to Ariminum on the Adriatic."},{"q":"Why did its military value show so quickly?","a":"Because within three years Fabius, made dictator after Trasimene, marched up it to meet the consul near Ocriculum."},{"q":"Why was holding it thought near to deciding a civil war?","a":"Because it was one of only three roads from Rome to Cisalpine Gaul, so in 69 CE both sides understood what controlling that line was worth."}]},
{"id":"rm-174","num":174,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The consul Marcus Aemilius Lepidus took his army into Gaul in 187 BCE and made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Placentia to Ariminum, to join the road coming up from Rome.","questions":["Almost straight across the plain for about 290 km, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave its name to the country between the Apennines and the Po, which is called after it still.","Strabo runs the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only as far as Bononia and then round the marshes to Aquileia, where Livy carries it the whole way from Placentia to Ariminum."],"answer":"Via Aemilia","answerText":"Via Aemilia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">187 BCE, by the consul Lepidus</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Placentia to Ariminum, about 290 km</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Via Aemilia</b> was the road driven across the plain of Cisalpine Gaul from Ariminum to Placentia, and the first great Roman highway built in the north of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy puts it in 187 BCE: the consul Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, having beaten the Ligurians and brought them down from their mountains into the plains, led his army into Gaul and made a road from Placentia to Ariminum to join the Via Flaminia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His colleague Gaius Flaminius, son of the man who had built the Flaminia, kept his own soldiers out of idleness the same year by making a road from Bononia to Arretium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both ends were Roman already: Ariminum, the colony of 268 BCE where the Flaminian road stopped,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and Placentia, planted in 219 BCE with 6,000 colonists on Latin rights and attacked by the Gauls before its walls were well finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carried the whole way through level country, the road holds an almost straight line for about 290 km (180 Roman miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo tells it differently, running Lepidus’ road only as far as Bononia and then round the marshes along the foot of the Alps to Aquileia, and crediting the Flaminia to the consul of 187 rather than to his father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smith’s dictionary follows Livy, taking Ariminum to Placentia as the line generally recognised as the Aemilian Way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nearly every town of the district stood on it, and the country between the Apennines and the Po came to be named after the road rather than the road after the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name outlasted the republic: the eighth region of Augustus was Aemilia, Martial could write of a man from the region of the Aemilian way, and the district was eventually made a province under that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line is still in use, and the name the road gave that stretch of northern Italy has stayed with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","technology","history","geography","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Via Aemilia","at":[11.3426,44.4949],"zoom":7,"within":"Bononia"},"sources":["William Smith, ed., “Via Aemilia,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=via-aemilia-geo. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 39.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=2. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Placentia,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=placentia-geo. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Ariminum,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=ariminum-geo. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.1.11, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5A*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Emilia_Bologna1_tango7174.jpg","title":"Bologna from above, with the old road's line through the middle","desc":"Roman Bononia stood on the Via Aemilia, and the straight street crossing the centre of the modern city keeps the line the road drew across the plain. Smith's dictionary noted in 1854 that the highway was still in use along its whole length, which it is.","credit":"tango7174, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emilia_Bologna1_tango7174.jpg","alt":"An aerial panorama over a city of red-tiled roofs, a long straight street running away through the middle of it, a tall medieval tower to the right and low hills on the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why could this road run almost straight for 180 miles?","a":"Because it was carried the whole way through level country, across the plain between Ariminum and Placentia."},{"q":"Why were its two ends chosen?","a":"Because both were Roman already — Ariminum the colony of 268 BCE where the Flaminian road stopped, and Placentia planted in 219 — so the new road joined them to the existing line."},{"q":"Why does the road’s name outlast the road’s builder?","a":"Because nearly every town of the district stood on it, so the country between the Apennines and the Po came to be named after the road rather than the road after the country."}]},
{"id":"rm-175","num":175,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome began paving its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the Samnite wars, when a quick and safe link between the city and its legions was first badly wanted.","questions":["Under four courses, from stones a hand could grasp up to fitted polygonal slabs, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried a surface so even that it was said to look like one solid mass.","Plutarch says Gaius Gracchus built his stretch of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> straight through the country, bridged the ravines, and set stone pillars at every mile with mounting-blocks between."],"answer":"Roman roads","answerText":"Roman roads","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Paved from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">312 BCE, with the Appian Way</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">great lines about 4 to 4.6 m wide</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman roads</b> of Italy began as muddy tracks and became paved highways in the Samnite wars, when a quick, safe link between the city and its legions was first wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman law divided them into viae publicae, whose soil, use and upkeep belonged to the state, viae privatae running over private ground, and the country cross-roads called vicinales, which might be either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The greatest public roads went by the names military, consular and praetorian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A road was set out between two shallow trenches and the loose earth dug away until firm ground was reached, or piles driven where the ground was swampy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that footing came four courses: a statumen of stones a hand could just grasp, a rudus of broken stone in lime 23 cm (9 inches) thick, a nucleus of brick and pottery fragments 15 cm (6 inches) thick, and a pavimentum of polygonal hard stone jointed so closely the surface read as one mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The great lines were about 4 to 4.6 m (13 to 15 feet) wide, where a lesser road, like the one up to the temple on the Alban Mount, is exactly 2.4 m (8 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making them was public work and often soldiers’ work: Livy has a consul of 187 BCE set his men to a road rather than leave them idle,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Augustus records that as consul for the seventh time he made the road from the city to Ariminum with every bridge on it but two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch says Gaius Gracchus carried his roads straight through the country on rammed foundations, filled the hollows, bridged the ravines and kept both sides level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He measured every road by miles and planted stone pillars to mark them, with other stones set between so that a rider could mount without help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo thought roads, aqueducts and sewers were where Roman foresight beat Greek, roads cut through hills and banked across valleys so a wagon could carry a boatload; stretches near Rome had gone a thousand years unrepaired and were still sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["building","technology","history","geography","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["William Ramsay, “Viae,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1191–95, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Viae.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Caius Gracchus</i> 6–7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.8, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]","Augustus, <i>Res Gestae Divi Augusti</i> 20, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/4*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 39.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Campidoglio_-_il_miliarium.JPG/1280px-Campidoglio_-_il_miliarium.JPG","title":"A Roman milestone, the first of the Appian Way","desc":"A marble column set up on the Capitol in Rome and described as the first milestone of the Via Appia. The numeral I is cut at the top and two later inscriptions run down the shaft, naming the emperor who repaired the road with his offices and the number of his consulship. This is the object Plutarch describes Gaius Gracchus planting at every mile of the roads he built.","credit":"Lalupa, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campidoglio_-_il_miliarium.JPG","alt":"A tall cylindrical white marble column on a stone plinth against a deep blue sky, with the Roman numeral one cut into a band near the top and two blocks of worn Latin lettering below it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did paved highways arrive with the Samnite wars?","a":"Because a quick, safe link between the city and its legions was first wanted then, where before there had been muddy tracks."},{"q":"Why were four courses laid rather than a surface alone?","a":"Because the loose earth was dug away to firm ground, or piles driven where it was swampy, and the stone, rubble, potsherd and paving courses above spread the load on that footing."},{"q":"Why did Gracchus set stones between his milestones?","a":"Because they were placed so that a rider could mount without help, the roads being measured by miles and marked with pillars throughout."}]},
{"id":"rm-176","num":176,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The senate could grant a general a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which he rode through the city behind his captives and spoils and sacrificed to Jupiter on the Capitol.","questions":["To be voted a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a commander had to have killed 5,000 of the enemy in one battle, in a foreign war, under his own auspices and with his own troops.","Plutarch spreads the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Aemilius Paullus over three days, ending with the captive king Perseus, his children, and the general in purple holding a laurel spray."],"answer":"Roman triumph","answerText":"Roman triumph","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Described</span><span class=\"dt-v\">167 BCE, Aemilius Paullus, 3 days</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the bar: 5,000 of the enemy in one battle</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>Roman triumph</b> was the procession in which a victorious general entered the city in a four-horse chariot, his captives and the spoils ahead of him and his army behind, and went up the Sacred Way to sacrifice to Jupiter on the Capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the height of Roman military glory and lay in the senate’s gift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius says the senate could brighten a general’s success or dim it, since no triumph could be held with splendour, or sometimes at all, without its consent and its money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rules were exacting: the man must have held the dictatorship, a consulship or a praetorship, fought under his own auspices with his own troops in his own province, and killed 5,000 of the enemy in one battle at small loss.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war had to be lawful, foreign and finished, the province quiet and the army free to come home, which is why Marcellus got only an ovation for Syracuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A civil war earned nothing: Caesar held no triumph after Pharsalus, and the one he took over Pompey’s sons gave general offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A claimant waited outside the walls, since to enter was to end his command, and Cicero trailed his lictors round the outskirts for weeks in hope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lesser honour, the ovation, went on foot in a magistrate’s toga under myrtle, with flute-players for trumpets and a sheep at the end of it instead of a bull.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch gives three days to the triumph of Aemilius Paullus in 167 BCE: statues and paintings on 250 chariots, Macedonian arms heaped so that they clashed as they went, 120 gilt-horned oxen, the captive king Perseus with his three small children, and the general in purple shot with gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A public slave held a golden crown over his head, which Ramsay reads as a charm against the evil eye; the whisper that he was only a man comes from Tertullian and no earlier writer,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and two of Aemilius’ sons died within days of his procession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","history","politics","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4853,41.8925],"zoom":7,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["William Ramsay, “Triumphus,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 1163–67, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Triumphus.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Aemilius Paulus</i> 32–34, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.15, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=6:chapter=15. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Aemilius Paulus</i> 35, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","William Ramsay, “Ovatio,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 846, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Ovatio.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Carrying_off_the_Menorah_from_the_Temple_in_Jerusalem_depicted_on_a_frieze_on_the_Arch_of_Titus_in_the_Forum_Romanum.JPG/1280px-Carrying_off_the_Menorah_from_the_Temple_in_Jerusalem_depicted_on_a_frieze_on_the_Arch_of_Titus_in_the_Forum_Romanum.JPG","title":"Spoils carried in a triumph, on the Arch of Titus","desc":"A relief inside the Arch of Titus in the Roman forum, showing the seven-branched lampstand and other spoils taken from the Temple in Jerusalem being carried on bearing-frames. It is a Roman monument's own picture of the procession the card describes, made two centuries after the triumph of Aemilius Paullus and for a different war.","credit":"Laurel Lodged, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carrying_off_the_Menorah_from_the_Temple_in_Jerusalem_depicted_on_a_frieze_on_the_Arch_of_Titus_in_the_Forum_Romanum.JPG","alt":"A weathered marble relief of a crowd of men in tunics carrying a large seven-branched lampstand and a table on poles above their shoulders, with trumpets and a placard behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a claimant wait outside the walls?","a":"Because entering the city ended his command, which is why Cicero trailed his lictors round the outskirts for weeks in hope of one."},{"q":"Why did the senate’s consent decide whether a triumph happened?","a":"Because no triumph could be held with splendour, or sometimes at all, without its approval and its money, so it could brighten a general’s success or dim it."},{"q":"Why did a civil war earn nothing?","a":"Because the war had to be lawful and foreign, so Caesar held none after Pharsalus and the one he took over Pompey’s sons gave general offence."}]},
{"id":"rm-177","num":177,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Roman priests opened a siege with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, calling the enemy town’s guardian god out of it with the promise of the same worship, or better, at Rome.","questions":["Livy has Camillus perform an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the assault on Veii, asking Queen Juno to leave the city and follow the victors to a temple worthy of her.","Rome kept the name of its own guardian god secret, Pliny says, for fear that an enemy might use the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the city in its turn."],"answer":"evocatio","answerText":"evocatio","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Veii taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">396 BCE, Juno called to Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">her temple on the Aventine vowed then</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Evocatio</b> was the Roman ritual of calling a besieged city’s own gods out of it, offering them worship at Rome if they would leave the people who had been keeping them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pliny cites Verrius Flaccus for the practice: at a siege the Roman priests summoned out the town’s tutelary god before anything else and promised him the same rites or better at Rome, and the ritual still belonged to the pontiffs’ discipline in Pliny’s own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the same reason Rome kept the name of its own guardian deity secret, lest an enemy do to it what it did to others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The famous case is Veii, where Livy has Camillus vow a tenth of the spoils to Pythian Apollo and then beseech Queen Juno, who dwelt in Veii, to follow the victors to a city that would soon be hers and to a temple worthy of her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Veientines, Livy says, had no notion that some of their gods had been promised a share of the spoils and others invited away to new homes among their enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When the city fell, men chosen from the whole army washed and put on white robes before laying hands on the statue, since by Etruscan usage only one priestly family might touch it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of them asked whether Juno was willing to go to Rome, the rest cried that she had nodded, and Livy passes on the further claim that she was heard to say she was willing as an addition to the story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The image proved light and easy to move, as though it came of its own accord, and it was carried to the Aventine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Camillus contracted there for her temple after a triumph he spoiled by driving in behind white horses, which Romans thought fit for Jupiter and the Sun rather than for a citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Smith’s dictionary records that the tutelary goddess of Veii was installed on the Aventine in a temple that stood until the end of paganism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","religion","history","warfare","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Veii","at":[12.3897,42.0233],"zoom":8,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 5.21, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0026:book=5:chapter=21. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 5.22, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0026:book=5:chapter=22. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 28.4, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=28:chapter=4. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Veii,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=veii-geo. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 5.23, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0026:book=5:chapter=23. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome keep its own guardian deity’s name secret?","a":"Because the ritual it used against others could be used against it, an enemy who knew the name being able to call the god out."},{"q":"Why is the ritual an argument as well as a rite?","a":"Because it offered the god the same rites or better at Rome, so it invited rather than compelled — Camillus asking Juno to follow the victors to a temple worthy of her."},{"q":"Why does Livy mark the further claim as an addition?","a":"Because he reports that the men chosen cried she had nodded, and passes on separately the story that she was heard to say she was willing."}]},
{"id":"rm-178","num":178,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Colonies, roads and grants of citizenship carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Italy, until peoples Rome had beaten were counting themselves Roman without giving up their old names.","questions":["Velleius listed every colony founded after the Gauls took Rome, with the citizenship grants beside them, as a measure of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Italy that followed the conquest.","Strabo shows the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Italy complete in the north, where the people were all Romans by his day and were still called Umbrians, Etruscans, Ligurians and Insubrians."],"answer":"Romanisation","answerText":"Romanisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Latin settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, towns taken into Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">all Romans in the north by Strabo’s day</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Romanisation</b> of Italy was the long process by which conquered peoples came to live and count themselves Roman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Velleius listed every colony the senate founded after the Gauls took Rome, with the grants of citizenship beside them, calling it the growth of the Roman name by giving others a share in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His catalogue runs from Sutrium and Setia through Cales, Tarracina, Luceria, Suessa, Interamna, Sora, Alba, Carseoli, Minturnae and Venusia, a new colony every few years for a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The grants run beside them: to Aricia, to the Campanians and part of the Samnites without the vote, to Fundi, Formiae and Acerra, and to the Sabines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smith’s dictionary describes the old colonies as garrisons planted in conquered towns, the settlers taking about a third of the land while the former owners kept the rest and lived beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The purpose was military: Narnia was founded to check the Umbrians, and Minturnae, Sinuessa, Cremona and Placentia for like reasons, which is why Cicero called them the bulwarks of the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Latin colony belonged to the Roman state without holding the citizenship, ran its own affairs, and had no political bond with the other Latin colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other route ran through the defeated towns: in 338 BCE Lanuvium was given the full citizenship with its cult of Juno Sospita shared between Romans and Lanuvians, Aricia, Nomentum and Pedum the same, and Velitrae lost its walls and its senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Colonising stopped while Hannibal was in Italy, since soldiers were wanted rather than discharged, and began again afterwards with Bononia, Pisaurum, Aquileia and Luca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo gives the outcome: Rome sent settlers to many places and preserved the older stocks as well, so that although the northerners were all Romans by his day they were still called Umbrians, Etruscans, Heneti, Ligurians and Insubrians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","history","politics","culture","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["“Colonia,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 315–20, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Colonia.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.14, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.15, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.1.10, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5A*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 8.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0026:book=8:chapter=14. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Remains_of_Alba_Fucens.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_Alba_Fucens.jpg","title":"A Latin colony in the mountains: Alba Fucens","desc":"The paved street, foundations and forum of Alba Fucens, high in the central Apennines in what had been Aequian country, with the modern village on the ridge above. Alba is one of the foundations in Velleius' catalogue, and the photograph shows what a colony amounted to on the ground: a Roman town laid out on somebody else's land.","credit":"PaestumPaestum, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_Alba_Fucens.jpg","alt":"A wide view of Roman ruins in an upland valley: a paved street of large flat stones in the foreground, low stone wall foundations and three standing column stumps across a grass field, with a modern village and bare hills behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the colonies planted where they were?","a":"Because the purpose was military — Narnia to check the Umbrians, Minturnae, Sinuessa, Cremona and Placentia for like reasons — which is why Cicero called them the bulwarks of the empire."},{"q":"Why did colonising stop during Hannibal’s war?","a":"Because soldiers were wanted rather than discharged, so the foundations began again only afterwards with Bononia, Pisaurum, Aquileia and Luca."},{"q":"Why were the old peoples still named after they had become Romans?","a":"Because Rome sent settlers to many places and preserved the older stocks as well, so Strabo’s northerners were all Romans and still called Umbrians, Etruscans and Ligurians."}]},
{"id":"rm-179","num":179,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"In 180 BCE the people of Cumae asked the senate for leave to use <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in their public business, which is the spread of the language seen from below.","questions":["In the Italy of Strabo’s day the Osci had vanished but their dialect was still staged at Rome, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had taken the country as far as Sinuessa.","Pliny praised Italy as the land chosen by the gods to bind the discordant tongues of many nations together in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the Roman account of what happened."],"answer":"Latin","answerText":"Latin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cumae asks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">180 BCE, for Latin in public business</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">a Greek city, taken earlier by Campanians</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Latin</b> began as the speech of one small plain in central Italy and ended as the language of the peninsula, taking the ground that Etruscan, Oscan, Umbrian and Greek had held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It travelled with the colonies, which were bodies of Roman and Latin settlers planted in conquered towns, holding about a third of the land and living beside the people they had beaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The clearest sign of the change is a request rather than an order: in 180 BCE the people of Cumae asked the senate for leave to use Latin in their public business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cumae had been a Greek foundation, was taken by Campanians about 420 BCE, and came under Rome in 338 BCE with the citizenship but without the vote, so the request came from a town whose public language had already changed once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Smith’s dictionary reads it as a measure of the steadily shrinking Greek element in the city rather than as a defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo, writing under Augustus, says the whole country as far as Sinuessa belonged by then to the Latini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds an oddity: the Osci had disappeared, yet their dialect survived among the Romans, and at a traditional competition poems in it were still brought on the stage and recited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A language could therefore outlast the people who had spoken it, kept up as a performance in the city that had absorbed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pliny gives the Roman account of the whole process, praising Italy as the land chosen by the gods to unite the discordant tongues of so many nations by the ties of one common language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is Rome describing itself, and the tongues were less united than replaced: the request from Cumae and the stage-Oscan at Rome are what the process looked like from underneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","history","culture","language","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Cumae","at":[14.0533,40.8483],"zoom":8,"within":"Cumae"},"sources":["Livy, <i>History of Rome</i> 40.43, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=40:chapter=43. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 5.3.6, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/5C*.html. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 3.6, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=3:chapter=6. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Cumae,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=cumae-geo. [Open access]","“Colonia,” in William Smith, ed., <i>A Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities</i> (London: John Murray, 1875), 315–20, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Colonia.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg/1280px-OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg","title":"An Oscan inscription from Pompeii, the language Latin displaced","desc":"A stone slab cut with Oscan letters filled in red, found in the inner arch of the Nola gate at Pompeii and dated by the British Museum to between about 300 and 100 BCE. It reads from right to left and records that Vibius Popidius, son of Vibius, chief magistrate, was responsible for the work and approved it. Public building at Pompeii was still being recorded in Oscan in the years when Cumae, along the same bay, asked leave to keep its records in Latin.","credit":"Félix Potuit, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OsqueBritishMuseum.jpg","alt":"A broken rectangular slab of pale stone photographed on a blue ground, cut with five lines of angular letters picked out in red, several of them reversed because the text runs from right to left."},"why":[{"q":"Why is a request the clearest sign of the change?","a":"Because in 180 BCE the people of Cumae asked the senate for leave to use Latin in their public business, which is the shift arriving from underneath rather than being imposed."},{"q":"Why is Cumae a revealing case?","a":"Because its public language had already changed once — a Greek foundation taken by Campanians, then Roman without the vote — so the request measures a shrinking Greek element rather than a defeat."},{"q":"Why should Pliny’s praise be read as Rome describing itself?","a":"Because the tongues were less united than replaced, and the stage-Oscan recited at Rome shows a language outlasting the people who spoke it as a performance."}]},
{"id":"rm-180","num":180,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"By 264 BCE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached from the Po to the strait, held by treaties, colonies and grants of citizenship rather than by any province or governor.","questions":["The last piece of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Rhegium, held by a Roman garrison that had taken the city it was sent to protect, and recovered with grain and soldiers from Hiero of Syracuse.","In the year that completed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the people voted to help the Mamertines, and Appius Claudius was sent across the strait to Messana."],"answer":"Roman Italy","answerText":"Roman Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Complete by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">264 BCE, all but the Celts</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Rhegium recovered shortly before</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Roman Italy</b> was complete by 264 BCE: Polybius says that having driven Pyrrhus out the Romans subdued everyone who had taken his side and reduced all the peoples of Italy except the Celts, and then turned on their own citizens holding Rhegium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That garrison had been sent to protect the city and had taken it instead, and Rome besieged it in earnest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The besiegers went short of food until Hiero of Syracuse sent grain and soldiers, after which the place was taken, the surviving townspeople were given their city back and the mutineers were punished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Frontinus records the punishment: four thousand men of the legion that had plundered Rhegium without orders were put under guard and executed, and the senate made it a crime to bury any of them or to mourn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What held the rest of Italy was not a province but a web of treaties, colonies and grants of citizenship, which Velleius catalogued from Sutrium to Venusia with the peoples admitted to the citizenship beside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the same year a question arrived from outside the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Mamertines, mercenaries holding Messana across the strait, asked Rome for help against Carthage and Hiero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius says the senate could not decide, since helping men who had seized a city was as inconsistent as the advantages were plain, and it left the question to the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They had suffered in the wars and wanted the losses repaired, and the commanders told them that individuals would gain manifest benefits too, so they voted for the aid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Appius Claudius was elected to the command and sent across to Messana, which is where the conquest of Italy ends and the war with Carthage begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["state","history","politics","geography","italy"],"locator":{"name":"Rome","at":[12.4964,41.9028],"zoom":6,"within":"Rome"},"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.6, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=6. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.11, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=11. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.14, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i>, Fragments of Book X, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/10*.html. [Open access]","Frontinus, <i>Strategemata</i> 4.1.38, trans. Charles E. Bennett, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Frontinus/Strategemata/4*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome besiege a city its own garrison held?","a":"Because the men had been sent to protect Rhegium and had taken it instead, so the survivors were given their city back and the mutineers punished."},{"q":"Why was the punishment extended to mourning?","a":"Because the senate made it a crime to bury any of the four thousand executed or to mourn them, so the penalty reached past the men themselves."},{"q":"Why could the senate not decide about the Mamertines?","a":"Because helping men who had seized a city was as inconsistent as the advantages were plain, so it left the question to the people, who voted for the aid."}]},
{"id":"rm-181","num":181,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Strabo describes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a walled peninsula of 360 stadia, its neck given over to elephant-stalls and its citadel called the Byrsa.","questions":["Velleius dates the founding of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to 65 years before Rome and gives the founder as the Tyrian Elissa, whom some authors call Dido.","Rome feared that if Sicily fell too, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would surround it on every side, being already mistress of Libya, part of Iberia and the islands of two seas."],"answer":"Carthage","answerText":"Carthage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">65 years before Rome, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">walled circuit of 360 stadia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicilian wars</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 410 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Carthage</b> stood on a peninsula of the North African coast, and Strabo gives its walled circuit as 360 stadia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sixty stadia of that circuit was the neck joining the peninsula to the mainland, a spacious place where the Carthaginians kept their elephant-stalls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Near the middle of the city rose the citadel they called the Byrsa, a fairly steep height built over on every side, with a temple of Asclepius at the top which the wife of Hasdrubal burnt along with herself when the city was taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Below the citadel lay the harbours and the Cothon, a circular island in a strait with ship-houses ranged round both sides of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo says the city was founded by Dido, who brought a host of people out of Tyre, and that the Phoenician colonising which followed reached Iberia and the country beyond the Pillars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Velleius puts the foundation 65 years before that of Rome and names the founder the Tyrian Elissa, called Dido by some authors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Smith’s geographical dictionary divides Carthaginian history at 410 BCE, when an appeal from Egesta drew Carthage into the quarrels of the Sicilian Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The century and a half that followed was spent against Dionysius, Timoleon and Agathocles, whose invasion of Africa was defeated but showed where Carthaginian power was vulnerable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the 260s BCE, Polybius says, that power was not confined to Libya but had taken in districts of Iberia as well, and Carthage was mistress of every island in the Sardinian and Tyrrhenian seas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was the prospect of Sicily going the same way that decided Rome, which feared neighbours who would surround it on every side and command the coasts of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["city","place","history","geography","africa"],"locator":{"name":"Carthage","at":[10.3233,36.8528],"zoom":7,"within":"Carthage"},"sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 17.3.14, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/17C*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 17.3.15, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/17C*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.6, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Carthago,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=carthago-geo. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.10, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=10. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Ancient_Carthage_site_map_in_1840s.png/1280px-Ancient_Carthage_site_map_in_1840s.png","title":"The site of Carthage, surveyed in the 1840s","desc":"An engraved plan of the peninsula and the lake of Tunis from Edward Cresy's encyclopaedia of civil engineering, published in 1847. It marks the line of the ancient city wall across the neck, the hill of the Byrsa, the Cothon and the remains of the quays — the same features Strabo lists in his description of the city.","credit":"Edward Cresy, An Encyclopaedia of Civil Engineering (1847), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Carthage_site_map_in_1840s.png","alt":"A hatched nineteenth-century engraved map showing a large lagoon with Tunis at one end and, at the other, a headland labelled with the ruins of Carthage, the Byrsa, the Cothon and a dotted line marked as the ancient city wall."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Sicily rather than Africa decide Rome?","a":"Because Carthage was already mistress of every island in the Sardinian and Tyrrhenian seas, and Rome feared neighbours who would surround it on every side and command the coasts of Italy."},{"q":"Why is the city’s history divided at 410 BCE?","a":"Because an appeal from Egesta drew Carthage into the quarrels of the Sicilian Greeks, and the century and a half after was spent against Dionysius, Timoleon and Agathocles."},{"q":"Why did the invasion of Africa matter though it failed?","a":"Because Agathocles’ defeat still showed where Carthaginian power was vulnerable, which is what an attack on the home country could reveal."}]},
{"id":"rm-182","num":182,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says the public war chest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was discharged entirely from the tribute paid by the people of Libya, while its private wealth came from the produce of the land.","questions":["Half of all agricultural produce and a doubled town tribute were the exactions by which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was held together, and they left the Libyan country people needing no urging to revolt.","Iberians, Celts, Ligurians, Balearic islanders and a main body of Libyans made up the hired armies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a mixture Polybius thought deliberate because such troops could not combine quickly."],"answer":"Carthaginian empire","answerText":"Carthaginian empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Invaded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">310 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">by Agathocles of Syracuse</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Libyan revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">241 – 237 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Carthaginian empire</b> rested on three things Polybius names together: private wealth came from the land, public spending on war material from the tribute paid by the people of Libya, and wars were fought by hired troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land was the richest part of it, and Diodorus, describing the country Agathocles marched through in 310 BCE, found gardens and plantations watered by small channels, stuccoed country houses, vines, olives, herds of cattle and grazing horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The leading Carthaginians had laid out their private estates there, so the countryside nearest the city belonged to the governing families as much as to the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beyond that home district lay the Libyan towns and tribes, whom Polybius calls simply Libyans, and whose own tribal names Smith takes to have been lost in their complete subjection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their neighbours the Numidians were not subjects but free peoples of Africa who served in Carthaginian armies for pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Armies were raised wherever Carthage could hire them, and the force mustered at Sicca in 241 BCE held Iberians, Celts, Ligurians, Balearic islanders, half-Greek deserters and slaves, with Libyans as its main body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius thought the mixture deliberate, since an army of several races cannot combine quickly to mutiny, but adds that it fails once anger has broken out, since no commander can know all their languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Subject cities paid in kind too, and the elephants stalled in the vaulted chambers of the landward wall were kept up partly by tribute demanded from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius states the exactions plainly: during the war with Rome Carthage took half of all agricultural produce, doubled the tribute of the towns, and reserved its rewards for generals like Hanno who squeezed hardest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So when the mercenaries mutinied the country people needed no urging to join them, and the women stripped off their ornaments to pay the rebels, as cities had gone over to Agathocles out of hatred for the Carthaginians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["state","history","warfare","geography","africa"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.71, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=71. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 20.8, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1954), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/20A*.html. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Carthago,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=carthago-geo. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.67, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=67. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.72, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=72. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 20.17, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1954), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/20A*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/D421-_Empire_Carthaginois.-L2-Ch9.png/1280px-D421-_Empire_Carthaginois.-L2-Ch9.png","title":"The reach of Carthage, mapped in 1905","desc":"A plate headed Empire Carthaginois from the second volume of Élisée Reclus’s L’Homme et la Terre, published in 1905. It leaves unshaded the sea and the coasts Reclus took to lie within Carthage’s reach, and names Carthage itself, Carthagena and Tartessos in Iberia, the Baleares, Corsica, Sardinia, Sicily and Malta, along with the Atlantic landmarks of the Carthaginian voyages — Cerne, the Fortunate Isles and the Cassiterides. The shading marks a sphere of activity as Reclus understood it and not a governed frontier.","credit":"Élisée Reclus, L’Homme et la Terre, tome 2 (Paris: Librairie Universelle, 1905), 421, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D421-_Empire_Carthaginois.-L2-Ch9.png","alt":"A grey engraved map of the western Mediterranean and the Atlantic coast of Africa, the sea and coastal fringe left white against a stippled interior, with Carthage, Carthagena, Tartessos, the Baleares, Corsica, Sardinia, Sicily and Malta named, and the Sahara filling the lower half."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Polybius think a mixed army an advantage?","a":"Because an army of several races cannot combine quickly to mutiny — though he adds it fails once anger has broken out, since no commander can know all their languages."},{"q":"Why did the country people join the mercenaries without urging?","a":"Because during the war Carthage had taken half of all agricultural produce, doubled the towns’ tribute and reserved its rewards for the generals who squeezed hardest."},{"q":"Why was the home district not simply state territory?","a":"Because the leading Carthaginians had laid out their private estates there, so the countryside nearest the city belonged to the governing families as much as to the state."}]},
{"id":"rm-183","num":183,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Aristotle judged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a good one, most like Sparta’s, noting that no tyrant had arisen and that the populace stayed faithful to it without serious civil strife.","questions":["Two chief magistrates, a council of Elders and a magistracy of 104 stood at the head of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose highest offices Aristotle said were in effect for sale.","Polybius thought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> well contrived at the outset but past its prime by the Hannibalic war, when the popular voice had become supreme and Rome’s senate was at its height."],"answer":"Carthaginian constitution","answerText":"Carthaginian constitution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Suffetes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, presiding over the senate</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Senate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 members</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In decline by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Carthaginian constitution</b> is known almost entirely from Greek and Roman observers, the city’s own libraries having gone to the princes of Numidia with only Mago’s 32 books on agriculture kept for translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Smith’s geographical dictionary, summarising what survives, warns that the facts are too few and too indistinct to show how it worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle nevertheless thought it good, most nearly resembling Sparta’s, and took as proof that the populace stayed faithful and that neither serious civil strife nor a tyrant had arisen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its magistracies answered to Spartan ones: common mess-tables, a Hundred and Four corresponding to the ephors but elected by merit, and kings and Elders not confined to one family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those kings were the two chief magistrates known as suffetes, a name probably the Hebrew shofetim, or judges, who presided over the senate and came from a few principal families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Kings and Elders put business to the assembly when they agreed, but anything they did not agree on went to the people, and anyone might speak against a proposal, a right Aristotle says no other constitution allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that he set the oligarchic features: Boards of Five filled by co-optation controlled much important business, elected the Hundred, held authority before and after their terms, and judged all lawsuits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Worse, he thought, was that office went by wealth as well as merit, so that the kingship and the generalship were in effect for sale, and holding several at once was counted a distinction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He noted too that the state kept its peace by sending parties of citizens out to posts in the subject cities, the work of fortune rather than of the lawgiver, the laws offering no remedy if those subjects revolted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius agreed that kings, a Gerousia with aristocratic powers and a multitude supreme in its own affairs had been well contrived, but held that by the Hannibalic war the popular voice was supreme at Carthage while the Roman senate was at its height.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","state","history","politics","africa"],"sources":["William Smith, ed., “Carthago,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=carthago-geo. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.11 (1272b), trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1272b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.11 (1273a), trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1273a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.11 (1273b), trans. H. Rackham, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1944), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1273b. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 6.51, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=6:chapter=51. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the constitution known almost only from outsiders?","a":"Because the city’s own libraries went to the princes of Numidia, with only Mago’s 32 books on agriculture kept for translation."},{"q":"Why did Aristotle count it a good constitution?","a":"Because the populace stayed faithful and neither serious civil strife nor a tyrant had arisen, which he took as proof of the arrangement rather than of luck."},{"q":"Why did he think its peace owed more to fortune than to law?","a":"Because it was kept by sending parties of citizens out to posts in the subject cities, and the laws offered no remedy if those subjects revolted."}]},
{"id":"rm-184","num":184,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome had not a single galley when it first crossed to Messana, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> having held undisputed command of the western sea for generations before its rival went afloat.","questions":["Appian describes the home port of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as an island ringed by shipyards for 220 vessels, behind a sea entrance that could be shut with iron chains.","At Drepana the faster, better-built hulls of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rowed round their pursuers and charged the broadsides of ships lurching under their own weight and unskilful crews."],"answer":"Carthaginian navy","answerText":"Carthaginian navy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Docks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">220 ships at Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome's fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">120 ships, 261 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500, to Rome's 700</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Carthaginian navy</b> held undisputed command of the western sea for generations before Rome went afloat: when the Romans first ferried troops to Messana they borrowed quinqueremes and triremes from Tarentum, Locri, Elea and Naples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius says they had no war-ship of their own, not a single galley, and learnt to build the type only when a Carthaginian decked vessel ran aground in the Strait and fell into their hands as a model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without that accident, he adds, want of constructive knowledge would have stopped the design; the senate resolved in 261 BCE on 100 quinqueremes and 20 triremes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fleet lay behind the merchant port at Carthage in a naval harbour Appian describes as enclosing an island, its embankments lined with shipyards for 220 vessels and a sea entrance about 20 metres (66 feet) wide that iron chains could close.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two Ionic columns stood before each dock so that harbour and island seemed ringed by one portico, and the admiral’s house on the island stood high enough to watch the sea while nobody outside could see in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the ships were for shows at Drepana, where Polybius credits the Carthaginian victory to faster, better-built hulls and to crews who could row round a pursuer and charge his broadside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Roman ships lurched awkwardly from their weight and the unskilfulness of their crews, and could not sail through an enemy line to come up astern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scale was without precedent: two of its sea-fights put over 500 quinqueremes and nearly 700 into the water, and across 24 years Rome lost 700 and Carthage 500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the last battle the positions were reversed, the Roman hulls lightened and their rowers trained together while the Carthaginian ships went out laden, their oarsmen untrained and their marines raw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius explains it by saying the Carthaginian government never expected Rome to dispute the sea again and had neglected the fleet, at a cost that day of 50 ships sunk and 70 taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["institution","warfare","history","technology","africa"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.20, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=20. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 96, trans. Horace White (London: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-20/. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.51, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=51. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.63, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=63. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.61, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=61. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Carthage-1958-PortsPuniques.jpg/1280px-Carthage-1958-PortsPuniques.jpg","title":"The naval harbour at Carthage, from the air","desc":"A hand-coloured aerial postcard of the Punic ports at Carthage, printed before 1958. The circular basin with an island at its centre is the naval harbour, the shape Appian describes as ringed by shipyards for 220 vessels with the admiral’s house on the island; the hill of the Byrsa rises behind it and the open sea lies along the right-hand edge.","credit":"Postcard of the Punic ports of Carthage, before 1958, author unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthage-1958-PortsPuniques.jpg","alt":"A hand-tinted aerial photograph of a coastal suburb of white flat-roofed houses, with a broad circular lagoon in the middle holding a low grassy island, a bare hill beyond it and the sea along the right-hand edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why could Rome build a fleet at all?","a":"Because a Carthaginian decked vessel ran aground in the Strait and fell into their hands as a model; Polybius says want of constructive knowledge would otherwise have stopped the design."},{"q":"Why did the Carthaginians win at Drepana?","a":"Because their hulls were faster and better built and their crews could row round a pursuer and charge his broadside, where Roman ships lurched from weight and unskilful rowing."},{"q":"Why were those advantages reversed in the last battle?","a":"Because the government never expected Rome to dispute the sea again and had neglected the fleet, so its ships went out laden with untrained oarsmen and raw marines."}]},
{"id":"rm-185","num":185,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius copied out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from tablets of brass in the treasury of the aediles, the earliest of them struck in the consulship of the year the kings were expelled.","questions":["No Roman ship might sail beyond the Fair Promontory under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and one driven ashore could take only what repaired it and served the gods before leaving within five days.","Polybius set out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in full to refute a claim that Rome had broken its oath by crossing into Sicily, saying that no such compact had ever existed."],"answer":"Roman-Carthaginian treaties","answerText":"Roman-Carthaginian treaties","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">509 – 508 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 306 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Third</span><span class=\"dt-v\">279 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kept on brass</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 texts, at Rome</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Roman-Carthaginian treaties</b> are three agreements Polybius says survived in his day on brass in the aediles’ treasury by the temple of Jupiter Capitolinus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He dates the first to the consulship of Lucius Junius Brutus and Marcus Horatius, the first after the kings were expelled, and warns that its Latin is so archaic that the best Roman scholars could hardly construe some of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It barred Romans and their allies from sailing beyond the Fair Promontory north of Carthage, and any driven ashore might take only what repaired his ship and served the gods before leaving in five days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Trade in Libya or Sardinia had to be struck before a herald or town-clerk, the price secured on the credit of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In return Carthage undertook to injure no Latin town subject to Rome, to hand over unharmed any it took that was not, to build no fort in Latium and to pass no night there in arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius reads the wording as a map of each power’s reach: Carthage speaks of Libya and Sardinia as wholly its own but only of its Sicilian province, while Rome stipulates for Latium and never mentions the rest of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second added Tyre and Utica to the Carthaginian side and Mastia and Tarseium to the forbidden line, and shut Romans out of Sardinia and Libya but for provisions and repairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The third, made as Pyrrhus crossed to Sicily, turned the two into allies: Carthage would furnish the ships, each side pay its own men, and no crew be forced ashore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each was sworn, the Carthaginians by their ancestral gods and the Romans by Jupiter Lapis, whose commissioner held a stone, asked to be cast out as it was if he broke faith, then threw it away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius sets the texts out to refute Philinus, who had written that a treaty kept Romans from Sicily and Carthaginians from Italy, broken by Rome in crossing; no such compact, he says, ever existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["event","history","politics","warfare","rome"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 3.26, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=3:chapter=26. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 3.22, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=3:chapter=22. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 3.24, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=3:chapter=24. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 3.25, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=3:chapter=25. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 3.23, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=3:chapter=23. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Polybius warn about the language of the first treaty?","a":"Because its Latin is so archaic that the best Roman scholars could hardly construe some of it, so the text he prints is already a reading of an obscure original."},{"q":"Why does the wording map each power’s reach?","a":"Because Carthage speaks of Libya and Sardinia as wholly its own but only of its Sicilian province, while Rome stipulates for Latium and never mentions the rest of Italy."},{"q":"Why does he set the texts out at all?","a":"Because they refute Philinus, who wrote that a treaty kept Romans from Sicily and was broken by their crossing — and Polybius says no such compact ever existed."}]},
{"id":"rm-186","num":186,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome and Carthage fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the possession of Sicily across 24 years, a contest Polybius calls the longest and most severely contested he knew of in history.","questions":["Rome built its first fleet during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, having resolved on 100 quinqueremes and 20 triremes at a time when its shipwrights had never seen a vessel of that description.","Xanthippus destroyed a Roman army in Africa during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, his elephants breaking the maniples in front of them while his cavalry outflanked the rest."],"answer":"First Punic War","answerText":"First Punic War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">264 – 241 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Africa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">255 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2,200 talents, raised to 3,200</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>First Punic War</b> was fought between Rome and Carthage for the possession of Sicily from 264 to 241 BCE, and Polybius treats it at greater length than later wars as the better basis for comparing the two powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It settled into a struggle for the Sicilian cities, where Carthaginian sea power held the coastal towns back even as the inland ones came over from dread of the Roman army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is why Rome built a navy from nothing, resolving on 100 quinqueremes and 20 triremes when its shipwrights had never seen a vessel of the kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Romans then carried the war to Africa, and by the spring of 255 BCE a defeat on land and another at sea, with a Numidian inroad besides, had driven the country people into Carthage, which faced famine and expected a siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Regulus, thinking the city as good as taken and anxious lest a successor rob him of the glory, offered terms the Carthaginian commissioners could hardly bear to hear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Their refusal was answered by Xanthippus, whose elephants broke the maniples in front of them while his cavalry outflanked the rest, and the Roman army in Africa was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war went back to Sicily and to the sea, and Polybius calls the whole of it the longest, most continuous and most severely contested war known to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It ended when Hamilcar Barca, holding out in Sicily with no supplies reaching him, judged nothing more could be saved and sent to treat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Lutatius listened because Rome’s resources were at their lowest ebb, and agreed that Carthage should leave Sicily, make no war on Hiero or the Syracusans, give up every prisoner without ransom and pay 2,200 Euboic talents over 20 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Roman people refused to ratify that, halved the time, added 1,000 talents and extended the evacuation to every island between Sicily and Italy, so a war of 24 years closed on terms harsher than its own victorious general had asked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","warfare","history","politics","rome"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.13, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=13. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.20, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=20. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.31, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=31. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.34, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=34. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.63, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=63. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.62, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=62. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Map_to_illustrate_Punic_Wars_-_Pg-41.jpg","title":"The theatre of the Punic wars, mapped in 1906","desc":"A line map of the western Mediterranean from Fred T. Jane's Heresies of Sea Power, published in 1906. Its Sicilian names are the places of this war — Drepanum, Lilybaeum, Ecnomus, Tyndaris and the Aegates islands off the western cape, where the last battle was fought — with Carthage, Utica and Tunis on the African shore opposite. The route across the Pyrenees and the names in Italy and Spain belong to the second war rather than the first.","credit":"Fred T. Jane, Heresies of Sea Power (London, 1906), 41, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_to_illustrate_Punic_Wars_-_Pg-41.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white outline map of the western Mediterranean showing Spain, France, Italy, Sicily and the North African coast, with battle sites and towns lettered in italic and a scale bar in geographical miles below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome have to build a navy from nothing?","a":"Because the war settled into a struggle for the Sicilian cities, where Carthaginian sea power held the coastal towns back even as the inland ones came over."},{"q":"Why did the African expedition fail after nearly succeeding?","a":"Because Regulus, thinking the city as good as taken and fearing a successor would rob him of the glory, offered terms the commissioners could hardly bear to hear, and the refusal was answered by Xanthippus."},{"q":"Why were the final terms harsher than the general had agreed?","a":"Because Lutatius listened when Rome’s resources were at their lowest ebb, and the people then refused to ratify, halving the time, adding 1,000 talents and extending the evacuation."}]},
{"id":"rm-187","num":187,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Campanian mercenaries of Agathocles who took Messana by treachery called themselves the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and levied tribute from many parts of Sicily until the Syracusans drove them back inside their walls.","questions":["A Roman garrison at Rhegium copied the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and seized the city it had been sent to guard, for which more than 300 survivors were scourged and beheaded in the forum.","Rome debated long before helping the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, having just executed its own citizens for the very thing they had done, and voted for it out of fear that Carthage would take Sicily."],"answer":"Mamertines","answerText":"Mamertines","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rhegium seized</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by a garrison of 4,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Executions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 300, in 271 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Appealed to Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">264 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Mamertines</b> were Campanian mercenaries of Agathocles who seized Messana, entering as friends and then driving out some of the citizens, killing others, and taking their wives, houses and land for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name they gave themselves means ‘the children of Mars’, from Mamers, an Oscan name of that god which is found in old Latin as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When they took the city is nowhere stated: it must fall after the death of Agathocles in 289 BCE and not long before the seizure of Rhegium, and the year 282 usually given for it is a conjecture rather than a record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their success found imitators at once, a Roman garrison of 4,000 sent to Rhegium under a Campanian named Decius Jubellius doing the same to that city in imitation of them and with their help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome could not move while other wars lasted, but afterwards besieged the garrison, killed most of them in the assault and sent more than 300 survivors to be scourged and beheaded in the forum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> While Rhegium held, the Mamertines ruled their own town and district undisturbed, gave trouble to Carthaginians and Syracusans alike, and levied tribute from many parts of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With that support gone they were defeated by Hiero of Syracuse and forced back inside their walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The survivors then split, some offering themselves and their citadel to Carthage while others sent to Rome to surrender the city and beg help on the ground of shared race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius says the Romans were long in doubt, having just put their own citizens to death for breaking faith at Rhegium and being asked now to assist men who had done precisely the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What decided it was the fear that Carthage, already holding Libya, districts of Iberia and the islands, would take Sicily too and gain what he calls a bridge into Italy; the senate could not agree, and the people voted the aid in 264 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["people","history","warfare","politics","sicily"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.7, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=7. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.8, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=8. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.10, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=10. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.11, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=11. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Messana,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=messana-geo. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Mamertini_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5562061.jpg","title":"A Mamertine bronze coin, both faces","desc":"A bronze piece of 6.9 grams (0.24 ounces) struck at Messana and dated by the Berlin cabinet to about 288 to 241 BCE. The obverse carries a laureate head of Ares with a spear point behind it, and the reverse an eagle with spread wings standing on a thunderbolt, around it the legend MAMEPTINΩN — the name these people had given themselves, from Mamers, the Oscan name of that same god.","credit":"Münzkabinett der Staatlichen Museen zu Berlin, photograph by Reinhard Saczewski, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamertini_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5562061.jpg","alt":"Two views of a small, heavily green-patinated bronze coin side by side: on the left a laureate male head in profile facing right, on the right a bird with spread wings standing on a bundled thunderbolt, both worn."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Rome long in doubt about helping them?","a":"Because it had just put its own citizens to death for doing at Rhegium precisely what these men had done at Messana, and was being asked to assist them."},{"q":"Why did their position collapse when Rhegium fell?","a":"Because while it held they ruled their district undisturbed and levied tribute across Sicily, and with that support gone Hiero beat them back inside their walls."},{"q":"Why did Rome decide as it did in the end?","a":"Because it feared Carthage, already holding Libya, districts of Iberia and the islands, would take Sicily too and gain what Polybius calls a bridge into Italy."}]},
{"id":"rm-188","num":188,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Anaxilas of Rhegium expelled the Samians from Zancle, settled a mixed population there and renamed the city <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after the land in Greece his own family came from.","questions":["Appius Claudius crossed the strait by night into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 264 BCE and drove off in turn the Carthaginians and Hiero of Syracuse, who were besieging it together.","Strabo puts <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only 60 stadia across the water from Rhegium, with Charybdis in the channel a short way off and a harbour closed by a natural mole of sand."],"answer":"Messana","answerText":"Messana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken by Samians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 493 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Messene, by 476 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman base</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 264 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Across to Italy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60 stadia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Messana</b> stood on the Sicilian side of the strait in a bay of Cape Pelorus, 60 stadia from Rhegium by Strabo’s reckoning, and is the modern Messina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its harbour is closed by a curving spit of sand making a natural mole, and held 600 ships by Diodorus’ reckoning; Charybdis lies in the channel a short way off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The older name was Zancle, which Thucydides says the Sicels gave it because the place is shaped like a sickle, ‘zanklon’ in their tongue, while Strabo derives it from the crookedness of the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two differ about the founding too: Thucydides has pirates from Cumae settle it, then Chalcidians and Euboeans under Perieres and Crataemenes, while Strabo makes it Naxian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those settlers were driven out by Samians and other Ionians fleeing the Medes, who seized the city at the urging of Anaxilas of Rhegium while its men were away besieging another town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Zanclaeans called in their ally Hippocrates of Gela, who instead chained their ruler Scythes for the loss, took half the goods and slaves, kept most of the citizens and handed 300 leading men to the Samians to kill, which they did not do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anaxilas then expelled the Samians in their turn, settled a mixed population there and changed the name to Messene, after the land his own family came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Doric form Messana was what the citizens used and the Romans wrote, and Strabo says the Mamertines so dominated the place that everyone called the people Mamertini and the wine Mamertine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 264 BCE the Mamertines held the town and a Carthaginian garrison the citadel, which they ousted when help came; Appius Claudius crossed the strait by night and drove off in turn the Carthaginians and Hiero of Syracuse, besieging it together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rome used it as the base of its Sicilian war thereafter, and at the peace the Mamertines had their treaty renewed and the standing of an allied city while passing in fact under Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["city","place","history","geography","sicily"],"sources":["William Smith, ed., “Messana,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=messana-geo. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.4, trans. Richard Crawley (London: J. M. Dent, 1910), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=4. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.23, trans. A. D. Godley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=23. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.11, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=11. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 6.2.3, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/6B*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/43/Zankle_%28Messana%29_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett%2C_Berlin_-_5499399.jpg","title":"A silver coin of Zancle, both faces","desc":"A tetradrachm of 17 grams (0.6 ounces) dated by the Berlin cabinet to 493 to 488 BCE, which is the span in which the Samians took the city. The obverse carries a lion's scalp seen from the front on a shield-like dome; the reverse shows a ship's prow with its ram to the left, its planking, its railing and the eye marked on the ornament above the beak, set in a shallow round depression within a circle of dots.","credit":"Münzkabinett der Staatlichen Museen zu Berlin, photograph by Reinhard Saczewski, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zankle_%28Messana%29_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5499399.jpg","alt":"Two views of a silver coin side by side: on the left a lion's face seen head-on filling a domed circle, on the right the beaked prow of a ship in profile pointing left, ringed by a border of dots."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the city’s name change more than once?","a":"Because Anaxilas expelled the Samians who had seized it, settled a mixed population and renamed it after the land his own family came from."},{"q":"Why did calling in an ally cost the Zanclaeans their city?","a":"Because Hippocrates of Gela chained their own ruler for the loss, took half the goods and slaves, kept most of the citizens and handed 300 leading men to the Samians."},{"q":"Why did Rome keep it as an allied city?","a":"Because it had been the base of the Sicilian war, so at the peace the Mamertines had their treaty renewed and the standing of allies while passing in fact under Rome."}]},
{"id":"rm-189","num":189,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> won the rule of Syracuse by his own abilities, inheriting neither wealth nor reputation, and became king without killing, banishing or harassing a single citizen.","questions":["Beating the Mamertines on the Mylaean plain by the river Longanus earned <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the title of king from all the allies on his return to Syracuse.","Rome accepted peace from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chiefly for his provisions, taking 100 talents and its prisoners back without ransom, and he supplied its needs for the rest of his life."],"answer":"Hiero II","answerText":"Hiero II","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">269 – 215 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Beat Mamertines</span><span class=\"dt-v\">268 BCE, at the Longanus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Paid Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 talents, 263 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 90 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hiero II</b> ruled Syracuse from 269 to 215 BCE, and Polybius says he won that place by his own abilities, inheriting neither wealth nor reputation nor any advantage of fortune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He came to it through the army, which had quarrelled with the citizens and elected its own commanders near Mergane, choosing Artemidorus and Hiero, then a young man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made his way into the city, got his opponents into his hands and governed so mildly that the Syracusans, who did not usually accept officers chosen by soldiers, approved him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then married the daughter of Leptines, the most influential man among the common people, so his interests would be represented at home whenever he took the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The old mercenaries were mutinous, so he marched against the Mamertines, thrust them into the front line, let them be cut to pieces and brought the citizens safely home, a stroke Polybius calls skilful and successful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> With mercenaries of his own hiring he armed and drilled the citizen levies, beat the Mamertines by the river Longanus, took their leaders prisoner and came home to be hailed as king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Mamertines called Rome in, he saw a chance to be rid of the foreigners in Messana for good, made a treaty with Carthage and camped against the city on the Chalcidian Mount.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> But when Rome sent both consuls with four legions in 263 BCE and the Sicilian cities began changing sides, he judged Roman prospects the better and asked for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Romans took it chiefly for the provisions, fearing they would be cut off at sea; he gave up his prisoners without ransom, paid 100 talents of silver and kept the bargain for life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius calls it the most astonishing thing about him that he became king without putting to death, banishing or harassing a single citizen, a claim about citizens, not the mercenaries he had spent, and that he reigned 54 years and lived past 90.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","sicily"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.8, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=8. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.9, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=9. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.11, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=11. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.16, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=16. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 7.8, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=7:chapter=8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Dinastie_della_grecia_occidentale%2C_hieron_II%2C_32_litri_di_siracusa%2C_274-216_ac_ca.JPG/1280px-Dinastie_della_grecia_occidentale%2C_hieron_II%2C_32_litri_di_siracusa%2C_274-216_ac_ca.JPG","title":"A silver 32-litra piece of Syracuse","desc":"A coin in the Altes Museum in Berlin, catalogued to Hiero's own reign and dated there to about 274 to 216 BCE. The diademed head facing left is taken for the king's portrait, struck by his own mint; the diadem is the band of cloth a Hellenistic king wore, and its appearance on Syracusan silver marks the change from the generalship the army gave him to the kingship the allies hailed after the Longanus.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinastie_della_grecia_occidentale,_hieron_II,_32_litri_di_siracusa,_274-216_ac_ca.JPG","alt":"A silver coin photographed against grey cloth, showing in high relief the head of a clean-shaven man in profile facing left, his curling hair bound by a plain flat band tied behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he marry the daughter of Leptines?","a":"Because Leptines was the most influential man among the common people, so his interests would be represented at home whenever he took the field."},{"q":"Why did he put the old mercenaries in the front line?","a":"Because they were mutinous, so he let them be cut to pieces by the Mamertines and brought the citizens safely home — a stroke Polybius calls skilful and successful."},{"q":"Why did he change sides in 263 BCE?","a":"Because Rome had sent both consuls with four legions and the Sicilian cities were changing sides, so he judged Roman prospects the better and asked for peace."}]},
{"id":"rm-190","num":190,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The two consuls shut 50,000 Carthaginians inside the city at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and after more than seven months walked into a town its commander had abandoned in the night.","questions":["Hanno’s Numidian horse drew the Roman cavalry onto his line during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and his mercenary vanguard later fell back onto his own elephants in the pitched battle that decided it.","News of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so raised the senate’s hopes that it resolved to drive Carthage out of Sicily altogether, though the siege is said to have cost it more than 30,000 men."],"answer":"siege of Agrigentum","answerText":"siege of Agrigentum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege</span><span class=\"dt-v\">262 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50,000 in the city</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 7 months</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enslaved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25,000 inhabitants</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>siege of Agrigentum</b> in 262 BCE was the first great land action of the First Punic War, fought for the city Carthage had made its Sicilian headquarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hiero’s change of side let Rome cut its army to two legions and moved Carthage to hire Ligurians, Celts and more Iberians still and ship them over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The consuls Lucius Postumius and Quintus Mamilius suspended the rest of the war, bore down with their whole army, camped eight stades off and shut the Carthaginians inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was harvest, so the Romans foraged more boldly than they should have and were caught by a sally, after which each side grew warier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They split into two camps, by the temple of Asclepius and on the Heracleia side, joined by two trenches: the inner against sallies, the outer against relief and smuggled supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The allied cities gathered food for them at nearby Herbesus, and for five months neither side gained anything beyond skirmishing casualties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside were 50,000 men, and Hannibal, commanding them, sent to Carthage again and again until troops and elephants were shipped to Hanno, who took Herbesus by a stratagem and left the besiegers short.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Disease and want thinned the Roman camp, and Hanno, with about 50 elephants, sent his Numidian horse to provoke the Roman cavalry, feign flight and draw it onto his line, where it was cut up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two months later Hannibal’s signals of famine and desertion decided him to fight; the Romans broke the mercenary vanguard, which fell back onto the elephants and threw the army into confusion, and that night the victors watched carelessly while Hannibal choked the trenches with chaff and slipped out at midnight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Romans walked into an undefended town at dawn and plundered it, carrying 25,000 inhabitants into slavery after a siege of over seven months said to have cost them more than 30,000 men, and the news so raised the senate’s hopes that it resolved to drive Carthage out of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","politics","sicily"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.17, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=17. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.18, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=18. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.19, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=19. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Agrigentum,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=agrigentum-geo. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.20, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=20. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Battle_of_Agrigentum_%28Histoire_de_Polybe%2C_Volume_1%29.jpg/1280px-Battle_of_Agrigentum_%28Histoire_de_Polybe%2C_Volume_1%29.jpg","title":"Blocus et bataille d'Agrigente, engraved in 1727","desc":"A plate from the first volume of Dom Vincent Thuillier's French Polybius, printed at Paris in 1727. The city stands on its ridge in the distance; in the foreground the engraver has laid out the lines of the blockade and the two armies drawn up for the battle, numbered to a key, with a row of elephants in the Carthaginian order. It is an eighteenth-century reading of Polybius' text rather than a record of the ground, and the elephants are drawn from the same chapters this card cites.","credit":"Plate from Polybius, Histoire de Polybe, trans. Dom Vincent Thuillier, vol. 1 (Paris, 1727), author unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Agrigentum_%28Histoire_de_Polybe,_Volume_1%29.jpg","alt":"A brown-ink engraving of a wide valley seen from a low hill, framed by trees, with a walled town on a distant ridge and, across the middle ground, long lines of small rectangles and marching figures representing two armies, several drawn as elephants."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Romans dig two trenches rather than one?","a":"Because the inner line was against sallies from the city and the outer against relief and supplies smuggled in from outside."},{"q":"Why did the besiegers end up short of food themselves?","a":"Because Hanno took Herbesus by a stratagem, and the allied cities had been gathering the Roman supplies there."},{"q":"Why did winning the battle not take the city?","a":"Because the victors watched carelessly that night, and Hannibal choked the trenches with chaff and slipped out at midnight, leaving them an undefended town at dawn."}]},
{"id":"rm-191","num":191,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Crews for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were taught to row on dry land, sitting on benches in the order they would keep aboard and pulling together at the word of the celeustes.","questions":["The shipwrights who built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had never seen a quinquereme and took their pattern from a Carthaginian vessel that ran aground in the Strait and fell into Roman hands.","A consul lost 17 ships of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Lipara when a Carthaginian squadron shut him in the harbour by night and his crews ran ashore at daybreak."],"answer":"first Roman fleet","answerText":"first Roman fleet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet voted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">120 ships, 261 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lost at Lipara</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 ships, 260 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Won at Mylae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 Carthaginian ships</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>first Roman fleet</b> was resolved on in 261 BCE, when the senate voted 100 quinqueremes and 20 triremes to break the Carthaginian hold on the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome had no war-ship of its own, not so much as a single galley, and had ferried its first troops across in vessels borrowed from Tarentum, Locri, Elea and Naples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its shipwrights had never built a quinquereme, nobody in Italy having used the type, and they took their pattern from a Carthaginian decked ship that charged too furiously in the Strait, ran aground and fell into Roman hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without that accident, Polybius says, want of constructive knowledge would have stopped the whole design.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> While the hulls were building the crews were taught to row on dry land, seated on benches in the order they would keep aboard with the celeustes among them, learning to draw in their hands together and swing forward again at his word.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> When the ships were finished they were launched, given a brief practice of real sea-rowing, and sent coasting down the Italian shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consul Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio ran ahead to Messana with 17 ships and, snatching too eagerly at an offer to surrender Lipara, anchored off that town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hannibal sent Boodes from Panormus with 20 ships, who came by night and shut him in the harbour; at daybreak the crews made for the shore and ran, and the consul gave himself up with every vessel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius’ verdict on the ships themselves is that they were badly fitted out and not easy to manage, which is why the Romans went looking for something to fight with other than seamanship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What they found was the boarding engine they called the crow, and with it Gaius Duilius beat a Carthaginian fleet off Mylae later the same year, an outcome Polybius insists was no accident but the fruit of discipline learned in the stern school of difficulty and danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","warfare","history","technology","rome"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.20, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=20. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.21, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=21. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.22, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=22. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.23, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.63, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=63. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Aes_grave_di_roma%2C_da_tarquinia_06_asse_con_prua_di_galea.jpg","title":"A Roman as with the prow of a galley","desc":"A cast bronze coin of the heavy Roman series called aes grave, in the Museo archeologico nazionale at Tarquinia. The face shown carries the prow of a galley, worn but legible under the green patina: the beak low on the left, the deck line above it and the hull between. The beak makes it a warship rather than a merchantman, which is the kind of vessel this card is about, though the museum record gives the coin no date of its own.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aes_grave_di_roma,_da_tarquinia_06_asse_con_prua_di_galea.jpg","alt":"A thick, roughly circular bronze disc with a heavy green and brown patina, photographed on a white ground, showing in low worn relief the beaked prow of a ship pointing left with a line of deck above the hull."},"why":[{"q":"Why could Romans learn to row before the ships existed?","a":"Because the crews were seated on benches on dry land in the order they would keep aboard, with the celeustes among them, learning to draw in their hands and swing forward together."},{"q":"Why did the first squadron surrender without a fight?","a":"Because the consul snatched too eagerly at an offer to surrender Lipara and anchored off the town, where 20 Carthaginian ships shut him in the harbour by night."},{"q":"Why did Rome look for something other than seamanship?","a":"Because its ships were badly fitted out and not easy to manage, so the answer was the boarding engine rather than better sailing."}]},
{"id":"rm-192","num":192,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> looked like a machine for braising corn: a pole in the prow, a swinging plank gangway and an iron spike dropped onto an enemy deck.","questions":["Roman marines crossed two abreast over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the leading pair holding their shields before them while those behind rested the rims of theirs on its knee-high rail.","Carthaginian captains at Ecnomus penned a Roman squadron against the shore but would not charge home, for fear of being caught by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its grappling iron."],"answer":"corvus","answerText":"corvus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First used</span><span class=\"dt-v\">260 BCE, off Mylae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">256 BCE, at Ecnomus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gangway</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 m (36 feet) long</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>corvus</b> was a boarding bridge the Romans mounted in the bows of their new warships, contrived, Polybius says, because those ships were badly fitted out and not easy to manage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A round pole about 7.3 m (24 feet) high and four palms thick stood in the prow with a pulley at its top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A gangway of planks nailed together, 1.2 m (4 feet) wide and 11 m (36 feet) long, swung round that pole through an oval hole cut 3.7 m (12 feet) from one end, and carried a knee-high wooden rail down each side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At its far end hung an iron spike like a miller’s pestle, sharpened below and ringed above, and a rope from the ring ran over the pulley so the whole thing could be hauled up and dropped on an enemy deck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once the spike bit into the planking the two ships were grappled fast, and the Romans crossed two abreast, the leading pair holding their shields before them while those behind rested the rims of theirs on the rail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius says the contrivance looked like the machines used for braising corn, and that the Carthaginians, meeting it off Mylae, were perplexed because they were strangers to such engines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They charged anyway out of contempt for Roman seamanship and lost the first 30 ships engaged, crews and all, in a fight that became exactly like a land battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the rest sheered off and tried to come round on the broadside and the stern, the engines swung to meet them in every direction, and the Carthaginians fled with 50 ships lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It served again at Ecnomus, where a Roman squadron was penned against the shore by Carthaginians who would not charge home for fear of being caught by the grappling-irons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After that it disappears from the narrative without explanation, and when a fleet of 364 ships was wrecked off Camarina with 80 left, Polybius blamed commanders who ignored their pilots rather than anything the ships carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","warfare","technology","history","rome"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.22, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=22. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.23, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.27, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=27. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.28, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=28. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.37, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=37. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/Corvus_%C3%A4nterbrygga.png","title":"A 1926 reconstruction of the boarding bridge","desc":"A line engraving from the Swedish encyclopaedia Uppfinningarna, published in 1926, showing a warship's bow with the bridge raised: the pole marked B with a rope running over a block at its head down to a windlass on the deck, and the gangway marked A with its side rails and a hooked spike at the outer end, above a three-finned ram. It is a modern draughtsman's reading of Polybius' description and not evidence of its own, and it differs from the text in places — Polybius has the gangway swing round the pole through a hole cut in it, and calls the spike a pestle rather than a hook.","credit":"Figure from Uppfinningarna, vol. 5 (1926), author unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corvus_%C3%A4nterbrygga.png","alt":"A black-and-white engraving of the bow of an ancient galley cutting through waves, with a tall post on the foredeck lettered B, a long railed gangway lettered A slung from it and projecting forward over the water, and a hooked spike hanging from the gangway's outer end."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the engine turn a sea fight into a land battle?","a":"Because once the spike bit into the enemy planking the two ships were grappled fast, and the Romans crossed two abreast behind their shields."},{"q":"Why did the Carthaginians charge into it anyway?","a":"Because they were strangers to such engines and were perplexed by it, and charged out of contempt for Roman seamanship."},{"q":"Why is its disappearance from the narrative not explained by the losses?","a":"Because when 364 ships were wrecked off Camarina with 80 left, Polybius blamed commanders who ignored their pilots rather than anything the ships carried."}]},
{"id":"rm-193","num":193,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Gaius Duilius won the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 260 BCE after handing his legions to the tribunes, taking over the fleet and sailing against Carthaginians who were plundering the coast.","questions":["A Carthaginian admiral aboard a seven-banked galley that had once been Pyrrhus’s lost 80 ships at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and escaped in his own ship’s boat.","Rome’s first naval triumph followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the column set up in the forum to mark it was still standing there when Pliny wrote."],"answer":"battle of Mylae","answerText":"battle of Mylae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">260 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enemy fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">130 sail, or 200</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30, then 50 more</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>battle of Mylae</b> in 260 BCE was Rome’s first victory at sea, won off northern Sicily by the consul Gaius Duilius with the fleet just built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was commanding the land army when word came that his colleague had lost 17 ships at Lipara, so he handed his legions to the tribunes and took the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hearing that the Carthaginians were plundering the territory of Mylae, he sailed against them with his whole force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They came out with 130 sail, or 200 by Diodorus’ count, in such contempt for Roman seamanship that they kept no order at all but advanced as though to pick up a prize left out for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their admiral was the Hannibal who had slipped out of Agrigentum by night, aboard a galley of seven banks that had once belonged to Pyrrhus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Seeing the boarding engines raised on the Roman prows they were perplexed, being strangers to such things, but charged all the same and lost the first 30 ships engaged, crews and all, in a fight that became exactly like a land battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest sheered off to come round on the broadside and the stern, but the engines swung to meet them, and the Carthaginians fled with 50 more ships gone while Hannibal escaped in the ship’s boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who devised those engines is disputed: Polybius says only that someone suggested them, where Zonaras gives the credit to Duilius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Duilius went on to relieve Egesta and storm Macella, and at Rome he had the city’s first naval triumph, a column in the forum decked with the beaks of the captured ships, and the right ever after to be walked home from a late dinner by a torch-bearer and a flute-player.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The inscription from that column was dug up in the 16th century in a mutilated state, and its spelling and its Greek marble, a stone not used at Rome in his day, suggest it is a restoration rather than the original.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","politics","sicily"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.23, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.22, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=22. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.21, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=21. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Duilius,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology</i> (London: John Murray, 1873), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0104:entry=duilius-bio-5. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>Natural History</i> 34.11, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=34:chapter=11. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/MCR_-_colonna_rostrata_di_C_Duilio_base_1150131.JPG/1280px-MCR_-_colonna_rostrata_di_C_Duilio_base_1150131.JPG","title":"The Duilius inscription, in a museum copy","desc":"A modern reproduction, in the Museo della Civiltà Romana in Rome, of the inscribed base of the rostral column. It stands at two removes from the consul: the marble it copies was itself dug up in the 16th century and is thought to be a later restoration. The archaic spellings the card describes are legible in it — OPSIDIONED for obsidione, PVCNANDOD for pugnando, MACISTRATOS for magistratus — and so are the names of the two places Duilius took after the battle, SECESTANOS and MACELAM, and the rows of numerals recording the gold and silver captured.","credit":"Photograph by Lalupa, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MCR_-_colonna_rostrata_di_C_Duilio_base_1150131.JPG","alt":"A pale stone plinth in a museum gallery, its front face cut with about sixteen lines of Latin capitals in an archaic spelling, the lower lines broken up by long rows of repeated numeral signs."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Carthaginians advance in no order at all?","a":"Because they held Roman seamanship in such contempt that they came on as though to pick up a prize left out for them."},{"q":"Why did losing the first thirty ships settle the battle?","a":"Because the engines made it exactly like a land battle, and when the rest sheered off to come round on the broadside and stern the engines swung to meet them."},{"q":"Why is the column’s inscription thought to be a restoration?","a":"Because its spelling and its Greek marble, a stone not used at Rome in his day, point to a later copy rather than the original."}]},
{"id":"rm-194","num":194,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to force a crossing into Africa, drawing its 330 ships into a wedge with the two consuls in six-banked vessels at its point.","questions":["The Carthaginian centre fled by design at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to pull the Roman formation apart, leaving two squadrons behind while the other two gave chase.","Polybius counts nearly 140,000 Romans against over 150,000 Carthaginians at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which makes it one of the largest actions ever fought at sea."],"answer":"battle of Cape Ecnomus","answerText":"battle of Cape Ecnomus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships</span><span class=\"dt-v\">330 Roman, 350 Punic</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Men</span><span class=\"dt-v\">140,000 and 150,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punic losses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 sunk, 64 taken</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>battle of Cape Ecnomus</b> was fought in 256 BCE off a hill on the south coast of Sicily, at the mouth of the Himera, to decide whether Rome could carry the war into Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Romans came with 330 decked ships and the Carthaginians with 350, and with 300 rowers and 120 soldiers to a Roman vessel, Polybius reckons nearly 140,000 men on one side and over 150,000 on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Carthage meant to stop the crossing, knowing Libya was easily invaded and would resist little once a landing had been made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Facing open water against faster ships, the Romans formed a wedge: the two consuls in six-banked vessels side by side at the point, the first and second squadrons in files diverging behind them, the third in a line across the base towing the horse-transports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind came the fourth squadron, the Triarii, overlapping that line at both ends, so the whole made a triangle with an open apex and a compact base, hard to break up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hanno, beaten at Agrigentum, took the Carthaginian right with the fastest quinqueremes to work round the Roman flank, while Hamilcar held the centre and a fourth of the fleet lay at right angles inshore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Romans charged the thinned centre, which turned and fled by design to pull their order apart, and the first two squadrons chased it while the third and fourth were left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was the stratagem, and it half worked: the third squadron was penned against the shore, but the Carthaginians would not charge home for fear of the grappling-irons, and the returning consuls took 50 ships with their crews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wherever the vessels locked together the fight became a matter of sheer strength, the engines grappling all that came to close quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rome lost 24 ships destroyed and not one taken with its crew, Carthage over 30 destroyed and 64 captured, and the road to Africa was open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","politics","sicily"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.25, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=25. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.26, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=26. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.27, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=27. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.28, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=28. [Open access]","William Smith, ed., “Ecnomus,” in <i>Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography</i> (London: Walton and Maberly, 1854), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=ecnomus-geo. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Battle_of_Cape_Ecnomus.svg/1280px-Battle_of_Cape_Ecnomus.svg.png","title":"The battle in three stages, diagrammed","desc":"A modern diagram of the action, drawn from Polybius' account. The top panel is the opening: the Roman squadrons in blue with the first and second forming the point of the wedge, the transports inside it and the third across the base, against the Carthaginian line in green with the coast of Sicily above. The two lower panels follow the Carthaginian centre's feigned flight and the separate engagements it produced. It is one reconstruction among several and departs from the text in places, and the ships in it are not to scale.","credit":"Diagram by Amitchell125, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Cape_Ecnomus.svg","alt":"A three-panel schematic diagram on a white ground, each panel showing a hatched band labelled Coast of Sicily above blue bars numbered with Roman numerals arranged in a V, and green bars numbered in Arabic figures facing them, with curved arrows in the lower two panels."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Carthage have to fight rather than fall back?","a":"Because it knew Libya was easily invaded and would resist little once a landing had been made, so the crossing had to be stopped at sea."},{"q":"Why did the Romans form a wedge?","a":"Because they faced open water against faster ships, so a triangle with an open apex and a compact base was hard to break up."},{"q":"Why did the feigned flight only half work?","a":"Because it did pull the Roman order apart and pen the third squadron against the shore, but the Carthaginians would not charge home for fear of the grappling-irons."}]},
{"id":"rm-195","num":195,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome beached its fleet behind a stockade under the Hermaeum headland at the start of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, took Aspis, and carried more than 20,000 slaves back to the ships.","questions":["A Spartan named Xanthippus ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by telling Carthage its defeats were owed to unskilful generals, then drawing the army up on flat ground with the elephants in front.","Carthage lost at Adys during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it camped on a steep hill where its cavalry and elephants were useless, the very arms on which its hopes rested."],"answer":"African expedition of 256 BCE","answerText":"African expedition of 256 BCE","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Landed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">256 BCE, at Aspis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Slaves taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">255 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>African expedition of 256 BCE</b> carried a Roman army over from Sicily after Ecnomus, landing under the Hermaeum headland at the east of the Gulf of Carthage and beaching the fleet behind a trench and stockade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Romans took the town of Aspis, garrisoned it and sent home for instructions while they plundered the country unopposed, wrecking houses of remarkably fine construction, driving off cattle and taking more than 20,000 slaves to the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome answered that one consul should stay with a sufficient force and the other bring the fleet home, so Marcus Atilius Regulus was left there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carthage made Hasdrubal son of Hanno and Bostarus generals and called Hamilcar back from Heracleia with 500 cavalry and 5,000 infantry, resolving to defend the country rather than let it be stripped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Adys they took a hill overlooking the Romans that was steep and hard of access, so the cavalry and elephants their hopes rested on were useless, and Regulus came at them from both sides of it at daybreak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> With a defeat on land and another at sea, a Numidian inroad doing more damage than the Romans, and the country people crowded inside the walls, Carthage faced famine and the expectation of a siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Regulus, thinking the city as good as taken and anxious lest a successor rob him of the glory, offered terms the Carthaginian commissioners could hardly bear to hear, and was refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then a recruiting agent came home from Greece with a Spartan named Xanthippus, who said the defeats were owed not to Roman superiority but to unskilful generals, and that keeping to flat ground would settle it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The magistrates heard him and handed him the army, and he drew it up with the elephants in one line across the front, the phalanx behind them, and the mercenaries and cavalry on both wings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The elephants broke the maniples in front of them, the cavalry outflanked the rest, and the Roman army in Africa was destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","politics","africa"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.29, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=29. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.30, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=30. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.31, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=31. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.32, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=32. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.33, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=33. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.34, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=34. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/First_Punic_War_Africa_256-255BC.svg/1280px-First_Punic_War_Africa_256-255BC.svg.png","title":"The campaign in Africa, mapped","desc":"A modern map of the Cape Bon peninsula and the Gulf of Tunis, drawn after a plan in a military history of the war. Its numbers are the stages: 1 the landing and capture of Aspis, 2 the victory at Adys, 3 the move on Tunis, 4 the Carthaginian army coming out under Xanthippus, 5 the battle in which the Roman force was destroyed and Regulus taken, and 6 the retreat to Aspis. The place names are given in Spanish, so Carthage is Cartago, Tunis is Túnez and Aspis is Apsis.","credit":"Map by RedTony after User:Vercingetorix, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Punic_War_Africa_256-255BC.svg","alt":"A simplified coloured map of a North African coastline with a wide gulf, four towns marked with dots, and red arrows numbered one to six curving from a landing point on the eastern cape inland to a town on the gulf and back again, with crossed-sword symbols at two places."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the Carthaginians’ own strengths useless at Adys?","a":"Because they took a hill that was steep and hard of access, so the cavalry and elephants their hopes rested on could not be used."},{"q":"Why did Carthage hand its army to a foreigner?","a":"Because Xanthippus said the defeats were owed not to Roman superiority but to unskilful generals, and that keeping to flat ground would settle it."},{"q":"Why did his line beat the one that had just won twice?","a":"Because the elephants across the front broke the maniples facing them while the cavalry on both wings outflanked the rest."}]},
{"id":"rm-196","num":196,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused all pity to the fallen and was soon begging for his own life, which he offers as a warning against confidence in Fortune.","questions":["Aulus Gellius preserves two accounts of how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died in Carthaginian hands, one with his eyelids sewn open in the sun and the other by being kept from sleep.","Left in Africa with half the fleet gone home, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered terms so harsh that the Carthaginians preferred to fight and hired a Spartan to command for them."],"answer":"Marcus Atilius Regulus","answerText":"Marcus Atilius Regulus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">256 BCE, left in Africa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Captured</span><span class=\"dt-v\">255 BCE, with 500 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Survivors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 2,000 Romans</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Marcus Atilius Regulus</b> was the consul left in Africa in 256 BCE when Rome ordered his colleague home with the fleet, and is remembered less for what he did than for what was later said about his death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He plundered the country, storming unwalled towns and besieging walled ones, and beat a Carthaginian army at Adys that had cooped itself where its cavalry and elephants were useless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thinking the city as good as taken, and anxious that a successor might arrive and rob him of the glory, he offered terms the Carthaginian commissioners could hardly bear to listen to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They concluded that capture could impose nothing worse, refused, and put their army under the Spartan Xanthippus, whose elephants and cavalry destroyed the Roman force the next year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Regulus fled with some 500 men and all were taken; about 2,000 Romans survived the day, and the Carthaginians carried the general back to their capital in exultation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius draws the lesson himself: nobody should be confident of Fortune in the hour of success, since here was a man who had lately refused all pity to the fallen and was soon begging for his own life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What Rome made of him afterwards is a different story, and none of it is in Polybius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aulus Gellius reports from Tuditanus that he was sent home as a prisoner to argue for an exchange, spoke in the senate against one, and said the Carthaginians had given him a slow poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tubero, Gellius adds, wrote that he went back and was killed by tortures of a novel kind, shut in a dark dungeon and then held in the sun with his eyelids sewn open; Tuditanus has him kept from sleep until he died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The same Tubero recorded that he had fought a serpent of extraordinary size at the Bagradas, beaten down with catapults, whose skin was 37 m (120 feet) long and was sent to Rome — a fair measure of how the stories grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","rome"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.29, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=29. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.30, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=30. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.31, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=31. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.34, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=34. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.35, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=35. [Open access]","Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 7.3–4, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/7*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/Rosa_-_The_Death_of_Regulus%2C_ca._1650-1652.jpg","title":"The Death of Regulus, painted about 1650","desc":"An oil painting by Salvator Rosa in the Virginia Museum of Fine Arts, showing workmen driving nails through a great cask under a stormy sky while a troop of horse and a mourning group look on. It illustrates a third version of the death, the spiked barrel, which is in neither of the accounts Aulus Gellius preserves and in no source of Regulus's own time. The picture is evidence of how the story was being told in the 17th century, and of nothing that happened in the 3rd century BCE.","credit":"Salvator Rosa, The Death of Regulus, ca. 1650-1652, Virginia Museum of Fine Arts, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_-_The_Death_of_Regulus,_ca._1650-1652.jpg","alt":"A dark Baroque oil painting of a crowd out of doors under heavy cloud, a dozen bare-armed men hammering nails into the side of a huge wooden barrel laid on the ground, with armoured horsemen at the right and cloaked figures raising their hands at the left."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Carthaginians refuse his terms?","a":"Because they concluded that capture could impose nothing worse, so there was no advantage in accepting them."},{"q":"Why does Polybius draw a lesson from his fall?","a":"Because a man who had lately refused all pity to the fallen was soon begging for his own life, which is why nobody should be confident of Fortune in success."},{"q":"Why should the story of his death be held at arm’s length?","a":"Because none of it is in Polybius, the later authorities disagree about how he died, and the same writer recorded a serpent 120 feet long killed at the Bagradas."}]},
{"id":"rm-197","num":197,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Alexon the Achaean uncovered a plot by mercenary officers to hand over the town during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the traitors were driven from the wall with stones and darts.","questions":["Hannibal son of Hamilcar relieved the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by running 50 ships into the harbour under full sail while the Romans stood out to sea rather than be carried in with him.","A storm wrecked the Roman pent-houses during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Greek mercenaries fired the siege-works at three points while the wind blew smoke into their rescuers’ faces."],"answer":"siege of Lilybaeum","answerText":"siege of Lilybaeum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">250 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 ships</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relief force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 ships, 10,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>siege of Lilybaeum</b> opened in 250 BCE, the 14th year of the war, when both consuls brought 200 ships against Carthage’s last foothold in Sicily but Drepana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The town stands on the island's western promontory, about 1,000 stades from the headlands covering Carthage, its harbour reached through lagoons hard to steer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A deep ditch ran round the walls, so the Romans camped on each side of the town, joined the camps with ditch, stockade and wall, and pushed their works against the seaward tower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They brought down that tower and the six next to it, battered the rest with rams, and drove their lines further in every day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officers of the mercenary garrison then slipped out at night to offer the place to the Romans, and Alexon the Achaean, who had caught a like plot at Agrigentum, told the Carthaginian general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He bought the remaining officers with promised bounties and sent them among the troops with Alexon, who was trusted, so that when the traitors came back to the wall they were driven off with stones and darts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carthage, reckoning what a besieged garrison must need, sent Hannibal son of Hamilcar with 50 ships and 10,000 men, who lay at the Aegusae until the wind served and ran into the harbour under full sail with his soldiers armed on deck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Romans, astonished and afraid of being carried in with him, stood out to sea and let him pass while the townspeople cheered him in from the walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A nobleman called Hannibal the Rhodian later ran the blockade in his own ship, sailing in at mid-morning in full view of the enemy and out next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What broke the assault was a storm that wrecked the pent-houses and carried off the towers: Greek mercenaries fired the works at three points, the wind blew smoke and sparks into the faces of the rescuers, and the Romans gave up storming the place for a blockade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","politics","sicily"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.41, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=41. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.42, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=42. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.43, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=43. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.44, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=44. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.46, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=46. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.48, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=48. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Siege_of_Lilybaeum_%28Histoire_de_Polybe%2C_Volume_1%29.jpg/1280px-Siege_of_Lilybaeum_%28Histoire_de_Polybe%2C_Volume_1%29.jpg","title":"The relief of Lilybaeum, engraved in 1727","desc":"A plate from Dom Vincent Thuillier's French Polybius, printed at Paris in 1727, headed Secours de Lylibée en présence de l'armée navale des Romains. Its key names four things the card describes: Hannibal's fleet entering the harbour on a breeze contrary to the Roman fleet, the harbour of Lilybaeum, the Roman line of circumvallation, and the Roman attack. Like the same volume's plate of Agrigentum it is an eighteenth-century reading of the text rather than a record of the ground.","credit":"Plate from Polybius, Histoire de Polybe, trans. Dom Vincent Thuillier, vol. 1 (Paris, 1727), author unknown, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_Lilybaeum_%28Histoire_de_Polybe,_Volume_1%29.jpg","alt":"A brown-ink engraving of a walled town on a low headland seen from above, a line of galleys running in from the open sea at the left, and lines of trenches, tents and small figures drawn up in blocks across the land side."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a plot to betray the town beaten without a fight?","a":"Because Alexon told the general, who bought the remaining officers with promised bounties and sent them among the troops, so the traitors were driven from the wall with stones and darts."},{"q":"Why did the Romans let a relief squadron sail past them?","a":"Because it came in under full sail with its soldiers armed on deck, and they were afraid of being carried into the harbour with it."},{"q":"Why did the assault turn into a blockade?","a":"Because a storm wrecked the pent-houses and carried off the towers, and Greek mercenaries fired the works at three points with the wind blowing smoke into the rescuers’ faces."}]},
{"id":"rm-198","num":198,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius likens <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his Roman opponents to boxers trading blows too fast to count, and to game-cocks that strike until one of them drops.","questions":["Having no friendly city to rely on, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> entrenched himself on the flat-topped mountain of Hercte in the middle of his enemies and raided the Italian coast from it.","Carthage sent <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Iberia with his nine-year-old son Hannibal, where he spent nine years reducing tribes before dying in battle against the most warlike of them."],"answer":"Hamilcar Barca","answerText":"Hamilcar Barca","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Took command</span><span class=\"dt-v\">247 BCE, in Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Eryx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">244 – 241 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Iberia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">237 – 228 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hamilcar Barca</b> took the Carthaginian command in Sicily in 247 BCE, the 18th year of the war, with the fleet put into his hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He raided the Italian coast about Locri and Bruttium, then seized Hercte, a flat-topped mountain between Eryx and Panormus that Polybius calls the best site in the district for a permanent camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its summit carried a hundred stades of pasture and arable, was healthy from the sea breeze, stood ringed by precipices and could be reached by only three difficult ways, with an eminence for an acropolis and a deep harbour below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had no friendly city and no other hope, so he entrenched himself in the middle of his enemies and kept the Romans fighting for years without a decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius compares the two sides to boxers trading blows too fast to count, and says no reader would endure a day-by-day account of the ambushes and stratagems that changed nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In 244 BCE he moved the contest to Eryx, taking the town between the Roman garrison on the summit and their guard at its foot, so each side was besieged by the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own supplies reached him by one road and one landing, and both armies held out through every privation, surprise and pitched battle for two years more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius insists they broke off level — not, as Fabius says, from utter weakness and misery, but like men still unbroken and unconquered — and likens them to game-cocks striking until one drops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What decided the war was the fleet rather than Sicily: with no supplies reaching him, Hamilcar judged nothing more could be saved and sent to treat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the Libyan war Carthage sent him to Iberia with his son Hannibal, then nine years old, and he spent nine years reducing tribes by war and diplomacy before dying in battle against the most warlike of them, with a conspicuous and even reckless gallantry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","africa"],"sources":["Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.56, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=56. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.57, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=57. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.58, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=58. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 1.62, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=1:chapter=62. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>Histories</i> 2.1, trans. Evelyn S. Shuckburgh (London: Macmillan, 1889), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0234:book=2:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Mount_Erice.jpg/1280px-Mount_Erice.jpg","title":"Monte Erice, the ancient Eryx, seen from the plain below","desc":"The mountain Hamilcar Barca seized in 244 BCE, moving down from Hercte and taking the town on its slopes while a Roman garrison held the summit above him and a Roman guard the ground at its foot, so that each side was besieging the other. The buildings along the ridge are those of the modern town.","credit":"Civa61, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Erice.jpg","alt":"A steep grey limestone mountain rising alone from a flat coastal plain under a clear blue sky, with the walls and buildings of a town strung along its long summit ridge."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Hercte the position he wanted?","a":"Because its summit carried pasture and arable, was healthy from the sea breeze, stood ringed by precipices with only three difficult ways up, and had a deep harbour below."},{"q":"Why does Polybius refuse to narrate those years in detail?","a":"Because the two sides traded blows too fast to count, and no reader would endure a day-by-day account of ambushes and stratagems that changed nothing."},{"q":"Why did Sicily not decide the war?","a":"Because it was settled by the fleet: with no supplies reaching him, Hamilcar judged nothing more could be saved and sent to treat while still unbeaten on land."}]},
{"id":"rm-199","num":199,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Rome's third fleet, paid for by leading citizens on a promise of repayment, caught Hanno's loaded relief squadron off the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 241 BCE and so ended the first war with Carthage.","questions":["Choosing to fight into a rough headwind rather than let Hamilcar Barca come aboard the enemy ships, Lutatius won the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and cut the supply line to Eryx.","Polybius counts 50 ships sunk and 70 captured at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Diodorus reports 117 Carthaginian ships lost and 80 Roman ones, a price Polybius passes over."],"answer":"Battle of the Aegates Islands","answerText":"Battle of the Aegates Islands","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">241 BCE, off western Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 quinqueremes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punic losses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 sunk, 70 taken</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","sicily","africa"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Aegates Islands</b>, fought off the western tip of Sicily in 241 BCE, cut the sea route to Hamilcar Barca's army at Eryx and ended the first war between Rome and Carthage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rome had kept off the sea for nearly five years after its disasters there, expecting to settle the war on land. When the Carthaginian general's boldness in Sicily showed that this was not working, the Romans resolved for a third time to try their fortune at sea, believing that only a decisive blow there could finish it. There was nothing in the public treasury for it, so leading citizens undertook singly or in twos and threes to equip a quinquereme each, on the understanding that they would be repaid if all went well. Two hundred were built to one model, that of the ship known as the Rhodian's, and Gaius Lutatius sailed at the beginning of summer to seize Drepana's harbour and the roadsteads by Lilybaeum while the enemy fleet was at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He began siege works against Drepana, but spent every day drilling his crews and feeding them well, expecting the relief fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carthage loaded its ships with corn and provisions and sent Hanno to the Holy Isle, meaning to cross unseen to Eryx, unload, take on the best mercenaries and Hamilcar himself as marines, and only then give battle. At dawn off Aegusa the breeze favoured the enemy and the sea ran rough, and Lutatius chose to fight loaded ships in bad weather rather than lightened ones carrying the man both sides most feared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything at Drepana was reversed, trained crews in stripped hulls against laden ships and newly levied marines, and 50 Carthaginian ships were sunk, 70 taken and nearly 10,000 men made prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus describes a larger and costlier engagement, 300 Roman warships against 250, with 117 Carthaginian ships lost and 80 Roman, 30 of them destroyed outright, a Roman loss Polybius does not mention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.59, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.60, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.61, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.62, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 24.11, trans. Francis R. Walton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1957), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/24*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Carthaginian_naval_ram_%282%29.jpg/1280px-Carthaginian_naval_ram_%282%29.jpg","title":"A bronze warship ram raised from the sea off the Egadi Islands","desc":"One of the rams recovered from the waters where the battle was fought, three fins tapering to a point, with the sockets that held the bow timbers behind them. A line of Punic letters runs along the casting, and the scarring across the fins is read as the mark of one warship striking another bow to bow. A second ram stands behind it in the case.","credit":"Sb2s3, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthaginian_naval_ram_%282%29.jpg","alt":"A large corroded bronze ship's ram displayed in a dark museum case, its three horizontal fins tapering forward to a blunt point, the metal mottled green and brown, with a second smaller ram standing behind it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome go back to sea after nearly five years?","a":"Because Hamilcar’s boldness in Sicily showed that settling the war on land was not working, and only a decisive blow at sea could finish it."},{"q":"Why did private citizens pay for the fleet?","a":"Because there was nothing in the public treasury, so leading men undertook singly or in twos and threes to equip a quinquereme each, to be repaid if all went well."},{"q":"Why did Lutatius fight in bad weather?","a":"Because he preferred loaded ships in a rough sea to lightened ones carrying the best mercenaries and Hamilcar himself as marines."}]},
{"id":"rm-200","num":200,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Rejected by the assembly and then stiffened by ten commissioners, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Carthage out of Sicily and set an indemnity of 3,200 talents payable within ten years.","questions":["Hamilcar Barca sent to treat after the defeat at sea, and the commander besieging Drepana settled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Roman people then refused until its terms were made harsher.","Naming only Sicily and the islands between Sicily and Italy, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Rome no ground whatever for seizing Sardinia at the end of the Libyan war a few years afterwards."],"answer":"Treaty of Lutatius","answerText":"Treaty of Lutatius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">241 BCE, in Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revised by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 commissioners at Rome</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3,200 talents over 10 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","warfare","sicily"],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Lutatius</b> ended 24 years of war between Rome and Carthage in 241 BCE, and it was made twice over: once by the commander in the field and once by the Roman people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the defeat at sea Carthage gave Hamilcar Barca full powers, and he, seeing no reasonable prospect left of saving his army, sent to treat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus records a round of talks in which Roman envoys read out terms requiring the surrender of arms and the handing over of deserters, and Hamilcar broke off rather than agree to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lutatius, knowing that the Romans were themselves worn out and enfeebled by the war, was ready to negotiate. Carthage was to leave the whole of Sicily, make no war on Hiero or the Syracusans or their allies, give up every prisoner without ransom, and pay 2,200 Euboean talents by instalments over twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The clause making all this conditional on the approval of the Roman people was no formality, for the assembly refused the treaty and sent ten commissioners to examine it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They altered nothing of substance but made three changes, each of them harder on Carthage: the term of payment was halved, a thousand talents were added, and every island lying between Sicily and Italy was to be evacuated as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The text given elsewhere in the same history adds that the allies of each side were to be secure from attack by the other, and that neither might levy contributions, enrol soldiers or make alliances in the other's dominions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sardinia is named nowhere in it, which is why the seizure of that island at the end of the Libyan war, with a further 1,200 talents exacted, is called a thing done contrary to all justice and without any reasonable pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war itself is judged the longest and greatest then known, with nearly 700 quinqueremes engaged on one occasion and losses of about 700 Roman ships and 500 Carthaginian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.62, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.63, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.28, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 24.13, trans. Francis R. Walton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1957), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/24*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the treaty made twice?","a":"Because the clause making it conditional on the approval of the Roman people was no formality: the assembly refused it and sent ten commissioners to examine it."},{"q":"Why was Lutatius willing to negotiate at all?","a":"Because he knew the Romans were themselves worn out and enfeebled by the war, so the terms in hand were worth more than another campaign."},{"q":"Why is the later seizure of Sardinia called unjustified?","a":"Because the island is named nowhere in the treaty, so taking it at the end of the Libyan war with a further 1,200 talents had no pretext in the agreement."}]},
{"id":"rm-201","num":201,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"By the peace of 241 BCE Carthage left the island, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Rome its first overseas territory, though Hiero's kingdom in the south-east stayed outside it.","questions":["Two federate cities, five free and immune ones and a tenth of everyone else's produce were the shape the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took, on rules the Sicilians had made themselves.","Rather than invent a tax system after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Rome kept the auctions, the times and the places that the law of Hiero had laid down, and the king's name with them."],"answer":"Roman annexation of Sicily","answerText":"Roman annexation of Sicily","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">241 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federate cities</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 — Messana, Tauromenium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Free and immune</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 more cities</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","sicily","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman annexation of Sicily</b> followed the peace of 241 BCE, by which Carthage undertook to leave the whole of the island and every island lying between it and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What passed to Rome was the western districts Carthage had governed, together with the cities that had come over during the long war. The island did not pass whole: the same treaty bound Carthage to make no war on Hiero or the Syracusans, and Hiero's kingdom in the south-east stayed outside the annexed territory as an allied monarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome had acquired ground overseas for the first time and had no standing machinery for governing it. What it did instead is described by Cicero two centuries afterwards, arguing a case about the island's misgovernment, and his account is the fullest that survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Very few Sicilian cities, he says, had been subdued in war by earlier Romans, and the land of those that were, though made public domain, was afterwards given back to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two cities held treaties, the Mamertines of Messana and Tauromenium, and five more were free and immune without any treaty at all: Centuripa, Halaesa, Segesta, Halicyae and Panormus. Every other territory paid a tenth of its produce, and Cicero insists that it had done so before Roman rule, by the will and the laws of the Sicilians themselves. Rome imposed no new tax and left even the time, the place and the manner of auctioning the tithe contracts as the law of Hiero had directed, being unwilling to disturb the island with so much as a new name for a law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The auctions went on being held under that law, so that when the supreme power changed the Sicilians kept not only the rules of a king who was dear to them but his name with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.62, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.3.13, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=3:section=13. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.3.14, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=3:section=14. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.3.15, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=3:section=15. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Tempio_di_Segesta_2026.jpg/1280px-Tempio_di_Segesta_2026.jpg","title":"The unfinished Doric temple outside Segesta","desc":"Segesta is one of the five Sicilian cities Cicero names as free and immune, holding that standing under Rome without any treaty at all, while every territory outside the seven privileged cities paid a tenth of its produce. The temple stands on a ridge above the countryside that tithe was drawn from.","credit":"Aritrek, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tempio_di_Segesta_2026.jpg","alt":"A Doric temple of pale limestone standing on open ground, its columns and entablature complete but unfluted and roofless, with bare Sicilian hills behind it under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the island not pass to Rome whole?","a":"Because the same treaty bound Carthage to make no war on Hiero or the Syracusans, so his kingdom in the south-east stayed outside the annexed territory as an allied monarchy."},{"q":"Why did Rome leave the tithe arrangements untouched?","a":"Because the tenth had been paid before Roman rule by the Sicilians’ own laws, so Rome kept even the time, place and manner of the auctions as the law of Hiero directed."},{"q":"Why should Cicero’s account be read as a case rather than a description?","a":"Because it was delivered two centuries later while prosecuting a governor for plundering the island, so its warmth about Sicilian loyalty is part of the argument."}]},
{"id":"rm-202","num":202,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Cicero says Sicily was the first of all foreign nations to be called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the first to teach Romans how glorious a thing it was to rule other peoples.","questions":["Some praetors were created for city business and some for the affairs of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the jurist Pomponius wrote, one for each territory that came under Rome's sway.","Corn, hides and tunics came out of Sicily at no expense to Rome, which is why a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could be described as one of the farms belonging to the Roman people."],"answer":"provincia","answerText":"provincia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First province</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sicily, from 241 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","politics","rome","sicily"],"abstract":"A <b>provincia</b> was a territory held outside Italy and governed by a magistrate sent from the city, and Cicero says Sicily was the first of all foreign nations to be called one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says too that it was the first to join the friendship and alliance of the Roman people, the first to teach Romans how glorious a thing it was to rule over foreign nations, and the one whose states never revolted once they had come into the alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that province, he argues, Rome made its first strides towards dominion over Africa, and Carthage would not have fallen so soon had Sicily not been open as a granary and a harbour for its fleets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cato had called the island the storehouse of the republic and the nurse of the Roman people, and in the long Italian war it clothed, armed and fed Rome's largest armies at no expense at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Provinces, Cicero adds, are as it were farms belonging to the Roman people, and this one had the merit of lying nearest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> All of it is a prosecutor's brief rather than a description, delivered against a governor charged with plundering the island, and the warmth about Sicilian loyalty is part of the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The institutional side is put briefly by the jurist Pomponius, writing three and a half centuries after the event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> As Sardinia was taken, and soon Sicily, then Spain, then Narbonese Gaul, he writes, as many praetors were created as there were provinces come under Rome's sway, some to preside over the city's business and some over the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His order is wrong, since Sardinia was demanded only at the end of the Libyan war, some years after Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Between them the two accounts give a province its two faces: corn and money on one side, and on the other a magistracy that had to be filled every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.2.2, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=2. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.2.3, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=3. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.2.5, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=5. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.2.7, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=7. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, Against Verres 2.3.6, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=3:section=6. [Open access]","Pomponius, <i>Digesta</i> 1.2.2.32, in <i>Corpus Iuris Civilis</i>, ed. Theodor Mommsen and Paul Krueger, Bibliotheca Iuris Antiqui, Universite Grenoble Alpes, https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Corpus/d-01.htm. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Modius_%28grain_measure%29_with_inscriptions_%2851357279746%29.jpg/1280px-Modius_%28grain_measure%29_with_inscriptions_%2851357279746%29.jpg","title":"A bronze modius, the Roman standard measure for grain","desc":"From Herculaneum, and two centuries later than the settlement of Sicily, so it shows the instrument rather than the moment. A province's tribute was reckoned in measures like this one: what Sicily owed was a tenth of the corn crop, and the punched inscription on the side records the standard the vessel was checked against. Museo Archeologico Nazionale di Napoli, MANN 6531.","credit":"Tyler Bell, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modius_%28grain_measure%29_with_inscriptions_%2851357279746%29.jpg","alt":"A squat cylindrical bronze vessel on small feet, open at the top with three internal bars meeting at the centre, its dark side carrying a punched Latin inscription."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Sicily worth so much more than its tribute?","a":"Because it was a granary and a harbour for Rome’s fleets, and in the long Italian war it clothed, armed and fed Rome’s largest armies at no expense at all."},{"q":"Why did each new province mean another magistracy?","a":"Because as many praetors were created as there were provinces come under Rome’s sway, some to preside over the city’s business and some over the provinces."},{"q":"Why is the jurist’s order of events wrong?","a":"Because he puts Sardinia before Sicily, where Sardinia was demanded only at the end of the Libyan war, some years after."}]},
{"id":"rm-203","num":203,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Unpaid after Sicily and moved to Sicca with a gold stater apiece, Carthage's hired soldiers began the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which nearly cost the city its liberty and its own soil.","questions":["Hiero supplied Carthage all through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on the reasoning that no state should be left so preponderant that nobody dares dispute even its acknowledged rights.","Three years and four months of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with the rebels penned behind a trench and palisade, eating their own prisoners, and Mathos led through Carthage under torture."],"answer":"Mercenary War","answerText":"Mercenary War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">241 – 238 BCE, in Libya</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 years, 4 months</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rebel army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 50,000 at the end</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","africa","libya"],"abstract":"The <b>Mercenary War</b>, which Polybius calls a truceless war, was the revolt of Carthage's own hired soldiers and of the Libyans who joined them, and it put the city in danger of losing not only its territory but its liberty and its native soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gesco, left to send the army home from Sicily, shipped it across in detachments so that each batch could be paid off and sent home before the next arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Carthage was short of money and expected the men to remit part of their arrears once they were all together, so it kept them in the city instead. When their offences by day and night became intolerable the government moved them to Sicca with a gold stater apiece and, afraid they would drift back to their families, forced them to take their baggage in a manner calculated to give offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Idle there, they reckoned up what was owed them, every man to his own advantage, and demanded the figure they arrived at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hanno told them it could not be met and asked them to give up part of their wage; being neither of one nation nor one language, the camp filled with confusion and sedition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mathos, Spendius and the Gaul Autaritus kept the revolt alive with forged letters and the murder of prisoners, precisely so that Hamilcar's leniency could tempt nobody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The garrison of Sardinia rose in imitation, crucified the general sent against it and killed every Carthaginian there, holding the island until its people drove them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hiero supplied Carthage throughout, judging that no state should hold a power so preponderant that none dare dispute even its rights, and Rome kept the treaty, licensing trade to Carthage but not to the rebels and refusing both Sardinia and Utica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hamilcar finally shut the rebels behind a trench and palisade until they ate their prisoners and their slaves, and the war closed after three years and four months with Mathos led through the city under torture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.65, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.66, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.67, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.79, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.83, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.84, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.88, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Carthage keep the returning army in the city?","a":"Because it was short of money and expected the men to remit part of their arrears once they were all together, so it did not pay them off in detachments as intended."},{"q":"Why did the rebel leaders murder their prisoners?","a":"Because they wanted the revolt kept alive, precisely so that Hamilcar’s leniency could tempt nobody back."},{"q":"Why did Hiero supply the enemy he had lately fought?","a":"Because he judged that no state should hold a power so preponderant that none dare dispute even its rights."}]},
{"id":"rm-204","num":204,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Carthage was still exhausted from the Libyan war when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost it two islands and another 1,200 talents, which Polybius counts the principal cause of the next war.","questions":["Mutineers who had been driven off the island invited Rome in, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed, with a war declared over Carthage's preparations to punish them.","Rome managed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without a battle at all, on a charge about shipping, because Carthage had not recovered its strength and feared the threats."],"answer":"seizure of Sardinia and Corsica","answerText":"seizure of Sardinia and Corsica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sardinia ceded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">238 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Extra indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,200 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","warfare","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>seizure of Sardinia and Corsica</b> took both islands from Carthage after the Mercenary War, when the city was too exhausted to refuse anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mercenaries who had held Sardinia, and had been driven off it by the islanders, invited Rome to occupy it, and Rome went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carthage objected that the island's sovereignty was rather its own and prepared to punish those who had caused the revolt; Rome made that the pretext for declaring war, alleging the armament was aimed at itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carthage, barely escaped destruction in the war just ended, gave up the island and paid 1,200 talents beyond the sum already exacted rather than fight again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dio puts it more bluntly: Rome secured Sardinia without a battle, and money with it, on a charge of injuring Roman shipping, because Carthage had not recovered its strength and feared the threats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Corsica was taken by fighting rather than threat, and untidily: Claudius made peace with the Corsicans as though he had authority for it, Varus ignored the agreement and subdued them, and Rome, to shift the blame for breaking the compact, offered Claudius up and exiled him when they would not have him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither island then stayed quiet, and in one year Rome divided its forces three ways, sending one commander into Liguria, one against the Corsicans and the urban praetor to Sardinia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pomponius, unable to find Sardinians who had slipped into caves in the forest, sent to Italy for keen-scented dogs and hunted them out with those.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome later ordered Carthage to take its ships out of all these islands, the ports being Roman now, and sent a spear and a herald's staff with the demand, bidding them choose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius makes the seizure the second and principal cause of the next war, done contrary to all justice and merely because the occasion allowed it; a Carthaginian envoy later told the senate that with Sardinia and Sicily they had bought not a truce but eternal friendship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.88, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 12, Zonaras 8.18, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/12*.html. [Open access]","Cassius Dio, <i>Roman History</i> 12, fragment 46, trans. Earnest Cary, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cassius_Dio/12*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.10, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.28, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.79, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Area_archeologica_tarros.JPG/1280px-Area_archeologica_tarros.JPG","title":"Tharros on the west coast of Sardinia","desc":"A Phoenician and Punic port that stood on this headland for centuries before the island changed hands, and went on standing under Rome. What Carthage gave up in Sardinia was a string of coastal towns like this one, together with the sea lanes they served.","credit":"Vittoria serra, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Area_archeologica_tarros.JPG","alt":"Low stone foundations and two standing columns on a bare headland above a deep blue sea, with yachts at anchor in the bay beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why could Carthage not refuse?","a":"Because it had barely escaped destruction in the war just ended, so it gave up the island and paid 1,200 talents beyond the sum already exacted rather than fight again."},{"q":"Why was Rome’s pretext the opposite of the facts?","a":"Because Carthage was preparing to punish the men who had caused the revolt, and Rome alleged that the armament was aimed at itself."},{"q":"Why did Rome offer up its own commander over Corsica?","a":"Because Claudius had made peace as though he had authority for it and Varus then subdued the island anyway, so Rome shifted the blame for breaking the compact onto him."}]},
{"id":"rm-205","num":205,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Hamilcar crossed the strait with his army and his nine-year-old son, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed gave Carthage a dominion larger than the one it had lost.","questions":["A son-in-law who married an Iberian prince's daughter and was proclaimed general by the Iberians themselves carried on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for nine years before a household slave killed him.","Rome had been asleep while the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went on, and with a Celtic invasion expected daily it sent envoys rather than an army, asking only that the Ebro not be crossed in arms."],"answer":"Barcid conquest of Spain","answerText":"Barcid conquest of Spain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hamilcar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 237 BCE, about 9 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hasdrubal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 years in command</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","warfare","spain","africa"],"abstract":"The <b>Barcid conquest of Spain</b> was the subjugation of much of the Iberian peninsula by Hamilcar Barca and his successors between the loss of Sicily and the second war with Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carthage sent Hamilcar out as soon as Libya was in order, and he crossed the strait with his army and his son Hannibal, then nine years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He spent about nine years there, bringing tribe after tribe under Carthage by arms or by diplomacy, and died in battle after freely exposing himself on the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus tells the end differently: routed by a feigned offer of friendship, Hamilcar turned aside to save his sons and drowned under his horse in a river, having earlier crucified a defeated chief, released 10,000 prisoners and founded Acra Leuce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Command passed to his son-in-law Hasdrubal, who in that account had learned that fair dealing is more effective than force and preferred peace to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Hasdrubal destroyed those responsible for Hamilcar's death and took their twelve cities, married an Iberian prince's daughter, and was proclaimed general with unlimited power by the Iberians themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He founded New Carthage on the coast and by prudent administration made the country a material addition to Carthage's resources, the site chosen for action in Spain or Libya alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rome, asleep while all this was built, saw a dominion forming larger and more formidable than the one Carthage had lost, and set about making up the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With a Celtic invasion expected daily it dared not give orders or go to war, so it sent envoys and got an engagement not to cross the Ebro in arms, with nothing said about the rest of Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hasdrubal was killed by a household slave after nine years, the army chose Hannibal and the assembly ratified it, and Hannibal would later say his father had made him swear at the altar never to be a friend of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 25.10–12, trans. Francis R. Walton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1957), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/25*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Monedas_hispano-cartaginesas_pertenecientes_al_Tesoro_de_La_Puebla_de_los_Infantes_-_M.A.N.jpg/1280px-Monedas_hispano-cartaginesas_pertenecientes_al_Tesoro_de_La_Puebla_de_los_Infantes_-_M.A.N.jpg","title":"Two Carthaginian silver coins struck in Spain, from the La Puebla de los Infantes hoard","desc":"The quarter-shekel on the left, dated 237 to 227 BCE, carries an elephant; the shekel on the right, dated 237 to 235 BCE, carries a head the museum identifies as Melqart-Heracles. Both were struck in the peninsula in the first years of the conquest, which is what Carthaginian silver coinage in Spain records. Museo Arqueologico Nacional de Espana, Madrid.","credit":"Dorieo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monedas_hispano-cartaginesas_pertenecientes_al_Tesoro_de_La_Puebla_de_los_Infantes_-_M.A.N.jpg","alt":"Two small worn silver coins mounted on a pale museum wall, the smaller showing an elephant in relief, the larger a diademed head in profile."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome not stop the conquest while it was happening?","a":"Because with a Celtic invasion expected daily it dared not give orders or go to war, so it sent envoys and got only the promise about the Ebro."},{"q":"Why did Hasdrubal prefer diplomacy to force?","a":"Because he had learned that fair dealing is more effective, and was proclaimed general with unlimited power by the Iberians themselves after marrying an Iberian prince’s daughter."},{"q":"Why did Spain matter so much to Carthage after Sicily?","a":"Because by prudent administration it became a material addition to Carthaginian resources, a dominion larger and more formidable than the one that had been lost."}]},
{"id":"rm-206","num":206,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Hasdrubal founded <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a peninsula in a land-locked gulf, and Carthage kept its money, its war material and its Spanish hostages inside the walls.","questions":["Silver mines four hundred stades round, worked by 40,000 men, lay twenty stades from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose harbours were almost the only ones in Spain fit for a fleet.","A thousand trained soldiers held the citadel of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since nobody imagined a siege while Carthage was master of nearly the whole peninsula."],"answer":"New Carthage","answerText":"New Carthage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Hasdrubal, c. 228 – 221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The gulf</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 stades long, 10 broad</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Silver mines</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 stades off, 40,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["city","place","history","spain","africa"],"abstract":"<b>New Carthage</b> was the city Hasdrubal founded on the Spanish coast as the seat of Barcid power, and Polybius says it added materially to Carthage's resources, its site chosen for action in Spain or Libya alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It lies halfway down the coast in a gulf facing south-west, twenty stades long and ten broad at the entrance, with an island at the mouth that breaks the waves and leaves the whole of it calm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The town stands on a hill jutting out as a peninsula in the innermost nook, sea on the east and south and a lagoon on the west, with two stades of ground joining it to the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Five hills ring it: a temple of Aesculapius on the tallest, to the east, and opposite it a magnificent palace said to have been built by Hasdrubal when he aspired to royal power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The three lower hills to the north are named for Vulcan, for Saturn, and for Aletes, who was said to have received divine honours for discovering the silver mines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Those mines lay twenty stades from the city and ran four hundred stades round, and Polybius reports 40,000 workmen at them bringing in 25,000 drachmae a day to the treasury of his own Roman time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A channel had been cut between lagoon and sea for shipping, with a bridge over it for the carts bringing supplies in from the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It stood almost alone among Spanish cities in having harbours fit for a fleet, and lay conveniently for the direct crossing from Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Carthage kept the bulk of its money and its war material there, together with the hostages taken from the whole of Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The garrison was about a thousand trained soldiers, because nobody imagined a siege while Carthage held nearly the whole peninsula, and the rest of a very large population were artisans, tradesmen and sailors with no experience of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 25.12, trans. Francis R. Walton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1957), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/25*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.10, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 3.2.10, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/3B*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.8, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Muralla_P%C3%BAnica_de_Cartagena.jpg/1280px-Muralla_P%C3%BAnica_de_Cartagena.jpg","title":"The excavated Punic wall at Cartagena","desc":"Two parallel faces of large sandstone blocks, built on a Hellenistic pattern, now roofed and glazed as an interpretation centre in the modern city. It is what survives of the defences of the town Hasdrubal founded, on the peninsula Polybius describes.","credit":"Maria Rodriguez, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muralla_P%C3%BAnica_de_Cartagena.jpg","alt":"Excavated stone footings of a thick double wall in a pit below floor level, lit through a glazed modern wall, with the street outside visible beyond the glass."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the site chosen?","a":"Because it suited action in Spain or Libya alike, standing on a peninsula in a sheltered gulf and lying conveniently for the direct crossing from Africa."},{"q":"Why was it the one Spanish city that could hold a fleet?","a":"Because an island at the gulf’s mouth breaks the waves and leaves the whole of it calm, so it stood almost alone in having harbours fit for warships."},{"q":"Why was so rich a place held by a thousand men?","a":"Because nobody imagined a siege while Carthage held nearly the whole peninsula, and the rest of the population were artisans, tradesmen and sailors with no experience of war."}]},
{"id":"rm-207","num":207,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"With a Celtic invasion expected daily, Rome sent envoys rather than orders and obtained the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, by which Carthage undertook not to cross that river in arms.","questions":["Carthage argued that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not bind it at all, since a commander had made it without the people's approval, exactly as Rome had once repudiated Lutatius' terms.","The Saguntines had put themselves under Roman protection years earlier, which on one reading puts Carthage in the wrong under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and under the peace of 241 alike."],"answer":"Ebro treaty","answerText":"Ebro treaty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">with Hasdrubal, c. 226 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Disputed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 219 BCE, over Saguntum</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","spain","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Ebro treaty</b> was the agreement Rome made with Hasdrubal in Spain, by which the Carthaginians undertook not to cross the Ebro in arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome had watched a dominion form there larger and more formidable than the one Carthage had lost and wanted to act, but a Celtic invasion was expected from day to day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So it sent envoys to conciliate Hasdrubal rather than impose orders, and came away with an engagement about the river and nothing said about the rest of Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Polybius's list of the agreements between the two powers the whole of it is a single line, that the Carthaginians are not to cross the Ebro in arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it left unsaid became the entire argument years later, when Hannibal attacked Saguntum, a city on the Roman side of the question but south of the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Carthaginian envoys did not mention the agreement at all, holding it either not to exist or not to bind them, since it had been made without their approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They cited Rome's own precedent for that, the terms Lutatius had settled in Sicily and the Roman people had then refused as made without their consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their case rested instead on the peace of 241, which said nothing of Iberia and provided that the allies of each power should be secure from attack, the Saguntines having been no allies of Rome at that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Roman answer, made later rather than at the time, was that Hasdrubal's agreement carried no ratifying clause where the Lutatius terms had carried one, and that a promise about allies covers allies acquired afterwards unless it says otherwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saguntum had in fact placed itself under Roman protection years before, so that if its destruction is the cause of the war then Carthage was in the wrong on both agreements at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.21, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.29, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.30, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Cuenca_del_r%C3%ADo_Ebro%2C_proximidades_de_Mora_d%27Ebre_%28Tarragona%29.jpg/1280px-Cuenca_del_r%C3%ADo_Ebro%2C_proximidades_de_Mora_d%27Ebre_%28Tarragona%29.jpg","title":"The Ebro in its lower valley, near Mora d'Ebre in Tarragona","desc":"The whole of the agreement was one line about this river. It is the modern landscape, with its fields and its towns, but the shape of the thing is the point: a wide river crossing a plain, easy to name in a sentence and impossible to argue about once a city on the wrong side of it is under attack.","credit":"Juan Emilio Prades Bel, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuenca_del_r%C3%ADo_Ebro%2C_proximidades_de_Mora_d%27Ebre_%28Tarragona%29.jpg","alt":"A broad green-brown river winding across a cultivated plain, seen from a pine-covered height, with low mountains along the horizon under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did what the treaty left out matter more than what it said?","a":"Because it named only the river, so when Hannibal attacked a city south of the Ebro the agreement had nothing to say about it."},{"q":"Why did the Carthaginians cite Rome’s own precedent?","a":"Because the terms Lutatius settled in Sicily had been refused by the Roman people as made without their consent, which is the argument they used against Hasdrubal’s promise."},{"q":"Why does the Roman reply turn on a missing clause?","a":"Because Hasdrubal’s agreement carried no ratifying clause where the Lutatius terms had carried one, so on Rome’s reading it bound Carthage as it stood."}]},
{"id":"rm-208","num":208,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Eight months of siege ended with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stormed, its money kept by Hannibal, its people shared out among the troops by rank, and the rest shipped to Carthage.","questions":["Roman legates found Hannibal wintering at New Carthage and protested against any attack on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which they said was under their protection.","The gold and silver of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was melted with copper and lead to make it worthless before the last sally, and the women killed their children and hanged themselves."],"answer":"Saguntum","answerText":"Saguntum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 months, 219 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">From the sea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 7 stades</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["city","place","history","warfare","spain"],"abstract":"<b>Saguntum</b> was the Iberian city whose fall opened the second war between Rome and Carthage, standing on the seaward foot of the hills seven stades from the sea, in what Polybius calls the most fertile territory in Iberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It had placed itself under Roman protection years before Hannibal, and when a civil quarrel broke out there it called in Rome rather than Carthage, though Carthage was close at hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Saguntines sent message after message as they saw what was coming, and Rome, having often paid them little attention, at last sent legates to look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They found Hannibal wintering at New Carthage and protested against an attack on the city or a crossing of the Ebro; he answered that he was protecting the Saguntines, and accused Rome of unjustly putting their leading men to death when called in to arbitrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius calls that a groundless pretext, and says the real grievance was Sardinia and the tribute exacted with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He besieged it for the advantages it promised: no Roman base in Iberia, terror among the tribes, no enemy behind him, money and supplies for the march, booty for the troops and spoils to send home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He shared the labour of the battering himself and exposed himself recklessly, and after eight months of hardship the place was taken by storm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus says the Saguntines melted the gold and silver of their houses and their women's ornaments with copper and lead to make them worthless, then sallied out and were cut down, while the women killed their children and hanged themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The money he set aside for his own purposes, the slaves he distributed among his men by rank, and the rest of the property he sent off at once to Carthage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome held no debate about whether to fight, Polybius insists against the historians who staged one, but sent ambassadors to Carthage with two alternatives: give up Hannibal and his council, or have war declared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.15, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 25.15, trans. Francis R. Walton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1957), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Diodorus_Siculus/25*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.20, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.30, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Castillo_de_Sagunto%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-01-03%2C_DD_09-11_HDR.JPG/1280px-Castillo_de_Sagunto%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-01-03%2C_DD_09-11_HDR.JPG","title":"The hilltop of Sagunto, above the coastal plain","desc":"The ridge the ancient city held, with excavated foundations in the foreground and the plain running to the sea beyond. The long circuit of walls along the crest is later work, medieval and after, built on the same ground; what is worth seeing here is the position, which is why the siege took eight months.","credit":"Diego Delso, delso.photo, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_de_Sagunto%2C_Valencia%2C_Espa%C3%B1a%2C_2015-01-03%2C_DD_09-11_HDR.JPG","alt":"A long rocky ridge at sunset carrying fortification walls and a small colonnaded building, with low excavated foundations in the foreground and a flat coastal plain stretching away below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Hannibal want this city in particular?","a":"Because taking it left no Roman base in Iberia and no enemy behind him, terrified the tribes, and gave money, supplies, booty for the troops and spoils to send home."},{"q":"Why does Polybius reject Hannibal’s stated grievance?","a":"Because he calls the charge about Saguntine leading men a groundless pretext, and says the real grievance was Sardinia and the tribute exacted with it."},{"q":"Why did Rome send an ultimatum rather than debate?","a":"Because Polybius insists against the historians who staged a debate that it simply offered two alternatives: give up Hannibal and his council, or have war declared."}]},
{"id":"rm-209","num":209,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Hamilcar's anger, the seizure of Sardinia and the wealth of Spain are the three causes Polybius gives for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose occasion was the storming of Saguntum.","questions":["Carthage came out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeping its African cities and its own laws, and losing all its warships but ten, all its elephants and the right to fight outside Africa.","Tarentum surrendered and Campanian towns sent invitations during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Rome gave up hope of holding Italy and expected Hannibal to appear at any moment."],"answer":"Second Punic War","answerText":"Second Punic War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 – 201 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 talents over 50 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100, aged 14 to 30</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","warfare","rome","africa"],"abstract":"The <b>Second Punic War</b>, which Polybius calls the Hannibalic war, is one of the three conflicts he takes as the starting point of his account of Rome's rise to world dominion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gives it three causes: Hamilcar Barca's unbroken anger after Sicily; the seizure of Sardinia with the tribute exacted for it, which he calls the second and principal cause; and the Carthaginian success in Spain, which supplied the means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The immediate occasion was Hannibal's storming of Saguntum, after which Rome demanded that Carthage give him up or accept war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius sums up what followed in a sentence of his own plan: the Carthaginians invaded Italy, broke up the dominion of Rome, and cast the Romans into fear for their native soil, while entertaining hopes they had never dared before of taking Rome itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Cannae the coast was almost all theirs, Tarentum surrendered at once, Campanian towns invited Hannibal in, and every eye turned to Carthage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Rome abandoned all hope of holding Italy and expected Hannibal at any moment; a few days later, the city still panic-stricken, the army sent to Cisalpine Gaul was ambushed by the Celts and destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He credits the recovery to the constitution and to cool counsel: the senate strengthened the defences and deliberated calmly, and Rome went on to regain Italy, beat Carthage and within a few years master the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fighting was never confined to Italy: Spain, Africa and Sicily each had campaigns of their own, and Philip of Macedon opened another war by seeking a Carthaginian alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The terms that ended it left Carthage its former African cities and territory, its property, its own laws and no garrison, and took the rest: all its warships but ten, all its elephants, and any war outside Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was to restore to Massinissa what had belonged to his ancestors, pay 10,000 talents over 50 years, and hand over 100 hostages chosen from its young men between 14 and 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.1–2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.9–10, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.20, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.118, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.18, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Carthage_Tetradrachm_Tanit_%26_Horse%2C_with_a_Serrated_Edge._About_200_BC.jpg/1280px-Carthage_Tetradrachm_Tanit_%26_Horse%2C_with_a_Serrated_Edge._About_200_BC.jpg","title":"A Carthaginian silver tetradrachm of about 200 BCE, both faces","desc":"Tanit on one side, wreathed with corn and wearing a necklace and a single drop earring; a horse with its left foreleg raised on the other, a Punic letter beneath it. The edge is serrated. These are the city's standard types rather than anything struck for the war, and the coin is money of the years in which it ended. 25 mm, 12.68 g.","credit":"Berlin-George, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthage_Tetradrachm_Tanit_%26_Horse%2C_with_a_Serrated_Edge._About_200_BC.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin with a notched edge, side by side against blue cloth: a woman's head in profile with curled hair on the left, a standing horse on the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why is Sardinia called the principal cause rather than Saguntum?","a":"Because Saguntum was the occasion, where the seizure of Sardinia with the tribute exacted for it is what made the anger after Sicily into a grievance Carthage would act on."},{"q":"Why did Rome recover after Cannae?","a":"Because the senate strengthened the defences and deliberated calmly, which Polybius credits to the constitution rather than to any turn of fortune."},{"q":"Why do the peace terms leave Carthage its cities and laws?","a":"Because what was taken was the power to fight — all but ten warships, all the elephants and any war outside Africa — rather than the country itself."}]},
{"id":"rm-210","num":210,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Of all that befell both nations the cause was one man and one mind, Polybius writes of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who ran Italy himself and Spain, Sicily and Greece through others.","questions":["Sixteen years of fighting without once breaking up an army of Africans, Spaniards, Celts and Greeks is what Polybius most admires in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose troops shared neither laws nor language.","Massinissa told Polybius that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his friend Mago never took a city together, so carefully did each manoeuvre to keep the other from sharing the profits."],"answer":"Hannibal","answerText":"Hannibal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Left Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at 9, c. 237 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 years in Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Returned home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">past 45, c. 202 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","history","warfare","africa","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Hannibal</b> commanded against Rome in the second war between the two powers, and Polybius writes that of all that befell both nations the cause was one man and one mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He managed Italy himself, Spain through his brother Hasdrubal and then Mago, Sicily through Hippocrates and Myttonus the African, and kept Rome distracted in Greece and Illyria through his understanding with Philip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had gone to Spain at nine with his father, who made him lay his hand on the sacrifice and swear never to be a friend of Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Returning to Carthage at the end of the war he pulled a speaker down from the tribune, then apologised by saying he had left the city at nine and come back past 45.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Polybius treats the two charges against him, excessive cruelty and avarice, as questions his actions cannot settle, since circumstance and the advice of friends make men act against their natures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One piece of that advice survives: when the march to Italy was debated, a friend called Hannibal Monomachus said the only way through was to teach the troops to eat human flesh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He could persuade neither himself nor his friends to entertain it, and the cruelties later charged against him in Italy were said to be that man's work, though Polybius adds that circumstances did as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the avarice he is less doubtful, citing Massinissa, who told him that Hannibal and Mago never took a city together, each manoeuvring more carefully against the other than against the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He fought the Romans for sixteen years without once breaking up his army, holding together Africans, Spaniards, Ligurians, Celts, Phoenicians, Italians and Greeks with no laws, customs or language in common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The commander's ability forced men so different to obey a single word of command, and Polybius judges that had he begun with the other parts of the world and left the Romans to the last, none of his plans would have failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 9.22, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/9*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 9.24, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/9*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 9.25, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/9*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg/1280px-Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg","title":"The marble bust from Capua traditionally called Hannibal","desc":"Found at Capua in 1667 and now in Naples, photographed by the Alinari brothers about 1900. The name is traditional rather than secure: the museum dates the carving anywhere between the 1st century BCE and the 4th century CE, which puts it at least a century and a half after the man it is said to show. No likeness taken from life survives.","credit":"Fratelli Alinari, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg","alt":"A monochrome photograph of a marble bust of a bearded man in a patterned close-fitting cap and a draped cloak, his head turned to one side, against a dark background."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Polybius call one mind the cause of the whole war?","a":"Because he managed Italy himself, Spain through his brothers, Sicily through others, and kept Rome distracted in Greece and Illyria through his understanding with Philip."},{"q":"Why can the charge of cruelty not be settled from his actions?","a":"Because circumstance and the advice of friends make men act against their natures, and the cruelties charged in Italy were said to be another man’s work."},{"q":"Why is holding his army together the greater feat?","a":"Because for sixteen years he never broke it up, keeping Africans, Spaniards, Ligurians, Celts, Phoenicians, Italians and Greeks obedient with no laws, customs or language in common."}]},
{"id":"rm-211","num":211,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Fifteen days in the mountains ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and brought 20,000 foot and 6,000 horse down into the plain of the Po, five months out from New Carthage.","questions":["Rocks rolled from the slopes above and an army sliding on frozen snow are Polybius' picture of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, along with elephants that took three days to pass one landslip.","Writers who put a god on the road to guide the army made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a miracle, which Polybius says would make its commander the most imprudent general alive."],"answer":"Hannibal's crossing of the Alps","answerText":"Hannibal's crossing of the Alps","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE, in 15 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 days, 2 on the summit</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Came down with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000 foot, 6,000 horse</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","warfare","africa","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Hannibal's crossing of the Alps</b> brought a Carthaginian army from Spain into the plain of the Po, five months out from New Carthage and fifteen days in the mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ascent took nine days under attack from tribes on the higher ground, who skirted the slopes rolling rocks down or throwing stones, and cost many men, horses and pack-animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hannibal had put the pack-train and cavalry at the head of the column and the heavy infantry behind, which is what kept the army from destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The elephants were of the greatest service, since the tribesmen never dared approach the part of the column they were in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He camped two days on the summit to rest the survivors and wait for stragglers, and horses that had bolted came back into camp, having followed the track of the march.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Snow had gathered by then, and to raise the men's spirits he showed them the plain of the Po and pointed out where Rome lay, the Alps standing to Italy as a citadel to a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The descent cost nearly as much as the climb: the path was narrow and steep, and under snow neither men nor animals could tell what they trod on, so that whoever stumbled went over the edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where a landslip had carried away about a stade and a half of the mountain face, new snow lay on old: men who fell slid faster on hands and knees, and the animals broke through and stuck as though frozen in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hannibal swept the ridge clear, camped, and set the army to building a path along the cliff: in one day it was wide enough for the horses and pack-train, and after three more the elephants were across, starving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius attacks the writers who made this a marvel: a god who shows Hannibal the road makes him the most imprudent general alive, and gods enter sober history when a series of falsehoods cannot otherwise be closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.53, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.54, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.55, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.56, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.47–48, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Joseph_Mallord_William_Turner_-_Snow_Storm%2C_Hannibal_and_his_Army_Crossing_the_Alps_-_WGA23167.jpg/1280px-Joseph_Mallord_William_Turner_-_Snow_Storm%2C_Hannibal_and_his_Army_Crossing_the_Alps_-_WGA23167.jpg","title":"Turner, Snow Storm: Hannibal and his Army Crossing the Alps","desc":"A painting of 1812 and not a record of anything. The army is a scatter of small dark figures along the bottom edge, the mountains are barely there, and the subject of the picture is the black vortex of the storm with a pale sun caught inside it. It is worth looking at for what the crossing came to mean rather than for what happened.","credit":"Joseph Mallord William Turner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Mallord_William_Turner_-_Snow_Storm%2C_Hannibal_and_his_Army_Crossing_the_Alps_-_WGA23167.jpg","alt":"A dark oil painting in which a black curving storm fills the sky over a pale sun, with faint mountains behind and small struggling figures scattered along the foreground rocks."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the order of march save the army?","a":"Because the pack-train and cavalry went at the head and the heavy infantry behind, so the tribesmen rolling rocks from the higher ground could not cut the column apart."},{"q":"Why was the descent worse than the climb?","a":"Because under snow neither men nor animals could tell what they trod on, so whoever stumbled went over the edge, and where new snow lay on old they broke through and stuck."},{"q":"Why does Polybius attack the writers who made it a marvel?","a":"Because a god who shows Hannibal the road makes him the most imprudent general alive, and gods enter sober history when a series of falsehoods cannot otherwise be closed."}]},
{"id":"rm-212","num":212,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Unable to bear the smell and trumpeting of the Indian kind, an African <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would turn tail before closing, which made the line as dangerous behind as in front.","questions":["A pier of rafts piled with earth and led by two females carried each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over the Rhone, until the end sections were cut loose and towed across.","Trumpets and open lanes in the Roman line blunted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Zama, where the same animals that had trampled a consular army at Tunis did no damage."],"answer":"war elephant","answerText":"war elephant","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Tunis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">255 BCE, broke a consular army</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On the Rhone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE, ferried on rafts</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Zama</span><span class=\"dt-v\">202 BCE, let through lanes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["animal","warfare","history","technology","africa"],"abstract":"A <b>war elephant</b> was a trained animal used as a shock weapon, carried by Carthage into its wars with Rome and by the Hellenistic kingdoms into theirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius describes how they fought each other: tusks interlocked, each shoving to force the other back, and when one had turned the other's trunk aside it gored him in the flank as a bull does with its horns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men in towers on their backs struck at each other with pikes while the animals pushed forehead to forehead below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> African elephants, he says, mostly declined the combat, unable to bear the smell and trumpeting of Indian ones and turning tail before they came near.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why such a line was as dangerous behind as in front: at Raphia the animals driven back broke Ptolemy's own guard and threw his light troops into confusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Moving them was itself an operation: to cross the Rhone the Carthaginians built a pier of rafts about 60 metres (200 feet) long, piled it with earth until it looked like the path ashore, and drove the animals out along it behind two females.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The end rafts were then cut loose and towed over, and the animals ran about in alarm until, shut in by the stream, they grew afraid and stood still; those that threw themselves in half way across came out alive with their trunks above the surface, though their mahouts drowned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the Alps they were of the greatest service, since the tribes attacking the column never dared to come near the part of it they were in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against infantry they could still decide a battle: at Tunis the Roman front ranks were trodden under foot, and the men who forced a passage through met a fresh phalanx behind the beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Zama the trumpets frightened some of them back onto Carthage's own Numidians, and the rest were let through lanes the Roman commander had left open, so that his troops took no harm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.84, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.46, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.53, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.34, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Siamese_War_Elephants_%28Thai%29_Photo_c1870.jpg/1280px-Siamese_War_Elephants_%28Thai%29_Photo_c1870.jpg","title":"Siamese war elephants photographed about 1870","desc":"Not Carthaginian and two thousand years too late, but a photograph rather than an imagining, and it shows the arrangement the ancient accounts describe: a driver sitting forward on the neck, a fighting platform strapped over the back, and men in it with long spears and standards. No picture survives of a Carthaginian elephant in the field.","credit":"Unknown author, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siamese_War_Elephants_%28Thai%29_Photo_c1870.jpg","alt":"A sepia photograph of two elephants on open ground, the nearer one carrying a driver on its neck and a wooden platform on its back holding men with long spears and flags."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a line of elephants dangerous to its own side?","a":"Because driven back they broke the army behind them, as at Raphia where they threw Ptolemy’s own guard and light troops into confusion."},{"q":"Why did the Rhone crossing need a pier of rafts?","a":"Because the animals had to be walked onto ground that looked like the bank, so earth was piled on the rafts and two females led them out before the end was cut loose and towed."},{"q":"Why did they do the Romans no harm at Zama?","a":"Because the trumpets frightened some back onto Carthage’s own Numidians and the rest were let through lanes the Roman commander had left open."}]},
{"id":"rm-213","num":213,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Both commanders rode out to reconnoitre and formed up on seeing each other's dust, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as a cavalry action rather than a fight between legions.","questions":["A consul's seventeen-year-old son charged alone into the ring around his wounded father at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and was saluted afterwards as his preserver.","Numidians working round into the rear decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which about 2,000 Celts in Roman service killed their neighbours in the night and went over."],"answer":"Battle of the Ticinus","answerText":"Battle of the Ticinus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE, near the Po</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prisoners</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 600 at the bridge</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Celts defected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2,000 foot, 200 horse</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","rome","africa"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Ticinus</b> was the first meeting of Hannibal and a Roman army in Italy, fought near the Po in 218 BCE after Publius Scipio had come round by sea and taken over the frontier legions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both commanders had ridden out with their whole cavalry to reconnoitre, Scipio with his javelineers too, and each formed for action on seeing the other's dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scipio put the javelineers and the Gallic horse in front and the rest behind; Hannibal led with his bridled and heavier cavalry, keeping the Numidians on the wings to work round the flanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both sides were so eager to close that the javelineers never got their first volley away, retiring through the gaps between their own squadrons for fear of being trodden underfoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cavalry met front to front and held even for a time, the fight becoming partly an infantry one as men dismounted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Then the Numidians came round into the Roman rear, rode down the javelineers who had escaped the first charge, and the Roman horse broke, most scattering while a few gathered closely round the wounded consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius reports on Laelius' authority that the consul's son, seventeen and on his first campaign, saw his father ringed by the enemy with two or three horsemen left, and charged the ring alone when his own men hung back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest were forced to follow, the enemy broke up, and the father saluted his son as his preserver in the hearing of all; afterwards, Polybius adds, he never exposed himself without sufficient reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scipio withdrew over the Po; Hannibal, finding the bridge stripped of most of its planking, took its guard of about 600 prisoner and crossed two days upstream on a bridge of boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soon after, the Celts serving with the Romans fell in the night on the men camped nearest them, cut the heads from the dead and went over, some 2,000 foot and under 200 horse, and Scipio marched away to the Trebia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.65, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.66, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.67, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.56, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/D%C3%A9faite_de_Scipion_pr%C3%A8s_du_T%C3%A9ssin_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-D%C3%A9faite_de_Scipion_pr%C3%A8s_du_T%C3%A9ssin_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"The rescue of the consul, in a French engraving of the 1790s","desc":"Plate 74 of Mirys's Figures de l'histoire de la republique romaine, headed The defeat of Scipio near the Ticinus. It is a neoclassical arrangement rather than a record: the son stands over his seated father on foot with a shield, where Polybius has him charge the ring of enemies on horseback at the head of a troop.","credit":"J. D. Mirys and Malapeau, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%A9faite_de_Scipion_pr%C3%A8s_du_T%C3%A9ssin_%28cropped%29.jpg","alt":"A monochrome engraving of a helmeted young man striding forward with a raised shield to defend a seated wounded older man, while armed figures fall back on either side and a skirmish continues on the slope behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the javelineers never get their volley away?","a":"Because both sides were so eager to close that they retired through the gaps between their own squadrons for fear of being trodden underfoot."},{"q":"Why did the Numidians decide the action?","a":"Because they were kept on the wings to work round the flanks, and coming into the Roman rear they rode down the javelineers and broke the Roman horse."},{"q":"Why does the story of the consul’s son carry a coda?","a":"Because after being saluted as his father’s preserver he never exposed himself without sufficient reason, which is Polybius marking the difference between courage and rashness."}]},
{"id":"rm-214","num":214,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"A thousand horse and a thousand foot lay hidden in a brambled watercourse on open ground until the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was joined, then came out into the Roman rear.","questions":["Romans who had crossed a breast-high river unfed in snow met at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an enemy that had eaten in its tents and armed itself beside the fires.","About 10,000 men broke clean through the Carthaginian line at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and marched off in order to Placentia, unable to help either wing."],"answer":"Battle of the Trebia","answerText":"Battle of the Trebia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE, near the solstice</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16,000 Romans, 20,000 allies</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carthaginian</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000 foot, 10,000 horse</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","rome","africa"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Trebia</b> was Hannibal's first full victory in Italy, fought near the winter solstice in 218 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tiberius Sempronius wanted a decisive blow while his wounded colleague was out of action, so that Scipio could not share it and the incoming consuls would not take office first — and Scipio argued for a winter's drilling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hannibal wanted the same battle for the opposite reasons, the Celtic enthusiasm still fresh and the legions newly levied, and knew Sempronius would take it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had marked a treeless plain crossed by a watercourse with steep banks thick with brambles, knowing Romans distrusted wooded ground and were not at all afraid of open country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Open ground hides an ambush better, Polybius explains, because the men can see far around them, and a bank with thorn is cover enough if the shields are laid device-side down over the helmets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He chose a hundred horse and a hundred foot, had each pick ten more, and sent the resulting thousand of each under his brother Mago into the watercourse by night, then had the Numidians ride up at daybreak and shoot at the Roman camp to draw the army out unfed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 16,000 Romans, 20,000 allies and 4,000 horse crossed the Trebia breast-high in snow and rain, while the Carthaginians ate in their tents and armed themselves round the fires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hannibal drew up 20,000 foot in a single line, with over 10,000 horse and the elephants split between the wings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Roman skirmishers had spent their missiles on the Numidians and the wet had spoiled the rest, so the flanks gave first, and then the ambush came out of the watercourse into the Roman rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both wings were driven back onto the river, but some 10,000 of the centre broke clean through the Carthaginian line and marched off in order to Placentia, while most of the rest died at the water; the storm that followed killed every elephant but one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.71, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.72, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.73, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.74, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.70, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Fiume_Trebbia_a_Rivergaro.JPG/1280px-Fiume_Trebbia_a_Rivergaro.JPG","title":"The Trebbia at Rivergaro, in its lower valley","desc":"A shallow river spread in braids across a wide gravel bed, with open ground on both banks and low hills behind. This is the modern river and not the crossing place, but it is the kind of water the account describes: fordable most of the year, and breast-high after a night of rain higher up the valley.","credit":"Dani4P at Italian Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fiume_Trebbia_a_Rivergaro.JPG","alt":"A wide braided river running over pale gravel flats at dusk, autumn trees along the far bank, low wooded hills and a hazy sun behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did both commanders want the same battle?","a":"Because Sempronius wanted a decisive blow before his colleague recovered and the new consuls took office, and Hannibal wanted it while the Celts were enthusiastic and the legions newly levied."},{"q":"Why hide an ambush on open ground?","a":"Because open ground hides one better, the men seeing far around them and suspecting nothing, and a bank with thorn is cover enough if the shields are laid device-side down over the helmets."},{"q":"Why did the Roman skirmishers count for nothing?","a":"Because they had spent their missiles on the Numidians who drew the army out unfed, and the wet had spoiled the rest."}]},
{"id":"rm-215","num":215,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"A misty morning and a road pinched between hills and water let Hannibal attack from every side at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where a consul and about 15,000 men died.","questions":["Burning the country beyond the Roman camp was calculated to make Flaminius follow rather than be jeered at, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was brought about.","Men forced into the water at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood with only their heads above it and were killed by the cavalry, or asked their comrades to do it."],"answer":"Battle of Lake Trasimene","answerText":"Battle of Lake Trasimene","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">217 BCE, in Etruria</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 15,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Surrendered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 6,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","history","warfare","rome","africa"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Lake Trasimene</b> destroyed a Roman consular army in Etruria in 217 BCE, and Polybius presents it as a victory won by reading the enemy commander before choosing the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hannibal had learnt that Flaminius was a mob-courtier and a demagogue, with no talent for war and exceedingly self-confident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calculated that if he burnt the country beyond the Roman camp, the consul would not endure his own soldiers' jeering and would follow anywhere rather than wait for his colleague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius draws the rule out of it: a commander should look for the exposed part of his opponent's mind rather than his body, rashness and conceit being the openings an enemy plots against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It worked as reckoned: the smoke rising from all quarters made Flaminius furious, and he would not hear the officers who urged him to wait for his colleague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The road ran along a narrow strip of level ground between high hills and the lake, closed at the far end by a steep hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He camped on it with his Spaniards and Africans, laid his slingers and pikemen under the heights on one side and his cavalry and Celts under the other, the last at the narrow entrance between hillside and water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The morning was unusually misty, and when most of the column was inside he attacked from every side at once, so that the officers could not make out what was happening and men were cut to pieces in marching order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Flaminius was killed by Celts and about 15,000 fell in the valley, while those shut in between hillside and lake waded out until only their heads showed and were dispatched by the cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 6,000 who had broken through in front reached high ground, saw the disaster when the mist lifted and surrendered; Hannibal said Maharbal had no authority to promise their lives, kept the Romans and sent the allies home free, saying he came to fight Rome for the freedom of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.80, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.81, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.82, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.83, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.84, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.85, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Battaglia_del_lago_Trasimeno_%28Galleria_delle_carte_geografiche%29.jpg/1280px-Battaglia_del_lago_Trasimeno_%28Galleria_delle_carte_geografiche%29.jpg","title":"The battle painted into a wall map of Perugia, in the Vatican Gallery of Maps","desc":"A detail from the frescoed map gallery, painted in the 1580s and so seventeen centuries after the event. The lake is at the top left with its islands named, the shore villages are labelled, and tents, marching columns and fighting figures are scattered over the ground between water and hills. The cartouche reads that here Hannibal, the consul being killed, routed the Romans, and dates it 537 from the founding of the city.","credit":"Pietro Oldrado, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battaglia_del_lago_Trasimeno_%28Galleria_delle_carte_geografiche%29.jpg","alt":"A painted map in fresco showing a blue lake with two islands at the upper left, named villages along its shore, and small figures of tents, marching soldiers and skirmishes spread across green hills, with a Latin inscription on a scroll."},"why":[{"q":"Why did burning the country beyond the camp work as a lure?","a":"Because Hannibal had learnt the consul was a demagogue with no talent for war, who would not endure his own soldiers’ jeering and would follow rather than wait."},{"q":"Why does Polybius draw a general rule from it?","a":"Because a commander should look for the exposed part of an opponent’s mind rather than his body, rashness and conceit being the openings an enemy plots against."},{"q":"Why did Hannibal free the allies and keep the Romans?","a":"Because he said he had come to fight Rome for the freedom of Italy, so releasing them without ransom was the claim made in action."}]},
{"id":"rm-216","num":216,"category":"The Punic wars","question":"Made dictator after Trasimene, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused every set battle and made the war turn on Rome's inexhaustible supplies of provisions and men.","questions":["Rome took the unprecedented step of giving a Master of the Horse power equal to that of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that two dictators held the same command at once.","Five hundred horse and 5,000 foot hidden in the hollows round a treeless hill nearly destroyed Minucius, until <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came up with fresh legions and Hannibal broke off."],"answer":"Quintus Fabius Maximus","answerText":"Quintus Fabius Maximus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dictator</span><span class=\"dt-v\">217 BCE, after Trasimene</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lictors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24, against a consul's 12</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Camps apart</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 12 stades</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Quintus Fabius Maximus</b> was made dictator after Trasimene, a man of admirable judgement whose family, Polybius says, still bore the name Maximus in his own day because of what this one achieved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dictator is attended by 24 lictors where a consul has 12, and is a general with absolute power, every magistrate in Rome but the tribunes going out of office on his appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He judged a set battle must be lost: the enemy had trained in war from their earliest youth under a general brought up among them, had beaten the Romans twice running, and had thrown everything else away so that their only hope of safety lay in victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So he fell back on the advantages Rome had and Carthage had not, inexhaustible supplies of provisions and men, and regulated the war by them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He moved parallel to Hannibal, taking the best ground first, never letting a man forage or straggle, and killing or taking those of the enemy who wandered off after plunder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He was despised for it at first and said to be playing the coward, and his own Master of the Horse, Minucius, ran him down for slowness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When an exaggerated account of a success of Minucius' reached Rome, the people gave him power equal to the dictator's, so that two dictators held one command at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fabius returned unchanged and offered him alternate days of command or half the army; Minucius took the half, and they camped about twelve stades apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hannibal, who had learnt of the rivalry from prisoners, hid 500 horse and about 5,000 foot in the hollows round a treeless hill in parties of two or three hundred, then seized the crest at daybreak to draw Minucius on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ambush all but destroyed him until Fabius came up with his own fresh legions and Hannibal broke off, and it was plain to all present that rashness had lost the day and caution saved it, as before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.87, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.89, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.90, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.103, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.104, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.105, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/N26FabiusCunctator.jpg/960px-N26FabiusCunctator.jpg","title":"Fabius Cunctator in the gardens of Schoenbrunn","desc":"One of the marble figures set along the Great Parterre at Vienna between 1773 and 1780, carved by Joseph Baptist Hagenauer. It is an invention of its own century rather than a portrait, but the pose is an argument about the man: armoured for war, a hand at the chin, leaning on the bundled rods that stand for the office he held.","credit":"schurl50, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N26FabiusCunctator.jpg","alt":"A white marble statue of a man in Roman armour and a plumed helmet standing against a clipped hedge, one hand raised to his chin in thought, the other resting on a bundle of rods."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he judge a set battle certain to be lost?","a":"Because the enemy had trained in war from earliest youth under a general brought up among them, had beaten Rome twice running, and had no hope of safety but victory."},{"q":"Why was avoiding battle the way to use Rome’s advantages?","a":"Because those advantages were inexhaustible provisions and men, which tell in a long war and not in one afternoon."},{"q":"Why did dividing the command nearly destroy the army?","a":"Because Hannibal learnt of the rivalry from prisoners and hid 5,000 foot and 500 horse in the hollows round a bare hill to draw Minucius onto them."}]},
{"id":"rm-217","num":217,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The wearing down of a stronger enemy by refusing him a set battle is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after the dictator who shadowed Hannibal through Italy and was reproached as a coward for it.","questions":["Camping in hills beyond the reach of Carthaginian cavalry and forbidding his men to forage, the dictator waged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that took stragglers rather than risking a battle he judged lost.","When Hannibal escaped the pass at Falernum by driving oxen with blazing horns up a ridge, Fabius stayed in camp, holding to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> even as Rome called him a coward."],"answer":"Fabian strategy","answerText":"Fabian strategy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">217 BCE, after Trasimene</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At the pass</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 4,000 men posted</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oxen driven</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 2,000, by night</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","warfare","history","italy","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Fabian strategy</b> is the wearing down of a stronger enemy by refusing him a set battle, named after Quintus Fabius Maximus, who fought Hannibal in this way from 217 BCE. Polybius says Fabius, judging a general engagement certain to end in a reverse, fell back on the advantage the Romans did have, inexhaustible supplies of provisions and men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He moved parallel to the Carthaginians, always occupying in advance the positions that his knowledge of the country told him were the most advantageous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With stores always in his rear he never allowed his soldiers to forage or to straggle from the camp on any pretext, but kept them massed together, watching for openings. In this way he took or killed numbers of the enemy who had strayed far from their own camp, reducing an army whose numbers were strictly limited while restoring the spirits of his own. <br><br> Plutarch describes him pitching camp in hilly country, out of reach of the Carthaginian cavalry, meaning to wear out and consume gradually an enemy whose vigour was at its height but whose resources were scanty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method gave up the countryside, and when Hannibal laid waste the plain of Falernum, Fabius kept to the hills alongside rather than come down into open ground where the enemy were much his superiors in horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Expecting the Carthaginians to leave by the pass they had entered by, he set about 4,000 men at the pass itself and camped with the rest on a hill above it. Hannibal had faggots bound to the horns of about 2,000 captured plough oxen, lit, and driven up a ridge in the night, whereupon the guards left the narrow ground to meet them and the army marched out through the gorge with its booty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fabius, at a loss and holding to his resolve not to hazard a general engagement, stayed in camp until daylight, and though generally reproached for letting the enemy escape he did not abandon the policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.89, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.90, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Fabius Maximus</i> 5, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1916), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Fabius_Maximus*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.92, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.93, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.94, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Diretto_in_Apulia_Annibale_sfugge_al_Temporeggiatore_applicando_torce_sulle_corna_dei_buoi.jpg/1280px-Diretto_in_Apulia_Annibale_sfugge_al_Temporeggiatore_applicando_torce_sulle_corna_dei_buoi.jpg","title":"Hannibal's oxen, in an 1885 Italian print","desc":"Faggots are lashed to the horns of captured plough oxen and lit while Hannibal watches from horseback. The print is a 19th-century imagining rather than a record; Polybius has the beasts driven up a ridge away from the pass, so that the Romans guarding the narrow ground would leave it to meet them.","alt":"An engraving of men holding down long-horned oxen on the ground and tying bundles of sticks to their horns by firelight, with a mounted commander and helmeted soldiers looking on.","credit":"Unknown artist, 19th-century print, 1885. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diretto_in_Apulia_Annibale_sfugge_al_Temporeggiatore_applicando_torce_sulle_corna_dei_buoi.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did keeping the men massed together wear the enemy down?","a":"Because with stores always in his rear he could kill or take those who strayed from their own camp, reducing an army whose numbers were strictly limited."},{"q":"Why did the method mean giving up the countryside?","a":"Because coming down to defend the plain meant open ground where the enemy were much his superiors in horse, so he kept to the hills while Falernum was laid waste."},{"q":"Why did the oxen trick work at the pass?","a":"Because the lights moving up a ridge in the night drew the guards off the narrow ground to meet them, and the army marched out through the gorge with its booty."}]},
{"id":"rm-218","num":218,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome put eight legions into the field, a thing never done before, and lost most of them in a day at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Hannibal surrounded and destroyed the largest army it had raised.","questions":["Because the consuls held command on alternate days and disagreed about the ground, the battle of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fought on a treeless plain that suited Hannibal's cavalry and destroyed a Roman army.","Polybius counts about 70,000 Roman dead at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Livy 40,000 foot with 2,700 horse, after which Tarentum surrendered and Rome gave up Italy."],"answer":"Battle of Cannae","answerText":"Battle of Cannae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">216 BCE, in Apulia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman legions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8, never done before</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 70,000, per Polybius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">40,000 foot and 2,700 horse, per Livy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","italy","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Cannae</b> was fought in Apulia in 216 BCE, and destroyed the largest army Rome had ever put in the field. Hannibal, judging that he must compel the Romans to fight, seized the citadel of Cannae, where they had collected the corn and other supplies from the country round Canusium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Senate decided to give battle and to bring eight legions into the field, which Polybius says had never been done before: Rome normally sent out four, of about 4,000 foot apiece, raised to 5,000 on occasions of exceptional gravity. Both consuls went, Lucius Aemilius Paullus and Gaius Terentius Varro, holding command on alternate days by the usual practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aemilius, seeing the district flat and treeless, was against attacking there because the enemy were superior in cavalry, and Terentius was of the contrary opinion, so that disputes arose between the generals. <br><br> Hannibal for his part told his assembled men to look at the country round and ask what greater boon they could crave than to fight the decisive battle on such ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When his cavalry under Hasdrubal had cut up nearly all the Roman horse by the river, he left the Numidians to the pursuit and led his squadrons against the rear of the legions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Romans held out while they could still present a front on every side, but as the outer ranks fell the rest were gradually huddled in and killed where they stood, Aemilius among them and both consuls of the previous year with him. Polybius puts about 70,000 dead, 10,000 taken at the camp and perhaps 3,000 escaped, against Carthaginian losses of about 4,000 Celts, 1,500 Spaniards and Africans and 200 horse; Livy counts 40,000 foot and 2,700 horse killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Tarentum surrendered at once and Campanian towns invited Hannibal in, while Rome abandoned all hope of retaining its supremacy in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.107, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.110, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.111, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.116, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.117, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.49, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.118, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Punti_storici_d%E2%80%99Italia_campo_della_battaglia_di_Canne.jpg/1280px-Punti_storici_d%E2%80%99Italia_campo_della_battaglia_di_Canne.jpg","title":"The field of Cannae, engraved in 1885","desc":"A view over the plain of the Aufidus, the river winding across it, from the hill where the town stood. The engraving records the ground rather than the battle: Aemilius argued against fighting on open country like this, where the Carthaginians had the better cavalry.","alt":"A wood engraving of a wide flat plain with a river meandering across it, seen from rough rocky ground in the foreground, with low hills on the horizon.","credit":"Gustav Adolf Closs, in L'illustrazione popolare, Milan, 1885. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Punti_storici_d%E2%80%99Italia_campo_della_battaglia_di_Canne.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did seizing the citadel of Cannae compel a battle?","a":"Because the Romans had collected there the corn and other supplies from the country round Canusium, so it could not be left in enemy hands."},{"q":"Why did the consuls disagree about fighting?","a":"Because the district was flat and treeless, which Aemilius saw as favouring an enemy superior in cavalry and Terentius did not."},{"q":"Why did the Roman numbers stop counting once surrounded?","a":"Because they held out while they could present a front on every side, and as the outer ranks fell the rest were huddled in and killed where they stood."}]},
{"id":"rm-219","num":219,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Hannibal's crescent at Cannae gave way in the centre, drawing the legions forward until his Africans could charge their flanks, a manoeuvre called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which closes on an enemy from both sides.","questions":["Polybius says the Romans crowded from the wings towards the centre and so found Africans on either flank, completing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Hannibal had designed before the fighting began.","An army attacked on both flanks at once cannot bring its numbers to bear, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed a Roman force at Cannae that outnumbered its enemy nearly two to one."],"answer":"double envelopment","answerText":"double envelopment","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Used at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Cannae, 216 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punic foot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 40,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman foot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 80,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","warfare","history","italy","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Double envelopment</b> closes on both flanks of an enemy at once, so that his numbers cannot be brought to bear; the classic instance is Hannibal's at Cannae in 216 BCE. Polybius says he drew his line straight and then advanced with the central companies of the Spaniards and Celts, the rest keeping contact but gradually falling off, to produce a crescent with its convex face towards the Romans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His object was to open the action with them and hold the heavy-armed Africans, equipped with the choicest arms captured in earlier battles, as a reserve on either wing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against about 10,000 Carthaginian horse and an infantry not much exceeding 40,000, the Romans had some 80,000 foot and rather more than 6,000 horse, their maniples set closer together than usual and each far deeper than its front. Superior cavalry was the condition of the design, and Polybius drew the moral that it is better to fight with half the enemy's infantry and an overwhelming force of horse than to equal him in everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Spanish and Celtic horse beat the Roman cavalry by the river in a struggle Polybius calls truly barbaric, the men dismounting to fight hand to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Spaniards and Celts in the centre held for a time, then gave way under the weight of the legions and fell back, breaking up the crescent. The maniples pursued furiously and crowded in from the wings towards the middle until they had the Africans on both flanks, whereupon those on the right faced left and those on the left faced right and charged, the situation itself indicating to them how to act. Caught between the two divisions, the Romans could no longer keep formation and turned singly or in companies to meet the attack on either side. Polybius has Hasdrubal's cavalry close the trap by leaving the pursuit to strike the legions from behind, where Livy makes the Africans extend their own wings and enclose the Romans in the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.113, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.114, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.117, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.115, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.116, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.47, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Battle_cannae_destruction.svg/1280px-Battle_cannae_destruction.svg.png","title":"The closing of the trap at Cannae","desc":"The Roman army in red, enclosed by the African infantry turning inward on both flanks while the Carthaginian cavalry comes round behind. A modern staff diagram rather than an ancient one, drawn from Polybius' account; the plotted positions are a reconstruction.","alt":"A battle map showing a red arc of Roman formations pressed into a blue crescent, with blue blocks closing on both of its flanks and dashed arrows sweeping round to its rear.","credit":"Department of History, United States Military Academy; vector version by Hogweard. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_cannae_destruction.svg"},"why":[{"q":"Why advance the centre into a crescent?","a":"Because it opened the action with the Spaniards and Celts while holding the heavy-armed Africans as a reserve on either wing, ready when the centre gave way."},{"q":"Why was superior cavalry the condition of the whole design?","a":"Because the horse had to beat the Roman cavalry and then close the trap from behind, which is why Polybius says it is better to fight with half the enemy’s infantry and an overwhelming force of horse."},{"q":"Why did the legions’ own success trap them?","a":"Because they pursued furiously and crowded in from the wings towards the middle until they had the Africans on both flanks, who then faced inwards and charged."}]},
{"id":"rm-220","num":220,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy's account of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has mourning limited to thirty days, boys of seventeen enrolled, eight thousand slaves bought and armed, and the captives left unransomed.","questions":["Two Vestals were condemned, a Gaulish and a Greek couple were buried alive in the cattle market, and allies began to defect, yet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced no offer of peace.","Crowds of every rank met the surviving consul of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and thanked him for not despairing of the republic, though he had been the principal cause of the defeat."],"answer":"Rome after Cannae","answerText":"Rome after Cannae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">After</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Cannae, 216 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mourning</span><span class=\"dt-v\">limited to 30 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New levy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 legions from age 17</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Slaves armed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8,000, bought publicly</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","politics","religion","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Rome after Cannae</b>, in 216 BCE, had lost its largest army and was watching its southern allies go over to the enemy, yet made no offer of peace. Livy has Maharbal urge an immediate march, promising Hannibal would dine on the Capitol in five days, and answer his hesitation by saying the gods have not given all things to the same man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The losses were made known house by house, and the rite of Ceres left undone because no matron was out of mourning, so the Senate limited mourning to thirty days lest other rites lapse with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A dictator was named, and the levy took men from seventeen and some still in the boy's toga, making four legions and a thousand horse, armed with old spoils taken from the temples. Eight thousand slaves were bought at public expense and armed, each asked first whether he was willing to serve, and preferred, Livy says, though the captives could have been redeemed for less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Hannibal had offered exactly that, dismissing the allied prisoners without ransom and addressing the Roman ones gently; the Senate resolved they should not be bought back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two Vestals were convicted of unchastity that year, one buried alive at the Colline gate and the other a suicide; the Sibylline books were consulted and Fabius Pictor sent to ask Delphi how the gods might be appeased. Then a Gaulish and a Greek couple were let down alive into a walled pit in the cattle market, a rite Livy calls by no means Roman. Allies that had held firm now wavered: the Hirpini, the Samnites but for the Pentri, the Bruttians and Lucanians, and most of the Greek coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius says the Senate neglected no means in its power but encouraged the people, strengthened the defences and deliberated with manly coolness; Livy records that no mention of peace was made, and that crowds of every rank thanked the returning consul for not despairing of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.51, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.56, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.57, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.58, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 22.61, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.118, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Lodovico_Pogliaghi_-_Roman_women_in_the_temple_of_Mars_after_Cannae%2C_215_BC.png/1280px-Lodovico_Pogliaghi_-_Roman_women_in_the_temple_of_Mars_after_Cannae%2C_215_BC.png","title":"Roman women mourning after Cannae, drawn in 1885","desc":"Women prostrate before a statue of Mars while an altar smokes, illustrating the grief Livy describes filling every house in the city. An engraving made for a popular history in 1885, not a record of the scene; its file name gives the year as 215 where the battle was fought in 216 BCE.","alt":"A wood engraving of a temple interior in which many veiled women kneel or lie on the floor before a smoking altar and an armed statue holding a spear.","credit":"Lodovico Pogliaghi, in Francesco Bertolini, Storia di Roma, Milan, 1886. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lodovico_Pogliaghi_-_Roman_women_in_the_temple_of_Mars_after_Cannae%2C_215_BC.png"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Senate limit mourning to thirty days?","a":"Because the rite of Ceres had been left undone, no matron being out of mourning, and other rites would have lapsed with it."},{"q":"Why buy and arm slaves rather than ransom the prisoners?","a":"Because the Senate resolved the captives should not be bought back, though Livy says they could have been redeemed for less than the eight thousand slaves cost."},{"q":"Why does Livy call one of the year’s rites un-Roman?","a":"Because a Gaulish and a Greek couple were let down alive into a walled pit in the cattle market, which he says is by no means a Roman practice."}]},
{"id":"rm-221","num":221,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy blames long prosperity, a demagogue's grip on the senate and a consul's frank account of Rome's losses for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the richest city in Campania going over to Hannibal.","questions":["The treaty behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised the city its own laws and magistrates and three hundred captives to exchange for its horsemen garrisoning Sicily for Rome.","Roman citizens were shut in the baths to suffocate during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an atrocity Livy records as going beyond anything the treaty with Hannibal had stipulated."],"answer":"defection of Capua","answerText":"defection of Capua","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">216 BCE, after Cannae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Campanian horse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300, garrisoning Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Captives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300, promised in exchange</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","italy","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>defection of Capua</b> in 216 BCE took the largest and richest city of Campania over to Hannibal, and much of the country round it with her. Livy describes a place wantoning under long prosperity, where Pacuvius Calavius had rescued the senate from a commons intent on murdering it and had thereby made it subservient to himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What held Capua back was intermarriage with Roman families and the three hundred Campanian horsemen, the flower of the nobility, whom Rome had sent to garrison the cities of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ambassadors found the consul at Venusia with a few half-armed men, telling them that legions, cavalry, arms, standards, horses, money and provisions had all been lost, and asking Campania for thirty thousand foot and four thousand horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the road home Vibius Virius argued that Hannibal would grant whatever terms they pleased, and that once he had gone back to Africa the sovereignty of Italy would be left to the Campanians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The treaty they made gave Capua her own laws and magistrates, exempted every Campanian from Carthaginian command and from serving against his will, and promised three hundred captives of their own choosing to exchange for the horsemen in Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Beyond its terms the commons seized Roman officials and citizens and shut them in the baths, where they died of the heat and vapour. Decius Magius opposed both the embassy and the admission of a Carthaginian garrison, recalling the tyranny of Pyrrhus and the slavery of the Tarentines, and was summoned to Hannibal's camp for it. Livy also reports an annalistic story that the Campanians had first demanded one of the two consulships from Rome and been escorted out of the city, but declines to vouch for it, since Coelius and other writers do not mention it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hannibal wintered his army in the city, and Livy has him complain afterwards that he had led one sort of troops into those quarters and brought another out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.2, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.4, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.5, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.6, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.7, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.18, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.45, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Mater_Matuta.jpg/960px-Mater_Matuta.jpg","title":"A tufa votive from ancient Capua","desc":"An enthroned woman holding swaddled infants, one of the votives from the Capuan sanctuary now in the Museo Archeologico dell'Antica Capua. It shows the city's own cult rather than the revolt: what the treaty with Hannibal preserved was exactly this, Capua's own laws, magistrates and gods.","alt":"A weathered pale stone statue of a seated woman in a high-backed chair, her lap and arms filled with tightly swaddled babies.","credit":"Nicola D'Orta, 2012. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mater_Matuta.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why had the city held to Rome until then?","a":"Because of intermarriage with Roman families and the three hundred Campanian horsemen, the flower of the nobility, whom Rome had sent to garrison the cities of Sicily."},{"q":"Why did the terms look worth taking?","a":"Because they gave Capua her own laws and magistrates and exempted every Campanian from Carthaginian command, on the argument that once Hannibal went home the sovereignty of Italy would be Campanian."},{"q":"Why does Livy refuse to vouch for the demand for a consulship?","a":"Because Coelius and other writers do not mention it, so he reports the annalistic story without standing behind it."}]},
{"id":"rm-222","num":222,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Archimedes kept the Romans off for eight months during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until a carelessly guarded tower and a festival let Marcellus over the wall by night.","questions":["Ladders called sambucae were raised from lashed pairs of warships in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and beams above the wall dropped ten-talent stones on them until the fleet gave up.","Polybius drew from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the lesson that one man's genius can outweigh any number of hands, since Rome hoped to take the city only if one old Syracusan were removed."],"answer":"siege of Syracuse","answerText":"siege of Syracuse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">212 BCE, to Marcellus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Investment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 months, no assault</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60 quinqueremes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sambucae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on 8 lashed ships</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","sicily","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>siege of Syracuse</b> was the Roman attack on the greatest Greek city of Sicily, which had left Rome's friendship after its old king died, and which fell in 212 BCE. Polybius says Appius Claudius went at the walls by land while Marcellus attacked from the sea with sixty quinqueremes, expecting to be well ahead within five days and not reckoning with Archimedes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eight more had the oars stripped from one side and were lashed in pairs to carry sambucae, ladders four feet broad hauled upright by pulleys from the masts to set men on the battlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archimedes met them with engines ranged for every distance, using smaller ones as the ships closed, and loopholes a palm wide cut through the wall, behind which archers and light catapults shot the decks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beams reared above the battlements dropped ten-talent stones on the sambucae, and an iron hand on a chain caught a ship by the prow, stood her on her stern and let her drop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Marcellus made fun of his own failure, saying Archimedes used his ships to ladle sea-water into his wine cups and had his sambuca band flogged from the banquet. Appius called a council of his tribunes, which resolved on any means rather than an assault, and in eight months of investment they never attempted one again, hoping to reduce the city by famine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch says it fell to a trick: negotiating over a captured Spartan, Marcellus noticed a carelessly guarded tower, measured its height, and went over the wall while the city kept a festival of Artemis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Looking down at daybreak he is said to have wept for what was coming, and though he forbade his men to kill, outrage or enslave any Syracusan, he could not refuse them plunder. Archimedes was killed by a soldier, in one account while working at a diagram he would not leave; Plutarch gives three versions and says Marcellus turned from the slayer as from a polluted man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 8.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/8*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 8.4, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/8*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 8.5, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/8*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 8.6, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/8*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 8.7, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/8*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Marcellus</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marcellus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Life of Marcellus</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marcellus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Siracusa_Castello_Eurialo_Vorfestung.jpg/1280px-Siracusa_Castello_Eurialo_Vorfestung.jpg","title":"The Euryalus fortress at the western end of Syracuse","desc":"Masonry of the outworks guarding the landward approach to the city. The fortress was built long before this siege, and gives an idea of the walls Polybius describes running along a chain of hills with overhanging brows, hard to approach even unopposed.","alt":"Two tall blocks of weathered limestone masonry from an ancient fortification, with fallen squared blocks and dry grass around them under a clear sky.","credit":"Clemensfranz, 2006. CC BY 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siracusa_Castello_Eurialo_Vorfestung.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Romans give up assaulting the walls?","a":"Because Archimedes met them with engines ranged for every distance, dropping ten-talent stones on the sambucae and lifting ships by the prow, so a council resolved on any means rather than an assault."},{"q":"Why did the city fall to a trick rather than a siege?","a":"Because negotiating over a captured Spartan let Marcellus notice a carelessly guarded tower and measure its height, and the wall was crossed while the city kept a festival of Artemis."},{"q":"Why could a general who wept for the city not save it?","a":"Because he forbade his men to kill, outrage or enslave any Syracusan and still could not refuse them plunder."}]},
{"id":"rm-223","num":223,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"For eight years the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held Carthage's richest province in check, until twenty thousand Celtiberians were bought out of the Roman camp and both brothers were killed.","questions":["Beginning at Emporium and crossing the Ebro for the first time, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept reinforcements from reaching Hannibal in Italy for the better part of a decade.","Livy draws from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the lesson that a general should never trust foreign auxiliaries so far as to leave his own strength the smaller part."],"answer":"Spanish campaigns of the Scipios","answerText":"Spanish campaigns of the Scipios","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE, at Emporium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">211 BCE, both killed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Celtiberians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000, bought off</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gnaeus died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 days after Publius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","spain","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Spanish campaigns of the Scipios</b> kept Carthage's richest province, and the reinforcements it could send Hannibal, occupied for eight years from 218 BCE. Polybius says Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio, left with the fleet when his brother turned back for Italy, sailed to Emporium, reduced the coastal towns as far as the Ebro, and beat Hanno in a pitched battle at Cissa, taking that general and the Iberian chief Andobales prisoner along with the baggage of Hannibal's army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Senate, afraid that Spain would furnish Hannibal with supplies, soldiers and money, fitted out twenty ships and sent Publius to join his brother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Together they crossed the Ebro, which the Romans had never before ventured to do, and for the first time aimed at dominion on the far bank. Years later, with three Carthaginian armies in the field against them and twenty thousand Celtiberians newly hired, the brothers judged themselves strong enough to end the war and divided their forces to catch all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Publius took two thirds of the army against Hasdrubal son of Gisgo and Mago, while Gnaeus kept the rest and the Celtiberians and went after Hasdrubal Barca. Seeing how few Romans were in that camp, Hasdrubal paid the Celtiberians a large sum to go home, and they marched off, telling the Romans who begged them to stay only that a war called them back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy makes the moral explicit: no general should trust foreign auxiliaries so far as to leave his own troops the smaller part of his camp. Publius, harried day and night by Masinissa's Numidians and expecting Indibilis with seven thousand five hundred Suessetani, marched out at midnight to meet him, was surrounded by three separate forces and run through the side with a lance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gnaeus was killed 29 days afterwards, in the eighth year after his arrival, some accounts putting his death on the hill at the first onset and others in a tower burnt down over him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.76, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.97, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 25.32, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 25.33, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 25.34, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 25.36, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Emp%C3%BAries.jpg/1280px-Emp%C3%BAries.jpg","title":"The bay at Empúries, where the Roman fleet put in","desc":"The shore of the Greek city Polybius calls Emporium, on the Gulf of Roses in north-east Spain. Gnaeus Scipio landed here and worked down the coast to the Ebro; nothing of the war is visible, and the picture is here to show where it began.","alt":"A wide low view across a shallow rocky bay at sunset, with a headland at the left and a long range of hills across the water behind.","credit":"Alba Sabaté, 2017. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emp%C3%BAries.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome fight so hard for Spain?","a":"Because the Senate feared it would furnish Hannibal with supplies, soldiers and money, so it fitted out twenty ships and sent a second Scipio to hold the province."},{"q":"Why did dividing the army prove fatal?","a":"Because Hasdrubal, seeing how few Romans were in one camp, paid the twenty thousand Celtiberians a large sum to go home, and they marched off saying only that a war called them back."},{"q":"Why does Livy draw a rule from it?","a":"Because no general should trust foreign auxiliaries so far as to leave his own troops the smaller part of his camp."}]},
{"id":"rm-224","num":224,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius insists that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not fortune's favourite but a calculator, who let his men believe his plans came from the gods because it made them bolder.","questions":["When nobody would stand for the Spanish command after two generals had died there, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered himself at about 24 and every century voted for him.","Aged seventeen at the Po, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> charged the horsemen surrounding his wounded father, who afterwards saluted him before the army as his preserver."],"answer":"Scipio Africanus","answerText":"Scipio Africanus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At the Ticinus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 years old, his first campaign</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elected to Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 24, in 210 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Predecessors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, killed 29 days apart</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Scipio Africanus</b> carried the war against Hannibal into Spain and then Africa, and Polybius wrote about him partly to correct other writers. They made him a man favoured by fortune who owed most of his success to chance, which Polybius says deserves congratulation rather than praise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He compares him to Lycurgus, who invoked the Pythia to get his own scheme believed: Scipio likewise made his men readier to face danger by letting them think his plans divinely inspired. His informant was Gaius Laelius, who had shared every word and deed from his youth, and who put Scipio's first distinction at seventeen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seeing his father surrounded and badly wounded in the cavalry action by the Po, he charged the encircling enemy alone; his father saluted him before everyone as his preserver, and he never again exposed himself needlessly. <br><br> His second story is the aedileship, which his elder brother was unlikely to win: Scipio told their mother he had twice dreamt both of them elected and met by her at their door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She wished aloud to see the day and agreed, as though to a joke, that they might try; he had a white toga made ready, appeared in the forum while she slept, and the people elected his brother for his sake as well as him. All who heard of the dreams believed he communed with the gods; Polybius says there was no dream, only a man who had judged what the people and his mother felt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With his father and uncle both killed in Spain and nobody willing to stand, Livy has him offer himself at about 24; every century and every man voted for him, and the assembly then fell silent, wondering whether zeal had beaten judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy adds that from the day he put on the man's toga he did no business without first sitting alone in the temple on the Capitol, and leaves open whether he was superstitious or only wanted his orders obeyed as oracles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.4, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.5, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 26.18, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 26.19, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Escipi%C3%B3n_africano.JPG/960px-Escipi%C3%B3n_africano.JPG","title":"A bronze bust long called Scipio Africanus","desc":"Found in the Villa of the Papyri at Herculaneum and traditionally given his name. The museum dates it to the middle of the 1st century BCE, well over a century after Scipio died, and the identification is no longer accepted; no portrait known to be him survives.","alt":"A dark bronze bust of a bald, heavily lined middle-aged man with inlaid pale eyes, wearing a draped cloak, on a marble stand.","credit":"Miguel Hermoso Cuesta, 2013, Naples National Archaeological Museum inv. 5634. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Escipi%C3%B3n_africano.JPG"},"why":[{"q":"Why does Polybius set out to correct other writers about him?","a":"Because they made him a man favoured by fortune who owed most of his success to chance, which deserves congratulation rather than praise."},{"q":"Why did he let his men think his plans divinely inspired?","a":"Because it made them readier to face danger, which is the comparison with Lycurgus invoking the Pythia to get his own scheme believed."},{"q":"Why did the assembly fall silent after electing him?","a":"Because he was about 24 and nobody else would stand, so having voted for him unanimously they wondered whether zeal had beaten judgement."}]},
{"id":"rm-225","num":225,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Learning from fishermen that the lagoon fell every evening, Scipio timed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that five hundred men waded to an undefended wall while the garrison fought at the gate.","questions":["Because three Carthaginian armies were ten days' march away, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave Rome the enemy's treasury, war material and Spanish hostages in a single day.","Polybius says the men who forded the shallows in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> thought a god had done it, though their general had the fishermen's word for the ebb."],"answer":"capture of New Carthage","answerText":"capture of New Carthage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">209 BCE, by Scipio</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Garrison</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,000 trained men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25,000 foot, 2,500 horse</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 300, sent home</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","spain","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>capture of New Carthage</b> in 209 BCE gave Rome the Carthaginian base in Spain in one day's fighting. Polybius says Scipio found the three Carthaginian armies scattered, none within ten days' march of the city, and thought attacking any one of them too dangerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He spent the winter asking about the place, and learnt that it alone among Spanish cities had harbours fit for a fleet, that the Carthaginians kept their money, war material and Spanish hostages there, and that a thousand trained soldiers held the citadel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fishermen who worked there told him the lagoon around the city was shallow, fordable in most parts, and receded towards evening every day. He told nobody but Gaius Laelius, sent him ahead with the fleet, and marched overland with about 25,000 foot and 2,500 horse, arriving on the seventh day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The first assault was called off with the men worn out; then, waiting for the tide, Scipio put five hundred men with ladders on the lagoon shore and covered the whole wall with escaladers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the water drew off they went in as he had promised Neptune would allow, found the battlements above the lagoon deserted, and climbed them unopposed while the defenders crowded to the gate. With the walls taken he sent most of his troops against the inhabitants, with orders to kill everyone they met and begin no pillaging until the signal, which Polybius says is done to inspire terror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About two thousand artisans were made public slaves with freedom promised if Carthage were beaten, the strongest prisoners went into the fleet on the same terms, and over six hundred talents of Carthaginian money to the quaestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He called the three hundred hostages to him, gave the girls earrings and the young men swords and promised them home if their families joined Rome; when the wife of Mandonius knelt to ask for something she would not name, he understood she meant the young women's safety.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.7, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.8, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.14, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.15, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 10.18, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/10*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Muralla_P%C3%BAnica_de_Cartagena.jpg/1280px-Muralla_P%C3%BAnica_de_Cartagena.jpg","title":"Inside the Punic wall of Qart-Hadasht","desc":"Excavated footings of the city wall, kept under cover in modern Cartagena. The site dates them to the end of the 3rd century BCE, with the city's foundation by Hasdrubal in 227, so this is the circuit the Romans escaladed eighteen years later.","alt":"Stone footings of a thick double wall excavated below present ground level and roofed over, with a glazed modern wall and a street visible on the right.","credit":"María Rodriguez, 2019. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muralla_P%C3%BAnica_de_Cartagena.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why go for the city rather than one of the armies?","a":"Because the three Carthaginian armies were scattered and none within ten days’ march, so attacking any one of them was too dangerous while the base held everything they depended on."},{"q":"Why did fishermen decide the assault?","a":"Because they told him the lagoon round the city was shallow, fordable in most parts, and receded towards evening every day, which is where the wall was left unguarded."},{"q":"Why did he order killing before plundering?","a":"Because beginning no pillage until the signal is done to inspire terror, so the troops went at the inhabitants rather than at the property."}]},
{"id":"rm-226","num":226,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"For days Scipio matched Hasdrubal's array, then at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reversed it, putting his legions opposite the Spanish wings and his own Spaniards opposite the Libyan centre.","questions":["Because the Romans had eaten before dawn and the Carthaginians were rushed out fasting, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was decided partly by which army wilted first in the afternoon heat.","At the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the 32 elephants did as much damage to their own side as to Rome's, and the Libyan centre stood all day unable to act."],"answer":"Battle of Ilipa","answerText":"Battle of Ilipa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">206 BCE, near Ilipa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carthaginians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70,000 foot, 4,000 horse</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elephants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">32, on the wings</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Romans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">45,000 foot, 3,000 horse</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","spain","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Ilipa</b> in 206 BCE destroyed the last Carthaginian field army in Spain. Polybius gives Hasdrubal son of Gisgo about 70,000 foot, 4,000 horse and 32 elephants, encamped under the hills with level ground in front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scipio came up with about 45,000 foot and 3,000 horse, most of them Spanish allies he would not depend on in a decisive battle and meant to use for show. Mago and Masinissa charged the Romans as they were making camp and were beaten off by cavalry Scipio had already posted under a hill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For days both armies drew up on the plain between them, Hasdrubal always coming out late with his Libyans in the centre and the elephants before his wings, Scipio always matching him with legions in the centre and Spaniards on the flanks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On the day he had chosen he did the opposite: at first light his men ate and armed, the cavalry rode up and shot into the enemy camp, and the infantry formed at sunrise with the Spaniards in the centre and the Romans on the wings. Hasdrubal, his men still fasting, had to arm and deploy in haste and in his usual order. Scipio then held his centre back and sent both wings forward, wheeling outward to overlap the enemy, so that his best troops met the worst of theirs while the Spaniards came on slowly and never closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The elephants, shot at from every side, trampled their own line; the Libyan centre could neither leave its ground to help the wings nor reach the men facing it; and the Carthaginians, who had eaten nothing, gave way in the day's heat and were driven back to camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A storm of rain saved them from being driven out of it; and when Scipio was congratulated on finishing the war he answered that now was the time to think how to begin one on Carthage, since until then the Carthaginians had been making war on Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.20, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.21, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.22, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.23, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.24, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.24a, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Ilipa1.svg/1280px-Ilipa1.svg.png","title":"The array both armies used before the battle","desc":"Scipio's army in red at the bottom, Hasdrubal's in blue at the top, each with its heavy infantry in the centre. This is the order Scipio repeated for days and then reversed on the morning of the battle, sending his legions to the wings. A modern diagram drawn from a modern reconstruction, not an ancient plan.","alt":"A battle map with two facing lines of blocks and triangles, blue at the top and red at the bottom, each line with a hatched infantry block in the middle, and a key at the right naming cavalry, elephants, skirmishers and camps.","credit":"Citypeek, after a map in Adrian Goldsworthy, The Fall of Carthage. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilipa1.svg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did he use his Spanish allies for show?","a":"Because he would not depend on them in a decisive battle, so they were placed where they came on slowly and never closed."},{"q":"Why did reversing his usual order win the day?","a":"Because it put his best troops against the enemy’s worst, and Hasdrubal, whose men were still fasting, had to arm and deploy in haste in his own accustomed formation."},{"q":"Why did he answer congratulations by talking of a new war?","a":"Because until then the Carthaginians had been making war on Rome, so finishing Spain was the moment to think how to begin one on Carthage."}]},
{"id":"rm-227","num":227,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Where his brother had fought the mountains for five months, Hasdrubal found roads already made and tribes grown used to armies, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took far less time than Rome expected.","questions":["A letter naming Umbria as the brothers' meeting place was taken from six riders near Tarentum, turning <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from a threat into an opportunity for the consuls.","Livy says the speed gained in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was thrown away at Placentia, where a fruitless siege held the army up and kept Hannibal in his winter quarters."],"answer":"Hasdrubal's march into Italy","answerText":"Hasdrubal's march into Italy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">207 BCE, over the Alps</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ligurians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8,000, waiting to join</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alps open</span><span class=\"dt-v\">passable 12 years, Livy says</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Letter taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 riders, near Tarentum</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","italy","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Hasdrubal's march into Italy</b> in 207 BCE was the attempt to reinforce Hannibal after eleven years of war, and it came near to succeeding. Livy says it was decided in Spain rather than at Carthage: Hasdrubal should go to Italy, the seat of the war, and take the Spanish soldiers out of reach of Scipio, whose good offices had made desertion continuous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Rome the consuls' provinces were set at opposite ends of Italy, one against Hannibal in the south and the other against Hasdrubal in Gaul, so the brothers should not join.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was to be met coming down from the Alps, before he could work on the Cisalpine Gauls and an Etruria watching for revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Envoys from Massilia reported that he had passed into Gaul, that Gallic expectations were high, and that he had an immense army and was held back only by winter in the passes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Then a letter from the praetor Lucius Porcius said he had left his winter quarters and was crossing, with eight thousand Ligurians armed and waiting to join him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy explains why it went so quickly where Hannibal had spent five months fighting the Rhone, the Alps, the men and the ground: the roads were made, the Arverni and the Alpine peoples received him and followed him to the war, and twelve years of armies had left the tribes less savage to strangers. These, he says, were the causes that opened the Alps to Hasdrubal. What speed had gained he lost at Placentia, in a fruitless siege of a colony on level ground he thought easy to storm, and the delay held Hannibal back too, who remembered how slow towns are and his own failure at that same colony after the Trebia. Leaving the siege, he sent four Gallic horsemen and two Numidians to his brother; they crossed almost the whole length of Italy, mistook the roads near Tarentum, and were taken with the letter, which named Umbria as the meeting place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.20, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.35, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.38, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.36, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.39, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.43, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Vallon_du_Col_du_Petit-Saint-Bernard_vu_de_la_Redoute_Ruin%C3%A9e_%28%C3%A9t%C3%A9_2019%29.JPG/1280px-Vallon_du_Col_du_Petit-Saint-Bernard_vu_de_la_Redoute_Ruin%C3%A9e_%28%C3%A9t%C3%A9_2019%29.JPG","title":"A western Alpine pass into Italy","desc":"The valley below the Petit-Saint-Bernard at 2,188 m, on the French-Italian border. Which pass Hasdrubal used is not recorded, and this stands for the ground rather than his route; the tracks and the pylon are modern.","alt":"A broad green alpine valley photographed from above, threaded by winding tracks, with bare rock ridges around it and a snow-capped massif on the skyline.","credit":"Florian Pepellin, 2019. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vallon_du_Col_du_Petit-Saint-Bernard_vu_de_la_Redoute_Ruin%C3%A9e_%28%C3%A9t%C3%A9_2019%29.JPG"},"why":[{"q":"Why was the march decided in Spain rather than at Carthage?","a":"Because it took the Spanish soldiers out of reach of Scipio, whose good offices had made desertion continuous, as well as bringing them to the seat of the war."},{"q":"Why was the crossing so much easier than his brother’s?","a":"Because the roads were made, the Arverni and Alpine peoples received him and followed him to the war, and twelve years of armies had left the tribes less savage to strangers."},{"q":"Why did the siege of Placentia cost him the campaign?","a":"Because it was a fruitless delay before a colony on level ground he thought easy to storm, and it held Hannibal back too, who remembered his own failure there."}]},
{"id":"rm-228","num":228,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Leaving his camp standing in front of Hannibal, Nero marched six thousand men north in secret and joined his colleague, so that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fought two consuls to one.","questions":["Hasdrubal saw unfamiliar shields and lean horses and counted the trumpet twice in one camp, but too late: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed his army before it could reach his brother.","Livy counts 56,000 Carthaginian dead at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which the victor had Hasdrubal's head thrown before Hannibal's outposts in southern Italy."],"answer":"Battle of the Metaurus","answerText":"Battle of the Metaurus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">207 BCE, on the Metaurus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nero's march</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 men, north in secret</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elephants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10, six killed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enemy dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">56,000, per Livy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","italy","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Metaurus</b> in 207 BCE destroyed Hasdrubal's army before it could join his brother's. Having read the intercepted letter, the consul Gaius Claudius Nero left his camp standing before Hannibal and marched north with six thousand men, which alarmed Rome as much as a Carthaginian camp at the walls had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once far enough off he told his men that rumour decides wars and news of a second consul would settle this one; the roads were lined with people calling them the safeguards of the state and pressing food on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He came in by night and no tent was added, each tribune taking a tribune and each horseman a horseman, so the camp should not grow and give him away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hasdrubal, riding out before his line, noticed unfamiliar shields and lean horses, sent men to look for sunburnt faces at the water and count the trumpet calls, and learnt it sounded twice in the consul's camp, once in the praetor's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius has him put his ten elephants in front, deepen and narrow his line, and fall on the Roman left, resolved to conquer or die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nero could not work round the enemy on his own wing because of the ground, so he took his men from the right, passed behind the Roman line, and struck their flank. The elephants, penned between the armies and shot at from both, threw Roman and Spanish ranks alike into confusion; six were killed with their drivers and four taken afterwards. Livy counts 56,000 of the enemy killed and 5,400 taken against about 8,000 Romans and allies, and has Livius refuse to hunt the fleeing Gauls so some might carry the news.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Hasdrubal fell fighting, and Polybius, who thought most commanders plan only for success, praises him for guarding his life while there was hope and spending it rather than live in shame; Nero had the head thrown before Hannibal's outposts, and Hannibal said he saw the destiny of Carthage in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.44, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.45, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.46, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.47, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.49, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.51, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Hannibal_bij_het_hoofd_van_Hasdrubal_Essendo_stata_gettata_nel_Campo_di_Annibale%2C_la_testa_di_Asdrubale_suo_fratello%2C_disfatto_da_Livio%2C_e_Nerone%2C_riconosce_da_un_tale_successo_l%27inevitabile_sua_rovina_%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-P-1929-231.jpg/1280px-Hannibal_bij_het_hoofd_van_Hasdrubal_Essendo_stata_gettata_nel_Campo_di_Annibale%2C_la_testa_di_Asdrubale_suo_fratello%2C_disfatto_da_Livio%2C_e_Nerone%2C_riconosce_da_un_tale_successo_l%27inevitabile_sua_rovina_%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-P-1929-231.jpg","title":"Hannibal shown his brother's head, engraved in 1818","desc":"A neoclassical print of the moment Livy ends the year on. It is an imagining, not a record, and it stages the scene indoors among the tents where Livy has the head thrown down in front of the outposts and two freed prisoners sent in to explain it.","alt":"An engraving of a helmeted commander recoiling, arm outstretched, as a soldier beside him holds up a severed head by the hair, with tents and armed men behind.","credit":"Rijksmuseum, RP-P-1929-231, 1818. CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_bij_het_hoofd_van_Hasdrubal_Essendo_stata_gettata_nel_Campo_di_Annibale%2C_la_testa_di_Asdrubale_suo_fratello%2C_disfatto_da_Livio%2C_e_Nerone%2C_riconosce_da_un_tale_successo_l%27inevitabile_sua_rovina_%28titel_op_object%29%2C_RP-P-1929-231.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Nero forbid new tents in the camp?","a":"Because each tribune took a tribune and each horseman a horseman, so the camp should not grow and betray that a second army had arrived."},{"q":"Why did Hasdrubal count the trumpet calls?","a":"Because unfamiliar shields and lean horses had made him suspicious, and hearing it sound twice in the consul’s camp told him two consular armies were present."},{"q":"Why does Polybius praise the losing commander?","a":"Because most commanders plan only for success, where he guarded his life while there was hope and spent it rather than live in shame."}]},
{"id":"rm-229","num":229,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Envoys discussing peace brought disguised officers to walk round the gates, and when the talks broke off <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned on two enemy camps burnt in one night.","questions":["Polybius calls the night attack the most splendid thing in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though it followed months of peace talks that Scipio had kept going as cover.","The Celtiberian mercenaries who stood and died on the Great Plain let Syphax and Hasdrubal escape, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not end there."],"answer":"Scipio's African campaign","answerText":"Scipio's African campaign","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Landed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">204 BCE, near Utica</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Camps burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, in one night</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punic camp</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30,000 foot, 3,000 horse</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Plain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 30,000 opposed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","africa","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Scipio's African campaign</b> carried the war into Carthage's own country from 204 BCE and forced her to recall Hannibal. Wintering outside Utica, he kept sending to the Numidian king Syphax about terms, his envoys having reported that the Numidian huts were built of reeds and matting and the Carthaginian ones of branches with no earth in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One camp held Hasdrubal with about 30,000 foot and 3,000 horse, the other 50,000 foot and 10,000 Numidian horse ten stades away. With the envoys he sent officers dressed as slaves to walk about the approaches and gateways while the talking went on. When Syphax believed peace was settled, Scipio sent again to say that he favoured it but his council did not — done, Polybius explains, so that whatever happened next nobody could accuse him of breaking a truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He gave out that he meant to surprise Utica, fed his legions early, marched at the end of the first watch, and near the third sent Laelius and Masinissa at the Numidian camp while he waited for it to burn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reed huts caught at once, and men who ran out, some woken and some still drinking, were trampled in the gateways, burnt, or cut down by troops posted at the exits, never learning they were under attack. The Carthaginians took the blaze for an accident and came out unarmed to help, whereupon Scipio fell on them and fired their camp too; both generals escaped with a few horsemen, and Polybius calls the night the most splendid thing Scipio ever did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Syphax was talked out of going home by his wife, and a report that four thousand Celtiberian mercenaries were ten thousand and unbeatable put enough heart into Carthage to field 30,000 men on the Great Plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> There both wings broke at once, but the Celtiberians, who could neither flee through a country they did not know nor expect quarter, stood and were cut to pieces, which let Syphax and Hasdrubal get away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.4, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.5, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.6, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.7, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.8, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Ancient_Utica_in_2019_05.jpg/1280px-Ancient_Utica_in_2019_05.jpg","title":"The ruins of Utica in Tunisia","desc":"Scipio besieged this town through the whole campaign and wintered his army beside it, and it was to look as though he were storming it that he moved on the night of the fires. The standing walls are of the Roman city built here afterwards.","alt":"Low ruined walls of dressed stone enclosing a paved courtyard grown through with weeds, with a bare hill on the horizon.","credit":"Kritzolina, 2019. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Utica_in_2019_05.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the peace talks matter more than any terms?","a":"Because officers dressed as slaves walked the approaches and gateways while the talking went on, so the negotiation was how the camps were reconnoitred."},{"q":"Why send word that his council opposed peace?","a":"Because it meant that whatever happened next nobody could accuse him of breaking a truce."},{"q":"Why did the Celtiberians stand when both wings had broken?","a":"Because they could neither flee through a country they did not know nor expect quarter, so they were cut to pieces where they stood — which let Syphax and Hasdrubal escape."}]},
{"id":"rm-230","num":230,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Having fought for Carthage in Spain, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went over to Rome, guided the night attack on the Numidian camp and took Cirta with Syphax in chains.","questions":["Told he could not keep the wife he had just married because she was a Roman prisoner, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent her poison, which she drank calling it a wedding present.","Polybius says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reigned more than sixty years, died at ninety leaving ten sons, and first proved that Numidia could grow crops."],"answer":"Masinissa","answerText":"Masinissa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">With Scipio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 204 BCE, in Africa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 60 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 90, per Polybius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sons</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10, the youngest aged 4</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","person","history","africa","warfare"],"abstract":"<b>Masinissa</b> was a Numidian prince who fought first for Carthage and then for Rome, and ended as king of a united Numidia for more than sixty years. Livy has him harassing the elder Publius Scipio in Spain with his Numidian horse, cutting off stragglers and riding up to the gates of the Roman camp night and day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He changed sides before the African campaign, and Polybius has Scipio question his spies about the approaches to the two enemy camps and then defer to Masinissa, who knew the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He led half the attacking force with Laelius against the camp of Syphax and posted his own men at the ways out to take those who escaped the fire. After the victory on the Great Plain he rode on to Cirta with the captured king, and when the townsmen would not believe he was taken had him shown to them in chains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In the palace he was met at the threshold by Sophonisba, wife of Syphax and daughter of the Carthaginian general Hasdrubal, and he married her. Scipio held that she was a Roman prisoner and could not be his; Livy has Masinissa leave the tent in tears, having already promised her that she would not be handed to anyone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He sent her the poison that kings kept by them against the turns of fortune, with a message that he would gladly have kept his first obligation as a husband and was now performing his second, that she should not come alive into Roman hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> She answered that she accepted the wedding present, and would have died more willingly had she not married so near her death, and drank it without a tremor. Polybius, writing of his death at ninety after a reign of over sixty years, calls it his greatest and most godlike achievement that he showed a country thought naturally barren could bear crops of every kind, and records that he left nine grown sons and a child of four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 25.34, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 14.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/14*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 30.12, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 30.14, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 30.15, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.16, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Coin%2C_Masinissa.png/1280px-Coin%2C_Masinissa.png","title":"A Numidian bronze catalogued as Masinissa's","desc":"The obverse shows a bearded king in a laurel wreath. It is the royal type of the Numidian coinage rather than a portrait taken from life, and only this face of the coin is shown here.","alt":"A worn dark bronze coin filling the frame, showing the left-facing head of a bearded man wearing a laurel wreath.","credit":"British Museum, London. © The Trustees of the British Museum. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin,_Masinissa.png"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Scipio defer to him over the attack on the camps?","a":"Because he knew the ground, so after questioning his own spies about the approaches Scipio took the Numidian’s judgement."},{"q":"Why did the marriage to Sophonisba have to be undone?","a":"Because Scipio held that she was a Roman prisoner and could not be his, and Masinissa had already promised she would not be handed to anyone."},{"q":"Why does Polybius call farming his most godlike achievement?","a":"Because he showed that a country thought naturally barren could bear crops of every kind, which outlasted the reign itself."}]},
{"id":"rm-231","num":231,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Scipio left straight lanes through his lines rather than covering the gaps, so that the elephants could be funnelled harmlessly to the rear, at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 202 BCE.","questions":["Hannibal drew up three lines with his veterans from Italy more than a stade behind the rest, and lost the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when the Roman and Numidian cavalry returned.","Polybius says the mercenaries in Hannibal's front line turned on the Carthaginians behind them during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended the war in Africa in 202 BCE."],"answer":"battle of Zama","answerText":"Battle of Zama","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">202 BCE, at Zama</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elephants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 80</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 1,500</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punic dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000, as many taken</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","africa","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>battle of Zama</b> was fought in 202 BCE inland from Carthage, and it decided the second war between Rome and Carthage. Before it Hannibal asked for a meeting, and the two generals met alone but for an interpreter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He offered Rome Sicily, Sardinia, Spain and the islands lying between Italy and Africa;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scipio answered that Carthage had already accepted harsher terms and then broken the peace, and the talks came to nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Scipio drew his legions up in three lines, but set the principes straight behind the hastati instead of covering the gaps, because of the number of the enemy elephants, so that lanes ran through the whole depth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The light troops filled those lanes with orders to open the fight and fall back down them if driven, while Laelius took the Italian horse on the left and Masinissa all the Numidians on the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Hannibal put more than 80 elephants in front, then about 12,000 mercenaries of Ligurians, Celts, Balearic islanders and Moors, then the Libyans and Carthaginians, and last the army he had brought home from Italy, standing more than a stade behind the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The elephants broke at the trumpets, some wheeling back on the Numidian cavalry and the others running down the lanes without much harm, and both Carthaginian wings were then driven from the field and pursued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The mercenaries had the better of the first clash, but the Carthaginians behind them would not come up, and the mercenaries turned on their own second line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Scipio recalled the hastati by bugle, brought the principes and triarii up on either flank, and advanced over ground made slippery with bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The last struggle was even until Masinissa and Laelius returned from the pursuit and fell on the rear; Polybius counts more than 1,500 Roman dead against 20,000 Carthaginians killed and nearly as many taken,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> and says Hannibal, who escaped to Hadrumetum, had done all that a good general could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.6, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.7, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.8, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.14, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.15, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Schlacht_bei_Zama_Gem%C3%A4lde_H_P_Motte.jpg/1280px-Schlacht_bei_Zama_Gem%C3%A4lde_H_P_Motte.jpg","title":"The battle of Zama, painted by Henri-Paul Motte in 1906","desc":"A modern imagining rather than evidence: no ancient picture of the battle survives, and the painter's elephants charge home where Polybius has most of them turned aside or driven down the lanes.","credit":"Henri-Paul Motte, 1906. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_Zama_Gem%C3%A4lde_H_P_Motte.jpg","alt":"A painting of war elephants and armoured men colliding in a dust cloud on open ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Scipio abandon the usual chequered formation?","a":"Because of the number of the enemy elephants: setting the principes straight behind the hastati left lanes running through the whole depth for the beasts to pass down."},{"q":"Why did the mercenaries turn on their own second line?","a":"Because they had the better of the first clash and the Carthaginians behind them would not come up to support them."},{"q":"Why did the returning cavalry decide it?","a":"Because the last struggle was even until Masinissa and Laelius came back from the pursuit and fell on the rear."}]},
{"id":"rm-232","num":232,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Carthage kept its African cities and its own laws, but gave up all but ten warships and every elephant, under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that ended the war.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Carthage undertook to fight no war outside Africa at all, and none inside it without first asking Rome's permission.","Livy says Hannibal pulled a speaker down from the tribune for opposing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then apologised for not knowing the customs of a city he left aged nine."],"answer":"peace of 201 BCE","answerText":"Peace of 201 BCE","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">201 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 talents over 50 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Warships left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 triremes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100, aged 14 to 30</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","africa","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>peace of 201 BCE</b> ended the second war between Rome and Carthage, on terms Scipio dictated in Africa and the Roman people afterwards ratified. Scipio told the Carthaginian envoys that Rome owed them no leniency since they had begun the war by taking Saguntum against the treaty and had lately broken a written agreement they had sworn to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lenient half of the settlement left Carthage the cities and territory she had held in Africa before the war, with her flocks, herds, slaves and other property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was to be governed by her own laws and customs, take no garrison, and suffer no injury from that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against that she was to hand over every prisoner and deserter, surrender all her warships but ten triremes and all her elephants, and make good the shipping seized during the truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> She was to fight no war at all outside Africa, and none inside it without consulting Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She was to restore to Masinissa the houses, lands and cities that had belonged to him or his ancestors, within boundaries to be assigned later, pay 10,000 talents in yearly instalments spread over 50 years, and give 100 hostages aged between 14 and 30.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the terms were read at Carthage and a member rose to oppose them, Hannibal pulled him down from the tribune, then apologised for not knowing the usages of a city he had left at nine and returned to aged over 45.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy gives the same conditions with the same figures, adds that Carthage was to form a league with Masinissa, and reports that the surrendered warships were towed out to sea and burnt,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> a sight said to have grieved the city as though Carthage herself were in flames.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also has Hannibal laughing in the senate house as the first instalment was raised, telling those who wept over a levy on private property that they should have wept when the arms were taken and the ships burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.18, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 15.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/15*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 30.37, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 30.43, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 30.44, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/L%27%C3%AElot_du_port_militaire.jpg/1280px-L%27%C3%AElot_du_port_militaire.jpg","title":"The circular basin of the military harbour at Carthage","desc":"The basin stands for what the treaty took away rather than showing it: the fleet moored here was reduced to ten triremes, and Livy says the surrendered ships were towed out and burnt.","credit":"Photograph by Cheima fezzani. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27%C3%AElot_du_port_militaire.jpg","alt":"A round lagoon with a low wooded island at its centre, ringed by modern houses."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Carthage left her cities, laws and property?","a":"Because what the settlement took was the power to fight — all warships but ten, all elephants, and any war outside Africa — rather than the country or its government."},{"q":"Why is the boundary clause about Masinissa dangerous?","a":"Because Carthage was to restore what had belonged to him or his ancestors within boundaries to be assigned later, so the limits were left for others to fix."},{"q":"Why did Hannibal laugh at those weeping over the indemnity?","a":"Because they should have wept when the arms were taken and the ships burnt, that being when the city lost what mattered."}]},
{"id":"rm-233","num":233,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy's account of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns on the confiscation of the whole Capuan territory and on twelve of the thirty Latin colonies declaring that they could furnish no more men.","questions":["One mark of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that Rome could not repay its wartime creditors and sold them public land near the city instead, at one as the acre.","In Appian's account of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the undistributed public land was taken up by the rich and worked with slaves, while the free Italian population dwindled."],"answer":"Italy after Hannibal","answerText":"Italy after Hannibal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capua seized</span><span class=\"dt-v\">211 BCE, with its territory</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Colonies short</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 of 30, in 209 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Campanians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">barred 15 miles from the sea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Creditors paid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in land, from 200 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","warfare","italy","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Italy after Hannibal</b> is the countryside the war left behind, marked less by the battles than by the confiscations that followed each revolt and by 15 years of levies. The heaviest single seizure was Capua's: when the city fell in 211 BCE all its land and buildings were made the property of the Roman state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says the city was spared and kept as a settlement of husbandmen, but left with no senate, no assembly and no magistrates, and administered by a prefect sent out yearly from Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Campanians themselves were dispersed by decree, some sold with their families and the rest sent beyond the Tiber or short of the Liris, none of them to hold land within 15 miles of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their cattle and their slaves, except the adult men, were given back, so what the state kept was the soil itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The drain of men told as heavily, and in 209 BCE twelve of Rome's thirty Latin colonies informed the consuls that they had nothing further to give in men or money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy has the allies complain that ten years of levies had drained them, that a countryman enlisted by Rome was more lost to them than one taken prisoner by Carthage, and that the survivors of Cannae were growing old in Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the peace the treasury could not repay the citizens who had lent it money in 210 BCE, and settled instead by letting them buy public land within 50 Roman miles of the city at one <i>as</i> the acre, in acknowledgement that it stayed public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That land was called the Trientius Tabulius because it stood for the third instalment, and could be handed back for the money whenever the state was able to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Appian, writing much later and about the conquest of Italy at large rather than this war, says the undistributed public land was taken up by the rich, who worked great tracts with slaves rather than free labourers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 26.16, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 26.34, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 27.9, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 31.13, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.7, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/CenturiazioneProvPadova.jpg/1280px-CenturiazioneProvPadova.jpg","title":"Roman land division surviving in the field pattern near Padua","desc":"A northern example rather than a Campanian one, and later than this war: it shows what Roman surveyed land looks like on the ground, which is the form confiscated territory took once it was assigned.","credit":"Satellite view by Mistersimon75. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CenturiazioneProvPadova.jpg","alt":"A satellite view of farmland cut by long straight roads and ditches into a regular grid."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome keep Capua standing and take everything else?","a":"Because it was kept as a settlement of husbandmen with no senate, assembly or magistrates, so what the state took was the soil itself and the government."},{"q":"Why did the allies say a soldier was worse lost than a prisoner?","a":"Because a countryman enlisted by Rome never came back to them, and after ten years of levies twelve Latin colonies told the consuls they had nothing further to give."},{"q":"Why was the loan repaid in land rather than money?","a":"Because the treasury could not pay, so lenders bought public land at one as the acre while it stayed public and could be handed back for the money later."}]},
{"id":"rm-234","num":234,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Rome demanded 300 hostages, then every weapon in the city, and only then ordered Carthage abandoned, in the opening of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 149 BCE.","questions":["Polybius says Rome wanted a pretext that would satisfy foreign opinion before opening the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a war that looks just makes victory greater and defeat less dangerous.","Carthage broke the peace of 201 BCE by fighting Masinissa without Roman consent, which gave Rome the occasion for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the siege that followed."],"answer":"Third Punic War","answerText":"Third Punic War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">149 – 146 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300, within 30 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arms given up</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200,000 suits of mail</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ordered inland</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 km from the sea</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","warfare","history","africa","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Third Punic War</b> was fought in Africa between 149 and 146 BCE and ended with Carthage taken and destroyed. Its occasion was Carthage's war with Masinissa: barred by the peace of 201 BCE from fighting in Africa without Rome's consent, the city at last did so and lost an army of 58,000, of whom few came home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carthage condemned the general who had led it and sent embassies to disown the war, but Appian says the senate had already resolved on war and was only looking for a pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius says the same and explains why the pretext mattered: a war whose beginning seems just makes a victory greater and a defeat less dangerous, so Rome would not move without a case that satisfied foreign opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Utica then surrendered itself outright, which took from Carthage the one gesture it had kept in reserve, and its envoys reached Rome to find war already voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They committed the city to the faith of Rome, a formula that gave up territory, towns, people, rivers, harbours, temples and tombs, and left those who used it holding nothing at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The senate answered by granting Carthage its freedom, its laws and all its property, on condition that 300 hostages, sons of its senators, reached Lilybaeum within 30 days and that the consuls were obeyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hostages went with great lamentation,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the consuls then required every weapon in the city: more than 200,000 suits of mail and 2,000 catapults were handed over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Only then did the consul Censorinus give the last order, that the Carthaginians quit their city and settle at least 15 kilometres (10 miles) from the sea, since Rome meant to raze it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Polybius records that Greek opinion divided sharply over this, some calling it far-sighted statesmanship, some a betrayal disclosed by degrees, and some holding that a people who had surrendered unconditionally could not be wronged by the orders that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 74, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-15/. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.4, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.5, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.6, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 81, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-17/. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/City_of_Carthage_circa_149_BC.png/1280px-City_of_Carthage_circa_149_BC.png","title":"Carthage as besieged, in a modern reconstruction of the city about 149 BCE","desc":"A drawing rather than a survey: the walls, the two harbours and the Byrsa are placed from the written sources and from excavation, and the coastline has moved since.","credit":"Map by Harrias. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Carthage_circa_149_BC.png","alt":"A plan of the walled city on its peninsula, with the citadel hill and the two harbours marked."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome need a pretext at all?","a":"Because a war whose beginning seems just makes a victory greater and a defeat less dangerous, so Rome would not move without a case that satisfied foreign opinion."},{"q":"Why did surrendering to Rome’s faith leave Carthage nothing?","a":"Because the formula gave up territory, towns, people, rivers, harbours, temples and tombs, so those who used it held nothing to bargain with afterwards."},{"q":"Why was the order to move inland given last?","a":"Because the hostages and then every weapon in the city had already been surrendered, so it came when Carthage could no longer refuse."}]},
{"id":"rm-235","num":235,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Scipio fired three streets of six-storey houses at once on reaching the citadel, and six days of fighting inside the walls ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 146 BCE.","questions":["Appian says 50,000 people came out of the Byrsa under guard during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while about 900 Roman deserters burnt the temple of Aesculapius over themselves.","Scipio is said to have wept at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and quoted Homer's lines on the fall of Troy, telling Polybius that he was thinking of Rome."],"answer":"destruction of Carthage","answerText":"Destruction of Carthage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE, in spring</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Street fighting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 days and nights</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Came out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50,000, under guard</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deserters</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 900, burnt</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","warfare","history","africa","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>destruction of Carthage</b> in 146 BCE ended the Third Punic War, after a siege of about three years and six days of fighting inside the city itself. Scipio Aemilianus pushed towards the Byrsa up three streets of six-storey houses, taking them one at a time and crossing from roof to roof on timbers thrown over the alleys, while a second battle went on below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On reaching the citadel he fired all three streets together, and had the passages kept clear so the army could move.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian says the parties clearing the rubble tipped the dead and the living together into trenches with axes and forks, and that horses went over them in the rush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The soldiers were changed in shifts so that they should not be worn out, Scipio alone taking no rest, until on the seventh day suppliants came out under the garlands of the temple of Aesculapius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He spared all who wished to leave the Byrsa except the deserters, and 50,000 men and women came out through a single gate under guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 900 Roman deserters held the temple with Hasdrubal, who slipped away and gave himself up; his wife reproached him from the roof, killed her children, threw them into the fire and went in after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Scipio is said to have wept at a city 700 years old brought to nothing and to have quoted Homer on the day when sacred Troy would perish; Polybius, who was beside him, asked what he meant and wrote down the answer, that he feared for Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The plunder went to the soldiers, with the gold, silver and temple offerings held back, and the Sicilian cities were invited to reclaim whatever they could identify as taken from them in earlier wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A senatorial commission then ordered whatever remained obliterated and forbade anyone to live on the site, though the decree Appian records does not forbid going on the ground and says nothing of sowing it with salt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 128, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-26/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 129, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-26/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 130, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-26/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 131, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-27/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 132, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-27/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 133, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-27/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Punic Wars</i> 135, trans. Horace White (New York: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-punic-wars/appian-the-punic-wars-27/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Carthage_Byrsa_2.jpg/1280px-Carthage_Byrsa_2.jpg","title":"The excavated Punic quarter on the slope of the Byrsa","desc":"Housing of the last decades of the city, cut away and buried when the Romans levelled the hill for their own forum, which is why this much of it survives.","credit":"Photograph by Noomen9. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carthage_Byrsa_2.jpg","alt":"Low stone walls of narrow houses and streets excavated on a hillside above the sea."},"why":[{"q":"Why was fighting through the streets so slow?","a":"Because the houses were six storeys high and had to be taken one at a time, the soldiers crossing from roof to roof on timbers thrown over the alleys while a second battle went on below."},{"q":"Why did Scipio change his men in shifts?","a":"Because six days of fighting inside the city would otherwise have worn them out, and he alone took no rest."},{"q":"Why is the story about sowing the site with salt not in the record?","a":"Because the decree Appian records forbids living on the site and does not forbid going on the ground, and says nothing of salt."}]},
{"id":"rm-236","num":236,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius dates the moment when Greek, Italian and African affairs were first brought into contact to a conference of 217 BCE, opening the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his history.","questions":["Agelaus warned the Greeks not to wait for the clouds looming in the west to settle on them, which is where Polybius begins his account of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its consequences.","Polybius claims that in under 53 years Rome brought nearly the whole inhabited world under one government, which is the scale of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as he tells it."],"answer":"Rome and the Hellenistic world","answerText":"Rome and the Hellenistic world","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Polybius starts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">220 BCE, the 140th Olympiad</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naupactus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">217 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rome's rise</span><span class=\"dt-v\">53 years, per Polybius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","rome","greece"],"abstract":"<b>Rome and the Hellenistic world</b> is the meeting of the western republic with the kingdoms that Alexander's successors had left across the eastern Mediterranean. Polybius begins his account at the 140th Olympiad, 220 to 216 BCE, with three wars running at once: the Social War in Greece, the war for Coele-Syria between Antiochus and Ptolemy in Asia, and the war between Rome and Carthage in Italy and Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before that date, he says, the doings of the world had been dispersed, held together by no unity of initiative, results or locality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fixes the change at the conference of Naupactus in 217 BCE, where the affairs of Greece, Italy and Africa were first brought into contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There Agelaus of Naupactus told the Greeks that whichever side won in the west would not be content with Italy and Sicily, and begged Philip not to wait for the clouds looming in the west to settle on Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius says the speech carried the conference, and that Philip and the leading Greek statesmen thereafter made war and peace with their eyes on Italy rather than on Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islanders and the cities of Asia Minor soon did the same, sending embassies west to Rome and Carthage instead of south and east to Antiochus and Ptolemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome came the other way after Hannibal: Polybius says the defeat of Carthage was the step that emboldened them to reach for the rest and cross with an army into Greece and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale of what followed is his whole subject, since in less than 53 years, he says, the Romans brought nearly the whole inhabited world under one government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He sets that beside the empires before it, noting that Persia risked its own existence whenever it left Asia, that Sparta held the hegemony of Greece for scarcely twelve years, and that Macedon never attempted Sicily, Sardinia or Libya at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.105, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.104, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 1.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/1*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Diadochi_kingdoms_301_200.jpg/1280px-Diadochi_kingdoms_301_200.jpg","title":"The successor kingdoms in 301 and in 200 BCE, from Shepherd's atlas of 1911","desc":"A century of the eastern Mediterranean in two frames, showing the world Rome entered; the borders are an atlas-maker's simplification of holdings that shifted constantly.","credit":"William R. Shepherd, Historical Atlas, 1911. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diadochi_kingdoms_301_200.jpg","alt":"Two long map panels of the eastern Mediterranean with the successor kingdoms in different colours."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Polybius begin his account at that particular Olympiad?","a":"Because before it the doings of the world had been dispersed, held together by no unity of initiative, results or locality."},{"q":"Why was a conference in Greece the turning point?","a":"Because at Naupactus Agelaus warned that whichever side won in the west would not be content with Italy and Sicily, and afterwards Greek statesmen made war and peace with their eyes on Italy."},{"q":"Why does he compare Rome’s reach with earlier empires?","a":"Because Persia risked its existence whenever it left Asia, Sparta held Greece scarcely twelve years, and Macedon never attempted Sicily, Sardinia or Libya at all."}]},
{"id":"rm-237","num":237,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Teuta had a Roman envoy killed for telling her that Rome punished private wrongs publicly, which brought on the first of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 229 BCE.","questions":["Polybius treats the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the first time a Roman army crossed the Adriatic, and dates Rome's first embassies to the Greek leagues from them.","Demetrius of Pharos sailed past Lissus with 50 boats against the terms of the treaty, which brought on the second of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 219 BCE."],"answer":"Illyrian Wars","answerText":"Illyrian Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">229 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">219 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000 foot, 200 ships</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty limit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 unarmed ships past Lissus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Illyrian Wars</b> were two short Roman campaigns across the Adriatic, in 229 and in 219 BCE, and Polybius treats the first as the occasion on which the Romans first crossed with an army into Illyria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agron of Illyria had beaten the Aetolians at Medion and died soon after of a pleurisy caught in the celebrations, leaving the kingdom to his wife Teuta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She gave letters of marque to privateers to plunder any ship they met, and Illyrian ships had long preyed on vessels sailing out of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Italian traders were robbed and killed, Rome sent Gaius and Lucius Coruncanius to look into it, and Teuta answered that Rome would suffer no public wrong from Illyria, but that Illyrian kings did not stop their subjects taking booty at sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The younger envoy told her that Romans punished private wrongs publicly, and she had him killed as he sailed for home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two consuls crossed in 229 BCE with 200 ships and about 20,000 foot and 2,000 horse, lifted the sieges of Corcyra, Epidamnus and Issa, and took them and Apollonia into Roman protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Demetrius of Pharos, who had given Corcyra up to them, was left ruling much of Illyria, and Teuta made a treaty agreeing to pay tribute and not to sail beyond Lissus with more than two unarmed vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome then sent legates to the Aetolian and Achaean leagues to explain the war and read them the treaty, and afterwards to Athens and Corinth, where the Corinthians first admitted them to the Isthmian games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second war came in 219 BCE, when Demetrius sacked Illyrian cities under Roman protection and sailed past Lissus with 50 boats, reckoning Rome too busy with the Gauls and Carthage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Lucius Aemilius took Dimale in seven days, then drew the garrison out of Pharos by sailing openly into the harbour with 20 ships while the rest of his army lay hidden ashore, razed it, and Demetrius escaped to the court of Philip V.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.4, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.8, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 2.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/2*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.16, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 3.18–19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/3*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/2024-02-21_A_view_of_Risan_from_Gradina_hill_1.jpg/1280px-2024-02-21_A_view_of_Risan_from_Gradina_hill_1.jpg","title":"Risan and its bay, seen from the Gradina hill above the town","desc":"Risan stands on the site of Rhizon, the fortified place inland from the sea to which Polybius says Teuta escaped with a few followers, and from which she sent her envoys to make terms.","credit":"Photograph by Alexkom000. CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2024-02-21_A_view_of_Risan_from_Gradina_hill_1.jpg","alt":"A small coastal town on a deep bay enclosed by steep grey mountains."},"why":[{"q":"Why did an embassy about piracy end in a war?","a":"Because Teuta answered that Illyrian kings did not stop their subjects taking booty at sea, the younger envoy replied that Romans punish private wrongs publicly, and she had him killed as he sailed home."},{"q":"Why did Rome send legates round Greece after winning?","a":"Because it explained the war and read the treaty to the Aetolian and Achaean leagues and then at Athens and Corinth, where the Corinthians first admitted Romans to the Isthmian games."},{"q":"Why did Demetrius think he could act with impunity in 219?","a":"Because he reckoned Rome too busy with the Gauls and Carthage to answer, so he sacked cities under Roman protection and sailed past Lissus with 50 boats."}]},
{"id":"rm-238","num":238,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Philip's envoys were caught at sea carrying Hannibal's letter, which is how Rome learned of the alliance that began the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 215 BCE.","questions":["Rome fought most of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> through the Aetolians, who were promised the walls and lands of captured cities while the Romans kept the movable booty.","The Aetolians made a separate peace with Philip and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended at Phoenice in 205 BCE, leaving Rome free to finish the war in Africa."],"answer":"First Macedonian War","answerText":"First Macedonian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">214 – 205 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Punic treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">215 BCE, with Hannibal</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aetolian pact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">211 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">205 BCE, at Phoenice</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>First Macedonian War</b> was fought between Rome and Philip V of Macedon from 214 to 205 BCE, in the shadow of the war against Hannibal and largely through allies on both sides. Livy says Philip watched the Italian war closely, and after Hannibal's third victory sent Xenophanes of Athens to make terms with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stopped by Roman troops in Apulia, Xenophanes told the praetor that he had come to seek Rome's friendship, and was given guides through the Roman lines to Hannibal's camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius gives the treaty they swore, by which Carthage and Macedon bound themselves as friends and brothers, each to be the enemy of the other's enemies and to make no peace that did not take in the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its terms required that the Romans cease to be masters of Corcyra, Apollonia, Epidamnus, Pharos, Dimale, the Parthini and Atintania, which is every gain of the Illyrian wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The second embassy, carrying the ratification, was intercepted at sea, and Hannibal's letter and the agreement itself were found on the envoys and sent to Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rome answered on ground it did not have to garrison, and in 211 BCE Marcus Valerius Laevinus made an alliance with the Aetolian league, offering it the walls and lands of captured cities while Rome kept the movable booty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Attalus of Pergamum and the Illyrian and Thracian kings Pleuratus and Scerdilaedas were named in it, and the Aetolians were to attack Philip by land with Roman help by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy says Greek affairs were then neglected for two years, so that the Aetolians, deserted by the Romans on whom alone they depended, made peace with Philip on whatever terms he pleased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rome came to terms at Phoenice in 205 BCE, keeping the Parthini and three towns while Atintania was to pass to Philip if the senate agreed, since with the war moved into Africa every tribe wanted to be rid of the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.33, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 7.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/7*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 23.34, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 26.24, trans. D. Spillan and Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1849), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10907/pg10907.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 29.12, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Aitolian_League_-_279-168_BC_-_silver_drachma_-_head_of_Artemis_-_Aitolos_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg/1280px-Aitolian_League_-_279-168_BC_-_silver_drachma_-_head_of_Artemis_-_Aitolos_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","title":"A silver drachma of the Aetolian League, the issue dated to about 220 to 205 BCE","desc":"The coinage of the league Rome fought the war through, with the head of Artemis on one face and the hero Aitolos on the other; the museum dates this issue to the years of the war.","credit":"Staatliche Münzsammlung München. Photograph by ArchaiOptix. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aitolian_League_-_279-168_BC_-_silver_drachma_-_head_of_Artemis_-_Aitolos_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin, a head in profile and a seated male figure."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the treaty with Hannibal name Illyrian towns?","a":"Because its terms required that the Romans cease to be masters of Corcyra, Apollonia, Epidamnus, Pharos, Dimale, the Parthini and Atintania — every gain of the Illyrian wars."},{"q":"Why did Rome answer with an alliance rather than an army?","a":"Because it could fight on ground it did not have to garrison: the Aetolians attacked Philip by land with Roman help by sea, taking captured cities’ walls and lands while Rome kept the movable booty."},{"q":"Why did the Aetolians make peace on Philip’s terms?","a":"Because Greek affairs were neglected for two years and they were deserted by the Romans on whom alone they depended."}]},
{"id":"rm-239","num":239,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Polybius calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the darling of Greece for his early conduct, and then traces the change that made him, in his own words, a savage tyrant.","questions":["At Thermon the army of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burnt the colonnades and threw down some 2,000 statues, sparing those of gods and those inscribed as dedications to them.","Aratus warned <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that seizing the citadel of Messene would cost him every other citadel, and Polybius dates his first great crime to that affair."],"answer":"Philip V","answerText":"Philip V","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 – 179 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thermon sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">218 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statues down</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,000, at Thermon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">179 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","person","history","greece","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Philip V</b> was king of Macedon from 221 to 179 BCE, and Rome's opponent in its first two Macedonian wars. He came to the throne as a boy, and Polybius doubts that a boy of seventeen could have decided the grave matters attributed to him, telling readers to suspect his associates instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius rates his natural gifts very high: a quick intelligence, a retentive memory, great charm, the presence and authority that becomes a king, and above all ability and courage as a general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also asks what defeated all those advantages and turned so well endowed a prince into a savage tyrant, saying the question cannot be briefly answered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His answer begins at Thermon in 218 BCE, where the Macedonians burnt the colonnades of the Aetolian sanctuary and threw down some 2,000 statues, sparing those of gods and those inscribed as dedications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius says this went past the laws of war, and sets it against Antigonus restoring Sparta its constitution and liberty, and against Philip II winning Athens by clemency after Chaeronea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He calls the opening of hostilities with Rome the beginning of the revolution in his character, and says that until then Philip had been the darling of Greece, chosen president of a united Crete with no appeal to arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The turn he dates to Messene, where Demetrius of Pharos urged him to hold Ithome and the Acrocorinth as the two horns by which the Peloponnese is held down, while Aratus said that seizing the citadel would cost him every other one and the good faith guarding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Philip took Aratus' advice that day, but Polybius says the matter was settled later by a difference of one day, Demetrius arriving and Aratus too late, and that Philip then committed the first of his great crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In his last years Polybius has him deporting the political classes of his cities inland, imprisoning the children of Macedonians he had put to death, and watching his sons plot against each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 4.24, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/4*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 4.77, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1922), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/4*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 7.11, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/7*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 7.12–13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/7*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 23.10, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/23*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Macedonia_-_king_Philippos_V_-_220_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Philippos_V_-_Athena_Alkidemos_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS_01.jpg/1280px-Macedonia_-_king_Philippos_V_-_220_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Philippos_V_-_Athena_Alkidemos_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS_01.jpg","title":"A silver tetradrachm of Philip V, the issue dated to about 220 BCE","desc":"Struck early in the reign, about the years Polybius is describing when he calls the king the darling of Greece; Athena Alkidemos strides on the reverse.","credit":"Staatliche Münzsammlung München. Photograph by ArchaiOptix. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_-_king_Philippos_V_-_220_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Philippos_V_-_Athena_Alkidemos_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS_01.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin, a diademed head in profile and a striding armed goddess."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Polybius tell readers to suspect the king’s associates?","a":"Because he doubts a boy of seventeen could have decided the grave matters attributed to him at his accession."},{"q":"Why is Thermon the turning point in his account?","a":"Because burning the colonnades and throwing down 2,000 statues went past the laws of war, against the example of Antigonus restoring Sparta and Philip II winning Athens by clemency."},{"q":"Why does one day decide the story of Messene?","a":"Because Philip took Aratus’ advice against seizing the citadel that day, and the matter was settled later when Demetrius arrived and Aratus came too late."}]},
{"id":"rm-240","num":240,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"The Roman people voted down the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the first assembly, and carried it only after Sulpicius asked whether they would fight in Macedonia or in Italy.","questions":["Marcus Aemilius put Rome's terms to Philip outside Abydos at the opening of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and asked what the Athenians and the Abydenes had done to him first.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with Philip giving up his garrisons in Greece, all but six warships and 1,000 talents, which the Aetolians called an arrangement of words."],"answer":"Second Macedonian War","answerText":"Second Macedonian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 – 197 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abydos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Warships left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 to Philip</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Second Macedonian War</b> was fought between Rome and Philip V of Macedon from 200 to 197 BCE and ended his power in Greece. The consul Publius Sulpicius put it to the people on the ground of injuries done to Rome's allies, and at the first assembly almost every tribe voted it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy says the people were worn out by the late war, and that a tribune had charged the senate with multiplying wars one after another so that they should never have peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sulpicius called them back to the Field of Mars and told them the question was not peace or war, since Philip had begun already, but whether they carried the legions to Macedonia or admitted the enemy into Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He asked them to remember Saguntum, and at the second vote the centuries declared for the war, after which three days of supplication were proclaimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A Roman embassy was in the east, and its youngest member, Marcus Aemilius, found Philip besieging Abydos and put the senate's terms to him: to make war on no other Greeks, to touch nothing of Ptolemy's, and to submit the damage done to Attalus and the Rhodians to a tribunal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Philip argued that the Rhodians had been the aggressors, Aemilius asked what the Athenians, the Cianians and the Abydenes had done to him first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius has the king answer that he forgave the young man his haughtiness because he was young, because he was the handsomest man of his time, and chiefly because he was a Roman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Philip took the town to find the Abydenes destroying themselves and their families, and granted a respite of three days to those who wished to die.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The war ended with a decree declaring the Greeks of Asia and Europe free, Philip surrendering his garrisoned cities, his prisoners, all his warships but six and 1,000 talents,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> terms the Aetolians alone called an arrangement of words and not of things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 31.6, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 31.7, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 31.8, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 16.34, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/16*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.44, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.45, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Second_Roman%E2%80%93Macedonian_War-en.svg/1280px-Second_Roman%E2%80%93Macedonian_War-en.svg.png","title":"The theatre of the war, in a modern map","desc":"A modern reconstruction of the campaigns rather than an ancient document, and the borders shown are the map-maker's reading of a situation the sources describe only in passing.","credit":"Map by Pasztilla aka Attila Terbócs and others. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Second_Roman%E2%80%93Macedonian_War-en.svg","alt":"A map of Greece, Macedonia and the Aegean with campaign routes marked by arrows."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the people vote the war down at first?","a":"Because they were worn out by the late war, and a tribune had charged the senate with multiplying wars one after another so that they should never have peace."},{"q":"Why did the second vote go the other way?","a":"Because the consul put it that the question was not peace or war, Philip having begun already, but whether they carried the legions to Macedonia or admitted the enemy into Italy."},{"q":"Why did the Aetolians call the settlement words rather than things?","a":"Because the decree declared the Greeks free while Philip merely gave up his garrisoned cities, his prisoners, all but six warships and 1,000 talents."}]},
{"id":"rm-241","num":241,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A tribune took 20 maniples off the winning Roman right and struck the phalanx in the rear, which decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 197 BCE.","questions":["Two armies groping through mist on either side of a ridge blundered into one another, and Philip fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on ground he had judged unsuitable.","Polybius uses the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to argue that a phalanx has only one time and one place in which it can do its work, and cannot turn about."],"answer":"Battle of Cynoscephalae","answerText":"Battle of Cynoscephalae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">197 BCE, in Thessaly</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 700</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Macedonian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 8,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prisoners</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,000 or more</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","greece","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Cynoscephalae</b> was fought in Thessaly in 197 BCE and decided the Second Macedonian War in a single day. Polybius says the armies had marched two days on opposite sides of a range of hills, each ignorant of where the other was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A night of thunder and rain left a mist so thick a man could not see those close at hand,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Flamininus pushed out ten squadrons of horse and 1,000 light infantry to feel for the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They met Philip's covering force by accident, and the fight grew as each side fed in reinforcements, the Aetolian cavalry checking the Macedonians where the Roman line had begun to give way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Philip had not expected a general engagement and had sent men out foraging, and he disliked ground Polybius calls rough and broken and known as the Dog's Heads, but messengers shouting that the enemy were flying provoked him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He led out his right wing, doubled the depth of the phalanx, ordered the spears lowered and charged downhill, while Flamininus received his light troops back through the gaps between the maniples and met him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Macedonian right prevailed by weight and slope, but the left was still crossing the ridge in marching order, and Polybius says it broke before the elephants alone without coming to close quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A tribune Polybius does not name then took not more than 20 maniples off the victorious Roman right, wheeled behind the phalanx and fell on it from the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A phalanx cannot turn about or fight man to man, so the Macedonians threw away their shields; some raised their spears in the customary sign of surrender, which Flamininus understood and meant to honour, until men who had pushed ahead cut them down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 700 Romans fell against some 8,000 Macedonians killed and not fewer than 5,000 taken,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and Polybius uses the day to argue that the phalanx has only one time and one place in which it can do its work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.20, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.21, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.22, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.24, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.25, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.26, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.31, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Cynoscephalae.png/960px-Cynoscephalae.png","title":"The battle in three phases, in a modern diagram","desc":"A modern reconstruction: Polybius gives the sequence and the ground but no plan, so the positions and the frontages here are the draughtsman's reading of his account.","credit":"Diagram by Javierfv1212. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cynoscephalae.png","alt":"Three stacked battle diagrams showing blocks of troops on a ridge and their movements."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a general engagement begin by accident?","a":"Because a night of thunder and rain left a mist so thick a man could not see those close at hand, and the covering forces of the two armies met while feeling for each other."},{"q":"Why did Philip fight on ground he disliked?","a":"Because messengers came shouting that the enemy were flying, which provoked him though he had not expected a battle and had sent men out foraging."},{"q":"Why does Polybius use the day to argue about the phalanx?","a":"Because it broke when 20 maniples wheeled behind it, a phalanx being unable to turn about or fight man to man, so it has only one time and place in which it can work."}]},
{"id":"rm-242","num":242,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Plutarch says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> skipped the tribunate, praetorship and aedileship and was elected consul before he was thirty, over two tribunes' objections.","questions":["Philip would not come ashore to meet <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Nicaea, saying that if he died there was nobody left to take the Macedonian throne.","Sent to Prusias of Bithynia on other business, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> found Hannibal at the court and would not relent, which Plutarch says made him odious."],"answer":"Titus Quinctius Flamininus","answerText":"Titus Quinctius Flamininus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">198 BCE, under 30</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nicaea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">198 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cynoscephalae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">197 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","politics","rome","greece"],"abstract":"<b>Titus Quinctius Flamininus</b> was the Roman commander who defeated Philip V of Macedon and then presided over the settlement of Greece. Plutarch says he passed over the offices young men usually sought, the tribunate, praetorship and aedileship, and stood straight for the consulship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two tribunes objected that it was monstrous for a young man to force his way into the highest office before being initiated into the first rites of government, but the senate referred the matter to the people, who elected him before he was thirty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch adds that the command suited him, since Philip's phalanx depended on the Greek states for supplies and refuge, so the war wanted a man who could win them over rather than one relying on force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius rates his judgement as high as any Roman's and says that in Greece chance contributed little, nearly everything being due to his own prudent management.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Nicaea in 198 BCE he met Philip on the shore, and the king would not come off his ship, saying he feared no one but the gods but distrusted most of those present, and that if he died there was nobody to take his throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Asked what would keep the peace, Flamininus said his answer was simple and obvious: that Philip withdraw from the whole of Greece and give up his prisoners and deserters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Philip replied by asking what that Greece was and how it was defined, observing that most of the Aetolians who were demanding his withdrawal were not Greeks themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The talks failed, and the war was settled the next year at Cynoscephalae, where a detachment taken off his winning right wing struck the phalanx in the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch says his later years told against him, that once out of office he could not restrain his passion for glory, and that being sent to Prusias of Bithynia on other business he found Hannibal at the court and would not relent, which made him odious to most people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Flamininus</i> 2, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Flamininus*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.5, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.26, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Flamininus</i> 20, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Flamininus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Macedonia_-_T_Quinctius_Flamininus_-_196_BC_-_gold_stater_-_head_of_T_Quinctius_Flamininus_-_Victoria_-_London_BM_1954-1009-1.jpg/1280px-Macedonia_-_T_Quinctius_Flamininus_-_196_BC_-_gold_stater_-_head_of_T_Quinctius_Flamininus_-_Victoria_-_London_BM_1954-1009-1.jpg","title":"A gold stater struck for Flamininus about 196 BCE, with his own head on it","desc":"Struck in Greece with a Latin legend and a Victory on the reverse; a living Roman's portrait on a coin was a Hellenistic royal habit rather than a Roman one at this date.","credit":"British Museum, 1954-1009-1. Photograph by ArchaiOptix. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_-_T_Quinctius_Flamininus_-_196_BC_-_gold_stater_-_head_of_T_Quinctius_Flamininus_-_Victoria_-_London_BM_1954-1009-1.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a gold coin, a bare head in profile and a standing winged Victory."},"why":[{"q":"Why was he thought the right man for this war?","a":"Because Philip’s phalanx depended on the Greek states for supplies and refuge, so the war wanted a man who could win them over rather than one relying on force."},{"q":"Why would Philip not come off his ship at Nicaea?","a":"Because he said he feared no one but the gods, distrusted most of those present, and that if he died there was nobody to take his throne."},{"q":"Why did Philip ask what Greece was?","a":"Because he was told to withdraw from the whole of it, and observed that most of the Aetolians demanding his withdrawal were not Greeks themselves."}]},
{"id":"rm-243","num":243,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"A herald at the Isthmian games of 196 BCE declared eight peoples free of garrison and tribute, which is the proclamation of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by Rome.","questions":["The Aetolians said the decree announcing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was an arrangement of words and not of things, since Rome kept the cities Philip called the fetters of Greece.","Polybius says the shout that greeted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drowned the herald's words, and Plutarch says ravens flying over the stadium fell out of the air."],"answer":"freedom of the Greeks","answerText":"Freedom of the Greeks","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">196 BCE, at the Isthmus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peoples named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8, in the decree</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The fetters</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commissioners</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10, sent from Rome</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>freedom of the Greeks</b> was the settlement Rome proclaimed at the Isthmian games of 196 BCE, after the defeat of Philip V of Macedon. The senate's decree declared all the Greeks of Asia and Europe free and subject to their own laws, and required Philip to surrender his garrisoned cities to the Romans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Aetolians alone objected, saying that the decree contained an arrangement of words and not an arrangement of things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They read it closely: the towns Philip was to set free were named and lay in Asia, while those he was to hand over to Rome were the European ones, Oreum, Eretria, Chalcis, Demetrias and Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says the ten commissioners did advise keeping Corinth, Chalcis and Demetrias garrisoned against Antiochus, that the Aetolians called these the fetters of Greece, Philip's own name for them, and asked whether the new shackle was smoother but heavier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Flamininus was troubled by this and worked on the commission until it agreed to leave those cities ungarrisoned too, so that the gift should be whole; his acts then matched his words, officers going to free Bargylia in Asia and the cities Philip held in Thrace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the games a herald proclaimed that the senate and Titus Quinctius, having conquered Philip, left free, ungarrisoned, untaxed and under their own laws the Corinthians, Phocians, Locrians, Euboeans, Phthiotic Achaeans, Magnesians, Thessalians and Perrhaebians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius says the shout drowned the words, that many asked to hear them again, and that when the herald repeated them in mid-stadium nobody took any further interest in the athletes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch adds that ravens flying overhead fell into the stadium, and explains it by the air being torn apart by the volume of sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius calls it a wonderful thing that Rome should meet every expense and danger for the freedom of Greece, and Plutarch has the Greeks saying that night that valour and wisdom are rare among men but the rarest blessing of all is the just man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.44, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.45, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Flamininus</i> 10, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Flamininus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Flamininus</i> 12, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Flamininus*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.46, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Flamininus</i> 11, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Flamininus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Titus_Quincticus_Flaminius_Granting_Liberty_to_Greece_at_the_Isthmian_Games_LACMA_M.86.65.jpg/1280px-Titus_Quincticus_Flaminius_Granting_Liberty_to_Greece_at_the_Isthmian_Games_LACMA_M.86.65.jpg","title":"The proclamation at the Isthmus, painted by Jean-Pierre Saint-Ours in 1780","desc":"An 18th-century imagining, and one that puts Flamininus himself before the crowd: both ancient accounts have a herald read the decree while the general keeps out of the way.","credit":"Los Angeles County Museum of Art, M.86.65. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titus_Quincticus_Flaminius_Granting_Liberty_to_Greece_at_the_Isthmian_Games_LACMA_M.86.65.jpg","alt":"A wide painted scene of a robed man addressing a crowd of Greeks in a stadium."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Aetolians call the decree words rather than things?","a":"Because the towns Philip was to set free lay in Asia, while the European ones — Oreum, Eretria, Chalcis, Demetrias and Corinth — he was to hand over to Rome."},{"q":"Why did Flamininus press the commission to give up the three garrisons?","a":"Because the Aetolians called them the fetters of Greece and asked whether the new shackle was smoother but heavier, so the gift had to be whole for his words to hold."},{"q":"Why did the herald have to repeat the proclamation?","a":"Because the shout drowned the words and many asked to hear them again, after which nobody took any further interest in the athletes."}]},
{"id":"rm-244","num":244,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Roman commissioners at Lysimacheia told <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it was ridiculous to come in when all was over and carry off the prizes of their war with Philip.","questions":["Euthydemus of Bactria warned <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that if the two of them fought, the nomads beyond would come in and the country would relapse into barbarism.","Ptolemy IV beat <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Raphia in 217 BCE, killing nearly 10,000 of his infantry and taking 4,000 prisoners in a battle for Coele-Syria."],"answer":"Antiochus III","answerText":"Antiochus III","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">223 – 187 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raphia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">217 BCE, a defeat</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elephants</span><span class=\"dt-v\">150, after India</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lysimacheia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">196 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","person","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"<b>Antiochus III</b>, called the Great, ruled the Seleucid empire from 223 to 187 BCE and was the first eastern king Rome fought. He was the younger son of Seleucus Callinicus and came to the throne when his elder brother was assassinated crossing the Taurus, giving Asia west of that range to Achaeus and the upper provinces to Molon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius introduces him through the revolts that opened the reign and the governor of Coele-Syria who offered to hand its cities over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His war for Coele-Syria ended at Raphia in 217 BCE, where Ptolemy IV beat him: he lost nearly 10,000 foot and more than 300 horse with 4,000 taken, and Ptolemy recovered Raphia and the other towns unopposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then campaigned for years in the east, and Polybius records his settlement with Euthydemus of Bactria, who argued that if the two of them fought, the nomads approaching would come in and the country would relapse into barbarism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Antiochus accepted, judged Euthydemus' son Demetrius worthy of royal rank on sight, promised him a daughter, allowed his father the title of king, and took his elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He crossed into India, renewed an alliance with Sophagasenus, collected elephants until he had 150, and came home through Arachosia and Carmania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At Lysimacheia in 196 BCE Roman commissioners asked him to leave the cities that had been Ptolemy's and Philip's, since it was ridiculous that he should come in when all was over and carry off the prizes of their war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He answered that they were the last people entitled to dispute his possession of Asian towns, that he did not concern himself with the affairs of Italy, and that he had crossed to Europe to recover the Chersonese and Thrace, won by Seleucus by right of conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He added that the free cities of Asia ought not to receive their liberty by Roman order but by his own act of grace, and that he would settle matters with Ptolemy himself by a marriage alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.40, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 5.86, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/5*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 11.34, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1925), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/11*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.50, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 18.51, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/18*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Syria_-_king_Antiochos_III_-_223-187_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Antiochos_III_-_Apollon_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg/1280px-Syria_-_king_Antiochos_III_-_223-187_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Antiochos_III_-_Apollon_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","title":"A silver tetradrachm of Antiochus III, struck between 223 and 187 BCE","desc":"The king diademed on one face and Apollo seated on the omphalos on the other, the standard Seleucid reverse; the portrait is idealised and no securely identified likeness of him survives.","credit":"Staatliche Münzsammlung München. Photograph by ArchaiOptix. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syria_-_king_Antiochos_III_-_223-187_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Antiochos_III_-_Apollon_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin, a diademed head in profile and a seated god holding a bow."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he settle with Euthydemus rather than beat him?","a":"Because Euthydemus argued that if the two of them fought, the nomads approaching would come in and the country would relapse into barbarism."},{"q":"Why did he deny Rome any standing in Asia?","a":"Because he said they were the last people entitled to dispute his possession of Asian towns, that he did not concern himself with Italy, and that Thrace had been won by Seleucus by right of conquest."},{"q":"Why did he insist the cities’ freedom be his own gift?","a":"Because he held they ought not to receive their liberty by Roman order but by his act of grace, which is a claim about who is their sovereign."}]},
{"id":"rm-245","num":245,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy says Antiochus opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by landing near Demetrias with 10,000 foot and six elephants, scarcely enough to take Greece and far less to fight Rome.","questions":["Hannibal urged at a council of war in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Philip of Macedon be brought in, so that two kings with Asia and Europe would face a single state.","Scipio closed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by saying victory had never made the Romans more exacting nor defeat less so, repeating the terms offered before the battle."],"answer":"Roman-Seleucid War","answerText":"Roman-Seleucid War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">192 – 188 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Landed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 foot, 6 elephants</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15,000 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20, demanded</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman-Seleucid War</b> was fought between Rome and Antiochus III from 192 to 188 BCE, in Greece first and then in Asia Minor. The Aetolians, disappointed by the settlement Rome had made in Greece, invited the king over and passed a decree calling him in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hannibal was at his court, and a plan to send him with a fleet to Africa was talked out of existence by the Aetolian Thoas, who argued that his fame was too splendid for an officer under a king, and that any success would be credited to Hannibal rather than Antiochus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king sacrificed to Athena at Ilium and crossed with 40 decked ships, 60 open ones and 200 transports, landing near Demetrias with 10,000 foot, 500 horse and six elephants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls that a force scarcely sufficient to take possession of Greece alone, much less to sustain a war with Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At a council of war Hannibal urged that Philip of Macedon be brought in by any means, since the Aetolians who had beaten Philip would then be fighting beside him, and two kings with the strength of Asia and Europe would face a single state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the decisive battle Antiochus offered to give up Lampsacus, Smyrna and Alexandria and to pay half the cost of the war, asking the Romans to remember that they were men, not to test fortune too far, and to set some limit on their empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Roman council answered that he should pay the whole cost, since the war was his doing, and should retire not only from those cities but from every part of Asia on this side of the Taurus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the battle Scipio told the king's envoys that victory had never made the Romans more exacting nor defeat less so, and gave them the same answer they had received before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> To that were added 15,000 talents payable over twelve years, the debts owed to Eumenes, twenty hostages, and the surrender of Hannibal himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 35.43, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 35.42, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 36.7, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 21.14, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/21*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 21.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/21*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Roman-Syrian-War.png/1280px-Roman-Syrian-War.png","title":"The war mapped from Greece to Asia Minor, 192 to 188 BCE","desc":"A modern map of the campaigns; the arrows compress four years of marching, sailing and negotiation into a scheme the ancient accounts never draw.","credit":"Map by Kryston. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman-Syrian-War.png","alt":"A map of Greece and Asia Minor with campaign arrows and battle sites marked."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Hannibal not sent to Africa with a fleet?","a":"Because Thoas argued that his fame was too splendid for an officer under a king, and that any success would be credited to Hannibal rather than Antiochus."},{"q":"Why did Hannibal want Philip brought into the war?","a":"Because the Aetolians who had beaten Philip would then be fighting beside him, so two kings with the strength of Asia and Europe would face a single state."},{"q":"Why were the terms after the battle the same as before it?","a":"Because Scipio told the envoys that victory had never made the Romans more exacting nor defeat less so, with only the indemnity and hostages added."}]},
{"id":"rm-246","num":246,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Cato climbed Callidromus and caught the Aetolian guards asleep, which broke the Seleucid position at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and drove Antiochus out of Greece.","questions":["Antiochus walled the pass and set his Macedonians to level their spears over the rampart at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the heights above him were not held.","Livy reports 500 men escaping with the king from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then gives a rival account of 60,000 in the army and 40,000 dead."],"answer":"Battle of Thermopylae, 191 BCE","answerText":"Battle of Thermopylae, 191 BCE","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">191 BCE, at the pass</span><span class=\"dt-k\">The pass</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60 paces wide</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 150</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Escaped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500, with the king</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","greece","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Thermopylae, 191 BCE</b> was fought in the same pass as the battle of 480 BCE and ended the Greek campaign of Antiochus III. Livy has the king wintering at Chalcis with nothing gained but quarters and a marriage, blaming the Aetolian Thoas for the promises that brought him over, and admiring Hannibal as a prudent man who had predicted it all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fell back on the pass with 10,000 foot and 500 horse, and Livy describes the place: a road no broader than sixty paces between the mountains and the sea, called Pylae, the gate, and Thermopylae from the hot spring at its mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fortified it with a double rampart and trench and a wall of the loose stone lying about, and split the 4,000 Aetolians with him between Heraclea and Hypata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the consul in front of him he recalled that the Spartans had been surrounded there by the Persians, and sent to the Aetolians to hold the heights above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They quarrelled over it, and half stayed at Heraclea while 2,000 went up and divided themselves between the summits of Callidromus, Rhoduntia and Tichius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consul sent Lucius Valerius Flaccus against the last two and Marcus Porcius Cato against Callidromus, and attacked the works with a narrow line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Macedonians gave ground to the rampart and held it by levelling their long spears over the top, the wall giving them height while the spears kept the Romans below at reach, until Cato appeared on the hill above the camp, having caught the Aetolians mostly asleep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Flaccus had failed at his two summits, but the camp took Cato's troops for Aetolians coming to help until they made out the standards, then threw down their arms and ran, their own elephants blocking the retreat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy says 500 got away with the king and 150 Romans fell, then reports Valerius Antias giving Antiochus 60,000 men and 40,000 dead, against the 10,000 he says Polybius brought from Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 36.15, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 36.16, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 36.17, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 36.18, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 36.19, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Thermopylae_ancient_coastline_large.jpg/1280px-Thermopylae_ancient_coastline_large.jpg","title":"The pass at Thermopylae today, from the site of the Phocian wall","desc":"The ground has changed more than any other battlefield on this shelf: the sea reached about as far as the modern road, so the sixty-pace gap Livy describes is now an open plain.","credit":"Photograph by Fkerasar. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thermopylae_ancient_coastline_large.jpg","alt":"A wide flat plain below a steep wooded mountainside, with a road running across it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the king send men up to the heights?","a":"Because he recalled that the Spartans had been surrounded in that same pass by the Persians, so the mountain paths above had to be held."},{"q":"Why did the rampart hold against the legions?","a":"Because the Macedonians levelled their long spears over the top, the wall giving them height while the spears kept the Romans below at reach."},{"q":"Why does Livy set two accounts of the numbers side by side?","a":"Because Valerius Antias gives Antiochus 60,000 men and 40,000 dead, against the 10,000 Livy says Polybius brought from Asia."}]},
{"id":"rm-247","num":247,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Eumenes turned the scythed chariots back on their own army at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by frightening the horses with archers and slingers instead of meeting them head on.","questions":["A phalanx of 16,000 with elephants between its blocks was broken at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when fugitives ran through the ranks and the long spears could not be used.","A tribune had his own men cut down the foremost runaways at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, driving the rest back into the fight because the greater fear overcame the less."],"answer":"Battle of Magnesia","answerText":"Battle of Magnesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">190 BCE, near Sipylus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phalanx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16,000, 32 deep</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Seleucid dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">up to 50,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">324, per Livy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Magnesia</b> was fought near Magnesia by Sipylus in 190 BCE and destroyed the field army of Antiochus III. Livy describes the king's line as chequered with many nations: 16,000 men in a Macedonian phalanx at the centre, divided into ten blocks 32 ranks deep, with two elephants standing in each gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Around them were 3,000 mailed cataphracts, a picked Median guard called the Agema, the silver-shielded royal cohort, Dahan horse-archers, Mysian and Elymaean archers, Cyrtaean slingers, and Arab archers on dromedaries carrying swords four cubits long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A morning mist wetted everything, which cost the Romans little but slackened the strings of the king's bows and slings and the thongs of his javelins, and hid his own wings from his centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His scythed chariots, meant to break the Roman line, were turned back on his own troops by Eumenes, who sent archers, slingers and javelin-men against the horses rather than meeting the chariots in a regular fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The reserves behind them fled, the cataphracts broke at the first charge, and the panic reached the centre, where men running between the ranks left the phalanx unable to use its sarissas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The legions threw their javelins into the disorder, and Livy says the elephants did not deter them, since they had learned in the African wars to step aside and cut the beasts' sinews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the other wing Antiochus had seen that the Roman left rested on the river with only four troops of horse, and he broke it and drove it back on the camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There the tribune Marcus Aemilius met the fugitives, ordered his own men to kill the foremost of them and drive the rest back with sword-wounds, and the greater fear overcame the less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy gives up to 50,000 foot and 3,000 horse killed with 1,400 taken, against 300 Roman infantry and 24 horsemen, and has Antiochus riding to Sardis and then to Apamea while the cities sent to surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 37.40, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 37.41, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 37.42, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 37.43, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 37.44, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Cataphract_fighting_a_lion%2C_made_between_the_3rd_BC-2nd_BC_century%2C_in_the_Seleucid_or_Parthian_period.jpg/1280px-Cataphract_fighting_a_lion%2C_made_between_the_3rd_BC-2nd_BC_century%2C_in_the_Seleucid_or_Parthian_period.jpg","title":"A mailed horseman on a plaque from Iraq, of the 3rd or 2nd century BCE","desc":"The kind of cavalry Livy calls cataphracts, armoured horse and rider alike; the plaque is dated to the Seleucid or the Parthian period and does not depict this battle.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cataphract_fighting_a_lion%2C_made_between_the_3rd_BC-2nd_BC_century%2C_in_the_Seleucid_or_Parthian_period.jpg","alt":"An engraved plaque showing an armoured rider on an armoured horse spearing a lion."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the morning mist hurt one side only?","a":"Because it cost the Romans little but slackened the strings of the king’s bows and slings and the thongs of his javelins, and hid his wings from his centre."},{"q":"Why were the scythed chariots turned against their own army?","a":"Because Eumenes sent archers, slingers and javelin-men against the horses rather than meeting the chariots in a regular fight."},{"q":"Why could the phalanx not use its sarissas?","a":"Because the panic reached the centre and men running between the ranks left it disordered, into which the legions threw their javelins."}]},
{"id":"rm-248","num":248,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Antiochus gave up every elephant, kept only ten decked ships and withdrew beyond the Taurus under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which closed the war Rome had fought in Asia.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Rhodes received Lycia and Caria south of the Maeander, while Eumenes was given the Chersonese, both Phrygias, Lydia and Ephesus.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> required twenty hostages between eighteen and forty, replaced every three years, and the surrender of Hannibal and the Aetolian Thoas."],"answer":"Treaty of Apamea","answerText":"Treaty of Apamea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">188 BCE, at Apamea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12,000 talents, per Livy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Warships left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 decked ships</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20, aged 18 to 40</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Apamea</b> was the settlement Rome imposed on Antiochus III in 188 BCE, two years after his defeat at Magnesia. Its shape had been fixed immediately after the battle, when Scipio told the king's envoys that victory had never made the Romans more exacting nor defeat less so, and repeated the terms offered before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Antiochus was to let no enemy of Rome pass through his territory, to make no war on the islands or in Europe, and to evacuate every city, village and fort on this side of the Taurus as far as the river Halys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was to surrender every elephant and keep none in future, give up his warships and hold no more than ten decked vessels, and send no ship west of the Calycadnus and the Sarpedonian promontory except to carry money, envoys or hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was to hire no mercenaries from Roman territory, restore Rhodian property and debts, and hand over Hannibal, Thoas the Aetolian and the other named exiles at his court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Livy gives the indemnity as 12,000 Attic talents paid over twelve years in equal instalments, with 540,000 pecks of wheat, and 350 talents to Eumenes over five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius' text as it stands makes the same sum an annual payment rather than a total, which is the largest difference between the two versions of the document.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Twenty hostages were to be given, none under eighteen or over forty, and replaced every three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy says fifty of the king's decked ships were broken up or burnt at Patara, and that Manlius took the surrendered elephants and gave the whole herd to Eumenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ten commissioners freed the towns that had paid Antiochus and stayed loyal, made those that had joined him pay the same tribute to Eumenes instead, gave Rhodes Lycia and Caria south of the Maeander, and gave Eumenes the Chersonese, both Phrygias, Lycaonia, Lydia and Ephesus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 21.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/21*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 21.42, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/21*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 38.38, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 38.39, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 21.45, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/21*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Map_Apamea_en.svg/1280px-Map_Apamea_en.svg.png","title":"Asia Minor as the treaty left it, in a modern map","desc":"A modern reconstruction of the division: the ancient texts list cities and districts rather than drawing a frontier, so the lines here are the map-maker's inference from those lists.","credit":"Map by Rowanwindwhistle. CC BY-SA 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Apamea_en.svg","alt":"A coloured map of Asia Minor showing the territories assigned to Pergamon and Rhodes."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the naval clauses name particular headlands?","a":"Because the king might keep no more than ten decked ships and send none west of the Calycadnus and the Sarpedonian promontory except to carry money, envoys or hostages."},{"q":"Why does the indemnity figure differ between the two accounts?","a":"Because Polybius’ text as it stands makes the 12,000 talents an annual payment where Livy makes it a total paid over twelve years."},{"q":"Why did the commissioners treat the cities differently?","a":"Because those that had paid Antiochus and stayed loyal were freed, while those that had joined him paid the same tribute to Eumenes instead."}]},
{"id":"rm-249","num":249,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy says older senators could find nothing of the Roman genius in the trick that opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a truce offered only to gain time to prepare.","questions":["Perseus had Eumenes ambushed on the path to Delphi before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, by men who built steps behind a wall where the road narrowed to single file.","Perseus mustered 43,000 men at Cytium for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, about half of them a phalanx, after 26 years in which Macedon had been at peace."],"answer":"Third Macedonian War","answerText":"Third Macedonian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">171 – 168 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Perseus' army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">43,000, half a phalanx</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace before</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 years, per Livy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Third Macedonian War</b> was fought between Rome and Perseus of Macedon from 171 to 168 BCE and ended the Macedonian kingdom. Livy says Eumenes of Pergamum came to Rome in person to lay the charges, noting that Valerius Antias made it his brother Attalus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eumenes traced the danger to Philip, who he said had murdered his son Demetrius for opposing a war with Rome, had called in the Bastarnae so as to cross into Italy, and had left his kingdom to Rome's bitterest enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On his way home Perseus had him ambushed above Delphi, where hired men built steps behind a wall on the path up from Cirrha and waited where the road thinned his escort to single file.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the envoys Marcius and Atilius reported at Rome they claimed most credit for overreaching the king with a truce and the hope of peace, so that Rome could prepare while he could not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Much of the senate approved this as consummate wisdom, but Livy says the older members, who kept the ancient simplicity of manners, could discover nothing of the Roman genius in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their ancestors, they held, proclaimed war before waging it and sometimes fixed the day and the ground, warned Pyrrhus that his physician meant to poison him, and sent the betrayer of the Faliscan children back bound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Winning by cunning was Carthaginian craft or Greek subtlety, and an enemy is only broken for good when he admits he was beaten by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Perseus held his council at Pella, sacrificed a hundred victims to Athena Alcidemos, and drew up 43,000 armed men at Cytium, about half of them the phalanx.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy has him tell that army that Macedon had been 26 years at peace, that Rome sent envoys at the same time as soldiers, and that it spun out the winter in a fallacious conference to gain time, which is the manoeuvre Marcius had claimed credit for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 42.11, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 42.15, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 42.47, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 42.51, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 42.52, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Kolmasmakedonialaissota.png","title":"Greece and Macedonia during the war, in a modern map","desc":"A modern reconstruction of the theatre; Livy's narrative gives marches and places rather than the fronts and blocks of colour a map of this kind has to supply.","credit":"Map after Bibi Saint-Pol. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolmasmakedonialaissota.png","alt":"A map of Greece and Macedonia with regions coloured and places named."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the older senators object to a diplomatic success?","a":"Because the envoys claimed credit for overreaching the king with a truce and the hope of peace, and the older members could discover nothing of the Roman genius in winning that way."},{"q":"Why did they hold that force alone settles a war?","a":"Because an enemy is only broken for good when he admits he was beaten by force, where cunning was Carthaginian craft or Greek subtlety."},{"q":"Why is Perseus’ speech to his army worth setting beside that debate?","a":"Because he told them Rome sent envoys at the same time as soldiers and spun out the winter in a fallacious conference to gain time — the manoeuvre Marcius had claimed credit for."}]},
{"id":"rm-250","num":250,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Livy says a forged letter from Flamininus helped <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> persuade his father to have the younger brother Demetrius poisoned and then suffocated with blankets.","questions":["Polybius blames avarice for the ruin of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who would not buy the help of Eumenes even though publishing the offer would have destroyed him.","Asked what injuries had driven him to war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fixed his eyes on the ground and wept a long time without giving Paullus any answer."],"answer":"Perseus","answerText":"Perseus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">179 – 168 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Surrendered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">168 BCE, to Paullus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held at</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Alba, from 167 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","person","history","greece","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Perseus</b> was the last king of Macedon, reigning from 179 to 168 BCE, and Rome's opponent in the third and final Macedonian war. He reached the throne by destroying his younger brother: Livy has him lay a second accusation before their father Philip, resting on a forged letter from Flamininus, after which Demetrius was poisoned at a supper and, the poison working slowly, suffocated with blankets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philip afterwards learned what had been done, and Livy says he meant to leave the kingdom to Antigonus instead, going round the Macedonian cities to commend him, but died at Amphipolis before he could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eumenes of Pergamum told the Roman senate that Perseus had received a war with Rome as a legacy from his father and was improving on it, being strong in the young men a long peace had bred and in the resources of his kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polybius is harder on him than on anyone else in the war and blames avarice, since Perseus would not part with the money that might have bought the help of Eumenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He should have paid either way, Polybius argues, since publishing the transaction afterwards would have made his worst enemy an enemy of Rome, and he says the king behaved the same way towards the Galatians and Genthius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After the defeat at Pydna he came into the Roman camp in mourning, attended by nobody but his son, and could not get through the crowd until lictors cleared a way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy says Aemilius Paullus rose to do him honour, gave him his right hand, raised him when he tried to throw himself at his feet, and seated him opposite the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Asked what injuries had driven him into a war that brought his kingdom to ruin, Perseus fixed his eyes on the ground and wept a long time without answering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The senate afterwards sent him with his son Alexander to be kept at Alba, allowing him his attendants, his money, his plate and his furniture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 40.24, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 40.56, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 42.11, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 29.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/29*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.7, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.8, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.42, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Macedonia_-_king_Perseus_-_179-168_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_king_Perseus_-_eagle_-_London_BM_BNK-G-241.jpg/1280px-Macedonia_-_king_Perseus_-_179-168_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_king_Perseus_-_eagle_-_London_BM_BNK-G-241.jpg","title":"A silver tetradrachm of Perseus, struck between 179 and 168 BCE","desc":"The king diademed on one face and an eagle on a thunderbolt within an oak wreath on the other; these are the coins that paid for the war and, Polybius says, the coins he would not spend.","credit":"British Museum, BNK G.241. Photograph by ArchaiOptix. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_-_king_Perseus_-_179-168_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_king_Perseus_-_eagle_-_London_BM_BNK-G-241.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a silver coin, a diademed head in profile and an eagle within a wreath."},"why":[{"q":"Why did his father mean to leave the kingdom elsewhere?","a":"Because Philip afterwards learned what had been done to Demetrius, and went round the Macedonian cities commending Antigonus before dying at Amphipolis."},{"q":"Why does Polybius blame avarice above everything?","a":"Because Perseus would not part with money that might have bought help, and publishing the transaction afterwards would in any case have made his worst enemy an enemy of Rome."},{"q":"Why did he not answer the question put to him after the defeat?","a":"Because asked what injuries had driven him into a war that ruined his kingdom, he fixed his eyes on the ground and wept a long time without speaking."}]},
{"id":"rm-251","num":251,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Paullus said he had never seen anything more fearful than the phalanx he met at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and won by sending cohorts into the gaps that opened in it.","questions":["A tribune warned the army before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the moon would be eclipsed that night, so that nobody should mistake a natural event for a portent.","Livy puts 20,000 Macedonians dead at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against no more than a hundred Romans, most of them Pelignians who had charged the spears head on."],"answer":"Battle of Pydna","answerText":"Battle of Pydna","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">168 BCE, in Macedonia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Macedonian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prisoners</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11,000 in all</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roman dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">not above 100</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["battle","warfare","history","greece","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Pydna</b> was fought in Macedonia in 168 BCE and ended the Third Macedonian War. The night before, the tribune Gaius Sulpicius Gallus told the soldiers that the moon would be eclipsed between the second and fourth hours, so that none should take it for a portent, such things happening at stated times in the course of nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says that when Aemilius Paullus came up and saw the long spears already planted in the Roman shields and the Macedonian shields locked, amazement and fear took hold of him, and that he often spoke of never having seen a more fearful sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Pelignian commander threw his standard among the enemy and his men went after it, abandoning a standard being unthinkable among Italians, and died against spears that pierced shield and breastplate alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What saved them was the ground, uneven under a long line, so that clefts opened in the phalanx and Paullus divided his cohorts into the gaps to fight many separate battles instead of one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Livy says the same: the phalanx is irresistible while compact and bristling with extended spears, and falls into irretrievable disorder once it must turn them about, and the victory had no more evident cause than those many distinct fights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He adds that had the Romans gone straight at it in order they would have impaled themselves, and that the elephants were a mere name without the least use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch reports that Polybius has Perseus leave at the outset under pretence of sacrificing to Heracles, while Poseidonius, who says he was there, has him ride out on a pack-horse after a kick from a horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy gives 20,000 Macedonians killed, 6,000 taken and 5,000 caught straggling, against not more than 100 Romans dead, most of them Pelignians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He describes men swimming out to the Roman fleet with their hands held out, cut down from the boats, and those who regained the land trodden to death by elephants driven to the shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 44.37, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paulus</i> 19, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paulus</i> 20, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 44.41, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 44.42, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Diorama_Pydna.jpg/1280px-Diorama_Pydna.jpg","title":"A museum diorama of the battle","desc":"A modern model, not evidence: it makes the phalanx a solid block, where both ancient accounts turn on the gaps that opened in it once the ground stopped being level.","credit":"Photograph by Spads. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diorama_Pydna.jpg","alt":"A model of ranks of miniature soldiers with levelled pikes facing a Roman line."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a tribune explain the eclipse in advance?","a":"Because such things happen at stated times in the course of nature, so telling the soldiers beforehand kept them from taking it for a portent."},{"q":"Why did the ground rather than the fighting decide it?","a":"Because it was uneven under a long line, so clefts opened in the phalanx and Paullus put his cohorts into the gaps to fight many separate battles instead of one."},{"q":"Why would attacking in order have been fatal?","a":"Because the phalanx is irresistible while compact and bristling with extended spears, so a line going straight at it would have impaled itself."}]},
{"id":"rm-252","num":252,"category":"Ancient Rome","question":"Plutarch says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> treated the augurship as one of the higher arts, arguing that men who relax in small matters break down the guard over greater ones.","questions":["Livy says the army of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> plundered seventy Epirote towns on one signal, leading away 150,000 people after promising to withdraw the garrisons.","Two young sons of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> died within days of his triumph, and he told the people that Perseus, though conquered, still had children while he had none."],"answer":"Lucius Aemilius Paullus","answerText":"Lucius Aemilius Paullus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pydna</span><span class=\"dt-v\">168 BCE, in Macedonia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Epirus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70 towns, 150,000 taken</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sons lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, at his triumph</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","history","warfare","rome","greece"],"abstract":"<b>Lucius Aemilius Paullus</b> was the Roman commander who destroyed the Macedonian kingdom at Pydna in 168 BCE. Plutarch says his father of the same name died at Cannae, having failed to talk his colleague out of the battle and then refused to share his flight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was elected aedile over twelve competitors who all became consuls afterwards, and as an augur he made a priesthood most men sought for its reputation into one of the higher arts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch has him dispute small points of ceremony with his colleagues, on the ground that men who relax their strictness in small matters break down the guard set over greater ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy shows that exactness turned to another use in Epirus, where the senate had granted his soldiers the plunder of the cities that had gone over to Perseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Centurions went ahead saying they had come to withdraw the garrisons so that the Epirotes might be free, the gold and silver was called in, the cohorts were timed to arrive on one day, and the signal to plunder was given at the fourth hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy says 150,000 people were led away captive and the walls of about seventy towns razed, and that the soldiers were still not satisfied, being angry at getting no share of the king's own treasure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His triumph at home was shadowed: of his four sons the two elder had been adopted into other houses, and of the two remaining one died five days before it and the other three days after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch has him tell the assembled people that he had never feared any human agency but had always feared Fortune, and had distrusted her the more because the war had run so smoothly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He said she had used him to show human weakness as plainly as she had used his captive, except that Perseus, though conquered, still had his children, while Aemilius, though conqueror, had lost his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paulus</i> 2, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paulus</i> 3, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.34, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paulus</i> 35, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paulus</i> 36, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Monument_of_Aemilus_Paullus_%28Reinach%2C_R%C3%A9pertoire_de_reliefs_I%2C_p118%29.png/1280px-Monument_of_Aemilus_Paullus_%28Reinach%2C_R%C3%A9pertoire_de_reliefs_I%2C_p118%29.png","title":"The frieze of his monument at Delphi, in a 19th-century line drawing","desc":"A drawing after the marble rather than a photograph of it: the frieze of the victory monument at Delphi, showing cavalry and infantry engaged.","credit":"After Salomon Reinach, Répertoire de reliefs grecs et romains I. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_of_Aemilus_Paullus_%28Reinach%2C_R%C3%A9pertoire_de_reliefs_I%2C_p118%29.png","alt":"A line drawing of a carved frieze with horsemen and foot soldiers in combat."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he argue over small points of ceremony?","a":"Because he held that men who relax their strictness in small matters break down the guard set over greater ones."},{"q":"Why was the sack of Epirus organised so precisely?","a":"Because centurions went ahead saying they came to withdraw the garrisons, the gold and silver was called in, and the cohorts were timed to arrive on one day for a signal at the fourth hour."},{"q":"Why did he say Fortune had used him as she used his captive?","a":"Because Perseus, though conquered, still had his children, while he, though conqueror, had lost his — the two remaining sons dying within days of his triumph."}]},
{"id":"rm-253","num":253,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, imposed in 167 BCE, the kingdom was divided into four districts whose people could not marry, buy land or trade across the new boundaries drawn between them.","questions":["Aemilius Paullus proclaimed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Amphipolis in Latin while a praetor read it out in Greek, shutting the gold and silver mines and forbidding the cutting of ship timber.","Livy writes that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left Macedon looking dismembered, like an animal torn into limbs that needed each other, because commerce between its four new councils had been stopped."],"answer":"settlement of Macedonia","answerText":"settlement of Macedonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">167 BCE, at Amphipolis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Districts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, each with its own council</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 talents, half the old</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>settlement of Macedonia</b> was the arrangement Rome imposed on the defeated kingdom in 167 BCE, and Livy says its outline was fixed in the senate before the commissioners left for Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Macedonians and Illyrians were to be free, so that the world might see that Roman arms brought freedom to slaves rather than slavery to the free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The contracts for the Macedonian mines and the crown lands were abolished, on the reasoning that such business needed tax-farmers, and that wherever a contractor worked either the law counted for nothing or the allies' freedom did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Macedonia was to be divided into four districts, each with a council of its own, and to pay Rome half the tribute it had paid its kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aemilius Paullus read the terms out in Latin at Amphipolis while the praetor Gnaeus Octavius translated them into Greek for the crowd.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The districts were marked off by the rivers Nessus, Strymon, Axius and Peneus, with their councils, elections and public money at Amphipolis, Thessalonica, Pella and Pelagonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There was to be no marriage and no buying of land or houses across a boundary, the gold and silver mines were shut while iron and copper might still be worked, imported salt was banned, and nobody was to cut ship timber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy says the liberty and the lighter tribute delighted them, but that with commerce between the districts stopped Macedon looked dismembered, like an animal torn into limbs that each needed the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch puts the tribute at 100 talents and calls it less than half of what the kings had taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy adds that the king's friends, generals and officers were ordered to Italy with their sons over fifteen on pain of death, and that the laws Paullus then framed looked like laws for deserving allies rather than for a beaten enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.18, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.29, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.30, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paulus</i> 28, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.32, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Macedonia_-_first_region_-_158-150_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_bust_of_Artemis_on_Macedonian_shield_-_club_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg/1280px-Macedonia_-_first_region_-_158-150_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_bust_of_Artemis_on_Macedonian_shield_-_club_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg","title":"Tetradrachm of the first district, struck at Amphipolis","desc":"The coin names its issuer as the first district, ΠΡΩΤΗ ΜΕΡΙΣ, and was struck at Amphipolis, where the terms had been read out. The museum record dates it to about 158 to 146 BCE, so it is a decade or so after the settlement rather than a coin of the moment.","alt":"Silver coin: a bust of Artemis inside a Macedonian shield on one face, a club within a wreath on the other.","credit":"Münzkabinett, Staatliche Museen zu Berlin. Photograph by ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonia_-_first_region_-_158-150_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_bust_of_Artemis_on_Macedonian_shield_-_club_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why were the mine and land contracts abolished?","a":"Because such business needed tax-farmers, and wherever a contractor worked either the law counted for nothing or the allies’ freedom did."},{"q":"Why did a settlement granting freedom leave the country crippled?","a":"Because with no marriage, no buying of land and no commerce across the four boundaries, Macedon looked dismembered, like an animal torn into limbs that each needed the others."},{"q":"Why were the king’s friends and officers ordered to Italy?","a":"Because they were required to go with their sons over fifteen on pain of death, which removed the men who could have gathered a following."}]},
{"id":"rm-254","num":254,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"On the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Roman envoy drew a circle round Antiochus IV with a vine stick and told him to decide about leaving Egypt before he stepped out of it.","questions":["Polybius reckoned that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went as it did only because Perseus had already been beaten, and that Antiochus would never have obeyed had he not been certain of that.","Livy places the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about 6 km (four Roman miles) outside Alexandria, where an envoy handed the king a tablet instead of the hand he had held out in greeting."],"answer":"Day of Eleusis","answerText":"Day of Eleusis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Meeting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">168 BCE, at Eleusis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 6 km from Alexandria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Day of Eleusis</b> is the name given to a meeting outside Alexandria in 168 BCE, at which one Roman envoy stopped a Seleucid king from taking Egypt. Polybius says the senate, hearing that Antiochus had become master of Egypt and very nearly of Alexandria itself, judged that the king's aggrandisement concerned them and sent Gaius Popillius Laenas to bring the war to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polybius thought the king had forgotten everything he had written and said, and quotes Simonides that it is hard to be good, since meaning well up to a point is easy and holding to it is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy says Antiochus had told Ptolemy's envoys that he would neither recall his fleet nor halt his army except for the cession of all Cyprus and of Pelusium with the land at that mouth of the Nile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He came down towards Alexandria by short marches, and Livy places the meeting at Eleusis, about 6 km (four Roman miles) from the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Popillius refused the offered hand and put a written tablet into it instead, holding back the sign of friendship, Polybius says, until he knew whether the greeting was friendly or hostile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Antiochus said he would consult his friends, Popillius drew a circle round him with a stick cut from a vine and told him to answer before he stepped out of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy has the king hesitate and then say he would do as the senate directed, at which Popillius took his hand as that of a friend and ally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The letter ordered the war with Ptolemy stopped at once, and Antiochus led his army back to Syria within the days allowed, hurt and complaining but yielding for the present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius says Fortune had so arranged matters that Egypt was saved simply because the fate of Perseus had already been decided, and that had the king not been certain of it he would never have obeyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 29.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/29*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 29.26, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/29*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.11, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.12, trans. William A. McDevitte (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/44318/pg44318.txt. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 29.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/29*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Laenas_trekt_een_cirkel_rond_Antiochus_IV_Epiphanes_in_het_zand%2C_RP-T-00-1082.jpg/1280px-Laenas_trekt_een_cirkel_rond_Antiochus_IV_Epiphanes_in_het_zand%2C_RP-T-00-1082.jpg","title":"Laenas draws his circle, in a Dutch drawing of the scene","desc":"Pen and wash by Jurriaan Andriessen: soldiers at the left, the king and his companions at the right, and a circle scratched on the ground between them. It is an imagining made nearly two thousand years afterwards and no kind of record of the meeting.","alt":"Ink and grey wash drawing of two groups of ancient figures facing each other, with a faint circle marked on the ground between them.","credit":"Jurriaan Andriessen, Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam, RP-T-00-1082, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laenas_trekt_een_cirkel_rond_Antiochus_IV_Epiphanes_in_het_zand%2C_RP-T-00-1082.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the envoy refuse to take the king’s hand?","a":"Because he held back the sign of friendship until he knew whether the greeting was friendly or hostile, putting a written tablet into it instead."},{"q":"Why draw a circle round him in the dust?","a":"Because the king had said he would consult his friends, and the circle required an answer before he stepped out of it."},{"q":"Why does Polybius credit Egypt’s escape to the timing?","a":"Because the fate of Perseus had already been decided, and had the king not been certain of it he would never have obeyed."}]},
{"id":"rm-255","num":255,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 146 BCE the Achaean League voted for a fight that Polybius says was aimed nominally at Sparta but really at Rome, and lost it within a single season.","questions":["Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, an Achaean assembly at Corinth jeered a Roman embassy and hustled it out of the meeting, and its general had debts left uncollected and debtors left out of prison.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost the League two generals in a season: one vanished after the rout at Scarpheia, and the other fled to Megalopolis, killed his wife and drank poison."],"answer":"Achaean War","answerText":"Achaean War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE, in one season</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Scarpheia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1,000 Achaeans taken</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Diaeus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 horse, 14,000 foot</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>Achaean War</b> was the short war of 146 BCE in which Rome broke the Achaean League, and Polybius called his account of it the completion of the disaster of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was an Achaean himself, and was in Africa with Scipio while it happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He judged it worse than the fall of Carthage, since the Carthaginians at least left posterity some ground for defending their cause and the Greeks gave none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The occasion was a quarrel over whether Sparta might leave the League, and Polybius says the envoys under Aurelius Orestes came home exaggerating the danger they had been in, while the senate's next commission was told only to censure mildly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When a further embassy addressed the Achaean assembly at Corinth the people jeered, hooted and hustled them out of the meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Critolaus then carried a vote for war, Polybius says nominally against Sparta but really against Rome, together with a decree giving the elected generals absolute power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He had the magistrates stop collecting debts and stop gaoling debtors, and Diaeus after him freed and armed 12,000 slaves and exacted contributions from women's jewellery as well as from men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pausanias says Critolaus refused Metellus' terms, sat down before Heraclea, and on hearing the Romans had crossed the Spercheius fled to Scarpheia, where his army was overtaken and he was never seen again, alive or dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Diaeus raised about 600 horse and 14,000 foot, lost them at the Isthmus to Mummius, and fled to Megalopolis, where he killed his wife and drank poison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Polybius thought some resourceful fortune had saved Achaea by the only device left to her, a quick and easy defeat, and reports the proverb everyone repeated: 'Had we not perished so soon we would never have been saved.'<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.14, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.9, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.12, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.13, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.15, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 7.15, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=7:chapter=15. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 7.16, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=7:chapter=16. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.18, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Relief_aus_Kleitor.JPG/960px-Relief_aus_Kleitor.JPG","title":"The relief from Kleitor, thought to show Polybius","desc":"An old photograph of a plaster cast, not of the stone: the relief was found at Kleitor in Arcadia in 1880 and is thought to represent the historian, whose account this card mostly follows.","alt":"Photograph of a relief showing a standing draped figure with a spear, a shield resting at his feet.","credit":"Cast of the Kleitor relief, German Archaeological Institute at Athens, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_aus_Kleitor.JPG"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Polybius judge this worse than the fall of Carthage?","a":"Because the Carthaginians at least left posterity some ground for defending their cause, and the Greeks gave none."},{"q":"Why did the League’s leaders stop collecting debts and free slaves?","a":"Because they needed men and money at once, so Critolaus halted debt collection and gaoling and Diaeus armed 12,000 slaves and exacted contributions from women’s jewellery."},{"q":"Why did people say they were saved by perishing quickly?","a":"Because Polybius thought a quick and easy defeat was the only device fortune had left, a longer war meaning a heavier destruction."}]},
{"id":"rm-256","num":256,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"In the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 146 BCE Lucius Mummius put those found in the city to the sword, sold the women and children, and carried its most admired works of art away to Rome.","questions":["Polybius watched the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and reported seeing paintings thrown down on the ground with soldiers playing dice on them, among them a Dionysus by Aristeides that Strabo later saw at Rome.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the site lay deserted for a long time until Caesar refounded it, and the settlers dug open its graves and sold what they found."],"answer":"sack of Corinth","answerText":"sack of Corinth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE, by Lucius Mummius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stormed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd day after the battle</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>sack of Corinth</b> was the destruction of the city by Lucius Mummius in 146 BCE, at the end of the Achaean War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias says Mummius hesitated to enter although the gates stood open, suspecting an ambush, and stormed and fired the city on the third day after the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of those found in it were put to the sword, the women and children were sold, and so were the slaves who had been freed to fight and had not fallen on the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He carried off the most admired offerings and works of art and gave the lesser ones to the general Attalus had sent from Pergamum, so that Corinthian spoils were still to be seen there in Pausanias' own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo says the city was razed to the ground and the Sicyonians obtained most of its territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius was present and says he saw paintings flung on the ground with soldiers playing dice on them, among them the Dionysus by Aristeides and the Heracles tortured by the robe of Deianeira.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo says he saw that Dionysus himself, on a wall of the temple of Ceres at Rome, and that it perished when the temple burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mummius, he adds, was magnanimous rather than fond of art and shared readily with anyone who asked, which is how Lucullus came to keep statues he had only borrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Polybius argued the Roman commissioners out of pulling down the statues of Philopoemen the Achaean,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and then refused to take anything at all from the sale of the condemned men's property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strabo says the site lay deserted a long while and was refounded by Caesar with settlers drawn mostly from the freedman class, who dug open the old graves and sold what they found in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 7.16, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=7:chapter=16. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 8.6.23, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 4 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/8F*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 39.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/39*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 39.3, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/39*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 39.4, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/39*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/The_Temple_of_Apollo_in_Ancient_Corinth_on_June_6%2C_2018.jpg/1280px-The_Temple_of_Apollo_in_Ancient_Corinth_on_June_6%2C_2018.jpg","title":"The archaic temple of Apollo at Corinth, with Acrocorinth behind","desc":"Seven Doric columns of the archaic temple still stand above the site, under the citadel hill. Nothing in the view is of the sack itself; it is the ground the sources are describing.","alt":"Seven weathered Doric columns standing on open ground, with a steep rocky hill rising behind them.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Temple_of_Apollo_in_Ancient_Corinth_on_June_6%2C_2018.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the general hesitate before an open gate?","a":"Because he suspected an ambush, and stormed and fired the city only on the third day after the battle."},{"q":"Why does Polybius’ eyewitness detail matter?","a":"Because he saw paintings flung on the ground with soldiers playing dice on them, among them the Dionysus by Aristeides and the Heracles tortured by the robe of Deianeira."},{"q":"Why were borrowed statues never returned?","a":"Because Mummius was magnanimous rather than fond of art and shared readily with anyone who asked, which is how Lucullus kept what he had only borrowed."}]},
{"id":"rm-257","num":257,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Rome governed the kingdom as four separate republics until a pretender overran it, after which the country became a single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed from coast to coast by a measured road.","questions":["Strabo lists the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among the ten praetorian commands held by the senate, and describes the Egnatian Road running east across it, marked with pillars as far as the Hebrus.","Before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was settled, a man Livy calls Andriscus claimed to be Perseus' son, took the country, destroyed a praetor's army, and was beaten by Quintus Caecilius Metellus."],"answer":"province of Macedonia","answerText":"province of Macedonia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">put down 147 BCE, by Q. Metellus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Egnatia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 860 km, marked by pillars</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of 10 praetorian, per Strabo</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","politics","rome","greece"],"abstract":"The <b>province of Macedonia</b> is what Rome eventually made of the kingdom it had broken into four republics, and Strabo lists it among the ten praetorian provinces held by the senate in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What ended the republics was a pretender, and Polybius says the story seemed at first inadmissible: here was a Philip fallen from the skies, when the real Philip was known to have died at about eighteen at Alba in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy's summary calls him Andriscus, a man of the lowest kind, sent to Rome by Demetrius of Syria precisely because of the lie, who escaped, gathered an army and occupied all Macedonia whether the people wanted it or not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own account was that Perseus had fathered him on a courtesan and given him to a Cretan to raise at Adramyttion, with a sealed writing to be handed over when he came of age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Velleius says he took armed possession of the country and assumed the insignia of royalty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He destroyed the praetor Publius Juventius and his army before Quintus Caecilius defeated and captured him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Velleius says that praetor took the name Macedonicus from the war, and brought home the group of equestrian statues Lysippus had made of the horsemen killed at the Granicus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias, writing later, calls the rebel plainly the son of Perseus, so the ancient accounts do not agree even about who he was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Polybius found the whole business inexplicable: Macedonia had been freed from autocratic rule and taxation and from its own civil massacres, yet saw more citizens exiled, tortured and murdered by this false Philip than by any real king, and beat Roman armies for him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Strabo describes the Egnatian Road running east from Apollonia, measured in Roman miles and marked with pillars as far as Cypsela on the Hebrus, about 860 km (535 Roman miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 17.3.25, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 8 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/17C*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.10, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 49, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-46-50/. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 1.11, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/1*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 50, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-46-50/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 7.13, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press; London: William Heinemann, 1918), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=7:chapter=13. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 36.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/36*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.7.4, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/7G*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Via_Egnatia%2C_Philippi_%287272784744%29.jpg/1280px-Via_Egnatia%2C_Philippi_%287272784744%29.jpg","title":"Paving on the line of the Egnatian Way at Philippi","desc":"The road ran through the province from the Adriatic to the Aegean and beyond. It was rebuilt and repaired for centuries, so the slabs a visitor walks on today need not be the ones Strabo's mile pillars stood beside.","alt":"A broad paved road of large stone slabs, grass growing between them, running away between ruined walls and fallen columns.","credit":"Photograph by Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Via_Egnatia,_Philippi_%287272784744%29.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the pretender’s claim seem inadmissible at first?","a":"Because the real Philip was known to have died at about eighteen at Alba in Italy, so here was a Philip fallen from the skies."},{"q":"Why did Polybius find the rising inexplicable?","a":"Because Macedonia had been freed from autocratic rule, taxation and its own civil massacres, yet saw more citizens exiled, tortured and murdered by this false Philip than by any real king."},{"q":"Why do the ancient accounts not agree about who he was?","a":"Because Livy calls him Andriscus, a man of the lowest kind, where Pausanias plainly calls him a son of Perseus, and his own story was a sealed writing given to a Cretan."}]},
{"id":"rm-258","num":258,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Rome inherited a kingdom in western Anatolia and turned it into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the richest of its commands and the one whose revenues Cicero said the money-market at Rome could not do without.","questions":["Strabo says the last Attalid king left the Romans his heirs and they proclaimed the country the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, naming it after the continent, with Ephesus as its largest trading city.","Tax contractors kept slave households in the salt-works, fields, harbours and custom-houses of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Cicero warned a single cavalry raid could carry off a whole year's revenue."],"answer":"province of Asia","answerText":"province of Asia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Secured</span><span class=\"dt-v\">130 BCE, after a revolt</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of 2 consular provinces</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last king</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reigned 5 years, then died</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","history","politics","rome","anatolia"],"abstract":"The <b>province of Asia</b> was the Roman command formed out of the Attalid kingdom of Pergamum, and Strabo says its last king died of disease after a reign of five years and left the Romans his heirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Romans, he adds, proclaimed the country a province and called it Asia, by the same name as the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo lists it later as one of only two consular provinces the senate held, the other being Libya, where ten more, Macedonia among them, were given to praetors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its greatest city was Ephesus, which Strabo calls the largest emporium in Asia this side the Taurus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its harbour had been made worse rather than better by an Attalid mole, which hemmed the Cayster's silt in instead of letting the tides carry it out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What made the province matter at Rome was its revenue, and Cicero, arguing for Pompey's command against Mithridates, told an assembly that the other provinces yielded barely enough to pay for their own defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Asia, he said, was so rich and so productive that in the fertility of its soil, in its fruits, in its pasture and in its exports it surpassed every other country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was farmed by contractors, and Cicero describes their households of slaves in the salt-works, the fields, the harbours and the custom-houses, and one inroad of cavalry carrying off a whole year's revenue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He calls the revenues the sinews of the republic, and the order of men who collected them the prop and support of all the other orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He also remembered what had happened when Mithridates struck: fortunes were lost in Asia and all credit failed at Rome, since many men could not lose their property in one city without drawing others into the same disaster.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 13.4.2, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1928), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/13D*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 17.3.25, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 8 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/17C*.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.24, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1928), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/14A*.html. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 14, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=14. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 16, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=16. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 17, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=17. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 19, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i>, trans. C. D. Yonge (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=19. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Pergamon_theatre_panorama_2.jpg/1280px-Pergamon_theatre_panorama_2.jpg","title":"The acropolis of Pergamum, looking down over the plain","desc":"The Attalid capital Rome inherited, its theatre cut into the side of the hill. The modern town of Bergama lies below on the Caicus plain that Strabo calls about the best land in Mysia.","alt":"A steep hilltop of ruined walls with a very steep theatre cut into the slope, looking out over a wide plain and a modern town.","credit":"Photograph by Tomisti, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pergamon_theatre_panorama_2.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did this province matter at Rome more than the others?","a":"Because the others yielded barely enough to pay for their own defence, where this one surpassed every country in the fertility of its soil, its fruits, its pasture and its exports."},{"q":"Why did a king’s harbour works make Ephesus worse?","a":"Because the Attalid mole hemmed the Cayster’s silt in instead of letting the tides carry it out."},{"q":"Why could trouble in Asia shake credit at Rome?","a":"Because so many fortunes were tied to the tax contracts that men could not lose their property in one city without drawing others into the same disaster."}]},
{"id":"rm-259","num":259,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Justin says the king spent his last years poisoning his own garden herbs and casting bronze, and that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the Roman people heir to his whole kingdom.","questions":["Tiberius Gracchus proposed spending the money that came to Rome under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on farm equipment for the citizens his land law had settled, which offended the senate more than ever.","Velleius says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not unique, since Bithynia was later left to Rome the same way, and Plutarch records the envoy who carried the will was accused of offering Gracchus a diadem."],"answer":"bequest of Attalus III","answerText":"bequest of Attalus III","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE, of a fever</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 years, per Strabo</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Illness</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 days, per Justin</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome","anatolia"],"abstract":"The <b>bequest of Attalus III</b> was the will by which the last king of Pergamum left his kingdom to the Roman people, and Strabo says only that he reigned five years, died of disease, and made the Romans his heirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin, epitomising a lost history, gives a much longer and far less friendly account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He says Attalus polluted a flourishing kingdom with murders of his friends and executions of his relatives, then put on mean clothes, let his hair and beard grow, and stopped showing himself in public at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He abandoned the government for his garden, where he mixed noxious herbs with harmless ones and sent them, moistened with poisonous juices, as presents to his friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He turned from that to working in brass, modelling in wax and casting figures, and died on the seventh day after taking a fever from the sun while building a monument for his mother.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Justin closes the story in one sentence: by his will the Roman people was appointed his heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says Eudemus of Pergamum brought the will to Rome, and that Tiberius Gracchus at once proposed that the king's money be given to the citizens who had received public land, to help them stock and till their farms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the cities of the kingdom, Gracchus said it did not belong to the senate to deliberate about them, and that he would put a resolution to the people himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch adds that Pompeius told the senate Eudemus had given Tiberius a royal diadem and a purple robe, believing he meant to be king in Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Velleius notes the bequest was not unique, Bithynia being left to Rome later by Nicomedes,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and Livy's summary says the country Attalus left was supposed to be free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 13.4.2, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1928), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/13D*.html. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 36.4, trans. J. S. Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), https://www.attalus.org/translate/justin5.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 14, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 58, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.4, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 59, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Berlin_-_Pergamonmuseum_-_Altar_01.jpg/1280px-Berlin_-_Pergamonmuseum_-_Altar_01.jpg","title":"The great altar from Pergamum, re-erected in Berlin","desc":"The most famous monument of the kingdom the will disposed of. It stood at Pergamum long before the museum did, and much of what is seen here is modern masonry built around the surviving sculpture.","alt":"A wide marble stairway rising between two colonnaded wings, with a carved frieze along the base, inside a museum hall.","credit":"Photograph by Lestat (Jan Mehlich), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin_-_Pergamonmuseum_-_Altar_01.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the two accounts of the king differ so sharply?","a":"Because Strabo says only that he reigned five years, died of disease and made the Romans his heirs, where Justin gives murders of friends, poisoned presents and a retreat into a garden."},{"q":"Why was the will immediately a domestic quarrel?","a":"Because Tiberius Gracchus proposed giving the king’s money to citizens who had received public land, and said the cities were not for the senate to deliberate about."},{"q":"Why was the accusation about a diadem so damaging?","a":"Because Pompeius told the senate that Eudemus had given Tiberius a royal diadem and purple robe, believing he meant to be king in Rome."}]},
{"id":"rm-260","num":260,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Strabo says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised destitute men and slaves who were promised freedom and called Heliopolitae, and cost Rome a consul before it was put down.","questions":["Justin says a Roman commander sent to end the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was keener to plunder the dead king's treasures than to win a battle, and paid for that avarice with his life.","Blossius of Cumae, acquitted after the killing of Tiberius Gracchus, went out to join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Asia and killed himself once the cause was lost."],"answer":"revolt of Aristonicus","answerText":"revolt of Aristonicus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Crassus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">killed 131 BCE, a consul</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aristonicus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">taken 130 BCE, died in prison</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 lieutenants, under Aquillius</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","anatolia"],"abstract":"The <b>revolt of Aristonicus</b> was the rising against Rome's inheritance of the Attalid kingdom, and Strabo says it began at Leucae, a small town, after the death of Attalus Philometor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristonicus was reputed to belong to the royal family and meant to usurp the kingdom, though the country he claimed had been left to the Roman people by will and was supposed to be free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Justin makes him a son of Eumenes by an Ephesian mistress, the daughter of a harp player,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> while Velleius calls him a man falsely claiming to be of the royal house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beaten in a sea fight off the Cymaean coast by the Ephesians and driven out of Smyrna, he went up into the interior instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There he quickly gathered a large number of destitute people and of slaves, invited with a promise of freedom, and called them Heliopolitae, citizens of the city of the Sun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He fell on Thyateira without warning and took Apollonis, but the cities sent troops against him at once, helped by Nicomedes of Bithynia and by the kings of Cappadocia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy's summary says Publius Licinius Crassus, consul and pontifex maximus at the same time, set out from Italy against him and was defeated and killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Justin says Crassus was more eager to plunder the treasures of Attalus than to distinguish himself in the field, and paid for his blind avarice with his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Perperna ended the war, took Aristonicus alive and sent him to Rome, where he died in prison, and Aquillius then came out as consul with ten lieutenants and organised the province in the form that still held in Strabo's own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch adds that Blossius of Cumae, acquitted after the killing of Tiberius Gracchus, went out to Aristonicus and killed himself when the cause was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.38, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1928), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/14A*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 59, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 36.4, trans. J. S. Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), https://www.attalus.org/translate/justin5.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.4, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 20, in <i>Lives</i>, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Ephesus_Arcadian_Street%2C_Ephesus_Archaeological_Site%2C_Selcuk%2C_Turkiye_%2853527773981%29.jpg/1280px-Ephesus_Arcadian_Street%2C_Ephesus_Archaeological_Site%2C_Selcuk%2C_Turkiye_%2853527773981%29.jpg","title":"The colonnaded street running down to the old harbour at Ephesus","desc":"Ephesus supplied the fleet that beat Aristonicus at sea. The colonnade shown belongs to the Roman city that grew after the revolt rather than to the town that fought it, and the harbour it leads to has long since silted up.","alt":"A line of broken stone column shafts along one side of a broad paved street, running into the distance.","credit":"Photograph by Warren LeMay, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ephesus_Arcadian_Street,_Ephesus_Archaeological_Site,_Selcuk,_Turkiye_%2853527773981%29.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did he turn inland after his defeats on the coast?","a":"Because beaten at sea off Cyme and driven out of Smyrna, he could raise in the interior the destitute and the slaves he invited with a promise of freedom."},{"q":"Why does the name he gave his followers matter?","a":"Because calling them Heliopolitae, citizens of the city of the Sun, made the rising a promise of a new order rather than only a claim to a throne."},{"q":"Why does Justin blame the Roman commander for his own death?","a":"Because he says Crassus was more eager to plunder the treasures of Attalus than to distinguish himself in the field, and paid for his blind avarice with his life."}]},
{"id":"rm-261","num":261,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Livy calls the country in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first province on the continent the Romans entered and the last one they reduced, which took them about two centuries.","questions":["Strabo blames the length of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on both sides, saying Rome fought piecemeal and the Iberians raided in small parties instead of building confederations.","Six praetors were elected for the first time during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and two of them drew the Hither and the Farther province of that country by lot."],"answer":"Roman conquest of Spain","answerText":"Roman conquest of Spain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Provinces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 from 197 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 200 years, per Strabo</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Praetors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6, for the first time</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","spain"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman conquest of Spain</b> took about two centuries, and Livy, writing under Augustus, calls it the first province on the continent the Romans entered and the last that was at length reduced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rome went in during the war with Hannibal, and Justin says the Scipios first drove the Carthaginians out of the country and then began terrible wars with the Spaniards themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy thought the peninsula better suited than Italy or any other part of the world for renewing a war, both by the nature of the ground and by the genius of the people living on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not long after Hannibal was beaten, six praetors were elected for the first time, because the provinces had increased and the bounds of the empire had been extended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two of them drew Spain by lot, Sempronius the Hither province and Helvius the Farther, and the two Spains date from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Strabo explains the length of the business as a matter of method: the Romans fought a piecemeal war, attacking one territory at a time and subjecting first one group and then another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They got the whole country under control only after about two hundred years or longer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He puts as much of the cause on the Iberian side, saying they were inclined to attack and to rob but ventured only on petty undertakings, because they would not build large forces or confederations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Had they been willing to be shield-fellows with one another, he argues, neither the Carthaginians nor the Tyrians before them could have overrun so much of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Justin says the Spaniards would not submit to the yoke even after their land was overrun, until Augustus, having subdued the rest of the world, turned his arms on them, reduced them into the form of a province, and brought them by the influence of laws to a more civilised way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 28.12, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 44.5, trans. J. S. Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), https://www.attalus.org/translate/justin7.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 32.27, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 32.28, trans. Cyrus Edmonds (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1850), https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582.txt. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 3.4.5, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/3D*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Teatro_Romano_de_Cartagena.jpg","title":"The Roman theatre at Cartagena, the old New Carthage","desc":"Cartagena is the New Carthage that Strabo calls by far the most powerful city in the country, founded by Hasdrubal and taken by Rome during the Hannibalic war. The theatre in the photograph is centuries later than that fighting, and the apartment blocks later still.","alt":"An excavated Roman theatre, its stone seating and stage sunk below the level of a modern town of apartment blocks.","credit":"Photograph by DaniRJ, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_Romano_de_Cartagena.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the first continental province take longest to reduce?","a":"Because the Romans fought a piecemeal war, attacking one territory at a time and subjecting first one group and then another, over about two hundred years."},{"q":"Why does Strabo put part of the cause on the Iberian side?","a":"Because they would not build large forces or confederations, so they ventured only on petty undertakings — had they been shield-fellows, neither Carthage nor Tyre could have overrun so much."},{"q":"Why did the number of praetors have to be raised?","a":"Because the provinces had increased and the bounds of the empire had been extended, so six were elected and two drew the Spains, which is where the two provinces date from."}]},
{"id":"rm-262","num":262,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Polybius says the Romans called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the fiery war, because the fighting stopped only at nightfall and only winter checked it at all.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke out again when a town raised a wall the senate had forbidden, and Appian concedes the townsmen were right about what the old treaty actually said.","Reports brought home from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so frightened Rome that recruits dodged enrolment and officers refused to serve, until the young Scipio Aemilianus offered to go himself."],"answer":"Celtiberian Wars","answerText":"Celtiberian Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">179 BCE, by Gracchus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War renewed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">153 BCE, over Segeda</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nobilior</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly 30,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","spain"],"abstract":"The <b>Celtiberian Wars</b> were the long fighting in central Spain, and Polybius says the Romans called this one the fiery war, so remarkable was the uninterrupted character of its engagements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where a war in Greece or Asia was usually decided by one battle or two, he says, here the fighting stopped only at nightfall and only winter checked it, the men refusing to let their courage flag and rallying to begin afresh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earlier phase had ended in a settlement: Appian says Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus divided land among the poor, settled them on it, and made carefully defined treaties with all the tribes, which were often longed for in the wars that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Trouble began again when Segeda, a town of the Belli covered by those treaties, drew in its smaller neighbours and threw a wall about seven kilometres (four miles) round itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The senate forbade the wall and demanded the tribute and the troops the treaty had stipulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Celtiberians answered that Gracchus had forbidden new cities but not the fortifying of old ones, and that Rome had since released them from the tribute and the levy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Appian says this was true, but that the senate, when it granted such exemptions, always added that they held only during the pleasure of the Roman people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The praetor Nobilior was sent against them with nearly 30,000 men, and the Segedians fled to the Arevaci, who chose a Segedian named Carus as their general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When embassies later reached Rome, the Arevaci offered to pay a fixed penalty but asked to go back to the terms agreed in Gracchus' time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nobilior's men had brought home such reports of unbroken battles and heavy losses that recruits avoided enrolment and officers would not serve, until the young Scipio Aemilianus asked to be sent himself and shamed the rest into volunteering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 35.1, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/35*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 43, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-9/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 44, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-9/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 45, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-9/. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 35.2, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/35*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 35.4, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/35*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Bronce_de_Botorrita_I.jpg/1280px-Bronce_de_Botorrita_I.jpg","title":"Inscribed bronze plaques from Botorrita, in the Ebro valley","desc":"The text is in the Celtiberian language and its own script, so this is the people of these wars writing about their own affairs rather than a Roman account of them.","alt":"Two long green bronze plaques covered in closely inscribed lettering, mounted in a clear museum case.","credit":"Photograph by ecelan, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronce_de_Botorrita_I.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Romans call it the fiery war?","a":"Because the engagements were uninterrupted, the fighting stopping only at nightfall and checked only by winter, where a war in Greece was usually decided by one battle or two."},{"q":"Why did both sides think the treaty was on their side?","a":"Because Gracchus had forbidden new cities but not the fortifying of old ones, and Rome had released them from tribute and levy — with the reservation that exemptions held only during the pleasure of the Roman people."},{"q":"Why did Romans avoid enrolment for this war?","a":"Because Nobilior’s men had brought home reports of unbroken battles and heavy losses, until Scipio Aemilianus asked to be sent and shamed the rest into volunteering."}]},
{"id":"rm-263","num":263,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Appian says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced the worst Roman act of the Spanish campaigns, when a praetor promised land to men who had asked for peace and then had them disarmed and killed.","questions":["Early in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a leader named Punicus put two praetors to flight and killed about 6,000 Romans, and his successor took standards that were paraded through Celtiberia in derision.","A praetor prosecuted for his conduct in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saved himself, Livy's summary says, by embracing his two young sons and his ward and speaking so pitiably that the case was dropped."],"answer":"Lusitanian War","answerText":"Lusitanian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Outbreak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">155 BCE, under Punicus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Galba</span><span class=\"dt-v\">arrived 151 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Massacre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 divisions, killed in turn</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","spain"],"abstract":"The <b>Lusitanian War</b> was the fighting in the west of the peninsula that ran alongside the Celtiberian wars, and Appian says it opened with a leader named Punicus ravaging the fields of Rome's subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two praetors were put to flight and about 6,000 Romans killed, among them the quaestor Terentius Varro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Punicus was killed by a stone to the head, and his successor Caesarus, beaten by Mummius and then rallying against a disorderly pursuit, killed some 9,000 Romans and took standards the Lusitanians carried in derision through Celtiberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mummius drilled the 5,000 men he had left and would not go down into the plain until their courage had come back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Servius Sulpicius Galba, arriving a few years later, marched about 90 km (55 miles) in a day and a night, sent his tired army straight into battle, broke the enemy and then lost about 7,000 men in a scattered pursuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When Lusitanian envoys came asking to renew an earlier treaty, Galba received them kindly and said that poor soil and penury had forced them to rob, offering good land for their poor in three settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They left their homes and gathered where he directed, and he divided them into three parts on three separate plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He went to each in turn, told them as friends to lay down their arms, ringed them with a ditch and sent in swordsmen, so that each division was destroyed while the next still knew nothing of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Appian says he avenged treachery with treachery in a manner unworthy of a Roman, kept most of the plunder although he was already one of the richest men in Rome, and let a few escape, among them Viriathus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy's summary has him prosecuted over the survivors he had sold into slavery, and saved when he embraced his two young sons and his ward and spoke so pitiably that the case was dropped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 56, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-12/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 57, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-12/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 58, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-12/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 59, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-12/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 60, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-12/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 49, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-46-50/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Cit%C3%A2nia_de_Briteiros_2.jpg/1280px-Cit%C3%A2nia_de_Briteiros_2.jpg","title":"A street between drystone walls at the Citânia de Briteiros","desc":"A hillfort of the kind the peoples of the Iberian north-west lived in. Briteiros lies north of the country Appian calls Lusitania, so this is the settlement form of the region rather than a place named in the war.","alt":"A rocky path climbing between two low drystone walls among oak trees on a hillside.","credit":"Photograph by CTHOE, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cit%C3%A2nia_de_Briteiros_2.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Mummius keep his army out of the plain?","a":"Because he had 5,000 men left after a disaster and drilled them, refusing to go down until their courage had come back."},{"q":"Why did the offer of land work as a trap?","a":"Because Galba said poor soil and penury had forced them to rob and offered three settlements, then divided them across three plains so each was destroyed while the next knew nothing."},{"q":"Why did the prosecution of Galba fail?","a":"Because he embraced his two young sons and his ward and spoke so pitiably that the case was dropped."}]},
{"id":"rm-264","num":264,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Appian says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> saved a trapped army by telling it to scatter the moment he mounted his horse, then held the Romans on the field for two days alone.","questions":["A treaty ratified at Rome made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a friend of the Roman people, until the next commander wrote home that it was unworthy of the dignity of Rome.","Three trusted friends stabbed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the throat as he slept in his armour, that being the only part of him a Roman bribe could reach."],"answer":"Viriathus","answerText":"Viriathus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rose</span><span class=\"dt-v\">147 BCE, after Galba's massacre</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 140 BCE, in his sleep</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 years, without a mutiny</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","warfare","history","rome","spain"],"abstract":"<b>Viriathus</b> was the Lusitanian leader who fought Rome for eight years, and Appian first shows him among the survivors of Galba's massacre, trapped by Gaius Vetilius and about to accept terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reminded them of the bad faith of the Romans, of oaths already broken, and that this army was made of men who had escaped the perjuries of Galba and Lucullus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They chose him as their leader, and he drew them up as though to fight but ordered them to scatter the moment he mounted his horse and to make their own way to the town of Tribola.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Keeping a thousand men, he spent two days attacking and retreating on swift horses until he judged the rest safe, then slipped off by night along paths the armoured Romans could not follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That escape brought him fame and reinforcements from the tribes around, and it let him carry on the war for eight years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At Erisana he drove a Roman army among cliffs from which there was no escape and, Appian says, was not arrogant in the hour of victory but made an agreement to end the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was ratified at Rome, Viriathus was declared a friend of the Roman people, and his followers were confirmed in the land they held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Then Quintus Servilius Caepio, brother of the man who had made the treaty, wrote home that it was unworthy of the dignity of the Roman people, and the senate first let him harass Viriathus secretly before allowing the treaty to be broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Caepio bribed three of his most trusted friends, sent to negotiate, and they stabbed him in the throat as he slept in his armour, that being the only part of him unprotected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Appian says his men burned him on a high pyre with sacrifices and singing and held contests at his tomb, mourning a commander who never took the lion's share and in whose mixed army there had never once been a mutiny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 61, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-13/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 62, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-13/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 69, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-14/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 70, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-14/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 74, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-15/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 75, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-15/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Nt-Viriato_Viseu.jpg/1280px-Nt-Viriato_Viseu.jpg","title":"The monument to Viriato at Viseu, in Portugal","desc":"A modern bronze on a granite boulder, with a small round shield of the Iberian kind. No ancient likeness of him survives, so every image of Viriathus is a later imagining.","alt":"A bronze warrior with a small round shield standing on a granite boulder, with three more bronze figures climbing the rock below him.","credit":"Photograph by Nuno Tavares, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nt-Viriato_Viseu.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the survivors choose him as their leader?","a":"Because he reminded them of Roman bad faith and of oaths already broken, this army being made of men who had escaped the perjuries of Galba and Lucullus."},{"q":"Why did the escape from Tribola make his name?","a":"Because a thousand men held the Romans two days while the rest got clear, and then slipped away by night along paths the armoured Romans could not follow."},{"q":"Why was a ratified treaty broken?","a":"Because Caepio wrote home that it was unworthy of the dignity of the Roman people, and the senate first let him harass Viriathus secretly and then allowed the treaty to be broken."}]},
{"id":"rm-265","num":265,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the senate handed one of its own consuls over to the enemy naked for a treaty it had not authorised, and the enemy refused to take him.","questions":["Scipio Aemilianus ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by refusing battle and starving the town instead, ringing it with a ditch, a palisade and a wall more than twice its own circuit.","Appian says the defenders in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ate boiled hides and then their own dead, and asked for a day to arrange their deaths before they would assemble."],"answer":"Numantine War","answerText":"Numantine War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mancinus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">surrendered 137 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE, by starvation</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Numantia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8,000 fighters against 60,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","warfare","history","rome","spain"],"abstract":"The <b>Numantine War</b> was the long struggle for one Celtiberian town, and Appian says Rome's difficulty began with two commanders who made terms and then denied them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Quintus Pompeius advised the Numantines publicly to surrender at discretion and told them privately what he would really take, then denied the whole agreement once his successor had arrived, and the senate voted to continue the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gaius Hostilius Mancinus, beaten again and again, put out his fires and fled by night on a false rumour, was surrounded at daybreak in an unfortified place, and swore to terms like the ones before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rome was indignant, and the senate resolved to give him up, following the example of the fathers who once delivered twenty generals to the Samnites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mancinus was taken to Spain and handed over naked to the Numantines, who refused to receive him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The people then made Scipio Aemilianus consul a second time, the tribunes repealing the law on the age limit for the year and re-enacting it afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He found the army idle and demoralised, expelled the traders, the harlots and the diviners, allowed each man a spit, a brass kettle and one cup, forbade beds, and was himself the first to sleep on straw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He refused every offer of battle and shut the town in instead, ringing a circuit of about 4 km (2.5 miles) with works more than twice as long, a ditch and palisade, a wall and towers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Inside, Appian says, they ate boiled hides, then the bodies of their dead, and at last the weaker among the living, before surrendering and asking for a day to arrange their own deaths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He reckons 8,000 fighting men at the outset against a last Roman army of 60,000; fifty were kept for the triumph, the rest sold, the town razed, and its captor took the name Numantinus to set beside Africanus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 79, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-16/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 80, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-16/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 83, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-17/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 84, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-17/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 85, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-17/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 90, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-18/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 96, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-20/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 97, trans. Horace White, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-20/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Numancia.jpg/1280px-Numancia.jpg","title":"Reconstructed timber towers on the site of Numantia","desc":"These towers and the wall beside them are a modern reconstruction built for visitors to the excavated site at Garray. The ancient remains are the low stone footings underneath.","alt":"Two thatched timber towers and a section of mud wall standing over stone footings, with wooden visitor decking in front.","credit":"Photograph by Multitud, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Numancia.jpg"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome’s own commanders make the war harder?","a":"Because two of them made terms and then denied them — Pompeius disowning the agreement once his successor arrived, and Mancinus swearing to terms after a rout — so the town would trust nothing."},{"q":"Why was Mancinus handed over naked and not received?","a":"Because the senate followed the precedent of delivering generals to the Samnites, and the Numantines refused him, so the surrender settled nothing."},{"q":"Why did Scipio begin by expelling traders and diviners?","a":"Because he found the army idle and demoralised, and stripped it to a spit, a kettle and one cup with no beds, sleeping on straw himself."}]},
{"id":"rm-266","num":266,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Polybius says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set out to be thought temperate at a time when Cato was complaining that pretty boys fetched more at Rome than fields did.","questions":["Owing his adoptive father's sisters 25 talents each, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had three years by law to pay and told his banker to pay both in full within ten months.","Standing beside Polybius as Carthage burned, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called it a glorious moment and said he dreaded that the same doom would one day fall on Rome."],"answer":"Scipio Aemilianus","answerText":"Scipio Aemilianus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">destroyed 146 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Numantia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">destroyed 133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dowries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 talents each, paid in 10 months</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Scipio Aemilianus</b> was the son of Aemilius Paullus adopted into the house of the Scipios, and Polybius, who knew him, says his first ambition was to be thought temperate, a reputation he had established within about five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was an easy course to pursue at Rome then, Polybius says, because many young men had given themselves to boys, to courtesans, to music and to banquets since the war with Perseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He quotes Cato saying it was the surest sign of the republic's decline when pretty boys fetched more than fields and jars of caviar more than ploughmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two men met young: Scipio took Polybius by the hand and said he wished for the day when Polybius would join his life to his, and from then on he never left his side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When his adoptive grandmother Aemilia died he inherited her fortune and gave her whole equipage at once to his own mother, long separated from her husband and unable to keep up her rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Polybius says the women who saw her drive out in it prayed every blessing on him, and that in Rome this was a marvel, since nobody there gives anything away if he can help it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He then owed his adoptive father's sisters the unpaid halves of their dowries, 25 talents each, which Roman law allowed him three years to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He ordered his banker to pay both in full within ten months, and their husbands went to see him, certain that there had been a mistake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He destroyed Carthage and then Numantia, and one account has him weep openly for his enemies as the first burned, quoting Homer's line that a day will come when sacred Troy shall perish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius stood beside him there and records that he took his hand, called it a glorious moment, and said he had a dread foreboding that the same doom would one day be pronounced on his own country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.25, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.24, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.26, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.27, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.22, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 38.21, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/38*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a reputation for temperance easy to win at Rome then?","a":"Because since the war with Perseus many young men had given themselves to boys, to courtesans, to music and to banquets, so restraint stood out."},{"q":"Why did paying the dowries early astonish the husbands?","a":"Because Roman law allowed him three years and he ordered both paid in full within ten months, so they came to him certain there had been a mistake."},{"q":"Why did he weep at the burning of Carthage?","a":"Because he had a dread foreboding that the same doom would one day be pronounced on his own country, which he told Polybius standing beside him."}]},
{"id":"rm-267","num":267,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Cicero called Sicily the first place ever named a province, the beginning of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by which Rome governed conquered territory through magistrates, tribute and settlements drawn up by senators.","questions":["Livy records six praetors appointed in 197 BCE because the empire's bounds had widened, an early step in the growth of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that turned conquest into permanent administration.","Ten senators sent to Spain, a consul with ten lieutenants organising Asia, and Macedonia cut into four cantons are all examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> taking shape across the Mediterranean."],"answer":"Roman provincial system","answerText":"Roman provincial system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st province, per Cicero</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Praetors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">six from 197 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Augustus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 consular, 10 praetorian</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","institution","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Roman provincial system</b> was the machinery by which Rome governed the territory it had taken, and Cicero says Sicily was the first place ever to be called a province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls the provinces a great ornament to the empire, and says Sicily first taught Rome's ancestors how glorious a thing it was to rule over foreign nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere in the same speech he compares the tributary nations and provinces to farms belonging to the Roman people, which states plainly enough what the arrangement was for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A province began as a sphere of duty allotted to a magistrate rather than a mapped territory, and Livy records six praetors elected for the first time in 197 BCE, in consequence of the increase in the provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What a province supplied mattered as much as who governed it, and Cicero describes Sicily as both a granary to supply Rome with corn and a harbour for her fleets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Organising newly won ground was a formal act, and Appian says the Romans by their custom sent ten senators to the provinces of Spain to settle their affairs on a peace basis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strabo reports that Manius Aquillius came to Asia as consul with ten lieutenants and organised the province into the form of government that still endured in Strabo's own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Livy's account of the Macedonian settlement shows the pattern in detail, the country broken into four cantons, each with a capital where its council met and its tribute was deposited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Under Augustus the provinces were divided in two, Strabo distinguishing the provinces of Caesar, which received legates and procurators, from the provinces of the people, which received praetors or proconsuls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Two of the people's provinces were consular, Libya and the part of Asia this side the Halys, and ten were praetorian, Sicily and Macedonia among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Cicero, <i>Against Verres</i> 2.2.2, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=2. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Against Verres</i> 2.2.7, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=7. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 32.27, trans. Canon Roberts (London: J. M. Dent & Sons, 1905), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=32:chapter=27. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Against Verres</i> 2.2.3, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=3. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Spanish Wars</i> 99, trans. Horace White (London: Macmillan, 1899), https://www.livius.org/sources/content/appian/appian-the-spanish-wars/appian-the-spanish-wars-20/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.1.38, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1929), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/14A*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.29, trans. Canon Roberts (London: J. M. Dent & Sons, 1905), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=45:chapter=29. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 17.3.25, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 8 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1932), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/17C*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a province at first not a piece of territory?","a":"Because it began as a sphere of duty allotted to a magistrate, which is why more praetors had to be elected as the provinces increased."},{"q":"Why does Cicero call the provinces farms?","a":"Because he compares the tributary nations to estates belonging to the Roman people, which states plainly what the arrangement was for."},{"q":"Why was organising new ground a formal act?","a":"Because ten senators were sent to settle a conquered country’s affairs, and the form given to Asia by Aquillius and his ten lieutenants still endured in Strabo’s day."}]},
{"id":"rm-268","num":268,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Polybius says the censors let out contracts for rivers, harbours, mines and lands, and the men who bought them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, took partners and sureties to spread the risk.","questions":["Livy records one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> loading old ships with worthless cargo and sinking them at sea, then claiming from the treasury for a cargo declared at many times its value.","Cicero called the revenues the sinews of the republic and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who gathered them the prop and support of every other order at Rome."],"answer":"publicani","answerText":"publicani","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Censors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">let contracts, 5-year terms</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cicero's speech</span><span class=\"dt-v\">66 BCE, on Asia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Macedonian mines</span><span class=\"dt-v\">closed to them, 167 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>publicani</b> were the private contractors who did for the Roman state the work it kept no permanent staff to do, and Polybius says the censors let out a vast number of contracts throughout Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists navigable rivers, harbours, gardens, mines and lands among the things farmed out, in fact everything that formed part of the Roman dominion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some men bought a contract from the censors outright, others were their partners, others stood surety for them, and others pledged their own fortunes to the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Senate held the advantage over all of them, since it could extend a deadline, relieve a contractor when an accident occurred, or release him from work that proved impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their business went well beyond gathering money, and Cicero describes the companies keeping large households of slaves in the salt-works, the fields, the harbours and the custom-houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The men who ran them were Roman knights, and Cicero says letters came from Asia daily to men of that order whose whole property was employed in collecting Rome's revenues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He calls the revenues the sinews of the republic and the order that collected them the prop and support of all the other orders, which is a politician addressing an audience of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same speech shows how far the reach went, since when many lost large fortunes in Asia all credit failed at Rome and payments were hindered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The system invited fraud, and Livy records contractors putting worthless cargo on old ships, sinking them at sea, and then declaring the loss at many times its real value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Senate could see the cost as well, closing Macedonia's mines and royal domains because they could not be worked without a tax-farmer, and where he flourished the law lost its authority or the subjects their liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.17, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 16, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=16. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 4, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=4. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 17, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=17. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 19, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=19. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 25.3, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=25:chapter=3. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.18, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=45:chapter=18. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome contract out the work of the state?","a":"Because it kept no permanent staff for it, so the censors let out rivers, harbours, gardens, mines and lands — everything that formed part of the Roman dominion."},{"q":"Why did the Senate keep the advantage over the companies?","a":"Because it could extend a deadline, relieve a contractor when an accident occurred, or release him from work that proved impossible."},{"q":"Why did the Senate close Macedonia’s mines rather than farm them?","a":"Because they could not be worked without a tax-farmer, and where he flourished the law lost its authority or the subjects their liberty."}]},
{"id":"rm-269","num":269,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Sicily paid a tenth of its crop, Spain a twentieth and Macedonia half its old royal tribute, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Republic was never one system but a patchwork of local bargains.","questions":["Cicero praised his ancestors for imposing no new tax on Sicily and keeping the law of Hiero, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> working by inheritance rather than by any scheme designed at Rome.","Harbour dues, tithes of grain and a charge on pasture land were among the sources Cicero names when describing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, all of them ruinable by a single rumour of war."],"answer":"provincial taxation","answerText":"provincial taxation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a 10th of the crop, by Hiero's law</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a 20th, regulated 171 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Macedonia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">half the king's tribute, 167 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","province","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Provincial taxation</b> under the Republic was not one system but many, and Cicero says the land of most Sicilian cities paid a tenth, as it had done before Rome ruled the island, by the Sicilians' own laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two federate cities, the city of the Mamertines and Tauromenium, had tenths that were never put up for auction, and five more were free and enfranchised, so exemption was as much a part of the arrangement as payment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Land taken in war had become the public domain of the Roman people, and that domain was let out to farmers by the censors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cicero praises his ancestors for imposing no new tax on Sicilian land and keeping the law of Hiero, with the contracts sold at the same season and in the same place on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He says that law was framed so carefully that no more than a tenth could be extorted from the cultivator against his will, and equally that the cultivator could not cheat the collector of a grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Spain paid differently, and Livy records the Senate ruling that a Roman magistrate should not value the corn nor compel the Spaniards to sell their twentieths at whatever price he chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same decree forbade officers being forced on Spanish towns to collect taxes and tribute, a concession won by envoys who complained on their knees of robbery by Roman magistrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Macedonia after its defeat paid Rome half the tribute it had paid its own king, divided among four cantons that each had a council, with Illyria treated the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Revenue came from more than crops, Cicero naming harbour dues, tenths and a tax on pasture land among the things a rumour of war could destroy in a season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He adds that the other provinces yielded barely enough to pay for their own protection, while Asia surpassed every other country in soil, pasture and goods for export.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Cicero, <i>Against Verres</i> 2.3.13, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=3:section=13. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Against Verres</i> 2.3.14, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=3:section=14. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Against Verres</i> 2.3.20, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=3:section=20. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 43.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=43:chapter=2. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.18, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=45:chapter=18. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 15, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=15. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 14, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=14. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Modius_%28grain_measure%29_with_inscriptions_%2851357279746%29.jpg/960px-Modius_%28grain_measure%29_with_inscriptions_%2851357279746%29.jpg","title":"A Roman modius, the standard measure for grain","desc":"A bronze modius with internal cross-bars and a punched inscription, from Herculaneum and dated by the museum to 50–79 CE, so it is imperial rather than Republican; a tenth or a twentieth of a crop was reckoned in measures like this one.","credit":"Photograph by Tyler Bell, CC BY 2.0. Museo Archeologico Nazionale di Napoli, MANN 6531. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modius_%28grain_measure%29_with_inscriptions_%2851357279746%29.jpg","alt":"A dark bronze cylindrical vessel with a rim, two cross-bars forming a cross inside the mouth, and faint punched lettering on its side, standing on a pale surface."},"why":[{"q":"Why is exemption as much part of the system as payment?","a":"Because two federate cities had tenths never put up for auction and five more were free and enfranchised, so what a community owed depended on how it had come under Rome."},{"q":"Why did Rome keep the law of Hiero in Sicily?","a":"Because it was framed so carefully that no more than a tenth could be extorted from the cultivator against his will, and equally that he could not cheat the collector of a grain."},{"q":"Why did Spanish envoys have to complain on their knees?","a":"Because Roman magistrates were valuing the corn and compelling sales at whatever price they chose, and forcing officers on the towns to collect the taxes."}]},
{"id":"rm-270","num":270,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Plutarch says Paullus brought so much money into the treasury that Romans stopped paying war taxes, one measure of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that conquest was pouring into the city.","questions":["Bronze couches, tapestries, silver salvers and hired harp-girls arrived with the army returning from Asia, which is how Livy dates the moment the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began changing how Romans lived.","Pliny lists the gold, silver and coin held in the treasury seven years before the Third Punic War, a rare figure for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that conquest had accumulated."],"answer":"wealth of empire","answerText":"wealth of empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pydna triumph</span><span class=\"dt-v\">167 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Coin displayed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">120,000,000 sesterces</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treasury</span><span class=\"dt-v\">157 BCE, per Pliny</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","warfare","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>wealth of empire</b> reached Rome first as plunder carried in a triumph, and Livy reports that Valerius Antias put the gold and silver coin in Aemilius Paullus' procession at 120,000,000 sesterces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy adds that the true figure must have been higher, since Antias' own count of the wagons and the weight each carried implies more than he states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second sum as large again was said to have been spent in the war or scattered by Perseus in flight, money the Macedonian kings had gathered over thirty years from their mines and other revenues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says so much was brought into the public treasury by Paullus that the Roman people no longer needed to pay special taxes until the consulships of Hirtius and Pansa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pliny records what the treasury held seven years before the Third Punic War: 17,410 pounds of uncoined gold, 22,070 pounds of silver, and 6,135,400 sesterces in coin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Regular revenue mattered more than plunder in the long run, and Cicero says Asia alone surpassed every other country in the fertility of its soil, the vastness of its pastures and the multitude of things it exported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other provinces, he says, yielded barely enough to pay for their own protection, so the profit of empire was concentrated rather than spread evenly across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The money changed how Romans lived, and Livy dates the arrival of foreign luxury in the city to the return of the army that had served in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those soldiers brought bronze couches, costly coverlets, tapestry, pedestal tables and silver salvers, and banquets were made more attractive by girls hired to play the harp, sing and dance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius, watching the next generation, says many young Romans paid a talent for a favourite and three hundred drachmas for a jar of caviar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 45.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=45:chapter=40. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paullus</i> 38, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 33.17, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=33:chapter=17. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 14, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=6. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.25, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/The_Triumph_of_Aemilius_Paulus_MET_DT11657.jpg/1280px-The_Triumph_of_Aemilius_Paulus_MET_DT11657.jpg","title":"Carle Vernet's Triumph of Aemilius Paulus","desc":"Painted in 1789, more than nineteen centuries after the event, so the arch, the temples and the crowd are the painter's Rome rather than the city Paullus rode through; what it does show plainly is the shape of a triumph, with the general in his chariot behind the spoils and the captives.","credit":"Carle Vernet, 1789. Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Triumph_of_Aemilius_Paulus_MET_DT11657.jpg","alt":"A wide neoclassical painting of a Roman triumphal procession passing under an arch, with soldiers, captives, carried spoils and a general standing in a chariot drawn by white horses."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Livy doubt a figure that already sounds enormous?","a":"Because Antias’ own count of the wagons and the weight each carried implies more than the 120,000,000 sesterces he states."},{"q":"Why was regular revenue worth more than plunder?","a":"Because the other provinces yielded barely enough to pay for their own protection, so the profit of empire was concentrated in Asia rather than spread across it."},{"q":"Why is one army’s return dated as the arrival of luxury?","a":"Because the men who had served in Asia brought bronze couches, coverlets, tapestry and silver salvers, with girls hired to play, sing and dance at banquets."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Syracuse taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">211 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Army from Asia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">187 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">155 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-271","num":271,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Livy says the statues carried home from Syracuse in 211 BCE began the Roman taste for Greek art, one strand of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that reshaped how the city looked and thought.","questions":["Carneades and Diogenes came from Athens as ambassadors and left the young men of Rome possessed about philosophy, which alarmed Cato and shows the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaching beyond objects.","Bronze couches, tapestries and silver salvers arrived with the army from Asia, and Livy treats them as part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that older Romans regarded as a corruption."],"answer":"Greek influence on Roman culture","answerText":"Greek influence on Roman culture","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","art","philosophy","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Greek influence on Roman culture</b> is dated by Livy to a particular moment, the capture of Syracuse in 211 BCE, after which Marcellus removed to Rome the statues and pictures in which the city abounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls this the beginning of Roman admiration for Greek works of art, and blames it for the reckless spoliation of treasure sacred and profane that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Rome before that neither had nor knew such elegant productions, being filled instead with barbaric arms and bloody spoils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The common people were pleased by what Marcellus brought, but older citizens preferred Fabius Maximus, who had left Tarentum's statues where they stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Objects were only part of it, and Livy dates the arrival of foreign luxury in the city to the return of the army that had served in Asia, with its bronze couches, coverlets, tapestry and silver salvers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ideas came the same way, and when Carneades and Diogenes arrived from Athens as ambassadors the most studious of Rome's youth became their devoted listeners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch says a report spread that a Greek of amazing talent had infused a tremendous passion into the young, who forsook their other pursuits and were possessed about philosophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cato was alarmed rather than pleased, and urged the Senate to settle the embassy's business so that the philosophers might go back to lecturing the sons of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not every leading Roman resisted, and Polybius says his own friendship with Scipio Aemilianus began in the loan of some books and conversation about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius adds that Scipio deliberately set himself to be temperate while many of his contemporaries had been infected by Greek laxity after the war with Perseus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 25.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=25:chapter=40. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marcellus</i> 21, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marcellus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=6. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cato the Elder</i> 22, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cato_Major*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.23, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.25, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why is one siege made the beginning of Roman taste?","a":"Because Marcellus removed to Rome the statues and pictures Syracuse abounded in, and Livy blames that for the reckless spoliation of treasure sacred and profane that followed."},{"q":"Why did older citizens prefer the general who took nothing?","a":"Because Fabius had left Tarentum’s statues where they stood, and to them a city filled with barbaric arms and bloody spoils was the Roman one."},{"q":"Why did Cato want the Athenian embassy sent home quickly?","a":"Because a report had spread that a Greek of amazing talent had infused a tremendous passion into the young, who forsook their other pursuits for philosophy."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Isthmian games</span><span class=\"dt-v\">196 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Paullus's tour</span><span class=\"dt-v\">167 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">155 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-272","num":272,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Flamininus settled Greek constitutions and presided at the Nemean games while Paullus toured Greece giving grain and oil, both of them displaying the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the ruling class.","questions":["Paullus ordered his own statue set on the pillar at Delphi meant for Perseus, a gesture in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and conquest are impossible to separate.","Fabius left the statues of Tarentum where they stood and Cato wanted the Athenian philosophers sent home, which shows how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was from being universal at Rome."],"answer":"philhellenism","answerText":"philhellenism","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","art","rome"],"abstract":"Roman <b>philhellenism</b> was the admiration of Greek culture professed by many of the Republic's leading men, and it was displayed in public as well as felt in private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Flamininus visited Chalcis and Magnesia, removed garrisons, restored constitutions and presided over the Nemean games at Argos, where he proclaimed the freedom of the Greeks a second time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then went from city to city settling their factions and establishing law, order and concord among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aemilius Paullus did much the same after Pydna, touring Greece, restoring popular governments and giving grain from the royal stores to some cities and oil to others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Olympia he said that Pheidias had moulded the Zeus of Homer, a remark Plutarch reports as being in every mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The same tour shows the other face of it, for at Delphi Paullus saw the pillar prepared for a golden statue of Perseus and ordered his own statue set there instead, saying the conquered should make room for their conquerors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Friendship could be genuine all the same, and Polybius says his intimacy with Scipio Aemilianus began in the loan of some books and grew until it was talked of in Italy and Greece alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nothing about it was universal, and Plutarch says that when Marcellus adorned Rome with Syracusan art the older citizens preferred Fabius Maximus, who had left Tarentum's statues untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cato went further, urging the Senate to conclude its business with the Athenian embassy so that Carneades and Diogenes could return to lecturing the sons of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polybius, who admired Scipio, still wrote that many young Romans had been infected by Greek laxity after the war with Perseus, so even a friendly witness treated the influence as double-edged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Flamininus</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Flamininus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aemilius Paullus</i> 28, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aemilius*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.23, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marcellus</i> 21, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 5 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1917), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marcellus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cato the Elder</i> 22, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cato_Major*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 31.25, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 6 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/31*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the admiration performed in public?","a":"Because it was policy as well as taste: Flamininus removed garrisons, restored constitutions and proclaimed the freedom of the Greeks at the Nemean games."},{"q":"Why is the pillar at Delphi the other face of it?","a":"Because Paullus found one prepared for a golden statue of Perseus and ordered his own set there instead, saying the conquered should make room for their conquerors."},{"q":"Why is even a friendly witness double-edged about it?","a":"Because Polybius, who admired Scipio, still wrote that many young Romans had been infected by Greek laxity after the war with Perseus."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">234 – 149 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">195 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Censor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">184 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-273","num":273,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Livy calls him a doughty fighter, a consummate general, an able lawyer and a most eloquent pleader, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one Roman of his age whose speeches still survived to be read.","questions":["As censor he had jewellery, furniture and plate above fifteen hundred drachmas assessed at ten times its value, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> attacked extravagance without banning it outright.","A senator who wanted Carneades and Diogenes sent back to lecturing the sons of Greece rather than the youth of Rome, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was at his most suspicious of Greek philosophy."],"answer":"Cato the Elder","answerText":"Cato the Elder","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","politics","law","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Cato the Elder</b> is described by Livy as a man of such ability and force of character that in whatever station he had been born he must have been successful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy calls him equally versed in the affairs of town and country, a doughty fighter in war, a consummate general in high command, an able lawyer in counsel and a most eloquent pleader in court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His eloquence outlived him, enshrined in writings on all sorts of subjects, among them a great number of speeches made in his own defence and against other men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch shows a harder side, reporting that Cato lent money to his own slaves so that they could buy boys, train them for a year at his expense and sell them on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He told his son that it was the part of a widow rather than a man to lessen his substance, and said a man deserved glory like a god if his final inventory showed more than he had inherited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As censor he attacked extravagance sideways rather than head on, having apparel, equipages, jewellery, furniture and plate worth more than fifteen hundred drachmas assessed at ten times their value and then taxed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Speaking against the repeal of the Oppian law he said the community suffered from two opposite vices, avarice and luxury, pestilential diseases that had ruined all great empires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same speech he called it a bad day for the city when the statues were brought from Syracuse, and complained that too many Romans now laughed at the clay images of their own gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Carneades and Diogenes arrived from Athens he urged the Senate to settle their business quickly so that the philosophers might return to lecturing the sons of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was not simply hostile to provincials, and Livy records the deputies of Hither Spain choosing him as their counsel when they came to Rome complaining of robbery by Roman magistrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.40, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=40. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cato the Elder</i> 21, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cato_Major*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cato the Elder</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cato_Major*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 34.4, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=34:chapter=4. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cato the Elder</i> 22, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cato_Major*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 43.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=43:chapter=2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he tax luxury rather than forbid it?","a":"Because as censor he had apparel, equipages, jewellery, furniture and plate above fifteen hundred drachmas assessed at ten times their value, which attacked extravagance sideways."},{"q":"Why did he link the statues from Syracuse to Rome’s decline?","a":"Because he complained that too many Romans now laughed at the clay images of their own gods, so the imported art was a change of loyalty as well as of taste."},{"q":"Why is he not simply an enemy of provincials?","a":"Because the deputies of Hither Spain chose him as their counsel when they came to Rome complaining of robbery by Roman magistrates."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Affair</span><span class=\"dt-v\">186 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Implicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 7,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 times a month, not 3 a year</span></div>","id":"rm-274","num":274,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Livy says the consuls of 186 BCE were diverted from armies and provinces by a domestic conspiracy, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed put thousands of initiates on trial across Italy.","questions":["A freedwoman told the consul that the rites had once admitted only women on three days a year, until a Campanian priestess changed them, the account that opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Livy's telling.","More than seven thousand people of both sexes were said to be implicated, and the executions outnumbered the imprisonments, which gives the scale of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across Italy."],"answer":"Bacchanalian affair","answerText":"Bacchanalian affair","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","religion","law","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Bacchanalian affair</b> was the Roman Senate's suppression of the Bacchic cult in Italy, and Livy says the consuls of that year were diverted from the armies, the wars and the provinces by the need to put down a domestic conspiracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He traces the rites to a low-born Greek who went first into Etruria, a hedge-priest and wizard who ran secret nocturnal mysteries rather than teaching a superstition openly for money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wine and feasting drew followers, and Livy says that once all sense of modesty was gone every kind of debauchery followed, each man finding the pleasure he was most prone to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The thing reached Rome like a contagious disease, and the consul learned of it through a young man whose mother and stepfather meant to get a hold over him by having him initiated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A freedwoman, Hispala Fecenia, gave the account of the cult's origin, saying it had once been confined to women, held by day on three fixed days a year, with matrons chosen as priestesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> She said a Campanian priestess, Paculla Annia, admitted men for the first time, initiated her own two sons, made the rite nocturnal and held it five times a month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Senate, alarmed at secret conspiracies and nocturnal gatherings, armed the consuls with extraordinary powers, protected the two informants and offered rewards to bring others forward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Speaking to the people the consul argued that the association still confined itself to individual crimes but was creeping on, and that an assembly gathered in darkness would soon face the one convened in daylight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than seven thousand of both sexes were said to be implicated, and four men were named as the authors of the crimes and the founders of the cult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those merely initiated were imprisoned and those who had committed outrage or murder were put to death, the executed outnumbering the imprisoned, while convicted women were handed to their families for punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.8, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.9, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=9. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.13, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=13. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.14, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=14. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.16, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=16. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.17, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=17. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.18, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=18. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the Senate treat a cult as a conspiracy?","a":"Because it was alarmed at secret associations and nocturnal gatherings, and the consul argued that an assembly gathered in darkness would soon face the one convened in daylight."},{"q":"Why was the freedwoman’s account decisive?","a":"Because she described how the rite had changed: once confined to women and held by day three times a year, until a Campanian priestess admitted men, made it nocturnal and held it five times a month."},{"q":"Why were the punishments graded as they were?","a":"Because those merely initiated were imprisoned while those who had committed outrage or murder were put to death, and convicted women were handed to their families."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oppian law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">215 BCE, repealed 195 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lex Fannia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">161 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cato censor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">184 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-275","num":275,"category":"Rome and the Mediterranean","question":"Gellius records dinners capped at a hundred asses on festival days and thirty on ten more each month, the arithmetic by which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tried to hold private spending down.","questions":["The Oppian law forbade a woman more than half an ounce of gold or a carriage within a mile of a town, the best-known example of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the one that was repealed.","Cato told the assembly that avarice and luxury had ruined all great empires, which is the argument on which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Rome always rested."],"answer":"sumptuary law","answerText":"sumptuary law","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","law","politics","rome"],"abstract":"Roman <b>sumptuary law</b> restricted private spending on food, dress and display, and Gellius says early Roman frugality was secured not only by training at home but by public penalties and the provisions of numerous laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He cites an old decree of the Senate requiring leading citizens to swear before the consuls that they would spend no more than a hundred and twenty asses on a dinner, beyond vegetables, bread and wine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same decree bound them to serve native rather than foreign wine and to use no more than a hundred pounds' weight of silverware at table.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The law of Fannius that followed allowed a hundred asses a day at named games and festivals, thirty on ten further days each month and ten on all the rest, while the Licinian law later conceded two hundred for weddings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy dates the appetite these laws were answering to the return of the army that had served in Asia, which brought bronze couches, costly coverlets, tapestry and silver salvers into the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The best known was the Oppian law, passed when the strain of the Punic War was worst, forbidding a woman more than half an ounce of gold, a dress of several colours, or a carriage within a mile of a town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When two tribunes proposed its repeal the matrons filled the streets and blocked the approaches to the Forum, asking to resume their adornments now that private fortunes were growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cato spoke against the repeal, saying the community suffered from two opposite vices, avarice and luxury, pestilential diseases that had proved the ruin of all great empires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He said it had been a bad day for the city when the statues were brought from Syracuse, and that he preferred Rome's clay gods to the works admired at Athens and Corinth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As censor he taxed rather than forbade, having apparel, equipages, jewellery, furniture and plate above fifteen hundred drachmas assessed at ten times their worth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aulus Gellius, <i>Attic Nights</i> 2.24, trans. John C. Rolfe, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 1 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1927), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Gellius/2*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 39.6, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=39:chapter=6. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 34.1, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=34:chapter=1. [Open access]","Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 34.4, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=34:chapter=4. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cato the Elder</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 2 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cato_Major*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were leading citizens made to swear an oath about dinners?","a":"Because early frugality was secured by public penalties as well as training at home, so a decree bound them to a hundred and twenty asses, native wine and limited silverware."},{"q":"Why was the Oppian law contested once the war was over?","a":"Because it had been passed when the strain of the Punic War was worst, and the matrons filled the streets asking to resume their adornments now that private fortunes were growing."},{"q":"Why did Cato argue that avarice and luxury go together?","a":"Because he called them two opposite vices afflicting one community, pestilential diseases that had proved the ruin of all great empires."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Holding cap</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 iugera</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet service</span><span class=\"dt-v\">census under 400 drachmae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gracchus spoke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-278","num":278,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Tiberius told the crowd that the wild beasts of Italy each had a lair while the men who fought for it had only air and light, the sharpest statement of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that survives.","questions":["Appian says the Italian people dwindled in numbers and strength, oppressed by penury, taxes and military service, while slaves multiplied, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one sentence.","Plutarch says the ejected poor lost their appetite for service and neglected the rearing of children, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a military problem as much as a social one."],"answer":"decline of the Italian smallholder","answerText":"decline of the Italian smallholder","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","agriculture","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>decline of the Italian smallholder</b> is the process ancient writers put at the root of the Republic's troubles, and Appian describes it as a reversal of Rome's own intention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Land taken in war was meant to multiply the Italian race, which Rome considered the most laborious of peoples, so that it might have plenty of allies at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead the rich took the greater part of the undistributed land and worked it with slaves and herdsmen, deliberately, so that free labourers should not be drawn out of agriculture into the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Slave families grew because their children were exempt from military service, while the Italian people dwindled in numbers and strength, oppressed by penury, taxes and service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If the poor had any respite from these evils they passed the time in idleness, Appian says, because the land was held by men who employed slaves rather than freemen to till it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Romans grew troubled at this, he adds, fearing both that they would run short of allies of Italian stock and that the government itself might be endangered by so vast a number of slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch tells the same story as a sequence of evictions, with rich men outbidding the poor for the rent of public land and later transferring the tenancies to themselves through fictitious persons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ejected, he says, no longer showed themselves eager for military service and neglected the bringing up of children, so that Italy soon felt a dearth of freemen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Property decided where a man served, and Polybius says those whose census fell below four hundred drachmae were employed in the fleet rather than in the legions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tiberius Gracchus put the case in public, saying the wild beasts that roam over Italy each have a cave or lair, while the men who fight and die for Italy enjoy the common air and light and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.7, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.8, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Rome want a numerous Italian peasantry in the first place?","a":"Because the land taken in war was meant to multiply the Italian race, considered the most laborious of peoples, so that Rome might have plenty of allies at home."},{"q":"Why did the rich prefer slaves to free labourers?","a":"Because free men were drawn out of agriculture into the army, while slave families grew since their children were exempt from military service."},{"q":"Why did the Romans grow alarmed at their own arrangement?","a":"Because they feared both running short of allies of Italian stock and that the government itself might be endangered by so vast a number of slaves."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-v\">prosperous 60 years, then revolt</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Varro</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 kinds of instrument</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">135 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-279","num":279,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Varro divided the tools of farming into the articulate, the inarticulate and the mute, a classification that says plainly what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was thought to be.","questions":["Cato advised the owner to sell worn-out oxen, an old wagon, old tools, an old slave and a sickly slave, which is the household economy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one line.","Diodorus says the branded herdsmen of Sicily were given neither food nor clothing and so robbed to live, the condition that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dangerous as well as profitable."],"answer":"slavery in Italian agriculture","answerText":"slavery in Italian agriculture","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","history","agriculture","politics","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Slavery in Italian agriculture</b> grew with the estates it worked, and Appian says the rich used slaves as labourers and herdsmen precisely so that free men should not be drawn out of farming into the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Owning slaves also brought great gain from the multitude of their children, who increased because they were exempt from military service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch describes the result from the other side, saying that as the poor were ejected Italy felt a dearth of freemen and was filled with gangs of foreign slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The agricultural writers treat these people as stock, and Cato tells the owner to sell worn-out oxen, blemished cattle, wool, hides, an old wagon, old tools, an old slave and a sickly slave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The master, he adds, should have the selling habit rather than the buying habit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Varro sets out the classification most often quoted, dividing the means by which land is tilled into instruments that are articulate, inarticulate and mute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The articulate are the slaves, the inarticulate the cattle and the mute the vehicles, though he notes that agriculture is carried on by freemen too, poor men working with their families or hired for the heavier tasks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus describes the scale it reached in Sicily, where owners grew rich after Carthage fell and bought slaves in droves, driven like herds of cattle and branded with marks burnt into their bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their masters gave them neither food nor clothing, he says, so that most were forced to rob and steal to live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The young were set to herding, armed, and left in the open day and night, until men in wolf and boar skins with clubs and mastiffs made the roads of Sicily unsafe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.7, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Cato the Elder, <i>On Agriculture</i> 2, trans. William Davis Hooper and Harrison Boyd Ash, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1934), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Cato/De_Agricultura/A*.html. [Open access]","Varro, <i>On Agriculture</i> 1.17, trans. William Davis Hooper and Harrison Boyd Ash, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1934), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Varro/de_Re_Rustica/1*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Historical Library</i> 34/35.2, English translation compiled from G. Booth (1814), F. Hoefer (1865) and new renderings and checked against the Greek, Attalus, https://www.attalus.org/translate/diodorus34.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the farming writers treat people as equipment?","a":"Because Varro divides the means of tilling land into articulate, inarticulate and mute instruments, the first being the slaves, and Cato lists an old slave among things to sell."},{"q":"Why were slaves in Sicily driven to robbery?","a":"Because their masters gave them neither food nor clothing, so most were forced to rob and steal to live."},{"q":"Why did that make the roads unsafe?","a":"Because the young were set to herding, armed, and left in the open day and night, going about in wolf and boar skins with clubs and mastiffs."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">135 – 132 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Under Cleon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70,000 slaves</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suppressed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Rupilius, 132 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-280","num":280,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"A Syrian slave who claimed the goddess had promised him a kingdom was crowned at Enna and led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the praetors could not put down.","questions":["Cleon raised a second rising in Sicily and, against everyone’s expectation, joined Eunus rather than fighting him, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became unmanageable.","Strabo says Enna suffered most of all and was only dislodged with difficulty, one town’s memory of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still current two generations later."],"answer":"First Servile War","answerText":"First Servile War","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","practice","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>First Servile War</b> began in Sicily, and Diodorus says the island had been prosperous for sixty years after the overthrow of Carthage, its owners buying slaves in droves and branding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rising itself started at Enna with Damophilus, a man of great wealth whose cruelty to his slaves his wife Megallis tried to exceed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The slaves consulted a Syrian from Apameia, a conjuror who claimed the goddess had told him in dreams that he should reign, and who had amused his own master's dinner guests with the boast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name was Eunus, and after the killing he put a diadem on his head, called his wife queen and chose councillors, among them an Achaean named Achaeus whom Diodorus calls a wise man and a good soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within three days he had six thousand men armed with axes, slings, bills and stakes burnt hard at one end, and before long above ten thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Cilician called Cleon then raised a second revolt, and everyone expected the two bands to destroy each other; instead Cleon obeyed Eunus in everything and served him as general with five thousand of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy's summary gives the war a larger shape, saying that Eunus opened the workhouses and expanded his band to the size of an army, that Cleon gathered seventy thousand, and that Roman armies were often beaten once the two had joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo, writing long afterwards, says Enna suffered most at the hands of Eunus and his runaway slaves, who were besieged there and dislodged only with difficulty, and that Catana and Tauromenium fared the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The praetors could not finish it and a consul was sent, and Livy's summary records that Publius Rupilius at last defeated the Sicilian runaway slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cicero shows what followed, since Rupilius drew up laws for Sicily with the advice of ten commissioners, and those laws still governed the island's courts in Cicero's own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Historical Library</i> 34/35.2, English translation compiled from G. Booth (1814), F. Hoefer (1865) and new renderings and checked against the Greek, Attalus, https://www.attalus.org/translate/diodorus34.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 56, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 6.2.6, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Strabo/6B*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 59, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Against Verres</i> 2.2.40, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0018:text=Ver.:actio=2:book=2:section=40. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Enna_-_Vue_de_la_Rocca_di_Cerere.jpg/1280px-Enna_-_Vue_de_la_Rocca_di_Cerere.jpg","title":"The rock at Enna, where the rising began","desc":"Diodorus places the outbreak at Enna and Strabo says the town suffered most of all, its slaves besieged on the height and dislodged only with difficulty; nothing in the view dates from the war, and the house, the road and the car park are modern.","credit":"Photograph by HaguardDuNord, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enna_-_Vue_de_la_Rocca_di_Cerere.jpg","alt":"A broad limestone outcrop with cut terraces and steps, standing above a wide plain of ploughed fields and low hills, with a small white house and a car park at its foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the slaves consult a conjuror before rising?","a":"Because Eunus claimed the goddess had told him in dreams that he should reign, which gave the rising a leader with a sanction rather than only a grievance."},{"q":"Why did the two rebel bands not destroy each other?","a":"Because everyone expected them to, and instead Cleon obeyed Eunus in everything and served him as general with five thousand of his own."},{"q":"Why does the war outlive itself in Sicilian law?","a":"Because Rupilius drew up laws for the island with ten commissioners after ending it, and those laws still governed its courts in Cicero’s own day."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carthage fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">146 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tiberius killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-276","num":276,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Appian says the sword was never carried into the assembly until Tiberius Gracchus was killed there, which is where he begins the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that ended in one-man rule.","questions":["Sallust wrote that once Carthage was destroyed and sea and land lay open, Fortune began to exercise her tyranny, a diagnosis of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> written from inside it.","Polybius predicted that a state which had weathered great perils and won uncontested supremacy would grow extravagant and quarrelsome, which reads as a forecast of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed."],"answer":"crisis of the Roman Republic","answerText":"crisis of the Roman Republic","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","politics","event","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>crisis of the Roman Republic</b> is the long breakdown of its politics into violence, and Appian marks its beginning precisely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says the plebeians and Senate had often quarrelled over laws, debts, land and elections, but that internal discord did not bring them to blows and disputes were composed within the limits of the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the secession to the Sacred Mount, he notes, was made without violence by men who had weapons in their hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sword was never carried into the assembly, Appian writes, and there was no civil butchery until Tiberius Gracchus was killed as tribune, with many others around the temple on the Capitol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After that the parties came into open conflict repeatedly, often carrying daggers, and from time to time a tribune, praetor, consul or candidate would be killed in a temple, an assembly or the forum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Ancient writers disagreed about the cause while agreeing about the date, and Sallust puts it in the moment of victory rather than in any later failure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once Carthage was destroyed and sea and land lay open on every side, he says, Fortune began to exercise her tyranny, and ease and wealth became a burden to men who had endured toil easily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy's summary shows what that looked like in practice, with Tiberius Gracchus carrying a land bill against the wishes of the Senate and the equestrian order, and removing his own colleague from office by a special enactment when he opposed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polybius, writing a generation earlier, had argued that every state decays from within as well as from without, and that the process is regular enough to be predicted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A state that has weathered many perils and reached uncontested supremacy will grow extravagant, he wrote, and its citizens fiercer in their rivalry for office than they ought to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.1, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.2, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Conspiracy of Catiline</i> 10, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0124:chapter=10. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 58, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.57, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"why":[{"q":"Why does Appian date the crisis so precisely?","a":"Because earlier quarrels over laws, debts, land and elections were composed within the limits of the law, and no sword was carried into the assembly until Tiberius Gracchus was killed."},{"q":"Why does Sallust put the cause in the moment of victory?","a":"Because once Carthage was destroyed and sea and land lay open, ease and wealth became a burden to men who had endured toil easily."},{"q":"Why did Polybius expect something of the kind in advance?","a":"Because he held that a state which has weathered many perils and reached uncontested supremacy grows extravagant, its citizens fiercer in their rivalry for office than they ought to be."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Holding cap</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 iugera of public land</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Stock limit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 cattle, 500 sheep</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reform attempted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-277","num":277,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Pliny wrote that six proprietors owned half of Africa before Nero killed them, the extreme case of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> he blamed for the ruin of Italy.","questions":["A law limited any one holder to five hundred iugera of public land with a hundred cattle or five hundred sheep, a cap the growth of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made a dead letter.","Rich neighbours took over the rentals of ejected tenants through fictitious names and at last held the ground openly, which is how Plutarch describes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> forming."],"answer":"latifundium","answerText":"latifundium","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","agriculture","politics","rome"],"abstract":"A <b>latifundium</b> was a great estate, and Pliny gives the judgement on them that later writers have repeated ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the wide-spread domains, he writes, that have been the ruin of Italy, and will soon be the ruin of the provinces as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He offers a figure to go with it, that six proprietors were in possession of one half of Africa at the time the emperor Nero had them put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian explains how such holdings were assembled, since Rome took part of the land of each conquered Italian people and had no leisure to allot the war-wasted portion, which was the greater part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Anyone willing to work it might do so for a tenth of the grain and a fifth of the fruit, with a toll on the animals of those who kept flocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The intention, Appian says, was to multiply the Italian race, and the very opposite happened, because the rich took possession of most of the undistributed land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A law was eventually carried forbidding anyone to hold more than five hundred iugera of it, or to pasture on it more than a hundred cattle or five hundred sheep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Freemen were to be employed on the farms in fixed numbers to watch and report, an oath was sworn over and above the law, and penalties were fixed; Appian says not the smallest consideration was shown to any of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch describes the same process from the tenants' side, with rich neighbours offering larger rents, then transferring the rentals to themselves through fictitious persons, and at last holding the land openly in their own names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus shows the same thing in Sicily, where men with large possessions bought up whole slave markets to till their lands, shackling some and branding every one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Pliny the Elder, <i>The Natural History</i> 18.7, trans. John Bostock and H. T. Riley (London: Taylor and Francis, 1855), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0137:book=18:chapter=7. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.7, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.8, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Historical Library</i> 34/35.2, English translation compiled from G. Booth (1814), F. Hoefer (1865) and new renderings and checked against the Greek, Attalus, https://www.attalus.org/translate/diodorus34.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did land taken by the state end up in a few hands?","a":"Because Rome had no leisure to allot the war-wasted portion and let anyone work it for a share of the crop, and the rich took possession of most of it."},{"q":"Why did the law capping holdings change nothing?","a":"Because although an oath was sworn over and above the statute and penalties were fixed, Appian says not the smallest consideration was shown to any of it."},{"q":"Why did tenants lose land they were already farming?","a":"Because rich neighbours offered larger rents, then transferred the rentals to themselves through fictitious persons, and at last held the land openly in their own names."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribune</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Quaestor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">137 BCE, under Mancinus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE, on the Capitol</span></div>","id":"rm-281","num":281,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Velleius says he had negotiated the treaty at Numantia and feared a trial for it, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sought the tribunate in the first place.","questions":["Appian says he told the assembly that a people valiant in war was declining into pauperism and paucity of numbers, the speech with which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened his tribunate.","When Attalus left the Roman people his heir, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposed that the king’s money go to the citizens receiving land, to help them stock and till their farms."],"answer":"Tiberius Gracchus","answerText":"Tiberius Gracchus","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","politics","agriculture","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Tiberius Gracchus</b> was a Roman tribune of 133 BCE, and Velleius describes him as the son of an illustrious citizen and, on his mother's side, the grandson of Scipio Africanus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had been quaestor in the army of Mancinus and had negotiated the treaty made there; indignant that his acts should be disavowed and fearing a trial of his own, he had himself elected tribune.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian says he then delivered an eloquent discourse about the Italian race, lamenting that a people so valiant in war was declining into pauperism and paucity of numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He inveighed against the multitude of slaves as useless in war and never faithful to their masters, and recalled the long and dangerous war the Romans had just fought against the slaves of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His bill kept the old limit of five hundred iugera, let each son hold half as much again, and gave the remainder to three commissioners changed every year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch describes the day of the vote, when the urns were stolen away by the party of the rich and two men of consular rank fell down before Tiberius, clasped his hands and begged him in tears to desist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The deadlock was constitutional as much as political, since another tribune, Marcus Octavius, interposed his veto, and among the Romans a negative always defeated an affirmative proposal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Attalus died and left the Roman people his heir, Plutarch says Tiberius brought in a bill that the king's money should go to the citizens who received a parcel of land, to help them stock and till their farms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He added that it did not belong to the Senate to deliberate about the cities of that kingdom, which was a claim on the conduct of foreign affairs as well as on the money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was killed on the Capitol while still tribune, and Appian calls it an abominable crime, the first ever perpetrated in a public assembly and one that was seldom without parallels afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.2, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.9, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.12, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.17, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he stand for the tribunate at all?","a":"Because he was indignant that the treaty he had negotiated at Numantia should be disavowed, and feared a trial of his own."},{"q":"Why did his argument turn on slaves rather than on land?","a":"Because he inveighed against them as useless in war and never faithful, recalling the long and dangerous war Rome had just fought against the slaves of Sicily."},{"q":"Why was the deadlock constitutional as well as political?","a":"Because another tribune interposed his veto, and among the Romans a negative always defeated an affirmative proposal."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cap</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 iugera, +250 per son</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 men, changed yearly</span></div>","id":"rm-282","num":282,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"The bill kept the old ceiling on public land and let each son hold half as much again, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was less a confiscation than an old law given commissioners to enforce it.","questions":["Appian says the rich could no longer disregard the ceiling once three commissioners existed to divide the surplus, which is what made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> intolerable to them.","Livy’s summary puts the ceiling at a thousand iugera where Appian puts it at five hundred, so the sources disagree about what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually allowed."],"answer":"lex Sempronia agraria","answerText":"lex Sempronia agraria","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","law","agriculture","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>lex Sempronia agraria</b> of 133 BCE was Tiberius Gracchus's land law, and Appian says it re-enacted an existing rule that nobody should hold more than five hundred iugera of the public domain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To that it added a provision that the sons of the occupiers might each hold half as much again, and that everything beyond those limits be divided among the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The division was to be carried out by three elected commissioners who were to be changed annually, which is what gave the old ceiling teeth for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy's summary states the ceiling differently, saying that no one was to own more than a thousand iugera of public land, so the two sources do not agree about what the law allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same summary records that the bill was carried against the wishes of both the Senate and the equestrian order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Appian says the rich were extremely disturbed, because the commissioners meant they could no longer disregard the law as they had before, and because Gracchus had forbidden the sale of allotments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They gathered in groups and complained that the poor were appropriating the results of their tillage, their vineyards and their dwellings, and that some of them had paid neighbours for the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What Gracchus had in mind, Appian writes, was not money but men, and he believed nothing more advantageous could happen to Italy than the work he was attempting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Arguing at length before the vote, he asked the assembly whether it was not just to let the commons divide what was common property.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first commissioners were Gracchus himself, his brother of the same name and his father-in-law Appius Claudius, since the people feared the law would not be executed unless he led with his whole family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.9, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 58, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.10, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.11, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.13, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did an old limit suddenly bite?","a":"Because the law added three elected commissioners changed annually to carry the division out, so the ceiling could no longer be disregarded as before."},{"q":"Why did the occupiers think themselves wronged?","a":"Because they complained the poor were appropriating the results of their tillage, their vineyards and their dwellings, some having paid neighbours for the ground."},{"q":"Why do the sources disagree about what the law allowed?","a":"Because Appian gives a ceiling of five hundred iugera with half as much again per son, where Livy’s summary says no one was to own more than a thousand."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First board</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Members</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, changed yearly</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land cap</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 iugera</span></div>","id":"rm-283","num":283,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Three men changed yearly were to measure the public land and divide the surplus, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went on doing so for years after the man who created it was dead.","questions":["When holders would not hand in lists of what they occupied, the board resorted to informers, and a crop of lawsuits followed wherever the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tried to fix a boundary.","Fulvius Flaccus and Papirius Carbo joined the younger Gracchus on it after Appius Claudius died, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlived its founder."],"answer":"Gracchan land commission","answerText":"Gracchan land commission","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","politics","agriculture","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Gracchan land commission</b> was the board of three men created by the land law of 133 BCE to measure the public domain and divide the surplus among the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian says the commissioners were to be elected and changed annually, which is what turned a long-ignored ceiling on holdings into something that had to be reckoned with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy's summary names the first board as Tiberius Gracchus himself, his brother Gaius and his father-in-law Appius Claudius, elected as a triumviral board to divide land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Appian gives the same three and explains the reason, that the people feared the law would fail of execution unless Gracchus took the lead with his whole family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds that Gracchus became immensely popular by it and was escorted home as though he were the founder not of a single city or race but of all the nations of Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The board survived its founder, and after Gracchus was killed and Appius Claudius died, Fulvius Flaccus and Papirius Carbo were appointed to it alongside the younger Gracchus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its work then ran into the difficulty that decides most land reform, since the men in possession neglected to hand in lists of their holdings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A proclamation was issued inviting informers to give testimony against them, and Appian says a great number of embarrassing lawsuits sprang up at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those in possession kept postponing the division on various pretexts for a very long time, and the Italian allies resisted it hardest of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some proposed admitting the allies to Roman citizenship so that gratitude for the greater favour would stop them quarrelling about the land, which Appian says the Italians were ready to accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.9, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 58, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.13, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.18, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.21, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Museo_archeologico_%28Fano%29_136.jpg/960px-Museo_archeologico_%28Fano%29_136.jpg","title":"A boundary stone naming the three commissioners","desc":"The inscription records Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus, as propraetor, restoring by decree of the Senate the markers that Publius Licinius, Appius Claudius and Gaius Gracchus had set as IIIviri agris dandis adsignandis iudicandis, the three men for granting, assigning and adjudging land; the restoration is later than the commission itself, and the trio named is not the board Appian gives.","credit":"Photograph by Accurimbono, CC BY-SA 4.0. Museo Archeologico, Fano; CIL XI 6331. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museo_archeologico_%28Fano%29_136.jpg","alt":"A tapering limestone pillar standing on a tiled museum floor, its upper half carved with nine lines of Latin capitals, with a small printed museum label on the wall beside it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did one family take all three places on the board?","a":"Because the people feared the law would fail of execution unless Gracchus took the lead with his whole family."},{"q":"Why did the division stall?","a":"Because the men in possession neglected to hand in lists of their holdings, and the proclamation inviting informers produced a great number of embarrassing lawsuits."},{"q":"Why was citizenship proposed as a way through?","a":"Because the Italian allies resisted the division hardest, and gratitude for the greater favour was expected to stop them quarrelling about the land."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 BCE, on the Capitol</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a 2nd tribunate</span></div>","id":"rm-284","num":284,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Livy’s summary says he was struck first with a piece of a chair and thrown into the river without a funeral, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is recorded.","questions":["Appian is astonished that the Senate never thought of appointing a dictator, and instead the pontifex maximus led senators up the hill, which is the sequence that ends in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Capitol.","Plutarch calls it the first sedition since the abolition of the monarchy to end in bloodshed, meaning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the men who fell with him."],"answer":"death of Tiberius Gracchus","answerText":"death of Tiberius Gracchus","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>death of Tiberius Gracchus</b> in 133 BCE happened on the Capitol, where he had gone to seek a second tribunate, and Plutarch describes his supporters girding up their togas and breaking the officers' spear-shafts to arm themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tiberius put his hand to his head as a visible sign that his life was in danger, since those at a distance could not hear him over the noise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian says the Senate had meanwhile assembled at the temple of Fides, and finds it astonishing that nobody thought of appointing a dictator, a resource that had often protected the state in such emergencies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Having reached the decision it did reach, the Senate marched up to the Capitol with Cornelius Scipio Nasica, the pontifex maximus, at its head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Livy's summary agrees on the leader and adds the manner, saying Gracchus was first hit with a piece of a chair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The bodies of those who fell with him were thrown into the river without funeral, which was a punishment as well as a killing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Appian notes that he died while still tribune, holding an office the Romans counted sacrosanct, and that he was the son of a man twice consul and of Cornelia, daughter of the Scipio who broke Carthage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He calls it an abominable crime, the first ever perpetrated in a public assembly, and says such things were seldom without parallels from that time on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city, he adds, was divided between sorrow and joy, some mourning for themselves as well as for him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch draws the same line, saying this was the first sedition at Rome since the abolition of royal power to end in bloodshed and the death of citizens, earlier quarrels having been settled by mutual concession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.16, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 58, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.17, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Tiberius Gracchus</i> 20, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Tiberius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he put his hand to his head?","a":"Because those at a distance could not hear him over the noise, so it was a visible sign that his life was in danger."},{"q":"Why does Appian find the Senate’s course astonishing?","a":"Because nobody thought of appointing a dictator, a resource that had often protected the state in such emergencies."},{"q":"Why is the day treated as a break in Roman history?","a":"Because it was the first sedition since the abolition of royal power to end in bloodshed, earlier quarrels having been settled by mutual concession."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribune</span><span class=\"dt-v\">123 – 122 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">121 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-285","num":285,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Livy’s summary calls him the brother of Tiberius and a better orator, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried in two years a programme his brother had not attempted.","questions":["Moving from the Palatine to the streets by the forum, and facing the crowd rather than the Senate house when he spoke, were small gestures by which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> announced what he was.","Velleius says he left nothing undisturbed, nothing untouched, nothing as it had been, a hostile verdict on what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did in office."],"answer":"Gaius Gracchus","answerText":"Gaius Gracchus","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","politics","law","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Gaius Gracchus</b> was tribune in 123 and 122 BCE, and Livy's summary calls him the brother of Tiberius and a better orator, who carried several dangerous laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch lists a programme rather than a measure: an agrarian law dividing public land among the poor, a military law providing soldiers' clothing at public cost and forbidding the enrolment of anyone under seventeen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Another gave the Italians equal voting rights with Roman citizens, another lowered the price of grain, and another dealt with the appointment of judges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That last most curtailed the senators, who alone had served as judges in criminal cases, a privilege that made them formidable to the common people and to the knights alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people not only passed the law but entrusted the selection of the judges to its author, so that Plutarch says he found himself invested with something like monarchical power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He used it in both directions, and Plutarch records him inducing the Senate to sell the grain that Fabius sent from Spain and return the money to the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Appian says he was tribune a second time, and that having bought the plebeians he next courted the equestrian order, which held the middle place between the Senate and the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch notices the smaller signals too, since Gaius moved his house from the Palatine to the quarter by the forum where the poor lived, and turned to face the crowd instead of the Senate house when he spoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Opposition took the same popular form, and the Senate persuaded the consul Fannius to expel from the city everyone who was not a Roman while Gaius was gathering support from all over Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Velleius, hostile to him, sums the tribunates up by saying he left nothing undisturbed, nothing untouched, nothing unmolested and nothing as it had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 60, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 5, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 6, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.22, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.6, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the law about judges cut deepest?","a":"Because senators alone had served as judges in criminal cases, a privilege that made them formidable to the common people and to the knights alike."},{"q":"Why did the people’s trust give him something like monarchical power?","a":"Because they not only passed the law but entrusted the selection of the judges to its author."},{"q":"Why did the Senate answer him by expelling non-Romans?","a":"Because Gaius was gathering support from all over Italy, so it persuaded the consul to expel from the city everyone who was not a Roman."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">123 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Price</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 1/3 asses, per Livy</span></div>","id":"rm-286","num":286,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Livy’s summary prices the grain at six and one-third asses for the plebs, the figure that makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first standing food subsidy at Rome.","questions":["Plutarch lists it among the laws that gratified the people and overthrew the Senate, since the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lowered the market price of grain to the poor.","Velleius, who disliked everything about its author, says he began the practice of distributing grain to the people, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seen by an enemy."],"answer":"lex frumentaria","answerText":"lex frumentaria","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","law","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>lex frumentaria</b> of Gaius Gracchus put the supply of grain to the city on a statutory footing, and Plutarch lists it among the laws proposed by way of gratifying the people and overthrowing the Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He describes it simply as a law relating to the supplies of grain, which lowered the market price to the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy's summary is more exact about the terms and calls it one of several dangerous laws, saying the grain was to be sold to the plebs at six and one-third asses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same summary sets it beside a land bill like his brother's and a third law aimed at the equestrian order, so it was one measure of a programme rather than a stray concession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a sale rather than a dole, which is worth stating plainly, since the price was fixed below the market and not reduced to nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Velleius, who disliked everything about its author, treats it as a beginning rather than an episode, saying Gaius began the practice of distributing grain to the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He puts it in a list with the planting of colonies in the provinces and the transfer of the courts, as part of a programme that left nothing as it had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch shows the same man handling grain in the opposite direction, when Fabius the propraetor sent a supply to the city from Spain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gaius induced the Senate to sell that grain and send the money back to the province, and to censure Fabius for making Roman rule burdensome there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Appian puts the political reading bluntly, saying that Gaius had bought the plebeians before turning to court the equestrian order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 5, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 60, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.6, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 6, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.22, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a sale not the same as a dole?","a":"Because the price was fixed below the market at six and one-third asses and not reduced to nothing, so the state was subsidising a purchase rather than giving grain away."},{"q":"Why send Spanish grain back as money?","a":"Because he induced the Senate to sell it and return the proceeds to the province, censuring the propraetor for making Roman rule burdensome there."},{"q":"Why did his enemies treat this law as a beginning?","a":"Because Velleius puts it in a list with colonies in the provinces and the transfer of the courts, saying he began the practice of distributing grain to the people."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jury law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">123 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Judges</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 senators + 300 knights</span></div>","id":"rm-287","num":287,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Appian places them between the Senate and the plebeians, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the group Gaius Gracchus courted once he had the people behind him.","questions":["Cicero told a Roman audience that the men who collect the revenues are the prop and support of every other order, meaning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> whose money was invested in Asia.","Livy’s summary says a law was aimed at corrupting a body that had been collaborating with the Senate, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seen from a hostile angle."],"answer":"equestrian order","answerText":"equestrian order","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>equestrian order</b> was the Roman propertied class below the Senate, and Appian defines it by position, saying the knights held the middle place between the Senate and the plebeians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What gave them weight was business rather than office, and Cicero says letters came from Asia every day to Roman knights whose whole property was employed in collecting the revenues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same speech he calls the revenues the sinews of the republic and the order that collects them the prop and support of all the other orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That was said to persuade an assembly, and it describes an order whose fortunes lay in the provinces rather than in the curia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their political weight arrived suddenly, and Livy's summary says Gaius Gracchus carried a law aimed at corrupting the equestrian order, which at that time was collaborating with the Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The law in question concerned the courts, and Plutarch says senators alone had served as judges in criminal cases, a privilege that made them formidable to the common people and to the knights alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His account is that Gracchus added three hundred men of the equestrian order to the three hundred senators and made service as a judge the prerogative of all six hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Appian tells it differently, saying the courts were transferred from the senators to the knights outright, so the two sources disagree about how far the change went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian also draws out the consequence, that the power of sitting in judgment on all Romans and Italians, senators included, exalted the knights into rulers over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Senators, on his reading, were put on the level of subjects, and he reports Gracchus remarking that he had broken the power of the Senate once for all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.22, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 4, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=4. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>On Pompey's Command</i> 17, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1856), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Man.:section=17. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 60, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 5, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did this order matter without holding office?","a":"Because its weight came from business: letters reached Roman knights from Asia daily, their whole property employed in collecting Rome’s revenues."},{"q":"Why did control of the courts change the balance so far?","a":"Because the power of sitting in judgment on all Romans and Italians, senators included, exalted the knights into rulers over them."},{"q":"Why is it unclear how far the change actually went?","a":"Because Appian says the courts were transferred to the knights outright, where Plutarch says three hundred knights were added to three hundred senators."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spanish cases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">171 BCE, 5 recuperatores</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Juries moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">123 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-288","num":288,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Before any standing court existed, Livy says five recuperatores were appointed to try each Spanish complaint, the ad-hoc arrangement the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> later replaced.","questions":["Appian names three governors acquitted of taking bribes while the ambassadors sent to complain of them were still in Rome, the scandal that discredited the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and moved its juries.","Plutarch and Appian disagree about whether the juries were shared with senators or handed to the knights outright, so the composition of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not settled."],"answer":"extortion court","answerText":"extortion court","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","law","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>extortion court</b> was the tribunal before which Roman magistrates answered for money taken from the people they governed, and Livy shows what preceded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When deputations from both Spanish provinces complained of the rapacity of Roman magistrates and begged the Senate not to let allies be robbed worse than enemies, five recuperatores from the senatorial order were appointed to try each case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The complainants were allowed to choose their own counsel and named four leading men, but the first defendant was acquitted at the third hearing and two others withdrew into voluntary exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The praetor then dropped the business altogether and left for his province to stop any more people being troubled by the Spaniards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was a court assembled for a single occasion and dissolved with it, and what turned the matter into a standing question at Rome was who should be allowed to sit on such a bench.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Appian says the courts had become discredited by bribery and names three men, Aurelius Cotta, Salinator and Manius Aquilius, notorious bribe-takers acquitted while the ambassadors sent to complain of them were still in Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Senate, he says, was extremely ashamed and yielded, and the courts passed from the senators to the knights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch describes the same law differently, saying three hundred men of the equestrian order were added to the three hundred senators and that all six hundred were made eligible to judge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Velleius, listing what he disliked, says only that the judicial powers were transferred from the Senate to the equites, which follows Appian rather than Plutarch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy's summary calls the same measure a law aimed at corrupting the equestrian order, which shows how differently one statute could be described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>The History of Rome</i> 43.2, trans. Canon Roberts (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1912), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0144:book=43:chapter=2. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.22, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 5, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.6, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 60, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-56-60/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was a court assembled for one occasion not enough?","a":"Because the first defendant was acquitted at the third hearing, two others withdrew into exile, and the praetor then dropped the business and left for his province."},{"q":"Why did the Senate give the courts up?","a":"Because three notorious bribe-takers were acquitted while the ambassadors sent to complain of them were still in Rome, and it was extremely ashamed."},{"q":"Why can one statute be described so differently?","a":"Because Velleius calls it a transfer of judicial power, Plutarch an enlargement of the panel, and Livy’s summary a law aimed at corrupting the equestrian order."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">121 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Invoked again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">63 BCE, against Catiline</span></div>","id":"rm-289","num":289,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Sallust records the wording, that the consuls should make it their care that the commonwealth receive no injury, which is the whole text of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as he gives it.","questions":["Plutarch says the Senate formally enjoined Opimius to save the city as best he could and put down the tyrants, the first occasion of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Roman history.","Sallust calls it the greatest power the Senate grants a magistrate, authorising levies, war and unlimited command, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suspended the ordinary rules."],"answer":"senatus consultum ultimum","answerText":"senatus consultum ultimum","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","law","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>senatus consultum ultimum</b> was the Senate's declaration of emergency, and Sallust preserves its wording, that the consuls should make it their care that the commonwealth receive no injury.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He calls it the greatest power the Senate grants a magistrate at Rome, one that authorises him to raise troops, to make war, and to assume unlimited control over allies and citizens alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gave the consul chief command and jurisdiction at home and in the field, rights which without an order of the people he was not otherwise permitted to exercise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first occasion was the crisis of 121 BCE, and Plutarch says the senators went back into the senate house and formally enjoined the consul Opimius to save the city as best he could and put down the tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Opimius then ordered the senators to take up arms, and every member of the equestrian order was told to come next morning with two armed servants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Livy's summary states the same thing in a sentence, that the Senate decided to summon the people to arms, and that Gaius Gracchus was routed and killed by the consul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Appian shows the procedure that went with it, since Gracchus and Flaccus were first summoned from their homes to the senate house to defend themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They ran instead to the Aventine, hoping that if they seized it first the Senate would come to terms, and fortified the temple of Diana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Velleius, writing later and approvingly, records simply that the consul Opimius hunted Gracchus down with armed men and put him to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What no source of this period settles is whether the decree could make lawful the killing of citizens without trial, which is the question every later use of it raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sallust, <i>The Conspiracy of Catiline</i> 29, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0124:chapter=29. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 61, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.26, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.6, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is this the greatest power the Senate can grant?","a":"Because it authorises a magistrate to raise troops, make war and assume unlimited control over allies and citizens, rights he could not otherwise exercise without an order of the people."},{"q":"Why did the decree not settle the question it raised?","a":"Because no source of this period settles whether it could make lawful the killing of citizens without trial, which is the question every later use of it raised."},{"q":"Why were the equestrians told to bring armed servants?","a":"Because the consul had been enjoined to save the city as best he could, so the emergency was met by arming the propertied orders rather than by a levy."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">121 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bridge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">overtaken at the Tiber, 121 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-290","num":290,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Overtaken at the wooden bridge over the Tiber while two friends held the crossing until they were killed: that is Plutarch’s account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 121 BCE.","questions":["Not a man in the watching crowd would lend him a horse, though they urged him on as at a race, which is the detail Plutarch keeps from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its last hour.","Appian says he offered freedom to the slaves as he ran and none listened, one measure of how little support remained before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed."],"answer":"death of Gaius Gracchus","answerText":"death of Gaius Gracchus","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>death of Gaius Gracchus</b> in 121 BCE followed the killing of a consular attendant named Antyllius, whose body was carried through the forum past the senate house with wailing and lamentation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the senators denounced a monstrous crime while the people answered that these were the men who had murdered Tiberius Gracchus, a tribune, and flung his body away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Senate then enjoined the consul Opimius to save the city as best he could, and the knights were told to come armed the next morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Appian says Gracchus and Flaccus were summoned to defend themselves and ran armed to the Aventine instead, hoping that holding it would bring the Senate to terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They offered freedom to the slaves as they ran through the city, and Appian records that nobody listened to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch describes the attempt to negotiate, when Flaccus sent his younger son into the forum with a herald's wand to speak for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of those present were not disinclined to accept terms, but Opimius answered that petitioners should not try to persuade the Senate by messenger and must come down and surrender themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When it broke Gaius fled and was overtaken at the wooden bridge over the Tiber, where two friends held the head of the bridge and let nobody pass until they were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He had one servant with him, Philocrates, and Plutarch notes that the spectators urged him on as at a race while not a man would help him or lend him a horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy's summary records the outcome flatly, that Gaius was routed and killed by the consul Lucius Opimius, and that the former consul Fulvius Flaccus died with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Appian, <i>The Civil Wars</i> 1.26, trans. Horace White, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1913), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Appian/Civil_Wars/1*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Caius Gracchus</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 10 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1921), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Caius_Gracchus*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 61, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did one attendant’s death bring on the crisis?","a":"Because his body was carried through the forum past the senate house with wailing, and the senators denounced a monstrous crime while the people recalled Tiberius Gracchus flung into the river."},{"q":"Why did seizing the Aventine fail as a bargaining position?","a":"Because they had hoped holding it would bring the Senate to terms, and Opimius answered that petitioners must come down and surrender themselves."},{"q":"Why does the flight to the bridge tell the political story?","a":"Because the spectators urged him on as at a race while not a man would help him or lend him a horse, and the slaves offered freedom had not listened either."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Division dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 146 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gracchi killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">133 and 121 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cicero's defence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">56 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-291","num":291,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Sallust says the mutual moderation of Senate and people lasted only while an enemy frightened them, after which Rome divided into the two camps called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ever after.","questions":["Cicero, asked in court what he meant by the race of best men, answered that they were every good man, which is one half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> defining itself.","The words describe methods rather than membership, since a man who worked through the assembly and one who worked through the Senate were what Romans meant by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in practice."],"answer":"optimates and populares","answerText":"optimates and populares","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Optimates and populares</b> are the two labels Roman politics used for the men who worked through the Senate and the men who worked through the popular assembly, and Sallust dates the division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before the destruction of Carthage, he says, the Senate and people managed the republic with mutual moderation, and there were no contests among the citizens for honour or ascendancy because the dread of an enemy kept the state in order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once that fear was removed, licentiousness and pride took hold, the patricians carried their authority and the people their liberty to excess, and there was a complete division into two factions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sallust ties the labels to particular men, saying that when the Gracchi began to vindicate the liberty of the people the nobility worked through the allies and through the equestrian order to stop them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> First Tiberius was killed and a few years later Gaius, and Sallust adds that in the Gracchi themselves there was not sufficient prudence for all their ardour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Cicero gives the other side's account of itself, and he gives it under pressure, since the prosecution in a trial had asked him repeatedly what he meant by the race of best men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His answer was that they are every good man, that their number is innumerable, and that they include the chief men of the public council, citizens of the country towns, men engaged in business and even some freedmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He defines them by what they are not, saying all belong to the best party who are not criminal, wicked by nature, mad, or embarrassed by domestic difficulties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their object, he says, is ease conjoined with dignity, which is a statement of purpose rather than of policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two words therefore describe methods and self-descriptions rather than organised parties, and Sallust's own judgement is that this was where opposition to the power of the nobility began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 41, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=41. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 42, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=42. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Sestio</i> 96, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0020:text=Sest.:section=96. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Sestio</i> 97, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0020:text=Sest.:section=97. [Open access]","Cicero, <i>Pro Sestio</i> 98, trans. C. D. Yonge, in <i>The Orations of Marcus Tullius Cicero</i> (London: George Bell & Sons, 1891), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0020:text=Sest.:section=98. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 5, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Sallust date the division to the fall of Carthage?","a":"Because until then the dread of an enemy kept the state in order, and once that fear was removed the patricians carried their authority and the people their liberty to excess."},{"q":"Why did Cicero have to define the best men in court?","a":"Because the prosecution asked him repeatedly what he meant by the race of best men, so he answered that they are every good man, innumerable, including townsmen, businessmen and some freedmen."},{"q":"Why are these labels not the names of parties?","a":"Because they describe methods and self-descriptions — working through the Senate or through the assembly — with Cicero defining his side by what it is not and stating an object rather than a policy."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">112 – 105 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cirta fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">112 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jugurtha taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">105 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-292","num":292,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Sallust says it was the first war in which opposition was offered to the power of the nobility, which is why he wrote the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> up as a political subject.","questions":["Adherbal was killed at Cirta in defiance of a senatorial decree and a consul then made a treaty nobody had authorised, the opening moves of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Numidia.","Bocchus of Mauretania put his own son-in-law in chains and handed him to Marius, with Sulla as the main actor, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended."],"answer":"Jugurthine War","answerText":"Jugurthine War","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Jugurthine War</b> was fought in Numidia between 112 and 105 BCE, and Sallust says he chose it because it was great, sanguinary and of varied fortune, and because in it opposition was for the first time offered to the power of the nobility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy's summary gives the trigger: Adherbal, besieged by Jugurtha in the town of Cirta, was killed in violation of a decree of the Senate, and war was declared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consul Calpurnius Bestia, sent to conduct it, concluded a treaty with Jugurtha without being ordered to do so by either the people or the Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jugurtha was then invited to Rome under safe conduct to name the men he had bribed, and while there had a rival claimant killed, so that he risked standing trial for his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sallust reports his verdict on the city as he left it, that it was for sale and would soon perish if only it could find a purchaser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The fighting was long and inconclusive, and Livy's summary records the consul Quintus Caecilius Metellus defeating Jugurtha in two battles and devastating the whole of Numidia without ending the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gaius Marius took over the command after being elected consul, and it was under him that the campaign was finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end came by diplomacy rather than by battle, since Jugurtha, driven out of Numidia, took refuge with Bocchus, king of the Mauri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bocchus, after losing a battle, put him in chains and handed him over, and Livy's summary names Sulla, then Marius's quaestor, as the main actor in that affair.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sallust closes on the political consequence, that with Jugurtha coming to Rome in chains Marius was created consul in his absence and Gaul was decreed to him as his province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 5, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=5. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 64, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 35, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 65, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 66, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 114, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=114. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sallust choose this war as his subject?","a":"Because it was great, sanguinary and of varied fortune, and because in it opposition was for the first time offered to the power of the nobility."},{"q":"Why did bringing Jugurtha to Rome make matters worse?","a":"Because he came under safe conduct to name the men he had bribed and had a rival claimant killed while there, so that he risked standing trial for his life."},{"q":"Why did the war end by diplomacy rather than battle?","a":"Because driven out of Numidia he took refuge with Bocchus, who after losing a battle put him in chains and handed him over."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Numantia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">134 – 133 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">110 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Handed over</span><span class=\"dt-v\">105 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-293","num":293,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Scipio took him aside at Numantia and told him to court the Romans as a body and bribe no one, advice <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent the next twenty years ignoring.","questions":["Roman officers at Numantia told him that anything could be purchased at Rome, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have tested the claim for the rest of his life.","Looking back at the city as he was ordered to leave it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called it venal and said it would soon perish if it could find a purchaser."],"answer":"Jugurtha","answerText":"Jugurtha","difficulty":3,"tags":["ruler","person","history","politics","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Jugurtha</b> was a Numidian prince who seized the whole kingdom his grandfather Masinissa had built as a Roman ally, and Sallust dates his political education to his service at Numantia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roman officers there, men to whom wealth was more attractive than honour, told him that if Micipsa died he might have Numidia to himself, and that anything might be purchased at Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scipio, dismissing the auxiliaries, took him privately into his own tent and advised him to court the friendship of the Romans as a body rather than by practising on individuals, and to bribe nobody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The advice was sound and was disregarded, and Livy's summary records Adherbal besieged in Cirta and killed there in violation of a decree of the Senate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jugurtha was said to have corrupted many senators with bribes, and was invited to Rome under safe conduct to name them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> While in the city he had a rival dealt with, and Sallust says he instructed Bomilcar, his most faithful adherent, to procure assassins for Massiva, secretly if possible and by any means if not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bomilcar was arrested for it, but Jugurtha sent him privately back to Numidia rather than let him be executed, fearing that his other subjects would be deterred from obeying him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ordered by the Senate to quit Italy, he is said to have looked back at Rome in silence several times and then called it a venal city that would soon perish if it could find a purchaser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The war that followed lasted years, and he was taken in the end not in battle but by his father-in-law Bocchus, who put him in chains and handed him over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sallust's judgement on the whole affair is that it was the first occasion on which the power of the Roman nobility was seriously opposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 8, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=8. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 64, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 35, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=35. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 66, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 5, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Sallust date his political education to Numantia?","a":"Because Roman officers there, men to whom wealth was more attractive than honour, told him he might have Numidia to himself and that anything might be purchased at Rome."},{"q":"Why was Scipio’s advice the opposite of theirs?","a":"Because he told him privately to court the friendship of the Romans as a body rather than practise on individuals, and to bribe nobody."},{"q":"Why did he protect the man arrested for the assassination?","a":"Because he feared that letting Bomilcar be executed would deter his other subjects from obeying him, so he sent him privately back to Numidia."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">157 – 86 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 times</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First consulship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">107 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-294","num":294,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Plutarch says his parents were poor people who lived by the labour of their own hands, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached seven consulships from as far outside the nobility as a Roman could start.","questions":["Sallust says he had every qualification for the consulship except antiquity of family, which is the whole difficulty <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to argue his way past.","Soldiers who carried their own baggage without complaint were nicknamed after him, and Plutarch offers two explanations of why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave his name to the mules."],"answer":"Gaius Marius","answerText":"Gaius Marius","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","politics","warfare","rome"],"abstract":"<b>Gaius Marius</b> was a Roman general and seven times consul, and Plutarch says he was born of altogether obscure parents, poor people who lived by the labour of their own hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He grew up in a village in the territory of Arpinum and did not see the city until late, living in a manner Plutarch calls rude beside the polish of city life but temperate and in the old Roman style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sallust says he had every qualification for the consulship except antiquity of family, listing industry, integrity, great knowledge of war and a spirit undaunted in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was temperate in private life, superior to pleasure and riches, and ambitious only of glory, and he had studied neither Greek eloquence nor the manners of the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his own speech to the people, as Sallust gives it, he says that where other men can fall back on ancient rank and the deeds of ancestors, his whole hopes rest upon himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Cimbric terror made him indispensable, and Plutarch says the people would have nothing to do with high birth or wealth at the consular elections and proclaimed Marius consul in his absence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On campaign he drilled the army as it marched, practising the men in running and long marches and compelling them to carry their own baggage and prepare their own food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From this, Plutarch says, men who bore toil without a murmur were afterwards called Marian mules, though he records another explanation of the nickname as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Livy's summary shows the political result, with Marius elected consul for the fifth time while away from home, and postponing a triumph until he had also beaten the Cimbri.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The same summary adds that the first men in the state, who had envied the new man for reaching so many high posts, now admitted that he had rescued it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 3, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 63, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=63. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 85, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=85. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 68, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Bust_of_Marius_%28GL_319%29_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg/960px-Bust_of_Marius_%28GL_319%29_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg","title":"A portrait head conventionally called Marius","desc":"The museum's own name for the piece, not a fact the marble states: the head carries no inscription and the identification is traditional rather than established. What it does show is the severe Republican portrait style, which favoured lined and ageing faces over flattery.","credit":"Photograph by José Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0. Glyptothek, Munich, inv. GL 319. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Marius_%28GL_319%29_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg","alt":"A white marble bust of an older man with a deeply lined forehead, hollow cheeks, thin lips and close-cropped hair, the nose broken away, mounted on a plain stand against a brick wall."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the people elect him in his absence?","a":"Because the Cimbric terror made him indispensable, so at the consular elections they would have nothing to do with high birth or wealth."},{"q":"Why does he say his hopes rest upon himself?","a":"Because other men can fall back on ancient rank and the deeds of ancestors, and he had every qualification for the consulship except antiquity of family."},{"q":"Why did his men carry their own baggage and food?","a":"Because he drilled the army as it marched, practising them in running and long marches, from which those who bore it without a murmur were called Marian mules."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Levy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">107 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Old bar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">400 drachmae census</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Javelin pin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">101 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-295","num":295,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Sallust says he enlisted not by the ancient method or from the classes but took all who were willing, the change that stands at the centre of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as usually described.","questions":["Plutarch says arms had until then been given only to men whose property assessment made them worthy, each soldier pledging his substance to the state, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> abandoned.","A javelin pin of wood that broke on impact, and men trained to carry their own food, are the small practical measures that modern writers gather under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as one programme."],"answer":"Marian reforms","answerText":"Marian reforms","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","warfare","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Marian reforms</b> are a modern collective name for a set of changes the ancient sources report separately rather than as a programme, and the best attested is the manner of recruitment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sallust says that Marius, raising troops for Africa, enlisted them not after the ancient method or from the classes, but took all who were willing to join him, the greater part from the lowest ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gives two explanations without choosing between them: that there was a scarcity of better men, and that the consul wished to pay court to the poor, by whom he had been promoted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch confirms the change and states the rule it broke, that former commanders had bestowed arms only on men whose property assessment made them worthy, each soldier pledging his substance to the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He calls the enlistment contrary to law and custom, and says it was not this but Marius's insolent speeches against the nobles that brought him most odium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Other measures were practical rather than constitutional, and Plutarch describes an army drilled as it marched, the men running, marching long distances and carrying their own baggage and food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soldiers who bore this without complaint were afterwards nicknamed Marian mules, though Plutarch records a rival explanation of the phrase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before Vercellae, he says, Marius altered the javelin, replacing one of the two iron nails that held the head with a wooden pin that would break.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The shaft would then twist rather than stand out straight from an enemy's shield, which encumbered the man carrying it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy's summary records the results rather than the methods, with the Teutones destroyed at Aquae Sextiae and the Cimbri the following year, after which the nobility conceded that the new man had saved the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 86, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=86. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 68, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the collective name a modern one?","a":"Because the ancient sources report the changes separately rather than as a programme, the best attested being the manner of recruitment."},{"q":"Why does Sallust give two explanations for the levy?","a":"Because he will not choose between a scarcity of better men and the consul’s wish to pay court to the poor, by whom he had been promoted."},{"q":"Why replace an iron nail in the javelin with a wooden pin?","a":"Because the pin would break, so the shaft twisted rather than standing out straight from an enemy’s shield, encumbering the man carrying it."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Census bar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">400 drachmae, per Polybius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enrolled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 107 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-296","num":296,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Polybius says men assessed below four hundred drachmae served in the fleet rather than the legions, which is the rule that had kept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of the army.","questions":["Plutarch says arms had always been given only to men whose property made them worthy, each pledging his substance to the state, so enlisting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke law and custom together.","Sallust reports two explanations for the change, a scarcity of better men and a consul courting the poor, and does not choose between them when he describes enlisting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for Africa."],"answer":"capite censi","answerText":"capite censi","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","history","warfare","politics","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>capite censi</b> were the poorest Roman citizens, registered by the censors at a head count because they had too little property to be rated in the classes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What that meant in practice was exclusion from the legions, and Polybius states the rule, that men whose census fell below four hundred drachmae were all employed in naval service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch explains the reasoning behind it, that arms were bestowed only on those whose property assessment made them worthy to receive them, each soldier being supposed to put his substance in pledge to the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A man with nothing to lose could therefore not be trusted with a shield, which made military service a mark of standing as much as a duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Marius broke the rule in 107 BCE, and Plutarch says he enlisted many a poor and insignificant man contrary to law and custom, since former commanders had not accepted such persons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Sallust describes the same levy and is more precise about it, saying Marius enrolled soldiers not after the ancient method or from the classes, but took all who were willing, the greater part from the lowest ranks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He reports two explanations current at the time without choosing between them, saying some ascribed it to a scarcity of better men and others to the consul's wish to court the poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own comment is harder than either, that to a man grasping at power the most needy are the most serviceable, since they care nothing for property they do not have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his speech to the people Marius made the same point from the other side, saying that where others fall back on ancient rank and the deeds of ancestors, his whole hopes rested upon himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Livy's summary records the outcome the levy was raised for, an army that destroyed the Teutones and the Cimbri and a nobility that conceded the new man had rescued the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Polybius, <i>The Histories</i> 6.19, trans. W. R. Paton, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 3 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1923), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Polybius/6*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 86, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=86. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 85, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=85. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 68, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why were the poorest citizens kept out of the legions?","a":"Because arms were bestowed only on those whose property assessment made them worthy, each soldier being supposed to put his substance in pledge to the state."},{"q":"Why did that make service a mark of standing?","a":"Because a man with nothing to lose could not be trusted with a shield, so the levy sorted citizens by what they owned rather than by what they could do."},{"q":"Why is Sallust’s own comment harder than either explanation?","a":"Because he says that to a man grasping at power the most needy are the most serviceable, since they care nothing for property they do not have."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Illyricum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">113 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported host</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300,000 fighting men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">102 and 101 BCE</span></div>","id":"rm-297","num":297,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Plutarch says nobody could establish what people they were or where they had set out from, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> descended on Gaul and Italy like a cloud.","questions":["The Senate ignored envoys who asked simply for land and a place to settle, after which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed four Roman armies in eight years.","A captured Roman officer told their council that Rome was unconquerable and was killed by a young man named Boiorix, one of the few personal details recorded about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on their own side."],"answer":"Cimbri and the Teutones","answerText":"Cimbri and the Teutones","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","history","warfare","migration","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Cimbri and the Teutones</b> were migrating peoples who defeated a succession of Roman armies between 113 and 105 BCE before being destroyed by Marius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says three hundred thousand armed fighting men were advancing, with much larger numbers of women and children, in quest of land to support so vast a multitude and cities to settle in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is candid about how little was known of them, since they had not mixed with other peoples and had crossed a great stretch of country, so that it could not be ascertained who they were or where they had set out from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The prevailing conjecture made them German, a guess based on their great stature, their light-blue eyes, and the fact that the Germans call robbers Cimbri, though Plutarch records other opinions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Livy's summary first meets them in Illyricum, where they came in pursuit of plunder and defeated the consul Papirius Carbo and his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Defeats followed one another, and the same summary records the consul Marcus Junius Silanus beaten by them and the Senate ignoring their envoys, who demanded land and a place to settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One episode survives in personal detail: Marcus Aurelius Scaurus, captured after his army's defeat, was called before their council and told them the Romans were unconquerable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was killed for it by a young man named Boiorix, which is one of the very few names on that side that the Roman record preserves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The worst disaster came at Arausio, where a consul and a proconsul were stripped of both their camps, and after it Marius was given the command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The peoples then separated, and Livy's summary has Marius destroying the Teutones at Aquae Sextiae and, with Catulus the following year, the Cimbri who had already forced the Alpine passes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 63, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 65, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 67, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 68, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could nobody at Rome say who these peoples were?","a":"Because they had not mixed with other peoples and had crossed a great stretch of country, so it could not be ascertained who they were or where they had set out from."},{"q":"Why was the guess that they were German only a conjecture?","a":"Because it rested on their great stature, their light-blue eyes and the fact that the Germans call robbers Cimbri, and Plutarch records other opinions."},{"q":"Why does the Roman record preserve so few names on that side?","a":"Because it names them only where they touch a Roman story, as with the young man who killed a captured commander for telling their council that the Romans were unconquerable."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">105 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Antias gives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">80,000 soldiers killed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Camps lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2</span></div>","id":"rm-298","num":298,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Two Roman commanders who would not cooperate lost both their camps on the Rhône, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost more men than any defeat since Cannae.","questions":["Livy’s summary attributes the figure of eighty thousand soldiers to Valerius Antias rather than stating it outright, which is a warning about the casualty count for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and for Roman figures generally.","Caepio was convicted of causing the disaster by his rashness and had his property confiscated, the first such confiscation since the kings, in the reckoning after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was over."],"answer":"Battle of Arausio","answerText":"Battle of Arausio","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","event","history","warfare","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Arausio</b> was fought on the Rhône in 105 BCE, and Livy's summary records that the consul Gnaeus Manlius and the proconsul Quintus Servilius Caepio were defeated by the Cimbri and stripped of both their camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the losses it names its authority rather than asserting a figure, saying that according to Valerius Antias eighty thousand soldiers and forty thousand servants and camp followers were killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That attribution is worth keeping, since Antias was the annalist Roman writers most often accused of inflating numbers, and the summary does not endorse him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The blame was assigned in court, and Caepio, who had caused the defeat by his rashness, was convicted and had his possessions confiscated, which the summary says had not happened since the kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sallust, writing of the same moment, says war was carried on unsuccessfully by Caepio and Manlius against the Gauls and that all Italy was thrown into consternation by the terror of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He adds a remark about Roman attitudes that explains the scale of the alarm, that Romans held all other nations must yield to their valour but that against the Gauls they fought not for glory but in self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch describes the same reports reaching the city and says that what was said about the numbers and strength of the invaders was disbelieved at first and afterwards found to be short of the truth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The defeat was the last of a series, and Livy's summary had already recorded Carbo, Silanus and Cassius Longinus beaten in the preceding years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its political consequence was immediate, since the people would have nothing to do with high birth at the consular elections and proclaimed Marius consul although he was not in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy's summary follows the war through to its end, with the Teutones destroyed at Aquae Sextiae and the Cimbri the year after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 67, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]","Sallust, <i>The Jugurthine War</i> 114, trans. John Selby Watson (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1899), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0126:chapter=114. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 65, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-61-65/. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 68, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the summary name its authority for the casualties?","a":"Because Antias was the annalist Roman writers most often accused of inflating numbers, so the figures are attributed to him rather than endorsed."},{"q":"Why was the confiscation of a commander’s property remarkable?","a":"Because nothing of the kind had happened since the kings, and Caepio had caused the defeat by his rashness."},{"q":"Why did the alarm at Rome run so deep?","a":"Because Romans held that all other nations must yield to their valour, but that against the Gauls they fought not for glory but in self-defence."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">102 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">March past camp</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">150,000 – 200,000</span></div>","id":"rm-299","num":299,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"The fighting began when camp servants went down to the river for water with hatchets and jars, which is how Plutarch opens his account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 102 BCE.","questions":["Marcellus waited in the hills with a detachment and fell on the enemy rear when he heard the shouting, the manoeuvre that decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for Marius.","Livy’s summary counts two hundred thousand killed where Velleius counts a hundred and fifty thousand, so the sources disagree about the scale of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by fifty thousand men."],"answer":"Battle of Aquae Sextiae","answerText":"Battle of Aquae Sextiae","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","event","history","warfare","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Aquae Sextiae</b> was fought in 102 BCE between Marius and the Teutones and Ambrones in southern Gaul, and Plutarch describes the approach to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Teutones tried to storm the Roman camp, lost men to the missiles thrown from the fortifications, and decided instead to march past it and cross the Alps unmolested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their numbers were made plain by the length of the column, since it is said they were six days passing the camp although they moved continuously.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting itself began over water rather than by plan, and Plutarch says the camp servants, having none for themselves or their beasts, went down in a body to the river with hatchets, axes and jars, determined to get it even if they had to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only a few of the enemy engaged them at first, since the main body were taking their meal after bathing and some were still in the water, which was warm and came from springs in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Livy's summary sets the sequence out plainly, with Marius first defending his camp against a violent attack by the Teutones and Ambrones and then beating them in two battles near Aquae Sextiae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The decisive move was an ambush, and Plutarch says the Romans checked the enemy's uphill rush, forced them back into the plain, and then heard shouting behind the barbarian line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Marcellus had waited in the hills with a detachment for exactly this, and when the noise of battle carried up to him he put his men to the run and fell on the rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those behind pushed forward on those in front, and the line collapsed between the two attacks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reported losses are large and the sources do not agree: Livy's summary says two hundred thousand were killed and ninety thousand captured, prefacing the figures with the words they say, while Velleius gives more than a hundred and fifty thousand slain over that day and the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 18, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 68, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 21, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.12, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the enemy decide to march past the camp?","a":"Because they had tried to storm it and lost men to the missiles thrown from the fortifications, so they meant to cross the Alps unmolested instead."},{"q":"Why did the battle begin over water?","a":"Because the camp servants, having none for themselves or their beasts, went down to the river in a body with hatchets, axes and jars, determined to get it even if they had to fight."},{"q":"Why did the barbarian line collapse between two attacks?","a":"Because Marcellus had waited in the hills for the noise of battle and fell on the rear, so those behind pushed forward on those in front."}]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">101 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100,000 – 160,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Javelin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 nail replaced by wood</span></div>","id":"rm-300","num":300,"category":"The Republic in crisis","question":"Marius replaced one iron nail in the javelin head with a wooden pin so the shaft would twist in an enemy shield, an alteration Plutarch dates to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 101 BCE.","questions":["The front ranks were chained together through their belts so that the line could not be broken, a detail Plutarch keeps from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and from nowhere else.","Velleius calls the ground the Raudian Plain and counts a hundred thousand dead where Livy’s summary counts a hundred and sixty thousand, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reported two ways."],"answer":"Battle of Vercellae","answerText":"Battle of Vercellae","difficulty":4,"tags":["battle","event","history","warfare","rome"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Vercellae</b> was fought in 101 BCE, when Marius and the proconsul Quintus Lutatius Catulus destroyed the Cimbri in northern Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says that in preparation for it Marius altered the javelin, which until then had its head fastened to the shaft by two iron nails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He left one nail as it was and replaced the other with a wooden pin that would easily break, so that a javelin striking a shield would not stand out straight from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The enemy cavalry opened the action by swerving to the right, trying to draw the Romans between themselves and their own infantry on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says the Roman commanders saw the design but could not hold their men, since one of them shouted that the enemy was in flight and the whole line set off in pursuit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The barbarian infantry then came on, and Plutarch describes it advancing like a vast sea in motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He records that the best of them were cut to pieces where they stood, because the front ranks had been bound to one another with long chains passed through their belts to stop the line being broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the waggons the survivors met the women, who killed the fugitives, strangled their own children and then killed themselves, which is the scene Plutarch calls a most tragic spectacle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Livy's summary gives the campaign and the figures, saying the Cimbri had driven back Catulus from the Alpine passes and invaded Italy before the united forces beat them, with a hundred and sixty thousand killed and sixty thousand captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Velleius calls the ground the Raudian Plain and counts more than a hundred thousand of the enemy, so the two accounts differ by a wide margin about the scale of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 26, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Marius</i> 27, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, vol. 9 (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Marius*.html. [Open access]","Livy, <i>Periochae</i> 68, trans. Jona Lendering, Livius.org, https://www.livius.org/sources/content/livy/livy-periochae-66-70/. [Open access]","Velleius Paterculus, <i>Roman History</i> 2.12, trans. Frederick W. Shipley, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1924), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Velleius_Paterculus/2A*.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why alter the javelin before the battle?","a":"Because with one iron nail replaced by a wooden pin that breaks, a javelin striking a shield would not stand out straight from it."},{"q":"Why did the Roman commanders lose control of their own line?","a":"Because the enemy cavalry swerved to draw them in, and though the design was seen, one man shouted that the enemy was in flight and the whole line set off in pursuit."},{"q":"Why were the best of the enemy cut down where they stood?","a":"Because the front ranks had been bound to one another with long chains passed through their belts to stop the line being broken."}]},
{"id":"gw-542","num":542,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Warsaw","answerText":"Warsaw","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 May 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ghetto sealed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City razed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pact signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 May 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Poland","dot":"Warsaw"},"facts":[["Country","Poland"],["Population","1.80M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+2.1% (2016–2019)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","poland"],"abstract":"The first American post in <b>Warsaw</b> was a consulate opened in 1871, when the city lay under Russian administration and there was no Polish state to receive it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Polish republic was proclaimed on 3 November 1918, the United States recognised its provisional government the following January, and an American legation opened here on 2 May 1919 under its first minister, Hugh S. Gibson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy in 1930, after the first appointee died before he could be sworn in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city then held about 1.3 million people, more than 350,000 of them Jews, which made it the largest Jewish community in Europe and the second largest anywhere after New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany invaded on 1 September 1939, the ambassador left four days later and followed the government to France and then London, and German troops took the city on 27 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> German officials decreed a ghetto here in October 1940 and sealed it the next month behind a wall over three metres (ten feet) high, forcing an estimated 400,000 people into 3.4 square kilometres (1.3 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 22 July and 21 September 1942 SS and police units deported about 265,000 of them to the Treblinka killing centre, and those left rose on 19 April 1943 and resisted four weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Home Army rose in August 1944 while Soviet forces nearby refused to intervene, the Germans crushed the revolt and razed the city centre that October, and Soviet troops entered the ruins on 17 January 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American embassy was re-established here on 31 July 1945, the state governed from the city has held a United Nations seat since 24 October 1945, and the alliance signed at the Presidential Palace here on 14 May 1955 took the city’s name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations table gives the capital 1,775,900 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, exactly what the World Bank series gives for that year, on which the city now stands at about 1.80 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Poland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pl.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Poland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/poland. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, “Warsaw,” Holocaust Encyclopedia, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/warsaw. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Warsaw Treaty Organization, 1955,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1953-1960/warsaw-treaty. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Poland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/POL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Skyline_of_Warsaw%2C_Poland.jpg/1920px-Skyline_of_Warsaw%2C_Poland.jpg","title":"Warsaw","desc":"The centre of Warsaw in winter, the Palace of Culture and Science at its middle.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Warsaw,_Poland.jpg","alt":"A wide aerial view of central Warsaw under snow, with high-rise towers behind a tall stepped skyscraper."}},
{"id":"gw-543","num":543,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tashkent","answerText":"Tashkent","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consul held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 20 October 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uzbekistan","dot":"Tashkent"},"facts":[["Country","Uzbekistan"],["Population","2.70M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+9.3% (2018–2021)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","uzbekistan"],"abstract":"<b>Tashkent</b> was a city of Turkestan when the United States first kept a consul there, and that consul, Roger C. Tredwell, had been under arrest in it since 20 October 1918, with the Department labouring in vain for his release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American guide to recognition records no consular post in the country at all and dates relations with Uzbekistan only to the break-up of the Soviet Union, of which it had been a constituent republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city later became the venue for somebody else’s peace, President Ayub of Pakistan and Prime Minister Shastri of India meeting here from 4 to 10 January 1966 over the conflict grown out of the Kashmir dispute, with Soviet Chairman Kosygin promoting the agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the terms of the Tashkent Declaration signed on the last of those days, both withdrew their forces to the positions held before 5 August 1965 and reaffirmed their Charter obligation not to have recourse to force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Shastri died in the city of a heart attack the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Independence came with the Soviet Union’s dissolution, and the United States recognised it on 25 December 1991, in the address President George H. W. Bush made to the nation that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly resolved to admit the Republic of Uzbekistan to membership of the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations had been established a fortnight before that, on 19 February, and the American embassy in the city followed on 16 March 1992 with Michael Mozur as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has gone on being where other states sign things, the heads of the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation adopting a second Tashkent Declaration at their summit here on 17 June 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The World Bank’s series for the country’s largest city puts it at about 2.67 million in 2025, while the United Nations table prints 2,490,300 under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, which is the same series six years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Uzbekistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/uz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uzbekistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uzbekistan. [Open access]","“The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in France (Sharp), Washington, February 4, 1919,” document 136 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1919, Russia</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Russia/d136. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 278 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1964–1968, Volume XXV, South Asia</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1964-68v25/d278. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Uzbekistan to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution 46/226 of 2 March 1992, A/RES/46/226, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/147690. [Open access]","Permanent Representative of Uzbekistan to the United Nations, letter dated 1 July 2004 addressed to the Secretary-General, transmitting the Tashkent Declaration of Heads of Shanghai Cooperation Organization States, A/59/124–S/2004/532, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/525959. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Uzbekistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/UZB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Tashkent_skyline_2019.jpg","title":"Tashkent","desc":"Tashkent at night, seen from above.","credit":"Guidecity, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tashkent_skyline_2019.jpg","alt":"A wide night view of Tashkent, its lit avenues running out across the city."}},
{"id":"gw-544","num":544,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the national capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kuala Lumpur","answerText":"Kuala Lumpur","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In GATT</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1957</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malaysia","dot":"Kuala Lumpur"},"facts":[["Country","Malaysia"],["Population","9.00M in Greater Kuala Lumpur (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+24.8% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","malaysia"],"abstract":"<b>Kuala Lumpur</b> had an American consular post from 1948, nine years before the state it now heads existed, the United States having opened its first consulate in the country at Penang in 1918.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Federation of Malaya became independent on 31 August 1957 and was recognised the same day, in a letter from Secretary of State John Foster Dulles to Prime Minister Tunku Abdul Rahman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on that date by raising the consulate general in this city to an embassy, with the consul general Thomas K. Wright placed in charge as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new state joined the Commonwealth that year, its membership dated by the Secretariat to the federation’s independence from Britain, and the United Nations on 17 September 1957.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade five weeks after independence, on 24 October 1957, and became a member of the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> In 1963 the federation joined with Singapore, Sarawak and Borneo to form the Federation of Malaysia, after which every American ambassador was accredited to that federation and resident in this city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Johnson landed here at 10:11 in the morning of 30 October 1966 and left before eight the next morning, his delegation’s time spent in ceremonial activities and a state dinner at Parliament House.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city is the capital without being the whole of the government, and the United Nations profile says so in a footnote to its capital field: Kuala Lumpur is the capital and Putrajaya the administrative capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat gives the country 330,000 square kilometres (127,000 square miles) against the United Nations figure of 330,621 for 2023, a difference of two hundredths of one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s series for the largest city puts Greater Kuala Lumpur at about 9.0 million in 2025, while the United Nations table prints 7,780,300 under footnotes saying that it too refers to the greater city and dates it to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Malaysia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/my.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malaysia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malaysia. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Malaysia,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/malaysia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Malaysia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/malaysia_e.htm. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 275 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1964–1968, Volume XXVI, Indonesia; Malaysia-Singapore; Philippines</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1964-68v26/d275. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Malaysia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MYS/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Kuala_Lumpur_Skyline_from_KL_Tower.jpg/1920px-Kuala_Lumpur_Skyline_from_KL_Tower.jpg","title":"Kuala Lumpur","desc":"Kuala Lumpur seen from the KL Tower, the Petronas Towers at its centre.","credit":"Sławomir Gajowniczek, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuala_Lumpur_Skyline_from_KL_Tower.jpg","alt":"A dense city of towers seen from above, twin spired skyscrapers at its middle and hills behind."}},
{"id":"gw-545","num":545,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Riyadh","answerText":"Riyadh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1942, at Jidda</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 September 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saudi Arabia","dot":"Riyadh"},"facts":[["Country","Saudi Arabia"],["Population","8.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+7.7% (2016–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","saudi arabia"],"abstract":"<b>Riyadh</b> was the king’s seat long before it was where foreign governments kept their embassies, and the United States recognised the Kingdom of Hejaz and Nejd and its Dependencies on 1 May 1931, the state taking its present name by a decree of 18 September 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 4 February 1940, when Bert Fish presented his credentials as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary, accredited also to Egypt and resident at Cairo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American legation opened at Jidda on 1 May 1942 with James S. Moose, Jr. as chargé d’affaires ad interim, and was raised to an embassy on 18 March 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Business with the king was therefore done by travelling inland, and in May 1945 the American minister flew from Jidda to this city with an army colonel and came back three days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He brought the king’s permission for an airfield at Dhahran and his wish that a survey team should settle with him the route of a road from here to the Persian Gulf, on which he held strong views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A consulate general has stood at Dhahran, on the coast that road was meant to reach, since 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy itself did not follow the government inland until 26 September 1984, when the American liaison office here was raised to embassy rank and the one at Jidda became a consulate general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state governed from this city has held a United Nations seat since 24 October 1945, forty years before the embassy arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 11 December 2005, later than most of its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank’s series for the country’s largest city puts Riyadh at about 7.95 million in 2025, while the United Nations table prints 7,070,700 under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, which is the same series six years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saudi Arabia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sa.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saudi Arabia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saudi-arabia. [Open access]","“The Minister in Saudi Arabia (Eddy) to the Secretary of State, Jidda, May 13, 1945,” document 874 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1945, The Near East and Africa, Volume VIII</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1945v08/d874. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Saudi Arabia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/saudi_arabia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Saudi Arabia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SAU/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Riyadh_Skyline.jpg/1920px-Riyadh_Skyline.jpg","title":"Riyadh","desc":"Riyadh at dusk, its towers above the low city around them.","credit":"B.alotaby, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riyadh_Skyline.jpg","alt":"A city skyline at dusk under a violet sky, several tall towers rising from low buildings."}},
{"id":"gw-546","num":546,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Maputo","answerText":"Maputo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 June 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mozambique","dot":"Maputo"},"facts":[["Country","Mozambique"],["Population","1.10M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-4.9% (2018–2023)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","mozambique"],"abstract":"<b>Maputo</b> was called Lourenço Marques while the territory was Portuguese, and American shipping used the port long before there was a country to have relations with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1889 the captain of the American bark <i>Carrie Heckle</i> was fined £15 by the customs authorities there for breaking a port regulation, and the Portuguese foreign ministry wrote to the American legation at Lisbon that August to say the money would be returned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Portugal agreed with the Front for the Liberation of Mozambique in September 1974 that the territory would become independent on 25 June 1975, after an eleven-year guerrilla war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seven weeks before that date the President’s assistant for national security affairs put it to President Ford that the consulate general in this city should be raised to an embassy, arguing that the new state would be recognised promptly by most nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recognition came on the day of independence itself, in a letter from Ford to President Samora Moises Machel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations were established on 23 September 1975, when Secretary of State Henry Kissinger and Foreign Minister Joachim Alberto Chissano initialled a joint communiqué.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy opened here on 8 November 1975 under Johnnie Carson as chargé d’affaires ad interim, and the guide still files it under the city’s Portuguese name with the present one in brackets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state governed from here had taken its United Nations seat on 16 September 1975, and joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1992 and the World Trade Organization on 26 August 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It entered the Commonwealth in the same year, the Secretariat dating that to the country’s first democratic elections rather than to any British rule, of which there was none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations table gives the city about 1,104,300 people under a footnote dating the figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mozambique,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mozambique,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mozambique. [Open access]","“Memorandum From the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs (Kissinger) to President Ford, Washington, May 5, 1975,” document 104 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1969–1976, Volume XXVIII, Southern Africa</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76v28/d104. [Open access]","“Mr. Loring to Mr. Blaine, Legation of the United States, Lisbon, August 9, 1889,” document 288 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, Transmitted to Congress, With the Annual Message of the President, December 3, 1889</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1889/d288. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mozambique and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mozambique_e.htm. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Mozambique,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/mozambique. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Maputo_skyline_2006.jpg/1920px-Maputo_skyline_2006.jpg","title":"Maputo","desc":"Maputo rising behind the cranes and sheds of its port.","credit":"Julien Lagarde, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maputo_skyline_2006.jpg","alt":"A city of apartment blocks on a slope, seen across a harbour with a crane, sheds and tugs in front of it."}},
{"id":"gw-547","num":547,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Accra","answerText":"Accra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 March 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 March 1957</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15–22 April 1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In GATT</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1957</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ghana","dot":"Accra"},"facts":[["Country","Ghana"],["Population","2.50M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+37.4% (2000–2013)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ghana"],"abstract":"<b>Accra</b> was the seat of a British colony called the Gold Coast, and the American post there was only a consulate until the approach of self-government made Washington think again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A memorandum of 25 June 1953 proposed raising it to a consulate general with a principal officer of that rank, on the ground that the colony had won a considerable measure of self-government by constitutional reform since the war and was, after Southern Rhodesia, the most politically advanced African territory under European rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That memorandum was written five days after a Gold Coast white paper proposed further constitutional changes ahead of a request to Britain for complete independence within the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Independence from the British Empire was proclaimed on 6 March 1957 for the territory formerly known as the Gold Coast, and the United States recognised the new state the same day in a press statement from the Department of State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that date the consulate general in this city became an embassy, with the consul general Donald W. Lamm left in charge as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The state took its United Nations seat two days later, on 8 March 1957, and joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade that October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It entered the Commonwealth in the same year, the Secretariat dating that membership to independence from Britain and rounding the country’s area to 238,500 square kilometres where the United Nations gives 238,537.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city then became a meeting place for the continent: the first Conference of Independent African States met here from 15 to 22 April 1958 and issued a joint declaration on the last day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Secretary of State Dulles had sent Prime Minister Kwame Nkrumah a message of good wishes for the conference on 14 April, the day before it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations table gives the city about 2,475,200 people under a footnote dating the figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ghana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gh.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ghana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ghana. [Open access]","“Memorandum by the Deputy Assistant Secretary of State for Near Eastern, South Asian, and African Affairs (Jernegan) to the Under Secretary of State for Administration (Lourie), June 25, 1953,” document 118 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1952–1954, Africa and South Asia, Volume XI, Part 1</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v11p1/d118. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Ghana and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/ghana_e.htm. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Ghana,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/ghana. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 2 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960, Africa, Volume XIV</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v14/d2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/View_of_Greater_Accra%2C_Kitase_%28P1090797%29.jpg/1920px-View_of_Greater_Accra%2C_Kitase_%28P1090797%29.jpg","title":"Greater Accra","desc":"Greater Accra seen from the hills at Kitase.","credit":"Matti Blume, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Greater_Accra%2C_Kitase_%28P1090797%29.jpg","alt":"A wide city plain of low houses stretching to the horizon under a rain cloud, seen from high ground."}},
{"id":"gw-548","num":548,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lima","answerText":"Lima","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 May 1827</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 April 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 December 1938</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Peru","dot":"Lima"},"facts":[["Country","Peru"],["Population","11.5M with Callao (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+16.0% (2017–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","peru"],"abstract":"<b>Lima</b> has held an American mission since nearly the beginning of Peruvian independence, relations having been established in 1826, five years after independence was proclaimed in July 1821 under General San Martin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation in the city opened on 21 May 1827, when James Cooley presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires, and it was raised to an embassy almost a century later, on 24 April 1920, under Ambassador William E. Gonzales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consular post was styled the consulate general at Callao-Lima, pairing the capital with its port in one office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From it, at eight in the morning of 4 July 1919, the consul general cabled that two regiments had taken the Government Palace three hours earlier, imprisoned President Pardo and most of his cabinet and installed Augusto B. Leguia as president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The army was patrolling the city, he added, and he did not anticipate trouble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Nineteen years later the city gave its name to an agreement covering the whole hemisphere, when the Eighth International Conference of American States met here from 9 to 27 December 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 24 December the chairman of the American delegation, Secretary of State Cordell Hull, cabled that 21 countries had signed the solidarity declaration and that he would speak at the plenary session that afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The declaration’s fifth article laid down that it should be known as the Declaration of Lima.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state governed from this city has held a United Nations seat since 31 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations table prints 10,554,700 people for 2019, which is what the World Bank’s series gives for that year, and its footnote says what is being counted: the Province of Lima together with the Constitutional Province of Callao, which on the same series held about 11.5 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Peru,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pe.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Peru,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/peru. [Open access]","“The Chairman of the American Delegation (Hull) to the Acting Secretary of State, Lima, December 24, 1938,” document 66 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The American Republics, Volume V</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v05/d66. [Open access]","“The Consul General at Callao-Lima (Handley) to the Acting Secretary of State, Lima, July 4, 1919,” document 693 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1919, Volume II</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919v02/d693. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Peru,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PER/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Lima%2C_Urban_Skyline--Ciudad_de_Lima%2C_Skyline_urbano.jpg/1920px-Lima%2C_Urban_Skyline--Ciudad_de_Lima%2C_Skyline_urbano.jpg","title":"Lima","desc":"Lima along the cliffs of its Pacific shore.","credit":"Joshua87, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lima%2C_Urban_Skyline--Ciudad_de_Lima%2C_Skyline_urbano.jpg","alt":"A long beach below brown cliffs, with the buildings of a city running along the clifftop into the haze."}},
{"id":"gw-549","num":549,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Antananarivo","answerText":"Antananarivo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 8 July 1916</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In WTO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 November 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Madagascar","dot":"Antananarivo"},"facts":[["Country","Madagascar"],["Population","4.20M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-9.1% (2001–2018)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","madagascar"],"abstract":"<b>Antananarivo</b> is inland, and for half a century the American post on the island was not there but at the port of Tamatave, where a commercial agent was appointed in 1866 and a consul in 1874.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> France assumed sovereignty over the island as a colony in 1890, and the American consulate moved up from the coast to the highland capital, then spelled Tananarivo, on or about 8 July 1916.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From that office in March 1942 the consul general reported a conversation with the governor general, who had just declared that the island “has but one will, to remain French”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Malagasy Republic became independent on 26 June 1960, and a message from President Eisenhower dated the day before was delivered that morning by the consul general, J. Roland Jacobs, as he presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Department of State had announced the day before that the consulate general in the city would become an embassy as of independence day itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Frederic P. Bartlett was appointed the first ambassador on 27 August 1960 and presented his credentials on 5 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state governed from here took its United Nations seat on 20 September 1960, between those two dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 17 November 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city is spelled three ways in that American record — Tananarivo when the consulate moved in 1916, Tananarive in 1942 and in 1960 — and the guide still files the post under the old name with the present one in brackets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That table gives it about 3,210,400 people under a footnote dating the figure to 2019, and the same series puts it at roughly 4.2 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Madagascar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mg.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Madagascar,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/madagascar. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 60 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960, Africa, Volume XIV</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v14/d60. [Open access]","“The Consul General at Tananarive (Carter) to the Secretary of State, Tananarive, March 14, 1942,” document 689 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1942, Europe, Volume II</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1942v02/d689. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Madagascar and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/madagascar_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Madagascar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDG/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Panorama_of_Antananarivo_%283953594664%29.jpg/1920px-Panorama_of_Antananarivo_%283953594664%29.jpg","title":"Antananarivo","desc":"Antananarivo from the hills above it, with Lake Anosy and the stadium in the middle distance.","credit":"Antony Stanley, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_Antananarivo_%283953594664%29.jpg","alt":"A wide panorama of a hill city of red-roofed houses, with a lake and a stadium among them."}},
{"id":"gw-550","num":550,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the official capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Yamoussoukro","answerText":"Yamoussoukro","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Côte d’Ivoire, 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accord</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29–30 October 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Côte d'Ivoire","dot":"Yamoussoukro"},"facts":[["Country","Côte d’Ivoire"],["Population","231k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","+20.8% (2014–2021)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ivory coast"],"abstract":"<b>Yamoussoukro</b> is the capital of Côte d’Ivoire and the smaller of its two seats, the United Nations profile footnoting its capital field to say that Yamoussoukro is the capital and Abidjan the administrative capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difference shows in the figures: the same table gives this city about 231,100 people against some 32.7 million in the country as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every step of the American relationship happened in the other city, beginning with recognition of the Republic of the Ivory Coast on 7 August 1960, in a letter from President Eisenhower to Prime Minister Felix Houphouet-Boigny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That letter was delivered the same day by the consul general at Abidjan, Donald R. Norland, as he presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires, and the consulate general there was raised to an embassy on that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first ambassador presented his credentials on 20 November, again at Abidjan, and the state’s name changed from Ivory Coast to Côte d’Ivoire in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> This city has nevertheless given its name to an agreement of its own, made while the war in a neighbouring country was being negotiated here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Economic Community of West African States held the fourth meeting of its Committee of Five on the Liberian crisis here on 29 and 30 October 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The final communiqué of that meeting was circulated to the Security Council a year later by the permanent representative of Benin, and the United Nations catalogues what it produced as the Yamoussoukro Accord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade at the end of 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has held a United Nations seat since 20 September 1960, six weeks after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Côte d’Ivoire,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ci.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cote d’Ivoire (Ivory Coast),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cote-divoire. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 67 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960, Africa, Volume XIV</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v14/d67. [Open access]","Permanent Representative of Benin to the United Nations, letter dated 17 November 1992 addressed to the President of the Security Council, transmitting the final communiqué of the fourth meeting of the ECOWAS Committee of Five on the Liberian crisis, Yamoussoukro, 29–30 October 1991, S/24815, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/154389. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Côte d’Ivoire and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cote_ivoire_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Yamoussoukro_panorama.jpg/1920px-Yamoussoukro_panorama.jpg","title":"Yamoussoukro","desc":"Yamoussoukro among its lakes and palm groves.","credit":"Mickaël T., CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamoussoukro_panorama.jpg","alt":"A low town spread along a wooded plain, with lakes and rows of palms in front of it."}},
{"id":"gw-551","num":551,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kathmandu","answerText":"Kathmandu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In WTO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nepal","dot":"Kathmandu"},"facts":[["Country","Nepal"],["Population","1.70M in the municipality (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","nepal"],"abstract":"<b>Kathmandu</b> received an American mission before the United States had an embassy anywhere in the country: Joseph C. Satterthwaite came on a special mission as the President’s personal representative and, on 21 April 1947, presented a letter from President Truman to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nepal set up its own legation in Washington on 16 February 1948, but its minister was also ambassador in London and the whole staff lived at the Nepalese embassy there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy in this city was not established until 6 August 1959, with L. Douglas Heck as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By then the city had become a place where the great powers competed for a small kingdom’s attention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A country team message of April 1959 listed what the American mission had counted: more visits by Soviet officials, 15 scholarships to Soviet universities, gifts to the royal family including an Ilyushin aircraft, Soviet films, a Nepali edition of a Soviet magazine and an effort to acquire a printing press in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The state had joined the United Nations three and a half years earlier, on 14 December 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization much later, on 23 April 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations table gives the capital about 1,376,100 people under two footnotes, one dating the figure to 2019 and one saying that it refers to the municipality rather than to any wider valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the same figure the World Bank’s series gives for 2019, and the series has the municipality at roughly 1.67 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American economic aid to the country had begun in 1951, four years after that first letter and eight before the embassy opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nepal,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/np.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nepal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nepal. [Open access]","“Telegram From the Embassy in India to the Department of State, New Delhi, April 30, 1959,” document 280 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960, South and Southeast Asia, Volume XV</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v15/d280. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Nepal and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/nepal_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Nepal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NPL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/View_of_Kathmandu_from_Swayambhunath%2C_Nepal.jpg/1920px-View_of_Kathmandu_from_Swayambhunath%2C_Nepal.jpg","title":"Kathmandu","desc":"Kathmandu filling its valley, seen from Swayambhunath.","credit":"Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Kathmandu_from_Swayambhunath%2C_Nepal.jpg","alt":"A dense city spread across a valley floor to distant hills, with strings of prayer flags in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gw-552","num":552,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Yaoundé","answerText":"Yaoundé","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Union</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cameroon","dot":"Yaoundé"},"facts":[["Country","Cameroon"],["Population","4.90M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+34.3% (2005–2012)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","cameroon"],"abstract":"<b>Yaoundé</b> was the seat of a United Nations trust territory before it was the capital of a state, and the American post there was a consulate general until the trusteeship ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 1 January 1960 its consul general, Bolard More, notified the authorities that the post was being raised to an embassy, which was how the United States recognised the Republic of Cameroon, and the trusteeship France had administered ended the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The other Cameroons, under British administration, were still a trust territory, and the General Assembly settled their future on 21 April 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It endorsed the results of two plebiscites held that February, in which the north had decided to become independent by joining the Federation of Nigeria and the south by joining the Republic of Cameroun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Assembly therefore terminated the trusteeship agreement of 1946 in two stages: for the Northern Cameroons on 1 June 1961 and for the Southern Cameroons on 1 October 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The state governed from this city had taken its United Nations seat on 20 September 1960, between the two dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade in 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 13 December 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations table gives the capital about 3,822,400 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s series for the country’s largest city gives the same number for the same year, so it is measuring this city and not the port at Douala, and it puts it at roughly 4.85 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country it governs holds about 29.9 million people in 475,650 square kilometres (183,650 square miles), so roughly one Cameroonian in six lives in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cameroon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cameroon,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cameroon. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “The Future of the Trust Territory of the Cameroons under United Kingdom Administration,” resolution 1608 (XV) of 21 April 1961, A/RES/1608(XV), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206162. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cameroon and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cameroon_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Cameroon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CMR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Yaound%C3%A9_view_from_central_market_%282014%29.JPG/1920px-Yaound%C3%A9_view_from_central_market_%282014%29.JPG","title":"Yaoundé","desc":"Central Yaoundé seen from the central market, with the hills behind it.","credit":"Bdx, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaound%C3%A9_view_from_central_market_%282014%29.JPG","alt":"A city centre of low shops and tall blocks seen from a roof, with wooded hills on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-553","num":553,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Caracas","answerText":"Caracas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1835</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 November 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1–28 March 1954</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Venezuela","dot":"Caracas"},"facts":[["Country","Venezuela"],["Population","3.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+15.5% (2011–2017)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","venezuela"],"abstract":"<b>Caracas</b> did not become the seat of a separate state until 1830, the country having achieved independence from Spain by 1819 as part of the Republic of Colombia, from which it then separated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American consulate in what is now Venezuela had opened at the port of Maracaibo in 1824 and stayed open until 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition came on 28 February 1835 with an exequatur to a Venezuelan consul in New York, and diplomatic relations followed on 30 June, when John G. A. Williamson presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation in this city was not raised to an embassy until 1939, with Frank P. Corrigan as the first ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before that, its ministers reported from a capital that was periodically cut off: in October 1899 one of them cabled that the president had sailed the night before, for an unknown destination, while communication with the city was cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The state governed from here has held a United Nations seat since 15 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was host in 1954 to the Tenth Inter-American Conference, which sat there from 1 to 28 March, the whole month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It adopted 117 resolutions, declarations and agreements, of which the American delegation voted against four and abstained on twelve, a record of dissent set down item by item in the American diplomatic papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two of the conventions signed on the last day, on diplomatic and on territorial asylum, the American delegation did not sign at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives the capital about 2,935,500 people under footnotes dating the figure to 2019 and saying that it covers several municipalities and parishes; the World Bank’s series, which gives the same number for that year, puts it near 3.0 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Venezuela (Bolivarian Republic of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ve.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Venezuela,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/venezuela. [Open access]","“Mr. Loomis to the Secretary of State, Legation of the United States, Caracas, October 21, 1899,” document 790 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, With the Annual Message of the President Transmitted to Congress December 5, 1899</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1899/d790. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 79 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1952–1954, The American Republics, Volume IV</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1952-54v04/d79. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Venezuela,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VEN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Caracas_skyline_and_Avila_mountain_from_my_penthouse.JPG/1920px-Caracas_skyline_and_Avila_mountain_from_my_penthouse.JPG","title":"Caracas","desc":"Caracas below the Ávila range that closes it off from the coast.","credit":"Flauta, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caracas_skyline_and_Avila_mountain_from_my_penthouse.JPG","alt":"A dense city of apartment towers running towards a long mountain ridge under cloud."}},
{"id":"gw-554","num":554,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Canberra","answerText":"Canberra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1901</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 July 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 1946</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Australia","dot":"Canberra"},"facts":[["Country","Australia"],["Population","453k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","8th largest"],["Population growth","+24.2% (2014–2024)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","australia"],"abstract":"<b>Canberra</b> is the seat of a federation made on 1 January 1901, when six colonies were joined together as the Commonwealth of Australia, a self-governing dominion whose external relations the United Kingdom still controlled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 called the dominions equal in status and in no way subordinate, and the Statute of Westminster of December 1931 put that in law, though it took effect for this country only when its own adoption act became law on 9 October 1942, retroactive to the day war began in 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat accordingly dates Australia’s membership of it to 1931 rather than to the federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For most of that time the United States kept no mission in the capital at all, but a consul general at Sydney who travelled to it: in December 1937 he cabled that an official had telephoned from Melbourne to ask him to come to Canberra on the Monday to go over the position on trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were announced on 8 January 1940 and an American legation was established here on 17 July, when Clarence E. Gauss presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legations became embassies in 1946, the White House announcing it on 9 July and the first American ambassador presenting his credentials on 25 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state had joined the United Nations a year earlier, on 1 November 1945, and the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade at its beginning in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has been a member of the World Trade Organization since 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The city itself is small for a capital: the United Nations gives it about 452,500 people for 2019, in a country of nearly 27 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country it governs covers 7,692,024 square kilometres (2,969,900 square miles) on the United Nations figure, which counts Norfolk Island in, where the Commonwealth Secretariat rounds the same area to 7.7 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Australia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/au.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Australia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/australia. [Open access]","“The Consul General at Sydney (Wilson) to the Secretary of State, received December 17, 1937,” document 115 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The British Commonwealth, Europe, Near East and Africa, Volume II</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v02/d115. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Australia,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/australia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Australia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/australia_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Canberra_panorama_from_Mount_Ainslie.jpg/1920px-Canberra_panorama_from_Mount_Ainslie.jpg","title":"Canberra","desc":"Canberra from Mount Ainslie, looking down the land axis of the planned city.","credit":"The 3B's, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canberra_panorama_from_Mount_Ainslie.jpg","alt":"A city laid out around a lake in a wooded basin, with a straight avenue running to a hill beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-555","num":555,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Niamey","answerText":"Niamey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 February 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In WTO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 December 1996</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Niger","dot":"Niamey"},"facts":[["Country","Niger"],["Population","1.60M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+48.8% (2001–2012)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","niger"],"abstract":"<b>Niamey</b> became a capital on 3 August 1960, when the Republic of Niger became independent and President Eisenhower sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Hamani Diori.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The message was delivered by Donald R. Norland as he presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires, but Norland was the consul general at Abidjan and lived there, not here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So did the first American ambassador to Niger, J. Borden Reams, who presented his credentials on 26 November of that year and was also resident in another country’s capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American embassy was not established in this city until 3 February 1961, six months after independence, with Joseph W. Schutz as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state had taken its United Nations seat on 20 September 1960, in the interval between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade at the end of 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 13 December 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American guide describes it as a charter member of the African Union and of the West African Monetary Union, and a member of the river and lake basin commissions that manage the water it shares.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It records too that an American Peace Corps programme has run in the country since 1962, and that American aid is administered through non-governmental organisations rather than through a development mission of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the capital about 1,251,500 people under a footnote dating the figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s series for the largest city gives the same number for that year, so it is measuring this city, and puts it at roughly 1.56 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Niger,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ne.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Niger,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/niger. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 64 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960, Africa, Volume XIV</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v14/d64. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Niger and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/niger_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Niger,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NER/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Niamey_from_the_sky.jpg/1920px-Niamey_from_the_sky.jpg","title":"Niamey","desc":"Niamey from the air, its flat-roofed compounds running to the edge of the city.","credit":"Jean Rebiffé, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niamey_from_the_sky.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a city of low earth-coloured compounds and sandy streets."}},
{"id":"gw-556","num":556,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Pyongyang","answerText":"Pyongyang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan approved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 September 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Summits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13–15 June 2000</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">2–4 October 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"North Korea","dot":"Pyongyang"},"facts":[["Country","North Korea"],["Population","3.20M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+17.6% (1909–1912)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","north korea"],"abstract":"<b>Pyongyang</b> has been the seat of the northern Korean government since the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea was established in September 1948, three months before Soviet troops left the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American intelligence assessment of 1950 set the words ‘capital city’ in quotation marks, and reported that the Soviet embassy there headed a mission of four to five thousand advisers placed through the government and the economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The division of the peninsula was plain in the city two years earlier, when a liaison officer delivered a letter from the United Nations commission on Korea to the Soviet chief of staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was told that Soviet forces would have no dealings with the commission, and that unification could be accomplished only at a level such as Molotov and Marshall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the city about 3,060,900 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, and the World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for that year and 3.21 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> When the war came the city was a named objective: the plan approved on 29 September 1950 sent the Eighth Army up the western coastal corridor to capture it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the following July the fighting had settled, and the two commands arranged their first liaison meeting by messages about a convoy of jeeps and trucks leaving the area under white flags.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those messages had to say whose clock they meant, the northern party setting out at five in the afternoon Pyongyang time and the other delegates crossing the Imjin at nine Tokyo time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The city has lent its name to an agreement too: the Pyongyang Declaration and Plan of Action on South-South Co-operation came from a conference of non-aligned countries held here in June 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The General Assembly has twice welcomed a meeting of the two Korean leaders here, the summit of 13 to 15 June 2000 and that of 2 to 4 October 2007 with its declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["“Memorandum by the Central Intelligence Agency,” document 57 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1950, Korea, Volume VII</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1950v07/d57. [Open access]","“The Political Adviser in Korea (Jacobs) to the Secretary of State,” 31 January 1948, document 715 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1948, The Far East and Australasia, Volume VI</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1948v06/d715. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic People’s Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kp.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Korea, Dem. People’s Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRK/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","“The Secretary of Defense (Marshall) to the Commander in Chief, Far East (MacArthur),” 29 September 1950, document 573 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1950, Korea, Volume VII</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1950v07/d573. [Open access]","“Editorial Note,” document 409 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1951, Korea and China, Volume VII, Part 1</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1951v07p1/d409. [Open access]","UNCTAD Secretariat, “Pyongyang Declaration and Plan of Action on South-South Co-operation,” TD/339/Add.1, 21 July 1987, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/137773. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Resolution 55/11: Peace, security and reunification on the Korean peninsula,” adopted 31 October 2000, A/RES/55/11, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/425985. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Resolution 62/5: Peace, security and reunification on the Korean peninsula,” adopted 31 October 2007, A/RES/62/5, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/610691. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Pyongyang_view_from_Juche_Tower_%2815957081049%29.jpg/1920px-Pyongyang_view_from_Juche_Tower_%2815957081049%29.jpg","title":"Pyongyang from the Juche Tower","desc":"The city seen from the Juche Tower, its blocks running out to the hills.","credit":"Uri Tours, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pyongyang_view_from_Juche_Tower_%2815957081049%29.jpg","alt":"A wide high view over a city of regular apartment blocks and wide avenues, with low hills on the horizon."},"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":""},
{"id":"gw-557","num":557,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Damascus","answerText":"Damascus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 September 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Downgraded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 February 1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy restored</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 June 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Syria","dot":"Damascus"},"facts":[["Country","Syria"],["Population","2.80M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+12.9% (2003–2011)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","syria"],"abstract":"<b>Damascus</b> was the seat of a French mandate before it was the capital of a state, and in the autumn of 1925 it was in revolt against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 19 October the American consul at Beirut reported that two thirds of the city was in the hands of revolutionists, who were expected to attack about 2,000 French troops, and that the railway from Beirut had been cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four days later he reported a demand on the city for 100,000 Turkish gold pounds and 3,000 rifles by noon the following day, failing which the bombardment would recommence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A commission of seven representing the people of the city went to Beirut to tell the French High Commissioner that it could pay neither the money nor the arms, and that if he began again it must be on his own responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised Syrian independence on 8 September 1944, after the foreign minister gave written assurances about American rights there, and George Wadsworth presented his credentials as minister on 17 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Syria joined Egypt as the United Arab Republic in February 1958 the new state governed from Cairo, and the American embassy here was reclassified as a consulate general on the 25th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was raised to an embassy again on 10 October 1961, twelve days after Syria left the union, with Ridgway B. Knight as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Syria severed relations on 6 June 1967, and it was nearly seven years before an American interests section opened here, inside the Italian embassy, on 8 February 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From that interests section on 21 May Henry Kissinger cabled Algiers while negotiating a disengagement agreement, and the embassy itself was restored on 16 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives the city about 2,353,600 people under footnotes dating that figure to 2019 and warning that such estimates are drawn from scarce data, and the World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for that year and 2.8 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Syria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/syria. [Open access]","“The Consul at Beirut (Knabenshue) to the Secretary of State,” 19 October 1925, document 78 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1925, Volume II</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1925v02/d78. [Open access]","“The Consul at Beirut (Knabenshue) to the Secretary of State,” 23 October 1925, document 81 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1925, Volume II</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1925v02/d81. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Syrian Arab Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sy.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Syrian Arab Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","“Telegram 828/Secto 574 From the Interests Section in Syria to the Interests Section in Algeria,” 21 May 1974, document 2 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1969–1976, Volume E-9, Part 1, Documents on the Middle East Region, 1973–1976</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76ve09p1/d2. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Damascus%2C_Syria%2C_Panoramic_view_of_Damascus.jpg/1920px-Damascus%2C_Syria%2C_Panoramic_view_of_Damascus.jpg","title":"Damascus","desc":"Damascus seen from the slope of Mount Qasioun, running out across its plain.","credit":"Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damascus%2C_Syria%2C_Panoramic_view_of_Damascus.jpg","alt":"A very wide high view over a dense low-rise city stretching to hills on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-558","num":558,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bamako","answerText":"Bamako","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Federation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic of Mali</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 September 1960</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mali","dot":"Bamako"},"facts":[["Country","Mali"],["Population","3.20M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+54.4% (1987–1998)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","mali"],"abstract":"<b>Bamako</b> was not the capital of the state that first became independent here: on 20 June 1960 the Federation of Mali, made up of Senegal and the Soudanese Republic, left French sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> President Eisenhower recognised the federation that day in a message to President Modibo Keita, and the American consulate general at Dakar was raised to an embassy under Donald A. Dumont.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Senegal withdrew on 20 August, and on 22 September a party conference held in this city decided that the Soudanese Republic would take the name Republic of Mali and withdraw the powers it had granted the federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The acting secretary of state told Eisenhower the next day that by tacitly admitting the federation no longer existed the Soudanese had made it easier to recognise the two countries separately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He added that Washington was anxious to avoid a repetition of the Guinean experience, and proposed letters to both heads of state, to be delivered by the posts at Dakar and here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Recognition followed on 24 September, when the American consul in this city, John Gunther Dean, informed the Malian government on instructions from Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The consulate general here was raised to an embassy the same day with Dean as chargé d’affaires ad interim, and the embassy at Dakar was reaccredited to Senegal, which formally ended relations with the federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly admitted the two states at one sitting four days later, its 876th plenary meeting on 28 September 1960, on Security Council recommendations made that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 11 January 1993 and the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives the city about 2,529,300 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, and the World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for that year and 3.18 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mali,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mali. [Open access]","“Memorandum From Acting Secretary of State Dillon to President Eisenhower,” 23 September 1960, document 75 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1958–1960, Africa, Volume XIV</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1958-60v14/d75. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Mali to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1491 (XV) of 28 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206141. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Mali and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/mali_e.htm. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Mali,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ml.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Mali,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Bamako_ACI_2000_Aeriel.jpg/1920px-Bamako_ACI_2000_Aeriel.jpg","title":"Bamako","desc":"The ACI 2000 quarter of Bamako from the air, with the Niger beyond it.","credit":"Mark Fischer, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamako_ACI_2000_Aeriel.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a low-rise city of red earth and scattered blocks, with a river across the far distance."}},
{"id":"gw-559","num":559,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ouagadougou","answerText":"Ouagadougou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 December 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Burkina Faso","dot":"Ouagadougou"},"facts":[["Country","Burkina Faso"],["Population","3.50M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+10.3% (2006–2012)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","burkina faso"],"abstract":"<b>Ouagadougou</b> has been a capital under two names: the state became independent as Upper Volta on 5 August 1960 and changed its name to Burkina Faso in 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> President Eisenhower recognised it on the day of independence in a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Maurice Yameogo, and diplomatic relations began the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chargé d’affaires ad interim who took up the post was Donald R. Norland, who had presented his credentials the day before to take effect on the 5th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American guide records the same man presenting his credentials to Niger two days earlier as the consul general at Abidjan, resident there, so one officer opened relations with two new states in one week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No American embassy was established in this city until 31 December 1960, five months after independence, with Robert McKinnon as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state took its United Nations seat on 20 September 1960 under the name Upper Volta, admitted at the 864th plenary meeting of the General Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A year later the post here was among those asked what its host country would make of the American decision against the Volta River Project in neighbouring Ghana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It replied that strength was respected by the Voltans, and that millions for Ghana beside a plea of no funds for the friendly Entente states would be almost impossible to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 3 May 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 3 June 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations gives the city about 2,652,700 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, and the World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for that year and 3.52 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burkina Faso (Upper Volta),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burkina-faso. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Niger,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/niger. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of the Upper Volta to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1483 (XV) of 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206133. [Open access]","“Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in Chad,” 10 October 1961, document 236 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1961–1963, Africa, Volume XXI</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1961-63v21/d236. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Burkina Faso and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/burkina_faso_e.htm. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Burkina Faso,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bf.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Burkina Faso,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BFA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Ouaga_aerial_view.jpg/1920px-Ouaga_aerial_view.jpg","title":"Ouagadougou","desc":"Ouagadougou from the air, its compounds laid out along red laterite streets.","credit":"kyselak, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ouaga_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"A low aerial view of a city of flat-roofed single-storey compounds along wide red earth streets, with scattered trees."}},
{"id":"gw-560","num":560,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the seat of government of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Taipei","answerText":"Taipei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1914</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy came</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the WTO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Taiwan","dot":"Taipei"},"facts":[["Country","Taiwan"],["Population","2.43M (2026)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","-3.8% (2015–2020)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","taiwan"],"abstract":"<b>Taipei</b> had an American consulate long before it had a government: one was opened there in 1914, when the island was a Japanese colony and the city was called Taihoku.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a seat of government by retreat, the American embassy following the Republic of China from Nanjing to Guangzhou, then to Chongqing on 18 October 1949 and to this city on 19 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 24 May 1957 that embassy was wrecked, after the widow of the victim in a court martial that had just returned a verdict of not guilty began a hunger strike outside it that morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army attaché reported nine Americans injured, the information service building wrecked as well, six rioters killed by police fire, and three and a half divisions moved into the city under an unproclaimed curfew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ambassador wrote two days later that he was placing on the Chinese authorities the burden of proving there had been adequate excuse for leaving the embassy in the hands of an unarmed mob for several hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He put the resentment behind it partly down to the nearly 11,000 Americans then on the island, who in his words occupied themselves with almost every phase of Chinese life on a scale scarcely less extensive than colonial powers in their colonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1971 the General Assembly restored the seat of the People’s Republic of China and expelled the representatives of Chiang Kai-shek, whose government sat here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 1 January 1979 the United States recognised the People’s Republic as the sole legitimate government of China and withdrew its recognition of the Republic of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The embassy here closed on 28 February 1979 and the liaison office in Beijing became an embassy the next day, so the city gained and lost an embassy within thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory governed from here trades in the World Trade Organization as the Separate Customs Territory of Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu, admitted on 1 January 2002 and known there as Chinese Taipei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: China,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","“Telegram From the Army Attaché in the Republic of China (Barker) to the Department of the Army,” Taipei, 25 May 1957, document 256 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1955–1957, China, Volume III</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1955-57v03/d256. [Open access]","“Telegram From the Ambassador in the Republic of China (Rankin) to the Department of State,” Taipei, 26 May 1957, document 259 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1955–1957, China, Volume III</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1955-57v03/d259. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Restoration of the Lawful Rights of the People’s Republic of China in the United Nations,” resolution 2758 (XXVI) of 25 October 1971, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192054. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Separate Customs Territory of Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and Matsu (Chinese Taipei) and the WTO,” Member Information, https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/chinese_taipei_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/2014-02-23_%E5%8F%B0%E5%8C%97_Taipei_from_%E8%B2%93%E7%A9%BA_01.jpg/1920px-2014-02-23_%E5%8F%B0%E5%8C%97_Taipei_from_%E8%B2%93%E7%A9%BA_01.jpg","title":"Taipei from Maokong","desc":"Taipei filling its basin, seen from the hills at Maokong.","credit":"othree, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014-02-23_%E5%8F%B0%E5%8C%97_Taipei_from_%E8%B2%93%E7%A9%BA_01.jpg","alt":"A dense city of towers and apartment blocks spreading across a basin, with wooded hills in front and mountains behind."}},
{"id":"gw-561","num":561,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the legislative capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte","answerText":"Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 February 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In GATT</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 July 1948</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the WTO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sri Lanka","dot":"Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte"},"facts":[["Country","Sri Lanka"],["Population","116k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","sri lanka"],"abstract":"<b>Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte</b> is a capital that shares the work: a footnote on the United Nations country profile says that Colombo is the capital of Sri Lanka and that this city is the legislative capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat divides the same pair by function, giving Colombo the executive and judicial roles and this city the legislature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both bodies write the name Sri Jayewardenepura Kotte, one letter different from the spelling used here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state it legislates for was recognised by the United States on 4 February 1948 as a dominion within the British Commonwealth, on a date set in an agreement between the British and Ceylonese governments, President Truman having written the day before to the Governor General of Ceylon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ceylon’s own embassy opened in Washington on 29 October 1948, and the American embassy, which stands at Colombo rather than here, followed on 3 August 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country had already joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 29 July 1948, before that embassy opened, and it has been in the World Trade Organization since 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A United Nations seat took longer, the Assembly admitting it only on 14 December 1955 in a resolution headed Admission of New Members to the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth in 1948, following independence from Britain, and the Secretariat counts about 21.9 million people in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population the United Nations publishes for the capital is Colombo’s, about 606,200 people under a footnote dating the figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for the same year, so it is measuring Colombo too, and neither source publishes a figure for this city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sri Lanka,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lk.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Sri Lanka,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/sri-lanka. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sri Lanka,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sri-lanka. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X) of 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Sri Lanka,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LKA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Sri Lanka and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/sri_lanka_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/New_Sri_Lankan_Parliament_in_Kotte.jpg/1920px-New_Sri_Lankan_Parliament_in_Kotte.jpg","title":"The parliament at Kotte","desc":"The Sri Lankan parliament on its island in the Diyawanna, seen through trees from the far bank.","credit":"Dennis Sylvester Hurd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Sri_Lankan_Parliament_in_Kotte.jpg","alt":"A long low building with wide copper-green pavilion roofs standing across a stretch of water, framed by trees."}},
{"id":"gw-562","num":562,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lilongwe","answerText":"Lilongwe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 July 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In GATT</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 August 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 April 1976</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malawi","dot":"Lilongwe"},"facts":[["Country","Malawi"],["Population","1.40M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+26.6% (2012–2018)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","malawi"],"abstract":"<b>Lilongwe</b> has been the home of the American embassy to Malawi only since 1 April 1976, when the mission moved here from Blantyre, a dozen years after the country became independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before that the mission’s name and its address disagreed, since the consulate raised to embassy status on independence was designated as Zomba until 1970 but stood physically at Blantyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For six years, then, the United States kept an embassy named for one town while it sat in another, and neither of the two was this city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition and independence were one act on one day: on 6 July 1964 that consulate was elevated, the elevation being the act of recognition itself, with Edward W. Holmes as chargé d’affaires ad interim and the country having been the British territory of Nyasaland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It joined the Commonwealth the same year, following independence from Britain, and the Secretariat now gives its capital as this city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade seven weeks after independence, on 28 August 1964, and has been in the World Trade Organization since 31 May 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its United Nations seat followed on 1 December 1964, five months after the flag changed, and the profile that organisation publishes today names this city as the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives this city about 1,074,700 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for the same year, so it is measuring this city, and puts it near 1.39 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Commonwealth counts about 19.1 million people in the country as a whole, across 118,500 square kilometres (45,800 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malawi,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malawi. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Malawi,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/malawi. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Malawi and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/malawi_e.htm. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Malawi,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Malawi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MWI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Bwaila_South%2C_Lilongwe%2C_Malawi_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Bwaila_South%2C_Lilongwe%2C_Malawi_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Lilongwe","desc":"A street in Lilongwe, with shops along the road and a market on the ground beyond.","credit":"Timon Zingg, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bwaila_South%2C_Lilongwe%2C_Malawi_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A wide roadside in a city, with low shopfronts, people walking on a dirt path and market stalls in the distance."}},
{"id":"gw-563","num":563,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lusaka","answerText":"Lusaka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In GATT</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 1982</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the WTO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Zambia","dot":"Lusaka"},"facts":[["Country","Zambia"],["Population","3.50M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+41.6% (2010–2017)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","zambia"],"abstract":"<b>Lusaka</b> was an American consular post before it was the capital of a state: on 24 October 1964 the consulate here was raised to embassy status, and that elevation was itself the act of recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Independence fell on the same day, the country having been the British territory of Northern Rhodesia, and Robert C. Foulon took the post as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zambia joined the Commonwealth that year, following independence from Britain, and took its United Nations seat on 1 December 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade much later, on 10 February 1982, and has been in the World Trade Organization since 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Secretariat counts about 18.4 million people in the country, spread across 752,614 square kilometres (290,600 square miles), which the United Nations works out at about 29.5 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The city was also where southern Africa was negotiated: in September 1976 Henry Kissinger flew here from Pretoria to report to President Kenneth Kaunda what he had obtained from Vorster and Smith.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He went over a Rhodesia programme promising majority rule in two years and an immediate provisional government, and wrote afterwards that Kaunda was speechless and asked first what guarantee there was that it would be carried out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kaunda sent three of his closest advisers on an immediate tour of neighbouring capitals, and Kissinger agreed to return to this city or to Dar es Salaam once he had seen Nyerere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same message records that SWAPO was based here, which Kissinger gave as the reason Kaunda was attuned to its thinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives the city about 2,646,600 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019, and the World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for that year and 3.47 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Zambia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/zambia. [Open access]","“Telegram From Secretary of State Kissinger to the President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs (Scowcroft),” 21 September 1976, document 207 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1969–1976, Southern Africa, Volume XXVIII</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1969-76v28/d207. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Zambia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/zm.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Zambia,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/zambia. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Zambia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/zambia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Zambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ZMB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Downtown_Lusaka.JPG/1920px-Downtown_Lusaka.JPG","title":"Downtown Lusaka","desc":"The centre of Lusaka, with the city’s towers standing behind a fountain topped by a golden eagle.","credit":"Matthew Grollnek, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Lusaka.JPG","alt":"A city centre of tall office blocks behind a dark fountain topped by a golden eagle, with lawns and shrubs in front."}},
{"id":"gw-564","num":564,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Astana","answerText":"Astana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the WTO</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 2015</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Syria talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 January 2017</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kazakhstan","dot":"Astana"},"facts":[["Country","Kazakhstan"],["Population","1.10M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+20.3% (2018–2022)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","kazakhstan"],"abstract":"<b>Astana</b> has been published under more than one name by the same organisation: a United Nations city profile of 2020 is headed Nur-Sultan, while the country profile now gives Kazakhstan’s capital as Astana.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That profile measured the city against indicators for smart sustainable cities and proposed actions towards the development goals set for 2030.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations counts about 1,117,600 people here under a footnote dating the figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s series for the country’s largest city gives a different number for the same year, about 1,862,800, and about 2.04 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two must be measuring different cities: the United Nations names Astana as the capital and counts the people in it, while the World Bank follows whichever city in the country is largest, and here that is not the same place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The larger city is where the American embassy is: it was established at Alma-Ata, now Almaty, on 3 February 1992, with William Harrison Courtney as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition had come six weeks earlier, on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced it in an address on the dissolution of the Soviet Union, Kazakhstan having been a constituent republic of the USSR.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state took its United Nations seat on 2 March 1992 and joined the World Trade Organization much later, on 30 November 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It governs 2,724,902 square kilometres (1,052,100 square miles) with about 20.8 million people, which the United Nations works out at 7.7 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city has become a place where other countries’ wars are talked about: on 31 January 2017 the Security Council issued a press statement on an international meeting on Syria held here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kazakhstan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kazakhstan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kazakhstan. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Kazakhstan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KAZ/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Kazakhstan and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kazakhstan_e.htm. [Open access]","UN Economic Commission for Europe, <i>Smart Sustainable Cities Profile: Nur-Sultan, Kazakhstan</i>, ECE/HBP/197 (Geneva: United Nations, 2020), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4032498. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Security Council Press Statement on International Meeting on Syria in Astana,” SC/12701, 31 January 2017, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3868341. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Astana_Esil_view.jpg/1920px-Astana_Esil_view.jpg","title":"Astana across the Esil","desc":"Astana seen across the Esil, with the turquoise roof of the concert hall among the towers.","credit":"Dauren Nabijan, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astana_Esil_view.jpg","alt":"A skyline of glass towers and a long turquoise-roofed building seen across a river, with reeds in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gw-565","num":565,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"N'Djamena","answerText":"N'Djamena","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 February 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Closed, reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980, 1982</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Chad","dot":"N'Djamena"},"facts":[["Country","Chad"],["Population","1.70M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+24.5% (2012–2019)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","chad"],"abstract":"<b>N'Djamena</b> was a capital under another name first: the American mission opened here on 1 February 1961 was Embassy Fort Lamy, with Frederic L. Chapin as chargé d’affaires ad interim, and the American guide still lists the post under both names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the six months before that, Chad was dealt with from another country altogether, its first American chargé being Alan W. Lukens, the consul at Brazzaville.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He presented his credentials on 11 August 1960, the day independence from French Equatorial Africa was proclaimed and President Eisenhower sent a congratulatory message to Prime Minister Tombalbaye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state took its United Nations seat six weeks later, on 20 September 1960, admitted by a resolution of the General Assembly headed Admission of the Republic of Chad to membership in the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 12 July 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 19 October 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy did not stay open: on 24 March 1980 it was closed and its staff evacuated after heavy fighting broke out in the city, which is the reason the guide gives for the evacuation of the American diplomatic staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reopened on 15 January 1982, with John Blane as principal officer and chargé d’affaires ad interim, after nearly two years shut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations gives the city about 1,371,500 people under a footnote dating that figure to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank’s largest-city series gives the same number for that year, so it is measuring this city, and puts it near 1.72 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country around it holds about 21 million people in 1,284,000 square kilometres (495,800 square miles), which the United Nations works out at 16.7 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Chad,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/chad. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Chad,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/td.html. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Chad to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1485 (XV) of 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206135. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Chad and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/chad_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Chad,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TCD/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Rond-Point_de_la_Grande_arm%C3%A9e%2C_N%27Djamena.jpg/1920px-Rond-Point_de_la_Grande_arm%C3%A9e%2C_N%27Djamena.jpg","title":"N'Djamena","desc":"A boulevard in N'Djamena running towards the Grande Armée roundabout and its horseman.","credit":"Souleymane Torbo, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rond-Point_de_la_Grande_arm%C3%A9e%2C_N%27Djamena.jpg","alt":"A wide tree-lined avenue with motorcycles and cars, leading to a tall bronze statue of a rider on a rearing horse."}},
{"id":"gw-566","num":566,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Santiago","answerText":"Santiago","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1824</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1914</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Coup</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 September 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Chile","dot":"Santiago"},"facts":[["Country","Chile"],["Population","7.00M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","chile"],"abstract":"<b>Santiago</b> waited ninety years between receiving an American minister and receiving an ambassador: the legation here was raised to embassy status on 1 October 1914, when Henry P. Fletcher was appointed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition itself had come on 27 January 1823, when the Senate confirmed President Monroe’s nomination of Heman Allen of Vermont as minister to Chile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Monroe had written to Congress the previous March asking it to fund ministers for Chile, La Plata, Colombia, Peru and Mexico, having concluded that Spain could not recover its American colonies; Spain protested and the bill was signed on 4 May 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allen presented his credentials in this city on 23 April 1824 to Ramón Freire Serrano, who had replaced Bernardo O’Higgins as Supreme Director.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chilean revolutionaries had declared independence on 18 September 1810, and several years of fighting followed before the question was settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state was among the founding members of the United Nations on 24 October 1945, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 16 March 1949 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the morning of 11 September 1973 the military launched a coup against the government of Salvador Allende.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At ten past nine he made his final broadcast from the presidential palace, saying that he would not resign and rallying his supporters with three cries of long life, to Chile, to the people and to the workers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The palace was under heavy attack as he spoke, and the American account says he purportedly joined in defending it afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 6,723,500 people here under footnotes dating the figure to 2019 and saying it refers to the urban population of the Santiago Metropolitan Area Region, and the World Bank’s series gives the same number for that year and about 7.0 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Chile,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/chile. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Allende Years and the Pinochet Coup, 1969–1973,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1969-1976/allende. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Chile,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cl.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Chile and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/chile_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Chile,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Santiago_City_%28Southeast_view_01%29.JPG/1920px-Santiago_City_%28Southeast_view_01%29.JPG","title":"Santiago and the Andes","desc":"Santiago spreading east across its basin towards the wall of the Andes.","credit":"3BRBS, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santiago_City_%28Southeast_view_01%29.JPG","alt":"A city of low blocks and scattered towers seen from above, running to a long mountain range on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-567","num":567,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bucharest","answerText":"Bucharest","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 June 1866</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1881</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sole minister</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 April 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 June 1964</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Romania","dot":"Bucharest"},"facts":[["Country","Romania"],["Population","1.80M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-8.8% (2011–2021)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","romania"],"abstract":"<b>Bucharest</b> had an American consul before Romania was a country: Louis J. Czapkay was appointed here on 20 June 1866, while the principalities were still an autonomous part of the Ottoman Empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His successor, Benjamin Franklin Peixotto, was appointed in 1870 as an expression of American concern at the status of Romania’s Jewish community, which was denied citizenship and subject to persecution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was so little American commerce with Romania that a consortium of American, British and French Jews paid for the post, and Peixotto spent it remonstrating on that community’s behalf and founding schools and cultural societies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition of the kingdom came on 7 April 1881, when the chargé d’affaires Eugene Schuyler had an audience with King Charles, the Great Powers of Europe having recognised it first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A legation followed in 1882, but Schuyler was also accredited to Greece and Serbia and moved his residence to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For forty years no American minister was commissioned to Romania alone: from 1885 to 1905 the commissions covered Greece, Romania and Serbia with residence at Athens, and from 1905 the minister lived here but was still accredited to Serbia and, for a time, Bulgaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peter Augustus Jay, appointed on 18 April 1921, was the first commissioned exclusively to this country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were severed when Romania declared war on the United States on 12 December 1941, and the American minister Franklin Mott Gunther died here ten days later before he could leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation reopened in 1947 and was raised to embassy status on 1 June 1964, and the state joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 14 November 1971, the European Union on 1 January 2007 and the Schengen area in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives the city about 1,812,300 people for 2019 and the World Bank’s series gives the same number, a series that has fallen in every year of the past decade, to about 1.76 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Romania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/romania. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Romania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ro.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Romania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ROU/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, “Romania,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/romania_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Romania and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/romania_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/Bucuresti_de_sus.jpg","title":"Bucharest from above","desc":"Bucharest from the air, with the lake and park at Herăstrău and the blocks beyond.","credit":"Laubz83, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucuresti_de_sus.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a leafy city with a long lake and park, villas and apartment blocks around them."}},
{"id":"gw-568","num":568,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mogadishu","answerText":"Mogadishu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 January 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 December 2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Somalia","dot":"Mogadishu"},"facts":[["Country","Somalia"],["Population","2.80M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","somalia"],"abstract":"<b>Mogadishu</b> was the seat of a United Nations trust territory before it was a national capital, the surrounding country having been administered by Italy under trusteeship while Somaliland to the north was a British protectorate until 26 June 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Trusteeship Council fixed the date of independence at its 1104th plenary meeting on 7 June 1960, three weeks before it arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The General Assembly had settled the same question in a resolution of its own on the date of the independence of that trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two territories united as the Somali Republic on 1 July 1960, and President Eisenhower recognised it that day in a message to President Aden Abdulla Osman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations began the same day, when the American consulate general here, spelled Mogadiscio then, was elevated to embassy status with Andrew G. Lynch as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy closed on 5 January 1991 and all American personnel were withdrawn after the collapse of the central government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were not severed, and for the next 28 years the United States dealt with Somalia through its embassy in Nairobi, in another country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 17 January 2013 that dialogue was with a newly recognised central government rather than with transitional authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The embassy here reopened on 2 December 2018 under Ambassador Donald Yamamoto, though the Nairobi mission went on handling consular work for Somalia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 20 September 1960, and the United Nations gives about 2,179,900 people here for 2019 under a footnote saying the figure refers to the urban agglomeration, a number the World Bank’s series repeats and carries to about 2.85 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Somalia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/somalia. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Date of the independence of the Trust Territory of Somaliland under Italian administration,” resolution 1418 (XIV), https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206573. [Open access]","UN Trusteeship Council, “Date of independence of the Trust Territory of Somaliland under Italian administration,” resolution 2015 (XXVI), adopted at its 1104th plenary meeting, 7 June 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/219379. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Somalia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/so.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Somalia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SOM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Lido_beach_from_the_sea_in_Mogadishu.jpg/1920px-Lido_beach_from_the_sea_in_Mogadishu.jpg","title":"Mogadishu from the sea","desc":"The Lido seafront at Mogadishu seen from the water, with crowds along the beach.","credit":"AMISOM, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lido_beach_from_the_sea_in_Mogadishu.jpg","alt":"A city seafront seen from the sea, with a tall white block, low buildings and a crowded beach under parasols."}},
{"id":"gw-569","num":569,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dakar","answerText":"Dakar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Senegal","dot":"Dakar"},"facts":[["Country","Senegal"],["Population","3.70M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","senegal"],"abstract":"<b>Dakar</b> became a national capital by subtraction rather than by transfer, the American mission here having been accredited to the Federation of Mali before it was accredited to the country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Republic of Senegal withdrew from that federation and declared its independence on 20 August 1960, leaving the federal capital at Bamako without the larger of its two members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the republic on 24 September 1960, when the American embassy here informed the Senegalese foreign minister that it had done so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established the same day, the embassy here being established as a mission to Senegal with Roswell D. McClelland as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American record is explicit that the embassy had been accredited previously to the Federation of Mali, which no longer existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Four days after that recognition the General Assembly admitted the republic to the United Nations, at its 876th plenary meeting on 28 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations dates the membership from that day, and files the country in Western Africa with a land area of 196,712 square kilometres (75,951 square miles) and a population of about 18.9 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state has been a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade since 27 September 1963 and a member of the World Trade Organization since 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 3,057,100 people here for 2019, under a footnote saying the figure is the sum of three departments of the Dakar Region rather than of the city alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The World Bank’s largest-city series returns the identical number for that year, which is what the relay between the two looks like where the capital is also the largest city, and carries it to about 3.66 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Senegal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/senegal. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Senegal to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1490 (XV), adopted at the 876th plenary meeting, 28 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/206140. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Senegal,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sn.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Senegal and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/senegal_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Senegal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SEN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Panorama_Dakra.jpg/1920px-Panorama_Dakra.jpg","title":"Dakar from a hillside","desc":"The city seen from a hillside above a dual carriageway, dense low blocks running to the horizon.","credit":"August Dominus, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_Dakra.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a dense low-rise city from a hillside, with a road curving below and haze on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-570","num":570,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Guatemala City","answerText":"Guatemala City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1821, from Spain</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 April 1844</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 November 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 July 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guatemala","dot":"Guatemala City"},"facts":[["Country","Guatemala"],["Population","3.20M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","guatemala"],"abstract":"<b>Guatemala City</b> was a federal capital before it was a national one, the country around it having become independent from Spain in 1821 and joined the Federation of Central American States in 1823 alongside Honduras, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and El Salvador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised that federation on 4 August 1824, when President James Monroe received Antonio José Cañaz as envoy extraordinary and minister plenipotentiary, and relations were established the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An independent Guatemala was recognised separately on 5 April 1844, by the issuing of an exequatur to a Guatemalan consul-general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chargé d’Affaires Elijah Hise presented his credentials to the republic on or shortly before 21 January 1849, and the American legation here was raised to embassy status on 10 August 1882.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American record states that in June 1954 the Central Intelligence Agency assisted in the overthrow of the elected government of President Jacobo Árbenz Guzmán, and that normal relations were interrupted until Secretary of State John Foster Dulles instructed the embassy here to establish them with the new government on 12 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Security Council took the same crisis up at its 675th meeting on 20 June 1954, on a cablegram from the Guatemalan minister for external relations to its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It called unanimously for the immediate termination of any action likely to cause bloodshed, and asked all members to abstain from rendering assistance to any such action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state had held a United Nations seat since 21 November 1945, and is filed under Central America with a land area of 108,889 square kilometres (42,042 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 10 October 1991 and the World Trade Organization on 21 July 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 2,891,200 people here for 2019, the figure the World Bank’s largest-city series returns for the same year and carries to about 3.23 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guatemala,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guatemala. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 104 (1954) of 20 June 1954,” adopted at the 675th meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/112077. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Guatemala,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gt.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guatemala and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guatemala_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Guatemala,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GTM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Guatemala_City_-_Cityscape.jpg/1920px-Guatemala_City_-_Cityscape.jpg","title":"Guatemala City from the air","desc":"Apartment towers and low roofs running to the mountains that ring the city.","credit":"MHernandezp05, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guatemala_City_-_Cityscape.jpg","alt":"A wide aerial view of a city of tower blocks and low roofs, with wooded ravines and a mountain ridge behind."}},
{"id":"gw-571","num":571,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Quito","answerText":"Quito","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1830, from Colombia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 June 1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 December 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 January 1996</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ecuador","dot":"Quito"},"facts":[["Country","Ecuador"],["Population","1.80M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+25.1% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ecuador"],"abstract":"<b>Quito</b> is the seat of a state that separated from another republic rather than from an empire, the United States having recognised the independence of Colombia in 1822 while present-day Ecuador still formed part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ecuador withdrew from that federation in 1830, and was effectively recognised on 4 June 1832 in a letter from Secretary of State Edward Livingston to President Juan José Flores, written at the direction of President Andrew Jackson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American account treats recognition as a sequence of acts rather than a moment, adding a consul’s exequatur accepted from the Ecuadorian president on 24 February 1835 and an instruction of 1836 authorising a treaty with an Ecuadorian minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ratifications of a treaty of peace, friendship, navigation and commerce were exchanged here on 9 April 1842, and it took effect that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 12 August 1848, when Chargé d’Affaires Van Brugh Livingston presented his credentials and became the first resident American agent in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The legation here was raised to embassy rank on 5 March 1942, and the same office’s roll of chiefs of mission dates Boaz Walton Long’s own commission as ambassador to 14 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 21 December 1945 and joined the World Trade Organization on 21 January 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under South America with a land area of 257,217 square kilometres (99,312 square miles), which the World Bank measures independently at 256,370.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It gives about 1,847,700 people here for 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That figure stands alone rather than beside a second, because the largest-city series counts Guayaquil on the coast instead, this being one of the countries whose capital is not its biggest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ecuador,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ecuador. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Chiefs of Mission for Ecuador,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/chiefsofmission/ecuador. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Ecuador,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ec.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Ecuador and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/ecuador_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Ecuador,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ECU/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2019:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Vista_de_Quito_desde_la_Loma_de_Puengasi.jpg/1920px-Vista_de_Quito_desde_la_Loma_de_Puengasi.jpg","title":"Quito in its basin","desc":"The city filling an Andean basin, with the Pichincha ridge behind it.","credit":"Josuadaniel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_de_Quito_desde_la_Loma_de_Puengasi.jpg","alt":"A city filling a wide valley below a long mountain ridge, under a sky of scattered cloud."}},
{"id":"gw-572","num":572,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Amsterdam","answerText":"Amsterdam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">proclaimed 1814</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Netherlands","dot":"Amsterdam"},"facts":[["Country","Netherlands"],["Population","1.20M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+4.2% (2015–2018)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","netherlands"],"abstract":"<b>Amsterdam</b> is a capital that does not hold the government it names, and two institutions say so in their own words: the United Nations carries a footnote reading that Amsterdam is the capital and The Hague the seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The European Union’s country page states the same division, giving the capital as Amsterdam and adding that parliament and government are located in The Hague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American diplomatic record follows the government rather than the capital throughout: relations and the American legation were established at The Hague on 19 April 1782, when Minister John Adams presented his credentials and the Republic of the Netherlands became the second nation to recognise the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This city’s own entry in that record is consular, Sylvanus Bourne having been acknowledged as consul general here on 2 January 1798.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the legation at The Hague closed in May 1801, Bourne oversaw American interests from here until relations reopened in 1814.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The legation moved to London on 15 August 1940 after the German invasion, was raised to embassy rank on 8 May 1942 with credentials presented to the government in exile, and returned to The Hague on 17 August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 10 December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has been an EU member state since 1 January 1958, a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade since 1 January 1948 and a member of the World Trade Organization since 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 1,140,300 people here for 2019, the number the World Bank’s largest-city series returns for that year and carries to about 1.19 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same two bodies size the country differently, at 41,543 square kilometres against 37,391, which is total area measured against land area rather than a disagreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Netherlands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/netherlands. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Netherlands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nl.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Netherlands,” EU country profiles, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/netherlands_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Netherlands and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/netherlands_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Netherlands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NLD/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Amsterdam_aerial_photograph.jpg/1920px-Amsterdam_aerial_photograph.jpg","title":"Amsterdam from the air","desc":"The city between its canals and the water of the IJ, with the port beyond.","credit":"acediscovery, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amsterdam_aerial_photograph.jpg","alt":"A high aerial view of a dense city cut through by canals, with a broad waterway and docks behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-573","num":573,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Phnom Penh","answerText":"Phnom Penh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 May 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cambodia","dot":"Phnom Penh"},"facts":[["Country","Cambodia"],["Population","2.40M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+41.8% (2012–2019)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","cambodia"],"abstract":"<b>Phnom Penh</b> holds a mission that has been opened, severed, reopened, closed and opened again, a sequence that begins with the United States recognising the Kingdom of Cambodia on 7 February 1950, the country having become an independent state within the French Union the year before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were established on 11 July 1950, when Envoy Extraordinary and Minister Plenipotentiary Donald R. Heath presented his credentials while resident at Saigon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American legation here opened on 14 November 1950 under Chargé d’Affaires ad interim Don V. Catlett, and was raised to embassy rank on 24 June 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heath stayed at Saigon even then, and the first ambassador resident in this city was Robert M. McClintock, who presented his credentials on 2 October 1954.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cambodia severed relations on 3 May 1965, holding the United States responsible for cross-border air attacks by South Vietnam that had killed Cambodian citizens; they were reestablished on 2 July 1969 and the embassy here reopened that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That embassy closed on 12 April 1975 once all American personnel had been evacuated, and Secretary of State Henry Kissinger afterwards explained that the incoming government had refused to establish relations with any country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American mission was established here on 11 November 1991 and the embassy was reopened on 17 May 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between those two dates the Security Council established the United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia, at its 3057th meeting on 28 February 1992, for a period not exceeding eighteen months and with elections judged vital by May 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 14 December 1955 and joined the World Trade Organization on 13 October 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 2,014,000 people here for 2019, under a footnote saying the figure refers to the municipality including suburban areas, and the World Bank carries the same number to about 2.43 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cambodia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cambodia. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 745 (1992) of 28 February 1992,” adopted at the 3057th meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/138379. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cambodia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kh.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cambodia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cambodia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Cambodia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KHM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Aerial_view_of_Phnom_Penh_2019.png/1920px-Aerial_view_of_Phnom_Penh_2019.png.jpg","title":"Phnom Penh from the river","desc":"The riverfront from the air, the Royal Palace among the trees and the newer towers behind.","credit":"Wise World Contributor, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Phnom_Penh_2019.png","alt":"An aerial view along a broad river past a palace with gilded roofs, with a city of towers stretching inland."}},
{"id":"gw-574","num":574,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Harare","answerText":"Harare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Measures</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Terminated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 December 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 April 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1980</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Zimbabwe","dot":"Harare"},"facts":[["Country","Zimbabwe"],["Population","1.60M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+0.4% (2012–2022)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","zimbabwe"],"abstract":"<b>Harare</b> spent fourteen years as the seat of a government no state would recognise, the colony’s minority white government having issued a unilateral declaration of independence in 1965 under the name Rhodesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American record is explicit that the United States never recognised that independence, the territory having been under British sovereignty as Southern Rhodesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Security Council acted against that government under Chapter VII of the Charter, in resolution 232 of 16 December 1966 and in later resolutions on the situation in Southern Rhodesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It lifted them on 21 December 1979, at its 2181st meeting, having noted with satisfaction that the conference at Lancaster House in London had produced agreement on a constitution for a free and independent Zimbabwe providing for genuine majority rule, and having deplored the loss of life caused by what it called fourteen years of rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That resolution also reaffirmed the inalienable right of the people of Zimbabwe to self-determination, freedom and independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Republic of Zimbabwe on 18 April 1980, the day the country attained independence, and its own summary claims it was the first country to establish an embassy there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were established the same day, with Jeffrey Davidow as chargé d’affaires ad interim at a mission the record calls the American Embassy at Salisbury, that being the city’s name at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 25 August 1980, and joined the World Trade Organization on 5 March 1995 after nearly half a century as a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is filed under Eastern Africa with a land area of 390,757 square kilometres (150,872 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 1,521,300 people here for 2019, the figure the World Bank returns for that year and carries to about 1.63 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Zimbabwe,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/zimbabwe. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 460 (1979) of 21 December 1979,” adopted at the 2181st meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/10494. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Zimbabwe,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/zw.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Zimbabwe and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/zimbabwe_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Zimbabwe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ZWE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/View_of_Harare_from_Chiremba_road.jpg/1920px-View_of_Harare_from_Chiremba_road.jpg","title":"Harare across the veld","desc":"The city's towers seen across dry grass, with a railway line running towards them.","credit":"Itaisibanda, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Harare_from_Chiremba_road.jpg","alt":"A cluster of tower blocks on the horizon seen across an expanse of dry grass, with a railway track curving towards them."}},
{"id":"gw-575","num":575,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Conakry","answerText":"Conakry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1958</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 February 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guinea","dot":"Conakry"},"facts":[["Country","Guinea"],["Population","2.30M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+105.1% (2014–2025)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Conakry</b> became a national capital ten weeks before it had an American embassy in it, and the gap between the two dates is the whole of what the record shows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the Republic of Guinea on 1 November 1958, when President Dwight D. Eisenhower sent a letter to that effect to Prime Minister Sékou Touré.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory had previously been under French sovereignty as part of French West Africa, so the state the letter recognised was ten weeks old and the city had not yet held a foreign mission of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 13 February 1959, when the American embassy here was established with Robert W. Rinden as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between those two events the General Assembly admitted the republic to the United Nations, at its 789th plenary meeting on 12 December 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The sequence therefore runs recognition, then a seat at the United Nations, then an embassy, rather than the more usual order in which a mission is opened before the seat is granted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations dates the membership from that day and files the country under Western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its land area is given as 245,836 square kilometres (94,918 square miles), for a population of about 15.1 million and a density of 61.5 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 8 December 1994 and the World Trade Organization on 25 October 1995, so its accession to the older agreement came within a year of the newer body replacing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 1,889,200 people here for 2019, the number the World Bank’s largest-city series returns for that year and carries to about 2.25 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guinea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guinea. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Guinea to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1325 (XIII), adopted at the 789th plenary meeting, 12 December 1958, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207051. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Guinea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gn.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Guinea and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/guinea_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GIN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Conakry.jpg/1920px-Conakry.jpg","title":"Conakry from the air","desc":"The city on its peninsula, the port and its breakwater along the northern shore.","credit":"Joelguinea, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conakry.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a city covering a narrow peninsula, with docks and a long breakwater on one side and the ocean beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-576","num":576,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Porto-Novo","answerText":"Porto-Novo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 February 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed Benin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Benin","dot":"Porto-Novo"},"facts":[["Country","Benin"],["Population","285k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","-1.1% (2009–2013)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","benin"],"abstract":"<b>Porto-Novo</b> is a capital that holds neither the country’s largest city nor its foreign missions, and the United Nations says so in a footnote of its own: Porto-Novo is the constitutional capital and Cotonou the economic capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American record makes the same division without commenting on it, the mission here being called Embassy Cotonou and having been established there on 15 February 1961 with Converse Hettinger as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state itself had been recognised earlier, on 1 August 1960, in a congratulatory message from President Dwight D. Eisenhower to Prime Minister Maga, on the day independence from French sovereignty was proclaimed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were established that same day, when Donald R. Norland took up his position as chargé d’affaires ad interim, having presented his credentials on 31 July to take effect the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country was called the Republic of Dahomey then, and was re-named the Republic of Benin in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Under that older name it was admitted to the United Nations at the 864th plenary meeting of the General Assembly, on 20 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations dates the membership from that day and files the country under Western Africa, with a land area of 114,763 square kilometres (44,310 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 12 September 1963 and the World Trade Organization on 22 February 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 285,300 people are given here for 2018, a figure for the constitutional capital itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No second count stands beside it, because the largest-city series measures Cotonou instead, at about 991,000 for 2019 and 1.38 million in 2025, so the two numbers are about different cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Benin (Dahomey),” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/benin. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Benin,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bj.html. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of Dahomey to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1481 (XV), adopted at the 864th plenary meeting, 20 September 1960, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205974. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Benin and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/benin_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Benin,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Mairie_de_Porto-Novo_B%C3%A9nin.jpg/1920px-Mairie_de_Porto-Novo_B%C3%A9nin.jpg","title":"The town hall at Porto-Novo","desc":"The street outside the Mairie de Porto-Novo, the city being too small for a skyline.","credit":"Ksperentos, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mairie_de_Porto-Novo_B%C3%A9nin.jpg","alt":"A wide red-earth street with motorcycles parked outside a low blue and white town hall under trees."}},
{"id":"gw-577","num":577,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kigali","answerText":"Kigali","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trusteeship ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UNAMIR</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 October 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Rwanda","dot":"Kigali"},"facts":[["Country","Rwanda"],["Population","1.30M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+2.1% (2012–2019)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","rwanda"],"abstract":"<b>Kigali</b> became a national capital on a date the General Assembly had fixed three days earlier, in a resolution that also explains why there are two countries here rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Resolution 1746, adopted at the 118th plenary meeting on 27 June 1962, noted that efforts to maintain the unity of Ruanda-Urundi had not succeeded and decided to terminate the Trusteeship Agreement of 13 December 1946 on 1 July 1962, on which date Rwanda and Burundi were to emerge as two independent and sovereign states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also called on Belgium, the administering authority, to complete the evacuation of its remaining forces by 1 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Republic of Rwanda on that independence day, in a congratulatory message from President John F. Kennedy to President Grégoire Kayibanda dated 28 June 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were established the same day, when the American embassy here was established with David J. S. Manbey as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The republic took a United Nations seat on 18 September 1962, and 31 years later the Security Council named this city in a mandate of its own, establishing the United Nations Assistance Mission for Rwanda on 5 October 1993 and instructing it to contribute to the security of the city of Kigali within a weapons-secure area in and around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That resolution authorised a first contingent to be deployed here for an initial six months, following the peace agreement signed at Arusha that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 January 1966 and the World Trade Organization on 22 May 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is filed under Eastern Africa with a land area of 26,338 square kilometres (10,169 square miles) and a population density of 601.8 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 1,094,800 people here for 2019, the figure the World Bank returns for that year and carries to about 1.33 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Rwanda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/rwanda. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “The future of Ruanda-Urundi,” resolution 1746 (XVI), adopted at the 118th plenary meeting, 27 June 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205656. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 872 (1993) of 5 October 1993,” adopted at the 3288th meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/197341. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Rwanda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/rw.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Rwanda and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/rwanda_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Rwanda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/RWA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Kigali2018Cropped.jpg/1920px-Kigali2018Cropped.jpg","title":"Kigali at dusk","desc":"The city centre on its ridge at dusk, the lower districts spread across the hills below.","credit":"Adrien K, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kigali2018Cropped.jpg","alt":"A city at dusk seen from a hill, a cluster of lit towers on a ridge with low houses spread over the slopes below."}},
{"id":"gw-762","num":762,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the economic capital and largest city of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bujumbura","answerText":"Bujumbura","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1963</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Burundi","dot":"Bujumbura"},"facts":[["Country","Burundi"],["Population","1.40M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","burundi"],"abstract":"<b>Bujumbura</b> is the largest city of Burundi but not the seat the United Nations names as its capital, which is Gitega, inland to the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It has held the American mission from the country’s first day, the record calling it Embassy Usumbura and giving this name in a parenthesis beside the older one, established on 1 July 1962 with Herbert V. Olds as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That date was fixed by the General Assembly four days earlier, in the resolution that also created the country’s neighbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Resolution 1746, adopted at the 118th plenary meeting on 27 June 1962, noted that efforts to maintain the unity of Ruanda-Urundi had not succeeded and terminated the Trusteeship Agreement of 13 December 1946 on 1 July, on which date Rwanda and Burundi were to emerge as two independent and sovereign states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President John F. Kennedy extended recognition of the Kingdom of Burundi in a letter sent on 28 June 1962 to Mwami Mwambutsa IV.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The mission here then went backwards before it went forwards, its rank being changed to legation with effect from 15 December 1962 and to embassy again from 16 September 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state took a United Nations seat on 18 September 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 13 March 1965 and the World Trade Organization on 23 July 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations files it under Eastern Africa with a land area of 27,834 square kilometres (10,747 square miles) and a population of about 14.4 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The World Bank’s largest-city series, which counts this city rather than the political capital, gives about 954,400 people here for 2019 and about 1.35 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burundi,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burundi. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “The future of Ruanda-Urundi,” resolution 1746 (XVI), adopted at the 118th plenary meeting, 27 June 1962, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/205656. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Burundi,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bi.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Burundi and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/burundi_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Burundi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BDI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/BujumburaView.jpg/1920px-BujumburaView.jpg","title":"Bujumbura from above","desc":"The city spread along its avenues below the hills that rise behind it.","credit":"SteveRwanda, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BujumburaView.jpg","alt":"A green city of low buildings along a straight avenue, with wooded hills rising behind it under a pale sky."}},
{"id":"gw-579","num":579,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the constitutional capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sucre","answerText":"Sucre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 May 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 January 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1942</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bolivia","dot":"Sucre"},"facts":[["Country","Bolivia"],["Population","278k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","6th largest"],["Population growth","+20.2% (2011–2021)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","bolivia"],"abstract":"<b>Sucre</b> is the constitutional capital of Bolivia and does not hold its government, a division the United Nations states in a footnote of its own: La Paz is the seat of government and Sucre the constitutional capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American record follows the government rather than the capital, the legation having opened at La Paz on 3 January 1849 under Chargé d’Affaires John Appleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bolivia had declared its independence from Spain on 6 August 1825, but the United States first recognised it only as part of something larger, the Peru-Bolivian Confederation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> James B. Thornton was appointed chargé d’affaires to that confederation on 16 March 1837, commissioned to Peru and received by the confederation rather than by either state alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It dissolved in 1839, and the republic was officially recognised as a separate state on 30 May 1848, when the Department of State appointed Appleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The legation at La Paz was raised to embassy rank after a joint announcement on 4 January 1942, the change taking effect on 31 March and the American ambassador presenting his credentials on 23 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 14 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 8 September 1990 and the World Trade Organization on 12 September 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations files it under South America with a land area of 1,098,581 square kilometres (424,164 square miles), which the World Bank measures independently at 1,098,580.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 277,900 people are given here for 2018, and no second count stands beside that: the largest-city series measures whichever city in the country is largest, at about 1,835,400 for 2019, which is many times this city’s size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bolivia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bolivia. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Bolivia (Plurinational State of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bo.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Plurinational State of Bolivia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/bolivia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Bolivia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BOL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Bolivia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BOL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/20170805_Bolivia_1128%2B31_Pano_crop_Sucre_sRGB_%2837926300676%29.jpg/1920px-20170805_Bolivia_1128%2B31_Pano_crop_Sucre_sRGB_%2837926300676%29.jpg","title":"Sucre from the hillside","desc":"The white city spread across its valley, with the ranges of the eastern Andes behind.","credit":"Dan Lundberg, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20170805_Bolivia_1128%2B31_Pano_crop_Sucre_sRGB_%2837926300676%29.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a city of pale roofs in a high valley, seen past thatched parasols on a terrace, with mountains beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-580","num":580,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tunis","answerText":"Tunis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 March 1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 May 1881</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">recognised 22 March 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1956</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tunisia","dot":"Tunis"},"facts":[["Country","Tunisia"],["Population","2.50M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-5.7% (2014–2022)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tunisia"],"abstract":"<b>Tunis</b> is the rare capital in the American diplomatic record that recognised the United States rather than the other way about, its authorities having accepted the appointment of an American consular representative at some point in mid-1795.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Joseph Donaldson was named consul for Tunis on 28 March 1795 but remained at his post in Algiers, so a French merchant here, Joseph Étienne Famin, was deputised to act for the United States and arranged a truce that took effect on 15 June 1796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Famin went on to negotiate a Treaty of Peace and Friendship with the Bey of Tunis, signed in 1797, and William Eaton presented his credentials to Hamouda Pasha on 15 March 1798.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That arrangement ended when France established control by the Treaty of Bardo on 12 May 1881, and the La Marsa Convention of 8 June 1883 stated explicitly that Tunisia was a French protectorate and ended its power to conduct international diplomacy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American consular presence continued here through the protectorate with several closings, and the office became a consulate general on 22 May 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised Tunisian independence from France on 22 March 1956, in a congratulatory message from the consul general here to the Bey and the Tunisian government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That consulate general was elevated to an embassy on 5 June 1956, and relations were established the next day when Morris N. Hughes presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Admission to the United Nations took the Charter’s two steps: the Security Council recommended it at its 732nd meeting on 26 July 1956, and the General Assembly admitted the state at its 574th plenary meeting on 12 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 19 August 1990 and the World Trade Organization on 29 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 2,327,800 people here for 2019 under a footnote saying the figure refers to Grand Tunis, the number the World Bank returns for that year and carries to about 2.55 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tunisia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tunisia. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 116 (1956) of 26 July 1956,” adopted at the 732nd meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/112093. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of Tunisia to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 1112 (XI), adopted at the 574th plenary meeting, 12 November 1956, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/207994. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Tunisia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tn.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tunisia and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tunisia_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Tunisia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TUN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Panoramique_du_centre_de_Tunis_%283197369985%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1920px-Panoramique_du_centre_de_Tunis_%283197369985%29_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Tunis from the air","desc":"The city at dusk, its avenues running inland from the lagoon on the left.","credit":"Citizen59, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramique_du_centre_de_Tunis_%283197369985%29_%28cropped%29.jpg","alt":"A wide aerial view of a pale low-rise city at dusk, with a shallow lagoon at the left and hills on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-581","num":581,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Juba","answerText":"Juba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 2005</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 July 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 2011</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"S. Sudan","dot":"Juba"},"facts":[["Country","South Sudan"],["Population","386k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+6.7% (2014–2017)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","south sudan"],"abstract":"<b>Juba</b> is the capital of the newest state to hold a seat at the United Nations, and it kept an American consulate for nearly six years before it held an embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That consulate was established here on 11 November 2005, after the signing of the Comprehensive Peace Agreement that officially ended the conflict between the north and the south of Sudan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The agreement set the date for a referendum on South Sudan’s self-determination in January 2011, at which voters overwhelmingly chose independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Republic of South Sudan declared its independence on 9 July 2011, and President Barack Obama declared formal American recognition of it as a sovereign and independent state the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The consulate general here was elevated to an embassy on that date, with the consul general Barrie Walkley serving as chargé d’affaires until Susan D. Page was confirmed as ambassador designate on 18 October 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Membership of the United Nations followed within five days of that declaration, and both of the steps the Charter requires are on the record with their meeting numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Security Council, having examined the application, recommended at its 6582nd meeting on 13 July 2011 that the republic be admitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The General Assembly decided to admit it at its 108th plenary meeting the following day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under Eastern Africa and gives it about 12.2 million people for 2025, but the profile carries no land area at all, and the World Bank measures 646,883 square kilometres (249,763 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 385,800 people are given here for 2019, the number the World Bank’s largest-city series returns for that year and carries to about 500,100 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: South Sudan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/south-sudan. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1999 (2011) of 13 July 2011,” adopted at the 6582nd meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/706967. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the Republic of South Sudan to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 65/308, adopted at the 108th plenary meeting, 14 July 2011, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/710041. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “South Sudan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ss.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: South Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SSD/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: South Sudan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SSD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Rock_City_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Rock_City_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The edge of Juba","desc":"A stone-breaking yard on the outskirts, with the low city on the horizon under a storm.","credit":"Michael Walsh, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_City_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"Heaps of broken stone and thatched shelters on green ground, with a low city on the horizon under a dark storm sky."}},
{"id":"gw-582","num":582,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Brussels","answerText":"Brussels","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 4 October 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 January 1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty of London</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 December 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belgium","dot":"Brussels"},"facts":[["Country","Belgium"],["Population","2.10M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+3.1% (2020–2025)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","belgium"],"abstract":"<b>Brussels</b> became the seat of a kingdom that declared itself out of another one, the Kingdom of Belgium having declared its independence from the Kingdom of the Netherlands on 4 October 1830.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the European powers recognised that independence in fact on 20 December 1830, but the former ruler did not accept it until the Netherlands signed the Treaty of London on 19 April 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States moved in between the two, recognising Belgian independence on 6 January 1832 by issuing an exequatur to the Belgian consul at New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 18 June 1832, when Baron D. Behr sent a note attaching his full powers to Secretary of State Edward Livingston, and the American legation here was established on 25 September that year when Chargé d’Affaires Hugh Legaré presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That legation was elevated to an embassy on 3 October 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The embassy here closed on 15 July 1940 after the German invasion, and a chargé d’affaires was designated in November to serve near the Belgian government established in England, until the embassy was reestablished here on 14 September 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 27 December 1945 and has been a member of the European Union since 1 January 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 January 1948 and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two bodies size the country slightly differently, at 30,528 square kilometres against 30,667, and the European Union lists three official languages here: Dutch, French and German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations gives about 2,065,300 people for 2019 under a footnote saying the figure covers the Brussels-Capital Region with the communes of the agglomeration and suburbs, the number the World Bank returns for that year and carries to about 2.14 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belgium,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belgium. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Belgium,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/be.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Belgium,” EU country profiles, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/belgium_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Belgium and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/belgium_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Belgium,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Brussels_skyline.jpg/1920px-Brussels_skyline.jpg","title":"Brussels from the south","desc":"The office towers of the northern quarter above the red roofs of the older city.","credit":"James Cridland, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brussels_skyline.jpg","alt":"A cluster of dark office towers on the skyline above a wide field of red-tiled roofs, with woodland in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gw-583","num":583,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Port-au-Prince","answerText":"Port-au-Prince","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from France, 1804</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Haiti","dot":"Port-au-Prince"},"facts":[["Country","Haiti"],["Population","2.70M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","haiti"],"abstract":"<b>Port-au-Prince</b> was the capital of an independent state for 58 years before the United States would deal with it, the American record noting that although Haiti won its independence from France in 1804, it did not receive recognition until 1862.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That record calls the country the second-oldest republic in the Western Hemisphere, after the United States itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition came on 12 July 1862, when President Abraham Lincoln commissioned Benjamin F. Whidden to act as an American diplomatic representative under the title of commissioner and consul-general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation here were established on 1 October 1862, when Whidden presented his credentials to the government of the Republic of Haiti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation was raised to embassy rank after a joint announcement of 23 March 1943 covering seven American republics, and John Campbell White presented his credentials as ambassador on 14 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Between those two centuries the same record sets down a military intervention in 1915 and a 19-year occupation, from which American forces were withdrawn in 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 24 October 1945, and joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 January 1950 and the World Trade Organization on 30 January 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A week after the devastating earthquake of 12 January 2010, the Security Council met to increase the stabilisation mission in the country to 8,940 troops and 3,711 police, in support of immediate recovery and reconstruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under the Caribbean with a land area of 27,750 square kilometres (10,714 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It gives about 2,704,200 people here for 2019, the number the World Bank returns for that year and carries to about 3.13 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Haiti,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/haiti. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 1908 (2010) of 19 January 2010,” adopted at the 6261st meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/675259. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Haiti,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ht.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Haiti and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/haiti_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Haiti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HTI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Port_au_Prince_vu_depuis_l%27olofson.jpg/1920px-Port_au_Prince_vu_depuis_l%27olofson.jpg","title":"Port-au-Prince from the hillside","desc":"The city running down to the bay, seen from the slopes above it.","credit":"Bruno Le Bansais, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_au_Prince_vu_depuis_l%27olofson.jpg","alt":"A low city of pale roofs and palms running down a slope to a blue bay, with a radio mast in the middle distance."}},
{"id":"gw-584","num":584,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Amman","answerText":"Amman","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 May 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 January 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Jordan","dot":"Amman"},"facts":[["Country","Jordan"],["Population","2.30M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+41.0% (2010–2015)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","jordan"],"abstract":"<b>Amman</b> was the seat of a kingdom for nearly three years before the United States would call it independent, the country around it having been a mandated territory placed under the protection of Great Britain after the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Transjordan declared its independence with the agreement of Great Britain on 25 May 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of Transjordan as an independent state in a White House announcement on 31 January 1949, and the American record still calls it by that older name throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation were established here on 18 February 1949, when Wells Stabler presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires ad interim at Amman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That announcement, that declaration and that credentialing are the whole of what the American record sets down about the country, so the mandate and the three-year wait for recognition carry the city's diplomatic history between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Admission to the United Nations came later still, and not on its own: the General Assembly took it at the 555th plenary meeting on 14 December 1955, in a resolution headed simply Admission of New Members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state joined the World Trade Organization on 11 April 2000, and the organization's own page for it records no earlier accession to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under Western Asia with a land area of 89,318 square kilometres (34,486 square miles) and a population of about 11.5 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It gives about 2,108,500 people here for 2019, at a national density of 129.7 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank’s largest-city series returns the same number for that year and carries it to about 2.27 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Jordan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/jordan. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of New Members to the United Nations,” resolution 995 (X), adopted at the 555th plenary meeting, 14 December 1955, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/209584. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Jordan and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/jordan_e.htm. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Jordan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jo.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Jordan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JOR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Aerial_photograph_of_Amman_%283%29.JPG/1920px-Aerial_photograph_of_Amman_%283%29.JPG","title":"Amman from the air","desc":"The pale stone city filling its hills to the horizon, with one dark tower near the centre.","credit":"High Contrast, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_photograph_of_Amman_%283%29.JPG","alt":"An aerial view of a vast city of pale stone buildings covering rolling hills, with a single dark tower near the centre."}},
{"id":"gw-585","num":585,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Santo Domingo","answerText":"Santo Domingo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from Haiti, 1844</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regained</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1865, from Spain</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1866</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 July 1904</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dominican Rep.","dot":"Santo Domingo"},"facts":[["Country","Dominican Republic"],["Population","3.20M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-24.0% (2010–2021)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","dominican republic"],"abstract":"<b>Santo Domingo</b> is the seat of a state that has won its independence twice, from two different neighbours, and the American record sets down both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After French and Spanish rule from as early as the 16th century, the country declared itself independent of neighbouring Haiti in 1844; it reverted to Spanish rule in 1861 and won its independence again in 1865.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 17 September 1866, the day an exequatur was issued to J. W. Currier as Dominican consul general at New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 26 March 1884, when John M. Langston presented his credentials as chargé d’affaires; he was accredited to Haiti as well and resident at Port-au-Prince.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American legation was established in this city only on 23 July 1904, when Thomas C. Dawson presented his credentials as minister resident and consul general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That legation was raised to embassy rank after a joint announcement of 23 March 1943 covering seven American republics, and Avra M. Warren presented his credentials as ambassador on 17 April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same record notes that American military occupations have at times strained relations between the two states, and in 1965 the Security Council, deeply concerned at what it called the grave events in the country, called for a strict cease-fire and invited the Secretary-General to send a representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 24 October 1945, and it joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 19 May 1950 and the World Trade Organization on 9 March 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under the Caribbean with a land area of 48,671 square kilometres (18,792 square miles), and gives about 3,245,000 people here for 2019 under a footnote saying the figure is the urban population of four municipalities together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank’s largest-city series returns the same number for that year and carries it to about 3.65 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Dominican Republic,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/dominican-republic. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Dominican Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/do.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Dominican Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/dominican_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Dominican Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DOM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 203 (1965) of 14 May 1965,” adopted at the 1208th meeting, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/90485. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Santo_Domingo_%28Dominican_Republic%29_taken_atop_Novocentro_tower_viewing_the_city_to_the_southwest_2010.jpg/1920px-Santo_Domingo_%28Dominican_Republic%29_taken_atop_Novocentro_tower_viewing_the_city_to_the_southwest_2010.jpg","title":"Santo Domingo from a tower","desc":"The city looking south-west towards the Caribbean, apartment blocks among the trees.","credit":"Jjc09, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santo_Domingo_%28Dominican_Republic%29_taken_atop_Novocentro_tower_viewing_the_city_to_the_southwest_2010.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a city of white apartment blocks and trees, with taller towers on the horizon and the sea beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-586","num":586,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Abu Dhabi","answerText":"Abu Dhabi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 December 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 December 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 June 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"United Arab Emirates","dot":"Abu Dhabi"},"facts":[["Country","United Arab Emirates"],["Population","1.50M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+43.6% (2018–2023)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","united arab emirates"],"abstract":"<b>Abu Dhabi</b> was one of seven Trucial Sheikdoms under a British protectorate before it was a national capital, alongside Dubai, Sharjah, Ajman, Umm al-Qaiwain, Fujairah and Ras al-Khaimah.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those sheikdoms formed the United Arab Emirates and declared independence on 2 December 1971, on the completion of treaties with Great Britain, and the United States recognised the new state the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Security Council recommended admission to the United Nations on 8 December, unanimously, and the General Assembly admitted the state at its 2007th plenary meeting on 9 December, one week after the declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 20 March 1972, when William A. Stoltzfus Jr. presented his credentials; he was accredited to several other Persian Gulf states as well and resided in Kuwait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American embassy was established in this city on 24 June 1974, when the first resident ambassador, Michael Edmund Sterner, presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 8 March 1994 and the World Trade Organization on 10 April 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations files it under Western Asia and gives its area as 71,024 square kilometres (27,423 square miles) under a footnote saying that is land area only, where the World Bank gives 98,648 square kilometres (38,088 square miles) for the same country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> About 1,452,100 people are given here for 2019, in a country whose recorded population is about 11.3 million and whose sex ratio is 176 men to every hundred women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No second count stands beside that figure, because the largest-city series measures whichever city is largest and this capital is not it: that series gives about 2,833,100 for the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That larger city is Dubai, which the American record lists among the six sheikdoms that federated with this one in 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The United Arab Emirates,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/united-arab-emirates. [Open access]","UN Security Council, “Resolution 304 (1971) on admission of the United Arab Emirates to membership in the United Nations,” https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/280357. [Open access]","UN General Assembly, “Admission of the United Arab Emirates to membership in the United Nations,” resolution 2794 (XXVI), adopted at the 2007th plenary meeting, 9 December 1971, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192057. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “United Arab Emirates,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ae.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “United Arab Emirates and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/united_arab_emirates_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: United Arab Emirates,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: United Arab Emirates,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Abu_Dhabi_Skyline_from_Breakwaters_Marina.jpg/1920px-Abu_Dhabi_Skyline_from_Breakwaters_Marina.jpg","title":"Abu Dhabi from the Breakwater","desc":"The towers of the corniche at sunset, seen across the marina with the national flag flying.","credit":"Raganesh, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Dhabi_Skyline_from_Breakwaters_Marina.jpg","alt":"A line of glass towers along a waterfront at sunset, seen across a marina of moored boats, with a large flag on a mast."}},
{"id":"gw-587","num":587,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Havana","answerText":"Havana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 May 1902</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 May 1902</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 January 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 July 2015</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cuba","dot":"Havana"},"facts":[["Country","Cuba"],["Population","2.10M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-17.9% (2018–2024)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","cuba"],"abstract":"<b>Havana</b> held an American mission, then no mission, then a mission that was not called one, then an embassy again, and the American record dates every step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spain relinquished sovereignty over Cuba after its defeat in the war of 1898, and American forces occupied the island until the Republic of Cuba was formally installed on 19 May 1902, the United States relinquishing its occupation authority the following day while claiming a continuing right to intervene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That right, granted under the Platt Amendment as a condition of independence, was repealed in 1934 when the two states signed a Treaty of Relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation here were established on 27 May 1902, when Herbert Goldsmith Squiers presented his credentials, and the legation was raised to embassy rank on 10 February 1923 following an act of Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were severed on 3 January 1961, the American record citing limitations placed on its mission by the Cuban government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> An American Interests Section was established here on 1 September 1977, under the protection of the Swiss government, and it stood in for an embassy for 38 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations resumed on 20 July 2015, when both countries raised their respective Interests Sections to embassy status, on a date the two presidents had agreed in an exchange of letters dated 30 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state has held a United Nations seat since 24 October 1945, and it joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 1 January 1948 and the World Trade Organization on 20 April 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations files the country under the Caribbean with a land area of 109,884 square kilometres (42,426 square miles), which the World Bank measures independently at 109,880.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It gives about 2,138,400 people here for 2019, the number the World Bank returns for that year and carries to about 2.16 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cuba,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cuba. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Cuba,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cu.html. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Cuba and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/cuba_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Cuba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Cuba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Centro_Habana_y_Malec%C3%B3n_%2833618061061%29.jpg/1920px-Centro_Habana_y_Malec%C3%B3n_%2833618061061%29.jpg","title":"Centro Habana and the Malecón","desc":"The sea wall and its road curving along the shore at dusk, the old city packed behind it.","credit":"Antonio Schubert, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centro_Habana_y_Malec%C3%B3n_%2833618061061%29.jpg","alt":"A coastal road curving along a sea wall at dusk, with densely packed older buildings behind it and a ship in the harbour."}},
{"id":"gw-588","num":588,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Prague","answerText":"Prague","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1869</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Czechia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Czechia","dot":"Prague"},"facts":[["Country","Czechia"],["Population","1.30M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+9.6% (2022–2025)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","czechia"],"abstract":"<b>Prague</b>’s diplomatic history is set down under two country headings rather than one, because the state around it changed while the city did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American consular post in Bohemia was here, a consulate opened in 1869 and closed on 9 April 1917 after Austria-Hungary severed relations with the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition came to a government in exile before it came to the city: on 3 September 1918 Secretary of State Robert Lansing announced that the United States recognised the Czecho-Slovak National Council, resident in Paris, as a de facto government, and independence from Austria-Hungary was declared on 18 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American legation was established here when Richard Crane, appointed minister on 23 April 1919, presented his credentials on 11 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It closed on 21 March 1939, six days after German forces occupied the city, and the United States did not recognise the protectorate established over Bohemia and Moravia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The other heading begins on 1 January 1993, when Czechia was established out of the former Czech and Slovak Federal Republic, which had ceased to exist the previous day in what is commonly called the Velvet Divorce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the new state and established relations with it that same day, its ambassador to the federal republic simply continuing in post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state took a United Nations seat on 19 January 1993, joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade that April and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995, and became an EU member state on 1 May 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations and the European Union give the country the same area to the square kilometre, 78,871 square kilometres (30,452 square miles), and different populations, 10,609,000 against 10,909,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 1,298,800 people are given here for 2019, the number the World Bank returns for that year and carries to about 1.33 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Czechoslovakia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/czechoslovakia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Czechia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/czech-republic. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Czechia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cz.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Czechia,” EU country profiles, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/czechia_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Czech Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/czech_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Czechia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CZE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/2018-08-14_CZ_Praha_01_%26_Praha_03%2C_Pet%C5%99%C3%ADnsk%C3%A9_sady._Vltava%2C_V%C3%ADtkov_%2850094971532%29.jpg/1920px-2018-08-14_CZ_Praha_01_%26_Praha_03%2C_Pet%C5%99%C3%ADnsk%C3%A9_sady._Vltava%2C_V%C3%ADtkov_%2850094971532%29.jpg","title":"Prague from Petřín","desc":"The red roofs of the old city and the Vltava, seen from the orchards on Petřín hill.","credit":"Paul Korecky, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018-08-14_CZ_Praha_01_%26_Praha_03%2C_Pet%C5%99%C3%ADnsk%C3%A9_sady._Vltava%2C_V%C3%ADtkov_%2850094971532%29.jpg","alt":"A city of red-tiled roofs and green domes along a river, seen from a wooded hillside under a sky of scattered cloud."}},
{"id":"gw-589","num":589,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tegucigalpa","answerText":"Tegucigalpa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 February 1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 December 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Honduras","dot":"Tegucigalpa"},"facts":[["Country","Honduras"],["Population","1.40M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+3.6% (2020–2023)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","honduras"],"abstract":"<b>Tegucigalpa</b> is the capital of Honduras, a Central American state of about 11 million people<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> whose 112,492 km<sup>2</sup> (43,433 sq mi) reach from the Caribbean coast south to the Pacific inlet of the Gulf of Fonseca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands in the highlands of the south, in a bowl of hills on the Choluteca river, and shares a central district with Comayagüela on the opposite bank. About 1.4 million people are counted in the country's largest city for 2019, the figure the World Bank returns for that year and carries above 1.6 million by 2025,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the same figure the United Nations Statistics Division gives for the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Honduras won its independence from Spain in 1821 and two years later joined the Federation of Central American States, a union of five provinces that broke apart between 1838 and 1840.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the Federation on 4 August 1824, when President Monroe received Antonio José Cañaz as its minister, and recognised Honduras as a state in its own right only after that union had dissolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That second recognition came on 19 April 1853, with the appointment of Solon Borland as minister to Honduras, Costa Rica, Nicaragua and El Salvador at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Borland did not present his credentials at Tegucigalpa, though he was accredited to Honduras, so the recognition of the state was marked by no ceremony in its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American legation opened on 22 February 1856 under the names of two towns rather than one, Comayagua and Tegucigalpa, which had alternated as the seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> James R. Partridge, presenting his credentials on 25 April 1862, was the first American minister to reside in the country, and the legation became an embassy after a joint announcement of 23 March 1943 covering seven American republics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Honduras took a seat at the United Nations on 17 December 1945,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 10 April 1994 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Honduras,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/honduras. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Honduras,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hn.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Honduras,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HND/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Honduras,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HND/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Honduras and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/honduras_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Centro_historico_de_Tegucigalpa_y_Comayag%C3%BCela.jpg/1920px-Centro_historico_de_Tegucigalpa_y_Comayag%C3%BCela.jpg","title":"Tegucigalpa and Comayagüela","desc":"The centre of Tegucigalpa and Comayagüela in their bowl of hills, the national stadium in the foreground.","credit":"Luis Alfredo Romero, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centro_historico_de_Tegucigalpa_y_Comayag%C3%BCela.jpg","alt":"A dense city of low buildings filling a valley, a large stadium in front and green hills rising behind."}},
{"id":"gw-590","num":590,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lisbon","answerText":"Lisbon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 May 1791</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation in Rio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1810 – 1821</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1986</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Portugal","dot":"Lisbon"},"facts":[["Country","Portugal"],["Population","2.90M (2019, Lisbon region)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+20.6% (2021–2025)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","portugal"],"abstract":"<b>Lisbon</b> is the capital of Portugal, a state of about 92,226 km<sup>2</sup> (35,609 sq mi)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> on the western edge of the Iberian peninsula whose territory takes in the Atlantic archipelagos of the Azores and Madeira. The city stands on the north bank of the Tagus, where that river opens into a broad estuary a few kilometres above the ocean, on a run of hills its streets climb in flights of steps. The country's population is given as about 10.4 million by the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and as 10,749,635 by Eurostat, a difference of some 3 per cent that the two bodies have not reconciled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The capital figure the United Nations publishes, about 2.9 million for 2019, is not the city at all but a region its own footnote defines as Greater Lisbon, the Setúbal peninsula and the municipality of Azambuja.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank returns the same number to the person for its largest-city series in that year, and carries it above 3.0 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Portugal was the first neutral state to establish diplomatic relations with the United States, which it recognised on 13 May 1791 by accepting the credentials of David Humphreys at Lisbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American legation was established in the city on that same day, and has sat there ever since but for one interruption of eleven years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the King of Portugal fled to Brazil during the Napoleonic Wars the legation followed him, sitting at Rio de Janeiro from 1810 until it closed in July 1821, and returning to Lisbon with the King the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became an embassy on 20 June 1944, and a United States consulate has run in the city continuously since 1863.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Portugal took a seat at the United Nations on 14 December 1955,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> joined what is now the European Union on 1 January 1986, entered the Schengen area on 26 March 1995 and adopted the euro on 1 January 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Portugal,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/portugal. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Portugal,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pt.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Portugal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Portugal,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Portugal,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/portugal_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/View_of_Lisbon_and_Tejo_-_01.jpg/1920px-View_of_Lisbon_and_Tejo_-_01.jpg","title":"Lisbon and the Tagus","desc":"The roofs of the Baixa looking west to the Tagus estuary and the 25 de Abril bridge.","credit":"Norbert Nagel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Lisbon_and_Tejo_-_01.jpg","alt":"A city of orange tiled roofs running down to a wide grey river, with a red suspension bridge in the distance under a blue sky."}},
{"id":"gw-591","num":591,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dushanbe","answerText":"Dushanbe","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tajikistan","dot":"Dushanbe"},"facts":[["Country","Tajikistan"],["Population","894k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+49.8% (2016–2022)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tajikistan"],"abstract":"<b>Dushanbe</b> is the capital of Tajikistan, a landlocked Central Asian state of about 10.8 million people whose territory is mostly mountain, and whose extent the United Nations Statistics Division gives as 141,400 km<sup>2</sup> (54,600 sq mi)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> against the World Bank's 141,379.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands in a valley in the west of the country, well clear of the high ranges that fill the east. About 894,000 people are counted there for 2019, the figure the United Nations gives for the capital<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the same number the World Bank returns that year for the country's largest city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the growth behind that figure is recent: the same series adds roughly 150,000 people to the city between 2019 and 2025, when it passes one million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tajikistan had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and became a state of its own when that union dissolved at the end of 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, the day President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 19 February 1992, when the same president announced that decision in a press statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American embassy in Dushanbe was established on 13 March 1992, with Edmund McWilliams as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tajikistan had taken a seat at the United Nations on 2 March 1992, eleven days before that embassy opened, so the city was the seat of a United Nations member state before it held an American mission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 2 March 2013, the same date of the year as its United Nations seat, 21 years on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tajikistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tajikistan. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Tajikistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tj.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Tajikistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TJK/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Tajikistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TJK/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Tajikistan and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/tajikistan_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/%D0%94%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B1%D0%B5_%28%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%29.jpg/1280px-%D0%94%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B1%D0%B5_%28%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%29.jpg","title":"Dushanbe from the hills","desc":"The city spread across its valley, with mountains along the far side.","credit":"Шухрат Саъдиев, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%94%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B1%D0%B5_%28%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B0%29.jpg","alt":"A low city filling a green valley under a pale sky, with a long line of hazy mountains behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-592","num":592,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Port Moresby","answerText":"Port Moresby","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 1 July 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Papua New Guinea","dot":"Port Moresby"},"facts":[["Country","Papua New Guinea"],["Population","375k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","papua new guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Port Moresby</b> is the capital of Papua New Guinea, a Melanesian state holding the eastern half of the island of New Guinea along with a scatter of smaller islands, and covering 462,840 km<sup>2</sup> (178,700 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands on the south coast of that island, on a harbour facing the Coral Sea. About 375,000 people are counted there for 2019, the figure the United Nations Statistics Division gives for the capital<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the number the World Bank returns that year for the country's largest city, carrying it above 430,000 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a small share of a national population the Commonwealth Secretariat put at 8.9 million in 2022<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the United Nations projects at 10.8 million for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state was formed from Papua and New Guinea, the second of them a trust territory administered by Australia, together with nearby islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The United States opened its first consulate in the then self-governing Territory of Papua and New Guinea at Port Moresby on or about 1 July 1974, when Mary S. Olmsted arrived to set the office up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It recognised the new state on 16 September 1975, when Robert D. Ray, Governor of Iowa, presented a letter to that effect from President Ford at the independence ceremonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established the same day by raising that consulate general to an embassy, with Olmsted, who had opened it, now in charge as ambassador-designate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ambassador at Port Moresby has since been accredited to the Solomon Islands and Vanuatu as well, and has remained resident in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Papua New Guinea took a seat at the United Nations on 10 October 1975,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and joined the Commonwealth the same year on its independence from that Australian-administered trusteeship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Papua New Guinea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/papua-new-guinea. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Papua New Guinea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pg.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Papua New Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PNG/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Papua New Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PNG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Papua New Guinea,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/papua-new-guinea. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Aerial_view_of_Port_Moresby.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_of_Port_Moresby.jpg","title":"Port Moresby from the air","desc":"The city on its peninsula, with the harbour and the wharves to the east.","credit":"Mark Matson, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Port_Moresby.jpg","alt":"A town on a green headland running into a wide blue bay, with ships offshore and hills beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-593","num":593,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Stockholm","answerText":"Stockholm","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 April 1783</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 April 1818</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 November 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sweden","dot":"Stockholm"},"facts":[["Country","Sweden"],["Population","1.60M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+16.3% (2010–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","sweden"],"abstract":"<b>Stockholm</b> is the capital of Sweden, a Northern European state of about 10.6 million people on the eastern side of the Scandinavian peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city stands on the Baltic coast, in the south-east of the country. About 1.6 million people are counted there for 2019, and the United Nations Statistics Division's own footnote says what is being counted: the “tätort”, the Swedish statistical locality, on the administrative divisions of 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank returns that number to the person for the country's largest city, and carries it above 1.7 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country's extent is harder to put in one figure: 438,574 km<sup>2</sup> at the United Nations,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 447,424 km<sup>2</sup> (172,800 sq mi) at Eurostat,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and 528,860 km<sup>2</sup> at the World Bank, whose indicator counts inland water and some coastal waterways as well as land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Sweden recognised the United States rather than the other way about, on 3 April 1783, when the Treaty of Amity and Commerce between the two was signed at Paris.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The approach had come from the Swedish minister there, who hoped it would be remembered that “Sweden was the first power in Europe which had voluntarily and without solicitation offered its friendship to the United States”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Benjamin Franklin negotiated that treaty without ever going to Sweden, staying in Paris where he was already minister to France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 29 April 1818, when Jonathan Russell presented his credentials and took up residence at Stockholm as minister to the court of Sweden and Norway, which were not then separate countries; the legation became an embassy in 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sweden took a seat at the United Nations on 19 November 1946,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade on 30 April 1950 and the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> the day it became a member state of the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sweden,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sweden. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Sweden,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/se.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Sweden,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Sweden,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Sweden,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/sweden_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Sweden and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/sweden_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Old_Town_Stockholm_August_2015.JPG/1920px-Old_Town_Stockholm_August_2015.JPG","title":"Gamla Stan across the water","desc":"The old town of Stockholm on its island, seen across the water from the quays of Södermalm.","credit":"Erik, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Town_Stockholm_August_2015.JPG","alt":"A waterfront of tall old houses and church spires on the far side of a channel, with a ferry crossing and quays in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gw-594","num":594,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Athens","answerText":"Athens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 November 1837</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First minister</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 June 1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy back</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1944</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Greece","dot":"Athens"},"facts":[["Country","Greece"],["Population","3.20M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-3.1% (2011–2021)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","greece"],"abstract":"<b>Athens</b> is the capital of Greece, a Southern European state holding the southern end of the Balkan peninsula together with a great many islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its two European sources disagree about the country's population by about 5 per cent: the United Nations Statistics Division projects 9.9 million for 2025<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> where Eurostat gives 10,409,547.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 3.2 million people are counted in the capital for 2019, and the United Nations footnote names what is being counted — “the localities of Calithèa, Peristérion and Piraeus, among others” — rather than the city of Athens alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank returns 3,154,152 for the country's largest city in that same year, the same figure, and shows it almost flat through to 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the country's extent the three agree closely: 131,957 km<sup>2</sup> (50,949 sq mi) at the United Nations,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 131,960 at the World Bank<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and 131,694 at Eurostat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised Greek independence on 7 November 1837, by empowering its minister at London to negotiate a treaty of commerce and navigation with the King of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had been asked four years earlier, by Britain, France and Russia, to acknowledge Otto of Bavaria as that king, and had answered with a note expressing willingness to do so without quite doing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gregory A. Perdicaris, appointed the first American consul in June 1837, took up his post at Athens on 7 January 1838, and the first minister resident, Charles K. Tuckerman, presented his credentials there on 16 June 1868.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation at Athens closed on 14 July 1941 under the German occupation, and the mission followed the Greek government first to London, where its head was commissioned to seven other exile governments as well, and then to Cairo, returning to Athens on 27 October 1944.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Greece took a seat at the United Nations on 25 October 1945<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and joined what is now the European Union on 1 January 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Greece,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/greece. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Greece,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Greece,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRC/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Greece,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Greece,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/greece_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Acropolis_of_Athens_view_from_Exarchia.jpg/1920px-Acropolis_of_Athens_view_from_Exarchia.jpg","title":"Athens and the Acropolis","desc":"The rooftops of central Athens with the Acropolis rising out of them and the sea beyond.","credit":"Contaminadas, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acropolis_of_Athens_view_from_Exarchia.jpg","alt":"A dense field of white flat-roofed buildings with a rocky hill and ancient temple standing above them, hazy sea on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-595","num":595,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Baku","answerText":"Baku","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 16 July 1997</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Azerbaijan","dot":"Baku"},"facts":[["Country","Azerbaijan"],["Population","2.30M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+1.7% (2018–2021)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","azerbaijan"],"abstract":"<b>Baku</b> is the capital of Azerbaijan, a Western Asian state of 86,600 km<sup>2</sup> (33,400 sq mi) between the Caucasus mountains and the Caspian Sea, a figure on which the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank agree exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands on the western shore of that sea. About 2.3 million people are counted there for 2019, and the United Nations footnote says what has been counted: the city together with “communities under the authority of the Town Council”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank returns 2,313,138 for the country's largest city in the same year, the same figure, and carries it towards 2.5 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its footnote on the national population of about 10.4 million is of a different kind, recording that the count includes Nagorno-Karabakh,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the territory at the centre of the Azeri-Armenian conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Azerbaijan had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and had been briefly independent once before, as a state recognised de facto by the Allies in January 1920 whose independence ended when the Red Army arrived that April.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the present state on 25 December 1991, the day President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 19 February 1992, by a press statement from the same president, and the American embassy in Baku opened on 16 March 1992 with Robert Finn as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Azerbaijan took a seat at the United Nations on 2 March 1992,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> twelve days after those relations were established and a fortnight before that embassy opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a member of the World Trade Organization, whose working party on Azerbaijan's accession was established on 16 July 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Azerbaijan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/azerbaijan. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Azerbaijan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/az.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Azerbaijan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AZE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Azerbaijan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AZE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accessions: Azerbaijan,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_azerbaidjan_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/A_view_to_Baku_and_it%27s_Boulevard.JPG/1920px-A_view_to_Baku_and_it%27s_Boulevard.JPG","title":"Baku and its bay","desc":"The curve of the boulevard along Baku bay, with the city's towers behind and the Caspian to the right.","credit":"Okinawa, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_view_to_Baku_and_it%27s_Boulevard.JPG","alt":"A curving waterfront park along a wide blue bay, with a city of tall modern buildings rising behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-597","num":597,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Budapest","answerText":"Budapest","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First consul</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1869, at Pesth</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 July 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Hungary","dot":"Budapest"},"facts":[["Country","Hungary"],["Population","1.80M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-2.9% (2015–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","hungary"],"abstract":"<b>Budapest</b> is the capital of Hungary, a landlocked state of Eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The three sources used here put the country's extent within twenty square kilometres of one another: 93,023 km<sup>2</sup> (35,900 sq mi) at the United Nations,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 93,030 at the World Bank<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and 93,012 at Eurostat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 1.8 million people are counted in the capital for 2019,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank returns 1,763,913 for the country's largest city in that same year, the same figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national population is about 9.6 million on the United Nations projection<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 9,539,502 on Eurostat's count, the two within about 1 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hungary has belonged to the World Trade Organization since 1 January 1995, and was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 9 September 1973,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> while its foreign policy was still generally aligned with the Soviet Union's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Kingdom of Hungary had an American consular agent from 1869, appointed at Pesth; a consul followed in 1874, and those appointed after 1888 were accredited to Budapest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition of Hungary as a separate state came late, because the United States ratified neither the Treaty of St-Germain, which recognised its independence from Austria, nor the Treaty of Trianon, which drew its postwar borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state of war with the former Austro-Hungarian Empire was ended instead by a Joint Resolution of Congress on 2 July 1921, and a treaty of friendly relations was signed that 29 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American legation at Budapest opened on 26 December 1921 under Ulysses Grant-Smith; it was broken off in December 1941, and a mission returned to the city in May 1945 before becoming a legation again in 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hungary took a seat at the United Nations on 14 December 1955<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and joined the European Union on 1 May 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Hungary,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/hungary. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Hungary,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hu.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Hungary,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HUN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Hungary,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HUN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Hungary,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/hungary_en. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Hungary and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/hungary_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/2018-07-05-budapest-buda-hill.jpg/1920px-2018-07-05-budapest-buda-hill.jpg","title":"Budapest from Buda hill","desc":"The Danube between the two halves of the city, Buda Castle at the left and the Parliament on the far bank.","credit":"Mmullie (WMF), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018-07-05-budapest-buda-hill.jpg","alt":"A wide view along a broad river dividing a city, a domed palace on a hill at the left and bridges crossing to the flat bank opposite."}},
{"id":"gw-598","num":598,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Vienna","answerText":"Vienna","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 August 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 April 1938</span><span class=\"dt-k\">State Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 May 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Austria","dot":"Vienna"},"facts":[["Country","Austria"],["Population","1.90M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+6.1% (2020–2025)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","austria"],"abstract":"<b>Vienna</b> is the capital of Austria, a landlocked state at the eastern edge of Western Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The three sources used here put the country's extent within four square kilometres of one another: 83,878 km<sup>2</sup> (32,400 sq mi) at the United Nations,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 83,879 at the World Bank<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and 83,882 at Eurostat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> About 1.9 million people are counted in the capital for 2019,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> the figure the World Bank returns that year for the country's largest city and carries just past two million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national population is about 9.1 million on the United Nations projection<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 9,197,213 on Eurostat's count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Austria joined the European Union on 1 January 1995, entered the Schengen area on 1 December 1997 and has used the euro since 1 January 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Republic of Austria was proclaimed on 13 November 1918, the day after Emperor Charles I abdicated, and is kept in the American diplomatic record as a different state from the Austrian Empire before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States never ratified the Treaty of St-Germain, so Congress ended the state of war with Austria-Hungary by Joint Resolution on 2 July 1921, and recognition came with a treaty of friendly relations signed at Vienna that 24 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation there closed on 30 April 1938 after the Anschluss and became a consulate general, which itself closed on 9 July 1941 along with every other American consulate in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Moscow Conference declared the annexation “null and void” on 1 November 1943; Austria and Vienna were divided into four occupation zones in August 1945, and the Four Powers recognised the republic within its 1937 boundaries on 7 January 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Austrian State Treaty, signed at Vienna on 15 May 1955, ended that occupation and declared the country free, independent and neutral,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Austria took a seat at the United Nations that 14 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Austria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/austria. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Austria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/at.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Austria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Austria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AUT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Austria,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/austria_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/03_Vienna%2C_Austria_-_aerial_view.jpg/1920px-03_Vienna%2C_Austria_-_aerial_view.jpg","title":"Central Vienna from the air","desc":"The inner city inside the ring of boulevards, with the Danube canal curving through and the river beyond.","credit":"Marek Ślusarczyk (Tupungato), CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:03_Vienna%2C_Austria_-_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"A dense old city seen from directly above, ringed by a broad boulevard, with a canal and a river across the top of the frame."}},
{"id":"gw-599","num":599,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Minsk","answerText":"Minsk","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sovereignty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 July 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 January 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belarus","dot":"Minsk"},"facts":[["Country","Belarus"],["Population","2.00M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","0.0% (2023–2026)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","belarus"],"abstract":"<b>Minsk</b> is the capital of Belarus, a landlocked state of Eastern Europe covering 207,600 km<sup>2</sup> (80,200 sq mi) by the United Nations count<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 207,629 by the World Bank's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About two million people are counted in the capital for 2019, on a definition the United Nations footnote gives as the city together with “communities under the authority of the Town Council”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank returns 2,016,677 for the country's largest city in that year, the same figure, and carries it past 2.07 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Belarus has held a seat at the United Nations since 24 October 1945,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> 46 years before it declared independence from the Soviet Union on 25 August 1991,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> so that membership date is not the date the present state began. It does not belong to the World Trade Organization, whose working party on its accession was established on 27 October 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Belarus had been held by the Russian empire from the end of the 18th century, and declared a short-lived National Republic on 25 March 1918 which the Bolsheviks absorbed into what became the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was occupied by Nazi Germany, retaken in 1944, and remained under Soviet control until it declared sovereignty on 27 July 1990 and independence the following August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised that independence on 25 December 1991 and established diplomatic relations the same day, both announced in President George H. W. Bush's address on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American embassy at Minsk was established on 31 January 1992, with John Ford as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2008 the American ambassador was recalled on 12 March after a threat of expulsion, Belarus recalled its own ambassador from Washington, and each country's missions were cut to five diplomats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belarus,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belarus. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Belarus,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/by.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Belarus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Belarus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accessions: Belarus,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_belarus_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/%D0%9C%D1%96%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA._%D0%A1%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%BF%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%88%D1%87%D1%8B_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%86%D1%96.jpg/1920px-%D0%9C%D1%96%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA._%D0%A1%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%BF%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%88%D1%87%D1%8B_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%86%D1%96.jpg","title":"Independence Square, Minsk","desc":"The square and its gardens from above, with the red brick church at the left and the avenue running north-east into the city.","credit":"Zedlik, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D1%96%D0%BD%D1%81%D0%BA._%D0%A1%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%BF%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%88%D1%87%D1%8B_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%B6%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%86%D1%96.jpg","alt":"A large paved square with lawns and a glass dome, a red brick twin-towered church at one side and a broad avenue leading away between low blocks."}},
{"id":"gw-600","num":600,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bern","answerText":"Bern","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1829</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1853</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 September 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Switzerland","dot":"Bern"},"facts":[["Country","Switzerland"],["Population","426k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","5th largest"],["Population growth","+1.6% (2020–2023)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","switzerland"],"abstract":"<b>Bern</b> is the capital of Switzerland, a landlocked Alpine state whose area the United Nations Statistics Division gives as 41,291 km<sup>2</sup> (15,900 sq mi)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank as 41,290.7.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands in the west of the country and is not the largest in it: the United Nations gives the capital about 426,000 people for 2019,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> where the World Bank's largest-city series returns 1,383,092 for that same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two are measuring different places, because that series is defined as “the urban population living in the country's largest metropolitan area” rather than the population of a capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Switzerland joined the United Nations only on 10 September 2002, more than half a century after the organisation was founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has belonged to the World Trade Organization since 1 July 1995 and was a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade from 1 August 1966.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The American record of relations with Switzerland does not name the capital anywhere: recognition is dated instead to the appointment of John Godfrey Boeker as consul general at Basel on 30 November 1829.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That date is hedged in the original, which calls the appointment the first official record of recognition that “appears to have been” made, and notes that Switzerland had sent honorary consuls to Washington and New York as early as 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two countries concluded a Convention for the Disposal of Property at Washington on 18 May 1847.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation were established on 29 June 1853, when Theodore S. Fay presented his credentials as minister resident in Switzerland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation became an embassy a century later, Frances E. Willis presenting her credentials on 9 October 1953 as the first American ambassador to the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Switzerland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/switzerland. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Switzerland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ch.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Switzerland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Switzerland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Switzerland and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/switzerland_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Central_Bern_from_north.jpg/1920px-Central_Bern_from_north.jpg","title":"The old town of Bern","desc":"The old town on its spur in the loop of the Aare, with the river and its bridges below and wooded hills behind.","credit":"CucombreLibre, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Bern_from_north.jpg","alt":"A close-packed old town of red-tiled roofs and a green spire on a spur above a wide turquoise river crossed by stone bridges."}},
{"id":"gw-601","num":601,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Freetown","answerText":"Freetown","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Message dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 April 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 September 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sierra Leone","dot":"Freetown"},"facts":[["Country","Sierra Leone"],["Population","1.20M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+27.6% (2015–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","sierra leone"],"abstract":"<b>Freetown</b> is the capital of Sierra Leone, a Western African state on the Atlantic coast whose area the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank both give as 72,300 km<sup>2</sup> (27,900 sq mi),<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> where the Commonwealth Secretariat gives 71,740.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The capital is also the country's largest urban area, so the two figures published for it agree: about 1.17 million people for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 1,168,424 at the World Bank, whose series counts a country's largest metropolitan area rather than its capital as such.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both show it growing quickly, past 1.38 million by 2025, an increase of about a fifth in six years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national population is about 8.8 million on the United Nations projection for 2025<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and was 7.997 million on the Commonwealth's figure for 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sierra Leone had been under British sovereignty, and became independent on 27 April 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised it that day, in a congratulatory message from President Kennedy to the government and people of Sierra Leone dated the 26th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on the 27th as well, when the American consulate general at Freetown was raised to embassy status with Herbert Reiner, Jr. as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mission in the capital was therefore not new: it changed rank on the day the state changed status.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth that same year, on its independence from Britain,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and took a seat at the United Nations on 27 September 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American summary of the relationship since then names two things: support for international efforts to end a decade-long civil war, and efforts to prevent the illegal sale of diamonds from the country, with economic and humanitarian aid in the years since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sierra Leone,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sierra-leone. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Sierra Leone,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sl.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Sierra Leone,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Sierra Leone,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Sierra Leone,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/sierra-leone. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Freetown-aerialview.jpg/1920px-Freetown-aerialview.jpg","title":"Freetown from the air","desc":"The city along the shore of its estuary, running up into the wooded hills behind.","credit":"David Hond, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freetown-aerialview.jpg","alt":"A low green city following a curving shoreline, with grey water in the foreground and forested hills rising behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-602","num":602,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Lomé","answerText":"Lomé","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Togo","dot":"Lomé"},"facts":[["Country","Togo"],["Population","1.80M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+46.1% (1997–2010)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","togo"],"abstract":"<b>Lomé</b> is the capital of Togo, a narrow Western African state on the Gulf of Guinea, of 56,785 km<sup>2</sup> (21,900 sq mi) by the United Nations count<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 56,790 by the World Bank's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands on the coast at the country's western edge, and is its largest urban area as well as its capital: about 1.79 million people are counted there for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 1,785,310 at the World Bank, which is the same figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That series carries it past 2.1 million by 2025, a rise of about a fifth in six years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national population of about 8.6 million comes with a note explaining itself: the United Nations marks the figure as an interim update of Togo's estimates released on 19 January 2026, taking in fuller results from the country's census of 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Togo joined the World Trade Organization on 31 May 1995 and had been a party to the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade since 20 March 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Togoland had been administered by France as a United Nations trust territory before the republic became independent, so the state that emerged was not a colony being released but a trusteeship ending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised the Republic of Togo on 27 April 1960, when Attorney General William Rogers, acting as personal representative of President Eisenhower, delivered a message to that effect to Prime Minister Sylvanus Olympio.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established the same day, when the American consulate at Lomé was raised to embassy status with Jesse M. MacKnight as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mission in the capital was therefore already in place, and changed rank on the day the state did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Togo took a seat at the United Nations that 20 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Togo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/togo. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Togo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tg.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Togo,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TGO/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Togo,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TGO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Togo and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/togo_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Quartier_des_administrations_%28Lom%C3%A9%2C_Togo%29.jpg/1280px-Quartier_des_administrations_%28Lom%C3%A9%2C_Togo%29.jpg","title":"Lomé from above","desc":"The administrative quarter of Lomé with the tower of the West African central bank, and the coast running east.","credit":"SebEsteban, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quartier_des_administrations_%28Lom%C3%A9%2C_Togo%29.jpg","alt":"A green low-rise city along a surf-lined coast, with a single tall pale office tower standing among the trees."}},
{"id":"gw-603","num":603,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Vientiane","answerText":"Vientiane","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 August 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1955</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ambassador back</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Laos","dot":"Vientiane"},"facts":[["Country","Laos"],["Population","673k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+15.5% (2015–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","laos"],"abstract":"<b>Vientiane</b> is the capital of Laos, a landlocked South-east Asian state of 236,800 km<sup>2</sup> (91,400 sq mi), a figure the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank give identically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands in the west of the country and is its largest urban area as well as its capital: about 673,000 people are counted there for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 673,061 at the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That series carries it to about 756,000 by 2025, so the capital holds under a tenth of the country's 7.9 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Laos had been a protectorate of France, and became an independent state as part of the French Union in 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It took a seat at the United Nations on 14 December 1955<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and joined the World Trade Organization much later, on 2 February 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the Kingdom of Laos on 7 February 1950, by an announcement from its Department of State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first officers in the country were consular and shared: a vice consul was assigned to Vientiane on 25 April 1950 and a consul on 7 December 1952, both also posted to Saigon and Phnom Penh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 29 July 1950, when Donald R. Heath presented his credentials to the King, though Heath was accredited to Cambodia and Vietnam as well and lived at Saigon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation at Vientiane opened in its own right on 22 August 1950 under a chargé d'affaires, and was raised to an embassy by a joint announcement of 10 August 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the founding of the Lao People's Democratic Republic in December 1975 the United States downgraded its head of mission at Vientiane from ambassador to chargé d'affaires, and posted no ambassador again until 6 August 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Laos,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/laos. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Lao People's Democratic Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/la.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Lao PDR,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LAO/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Lao PDR,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LAO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Lao People's Democratic Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/lao_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/20171118_Vientiane_3220_DxO.jpg/1920px-20171118_Vientiane_3220_DxO.jpg","title":"Vientiane from above","desc":"The low city of Vientiane among its trees, with a tiered red-roofed hall in the middle distance.","credit":"Jakub Hałun, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20171118_Vientiane_3220_DxO.jpg","alt":"A low green city under a blue sky with scattered cloud, a large white building with tiered orange roofs at its centre."}},
{"id":"gw-605","num":605,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ashgabat","answerText":"Ashgabat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Neutrality</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turkmenistan","dot":"Ashgabat"},"facts":[["Country","Turkmenistan"],["Population","828k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+34.8% (1989–1995)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","turkmenistan"],"abstract":"<b>Ashgabat</b> is the capital of Turkmenistan, a Central Asian state whose area the United Nations Statistics Division gives as 488,100 km<sup>2</sup> (188,500 sq mi)<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank as 491,209, about half a per cent more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands in the south of the country and is its largest urban area as well as its capital: about 828,000 people are counted there for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 828,065 at the World Bank, which is the same figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country as a whole holds about 7.6 million people at a density of roughly 16 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Turkmenistan had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and took a seat at the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three years later the General Assembly adopted, without a vote, a resolution titled “Permanent neutrality of Turkmenistan” on 12 December 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the country's independence on 25 December 1991, the day President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 19 February 1992, by a press statement from the same president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American embassy was established on 17 March 1992, with Jeffrey White as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The heading under which that embassy is recorded names the city twice over, as “Ashkabad (now Ashgabat)”, the older transliteration standing beside the current one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The summary of relations since then gives one dated step: an agreement of February 2007 to find ways of cooperating on political and human rights reform, on economic and agricultural reform, and on education, health care, energy and security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Turkmenistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/turkmenistan. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Turkmenistan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Turkmenistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TKM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Turkmenistan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TKM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Permanent neutrality of Turkmenistan,” resolution A/RES/50/80A, 12 December 1995, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/284240. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/A%C5%A1kabat_spomenik_nevtralnosti_%2811%29.jpg/1920px-A%C5%A1kabat_spomenik_nevtralnosti_%2811%29.jpg","title":"Ashgabat's white blocks","desc":"Apartment blocks faced in white marble across an open plaza in Ashgabat, with the hills of the south behind.","credit":"Ljuba brank, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A%C5%A1kabat_spomenik_nevtralnosti_%2811%29.jpg","alt":"Rows of tall white apartment blocks with red roofs across a wide empty paved square under a pale sky."}},
{"id":"gw-606","num":606,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tripoli","answerText":"Tripoli","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised US</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 November 1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 December 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 September 1954</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Office to Tunis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 2015</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Libya","dot":"Tripoli"},"facts":[["Country","Libya"],["Population","1.20M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","libya"],"abstract":"<b>Tripoli</b> is the capital of Libya, a North African state on the Mediterranean whose area the two standard sources give 5 per cent apart: 1,676,198 km<sup>2</sup> (647,200 sq mi) at the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 1,759,540 km<sup>2</sup> (679,400 sq mi) at the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands on that coast and is the country's largest urban area as well as its capital: about 1.16 million people are counted there for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 1,160,918 at the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country holds about 7.5 million people at a density of some four to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before independence the territory had been a province of the Ottoman Empire, an Italian colony from 1912 until 1947, and under joint British and French occupation from 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Libya declared its independence on 24 December 1951 and took a seat at the United Nations only on 14 December 1955, four years later,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and it is not a member of the World Trade Organization, whose working party on its accession was established on 27 July 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The American record here runs the other way about at its start: Tripoli recognised the United States, by a peace treaty signed in the city on 4 November 1796.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American consul, James Leander Cathcart, took up his post there on 5 April 1799, and the consulate closed on 14 May 1801 at the outbreak of the Tripolitan War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern relations began with independence: a consulate general became a legation on 24 December 1951 and an embassy on 25 September 1954.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That embassy closed on 2 May 1980 without relations being formally severed, and did not reopen until 31 May 2006, by way of an interests section inside the Belgian embassy and then a liaison office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its operations were suspended in 2011 and again on 26 July 2014, and since March 2015 the mission has worked from Tunisia as the Libya External Office in Tunis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Libya,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/libya. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Libya,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ly.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Libya,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBY/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Libya,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBY/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accessions: Libya,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_libya_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%85%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%8A%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%B7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%84%D8%B3_Tripoli.jpg/1280px-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%85%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%8A%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%B7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%84%D8%B3_Tripoli.jpg","title":"The Tripoli seafront","desc":"The towers of central Tripoli along the Mediterranean at dusk, seen across the water from the shore.","credit":"Sanadalahlafi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%85%D8%A9_%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%84%D9%8A%D8%A8%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%B7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%84%D8%B3_Tripoli.jpg","alt":"A line of tall modern towers along a low seafront at dusk, seen across calm water from a rocky foreshore."}},
{"id":"gw-607","num":607,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bishkek","answerText":"Bishkek","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">WTO member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 December 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kyrgyzstan","dot":"Bishkek"},"facts":[["Country","Kyrgyzstan"],["Population","1.00M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+27.1% (2015–2025)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","kyrgyzstan"],"abstract":"<b>Bishkek</b> is the capital of Kyrgyzstan, a landlocked Central Asian state of 199,949 km<sup>2</sup> (77,200 sq mi), a figure on which the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank agree to within a square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city stands in the north of the country and is its largest urban area as well as its capital: about 1.02 million people are counted there for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 1,017,166 at the World Bank, which is the same figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That series carries it past 1.15 million by 2025, so roughly a seventh of the country's 7.3 million people live in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kyrgyzstan had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and took a seat at the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the World Trade Organization on 20 December 1998, under the name the organisation still files it by, the Kyrgyz Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised the country's independence on 25 December 1991, and established diplomatic relations the same day, both announced in President George H. W. Bush's address to the nation on the dissolution of the Soviet Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American embassy at Bishkek was established on 1 February 1992, with Edmund McWilliams as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> McWilliams opened the embassy at Dushanbe under the same title six weeks later, on 13 March 1992, so one officer began two Central Asian missions in a single winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The heading over that entry gives the year as 2004, which its own sentence contradicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beyond those three dates the American record carries nothing further at all, which is the usual shape for a state that had no diplomatic history of its own before 1991 and so has no earlier consulate or legation to list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kyrgyzstan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kyrgyzstan. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Kyrgyzstan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kg.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Kyrgyz Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KGZ/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Kyrgyz Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KGZ/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “The Kyrgyz Republic and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/kyrgyz_republic_e.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tajikistan,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tajikistan. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%88%D0%BA%D0%B5%D0%BA%2C_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%82_%D0%A7%D1%83%D0%B9_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%85%D1%83.jpg/1920px-%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%88%D0%BA%D0%B5%D0%BA%2C_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%82_%D0%A7%D1%83%D0%B9_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%85%D1%83.jpg","title":"Bishkek from above","desc":"The low centre of Bishkek along Chuy Avenue, with the mountains of the Tien Shan on the horizon.","credit":"Nikolay Bulykin, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D0%B8%D1%88%D0%BA%D0%B5%D0%BA%2C_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%82_%D0%A7%D1%83%D0%B9_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%B8%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B2%D0%B5%D1%80%D1%85%D1%83.jpg","alt":"A grid of low city blocks among bare trees seen from the air, with snow-topped mountains along the far horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-608","num":608,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Asunción","answerText":"Asunción","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 May 1811</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1852</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 November 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1942</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Paraguay","dot":"Asunción"},"facts":[["Country","Paraguay"],["Population","3.30M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-12.7% (2012–2022)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","paraguay"],"abstract":"<b>Asunción</b> is the capital of Paraguay, a landlocked South American state of 406,752 km<sup>2</sup> (157,000 sq mi), a figure the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank give identically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 3.28 million people are counted there for 2019, on a definition the United Nations footnote states outright: the district of Asunción together with the 19 districts of Central Department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank returns 3,279,160 for the country's largest metropolitan area in the same year, the same figure, and carries it past 3.6 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against a national population of about 7.0 million, that puts nearly half the country inside the capital's own statistical area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Paraguay's United Nations membership dates from 24 October 1945, the day the Charter came into force,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and joined the World Trade Organization on 1 January 1995 after entering the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade the previous January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Paraguay declared its independence from Spain on 15 May 1811.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of its history after that was marked, in the American record's summary, by civil war and military coups; the United States recognised it as a separate and independent state 41 years after the declaration, on 27 April 1852, by giving John M. Pendleton, its chargé d'affaires at Buenos Aires, full power to negotiate a treaty of commerce with the Paraguayan government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So the act of recognition was performed from another country's capital, and no American mission stood in Asunción for another nine years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation there were established on 26 November 1861, when Commissioner Charles A. Washburn presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A joint announcement of 4 January 1942 raised both countries' legations to embassies, the change taking effect on 31 March that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Paraguay,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/paraguay. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Paraguay,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/py.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Paraguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRY/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Paraguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRY/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Paraguay and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/paraguay_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Centro_Hist%C3%B3rico_de_Asunci%C3%B3n.jpg/1920px-Centro_Hist%C3%B3rico_de_Asunci%C3%B3n.jpg","title":"The centre of Asunción","desc":"The historic centre of Asunción from above, with the Paraguay river running past beyond it.","credit":"FF MM, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centro_Hist%C3%B3rico_de_Asunci%C3%B3n.jpg","alt":"A city of red-tiled roofs and scattered tower blocks seen from above, with a broad brown river along the far edge."}},
{"id":"gw-609","num":609,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Managua","answerText":"Managua","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 November 1838</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 December 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First mission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 1943</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nicaragua","dot":"Managua"},"facts":[["Country","Nicaragua"],["Population","1.10M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+8.5% (1995–2005)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","nicaragua"],"abstract":"<b>Managua</b> is the capital of Nicaragua, a Central American state of 130,374 km<sup>2</sup> (50,338 sq mi) with coasts on both the Pacific and the Caribbean, a figure the United Nations Statistics Division<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the World Bank agree on to within four square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city is the country's largest urban area as well as its capital: about 1.06 million people are counted there for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 1,055,472 at the World Bank, which is the same figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That series carries it past 1.12 million by 2025, against a national population of about 7.0 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nicaragua's United Nations membership dates from 24 October 1945, the day the Charter came into force,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and it joined the World Trade Organization on 3 September 1995 after four decades in the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its American diplomatic record begins not with the country but with a union: Nicaragua joined the Federation of Central American States in 1823, and the United States recognised that Federation on 4 August 1824.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nicaragua withdrew from the Federation on 5 November 1838, and the United States recognised it separately on 24 December 1849, when President Polk received the Nicaraguan chargé d'affaires Eduardo Carcache.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first American mission in the country followed on 18 February 1851, when Chargé d'Affaires John B. Kerr presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were severed twice from the American side, on 1 December 1909 and again in 1947, and re-established on 21 February 1911 and afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between those breaks came a period in 1926 when the United States refused to recognise a government that had taken power by force, and recognised its successor only in November of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legation at Managua was raised to an embassy on 27 March 1943, under a heading that gives the year as 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nicaragua,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nicaragua. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Nicaragua,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ni.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Nicaragua,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NIC/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Nicaragua,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NIC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2020:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Nicaragua and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/nicaragua_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Managua-panorama.jpg/1920px-Managua-panorama.jpg","title":"Managua and its lake","desc":"The low centre of Managua among trees, with Lake Managua and the hills beyond it.","credit":"Jon Evans, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Managua-panorama.jpg","alt":"A green city of low buildings and one tall tower seen over treetops, with a wide pale lake and distant hills behind."}},
{"id":"gw-610","num":610,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belgrade","answerText":"Belgrade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Envoy recalled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 2000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 2000</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Serbia","dot":"Belgrade"},"facts":[["Country","Serbia"],["Population","1.40M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+2.7% (2011–2022)"]],"difficulty":1,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","serbia"],"abstract":"<b>Belgrade</b> is the capital of Serbia, a landlocked state of southern Europe whose area the United Nations Statistics Division gives as 88,444 km<sup>2</sup> (34,100 sq mi).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank's series does not sit still beside it: 88,360 for 2019 and 84,990 from 2020 onwards, a step of nearly 4 per cent with no explanation attached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> About 1.39 million people are counted in the capital for 2019, on a definition the United Nations gives simply as the urban population of the Belgrade area, and the World Bank returns 1,393,717 for the same year, which is the same figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national population of about 6.7 million comes with a footnote of its own, stating that the count excludes Kosovo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Serbia's United Nations membership dates from 1 November 2000, and it does not belong to the World Trade Organization, whose working party on its accession was established on 15 February 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The American diplomatic record keeps the modern republic separate from the Kingdom of Serbia and from the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, and says so at the head of the entry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Serbia and Montenegro declared a new Federal Republic of Yugoslavia in April 1992, the United States announced on 21 May that it would not recognise it as the old federation's successor, and recalled its ambassador from Belgrade while leaving the mission open under a chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relations were severed outright on 23 March 1999, the day before the NATO bombing began, and Embassy Belgrade closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Full relations were established by an exchange of letters dated 12 November 2000 between Presidents Clinton and Koštunica, which also constituted recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The embassy reopened in May 2001 and an ambassador was appointed that 26 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Serbia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/serbia. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Serbia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/rs.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Serbia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SRB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Serbia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SRB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2019:2023. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “Accessions: Serbia,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/acc_e/a1_serbia_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/%D0%A3%D1%88%D1%9B%D0%B5_%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5_%D1%83_%D0%94%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0.jpg/1920px-%D0%A3%D1%88%D1%9B%D0%B5_%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5_%D1%83_%D0%94%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0.jpg","title":"Where the Sava meets the Danube","desc":"The confluence below Belgrade, the fortress park on the point and the city spreading to the right.","credit":"Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A3%D1%88%D1%9B%D0%B5_%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B5_%D1%83_%D0%94%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B4_%D0%B4%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D0%BE%D1%81%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B2%D0%B0_%D0%B8_%D0%9A%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%B4%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0.jpg","alt":"Two rivers of different colours joining below a wooded fortress headland, with a city along the right bank seen from the air."}},
{"id":"gw-611","num":611,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sofia","answerText":"Sofia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 October 1908</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 May 1909</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2007</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bulgaria","dot":"Sofia"},"facts":[["Country","Bulgaria"],["Population","1.30M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+1.9% (2022–2025)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","bulgaria"],"abstract":"<b>Sofia</b> is the capital of Bulgaria, a state of eastern Europe whose area is given as 110,996 km<sup>2</sup> (42,900 sq mi) by Eurostat<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and by the World Bank alike,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> where the United Nations Statistics Division gives 110,372.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city stands in the west of the country and is its largest urban area as well as its capital: about 1.28 million people are counted there for 2019 at the United Nations<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 1,276,937 at the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That series has the capital turning over rather than growing, peaking at 1,288,114 in 2023 and easing to 1,286,460 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country's own population is about 6.7 million on the United Nations projection<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and 6,437,360 on Eurostat's count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bulgaria took a seat at the United Nations on 14 December 1955<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and joined the European Union on 1 January 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Its diplomatic history with the United States begins the wrong way round: relations were established on 19 September 1903, more than five years before the recognition of 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bulgaria declared independence from the Ottoman Empire on 5 October 1908, and the United States recognised it on 3 May 1909, congratulating Tsar Ferdinand “upon the admission of Bulgaria to the community of sovereign and independent States”.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its American representatives were shared for years either side of that: the first, appointed in 1901, was concurrently consul general at Constantinople and never presented credentials at Sofia, and later agents were also ministers to Romania and Serbia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only after the First World War was a representative commissioned to Bulgaria alone, and the legation at Sofia was established on 18 March 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bulgaria declared war on the United States on 13 December 1941, the legation reopened on 27 September 1947, and Bulgaria severed relations again on 20 February 1950, a month after declaring the American minister persona non grata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bulgaria,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bulgaria. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Bulgaria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bg.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Bulgaria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BGR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2019:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Bulgaria,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BGR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2019:2023. [Open access]","European Union, “Bulgaria,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/bulgaria_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Panoramic_view_over_central_Sofia_and_the_Vitosha_Mountain_2017-10-08.jpg/1920px-Panoramic_view_over_central_Sofia_and_the_Vitosha_Mountain_2017-10-08.jpg","title":"Central Sofia beneath Vitosha","desc":"The centre of Sofia seen from above, with the Vitosha massif rising behind the city to the south.","credit":"Photograph by Deensel, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramic_view_over_central_Sofia_and_the_Vitosha_Mountain_2017-10-08.jpg","alt":"A wide view over the rooftops of central Sofia with a long forested mountain ridge behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-612","num":612,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"San Salvador","answerText":"San Salvador","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 June 1863</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"El Salvador","dot":"San Salvador"},"facts":[["Country","El Salvador"],["Population","1.10M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+4.6% (2007–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","el salvador"],"abstract":"<b>San Salvador</b> is the capital and largest city of El Salvador, a Central American state of 21,040 km² (8,120 sq mi) holding about 6.4 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 1.11 million people for 2019 and names its constituents outright: the urban parts of San Salvador itself and of Mejicanos, Soyapango, Delgado, Ilopango, Cuscatancingo, Ayutuxtepeque and San Marcos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank returns 1,105,662 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same number to the person, and its series climbs to about 1.13 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two bodies agree on the country's area to within a single square kilometre, the statistics division giving 21,041 km² and the World Bank 21,040.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> El Salvador joined the United Nations on 24 October 1945, the day the Charter came into force, and the World Trade Organization on 7 May 1995, having joined its predecessor agreement in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> El Salvador declared independence from Spain in 1821 alongside the other Central American provinces, joining them in 1823 in the United Provinces of Central America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised that federation on 4 August 1824 and Salvador as a separate independent state only on 1 May 1849, after the federation broke up between 1838 and 1840 and El Salvador declared itself a republic in 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 15 June 1863, when James R. Partridge presented his credentials in San Salvador, and the American legation opened in the city the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between 1896 and 1898 those relations ran through a union rather than the state: Honduras, Nicaragua and Salvador formed the Greater Republic of Central America to exercise their external sovereignty, and Washington received a minister from it that December, though President Grover Cleveland noted that the individual republics' responsibilities remained wholly unaffected; the union dissolved on 29 November 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Relations were interrupted again on 4 December 1931, when Washington declined to recognise a revolutionary government; they resumed in January 1934 and the legation became an embassy in 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “El Salvador,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sv.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: El Salvador,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLV/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: El Salvador,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: El Salvador,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/el-salvador. [Open access]","World Trade Organization, “El Salvador and the WTO,” https://www.wto.org/english/thewto_e/countries_e/el_salvador_e.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Complete_Skyline_2011.jpg/1920px-Complete_Skyline_2011.jpg","title":"San Salvador below its volcano","desc":"San Salvador spread across its valley, with the San Salvador volcano rising behind the city.","credit":"Photograph by Sammiethedeadrat, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Complete_Skyline_2011.jpg","alt":"A city of low buildings and scattered towers among trees, with a large green volcanic cone behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-632","num":632,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Asmara","answerText":"Asmara","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 May 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Eritrea","dot":"Asmara"},"facts":[["Country","Eritrea"],["Population","929k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","eritrea"],"abstract":"<b>Asmara</b> is the capital and largest city of Eritrea, a state of about 121,766 km² (47,000 sq mi) in Eastern Africa holding some 3.6 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division names Asmara as the capital and gives it about 929,000 people for 2019, and the World Bank returns 928,758 for the same year as the population of the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person, though neither says what boundary it is drawn around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank series climbs steeply from about 803,000 in 2015, passing a million in 2022 and reaching some 1.15 million in 2025, an increase of more than two fifths in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its area series is restless for a figure of that kind, moving between 121,630 and 121,766 km² across the same years before settling at the higher value from 2021, against the statistics division's 121,144.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eritrea was admitted to the United Nations on 28 May 1993, a date given both by the statistics division and by the organisation's own list of member states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Eritrea had previously been under Ethiopian sovereignty, and declared its independence on 27 April 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised the Republic of Eritrea the same day, when the American consulate at Asmara informed the Eritrean authorities of the decision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The capital is therefore the instrument of recognition here: the guide records no exchange of notes between governments and no envoy sent for the purpose, but a message delivered in the city on the day the state came into being.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 11 June 1993, when that same consulate was raised to embassy status under Joseph P. O'Neill as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There was no legation stage and no minister presenting credentials, and the whole sequence from declaration to recognition to embassy ran through one post in the capital in under seven weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Eritrea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/er.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Eritrea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ERI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Eritrea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ERI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Eritrea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/eritrea. [Open access]","United Nations, “Member States,” https://www.un.org/en/about-us/member-states. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Asmara%2C_2024.jpg/1920px-Asmara%2C_2024.jpg","title":"Asmara from above","desc":"The rooftops of Asmara in 2024, with a minaret and a church tower among them and the highland ridges beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Mheidegger, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asmara%2C_2024.jpg","alt":"A dense spread of low corrugated rooftops with a white minaret and a church spire, low hills behind."}},
{"id":"gw-630","num":630,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Zagreb","answerText":"Zagreb","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Croatia","dot":"Zagreb"},"facts":[["Country","Croatia"],["Population","685k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-2.9% (2011–2021)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","croatia"],"abstract":"<b>Zagreb</b> is the capital and largest city of Croatia, a state of 56,594 km² (21,850 sq mi) in southern Europe holding about 3.85 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 685,000 people for 2019 and says the figure refers to the settlement of Zagreb, the Croatian statistical unit rather than any administrative boundary, and the World Bank returns 685,233 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That series falls year on year, from 686,652 in 2015 to 684,114 in 2023, before easing back a little by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same two bodies disagree about the country's size by more than half: the statistics division and the European Union both give 56,594 km², where the World Bank's surface-area series returns 88,070, that series counting inland waters and some coastal waterways where the others do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Croatia was admitted to the United Nations on 22 May 1992, 45 days after the United States recognised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Croatia had been a constituent part of Yugoslavia, and the United States recognised it as an independent state on 7 April 1992, when the George H. W. Bush administration announced the decision in a White House statement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 6 August 1992, announced by the president himself during remarks to the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The American embassy in Zagreb opened nineteen days later, on 25 August 1992, with Ronald Nietzke as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both steps were therefore taken in public: the guide records a statement and a press remark rather than an exchange of notes between governments or an envoy presenting his credentials in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Croatia joined the European Union on 1 July 2013, and adopted the euro and entered the Schengen area together on 1 January 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Croatia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/hr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Croatia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HRV/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Croatia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/HRV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Croatia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/croatia. [Open access]","European Union, “Croatia,” EU countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/croatia_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Zagreb%2C_Panorama_001.jpg/1920px-Zagreb%2C_Panorama_001.jpg","title":"Zagreb from the upper town","desc":"Zagreb seen over the tiled roofs of its old upper town, with the lower city and its towers beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Vojtěch Dočkal, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zagreb%2C_Panorama_001.jpg","alt":"Red-tiled roofs in the foreground, with a green-domed civic building and a glass tower in the city beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-639","num":639,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Vilnius","answerText":"Vilnius","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restored</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lithuania","dot":"Vilnius"},"facts":[["Country","Lithuania"],["Population","538k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+7.0% (2021–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","lithuania"],"abstract":"<b>Vilnius</b> is the capital and largest city of Lithuania, a state of 65,286 km² (25,207 sq mi) in northern Europe holding about 2.83 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 538,000 people for 2019 with no definition attached, and the World Bank returns 537,563 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same number to the person, on a series flattening near 541,500 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three institutions agree on the area to within six square kilometres: 65,286 km² at the statistics division, 65,290 at the World Bank and 65,284 at the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That closeness follows from the definitions: the World Bank's series counts inland waters and some coastal waterways, and a country with a short coast leaves it almost nothing to add.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lithuania joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991 and the European Union on 1 May 2004, entering the Schengen area on 21 December 2007 and adopting the euro on 1 January 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised Lithuania on 28 July 1922, when its commissioner at Riga, Evan Young, informed the Lithuanian foreign office of the decision, and relations were established the same day, with Young continuing as representative to all three Baltic states at the new rank of minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No American post stood in Lithuania for another eight years: the legation at Kovno, later Kaunas, opened on 31 May 1930, earlier representatives having been accredited to Estonia and Latvia too and resident in Riga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That legation closed on 5 September 1940, after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the three Baltic states in August of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States never recognised the incorporation, let representatives accredited by the last independent government keep diplomatic status, and held that relations had continued uninterrupted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Recognition of the restored independence followed on 2 September 1991, normal relations resumed four days later by a signed memorandum, and an American embassy at Vilnius was established on 2 October 1991, the guide recording no earlier American post there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lithuania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lt.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Lithuania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LTU/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Lithuania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LTU/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lithuania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lithuania. [Open access]","European Union, “Lithuania,” EU countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/lithuania_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/View_from_Gediminas_01.jpg/1920px-View_from_Gediminas_01.jpg","title":"Vilnius from Gediminas hill","desc":"The old town of Vilnius seen from Gediminas hill, with a gothic brick church in the foreground and wooded slopes beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Averater, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Gediminas_01.jpg","alt":"Red-tiled roofs and brick church gables among trees, with a wooded ridge and two towers on the skyline."}},
{"id":"gw-645","num":645,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Chișinău","answerText":"Chișinău","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Moldova","dot":"Chișinău"},"facts":[["Country","Moldova"],["Population","504k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-3.8% (2004–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","moldova"],"abstract":"<b>Chișinău</b> is the capital and largest city of Moldova, a state of 33,847 km² (13,068 sq mi) in eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 504,000 people for 2019, and the World Bank returns 504,342 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both stand near the top of a falling series, which runs from 526,146 in 2015 down to 484,352 in 2025, a loss of about a twelfth of the city in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country's own count falls faster still on the World Bank's figures, from 2.84 million in 2015 to 2.36 million in 2025, where the statistics division gives 2.996 million for 2025 on a figure whose footnote says it includes the Transnistria region, so the two are not counting the same ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On area they agree closely, 33,847 km² at the statistics division against 33,850 at the World Bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Moldova had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation about the dissolution of that union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recognition therefore arrived inside a speech about the ending of another state rather than in a note addressed to Moldova.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 18 February 1992, agreed between Bush and the Moldovan president Mircea Snegur at a meeting in the White House, with the administration confirming that date in a statement issued the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Moldova was admitted to the United Nations on 2 March 1992, while the United States still had no post in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American embassy in Chișinău was established eleven days later, on 13 March 1992, with Howard Steers as chargé d'affaires ad interim, less than three months after the recognition announced in that address.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Moldova,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/md.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Moldova,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Moldova,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Moldova,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Moldova,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/moldova. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Chisinau%2C_Republic_of_Moldova.jpg/1920px-Chisinau%2C_Republic_of_Moldova.jpg","title":"Chișinău across its wooded valley","desc":"Chișinău seen across the wooded ground that runs through it, with apartment blocks on both slopes and a church dome at the right.","credit":"Photograph by Photobank MD, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chisinau%2C_Republic_of_Moldova.jpg","alt":"A green city seen from a rise, with white apartment blocks on the far slope above a band of woodland."}},
{"id":"gw-631","num":631,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tbilisi","answerText":"Tbilisi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 March 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 July 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Georgia","dot":"Tbilisi"},"facts":[["Country","Georgia"],["Population","1.10M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+16.3% (2016–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","georgia"],"abstract":"<b>Tbilisi</b> is the capital and largest city of Georgia, a state of 69,700 km² (26,911 sq mi) that the United Nations files under western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The statistics division gives the capital about 1.077 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 1,077,346 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person, on a series that barely moves across the decade, from 1,078,125 in 2015 to 1,087,022 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two agree exactly on the country's area as well, both giving 69,700 km².<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national population is another matter: the statistics division gives 3.807 million for 2025 on a figure whose footnote says it includes Abkhazia and South Ossetia, where the World Bank's total sits near 3.72 million from 2015 through 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That series then steps to 3.94 million by 2025, a rise its own footnote attributes to the results of the 2024 census rather than to any change on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Georgia had been a constituent republic of the Soviet Union, and the United States recognised its independence on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced the decision in an address to the nation about the dissolution of that union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 24 March 1992, announced in a statement from the press secretary, which the guide cites to the printed Public Papers of the Presidents rather than to a page of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American embassy in Tbilisi was established a month later, on 23 April 1992, with Carey Cavanaugh as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Georgia was admitted to the United Nations on 31 July 1992, three months after that embassy opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The entries sit under the wrong headings on the guide's own page, the establishment of relations appearing beneath Recognition and the embassy beneath Diplomatic Relations, with the recognition itself stated only in the summary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Georgia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ge.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Georgia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GEO/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Georgia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GEO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Georgia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GEO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025&footnote=y. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Georgia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/georgia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/2023-12-02_View_of_Tbilisi_01.jpg/1920px-2023-12-02_View_of_Tbilisi_01.jpg","title":"Tbilisi across the Mtkvari","desc":"Tbilisi seen over the tin roofs of its old quarter, with the river, a curved glass footbridge and mountains behind the city.","credit":"Photograph by Alexkom000, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2023-12-02_View_of_Tbilisi_01.jpg","alt":"Corrugated roofs in the foreground above a river, a curved glass bridge and a city under distant snow-capped mountains."}},
{"id":"gw-637","num":637,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Yerevan","answerText":"Yerevan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognised again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 February 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Armenia","dot":"Yerevan"},"facts":[["Country","Armenia"],["Population","1.10M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+2.1% (2020–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","armenia"],"abstract":"<b>Yerevan</b> is the capital and largest city of Armenia, a state of 29,743 km² (11,484 sq mi) that the United Nations files under western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The statistics division gives the capital about 1.083 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 1,083,323 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person, on a series rising steadily to about 1.1 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the World Bank's own figures for 2025 the city holds about 36 per cent of Armenia's people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two bodies do not agree on that national total, the statistics division giving 2.952 million for 2025 where the World Bank gives 3.087 million, about four and a half per cent apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On area they are close, the statistics division giving 29,743 km² and the World Bank 29,740.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States has recognised Armenia twice, 71 years apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first was on 23 April 1920, when Secretary of State Bainbridge Colby delivered a note to the republic's representative in Washington informing him of President Woodrow Wilson's decision, a note stating that the recognition in no way predetermined the territorial frontiers, which were matters for later delimitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wilson went on to arbitrate the boundary between Armenia and Turkey at the request of the Paris Peace Conference and submitted his determinations on 22 November 1920, by which time the territory had been attacked by Turkish and Bolshevik troops, and by the end of that year the republic had ceased to exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recognition came a second time on 25 December 1991, when President George H. W. Bush announced it in an address to the nation about the dissolution of the Soviet Union, the same address that established diplomatic relations, so the two share a date here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American embassy in Yerevan opened on 3 February 1992 with Steven Mann as chargé d'affaires ad interim, and Armenia was admitted to the United Nations a month later, on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Armenia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/am.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Armenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Armenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Armenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ARM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Armenia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/armenia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/2025-05-20_View_from_Erebuni_Fortress_6.jpg/1920px-2025-05-20_View_from_Erebuni_Fortress_6.jpg","title":"Yerevan from Erebuni","desc":"Yerevan seen from the Erebuni hill, a straight tree-lined avenue running down through the low roofs towards the city beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Alexkom000, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025-05-20_View_from_Erebuni_Fortress_6.jpg","alt":"A broad avenue lined with trees running away through a city of low roofs and apartment blocks under haze."}},
{"id":"gw-633","num":633,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ulaanbaatar","answerText":"Ulaanbaatar","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plebiscite</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 1987</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mongolia","dot":"Ulaanbaatar"},"facts":[["Country","Mongolia"],["Population","1.60M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+28.1% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","mongolia"],"abstract":"<b>Ulaanbaatar</b> is the capital and largest city of Mongolia, a state of 1,564,116 km² (603,908 sq mi) in eastern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 1.553 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 1,552,654 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person, on a series climbing by a quarter across the decade, from 1.36 million in 2015 to 1.72 million in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That growth is concentrating the country in one place: on the World Bank's figures the city held about 45 per cent of all Mongolians in 2015 and about 48 per cent in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Away from it the country is thinly peopled, the statistics division putting the density at 2.3 people per square kilometre for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two bodies agree on the area to the square kilometre, both giving 1,564,116 km².<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> For roughly 200 years before 1911 the territory lay within the borders of the Empire of China, and after that empire collapsed Mongolian nationalists established a republic in 1924 with Soviet assistance, independence being affirmed by a plebiscite in October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relations with the United States were slow to develop, and recognition was entertained under the Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Carter administrations without success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came at last on 27 January 1987, when the two states issued a joint communiqué which established diplomatic relations at the same moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The guide is unusually explicit about what had not counted: the United States did not block Mongolia's entry to the United Nations in 1961 but abstained, and had signed multilateral treaties to which Mongolia was also a party, and neither act constituted recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American embassy at Ulaanbaatar opened on 17 April 1988 with Steven Mann as chargé d'affaires ad interim, 27 years after Mongolia had taken its seat at the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mongolia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mn.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Mongolia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNG/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Mongolia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mongolia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mongolia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mongolia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/View_of_Ulaanbaatar_01.jpg/1920px-View_of_Ulaanbaatar_01.jpg","title":"Ulaanbaatar in 2023","desc":"Ulaanbaatar in 2023, with monastery roofs and round felt tents among the low houses in the foreground and apartment towers behind.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Ulaanbaatar_01.jpg","alt":"A city of low fenced houses and white round tents in the foreground, with tall apartment blocks and hills beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-640","num":640,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Doha","answerText":"Doha","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 February 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Qatar","dot":"Doha"},"facts":[["Country","Qatar"],["Population","637k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+37.7% (1986–2001)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","qatar"],"abstract":"<b>Doha</b> is the capital and largest city of Qatar, a state of 11,627 km² (4,489 sq mi) that the United Nations files under western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Here the usual agreement between the two institutions breaks: the statistics division gives the capital about 637,000 people for 2019, where the World Bank returns 715,997 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, a gap of some 12 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statistics division's own footnote says what its figure leaves out, namely the populations of the industrial area and of zone 58, so the two are measuring different ground rather than disagreeing about the same ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are rising steeply all the same, the World Bank's series running from 595,462 in 2015 to 831,689 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The national total moves sharply from year to year, falling on the World Bank's figures from 2.79 million in 2020 to 2.50 million in 2021 before reaching 2.97 million by 2025, and the statistics division reports 245.9 men for every 100 women in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Qatar had been a protectorate under Great Britain and declared independence on 3 September 1971, following the termination date of its treaties with that country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised the State of Qatar two days later, on 5 September 1971, in an announcement by the Department of State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Qatar was admitted to the United Nations on 21 September 1971, sixteen days after that announcement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 19 March 1972, when the American envoy William A. Stoltzfus presented his credentials, although he was accredited to several other Gulf states as well and lived in Kuwait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The embassy in Doha followed on 24 February 1973 under a chargé d'affaires ad interim, and Robert P. Paganelli became the first resident ambassador when he presented his credentials on 22 August 1974, almost three years after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Qatar,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/qa.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Qatar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/QAT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Qatar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/QAT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Qatar,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/QAT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Qatar,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/qatar. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Doha_Corniche_Skyline_View_1.jpg/1920px-Doha_Corniche_Skyline_View_1.jpg","title":"Doha across the bay","desc":"The towers of Doha seen across the bay from the corniche in 2023, with palms and a small harbour in the foreground.","credit":"Photograph by Zairon, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doha_Corniche_Skyline_View_1.jpg","alt":"A line of tall towers rising across a bay, with palm trees and moored boats on the near shore."}},
{"id":"gw-613","num":613,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Brazzaville","answerText":"Brazzaville","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1977</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Congo","dot":"Brazzaville"},"facts":[["Country","Republic of the Congo"],["Population","2.30M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+56.2% (2007–2023)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","republic of the congo"],"abstract":"<b>Brazzaville</b> is the capital and largest city of the Republic of the Congo, a state of 342,000 km² (132,047 sq mi) in middle Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two institutions that measure such things agree at every point here: both give the capital about 2.31 million people for 2019 and the same figure to the person, both give the area as 342,000 km², and their national totals for 2025 match to the thousand at roughly 6.48 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The capital's series climbs steadily across the decade, from 1,957,714 in 2015 to 2,813,478 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is growing as a share of the country as well, holding about 41 per cent of all Congolese in 2019 and about 43 per cent in 2025 on the World Bank's own figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Away from the city the country is thinly settled, the statistics division giving 19 people per square kilometre for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Before independence the territory was part of French Equatorial Africa, whose administration sided with Charles de Gaulle during the Second World War, and Brazzaville served as the symbolic capital of Free France from 1940 to 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Independence was proclaimed on 15 August 1960, and the United States recognised the republic that same day in a congratulatory message from President Dwight D. Eisenhower to President Fulbert Youlou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the embassy at Brazzaville were established on that date too, so recognition, relations and a post in the capital all fall on one day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Five years later to the day they were gone: the American diplomatic and consular staff left by 15 August 1965, the withdrawal citing serious mistreatment of American diplomats and officials that included arrest, detention, incommunicado interrogation and forced departure, and Congo moved its own mission from Washington to New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two states agreed to resume relations on 7 June 1977, and the embassy at Brazzaville reopened on 30 October of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Congo,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cg.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Congo, Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COG/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Congo, Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Congo, Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Republic of the Congo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/congo-republic. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Vue_a%C3%A9rienne_de_Brazzaville_13.jpg","title":"Brazzaville from the air","desc":"Brazzaville seen from above in 2025, low houses and trees running out to the haze, with a stadium at the right.","credit":"Photograph by Africany, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_a%C3%A9rienne_de_Brazzaville_13.jpg","alt":"A low green city photographed from a height, with an oval stadium among the houses and hazy country beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-615","num":615,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Copenhagen","answerText":"Copenhagen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 June 1792</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 October 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1827</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1973</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Denmark","dot":"Copenhagen"},"facts":[["Country","Denmark"],["Population","1.30M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+5.5% (2020–2025)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","denmark"],"abstract":"<b>Copenhagen</b> is the capital and largest city of Denmark, a state of 42,938 km² (16,578 sq mi) in northern Europe whose figures, the United Nations Statistics Division notes, leave out the Faroe Islands and Greenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The division gives the capital about 1.334 million people for 2019 and defines that as the Greater Copenhagen Region, made up of parts of 16 municipalities, and the World Bank returns 1,333,888 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three institutions put the country's area within 18 km² of one another, the division giving 42,938, the European Union 42,925 and the World Bank 42,920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The capital's series climbs to about 1.4 million by 2025 and the country's to about 6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Denmark took its seat at the United Nations on 24 October 1945 as a founding member and joined the European Union on 1 January 1973, entering the Schengen area in 2001 while keeping an opt-out from the euro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The recognition on the American guide's page runs the other way round: it is Denmark recognising the United States, when the American consul at Copenhagen, Hans Saabye, received an exequatur from the Danish government on or about 9 June 1792.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 12 October 1801, when the Danish minister resident presented his credentials to the American government, and an American legation at Copenhagen only 26 years after that, on 20 September 1827.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It closed on 20 December 1941, after war broke out between the United States and Germany; German forces had occupied the city on 9 April 1940 and the American minister had left it that June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He then took up residence in Ottawa as minister to a Danish government that remained in Danish hands until German authorities seized complete control in August 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The legation reopened on 16 June 1945 after the liberation and became an embassy on 18 March 1947, and the guide records that relations themselves have never been interrupted since 1801.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Denmark,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dk.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Denmark,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DNK/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Denmark,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DNK/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Denmark,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DNK/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, “Denmark,” EU countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/denmark_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Denmark,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/denmark. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Downtown_Copenhagen.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Copenhagen.jpg","title":"Central Copenhagen from above","desc":"The centre of Copenhagen from a height, with a green copper spire at the left, tiled roofs across the middle and the sea on the horizon.","credit":"Photograph by Mojnsen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Copenhagen.jpg","alt":"Red and green roofs of an old city seen from above, a tall copper-clad spire on the left and open water beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-616","num":616,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Beirut","answerText":"Beirut","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agent at Beirut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 October 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 September 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 November 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lebanon","dot":"Beirut"},"facts":[["Country","Lebanon"],["Population","2.40M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+19.9% (2012–2017)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","lebanon"],"abstract":"<b>Beirut</b> is the capital and largest city of Lebanon, a state of 10,452 km² (4,036 sq mi) that the United Nations files under western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The statistics division gives the capital about 2.407 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 2,406,875 for the same year, the same figure to the person, but the division attaches two warnings to its own number: that the estimate is drawn from scarce data, and that it excludes Syrian refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The capital's series turns over inside the decade, climbing to 2,434,609 in 2021 and falling back to 2,379,326 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The national total moves further still, from 6.47 million in 2015 down to 5.70 million in 2020 and back to 5.85 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On area the two bodies agree, the division giving 10,452 km² against the World Bank's 10,450, and Lebanon took its seat at the United Nations on 24 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> When the United States became independent Lebanon was part of the Ottoman Empire, and after that empire collapsed the League of Nations approved a French Mandate for Lebanon and Syria in 1922, which gave France exclusive control of their foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States appointed George Wadsworth as Agent and Consul General on 9 October 1942, a quasi-diplomatic presence in Beirut two years before it recognised the country at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recognition, when it came, was an exchange of letters with terms: on 7 September 1944 the American agent offered full and unconditional recognition upon receipt of written assurances about existing American rights, and the recognition dates from the Lebanese foreign minister's reply the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wadsworth presented his credentials as minister on 16 November 1944, and the legation at Beirut was raised to an embassy on 3 October 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its staff were withdrawn on 6 September 1989, after supporters of a faction in the civil war surrounded the building and stopped it working, and it reopened on 29 November 1990 when a new ambassador presented his credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lebanon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lb.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Lebanon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Lebanon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Lebanon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lebanon,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lebanon. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/42/Beirut_seen_from_Mansourieh.jpg","title":"Beirut from the hills","desc":"Beirut seen from the hills above it, the city spread along its headland with the Mediterranean beyond and tiled roofs in the foreground.","credit":"Photograph by Naxh, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beirut_seen_from_Mansourieh.jpg","alt":"A city on a coastal headland seen hazily from wooded hills, with red-tiled houses in front and open sea behind."}},
{"id":"gw-617","num":617,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Helsinki","answerText":"Helsinki","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 May 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restored</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 September 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Finland","dot":"Helsinki"},"facts":[["Country","Finland"],["Population","1.30M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+4.4% (2022–2025)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","finland"],"abstract":"<b>Helsinki</b> is the capital and largest city of Finland, a state of 336,884 km² (130,072 sq mi) in northern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 1.292 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 1,292,232 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The division's own footnotes then treat the Åland Islands two ways at once, the population figure including them and the area figure excluding them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That shows in the totals, its 336,884 km² sitting about 1,600 km² below the World Bank's 338,480 and the European Union's 338,363, and its stated density of 18.6 people per square kilometre is not the quotient of its own two figures, which would give 16.7.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Finland was admitted to the United Nations on 14 December 1955 and joined the European Union on 1 January 1995, adopting the euro in 1999 and entering the Schengen area in 2001.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Finland declared its independence from the Russian Empire on 6 December 1917, and the United States recognised it only on 7 May 1919, having held back over concerns about the country's political instability and its ties with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relations followed on 27 May 1919, when the acting secretary of state received the Finnish envoy provisionally because President Woodrow Wilson was still in Europe for the peace conference, and the full recognition waited for his return that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American legation opened in the city on 19 March 1920, under its Swedish name of Helsingfors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relations were severed on 30 June 1944 and the legation closed the same day, after the Finnish government formally admitted a military partnership with Nazi Germany against the allies of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A Special Mission was established at Helsinki on 16 January 1945 which the guide is careful to say was not a resumption of formal relations; those were restored on 1 September 1945, and the legation became an embassy in November 1952.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Finland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fi.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Finland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FIN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Finland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FIN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, “Finland,” EU countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/finland_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Finland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/finland. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/View_over_Siltavuorensalmi_towards_Merihaka_in_Kluuvi%2C_Helsinki%2C_Finland%2C_2022_April.jpg/1920px-View_over_Siltavuorensalmi_towards_Merihaka_in_Kluuvi%2C_Helsinki%2C_Finland%2C_2022_April.jpg","title":"Helsinki across the water","desc":"Helsinki seen across one of the inlets that run into the city, with apartment blocks and a raised road along the far shore.","credit":"Photograph by Ximonic (Simo Räsänen), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_over_Siltavuorensalmi_towards_Merihaka_in_Kluuvi%2C_Helsinki%2C_Finland%2C_2022_April.jpg","alt":"Pale apartment blocks and an office tower along a waterfront under a blue sky with scattered cloud."}},
{"id":"gw-618","num":618,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Monrovia","answerText":"Monrovia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 September 1862</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 February 1864</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Liberia","dot":"Monrovia"},"facts":[["Country","Liberia"],["Population","1.50M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","liberia"],"abstract":"<b>Monrovia</b> is the capital and largest city of Liberia, a state of 111,369 km² (43,000 sq mi) in western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 1.467 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 1,467,026 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person, and the two also agree on the area to a single square kilometre and on a national population of about 5.73 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The capital's series grows by more than two fifths across the decade, from 1,269,832 in 2015 to 1,794,648 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is growing faster than the country around it, holding about 29 per cent of all Liberians in 2019 and about 31 per cent by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Away from the capital the country is thinly settled, the statistics division giving 59.5 people per square kilometre for 2025, and Liberia took its seat at the United Nations on 2 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state began in 1822 as a settlement founded by the American Colonization Society for freedmen and recaptured slaves, and in 1847 that settlement constituted itself as a republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Several European states recognised the new republic, but the United States did not do so for another fifteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recognition came on 23 September 1862, when the American minister to England, Charles F. Adams, was empowered to conclude a treaty of commerce and navigation with Liberia, and he and the Liberian president signed it on 25 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The recognition of a republic founded from the United States was therefore transacted through the American legation in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation at Monrovia followed on 23 February 1864, when the American commissioner and consul general Abraham Hanson presented his credentials, and that legation was raised to an embassy on 6 May 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Liberia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Liberia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Liberia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Liberia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LBR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Liberia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/liberia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Liberia%2C_Africa_2013_-_panoramio_%288%29.jpg/1920px-Liberia%2C_Africa_2013_-_panoramio_%288%29.jpg","title":"Monrovia over the water","desc":"Monrovia seen from a rise above it, the roofs running down to a broad tidal water with the rest of the city on the far side.","credit":"Photograph by blk24ga, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liberia%2C_Africa_2013_-_panoramio_%288%29.jpg","alt":"A dense low city among palms sloping down to a wide river mouth, with more of the city on the opposite shore."}},
{"id":"gw-619","num":619,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Oslo","answerText":"Oslo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Union ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 June 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 October 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 August 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Returned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Norway","dot":"Oslo"},"facts":[["Country","Norway"],["Population","1.00M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+5.4% (2019–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","norway"],"abstract":"<b>Oslo</b> is the capital and largest city of Norway, a state of 323,772 km² (125,009 sq mi) by the United Nations Statistics Division's reckoning, filed under northern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The division gives the capital about 1.027 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 1,026,758 for the same year, the same figure to the person, on a series climbing to about 1.12 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the country's size the two are almost a factor of two apart, that 323,772 km² against the World Bank's 624,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither states the reason outright: the World Bank's series counts inland waters and some coastal waterways, and the division's population footnote says its people include Svalbard and Jan Mayen while its area row carries no note at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national totals agree closely all the same, about 5.62 million on one and 5.61 million on the other, and Norway took its seat at the United Nations on 27 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Treaty of Kiel transferred Norway from Danish to Swedish rule in 1814 and it accepted union with Sweden under a common monarch, until its assembly declared that union at an end on 7 June 1905 and a treaty of separation was signed in October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised the separation on 30 October 1905, in a message from Secretary of State Elihu Root which established diplomatic relations in the same breath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American consulate had stood in the capital since 1809, when the city was called Christiania, a name it kept until 1925.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Germany invaded on 9 April 1940 and the government left Oslo for England; the legation closed on 15 July, and a Legation Near the Government of Norway opened in London on 2 August, a post defined by the government it was accredited to rather than by where it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its head was in time accredited to seven other governments in exile as well, and the embassy was transferred from London back to Oslo on 31 May 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Norway,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/no.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Norway,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NOR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Norway,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NOR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Norway,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NOR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Norway,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/norway. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Oslo_cityscape_2006.jpg/1920px-Oslo_cityscape_2006.jpg","title":"Oslo below its wooded ridge","desc":"Central Oslo seen from the water, with the harbour front in view and the forested ridge that closes the city to the north behind it.","credit":"Photograph by WayneRay, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oslo_cityscape_2006.jpg","alt":"A low waterfront city of pale blocks and a green church spire, with a long dark wooded ridge rising behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-620","num":620,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bratislava","answerText":"Bratislava","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 January 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Slovakia","dot":"Bratislava"},"facts":[["Country","Slovakia"],["Population","433k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+12.0% (2018–2025)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","slovakia"],"abstract":"<b>Bratislava</b> is the capital and largest city of Slovakia, a state of 49,035 km² (18,933 sq mi) that the United Nations files under eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The statistics division gives the capital about 432,500 people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 432,508 for the same year, the same figure to the person, on a series rising slowly to about 444,000 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three institutions put the country's area within five square kilometres of one another, the division and the European Union both giving 49,035 km² and the World Bank 49,030.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the national population they differ in direction as well as size, the division putting it at about 5.475 million for 2025 where the World Bank's series peaks at 5,458,827 in 2020 and falls to 5,413,813, and Eurostat gives 5,419,451.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The division's own footnote marks its figure a projected estimate on the medium fertility variant; Slovakia joined the United Nations on 19 January 1993 and the European Union on 1 May 2004, entering the Schengen area in 2007 and adopting the euro in 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Slovakia declared itself a sovereign state in July 1992 and opened negotiations with the Czech Republic to disband the country that had been Czechoslovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Once parliament had approved, the two became separate countries on 1 January 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United States recognised both as sovereign states on that same day, in a public statement saying that an ambassador to the Slovak Republic would be appointed shortly afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American embassy in Bratislava were established three days later, on 4 January 1993, under a chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first American ambassador, Theodore E. Russell, presented his credentials on 22 November 1993, so recognition, an embassy and a resident ambassador all fell inside the state's first year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Slovakia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sk.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Slovak Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVK/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Slovak Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVK/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, “Slovakia,” EU countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/slovakia_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Slovak Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVK/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Slovakia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/slovakia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Bratislava%2C_hrad%2C_pohled_na_m%C4%9Bsto.jpg/1920px-Bratislava%2C_hrad%2C_pohled_na_m%C4%9Bsto.jpg","title":"Bratislava from the castle hill","desc":"Bratislava seen from the castle hill, the tiled roofs of the old town in front and a line of glass towers along the skyline.","credit":"Photograph by Aktron, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bratislava%2C_hrad%2C_pohled_na_m%C4%9Bsto.jpg","alt":"An old town of red roofs among trees, with modern office towers on the horizon and a green church spire at the right."}},
{"id":"gw-621","num":621,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dublin","answerText":"Dublin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 December 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 October 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 July 1927</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Ireland","dot":"Dublin"},"facts":[["Country","Ireland"],["Population","1.20M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+9.3% (2006–2016)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","ireland"],"abstract":"<b>Dublin</b> is the capital and largest city of Ireland, a state of 69,825 km² (26,960 sq mi) that the United Nations files under northern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The statistics division gives the capital about 1.215 million people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 1,214,666 for the same year, the same figure to the person, on a series climbing to about 1.30 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Three institutions give three different areas, that 69,825 km² against the European Union's 69,947 and the World Bank's 70,280, spanning 455 km² with no two of them agreeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The national populations disagree in direction as well, the division projecting about 5.308 million for 2025 where Eurostat gives 5,439,898 and the World Bank 5,484,367, a figure its own footnote marks as extrapolated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ireland was admitted to the United Nations on 14 December 1955 and joined the European Union on 1 January 1973, adopting the euro in 1999 while keeping an opt-out from the Schengen area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Ireland gained independence from the United Kingdom on 6 December 1921, when representatives of the two states signed the Anglo-Irish Treaty, which made 26 counties the Irish Free State while six counties of northeast Ulster remained British.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> American recognition followed on 28 June 1924, and it was addressed to the British ambassador in Washington rather than to Ireland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Secretary of State Charles E. Hughes told him the President would be pleased to receive a duly accredited Irish minister, replying to a British letter of 24 June that had asked American approval for the British plan to have an Irish minister handle Irish business at Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Diplomatic relations date from 7 October 1924, when Timothy A. Smiddy presented his credentials in Washington as minister plenipotentiary of the Irish Free State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The American legation in Dublin was established only on 27 July 1927, three years after that, when Frederick A. Sterling presented his own credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Ireland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ie.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Ireland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Ireland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, “Ireland,” EU countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/ireland_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Ireland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IRL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025&footnote=y. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Ireland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/ireland. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Dublin_city_panorama.JPG/1920px-Dublin_city_panorama.JPG","title":"Dublin along the Liffey","desc":"Dublin from the river, with quays on both banks, a curved footbridge across the water and a tall spire behind the domed customs house.","credit":"Photograph by Dowetz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dublin_city_panorama.JPG","alt":"A city river between built-up quays, with a low white footbridge, a green-domed building and a slender spire beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-622","num":622,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bangui","answerText":"Bangui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Central African Rep.","dot":"Bangui"},"facts":[["Country","Central African Republic"],["Population","870k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+9.2% (2005–2013)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","central african republic"],"abstract":"<b>Bangui</b> is the capital and largest city of the Central African Republic, a state of 622,984 km² (240,535 sq mi) in middle Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations Statistics Division gives the capital about 869,600 people for 2019 and the World Bank returns 869,599 for the same year as the country's largest metropolitan area, the same figure to the person, and the two agree as closely on the area, to four square kilometres, and on the national population, to the thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The capital's series grows by more than a quarter across the decade, from 797,960 in 2015, and passes a million in 2025 at 1,016,148.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Away from it the country is thinly settled, the statistics division giving 8.8 people per square kilometre for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The national total climbs from 4.63 million in 2015 to 5.51 million in 2025, with a single dip in 2022 when it fell back by about 14,000, and the republic was admitted to the United Nations on 20 September 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The independence of the Central African Republic from French Equatorial Africa was proclaimed on 13 August 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised the new state the same day, in a congratulatory message from President Dwight D. Eisenhower to Prime Minister David Dacko.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on that date as well, when the American consul at Brazzaville, Alan W. Lukens, presented his letter of credence as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Relations therefore began from a post in another country's capital, the consul acting for a state whose own capital had no American mission in it at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Embassy Bangui was established on 10 February 1961, nearly six months after independence, with the same man now resident in the capital as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Central African Republic,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cf.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Central African Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAF/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Central African Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAF/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Central African Republic,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CAF/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Central African Republic,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/central-african-republic. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Bangui_City_Centre.jpg/1920px-Bangui_City_Centre.jpg","title":"The centre of Bangui","desc":"Central Bangui from above, its red laterite streets meeting at a roundabout with the Ubangi river along the far side of the city.","credit":"Photograph by Alllexxxis, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangui_City_Centre.jpg","alt":"A low city of red-earth streets around a large roundabout, with a broad river and flat country beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-623","num":623,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Wellington","answerText":"Wellington","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 September 1907</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 February 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 April 1942</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1947</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"New Zealand","dot":"Wellington"},"facts":[["Country","New Zealand"],["Population","413k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","3rd largest"],["Population growth","+0.5% (2020–2023)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","new zealand"],"abstract":"<b>Wellington</b> is the capital of New Zealand, a state of 268,107 km² (103,517 sq mi) that the United Nations files under Oceania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not the country's largest city: the statistics division gives the capital about 413,000 people for 2019, where the World Bank's figure for the country's largest metropolitan area in the same year is 1,582,028, nearly four times as many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That larger series rises to 1,711,127 by 2025, so the gap between the capital and the biggest city widens rather than closes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two bodies agree closely on the country's size, the division giving 268,107 km² against the World Bank's 267,710.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The division's national population of about 5.252 million for 2025 is a projection and sits below the World Bank's 5,324,700, and its own footnote says the New Zealand data leave out the Cook Islands, Niue and Tokelau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> New Zealand became a self-governing Dominion of the British Empire on 26 September 1907, sovereign in its domestic affairs while the United Kingdom kept control of its relations with the wider world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Balfour Declaration of 1926 called the United Kingdom and the Dominions autonomous communities equal in status and subordinate to one another in nothing, and the Statute of Westminster codified that in December 1931, though its provisions were not to take effect until adopted by New Zealand's own government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised New Zealand as an independent state with autonomous control over its foreign relations on 16 February 1942, when Walter Nash presented his credentials to President Franklin Roosevelt in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American legation was established in Wellington on 1 April 1942 under Patrick Hurley, and both legations were raised to embassies in 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> New Zealand's own Statute of Westminster Adoption Act became law only on 25 November 1947, more than five years after the United States had recognised it as running its own foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “New Zealand,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nz.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: New Zealand,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NZL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: New Zealand,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NZL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: New Zealand,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NZL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: New Zealand,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/new-zealand. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Wellington_%286793348687%29.jpg","title":"Wellington on its harbour","desc":"The centre of Wellington along its harbour, with wharves and a marina in front and steep green hills rising close behind the city.","credit":"Photograph by itravelNZ, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wellington_%286793348687%29.jpg","alt":"A compact city of towers along a blue harbour, with moored yachts in the foreground and wooded hills behind."}},
{"id":"gw-625","num":625,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Muscat","answerText":"Muscat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1833</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 October 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 April 1972</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1972</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Oman","dot":"Muscat"},"facts":[["Country","Oman"],["Population","1.50M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+104.4% (2005–2019)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","oman"],"abstract":"<b>Muscat</b> is the capital of Oman, the sultanate that occupies the south-eastern corner of the Arabian Peninsula, and which the United Nations files under Western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city stands on the Gulf of Oman along a stretch of coast where the bare ridges of the Hajar mountains run down almost to the water, so its older quarters sit in pockets between rock and sea rather than spreading inland. The modern capital is consequently a long coastal conurbation rather than a single compact centre, and the figure usually quoted for its size is a regional one. The United Nations puts about 1.50 million people there in 2019 and notes in the same line that the count refers to the governorate of Muscat rather than to any smaller settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for Oman's largest urban agglomeration gives 1,501,635 for that year, the same number to the person, and carries it to roughly 1.70 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Oman covers about 309,980 square kilometres (119,700 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 309,500 by the World Bank's, a difference of well under one per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is put at about 5.49 million for 2025, against roughly 4.18 million a decade earlier, though the series does not climb smoothly: it fell between 2018 and 2021 before rising sharply again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same national profile records 166 men for every 100 women, a ratio far from the near-parity most countries report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American recognition of Oman is unusually early, a treaty of amity and commerce having been signed at Muscat on 21 September 1833 by a United States special agent and Sultan Sayyid Said bin Sultan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A consulate followed at Muscat in 1880 and lasted until 1915, after which American interests in Oman were handled by diplomats posted in other countries, and formal diplomatic relations began only on 17 April 1972, with an embassy opening at Muscat that July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Oman,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/om.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Oman,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/OMN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Oman,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/OMN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Oman,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/OMN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Oman,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/oman. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Muscat_%2852666089866%29.jpg/1920px-Muscat_%2852666089866%29.jpg","title":"Old Muscat between the Hajar mountains and the Gulf of Oman","desc":"The white quarter of Old Muscat seen from the hills behind it in 2022, hemmed between bare ridges and the sea.","credit":"Christopher Harriot, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muscat_%2852666089866%29.jpg","alt":"A low white town beside blue sea, seen across ranks of bare brown mountain ridges."}},
{"id":"gw-626","num":626,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nouakchott","answerText":"Nouakchott","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 November 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 December 1969</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mauritania","dot":"Nouakchott"},"facts":[["Country","Mauritania"],["Population","1.26M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+51.0% (2013–2023)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","mauritania"],"abstract":"<b>Nouakchott</b> is the capital of Mauritania, a country the United Nations files under Western Africa, and it stands on the Atlantic coast a few kilometres inland from the shore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site is flat sand at the edge of the Sahara, with no river, no harbour and no high ground, which is why the city has been able to spread outward in every direction as fast as people have arrived. It is one of the fastest-growing capitals in the deck: the World Bank's series for Mauritania's largest urban agglomeration runs from 1,057,796 in 2015 to 1,612,940 in 2025, a rise of more than half in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations gives 1,259.0 thousand, and its footnote dates that figure to 2019 rather than to the 2025 heading of the column it sits in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read against the World Bank's 1,258,973 for 2019 the two agree almost to the person, but a reader who took the heading at face value would be understating the city by more than a quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Mauritania itself covers 1,030,700 square kilometres (398,000 square miles), a figure the United Nations and the World Bank give identically rather than merely closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is put at about 5.32 million for 2025 against 3.97 million a decade earlier, climbing steadily throughout with no dip anywhere in the series.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 27 October 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States had recognised it nearly a year before, on 28 November 1960, and established diplomatic relations the same day, though the first American ambassadors to Mauritania were accredited to Senegal as well and lived in Dakar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An embassy opened at Nouakchott on 14 July 1962; Mauritania severed relations after the Arab-Israeli war of 1967, the two governments resumed them by joint communique on 22 December 1969, and the embassy reopened the following March.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mauritania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Mauritania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MRT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Mauritania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MRT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mauritania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MRT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mauritania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mauritania. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Nouakchott.jpg","title":"Nouakchott from the air, 2017","desc":"The city running flat to the horizon over sand, with a palm grove left inside it.","credit":"Laminesall96, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nouakchott.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a low, dense, sand-coloured city stretching unbroken to a flat horizon, with a dark palm grove at its centre."}},
{"id":"gw-627","num":627,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"San José","answerText":"San José","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence notified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 March 1851</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resident minister</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 January 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Costa Rica","dot":"San José"},"facts":[["Country","Costa Rica"],["Population","1.38M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","costa rica"],"abstract":"<b>San José</b> is the capital of Costa Rica, a country the United Nations files under Central America, and it lies in the interior highlands rather than on either of the country's two coasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure usually quoted for its size is a composite one, and unusually the United Nations says exactly what it is composed of: 1,378.5 thousand people, in a row footnoted both as referring to the urban population of cantons and as dating from 2019 rather than from the heading of the column it sits under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 1,378,546 for that same year, which is the two agreeing to within fifty people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That series reaches about 1.50 million by 2025, a rise of roughly a sixth across the decade. Beside capitals such as Nouakchott, which grew by more than half over the same ten years, San José is a slow-growing city in a slow-growing country. <br><br> Costa Rica covers 51,100 square kilometres (19,700 square miles), a figure the United Nations and the World Bank publish identically rather than merely closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is put at about 5.15 million for 2025 against 4.82 million a decade earlier, a rise of some seven per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 2 November 1945, in its first weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Costa Rica notified the United States of its independence on 5 September 1848, and the following April an American charge d'affaires resident in Guatemala was given powers to deal with it; relations were formally established on 24 March 1851, when the Costa Rican minister presented his credentials in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No American minister actually lived in San José for another forty-seven years, the first resident one presenting his credentials there on 15 January 1898.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Costa Rica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Costa Rica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CRI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Costa Rica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CRI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Costa Rica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CRI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Costa Rica,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/costa-rica. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Aerial_Central_Valley_Sabana_CRI_01_2020_2184.jpg/1920px-Aerial_Central_Valley_Sabana_CRI_01_2020_2184.jpg","title":"San José filling the Central Valley, 2020","desc":"The city seen from the air across the valley floor, with the mountains that enclose it behind.","credit":"Mario Roberto Duran Ortiz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_Central_Valley_Sabana_CRI_01_2020_2184.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a wide city covering a valley floor, with green mountain ridges rising behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-628","num":628,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kuwait City","answerText":"Kuwait City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 June 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 September 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 May 1963</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 March 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kuwait","dot":"Kuwait City"},"facts":[["Country","Kuwait"],["Population","3.05M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","kuwait"],"abstract":"<b>Kuwait City</b> is the capital of Kuwait, a state at the head of the Persian Gulf which the United Nations files under Western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population figure quoted for it is a metropolitan one, and the United Nations names its parts: 3,052.5 thousand people across the governorates of Capital, Hawalli, Al-Farwaniya and Mubarak Al-Kabeer, in a row its footnotes also date to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 3,052,493 for that year, and carries the figure to about 3.40 million by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is unusual is the share: Kuwait held some 4.44 million people in 2019, so the four governorates counted as the capital accounted for close to seven in every ten of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Very little of the country lies outside that agglomeration, and what does is largely desert. <br><br> Kuwait covers 17,818 square kilometres (6,880 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 17,820 by the World Bank's, a difference of two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its measured population rose from about 3.83 million in 2015 to roughly 4.87 million in 2025, but not evenly: the series falls between 2019 and 2021, climbs steeply to 2024 and slips again in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations projects a higher 5.03 million for 2025, some three per cent above the measured series, which is a reminder that its headline population is an estimate rather than a count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same profile records 156.9 men for every 100 women, a ratio matched in this deck only by other Gulf states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States opened a consulate at Kuwait on 27 June 1951, a decade before the exchange of notes with the United Kingdom that made Kuwait fully independent on 19 June 1961, and diplomatic relations followed that September; the embassy was evacuated on 13 December 1990 during the Iraqi occupation and reopened on 1 March 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kuwait,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Kuwait,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KWT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Kuwait,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KWT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kuwait,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KWT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kuwait,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kuwait. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Kuwait_City_skyline_%2839710595225%29.jpg/1920px-Kuwait_City_skyline_%2839710595225%29.jpg","title":"Kuwait City across the bay, 2018","desc":"The capital's towers and low blocks running the width of the shore.","credit":"Francisco Anzola, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuwait_City_skyline_%2839710595225%29.jpg","alt":"A city of towers and low blocks stretching along a flat shoreline, seen across calm blue water."}},
{"id":"gw-629","num":629,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Panama City","answerText":"Panama City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 November 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 December 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 November 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Panama","dot":"Panama City"},"facts":[["Country","Panama"],["Population","1.82M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","panama"],"abstract":"<b>Panama City</b> is the capital of Panama, at the Pacific end of the canal that crosses the country, in a region the United Nations files as Central America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 1,821.7 thousand in a row footnoted as referring to the metropolitan area of Panama City and dated to 2019, which is the least specific of the definitions this deck has met: it names the concept without naming the districts it is built from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 1,821,693 for that year and about 2.05 million for 2025, a rise of roughly a quarter across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Panama held some 4.23 million people in 2019, so the metropolitan area accounted for about two in every five of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is a high share for a country of this size, though well short of Kuwait's seven in ten. <br><br> Panama covers 75,320 square kilometres (29,080 square miles), a figure the United Nations and the World Bank publish identically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their populations agree as closely: the United Nations projects 4,571 thousand for 2025 and the measured series gives 4,571,189, a match to the thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 13 November 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An American commercial agent was appointed at Panama in 1823, eighty years before there was a Panama for him to be posted to, the isthmus then being part of Colombia; the United States recognised the new republic on 6 November 1903, three days after it declared independence, and exchanged ministers that December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The relationship is the most repeatedly broken in this deck, interrupted and restored in 1931, 1949, 1964 and 1968, with the American ambassador recalled again in 1989.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Panama,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pa.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Panama,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PAN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Panama,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PAN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Panama,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PAN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Panama,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/panama. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Ciudad_de_Panam%C3%A1_-_Panam%C3%A1.jpg/1920px-Ciudad_de_Panam%C3%A1_-_Panam%C3%A1.jpg","title":"Panama City along the bay, 2020","desc":"The tower district and coast road seen from the air, with the marina in the foreground.","credit":"Neoredacturus, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_de_Panam%C3%A1_-_Panam%C3%A1.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a dense district of glass towers along a curving bay, with a marina and coast road below."}},
{"id":"gw-634","num":634,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Montevideo","answerText":"Montevideo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 January 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 July 1870</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1945</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Uruguay","dot":"Montevideo"},"facts":[["Country","Uruguay"],["Population","1.74M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-1.2% (2011–2023)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","uruguay"],"abstract":"<b>Montevideo</b> is the capital of Uruguay, on the north bank of the Río de la Plata, in a region the United Nations files as South America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population figure is the most precisely delimited in this deck: the United Nations footnotes it as covering the department of Montevideo together with two named localities in two neighbouring departments, and dates it to 2019 rather than to the heading of the column it appears under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure is 1,744.7 thousand, which the World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration matches at 1,744,700 for that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Uruguay held about 3.40 million people in 2019, so slightly more than half the country lived inside that boundary — a share between Kuwait’s seven in ten and Panama’s two in five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city is also nearly static: the same series runs from 1,707,475 in 2015 to 1,788,172 in 2025, a rise of under five per cent across the decade, where Nouakchott grew by more than half over the same years and Panama City by a quarter. <br><br> The country's area is given as 173,626 square kilometres (67,030 square miles) by the United Nations and 176,220 by the World Bank, about one and a half per cent apart, which breaks a run of three cards on which the two agreed exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is not merely slow but falling: the measured series peaks at 3,398,968 in 2020 and declines to 3,384,688 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Uruguay joined the United Nations on 18 December 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American recognition, on 25 January 1836, took an unusual form: it was performed in the United States rather than in Uruguay, by the issuing of an exequatur to a Uruguayan consul general at New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 2 October 1867, with the American minister resident in Buenos Aires and accredited to Argentina as well, and a legation of its own opened at Montevideo only in July 1870.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Uruguay,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/uy.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Uruguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/URY/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Uruguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/URY/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Uruguay,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/URY/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Uruguay,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/uruguay. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Montevideo_desde_arriba.jpg/1920px-Montevideo_desde_arriba.jpg","title":"Montevideo from the air, 2017","desc":"The city spreading north from the Río de la Plata, seen from above.","credit":"Marcelo Campi, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montevideo_desde_arriba.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a low, green, closely built city running back from a distant shoreline."}},
{"id":"gw-635","num":635,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"San Juan","answerText":"San Juan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded by Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 1898</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 February 1899</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Puerto Rico","dot":"San Juan"},"facts":[["Country","Puerto Rico"],["Population","2.45M (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","puerto rico"],"abstract":"<b>San Juan</b> is the capital of Puerto Rico, a Caribbean territory of the United States, and stands on the island's north coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations profiles it separately from the United States and says so outright, footnoting the territory's figures with the note that American data do not include this area; the same profile carries no United Nations membership date at all, because Puerto Rico is not a member state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital figure is 2,451.4 thousand for 2019, and the footnote names a unit belonging to the sovereign state rather than to the island: the Metropolitan Statistical Area, a category defined by the United States census.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the territory's largest urban agglomeration gives 2,451,375 for that year, matching to within twenty-five people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is close to three quarters of everybody on the island, which is the highest share any capital reaches in this deck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Puerto Rico covers 8,868 square kilometres (3,424 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 8,870 by the World Bank's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What makes the island unusual in this deck is that both its populations are shrinking, and shrinking in every single year: the urban series falls from 2,463,243 in 2015 to 2,433,680 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territory's own population falls faster still, from 3,535,167 to 3,184,835 across the same decade, a loss of about a tenth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The profile also records 88.8 men for every 100 women, the most female-weighted ratio this deck has met, where Kuwait and Oman run at over 150 the other way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spain ceded the island to the United States by the treaty signed at Paris on 10 December 1898, which the American Senate ratified the following February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Puerto Rico,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Puerto Rico,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Puerto Rico,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Puerto Rico,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PRI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Spanish-American War, 1898,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1866-1898/spanish-american-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Aerial_View_of_Condado_San_Juan.jpg/1920px-Aerial_View_of_Condado_San_Juan.jpg","title":"San Juan from the air, 2022","desc":"The Condado shore and lagoon with the city running inland behind them.","credit":"Coolcaesar, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_View_of_Condado_San_Juan.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a dense coastal city with a beach and lagoon in front and low buildings spreading inland."}},
{"id":"gw-636","num":636,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Sarajevo","answerText":"Sarajevo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bosnia and Herz.","dot":"Sarajevo"},"facts":[["Country","Bosnia and Herzegovina"],["Population","343k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+2.7% (2013–2022)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","bosnia and herzegovina"],"abstract":"<b>Sarajevo</b> is the capital of Bosnia and Herzegovina, a country the United Nations files under Southern Europe, and it lies inland in the country's eastern half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population figure is both small and closely bounded: 342.7 thousand for 2019, in a row footnoted as covering five municipalities, each of them named individually rather than gathered under a regional label.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 342,743 for that year, which is the two agreeing to within fifty people again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bosnia and Herzegovina held about 3.35 million people that year, so the capital accounted for roughly one in ten of them — the smallest share of any capital in this stretch of the deck, where San Juan reaches three quarters and Kuwait City seven in ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city is also almost perfectly static, rising only from 342,373 in 2015 to 348,404 in 2025, under two per cent across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The country covers 51,209 square kilometres (19,772 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 51,210 by the World Bank's, one square kilometre apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population, unlike its capital's, is falling steeply: from 3,518,541 in 2015 to 3,140,095 in 2025, a loss of about an eighth in ten years, so Sarajevo has held its numbers while the country around it emptied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bosnia and Herzegovina joined the United Nations on 22 May 1992, a date that falls between the two American ones: recognition came on 7 April 1992 in a White House statement, and diplomatic relations on 6 August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An ambassador presented his credentials in June 1993, but the embassy worked out of Vienna, in another country, from that November onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American embassy was established at Sarajevo itself only on 4 July 1994, with the same ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bosnia and Herzegovina,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ba.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BIH/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BIH/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BIH/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bosnia-herzegovina. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Panorama_of_Sarajevo_in_2016.png/1280px-Panorama_of_Sarajevo_in_2016.png","title":"Sarajevo in its valley, winter 2016","desc":"The city under snow, filling the valley floor between the ridges that enclose it.","credit":"Tomica, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_of_Sarajevo_in_2016.png","alt":"A snow-covered city filling a narrow valley, with wooded ridges rising on both sides."}},
{"id":"gw-638","num":638,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Windhoek","answerText":"Windhoek","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 March 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 March 1990</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1990</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Namibia","dot":"Windhoek"},"facts":[["Country","Namibia"],["Population","417k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+23.1% (2016–2023)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","namibia"],"abstract":"<b>Windhoek</b> is the capital of Namibia, a country the United Nations files under Southern Africa, and it stands inland in the country's centre rather than on either of its coasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 417.4 thousand, in a row carrying only a date and no definition at all: the footnote says 2019 and says nothing about what the figure counts, where the profiles of its neighbours in this deck name governorates, cantons or municipalities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 417,380 for that year, so the two agree even without agreeing on what has been measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city is growing quickly, from 367,802 in 2015 to 511,369 in 2025, close to two fifths in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so it held only about one Namibian in six in 2019, the country's people being spread thinly across it rather than gathered into the capital as they are in Kuwait or Puerto Rico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Namibia covers 825,229 square kilometres (318,600 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 824,290 by the World Bank's, about a tenth of a per cent apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across that expanse the same profile records a density of 3.8 people per square kilometre, which is among the lowest of any country in this deck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is nonetheless rising fast, from 2,374,300 in 2015 to 3,092,816 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Namibia joined the United Nations on 23 April 1990, about a month after becoming independent of South African administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American recognition, diplomatic relations and an embassy all began on one day, 21 March 1990, because a United States liaison office already stood at Windhoek under South African administration and was simply raised to embassy rank on the day the country became independent, so recognition, relations and a resident mission all arrived together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Namibia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/na.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Namibia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NAM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Namibia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NAM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Namibia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NAM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Namibia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/namibia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Windhoek_Luftaufnahme.jpg/1920px-Windhoek_Luftaufnahme.jpg","title":"Windhoek from the air, 2016","desc":"The city spread along its valley with dry hill country beginning at its edge.","credit":"Hp.Baumeler, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Windhoek_Luftaufnahme.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a low city following a valley, with bare brown hills and open scrub around it."}},
{"id":"gw-641","num":641,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kingston","answerText":"Kingston","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Jamaica","dot":"Kingston"},"facts":[["Country","Jamaica"],["Population","590k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","jamaica"],"abstract":"<b>Kingston</b> is the capital of Jamaica, on the island's south coast, in a region the United Nations files as the Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 589.8 thousand in a row whose only footnote is a date, 2019, with no statement of what the figure covers — the second capital in a row on which the profile declines to say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 589,849 for that year, agreeing to within fifty people all the same, so the two are counting the same thing even where only one of them will name it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has barely moved since, reaching 603,770 by 2025, under three per cent across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It held about one Jamaican in five in 2019, a middling share beside Kuwait City's seven in ten and Sarajevo's one in ten, so about four Jamaicans in five live somewhere other than the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Jamaica covers 10,990 square kilometres (4,243 square miles), a figure the United Nations and the World Bank publish identically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country's population is as static as its capital's, rising only from 2,802,534 in 2015 to 2,837,077 in 2025 and slipping slightly after a peak in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jamaica became independent on 6 August 1962, remaining in the Commonwealth, and joined the United Nations that September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised it ten days after independence by the act of opening an embassy at Kingston, so recognition and the post were one event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> American consuls had served on the island long before that, but in the ports rather than the capital — the earliest at Montego Bay and Falmouth from 1861 — and every one of those posts had been closed by September 1919, leaving no American presence at all for the forty-three years before the embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Jamaica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Jamaica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JAM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Jamaica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JAM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Jamaica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JAM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Jamaica,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/jamaica. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gw-642","num":642,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Banjul","answerText":"Banjul","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 September 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gambia","dot":"Banjul"},"facts":[["Country","The Gambia"],["Population","443k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","-10.0% (2003–2013)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","gambia"],"abstract":"<b>Banjul</b> is the capital of The Gambia, a country the United Nations files under Western Africa, and it stands on the Atlantic coast at the western end of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure quoted for it is 443.4 thousand for 2019, and the footnote is worth reading carefully: it refers to the local government areas of Banjul and Kanifing, so only one of the two units counted is the capital itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 443,428 for that year, agreeing with the United Nations almost exactly, so the two are counting the same pair of local government areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That area has grown to about 510,000 by 2025, a rise of roughly a fifth across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It holds close to one Gambian in five, a middling share for this deck, well below Kuwait City's seven in ten and well above Sarajevo's one in ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Gambia covers 11,295 square kilometres (4,361 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 11,300 by the World Bank's, five square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is rising quickly, from 2,224,529 in 2015 to 2,822,093 in 2025, more than a quarter in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The whole of its diplomatic beginning with the United States falls inside eight months of 1965: recognition on 18 February, when an American representative attended the independence ceremonies in person at the capital, then still called Bathurst; relations on 9 August, with the ambassador accredited to Senegal as well and living in Dakar; and an embassy of its own on 9 September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Gambia joined the United Nations twelve days after that, on 21 September 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city has been renamed since, and the American record of the post still carries both forms, listing the embassy as having been established at Bathurst, now Banjul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Gambia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gm.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: The Gambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GMB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: The Gambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GMB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: The Gambia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GMB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Gambia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/gambia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Banjul-King-Fahad-Mosque-2007.jpg/1280px-Banjul-King-Fahad-Mosque-2007.jpg","title":"Banjul from above its rooftops","desc":"The low city spread out under corrugated roofs and trees, the twin minarets of its central mosque behind, and the flat estuary shore closing the horizon.","credit":"Photograph by Bjørn Christian Tørrissen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banjul-King-Fahad-Mosque-2007.jpg","alt":"A view over a low-built African city of rust-coloured tin roofs and trees, with two white minarets rising in the middle distance."}},
{"id":"gw-643","num":643,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Libreville","answerText":"Libreville","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 March 1961</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Gabon","dot":"Libreville"},"facts":[["Country","Gabon"],["Population","824k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","gabon"],"abstract":"<b>Libreville</b> is the capital of Gabon, on the Atlantic coast, in the region the United Nations files as Middle Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 823.9 thousand in a row whose only footnote is a date, 2019, with no statement of what the figure counts — the third capital in this deck running whose profile declines to say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 823,870 for that year and about 899,000 for 2025, a rise of roughly a fifth across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gabon held some 2.27 million people in 2019, so more than a third of them lived in the capital — a share close to Kuwait City's and far above the one in ten Sarajevo holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is a high share, and it sits beside a very low national density: the same profile gives 10.1 people to the square kilometre, so the country is at once thinly peopled overall and heavily concentrated in one city, where Namibia is thinly peopled and keeps only a sixth of its people in Windhoek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Gabon covers 267,668 square kilometres (103,347 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 267,670 by the World Bank's, two square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population is growing quickly, from 2,040,989 in 2015 to 2,593,130 in 2025, more than a quarter in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gabon joined the United Nations on 20 September 1960, a few weeks after the United States recognised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That recognition came on 17 August 1960, in a congratulatory message from the American president, and diplomatic relations began the same day — though the chargé who presented his credentials was stationed at Brazzaville, in the neighbouring country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An embassy at Libreville itself followed seven months later, on 20 March 1961, so for its first seven months the relationship was conducted from a post in another country, as Mauritania's and The Gambia's were from Dakar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Gabon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ga.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Gabon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GAB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Gabon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GAB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Gabon,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GAB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Gabon,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/gabon. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Libreville_frond_de_mer.jpg","title":"Libreville's seafront from the air, 2025","desc":"The coast boulevard with the city's buildings running along the shore of the estuary.","credit":"Shirmy25, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Libreville_frond_de_mer.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a coastal boulevard with tall buildings along it and open sea to the right."}},
{"id":"gw-644","num":644,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Gaborone","answerText":"Gaborone","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Botswana","dot":"Gaborone"},"facts":[["Country","Botswana"],["Population","269k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+6.3% (2011–2022)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","botswana"],"abstract":"<b>Gaborone</b> is the capital of Botswana, inland in the south-east of a country the United Nations files under Southern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 269.3 thousand, footnoted to 2018 and to nothing else — no statement of what the figure counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the one capital in this stretch of the deck whose figure cannot be checked against a second source at all: the World Bank publishes no series for Botswana's largest urban agglomeration, so where every neighbouring card can set two independent counts side by side, this one rests on the United Nations alone. That matters less than it might, because the number is small and the share it represents is easy to read: Botswana held about 2.30 million people in 2018, so roughly one person in nine lived in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country is also nearly empty by any measure, at 4.5 people to the square kilometre, close to Namibia's 3.8 and among the lowest figures any country in this deck reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its area is given as 581,730 square kilometres (224,600 square miles) by both the World Bank and the Commonwealth Secretariat, while the United Nations rounds the same figure to 582,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has risen from 2,203,273 in 2015 to 2,562,122 in 2025, about a sixth in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Botswana became independent of Britain on 30 September 1966, joined the Commonwealth in the same year, following that independence, and the United Nations on 17 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American recognition, diplomatic relations and an embassy all began on independence day itself, which makes this the second capital in this run where the three arrived together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The town was spelled Gaberones at the time, and the American record of the post still carries both forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Botswana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Botswana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BWA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Botswana,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/botswana. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Botswana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BWA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Botswana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/botswana. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/View_from_I-Tower_SE_20200712.jpg/1920px-View_from_I-Tower_SE_20200712.jpg","title":"Gaborone from its tallest tower, 2020","desc":"The central business district looking south-east, with dry hills and the city's reservoir beyond.","credit":"CNJerem, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_I-Tower_SE_20200712.jpg","alt":"A view over a low city of office blocks towards distant brown hills and a stretch of water."}},
{"id":"gw-646","num":646,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tirana","answerText":"Tirana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 December 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations severed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 June 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resumed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 March 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Albania","dot":"Tirana"},"facts":[["Country","Albania"],["Population","485k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-7.0% (2011–2023)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","albania"],"abstract":"<b>Tirana</b> is the capital of Albania, inland in the centre of a country the United Nations files under Southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 484.6 thousand in a row footnoted only with a year, 2019, and the World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 484,624 for that same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city grows in every year of that series, reaching 535,702 by 2025, a rise of about a fifth across the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country moves the other way, and steeply: its measured population falls in every year from 2,731,293 in 2015 to 2,349,580 in 2025, a loss of roughly a seventh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Tirana has therefore gained about a fifth of itself while Albania has lost about a seventh of itself, the sharpest such divergence in this collection. <br><br> The two agencies also disagree about how many Albanians there are, and by a wide margin: the United Nations projects 2,772 thousand for 2025 where the World Bank measures 2,349,580, some eighteen per cent apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What makes that worth stating is that the same United Nations profile agrees with the World Bank exactly about the capital, so its two population rows plainly do not come from the same place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On area they are as close as the deck ever gets, 28,748 square kilometres (11,100 square miles) against 28,750.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Albania joined the United Nations on 14 December 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised it in July 1922 and exchanged ministers that December, but relations were severed on 5 June 1939, when Albania's foreign minister reported that Italy had taken control of the country's foreign affairs, and were resumed only on 15 March 1991 — a break of fifty-two years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Albania,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/al.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Albania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ALB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Albania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ALB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Albania,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ALB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Albania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/albania. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Tirana_2023_Wide.jpg/1920px-Tirana_2023_Wide.jpg","title":"Tirana from the hills, 2023","desc":"The city on its plain with the park lake in front of it and mountains behind.","credit":"BBB2021, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tirana_2023_Wide.jpg","alt":"A wide view of a city on a plain, with a lake and wooded hills in front of it and mountains behind."}},
{"id":"gw-647","num":647,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Maseru","answerText":"Maseru","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 October 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Lesotho","dot":"Maseru"},"facts":[["Country","Lesotho"],["Population","202k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+50.8% (2006–2016)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","lesotho"],"abstract":"<b>Maseru</b> is the capital of Lesotho, on the country's western border, in the region the United Nations files as Southern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 201.9 thousand, footnoted to 2018 and to nothing else, with no statement of what the figure counts — the sixth capital in this run whose profile gives a date and declines to define the number under it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the second capital in four cards whose figure cannot be checked against a second source, because the World Bank publishes no series for Lesotho's largest urban agglomeration — as it publishes none for Botswana's — so the number rests on the United Nations alone. Lesotho held about 2.18 million people in 2018, so roughly one person in eleven lived in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is a small share, and the country is correspondingly rural: its people are spread at 77.8 to the square kilometre, which is high beside Botswana's 4.5 but sits in a state a twentieth of Botswana's size, so the comparison says more about the shape of the two countries than about how crowded either feels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three sources give that size within ten square kilometres of one another: 30,355 by the United Nations, 30,360 by the World Bank and 30,350 square kilometres (11,720 square miles) by the Commonwealth Secretariat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The population has risen from 2,104,611 in 2015 to 2,363,325 in 2025, about an eighth in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lesotho became independent of Britain on 4 October 1966 and joined the Commonwealth in that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American recognition, diplomatic relations and an embassy at Maseru all began on that same day, which makes this the third capital in this run where the three arrived together rather than in sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 17 October 1966, thirteen days after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Lesotho,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ls.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Lesotho,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LSO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Lesotho,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/lesotho. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Lesotho,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LSO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Lesotho,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/lesotho. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Maseru_from_Parliament_Hill.jpg/1920px-Maseru_from_Parliament_Hill.jpg","title":"Maseru from Parliament Hill, 2013","desc":"The capital seen across its rooftops, with the flat-topped hills beyond the border behind it.","credit":"OER Africa, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maseru_from_Parliament_Hill.jpg","alt":"A view over a low city of houses and small office blocks towards flat-topped hills in the distance."}},
{"id":"gw-648","num":648,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bissau","answerText":"Bissau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 September 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 June 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guinea-Bissau","dot":"Bissau"},"facts":[["Country","Guinea-Bissau"],["Population","579k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+24.3% (2010–2015)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","guinea-bissau"],"abstract":"<b>Bissau</b> is the capital of Guinea-Bissau, on the Atlantic coast of a country the United Nations files under Western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 578.8 thousand in a row footnoted only with a year, 2019, and with no statement of what the figure covers, while the World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 578,829 for that same year, so the two agree to within thirty people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city is growing very fast, reaching 709,092 by 2025 — more than two fifths added in a decade, a rate matched in this collection only by Nouakchott.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country grew by about a quarter over the same years, from 1,786,457 to 2,249,515, so the capital is pulling away from it rather than merely keeping pace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Close to three people in ten now live there, a share close to Gabon's and well above the one in ten Sarajevo and Maseru each hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Guinea-Bissau covers 36,125 square kilometres (13,948 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 36,130 by the World Bank's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 17 September 1974, a week after the United States recognised it in a letter from the American president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed nearly a year after that, on 3 September 1975, and were begun from the other side: it was Guinea-Bissau's ambassador who presented credentials in Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American embassy opened at Bissau on 30 June 1976 and suspended operations on 14 June 1998, at the start of the country's civil war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has not reopened: since February 2003 the American ambassador to Senegal has been accredited to Guinea-Bissau as well, so the relationship is conducted from Dakar, as Mauritania's and The Gambia's once were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guinea-Bissau,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gw.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Guinea-Bissau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Guinea-Bissau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guinea-Bissau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guinea-Bissau,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guinea-bissau. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Pra%C3%A7a_dos_Herois_Nacionais_%C3%A0_tarde.jpg/1280px-Pra%C3%A7a_dos_Herois_Nacionais_%C3%A0_tarde.jpg","title":"The Praça dos Heróis Nacionais, Bissau","desc":"The city's central square at dusk, its monument to the national heroes standing among planted beds with government buildings and low tiled roofs around it.","credit":"Photograph by Joehawkins, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pra%C3%A7a_dos_Herois_Nacionais_%C3%A0_tarde.jpg","alt":"A wide public square at sunset with a tall angular monument at its centre, lamp posts and planters around it and low buildings beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-649","num":649,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Ljubljana","answerText":"Ljubljana","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 May 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 August 1992</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Slovenia","dot":"Ljubljana"},"facts":[["Country","Slovenia"],["Population","287k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","0.0% (2019–2022)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","slovenia"],"abstract":"<b>Ljubljana</b> is the capital of Slovenia, inland in the centre of a country the United Nations files under Southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 286.5 thousand, footnoted to 2018 and to nothing else, with no statement of what the figure counts — the eighth capital in this run whose profile gives a year and defines nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the third whose figure has no second source to be checked against, the World Bank publishing no series for Slovenia's largest urban agglomeration. That is worth saying plainly, because the two before it were Botswana and Lesotho and this one is neither small nor African but a member of the European Union, so the gap has nothing to do with region or size. About one Slovene in seven lives in the capital, in a country whose population has barely moved, rising only from 2,063,531 in 2015 to 2,130,986 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Its area is where the sources part company: the United Nations and the European Union both give 20,273 square kilometres (7,827 square miles), while the World Bank gives 20,480 — and revised its own figure down from 20,675 in 2017, so even a series that looks constant across a decade is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On population they very nearly agree, the European Union's page giving 2,130,850 from its own statistical office against the World Bank's 2,130,986, a difference of about a hundred people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Slovenia joined the European Union on 1 May 2004 and the United Nations on 22 May 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 7 April 1992 in a White House statement and established relations on 6 August, which are the same two dates it gave Bosnia and Herzegovina, admitted to the United Nations on the same day as Slovenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The embassies are where the two part company: Ljubljana's opened nineteen days after relations began, where the mission to Bosnia worked out of Vienna until an embassy reached Sarajevo in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Slovenia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/si.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Slovenia,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/slovenia_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Slovenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Slovenia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SVN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Slovenia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/slovenia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bosnia and Herzegovina,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bosnia-herzegovina. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/City_from_above_%2850208524998%29.jpg/1920px-City_from_above_%2850208524998%29.jpg","title":"Ljubljana from the castle hill, 2020","desc":"The centre of the city with the mountains of the north behind it.","credit":"Julien Seguinot, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_from_above_%2850208524998%29.jpg","alt":"A view over a city of church domes and office blocks towards wooded hills and distant mountains."}},
{"id":"gw-650","num":650,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malabo","answerText":"Malabo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 October 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 March 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1995</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Eq. Guinea","dot":"Malabo"},"facts":[["Country","Equatorial Guinea"],["Population","297k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","+58.6% (2012–2018)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","equatorial guinea"],"abstract":"<b>Malabo</b> is the capital of Equatorial Guinea, a country the United Nations files under Middle Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 296.8 thousand, footnoted to 2018 and to nothing else — the ninth capital in this run whose profile supplies a year and defines nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Here the usual cross-check breaks down: the World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 375,896 for that same year, about a quarter more than the United Nations gives for the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two figures are not in conflict, because they are not describing the same place: that series follows whichever city in a country is the biggest, and where the biggest city is not the seat of government the two rows part company, exactly as they do for Switzerland, New Zealand and Qatar. The larger figure is also rising very fast, from 312,579 in 2015 to 512,750 in 2025, close to two thirds added in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Equatorial Guinea covers 28,052 square kilometres (10,831 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 28,050 by the World Bank's, two square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population has grown by about a third in a decade, from 1,453,923 to 1,938,431, and the same profile records 111.4 men for every 100 women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 12 November 1968, a fortnight after the United States recognised it by appointing its ambassador to Togo, resident in Lome, to serve here as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An American embassy opened in 1969 at Santa Isabel, as Malabo was then called, but relations were suspended in March 1976 after the American ambassador and consul were declared personae non gratae, and were resumed only in 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The embassy closed again on 31 October 1995, its work moving the next day to Yaounde in Cameroon, and no resident ambassador returned until 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Equatorial Guinea,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gq.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Equatorial Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNQ/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Equatorial Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNQ/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Equatorial Guinea,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GNQ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Equatorial Guinea,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/equatorial-guinea. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Panoramic_view_of_Malabo.jpg/1920px-Panoramic_view_of_Malabo.jpg","title":"Malabo at dusk, 2018","desc":"A wide view across the newer districts of the capital, with the sea beyond.","credit":"Amitsawant0812, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panoramic_view_of_Malabo.jpg","alt":"A wide dusk view over apartment blocks, roundabouts and wide roads, with open sea on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-651","num":651,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Riga","answerText":"Riga","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Latvia","dot":"Riga"},"facts":[["Country","Latvia"],["Population","634k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-3.5% (2023–2026)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","latvia"],"abstract":"<b>Riga</b> is the capital of Latvia, in the region the United Nations files as Northern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 633.8 thousand, footnoted to 2019 and to nothing else, and the World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 633,820 for that same year — the two agreeing to the person while only one of them will say what is being counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city is shrinking: that series falls in every year of the decade, from 645,815 in 2015 to 615,764 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country is shrinking faster, from 1,977,527 to 1,847,785 across the same years, falling in every year but one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same profile records 86.6 men for every 100 women, the most female-weighted ratio anywhere in this collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Latvia's area is given as 64,594 square kilometres (24,940 square miles) by both the United Nations and the European Union, and by the World Bank too until 2019, after which it prints 64,590 instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is the second area series in three cards to be revised inside the same ten-year window, which is worth noticing about a figure that looks like a constant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Latvia joined the European Union on 1 May 2004 and the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised it much earlier, on 28 July 1922 — the same day it recognised Albania — and opened a legation at Riga that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That legation closed on 5 September 1940 after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the country, but the United States held that diplomatic relations continued uninterrupted throughout, and an embassy returned to Riga in October 1991; where Albania's relations were severed for fifty-two years, Latvia's post was gone for fifty-one while the relationship was maintained never to have lapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Latvia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lv.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Latvia,” EU Countries, https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/latvia_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Latvia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LVA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Latvia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LVA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Latvia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LVA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Latvia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/latvia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Albania,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/albania. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Old_Riga_panorama.jpg/1920px-Old_Riga_panorama.jpg","title":"Riga across the Daugava, 2022","desc":"The spires and roofs of the old city seen from the far bank of the river.","credit":"Kārlis Kalviškis, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Riga_panorama.jpg","alt":"A city of church spires and tiled roofs seen across a wide river, with a road bridge in front of it."}},
{"id":"gw-652","num":652,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Skopje","answerText":"Skopje","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 April 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Liaison office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 December 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 February 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 July 1996</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"North Macedonia","dot":"Skopje"},"facts":[["Country","North Macedonia"],["Population","590k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+49.2% (2002–2021)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","north macedonia"],"abstract":"<b>Skopje</b> is the capital of North Macedonia, in the region the United Nations files as Southern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 589.8 thousand, footnoted to 2019 and to nothing else, and the World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 589,751 for that same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has grown in every year of the decade, reaching 620,825 by 2025, about a ninth more than it held in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country has moved the other way in every year, from 1,912,430 in 2015 to 1,820,909 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So close to a third of all North Macedonians now live in the capital, and this is the third country in quick succession here whose capital rises while the state around it falls, after Albania and Bosnia and Herzegovina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country covers 25,713 square kilometres (9,928 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 25,710 by the World Bank's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 8 April 1993, which is more than ten months before the United States recognised it, and it was admitted under a provisional designation rather than under the name it wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Americans opened a liaison office at Skopje in December 1993, recognised the country as the Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia on 9 February 1994, established diplomatic relations in September 1995 and sent a first ambassador in July 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name then changed twice more in their own record: to the Republic of Macedonia in 2004, and to the Republic of North Macedonia in February 2019, after the Prespa Accord signed with Greece the previous June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three capitals earlier in this collection were renamed after independence; here it is the country itself that was, and its name was the thing being negotiated.","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “North Macedonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mk.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: North Macedonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MKD/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: North Macedonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MKD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: North Macedonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MKD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: North Macedonia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/macedonia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Noviji_dio_Skoplja_2019.jpg/1920px-Noviji_dio_Skoplja_2019.jpg","title":"Skopje across the Vardar, 2019","desc":"The newer districts of the capital seen from above the river, with the mountains behind.","credit":"August Dominus, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noviji_dio_Skoplja_2019.jpg","alt":"A view over a city on a river plain, with tree-lined embankments in front and mountains behind."}},
{"id":"gw-653","num":653,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Pristina","answerText":"Pristina","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 February 2008</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 2008</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foreign ministry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 June 2008</span><span class=\"dt-k\">ICJ opinion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 July 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kosovo","dot":"Pristina"},"facts":[["Country","Kosovo"],["Population","227k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+14.4% (2011–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","kosovo"],"abstract":"<b>Pristina</b> is the capital of Kosovo, which declared its independence from Serbia on 17 February 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only capital in this collection whose card gives no population figure, and the reason is that none of the sources this deck relies on publishes one: the World Bank's series for a country's largest urban agglomeration returns nothing for Kosovo, and so does its series for surface area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What that source does carry is the country's own population, and it is falling steeply — 1,788,274 in 2015 against 1,576,876 in 2025, about an eighth gone, with the sharpest drop after 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rather than borrow a figure for the city from somewhere a reader could not check, the card leaves its size unstated. The gaps follow from Kosovo's standing rather than from any lack of interest in it: the territory has been administered under a United Nations Security Council resolution since 1999, which suspended Belgrade's governance and placed it under an interim United Nations administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The United States recognised Kosovo's independence and agreed to establish diplomatic relations on 18 February 2008, the day after the declaration, in a press statement from the Secretary of State.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Kosovo passed legislation to create a foreign ministry the following month, and it took effect on 15 June 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 2010 the International Court of Justice, asked whether the declaration had been lawful, advised that the declaration of independence adopted on 17 February 2008 did not violate international law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Recognition is nonetheless not universal, and the American record describes it as coming from a robust majority of European states together with the United States, Japan, Canada and others in the Americas, Africa and Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The European Commission lists Kosovo among the potential candidates for membership of the European Union.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kosovo,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kosovo. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Kosovo,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/XKX/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Kosovo,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/XKX/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kosovo,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/XKX/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1244 (1999), adopted at its 4011th meeting, on 10 June 1999,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/274488. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “Accordance with International Law of the Unilateral Declaration of Independence in Respect of Kosovo,” Advisory Opinion of 22 July 2010, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/141. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Enlargement and Eastern Neighbourhood, “Kosovo,” https://enlargement.ec.europa.eu/kosovo_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/DJI_20250107162046_0009_D_v1.JPG/1920px-DJI_20250107162046_0009_D_v1.JPG","title":"Pristina from the air, 2025","desc":"Apartment blocks and new building running back towards the hills behind the city.","credit":"Arianit Dobroshi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DJI_20250107162046_0009_D_v1.JPG","alt":"An aerial view of a dense city of apartment blocks with a roundabout in the foreground and hills behind."}},
{"id":"gw-654","num":654,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Manama","answerText":"Manama","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resident ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 June 1974</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bahrain","dot":"Manama"},"facts":[["Country","Bahrain"],["Population","600k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+12.1% (1991–2001)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","bahrain"],"abstract":"<b>Manama</b> is the capital of Bahrain, in the region the United Nations files as Western Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 600.4 thousand for 2019, and the footnote is unusually exact about what that covers: the urban area of the municipality of Al-Manamah, which is a part of a named unit rather than the unit itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 600,433 for the same year, and it then grows faster than any other city series in this collection, reaching 743,066 by 2025 — about two thirds added in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bahrain is also the most crowded country the deck has met, with 2,098.8 people to the square kilometre against Namibia's 3.8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same profile records 163.4 men for every 100 women, the Gulf pattern already seen in Oman and Kuwait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its area is the strangest series here: the World Bank's figure rises almost every year, from 778 square kilometres (300 square miles) in 2015 to 800 in 2023, so this is not a number being revised but one that keeps moving in the same direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations gives 778 for 2023, which is exactly the World Bank's figure for 2015, so the two sources are eight years apart on a quantity that has not stood still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its population has risen from 1,370,322 in 2015 to 1,600,366 in 2025, though not smoothly: the series dips between 2018 and 2020 before climbing again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bahrain joined the United Nations on 21 September 1971, the same day an American embassy opened at Manama, the United States having recognised the state a month earlier in a press release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The first American ambassador presented credentials in February 1972 but lived at Kuwait, and no ambassador was resident at Manama until June 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bahrain,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bh.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Bahrain,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Bahrain,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bahrain,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Bahrain,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bahrain. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Manama_Cityline.jpg/1920px-Manama_Cityline.jpg","title":"The towers of Manama, 2013","desc":"The capital's tower district seen across open ground at its edge.","credit":"Shmyg, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manama_Cityline.jpg","alt":"A line of glass and concrete towers under a clear sky, seen across flat open ground."}},
{"id":"gw-655","num":655,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Dili","answerText":"Dili","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN administration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 October 1999</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 May 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 September 2002</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 2002</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Timor-Leste","dot":"Dili"},"facts":[["Country","Timor-Leste"],["Population","281k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+14.9% (2010–2015)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","timor-leste"],"abstract":"<b>Dili</b> is the capital of Timor-Leste, in the region the United Nations files as South-eastern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 281.1 thousand, footnoted to 2018 and to nothing else, with no statement of what the figure counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the fourth capital in this stretch with no second source to check that figure against, the World Bank publishing no series at all for the country's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The four are worth naming together, because they refute the obvious explanation: Botswana, Lesotho, Slovenia and Timor-Leste are a southern African state, another beside it, a member of the European Union and a South-East Asian one, so the gap follows neither region nor size nor wealth. About one Timorese in five lives in the capital, in a country whose population has risen steadily from 1,205,131 in 2015 to 1,418,517 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Timor-Leste covers 14,919 square kilometres (5,760 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 14,870 by the World Bank's, about a third of one per cent apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The territory was placed under a United Nations transitional administration in October 1999, which governed it until independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That came on 20 May 2002, and American recognition, diplomatic relations and an embassy at Dili all date from that same day — the fourth capital in this collection where the three arrived together rather than in sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A first American ambassador presented his credentials the following December, and the country joined the United Nations on 27 September 2002, one of the most recent admissions any card here records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The guide describes the country as “then known as East Timor”, so this is the second state in a few cards to carry more than one name in the American record, after North Macedonia's three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Timor-Leste,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tl.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Timor-Leste,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TLS/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Timor-Leste,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TLS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Timor-Leste,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TLS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations Security Council, “Resolution 1272 (1999), adopted at its 4057th meeting, on 25 October 1999,” United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/291410. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Timor-Leste,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/timor-leste. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Dili_municipality.jpg/1280px-Dili_municipality.jpg","title":"Dili from the hills behind it","desc":"The city on its coastal plain seen from high ground, running along the shore with the sea beyond and the mountains of Atauro Island on the horizon.","credit":"Photograph by Diamantino Soares, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dili_municipality.jpg","alt":"A low coastal city among trees seen from a wooded hillside, with a wide blue sea behind it and a mountainous island on the far horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-656","num":656,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tallinn","answerText":"Tallinn","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Estonia","dot":"Tallinn"},"facts":[["Country","Estonia"],["Population","441k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+4.4% (2021–2024)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","estonia"],"abstract":"<b>Tallinn</b> is the capital of Estonia, in the region the United Nations files as Northern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is given as 441.3 thousand, footnoted to 2019 and to nothing else, and the World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 441,321 for that same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has grown in every year of the decade, reaching 457,657 by 2025, about a tenth more than it held in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Estonia's own population has risen too, from 1,315,407 to 1,366,475 across the same years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is what separates it from Latvia next door, where the capital and the country are both shrinking, although the two states are almost equally thinly settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Estonia covers 45,261 square kilometres (17,475 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 45,340 by the World Bank's, about a sixth of one per cent apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991, the same day as Latvia, and the parallels run much further back than that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised Estonia on 28 July 1922 — the same day, through the same commissioner at Riga, as it recognised Latvia and Albania — and relations began that day, though a legation of its own reached Tallinn only in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That legation was closed on 5 September 1940, the same day as the one at Riga, after the Soviet Union occupied and annexed the Baltic states; the United States maintained that diplomatic relations continued uninterrupted and never recognised the incorporation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It recognised the restoration of independence on 2 September 1991, resumed relations two days later, and opened an embassy at Tallinn on 2 October 1991 — again the same day as the one at Riga.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Estonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ee.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Estonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/EST/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Estonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/EST/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Latvia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LVA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Estonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/EST/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Estonia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/estonia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Latvia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/latvia. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Aerial_view_of_Tallinn.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_of_Tallinn.jpg","title":"Tallinn from the air, 2018","desc":"The red roofs and spires of the old town at the centre, with the modern city around it and the Gulf of Finland beyond.","credit":"Ad Meskens, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Tallinn.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a coastal city with a compact old town of red roofs and church spires at its centre."}},
{"id":"gw-657","num":657,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Port-of-Spain","answerText":"Port-of-Spain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 August 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1962</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Trinidad and Tobago","dot":"Port-of-Spain"},"facts":[["Country","Trinidad and Tobago"],["Population","544k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","3rd largest"],["Population growth","-24.4% (2000–2011)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","trinidad and tobago"],"abstract":"<b>Port-of-Spain</b> is the capital of Trinidad and Tobago, in the region the United Nations files as the Caribbean, and the sources cannot even agree on how to spell it: the American recognition guide hyphenates the name and the United Nations does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its population is given as 544.3 thousand for 2019, in a row footnoted only as referring to the urban agglomeration, which names a concept rather than any district or borough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the country's largest urban agglomeration gives 544,259 for that same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That series is the flattest this collection has met: it falls very slightly to 2020 and then recovers, ending at 546,564 in 2025, a change of under a third of one per cent across a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About two people in five in the country live there, which is a high share for a state whose population is spread across two islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Trinidad and Tobago covers 5,127 square kilometres (1,980 square miles) by the United Nations' figure and 5,130 by the World Bank's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On population the two part company sharply: the United Nations projects 1,511 thousand for 2025 where the World Bank measures 1,367,764, about a tenth apart, and the measured series is itself nearly flat across the decade, rising only from 1,349,667 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country became independent on 31 August 1962, and American recognition, diplomatic relations and an embassy at Port-of-Spain all date from that same day, the fifth capital in this collection where the three arrived together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A first ambassador presented his credentials that December, three months after the flag changed and the embassy opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 18 September 1962, the same day as Jamaica, which had become independent three weeks earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Trinidad and Tobago,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tt.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Trinidad and Tobago,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TTO/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Trinidad and Tobago,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TTO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Trinidad and Tobago,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TTO/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Trinidad and Tobago,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/trinidad-and-tobago. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Jamaica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jm.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/POS_panorama.JPG","title":"Port-of-Spain from the hills, 2008","desc":"The downtown towers and harbour with the residential city in front of them and the Gulf of Paria behind.","credit":"Christianwelsh, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POS_panorama.JPG","alt":"A dense city of low coloured roofs with a cluster of tall towers behind it and open water beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-658","num":658,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nicosia","answerText":"Nicosia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":["Cyprus","N. Cyprus","Cyprus U.N. Buffer Zone"],"dot":"Nicosia"},"facts":[["Country","Cyprus"],["Population","270k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+19.4% (2012–2015)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","cyprus"],"abstract":"<b>Nicosia</b> is the capital of Cyprus, an island the United Nations places in Western Asia and credits with 1,371,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same source puts the capital's own population at 269,500, a figure its footnotes date to 2018 although the table prints it in a column headed 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The number carries no statement of what it counts, and nowhere in this deck does that omission matter more, because the north of the island lies outside the government's control and Cypriot statistics are repeatedly footnoted as excluding it or as referring to government-controlled areas alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figure also cannot be checked against a second count, because the World Bank publishes no series whatever for the largest urban agglomeration of Cyprus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country's published populations count different territories rather than disagreeing with one another: the World Bank gives 1,370,754 for 2025 and the United Nations 1,371,000 with a footnote saying the figure refers to the whole country, while the European Union quotes Eurostat's 979,865, which is the area the government administers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The island had been a British Crown Colony since 1925 and became independent in 1960, and the United States recognised the new state on 16 August that year in a Department of State press release.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American Embassy at Nicosia began on the same day, when the American Consulate General already in the city was elevated to an embassy under L. Douglas Heck as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ambassador Fraser Wilkins presented his credentials on 19 September 1960, the day before Cyprus joined the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cyprus joined the Commonwealth in 1961, a year after independence from Britain, and the Commonwealth Secretariat gives the country 9,251 square kilometres and 1.2 million people for 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the European Union on 1 May 2004, which gives the island 9,253 square kilometres against the United Nations' 9,251, while the World Bank's own surface-area series was revised from 9,251 to 9,250 at 2018 and has stood there since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cyprus,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cy.html. [Open access]","European Union, “Cyprus – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/cyprus_en. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cyprus,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cyprus. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Cyprus,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/cyprus. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Cyprus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYP/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cyprus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Cyprus,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Nicosia%27s_skyline_2024.jpg/1920px-Nicosia%27s_skyline_2024.jpg","title":"The skyline of Nicosia seen from the Shacolas Tower, 2024","desc":"The low roofs of the old city in the foreground, with the towers of the modern centre behind them, photographed at sunset in January 2024.","credit":"Photograph by PomposPompou, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicosia%27s_skyline_2024.jpg","alt":"A wide view across Nicosia at sunset, densely packed low buildings in the foreground and a line of high-rise towers behind them under an orange sky."}},
{"id":"gw-659","num":659,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Port Louis","answerText":"Port Louis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 March 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 March 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 April 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mauritius","dot":"Port Louis"},"facts":[["Country","Mauritius"],["Population","149k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","mauritius"],"abstract":"<b>Port Louis</b> is the capital of Mauritius, an island state the United Nations places in Eastern Africa and credits with 1,268,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same source gives the city 149,400, a figure its footnotes date to 2018 although the table prints it in a column headed 2025, and it says nothing about what territory that count covers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor can the number be set beside a second one, because the World Bank publishes no series at all for the largest urban agglomeration of Mauritius, so the capital rests on the United Nations alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country's population is falling, from 1,266,334 in 2021 to 1,243,741 in 2025 in the World Bank's own count, which is a little below the United Nations' projection for the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three areas are published for the country, and the difference between them is a question of which islands are counted rather than of measurement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The Commonwealth Secretariat sets the arithmetic out: the island of Mauritius is 1,864 square kilometres, Rodrigues is 104, and the total including the other islands is 2,050.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations gives 1,979 with a footnote saying its figure excludes Saint Brandon and Agalega, and the World Bank's surface-area series gives 2,010, revised up from 2,007 at 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One table counts two different countries in two of its own rows, because the population footnote runs the other way and says that 1,268,000 includes Agalega, Rodrigues and Saint Brandon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mauritius had been under British sovereignty and became independent on 12 March 1968, and the United States recognised it that day, when the American Embassy at Port Louis was established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on the same date under William B. Hussey as chargé d'affaires ad interim, the country joined the Commonwealth in 1968 following independence from Britain, and it joined the United Nations on 24 April that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mauritius,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mu.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Mauritius,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/mauritius. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Mauritius,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/mauritius. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Mauritius,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MUS/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Mauritius,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MUS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Mauritius,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MUS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Evening_Port_Louis.jpg/1920px-Evening_Port_Louis.jpg","title":"Port Louis and its harbour at dusk, seen from Signal Mountain","desc":"The capital spread between the hills and the harbour, with the docks lit along the waterfront on the left, photographed in April 2011.","credit":"Photograph by Peter Kuchar, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_Port_Louis.jpg","alt":"An evening view down over Port Louis from a hillside, the lit city filling a coastal plain with its harbour and docks on the left and the sea beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-660","num":660,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the administrative capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Mbabane","answerText":"Mbabane","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"eSwatini","dot":"Mbabane"},"facts":[["Country","Eswatini"],["Population","68.0k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+3.2% (2007–2015)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","eswatini"],"abstract":"<b>Mbabane</b> is the administrative capital of Eswatini, a landlocked country the United Nations places in Southern Africa and credits with 1,256,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not the country's only capital, and it is one of the rare cases where the profile says so itself: the United Nations attaches a footnote to the capital field reading that Mbabane is the administrative capital and Lobamba the legislative one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three institutions that describe the arrangement agree about this city and differ about the rest of it, the Commonwealth Secretariat calling Mbabane the administrative and judicial capital, and the Southern African Development Community giving Lobamba the royal seat as well as the legislature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city is small for a capital at 68,000 people, in a figure the United Nations footnotes to 2018 and which cannot be set beside a second count, the World Bank publishing no series at all for the country's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country's population is rising steadily instead, from 1,142,813 in 2015 to 1,256,174 in 2025, within a thousand of the United Nations' own projection for that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Four published areas for the country fall within about 40 square kilometres of one another: 17,360 from the World Bank, 17,363 from the United Nations, 17,364 from the Southern African Development Community and a rounded 17,400 from the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The country had been under British sovereignty and became independent on 6 September 1968, and the United States recognised it that day, when the American Embassy at Mbabane was established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on the same date with Chris C. Pappas, Jr. as chargé d'affaires ad interim, and the country joined the United Nations on 24 September 1968 and the Commonwealth the same year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> All of that happened under the name Swaziland, which the country carried until it was renamed Eswatini in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department's guide is still filed under the old name, its page headed Swaziland with the flag beside it captioned Eswatini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Eswatini,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Swaziland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/swaziland. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Kingdom of Eswatini,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kingdom-eswatini. [Open access]","Southern African Development Community, “Kingdom of Eswatini,” Member States, https://www.sadc.int/member-states/kingdom-eswatini. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Eswatini,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWZ/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Eswatini,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWZ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Eswatini,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWZ/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Mbabane_%286035476964%29.jpg/1920px-Mbabane_%286035476964%29.jpg","title":"Mbabane in its valley, seen from the surrounding hills","desc":"The capital spread along the floor and lower slopes of its valley in the Eswatini highveld, photographed in July 2011.","credit":"Photograph by Athena Lao, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mbabane_%286035476964%29.jpg","alt":"A distant view of Mbabane from a hillside, the low buildings of the city spread across a valley floor with wooded hills rising behind it."}},
{"id":"gw-661","num":661,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Djibouti","answerText":"Djibouti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 June 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 June 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cercle count</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2019</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Djibouti","dot":"Djibouti City"},"facts":[["Country","Djibouti"],["Population","569k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+20.7% (2018–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","djibouti"],"abstract":"<b>Djibouti</b> is the capital of the country of the same name, which the United Nations places in Eastern Africa and credits with 1,184,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital-city figure is 568,800, and for once the footnote says what is being counted rather than only when: it refers to the population of the <i>cercle</i>, the administrative district inherited from French rule, and it is dated 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That single district holds close to half of everybody in the country, 568,800 against a national 1,184,000, although the two figures are six years apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series for the largest urban agglomeration returns after three countries of silence, and it is not an independent check but the same estimate: its value for 2019 is 568,804, the United Nations' figure to the hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it adds is the years the profile does not, carrying the city from 531,499 in 2015 to 616,332 in 2025, so the number on this card is already six years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The country's other figures agree just as exactly, the World Bank giving 1,184,076 people for 2025 against the United Nations' 1,184,000 and both giving the country 23,200 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territory that became Djibouti had been under French sovereignty, latterly as the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas, and it became independent on 27 June 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the new republic the same day, in a congratulatory message from President Jimmy Carter to President Hassan Gouled Aptidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on that date too, when the American consulate general to the former territory became the embassy at Djibouti under Walter S. Clarke as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 20 September 1977, three months after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Djibouti,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dj.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Djibouti,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/djibouti. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Djibouti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DJI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Djibouti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DJI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Djibouti,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DJI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/An_aerial_view_of_Djibouti_City.jpg","title":"Djibouti seen from the air, 2020","desc":"The centre of the capital from above, its low blocks and palm-lined avenues running down towards the shore of the Gulf of Tadjoura.","credit":"Photograph by Skilla1st, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_aerial_view_of_Djibouti_City.jpg","alt":"An aerial photograph of central Djibouti, low white and sand-coloured buildings among palms and wide avenues, with water at the right-hand edge."}},
{"id":"gw-662","num":662,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Suva","answerText":"Suva","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 October 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resident amb.</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 April 1978</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Fiji","dot":"Suva"},"facts":[["Country","Fiji"],["Population","178k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","fiji"],"abstract":"<b>Suva</b> is the capital of Fiji, an island country the United Nations places in Melanesia and credits with 933,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 178,300 people in a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it covers, and it cannot be checked against a second count, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country's own area is agreed to the kilometre for once, the United Nations and the Commonwealth Secretariat both giving 18,272 square kilometres and the World Bank 18,270, which is that figure rounded to its nearest ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The population has moved unusually, falling from 918,578 in 2015 to 914,899 in 2019 and then rising to 933,154 by 2025, so a decade that began and ended in growth has a dip in the middle of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fiji had been a British Crown Colony from 1874 and became independent on 10 October 1970, which the United States recognised the same day in a statement by Charles W. Yost at the Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Diplomatic relations followed on 22 July 1971, when Fiji's ambassador Semesa K. Sikivou presented his credentials, and the American Embassy at Suva was established on 1 November that year under Robert W. Skiff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No American ambassador lived in the city for another seven years: until John P. Condon presented his credentials at Suva on 11 April 1978 they were resident at Wellington, in New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The arrangement had a long precedent, the first United States consul to the Fiji Islands having been appointed in 1844 and resident at Auckland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fiji joined the United Nations on 13 October 1970 and the Commonwealth in the same year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its Commonwealth membership has since been suspended three times, from October 1987 to October 1997, from June 2000 to December 2001 and from December 2006 to September 2014, a sequence the State Department's guide says is widely called the coup cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Fiji,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fj.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Fiji,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/fiji. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Fiji,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/fiji. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Fiji,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FJI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Fiji,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FJI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Fiji,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FJI/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Suva_City_1_February_2015.jpg/1920px-Suva_City_1_February_2015.jpg","title":"Central Suva from above, looking over Prouds Triangle","desc":"The centre of the capital in February 2015, its colonial-era shopfronts and mid-rise blocks running down towards the harbour with the hills behind.","credit":"Photograph by Stemoc, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suva_City_1_February_2015.jpg","alt":"An elevated view over central Suva, low white-roofed shopfronts and taller office blocks among palms, with green hills rising in the distance."}},
{"id":"gw-663","num":663,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Moroni","answerText":"Moroni","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 August 1985</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1993</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Comoros","dot":"Moroni"},"facts":[["Country","Comoros"],["Population","62.4k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","comoros"],"abstract":"<b>Moroni</b> is the capital of the Comoros, an island country the United Nations places in Eastern Africa and credits with 883,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 62,400 people, in a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it counts, and the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration against which to check it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two published areas differ by nearly 400 square kilometres, the United Nations giving the country 2,235 square kilometres and the World Bank 1,861, and neither carries a footnote explaining the gap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country's population is growing quickly, from 726,682 in 2015 to 882,847 in 2025, a fifth again in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It joined the United Nations on 12 November 1975, but the United States did not recognise it for nearly two more years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Recognition came on 15 August 1977 through a joint communiqué announcing diplomatic relations, and the first American chargé d'affaires, Robert S. Barrett IV, presented his credentials the next day while living at Antananarivo in Madagascar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy at Moroni was not established until 26 August 1985, eight years after relations began, under Edward Brynn as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It closed again on 30 September 1993, and later American ambassadors to the Comoros were accredited from Port Louis, in Mauritius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 6 March 2006 responsibility moved again, from the embassy at Port Louis to the embassy at Antananarivo, so the duty returned to the city that had held it in 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two gaps are the same length: eight years passed between diplomatic relations and an embassy in the city, and eight more between that embassy and its closure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Comoros,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/km.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Comoros,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/comoros. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Comoros,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Comoros,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Comoros,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/COM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Moroni_City_Comoros.JPG/1280px-Moroni_City_Comoros.JPG","title":"Moroni from the sea, 2014","desc":"The waterfront of the capital with the white minaret of its old Friday mosque, and the wooded slopes of Grande Comore rising behind the town.","credit":"Photograph by Ikissai, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moroni_City_Comoros.JPG","alt":"Moroni seen from the water, a low waterfront town of pale buildings with a white minaret at its centre and green wooded hills behind."}},
{"id":"gw-664","num":664,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Georgetown","answerText":"Georgetown","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 May 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 May 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guyana","dot":"Georgetown"},"facts":[["Country","Guyana"],["Population","110k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-12.0% (2002–2012)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","guyana"],"abstract":"<b>Georgetown</b> is the capital of Guyana, a country the United Nations places in South America although it belongs to the Commonwealth and looks to the Caribbean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city is given 109,900 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it counts, and it cannot be checked against a second count because the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country is thinly peopled, 836,000 across 214,969 square kilometres at a density the profile puts at 4.2 to the square kilometre, and the capital's 2018 figure is about an eighth of the national one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its published areas are one figure at three degrees of rounding: 214,969 from the United Nations, 214,970 from the World Bank and 215,000 from the Commonwealth Secretariat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The populations do not agree so tidily, the Commonwealth giving 786,560 for 2022 where the World Bank gives 821,637 for that same year, a gap of four per cent between two figures dated to one year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Dutch colonies of Essequibo, Demerara and Berbice were ceded to Britain at the Congress of Vienna and united as a single British colony in 1831.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Guyana became independent on 26 May 1966, and the United States recognised it that day by the act of opening its embassy at Georgetown, with Delmar R. Carlson as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition, diplomatic relations and the embassy all carry that same date, so the guide's three headings for Guyana record one act rather than three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Guyana joined the Commonwealth in 1966, following independence from Britain, and the United Nations on 20 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population has grown by a tenth since 2015, from 758,781 to 835,986, with most of that increase falling after 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guyana,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gy.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Guyana,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/guyana. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Guyana,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/guyana. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Guyana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUY/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guyana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUY/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Guyana,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUY/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Georgetown_from_the_air_%2823284743859%29.jpg/1920px-Georgetown_from_the_air_%2823284743859%29.jpg","title":"Georgetown from the air, 2015","desc":"The capital's grid of low houses on the coastal plain, its drainage canals running between the streets, with the silt-brown Atlantic along the top.","credit":"Photograph by Dan Sloan, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgetown_from_the_air_%2823284743859%29.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of Georgetown, a wide grid of low houses and green gardens on flat ground, with a straight main road and the muddy sea beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-665","num":665,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Honiara","answerText":"Honiara","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 July 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 July 1988</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 July 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Re-opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 January 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Solomon Is.","dot":"Honiara"},"facts":[["Country","Solomon Islands"],["Population","81.8k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+30.8% (2009–2017)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","solomon islands"],"abstract":"<b>Honiara</b> is the capital of the Solomon Islands, a country the United Nations places in Melanesia and credits with 839,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 81,800 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what it counts, and the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration against which to check it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The country is growing quickly, from 639,415 people in 2015 to 838,645 in 2025, which is close to a third again in ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its area is given as 28,896 square kilometres by the United Nations and 28,900 by the World Bank, which is that figure rounded, but as 30,407 by the Commonwealth Secretariat, five per cent more than either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its populations disagree at a stated year as well, the Commonwealth giving 686,900 for 2022 where the World Bank gives 781,066, and neither source explains the difference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The country had been under British sovereignty and became independent in 1978, and the United States recognised it on 7 July that year by a letter from President Jimmy Carter carried to Prime Minister Peter Kenilorea by Senator John Glenn as the President's personal representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 9 October 1978, when Mary S. Olmsted presented her credentials as ambassador while accredited to Papua New Guinea as well and resident at Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American consulate already stood in Honiara and was raised to an embassy on 4 July 1988, only for it to close on 30 July 1993, after which ambassadors were again resident at Port Moresby.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy re-opened on 27 January 2023, thirty years later, under Russell Comeau as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide also records that the islands were the site of intense fighting between American and Japanese forces during the Second World War, particularly on Guadalcanal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Solomon Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sb.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Solomon Islands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/solomon-islands. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Solomon Islands,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/solomon-islands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Solomon Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Solomon Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Solomon Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SLB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Aerial_view_of_Honiara%2C_2013.jpg/1920px-Aerial_view_of_Honiara%2C_2013.jpg","title":"Honiara from the air, 2013","desc":"The capital along its shore with hillside settlement in the foreground, looking north across the water to the islands beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Jenny Scott, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Honiara%2C_2013.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of Honiara, scattered houses on green hillsides in the foreground and the town strung along a blue coastline, with distant islands on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-666","num":666,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Thimphu","answerText":"Thimphu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1971</span><span class=\"dt-k\">GA resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2751 (XXVI)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City counted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bhutan","dot":"Thimphu"},"facts":[["Country","Bhutan"],["Population","203k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+44.7% (2005–2017)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","bhutan"],"abstract":"<b>Thimphu</b> is the capital of Bhutan, a country the United Nations places in Southern Asia and credits with 797,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 203,300 people in a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it counts, which is about a quarter of the country by those two numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count to set beside it, because the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national figures agree exactly, the World Bank giving 796,682 people for 2025 where the profile gives 797,000, and the population has risen steadily from 740,618 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The area is given as 38,394 square kilometres by the United Nations, which is what the World Bank's own series carried until 2018, when it began giving 38,390 instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The country around the capital is thinly settled and heavily wooded, at 20.8 people to the square kilometre with forest over 71.6 per cent of the land area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only 41.6 per cent of Bhutanese live in towns at all, so a large share of those who do live in Thimphu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The profile also gives income per head as 3,839 US dollars for 2023 and internet use as 88.4 per hundred inhabitants, the second having more than doubled since 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bhutan joined the United Nations on 21 September 1971, and the admission is recorded in the resolution itself: the General Assembly adopted resolution 2751 (XXVI), admitting Bhutan to membership, at its 1934th plenary meeting that day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is the firmest date this card can give for the state Thimphu is the seat of, and the capital's own figure beside it is seven years old and undefined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bhutan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bt.html. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of Bhutan to membership in the United Nations,” A/RES/2751 (XXVI), United Nations Digital Library, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/192050. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Bhutan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BTN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bhutan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BTN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Bhutan,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BTN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/A_view_of_Thimphu_from_Talakha_Gonpa.jpg/1920px-A_view_of_Thimphu_from_Talakha_Gonpa.jpg","title":"Thimphu in its valley, seen from Talakha Gonpa","desc":"The capital running along the floor of its Himalayan valley, photographed from the monastery above it in May 2021.","credit":"Photograph by Christopher J. Fynn, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_view_of_Thimphu_from_Talakha_Gonpa.jpg","alt":"A distant view of Thimphu along a valley floor between forested mountain ridges, with higher ranges under cloud on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-668","num":668,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Luxembourg","answerText":"Luxembourg","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 May 1878</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 July 1903</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1956</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1958</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Luxembourg","dot":"Luxembourg City"},"facts":[["Country","Luxembourg"],["Population","120k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+8.9% (2021–2026)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","luxembourg"],"abstract":"<b>Luxembourg</b> is the capital of the country of the same name, which the United Nations places in Western Europe and credits with 680,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 119,800 people in a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it counts, and the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration against which to check it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The national counts sit close together for once: the United Nations projects 680,000 for 2025, the European Union quotes Eurostat's 681,973 and the World Bank gives 686,970, all within one per cent of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The population has grown by a fifth in a decade, from 569,604 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The areas are as close, 2,586 square kilometres from the United Nations, 2,590 from the World Bank, which is that figure to the nearest ten, and 2,595 from Eurostat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Luxembourg achieved full independence in 1867, and the United States recognised it on 31 May 1878, when President Rutherford B. Hayes issued an exequatur to its consul general at New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The accredited American minister then lived elsewhere for half a century: relations began on 17 July 1903 with him resident at The Hague, and from 23 October 1923 at Brussels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany occupied the country on 10 May 1940 and the American legation closed that July, after which the United States was accredited to Luxembourg's government in exile, first at Ottawa and from October 1943 in London.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation reopened on 23 September 1944, and the representation was elevated in 1956, when Wiley T. Buchanan Jr. presented his credentials on 21 September as the first American ambassador to Luxembourg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The European Union records the country as a member state since 1 January 1958, in the euro area since 1999 and in the Schengen area since 1995.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Luxembourg,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lu.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Luxembourg,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/luxembourg. [Open access]","European Union, “Luxembourg – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/luxembourg_en. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Luxembourg,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LUX/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Luxembourg,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LUX/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Luxembourg,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LUX/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Valley_of_Alzette_river_in_Luxembourg_City_02.jpg/1920px-Valley_of_Alzette_river_in_Luxembourg_City_02.jpg","title":"The Alzette valley below Luxembourg City, 2018","desc":"The old town on its cliffs at the right, the Pfaffenthal viaduct crossing the valley, and the towers of the modern business quarter on the plateau beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Krzysztof Golik, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valley_of_Alzette_river_in_Luxembourg_City_02.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a wooded river valley in autumn, with a stone railway viaduct crossing it, old buildings along the river and high-rise towers on the skyline."}},
{"id":"gw-669","num":669,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Paramaribo","answerText":"Paramaribo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 November 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 December 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">District counted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Suriname","dot":"Paramaribo"},"facts":[["Country","Suriname"],["Population","240k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-6.8% (2012–2019)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","suriname"],"abstract":"<b>Paramaribo</b> is the capital of Suriname, a country the United Nations places in South America and credits with 640,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital figure is one of the few in this deck that says what it counts as well as when: 239,500, footnoted as the total population of the District of Paramaribo and dated 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That district holds well over a third of the country, which is otherwise nearly empty at about four people to the square kilometre across 163,820 square kilometres, with forest over 94.5 per cent of the land area and two thirds of the population living in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The national figures agree closely, the World Bank giving 639,850 people for 2025 and the same 163,820 square kilometres, and its population series rises steadily from 581,627 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> There is no independent count of the city itself, because the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, so the district figure stands alone; income per head, meanwhile, has fallen from 8,814 US dollars in 2015 to 5,976 in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Suriname was a Dutch colony on the northern coast of South America dating from 1667, was given increasing autonomy from 1951, and became independent from the Netherlands in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ceremony proclaiming independence was held in Paramaribo on 25 November 1975, with the United States represented by its Secretary of the Navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> President Gerald Ford wrote to President Johan Ferrier that same day extending American recognition and hoping for the prompt establishment of relations, and diplomatic relations and an American embassy at Paramaribo were established on that date, under Robert L. Flanagin as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Suriname joined the United Nations nine days later, on 4 December 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The guide records that the elected government was overthrown in 1980 by a military regime under Desi Bouterse, and that elected government was re-established in 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Suriname,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sr.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Suriname,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/suriname. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Suriname,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SUR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Suriname,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SUR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Suriname,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SUR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Paramaribo_District_01.jpg/1920px-Paramaribo_District_01.jpg","title":"Paramaribo from the air, 2018","desc":"The capital's grid of brightly roofed houses running north across the coastal plain to the Suriname River and the sea beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Sn.fernandez, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paramaribo_District_01.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of Paramaribo, street after street of small houses with red, blue and green roofs stretching towards a wide river and the coast."}},
{"id":"gw-670","num":670,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Podgorica","answerText":"Podgorica","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 June 2006</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 June 2006</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 2006</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 October 2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Montenegro","dot":"Podgorica"},"facts":[["Country","Montenegro"],["Population","177k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+14.6% (2011–2023)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","montenegro"],"abstract":"<b>Podgorica</b> is the capital of Montenegro, a country the United Nations places in Southern Europe and credits with 633,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital figure, 177,200, is defined more narrowly than most: the footnote calls it the urban population of Podgorica municipality, and dates it to 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and its national population is a little below the United Nations' projection at 623,129 for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That figure has barely moved in a decade, rising from 624,651 in 2015 to 626,753 in 2019 and falling back since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The area is given as 13,812 square kilometres by the United Nations, and the World Bank's series carried 13,810 until 2023, when it moved to 13,888 instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> American recognition of Montenegro was granted, withdrawn and then granted again a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Great Powers recognised an independent principality at the Congress of Berlin in 1878, and the United States followed on 3 March 1905, when President Theodore Roosevelt approved an act assigning the American mission in Greece responsibility for representation in Montenegro.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations began on 30 October 1905, and the minister and his successors lived in Athens and paid annual visits to Cetinje, which was then the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the First World War Montenegro was merged into the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, and President Woodrow Wilson withdrew recognition on 30 December 1920, ending relations with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Montenegro declared independence again on 3 June 2006, was recognised on 13 June in a statement by Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice, and the American consulate at Podgorica became an embassy on 5 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Montenegro,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/me.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Montenegro,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/montenegro. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Montenegro,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNE/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Montenegro,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Montenegro,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/P064720-426794_-_Panoramic_view_of_Podgorica.jpg/1920px-P064720-426794_-_Panoramic_view_of_Podgorica.jpg","title":"Podgorica from the air, 2024","desc":"The centre of the capital with its blocks and boulevards, the Millennium Bridge beyond, and the mountains of the interior on the skyline.","credit":"Photograph by Savo Prelevic for the European Commission, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P064720-426794_-_Panoramic_view_of_Podgorica.jpg","alt":"An aerial view over Podgorica, low apartment blocks and tree-lined streets around a roundabout, with a cable-stayed bridge and mountain ridges in the distance."}},
{"id":"gw-672","num":672,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Valletta","answerText":"Valletta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1964</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 2004</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malta","dot":"Valletta"},"facts":[["Country","Malta"],["Population","213k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-19.4% (2014–2021)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","malta"],"abstract":"<b>Valletta</b> is the capital of Malta, a country the United Nations places in Southern Europe and credits with 545,000 people on 315 square kilometres, at more than 1,700 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital-city figure of 212,800 does not describe the city at all: the footnote says it refers to the localities of the Northern Harbour and Southern Harbour, two statistical districts around the port, and dates it to 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no separate count of the city itself in these sources, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national figures diverge more than usual, the United Nations projecting 545,000 for 2025 where Eurostat gives 574,250 and the World Bank 579,704.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reason is growth: the World Bank's count rises from 444,220 in 2015 to 579,704 in 2025, close to a third in ten years, which a projection built on fertility rather than migration will not follow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Malta was a Crown Colony of the United Kingdom from 1814 until 1964, when it was granted independence and joined the Commonwealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 18 September 1964, when President Lyndon Johnson sent a letter to Prime Minister Giorgio Borg Olivier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American embassy in Valletta was established three days later, on 21 September, with Harrison Lewis as chargé d'affaires ad interim, and the first ambassador, George J. Feldman, presented his credentials on 5 October 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Malta joined the United Nations on 1 December 1964.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became a member of the European Union on 1 May 2004, of the Schengen area in 2007 and of the euro area in 2008, and the areas the three sources publish agree closely at 315, 316 and a rounded 320 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Malta,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mt.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Malta,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/malta. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Malta,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Malta,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Malta,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MLT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","European Union, “Malta – EU country,” https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/eu-countries/malta_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Valletta%2C_Malta_%28Aerial_View%29.jpg/1920px-Valletta%2C_Malta_%28Aerial_View%29.jpg","title":"Valletta on its peninsula, 2019","desc":"The walled capital between its two harbours, with the built-up harbour localities the United Nations counts as its population spreading away on either side.","credit":"Photograph by Jonathan Mercieca, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valletta%2C_Malta_%28Aerial_View%29.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of Valletta, a compact honey-coloured walled city on a peninsula between two blue harbours, with a cruise ship at the quay and further towns beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-673","num":673,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Malé","answerText":"Malé","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 April 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Maldives","dot":"Malé"},"facts":[["Country","Maldives"],["Population","177k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+143.8% (2006–2021)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","maldives"],"abstract":"<b>Malé</b> is the capital of the Maldives, a country the United Nations places in Southern Asia and credits with 530,000 people on 300 square kilometres, at more than 1,760 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 176,900 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what it counts, so a third of the country lives in the capital on these two numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, though its national figure of 529,676 for 2025 matches the profile's almost exactly and both put the area at 300 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown by nearly a quarter in a decade, from 428,154 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat gives a higher figure again, 540,542 for 2022 against the World Bank's 524,106 for the same year, and it spells the capital Malé where the United Nations writes Male.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Maldives became independent from Britain in 1965, and the United States recognised it on 10 August that year in a message from President Lyndon Johnson to Prime Minister Ibrahim Nasir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began on 9 April 1966, when the American ambassador to Ceylon presented his credentials while remaining resident at Colombo, and his successor was reaccredited in 1969 after the country became a republic in 1968.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That arrangement has never ended: the guide states plainly that the United States has no embassy in Malé and that its ambassador to Sri Lanka is accredited to the Maldives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 21 September 1965 and the Commonwealth in 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It left the Commonwealth on 13 October 2016 and rejoined on 1 February 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Maldives,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mv.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Maldives,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/maldives. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Maldives,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/maldives. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Maldives,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Maldives,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Maldives,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MDV/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Male_City_Aerial%2C_The_Capital_city_of_Maldives_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Male_City_Aerial%2C_The_Capital_city_of_Maldives_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Malé from the air","desc":"The capital built out to its own sea wall, with the harbour, the breakwater and the reef edge falling away into deep water.","credit":"Photograph by Ibrahim Asad, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_City_Aerial%2C_The_Capital_city_of_Maldives_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of Malé, closely packed towers covering a small island to the water's edge, with boats in a harbour and a turquoise reef beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-674","num":674,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Praia","answerText":"Praia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 July 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 January 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2013</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cabo Verde","dot":"Praia"},"facts":[["Country","Cabo Verde"],["Population","168k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+22.1% (2010–2017)"]],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","cabo verde"],"abstract":"<b>Praia</b> is the capital of Cabo Verde, an island country the United Nations places in Western Africa and credits with 527,000 people on 4,033 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 167,500 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what it counts, so about a third of the country lives in the capital on these two numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, though its national total of 527,326 for 2025 matches the profile's exactly and its area of 4,030 square kilometres is the same figure rounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The population barely moved for five years, from 512,394 in 2015 to 514,104 in 2019, and has risen since; two thirds of it lives in towns, and the islands recorded 785,000 visitor arrivals in a recent year, more than the country's own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country's own name changed within that span: it had been Cape Verde in English, and the guide records that it was re-named Cabo Verde in 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Cabo Verde had been under Portuguese sovereignty, and the United States recognised the new republic on 5 July 1975 in a letter from President Gerald Ford to President Aristides Pereira.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established a fortnight later, on 19 July, when the Cape Verdean ambassador presented his credentials in Washington rather than the other way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first American ambassador, Melissa F. Wells, was accredited on 18 November 1976 while resident at Bissau in Guinea-Bissau, to which she was also accredited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy at Praia followed on 28 January 1978, two and a half years after recognition, with Howard McGowan as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country had joined the United Nations on 16 September 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cabo Verde,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cv.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cape Verde,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cape-verde. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Cabo Verde,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CPV/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cabo Verde,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CPV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Cabo Verde,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CPV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Praia_aerial.jpg/1920px-Praia_aerial.jpg","title":"Praia from the air, 2007","desc":"The old town on its flat-topped headland above the bay, with the rest of the capital spreading inland over the dry ground behind it.","credit":"Photograph by David Trainer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Praia_aerial.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of Praia, a town on a flat headland above a turquoise bay, with cloud shadows and dry brown country stretching inland."}},
{"id":"gw-675","num":675,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bandar Seri Begawan","answerText":"Bandar Seri Begawan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1865 to 1868</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Brunei","dot":"Bandar Seri Begawan"},"facts":[["Country","Brunei Darussalam"],["Population","40.8k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","brunei"],"abstract":"<b>Bandar Seri Begawan</b> is the capital of Brunei Darussalam, a country the United Nations places in South-eastern Asia and credits with 466,000 people on 5,765 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital-city figure is unusually small, 40,800 for 2018, under a tenth of the country, and the profile again says nothing about what territory it counts — which matters here, because the same profile puts 77.9 per cent of Bruneians in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count to compare it with, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national numbers agree well, the World Bank giving 466,330 people for 2025, almost exactly the profile's projection, and 5,770 square kilometres, which is its figure rounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That population has grown steadily from 422,212 in 2015, in a country that is 72.1 per cent forest, with income per head of 32,963 US dollars for 2023 and 99 internet users per hundred inhabitants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> American dealings with Brunei are older than with most countries in this collection: a treaty of peace, friendship, commerce and navigation was signed there on 23 June 1850, entered into force in 1853, and the guide records that it remains in effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A United States consulate followed at the post then called Brunei, Borneo, held from 1865 to 1868.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations with the sultan's government lapsed when Brunei became a British protectorate in 1888 and the United Kingdom took over its foreign affairs, which it kept until 1984 even after a constitution of 1959 declared the country self-governing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised full independence on 1 January 1984 and dates the opening of its embassy at Bandar Seri Begawan to that day in one paragraph and to 28 May, when Ambassador Barrington King presented his credentials, in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Brunei joined the United Nations on 21 September 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Brunei Darussalam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Brunei,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/brunei. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Brunei Darussalam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Brunei Darussalam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Brunei Darussalam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Aerial_of_Brunei_%282_August_2023%29_11.jpg/1920px-Aerial_of_Brunei_%282_August_2023%29_11.jpg","title":"Bandar Seri Begawan from the air, 2023","desc":"The compact centre with the dome of its principal mosque, the Brunei River curving past, and the stilt houses of the water village spreading along the far bank.","credit":"Photograph by Pangalau, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_of_Brunei_%282_August_2023%29_11.jpg","alt":"A hazy aerial view of Bandar Seri Begawan, a small city of white buildings and a golden dome beside a wide river, with a large village of houses on stilts along the water."}},
{"id":"gw-676","num":676,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Belmopan","answerText":"Belmopan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 March 1847</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 September 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 December 2006</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Belize","dot":"Belmopan"},"facts":[["Country","Belize"],["Population","23.0k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","2nd largest"],["Population growth","+49.1% (2010–2022)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","belize"],"abstract":"<b>Belmopan</b> is the capital of Belize, a country the United Nations places in Central America and credits with 423,000 people on 22,965 square kilometres, at about 19 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its capital-city figure is very small, 23,000 for 2018, about a twentieth of the country, and the profile does not say what territory it counts, though it puts 45.9 per cent of Belizeans in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and its national total of 422,924 for 2025 matches the profile's almost exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has risen from 356,333 in 2015, close to a fifth in a decade, in a country still 55 per cent forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's area series shows plainly what its figures do elsewhere: it gave 22,970 square kilometres until 2022 and 22,966 from 2023, where the United Nations and the Commonwealth Secretariat have both said 22,965 throughout, so the older value was the same measurement rounded to the nearest ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United States opened a consulate in British Honduras, as Belize then was, on 3 March 1847, and the colony took the name Belize in June 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Belize became independent from the United Kingdom on 21 September 1981, after negotiations with Britain and Guatemala over territory disputed since an 1859 treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it on 29 October 1981, when its consulate general was raised to embassy status under Malcolm R. Barnebey as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy stood in Belize City, and it moved to Belmopan only on 11 December 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Belize joined the United Nations on 25 September 1981 and the Commonwealth the same year, following independence from Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Belize,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bz.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Belize,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/belize. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Belize,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/belize. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Belize,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLZ/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Belize,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLZ/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Belize,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BLZ/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Belmopan_Belize_View.jpg/1920px-Belmopan_Belize_View.jpg","title":"Belmopan at dusk, 2016","desc":"The capital seen from above its main road, low buildings and palms among trees with the forested country of central Belize beyond.","credit":"Photograph by La Pulgarcita, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belmopan_Belize_View.jpg","alt":"An elevated view across Belmopan at dusk, a palm-lined road and scattered low buildings with dense green forest stretching to low hills on the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-677","num":677,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nassau","answerText":"Nassau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 July 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 July 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City counted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bahamas","dot":"Nassau"},"facts":[["Country","The Bahamas"],["Population","280k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","bahamas"],"abstract":"<b>Nassau</b> is the capital of the Bahamas, a country the United Nations places in the Caribbean and credits with 403,000 people for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 279,700, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it counts, which puts more than two thirds of the country in its capital even allowing for the seven years between the two numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city to test that against, because the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the profile puts 83.1 per cent of Bahamians in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The national figures agree closely, the World Bank giving 403,033 people for 2025 against the profile's 403,000, and the population has risen slowly from 385,346 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The areas do not: the United Nations gives 13,940 square kilometres, the World Bank 13,880 and the Commonwealth Secretariat a rounded 14,000, and the sixty kilometres between the first two are too many to be a rounding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Bahamas became independent from the United Kingdom on 10 July 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it formally that same day, by the act of establishing its embassy in Nassau, with Moncrieff J. Spear as chargé d'affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition, diplomatic relations and the embassy therefore carry one date between them, the guide filing all three under 10 July 1973.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 18 September 1973 and the Commonwealth in the same year, following independence from Britain; the profile gives its income per head as 35,896 US dollars for 2023 and records 1.45 million visitor arrivals, more than three times its own population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Commonwealth gives its population as 393,250 for 2022, where the World Bank gives 397,538 for that same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bahamas,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bs.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Bahamas,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/bahamas. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Bahamas,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/bahamas. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Bahamas, The,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHS/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bahamas, The,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHS/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Bahamas, The,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BHS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Nassau%2C_Bahamas_aerial_view.jpg/1920px-Nassau%2C_Bahamas_aerial_view.jpg","title":"Downtown Nassau from the air","desc":"The main street and harbour front of the capital, with the cruise berths along the quay and the resorts of Paradise Island across the channel.","credit":"Photograph by Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nassau%2C_Bahamas_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of downtown Nassau, low pastel buildings along a main street beside a harbour with cruise berths, and a pink resort on an island across the water."}},
{"id":"gw-678","num":678,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Reykjavík","answerText":"Reykjavík","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 June 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 November 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1955</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Iceland","dot":"Reykjavík"},"facts":[["Country","Iceland"],["Population","216k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+10.5% (2020–2026)"]],"difficulty":2,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","iceland"],"abstract":"<b>Reykjavík</b> is the capital of Iceland, a country the United Nations places in Northern Europe and credits with 398,000 people spread across 103,000 square kilometres, at four to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 216,400 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it counts, so more than half the country lives in the capital on these two numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and its national figure of 392,404 for 2025 sits a little below the profile's projection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country has grown quickly for its size, from 330,815 people in 2015, close to a fifth in a decade, and the two sources agree exactly on its area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United Nations writes the capital's name without its accent, as Reykjavik.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The American mission in the city is older than the republic it is accredited to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iceland took control of its own foreign relations on 10 April 1940, the day after Germany occupied Denmark, and asked the United States for protection under the Monroe Doctrine; American forces occupied the island on 7 July 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and an American legation at Reykjavík were established on 30 September 1941, when the consulate was raised to legation status and Lincoln MacVeagh presented his credentials, three years before independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iceland became a republic on 17 June 1944 after a plebiscite, and the United States recognised it that day in public statements of congratulation by President Franklin Roosevelt and Secretary of State Cordell Hull.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The legation became an embassy on 3 November 1955, nine years after Iceland joined the United Nations on 19 November 1946.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Iceland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/is.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Iceland,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/iceland. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Iceland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ISL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Iceland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ISL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Iceland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ISL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Reykjav%C3%ADk_Panorama_ma%C3%AD_2026.jpg/1920px-Reykjav%C3%ADk_Panorama_ma%C3%AD_2026.jpg","title":"Reykjavík looking north, May 2026","desc":"The coloured roofs of the older streets with the church tower on the right, the bay beyond and the flat-topped mass of Esja across the water.","credit":"Photograph by Steinninn, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reykjav%C3%ADk_Panorama_ma%C3%AD_2026.jpg","alt":"A wide view over Reykjavík, low houses with red, green and grey roofs running down to a blue bay, with a snow-streaked flat-topped mountain on the far shore."}},
{"id":"gw-679","num":679,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Port Vila","answerText":"Port Vila","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 July 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 September 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 September 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 July 2024</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Vanuatu","dot":"Port Vila"},"facts":[["Country","Vanuatu"],["Population","52.7k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-4.7% (2016–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","vanuatu"],"abstract":"<b>Port Vila</b> is the capital of Vanuatu, a country the United Nations places in Melanesia and credits with 335,000 people on 12,189 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 52,700 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what territory it counts, so about a sixth of the country lives in the capital on these two numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the national figures agree closely, its 335,169 for 2025 matching the profile and its 12,190 square kilometres being the same area rounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population has grown by about a quarter in a decade, from 266,101 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat spells the city Port-Vila with a hyphen, and gives the country 307,150 people for 2022 where the World Bank gives 313,046 for the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Vanuatu had been governed jointly by France and the United Kingdom as a condominium, and became independent on 30 July 1980, which the United States recognised soon afterwards in a message from President Jimmy Carter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed only on 30 September 1986, when the two states signed an agreement to that effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide notes that no American mission was established at the capital then: ambassadorial relations began on 10 April 1987, when the United States ambassador to Papua New Guinea presented his credentials to the government of Vanuatu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy opened in Port Vila only on 19 July 2024, forty-four years after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Vanuatu joined the United Nations on 15 September 1981 and the Commonwealth in 1980, at the end of the condominium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Vanuatu,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vu.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Vanuatu,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/vanuatu. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Vanuatu,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/vanuatu. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Vanuatu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VUT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Vanuatu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VUT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Vanuatu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VUT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Central_Port_Vila%2C_Vanuatu%2C_29_Nov._2006_-_Flickr_-_PhillipC.jpg/1920px-Central_Port_Vila%2C_Vanuatu%2C_29_Nov._2006_-_Flickr_-_PhillipC.jpg","title":"Central Port Vila from the air, 2006","desc":"The town along its harbour with the wooded island in the bay, a cruise ship at anchor and the reef shelving away into deep water.","credit":"Photograph by Phillip Capper, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Port_Vila%2C_Vanuatu%2C_29_Nov._2006_-_Flickr_-_PhillipC.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of Port Vila, a low town spread along a green shoreline beside a blue harbour with a small island and a cruise ship in it."}},
{"id":"gw-680","num":680,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nouméa","answerText":"Nouméa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Nouméa Accord</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 May 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 December 1998</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Latest resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 December 2025</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"New Caledonia","dot":"Nouméa"},"facts":[["Country","New Caledonia"],["Population","198k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+54.3% (1998–2013)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","new caledonia"],"abstract":"<b>Nouméa</b> is the capital of New Caledonia, a French territory the United Nations places in Melanesia and credits with 295,000 people on 19,100 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 197,800 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 283,541 for that year the capital holds about seventy per cent of the territory's people, and the profile's urban share is 71.1 per cent for 2019, so almost everyone counted as urban is counted at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city, the World Bank publishing no series for the territory's largest urban agglomeration, though the population totals agree, its 295,333 for 2025 matching the profile and growing slowly from 283,168 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The areas do not agree: the World Bank gives 18,580 square kilometres, 520 fewer than the profile, a gap too large to be rounding and unexplained by either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> New Caledonia is not a member of the United Nations and the profile carries no membership date, footnoting instead that for statistical purposes the data for France do not include this area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The General Assembly treats the territory as a decolonization question, and in 1998 welcomed the signing of the Nouméa Accord of 5 May that year between representatives of New Caledonia and the Government of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same resolution invited the administering Power to consider inviting a mission of information when the new institutions were established, and urged the parties to maintain their dialogue in a spirit of harmony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also invited them to promote a framework for the peaceful progress of the Territory towards an act of self-determination in which all options are open, in the spirit of the Matignon and Nouméa Accords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The question remains on the Assembly's agenda, a further resolution being adopted without a vote at the eightieth session's 55th plenary meeting on 5 December 2025, under the item on implementing the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “New Caledonia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nc.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: New Caledonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NCL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: New Caledonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NCL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: New Caledonia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NCL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Question of New Caledonia,” A/RES/53/65, 3 December 1998, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/265146. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Question of New Caledonia,” A/RES/80/98, 5 December 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4097588. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/View_over_Noumea.jpg/1920px-View_over_Noumea.jpg","title":"Nouméa seen from above the town centre, 2009","desc":"The cathedral towers over red and grey roofs, with the marina, the harbour and the lagoon islands beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Pilettes, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_over_Noumea.jpg","alt":"A view over Nouméa from a hillside, with a stone cathedral among red-roofed buildings and a marina full of yachts on the blue lagoon behind."}},
{"id":"gw-681","num":681,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Bridgetown","answerText":"Bridgetown","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 June 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1966</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 December 1966</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Barbados","dot":"Bridgetown"},"facts":[["Country","Barbados"],["Population","89.2k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+11.4% (2006–2014)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","barbados","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Bridgetown</b> is the capital of Barbados, a Caribbean country the United Nations credits with 283,000 people on 431 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 89,200 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 280,711 for that year the capital holds about a third of the country, and the profile's urban share is 31.2 per cent for 2019, so almost everyone counted as urban is counted at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no second count of the city, the World Bank publishing no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and its population figures track the profile closely, 282,623 for 2025 against 283,000 and barely moving over the decade from 278,990 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three sources agree on the area to within a square kilometre, the World Bank and the Commonwealth Secretariat both giving 430 where the profile gives 431, but they part on population, the Commonwealth putting 287,370 for 2022 where the World Bank gives 282,318.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Barbados was a British colony for more than three centuries, and the United States kept a consular presence there from 11 June 1823, long before independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became an independent state within the British Commonwealth on 30 November 1966, following a constitutional conference with Great Britain that June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised that independence the same day, and did so by opening an embassy in the capital: the mission at Bridgetown was established on 30 November 1966, with George Dolgin as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide records that the United States has since used Bridgetown as a base for its relations with the wider region as well as with Barbados.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations nine days after independence, on 9 December 1966, and the Commonwealth in 1966 on leaving British rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Barbados,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bb.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Barbados,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/barbados. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Barbados,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/barbados. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Barbados,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRB/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Barbados,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Barbados,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BRB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Bridgetown_aerial.JPG/1920px-Bridgetown_aerial.JPG","title":"Bridgetown from the air, 2012","desc":"The deep-water harbour and its breakwater on the left, the Careenage and its moorings below, and the city running inland along the coast.","credit":"Photograph by P. Lechien, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bridgetown_aerial.JPG","alt":"An oblique aerial photograph of Bridgetown, showing a container port behind a long breakwater, a marina, and dense low buildings spreading inland from a turquoise coast."}},
{"id":"gw-682","num":682,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Papeete","answerText":"Papeete","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sovereignty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1880</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Re-inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 2013</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Working paper</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 January 2025</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Fr. Polynesia","dot":"Papeete"},"facts":[["Country","French Polynesia"],["Population","136k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","-1.0% (2017–2022)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","french polynesia","polynesia"],"abstract":"<b>Papeete</b> is the capital of French Polynesia, a French territory the United Nations places in Polynesia and credits with 282,000 people on 3,687 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 136,000 people, and this is the one profile in the collection that says exactly what such a figure counts: the total population of the communes of Arue, Faaa, Mahina, Papara, Papeete, Pirae and Punaauia, footnoted to 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 279,815 for that year the seven communes hold about half the territory, where the profile's urban share is 61.9 per cent for 2019, so the capital is roughly four fifths of the urban population rather than all of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the city is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the territory's largest urban agglomeration, and the two totals agree, its 282,465 for 2025 matching the profile and barely moving from 277,866 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The areas differ and, unusually, both sides explain themselves: the profile counts 3,687 square kilometres including lakes Vaihiria and Temae and the Maiao lagoons, where the World Bank gives 3,471 and the Secretariat about 3,500 for the land alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> French Polynesia is a Non-Self-Governing Territory under the Charter of the United Nations, administered by France and represented by a High Commissioner of the Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its 118 islands lie in five archipelagos scattered over 2.5 million square kilometres of the South Pacific, with an exclusive economic zone of 5.5 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The harbour at Papeete is run by the Autonomous Port of Papeete, a public enterprise, and the one international airport stands at Faaa, another of the seven communes the capital figure counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Moetai Charles Brotherson has been Head of Government since 12 May 2023, and the Assembly of French Polynesia holds 38 seats for Tāvini Huiraʻatira and 16 for Tāpura Huiraʻatira, with three unaffiliated members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Special Committee on decolonization considers the Territory on a working paper prepared by the Secretariat, the current one dated 7 January 2025 and written originally in French.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “French Polynesia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pf.html. [Open access]","UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “French Polynesia: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2025/7, 7 January 2025, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4073636. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: French Polynesia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PYF/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: French Polynesia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PYF/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: French Polynesia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PYF/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Rade_de_Papeete.jpg/1920px-Rade_de_Papeete.jpg","title":"The harbour at Papeete, 2016","desc":"The quay and the boulevard along the water, yachts at their moorings, and houses climbing the green ridge behind the town.","credit":"Photograph by Jérémie Silvestro, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rade_de_Papeete.jpg","alt":"Papeete seen from the water: a low waterfront of offices and palms behind a quay, small boats moored along it, and wooded hills rising into cloud beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-683","num":683,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"São Tomé","answerText":"São Tomé","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 1975</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"São Tomé and Principe","dot":"São Tomé"},"facts":[["Country","São Tomé and Príncipe"],["Population","80.1k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+9.8% (2010–2015)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","sao tome and principe","africa"],"abstract":"<b>São Tomé</b> is the capital of São Tomé and Príncipe, a two-island state in the Gulf of Guinea that the United Nations places in Middle Africa and credits with 240,000 people on 964 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 80,100 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts, and spells without its accents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 210,187 for that year the capital held about 38 per cent of the country, well short of the urban share of 73.6 per cent recorded for 2019, so most town dwellers live outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the city is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, though the two national totals agree, its 240,254 for 2025 matching the profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The areas agree once rounded, the World Bank's flat 960 square kilometres being the profile's 964 to the nearest ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The population is among the fastest growing in the collection, up a fifth in a decade from 200,291 in 2015, so the capital figure is measured against a considerably smaller country than today’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The islands were under Portuguese sovereignty until 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Democratic Republic of São Tomé and Príncipe on 12 July 1975, when President Gerald R. Ford sent a letter to that effect to its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 10 October 1975 by an exchange of diplomatic notes, and the guide records no American embassy, consulate or resident ambassador in the capital at any point since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 16 September 1975, two months after that recognition and three weeks before those relations began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sao Tome and Principe,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/st.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Sao Tome and Principe,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/sao-tome-and-principe. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Sao Tome and Principe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/STP/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sao Tome and Principe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/STP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Sao Tome and Principe,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/STP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Cidade_de_S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9_%284238867188%29.jpg/1280px-Cidade_de_S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9_%284238867188%29.jpg","title":"A square in central São Tomé, 2010","desc":"Two-storey colonial buildings in ochre and pink around an open square, with a market arcade at the right.","credit":"Photograph by Maria Cartas, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cidade_de_S%C3%A3o_Tom%C3%A9_%284238867188%29.jpg","alt":"A square in São Tomé lined with faded two-storey colonial buildings with shuttered windows and balconies, cars parked under a large tree."}},
{"id":"gw-684","num":684,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Apia","answerText":"Apia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 May 1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 December 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 November 1988</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Samoa","dot":"Apia"},"facts":[["Country","Samoa"],["Population","36.1k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","samoa","polynesia"],"abstract":"<b>Apia</b> is the capital of Samoa, a Polynesian country the United Nations credits with 219,000 people on 2,842 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 36,100 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 207,582 for that year the capital held about 17 per cent of the country, and the profile's urban share is 18.1 per cent for 2019, so almost everyone counted as urban is counted at Apia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the city is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, though the two national totals agree, its 219,306 for 2025 matching the profile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The areas agree once rounded, the World Bank's flat 2,840 square kilometres being the profile's 2,842 to the nearest ten; the Commonwealth Secretariat rounds to 2,800 and puts the population at 198,400 for 2022, about eight per cent below the World Bank's figure for that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> American merchants called at the islands from the first half of the 19th century, and the United States had a commercial agent at Apia as early as 1844.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its first consulate there opened on 17 May 1856, when Jonathan S. Jenkins took up the post of consul, and that office closed on 23 September 1927.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Samoa had been a trust territory administered by New Zealand, and the United States recognised the independence of the State of Western Samoa on 1 January 1962, when Senator Oren Long delivered a congratulatory message from President John F. Kennedy to its heads of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations began at Apia on 14 July 1971, and an American embassy was established in the city on 15 November 1988 — but the ambassador has always remained resident at Wellington, so a chargé d’affaires has led it throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country changed its official name to the Independent State of Samoa in 1997, joined the Commonwealth in 1970 and the United Nations on 15 December 1976, fourteen years after independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Samoa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ws.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Samoa,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/samoa. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Samoa,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/samoa. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/WSM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/WSM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/WSM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Main_drag%2C_Apia_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Main_drag%2C_Apia_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Beach Road in central Apia, 2007","desc":"The main street with the clock tower at the centre of town, low shopfronts along it and wooded hills behind.","credit":"Photograph by Peter Gill, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_drag%2C_Apia_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A street in central Apia lined with low shops and palms, a white clock tower rising beside a church, and green hills under cloud behind the town."}},
{"id":"gw-685","num":685,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Castries","answerText":"Castries","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1815</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Associated state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 February 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saint Lucia","dot":"Castries"},"facts":[["Country","Saint Lucia"],["Population","22.3k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","saint lucia","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Castries</b> is the capital of Saint Lucia, a Caribbean island state the United Nations credits with 180,000 people on 616 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 22,300 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 177,017 for that year the capital held about 13 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 18.8 per cent for 2019, so roughly two thirds of the town-dwelling population lives at Castries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the city is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the national figures barely move, from 174,905 in 2015 to 180,149 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The three sources agree on the land and part on the people: the profile and the Commonwealth Secretariat both give 616 square kilometres and the World Bank a flat 620, the same figure to the nearest ten, while the Commonwealth puts the population at 183,630 for 2022 against the World Bank’s 178,781.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> British colonial rule was established on the island in 1815, and it took part in the West Indies Federation from 1958 until that collapsed in 1962.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With the other Windward Islands it became a state associated with the United Kingdom in 1967, and achieved full independence on 22 February 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States had recognised it informally as an associated state and did not do so formally until 17 May 1979, when Sally Angela Shelton was appointed ambassador; relations followed on 11 June, when she presented her credentials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No American mission has ever stood in the capital: the guide states that the United States maintains no official presence in the country, and that every ambassador to Saint Lucia has been resident at Barbados and travels over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth on independence in 1979 and the United Nations on 18 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Lucia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lc.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Lucia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-lucia. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Saint Lucia,” https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/saint-lucia. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: St. Lucia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LCA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Lucia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LCA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: St. Lucia,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LCA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/St._Lucia%2C_Karibik_-_View_from_the_Colonial_House_of_St._Marks_on_the_Port_of_Castries_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-St._Lucia%2C_Karibik_-_View_from_the_Colonial_House_of_St._Marks_on_the_Port_of_Castries_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Castries and its harbour, 2012","desc":"The town around its inner harbour, with a cruise berth on the left, the container terminal on the right and the hills behind.","credit":"Photograph by giggel, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Lucia%2C_Karibik_-_View_from_the_Colonial_House_of_St._Marks_on_the_Port_of_Castries_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"An elevated view over Castries: a sheltered harbour with cruise ships and a container quay, the low town spreading around it and green hills beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-686","num":686,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Hagåtña","answerText":"Hagåtña","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Governor since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 2019</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Delegate since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 2022</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Working paper</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 2026</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guam","dot":"Hagåtña"},"facts":[["Country","Guam"],["Population","147k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","guam","micronesia"],"abstract":"<b>Hagåtña</b> is the capital of Guam, a United States territory the United Nations places in Micronesia and credits with 169,000 people on 541 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 146,900 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 162,679 for that year that is about ninety per cent of the territory, and the profile's urban share is 94.9 per cent for 2019, so the figure plainly covers most of the island rather than the seat of government alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the city is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the territory's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The national totals disagree by more than rounding: the World Bank gives 162,158 for 2020 where the Secretariat’s working paper reports the 2020 census count of 153,836, and its series falls from 164,905 in 2015 to 161,808 in 2019 before rising again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Guam is a Non-Self-Governing Territory administered by the United States, an unincorporated organized territory whose administration falls to the Office of Insular Affairs in the Department of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the southernmost and largest of the Mariana Islands, about 2,200 kilometres south of Tokyo, with an exclusive economic zone of 214,059 square kilometres and a land area the paper gives as 544 square kilometres against the profile’s 541 and the World Bank’s flat 540.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its revenue comes principally from tourism and the United States military, and Apra Harbor, one of the largest deep-water ports in the Pacific, takes more than nine tenths of all goods reaching the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lourdes Leon Guerrero has headed the territorial government since January 2019, and the island elects a delegate to the United States Congress and a unicameral legislature of fifteen members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its legislature gave the Guam Electoral Commission power in 2000 to set a date for a plebiscite on statehood, independence or free association; the vote was scheduled for 2 November 2004 and postponed because eligible voters had not been identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Guam,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gu.html. [Open access]","UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “Guam: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/9, 16 March 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4107679. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Guam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Guam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Guam,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GUM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Hagatna_from_Fort_Santa_Agueda.JPG/1280px-Hagatna_from_Fort_Santa_Agueda.JPG","title":"Hagåtña from Fort Santa Águeda, 2010","desc":"The town below the old Spanish fort, its boat basin at the left and the bay running north past the hotel strip.","credit":"Photograph by Abasaa, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagatna_from_Fort_Santa_Agueda.JPG","alt":"Hagåtña seen from a hillside fort: low white buildings and green trees along a bay, a small boat harbour on the left and taller hotels in the distance."}},
{"id":"gw-687","num":687,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Willemstad","answerText":"Willemstad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 2010</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Curaçao","dot":"Willemstad"},"facts":[["Country","Curaçao"],["Population","144k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","curacao","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Willemstad</b> is the capital of Curaçao, a Caribbean country of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, which the United Nations credits with 186,000 people on 444 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 144,000 people, and the profile says outright what that covers: the total population of Curaçao excluding some neighbourhoods, footnoted to 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The field is therefore the island less a few districts rather than the town itself, which sits beside an urban share of 89.1 per cent for 2019 and no independent count, the World Bank publishing no series for the country’s largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two population estimates diverge sharply: the World Bank gives 156,263 for 2025 against the profile’s 186,000, about a fifth apart, and its series falls from 160,175 in 2017 to 153,321 in 2022 before rising again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The areas agree exactly, the profile and the World Bank both giving 444 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Curaçao became an autonomous country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands on 10 October 2010, when the Netherlands Antilles were dissolved and Bonaire, St Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each country of the Kingdom has its own government, and Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten each have a Governor who heads it and represents the King.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That Governor has no ministerial responsibility and takes no part in the day-to-day business of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Charter for the Kingdom of the Netherlands the Kingdom as a whole answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, while the individual countries set their own policy in fields such as healthcare, tourism and employment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dutch foreign policy is therefore made for the whole Kingdom, and the embassies and consulates of the Netherlands serve all four of its countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Curaçao,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cw.html. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Governance of Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/governance-of-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Responsibilities of the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/responsibilities-of-the-netherlands-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Curacao,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUW/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Curacao,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Curacao,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CUW/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Curacao_Willemstad_%2836699795245%29.jpg/1920px-Curacao_Willemstad_%2836699795245%29.jpg","title":"The Handelskade at Willemstad, 2017","desc":"The row of gabled merchant houses along the Sint Annabaai, seen from the water at the end of the afternoon.","credit":"Photograph by dronepicr, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curacao_Willemstad_%2836699795245%29.jpg","alt":"Willemstad's waterfront seen across the water: a line of tall Dutch-gabled houses in pink, yellow, orange and blue along a quay under a wide sky."}},
{"id":"gw-688","num":688,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tarawa","answerText":"Tarawa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 July 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 August 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 September 1999</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Kiribati","dot":"Tarawa"},"facts":[["Country","Kiribati"],["Population","3.20kat Bairiki (2015)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+24.5% (2010–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","kiribati","micronesia"],"abstract":"<b>Tarawa</b> is the capital of Kiribati, an atoll country the United Nations places in Micronesia and credits with 136,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sources do not agree on what to call it: the Commonwealth Secretariat names Tarawa, while the United Nations profile names Bairiki and gives that 3,200 people, a figure footnoted to 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Office of Te Beretitenti, the president’s own office, settles the relation between the two by printing its postal address as Bairiki, Tarawa, so the profile is naming the seat of government inside the capital rather than the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The figure follows from that: 3,200 people against an urban share of 54.8 per cent for 2019, which on the World Bank’s 123,921 for that year is about 68,000, so here the capital field is a village where on other profiles it has been a whole island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No independent count of the town is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country’s largest urban agglomeration, and its national figure has grown by about a sixth in a decade, from 116,815 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Gilbert Islands became independent from Britain on 12 July 1979 as Kiribati, and the United States recognised the new republic on 20 September that year, when the two countries signed a treaty of friendship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They agreed to establish diplomatic relations on 12 August 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> William Bodde Jr was appointed Envoy Extraordinary and Minister Plenipotentiary on 26 September 1980 and was stationed at Suva in Fiji, the guide recording that no diplomatic facilities exist in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kiribati joined the Commonwealth on independence in 1979 but did not join the United Nations until 14 September 1999, twenty years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The profile records the urban share rising from 51.6 to 54.8 per cent and urban population growing at 3.5 per cent a year, on a country whose land is 811 square kilometres by the Commonwealth’s reckoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Kiribati,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ki.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Kiribati,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/kiribati. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Kiribati,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kiribati. [Open access]","Office of Te Beretitenti, Republic of Kiribati, https://www.president.gov.ki/. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Kiribati,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KIR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Kiribati,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KIR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/South_Tarawa_from_the_air.jpg/1920px-South_Tarawa_from_the_air.jpg","title":"South Tarawa from the air, 2005","desc":"The strip of settlement between the lagoon and the ocean, houses and palms along the reef with the surf breaking on the seaward side.","credit":"Photograph by an employee of the Government of Kiribati, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Tarawa_from_the_air.jpg","alt":"An aerial view along South Tarawa: a narrow ribbon of land carrying houses and palms, a pale turquoise lagoon on one side and ocean surf on the other."}},
{"id":"gw-689","num":689,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Victoria","answerText":"Victoria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 June 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 1996</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reopened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Seychelles","dot":"Victoria"},"facts":[["Country","Seychelles"],["Population","28.1k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-1.1% (2002–2014)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","seychelles","africa"],"abstract":"<b>Victoria</b> is the capital of Seychelles, an island country the United Nations places in Eastern Africa and credits with 133,000 people on 457 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city is given 28,100 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 96,762 for that year the capital held about 29 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 57.1 per cent for 2019, so roughly half the town-dwelling population lives there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No independent count of the city is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the areas agree once rounded, its flat 460 square kilometres being the profile's 457 to the nearest ten, where the Commonwealth Secretariat gives 452.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The population series has a step in it: the World Bank shows 99,258 for 2021 and 119,878 for 2022, a rise of a fifth in one year, and the Commonwealth’s figure of 98,462 for 2022 is the World Bank’s value for 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Seychelles became independent from Britain on 29 June 1976, and the United States recognised it the same day by signing an agreement on establishing, operating and maintaining a tracking and telemetry facility on the island of Mahé.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide records that the agreement was signed at the capital, Victoria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations followed on 1 July 1976, when the American consulate at Victoria was raised to embassy rank under Gregory Matson as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That embassy closed on 30 August 1996 and Seychelles was handled from the American embassy in Mauritius for the next twenty-seven years, until the mission at Victoria reopened on 1 July 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth on independence in 1976 and the United Nations on 21 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Seychelles,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sc.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Seychelles,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/seychelles. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Seychelles,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/seychelles. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Seychelles,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYC/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Seychelles,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYC/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Seychelles,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SYC/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Victoria_Sunrise_%2814625666105%29.jpg/1920px-Victoria_Sunrise_%2814625666105%29.jpg","title":"Victoria at sunrise, 2014","desc":"The town along the shore of Mahé with ships at anchor off the port, seen from the wooded ridge above it.","credit":"Photograph by Andrew Moore, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victoria_Sunrise_%2814625666105%29.jpg","alt":"Victoria seen from a hillside at dawn: a low town spread along a green coast, a harbour with ships offshore, and heavy cloud lit gold over the sea."}},
{"id":"gw-690","num":690,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint George's","answerText":"Saint George's","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 November 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 February 1984</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Grenada","dot":"Saint George's"},"facts":[["Country","Grenada"],["Population","39.3kin Saint George Parish (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","grenada","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Saint George's</b> is the capital of Grenada, a Caribbean island state the United Nations credits with 117,000 people on 345 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 39,300 people, and the profile says what that covers: the figure refers to Saint George Parish, footnoted to 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 115,691 for that year the parish held about 34 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 36.4 per cent for 2019, so the parish takes in nearly all the town-dwelling population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No count of the town itself is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the national population barely moves, from 114,676 in 2015 to 117,303 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The three areas agree closely, the profile giving 345 square kilometres, the Commonwealth Secretariat 344 and the World Bank a flat 340, while the Commonwealth puts the population at 112,519 for 2022 against the World Bank’s 116,913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Grenada became independent from the United Kingdom on 7 February 1974, and the United States recognised it on the day of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diplomatic relations were established on 29 November 1974, when the Grenadian ambassador Marie J. McIntyre presented her credentials to President Gerald R. Ford.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American diplomatic mission followed on 25 February 1975, when Ambassador Theodore R. Britton presented his credentials at Saint George's, though he was also ambassador to Barbados and resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide records that the United States maintained relations through the 1983 joint American and Organization of Eastern Caribbean States military invasion of the country, which followed the assassination of Prime Minister Maurice Bishop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American embassy was established in Saint George's on 2 February 1984, and the guide states that the ambassador remains resident at Bridgetown while a chargé d’affaires runs the embassy and reports to him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Grenada,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gd.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Grenada,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/grenada. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Grenada,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/grenada. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Grenada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRD/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Grenada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRD/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Grenada,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/The_City_of_St._George%27s%2C_Grenada_-_February_2020.jpg/1920px-The_City_of_St._George%27s%2C_Grenada_-_February_2020.jpg","title":"Saint George's in the late afternoon, 2020","desc":"The town climbing its hillsides above the harbour, with two church towers among the red roofs and the ridge of the interior behind.","credit":"Photograph by Martin Falbisoner, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_City_of_St._George%27s%2C_Grenada_-_February_2020.jpg","alt":"Saint George's seen from above: densely packed houses with red and orange roofs rising up steep green slopes, two church towers standing out, mountains behind."}},
{"id":"gw-691","num":691,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Palikir","answerText":"Palikir","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Representative</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1987</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1989</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Micronesia","dot":"Palikir"},"facts":[["Country","Federated States of Micronesia"],["Population","7.00k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","micronesia","pacific"],"abstract":"<b>Palikir</b> is the capital of the Federated States of Micronesia, a country the United Nations places in Micronesia and credits with 114,000 people on 702 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 7,000 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 109,909 for that year that is about six per cent of the country, and the profile's urban share is 22.8 per cent for 2019, so only about a quarter of the town-dwelling population lives at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the two national totals agree, its 113,683 for 2025 against the profile's 114,000, on a population that has barely moved since 108,716 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The areas agree once rounded, the World Bank's flat 700 square kilometres being the profile's 702 to the nearest ten, on a country the profile records as 92.1 per cent forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were part of the United Nations Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands after the Second World War, administered by the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised their independence on 3 November 1986, when President Ronald Reagan noted in proclamation 5564 that it had fulfilled its obligations under the trusteeship agreement, so that the country left the trusteeship that day and a Compact of Free Association came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Office of the United States Representative opened the same day, and Michael G. Wygant presented his credentials on 2 October 1987.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That office was upgraded to embassy rank on 20 September 1989, and the first American ambassador, Aurelia Erskine Brazeal, presented her credentials on 18 September 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide records that the embassy stands at Kolonia rather than at the capital, and the country joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Micronesia (Federated States of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Micronesia,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/micronesia. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Micronesia, Fed. Sts.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FSM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Micronesia, Fed. Sts.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FSM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Micronesia, Fed. Sts.,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FSM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Palikir_Government_Buildings.jpg/1920px-Palikir_Government_Buildings.jpg","title":"The capitol complex at Palikir, 2025","desc":"The Congress and Supreme Court buildings of the Federated States of Micronesia, in pitched Micronesian style, along a palm avenue.","credit":"Photograph by MaxMonaco, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palikir_Government_Buildings.jpg","alt":"Low government buildings with steep pitched roofs facing each other across mown grass at Palikir, a paved avenue lined with palms running between them."}},
{"id":"gw-692","num":692,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Oranjestad","answerText":"Oranjestad","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town in 2018</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29,900 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aruba in 2019</span><span class=\"dt-v\">109,203 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aruba in 2022</span><span class=\"dt-v\">107,310 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aruba in 2025</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108,785 people</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Aruba","dot":"Oranjestad"},"facts":[["Country","Aruba"],["Population","29.9k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+23.6% (2010–2015)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","aruba","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Oranjestad</b> is the capital of Aruba, a Caribbean country of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, which the United Nations credits with 108,000 people on 180 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 29,900 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 108,908 for that year that is about 27 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 43.5 per cent for 2019, so roughly two thirds of the town-dwelling population lives at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the areas agree exactly, both giving 180 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population figures agree too, its 108,785 for 2025 against the profile’s 108,000, on a series that has barely moved in a decade and fell from 109,203 in 2019 to 107,310 in 2022 before rising again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Aruba is one of the four countries of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, each of which has its own government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten each have a Governor who heads that government and represents the King, without ministerial responsibility or any part in its day-to-day business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Charter for the Kingdom of the Netherlands the Kingdom as a whole answers for foreign relations, defence and Dutch nationality law, and Dutch embassies and consulates serve all four countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Netherlands keeps a single Representation across the three Caribbean countries, with its headquarters at Willemstad in Curaçao and offices at Oranjestad and Philipsburg, each of the latter headed by a Deputy Representative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island received 1,101,000 visitors in 2022, about ten for every resident, on a country the profile records as 2.3 per cent forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Aruba,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/aw.html. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Representation of the Netherlands in Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/representation-of-the-netherlands-in-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Governance of Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/governance-of-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Responsibilities of the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/responsibilities-of-the-netherlands-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Aruba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ABW/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Aruba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ABW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Aruba,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ABW/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/View_from_above_of_colorful_buildings_in_Oranjestad_on_the_island_of_Aruba_in_the_morning_sun.jpg/1920px-View_from_above_of_colorful_buildings_in_Oranjestad_on_the_island_of_Aruba_in_the_morning_sun.jpg","title":"The Oranjestad waterfront, 2011","desc":"The gabled shopfronts along the harbour, fishing boats at the pier and the low town running inland behind them.","credit":"Photograph by Navigator334, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_above_of_colorful_buildings_in_Oranjestad_on_the_island_of_Aruba_in_the_morning_sun.jpg","alt":"Oranjestad seen from above: a row of pastel Dutch-gabled buildings along a quay, small boats moored at a jetty and low red-roofed houses spreading inland."}},
{"id":"gw-693","num":693,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Charlotte Amalie","answerText":"Charlotte Amalie","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1754 – 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Transferred</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1917</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Organic Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 2026</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"U.S. Virgin Is.","dot":"Charlotte Amalie"},"facts":[["Country","United States Virgin Islands"],["Population","52.3k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","caribbean","united states"],"abstract":"<b>Charlotte Amalie</b> is the capital of the United States Virgin Islands, a Caribbean territory the United Nations credits with 84,000 people on 347 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 52,300 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts, and the Secretariat’s working paper places it on Saint Thomas, one of four main islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two totals are a quarter apart: the World Bank gives 103,792 for 2025 against the profile’s 84,000, and the working paper reports 104,377 for 2024, the World Bank’s figure for that year exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against the World Bank’s 107,001 for 2018 the capital holds about 49 per cent of the territory, not the 62 per cent the profile’s own figures would give, on an urban share of 95.8 per cent for 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the city is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the territory’s largest urban agglomeration, and the areas agree closely, its flat 350 square kilometres beside the profile’s 347 and the paper’s 352 of land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The islands belonged to Denmark from 1754 to 1917 as the Danish West Indies, and Denmark transferred them to the United States on 31 March 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They have been an organized, unincorporated territory ever since, and are a Non-Self-Governing Territory under the United Nations Charter, administered by the Office of Insular Affairs in the Department of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is governed under the Organic Act of the Virgin Islands of 1936, its revision in 1954 and later amendments, with a governor, a delegate to Congress and a legislature of fifteen senators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A sixth constitutional convention is drafting a constitution the administering Power expects to go to a referendum in November 2026, which would become the territory’s fundamental law on 31 March 2027 if a majority approved it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tourism is the principal industry, accounting for 80 per cent of output and employment, and visitor arrivals reached about 2.6 million in 2024, 8.9 per cent more than in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “United States Virgin Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vi.html. [Open access]","UN Special Committee on Decolonization, “United States Virgin Islands: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2026/16, 2026, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/4111024. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Virgin Islands (U.S.),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VIR/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Virgin Islands (U.S.),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VIR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Virgin Islands (U.S.),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VIR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Downtown_Charlotte_Amalie.jpg/1920px-Downtown_Charlotte_Amalie.jpg","title":"Charlotte Amalie from Bluebeard's Castle, 2025","desc":"The waterfront road curving round the harbour, Fort Christian in red at the centre and the town climbing the hillside behind.","credit":"Photograph by Calettso2, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Charlotte_Amalie.jpg","alt":"Charlotte Amalie seen from a hill: a palm-lined shore road round a green harbour, a red stone fort among red-roofed buildings, houses rising up the slope behind."}},
{"id":"gw-694","num":694,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nuku'alofa","answerText":"Nuku'alofa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1886</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1900</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 June 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 May 2023</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tonga","dot":"Nuku'alofa"},"facts":[["Country","Tonga"],["Population","22.9k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tonga","polynesia"],"abstract":"<b>Nuku'alofa</b> is the capital of Tonga, a Polynesian kingdom the United Nations credits with 104,000 people on 747 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 22,900 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 105,567 for that year that is about 22 per cent of the country, and the profile's urban share is 23.1 per cent for 2019, so almost everyone counted as urban is counted at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the three areas agree closely, the profile's 747 square kilometres beside the Commonwealth Secretariat's 748 and the World Bank's 750.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The population is falling, from 106,095 in 2015 to 103,742 in 2025 by the World Bank's count, and the Commonwealth's 105,690 for 2022 sits within a percentage point of that series.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Tongan islands were united in 1845 under King George Tupou I and became a constitutional monarchy in 1875.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised Tonga on 2 October 1886, when it and the King signed a Treaty of Amity, Commerce and Navigation at Nuku'alofa; the treaty came into force in 1888 and was terminated on 28 July 1920, apart from the article letting each country's warships enter the other's ports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A first American consul-general to Nuku'alofa was appointed on 30 June 1897, but the post was resident at Apia in Samoa and closed in 1901, Tongan external affairs having passed to the British Foreign Office under a treaty of friendship and protectorship in 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tonga withdrew from that agreement on 4 June 1970 and joined the Commonwealth, and the United States recognised its independence on 4 October 1972 by accrediting to it the ambassador already serving New Zealand, Fiji and Western Samoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From 1979 the ambassador to Fiji was accredited to Tonga and resident at Suva, until an American embassy opened at Nuku'alofa on 9 May 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Tonga,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/to.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tonga,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tonga. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Tonga,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/tonga. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Tonga,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TON/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tonga,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TON/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Tonga,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TON/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Nukualofa_Tonga_2.jpg/1920px-Nukualofa_Tonga_2.jpg","title":"Central Nuku'alofa, 2016","desc":"A corner of the town centre with the post office and shopfronts under a broad rain tree.","credit":"Photograph by Arne Müseler, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nukualofa_Tonga_2.jpg","alt":"A street corner in central Nuku'alofa: low shops and a post office building beside a wide spreading tree, with parked cars and people crossing the road."}},
{"id":"gw-696","num":696,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Kingstown","answerText":"Kingstown","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1763</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 October 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 September 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 November 1981</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Vin. and Gren.","dot":"Kingstown"},"facts":[["Country","Saint Vincent and the Grenadines"],["Population","26.6k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-6.8% (2001–2012)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","saint vincent and the grenadines","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Kingstown</b> is the capital of Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, a Caribbean island state the United Nations credits with 100,000 people on 389 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 26,600 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 104,926 for that year that is about 25 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 52.6 per cent for 2019, so about half the town-dwelling population lives at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the areas agree once rounded, the World Bank and the Commonwealth Secretariat both giving 390 square kilometres beside the profile's 389.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is falling, from 106,960 in 2015 to 99,924 in 2025 by the World Bank's count, where the Commonwealth gives 110,900 for 2022 against that series' 102,046 for the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Saint Vincent was ceded to Great Britain in 1763 and was under French rule from 1779 until 1796, after which it passed through several colonial and Commonwealth stages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Following a referendum it became the last of the Windward Islands to reach independence, on 27 October 1979, and it joined the United Nations on 16 September 1980.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States had recognised it informally as a federated state and did so formally only on 23 November 1981, when Milan D. Bish was appointed ambassador to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Relations were established on 8 December 1981, when the Vincentian ambassador Hudson Kemul Tannis presented his credentials and Bish presented his at Bridgetown the same day, being concurrently ambassador to Antigua and Barbuda, Barbados, Dominica and Saint Lucia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The guide states that the United States maintains no official presence in the country, its ambassador and embassy officers being resident in Barbados and travelling over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Vincent and the Grenadines,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vc.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Vincent and the Grenadines,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-vincent-grenadines. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “St Vincent and the Grenadines,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/st-vincent-and-grenadines. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: St. Vincent and the Grenadines,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VCT/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Vincent and the Grenadines,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VCT/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: St. Vincent and the Grenadines,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VCT/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Kingstown_Saint_Vincent.jpg/1920px-Kingstown_Saint_Vincent.jpg","title":"A street in central Kingstown, 2006","desc":"Arcaded shopfronts along a street sloping down to the water, with stalls under umbrellas on the pavement.","credit":"Photograph by ctsnow, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingstown_Saint_Vincent.jpg","alt":"A downtown street in Kingstown lined with two-storey buildings over stone arcades, cars and pedestrians on the road and market stalls on the pavement."}},
{"id":"gw-697","num":697,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint John's","answerText":"Saint John's","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 January 1982</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy closed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1994</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Antigua and Barb.","dot":"Saint John's"},"facts":[["Country","Antigua and Barbuda"],["Population","20.8k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","antigua and barbuda","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Saint John's</b> is the capital of Antigua and Barbuda, a Caribbean state the United Nations credits with 94,000 people on 442 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 20,800 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 90,926 for that year that is about 23 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 24.5 per cent for 2019, so nearly the whole town-dwelling population lives at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and its total is rising, from 89,409 in 2015 to 94,209 in 2025, against the Commonwealth Secretariat's 97,928 for 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The areas reconcile once the islands are added, the Secretariat giving Antigua 280 square kilometres, Barbuda 161 and Redonda 1.6, which sum to the profile's single 442, where the World Bank's series is flat at 440.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Antigua and Barbuda became independent within the Commonwealth on 1 November 1981, with Queen Elizabeth II as its first queen and Vere Cornwall Bird as its first prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day, raising its consulate general at Saint John's to embassy status under Paul Byrnes as principal officer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Milan D. Bish presented his credentials as the first ambassador on 8 January 1982, resident at Bridgetown in Barbados rather than at the capital he was accredited to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The embassy at Saint John's closed on 30 June 1994, since when every ambassador has remained resident in Barbados, a consular presence being kept in the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country joined the Commonwealth in the year of its independence and the United Nations ten days after it, on 11 November 1981.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Antigua and Barbuda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ag.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Antigua and Barbuda,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/antigua-barbuda. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Antigua and Barbuda,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/antigua-and-barbuda. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Antigua and Barbuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ATG/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Antigua and Barbuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ATG/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Antigua and Barbuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ATG/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/St_John%27s_-_Townscape_seen_from_the_Port_C_IMG_0505.JPG/1920px-St_John%27s_-_Townscape_seen_from_the_Port_C_IMG_0505.JPG","title":"Saint John's seen from the harbour","desc":"Low roofs and palms rising from the quayside to the twin towers of the cathedral on the ridge behind, with yachts moored in the foreground.","credit":"Photograph by Anneli Salo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_John%27s_-_Townscape_seen_from_the_Port_C_IMG_0505.JPG","alt":"A Caribbean waterfront town of low roofs and palm trees, with a twin-towered cathedral on the hill behind and boats moored at the quay."}},
{"id":"gw-698","num":698,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Douglas","answerText":"Douglas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">City status</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 May 2022</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Isle of Man","dot":"Douglas"},"facts":[["Country","Isle of Man"],["Population","27.2k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","isle of man","europe"],"abstract":"<b>Douglas</b> is the capital of the Isle of Man, a Crown Dependency the United Nations counts separately from the United Kingdom and credits with 84,000 people on 572 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 27,200 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 83,805 for that year that is about a third of the island, where the profile's urban share is 52.7 per cent for 2019, so roughly three fifths of the town-dwelling population lives at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published by either body, the World Bank carrying no series for the island's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island's population has barely moved in a decade, from 83,593 in 2015 to 84,118 in 2025, and the areas agree once rounded, the World Bank giving 570 square kilometres against the profile's 572.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The town is the seat of Tynwald, the parliament of the island, which describes itself as of Norse origin and over 1,000 years old, and thus the oldest parliament in the world with an unbroken existence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its two branches, the Legislative Council and the House of Keys, sit separately to consider legislation and sit together in Douglas, and annually at St John's, for other parliamentary purposes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Crown Dependencies are not part of the United Kingdom but are self-governing dependencies of the Crown, with their own directly elected parliament, government and courts of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Douglas was granted city status on 20 May 2022 in the Platinum Jubilee Civic Honours Competition, which for the first time was open to applicants from the Crown Dependencies and Overseas Territories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> It became the first and only city on the Isle of Man and the first place in a Crown Dependency to win civic honours, taking the award alongside Stanley in the Falkland Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Isle of Man,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/im.html. [Open access]","Tynwald, “Tynwald — the Oldest Continuous Parliament in the World,” Parliament of the Isle of Man, https://www.tynwald.org.im/. [Open access]","His Majesty’s Passport Office, “Isle of Man: Knowledge Base Profile,” Home Office, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/isle-of-man-knowledge-base-profile/isle-of-man-knowledge-base-profile. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Isle of Man,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IMN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Isle of Man,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IMN/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Isle of Man,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IMN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Cabinet Office, “Record Number of City Status Winners Announced to Celebrate Platinum Jubilee,” 20 May 2022, https://www.gov.uk/government/news/record-number-of-city-status-winners-announced-to-celebrate-platinum-jubilee. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/The_View_From_Douglas_Head%2C_Isle_Of_Man..jpg/1920px-The_View_From_Douglas_Head%2C_Isle_Of_Man..jpg","title":"Douglas seen from Douglas Head","desc":"The town, its harbour and the sweep of its promenade seen across the water from the headland to the south, with the island's hills behind.","credit":"Photograph by Jim Linwood, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_View_From_Douglas_Head,_Isle_Of_Man..jpg","alt":"A seaside town along a curving bay, with a harbour and ferry berths in front of it and green hills rising behind."}},
{"id":"gw-699","num":699,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Andorra la Vella","answerText":"Andorra la Vella","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 March 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1993</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 October 1998</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Andorra","dot":"Andorra la Vella"},"facts":[["Country","Andorra"],["Population","22.6k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+6.7% (2020–2023)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","andorra","europe"],"abstract":"<b>Andorra la Vella</b> is the capital of Andorra, a Pyrenean state the United Nations credits with 83,000 people on 468 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 22,600 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 75,162 for that year that is about 30 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 88 per cent for 2019, so barely a third of the town-dwelling population lives at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published by either body, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and the areas agree once rounded, its 470 square kilometres against the profile's 468.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is rising quickly, from 72,174 in 2015 to 82,904 in 2025, a gain of about 15 per cent in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Andorra is a co-principality, the Bishop of Urgell in Spain serving as one co-prince and the French president as the other, an arrangement that dates back centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The recognition guide records no date of American recognition, since Andorra has considered itself independent since medieval times and so was already independent when the United States declared its own independence in 1776.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It ratified a modern constitution on 14 March 1993, providing for its independence and sovereignty as a representative democracy, and joined the United Nations on 28 July of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations with the United States were established on 21 February 1995 by an exchange of letters, the consul general at Barcelona being appointed to represent the United States to Andorra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Edward L. Romero presented his credentials as the first ambassador on 30 October 1998, and every ambassador to Andorra has also been accredited to Spain and resident in Madrid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Andorra,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ad.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Andorra,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/andorra. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Andorra,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AND/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Andorra,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AND/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area, sq. km: Andorra,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/AND/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2021:2023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Andorra_la_Vella_view_2012.JPG/1920px-Andorra_la_Vella_view_2012.JPG","title":"Andorra la Vella across its valley, 2012","desc":"Roofs and apartment blocks filling the valley floor, with houses climbing the wooded mountainside behind.","credit":"Photograph by Cherubino, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andorra_la_Vella_view_2012.JPG","alt":"A town filling a narrow mountain valley, its buildings running up the wooded slope behind."}},
{"id":"gw-700","num":700,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"George Town","answerText":"George Town","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">British control</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1670</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1730s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Separate colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1959</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cayman Is.","dot":"George Town"},"facts":[["Country","Cayman Islands"],["Population","34.9k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","cayman islands","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>George Town</b> is the capital of the Cayman Islands, a British Overseas Territory the United Nations counts separately from the United Kingdom and credits with 76,000 people on 264 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 34,900 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 65,941 for that year that is about 53 per cent of the territory, and the profile puts the urban share at 100 per cent for 2019, so its share of the town-dwelling population is the same figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published by either body, the World Bank carrying no series for the territory's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The population is rising steadily, from 61,642 in 2015 to 75,844 in 2025, a gain of about 23 per cent in a decade, and the two areas agree exactly at 264 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands remained largely uninhabited until the 17th century, and were settled by a mixture of pirates, refugees from the Spanish Inquisition, shipwrecked sailors, deserters from Oliver Cromwell's army in Jamaica, and slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Great Britain took formal control of them, along with Jamaica, under the Treaty of Madrid in 1670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Permanent settlement of the islands began in the 1730s, after several unsuccessful attempts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Administered at first as a dependency of Jamaica, they became a colony in their own right in 1959 and are now a self-governing British Overseas Territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Kingdom is responsible for their defence and external affairs, though important bilateral matters are often settled by negotiation between the Cayman government and foreign governments, the United States being the territory's most important foreign economic partner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cayman Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ky.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: The Cayman Islands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cayman-islands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Cayman Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Cayman Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Cayman Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CYM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/KY-Georgetown-01.jpg/1920px-KY-Georgetown-01.jpg","title":"The George Town waterfront","desc":"Low blue-roofed buildings along the harbour front, with tenders and a replica sailing ship in the water in front of them.","credit":"Photograph by Balou46, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KY-Georgetown-01.jpg","alt":"A low waterfront town of blue-roofed buildings seen across a harbour, with small boats and a replica sailing ship."}},
{"id":"gw-701","num":701,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Saint Peter Port","answerText":"Saint Peter Port","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In 1901</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18,264 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 1981</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15,587 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2023</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19,679 people</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guernsey","dot":"Saint Peter Port"},"facts":[["Country","Guernsey"],["Population","19.7k (2023)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","guernsey","europe"],"abstract":"<b>Saint Peter Port</b> is the capital of Guernsey, an island in the Channel Islands which with Alderney and Sark makes up one of the three Crown Dependencies of the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own government counts 19,679 people in the parish in March 2023, against 64,040 for Guernsey together with Herm and Jethou, so it holds about 31 per cent of the island's population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parish covers 6.5 square kilometres (2.5 square miles) of an island of 63 square kilometres (24.4 square miles), about a tenth of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No international body publishes figures for Guernsey alone, the World Bank reporting the Channel Islands as a single series that covers Jersey as well, at 168,466 for the two together in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The parish has barely grown in more than a century, standing at 18,264 in 1901, falling to 15,587 in 1981, and recovering since to its present figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Crown Dependencies are not part of the United Kingdom but are self-governing dependencies of the Crown, which means they have their own directly elected parliament, government and courts of law, though most rules and regulations are the same as the United Kingdom's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Channel Islands comprise the Bailiwick of Jersey and the Bailiwick of Guernsey, the latter including Herm, Sark and Alderney.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Kingdom's constitutional relationship with the three dependencies is managed by its Ministry of Justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Guernsey's own statistics count the island together with Herm and Jethou and leave out Alderney and Sark, so the figures they give describe the island rather than the whole bailiwick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Same-sex marriage became legal in Guernsey on 2 May 2017, in Alderney on 14 June 2018 and in Sark on 11 March 2020, one measure of how separately the three legislate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["States of Guernsey, Data and Analysis, “Facts and Figures Supplementary Information 2025,” https://www.gov.gg/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=194736&p=0. [Open access]","His Majesty’s Passport Office, “Guernsey, Alderney and Sark: Knowledge Base Profile,” Home Office, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guernsey-alderney-and-sark-knowledge-base-profile/guernsey-alderney-and-sark-knowledge-base-profile. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Channel Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Home Office, “Common Travel Area,” https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/common-travel-area/common-travel-area-accessible. [Open access]","Ministry of Justice, “Crown Dependencies: Jersey, Guernsey and the Isle of Man,” https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/crown-dependencies-jersey-guernsey-and-the-isle-of-man. [Open access]","States of Guernsey, “St Peter Port,” VisitGuernsey, https://www.visitguernsey.com/experiences/st-peter-port/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Saint_Peter_Port_Guernsey.jpg/1920px-Saint_Peter_Port_Guernsey.jpg","title":"Saint Peter Port from the harbour","desc":"The town rising in tiers behind its quay, with moored yachts in the foreground and towers on the skyline.","credit":"Photograph by Unukorno, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Peter_Port_Guernsey.jpg","alt":"A harbour town of pale terraced buildings climbing a slope above a quay, with small boats moored in front of it."}},
{"id":"gw-702","num":702,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Roseau","answerText":"Roseau","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 November 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ambassador</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Dominica","dot":"Roseau"},"facts":[["Country","Dominica"],["Population","14.9k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+1.1% (2001–2011)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","dominica","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Roseau</b> is the capital of Dominica, a Caribbean island state the United Nations credits with 66,000 people on 750 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 14,900 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 68,174 for that year that is about 22 per cent of the country, where the profile's urban share is 70.8 per cent for 2019, so under a third of the town-dwelling population lives at the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three sources agree exactly on the area at 750 square kilometres, and none of them publishes a second count of the town, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The population is falling, from 70,410 in 2015 to 65,871 in 2025 by the World Bank's count, where the Commonwealth Secretariat gives 72,000 for 2022 against that series' 66,826 for the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Great Britain granted independence to the Commonwealth of Dominica on 3 November 1978, the country joined the Commonwealth in the same year on independence from Britain, and it joined the United Nations six weeks after that, on 18 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised it the same day it became independent, its ambassador to Barbados, Frank V. Ortiz, Jr., attending the independence celebrations as personal representative of President Jimmy Carter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States established friendly bilateral relations with the former British colony, and maintained them as political discontent arising from long-standing domestic economic problems led to the establishment of an interim government the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sally Angela Shelton presented her credentials as the first American ambassador in 1979, at Bridgetown in Barbados, where she remained resident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States maintains no official presence in Dominica, its ambassador and embassy staff being resident at Bridgetown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Dominica,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/dm.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Dominica,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/dominica. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Dominica,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/dominica. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Dominica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DMA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Dominica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DMA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Dominica,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/DMA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Roseau_%28Dominica%29.jpg/1920px-Roseau_%28Dominica%29.jpg","title":"The Bayfront at Roseau","desc":"The sea wall and waterfront buildings of the capital, with the jetty running out into the bay and Dominica's mountains behind.","credit":"Photograph by Hans Hillewaert, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roseau_(Dominica).jpg","alt":"A seafront street of low buildings beside a stone sea wall, with a long jetty and steep green mountains behind."}},
{"id":"gw-703","num":703,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Hamilton","answerText":"Hamilton","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">City in 2010</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,032 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City in 2016</span><span class=\"dt-v\">854 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bermuda 2016</span><span class=\"dt-v\">63,779 people</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bermuda","dot":"Hamilton"},"facts":[["Country","Bermuda"],["Population","854 (2016 census)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","bermuda","north america"],"abstract":"<b>Hamilton</b> is the capital of Bermuda, a British Overseas Territory the United Nations counts separately from the United Kingdom and credits with 65,000 people on 54 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The profile gives the capital 10,100 people for 2018, but Bermuda's own census counts the City of Hamilton at 854 in 2016, down from 1,032 in 2010.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two figures describe different areas and the profile does not say which it means: the census reports nine parishes and two municipalities, and one of the parishes is separately named Hamilton, with 5,584 people, while the city itself is counted inside Pembroke, which had 11,160.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The census puts the whole territory at 63,779 on 20.68 square miles (53.6 square kilometres), close to the profile's 54, and the World Bank gives 63,449 for the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city holds about 1.3 per cent of the population on 0.28 square miles (0.73 square kilometres), at 3,050 people to the square mile against 3,084 for Bermuda as a whole, so it is no denser than the island around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The other municipality is the Town of St. George's, which had 1,527 people in 2016 within a parish of 5,659.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territory's population has been broadly flat, the census recording 63,779 in 2016 against 64,237 in 2010, and the World Bank 63,464 in 2015 against 64,555 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The World Bank publishes no series for the territory's largest urban agglomeration, so the census is the only count of the city itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Department of Statistics, Bermuda's official national statistics agency, conducts the census, which the United Nations encourages countries to hold at least every ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The 2016 report presents the final results of a census launched on Census Day, Friday 20 May 2016, and supersedes the preliminary results completed in December 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bermuda,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bm.html. [Open access]","Department of Statistics, <i>2016 Population and Housing Census Report</i> (Hamilton: Government of Bermuda, 2018), tables 3 and 4, https://www.gov.bm/files/media-library/20260413/fdce7ebe-2016_census_report.pdf. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Bermuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BMU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Bermuda,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BMU/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Government of Bermuda, “The Bermuda Census,” Department of Statistics, https://www.gov.bm/bermuda-census. [Open access]","Government of Bermuda, “2016 Population and Housing Census Report,” press release, 2 May 2018, https://www.gov.bm/articles/2016-population-and-housing-census-report. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/City_of_Hamilton%2C_from_North_Warwick_Parish%2C_Bermuda.jpg/1920px-City_of_Hamilton%2C_from_North_Warwick_Parish%2C_Bermuda.jpg","title":"The City of Hamilton across the harbour","desc":"The city's low waterfront seen from Warwick Parish, with the cathedral tower above the roofs and a cruise ship at the dock.","credit":"Photograph by R Danny A, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Hamilton,_from_North_Warwick_Parish,_Bermuda.jpg","alt":"A small waterfront city of pale low buildings seen across open blue water, with a church tower and a cruise ship at its quay."}},
{"id":"gw-704","num":704,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Nuuk","answerText":"Nuuk","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Danish colony</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1721</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Home rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Self-government</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 June 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Greenland","dot":"Nuuk"},"facts":[["Country","Greenland"],["Population","20.3k (2026)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+4.1% (2017–2020)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","greenland","north america"],"abstract":"<b>Nuuk</b> is the capital of Greenland, a self-governing region within the Kingdom of Denmark and the largest island in the world, whose total area of 2,166,086 square kilometres includes only 410,449 free of ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Statistics Greenland counted 20,298 people in the town on 1 January 2026 against 56,740 for the whole country, so more than a third of Greenlanders live in the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That share has been rising steadily, from 17,316 of 55,847 in 2016, because the town has grown by about 17 per cent in a decade while the country has grown by less than two, a flatness the World Bank's own series confirms at 56,114 in 2015 against 56,831 in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations gives the capital 18,400 for 2018 where the register gives 17,796 on 1 January of that year, a difference of about 3 per cent, and it puts the country's urban share at 87.1 per cent for 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Greenland has the world's lowest population density, 0.1 people to the square kilometre over the whole island and 0.3 counting the ice-free areas alone, which is also the figure the World Bank reports as the country's surface area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Greenland became a Danish colony in 1721, a Danish county in 1953, and was granted home rule in 1979.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Self-government was established on 21 June 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It entered the European Communities with Denmark in 1973 and, after a referendum in 1982, left in 1985.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> All towns and settlements lie along the coastline, no roads run between them, and travel is by sea or air only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the population lives on the southern west coast, Nuuk among them, and since the World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration the national register is the only running count of the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Greenland,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/gl.html. [Open access]","Statistics Greenland, <i>Greenland in Figures 2026</i> (Nuuk: Statistics Greenland, 2026), https://stat.gl/publ/en/GF/2026/pdf/Greenland%20in%20Figures%202026.pdf. [Open access]","Statistics Greenland, “Population in Localities January 1st 1977-2026 [BEESTD],” StatBank Greenland, https://bank.stat.gl/pxweb/en/Greenland/Greenland__BE__BE01__BE0120/BEXSTD.px/. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Greenland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Greenland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRL/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Greenland,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/GRL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Nuuk%2C_Greenland_skyline.jpg/1920px-Nuuk%2C_Greenland_skyline.jpg","title":"Nuuk from the rocks above the old harbour","desc":"The red timber Church of Our Saviour in front of the town's coloured houses and apartment blocks, with snow-streaked mountains behind.","credit":"Photograph by Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuuk,_Greenland_skyline.jpg","alt":"An Arctic town of brightly painted timber houses and apartment blocks on bare rock, with a red wooden church in front and snowy mountains behind."}},
{"id":"gw-705","num":705,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Tórshavn","answerText":"Tórshavn","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Town 2018</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,116 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Town 2026</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14,200 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Faroes 2026</span><span class=\"dt-v\">54,902 people</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Faeroe Is.","dot":"Tórshavn"},"facts":[["Country","Faroe Islands"],["Population","14.2k (2026)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+14.2% (2011–2024)"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","faroe islands","europe"],"abstract":"<b>Tórshavn</b> is the capital of the Faroe Islands, a self-governing nation under the external sovereignty of the Kingdom of Denmark, which the United Nations counts separately from Denmark and credits with 56,000 people on 1,393 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The profile gives the capital 20,800 for 2018, where Statistics Faroe Islands counted 13,116 in the town itself on 1 January of that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difference is a definition rather than an error: the register puts Tórshavn municipality at 21,100 on the same date, within about one per cent of the profile's figure, so what the profile calls the capital is the municipality and not the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That municipality takes in seventeen other settlements, among them Hoyvík, Argir, Kirkjubøur and Kollafjørður and the islands of Nólsoy, Hestur and Koltur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By January 2026 the town had grown to 14,200 against 54,902 for the whole country, so it holds about 26 per cent of the population where the municipality holds nearly 43.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The profile's urban share for the Faroes is 42.2 per cent for 2019, which is close to the municipality's own share of the country and far above the town's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank publishes no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, so the national register is the only running count of either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The population is rising steadily, the World Bank giving 48,898 for 2015 against 54,900 for 2025, a gain of about 12 per cent in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The register shows the town rising with it, from 12,761 in January 2016 to 14,200 in January 2026, so its share of the country has stayed near a quarter throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those figures are published monthly rather than annually, and by July 2026 they stood at 14,400 for the town and 55,505 for the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Faroe Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fo.html. [Open access]","Statistics Faroe Islands, “IB01031 Population by sex, age and village/city (1985M01-2026M07),” StatBank Faroe Islands, https://statbank.hagstova.fo/pxweb/en/H2/H2__IB__IB01/fo_abgd_md_t.px/. [Open access]","Statistics Faroe Islands, “IB01070 Average age 1st January by sex and municipality and village (1985-2026),” StatBank Faroe Islands, https://statbank.hagstova.fo/pxweb/en/H2/H2__IB__IB01/fo_midalal.px/. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Faroe Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FRO/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: Faroe Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/FRO/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Government of the Faroe Islands, “The Big Picture,” Føroya Landsstýri, https://www.faroeislands.fo/the-big-picture/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Vue_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale_de_T%C3%B3rshavn_%282%29.jpg/1920px-Vue_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale_de_T%C3%B3rshavn_%282%29.jpg","title":"Tórshavn and its harbour","desc":"The town's painted and turf-roofed houses running down to a harbour full of ships, with the island of Nólsoy across the sound behind.","credit":"Photograph by Jean-Pierre Bazard, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale_de_T%C3%B3rshavn_(2).jpg","alt":"A North Atlantic town of small painted houses sloping down to a working harbour, with a long flat-topped island across the water behind."}},
{"id":"gw-706","num":706,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Basseterre","answerText":"Basseterre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1623</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 September 1983</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 September 1983</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 August 1988</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Kitts and Nevis","dot":"Basseterre"},"facts":[["Country","Saint Kitts and Nevis"],["Population","14.4k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","saint kitts and nevis","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Basseterre</b> is the capital of Saint Kitts and Nevis, a two-island Caribbean federation the United Nations credits with 47,000 people on 261 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capital is given 14,400 people, a figure the profile footnotes to 2018 without saying what area it counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against the World Bank's 47,008 for that year that is about 31 per cent of the country, and the profile's urban share is 30.8 per cent for 2019, so on these figures the capital is very nearly the whole of the town-dwelling population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No second count of the town is published, the World Bank carrying no series for the country's largest urban agglomeration, and its area of 260 square kilometres agrees with the profile's 261 where the Commonwealth Secretariat's per-island figures, 176 for St Kitts and 93 for Nevis, sum to 269.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The population is almost flat, the World Bank giving 47,087 for 2015 against 46,922 for 2025, where the Commonwealth gives 53,000 for 2022 against that series' 46,709 for the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were the site of Great Britain's first Caribbean settlement, in 1623, and after centuries of British and French colonial rule they became, together with Anguilla, a self-governing state in association with Great Britain in 1967.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country declared independence on 19 September 1983 and the United States recognised it the same day, Milan D. Bish heading the American delegation at the ceremonies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It joined the United Nations four days later, on 23 September, and the Commonwealth in the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was known as St. Christopher and Nevis until 3 August 1988, and its first American ambassador, Thomas H. Anderson, Jr., had presented his credentials on 22 August 1984.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States maintains no official presence there: every ambassador has been resident at Bridgetown in Barbados, and a consular agent living in nearby Antigua assists American citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Kitts and Nevis,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kn.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Saint Kitts and Nevis,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/saint-kitts-nevis. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “St Kitts and Nevis,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/st-kitts-and-nevis. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: St. Kitts and Nevis,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KNA/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Kitts and Nevis,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KNA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: St. Kitts and Nevis,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/KNA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Basseterre_from_sea_2.JPG/1920px-Basseterre_from_sea_2.JPG","title":"Basseterre from the sea","desc":"The town's low painted houses and tin roofs behind its sea wall, with the green volcanic ridge of St Kitts rising into cloud behind.","credit":"Photograph by Gertjan R., CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basseterre_from_sea_2.JPG","alt":"A Caribbean town of low painted houses and tin roofs seen from the water, with a green mountain ridge behind it under cloud."}},
{"id":"gw-707","num":707,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Pago Pago","answerText":"Pago Pago","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tutuila ceded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1900</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manu'a ceded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1904</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Swains added</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1967</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"American Samoa","dot":"Pago Pago"},"facts":[["Country","American Samoa"],["Population","3.00k (2020 census)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","american samoa","oceania"],"abstract":"<b>Pago Pago</b> is the capital of American Samoa, a United States territory the United Nations counts separately from the United States and credits with 46,000 people on 199 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The profile gives the capital 48,500 for 2018, which is more than the whole territory's present population, where the 2020 census of American Samoa counts Pago Pago village at 3,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The profile's figure is plainly an urban agglomeration rather than the village: it is about 95 per cent of the territory's population for 2018 and above even the 87.1 per cent the profile itself counts as urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the census's own reckoning Pago Pago is only the fifth largest village, behind Tafuna at 7,988, Nu'uuli at 4,991 and Ili'ili at 3,073.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank publishes no series for the territory's largest urban agglomeration, so the decennial census is the only count of any village.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That census puts the territory at 49,710 in 2020 against 55,519 in 2010, a fall of 10.5 per cent, and the World Bank's series carries it on to 46,029 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only the Western District grew, by 1.6 per cent, while the Eastern District fell by a quarter and the Manu'a District by more, and Swains Island went from seventeen people to none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> American Samoa became a United States territory by deed of cession from 1900, when the matai of Tutuila ceded the largest island; Manu'a followed in 1904 and Swains Island was added by act of Congress in 1925.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territory was run by the United States Navy until 1951 and has been under the Department of the Interior since 1956, and with no organic act to govern it the people adopted their own constitution in 1967 and first held constitutional elections in 1977.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American Samoans are United States nationals rather than citizens, and the territory controls its own immigration and border matters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “American Samoa,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/as.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “2020 Census of American Samoa: Population and Housing Unit Counts,” tables 1 and 2, https://www.census.gov/data/tables/2020/dec/2020-american-samoa.html. [Open access]","Office of Insular Affairs, “American Samoa,” U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.doi.gov/oia/islands/american-samoa. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in largest city: American Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ASM/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: American Samoa,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/ASM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/PagoPago_Harbor_NPS.jpg/1920px-PagoPago_Harbor_NPS.jpg","title":"Pago Pago Harbor from the ridge","desc":"The village spread along the shore of a deep flooded crater, with the port and its ships across the water and forested peaks behind.","credit":"Photograph by Tavita Togia, National Park Service, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PagoPago_Harbor_NPS.jpg","alt":"A Pacific village of small roofs scattered among trees along the shore of a deep blue bay, with steep forested mountains behind."}},
{"id":"gw-708","num":708,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Cockburn Town","answerText":"Cockburn Town","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Order of 2011</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 October 2012</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elections</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 November 2012</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Turks and Caicos Is.","dot":"Cockburn Town"},"facts":[["Country","Turks and Caicos Islands"],["Population","?"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","turks and caicos islands","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Cockburn Town</b> is the capital of the Turks and Caicos Islands, a British Overseas Territory the United Nations counts separately from the United Kingdom and credits with 47,000 people on 948 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That area is measured to the shoreline at low water for all the islands, a definition the profile states outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its figure for the capital is a hundred people and is footnoted to 2001, and its column header carries no year at all where other profiles give one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory's own Statistics Authority publishes population by island rather than by settlement: at the 2012 census Grand Turk, where the Governor's office stands, held 4,831 people against Providenciales' 23,769.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its indicators give 52,299 for 2025 and its own series 50,828 for 2024, against the World Bank's 46,855 and 46,535, so the national office runs about nine per cent above the international one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The population is rising fast, the World Bank giving 36,605 for 2015 against 46,855 for 2025, a gain of 28 per cent in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The two series agree closely as far as 2020, at 36,689 against 36,605 in 2015 and 44,518 against 44,386 in 2020, and diverge after it, the Authority's later figures being marked provisional.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Almost all the growth is on one island: Providenciales held 518 people in 1960, 13,021 in 2001 and 23,769 in 2012, when the whole territory held 31,458.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands are a British Overseas Territory whose Constitution Order of 2011 provides for an elected government made up of a ministerial cabinet and an elected House of Assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That constitution came into force on 15 October 2012, ending a three-year interim administration led by the United Kingdom, and elections followed on 9 November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Turks and Caicos Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tc.html. [Open access]","Turks and Caicos Islands Statistics Authority, “Population,” Social Statistics, https://gov.tc/stats/statistics/social/5-population. [Open access]","Turks and Caicos Islands Statistics Authority, “2025 Indicators,” https://gov.tc/stats/. [Open access]","Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office, “Governor’s Office Waterloo Grand Turk,” https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/the-governors-office-turk-and-caicos-islands. [Open access]","Foreign and Commonwealth Office, “FCO Minister Welcomes New Constitution for the Turks and Caicos Islands,” 15 October 2012, https://www.gov.uk/government/news/fco-minister-welcomes-new-constitution-for-the-turks-and-caicos-islands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Turks and Caicos Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TCA/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Cockburn_Town%2C_Grand_Turk_-_panoramio_%289%29.jpg/1920px-Cockburn_Town%2C_Grand_Turk_-_panoramio_%289%29.jpg","title":"Cockburn Town along its sea wall","desc":"Old stone and plaster houses on the shore road, with the low town running away up the coast beside clear shallow water.","credit":"Photograph by socaltraveler, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cockburn_Town,_Grand_Turk_-_panoramio_(9).jpg","alt":"A low Caribbean town of old stone houses along a shore road beside a sea wall, with pale turquoise shallows on the right."}},
{"id":"gw-709","num":709,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Capitol Hill","answerText":"Capitol Hill","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Covenant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1975</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1977</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First government</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Borders federal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 2009</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"N. Mariana Is.","dot":"Capitol Hill"},"facts":[["Country","Northern Mariana Islands"],["Population","979 (2020 census)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","northern mariana islands","oceania"],"abstract":"<b>Capitol Hill</b> is the seat of government of the Northern Mariana Islands, a United States commonwealth the United Nations counts separately from the United States and credits with 44,000 people on 457 square kilometres, where the World Bank gives 460.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The governor's office is at Capitol Hill on Saipan, though the United Nations profile names Garapan as the capital instead, giving it 4,000 people in a figure it dates to 2010 and heads with no year at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census settles the sizes if not the title: Capitol Hill village held 979 people and Garapan village 3,096, the largest of the commonwealth's villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both stand in Saipan Municipality, which held 43,385 of the territory's 47,329 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The population is falling steeply, from 53,883 at the 2010 census to 47,329 in 2020, a drop of 12.2 per cent, and the World Bank carries it on to 43,541 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Rota Municipality fell by a quarter over the same decade, to 1,893, while the Northern Islands Municipality went from nobody at all to seven people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commonwealth emerged from the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, which the United States administered on behalf of the United Nations from 1947 until Palau, the last member to choose its own political future, became independent in 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The federal law making it a United States territory, known as the Covenant, passed in 1975.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It adopted its own constitution in 1977 and its first constitutional government took office in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Federal minimum wage rules reached it in 2007 and federal immigration law in 2008, and the Department of Homeland Security took over its immigration and border controls in June 2009.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Northern Mariana Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mp.html. [Open access]","U.S. Census Bureau, “2020 Census of the Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands: Population and Housing Unit Counts,” tables 1 and 2, https://www.census.gov/data/tables/2020/dec/2020-commonwealth-northern-mariana-islands.html. [Open access]","Office of Insular Affairs, “Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands,” U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.doi.gov/oia/islands/cnmi. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Northern Mariana Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNP/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Northern Mariana Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MNP/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/CNMI_Governor_Office.JPG/1920px-CNMI_Governor_Office.JPG","title":"The governor's office on Capitol Hill","desc":"The long white Juan Atalig Sablan Memorial Building behind its lawn and palms, on the government hill above Saipan.","credit":"Photograph by Abasaa, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CNMI_Governor_Office.JPG","alt":"A long low white government building with a blue roofline, set behind a lawn with palms and a slip road in front."}},
{"id":"gw-710","num":710,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answer":"Philipsburg","answerText":"Philipsburg","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Autonomous</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 October 2010</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hurricane Irma</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 2017</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emergency aid to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 2017</span></div>","map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sint Maarten","dot":"Philipsburg"},"facts":[["Country","Sint Maarten"],["Population","?"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","sint maarten","caribbean"],"abstract":"<b>Philipsburg</b> is the capital of Sint Maarten, a country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands that the United Nations counts separately from the Netherlands and credits with 44,000 people on 34 square kilometres, the same area the World Bank gives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its profile lists a capital population of 40,600 for 2018 and then explains in a footnote that the figure refers to the total population of Sint Maarten, so it is not a count of the town at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No count of the town alone appears in either source, and the profile puts the country at 100 per cent urban, so on its own reckoning every resident lives in a town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country is small and crowded, its 44,000 people giving a density of about 1,292 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population is rising steadily, the World Bank giving 37,471 for 2015 against 43,923 for 2025, a gain of about 17 per cent in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Sint Maarten became an autonomous country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands on 10 October 2010, when Curaçao did the same and Bonaire, St Eustatius and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Netherlands keeps a single Representation for the three countries, with its headquarters at Willemstad in Curaçao and offices at Oranjestad in Aruba and Philipsburg in Sint Maarten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 6 September 2017 Hurricane Irma struck Sint Maarten, Saba and St Eustatius, and Sint Maarten was the worst affected, with many homes and much infrastructure destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Netherlands gave emergency aid until 30 November 2017 and then began contributing to reconstruction, for which Sint Maarten, as an autonomous country, is itself responsible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It set aside 550 million euros for that work, on two conditions: the establishment of an Integrity Chamber, and improved border controls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sx.html. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Caribbean Parts of the Kingdom,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Reconstruction of St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/reconstruction-of-st-maarten. [Open access]","Government of the Netherlands, “Representation of the Netherlands in Aruba, Curaçao and St Maarten,” https://www.government.nl/topics/caribbean-parts-of-the-kingdom/representation-of-the-netherlands-in-aruba-curacao-and-st-maarten. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SXM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SXM/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Philipsburg_%28Sint_Maarten%29_2025_-_1.jpg/1920px-Philipsburg_%28Sint_Maarten%29_2025_-_1.jpg","title":"Philipsburg on its sand bar, 2025","desc":"The town seen from the hills, built along the strip of land between the Great Salt Pond and Great Bay, with green ridges beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Ymnes, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philipsburg_(Sint_Maarten)_2025_-_1.jpg","alt":"A Caribbean town built along a narrow strip of land between a lagoon and the sea, seen from wooded hills above."}},
{"id":"gw-711","num":711,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Vaduz","answerText":"Vaduz","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1150</span><span class=\"dt-k\">County of Vaduz</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1342</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Imperial principality</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1719</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1990</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","liechtenstein","europe"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Liechtenstein","dot":"Vaduz"},"facts":[["Country","Liechtenstein"],["Population","5.7k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","+4.4% (2015–2019)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Kil%C3%A1t%C3%A1s_a_vaduzi_v%C3%A1rb%C3%B3l.jpg/1920px-Kil%C3%A1t%C3%A1s_a_vaduzi_v%C3%A1rb%C3%B3l.jpg","title":"Vaduz from the castle","desc":"The town centre in the foreground, the flat floor of the Rhine valley beyond it, and the Swiss mountains on the far side.","credit":"Photograph by Molnár Bence, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kil%C3%A1t%C3%A1s_a_vaduzi_v%C3%A1rb%C3%B3l.jpg","alt":"A small Alpine town of low buildings seen from above, with a broad green valley floor behind it and forested mountains rising beyond."},"abstract":"<b>Vaduz</b> is the capital of Liechtenstein, a principality of about 40,000 people on 160 square kilometres between Switzerland and Austria, a density of about 251 per square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stands on the east bank of the Rhine below the Drei Schwestern massif, and the commune's own portrait describes a former farming village, known for its wine, now a busy residence-town of banks and offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The principality is barely urban: the World Bank counts about 6,000 town-dwellers in 2025, under fifteen per cent of the total, so Vaduz is a small town rather than a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations profile gives a capital-city population of 5,500 for 2018, but that is the World Bank's whole urban figure for that year, 5,567, so it counts town-dwellers nationwide rather than the town itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country is growing steadily, from 37,494 people in 2015 to 41,024 in 2025, about nine per cent in a decade, on an area the World Bank holds flat at 160 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Vaduz was first recorded in 1150, and in 1342 the division of the Sargans and Werdenberg lands created a county of Vaduz covering most of the present state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The castle above the town, built around 1300, was held in turn by the counts of Werdenberg, the barons of Brandis, the counts of Sulz and the counts of Hohenems, and Emperor Rudolf II granted market rights in 1592.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prince Johann Adam of Liechtenstein bought the county in 1712, and seven years later Emperor Charles VI joined it to the lordship of Schellenberg and raised the two to an imperial principality with Vaduz as its chief place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Liechtenstein reached sovereignty in 1806, joined the German Confederation in 1815 and was independent again in 1866, but for much of the twentieth century Switzerland conducted its foreign affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The castle has been the princes' permanent home since 1939, the country joined the United Nations on 18 September 1990, and an American ambassador was first accredited to it, at Bern, in 1997.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Liechtenstein,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/li.html. [Open access]","Gemeinde Vaduz, “Vaduz,” Portrait, https://www.vaduz.li/vaduz/portrait/vaduz. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Liechtenstein,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/liechtenstein. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Liechtenstein,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LIE/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Urban population: Liechtenstein,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LIE/indicator/SP.URB.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Liechtenstein,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LIE/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gw-712","num":712,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country or territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Road Town","answerText":"Road Town","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2007, replacing 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hurricane Irma</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 2017</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emergency declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 September 2017</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","british virgin islands","caribbean"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"British Virgin Is.","dot":"Road Town"},"facts":[["Country","British Virgin Islands"],["Population","12.6k (2012)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3d/TORT-blick-hafen-roadtown.jpg","title":"Road Town and its harbour, from the hills of Tortola","desc":"The town spread along the shore at the left, the working port and marinas below it, and the other Virgin Islands on the horizon.","credit":"Photograph by Bgabel, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TORT-blick-hafen-roadtown.jpg","alt":"A Caribbean harbour town seen from wooded hills above, with docks, moored boats and a cruise ship, and low islands on the horizon."},"abstract":"<b>Road Town</b> stands on Tortola and is the capital of the British Virgin Islands, a United Kingdom overseas territory of about 40,000 people that the United Nations counts separately from the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The territory covers 151 square kilometres by the UN's reckoning and 150 by the World Bank's, giving a density of about 258 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The UN profile puts the capital at 15,100 for 2018, a figure well below the World Bank's urban population of 17,894 for the same year, so unlike several small territories it is describing a place rather than the whole urban total.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its executive is headed by a Governor representing the British government beside an elected Premier and Cabinet who run the territory's day-to-day affairs, and the courts belong to the Eastern Caribbean Supreme Court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Virgin Islands Constitution Order 2007 replaced the constitution of 1976 and added, for the first time, a chapter of fundamental rights and freedoms with provisions for enforcing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That order also provides for the Premier and ministers, an elected House of Assembly, four service commissions, a Complaints Commissioner, a Register of Interests and a new National Security Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hurricane Irma struck on 6 September 2017, and the Foreign Secretary described catastrophic damage to the territory and to Anguilla, with a Royal Fleet Auxiliary ship already in the vicinity and staff on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two days later the Governor declared a state of emergency, reported casualties and deaths, and said the National Emergency Operation Centre had been forced to move to Peebles Hospital while radio and other communications remained extremely limited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The World Bank's count shows the shock plainly: the population fell from 34,498 in 2015 to 32,699 in 2017 before recovering to 39,732 by 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Road Town remains the working centre of the islands, and a 2026 government project to rebuild the pavements of DeCastro Street was announced as making a capital better able to support commerce, public services and community life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “British Virgin Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/vg.html. [Open access]","Government of the Virgin Islands, “Constitution,” https://bvi.gov.vg/constitution. [Open access]","Governor's Office Tortola, “Hurricane Irma: Message from the Governor of the British Virgin Islands,” 8 September 2017, https://www.gov.uk/government/news/hurricane-irma-message-from-the-governor-of-the-british-virgin-islands. [Open access]","Foreign and Commonwealth Office, “Hurricane Irma: Foreign Secretary's Statement on Anguilla and the British Virgin Islands,” 7 September 2017, https://www.gov.uk/government/news/hurricane-irma-foreign-secretarys-statement-on-anguilla-and-the-british-virgin-islands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: British Virgin Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VGB/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: British Virgin Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VGB/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Urban population: British Virgin Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/VGB/indicator/SP.URB.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Government of the Virgin Islands, “DeCastro Street Sidewalk Works Deliver Safer, More Accessible Road Town Corridor,” https://bvi.gov.vg/news/decastro-street-sidewalk-works-deliver-safer-more-accessible-road-town-corridor. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"gw-715","num":715,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Majuro","answerText":"Majuro","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Trust Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947 – 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compact in force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 October 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","marshall islands","oceania"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Marshall Is.","dot":"Majuro"},"facts":[["Country","Marshall Islands"],["Population","30.0k (2008)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Majuro</b> is the capital of the Marshall Islands, a Pacific republic of 29 coral atolls and five islands that its own embassy in Washington places midway between Hawaii and Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations credits the country with 36,000 people on 181 square kilometres, a density of about 202 to the square kilometre, and the World Bank measures the same land at 180.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The town lies on Majuro Atoll, and the UN puts its population at 30,700 for 2018, below the World Bank's urban total of 34,683 for the same year, so the entry describes one place rather than every town-dweller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Almost everybody in the country lives in a town by that reckoning, the urban share standing at 77.4 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The population is falling fast, from 48,800 in 2015 to 36,282 in 2025, a quarter of the people gone in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were a district of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands, which the United States administered on behalf of the United Nations from 1947 until 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The republic adopted its constitution in 1979 and came into being as a sovereign country in the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Negotiations for a Compact of Free Association with the United States opened on 25 June 1983, and the Compact entered into force on 21 October 1986, ending the islands' status as a United Nations strategic trust territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under it the United States defends the country's territorial integrity and provides economic aid, and in return holds exclusive access to its land and waters for strategic purposes, including the Army's Reagan Missile Test Site on Kwajalein Atoll.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first financial package ran from 1986 to 2003 and a second from 2003 to 2023, with a further agreement negotiated in 2024; an American office opened in the capital in 1986, became an embassy in 1989, and the country joined the United Nations on 17 September 1991.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Marshall Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mh.html. [Open access]","Embassy of the Republic of the Marshall Islands, Washington D.C., “Country Profile,” https://rmiembassyus.org/country-profile. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Marshall Islands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/marshall-islands. [Open access]","Office of Insular Affairs, “The Marshall Islands,” U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.doi.gov/oia/islands/marshallislands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Marshall Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MHL/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Marshall Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MHL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Urban population: Marshall Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MHL/indicator/SP.URB.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Majuro_-_Main_Atoll_of_the_RMI.jpg/1920px-Majuro_-_Main_Atoll_of_the_RMI.jpg","title":"Majuro from the air, looking along the atoll rim","desc":"The Rita end of the capital, its houses and road strung along a strip of land with the open ocean on the left and the lagoon on the right.","credit":"Photograph by Mark Uriona, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Majuro_-_Main_Atoll_of_the_RMI.jpg","alt":"An aerial view along a narrow coral atoll, houses and a single road following the strip of land between an ocean reef and a wide blue lagoon."}},
{"id":"gw-716","num":716,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"San Marino","answerText":"San Marino","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 May 1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declaration of Rights</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 July 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU customs union</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 December 1991</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 March 1992</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","san marino","europe"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"San Marino","dot":"City of San Marino"},"facts":[["Country","San Marino"],["Population","33.6k (2021)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","+6.3% (2014–2021)"]],"abstract":"<b>San Marino</b> is the capital of the Republic of San Marino, a landlocked state surrounded by Italy which the United Nations credits with 34,000 people on 61 square kilometres, a density of about 550 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank measures the same land at 60 square kilometres and counts 34,109 residents in 2025, a population that has barely moved in a decade from 32,897 in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The UN puts the capital's own population at 4,500 for 2018, against a World Bank urban total of 33,521 for the same year, so the great majority of the republic's town-dwellers live somewhere other than its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> By the UN's reckoning 97.4 per cent of the population is urban; the republic uses the euro and joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Palazzo Pubblico, on Piazza della Libertà in the capital, is the seat of the republic's principal institutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It holds the office of the Captains Regent, who are the heads of state, and is where the Grand and General Council, the republic's parliament, and its committees meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Congress of State, which is the government, gathers there too, as does the panel that guarantees the constitutionality of laws, until it is given a seat of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Council has 60 members and holds the legislative power under article 3 of the Declaration of the Rights of Citizens of 8 July 1974, its members elected by universal direct suffrage on a proportional system with the whole country as a single constituency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised San Marino in 1861, after the Captains Regent offered Abraham Lincoln honorary citizenship and he replied on 7 May that \"Although your dominion is small, your State is nevertheless one of the most honored, in all history\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The republic has had diplomatic relations with the European Union since 1983, and a cooperation and customs union agreement was signed at Brussels on 16 December 1991 and approved by the European Parliament on 9 July 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “San Marino,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sm.html. [Open access]","Consiglio Grande e Generale, “Palazzo Pubblico,” Repubblica di San Marino, https://www.consigliograndeegenerale.sm/on-line/home/listituzione/palazzo-pubblico.html. [Open access]","Consiglio Grande e Generale, “Funzione: poteri, attribuzioni, organizzazione, funzionamento,” Repubblica di San Marino, https://www.consigliograndeegenerale.sm/on-line/home/listituzione/funzione.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: San Marino,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/san-marino. [Open access]","Segreteria di Stato per gli Affari Esteri, “Storia delle relazioni con l’Unione europea,” Repubblica di San Marino, https://www.esteri.sm/pub2/EsteriSM/UE/Storia.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: San Marino,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SMR/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: San Marino,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SMR/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Urban population: San Marino,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SMR/indicator/SP.URB.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/San_Marino_dal_Monte_San_Paolo_al_tramonto.jpg/1920px-San_Marino_dal_Monte_San_Paolo_al_tramonto.jpg","title":"The city of San Marino on the crest of Monte Titano","desc":"The old town strung along the ridge above the wooded slopes, seen at sunset from Monte San Paolo with the plain running away behind it.","credit":"Photograph by Naioli, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Marino_dal_Monte_San_Paolo_al_tramonto.jpg","alt":"A small stone town along the top of a steep isolated ridge, wooded slopes below it and a hazy plain stretching to the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-717","num":717,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country or territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Mariehamn","answerText":"Mariehamn","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ceded to Russia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1809</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1861</span><span class=\"dt-k\">League of Nations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First Landsting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 June 1922</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","aland","europe"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Åland","dot":"Mariehamn"},"facts":[["Country","Åland, Finland"],["Population","12.0k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+2.4% (2017–2025)"]],"abstract":"<b>Mariehamn</b> is the capital and the only town of Åland, an autonomous, demilitarised and Swedish-speaking region of Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Åland's own statistics office counted 30,836 people at the end of 2025, of whom 11,957 lived in Mariehamn, 16,889 in the countryside and 1,990 in the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It counts 6,757 islands of at least a quarter of a hectare with about 20,000 smaller islands and skerries besides, and gave a preliminary 30,971 residents for 31 July 2026.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The islands cover 1,586 square kilometres (612 square miles), of which the main island holds about 70 per cent along with 90 per cent of the people, running 50 kilometres (31 miles) from north to south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mariehamn was founded in 1861, houses more than 40 per cent of the islanders, and is the centre of political and economic activity in Åland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Åland belonged to Sweden until the war of 1808 and 1809, when Sweden was forced to cede Finland and Åland to Russia and the islands became part of the Grand Duchy of Finland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In August 1917 representatives of every Åland municipality met in secret and resolved to ask for reunion with Sweden; Finland declared itself independent that December and in 1920 passed an autonomy act the islanders would not accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Council of the League of Nations settled the question in June 1921: Finland kept sovereignty but had to guarantee the islanders their Swedish language, culture and local customs, and a treaty was to be drawn up demilitarising and neutralising Åland so that it could never threaten Sweden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first Landsting was elected in 1922 and met on 9 June, a day still kept as Åland's autonomy day, and the autonomy act has since been wholly revised twice, in 1951 and 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The parliament and the government share the Självstyrelsegården on Strandgatan in Mariehamn, built after a Nordic architectural competition won by Helmer Stenros of Helsinki and opened in 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Ålands statistik- och utredningsbyrå, “Vanliga frågor om Åland,” https://www.asub.ax/sv/vanliga-fragor-om-aland. [Open access]","Government of Åland, “Facts about Åland,” Åland.ax, https://www.aland.ax/en/facts-about-aland. [Open access]","Ålands lagting, “Bakgrunden till självstyrelsen,” https://www.lagtinget.ax/sjalvstyrelsen/bakgrunden-sjalvstyrelsen. [Open access]","Ålands lagting, “Självstyrelsegården,” https://www.lagtinget.ax/sjalvstyrelsen/sjalvstyrelsegarden. [Open access]","Ålands statistik- och utredningsbyrå, “Välkommen till ÅSUB,” https://www.asub.ax/sv. [Open access]","Nordic Statistics Database, “AREA02: Land Use, Square Kilometres by Reporting Country, Unit and Time,” https://pxweb.nordicstatistics.org/pxweb/en/Nordic%20Statistics/Nordic%20Statistics__Geography%20and%20climate__Land%20use/AREA02.px/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Aerial_photo_of_Mariehamn_2026.jpg/1920px-Aerial_photo_of_Mariehamn_2026.jpg","title":"Mariehamn from the air","desc":"The town on its narrow neck of land between two harbours, with the airfield and the low wooded countryside of the main island beyond.","credit":"Photograph by CedarGlen86, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_photo_of_Mariehamn_2026.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a small low-built town among trees, water on both sides of it and flat wooded country stretching to the horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-718","num":718,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country or territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Marigot","answerText":"Marigot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Guadeloupe municipality</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 January 1823</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commune of Marigot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1837</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 December 2003</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collectivity law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 February 2007</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","saint martin","caribbean"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"St-Martin","dot":"Marigot"},"facts":[["Country","Saint Martin, France"],["Population","5.7k (2006)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Marigot</b> is the capital of Saint Martin, the French half of a Caribbean island that the collectivity's own government calls the smallest landmass in the world shared by two independent states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The northern, French part is run jointly by a prefecture representing the French state and by the Collectivity of Saint-Martin, which since 2007 has come under article 74 of the constitution, giving it more autonomy than an ordinary territorial collectivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The World Bank measures the French side at 50 square kilometres and counted 24,941 people there in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That population has fallen by a third in a decade, from 37,369 in 2015 and 36,837 in 2017 to under 25,000 today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch half of the same island moved the other way over exactly those years, rising from 37,471 in 2015 to 43,923 in 2025, so one small island holds two populations going in opposite directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Both French territories in these waters sit in the hurricane belt, and the prefecture warns that Saint-Martin and Saint-Barthélemy are exposed to cyclones every year from June to November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Marigot has been the island's administrative seat since the colonial period: on 29 January 1823 Saint-Martin, with its seat at Marigot, was made one of Guadeloupe's first five municipalities, beside Basse-Terre, Pointe-à-Pitre, Moule and Grand-Bourg.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A colonial decree of 20 September 1837 created the communes of Grand-Case and Marigot on the island, and the first municipal elections were held that 7 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A sub-prefecture for Saint-Martin and Saint-Barthélemy opened on 11 February 1963, with Guy Maillard as the first sub-prefect in post.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Islanders voted on 7 December 2003 to leave Guadeloupe for a collectivity of their own, parliament adopted the organic law creating it on 7 February 2007, and the first president of the territorial council was elected that 15 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["World Bank, “Population, total: St. Martin (French part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAF/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: St. Martin (French part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAF/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Collectivité de Saint-Martin, “Histoire de la Collectivité,” https://www.com-saint-martin.fr/thematiques/organisation-territoriale/votre-collectivite/histoire-de-la-collectivite. [Open access]","Collectivité de Saint-Martin, “Sint Maarten : voisin et partenaire,” https://www.com-saint-martin.fr/thematiques/organisation-territoriale/sint-maarten/sint-maarten-voisin-et-partenaire. [Open access]","Préfecture de Saint-Barthélemy et de Saint-Martin, “Risque cyclonique,” https://www.saint-barth-saint-martin.gouv.fr/Actions-de-l-Etat/Environnement-risques-naturels-et-technologiques/Risques-Naturels/Risque-Cyclonique. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SXM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/View_of_Marigot_and_Sandy_Ground_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-View_of_Marigot_and_Sandy_Ground_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Marigot and its bay, from the hills above","desc":"The town around its marina at the left, the beach and bay to the right, and the low spit of Sandy Ground running out beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Remy Gumbs, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Marigot_and_Sandy_Ground_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A Caribbean town of low coloured roofs around a yacht marina, seen from wooded hills, with a bay and a long sandy spit beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-719","num":719,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country or territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"The Valley","answerText":"The Valley","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Columbus sails by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1493</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anguilla Day</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 May 1967</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Separate territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 December 1980</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Official flag</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 May 1990</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","anguilla","caribbean"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Anguilla","dot":"The Valley"},"facts":[["Country","Anguilla, UK"],["Population","15.0k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-8.7% (2001–2011)"]],"abstract":"<b>The Valley</b> is the capital of Anguilla, a British territory in the eastern Caribbean which its own government calls the most northerly of the Leeward Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations credits the territory with 15,000 people on 91 square kilometres, a density of about 167 to the square kilometre, and Anguilla's own facts page gives the same area as 35 square miles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island runs about 26 kilometres (16 miles) long and 5 kilometres (3 miles) across at its widest, and Europeans renamed it Anguilla for that eel-like shape after Columbus sailed past in 1493 without landing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The UN puts the capital's own population at only 1,400 for 2018, under a tenth of the territory's people, although it counts Anguilla as entirely urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island uses the East Caribbean dollar, and its government gives a mean monthly temperature of about 27°C (80°F) and 890 millimetres (35 inches) of rain a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Amerindians reached the island by dugout canoe and raft from the South American mainland and called it Malliouhana, meaning 'arrow-shape sea serpent', leaving remains at the eastern end that are 3,300 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the 1830s Britain joined Anguilla to St Kitts and Nevis, a union most of the island's freeholders protested against and which left them one representative in a legislature that largely ignored them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The three islands entered the Federation of the West Indies in 1958; when it collapsed in 1962 they were made an associated state instead, and that decision set off the Anguilla Revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anguilla still keeps 30 May as Anguilla Day, marking the expulsion of the Royal St Kitts Police Force in 1967, after which Britain intervened and a peacekeeping committee was set up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Negotiations ran on for another decade until 19 December 1980, when Anguilla became a separate dependent territory; its official blue ensign was first hoisted on 30 May 1990, and the Governor now works with Anguilla's ministers and the United Kingdom on the island's security and good governance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Anguilla,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ai.html. [Open access]","Government of Anguilla, “Anguilla Facts,” https://www.gov.ai/service/anguilla-facts. [Open access]","Government of Anguilla, “Anguilla History,” https://www.gov.ai/service/anguilla-history. [Open access]","Government of Anguilla, “National Symbols: National Flags,” https://www.gov.ai/service/national-symbols-national-flags. [Open access]","Government of Anguilla, “H.E. The Governor,” Government Directory, https://www.gov.ai/government-directories/he-the-governor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Wallblake_House%2C_The_Valley.jpg/1920px-Wallblake_House%2C_The_Valley.jpg","title":"Wallblake House in The Valley","desc":"The old plantation house and its stone outbuildings behind a picket fence; the capital is a village of a few hundred houses and has no skyline to photograph.","credit":"Photograph by Josveek Huligar, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wallblake_House,_The_Valley.jpg","alt":"A low pink wooden house with a shingled roof beside two stone outbuildings, behind a white picket fence with grass in front and tall clouds above."}},
{"id":"gw-720","num":720,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Ngerulmud","answerText":"Ngerulmud","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sold to Germany</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trust Territory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1981</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1994</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","palau","oceania"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Palau","dot":"Ngerulmud"},"facts":[["Country","Palau"],["Population","17.7k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Ngerulmud</b> is the seat of government of Palau, a Pacific republic of eight principal islands and more than 250 smaller ones in the Caroline chain, lying about 800 kilometres (500 miles) south-east of the Philippines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The State Department records that the government's offices stand in a National Capitol complex at Ngerulmud in Melekeok State, while about 64 per cent of Palauans live in Koror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations profile names Melekeok as the capital instead, and the figure it prints beside that name, 11,400 for 2018, carries a footnote saying it refers to Koror.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country is small and steady: the World Bank counted 17,663 people in 2025 against 17,770 in 2015, on 460 square kilometres where the UN gives 459.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About four Palauans in five live in a town by the UN's reckoning, and the country uses the United States dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Palau was settled more than 4,000 years ago, probably by migrants from what is now Indonesia, and British traders became prominent visitors in the 18th century before Spanish influence grew in the 19th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spain sold Palau and most of the rest of the Caroline Islands to Germany in 1899 after losing the Spanish-American War, control passed to Japan in 1914, and the islands came to the United States under United Nations auspices in 1947 as part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Palau was the trust territory's last district to settle its future: four districts federated in 1979 and Palau stayed out, approving a constitution of its own in 1981 and signing a Compact of Free Association in 1982.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A presidential proclamation of 27 September 1994 ended the trusteeship on 1 October, when the Compact took effect and the United States recognised Palau's independence, and Palau joined the United Nations that 15 December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the fifty-year Compact the United States defends Palau's territorial integrity and provides economic assistance in return for exclusive access to its land and waterways, and an American embassy opened at Koror in 2004.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Palau,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pw.html. [Open access]","U.S. Department of State, “Background Note: Palau,” 7 February 2012, archived at https://2009-2017.state.gov/outofdate/bgn/palau/196572.htm. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Palau,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/palau. [Open access]","Office of Insular Affairs, “Republic of Palau,” U.S. Department of the Interior, https://www.doi.gov/oia/islands/palau. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Palau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PLW/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Palau,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/PLW/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Ngerulmud_01.jpg/1920px-Ngerulmud_01.jpg","title":"The Office of the President at Ngerulmud","desc":"The colonnaded entrance of the president's office in the national capitol complex, its lintel lettered OFFICE OF THE PRESIDENT and its piers carrying Palauan storyboard motifs.","credit":"Photograph by Zykasaa, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngerulmud_01.jpg","alt":"A pale yellow government building with fluted columns and a covered portico, a doorway beneath a sign reading Office of the President."}},
{"id":"gw-721","num":721,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Avarua","answerText":"Avarua","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Free association</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1965</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Centenary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2001</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 September 2023</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","cook islands","oceania"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Cook Is.","dot":"Avarua"},"facts":[["Country","Cook Islands"],["Population","15.0k (2021 census)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Avarua</b>, on the island of Rarotonga, is the capital of the Cook Islands, a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand lying between French Polynesia and American Samoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations credits the country with 13,000 people on 236 square kilometres, a density of about 55 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its own census of 2021 counted a total population of 15,040, of whom 14,920 were residents, and records that the number had reached 21,322 in 1971 before falling sharply in the years just after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cook Islanders hold New Zealand citizenship and a significant Cook Islands diaspora lives there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The UN names Avarua as the capital, but the figure printed beside the name, 13,100 for 2018, is footnoted as referring to the whole island of Rarotonga, and the census does not report the town separately at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The free-association arrangement with New Zealand was established in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Cook Islands is part of the Realm of New Zealand and shares its head of state, King Charles III, while conducting its own affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> New Zealand carries a constitutional responsibility to answer requests for assistance with foreign affairs, disasters and defence, and the principles of the partnership are set out in a Joint Centenary Declaration signed in 2001 and reaffirmed in a joint statement in 2015.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country has international legal personality and conducts its own international relations, and on 25 September 2023 the United States recognised it as a sovereign and independent state and announced the establishment of diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Secretary of State and the Cook Islands prime minister signed a joint statement that day to mark it, and the census behind the country's own figures is taken every five years under the Statistics Act 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Cook Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ck.html. [Open access]","Cook Islands Statistics Office, “2021 Census of Population and Dwellings,” https://stats.gov.ck/2021-census-of-population-and-dwellings/. [Open access]","Cook Islands Statistics Office, <i>Census of Population and Dwellings 2021: Report with Tables and Questionnaire</i> (Rarotonga: CISO, 2022), https://stats.gov.ck/download/83/census-2021/1497/2021-census-report-with-tables-and-questionnaire.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Cook Islands,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/cook-islands. [Open access]","New Zealand Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Trade, “Cook Islands,” https://www.mfat.govt.nz/en/countries-and-regions/pacific/cook-islands. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Avarua_Harbour.jpg/1920px-Avarua_Harbour.jpg","title":"Avarua Harbour, Rarotonga","desc":"The town's waterfront buildings and small boat harbour, with the green interior of Rarotonga rising behind.","credit":"Photograph by Nurg, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Avarua_Harbour.jpg","alt":"A small Pacific harbour with motorboats moored beside a low quay, single-storey buildings and palms along the shore and a forested ridge behind."}},
{"id":"gw-722","num":722,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Yaren","answerText":"Yaren","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phosphate found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1900</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First cargo shipped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1907</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 September 1999</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","nauru","oceania"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nauru","dot":"Yaren"},"facts":[["Country","Nauru"],["Population","12.0k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Yaren</b> is the district in which the government offices of Nauru stand, and the Commonwealth Secretariat records that the country has no official capital at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nauru's own government describes a tiny island north-east of Australia, 42 kilometres (26 miles) south of the equator, ringed by a coral reef and dotted with pinnacles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The United Nations gives 12,000 people on 21 square kilometres, a density of about 601 to the square kilometre, and counts the country as wholly urban.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank counted 12,025 people in 2025 against 10,954 in 2015, a rise of about a tenth in a decade, on an area it rounds to 20 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The UN profile does name Yaren as the capital city, but the figure printed beside the name, 11,300 for 2018, carries a footnote reading that it refers to Nauru — the whole country rather than the district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A rich deposit of phosphate was found in 1900, the Pacific Phosphate Company shipped the first cargo to Australia in 1907, and mining has shaped the island ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The Commonwealth reports that it has left the interior almost entirely barren, so most Nauruans live on the fertile coastal strip that rings the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nauruans reckon themselves twelve tribes, marked by the twelve-pointed star on the flag, and keep 31 January as Independence Day, which is also the anniversary of the return from Truk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After a series of colonial and military occupations the island became a United Nations trust territory after the Second World War, reaching full independence in 1968 and joining the Commonwealth that year as an associate member.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became a full Commonwealth member in 1999 and joined the United Nations on 14 September that year, and although the United States recognised it on 24 October 1976 it keeps no embassy in the Yaren District, its ambassador to Fiji being accredited from Suva since 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nauru,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nr.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Naoero,” Member Countries, https://www.thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/nauru. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Nauru,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/nauru. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Nauru,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NRU/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Nauru,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NRU/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Government of the Republic of Naoero, “About Naoero,” https://www.nauru.gov.nr/about-naoero.aspx. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Parliament_and_Courthouse_%2815222121087%29.jpg/1920px-Parliament_and_Courthouse_%2815222121087%29.jpg","title":"Parliament and courthouse at Yaren","desc":"The government buildings seen across the grass strip beside Nauru's airport runway, which runs along the south coast through the district.","credit":"Photograph by Sean Kelleher, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parliament_and_Courthouse_%2815222121087%29.jpg","alt":"Low white buildings with pitched roofs and palm trees behind them, seen from a road across a wide strip of mown grass."}},
{"id":"gw-723","num":723,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country or territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Mata-Utu","answerText":"Mata-Utu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Futuna sighted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1616</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Uvea sighted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1767</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1888</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 December 1959</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","wallis and futuna","oceania"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Wallis and Futuna Is.","dot":"Mata-Utu"},"facts":[["Country","Wallis and Futuna, France"],["Population","?"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Mata-Utu</b>, written Mata'Utu by the territory's own administration, is the chef-lieu of Wallis and Futuna, a French overseas collectivity in the South Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territory covers 142 square kilometres across three main islands, Wallis on one side and Futuna with the uninhabited Alofi 230 kilometres (140 miles) away on the other, and lies 22,000 kilometres (13,700 miles) from metropolitan France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wallis is a low volcanic island of 77.9 square kilometres ringed by a lagoon whose southern pass leads along a marked channel to the wharf at Mata'Utu, and only Wallis has an international airport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The census of 2023 counted 11,151 people in the territory against 11,558 in 2018, and nearly 60 per cent of them are under twenty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations profile, which spells the name Matu-Utu, gives the town about 1,000 people for 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The French state is represented by a prefect, who is also administrateur supérieur and heads the collectivity's executive, and a territorial assembly of twenty elected members sits at Mata'Utu and votes the budget.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> There are no communes at all: three administrative circumscriptions with legal personality answer to the kingdoms of Uvea on Wallis and Alo and Sigave on Futuna, each with a council presided over by its customary king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Uvea's circumscription was established by the statutory law of 29 July 1961 and an order of 20 March 1964, and performs the duties that fall to communes elsewhere in France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dutch navigators found Futuna and Alofi in 1616 and named them the Hoorn Islands, Samuel Wallis sighted Uvea in 1767 and gave that island his name, and Marist missionaries settled from 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wallis and Futuna separately declared themselves free and independent under the protection of France by treaties in 1842, a unified protectorate followed in 1888, and at the referendum of 27 December 1959 some 94 per cent voted to join the French Republic as an overseas territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Wallis and Futuna Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/wf.html. [Open access]","Administration supérieure des îles Wallis et Futuna, “Présentation générale,” https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/Actualites/Presentation-de-Wallis-et-Futuna/Presentation-generale. [Open access]","Administration supérieure des îles Wallis et Futuna, “Organisation institutionnelle,” https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/Actualites/Presentation-de-Wallis-et-Futuna/Organisation-institutionnelle. [Open access]","Administration supérieure des îles Wallis et Futuna, “Présentation économique,” https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/Actualites/Presentation-de-Wallis-et-Futuna/Presentation-economique. [Open access]","Administration supérieure des îles Wallis et Futuna, “Circonscription d’Uvéa,” https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/Services-de-l-Etat-et-du-Territoire/Circonscription-d-Uvea. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Mata-Utu_seen_from_a_drone_%28Wallis_and_Futuna%29.png/1280px-Mata-Utu_seen_from_a_drone_%28Wallis_and_Futuna%29.png","title":"Mata-Utu from the air","desc":"The cathedral with its twin red-roofed towers and the royal palace beside it, houses along the shore of the lagoon and forested hills behind.","credit":"Photograph by LymLog, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mata-Utu_seen_from_a_drone_%28Wallis_and_Futuna%29.png","alt":"An aerial view of a small Pacific village along a shore, with a large stone church, low houses among trees and green hills rising behind."}},
{"id":"gw-724","num":724,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country or territory shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Gustavia","answerText":"Gustavia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commune of Guadeloupe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947 – 2007</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Overseas collectivity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 2007</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU status changed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2012</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hurricane Irma</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 2017</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","saint barthelemy","caribbean"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"St-Barthélemy","dot":"Gustavia"},"facts":[["Country","Saint Barthélemy, France"],["Population","9.79k (2018)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Gustavia</b> is the chef-lieu of Saint Barthélemy, a French overseas collectivity at the junction of the Greater and Lesser Antilles, 230 kilometres (140 miles) north-west of Guadeloupe and 25 kilometres (16 miles) south-east of Saint Martin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island covers 21 square kilometres, or 25 counting its islets, and had 9,793 inhabitants in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is arid, its dry volcanic hills carrying thorny vegetation on a rocky soil unsuited to agriculture, though the wetter east coast is a good deal greener.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The collectivity's own offices stand at Pointe-Gustavia and the French state keeps a delegation in the town, on rue Lubin Brin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saint Barthélemy shares a prefect with Saint Martin, and the prefectural team includes a delegate of the prefect posted to the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> From 1947 until 2007 the island was administratively attached to Guadeloupe as a commune of that department.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organic law of 21 February 2007 turned that commune into an overseas collectivity, with a territorial council of nineteen members elected for five years as its legislature and the council's president as its executive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a letter of 30 June 2010 the French president asked the European Council to change the island's standing in the Union from outermost region to overseas country or territory, a request that reflected the wish of its elected representatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The European Council decided on 29 October 2010 that with effect from 1 January 2012 Saint-Barthélemy would cease to be an outermost region and take the status set out in Part Four of the treaty, France undertaking to keep the euro as the island's sole currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hurricane Irma crossed in September 2017, a category five storm with winds of nearly 300 kilometres an hour (190 miles an hour) that caused widespread damage, and European Union money has since gone to the island's emergency services and communications.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “Saint-Barthelemy,” https://www.overseas-association.eu/oct/saint-barthelemy/. [Open access]","European Council, “Decision 2010/718/EU of 29 October 2010 Amending the Status with Regard to the European Union of the Island of Saint-Barthélemy,” <i>Official Journal of the European Union</i> L 325 (9 December 2010): 4–5, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32010D0718. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for International Partnerships, “Saint Barthélemy,” https://international-partnerships.ec.europa.eu/countries/saint-barthelemy_en. [Open access]","Préfecture de Saint-Barthélemy et de Saint-Martin, “Consultation du public sur le projet de plan territorial de l’agriculture durable et de l’alimentation de Saint Barthélemy,” https://www.saint-barth-saint-martin.gouv.fr/Publications/Consultation-du-public-projet-de-PTAD-de-Saint-Barthelemy. [Open access]","Préfecture de Saint-Barthélemy et de Saint-Martin, “Équipe préfectorale,” https://www.saint-barth-saint-martin.gouv.fr/Services-de-l-Etat/Equipe-prefectorale. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Gustavia_Harbour_St_Barthelemy.JPG/1920px-Gustavia_Harbour_St_Barthelemy.JPG","title":"Gustavia and its harbour, from Fort Gustave","desc":"The red-roofed town wrapped around its inlet with yachts moored along both quays, seen past one of the fort's old cannon.","credit":"Photograph by Kevin Gabbert, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustavia_Harbour_St_Barthelemy.JPG","alt":"A Caribbean harbour town of red-roofed houses around a narrow inlet crowded with yachts, seen from a hill with an old cannon in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gw-725","num":725,"category":"Capital cities","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Funafuti","answerText":"Funafuti","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1978</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Diplomatic relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 May 1979</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 September 2000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","geography","capital","tuvalu","oceania"],"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Tuvalu","dot":"Funafuti"},"facts":[["Country","Tuvalu"],["Population","11.0k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Funafuti</b> is the atoll that holds the capital of Tuvalu, and the Commonwealth Secretariat sets the address out in three parts: Vaiaku, where the government offices are, on Fongafale islet, in Funafuti atoll.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations simply names Funafuti as the capital city and gives it 7,000 people for 2018, which is close to the whole of the country's urban population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tuvalu covers about 26 square kilometres by the UN's reckoning and 25.6 by the Commonwealth's, where the World Bank rounds it to 30, and the density is some 316 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How many people live there is unsettled rather than agreed: Tuvalu's own statistics division gives 10,954 for 2025, the Commonwealth 11,790 for 2022, the World Bank 9,492 for 2025 and the United Nations about 10,000 for the country as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank's series has fallen steadily from 10,963 in 2015, and about 63 per cent of Tuvaluans live in a town by the UN's count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The islands were the Ellice Islands under British sovereignty, part of the Gilbert and Ellice Islands Colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United States recognised Tuvalu on 1 October 1978, when Elizabeth Stevens, acting as the president's personal representative, delivered a letter to that effect from President Jimmy Carter to Governor-General Penitala Teo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tuvalu joined the Commonwealth in that same year, and the United Nations on 5 September 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diplomatic relations with the United States were established on 10 May 1979, and the first American ambassador presented his credentials on 24 January 1980, accredited to Fiji and Tonga as well and resident at Suva.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American ambassadors have stayed at Suva ever since, and no mission has ever been established at Funafuti.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Tuvalu,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/tv.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Tuvalu,” Member Countries, https://www.thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/tuvalu. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Tuvalu,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/tuvalu. [Open access]","Tuvalu Central Statistics Division, “Tuvalu Central Statistics Division,” https://stats.gov.tv/. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Tuvalu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TUV/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: Tuvalu,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/TUV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Tuvalu_Funafuti_Airport_%28cropped%29.JPG/1280px-Tuvalu_Funafuti_Airport_%28cropped%29.JPG","title":"Fongafale islet, Funafuti","desc":"An aerial view along Fongafale, the islet of Funafuti atoll that carries the town, its airstrip running through the settlement.","credit":"Gabriella Jacobi, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuvalu_Funafuti_Airport_%28cropped%29.JPG","alt":"A long narrow palm-covered islet in a blue lagoon, with an airstrip and low buildings along it."}},
{"id":"gw-726","num":726,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","answer":"Saint-Pierre","answerText":"Saint-Pierre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Overseas collectivity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 2007</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Maritime award</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 June 1992</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Twinned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1976, with Port-en-Bessin-Huppain</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Saint-Pierre</b> is the capital of Saint Pierre and Miquelon, a French overseas collectivity in the North Atlantic, east of the Canadian coast and made up of several islands and islets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archipelago covers 242 square kilometres (93 square miles), and the United Nations puts its population at about 6,000 for 2025 at a density of 24.2 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commune of Saint-Pierre itself is far smaller and far more crowded: its own council gives 30 square kilometres (12 square miles), 5,412 inhabitants at the 2016 census and 33 kilometres (21 miles) of municipal road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations names Saint-Pierre as the capital city and gives it 5,700 people for 2018, which is close to the whole of the archipelago's population, since about 90 per cent of it lives in a town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> French is the official language and the euro the currency, and the highest point is the Morne de la Grande Montagne on Miquelon, 240 metres (790 feet) above the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Saint Pierre and Miquelon has been a French overseas collectivity since the organic law of 21 February 2007, its local power exercised by a Territorial Council of nineteen members, with the French state represented by a prefect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is one of the European Union's overseas countries and territories, and the decision governing that association singles it out, providing that \"St Pierre et Miquelon shall be considered to be an isolated OCT\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The waters around the islands were disputed with Canada: in 1977 both extended their maritime jurisdiction, Canada declaring an exclusive fishing zone of 200 miles and France a zone reaching 188 miles beyond the archipelago's territorial waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two governments set up an arbitral tribunal by agreement on 30 March 1989, and it gave its decision on 10 June 1992.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It gave France a western sector out to 24 nautical miles and a southern corridor 10.5 nautical miles wide running 188 nautical miles south, its width the distance between the easternmost point of Saint-Pierre and the westernmost point of Miquelon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Pierre and Miquelon,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/pm.html. [Open access]","Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “Saint-Pierre-and-Miquelon,” OCTA Profile, https://overseas-association.eu/oct/saint-pierre-and-miquelon/. [Open access]","Council of the European Union, Council Decision (EU) 2021/1764 of 5 October 2021 on the Association of the Overseas Countries and Territories with the European Union, art. 9(4), https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32021D1764. [Open access]","Mairie de Saint-Pierre, “Saint-Pierre en quelques chiffres,” https://www.mairie-stpierre.fr/fr/253-en-quelques-chiffres.html. [Open access]","Court of Arbitration, “Case Concerning the Delimitation of Maritime Areas between Canada and France, Decision of 10 June 1992,” in <i>Reports of International Arbitral Awards</i>, vol. XXI (New York: United Nations, 2006), 265–341, https://legal.un.org/riaa/cases/vol_XXI/265-341.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"St. Pierre and Miquelon","dot":"Saint-Pierre"},"facts":[["Country","Saint Pierre and Miquelon"],["Population","5.41k (2016 census)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-2.5% (2015–2021)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Saint-Pierre%2C_Saint_Pierre_and_Miquelon%2C_France_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Saint-Pierre%2C_Saint_Pierre_and_Miquelon%2C_France_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"Saint-Pierre from the hills above the town","desc":"The town and its harbour seen from the high ground behind it, with the Ile aux Marins across the water.","credit":"Quintin Soloviev, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Pierre%2C_Saint_Pierre_and_Miquelon%2C_France_%28cropped%29.jpg","alt":"A densely built town of brightly painted houses on a peninsula, ringed by a harbour and breakwaters."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-727","num":727,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","answer":"Jamestown","answerText":"Jamestown","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Crown rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 April 1834</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Letters Patent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 June 1859</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Air access</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 2017</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jamestown</b> is the capital of Saint Helena, a small island of volcanic origin in the South Atlantic lying about 1,900 kilometres (1,200 miles) from Angola and 2,900 kilometres (1,800 miles) from Brazil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is the seat of a Non-Self-Governing Territory under the Charter of the United Nations, administered by the United Kingdom and made up of three island groups with three capitals between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The land area of all three comes to 310 square kilometres (120 square miles), while the exclusive economic zone around them runs to 1,641,294 square kilometres (633,700 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations gives the capital 600 people for 2018 and puts the whole territory at about 5,000 for 2025, at 13.3 people to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island's own count is more exact: the census of 7 February 2021 found 625 residents in the Jamestown district, 32 fewer than in 2016, on an island holding 4,439 people that night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Letters Patent of Queen Victoria dated 6 June 1859 declared Jamestown to be a city called the \"City of James Town\", a recital the territory's 2009 constitution still carries in its preamble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That preamble dates the link with Britain to 1659, recalls the charter granted by Charles II in 1673 and the East India Company's Laws and Constitution of 1682, and records the transfer of rule to the British government on 22 April 1834.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The town has been shrinking for half a century: successive censuses give it 1,516 people in 1976, 864 in 1998 and 625 in 2021, about 41 per cent of the figure it began with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Half Tree Hollow overtook it to become the island's largest district for the first time in 1998, so the capital is no longer the biggest settlement on Saint Helena.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island as a whole is ageing and shrinking, with 59 deaths against 17 births in 2024, though scheduled flights have run since October 2017 from its first airport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Saint Helena,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sh.html. [Open access]","St Helena Statistics Office, <i>St Helena 2021 Population &amp; Housing Census: Main Report</i> (St Helena Government, July 2021), https://www.sainthelena.gov.sh/documents/census-2021-main-report/. [Open access]","St Helena Government, “Population and Demography,” St Helena in Figures, https://www.sainthelena.gov.sh/st-helena/statistics/st-helena-in-figures/demography/. [Open access]","The St Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha Constitution Order 2009, SI 2009/1751, schedule, preamble, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/1751/schedule/made. [Open access]","Special Committee on the Situation with regard to the Implementation of the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples, “Saint Helena: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2024/13, 1 February 2024, https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?s=A/AC.109/2024/13&l=e&t=pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Saint Helena","dot":"Jamestown"},"facts":[["Country","Saint Helena"],["Population","625 (2021 census)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","-4.9% (2016–2021)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Looking_down_on_Jamestown_%2826208606467%29.jpg/1280px-Looking_down_on_Jamestown_%2826208606467%29.jpg","title":"Jamestown in James Valley","desc":"The town seen from the heights above it, filling the floor of the narrow valley that carries it down to the sea.","credit":"Luke McKernan, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Looking_down_on_Jamestown_%2826208606467%29.jpg","alt":"A small town of pale roofs packed into the floor of a steep, bare-sided valley."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-728","num":728,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","answer":"Brades","answerText":"Brades","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eruption began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1995</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plymouth left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2010</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Brades</b> is where the government of Montserrat sits, in the north of a British territory in the Leeward Islands of the eastern Caribbean, 43 kilometres (27 miles) south-west of Antigua.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The whole island is volcanic, and in July 1995 the Soufrière Hills volcano, dormant for more than 400 years, erupted with devastating effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That eruption destroyed the capital, Plymouth, which was abandoned in 1997, and about 70 per cent of the population was evacuated and relocated out of the southern part of the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations still records Plymouth as the capital and notes that most government offices are now based in Brades, while its statistics division simply names the capital city \"Brades Estate\" and gives it 500 people for 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the island's 103 square kilometres (40 square miles), only 40.1 (15 square miles) are habitable, and the exclusive economic zone around it runs to 7,582 square kilometres (2,927 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The population is about 4,400, at 42.3 people to the square kilometre, and gross domestic product was 80 million US dollars in 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legislature is set out in the Montserrat Constitution Order 2010: a Legislative Assembly of nine elected members and two ex officio members, the Attorney-General and the Financial Secretary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The island's small farmers and labourers were freed slaves, poorer Irish and English settlers and Catholic refugees from the English Civil War, who could buy only marginal land in and around Plymouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1811 some 94 per cent of the local population were enslaved African labourers, and the sugar industry had already been destabilised by market forces, natural hazards and disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The volcano has not finished with the island: the chance of a lahar at Soufrière Hills scales directly with rainfall intensity, and heavy rain has repeatedly triggered eruptive activity there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Montserrat,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ms.html. [Open access]","Special Committee on the Situation with regard to the Implementation of the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples, “Montserrat: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2024/10, 22 March 2024, https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?s=A/AC.109/2024/10&l=e&t=pdf. [Open access]","The Montserrat Constitution Order 2010, SI 2010/2474, s. 47-48, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2010/2474/made. [Open access]","Jenni Barclay et al., “Disaster Aid? Mapping Historical Responses to Volcanic Eruptions from 1800–2000 in the English-Speaking Eastern Caribbean: Their Role in Creating Vulnerability,” <i>Disasters</i> 46, no. S1 (2022): S10–S50, https://doi.org/10.1111/disa.12537. [Open access]","Jamie I. Farquharson and Falk Amelung, “Volcanic Hazard Exacerbated by Future Global Warming-Driven Increase in Heavy Rainfall,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 9, no. 7 (2022): 220275, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsos.220275. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Montserrat","dot":"Brades"},"facts":[["Country","Montserrat"],["Population","?"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Brades_administration_2019.jpg/1280px-Brades_administration_2019.jpg","title":"Brades, looking west to the sea","desc":"The government buildings along the coast at Brades, in the north of Montserrat, seen from the hillside above.","credit":"Martin Mergili, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brades_administration_2019.jpg","alt":"Low pale government buildings strung along a green coastal shelf above a dark blue sea."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-729","num":729,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","answer":"Stanley","answerText":"Stanley","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2008</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Stanley</b> is the capital of the Falkland Islands, a South Atlantic territory of two large islands, East and West Falkland, and hundreds of smaller ones, about 480 kilometres (300 miles) east of the South American mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The islands cover about 12,173 square kilometres (4,700 square miles) and hold roughly 4,000 people, at 0.3 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everyone lives in the capital: the census of 10 October 2021 counted 2,848 people present in Stanley out of 3,541 in the islands, and put its total usually resident population at 2,974 of 3,662.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which figure counts as Stanley's population depends on the definition — 2,538 were both usually resident and present that night — and the United Nations statistics division gives the capital 2,300 for 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was the 25th census held in the islands, which count themselves every five years, the first having been taken by a resident Governor in 1842.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Sovereignty is disputed, and the United Nations records that \"a dispute exists between the Government of Argentina and that of the United Kingdom concerning sovereignty over the Falkland Islands (Malvinas)\", as established by General Assembly resolution 2065 (XX) and later resolutions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The statistics division attaches the same note to every figure it publishes for the territory, which it files under South America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Falkland Islands Constitution Order 2008 divides the islands into two constituencies, Camp and Stanley, the first returning three elected members to the Legislative Assembly and the second five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That Assembly has eight elected members and two ex officio members without a vote, the Chief Executive and the Director of Finance, under a Governor appointed by the Crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Kingdom's Ministry of Defence keeps a garrison in the islands, British Forces South Atlantic Islands, based at Mount Pleasant Complex, where the census counted 324 resident civilians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Falkland Islands (Malvinas),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/fk.html. [Open access]","Special Committee on the Situation with regard to the Implementation of the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples, “Falkland Islands (Malvinas): Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2024/6, 26 February 2024, https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?s=A/AC.109/2024/6&l=e&t=pdf. [Open access]","Falkland Islands Government, <i>Falkland Islands 2021 Census Report</i> (Stanley: Policy, Economy and Corporate Services, 2024), table 1, https://www.falklands.gov.fk/policy/downloads?task=download.send&id=219:falkland-islands-2021-census-report&catid=13. [Open access]","The Falkland Islands Constitution Order 2008, SI 2008/2846, schedule, ss. 26-27, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2008/2846/schedule/made. [Open access]","Falkland Islands Government, “Census 2021 Findings,” https://www.falklands.gov.fk/policy/2021-census/census. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Falkland Is.","dot":"Stanley"},"facts":[["Country","Falkland Islands (Malvinas)"],["Population","2.97k (2021 census)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Aerial_photo_Port_Stanley_edit.jpg/1280px-Aerial_photo_Port_Stanley_edit.jpg","title":"Stanley from the air","desc":"The town strung along the south shore of Stanley Harbour, with the treeless camp of East Falkland behind it.","credit":"Tom L-C, edited by TSP, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_photo_Port_Stanley_edit.jpg","alt":"A small town of brightly roofed houses along a sheltered inlet, with bare moorland hills beyond."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-730","num":730,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","answer":"Kingston (Norfolk Island)","answerText":"Kingston (Norfolk Island)","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1788</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pitcairners arrived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 June 1856</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Heritage plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 October 2016</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Kingston</b> is the capital of Norfolk Island, an external territory of Australia lying in the Pacific between the Australian mainland and New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island held 2,188 people at the census of 2021, with a median age of 50, in 1,220 private dwellings at an average of 2.1 people to a household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ancestry is unlike anywhere else in the country: 550 islanders, a quarter of them, gave Pitcairn and 319 gave Norfolk Islander, against 1,123 and 970 respectively in the whole of Australia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So is its speech, since 667 people, 30.5 per cent of the island, speak Norf'k-Pitcairn at home out of 907 speakers in the entire country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A national park and botanic garden declared in 1986 covers 19 per cent of the island's land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Polynesian seafarers lived on the island between the 13th and 15th centuries, and it was empty when James Cook reached it in 1774.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Britain settled it in March 1788, five weeks after the First Fleet reached Sydney, for the pines and flax Cook had reported; neither served for masts or sails, but the soil made the island an early food supply for the mainland colony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That settlement was abandoned in 1814, a second convict settlement followed from 1825, and it was wound down once transportation to New South Wales ended, leaving 11 people by 1855.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On 8 June 1856 the 196 Pitcairn Islanders arrived, descendants of the Bounty mutineers and their Tahitian wives, and lived at first in the convict buildings of Kingston before taking up land grants of 50 acres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The territory is governed under the Norfolk Island Act 1979, and the historic area at Kingston and Arthur's Vale is managed under a Commonwealth heritage plan made on 13 October 2016.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Australian Bureau of Statistics, “Norfolk Island: 2021 Census All Persons QuickStats,” area code 901041004, https://www.abs.gov.au/census/find-census-data/quickstats/2021/901041004. [Open access]","Parks Australia, “About Us,” Norfolk Island National Park, https://parksaustralia.gov.au/norfolk/about/about-us/. [Open access]","Parks Australia, “History,” Norfolk Island National Park, https://parksaustralia.gov.au/norfolk/discover/history/. [Open access]","Norfolk Island Act 1979 (Cth), no. 35 of 1979, compilation C2025C00004, https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2004A02035/latest/text. [Open access]","Kingston and Arthur’s Vale Historic Area Heritage Management Plan 2016 (Cth), F2016L01891, made 13 October 2016, https://www.legislation.gov.au/F2016L01891/latest/text. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Norfolk Island","dot":"Kingston (Norfolk Island)"},"facts":[["Country","Norfolk Island (Australia)"],["Population","?"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Kingston_from_Flagstaff_Hill.jpg/1280px-Kingston_from_Flagstaff_Hill.jpg","title":"Kingston from Flagstaff Hill","desc":"The convict-era buildings of Kingston on the flat above Slaughter Bay, with Norfolk Island pines above and Nepean and Phillip Islands offshore.","credit":"Inas, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingston_from_Flagstaff_Hill.jpg","alt":"Pale Georgian stone buildings on a green coastal flat beside a reef-fringed bay, with tall conifers on the slope above."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-731","num":731,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Alofi","answerText":"Alofi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">New Zealand rule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1901</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 October 1974</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 September 2023</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Alofi</b> is the capital of Niue, a self-governing state in free association with New Zealand that lies in Polynesia and covers 260 square kilometres (100 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is two villages rather than one: the census of 2022 counted 423 people in Alofi South and 187 in Alofi North, together 610 of an island population of 1,681.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That island count was 38 fewer than in 2017 and continues a fall running since 1966, after the heavy emigration of the 1970s and 1980s; 1,564 people, 93 per cent, called Niue their usual residence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Density is 6 people to the square kilometre and the average household holds three, and Niueans are New Zealand citizens, which is why so many of them live in Auckland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division gives the capital 700 people for 2018 and records the New Zealand dollar as the currency in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After a plea from missionaries and island leaders the island became a British protectorate at the turn of the 20th century, and New Zealand took over responsibility for it in 1901.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stayed a New Zealand territory until the Constitution of Niue came into force on 19 October 1974 under the Niue Constitution Act 1974 of the New Zealand Parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Niue Assembly has 20 members, 14 elected by village constituencies — Alofi South and Alofi North returning one each — and 6 from a common roll, and they choose the premier from among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The head of state is King Charles III in right of New Zealand, represented by the Governor-General of New Zealand, though executive authority is exercised by the Niue Cabinet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United States recognised Niue as a sovereign and independent state on 25 September 2023, when Secretary of State Antony Blinken and Premier Dalton Tagelagi signed a joint statement establishing diplomatic relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Niue,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nu.html. [Open access]","Niue Statistics Office, <i>2022 Niue Census of Population and Household Report</i> (Alofi: Ministry of Finance and Planning, 2023), table 1.2, https://niuestatistics.nu/download/35/census/2793/2022-niue-census-of-population-and-housing-report.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Niue,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/niue. [Open access]","Government of Niue, “Government,” https://www.gov.nu/government. [Open access]","A. H. Angelo and Weichu Huang, comps., <i>Constitution of Niue and Associated Documents as at 31 December 2019</i> (Alofi: Government of Niue, 2020), 61, https://www.gov.nu/media/pages/information/5304ad08d7-1764012786/niue_constitution_ul2022.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Niue","dot":"Alofi"},"facts":[["Country","Niue"],["Population","610in Alofi (2022 census)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Main_street_of_Alofi.jpg/1280px-Main_street_of_Alofi.jpg","title":"The main street of Alofi","desc":"Alofi's main street late in the afternoon, looking north past shops and palms towards the interior of the island.","credit":"Pseudopanax, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_street_of_Alofi.jpg","alt":"A wide empty road through a small town of low buildings under coconut palms, with wooded hills behind."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-733","num":733,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the territory shaded around it.","answer":"Adamstown","answerText":"Adamstown","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sighted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1767</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mutineers landed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 January 1790</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 February 2010</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Marine reserve</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 2015</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Adamstown</b> is the capital of the Pitcairn Islands and their only settlement, on the one inhabited island of a British territory lying midway between New Zealand and South America at 25° south and 130° west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The group is four islands — Pitcairn, Henderson, Ducie and Oeno — whose land comes to 35.5 square kilometres (13.7 square miles), of which Pitcairn itself is 4.35 square kilometres (1.7 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its population was 42 in 2022, a figure that includes people temporarily abroad, and the official languages are English and Pitkern, a mixture of 18th-century English and Tahitian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Islanders live on tourism, the sale of stamps and coins, handicrafts and honey, with budgetary aid from the United Kingdom, and they use the New Zealand dollar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The exclusive economic zone around the islands, 842,000 square kilometres (325,000 square miles), is now a marine reserve, declared in March 2015 and among the largest on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Captain Philip Carteret of HMS Swallow sighted the island in July 1767 and could not land through the surf, naming it for the young gentleman who saw it first, a son of Major Pitcairn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Early European visitors found relics of a Polynesian settlement — stone gods, carvings in the cliff faces, burial sites, earth ovens and adzes — but nobody was living there when the Bounty arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fletcher Christian brought the mutineers to Pitcairn on 15 January 1790, with six Tahitian men and twelve Tahitian women, and the ship was run ashore in what is now Bounty Bay and burned so that nothing of her would show from the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They made rough leaf shelters where the village of Adamstown now stands, and John Adams, a Cockney orphan among them, outlived the rest to become the settlement's patriarch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Pitcairn Constitution Order 2010, made at Buckingham Palace on 10 February 2010, the territory has an elected Island Council, and the Governor's order of 29 July 2010 appoints Adamstown, and any place within New Zealand, as places where the Magistrate's Court may sit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Special Committee on the Situation with regard to the Implementation of the Declaration on the Granting of Independence to Colonial Countries and Peoples, “Pitcairn: Working Paper Prepared by the Secretariat,” A/AC.109/2024/12, 21 February 2024, https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?s=A/AC.109/2024/12&l=e&t=pdf. [Open access]","Government of the Pitcairn Islands, “Pitcairn’s History,” https://www.government.pn/history. [Open access]","Government of the Pitcairn Islands, “Pitcairn’s Environment,” https://www.government.pn/environment. [Open access]","<i>The Constitution of Pitcairn</i>, Revised Edition, order of the Governor under s. 43(4) appointing places at which the Magistrate’s Court may sit, 29 July 2010, https://www.government.pn/s/The-Constitution-of-Pitcairn-c2a9.pdf. [Open access]","The Pitcairn Constitution Order 2010, SI 2010/244, made 10 February 2010, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2010/244/made. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Pitcairn Is.","dot":"Adamstown"},"facts":[["Country","Pitcairn Islands"],["Population","42 (2022)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","-10.7% (2013–2019)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Adamstown.jpg/1280px-Adamstown.jpg","title":"Adamstown from Gannet's Ridge","desc":"The houses of Adamstown scattered among the green slopes above the sea, seen from the ridge behind the settlement.","credit":"doublecnz, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adamstown.jpg","alt":"Scattered white houses among dense green vegetation on a steep island slope above a deep blue sea."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-501","num":501,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"New Delhi","answerText":"New Delhi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Move announced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1911</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named New Delhi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 February 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 February 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 August 1947</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>New Delhi</b> is the capital of India, and it is a small municipality inside a much larger city: its council governs about 3 per cent of the area and 3 per cent of the population of the National Capital Territory of Delhi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That council area is the ground laid out as Lutyens' Delhi, and it holds Rashtrapati Bhavan, Parliament House, the Supreme Court, the North and South Blocks and every foreign diplomatic mission in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Government of India is nearly the sole landowner there and owns about 80 per cent of the buildings, private ownership being marginal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division names New Delhi as the capital and gives it 29.4 million people, but its own footnote says that figure is the Delhi metropolitan area, which crosses state boundaries to take in Faridabad, Gurgaon and Ghaziabad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> India itself covers 3,287,263 square kilometres (1,269,219 square miles), holds about 1.46 billion people at 492 to the square kilometre, and joined the United Nations on 30 October 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On 12 December 1911 the British government announced that the capital would move from Calcutta to Delhi, and that Delhi would be the viceroy's residence and the new administrative centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A committee examined several sites and chose Raisina Hill, and the English planners Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker laid out the avenues, the viceroy's palace and the secretariat buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Imperial Delhi Committee was constituted on 25 March 1913, and in February 1916 the Chief Commissioner created a Raisina Municipal Committee, whose first duty was the sanitation of the men building the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That body resolved on 22 February 1927 to take the name New Delhi, and the new capital was officially opened on 15 February 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Delhi is one of India's union territories, and the United States recognised the Union of India as an independent state on 15 August 1947, when President Truman sent a congratulatory message to Lord Mountbatten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “India,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/in.html. [Open access]","New Delhi Municipal Council, “History,” https://www.ndmc.gov.in/ndmc/history.aspx. [Open access]","New Delhi Municipal Council, “Introduction,” https://www.ndmc.gov.in/ndmc/introduction.aspx. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “India,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/india. [Open access]","Ministry of External Affairs, Government of India, “Glance @ India: States and Union Territories,” https://www.mea.gov.in/india-at-glance.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"India","dot":"New Delhi"},"facts":[["Country","India"],["Population","250k (2011)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","-14.7% (2001–2011)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Rajpath%2C_New_Delhi_%2829730313276%29.jpg/1280px-Rajpath%2C_New_Delhi_%2829730313276%29.jpg","title":"The central axis of New Delhi","desc":"Looking west up the ceremonial avenue towards Raisina Hill, the North and South Blocks on either side and the dome of Rashtrapati Bhavan closing the view.","credit":"Ankur Panchbudhe, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajpath%2C_New_Delhi_%2829730313276%29.jpg","alt":"A broad straight avenue running between lawns and trees towards two large sandstone blocks and a distant domed palace."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-502","num":502,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Beijing","answerText":"Beijing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Seat to Nanjing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1928</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 June 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">PRC recognised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 1979</span></div>","traditional":"北京","hanzi":"北京","pinyin":"Běijīng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Běijīng</span></div>","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Beijing</b> is the capital of China, named as such by article 143 of the constitution, and it is a municipality rather than a city inside a province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The seventh national census counted 21,893,095 permanent residents in that municipality in 2020, which makes it one of the largest cities on earth by any reckoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division gives the capital 20 million for 2019, but its footnote says the figure covers only the city districts, excluding Yanqing, that meet criteria such as contiguous built-up area or the seat of a local government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> China itself covers 9,600,000 square kilometres (3,700,000 square miles) and holds about 1.42 billion people at 148 to the square kilometre, figures that exclude Hong Kong, Macao and Taiwan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water has long been scarce in the city, and groundwater was drawn down so heavily that a diversion canal from the south is now refilling it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Beijing has not always been the capital: the Nationalist government moved the seat to Nanjing in 1928, and the United States kept its legation in the northern city all the same, raising it to an embassy on 18 June 1935.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It kept its mission there through the undeclared war with Japan from 1937, while its ambassador began in August 1936 to live intermittently near the Chinese government instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Formal recognition between the two states goes back much further, to about 16 June 1844, when Caleb Cushing presented his credentials to the Qing official Qiying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A United States consulate had opened in Beijing in 1861, one of more than twenty consular posts established in Chinese cities between 1844 and 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On 1 January 1979 the United States recognised the People's Republic as the sole legitimate government of China, and its liaison office in Beijing became an embassy on 1 March that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “China,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cn.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the People’s Republic of China</i>, art. 143, State Council of the People’s Republic of China, https://english.www.gov.cn/archive/lawsregulations/201911/20/content_WS5ed8856ec6d0b3f0e9499913.html. [Open access]","National Bureau of Statistics of China, “Communiqué of the Seventh National Population Census (No. 3) — Population by Region,” 11 May 2021, https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/PressRelease/202105/t20210510_1817188.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “China,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/china. [Open access]","Yuan Li, Rui Wang, Hongbo Ma, and Jian-Min Zhang, “Rising Groundwater Table Due to Restoration Projects Amplifies Earthquake Induced Liquefaction Risk in Beijing,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 1466, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-56525-2. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"China","dot":"Beijing"},"facts":[["Country","China"],["Population","21.9M (2020)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","+41.2% (2005–2017)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Jingshan_Farview_of_Beijing_CBD_-_Flickr.jpg/1280px-Jingshan_Farview_of_Beijing_CBD_-_Flickr.jpg","title":"Beijing from Jingshan","desc":"Looking east from the hill behind the Forbidden City, over the low grey courtyards of the old city to the towers of the central business district.","credit":"Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jingshan_Farview_of_Beijing_CBD_-_Flickr.jpg","alt":"A hazy view from a wooded hill over a pavilion roof and low grey blocks to a cluster of tall office towers on the horizon."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-504","num":504,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Jakarta","answerText":"Jakarta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 November 1801</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 December 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 September 1950</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nusantara budget</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2025 to 2029</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jakarta</b> is the capital of Indonesia, on the north-west coast of Java, and it is the seat from which the country is still governed while a new capital is built on another island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division gives the capital 10.6 million people for 2019, a figure its own footnote describes as the functional urban area rather than the city proper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Indonesia holds about 286 million people at 150 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files the country under South-eastern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its surface area is 1,910,931 square kilometres (737,814 square miles) by the United Nations reckoning and 1,916,907 (740,121 square miles) by the World Bank's, a difference of three parts in a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The country joined the United Nations on 28 September 1950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The city is older than the country and has been called other things: the United States opened its first consular post in what is now Indonesia there on 24 November 1801, when the place was a Dutch colony and the city was Batavia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States recognised the Republic of the United States of Indonesia on 28 December 1949, when Ambassador H. Merle Cochran presented his credentials to President Sukarno.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Indonesia is building a new capital called Nusantara in East Kalimantan, on the island of Borneo, where the president spoke at a groundbreaking in September 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The work is overseen by a Nusantara Capital Authority, and about 48.8 trillion rupiah was allocated to it for the years 2025 to 2029.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The State Palace and the Garuda Palace there were finished first, with the legislative and judicial buildings still to come, and the president was receiving the ministers responsible at the Merdeka Palace in Jakarta in February 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Indonesia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/id.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Indonesia,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/indonesia. [Open access]","Cabinet Secretariat of the Republic of Indonesia, “President Prabowo Urges Completion of Legislative, Judicial Infrastructure in IKN,” 3 February 2025, https://setkab.go.id/en/president-prabowo-urges-completion-of-legislative-judicial-infrastructure-in-ikn/. [Open access]","Cabinet Secretariat of the Republic of Indonesia, “Remarks of President of the Republic of Indonesia During Groundbreaking of Teras Hutan Ibu Kota Nusantara by Plataran, Nusantara Capital, E. Kalimantan Province, September 25, 2024,” https://setkab.go.id/en/remarks-of-president-of-the-republic-of-indonesia-during-groundbreaking-of-teras-hutan-ibu-kota-nusantara-by-plataran-nusantara-capital-e-kalimantan-province-september-25-2024/. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Indonesia,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/IDN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Indonesia","dot":"Jakarta"},"facts":[["Country","Indonesia"],["Population","11.1M (2024)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+5.4% (2020–2024)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Jakarta_CBD_from_South_Jakarta.jpg/1280px-Jakarta_CBD_from_South_Jakarta.jpg","title":"Jakarta from the south","desc":"The towers of the Golden Triangle business district standing above the low green suburbs of South Jakarta.","credit":"BimTja, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jakarta_CBD_from_South_Jakarta.jpg","alt":"A dense cluster of glass towers on the horizon above a wide expanse of low red-roofed houses and trees."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-506","num":506,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Abuja","answerText":"Abuja","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 October 1960</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Abuja</b> is the capital of Nigeria, and the constitution says so in terms: the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja \"shall be the Capital of the Federation and seat of the Government of the Federation\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same chapter vests the ownership of all land in the territory in the federal government and defines its boundaries in a schedule to the constitution itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territory is treated as though it were a state, so the powers that elsewhere belong to a state assembly, a governor and state courts belong instead to the National Assembly, the President and the federal courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division gives the capital 3.1 million people for 2019, a figure its footnote describes as the urban agglomeration rather than the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nigeria holds about 238 million people at 261 to the square kilometre, and roughly 151 million of them, some 64 per cent, live in towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The country covers 923,768 square kilometres (356,669 square miles) by the United Nations reckoning and 923,770 by the World Bank's, which is agreement to two parts in a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nigeria was under British sovereignty until 1960, and the United States recognised the Federation of Nigeria on 1 October that year in a message from Secretary of State Christian Herter broadcast on Voice of America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A congratulatory letter from President Eisenhower reached Prime Minister Abubakar Tafawa Balewa the next day, and Nigeria joined the United Nations on 7 October 1960.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Abuja was not then the capital: the American embassy opened on 1 October 1960 in Lagos, with Joseph Palmer II in charge until he presented his credentials three days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Nigerian embassy in Washington opened the same day under a chargé d'affaires, M. J. Garba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Nigeria,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ng.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria 1999</i> (with amendments through 2011), ss. 297-299, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Nigeria_2011. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Nigeria,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/nigeria. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Nigeria,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NGA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]","World Bank, “Urban Population: Nigeria,” indicator SP.URB.TOTL, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NGA/indicator/SP.URB.TOTL?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Nigeria","dot":"Abuja"},"facts":[["Country","Nigeria"],["Population","1.69M (2022)"],["Rank in the country","7th largest"],["Population growth","+26.9% (2011–2015)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Hilltop_view_of_Abuja.jpg/1280px-Hilltop_view_of_Abuja.jpg","title":"Abuja from the hills","desc":"The planned city spread below the surrounding hills, the gold dome of the national mosque at the left and the central business district on the horizon.","credit":"Umasoyee, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilltop_view_of_Abuja.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a low green city of scattered villas, with office towers and a gold-domed mosque in the hazy distance."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-508","num":508,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Dhaka","answerText":"Dhaka","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US consulate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 August 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 April 1972</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commonwealth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">joined 1972</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1974</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Dhaka</b> is the capital of Bangladesh, named as such by article 5 of the constitution, which adds that the boundaries of the capital shall be determined by law rather than fixing them itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division gives the city 20.3 million people for 2019, a figure its own footnote labels simply \"mega city\" without saying where the city is held to end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bangladesh holds about 176 million people at 1,350 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files the country under Southern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is 147,570 square kilometres (56,977 square miles) by both the Commonwealth Secretariat's reckoning and the World Bank's, against the 148,460 the United Nations gives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth's own profile puts the population at 164.7 million for 2022 and names Dhaka as the capital city, so the two bodies differ by about eleven million and by three years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The territory was the eastern part of Pakistan, and the United States had only limited contact with it as a British colony, establishing consular relations there after Pakistan was formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A United States consulate opened in Dhaka, then written Dacca, on 29 August 1949, when what is now Bangladesh was still the eastern wing of Pakistan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dates institutions give for what followed differ: the United States recognised Bangladesh on 4 April 1972, the Commonwealth records it joining in 1972 after independence from Pakistan, and it entered the United Nations on 17 September 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recognition came in a press statement from Secretary of State William Rogers, with Herbert Spivack, the principal American officer in Dhaka, carrying a message from President Nixon to Prime Minister Sheikh Mujibur Rahman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American embassy at Dhaka were established on 18 May 1972, and the first ambassador presented his credentials on 13 April 1974.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bangladesh,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bd.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the People’s Republic of Bangladesh</i> (with amendments through 2014), art. 5, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Bangladesh_2014. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Bangladesh,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/bangladesh. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Bangladesh,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/bangladesh. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Bangladesh,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BGD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bangladesh","dot":"Dhaka"},"facts":[["Country","Bangladesh"],["Population","16.8M (2017)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+52.6% (2011–2022)"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Dhaka_City_View_09.jpg/1280px-Dhaka_City_View_09.jpg","title":"Dhaka from a rooftop","desc":"Blocks of flats and trees running to the horizon in Dhaka, one of the most closely built cities in the world.","credit":"Asivechowdhury, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhaka_City_View_09.jpg","alt":"A view over a dense low-rise city of concrete blocks and green trees stretching to a flat horizon under a blue sky."},"questions":[]},
{"id":"gw-509","num":509,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Moscow","answerText":"Moscow","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 October 1803</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 July 1809</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 December 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Moscow</b> is the capital of Russia, named as such by article 70 of the constitution, which adds that the status of the capital shall be established by federal law rather than fixing it itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same text counts cities of federal significance among the kinds of constituent entity the federation is made of and files Moscow as one, so the city is a region of Russia in its own right as well as the seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division gives the city 12.5 million people, but the footnote on that row is a date rather than a boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It marks the figure 2019 in a column headed 2025 and says nothing about where the city is held to end, so the count is pinned in time and not in space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia itself holds about 144 million people at 8.8 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files the country under Eastern Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its area is 17,098,246 square kilometres (6,601,670 square miles) by that reckoning, while the World Bank holds that figure through 2019 and steps up to 17,125,190 from 2020, leaving the two about 27,000 square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised Russia on 28 October 1803, when Alexander I issued a decree accepting Levett Harris as American consul at St Petersburg; formal relations followed on 14 July 1809.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American mission stood at St Petersburg rather than Moscow for over a century, and in 1918 left for Vologda as German troops drew near.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It moved on to Archangel that August, after Bolshevik authorities pressed the missions at Vologda to come instead to Moscow, and closed in September 1919; relations resumed on 16 November 1933, and William Bullitt presented his credentials in Moscow that December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Moscow was the stage for the failed coup of August 1991, with Boris Yeltsin speaking from atop a tank outside the Russian parliament building, and the United States recognised the Russian Federation as the Soviet Union’s successor that 25 December, keeping its embassy there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Russian Federation,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ru.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Russian Federation</i> (with amendments through 2014), arts. 5, 65 and 70, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Russia_2014. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Russia,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/russia. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Collapse of the Soviet Union, 1989–1991,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1989-1992/collapse-soviet-union. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Russian Federation,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/RUS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Russia","dot":"Moscow"},"facts":[["Country","Russia"],["Population","13.3M (2025)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+1.1% (2021–2024)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Moscow_panoramic_October_2008.jpg/1280px-Moscow_panoramic_October_2008.jpg","title":"Moscow along the Moskva","desc":"The Moskva river running through central Moscow, with the government building on the far bank and one of the city's Stalin-era towers behind it.","credit":"Антон Денисов, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moscow_panoramic_October_2008.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a large city, a broad river curving through the middle of it past tall blocks, a white government building and a tiered tower with a spire."}},
{"id":"gw-511","num":511,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Mexico City","answerText":"Mexico City","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 December 1822</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 June 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 January 1899</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 November 1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mexico City</b> is the capital of Mexico, and article 44 of the constitution makes it the federal entity that is the seat of the Powers of the Union, rather than a city inside a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same article provides for the powers moving elsewhere, in which case the city would be erected into a state under the name Ciudad de México; article 122 already gives it autonomy over its internal affairs and a constitution of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division puts the capital at 21.7 million people, and the row carries two footnote letters where most countries carry one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first dates it to 2019 and the second says it counts the total population of 76 municipalities of the Metropolitan Area of Mexico City, so the number is metropolitan and far wider than the entity the constitution describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mexico itself holds about 132 million people at 67.3 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files the country under Central America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its area is 1,964,375 square kilometres (758,449 square miles) by that reckoning, a figure the World Bank matched for 2016 and 2017 before its series drifted down to 1,957,194 by 2023 with nothing said about why.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised Mexico on 12 December 1822, when President James Monroe received José Manuel Zozaya as Mexican minister, and the American legation opened in the city itself on 1 June 1825.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Joel Roberts Poinsett presented his credentials there to President Guadalupe Victoria, and the legation was raised to embassy status on 3 January 1899 under Porfirio Díaz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American forces captured the city in September 1847, and the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo that ended the war was signed there on 2 February 1848, ceding about 525,000 square miles of Mexican territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Holding the capital mattered in Mexico’s own politics too: in 1859 the United States recognised Benito Juárez’s liberal government as the only legitimate one despite its sitting outside the capital, which the rival claimant Miguel Miramón was occupying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Mexico,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/mx.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitución Política de los Estados Unidos Mexicanos</i> (last reform published in the Diario Oficial de la Federación, 2 June 2026), arts. 44 and 122, Cámara de Diputados del H. Congreso de la Unión, https://www.diputados.gob.mx/LeyesBiblio/pdf/CPEUM.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Mexico,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/mexico. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Annexation of Texas, the Mexican-American War, and the Treaty of Guadalupe-Hidalgo, 1845–1848,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/texas-annexation. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Mexico,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, values for 2016 and 2017, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MEX/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Mexico","dot":"Mexico City"},"facts":[["Country","Mexico"],["Population","9.21M (2020)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+7.6% (2010–2019)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ciudad_de_M%C3%A9xico%2C_a%C3%A9rea_1.jpg/1280px-Ciudad_de_M%C3%A9xico%2C_a%C3%A9rea_1.jpg","title":"Mexico City in its valley","desc":"The built-up area running to the hills on every side of the Valley of Mexico, with old volcanic cones standing out of it.","credit":"LBM1948, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_de_M%C3%A9xico,_a%C3%A9rea_1.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a vast low city filling a wide valley to the horizon, with green volcanic cones rising out of the built-up ground."}},
{"id":"gw-512","num":512,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Tokyo","answerText":"Tokyo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mutual recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 March 1854</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 July 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 May 1906</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1956</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Tokyo</b> is the capital of Japan, and the figure given for its population depends on which of three official boundaries is meant. Japan’s statistics bureau counts 9.73 million in the 23 Cities of Tokyo Metropolis, the largest single city in the country, and 14.05 million in Tokyo Metropolis as a whole, the most populous of the 47 prefectures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations statistics division gives the capital 37.4 million, marks it as referring to major metropolitan areas and dates it to 2019, and never says what such an area is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bureau does say: the Kanto major metropolitan area held 38.03 million in 2020 across 13,561 square kilometres, and its central cities are the 23 Cities together with Yokohama, Kawasaki, Sagamihara, Saitama and Chiba, plus surrounding towns and villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the United Nations figure counts five other cities as centres in their own right, and the three official numbers differ by a factor of almost four without any being wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Japan holds about 123 million people at 326.7 to the square kilometre and is filed under Eastern Asia, and its area is 377,976 square kilometres (145,937 square miles) as of January 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank comes within seven square kilometres of that, while the United Nations gives 377,930 and notes that its data refer to 1 October 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Commodore Matthew Perry sailed into the harbour of Edo, now Tokyo and then Japan’s capital, on 8 July 1853 with a letter from President Millard Fillmore addressed to the Emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Addressing it so showed how little the United States then understood the country, the emperor being little more than a figurehead while the Tokugawa shogunate held the leadership; he gained formal control only with the Meiji Restoration of 1868.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two states recognised each other on 31 March 1854, full relations followed on 29 July 1858 with a treaty signed at Edo, the American legation at Tokyo became an embassy on 26 May 1906, and it reopened on 9 May 1952 after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Japan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jp.html. [Open access]","Statistics Bureau, Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, <i>Statistical Handbook of Japan 2025</i>, tables 1.1, 2.10 and 2.11, https://www.stat.go.jp/english/data/handbook/pdf/2025all.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Japan,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/japan. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The United States and the Opening to Japan, 1853,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/opening-to-japan. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Japan,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/JPN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Japan","dot":"Tokyo"},"facts":[["Country","Japan"],["Population","14.3M (2022)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+3.5% (2015–2020)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Skyscrapers_of_Shinjuku_2009_January_%28revised%29.jpg/1280px-Skyscrapers_of_Shinjuku_2009_January_%28revised%29.jpg","title":"Tokyo, with Mount Fuji behind","desc":"The towers of Shinjuku standing out of the low city, with Mount Fuji on the horizon about a hundred kilometres away.","credit":"Morio, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyscrapers_of_Shinjuku_2009_January_%28revised%29.jpg","alt":"A cluster of tall office towers rising from a wide low-rise city, with a snow-capped volcano on the horizon behind them."}},
{"id":"gw-754","num":754,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the administrative capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Putrajaya","answerText":"Putrajaya","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kuala Lumpur</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Labuan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Putrajaya</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2001</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1957</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Putrajaya</b> is the administrative capital of Malaysia, and the United Nations statistics division records the split seat in a footnote on the capital-city field itself, saying that Kuala Lumpur is the capital and Putrajaya the administrative one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitution makes the arrangement territorial as well as administrative: article 1 excludes from the state of Selangor the Federal Territory of Putrajaya, established under the Constitution (Amendment) Act 2001, and counts it a territory of the federation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kuala Lumpur was taken out of Selangor the same way under a 1973 amendment, and Labuan out of Sabah in 1984, so all three federal territories are ground carved from a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the middle of the three by population: the Department of Statistics Malaysia gives it 121,400 people for 2026, against 101,100 in Labuan and 2.08 million in Kuala Lumpur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the fastest growing of the three, up from 73,400 in 2010, a rise of about two thirds where Kuala Lumpur rose by a quarter and Labuan by a seventh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The United Nations puts the capital-city population at 7.78 million, marks it as referring to Greater Kuala Lumpur, a region wider than either federal territory, and dates it to 2019.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So three official figures are in play for one seat, and the smallest of them, the one for the city where the government actually sits, is the one no international profile reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Malaysia itself holds about 36 million people at 109.5 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files the country under South-eastern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is 330,621 square kilometres (127,653 square miles) by that reckoning, against the World Bank’s 330,411 for 2021 to 2023, the two about 210 square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The federation became independent in 1957, and the United States records commercial contact with its ports going back to the 19th century, so Malaysia is four decades older than the territory that now administers it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Malaysia,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/my.html. [Open access]","<i>Federal Constitution of Malaysia</i> (with amendments through 2007), art. 1, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Malaysia_2007. [Open access]","Department of Statistics Malaysia, “Population by State,” open data series, https://storage.dosm.gov.my/population/population_state.csv. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Malaysia,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/malaysia. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Malaysia,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, values for 2021 to 2023, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MYS/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Malaysia","dot":"Putrajaya"},"facts":[["Country","Malaysia"],["Population","109k (2020)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","+23.7% (2015–2020)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Putrajaya%2C_Malaysia_2013.JPG/1280px-Putrajaya%2C_Malaysia_2013.JPG","title":"Putrajaya from the air","desc":"The domed Putra Mosque and the prime minister's offices beside the circular Dataran Putra, with government buildings ranged around Putrajaya Lake.","credit":"Rongomai Bailey, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Putrajaya%2C_Malaysia_2013.JPG","alt":"An oblique aerial view of a planned city built around a lake, with a domed mosque beside a circular paved square and a bridge crossing the water."}},
{"id":"gw-755","num":755,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the administrative capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Abidjan","answerText":"Abidjan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Côte d’Ivoire, 1986</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace agreement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 November 1996</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Abidjan</b> is the administrative capital of Côte d’Ivoire and its largest city, and the United Nations statistics division records the divided seat in a footnote on the capital-city field itself, saying that Yamoussoukro is the capital and Abidjan the administrative one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That footnote is the only statement of the division in any work reachable here, and the profile then gives no figure for Abidjan at all: its capital-city population row carries Yamoussoukro’s 231,100 for 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The World Bank’s series for the population of a country’s largest city does give one, and for Côte d’Ivoire that city is Abidjan, at 5.87 million in 2024 against 5.20 million in 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So the administrative capital holds about 25 times the people of the capital, and the two figures have to be taken from different works because no single profile reports both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Côte d’Ivoire itself holds about 32.7 million people at 102.9 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files the country under Western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its area is 322,462 square kilometres (124,503 square miles), which the World Bank matches to within two square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Abidjan was the seat before independence as well as after: the United States recognised the Republic of the Ivory Coast on 7 August 1960, and on the same day the American consulate at Abidjan was raised to embassy status under Donald R. Norland as chargé d’affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The territory had been under French sovereignty until that date, and the state changed its name from Ivory Coast to Côte d’Ivoire in 1986.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city has also served as ground for other countries’ settlements: the peace agreement between the government of Sierra Leone and the Revolutionary United Front was signed at Abidjan on 30 November 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Article 28 of that agreement names the government of Côte d’Ivoire, the United Nations, the Organization of African Unity and the Commonwealth as moral guarantors, and President Henri Konan Bédié signed beside the two Sierra Leonean parties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Côte d’Ivoire,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/ci.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Cote d’Ivoire (Ivory Coast),” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/cote-divoire. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in the Largest City: Côte d’Ivoire,” indicator EN.URB.LCTY, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CIV/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Côte d’Ivoire,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CIV/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]","Permanent Representative of Sierra Leone to the United Nations, letter dated 11 December 1996 addressed to the Secretary-General, transmitting the Peace Agreement between the Government of the Republic of Sierra Leone and the Revolutionary United Front of Sierra Leone, signed at Abidjan on 30 November 1996, S/1996/1034, https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?s=S/1996/1034&l=e&t=pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Côte d'Ivoire","dot":"Abidjan"},"facts":[["Country","Côte d’Ivoire"],["Population","5.87M (2024)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","+73.0% (1998–2016)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Abidjan_View_of_the_plateau_district.jpg/1280px-Abidjan_View_of_the_plateau_district.jpg","title":"The Plateau, Abidjan","desc":"The towers of the Plateau business district on the edge of the Ébrié Lagoon, with the Félix Houphouët-Boigny stadium in the foreground.","credit":"Sidkumar23, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abidjan_View_of_the_plateau_district.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a city of tall office towers beside a broad lagoon, with a stadium and green trees in the foreground under a cloudy sky."}},
{"id":"gw-756","num":756,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the commercial capital and largest city of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Colombo","answerText":"Colombo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 August 1949</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Colombo Plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 December 1955</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Colombo</b> is the largest city of Sri Lanka and, in the United Nations’ reckoning, its capital: the statistics division puts Colombo in the capital-city field and footnotes it to say that Sri Jayewardenepura Kotte is the legislative capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitution takes a different route, designating no capital anywhere in its text and instead placing the state’s courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Article 132 orders that the several jurisdictions of the Supreme Court shall ordinarily be exercised at Colombo unless the Chief Justice directs otherwise, and article 146 seats the Court of Appeal there on the same terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city is named eight times in all, the rest of them administrative: an action against a member who sits in Parliament unlawfully goes to the District Court of Colombo, and Colombo heads the First Schedule’s list of administrative districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Colombo and Sri Jayewardenepura Kotte are also carved together out of a province’s public-order powers, their limits to be fixed by the President by order published in the Gazette, so the two seats are treated as one exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The United Nations gives the city 606,200 people for 2019, and the country about 23.2 million at 370.5 to the square kilometre, filed under Southern Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is 65,610 square kilometres (25,332 square miles), a figure the World Bank matches exactly for every year from 2020 to 2023.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Foreign missions have stood in Colombo since before the seat was divided: the American embassy was established there on 3 August 1949, when Felix Cole presented his credentials as ambassador.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city has also lent its name to an intergovernmental organisation that still keeps its secretariat there, the Colombo Plan, established on 1 July 1951 by Australia, Canada, India, New Zealand, Pakistan, Sri Lanka and the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It has since grown to 26 members, taking in states outside the Commonwealth and members of the Association of South-East Asian Nations and of the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Sri Lanka,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/lk.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Democratic Socialist Republic of Sri Lanka</i> (with amendments through 2015), arts. 132 and 146 and the First Schedule, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Sri_Lanka_2015. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Sri Lanka,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/sri-lanka. [Open access]","The Colombo Plan Secretariat, “The Secretariat,” https://colombo-plan.org/the-secretariat/. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Sri Lanka,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/LKA/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Sri Lanka","dot":"Colombo"},"facts":[["Country","Sri Lanka"],["Population","606k (2019)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/A_view_of_Colombo%27s_skyline_from_the_Lotus_Tower.jpg/1280px-A_view_of_Colombo%27s_skyline_from_the_Lotus_Tower.jpg","title":"Colombo from the Lotus Tower","desc":"The lit towers of central Colombo at night, with the reclaimed ground of Port City and the sea beyond them.","credit":"Thilina Alagiyawanna, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_view_of_Colombo%27s_skyline_from_the_Lotus_Tower.jpg","alt":"A night view from a tower over a lit city of high blocks and low streets, with dark sea along the far edge."}},
{"id":"gw-757","num":757,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the national congress of the country shaded around it sits.","answer":"Valparaíso","answerText":"Valparaíso","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consular cable</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 November 1914</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 October 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Country area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">756,102 km²</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Valparaíso</b> is the Chilean city where the National Congress sits, though nothing in the United Nations profile of the country says so: its capital-city field gives Santiago and carries no footnote about a second seat at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That silence is the exception rather than the rule, since the profiles of several other countries with divided seats footnote the fact on the capital-city field itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Senate says it plainly enough on its own pages, describing itself as the upper chamber that forms the National Congress together with the Chamber of Deputies and giving its address as Avenida Pedro Montt in Valparaíso, with a second at Morandé 441 in Santiago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It offers guided visits to its buildings in both cities, so the division is a working arrangement rather than a formality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chilean constitution names Valparaíso only as one of the country’s regions, in the provisions allocating seats to representatives of indigenous peoples, and designates no seat for Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Chile holds about 19.9 million people at 26.4 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files it under South America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is 756,102 square kilometres (291,933 square miles) by that reckoning, against the World Bank’s 757,245, the two about 1,100 square kilometres apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Valparaíso was the country’s door to the world long before it held the legislature: the Office of the Historian calls it Chile’s primary seaport and notes that ships from the eastern United States had to round Cape Horn to reach it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An American consulate stood there through the 19th century and into the 20th, and its vice consul cabled Washington from Valparaíso on 21 November 1914 to report German warships taking cargo and sinking a French sailing vessel off the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city on the map is therefore a port that acquired a parliament, rather than a capital that acquired a harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Chile,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/cl.html. [Open access]","Senado de la República de Chile, “Acerca del Senado,” https://www.senado.cl/acerca-del-senado. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Chile,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/chile. [Open access]","The Vice Consul at Valparaiso (Easterling) to the Secretary of State, 21 November 1914, document 1013 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1914, Supplement, The World War</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1914Supp/d1013. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Chile,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Chile</i> (with amendments through 2021), Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Chile_2021. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Chile","dot":"Valparaíso"},"facts":[["Country","Chile"],["Population","297k (2017)"],["Rank in the country","10th largest"],["Population growth","-3.9% (2017–2024)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Valparaiso_desde_cerro_mariposas.jpg/1280px-Valparaiso_desde_cerro_mariposas.jpg","title":"Valparaíso and its bay","desc":"Cargo ships lying off Valparaíso, with the painted houses of the hillside in the foreground and the coast running away beyond the water.","credit":"Felipe Guerra Valderrama, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valparaiso_desde_cerro_mariposas.jpg","alt":"Cargo ships anchored in a wide blue bay seen over the rooftops and power lines of a hillside city, with a built-up headland in the distance."}},
{"id":"gw-758","num":758,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the seat of government of the country shaded around it.","answer":"The Hague","answerText":"The Hague","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 April 1782</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 October 1782</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy back</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 August 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 December 1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>The Hague</b> is the seat of the Dutch government, though the constitution does not say so: it names Amsterdam once, as the capital city, and does not mention The Hague anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That mention comes almost in passing, article 32 ordering that the King shall be sworn in and inaugurated as soon as possible in the capital city, Amsterdam, at a joint session of the two Houses of the States General.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations records the arrangement where the constitution does not, footnoting the capital-city field of its country profile: Amsterdam is the capital and The Hague is the seat of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What fixes The Hague in law is a treaty rather than a Dutch statute: article 22 of the Statute of the International Court of Justice provides that the seat of the Court shall be established there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same article requires the President and the Registrar to reside at that seat, and the Court records that of the six principal organs of the United Nations it is the only one not located in New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The city was doing this work long before the Court existed, the Netherlands becoming the second nation to recognise the United States when The Hague accepted Minister John Adams’s credentials on 19 April 1782.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diplomatic relations and the American legation there began the same day, and a Treaty of Amity and Commerce was signed in the city on 8 October that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The legation closed in May 1801 so that the government could reduce expenditures, as Secretary of State James Madison explained, Dutch policy being by then increasingly run by Napoleon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It left for London on 15 July 1940 after the German invasion, following the Dutch government into exile, and the embassy returned to The Hague on 17 August 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Netherlands holds about 18.3 million people at 544.9 to the square kilometre in 41,543 square kilometres (16,040 square miles), a figure the World Bank puts three square kilometres lower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Netherlands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/nl.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Kingdom of the Netherlands</i> (with amendments through 2008), art. 32, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Netherlands_2008. [Open access]","<i>Statute of the International Court of Justice</i>, arts. 22, 23 and 28, International Court of Justice, https://www.icj-cij.org/statute. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The Netherlands,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/netherlands. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Netherlands,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/NLD/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, “The Court,” https://www.icj-cij.org/en/court. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Netherlands","dot":"The Hague"},"facts":[["Country","Netherlands"],["Population","548k (2021)"],["Rank in the country","3rd largest"],["Population growth","+3.1% (2020–2023)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/View_of_The_Hague_and_Scheveningen_2022.jpg/1280px-View_of_The_Hague_and_Scheveningen_2022.jpg","title":"The Hague to the sea","desc":"The ministry towers and the old city below, with the dunes and the North Sea at Scheveningen on the horizon.","credit":"Joris van Rooden, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_The_Hague_and_Scheveningen_2022.jpg","alt":"A high view over a low city of red roofs and green trees, tall modern buildings in the foreground and the sea along the far horizon."}},
{"id":"gw-759","num":759,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the economic capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Cotonou","answerText":"Cotonou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">US relations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 August 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 September 1960</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassy here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 February 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">National Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19–28 February 1990</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cotonou</b> is the largest city of Benin, and the United Nations records the country’s divided seat in a footnote on the capital-city field of its profile: Porto-Novo is the constitutional capital and Cotonou the economic capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitution earns the first half of that description outright, article 1 declaring in capitals that the Capital of the Republic of Bénin shall be PORTO-NOVO, and naming no other seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It names Cotonou all the same, in its own preamble, which records that the National Conference of Active Forces of the Nation was held there from 19 to 28 February 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That conference, the preamble says, gave the people back their confidence and permitted national reconciliation and the advent of an era of democratic revival, and the constitution was drawn up on the day after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the text that makes Porto-Novo the capital was itself produced at Cotonou, which is the sort of thing a constitution rarely admits about a city it declines to name as its seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Cotonou is much the larger of the two: the World Bank’s series for a country’s largest city gives it 1.31 million people for 2024, against the 285,300 the United Nations publishes for Porto-Novo in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has grown quickly on that reckoning, from 1.06 million in 2020, a quarter more in four years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Benin itself holds about 14.8 million people at 131.4 to the square kilometre, and the United Nations files the country under Western Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its area is 114,763 square kilometres (44,310 square miles), which the World Bank puts three square kilometres lower.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American mission has stood at Cotonou rather than at the capital almost from the beginning: relations with what was then Dahomey were established on 1 August 1960, and Embassy Cotonou followed on 15 February 1961 with Converse Hettinger as chargé d’affaires ad interim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Benin,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bj.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Republic of Benin</i> (1990), preamble and art. 1, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Benin_1990. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Benin (Dahomey),” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/benin. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in the Largest City: Benin,” indicator EN.URB.LCTY, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEN/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Benin,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BEN/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Benin","dot":"Cotonou"},"facts":[["Country","Benin"],["Population","1.31M (2024)"],["Rank in the country","largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Vue_panoramique_de_Cotonou_avenue_Steimeiz_cotonou_Benin.jpg/1280px-Vue_panoramique_de_Cotonou_avenue_Steimeiz_cotonou_Benin.jpg","title":"Cotonou from a rooftop","desc":"Motorcycle taxis and cars along an avenue in Cotonou, with low white buildings and market stalls running to the horizon.","credit":"Adoscam, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_panoramique_de_Cotonou_avenue_Steimeiz_cotonou_Benin.jpg","alt":"A view from a rooftop over a busy road with motorcycles and cars, low white and painted buildings stretching away under a pale sky."}},
{"id":"gw-760","num":760,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the seat of government of the country shaded around it.","answer":"La Paz","answerText":"La Paz","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1825</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 May 1848</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Legation here</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 January 1849</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 November 1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>La Paz</b> is where the government of Bolivia sits, and the United Nations says so in a footnote on the capital-city field of its country profile: La Paz is the seat of government and Sucre is the constitutional capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitution says the second half of that and almost nothing of the first, article 6 stating flatly that Sucre is the Capital of Bolivia and designating nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The city is never named in the text at all: the words La Paz appear exactly once, and they mean the Department, in a clause listing the provinces that make up the Bolivian Amazon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where the constitution is silent the legislature is not, the Chamber of Deputies giving its own address as the new building of the Plurinational Legislative Assembly, at Calle Comercio and Colón, in La Paz.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American record has followed the government rather than the capital from the beginning, the legation at La Paz opening on 3 January 1849 under Chargé d’Affaires John Appleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Bolivia had declared its independence from Spain on 6 August 1825, and the United States recognised it as a separate state only on 30 May 1848, having first recognised the Peru-Bolivian Confederation in 1837.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The legation was raised to embassy rank after a joint announcement by the two governments in January 1942.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No source reachable here gives a population for La Paz on its own: the World Bank’s largest-city series measures whichever city in the country is largest without saying which, and the United Nations publishes only Sucre’s 277,900 for 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bolivia’s own census counted 11,365,333 people in 2024, of whom 7.85 million were urban, against the 12.6 million the United Nations projects for 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country’s area is 1,098,581 square kilometres (424,164 square miles) on a figure the United Nations attributes to the Superintendencia Agraria, and the World Bank measures 1,098,580.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Bolivia (Plurinational State of),” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bo.html. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Plurinational State of Bolivia</i> (2009), art. 6, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Bolivia_2009. [Open access]","Cámara de Diputados del Estado Plurinacional de Bolivia, “Inicio,” https://www.diputados.bo/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Bolivia,” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/bolivia. [Open access]","Instituto Nacional de Estadística de Bolivia, “Censo Bolivia 2024: Datos y Estadísticas Clave,” https://censo.ine.gob.bo/. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Bolivia,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BOL/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Bolivia","dot":"La Paz"},"facts":[["Country","Bolivia"],["Population","756k (2024)"],["Rank in the country","3rd largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Centro_de_La_Paz_y_el_Illimani_al_fondo.jpg/1280px-Centro_de_La_Paz_y_el_Illimani_al_fondo.jpg","title":"La Paz under Illimani","desc":"Brick houses climbing the walls of the canyon La Paz is built in, tower blocks in the centre, and the snow of Illimani behind.","credit":"Matteo Leoni, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centro_de_La_Paz_y_el_Illimani_al_fondo.jpg","alt":"A city filling a steep valley, brick houses stacked up the slopes and tower blocks below, with a snow-covered mountain on the skyline."}},
{"id":"gw-761","num":761,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the legislative capital of the country shaded around it.","answer":"Lobamba","answerText":"Lobamba","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 September 1968</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 October 2004</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Eswatini, 2018</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Lobamba</b> is the legislative capital of Eswatini, one of the two seats the United Nations names in a footnote on the capital-city field of its country profile: Mbabane is the administrative capital and Lobamba the legislative one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Commonwealth Secretariat divides the work three ways rather than two, giving Mbabane the administrative and the judicial roles and Lobamba the legislature alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The constitution names neither city and defines the seat by a person instead, providing that the official residence of the Ndlovukazi is the legislative and ceremonial capital of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Ndlovukazi, it says, is traditionally the mother of the iNgwenyama and the symbolic Grandmother of the Nation, selected and appointed in accordance with Swazi law and custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That residence is named in the same clause as the arena of the Incwala and the Umhlanga, so the constitution fixes the legislative capital by whose house it is and what is held there rather than by where it stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The text itself was accepted by the Swazi National Council assembled at Ludzidzini on 4 October 2004, after being vetted by the people at tinkhundla and Sibaya meetings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eswatini holds about 1.26 million people at 72.3 to the square kilometre in the United Nations’ reckoning, where the Commonwealth counted 1.16 million in 2022.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its area is 17,363 square kilometres (6,704 square miles) on the same reckoning, which the Commonwealth rounds to 17,400 and the World Bank gives as 17,360.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United Nations publishes 68,000 for the capital city in 2018, and that figure is Mbabane’s under the very footnote that names both seats; no count is given for Lobamba at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States recognised Swaziland on 6 September 1968, the day it became independent and the day the American embassy at Mbabane was established, and the country was renamed Eswatini in 2018.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Eswatini,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/sz.html. [Open access]","Commonwealth Secretariat, “Kingdom of Eswatini,” Our Member Countries, https://thecommonwealth.org/our-member-countries/kingdom-eswatini. [Open access]","<i>Constitution of the Kingdom of Swaziland Act</i> (2005), preamble and s. 229, Constitute Project, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Swaziland_2005. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “Eswatini (Swaziland),” in <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, https://history.state.gov/countries/swaziland. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface Area (sq. km): Eswatini,” indicator AG.SRF.TOTL.K2, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SWZ/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json. [Open access]"],"difficulty":5,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"eSwatini","dot":"Lobamba"},"facts":[["Country","Eswatini"],["Population","115k (2025)"],["Rank in the country","?"],["Population growth","?"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Parliament_building_of_Eswatini%2C_Lobamba.jpg/1280px-Parliament_building_of_Eswatini%2C_Lobamba.jpg","title":"The parliament at Lobamba","desc":"The round, green-roofed parliament building of Eswatini at Lobamba, the national flag flying beside it and hills behind.","credit":"Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parliament_building_of_Eswatini,_Lobamba.jpg","alt":"A low round stone building with a wide green roof, a flag on a tall pole beside it, lawns in front and wooded hills behind."}},
{"id":"gw-223","num":223,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Wallis and Futuna","answerText":"Wallis and Futuna","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Referendum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 December 1959</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statutory law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 July 1961</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Circonscriptions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Wallis and Futuna</b> is a French overseas collectivity in the South Pacific, three main islands — Wallis on one side and Futuna with Alofi on the other, 230 kilometres apart — covering 142 square kilometres (55 square miles) between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wallis itself is a low volcanic island of 77.9 square kilometres (30 square miles), and the territory lies 22,000 kilometres from metropolitan France, 2,000 from New Caledonia and 3,000 from French Polynesia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became a French overseas territory after a referendum on 27 December 1959 carried by 94.37 per cent of those voting, and is governed under law number 61-814 of 29 July 1961.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There are no communes: the territory is divided into three administrative circonscriptions with legal personality, each with a council presided over by its own customary king, corresponding to the kingdoms of Uvea on Wallis and Alo and Sigave on Futuna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The State is represented by a prefect, styled Administrateur supérieur, who also holds the executive of the territorial collectivity, and a territorial council seats the three kings as its vice-presidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Territorial Assembly, instituted by article 11 of the 1961 statutory law, has 20 members and sits at Mata’Utu, which the administration calls the chef-lieu of the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two bodies that name the place spell it differently, the administration writing Mata’Utu and the United Nations statistics division writing Matu-Utu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2023 census counted 11,151 people, and the same administration gives the 2018 census as 11,558 on one of its pages and 11,562 on another, while the Overseas Countries and Territories Association rounds the 2023 figure to about 11,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Close to 60 per cent of the population is under twenty and emigration is heavy: more than 25,000 people of Wallis and Futuna origin are now estimated to live in New Caledonia, against about 18,000 counted there in 1996.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Public money holds the economy up, the public sector accounting for nearly 70 per cent of all salaried employment and the private sector for 31 per cent of jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Wallis and Futuna Islands,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/wf.html. [Open access]","Administration supérieure des îles Wallis et Futuna, “Présentation générale,” https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/Actualites/Presentation-de-Wallis-et-Futuna/Presentation-generale. [Open access]","Administration supérieure des îles Wallis et Futuna, “Organisation institutionnelle,” https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/Actualites/Presentation-de-Wallis-et-Futuna/Organisation-institutionnelle. [Open access]","Administration supérieure des îles Wallis et Futuna, “Présentation économique,” https://www.wallis-et-futuna.gouv.fr/Actualites/Presentation-de-Wallis-et-Futuna/Presentation-economique. [Open access]","Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “Wallis and Futuna,” https://www.overseas-association.eu/wallis-and-futuna/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Wallis and Futuna Is."},"facts":[["Capital","Mata-Utu"],["Population","11.2k (2023 census)"],["Largest city","Mata-Utu"],["Area","142 km² (55 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Wallis_barrier_reef_%28no_watermark%29.jpg/1280px-Wallis_barrier_reef_%28no_watermark%29.jpg","title":"Wallis barrier reef","desc":"The exposed reef flat and one of the wooded islets along the barrier reef that encloses the lagoon of Wallis.","credit":"Skimel and G. Lamarche, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wallis_barrier_reef_%28no_watermark%29.jpg","alt":"A dark coral reef flat exposed at low tide, with a white sand beach and a low green islet topped by a palm tree beyond it."}},
{"id":"gw-230","num":230,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Norfolk Island","answerText":"Norfolk Island","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">EPBC Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1999</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Norfolk Island</b> is an Australian external territory in the South Pacific whose figures have to be looked for outside the usual profiles, the United Nations Demographic Yearbook giving its surface area as 36 square kilometres (14 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same table records a census of 9 August 2011 that counted 2,302 people, 1,082 of them male and 1,220 female.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the census of 2021 the Australian Bureau of Statistics counted 2,188, of whom 1,060 were male and 1,130 female, so the island has lost people between the two counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its median age was 50 against 38 for Australia as a whole, with 544 families, 1,220 private dwellings and an average of 2.1 people to a household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Median weekly household income was 1,184 dollars and there were 1.9 motor vehicles for every dwelling, which is what a small and elderly island population looks like in the returns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Local government is the Norfolk Island Regional Council, whose own offices are at the Bicentennial Complex, 39 Taylors Road, Burnt Pine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The older institutions are at Kingston instead, and Commonwealth law still treats that settlement as a place of its own: a heritage management plan for the Kingston and Arthur’s Vale Historic Area is in force under the Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Act 1999.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The island’s wharf is there too, appointed by a Commonwealth wharf notice for Sydney Bay at Kingston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the work is divided between the two settlements, and no source reachable for this card calls either of them the island’s capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Bureau files the territory among Australia’s Other Territories, a group of about 4,800 people with a median age of 43, so Norfolk Island is the smaller and the older part of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, <i>Demographic Yearbook 2023</i>, table 3, “Population by sex, annual rate of population change, surface area and density,” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/demographic-social/products/dyb/documents/dyb2023/table03.pdf. [Open access]","Australian Bureau of Statistics, “Norfolk Island, 2021 Census All persons QuickStats,” Statistical Area Level 2 code 901041004, https://www.abs.gov.au/census/find-census-data/quickstats/2021/901041004. [Open access]","Norfolk Island Regional Council, “Home,” https://www.norfolkisland.gov.nf/. [Open access]","“Kingston and Arthur’s Vale Historic Area Heritage Management Plan 2016,” F2016L01891, Federal Register of Legislation, Australian Government, https://www.legislation.gov.au/F2016L01891/latest/text. [Open access]","“Wharf Notice appointment (No. 2586) – Norfolk Island – Sydney Bay - Kingston Wharf,” C2016G00837, Federal Register of Legislation, Australian Government, https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2016G00837/latest/text. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Norfolk Island"},"facts":[["Capital","Kingston"],["Population","2.19k (2021 census)"],["Largest city","Burnt Pine"],["Area","36 km² (14 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/The_view_of_the_Kingston_Town_area_-_Taken_on_the_Saturday%2C_12th_July_2014_%40_11-49am_%28Sydney_Time%29_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-The_view_of_the_Kingston_Town_area_-_Taken_on_the_Saturday%2C_12th_July_2014_%40_11-49am_%28Sydney_Time%29_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Kingston from the hill","desc":"The white stone buildings of Kingston set among Norfolk Island pines, with the reef and the open Pacific beyond.","credit":"Christopher Wood, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_view_of_the_Kingston_Town_area_-_Taken_on_the_Saturday%2C_12th_July_2014_%40_11-49am_%28Sydney_Time%29_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A grassy hillside looking down on scattered white stone houses among tall conifers, with a surf-lined reef and dark blue sea behind."}},
{"id":"gw-195","num":195,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","answer":"Jersey","answerText":"Jersey","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Jersey</b> is a Crown Dependency in the Channel Islands, one of three the United Kingdom names in that class alongside the Bailiwick of Guernsey and the Isle of Man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three take part with the United Kingdom and Ireland in the Common Travel Area, an arrangement older than either country’s membership of the European Union and not dependent on it, under which British and Irish citizens may move freely and reside in either jurisdiction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island is divided into twelve parishes, and the census of 2021 counted 103,267 people across them at 859 to the square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> St Helier holds much the largest share, 35,822 people at 3,716 to the square kilometre, which is more than a third of the island and more than twice the next parish’s count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> St Mary, St John and Trinity are the smallest, at 1,818, 3,051 and 3,355, and across the twelve the density runs from 267 people to the square kilometre up to St Helier’s 3,716.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The census of 2011 had counted 97,857, of whom 33,522 were in St Helier, so the island gained about 5,400 people between the two counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Statistics Jersey put the population at 104,540 at the end of 2024, against 88,400 at the end of 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That growth is migration rather than birth: 2024 brought 730 births against 880 deaths, a natural change of minus 150, and net migration of 670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The direction can reverse, and did in 2021, when the population fell by 300 to 103,190 as immigration of 3,580 was outweighed by emigration of 3,950.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The United Nations Demographic Yearbook lists the island in its own right and records the same census total of 103,267 for 21 March 2021, which matters because that yearbook covers entities the statistics division’s country profiles do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Home Office and Cabinet Office, “Common Travel Area: rights of UK and Irish citizens,” GOV.UK, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/common-travel-area-guidance. [Open access]","Statistics Jersey, “2021 Census: population and population density by Parish,” Government of Jersey Open Data, https://opendata.gov.je/dataset/5840dfa1-8f67-4455-af42-241586ac8999/resource/8b9b106e-6bf6-400f-8a0b-a413204051e3/download/2021-census-parish-population-density.csv. [Open access]","Statistics Jersey, “Parish populations by age and gender (2011 census),” Government of Jersey Open Data, https://opendata.gov.je/dataset/parish-populations-by-age-and-gender. [Open access]","Statistics Jersey, “Total population, annual change, natural growth, net migration per year,” Government of Jersey Open Data, https://opendata.gov.je/dataset/total-population-annual-change-natural-growth-net-migration-per-year. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, <i>Demographic Yearbook 2023</i>, table 3, “Population by sex, annual rate of population change, surface area and density,” https://unstats.un.org/unsd/demographic-social/products/dyb/documents/dyb2023/table03.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Jersey"},"facts":[["Capital","St Helier"],["Population","105k (end 2024)"],["Largest city","St Helier"],["Area","119 km² (46 sq mi)"]],"questions":[],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Elizabeth_Castle_from_Le_Mont_Felard_Jersey.jpg/1280px-Elizabeth_Castle_from_Le_Mont_Felard_Jersey.jpg","title":"St Aubin's Bay and Elizabeth Castle","desc":"Rooftops above the bay on Jersey's south coast, with Elizabeth Castle standing on its islet off the shore.","credit":"Man vyi, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elizabeth_Castle_from_Le_Mont_Felard_Jersey.jpg","alt":"A wide bay of pale sand at low tide seen over the roofs and trees of a town, with a low stone fortress on a rocky islet beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-201","num":201,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Guernsey","answerText":"Guernsey","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In 1821</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,339 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 1901</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40,446 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2023</span><span class=\"dt-v\">64,040 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parishes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Guernsey"},"facts":[["Capital","Saint Peter Port"],["Population","64.0k (2023)"],["Largest city","Saint Peter Port"],["Area","63 km² (24.4 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Guernsey</b> is an island of the Channel Islands and the largest jurisdiction of the Bailiwick of Guernsey, which with the Bailiwick of Jersey and the Isle of Man makes up the three Crown Dependencies of the United Kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bailiwick is not one jurisdiction but three: Guernsey, which takes in the neighbouring islands of Herm and Jethou; Alderney; and Sark, which takes in Brecqhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its own government counted 64,040 people on Guernsey, Herm and Jethou in March 2023, against 40,446 in 1901 and 20,339 at the first census of 1821.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The island covers 63 square kilometres (24.4 square miles) in ten parishes, of which Castel is the largest by area and Saint Peter Port the most populous, with 19,679 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Saint Peter Port, the harbour town on the east coast, is the capital, and holds a little under a third of everybody on the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Crown Dependencies are not part of the United Kingdom, have never been its colonies, and are not Overseas Territories in the way that Gibraltar is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are self-governing dependencies of the Crown, with their own directly elected assemblies, their own administrative, fiscal and legal systems and their own courts of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That relationship runs through the Crown rather than through any formal constitutional document, and principal legislation made in Guernsey, Alderney and Sark takes effect only on Royal Assent or sanction by the monarch in Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The British government answers for the islands' defence and international relations, and in international law Guernsey counts not as a sovereign state but as a territory for which the United Kingdom is responsible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no immigration control between the island and Britain, the two belonging with Jersey, the Isle of Man and Ireland to the Common Travel Area, and no international body publishes a population for Guernsey alone, the World Bank reporting the Channel Islands as one series covering Jersey too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Ministry of Justice, “Fact Sheet on the UK’s Relationship with the Crown Dependencies,” February 2020, https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5e398990e5274a08e229ca0f/crown-dependencies-factsheet-february-2020.pdf. [Open access]","States of Guernsey, Data and Analysis, “Facts and Figures Supplementary Information 2025,” https://www.gov.gg/CHttpHandler.ashx?id=194736&p=0. [Open access]","States of Guernsey, “St Peter Port,” VisitGuernsey, https://www.visitguernsey.com/experiences/st-peter-port/. [Open access]","His Majesty’s Passport Office, “Guernsey, Alderney and Sark: Knowledge Base Profile,” Home Office, 5 May 2025, https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guernsey-alderney-and-sark-knowledge-base-profile/guernsey-alderney-and-sark-knowledge-base-profile. [Open access]","Home Office, “Common Travel Area,” https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/common-travel-area/common-travel-area-accessible. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Channel Islands,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/CHI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Guernsey_Coast_-_HDR_%2814415314134%29.jpg/1280px-Guernsey_Coast_-_HDR_%2814415314134%29.jpg","title":"The south-west coast of Guernsey","desc":"A shingle bay between granite outcrops, with a low headland closing the far side and clear green water over the shallows.","credit":"Photograph by Nicolas Raymond, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guernsey_Coast_-_HDR_%2814415314134%29.jpg","alt":"A curving pebble beach between rocky outcrops, with a green headland beyond and turquoise water in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gw-218","num":218,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Saint Martin","answerText":"Saint Martin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2015</span><span class=\"dt-v\">37,369 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2020</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31,786 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In 2025</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24,941 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collectivity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since 2007</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"St-Martin"},"facts":[["Capital","Marigot"],["Population","24.9k (2025)"],["Largest city","Marigot"],["Area","50 km² (19 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Saint Martin</b> is the northern, French part of a Caribbean island shared with the Dutch territory of Sint Maarten, and its own government calls it the smallest landmass in the world divided between two independent states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French side covers 50 square kilometres (19 square miles), and the World Bank counted 24,941 people living on it in 2025.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That figure has fallen by a third in ten years, from 37,369 in 2015 and 36,837 in 2017 to under 32,000 by 2020.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Dutch side moved the other way across the same years, rising from 37,471 in 2015 to 43,923 in 2025, so one small island holds two populations going in opposite directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The collectivity prints its own address at Marigot on the north-west coast, where its administration sits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> For most of its French history the island was governed from Guadeloupe, its municipality created in 1823 among the first five on those islands, and a sub-prefecture for Saint Martin and Saint Barthélemy opened only in 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Islanders voted in a referendum on 7 December 2003 to leave that arrangement, and an organic law passed on 7 February 2007 turned the commune into an overseas collectivity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It has been governed since under article 74 of the French constitution, which gives it more autonomy than an ordinary territorial collectivity, with a prefecture representing the state alongside an elected Territorial Council and its president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Saint Martin is also one of the European Union's nine outermost regions, so European law and all the rights and duties of membership apply on the French side of the island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Article 349 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union allows special measures for those regions to meet the difficulties of remoteness, insularity, small size and dependence on a narrow range of products.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Collectivité de Saint-Martin, “Sint Maarten : voisin et partenaire,” https://www.com-saint-martin.fr/thematiques/organisation-territoriale/sint-maarten/sint-maarten-voisin-et-partenaire. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: St. Martin (French part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAF/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Surface area: St. Martin (French part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/MAF/indicator/AG.SRF.TOTL.K2?format=json&date=2018:2022. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Sint Maarten (Dutch part),” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/SXM/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Collectivité de Saint-Martin, “Histoire de la Collectivité,” https://www.com-saint-martin.fr/thematiques/organisation-territoriale/votre-collectivite/histoire-de-la-collectivite. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Regional and Urban Policy, “The EU and Its Outermost Regions,” https://ec.europa.eu/regional_policy/policy/themes/outermost-regions_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Cote_de_Saint-Martin.jpg/1280px-Cote_de_Saint-Martin.jpg","title":"The coast of Saint Martin","desc":"Surf breaking over a stony shore below a low cliff, with a green headland and scattered white houses across the bay.","credit":"Photograph by Thierry Pierrard, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cote_de_Saint-Martin.jpg","alt":"A rocky Caribbean shoreline with turquoise water breaking over boulders, a scrubby cliff at the right and a green headland beyond."}},
{"id":"gw-224","num":224,"category":"Geography","question":"The country or territory shaded on the map is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>.","questions":[],"answer":"Saint Barthélemy","answerText":"Saint Barthélemy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commune</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947 – 2007</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collectivity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 February 2007</span><span class=\"dt-k\">EU status change</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 January 2012</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hurricane Irma</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 2017</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"St-Barthélemy"},"facts":[["Capital","Gustavia"],["Population","9.93k (2022)"],["Largest city","Gustavia"],["Area","21 km² (8 sq mi)"]],"abstract":"<b>Saint Barthélemy</b> is a French overseas collectivity in the Caribbean, a dry island of 21 square kilometres (8 square miles) — 25 counting its islets — at the junction of the Greater and Lesser Antilles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The European Commission put its population at 9,927 in 2022, on an island whose volcanic hills carry thorny scrub over a rocky soil that will not take agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only the wetter east coast is much greener, and the year divides into a dry season the islanders call lent, from December to May, and a rainy winter from July to October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its economy rests on high-end tourism and duty-free luxury trade serving visitors from North America, tourism alone providing more than 37 per cent of salaried jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 70,000 people a year stay in its hotels and villas and another 130,000 arrive by boat, while the cost of living keeps mass tourism away and all food, fuel and manufactured goods must be imported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From 1947 to 2007 the island was a commune of Guadeloupe, and the organic law of 21 February 2007 made it a collectivity in its own right, with a territorial council of nineteen members elected for five years and that council's president as its executive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The collectivity receives the public at Pointe-Gustavia, and the French state keeps its delegation in the same town, on rue Lubin Brin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a letter of 30 June 2010 the French president asked the European Council to move the island out of the Union's outermost regions, and it decided on 29 October 2010 that Saint-Barthélemy would leave them on 1 January 2012.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It took instead the status of an overseas country or territory under Part Four of the treaty, France undertaking that the euro would remain the island's sole currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> European money still reaches it through the association decision of 5 October 2021, with 2.5 million euros for 2021 to 2027 going to disaster preparedness and communications after hurricane Irma crossed in September 2017.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Overseas Countries and Territories Association, “Saint-Barthelemy,” https://www.overseas-association.eu/oct/saint-barthelemy/. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for International Partnerships, “Saint Barthélemy,” https://international-partnerships.ec.europa.eu/countries/saint-barthelemy_en. [Open access]","Préfecture de Saint-Barthélemy et de Saint-Martin, “Consultation du public sur le projet de plan territorial de l’agriculture durable et de l’alimentation de Saint Barthélemy,” https://www.saint-barth-saint-martin.gouv.fr/Publications/Consultation-du-public-projet-de-PTAD-de-Saint-Barthelemy. [Open access]","European Council, “Decision 2010/718/EU of 29 October 2010 Amending the Status with Regard to the European Union of the Island of Saint-Barthélemy,” <i>Official Journal of the European Union</i> L 325 (9 December 2010): 4–5, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:32010D0718. [Open access]","European Commission, Directorate-General for Regional and Urban Policy, “The EU and Its Outermost Regions,” https://ec.europa.eu/regional_policy/policy/themes/outermost-regions_en. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/St._Barthelemy_from_Ile_Forchue_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-St._Barthelemy_from_Ile_Forchue_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"Saint Barthélemy from Île Fourchue","desc":"The whole island seen low on the horizon across open water, its dry hills running from end to end, with a rocky foreshore in front.","credit":"Photograph by David Broad, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Barthelemy_from_Ile_Forchue_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A long low island of brown and green hills seen across a stretch of grey-blue sea, with a rubbly shoreline in the foreground."}},
{"id":"gw-578","num":578,"category":"Geography","question":"The dot on the map marks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the political capital of the country shaded around it.","questions":[],"answer":"Gitega","answerText":"Gitega","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 July 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1962</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2018</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"map":{"layer":"world","key":"Burundi","dot":"Gitega"},"facts":[["Country","Burundi"],["Population","135k (2020)"],["Rank in the country","4th largest"],["Population growth","?"]],"abstract":"<b>Gitega</b> is the political capital of Burundi, the city the United Nations gives in the capital-city field of its country profile, Bujumbura remaining the largest city and the economic capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The constitution does not say so: its article 9 fixes the capital at Bujumbura, and then provides that the law may move the capital to any other city of the republic, or separate the political capital from the economic capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gitega is the city that clause was used for, so the United Nations profile has followed the change while the constitutional text still names the older seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No international body publishes a population for Gitega itself, and the profile's capital-city figure of 899,000 for 2018 carries a footnote reading, in full, “Bujumbura”, attached to no row at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That figure belongs to the other seat, which the World Bank's series for a country's largest city puts at 1,350,147 people in 2025, up from 1,074,642 four years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Burundi itself held about 14.4 million people in 2025 on 27,834 square kilometres (10,747 square miles), which the United Nations files under Eastern Africa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became independent on 1 July 1962, the day the Belgian trusteeship over Ruanda-Urundi ended and the territory divided into the two states of Burundi and Rwanda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The United States recognised the Kingdom of Burundi that day and opened a mission in the city then called Usumbura, which is Bujumbura under its older name, with Herbert V. Olds as chargé d'affaires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That mission's rank went down before it went up, becoming a legation on 15 December 1962 and an embassy again only on 16 September 1963.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Burundi joined the United Nations on 18 September 1962, within three months of an independence its two seats now divide between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Burundi,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/bi.html. [Open access]","Constitute Project, “Burundi's Constitution of 2018,” article 9, https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Burundi_2018. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in the largest city: Burundi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BDI/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population, total: Burundi,” World Development Indicators, https://api.worldbank.org/v2/country/BDI/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL?format=json&date=2015:2025. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Burundi,” U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/burundi. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Burundi.Gitega_20.jpg/1280px-Burundi.Gitega_20.jpg","title":"Gitega from the hills above","desc":"The town spread over green slopes under a heavy sky, with red-earth roads running between low houses and wooded hills beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Edouard mhg, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burundi.Gitega_20.jpg","alt":"A wide view over a low-built African town of tin and tiled roofs among trees, with green hills rising behind it."}},
{"id":"gr-401","num":401,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Darius dismissed Mardonius and gave the command to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Mede and the king's own nephew, with orders to enslave Athens and Eretria and bring them before him.","questions":["Rather than round Athos where Mardonius' fleet was wrecked, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sailed from Samos island to island with 600 triremes, burning Naxos and sparing Delos on the way.","Herodotus has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry the enslaved Eretrians home to Darius after Marathon, while Ctesias says the Mede of the pair died on the field and Athens kept his body."],"answer":"Datis and Artaphernes","answerText":"Datis and Artaphernes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Expedition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 triremes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege of Eretria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Delos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 talents of frankincense</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Datis and Artaphernes</b> were the commanders Darius appointed for the expedition of 490 BCE against Athens and Eretria, after dismissing Mardonius, whose fleet had been wrecked rounding Athos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls Datis a Mede by birth and Artaphernes the king's own nephew, and gives their orders as to enslave both cities and bring the captives before Darius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They mustered on the Aleian plain in Cilicia, loaded horses into transports the king's tributary subjects had been ordered to build the year before, and sailed to Ionia with six hundred triremes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From Samos they crossed the Icarian sea island by island instead of following the Thracian coast, which Herodotus attributes to their fear of rounding Athos a second time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How large that force was is the first thing the sources fall out over, since Nepos counts five hundred ships with two hundred thousand foot and ten thousand horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Naxos was attacked first, its city and temples burnt, but at Delos the fleet anchored off Rheneia and Datis promised no harm to the birthplace of the two gods, burning three hundred talents of frankincense on the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carystus was besieged until it came over, and Eretria held its walls for six days before two of its own leading men betrayed it on the seventh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato remembered the campaign as Datis' alone, sent against Athens and Eretria with orders to bring them back in chains and a warning that failure would cost him his life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus preserves a message Datis is said to have sent to Athens, claiming descent from Medus and demanding back a kingdom he said the Athenians had taken from that ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beaten at Marathon, they shipped the Eretrians to Susa, and Herodotus has both men live on, Artaphernes leading Lydians in Xerxes' invasion and Datis' sons its cavalry, while Ctesias has Datis killed on the field and the Athenians refusing to give back his body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Sacred_Way%2C_Delos_03.jpg/1280px-Sacred_Way%2C_Delos_03.jpg","title":"The Sacred Way at Delos","desc":"The processional avenue through the sanctuary of Apollo on Delos, lined with the bases of dedications. Datis anchored his fleet across the water at Rheneia rather than land here, and burnt three hundred talents of frankincense on the altar of the island where the two gods were born.","credit":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacred_Way,_Delos_03.jpg","alt":"A wide paved avenue of pale stone running away from the viewer between rows of low marble blocks and broken column shafts, with tumbled masonry in the foreground and a bare brown hillside behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.94–6.102, 6.115, 6.118–6.119, 7.74, 7.88, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=94. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 10.27, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=10:chapter=27. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Photius, <i>Bibliotheca</i> 72, epitomising Ctesias, <i>Persica</i>, in <i>The Library of Photius</i>, vol. 1, trans. J. H. Freese (London: SPCK, 1920), 98–99, https://archive.org/details/libraryp01phot. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 3.698c, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=698c. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the fleet cross the open sea island by island?","a":"Because Herodotus attributes it to their fear of rounding Athos a second time, Mardonius's fleet having been wrecked there the year before."},{"q":"Why did Delos escape when Naxos was burnt?","a":"Because the fleet anchored off Rheneia and Datis promised no harm to the birthplace of the two gods, burning three hundred talents of frankincense on the altar."},{"q":"Why is even the fate of the commanders uncertain?","a":"Because Herodotus has both live on, Artaphernes leading Lydians in Xerxes' invasion and Datis' sons its cavalry, while Ctesias has Datis killed on the field."}]},
{"id":"gr-402","num":402,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Two leading citizens opened the gates on the seventh day and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed, with the city's temples burnt in revenge for Sardis and its people shipped to Darius as slaves.","questions":["Plato says Datis' men swept the island with a drag-net after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a later account counts 780 captives and has most of the population escape to the mountains.","Athens sent 4,000 settlers to help and then withdrew them on Eretrian advice, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was six days of fighting on the walls and no relief at all."],"answer":"sack of Eretria","answerText":"sack of Eretria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sacked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Walls held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Betrayed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 leading citizens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Captives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">780, by one later count</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>sack of Eretria</b> in 490 BCE was the destruction of the Euboean city by the Persian expedition under Datis and Artaphernes, ordered by Darius in return for its part in the burning of Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretria had sent five ships to the Ionian Revolt, and when the Persians sailed against it the city asked Athens for help and received the four thousand settlers holding the land of the Chalcidian horse-breeders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eretrian counsel was divided between abandoning the city for the heights of Euboea and betraying it, and when Aeschines told the Athenians how matters stood they withdrew across the strait to Oropus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persians landed at Temenos, Choereae and Aegilea, put their horses ashore, and attacked the walls, where for six days many fell on both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the seventh two men of repute, Euphorbus son of Alcimachus and Philagrus son of Cineas, gave up the city, and Pausanias later set the pair in his catalogue of those who betrayed Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The temples were plundered and burnt in return for the temple burnt at Sardis, and the townspeople were enslaved as Darius had commanded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plato has the news reach Athens as a boast that not one Eretrian had got away, Datis' soldiers having swept the territory with a drag-net, a story he says terrified the Greeks whether true or not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strabo knew the razed ground as Old Eretria, its foundations still visible, with the later city built on part of the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Philostratus preserves a very different count from the settlement's own descendants: 780 taken, of whom about four hundred men and perhaps ten women survived the march inland, most of Eretria having fled to Caphereus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Herodotus says Darius did the captives no harm but settled them at Ardericca in Cissia near Susa, where they kept their old language into his own day, and their graves carried Greek verses on how far from home they lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_ancient_Eretria_on_January_16%2C_2020.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_ancient_Eretria_on_January_16%2C_2020.jpg","title":"The fortification wall of ancient Eretria","desc":"Remains of the fortification wall of ancient Eretria, its blocks running away across the excavated ground toward the modern town. Herodotus says the walls were strongly attacked and that for six days many fell on both sides, before two leading citizens gave the city up on the seventh.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_ancient_Eretria_on_January_16,_2020.jpg","alt":"A course of large squared limestone blocks standing in a grassy field, with the line of a ruined wall and scattered masonry running away to the right, and a hillside town under a blue sky behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.77, 5.99, 6.31, 6.99–6.101, 6.119, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=100. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Laws</i> 3.698d, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0166:book=3:section=698d. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 7.10.1–7.10.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=7:chapter=10. [Open access]","Philostratus, <i>Life of Apollonius of Tyana</i> 1.23–1.24, trans. F. C. Conybeare, Loeb Classical Library (London: William Heinemann, 1912), 67–73, https://archive.org/details/lifeofapollonius00philuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 10.1.10, trans. H. L. Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=10:chapter=1:section=10. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why was this city the expedition's target beside Athens?","a":"Because it had sent five ships to the Ionian Revolt, and Darius ordered its destruction in return for its part in the burning of Sardis."},{"q":"Why did the Athenian reinforcement withdraw before the fighting?","a":"Because Eretrian counsel was divided between abandoning the city for the heights and betraying it, and when Aeschines told the Athenians how matters stood they crossed to Oropus."},{"q":"Why do the counts of the captives differ so widely?","a":"Because Plato reports a boast that the territory was swept with a drag-net, while Philostratus preserves the settlement's own figure of 780 taken, of whom about four hundred men survived the march inland."}]},
{"id":"gr-403","num":403,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Ten generals were split until the polemarch's vote sent them out, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with 6,400 dead on one side and 192 on the other.","questions":["Herodotus says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first time Greeks charged an enemy at a run, eight stades of open ground crossed by men the Persians took for madmen.","The Spartans would not march before the full moon and arrived after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was over, so Athens and a thousand Plataeans had fought it alone."],"answer":"Battle of Marathon","answerText":"Battle of Marathon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 6,400</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">192</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Charge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 stades</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Marathon</b> was fought in 490 BCE on the coastal plain of eastern Attica, where an Athenian army met the Persian force Datis and Artaphernes had brought over after destroying Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hippias, the exiled tyrant of Athens, led the Persians to that plain because it was the ground in Attica best suited to riding horses and the nearest to Eretria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens sent to Sparta for help, but the Spartans would not march before the full moon, and their two thousand men reached Attica after the fighting and went to Marathon to look at the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Plataeans came in full force to the Athenian camp at the precinct of Heracles, and Nepos gives them as a thousand men in an army of ten thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nepos also has the Athenians fell trees to break up the Persian cavalry, and puts a hundred thousand foot and ten thousand horse against them, a figure Herodotus never offers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The ten generals were split on whether to fight, and the polemarch Callimachus held the eleventh vote, which Miltiades asked him to cast between enslaving Athens and leaving her free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line was drawn as long as the Persian one, leaving the centre a few ranks deep and both wings strong, and the men crossed the eight stades at a run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persians and Sacae broke the centre and pursued inland, but both wings won, let the beaten go, closed on the breakthrough and drove it to the sea, where seven ships were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus counts about 6,400 dead among the invaders and 192 Athenians, Callimachus among them; Pausanias saw their grave on the plain, with a second for the Plataeans and the slaves who fought that day beside their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says burial on the field was an honour Athens gave to no other war dead, and a funeral oration a century later told it as a victory won without waiting for allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Plain_of_Marathon_4.jpg/1280px-Plain_of_Marathon_4.jpg","title":"The plain of Marathon","desc":"The plain of Marathon, looking south-east toward the bay from beside the Tomb of the Athenians. It is the flatness that matters: Herodotus says Hippias brought the Persians here because it was the ground in Attica best suited to riding horses.","credit":"Photograph by Bob K, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0 1.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plain_of_Marathon_4.jpg","alt":"A wide, flat field of dry yellow and green grass under a deep blue sky, with a line of olive trees along the far edge and low hills faint on the horizon."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.102, 6.106, 6.108–6.117, 6.120, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=112. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.32.3–1.32.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=32. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 5, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 312–13, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.34.5, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=34. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Funeral Oration</i> 23–25, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0154:speech=2:section=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"locator":{"name":"Marathon","at":[23.9619,38.1533],"kind":"battle"},"quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"6","text":"<p>When they had been set in order and the sacrifices were favorable, the Athenians were sent forth and charged the foreigners at a run. The space between the armies was no less than eight stadia. The Persians saw them running to attack and prepared to receive them, thinking the Athenians absolutely crazy, since they saw how few of them there were and that they ran up so fast without either cavalry or archers. So the foreigners imagined, but when the Athenians all together fell upon the foreigners they fought in a way worthy of record. These are the first Hellenes whom we know of to use running against the enemy.</p>","cite":"Book 6.112"},"why":[{"q":"Why was that plain chosen for the landing?","a":"Because Hippias led the Persians there: it was the ground in Attica best suited to riding horses and the nearest to Eretria."},{"q":"Why did the Athenians fight without the Spartans?","a":"Because the Spartans would not march before the full moon, and their two thousand men reached Attica after the fighting and went to Marathon to look at the dead."},{"q":"Why was the line drawn thin in the centre?","a":"Because it had to be made as long as the Persian one, so the centre was left a few ranks deep and both wings strong — and the wings won while the centre broke."}]},
{"id":"gr-404","num":404,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Acquitted of tyranny in the Chersonese and then elected general, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked the polemarch for the vote that sent Athens out to fight at Marathon.","questions":["Seventy ships were voted to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a promise of easy gold, and the failure at Paros brought a fine of fifty talents and a thigh wound that killed him.","Driven from the Chersonese by the Phoenician fleet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Athens to be tried for tyranny, was acquitted, and was elected one of the ten generals who fought at Marathon."],"answer":"Miltiades","answerText":"Miltiades","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Marathon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chersonese guard</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Paros</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70 ships</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Miltiades</b> was the Athenian general who commanded at Marathon in 490 BCE, son of Cimon and heir to a family lordship in the Thracian Chersonese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sons of Pisistratus sent him out when his brother Stesagoras died there, and he took the peninsula by seizing its leading men, kept a guard of five hundred, and married Hegesipyle, daughter of the Thracian king Olorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He held it as a subject of Darius, and Herodotus has him urge the Ionian tyrants at the Danube bridge to break it and leave the king to the Scythians, until Histiaeus talked them round.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scythians drove him out and the Dolonci brought him back; when the Phoenician fleet came he fled for good, losing one ship and the son aboard it, whom Darius then treated well and married to a Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Athens his enemies prosecuted him for tyranny in the Chersonese; he was acquitted and elected one of the ten generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Marathon the generals were split, and it was Miltiades who put the choice to the polemarch Callimachus, whose vote sent the army out to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin makes him the man who argued against waiting for Sparta, and Nepos the one whose authority carried the Athenians out of the city at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His reward, Nepos says, was to stand first among the ten in the Marathon painting in the Painted Stoa, urging his men on, and Pausanias found him there and a monument of his own on the plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He then got seventy ships on a promise of gold, besieged Paros without taking it, and came home with a ruined thigh to be prosecuted; the people spared his life and fined him fifty talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus has him die of the wound with his son Cimon paying the fine, where Diodorus and Nepos have him die in the state prison unable to pay, Cimon giving himself up to prison to get the body back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg/1280px-Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg","title":"The helmet with Miltiades' name","desc":"A bronze Corinthian helmet in the Archaeological Museum of Olympia, with the name ΜΙΛΤΙΑΔΕΣ cut in Greek letters along the lower edge of the cheekpiece.","credit":"Photograph by Oren Rozen, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helmet_of_Miltiades_050911.jpg","alt":"A battered green-patinated bronze helmet in a museum case, seen from the side, its crown broken away and a line of shallow incised Greek capitals running along the lower edge of the cheekpiece."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 4.137, 6.39–6.41, 6.103–6.104, 6.109–6.110, 6.132–6.136, 6.140, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=104. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 3–8, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 310–14, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.9, 2.15, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 27, 37, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 10.30, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=10:chapter=30. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.15.3, 1.32.4, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=15. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","ruler","history","warfare","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the victor of Marathon prosecuted for tyranny?","a":"Because he had held the Thracian Chersonese by seizing its leading men and keeping a guard of five hundred, as a subject of Darius; he was acquitted and elected one of the ten generals."},{"q":"Why did the choice at Marathon come down to one vote?","a":"Because the ten generals were split on whether to fight, and he put the choice to the polemarch Callimachus, who held the eleventh vote, as between enslaving Athens and leaving her free."},{"q":"Why do the accounts of his death disagree?","a":"Because Herodotus has him die of the Parian wound with his son Cimon paying the fine, where Diodorus and Nepos have him die in prison unable to pay, Cimon giving himself up to get the body back."}]},
{"id":"gr-405","num":405,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The eleventh vote belonged to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Miltiades told him it lay with him to enslave Athens or to leave her free and first among the cities of Greece.","questions":["Holding the right wing as the polemarch's place, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed in the fighting at the ships, and a marble Nike on an inscribed column went up for him on the Acropolis.","Pausanias calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the commander-in-chief the Athenians had elected, and found him among the most conspicuous figures in the Marathon painting in the Painted Stoa."],"answer":"Callimachus of Aphidnae","answerText":"Callimachus of Aphidnae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Polemarch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Casting vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th of 11</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at Marathon, 490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nike monument</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 fragments</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Callimachus of Aphidnae</b> was the Athenian polemarch of 490 BCE, whose vote sent the army out to fight at Marathon and who was killed in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The polemarch was the second of the old Athenian offices, added, the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says, because some of the kings had proved cowardly in war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the reform that work dates to 504, the ten generals were elected one from each tribe while the whole army stayed under the polemarch's command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus explains the arrangement differently, as an eleventh vote made by ancient custom equal in weight to the ten, and names the man who held it at Marathon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The generals were split on whether to fight, and Miltiades told him the choice was his: to enslave Athens, or to leave her free and first among the cities of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He voted to attack, and took the right wing when the line was drawn up, which Herodotus says was then the polemarch's place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was killed in the fighting at the ships, and Pausanias, who calls him the commander-in-chief the Athenians had elected, found him among the most conspicuous figures in the Marathon painting in the Painted Stoa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A marble Nike on an inscribed column was set up for him on the Acropolis, reassembled by the Acropolis Museum out of 28 fragments of Parian and Pentelic marble and dated after 490.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What survives of its text names him polemarch of the Athenians, who fought for the Greeks at Marathon, and calls the thing a monument for the sons of the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The office outlived the command: Aristotle's polemarch no longer led armies but sacrificed to Artemis the Huntress, to whom, Xenophon says, Athens had vowed a goat for every enemy killed at Marathon and still paid five hundred a year, and he arranged the funeral games for the city's war dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Ancient_Greek_sculptures_of_Attica_03.jpg/1280px-Ancient_Greek_sculptures_of_Attica_03.jpg","title":"The Nike of Kallimachos","desc":"The monument dedicated for Callimachus on the Athenian Acropolis, standing in the Acropolis Museum: a winged Nike and its inscribed column, put back together from 28 fragments and mounted on a modern armature.","credit":"Photograph by Deiadameian, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Greek_sculptures_of_Attica_03.jpg","alt":"Battered white marble fragments of a winged figure and a column, mounted one above another on a tall steel rod in a museum gallery, with visitors below and a concrete pillar behind."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.109–6.111, 6.114, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=109. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 3.2, 22.2, 58.1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.15.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=15. [Open access]","Acropolis Museum, “The ‘Nike of Kallimachos’,” accessed 3 September 2026, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/nike-kallimachos. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Anabasis</i> 3.2.12, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0202:book=3:chapter=2:section=12. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why did this magistrate hold a vote at all?","a":"Because Herodotus explains it as an eleventh vote made by ancient custom equal in weight to the ten generals', which is how the split among them could be broken."},{"q":"Why did he stand on the right of the line?","a":"Because that was then the polemarch's place, the office having been added, the Constitution of the Athenians says, because some of the kings had proved cowardly in war."},{"q":"Why did the office survive without the command?","a":"Because by Aristotle's day the polemarch no longer led armies but sacrificed to Artemis the Huntress and arranged the funeral games for the city's war dead."}]},
{"id":"gr-406","num":406,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The generals sent <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Sparta, and he was there the day after leaving Athens, only to be told that the law forbade marching before the full moon.","questions":["Above Tegea the god Pan called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by name and asked why Athens paid him no attention, which is why a precinct of Pan stands below the Acropolis.","The modern race is named after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though Plutarch gives the run from the battlefield to Thersippus or to Eucles and the earliest source sends him to Sparta instead."],"answer":"Pheidippides","answerText":"Pheidippides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">To Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sparta reached</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 day after leaving Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spartan delay</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9th day of the month</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pheidippides</b> was the Athenian long-distance runner sent to Sparta to ask for help when the Persians landed at Marathon in 490 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, the earliest account, calls him Philippides, a herald who made long-distance running his calling, and says he was in Sparta on the day after leaving Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Spartans agreed to send help but would not break their law: it was the ninth day of the rising month, and they could not march out before the moon was full.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Justin instead has them held at home four days by a religious scruple and the Athenians deciding not to wait, which is the same delay told as an Athenian choice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nepos calls him Phidippides and puts him among the couriers the Greeks called hemerodromoi, the day-runners who could cover great distances between sunrise and sunset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On the way, above Tegea on Mount Parthenium, he said Pan had called him by name and asked why the Athenians paid him no attention when he had often served them and would again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They believed him, and when they were prosperous they made a precinct of Pan beneath the Acropolis and kept his goodwill with yearly sacrifices and a torch-race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias saw the sanctuary on Mount Parthenium where Athenians and Tegeans agreed the meeting had happened, and reports that Pan promised to come to Marathon and fight on their side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The run everyone remembers is a different one, and no ancient writer gives it to him: Plutarch says the news of the victory was carried back by Thersippus of Eroeadae, or, as most had it, by Eucles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eucles is said to have run in armour straight from the field, burst in on the leading men, managed only 'Hail, we are victorious' and died on the spot, and it is that course the modern race is run on under Pheidippides' name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Panas_cave_7233916.JPG/1280px-Panas_cave_7233916.JPG","title":"The caves on the north slope of the Acropolis","desc":"Openings in the rock face below the Acropolis at Athens, one of them the cave of Pan. Herodotus says the Athenians gave Pan a precinct here, with a yearly sacrifice and a torch-race, after the runner reported that the god had spoken to him on the road to Sparta.","credit":"Photograph by C messier, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0 1.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panas_cave_7233916.JPG","alt":"A pale limestone cliff face in bright sun with three dark cave mouths opening at its foot, dry scrub growing from the rock and a modern rope barrier at the lower right."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.105–6.106, 6.120, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=105. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.4, 8.54.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=8:chapter=54. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>On the Glory of Athens</i> 347C, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, Loeb Classical Library, LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/De_gloria_Atheniensium*.html. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Miltiades</i> 4, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 311, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.9, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 27, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why would the Spartans not march when he asked them?","a":"Because it was the ninth day of the rising month and their law would not let them march out before the moon was full, though they agreed to send help."},{"q":"Why did Athens build a precinct of Pan after the battle?","a":"Because the runner said Pan had called him by name above Tegea and asked why the Athenians paid him no attention, and they believed him and kept his goodwill with sacrifices and a torch-race."},{"q":"Why is the race run in his name not his run?","a":"Because no ancient writer gives him the run from the battlefield: Plutarch says the news was carried by Thersippus of Eroeadae, or, as most had it, by Eucles, who died on delivering it."}]},
{"id":"gr-407","num":407,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The 192 Athenian dead were buried under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> where they fell, an honour Thucydides says the city gave to no other war dead of its own.","questions":["Pausanias saw slabs on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> naming the dead by tribe, and a second grave beside it for the Plataeans and for the slaves who fought that day.","The men under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were worshipped as heroes by the people of Marathon, who also honoured a ploughman the oracle named Echetlaeus after he vanished from the field."],"answer":"Soros","answerText":"Soros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenians in it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">192</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 6,400, no grave found</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Soros</b> is the burial mound raised on the plain of Marathon over the Athenians who fell in the battle of 490 BCE, and Pausanias saw it standing there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus counts 192 Athenian dead, out of an army that had been drawn up tribe by tribe with the polemarch on the right wing and the Plataeans last on the left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burying them where they fell was an exception: Thucydides says Athens laid its war dead in the public cemetery in the most beautiful suburb of the city, and Marathon was its one exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ordinary rite was elaborate, with the bones laid out for three days, then carried in procession in cypress coffins, one for each tribe, and an empty bier borne for the missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribal arrangement went out to Marathon with them, for Pausanias says the mound carried slabs giving the names of the dead according to their tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Beside it stood a second grave, for the Boeotian Plataeans and for the slaves, who, Pausanias says, fought then for the first time alongside their masters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also saw a trophy of white marble on the plain, the kind of thing Lysias says the Athenians set up on their own soil on behalf of Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Of the Persian dead he could find nothing: the Athenians said they had buried them, as divine law requires, but he saw neither mound nor trace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The men under the mound were worshipped as heroes by the people of Marathon, along with Marathon himself and Heracles, and an oracle told them to honour as a hero the ploughman Echetlaeus, who killed many of the enemy and vanished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The city's care for its war dead outlasted them, since the polemarch was still arranging the funeral games for the fallen in Aristotle's day, and Pausanias adds that horses and fighting men are heard on the plain every night, though nobody who goes to hear them profits by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Marathon_Tomb_of_the_Athenians_2.jpg/1280px-Marathon_Tomb_of_the_Athenians_2.jpg","title":"The Soros on the plain of Marathon","desc":"The burial mound of the Athenian dead, standing on the flat ground of the plain where the battle was fought. Pausanias saw slabs on it giving the names of the fallen by tribe; none is on it now.","credit":"Photograph by Tomisti, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marathon_Tomb_of_the_Athenians_2.jpg","alt":"A broad, smooth, dome-shaped mound of dry yellow grass rising from flat open ground against a clear blue sky, with a few small shrubs in front of it and cypresses to the left."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.111, 6.117, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=117. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.34.1–2.34.5, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=34. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.32.3–1.32.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=32. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 58.1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=58:section=1. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Funeral Oration</i> 25, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0154:speech=2:section=25. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","warfare","greece","attica"],"why":[{"q":"Why were these dead buried where they fell?","a":"Because Thucydides says Athens laid its war dead in the public cemetery in the most beautiful suburb of the city, and Marathon was its one exception."},{"q":"Why does the mound carry slabs arranged by tribe?","a":"Because the army had been drawn up tribe by tribe and the tribal arrangement of the funeral rite went out to Marathon with them."},{"q":"Why could Pausanias find nothing of the Persian dead?","a":"Because although the Athenians said they had buried them, as divine law requires, he saw neither mound nor trace of one."}]},
{"id":"gr-408","num":408,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus reports the charge that the Alcmaeonids arranged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to bring the beaten Persian fleet round Sunium to an undefended Athens, and refuses to believe it.","questions":["A family accursed since Cylon's suppliants were killed at the altars was blamed for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and ostracism began to be used two years after the battle.","One thing Herodotus will not deny is that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happened, and one thing he will not say is who was responsible for it."],"answer":"shield signal","answerText":"shield signal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First ostracism</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 years later</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Megacles exiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 year after that</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>shield signal</b> at Marathon is an accusation Herodotus reports and then refuses to believe, that somebody at Athens held up a shield to bring the beaten Persian fleet to the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the battle the Persians picked up the Eretrian captives they had left on an island and rounded Sunium, hoping to reach Athens before the army that had just beaten them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The charge at Athens was that the Alcmaeonids had arranged it, holding the shield up once the Persians were aboard their ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls this a wonder he cannot accept, since the family were tyrant-haters who had worked to bring the sons of Pisistratus down and, in his judgment, freed Athens more than Harmodius and Aristogeiton did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He allows that they might have had some grudge against the people and then dismisses it, since nobody at Athens was more esteemed or more honoured than they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What he will not give up is the fact itself: a shield was held up, he says, and that cannot be denied, but who did it he does not know and can say no further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The family had been accursed since the Cylonian affair, when suppliants were led from the altars and killed while the archon Megacles held the guard on the Acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch says Solon later persuaded the polluted men to stand trial before three hundred jurors, that the house of Megacles was condemned, the living banished and the bodies of the dead dug up and thrown beyond the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The years after Marathon were the right weather for such a charge, since ostracism was first used two years later, on a kinsman of the tyrants, then on the Alcmaeonid Megacles son of Hippocrates, and in the fourth year on Xanthippus son of Ariphron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The taint outlived them all, and Plutarch records that Sparta later demanded Athens drive out the Cylonian pollution in order to be rid of Xanthippus' son Pericles, whose mother was of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Attica_06-13_Sounion_05_Cape_Sounion.jpg/1280px-Attica_06-13_Sounion_05_Cape_Sounion.jpg","title":"Cape Sounion","desc":"The headland at the southern tip of Attica, where the land falls to open sea. Herodotus has the Persian fleet round this corner after the battle, hoping to reach Athens before the army that had beaten them at Marathon.","credit":"Photograph by A.Savin, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attica_06-13_Sounion_05_Cape_Sounion.jpg","alt":"A rocky headland covered in low grey-green scrub falling away to deep blue sea on the right, with a faint coastline on the horizon under a clear sky."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.115, 6.121–6.124, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=124. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.3–22.6, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.126, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=126. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Solon</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0063:chapter=12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 3, 33, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0055:chapter=33:section=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus reject the charge without rejecting the fact?","a":"Because he says a shield was held up and that cannot be denied, but who did it he does not know and can say no further."},{"q":"Why does he think the accused family the least likely culprits?","a":"Because they were tyrant-haters who had worked to bring the sons of Pisistratus down and, in his judgment, freed Athens more than Harmodius and Aristogeiton did."},{"q":"Why were the years after Marathon the right weather for such a charge?","a":"Because ostracism was first used two years later, on a kinsman of the tyrants, then on the Alcmaeonid Megacles, and in the fourth year on Xanthippus son of Ariphron."}]},
{"id":"gr-409","num":409,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Sent home from Sparta with an olive crown and three hundred knights for escort, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was reckoned by Herodotus the wisest man in Greece after Salamis.","questions":["Thucydides says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first who dared tell the Athenians to stick to the sea, and began the walls of Piraeus in his year as archon.","Ostracised and then condemned for dealing with Persia, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended as the king's man at Magnesia, on fifty talents a year for his bread."],"answer":"Themistocles","answerText":"Themistocles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sparta's escort</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 knights</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Magnesia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 talents a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Themistocles</b> was the Athenian who built the fleet that fought at Salamis and turned his city toward the sea, whom Thucydides calls the ablest judge of a sudden crisis of his generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says his family was too obscure to help him, his father Neocles being no conspicuous man and his mother an alien, and Nepos has him disinherited for living too freely before he threw himself into public affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As archon he began the walls of Piraeus, drawn by a place with three natural harbours, and Thucydides says he was the first who dared tell the Athenians to stick to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He read the oracle of the wooden wall as the ships, and Herodotus says that after Salamis he was reckoned by far the wisest man in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greek commanders would not award the prize for valour, so he went to Sparta for the honour instead, and was sent home with an olive crown, the best chariot in Sparta and three hundred knights to escort him to the Tegean border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> After the war he kept the Spartans talking while Athens rebuilt its walls in haste, out of whatever came to hand, tombstones and sculptured blocks among the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says the city tired of him as he kept reciting his own achievements to the assembly, and that he offended it further by building a temple of Artemis Best Counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was ostracised, then condemned in his absence for dealing with Persia, and went to the Persian king, spending the journey learning the language and the customs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says he reached a standing at court that no Greek ever had, and was given Magnesia for his bread at fifty talents a year, Lampsacus for his wine and Myus for the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He died there of illness, Thucydides says, though the story went that he took poison, and Diodorus has him drink a bull's blood rather than lead a Persian army against Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Busto_di_temistocle%2C_da_originale_greco_del_V_secolo_ac%2C_dal_decumano_presso_il_casamento_del_temistocle.JPG/1280px-Busto_di_temistocle%2C_da_originale_greco_del_V_secolo_ac%2C_dal_decumano_presso_il_casamento_del_temistocle.JPG","title":"The herm of Themistocles","desc":"A Roman marble copy of a Greek portrait of the early 5th century BCE, in the archaeological museum at Ostia. The name THEMISTOKLES is cut in Greek letters across the breast, which is what ties the face to the man.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busto_di_temistocle,_da_originale_greco_del_V_secolo_ac,_dal_decumano_presso_il_casamento_del_temistocle.JPG","alt":"A white marble bust of a bearded man with a broad face, heavy jaw and short curled hair, cut off at the shoulders, with faint Greek capitals incised across the chest."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.143–7.144, 8.124–8.125, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=124. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.90–1.93, 1.135–1.138, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=138. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 1, 22, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=1:section=1. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Themistocles</i> 1–2, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 315–16, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.58, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=58. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","politics","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why did he go to Sparta to be honoured after Salamis?","a":"Because the Greek commanders would not award the prize for valour, so he took the honour there instead and was sent home with an olive crown, the best chariot in Sparta and three hundred knights."},{"q":"Why were the walls of Athens rebuilt out of tombstones?","a":"Because he kept the Spartans talking while the city built in haste, out of whatever came to hand, sculptured blocks and grave markers among the rest."},{"q":"Why did the city he saved turn against him?","a":"Because Plutarch says it tired of him as he kept reciting his own achievements to the assembly, and he offended it further by building a temple of Artemis Best Counsellor."}]},
{"id":"gr-410","num":410,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Rather than take ten drachmas a man from the Laurium silver, Athens spent it on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the ships built for a war with Aegina fought at Salamis instead.","questions":["The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> has the hundred talents from Maroneia lent out a talent each under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that a hundred rich men each built a trireme.","Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew the charge that Themistocles had robbed the Athenians of spear and shield and brought them down to the rowing-pad and the oar."],"answer":"naval bill","answerText":"naval bill","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 483 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mine windfall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Share refused</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 drachmas a man</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 or 200, by different accounts</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>naval bill</b> was the measure by which Themistocles turned a windfall from the Attic silver mines into a war fleet instead of a payment to the citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the revenues from Laurium had brought great wealth into the treasury and each man was to have ten drachmas, until Themistocles persuaded the Athenians to build ships with the money instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> tells it differently: a strike at Maroneia gave the state a hundred talents, which Themistocles had lent to the hundred richest Athenians, a talent each, so that every one of them built a trireme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In that version he does not say what the money is for until the ships exist, and the state stays a creditor able to call the loans back in if the borrowers spend badly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The accounts differ on the size of the fleet as well, Herodotus giving two hundred ships where the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i>, Plutarch and Nepos all give a hundred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The argument used was not Persia: Plutarch says Themistocles worked on the city's bitter jealousy of Aegina rather than trying to frighten it with Darius, who was far off and inspired no serious fear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus agrees that the ships were built for the war with Aegina, and says it was that war which saved Greece by compelling the Athenians to become seamen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides allows both motives, naming the war with Aegina and the prospect of the barbarian invasion, and adds that the ships had no complete decks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nepos has the mine money going yearly to waste in the magistrates' hands until Themistocles got the hundred ships built, and puts their first use against Corcyra and the pirates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch also records the charge that followed the policy, that Themistocles had robbed his fellow-citizens of spear and shield and brought the people of Athens down to the rowing-pad and the oar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Athens_tetradrachm.jpg/1280px-Athens_tetradrachm.jpg","title":"An Athenian silver coin","desc":"Both faces of a silver coin of Athens in the Numismatic Museum there: the helmeted head of Athena on one side, her owl with an olive sprig and the letters ΑΘΕ on the other. Herodotus says the mine revenue was about to be shared out at ten drachmas a man before Themistocles had it spent on ships.","credit":"Photograph by Odysses, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_tetradrachm.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin side by side: on the left a helmeted profile head facing right, on the right a front-facing owl standing beside an olive sprig and three Greek letters."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=144. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=4:section=1. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Themistocles</i> 2, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 316, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.14, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=14. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why was Persia not the argument used for building the fleet?","a":"Because Plutarch says he worked on the city's bitter jealousy of Aegina rather than trying to frighten it with Darius, who was far off and inspired no serious fear."},{"q":"Why does the version with the loans work differently from the distribution?","a":"Because a talent lent to each of the hundred richest men buys a trireme without saying what the money is for, and leaves the state a creditor able to call the loans back in."},{"q":"Why did the policy draw the charge it did?","a":"Because Plutarch records the complaint that he had robbed his fellow-citizens of spear and shield and brought the people of Athens down to the rowing-pad and the oar."}]},
{"id":"gr-411","num":411,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The silver from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was about to be shared out at ten drachmas a man before Athens voted to build warships with it instead.","questions":["Herodotus puts the find at Laurium and the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> at Maroneia, but both are describing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that paid for the fleet of 483.","Nicias let out a thousand slaves at an obol a day each in the same mines as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Thucydides says twenty thousand deserted once Decelea was fortified."],"answer":"Laurion strike","answerText":"Laurion strike","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Strike</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 483 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Profit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nicias' slaves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000, at an obol a day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">After Decelea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000 slaves gone</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Laurion strike</b> of about 483 BCE was a rich new find in the silver mines of southern Attica, and its money is the money Athens spent on the fleet that fought at Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus names the source as the revenues from the mines at Laurium; the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> puts the find at Maroneia and the profit at a hundred talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What both agree on is the custom the windfall interrupted, that such a surplus was shared out among the citizens: ten drachmas a man in Herodotus, a proposal to distribute it in the <i>Constitution</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mines themselves were old by then, and Xenophon writes that nobody even attempts to date the beginning of the digging.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He points out how small the spoil heaps are beside the untouched silver-bearing hills, and says the mines stand as they stood in his ancestors' day however many men have worked them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The digging was done by slaves in numbers, and Xenophon reports that Nicias once owned a thousand men there and let them out to Sosias the Thracian at an obol a day each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How much hung on that labour shows when it stopped: after Sparta fortified Decelea, Thucydides says more than twenty thousand slaves deserted, a great part of them craftsmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Xenophon writes his own proposal for the mines against the memory of that, recalling what the charge for slave labour had brought in before the trouble at Decelea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo, later still, says the silver mines of Attica were important at first and were exhausted by his own day, and that the workmen were putting the old refuse and slag back through the furnace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They got very pure silver that way, he says, because the earlier workmen had smelted unskilfully; and Herodotus adds that the ships the strike paid for were never used for the purpose they were built for, but came to serve Greece in her need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Level_Washery_in_Soureza_Lavrion_Greece.jpg/1280px-Level_Washery_in_Soureza_Lavrion_Greece.jpg","title":"An ore washery at Soureza","desc":"A level washery in the Soureza valley at Laurion, where crushed silver-bearing lead ore was washed to concentrate it: the flat floor and the channel running round it are both visible, with two visitors at the left for scale.","credit":"Photograph by Petros Tzeferis, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Level_Washery_in_Soureza_Lavrion_Greece.jpg","alt":"A large flat rectangular stone floor cut into a dry hillside, bounded by a shallow channel and low rubble walls, with pines around it and two people in sun hats standing at the left edge."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=144. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.7, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22:section=7. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 4, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0210%3Atext%3DWays%3Achapter%3D4. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 7.27, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=27. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.1.23, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=1:section=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","technology","greece","attica"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the find an interruption rather than a windfall?","a":"Because the custom was that such a surplus was shared out among the citizens — ten drachmas a man in Herodotus, a proposal to distribute it in the Constitution of the Athenians."},{"q":"Why does Xenophon say the mines were nowhere near exhausted?","a":"Because the spoil heaps are small beside the untouched silver-bearing hills, and the mines stood as they had in his ancestors' day however many men had worked them."},{"q":"Why did the same ground pay again centuries later?","a":"Because the earlier workmen had smelted unskilfully, so in Strabo's day the old refuse and slag were being put back through the furnace and gave very pure silver."}]},
{"id":"gr-412","num":412,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the best and most just man in Athens, and has him cross from Aegina to warn Themistocles at Salamis that the fleet was encircled.","questions":["One of the ten generals at Marathon, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed his own day of supreme command to Miltiades, and later landed armed men on the islet of Psyttaleia.","After the allies turned from Sparta to Athens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was chosen to assess the tribute, which Thucydides puts at 460 talents with the common treasury on Delos."],"answer":"Aristides","answerText":"Aristides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Marathon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">490 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">460 talents, assessed 478 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Aristides</b> son of Lysimachus, of the Athenian deme Alopece, was the great rival of Themistocles in the generation that fought the Persian Wars, and later Greeks knew him by the surname 'the Just'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says the name fastened on him because justice was the virtue he practised most continually, and thought it remarkable that a poor man should carry a title no king had ever coveted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether he was poor at all was already argued over: Demetrius of Phalerum reasoned from his archonship, drawn by lot among the richest property class, that he had money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Marathon he was one of the ten generals, and Plutarch has him give up his own day of supreme command to Miltiades and bring the rest to do the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> makes him and Themistocles the two heads of the people, the one trained in war and the other in politics, so that Athens used one as general and the other as counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It also records his ostracism in the years of the naval programme, and the recall of every exile three years later as Xerxes came on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus brings him back at Salamis, crossing from Aegina to call Themistocles, his bitter enemy, out of the quarrelling council to tell him the fleet was encircled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus breaks off there to say that, having inquired into the man's character, he had come to believe him the best and most just man in Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the battle he took armed men from the Salamis shore, landed them on the islet of Psyttaleia and killed the Persians posted there; the next summer he led the eight thousand Athenians on the left of the line at Plataea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the allies turned from Sparta to Athens he was chosen to fix what each city should pay, an assessment Thucydides gives as 460 talents with the treasury on Delos; Nepos says he died too poor to pay for his own funeral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/The_island_of_Psyttaleia_on_August_20%2C_2021.jpg/1280px-The_island_of_Psyttaleia_on_August_20%2C_2021.jpg","title":"The islet of Psyttaleia","desc":"Psyttaleia seen across the water from the Saronic Gulf: a low, bare rock in the strait outside Salamis. Herodotus says Aristides took armed men off the Salamis shore, landed them here and killed every Persian on the islet.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_island_of_Psyttaleia_on_August_20,_2021.jpg","alt":"A long, low, treeless island lying across a stretch of dark blue sea, with pale rocky shores, a few small modern buildings along its spine and hills faint in the distance."},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 1, 5–6, 26–27, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, in <i>Plutarch: The Parallel Lives</i>, vol. 2, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.7–22.8, 23.3–23.5, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.79, 8.95, 9.28, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Aristides</i> 1–3, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 323–25, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","politics","warfare","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why was his poverty disputed in antiquity?","a":"Because Demetrius of Phalerum reasoned from his archonship, drawn by lot among the richest property class, that he must have had money."},{"q":"Why did the two rivals both serve the city at once?","a":"Because the Constitution of the Athenians makes them the two heads of the people, one trained in war and the other in politics, so Athens used one as general and the other as counsellor."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus interrupt his narrative to praise him?","a":"Because at Salamis he crossed from exile to warn his bitter enemy that the fleet was encircled, and Herodotus says he had inquired into the man's character and come to believe him the best and most just man in Athens."}]},
{"id":"gr-413","num":413,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 482 BCE a countryman who could not write handed the man himself a sherd, being tired of hearing him everywhere called the Just.","questions":["The Athenian Constitution puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the archonship of Nicomedes and says the exiles were all recalled three years later because Xerxes was marching on Attica.","Plutarch traces the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a story Themistocles set going, that his rival had abolished the public courts and set himself up as a monarch in all but the bodyguard."],"answer":"ostracism of Aristides","answerText":"ostracism of Aristides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 482 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sentence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recalled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd year after the vote</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Void below</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 sherds</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>ostracism of Aristides</b> was the vote, in the archonship of Nicomedes, that sent the Athenian his fellow citizens called 'the Just' out of the city for ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The law was Cleisthenes', aimed at first against friends of the tyrants; the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> says that by the fourth year it was being turned on anyone who seemed too great.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> explains the device as the resort of states that pursue equality above all things, banishing for fixed terms the men outstanding in wealth or popularity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch says liability fell on anyone above the common run in reputation, birth or eloquence, and that Themistocles set a story going that his rival had done away with the public courts and made himself a monarch in all but the bodyguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people, high after Marathon, were vexed by anyone towering over them, and Plutarch adds that they gave their envy of his reputation the name of fear of tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Every voter scratched a name on a potsherd and carried it to a railed enclosure in the market place, where the archons counted; under six thousand sherds the vote was void.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch's story of the day is that an unlettered countryman handed Aristides his own sherd and asked him to write the name on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked what wrong he had suffered, the man said none at all, that he did not even know the fellow, and that he was tired of hearing him everywhere called 'the Just'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristides wrote his own name and handed the sherd back, and as he left the city he prayed that no crisis might come to make the Athenians remember him; Nepos tells the anecdote in the same shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> puts the recall in the third year, under the archon Hypsechides, with a line from Geraestus to Scyllaeum the exiles were not to live inside, where Nepos makes it about six years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Ostraka_for_Ostracism_-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg/1280px-Ostraka_for_Ostracism_-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg","title":"Ostraka in the Agora Museum","desc":"A case of voting sherds in the Museum of the Ancient Agora at Athens. Numbers 16, 17 and 18 read ARISTEIDES LYSIMACHO; number 33, in the row below, names Themistocles son of Neocles.","credit":"Photograph by Joyofmuseums, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostraka_for_Ostracism_-_Museum_of_the_Ancient_Agora_-_Joy_of_Museum.jpg","alt":"Rows of numbered potsherds pinned to a pale museum wall, each with a personal name scratched through the black glaze or into the bare clay in Greek capitals."},"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 7–8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, in <i>Plutarch: The Parallel Lives</i>, vol. 2, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 22.1, 22.3–22.8, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=22. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1284a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=3:section=1284a. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Aristides</i> 1, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 323–24, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.79, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=79. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","politics","greece","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a reputation for justice make a man liable to it?","a":"Because liability fell on anyone above the common run in reputation, birth or eloquence, and the people, high after Marathon, were vexed by anyone towering over them."},{"q":"Why does Plutarch call the vote envy rather than fear?","a":"Because they gave their envy of his reputation the name of fear of tyranny, helped by a story Themistocles set going that he had done away with the public courts."},{"q":"Why were the sherds counted before the names were read?","a":"Because under six thousand in all the vote was void, so the total had to be established in the railed enclosure before any name could send a man away."}]},
{"id":"gr-414","num":414,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The Achaemenid king who succeeded Darius I and carried out the invasion of Greece his father had been preparing was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who put down an Egyptian revolt first.","questions":["Aeschylus put <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Athenian stage, with his mother dreaming that a yoked woman broke the harness and threw him down and his father's ghost blaming his youthful recklessness.","Diodorus says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was killed in his bedchamber by Artabanus, the captain of the royal bodyguard, who then persuaded one of his sons to execute another for the murder."],"answer":"Xerxes I","answerText":"Xerxes I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 486 – 465 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Invaded Greece</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Artabanus, 465 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Xerxes I</b> was the Achaemenid king who succeeded Darius I and carried out the invasion of Greece his father had been preparing when he died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the succession was disputed between Artobazanes, eldest of the sons born before Darius took the throne, and Xerxes, eldest of those born afterwards to Atossa daughter of Cyrus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes pleaded his descent from Cyrus, and the exiled Spartan king Demaratus, then at Susa, is said to have told him to add that he alone had been born to a reigning father.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus doubts the advice decided anything, since Atossa held complete sway, and says Xerxes was at first not eager to march west: he put down the Egyptian revolt first, laying Egypt under a harder slavery than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Persepolis the text cut four times above the winged bulls of his gateway calls him king of kings and king of the countries of many peoples, and says he made this portal of all lands by the grace of Ahura Mazda.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus, no friend of his, still allows that of all the tens of thousands he led there was not one worthier than himself, in looks and grandeur, to hold that command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says he watched Salamis from a gilded throne set on a height above the strait, with secretaries stationed beside him to record what was done in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aeschylus put him on the Athenian stage in the <i>Persians</i>, where his mother dreams of two women harnessed to his chariot and one of them snapping the yoke and throwing him down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same play his father's ghost calls it a disease of the soul that a mortal should have thought to shackle the sacred Hellespont like a slave and to master even Poseidon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus dates his death to the archonship of Lysitheus and gives it to Artabanus, captain of the bodyguard, let into the bedchamber at night, and says he had been king for more than twenty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/20101229_Gates_of_the_nations_Persepolis_Iran.jpg/1280px-20101229_Gates_of_the_nations_Persepolis_Iran.jpg","title":"The Gate of All Nations at Persepolis","desc":"The gateway Xerxes built at Persepolis, its doorway flanked by human-headed winged bulls. The inscription cut above them, four times over, is the one in which he calls himself king of the countries holding many kinds of people.","credit":"Photograph by Ggia, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20101229_Gates_of_the_nations_Persepolis_Iran.jpg","alt":"A tall stone doorway standing alone under a deep blue sky, its jambs carved as colossal winged bulls with human heads, with slender fluted columns visible through the opening."},"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.2–7.8, 7.187, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=2. [Open access]","Herbert Cushing Tolman, <i>Ancient Persian Lexicon and the Texts of the Achaemenidan Inscriptions</i> (New York: American Book Company, 1908), 39, https://archive.org/details/ancientpersianle00tolm_0. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=13:section=1. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 176–200, 739–752, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=176. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.69, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=69. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","politics","iran","greece"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the succession disputed when he was not the eldest son?","a":"Because Artobazanes was eldest of the sons born before Darius took the throne and he was eldest of those born afterwards, so he pleaded descent from Cyrus and birth to a reigning father."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus doubt that Demaratus's advice decided it?","a":"Because Atossa, the daughter of Cyrus, held complete sway, so the argument put in the king's mouth need not have been what carried the decision."},{"q":"Why does Aeschylus make the bridging a disease of the soul?","a":"Because his father's ghost says it was a mortal thinking to shackle the sacred Hellespont like a slave and to master even Poseidon."}]},
{"id":"gr-415","num":415,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A storm broke the first pair, so Xerxes had the water lashed three hundred times and the overseers beheaded, and new engineers built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across 674 moored ships.","questions":["Aeschylus calls them a yoke on the neck of the sea, and Isocrates says the king who built the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sailed his ships across the mainland and marched his troops across the main.","Herodotus says the army took seven days and seven nights to pass over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the lash, the ten thousand Persians leading the way wearing garlands."],"answer":"Hellespont bridges","answerText":"Hellespont bridges","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Strait</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 stadia across</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships moored</span><span class=\"dt-v\">360 and 314</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 days and 7 nights</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Hellespont bridges</b> were the two pontoon roads Xerxes laid across the strait between Abydos and the Chersonese so that his army could march out of Asia into Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the first pair ran from Abydos to a headland between Sestos and Madytus, one built of flaxen cables by the Phoenicians and one of papyrus by the Egyptians, over a crossing of seven stadia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A storm broke them apart as soon as they were finished, and Xerxes had the water given three hundred lashes and a pair of fetters thrown into it, with words telling the bitter sea that the king would pass whether it wished or not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus has heard that branders went down with the whips, and says the overseers were beheaded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> New engineers moored 360 ships and triremes to carry the bridge nearer the Black Sea and 314 for the other, set obliquely to the current, anchored at both ends and left with a gap for small craft to pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Six cables held each bridge, two of flax and four of papyrus, all of one thickness, though a cubit of the flax weighed a talent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over these went sawn timbers, then brushwood, then earth stamped down, with a fence on either side so that the animals should not see the water below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At sunrise Xerxes poured a libation into the strait and threw in a golden bowl and a Persian sword, and Herodotus cannot decide whether these were offerings to the sun or atonement for the whipping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crossing took seven days and seven nights without pause, and Diodorus says Xerxes left Sardis for Abydos only when word came that both this and the Athos canal were finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aeschylus calls the work a roadway cast as a yoke on the neck of the sea, Isocrates says the king sailed his ships across the mainland and marched his troops across the main, and Strabo records that the point opposite Abydos was still called the Zeugma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.33–7.36, 7.54–7.56, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=36. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 65–72, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=65. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Panegyricus</i> 89, trans. George Norlin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0144:speech=4:section=89. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 13.1.22, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=13:chapter=1:section=22. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","object","technology","warfare","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Canakkalebogaz.jpg/1280px-Canakkalebogaz.jpg","title":"The Dardanelles in rough weather","desc":"The strait the Greeks called the Hellespont, seen from a ship. Herodotus says a storm broke up the first pair of bridges as soon as they were finished, and that Xerxes had the water whipped three hundred times for it.","credit":"Photograph by Nerval, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canakkalebogaz.jpg","alt":"A wide channel of dark choppy water under heavy grey cloud, with a wooded headland and a small town along the far shore and two ships in the distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the strait whipped and fettered?","a":"Because a storm broke the first pair of bridges as soon as they were finished, and the king had the water given three hundred lashes and told that he would pass whether it wished or not."},{"q":"Why were the second bridges built as they were?","a":"Because the ships were set obliquely to the current and anchored at both ends, with a gap left for small craft, and six cables of flax and papyrus carried timber, brushwood and stamped earth."},{"q":"Why was a fence built along both sides?","a":"Because the animals were not to see the water below, the roadway carrying an army across seven days and seven nights without pause."}]},
{"id":"gr-416","num":416,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says the diggers were driven by whippings for three years, and that the Phoenicians alone widened the top of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that its sides would not cave in.","questions":["Herodotus thinks Xerxes ordered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> out of pride, wanting a memorial, since the ships could have been dragged over the twelve-stadia isthmus with no trouble at all.","Strabo says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could still be seen at Acanthus in his day, though Demetrius of Scepsis held that a rock ridge had always made it unnavigable."],"answer":"Athos canal","answerText":"Athos canal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 3 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Isthmus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 stadia across</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Width</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 triremes abreast</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Athos canal</b> was the channel Xerxes had cut through the isthmus at the landward end of the Athos peninsula so that his fleet could pass without rounding the mountain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says ships that had earlier tried to sail round Athos were wrecked, and that the work went on about three years, with triremes anchored off Elaeus as a base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men from every nation in the army were driven to dig by whippings, in relays, along with the people living about Athos, under two Persian overseers, Bubares and Artachaees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The isthmus runs about twelve stadia from the sea by Acanthus to the sea opposite Torone, and cutting it would have made islands of Sane and five neighbouring towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each nation dug its own stretch and passed the spoil up in stages, and all but the Phoenicians made the trench the same width top and bottom, so the sides caved in and doubled their labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus judges that Xerxes ordered it out of pride, to display his power and leave a memorial, since the ships could have been dragged across with no trouble; it was cut wide enough for two triremes to row abreast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus gives another reason, that Xerxes hoped the scale of the works would frighten the Greeks before he arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Artachaees died of illness at Acanthus, and Herodotus says he was the tallest man in Persia and the loudest-voiced on earth, that the whole army poured libations at his tomb, and that the Acanthians sacrifice to him as a hero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides, fifty years later, puts Sane close to the canal and calls the promontory one running out from the king's dike, and Isocrates makes the channelling of Athos a plan whose fame is on every tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Strabo says the cut could still be seen at Acanthus in his own day, but records Demetrius of Scepsis holding it had never been navigable, a flat rock nearly a stadium long having stopped the digging short of the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.22–7.24, 7.117, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=23. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 4.109, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=4:chapter=109. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.2, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=2. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Panegyricus</i> 89, trans. George Norlin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0144:speech=4:section=89. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7, fragments 33 and 35, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=7:chapter=fragments. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","technology","warfare","history","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus judge the cutting unnecessary?","a":"Because the ships could have been dragged across the isthmus with no trouble, so he reads the work as pride, a display of power meant to leave a memorial."},{"q":"Why did most of the diggers double their own labour?","a":"Because all but the Phoenicians made the trench the same width top and bottom, so the sides caved in and the spoil had to be moved twice."},{"q":"Why is it disputed whether the channel was ever usable?","a":"Because Strabo says the cut could still be seen in his day but records Demetrius of Scepsis holding it never navigable, a flat rock nearly a stadium long having stopped the digging short of the sea."}]},
{"id":"gr-417","num":417,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus says Xerxes spent four full years preparing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and set out before the fifth was out with a host he thought greater than any expedition ever known.","questions":["At Abydos, before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed into Europe, Xerxes watched his fleet from a seat of white stone, called himself blessed and then wept at how short human life is.","Aeschylus and Herodotus both put the fleet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at 1,207 ships, and Herodotus reckons the land army at a million and seven hundred thousand men."],"answer":"invasion of 480","answerText":"invasion of 480","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Preparations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,700,000, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,207 ships</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>invasion of 480</b> was the Persian attempt on Greece that Xerxes led in person, four full years in the preparing after Egypt had been subdued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls it by far the greatest expedition known, beside which Darius' Scythian march and the sons of Atreus against Troy are nothing, and asks what nation Xerxes did not bring out of Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says whole waters failed when the army drank, all but the greatest rivers, and Diodorus repeats the story that unfailing rivers ran dry and the sea was hidden by sails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The army crossed at Abydos and the fleet came through the cutting at Athos, and at Doriscus the whole force was counted by packing ten thousand men at a time inside a wall built to the height of a man's navel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Abydos Xerxes watched from a seat of white stone set up on a hill, and when he saw the ships and the shore covered with men he declared himself blessed and then wept, saying that of all that multitude not one would be alive in a hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The count gave a land army of a million and seven hundred thousand, and with the crews and the service train Herodotus reaches a total of 5,283,220 men brought as far as Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He puts the fleet at 1,207 warships from Asia, the figure Aeschylus also gives on the Athenian stage, where the Greeks are made to meet it with 310.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus counts differently and reaches not less than a million soldiers once the European allies had joined, with as many again in the warships and the supply train.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lysias, speaking two generations later, says only that twelve hundred ships came and that merely listing the nations of the land army would be a long task.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides, looking back from a longer war, calls the Median war the greatest achievement of past times and says it found a speedy decision in two actions by sea and two by land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.20–7.21, 7.44–7.46, 7.60, 7.184–7.186, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=60. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 337–343, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=337. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.5, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=5. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.23, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=23. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Funeral Oration</i> 27, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0154:speech=2:section=27. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the army counted inside a wall rather than mustered?","a":"Because at Doriscus the whole force was numbered by packing ten thousand men at a time inside a pen built to the height of a man's navel, and filling it again until all had passed through."},{"q":"Why did the king weep at the sight of his own army?","a":"Because looking from a seat of white stone on the ships and the shore covered with men he declared himself blessed and then reflected that of all that multitude not one would be alive in a hundred years."},{"q":"Why do the ancient totals disagree so widely?","a":"Because Herodotus reaches 5,283,220 men brought as far as Thermopylae, Diodorus counts not less than a million soldiers with as many again in ships and train, and Lysias gives only twelve hundred ships."}]},
{"id":"gr-418","num":418,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> swore that if they won they would give Delphi the goods of every Greek state that had gone over to the Persians of its own free will.","questions":["Athens gave up its claim to command at sea rather than let the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> break apart over the leadership, judging the safety of Hellas more important than its own claim.","Gelon of Syracuse refused to join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unless he led it, and told the envoys to go home and say that Hellas had lost the spring from her year."],"answer":"Hellenic League","answerText":"Hellenic League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War won</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Victory tithe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 gold tripod at Delphi</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Hellenic League</b> was the alliance of Greek states that agreed to resist Xerxes, made up of the cities that refused the Persian king and bound by a common oath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus names the Greeks who submitted instead, the Thebans and all the Boeotians but Thespiae and Plataea among them, and says the allies swore that in victory they would dedicate to Delphi the goods of every Greek who had surrendered of free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those concerned for the common welfare met, exchanged guarantees and resolved to end every feud among themselves, of which the greatest then running was between Athens and Aegina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They sent men into Asia to spy out the king's doings, and envoys to Argos, to Gelon of Syracuse, to Corcyra and to Crete, hoping that all of Greek blood might work to one end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus adds that the congress voted to make any Greek who joined the Persians willingly pay a tithe to the gods, and to send envoys urging in the neutrals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Not all came: Argos asked for a thirty-year truce and a share of the command, the Cretans put the question to Delphi and were told to stay out, and Gelon offered a great force on condition that he lead it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Told he could not, he sent the Athenian envoy home with the answer that Hellas had lost the spring from her year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sparta led by land and by sea, and Athens, which had a claim to the naval command, waived it when the allies resisted, judging that a quarrel over it would destroy Hellas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the victory the allies set apart a tithe for Delphi and made from it a gold tripod on a three-headed bronze serpent, on which the Spartan commander <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span> had a couplet cut claiming the monument for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Spartans erased it and cut instead the names of the cities that had helped overthrow the barbarian, and centuries later the bronze still stood at Delphi, though the gold had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.132, 7.145, 8.3, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=145. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.148–7.169, 9.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=162. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.132, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.13.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=13:section=9. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","warfare","politics","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Bronze_Serpent_Column_in_Istanbul.jpg/1280px-Bronze_Serpent_Column_in_Istanbul.jpg","title":"The Serpent Column","desc":"The bronze column that carried the allies' gold tripod at Delphi, standing today in a sunken pit in the Hippodrome at Istanbul. Its coils carry the names the Spartans cut there in place of Pausanias' couplet.","credit":"Photograph by Ian Scott, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 2.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_Serpent_Column_in_Istanbul.jpg","alt":"A tall bronze column of three intertwined snake bodies, green with age and broken off at the top, rising from a stone-lined pit below street level with railings and passers-by above."},"why":[{"q":"Why was ending the members' own feuds the alliance's first act?","a":"Because those concerned for the common welfare had to exchange guarantees before they could act together, and the greatest quarrel then running was between Athens and Aegina."},{"q":"Why did Athens give up the command at sea?","a":"Because although it had a claim to the naval leadership it waived it when the allies resisted, judging that a quarrel over it would destroy Hellas."},{"q":"Why was the couplet on the victory monument erased?","a":"Because Pausanias had it cut claiming the tripod for himself, so the Spartans erased it and cut instead the names of the cities that had helped overthrow the barbarian."}]},
{"id":"gr-419","num":419,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Thessalian messengers came to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asking for a force to hold the pass of Olympus, warning that they would otherwise have to make terms with the Persian.","questions":["After the force sent to Tempe came home, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> deliberated again and resolved to hold Thermopylae instead, with the fleet at Artemisium so each could learn the other's doings.","When the admirals voted at Poseidon's altar after Salamis, at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> each put himself first and most put Themistocles second, so no one won outright."],"answer":"congress at the Isthmus","answerText":"congress at the Isthmus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First met</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent to Tempe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prize of valour</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, after Salamis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>congress at the Isthmus</b> was the standing council through which the Greek states allied against Xerxes made their decisions, meeting at the sanctuary of Poseidon on the neck of land joining the Peloponnese to the mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus describes it as men chosen from the cities best disposed towards Hellas, assembled in council for the Greek cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first business was to end the feuds among its own members and to send envoys to the Greeks who had not joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus says it voted that any Greek who went over to the Persians willingly should pay a tithe to the gods, and sent to the neutral states urging them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also had to settle who commanded: Thucydides says the Spartans took the leadership in virtue of their superior power, and Diodorus records the Argives offering to join if they were given a share of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Thessalian messengers came asking for a force to hold the pass of Olympus, warning that they would otherwise make terms with the Persian, and the council resolved to send one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When that force came back from Tempe the Greeks deliberated again and chose to hold Thermopylae instead, as narrower and nearer home, with the fleet at Artemisium so that each could learn the other's doings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After Salamis the admirals came to the Isthmus to award the prize of valour, and at the altar of Poseidon each voted himself first, so every man had one vote and Themistocles far outstripped the rest in second places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch tells the same story and adds that the Spartans afterwards gave Eurybiades the crown for valour and Themistocles one for wisdom, with the best chariot in the city and three hundred picked youths to escort him to the border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the council resolved survives on stone as well as in books: Pausanias saw at Olympia the Zeus the allies dedicated after Plataea, with the cities that fought inscribed on its pedestal, the Lacedaemonians first and the Athenians second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.145, 7.172–7.175, 8.123, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=175. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.18, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=18. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.3, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=3. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.23.1–5.23.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=5:chapter=23. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=17:section=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","event","history","politics","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Isthmia_altar_of_Poseidon_with_wheel_ruts.jpg/1280px-Isthmia_altar_of_Poseidon_with_wheel_ruts.jpg","title":"The altar of Poseidon at Isthmia","desc":"The foundations of the great altar in the sanctuary of Poseidon on the Isthmus, worn with cart ruts. Herodotus says the admirals cast their votes for the prize of valour at this altar after Salamis.","credit":"Photograph by Mark Landon, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isthmia_altar_of_Poseidon_with_wheel_ruts.jpg","alt":"Long low courses of weathered limestone blocks running across dry scrubby ground, with further tumbled stone and a field wall behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Thermopylae chosen after the force came back from Tempe?","a":"Because on deliberating again the Greeks judged that pass narrower and nearer home, with the fleet at Artemisium so that each could learn the other's doings."},{"q":"Why did the vote on valour after Salamis settle nothing directly?","a":"Because at the altar of Poseidon each admiral voted himself first, so every man had one vote and the decision fell instead on who was placed second most often."},{"q":"Why did Sparta hold the leadership of the alliance?","a":"Because Thucydides says the Spartans took it in virtue of their superior power, and the Argives asked for a share of it as their condition for joining."}]},
{"id":"gr-420","num":420,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The Pythia first told the Athenians to flee to the ends of the earth, and only when they came back as suppliants did she give them the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead.","questions":["Some Athenians read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the thorn hedge that had once fenced the Acropolis, while Themistocles argued it meant the ships and won the assembly over.","Themistocles said the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called Salamis divine rather than cruel, so the deaths it promised must be the enemy's and the Athenians should fight at sea."],"answer":"wooden wall oracle","answerText":"wooden wall oracle","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consulted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Answers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, from the Pythia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manning money</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 drachmas a man</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>wooden wall oracle</b> was the second answer the Pythia at Delphi gave the Athenians as Xerxes advanced, and the argument over its meaning decided how Athens would fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, from the priestess Aristonice, told them to flee to the ends of the earth, since fire and the headlong god of war would bring the city low and its roofs run with gore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The envoys despaired, and on the advice of Timon son of Androbulus went back with suppliant boughs, refusing to leave until they were given a better answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second said that all within the sacred border of Cecrops would be taken, but that Zeus would grant the Trito-born a wall of wood, a stronghold for them and their children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended with the lines that would trouble them: await not the host from Asia but turn your back and withdraw, and divine Salamis, you will bring death to women's sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> At Athens the elder men said the wooden wall was the thorn hedge that once fenced the acropolis; others said it was the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The readers of oracles took the Salamis lines to mean that a sea fight there would end in Athenian defeat, which told against the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Themistocles answered that had the verse meant the Athenians the god would have called Salamis cruel rather than divine, so it spoke of the enemy, and the assembly judged him the better counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later writers keep the argument but not the ambiguity: Nepos and Justin each give a single answer telling the Athenians to defend themselves with wooden walls, and Justin's Themistocles argues that a city is its people, not its buildings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch says Themistocles also put the sign of the vanished sacred serpent into the priests' mouths, the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> credits the Areopagus with finding eight drachmas a man to man the ships, and Herodotus records that a few who stayed and fenced the acropolis with doors and logs died believing theirs was the wooden wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.140–7.143, 8.51, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=141. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=10:section=1. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Themistocles</i> 2, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 316–17, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.12, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 83, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 23.1, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","religion","politics","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Temple_of_Apollo_in_Delphoi.jpg/1280px-Temple_of_Apollo_in_Delphoi.jpg","title":"The temple of Apollo at Delphi","desc":"The standing columns of the temple of Apollo at Delphi, under the cliffs of Parnassus. It was here that the Athenian envoys sat in the inner hall and were told to flee to the ends of the earth, and here that they came back with suppliant boughs.","credit":"Photograph by Kyri Th., via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Apollo_in_Delphoi.jpg","alt":"Five weathered stone column drums standing on a ruined platform of large blocks, with a grey limestone cliff and dark cypresses rising behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the envoys go back to Delphi a second time?","a":"Because the first answer told them to flee to the ends of the earth, and on Timon's advice they returned with suppliant boughs, refusing to leave until they were given a better one."},{"q":"Why did the Salamis lines tell against reading the wall as ships?","a":"Because the readers of oracles took them to mean that a sea fight there would end in Athenian defeat, the verse promising death to women's sons."},{"q":"Why did Themistocles' reading prevail?","a":"Because he argued that had the verse meant the Athenians the god would have called Salamis cruel rather than divine, so it spoke of the enemy, and the assembly judged him the better counsellor."}]},
{"id":"gr-421","num":421,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Ten thousand hoplites went by sea to hold the pass between Olympus and Ossa, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came home after a few days without meeting the Persians at all.","questions":["Herodotus says fear ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, once the Greeks learned of a second way into Thessaly through Perrhaebia, and not the friendly warning from Alexander of Macedon.","Diodorus gives a different reason for abandoning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that the Thessalians and their neighbours had already given earth and water to the king's envoys."],"answer":"Tempe expedition","answerText":"Tempe expedition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 hoplites</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, without a fight</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Tempe expedition</b> was the first Greek attempt to stop Xerxes, a force sent north in 480 BCE to hold the pass where the Peneus leaves Thessaly for the sea between Olympus and Ossa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thessalian messengers had come to the allies asking that the pass of Olympus be guarded, warning that they would otherwise have to make terms with the Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus says about ten thousand men-at-arms went with cavalry, carried by sea through the Euripus and landed at Alus in Achaea to march up into Thessaly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Lacedaemonian general was Euaenetus son of Carenus, chosen from among the polemarchs though not of the royal house, and the Athenian was Themistocles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says Themistocles had wanted to meet the Persians at sea and led this army north only because many opposed him, Thessaly not yet being thought to be medizing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They stayed a few days and left: messengers from Alexander son of Amyntas of Macedon pointed out the size of the army and the number of the ships, and advised them not to stay and be trodden underfoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus judges that what really moved them was fear, since they had learned of another way into Thessaly through the hill country of the Perrhaebi near Gonnus, which was the road Xerxes in fact took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus gives a different account, naming the Spartan commander Synetus and saying the generals despaired because the Thessalians and their neighbours had already given earth and water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The place itself is a chasm: Strabo says an earthquake tore Ossa from Olympus and let the Peneus drain what had been a lake, and Herodotus reports the Thessalian tradition that Poseidon made the passage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Xerxes came to see it he asked whether the river had another outlet, and on being told it had none remarked that the Thessalians had been wise to yield, since a dam would have put their whole country under water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.128–7.130, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=130. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.172–7.174, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=173. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:2008.01.0066:chapter=7:section=1. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.2, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=2. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.5.2, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=5:section=2. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Tempi1.jpg","title":"The vale of Tempe","desc":"The Peneus running through the defile between Olympus and Ossa, the pass the Greeks were sent to hold in 480 BCE and left again after a few days.","credit":"Photograph by Mzmona, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tempi1.jpg","alt":"A green river winding between steep wooded slopes, with a pale limestone cliff face rising on the right and the gorge narrowing into the distance under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the allies go north at all?","a":"Because Thessalian messengers had come asking that the pass of Olympus be guarded, warning that they would otherwise have to make terms with the Persian."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus disbelieve the reason the force gave for leaving?","a":"Because he judges that what really moved them was fear, since they had learned of another way into Thessaly through the hill country of the Perrhaebi, which is the road Xerxes took."},{"q":"Why did Xerxes call the Thessalians wise to yield?","a":"Because the Peneus has no other outlet, so a dam at the chasm would have put their whole country under water."}]},
{"id":"gr-422","num":422,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus lists 300 Spartans, 700 Thespians, 400 Thebans and thousands of Arcadians among the Greeks who held the pass at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 480 BCE.","questions":["After a mountain path was betrayed to the Persians, the survivors of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell back behind the wall and were buried under missiles on a hillock.","The Amphictyons set up pillars after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one of which told any passing foreigner to go and say that the Spartans lie here obedient to their commands."],"answer":"Battle of Thermopylae","answerText":"Battle of Thermopylae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spartans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Epitaph</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,000 against 3 million</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Thermopylae</b> was the three-day action of 480 BCE in which a small Greek force held the coast road into central Greece against Xerxes until a mountain path was betrayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the pass is about 15 metres (50 feet) across at its narrowest, with a precipitous spur of Oeta above and nothing but marsh and sea below, and a wall the Phocians had built long before running across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists three hundred Spartans, seven hundred Thespians and four hundred Thebans, with a thousand from Tegea and Mantinea and more than a thousand other Arcadians, Corinthians, Phliasians and Mycenaeans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Locrians and a thousand Phocians came after messengers told them this was only an advance guard, the rest expected daily, and the invader a mortal who would fall from pride.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias, comparing that muster with the one raised against the Gauls, notes that Herodotus gives no number for the Locrians and works one out himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Greeks held two days, sallying from the narrows and falling back behind the wall; on the third a mountain path was shown to the Persians and Leonidas sent most of the allies away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Knowing they would die, those who stayed came out beyond the narrows, where officers whipped the Persians forward and men were pushed into the sea or trampled by their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says they routed the enemy four times over the body of Leonidas and, when the encircling force arrived, retired to a hillock and fought with swords, hands and teeth until the missiles buried them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus tells it differently, Leonidas keeping only five hundred and dismissing the rest to fight another day, while Justin gives him four thousand at the outset and twenty thousand Persians on the summit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Amphictyons set pillars over the dead, one saying four thousand from the Peloponnese fought three million here and one telling a passing foreigner to say the Spartans lie obedient to their commands; Strabo saw both mound and pillar still standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.176, 7.201–7.228, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=225. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.9–11.11, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=9. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.11, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 30–31, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.20.1–10.20.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=20. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.4.16, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=4:section=16. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/71/Thermopylae_panorama_1.jpg","title":"The pass at Thermopylae today","desc":"The wall of Mount Kallidromon above the road at Thermopylae. The flat ground in front of it was sea in 480 BCE; the Malian Gulf has since silted up, so the pass Herodotus measured at about 15 metres across is now a plain.","credit":"Photograph by Davide Mauro, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thermopylae_panorama_1.jpg","alt":"A steep wooded mountainside with pale limestone crags, rising above a modern road and a broad flat expanse of white gravel under a clear blue sky."},"locator":{"name":"Thermopylae","at":[22.5628,38.8053],"kind":"battle"},"quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"7","text":"<p>There is an inscription written over these men, who were buried where they fell, and over those who died before the others went away, dismissed by Leonidas. It reads as follows: Here four thousand from the Peloponnese once fought three million. That inscription is for them all, but the Spartans have their own: Foreigner, go tell the Spartans that we lie here obedient to their commands.</p>","cite":"Book 7.228"},"why":[{"q":"Why was this pass chosen to be held?","a":"Because it is about 15 metres across at its narrowest, with a precipitous spur of Oeta above and nothing but marsh and sea below, and a wall the Phocians had built long before running across it."},{"q":"Why did the Locrians and Phocians join a force so small?","a":"Because messengers told them this was only an advance guard with the rest expected daily, and that the invader was a mortal who would fall from pride."},{"q":"Why did the numbers on the Greek side become impossible to fix?","a":"Because Herodotus gives no figure for the Locrians, Diodorus has Leonidas keep only five hundred and dismiss the rest, and Justin gives him four thousand at the outset."}]},
{"id":"gr-423","num":423,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The Spartan king who commanded the Greeks at Thermopylae in 480 BCE and died holding the pass was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an Agiad who had come to the kingship unexpectedly.","questions":["Told the Persian arrows would blot out the sun, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have answered that it would then be pleasant to fight in the shade.","Xerxes had the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut off and impaled, which Herodotus reads as anger, since the Persians usually honoured a brave enemy."],"answer":"Leonidas I","answerText":"Leonidas I","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chose</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 Spartans, all with sons</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bones brought home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 years later</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Leonidas I</b> was the Agiad king of Sparta who commanded the Greeks at Thermopylae in 480 BCE and died holding the pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus traces his descent back through twenty names to Heracles and says he came to the kingship unexpectedly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was a younger son of Anaxandrides who had renounced any thought of ruling, and succeeded only when Cleomenes died without a son and Dorieus was killed in Sicily; he had also married Cleomenes' daughter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He took to Thermopylae three hundred men he chose himself, all of whom had living sons, and brought the Thebans deliberately because they were suspected of medizing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus says he asked the ephors for a thousand men only, and when pressed answered that they were few for holding the pass but many for the task they were really going to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus reports that the Pythia had told the Spartans either their city would fall or a king of Heracles' line must die, and thinks Leonidas sent the allies away so as to leave a great name behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sayings collected under Plutarch's name have him answer a warning that the arrows would hide the sun by remarking that they would fight in the shade, and Xerxes' demand for his arms with 'come and take them'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Justin has him tell his men before the last day that they must expect to perish, and take care not to show more resolution in staying than in fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus says the Greeks routed the enemy four times over his body, and that Xerxes had the head cut off and impaled, which he reads as unusual anger in a people known for honouring brave men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Forty years afterwards the bones were brought home from Thermopylae, and Pausanias saw the tomb at Sparta with a slab naming every man who had endured the fight, and yearly speeches and a contest open to Spartans alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.204–7.206, 7.220, 7.225, 7.238, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=220. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.4, 11.11, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=4. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Sayings of Spartans</i> 225B–225D, trans. Frank Cole Babbitt, in <i>Plutarch: Moralia</i>, vol. 3, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1931), LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Sayings_of_Spartans*/main.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.14.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=14. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.11, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 30–31, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","politics","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg/1280px-Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg","title":"The marble hoplite from Sparta","desc":"A marble warrior of about 480 BCE found on the Sparta acropolis, in the sanctuary of Athena of the Bronze House, and now in the museum there. It is traditionally called Leonidas, but nothing on it names him.","credit":"Photograph by Mary Harrsch, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_a_hoplite_known_as_Leonidas_480-470_BCE_Sparta_Acropolis_Sanctuary_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_01.jpg","alt":"The upper half of a marble statue of a helmeted warrior, the tall crest of the helmet rising behind, the cheek pieces framing a bearded face with an archaic smile."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this man come to the kingship at all?","a":"Because he was a younger son who had renounced any thought of ruling, and succeeded only when Cleomenes died without a son and Dorieus was killed in Sicily."},{"q":"Why were the three hundred chosen as they were?","a":"Because he picked men who all had living sons, and brought the Thebans deliberately because they were suspected of medizing."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus think he sent the allies away?","a":"Because the Pythia had told the Spartans either their city would fall or a king of Heracles' line must die, so he reads it as a wish to leave a great name behind him."}]},
{"id":"gr-424","num":424,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus names <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the man who told Xerxes of the mountain path, and says he did it expecting a great reward from the king.","questions":["A price was put on the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the Amphictyons at Pylae, and he was killed years later at Anticyra for some quite different reason.","Some said two other men had guided the Persians, but Herodotus rejects that and puts <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on record as the guilty one instead."],"answer":"Ephialtes of Trachis","answerText":"Ephialtes of Trachis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Betrayal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent by night</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rival guides</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, named by others</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Ephialtes of Trachis</b> was the man Herodotus blames for the fall of Thermopylae, having shown Xerxes the mountain path by which the Greeks holding the pass were surrounded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls him the son of Eurydemus and a Malian in one chapter and 'Epialtes the Trachinian' in the next, and says he came to the king expecting a great reward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes was delighted and sent Hydarnes out with his men at about lamp-lighting time, and Strabo's whole summary of the battle is that the barbarians made a circuit of the mountains by narrow paths and cut the Greeks to pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The path was not his discovery: Herodotus says the Malians had found it long before and used it to bring the Thessalians into Phocis when the Phocians had walled the pass off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fled to Thessaly in fear of the Spartans, and while he was in exile the Pylagori set a price on his head at the Amphictyonic meeting at Pylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He came back to Anticyra and was killed there by Athenades, a Trachinian, for a reason Herodotus says was quite different, though the Spartans honoured him for it all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another story made Onetes son of Phanagoras of Carystus and Corydallus of Anticyra the guides, and Herodotus rejects it because the price was set on Ephialtes and not on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus tells the same act without the name, calling him only a Trachinian who knew the mountains, and has Xerxes heap presents on him and send twenty thousand men out by night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Diodorus the Greeks are warned all the same, by a Persian named Tyrrhastiadas of Cyme who deserted in the dark to tell Leonidas, and Justin likewise puts twenty thousand on the summit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias, describing the Gauls two centuries later, says the Heracleots and Aenianians led Brennus over the same mountain, not from ill will to Greece but because they wanted the Celts gone from their country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.213–7.215, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=213. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.8, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=8. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.22.8–10.22.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=22. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.11, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 30–31, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.4.16, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0239:book=9:chapter=4:section=16. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Thermopylae_panorama_3.jpg/1280px-Thermopylae_panorama_3.jpg","title":"The ridge above Thermopylae","desc":"Mount Kallidromon on the left and the plain running away east, seen from above the pass. The path Ephialtes showed the Persians crossed this ridge and came down behind the Greek position.","credit":"Photograph by Davide Mauro, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thermopylae_panorama_3.jpg","alt":"A wide view along a wooded mountain flank on the left, with a modern road and pylons crossing a flat pale plain to the right and blue hills in the haze beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the betrayal not the same as a discovery?","a":"Because the Malians had found the path long before and used it to bring the Thessalians into Phocis when the Phocians had walled the pass off."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus reject the rival names for the guide?","a":"Because the price was set on Ephialtes at the Amphictyonic meeting and not on Onetes or Corydallus, which is what settles it for him."},{"q":"Why did the man who killed him still take the reward?","a":"Because Herodotus says the reason for the killing was quite different, and yet the Spartans honoured Athenades for it all the same."}]},
{"id":"gr-425","num":425,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"When Leonidas dismissed his allies on the last morning, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone chose to stay, saying they would not abandon him but would die beside the Spartans.","questions":["Herodotus counts 700 of these Boeotian hoplites at the pass, and their general Demophilus son of Diadromes led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who refused to leave with the other allies.","A year after the pass, 1,800 of the surviving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mustered at Plataea without hoplite armour, their town having been burnt by the Persians."],"answer":"Thespians","answerText":"Thespians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At the pass</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700, in 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">City burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, by Xerxes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,800 survivors, 479 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Thespians</b> were the men of Thespiae, a small Boeotian city whose 700 hoplites stayed with Leonidas at Thermopylae in 480 BCE and were killed there beside the Spartans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thespiae lay a little south of Mount Helicon in southern Boeotia, above the Crisaean Gulf, with a port of its own at Creusa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Xerxes' heralds asked for earth and water, every city in Boeotia gave them except Thespiae and Plataea, and the Greeks who resisted swore to tithe to Delphi the goods of any Greek who had submitted willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus lists 700 Thespians in the force that waited at the pass, beside 300 Spartans, 400 Thebans, 400 Corinthians and the Arcadian and other Peloponnesian contingents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the last morning, once the mountain path had been betrayed and Leonidas had sent the rest of the allies away, only the Thespians and the Thebans stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus distinguishes the two carefully: the Thebans were held back as hostages against their will, while the Thespians remained gladly under their general Demophilus son of Diadromes, saying they would not abandon Leonidas but would stay and die with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus tells it differently, keeping back the Thespians alone and giving Leonidas no more than 500 men in all, where the figures in Herodotus come to about 1,400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch, attacking Herodotus for malice against Thebes, quotes that passage back at him and accepts without argument that the Thespians stood by Leonidas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sailors who crossed from the fleet to view the field took all 4,000 of the Greek dead for Lacedaemonians and Thespians, though helots lay among them; the collective epigram at the pass counted only 'four thousand from the Peloponnese', and no Thespian stone is recorded there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xerxes burnt Thespiae, whose people had already left for the Peloponnese, and the following year 1,800 Thespian survivors mustered at Plataea with no hoplite armour at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.132, 7.202, 7.222, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=222. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.228, 8.25, 8.50, 9.30, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=25. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.9, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=9. [Open access]","Plutarch, “Of Herodotus's Malice,” in <i>Plutarch's Morals</i>, ed. William W. Goodwin, vol. 4 (Boston: Little, Brown, 1878), 352–53, https://archive.org/details/plutarchsmorals04plut. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.2.25, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=9:chapter=2:section=25. [Open access]"],"tags":["people","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Thespians_monument.jpg/1280px-Thespians_monument.jpg","title":"The monument to the 700 Thespians","desc":"A modern memorial beside the pass at Thermopylae, unveiled in 1997. The marble plinth is inscribed in Greek 'in memory of the seven hundred Thespians', and carries a headless winged bronze figure. No ancient portrait of the Thespians survives.","credit":"Photograph by Dmpexr, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thespians_monument.jpg","alt":"A white marble plinth on a paved terrace between dark cypresses, carrying a headless bronze figure with one outstretched wing; an electricity pylon rises behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus distinguish them from the Thebans who also stayed?","a":"Because the Thebans were held back as hostages against their will, while these men remained gladly under Demophilus, saying they would not abandon Leonidas but would stay and die with him."},{"q":"Why did this small city refuse the king's heralds?","a":"Because every city in Boeotia gave earth and water except it and Plataea, and the Greeks who resisted had sworn to tithe to Delphi the goods of any Greek who submitted willingly."},{"q":"Why is their part in the battle easy to lose sight of?","a":"Because the collective epigram at the pass counted only 'four thousand from the Peloponnese', no stone of theirs is recorded there, and Diodorus gives Leonidas no more than 500 men in all."}]},
{"id":"gr-426","num":426,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"While Leonidas held the pass, the Greek fleet fought three days off the north coast of Euboea in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, keeping Xerxes' ships out of the Euripus.","questions":["The Euboeans paid Themistocles thirty talents to keep the fleet from sailing away, which is how the Greeks came to fight the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> off their island rather than retreat south.","Pindar said the sons of the Athenians laid the corner-stone of freedom in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the fleet withdrew towards Salamis as soon as it heard that Leonidas was dead."],"answer":"Battle of Artemisium","answerText":"Battle of Artemisium","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, over three days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">271 triremes, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian loss</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 ships wrecked off Euboea</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Artemisium</b> was the naval action fought off the north coast of Euboea in 480 BCE, on the same days as Thermopylae, between the Greek fleet and Xerxes' navy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Artemisium is a beach on the Euboean shore where the Thracian sea narrows between Sciathus and Magnesia; a temple of Artemis Proseoea stood there, enclosed by slabs of white marble, one carrying an elegy on the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Greeks mustered 271 triremes under the Spartan Eurybiades, the Athenians providing 127 and manning twenty more for the Chalcidians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes' fleet had already been mauled by a storm off Cape Sepias in Magnesia, where ships were driven ashore and broken along the coast of Pelion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two positions were held as one: the fleet was to keep the Persians out of the Euripus while the men at Thermopylae held the pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Greeks nearly withdrew at the sight of the Persian fleet at Aphetae, and stayed only because the Euboeans paid Themistocles thirty talents, of which he passed five to Eurybiades and three to the Corinthian Adimantus, keeping the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scyllias of Scione, reckoned the best diver of his day, came over from the Persians with word that 200 ships had been sent round Euboea to trap the Greeks; that squadron was wrecked in a night storm on the Hollows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the first engagement the Greeks drew their sterns together with prows outward, took thirty ships and broke off at nightfall; on the third day the Persians came out at midday and neither side gave way, the Athenians ending with half their ships damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus tells it as two battles rather than three days, places it after Thermopylae had fallen, and counts 280 Greek triremes against Herodotus' 271.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Word of Leonidas' death was brought by Abronichus and the fleet withdrew, Themistocles cutting appeals to the Ionians into the rocks at the watering places; Plutarch quotes Pindar on the beach where \"Athenians' valiant sons set in radiance eternal Liberty's corner-stone\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.176, 8.1–8.2, 8.4–8.5, 8.8, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=1. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.11, 8.13–8.18, 8.21–8.23, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=15. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.12–11.13, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=12. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 9.5.22, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=9:chapter=5:section=22. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Beach_at_Cape_Artemisium_on_Euboea.jpg/1280px-Beach_at_Cape_Artemisium_on_Euboea.jpg","title":"The strait at Cape Artemisium","desc":"The beach on the north coast of Euboea where the Greek fleet lay, looking across the water to the mountains of Magnesia. The Persian fleet was moored on that far shore, at Aphetae.","credit":"Photograph by Marco Prins for Livius.org, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beach_at_Cape_Artemisium_on_Euboea.jpg","alt":"A pebbled shore and calm blue-green sea framed by an olive branch, with a long range of hazy mountains along the far horizon."},"locator":{"name":"Cape Artemisium","at":[23.2269,39.0125],"kind":"battle"},"why":[{"q":"Why was this position held at all?","a":"Because the fleet was to keep the Persians out of the Euripus while the men at Thermopylae held the pass, so the two positions were held as one."},{"q":"Why did the Greek fleet not withdraw at the sight of the Persian one?","a":"Because the Euboeans paid Themistocles thirty talents, of which he passed five to Eurybiades and three to the Corinthian Adimantus, keeping the rest."},{"q":"Why did the fleet leave a position it had not lost?","a":"Because word of Leonidas' death was brought by Abronichus, and the pass being gone the line at sea no longer held anything."}]},
{"id":"gr-427","num":427,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"With Xerxes' army through the pass and coming south, Themistocles carried a decree for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sending families to Troezen, Aegina and Salamis and every man of fighting age aboard the fleet.","questions":["The sacred snake on the acropolis left its honey-cake untouched, and the priestess read the sign as the goddess departing, which made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> easier for Themistocles to carry.","The Troezenians voted two obols a day to each refugee family after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, let the boys pick fruit wherever they liked, and hired teachers for them."],"answer":"evacuation of Athens","answerText":"evacuation of Athens","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Evacuated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, after Thermopylae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Areopagus gave</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 drachmas a man</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troezen paid</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 obols a day per family</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>evacuation of Athens</b> was the removal of the city's population to Troezen, Aegina and Salamis in 480 BCE, carried out in the days between the loss of Thermopylae and the arrival of Xerxes' army, which came down through Boeotia and took a deserted city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Greek fleet had come back from Artemisium to Salamis at the Athenians' request, and they had learned that the Peloponnesians were fortifying the Isthmus rather than standing in Boeotia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Landing in Attica, they proclaimed that every man should get his children and his household away as best he could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most sent their families to Troezen, and some to Aegina and to Salamis, both to obey the oracle of the wooden wall and for another reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A great snake was kept in the sacred precinct on the acropolis and given a honey-cake every month; that month the cake was untouched, and when the priestess explained what it meant they were the readier to go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch tells the same story as stage management, with Themistocles putting the interpretation into the priests' mouths once human argument had failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also gives the measure that carried it, a bill entrusting the city to Athena and ordering every man of fighting age aboard the ships, proposed by one Nicagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Troezenians voted to keep the refugees at public cost at two obols a day for each family, let the boys pick fruit wherever they liked, and hired teachers for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There was no public money to man the fleet, and Aristotle says the council of the Areopagus found eight drachmas a head, which is why he credits it with the victory at Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus compresses the whole thing into one movement of wives, children and portable goods across to Salamis; Plutarch keeps what was remembered afterwards, the aged left behind and the dog of Xanthippus that swam beside its master's trireme and died on the island, at the place still called Dog's Mound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.40–8.41, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=41. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>The Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.13, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=13. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.50–8.51, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=51. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Troezen_-_Acropolis_09.JPG/1280px-Troezen_-_Acropolis_09.JPG","title":"Troezen and its coast","desc":"The plain below the acropolis of Troezen, looking north across the Saronic Gulf towards Attica. Most Athenian families were sent here, and the Troezenians voted to keep them at public cost.","credit":"Photograph by Schuppi, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troezen_-_Acropolis_09.JPG","alt":"A scrubby rocky hillside in the foreground falling to a green cultivated plain, with a low wooded ridge beyond it and deep blue open sea across the top of the frame."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Athenians take their families out of Attica?","a":"Because they had learned the Peloponnesians were fortifying the Isthmus rather than standing in Boeotia, so on landing they proclaimed that every man should get his household away as best he could."},{"q":"Why did an untouched honey-cake make them readier to go?","a":"Because the snake kept in the sacred precinct left its monthly cake, and the priestess explained that the goddess had abandoned the acropolis."},{"q":"Why could the fleet be manned when there was no public money?","a":"Because Aristotle says the council of the Areopagus found eight drachmas a head, which is why he credits it with the victory at Salamis."}]},
{"id":"gr-428","num":428,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A stone found in the Argolid carries what claims to be the Athenian order of 480 BCE to abandon the city, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been argued over ever since it appeared.","questions":["Herodotus and Aristotle both describe an improvised proclamation rather than a plan, and it is against them that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to be weighed as evidence for what Athens actually did.","Demosthenes shows that Athenian orators were reading a text of Themistocles' own measures aloud in the 340s BCE, more than two thousand years before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of the ground."],"answer":"Troezen decree","answerText":"Troezen decree","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decree dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, if genuine</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unearthed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1932 or 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1959</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Troezen decree</b> is an inscribed stone from Troezen carrying what purports to be the Athenian decree of 480 BCE that ordered the city abandoned and its men put aboard the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was turned up by accident in 1932 or 1933 and then stood for a quarter of a century in a farmer's house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A newly founded local society of friends of antiquity brought it out again, a visiting epigraphist took a squeeze of it in the summer of 1959, and within months the text was on American radio and in the international press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing so orderly appears in the fifth-century account: Herodotus has the fleet come back from Artemisium and the Athenians only then proclaim that every man should save his household as best he could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle is blunter, saying the generals had given up hope and made that proclamation, and that the council of the Areopagus found eight drachmas a head to get the ships manned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Against them stands Plutarch, who reports a real bill entrusting the city to Athena and ordering every man of fighting age aboard the ships, moved by one Nicagoras.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A written decree of Themistocles certainly existed in the 4th century, because Demosthenes complains that a rival read it out to the assembly beside the decree of Miltiades and the oath sworn by the young men of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The stone therefore sits at the meeting point of two questions, whether Athens planned the evacuation in advance or improvised it, and whether a text read aloud in the 340s BCE went back to 480 or was made to look as though it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its find-story settles neither, since it was out of the ground and out of sight for 25 years before anyone competent saw it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the city acted on a decree or on a general's despairing proclamation is thus not a question the stone can answer by itself, and the two writers nearest to the event describe an improvisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Georges Daux, “Trézène,” in “Chronique des fouilles et découvertes archéologiques en Grèce en 1959,” <i>Bulletin de correspondance hellénique</i> 84 (1960): 685–88, https://www.persee.fr/doc/bch_0007-4217_1960_num_84_2_20447. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.40–8.41, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=41. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>The Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>On the False Embassy</i> 19.303, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0072:speech=19:section=303. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","history","archaeology","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Themistocle_decree_EM_13330.JPG/1280px-Themistocle_decree_EM_13330.JPG","title":"The stone itself","desc":"The stele in the Epigraphical Museum, Athens, inventoried as EM 13330 and published as SEG 22.274. The left half of the face is worn almost smooth; the lines that survive on the right carry the words for Salamis and for the acropolis.","credit":"Photograph by Schuppi, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Themistocle_decree_EM_13330.JPG","alt":"A tall rectangular slab of pale marble on a low plinth against a deep blue wall, its left side eroded blank and rows of Greek capitals still legible down the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why does this stone matter more than its own text?","a":"Because it sits at the meeting point of two questions: whether Athens planned the evacuation in advance or improvised it, and whether a text read aloud in the 340s went back to 480."},{"q":"Why do the earliest writers tell against a decree?","a":"Because Herodotus has the proclamation made only after the fleet returned from Artemisium, and Aristotle says the generals had given up hope when they made it."},{"q":"Why cannot its discovery settle the question?","a":"Because it was turned up by accident and then stood for a quarter of a century in a farmer's house before anyone competent saw it."}]},
{"id":"gr-429","num":429,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A handful of treasurers and poor men fenced the citadel with doors and logs, and their stand ended in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when Persians climbed the cliff by Aglaurus' shrine.","questions":["Herodotus says the olive of Athena put out a shoot a cubit long the day after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when Xerxes had the Athenian exiles go up and sacrifice in their own manner.","Mardonius came back the next year to pull down whatever the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had left standing, so the Athenians returned to a circuit wall surviving only in stretches."],"answer":"sack of the Acropolis","answerText":"sack of the Acropolis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Stormed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burnt again</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, by Mardonius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Olive shoot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 cubit, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>sack of the Acropolis</b> was the storming and burning of the Athenian citadel by Xerxes' army in 480 BCE, days after the city below it had been emptied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few temple treasurers and poor men had stayed behind and fenced the height with doors and logs, having decided that the wooden wall of the oracle meant this and not the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persians took the Areopagus opposite and shot arrows wrapped in tow and set alight, burning the barricade away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They held out even so, refusing the terms the Peisistratids brought and rolling boulders down on anyone who came at the gates, so that for a long time Xerxes could not take the place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Men then climbed the sheer face at the front, by the precinct of Cecrops' daughter Aglaurus, where nobody had been posted because nobody thought it could be climbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some threw themselves from the wall and others fled into the chamber; the first men up opened the gates, killed the suppliants, then plundered the sanctuary and set the whole citadel alight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes sent a rider to Susa with the news and next day had the Athenian exiles in his train go up and sacrifice in their own manner, which Herodotus puts down to a dream or to remorse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They came back saying the olive of Athena, burnt with the rest, had put out a shoot a cubit long; Pausanias, telling the story six centuries later, has it grow two cubits on the day of the fire itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The next year Mardonius came back and pulled down whatever wall, house or temple was still standing, so that when the Athenians came home only stretches of the circuit stood, with a few houses spared because Persian officers had quartered in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pausanias was still shown fire-blackened statues of Athena on the citadel in the 2nd century CE, scorched, he says, when the king took the city with its fighting men gone to the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.51–8.53, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=53. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.54–8.55, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=55. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.13, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=13. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.27.2, 1.27.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=27. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.89, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=89. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Acropolis_North_wall.jpg/1280px-Acropolis_North_wall.jpg","title":"Column drums in the north wall","desc":"The north side of the Acropolis from below. The row of column drums set into the wall near the top came from a temple wrecked in 480 BCE and was laid into the new wall whole, rather than re-cut.","credit":"Photograph by Seligmanwaite, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acropolis_North_wall.jpg","alt":"A steep pale limestone cliff rising to a masonry wall, with a horizontal row of stacked cylindrical column drums built into the wall just below its top."},"why":[{"q":"Why did anybody stay on the citadel at all?","a":"Because a few temple treasurers and poor men had decided that the wooden wall of the oracle meant this height fenced with doors and logs, and not the ships."},{"q":"Why did the place fall where it did?","a":"Because men climbed the sheer face at the front, by the precinct of Aglaurus, where nobody had been posted because nobody thought it could be climbed."},{"q":"Why did the Athenian exiles go up to sacrifice the next day?","a":"Because Xerxes sent them to do it in their own manner, which Herodotus puts down to a dream or to remorse — and they came back reporting the burnt olive had put out a shoot."}]},
{"id":"gr-430","num":430,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Fought in the strait between Salamis and the Attic mainland in 480 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed the greater part of the navy Xerxes had brought against Greece.","questions":["Themistocles sent Sicinnus to tell the Persians the Greeks were about to run, and the trap they closed that night became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at first light.","Xerxes had a gilded throne set where he could watch, and secretaries beside it to record the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended with his brother Ariabignes dead and his fleet broken."],"answer":"Battle of Salamis","answerText":"Battle of Salamis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">378 triremes, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,000, per Aeschylus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Salamis</b> was the sea-fight in the strait between Salamis and the Attic mainland in 480 BCE, in which the Greek fleet destroyed the greater part of the navy of Xerxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus counts 378 Greek triremes, Aeschylus 310, and Thucydides speaks of four hundred of which the Athenians found little short of two-thirds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> With Athens burnt and the Peloponnesians pressing to fall back on the Isthmus, Themistocles warned Eurybiades that if the fleet left he would take the Athenians away and settle at Siris in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the vote went against him he sent Sicinnus, his servant and his sons' attendant, across to the Persian commanders with word that the Greeks were about to run and were at odds among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persians believed it, put men ashore on the islet of Psyttaleia, and spent the night closing both ends of the strait so that nothing could get out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At dawn the Greeks sang the paean and came on, and Aeschylus has them shout to free their land, their children, their wives and the tombs of their fathers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the narrows the Persian line had no room, and ships in the rear pressing forward to be seen doing something for the king ran foul of their own front and splintered the banks of oars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ariabignes, a brother of Xerxes, was killed, and many Persians drowned who could not swim, while Greeks whose ships went down swam across to Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristides landed hoplites on Psyttaleia and killed the Persians posted there; Xerxes watched from a gilded throne with secretaries beside him writing down what was done, though Plutarch's own authorities put that seat in two different places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides has the Athenians claim afterwards that they gave the most ships, the ablest commander and the steadiest nerve, and that it was Themistocles above all who made the fighting happen in the straits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.48, 8.56–8.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=62. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.75–8.96, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=84. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 337–432, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=399. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 13–14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.74, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=74. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Battle_of_Salamis_battle_order.jpg/1280px-Battle_of_Salamis_battle_order.jpg","title":"The strait, with the fleets marked","desc":"An aerial view east along the strait, with the Greek line drawn inside the narrows on the left and the Persian fleet in the open water beyond. The dispositions are a modern reconstruction laid over a photograph of the modern coast.","credit":"Map by the Department of History, United States Military Academy; photograph by Jona Lendering; via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Salamis_battle_order.jpg","alt":"An oblique aerial photograph of a narrow sea channel between hilly coasts, with dark blue blocks marking a fleet inside the narrows and a much larger spread of red blocks in the open water to the east."},"locator":{"name":"Salamis","at":[23.5,37.9333],"kind":"battle"},"quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"8","text":"<p>Then the Hellenes set sail with all their ships, and as they were putting out to sea the barbarians immediately attacked them. The rest of the Hellenes began to back water and tried to beach their ships, but Ameinias of Pallene, an Athenian, charged and rammed a ship. When his ship became entangled and the crew could not free it, the others came to help Ameinias and joined battle.</p>","cite":"Book 8.84"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the fleet fight in the strait rather than off the Isthmus?","a":"Because Themistocles warned Eurybiades he would take the Athenians away to Siris, and when the vote went against him sent Sicinnus to tell the Persians the Greeks were about to run."},{"q":"Why did the Persian numbers work against them there?","a":"Because in the narrows their line had no room, and ships in the rear pressing forward to be seen doing something for the king ran foul of their own front and splintered the banks of oars."},{"q":"Why did drowning fall so unevenly between the two fleets?","a":"Because many Persians could not swim, while Greeks whose ships went down swam across to Salamis."}]},
{"id":"gr-431","num":431,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Sparta supplied the admiral of the allied fleet although Athens supplied most of the ships, so it was the Spartan <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who had to be talked into fighting in the strait.","questions":["Themistocles answered 'Smite, but hear me' when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised his staff, and the fleet stayed to fight instead of falling back on the Isthmus.","After Salamis it was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who argued against breaking the bridges on the Hellespont, on the ground that a cornered Xerxes would be more dangerous in Europe than at home."],"answer":"Eurybiades","answerText":"Eurybiades","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commanded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, the allied fleet</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Took</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 of Themistocles' 30 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships led</span><span class=\"dt-v\">378, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Eurybiades</b> son of Euryclides was the Spartan who commanded the allied Greek fleet in 480 BCE, at Artemisium and then at Salamis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta supplied the admiral although Athens supplied most of the ships, because the allies said they would break the fleet up rather than be led by the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Artemisium he was ready to withdraw once he saw the size of the Persian fleet, and the Euboeans, wanting time to get their households away, paid Themistocles thirty talents to keep the ships where they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Themistocles passed five of that money to him as though it were a present of his own, and he stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Salamis he was again for falling back on the Isthmus, where the Peloponnesian army was gathering behind a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch calls him faint-hearted in a crisis and preserves the exchange the tradition remembered: when he said that at the games those who start too soon are caned, Themistocles answered that those who lag behind win no crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When he raised his staff as though to strike, Themistocles said 'Smite, but hear me', and he listened and was won over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus states the arrangement without embarrassment: Eurybiades was the admiral, while Themistocles managed the fleet's affairs, and it was to him that everyone looked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The judgement of weakness is not the whole man, for after Salamis he argued against sailing to break the bridges on the Hellespont, saying that a Xerxes shut into Europe would have to make his cause prosper there and that a beaten enemy is better let go; the other Peloponnesian commanders agreed with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greeks who met at the Isthmus could not agree who deserved the prize and went home leaving it undecided, but at Sparta the Lacedaemonians gave him a crown of olive for valour and Themistocles another for wisdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.2–8.5, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=5. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.49, 8.56–8.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=62. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.108–8.110, 8.123–8.124, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=124. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Themistocles</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Themistocles%2A.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.12, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=12. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Sparta supply the admiral when Athens supplied the ships?","a":"Because the allies said they would break the fleet up rather than be led by the Athenians, so the command went with the alliance rather than with the contribution."},{"q":"Why did the fleet stay at Artemisium against his own judgement?","a":"Because the Euboeans paid Themistocles thirty talents to keep the ships there, and five of it was passed to him as though it were a present."},{"q":"Why is the verdict of faint-heartedness not the whole man?","a":"Because after Salamis he argued against breaking the Hellespont bridges, saying a Xerxes shut into Europe would have to make his cause prosper there and a beaten enemy is better let go."}]},
{"id":"gr-432","num":432,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Alone among Xerxes' captains, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> advised him not to fight at sea before Salamis, saying the Greeks were as much stronger there as men are stronger than women.","questions":["Chased by an Athenian trireme, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rammed an allied ship of Calynda, and Xerxes, told she had sunk an enemy, said his men had become women and his women men.","The Athenians offered ten thousand drachmas to anyone who took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alive, so angry were they that a woman was making war on Athens."],"answer":"Artemisia I of Caria","answerText":"Artemisia I of Caria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 cities: Halicarnassus, Cos, Nisyrus, Calydnos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 ships, 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Price on her</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 drachmas, alive</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Artemisia I of Caria</b> was the ruler of Halicarnassus who fought for Xerxes at Salamis in 480 BCE with five ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, a Halicarnassian himself, marvels most at her of all the king's captains: a woman who took over her dead husband's rule though she had a grown son, and went on campaign under no compulsion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her father was Lygdamis, her mother Cretan, and she led Halicarnassus, Cos, Nisyrus and Calydnos, all Dorian cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her ships were reckoned the best in the fleet after the Sidonians', and she gave the king the best advice of any of his allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked before the battle, she alone told him to spare his ships and keep off the sea, since the Greeks were as much stronger there as men are stronger than women and would scatter home if he waited; he was pleased and did the opposite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Chased in the fighting by an Athenian ship and hemmed in by her own side, she rammed and sank an allied Calyndian vessel with its king Damasithymus aboard, and the Athenian broke off, taking her for a Greek or a deserter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus will not say whether she meant it or the ship fell in her path, and notes that nobody from the Calyndian crew survived to accuse her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xerxes, watching, was told she had sunk an enemy and said that his men had become women and his women men; the Athenians, indignant that a woman was making war on them, had offered ten thousand drachmas for her alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Afterwards he asked her again, and she told him to march home and leave Mardonius behind, which was what he wanted to hear; he thanked her and sent her to Ephesus with his sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Polyaenus collected further stratagems under her name, among them that she carried both Greek and Persian colours and flew whichever suited; and Pausanias saw her among the marble Persians of the Persian Stoa at Sparta, raised from the spoils of the war she lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.99, 8.68A–8.69, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=99. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.87–8.88, 8.93, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=87. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.101–8.103, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=102. [Open access]","Polyaenus, <i>Stratagems of War</i> 8.53, trans. R. Shepherd, https://www.attalus.org/translate/polyaenus8B.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 3.11.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=3:chapter=11. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Herodotus marvel at her above the king's other captains?","a":"Because she took over her dead husband's rule though she had a grown son, and went on campaign under no compulsion."},{"q":"Why did ramming an allied ship save her?","a":"Because the pursuing Athenian broke off, taking her for a Greek or a deserter, and Xerxes was told she had sunk an enemy."},{"q":"Why will Herodotus not say whether she meant it?","a":"Because he notes that nobody from the Calyndian crew survived to accuse her, so there is no witness to say whether the ship fell in her path."}]},
{"id":"gr-433","num":433,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A march of 45 days took Xerxes back to the Hellespont, and Herodotus measures the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by saying he brought home as good as none of the host he came with.","questions":["Aeschylus sends the army home across a Strymon frozen out of season, and the sun then opening the ice, which is his version of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Herodotus records as plague and starvation.","Justin has the king cross to Abydos in a fishing-boat at the end of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cannot resist the contrast with the man the whole ocean could scarcely hold."],"answer":"Persian retreat","answerText":"Persian retreat","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Retreat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, after Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-k\">March home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">45 days, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Left behind</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300,000 men, per Herodotus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Persian retreat</b> was the withdrawal of Xerxes and most of his army from Greece after Salamis in 480 BCE, with Mardonius left in Thessaly and a picked force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mardonius held the season wrong for campaigning and chose his own troops — the Immortals, the thousand horse, and the Medes, Sacae, Bactrians and Indians entire — some 300,000 men by Herodotus' count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xerxes reached the Hellespont in 45 days and brought back, Herodotus says, as good as none of his host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They ate the produce of every place they passed, and where there was none they ate grass, bark and leaves; plague and dysentery went with them, and the sick were left in the cities of Thessaly and Macedonia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They found the bridges broken by a storm and were ferried to Abydos, where food was plentiful and so many died of gorging and of the change of water that fewer still reached Sardis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus knew another version, in which the king took ship from Eion and the pilot told him the only escape was to lighten the load, at which the Persians on deck bowed and leapt into the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He rejects it, arguing that a king would have sent his nobles into the hold and thrown an equal number of Phoenician rowers overboard, and that the return was by road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aeschylus, writing for men who had been there, brings the army home a different way again, through Boeotia, Phocis, Thessaly and Macedonia to the Strymon, which a god froze out of season so that they crossed on the ice; then the sun opened the middle and men went through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus makes the flight a second trick of Themistocles, who sent the same servant to warn Xerxes that the Greeks meant to destroy the bridge of boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Justin has him reach Abydos with a few attendants and cross in a fishing-boat, and cannot resist the contrast: the man whom the ocean could scarcely hold, shrunk into a little boat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.113–8.117, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=115. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.118–8.119, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=118. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 480–514, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=480. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.19, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=19. [Open access]","Justin, <i>Epitome of the Philippic History of Pompeius Trogus</i> 2.13, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 33–34, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Mount_Pangaion%2C_Mt_Paggaio_or_Paggeo%2C_winter_landscape%2C_horizontal_image%2C_Nikisiani%2C_Greece.jpg/1280px-Mount_Pangaion%2C_Mt_Paggaio_or_Paggeo%2C_winter_landscape%2C_horizontal_image%2C_Nikisiani%2C_Greece.jpg","title":"Pangaion in winter","desc":"The snowy summit of Mount Pangaion in Thrace, on the road the army took home. Aeschylus names this mountain immediately before the night on which, he says, a god brought winter early and froze the Strymon.","credit":"Photograph by Zsolyomi, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Pangaion,_Mt_Paggaio_or_Paggeo,_winter_landscape,_horizontal_image,_Nikisiani,_Greece.jpg","alt":"A snow-covered rocky peak rising out of a bank of cloud, with dark frost-whitened wooded ridges below it and blue sky above."},"why":[{"q":"Why did more men die on the march than in the fighting?","a":"Because they ate the produce of every place they passed and where there was none ate grass, bark and leaves, with plague and dysentery going with them and the sick left behind."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus reject the story of the king's sea voyage?","a":"Because he argues a king would have sent his nobles into the hold and thrown an equal number of Phoenician rowers overboard, and holds that the return was by road."},{"q":"Why did Abydos kill men that hunger had spared?","a":"Because food was plentiful there after the march, and so many died of gorging and of the change of water that fewer still reached Sardis."}]},
{"id":"gr-434","num":434,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Alexander of Macedon carried an offer of rebuilt temples and self-rule to Salamis, the opening move of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the campaign left behind when Xerxes went home.","questions":["The council stoned Lycidas for proposing that his city consider the terms, and the women stoned his family, which is how Athens answered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the spring of 479 BCE.","Attica was found empty a second time, ten months after Xerxes had taken it, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with the city burnt again and the army withdrawn into Boeotia."],"answer":"Mardonius in Greece","answerText":"Mardonius in Greece","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Winter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 – 479 BCE, in Thessaly</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens retaken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 months after Xerxes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Withdrew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, into Boeotia</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Mardonius in Greece</b> is the campaign of the winter and summer after Salamis, in which the general Xerxes left behind tried first to win Athens over and then took the city again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He wintered in Thessaly and sent a man of Europus named Mys round the oracles to test them, though Herodotus says nobody could tell him what the question was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The answers, he says, advised making the Athenians an ally, and the envoy chosen was Alexander of Macedon, tied by marriage to a Persian house and a friend of Athens besides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The terms were generous: by Diodorus' account the Athenians might have any land in Greece they chose, their walls and temples rebuilt, and their own laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They refused in two directions at once, telling Alexander that so long as the sun holds his present course they will make no agreement with Xerxes, and telling the Spartans that no gold or land could buy them, because of the burnt temples of their gods and because the Greeks are one in blood and speech, with shrines, sacrifices and ways of living in common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He marched south, found Attica empty again, and took a city with no men in it, ten months after Xerxes had taken it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From Athens he sent a second envoy, Murychides, with the same terms, expecting a captured Attica to soften them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A councillor named Lycidas proposed putting the offer to the people; the council stoned him to death, and the Athenian women, hearing of it, went to his house and stoned his wife and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Demosthenes told the same story to an Athenian jury a century and a half later but called the man Cyrsilus: the deed remembered and the name not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the answer was plain he burnt what was left of Athens, threw down every wall and house and temple still standing, and withdrew into Boeotia, where the ground suited his horse and Thebes was friendly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.133, 8.136, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=136. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.143–8.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=144. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.3–9.5, 9.13, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=5. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>On the Crown</i> 18.204, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0072:speech=18:section=204. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.28, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=28. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/View_of_the_Thessalian_plain_from_the_acropolis_of_ancient_Pharsalos.jpg/1280px-View_of_the_Thessalian_plain_from_the_acropolis_of_ancient_Pharsalos.jpg","title":"The Thessalian plain","desc":"The plain of Thessaly from the acropolis of ancient Pharsalos, with the modern town of Farsala below. This is the flat country the army wintered in, and the kind of ground its cavalry needed, which is why it went back to Boeotia rather than stay in Attica.","credit":"Photograph by Panegyrics of Granovetter, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Thessalian_plain_from_the_acropolis_of_ancient_Pharsalos.jpg","alt":"A wide green and gold farming plain seen from a wooded hillside, with a red-roofed town in the middle distance and low blue hills along the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Alexander of Macedon sent to make the offer?","a":"Because he was tied by marriage to a Persian house and was a friend of Athens besides, so the terms came from a man both sides could hear."},{"q":"Why did Athens refuse terms that would have rebuilt the city?","a":"Because of the burnt temples of their gods, and because the Greeks are one in blood and speech, with shrines, sacrifices and ways of living in common."},{"q":"Why did a councillor's proposal cost his family their lives?","a":"Because he proposed putting the Persian offer to the people, and the council stoned him while the Athenian women went to his house and stoned his wife and children."}]},
{"id":"gr-435","num":435,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"After days facing each other across the Asopus, the Greeks moved by night and were caught strung out at dawn, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began badly and ended the invasion.","questions":["Herodotus says only 91 Spartans, 17 Tegeans and 52 Athenians died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a figure Plutarch calls astonishing and replaces with 1,360.","Mardonius fell on a white horse among his thousand picked Persians, and with him the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned, the survivors running for a wooden stockade the Athenians had to breach."],"answer":"Battle of Plataea","answerText":"Battle of Plataea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, in Boeotia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek hoplites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">38,700, per Herodotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">160 to 10,000, by source</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Plataea</b> was the land battle in Boeotia in 479 BCE that ended the Persian invasion of Greece, fought between the allied army under <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Pausanias_the_regent\">Pausanias</span> and the force Mardonius had kept in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus counts 38,700 Greek hoplites and about 69,500 light-armed men, and puts Mardonius' army at 300,000 with perhaps 50,000 Greek allies; Diodorus makes it 100,000 against 500,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two sides faced each other across the Asopus for days, neither willing to attack on the other's ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Persian cavalry, mounted archers hard to close with, shot into the Greek lines and then fouled and blocked the Gargaphian spring, which was the whole army's water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Greeks moved by night to better ground; the move went wrong in the dark, and first light found the army strung out in pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Where Mardonius fought, on a white horse among a thousand picked Persians, the line held, and Herodotus says they were brave men but fought as good as naked, having no armour over their clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When he fell the rest gave way: killed by a Spartan named Aeimnestus, says Herodotus; by an Arimnestus who crushed his head with a stone, says Plutarch, who adds that an oracle had foretold exactly that death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The survivors ran for their wooden stockade, which the Spartans had no skill to storm; the Athenians came up and breached it, and the Tegeans were first through and took Mardonius' bronze manger, which they dedicated to Athena Alea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus then gives figures that have troubled every reader since: of 260,000 left after Artabazus fled with 40,000, scarcely 3,000 survived, while the Greeks lost 91 Spartans, 17 Tegeans and 52 Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch calls that astonishing, puts the Greek dead at 1,360, and argues that the graves themselves show the victory was shared; Diodorus says more than ten thousand were buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.28–9.32, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=29. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.49–9.52, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=49. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.63–9.70, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=70. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides%2A.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.30, 11.33, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=30. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/The_battlefield_of_Plataea_and_the_remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_the_city_on_April_10%2C_2019.jpg/1280px-The_battlefield_of_Plataea_and_the_remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_the_city_on_April_10%2C_2019.jpg","title":"The plain of Plataea","desc":"The plain north of Plataea, seen across the line of the city wall. The Asopus runs through the flat ground beyond, and the two armies faced each other across it for days before the fighting began.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_battlefield_of_Plataea_and_the_remains_of_the_fortification_wall_of_the_city_on_April_10,_2019.jpg","alt":"A meadow of yellow wildflowers in the foreground with a low line of tumbled squared blocks, opening onto a wide patchwork of green and ploughed fields and hazy hills under a cloudy sky."},"locator":{"name":"Plataea","at":[23.2739,38.22],"kind":"battle"},"quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"9","text":"<p>So long as Mardonius was alive the Persians stood their ground and defended themselves, overthrowing many Lacedaemonians. When, however, Mardonius was killed and his guards, who were the strongest part of the army, had also fallen, then the rest too yielded and gave ground before the men of Lacedaemon. For what harmed them the most was the fact that they wore no armor over their clothes and fought, as it were, naked against men fully armed.</p>","cite":"Book 9.63"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Greeks move position by night?","a":"Because the Persian cavalry had fouled and blocked the Gargaphian spring, which was the whole army's water — and the move went wrong in the dark, leaving the army strung out at first light."},{"q":"Why did the Persian line break when its general fell?","a":"Because it held only where he fought, on a white horse among a thousand picked Persians, and Herodotus says they were brave men fighting as good as naked, having no armour over their clothes."},{"q":"Why have the casualty figures troubled every reader?","a":"Because Herodotus has scarcely 3,000 of 260,000 survive against 91 Spartans, 17 Tegeans and 52 Athenians, while Plutarch puts the Greek dead at 1,360 and argues the graves show the victory was shared."}]},
{"id":"gr-436","num":436,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"The victor of Plataea ended walled into the temple of Athena of the Brazen House and starved, which is how Sparta dealt with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once his intriguing with the helots was proved.","questions":["Told to impale Mardonius' head as the Persians had done to Leonidas, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that the deed was fitter for barbarians than for Greeks.","A couplet cut on the Delphic tripod claiming the monument as his own was erased by the Spartans, and it was remembered against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when he was later recalled."],"answer":"Pausanias the regent","answerText":"Pausanias the regent","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Regent for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Pleistarchus, from 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commanded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, at Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statues owed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 in bronze, to the goddess</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pausanias the regent</b> was the Spartan who commanded the Greek army at Plataea in 479 BCE, son of Cleombrotus and guardian of the boy king Pleistarchus, son of Leonidas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The command was his because Pleistarchus was still a child, and he took a kinsman, Euryanax son of Dorieus, as his colleague.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus calls the victory he won there the most glorious of all the victories we know of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When an Aeginetan named Lampon urged him to impale Mardonius' head as the Persians had done to Leonidas, he answered that the deed was fitter for barbarians than for Greeks, and blameworthy even in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He had Mardonius' cooks lay out a Persian dinner beside a Laconian one, called the Greek generals in to look at both, and asked them to consider the folly of a man who came with all that to take away their poverty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two years later, commanding the allied fleet at Byzantium, he had become insufferable: Thucydides says there was more of the mimicry of a despot in him than of a general, and the Ionians went to Athens to be rid of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recalled to Sparta, he was censured for private oppressions and acquitted on the heaviest charge, which was medism, and was never given the command again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was remembered against him afterwards was the couplet he had cut on the tripod at Delphi claiming the monument for himself, which the Spartans erased and replaced with the names of the cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was also intriguing with the helots, promising them freedom and citizenship, and a courier from Argilos opened his last letter, found an order for his own death in it, and turned informer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took sanctuary in the temple of Athena of the Brazen House and was walled in and starved, his mother laying the first brick against the door if Diodorus is right, and Delphi afterwards made Sparta give the goddess two bronze statues in his place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.10, 9.64, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=10. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.78–9.79, 9.82, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.94–1.95, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=95. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.128–1.134, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.45, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=45. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","warfare","greece"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Remains_of_the_Temple_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_Temple_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","title":"The Brazen House","desc":"The site of the temple of Athena Chalkioikos on the acropolis of Sparta, with modern Sparta below and the Taygetus range beyond. Very little of the sanctuary is left above ground; this is where he took refuge and was walled in.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, via Wikimedia Commons (CC0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Temple_of_Athena_Chalkioikos_in_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","alt":"A fenced patch of low stone foundations among grass and pines, with a town spread across the valley below and a long snow-topped mountain range on the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a man who was not king command the army?","a":"Because the king Pleistarchus, son of Leonidas, was still a child and he was his guardian, taking a kinsman as his colleague."},{"q":"Why did he refuse to impale Mardonius' head?","a":"Because he answered that the deed was fitter for barbarians than for Greeks, and blameworthy even in them, though the Persians had done it to Leonidas."},{"q":"Why did his own letter destroy him?","a":"Because a courier from Argilos opened it, found an order for his own death inside, and turned informer, which is what brought the intrigue with the helots to light."}]},
{"id":"gr-437","num":437,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Samian envoys asked the Greek fleet at Delos to free the Greeks of Asia, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to be fought on a beach opposite Samos in 479 BCE.","questions":["A rumour of victory in Boeotia ran through the line as it advanced, and Herodotus says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and Plataea really did fall on the same day.","The Milesians, posted to guard the passes, led the beaten Persians into their enemies after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Ionia revolted from the king a second time."],"answer":"Battle of Mycale","answerText":"Battle of Mycale","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, the day of Plataea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persian army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60,000 or 100,000, by source</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greek fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">250 triremes, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Mycale</b> was fought on the Ionian coast opposite Samos in 479 BCE, when the Greek fleet landed and stormed the beached Persian ships and the camp round them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Samian envoys came to the Greeks at Delos asking them to free the Greeks of Asia, and the fleet sailed east under the Spartan Leotychides and the Athenian Xanthippus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Persians would not fight at sea; they sent the Phoenicians away, beached the rest under the army Xerxes had left to hold Ionia, and fenced them in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus puts that army at 60,000 under Tigranes, the tallest and noblest man in Persia; Diodorus makes it about 100,000, gathered from Sardis and nearby towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Leotychides sailed close inshore and called to the Ionians in the enemy camp to remember their freedom and the watchword 'Hebe', so as either to win them over or to have them mistrusted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> As the line advanced a rumour ran through it that Mardonius had been beaten in Boeotia, and a herald's wand was found at the water's edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus says the rumour was true and the battles fell on the same day, Plataea early and this one in the afternoon, with a precinct of Eleusinian Demeter on each field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenians and those with them had the beach and the level ground and pressed hard so the Spartans, circling through a ravine, should not have the victory; the shields went down and the Persians ran inside the wall, the Greeks coming in with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tigranes and Mardontes were killed, and the Milesians, posted to guard the passes, led the fleeing Persians into their enemies and killed them, so that Ionia revolted a second time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Greeks burnt the ships and the wall, then argued at Samos whether to move the Ionians west and leave Asia to the Persians; the Athenians refused, the islanders were sworn into the alliance, and the fleet sailed for the Hellespont, where the Athenians stayed the winter to take Sestos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.90–9.96, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=96. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.98–9.101, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=101. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.102–9.106, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=102. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.34, 11.36, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=34. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.89, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=89. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg/1280px-Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg","title":"Mount Mycale across the strait from Samos","desc":"The mainland headland of Mycale seen from the north coast of Samos. The Persian ships were beached on the shore beneath it in 479 BCE, and the Greek fleet crossed this water to attack them.","credit":"Photograph by Athens2004, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Mycale_and_Mycale_Strait.jpg","alt":"A hazy blue strait of sea seen from a scrub-covered hillside, with a long low rocky spit in the foreground and a high mountain ridge rising beyond the water."},"locator":{"name":"Mount Mycale","at":[27.1506,37.6611],"kind":"battle"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Persians beach their ships instead of fighting at sea?","a":"Because they sent the Phoenicians away and drew the rest up under the army Xerxes had left to hold Ionia, fencing them in behind a wall."},{"q":"Why did the Spartan commander call out to the enemy's Greeks?","a":"Because appealing to the Ionians in the camp to remember their freedom would either win them over or have them mistrusted by the Persians."},{"q":"Why did the Milesians make the defeat worse than the fighting had?","a":"Because they had been posted to guard the passes and led the fleeing Persians into their enemies and killed them, so Ionia revolted a second time."}]},
{"id":"gr-438","num":438,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"A tenth of the spoil taken at Plataea paid for a gold tripod at Delphi, and the twisted bronze that carried it is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, standing today in Istanbul.","questions":["Thucydides says the Spartans erased a couplet claiming the victory for Pausanias alone and cut the names of the allied cities on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its place.","The Phocian leaders took its gold and Constantine took what was left to his new capital, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives as three headless bronze snakes on a pedestal."],"answer":"Serpent Column","answerText":"Serpent Column","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dedicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, at Delphi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE, by the Phocians</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century CE, to Constantinople</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Serpent Column</b> is the bronze monument dedicated at Delphi from a tithe of the spoil taken at Plataea in 479 BCE, three snakes twined into one shaft whose heads carried a gold tripod.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus says the Greeks set that tripod nearest the altar and made two further offerings from the same tithe, a bronze Zeus of ten cubits at Olympia and a Poseidon of seven at the Isthmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides reports that Pausanias had a couplet cut on it claiming the victory for himself, that the Spartans erased the lines at once, and that they cut in their place the names of the cities which had helped overthrow the Persian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus gives a different couplet instead, in which the saviours of far-flung Hellas raise the gift after delivering their states from slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The list is what makes the object matter, being a record made at the time of which states claimed a share in the victory; Grosvenor counted 31 names on the coils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias the traveller, writing centuries later, saw only the bronze: the Phocian leaders, he says, had not left the gold as they left the metal under it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eusebius records that Constantine stripped the temples of their bronzes and had the Delphic tripods set up in the hippodrome of his new city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The coils have stood in that space ever since, and Grosvenor measured them at 5.7 metres (18 feet 9 inches), broken and headless, filled with stones dropped in by passers-by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> One head survives in the museum at Istanbul, its torn edges showing it was wrenched away; how and when the others went, Grosvenor says, nobody knows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He could find nothing above the tenth coil, the letters there having worn off, but read the lower ones where buried earth had protected them, naming the Tirynthians, Plataeans, Thespians, Mycenaeans, Ceans, Malians and Tenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=81. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.132, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.33, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=33. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.13.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=13. [Open access]","Eusebius, <i>Life of Constantine</i> 3.54, trans. Ernest Cushing Richardson, in <i>Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers</i>, 2nd ser., vol. 1, ed. Philip Schaff and Henry Wace, New Advent, https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/25023.htm. [Open access]","Edwin A. Grosvenor, <i>The Hippodrome of Constantinople and Its Still Existing Monuments</i>, London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle and Rivington, 1889, 32, 50-54, https://archive.org/details/hippodromeconst00grosgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","history","warfare","greece","art"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Y%C4%B1lanl%C4%B1_S%C3%BCtun.jpg/1280px-Y%C4%B1lanl%C4%B1_S%C3%BCtun.jpg","title":"The column today, in the old hippodrome","desc":"The three twined bronze snakes stand in a sunken enclosure in Istanbul, headless, with the gold tripod they carried long gone and one of the holes broken in the coils plainly visible.","credit":"Photograph by Cobija, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Y%C4%B1lanl%C4%B1_S%C3%BCtun.jpg","alt":"A tall shaft of green-patinated bronze, made of three snake bodies twisted together, rising from a paved circular pit below street level, with a ragged hole in one coil and nothing at the top."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the list of names matter more than the bronze?","a":"Because it is a record made at the time of which states claimed a share in the victory, cut by the Spartans in place of the couplet Pausanias had claimed it with."},{"q":"Why is the monument now in a hippodrome rather than at Delphi?","a":"Because Constantine stripped the temples of their bronzes and had the Delphic tripods set up in his new city, where the coils have stood ever since."},{"q":"Why can only the lower coils still be read?","a":"Because the letters above the tenth have worn off, while buried earth protected the ones below, which name the Tirynthians, Plataeans, Thespians, Mycenaeans, Ceans, Malians and Tenians."}]},
{"id":"gr-439","quote":{"book":"herodotus-histories","n":"1","cite":"Book 1.1","text":"<p>This is the display of the inquiry of Herodotus of Halicarnassus, so that things done by man not be forgotten in time, and that great and marvelous deeds, some displayed by the Hellenes, some by the barbarians, not lose their glory, including among others what was the cause of their waging war on each other.</p>"},"num":439,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus opens by asking what cause Greeks and barbarians had for making war on each other, and the nine books of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are his long answer to that question.","questions":["Rival accounts are left standing side by side in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Athenians say Adeimantus fled under sail from Salamis and the Corinthians deny it flatly.","Herodotus asks his reader to hold, for the whole of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that setting down what he has been told is not the same thing as believing it."],"answer":"Histories","answerText":"Histories","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Narrative ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, at Sestos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Books</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9, in the standard division</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Xerxes' army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,283,220, by his own count</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Histories</b> of Herodotus is the earliest surviving prose account of the Persian Wars, nine books tracing the rise of Persia and its collision with the Greek cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opens by naming itself an inquiry, set down so that human deeds should not fade with time and the great works of Greeks and barbarians alike should not lose their glory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That same sentence gives the subject as a question, namely what cause the two had for making war on one another, rather than as a chronicle of events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having reported Persian and Phoenician stories of the mythical abductions, he declines to say which is true and turns instead to the man he says he knows began the wrongs against the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds there that he will speak of small and great cities alike, since states once great have become small and human prosperity never stays in one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His rule for the rest is stated outright: it is his business to set down what he is told, and no business of his to believe it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So rival versions stand side by side, as when the Athenians said the Corinthian Adeimantus hoisted sail and fled from Salamis, the Corinthians denied it flatly, and the rest of Greece bore them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He names informants where he can, among them Thersander of Orchomenus, who described a Persian weeping at a Theban banquet over the ruin he knew was coming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where a figure is his own arithmetic he says so, doubling the fighting men to allow for the baggage train and reaching 5,283,220 for the force Xerxes led as far as Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thucydides, a generation later, set out a deliberately different practice, composing speeches to what each occasion demanded and testing his narrative against eyewitnesses rather than taking it from the first source to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.1, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.5, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=5. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.152, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=152. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.94, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=94. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.16, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=16. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.186, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=186. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.22, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=22. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg/1280px-POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg","title":"A papyrus fragment of Book VIII","desc":"Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, a scrap of the eighth book written out in Greek capitals with no spaces between the words, from the rubbish mounds of a town in Roman Egypt.","credit":"Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POxy_v0017_n2099_a_01_hires_-_cropped.jpg","alt":"A ragged brown papyrus fragment with about a dozen lines of large Greek capital letters written continuously, the edges torn and several holes through the sheet."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the work state its subject as a question?","a":"Because its opening asks what cause the Greeks and the barbarians had for making war on one another, rather than announcing a chronicle of events."},{"q":"Why do contradictory versions stand side by side in it?","a":"Because the stated rule is that it is his business to set down what he is told and no business of his to believe it, as with the charge that Adeimantus fled from Salamis."},{"q":"Why does the author mark out his own arithmetic?","a":"Because he says where a figure is his own, as when he doubles the fighting men to allow for the baggage train and reaches 5,283,220 for the force led to Thermopylae."}]},
{"id":"gr-440","num":440,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Born a subject of the Great King at Halicarnassus in Caria, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended his life as a colonist at Thurii, where the Suda says he lies buried in the market-place.","questions":["The writer who went upriver as far as Elephantine to look for himself, and took everything beyond it by question and hearsay, was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose subject was the war with Persia.","Aristotle quotes the opening sentence of the great war narrative as the exposition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Thurii, where the received text of the same sentence says Halicarnassus."],"answer":"Herodotus","answerText":"Herodotus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 484 BCE, per Aulus Gellius</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Halicarnassus, in Caria</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still writing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 431 BCE, into the Peloponnesian War</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Herodotus</b> was a Greek prose writer of the 5th century BCE, born at Halicarnassus in Caria, a city on the Anatolian coast that was then subject to the Persian king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Aulus Gellius put his birth in 484 BCE, and his own work shows him alive into the early years of the Peloponnesian War, so his life spanned the rise and fall of Athenian pre-eminence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tradition has him spend time on Samos, return home and help drive out the tyrant Lygdamis, then leave again when he found himself disliked by his countrymen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He went as a volunteer to the new colony at Thurii in southern Italy, and the Suda says he died there and lies buried in its market-place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The travels behind the work took in much of Asia Minor, Egypt as far south as Aswan, Cyrene, Syria, perhaps lands as far east as Mesopotamia, and the northern coast of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He tells the reader where his own eyes stop, having gone upriver to Elephantine to look for himself and taken everything beyond it by question and hearsay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> To settle how old the worship of Heracles was he took ship to Tyre, saw the temple there with its pillar of gold and its pillar of emerald, and asked the priests when it had been built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Part way through the Egyptian book he draws a line outright, saying that all he has written so far rests on his own observation, judgement and inquiry, and that what follows is Egyptian report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even his name is unsettled in antiquity: the work as received opens 'Herodotus of Halicarnassus', but Aristotle quotes that same sentence as the exposition of 'Herodotus of Thurii'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He was argued with from early on, and Plutarch devoted a whole treatise to the charge that he wrote of the Greek cities with malice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["A. D. Godley, general introduction to <i>Herodotus</i>, vol. 1, Loeb Classical Library (London: W. Heinemann, 1921), vii-xiv, https://archive.org/details/herodotus01hero. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.29, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=29. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.44, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=44. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 2.99, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=2:chapter=99. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Rhetoric</i> 3.9, trans. J. H. Freese, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0060:book=3:chapter=9. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 1.1, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","literature","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Erodoto%2C_replica_del_III_sec._dc_da_orig._greco_del_400-350_ac_ca.%2C_6146.JPG/1280px-Erodoto%2C_replica_del_III_sec._dc_da_orig._greco_del_400-350_ac_ca.%2C_6146.JPG","title":"A Roman herm with the name cut on it","desc":"A marble portrait from the Farnese collection, now in Naples, copying a Greek original. The name is cut in Greek capitals across the shaft below the shoulders, which is what makes the identification secure.","credit":"Photograph by Sailko, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erodoto%2C_replica_del_III_sec._dc_da_orig._greco_del_400-350_ac_ca.%2C_6146.JPG","alt":"A weathered white marble bust of a balding, heavily bearded man on a squared pillar, with worn Greek capital letters incised along the front of the pillar beneath him."},"why":[{"q":"Why does he mark where his own observation stops?","a":"Because he went upriver to Elephantine to look for himself and took everything beyond it by question and hearsay, saying so plainly, as he does again part way through the Egyptian book."},{"q":"Why did he sail to Tyre over a question about Heracles?","a":"Because to settle how old the worship was he wanted to see the temple with its pillar of gold and its pillar of emerald, and to ask the priests when it had been built."},{"q":"Why is even his own city unsettled in antiquity?","a":"Because the work as received opens 'Herodotus of Halicarnassus' while Aristotle quotes the same sentence as the exposition of 'Herodotus of Thurii'."}]},
{"id":"gr-441","num":441,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Aeschylus set his tragedy at Susa and gave every speaking part to the losing side, so that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands as the one surviving Greek tragedy on a historical rather than a mythical subject.","questions":["A messenger at Susa counts 310 Greek ships against a thousand of the king's in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then reports the shout that went up to free fatherland, children and wives.","Darius rises from his tomb to blame burnt temples and to foretell a defeat on Plataean soil in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was first produced at Athens in 472 BCE."],"answer":"Persians","answerText":"Persians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Produced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">472 BCE, at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">eight years after Salamis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Persians</b> is a tragedy of Aeschylus, first produced at Athens in 472 BCE, and the one surviving Greek tragedy that takes a recent historical event rather than a myth for its subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It is set at Susa rather than anywhere in Greece, and everyone on stage is on the losing side: a chorus of Persian elders, the queen mother Atossa, the ghost of Darius, and Xerxes himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Atossa has to ask her counsellors where Athens lies, which lets an Athenian audience hear its own city and its own strength described from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A messenger brings the news of Salamis and gives the numbers: 310 Greek ships against a thousand of the king's, of which 207 were the fast ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He describes the Greek line coming on at dawn behind a paean and a shout to free fatherland, children and wives, the temples of the fathers' gods and the tombs of the ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The retreat is told as suffering rather than as manoeuvre, the army starving through Thrace until a god froze the Strymon out of season and the ice gave way under the men crossing at sunrise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Darius, raised from his tomb, reads the disaster as payment for temples burnt and images thrown down, and foretells a further defeat still to come on Plataean soil at the Dorian spear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The play ends with Xerxes coming on in torn clothes with a thin retinue, and a long antiphonal lament between him and the elders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The risk in taking such a subject was known at Athens: Herodotus says Phrynichus was fined a thousand drachmas for a play on the fall of Miletus that reminded the audience of its own loss, and the piece was banned for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Aeschylus had fought at Marathon, as his own epitaph records, and his play was staged again later in Sicily at the wish of Hiero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 215-248, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=215. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 331-352, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=331. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 395-432, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=402. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 480-514, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=480. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 800-831, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=800. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 909-930, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=909. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.21, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=21. [Open access]","A. O. Prickard, introduction to <i>The Persae of Aeschylus</i> (London: Macmillan, 1879), ix-xxxvii, https://archive.org/details/gashlouprsaiper01aescgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["text","literature","history","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/The_Theatre_of_Dionysus_in_Athens_on_October_7%2C_2018.jpg/1280px-The_Theatre_of_Dionysus_in_Athens_on_October_7%2C_2018.jpg","title":"The theatre of Dionysus, below the Acropolis","desc":"The site where the play was first staged, seen from the slope above with the city beyond. Most of the stone now standing is later than 472 BCE, the theatre having been rebuilt more than once.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Theatre_of_Dionysus_in_Athens_on_October_7%2C_2018.jpg","alt":"A hillside above a semicircular ruined theatre with a flat paved orchestra and broken rows of stone seating, with a modern city and low hills spreading out behind it."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the play set at Susa rather than in Greece?","a":"Because everyone on stage is on the losing side, which lets an Athenian audience hear its own city and its own strength described from outside, Atossa having to ask where Athens lies."},{"q":"Why was staging a recent event a risk at Athens?","a":"Because Phrynichus had been fined a thousand drachmas for a play on the fall of Miletus that reminded the audience of its own loss, and that piece was banned for ever."},{"q":"Why does the dead king read the defeat as punishment?","a":"Because Darius's ghost takes it as payment for temples burnt and images thrown down, and foretells a further defeat still to come on Plataean soil at the Dorian spear."}]},
{"id":"gr-442","num":442,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Giving the Persian king earth and water, or marching in his army, was the charge of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Herodotus lists the peoples who did it without being compelled to.","questions":["The Greeks who declared war swore that a tenth of the property of everyone guilty of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would go to the god at Delphi if they came through the invasion.","Thebes was besieged after Plataea until it gave up the leading men accused of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whom Pausanias then carried off to Corinth and killed without a trial."],"answer":"Medism","answerText":"Medism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oath sworn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before the invasion of 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Thebes besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, after Plataea</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Medism</b> was the Greek charge of taking the Persian side, named from the Medes, and it covered everything from giving the king earth and water to marching in his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The demand for earth and water was the test, and Xerxes sent no heralds at all to Athens or Sparta, since heralds of Darius had been thrown into the Pit at one city and a well at the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus lists those who gave themselves up without being compelled: Thessalians, Dolopes, Enienes, Perrhaebians, Locrians, Magnesians, Melians, Achaeans of Phthia, Thebans, and all Boeotia except Thespiae and Plataea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Against them the Greeks who declared war swore that if they won they would dedicate to the god at Delphi a tenth of the property of every Greek who had gone over of free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The charge was already a weapon between Greek cities: when Aegina gave earth and water to Darius, the Athenians went to Sparta and accused the islanders of betraying Hellas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Refusing was not always a matter of principle, and Herodotus says so outright: the Phocians alone in their region held out, and would certainly have gone over had the Thessalians not been on the other side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Plataea the allied army marched on Thebes and refused to leave the ground before the city until the leading men were handed over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Attaginus escaped and Pausanias let his sons go, saying that sons were not party to the treason; the men who were surrendered expected to buy off the charge, so he sent the army home and had them killed at Corinth without a trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Theban defence, put by Thucydides two generations later, was that the city had then been held by a narrow cabal which brought the Persians in to secure its own power, so that the city was not its own mistress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That same speech turns the charge round, arguing that Plataea kept clear only because Athens did, and afterwards 'Atticized' just as readily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.49, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=49. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.132, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=132. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.133, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=133. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.30, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=30. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.86-9.88, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=86. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.62, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=62. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","politics","greece","iran"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Ruins_at_the_Sanctuary_of_Apollo_in_Delphi_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg/1280px-Ruins_at_the_Sanctuary_of_Apollo_in_Delphi_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg","title":"The sanctuary of Apollo at Delphi","desc":"Terrace walls and foundations below the Phaedriades cliffs. It was to the god here that the Greeks who fought swore to dedicate a tenth of the property of every city that had gone over to the king of its own accord.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_at_the_Sanctuary_of_Apollo_in_Delphi_on_October_4%2C_2020.jpg","alt":"Grassy terraces of tumbled ancient stone walls and cut blocks climbing a hillside, with dark cypresses in front of a high pale limestone cliff."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Persians send no heralds to Athens or Sparta?","a":"Because heralds of Darius had already been thrown into the Pit at one city and a well at the other, so the demand for earth and water was not put to them again."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus deny that every refusal was principled?","a":"Because he says the Phocians alone in their region held out, and would certainly have gone over had the Thessalians not been on the other side."},{"q":"Why did Pausanias let the accused men's sons go?","a":"Because he said sons were not party to the treason, where the men handed over expected to buy the charge off, so he sent the army home and had them killed at Corinth without a trial."}]},
{"id":"gr-443","num":443,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Cut on a stone or a bronze to say whom it stands for, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was written in elegiac couplets, and Herodotus quotes three of them from the graves at Thermopylae.","questions":["Athens set a four-horse chariot on the acropolis out of Chalcidian ransom money and had a four-line <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut on it naming whom the city had beaten in battle.","The Spartans erased the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Pausanias had cut on the Delphic tripod claiming the victory for himself, and put the names of the allied cities in its place."],"answer":"epigram","answerText":"epigram","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">At Thermopylae</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, three stones</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On the tripod</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, recut by Sparta</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"An <b>epigram</b> in Greek is an inscription, the word meaning exactly that, and the earliest are verse cut on a stone or an object to say what it is or whom it stands for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before prose was in use, anything an inscription said beyond a bare name had to be in verse, and the form settled on the elegiac couplet, which stayed the metre of inscriptions ever after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Persian Wars produced the most famous group of them, and Herodotus quotes three cut over the graves at Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One stands for the whole force, saying that four thousand from the Peloponnese once fought three million there; one is for the Spartans alone, telling the passer-by to report that they lie obedient to orders; the third names the seer Megistias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus adds that the Amphictyons put up all of them except the seer's, which Simonides had cut out of guest-friendship, so the earliest source gives him the least famous of the three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> They were not only for the dead: out of a tenth of Chalcidian ransom money Athens set a bronze four-horse chariot on the acropolis with four lines naming whom the city had beaten and what the defeat had cost them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus writes that the fetters of those same prisoners still hung on walls the Persian fire had charred, which is his own eye rather than a report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One could also be taken away: the Spartans erased the couplet Pausanias had cut on the Delphic tripod claiming the victory for himself, and put the names of the allied cities in its place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those names went on the bronze coils that carried the tripod, and 31 of them have been traced there since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Simonides is the name the later tradition attached to the whole class: 59 stand under it in the Palatine manuscript and 18 more in Planudes, with nine others doubtfully his.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["J. W. Mackail, introduction and biographical index to <i>Select Epigrams from the Greek Anthology</i> (London: Longmans, Green, and Co., 1890), 1-7, 287-88, https://archive.org/details/selectepigramsfr00mackuoft. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.228, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=228. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.77, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=77. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.132, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=132. [Open access]","Edwin A. Grosvenor, <i>The Hippodrome of Constantinople and Its Still Existing Monuments</i>, London: Sampson Low, Marston, Searle and Rivington, 1889, 32, 50-54, https://archive.org/details/hippodromeconst00grosgoog. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","literature","poetry","greece","history"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Cod._Pal._graec._23%2C_Anthologia_Palatina%2C_5r.jpg/1280px-Cod._Pal._graec._23%2C_Anthologia_Palatina%2C_5r.jpg","title":"A page of the Palatine manuscript","desc":"Codex Palatinus graecus 23 at Heidelberg, the Byzantine copy through which most surviving Greek epigrams have come down, including the couplet Herodotus read at Thermopylae.","credit":"Universitatsbibliothek Heidelberg, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cod._Pal._graec._23%2C_Anthologia_Palatina%2C_5r.jpg","alt":"A stained parchment page covered in close brown Greek minuscule writing in ruled lines, with red library stamps at the foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why were these inscriptions written in verse?","a":"Because before prose was in use anything an inscription said beyond a bare name had to be in verse, and the form settled on the elegiac couplet."},{"q":"Why is the attribution of the Thermopylae verses uncertain?","a":"Because Herodotus says the Amphictyons put up all of them except the seer's, which Simonides had cut out of guest-friendship, so the earliest source gives him the least famous of the three."},{"q":"Why could such a text be taken away again?","a":"Because the Spartans erased the couplet Pausanias had cut on the Delphic tripod claiming the victory for himself, and put the names of the allied cities in its place."}]},
{"id":"gr-444","num":444,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Sparta asked Athens not to rebuild after the invasion, so Themistocles went to argue the point while the city hurried up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sparing no building for its stone.","questions":["Mardonius had pulled down whatever was left standing, and the foundations of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still show column drums taken from tombs and unworked blocks laid in as they came.","Spartan inspectors were quietly detained at Athens until the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood high enough to defend, and only then did Themistocles tell his hosts the city was fortified."],"answer":"Themistoclean Wall","answerText":"Themistoclean Wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, after the invasion</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">on the ruins Mardonius left</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Themistoclean Wall</b> is the circuit Athens threw round itself in 479 BCE, as soon as the Persians had gone, and the one the city kept for the rest of the classical period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was little to repair: Mardonius had burnt Athens before withdrawing and pulled down whatever wall, house or temple was still standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides says only isolated stretches of the old circuit survived, and that most houses were ruins apart from a few the Persian grandees had taken as quarters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta sent an embassy asking Athens not to build, and to help pull down the walls of other cities outside the Peloponnese, arguing that a third invasion would then find no strong base of the kind Thebes had given it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says the real motive was another, that the allies were alarmed at the new Athenian navy and at what the city had shown in the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Themistocles had himself sent to Sparta at once and told the city to hold his colleagues back until the wall stood high enough to defend, with the whole population at work, men, women and children, and no building spared for its stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Sparta he denied the reports, advised his hosts to send men of their own to look, and had those inspectors quietly detained at Athens until his colleagues reached him and he could say outright that the city was walled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus tells it differently in one respect, making Themistocles offer himself as surety and be put under guard, so that the release becomes an exchange of hostages rather than a bluff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Sparta showed no open anger, and Thucydides adds that feeling there was still friendly towards Athens because of what she had done against the Mede.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The haste is visible in the masonry, the foundations laid of stones of every kind, some neither worked nor fitted but set in the order the carriers brought them, with column drums from tombs and carved blocks built in among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.89, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=89. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.90, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=90. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.91-1.92, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=91. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=93. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.13, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=13. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.39-11.40, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=39. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","history","warfare","greece","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Remains_of_the_Themistoclean_Wall_in_Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_April_12%2C_2021.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_Themistoclean_Wall_in_Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_April_12%2C_2021.jpg","title":"A surviving stretch at the Kerameikos","desc":"Courses of stones of visibly different sizes, colours and finishes, on the line of the circuit of 479 BCE. The wall was repaired and rebuilt more than once afterwards, so not every block standing here belongs to the first building.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Themistoclean_Wall_in_Kerameikos_Cemetery_on_April_12%2C_2021.jpg","alt":"A low ancient wall of mismatched limestone blocks in uneven courses running across a grassy excavated area, with a modern street railing and trees above it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Sparta ask Athens not to rebuild its walls?","a":"Because it argued that a third invasion would then find no strong base of the kind Thebes had given the last one, though Thucydides says the real motive was alarm at the new Athenian navy."},{"q":"Why did Themistocles go to Sparta himself?","a":"Because he could hold his colleagues back there while the wall rose, denying the reports, inviting inspectors, and having those men quietly detained at Athens until he could say the city was walled."},{"q":"Why is the haste visible in the stones?","a":"Because the foundations were laid of stones of every kind, some neither worked nor fitted but set in the order the carriers brought them, with column drums from tombs built in among them."}]},
{"id":"gr-445","num":445,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Themistocles thought three harbours better than the one at Phaleron, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went up two wagons thick, of squared blocks clamped on the outside with iron and lead.","questions":["Only about half the height Themistocles intended was reached on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose bulk was meant to let a garrison of men unfit to row hold it alone.","Diodorus says a second embassy went to Sparta about a first-rate harbour for the common Greek good, and under that cover the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was finished before anyone expected."],"answer":"Piraeus wall","answerText":"Piraeus wall","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 479 BCE, on his urging</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Harbours</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, against one at Phaleron</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Triremes added</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 a year, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Piraeus wall</b> is the fortification Themistocles pressed on Athens round the rocky peninsula that replaced Phaleron as the city's harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias says the Piraeus was an old deme and no port at all until Themistocles was archon, Athens having used Phaleron because the sea comes nearest to the city there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What recommended the change was three natural harbours against Phaleron's one, and Diodorus adds that the Phaleric bay was small where this could be made the best and largest harbour in Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says the walls had been begun in that archonship and that Themistocles now persuaded the city to finish them, having been the first to tell the Athenians to stick to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus puts the whole scheme after the war instead and tells it as a secret: Themistocles would not state his plan in the assembly, so the people chose Aristides and Xanthippus to hear it privately and report whether it was sound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On their word a second embassy went to Sparta, arguing that a first-rate harbour served the common Greek interest against a Persian return, and the work was finished before anyone expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The wall was carried up to a thickness two wagons could meet on, of great squared stones with neither rubble nor mortar between them, clamped on the outside with iron and lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About half the intended height was reached, and the point of the bulk was that a small garrison of men unfit for service could hold it while everyone else went to the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus adds the policies that went with the harbour, 20 triremes to be built every year and the tax on metics and artisans removed, to draw in the crafts a navy needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides says Themistocles rated the Piraeus above the upper city and always advised going down into it and defying the world with the fleet; two generations later a Peloponnesian squadron found it unguarded and open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=93. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.1.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=1. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.41, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=41. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.42-11.43, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=42. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=93. [Open access]"],"tags":["building","history","warfare","greece","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Plan_Piraeus.svg/1920px-Plan_Piraeus.svg.png","title":"A plan of the ancient town","desc":"The peninsula with its three harbours, the wall circuit marked as the heavy line running round the coast and across the neck, and the gridded street plan between them.","credit":"Plan by Tomisti, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plan_Piraeus.svg","alt":"A line plan of a peninsula with three pale blue inlets, a heavy black line following the whole shore and cutting across the landward neck, and a grid of streets filling the middle."},"why":[{"q":"Why was this peninsula preferred to the older harbour?","a":"Because it had three natural harbours against Phaleron's one, and the Phaleric bay was small where this could be made the best and largest harbour in Greece."},{"q":"Why was the wall built so thick and left half its height?","a":"Because the point of the bulk was that a small garrison of men unfit for service could hold it while everyone else went to the fleet."},{"q":"Why did the scheme go to two men rather than to the assembly?","a":"Because in Diodorus's version Themistocles would not state his plan publicly, so the people chose Aristides and Xanthippus to hear it privately and report whether it was sound."}]},
{"id":"gr-446","num":446,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Gelon's horsemen were let into the Carthaginian naval camp as allies, killed Hamilcar at his sacrifice and fired the ships, which settled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 480 BCE.","questions":["Herodotus was told in Sicily that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell on the very day of Salamis, while Diodorus puts it instead on the day of Thermopylae.","Carthage paid 2,000 talents and built two temples to hold copies of the treaty after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its captives quarried the stone for the temples at Acragas."],"answer":"Battle of Himera","answerText":"Battle of Himera","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, in northern Sicily</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Enemy dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">150,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Indemnity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2,000 talents of silver</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Himera</b> was fought on the north coast of Sicily in 480 BCE between the forces of Gelon of Syracuse and Theron of Acragas and a Carthaginian army under Hamilcar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus puts that army at 300,000 Phoenicians, Libyans, Iberians, Ligurians, Elisyci, Sardinians and Corsicans; Diodorus gives the same figure with over 200 warships and more than 3,000 transports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus says a storm on the crossing sank the ships carrying the horses and chariots, and that Hamilcar, putting in at Panormus, remarked that the war was already finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gelon came up with 50,000 foot and over 5,000 horse, dug in outside the city behind a ditch and palisade, and sent his cavalry against the enemy parties out foraging, bringing in more than 10,000 prisoners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What decided it was a ruse: at sunrise horsemen rode up to the Carthaginian naval camp, were let through as allies, killed Hamilcar at his sacrifice and set fire to the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The two armies were already engaged when the flames went up and word ran along the lines that the general was dead, and the Carthaginian side broke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gelon had ordered that no prisoners be taken; Diodorus puts the dead at 150,000, with 20 ships escaping only to founder in a storm and a few men reaching Carthage to report that everyone who had crossed to Sicily was gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How Hamilcar died is told three ways: Herodotus reports that he vanished and could not be found, that the Carthaginians said he threw himself on his own sacrificial pyre, and Diodorus that the disguised horsemen cut him down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The sources disagree about the day as well, the Sicilians telling Herodotus that it fell on the day of Salamis while Diodorus places it on the day of Thermopylae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The peace cost Carthage 2,000 talents of silver and two temples to hold copies of the treaty, and the captives were set to quarrying stone, so many of them going to Acragas that private households held 500 apiece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.165, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=165. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.166-7.167, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=166. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.20, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=20. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.21, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=21. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.22, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=22. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.24, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=24. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.25-11.26, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=25. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","warfare","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Himera_Viktoriatempel.jpg/1280px-Himera_Viktoriatempel.jpg","title":"The great temple at Himera","desc":"The Doric temple whose foundations still stand on the plain below the town, known by tradition as the Temple of Victory. Diodorus says Gelon had spoil from the battle nailed up on the most notable temples in the city.","credit":"Photograph by Clemensfranz, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himera_Viktoriatempel.jpg","alt":"Seen from a hillside above, the rectangular stone foundations and stepped platform of a Greek temple standing in a field between a country road and a railway line."},"locator":{"name":"Himera","at":[13.824,37.974],"kind":"battle"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the ruse decide a battle already joined?","a":"Because the horsemen were let through as allies, killed the general at his sacrifice and fired the ships, and word that he was dead ran along the lines as the flames went up."},{"q":"Why is the enemy commander's death told three ways?","a":"Because Herodotus reports that he vanished and could not be found, that the Carthaginians said he threw himself on his own pyre, and Diodorus that the disguised horsemen cut him down."},{"q":"Why did so many of the prisoners end up at Acragas?","a":"Because the captives were set to quarrying stone, and so many went there that private households held five hundred apiece."}]},
{"id":"gr-447","num":447,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Envoys from Sparta and Athens came out to Sicily for help against Xerxes, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told them that in refusing him the command their year had lost its spring.","questions":["When the Megarians in Sicily surrendered, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made citizens of the rich men who had fought him and sold into slavery abroad the commons who had not.","Cadmus of Cos was sent to Delphi with money and orders to hand it over with earth and water if Persia won, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did instead of joining the Greeks."],"answer":"Gelon","answerText":"Gelon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Won at Himera</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, over Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 years, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 stades from Syracuse</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Gelon</b> was the ruler of Gela and then of Syracuse who made his city the strongest power in Sicily and broke a Carthaginian army at Himera in 480 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His family had come from Telos at the founding of Gela and held the priesthood of the underworld goddesses; he began in the guard of the tyrant Hippocrates and rose to command his cavalry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having taken Syracuse he handed Gela to his brother Hiero and set about enlarging his new city by moving people into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus says he razed Camarina and brought its people in, moved half the men of Gela, and when the Megarians surrendered made citizens of the rich who had fought him while selling the commons into slavery out of Sicily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Envoys from Sparta and Athens came asking help against Xerxes, and he answered that when he had asked them against the Carthaginians, and to avenge Dorieus, not one of them had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He offered a great army and fleet on condition of commanding the Greeks, and when the Spartan refused that and the Athenian refused him even the ships, he told them to go home and say their year had lost its spring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What he did instead is the awkward part of the record: he sent Cadmus of Cos to Delphi with three ships and money, to watch the outcome and hand it over with earth and water if the Persians won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cadmus brought all of it back when the Greeks had won, which Herodotus records as an act of justice on Cadmus' part, not on Gelon's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus reads him quite differently, as a beneficent ruler who kept the city's law limiting funeral expense in his own case and handed the kingship to Hiero as he lay dying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He reigned seven years and was buried on his wife's estate 200 stades from the city, the whole population walking out behind him; the tomb was later destroyed by the Carthaginians and its towers thrown down by Agathocles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.153-7.154, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=153. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.156, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=156. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.157-7.158, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=157. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.159-7.162, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=159. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.163-7.164, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=163. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.38, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=38. [Open access]"],"tags":["ruler","history","politics","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Coin_of_Gelon_I%2C_struck_at_the_Syracuse_mint%2C_dated_480-478_BC.jpg","title":"A Syracusan tetradrachm of his reign","desc":"Silver, struck about 480 BCE: a charioteer driving a slow four-horse team with Nike flying above, and on the other face a nymph's head ringed by dolphins. The coin names the city, not the ruler.","credit":"Photograph by Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Gelon_I%2C_struck_at_the_Syracuse_mint%2C_dated_480-478_BC.jpg","alt":"Two faces of a worn silver coin side by side: on the left a four-horse chariot with a driver and a small winged figure above, on the right a woman's head in profile with four dolphins around it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he refuse the Greek envoys who asked for help?","a":"Because he answered that when he had asked them against the Carthaginians, and to avenge Dorieus, not one of them had come — and they would not give him the command."},{"q":"Why is the mission of Cadmus the awkward part of his record?","a":"Because he sent him to Delphi with money, to watch the outcome and hand it over with earth and water if the Persians won."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus credit the justice to the envoy rather than the ruler?","a":"Because Cadmus brought all of it back when the Greeks had won, which is recorded as an act of justice on his part and not on the man who sent him."}]},
{"id":"gr-448","num":448,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Herodotus makes it a quarrel between Sicilian tyrants where Diodorus makes it half a bargain struck with Xerxes, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 480 BCE has two quite different explanations.","questions":["Three years of hiring in Italy, Liguria, Galatia and Iberia went into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Diodorus puts at over 300,000 foot soldiers and 200 warships.","Once the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had failed, the city kept watch every night expecting Gelon to sail against it, and paid 2,000 talents of silver for peace."],"answer":"Carthaginian invasion of Sicily","answerText":"Carthaginian invasion of Sicily","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Launched</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE, from Carthage</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prepared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 years, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foot soldiers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300,000, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Carthaginian invasion of Sicily</b> was the expedition of 480 BCE that put in at Panormus under Hamilcar and was destroyed at Himera by Gelon of Syracuse and Theron of Acragas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Why it came is told two ways, and the two are not easily reconciled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus gives a purely Sicilian cause: Terillus, thrown out of Himera by Theron, brought Hamilcar in, helped by Anaxilas of Rhegium, who had married Terillus' daughter and handed his own children over as hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus instead makes it half of a concerted plan, Xerxes sending to Carthage and agreeing that he would attack the Greeks of Greece while Carthage subdued those of Sicily and Italy at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that account Carthage spent three years preparing, hiring mercenaries from Italy, Liguria, Galatia and Iberia and enrolling its own people from across Libya; Herodotus, who reports both the Sicilian and the Carthaginian accounts of the campaign, mentions no such agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Fighting Greeks in Sicily was not new to Carthage, since a generation earlier the Spartan Dorieus and the companions who sailed with him had been killed there by Phoenicians and Egestans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gelon threw that at the Greek envoys who came asking his help, saying he had once asked them against the Carthaginians and to avenge Dorieus, and that not one of them had come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus puts the armament at more than 300,000 foot and 200 warships with over 3,000 transports, and says a storm on the crossing took the vessels carrying the horses and chariots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the defeat the city kept watch every night in the belief that Gelon would sail against it, went into public mourning, and filled its houses with lamentation for the missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The peace cost 2,000 talents of silver and two temples to hold copies of the treaty, and no Carthaginian army came back to Sicily for seventy years, until Egesta asked for one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.1, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=1. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.20, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=20. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.165-7.167, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=165. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.46, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=46. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.158, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=158. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.24, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=24. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.26, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=26. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 13.43, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=13:chapter=43. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","italy"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/SicilyBC.svg/1280px-SicilyBC.svg.png","title":"Sicily before the invasion","desc":"Greek foundations in red, Phoenician and Punic ones in purple, Sicel and Elymian towns in white. Hamilcar put in at Panormus in the Punic north-west and marched east along the coast to Himera. The place names are in the mapmaker's German forms.","credit":"Map by Uwe Dedering, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SicilyBC.svg","alt":"A relief map of Sicily with coloured dots and labels round the coast, red for Greek towns in the south and east, purple for Punic ones in the west, white for inland settlements."},"why":[{"q":"Why are the two accounts of the invasion's cause hard to reconcile?","a":"Because Herodotus gives a purely Sicilian cause in Terillus, thrown out of Himera and bringing Hamilcar in, while Diodorus makes it half of a plan agreed with Xerxes."},{"q":"Why does the silence of the earlier source count against the wider plan?","a":"Because Herodotus reports both the Sicilian and the Carthaginian accounts of the campaign and mentions no such agreement with Persia."},{"q":"Why did Carthage keep watch every night after the defeat?","a":"Because it believed Gelon would sail against the city, and went into public mourning with its houses full of lamentation for the missing."}]},
{"id":"gr-449","num":449,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Raised where the enemy had turned and run, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a claim as much as a memorial, and after Sybota both sides put one up and each counted itself the winner.","questions":["Diodorus says the Greeks laid down that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> be made of whatever wood came to hand rather than of stone, so that a memorial of enmity would soon disappear.","What was meant to last was not the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the field but the tenth of the spoil sent to Delphi, Olympia and the Isthmus in bronze and gold."],"answer":"tropaion","answerText":"tropaion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Plataea tithe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">479 BCE, to three sanctuaries</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Olympia figure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Zeus of 10 cubits</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Isthmus figure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Poseidon of 7 cubits</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"A <b>tropaion</b> was the marker a Greek army raised on the ground where the enemy had turned and run, and the word behind the English trophy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides records the practice constantly, as when the Athenians at Naupactus set one up on the spot they had put out from and turned the day, giving the enemy dead back under truce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a claim as much as a memorial, and could be contested: after Sybota the Corinthians raised one on the mainland and the Corcyraeans another on the island, each side counting itself the victor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Naupactus the beaten side raised one too, for the ships it had disabled inshore, and moored a captured vessel beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diodorus reports a rule behind the custom: the Greeks laid down that such markers be made of whatever wood came to hand and not of stone, so that memorials of enmity should last a short time and disappear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What was meant to last went elsewhere, to the panhellenic sanctuaries, and was paid for out of a tenth of the spoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> From the tithe of Plataea the Greeks made a gold tripod on a bronze serpent for the god at Delphi, a bronze Zeus ten cubits high for Olympia, and a Poseidon of seven cubits for the Isthmus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens had done the same after Marathon, and Pausanias saw the group at Delphi that its tithe had paid for, with Athena, Apollo and the general Miltiades among the tribal heroes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He adds that the Athenians later put statues of Antigonus, Demetrius and Ptolemy in the same place, the Egyptian out of goodwill and the Macedonians out of the dread they inspired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two habits pull against each other: a wooden marker on the field that was left to rot, and bronze and gold in a sanctuary meant to be read for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.54, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=54. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.92, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=92. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 13.24, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=13:chapter=24. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.81, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=81. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 10.10, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=10:chapter=10. [Open access]"],"tags":["object","history","warfare","greece","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Trophee_bataille_marathon.JPG/1280px-Trophee_bataille_marathon.JPG","title":"The trophy on the plain of Marathon","desc":"A restored Ionic column standing where the Athenians raised a trophy after the battle of 490 BCE. Surviving ancient pieces of the monument are held in museum collections. A trophy in permanent marble is the exception rather than the rule.","credit":"Photograph by karta24, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trophee_bataille_marathon.JPG","alt":"A tall slender white marble column with a scrolled Ionic capital, standing alone on a stepped base against a clear blue sky, with a line of dark cypresses behind it."},"why":[{"q":"Why were these markers made of wood rather than stone?","a":"Because Diodorus reports a rule that they be made of whatever wood came to hand, so that memorials of enmity should last a short time and disappear."},{"q":"Why could two of them stand for one battle?","a":"Because raising one was a claim to victory as much as a memorial, so after Sybota the Corinthians raised one on the mainland and the Corcyraeans another on the island."},{"q":"Why do the two habits of commemoration pull against each other?","a":"Because a wooden marker on the field was left to rot, while the tithe of the spoil went to the panhellenic sanctuaries in bronze and gold, meant to be read for centuries."}]},
{"id":"gr-450","num":450,"category":"Persian Wars","question":"Thucydides noted that Homer never calls anyone a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and thought it was because the Greeks had not yet been marked off from the rest of the world under one name.","questions":["In Herodotus the Athenians refuse Persian terms by naming their kinship in blood and speech and their shared shrines, everything the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was taken not to have.","The force the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gained is caught by Aeschylus, whose Persian queen asks who is master of the Athenians and is told that they are the slaves of no man."],"answer":"barbarian","answerText":"barbarian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Delian League</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, treasury on Delos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Persians staged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">472 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian claim</span><span class=\"dt-v\">432 BCE, envoys at Sparta</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"abstract":"<b>Barbarian</b> renders the Greek <i>barbaros</i>, and in the earliest verse it is a word about speech rather than about character. Homer uses the related adjective once, in the catalogue of ships, of the Carians about Miletus and the Maeander, whom one translator calls uncouth of speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides noticed the absence and drew a conclusion: Homer nowhere calls the Greeks by one name and does not use the word at all, probably because they had not yet been marked off from the rest of the world under one appellation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After 480 BCE it carried a great deal more, and Aeschylus shows the freight eight years later, when a Persian queen asks who is set over the Athenians as shepherd and master and the chorus answers that they are called the slaves or subjects of no man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The antithesis that answer draws, free Greeks against a subject other, was the war's most durable export, and it was drawn by the side that won. <br><br> Herodotus puts a statement of Greekness in the mouths of Athenians refusing Persian terms: kinship of all Greeks in blood and speech, shrines and sacrifices held in common, and a like way of life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is spoken as a promise not to change sides rather than offered as a definition, which is how most of the war's thinking about Greekness survives. The political legacy went to Athens: the allies' contributions were fixed against the King, the professed object was to ravage his country, and the common treasury was placed on Delos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 432 BCE Athenian envoys at Sparta were saying they were tired of bringing the subject forward, and then bringing it forward: at Marathon they had faced the enemy alone, and at Salamis had gone aboard with all their people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Herodotus knew the claim was contested and made it anyway, prefacing his judgement that the Athenians had saved Greece with a warning that it would displease most of his readers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 2.867, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, Tufts University, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0134:book=2:card=867. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.3, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=3. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Persians</i> 241-242, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0012:card=242. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.144, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=144. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.73, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=73. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.139, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=139. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Greek-Persian_duel_1.jpg/1280px-Greek-Persian_duel_1.jpg","title":"A Greek and a Persian on a cup","desc":"Attic red-figure kylix of the 5th century BCE. The Greek fights naked but for helmet and shield, at close quarters; the Persian wears a patterned sleeved suit and a soft cap and draws a bow. The pairing is the antithesis Aeschylus puts into words.","credit":"Reproduction of an Attic kylix, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek-Persian_duel_1.jpg","alt":"The round interior of a Greek cup with a meander border, painted in orange on black: a helmeted nude warrior with a shield bearing a winged horse strides over a bearded archer in patterned trousers and a floppy cap who is drawing a bow."},"quote":{"book":"thucydides-peloponnesian-war","n":"1","text":"<p>The best proof of this is furnished by Homer. Born long after the Trojan War, he nowhere calls all of them by that name, nor indeed any of them except the followers of Achilles from Phthiotis, who were the original Hellenes: in his poems they are called Danaans, Argives, and Achaeans. He does not even use the term barbarian, probably because the Hellenes had not yet been marked off from the rest of the world by one distinctive appellation.</p>","cite":"Book 1.3"},"why":[{"q":"Why is the word about speech in the earliest verse?","a":"Because Homer uses the related adjective once, of the Carians about Miletus, whom one translator calls uncouth of speech, and does not use the noun at all."},{"q":"Why does Thucydides read that absence as significant?","a":"Because Homer nowhere calls the Greeks by one name either, probably because they had not yet been marked off from the rest of the world under one appellation."},{"q":"Why is the statement of Greekness in Herodotus not a definition?","a":"Because it is spoken by Athenians refusing Persian terms, as a promise not to change sides, which is how most of the war's thinking about Greekness survives."}]},
{"id":"gr-451","num":451,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The alliance now called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was formed when the Ionians asked the Athenians to lead in place of a Spartan commander they had found violent after Mycale.","questions":["Thucydides says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as independent allies acting on a common congress, and dates its change to the siege of Naxos, the first member forced back in.","Mytilenean envoys at Olympia said they had joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to free the Greeks from the Mede, not to help Athens subjugate them."],"answer":"Delian League","answerText":"Delian League","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, after the Persian retreat</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First assessment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, archonship of Timosthenes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">460 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Delian League</b> is the modern name for the alliance the ancient writers describe without one, as the Athenians and their allies. It grew out of the war it was meant to continue: after Mycale the Greeks took the Samians, Chians, Lesbians and other islanders into alliance and bound them by oath not to desert it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Command then changed hands, because the Ionians and the newly freed found Pausanias violent and asked the Athenians, as kinsmen, to lead instead; Sparta recalled him and the successor it sent found the allies no longer willing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristides administered the oath, by which the members swore to have the same friends and enemies, and lumps of iron were let sink in the sea to ratify it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Athens then fixed which cities were to give money and which ships, the professed object being to ravage the King's country, and the first assessment came to 460 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides is careful about what it was at the start: independent allies acting on the resolutions of a common congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It did not stay that way, and he dates the change precisely, to the siege of Naxos, the first ally forced back into an alliance it had tried to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His explanation puts much of the blame on the members, most of whom preferred to pay their share in money rather than serve, so that Athens grew her navy on their contributions while a revolt found them with neither ships nor practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The allies told it differently, and the Mytileneans arguing at Olympia for a Spartan alliance said they had become allies of the Greeks for liberation from the Mede and not allies of Athens for the subjugation of the Greeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> By their account only Lesbos and Chios still sent their own contingents, independent and free in name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.95, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=95. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.97, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=97. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.99, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=99. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.10, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=10. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 9.106, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=9:chapter=106. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/DelianLeagueAthenianEmpire.jpg/1280px-DelianLeagueAthenianEmpire.jpg","title":"The two alliances","desc":"Members of the alliance in pink and the Peloponnesian League in green, as they stood on the eve of the Peloponnesian War, two generations after the founding. Delos, Naxos, Lesbos, Chios and Samos are all on it. From a school atlas of 1912.","credit":"Map from W. L. Westermann, The Story of the Ancient Nations, 1912, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DelianLeagueAthenianEmpire.jpg","alt":"A tinted historical map of the Aegean with the coasts and islands of the eastern rim washed pink, the Peloponnese green, and a key at the lower left naming the Delian League, the Peloponnesian League and neutral states."},"quote":{"book":"thucydides-peloponnesian-war","n":"1","text":"<p>The Athenians, having thus succeeded to the supremacy by the voluntary act of the allies through their hatred of Pausanias, fixed which cities were to contribute money against the barbarian, which ships; their professed object being to retaliate for their sufferings by ravaging the King's country. Now was the time that the office of \"Treasurers for Hellas\" was first instituted by the Athenians. These officers received the tribute, as the money contributed was called. The tribute was first fixed at four hundred and sixty talents. The common treasury was at Delos, and the congresses were held in the temple.</p>","cite":"Book 1.96"},"why":[{"q":"Why did the leadership pass from Sparta to Athens?","a":"Because the Ionians and the newly freed found Pausanias violent and asked the Athenians, as kinsmen, to lead instead, and the successor Sparta sent found the allies no longer willing."},{"q":"Why does Thucydides date the change of character to Naxos?","a":"Because it was the first ally forced back into an alliance it had tried to leave, where the members had begun as independent allies acting on the resolutions of a common congress."},{"q":"Why does he put much of the blame on the members themselves?","a":"Because most preferred to pay their share in money rather than serve, so Athens grew her navy on their contributions while a revolt found them with neither ships nor practice."}]},
{"id":"gr-452","num":452,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides says the office of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first instituted by the Athenians, and that the alliance kept its common chest at Delos with the congresses held in the temple there.","questions":["The board called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took in the allies' contributions, which were fixed against the King and were known as tribute, and it outlived the island the money was first kept on.","Athens created the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to receive the money of the alliance, and Plutarch says moving that money to Athens was called unjust but advantageous."],"answer":"Hellenotamiai","answerText":"Hellenotamiai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Treasury at Delos</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 478 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Island purified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">426 BCE, all graves removed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Still an office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">411 BCE, in the oligarchs' list</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Hellenotamiai</b>, the treasurers of the Greeks, were the officials who received the money of the alliance formed after the Persian invasions. Thucydides sets three facts side by side without comment: the allies' contributions were fixed against the King, the money was called tribute, and the office was first instituted by the Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is equally plain about where the fund sat, saying that the common treasury was at Delos and that the congresses were held in the temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island was the obvious choice, being the old gathering-ground of the Ionians and the neighbouring islanders, who came to the festival with their wives and children for games, dancing and song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its standing was such that a Persian admiral respected it: Datis anchored across the water at Rhenea, sent word that he would harm neither the birthplace of the two gods nor its people, and burnt three hundred talents of frankincense on the altar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Athenian care for the sanctuary was of a different kind: Peisistratus had purified as much of the island as could be seen from the temple, and in 426 BCE the Athenians purified the whole of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every grave was taken up, and it was forbidden thereafter to die or to give birth on Delos, both being done across the water on Rhenea; the Athenians then held the Delian games for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides took an argument as well as a purification from those graves, since above half held Carians, identified by the arms buried with them and by the manner of interment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The money did not stay, and Plutarch, citing Theophrastus, says that when moving it from Delos to Athens was debated, and the Samians themselves proposed it, Aristides said the thing was unjust but advantageous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The board outlived the island: the oligarchic constitution drawn up at Athens in 411 BCE still lists the treasurers of the Greeks among its magistrates, and bars those managing the funds from the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.104, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=104. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.8, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=8. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.97, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=97. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 30, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=30. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","title"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Delos%2C_overview_of_a_site.jpg/1280px-Delos%2C_overview_of_a_site.jpg","title":"The sanctuary on Delos","desc":"Foundations and scattered blocks on the terrace of the sanctuary of Apollo, where Thucydides places the alliance's common chest and the congresses of its members. Nothing above ground survives of the buildings the money was kept in.","credit":"Photograph by ftrc, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delos,_overview_of_a_site.jpg","alt":"A wide field of pale stone foundations, fallen blocks and low walls under a bright blue sky, with a dry hillside behind and a modern museum building in the distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the fund kept on that island?","a":"Because Delos was the old gathering-ground of the Ionians and the neighbouring islanders, who came to the festival with their wives and children for games, dancing and song."},{"q":"Why did the purification make it forbidden to die there?","a":"Because every grave was taken up in 426 BCE, and dying and giving birth were both moved across the water to Rhenea."},{"q":"Why does Aristides' answer about moving the money matter?","a":"Because he is reported saying the thing was unjust but advantageous, which states the alliance's turn in the words of the man who assessed it."}]},
{"id":"gr-453","num":453,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The money an ally paid into the common fund was called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Thucydides says most members preferred to pay their share rather than serve with ships and leave home.","questions":["Pericles answered the charge that Athens gilded herself with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by saying the allies gave neither horse nor ship nor hoplite, but money, which belonged to those who took it.","Athens drew 600 talents of silver a year in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War, when only the Chians, Lesbians and Corcyraeans still served with ships."],"answer":"phoros","answerText":"phoros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First fixed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, 460 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yearly average</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 talents in 431 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reserve</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 talents on the Acropolis</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Phoros</b> was the money paid into the common fund of the alliance led by Athens, and Thucydides says the word was simply the name given to the contributions when they were first fixed at 460 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A member could serve with ships instead of paying, but most preferred to commute the obligation, arranging to pay their share of the expense in money rather than leave home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides thinks that was the allies' own undoing, since Athens increased her navy on the funds they contributed while a revolt found them without resources or experience for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War only the Chians, the Lesbians and the Corcyraeans still furnished ships, and the rest sent infantry and money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The paying districts were Caria upon the sea with her Dorian neighbours, Ionia, the Hellespont, the Thracian towns and all the Cyclades except Melos and Thera.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Pericles told the assembly that year that an average revenue of 600 talents of silver came in yearly from the allies, and that 6,000 talents of coined silver still lay on the Acropolis out of 9,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Collecting it was no formality: in 428 BCE, needing money for the siege of Mytilene, they raised 200 talents from their own citizens for the first time and sent twelve ships to levy subsidies from the allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their commander Lysicles went up the country from Myus in Caria, across the plain of the Maeander, and was attacked and killed with many of his soldiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the money meant was argued in Athens itself, where Pericles' enemies said Hellas was manifestly subjected to tyranny when the city gilded and bedizened itself with her enforced contributions for the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His answer was that the allies furnished not a horse, not a ship, not a hoplite, but money simply, and that this belonged not to those who gave it but to those who took it, so long as they furnished what they took it for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.99, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=99. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.9, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=9. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.19, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=19. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","politics","institution"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG/1280px-EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG","title":"The first stele of the tribute lists","desc":"Fragments of the Athenian record of the allies' payments, set into plaster in the Epigraphical Museum. The stele stood 3.56 metres (11 feet 8 inches) high, survives in 149 pieces, and carried a year's entries at a time from 454 to 440 BCE, each city's name followed by its sum in acrophonic numerals.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-1.JPG","alt":"Broken slabs of inscribed white marble mounted in grey plaster, carrying close-set columns of Greek capitals with red inventory numbers painted on the plaster below."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides call paying money the allies' own undoing?","a":"Because Athens increased her navy on the funds they contributed, while a revolt found them without resources or experience for war, having commuted the obligation to serve."},{"q":"Why did Athens have to send ships to collect in 428 BCE?","a":"Because it needed money for the siege of Mytilene, and having raised 200 talents from its own citizens for the first time sent twelve ships to levy subsidies from the allies."},{"q":"Why did Pericles say the money belonged to those who took it?","a":"Because the allies furnished not a horse, not a ship, not a hoplite, but money simply, and so long as Athens furnished what she took it for the payment was discharged."}]},
{"id":"gr-454","num":454,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Made two years after Salamis, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told each ally which should give money and which ships, and the total first came to 460 talents.","questions":["Plutarch says the allies asked for the man who made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that he inspected their territories and revenues, and that he went out poor and came back poorer.","The allies praised the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a golden age, which Plutarch says was easier once the tariff had been doubled and then trebled within a short time."],"answer":"assessment of Aristides","answerText":"assessment of Aristides","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assessed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">478 BCE, archonship of Timosthenes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First total</span><span class=\"dt-v\">460 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">By 431 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 talents a year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later total</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,300 talents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>assessment of Aristides</b> was the first fixing of what each member of the alliance led by Athens should pay, and the Athenian Constitution dates it two years after Salamis, in the archonship of Timosthenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides gives the result in a clause: the cities were told which should give money and which ships, and the tribute was first fixed at 460 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch gives the method, saying the allies asked the Athenians for Aristides and commissioned him to inspect their territories and revenues and then to fix the sums according to each member's worth and ability to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His reputation is what the whole arrangement rested on, and Herodotus, who had no reason to flatter Athens, calls him the best and most just man in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch adds the detail that fixed the reputation: master for a time of the property of Hellas, he went out poor on the commission and came back poorer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The allies remembered the figure as a golden age, praising it the way men of old praised the age of Cronus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch is careful to say why they did, since the tariff was doubled and then trebled within a short time, and a thing is easier to praise once it has been replaced by something worse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pericles added nearly a third, and by the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War the yearly revenue from the allies averaged 600 talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> After his death, Plutarch says, the sum was enlarged little by little until it reached 1,300 talents, which he blames less on the length of the war than on those who raised it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same man is remembered for the opposite too, since Theophrastus reports that when moving the money from Delos to Athens against the terms was debated, Aristides said the thing was unjust but advantageous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 23, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=23. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 24, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Aristides</i> 25, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Aristides*.html. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 8.79, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=8:chapter=79. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","institution"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/BL_Papyrus_131-10v_Constitution_of_Athens.jpg/1280px-BL_Papyrus_131-10v_Constitution_of_Athens.jpg","title":"The papyrus that dates the assessment","desc":"A leaf of British Library Papyrus 131, which carries the Athenian Constitution, the work that puts the assessment two years after Salamis in the archonship of Timosthenes. The main hand runs across the sheet with a second, smaller hand down the left margin.","credit":"British Library Papyrus 131, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BL_Papyrus_131-10v_Constitution_of_Athens.jpg","alt":"A pale brown papyrus sheet with visible vertical fibres, carrying eight lines of large Greek letters written without word spacing, and a column of smaller writing down the left edge."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the allies ask for this man in particular?","a":"Because the arrangement rested on his reputation, and they commissioned him to inspect their territories and revenues and fix the sums according to each member's worth and ability to pay."},{"q":"Why did they later remember his figure as a golden age?","a":"Because the tariff was doubled and then trebled within a short time, and Plutarch is careful to say a thing is easier to praise once it has been replaced by something worse."},{"q":"Why is the same man remembered for an unjust act as well?","a":"Because Theophrastus reports that when moving the money from Delos to Athens against the terms was debated, he said the thing was unjust but advantageous."}]},
{"id":"gr-455","num":455,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Left with his father's unpaid fine and a name for drink, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the fences off his farms, kept a daily table for the poor and named one of his twin sons Lacedaemonius.","questions":["Sparta called in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for Athenian skill at sieges, then sent his men home alone out of suspicion, which ended the policy that Greece needed two leaders.","Pericles could not match the private wealth of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and made jury service a paid office instead, a counter-measure the Athenian Constitution reports plainly."],"answer":"Cimon","answerText":"Cimon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Father fined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 talents, died in prison</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent from Ithome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 462 BCE, alliance broken</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last command</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200 ships to Cyprus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 450 BCE, at Citium</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Cimon</b> led Athens while the alliance against Persia was becoming an empire, and was the son of Miltiades by Hegesipyle, a daughter of the Thracian king Olorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He began with nothing but the name: his father, condemned to a fine of 50 talents, died in prison, and the son was left with an unmarried sister, a reputation for drink and, one contemporary says, no literary education.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His politics are best read from what he did with his money, since he had an estate large enough for a tyrant and spent it in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He took the fences off his farms so anyone might take the fruit and kept a daily table for the poor, though the sources differ on how wide the invitation was, Plutarch making it general and Aristotle restricting it to his own deme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Young men in fine clothes went with him, ready to change coats with any shabby elder they met and to press small change into the hands of poor men of quality in the market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Abroad his line was that Greece had two leaders and needed both, and he named one of his twin sons Lacedaemonius.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That policy died at Ithome, where Sparta called Athens in for her skill at sieges, then took fright at the enterprising and revolutionary character of her allies and sent them home alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They knew the reason was suspicion rather than the one given, went away deeply offended, and on reaching home broke the alliance made against the Mede and swore to Argos instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Athens the answer to his generosity was public money: Pericles, whose own means could not match it, made jury service a paid office as a counter-measure to his rival's wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His last command was 200 ships to Cyprus, and he died besieging Citium, telling those about him to sail away at once and conceal the death, so that neither enemy nor allies knew whom they followed home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 19, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.102, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=102. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.112, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=112. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Kimon_statue.JPG/1280px-Kimon_statue.JPG","title":"The monument at Larnaca","desc":"A modern bust on the seafront at Larnaca, the Kition he was besieging when he died, inscribed by the city to Cimon the Athenian. No ancient portrait of him has been identified, so what stands for him is a memorial put up by the town he was attacking.","credit":"Photograph by Valantis Antoniades, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kimon_statue.JPG","alt":"A helmeted bust silhouetted on a tall white plinth against an orange sunset, with palm trees, flagpoles and a beach behind it and a ship on the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why is his politics best read from what he did with his money?","a":"Because he had an estate large enough for a tyrant and spent it in public, taking the fences off his farms and keeping a daily table for the poor."},{"q":"Why did his policy toward Sparta collapse at Ithome?","a":"Because Sparta called Athens in for her skill at sieges, then took fright at the enterprising character of her allies and sent them home alone, and they knew the reason was suspicion."},{"q":"Why did Athens answer his generosity with public pay?","a":"Because Pericles' own means could not match it, so he made jury service a paid office as a counter-measure to his rival's wealth."}]},
{"id":"gr-456","num":456,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides gives the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one line, saying the Athenians took the town on the Strymon from the Medes and enslaved its people under the command of Cimon.","questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Athens voted three stone herms, and Aeschines says the generals were forbidden to inscribe their names so the honour would read as the people's.","The Persian who held the town during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused terms, burnt his household and himself, and threw the city's silver into the river."],"answer":"siege of Eion","answerText":"siege of Eion","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 476 BCE, Cimon commanding</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Honour voted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 stone herms, naming no general</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>siege of Eion</b> was the first operation Thucydides records for the new alliance, and he gives it in one line: the Athenians besieged and took the town on the Strymon from the Medes and enslaved its people, under the command of Cimon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch supplies the method, which was to beat the garrison in the field, shut it in the town, and then drive out the Thracians upstream who had been supplying it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Persian commander refused terms he was offered, and the two accounts of what he then did agree on everything but his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus calls him Boges and says he heaped a pyre, killed his children, wife, concubines and servants and threw them on it, scattered the city's gold and silver from the walls into the river, and cast himself into the fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch calls him Butes and has him fire the town and destroy his family, his treasures and himself with it, which is why Cimon took the place and got little from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Herodotus adds the detail that makes the story worth telling, since Boges was the only man driven out by the Greeks whom Xerxes thought valiant, and the Persians praised him still in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What Athens gained was land rather than plunder, and Plutarch says the fertile country round the town was handed over for settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The honour voted was three stone herms in the Stoa of the Hermae, and Aeschines says the condition was that the generals might not inscribe their own names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The point of that condition, he tells a fourth-century jury, was that the honour should read as the people's rather than the commanders'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch says the omission fooled nobody and that his contemporaries counted the herms a surpassing honour to Cimon, greater than anything Themistocles or his own father had been given.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.107, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=107. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Aeschines, <i>Against Ctesiphon</i> 183, trans. Charles Darwin Adams, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0002:speech=3:section=183. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Three_marble_heads_of_Herme%2C_AGMA%2C_225288.jpg/1280px-Three_marble_heads_of_Herme%2C_AGMA%2C_225288.jpg","title":"Three herm heads from the Agora","desc":"Marble herm heads in the Ancient Agora Museum at Athens, the museum dating them to 510 to 500 BCE, about 425 BCE, and the 2nd century CE. The three herms voted for this campaign do not survive; these show the kind of monument they were.","credit":"Photograph by Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_marble_heads_of_Herme%2C_AGMA%2C_225288.jpg","alt":"Three bearded marble heads on stone blocks in a museum case, one weathered almost featureless, one crisply carved with ringlets and a long beard, with a bilingual label below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Athens gain land rather than plunder from the town?","a":"Because the Persian commander fired the place and destroyed his family, his treasures and himself with it, so what was handed over was the fertile country round it."},{"q":"Why does Herodotus tell the defender's death at length?","a":"Because he was the only man driven out by the Greeks whom Xerxes thought valiant, and the Persians praised him still in Herodotus's own day."},{"q":"Why were the generals forbidden to put their names on the herms?","a":"Because the honour was to read as the people's rather than the commanders', though Plutarch says the omission fooled nobody."}]},
{"id":"gr-457","num":457,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Pirates on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> invited Cimon to take their own town after an Amphictyonic judgement went against it, and he drove out the Dolopians and settled the island.","questions":["An oracle sent Athens for the bones of Theseus, and on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Cimon dug where an eagle was tearing a mound and found a coffin holding a very large man.","Thucydides says Athens enslaved the people of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and colonised it, where Plutarch says only that the Dolopians were driven out."],"answer":"Scyros","answerText":"Scyros","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 475 BCE, Cimon commanding</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bones brought home</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after about 400 years away</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Scyros</b> is an island in the Aegean that Athens took and settled early in the life of its alliance, and the sources do not agree about who was living on it or what became of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides says the population was Dolopian, that the Athenians enslaved it and colonised the island; Diodorus says Pelasgians and Dolopes, and has Cimon appoint a founder and portion out the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plutarch gives the pretext, which was piracy: the Dolopians farmed badly and robbed at sea, and had lately taken some Thessalian merchants who put in at Ctesium and thrown them into prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The merchants escaped and won their case before the Amphictyonic assembly, whereupon the town refused to pay and told the men who held the plunder to give it back themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those men wrote to Cimon in terror, inviting him to bring his fleet and take the place, which he did, driving out the Dolopians and, in Plutarch's phrase, making the Aegean a free sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What made the conquest popular at home was a body, since an oracle had told the Athenians to bring back the bones of Theseus, who had fled there and been killed by its king Lycomedes, though nobody knew the grave and the islanders would allow no search.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch says Cimon saw an eagle tearing at a mound, dug there, and found a coffin holding a man of extraordinary size with a bronze spear and a sword beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The relics came home in Cimon's own trireme, after an absence of about 400 years, and were met with processions and sacrifices as though the hero himself were returning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tomb was built in the middle of the city and became a refuge for runaway slaves and poor men afraid of the powerful, which is what Theseus had been in life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias reads the whole episode backwards from that, describing the campaign as Cimon avenging the death of Theseus and fetching his bones to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Theseus</i> 36, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.17.6, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=17:section=6. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.60, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=60. [Open access]"],"tags":["place","history","greece","warfare","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Skyros-chora.JPG/1280px-Skyros-chora.JPG","title":"The town under its rock","desc":"The main village of the island, its white houses running down from the fortified crag that has been the citadel here since antiquity. The Dolopians Cimon expelled held the island from a settlement on this side of it.","credit":"Photograph by Han borg, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyros-chora.JPG","alt":"A steep brown crag topped by fortification walls, with several hundred flat-roofed white houses spilling down its lower slopes, dry hills behind and scrub in the foreground."},"locator":{"name":"Scyros","at":[24.525,38.8725],"zoom":6},"why":[{"q":"Why did the island's own men invite the fleet in?","a":"Because the town refused to pay the Thessalian merchants' award and told the men holding the plunder to give it back themselves, so those men wrote to Cimon in terror."},{"q":"Why did the conquest become popular at Athens?","a":"Because an oracle had told the Athenians to bring back the bones of Theseus, and the relics came home in Cimon's own trireme after an absence of about 400 years."},{"q":"Why did his tomb become a refuge for slaves and the poor?","a":"Because it was built in the middle of the city and stood for what Theseus had been in life, a protector of men afraid of the powerful."}]},
{"id":"gr-458","num":458,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides reports the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one sentence, saying the Athenians beat the Medes by land and by sea on the same day and destroyed a Phoenician fleet of 200 ships.","questions":["Plutarch lays out his sources' disagreements about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than settling them, one giving the enemy 600 ships and another 350, under two different commanders.","In Diodorus' account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Cimon manned captured ships with his own men in Persian dress, was welcomed into the camp as a friend, and attacked it at night."],"answer":"Battle of the Eurymedon","answerText":"Battle of the Eurymedon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 466 BCE, land and sea in a day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">200, per Thucydides</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ships taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">340, per Diodorus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of the Eurymedon</b> was a double victory won in Pamphylia, and Thucydides reports it in one sentence: the Athenians beat the Medes by land and sea on the same day and destroyed a Phoenician fleet of 200 ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Cimon sailed with 200 triremes Themistocles had built for speed, widened and decked over to carry more hoplites into a boarding fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the way he brought the Greek city of Phaselis over, the Chians in his fleet shooting messages to their old friends inside on arrows, and it agreed to pay 10 talents and join him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beyond that the accounts stop agreeing, and Plutarch lays the disagreement out rather than settling it, one authority giving the fleet to Tithraustes and another to Ariomandes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That fleet was 600 ships to one of his sources and 350 to another, and he takes refuge in the 200 captured, however many there had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Persians put into the river to avoid a fight, came out when pressed, achieved nothing and ran their ships ashore; Cimon then landed his hoplites straight from the sea fight against a fresh and larger army and broke it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch's verdict is that he beat Salamis with an infantry battle and Plataea with a naval one, and that he then destroyed the 80 Phoenician ships that arrived too late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus tells the land action differently, with Cimon manning the captured ships with his own men in Persian dress and tiaras and being welcomed into the camp as a friend before falling on it at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In that version most of them never knew who was attacking and took it for a raid by the neighbouring Pisidians, and the general Pherendates was killed in his tent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus gives the largest figures, 340 ships and over 20,000 men taken, and quotes the epigram on the tithe dedicated at Athens, claiming no such deed by land and sea had been done since the sea divided Europe from Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.100, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=100. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.61, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=61. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.62, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=62. [Open access]"],"tags":["battle","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/K%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%C3%A7ay_at_Eurymedon_Bridge_2013_5045.jpg/1280px-K%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%C3%A7ay_at_Eurymedon_Bridge_2013_5045.jpg","title":"The river the battle is named for","desc":"The Koprucay, the ancient Eurymedon, in its gorge in the Taurus foothills of southern Turkey. The fighting was far downstream from here, where the river reaches the Pamphylian plain and the sea.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6pr%C3%BC%C3%A7ay_at_Eurymedon_Bridge_2013_5045.jpg","alt":"A blue-green river running between rocky wooded banks in a narrow valley, with a low stone parapet on a ledge at the left and forested ridges rising behind."},"locator":{"name":"Eurymedon","at":[31.1722,36.9389],"kind":"battle"},"why":[{"q":"Why were the ships rebuilt before the campaign?","a":"Because the triremes Themistocles had built for speed were widened and decked over to carry more hoplites into a boarding fight."},{"q":"Why does Plutarch refuse to settle the size of the enemy fleet?","a":"Because his authorities give 600 ships and 350 and name two different commanders, so he takes refuge in the 200 captured, however many there had been."},{"q":"Why does Diodorus tell the land battle as a deception?","a":"Because in his account the captured ships were manned by Greeks in Persian dress and welcomed into the camp, so most defenders never knew who was attacking."}]},
{"id":"gr-459","num":459,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides marks the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one clause as the first time an allied city was brought back into an agreement it had tried to leave, and says the rest followed.","questions":["Plutarch explains what led to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by Cimon's leniency, since he took money from allies who would not sail and ships from those who sent them without crews.","An exiled Themistocles was blown onto the Athenian squadron blockading the island during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and had to tell the ship's master who he was to get past."],"answer":"revolt of Naxos","answerText":"revolt of Naxos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 469 BCE, the first ally reduced</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>revolt of Naxos</b> is the moment Thucydides picks out as the one that changed what the alliance was, and he marks it in one clause: the island left, a war followed, and it came back after a siege.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made it worth marking was the precedent rather than the fighting, since this was the first time a member city was brought back into an agreement it had tried to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He adds that the rest followed in the order that circumstances prescribed, which is the whole later history of the alliance stated in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The island was worth holding: Aristagoras had described it to a Persian satrap a generation earlier as small but rich in money and slaves, with Paros, Andros and the other Cyclades depending on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Thucydides gives the general cause of defections plainly: arrears of tribute and ships and failure of service, with Athenians severe and exacting towards men unused to hard work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He also blames the members themselves, since most had arranged to pay their share in money rather than serve, so that Athens grew her fleet on their contributions while a revolt found them with neither ships nor practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch explains the same outcome by the opposite behaviour, saying Cimon used no compulsion at all and simply took the money from those who would not sail and the ships from those who sent them without crews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In that account the allies were caught with the bait of their own ease, became farmers and merchants instead of fighters, and were tributary subjects before they knew it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both explanations end in the same place, which is a fleet paid for by men who could no longer man one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The siege has an incidental witness, since Thucydides has the exiled Themistocles carried by a storm onto the Athenian squadron blockading the island, and forced to tell the ship's master who he was to get past it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.98, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=98. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.99, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=99. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.137, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=137. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.31, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=31. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Gate_of_Temple_of_Apollo%2C_Palatia_and_akropolis_of_Naxos_Town%2C_530_BC%2C_144162.jpg/1280px-Gate_of_Temple_of_Apollo%2C_Palatia_and_akropolis_of_Naxos_Town%2C_530_BC%2C_144162.jpg","title":"The island town through its temple gate","desc":"Naxos town seen between the jambs of the marble gateway on the islet of Palatia, begun for a temple of Apollo about 530 BCE and never finished. It was already an old ruin, and the island already rich, when the revolt was put down.","credit":"Photograph by Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_of_Temple_of_Apollo%2C_Palatia_and_akropolis_of_Naxos_Town%2C_530_BC%2C_144162.jpg","alt":"Two massive white marble uprights and lintel blocks framing a view across water to a hillside town of flat-roofed white houses under a walled citadel."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides mark this revolt rather than the fighting?","a":"Because it was the first time a member city was brought back into an agreement it had tried to leave, and the rest followed in the order that circumstances prescribed."},{"q":"Why do two writers explain the same outcome by opposite behaviour?","a":"Because Thucydides blames Athenian severity towards men unused to hard work, where Plutarch says Cimon used no compulsion and simply took money from those who would not sail."},{"q":"Why did the allies end up unable to resist?","a":"Because most had arranged to pay their share in money rather than serve, so Athens grew her fleet on their contributions and a revolt found them with neither ships nor practice."}]},
{"id":"gr-460","num":460,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a quarrel over trading places on the Thracian coast and a mine, and it ended with walls razed, ships handed over and the mainland given up.","questions":["Herodotus, who had seen the island's mines, puts its yearly revenue at 200 talents, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fought to keep from Athens.","During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the besieged appealed to Sparta to invade Attica, and an earthquake and a helot rising stopped the promised invasion from ever coming."],"answer":"revolt of Thasos","answerText":"revolt of Thasos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 465 BCE, over marts and a mine</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlers lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 at Drabescus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Surrendered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 3 years, walls razed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>revolt of Thasos</b> was a member leaving the alliance over its own property rather than over its dues, the quarrel being about the trading places on the Thracian coast opposite and about a mine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus had seen those mines himself, and says the workings the Phoenicians opened between Aenyra and Coenyra had eaten away a whole hill in the searching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He puts the island's revenue at about 80 talents a year from the gold workings on the mainland and 200 on average from mainland and mines together, 300 at the best, with no tax paid on crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That wealth had already cost it once, since Darius made the Thasians pull down their walls and bring their ships to Abdera on a report from their neighbours that they meant to rebel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens beat them at sea, Plutarch putting the captured ships at 33, and landed on the island to besiege the town.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> In the same season Athens sent 10,000 settlers of her own citizens and of the allies to the place on the Strymon called Nine Ways, and they were cut off in the interior at Drabescus by the assembled Thracians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Thasians appealed to Sparta to invade Attica, and Sparta promised to do it without telling Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The invasion never came, because an earthquake and the secession of the helots to Ithome gave Sparta a war of her own to fight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the third year of the siege the island took terms: it razed its walls, handed over its ships, paid what was demanded at once and tribute thereafter, and gave up its mainland possessions along with the mine, which is the settlement Darius had imposed a generation before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch adds that Cimon was then accused of taking a bribe from the king of Macedonia not to invade it, and acquitted, with Pericles among the accusers taking the floor only once and as a formality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.100, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=100. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.101, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=101. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.46, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=46. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 6.47, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=6:chapter=47. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/%CE%9F_%CE%9B%CE%B9%CE%BC%CE%AD%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%BA%CE%B1%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF_%CF%81%CF%89%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%8A%CE%BA%CF%8C_%CE%98%CE%AD%CE%B1%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF_%CE%BD%CE%B7%CF%83%CE%AF_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%98%CE%AC%CF%83%CE%BF%CF%85_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-%CE%9F_%CE%9B%CE%B9%CE%BC%CE%AD%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%BA%CE%B1%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF_%CF%81%CF%89%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%8A%CE%BA%CF%8C_%CE%98%CE%AD%CE%B1%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF_%CE%BD%CE%B7%CF%83%CE%AF_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%98%CE%AC%CF%83%CE%BF%CF%85_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The harbour town from the sea","desc":"Limenas on Thasos, the ancient city, seen from the water as a besieging fleet would have seen it. The pale curve on the wooded slope above is the theatre, of the Roman period; the walls the islanders were made to raze ran along this shore.","credit":"Photograph by Ioannis Houvardas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9F_%CE%9B%CE%B9%CE%BC%CE%AD%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%BA%CE%B1%CE%B9_%CF%84%CE%BF_%CF%81%CF%89%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%8A%CE%BA%CF%8C_%CE%98%CE%AD%CE%B1%CF%84%CF%81%CE%BF_%CF%83%CF%84%CE%BF_%CE%BD%CE%B7%CF%83%CE%AF_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%98%CE%AC%CF%83%CE%BF%CF%85_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A low waterfront town of white and red-roofed buildings along a shore, backed by a steep pine-covered hill with a pale semicircular scar of ruins part way up it, under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this member leave over property rather than dues?","a":"Because the quarrel was about the trading places on the Thracian coast opposite and about a mine, the island's revenue running to 200 talents on average from mainland and mines together."},{"q":"Why did the promised Spartan invasion never come?","a":"Because an earthquake and the secession of the helots to Ithome gave Sparta a war of her own to fight."},{"q":"Why does the settlement look familiar?","a":"Because razing the walls, handing over the ships and giving up the mainland possessions is the settlement Darius had imposed on the island a generation before."}]},
{"id":"gr-461","num":461,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The rising called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began when helots and perioikoi seceded to Ithome after an earthquake, and it ended ten years later with the rebels leaving the Peloponnese for good.","questions":["Pausanias explains the earthquake behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Poseidon's punishment for the ephors dragging suppliants from the altar at Taenarum and killing them.","Sparta called Athens in during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for her skill at sieges and then sent her home alone, which Diodorus calls the first cause of the estrangement between them."],"answer":"Third Messenian War","answerText":"Third Messenian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rising began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 464 BCE, after the earthquake</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dead at Sparta</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 years on Ithome</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Third Messenian War</b> is the modern name for the rising that followed the Spartan earthquake, which the ancient writers call the secession to Ithome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides says the helots went out, with the Thuriats and Aethaeans of the perioikoi, and adds that most of them were descended from the old Messenians enslaved in the famous war, so that all of them came to be called Messenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus makes the rising a calculation: more than 20,000 Lacedaemonians had died in the earthquake, and men who had stayed quiet from fear of Spartan power saw how few the survivors were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias gives the Messenian version, in which the cause is a sacrilege, since the ephors had dragged Spartan suppliants from the altar at Taenarum and killed them, and Poseidon razed the city for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In Diodorus the rebels first marched on Sparta expecting to find it undefended, then learned that Archidamus had the survivors drawn up in a body and went instead to fortify a stronghold in Messenia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Sparta called in her allies, and the Athenians came under Cimon because they were thought good at sieges, which is the summons that ended in their being sent home alone under suspicion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus says the Athenians took that dismissal as the first cause of the estrangement between the two states, from which they later filled all Greece with calamities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The men on Ithome held out for ten years, and gave up only on terms: they were to leave the Peloponnese under safe conduct and never set foot in it again, and anyone found there afterwards was to be the slave of whoever caught him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides adds that Sparta had an old Delphic oracle about letting go the suppliant of Zeus at Ithome, and Pausanias has the same oracle threatening punishment if they harmed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They went out with their wives and children and were taken in by Athens, which settled them at Naupactus, a place it had lately taken from the Ozolian Locrians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.101, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=101. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.102, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=102. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.103, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=103. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.63, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=63. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.64, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=64. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.24, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=4:chapter=24. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","politics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Ithome_ancient_walls_01.jpg/1280px-Ithome_ancient_walls_01.jpg","title":"Ancient walling on Ithome","desc":"Courses of ancient masonry along the eastern summit of the mountain the rebels held, with the old Voulkano monastery on the crest beyond. The fortifications at Messene below are of the 4th century BCE, so how much of what stands here belongs to the siege is not settled.","credit":"Photograph by Mark Landon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ithome_ancient_walls_01.jpg","alt":"A line of large squared limestone blocks in two or three courses running along a bare rocky ridge, with scrub in front and a small tiled stone monastery on the skyline to the left."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the rising break out when it did?","a":"Because Diodorus makes it a calculation: more than 20,000 Lacedaemonians had died in the earthquake, and men who had stayed quiet from fear of Spartan power saw how few the survivors were."},{"q":"Why were the besieged allowed to walk away after ten years?","a":"Because Sparta had an old Delphic oracle about letting go the suppliant of Zeus at Ithome, so they left the Peloponnese under safe conduct never to return."},{"q":"Why does the Messenian account give a different cause?","a":"Because in Pausanias the ephors had dragged suppliants from the altar at Taenarum and killed them, and Poseidon razed the city for that sacrilege."}]},
{"id":"gr-462","num":462,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left five houses standing, tore peaks from Taygetus, and killed the young men in a colonnade who did not chase a hare outside.","questions":["Thucydides reports that the Spartans believed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was retribution for taking helot suppliants from the temple of Poseidon at Taenarum and killing them.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Archidamus had the alarm sounded so his people gathered under arms, which is what met the helots coming in to finish the survivors."],"answer":"Spartan earthquake","answerText":"Spartan earthquake","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Struck</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 464 BCE, Archidamus 4th year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000, per Diodorus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Houses left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, per Plutarch</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Spartan earthquake</b> struck in the fourth year of the reign of Archidamus, and Plutarch says it was greater than any before reported.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In his account it tore the Lacedaemonian land into chasms, shook Taygetus until peaks broke away, and left five houses standing in the whole city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus puts the dead above 20,000 and describes houses coming down over a long period as the ground kept moving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch preserves one detail that fixes the moment: young men and boys were exercising in a colonnade when a hare ran through, the boys chased it out oiled as they were, the older ones stayed, and the building fell on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their common tomb was still called Seismatias, the quake tomb, in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two accounts of what saved the survivors differ in method and agree in effect, Diodorus having Archidamus seize his armour and lead the citizens out of the city, Plutarch having him sound the trumpet as though an enemy were coming so that they would gather to him armed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Plutarch that is what preserved Sparta, since the helots came in from the country to finish the survivors and found them drawn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Greeks looked for a reason and found a sacrilege, though they disagreed about whose: Thucydides says the Spartans had taken helot suppliants from the temple of Poseidon at Taenarum and killed them, and believed the shock a retribution for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias reports a Messenian version in which the men dragged from the altar were Lacedaemonians condemned to death, and Poseidon razed the city for the offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The charge kept its edge, and a generation later the Athenians answered a Spartan demand about an old curse of their own by telling Sparta to drive out the curse of Taenarum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.63, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=63. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.128, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=128. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.24, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=4:chapter=24. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.101, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=101. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","religion","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/View_of_Mount_Taygetus_from_the_ruins_of_the_ancient_theatre_of_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg/1280px-View_of_Mount_Taygetus_from_the_ruins_of_the_ancient_theatre_of_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","title":"The city and the mountain","desc":"A fallen column drum and seat blocks in the grass at the ancient theatre of Sparta, with snow still on Taygetus behind. The standing remains here are later than the shock, which Plutarch says tore peaks from that range.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Mount_Taygetus_from_the_ruins_of_the_ancient_theatre_of_Sparta_on_15_May_2019.jpg","alt":"Long grass and olive trees over toppled grey stone blocks and a fluted column drum, with a high snow-streaked mountain range in the distance under a pale sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the boys survive when the older youths did not?","a":"Because a hare ran through the colonnade and the boys chased it out oiled as they were, while the older ones stayed and the building fell on them."},{"q":"Why did Sparta survive the helots who came in after the shock?","a":"Because the king had the survivors gathered under arms — leading them out of the city, or sounding the trumpet as though an enemy were coming — so the helots found them drawn up."},{"q":"Why did the Greeks look for a sacrilege behind it?","a":"Because they took the shock for retribution, though they disagreed whose: Thucydides says helot suppliants were killed at Taenarum, Pausanias that the men dragged from the altar were Lacedaemonians."}]},
{"id":"gr-463","num":463,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides says the first open quarrel between Athens and Sparta arose from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when the Spartans grew afraid of what their allies might attempt with the besieged.","questions":["Cimon carried the vote to help Sparta by saying Hellas should not be crippled, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the answer his city got for it.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Athens broke off the alliance made against the Mede and swore the same oaths to Sparta's enemy Argos instead."],"answer":"dismissal from Ithome","answerText":"dismissal from Ithome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Athens sent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 462 BCE, for skill at sieges</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cimon ostracised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">for 10 years, per Plutarch</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>dismissal from Ithome</b> was the moment Athens and Sparta stopped being allies, and Thucydides says plainly that the first open quarrel between them arose out of this expedition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sparta had asked for the Athenians because a long siege had shown her that she could not take the place by assault and they were reputed skilled at such work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sending them was argued about at Athens, where Ephialtes urged the people not to restore a rival city but to let haughty Sparta lie and be trodden underfoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says Cimon carried the vote with the phrase that Hellas should not be crippled nor Athens robbed of its yoke-fellow, and that the Spartan envoy Pericleidas was still remembered in comedy sitting at the altars in his purple cloak, pale, asking for an army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias notes what made Cimon the natural man to send, since he was Sparta's proxenos in Athens and her patron there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When the assault still failed, Thucydides says the Lacedaemonians grew afraid of the enterprising and revolutionary character of the Athenians, and of what men they thought of alien stock might attempt with the besieged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They dismissed them alone of all the allies without stating the suspicion, saying only that they had no further need of them, and Diodorus has them claim the other allies were sufficient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Athenians understood the reason well enough, went away deeply offended, and on reaching home broke off the alliance made against the Mede and swore the same oaths to Sparta's enemy Argos instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has two expeditions rather than one, an earlier that gave real help and a later that was sent off as a gathering of dangerous conspirators.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his account the returning army turned on the men who had argued for going, and Cimon was ostracised for ten years on a trifling pretext.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.102, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=102. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.64, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=64. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 4.24, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=4:chapter=24. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Messene%2C_Stadion%2C_Blick_nach_N%2C_Ithome_2015-09.jpg/1280px-Messene%2C_Stadion%2C_Blick_nach_N%2C_Ithome_2015-09.jpg","title":"Mount Ithome above the stadium of ancient Messene","desc":"The mountain the helots held against Sparta, seen from the north end of the stadium at Messene. The city below it was founded in 369 BCE, nearly a century after the siege the Athenians were sent to help with and then dismissed from.","credit":"Photograph by HerrAdams, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messene,_Stadion,_Blick_nach_N,_Ithome_2015-09.jpg","alt":"A long stone stadium with rows of seating, and beyond it a broad wooded mountain rising under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Athens asked for in the first place?","a":"Because a long siege had shown Sparta that she could not take the place by assault, and the Athenians were reputed skilled at such work."},{"q":"Why were they sent away without being told the reason?","a":"Because the Lacedaemonians had grown afraid of their enterprising and revolutionary character, and of what men they thought of alien stock might attempt with the besieged."},{"q":"Why did the dismissal end the alliance rather than strain it?","a":"Because the Athenians understood the reason well enough, went home deeply offended, broke off the alliance made against the Mede and swore the same oaths to Argos instead."}]},
{"id":"gr-464","num":464,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Plutarch says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was still in force when the exile came in arms to Tanagra to fight with his own tribe, but the generals were forbidden to receive him.","questions":["Plutarch says the charge behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was that the man loved Sparta and hated the people, while Cornelius Nepos names no charge and calls it the odium his father had met.","After the defeat at Tanagra Athens wanted back the man the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had sent away, and Plutarch says Pericles wrote the decree of recall out with his own hand."],"answer":"ostracism of Cimon","answerText":"ostracism of Cimon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ostracised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 461 BCE, for ten years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recalled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 457 BCE, after Tanagra</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>ostracism of Cimon</b> is the vote, around 461 BCE, that sent the foremost man in Athens away for ten years and left the field to Ephialtes and Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The campaign opened with a trial, not a vote: after taking Thasos he was accused of taking a bribe not to invade Macedonia, and Pericles was his most ardent accuser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle remembers that prosecution as the moment Pericles first made his name, challenging a serving general's audits while still young, and Plutarch says he spoke only once, as a formality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cimon was acquitted, but in the archonship of Conon the Council of the Areopagus was stripped of all but a few of its jurisdictions, which Plutarch places during an absence of his on service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On his return, Plutarch says, he tried to recall its jurisdiction and revive the aristocracy of Cleisthenes' day, whereupon his enemies banded together, renewed the slanders about his sister and called him Sparta's friend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What finished him was Ithome, where Sparta sent the Athenians home alone: they came back in a rage, moved against the laconisers and, on a trifling pretext, ostracised him for the decreed ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch's Pericles gives the charge outright, that he loved Sparta and hated the people, where Cornelius Nepos names none and says only that he met the odium his father had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The ten years were not seen out: when Sparta invaded Tanagra he came in arms to his own tribe, and Plutarch's two Lives disagree over who drove him off, the Council of Five Hundred or the friends of Pericles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He asked the comrades charged with laconising to clear themselves by fighting well; they set his armour in their midst and fell, a hundred by one account and all of them by the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beaten at Tanagra and expecting a Peloponnesian army in spring, Athens recalled him on a decree Pericles wrote himself, though Plutarch also reports a compact brokered by Elpinice dividing command abroad from power at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 14–15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 9, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Cornelius Nepos, <i>Cimon</i> 3, in <i>Justin, Cornelius Nepos, and Eutropius</i>, trans. John Selby Watson (London: Henry G. Bohn, 1853), 331–32, https://archive.org/details/justincorneliusn00watsuoft. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/1235_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Ostrakon_against_Kimon%2C_son_of_Milthiades_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Ort.jpg/1280px-1235_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Ostrakon_against_Kimon%2C_son_of_Milthiades_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Ort.jpg","title":"An ostrakon cast against Cimon","desc":"A sherd of black-glazed pottery scratched in two lines, KIMON MILTIADO, found in the bed of the Eridanos and now in the Kerameikos Archaeological Museum in Athens. Sherds like it were the ballots of an ostracism, and the one above it carries the end of a different name.","credit":"Photograph by Giovanni Dall'Orto, Attribution licence. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1235_-_Keramikos_Museum%2C_Athens_-_Ostrakon_against_Kimon%2C_son_of_Milthiades_-_Photo_by_Giovanni_Dall%27Ort.jpg","alt":"A curved fragment of black-glazed pottery lying on a white surface, with Greek letters scratched through the glaze in two lines to show the red clay beneath."},"why":[{"q":"Why did an acquittal not save him?","a":"Because the bribery trial was only the opening: the Areopagus was stripped of its jurisdictions during an absence of his, and on his return his enemies banded together and renewed the old slanders."},{"q":"Why did Ithome finish him?","a":"Because the army came back in a rage at being sent home alone, moved against the laconisers, and on a trifling pretext ostracised the man who had argued for going."},{"q":"Why was he recalled before the ten years were out?","a":"Because Athens was beaten at Tanagra and expecting a Peloponnesian army in spring, so Pericles wrote the recall decree himself."}]},
{"id":"gr-465","num":465,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first prosecuted many Areopagites over their conduct in office, then in the archonship of Conon stripped their council of the powers that made it the constitution's safeguard.","questions":["Plutarch names only Aristides and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as keeping their hands clean of public money, and says the second of them tried to dethrone the Council of the Areopagus.","Aristotle names Aristodicus of Tanagra as the man who craftily murdered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Diodorus says only that he was done to death by night and nobody ever knew how."],"answer":"Ephialtes","answerText":"Ephialtes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reform</span><span class=\"dt-v\">462 BCE, archonship of Conon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 461 BCE, by Aristodicus</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Ephialtes</b> son of Sophonides was the Athenian who broke the political power of the Council of the Areopagus, and whose few recorded years carried the city into a radical democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle says the constitution had stayed under the leadership of the Areopagites for about seventeen years after the Persian War, being gradually modified, until Ephialtes became head of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had the name of being incorruptible and just, and Plutarch lists him with Aristides alone among the politicians of the day as keeping his hands off the gains of public office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He first made away with many Areopagites by prosecuting them over their acts of administration, then in the archonship of Conon stripped the council of the added powers that made it the constitution's safeguard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he took he distributed, some to the Council of Five Hundred and the rest to the people and the jury-courts, and elsewhere Aristotle says that Ephialtes and Pericles together docked the Areopagus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He was no mere speaker: Callisthenes, quoted by Plutarch, has him take thirty ships past the Chelidonian isles, where Pericles took fifty, to show how far the Persians now kept away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At home he opposed Cimon, and when Sparta asked for help at Ithome he urged the Athenians not to restore a rival city but to let haughty Sparta lie and be trodden underfoot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plutarch's Pericles makes him one of the friends Pericles commissioned to carry out his policy, and quotes Plato against the result, that it poured out for the citizens too much undiluted freedom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> He was killed not long after: Aristotle says he was craftily murdered by Aristodicus of Tanagra, where Diodorus, calling the reform lawless, says only that he was done to death by night and nobody ever knew how.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Plutarch reports and rejects Idomeneus' charge that Pericles had his own friend assassinated out of envy, and Aristotle counts forward from the death, dating the archonship's opening to the zeugitai five years after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=26. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1274a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 10, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.77, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=77. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/View_of_the_Areopagus_from_the_Acropolis_in_Athens%2C_20240531_1329_9684.jpg/1280px-View_of_the_Areopagus_from_the_Acropolis_in_Athens%2C_20240531_1329_9684.jpg","title":"The Areopagus seen from the Acropolis","desc":"The bare limestone outcrop below the Acropolis that gave the council its name, with modern Athens beyond. What Ephialtes took from it went to the Council of Five Hundred, the assembly and the jury-courts.","credit":"Photograph by Jakub Halun, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Areopagus_from_the_Acropolis_in_Athens%2C_20240531_1329_9684.jpg","alt":"A bare pale rock outcrop rising out of trees in the middle distance, with people standing on its top and a city spreading to hills beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why did he prosecute Areopagites before moving against the council?","a":"Because making away with many of its members over their acts of administration weakened it before the vote that stripped its added powers."},{"q":"Why did he oppose sending help to Sparta at Ithome?","a":"Because he urged the Athenians not to restore a rival city but to let haughty Sparta lie and be trodden underfoot."},{"q":"Why is the manner of his death unsettled?","a":"Because Aristotle says he was craftily murdered by Aristodicus of Tanagra, while Diodorus says only that he was done to death by night and nobody ever knew how."}]},
{"id":"gr-466","num":466,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle credits Themistocles with part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, telling the Areopagites that men were conspiring against the constitution and telling Ephialtes that the council meant to arrest him.","questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the powers that had made a council of ex-archons the safeguard of the constitution sat instead with the Five Hundred, the assembly and the jury-courts.","Aristotle's Politics blames the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the pay Pericles later gave jurors for the stages by which Athens reached the democracy of his own day."],"answer":"reform of the Areopagus","answerText":"reform of the Areopagus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">462 BCE, archonship of Conon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ephialtes killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 461 BCE, soon afterwards</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>reform of the Areopagus</b> was the vote at Athens, in the archonship of Conon, that took from the council of former archons the powers which had made it the safeguard of the constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle says the constitution had run under the leadership of the Areopagites for about seventeen years after the Persian War, being gradually modified as the population grew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attack came in two stages: Ephialtes first made away with many Areopagites by prosecuting them over their acts of administration, and only then moved on the council itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was taken was distributed rather than abolished, some of it to the Council of Five Hundred, some to the people and some to the jury-courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle credits Themistocles with part of it, having him warn the Areopagites that men were conspiring against the constitution while telling Ephialtes the council meant to arrest him, until the two denounced the Areopagites in Council and assembly alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch sets it during an absence of Cimon's on service, when the populace confounded the established order and plunged the city into unmitigated democracy, Pericles being now a man of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The date is not agreed: Aristotle names the archon Conon, where Diodorus files the vote under Phrasicleides and calls it the destruction of renowned customs the fathers had followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The council that survived is the one Aeschylus put on stage, where Athena founds a tribunal on the hill of Ares to judge the first trial for bloodshed and tells Attica to keep neither anarchy nor tyranny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ephialtes was killed not long afterwards, and five years after his death the roll from which the nine archons were drawn by lot was opened to the zeugitai, the first of them to hold office being Mnesitheides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's Politics puts the vote, with the jury pay Pericles later introduced, among the stages by which the leaders of the people brought Athens to the democracy of his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 25, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=25. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=26. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=2:section=1274a. [Open access]","Aeschylus, <i>Eumenides</i> 681–710, trans. Herbert Weir Smyth, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0006:card=681. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.77, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=77. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg/1280px-Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg","title":"A kleroterion, the allotment machine of the Athenian courts","desc":"A marble slab cut with rows of slots, in the Ancient Agora Museum at Athens, into which citizens' bronze tickets were set so that jurors could be drawn by lot. Part of what the vote took from the Areopagus went to the courts these men sat in.","credit":"Photograph by Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleroterion%2C_AGMA%2C_225287.jpg","alt":"A rough-edged white marble slab standing in a museum case, its face cut with about twenty vertical columns of narrow horizontal slots."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this called a redistribution rather than an abolition?","a":"Because what was taken went to the Council of Five Hundred, to the people and to the jury-courts, and the council itself survived with the gravest homicide trials."},{"q":"Why does Aristotle bring Themistocles into the story?","a":"Because he has him warn the Areopagites that men were conspiring against the constitution while telling Ephialtes the council meant to arrest him, until the two denounced them together."},{"q":"Why do the sources file the vote under different archons?","a":"Because Aristotle names Conon where Diodorus files it under Phrasicleides, calling it the destruction of renowned customs the fathers had followed."}]},
{"id":"gr-467","quote":{"book":"thucydides-peloponnesian-war","n":"2","cite":"Book 2.65","text":"<p>Pericles indeed, by his rank, ability, and known integrity, was enabled to exercise an independent control over the multitude--in short, to lead them instead of being led by them; for as he never sought power by improper means, he was never compelled to flatter them, but, on the contrary, enjoyed so high an estimation that he could afford to anger them by contradiction. … In short, what was nominally a democracy became in his hands government by the first citizen.</p>"},"num":467,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"For forty years in the middle of the 5th century BCE, and for fifteen of them in unbroken command as general, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first man at Athens.","questions":["Thucydides writes that under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> what was nominally a democracy became government by the first citizen, since his rank, ability and known integrity let him lead the multitude rather than be led.","Plutarch says the head of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was long and out of proportion, which is why almost every statue of him wears a helmet, and why the comic poets called him Squill-head."],"answer":"Pericles","answerText":"Pericles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 495 – 429 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foremost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 years, 15 in unbroken command</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"<b>Pericles</b> led Athens through the middle of the 5th century BCE, and Thucydides' judgement is that what was nominally a democracy became in his hands government by the first citizen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was of the foremost family on both sides: his father Xanthippus beat the King's generals at Mycale, and his mother Agariste was granddaughter of the Cleisthenes who expelled the tyrants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plutarch says she dreamt she had borne a lion days before bearing him, and that his one fault of appearance was a long head, which is why nearly all his statues wear a helmet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The comic poets called him Squill-head for it, and the surname Olympian came chiefly from his speaking, since they said he thundered and lightened and wielded a thunderbolt in his tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Damon taught him music and used the lyre, Plutarch says, as a screen for politics; he heard Zeno of Elea, but the man who did most for him was Anaxagoras, whom that generation called Nous, Mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> As a young man he kept out of politics, thought to look and sound like the tyrant Peisistratus and afraid of being ostracised, and came forward only when Aristides was dead, Themistocles banished and Cimon abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The choice went against a nature Plutarch calls anything but popular, and he changed his way of living with it: one street only, to the market-place and the council-chamber, and no dining out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What Anaxagoras taught him against superstition shows in the one-horned ram, where Lampon the seer read the horn as a sign of sole rule and Anaxagoras cut the skull open to show the brain drawn to a point at its root.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plutarch adds the sting: Anaxagoras had the applause that day and Lampon a little later, when Thucydides son of Melesias was overthrown and Pericles stood alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He stood first for forty years, fifteen in unbroken command through his annual generalship, and Thucydides says his rank, ability and known integrity let him lead the multitude rather than be led.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.65, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=65. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 3, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 4, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 6, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 7, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 8, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["person","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Pericles_Townley_BM_549.jpg/1280px-Pericles_Townley_BM_549.jpg","title":"The Townley bust of Pericles","desc":"A Roman marble herm in the British Museum, copied after a Greek original, with the name cut across the base. Every such portrait wears the helmet that hides the long head the comic poets made fun of.","credit":"Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pericles_Townley_BM_549.jpg","alt":"A white marble bust of a bearded man wearing a helmet pushed back on his head, on a squared base carved with Greek capitals."},"why":[{"q":"Why does Thucydides call the democracy government by the first citizen?","a":"Because his rank, ability and known integrity let him lead the multitude rather than be led by it, standing first for forty years."},{"q":"Why did he keep out of politics when he was young?","a":"Because he was thought to look and sound like the tyrant Peisistratus and was afraid of being ostracised, coming forward only when Aristides was dead, Themistocles banished and Cimon abroad."},{"q":"Why does Plutarch let the seer have the last word on the ram?","a":"Because although Anaxagoras cut the skull open and had the applause that day, Lampon's reading of sole rule was borne out a little later, when Thucydides son of Melesias was overthrown."}]},
{"id":"gr-468","num":468,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle's list of what makes a constitution democratic reads like a description of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, running to allotment, short terms, no property qualification and an assembly sovereign over everything.","questions":["The pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon attacks <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for letting the worst be better off than the good, then grants that it is the poor who man the ships.","Aristotle says that under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the people made itself master of everything and administered it all by decrees and by jury-courts, and defends that as safer than trusting a few."],"answer":"radical democracy","answerText":"radical democracy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 462 BCE at Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Counted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 7th of 11 constitutions</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Radical democracy</b> is the modern name for the Athenian system after 462 BCE, when the old council's powers passed to bodies open to every citizen; Plutarch's own phrase for it is unmitigated democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle's list of what makes a constitution democratic reads like a description of Athens: officials elected by all from all, and the government of each by all and of all by each in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds allotment to every office that does not need skill, no property qualification or a very low one, short terms, no repeated tenure, judicial power exercised by all citizens and an assembly sovereign over everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind the list he sets two ideas of liberty: equality counted by number rather than by worth, so that the majority decides, and living as one likes, since to live otherwise is a slave's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> locates the people's power in the courts, saying that with the power of the vote the people becomes sovereign in the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It counts eleven changes of constitution and makes this the seventh, outlined by Aristides and completed by Ephialtes when he put down the Areopagite council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Of the constitution as it finally stood it says the people made itself master of everything, administering all by decrees and by jury-courts, and defends that, since a few are more easily corrupted by gain and influence than the many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hostile contemporary account is the pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon, whose author opens by saying he does not think well of a constitution that lets the worst be better off than the good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He grants the case all the same, since it is the poor who man the ships and give the city its strength, steersmen and boatswains and look-outs and shipwrights more than the hoplites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> And he notices what the people do not claim: no allotted share in the generalships or the cavalry commands, which they leave to the most influential men, while keeping the offices that are salaried and profitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 15, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 9, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=9. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=41. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/AGMA_St%C3%A8le_de_la_d%C3%A9mocratie.jpg","title":"The stele of the Athenian law against tyranny","desc":"A bearded seated Demos crowned by a standing Demokratia, above the text of a law of 337 BCE, in the Ancient Agora Museum at Athens. The stone is more than a century later than the vote this card is about, and is the clearest picture the Athenians left of what they thought they had.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AGMA_St%C3%A8le_de_la_d%C3%A9mocratie.jpg","alt":"A tall marble stele with a pediment, carved at the top with a seated bearded man being crowned by a standing draped woman, and closely inscribed with Greek below."},"why":[{"q":"Why is allotment central to the system rather than incidental?","a":"Because Aristotle lists allotment to every office that does not need skill, with short terms and no repeated tenure, among the things that make a constitution democratic."},{"q":"Why does the hostile pamphlet grant the case it dislikes?","a":"Because its author sees that it is the poor who man the ships and give the city its strength — steersmen, boatswains, look-outs and shipwrights more than the hoplites."},{"q":"Why did the people leave some offices to the influential?","a":"Because they claimed no allotted share in the generalships or cavalry commands, keeping instead the offices that are salaried and profitable."}]},
{"id":"gr-469","num":469,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aeschines says a purifying sacrifice was carried round before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened, and that the herald then asked who above fifty years of age wished to speak first.","questions":["Aristophanes opens a comedy with one farmer alone on the Pnyx at daybreak, waiting for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while everyone else gossips in the marketplace and dodges the vermilion rope.","Aristotle says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> met four times in each presidency, and that the chief of those four confirmed the magistrates and dealt with the food supply and the defence of the country."],"answer":"ecclesia","answerText":"ecclesia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sovereign from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Met</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 40 times a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>ecclesia</b> was the assembly of the Athenian citizens, and Aristotle counts an assembly sovereign over all matters among the marks that make a constitution democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It met four times in each presidency of the council's year, on days and at a place the presidents posted in advance along with the business to be taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of the four was the sovereign meeting, which confirmed the magistrates in office if they were thought to govern well and dealt with the food supply and the defence of the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that meeting informations were laid by anyone who wished, and the inventories of estates being confiscated were read out with the lists of suits over inheritances and heiresses, so that all knew of any estate falling vacant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the sixth presidency it also voted on whether an ostracism should be held that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second meeting was given to petitions, at which anyone who wished might lay a suppliant-branch and speak to the people on any matter he liked, public or private.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other two took the rest, three items of sacred business, three audiences for heralds and embassies and three of secular business, though sometimes matters were taken without a preliminary vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeschines describes the opening: a purifying sacrifice carried round, the herald's traditional prayers, and then the call asking who above fifty years of age wished to address the assembly, after which any other entitled Athenian might.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon puts the same openness sourly, saying that as things are any wretch who wants to can stand up and obtain what is good for him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristophanes opens the <i>Acharnians</i> with a farmer alone on the Pnyx at daybreak while the rest gossip in the marketplace and dodge the vermilion rope, and the presidents arrive at midday shoving for the front seats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=43. [Open access]","Aeschines, <i>Against Timarchus</i> 23, trans. Charles Darwin Adams (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0002:speech=1:section=23. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Acharnians</i> 1–42, in <i>The Eleven Comedies</i>, anonymous translation (New York: Liveright), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0240:card=1. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/The_Pnyx_plateau_on_March_13%2C_2021.jpg/1280px-The_Pnyx_plateau_on_March_13%2C_2021.jpg","title":"The Pnyx, where the assembly met","desc":"The open terrace on the hill west of the Acropolis, with the cut scarp and the steps of the speaker's platform on the right. The citizens stood on this ground; the Parthenon is on the height across the trees.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pnyx_plateau_on_March_13%2C_2021.jpg","alt":"A wide bare terrace of pale ground on a hilltop, bounded on the right by a low cut rock face, with the Acropolis and Parthenon rising beyond trees on the left."},"why":[{"q":"Why was one of the four meetings called the sovereign one?","a":"Because it confirmed the magistrates in office if they were thought to govern well and dealt with the food supply and the defence of the country."},{"q":"Why were confiscation inventories read out at that meeting?","a":"Because the lists of suits over inheritances and heiresses were read with them, so that everyone should know of any estate falling vacant."},{"q":"Why does the herald ask first for men over fifty?","a":"Because after the purifying sacrifice and the traditional prayers the call went to the eldest, and only then might any other entitled Athenian speak."}]},
{"id":"gr-470","num":470,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was drawn by lot to preside for a day and a night, keeping the state seal and the keys of the temples where the public money lay.","questions":["The fifty men of one tribe served as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a tenth of the year, dining in the Round-house at public expense and calling the meetings of the council and the people.","Xenophon says some of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused to put an illegal question after Arginusae, and that all gave way in fear except Socrates son of Sophroniscus."],"answer":"prytaneis","answerText":"prytaneis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">From</span><span class=\"dt-v\">508/507 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Number</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50, for a tenth of a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>prytaneis</b> were the fifty councillors of one Athenian tribe who presided over the council of five hundred and the assembly for a tenth of the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each tribe took the presidency in turn, in an order settled by lot, the first four drawn holding it for 36 days and the last six for 35.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They dined together in the Round-house at public expense and called the meetings of both the council and the people, posting the business, the day's agenda and the place in writing beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They gave heralds and ambassadors their first hearing, and dispatches were delivered into their hands by the men who carried them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aeschines says it was the presiding officers who were commanded to declare the order of business, the matters of religion first, then the heralds and embassies, then the secular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One of them was drawn by lot as their head for a day and a night, could not hold it twice, kept the state seal and the keys of the temples holding the public money and documents, and slept in the Round-house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When a meeting was called he drew nine chairmen by lot, one from each of the other tribes, and handed them the agenda; they brought business forward, counted the votes and could close the sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A man could be head only once in a year though he might be one of the nine chairmen once in every presidency, and the board also conducted the elections of generals and cavalry commanders when the omens allowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Xenophon shows what presiding could cost when the assembly wanted to try the ten generals together after Arginusae: some of them refused to put an illegal question, and the crowd shouted for them to be summoned to court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They gave way in fear, all but Socrates son of Sophroniscus, who said he would act in no case except in accordance with the law, and Plato has Socrates tell the same story of himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=43. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 44, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=44. [Open access]","Aeschines, <i>Against Timarchus</i> 23, trans. Charles Darwin Adams (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1919), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0002:speech=1:section=23. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 1.7.14–15, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1918), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Hell.%201.7.14. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Apology</i> 32b, trans. Harold North Fowler, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1966), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.%20Apol.%2032b. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/%CE%98%CF%8C%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82_1149.jpg/1280px-%CE%98%CF%8C%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82_1149.jpg","title":"The Tholos, the Round-house of the Agora","desc":"The circular foundation of the building in which the presiding fifty dined at public expense, and in which their head and a third of the board stayed through the night.","credit":"Photograph by C messier, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%98%CF%8C%CE%BB%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%91%CE%B3%CE%BF%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82_1149.jpg","alt":"A low circular wall of rough masonry enclosing a floor of scattered paving slabs, on bare ground with trees behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the presidency rotate rather than being held for the year?","a":"Because each tribe took it in turn in an order settled by lot, the first four drawn holding it for 36 days and the last six for 35."},{"q":"Why did the daily head hold the seal and the temple keys?","a":"Because he kept the public money and documents, could not hold the post twice, and slept in the Round-house for the day and night of his tenure."},{"q":"Why is the trial after Arginusae the hardest case of presiding?","a":"Because some of the board refused to put an illegal question and gave way in fear when the crowd shouted, all but Socrates, who said he would act only in accordance with the law."}]},
{"id":"gr-471","num":471,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says a juror in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> voted with two bronze pebbles, one pierced and one whole, and pressed them against the lamp-stand so nobody could see which he held.","questions":["Any Athenian over thirty who was not in debt to the Treasury or disfranchised might sit in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, carrying a boxwood ticket cut with his name, his father's name and his deme.","Aristophanes gives an old juror boasting that no king's power exceeds his, since men on trial before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> flatter him, tell him fables and drag their children forward to whine."],"answer":"dikasteria","answerText":"dikasteria","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">From Solon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">594/593 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Central from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">462/461 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jurors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>dikasteria</b> were the jury-courts of Athens, and Aristotle counts judicial power exercised by all citizens on the greatest matters among the marks of a democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Any citizen over thirty might sit who was not in debt to the Treasury or disfranchised, and a man who sat without the right was tried by a jury which fixed his punishment itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each juror carried a boxwood ticket cut with his name, his father's name and his deme, and one letter of the alphabet as far as kappa, each tribe's jurors being divided into ten sections.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The courts took their own letters from lambda onwards, drawn by lot, and an attendant went straight out and fixed each court's letter up at its entrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At each of the ten entrances stood staves as many as the jurymen, and lettered acorns for the courts to be filled were thrown into an urn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A juror voted with two bronze pebbles, one pierced through and one whole, and the herald proclaimed that the pierced one counted for the first speaker and the whole one for the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taking them from the lamp-stand he pressed them against it so neither party could see which he held, then dropped the one he meant to count into a copper jar and the other into a wooden one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The copper jar's lid had a hole big enough for one pebble only, so nobody could put in two, and no man could claim his token, and with it his fee, unless he had voted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were emptied on to a board pierced with as many holes as there were votes, counted in two sets where the litigants could see, and the herald called the number; an equal vote acquitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aristophanes gives the other side, an old juror boasting that no king's power is greater than his, since the accused flatter him, tell him fables out of Aesop and drag their children forward to whine at his feet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 63, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=63. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 68, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=68. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 69, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=69. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 548–575, in <i>The Complete Greek Drama</i>, vol. 2, ed. Eugene O'Neill, Jr. (New York: Random House, 1938), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristoph.%20Wasps%20548. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]"],"tags":["institution","history","greece","politics","athens"],"why":[{"q":"Why did a juror carry two pebbles rather than one?","a":"Because one was pierced and one whole, counting for the first and second speaker, and pressing them against the lamp-stand kept either party from seeing which he was casting."},{"q":"Why did the copper jar have a hole for one pebble only?","a":"Because no juror could then put in two, and no man could claim his token, and with it his fee, unless he had voted."},{"q":"Why were the courts assigned by lot on the day?","a":"Because the letters from lambda onwards were drawn and posted at each entrance only then, so nobody could know in advance which court he would sit in."}]},
{"id":"gr-472","num":472,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle says Pericles answered the wealth Cimon spent on the poor by making jury service the first office to carry <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on the advice of Damonides of Oea.","questions":["A drachma for an ordinary meeting of the assembly, half a drachma for the courts and five obols for the council were the rates of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Athenian Constitution sets out.","Aristophanes has a chorus of old jurors complain that their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must buy bread, wood and stew, and Philocleon's daughter fish the three obols from his mouth."],"answer":"misthos","answerText":"misthos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jury pay from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 450 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raised to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 obols, c. 425 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Misthos</b> was the pay an Athenian drew for serving the city, and Aristotle counts payment for public duties among the marks that make a constitution democratic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Athenian Constitution</i> says Pericles made service in the jury-courts a paid office as a popular counter-measure against Cimon's wealth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cimon had an estate large enough for a tyrant and spent it in public, and Pericles, whose own means could not match that, took the advice of Damonides of Oea and gave the multitude what was already their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reports the criticism too, that the courts deteriorated because it was always ordinary men rather than respectable ones who took care to cast lots for the duty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Aristotle's <i>Politics</i> makes a related point about the council: where pay is plentiful the people draw all the trials to itself and strip even that office of its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rates are set out plainly: a drachma for an ordinary meeting of the assembly and a drachma and a half for a sovereign one, half a drachma for the jury-courts, and five obols for the council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The presidents got an extra obol for food, the nine archons four obols each out of which they had to keep a herald and a flute-player, and the archon on Salamis a drachma a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pay for attending the assembly came last and was rejected the first time it was proposed, since men were not turning up and the presidents kept contriving devices to get a show of hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Agyrrhius then carried an obol, Heracleides of Clazomenae raised it to two, and Agyrrhius came back and made it three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Aristophanes gives the scale of it from below, a chorus of old jurors complaining that their small pay must buy bread, wood and stew, and Philocleon coming home to a daughter who kisses him and fishes the three obols out of his mouth with her tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 6.1317b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=6:section=1317b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 27, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=27. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 62, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=62. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 41, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=41. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Wasps</i> 291–300, 605–610, in <i>The Complete Greek Drama</i>, vol. 2, ed. Eugene O'Neill, Jr. (New York: Random House, 1938), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristoph.%20Wasps%20291. [Open access]"],"tags":["concept","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f7/Athens_-_450-425_BC_-_silver_obol_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg","title":"An Athenian silver obol","desc":"The owl, olive spray and first three letters of the city's name on the reverse of a coin about the size of a fingernail, in the Berlin cabinet. Six of these made a drachma, and three of them were a juror's day.","credit":"Photograph by ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_-_450-425_BC_-_silver_obol_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_Berlin_MK_AM.jpg","alt":"A small irregular silver coin held on two pins, stamped with a standing owl, an olive spray and a crescent, with three Greek letters at the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why was jury pay introduced at all?","a":"Because Pericles' own means could not match Cimon's public spending, so on Damonides' advice he gave the multitude what was already their own."},{"q":"Why was it said to have made the courts worse?","a":"Because it was always ordinary men rather than respectable ones who took care to cast lots for the duty once there was a fee for it."},{"q":"Why did pay for the assembly come last and start so low?","a":"Because it was rejected the first time it was proposed, and was carried only when men stopped turning up and the presidents were contriving devices to get a show of hands."}]},
{"id":"gr-473","num":473,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Every military officer at Athens was elected by a show of hands rather than drawn by lot, which made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the great exception among the city's offices.","questions":["In every presidency the assembly voted on whether each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was administering his command satisfactorily, and one who lost that vote went before a jury-court.","Aristotle says one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the heavy infantry abroad, one guarded the country, two had the Piraeus between them and one dealt with the trierarchs."],"answer":"strategos","answerText":"strategos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Board of ten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 501/500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"A <b>strategos</b> was one of the ten generals of Athens, and the office was the great exception to the lot, since every military officer there was elected by a show of hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were once chosen one from each tribe and later from the whole body of citizens together, the same vote deciding what each man was to command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One took the heavy infantry on foreign expeditions, one guarded the country and commanded any war fought in it, two had the Piraeus between them at Munychia and at the Point, and one dealt with the trierarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest were sent out on expeditions as occasion arose, and beneath them stood ten regimental commanders, one from each tribe, who appointed the company commanders in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The elections were held by the first board of presidents after the sixth presidency in whose term the weather-omens were favourable, and needed a preliminary resolution of the council first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A general was not left alone once elected, since in every presidency a confirmatory vote was taken on whether he was administering his command satisfactorily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One who lost that vote went before a jury-court, which assessed his penalty if it convicted him and returned him to his command if it did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the field he could punish a breach of discipline with imprisonment, exile or a fine, though the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> says a fine was not usual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was also the one office a man might hold again and again where the rest could be held once, and the pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon says the people knew their business in leaving the generalships to the most influential men rather than drawing them by lot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What that made possible is Pericles, who Plutarch says held an imperial sway continuous and unbroken for fifteen years by his annual tenure of the office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 61, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=61. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 43, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=43. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 44, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=44. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 62, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=62. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 16, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["title","history","greece","politics","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Casualty_list_of_the_Erechtheid_tribe_Louvre_MR1060.jpg/1280px-Casualty_list_of_the_Erechtheid_tribe_Louvre_MR1060.jpg","title":"An Athenian tribe's list of its dead","desc":"The Erechtheid tribe's casualty stele in the Louvre, the rank and file set out in columns. The separate block at the foot opens with the word STRATEGOS, the general being listed apart from them along with the seer and the archers.","credit":"Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casualty_list_of_the_Erechtheid_tribe_Louvre_MR1060.jpg","alt":"A tall weathered marble slab covered in columns of shallow-cut Greek capitals, with a short separate block of names at the bottom."},"why":[{"q":"Why was this office elected when the rest were allotted?","a":"Because every military officer at Athens was chosen by a show of hands, and the pamphlet ascribed to Xenophon says the people knew their business in leaving the generalships to the most influential men."},{"q":"Why was a general voted on ten times a year?","a":"Because a confirmatory vote was taken in every presidency on whether he was administering his command satisfactorily, and one who lost it went before a jury-court."},{"q":"Why did the office make an unbroken career possible?","a":"Because it was the one that might be held again and again where the rest could be held once, which is how Pericles held an imperial sway for fifteen years by annual tenure."}]},
{"id":"gr-474","num":474,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The Athenian Constitution dates <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the archonship of Antidotus and gives its reason as nothing more than the large number of the citizens.","questions":["When the king of Egypt sent forty thousand measures of grain to be shared among the citizens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> put nearly five thousand men of illegal birth into slavery.","With the last of his legitimate sons dead, Pericles asked the assembly to suspend <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they let him enrol his illegitimate son and give him his own name."],"answer":"Pericles' citizenship law","answerText":"Pericles' citizenship law","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">451 BCE, archonship of Antidotus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 429 BCE, for its own author</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Pericles' citizenship law</b> confined the standing of an Athenian to men of citizen birth on both sides, and the <i>Athenian Constitution</i> dates it to the archonship of Antidotus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason it gives is the large number of the citizens, and nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule was not peculiar to Athens: Aristotle says citizenship is in practice limited to the child of citizens on both sides rather than one, and that some states carry the requirement back two or three generations or further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own definition runs differently, a citizen being whoever has the right to share in deliberative or judicial office, and a state a body of such men numerous enough to live independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He adds that not everyone a state cannot do without is counted among them, since artisans and hired labourers are indispensable and are not citizens in every constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch shows the law at work when the king of Egypt sent the people forty thousand measures of grain to be shared out among the citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A crop of prosecutions followed against men of illegal birth who had gone unnoticed until then, and many of them suffered at the hands of informers as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A little less than five thousand were convicted and sold into slavery, and the scrutiny left fourteen thousand and forty men adjudged to be Athenians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Years afterwards the plague took most of his relatives and friends, and Plutarch says he was not seen to weep until the last of his legitimate sons was dead and he broke down laying the wreath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He then asked the assembly to suspend the law he had carried himself; they allowed it, his illegitimate son was enrolled and given his own name, and that son won a sea battle at Arginusae and was put to death by the people with his fellow generals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 26, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=26. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 37, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1275b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=3:section=1275b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 3.1278a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0058:book=3:section=1278a. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 36, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Pericles*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","politics","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/%D0%A4%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B5_%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8_%D0%9B%D0%9F%28%D0%B8%D0%B7_%D0%B8%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%9A%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%29.jpg","title":"The papyrus of the Athenian Constitution","desc":"A facsimile of part of London Papyrus 131 in the British Museum, published in Kenyon's edition. This is the manuscript that carries the archonship the law is dated by, and it lay unread until the end of the 19th century.","credit":"Facsimile from F. G. Kenyon's edition, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A4%D0%B0%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%B5_%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B8_%D0%9B%D0%9F%28%D0%B8%D0%B7_%D0%B8%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%9A%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%29.jpg","alt":"A rectangular sheet of papyrus covered in nine lines of small brown cursive Greek writing, the fibres of the sheet visible across it."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the law passed at all?","a":"Because the Athenian Constitution gives the reason as the large number of the citizens, and nothing else."},{"q":"Why did a gift of grain expose so many people?","a":"Because it was to be shared out among the citizens, so a scrutiny followed in which a little less than five thousand were convicted of illegal birth and sold."},{"q":"Why did its author ask for it to be set aside?","a":"Because the plague had taken his legitimate sons, and the assembly allowed his illegitimate son to be enrolled and given his own name."}]},
{"id":"gr-475","num":475,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides gives the defection of Megara from Sparta as the opening of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and as the principal cause of the deadly hatred the Corinthians felt for Athens afterwards.","questions":["Athens met a Corinthian invasion of the Megarid during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the old men and the boys left in the city, since her armies were away at Aegina and in Egypt.","Euboea revolted, Megara cut down its Athenian garrison and King Pleistoanax came as far as Eleusis, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in a truce meant to last thirty years."],"answer":"First Peloponnesian War","answerText":"First Peloponnesian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 460 – 446 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Thirty Years&#39; Peace, 446 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>First Peloponnesian War</b> is a modern name for the fighting between Athens and the Peloponnesians between the Persian invasions and the great war of 431, which Thucydides sets out in a digression he says explains the growth of Athenian power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It opened with a defection rather than a battle: Megara left the Spartan alliance for Athens, annoyed by a boundary war Corinth had forced on her, and the Athenians took Megara and Pegae and built her long walls down to Nisaea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides calls that the principal cause of the deadly hatred the Corinthians felt for Athens afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting that followed was scattered rather than pitched, a defeat at Halieis, a victory off Cecryphaleia, and then a great sea battle off Aegina where seventy ships were taken and a siege begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Corinthians came down into the Megarid reckoning that with armies away at Aegina and in Egypt Athens could not answer, and Athens answered with the old men and the boys left in the city, under Myronides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That battle was drawn, and in the second a division of the retreating Corinthians blundered into a walled field with a deep trench round it and no way out, where the Athenians ringed them and stoned every man inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens then beat the Boeotians at Oenophyta and took Boeotia and Phocis, pulled down the walls of Tanagra, held a hundred of the richest Locrians as hostages, finished her own long walls and reduced Aegina to tribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A five years' truce followed, and Cimon died besieging Kitium on Cyprus while the fleet he had brought was running short of food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The end came when Euboea revolted, Megara revolted and cut down its Athenian garrison, and a Peloponnesian army under King Pleistoanax came into Attica as far as Eleusis and then went home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Pericles brought Euboea back and the Athenians made a truce for thirty years, giving up Nisaea, Pegae, Troezen and Achaea, the mainland footholds the whole war had been about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.97, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=97. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.103, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=103. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.105, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=105. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.106, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=106. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.108, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=108. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.112, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=112. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.114, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=114. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.115, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=115. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A7%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%B5%CF%85%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_4.jpg/1280px-%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A7%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%B5%CF%85%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_4.jpg","title":"The sanctuary at Eleusis","desc":"Rock-cut steps and fallen blocks on the archaeological site, with the modern town behind. A Peloponnesian army under King Pleistoanax came this far into Attica in the last year of the war, ravaged the country and turned back without going on to Athens.","credit":"Photograph by Meltedrainbow, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A7%CF%8E%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%B5%CF%85%CF%83%CE%AF%CE%BD%CE%B1%CF%82_4.jpg","alt":"Wet rock-cut steps and worn paving running up a low slope on an archaeological site under a grey sky, with a bell tower and modern houses beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Megara's defection matter more than a battle?","a":"Because Thucydides calls it the principal cause of the deadly hatred the Corinthians felt for Athens afterwards, Athens taking Megara and Pegae and walling her down to Nisaea."},{"q":"Why did the Corinthians think the Megarid safe to enter?","a":"Because they reckoned that with armies away at Aegina and in Egypt Athens could not answer — and Athens answered with the old men and the boys left in the city."},{"q":"Why did the thirty years' truce look like a defeat after so many victories?","a":"Because Athens gave up Nisaea, Pegae, Troezen and Achaea, the mainland footholds the whole war had been about."}]},
{"id":"gr-476","num":476,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides says the victory at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went to the Lacedaemonians after heavy loss on both sides, where Diodorus says the fight ended in a tie and both armies claimed it.","questions":["A Spartan army returning from Doris found the pass over Geraneia held and the gulf unsafe, so it waited in Boeotia and fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there.","Thessalian cavalry riding with the Athenians went over to the Spartans during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Diodorus says they then ambushed an Athenian supply train by night."],"answer":"Battle of Tanagra","answerText":"Battle of Tanagra","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 457 BCE, in Boeotia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Athenian levy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14,000, with 1,000 Argives</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Tanagra</b> was fought in Boeotia during the First Peloponnesian War, and the two surviving accounts of it do not agree on who won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Lacedaemonians had marched north to help the Dorians of Boeum, Kitinium and Erineum, the old home of their own people, against a Phocian attack, with fifteen hundred hoplites and ten thousand allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having made the Phocians give the captured town back, they found the way home shut: the Athenians held Megara and Pegae and guarded the pass over Geraneia, and the Athenian fleet made the gulf unsafe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> So they waited in Boeotia, and Thucydides adds a second reason, that a party in Athens had secretly encouraged them in the hope of ending the democracy and stopping the long walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Athenians came out with their whole levy, a thousand Argives and their other allies, fourteen thousand men in all, with Thessalian cavalry that went over to the Lacedaemonians during the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Thucydides gives the fight in one sentence, that after heavy loss on both sides the victory declared for the Lacedaemonians, who cut down the fruit trees of the Megarid on their way home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The suspicion ran both ways: Plutarch says the exiled Cimon came in arms to stand with his tribe, was refused, and asked the comrades charged with laconising to clear themselves by fighting, which they did and fell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Diodorus tells it longer and differently, with the Athenians and Argives fighting the battle through until night ended it undecided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In his account the Thessalians then ambushed a supply train from Attica by night, taken for friends by guards who did not know they had changed sides, and the second fight that followed ended in a tie, both armies claiming it and agreeing a truce of four months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pausanias saw a slab in the Athenian public cemetery carved with horsemen fighting, set up for Melanopus and Macartatus, who died against Lacedaemonians and Boeotians on the borders of Eleon and Tanagra.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.107, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=107. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.108, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=108. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.80, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=80. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.29, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0160:book=1:chapter=29. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 17, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Loeb Classical Library, at LacusCurtius, https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Cimon*.html. [Open access]"],"tags":["event","history","greece","warfare","athens"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/09/Asopos_River_at_AgiosThomasTanagras.jpg","title":"The Asopos at the pass of ancient Tanagra","desc":"The Asopos in winter, at the pass below the site of ancient Tanagra in eastern Boeotia. The ancient accounts place the battle at Tanagra and no closer, so the exact ground is not known.","credit":"Photograph by Agiosthomas, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asopos_River_at_AgiosThomasTanagras.jpg","alt":"A narrow brown river winding between banks of bare winter scrub and leafless trees, with a low bridge crossing it in the distance and pale hills beyond."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a Spartan army waiting in Boeotia at all?","a":"Because having helped the Dorians against Phocis it found the way home shut, the Athenians holding Megara and Pegae and guarding the pass over Geraneia while the fleet made the gulf unsafe."},{"q":"Why does Thucydides give a second reason for their delay?","a":"Because a party in Athens had secretly encouraged them, hoping to end the democracy and stop the long walls."},{"q":"Why do the accounts disagree about who won?","a":"Because Thucydides says that after heavy loss the victory declared for the Lacedaemonians, while Diodorus has the battle end undecided at nightfall and a second fight end in a tie with both sides claiming it."}]},
{"id":"gr-477","num":477,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Thucydides dates the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 62 days after the defeat at Tanagra, and says that after it the Athenians became masters of Boeotia and Phocis.","questions":["Diodorus rates the Athenian victory at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Marathon and Plataea, and complains that not one historian has described how it was fought or how the troops stood.","The funeral oration in Plato's <i>Menexenus</i> calls the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a two days' fight in which Athens defended the liberties of the Boeotians and restored men wrongly driven out."],"answer":"Battle of Oenophyta","answerText":"Battle of Oenophyta","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 457 BCE, in Boeotia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">After Tanagra</span><span class=\"dt-v\">62 days, per Thucydides</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Oenophyta</b> was fought in Boeotia in about 457 BCE, when an Athenian army under Myronides beat the Boeotians and brought central Greece under Athenian control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides dates it 62 days after the defeat at Tanagra: the Athenians became masters of Boeotia and Phocis, pulled down the walls of Tanagra, took a hundred of the richest Opuntian Locrians as hostages and finished their own long walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus gives the quarrel behind it: the Thebans, humbled for having sided with Xerxes, asked Sparta to help them win the leadership of all Boeotia, promising in return to make war on Athens alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sparta agreed, judging a stronger Thebes a counterweight to Athens, and with an army already at Tanagra enlarged the Theban circuit wall and compelled the Boeotian cities to submit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Athenians elected Myronides son of Callias, who marched on the day he had named with whoever had turned up, saying that men late by choice would play an ignoble part in battle too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In Diodorus the Boeotians take the field as a nation once their land is wasted, hold the struggle all day, and break only after a hard fight, leaving Myronides master of every Boeotian city except Thebes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That exception is not in Thucydides, and the accounts differ in shape too: Thucydides has one battle after Tanagra where Diodorus puts an earlier victory, the taking of Tanagra and the wasting of Boeotia first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The funeral oration in Plato's <i>Menexenus</i> calls it a two days' battle fought for the liberties of the Boeotians, which restored men wrongly driven out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Diodorus ranks it with Marathon and Plataea, won single-handed against the people he thought the staunchest fighters in Greece, and complains that although it became famous no historian described how it was fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What it won lasted about ten years: in 447 the Boeotian exiles holding Orchomenus and Chaeronea beat Tolmides at Coronea, and Athens gave up all Boeotia to get its prisoners back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","boeotia"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.108, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=108. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.81, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=81. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.83, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=83. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Menexenus</i> 242b, trans. R. G. Bury, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.%20Menex.%20242b. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.82, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=82. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.113, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=113. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Remains_of_the_Long_Walls_at_Moschato_on_25_February_2019.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_Long_Walls_at_Moschato_on_25_February_2019.jpg","title":"What the battle paid for: a fragment of the Long Walls","desc":"The footings of a tower of the Long Walls, in a park at Moschato between Athens and Piraeus. Thucydides puts the finishing of those walls among the things the Athenians did in the season that followed Oenophyta.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC0 1.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_Long_Walls_at_Moschato_on_25_February_2019.jpg","alt":"A low rectangle of weathered stone blocks, the foundation course of a square tower, set in a patch of grass in a small urban park with pine trees and a graffitied wall behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Sparta agree to strengthen Thebes?","a":"Because it judged a stronger Thebes a counterweight to Athens, and Thebes promised in return to make war on Athens alone."},{"q":"Why did the general march with only those who had turned up?","a":"Because he had named the day and said that men late by choice would play an ignoble part in battle too."},{"q":"Why does Diodorus complain about the battle's fame?","a":"Because although he ranks it with Marathon and Plataea, won single-handed against the staunchest fighters in Greece, no historian described how it was fought."}]},
{"id":"gr-478","num":478,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"200 Athenian ships were diverted from Cyprus into the Nile at the start of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and took two-thirds of Memphis before the White Castle held them.","questions":["Megabyzus ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by diverting a canal, which left the Athenian ships high and dry on Prosopitis and let his army march over on foot and take it.","Thucydides and Diodorus disagree about how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended, one saying most of the army perished and the other that the Persians granted a truce letting them go."],"answer":"Egyptian expedition","answerText":"Egyptian expedition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 460 – 454 BCE, in Egypt</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 years, per Thucydides</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On Prosopitis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">besieged 18 months</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Egyptian expedition</b> was the Athenian intervention in a revolt of Egypt against Persia between about 460 and 454 BCE, and it ended in the heaviest defeat the Delian League ever suffered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Thucydides says Inaros son of Psammetichus, a Libyan king based at Marea, brought almost the whole of Egypt out in revolt from Artaxerxes and invited the Athenians in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They broke off a campaign in Cyprus, took 200 ships into the Nile, mastered the river and two-thirds of Memphis, and attacked the last third, the White Castle, where the Persians had taken refuge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus, who has the Egyptians rise on the news of Xerxes' death and make Inaros king, says Athens voted 300 triremes and that Artaxerxes sent more than 300,000 men against them under Achaemenes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herodotus, writing nearest the events, calls that Achaemenes a son of Darius set over Egypt by Xerxes, and says Inaros killed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The King's next move was money rather than men: he sent an agent to Sparta to buy an invasion of Attica that would draw the Athenians home, and recalled him when it failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Megabyzus son of Zopyrus then arrived with a large army, beat the Egyptians and their allies, drove the Greeks out of Memphis and shut them on the island of Prosopitis for a year and six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He ended it by diverting the canal, which left the Athenian ships high and dry and joined most of the island to the mainland, so that his men marched over on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides says the enterprise came to ruin after six years, that most of the host perished with only a few reaching Cyrene, that Inaros was betrayed and crucified, and that a relief squadron of fifty ships was destroyed at the Mendesian mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diodorus ends it quite differently: the Egyptians deserted, the Athenians burned their own ships, and the Persian generals, unwilling to lose many thousands against such men, granted a truce that let them leave Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","warfare","greece","egypt"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.104, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=104. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.109, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=109. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.110, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=110. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.71, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=71. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.74, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=74. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.7, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=7. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 11.77, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=11:chapter=77. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Ruins_at_Mit_Rahina.jpg/1280px-Ruins_at_Mit_Rahina.jpg","title":"What is left of Memphis","desc":"Fallen blocks and column drums among the palms at Mit Rahina, the site of Memphis. Thucydides says the Athenians mastered the Nile and two-thirds of the city, and were still attacking the last third when the Persian army arrived.","credit":"Photograph by Darer101, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_at_Mit_Rahina.jpg","alt":"Scattered limestone blocks and round column drums lying on bare sandy ground among tall palms and a dark evergreen tree, with low modern buildings behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the King try to buy a war in Greece?","a":"Because he sent an agent to Sparta to purchase an invasion of Attica that would draw the Athenians home, and recalled him when it failed."},{"q":"Why did diverting a canal end the siege?","a":"Because it left the Athenian ships high and dry and joined most of the island to the mainland, so the Persian army marched over on foot."},{"q":"Why are the two accounts of the end irreconcilable?","a":"Because Thucydides has most of the host perish with a relief squadron destroyed at the Mendesian mouth, where Diodorus has the Persians grant a truce that let the Greeks leave Egypt."}]},
{"id":"gr-479","num":479,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The first of the Athenian quota lists, cut in the archonship of Ariston, is the only dating evidence for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which no surviving historian narrates.","questions":["Plutarch says Pericles' enemies attacked the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the assembly, complaining that Athens was gilding herself like a wanton woman with money the allies were forced to pay.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the goddess's sixtieth of the tribute was declared at Athens and published on the Acropolis, city by city, at one mina per talent."],"answer":"transfer of the treasury","answerText":"transfer of the treasury","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 454 BCE, Delos to Athens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First quota list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">454/3, under Ariston</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rate to Athena</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 mina per talent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>transfer of the treasury</b> was the removal of the Delian League's common fund from Delos, where Thucydides says the congresses were held in the temple, to Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No surviving historian narrates the move, and its date rests on a stone: the first Athenian quota list, recording the sixtieth of the allied tribute paid to the goddess in the archonship of Ariston, 454/3 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were cut on the <i>lapis primus</i>, a stele 3.8 metres (12 feet) high and the most massive of Athenian inscriptions, which carried fifteen years of them and is put together from 184 fragments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rate was one mina per talent and the entries run city by city: 3,000 drachmas from the Aeginetans, 1,285 from the Abderites, 300 from the Thasians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What changed was the accounting rather than the tax: the goddess's share was declared at Athens and published on the Acropolis, where those sixtieths are usually thought to have paid for the building programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch says Pericles' enemies cried out in the assembly that the people had lost its good name by removing the public money of the Greeks from Delos into its own keeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pericles had robbed it of the one seemly excuse it had, that fear of the barbarian made it take the fund from a sacred island and guard it in a stronghold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hellas was insulted, they said, when she saw her own forced contributions gilding a city that, like a wanton woman, added precious stones and costly temples to her wardrobe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pericles answered that Athens owed no account so long as she kept the barbarian off: the allies furnished not a horse, not a ship, not a hoplite but money, which belongs to those who take it rather than those who give.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sums are not agreed: Diodorus, telling the story to explain a war begun to escape an audit, has 8,000 talents moved and given to Pericles to guard, then puts the fund at 10,000, where Thucydides makes its peak 9,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Athenian Tribute List, 454/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 259), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/259. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2012. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.96, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=96. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.38, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=38. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.40, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=40. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-2.JPG/1280px-EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-2.JPG","title":"The stone the date comes from","desc":"Part of the first stele of the Athenian tribute lists in the Epigraphic Museum, Athens, its surviving inscribed fragments set into a plaster ground. Each year from 454/3 the allied cities were entered on it with the sixtieth of their tribute paid to Athena.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-2.JPG","alt":"A tall panel of pale plaster into which dozens of irregular marble fragments have been set, most of them carrying close-ruled lines of small incised Greek capitals."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the date of the move rest on a stone?","a":"Because no surviving historian narrates it, and the evidence is the first Athenian quota list recording the sixtieth of the tribute paid to the goddess."},{"q":"Why was it the accounting that changed rather than the tax?","a":"Because the goddess's share was now declared at Athens and published on the Acropolis, where those sixtieths are usually thought to have paid for the building programme."},{"q":"Why did Pericles' enemies say the city had lost its excuse?","a":"Because the one seemly plea had been that fear of the barbarian made Athens take the fund from a sacred island and guard it in a stronghold."}]},
{"id":"gr-480","num":480,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Diodorus says the Greek cities of Asia were to be free and no Persian warship to sail inside Phaselis under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, negotiated about 449 BCE.","questions":["Plutarch reports that Callisthenes denied the King had ever made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and said he kept his distance only out of fear of the defeat at the Eurymedon.","Thucydides narrates the Cyprus campaign and Cimon's death and then passes straight to the Sacred War, never mentioning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all."],"answer":"Peace of Callias","answerText":"Peace of Callias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 449 BCE, if at all</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Satraps kept</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 days' journey from the sea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Doubted since</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Peace of Callias</b> is the treaty said to have ended the wars between Athens and Persia, negotiated about 449 BCE by Callias son of Hipponicus, and it may never have existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus gives the terms: the Greek cities of Asia to live under laws of their own making, Persian satraps to come no nearer the sea than a three days' journey, and no Persian warship inside Phaselis or the Cyanean Rocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In return the Athenians were not to send troops into the King's territory, and Diodorus places the agreement after the Cyprus campaign in which Cimon died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch has it differently, as the fruit of the Eurymedon a generation earlier, with the King to keep from the Greek coast as far as a horse could travel in a day and not to sail west of the Cyanean and Chelidonian isles in armoured warships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds that Athens built an altar of Peace for it and paid distinguished honours to Callias, and that a copy of the treaty stood in its place among the decrees Craterus collected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The doubts are ancient: Plutarch reports that Callisthenes denied the King ever made such terms, and said he simply kept his distance out of fear of that defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proof offered was that Pericles with fifty ships and Ephialtes with thirty sailed beyond the Chelidonian isles and met no Persian fleet at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The heaviest difficulty is Thucydides, who narrates the Cyprus campaign, Cimon's death and the double victory off Salamis, then passes to the Sacred War without a word of any peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus supplies the one contemporary trace, that Callias son of Hipponicus and other Athenian envoys were at Susa on some other business, which he does not describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isocrates, writing in the 4th century, boasts that the Persians launched no warship this side of Phaselis, which is a term of the treaty stated as something Athens had achieved rather than something the King had signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.4, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=4. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Cimon</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Cim.%2013. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.112, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=112. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.151, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=151. [Open access]","Isocrates, <i>Panegyricus</i> 118, trans. George Norlin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Isoc.%204%20118. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Phaselis_City_Harbour_4761.jpg/1280px-Phaselis_City_Harbour_4761.jpg","title":"The line the King's ships were not to cross","desc":"The city harbour of Phaselis on the Lycian coast, its ancient quay blocks awash in the shallows. Both Diodorus and Isocrates use this town as the western limit of Persian naval movement, one as a term of the treaty and the other as a fact about Athenian power.","credit":"Photograph by Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaselis_City_Harbour_4761.jpg","alt":"A quiet green bay enclosed by a wooded headland, with lines of squared ancient stone blocks lying just above and below the waterline in the foreground."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the treaty's existence doubted?","a":"Because Callisthenes denied the King ever made such terms, and Thucydides narrates the Cyprus campaign, Cimon's death and the victory off Salamis without a word of any peace."},{"q":"Why was sailing past the Chelidonian isles offered as proof?","a":"Because Pericles with fifty ships and Ephialtes with thirty went beyond them and met no Persian fleet at all, which was read as fear rather than agreement."},{"q":"Why do the two accounts of the terms sit twenty years apart?","a":"Because Diodorus places the agreement after the Cyprus campaign where Cimon died, while Plutarch makes it the fruit of the Eurymedon a generation earlier."}]},
{"id":"gr-481","num":481,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Pericles told the assembly that the Spartans had never sought the trials of judgment the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> provided for, nor accepted them when Athens offered.","questions":["The Corcyraeans argued that an alliance with Athens would not break the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a city in league with neither side could lawfully join either.","Athens gave back Nisaea, Achaea, Pegae and Troezen under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held for fourteen of its stated term before the Thebans entered Plataea."],"answer":"Thirty Years' Peace","answerText":"Thirty Years' Peace","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Agreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 446 BCE, for 30 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 years, per Thucydides</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Broken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">431 BCE, at Plataea</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Thirty Years' Peace</b> was the treaty of about 446 BCE that closed the first war between Athens and the Peloponnesians, and Thucydides says it lasted fourteen of its thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was made once Athens had recovered Euboea, and by it the Athenians gave back Nisaea, Achaea, Pegae and Troezen, the places on the mainland they held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus adds that it followed the defeat at Coronea and a general revolt, that Pericles stormed Hestiaea and removed its people, and that Callias and Chares negotiated the peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its central article, which Pericles quotes in the assembly, bound each side to give and receive trials of judgment in their disputes and meanwhile to hold what it possessed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A second article let a city in league with neither side join whichever it pleased, and that is the clause the Corcyraeans put to the Athenians when they came asking for an alliance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Corinthians answered that it covered only a state bringing protection rather than a war, and reminded Athens that they had not voted against her over Samos, though the Peloponnese was divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That restraint had been real: the Samian revolt fell in the sixth year of the peace, Athens put it down and set up a democracy there, and no Peloponnesian army moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What came in 432 was demands rather than arbitration — raise the siege of Potidaea, restore Aegina to her own laws, repeal the decree against Megara, and from the last embassy, give all the Greeks their liberty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pericles told the Athenians that the Lacedaemonians had never sought the trial the articles provided nor accepted it when offered, and meant to clear themselves by war rather than by words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The peace ended in its fifteenth year when Thebans entered Plataea by night while it still held, and long afterwards the Spartans judged the fault their own, for that and for refusing the trial Athens had offered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.115, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=115. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.2, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=2. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.7, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=7. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.140, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=140. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.35, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=35. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.40, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=40. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 7.18, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=18. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Troezen_-_Acropolis_05.JPG/1280px-Troezen_-_Acropolis_05.JPG","title":"One of the four places handed back","desc":"The acropolis of Troezen above the Saronic Gulf. Thucydides lists Troezen with Nisaea, Achaea and Pegae as the mainland holdings Athens gave up when the treaty was made.","credit":"Photograph by Schuppi, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troezen_-_Acropolis_05.JPG","alt":"A steep scrub-covered hill rising to a bare rocky summit, with a blue gulf and a line of mountains beyond it under a clear sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why was arbitration the article that mattered most?","a":"Because each side was bound to give and receive trials of judgment in their disputes and meanwhile to hold what it possessed, which is the clause Pericles quotes in the assembly."},{"q":"Why did the Corinthians dispute the clause about neutral cities?","a":"Because they answered that letting a state in league with neither side join whichever it pleased covered a state bringing protection rather than a war."},{"q":"Why did the Spartans afterwards judge the fault their own?","a":"Because Thebans entered Plataea by night while the peace still held, and because they had refused the trial the articles provided and Athens had offered."}]},
{"id":"gr-482","num":482,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Herodotus says the Athenians left four thousand settlers on the lands of the Hippobotai in 506 BCE, the earliest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the sources record.","questions":["Plutarch says Pericles used a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to lighten the city of an idle crowd, relieve the poor, and set a garrison among the allies against revolt.","Thucydides says the Histiaeans were driven from their homes and their land taken over, which is the harshest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the record of the Athenian empire."],"answer":"cleruchy","answerText":"cleruchy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First</span><span class=\"dt-v\">506 BCE, on Euboea</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Periclean waves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 450 – 440 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Histiaea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">446 BCE, expelled</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"A <b>cleruchy</b> was a settlement of Athenians planted on land taken from another state, each man holding a kleros, or lot, where he stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus records the earliest: after beating the Boeotians and the Chalcidians on a single day in 506 BCE, the Athenians left four thousand kleruchs on the lands of the Hippobotai, the men of substance among the Chalcidians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those Hippobotai they took alive were kept in fetters and later ransomed at two minas a head, their chains hung in the acropolis where Herodotus still saw them, and a tenth of the ransom bought a bronze four-horse chariot with a boasting epigram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plutarch says Pericles sent a thousand settlers to the Chersonese, five hundred to Naxos, half that number to Andros and a thousand into Thrace to live among the Bisaltae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The purposes he gives are three at once: to lighten the city of an idle crowd, to relieve the embarrassments of the poor, and to give the allies a neighbouring garrison that would prevent rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The harshest case is Histiaea on Euboea in 446 BCE, where Thucydides says the rest of the island was settled by agreement while the Histiaeans were driven from their homes and their territory taken over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch adds why they alone were used so inexorably, that they had captured an Attic ship and killed its crew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the same settlement Athens swore an oath to Chalcis, preserved on stone, not to expel the Chalcidians from their city nor lay it waste, and to disfranchise, exile, imprison, execute or fine no man without an Athenian vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Chalcidians swore in return that they would not revolt by any means or device, would denounce anyone who did, and would pay whatever tribute they could persuade the Athenians to accept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two accounts of Chalcis do not sit together, since Plutarch has Pericles banish the Hippobotai in 446, which is most probably the confiscation Herodotus dates to 506 put in the wrong place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 5.77, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=5:chapter=77. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 11, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2011. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.114, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=114. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 23, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2023. [Open access]","Athenian Regulations for Chalkis (IG I<sup>3</sup> 40), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/40. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/The_oldest_known_decree_of_the_Athenian_assembly_508-500_BC_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-The_oldest_known_decree_of_the_Athenian_assembly_508-500_BC_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"The oldest decree of the Athenian assembly","desc":"IG I3 1 in the Epigraphic Museum, Athens, cut about 500 BCE in early Attic letters. It concerns Athenians settled on Salamis, and is read either as establishing cleruchs there or as regulating their residence.","credit":"Photograph by TimeTravelRome, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_oldest_known_decree_of_the_Athenian_assembly_508-500_BC_%28cropped%29.jpg","alt":"A tall narrow marble stele standing against a dark blue wall, its upper half made of inscribed ancient fragments set into a plain restoration, the letters cut in an early angular Greek script."},"why":[{"q":"Why were these settlements sent out for three reasons at once?","a":"Because they lightened the city of an idle crowd, relieved the embarrassments of the poor, and gave the allies a neighbouring garrison that would prevent rebellion."},{"q":"Why was one city on Euboea treated worse than the rest?","a":"Because the Histiaeans had captured an Attic ship and killed its crew, so they were driven from their homes where the rest of the island was settled by agreement."},{"q":"Why do the two accounts of Chalcis not sit together?","a":"Because Plutarch has Pericles banish the Hippobotai in 446, which is most probably the confiscation Herodotus dates to 506 put in the wrong place."}]},
{"id":"gr-483","num":483,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> record only the sixtieth given to Athena, a city's actual payment has to be reached by multiplying what the stone says by sixty.","questions":["One stele of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried the first fifteen years and is put together from 184 broken pieces, parts of whose arrangement are still disputed.","Samothrace appealed against its assessment by pleading that it was small and rough, one of the workings of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Thucydides never describes."],"answer":"Athenian tribute lists","answerText":"Athenian tribute lists","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">454 – 440 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">439 – 432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rate recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 mina per talent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Athenian tribute lists</b> are the inscriptions on which Athens published, year by year, the sixtieth part of the allies' tribute given to Athena, and they are the fullest record the empire left of itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They give that sixtieth and not the tribute, at one mina per talent, so a city's payment has to be reached by multiplying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first fifteen years stood on one stele, the <i>lapis primus</i>, and the next eight on a second, after which each year had a stone of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both are reconstructions from broken pieces, the first from 184 fragments and the second from 73, and parts of the arrangement have been questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reassessments fell in the Great Panathenaic years 450 and 446 with a special one in 443/2, and a city could appeal: Samothrace pleaded that it was small, its land high and rough with little fit for farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> No list seems to have been cut for 449/8, which may belong with an episode Antiphon recalls, when the whole board of hellenotamiai was accused of embezzlement and put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of them, Sosias, was condemned but not yet executed when it came out how the money had really gone, and the people took him from the hands of the Eleven, the rest having died innocent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stones carry headings as well as sums, and those of 434/3 divide the entries into cities that assessed themselves for tribute, cities that individuals signed up to pay for, and one unassessed city paying five drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those headings show that a city, or a faction inside one, could take the initiative and offer to pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They also record machinery no historian describes: in 425/4 Thoudippos carried a reassessment with heralds sent to four districts, ten assessors sworn in and fined for each day of delay, and a new court of a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["text","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Athenian Tribute List, 454/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 259), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/259. [Open access]","Assessment of Tribute, 425/4 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 71), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/71. [Open access]","Athenian Tribute List 13, 442/1 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 270), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/270. [Open access]","Antiphon, <i>On the Murder of Herodes</i> 69-70, trans. K. J. Maidment, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Antiph.%205%2069. [Open access]","Athenian Tribute List 21, 434/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 278), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/278. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-3.JPG/1280px-EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-3.JPG","title":"A year of the list, read off the stone","desc":"Fragments of the Athenian tribute lists in the Epigraphic Museum, Athens. A district heading is legible down the left, with the cities of that panel and their sums entered under it in early Attic letters.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EPMA-IGI%283%29259-272-Tribute_list-3.JPG","alt":"Irregular white marble fragments set into pale plaster, closely covered with short lines of incised Greek capitals: a heading, then city names each followed by a figure."},"why":[{"q":"Why does a city's payment have to be calculated from these stones?","a":"Because they publish the sixtieth part given to Athena rather than the tribute itself, at one mina per talent, so the sum has to be multiplied out."},{"q":"Why does the missing year of 449/8 matter?","a":"Because it may belong with the episode Antiphon recalls, when the whole board of treasurers was accused of embezzlement and put to death, all but one who proved innocent too late for the rest."},{"q":"Why are the headings of 434/3 worth as much as the sums?","a":"Because they divide the entries into cities that assessed themselves, cities individuals signed up to pay for, and one unassessed city, which shows a city or a faction could take the initiative."}]},
{"id":"gr-484","num":484,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Copies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have been dug up at Smyrna, Olbia, Cos, Siphnos, Aphytis and Hamaxitos, and they do not all say the same thing.","questions":["Every allied city had to cut the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on a stele in its own agora at its own expense, which is asked of no other Athenian decree.","Aristophanes' joke about weights and measures for the Olophyxians is the best clue to when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was passed, since it carries no date itself."],"answer":"Coinage Decree","answerText":"Coinage Decree","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">uncertain, 450 – 410 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copies found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 7 allied cities</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Herald's fine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 drachmas</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Coinage Decree</b> required the allies of Athens to use Athenian coins, weights and measures, and survives only in the copies the allied cities were made to cut, found at Smyrna, Olbia, Cos, Siphnos, Aphytis and Hamaxitos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the only decree of the Athenian empire known to have been required in every allied city, on a stele in that city's own agora and at its own expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heralds were to carry it to the Islands, Ionia, the Hellespont and the Thraceward region on pain of a ten-thousand-drachma fine, and anyone proposing to spend the reserved silver on anything else was to be taken to the Eleven and put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same districts and the same kind of penalty run through the tribute assessment of 425/4, so this is the empire's ordinary machinery rather than a special measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The copies do not agree, each cut locally from a text copied more than once on its way from the assembly: Aphytis leaves out two clauses about reminting that stand in the Smyrna copy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Aphytis has the fullest form of the oath added to the Council's, that no one shall strike silver coinage in the cities or use coinage, weights or measures other than the Athenians'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decree carries no date and no historian mentions it, so it has been placed anywhere between the 450s and the 410s BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best clue is a joke: in the <i>Birds</i> of 414 a dealer in decrees offers Cloudcuckooland one saying its people shall use the weights, measures and decrees of the Olophyxians, and is beaten off the stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> If that was fresh, the decree stands near the year Thucydides describes, when Athens with its treasury low replaced the tribute with a twentieth on all goods carried by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens was still policing its coin two generations later, when Nikophon's law of 375/4 set a public approver among the bankers' tables to cut through any coin with a core of bronze or lead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["text","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Athenian Decree on Coinage, Weights and Measures (IG I<sup>3</sup> 1453), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/1453. [Open access]","Assessment of Tribute, 425/4 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 71), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/71. [Open access]","Aristophanes, <i>Birds</i> 1035-45, trans. Eugene O'Neill Jr., Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristoph.%20Birds%201040. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 7.28, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=7:chapter=28. [Open access]","Law on approvers (dokimastai) of silver coinage, 375/4 BC (AIO 819), trans. Stephen Lambert, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIO/819. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Athens_-_454-404_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg/1280px-Athens_-_454-404_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","title":"The coin the allies were told to use","desc":"An Athenian silver tetradrachm of the empire years, its obverse showing Athena in a crested helmet, in the State Coin Collection at Munich. It is the currency the decree required across the alliance in place of the cities' own.","credit":"Photograph by ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_-_454-404_BC_-_silver_tetradrachm_-_head_of_Athena_-_owl_-_M%C3%BCnchen_SMS.jpg","alt":"A worn oval silver coin on a blue cloth mount, struck with the head of a helmeted woman in profile facing right, the helmet crest and a spray of leaves visible above her brow."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the surviving copies of the decree disagree?","a":"Because each was cut locally from a text copied more than once on its way from the assembly, so Aphytis leaves out two clauses about reminting that stand in the Smyrna copy."},{"q":"Why is it read as ordinary imperial machinery rather than an emergency?","a":"Because the same districts and the same kind of penalty run through the tribute assessment of 425/4, heralds carrying it out under threat of a heavy fine."},{"q":"Why does a joke in a comedy help date it?","a":"Because a dealer in decrees in the Birds of 414 offers a city one saying its people shall use the weights, measures and decrees of the Olophyxians, which only works if the real decree was fresh."}]},
{"id":"gr-485","num":485,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"A philosopher serving as general, Melissus son of Ithagenes, led a sortie during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that gave his city command of its own sea for a fortnight.","questions":["A quarrel with Miletus over Priene grew into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Athens put down only after a siege that ran into its ninth month.","Elpinice told Pericles that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had cost brave citizens not against Persians but in the subversion of an allied and kindred city."],"answer":"revolt of Samos","answerText":"revolt of Samos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">440 BCE, with Byzantium</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hostages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">50 boys and 50 men, to Lemnos</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Siege</span><span class=\"dt-v\">into its 9th month</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>revolt of Samos</b> in 440 BCE was the most dangerous rebellion the Athenian empire faced before the Peloponnesian War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It grew out of a war between Samos and Miletus over Priene: the Milesians got the worst of it, complained at Athens, and were joined by Samians who wanted their own constitution changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens sent forty ships, set up a democracy on the island and carried fifty boys and fifty men off to Lemnos as hostages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Samian exiles then hired seven hundred soldiers, made terms with Pissuthnes the satrap at Sardis, crossed at evening to seize the popular party, stole the hostages back out of Lemnos and handed the Athenian garrison to the Persian, and Byzantium revolted with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pericles beat seventy Samian ships off Tragia with 44 of his own, and once the reinforcements came he walled the city about with a triple wall and shut it in by sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> While he was away with sixty ships to meet a reported Phoenician fleet, Melissus son of Ithagenes, a philosopher then serving as general, led a sortie that sank the guard ships and left Samos master of its own sea for fourteen days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both sides branded their prisoners, the Samians marking Athenians with an owl because the Athenians had marked Samians with the samaina, the boar-prowed ship Polycrates had built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city held out into the ninth month and then gave up its walls, its warships and hostages, and agreed to repay the cost of the war by instalments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Duris of Samos wrote that Pericles had the Samian captains crucified in the market-place of Miletus and clubbed to death after ten days, which Plutarch rejects because no other writer records it and Duris was writing about his own city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elpinice told Pericles after his victory speech that it was admirable to have lost so many brave citizens, not against Phoenicians or Medes as her brother Cimon had, but in the subversion of an allied and kindred city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","greece","athens","warfare"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.115, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=115. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.116, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=116. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.117, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=117. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 26, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2026. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 28, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2028. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/2019-08-28_Pythagorion_%28KPFC%29_01.jpg/1280px-2019-08-28_Pythagorion_%28KPFC%29_01.jpg","title":"The city Pericles besieged","desc":"Pythagoreio on Samos, which stands on the site of the ancient city, seen from the hills above with the Anatolian coast beyond. Athens shut this place in with a triple wall on the land side and with ships by sea.","credit":"Photograph by KPFC, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019-08-28_Pythagorion_%28KPFC%29_01.jpg","alt":"A small red-roofed town on a low headland beside a wide calm sea, seen from a hillside of dry grass and olive trees, with a far coastline on the horizon."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a quarrel between two other cities pull Athens in?","a":"Because the Milesians got the worst of the war over Priene, complained at Athens, and were joined by Samians who wanted their own constitution changed."},{"q":"Why did the island regain its own sea for a fortnight?","a":"Because Pericles was away with sixty ships to meet a reported Phoenician fleet, and Melissus led a sortie that sank the guard ships."},{"q":"Why does Plutarch reject the story of the crucified captains?","a":"Because no other writer records it and Duris, who does, was writing about his own city."}]},
{"id":"gr-486","num":486,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Making allies sail to Athens for their lawsuits kept jurors in pay all year and let the people manage the cities of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without leaving home.","questions":["Pericles told the assembly that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was by now in the nature of a tyranny, unjust to have taken up and unsafe to lay down.","A decree let Chalcis try its own officials but reserved exile, death and loss of rights to a court at Athens, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in one city."],"answer":"Athenian empire","answerText":"Athenian empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chalcis decree</span><span class=\"dt-v\">446 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Piraeus duty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 per cent on goods</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Athenian empire</b> is the rule Athens came to exercise over the cities of its alliance, and its daily working was as much legal as military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Allies with a case against Athens or each other came there for it, and the Athenians told the Spartans that trials ran under laws equal to both sides, that judgment often went against Athens, and that they were called contentious for allowing law at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their argument was that a power able to compel need not go to law at all, and that men resent injustice from an equal more than violence from a stronger, so moderation is read as robbery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Old Oligarch puts the same practice the other way up: making the allies sail to Athens keeps the jurors in pay through the year and lets the people run the allied cities from home, protecting democrats there and ruining their opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds the smaller profits, the one per cent at Piraeus, lodgings let and animals hired, and concludes that every ally must flatter the populace and take by the hand whoever comes into court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A settlement with Chalcis shows it inside one city, where the Chalcidians were to hold their own officials to account at home just as the Athenians did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The exception was any case carrying exile, execution or loss of civic rights, which went on appeal to Athens; and the decree was to stand on the Athenian acropolis at Chalcidian expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Athens also settled who paid tax to whom, leaving foreigners in Chalcis to pay the Chalcidians unless they already paid Athens or held an Athenian exemption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What to call it was argued in Athens itself: Pericles told the assembly their rule was by now in the nature of a tyranny, unjust to have taken up and unsafe to lay down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cleon said the same without the regret, that it was a tyranny over men held against their will, who plotted continually and obeyed only because Athens was stronger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","politics"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.77, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=77. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.16, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=16. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.17-18, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=17. [Open access]","Athenian Regulations for Chalkis (IG I<sup>3</sup> 40), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/40. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.63, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=63. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.37, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=37. [Open access]"],"quote":{"book":"thucydides-peloponnesian-war","n":"2","text":"<p>You should remember also that what you are fighting against is not merely slavery as an exchange for independence, but also loss of empire and danger from the animosities incurred in its exercise. Besides, to recede is no longer possible, if indeed any of you in the alarm of the moment has become enamoured of the honesty of such an unambitious part. For what you hold is, to speak somewhat plainly, a tyranny; to take it perhaps was wrong, but to let it go is unsafe.</p>","cite":"Book 2.63"},"why":[{"q":"Why did Athens claim credit for hearing allied cases at all?","a":"Because a power able to compel need not go to law, and the Athenians argued that men resent injustice from an equal more than violence from a stronger, so moderation is read as robbery."},{"q":"Why does the Old Oligarch call the same practice a profit?","a":"Because making the allies sail to Athens keeps the jurors in pay through the year and lets the people run the allied cities from home, besides the harbour dues, lodgings and hire."},{"q":"Why did Athenians themselves call the rule a tyranny?","a":"Because Pericles told the assembly it was unjust to have taken up and unsafe to lay down, and Cleon said the same without the regret, that the subjects obeyed only because Athens was stronger."}]},
{"id":"gr-487","num":487,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Cargoes were shown by sample at the Deigma in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a Spartan raiding party once leapt ashore there and carried merchants off to its ships.","questions":["Xenophon would have given shipowners front seats at the theatre to draw more trade to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose moorings he called the finest and safest anywhere.","Socrates says he went down to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to pray to a goddess whose festival was then being held for the first time, in the opening line of the Republic."],"answer":"Piraeus","answerText":"Piraeus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eetioneia wall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">411 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deigma raided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">388 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Piraeus</b> was the harbour town of Athens and the busiest port in the Greek world, whose moorings Xenophon calls the finest and safest anywhere, where ships could ride out bad weather at anchor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Athens courted the merchants who used it, and Xenophon would have offered prizes to the market officials who settled disputes quickest, so that sailings were not held up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He would also have given shipowners front seats at the theatre and entertained them publicly, so that they came for the honour as well as the profit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Business was done at the Deigma, the exchange where cargoes were shown by sample, and when a Spartan squadron raided the port in 388 BCE its men leapt ashore there and carried merchants and shipowners off to the ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias found the place with a market of its own for those living by the sea, a long portico, a precinct of Athena and Zeus in bronze, and a sanctuary of Aphrodite that Conon had built after Cnidus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The entrance was commanded from Eetioneia, the pier beside the harbour mouth, where in 411 BCE the Four Hundred built a wall so placed that a few men inside it could shut the port.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Theramenes said aloud what it was for, not to keep the fleet at Samos out but to let the enemy's ships and army in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beside the wall they raised a great open gallery and made all the grain already landed be brought there, and everything that came in afterwards be unloaded and sold from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The port also carried what the city did not, and Plato opens the <i>Republic</i> there, with Socrates gone down to pray to a goddess whose festival was then being held for the first time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He thought the citizens' procession fine and the marching of the Thracian contingent no worse, a foreign rite honoured in the harbour town at its own first festival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["place","history","greece","athens","geography"],"sources":["Xenophon, <i>Ways and Means</i> 3.1-4, trans. E. C. Marchant, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Ways%203.1. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 5.1.21, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Hell.%205.1.21. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.1.3, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.1.3. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 8.90, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=8:chapter=90. [Open access]","Plato, <i>Republic</i> 327a, trans. Paul Shorey, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plat.%20Rep.%20327a. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Remains_of_the_ancient_harbour_of_Zea_in_Piraeus_on_24_February_2019.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_the_ancient_harbour_of_Zea_in_Piraeus_on_24_February_2019.jpg","title":"Ancient stonework at Zea","desc":"Cut blocks at the water's edge of Zea, one of the harbours of Piraeus, with the modern town standing over them. What the stones belonged to is not settled; the uploader records them as fortifications with a question mark.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_the_ancient_harbour_of_Zea_in_Piraeus_on_24_February_2019.jpg","alt":"Weathered squared blocks of pale stone lying in shallow green water and on the bank above it, with pines and a palm behind and modern apartment blocks rising over them."},"locator":{"name":"Piraeus","at":[23.6469,37.943]},"why":[{"q":"Why did Athens court the merchants who used the port?","a":"Because Xenophon would have given prizes to the officials who settled disputes quickest and front seats at the theatre to shipowners, so that they came for the honour as well as the profit."},{"q":"Why was a wall at the harbour mouth read as treason?","a":"Because Theramenes said aloud what it was for, not to keep the fleet at Samos out but to let the enemy's ships and army in, a few men inside it being able to shut the port."},{"q":"Why is the opening of the Republic set down there?","a":"Because Socrates had gone down to pray at a goddess's festival held then for the first time, a foreign rite honoured in the harbour town."}]},
{"id":"gr-488","num":488,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Cratinus joked that Pericles had for ever so long been pushing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on in words while in fact never budging the thing.","questions":["When Attica was abandoned the country people camped in temples and towers, and afterwards the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were divided among them to live along.","Some Athenians privately invited a Spartan army into the city, hoping to end the democracy and demolish the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that were then being built."],"answer":"Long Walls","answerText":"Long Walls","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 458 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">To Piraeus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 stades</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pulled down</span><span class=\"dt-v\">404 BCE, to the flutes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>Long Walls</b> were the fortifications the Athenians began about the middle of the 5th century BCE to join their city to the sea, one running down to the haven of Phaleron and the other to Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were a party question from the start, for while a Spartan army sat in Boeotia some Athenians privately invited it in, hoping to put down the democracy and pull down the walls then building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third wall was added later beside the one to Piraeus, and Plutarch names Callicrates as its contractor and reports that Socrates had heard Pericles propose the measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The comic poet Cratinus mocked how slowly it went, saying that Pericles had for ever so long been pushing the thing on in words while in fact he did not budge it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thucydides gives the measurements Pericles laid before the assembly: the Phaleric wall 35 stades, the walls down to Piraeus 40, and the whole circuit of Piraeus with Munychia 60.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He records which stretches were manned as well, a watch on the outermost of the long walls only, and on half the circuit of Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the war came and Attica was given up to the enemy, the country people crowded into a city that had no room for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They camped in the empty places and the temples and the shrines of the heroes, in the turrets of the walls, and even in the Pelasgicum, which an oracle had said was best left empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Afterwards the Long Walls themselves were divided among them, and they lived along them and through most of Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The surrender of 404 BCE took the walls down, and Xenophon says the Peloponnesians fell to the work with enthusiasm to the music of flute-girls, thinking that day the beginning of freedom for Greece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["building","history","greece","athens","warfare"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.107, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=107. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13.5, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013.5. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.17, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=17. [Open access]","Xenophon, <i>Hellenica</i> 2.2.23, trans. Carleton L. Brownson, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Xen.%20Hell.%202.2.23. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Remains_of_two_towers_from_the_Walls_of_Conon_%28Akti_Themistokleous%2C_Piraeus%29.jpg/1280px-Remains_of_two_towers_from_the_Walls_of_Conon_%28Akti_Themistokleous%2C_Piraeus%29.jpg","title":"What was built back afterwards","desc":"Towers of the Walls of Conon on the shore at Piraeus. These belong to the rebuilding of the early 4th century BCE, after the walls the card describes had been pulled down.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, CC0 1.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_two_towers_from_the_Walls_of_Conon_%28Akti_Themistokleous%2C_Piraeus%29.jpg","alt":"Two low squared-stone tower stubs standing on a shelf of bare rock above the sea, with a modern seafront wall and apartment block behind and hazy hills across the water."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the walls a party question while they were being built?","a":"Because while a Spartan army sat in Boeotia some Athenians privately invited it in, hoping to put down the democracy and pull down the walls then building."},{"q":"Why did the walls end up lived in?","a":"Because when Attica was given up the country people crowded into a city with no room for them, camping in temples, turrets and the Pelasgicum, and the Long Walls were then divided among them."},{"q":"Why did the Peloponnesians pull them down to music?","a":"Because they thought that day the beginning of freedom for Greece, and fell to the work with enthusiasm to the flute-girls' playing."}]},
{"id":"gr-489","num":489,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Aristotle credits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with inventing the division of cities into blocks and says he cut up Piraeus, while noting his long hair and fussy reputation.","questions":["The city planned by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held ten thousand people in three classes and divided its land into sacred, common and private portions.","Straight streets were the modern fashion introduced by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Aristotle thought pleasanter but less safe than the tangled plan of older towns."],"answer":"Hippodamus of Miletus","answerText":"Hippodamus of Miletus","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">His city</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10,000 people in 3 classes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lawsuit kinds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3: outrage, damage, homicide</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Hippodamus of Miletus</b>, son of Euryphon, is the man Aristotle credits with inventing the division of cities into blocks, and with cutting up Piraeus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Aristotle thought him an oddity too, wearing his hair long and expensive ornaments with it, the same cheap but warm clothes in summer as in winter, and wanting to be thought learned about nature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was, on the same account, the first man outside politics to try to say what the best constitution would be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His city held ten thousand people in three classes, artisans, farmers and the armed men who fought for it, on land divided three ways: sacred to pay for offerings, common to feed the soldiers, private for the farmers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He held that lawsuits were of only three kinds, outrage, damage and homicide, and would have set one supreme court of chosen elders to hear the cases thought to have been decided wrongly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He would also have had each juror write a verdict on a tablet rather than drop a ballot, so that one who thought the accused guilty on some counts and not others need not perjure himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He proposed honours for anyone who found out something useful to the city, and public maintenance for the children of men killed in war, which Aristotle notes was already law at Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On streets Aristotle is divided: houses laid out straight, in the modern fashion Hippodamus introduced, are pleasanter and handier, while the tangled plan of old towns is safer, being hard for a foreign army to find its way about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own answer was to lay out only parts of a city straight and the rest on the slant, as some farmers plant vines, and so have both beauty and security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Later writers gave him more than Aristotle does: Strabo says Rhodes was founded by the same architect as Piraeus, adding ‘as they say', and Diodorus describes Thurii cut lengthwise by four named streets and across by three without naming him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["person","history","greece","architecture","politics"],"sources":["Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1267b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%202.1267b. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1268a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%202.1268a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 7.1330b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%207.1330b. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 14.2.9, trans. Horace Leonard Jones, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0198:book=14:chapter=2:section=9. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.10, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0084:book=12:chapter=10. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Aristotle think the straight plan a mixed blessing?","a":"Because houses laid out straight are pleasanter and handier, while the tangled plan of old towns is safer, being hard for a foreign army to find its way about."},{"q":"Why would he have jurors write a verdict instead of dropping a ballot?","a":"Because a juror who thought the accused guilty on some counts and not others need not then perjure himself by casting a single vote either way."},{"q":"Why do later writers credit him with more than Aristotle does?","a":"Because Strabo says Rhodes was founded by the same architect as Piraeus while adding 'as they say', and Diodorus describes the streets of Thurii without naming him at all."}]},
{"id":"gr-490","num":490,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"The state paid each man of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crew a drachma a day, and the trierarchs added more of their own for the thranites of the uppermost bank.","questions":["Herodotus reckons two hundred men to a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them rowers, an art the Old Oligarch says Athenians picked up almost without noticing.","The crew of one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ate barley cakes kneaded with oil and wine at the oar and slept by turns, and reached Mytilene as the sentence was being read."],"answer":"trireme","answerText":"trireme","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 BCE, at Corinth</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 200 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pay</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 drachma a day</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>trireme</b> was the oared warship on which Athenian power rested, a galley rowed at three levels whose uppermost bank of rowers were called thranites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Thucydides says the Corinthians were the first in Greece to build ships in the form then in use, and that Ameinocles the Corinthian shipwright made four for the Samians about three hundred years before the end of his war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He puts the oldest sea battle he knows of, between the Corinthians and the Corcyraeans, 260 years before that same point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A ship was reckoned at two hundred men, the figure Herodotus uses for the vessels of the Persian fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of them pulled an oar, and the Old Oligarch says the Athenians came by the skill almost without noticing, since a man often at sea takes up an oar, and so does his slave, and both learn the language of ships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The state paid each man of a crew a drachma a day, and the trierarchs added something of their own for the thranites and for their servants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They spent on the ships as well, on badges and rigging, each trying to have the handsomest and the swiftest galley in the fleet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a rower carried was little enough to walk to another sea with: when the Peloponnesians planned a raid on Piraeus, every man was to take his oar, his cushion and the thong his oar turned in, and cross from Corinth on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The best measure of what the ship could do is the second galley sent to Mytilene, whose crew were promised a reward and rowed on barley cakes kneaded with oil and wine, eating at the oar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They slept and rowed by turns, no wind blew against them, and they came in just as the sentence was being read out, so that the city was saved by the margin of that one crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["object","history","greece","warfare","technology"],"sources":["Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 1.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=1:chapter=13. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>Histories</i> 7.184, trans. A. D. Godley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0126:book=7:chapter=184. [Open access]","[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.19, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=19. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.31, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=31. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.93, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=93. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 3.49, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=3:chapter=49. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg/1280px-ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg","title":"The Lenormant relief","desc":"A fragment of a marble relief in the Acropolis Museum, Athens, showing nine rowers of an Athenian trireme. The heads and arms of the upper bank stand above the outrigger, with the oars of the lower banks running out beneath them.","credit":"Photograph by Marsyas, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_Relief_Lenormant.jpg","alt":"A broken block of pale marble carved in low relief with a row of seated rowers seen from the side, their oars sloping down in close ranks below them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Athenians take to the oar so readily?","a":"Because a man often at sea takes up an oar, and so does his slave, and both learn the language of ships, so the skill came almost without their noticing."},{"q":"Why could a crew cross the Isthmus on foot to another sea?","a":"Because a rower carried only his oar, his cushion and the thong his oar turned in, the hull being what stayed behind."},{"q":"Why is the second ship to Mytilene the measure of what the vessel could do?","a":"Because its crew were promised a reward and rowed on barley cakes kneaded with oil and wine, eating at the oar and sleeping by turns, arriving as the sentence was being read."}]},
{"id":"gr-491","num":491,"category":"Athenian Empire","question":"Apollodorus says he mortgaged his property to man his ship first, hiring the best rowers with bonuses, which is what one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could cost the man who bore it.","questions":["The state gave the hull and a drachma a day to each man, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laid the rest of the cost on one rich citizen for a year.","Demosthenes accuses two men of hiring out their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to whoever would take it cheapest and then asking for the crown."],"answer":"trierarchy","answerText":"trierarchy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sicilian fleet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">415 BCE, trierarchs vying</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Term</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 year per ship</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One man's bill</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 talents over 7 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>trierarchy</b> was the duty laid on a rich Athenian to command a warship for a year and keep her in service largely at his own charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Old Oligarch puts the arithmetic of it plainly: the wealthy fit out the triremes, and the people are carried in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state provided the hull and paid each man of the crew a drachma a day, and the trierarch found the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the fleet that sailed for Sicily the trierarchs added something of their own for the thranites and their servants, and spent besides on badges and rigging, each trying to have the handsomest and swiftest ship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An Athenian law of the last years of the war shows the duty as machinery: a trierarch who did not receive the rigging or the wooden gear from his predecessor could summon him before the superintendents on two witnesses, and the case went to court the next day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Apollodorus describes what bearing it took: the sailors his demesmen had listed did not appear except a very few, and those incompetent, so he dismissed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He mortgaged his property and borrowed money to be the first to man his ship, hiring the best sailors he could get with large bonuses and advance payments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He furnished her wholly from his own stores, taking nothing from the public ones, and made everything as magnificent as possible, outdoing all the other trierarchs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What that came to is stated by a speaker in Lysias, who says that for seven years he equipped warships at a cost of six talents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Not everyone served in person, and Demosthenes says of two men that they sought out whoever would take their trierarchy on the lowest terms and let the service to him, and then asked for the crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","warfare"],"sources":["[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.13, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=13. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 6.31, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=6:chapter=31. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Polycles</i> 7, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2050%207. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Defence Against a Charge of Taking Bribes</i> 2, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Lys.%2021%202. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>On the Trierarchic Crown</i> 7, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2051%207. [Open access]","Law about the Trierarchy, 410-404 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 236a), trans. Stephen Lambert, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/236a. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did the duty cost more than the state's own outlay?","a":"Because the state provided the hull and a drachma a day for each man, and the trierarch found the rest — bonuses for sailors, badges and rigging, each competing for the handsomest and swiftest ship."},{"q":"Why was a law needed about handing a ship on?","a":"Because a trierarch who did not receive the rigging or the wooden gear from his predecessor could summon him before the superintendents on two witnesses, with the case heard the next day."},{"q":"Why could the honour be had without the service?","a":"Because Demosthenes says of two men that they sought out whoever would take their trierarchy on the lowest terms, let the service to him, and then asked for the crown."}]},
{"id":"gr-492","num":492,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A speaker in Lysias lists thirty minae for a tragic chorus and five thousand drachmae for a chorus at the Dionysia, each of them one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> he had borne.","questions":["Aristotle says the archon appointed the three wealthiest men in Athens to lead the tragic choruses and heard claims that a second <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had fallen too soon after the first.","A man who thought a richer citizen should bear a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in his place could offer to exchange estates with him, a procedure a speaker in Demosthenes credits to Solon."],"answer":"liturgy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tragic chorus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 minae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dionysia chorus</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,000 drachmae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Promethea games</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 minae</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Choregic stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 440 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>liturgy</b> was a duty laid on a rich Athenian to pay for something the city wanted out of his own purse, and to be seen paying. Aristotle's <i>Athenian Constitution</i> shows the burden being placed: the archon appointed three chorus-leaders for the tragedies, the wealthiest men in Athens, and received those the tribes returned for the other competitions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He heard their pleas of exemption too, from having done the service before, from one not yet expired, or from age, a chorus-leader for the boys having to be over forty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A man who thought a richer citizen should carry it instead could offer to exchange estates with him, and a speaker in Demosthenes blesses Solon for laying down the order of that procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The load fell unevenly: Demosthenes says some services fell on resident aliens and others on citizens, but that from war levies and warship duty nobody was exempt, not even the descendants of Harmodius and Aristogiton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> What one cost is itemised by a speaker in Lysias: thirty minae on a tragic chorus, two thousand drachmae at the Thargelia, eight hundred on pyrrhic dancers at the Great Panathenaea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Then five thousand drachmae on a men's chorus at the Dionysia, the tripod included, three hundred on a cyclic chorus at the Little Panathenaea,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and twelve minae on games at the Promethea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adds fifteen minae for winning the warship race at Sunium and more than thirty on sacred missions and processions, and says that by the letter of the law he need not have spent a quarter of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The work was as real as the money: Antiphon's choregos fitted a training-room in the best part of his house and recruited his chorus without indicting a fine or extorting a pledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the outlay bought stands on a stone from Anagyrous, where Sokrates records his dedication, names Euripides as director, and lists fourteen members of the tragic chorus beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"liturgy","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","greece","athens","politics","religion"],"sources":["Lysias, <i>Defence Against a Charge of Taking Bribes</i> 1–2, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Lys.%2021%201. [Open access]","Lysias, <i>Defence Against a Charge of Taking Bribes</i> 3–5, trans. W. R. M. Lamb, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Lys.%2021%203. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 56, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0046:chapter=56. [Open access]","Antiphon, <i>On the Chorus Boy</i> 11–13, trans. K. J. Maidment, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Antiph.%206%2011. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Phaenippus</i> 1, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2042%201. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Leptines</i> 18, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Dem.%2020%2018. [Open access]","Choregic dedication from Anagyrous, after ca. 440 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 969), trans. Irene Vagionakis and Claudio Huayna Avila, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/969. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/20100410_athina153.JPG/1920px-20100410_athina153.JPG","title":"The Choragic Monument of Lysicrates","desc":"The monument raised in Athens in 335/4 BCE to carry the bronze tripod Lysicrates won as sponsor of the victorious boys' chorus at the Dionysia; the prize tripod stood on the acanthus finial at the top.","credit":"Photograph by Jean Housen, 2010, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20100410_athina153.JPG","alt":"A small round marble monument with engaged columns and a carved finial, standing on a square stone base in a sunlit square with neoclassical houses behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why could a man be made to swap estates over one of these duties?","a":"Because he could answer that a richer citizen should carry it instead by offering to exchange property with him, a procedure whose order Solon was thanked for laying down."},{"q":"Why were the pleas of exemption part of the system?","a":"Because the archon heard them — from having done the service before, from one not yet expired, or from age — while from war levies and warship duty nobody was exempt at all."},{"q":"Why was the work as real as the money?","a":"Because a chorus-leader fitted a training-room in the best part of his house and recruited his chorus without indicting a fine or extorting a pledge."}]},
{"id":"gr-493","num":493,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Aristotle says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the naval multitude that won at Salamis and gave Athens her power at sea, made the democracy stronger than Solon had ever designed.","questions":["The Old Oligarch grants the poor their say because they man the ships, which is the argument behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that Aristotle would rather have kept out of the citizen body.","At Samos the fleet swore itself to democracy against the Four Hundred, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could do once Athens had put her power on the water."],"answer":"nautikos ochlos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Salamis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fed by tribute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20,000 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dock guardians</span><span class=\"dt-v\">500 men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Guard ships</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20, and 200 more</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>nautikos ochlos</b>, the naval crowd, is Aristotle's name for the poor Athenians who rowed the fleet and for the political weight rowing gave them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His <i>Politics</i> puts it in a clause: the naval multitude, cause of the victory off Salamis and thereby of the leadership of Athens by sea, made the democracy stronger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He treats that as accident rather than design: the people, having caused the naval victories of the Persian invasion, grew proud and took bad men as leaders, where Solon had given the fourth class no office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Old Oligarch says the same from the other side and without apology, that the poor are right to have more than the highborn because it is they who man the ships and give the city its strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He names the steersmen, boatswains, sub-boatswains, look-outs and shipwrights, and says these strengthen the city far more than the hoplites do, so that everyone should share the offices and speak his mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The empire paid for it: the <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> says the tributes, the taxes and the allies together fed more than twenty thousand men, including six thousand jurymen and five hundred guardians of the docks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same list counts twenty guard-ships and two hundred more ships whose crews were chosen by lot, all maintained from public funds beside the archers, cavalry and council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Aristotle drew the opposite conclusion from the same facts, writing that a state need not include the teeming population growing up around common sailors, since there is no need for these to be citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What that could come to was shown at Samos, where Thrasybulus and Thrasyllus bound the whole force by the most tremendous oaths to accept democracy and be enemies of the Four Hundred at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The soldiers deposed the generals they suspected and chose others, telling one another that they had the whole fleet and a city in Samos, and had lost nothing in losing men who could send them no money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"nautikos ochlos","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","history","greece","athens","politics","warfare"],"sources":["[Xenophon], <i>Constitution of the Athenians</i> 1.2, in <i>Xenophon in Seven Volumes</i>, vol. 7, ed. E. C. Marchant (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1984), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0158:chapter=1:section=2. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 5.1304a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%205.1304a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Aristotelis Politica</i> 5.1304a, ed. W. D. Ross (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1957), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0057:book=5:section=1304a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 2.1274a, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%202.1274a. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 24, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Ath.%20Pol.%2024. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Politics</i> 7.1327b, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Pol.%207.1327b. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 8.75, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=8:chapter=75. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 8.76, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=8:chapter=76. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does Aristotle treat their power as an accident?","a":"Because the naval multitude caused the victory off Salamis and thereby the leadership of Athens by sea, then grew proud and took bad men as leaders, where Solon had given the fourth class no office."},{"q":"Why does the Old Oligarch grant the case without apology?","a":"Because it is the poor who man the ships and give the city its strength, steersmen, boatswains, look-outs and shipwrights strengthening it far more than the hoplites do."},{"q":"Why could the fleet at Samos act as a city of its own?","a":"Because the soldiers deposed the generals they suspected and chose others, telling one another that they had the whole fleet and a city in Samos, and had lost nothing in losing men who could send them no money."}]},
{"id":"gr-494","num":494,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch says the works of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were each thought to need many generations and were all finished inside a single administration, which is what made them a wonder.","questions":["Pericles offered to pay for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> himself and put his own name on the dedications, at which the assembly told him instead to spare no public expense.","Accounts cut on stone in 434 BCE record the receipts and wages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, set against payments to the workers at Pentele who also placed the stones."],"answer":"Periclean building programme","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Accounts cut</span><span class=\"dt-v\">434 BCE, at 25,000 dr.</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Propylaea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built in 5 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Acropolis grant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 talents a year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Periclean building programme</b> is the work Athens put up on the Acropolis and elsewhere in the middle of the 5th century BCE. Plutarch gives his argument: once the city was equipped for the war she should apply her abundance to works that would bring everlasting glory when finished and, in progress, bring the whole city under pay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The point was to reach the men who did not sail or serve, so that the unwarlike throng of labourers should be neither shut out of the public receipts nor paid for idleness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lists the materials, stone and bronze and ivory and gold and ebony and cypress, and the trades: carpenter, moulder, bronze-smith, stone-cutter, dyer, painter, embroiderer, embosser.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thucydides son of Melesias said he was playing fast and loose with the public money, so Pericles offered to pay for the works himself and inscribe his own name on them, at which the assembly bade him spare no expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What Plutarch found most wonderful was the speed, since each work was thought to need many generations and all were finished within a single administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Phidias was general overseer and the works had architects of their own: Callicrates and Ictinus for the Parthenon, Coroebus then Metagenes then Xenocles at Eleusis, Mnesicles for the Propylaea, finished in five years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Odeon, roofed in a circular slope from one peak, was said to reproduce the Great King's pavilion,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and Pausanias found the gateway's marble roof still unrivalled for the beauty and size of its stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The money was decreed rather than merely spent: the decrees Callias proposed order ten talents a year on the Acropolis, leave the plan to the architect of the Propylaea, and forbid Athena's other funds to be drawn on beyond ten thousand drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The accounts were published yearly: the Parthenon's for 434 BCE set receipts of 25,000 drachmas and gold staters of Lampsacus and Cyzicus against purchases, wages, and payments to the workers at Pentele who placed the stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Periclean building programme","difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","history","greece","athens","architecture","politics"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 12, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2012. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 14, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2014%201. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.22.4, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.22.4. [Open access]","Decrees relating to the treasury of the Other Gods (Kallias' decrees), 434/3 BC (OR 144), trans. Stephen Lambert and P. J. Rhodes, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/OR/144. [Open access]","Building accounts of the Parthenon, 434/3 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 449), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/449. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/2016-08-03_Greece%2C_Athens%2C_Acropolis%2C_Propylaea_DSC_9901_DxO_1.jpg/1920px-2016-08-03_Greece%2C_Athens%2C_Acropolis%2C_Propylaea_DSC_9901_DxO_1.jpg","title":"The Propylaea, and the temple beside it","desc":"Mnesicles' gateway to the Acropolis on the left, brought to completion in five years, with the small temple of Athena Nike on its bastion to the right.","credit":"Photograph by Laurens R. Krol, 2016, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016-08-03_Greece,_Athens,_Acropolis,_Propylaea_DSC_9901_DxO_1.jpg","alt":"Marble columns and steps of a ruined gateway in warm evening light, with a small colonnaded temple standing on a stone bastion beyond it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the works have to reach men who did not row or serve?","a":"Because the unwarlike throng of labourers was to be neither shut out of the public receipts nor paid for idleness, so the building brought the whole city under pay."},{"q":"Why did an offer to pay privately silence the criticism?","a":"Because Pericles proposed to meet the cost himself and inscribe his own name on the works, at which the assembly bade him spare no expense."},{"q":"Why is the speed of the programme what Plutarch found most wonderful?","a":"Because each work was thought to need many generations, and all of them were finished within a single administration."}]},
{"id":"gr-495","num":495,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Built between 447 and 438 BCE on the Athenian acropolis, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the Doric temple of Athena whose colonnade runs eight columns across the ends and seventeen along the flanks.","questions":["The Athenians' own inventories call the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the hundred-foot temple and hand over its gold dishes and silver bowls from one board of treasurers to the next each Panathenaia.","Venetian bombardment in 1687 fired the gunpowder stored inside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had already served as a temple, a church and a mosque in turn."],"answer":"Parthenon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 438 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pediments done</span><span class=\"dt-v\">432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Columns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 by 17</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wrecked</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1687 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Parthenon</b> is the Doric temple of Athena on the Athenian acropolis, and the first building the city dedicated to her after the decision of 448 BCE to rebuild the sanctuaries the Persians had destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is peripteral, with eight columns at the narrow ends and seventeen along the flanks, and it stands on the site of an earlier temple begun between 490 and 480 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias, who called it the temple they name the Parthenon, found the birth of Athena worked in one gable and her contest with Poseidon for the land in the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The architects are given as Ictinus and Callicrates with Phidias in general charge,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and Pausanias says elsewhere that Ictinus was a contemporary of Pericles who also built the temple at Phigalia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vitruvius reports that Ictinus and Carpion published a book on the Doric temple of Minerva on the acropolis of Athens, so the men who raised it set out its proportions themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Plutarch calls its cella a hundred feet long,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the city's own inventories use that name, handing the contents of the <i>hekatompedon</i> temple from one board of treasurers to the next at each Panathenaia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What they list is treasure rather than furniture: three gold dishes weighing 2,544 drachmas, a gold maiden on a stele that nobody weighed, and a silver incense-burner of a thousand drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The front porch was inventoried separately, with 121 silver libation bowls at two talents and 432 drachmas, three silver drinking horns, and a golden bowl for lustrations left unweighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The building carries a set of refinements meant to lighten the Doric order: the curve of the crepis and the stylobate, the entasis of the columns, tighter corner intercolumniations, and metopes narrowing towards the corners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stood whole for centuries, took serious fire damage in late Roman times, served as a church and then as a mosque, and was wrecked in 1687 CE when a Venetian bombardment fired the gunpowder stored inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Parthenon","difficulty":1,"tags":["building","history","greece","athens","architecture","religion"],"sources":["“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.24.5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.9. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 7, preface 12, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D7. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Inventory of the Hekatompedon, 418/7 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 329), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/329. [Open access]","Inventory of the Pronaos, 426/5-423/2 BC and 414/3-411/0 BC (AIUK 4.4 no. 3), trans. Peter Liddel and Polly Low, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIUK44/3. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/1010_Parthenon_of_the_Acropolis_of_Athens_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/1920px-1010_Parthenon_of_the_Acropolis_of_Athens_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg","title":"The Parthenon from one corner","desc":"The temple seen at an angle, so that the columns of the short end and the ring running down the long flank are both in view; the frame at the left is restoration work.","credit":"Photograph by Giles Laurent, 2025, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1010_Parthenon_of_the_Acropolis_of_Athens_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg","alt":"A roofless marble temple on a rock, its fluted columns standing in two rows meeting at a corner under a grey sky, with fallen blocks in the foreground."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the city's records call the building by another name?","a":"Because the inventories hand on the contents of the hekatompedon temple, the hundred-foot cella, from one board of treasurers to the next at each Panathenaia."},{"q":"Why did the builders set out their own proportions in a book?","a":"Because Vitruvius reports that Ictinus and Carpion published one on the Doric temple of Minerva on the acropolis, so the refinements were meant to be understood as a scheme."},{"q":"Why did a building that stood for two millennia come down when it did?","a":"Because in 1687 a Venetian bombardment fired the gunpowder that was being stored inside it."}]},
{"id":"gr-496","num":496,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"An inscription under the feet of the Zeus at Olympia read that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, son of Charmides, an Athenian, made me, which is the only signature Pausanias records there.","questions":["Plutarch says an assistant named Menon was put up to accuse <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of embezzlement, which failed because the statue's gold could all be taken off and weighed.","The bronze Athena that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made from the Marathon tithe carried a spear-point and helmet-crest that sailors could see once they had passed Sunium."],"answer":"Phidias","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Acropolis art</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 432 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Phidias</b> was the Athenian sculptor who had charge of the art of the Periclean acropolis<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> and made the gold and ivory Zeus at Olympia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plutarch says he was contractor for the great statue at Athens, is inscribed on the tablet as the workman who made it, and had almost everything under his superintendence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pausanias saw a bronze Athena of his there, a tithe from the Persians who landed at Marathon, its spear-point and helmet-crest visible to men sailing to Athens once they had passed Sunium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage calls the Lemnian Athena the work of his best worth seeing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Olympia temple and image alike were made from spoils taken when Pisa was crushed by the Eleans, and an inscription under the feet of Zeus read: Phidias, son of Charmides, an Athenian, made me.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The god sat on a throne in gold and ivory, a garland copying olive shoots on his head, a Victory of ivory and gold in his right hand and a sceptre in his left with an eagle on it, his sandals and robe of gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Pausanias would not praise the men who had measured it, their records falling far short of the impression it made, and reports that the sculptor prayed for a sign and a thunderbolt fell where a bronze jar still covered the floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Outside the sacred grove he was shown the building called the workshop, where the image had been wrought piece by piece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At Athens it ended badly: an assistant named Menon was put up to accuse him, and embezzlement could not be proved, the gold having been cast about the statue on Pericles' advice so that it could all be taken off and weighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> What told against him was the shield, where he had carved a bald old man lifting a stone who suggested himself, and a likeness of Pericles fighting an Amazon; he was led to prison and died there of sickness, or of poison as some say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Phidias","difficulty":2,"tags":["person","history","greece","athens","art","religion"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.28.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.28.2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.10.2, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.10.2. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.11.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.11.1. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.11.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.11.9. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 5.15.1, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%205.15.1. [Open access]","Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 31, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2031%202. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/View_of_the_Workshop_of_Pheidias_in_Ancient_Olympia_on_October_14%2C_2020.jpg/1920px-View_of_the_Workshop_of_Pheidias_in_Ancient_Olympia_on_October_14%2C_2020.jpg","title":"The workshop at Olympia","desc":"The site identified as the building Pausanias was shown as Phidias' workshop; the standing brick walls are those of an Early Christian basilica later raised within it, and the stone bases in front are ancient.","credit":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios, 2020, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Workshop_of_Pheidias_in_Ancient_Olympia_on_October_14,_2020.jpg","alt":"Two tall walls of ancient brick standing in a sunlit grove, with rows of squared stone blocks and column bases laid out on the ground before them."},"why":[{"q":"Why could the charge of embezzlement not be proved?","a":"Because on Pericles' advice the gold had been cast about the statue so that it could all be taken off and weighed."},{"q":"Why did the shield destroy him where the accounts did not?","a":"Because he had carved on it a bald old man lifting a stone who suggested himself, and a likeness of Pericles fighting an Amazon."},{"q":"Why would Pausanias not repeat the measurements of the Zeus?","a":"Because he thought the records of the men who had measured it fell far short of the impression the image made."}]},
{"id":"gr-497","num":497,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Pericles told the assembly that the forty talents of pure gold on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were all removable, a reserve the city might spend on itself and afterwards restore.","questions":["Pausanias saw the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> standing in a tunic to the feet, a Victory four cubits high in one hand and the birth of Pandora carved on its pedestal.","The final account for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is cut in letters nearly three times the ordinary height, so that gods and men alike should read what it had cost."],"answer":"Athena Parthenos","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Installed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">438 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold on it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 – 50 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Total cost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700 – 1,000 talents</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nike held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 cubits high</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Athena Parthenos</b> was the colossal gold and ivory statue of Athena that stood inside the Parthenon, made by Phidias and apparently installed in 438 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Athenians accounted for it apart from the temple, under a board of overseers of its own, and the surviving fragments record gold bought by weight: six talents, 1,618 drachmas and one obol in a single entry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The price paid shows silver exchanged for that gold at fourteen to one, the same ratio the accounts give whenever surplus gold was sold off again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The final summary account of 438/7 BCE is cut in extraordinary letters, the totals nearly three times the height of the ordinary script on a carefully polished surface, and was plainly meant to state to gods and men what the thing had cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Enough of that total survives to show it lay between 700 and 1,000 talents, against a weight of gold given as 44 talents by Philochorus, rounded to 40 by Thucydides and put at 50 by Diodorus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Pausanias saw the statue standing upright in a tunic reaching to the feet, with the head of Medusa worked in ivory on the breast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She held a Victory about four cubits high in one hand and a spear in the other, a shield lay at her feet and a serpent near the spear, which he took for Erichthonius, and the birth of Pandora was in relief on the pedestal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the middle of her helmet was set a likeness of the Sphinx, and on either side of it griffins in relief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city's treasurers weighed what she carried, an inventory of the hundred-foot temple listing a gold crown which Nike has at sixty drachmas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pericles told the assembly that the gold on the goddess was all removable, a reserve the city might spend on its own preservation provided every penny of it was afterwards restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Athena Parthenos","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","history","greece","athens","art","religion"],"sources":["Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.5, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.24.5. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 1.24.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%201.24.7. [Open access]","Thucydides, <i>History of the Peloponnesian War</i> 2.13, trans. Richard Crawley, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0200:book=2:chapter=13. [Open access]","Accounts of Pheidias' statue of Athena, 445-438 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 458), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/458. [Open access]","Accounts of Pheidias' statue of Athena, final summary account, 438/7 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 460), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/460. [Open access]","Inventory of the Hekatompedon, 418/7 BC (IG I<sup>3</sup> 329), trans. Stephen Lambert and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/IGI3/329. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Varvakeion_Athena.jpg/1280px-Varvakeion_Athena.jpg","title":"The Varvakeion Athena","desc":"A small Roman marble statuette in Athens, the closest surviving reflection of the lost statue: the helmet with its sphinx and griffins, the aegis and Gorgon head, the Victory on a column and the shield with the serpent coiled inside it.","credit":"Photograph by Nikos Kitsakis, 2024, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varvakeion_Athena.jpg","alt":"A marble statuette of a helmeted goddess in a long tunic, a small winged figure on a column in her right hand and a round shield with a coiled snake at her left side."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the final accounts cut in outsized letters?","a":"Because the totals were nearly three times the height of the ordinary script on a carefully polished surface, plainly meant to state to gods and men what the thing had cost."},{"q":"Why is the weight of gold used given three different figures?","a":"Because Philochorus gives 44 talents, Thucydides rounds it to 40 and Diodorus puts it at 50, so the surviving accounts fix the price better than the mass."},{"q":"Why was the gold made removable?","a":"Because Pericles told the assembly it was a reserve the city might spend on its own preservation, provided every penny of it was afterwards restored."}]},
{"id":"gr-498","num":498,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"A band of 115 blocks running 160 metres (525 feet) round the top of the cella wall, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries some 378 figures and at least 220 animals.","questions":["The ancient sources that mention the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are all later than the building and disagree with it at points, so what moment it shows is still argued.","Above the east door the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows the peplos being handed over between the seated Olympian gods, which is where both halves of the procession arrive."],"answer":"Parthenon frieze","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Blocks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">115, running 160 m (525 feet)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.02 m (3 feet 4 inches)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sculpture done</span><span class=\"dt-v\">432 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elgin took</span><span class=\"dt-v\">56 blocks</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Parthenon frieze</b> is the continuous band of relief that ran round the top of the cella wall inside the outer colonnade, an Ionic frieze on a Doric building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was 115 blocks long, about 160 metres (525 feet) in all and 1.02 metres (3 feet 4 inches) high,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the temple's sculpture was finished six years after the building itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Some 378 human and divine figures move across it with at least 220 animals, most of them horses, whose weapons and bridle attachments were once applied in bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The procession begins at the south-west corner and divides into two files, one along the south side and one across the west end and up the north, both ending above the door at the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is shown is taken to be the procession of the Great Panathenaia, held every fourth year in the month of Hekatombaion, whose ceremonies reached their height on the day held to be Athena's birthday.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The east end is nine blocks, longer than those of the other sides, and has a symmetry the rest lacks, being where both files arrive and where the peplos is handed over between the seated Olympian gods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That robe was a public commission: ten Stewards of the Games, chosen by lot and holding office for four years, administered the procession and the contests and had the Robe made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An Athenian decree of 108 BCE honours the maidens who worked the wool for it, who had walked in the procession as prescribed and dedicated a silver bowl worth a hundred drachmas out of their own means.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The reading is not secure: the ancient sources that mention the frieze are all later than the building, and the carving leaves out the Panathenaic ship on whose mast the peplos was raised like a sail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is scattered, some 50 metres (164 feet) of it in Athens and 80 metres (262 feet) in London, one block in Paris and fragments in five other museums,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> after Lord Elgin took 56 blocks away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Parthenon frieze","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","art","history","greece","athens","religion"],"sources":["“About the frieze,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/about-the-frieze/. [Open access]","“East frieze,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/explore-the-frieze/east-frieze/. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]","Aristotle, <i>Athenian Constitution</i> 60, trans. H. Rackham, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Aristot.%20Ath.%20Pol.%2060. [Open access]","Honours for the girls who worked on the robe for Athena, 108/7 BC (AIUK 1 no. 1), trans. Stephen Lambert, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIUK1/1. [Open access]","“West frieze,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/explore-the-frieze/west-frieze/. [Open access]","“The Parthenon,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/the-parthenon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Peplos_scene_BM_EastV_cropped.JPG/1920px-Peplos_scene_BM_EastV_cropped.JPG","title":"The peplos scene","desc":"Block V of the east frieze, the central scene above the door: two maidens carrying stools on their heads, a woman turned towards them, and a man folding a cloth with a child.","credit":"Photograph by Twospoonfuls, 2008, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 3.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peplos_scene_BM_EastV_cropped.JPG","alt":"A weathered marble relief panel of five draped figures in low relief, two of them balancing flat objects on their heads and two handling a large folded cloth."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the east end unlike the other three sides?","a":"Because both files of the procession arrive there, so it is longer than the others and has a symmetry the rest lacks, with the peplos handed over among the seated gods."},{"q":"Why is the identification of the subject not secure?","a":"Because every ancient source that mentions the frieze is later than the building, and the carving leaves out the Panathenaic ship on whose mast the robe was raised like a sail."},{"q":"Why do the horses look unfinished today?","a":"Because their weapons and bridle attachments were once applied in bronze, and it is those additions rather than the marble that have gone."}]},
{"id":"gr-499","num":499,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"All 92 panels of this temple's Doric frieze carry sculpture, which is unique in Greek architecture, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the first of its carving to be cut.","questions":["Gigantomachy east, Amazonomachy west, the sack of Troy north and Centauromachy south: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> give four versions of one subject, which is a contest between two adversaries.","In Athens the surviving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hang in pairs between steel columns as many as the temple's own, the original marble mixed with casts of the 15 in London."],"answer":"Parthenon metopes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Panels</span><span class=\"dt-v\">92, every one carved</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 447 BCE, first of the sculpture</span><span class=\"dt-k\">South side</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 Centauromachy, 9 others</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elgin took</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 metopes</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Parthenon metopes</b> are the 92 square panels of the Doric frieze that ran round the whole building between the architrave and the cornice, every one of them carved, an arrangement unique in Greek architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were the first of the temple's sculpture to be made, cut on the ground by separate teams of sculptors and stone-cutters, among them resident aliens who had come to Athens from the Aegean islands, which is why the styles differ a little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relief stands so high that seen level with them the triglyphs between would hardly show and the carving would read as one continuous band.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The subjects are set by side: the Gigantomachy on the east front, the Amazonomachy at the west, the sack of Troy along the north and the Centauromachy on the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> All four are the same subject in different dress, a contest between two adversaries, and all four are known from the sculpture of many other Greek temples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The stories were old: Homer has a suitor warn a beggar off the wine with the tale of the centaur Eurytion, whom it made foolish in the hall of Peirithous when he went to the Lapiths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The heroes leapt up and dragged him out through the gateway with his ears and nostrils shorn off, and from that, Homer says, the feud arose between the centaurs and mankind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the giants, Apollodorus has Earth bring them forth in her anger over the Titans, matchless in bulk and terrible to look at, with the scales of dragons for feet, darting rocks and burning oaks at the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The south side does not keep to one story: 23 of its metopes are given to the Centauromachy and the nine in the middle to some other subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifteen were carried off to London by Lord Elgin,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and in Athens the surviving marble hangs in pairs between steel columns as many as the temple's own, mixed with casts of what is in foreign museums.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Parthenon metopes","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","history","greece","athens","religion"],"sources":["“The Parthenon,” The Parthenon Frieze, Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://parthenonfrieze.gr/en/the-parthenon/. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]","Homer, <i>Odyssey</i> 21.293-304, trans. A. T. Murray, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0136:book=21:card=288. [Open access]","Apollodorus, <i>Library</i> 1.6.1, trans. James George Frazer, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Apollod.%201.6.1. [Open access]","“The Parthenon Gallery,” Acropolis Museum, https://www.theacropolismuseum.gr/en/exhibit-halls/parthenon-gallery. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/South_metope_1_from_the_Parthenon_at_the_Acropolis_Museum_in_Athens.jpg/1920px-South_metope_1_from_the_Parthenon_at_the_Acropolis_Museum_in_Athens.jpg","title":"South metope 1, in Athens","desc":"A Lapith and a centaur locked together, carved so deep that the figures stand almost clear of the panel behind them; the heads are lost, as on most of the metopes.","credit":"Photograph by Nikos Kitsakis, 2022, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_metope_1_from_the_Parthenon_at_the_Acropolis_Museum_in_Athens.jpg","alt":"A square marble panel in a museum, carved in very deep relief with a naked man and a rearing horse-bodied centaur struggling against each other, both headless."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the panels differ a little in style?","a":"Because they were the first sculpture made, cut on the ground by separate teams of sculptors and stone-cutters, among them resident aliens who had come from the Aegean islands."},{"q":"Why would the frieze have read as continuous from below?","a":"Because the relief stands so high that seen level with them the triglyphs between would hardly show."},{"q":"Why are the four sides said to carry one subject in different dress?","a":"Because gods against giants, Greeks against Amazons, Greeks against Trojans and Lapiths against centaurs are all a contest between two adversaries, and all are known from many other temples."}]},
{"id":"gr-500","num":500,"category":"Ancient Greece · History","question":"Plutarch names <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the architects of the Parthenon, working under Phidias, who is said to have had almost everything on the Acropolis under his superintendence.","questions":["An Athenian decree orders a temple and stone altar for the goddess of Victory built however Callicrates may specify, which is how one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is documented rather than described.","Vitruvius has one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> writing a book on the Doric temple of Minerva at Athens and roofing the vast cella of Ceres at Eleusis."],"answer":"Ictinus and Callicrates","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Parthenon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">447 – 438 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phigalia temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1st after Tegea, per Pausanias</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nike temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 men to specify it with him</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Ictinus and Callicrates</b> are the two architects Plutarch names for the Parthenon,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> working under Phidias, who had general charge of the whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Almost everything else known of Ictinus is a book and two more temples, and of Callicrates a decree and a wall. Vitruvius lists him with Carpion among architects who published on their own buildings, theirs being the Doric temple of Minerva on the acropolis of Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same passage says the cella of Ceres and Proserpine at Eleusis, of vast size, was carried up to the roof by Ictinus in the Doric style, without exterior columns and with plenty of room for the customary sacrifices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pausanias found a third building of his in Arcadia: on Mount Cotilius above Phigalia, at a place called Bassae, the temple of Apollo the Helper, which including its roof is of stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Of the temples in the Peloponnese, he says, this might be placed first after the one at Tegea for the beauty of its stone and for its symmetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He names its architect as Ictinus, a contemporary of Pericles who built for the Athenians what is called the Parthenon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Callicrates is documented rather than described: an Athenian decree, which also installs a priestess for Athena Nike to be allotted from all Athenian women, orders that the sanctuary be given gates in whatever way Callicrates may specify.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It orders further that a temple be built in whatever way he may specify, with a stone altar, and that three men chosen from the council draw up the specifications with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plutarch adds that he was the contractor for one of the walls running down to the sea, so what survives of either man is a name attached to commissions rather than any account of a life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ictinus and Callicrates","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","greece","athens","architecture","art"],"sources":["Plutarch, <i>Pericles</i> 13, trans. Bernadotte Perrin, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Plut.%20Per.%2013. [Open access]","Vitruvius, <i>On Architecture</i> 7, preface 12 and 16, trans. Morris Hicky Morgan (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1914), Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.02.0073%3Abook%3D7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.7, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.7. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.8, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.8. [Open access]","Pausanias, <i>Description of Greece</i> 8.41.9, trans. W. H. S. Jones and H. A. Ormerod, Perseus Digital Library, https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Paus.%208.41.9. [Open access]","Decree about priestess and temple of Athena Nike, c. 450 or c. 438 BC (AIO 314), trans. Stephen Lambert, Josine Blok and Robin Osborne, Attic Inscriptions Online, https://www.atticinscriptions.com/inscription/AIO/314. [Open access]","“Parthenon,” Acropolis Restoration Service (YSMA), Hellenic Ministry of Culture, https://www.ysma.gr/en/monuments/parthenon/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/1972_bassai_temple.jpg/1920px-1972_bassai_temple.jpg","title":"Bassae, photographed in 1972","desc":"The temple of Apollo the Helper on Mount Cotilius, which Pausanias attributes to Ictinus, seen before the conservation work of the 1980s; a tent has covered it since.","credit":"Photograph by Tuh de, 1972, via Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0). https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1972_bassai_temple.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a Doric temple standing almost complete on a bare hillside, its colonnades roofless, with mountains behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why is so little known about either man?","a":"Because what survives is a name attached to commissions rather than any account of a life — a book and two more temples for one, a decree and a wall for the other."},{"q":"Why is the temple at Bassae attributed to one of them?","a":"Because Pausanias names its architect as Ictinus, a contemporary of Pericles who built for the Athenians what is called the Parthenon."},{"q":"Why does a decree name an architect rather than describe a building?","a":"Because it orders that the sanctuary be given gates and a temple built in whatever way Callicrates may specify, with three men from the council drawing up the specifications with him."}]},
{"id":"bio-001","num":1,"category":"What biology is","question":"The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers work at scales from a single protein to a whole ocean, and what holds all of it together as one subject is common descent.","questions":["Order, response, reproduction, growth, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution are the eight properties textbooks list to mark out what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes as its subject matter.","About 86 per cent of species were still undescribed on one 2011 estimate, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a science whose own subject matter has mostly not yet been catalogued."],"answer":"biology","answerText":"biology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Biology</b> is the study of life, and its scope is unusually wide even among the sciences, reaching from a single molecule inside one cell to whole ecosystems and the living planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No one worker covers that range, so it is divided into fields defined by the scale or the group they work on, among them cell biology, physiology, botany, zoology, microbiology, ecology and palaeontology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they all study is picked out less by a definition than by a list of shared properties: order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth and development, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those properties appear along a hierarchy running from atoms and molecules through organelles, cells, tissues, organs and organisms to populations, communities, ecosystems and the biosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each level is assembled from the one below, so a question asked at one level rarely has its answer there and a physiologist and an ecologist studying the same animal may be asking unrelated questions. <br><br> What holds so scattered a subject together is heredity, which separates a living process from the growth of a crystal or the burning of a candle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every cell yet examined stores its hereditary information as double-stranded DNA built from the same four units, transcribes it into RNA and translates that into protein by the same machinery, and reads very nearly the same genetic code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That uniformity is inherited rather than coincidental, and the pattern of resemblances lets biologists reconstruct a family tree of living species with three great branches, the bacteria, the archaea and the eukaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Common descent is therefore the frame the rest of the subject is written inside, and why a result obtained in yeast or a fruit fly bears on human medicine at all. It is also why the work is nowhere near done: one widely cited 2011 estimate put the number of eukaryote species at about 8.7 million against some 1.2 million then catalogued, leaving roughly 86 per cent undescribed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Diversity of Genomes and the Tree of Life,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26866/. [Open access]","Camilo Mora, Derek P. Tittensor, Sina Adl, Alastair G. B. Simpson, and Boris Worm, “How Many Species Are There on Earth and in the Ocean?” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 9, no. 8 (2011): e1001127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001127. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b7/PhylogeneticTree.png","title":"A phylogenetic tree of living things, from ribosomal RNA","desc":"The three domains of life drawn from ribosomal RNA sequence comparisons: bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, all descending from a single trunk. Public domain, redrawn from a NASA Astrobiology Institute figure.","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhylogeneticTree.png","alt":"A branching diagram on a white ground with three coloured groups of branches rising from one black trunk, labelled Bacteria in purple on the left, Archaea in red in the middle and Eucarya in dark red on the right, each branch tipped with the name of a group such as Cyanobacteria, Halophiles, Animalia, Fungi and Plantae"},"why":[{"q":"Why is a subject this scattered treated as one science?","a":"Because heredity holds it together: every cell yet examined stores its information as double-stranded DNA of the same four units and reads very nearly the same genetic code, and that uniformity is inherited rather than coincidental."},{"q":"Why does a result obtained in yeast bear on human medicine?","a":"Because common descent is the frame the subject is written inside — the pattern of shared machinery lets biologists reconstruct one family tree, so a mechanism found in one branch is likely the same mechanism in another."},{"q":"Why is the work nowhere near finished?","a":"Because a widely cited 2011 estimate put eukaryote species at about 8.7 million against some 1.2 million then catalogued, leaving roughly 86 per cent of them undescribed."}]},
{"id":"bio-002","num":2,"category":"What biology is","question":"Fire consumes fuel, gives off waste, moves and grows, which is why no checklist of properties on its own has ever settled what counts as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and what does not.","questions":["Textbooks introduce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a list of eight properties rather than a definition, among them order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.","A committee working for NASA settled on a self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution as a working definition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, chosen because it is the requirement fire and growing crystals fail."],"answer":"life","answerText":"life","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Life</b> has no definition biologists agree on, so textbooks introduce it with a list of properties instead: order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth and development, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each item does real work: a living thing is highly organised, holds its internal conditions steady, takes in energy and passes on what it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trouble is that no item settles the matter by itself, since fire consumes fuel, gives off waste, moves and grows, and a crystal in solution grows and hands its handedness on to those that form from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Definitions built as checklists inherit that trouble, since a list requiring every property at once will not admit a single rabbit, which neither reproduces on its own nor evolves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind all of it lies a harder problem: with only one example to generalise from, which features of life on Earth are necessary and which are accidents of its history cannot be told apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The most used working definition calls life a self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution, which fire fails because nothing it produces inherits from it and the crystal because its defects are not passed on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a working definition rather than a settled one, framed so it can be applied without knowing which of the familiar properties are universal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In practice most definitions rest on the cell, the minimal self-reproducing unit and the vehicle by which genetic information is handed on in every living species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why viruses are the standing hard case: they reproduce, evolve and carry genes, but a virion has no metabolism of its own, and whether they are alive is a decision about the definition rather than a discovery about viruses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The list is therefore not a failure of rigour but an honest report of where the subject stands, and biology proceeds without settling it, because what makes one organism work is answerable where the general question is not.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Steven A. Benner, “Defining Life,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 10, no. 10 (2010): 1021–30, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2010.0524. [Open access]","Chris P. McKay, “What Is Life—and How Do We Search for It in Other Worlds?” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 2, no. 9 (2004): e302, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0020302. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Patrick Forterre, “Defining Life: The Virus Viewpoint,” <i>Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres</i> 40, no. 2 (2010): 151–60, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11084-010-9194-1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg/1920px-Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg","title":"Onion root tip cells under a phase-contrast microscope","desc":"Cells from the growing tip of an onion root, with blue arrows marking two of them caught dividing. Photograph by Catfaster, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by Catfaster, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg","alt":"A pale blue-grey micrograph of about a dozen box-shaped plant cells packed together, their walls picked out as bright lines, with two blue arrows labelled A and B pointing at cells whose interiors are divided or filled with fibrous strands"},"why":[{"q":"Why do textbooks open with a list of properties instead of a definition?","a":"Because no single item settles the matter: fire consumes fuel, gives off waste and grows, and a crystal in solution grows and hands its handedness on to those that form from it."},{"q":"Why does a checklist definition fail even on ordinary animals?","a":"Because a list requiring every property at once will not admit a single rabbit, which neither reproduces on its own nor evolves."},{"q":"Why can biology not simply settle the question with more work?","a":"Because with only one example to generalise from, which features of life on Earth are necessary and which are accidents of its history cannot be told apart."}]},
{"id":"bio-003","num":3,"category":"What biology is","question":"Atoms make molecules, molecules make organelles and cells make tissues, a nesting biologists call <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which each level is composed of the one beneath it.","questions":["Because each works at a different level of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a biochemist, a cytologist, a physiologist and an ecologist can study the same animal and never once overlap.","Philosophers of biology point out that no single ladder of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fits every organism, so the textbook hierarchy is best read as a teaching scaffold rather than a law."],"answer":"biological organisation","answerText":"biological organisation","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Biological organisation</b> is the nesting of living matter into levels, each composed of the one below, from atoms and molecules through organelles, cells, tissues, organs and organ systems to organisms, populations, communities, ecosystems and the biosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lower rungs are chemistry: atoms bond into molecules, molecules into the macromolecules and organelles a cell is built from, and a human red blood cell is about eight micrometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Above the cell the definitions turn functional: a tissue is a group of similar cells doing one job, an organ a structure of two or more tissues, and an organ system a set of organs meeting one need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organism is the level at which the assembly can independently perform every function life requires, and where the ladder stops describing a body and starts describing a world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Above it the units are ecological: a population is one species interbreeding in one place, a community the species sharing it and their interactions, and an ecosystem the non-living surroundings as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Convention on Biological Diversity defines the last of those as a dynamic complex of plant, animal and micro-organism communities and their non-living environment interacting as a functional unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ladder's use is organising the subject, since each level has its own methods and questions, and a biochemist and an ecologist can work on one animal without ever meeting. It organises explanation too, though it usually works in the background as an implicit framework rather than an explicit theory, and general definitions of it have not met wide acceptance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The uniformity of composition a single ladder assumes does not hold across all of biology, which is the standing objection, and recent work treats levels contextually instead, case by case rather than as one layer cake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> None of that makes the hierarchy useless: it is how the subject is taught and how a question is placed, but it maps the science rather than a structure discovered in the world.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “1.2 Structural Organization of the Human Body,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-2-structural-organization-of-the-human-body. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “44.1 The Scope of Ecology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology. [Open access]","United Nations, <i>Convention on Biological Diversity</i>, Rio de Janeiro, 5 June 1992, article 2, https://www.cbd.int/convention/articles/?a=cbd-02. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Daniel Stephen Brooks, “Levels of Organization in Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2024/entries/levels-org-biology/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the definitions change character above the level of the cell?","a":"Because the lower rungs are chemistry — atoms into molecules into organelles — while a tissue, an organ and an organ system are defined by the job they do rather than by what they are made of."},{"q":"Why is the ladder useful even to workers who never use it?","a":"Because each level has its own methods and questions, so it places a question and organises how the subject is taught, letting a biochemist and an ecologist work on one animal without ever meeting."},{"q":"Why is the hierarchy said to map the science rather than the world?","a":"Because the uniformity of composition a single ladder assumes does not hold across all of biology, and recent work treats levels contextually, case by case, rather than as one layer cake."}]},
{"id":"bio-004","num":4,"category":"What biology is","question":"A bacterium with the smallest known genome still needs 477 genes, because the thing it has to build is a whole <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the smallest object that is alive.","questions":["Everything alive is either one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or an assembly of them, and every one of them comes from a pre-existing one rather than forming out of nothing.","Enclosed by a membrane that separates its chemistry from the outside, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the smallest thing that can grow, respond and reproduce on its own."],"answer":"cell","answerText":"cell","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"The <b>cell</b> is the smallest unit that is alive, and the three claims making that precise are that every living thing is made of cells, that the cell is biology’s basic unit, and that new cells arise only from existing ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes one a unit rather than a patch of chemistry is its plasma membrane, a bilayer of phospholipids and proteins that provides the functional separation between inside and outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything needed to keep going and to copy itself sits within that boundary, which is why it is the minimal self-reproducing unit and the vehicle by which genetic information passes on in every living species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The requirement is not a light one: the bacterium with the smallest known genome is a parasite handed many of its small molecules ready-made, and it still carries 477 genes, with the floor for a viable cell probably 200 to 300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cells are correspondingly small, an <i>Escherichia coli</i> measuring about one micrometre across and two long, so a body is not one thing but an enormous number of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One recent estimate puts a 70 kg reference adult at about 30 trillion human cells, alongside roughly 38 trillion bacterial ones living in and on the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That second figure retires a much-repeated claim that bacteria outnumber human cells ten to one, since the ratio turns out to be close to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every organism yet examined is cellular, across the bacteria, the archaea and the eukaryotes, and the resemblance of their machinery is why all three are taken to descend from one ancestral cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A multicellular body is an assembly of that same unit rather than a different kind of thing, its cells specialised for different jobs but each of them still a cell. The unit is therefore where biology’s explanations bottom out: below it there is chemistry and above it there are assemblies, but the cell is the smallest thing that does what living things do.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Ron Sender, Shai Fuchs, and Ron Milo, “Revised Estimates for the Number of Human and Bacteria Cells in the Body,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 14, no. 8 (2016): e1002533, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002533. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Diversity of Genomes and the Tree of Life,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26866/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Ecoli_dividing.jpg/1920px-Ecoli_dividing.jpg","title":"A dividing Escherichia coli, magnified 21,674 times","desc":"A scanning electron micrograph of one Escherichia coli cell part-way through dividing in two, with a scale bar of one micrometre. Public domain, from the CDC Public Health Image Library, image 7137, by Evangeline Sowers and Janice Haney Carr.","credit":"CDC Public Health Image Library, image 7137, by Evangeline Sowers and Janice Haney Carr. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecoli_dividing.jpg","alt":"A grey scanning electron micrograph in which a single pale rod-shaped bacterium lies against a pitted background, pinched at its middle into two rounded halves still joined, with a one-micrometre scale bar and the microscope settings printed along the bottom"},"why":[{"q":"Why is a cell a unit rather than a patch of chemistry?","a":"Because its plasma membrane, a bilayer of phospholipids and proteins, provides the functional separation between inside and outside, and everything needed to keep going and copy itself sits within that boundary."},{"q":"Why is being the minimal self-reproducing unit not a light requirement?","a":"Because the bacterium with the smallest known genome is a parasite handed many small molecules ready-made and still carries 477 genes, with the floor for a viable cell probably 200 to 300."},{"q":"Why does the ten-to-one bacteria claim no longer stand?","a":"Because one recent estimate puts a 70 kg reference adult at about 30 trillion human cells against roughly 38 trillion bacterial ones, which is a ratio close to one."}]},
{"id":"bio-005","num":5,"category":"What biology is","question":"Sweating when hot and shivering when cold are one loop run in opposite directions, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name for that reversal of any departure from a set point.","questions":["A sensor, a control centre and an effector are the parts every loop of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs, and the loop works by reversing the deviation rather than switching on and off.","Insulin released after a meal brings blood glucose back into its normal range, one of the loops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a body runs continuously and without any conscious control."],"answer":"homeostasis","answerText":"homeostasis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Homeostasis</b> is the maintenance of a relatively stable internal environment in the face of a changing outside world and varying internal activity, worked by negative feedback that reduces an error signal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A loop needs a sensor measuring the regulated variable, a mechanism setting its normal range, a detector comparing the two, a controller deciding what to do and effectors that do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperature is the familiar case and it divides the animal kingdom: an ectotherm takes its body temperature from its surroundings, an endotherm holds one from internal sources, and heat moves by radiation, evaporation, convection and conduction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a human that comes to sensors reporting a departure, a control centre comparing it with the normal range, and effectors sweating, shivering or altering blood flow until the departure is reversed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blood glucose runs on the same shape, cells in the pancreas detecting a rise after a meal and releasing insulin that takes glucose out of circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Positive feedback does the opposite and is much rarer: in childbirth the stretching of the cervix releases oxytocin, contractions strengthen, the stretching increases, and the loop stops only when the baby is born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two things commonly taught are wrong: a set point is a range rather than a single value and can be shifted, and the loops are not switches, since control signals are present the whole time and continuously set the output.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fever is the clearest case of the first, an actively driven rise of 1 to 4 °C (2 to 7 °F) rather than the temperature loop breaking down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the most famous set point has moved, since the 37 °C (98.6 °F) standard rests on measurements taken in Leipzig in 1851, and one analysis of American records finds men born in the 1990s about 0.59 °C (1.1 °F) cooler than men born in the early 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word therefore names regulation rather than constancy, and what looks like a body holding still is a body correcting continuously.","sources":["Harold Modell, William Cliff, Joel Michael, Jenny McFarland, Mary Pat Wenderoth, and Ann Wright, “A Physiologist’s View of Homeostasis,” <i>Advances in Physiology Education</i> 39, no. 4 (2015): 259–66, https://doi.org/10.1152/advan.00107.2015. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “33.3 Homeostasis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/33-3-homeostasis. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “1.5 Homeostasis,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-5-homeostasis. [Open access]","Sharon S. Evans, Elizabeth A. Repasky, and Daniel T. Fisher, “Fever and the Thermal Regulation of Immunity: The Immune System Feels the Heat,” <i>Nature Reviews Immunology</i> 15, no. 6 (2015): 335–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/nri3843. [Open access]","Myroslava Protsiv, Catherine Ley, Joanna Lankester, Trevor Hastie, and Julie Parsonnet, “Decreasing Human Body Temperature in the United States since the Industrial Revolution,” <i>eLife</i> 9 (2020): e49555, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.49555. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg/1920px-Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg","title":"A chuckwalla basking on a rock in Joshua Tree National Park","desc":"An ectotherm at work: the lizard takes its body temperature from its surroundings, and flattening itself against sunlit rock is how it raises one. Photo by NPS/Robert Zuniga, public domain.","credit":"Photo by NPS/Robert Zuniga, Joshua Tree National Park. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg","alt":"A dark grey lizard with a pale speckled back lying flattened along the top of a sunlit granite boulder, its head turned towards the camera and a shadowed rock face behind it"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the word name regulation rather than constancy?","a":"Because what looks like a body holding still is a body correcting continuously: negative feedback loops are not switches, since control signals are present the whole time and continuously set the output."},{"q":"Why is a fever not the temperature loop breaking down?","a":"Because a set point is a range rather than a single value and can be shifted, and a fever is an actively driven rise of 1 to 4 °C rather than a failure of control."},{"q":"Why is the 37 °C standard itself unreliable?","a":"Because it rests on measurements taken in Leipzig in 1851, and one analysis of American records finds men born in the 1990s about 0.59 °C cooler than men born in the early 19th century."}]},
{"id":"bio-006","num":6,"category":"What biology is","question":"Breaking food down and building a body up are the two halves of one linked network of reactions, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name for the whole of it.","questions":["Enzymes let reactions run at body temperature that would otherwise need far hotter conditions, which is what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible inside a cell at all.","A single gut bacterium’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been reconstructed as 2,251 reactions among 1,136 different molecules, which is what a cell runs simply to stay alive."],"answer":"metabolism","answerText":"metabolism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Metabolism</b> is the whole set of chemical reactions going on inside a living thing, both those that release energy and those that use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into two halves that depend on each other: catabolic pathways break foodstuffs down into smaller molecules, yielding a usable form of energy and some of the building blocks a cell needs, while anabolic pathways spend that energy synthesising the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost none of it would happen unaided, since these reactions would normally proceed only at temperatures far above the one inside a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Enzymes are what make it possible at body temperature, and a pathway is a series of them, the product of one reaction becoming the starting material for the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those series are linked to one another into a maze of interconnected reactions, which is why metabolism is better pictured as a network than as a line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The size of that network can be counted: a genome-scale reconstruction of <i>Escherichia coli</i> accounts for 1,366 genes, 2,251 reactions and 1,136 different metabolites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In an animal the breakdown runs in three stages, digestion cutting large molecules into their subunits, glycolysis converting each glucose into two pyruvate, and a third stage in the mitochondria yielding most of the cell’s energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of that energy is used where it is released: ATP carries it, serving as a convenient and versatile currency, made by adding a phosphate group to ADP and spent by taking it off again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metabolism never stops, and the rate it runs at is what a body’s food supplies, but the familiar rule that this rate scales with body mass to the three-quarter power is not a law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Across 358 studies the scaling exponent varies, mostly between 0.5 and 1.0, and the variation is real biological difference rather than noise, which is worth knowing early about any relationship that fits a graph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.1 Energy and Metabolism,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-1-energy-and-metabolism. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Jeffrey D. Orth, Tom M. Conrad, Jessica Na, Joshua A. Lerman, Hojung Nam, Adam M. Feist, and Bernhard Ø. Palsson, “A Comprehensive Genome-Scale Reconstruction of <i>Escherichia coli</i> Metabolism—2011,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 7 (2011): 535, https://doi.org/10.1038/msb.2011.65. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “How Cells Obtain Energy from Food,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26882/. [Open access]","Douglas S. Glazier, “Variable Metabolic Scaling Breaks the Law: From ‘Newtonian’ to ‘Darwinian’ Approaches,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 289, no. 1985 (2022): 20221605, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2022.1605. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Carbon_Catabolism.png/1920px-Carbon_Catabolism.png","title":"A map of carbon catabolism","desc":"Glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, the beta-oxidation of fats and the electron transport chain drawn as one connected diagram, with every enzyme named. This is only the energy-releasing half of metabolism, and only for carbon compounds. Diagram by Mplanine, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Diagram by Mplanine, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carbon_Catabolism.png","alt":"A dense white chart covered in chemical structures joined by hundreds of curved arrows, grouped into labelled blocks headed Carbohydrate Metabolism, Citric Acid Cycle and Lipid Metabolism, with small coloured tags naming the enzyme at each step and a rainbow bar at lower right labelled Electron Transport Chain"},"why":[{"q":"Why would almost none of a cell’s chemistry happen unaided?","a":"Because these reactions would normally proceed only at temperatures far above the one inside a cell, so enzymes are what make them possible at body temperature."},{"q":"Why is metabolism better pictured as a network than a line?","a":"Because pathways are linked to one another into a maze of interconnected reactions — a reconstruction of Escherichia coli accounts for 1,366 genes, 2,251 reactions and 1,136 different metabolites."},{"q":"Why is quarter-power scaling with body mass not a law?","a":"Because across 358 studies the exponent varies, mostly between 0.5 and 1.0, and the variation is real biological difference rather than noise."}]},
{"id":"bio-007","num":7,"category":"What biology is","question":"Comparing a treated group with one identical to it except for the treatment is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns a biological guess into a claim that evidence can settle.","questions":["Because no two organisms are alike, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in biology rests on independent replicates rather than on one animal, however striking that animal’s result looks.","Where the subject is a lineage that died out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proceeds by predicting what will be found in rocks of a given age and then going to look."],"answer":"scientific method","answerText":"scientific method","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"The <b>scientific method</b> is the procedure by which a claim is made answerable to evidence, running from an observation to a question, a hypothesis, a prediction, a test and a report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Biology uses it in two modes: descriptive science, usually inductive, which observes in order to find what is there, and hypothesis-based science, usually deductive, which starts from a proposed answer and tests it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A test holds everything constant but one thing, comparing an experimental group with a control identical to it except for the manipulation, so that any difference can be laid at the manipulation’s door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes this harder than in chemistry is that no two organisms are alike, so a result rests on replicates, and the replicates have to be independent of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They often are not: in one journal issue surveyed, 12 per cent of papers had treated repeated measures from the same animal as independent samples, and another 36 per cent were suspected of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Much of biology cannot be manipulated at all, since a lineage’s history is over, and there the test is a prediction about what will be found in rocks of a given age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tetrapod-like fish <i>Tiktaalik roseae</i> came from one Late Devonian locality on Ellesmere Island worked over six field seasons, which is what a test looks like when the subject died out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Work in a model organism rests on shared machinery, since every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it by the same mechanism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It does not always carry across: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The method is the one every science uses, and what changes is how much of it can be done to the subject; where manipulation is impossible, prediction and independent evidence carry the weight.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Stanley E. Lazic, “The Problem of Pseudoreplication in Neuroscientific Studies: Is It Affecting Your Analysis?” <i>BMC Neuroscience</i> 11 (2010): 5, https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2202-11-5. [Open access]","Thomas A. Stewart, Justin B. Lemberg, Emily J. Hillan, Isaac Magallanes, Edward B. Daeschler, and Neil H. Shubin, “The Axial Skeleton of <i>Tiktaalik roseae</i>,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 15 (2024): e2316106121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2316106121. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Junhee Seok, H. Shaw Warren, Alex G. Cuenca, Michael N. Mindrinos, Henry V. Baker, Weihong Xu, Daniel R. Richards, et al., “Genomic Responses in Mouse Models Poorly Mimic Human Inflammatory Diseases,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 9 (2013): 3507–12, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1222878110. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Tiktaalik_roseae_%28fish_to_amphibian_transitional_fossil%29_%28Fram_Formation%2C_Upper_Devonian%3B_Ellesmere_Island%2C_Arctic_Canada%29_1_%2849755118288%29.jpg","title":"A cast of the Tiktaalik roseae specimens, Field Museum","desc":"A cast of two of the Tiktaalik specimens from the Fram Formation of Ellesmere Island, on display at the Field Museum in Chicago; the flat skull is at the left and the scaled body at the right. Photograph by James St. John, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Photograph by James St. John, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiktaalik_roseae_%28fish_to_amphibian_transitional_fossil%29_%28Fram_Formation%2C_Upper_Devonian%3B_Ellesmere_Island%2C_Arctic_Canada%29_1_%2849755118288%29.jpg","alt":"A wide fossil slab photographed against a pale grey mount, with a flattened crocodile-like skull at the left and a long body of overlapping diamond scales running to the right, the bone a mottled brown and grey"},"why":[{"q":"Why is testing harder in biology than in chemistry?","a":"Because no two organisms are alike, so a result rests on replicates, and the replicates have to be independent — in one journal issue surveyed, 12 per cent of papers treated repeated measures from one animal as independent samples."},{"q":"Why can a lineage’s history still be tested at all?","a":"Because where manipulation is impossible the test is a prediction about what will be found in rocks of a given age, which is how Tiktaalik roseae was sought over six field seasons on Ellesmere Island."},{"q":"Why does work in a model organism not always carry across?","a":"Because shared machinery is not shared response: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model."}]},
{"id":"bio-008","num":8,"category":"What biology is","question":"An experiment cannot show that a treatment works; what it can do is give evidence against the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the claim that the treatment makes no difference at all.","questions":["A biologist who finds nothing has not shown there is nothing to find, because failing to reject the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may only mean the data could not tell the possibilities apart.","Stated before the data are collected, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what stops a surprising result being written up afterwards as though it had been predicted from the start."],"answer":"null hypothesis","answerText":"null hypothesis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"A hypothesis in biology is a testable proposal about how something works, and it earns its place by yielding a prediction that an experiment could contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <b>null hypothesis</b> is the version a statistical test is applied to: the claim that there is no difference between the groups being compared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The biologist’s own idea is the alternative, and a result is reported either as rejecting the null, where a difference is too large to be likely by chance, or as failing to reject it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A p-value is the probability of seeing an effect at least this large if there were no true effect, and it is not the probability that the null is true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That misreading is the commonest one, and its partner is treating a large p-value as evidence for the null, when it usually means only that the data cannot discriminate among competing hypotheses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In biology the design matters as much as the arithmetic, since a comparison is fair only if animals are assigned at random, the measurer does not know which group is which, and the sample size is set in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reporting guidelines for animal experiments exist because those items were routinely missing from published papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other failure is to the hypothesis rather than the experiment: of 807 ecologists and evolutionary biologists surveyed, 51 per cent had reported an unexpected finding as though it had been hypothesised from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the same survey 64 per cent had at least once not reported a result because it was not statistically significant, and 42 per cent had collected more data after checking whether it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of that is fraud, and all of it is why a hypothesis stated before the data are seen, and an experiment built so that it could refute one, are worth more than a striking result.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Jacob Shreffler and Martin R. Huecker, “Hypothesis Testing, P Values, Confidence Intervals, and Significance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557421/. [Open access]","Sander Greenland, Stephen J. Senn, Kenneth J. Rothman, John B. Carlin, Charles Poole, Steven N. Goodman, and Douglas G. Altman, “Statistical Tests, P Values, Confidence Intervals, and Power: A Guide to Misinterpretations,” <i>European Journal of Epidemiology</i> 31, no. 4 (2016): 337–50, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10654-016-0149-3. [Open access]","Nathalie Percie du Sert, Amrita Ahluwalia, Sabina Alam, Marc T. Avey, Monya Baker, et al., “Reporting Animal Research: Explanation and Elaboration for the ARRIVE Guidelines 2.0,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 7 (2020): e3000411, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000411. [Open access]","Hannah Fraser, Tim Parker, Shinichi Nakagawa, Ashley Barnett, and Fiona Fidler, “Questionable Research Practices in Ecology and Evolution,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 7 (2018): e0200303, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0200303. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a p-value not the probability that the null is true?","a":"Because it is the probability of seeing an effect at least this large if there were no true effect, which is a different quantity, and reading it the other way round is the commonest misreading there is."},{"q":"Why does a large p-value not support the null hypothesis?","a":"Because it usually means only that the data cannot discriminate among competing hypotheses, rather than that no difference exists."},{"q":"Why is a hypothesis stated before the data worth more than a striking result?","a":"Because of 807 ecologists and evolutionary biologists surveyed, 51 per cent had reported an unexpected finding as though it had been hypothesised from the start, and 42 per cent had collected more data after checking significance."}]},
{"id":"bio-009","num":9,"category":"What biology is","question":"Every feature of the treated group except the treatment itself is what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries, which is the whole reason a difference between them can be blamed on the treatment.","questions":["Without a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a biologist has a measurement and no comparison, so any change could as easily be the season, the cage or the batch of reagent.","When cases and controls are processed on different days, the processing day tracks the disease, and a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot rescue an experiment whose groups differ in more than one way."],"answer":"control group","answerText":"control group","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"A variable is anything in an experiment that can change, and a <b>control group</b> is a group carrying every feature of the experimental one except the manipulation being tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what licenses the inference: if the two groups differ afterwards, the difference can be laid at the manipulation rather than at some outside factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The licence is only as good as the sameness, so a controlled biological experiment has to say how individuals were allocated to groups, who knew which group was which, and how the sample size was arrived at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also has to say what the experimental unit was, since the thing that was allocated is the thing that counts, however many measurements were taken from each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where some difference other than the manipulation tracks the groups themselves, that difference is a confound, and no amount of later analysis will separate it from the result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> High-throughput biology gives the clearest cases, since measurements made in different batches behave differently for technical reasons unrelated to the biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In one published proteomic test for ovarian cancer the disease turned out to be almost perfectly correlated with the day each sample was processed, and the assay was blocked pending further validation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blinding is the control on the observer rather than on the material, and its absence is measurable: across paired studies, not recording data blind inflates the mean reported effect size by about 27 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also rare, reported in 6.3 per cent of 960 animal behaviour studies,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while a survey of 2,671 in vivo publications found randomisation in 24.8 per cent and a sample size calculation in 0.7 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this is bureaucracy: the control is the part of an experiment that decides whether its result means anything, and the figures say it is the part most often left out.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Nathalie Percie du Sert, Amrita Ahluwalia, Sabina Alam, Marc T. Avey, Monya Baker, et al., “Reporting Animal Research: Explanation and Elaboration for the ARRIVE Guidelines 2.0,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 7 (2020): e3000411, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000411. [Open access]","Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert B. Scharpf, Héctor Corrada Bravo, David Simcha, Benjamin Langmead, W. Evan Johnson, Donald Geman, Keith Baggerly, and Rafael A. Irizarry, “Tackling the Widespread and Critical Impact of Batch Effects in High-Throughput Data,” <i>Nature Reviews Genetics</i> 11, no. 10 (2010): 733–39, https://doi.org/10.1038/nrg2825. [Open access]","Luke Holman, Megan L. Head, Robert Lanfear, and Michael D. Jennions, “Evidence of Experimental Bias in the Life Sciences: Why We Need Blind Data Recording,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 13, no. 7 (2015): e1002190, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002190. [Open access]","Malcolm R. Macleod, Aaron Lawson McLean, Aikaterini Kyriakopoulou, Stylianos Serghiou, Arno de Wilde, et al., “Risk of Bias in Reports of In Vivo Research: A Focus for Improvement,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 13, no. 10 (2015): e1002273, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002273. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does having a control group license the inference at all?","a":"Because the control carries every feature of the experimental group except the manipulation, so if the two differ afterwards the difference can be laid at the manipulation rather than at some outside factor."},{"q":"Why can a confound not be removed by analysis afterwards?","a":"Because it is a difference that tracks the groups themselves — in one proteomic test for ovarian cancer the disease was almost perfectly correlated with the day each sample was processed, and the assay was blocked."},{"q":"Why is blinding treated as part of the control rather than a nicety?","a":"Because its absence is measurable: across paired studies, not recording data blind inflates the mean reported effect size by about 27 per cent, and it was reported in only 6.3 per cent of 960 animal behaviour studies."}]},
{"id":"bio-010","num":10,"category":"What biology is","question":"Weighing the same mouse twice gives two answers, and the gap between them is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which biology reports alongside a figure rather than hiding.","questions":["Bias and variability are the two kinds of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only the second of them gets smaller as a biologist takes more measurements of the same thing.","Where 47 per cent of herbarium specimens of one plant genus were identified as the wrong species, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies in naming the organism rather than in any instrument."],"answer":"measurement error","answerText":"measurement error","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"Biology begins in observation, and every observation carried into a result is a measurement with a <b>measurement error</b> attached to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Error comes in two kinds that behave differently: bias, the difference between the average of repeated measurements and the true value, and variability, the tendency of a process to give slightly different answers each time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bias does not average out with more data, which is why an instrument is calibrated against a reference rather than trusted, while variability is quantified as a standard deviation and shrinks as measurements are repeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The variability has more than one time scale, since short-term scatter is the precision of the instrument, and day-to-day variability from small changes in conditions and handling often exceeds it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a fact about biology as much as about instruments, since a body, a cage and a field all change between one morning and the next. <br><br> The measurement a biologist makes first is usually the identity of the thing being measured, and identification is itself error-prone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A review of European herbarium specimens of ivy found an average error rate of 47 per cent in the original species identification, rising to 55 per cent for one species and falling to 23 per cent for the commonest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the field the problem is not misreading but missing, since observed counts usually represent only a subset of the community actually present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Failing to allow for that biases everything derived from the counts, species richness included, and it does so worst where the chance of detecting an animal is lowest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this makes measurement futile; it makes the error part of the result, which is why a figure in biology is reported with a range around it rather than on its own.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","“2.1.1.3. Bias and Accuracy,” in <i>NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods</i> (Gaithersburg, MD: National Institute of Standards and Technology), https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section1/mpc113.htm. [Open access]","“2.1.1.4. Variability,” in <i>NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods</i> (Gaithersburg, MD: National Institute of Standards and Technology), https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section1/mpc114.htm. [Open access]","Marina Coca-de-la-Iglesia, Angélica Gallego-Narbón, Alejandro Alonso, and Virginia Valcárcel, “High Rate of Species Misidentification Reduces the Taxonomic Certainty of European Biodiversity Databases of Ivies (<i>Hedera</i> L.),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 4876, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-54735-0. [Open access]","Ciar D. Noble, Carlos A. Peres, and James J. Gilroy, “Accounting for Imperfect Detection When Estimating Species-Area Relationships and Beta-Diversity,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 7 (2024): e70017, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70017. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg/1920px-Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg","title":"A herbarium sheet, collected in Cameroon in 1975","desc":"A pressed orchid mounted with its collector’s label and a colour reference strip, held at Meise Botanic Garden. The sheet carries the plant and the name somebody gave it, and the name is a measurement like any other. Photograph by Meise Botanic Garden, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Meise Botanic Garden, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg","alt":"A tall cream sheet of card with a slender dried orchid taped down the middle, a single flower at its top and two leaves half way, a typed collector’s label at the lower left and a printed colour and greyscale calibration strip down the right-hand edge"},"why":[{"q":"Why does bias have to be handled differently from variability?","a":"Because bias does not average out with more data, so an instrument is calibrated against a reference, while variability is quantified as a standard deviation and shrinks as measurements are repeated."},{"q":"Why is day-to-day variability a fact about biology, not just instruments?","a":"Because a body, a cage and a field all change between one morning and the next, and that variability from small changes in conditions and handling often exceeds the instrument’s own precision."},{"q":"Why is the first measurement usually the least trusted one?","a":"Because it is the identity of the thing being measured: a review of European herbarium ivy specimens found an average error rate of 47 per cent in the original species identification."}]},
{"id":"bio-011","num":11,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because a typical animal cell is about a fifth the size of the smallest thing an unaided eye can resolve, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what made cell biology possible at all.","questions":["No radiation can resolve detail much smaller than its own wavelength, which is why a light <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stops at just under 0.2 micrometres however good its lenses are.","Fixing, sectioning and staining are what a specimen undergoes before a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is pointed at it, and each of those steps can alter the structure being looked at."],"answer":"microscope","answerText":"microscope","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","technology"],"abstract":"A typical animal cell is 10 to 20 micrometres across, about a fifth of the smallest particle the naked eye can make out, so a <b>microscope</b> is what puts cells within reach at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A compound light microscope magnifies up to about a thousand times, but contrast is too poor to see much that way, so cells are fixed in alcohol, acetic acid or formaldehyde, sectioned thinly and stained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What that bought biology was the cell itself: good light microscopes in the early 19th century are what showed plant and animal tissues to be aggregates of individual cells,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and cell theory followed from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It carries a standing caution, since any treatment used for fixing and embedding may alter the structure of the cell or its molecules in undesirable ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The instrument also has a hard floor, because no radiation resolves detail much smaller than its own wavelength, which for visible light gives a limit of just under 0.2 micrometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Electrons have a far shorter wavelength, and an electron microscope reaches about 2 nanometres on biological material, roughly a hundred times better than light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The price is that electrons are scattered by air, so the specimen sits in a vacuum, in which liquid water evaporates and unprotected biomolecules collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Freezing water fast enough that it solidifies without crystallising lets a molecule keep its shape inside that vacuum, which is what cryo-electron microscopy does and what the 2017 chemistry Nobel Prize was given for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Light was thought finished as a probe below 0.2 micrometres, a limit stated in 1873, until fluorescent labels switched on and off one at a time were used to get underneath it, work recognised by the 2014 chemistry Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A microscope therefore does not simply show what is there: what it shows is set by the radiation used, by what the specimen had to survive, and by what was labelled.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2014: Press Release,” 8 October 2014, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2014/press-release/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/Indian_Muntjac_fibroblast_cells_%2824271618921%29.jpg","title":"Cultured muntjac fibroblasts under a fluorescence microscope","desc":"Three dyes and three targets: the nuclei stained blue, the actin filaments green and the mitochondria red, with everything unlabelled left black. The scale bar is 20 micrometres. Photograph by ZEISS Microscopy, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"ZEISS Microscopy, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Muntjac_fibroblast_cells_%2824271618921%29.jpg","alt":"Against a black ground, four flat sprawling cells glow in three colours: bright green fibres run the length of each cell, an oval blue nucleus sits at the centre of each, and small red specks are scattered through the green, with a red scale bar marked 20 micrometres at the lower right"},"why":[{"q":"Why must cells be fixed and stained rather than simply looked at?","a":"Because a compound light microscope magnifies up to about a thousand times but contrast is too poor to see much that way, so cells are fixed, sectioned thinly and stained."},{"q":"Why does that preparation carry a standing caution?","a":"Because any treatment used for fixing and embedding may alter the structure of the cell or its molecules in undesirable ways, so what is seen has been through something first."},{"q":"Why does an electron microscope need frozen specimens?","a":"Because electrons are scattered by air, so the specimen sits in a vacuum where liquid water evaporates and unprotected biomolecules collapse; freezing water without crystallising lets a molecule keep its shape."}]},
{"id":"bio-012","num":12,"category":"What biology is","question":"Projecting a micrograph onto a wall enlarges it without limit, but two points closer than 0.2 micrometres still merge, because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and magnification are not the same thing.","questions":["The wavelength of the light and the numerical aperture of the lens together fix the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and no refinement of the glass can get past what those two allow.","A teaching light microscope magnifies about 400 times at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 200 nanometres, while an electron microscope magnifies 100,000 times and reaches 50 picometres."],"answer":"resolution","answerText":"resolution","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","technology"],"abstract":"Magnification is the enlargement of an object’s appearance, while <b>resolution</b> is an instrument’s ability to show two adjacent things as two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two come apart because an image can be enlarged as much as anyone likes, by projecting it on a screen, without ever separating two points the optics has already merged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The merging has a physical cause, since light behaves as a wave: a single point seen through a microscope appears as a blurred disc, and two close points give overlapping discs that read as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The limiting separation at which two objects stay distinct depends on the wavelength of the light and on the numerical aperture of the lens, which measures how wide the instrument opens its eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With violet light and a numerical aperture of 1.4 the floor is just under 0.2 micrometres, a figure reached by microscope makers at the end of the 19th century and rarely matched by factory optics since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In a teaching laboratory a light microscope magnifies about 400 times at a resolution near 200 nanometres,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and oil immersion takes the magnification to about a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An electron microscope magnifies 100,000 times and resolves about 50 picometres in principle,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> though lens aberrations and specimen damage hold it to a few nanometres on biological material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wall itself was stated in 1873, as half the wavelength of the light being used, and it stood for more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fluorescent labels switched on and off one at a time were used to get underneath it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while vitrified water let electron microscopy hold molecules in something near their real shape,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> so the ceiling has moved twice in living memory. The distinction is what survives all of it: magnification is cheap and resolution is not, which is why a micrograph is judged by the scale bar on it rather than by the number printed on the objective.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2014: Press Release,” 8 October 2014, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2014/press-release/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does more magnification not give a clearer picture?","a":"Because an image can be enlarged as much as anyone likes without ever separating two points the optics has already merged, which is resolution rather than magnification."},{"q":"Why is there a physical floor at all?","a":"Because light behaves as a wave, so a single point appears as a blurred disc and two close points give overlapping discs; with violet light and a numerical aperture of 1.4 that floor is just under 0.2 micrometres."},{"q":"Why is a micrograph judged by its scale bar?","a":"Because magnification is cheap and resolution is not, so the number printed on the objective says nothing about whether two adjacent things are actually shown as two."}]},
{"id":"bio-013","num":13,"category":"What biology is","question":"Yeast, a weed, a fly, a worm and a mouse are the species biology knows best, each chosen as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for being cheap and quick to breed rather than for being typical.","questions":["Because a nematode grows into an adult of exactly 959 cells, it serves as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which a whole course of development can be written down cell by cell.","Findings in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travel only as far as the shared machinery does, which is why mouse responses to burns and trauma turned out to match the human ones hardly at all."],"answer":"model organism","answerText":"model organism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"A <b>model organism</b> is a species studied in depth on the understanding that what is found in it will hold, with adjustments, for others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The set is small and each member was picked for convenience: yeast grows almost as fast as a bacterium, <i>Arabidopsis</i> produces thousands of seeds in eight to ten weeks, and a fruit fly goes from egg to adult in nine days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nematode <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i> was established as a model on those grounds, and every cell division from the fertilised egg to the 959 cells of the adult was then followed under a microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That lineage proved invariant, every worm running the same programme, and the genes controlling its cell deaths were found to have counterparts in higher animals including humans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of it would work if organisms did not share their machinery: every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it the same way,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and some cell-cycle genes work interchangeably in yeast and human cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The mouse is the foremost vertebrate model for the same reason, since about 85 per cent of the amino acids in a human protein are typically identical to those in an elephant’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inference is not guaranteed and can fail badly: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The second cost is what the choice does to the map, since effort concentrates where the tools already are. In one enzyme database the top eight eukaryotic species account for 37 per cent of all entries, 88 per cent of the species present carry fewer than ten entries each, and of 111 bacterial phyla only 24 appear at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A model organism is therefore a bargain rather than a representative sample: it buys depth in a few species, and the rest of the living world is known in proportion to how easily it keeps on a bench.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Genetic Information in Eucaryotes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26909/. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2002: Press Release,” 7 October 2002, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2002/press-release/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Junhee Seok, H. Shaw Warren, Alex G. Cuenca, Michael N. Mindrinos, Henry V. Baker, Weihong Xu, Daniel R. Richards, et al., “Genomic Responses in Mouse Models Poorly Mimic Human Inflammatory Diseases,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 9 (2013): 3507–12, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1222878110. [Open access]","Chelsea J. Vickers, Dean Fraga, and Wayne M. Patrick, “Quantifying the Taxonomic Bias in Enzymology,” <i>Protein Science</i> 30, no. 4 (2021): 914–21, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.4041. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Genetic_map_of_drosophila%2C_as_of_1926.png","title":"A linkage map of the fruit fly, published in 1926","desc":"The four linkage groups of Drosophila melanogaster set out as four lines, each mutant named and placed at its map distance from the top. This is what made the fly worth keeping: a species whose genetics could be written down. Public domain, from Thomas Hunt Morgan, The Theory of the Gene, 1926.","credit":"Thomas Hunt Morgan, The Theory of the Gene, 1926. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genetic_map_of_drosophila,_as_of_1926.png","alt":"A printed chart of four vertical lines headed with the Roman numerals one to four, each crossed by short tick marks with a number and a mutant name beside it, among them yellow, white, Notch, vermilion, black, purple, vestigial, ebony and eyeless"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the same few species keep being studied?","a":"Because each was picked for convenience: yeast grows almost as fast as a bacterium, Arabidopsis makes thousands of seeds in eight to ten weeks, and a fruit fly goes from egg to adult in nine days."},{"q":"Why should a finding in a worm be expected to hold in a human?","a":"Because organisms share their machinery — every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it the same way, and some cell-cycle genes work interchangeably in yeast and human cells."},{"q":"Why does the choice of models distort the map of life?","a":"Because effort concentrates where the tools already are: in one enzyme database the top eight eukaryotic species account for 37 per cent of entries, and of 111 bacterial phyla only 24 appear at all."}]},
{"id":"bio-014","num":14,"category":"What biology is","question":"Botany, zoology and microbiology divide life by group and physiology by process, while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cuts across every one of them by working at the scale where all of them are the same.","questions":["A sequence from a yeast, a fly, a worm or a bacterium can be lined up against the human one, which is what gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a claim on every other part of the subject.","Biochemistry, biophysics and bioinformatics are hybrid names of recent date, but <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one that reorganised the older divisions by group and by process."],"answer":"molecular biology","answerText":"molecular biology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"<b>Molecular biology</b> is the study of living things at the scale of the molecules that carry life out, and it is the field that cuts across every other way of dividing the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Biology divides first by group, so that botany, zoology and microbiology take a slice of the living world each,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with virology, mycology and entomology taking narrower slices still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It divides again by process, so that physiology asks how an organism works, genetics how it passes on what it is, developmental biology how it is built from one cell, and ecology how it lives among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It divides a third time by level, ecology alone running from the single organism through populations and communities to whole ecosystems, and the same ladder holding below the organism as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fourth division is by what the work is for, which is where medicine, agriculture, conservation, biotechnology and forensic science sit, each drawing on all of the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One field can cut across the rest because the machinery is shared, so a result in yeast, a fly, a worm, a plant or a bacterium can be lined up against the human version by sequence similarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why the boundaries are administrative rather than natural, and why so many of the newer names are hybrids: biochemistry, biophysics, neurobiology, bioinformatics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The divisions are how a person gets a purchase on the subject rather than how the subject is built, and no working biologist commands more than a corner of it. The reason is arithmetic as much as intellectual, since by 2023 the PubMed database held more than 36 million citations, was adding over 1.5 million a year, and indexed some 5,294 journals for MEDLINE alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A field is therefore also a way of choosing what to read, and the choice is forced: biology now produces more literature than any one reader can hold.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Genetic Information in Eucaryotes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26909/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “44.1 The Scope of Ecology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, “MEDLINE PubMed Production Statistics,” accessed 5 September 2026, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/bsd/medline_pubmed_production_stats.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can one field cut across every other division of biology?","a":"Because the machinery is shared, so a result in yeast, a fly, a worm, a plant or a bacterium can be lined up against the human version by sequence similarity."},{"q":"Why are the boundaries between fields called administrative?","a":"Because the divisions are how a person gets a purchase on the subject rather than how the subject is built, which is why so many newer names are hybrids like biochemistry and bioinformatics."},{"q":"Why is choosing a field also a way of choosing what to read?","a":"Because by 2023 PubMed held more than 36 million citations and was adding over 1.5 million a year, so biology now produces more literature than any one reader can hold."}]},
{"id":"bio-015","num":15,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because a cell obeys the same chemical rules as a test tube, the science of what happens inside one is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a field with a foot in each subject.","questions":["Oxygen, carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen make up most of a body, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the study of what those elements do once they are assembled into a living thing.","An organism can neither create energy nor escape the loss of some as heat, which is the sort of constraint <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exists to trace through a living system."],"answer":"biochemistry","answerText":"biochemistry","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Biochemistry</b> is the chemistry of living things, and it exists because the boundary between biology and chemistry is a boundary of interest rather than of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A body is made of the same elements as everything else, four of them accounting for most of it — oxygen at about 65 per cent, carbon 18, hydrogen 10 and nitrogen 3 — and the reactions between them obey the same rules inside a cell as outside one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Physics binds just as tightly: an organism cannot create energy, only take it in and transform it, and every transformation loses some of it as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is why a living thing is a costly arrangement, since it is highly ordered and holds that order only by spending energy continuously and pushing disorder into its surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What biology adds to the chemistry is not an exemption but a catalyst, and reactions that would otherwise need far higher temperatures are made to run at the temperature of a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The traffic runs the other way too, since physics supplies the instruments that make molecules visible, from the microscope to the imaging of frozen biomolecules that won the 2017 chemistry Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mathematics supplies the models, and population genetics is the clearest case, an account of how allele frequencies behave that is arithmetic before it is anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Geology supplies the timescale and the record, and the earth sciences are where the world a species evolved in has to be reconstructed from. None of this makes biology a branch of chemistry, because the questions differ: a chemist can say what a protein does, while a biologist asks why this organism has that protein and not another. The subject is therefore constrained by the sciences beneath it without being reducible to a list of their results, which is why its borders are the busiest part of it.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “2.1 Atoms, Isotopes, Ions, and Molecules: The Building Blocks,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/2-1-atoms-isotopes-ions-and-molecules-the-building-blocks. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.3 The Laws of Thermodynamics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-3-the-laws-of-thermodynamics. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “19.2 Population Genetics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-2-population-genetics. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the line between biology and chemistry one of interest only?","a":"Because a body is made of the same elements as everything else, and the reactions between them obey the same rules inside a cell as outside one."},{"q":"Why is being alive an expensive arrangement?","a":"Because an organism is highly ordered and can create no energy of its own, so it holds that order only by spending energy continuously and pushing disorder into its surroundings."},{"q":"Why is biology not simply a branch of chemistry?","a":"Because the questions differ: a chemist can say what a protein does, while a biologist asks why this organism has that protein and not another."}]},
{"id":"bio-016","num":16,"category":"What biology is","question":"A molecule is not alive and a cell is, which is the standard case of an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: something a whole has that none of its parts has on its own.","questions":["Each starling in a flock attends to six or seven neighbours and to nothing else, so the shape of the whole flock is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that no bird in it represents.","Philosophers hold that an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must both depend on its base and be somewhat autonomous of it, so an account dropping either half is describing something else."],"answer":"emergent property","answerText":"emergent property","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","nature"],"abstract":"An <b>emergent property</b> is one a whole has and its parts do not, which in biology is the ordinary case rather than a curiosity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell gathers raw materials from its surroundings and builds a new cell in its own image, and nothing less than a cell can do that, so being alive appears at that level and not below it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Philosophers pair two claims deliberately here, since emergence is meant to join dependence on a base with some autonomy from it, and an account that drops either half stops being about emergence at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also divide the notion, weak emergence treating the higher-level facts as nothing over and above the base, and strong emergence claiming a greater distinctness between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What emergence is not, in either version, is the merely surprising, since being hard to predict is a fact about the predictor rather than about the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Biology supplies the clearest examples because its hierarchy is built by composition, running from atoms and molecules through cells and tissues to populations, communities and ecosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A flock is a case that has been measured rather than asserted, since each starling attends to a fixed number of neighbours, six or seven, rather than to every bird within a set distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No bird holds the shape of the flock, and the rule each one follows contains no description of it, yet the flock keeps its cohesion as its density changes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same shape of explanation runs downwards, since regulation is a property of a loop rather than of any part of it, and a sensor, a set point and an effector on their own regulate nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Emergence is therefore a reason to work at more than one level rather than a licence to stop explaining, and the useful question is always which properties of a whole its parts already fix.","sources":["Timothy O’Connor, “Emergent Properties,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2021 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2021/entries/properties-emergent/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","M. Ballerini, N. Cabibbo, R. Candelier, A. Cavagna, E. Cisbani, I. Giardina, V. Lecomte, et al., “Interaction Ruling Animal Collective Behavior Depends on Topological Rather than Metric Distance: Evidence from a Field Study,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 4 (2008): 1232–37, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0711437105. [Open access]","Harold Modell, William Cliff, Joel Michael, Jenny McFarland, Mary Pat Wenderoth, and Ann Wright, “A Physiologist’s View of Homeostasis,” <i>Advances in Physiology Education</i> 39, no. 4 (2015): 259–66, https://doi.org/10.1152/advan.00107.2015. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is emergence not the same as being surprising?","a":"Because being hard to predict is a fact about the predictor rather than about the world, whereas emergence pairs dependence on a base with some autonomy from it."},{"q":"Why is a starling flock the example that has been measured?","a":"Because each bird attends to a fixed number of neighbours, six or seven, rather than to every bird within a set distance, and the rule each follows contains no description of the flock’s shape."},{"q":"Why is emergence not a licence to stop explaining?","a":"Because it is a reason to work at more than one level, and the useful question is always which properties of a whole its parts already fix."}]},
{"id":"bio-017","num":17,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because a shape that looks purpose-built is only a hypothesis until performance is measured, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tests how far a structure's form is explained by what it does.","questions":["Wrasses and cichlids keep a similar jaw linkage across very different diets, a many-to-one result that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached by measuring mechanics rather than by comparing shapes.","Bone responds to the loads placed on it and also to hormones and to age, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> treats internal architecture as evidence rather than as proof."],"answer":"functional morphology","answerText":"functional morphology","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"<b>Functional morphology</b> studies the relationship between the form of a biological structure and what that structure does, and its working question is how far functional demand explains a shape as against inheritance and development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tightest case is molecular, since the precise shape of a protein molecule determines its function in the cell, and that shape is specified by the order of amino acids along the chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The evidence there is an experiment rather than an inference, because a protein unfolded by a denaturing solvent will often refold into its original conformation once the solvent is taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the level of a tissue the relationship is looser but still measurable, since bone responds to the mechanical loads placed on it, which is why the internal architecture of a bone is read as a record of how an animal moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even there the reading is not clean, since bone is also affected by hormones, metabolism and age, so a difference in architecture need not be a difference in loading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Whole organs are studied by measuring performance rather than by inspecting a shape, and the answer is often not the one the anatomy suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In fishes that feed by suction, skeletal levers and linkages transmit muscle force into a three-dimensional expansion of the mouth cavity, but the power for that expansion comes mostly from large regions of the body muscles rather than from the head alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The relationship also runs many to one, since different morphologies can produce similar mechanical properties, so several shapes may do a job equally well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wrasses and cichlids show that, their four-bar jaw linkages keeping much the same geometry across diets that differ in hardness and in how far the prey can flee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A structure's use therefore has to be established rather than assumed, and a shape that looks purpose-built is a hypothesis about performance until somebody measures it.","sources":["Alexander Blanke, Helmut Schmitz, Alessandra Patera, Hugo Dutel, and Michael J. Fagan, “Form–Function Relationships in Dragonfly Mandibles under an Evolutionary Perspective,” <i>Journal of the Royal Society Interface</i> 14, no. 128 (2017): 20161038, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2016.1038. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Christine M. Harper and Biren A. Patel, “Functional Morphology of Trabecular Bone in the Calcaneus of African Apes,” <i>Journal of Anatomy</i> 249, no. 4 (2026): 883–98, https://doi.org/10.1111/joa.70141. [Open access]","Ariel L. Camp and Sam Van Wassenbergh, “A Mechanical Perspective on Suction Feeding in Fishes,” <i>Journal of Experimental Biology</i> 228, no. 18 (2025): jeb250567, https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.250567. [Open access]","H. Camarillo, E. D. Burress, and M. M. Muñoz, “Four-Bar Geometry Is Shared among Ecologically Divergent Fish Species,” <i>Integrative Organismal Biology</i> 6, no. 1 (2024): obae019, https://doi.org/10.1093/iob/obae019. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is protein shape the tightest case of form following function?","a":"Because the shape is specified by the order of amino acids, and it can be shown experimentally: a protein unfolded by a denaturing solvent will often refold into its original conformation once the solvent is removed."},{"q":"Why is bone architecture not a clean record of how an animal moved?","a":"Because bone is also affected by hormones, metabolism and age, so a difference in internal architecture need not be a difference in mechanical loading."},{"q":"Why must a structure’s use be measured rather than read off its shape?","a":"Because the relationship runs many to one and the anatomy can mislead: in suction-feeding fishes the power for expanding the mouth comes mostly from the body muscles rather than the head."}]},
{"id":"bio-018","num":18,"category":"What biology is","question":"Because the rules of translation are partly arbitrary and yet the same in nearly every cell, biologists read that shared machinery as evidence of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than of convergence.","questions":["The genes of the translation system are conserved across bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, which is the usual evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a formal proof of it is thought unlikely.","A single origin for every cellular organism alive is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asserts, and the ciliates that read stop codons as amino acids are the qualification it has to carry."],"answer":"common descent","answerText":"common descent","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Common descent</b> is the claim that all cellular life on Earth has a single origin, and it rests on the universality of the genetic code and on the conservation of the genes that carry out translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shared machinery is specific rather than vague, since every cell stores its hereditary information in DNA, transcribes portions of it into RNA, and translates that RNA into protein in a way that is identical, with minor exceptions, in all living things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What makes the sharing evidence is that the rules are in part arbitrary, so a code with no chemical necessity behind it is better explained as an accident frozen into the earliest organisms and inherited since than as a solution each lineage found for itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument does not rest on the code alone, the genes of the translation system being conserved across the whole of cellular life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its own advocates state the limit plainly, holding that the comparative-genomic evidence is overwhelming while a formal demonstration is unlikely to be feasible in principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The word universal has to be qualified, because ciliates reassign stop codons to amino acids, and of 24 species whose codes were inferred from marine transcriptomes, nine matched no code table then on record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of them, <i>Condylostoma magnum</i>, reads all three standard stop codons as amino acids inside a message and as a stop only close to its far end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that unity the diversity is enormous and badly counted, the most cited estimate putting eukaryotic species at about 8.7 million, give or take 1.3 million, with some 1.2 million catalogued so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Published estimates run from 2 million to 3 trillion, about 80% of the 8.7 million are hypothetical, and the disagreement cannot be settled without a better count of bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Unity and diversity are therefore claims of very different quality, one inferred from machinery every cell carries and the other extrapolated from the small fraction of the planet anyone has yet examined.","sources":["Eugene V. Koonin and Yuri I. Wolf, “The Common Ancestry of Life,” <i>Biology Direct</i> 5 (2010): 64, https://doi.org/10.1186/1745-6150-5-64. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Stephen M. Heaphy, Marco Mariotti, Vadim N. Gladyshev, John F. Atkins, and Pavel V. Baranov, “Novel Ciliate Genetic Code Variants Including the Reassignment of All Three Stop Codons to Sense Codons in <i>Condylostoma magnum</i>,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 33, no. 11 (2016): 2885–89, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msw166. [Open access]","Camilo Mora, Derek P. Tittensor, Sina Adl, Alastair G. B. Simpson, and Boris Worm, “How Many Species Are There on Earth and in the Ocean?,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 9, no. 8 (2011): e1001127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001127. [Open access]","John J. Wiens, “How Many Species Are There on Earth? Progress and Problems,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 21, no. 11 (2023): e3002388, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3002388. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a shared genetic code taken as evidence of one origin?","a":"Because the rules are in part arbitrary, so a code with no chemical necessity behind it is better explained as an accident frozen into the earliest organisms than as a solution each lineage found separately."},{"q":"Why does the word universal have to be qualified?","a":"Because ciliates reassign stop codons to amino acids, and of 24 species whose codes were inferred from marine transcriptomes, nine matched no code table then on record."},{"q":"Why are the claims of unity and of diversity of different quality?","a":"Because unity is inferred from machinery every cell carries, while the species count is extrapolated from a small fraction of the planet, with published estimates running from 2 million to 3 trillion."}]},
{"id":"bio-019","num":19,"category":"What biology is","question":"Twelve identical bacterial populations went 30,000 generations before one of them began using citrate, which is the clearest measurement anyone has made of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in evolution.","questions":["Because the exponent linking metabolic rate to body mass turns out to vary systematically, biology's nearest thing to a law is better read as a product of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than of physical necessity.","Philosophers hold that a species is an individual rather than a kind, one reason the subject states so few laws and leans instead on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to explain what it finds."],"answer":"contingency","answerText":"contingency","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Contingency</b> is the dependence of an outcome on the particular sequence of events that produced it, so that the same starting point run again need not arrive anywhere near the same place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philosophers of science once treated the absence of physics-style laws in biology as a shortcoming of the subject and now treat it as a legitimate practice of a different kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There are hardly any universal laws of the Newtonian sort in biology, and its best-known generalisations, Mendel's among them, are described as having exceptions and as holding only other things being equal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One argument for that is that biology adds nothing to the laws of nature, and shows instead how the laws of physics and chemistry play out under particular initial and boundary conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another is that a species is an individual rather than a kind, its organisms being parts of it rather than instances of it, so there can be no law about a species in the traditional sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The nearest thing biology has to a law is the scaling of metabolic rate with body mass, long assumed to follow an exponent of two-thirds or three-quarters because physical constraint required it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hundreds of studies have since documented systematic variation in that exponent, which has turned the subject from a search for a constant into an inquiry into why the value moves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Contingency itself has been measured rather than argued: twelve identical populations of <i>Escherichia coli</i> founded in 1988 went more than 30,000 generations without any of them using the citrate in their medium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One population then did, and replaying its history from frozen samples produced no citrate-users among 8.4 trillion ancestral cells while later clones evolved it readily, so a potentiating mutation had arisen by 20,000 generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the near-universal genetic code is a case of the same thing, its arbitrary features being read as an accident frozen into the earliest cells rather than as a necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Zachary D. Blount, Christina Z. Borland, and Richard E. Lenski, “Historical Contingency and the Evolution of a Key Innovation in an Experimental Population of <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 23 (2008): 7899–906, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0803151105. [Open access]","Alexander Reutlinger, Gerhard Schurz, Andreas Hüttemann, and Siegfried Jaag, “Ceteris Paribus Laws,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2024/entries/ceteris-paribus/. [Open access]","Jay Odenbaugh and Paul Griffiths, “Philosophy of Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2024/entries/biology-philosophy/. [Open access]","Douglas S. Glazier, “Variable Metabolic Scaling Breaks the Law: From ‘Newtonian’ to ‘Darwinian’ Approaches,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 289, no. 1985 (2022): 20221605, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2022.1605. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does biology have so few laws of the Newtonian sort?","a":"Because it adds nothing to the laws of nature and shows instead how physics and chemistry play out under particular initial and boundary conditions, so its generalisations hold only other things being equal."},{"q":"Why can there be no law about a species in the traditional sense?","a":"Because a species is an individual rather than a kind, its organisms being parts of it rather than instances of it, so there is nothing for a law to quantify over."},{"q":"Why is the Escherichia coli experiment evidence rather than illustration?","a":"Because replaying the citrate-user’s history from frozen samples produced none among 8.4 trillion ancestral cells while later clones evolved it readily, showing a potentiating mutation had arisen on the way."}]},
{"id":"bio-020","num":20,"category":"What biology is","question":"A correlation between two measurements may be the work of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than of any influence between them, which is why biologists reach for an intervention.","questions":["Germ-free mice colonised with another animal's microbiota gained fat while eating less, an intervention worth running because an association can always be produced by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead.","Genetic variants serve as natural experiments because they are fixed at conception, though shared variation can still produce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of its own between a risk factor and an outcome."],"answer":"confounding","answerText":"confounding","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","research methods"],"abstract":"<b>Confounding</b> is what most often makes a correlation fail as evidence of cause, some third factor moving both of the things compared so that they rise and fall together with neither driving the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other standard failure is reverse causation, where the outcome alters the supposed cause: people with symptoms of heart disease may drink less alcohol, which makes drinking look protective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One way round both is to use genetic variants as natural experiments, a variant being fixed at conception and so beyond the reach of the disease it is being tested against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not a clean escape either, since shared genetic variation can itself influence a supposed risk factor and an outcome at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older biological answer is a sequence rather than a statistic: establish the association, isolate the suspected agent, then show that the agent alone reproduces the effect in something that did not have it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The gut microbiome is where that sequence is being worked through now, its composition having been associated with obesity and with insulin resistance in large cohorts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One such association did not survive sensitivity analyses using twin and sibling-matched data, which is what residual confounding looks like when somebody catches it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The causal step is an intervention, and germ-free mice colonised with the microbiota of normal animals gained adipose tissue and insulin resistance while eating less chow than the animals they were compared with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where the experiment can be run on people it sometimes reverses the expectation, as when beta-carotene given to prevent lung cancer raised the risk instead, at a pooled relative risk of 1.16 across eight trials and 167,141 participants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A correlation is the beginning of a biological question rather than an answer to one, and what settles it is an intervention somebody can actually perform.","sources":["Neil M. Davies, Michael V. Holmes, and George Davey Smith, “Reading Mendelian Randomisation Studies: A Guide, Glossary, and Checklist for Clinicians,” <i>BMJ</i> 362 (2018): k601, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k601. [Open access]","Roshni A. Patel, Joshua G. Schraiber, Matt Pennell, and Michael D. Edge, “Observational Epidemiological Studies Can Mitigate Genetic Confounding with Genetic Relatedness Matrices,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 19 (2026): e2533909123, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2533909123. [Open access]","David W. Evans, “How to Gain Evidence for Causation in Disease and Therapeutic Intervention: From Koch's Postulates to Counter-Counterfactuals,” <i>Medicine, Health Care and Philosophy</i> 25, no. 3 (2022): 509–21, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11019-022-10096-x. [Open access]","Connor Prosty, Khaled Katergi, Jesse Papenburg, Alexander Lawandi, Todd C. Lee, et al., “Causal Role of the Gut Microbiome in Certain Human Diseases: A Narrative Review,” <i>eGastroenterology</i> 2, no. 3 (2024): e100086, https://doi.org/10.1136/egastro-2024-100086. [Open access]","Jacek Kordiak, Filip Bielec, Sławomir Jabłoński, and Dorota Pastuszak-Lewandoska, “Role of Beta-Carotene in Lung Cancer Primary Chemoprevention: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis and Meta-Regression,” <i>Nutrients</i> 14, no. 7 (2022): 1361, https://doi.org/10.3390/nu14071361. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a correlation so often fail as evidence of cause?","a":"Because some third factor may move both things compared, or the outcome may alter the supposed cause — people with symptoms of heart disease drink less, which makes drinking look protective."},{"q":"Why are genetic variants used as natural experiments?","a":"Because a variant is fixed at conception and so beyond the reach of the disease being tested against, though shared genetic variation can still influence a risk factor and an outcome at once."},{"q":"Why does an intervention settle what a cohort cannot?","a":"Because it can reverse the expectation: beta-carotene given to prevent lung cancer raised the risk instead, at a pooled relative risk of 1.16 across eight trials and 167,141 participants."}]},
{"id":"bio-021","num":21,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur are common to all known life, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what an instrument looks for when testing whether somewhere could be habitable.","questions":["Four of the six elements of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> account for 96.5% of an organism's weight, a composition quite unlike that of the rock the organism is standing on.","Nitrogen limits plankton growth in the subtropical gyres and iron does so in upwelling regions, so even the elements of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not equally available everywhere."],"answer":"CHNOPS","answerText":"CHNOPS","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>CHNOPS</b> is the set of six elements common to all known life — carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur — and their presence is one of the conditions of habitability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 92 naturally occurring elements, organisms are built from only a small selection, four of which, carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen, make up 96.5% of an organism's weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That composition differs markedly from the abundances in the Earth's crust, which is itself evidence of a distinctive chemistry rather than of life simply using whatever lay to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Carbon supplies the frame, having four electrons and four vacancies in its outer shell, so it forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms in chains and rings with no obvious upper limit on size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phosphorus shows how specific a job an element can hold: about 0.9 kg (2 pounds) sits in a human body, up to 90% of it as apatite in bones and teeth, the rest largely in the phosphate links of DNA and RNA and in the esters that carry energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The proportions are regular enough at sea to be assumed, and models of the carbon cycle give ocean particles a mean carbon to nitrogen to phosphorus ratio of 106 to 16 to 1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured instead, the ratio shifts from one ocean region to another with how much protein, carbohydrate and lipid the plankton are carrying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which element runs short first shifts as well, nitrogen limiting growth in the stratified subtropical gyres, iron in upwelling regions, and the two together in the water between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Manganese can be limiting alongside iron in parts of the Southern Ocean, so an element present in traces can still be the one that stops everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deciding which elements are genuinely essential is harder than a list of six suggests, since genes do not code for elements but for particular chemical species, an element in a given oxidation state with particular groups around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Karen Donaldson, Jonah Mack, Yuchen Shang, Ian Underwood, and Charles Cockell, “The Design of a Bioinspired Integrated Total Habitability Instrument for Planetary Exploration,” <i>Biomimetics</i> 10, no. 11 (2025): 742, https://doi.org/10.3390/biomimetics10110742. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Prinessa Chellan and Peter J. Sadler, “The Elements of Life and Medicines,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2037 (2015): 20140182, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0182. [Open access]","Justin D. Liefer, Angelicque E. White, Zoe V. Finkel, Andrew J. Irwin, et al., “Latitudinal Patterns in Ocean C:N:P Reflect Phytoplankton Acclimation and Macromolecular Composition,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 46 (2024): e2404460121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2404460121. [Open access]","Thomas J. Browning and C. Mark Moore, “Global Analysis of Ocean Phytoplankton Nutrient Limitation Reveals High Prevalence of Co-limitation,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 14 (2023): 5014, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-40774-0. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is life’s composition read as evidence of a distinctive chemistry?","a":"Because organisms are built from a small selection of the 92 natural elements, four of them making up 96.5 per cent of an organism’s weight, and that differs markedly from the abundances in the Earth’s crust."},{"q":"Why is carbon the element that supplies the frame?","a":"Because it has four electrons and four vacancies in its outer shell, so it forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms in chains and rings with no obvious upper limit on size."},{"q":"Why is a list of six elements harder to justify than it looks?","a":"Because genes do not code for elements but for particular chemical species — an element in a given oxidation state with particular groups around it — and an element present only in traces can still be the one that stops everything."}]},
{"id":"bio-022","num":22,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Because the character of a cell's materials depends on how its atoms are linked rather than on which elements are present, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the working unit of biological chemistry.","questions":["A cubic micrometre of bacterium or yeast is estimated to hold two to four million proteins, which is the order of how many of one kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a cell carries.","One carbon atom is about 0.2 nm across, and a cluster of such atoms joined by covalent bonds is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose mass is quoted in daltons."],"answer":"molecule","answerText":"molecule","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>molecule</b> is a cluster of atoms held together by covalent bonds, and it is where the materials a cell is built from take on their character, which depends on how the atoms are linked rather than on which elements are present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An atom is a nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons; the proton count is the atomic number and fixes the chemistry, while the neutrons may vary, giving isotopes that are physically distinguishable and chemically identical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carbon atom is roughly 0.2 nm across, so about 5 million of them in a line would span a millimetre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Masses are quoted in daltons, and quantities are counted in moles, one mole being Avogadro's number of units, a figure that is now exact by definition at 6.02214076 × 10<sup>23</sup> per mole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The small organic molecules of a cell have molecular weights between 100 and 1000 and carry up to about 30 carbon atoms, sitting free in solution as subunits for larger molecules, as fuel, or as both at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A cell is water, inorganic ions and carbon-containing molecules, with water the most abundant of the three at 70% or more of the total mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting the rest is harder than it sounds, and an estimate built from the protein fraction of cell mass and the average length of a protein gives 2 to 4 million protein molecules in a cubic micrometre of bacterium, yeast or mammalian cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured proteome censuses of yeast and mammalian cells often come out 3 to 10 times lower than that, a discrepancy the estimate was published to expose rather than to settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Numbers of this kind are scattered so widely that finding even a simple one takes a long time, which is why they are gathered into a curated database with each figure's source attached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Counting is the point: a cell is a definite number of definite objects, and the chemistry becomes biology when somebody says how many.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Avogadro Constant,” CODATA Internationally Recommended 2022 Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?na. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ron Milo, “What Is the Total Number of Protein Molecules per Cell Volume? A Call to Rethink Some Published Values,” <i>BioEssays</i> 35, no. 12 (2013): 1050–55, https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.201300066. [Open access]","Ron Milo, Paul Jorgensen, Uri Moran, Griffin Weber, and Michael Springer, “BioNumbers—the Database of Key Numbers in Molecular and Cell Biology,” <i>Nucleic Acids Research</i> 38, suppl. 1 (2010): D750–53, https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkp889. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a molecule’s character depend on more than its elements?","a":"Because it depends on how the atoms are linked by covalent bonds rather than on which elements are present, which is why the same elements build materials of quite different kinds."},{"q":"Why can isotopes be used as labels without changing the chemistry?","a":"Because the proton count fixes an atom’s chemistry while the neutron number may vary, so isotopes are physically distinguishable and chemically identical."},{"q":"Why was an estimate of protein numbers published to be contradicted?","a":"Because measured proteome censuses of yeast and mammalian cells come out 3 to 10 times lower than the estimate built from cell mass, and the point was to expose that discrepancy rather than settle it."}]},
{"id":"bio-023","num":23,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"The energy needed to pull two atoms apart is what defines the strength of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that number decides whether heat alone can undo it inside a cell.","questions":["Streptavidin holds biotin at a dissociation constant near 10 femtomolar without a single covalent link, which shows how far a crowd of weak examples of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can go.","A cell keeps its structures because one kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is about a hundred times stronger than the knocks molecules take from their neighbours."],"answer":"chemical bond","answerText":"chemical bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>chemical bond</b> is an attraction holding atoms together, and its strength is measured by the energy that must be supplied to break it, conventionally quoted in kilocalories per mole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That figure is what decides how the bond behaves inside a cell, since a typical covalent bond is about a hundred times stronger than the thermal knocks molecules take from their neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Covalent bonds are therefore not undone by heat but by particular chemical reactions, which in cells are controlled by highly specific catalysts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Noncovalent bonds are as a rule much weaker, and that weakness is the point, since a great deal of biology consists of molecules that must associate and come apart again readily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their strength also depends on where they are: water forms hydrogen bonds with polar molecules and surrounds ions, which is why ionic compounds and polar molecules dissolve in it while covalent bond strengths are unaffected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The weak ones matter because they add up, and large numbers of hydrogen bonds between adjacent regions of a folded protein chain help to hold its three-dimensional shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A crowd of them can be formidable: streptavidin binds the small molecule biotin at a dissociation constant of about 10<sup>−14</sup> M, one of the strongest noncovalent interactions known, with no covalent link involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In DNA the same arithmetic is predictive rather than descriptive, the stability of a double helix being calculated from its sequence by adding up the contributions of neighbouring base pairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One such set of parameters, derived from 108 short duplexes, reconciled seven laboratories whose published values had appeared to disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bond strength is not a chemical footnote but the reason a cell can be stable and quick at once: covalent where nothing should change, weak and numerous where things must.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Ian D. Wadsworth, Komal Sethi, Sara Abu Jalboush, LeAnn Rogers, Thomas Hollis, and Adam R. Hall, “Scalable Production of Monovalent Streptavidin with High Yield and Purity,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 20814, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-51631-7. [Open access]","John SantaLucia, “A Unified View of Polymer, Dumbbell, and Oligonucleotide DNA Nearest-Neighbor Thermodynamics,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 4 (1998): 1460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.4.1460. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a covalent bond not undone by heat inside a cell?","a":"Because it is about a hundred times stronger than the thermal knocks molecules take from their neighbours, so it is broken instead by particular reactions under highly specific catalysts."},{"q":"Why is the weakness of a noncovalent bond the point of it?","a":"Because a great deal of biology consists of molecules that must associate and come apart again readily, which a bond as strong as a covalent one would prevent."},{"q":"Why can weak bonds still produce an enormously tight grip?","a":"Because they add up: streptavidin binds biotin at a dissociation constant of about 10−14 M, one of the strongest noncovalent interactions known, with no covalent link involved."}]},
{"id":"bio-024","num":24,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Two atoms that complete their outer shells by sharing a pair of electrons rather than transferring them are joined by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the strongest link a cell builds with.","questions":["A single bond lets one part of a molecule rotate about it while a double one does not, so the rigidity of a molecule is set by which kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it carries.","Penicillin works by leaving a permanent chemical adduct on its target, which is to say that the drug forms a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the enzyme it disables."],"answer":"covalent bond","answerText":"covalent bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>covalent bond</b> is formed when two atoms complete their outer electron shells by sharing a pair of electrons rather than by transferring them from one to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sharing is often unequal, one atom drawing the pair towards itself, and the result is a polar covalent bond that carries a partial charge at each end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two atoms may share more than one pair, and a double bond is shorter and stronger than a single one and, unlike a single bond, prevents the two parts of the molecule from rotating about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbon's four bonds point towards the corners of a regular tetrahedron, which is where the three-dimensional geometry of organic molecules comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a protein each amino acid is joined to the next by a covalent peptide bond, formed as a molecule of water is lost between them, and the repeating chain of atoms that results is the backbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some proteins carry covalent cross-links as well, and the commonest are sulfur-sulfur bonds made as a protein is prepared for export, which act as staples reinforcing a shape the chain has already found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peptide bond is planar, and quantum-chemical work attributes the tight backbone hydrogen-bond angle of about 155 degrees to the distribution of electrons around that plane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Planarity is an approximation rather than a law, and a neutron structure at 1.2 Å resolution shows the amide protons sitting off the plane, shifted towards the atoms they hydrogen-bond to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Covalent bonding is also how a large family of drugs works, since penicillin and the other beta-lactams disable their target by forming a covalent adduct with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same property is the risk: a reactive drug can form adducts with proteins it was never aimed at, which is why covalent medicines are watched for side effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Kuan Pern Tan, Khushboo Singh, Anirban Hazra, and M. S. Madhusudhan, “Peptide Bond Planarity Constrains Hydrogen Bond Geometry and Influences Secondary Structure Conformations,” <i>Current Research in Structural Biology</i> 3 (2021): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crstbi.2020.11.002. [Open access]","Yuya Hanazono, Yu Hirano, Kazuki Takeda, Katsuhiro Kusaka, Taro Tamada, and Kunio Miki, “Revisiting the Concept of Peptide Bond Planarity in an Iron-Sulfur Protein by Neutron Structure Analysis,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 20 (2022): eabn2276, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abn2276. [Open access]","Ghazaleh Jafari and Dustin Duncan, “Covalent Inhibitors in Antimicrobial Drug Development—Beyond β-Lactams,” <i>Molecules</i> 31, no. 12 (2026): 2186, https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules31122186. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a double bond change a molecule’s shape and not just its strength?","a":"Because it is shorter and stronger than a single bond and, unlike a single bond, prevents the two parts of the molecule from rotating about it."},{"q":"Why do organic molecules have the three-dimensional geometry they do?","a":"Because carbon’s four bonds point towards the corners of a regular tetrahedron, which sets the shapes the rest of the chemistry is built out of."},{"q":"Why is the same property that makes a covalent drug work also its risk?","a":"Because penicillin and the other beta-lactams disable their target by forming a covalent adduct with it, and a reactive drug can form adducts with proteins it was never aimed at."}]},
{"id":"bio-025","num":25,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Because one atom hands electrons to another instead of sharing them, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> leaves two ions of opposite charge attracting each other across the gap.","questions":["Sodium chloride is called a salt rather than a molecule because its crystal is a balanced lattice of ions held by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a cluster of shared electrons.","An ion in water travels with a shell of water molecules around it, which is why an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is far weaker in a cell than in a dry crystal."],"answer":"ionic bond","answerText":"ionic bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"An <b>ionic bond</b> forms when one atom transfers electrons to another rather than sharing them, leaving two ions of opposite charge that attract one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A solid held together this way is a lattice rather than a molecule, which is why sodium chloride is called a salt: a crystal a millimetre across holds about 2 × 10<sup>19</sup> ions of each kind in a regular array with the charges exactly balanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water takes such a solid apart, since ionic bonds are greatly weakened in it and each ion leaves surrounded by its own group of water molecules, while covalent bond strengths are untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a fact about water rather than about the salt, water molecules being polar and able to interact with charged ions as well as with other polar molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside a protein the same attraction turns up as one of the three kinds of weak bond that fold the chain, each 30 to 300 times weaker than a covalent bond and useful only in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because an ion in water carries a shell of water with it, moving one across a membrane means stripping that shell away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ion channels do exactly that, a permeating ion shedding most or all of its water to pass the narrowest part of the channel, which is what makes the channel selective and puts a ceiling on its rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The gradients that result are a cell's electrical state, and in animal cells the membrane potential rests mainly on potassium leak channels and the potassium gradient across the plasma membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ions also hold larger molecules in shape, magnesium being the clearest case, since it defines much of the structural and catalytic chemistry of RNA and binds either directly or through its own shell of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An ionic bond is therefore weak, wet and everywhere, which is the opposite of the covalent bond's role in a cell and the reason both are needed.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/. [Open access]","J. A. Cowan, “Understanding the Thermodynamics of Magnesium Binding to RNA Structural Motifs,” <i>Life</i> 14, no. 6 (2024): 765, https://doi.org/10.3390/life14060765. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is an ionic solid called a salt rather than a molecule?","a":"Because it is a lattice: a sodium chloride crystal a millimetre across holds about 2 × 10¹⁹ ions of each kind in a regular array with the charges exactly balanced."},{"q":"Why does water dissolve a salt but leave covalent molecules intact?","a":"Because ionic bonds are greatly weakened in water, each ion leaving surrounded by its own group of water molecules, while covalent bond strengths are untouched — a fact about water rather than about the salt."},{"q":"Why does moving an ion across a membrane have a speed limit?","a":"Because an ion in water carries a shell of water with it, and a permeating ion must shed most or all of that water to pass the narrowest part of a channel, which is also what makes the channel selective."}]},
{"id":"bio-026","num":26,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water is a liquid at room temperature only because each molecule is drawn to its neighbours by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a link so weak that thermal motion breaks it constantly.","questions":["The alpha helix and the beta sheet form from links between the N-H and C=O groups of a protein's backbone alone, so this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be made by almost any sequence.","A always pairs with T and G with C because a purine and a pyrimidine of matching width can be joined across the helix by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at each contact."],"answer":"hydrogen bond","answerText":"hydrogen bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>hydrogen bond</b> is the weak attraction between a hydrogen atom already bound to an electron-hungry atom and another such atom nearby, as when one of water's hydrogens is drawn towards a neighbouring molecule's oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taken singly they are feeble and are broken by ordinary thermal motion, so each lasts an exceedingly short time and liquid water is a network of them continually breaking and re-forming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aggregate effect is not small: it is only because of these bonds between its molecules that water is a liquid at room temperature, with a high boiling point and high surface tension, rather than a gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are at their strongest when the three atoms involved lie on a straight line, and water weakens hydrogen bonds between dissolved molecules by offering competing partners of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In proteins the two commonest folding patterns, the alpha helix and the beta sheet, arise from hydrogen bonds between the N-H and C=O groups of the backbone alone, which is why many different amino acid sequences can form them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The geometry is measured rather than assumed: across a large body of solved structures the backbone-to-backbone bonds sit at a tightly restricted angle near 155 degrees, and the donor-acceptor distance varies with how deeply the pair is buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In DNA the two chains are held to each other by hydrogen bonds between the bases, a bulky two-ring purine always facing a single-ring pyrimidine, with A opposite T and G opposite C.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is what lets the double helix be regular, since every pair is the same width and the sugar-phosphate backbones can run outside the stack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The arrangement is quantitative as well as neat, a duplex's stability being computed from its sequence by summing neighbouring pairs, which is why one stretch of DNA comes apart before another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A bond that cannot hold anything on its own turns out to hold water together, fold proteins and carry the genetic code, entirely by being made in numbers.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Kuan Pern Tan, Khushboo Singh, Anirban Hazra, and M. S. Madhusudhan, “Peptide Bond Planarity Constrains Hydrogen Bond Geometry and Influences Secondary Structure Conformations,” <i>Current Research in Structural Biology</i> 3 (2021): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crstbi.2020.11.002. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Structure and Function of DNA,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/. [Open access]","John SantaLucia, “A Unified View of Polymer, Dumbbell, and Oligonucleotide DNA Nearest-Neighbor Thermodynamics,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 4 (1998): 1460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.4.1460. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is water a liquid at room temperature?","a":"Because of the hydrogen bonds between its molecules, which also give it a high boiling point and high surface tension, even though each individual bond lasts an exceedingly short time."},{"q":"Why can many different amino acid sequences form the same fold?","a":"Because the alpha helix and the beta sheet arise from hydrogen bonds between the N-H and C=O groups of the backbone alone, which every sequence carries."},{"q":"Why does base pairing let the double helix stay regular?","a":"Because a bulky two-ring purine always faces a single-ring pyrimidine, A opposite T and G opposite C, so every pair is the same width and the backbones can run outside the stack."}]},
{"id":"bio-027","num":27,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"About 70% of a cell's weight is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and nearly every reaction inside one takes place dissolved in it rather than dry.","questions":["Nonpolar side chains pack into a protein's interior because they disturb the hydrogen-bonded network of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around them, which is what folds much of the chain.","A tardigrade in anhydrobiosis survives losing almost all its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the longer it stays in that state the longer it takes to revive."],"answer":"water","answerText":"water","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Water</b> is the most abundant substance in a cell, accounting for about 70% of its weight, and most reactions inside a cell take place in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell is water, inorganic ions and carbon-containing molecules, and a great deal of biological chemistry is simply the dealings between the first of those and the other two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The molecule is polar, its hydrogens carrying a slight positive charge and its oxygen a slight negative one, so it hydrogen-bonds to itself and interacts with ions and other polar molecules, which is why they dissolve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Life began in the ocean, and those conditions left a permanent mark on the chemistry of living things; water is also what pushes nonpolar molecules together, since they disturb its hydrogen-bonded network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A folded protein shows the result directly, its nonpolar side chains packed away in the interior while the polar ones face outward where they can meet water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That exclusion does mechanical work as well as structural, since elastin, the protein that lets an artery or a lung spring back, recoils because of the hydrophobic effect, with the ordering of water at its surface changing as the protein is stretched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The scale of that job is the point: arterial elastin goes through more than two billion stretch-and-release cycles in a human lifetime without ever being replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because so much rests on water, losing it is normally fatal, yet some small animals survive almost complete dehydration without damage, a state called anhydrobiosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among tardigrades that capacity differs between species and appears to follow the habitat rather than the diet, and the longer an animal has spent dried out, the longer it needs to come back to activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Water is therefore not the setting biology happens in but a participant in it, which is why so much of the chemistry ahead is really chemistry in water.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Nour M. Jamhawi, Ronald L. Koder, and Richard J. Wittebort, “Elastin Recoil Is Driven by the Hydrophobic Effect,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 12 (2024): e2304009121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2304009121. [Open access]","Milena Roszkowska, Bartłomiej Gołdyn, Daria Wojciechowska, Zofia Księżkiewicz, et al., “How Long Can Tardigrades Survive in the Anhydrobiotic State? A Search for Tardigrade Anhydrobiosis Patterns,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0270386, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0270386. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a protein bury its nonpolar side chains?","a":"Because water pushes nonpolar molecules together, since they disturb its hydrogen-bonded network, so the polar side chains face outward where they can meet water and the nonpolar ones pack inside."},{"q":"Why is the hydrophobic effect described as doing mechanical work?","a":"Because elastin recoils because of it, the ordering of water at its surface changing as the protein is stretched — arterial elastin doing that more than two billion times in a lifetime without replacement."},{"q":"Why is water called a participant rather than a setting?","a":"Because life began in the ocean and those conditions left a permanent mark on the chemistry, so much of biological chemistry is the dealings between water and everything else in the cell."}]},
{"id":"bio-028","num":28,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Oxygen pulls hard on the shared electrons and hydrogen pulls weakly, which makes water a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with negative charge at one end and positive at the other.","questions":["A membrane lipid has a water-loving head and water-fearing tails, so half of it behaves as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and half does not, which is why a bilayer assembles itself.","Proteins find their partners by matching patterns of partial charge across a surface, and that pattern exists only because each group in it either is or is not a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself."],"answer":"polar molecule","answerText":"polar molecule","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>polar molecule</b> is one whose charge is spread unevenly, gathered positive towards one end and negative towards the other, and water is the standard case, since its oxygen attracts the shared electrons strongly while its hydrogens attract them weakly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule is worth carrying about: an O-H or an N-H bond is polar, while a C-H bond is not, carbon and hydrogen attracting the shared pair about equally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polar covalent bonds create permanent dipoles, so a large molecule carrying many polar groups has a pattern of partial positive and negative charges spread over its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two molecules whose patterns are complementary attract one another through those dipoles, by interactions that resemble ionic bonds and are weaker than them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The property sorts molecules into two kinds, since polar ones interact readily with water and dissolve in it while nonpolar ones do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Membrane lipids are deliberately both at once, each one amphipathic, with a polar head group and two hydrophobic hydrocarbon tails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Put in water they arrange themselves, burying the tails and turning the heads outward, which is why a bilayer forms spontaneously and why about 5 million lipid molecules sit in a single square micrometre of one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Patterns of surface charge are also how proteins find each other, and electrostatic complementarity across an interface has been shown to drive the brief encounters that much of cell signalling depends on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What such complementarity is worth can be measured rather than assumed, and in one enzyme's active site it accounts for at most a modest 300-fold of the catalysis, even though the hydrogen bonds shorten as charge builds up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polarity is a small asymmetry inside one molecule, and most of what water does to biology follows from it.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Lipid Bilayer,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/. [Open access]","Greta Grassmann, Lorenzo Di Rienzo, Giorgio Gosti, Marco Leonetti, Giancarlo Ruocco, Mattia Miotto, and Edoardo Milanetti, “Electrostatic Complementarity at the Interface Drives Transient Protein-Protein Interactions,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 10207, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-37130-z. [Open access]","Daniel A. Kraut, Paul A. Sigala, Brandon Pybus, Corey W. Liu, Dagmar Ringe, Gregory A. Petsko, and Daniel Herschlag, “Testing Electrostatic Complementarity in Enzyme Catalysis: Hydrogen Bonding in the Ketosteroid Isomerase Oxyanion Hole,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 4, no. 4 (2006): e99, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0040099. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is an O-H bond polar where a C-H bond is not?","a":"Because oxygen attracts the shared electrons strongly while carbon and hydrogen attract the shared pair about equally, so no appreciable charge separation arises in the second case."},{"q":"Why does a lipid bilayer form on its own in water?","a":"Because each membrane lipid is amphipathic, with a polar head and two hydrophobic tails, so in water they arrange themselves burying the tails and turning the heads outward."},{"q":"Why is electrostatic complementarity worth measuring rather than assuming?","a":"Because its contribution can be modest: in one enzyme’s active site it accounts for at most a 300-fold of the catalysis, even though the hydrogen bonds shorten as charge builds up."}]},
{"id":"bio-029","num":29,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water sticking to itself is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and water sticking to a surface is adhesion, the pair of them holding a column of sap together in a tree.","questions":["A water strider stands on a film it cannot break, which exists because the hydrogen bonds between water molecules give the liquid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that produces surface tension.","Sap in a tree is pulled rather than pushed, so the column depends on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and fails when drought raises the tension enough to admit a gas bubble."],"answer":"cohesion","answerText":"cohesion","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Cohesion</b> is the tendency of a liquid to hold to itself, and in water it comes from the hydrogen bonds between neighbouring molecules, which is why water is a liquid at room temperature with a high boiling point and a high surface tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Adhesion is the same attraction pointed outwards, water forming hydrogen bonds with other polar molecules and interacting with ions, so it clings to surfaces built from either rather than beading away from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pair produce capillarity, the rise of a liquid into a narrow space, though the word covers more than that, standing for all the physical consequences of the thin skin that separates water from air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those consequences dominate at small scales, governing the transport of water inside plants, the dispersal of fungal spores, the water-repellency of arthropods and the fate of bacteria and viruses in respiratory drops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A water strider lives on that film, its legs and its turning gait built around a surface it can push against without breaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Cohesion is also what allows a tree to lift water, since sap in the xylem is pulled rather than pushed and travels under tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A column held that way can fail, and under drought the rising tension lets gas emboli form and spread through the conduits, blocking them and in the worst case killing the tissue they feed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much tension a species tolerates is measured as the water potential at which half its conduits have embolised, and across 22 eucalypts that resistance trades off against how fast the plant grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The faster growers had wider vessels, better at conducting and worse at holding on, while the slower ones had narrower vessels with thicker walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two ordinary stickinesses therefore set both what small animals can walk on and how tall a tree can afford to be.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","David L. Hu, Ho-Young Kim, and Dominic Vella, “Editorial: Capillarity and Elastocapillarity in Biology,” <i>Interface Focus</i> 15, no. 2 (2025): 20250020, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsfs.2025.0020. [Open access]","Javad Meshkani, Hamed Rajabi, Alexander Kovalev, and Stanislav N. Gorb, “Kinematics and Directionality of Body Turning in Water Striders,” <i>Insect Science</i> 33, no. 1 (2026): 298–312, https://doi.org/10.1111/1744-7917.13486. [Open access]","Tiantian Pan, Vinod Jacob, Travis G. Britton, Julian Schrader, et al., “Evidence for a Trade-off between Growth Rate and Xylem Embolism Resistance in 22 <i>Eucalyptus</i> Species,” <i>New Phytologist</i> 251, no. 5 (2026): 2407–26, https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.71345. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a tree lift water hundreds of feet without a pump?","a":"Because cohesion lets sap in the xylem be pulled rather than pushed, so the column travels under tension held together by hydrogen bonds between the water molecules."},{"q":"Why can that column fail in a drought?","a":"Because rising tension lets gas emboli form and spread through the conduits, blocking them and in the worst case killing the tissue they feed."},{"q":"Why does resistance to embolism trade off against growth?","a":"Because across 22 eucalypts the faster growers had wider vessels, better at conducting and worse at holding on, while slower ones had narrower vessels with thicker walls."}]},
{"id":"bio-030","num":30,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Because much of the energy water absorbs goes into rearranging its hydrogen bonds, its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is high and a large heat input changes its temperature very little.","questions":["Flight raised the internal temperature of a dove by only six tenths of a degree, which is what a body mostly made of a liquid with a high <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> does with waste heat.","The ocean has absorbed most of the planet's extra heat, and the reason it can is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the seawater rather than anything about the water's depth."],"answer":"specific heat capacity","answerText":"specific heat capacity","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"The <b>specific heat capacity</b> of a substance is the energy needed to raise the temperature of a given mass of it, and water's is high, so large amounts of absorbed energy produce only small changes in how hot it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason is the hydrogen-bond network, since much of the energy goes into rearranging that before any of it appears as the motion of the molecules themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The magnitude is easy to hold onto, the kilocalorie having been defined as the energy needed to raise one litre of water by one degree centigrade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The effect operates at every scale at once, buffering a cell against the heat it makes, steadying the temperature of a pond or a lake, and moderating the seasons of a planet mostly covered in ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A working animal shows the small version of it, since in ringed turtle-doves a bout of flight raised internal body temperature by only 0.6°C (1.1°F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Buffering is not the same as escaping the problem, because the heat still has to leave in the end, and a larger animal needs longer to shed what its muscles produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The consequence is visible in how animals move, the travel speeds of the largest species falling short of what their energy budgets alone would allow, because the capacity to dissipate heat gives out first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the far end of the scale the ocean is where most of the planet's additional heat has gone, and its heat content is now tracked from year to year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 2022 the upper 2,000 metres (6,600 feet) held 10.9 ± 8.3 zettajoules more heat than in 2021, a record, with four basins at their highest since the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One property therefore keeps a cell from cooking itself and makes the ocean the planet's memory for heat.","sources":["Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Kristen E. Crandell, Donald R. Powers, and Bret W. Tobalske, “The Role of Plumage and Heat Dissipation Areas in Thermoregulation in Doves,” <i>Journal of Experimental Biology</i> 228, no. 4 (2025): jeb248200, https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.248200. [Open access]","Alexander Dyer, Ulrich Brose, Emilio Berti, Benjamin Rosenbaum, and Myriam R. Hirt, “The Travel Speeds of Large Animals Are Limited by Their Heat-Dissipation Capacity,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 21, no. 4 (2023): e3001820, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3001820. [Open access]","Lijing Cheng, John Abraham, Kevin E. Trenberth, John Fasullo, Tim Boyer, Michael E. Mann, et al., “Another Year of Record Heat for the Oceans,” <i>Advances in Atmospheric Sciences</i> 40, no. 6 (2023): 963–74, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00376-023-2385-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does water absorb so much heat for so little change in temperature?","a":"Because much of the energy goes into rearranging its hydrogen-bond network before any of it appears as the motion of the molecules themselves."},{"q":"Why is buffering not the same as escaping the problem?","a":"Because the heat still has to leave in the end, and a larger animal needs longer to shed what its muscles produce."},{"q":"Why do the largest animals travel slower than their energy budgets allow?","a":"Because the capacity to dissipate heat gives out before the energy does, so shedding heat rather than supplying fuel is what sets their top travel speeds."}]},
{"id":"bio-031","num":31,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water is biology's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taking up ions and polar molecules readily while pushing nonpolar ones together and leaving them out.","questions":["A row of negatively charged phosphates could not sit along a DNA backbone at all if the surrounding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not weaken the repulsion between them.","Zooplankton in an oxygen minimum zone respond to changes of one per cent or less, because oxygen is a gas held in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that carries very little of it."],"answer":"solvent","answerText":"solvent","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>solvent</b> is a liquid that other substances dissolve in, and water is biology's, taking up ions and polar molecules readily while leaving nonpolar ones out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A salt goes into solution because the ionic bonds holding its lattice together are greatly weakened in water, each ion departing surrounded by its own group of water molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure behind that is water's dielectric constant, about 80 at room temperature, which greatly attenuates the electrical attraction between charges and lets electrolytes dissociate and stay apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same screening lowers the cost of holding like charges close together, which is what allows the row of negatively charged phosphates along a nucleic acid backbone to exist at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What water will not dissolve matters as much, since nonpolar molecules disturb its hydrogen-bonded network and are pushed together rather than taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Water is a participant as well as a medium, since it solvates nucleophiles, electrophiles and charged intermediates, lowering activation barriers and making reactions run that otherwise would not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The solution inside a cell is also nothing like dilute, an estimate built from cell composition giving 2 to 4 million protein molecules in a cubic micrometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Oxygen is the hard case, since large regions of the middle ocean hold very little of it, and those oxygen minimum zones are expected to spread as the climate warms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Animals there live near their physiological limits, and in the eastern tropical North Pacific many zooplankton groups shift their distribution in response to changes in oxygen of 1% or less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Being a good solvent is therefore a specification rather than a virtue, and life is built around the particular one water happens to be.","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Ron Milo, “What Is the Total Number of Protein Molecules per Cell Volume? A Call to Rethink Some Published Values,” <i>BioEssays</i> 35, no. 12 (2013): 1050–55, https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.201300066. [Open access]","K. F. Wishner, B. A. Seibel, C. Roman, C. Deutsch, et al., “Ocean Deoxygenation and Zooplankton: Very Small Oxygen Differences Matter,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaau5180, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aau5180. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does water pull an ionic solid apart?","a":"Because its dielectric constant of about 80 greatly attenuates the electrical attraction between charges, letting electrolytes dissociate and each ion leave surrounded by water molecules."},{"q":"Why can a nucleic acid backbone carry a row of like charges at all?","a":"Because water’s screening lowers the cost of holding like charges close together, which is what allows the line of negatively charged phosphates to exist."},{"q":"Why is being a good solvent called a specification rather than a virtue?","a":"Because what water will not dissolve matters as much as what it will, and life is built around the particular solvent water happens to be, nonpolar exclusion included."}]},
{"id":"bio-032","num":32,"category":"The chemistry of life","question":"Water is one of the few substances that expands as it freezes, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> floats on the liquid it came from instead of sinking through it.","questions":["A lake in winter stays liquid underneath because the layer of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on top is lighter than the water below and insulates it from the air.","Fishes in polar seas carry proteins that bind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and stop the crystals growing, which lowers the temperature at which their tissue freezes."],"answer":"ice","answerText":"ice","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Ice</b> is the solid form of water, and water is one of the few substances that expands as it freezes, the molar volume of the liquid being about 8% less than that of the solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The liquid is densest at about 4°C (39°F) and swells again as it cools from there towards freezing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason is geometric, since each molecule can hydrogen-bond to its neighbours in a network,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and as the temperature falls from 4 to 0°C the ideal tetrahedral arrangements become more probable and longer-lived, holding molecules further apart than the less regular packing above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The solid therefore forms on top rather than at the bottom, and that layer, with water's high heat capacity, insulates what lies beneath and keeps it liquid while the air above is below freezing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life carries on underneath on that account, and the same substance is odd under pressure too, its melting point falling rather than rising, which is what lets a glacier slide over its bed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Freezing is still dangerous to a cell, and animals that meet it have evolved proteins that bind ice crystals, inhibit their growth and lower the temperature at which tissue freezes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four kinds of antifreeze protein have evolved separately in fishes, which is what has allowed teleosts to colonise polar seas again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The trick is not theirs alone, since winter-active spiders carry an ice-binding protein that halts crystal growth at −4°C (25°F) and is folded unlike any of the fish types, so it arose on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A plant cannot move away from it, so what matters there is where the ice forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Outside the cells it can even protect them, drawing water out and concentrating what remains until the inside freezes at a lower temperature, though prolonged drying of that kind ruptures the membrane instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","S. N. Bogan, N. Surendran, S. Hotaling, T. Desvignes, et al., “Temperature and Pressure Shaped the Evolution of Antifreeze Proteins in Polar and Deep Sea Zoarcoid Fishes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 42, no. 10 (2025): msaf219, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msaf219. [Open access]","Laurie A. Graham, Stano Pekár, Ina M. Hainer, and Peter L. Davies, “Winter-Active Spiders (<i>Clubiona</i>) Have a Hyperactive Antifreeze Protein,” <i>The FEBS Journal</i> 293, no. 8 (2026): 2270–90, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.70323. [Open access]","Lia Lamacque, Nicolas Dusart, Pierre Amato, Katline Charra-Vaskou, et al., “Ice Nucleation and Freezing Consequences in Perennial Plants,” <i>Physiologia Plantarum</i> 178, no. 3 (2026): e70961, https://doi.org/10.1111/ppl.70961. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does ice float, and why does that matter to life?","a":"Because water expands as it freezes, the liquid being about 8 per cent denser, so the solid forms on top and insulates what lies beneath, keeping it liquid while the air is below freezing."},{"q":"Why does the liquid swell as it cools below 4 °C?","a":"Because the ideal tetrahedral hydrogen-bond arrangements become more probable and longer-lived as the temperature falls, holding molecules further apart than the less regular packing above."},{"q":"Why is antifreeze protein evidence of repeated invention?","a":"Because four kinds evolved separately in fishes, and winter-active spiders carry an ice-binding protein folded unlike any of them, so it arose on its own."}]},
{"id":"bio-033","num":33,"category":"Biology","question":"A substance that gives up protons when it dissolves in water, each one joining a water molecule to make a hydronium ion, is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a base is what accepts them instead.","questions":["The vertebrate stomach shows how much <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a body will make on purpose, obligate scavengers holding theirs near pH 1.3 while foregut herbivores sit closer to 6.1.","Aspartate and glutamate carry carboxyl groups that hand a proton away inside the cell, which makes each of them an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and leaves its side chain negatively charged."],"answer":"acid","answerText":"acid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"An <b>acid</b> is a substance that releases protons when it dissolves in water, each bare hydrogen nucleus attaching itself to a water molecule to make a hydronium ion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A base does the reverse, raising the concentration of hydroxyl ions, which are formed by removing a proton from a water molecule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water is faintly both, since a proton can leave one water molecule for another, so pure water carries equal traces of hydronium and hydroxyl and sits at the middle of the logarithmic pH scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inside of a cell is held close to that neutral point, which is one of the conditions its chemistry is built around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Acids and bases are ordinary furniture there: the carboxyl groups of aspartate and glutamate give a proton away and carry a negative charge, while the side chains of lysine and arginine take one on and carry a positive one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Away from that guarded interior, animals make acid deliberately, and the vertebrate stomach is the clearest case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across mammals and birds the acidity of the stomach tracks diet rather than size: obligate scavengers average about pH 1.3, generalist carnivores 2.2, omnivores 2.9 and foregut herbivores 6.1, with the human stomach near 1.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The suggestion is that the stomach works as an ecological filter, killing most of what arrives before it can settle further down the gut, so the animals eating the dirtiest food keep the fiercest acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A few organisms live in acid rather than making it, among them <i>Oxyplasma meridianum</i>, an archaeon taken from a rock on the Italian island of Vulcano, which grows between pH 0.5 and 4.0 and does best at 1.0; relatives in its order endure below zero on the scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Small shifts tell as well as large ones: <i>Pocillopora</i> corals in the southern Mexican Pacific lost 28.6 per cent of their skeletal density over 23 years, a decline reported as running alongside falling seawater pH and carbonate saturation rather than proved to be caused by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","DeAnna E. Beasley, Amanda M. Koltz, Joanna E. Lambert, Noah Fierer, and Rob R. Dunn, “The Evolution of Stomach Acidity and Its Relevance to the Human Microbiome,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0134116, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0134116. [Open access]","Olga V. Golyshina, Evgenii A. Lunev, Marco A. Distaso, Rafael Bargiela, Matthew C. Gaines, Bertram Daum, Manuel Ferrer, et al., “Oxyplasma meridianum gen. nov., sp. nov., an Extremely Acidophilic Organotrophic Member of the Order Thermoplasmatales,” <i>International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology</i> 74, no. 8 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1099/ijsem.0.006499. [Open access]","Andrés López-Pérez, Omar Jiménez-Gutiérrez, Héctor Reyes-Bonilla, Rafael A. Cabral-Tena, Gerardo Leyte-Morales, Francisco Medellín-Maldonado, et al., “Two Decades of Skeletal Density Decline in Pocillopora spp. Corals in the Mexican Pacific Ocean: Insight into a Tropical Eastern Pacific Acidification Scenario?” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0342741, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0342741. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is pure water counted as faintly acid and faintly basic at once?","a":"Because a proton can leave one water molecule for another, so pure water carries equal traces of hydronium and hydroxyl and sits at the middle of the pH scale."},{"q":"Why is stomach acidity read as an ecological filter?","a":"Because across mammals and birds it tracks diet rather than size — obligate scavengers near pH 1.3 against foregut herbivores at 6.1 — as though the dirtiest food demanded the fiercest acid."},{"q":"Why is the coral figure reported as an association rather than a cause?","a":"Because the 28.6 per cent loss of skeletal density over 23 years is stated as running alongside falling seawater pH and carbonate saturation, which is not the same as having been shown to follow from it."}]},
{"id":"bio-034","num":34,"category":"Biology","question":"On the logarithmic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> scale a solution at 5 carries a hundred times more protons than one at 7, so a shift of a single unit is never a small change.","questions":["A lysosome digests what a cell takes in at about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 5, two units below the cytosol around it, and its enzymes fall idle if they ever leak into that neutral surrounding.","<i>Sulfolobus acidocaldarius</i> grows best at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 2 and 3 while a soda-lake <i>Bacillus</i> prefers 8.5 to 11.5, each carrying enzymes whose optima match its own water."],"answer":"pH","answerText":"pH","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>pH</b> measures how many protons a solution carries, on a logarithmic scale that fits an enormous range of concentrations into a handful of numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pure water sits at 7, where hydronium and hydroxyl ions are equally common; below that a solution is acidic and above it basic, and the inside of a cell is held close to that neutral point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The logarithm is the substance of the scale rather than a convenience, since each unit stands for a tenfold change in the concentration of protons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lysosome, the compartment in which a cell digests what it has taken in, runs near pH 5 against a cytosol of about 7.2, and a pump in its membrane keeps roughly a hundredfold difference across a single membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its enzymes are built to work at that acidity and go idle at the cytosol's, so a leaking lysosome does not digest the cell around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Newer work puts the lysosome lower still, near 4.5, and finds that it is not filled from the cytosol alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In yeast and in human cells the compartment also takes up protons pumped out by mitochondria across direct membrane contacts, and ageing breaks those contacts and lets the lysosome slip towards neutral, a loss an engineered linker between the two organelles can reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whole organisms are built around their own values: <i>Sulfolobus acidocaldarius</i>, from a Yellowstone spring measured at 2.4, grows best between 2 and 3, while a <i>Bacillus</i> from a soda lake in Inner Mongolia grows between 8.5 and 11.5, each carrying enzymes whose optima match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Human tissue can run the gradient backwards: in many tumours the fluid outside the cells turns acidic while the cytosol stays in a narrow alkaline band near 7.2 to 7.4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That reversal is now read as a feature of malignant progression rather than a by-product of it, one that contributes to invasion, immune evasion and resistance to treatment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Lysosomes,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9953/. [Open access]","Qingqing Liu, Seungmin Yoo, Zhixin A. Zhang, Liying Li, Hetian Su, Lingraj Vannur, Alexandra C. Wooldredge, et al., “Mitochondria-Lysosome Coupling Contributes to Lysosome Acidification and Aging,” <i>Molecular Cell</i> 86, no. 12 (2026): 2425–42, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molcel.2026.05.004. [Open access]","Peng Zhou, Zu-Xin Zhang, Xiaoyong Pan, Xinfeng Dai, and Qiaoli Chen, “Leveraging AI-Driven Predictors of Enzyme pH Optima to Unravel Microbial Adaptation to Environmental pH,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 17 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2026.1788098. [Open access]","Fangquan Chen, Xiutao Cai, Qile Zhou, Zhen Huang, Jie Wang, Rui Kang, Daolin Tang, and Jiao Liu, “Compartmental pH Regulation in Cancer and Antitumor Immunity: Therapeutic Opportunities and Challenges,” <i>Apoptosis</i> 31, no. 8 (2026): 197, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10495-026-02408-w. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the logarithm the substance of the scale rather than a convenience?","a":"Because each unit stands for a tenfold change in the concentration of protons, so a small-looking difference in pH is an enormous difference in what the solution contains."},{"q":"Why does a leaking lysosome not digest the cell around it?","a":"Because its enzymes are built to work near pH 5 and go idle at the cytosol’s 7.2, so the hundredfold difference a pump maintains across one membrane is also a safety catch."},{"q":"Why is the acidity outside a tumour treated as a feature of it?","a":"Because the fluid outside the cells turns acidic while the cytosol stays in a narrow alkaline band, and that reversal is now read as contributing to invasion, immune evasion and resistance to treatment."}]},
{"id":"bio-035","num":35,"category":"Biology","question":"Because a mixture of a weak acid and the base it becomes soaks up added protons and replaces lost ones, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds the pH of a solution nearly steady while its capacity lasts.","questions":["Human blood is kept between 7.35 and 7.45 by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built from carbon dioxide, carbonic acid and bicarbonate, with phosphate, proteins and haemoglobin doing the same work beside it.","The Revelle factor measures how strong a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the ocean's carbonate system makes, a high value meaning a weak one, so that the same added carbon dioxide moves the pH further."],"answer":"buffer","answerText":"buffer","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>buffer</b> is a mixture that resists a change in pH, holding a weak acid together with the base it turns into, so that added protons are taken up and lost ones replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Blood is the clearest case: carbon dioxide from respiration joins water to make carbonic acid, which dissociates into bicarbonate and a proton, and the reaction stands in equilibrium, shifting whichever way its surroundings demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Human blood is held between 7.35 and 7.45 and averages 7.40, a value below that band being an acidemia and one above it an alkalemia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bicarbonate is not the only system at work, since phosphate, the body's proteins and haemoglobin all buffer as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Something of the kind underlies a cell's near-neutral interior, because metabolism makes acid continuously and neutrality is not a resting state that comes for free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside muscle much of the work is done by particular molecules rather than dissolved salts, carnosine being the most abundant histidine dipeptide there and known chiefly for its pH buffering alongside antioxidant and metal-binding roles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sea is buffered by its carbonate system, and how strongly is measured by the Revelle factor, a high value meaning weak buffering, so that a given amount of added carbon dioxide moves the pH further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Weak buffering does not settle the outcome on its own: in the least ventilated and most poorly buffered oxygen-minimum waters acidification turns out to be negligible, little industrial carbon dioxide having reached them, while it is plain in the moderately ventilated water above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A buffer can also be thought of as a reserve that is spent, and one recent model treats the ageing body's declining capacity to buffer acid, excrete it through the kidney and breathe it off as a hidden erosion behind frailty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether that model holds is not settled, but its shape is the general point: a buffer does not abolish a problem, it holds a value steady until the reserve behind it is spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Erin Hopkins, Terrence Sanvictores, and Sandeep Sharma, “Physiology, Acid Base Balance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2022), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507807/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Belisa Parmeggiani, Bruna Klippel Ferreira, Patricia Fernanda Schuck, and Gustavo Costa Ferreira, “Considerations for Carnosine Actions in Biology,” <i>Neurochemical Research</i> 51, no. 2 (2026): 126, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11064-026-04735-5. [Open access]","Liang Xue, Christopher Sabine, Jianfang Chen, Siv K. Lauvset, Qinsheng Wei, Kuiping Li, and Wei-Jun Cai, “Ventilation and Buffering Capacity Effects on Ocean Acidification in Low Oxygen Environments,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2025): 228, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-67807-0. [Open access]","Wan-Hui Liao, Wolfgang Langhans, and Maciej Henneberg, “Acid–Base Dysregulation Links Aging Metabolism to Frailty,” <i>Aging Cell</i> 25, no. 4 (2026): e70466, https://doi.org/10.1111/acel.70466. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a buffer hold a pH steady at all?","a":"Because it holds a weak acid together with the base it turns into, so added protons are taken up and lost ones replaced, and the equilibrium shifts whichever way its surroundings demand."},{"q":"Why is a cell’s neutrality not a resting state that comes for free?","a":"Because metabolism makes acid continuously, so the near-neutral interior has to be held there by buffering rather than simply settling at it."},{"q":"Why does weak buffering not settle whether a water will acidify?","a":"Because in the least ventilated, most poorly buffered oxygen-minimum waters acidification is negligible, little industrial carbon dioxide having reached them, while it is plain in the water above."}]},
{"id":"bio-036","num":36,"category":"Biology","question":"The chemistry of carbon compounds is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its whole subject rests on an atom that makes four covalent bonds and links to its own kind in chains and rings.","questions":["Because carbon links to itself with no obvious limit, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counts its possible molecules in the billions — 166.4 billion for structures of no more than seventeen heavy atoms.","All five canonical nucleobases turned up in samples of the asteroid Bennu, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer taken to mean the chemistry of things that are alive."],"answer":"organic chemistry","answerText":"organic chemistry","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Organic chemistry</b> is the chemistry of carbon compounds, and its whole subject rests on one atom's habits: carbon forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms through stable carbon-carbon bonds to make chains and rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no obvious upper limit to the size of what follows, so the same element frames a sugar of six carbons and a protein of many thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Perhaps a thousand kinds of small carbon compound are present in a typical cell, and these together with the large ones are what a biologist means by organic molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside a cell they fall into four families — carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids — each assembled from a small set of repeating parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That the frame is carbon rather than something else has been tested rather than assumed: a systematic assessment of silicon, the obvious alternative, finds no planetary environment in which a life built primarily on silicon chemistry is plausible, since in water its capacity is sharply limited by the ubiquitous formation of silica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The reach of the chemistry is hard to overstate: a database enumerating every possible molecule of no more than seventeen atoms of carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, sulfur and the halogens holds 166.4 billion of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That counts what the rules of bonding allow rather than what exists, and the largest catalogue of compounds thought readily synthesisable holds 13.6 billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word organic no longer carries its old sense of something made by life, and returned asteroid samples are where that is plainest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> All five canonical nucleobases have been found in material brought back from the asteroid Bennu, evidence that some of life's ingredients were put together without life in that asteroid's parent body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The detail matters as much as the headline: pyrimidines outnumber purines in the Bennu sample, as they do in the Orgueil meteorite but unlike Murchison, so this chemistry differs from body to body rather than arriving everywhere in one mixture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Janusz Jurand Petkowski, William Bains, and Sara Seager, “On the Potential of Silicon as a Building Block for Life,” <i>Life</i> 10, no. 6 (2020): 84, https://doi.org/10.3390/life10060084. [Open access]","Aylin Del Moral-Morales, Francisco L. Feitosa, Carolina Horta Andrade, and José L. Medina-Franco, “The (r)evolution of Chemical Space and Molecular Modeling: A Time-Resolved Perspective,” <i>Journal of Computer-Aided Molecular Design</i> 40, no. 1 (2026): 136, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10822-026-00850-1. [Open access]","Yasuhiro Oba, Toshiki Koga, Yoshinori Takano, Hiroshi Naraoka, Yuta Hirakawa, Sako Sunami, Yoshihiro Furukawa, et al., “Distribution of Extraterrestrial Nucleobases, Other N-Heterocycles, and Their Precursors in a Sample from Asteroid Bennu,” <i>Communications Chemistry</i> 9, no. 1 (2026): 132, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-026-01966-z. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one atom’s habits carry a whole branch of chemistry?","a":"Because carbon forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbons through stable carbon-carbon bonds, so the same element frames a six-carbon sugar and a protein of many thousands."},{"q":"Why is silicon not a plausible alternative frame for life?","a":"Because a systematic assessment finds no planetary environment where it would work: in water silicon’s capacity is sharply limited by the ubiquitous formation of silica."},{"q":"Why does the word organic no longer mean made by life?","a":"Because all five canonical nucleobases have been found in material returned from the asteroid Bennu, so some of life’s ingredients were assembled without life in that asteroid’s parent body."}]},
{"id":"bio-037","num":37,"category":"Biology","question":"Small recurring clusters of atoms such as hydroxyl, carboxyl and amino are each called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it is these rather than the carbon skeleton that largely decide how a molecule behaves.","questions":["Collagen's proline is hydroxylated in a step that depends on vitamin C, so scurvy is what follows when a single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fails to be attached where it belongs.","One survey counted 64,397 phosphorylation sites and 14,245 acetylation sites across proteins, each of them a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added after the protein was built and altering what it does."],"answer":"functional group","answerText":"functional group","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>functional group</b> is one of the small clusters of atoms that occur over and over again attached to a carbon skeleton — the methyl, hydroxyl, carboxyl, carbonyl, phosphate and amino groups among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each has chemical and physical properties of its own, and it is these rather than the skeleton beneath that largely settle how the molecule containing them behaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same frame carrying a different group is therefore a different substance, which is how a short list of groups generates an enormous variety of molecules from a limited stock of shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carboxyl group gives a proton away inside a cell and leaves what carries it negatively charged, while an amino group takes one on and leaves it positive, which is what makes aspartate and glutamate acidic residues and lysine and arginine basic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The groups are not fixed at the moment a molecule is built, and a cell attaches and removes them afterwards, changing what a protein does without altering the sequence it was made from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That editing has been counted: one recent survey identified 64,397 phosphorylation sites across proteins — 43,721 on serine, 8,414 on threonine and 12,262 on tyrosine — along with 14,245 acetylation and 1,461 mono-methylation sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most such sites sit where an enzyme can reach them, but a 2025 study describes cryptic phosphosites buried inside a protein's core, exposed only while the chain is folding and more conserved than the ones on the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modifying one of those can destabilise the folded structure and send the protein to be broken up, which makes the position of a group as consequential as its identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A single hydroxyl can decide whether a tissue holds together at all: the proline and lysine of collagen are hydroxylated inside the endoplasmic reticulum in a step that depends on vitamin C.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the vitamin is short that hydroxylation fails, and the result is scurvy, with weak connective tissue, bleeding gums and poor wound healing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Mukesh Kumar, Anthony P. Possemato, Barry M. Zee, Srikanth Subramanian, Jian Min Ren, Alissa J. Nelson, Bin Zhang, et al., “Expanding the Global Map of Protein Post-Translational Modifications with Immunoaffinity Enrichment and nDIA Analysis on the Orbitrap Astral Mass Spectrometer,” <i>Molecular &amp; Cellular Proteomics</i> 25, no. 5 (2026): 101562, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2026.101562. [Open access]","Dino Gasparotto, Annarita Zanon, Valerio Bonaldo, Elisa Marchiori, Massimo Casagranda, Erika Di Domenico, Laura Copat, et al., “Mapping Cryptic Phosphorylation Sites in the Human Proteome,” <i>The EMBO Journal</i> 44, no. 22 (2025): 6704–31, https://doi.org/10.1038/s44318-025-00567-1. [Open access]","Nader Rahimi and Marjorie V. Launico, “Biochemistry, Collagen Synthesis,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2026), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507709/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a short list of groups generate so many molecules?","a":"Because each group has chemical properties of its own and largely settles how the molecule behaves, so the same carbon skeleton carrying a different group is a different substance."},{"q":"Why can a cell change what a protein does without changing its sequence?","a":"Because groups are attached and removed after the chain is built — one survey identified 64,397 phosphorylation sites alongside 14,245 acetylation and 1,461 mono-methylation sites."},{"q":"Why does a single hydroxyl group decide whether tissue holds together?","a":"Because collagen’s proline and lysine are hydroxylated in a step depending on vitamin C, and where the vitamin is short that step fails, giving weak connective tissue, bleeding gums and poor healing."}]},
{"id":"bio-038","num":38,"category":"Biology","question":"Molecules that share a chemical formula but arrange their atoms differently are called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the pairs among them that are mirror images of one another are the optical kind.","questions":["Because 11-cis-retinal flips to an all-trans <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the instant it absorbs a photon, vision begins with a change of shape rather than a change of composition.","Only the l-form of each amino acid is found in proteins, so life uses one member of every mirror-image <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pair and leaves the other very largely alone."],"answer":"isomer","answerText":"isomer","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"Molecules that share a chemical formula but differ in structure are called <b>isomers</b>, and the ones that come as mirror-image pairs are called optical isomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are widespread among organic molecules and are a large part of what makes the sugars so various, since each simple sugar exists in a d-form and an l-form that are mirror images of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every amino acid but glycine does the same, and life uses one hand of each: only l-forms are ever found in proteins, though d-amino acids occur in bacterial cell walls and in some antibiotics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once that choice was established it could not be changed, because too much had come to depend on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difference need not be handedness at all: the glucose units of cellulose are joined in the β configuration where those of the storage polysaccharides are α, the same sugar differently arranged, and the reason one is a structural material and the other food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An isomerisation can be the working part of a mechanism rather than a curiosity of nomenclature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vision begins when 11-cis-retinal absorbs a photon and is reconfigured to all-trans-retinal, and the spent form has to be re-isomerised before the photoreceptor can respond again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fatty acids come in geometrical isomers, which differ in whether a double bond is cis or trans, and positional isomers, which differ in where along the chain that bond sits; industrial partial hydrogenation produces trans forms now studied as a cause of dyslipidaemia and cardiovascular disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mirror images can even carry different histories, and both forms of glutamic acid are present in the material returned from the asteroid Bennu with nitrogen isotope values differing by 87 parts per thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since the two are chemically near-identical, a difference that wide implies they were formed or altered under unlike conditions, which unsettles the assumption that a molecule's two hands share one isotopic record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ala Moshiri and Akrit Sodhi, “Illuminating Retinoid Flux in the Neurosensory Retina,” <i>Journal of Clinical Investigation</i> 136, no. 3 (2026): e201863, https://doi.org/10.1172/jci201863. [Open access]","Mona A. Hegazy, Bojana Vidovic, Shimaa Abobakr, Aleksandra Zeljkovic, Aleksandra Stefanovic, and Jelena Vekic, “Hidden Industrial Trans-Fatty Acids: Mechanistic Insights into Dyslipidemia, Cardiovascular Disease, and Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 26, no. 23 (2025): 11715, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms262311715. [Open access]","Allison A. Baczynski, Ophélie M. McIntosh, Danielle N. Simkus, Hannah L. McLain, Jason P. Dworkin, Daniel P. Glavin, Jamie E. Elsila, et al., “Multiple Formation Pathways for Amino Acids in the Early Solar System Based on Carbon and Nitrogen Isotopes in Asteroid Bennu Samples,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 8 (2026): e2517723123, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2517723123. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could life’s choice of one hand not be reversed later?","a":"Because once the choice was established too much had come to depend on it, which is why only l-forms are ever found in proteins even though d-amino acids exist elsewhere."},{"q":"Why is cellulose a structural material where starch is food?","a":"Because the glucose units of cellulose are joined in the β configuration and those of the storage polysaccharides in α — the same sugar differently arranged."},{"q":"Why does the Bennu glutamic acid finding unsettle an assumption?","a":"Because its two mirror forms differ by 87 parts per thousand in nitrogen isotopes, and since the two are chemically near-identical that implies they formed or were altered under unlike conditions."}]},
{"id":"bio-039","num":39,"category":"Biology","question":"Because water is consumed rather than expelled when a bond between two subunits is broken, digesting a polymer is a matter of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the reverse of the reaction that built it.","questions":["Lactose is split into glucose and galactose by lactase in the small intestine, and where that enzyme has declined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fails and the sugar reaches the colon undigested.","Every biological polymer is assembled by expelling water, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name for the reverse reaction, the one that consumes a molecule of water to break each bond."],"answer":"hydrolysis","answerText":"hydrolysis","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Hydrolysis</b> is the breaking of a bond by a molecule of water, and it is the reverse of the condensation reaction that made the bond in the first place, in which a molecule of water was expelled as the two parts were joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same pair of reactions builds and dismantles all three great families of biological polymer, so sugars, nucleic acids and proteins are each assembled by losing one water molecule per subunit added and taken apart by consuming one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The condensation half also goes by the name of a dehydration reaction, and it is what joins two monosaccharides through a glycosidic bond between two of their carbons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hydrolysis is not only destructive: the terminal phosphate of ATP is split off by it, and the energy released is what drives reactions that would not otherwise run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nowhere in biology is a peptide, phosphodiester or glycosidic bond made by any other means than this expulsion of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That universality sits oddly with the medium, since water is also what spontaneously takes those polymers apart, and one recent review calls the arrangement a paradox at the heart of biochemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prebiotic chemistry answers it by drying the mixture out and wetting it again, and an experimental study of wet-dry cycling under gamma irradiation reports infrared evidence of peptide bond formation, though its authors describe the results as preliminary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a living body the reaction is put to work rather than waited for, and lactose is hydrolysed into glucose and galactose by lactase at the brush border of the small intestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That enzyme peaks shortly after birth and then declines whether or not milk keeps being drunk, a pattern shared with most other mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> People of northern European and northwestern Indian ancestry more often keep it into adult life, so whether one particular bond is hydrolysed at all differs from person to person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","E. Fonseca-González, C. A. Fuentes-Carreon, and A. Heredia-Barbero, “Experimental and Computational Simulation of the Prebiotic Peptide Bond Formation Driven by Wet-Dry Cycles and Gamma Ionizing Radiation: An Insight into Molecular Evolution,” <i>Journal of Molecular Evolution</i> 94, no. 2 (2026): 369–82, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00239-026-10309-4. [Open access]","Eric Goosenberg and Muriam Afzal, “Lactose Intolerance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532285/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the same pair of reactions enough to build and dismantle life’s polymers?","a":"Because sugars, nucleic acids and proteins are each assembled by expelling one water molecule per subunit added and taken apart by consuming one, and no other means is used anywhere in biology."},{"q":"Why is that arrangement called a paradox?","a":"Because water is both the thing expelled to make a bond and the thing that spontaneously takes those polymers apart again, so the polymers are built in the medium that destroys them."},{"q":"Why does whether one bond is hydrolysed differ from person to person?","a":"Because lactase peaks shortly after birth and then declines whether or not milk keeps being drunk, and only some ancestries more often keep it into adult life."}]},
{"id":"bio-040","num":40,"category":"Biology","question":"Adding one subunit at a time to the end of a growing chain builds a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is how a cell manufactures a very large molecule while repeating one simple step.","questions":["Glycogen, starch and cellulose are made entirely of glucose, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can differ from another built of the very same subunit in nothing but the bonds joining them.","Ribosome measurements put the addition of subunits at between 0.4 and 5 per second depending on the method, which is the rate at which a protein <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is actually assembled."],"answer":"polymer","answerText":"polymer","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>polymer</b> is a long molecule built by joining many small subunits, called monomers, one after another into a chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each such chain grows by adding a monomer onto its end, so the stepwise repetition of one simple reaction is enough to manufacture a molecule of enormous size and complexity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The economy of the strategy is that the set of subunits is small: proteins, nucleic acids and polysaccharides are each drawn from a limited stock, and what varies between one chain and the next is the order rather than the ingredients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Glycogen, starch and cellulose are made entirely of glucose residues, and differ in the bonds joining them and in the branching that results, one storing sugar and another serving as a structural material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two polymers can therefore share every atom of their composition and do completely unrelated jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The assembly can be watched, and how fast it runs depends on how you look: measurements of ribosomes putting amino acids onto a growing chain give about 0.4 per second by single-molecule fluorescence, 0.7 to 1.5 in cell extracts and 3 to 5 by ribosome profiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The spread is a fact about the methods rather than about the cells, and any figure quoted for the speed of protein synthesis carries the technique that produced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the other end of the scale the strategy dominates the planet's chemistry, lignocellulosic biomass — cellulose, hemicellulose and lignin together — being the most abundant renewable source of organic matter on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What biology does that industrial chemistry still cannot is control the order exactly, and a chemist's polymer is usually a mixture of chain lengths and sequences rather than one defined molecule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Work on sequence-defined synthetic chains is explicit that the fine-tuning nature achieves through controlled structure remains beyond abiotic macromolecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Leslie Watkins, Mulin Li, and Bin Wu, “Translation Elongation: Measurements and Applications,” <i>RNA Biology</i> 22, no. 1 (2025): 1–10, https://doi.org/10.1080/15476286.2025.2504727. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Sara Njoku, Ariel F. Perez Mellor, Johanna Brazard, Simone G. Giuffrida, Wojciech Dudziak, Céline Besnard, Thomas Bürgi, Takuji B. M. Adachi, and Róża Szweda, “Stereocontrol as a Tool for Shaping Abiotic, Sequence-Defined Oligourethanes,” <i>Polymer Chemistry</i> 17, no. 24 (2026): 2619–28, https://doi.org/10.1039/d6py00299d. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is building from a small stock of subunits an economy?","a":"Because the stepwise repetition of one simple reaction manufactures molecules of enormous size, and what varies from one chain to the next is the order rather than the ingredients."},{"q":"Why can two polymers of identical composition do unrelated jobs?","a":"Because glycogen, starch and cellulose are all glucose and differ only in the bonds joining the residues and in the branching that results, one storing sugar and another serving as structure."},{"q":"Why does a quoted rate of protein synthesis carry its method with it?","a":"Because measurements give about 0.4 amino acids per second by single-molecule fluorescence, 0.7 to 1.5 in cell extracts and 3 to 5 by ribosome profiling, and that spread is a fact about the methods."}]},
{"id":"bio-041","num":41,"category":"Biology","question":"Since electrons are conserved, one molecule can only lose them if another gains them, so oxidation and reduction are two halves of a single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaction rather than separate events.","questions":["Cells burn their food in small steps rather than in a flame, taking the energy out through a long series of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reactions that pass electrons from carrier to carrier.","Cable bacteria move electrons along conductive fibres for centimetres through sediment, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chemistry stretched across a distance no single cell could otherwise span."],"answer":"redox","answerText":"redox","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>redox</b> reaction is one in which electrons move from one atom to another, and its two halves have separate names: oxidation is the removal of electrons and reduction their addition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oxidation does not require oxygen at all, so an iron atom is oxidised when it loses an electron to become Fe³⁺, and a chlorine atom reduced when it gains one to become Cl⁻.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the number of electrons is conserved, the two always happen together: whatever one molecule gains, another has lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell does not oxidise its food in one step, the way organic material burns in a fire, but takes it apart gradually through enzyme-catalysed stages, which is how the energy is captured rather than released as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The electrons are carried between those stages: NAD⁺ accepts a pair to become NADH, and in an aerobic organism they are handed down the electron transport chain until oxygen is reduced to water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Not every organism keeps that traffic inside itself, and cable bacteria run electrons along conductive fibres in their own envelopes over centimetre-scale distances through layered sediment, so that sulphide is oxidised in one place and oxygen reduced in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One freshwater species also passes electrons to solid material outside the cell altogether, growing under anoxic conditions at rates comparable to aerobic respiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oxygen that most of this chemistry now depends on is itself a product of it, having accumulated in the atmosphere about 2.45 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much accumulated is still argued over, with published estimates of the level spanning orders of magnitude and several divergent readings all defensible on the present evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor is oxidation only damage: plants generate reactive oxygen species in every compartment of the cell and use them as signalling molecules, including in a long-distance cell-to-cell wave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Kartik Aiyer, Leonid Digel, Markéta Linhartová, Andreas Schramm, Ugo Marzocchi, Lars Peter Nielsen, and Ian P. G. Marshall, “Extracellular Electron Transfer in Cable Bacteria Enables Growth Rates Comparable to Aerobic Respiration,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 6622, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-73282-y. [Open access]","Peter W. Crockford, Ichiko Sugiyama, Michael A. Kipp, Jihua Hao, Lyle L. Nelson, Jordon D. Hemingway, Sabine Wimmer, and Mojtaba Fakhraee, “Revisiting the Greatness of Earth's Great Oxidation,” <i>Communications Earth &amp; Environment</i> 7, no. 1 (2026): 348, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03518-8. [Open access]","Christine H. Foyer, “Reactive Oxygen Species and Oxidative Signalling in Plants,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 70, no. 1 (2026): 25–33, https://doi.org/10.1042/ebc20250047. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must oxidation and reduction always happen together?","a":"Because the number of electrons is conserved, so whatever one molecule gains another has lost, and the two named halves are two views of a single transfer."},{"q":"Why does a cell take its food apart in stages rather than burning it?","a":"Because gradual enzyme-catalysed steps let the energy be captured as electrons are carried between them, where burning would release it all as heat."},{"q":"Why is oxidation not simply damage?","a":"Because plants generate reactive oxygen species in every compartment of the cell and use them as signalling molecules, including in a long-distance cell-to-cell wave."}]},
{"id":"bio-042","num":42,"category":"Biology","question":"Because a reaction gives out energy only when the bonds it forms are stronger than those it breaks, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is held in the arrangement of a molecule rather than inside any one bond.","questions":["A gram of fat carries about 9 kcal against 4 for a gram of carbohydrate, a difference in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> packed into each by the way its atoms are put together.","Breaking a bond always costs energy, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a cell draws from ATP comes from the bonds formed afterwards and not from the one that was broken."],"answer":"chemical energy","answerText":"chemical energy","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Chemical energy</b> is the energy a substance holds in the arrangement of its atoms and the bonds between them, and a reaction releases or takes up energy by rearranging them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A reaction gives energy out when the bonds formed in its products are stronger overall than the bonds broken in its reactants, so it is the balance across the whole change that decides the direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Covalent bonds are stable enough to build a cell from because they are roughly a hundred times stronger than the thermal energies jostling the molecules around them, and so are not broken by ordinary motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Living things release the energy in food by oxidising it gradually in enzyme-catalysed steps rather than burning it in one, and couple that release to the reactions that need paying for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The usual shorthand for all this is wrong in a specific and well-documented way: breaking a bond requires energy rather than releasing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The error has been watched happening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In interviews, most introductory biology students explained the energy from ATP hydrolysis by saying that a bond had been broken, and about a third corrected themselves unprompted once they were shown a reaction-coordinate diagram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The authors read that as chemistry the students already knew but did not bring into a biology setting, rather than as chemistry they had never learnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The vocabulary makes it easier to go wrong, since calling a molecule with weak bonds energy-rich invites the misreading, and some authors would rather speak of a high group transfer potential; that replacement suits nucleoside triphosphates and thioesters, which transfer a phosphoryl or acyl group, but not dioxygen, so the two notions are kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measured out per gram, the chemical energy in food differs by fuel, a gram of carbohydrate or of protein carrying about 4 kcal and a gram of fat about 9.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Lucien Bettendorff and Pierre Wins, “Energy-Rich Molecules and Group Transfer Potentials in Energetic Coupling Reactions,” <i>Molecules</i> 31, no. 2 (2026): 242, https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules31020242. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Heidi Hall and Mingyu Yang, “Making Sense of ATP Hydrolysis: How Students Reconcile Conflicting Ideas from Chemistry and Biology,” <i>Journal of Microbiology &amp; Biology Education</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): e00279-25, https://doi.org/10.1128/jmbe.00279-25. [Open access]","Alyssa L. Morris and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Nutrients,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554545/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a reaction give energy out at all?","a":"Because the bonds formed in its products are stronger overall than the bonds broken in its reactants, so it is the balance across the whole change that decides the direction."},{"q":"Why is the usual account of ATP energy wrong?","a":"Because breaking a bond requires energy rather than releasing it, so saying the energy comes from breaking ATP’s bond gets the arithmetic backwards."},{"q":"Why is that error read as chemistry not brought across rather than not learnt?","a":"Because in interviews about a third of introductory biology students corrected themselves unprompted once they were shown a reaction-coordinate diagram."}]},
{"id":"bio-043","num":43,"category":"Biology","question":"Sodium, potassium, chloride, magnesium, calcium, phosphate and bicarbonate are each an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the charged inorganic species a body has to hold within narrow limits.","questions":["About 99 per cent of a body's calcium sits in the skeleton, a store as well as a structure, since the same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is needed for clotting and for nerve and muscle.","Because it carries a charge, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as potassium can be concentrated on one side of a membrane, and the difference is what a nerve cell spends to keep itself ready to fire."],"answer":"electrolyte","answerText":"electrolyte","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"An <b>electrolyte</b> is an inorganic substance that carries an electric charge in solution, and the ones that matter in a body are sodium, potassium, chloride, magnesium, calcium, phosphate and bicarbonate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They come in with food and drink, and they maintain electrical neutrality in cells and generate and conduct the impulses of nerve and muscle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell exploits them by keeping concentrations different inside and out: ion channels are narrow selective pores that let particular ions — chiefly sodium, potassium, calcium or chloride — move rapidly down the electrochemical gradients across the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Channels open mainly to potassium are found in almost every animal cell, and by leaving the membrane far more permeable to that ion than to others they hold the membrane potential.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the transport through a channel is always downhill, none of this works unless the difference is set up and paid for beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The quantities are held between narrow bounds, and serum sodium runs from 135 to 145 mmol per litre, with low sodium the most frequent of all the electrolyte disorders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the supply is structural as well as functional, since calcium is the fifth most abundant element in a human body, about a kilogram of it in a person of seventy, of which roughly 99 per cent is in the skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same ions turn up regulating things far from nerve and bone: a 2026 study reports that high potassium inside a resting T lymphocyte keeps a signalling protein from being recruited to its receptor, and that raising potassium outside suppresses the response.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The equivalent module in the related kinase of B cells is not sensitive to potassium at all, so this is a specific arrangement rather than a general effect of charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much a diet carries matters at the scale of populations, and replacing part of the sodium in table salt with potassium is under study for its effect on blood pressure and on recurrent stroke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Isha Shrimanker and Sandeep Bhattarai, “Electrolytes,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541123/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/. [Open access]","Taylor M. Drake and Muhammad Zubair, “Calcium,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2024), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557683/. [Open access]","Swarnendu Roy, Soumee SenGupta, Kaustav Gangopadhyay, Sudipta Majumder, Jibitesh Das, Anushka Sinha, Prosad Kumar Das, Bidisha Sinha, and Rahul Das, “Potassium Suppresses Allosteric Activation of ZAP-70-Dependent T Cell Receptor Signaling,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 302, no. 6 (2026): 113083, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2026.113083. [Open access]","Priya Patel, Biff F. Palmer, and Richard A. Lange, “Sodium Reduction and Salt Substitutes: Impact on Blood Pressure and Recurrent Stroke,” <i>Current Opinion in Cardiology</i> 41, no. 4 (2026): 286–90, https://doi.org/10.1097/HCO.0000000000001300. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a channel need a gradient set up beforehand?","a":"Because transport through a channel is always downhill, so nothing moves unless the concentration difference across the membrane has already been established and paid for."},{"q":"Why does a cell rest at the potential it does?","a":"Because channels open mainly to potassium are found in almost every animal cell, leaving the membrane far more permeable to that ion than to others."},{"q":"Why is the potassium effect on T cells called specific rather than general?","a":"Because the equivalent module in the related kinase of B cells is not sensitive to potassium at all, so it is an arrangement rather than a consequence of charge."}]},
{"id":"bio-044","num":44,"category":"Biology","question":"Copper is needed for connective tissue and the coverings of nerves and damages the liver in excess, which is why a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is described by a window of intake rather than a requirement.","questions":["Iron limits the growth of phytoplankton across whole upwelling regions, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can decide the productivity of an ocean as readily as the health of a body.","A human body holds at least 60 detectable elements and only about 25 that take part in its functioning, most of those being needed as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than in bulk."],"answer":"trace element","answerText":"trace element","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>trace element</b> is one an organism requires in very small quantity, where the word trace describes the amount and not the importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deciding which elements belong on the list is harder than it looks, since a human body contains at least 60 detectable chemical elements and only about 25 are thought to take part in its healthy functioning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper is the standard illustration, about 80 mg of it in a body, needed for the development of connective tissue, bone and the coverings of nerves, while chronic copper toxicity can damage the liver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A trace element therefore has a window rather than a threshold, the same substance being a requirement at one intake and a poison at another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iodine, selenium, manganese, molybdenum, chromium and boron have all been counted as ultratrace elements whose health benefits merit intakes of their own, along with cobalt in the form of vitamin B12.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Falling short is not a small matter: zinc supplementation is estimated to be capable of reducing global child mortality by about 4 per cent, and in 2004 zinc deficiency was held responsible for over 260,000 deaths in Africa and 182,000 in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iodine shows how carefully such findings have to be read, because a study of 1,211 mother and child pairs found lower first-trimester iodine associated with lower vocabulary scores at fifteen but not with matrix reasoning or full-scale IQ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within that cohort the children of severely deficient mothers scored 4.25 points lower on vocabulary and 3.3 points lower on full-scale IQ than those whose mothers had sufficient iodine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same shortages govern whole oceans, iron being the element most commonly limiting phytoplankton in upwelling regions, with manganese co-limiting alongside it in parts of the Southern Ocean and cobalt limiting in some settings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Grown at Southern Ocean concentrations, the Antarctic diatom <i>Fragilariopsis cylindrus</i> expresses its iron-stress proteins consistently under low iron, and one of its two flavodoxins responds to the shortage while the other does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Prinessa Chellan and Peter J. Sadler, “The Elements of Life and Medicines,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2037 (2015): 20140182, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0182. [Open access]","David Rabinovich and Yamen Smadi, “Zinc,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547698/. [Open access]","Sarai M. Keestra, Marsh Königs, Nienke van Welie, Kim Dreyer, Jaap Oosterlaan, Velja Mijatovic, Tessa J. Roseboom, and Martijn J. J. Finken, “Iodine Deficiency in the First Pregnancy Trimester and Intelligence in Adolescence,” <i>European Journal of Nutrition</i> 65, no. 3 (2026): 111, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00394-026-03955-3. [Open access]","Thomas J. Browning and C. Mark Moore, “Global Analysis of Ocean Phytoplankton Nutrient Limitation Reveals High Prevalence of Co-limitation,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 14 (2023): 5014, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-40774-0. [Open access]","Loay J. Jabre, Elden Rowland, J. Scott P. McCain, and Erin M. Bertrand, “Deep Proteomic Profiles of the Antarctic Diatom <i>Fragilariopsis cylindrus</i> under Varying Iron and Manganese Conditions,” <i>Proteomics</i> 26, no. 5 (2026): 112–19, https://doi.org/10.1002/pmic.70109. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a trace element have a window rather than a threshold?","a":"Because the same substance is a requirement at one intake and a poison at another: copper is needed for connective tissue, bone and nerve coverings, and chronic excess damages the liver."},{"q":"Why is deciding which elements belong on the list difficult?","a":"Because a human body contains at least 60 detectable elements and only about 25 are thought to take part in its healthy functioning, so presence is not evidence of a role."},{"q":"Why does the iodine study have to be read carefully?","a":"Because lower first-trimester iodine was associated with lower vocabulary scores at fifteen but not with matrix reasoning or full-scale IQ, so the effect found was narrower than the headline."}]},
{"id":"bio-045","num":45,"category":"Biology","question":"The doctrine that living matter runs on some immaterial force of its own, unavailable to ordinary chemistry, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and biology abandoned it rather than refuting it in a single stroke.","questions":["All five canonical nucleobases have been recovered from an asteroid, which removes the premise <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on, that the compounds of living things could arise only inside something alive.","Chemists can now grow membrane-bounded compartments from small prebiotic molecules in one pot, so the organisation <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> attributed to a vital force is being assembled out of ordinary reagents."],"answer":"vitalism","answerText":"vitalism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Vitalism</b> is the doctrine that living systems are animated by something immaterial, a force or principle not reducible to the matter they are made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is worth naming because the whole of this deck's chemistry is an answer to it, and because the position was neither foolish nor quickly overturned: the substances peculiar to organisms really did seem to arise only within them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Organisms turn out to be built from the same elements as everything else, of which a human body holds at least 60 detectable and only about 25 that take any part in its functioning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reactions between them run on the same rules as reactions anywhere, and the properties of water — dehydration and condensation, hydrolysis, the hydrophobic effect — are described in one recent review not as obstacles that life overcame but as channels that guided chemical evolution from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that reading nothing extra is being added to the chemistry; the chemistry is being used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The old premise has since been tested directly, and all five canonical nucleobases have been found in material returned from the asteroid Bennu, put together without life in that asteroid's parent body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The compounds are not merely present but various, and pyrimidines outnumber purines there as they do in the Orgueil meteorite and unlike Murchison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Organisation is being approached the same way: a 2026 study starts from acetaldehyde and a prebiotically plausible catalyst and obtains lipid-like molecules up to twenty carbons long that assemble themselves into membrane-bounded compartments, with the catalyst embedded in the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of that amounts to making life, and the question vitalism was reaching for — what distinguishes a living arrangement of matter from a dead one — is not settled by any of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What has gone is the idea that the answer lies in the material, since the atoms of an organism are the atoms of everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Sandy C. Boucher, “What Was Really Wrong (and Right) with Vitalism? Methodological Naturalism, Organicism and Immaterialist Theories in Biology,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 48, no. 1 (2026): 11, https://doi.org/10.1007/s40656-026-00719-4. [Open access]","Prinessa Chellan and Peter J. Sadler, “The Elements of Life and Medicines,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2037 (2015): 20140182, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0182. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Yasuhiro Oba, Toshiki Koga, Yoshinori Takano, Hiroshi Naraoka, Yuta Hirakawa, Sako Sunami, Yoshihiro Furukawa, et al., “Distribution of Extraterrestrial Nucleobases, Other N-Heterocycles, and Their Precursors in a Sample from Asteroid Bennu,” <i>Communications Chemistry</i> 9, no. 1 (2026): 132, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-026-01966-z. [Open access]","Marian Simon Rafael Ebeling, Otto Berninghausen, Khang Hoang Nguyen, Roland Beckmann, and Oliver Trapp, “Organocatalyzed Bottom-Up Formation of Protocells,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 1983, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69597-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was vitalism neither foolish nor quickly overturned?","a":"Because the substances peculiar to organisms really did seem to arise only within them, so the doctrine was an inference from the evidence available rather than a superstition."},{"q":"Why does the Bennu material bear on the doctrine directly?","a":"Because all five canonical nucleobases were found in returned asteroid material, put together without life in that asteroid’s parent body, which tests the old premise rather than arguing with it."},{"q":"Why has the question vitalism was reaching for not been settled?","a":"Because none of this amounts to making life; what has gone is the idea that the answer lies in the material, the atoms of an organism being the atoms of everything else."}]},
{"id":"bio-046","num":46,"category":"Biology","question":"Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids are the four families a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> almost always belongs to, and three of the four are polymers while one is not.","questions":["Lipids are grouped by what they do rather than by any repeating subunit, which makes them the family of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a definition built on polymers cannot reach.","Macromolecules make up 80 to 90 per cent of the dry weight of most cells, so a large <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> accounts for nearly everything a cell is built from once water is set aside."],"answer":"biomolecule","answerText":"biomolecule","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>biomolecule</b> is a molecule made and used by a living thing, and most of them belong to one of four classes: carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three of those classes are macromolecules built by joining small precursors — sugars, amino acids and nucleotides — and together they make up 80 to 90 per cent of the dry weight of most cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lipids are the exception and the other major constituent, grouped by what they do rather than by any repeating subunit, since they store energy, form the bulk of cell membranes and carry signals as steroid hormones and messengers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of this outweighs water, which accounts for about 70 per cent of a cell's weight and is where nearly every reaction between the four takes place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest of the mass is small organic molecules, perhaps a thousand kinds of them in a typical cell, serving as the subunits the large ones are made from and the intermediates they are broken into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Each class is more varied than its name suggests, and lipidomics finds even a single cell type carrying hundreds to thousands of distinct lipid species, differing in headgroup, in chain length and in how saturated those chains are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Why cells maintain such complex lipid inventories is not settled, and the review that asks the question says plainly that the significance of the diversity remains incompletely understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The carbohydrates behave least like the rest, because unlike nucleic acids and proteins their assembly is not template-driven but emerges from the competing activity of the enzymes that build and trim them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the sugar structures attached to proteins are the commonest and most diverse modification in eukaryotic cells, and why they carry information that no gene spells out directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The four are measurable outside a cell as well: across a transect of the North Pacific, a falling carbon-to-nitrogen ratio in seawater particles reflected a rise in protein relative to carbohydrate and lipid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Katrin Mani, “Glycobiology in Human Health and Disease,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 7 (2026): 2951, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27072951. [Open access]","Justin D. Liefer, Angelicque E. White, Zoe V. Finkel, Andrew J. Irwin, Mathilde Dugenne, Keisuke Inomura, François Ribalet, et al., “Latitudinal Patterns in Ocean C:N:P Reflect Phytoplankton Acclimation and Macromolecular Composition,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 46 (2024): e2404460121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2404460121. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are lipids grouped differently from the other three classes?","a":"Because they are grouped by what they do rather than by any repeating subunit, storing energy, forming the bulk of membranes and carrying signals as steroid hormones and messengers."},{"q":"Why do carbohydrates behave least like the other classes?","a":"Because their assembly is not template-driven but emerges from the competing activity of the enzymes that build and trim them, so they carry information no gene spells out directly."},{"q":"Why is the diversity of a cell’s lipids an open question?","a":"Because lipidomics finds a single cell type carrying hundreds to thousands of distinct species, and the review that asks why says the significance of that diversity remains incompletely understood."}]},
{"id":"bio-047","num":47,"category":"Biology","question":"Most members of the family have the basic formula (CH2O)n, which is where the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from, and they run from a single sugar to a chain of thousands.","questions":["Glycogen, starch and cellulose are built only from glucose and differ in their linkages, so one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can store energy where another holds a plant upright.","Because the sugars attached to a protein are assembled without any template, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can carry information that no gene spells out directly."],"answer":"carbohydrate","answerText":"carbohydrate","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>carbohydrate</b> is a sugar or a chain of sugars, most of them having the basic formula (CH2O)n, which is where the name comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Simple sugars such as glucose are the major nutrients of cells, and they are joined into longer chains by dehydration reactions that link them through a glycosidic bond between two of their carbons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No other family of biological molecule can be arranged in so many ways from so few parts, since each simple sugar exists in mirror-image d- and l-forms and those with five or more carbons can close into rings that differ in the position of a single group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Isomers of that kind play a major part in generating the enormous variety of sugars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Glycogen, starch and cellulose are composed entirely of glucose residues and differ in the bonds joining them, two of the three storing sugar while the third is the principal structural material of plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That structural role makes this the most abundant kind of organic matter on the planet, lignocellulosic biomass — cellulose and hemicellulose together with lignin — being the largest renewable source of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carbohydrates also carry information, because the sugar structures attached to proteins and lipids are not built from a template but emerge from the competing activity of the enzymes that add, trim and modify them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Glycosylation is on that account the commonest and most diverse modification of proteins in eukaryotes, and the sugar layer at a cell's surface mediates adhesion and recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In nutrition the single word covers foods that behave very differently, and one measure of quality is simply the ratio of carbohydrate to fibre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among 3,171 participants in a long-running cohort, better carbohydrate quality and a higher intake of fibre-rich foods were favourably associated with the mass and function of the left ventricle, with effect sizes between 10 and 26 per cent of a standard deviation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Katrin Mani, “Glycobiology in Human Health and Disease,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 7 (2026): 2951, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27072951. [Open access]","So-Yun Yi, Lyn M. Steffen, Weihua Guan, Daniel Duprez, Kamakshi Lakshminarayan, and David R. Jacobs, “Dietary Carbohydrate Quality, Fibre-Rich Food Intake, and Left Ventricular Structure and Function: The CARDIA Study,” <i>European Heart Journal</i> 46, no. 41 (2025): 4329–37, https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehaf406. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can so few parts be arranged in so many ways?","a":"Because each simple sugar exists in mirror-image d- and l-forms, and those with five or more carbons close into rings differing in the position of a single group."},{"q":"Why is one glucose polymer food and another a structural material?","a":"Because glycogen, starch and cellulose are all glucose residues and differ only in the bonds joining them, which is enough to make two stores of sugar and one skeleton for plants."},{"q":"Why is glycosylation the most diverse protein modification in eukaryotes?","a":"Because the sugar structures are not built from a template but emerge from enzymes competing to add, trim and modify them, so the possibilities are not fixed in advance."}]},
{"id":"bio-048","num":48,"category":"Biology","question":"A single sugar unit is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the smallest carbohydrate there is and the subunit every longer chain of sugars is assembled from.","questions":["Ribose carries five carbons and glucose six, so counting the carbons is the usual way of sorting one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from another before anything else is said about it.","Blood is held near 80 to 90 mg per decilitre of one particular <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between meals, rising to 120 to 140 afterwards before the body brings it down again."],"answer":"monosaccharide","answerText":"monosaccharide","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>monosaccharide</b> is a single sugar unit, the smallest carbohydrate and the subunit from which every longer chain of sugars is built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The basic formula is (CH2O)n, and the familiar ones are sorted by how many carbons they carry: five in ribose and deoxyribose, six in glucose, fructose and galactose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A sugar with five or more carbons can close into a ring, and it does so in either of two alternative forms according to the arrangement at one particular carbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each also exists in mirror-image d- and l-forms, and isomers of these kinds are a large part of what makes the sugars so various.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Glucose is the one most of biology runs on, the primary metabolic fuel of mammals and the universal fuel of the fetus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its concentration in blood is held within a narrow band, about 80 to 90 mg per decilitre three or four hours after eating, rising to between 120 and 140 after a meal before the body's own feedback brings it back down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Simple sugars are not confined to living things, and an analysis of the Orgueil meteorite published in 2026 detected ribose, arabinose, xylose and lyxose in it, along with the ketopentose ribulose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Several of them show near-racemic distributions, both mirror images present in nearly equal amount, which is consistent with their having formed without any life involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same work is candid about its limits, noting that sugars remain rare and poorly characterised in meteorites beside amino acids and nucleobases, and that recovery experiments show their abundances are severely underestimated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Why life then uses only the d-forms is unsettled, and one 2026 hypothesis proposes that d-ribose crosses a lipid membrane more readily than its mirror image and is trapped inside once phosphorylated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Mihir N. Nakrani, Robert H. Wineland, and Fatima Anjum, “Physiology, Glucose Metabolism,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560599/. [Open access]","Vanessa Leyva, Manuel Robert, Raphaël Pepino, Jana Bocková, and Cornelia Meinert, “Abiotic Sugar Enantiomers in the CI Carbonaceous Chondrite Orgueil,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 2060, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-68709-5. [Open access]","Vladimir M. Subbotin and Gennady Fiksel, “Stereoselective Phosphorylation of d-Ribose as a Driver of Life's Homochirality,” <i>Life</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 846, https://doi.org/10.3390/life16050846. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is blood glucose held in so narrow a band?","a":"Because it is the primary metabolic fuel of mammals and the universal fuel of the fetus, so the body’s own feedback returns it to about 80 to 90 mg per decilitre after each meal."},{"q":"Why do near-equal mirror images in a meteorite matter?","a":"Because a near-racemic distribution is consistent with the sugars having formed without any life involved, life using only one hand of each."},{"q":"Why is the meteorite work candid about being incomplete?","a":"Because sugars remain rare and poorly characterised in meteorites beside amino acids and nucleobases, and recovery experiments show their abundances are severely underestimated."}]},
{"id":"bio-049","num":49,"category":"Biology","question":"Two simple sugars joined by a glycosidic bond make a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the water expelled as the link forms has to be put back before it can be taken apart again.","questions":["Maltose is two glucoses, sucrose is glucose with fructose and lactose is glucose with galactose, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named by which pair was joined and how.","Sucrose travels out of a leaf through the phloem to wherever a plant needs it, which makes that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a transport form rather than a fuel in its own right."],"answer":"disaccharide","answerText":"disaccharide","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>disaccharide</b> is two simple sugars joined together, linked through a glycosidic bond between two of their carbons in a dehydration reaction that expels a molecule of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each monosaccharide carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form that link, so the same two sugars can be joined at different positions to give different substances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The three met most often are maltose, which is two glucoses; sucrose, which is glucose with fructose; and lactose, which is glucose with galactose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of them can be absorbed as it stands, and each must first be split back into its units by an enzyme that puts the water back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lactose is hydrolysed into glucose and galactose by lactase at the brush border of the small intestine, an enzyme whose activity peaks after birth and declines thereafter in most mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Sucrose is the form in which a plant moves sugar about, exported from the leaves through the phloem to wherever growth or storage requires it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How it is loaded differs between species, being driven actively into the apoplast in many herbaceous plants, trapped as larger oligosaccharides in others, or carried passively through interconnected cytoplasm in large trees, whose foliage reaches higher sugar concentrations in consequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trehalose, two glucoses joined differently again, is neither food nor freight but protection, a non-reducing sugar that stabilises proteins and membranes against drying out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tardigrades that survive desiccation make it, and a 2026 study found predicted trehalose-synthase genes rising in their eggs and dried tuns — though those genes belonged to the animals' bacteria, and whether the microbiome contributes is still unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What separates these molecules is largely which hydroxyl groups were joined and in what orientation, which is how two sugars of the same size become a food, a freight and a preservative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Eric Goosenberg and Muriam Afzal, “Lactose Intolerance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532285/. [Open access]","Pierre Vollenweider, Carlo di Bello, Nathalie Valle, Marco M. Lehmann, Matthias Saurer, Arthur Gessler, and Stanislaus J. Schymanski, “Towards Better Understanding of Assimilate Transfer and Phloem Loading in Conifer Trees,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 77, no. 10 (2026): 2805–9, https://doi.org/10.1093/jxb/erag016. [Open access]","Monika Mioduchowska, Pushpalata Kayastha, Magdalena M. Bartylak, Edyta Konecka, Bayu Brahmantio, Julita Mackiewicz, Wojciech Przybyszewski, et al., “Let's Dry up and Survive Together: Is Anhydrobiosis in Two Paramacrobiotus Species (Tardigrada) Associated with a Specific Microbiome Community?” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 12 (2026): 5256, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27125256. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can the same two sugars make different substances?","a":"Because each monosaccharide carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form the link, so the pair can be joined at different positions and in different orientations."},{"q":"Why must a disaccharide be split before it can be absorbed?","a":"Because none of them can be taken up as it stands: an enzyme has to put the water back and separate the units, as lactase does with lactose at the brush border."},{"q":"Why is the tardigrade trehalose finding not yet an answer?","a":"Because the predicted trehalose-synthase genes rising in eggs and dried tuns belonged to the animals’ bacteria, and whether the microbiome contributes is still unknown."}]},
{"id":"bio-050","num":50,"category":"Biology","question":"Because a chain of sugars can branch where a protein or a nucleic acid cannot, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no fixed length and no single sequence to be read off it.","questions":["Starch joins its glucose units one way and cellulose joins them the other, so the bond alone decides whether a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a store of sugar or a structural material.","Dietary fibre is largely the kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a human gut cannot break down, left instead to bacteria in the large intestine that ferment it into short-chain fatty acids."],"answer":"polysaccharide","answerText":"polysaccharide","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>polysaccharide</b> is a chain of many simple sugars joined by glycosidic bonds, and unlike a protein or a nucleic acid it has neither a fixed length nor a sequence that must be reproduced exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each sugar unit carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form a link, so a chain can branch as well as extend, which is something the other great polymer families cannot do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Glycogen, starch and cellulose are all built from glucose alone and differ in the bond that joins the units and in how often the chain divides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The glucose residues of starch and glycogen are joined in the α configuration and those of cellulose in the β, and that difference alone separates a store of sugar from a structural material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Glycogen is the storage form in animals and starch its counterpart in plants, while cellulose is the principal structural component of the plant cell wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The structural kind dominates the planet: lignocellulosic biomass, largely cellulose and hemicellulose, is the most abundant renewable source of organic matter on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Human digestion cannot touch most of it, and dietary fibre is defined as the carbohydrates and lignin that resist digestion in the small intestine and are then partly or wholly fermented in the large one, yielding short-chain fatty acids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between 2017 and 2020 American adults averaged 17 g of fibre a day against recommended intakes of 25 to 38 g, so this is a dietary role that most people undersupply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bacteria build with these molecules too, and <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> defends itself in the cystic fibrosis lung with exopolysaccharides called Psl and Pel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Enzymes that cut those chains improve the reach of antibiotics, though unevenly: one targeting Psl disrupted nine of fourteen clinical isolates and helped in 24 of 42 strain-and-drug pairings, where the Pel-cutting enzyme managed three and five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Sharon F. Daley and Aparna P. Shreenath, “The Role of Dietary Fiber in Health Promotion and Disease Prevention: A Practical Guide for Clinicians,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559033/. [Open access]","Isaac Martin, Jonathan Chung, Deepa Raju, Yvonne Yau, Amanda J. Morris, Shafinaz Eisha, P. Lynne Howell, and Valerie Waters, “Glycoside Hydrolases Enhance Antibiotic Activity against <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> Biofilms from Cystic Fibrosis Airways,” <i>Microbiology Spectrum</i> 14, no. 4 (2026): e01354-25, https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.01354-25. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a polysaccharide branch where a protein cannot?","a":"Because each sugar unit carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form a link, so a chain can divide as well as extend, and it has no sequence that must be reproduced exactly."},{"q":"Why does one bond configuration separate food from structure?","a":"Because starch and glycogen join their glucose residues in the α configuration and cellulose in the β, and that difference alone makes one a store of sugar and the other a structural material."},{"q":"Why is fibre defined by what digestion cannot do to it?","a":"Because it is the carbohydrate and lignin that resist digestion in the small intestine and are then fermented in the large one, yielding short-chain fatty acids."}]},
{"id":"bio-051","num":51,"category":"Biology","question":"The form a plant lays down when it has more sugar than it needs is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, built from glucose in the α configuration and opened again by the enzyme amylase.","questions":["Amylose is all but unbranched and amylopectin is branched, yet both sit in the same granule of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which makes up about seventy per cent of a wheat grain.","Copy number of the salivary amylase gene runs highest in Indigenous Andean populations, where selection for digesting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dates to roughly the time the potato was domesticated."],"answer":"starch","answerText":"starch","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Starch</b> is the storage polysaccharide of plants, a chain of glucose units joined in the α configuration and laid down wherever a plant has more sugar than it presently needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It exists as two molecules within the same granule: amylose, which is essentially unbranched, and amylopectin, which divides repeatedly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proportions are not fixed, and in one 2026 comparison of wheat cultivars amylose ran between 27 and 31 per cent of the starch, with the grain itself about 70 per cent starch by weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The granules differ too, medium ones measuring roughly 44 to 47 micrometres across and about a quarter of the material being crystalline, and those differences change how a flour behaves in the hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An animal reaches the glucose inside by hydrolysing those α bonds with amylase, secreted chiefly by the salivary glands and the pancreas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Amylase was among the earliest enzymes ever described, in the first decades of the 19th century, and falls into three classes that differ in where along a chain they cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not all of the starch is reached: the fraction that resists digestion in the small intestine passes on to be fermented in the large one, which is why resistant starch is counted as dietary fibre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much of it a person can digest varies genetically, since the salivary amylase gene AMY1 differs in copy number between individuals and that variation is linked to starch-rich diets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A study of 3,723 people from 85 populations found the highest copy numbers anywhere in Indigenous Peruvian Andean populations, with evidence of positive selection on a haplotype carrying at least five copies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That expansion is dated to about 10,000 years ago, which coincides with the domestication of the potato in the same region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Zhenbing Ma, Yuanyuan Tian, Rong Xiong, Haibo Lan, Jia Zheng, Shunchuan Sun, Jian Su, Chenyang Wang, and Yi Ma, “Comparative Analysis of the Fine Molecular Structures and Pasting Properties of Zhiqu and Bread Wheat Starch,” <i>Food Chemistry: X</i> 37 (2026): 104080, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fochx.2026.104080. [Open access]","Muhammad Zubair and Thiruvengadam Muniraj, “Analytical and Clinical Perspectives on Amylase,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2026), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557738/. [Open access]","Sharon F. Daley and Aparna P. Shreenath, “The Role of Dietary Fiber in Health Promotion and Disease Prevention: A Practical Guide for Clinicians,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559033/. [Open access]","Kendra Scheer, Luane J. B. Landau, Kelsey Jorgensen, Charikleia Karageorgiou, Lindsey Siao, Can Alkan, Angelis M. Morales Rivera, et al., “Rapid Adaptive Increase of Amylase Gene Copy Number in Indigenous Andeans,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 3822, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-71450-8. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does starch behave differently from one flour to the next?","a":"Because the proportions are not fixed — amylose ran between 27 and 31 per cent across wheat cultivars — and the granules differ in size and in how much of the material is crystalline."},{"q":"Why is some starch counted as dietary fibre?","a":"Because the fraction that resists digestion in the small intestine passes on to be fermented in the large one rather than being absorbed as glucose."},{"q":"Why is the Andean amylase result read as selection rather than chance?","a":"Because the highest copy numbers anywhere were found there with evidence of positive selection on a haplotype of at least five copies, dated to about 10,000 years ago, coinciding with potato domestication."}]},
{"id":"bio-052","num":52,"category":"Biology","question":"The animal store built from glucose in the α configuration and branched here and there by an α(1→6) link is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and every particle of it begins on a protein core.","questions":["After a meal the liver lays down <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the influence of insulin, giving it back over the following hours as the concentration of glucose in the blood falls.","A faulty enzyme in one storage disease leaves <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> piling up in the liver and kidneys while the patient suffers severe hypoglycaemia between meals."],"answer":"glycogen","answerText":"glycogen","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Glycogen</b> is the storage carbohydrate of animals, composed entirely of glucose molecules in the α configuration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its principal linkage joins carbon 1 of one glucose to carbon 4 of the next, while occasional α(1→6) links attach a new chain to the side of an existing one, so that the molecule divides again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Starch is built the same way, its amylopectin branched and its amylose not, which makes the animal store and the plant store a difference of degree rather than of kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each particle is begun on a protein rather than from nothing: glycogenin attaches the first glucose units to itself, and the chains grow outward from that core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Humans carry two glycogenins, and a 2025 study reports that GYG2 has minimal self-glucosylating activity and acts as a suppressor of glycogen formation while still driving particle assembly, which GYG1 does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The liver's store serves the whole body: after a meal, insulin has glucose deposited there as glycogen, and over the following hours it is handed back as the concentration in the blood falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the handing back fails the consequences are severe, and in glycogen storage disease type I a defective glucose-6-phosphatase leaves glycogen accumulating in the liver and kidneys with severe fasting hypoglycaemia, high blood lipids and an enlarged liver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That condition occurs in about 1 in 100,000 live births, four fifths of cases being the type Ia form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Muscle keeps a separate store, and how much of it a trained athlete needs is genuinely disputed, a 2026 nutrition journal setting the cases for and against low-carbohydrate diets side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their agreed statement holds that promoting carbohydrate availability remains the best approach, while allowing that a ketogenic diet raises the capacity to oxidise fat and that even then carbohydrate taken during long events improves performance by preventing hypoglycaemia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Tzu-Han Weng, Yu-Chung Pien, Ching-Jou Chen, Po-Pang Chen, Yu-Ting Tseng, Ying-Chen Chen, Wen-Po Hsiao, et al., “Human Glycogenins Maintain Glucose Homeostasis by Regulating Glycogen Metabolism,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 6556, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-61862-3. [Open access]","Mihir N. Nakrani, Robert H. Wineland, and Fatima Anjum, “Physiology, Glucose Metabolism,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560599/. [Open access]","Mitan Nana, Catherine Anastasopoulou, Nirzar S. Parikh, and Rajni Ahlawat, “Glycogen Storage Disease Type I,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534196/. [Open access]","Louise M. Burke and Timothy D. Noakes, “Does a Low-Carbohydrate Diet Impede Endurance Sports Performance? Debate Consensus,” <i>The American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</i> 123, no. 5 (2026): 101271, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajcnut.2026.101271. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the animal and plant stores a difference of degree?","a":"Because starch is built the same way, its amylopectin branched and its amylose not, so glycogen differs from it in how often the chain divides rather than in kind."},{"q":"Why does a glycogen particle need a protein at its centre?","a":"Because it is not begun from nothing: glycogenin attaches the first glucose units to itself, and the chains grow outward from that core."},{"q":"Why is failing to hand the store back so serious?","a":"Because in glycogen storage disease type I a defective glucose-6-phosphatase leaves glycogen accumulating in liver and kidneys, with severe fasting hypoglycaemia, high blood lipids and an enlarged liver."}]},
{"id":"bio-053","num":53,"category":"Biology","question":"Built from glucose like starch but joined in the β configuration, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> forms straight chains that bind side by side into fibres no starch enzyme can open.","questions":["Termites live on wood by proxy, depending on gut microbes and flagellated protists to break down the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in it that no animal enzyme of their own can touch.","Even though it is the most abundant renewable organic matter on Earth, the packing of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> microfibrils at the nanoscale is still being argued over."],"answer":"cellulose","answerText":"cellulose","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Cellulose</b> is the principal structural material of the plant cell wall, composed entirely of glucose as starch and glycogen are, but with its units joined in the β rather than the α configuration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That single difference straightens the chain, so that many of them lie alongside one another and bind into fibres, and it puts the linkage out of reach of the enzymes that open a store of starch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a plant it is a building material rather than a reserve, which is how the same sugar that fuels an animal can also hold a tree upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result is the most abundant renewable source of organic matter on the planet, lignocellulosic biomass being largely cellulose together with hemicellulose and lignin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fine structure is nonetheless still being worked out, and a 2025 study opens by calling the structure of plant cellulose microfibrils elusive despite the abundance of the material and its industrial use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Solid-state NMR of never-dried plants resolved six distinct glucose environments common to softwood, hardwood and grasses, of which only two lie within the core of a microfibril.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second study of wheat straw published in 2026 notes that no explicit structural model had yet satisfied X-ray scattering and NMR observations at the same time, and proposes one that does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> So the commonest structural polymer in the living world is one whose arrangement at the nanometre scale is still being settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Animals that live on it do so at second hand: the gut microbiome of termites is what degrades the lignocellulose they eat, and flagellated protists are essential symbionts of the lower termites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A 2026 metagenomic comparison of two <i>Anacanthotermes</i> species found bacteria making up about 53 per cent of the gut community in both, while the diversity of that community differed between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Rosalie Cresswell, Parveen Kumar Deralia, Yoshihisa Yoshimi, Tomohiro Kuga, Alberto Echevarría-Poza, W. Trent Franks, Steven P. Brown, Ray Dupree, and Paul Dupree, “Using Solid-State NMR to Understand the Structure of Plant Cellulose,” <i>Journal of the American Chemical Society</i> 147, no. 51 (2025): 47223–36, https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.5c14452. [Open access]","Yucheng Hu, Pan Chen, Peng Xiao, Shixu Yu, Lingfeng Zhou, Zhe Ling, Yutong Zhu, et al., “Native Architecture of Wheat Straw Cell Walls: A Unified Model from X-ray Scattering and Solid-State NMR,” <i>Journal of the American Chemical Society</i> 148, no. 20 (2026): 20448–63, https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.5c23116. [Open access]","Ulugbek Togaev, Vartika Mathur, Aziza Rakhmonkulova, Surbhi Agarwal, Aarav Mathur, Shuhrat Turageldiyev, Rasul Ruzmetov, et al., “Comparative Metagenomic Analysis of Gut Microbiota in Anacanthotermes turkestanicus and A. ahngerianus Reveals Diet- and Habitat-Driven Functional Divergence,” <i>Frontiers in Insect Science</i> 6 (2026): 1807673, https://doi.org/10.3389/finsc.2026.1807673. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one bond configuration turn a food into a building material?","a":"Because the β linkage straightens the chain, so many lie alongside one another and bind into fibres, and it puts the bond out of reach of the enzymes that open a store of starch."},{"q":"Why is the commonest structural polymer still not fully described?","a":"Because its arrangement at the nanometre scale is still being settled: a 2025 study calls plant cellulose microfibril structure elusive, and no model had satisfied X-ray scattering and NMR at once until 2026."},{"q":"Why do animals that live on cellulose do so at second hand?","a":"Because it is the gut microbiome that degrades the lignocellulose — in termites, with flagellated protists as essential symbionts of the lower species."}]},
{"id":"bio-054","num":54,"category":"Biology","question":"Built from a nitrogen-bearing sugar rather than plain glucose but linked the same &beta; way, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stiffens the cuticle of every insect and the wall of every fungus.","questions":["An insect can only grow by shedding its old cuticle, so it must carry enzymes that digest its own <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and silencing one of them is lethal.","Second only to cellulose in abundance, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> barely accumulates in marine sediments, because bacteria in the sea carry the enzymes to break it apart again."],"answer":"chitin","answerText":"chitin","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Chitin</b> is a polysaccharide built from N-acetylglucosamine, a glucose ring carrying an acetylamino group, with the sugars joined end to end by &beta;-1,4 glycosidic bonds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That &beta; linkage is what makes cellulose a fibre rather than a store, and it does the same work here, holding the chains straight so they pack side by side instead of coiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the structural material of fungal cell walls and of the cuticle of crustaceans and insects, and some algae build with it as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only cellulose is more abundant: the living world is estimated to make something on the order of a hundred billion tons of chitin a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost none of it accumulates in marine sediments, because heterotrophic bacteria in the sea carry the enzymes to take it apart and return the carbon and nitrogen locked in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the material is rigid it cannot stretch, so an arthropod grows only by shedding it, which means digesting part of its own cuticle with chitinases it makes itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the pine sawyer beetle <i>Monochamus alternatus</i>, silencing one such enzyme, MaCht-2, caused molting failure, disordered cuticle ultrastructure, impaired movement and heavy mortality in larvae, pupae and adults alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Animals that eat arthropods meet the same polymer from the other side, and mammals break it down with an enzyme called acidic mammalian chitinase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Across 37 rodent species the gene for it, CHIA, is often deleted or turned into a pseudogene in obligate herbivores whose food holds almost no chitin, and the strength of selection on it tracks how much of the diet is invertebrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chitin is also a signal the immune system watches for, and fungi such as <i>Cryptococcus neoformans</i> carry deacetylases that convert wall chitin into chitosan, which blunts the response that exposed chitin would provoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Xuebing Ren, Xin Song, Hang Zhang, Guangqiang Wang, Fan Xie, Haerlihashi Muhetaerhan, Lianzhong Ai, and Yanjun Tian, “Genomic and Transcriptomic Analysis Reveals Chitin Metabolic Pathways in the Marine Bacterium <i>Microbulbifer harenosus</i> CGMCC 1.13584T,” <i>BMC Microbiology</i> 26, no. 1 (2026): 562, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12866-026-05115-3. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Siming Fang, Xiaoxiao Chang, Han Chen, and Juan Shi, “RNA Interference Targeting MaCht-2 Induces Severe Molting Defects and Lethality in <i>Monochamus alternatus</i>,” <i>Insects</i> 17, no. 5 (2026): 530, https://doi.org/10.3390/insects17050530. [Open access]","Yuntao Tian, Zhenyu Xiong, Chengyao Yang, Chaoyang Luo, Xionghui Xu, Yangsong Wu, Qiurong Li, et al., “Evolution of Acidic Mammalian Chitinase Gene (<i>CHIA</i>) Is Related to Insectivory Feeding in Rodents,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 7 (2026): e74062, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.74062. [Open access]","Margareta J. Hellmann, Rajendra Upadhya, Evelyn Tchoub, Bruno M. Moerschbacher, Jennifer K. Lodge, and Stefan Cord-Landwehr, “Acetylation and Accessibility of <i>Cryptococcus neoformans</i> Cell Wall Chitosans Influence the Strength of Host Immune Responses,” <i>The Cell Surface</i> 15 (2026): 100175, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcsw.2026.100175. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does chitin behave like cellulose rather than like a sugar store?","a":"Because its sugars are joined by the same β-1,4 bonds, which hold the chains straight so they pack side by side instead of coiling."},{"q":"Why must an arthropod digest part of itself in order to grow?","a":"Because the material is rigid and cannot stretch, so growth means shedding the cuticle, which it does with chitinases of its own making."},{"q":"Why does the CHIA gene’s fate track diet across rodents?","a":"Because it is often deleted or turned into a pseudogene in obligate herbivores whose food holds almost no chitin, with the strength of selection tracking how much of the diet is invertebrate."}]},
{"id":"bio-055","num":55,"category":"Biology","question":"Defined by behaviour rather than by a shared backbone, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is any of the water-shunning molecules a cell uses to store energy, build its membranes and carry signals.","questions":["A gram of fat yields more than twice the energy a gram of carbohydrate does, which is why an animal that must carry its reserves banks them in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> form rather than sugar.","Mass-spectrometry surveys find more than a thousand distinct <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> species in a single mammalian cell, and nobody yet knows why a cell should need so many."],"answer":"lipid","answerText":"lipid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Lipids</b> are a diverse group of small molecules, hydrophobic or amphipathic, whose chemical structures vary widely, so the category is held together by how its members behave in water rather than by a common backbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The simplest are the fatty acids, long hydrocarbon chains ending in a carboxyl group, whose nonpolar carbon-hydrogen bonds cannot interact with water; that hydrophobicity governs much of how the more complex lipids behave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cells put them to three uses: storing energy, building membranes and carrying signals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fats, three fatty acids joined to a glycerol, are the storage form, and they yield more than twice as much energy per unit weight as carbohydrates, which lets an animal hold the same reserve in less than half the body weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phospholipids, two fatty acids attached to a polar head group, are the principal components of cell membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That tidy list understates the category, because a single cell type can hold hundreds to thousands of distinct lipid species, and mammalian cells more than a thousand, differing in head group, chain length and degree of unsaturation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Why a cell should maintain so many is unsettled, and the explanations on offer run from the physics of membranes through specific binding to proteins to signalling and evolutionary history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Part of the answer is that the mixture is tuned rather than fixed: cold packs membrane lipids more tightly, so organisms vary the proportion carrying unsaturated chains to hold fluidity steady, a response called homeoviscous adaptation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <i>Escherichia coli</i> completes it within a single generation of a temperature shock, using a valve that shunts synthesis between the saturated and unsaturated branches and a feedback loop that deliberately overshoots before settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much of the variety is strictly necessary is another open question, since a synthetic compartment can be made from a single lipid species and how complex a lipidome a self-reproducing cell needs is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Maria Fedorova, Anne K. Bendt, Justine Bertrand-Michel, Oliver Fiehn, Joanna Godzien, Laura Goracci, Florian Gruber, et al., “A Lipidomics Roadmap: From Basic Research to Societal Challenges,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 4778, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-73797-4. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Loles Hoogerland, Stefan Pieter Hendrik van den Berg, Yixing Suo, Yuta W. Moriuchi, Adja Zoumaro-Djayoon, Esther Geurken, Flora Yang, et al., “A Temperature-Sensitive Metabolic Valve and a Transcriptional Feedback Loop Drive Rapid Homeoviscous Adaptation in <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15, no. 1 (2024): 9386, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-53677-5. [Open access]","Sergiy Gan, Victoria Scarpelli, and Marten Exterkate, “Exploring Lipid Diversity and Minimalism to Define Membrane Requirements for Synthetic Cells,” <i>FEBS Letters</i> 599, no. 24 (2025): 3569–90, https://doi.org/10.1002/1873-3468.70131. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is this category defined by behaviour rather than by structure?","a":"Because its members' chemical structures vary widely and what they share is being hydrophobic or amphipathic — how they behave in water rather than any common backbone."},{"q":"Why is fat the storage form rather than carbohydrate?","a":"Because fats yield more than twice as much energy per unit weight, which lets an animal hold the same reserve in less than half the body weight."},{"q":"Why do organisms change their lipid mixture with temperature?","a":"Because cold packs membrane lipids more tightly, so they vary the proportion carrying unsaturated chains to hold fluidity steady — homeoviscous adaptation, done within a generation in E. coli."}]},
{"id":"bio-056","num":56,"category":"Biology","question":"Three fatty acids esterified to one glycerol leave no polar group at all, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> will not dissolve in water and collects instead as a droplet.","questions":["Adrenaline does not reach the fat itself but unlocks it, phosphorylating the protein that coats the droplet so the lipases can begin cutting a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart.","Because it cannot dissolve in blood, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travels in a lipoprotein particle, and the level of those particles is what a routine blood test reports."],"answer":"triglyceride","answerText":"triglyceride","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>triglyceride</b>, or triacylglycerol, is three fatty acids joined to a single glycerol molecule, and it is the form in which fatty acids are stored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With all three of the glycerol's hydroxyls taken up in ester bonds the molecule keeps no polar group, so it is insoluble in water and gathers as fat droplets in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what makes it worth using as a fuel store, since fats yield more than twice as much energy per unit weight as carbohydrates and let an animal carry a given reserve in less than half the body weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building one ends with diacylglycerol acyltransferase, which esterifies the third fatty acid onto a diacylglycerol and catalyses the committed, rate-limiting step of the pathway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plants run the same chemistry into their seeds, which is where seed oil comes from; oats hold more of it than any other cereal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Getting the energy back out is lipolysis, and three enzymes take the fatty acids off in turn: adipose triglyceride lipase, hormone-sensitive lipase and monoacylglycerol lipase, leaving free fatty acids and glycerol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The store is locked rather than merely kept, because perilipin-1 coats the droplet and holds the first lipase's coactivator away from it until adrenaline, acting through protein kinase A, phosphorylates the perilipin and lets go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is not the only switch, and work on adipose tissue in the bone marrow, which resists the usual cues, describes a route driven by low glucose and low insulin that needs no adrenaline at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because a triglyceride will not dissolve in blood either, it travels inside lipoprotein particles, chylomicrons from the gut after a fatty meal and very-low-density lipoprotein from the liver, whose cargo lipoprotein lipase cuts up at the tissue that needs it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those particles and the remnants they leave are what a blood triglyceride reading measures, and their disordered handling raises the risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Yan Sun, Jinzhou Yang, Ruirui Hu, Chen Li, Xiping Sun, Zhiwei Zhang, Runzhi Li, and Jinai Xue, “Systematic Analysis of the Diacylglycerol Acyltransferase (DGAT) Genes in Oat and Functional Characterization of AsDGAT2-1 Involved in Oil Accumulation and Cold Stress Tolerance,” <i>BMC Plant Biology</i> 26, no. 1 (2026): 398, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12870-026-08254-x. [Open access]","Courtney L. Bordelon and Jacqueline M. Stephens, “Lipolysis in Health and Disease: Pathways, Regulation, and Metabolic Consequences,” <i>Current Diabetes Reports</i> 26, no. 1 (2026): 8, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11892-026-01624-7. [Open access]","Xiao Zhang, Sreejith S. Panicker, Jordan M. Bollinger, Anurag Majumdar, Rami Kheireddine, Lila F. Dabill, Clara Kim, et al., “A Catecholamine-Independent Pathway Controlling Adaptive Adipocyte Lipolysis,” <i>Nature Metabolism</i> 8, no. 1 (2026): 96–115, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42255-025-01424-5. [Open access]","Henry N. Ginsberg and Karin E. Bornfeldt, “Triglyceride-Rich Lipoprotein Metabolism and Cardiovascular Risk in Diabetes: Bedside to Bench to Bedside,” <i>Circulation Research</i> 138, no. 12 (2026): e327267, https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCRESAHA.125.327267. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a triglyceride gather as droplets rather than dissolving?","a":"Because all three of the glycerol’s hydroxyls are taken up in ester bonds, so the molecule keeps no polar group and is insoluble in water."},{"q":"Why is the store described as locked rather than merely kept?","a":"Because perilipin-1 coats the droplet and holds the first lipase’s coactivator away until adrenaline, acting through protein kinase A, phosphorylates the perilipin and lets go."},{"q":"Why must fat travel inside particles in the blood?","a":"Because it will not dissolve there either, so it moves as chylomicrons from the gut and very-low-density lipoprotein from the liver, cut up by lipoprotein lipase at the tissue that needs it."}]},
{"id":"bio-057","num":57,"category":"Biology","question":"A long hydrocarbon tail with a carboxyl group at one end, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the simplest of the lipids and the piece the rest of them are assembled from.","questions":["Mitochondria dismantle one by clipping two carbons off the end at a time, and the chain has to be carried in on a carnitine shuttle before any <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be burnt.","Two of them, linoleic and alpha-linolenic, are called essential because no human enzyme can make either, so this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to come from food."],"answer":"fatty acid","answerText":"fatty acid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>fatty acid</b> is the simplest kind of lipid: a long hydrocarbon chain with a carboxyl group at one end, most often sixteen or eighteen carbons long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain carries only nonpolar carbon-hydrogen bonds and cannot interact with water, and that hydrophobicity accounts for much of how the larger lipids built from fatty acids behave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chains are written as a pair of numbers giving length and the count of double bonds, so palmitate is 16:0, stearate 18:0 and oleate 18:1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Making one is the work of fatty acid synthase, which assembles saturated long chains, predominantly palmitate, from acetyl-CoA and malonyl-CoA using NADPH.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Taking one apart runs the other way, and mitochondrial beta-oxidation clips off acetyl-CoA to leave a chain two carbons shorter, over and over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A long chain cannot simply drift into a mitochondrion, so it is attached to carnitine and carried across, a transfer that is the rate-limiting step of the whole oxidation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each round yields one acetyl-CoA along with a reduced flavin adenine dinucleotide and a reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, which is why fat is such a productive fuel during fasting and hard exercise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two fatty acids are exceptions to all this, because humans lack the enzymes to build linoleic acid or alpha-linolenic acid and must eat them instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> George Burr showed as much in 1929, when rats on a fat-free diet fell ill and a little linoleic acid put them right again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those chains have to be made by something, and much of the world's supply begins in marine diatoms and other microalgae at the base of the ocean food web, whose fatty acid profiles shift measurably under warming and acidification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Jacob T. Talley and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Fatty Acid Oxidation,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556002/. [Open access]","Tongchun Lan, Xinru Zhang, Jiarui Xie, Xu Xing, Chunyan Wang, and Xiaofeng Ma, “Inhibitory Effects and the Potential Mechanism of Phloretin on Animal Fatty Acid Synthase,” <i>ACS Omega</i> 10, no. 35 (2025): 40392–401, https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.5c05729. [Open access]","Jodi Wolff, Mary Petrea Cober, and Katie A. Huff, “Essential Fatty Acid Deficiency in Parenteral Nutrition: Historical Perspective and Modern Solutions, a Narrative Review,” <i>Nutrition in Clinical Practice</i> 40, no. 2 (2025): 350–67, https://doi.org/10.1002/ncp.11278. [Open access]","Maria Nicoară, Charlotte L. Briddon, Alexandra Mortu, Adriana Hegedűs, Emese Gal, Horia Leonard Banciu, and Bogdan Drugă, “Warming and Acidification Alter Essential Fatty Acid Profiles in Marine Diatom <i>Skeletonema marinoi</i>,” <i>Journal of Plankton Research</i> 48, no. 4 (2026): fbag057, https://doi.org/10.1093/plankt/fbag057. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must a long fatty acid be carried into a mitochondrion?","a":"Because it cannot simply drift in, so it is attached to carnitine and ferried across, a transfer that is the rate-limiting step of the whole oxidation."},{"q":"Why is fat so productive a fuel during fasting and hard exercise?","a":"Because each round of beta-oxidation clips off an acetyl-CoA along with a reduced flavin adenine dinucleotide and a reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, over and over down the chain."},{"q":"Why are two fatty acids exceptions that must be eaten?","a":"Because humans lack the enzymes to build linoleic acid or alpha-linolenic acid, which Burr showed in 1929 when rats on a fat-free diet fell ill and a little linoleic acid put them right."}]},
{"id":"bio-058","num":58,"category":"Biology","question":"Because every carbon along the chain already holds as many hydrogens as it can, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> packs tightly against its neighbours and leaves a membrane in a more ordered state.","questions":["Nine randomised trials covering 13,532 people found no significant fall in mortality from cutting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> intake, which is not the answer the food labels imply.","Invented in the early 1900s as a cheap stand-in for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from butter and animals, partially hydrogenated oil kept longer and survived repeated deep frying."],"answer":"saturated fat","answerText":"saturated fat","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","nutrition"],"abstract":"A <b>saturated fat</b> is one whose fatty acid chains carry no carbon-carbon double bonds, every carbon being bonded to as many hydrogen atoms as it can hold, where an unsaturated fat has one or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That single difference decides how the chains pack against one another, and unsaturated lipids tend to raise membrane fluidity while saturated ones promote a more ordered state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cold reduces fluidity by pressing membrane lipids closer together, so organisms counteract it by varying the proportion of chains that disrupt the packing, unsaturated ones among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saturation and chain length are the two variables a cell routinely adjusts to keep membrane fluidity inside the narrow range it has to stay in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difference tells in a kitchen as well as a membrane, and partially hydrogenated oils were developed in the early 1900s as a cheap replacement for butter and other animal fats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Industry liked them because they are inexpensive, they extend the shelf life of processed food and they need changing less often than natural oils during deep frying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The process also yields industrially produced trans fat, estimated to cause up to 500,000 deaths a year and entirely replaceable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since 2018 the World Health Organization has run a global elimination programme against it, and 43 countries have adopted best-practice regulations covering a further 3.2 billion people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saturated fat itself is a harder case than the labels suggest: a meta-analysis of nine randomised trials with 13,532 participants found no significant difference in cardiovascular or all-cause mortality between restricting it and not, and concluded that restriction cannot be recommended at present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is a finding about the trials rather than about food, and commentary on it argues the question turns on what the saturated fat is replaced by and which foods it arrives in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Loles Hoogerland, Stefan Pieter Hendrik van den Berg, Yixing Suo, Yuta W. Moriuchi, Adja Zoumaro-Djayoon, Esther Geurken, Flora Yang, et al., “A Temperature-Sensitive Metabolic Valve and a Transcriptional Feedback Loop Drive Rapid Homeoviscous Adaptation in <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15, no. 1 (2024): 9386, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-53677-5. [Open access]","Lindsay Steele, Elizabeth Drummond, Chizuru Nishida, Rain Yamamoto, Francesco Branca, Cristina Parsons Perez, Lorena Allemandi, et al., “Ending Trans Fat: The First-Ever Global Elimination Program for a Noncommunicable Disease Risk Factor,” <i>Journal of the American College of Cardiology</i> 84, no. 7 (2024): 663–74, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2024.04.067. [Open access]","Satoru Yamada, Tomomi Shirai, Sakiko Inaba, Gaku Inoue, Minami Torigoe, and Naoto Fukuyama, “Saturated Fat Restriction for Cardiovascular Disease Prevention: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials,” <i>JMA Journal</i> 8, no. 2 (2025): 395–407, https://doi.org/10.31662/jmaj.2024-0324. [Open access]","Atsushi Mizuno, “There Is More Than Meets the Label: Rethinking Saturated Fat and Cardiovascular Health,” <i>JMA Journal</i> 8, no. 2 (2025): 408–10, https://doi.org/10.31662/jmaj.2025-0120. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one difference in the chain change a membrane?","a":"Because carbon-carbon double bonds decide how chains pack against one another, so unsaturated lipids tend to raise membrane fluidity while saturated ones promote a more ordered state."},{"q":"Why did industry adopt partially hydrogenated oils?","a":"Because they are inexpensive, extend the shelf life of processed food and need changing less often than natural oils during deep frying, which is why they replaced butter and animal fats."},{"q":"Why is saturated fat a harder case than the labels suggest?","a":"Because a meta-analysis of nine randomised trials with 13,532 participants found no significant difference in cardiovascular or all-cause mortality between restricting it and not."}]},
{"id":"bio-059","num":59,"category":"Biology","question":"Two water-hating tails on one water-loving head make a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> amphipathic, and that split alone is enough to send a sheet of them assembling into a membrane.","questions":["Flippases and scramblases spend energy keeping each kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the face of the membrane where it belongs, and a dying cell stops paying.","Some enveloped viruses carry a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> normally hidden inside a living cell on their outer surface instead, so a scavenging cell swallows them as apoptotic debris."],"answer":"phospholipid","answerText":"phospholipid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>phospholipid</b> is two fatty acids joined to a polar head group, and in the glycerol phospholipids those chains are bound to a glycerol whose third carbon carries a phosphate instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That phosphate usually has a small polar molecule attached to it, choline or ethanolamine or serine or inositol, and which one it carries is what names the lipid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sphingomyelin is the exception, the only non-glycerol phospholipid in cell membranes, its two chains hanging from a head group built on serine rather than glycerol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of them therefore have two hydrophobic tails and a hydrophilic head, which makes the molecule amphipathic, part water-soluble and part not, and that property is the basis on which biological membranes form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a strong enough property to work alone, since a synthetic compartment can be assembled out of a single lipid species with nothing else present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The head group is not decoration, and phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate is cut by phospholipase C into diacylglycerol and inositol trisphosphate, which in turn releases calcium inside the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two faces of a membrane are not alike either, since phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin sit mostly in the outer leaflet while phosphatidylserine, phosphatidylethanolamine and phosphatidylinositol are enriched in the inner one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That arrangement is held rather than settled, maintained by ATP-driven flippases, floppases and scramblases that go on moving lipids across the bilayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When a cell dies the arrangement collapses and phosphatidylserine appears on the outside, where it acts as an eat-me signal for scavenging cells, although on its own it is not enough to make one engulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Several enveloped viruses exploit exactly that, carrying phosphatidylserine on their own membranes so that a cell takes them in as though they were the debris of a dead one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Sergiy Gan, Victoria Scarpelli, and Marten Exterkate, “Exploring Lipid Diversity and Minimalism to Define Membrane Requirements for Synthetic Cells,” <i>FEBS Letters</i> 599, no. 24 (2025): 3569–90, https://doi.org/10.1002/1873-3468.70131. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Metabolic Control of Membrane Lipid Asymmetry in Cancer,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 3846, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27093846. [Open access]","Melinda A. Brindley and Michael A. Pisciotta, “Viral Apoptotic Mimicry and the Role of Phosphatidylserine Receptors,” <i>mSphere</i> 10, no. 10 (2025): e00089-25, https://doi.org/10.1128/msphere.00089-25. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a membrane form from these molecules alone?","a":"Because each has two hydrophobic tails and a hydrophilic head, and that amphipathic property is strong enough on its own that a compartment can be assembled from a single lipid species."},{"q":"Why is the difference between a membrane’s two faces maintained rather than settled?","a":"Because ATP-driven flippases, floppases and scramblases go on moving lipids across the bilayer to keep phosphatidylserine and its neighbours on the inner leaflet."},{"q":"Why does that arrangement matter when a cell dies?","a":"Because it collapses and phosphatidylserine appears on the outside as an eat-me signal — which several enveloped viruses exploit by carrying it on their own membranes."}]},
{"id":"bio-060","num":60,"category":"Biology","question":"Built on four fused hydrocarbon rings instead of the long straight chains of a fat, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the odd shape among the lipids and the one hormones are cut from.","questions":["Being a lipid, one of these crosses the membrane rather than stopping at it, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is met by a receptor inside the cell that switches genes on directly.","Fungi build ergosterol where animals build cholesterol, and an antifungal azole exploits that gap in the pathway to this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without harming its patient."],"answer":"steroid","answerText":"steroid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>steroid</b> is a lipid built on four fused hydrocarbon rings rather than on the long straight chains of a fat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cholesterol is the familiar one, and its rings are strongly hydrophobic while the single hydroxyl group at one end is weakly hydrophilic, which makes the whole molecule amphipathic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the precursor of the steroid hormones, a diverse set of chemical messengers that all carry those same four rings and differ in the functional groups attached to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making one begins with the steroidogenic acute regulatory protein, which moves cholesterol to the inner mitochondrial membrane, where the enzyme CYP11A1 converts it to pregnenolone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest follows from there, pregnenolone giving progesterone and the corticosteroids while dehydroepiandrosterone gives the androgens and oestrogens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Because a steroid is a lipid it crosses a membrane rather than stopping at it, and its receptor waits inside the cell and works as a ligand-activated transcription factor, so the hormone reaches the genes directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vitamin D belongs to the family as well, a secosteroid made in skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol, an intermediate of cholesterol synthesis, under ultraviolet B light, and acting through a receptor of exactly that kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plants build their own, and the brassinosteroids, the sixth class of plant hormone to be identified, govern growth and environmental adaptation in rice and are now a target for breeding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fungi build ergosterol where animals build cholesterol, which is why irradiating a mushroom yields vitamin D2 rather than D3.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That same gap is what the azole antifungals exploit, blocking lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase in the ergosterol pathway, and point mutations in the gene for it now turn up in <i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i> worldwide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Kha Chin Long and Elena Aisha Azizan, “The Adrenal Gland and Primary Aldosteronism: Anatomy, Steroidogenesis, Regulation, and Genetic Insights,” <i>Frontiers in Endocrinology</i> 16 (2026): 1736534, https://doi.org/10.3389/fendo.2025.1736534. [Open access]","Marianna Raczyk and Carsten Carlberg, “From Sunlight to Signaling: Evolutionary Integration of Vitamin D and Sterol Metabolism,” <i>Metabolites</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 74, https://doi.org/10.3390/metabo16010074. [Open access]","Shuai Li, HanJing Sha, JinHui Zhang, ZhongHua Wei, LiChao Liu, LongNan Men, ZhongHua Sun, et al., “Harnessing Brassinosteroid Signaling in Rice: From Molecular Pathways to Environmentally Adaptive Breeding,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 17 (2026): 1781641, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1781641. [Open access]","Anneke Fengler, Oliver Bader, Oliver Cornely, Oliver Kurzai, Jacques F. Meis, Florent Morio, and Axel Hamprecht, “Global Epidemiology of Azole Resistance in <i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i>,” <i>JAC-Antimicrobial Resistance</i> 8, no. 1 (2026): dlaf219, https://doi.org/10.1093/jacamr/dlaf219. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a steroid hormone reach the genes directly?","a":"Because it is a lipid and crosses a membrane rather than stopping at it, so its receptor waits inside the cell and works as a ligand-activated transcription factor."},{"q":"Why do so many different messengers come from one molecule?","a":"Because the steroid hormones all carry cholesterol’s four fused rings and differ only in the functional groups attached to them, branching from pregnenolone onwards."},{"q":"Why can an antifungal drug attack a pathway animals also have?","a":"Because fungi build ergosterol where animals build cholesterol, so the azoles block lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase in a step that is the fungus’s alone."}]},
{"id":"bio-061","num":61,"category":"Biology","question":"Named from the Greek for 'of the first rank', a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a polymer of twenty kinds of amino acid and, as an enzyme, catalyses nearly every reaction in a cell.","questions":["Deep learning now predicts the folded shape of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> straight from its sequence, though it still does poorly on disordered regions and large assemblies.","Enamel and collagen sequences read from fossils a million years old show that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlasts the DNA that specified it, which is why palaeontologists now look for them."],"answer":"protein","answerText":"protein","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","genetics"],"abstract":"A <b>protein</b> is a polymer of amino acids, of which twenty kinds are used, and proteins are the most diverse of all the macromolecules, a single cell holding several thousand different ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They serve as structural components of cells and tissues, they transport and store small molecules as haemoglobin carries oxygen, they carry information between cells as protein hormones, and they defend against infection as antibodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most fundamental role is catalysis, since as enzymes they speed nearly every chemical reaction in a living system, so that proteins direct virtually all of a cell's activities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name records that judgement, coming from the Greek <i>proteios</i>, meaning 'of the first rank'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Which protein a chain becomes depends on the order of its amino acids, each of which carries a distinctive side chain whose chemistry decides the part it plays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Working out what shape a given sequence folds into was for decades a problem solved one protein at a time, by measurement rather than calculation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deep learning changed that, and millions of structure models have been generated in a very short space of time, led by AlphaFold, which predicts to near-experimental accuracy directly from a sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has not closed the problem, because prediction remains weakest for intrinsically disordered regions, for ligand-bound states, for post-translational modifications and for large multi-component assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting proteins is harder than counting genes, since one gene no longer means one protein: alternative translation start sites alone yield several proteoforms from a single transcript, differing in activity, location and dynamics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Proteins also outlast the DNA that specified them, and enamel and collagen sequences have been recovered from fossil material at least a million years old and used to test how extinct hominids are related.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Martin Luke Rennie and Michael R. Oliver, “Emerging Frontiers in Protein Structure Prediction Following the AlphaFold Revolution,” <i>Journal of the Royal Society Interface</i> 22, no. 225 (2025): 20240886, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2024.0886. [Open access]","Mandarina Qing Cheng Li, Sihan Wang, Shi-Ruei Lin, Li Eric Ngok Ting, Zhi-Hong Wan, Guodong Xie, and Jane Zhang, “Advantages and Limitations of AlphaFold in Structural Biology: Insights from Recent Studies,” <i>The Protein Journal</i> 45, no. 1 (2026): 22–38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10930-025-10310-8. [Open access]","Evan J. Morrison and Olivia S. Rissland, “The Generation and Consequences of N-terminal Proteoform Diversity,” <i>Cell Reports</i> 44, no. 9 (2025): 116275, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2025.116275. [Open access]","Ioannis Patramanis, Laurits Skov, Enrico Cappellini, and Fernando Racimo, “Assessing the Potential of Ancient Protein Sequences in the Study of Hominid Evolution,” <i>Genome Biology and Evolution</i> 18, no. 3 (2026): evag035, https://doi.org/10.1093/gbe/evag035. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is catalysis called the most fundamental of the roles?","a":"Because as enzymes proteins speed nearly every chemical reaction in a living system, so they direct virtually all of a cell’s activities rather than merely taking part in them."},{"q":"Why has structure prediction not closed the folding problem?","a":"Because prediction remains weakest for intrinsically disordered regions, for ligand-bound states, for post-translational modifications and for large multi-component assemblies."},{"q":"Why is counting proteins harder than counting genes?","a":"Because one gene no longer means one protein: alternative translation start sites alone yield several proteoforms from a single transcript, differing in activity, location and dynamics."}]},
{"id":"bio-062","num":62,"category":"Biology","question":"One carbon carrying a carboxyl group, a nitrogen group, a hydrogen and one distinctive side chain makes an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that side chain decides everything about its role.","questions":["Nine of them cannot be built by any human cell, so this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to come from food and is called essential for that reason alone.","Every living thing builds proteins from the left-handed mirror form only, and why an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> should have that handedness at all is still an unsettled question."],"answer":"amino acid","answerText":"amino acid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"An <b>amino acid</b> is a carbon atom bonded to a carboxyl group, an amino group, a hydrogen atom and a distinctive side chain, and the chemistry of that side chain decides what part the molecule plays in a protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The twenty used in proteins fall into four groups by side chain: ten nonpolar, five polar but uncharged, three basic and two acidic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nonpolar side chains do not interact with water and tend to end up in the interior of a folded protein, while the polar ones form hydrogen bonds with water and sit on the outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cysteine is worth singling out, because the sulfhydryl group on its side chain lets disulfide bonds form between two cysteines and hold a structure together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine of the twenty cannot be made by human or other mammalian cells and must come from food: histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan and valine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A further group counts as semi-essential, made by the body ordinarily but needed from the diet during growth, pregnancy and other periods of stress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The twenty are not a hard limit either, and bacteria have been engineered to make non-canonical amino acids and read them into proteins through an expanded genetic code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is universal is the handedness: every organism builds its proteins from the left-handed mirror form alone, and how that single-handedness arose is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After an organism dies the handedness slowly decays towards a mixture, so the ratio of right-handed to left-handed forms measures how far the protein has broken down and, where the rate is predictable, how long ago that was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dating the Schöningen hunting spears that way moved them from roughly 300,000 years old to about 200,000, and with them the record of when hunting became a communal business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Michael J. Lopez and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Essential Amino Acids,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2024), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557845/. [Open access]","Jingxuan Zhang, Keying Yu, Yali Xu, Wushuang Zhao, Yulian Li, Ying Wang, Florian P. Seebeck, et al., “A Robust Platform Streamlining Aromatic Noncanonical Amino Acid Biosynthesis and Genetic Code Expansion in <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 8605, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63679-6. [Open access]","S. Furkan Ozturk and Dimitar D. Sasselov, “Life's Homochirality: Across a Prebiotic Network,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 34 (2025): e2505126122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2505126122. [Open access]","Jarod M. Hutson, Felix Bittmann, Peter Fischer, Alejandro García-Moreno, Sabine Gaudzinski-Windheuser, Ellie Nelson, José E. Ortiz, et al., “Revised Age for Schöningen Hunting Spears Indicates Intensification of Neanderthal Cooperative Behavior around 200,000 Years Ago,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 19 (2025): eadv0752, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adv0752. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a side chain decide where a residue ends up in a fold?","a":"Because nonpolar side chains do not interact with water and tend to end up in the interior, while polar ones form hydrogen bonds with water and sit on the outside."},{"q":"Why must nine amino acids come from food?","a":"Because human and other mammalian cells cannot make them — histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan and valine."},{"q":"Why can handedness be used to date a fossil protein?","a":"Because after death the single left-handed form slowly decays towards a mixture, so the ratio measures how far the protein has broken down and, where the rate is predictable, how long ago."}]},
{"id":"bio-063","num":63,"category":"Biology","question":"Joining the carboxyl end of one residue to the nitrogen end of the next, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is planar and cannot rotate, which limits the shapes a chain can fold into.","questions":["The ribosome makes each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at a centre built of RNA rather than protein, so the central reaction of protein synthesis is catalysed by a ribozyme.","Chains of amino acids must have existed before ribosomes did, so how the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made without one is still an open question about life's origin."],"answer":"peptide bond","answerText":"peptide bond","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"A <b>peptide bond</b> joins the amino group of one amino acid to the carboxyl group of another, and a chain of them is a polypeptide, usually hundreds or thousands of residues long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a chain has two unlike ends, an amino or N terminus and a carboxyl or C terminus, and it is both built and written from the N end to the C end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bond itself is planar and permits no rotation, while the other two backbone bonds each residue contributes do rotate, so the chain behaves as a run of rigid plates hinged between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That constraint, together with the requirement that no two atoms overlap, severely restricts the conformations a chain can take, although a long one can still fold in an enormous number of ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a protein is, then, is a polypeptide with one particular sequence, a point settled in 1953 when Frederick Sanger worked out the first complete one, for insulin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In a living cell the bond is made by the ribosome, at the peptidyl transferase centre in its large subunit, which also carries out the hydrolysis that releases a finished chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That centre is built from ribosomal RNA rather than protein, so the reaction at the heart of protein synthesis is catalysed by a ribozyme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ribosome is not the only route, and a class of enzymes called peptide aminoacyl-tRNA ligases extends a peptide at its C terminus without any template, adding amino acids the genetic code does not encode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bond will also form with no enzyme at all, and simulations of an air-water interface have it assembling rapidly there by an isomerisation and elimination route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That last point matters because chains of amino acids had to exist before ribosomes did, and how the bond was first made in a prebiotic world remains unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Letian Bao and Anthony C. Forster, “Fast Peptide Bond Formation and Release by the Ribosomal Large Subunit,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 301, no. 7 (2025): 110336, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2025.110336. [Open access]","Amy M. Weeks, “PEARLs of Wisdom for Ribosome-Independent Peptide Bond Synthesis,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 17 (2025): e2504930122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2504930122. [Open access]","Deming Xia, Fanqi Zeng, Wanting Chen, Hui Zhao, Hong-bin Xie, Jingwen Chen, and Joseph S. Francisco, “Accelerated Peptide Bond Formation at Air-Water Interfaces,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 12 (2025): e2501323122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2501323122. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a chain behave as rigid plates on hinges?","a":"Because the peptide bond itself is planar and permits no rotation, while the other two backbone bonds each residue contributes do rotate."},{"q":"Why does that constraint matter for folding?","a":"Because it, together with the requirement that no two atoms overlap, severely restricts the conformations a chain can take, though a long one can still fold in an enormous number of ways."},{"q":"Why is the reaction at the heart of protein synthesis unusual?","a":"Because the peptidyl transferase centre is built from ribosomal RNA rather than protein, so the bond is made by a ribozyme."}]},
{"id":"bio-064","num":64,"category":"Biology","question":"The order of the amino acids in a chain, written from the amino end to the carboxyl end, is a protein's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the gene's nucleotide order fixes it.","questions":["Swapping one glutamate for a valine at the sixth position of the beta-globin chain is a change to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that produces haemoglobin S and, inherited twice, sickle cell anaemia.","Deep mutational scanning measures thousands of variants of one protein at once, which is a way of asking which changes to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually matter."],"answer":"primary structure","answerText":"primary structure","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","genetics"],"abstract":"The <b>primary structure</b> of a protein is the order of the amino acids along its chain, and that order is the defining characteristic of a protein: not a class of similar molecules but one particular sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is written from the amino terminus to the carboxyl terminus, which is the order the chain is built in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor is it the protein's own choice, since the sequence is determined by the order of nucleotides in the gene that encodes it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A single position in that order can matter enormously, and in the sickle cell mutation a negatively charged glutamate is replaced by a neutral valine at the sixth position of the beta-globin chain, a substitution Vernon Ingram identified in 1956 by a fingerprinting technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The result is haemoglobin S, and inheriting the variant from both parents gives sickle cell anaemia, the commonest and most severe form of the disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It does not follow that every substitution behaves that way, and the globin genes, HBB above all, carry a wide spread of variants whose relations to the clinical picture are complicated enough to make diagnosis genuinely difficult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which changes matter is now measured rather than argued about, since deep mutational scanning assays many thousands of variants of a single protein in one pooled experiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those results are used to classify clinical variants and to test how well the predictive models actually do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading a sequence has changed as much, and single-molecule instruments now probe individual peptides in real time, at single amino acid resolution, rather than working on bulk samples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They do not replace mass spectrometry, because neither approach on its own achieves full coverage of a sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ankit Mangla, Nikki Agarwal, and Smita Maruvada, “Sickle Cell Anemia,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482164/. [Open access]","Emelie Foord, Darius Sairafi, Monika Berg, Sofia Westerling, Toheeb Adigun, Mehmet Uzunel, Thessalia Papasavva, and Michael Uhlin, “Decoding Thalassemia and Sickle Cell Disease: Advances in Molecular Technologies for Comprehensive Variant Detection,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 17 (2026): 1810737, https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2026.1810737. [Open access]","Hasan Çubuk, Xinyi Jin, Belinda Phipson, Joseph A. Marsh, and Alan F. Rubin, “Variant Scoring Tools for Deep Mutational Scanning,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 21, no. 10 (2025): 1293–305, https://doi.org/10.1038/s44320-025-00137-x. [Open access]","Tomasz A. Leski, Sean M. Brown, Zachary T. Johnson, Scott N. Dean, Ellen R. Goldman, and David A. Stenger, “Optimization of the Quantum-Si Platinum Single-Molecule Protein Sequencing Platform toward Improved Complex-Matrix Protein Identification,” <i>ACS Omega</i> (2026), https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.6c01140. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the sequence not the protein’s own choice?","a":"Because it is determined by the order of nucleotides in the gene that encodes it, so the defining characteristic of a protein is set before the chain exists."},{"q":"Why can one position out of hundreds matter so much?","a":"Because in the sickle cell mutation a negatively charged glutamate is replaced by a neutral valine at the sixth position of the beta-globin chain, giving haemoglobin S."},{"q":"Why is which changes matter now measured rather than argued about?","a":"Because deep mutational scanning assays many thousands of variants of one protein in a single pooled experiment, and those results are used to classify clinical variants and test the predictive models."}]},
{"id":"bio-065","num":65,"category":"Biology","question":"Because the alpha helix and beta sheet are held by backbone hydrogen bonds rather than side chains, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be formed by many unrelated amino acid sequences.","questions":["Nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain long segments that never settle into any <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, and those segments do real work.","In amyloid the sheets stack and pack against one another through their side chains, so a change of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what turns a soluble protein into a fibril."],"answer":"secondary structure","answerText":"secondary structure","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"The <b>secondary structure</b> of a protein is the regular arrangement its chain takes up over short stretches, and two forms are common: the alpha helix and the beta sheet, first proposed by Linus Pauling and Robert Corey in 1951.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are held together by hydrogen bonds between the CO and NH groups of peptide bonds, in a helix between groups four residues apart, and in a sheet between two stretches of chain lying side by side, either parallel or running in opposite directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decisive point is that those bonds involve the backbone and not the side chains, so the same pattern can be formed by many unrelated sequences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A helix makes a complete turn every 3.6 amino acids, and both patterns were first found in ordinary material, the helix in the keratin of hair and the sheet in the fibroin of silk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Helices also wrap around one another into coiled-coils, which frame elongated proteins such as keratin and myosin, and a membrane protein usually crosses the lipid bilayer as a helix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Not every part of a protein has any regular structure, and nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain long disordered segments that exist as shifting ensembles rather than fixed shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those regions are not failures of folding, since they carry out signalling, transcriptional regulation, chromatin organisation and the assembly of organelles that have no membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Regular structure can also be built in the wrong way, and in an amyloid fibril beta sheets pack against one another through the side chains projecting from them, an arrangement called cross-beta packing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A single protein can take up several different amyloid folds, and those adopted by prion protein, tau, alpha-synuclein and TDP-43 differ from one neurodegenerative disease to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Disordered regions remain among the hardest cases for structure prediction, whose methods principally return a single static conformation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Sami N. Al Harake, Said Btadini, Abrar H. Qadri, Frederic Harb, and Iqball Faheem, “Beyond the Structure-Function Paradigm: A Comprehensive Review of Intrinsically Disordered Proteins,” <i>Biochemistry and Biophysics Reports</i> 47 (2026): 102706, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbrep.2026.102706. [Open access]","Sjors H. W. Scheres, “The Amyloid Packing Difference: A Pairwise Comparison Metric for Amyloid Structures,” <i>Structure</i> 34, no. 7 (2026): 1061–71, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.str.2026.04.014. [Open access]","Mandarina Qing Cheng Li, Sihan Wang, Shi-Ruei Lin, Li Eric Ngok Ting, Zhi-Hong Wan, Guodong Xie, and Jane Zhang, “Advantages and Limitations of AlphaFold in Structural Biology: Insights from Recent Studies,” <i>The Protein Journal</i> 45, no. 1 (2026): 22–38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10930-025-10310-8. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can unrelated sequences form the same regular structure?","a":"Because the hydrogen bonds holding a helix or a sheet involve the CO and NH groups of the backbone rather than the side chains, and every sequence carries the same backbone."},{"q":"Why are disordered regions not failures of folding?","a":"Because nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain them, and they carry out signalling, transcriptional regulation, chromatin organisation and the assembly of membraneless organelles."},{"q":"Why can regular structure be built the wrong way?","a":"Because in an amyloid fibril beta sheets pack against one another through their side chains, and the folds adopted by tau, alpha-synuclein and TDP-43 differ from one neurodegenerative disease to another."}]},
{"id":"bio-066","num":66,"category":"Biology","question":"Hydrophobic side chains gathering inside and hydrophilic ones facing the water outside is what drives a chain into its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the shape it actually works in.","questions":["Individually the bonds holding it are thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent one, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is stable only because so many of them act together.","Deep-learning methods can now generate sequences meant to fold into a chosen <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, including shapes that no organism has ever used."],"answer":"tertiary structure","answerText":"tertiary structure","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"The <b>tertiary structure</b> of a protein is the folding of one polypeptide chain into its three-dimensional shape, produced by interactions between side chains that lie in quite different regions of the sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decisive one is the sorting of hydrophobic side chains into the interior and hydrophilic ones onto the surface, where they meet water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Helices and sheets can sit in that hydrophobic core because backbone hydrogen bonding has already neutralised the polar character of their CO and NH groups, while the loops joining them lie on the outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hydrogen bonds and ionic bonds between polar side chains at the surface matter as well, and covalent disulfide bonds between cysteines stabilise many secreted and cell-surface proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taken singly the noncovalent bonds are thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so a fold holds only because a great many of them act in parallel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Helices and sheets joined by loops fold into compact globular units called domains, the basic units of tertiary structure; a small protein such as myoglobin has one, and a large protein several, often with distinct functions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because a domain is a unit it can be reused, and gene duplication has produced families whose members share sequence and fold alike, among them the serine proteases: chymotrypsin, trypsin, elastase and several enzymes of blood clotting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Comparing genomes shows the same process at scale, and a survey of 446 plant genomes found land plants carrying more domains and more domain combinations than algae, with shuffling generating the new combinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A fold is not fixed once made, and non-reducing proteomics finds oxidative stress remodelling disulfide bonding across more than a thousand proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Folds no organism has used can now be designed as well, since deep learning has shifted protein design from optimising energy functions to generating sequence and structure together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Jia-Yu Xue, Cheng-Ao Yang, Shuaiya Hu, Hai-Yun Fan, Yan-Mei Zhang, Zhu-Qing Shao, Linzhou Li, et al., “Evolution of Protein Domains and Protein Domain Combinations Provides Insights into the Origin and Diversification of Land Plants,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 6, no. 4 (2026): 101107, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xgen.2025.101107. [Open access]","Yeonjoo Lee, Tae-Kyung Kim, Seungjin Na, Kong-Joo Lee, Jaeho Jeong, and Eun Joo Song, “Non-Reducing Proteomics Reveals Disulfide-Dependent Proteoform Remodeling under Oxidative Stress,” <i>Molecular and Cellular Proteomics</i> 25, no. 4 (2026): 101548, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2026.101548. [Open access]","Shaotong Luo and Bo Zhou, “Generative Protein Design: From Deep Learning Algorithms to Translational Applications,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 3917, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27093917. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a helix sit inside a hydrophobic core at all?","a":"Because backbone hydrogen bonding has already neutralised the polar character of its CO and NH groups, while the loops joining helices and sheets lie on the outside."},{"q":"Why does a fold need so many weak bonds?","a":"Because taken singly the noncovalent bonds are thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so a shape holds only because a great many of them act in parallel."},{"q":"Why does a domain being a unit matter evolutionarily?","a":"Because it can be reused: gene duplication has produced families sharing sequence and fold, such as the serine proteases, and domain shuffling generates new combinations across genomes."}]},
{"id":"bio-067","num":67,"category":"Biology","question":"When a folded chain's surface binds the surface of another folded chain, the two become subunits of one molecule with a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a defined geometry.","questions":["Structure prediction stalls until you know how many copies of each subunit are present, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs that count established first.","Ferritin shells and virus-like particles are being used as vaccine scaffolds because their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> displays many copies of an antigen at once."],"answer":"quaternary structure","answerText":"quaternary structure","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"The <b>quaternary structure</b> of a protein is the set of interactions between the separate polypeptide chains of a molecule built from more than one, and they are the same interactions that hold a single chain in its fold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each chain in such a molecule is called a subunit, and the assembly happens because a binding site on one folded chain recognises the surface of another, which gives the pair a precisely defined geometry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where two identical chains bind head to head through two identical sites the result is a symmetric dimer, as in the Cro repressor that switches off genes in a bacterium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Repeating one binding interaction can close a ring instead, and the enzyme neuraminidase is four identical chains joined head to tail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many assemblies mix chain types, haemoglobin being two identical alpha-globin subunits and two identical beta-globin subunits arranged symmetrically, and such multisubunit proteins are both common and often very large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Knowing that a complex exists is not the same as knowing its composition, and predicting a structure requires the stoichiometry first, meaning how many copies of each subunit it contains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That information is often simply unavailable, and few computational methods address it, so recent work generates plausible subunit combinations, models each and ranks them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Predicting the interface between two chains is not settled either, and current models have been assessed against experimental structures rather than assumed to replace them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Assembly can also be put to work, since ferritin shells, encapsulins and virus-like particles display many copies of an antigen at once and are being developed as vaccine scaffolds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Designed particles go further, allowing the geometry itself to be optimised for how an antigen is shown to the immune system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Jian Liu, Pawan Neupane, and Jianlin Cheng, “PreStoi Allows Accurate Prediction of Protein Complex Stoichiometry by Integrating AlphaFold3 and Template Information,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 9, no. 1 (2026): 891, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-026-10126-x. [Open access]","Xiao Liu, Kaiwen Pang, Hangfei Wu, Bin Chong, Zhixiang Yin, Zhaoxi Sun, and Xiaohui Wang, “AlphaFold3 Prediction of Protein-Protein Complex: Is It Ready for Thermodynamic Analysis?,” <i>Computational and Structural Biotechnology Journal</i> 27 (2025): 4373–83, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csbj.2025.10.015. [Open access]","Yue Zhang, Yi Ru, Xiuping Li, Guanghua Wang, Yong Hu, Yingna Jian, and Liqing Ma, “Application and Research Progress of Self-Assembling Protein Nanoparticles in Vaccine Development,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 10 (2026): 4503, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27104503. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do separate chains assemble into a precise shape?","a":"Because a binding site on one folded chain recognises the surface of another, which gives the pair a precisely defined geometry rather than an arbitrary contact."},{"q":"Why is knowing a complex exists not enough to model it?","a":"Because predicting a structure requires the stoichiometry first — how many copies of each subunit it contains — and that information is often simply unavailable."},{"q":"Why are such assemblies useful as vaccine scaffolds?","a":"Because ferritin shells, encapsulins and virus-like particles display many copies of an antigen at once, and designed particles let the geometry itself be optimised."}]},
{"id":"bio-068","num":68,"category":"Biology","question":"Denatured by heat and then returned to mild conditions, many proteins go back to their native shape unaided, which showed that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs no information but the sequence.","questions":["A 1969 paradox pointed out that a chain cannot search its conformations at random, and the funnel-shaped energy landscape is the answer to how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happens so fast.","Chaperones stop a new chain's exposed hydrophobic patches from sticking together in the crowded cytoplasm, making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> more reliable without changing where it ends up."],"answer":"protein folding","answerText":"protein folding","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Protein folding</b> is the process by which a newly made polypeptide reaches the single compact conformation it works in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That the conformation is set by the sequence was shown by Christian Anfinsen, who disrupted proteins by treatments such as heating, which break noncovalent bonds, and found that under milder conditions they often returned to their native shape unaided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How a chain finds that shape is a separate question, and Levinthal's paradox of 1969 pointed out that sampling conformations at random is implausible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Energy landscape theory answered it in the 1990s by treating folding as a funnel-guided process, with the native state sitting at an energy minimum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A native-like nucleus forms first, which holds down the entropic cost of organising the chain, and the funnel then guides the rest into place rapidly and cooperatively in spite of kinetic traps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside a cell the process is usually assisted, by proteins called molecular chaperones that bind partly folded chains and keep them on the most energetically favourable pathway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They earn their place because the cytoplasm is crowded: they stop the hydrophobic regions temporarily exposed on a new chain from associating with one another into aggregates, and they change nothing about the destination, only make reaching it more reliable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Folding also begins well before a chain is finished, since a cytosolic protein starts to fold as soon as it emerges from the ribosome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those first steps are hard to observe, and measuring them in live cells shows that a domain's topology shapes the pathway it takes and that different chaperones engage the growing chain at different points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A textbook of 2000 could still state that predicting a structure directly from its sequence was impossible, and deep learning has since produced millions of structure models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Omer Faruk Kuzu, Lars Jørgen Tvenge Granerud, and Fahri Saatcioglu, “Navigating the Landscape of Protein Folding and Proteostasis: From Molecular Chaperones to Therapeutic Innovations,” <i>Signal Transduction and Targeted Therapy</i> 10, no. 1 (2025): 358, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41392-025-02439-w. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Xiuqi Chen, Vincent J. Hilser, and Christian M. Kaiser, “Arrest Peptide Profiling Resolves Co-translational Folding Pathways and Chaperone Interactions in Vivo,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 6833, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-61398-6. [Open access]","Martin Luke Rennie and Michael R. Oliver, “Emerging Frontiers in Protein Structure Prediction Following the AlphaFold Revolution,” <i>Journal of the Royal Society Interface</i> 22, no. 225 (2025): 20240886, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2024.0886. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the working shape said to be set by the sequence?","a":"Because Anfinsen disrupted proteins by treatments such as heating that break noncovalent bonds, and under milder conditions they often returned to their native shape unaided."},{"q":"Why can a chain not simply search for its shape at random?","a":"Because Levinthal’s paradox showed that sampling conformations at random is implausible, which energy landscape theory answered by treating folding as a funnel-guided process."},{"q":"Why does a crowded cytoplasm need chaperones?","a":"Because they stop the hydrophobic regions temporarily exposed on a new chain from associating into aggregates; they change nothing about the destination, only make reaching it reliable."}]},
{"id":"bio-069","num":69,"category":"Biology","question":"Break the noncovalent bonds holding a folded chain together and you have <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which leaves the chain whole but takes away the shape it works in.","questions":["Heat, molar urea and hydrostatic pressure all produce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but put on one thermodynamic footing they turn out not to do it the same way.","A drug bound to its target shifts that protein's melting temperature, so heating a whole proteome and watching for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reveals what the drug binds."],"answer":"denaturation","answerText":"denaturation","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Denaturation</b> is the disruption of a protein's three-dimensional structure by treatments that break the noncovalent bonds holding it together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is left is a flexible polypeptide chain that has lost its natural shape while remaining one continuous chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Take the denaturing agent away and the protein often refolds, or renatures, spontaneously into its original conformation, which is how the shape was shown to be specified by the sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several quite different treatments will do it, among them heating, hydrostatic pressure, and molar concentrations of a chaotrope such as urea, which disrupts noncovalent interactions directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are not interchangeable, and when the three are put on a single thermodynamic footing heat-induced unfolding turns out to be the most cooperative of the three, a result obtained on apolipoprotein A-1 and lysozyme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Unfolding need not be a single step from folded to not, and mass spectrometry, which captures the states actually populated rather than an average over them, finds intermediates appearing as the urea concentration rises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Optical methods report an ensemble average and can struggle to resolve those intermediates at all, so how simple the process looks depends partly on the instrument watching it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sensitivity of a fold to its conditions has been turned into an instrument in its own right, since a small molecule bound to a protein shifts that protein's melting temperature, stabilising or destabilising it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heating cells across a range of temperatures and measuring which proteins are still soluble therefore identifies what a drug binds, unbiased and across a whole proteome at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both of the classic accounts are careful to say that a denatured protein often refolds rather than that it always does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Joachim Seelig and Anna Seelig, “Pressure Protein Denaturation Compared to Thermal and Chemical Unfolding: Analyses with Cooperative Models,” <i>The Journal of Physical Chemistry B</i> 129, no. 4 (2025): 1229–36, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jpcb.4c07703. [Open access]","Nicklas Österlund, Jacob S. Jordan, Eleonora Renzi, Gergo Peter Szekeres, and Kevin Pagel, “Mass Spectrometry Detects Folding Intermediates Populated during Urea-Induced Protein Denaturation,” <i>Chemical Science</i> 16, no. 47 (2025): 22407–16, https://doi.org/10.1039/d5sc05773f. [Open access]","Camilla Johansson, Martin Johansson, Phanindra Babu Kasi, Mårten Larsson, Per-Johan Jakobsson, Ulf Göransson, Jordi Carreras Puigvert, et al., “Cell Painting and Thermal Proteome Profiling for Inference of Drug Targets and Mechanism of Action,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 22, no. 8 (2026): 1270–91, https://doi.org/10.1038/s44320-026-00214-9. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is denaturation the experiment that proved sequence sets shape?","a":"Because taking the denaturing agent away often lets the flexible chain refold spontaneously into its original conformation, with nothing but the sequence left to specify it."},{"q":"Why are the different denaturing treatments not interchangeable?","a":"Because put on a single thermodynamic footing they differ, heat-induced unfolding proving the most cooperative of the three on apolipoprotein A-1 and lysozyme."},{"q":"Why does how simple unfolding looks depend on the instrument?","a":"Because mass spectrometry captures the states actually populated and finds intermediates as urea rises, where optical methods report an ensemble average and can struggle to resolve them."}]},
{"id":"bio-070","num":70,"category":"Biology","question":"Built by joining the 5' phosphate of one nucleotide to the 3' hydroxyl of the next, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a chain with a direction as well as a sequence.","questions":["Four drugs approved for Duchenne muscular dystrophy work by making the splicing machinery skip an exon, which is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> used as a medicine rather than as information.","At up to 455 exabytes per gram and stable for millennia, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is being developed as a storage medium for ordinary computer files."],"answer":"nucleic acid","answerText":"nucleic acid","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","genetics"],"abstract":"The <b>nucleic acids</b>, DNA and RNA, are the principal informational molecules of the cell, deoxyribonucleic acid having the unique role of genetic material and the several kinds of ribonucleic acid taking part in a range of cellular activities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> RNA is not only informational, since in present-day cells it also catalyses reactions, among them steps of protein synthesis and of RNA processing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are polymers of nucleotides, each a purine or pyrimidine base linked to a phosphorylated sugar, ribose in RNA and deoxyribose in DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain is built by phosphodiester bonds between the 5' phosphate of one nucleotide and the 3' hydroxyl of another, so it has a direction as well as a sequence, and it is always synthesised and written from the 5' end towards the 3'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A short chain is an oligonucleotide; the polynucleotides making up cellular RNA and DNA run to thousands and to millions of units respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Short chains made deliberately are now medicines, and antisense oligonucleotides work by binding a target transcript and redirecting how it is spliced or how much of it survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four such drugs are approved in the United States for Duchenne muscular dystrophy, eteplirsen, golodirsen, viltolarsen and casimersen, each making the splicing machinery skip an exon so that some dystrophin is made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small interfering RNAs work differently again, intercepting genetic information in transit rather than altering the genome, which has taken them from a niche technique to the routine management of chronic conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The molecule is also being taken out of biology altogether, since DNA offers an information density of up to 455 exabytes per gram and stability over thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What has held that back is not writing the data but reading a chosen part of it back, and electric fields are one way now being used to address stored strands individually.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Liping Xu, Huaqun Zhang, Bingchen Jiang, Yuanying Jiang, and Hui Lu, “Antisense Oligonucleotides: Technological Advances, Clinical Progress, and Expanding Therapeutic Frontiers,” <i>Pharmaceutics</i> 18, no. 4 (2026): 446, https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18040446. [Open access]","Hidenori Moriyama, Shiori Moriyama, and Toshifumi Yokota, “FDA-Approved Antisense Oligonucleotide Therapies for Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy: Current Status and Future Outlook,” <i>RNA Biology</i> 23, no. 1 (2026): 1–17, https://doi.org/10.1080/15476286.2026.2689117. [Open access]","Quoc-Viet Le and Gayong Shim, “Evolution of siRNA Therapeutics: From Mechanistic Foundations to Clinical Expansion,” <i>Pharmaceutics</i> 18, no. 5 (2026): 593, https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18050593. [Open access]","Doyeon Lim, Taeseok Kang, Wonjin Lee, Zeeshan Haider, Seunghwan Noh, Minsang Yu, Xiaohua Huang, Hyuk Soo Eun, and Youngjun Song, “Electric Field-Guided Random-Access DNA Data Storage,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 23 (2026): eaee4328, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aee4328. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a nucleic acid chain have a direction as well as a sequence?","a":"Because phosphodiester bonds join the 5' phosphate of one nucleotide to the 3' hydroxyl of another, so the chain is always synthesised and written 5' to 3'."},{"q":"Why is RNA not simply an informational molecule?","a":"Because in present-day cells it also catalyses reactions, among them steps of protein synthesis and of RNA processing."},{"q":"Why has DNA data storage not arrived despite its density?","a":"Because what has held it back is not writing the data but reading a chosen part of it back, which is why electric fields are being used to address stored strands individually."}]},
{"id":"bio-071","num":71,"category":"Biology","question":"A base joined to a sugar alone is a nucleoside, and attaching a phosphate to that sugar's 5' carbon is what makes it a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead.","questions":["Blocking the first enzyme that builds purines from scratch kills tuberculosis bacteria, which makes the manufacture of every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a target for a new class of antibiotic.","An antiviral drug that mimics a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closely enough to be incorporated can be cut out again by a coronavirus that carries a proofreading enzyme."],"answer":"nucleotide","answerText":"nucleotide","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","genetics"],"abstract":"A <b>nucleotide</b> is a nitrogen-containing base linked to a sugar, with one or more phosphate groups attached to that sugar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bases come in two chemical families, the purines and the pyrimidines: DNA uses the purines adenine and guanine with the pyrimidines cytosine and thymine, and RNA replaces thymine with uracil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The distinction worth holding is that a base joined to a sugar alone is a nucleoside, and it becomes a nucleotide only once a phosphate is attached to the 5' carbon of that sugar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sugar itself is ribose in RNA and 2'-deoxyribose in DNA, and that one missing oxygen is what separates the two families of nucleic acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A nucleotide may carry one, two or three phosphates in a row, and joining nucleotides into a chain is only one of the things a cell does with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Cells both build purines from scratch and salvage them from breakdown products, and the first route matters enough that a small-molecule inhibitor of PurF, the first enzyme of mycobacterial de novo purine synthesis, kills tuberculosis bacteria at nanomolar concentrations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Supply matters in less obvious places too, and enterocytes lean on purine synthesis and salvage to raise the ATP that absorbing dietary fat demands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A close enough imitation can be slipped into a growing chain to stop it, which is how nucleotide analogue antivirals work, and SARS-CoV-2 resists two of them because its proofreading exoribonuclease excises the intruder again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nucleotides serve as signals as well as parts, cyclic AMP being the long-established second messenger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where it operates is still being revised, and the plant auxin receptor TIR1 turns out to make cyclic AMP itself, which is required for the transcriptional response to the hormone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Dirk A. Lamprecht, Richard J. Wall, Annelies Leemans, Barry Truebody, Joke Sprangers, Patricia Fiogbe, Cadi Davies, et al., “Targeting De Novo Purine Biosynthesis for Tuberculosis Treatment,” <i>Nature</i> 644, no. 8075 (2025): 214–20, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09177-7. [Open access]","Yu Wang, Li Chen, Yingze Ma, Mingqi Zhou, Aleksander Geske, Marcus Seldin, Jiangjiang Zhu, et al., “Enterocytes Rely on Purine Biosynthesis/Salvage Pathway to Facilitate Dietary Fat Absorption,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 3888, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70332-3. [Open access]","Chang Liu, Yu Li, Xiaocong Cao, Ryan J. Gleason, Bin Liu, and Yang Yang, “Mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 Resistance to Nucleotide Analog-Based Antivirals,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 1601, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-68304-8. [Open access]","Huihuang Chen, Linlin Qi, Minxia Zou, Mengting Lu, Mateusz Kwiatkowski, Yuanrong Pei, Krzysztof Jaworski, and Jiří Friml, “TIR1-Produced cAMP as a Second Messenger in Transcriptional Auxin Signalling,” <i>Nature</i> 640, no. 8060 (2025): 1011–16, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08669-w. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a nucleoside not yet a nucleotide?","a":"Because a base joined to a sugar alone is a nucleoside, and it becomes a nucleotide only once a phosphate is attached to the 5' carbon of that sugar."},{"q":"Why does one missing oxygen separate two families of nucleic acid?","a":"Because the sugar is ribose in RNA and 2'-deoxyribose in DNA, and that difference at the 2' position is what distinguishes the two polymers."},{"q":"Why does SARS-CoV-2 resist some nucleotide analogue drugs?","a":"Because the drug works by being slipped into a growing chain to stop it, and the virus’s proofreading exoribonuclease excises the intruder again."}]},
{"id":"bio-072","num":72,"category":"Biology","question":"Adenine on a ribose with three phosphates in a row makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the molecule a cell spends whenever it needs a reaction driven the wrong way.","questions":["A human body hydrolyses between 100 and 150 moles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a day, which is far more than it contains at any moment and shows how fast the molecule is remade.","Restoring the ion balance after one nerve impulse costs a neuron close to a billion molecules of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is where most of the brain's energy goes."],"answer":"ATP","answerText":"ATP","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>ATP</b>, adenosine triphosphate, is the principal form of chemical energy inside cells, and it is a nucleotide: an adenine base on a ribose sugar carrying three phosphate groups bonded one after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Removing the outermost phosphate gives ADP, and that hydrolysis is energetically favourable, which is what lets a cell couple it to reactions that would not otherwise run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The metaphor of a currency is apt because the molecule is spent and remade rather than stored, and a human body hydrolyses somewhere between 100 and 150 moles of it in a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bill falls hardest on tissues that pump ions, and a neuron hydrolyses close to a billion molecules to reset its sodium and potassium after a single depolarisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other nucleotides do similar work, carrying energy or reactive chemical groups through a wide range of metabolic reactions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Energy is not the whole of what ATP does, and it is co-stored and co-released with neurotransmitters, which makes it a mediator of purinergic signalling in the nervous system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Released from damaged cells it becomes a warning: in the liver, extracellular ATP acts on neighbouring and on its own cells as a signal in the progression of fibrosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A third role has nothing to do with either, since at the concentrations found inside a cell ATP alters how proteins interact with one another and how stable they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That effect is being examined on proteins which cluster into droplets, where ATP and other small amphiphilic molecules tune how those clusters form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A molecule known for a century as the cell's fuel is turning out also to be part of how its interior stays soluble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Jacob Dunn and Michael H. Grider, “Physiology, Adenosine Triphosphate,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553175/. [Open access]","Blanca Verónica Ramos-Rosillo, Esperanza Mata-Martínez, Mauricio Díaz-Muñoz, and Francisco G. Vázquez-Cuevas, “Liver Fibrosis and Purinergic Signaling: Autocrine-Paracrine Role of ATP in Liver Damage,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 13 (2026): 6030, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27136030. [Open access]","Shuyuan Tan and Robin Curtis, “Adenosine Triphosphate as a Modulator of Protein Interactions and Stability,” <i>FEBS Open Bio</i> (2026), https://doi.org/10.1002/2211-5463.70312. [Open access]","Mrityunjoy Kar, “ATP and Small Amphiphilic Molecules Act as Molecular Matchmakers to Fine-Tune FET Protein Clusters,” <i>Communications Chemistry</i> 8, no. 1 (2025): 386, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-025-01807-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is ATP called a currency rather than a store?","a":"Because the molecule is spent and remade rather than kept: a human body hydrolyses somewhere between 100 and 150 moles of it in a day."},{"q":"Why does hydrolysing it drive reactions that would not otherwise run?","a":"Because removing the outermost phosphate to give ADP is energetically favourable, and a cell couples that favourable change to the unfavourable one it needs."},{"q":"Why is the fuel account of ATP incomplete?","a":"Because it is co-released with neurotransmitters as a purinergic signal, warns of damage in liver fibrosis, and at cellular concentrations alters how proteins interact and how stable they are."}]},
{"id":"bio-073","num":73,"category":"Biology","question":"Copper forming chelates with peptide linkages under alkaline conditions turns a solution violet, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the standard way to show protein is present.","questions":["Because it reports the peptide bond rather than any particular molecule, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answers to anything carrying enough of them, which is a limit as well as a convenience.","Clinical laboratories measure serum total protein with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a refractometer is quicker and cheaper and agrees with it closely enough to substitute."],"answer":"biuret test","answerText":"biuret test","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"Cells are built from four classes of molecule, carbohydrates, lipids, nucleic acids and proteins, and the oldest way of asking which of them is in a sample is a reagent that changes colour when it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <b>biuret test</b> is the one for protein: at alkaline pH copper forms chelates with the peptide linkages of a protein, and the solution turns violet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it reports is therefore the peptide bond rather than any particular protein, so anything carrying enough of them will answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is no classroom relic, and the biuret method is used routinely in reference and in-clinic laboratories to measure serum total protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor is it irreplaceable, and a refractometer, quicker and cheaper, correlates strongly enough with it to be used instead in cattle and goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Sugars have their own colour reactions, and reducing sugars have been determined by the Somogyi-Nelson, phenol-sulfuric, anthrone, Fehling and dinitrosalicylic acid methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of the older ones are laborious, Fehling's involving precipitation and titration, and newer routes are designed to report not only that the sugar is there but how much of it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is the direction of travel: a colour change answers whether, and a calibrated assay answers how much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The descendant of these tests that a reader is most likely to meet is the urine dipstick, whose reagent pads report protein, glucose, blood and several other things by colour, each read against a printed chart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every pad has its documented false positives and negatives, the protein pad reading positive in alkaline or concentrated urine and negative when the protein present is not albumin, and reference ranges themselves shift with the substrates and reagents an assay uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Sileshi Mesele Mesa and Yoseph Cherinet Megerssa, “Comparison of Biuret and Refractometery Method for Serum Total Protein Measurements in Cattle and Goat,” <i>BMC Research Notes</i> 17, no. 1 (2024): 234, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-024-06906-5. [Open access]","Reda M. El-Shishtawy, Yasser M. Al Angari, Maha M. Alotaibi, and Yaaser Q. Almulaiky, “Novel and Facile Colorimetric Detection of Reducing Sugars in Foods via In Situ Formed Gelatin-Capped Silver Nanoparticles,” <i>Polymers</i> 15, no. 5 (2023): 1086, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym15051086. [Open access]","Daniel A. Queremel Milani and Ishwarlal Jialal, “Urinalysis,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557685/. [Open access]","Muhammad Zubair and Thiruvengadam Muniraj, “Analytical and Clinical Perspectives on Amylase,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2026), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557738/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why will the biuret test answer for almost any protein?","a":"Because what it reports is the peptide linkage itself, copper forming chelates with those bonds at alkaline pH, so anything carrying enough of them turns the solution violet."},{"q":"Why is a colour change not the same as a measurement?","a":"Because a colour reaction answers whether a substance is there while a calibrated assay answers how much, which is why newer routes for sugars were designed to quantify rather than detect."},{"q":"Why must a dipstick result be read with its known failures in mind?","a":"Because every pad has documented false positives and negatives — the protein pad reads positive in alkaline or concentrated urine and negative when the protein present is not albumin."}]},
{"id":"bio-074","num":74,"category":"Biology","question":"Shape follows from how a molecule is built, and what it can do follows from its shape: that chain of reasoning is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> molecular biology runs on.","questions":["Nearly half of human protein-coding genes carry long segments with no stable fold, which is the standing exception to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a failure of folding.","One protein taking several different amyloid folds, each tied to a different disease, shows that one sequence does not always fix one shape, which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> quietly assumes."],"answer":"structure-function relationship","answerText":"structure-function relationship","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"The <b>structure-function relationship</b> is the organising assumption of molecular biology, so settled that the papers questioning it are titled as going beyond a paradigm: what a molecule does follows from its shape, and its shape follows from how it is built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A protein is the standard demonstration, since its conformation is determined by its amino acid sequence, with hydrophobic side chains sorting into the interior and hydrophilic ones onto the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chain has preferred shapes at all only because the peptide bond is planar and permits no rotation, while the other backbone bonds do, which sharply restricts what conformations are available.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bonds that then hold a fold are individually thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so a structure is stable without being rigid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the reasoning bites hardest is at a single position: replacing a glutamate with a valine at the sixth place in the beta-globin chain gives haemoglobin S.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The relationship is not universal, and nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain long disordered segments that never settle into a stable three-dimensional fold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those regions are not failures of folding but work as shifting ensembles, carrying out signalling, transcriptional regulation, chromatin organisation and the assembly of organelles without membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other half of the assumption fails too, since a single protein can take up several different amyloid folds, and the ones adopted by prion protein, tau, alpha-synuclein and TDP-43 differ between neurodegenerative diseases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So one sequence does not always specify one shape, and the flexibility comes from the packing interactions themselves rather than from any change in the chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is a rule of thumb of enormous power rather than a law, and knowing where it does not hold is part of knowing how to use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sami N. Al Harake, Said Btadini, Abrar H. Qadri, Frederic Harb, and Iqball Faheem, “Beyond the Structure-Function Paradigm: A Comprehensive Review of Intrinsically Disordered Proteins,” <i>Biochemistry and Biophysics Reports</i> 47 (2026): 102706, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbrep.2026.102706. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Ankit Mangla, Nikki Agarwal, and Smita Maruvada, “Sickle Cell Anemia,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482164/. [Open access]","Sjors H. W. Scheres, “The Amyloid Packing Difference: A Pairwise Comparison Metric for Amyloid Structures,” <i>Structure</i> 34, no. 7 (2026): 1061–71, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.str.2026.04.014. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a chain have preferred shapes at all?","a":"Because the peptide bond is planar and permits no rotation while the other backbone bonds do, which sharply restricts what conformations are available to it."},{"q":"Why is a fold stable without being rigid?","a":"Because the bonds holding it are individually thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so the structure holds in numbers rather than by strength."},{"q":"Why is the relationship a rule of thumb rather than a law?","a":"Because nearly half of human protein-coding genes carry long disordered segments, and one protein can take several amyloid folds that differ between neurodegenerative diseases."}]},
{"id":"bio-075","num":75,"category":"Biology","question":"Passing a mixture through a column of porous beads so its components are held back by different amounts is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and what the beads are made of decides what it sorts by.","questions":["In the gel filtration form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the small molecules linger inside the beads while the large ones flow past, so the biggest leave the column first.","Measuring a mixture measures an average, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now the front half of nearly every method that reports on individual molecules."],"answer":"chromatography","answerText":"chromatography","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"tags":["practice","science","biology","chemistry"],"abstract":"<b>Chromatography</b> separates a mixture by passing it through a column packed with a porous solid matrix, in which the different components are retarded to different extents and can be collected separately as they flow out of the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the matrix is made of decides what the column sorts by: charge in ion-exchange chromatography, exposed hydrophobic regions in hydrophobic chromatography, size in gel filtration, and the ability to bind a particular molecule in affinity chromatography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gel filtration is the one worth thinking through, since its beads are porous and small molecules linger inside them while larger ones stay in the flowing solvent between, so the biggest leave the column first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charge can also be used without a column at all, and in electrophoresis an electric field moves a protein at a rate set by its net charge, its size and its shape, which SDS polyacrylamide-gel electrophoresis turned into routine analysis in the mid-1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before any of that, whole organelles are sorted by differential centrifugation, a method developed by Albert Claude, Christian de Duve and their colleagues in the 1940s and 1950s that separates components by size and density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Separation is now almost always the front half of a measurement rather than an end in itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lipidomics is the clear case, resting on advances in mass spectrometry, chromatography and computational analysis together, which is what has allowed lipid networks to be mapped at high resolution across molecular, cellular and organismal scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A working study now combines several such methods at once, one recent example pairing liquid chromatography and mass spectrometry for metabolites with non-reducing tandem mass tag proteomics in the same cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that buys is not only sensitivity, since electrospray mass spectrometry captures the states actually present in a sample rather than an average over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Optical methods that report an ensemble average can struggle to resolve those states at all, which is the whole argument for separating a mixture before asking anything of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Fractionation of Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26936/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Maria Fedorova, Anne K. Bendt, Justine Bertrand-Michel, Oliver Fiehn, Joanna Godzien, Laura Goracci, Florian Gruber, et al., “A Lipidomics Roadmap: From Basic Research to Societal Challenges,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 4778, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-73797-4. [Open access]","Yeonjoo Lee, Tae-Kyung Kim, Seungjin Na, Kong-Joo Lee, Jaeho Jeong, and Eun Joo Song, “Non-Reducing Proteomics Reveals Disulfide-Dependent Proteoform Remodeling under Oxidative Stress,” <i>Molecular and Cellular Proteomics</i> 25, no. 4 (2026): 101548, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2026.101548. [Open access]","Nicklas Österlund, Jacob S. Jordan, Eleonora Renzi, Gergo Peter Szekeres, and Kevin Pagel, “Mass Spectrometry Detects Folding Intermediates Populated during Urea-Induced Protein Denaturation,” <i>Chemical Science</i> 16, no. 47 (2025): 22407–16, https://doi.org/10.1039/d5sc05773f. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the largest molecules leave a gel filtration column first?","a":"Because the beads are porous, so small molecules linger inside them while larger ones stay in the flowing solvent between and are carried straight through."},{"q":"Why does the packing material decide what a column sorts by?","a":"Because the matrix is what retards the components: charge in ion exchange, exposed hydrophobic regions in hydrophobic chromatography, size in gel filtration, binding in affinity."},{"q":"Why separate a mixture before asking anything of it?","a":"Because mass spectrometry captures the states actually present in a sample, where optical methods report an ensemble average and can struggle to resolve those states at all."}]},
{"id":"bio-076","num":76,"category":"Biology","question":"Because a cell must maintain order in a universe tending to disorder, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> treats a constant supply of energy as the basic condition of being alive rather than an optional extra.","questions":["Sunlight, photosynthesis, the oxidation of sugars and the turnover of ATP are all accounted for within <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the study of how energy moves through living systems.","Sulfur-oxidising bacteria that fix carbon in total darkness are the standing exception, in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, to the rule that sunlight is the ultimate source of the energy living things use."],"answer":"bioenergetics","answerText":"bioenergetics","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","energy"],"abstract":"<b>Bioenergetics</b> is the study of how energy is captured, stored, transformed and spent by living things, and is the thread tying photosynthesis, respiration and every act of cellular work together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell is never idle: it builds macromolecules, pumps ions against concentration gradients, hauls cargo along its own filaments and, in many organisms, moves the whole body about. All of that runs against the grain of physics, since cells create and maintain order in a universe tending always to greater disorder, so a constant input of energy is needed simply to stay alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Energy is never created, only converted from one form into another, and biologists keep the accounts in free energy, a quantity combining the heat content of a system with its degree of disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For virtually all metabolic energy in the living world the ultimate source is sunlight, turned into usable chemical energy by photosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Autotrophs assemble their organic molecules from inorganic starting materials, plants drawing their carbon from atmospheric carbon dioxide, their hydrogen and oxygen from water and their nitrogen from ammonia and nitrates, while heterotrophs obtain theirs by eating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those molecules hold energy in their chemical bonds, and a cell releases it by oxidising them through a long series of small enzyme-catalysed steps to carbon dioxide and water, so that free energy arrives in conveniently sized packets instead of the single destructive flare of ordinary burning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of what is captured is held briefly in adenosine triphosphate, of which an adult turns over some 100 to 150 moles a day,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while a large share of the total is dissipated as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not everything runs on the sun, and sulfur-oxidising bacteria in coastal sediments fix carbon in complete darkness on energy stripped from inorganic chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this is mere bookkeeping: crippling the mitochondrial bioenergetics of one supporting cell type in the mouse brain weakened the neurons around it and impaired memory, a deficit reversed by supplying lactate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “How Cells Obtain Energy from Food,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26882/. [Open access]","Jacob Dunn and Michael H. Grider, “Physiology, Adenosine Triphosphate,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553175/. [Open access]","Xiao-Lan Yue et al., “13C-Labeled Single-Cell Raman Sorting Reveals Sulfur-Driven Dark Carbon Fixation in Coastal Sediments,” <i>ISME Communications</i> 6, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1093/ismeco/ycag073. [Open access]","Kateryna Murlanova et al., “NPAS3-Regulated Astrocyte Mitochondrial Bioenergetics Is Required for Cognition,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 25 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adt2527. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does staying alive require a constant input of energy?","a":"Because cells create and maintain order in a universe tending always to greater disorder, and they are never idle, building molecules, pumping ions and hauling cargo."},{"q":"Why oxidise food in many small steps rather than burning it?","a":"Because a long series of enzyme-catalysed steps delivers free energy in conveniently sized packets instead of the single destructive flare of ordinary burning."},{"q":"Why is bioenergetics not merely bookkeeping?","a":"Because crippling the mitochondrial bioenergetics of one supporting cell type in the mouse brain weakened the neurons around it and impaired memory, a deficit reversed by supplying lactate."}]},
{"id":"bio-077","num":77,"category":"Biology","question":"Because a growing organism builds order while discharging heat into its surroundings, nothing alive breaks the laws of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, however much a living cell may appear to.","questions":["The first and second laws of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> together say that energy is never created and that disorder can only increase, which sets the terms every organism must live on.","An organism counts as an open system in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, exchanging both energy and matter with its surroundings, which is precisely why building order inside itself is allowed."],"answer":"thermodynamics","answerText":"thermodynamics","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","energy"],"abstract":"<b>Thermodynamics</b> is the study of energy and its transfer, and its laws bind a bacterium and a blue whale as tightly as a steam engine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It divides the world into a system and the surroundings outside it, and living organisms count as open systems, exchanging both energy and matter with the world around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first law states that the total energy of the universe is constant, so energy may be moved or changed in form but never created or destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A plant therefore makes no energy at all, and instead converts the energy of sunlight into chemical energy held in the bonds of organic molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second law states that in the universe, or in any isolated system, the degree of disorder can only increase, and the measure of that disorder is entropy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Living things grow and assemble elaborate structures and so appear to defy it, but a cell is no isolated system: it takes in energy as food or as sunlight, spends it generating order within itself, and discharges part of it as heat, disordering its surroundings by more than it orders itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No energy transfer is ever completely efficient, so some is always lost in a form that cannot be used, usually heat, which is why a mammal is warm to the touch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two laws are folded into one quantity, free energy, which sets the heat content of a system against its disorder and so predicts which reactions will run without being pushed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That waste heat can itself become an adaptation, and two honeybee species have independently evolved mitochondria whose membranes leak protons more freely, generating extra warmth and letting both spread from the tropics into temperate country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Heat production and heat loss can now be tracked in a person in real time, and the resulting estimates of entropy production and export are being explored, as a hypothesis rather than a clinical tool, as a measure of health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.3 The Laws of Thermodynamics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-3-the-laws-of-thermodynamics. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Ye Xiao et al., “Convergent Elevation of Mitochondrial Proton Leakage Improves Thermogenesis in Honeybees,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 43, no. 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msag174. [Open access]","Nicolas Brodeur, André Longtin, Glen P. Kenny, and Andrew J. E. Seely, “Real-Time Measurement of Entropy Production and Export in Humans: A Step toward Entropically Informed Medicine,” <i>Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences</i> 1550, no. 1 (2025): 283–95, https://doi.org/10.1111/nyas.15385. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a growing organism not break the second law?","a":"Because a cell is no isolated system: it takes in energy, spends it generating order within itself, and discharges part of it as heat, disordering its surroundings by more than it orders itself."},{"q":"Why is a mammal warm to the touch?","a":"Because no energy transfer is ever completely efficient, so some is always lost in a form that cannot be used, which is usually heat."},{"q":"Why can wasted heat become an adaptation?","a":"Because two honeybee species have independently evolved mitochondria whose membranes leak protons more freely, generating extra warmth and letting both spread from the tropics into temperate country."}]},
{"id":"bio-078","num":78,"category":"Biology","question":"A reaction whose <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> falls releases usable energy and needs no push, while one in which it rises will not run until something else drives it.","questions":["Subtracting the energy lost to entropy from a reaction's total energy change gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the part that is actually available to do work in a cell.","Rust forms without being pushed and still takes years, which is why a fall in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tells you a reaction can happen but says nothing whatever about how fast."],"answer":"Gibbs free energy","answerText":"Gibbs free energy","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","energy"],"abstract":"<b>Gibbs free energy</b>, written G and named for Josiah Willard Gibbs, is the part of a system's energy that remains available to do work once the price paid in entropy has been deducted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every reaction involves a change in it, written as delta G, and that change is the reaction's total energy change, its enthalpy, less the energy surrendered to disorder multiplied by the absolute temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A reaction that releases free energy has a negative value and is called exergonic, its products holding less free energy than its reactants, and it can proceed without being pushed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A reaction that must be supplied with energy has a positive value and is called endergonic, its products storing more than went into them, and it will not happen on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spontaneous here means unforced rather than immediate: iron rusts spontaneously over years, and a book does not burst into flames because its molecules must first be kicked over a barrier of activation energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The figure depends not only on the molecules involved but on their concentrations and conditions, so biochemists quote a standard change measured in water at pH 7, roughly the conditions inside a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At equilibrium the change is zero and the reaction runs no further in either direction, and the standard value fixes where that equilibrium sits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many of the reactions life depends on, the assembly of large molecules above all, are unfavourable under cellular conditions and can run only if something else drives them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sign is a hard physical constraint rather than a bookkeeping convenience, and metabolic models are now written to exclude loops of reactions that would circulate for ever with nothing going in, which no real cell can do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Landscapes of it can also be measured for a structure rather than a reaction, and one mapped for compacted chromatin separated three ways in which linker histones tighten the coil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.2 Potential, Kinetic, Free, and Activation Energy,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Nicolás Améstica-Toledo, Maximiliano Farías-Miño, Raúl Conejeros, David Tourigny, and Marcelo Rivas-Astroza, “Thermodynamically Enabled and Reaction Attuned Estimation of Metabolic Fluxes,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 6 (2026): 115822, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115822. [Open access]","Kalven Bonin et al., “Free Energy Spectroscopy Reveals the Mechanistic Landscape of Chromatin Compaction,” <i>Nucleic Acids Research</i> 54, no. 14 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkag723. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a favourable reaction not always happen quickly?","a":"Because spontaneous means unforced rather than immediate: iron rusts over years, and a book does not burst into flames because its molecules must first be kicked over a barrier of activation energy."},{"q":"Why do biochemists quote a standard change at pH 7 in water?","a":"Because the figure depends not only on the molecules involved but on their concentrations and conditions, so the standard is set at roughly the conditions inside a cell."},{"q":"Why must a metabolic model exclude looping reactions?","a":"Because the sign of the free energy change is a hard physical constraint, and a loop circulating for ever with nothing going in is something no real cell can do."}]},
{"id":"bio-079","num":79,"category":"Biology","question":"A reaction that would never run alone can be made to run by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ties it to a second reaction releasing more free energy than the first one needs.","questions":["Hydrolysing one ATP to shift three sodium ions out of a cell and two potassium ions in is a textbook case of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, done by transferring a phosphate.","Because the free energy changes of two reactions run together simply add up, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lets a cell pay for building work out of the energy released by breaking food down."],"answer":"energy coupling","answerText":"energy coupling","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","energy"],"abstract":"<b>Energy coupling</b> is the trick by which a cell drives a reaction that would never happen on its own, running it together with a second reaction that releases more energy than the first one needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The free energy change of the combined reaction is simply the sum of the changes of its parts, so the pair will run as long as that total is negative, and enzymes are what hold the two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Without it almost all the energy released by oxidising food would escape as heat, and what coupling does is catch that release in a chemically useful form instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The catch is held in a small set of activated carrier molecules, of which ATP is the chief, and these diffuse through the cell carrying energy from where it is made to where it is spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hydrolysing ATP to ADP releases about 30 kJ per mole under standard conditions,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and almost double that inside a cell, where the concentrations are nothing like standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nothing is chemically remarkable about the bonds between its phosphates, which are called high-energy only because breaking them in a cell releases so much free energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commonest way of spending that release is phosphorylation, in which the outermost phosphate is handed to another molecule rather than drifting off, leaving it unstable and making it change shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sodium-potassium pump works exactly so, hydrolysing one ATP for each cycle that carries three sodium ions out of the cell and two potassium ions in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That ratio has consequences, because exporting a net positive charge every cycle makes the pump electrogenic, which helps prevent runaway firing but also shifts a busy neuron's baseline and can disturb what it computes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Coupling is a drug target too, and the bacterial transporters that spend ATP to pull vitamins in, having no counterpart in us, are being pursued as an antibiotic route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.4 ATP: Adenosine Triphosphate,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Liz Weerdmeester, Jan-Hendrik Schleimer, and Susanne Schreiber, “The Electrogenicity of the Na+/K+-ATPase Poses Challenges for Computation in Highly Active Spiking Cells,” <i>eLife</i> 14 (2025), https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.103781. [Open access]","Eleonora Diamanti et al., “Targeting the Energy-Coupling Factor Transporters: A Novel Antibiotic Drug Target in <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>,” <i>Journal of Medicinal Chemistry</i> 69, no. 12 (2026): 14054–68, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jmedchem.5c03638. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can coupling drive a reaction that would never run alone?","a":"Because the free energy change of the combined reaction is the sum of its parts, so the pair runs as long as that total is negative, with enzymes holding the two together."},{"q":"Why is nothing chemically remarkable about ATP’s so-called high-energy bonds?","a":"Because they are called that only because breaking them in a cell releases so much free energy, not because the bonds themselves are unusual."},{"q":"Why does the sodium-potassium pump’s ratio have consequences?","a":"Because carrying three sodium out for two potassium in exports a net positive charge each cycle, making the pump electrogenic, which shifts a busy neuron’s baseline as well as restraining firing."}]},
{"id":"bio-080","num":80,"category":"Biology","question":"A cell holds thousands of different kinds, and each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> speeds one reaction along without being used up and without moving where the equilibrium finally lies.","questions":["Reactions that would take years at body temperature run in a fraction of a second when the right <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds the reacting molecules in position for each other.","Ribonuclease P is unusual because it is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made of RNA rather than protein, which almost every other one in the cell is built from."],"answer":"enzyme","answerText":"enzyme","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","protein"],"abstract":"An <b>enzyme</b> is a biological catalyst: a molecule that makes a chemical reaction go faster without itself being consumed or permanently altered by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of them are proteins, chains of amino acids folded so that a pocket on the surface, the active site, can hold the reacting molecules in the positions the reaction needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The molecules an enzyme works on are its substrates, and how closely they fit the active site is what makes enzymes specific, most handling one reaction or a narrow family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Without them almost nothing in a cell would happen fast enough to matter, because a molecule needs a kick over an energy barrier before it will react, and at the temperatures life runs at those kicks are rare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They speed reactions up by well over a millionfold, so a change that would otherwise take years is done in a fraction of a second, and a single cell carries thousands of different ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What an enzyme changes is the rate and nothing else: it leaves the free energy of reactants and products as it found them, so it cannot make an unfavourable reaction favourable or shift where the equilibrium sits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also returns to its original state once the reaction is finished, which is why one molecule of it can work on substrate after substrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cells do not run every enzyme they can make at full tilt, and controlling which are present and how active each is remains a chief way metabolism is governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not all enzymes are proteins, since RNA catalyses reactions too, and ribonuclease P, which is found in all three domains of life, has now been imaged working through its RNA component alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They are also designed now rather than only discovered, and one workflow produced an enzyme differing from any natural protein at more than 140 positions that matches natural enzymes for efficiency on a reaction nature never invented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Dina Listov et al., “Complete Computational Design of High-Efficiency Kemp Elimination Enzymes,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8074 (2025): 1421–27, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09136-2. [Open access]","Yun-Tzai Lee et al., “Structural Basis for Protein-Free Catalysis by Ribonuclease P Ribozyme,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-71597-4. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would a cell’s chemistry not happen without enzymes?","a":"Because a molecule needs a kick over an energy barrier before it will react, and at the temperatures life runs at those kicks are rare, so reactions would take years rather than moments."},{"q":"Why can an enzyme not make an unfavourable reaction favourable?","a":"Because it changes the rate and nothing else, leaving the free energy of reactants and products as it found them, so it cannot shift where the equilibrium sits."},{"q":"Why can one enzyme molecule work on substrate after substrate?","a":"Because it returns to its original state once the reaction is finished, being neither consumed nor permanently altered by the change it speeds up."}]},
{"id":"bio-081","num":81,"category":"Biology","question":"Even a reaction that ends up releasing energy has to climb first, and the height of that climb is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> which decides how fast anything actually happens.","questions":["An enzyme speeds a reaction up by lowering its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and by nothing else, which is why it cannot move an equilibrium or make an unfavourable reaction favourable.","Because forward and reverse reactions pass through the same transition state, a catalyst that lowers the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must speed both directions up by exactly the same factor."],"answer":"activation energy","answerText":"activation energy","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","energy"],"abstract":"The <b>activation energy</b> is what a molecule must first acquire before it can react at all, since even a reaction that releases energy overall has to pass through a higher-energy arrangement on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That arrangement is called the transition state, and the height of the barrier leading up to it, rather than the energy of the products, is what decides how fast the reaction goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An enzyme catalyses precisely by lowering that barrier, so that at any moment far more molecules carry enough energy to cross it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It leaves the energies of the reactants and the products where they were, and therefore cannot make an unfavourable reaction favourable or move the equilibrium an inch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also lowers the barrier equally in both directions, because the forward and the reverse reaction must pass through the very same transition state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The work is done by binding, the substrate settling into the active site where the surrounding chemistry suits the shape of the transition state better than it suits the substrate itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An enzyme may also strain or contort what it holds, or lend chemical groups of its own that take part in the reaction directly, and it comes back to its original state once the product leaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The gains are enormous, since a serine protease runs something like a million million times faster than the same reaction unhelped, and the textbook picture of a catalytic triad and an oxyanion hole does not on its own account for that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A quantitative reckoning instead finds features that are destabilising while the enzyme sits at rest, among them strained bond rotations, cramped distances and poor hydrogen bonds, each of them relieved as the transition state forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured barriers want reading with care, because fitting reaction rate against temperature can give activation energies well away from the real ones when the underlying quantities themselves shift with temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Daniel Herschlag and Siyuan Du, “Understanding How Enzymes Work: The Journey to Ensemble–Function Studies,” <i>The FEBS Journal</i> 293, no. 14 (2026): 4106–19, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.70456. [Open access]","Matthew J. McLeod and Robert E. Thorne, “Activation Parameters, Enthalpy–Entropy Compensation and the Temperature-Dependent Activity of Enzymes,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70722. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the barrier’s height rather than the products’ energy what sets the rate?","a":"Because even a reaction that releases energy overall must pass through a higher-energy transition state, and how many molecules carry enough energy to cross that decides how fast it goes."},{"q":"Why does lowering the barrier speed both directions equally?","a":"Because the forward and reverse reactions must pass through the very same transition state, so the catalysis cannot favour one over the other or move the equilibrium."},{"q":"Why does a catalytic triad not explain the speed on its own?","a":"Because a quantitative reckoning finds features that are destabilising while the enzyme rests — strained bond rotations, cramped distances, poor hydrogen bonds — each relieved as the transition state forms."}]},
{"id":"bio-082","num":82,"category":"Biology","question":"Amino acids lying far apart in the chain are brought together when a protein folds, forming the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into which its substrate must fit.","questions":["Hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and hydrophobic contacts hold a substrate in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> weakly enough that the product can leave once the reaction is done.","Mutations far from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can still improve catalysis by widening its entrance, which shows that a well-organised one is necessary but not sufficient."],"answer":"active site","answerText":"active site","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","protein"],"abstract":"The <b>active site</b> is the small part of an enzyme where the reaction actually takes place, a cleft or a groove in the surface into which the substrate settles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a place rather than a stretch of the chain, since the amino acids lining it usually come from parts of the sequence lying far apart, and are brought into company only when the protein folds into its final shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those residues differ in size, in charge and in whether they welcome water or shun it, and their particular combination builds a chemical environment that suits one substrate or a narrow family of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The substrate is held by noncovalent interactions, among them hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and hydrophobic contacts, each weak enough on its own that the product can leave again afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where a reaction needs two substrates the site holds both at once, in the position and orientation that let them meet, acting as a template on which they are assembled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is also where the enzyme's own chemistry does its work, and acidic and basic side chains within the site may react with the substrate directly and form bonds with the intermediates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the product is released the site is left exactly as it was, which is what allows one enzyme molecule to work through substrate after substrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A site is not a bare pocket in a solid lump, and in the heme peroxidases conserved water-filled channels lead into it while a continuous electrostatic funnel draws protons along them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest of the protein is not scaffolding either, since engineering three designed enzymes showed that mutations far from the site improved catalysis by widening its entrance and reorganising the surface loops, so a well-built site is necessary and not sufficient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Enzyme design now starts from the site: a generative model given only the geometry of the catalytic groups built scaffolds for all 41 sites in one benchmark, where earlier methods managed 16.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Reynier Suardíaz et al., “Solvent Channels and Electric Fields Guide Proton Delivery to the Active Site of Heme Peroxidases,” <i>Angewandte Chemie International Edition</i> 64, no. 50 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1002/anie.202515743. [Open access]","Niayesh Zarifi et al., “Distal Mutations Enhance Catalysis in Designed Enzymes by Facilitating Substrate Binding and Product Release,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63802-7. [Open access]","Woody Ahern et al., “Atom-Level Enzyme Active Site Scaffolding Using RFdiffusion2,” <i>Nature Methods</i> 23, no. 1 (2026): 96–105, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-025-02975-x. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the active site a place rather than a stretch of the chain?","a":"Because the amino acids lining it usually come from parts of the sequence lying far apart, and are brought into company only when the protein folds into its final shape."},{"q":"Why must the substrate be held by weak interactions?","a":"Because hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and hydrophobic contacts are each weak enough on their own that the product can leave again afterwards, freeing the site for the next molecule."},{"q":"Why is a well-built active site necessary but not sufficient?","a":"Because engineering three designed enzymes showed mutations far from the site improving catalysis by widening its entrance and reorganising the surface loops."}]},
{"id":"bio-083","num":83,"category":"Biology","question":"Because an enzyme and its substrate mould each other as they bind, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced the older picture of a key entering a lock already cut to receive it.","questions":["Bending a substrate towards the shape it must take in the transition state is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explains catalysis, where the lock-and-key picture explained only specificity.","Stopped-flow mixing and NMR can tell <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the ligand binds first, from conformational selection, in which the protein changes shape before anything binds."],"answer":"induced fit","answerText":"induced fit","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["theory","science","biology","chemistry","protein"],"abstract":"<b>Induced fit</b> is the idea that an enzyme and its substrate each change shape as they come together, moulding one another rather than meeting like a key in a lock already cut to receive it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That older picture is the lock-and-key model, in which the substrate fits precisely into a site that is the right shape for it before it arrives, and which accounts neatly for specificity and for nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under induced fit the binding alters the conformation of both partners, and the substrate is bent towards the shape it will have to take in the transition state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strain of that distortion weakens the very bonds that must break, and the transition state, once reached, is held tightly by the enzyme, which is where the drop in activation energy comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The enzyme takes no lasting harm from any of it and settles back to its resting shape as soon as the product has gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Neither model belongs to enzymes alone, and both are used to describe any protein taking up a small molecule, a drug included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A third description, conformational selection, has the protein already flickering between shapes of its own accord, with the ligand binding whichever suits it, so that the change comes before the binding rather than after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two can be told apart by how fast things happen, and for the neurotensin receptor 1 both stopped-flow mixing and nuclear magnetic resonance point to induced fit, the ligand binding the inactive shape first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of the three is a full account, and one complaint is that they describe how a ligand arrives while saying nothing about how it leaves, although how tightly it is held depends on both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The subject has moved past single shapes altogether, towards treating a protein as a whole population of conformations, since the textbook drawings do not by themselves explain the rates enzymes reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Danislav S. Spassov, “Binding Affinity Determination in Drug Design: Insights from Lock and Key, Induced Fit, Conformational Selection, and Inhibitor Trapping Models,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 25, no. 13 (2024): 7124, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms25137124. [Open access]","Kazem Asadollahi, Paul R. Gooley, and Thomas R. Weikl, “The Allosteric Mechanism of G-Protein-Coupled Receptors Is Induced Fit, Not Conformational Selection,” <i>BioEssays</i> 47, no. 12 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.70082. [Open access]","Daniel Herschlag and Siyuan Du, “Understanding How Enzymes Work: The Journey to Ensemble–Function Studies,” <i>The FEBS Journal</i> 293, no. 14 (2026): 4106–19, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.70456. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the lock-and-key picture not enough?","a":"Because it accounts neatly for specificity and for nothing else, saying nothing about where the drop in activation energy comes from."},{"q":"Why does moulding the substrate help the reaction along?","a":"Because the substrate is bent towards the shape it must take in the transition state, and the strain of that distortion weakens the very bonds that have to break."},{"q":"Why can conformational selection be told apart from induced fit?","a":"Because the two differ in how fast things happen: for neurotensin receptor 1, stopped-flow mixing and nuclear magnetic resonance both point to the ligand binding the inactive shape first."}]},
{"id":"bio-084","num":84,"category":"Biology","question":"Plotting rate against substrate concentration and reading off the ceiling and the half-way point is the everyday business of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a century old and still argued over.","questions":["The Michaelis constant, meaning the substrate concentration at which a reaction runs at half its maximum rate, is the quantity <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exists to measure alongside the maximum itself.","Piling on more substrate stops helping once every active site is refilled the moment it empties, which is the saturation <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built to describe."],"answer":"enzyme kinetics","answerText":"enzyme kinetics","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","enzyme"],"abstract":"<b>Enzyme kinetics</b> is the study of how fast an enzyme-catalysed reaction runs and of what that speed depends on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mechanism is easily stated: an enzyme binds its substrate reversibly, and the complex either goes forward irreversibly to product or falls apart again with nothing made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measure the rate at a series of substrate concentrations and two numbers fall out: the maximal rate the reaction can reach, and the concentration at which it reaches half of that, the Michaelis constant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relation they belong to is the Michaelis-Menten equation, proposed by Henri and by Michaelis and Menten and put into its familiar form by Briggs and Haldane, on the assumption that the enzyme-substrate complex holds a steady concentration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Michaelis constant is not a measure of binding strength but a ratio of rate constants, setting how fast the complex forms against how fast it either breaks up or turns over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At low substrate concentrations most enzyme molecules are standing idle and the rate climbs almost in proportion to what is on offer, a regime with conditions of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Piling on more cannot raise it for ever, since every active site is refilled the moment it empties, and the rate levels off at a ceiling set by how fast the enzyme itself can work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The catalytic constant and the Michaelis constant are how an enzyme's efficiency and its specificity are put into numbers, and they are now being tabulated against the three-dimensional structures of the complexes they describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The equation rests on two idealisations, that the mixture is uniform in space and that the complex holds steady in time, and neither is guaranteed outside a stirred flask, as a wearable enzyme sensor worn under the skin has shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two constants also cannot hold everything a turnover contains, and higher-order forms of the equation now recover the lifetime of the complex and the odds that a binding yields any product at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Divya Singh, Tal Robin, Michael Urbakh, and Shlomi Reuveni, “High-Order Michaelis-Menten Equations Allow Inference of Hidden Kinetic Parameters in Enzyme Catalysis,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-57327-2. [Open access]","Seolah Shin, Seok Joo Chae, Seunggyu Lee, and Jae Kyoung Kim, “Beyond Homogeneity: Assessing the Validity of the Michaelis-Menten Rate Law in Spatially Heterogeneous Environments,” <i>PLOS Computational Biology</i> 20, no. 6 (2024): e1012205, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1012205. [Open access]","Marco Fratus and Muhammad A. Alam, “Can We Still Use the Michaelis-Menten Model for Enzymatic Microneedle Sensors?,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 30 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2418168122. [Open access]","Justin Eilertsen, Santiago Schnell, and Sebastian Walcher, “The Michaelis-Menten Reaction at Low Substrate Concentrations: Pseudo-First-Order Kinetics and Conditions for Timescale Separation,” <i>Bulletin of Mathematical Biology</i> 86, no. 6 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1007/s11538-024-01295-z. [Open access]","Sowmya Ramaswamy Krishnan, Nishtha Pandey, Rajgopal Srinivasan, and Arijit Roy, “A Structure-Oriented Kinetics Dataset of Enzyme-Substrate Interactions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12, no. 1 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-025-05829-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the rate level off as substrate is piled on?","a":"Because every active site is refilled the moment it empties, so the ceiling is set by how fast the enzyme itself can work rather than by what is on offer."},{"q":"Why is the Michaelis constant not a measure of binding strength?","a":"Because it is a ratio of rate constants, setting how fast the complex forms against how fast it either breaks up or turns over."},{"q":"Why do the equation’s idealisations matter outside a flask?","a":"Because it assumes the mixture is uniform in space and the complex holds steady in time, neither of which is guaranteed — as a wearable enzyme sensor worn under the skin has shown."}]},
{"id":"bio-085","num":85,"category":"Biology","question":"Warming speeds a reaction until the protein begins to come apart, and the peak between those two effects is called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which differs from enzyme to enzyme.","questions":["An enzyme from a hot spring holds its shape with extensive salt bridges, which is why its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies far above that of the same enzyme from a cold-water organism.","Many enzymes reach their fastest rate well below the temperature at which they unfold, so unfolding cannot be the whole reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits where it does."],"answer":"optimum temperature","answerText":"optimum temperature","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","enzyme"],"abstract":"An enzyme's <b>optimum temperature</b> is the temperature at which it works fastest, the peak of a curve that climbs steadily and then falls away sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climb is ordinary chemistry, since warmth makes molecules move faster and collide harder, so more of them arrive carrying enough energy to clear the barrier, and reaction rates in general rise with temperature whether an enzyme is present or not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Move the temperature outside a fairly narrow range in either direction and the bonds within the active site are altered, so that it no longer holds its substrate as well as it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Push it far enough upward and the enzyme denatures outright, its folded shape coming apart, after which it can bind nothing at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Different organisms work at very different temperatures, and an organism's own is written into its proteins clearly enough that it can be predicted from genome sequence alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The molecular differences are known in outline: enzymes from hot environments hold their shape with extensive salt bridges and hydrophobic networks, while cold-adapted ones rely instead on a few carefully placed interactions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The textbook curve is nonetheless too simple, because many enzymes reach their fastest rate well below the temperature at which they unfold, so denaturation cannot be the whole reason the rate falls away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The favoured explanation has the enthalpy and entropy of activation themselves shifting with temperature, and fitting it to those enzymes yields physically implausible numbers, so something else is contributing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Adaptation itself can be startlingly economical, and in one bacterial enzyme the shift to a different working temperature came down largely to a single change of residue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That same change turns up in diverse backgrounds, and residues associated with an organism's growth temperature have been identified across 1,005 bacterial enzyme families, which suggests the adjustment has been made over and over independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Mensur Dlakić and William P. Inskeep, “Improved Prediction of Microbial Optimal Growth Temperatures with Neural Networks and Protein Language Models,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 17 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2026.1874451. [Open access]","Dulitha P. Kulathunga and Davit A. Potoyan, “Thermal Adaptation of Extremozymes: Temperature-Sensitive Contact Analysis of Serine Proteases,” <i>Biophysical Journal</i> 124, no. 14 (2025): 2291–2302, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpj.2025.06.001. [Open access]","Matthew J. McLeod and Robert E. Thorne, “Activation Parameters, Enthalpy–Entropy Compensation and the Temperature-Dependent Activity of Enzymes,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70722. [Open access]","Margaux M. Pinney et al., “Parallel Molecular Mechanisms for Enzyme Temperature Adaptation,” <i>Science</i> 371, no. 6533 (2021), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13050526/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the rate climb with temperature at first?","a":"Because warmth makes molecules move faster and collide harder, so more of them arrive carrying enough energy to clear the barrier — ordinary chemistry, enzyme or no enzyme."},{"q":"Why is denaturation not the whole reason the curve falls away?","a":"Because many enzymes reach their fastest rate well below the temperature at which they unfold, so something else must be pulling the rate down first."},{"q":"Why does adapting to a new working temperature look economical?","a":"Because in one bacterial enzyme the shift came down largely to a single change of residue, and such residues have been identified across 1,005 bacterial enzyme families."}]},
{"id":"bio-086","num":86,"category":"Biology","question":"Each enzyme works fastest at its own <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because the acidic and basic groups in its active site have to be in the right ionised state.","questions":["Because pepsin works in the stomach's strong acid and the pancreatic proteases in an alkaline intestine, their values of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lie far apart, which is why the body separates them.","For the AlkB repair enzymes a shift in acidity changes not just the rate but which DNA strand they prefer, so their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not the whole story."],"answer":"optimum pH","answerText":"optimum pH","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","enzyme"],"abstract":"An enzyme's <b>optimum pH</b> is the acidity at which it works fastest, and every enzyme is suited to a certain range on either side of it rather than to any acidity at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason lies in the active site, whose amino acid residues carry acidic and basic groups of their own that have to be in the right state for catalysis to happen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Changing the acidity adds protons to those groups or takes them away, so the site binds its substrate less well than it did, and a large enough change unfolds the enzyme altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What sets the figure can be traced to particular residues: in two bacterial enzymes it turned on a single pair beside the cofactor, and altering the charges there moved both towards working in mildly acidic conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One catalytic residue's state can be worth a study of its own, as it has been for the cysteine that does the cutting in the protease of the parasite behind Chagas disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The body puts the same principle to work by holding different compartments at different acidities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chief cells release pepsinogen into the stomach, where hydrochloric acid both cleaves it into the working enzyme pepsin and unfolds the dietary protein it is about to cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Further along, bicarbonate from the pancreas raises the acidity of the intestinal contents, and it is that rise which wakes the pancreatic proteases from the inactive forms they are secreted in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured across a wide range, enzymes generally give bell-shaped activity curves with peaks of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Acidity can also change more than the rate, and for the AlkB family of DNA repair enzymes it shifts which strand they prefer to work on, one of them favouring single-stranded DNA in acid and double-stranded DNA nearer neutral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Yuanxiang Liu et al., “Electrostatic Tuning of the Pyridoxal-5′-Phosphate Cofactor Site Defines pH Dependence in Type I Cystathionine β-Lyases,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 302, no. 6 (2026): 111469, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2026.111469. [Open access]","Clauber H. S. da Costa et al., “Evaluating the Protonation State of the Catalytic Cys25 in Cruzain Cysteine Protease: A Target for Chagas Disease,” <i>Protein Science</i> 34, no. 10 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70283. [Open access]","Tatyana S. Gurina and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Protein Catabolism,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2022), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556047/. [Open access]","Samuel D. Howarth et al., “pH Tunes the DNA Repair Efficiency and Strand Preference of the AlkB Family Enzymes,” <i>Chemical Research in Toxicology</i> 39, no. 7 (2026): 1418–30, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.chemrestox.6c00202. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does acidity change what an enzyme can do?","a":"Because the active site’s residues carry acidic and basic groups that must be in the right state, and changing the pH adds protons to them or takes them away."},{"q":"Why does the body hold its compartments at different acidities?","a":"Because it uses pH as a switch: stomach acid both cleaves pepsinogen into pepsin and unfolds the dietary protein, while pancreatic bicarbonate wakes the proteases further along."},{"q":"Why can acidity change more than the rate?","a":"Because for the AlkB family of DNA repair enzymes it shifts which strand they prefer, one favouring single-stranded DNA in acid and double-stranded nearer neutral."}]},
{"id":"bio-087","num":87,"category":"Biology","question":"A molecule that binds an enzyme and slows or stops it is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whether it occupies the active site or fastens somewhere else entirely.","questions":["Isoleucine shutting down the first enzyme of the pathway that makes it is feedback inhibition, which means the amino acid is acting as an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on its own supply.","A reactive cysteine that LIMK1 has and its near-twin LIMK2 has not lets a covalent <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pick one kinase out of more than a hundred."],"answer":"enzyme inhibitor","answerText":"enzyme inhibitor","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","enzyme"],"abstract":"An <b>enzyme inhibitor</b> is a molecule that binds an enzyme and reduces or stops what it does, and cells and pharmacists alike depend on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some are similar enough to the substrate to settle into the active site itself and simply occupy the place the substrate would have taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others fasten somewhere else entirely, at what is called an allosteric site, and alter the enzyme's shape so that the active site holds its substrate less willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same trick runs in reverse, since allosteric activators bind away from the active site and change the shape the other way, raising rather than lowering the enzyme's appetite for its substrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cells govern themselves with inhibition: in feedback inhibition the end product of a pathway shuts down the first enzyme in it, so that isoleucine switches off threonine deaminase and no more is made than is wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inhibition may be reversible or permanent, and a covalent inhibitor forms an actual bond with the enzyme rather than merely resting in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That permanence can buy precision: LIM domain kinase 1 carries a reactive cysteine near its active site which its near-twin LIM domain kinase 2 lacks, and an inhibitor built to react with it proved selective over that twin and over a panel of more than a hundred other kinases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which kind of inhibitor a molecule is cannot be assumed and has to be measured, and one recent method tells rapid reversible, slow-binding and covalent inhibition apart from the heat a single reaction gives off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was tested on the main protease of the coronavirus behind the pandemic, against four of its inhibitors including the one in the antiviral pill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most drugs work by inhibiting an enzyme, and fresh targets are still being opened, among them bacterial transporters with no counterpart in us whose inhibitors cut pneumococcal load in mice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Jon B. Patteson et al., “A Covalent Inhibitor Targeting Cys-349 of LIMK1 Confers Selectivity over LIMK2,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 302, no. 9 (2026): 113322, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2026.113322. [Open access]","Luca Mazzei, Sofia Ranieri, Davide Silvestri, Gaetano T. Montelione, and Stefano Ciurli, “Decoding Enzyme-Inhibitor Kinetic Mechanisms by Isothermal Titration Calorimetry: The Case of SARS-CoV-2 3CLpro,” <i>Analytical Chemistry</i> 98, no. 26 (2026): 19520–32, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.analchem.6c00471. [Open access]","Eleonora Diamanti et al., “Targeting the Energy-Coupling Factor Transporters: A Novel Antibiotic Drug Target in <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>,” <i>Journal of Medicinal Chemistry</i> 69, no. 12 (2026): 14054–68, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jmedchem.5c03638. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can an inhibitor work without touching the active site?","a":"Because it can fasten at an allosteric site and alter the enzyme’s shape, so that the active site holds its substrate less willingly."},{"q":"Why is feedback inhibition a sensible way to govern a pathway?","a":"Because the end product shuts down the first enzyme in it — isoleucine switching off threonine deaminase — so no more is made than is wanted."},{"q":"Why can permanence buy precision in a drug?","a":"Because LIM domain kinase 1 carries a reactive cysteine near its active site that its near-twin lacks, so an inhibitor built to react with it was selective over that twin and a hundred other kinases."}]},
{"id":"bio-088","num":88,"category":"Biology","question":"An inhibitor shaped enough like the substrate to sit in the active site works by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and enough substrate will still drive the reaction to its usual maximum.","questions":["Because the two molecules are contending for the same pocket, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> slows the early part of a reaction while leaving its top speed exactly where it was.","Where an inhibitor binds away from the active site the maximum rate itself drops, which is not what happens under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the pocket is contested."],"answer":"competitive inhibition","answerText":"competitive inhibition","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","enzyme"],"abstract":"<b>Competitive inhibition</b> is what happens when an inhibitor resembles an enzyme's substrate closely enough to occupy the active site, so that the two contend for the same pocket and whichever arrives first is served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In non-competitive inhibition the inhibitor binds somewhere else on the enzyme altogether, at an allosteric site, and still manages to stop the substrate being turned over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two leave different marks on the rate: a competitive inhibitor slows the early part of the reaction and leaves its maximum untouched, while a non-competitive one pulls the maximum itself down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That difference follows from where each one sits, because a rival for the pocket can always be outnumbered by piling on more substrate whereas a molecule bound elsewhere cannot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An allosteric site is a real place on the protein, distinct from the catalytic one, and the cell's own regulators use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Both patterns are found in real enzymes and both are pursued as drugs, and a non-competitive inhibitor of the DNA repair polymerase beta, built from unnatural nucleotides, is among the most potent known for that enzyme, which is overexpressed in some cancers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which pattern an inhibitor follows is settled by measurement rather than by looking at its shape, and calorimetry can now sort rapid reversible inhibition from slow-binding and covalent kinds within a single experiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The textbook pair is also a simplification, since an inhibitor may show a mixed pattern with a share of each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Acetic acid released during the pretreatment of plant biomass inhibits two families of xylanase differently, one in a mixed fashion that is mostly non-competitive and the other in a third way again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Knowing which is not academic there, since it decides how a biorefinery should be run: both enzymes lose only a tenth or so of their activity up to 0.8 per cent acetic acid and almost all of it at 1.0.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","A. Hasan Howlader, Xuanhe Jiang, Zehui Zhou, and Marc M. Greenberg, “Noncompetitive Inhibition of DNA Polymerase β by a Nonnative Nucleotide,” <i>The Journal of Organic Chemistry</i> 90, no. 37 (2025): 12849–59, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.joc.5c01529. [Open access]","Luca Mazzei, Sofia Ranieri, Davide Silvestri, Gaetano T. Montelione, and Stefano Ciurli, “Decoding Enzyme-Inhibitor Kinetic Mechanisms by Isothermal Titration Calorimetry: The Case of SARS-CoV-2 3CLpro,” <i>Analytical Chemistry</i> 98, no. 26 (2026): 19520–32, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.analchem.6c00471. [Open access]","Dan-Gyeong Han, Beom Soo Kim, and In Jung Kim, “Distinct Mechanisms of Acetic Acid Inhibition in Thermophilic GH10 and GH11 Xylanases,” <i>Journal of Microbiology and Biotechnology</i> 36 (2026), https://doi.org/10.4014/jmb.2602.02022. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can more substrate overcome one kind of inhibitor and not the other?","a":"Because a rival for the same pocket can always be outnumbered, whereas a molecule bound elsewhere on the enzyme cannot be crowded out of a place it does not occupy."},{"q":"Why do the two kinds leave different marks on the rate?","a":"Because a competitive inhibitor slows the early part of the reaction and leaves its maximum untouched, while a non-competitive one pulls the maximum itself down."},{"q":"Why does knowing which pattern applies matter industrially?","a":"Because it decides how a biorefinery is run: the two xylanase families lose only a tenth of their activity up to 0.8 per cent acetic acid and almost all of it at 1.0."}]},
{"id":"bio-089","num":89,"category":"Biology","question":"Many enzymes do nothing at all until a non-protein helper is bound to them, and a metal ion such as the zinc DNA polymerase needs is one kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among several.","questions":["Because DNA polymerase will not build anything without a bound zinc ion, that ion counts as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than as a substrate, and it leaves the reaction unchanged.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase needs one magnesium ion and five different organic helpers, so a single reaction can turn on more than half a dozen kinds of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at once."],"answer":"cofactor","answerText":"cofactor","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","science","biology","chemistry","enzyme"],"abstract":"A <b>cofactor</b> is a non-protein helper that many enzymes will not work without, bound to them either loosely by weak forces or permanently by covalent ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commonest are metal ions, and the enzyme that builds DNA needs a zinc ion in place before it will assemble anything at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Organic helpers are called coenzymes, most of them made from vitamins in the diet, so that vitamin C is a coenzyme for the enzymes that build collagen and a deficiency shows in the connective tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The words are not used identically everywhere, since some texts keep cofactor for the inorganic ions and coenzyme for the organic helpers,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> while others treat cofactor as the wider word covering both, along with the prosthetic groups that stay bound for good, as haem does in haemoglobin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Coenzymes differ from substrates in not being used up, since they are recycled and go round again, and NAD carries electrons from one reaction to another in exactly that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A single reaction can call on several at once, and pyruvate dehydrogenase, which opens the aerobic breakdown of sugar, needs one magnesium ion and five different organic coenzymes together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because most coenzymes are built from vitamins, an animal that has lost the ability to make one must eat it, where a bacterium such as <i>E. coli</i> needs none from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The protein does not simply hold its helper but tunes it, and across the flavoenzymes the same cofactor is made to work over an extremely wide range of redox conditions, its range set by which of its nitrogens the surrounding protein keeps protonated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which metal is needed is a real distinction rather than a detail, and two newly described enzymes from bacteriophages attacking a urinary pathogen proved to need different metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some cofactors are elaborate enough to need enzymes of their own to build them, and the nickel-containing one assembled by a hexameric enzyme with a histidine-rich pocket has now been imaged mid-assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Wolfgang Nitschke, Simon Duval, Kilian Zuchan, Jostin Monge-Ruiz, Frauke Baymann, and Barbara Schoepp-Cothenet, “pK Values of the Cofactor Tune the Redox Regime of Flavoenzymes,” <i>Life</i> 16, no. 8 (2026): 1277, https://doi.org/10.3390/life16081277. [Open access]","Akash Shambharkar et al., “First Experimentally Characterised Proteus Phage Endolysins Have Distinct Metal Cofactor Dependencies,” <i>Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology</i> 110, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-026-13891-1. [Open access]","Robert Wolfe et al., “Structural and Functional Analysis of LarC, a CTP-Dependent Cyclometallase Required for Nickel-Pincer Nucleotide Cofactor Biosynthesis,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 35 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aeh6867. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a coenzyme not simply another substrate?","a":"Because it is not used up: it is recycled and goes round again, as NAD does in carrying electrons from one reaction to another."},{"q":"Why must an animal eat vitamins where a bacterium need not?","a":"Because most coenzymes are built from vitamins, so an animal that has lost the ability to make one has to take it in, while E. coli needs none from outside."},{"q":"Why does the protein around a cofactor matter as much as the cofactor?","a":"Because it tunes it: across the flavoenzymes the same cofactor works over an extremely wide range of redox conditions, its range set by which nitrogens the protein keeps protonated."}]},
{"id":"bio-090","num":90,"category":"Biology","question":"Running a chemical process on enzymes rather than on heat and harsh solvents is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its chief practical problem is that factories are hard on proteins.","questions":["Fixing an enzyme to a solid support so that it can be filtered out and used again is the standard economy of industrial <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since the enzyme is the expensive part.","An asparagine synthetase evolved in the laboratory to make an anti-epileptic intermediate in one step shows how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be given reactions that no organism performs."],"answer":"biocatalysis","answerText":"biocatalysis","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","science","biology","chemistry","enzyme"],"abstract":"<b>Biocatalysis</b> is the use of enzymes to run chemical processes on purpose, and its attractions over ordinary industrial chemistry are mild conditions, high efficiency and products that break down in the environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difficulty is that a factory is a hard place for a protein, and the conditions of real manufacturing destabilise enzymes and shorten their working lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual answer is immobilisation, fixing the enzyme to a solid support so that it can be recovered and used over and over, which is what makes the economics work when the enzyme is the expensive part of the process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Immobilisation is not merely a way of holding an enzyme still, since the method chosen alters how the enzyme behaves and so changes the product as well as the cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the enzymes come from is a question in itself, and the guts of insects are one reservoir being searched for cellulases, amylases, proteases and lipases that are already stable because their owners live in hard places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An enzyme need not be taken as found, and directed evolution builds better ones by making many variants and keeping the best.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Screening is the bottleneck there, so one group rebuilt a bacterial transcription factor into a sensor that lights up for the product it wanted and used it to search mutant libraries, ending with an asparagine synthetase that makes an anti-epileptic intermediate in a single step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That reaction is one no organism performs, which is the direction the field is going.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enzymes are also now designed outright rather than evolved, and a computational workflow has produced one differing from any natural protein at more than 140 positions that matches natural enzymes for efficiency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The thermal end is understood well enough to engineer, since enzymes from hot environments hold together through extensive salt bridges and hydrophobic networks that a designer can copy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Marina G. Holyavka and Valeriy G. Artyukhov, “Prospects and Problems in Enzyme Immobilization Methodology: Comprehensive Review,” <i>Biophysical Reviews</i> 17, no. 5 (2025): 1471–1509, https://doi.org/10.1007/s12551-025-01360-4. [Open access]","Aswin Viswan and Neenu Augustine, “Insect Gut Microbiota as a Reservoir of Industrially Relevant Enzymes: A Comprehensive Review,” <i>Microbial Ecology</i> 89, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1007/s00248-026-02726-1. [Open access]","Qianpeng Lai et al., “Creating a Non-Native Bio-Amidation Activity in Asparagine Synthetase via Biosensor-Guided Directed Evolution for the Synthesis of (S)-2-Aminobutyramide,” <i>Chemical Science</i> (2026), https://doi.org/10.1039/D6SC04510C. [Open access]","Dina Listov et al., “Complete Computational Design of High-Efficiency Kemp Elimination Enzymes,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8074 (2025): 1421–27, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09136-2. [Open access]","Dulitha P. Kulathunga and Davit A. Potoyan, “Thermal Adaptation of Extremozymes: Temperature-Sensitive Contact Analysis of Serine Proteases,” <i>Biophysical Journal</i> 124, no. 14 (2025): 2291–2302, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpj.2025.06.001. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a factory a hard place for an enzyme?","a":"Because the conditions of real manufacturing destabilise proteins and shorten their working lives, which is what immobilisation on a solid support is meant to answer."},{"q":"Why is immobilisation more than a way of holding an enzyme still?","a":"Because the method chosen alters how the enzyme behaves, so it changes the product as well as the cost of recovering and reusing it."},{"q":"Why is screening the bottleneck in directed evolution?","a":"Because the method makes many variants and keeps the best, so the limit is telling which is best — answered in one case by rebuilding a transcription factor into a sensor that lights up for the product."}]},
{"id":"bio-091","num":91,"category":"Biology","question":"Nothing smaller than a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can gather raw materials from its surroundings and build a fresh copy of itself, complete with the instructions for doing so.","questions":["Every living thing on Earth stores its instructions in DNA, copies them the same way and reads them into protein by the same code, which is what makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a universal unit.","A membrane encloses each one, holding useful molecules in and waste out, so that without it a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not stay a coordinated chemical system at all."],"answer":"cell","answerText":"cell","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["object","science","biology","nature","cell"],"abstract":"Every organism on Earth is either a single <b>cell</b> or an assembly of them descended by division from one, so the cell is the vehicle carrying the hereditary information that defines a species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing smaller can do what it does, since a cell gathers raw materials from its surroundings and builds out of them a new cell in its own image, complete with a fresh copy of the instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four things are true of all of them without exception: the information is stored in DNA, copied by laying down a new strand against an old one, transcribed into RNA and translated into protein by very nearly the same code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That code is in some respects arbitrary, and its being identical in almost every living thing is read as a frozen accident of the earliest organisms rather than as the only arrangement that could work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every cell is also bounded by a plasma membrane, which lets it concentrate what it has gathered and expel what it does not want, and without which it could not hold together as a chemical system at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> All of them run on the same small molecules too, among them sugars, nucleotides, amino acids and the ATP that carries energy about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cells are small, a bacterium measuring a micrometre or so across, and they are not for that reason simple, the species with the smallest genome yet found carrying 477 genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> New ones come only from existing ones, which is why the ancestry runs unbroken back to a single population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reconstruction puts that last universal common ancestor at about 4.2 billion years ago with a genome of at least 2.5 million bases and some 2,600 proteins, which is a modern prokaryote's worth rather than anything rudimentary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even earlier events can be glimpsed through gene families that had already duplicated before it, which carry a phylogenetic record from before the ancestor of everything alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Edmund R. R. Moody et al., “The Nature of the Last Universal Common Ancestor and Its Impact on the Early Earth System,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8, no. 9 (2024): 1654–66, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-024-02461-1. [Open access]","Aaron D. Goldman, Gregory P. Fournier, and Betül Kaçar, “Universal Paralogs Provide a Window into Evolution before the Last Universal Common Ancestor,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 6, no. 3 (2026): 101140, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xgen.2026.101140. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can nothing smaller than a cell do what a cell does?","a":"Because it gathers raw materials from its surroundings and builds out of them a new cell in its own image, complete with a fresh copy of the instructions."},{"q":"Why is the shared genetic code read as a frozen accident?","a":"Because it is in some respects arbitrary, so its being identical in almost every living thing points to inheritance from the earliest organisms rather than to the only arrangement that could work."},{"q":"Why was the last universal common ancestor not rudimentary?","a":"Because reconstruction gives it a genome of at least 2.5 million bases and some 2,600 proteins, which is a modern prokaryote’s worth."}]},
{"id":"bio-092","num":92,"category":"Biology","question":"That all living things are made of cells, that the cell is the basic unit of life and that new cells come only from existing ones is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, settled in the 1830s.","questions":["Two men studying plant and animal tissues in the late 1830s arrived at the same conclusion from opposite directions, and the statement they arrived at is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, still the frame of the subject.","The claim in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that every cell descends from an earlier one is what makes the ancestry of everything alive an unbroken chain running back to a single population."],"answer":"cell theory","answerText":"cell theory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Stated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 1840</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["theory","science","biology","cell"],"abstract":"<b>Cell theory</b> holds three things at once: that one or more cells make up every living thing, that the cell is the basic unit of life, and that new cells arise only from cells that already exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was put together in the late 1830s by a botanist and a zoologist working on plant and animal tissue respectively, who found they were describing the same unit, and it was sharpened afterwards by others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first claim is a statement about scope: an organism is either one cell, as a bacterium is, or an assembly of them organised into tissues, organs and systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second is a statement about limits, because nothing smaller than a cell can gather materials and build a fresh copy of itself complete with its own instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third is the one that does the most work, since it turns every organism alive into the end of an unbroken chain of divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That chain has a direction and a length, and reconstruction places its origin at about 4.2 billion years ago in a population already carrying some 2,600 proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The theory is also what makes the origin of life a hard problem rather than a vague one, because the first cell is precisely the thing the third claim forbids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its evidence was optical from the start, and it could not have been framed before lenses good enough to show that tissue is made of separate units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A single ground lens in the hands of a Delft draper had already resolved bacteria, red blood cells and the flow in a capillary, a century and a half before the theory was stated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the theory added was not a new sight but a claim about everything, which is why it counts as the frame the subject has been built in ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Edmund R. R. Moody et al., “The Nature of the Last Universal Common Ancestor and Its Impact on the Early Earth System,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8, no. 9 (2024): 1654–66, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-024-02461-1. [Open access]","Hyunjeong Lee and Im Joo Rhyu, “Antonie van Leeuwenhoek and the Dawn of Microscopic Observation: A Narrative Review from Delft's Lens to the Modern Microscope,” <i>Applied Microscopy</i> 56, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1186/s42649-026-00123-z. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the third claim do the most work?","a":"Because holding that new cells arise only from existing ones turns every organism alive into the end of an unbroken chain of divisions running back to one population."},{"q":"Why does the theory make the origin of life a sharp problem?","a":"Because the first cell is precisely the thing the third claim forbids, so the theory names what has to be explained rather than leaving it vague."},{"q":"Why could the theory not have been stated earlier?","a":"Because its evidence was optical from the start and needed lenses good enough to show that tissue is made of separate units."}]},
{"id":"bio-093","num":93,"category":"Biology","question":"The word cell entered biology in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a folio of 1665 whose engravings showed what a compound microscope could make of cork and of the pith of eight other plants.","questions":["Rows of cavities in cork were read in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as narrow pipes divided at intervals by valves, and it was a segment of one such pipe that got the name we still use.","The tale that the boxes were named after the rooms of a monastery is a myth, since no monk and no monastery appears anywhere in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or in its author's other writings."],"answer":"Micrographia","answerText":"Micrographia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1665</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","science","biology","history","cell"],"abstract":"<b>Micrographia</b> is the folio of 1665 in which the word cell entered biology, a set of engravings of what a compound microscope showed of very small things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its author, the Royal Society's Curator of Experiments, was not idly squinting at a slice of cork: he lit specimens indirectly and cut sections in several planes to reconstruct plant material in three dimensions, and found the same texture in the pith of eight further species he names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What he called a cell was a segment of pipe, since he read the rows of cavities as narrow tubes divided by valves that regulated the flow of fluid, which is what the biology of his day expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He did not see only empty walls either, recording that cells in fresh material were filled with juices and sweated their contents out as the specimen dried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The familiar story that he named them after a monastery's rooms is a myth, repeated in textbooks and on educational sites: no monk and no monastery appears anywhere in his writings on cells, nor the Latin word they supposedly come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A better instrument was at work within a decade, and it was far simpler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A draper in Delft ground single lenses that magnified 270 times and sometimes 500, where the microscopes before them managed about 20, the gain coming from fewer optical surfaces rather than more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Looking at pond water in 1674 he described green globules and, among them, very many little animalcules, and he was probably the first person to see a protozoan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The claim was doubted, so the Royal Society sent a party to Delft in 1680 and confirmed it, and his letters ran for fifty years, reporting red blood cells, capillary flow and the animalcules in a healthy mouth's plaque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither man had a theory of the cell, and what neither could guess is that the box named here would prove to be the smallest thing able to gather materials and build a copy of itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Winfried S. Peters, “Will the Real Robert Hooke Please Stand Up?,” <i>The Plant Cell</i> 36, no. 11 (2024): 4680–82, https://doi.org/10.1093/plcell/koae244. [Open access]","Hyunjeong Lee and Im Joo Rhyu, “Antonie van Leeuwenhoek and the Dawn of Microscopic Observation: A Narrative Review from Delft's Lens to the Modern Microscope,” <i>Applied Microscopy</i> 56, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1186/s42649-026-00123-z. [Open access]","Robert Gaynes, “Antony van Leeuwenhoek, Pioneer of the Microscopic World,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 32, no. 6 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3206.260531. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hooke read his cavities as pipes rather than as cells?","a":"Because he took the rows of cavities for narrow tubes divided by valves regulating the flow of fluid, which is what the biology of his day expected to find in a plant."},{"q":"Why is the monastery story about the word’s origin a myth?","a":"Because no monk, no monastery and not even the Latin word they supposedly come from appears anywhere in his writings on cells, though textbooks repeat it."},{"q":"Why did a single-lens instrument beat the compound microscopes?","a":"Because the gain came from fewer optical surfaces rather than more: a Delft draper’s ground lenses magnified 270 times and sometimes 500 where the compound instruments managed about 20."}]},
{"id":"bio-094","num":94,"category":"Biology","question":"Bacteria and archaea are grouped together as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because neither keeps its DNA behind a membrane, holding it instead in an open region of the cytoplasm.","questions":["A cell of this kind carries its chromosome in a nucleoid, makes its proteins on ribosomes lying loose in the cytoplasm and usually wears a wall, which is what being a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> amounts to.","At a tenth of a micrometre to five micrometres across, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is small enough that anything entering it diffuses across the whole cell quickly, which is one reason such cells work."],"answer":"prokaryote","answerText":"prokaryote","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","science","biology","cell","nature"],"abstract":"A <b>prokaryote</b> is a cell that keeps no nucleus, and the name says so: the two domains it covers, the bacteria and the archaea, hold their DNA in an open region of the cytoplasm called the nucleoid rather than behind a membrane of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They share with every other cell a plasma membrane, a jelly-like cytoplasm, DNA and ribosomes on which to build proteins, and what they lack is the set of membrane-walled compartments a eukaryote is organised into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most bacteria wear a wall of peptidoglycan that keeps their shape and stops them drying out, and many add a polysaccharide capsule that lets them stick to surfaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some carry flagella to swim with, pili through which one cell passes genetic material directly to another, or fimbriae for gripping a host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are small, a tenth of a micrometre to about five, which matters because anything entering such a cell diffuses across the whole of it quickly and any waste made inside it leaves as fast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Small does not mean primitive, and the same universal machinery runs in them as in us: DNA copied by templating, transcribed to RNA and translated by nearly the same code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ancestor of everything alive is reconstructed as a cell of roughly this kind, at about 4.2 billion years ago with a genome of 2.5 million bases and some 2,600 proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The category has a ragged edge, and one sulphur bacterium from a Caribbean mangrove grows to a centimetre in length, visible without a lens, with its DNA held inside membrane-bound compartments that are metabolically active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the nucleoid is not a loose heap, since small RNAs and their partner proteins take part in folding the chromosome into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word therefore names an absence rather than a group with a common ancestor of its own, which is worth remembering whenever it is used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.2 Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Edmund R. R. Moody et al., “The Nature of the Last Universal Common Ancestor and Its Impact on the Early Earth System,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8, no. 9 (2024): 1654–66, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-024-02461-1. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]","Gabriela Mistygacz et al., “Dual Role of Small Noncoding RNA and Hfq in Bacterial DNA Compaction: A New Perspective on Nucleoid Architecture,” <i>ACS Omega</i> 11, no. 21 (2026): 31559–69, https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.6c02202. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does being small suit a cell with no internal compartments?","a":"Because anything entering such a cell diffuses across the whole of it quickly and any waste made inside it leaves as fast, so no transport machinery is needed."},{"q":"Why is small not the same as primitive?","a":"Because the same universal machinery runs in them as in us — DNA copied by templating, transcribed to RNA and translated by nearly the same code."},{"q":"Why does the word name an absence rather than a group?","a":"Because it covers two domains defined by lacking a nucleus rather than by descent from a common ancestor of their own."}]},
{"id":"bio-095","num":95,"category":"Biology","question":"Animals, plants, fungi and protists are all built of the kind of cell called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose defining feature is a nucleus enclosed in a membrane of its own.","questions":["Compartmenting different jobs into organelles walled off by membranes is what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> does and a bacterium does not, and it is why such a cell can afford to be large.","Ten to a hundred micrometres across, with rod-shaped chromosomes and an endoplasmic reticulum, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an order of magnitude bigger than the cells it evolved from."],"answer":"eukaryote","answerText":"eukaryote","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","science","biology","cell","nature"],"abstract":"A <b>eukaryote</b> is a cell with a true nucleus, which is what the name says: its DNA is enclosed in a membrane of its own rather than lying loose in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Animals, plants, fungi and protists are all built this way, and beside the nucleus such a cell carries numerous other membrane-walled organelles, among them the endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi apparatus and the mitochondria, with chloroplasts in plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chromosomes are several and rod-shaped where a bacterium's genome is usually one closed loop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organelles are the point rather than an ornament, since walling a job off in its own compartment lets incompatible chemistries run at once in one cell, which is the principle of form following function applied inside a membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also what makes size affordable, and a eukaryotic cell runs ten to a hundred micrometres across where a prokaryote manages a tenth of a micrometre to five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Being bigger costs something, because a large cell cannot rely on diffusion to move materials about and needs structural arrangements for transport instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Underneath all of it the machinery is the same as any other cell's, with information in DNA, transcribed to RNA and translated by nearly the same code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Size within the group varies enormously and is not fixed by the plan, and yeast selected in the laboratory for smallness shrank steadily through mutations in nutrient-sensing and cell-cycle pathways at very little cost to fitness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The boundary with the prokaryotes is real but not perfectly sharp, and a bacterium has been described that is a centimetre long and keeps its DNA in membrane-bound compartments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How a cell of this kind arose at all is a separate question and a hard one, since the nucleus and the mitochondria both had to be acquired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Ana Garoña, Morgane V. Lemos, Andrea Giometto, and Marco Fumasoni, “Experimental Evolution of Cellular Miniaturization Reveals a Putative Mechanism for Cell Size Evolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 24 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2531280123. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the organelles the point rather than an ornament?","a":"Because walling a job off in its own compartment lets incompatible chemistries run at once in one cell, which is form following function applied inside a membrane."},{"q":"Why does compartmentalisation make size affordable?","a":"Because a cell of ten to a hundred micrometres cannot rely on diffusion to move materials about, and the compartments come with the structural arrangements for transport."},{"q":"Why is the origin of such a cell a separate and hard question?","a":"Because the nucleus and the mitochondria both had to be acquired, so the plan cannot be reached by gradual enlargement of a prokaryote."}]},
{"id":"bio-096","num":96,"category":"Biology","question":"A bacterium's chromosome sits in an open region of cytoplasm called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the direct counterpart of the nucleus a eukaryotic cell keeps behind a membrane.","questions":["No membrane surrounds the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so transcription and translation happen in the same space, where a eukaryote separates the two by a wall.","Small RNAs and their partner proteins help fold a bacterial chromosome into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is therefore an organised structure rather than a loose heap of DNA."],"answer":"nucleoid","answerText":"nucleoid","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","science","biology","cell"],"abstract":"The <b>nucleoid</b> is the region of a prokaryotic cell in which its chromosome sits, and it is the one structure that most sharply marks such a cell off from a eukaryotic one, because no membrane surrounds it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A eukaryote encloses its DNA in a nucleus of its own and fills the rest of the cell with further membrane-walled organelles; a prokaryote has neither, and its ribosomes lie loose in the same cytoplasm as its genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One consequence is that a bacterium transcribes and translates in the same space, with no wall between the two operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest of the differences follow from that division of labour rather than standing beside it: several rod-shaped chromosomes against one loop, ten to a hundred micrometres across against a tenth of a micrometre to five, and structural machinery for transport against plain diffusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the two kinds share is more fundamental still, since every cell has a plasma membrane, a cytoplasm, DNA and ribosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The absence of a membrane is not an absence of organisation, and the chromosome is folded into its region rather than left in a heap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small non-coding RNAs and the protein that partners them take part in that compaction, which makes the nucleoid a structure with an architecture to study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dividing line is also not quite absolute, and a centimetre-long sulphur bacterium has been found holding its DNA inside membrane-bound compartments that are metabolically active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That single organism breaks both halves of the textbook contrast at once, being neither small nor free of internal membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The contrast is a good working rule all the same, and the exception is what makes it worth stating as a rule rather than a law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.2 Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Gabriela Mistygacz et al., “Dual Role of Small Noncoding RNA and Hfq in Bacterial DNA Compaction: A New Perspective on Nucleoid Architecture,” <i>ACS Omega</i> 11, no. 21 (2026): 31559–69, https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.6c02202. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a bacterium transcribe and translate in one space?","a":"Because no membrane surrounds its chromosome, so its ribosomes lie loose in the same cytoplasm as its genes with no wall between the two operations."},{"q":"Why is the lack of a membrane not a lack of organisation?","a":"Because the chromosome is folded into its region rather than left in a heap, with small non-coding RNAs and their partner protein taking part in that compaction."},{"q":"Why is the textbook contrast a rule rather than a law?","a":"Because a centimetre-long sulphur bacterium holds its DNA inside metabolically active membrane-bound compartments, breaking both halves of the contrast at once."}]},
{"id":"bio-097","num":97,"category":"Biology","question":"Doubling a cell's radius multiplies its surface by four and its volume by eight, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> falls as it grows, and that is why cells stay small.","questions":["A membrane can only carry so much traffic per square micrometre, so once a growing cell's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has fallen far enough it must either divide or die.","Flattening, elongating and folding the membrane are all ways of raising a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without shrinking, which is what lets some cells grow far past the usual limit."],"answer":"surface-area-to-volume ratio","answerText":"surface-area-to-volume ratio","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","science","biology","cell"],"abstract":"The <b>surface-area-to-volume ratio</b> is the amount of boundary a cell has for each unit of interior, and it is the arithmetic that keeps cells small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most cells approximate a sphere, whose surface goes as the square of the radius and whose volume goes as the cube, so doubling the radius multiplies the surface by four and the contents by eight and the ratio falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cube behaves the same way: one of a millimetre on a side has six square millimetres of face to one cubic millimetre of body, and doubling its edge takes that from six to one down to three to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything a cell takes in or throws out crosses that boundary, which is a selective barrier letting it concentrate what it gathers and expel what it does not want,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> so once the interior outgrows the membrane serving it the cell becomes less efficient the larger it gets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that point it either divides or dies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dividing is not the only answer, and the alternatives are all ways of buying back boundary: folding the membrane, flattening, drawing out into a long thin shape, or walling the interior into organelles that do their own work where they stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last of those is what a eukaryotic cell is, which is how it manages ten to a hundred micrometres where a bacterium is held to a tenth of a micrometre to five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rule bites hardest on the small end, where diffusion alone carries material across the whole cell almost at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a constraint rather than a fixed setting, and yeast selected in the laboratory for smallness shrank steadily through mutations in nutrient-sensing and cell-cycle pathways with very little loss of fitness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other end is stretched by a sulphur bacterium a centimetre long, which is possible only because almost all of that length is fluid-filled sac rather than working cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.2 Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Ana Garoña, Morgane V. Lemos, Andrea Giometto, and Marco Fumasoni, “Experimental Evolution of Cellular Miniaturization Reveals a Putative Mechanism for Cell Size Evolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 24 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2531280123. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does growing bigger make a cell less efficient?","a":"Because surface goes as the square of the radius and volume as the cube, so doubling the radius multiplies the boundary by four and the interior by eight, and everything must cross that boundary."},{"q":"Why is dividing not the only answer to the arithmetic?","a":"Because the alternatives all buy boundary back: folding the membrane, flattening, drawing out into a long thin shape, or walling the interior into organelles that work where they stand."},{"q":"Why can a bacterium be a centimetre long at all?","a":"Because almost all of that length is fluid-filled sac rather than working cytoplasm, so the interior actually served by the membrane stays small."}]},
{"id":"bio-098","num":98,"category":"Biology","question":"Because visible light has a wavelength of about half a micrometre, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot separate two details closer together than roughly 0.2 micrometres however much it magnifies.","questions":["Living cells can be watched under a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and are usually too transparent to show their parts, so they are stained, which generally kills them.","Enlarging an image beyond the point where a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can separate two adjacent structures adds size without adding detail, which is why resolution matters more than magnification."],"answer":"light microscope","answerText":"light microscope","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","science","biology","technology"],"abstract":"A <b>light microscope</b> passes visible light through a specimen and two sets of lenses, and it is the instrument almost all biology was done with before the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The compound form is the older idea, though a single well-ground lens long beat it, having fewer surfaces to spoil the image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two quantities describe it and only one matters much: magnification is how far the image is enlarged, resolving power the ability to show two adjacent structures as two rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No radiation can probe details much smaller than its own wavelength, and visible light runs from about 0.4 micrometres in the violet to 0.7 in the deep red, which sets a hard floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At best, with violet light and a wide aperture, that floor is just under 0.2 micrometres, a figure the instrument makers reached at the end of the 19th century and few factory microscopes match today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A bacterium or a mitochondrion is about 0.5 micrometres across, so those are roughly the smallest things whose shape can be made out at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enlarging past that point adds size and no information, which is why a teaching microscope stops around 400 times and an oil-immersion lens around 1,000,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> where a single 17th-century lens already reached 270 and sometimes 500 against the 20 of the magnifying glasses before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its great advantage is that it can be pointed at something alive, and its difficulty is that cells are largely transparent, so their parts have to be coloured with stains, which generally kills them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A permanent preparation is made by fixing the tissue first, usually with an aldehyde that cross-links proteins and locks everything in place, then cutting sections thin enough for light to pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The floor has since been got round rather than removed, and fluorescence methods that switch emission on and off now resolve structures far finer than 0.2 micrometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Hyunjeong Lee and Im Joo Rhyu, “Antonie van Leeuwenhoek and the Dawn of Microscopic Observation: A Narrative Review from Delft's Lens to the Modern Microscope,” <i>Applied Microscopy</i> 56, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1186/s42649-026-00123-z. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Robert Gaynes, “Antony van Leeuwenhoek, Pioneer of the Microscopic World,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 32, no. 6 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3206.260531. [Open access]","Wenxin Zhu et al., “Luminescent-Reaction-Enabled Super-Resolution Imaging,” <i>Nature</i> 657, no. 8130 (2026): 127–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10889-7. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does enlarging past a certain point add nothing?","a":"Because no radiation probes detail much smaller than its own wavelength, so beyond about 0.2 micrometres more magnification adds size and no information."},{"q":"Why must cells usually be killed to be looked at?","a":"Because they are largely transparent, so their parts have to be coloured with stains, which generally kills them, and a permanent preparation is fixed and sectioned first."},{"q":"Why is the wavelength floor no longer the end of the matter?","a":"Because it has been got round rather than removed: fluorescence methods that switch emission on and off resolve structures far finer than 0.2 micrometres."}]},
{"id":"bio-099","num":99,"category":"Biology","question":"Because an electron accelerated through 100,000 volts has a wavelength of 0.004 nanometres, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can resolve detail a hundred times finer than light allows.","questions":["Electrons travel properly only in a vacuum and the preparation kills the specimen, so nothing living has ever been watched through an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whatever it shows of the dead.","Sweeping a beam across a surface gives one kind of picture and driving it through a thin slice gives another, which are the two ways an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is ordinarily used."],"answer":"electron microscope","answerText":"electron microscope","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","science","biology","technology"],"abstract":"An <b>electron microscope</b> illuminates a specimen with a beam of electrons instead of light, and the reason for doing so is the same rule that limits every microscope: no radiation resolves detail much finer than its own wavelength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An electron's wavelength shortens as it speeds up, and one accelerated through 100,000 volts has a wavelength of 0.004 nanometres, which in theory would allow a resolution ten thousand times better than visible light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Practice falls well short of theory, because the aberrations of an electron lens are harder to correct than those of glass and only the very centre of the lens can be used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best modern instruments reach about 0.1 nanometres, and specimen preparation, contrast and radiation damage hold biological work to roughly 2 nanometres, which is still a hundred times better than a light microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are two common arrangements: a beam swept back and forth across a surface, which gives the shape of the outside, and a beam driven through a thin section, which shows what is inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The price is that nothing alive has ever been seen this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Electrons move properly only in a vacuum and the preparation kills the specimen, so every image is of something dead and treated, which is a standing caution when reading one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Freezing rather than fixing has changed that considerably, and cryo-electron tomography now images cells and tissue in something close to their native state, tilting the specimen through a series of angles and reconstructing the volume from the views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How finely to space those tilts is a real trade, since more views mean more information and also more radiation damage to the very thing being looked at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method has moved out of structural biology and into drug discovery, where proteins are seen at work inside cells rather than purified out of them,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> though a tomogram shows shapes and not names, so labelling strategies are needed to say which molecule is which.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts et al., “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Maarten Willem Tuijtel, Tomáš Majtner, Beata Turoňová, and Martin Beck, “Optimising the Tilt Increment for In Situ Cryo-Electron Tomography,” <i>eLife</i> 15 (2026), https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.111639. [Open access]","Camila M. Clemente, Juan-Carlos Mobarec, and Tanmay A. M. Bharat, “Applications and Prospects of Cryo-Electron Tomography in Drug Discovery and Understanding Disease,” <i>Current Opinion in Structural Biology</i> 98 (2026): 103283, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sbi.2026.103283. [Open access]","Yoon Ho Park, Rana Kim, Kun-Ho Song, and Hyun Suk Jung, “Labeling and Localization Strategies for In Situ Cryo-Electron Tomography across the Viral Life Cycle,” <i>Viruses</i> 18, no. 7 (2026): 790, https://doi.org/10.3390/v18070790. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a beam of electrons resolve more than light?","a":"Because an electron’s wavelength shortens as it speeds up, one accelerated through 100,000 volts having a wavelength of 0.004 nanometres."},{"q":"Why does practice fall so far short of that theory?","a":"Because the aberrations of an electron lens are harder to correct than those of glass and only the very centre can be used, with preparation, contrast and radiation damage holding biology to about 2 nanometres."},{"q":"Why is every electron micrograph a picture of something dead?","a":"Because electrons move properly only in a vacuum and the preparation kills the specimen, which is a standing caution when reading one."}]},
{"id":"bio-100","num":100,"category":"Biology","question":"Breaking cells open gently and then spinning the slurry at rising speeds to settle out nuclei, then mitochondria, then vesicles is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the standard way to study an organelle apart.","questions":["Because a nucleus is heavier than a mitochondrion and both outweigh a ribosome, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can separate them by spinning the same homogenate faster and faster in turn.","Studying what an organelle does chemically means having a great many of one kind and nothing else, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was invented to supply."],"answer":"cell fractionation","answerText":"cell fractionation","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["practice","science","biology","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Cell fractionation</b> is the business of taking a cell apart into its working components without stopping them working, so that a single kind of organelle can be studied on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is needed because microscopy alone cannot say what a component actually does, and a eukaryotic cell walls its different jobs into separate membrane-bound compartments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cells are broken open first, by osmotic shock, ultrasound, forcing through a narrow opening or grinding, which shatters the plasma membrane and the endoplasmic reticulum into fragments that reseal at once into small closed vesicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Done carefully the same treatment leaves nuclei, mitochondria, lysosomes and the rest largely whole, and what results is a thick slurry holding organelles of many sizes and densities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The medium the cells are broken in has to be found by trial and error for each organelle, and getting it right is what keeps the parts biochemically like themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The slurry is separated by spinning, which became possible only when the preparative ultracentrifuge was built commercially in the early 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Larger and denser things feel more force and settle faster, so a slow spin drops the nuclei into a pellet, a faster one the mitochondria, and a longer, faster one the sealed vesicles and then the ribosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No fraction comes out pure, and the usual remedy is to resuspend a pellet and spin it again several times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method was worked out largely in the 1940s and 1950s and is what made biochemistry of the cell possible, since asking what a mitochondrion does chemically means having a great many mitochondria and very little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its descendants ask the same question without a centrifuge, and mass spectrometry applied to one cell type at a time has found differences between astrocytes from different regions of the brain that a whole-tissue measurement would have averaged away,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while cryo-electron tomography looks at organelles where they stand, in a cell frozen rather than taken apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts et al., “Fractionation of Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26936/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Chien-Chang Huang, Chiung-Yun Chang, Po-Chao Chan, Weng Man Chong, Hsiao-Jen Chang, and Jung-Chi Liao, “Single Cell-Type Spatial Proteomics Uncovers Regional Heterogeneity of Astrocytes,” <i>Journal of Proteome Research</i> 25, no. 8 (2026): 3826–34, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jproteome.6c00321. [Open access]","Maarten Willem Tuijtel, Tomáš Majtner, Beata Turoňová, and Martin Beck, “Optimising the Tilt Increment for In Situ Cryo-Electron Tomography,” <i>eLife</i> 15 (2026), https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.111639. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is taking a cell apart necessary at all?","a":"Because microscopy alone cannot say what a component does, and asking what a mitochondrion does chemically means having a great many mitochondria and very little else."},{"q":"Why does spinning separate the organelles?","a":"Because larger and denser things feel more force and settle faster, so a slow spin drops the nuclei, a faster one the mitochondria, and a longer, faster one the vesicles and ribosomes."},{"q":"Why must the breaking medium be found by trial and error?","a":"Because getting it right for each organelle is what keeps the isolated parts biochemically like themselves rather than merely intact."}]},
{"id":"ru-001","num":1,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Grassland runs almost unbroken from the lower Danube to Manchuria across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the belt of open country that carried horses, herders and armies between Europe and China.","questions":["Low and seasonal rainfall keeps trees off the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so forest lies along its northern edge and desert and mountain shut it in on the south.","Iron Age herders left closely similar weapons, harness and animal ornament across more than 3,500 kilometres (2,200 miles) of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why it is read as one world rather than many."],"answer":"Eurasian Steppe","answerText":"Eurasian Steppe","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","geography","history","nature","prehistory"],"abstract":"The <b>Eurasian Steppe</b> is the belt of temperate grassland that crosses the middle of the continent, reaching from the plains of the lower Danube and Hungary in the west to Manchuria in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is open country because the rain is scarce and seasonal, too little for forest and enough for grass, so trees keep to the river valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Woodland and then taiga close it in on the north, while deserts and the mountain walls of the Caucasus, the Tian Shan and the Altai shut it off on the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its western third, the Pontic-Caspian steppe, runs from the southern Urals to the lands north of the Black Sea, and it is the part on which Russian history opens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The lower Volga and Don, inside that western third, is where the ancestors of every modern domestic horse were bred, and their descendants spread across Eurasia from about 2000 BCE with the spoke-wheeled chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the grass made possible was a life built on herds that moved and people who moved with them, and by the 1st millennium BCE it ran the whole length of the belt. Communities from the Altai to the Carpathians buried their dead under mounds with horses and gold, in weapons and ornament so alike across more than 3,500 kilometres (2,200 miles) that they are handled as one culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Greek writers who met the westernmost of them thought the arrangement unbeatable: a people with no cities and no forts, all mounted archers, carrying their houses on wagons, could neither be caught nor escaped from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The belt carried far more than herds, and confederations formed along it moved west again and again, though whether the Huns of Europe descended from the Xiongnu of Mongolia is still unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> None of the steppe peoples of these centuries wrote anything down, so nearly everything said about them comes either from their neighbours' books or out of the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Jürgen Dengler, Idoia Biurrun, Steffen Boch, Iwona Dembicz and Péter Török, “Grasslands of the Palaearctic Biogeographic Realm: Introduction and Synthesis,” in <i>Encyclopedia of the World's Biomes</i>, vol. 3 (Amsterdam: Elsevier, 2020), 617–37, http://www.grassland-restoration.eu/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Dengler-et-al-2020.pdf. [Open access]","Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Pablo Librado et al., “The Origins and Spread of Domestic Horses from the Western Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04018-9. [Open access]","Martina Unterländer et al., “Ancestry and Demography and Descendants of Iron Age Nomads of the Eurasian Steppe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 14615, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14615. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.46, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D46. [Open access]","Alexander Savelyev and Choongwon Jeong, “Early Nomads of the Eastern Steppe and Their Tentative Connections in the West,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e20, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.18. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Astana-steppe-7748.jpg","title":"The steppe of central Kazakhstan","desc":"Open grassland in Akmola province, north of Astana, photographed from a train. This is the middle section of the belt, between the Pontic-Caspian steppe in the west and the Mongolian steppe in the east.","credit":"Photograph by Vmenkov, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astana-steppe-7748.jpg","alt":"Flat grassland running to a low horizon under a pale sky, with no trees, buildings or hills in sight."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this belt open country rather than forest?","a":"Because the rain is scarce and seasonal, too little for trees and enough for grass, so woodland keeps to the river valleys."},{"q":"Why can weapons and ornament from 3,500 kilometres apart be handled as one culture?","a":"Because communities from the Altai to the Carpathians buried their dead under mounds with horses and gold in forms so alike across that distance."},{"q":"Why does nearly everything known about these peoples come from outside?","a":"Because none of them wrote anything down, so the record is their neighbours' books or what comes out of the ground."}]},
{"id":"ru-002","num":2,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Reaching from the southern Urals to the lower Danube, north of the Black Sea and the Caspian, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the western third of the Eurasian grassland belt.","questions":["The Volga, the Don and the Dnieper all run south across the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose grass carried the Srubnaya herders, then the Cimmerians, then the Scythians and finally the Sarmatians.","Ancient genomes from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show that its Bronze and Iron Age peoples were linked but that none of them was the direct ancestor of the group that followed."],"answer":"Pontic-Caspian steppe","answerText":"Pontic-Caspian steppe","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","geography","history","prehistory","migration"],"abstract":"The <b>Pontic-Caspian steppe</b> is the western third of the Eurasian grassland belt, reaching from the southern Urals to the lower Danube and lying north of the Black Sea and the Caspian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus, writing in the 5th century BCE from what travellers told him, called the country flat and grassy and well watered, with rivers running through it almost as many as the canals of Egypt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those rivers run south across the grass, and the greatest of them, the Dnieper, he thought the most productive water anywhere outside the Nile, with the finest pasture and the best fishing on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Along the northern edge the grass thins into forest-steppe, and it was there that Iron Age communities grew millet and lived in large fortified places rather than roaming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The lower Volga and Don, at the eastern end, is where the ancestors of all modern domestic horses were bred, from which they spread across Eurasia from about 2000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The steppe was held in turn by a sequence of peoples: the Srubnaya and Alakulskaya cultures from about 1800 BCE, the Cimmerians from about 1000, the Scythians from the 7th century, and the Sarmatians after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last succession ended around 400 CE, under the attack of the Goths and then the invasion of the Huns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sequence is not a family tree, and this is the most useful thing genetics has said about it: the four groups are linked, but none is a direct ancestor of the next, and each was mixed in its own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Movement out of these grasslands mattered as much as movement across them, and the Bronze Age migration of about 3000 BCE carried steppe ancestry deep into Europe, where it is still measurable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rus', the Golden Horde and Muscovy all formed at this end of the belt, so this is the ground on which the rest of the collection opens.","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.47, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D47. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.53, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D53. [Open access]","Alicia R. Ventresca Miller et al., “Re-evaluating Scythian Lifeways: Isotopic Analysis of Diet and Mobility in Iron Age Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0245996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245996. [Open access]","Pablo Librado et al., “The Origins and Spread of Domestic Horses from the Western Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04018-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Askania-Nova_steppe_fall_scape.jpg/1920px-Askania-Nova_steppe_fall_scape.jpg","title":"Feather grass in the Askania-Nova reserve","desc":"One of the last unploughed pieces of the Pontic steppe, in Kherson province in southern Ukraine. Almost all of the grassland described here is now under crops.","credit":"Photograph by NativePlanting, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Askania-Nova_steppe_fall_scape.jpg","alt":"Dry pale grass, waist high and going to seed, filling the lower half of the frame under an empty blue sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the northern edge of this grassland behave differently?","a":"Because there the grass thins into forest-steppe, and Iron Age communities grew millet and lived in large fortified places rather than roaming."},{"q":"Why is the sequence of peoples here not a family tree?","a":"Because genetics shows the four groups are linked but none is a direct ancestor of the next, and each was mixed in its own way."},{"q":"Why does movement out of this ground matter as much as movement across it?","a":"Because the Bronze Age migration of about 3000 BCE carried steppe ancestry deep into Europe, where it is still measurable."}]},
{"id":"ru-003","num":3,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Herds of sheep, cattle and horses moved between seasonal pastures, and the households moved with them, in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the grasslands north of the Black Sea supported for millennia.","questions":["Because a people practising <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had no cities and no forts and carried their dwellings on wagons, Herodotus thought them impossible either to catch or to escape from.","Isotopes and ancient proteins from Iron Age Ukraine have complicated the old picture of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there, showing millet farming, large settlements and people who mostly stayed put."],"answer":"pastoral nomadism","answerText":"pastoral nomadism","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","history","geography","prehistory","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Pastoral nomadism</b> is a way of living off herds of grazing animals by moving with them, so that a household follows the grass and the water instead of waiting on a harvest in one place. Herodotus thought the Scythian version of it unbeatable: a people with no cities and no forts, all mounted archers, living off their cattle and carrying their dwellings on wagons, could neither be caught nor escaped from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strabo describes the same arrangement four centuries later, with woollen tents fixed on the wagons, the herds spread round them, and the people passing their lives inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The horse is what made the steppe version of it work, and horses bred on the lower Volga and Don spread across Eurasia from about 2000 BCE, replacing almost every other local population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Proteins preserved in the dental calculus of Scythian-era people in Ukraine show that they drank the milk of ruminants and of horses alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The picture of an entire population permanently on the move is, for the western steppe at least, wrong. Isotopes from Iron Age cemeteries in Ukraine show most people living and dying near one place, in and around large settlements, and eating millet grown close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A handful of individuals in those same cemeteries had come a long way, which suits a society with wide trading connections better than one that moved as a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Herders and farmers were not separate worlds either, and Strabo says the steppe peoples let others work the land in return for a fixed and moderate tribute, going to war only when it was withheld.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That mixture of herds, some farming, tribute from settled neighbours and the ability to move faster than anyone else is what the Greek cities on the Black Sea met, and what later states on the same frontier met after them.","sources":["Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.46, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D46. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.17, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D17. [Open access]","Pablo Librado et al., “The Origins and Spread of Domestic Horses from the Western Eurasian Steppes,” <i>Nature</i> 598, no. 7882 (2021): 634–40, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04018-9. [Open access]","Jaruschka Pecnik et al., “Paleo-proteomic Analysis of Iron Age Dental Calculus Provides Direct Evidence of Scythian Reliance on Ruminant Dairy,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 1 (2026): e0339464, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0339464. [Open access]","Alicia R. Ventresca Miller et al., “Re-evaluating Scythian Lifeways: Isotopic Analysis of Diet and Mobility in Iron Age Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0245996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245996. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Mongolia%2C_horses_and_cowboys.jpg/1920px-Mongolia%2C_horses_and_cowboys.jpg","title":"Herders and their animals on the Mongolian steppe","desc":"A herd strung out along the grass at the eastern end of the same grassland belt. The practice is illustrated here because the western steppe it also fed has been under the plough for a century and more.","credit":"Photograph by Jeanne Menj, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongolia,_horses_and_cowboys.jpg","alt":"A long line of grazing animals and a mounted herder strung out across bare rolling grassland under a wide cloudy sky."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Herodotus think the arrangement unbeatable?","a":"Because a people with no cities and no forts, all mounted archers, living off their cattle and carrying their dwellings on wagons, could neither be caught nor escaped from."},{"q":"Why is the picture of a whole population on the move wrong for the western steppe?","a":"Because isotopes from Iron Age cemeteries in Ukraine show most people living and dying near one place, in and around large settlements, and eating millet grown close by."},{"q":"Why were herders and farmers not separate worlds?","a":"Because Strabo says the steppe peoples let others work the land in return for a fixed and moderate tribute, going to war only when it was withheld."}]},
{"id":"ru-004","num":4,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Assyrian kings recorded killing their chieftain and Herodotus recorded their flight, which makes the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the first steppe people north of the Black Sea whose name survives.","questions":["Pressed out of their grasslands by the Scythians, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went south into Asia Minor, and Sardis fell to them under Ardys, all but its citadel.","Because no single set of graves or metalwork can be matched to them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are far easier to find in other people's writing than in the ground."],"answer":"Cimmerians","answerText":"Cimmerians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the record</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 600 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Succeeded by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Scythians, from the 7th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","history","prehistory","warfare","migration"],"abstract":"The <b>Cimmerians</b> are the earliest people of the steppe north of the Black Sea whose name survives, appearing in the written record from about 1000 BCE and gone from it within four centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They left no uniform material culture behind them, so the name is far easier to find in other people's texts than in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Assyrian royal inscriptions know them as a danger from beyond the northern mountains, and Esarhaddon boasts of cutting down Teushpa the Cimmerian, 'a barbarian whose home was afar off', with all his troops in the land of Hubushna.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A generation later Gyges of Lydia sent Ashurbanipal two captured Cimmerian chieftains in irons, and when Gyges then backed Egypt against Assyria the Assyrian record says the Cimmerians he had beaten overran his whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herodotus tells it from the other end: pressed by the Scythians, the common people wanted to leave and the princes to fight, so the princes killed one another, were buried by the Dniester, and the rest went.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He adds that Cimmerian walls, a Cimmerian ferry, a district called Cimmeria and a strait of the same name were all still to be seen in Scythia in his own day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their move south was no retreat into obscurity, for while Ardys ruled at Sardis they took the city, all but its acropolis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Strabo has them and a branch called the Treres raiding Paphlagonia and Phrygia again and again, with a leader named Lygdamis taking Sardis and dying in Cilicia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Genomes from burials of the period on the western steppe show a varied population with connections running east, rather than one incoming people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much of the rest is history is hard to weigh, because everything written about them was written by their enemies, or by Greeks a long way off.","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), §530, https://archive.org/details/ancient_records_assyria2. [Open access]","Daniel David Luckenbill, <i>Ancient Records of Assyria and Babylonia</i>, vol. 2, <i>Historical Records of Assyria from Sargon to the End</i> (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1927), §§784–85, https://archive.org/details/ancient_records_assyria2. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.11, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D11. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.12, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D12. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 1.15, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D15. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 1.3.21, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D1%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D21. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Cimmerian_Stelae_Odessa.jpg","title":"Cimmerian stelae in the Odesa Archaeological Museum","desc":"Carved stone posts of about 900 to 800 BCE from the north Black Sea steppe, of the kind conventionally assigned to the Cimmerians. The attribution rests on date and region rather than on any inscription.","credit":"Photograph by Venzz, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cimmerian_Stelae_Odessa.jpg","alt":"A tall weathered stone slab, roughly rectangular and taller than a person, standing on a plinth in a museum gallery."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this people easier to find in texts than in the ground?","a":"Because they left no uniform material culture behind them, so the name survives in other people's writing rather than in an assemblage."},{"q":"Why is what is told of them hard to weigh?","a":"Because everything written about them was written by their enemies, or by Greeks a long way off."},{"q":"Why does the genetic evidence complicate the story of an incoming people?","a":"Because genomes from burials of the period on the western steppe show a varied population with connections running east rather than one arriving group."}]},
{"id":"ru-005","num":5,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Between about 700 and 300 BCE the grass from the Altai to the Carpathians belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who supplied the Greek world with its whole idea of the nomad.","questions":["Herodotus reports that the people the Greeks called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> named themselves Skoloti, and that their own origin story had gold implements fall burning from the sky.","Ancient genomes show that the eastern and western <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arose separately from one another, which is why their graves look so alike while the people in them do not."],"answer":"Scythians","answerText":"Scythians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Displaced by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Sarmatians, from the 3rd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["people","history","prehistory","warfare","art"],"abstract":"The <b>Scythians</b> are the Iron Age people who held the steppe from the Altai to the Carpathians between about 700 and 300 BCE, and who gave the Greek world its idea of the nomad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a Greek one: Herodotus says they called themselves Skoloti after a king, and that their own account of their origin had a plough, a yoke, a sword and a flask, all of gold, fall burning from the sky.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient genomes have taken the single people apart, showing eastern and western groups of independent origin, each a mixture of Bronze Age steppe herders and an eastern Asian component, with movement between them afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is why the weapons, harness and animal ornament look so alike over such distances while the populations beneath them do not, and why the eastern half of the steppe looks like the source of the western groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not all on the move either, since isotopes from cemeteries in Iron Age Ukraine show most people living and dying near one place, in large settlements, on millet grown close by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Their kings were another matter, and were buried out on the grass in a deep pit under a raised mound, with strangled servants, horses, golden cups and first-fruits of everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A year later, Herodotus says, fifty of the best young men and fifty horses were killed, set upright on stakes and stood in a ring about the tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also says that a warrior flayed the enemies he killed and covered his quiver with the skin taken from their right hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That was long read as travellers' slander, until protein analysis of 45 leather objects from graves in southern Ukraine turned up two samples of human skin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Their hold on the western steppe broke around 300 BCE, under pressure from the Macedonians on one side and the arriving Sarmatians on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.5–6, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D5. [Open access]","Martina Unterländer et al., “Ancestry and Demography and Descendants of Iron Age Nomads of the Eurasian Steppe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 14615, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14615. [Open access]","Alicia R. Ventresca Miller et al., “Re-evaluating Scythian Lifeways: Isotopic Analysis of Diet and Mobility in Iron Age Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 3 (2021): e0245996, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245996. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.71–72, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.64, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D64. [Open access]","Luise Ørsted Brandt, Meaghan Mackie, Marina Daragan, Matthew J. Collins and Margarita Gleba, “Human and Animal Skin Identified by Palaeoproteomics in Scythian Leather Objects from Ukraine,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 12 (2023): e0294129, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0294129. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Vas_d%27or_amb_representaci%C3%B3_d%27escites%2C_kurgan_de_Kul-Oba%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_IV_aC.JPG/1280px-Vas_d%27or_amb_representaci%C3%B3_d%27escites%2C_kurgan_de_Kul-Oba%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_IV_aC.JPG","title":"The gold vessel from the Kul-Oba barrow","desc":"Made in a Greek workshop on the Black Sea in the later 4th century BCE and buried in a Scythian tomb near Kerch. Its figures are the closest thing there is to a contemporary portrait of Scythian dress, hair and weapons.","credit":"Photograph by Joanbanjo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vas_d%27or_amb_representaci%C3%B3_d%27escites%2C_kurgan_de_Kul-Oba%2C_segona_meitat_del_segle_IV_aC.JPG","alt":"A round gold vessel with a flaring rim, its body worked in relief with bearded men in belted tunics and soft caps, one stringing a bow."},"why":[{"q":"Why do the objects look uniform when the people do not?","a":"Because ancient genomes show eastern and western groups of independent origin beneath one set of weapons, harness and animal ornament that travelled across the whole belt."},{"q":"Why was one of Herodotus's grimmest claims believed only recently?","a":"Because the flaying of enemies was long read as travellers' slander, until protein analysis of 45 leather objects from graves in southern Ukraine turned up two samples of human skin."},{"q":"Why do the royal burials sit oddly with the settlements?","a":"Because isotopes show most people living and dying near one place on millet grown close by, while kings were buried out on the grass under raised mounds with horses and gold."}]},
{"id":"ru-006","num":6,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Folded deer, crouching cats, birds of prey and coiled snakes, worked in gold and bronze across the whole steppe, are the repertoire of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the 1st millennium BCE.","questions":["On the earliest securely dated pieces, from a Tuvan royal mound of the late 9th century BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appears on horse gear and on almost nothing else.","Because the finest pieces in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were buried with the dead, what survives of it comes almost entirely out of tombs rather than out of settlements."],"answer":"animal style","answerText":"animal style","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tunnug 1, Tuva, late 9th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","history","prehistory","practice"],"abstract":"The Scytho-Siberian <b>animal style</b> is the ornament that ties the steppe's Iron Age material culture together: stylised deer, felines, birds of prey and snakes, sometimes combined into one creature, worked in gold, bronze, wood, bone and felt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not confined to treasure, and the same animals turn up on bridle fittings, weapons, clothing appliqués, vessels and rock art from the seventh to the 2nd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The images are not taken to be decoration alone, and are usually read as carrying meaning about the ordering of the world, about rank and about who the wearer was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest securely dated group of them comes from Tunnug 1, a royal mound raised in the Tuvan valley of the Uyuk in the late 9th century BCE, half a continent east of the Black Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There the style sits on horse equipment and on almost nothing else, and only four kinds of animal appear at all: sheep and goats, cats, birds of prey and serpents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That narrow beginning is worth knowing, because the tradition a reader meets later is a broad one, spread across nearly everything a wealthy household owned. Its uniformity is the striking part: weapons, harness and ornament in this manner are found over more than 3,500 kilometres (2,200 miles) of grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the west the type piece is the golden stag from the Kostromskaya barrow in the Kuban, its legs folded under it and its antlers laid back along its spine, found fixed to the centre of a round iron shield.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Outside the burial chamber there lay 22 horses arranged in pairs, and the whole timber structure had been daubed with clay and then burnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost all of this work survives because somebody buried it, and Herodotus says as much from the other side, reporting that a Scythian king went into the ground with golden cups and that his people used neither silver nor bronze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Timur Sadykov, Jegor Blochin, Sergey Khavrin and Gino Caspari, “Expanding the Corpus of the Earliest Scythian Animal-Style Artefacts,” <i>Antiquity</i> 99, no. 408 (2025): 1569–85, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2025.10214. [Open access]","Timur Sadykov et al., “A Spectral Cavalcade: Early Iron Age Horse Sacrifice at a Royal Tomb in Southern Siberia,” <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 402 (2024): 1538–57, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.145. [Open access]","Martina Unterländer et al., “Ancestry and Demography and Descendants of Iron Age Nomads of the Eurasian Steppe,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 14615, https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms14615. [Open access]","Ellis H. Minns, <i>Scythians and Greeks: A Survey of Ancient History and Archaeology on the North Coast of the Euxine from the Danube to the Caucasus</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), 224–26, https://archive.org/details/scythiansgreekss00minn. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.71, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Placa_en_forma_de_c%C3%A9rvol_tombat%2C_trobada_al_t%C3%BAmul_de_Kostromskoy_a_Kuban%2C_segle_VII_aC.JPG/1920px-Placa_en_forma_de_c%C3%A9rvol_tombat%2C_trobada_al_t%C3%BAmul_de_Kostromskoy_a_Kuban%2C_segle_VII_aC.JPG","title":"The golden stag from Kostromskaya","desc":"A gold plaque of the 7th century BCE from a barrow in the Kuban, found at the centre of an iron shield. The folded legs, the long muzzle and the antlers running back over the body are the standard vocabulary of the style.","credit":"Photograph by Joanbanjo, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Placa_en_forma_de_c%C3%A9rvol_tombat%2C_trobada_al_t%C3%BAmul_de_Kostromskoy_a_Kuban%2C_segle_VII_aC.JPG","alt":"A gold plaque of a stag lying with its legs tucked beneath it, muzzle stretched forward and a row of scrolled antler tines sweeping back along its spine."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the earliest form of the style worth knowing?","a":"Because at Tunnug 1 it sits on horse equipment and almost nothing else and uses only four kinds of animal, where the later tradition covers nearly everything a wealthy household owned."},{"q":"Why is this ornament not read as decoration alone?","a":"Because the images are usually taken to carry meaning about the ordering of the world, about rank and about who the wearer was."},{"q":"Why does almost all of it survive?","a":"Because somebody buried it, and Herodotus reports from the other side that a Scythian king went into the ground with golden cups."}]},
{"id":"ru-007","num":7,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Russian simply borrowed the local word for a barrow, so a burial mound anywhere on the steppe is called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and thousands of them still stand above the grass.","questions":["Herodotus says the mourners at a Scythian royal funeral competed to pile the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as high as they possibly could over the pit they had dug.","Because the herders who raised them left few settlements and little else standing, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is very nearly the whole visible archaeology of the grasslands."],"answer":"kurgan","answerText":"kurgan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Steppe use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3000 BCE onward</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Scythian kurgans</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 300 BCE</span></div>","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","archaeology","history","prehistory","practice"],"abstract":"A <b>kurgan</b> is a mound of earth heaped over a grave, the word being simply the Russian for a barrow, and it is the characteristic monument of the whole Eurasian grassland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burials of this kind run right across it, from the upper waters of the Ob to the Elbe and south as far as Thessaly and Anatolia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus describes how one was built for a Scythian king: a great four-cornered pit was dug, the body laid on a couch inside it, spears planted at either side, and planks and braided osiers laid across them for a roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Into the space left in the tomb went a strangled concubine, cupbearer, cook, groom, squire and messenger, together with horses, golden cups and first-fruits of everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A year afterwards, he says, fifty of the king's servants and fifty horses were killed, stuffed, mounted on stakes and stood upright in a ring around the mound before the mourners rode away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That ring of dead riders was for a long time the least believable thing in his account. Then a royal mound at Tunnug in Tuva, raised in the late 9th century BCE, gave up the remains of at least eighteen horses and one person laid around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Something similar had already been found in the Kuban at Kostromskaya, where 22 horses lay in pairs outside a timber chamber that had been daubed with clay and set on fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rite was not a Scythian invention and did not end with them, having been practised on these grasslands from the Bronze Age and still in use among steppe peoples of the 13th century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scale is what turns a grave into a monument, and Herodotus puts his finger on the reason when he says the mourners vied with one another to make the barrow as great as they could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Ellis H. Minns, <i>Scythians and Greeks: A Survey of Ancient History and Archaeology on the North Coast of the Euxine from the Danube to the Caucasus</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), 145, https://archive.org/details/scythiansgreekss00minn. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.71, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D71. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.72, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D72. [Open access]","Timur Sadykov et al., “A Spectral Cavalcade: Early Iron Age Horse Sacrifice at a Royal Tomb in Southern Siberia,” <i>Antiquity</i> 98, no. 402 (2024): 1538–57, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2024.145. [Open access]","Ellis H. Minns, <i>Scythians and Greeks: A Survey of Ancient History and Archaeology on the North Coast of the Euxine from the Danube to the Caucasus</i> (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1913), 224–26, https://archive.org/details/scythiansgreekss00minn. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Solokha_barrow._V_c._BC_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The Solokha barrow above the Dnieper","desc":"A Scythian royal mound of the 5th century BCE in southern Ukraine, standing well above ploughed fields. A mound of this size is visible from a long way off, which is a good part of what it was for.","credit":"Photograph by Валерий Дед, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solokha_barrow._V_c._BC_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A broad grass-covered mound rising out of an otherwise flat ploughed field, with a track curving past its foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the least believable part of Herodotus's account gain support?","a":"Because a royal mound at Tunnug gave up at least eighteen horses and one person laid around it, and at Kostromskaya 22 horses lay in pairs outside a burnt timber chamber."},{"q":"Why does scale turn a grave into a monument?","a":"Because the mourners vied with one another to make the barrow as great as they could, which is Herodotus's own explanation of the size."},{"q":"Why is the rite not a Scythian invention?","a":"Because it had been practised on these grasslands since the Bronze Age and was still in use among steppe peoples of the 13th century CE."}]},
{"id":"ru-008","num":8,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Iranian-speaking horse peoples pushed the Scythians off the grass north of the Black Sea, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> then held it from about 300 BCE until roughly 400 CE.","questions":["Two of the many groups gathered under the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the Iazyges on the Danube and the Roxolani, who grazed the plains between the Don and the Dnieper.","In 175 CE Marcus Aurelius drafted defeated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cavalry into his legions and sent five and a half thousand of them to Britain, a Roman historian records."],"answer":"Sarmatians","answerText":"Sarmatians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE – 400 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended with</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Goths and the Huns, c. 400 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","history","warfare","migration","prehistory"],"abstract":"The <b>Sarmatians</b> are the Iranian-speaking horse peoples who took the steppe north of the Black Sea over from the Scythians and held it from about the 3rd century BCE until roughly 400 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were never one nation but a set of groups of similar background, and Strabo names the Iazyges on the Danube and the Roxolani grazing the plains between the Don and the Dnieper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He describes them living in woollen tents fixed on their wagons with the herds spread about, wearing helmets and breastplates of untanned ox-hide, and carrying wicker shields, lances, bows and swords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also records what that equipment was worth against a phalanx: fifty thousand of them, fighting as allies of a Crimean king, were almost all cut down by six thousand men under Diophantus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herodotus, writing of an earlier people he calls the Sauromatae, says their women rode out hunting with the men or without them, went to war, and dressed as the men dressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He adds that no girl among them married until she had killed an enemy, and that some grew old unmarried because they never managed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Rome came to know them well, and by the 2nd century CE they were the most politically weighty force along the empire's eastern edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far they were a genuinely new population is still open, though the genomes read so far resemble Bronze Age groups of the southern Urals more than the Scythians they displaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Individuals could travel extraordinary distances inside the empire: a man buried at a farmstead in what is now Cambridgeshire between 126 and 228 CE carried Sarmatian-related ancestry and had grown up abroad, which fits the 5,500 Sarmatian cavalry that Marcus Aurelius sent to Britain in 175.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their own dominance ended around 400 CE, broken by the Goths and then by the Huns arriving from the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Maja Krzewińska et al., “Ancient Genomes Suggest the Eastern Pontic-Caspian Steppe as the Source of Western Iron Age Nomads,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 10 (2018): eaat4457, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6223350/. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.17, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D17. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.116, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D116. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.117, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D117. [Open access]","Marina Silva et al., “An Individual with Sarmatian-Related Ancestry in Roman Britain,” <i>Current Biology</i> 34, no. 1 (2024): 204–12.e6, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2023.11.049. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/028_Conrad_Cichorius%2C_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule%2C_Tafel_XXVIII_%28Ausschnitt_01%29.jpg","title":"Armoured cavalry on Trajan's Column","desc":"A detail from the 1896 plates of the column in Rome, showing scale-armoured horsemen breaking before Roman riders. Horsemen of this kind are conventionally identified as Sarmatian Roxolani, whose horses as well as their men were armoured.","credit":"Plate by Conrad Cichorius, 1896, after the column attributed to Apollodorus of Damascus, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:028_Conrad_Cichorius,_Die_Reliefs_der_Traianss%C3%A4ule,_Tafel_XXVIII_%28Ausschnitt_01%29.jpg","alt":"A carved relief of horsemen whose riders and mounts are both covered in overlapping scales, tumbling away to the right as Roman cavalry ride them down."},"why":[{"q":"Why is this name not that of a nation?","a":"Because it covers a set of groups of similar background, Strabo naming the Iazyges on the Danube and the Roxolani between the Don and the Dnieper."},{"q":"Why does one British burial bear on their history?","a":"Because a man buried in Cambridgeshire between 126 and 228 CE carried Sarmatian-related ancestry and had grown up abroad, which fits the 5,500 cavalry Marcus Aurelius sent to Britain in 175."},{"q":"Why is it open whether they were a new population at all?","a":"Because the genomes read so far resemble Bronze Age groups of the southern Urals more than the Scythians they displaced."}]},
{"id":"ru-009","num":9,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Settlers from Miletus built <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the joined estuary of the Bug and the Dnieper, and Strabo still calls it a great trading centre four centuries later.","questions":["Herodotus knew the biggest Greek city of the northern Black Sea as the trading post of the Borysthenites, though its own people called it <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and said they were Milesians.","The Scythian king Scyles kept a fine house inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, changing out of his Scythian clothes at the gate, and lost his throne when his people found out."],"answer":"Olbia","answerText":"Olbia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 BCE, from Miletus</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Berezan pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 6th centuries BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["city","history","greece","ukraine","geography"],"abstract":"<b>Olbia</b> stood at the joined estuary of the Bug and the Dnieper in what is now southern Ukraine, and Strabo, writing long after its founding, still describes it as a great trading centre and a foundation of the Milesians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Herodotus knew it as the trading post of the Borysthenites, who told him that they were Milesians themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was one of a ring of such places: on the island of Berezan close by lay a fishing village whose pottery belongs to the seventh and 6th centuries BCE and which may be older than the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eastward along the coast came Chersonesus, then Theodosia, Nymphaeum and Panticapaeum, with more settlements across the strait, so that Greek ports ran from the Danube to the Sea of Azov.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ships reached them either by working along the western shore or by crossing open water from Sinope, Amisos and Heracleia to the Crimean coast, which is visible from Asia in clear weather.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the cities were there for was trade, and the Greeks had been taking grain out of this country, and salted fish out of the Sea of Azov, since early times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That put a few thousand townsmen among peoples far more numerous, and the arrangement lasted because it paid both sides. How awkward it could be shows in the story of Scyles, a Scythian king with a Greek mother who kept a costly house in the city and, on each visit, left his army in the suburb, shut the gates and lived, dressed and worshipped as a Greek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His own leading men were taken up a tower to watch him celebrating the rites of Dionysus, and the kingship was lost to him over it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Greek written in these ports is the reason so much of what is known about the early steppe was recorded at all, and the reason all of it was recorded by outsiders.","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.3.17, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D3%3Asection%3D17. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.78, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D78. [Open access]","M. Rostovtzeff, <i>Iranians and Greeks in South Russia</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), 63–64, https://archive.org/details/iraniansgreeksin00rostuoft. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]","Herodotus, <i>The Histories</i> 4.79, trans. A. D. Godley (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1920), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0126%3Abook%3D4%3Achapter%3D79. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/AncientTownOlvia.jpg","title":"The site of Olbia above the Bug estuary","desc":"Excavated house walls and streets on the terrace above the water, in Mykolaiv province, Ukraine. The estuary in the background is the reason the city stood here.","credit":"Photograph by Ivengo, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AncientTownOlvia.jpg","alt":"Low stone foundations of streets and small rooms laid bare on a grassy headland, with a wide estuary and a far shore behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a few thousand Greeks manage to live among far more numerous peoples?","a":"Because the arrangement paid both sides: the cities existed for trade, taking grain out of the country and salted fish out of the Sea of Azov, and it lasted as long as that exchange held."},{"q":"Why does the story of Scyles show how awkward the arrangement was?","a":"Because a Scythian king had to leave his army in the suburb and shut the gates to live as a Greek inside the city, and when his leading men were taken up a tower to watch him at the rites of Dionysus he lost the kingship over it."},{"q":"Why is what we know of the early steppe knowledge of a particular kind?","a":"Because the Greek written in these ports is the reason any of it was recorded at all, which means all of it was recorded by outsiders looking in."}]},
{"id":"ru-010","num":10,"category":"Before Rus'","question":"Straddling the strait between the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov, with its capital at Panticapaeum, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew rich on the grain trade with Greece.","questions":["Demosthenes told an Athenian jury that the corn reaching the city from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to about four hundred thousand measures a year, free of the usual export toll.","After a dynasty of 42 years, the throne of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> passed in 438 BCE to Spartocus, whose successors bore Thracian and Greek names alike."],"answer":"Bosporan Kingdom","answerText":"Bosporan Kingdom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Archaeanactids</span><span class=\"dt-v\">42 years, to 438 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spartocids</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 438 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["state","history","greece","geography","trade"],"abstract":"The <b>Bosporan Kingdom</b> was the state that grew up on both shores of the Cimmerian Bosporus, the strait now called Kerch, with its capital at Panticapaeum, a Milesian foundation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Diodorus records that a line called the Archaeanactidae ruled there for 42 years before the kingship passed to Spartocus, who reigned seven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The year was 438 BCE, and the Spartocids who followed bore Thracian and Greek names side by side, Pairisades and Spartocos beside Leucon and Satyros, styling themselves archons rather than kings over the Greek cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Strabo, who calls them tyrants, allows that most of them governed moderately and justly from the time of Pairisades and Leucon onward, and says one of the Pairisades was reckoned a god.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What made the kingdom matter far beyond its own coast, though, was neither its rulers nor its harbours but the corn grown on the plains behind them and shipped out through Theodosia and Panticapaeum. <br><br> Demosthenes told an Athenian jury that the grain reaching the city from the Black Sea equalled everything imported from every other source put together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He gave the yearly figure as about four hundred thousand measures out of the Bosporus, checkable against the books of the grain commissioners, and said Leucon had waived his thirtieth on cargoes bound for Athens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Strabo reports a still larger consignment, two million one hundred thousand measures sent to the Athenians out of Theodosia alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The land could bear it, since he says these plains yielded thirtyfold under the most ordinary ploughing, and that the tribute later paid to Mithridates ran to 180,000 measures of corn and 200 talents of silver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The last of the Pairisades, unable to meet the heavy tribute the steppe peoples were demanding of him, handed the kingdom to Mithridates, and after him it passed under Rome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.4, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D4. [Open access]","Diodorus Siculus, <i>Library of History</i> 12.31.1, trans. C. H. Oldfather, Loeb Classical Library (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1946), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0084%3Abook%3D12%3Achapter%3D31%3Asection%3D1. [Open access]","M. Rostovtzeff, <i>Iranians and Greeks in South Russia</i> (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1922), 67–68, https://archive.org/details/iraniansgreeksin00rostuoft. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Leptines</i> 31, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0072%3Aspeech%3D20%3Asection%3D31. [Open access]","Demosthenes, <i>Against Leptines</i> 32, trans. C. A. Vince and J. H. Vince (Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Press, 1926), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0072%3Aspeech%3D20%3Asection%3D32. [Open access]","Strabo, <i>Geography</i> 7.4.6, trans. H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer (London: George Bell and Sons, 1903), https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0239%3Abook%3D7%3Achapter%3D4%3Asection%3D6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Panticapaeum_01.jpg/1920px-Panticapaeum_01.jpg","title":"The ruins of Panticapaeum above Kerch","desc":"Foundations on the slope of Mount Mithridat, the acropolis hill of the Bosporan capital, with the modern city of Kerch below. The strait the kingdom straddled lies just beyond.","credit":"Photograph by Dmytro Petishkin, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panticapaeum_01.jpg","alt":"Grassy terraces scattered with cut stone blocks and wall footings on a hillside, with roofs and trees of a modern town spreading across the valley below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did this kingdom matter far beyond its own coast?","a":"Because of neither its rulers nor its harbours but the corn grown on the plains behind them, shipped out through Theodosia and Panticapaeum in quantities Athens depended on."},{"q":"Why could Demosthenes expect a jury to accept his grain figure?","a":"Because he gave about four hundred thousand measures a year out of the Bosporus as a figure checkable against the books of the grain commissioners, and added that Leucon had waived his thirtieth on cargoes bound for Athens."},{"q":"Why is such a yield credible from these plains at all?","a":"Because Strabo says they returned thirtyfold under the most ordinary ploughing, which is also why the later tribute to Mithridates could be set at 180,000 measures of corn and 200 talents of silver."}]},
{"id":"ww2-001","num":"1","category":"World War II · History","question":"Fighting began in Asia in July 1937 and in Europe in September 1939, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no single start date that every combatant nation would recognise.","questions":["Germany's pursuit of living space in the east and Japan's of an empire in East Asia gave the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two separate beginnings, in different years and different continents.","Around 60 million people died in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them civilians, and the estimates still vary by millions between one authority and another."],"answer":"Second World War","answerText":"Second World War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Asia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 7 July 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1 September 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 May and 2 September 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 60 million, estimates vary widely</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Second World War</b> was the largest armed conflict in history, fought between the Axis powers led by Germany, Italy and Japan and a coalition that came to include Britain, France, the Soviet Union, China and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had no single beginning: Japan invaded China proper on 7 July 1937, opening the war in Asia, while Germany's attack on Poland on 1 September 1939 opened it in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain and France, having guaranteed Poland's borders that spring, declared war on 3 September, and the Soviet Union and the United States were both brought in during 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Germany fought for what its leadership called living space in eastern Europe, and Japan for rule and influence throughout East Asia and the Pacific; neither aim could be had without conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting closed in Europe with the unconditional German surrender of 7 and 9 May 1945, and in Asia with Japan's formal surrender on 2 September that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its origins are conventionally traced back past the 1930s to the peace settlement of 1919 and 1920, which dismantled four empires and redrew the map of Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That settlement also barred Austria from union with Germany, a prohibition the Austrian-born Adolf Hitler and his National Socialist party made an active aim to overturn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler set out the eastern war he intended to the German army's most senior officers on 3 February 1933, four days after his appointment as chancellor, with a war against Poland as its precondition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan's road ran through the seizure of Manchuria in September 1931 and the clash at the Marco Polo Bridge in July 1937, after which the United States shifted by stages from aid for China to confrontation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The cost is the one figure no account omits: something near 60 million dead, most of them civilians, and national totals are still published as ranges differing by millions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","warfare","20th century","europe","asia","germany","japan"],"sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War II: In Depth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-in-depth. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War II Dates and Timeline,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, last edited 15 November 2021, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-ii-key-dates. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War I: Treaties and Reparations,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-i-treaties-and-reparations. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Zweite Weltkrieg,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/der-zweite-weltkrieg. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1937–1945</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","The National WWII Museum, \"Research Starters: Worldwide Deaths in World War II,\" https://www.nationalww2museum.org/students-teachers/student-resources/research-starters/research-starters-worldwide-deaths-world-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Mamoru_Shigemitsu_signs_the_Instrument_of_Surrender%2C_officially_ending_the_Second_World_War.jpg/1920px-Mamoru_Shigemitsu_signs_the_Instrument_of_Surrender%2C_officially_ending_the_Second_World_War.jpg","title":"The Japanese surrender, 2 September 1945","desc":"Japanese Foreign Minister Mamoru Shigemitsu signs the Instrument of Surrender aboard the USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay, the last of the war's surrenders and the formal end of the fighting.","credit":"Photograph by Lieutenant Stephen E. Korpanty, US Army Signal Corps; restored by Adam Cuerden. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mamoru_Shigemitsu_signs_the_Instrument_of_Surrender,_officially_ending_the_Second_World_War.jpg","alt":"A man in a dark morning coat and striped trousers leans over a cloth-covered table on a warship's deck to sign a document, watched by an American general and ranks of Allied officers."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the war have no single starting date?","a":"Because Japan invaded China proper on 7 July 1937, opening the war in Asia, while Germany’s attack on Poland on 1 September 1939 opened it in Europe, and the Soviet Union and United States entered only in 1941."},{"q":"Why could neither Axis aim be pursued without conquest?","a":"Because Germany fought for what its leadership called living space in eastern Europe and Japan for rule throughout East Asia and the Pacific, and both meant taking territory other states held."},{"q":"Why are the casualty figures still published as ranges?","a":"Because the total is near 60 million, most of them civilians, and national totals differ by millions between the counts that are made."}]},
{"id":"ww2-002","num":"2","category":"World War II · History","question":"After more than four years of continuous fighting the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> silenced the guns of the Western Front at eleven in the morning, though it suspended the fighting rather than ending the war.","questions":["Germany gave up 5,000 guns, its submarines and the left bank of the Rhine under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ran for thirty days at a time and left the blockade in place.","A civilian secretary of state signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for a republic two days old, on behalf of the same high command that had demanded it in September."],"answer":"Armistice of 11 November 1918","answerText":"Armistice of 11 November 1918","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 November 1918, 5 o'clock</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Guns silent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 a.m. the same day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 days, renewable</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Armistice of 11 November 1918</b> ended the fighting of the First World War: the guns of the Western Front fell silent at eleven that morning, after more than four years of continuous war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German high command demanded it, not the politicians who signed it: on 29 September 1918 the Supreme Army Command told the government its position was militarily hopeless and required negotiations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A parliamentary government was formed under Prince Max von Baden, and the October reforms of 28 October turned a constitutional monarchy into a parliamentary one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Revolution then overtook the negotiations: the Bavarian monarchy fell on 7 November, and on 9 November Max von Baden announced the Kaiser's abdication without authorisation, handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert, and Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from the Reichstag that afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Matthias Erzberger, a civilian secretary of state, signed at Compiègne at five o'clock French time for both the Supreme Command and the new government, opposite Foch and Wemyss for the Allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> An armistice suspends fighting rather than concluding a war: this one ran for thirty days with an option to extend, and either side could denounce it on forty-eight hours' notice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its terms disarmed Germany where it stood: 5,000 guns, 25,000 machine guns and 1,700 aircraft surrendered, the submarines given up and the principal warships interned, the left bank of the Rhine evacuated and Allied bridgeheads planted at Mainz, Koblenz and Cologne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Germany renounced the treaties of Brest-Litovsk and Bucharest, and the Allied naval blockade stayed in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What no armistice could supply was the experience of defeat: the fighting had never reached German soil and German armies still held large tracts of eastern Europe, which left room for conspiracy theories blaming the collapse on the home front rather than on the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those theories became a weapon against the men who had signed, and a right-wing terrorist group murdered Erzberger in 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","warfare","politics","20th century","europe","germany","france"],"sources":["Australian War Memorial, \"Remembrance Day,\" https://www.awm.gov.au/commemoration/remembrance. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Der Erste Weltkrieg,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/erster-weltkrieg. [Open access]","Boris Barth, \"Stab-in-the-back Myth,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/stab-in-the-back-myth/. [Open access]","Alexander Gallus, \"Revolutions (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-germany/. [Open access]","Christopher Dowe, \"Erzberger, Matthias,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/erzberger-matthias/. [Open access]","\"The Special Representative in France House to the Secretary of State, Paris, November 11, 1918,\" document 420 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1918, Supplement 1, The World War, Volume I</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1933), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1918Supp01v01/d420. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Foch_train.jpg/1920px-Foch_train.jpg","title":"The carriage at Compiègne","desc":"Wagons-Lits dining car No. 2419 D, in which the negotiations were held and the armistice signed at five in the morning on 11 November 1918.","credit":"US Army Signal Corps, from the US Army Military History Institute collection. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foch_train.jpg","alt":"A dark railway dining car lettered Dining-Car and numbered 2419 D stands at a platform beside a brick building, with a second carriage coupled behind it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the army rather than the politicians demand it?","a":"Because on 29 September 1918 the Supreme Army Command told the government its position was militarily hopeless and required negotiations, and a parliamentary government was formed to conduct them."},{"q":"Why is an armistice not the same as a peace?","a":"Because it suspends fighting rather than concluding a war: this one ran thirty days with an option to extend, and either side could denounce it on forty-eight hours’ notice."},{"q":"Why did the terms leave room for a legend of betrayal?","a":"Because the fighting had never reached German soil and German armies still held large tracts of eastern Europe, so no armistice could supply the experience of defeat."}]},
{"id":"ww2-003","num":"3","category":"World War II · History","question":"Thirty-two countries sent delegations but the plenary met only nine times, and the real decisions at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were taken by a council of four.","questions":["Neither Germany nor Bolshevik Russia was invited to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the German delegation reached Paris only at the end of April, to be handed a finished draft.","Poincaré opened the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at three in the afternoon on 18 January 1919, the anniversary, by accident, of the German Empire's proclamation at Versailles."],"answer":"Paris Peace Conference","answerText":"Paris Peace Conference","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 January 1919 – 21 January 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Delegations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">32 countries</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plenary met</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 times</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, from Versailles to Sèvres</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Paris Peace Conference</b> was the gathering at which the victors of the First World War wrote the peace, running from 18 January 1919 to 21 January 1920, longer than the war it was settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Raymond Poincaré opened it at three in the afternoon in the Peace Rooms of the French foreign ministry, before delegates of the allied and associated belligerents and of states that had broken off relations with the enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The date fell on the anniversary of the proclamation of the German Empire in the Hall of Mirrors in 1871, which was not deliberate — the Italian delegates had arrived late — though it gave the opening speech its theme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thirty-two countries were represented, but the plenary met only nine times and the smaller powers had a formal part in it and no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Business began in a Council of Ten — the heads of government and foreign ministers of the United States, Britain, France and Italy, with two Japanese representatives — which met seventy-two times and set up fifty-eight subcommittees without reaching conclusions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> From March 1919 it split: a Council of Four of Wilson, Lloyd George, Clemenceau and Orlando took the decisions, while their foreign ministers formed a Council of Five and did the detailed work on Europe's frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The conference had opened with no agreed agenda, so its expert commissions drafted maximum demands on the assumption that the Germans would be negotiated with afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not: the victors, convinced of German guilt and afraid that argument would divide them, refused to meet the German delegation, which was invited only at the end of April and handed a finished draft on 7 May.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bolshevik Russia was not invited at all, its government unrecognised, and the defeated Central Powers were excluded with Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Five treaties came out of the conference — Versailles, Saint-Germain, Neuilly, Trianon and Sèvres — and a sixth, Lausanne in 1923, replaced Sèvres after the only real negotiation with a former enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","politics","20th century","europe","france","germany","institution"],"sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","\"Protocol No. 1, Session of January 18, 1919,\" document 3 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d3. [Open access]","\"Organization of the Conference, October 1, 1919,\" document 2 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d2. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Big_four.jpg/1920px-Big_four.jpg","title":"The Council of Four, 27 May 1919","desc":"Lloyd George and Orlando talking at the left, Clemenceau at the centre and Wilson at the right — the four heads of government who took the conference's decisions from March 1919.","credit":"Photograph by Edward N. Jackson, US Army Signal Corps. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_four.jpg","alt":"Four elderly men in dark suits stand on the stone steps of a building; two on the left are talking, one gesturing, while the other two stand apart looking towards them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the smaller powers count for so little?","a":"Because the plenary met only nine times, and business was done first in a Council of Ten and then in a Council of Four that took the decisions."},{"q":"Why were the German delegates never negotiated with?","a":"Because the victors, convinced of German guilt and afraid that argument would divide them, refused to meet them and handed over a finished draft on 7 May."},{"q":"Why did the expert commissions draft maximum demands?","a":"Because the conference opened with no agreed agenda and they worked on the assumption that the Germans would be negotiated with afterwards."}]},
{"id":"ww2-004","num":"4","category":"World War II · History","question":"Germany's army was capped at 100,000 men, conscription abolished and an air force forbidden outright, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left it able to keep order at home and little else.","questions":["Hermann Müller and Johannes Bell signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the Hall of Mirrors on 28 June 1919, a cabinet having resigned rather than put its name to it.","Running to 440 articles in fifteen parts and opening with the Covenant of the League of Nations, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came into force on 10 January 1920."],"answer":"Treaty of Versailles","answerText":"Treaty of Versailles","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1919, at Versailles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">440 articles in 15 parts</span><span class=\"dt-k\">German army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">capped at 100,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Versailles</b> was the peace imposed on Germany at the end of the First World War, running to 440 articles in fifteen parts and signed on 28 June 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German delegation received the draft on 7 May and was allowed to answer only in writing, an exchange of notes that changed little; the speech its foreign minister made at the handover so angered the Allies that they sharpened the war-guilt wording instead of softening it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Faced with an ultimatum, the National Assembly approved signature only just before it expired, the cabinet resigned over the question, and Brockdorff-Rantzau would not sign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hermann Müller and Johannes Bell went instead, and in the Galerie des Glaces at three in the afternoon, with Clemenceau presiding, the Germans signed first and the Allied and Associated powers after them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was ratified by the National Assembly on 9 July and came into force on 10 January 1920, once France, Britain and Italy had ratified too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Part I was the Covenant of the League of Nations, which the defeated could not at first join, and Part XIII created the International Labour Organization on the argument that peace could rest only on social justice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The military clauses were built to leave Germany unable to attack anyone: an army of 100,000 men including 4,000 officers, conscription abolished, submarines and any air force forbidden, the navy cut to six battleships.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No fortifications, troops or manoeuvres were allowed on the left bank of the Rhine or within 50 kilometres (30 miles) east of it, and a breach was to count as a hostile act.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Germany lost about an eighth of its territory and a tenth of its people, its colonies passing to the victors as League mandates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost nobody in Germany accepted it: most called it a dictated peace of shame, no political camp granted it legitimacy, and the national right made the fight against the fetters of Versailles a question of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["event","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany","warfare"],"sources":["Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","\"Protocol No. 2, Plenary Session of June 28, 1919,\" document 12 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i> (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1943), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d12. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part V, arts. 160, 173, 181, 191 and 198, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partv.asp. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part III, arts. 42–44, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiii.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Weimarer Republik,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Treaty_of_Versailles_Signing%2C_Hall_of_Mirrors.jpg/1920px-Treaty_of_Versailles_Signing%2C_Hall_of_Mirrors.jpg","title":"The signing in the Hall of Mirrors, 28 June 1919","desc":"The delegations and spectators packed into the Galerie des Glaces at Versailles for the signature, in the room where the German Empire had been proclaimed in 1871.","credit":"Photograph by Helen Johns Kirtland and Lucian Swift Kirtland, US National Archives. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Versailles_Signing,_Hall_of_Mirrors.jpg","alt":"A long mirrored and vaulted gallery crowded from end to end with hundreds of men in dark suits, standing and seated around tables in the middle distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the war-guilt wording end up harder rather than softer?","a":"Because the speech the German foreign minister made at the handover so angered the Allies that they sharpened it instead."},{"q":"Why were the military clauses shaped as they were?","a":"Because they were built to leave Germany unable to attack anyone: 100,000 men, no conscription, no submarines or air force, and no fortifications or troops within 50 kilometres east of the Rhine."},{"q":"Why did no German political camp grant it legitimacy?","a":"Because most called it a dictated peace of shame, the country having lost about an eighth of its territory and a tenth of its people without negotiating any of it."}]},
{"id":"ww2-005","num":"5","category":"World War II · History","question":"Known in Germany as the war guilt clause, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Treaty of Versailles nowhere mentions guilt, and the article following it concedes that Germany cannot pay in full.","questions":["Reparations needed a legal foundation, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made Germany accept responsibility for the loss and damage its aggression had caused, and the next article conceded that its resources could not cover the whole.","The clause blamed for humiliating Germany is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the treaty's actual moral charge is in Part VII, which arraigns Wilhelm II for an offence against international morality."],"answer":"Article 231","answerText":"Article 231","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Place in treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Part VIII, article 231</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Article 231</b> opens the reparations part of the Treaty of Versailles, and in it the Allied and Associated Governments affirm and Germany accepts responsibility for the loss and damage caused by a war imposed through German and allied aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It became known in Germany as the war guilt clause, although the word guilt appears nowhere in it, nor anywhere else in Part VIII.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its work was legal rather than moral: it established liability, and the article immediately after it concedes that German resources were not adequate to make complete reparation for all such loss and damage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That wording settled an argument among the victors over whether Germany owed only for the illegal invasion of Belgium and the occupied French departments, or for the whole cost of the war down to the pensions of veterans and their dependants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sharper accusation the Germans actually received came in the covering note of 16 June 1919, which is not part of the treaty and appears nowhere in its text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The treaty's genuine moral charge sits in Part VII, whose article 227 publicly arraigns Wilhelm II for a supreme offence against international morality and the sanctity of treaties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German foreign minister's speech at the handover of the draft so angered the Allies that they hardened the responsibility wording rather than softening it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No political camp in Germany granted the clause any legitimacy, revising the treaty became the fixed aim of Weimar foreign policy, and the struggle against it was the one domestic theme that united every social and political group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most Germans rejected the settlement as a dictated peace of shame, and the national right made the fight against the fetters of Versailles a question of honour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> For the Allies the clause was the foundation of a bill; for Germans it was a verdict passed upon them, and it was as a verdict that they refused it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","tags":["text","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany"],"sources":["<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part VIII, arts. 231–232, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partviii.asp. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part VII, art. 227, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partvii.asp. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Weimarer Republik,\" <i>LeMO — Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/28._Juni_1919%2C_Weimar_Markt%2C_Stra%C3%9Fenbahn_Weimar_h%C3%A4lt_wegen_der_Demo_gegen_den_Versailler_Vertrag.jpg/1280px-28._Juni_1919%2C_Weimar_Markt%2C_Stra%C3%9Fenbahn_Weimar_h%C3%A4lt_wegen_der_Demo_gegen_den_Versailler_Vertrag.jpg","title":"Weimar, 28 June 1919","desc":"The market square filled by a demonstration against the treaty on the day it was signed, with a tram halted in the middle of the crowd.","credit":"Stadtmuseum Weimar. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:28._Juni_1919,_Weimar_Markt,_Straßenbahn_Weimar_hält_wegen_der_Demo_gegen_den_Versailler_Vertrag.jpg","alt":"A town square packed shoulder to shoulder with a crowd in hats, seen from above between tall old buildings, with a tram stopped among them."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the name it acquired in Germany misleading?","a":"Because the word guilt appears nowhere in the article or in the rest of Part VIII: its work was legal, establishing liability for loss and damage."},{"q":"Why did the wording have to settle an argument among the victors?","a":"Because they disagreed over whether Germany owed only for the illegal invasion of Belgium and occupied France, or for the whole cost of the war down to veterans’ pensions."},{"q":"Why did Germans read it as a verdict rather than a bill?","a":"Because for the Allies it was the foundation of a claim, and it was as a moral judgement passed upon them that no political camp in Germany granted it legitimacy."}]},
{"id":"ww2-006","num":"6","category":"World War II · History","question":"The treaty named no figure, leaving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a commission that reported 132 billion gold marks in April 1921 and set the terms of payment a week later.","questions":["Two billion gold marks a year plus 26 per cent of the value of exports was the schedule for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Germany asked for a postponement within seven months.","Paid partly in coal and impossible to transfer abroad without an export surplus, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also created demand in a depression and brought work to German industry."],"answer":"reparations","answerText":"reparations","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sum fixed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 April 1921, 132 billion gold marks</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Schedule</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 May 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annuity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 billion marks plus 26% of exports</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Postponed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 1922</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"<b>Reparations</b> were the compensation Germany was required to make for the damage of the First World War, and the Treaty of Versailles deliberately named no figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Article 233 handed that to an Inter-Allied Reparation Commission, which was to give Germany a hearing, report the amount on or before 1 May 1921, and draw up a schedule discharging the whole obligation within thirty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 28 April 1921 the Commission notified the German government that the damage for which it was responsible came to 132 billion gold marks, about 32 billion dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A week later it issued the Schedule of Payments: a fixed annuity of 2 billion gold marks and a variable one worth 26 per cent of the value of German exports, with 1 billion due inside 25 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some of it was taken in kind, coal to France and Belgium above all, and the rest in foreign currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Germany asked for a partial postponement on 14 December 1921, and the Commission's Cannes decision of January 1922 replaced the instalments with payments of 31 million gold marks every ten days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difficulty was the transfer as much as the size: paying abroad required an export surplus, and where the money was raised instead on international capital markets the real economic transfer approached nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Allied tariffs made that surplus harder to earn and gave the German government an incentive to devalue in order to undercut them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against all that, the obligation to deliver coal kept output high in the Ruhr and the Saar, and the payments created demand in a post-war depression that brought orders and work to German industry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Payment in practice was poor and only small amounts were transferred during the 1920s, which is part of why the economic legacy of the treaty is still discussed as much through myth as through figures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","tags":["concept","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany"],"sources":["<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part VIII, arts. 231–233, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partviii.asp. [Open access]","Reparation Commission, <i>Statement of Germany's Obligations Under the Heading of Reparations, etc., at April 30th, 1922</i> (London: His Majesty's Stationery Office, 1922), Introduction, https://archive.org/details/reparationpapers00repa. [Open access]","Marcel Boldorf, \"Post-war Economies (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/post-war-economies-germany/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/%28De_g._%C3%A0_d._les_repr%C3%A9sentants_allemands_de_la_Kriegslasten_Kommission%29_Michaelis%2C_Fischer%2C_von_Oertzen%2C_29-12-21_%28%C3%A0_l%27h%C3%B4tel%29_Astoria_-_btv1b530743143.jpg/1280px-%28De_g._%C3%A0_d._les_repr%C3%A9sentants_allemands_de_la_Kriegslasten_Kommission%29_Michaelis%2C_Fischer%2C_von_Oertzen%2C_29-12-21_%28%C3%A0_l%27h%C3%B4tel%29_Astoria_-_btv1b530743143.jpg","title":"The German war-burdens delegation in Paris, 29 December 1921","desc":"Michaelis, Fischer and von Oertzen of the Kriegslasten-Kommission on the stairs of the Hôtel Astoria, a fortnight after Germany asked for the schedule to be postponed.","credit":"Agence Rol, Bibliothèque nationale de France. Public domain, via Gallica. https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/btv1b530743143","alt":"Three men in dark suits and striped trousers stand on the curving marble staircase of a hotel lobby, one holding a briefcase and one a folded paper."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the treaty name no sum?","a":"Because it handed the figure to an Inter-Allied Reparation Commission, which was to hear Germany and report the amount by 1 May 1921 with a schedule over thirty years."},{"q":"Why was transferring the money harder than raising it?","a":"Because paying abroad required an export surplus, and where the money was raised on international capital markets instead the real economic transfer approached nothing."},{"q":"Why did Allied tariffs work against the Allies’ own aim?","a":"Because they made that export surplus harder to earn and gave the German government an incentive to devalue in order to undercut them."}]},
{"id":"ww2-007","num":"7","category":"World War II · History","question":"Wilson's thirteenth point had promised Poland access to the sea, and the strip of former German land that gave it one, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, cut East Prussia off from the rest of Germany.","questions":["Lloyd George fought at the conference to limit German losses in Upper Silesia and on the fringes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and won a plebiscite for the one and little else.","Danzig lay outside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and was made a Free City under League protection instead, inside the Polish customs frontier and with its port at Poland's use."],"answer":"Polish Corridor","answerText":"Polish Corridor","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 June 1919, Treaty of Versailles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 70,000 km² (27,000 sq mi)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">People lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 6.5 million</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Saar</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to the League for 15 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Polish Corridor</b> was the strip of former German territory awarded to Poland at Versailles to give it access to the Baltic, and it separated East Prussia from the rest of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came out of a wider settlement: article 87 recognised Poland's complete independence and renounced Germany's title to the land, and Wilson's thirteenth point had already promised an independent Poland with access to the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the west, Alsace-Lorraine was restored to France as from the date of the armistice, and Eupen and Malmedy went to Belgium, where inhabitants were given six months to register a wish to remain German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Saar coal mines were ceded to France as compensation for the mines wrecked in the north of France, while the territory itself passed to the League of Nations as trustee for fifteen years, after which its people would choose their sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Danzig, which the corridor did not include, was made a Free City under the League's protection, inside the Polish customs frontier and with its port at Poland's unrestricted use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Elsewhere the treaty put the question to the inhabitants: the Danish frontier was to be fixed in conformity with the wishes of the population, and Upper Silesia was to vote on whether to join Germany or Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Memel was renounced to the Allied powers and went in the end to Lithuania.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Article 80 bound Germany to respect Austrian independence as inalienable except with the consent of the League Council, closing off any union with the rump Austrian state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Altogether Germany gave up roughly 70,000 square kilometres (27,000 square miles) and more than 6.5 million people, and with them half its iron ore supply and a quarter of its hard coal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lloyd George had pressed at the conference to limit the German losses in Upper Silesia and on the fringes of the corridor, and secured the Silesian plebiscite and little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["place","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany","geography"],"sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part III, arts. 34, 45, 49, 51, 80, 87, 88, 99, 102, 104 and 109, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiii.asp. [Open access]","Piotr Szlanta, \"Poland,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/poland/. [Open access]","Susanne Brandt, \"Versailles, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/versailles-treaty-of/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, US Department of State, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, 1914–1920</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Polish_Corridor.PNG","title":"The Polish Corridor and Danzig","desc":"The strip of former German territory that gave Poland access to the Baltic, with the Free City of Danzig at its mouth and East Prussia separated from the rest of Germany to its east.","credit":"Map by Space Cadet at English Wikipedia, via Wikimedia Commons. CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish_Corridor.PNG","alt":"A map of the Baltic coast in which Germany is green and Poland grey: a grey corridor labelled CORRIDOR reaches the sea between two green blocks, with an orange Danzig at its mouth and Germany East Prussia to the east."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a strip of German territory given to Poland at all?","a":"Because Wilson’s thirteenth point had promised an independent Poland with access to the sea, and the treaty renounced Germany’s title to the land."},{"q":"Why were some frontiers put to a vote and others not?","a":"Because the Danish frontier was to follow the wishes of the population and Upper Silesia was to choose between Germany and Poland, while other transfers were simply awarded."},{"q":"Why did the territorial losses matter economically as well?","a":"Because with roughly 70,000 square kilometres and 6.5 million people went half of Germany’s iron ore supply and a quarter of its hard coal."}]},
{"id":"ww2-008","num":"8","category":"World War II · History","question":"Finland, the Baltic republics, Poland, Czechoslovakia, Austria and Hungary were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that filled the vacuum left in Europe by four collapsed empires.","questions":["Not one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the national entity self-determination had promised, each holding minorities it feared and none content with its frontiers.","Czechoslovakia inherited three million German speakers and quarrelled with Poland over Teschen, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not combine against a revived Germany or Russia."],"answer":"successor states","answerText":"successor states","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Empires ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4, by 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New states</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 in Central and Eastern Europe</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hungary lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">58% of its population</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Poland</span><span class=\"dt-v\">69% of its people Polish, 1921</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>successor states</b> of Central and Eastern Europe were the countries that took over territory and international responsibilities from the German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman empires when those collapsed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland, Czechoslovakia, Austria and Hungary, together with the Soviet Union, filled the political vacuum the collapse left behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The settlement consolidated the Balkans and fragmented Eastern Europe: the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes, renamed Yugoslavia in 1929, grew from Serbia's 4.6 million people to 13.6 million, and Romania's population more than doubled to 16 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Austria became independent reluctantly, its 8 million people mostly in or near a Vienna stripped of the empire that had been its reason for existing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hungary lost two-thirds of its pre-war territory and 58 per cent of its population, a third of the Magyars among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Czechoslovakia was assembled from Bohemia, Moravia and Slovakia, owing much to the exile campaigns of Tomáš Masaryk and Eduard Beneš, and it was given three million German speakers, allocated to it against self-determination and in favour of defensible frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Polish independence had rested on the improbable defeat of all three empires that had partitioned the country, and after the 1920 war with Russia it fixed a frontier far east of the line the conference recommended, leaving only 69 per cent of its people Polish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None was the national entity the principle promised: centuries of war, migration and intermarriage had left an ethnic patchwork, so each held minorities it resented and feared, and all were dissatisfied with their frontiers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peacemakers wrote a system of minority protection into the settlement, partly to encourage assimilation and partly to deny neighbouring kin-states a pretext, then handed responsibility to the League of Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Czechoslovakia and Poland fell out at once over the coalfield of Teschen, and that inability to combine was the region's standing weakness should Germany or Russia revive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","tags":["state","history","politics","20th century","europe"],"sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","<i>Vienna Convention on Succession of States in respect of Treaties</i>, 23 August 1978, art. 2, United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 1946, p. 3, https://legal.un.org/ilc/texts/instruments/english/conventions/3_2_1978.pdf. [Open access]","Piotr Szlanta, \"Poland,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/poland/. [Open access]","<i>The Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Germany</i>, 28 June 1919, Part III, arts. 80–86, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiii.asp. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"World War I: Treaties and Reparations,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/world-war-i-treaties-and-reparations. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Partition_of_Austria-Hungary_1919_map.jpg/1920px-Partition_of_Austria-Hungary_1919_map.jpg","title":"The partition of Austria-Hungary, 1919","desc":"A map published in The Times History of the War showing the boundaries of the successor states as defined in the peace treaties, with the pre-war frontiers of Austria-Hungary marked as a dotted line.","credit":"The Times History of the War, vol. 21 (1919), via the Internet Archive and Wikimedia Commons. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partition_of_Austria-Hungary_1919_map.jpg","alt":"A black and white map of central Europe in 1919, with Austria, Hungary, Czecho-Slovakia, Yugoslavia, Poland and Rumania outlined in heavy new borders across the dotted pre-war frontier of Austria-Hungary."},"why":[{"q":"Why could none of them be the nation-state the principle promised?","a":"Because centuries of war, migration and intermarriage had left an ethnic patchwork, so each held minorities it resented and feared and all were dissatisfied with their frontiers."},{"q":"Why was minority protection written into the settlement?","a":"Because it was meant partly to encourage assimilation and partly to deny neighbouring kin-states a pretext, with responsibility handed to the League of Nations."},{"q":"Why was the region’s weakness its inability to combine?","a":"Because Czechoslovakia and Poland fell out at once over the coalfield of Teschen, leaving them divided should Germany or Russia revive."}]},
{"id":"ww2-009","num":"9","category":"World War II · History","question":"Ágost Benárd and Alfréd Drasche-Lázár signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the Grand Trianon on 4 June 1920, leaving Hungary with a third of the land the kingdom had held.","questions":["Count Albert Apponyi asked the Supreme Council for plebiscites in the disputed lands and was refused, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was handed to Hungary with its frontiers essentially unchanged.","Interwar governments made the revision of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the object of Hungarian foreign policy, which drew the country towards the Axis and won back half the lost lands between 1938 and 1941."],"answer":"Treaty of Trianon","answerText":"Treaty of Trianon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 June 1920, at the Grand Trianon</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 November 1920, by Hungary</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hungary kept</span><span class=\"dt-v\">92,963 km² of 282,870 (35,893 of 109,217 sq mi)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Army capped</span><span class=\"dt-v\">35,000 men</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","20th century","europe"],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Trianon</b> was the peace imposed on Hungary at the end of the First World War, signed at the Grand Trianon Palace in the park at Versailles on 4 June 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Austria-Hungary came apart in the autumn of 1918, and while the Hungarian government waited for the conference to settle the frontiers by negotiation, Czechoslovak, Romanian and Serbian troops occupied territory far beyond the armistice lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A communist government took power in March 1919, fought and lost a war with Romania, and Romanian troops entered Budapest that August, so Hungary reached the conference with no army and no leverage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its delegation was not invited to Paris until December 1919 and received terms drafted without it; Count Albert Apponyi was allowed one speech to the Supreme Council, asked for plebiscites, and was refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two delegates were sent to sign, and the treaty ran to fourteen parts, opening with the Covenant of the League of Nations and describing the new borders in cartographic detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Hungary was left with 92,963 of the 282,870 square kilometres (35,893 of 109,217 square miles) the kingdom had held outside Croatia, and with 7.6 million of its 18.3 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Romania took the largest share, Czechoslovakia and the new Serb-Croat-Slovene Kingdom most of the rest, and Austria received the western strip that became the Burgenland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 3.2 million people who had given Hungarian as their mother tongue in the 1910 census now lived outside the state, and more than 400,000 refugees crossed into it between 1918 and 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army was capped at 35,000 men, reparations were charged, and a covering letter from Alexandre Millerand hinted at frontier adjustments that came to nothing beyond a plebiscite at Sopron in 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every interwar government worked for revision, and the treaty is still felt in Hungary as a national wound<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> — which is why the country aligned with the powers that wanted the settlement broken, and recovered about half the lost territory between 1938 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Miklós Zeidler, \"Trianon, Treaty of,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/trianon-treaty-of/. [Open access]","Tamás Révész, \"Post-war Turmoil and Violence (Hungary),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2020), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/post-war-turmoil-and-violence-hungary/. [Open access]","<i>Treaty of Peace Between the Allied and Associated Powers and Hungary</i>, 4 June 1920, Parts I–IV, World War I Document Archive, Harold B. Lee Library, Brigham Young University, https://wwi.lib.byu.edu/index.php/Treaty_of_Trianon. [Open access]","Peter Ferwagner, \"Centenary (Hungary),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/centenary-hungary/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Holocaust in Hungary,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hungary-before-the-german-occupation. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Le_comte_Apponyi%2C_chef_de_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_hongroise%2C_arrive_au_quai_d%27Orsay_pour_la_remise_du_trait%C3%A9_de_paix.jpg/1920px-Le_comte_Apponyi%2C_chef_de_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_hongroise%2C_arrive_au_quai_d%27Orsay_pour_la_remise_du_trait%C3%A9_de_paix.jpg","title":"Count Apponyi arrives for the peace terms, 15 January 1920","desc":"Count Albert Apponyi, head of the Hungarian delegation, arriving at the Quai d'Orsay in Paris on the day the conditions of peace were handed over. He addressed the Supreme Council the following morning and was refused.","credit":"Agence Rol, Bibliothèque nationale de France. Public domain, via Gallica. https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/btv1b53036032b","alt":"A black and white press photograph of an elderly bearded man in a top hat and long overcoat standing at the foot of stone steps beside a motor car, watched by French officers in uniform."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Hungary reach the conference with no leverage?","a":"Because a communist government had taken power and lost a war with Romania, whose troops entered Budapest that August, so the country arrived with no army."},{"q":"Why did waiting for negotiation cost it territory?","a":"Because while its government waited for the conference to settle the frontiers, Czechoslovak, Romanian and Serbian troops occupied territory far beyond the armistice lines."},{"q":"Why did the treaty shape Hungarian policy for twenty years?","a":"Because about 3.2 million Hungarian speakers were left outside the state, so every interwar government worked for revision and the country aligned with the powers that wanted the settlement broken."}]},
{"id":"ww2-010","num":"10","category":"World War II · History","question":"Mustafa Kemal's victory made the terms of Sèvres unenforceable, so the Allies negotiated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with his government instead, the only peace of 1919 to 1923 that was actually bargained over.","questions":["Signed on 24 July 1923, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> returned eastern Thrace, Anatolia and Izmir to Turkey and abolished the capitulations that had given foreign powers privileges inside the country.","A convention compelling Orthodox Christians and Muslims to move between the two countries was given the force of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which outlasted every other treaty made after the First World War."],"answer":"Treaty of Lausanne","answerText":"Treaty of Lausanne","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 July 1923, at Lausanne</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Talks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 Nov 1922 – July 1923, in two rounds</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capitulations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">abolished by article 28</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declared 29 October 1923</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","20th century","europe"],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Lausanne</b>, signed on 24 July 1923, was the settlement between Turkey and the Allied powers, and the only one of the post-war treaties that was genuinely negotiated rather than imposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ottoman Empire had been left until last: its terms were drafted in London and San Remo in the spring of 1920 and signed at Sèvres that August.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sèvres recognised an independent Armenia, restricted the Turkish army, put the Straits under international control, gave France and Italy spheres in Anatolia, and handed Greece most of Thrace and the government of Izmir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sultan signed under duress, but the nationalist movement Mustafa Kemal was building at Ankara rejected it, and the treaty was never ratified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kemal’s forces halted the Greek advance at Sakarya in 1921 and drove it back through 1922, ending in a massacre at Izmir and a stand-off with British troops at Chanak; the Ankara assembly abolished the sultanate that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Negotiations opened at Lausanne on 20 November 1922 and, after a break, closed in July 1923, with George Curzon and later Horace Rumbold leading for Britain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new treaty returned eastern Thrace, Anatolia, Izmir and some Aegean islands to Turkey, and abolished the capitulations, the financial and legal privileges foreign powers had long enjoyed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Armenia went unmentioned, its independence having been destroyed in December 1920, and the Arab provinces stayed outside the new state as British and French mandates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A separate convention signed in January 1923 compelled an exchange of populations between Greece and Turkey, and article 142 gave it the force of the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The republic was declared on 29 October 1923, and Lausanne has proved the longest-lasting of the settlements, the only one both sides had bargained over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","Yücel Yanıkdağ, \"Ottoman Empire/Middle East,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/ottoman-empiremiddle-east/. [Open access]","<i>Treaty of Peace with Turkey Signed at Lausanne</i>, 24 July 1923, arts. 28 and 142, in <i>The Treaties of Peace 1919-1923</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Carnegie Endowment for International Peace, 1924), World War I Document Archive, Brigham Young University, https://wwi.lib.byu.edu/index.php/Treaty_of_Lausanne. [Open access]","<i>The Peace Treaty of Sèvres</i>, 10 August 1920, World War I Document Archive, Harold B. Lee Library, Brigham Young University, https://wwi.lib.byu.edu/index.php/Peace_Treaty_of_S%C3%A8vres. [Open access]","Loukianos Hassiotis, \"Greece,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/greece/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Lausanne_-_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_turque_-_Ismet_Pacha_-_btv1b90539548.jpg/1920px-Lausanne_-_la_d%C3%A9l%C3%A9gation_turque_-_Ismet_Pacha_-_btv1b90539548.jpg","title":"The Turkish delegation at Lausanne, 1922","desc":"İsmet Pasha and his colleagues outside their hotel during the conference. Turkey came to Lausanne as the winner of a war rather than as a defeated power, and the terms were bargained over for eight months.","credit":"Agence Meurisse, Bibliothèque nationale de France. Public domain, via Gallica. https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/btv1b90539548","alt":"A black and white press photograph of four men in dark overcoats and hats standing on a pavement outside a stone hotel entrance, one of them holding a cane."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the first Ottoman settlement never ratified?","a":"Because the sultan signed Sèvres under duress and the nationalist movement Mustafa Kemal was building at Ankara rejected it."},{"q":"Why could Turkey negotiate where the other defeated powers could not?","a":"Because its forces had halted the Greek advance at Sakarya and driven it back, ending in a stand-off with British troops at Chanak."},{"q":"Why has this treaty lasted where the others did not?","a":"Because it is the only one both sides had bargained over rather than received, which is what the negotiation at Lausanne produced."}]},
{"id":"ww2-011","num":"11","category":"World War II · History","question":"Article 22 of the Covenant called the well-being of such peoples a sacred trust of civilisation and placed each conquered territory under a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, exercised by an advanced nation on the League's behalf.","questions":["Graded A, B or C by how far its people were held to have developed, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> obliged its holder to report every year to a permanent commission in Geneva.","Of all the territories placed under a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only Iraq and Lebanon became independent before 1945, and the rest passed to the United Nations as trust territories."],"answer":"League of Nations mandate","answerText":"League of Nations mandate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Covenant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">article 22, signed 28 June 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first met in Geneva, 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Classes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3, lettered A, B and C</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Iraq 1932, Lebanon 1943</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","law","20th century"],"abstract":"A <b>League of Nations mandate</b> was a former German or Ottoman territory placed under the administration of a victorious power, which governed it on the League’s behalf rather than owning it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea came from a proposal by Jan Smuts and answered Woodrow Wilson’s wish to end the war without annexations, and it was written into article 22 of the Covenant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That article held the well-being of peoples “not yet able to stand by themselves under the strenuous conditions of the modern world” to be a sacred trust of civilisation, and gave their tutelage to advanced nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mandates were graded A, B and C by how far their inhabitants were judged to have developed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The A mandates, former Ottoman provinces, were provisionally recognised as independent nations needing advice; the B mandates in central Africa were administered outright under conditions barring the slave trade; and the C mandates, South West Africa and the Pacific islands, were governed as parts of the holder’s own territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Britain and France took most of them, with smaller shares to Japan, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand, and none at all to Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each holder had to report every year to a Permanent Mandates Commission, which met in Geneva from 1921 and advised the Council on whether the terms were being kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system was meant to end as its territories grew able to stand alone, but before 1945 only Iraq, in 1932, and Lebanon, in 1943, reached independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It both undermined and underpinned colonial rule: it constrained annexation and created an obligation to report, while dressing continued European government in new legal clothes at the moment self-determination had been promised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United Nations Charter brought the remaining mandates into its trusteeship system, and the one that stayed outside, South West Africa, was ruled by South Africa until the International Court declared that presence illegal in 1971.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, 28 June 1919, art. 22, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","Anita Ziegerhofer, \"League of Nations,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/league-of-nations/. [Open access]","Alan Sharp, \"The Paris Peace Conference and its Consequences,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2022), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/the-paris-peace-conference-and-its-consequences-1-1/. [Open access]","<i>Charter of the United Nations</i>, 26 June 1945, arts. 75–77, United Nations, https://www.un.org/en/about-us/un-charter/full-text. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, \"Legal Consequences for States of the Continued Presence of South Africa in Namibia (South West Africa) notwithstanding Security Council Resolution 276 (1970),\" advisory opinion of 21 June 1971, https://www.icj-cij.org/case/53. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was the system invented at all?","a":"Because it answered Wilson’s wish to end the war without annexations, holding the well-being of the peoples concerned to be a sacred trust of civilisation rather than a possession."},{"q":"Why were the territories graded into three classes?","a":"Because the grades ranked how far their inhabitants were judged able to stand alone, so an A mandate was provisionally recognised as a nation while a C mandate was governed as part of the holder’s own territory."},{"q":"Why is the system said to have both restrained and dressed up colonial rule?","a":"Because it constrained annexation and created an obligation to report, while giving continued European government new legal clothes at the moment self-determination had been promised."}]},
{"id":"ww2-012","num":"12","category":"World War II · History","question":"Wilson’s fourteenth point asked for a general association of nations, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that came out of it opened its first assembly at Geneva in November 1920 with forty-one member states.","questions":["Every decision of its Assembly and Council had to be unanimous, which is one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> imposed sanctions only once, against Italy over Abyssinia, and to no effect.","Japan left in 1933 over Manchuria and Germany the same year, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also ran the Saar, resettled refugees, and its technical work outlasted it in bodies the United Nations inherited."],"answer":"League of Nations","answerText":"League of Nations","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In existence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 January 1920 to 1946</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First Assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 November 1920, in Geneva</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Covenant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 articles, Part I of Versailles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Members</span><span class=\"dt-v\">63 in all, at most 60 at once</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["institution","history","politics","law","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>League of Nations</b> was the first permanent international organisation founded to keep the peace, created by a Covenant of twenty-six articles that formed Part I of the Treaty of Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It came into existence on 10 January 1920, and forty-one states opened its first Assembly at Geneva that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its members renounced secret diplomacy, undertook to reduce their armaments, and pledged to respect one another’s territorial integrity and political independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Assembly gave every member one vote, a Council of great and lesser powers met at least once a year, and a permanent Secretariat ran the business; decisions in both bodies had to be unanimous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sixty-three states belonged at one time or another and never more than sixty at once, and the United States, whose president had proposed the thing, never joined at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Article 16 declared that a member going to war in defiance of the Covenant had made war on every other member, but sanctions were left to each state’s own judgement and needed a unanimous Council.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League settled the Åland islands in 1920, Upper Silesia in 1921 and a Greek-Bulgarian border war in 1925, and of twenty-four disputes brought to its Council between 1921 and 1936 it resolved five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It administered the Saar, supervised Danzig and the minority treaties, and oversaw the mandates; its commissions worked on refugees, the opium trade, slavery and public health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan walked out in 1933 rather than accept the Council’s finding on Manchuria, Germany left the same year and Italy in 1937, and article 16 was invoked exactly once, against Italy over Abyssinia, without effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League was wound up in 1946, its court giving way to the International Court of Justice, and its technical bodies and its idea of collective security passing to the United Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Anita Ziegerhofer, \"League of Nations,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/league-of-nations/. [Open access]","<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, 28 June 1919, arts. 5, 8, 10 and 16, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","United Nations Office at Geneva, \"The League of Nations,\" UN Library and Archives Geneva, https://www.ungeneva.org/en/library-archives/league-of-nations. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"The League of Nations, 1920,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/league. [Open access]","International Court of Justice, \"History,\" https://www.icj-cij.org/history. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/1923_League_of_Nations_map_by_R._J._Walrath.png/1920px-1923_League_of_Nations_map_by_R._J._Walrath.png","title":"The extent of the League of Nations, 1923","desc":"A newspaper map published under the headline “Has League Failed? 30 Nations Answer”, shading member states and the territory they controlled, the mandates, and the countries outside the League — among them the United States and Russia.","credit":"Map by R. J. Walrath, The New York Times, 23 December 1923. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1923_League_of_Nations_map_by_R._J._Walrath.png","alt":"A black and white world map of 1923 in which member states of the League and their colonies are shaded dark, mandated territories are hatched, and non-member countries including the United States and Russia are left white."},"why":[{"q":"Why was collective security so hard to enforce?","a":"Because although article 16 declared war on one member war on all, sanctions were left to each state’s own judgement and needed a unanimous Council."},{"q":"Why did unanimity weaken the organisation?","a":"Because decisions in both the Assembly and the Council had to be unanimous, so any state with an interest in the outcome could stop a finding against it."},{"q":"Why did members leaving matter more than members joining?","a":"Because Japan walked out rather than accept the finding on Manchuria, Germany left the same year and Italy in 1937, so the states most likely to be judged simply removed themselves."}]},
{"id":"ww2-013","num":"13","category":"World War II · History","question":"A majority of senators favoured the treaty but it needed two-thirds, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who chaired the Foreign Relations Committee, led the opposition that denied Wilson his majority.","questions":["Republicans feared that Article X of the Covenant bound America to defend other members’ borders, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pressed reservations that Wilson, half-paralysed by a stroke, refused to accept.","Nine months after the Senate’s refusal Harding won the presidency on a platform against the League, and the country <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had kept out of it made its own peace with Germany in 1921."],"answer":"Henry Cabot Lodge","answerText":"Henry Cabot Lodge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wilson’s stroke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 September 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaty defeated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1920, by 49 votes to 35</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Harding elected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1920, opposing the League</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Separate peace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Treaty of Berlin, 25 August 1921</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","politics","20th century","united states"],"abstract":"<b>Henry Cabot Lodge</b> was the Republican Senate majority leader and chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee who led the opposition that kept the United States out of the League of Nations its own president had proposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wilson had attached the Covenant to the Treaty of Versailles as its first part, believing that an effective League would in time soften whatever was unjust in the peace terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He returned to Washington in February 1919 to find opposition already building in Congress and the press.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objection that mattered was not the treaty’s harshness towards Germany but Article X of the Covenant, in which members undertook to preserve one another’s territorial integrity and political independence against external aggression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Opponents feared losing control of American foreign policy, transferring the power to declare war from Congress to the League’s council, and inviting scrutiny of the interventions the United States was used to making in Central America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A majority of the Senate was for the treaty, but ratification needed two-thirds, and the opposition split into irreconcilables who would take it on no terms and reservationists who would take it with amendments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reservations asserted Congress’s right to declare war, a right to withdraw from the League, and international recognition of the Monroe Doctrine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wilson took his case to the country in a long speaking tour, collapsed, and suffered a crippling stroke on 27 September 1919; from his sickroom he refused every reservation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In March 1920 the treaty was defeated by forty-nine votes to thirty-five, and nine months later Warren Harding won the presidency on a platform against the League.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Congress ended the war by joint resolution in July 1921 and the Treaty of Berlin restored relations with Germany that August, so the organisation built to keep the peace began without the strongest power on earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, \"The League of Nations, 1920,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/league. [Open access]","Jennifer D. Keene, \"United States of America,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/united-states-of-america/. [Open access]","Mark E. Benbow, \"Wilson, Woodrow,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/wilson-woodrow/. [Open access]","<i>The Covenant of the League of Nations</i>, 28 June 1919, art. 10, Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, \"Germany,\" <i>A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776</i>, United States Department of State, https://history.state.gov/countries/germany. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/Henry_Cabot_Lodge.jpg","title":"Henry Cabot Lodge","desc":"A studio portrait from the Senate years by the Washington firm Harris and Ewing. Lodge chaired the Foreign Relations Committee and led the opposition that kept the United States out of the League of Nations.","credit":"Harris and Ewing, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Cabot_Lodge.jpg","alt":"A black and white studio portrait of an elderly man with a full white beard and moustache and swept-back hair, in a dark suit and tie, looking towards the camera."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the opposition fasten on Article X?","a":"Because members undertook to preserve one another’s territorial integrity, which opponents feared would transfer the power to declare war from Congress to the League’s council."},{"q":"Why did a Senate majority for the treaty not ratify it?","a":"Because ratification needed two-thirds, and the opposition split into irreconcilables who would take it on no terms and reservationists who would take it with amendments."},{"q":"Why did Wilson’s own conduct help defeat it?","a":"Because he took his case to the country, collapsed and suffered a crippling stroke, and from his sickroom refused every reservation."}]},
{"id":"ww2-014","num":"14","category":"World War II · History","question":"Germany’s army command had asked for an armistice in October 1918, yet the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that the undefeated army had been betrayed at home by socialists, republicans and Jews.","questions":["Testifying to a parliamentary committee in November 1919, Hindenburg said the army had been stabbed from behind, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried his authority into every campaign of the nationalist right.","German Jews had served in the army out of proportion to their numbers, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was believed anyway, and it helped make the republic seem the creature of traitors."],"answer":"stab-in-the-back myth","answerText":"stab-in-the-back myth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Army beaten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Allied counteroffensive from July 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Armistice sought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the army command, October 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hindenburg</span><span class=\"dt-v\">testified 18 November 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hitler appointed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Hindenburg, 30 January 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>stab-in-the-back myth</b>, in German the <i>Dolchstoßlegende</i>, was the false claim that the German army of 1918 was never beaten in the field but was betrayed from behind by revolutionaries, socialists and Jews at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany was in fact beaten: the Allied counteroffensive that began in July 1918 broke the spring offensives, and by the end of September Ludendorff himself admitted the war was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the army command that asked the government to seek an armistice, and Ludendorff put the blame for the defeat on the politicians in Berlin as he did so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ordinary Germans found the surrender hard to credit, because no fighting had reached German soil and German troops still stood deep in occupied Europe, while the collapse of the home front was visible daily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wartime propaganda had allowed no doubt of victory to the last, so the request for an armistice in October 1918 arrived as a shock demanding an explanation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Several versions circulated at once, blaming parliament, the socialists, the trade unions or the Jews, and the Pan-German League decided in 1918 to place the whole blame on the Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The legend gained its authority in November 1919, when Hindenburg told a parliamentary committee of inquiry that the army had been stabbed from behind, citing an English officer for the phrase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was entirely untrue — German Jews had served loyally and out of proportion to their numbers — but it was believed, and it made the republic look like the creature of traitors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German National People’s Party campaigned on it and gained ground in 1924, and the National Socialists took it up, while Ludendorff joined Hitler’s Munich putsch in 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hindenburg was elected president in 1925 and appointed Hitler chancellor on 30 January 1933, by which time the myth had helped break off any argument between the republic’s defenders and its enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Boris Barth, \"Stab-in-the-back Myth,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/stab-in-the-back-myth/. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Dolchstoßlegende,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/dolchstosslegende.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitism in History: World War I,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism-in-history-world-war-i. [Open access]","Anna von der Goltz, \"Hindenburg, Paul von,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/hindenburg-paul-von/. [Open access]","Roger Chickering, \"Ludendorff, Erich,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/ludendorff-erich/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/1920_poster_12000_Jewish_soldiers_KIA_for_the_fatherland.jpg","title":"An die deutschen Mütter, 1920","desc":"A leaflet answering the myth in its own time. Issued by the Reich Association of Jewish Front-Line Soldiers, it reads “To German mothers! 12,000 Jewish soldiers fell for the fatherland on the field of honour”, and asks German women not to let a Jewish mother be mocked in her grief.","credit":"Reichsbund jüdischer Frontsoldaten, 1920. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1920_poster_12000_Jewish_soldiers_KIA_for_the_fatherland.jpg","alt":"A black and white leaflet in which a woman in mourning sits beside a soldier’s grave marked with an Iron Cross, the grave inscribed in German with the words twelve thousand Jewish soldiers fell for the fatherland on the field of honour."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the defeat seem to need explaining at all?","a":"Because no fighting had reached German soil and German troops still stood deep in occupied Europe, while wartime propaganda had allowed no doubt of victory to the last."},{"q":"Why is the charge inverted as well as false?","a":"Because it was the army command that asked the government to seek an armistice, Ludendorff blaming the politicians in Berlin as he did so."},{"q":"Why did Hindenburg’s testimony matter so much?","a":"Because it gave the legend authority: telling a parliamentary committee that the army had been stabbed from behind made the republic look like the creature of traitors."}]},
{"id":"ww2-015","num":"15","category":"World War II · History","question":"Named for the town where its constituent assembly met away from the fighting in the capital, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced the Kaiser with a president elected by the whole people for seven years.","questions":["Seats in the Reichstag of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were shared out in proportion to the vote, and article 48 let the president suspend basic rights and govern without it.","Sixteen governments came and went in the fourteen years of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which enfranchised women, set an eight-hour day, and ended when Hindenburg appointed Hitler chancellor."],"answer":"Weimar Republic","answerText":"Weimar Republic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proclaimed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 November 1918, in Berlin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">signed 11 August 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Governments</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 in 14 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hitler chancellor, 30 January 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>Weimar Republic</b> is the name given to the German state between the fall of the empire in 1918 and the Nazi seizure of power in 1933, after the town in Thuringia where its constituent assembly met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The republic was proclaimed in Berlin on 9 November 1918, and the assembly gathered at Weimar rather than in a capital still torn by street fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A draft written largely by the constitutional lawyer Hugo Preuß was approved by 262 votes to 75 on 31 July 1919, signed by President Friedrich Ebert on 11 August and in force three days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was the first democratic constitution in German history: political authority came from the people, men and women over twenty voted by universal secret suffrage, and women were made equal before the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Reichstag was elected by proportional representation, and the president by the whole people for a seven-year term, with command of the armed forces and the power to dissolve parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Article 48 allowed the president, when public order was seriously disturbed, to suspend basic rights and take whatever measures he thought necessary, if need be with the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Proportional representation gave small parties seats and a voice that no German had had before, but it also made majorities hard to assemble and later helped extremist parties into the chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sixteen governments held office in fourteen years; the republic was fought by armed extremists of left and right in its early years, found relative calm from 1924, and lost it in the slump that began in 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also expanded unemployment insurance, established bargaining between workers and employers, and set an eight-hour working day, while Berlin became one of the most socially liberal cities in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended on 30 January 1933, when Hindenburg, who had been elected president in 1925 with the backing of the right, appointed Adolf Hitler chancellor of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Weimar Republic,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-weimar-republic. [Open access]","\"The Weimar Constitution (August 11, 1919),\" arts. 1, 22, 41, 43, 48 and 109, in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, German Historical Institute, Washington, DC, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/the-weimar-constitution-august-11-1919. [Open access]","Erik Jensen with Insa Kummer, \"Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, 2nd ed., German Historical Institute, Washington, DC, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933. [Open access]","Deutsches Historisches Museum, \"Die Weimarer Republik,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik.html. [Open access]","Anna von der Goltz, \"Hindenburg, Paul von,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/hindenburg-paul-von/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/61/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-08215%2C_Berlin%2C_Verfassungsfeier_im_Stadion.jpg","title":"Constitution Day in Berlin, 11 August 1929","desc":"The republic marking the tenth anniversary of its constitution. Young men in tabards bearing the Reich eagle carry an enormous black, red and gold flag across a stadium field — the colours the constitution gave the republic, against the black, white and red of the empire.","credit":"Georg Pahl, Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-08215. CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-08215,_Berlin,_Verfassungsfeier_im_Stadion.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph looking down on a stadium field where about twenty young men in tabards marked with an eagle carry a very large flag stretched flat between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the assembly meet in a small Thuringian town?","a":"Because the capital was still torn by street fighting, so the constituent assembly gathered at Weimar instead of in Berlin."},{"q":"Why did proportional representation cut both ways?","a":"Because it gave small parties seats and a voice no German had had before, and also made majorities hard to assemble and later helped extremist parties into the chamber."},{"q":"Why was Article 48 so consequential?","a":"Because it let the president suspend basic rights and take whatever measures he thought necessary when public order was seriously disturbed, if need be with the army."}]},
{"id":"ww2-016","num":"16","category":"World War II · History","question":"Sailors at Wilhelmshaven and Kiel refused to sail out for a last battle in November 1918, and the mutiny grew into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that swept away the Kaiser and every German prince.","questions":["Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from the Reichstag to forestall Liebknecht’s free socialist one, and that double announcement showed how divided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> already was.","Ebert’s bargain with the army let the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> end in a parliamentary republic rather than a council dictatorship, at the cost of Freikorps killings and a permanently split left."],"answer":"German Revolution","answerText":"German Revolution","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Naval mutinies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early November 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Republic</span><span class=\"dt-v\">proclaimed 9 November 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly elected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 January 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Berlin fighting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly 1,200 dead, March 1919</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>German Revolution</b> of 1918 and 1919 destroyed the monarchy, replaced it with a parliamentary republic, and left the German left permanently divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began at the end of October 1918, when the naval command ordered the fleet out for a last battle although armistice talks had already opened, and war-weary sailors at Wilhelmshaven and Kiel mutinied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The revolt spread within days from sailors to workers, and by 10 November councils of workers and soldiers had taken over the municipal government of practically every large German city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first throne to fall was Bavaria’s, where a republic was proclaimed at Munich on 7 November under the Independent Social Democrat Kurt Eisner; the other princes went within days, and almost no blood was shed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 9 November Berlin struck, and Chancellor Max von Baden announced the Kaiser’s abdication on his own authority and handed the chancellorship to Friedrich Ebert, both unconstitutionally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That afternoon Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed a republic from the Reichstag to forestall Karl Liebknecht’s free socialist republic, and the double proclamation showed how far the movement had already split.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next day the Social Democrats and Independents formed a Council of People’s Deputies, and General Wilhelm Groener promised the army’s support against the radical left in return for the officer corps keeping its autonomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In December a congress of the councils voted overwhelmingly for elections to a national assembly, which were held on 19 January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A rising in Berlin from 5 to 12 January, named after the Spartacists though they did little to direct it, was crushed, and Liebknecht and Rosa Luxemburg were murdered on the night of 15 January.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nearly 1,200 people died in further Berlin fighting that March, the Munich council republic was put down in April, and many workers never forgave the Social Democrats for the alliance that did it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Revolution von 1918/19,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/revolution.html. [Open access]","\"The Spread of the 1918 Revolution,\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, German Historical Institute, Washington, DC, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/the-spread-of-the-1918-revolution. [Open access]","Alexander Gallus, \"Revolutions (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-germany/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Weimar Republic,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-weimar-republic. [Open access]","Ingrid Sharp, \"Luxemburg, Rosa,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/luxemburg-rosa/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-810%2C_Berlin%2C_Brandenburger_Tor%2C_Novemberrevolution.jpg","title":"Revolutionaries at the Brandenburg Gate, 9 November 1918","desc":"An open car of armed soldiers with a machine gun on the roof and a flag streaming behind drives through the gate on the day the Kaiser's abdication was announced and the republic proclaimed, with crowds watching from the pavement.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-B0527-0001-810 / unknown author / CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-B0527-0001-810,_Berlin,_Brandenburger_Tor,_Novemberrevolution.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of an open motor car crowded with armed soldiers, a machine gun mounted above the windscreen and a flag flying, driving through the columns of the Brandenburg Gate past onlookers."},"why":[{"q":"Why did sailors mutiny in the last days of the war?","a":"Because the naval command ordered the fleet out for a last battle although armistice talks had already opened."},{"q":"Why did two republics get proclaimed on one afternoon?","a":"Because Scheidemann proclaimed one from the Reichstag to forestall Liebknecht’s free socialist republic, which showed how far the movement had already split."},{"q":"Why did many workers never forgive the Social Democrats?","a":"Because Groener promised the army’s support against the radical left in return for the officer corps keeping its autonomy, and that alliance put the risings down."}]},
{"id":"ww2-017","num":"17","category":"World War II · History","question":"Ordered to disband under the peace terms, a naval brigade marched on the capital instead in March 1920, and the cabinet fled rather than face the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the army refusing to fire.","questions":["A general strike and the refusal of the civil service to take orders broke the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within four days, without a shot fired by the republic’s own soldiers.","Wearing swastikas on their helmets, the men who mounted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were sent weeks later to crush workers in the Ruhr who had risen against them."],"answer":"Kapp Putsch","answerText":"Kapp Putsch","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ehrhardt brigade</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ordered dissolved, 29 February 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Putsch began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 March 1920, in Berlin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Collapsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 March 1920, after four days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rathenau shot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 June 1922, in Berlin</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>Kapp Putsch</b> was an attempt by army officers and Freikorps troops to overthrow the Weimar Republic in March 1920, and the first serious challenge to it from the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On 29 February 1920, following an instruction from the Inter-Allied Military Control Commission, the defence minister Gustav Noske ordered the disbandment of Hermann Ehrhardt’s marine brigade of about 6,000 men and the Loewenfeld Freikorps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> General Walther von Lüttwitz, the senior commander of the provisional Reichswehr, refused, and early on 13 March the Ehrhardt brigade occupied the government quarter of Berlin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The men marched with swastikas painted on their steel helmets, and Wolfgang Kapp, a co-founder of the wartime German Fatherland Party, made himself chancellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The army would not act against them, so the cabinet, the chancellor Gustav Bauer and President Ebert left the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> From 15 March about twelve million workers stopped work in the largest strike movement in German history, cutting Berlin off from news and transport and shutting its gas, water and power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The putschists could not restart the utilities and gave up on 17 March; what also broke them was the ministerial bureaucracy’s refusal to carry out Kapp’s orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Saxony, Thuringia and above all the Ruhr the left tried to carry the strike on into a workers’ revolution, and the government sent Freikorps against them, the Ehrhardt brigade among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political murder followed: Matthias Erzberger in 1921 and, on 24 June 1922, the foreign minister Walther Rathenau, shot in Berlin by members of the far-right Organisation Consul after nationalist newspapers had all but called for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The republic survived every one of these attacks, but it had learned that its own army would put down the left and stand aside for the right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Burkhard Asmuss, \"Der Lüttwitz-Kapp-Putsch 1920,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/luettwitz-kapp-putsch-1920. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Generalstreik 1920,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/generalstreik-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Weimar Republic,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-weimar-republic. [Open access]","Vera Hierholzer, \"Die Ermordung Walter Rathenaus,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/ermordung-rathenaus-1922. [Open access]","Alexander Gallus, \"Revolutions (Germany),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://encyclopedia.1914-1918-online.net/article/revolutions-germany/. [Open access]","Levke Harders, \"Wolfgang Kapp 1858–1922,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/biografie/wolfgang-kapp. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Bonn_Marktplatz_Demonstration_Kapp-Putsch_1920.jpg/1280px-Bonn_Marktplatz_Demonstration_Kapp-Putsch_1920.jpg","title":"Demonstration against the Kapp Putsch, Bonn, 15 March 1920","desc":"A crowd fills the market square in Bonn on 15 March 1920, the first day of the general strike called against the putsch, photographed from an upper window of the old town hall.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Stadtarchiv Bonn. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonn_Marktplatz_Demonstration_Kapp-Putsch_1920.jpg","alt":"A dense crowd of men in hats and overcoats packing a paved market square, seen from above, with shop fronts and a large arched doorway along one side."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the government have to leave Berlin?","a":"Because the army would not act against the putschists who had occupied the government quarter, so the cabinet and the president went rather than be taken."},{"q":"Why did the attempt collapse without fighting?","a":"Because about twelve million workers stopped work, cutting Berlin’s gas, water and power, and the ministerial bureaucracy refused to carry out the new chancellor’s orders."},{"q":"Why was the episode a lesson about the republic’s own army?","a":"Because it had learned that its army would put down the left and stand aside for the right, the same Freikorps then being sent against the workers in the Ruhr."}]},
{"id":"ww2-018","num":"18","category":"World War II · History","question":"Told by the reparations commission that deliveries were in arrears, France sent five divisions into Germany’s coal and steel district in January 1923, beginning the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the government answered with passive resistance.","questions":["Miners and officials were told not to obey the occupiers, and the state printed money to pay them, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended in a currency collapse rather than in coal.","The government that had urged passive resistance during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called it off in September 1923, by which time only four of the district’s 89 blast furnaces were still alight."],"answer":"Occupation of the Ruhr","answerText":"Occupation of the Ruhr","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Troops entered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 January 1923, five French divisions</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Expelled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">120,000 to 150,000 people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resistance ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 September 1923, under Stresemann</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Troops left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925, under the Dawes Plan</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","20th century","europe","germany","france"],"abstract":"The <b>Occupation of the Ruhr</b> was the entry of French and Belgian troops into Germany’s coal and steel district in January 1923, to take reparations by force after the Allied Reparations Commission found German deliveries in arrears at the end of 1922.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The shortfall was a small one, and the French prime minister Raymond Poincaré wanted more from the occupation than coal: his aim was to revise the Treaty of Versailles in France’s favour and push Germany’s western frontier eastward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 60,000 soldiers, five French divisions with some Belgian units among them, crossed into the district on 11 January and at once imposed a state of emergency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The government of Wilhelm Cuno could not resist militarily and did not try; it called instead for passive resistance, and forbade officials to carry out the occupiers’ orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The population held together, and the occupation authorities expelled between 120,000 and 150,000 people from the Ruhr and from the Rhineland into unoccupied Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Active resistance grew beside it, with nationalists and communists carrying out sabotage and bombings together, and saboteurs who were executed, Albert Leo Schlageter among them, were made martyrs at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Paying the strikers meant printing money, and the presses that funded the resistance drove the mark’s collapse into hyperinflation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cost was plain on the ground: a British correspondent walking through Essen in late September found four of the district’s 89 blast furnaces still working, the rest cold and most fallen in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gustav Stresemann, chancellor since 13 August, called off passive resistance on 26 September 1923 to save the currency, and was denounced for it by nationalists and separatists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The troops stayed until 1925 and went only after the Dawes Plan rescheduled reparations and provided for their evacuation, so the resistance had cost Germany its currency without shortening the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Ruhrbesetzung,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/ruhrbesetzung-1923. [Open access]","\"Reichstag Hearing on the Occupation of the Ruhr (January 17, 1923),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:document-4341. [Open access]","Morgan Philips Price, \"On the End of Passive Resistance in the Ruhr (September 25, 1923),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:document-4420. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","\"The Occupation of the Rhineland after the First World War,\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:map-5018. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Inflation,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/inflation. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/French_enter_Essen.jpg","title":"French cavalry entering Essen, 1923","desc":"A column of French cavalry in steel helmets rides down a cobbled street in Essen past a corner cafe, in the first weeks of the occupation.","credit":"Bain News Service, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_enter_Essen.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of mounted soldiers in steel helmets riding in a broad column down a wet cobbled street, with shop fronts and a motor car on the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why was a small shortfall answered with an occupation?","a":"Because Poincaré wanted more than coal: his aim was to revise the Treaty of Versailles in France’s favour and push Germany’s western frontier eastward."},{"q":"Why did passive resistance wreck the currency?","a":"Because paying the strikers meant printing money, and the presses that funded the resistance drove the mark’s collapse into hyperinflation."},{"q":"Why did calling the resistance off not end the occupation?","a":"Because the troops stayed until 1925 and went only after the Dawes Plan rescheduled reparations, so the resistance cost Germany its currency without shortening the occupation."}]},
{"id":"ww2-019","num":"19","category":"World War II · History","question":"Wages were paid daily and a restaurant bill could double during the meal, until the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was ended in November 1923 by a new currency worth a trillion paper marks.","questions":["Paying strikers in the occupied Ruhr with freshly printed notes turned a bad currency into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the Reich’s whole war debt came to just over sixteen pfennigs.","By the autumn of 1923, 133 outside firms were printing notes for the Reich day and night, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had reached a note for 100 trillion marks."],"answer":"German hyperinflation","answerText":"German hyperinflation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Highest note</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 trillion marks, November 1923</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Real wages</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 40 per cent of pre-war level</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rentenmark</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 November 1923, one to a trillion</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reichsmark</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 August 1924, under the Dawes Plan</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","economics","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>German hyperinflation</b> was the destruction of the mark’s value in 1922 and 1923, at the end of which the new currency was fixed at one Rentenmark to a trillion paper marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It began in the way the First World War was paid for: Germany borrowed rather than taxed, and of 164 billion marks of war costs only 10 billion came from levies and taxes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The money in circulation had already grown fivefold by 1918, and pensions for the wounded and the bereaved, the return to peacetime production and a reparations bill of 132 billion gold marks kept the deficits growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By 1922 the Reich’s debt service stood at 126 per cent of its revenue, and the murder of Walther Rathenau in June shook what confidence in the republic was left at home and abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The French and Belgian entry into the Ruhr in January 1923 turned decline into catastrophe, because the government answered it with passive resistance and paid the strikers with printed money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> By the autumn of 1923 as many as 133 outside firms and 1,783 presses were printing notes for the Reich day and night, and in November the Reichsbank issued a note for 100 trillion marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prices moved faster than the presses: a pound of rice cost 80,000 marks one day and 160,000 the next, wages were often paid daily, and a restaurant bill could double during the meal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Real wages fell to about 40 per cent of their pre-war level and savings built over generations were wiped out; a British correspondent wrote in October that Germany’s condition beggared description.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There were winners, since industry borrowed in good money and repaid in worthless money, and the state’s entire war debt of 164 billion marks came to just over sixteen pfennigs at the changeover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That changeover came on 15 November 1923, when the Rentenmark was issued against a mortgage on German farmland and industry, and the Reichsmark replaced it in August 1924 under the Dawes Plan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Inflation,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/inflation. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Währungsreform 1923,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/waehrungsreform-1923. [Open access]","Friedrich Kroner, \"Overwrought Nerves\" (1923), <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/friedrich-kroner-overwrought-nerves-1923. [Open access]","Morgan Philips Price, \"On Hyperinflation and its Effects (October 18, 1923),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/morgan-philips-price-on-hyperinflation-and-its-effects-october-18-1923. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Ruhrbesetzung,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/ruhrbesetzung-1923. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/In_a_Berlin_Bank_LCCN2014716642.jpg/1280px-In_a_Berlin_Bank_LCCN2014716642.jpg","title":"In a Berlin bank, 1923","desc":"A clerk reaches up into a wall of banknote bundles stacked from floor to ceiling in a Berlin bank, photographed for an American news agency in 1923.","credit":"Bain News Service, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, ggbain.36493. No known restrictions on publication, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:In_a_Berlin_Bank_LCCN2014716642.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a man in a dark suit standing beside stacks of banded banknotes piled from the floor to the ceiling and filling most of the room, reaching up to the top of one stack."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the collapse begin with how the war was financed?","a":"Because Germany borrowed rather than taxed — of 164 billion marks of war costs only 10 billion came from levies and taxes — so the money in circulation had already grown fivefold by 1918."},{"q":"Why did prices outrun even the printing presses?","a":"Because a pound of rice could cost 80,000 marks one day and 160,000 the next, so wages were often paid daily and a restaurant bill could double during the meal."},{"q":"Why did some parties gain from the destruction?","a":"Because industry borrowed in good money and repaid in worthless money, and the state’s whole war debt of 164 billion marks came to just over sixteen pfennigs at the changeover."}]},
{"id":"ww2-020","num":"20","category":"World War II · History","question":"Germany was to pay only what its economy could bear, with no total and no end date, under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1924, which also brought American loans and an end to the Ruhr occupation.","questions":["Half the German National People’s Party voted for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in August 1924 despite calling it a surrender of sovereignty, because France had promised to leave the Ruhr in return.","American loans made under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> paid German reparations, which paid French and British war debts, which were owed back to the United States."],"answer":"Dawes Plan","answerText":"Dawes Plan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Report</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 April 1924, committee under Dawes</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ratified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 August 1924, London Conference</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annual payment</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 billion marks, rising to 2.5 billion</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Replaced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1930, by the Young Plan</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","economics","politics","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>Dawes Plan</b> was the settlement of 1924 that rescheduled German reparations, ended the occupation of the Ruhr and brought American money into the German economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It followed the collapse of the old arrangement: Germany had fallen behind on the payments set by the London Schedule of 1921, France and Belgium had occupied the Ruhr, and the mark had been destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Allies now feared that Germany could not pay at all, and Washington feared that France and Britain would then default on the war loans they owed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A committee of experts chaired by Charles G. Dawes, a Chicago banker who was then the first director of the American budget bureau, reported on 9 April 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its principle was that Germany should pay only what its economy could bear, so the plan fixed neither a total sum nor a date by which the debt would be discharged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Annual payments were to begin at one billion gold marks and rise to 2.5 billion after five years, and the Reichsbahn and the Reichsbank were put under international control as security.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It came with money: a loan of 800 million marks to steady the Reichsbank, floated on the American market by J. P. Morgan and oversubscribed at once, after which American banks went on lending for four more years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That produced a circle in which American loans paid German reparations, which paid French and British war debts, which were owed back to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German right attacked the plan as a surrender of sovereignty, but half the deputies of the German National People’s Party voted for it on 29 August 1924, because France had promised in return to leave the Ruhr.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The money and the calm began a recovery in which German output grew by half between 1924 and 1929, and Dawes shared the Nobel Peace Prize for 1925 with Austen Chamberlain, both of them receiving it a year later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Dawes-Plan,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/dawesplan. [Open access]","\"The Dawes Plan (1924),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/the-dawes-plan-1924. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","\"The Nobel Peace Prize 1925,\" <i>NobelPrize.org</i>, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1925/summary/. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Ruhrbesetzung,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/ruhrbesetzung-1923. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Allied_Experts_in_Berlin_-_Flora_-_Sir_R._Kindersley_-_McFadyean_-_Pirelli_-_Allix_-_Parmentier_-_Dawes_-_Young_-_Houtart_-_Sir_J._Stamp_-_Francqui_LCCN2014716971.jpg/1280px-Allied_Experts_in_Berlin_-_Flora_-_Sir_R._Kindersley_-_McFadyean_-_Pirelli_-_Allix_-_Parmentier_-_Dawes_-_Young_-_Houtart_-_Sir_J._Stamp_-_Francqui_LCCN2014716971.jpg","title":"The Allied experts in Berlin","desc":"Eleven of the Allied experts round a table in Berlin, their names written along the top and foot of the negative: Flora, Kindersley, McFadyean, Pirelli, Allix, Parmentier, Dawes, Young, Houtart, Stamp and Francqui. No date was recorded on the caption card.","credit":"Bain News Service, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, ggbain.36823. No known restrictions on publication, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allied_Experts_in_Berlin_-_Flora_-_Sir_R._Kindersley_-_McFadyean_-_Pirelli_-_Allix_-_Parmentier_-_Dawes_-_Young_-_Houtart_-_Sir_J._Stamp_-_Francqui_LCCN2014716971.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of eleven men in dark suits seated and standing behind a long table with papers in front of them, names handwritten across the top and bottom of the negative."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States care about a European settlement?","a":"Because Washington feared that if Germany could not pay, France and Britain would default on the war loans they owed it."},{"q":"Why did the plan fix no total and no end date?","a":"Because its principle was that Germany should pay only what its economy could bear, so the annuities were set and the final sum was not."},{"q":"Why did half the nationalist deputies vote for it?","a":"Because France had promised in return to leave the Ruhr, which was worth more to them than the sovereignty the plan gave up."}]},
{"id":"ww2-021","num":"21","category":"World War II · History","question":"Germany accepted its western border and the demilitarised Rhineland under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of October 1925, with Britain and Italy as guarantors, while saying nothing about its border with Poland.","questions":["Two foreign ministers shared a Nobel Peace Prize for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which took Germany out of isolation and into the League of Nations the following September.","The western guarantee given by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted a decade, until German troops marched back into the demilitarised Rhineland in March 1936 in breach of it."],"answer":"Locarno Treaties","answerText":"Locarno Treaties","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 5 October 1925, at Locarno</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Treaties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">concluded 16 October 1925</span><span class=\"dt-k\">League entry</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Germany joined, 10 September 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Broken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 March 1936, Rhineland remilitarised</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>Locarno Treaties</b> were the agreements concluded at Locarno in Switzerland on 16 October 1925, by which Germany accepted its western frontier and Europe’s statesmen believed the war had at last been closed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The conference had opened on 5 October, with the German chancellor Hans Luther and the foreign minister Gustav Stresemann meeting the leading statesmen of France, Britain, Italy, Belgium, Poland and Czechoslovakia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the main treaty Germany, France and Belgium guaranteed the frontiers between them as fixed at Versailles, along with the demilitarised zone of the Rhineland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain and Italy signed as guarantors, undertaking to come to the help of whichever side a breach was committed against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four arbitration treaties went with it, binding Germany to settle disputes with France, Belgium, Poland and Czechoslovakia before an international commission rather than by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What Germany did not do was recognise its eastern frontier: Stresemann took the western guarantee while expressly keeping open the revision of the border with Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That asymmetry was the point for each side, since France gained a British guarantee and an end to isolation, Britain gained peaceful change at little cost, and Germany gained a step back towards great-power standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The British foreign secretary Austen Chamberlain called Locarno the real dividing line between the years of war and the years of peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German isolation was over: the country joined the League of Nations on 10 September 1926, and Briand and Stresemann shared the Nobel Peace Prize for 1926.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The western guarantee held for a decade, until about 30,000 German soldiers marched into the demilitarised Rhineland on 7 March 1936 in breach of both Locarno and Versailles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Burkhard Asmuss and Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Konferenz von Locarno 1925,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/locarno. [Open access]","\"Treaty of Mutual Guarantee between Germany, Belgium, France, Great Britain and Italy; October 16, 1925,\" <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/locarno_001.asp. [Open access]","\"Negotations between Gustav Stresemann, Austen Chamberlain, and Aristide Briand in Locarno (October 1925),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/negotations-between-gustav-stresemann-joseph-austen-chamberlain-and-aristide-briand-in-locarno-october-1925. [Open access]","\"The Nobel Peace Prize 1926,\" <i>NobelPrize.org</i>, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1926/summary/. [Open access]","\"Wehrmacht Units Occupy the Demilitarized Rhineland Zone: The First Troops March Into Koblenz (March 1936),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/wehrmacht-units-occupy-the-demilitarized-rhineland-zone-the-first-troops-march-into-koblenz-march-1936. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Conf%C3%A9rence_de_Locarno_-_de_gauche_%C3%A0_droite_Luther%2C_Stressmann%2C_Von_Schubert%2C_Kiep_-_btv1b9024864s.jpg/1280px-Conf%C3%A9rence_de_Locarno_-_de_gauche_%C3%A0_droite_Luther%2C_Stressmann%2C_Von_Schubert%2C_Kiep_-_btv1b9024864s.jpg","title":"The German delegation at Locarno, October 1925","desc":"Five men of the German delegation standing under a pergola in a garden at Locarno. The agency caption names four of them, among them Chancellor Hans Luther and Foreign Minister Gustav Stresemann, and the library dates the print to 6 October 1925.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, Bibliotheque nationale de France, Meurisse 27136 A. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conf%C3%A9rence_de_Locarno_-_de_gauche_%C3%A0_droite_Luther%2C_Stressmann%2C_Von_Schubert%2C_Kiep_-_btv1b9024864s.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of five men in suits standing on a lawn under a wooden pergola covered with climbing plants, garden chairs and a bench behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany guarantee one frontier and not the other?","a":"Because Stresemann took the western guarantee while expressly keeping open the revision of the border with Poland, so the arbitration treaties in the east promised only procedure."},{"q":"Why did each side think it had gained?","a":"Because France gained a British guarantee and an end to isolation, Britain gained peaceful change at little cost, and Germany gained a step back towards great-power standing."},{"q":"Why is the western guarantee dated to a decade?","a":"Because it held until about 30,000 German soldiers marched into the demilitarised Rhineland on 7 March 1936, in breach of both Locarno and Versailles."}]},
{"id":"ww2-022","num":"22","category":"World War II · History","question":"Fifteen states signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Paris in August 1928, renouncing war as an instrument of national policy, though nothing in it said how the promise would be enforced.","questions":["Only wars of aggression were covered by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so a state could sign it in 1928 without giving up anything it expected to need.","When Japan invaded Manchuria in 1931, appeals under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> failed, and the treaty that had outlawed war turned out to have no machinery behind it."],"answer":"Kellogg-Briand Pact","answerText":"Kellogg-Briand Pact","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Briand’s letter</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1927, to the United States</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 August 1928, in Paris</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Signatories</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 at Paris, 63 by 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First test</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1931, Japan invaded Manchuria</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","law","20th century","europe"],"abstract":"The <b>Kellogg-Briand Pact</b> was a treaty signed in Paris on 27 August 1928 by which the signatories renounced war as an instrument of national policy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Until the First World War a sovereign state could make war at its own discretion, and the League of Nations had limited that right without making a war of aggression unlawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pact began as a French proposal: in an open letter of April 1927 the foreign minister Aristide Briand offered the United States a bilateral agreement to outlaw war between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> President Coolidge and his secretary of state Frank B. Kellogg were wary, fearing a bilateral pact would read as an alliance obliging America to defend France, and proposed instead that the two invite every nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wider version was popular, partly because only wars of aggression were covered and self-defence was not, so a state could sign without giving up anything it expected to need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The treaty has two substantive articles: the parties condemn recourse to war and renounce it as an instrument of national policy, and agree to settle all disputes between them by peaceful means alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fifteen states signed at Paris, among them Germany, whose delegation under the already gravely ill Stresemann had pressed for a treaty, and by 1929 sixty-three states had joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American Senate ratified it by 85 votes to 1, but only after recording that it neither limited the right of self-defence nor obliged the United States to act against a signatory who broke it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was tested in 1931, when Japan invaded Manchuria after an explosion on a railway near Mukden, and the United States sat in on League of Nations council meetings for the first time to press for enforcement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those appeals failed, and with no enforcement machinery and no agreed definition of self-defence the pact did little to prevent the war it was written against, though Kellogg received the Nobel Peace Prize for it in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Kellogg-Briand Pact, 1928,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/kellogg. [Open access]","\"Kellogg-Briand Pact 1928,\" <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/kbpact.asp. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Briand-Kellogg-Pakt,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/briandkellog. [Open access]","\"The Nobel Peace Prize 1929,\" <i>NobelPrize.org</i>, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1929/summary/. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/President_Coolidge_signs_Kellogg_Treaty._President_Coolidge_today_formally_signed_the_document_of_ratification_of_the_Kellogg_treaty_before_a_distinguished_gathering_in_the_east_room_of_the_LCCN2016889136.jpg/1280px-President_Coolidge_signs_Kellogg_Treaty._President_Coolidge_today_formally_signed_the_document_of_ratification_of_the_Kellogg_treaty_before_a_distinguished_gathering_in_the_east_room_of_the_LCCN2016889136.jpg","title":"Coolidge signs the American ratification, 1929","desc":"President Calvin Coolidge signing the American instrument of ratification in the East Room of the White House in 1929, watched by Vice President Charles Dawes, Secretary of State Frank B. Kellogg and members of the Cabinet and the Senate.","credit":"National Photo Company Collection, Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, LCCN 2016889136. No known restrictions on publication, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Coolidge_signs_Kellogg_Treaty._President_Coolidge_today_formally_signed_the_document_of_ratification_of_the_Kellogg_treaty_before_a_distinguished_gathering_in_the_east_room_of_the_LCCN2016889136.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a man signing a document at a long table in a grand room with two chandeliers, some thirty men in dark suits seated and standing behind him."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States want the pact widened?","a":"Because a bilateral agreement with France would read as an alliance obliging America to defend her, so Washington proposed that the two invite every nation instead."},{"q":"Why was so sweeping a treaty easy to sign?","a":"Because only wars of aggression were covered and self-defence was not, so a state could sign without giving up anything it expected to need."},{"q":"Why did it fail its first test?","a":"Because it had no enforcement machinery and no agreed definition of self-defence, so the appeals over Manchuria came to nothing."}]},
{"id":"ww2-023","num":"23","category":"World War II · History","question":"German production grew by half between 1924 and 1929, the years remembered as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though want remained widespread among working families throughout them.","questions":["Cinema and spectator sport became mass entertainments on the American model during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lasted from the end of the hyperinflation to the crash of 1929.","The unemployment insurance passed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was calculated for 1.4 million claimants, and the government fell in 1930 when the figure passed five million."],"answer":"Golden Twenties","answerText":"Golden Twenties","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recovery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924 to 1929, output up by half</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Insurance law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1927, 26 weeks of benefit</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unemployed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.4 million planned for, 5 million by 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Coalition broke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1930, over the insurance</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","economics","culture","20th century","europe","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>Golden Twenties</b> is the name given to Germany’s brief recovery between the end of the hyperinflation in 1923 and the crash of 1929, and it is a label its own users qualify, since the years were golden only for a wealthy few.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A stable currency and the foreign loans that came with the Dawes Plan started a real upswing, and German output grew by half between 1924 and 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Industry was reorganised and rationalised on American lines, and by the end of 1928 the modernised plant employed four million more workers than before the war within the same borders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wages followed: the standard of living rose appreciably, and for much of the working class it reached or passed the pre-war level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In July 1927 the Reichstag voted by an overwhelming majority for a national unemployment insurance, the first legal right to support for workers and salaried employees, paid for equally by employers and employees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Benefit ran for 26 weeks at between a quarter and three-quarters of the wage the claimant had been earning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The culture is what the name remembers: cinema and spectator sport became mass entertainments on the American model, electric appliances and ready-made clothes were sold as ways of saving time, and the new white-collar class had novels of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That was not the whole country, since society remained deeply divided and want was widespread among working families living at the edge of subsistence throughout the same years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The recovery was bought on credit, and the Reichsbank’s foreign commissioner said so in December 1928: the price was an annual interest burden abroad of well over five hundred million reichsmarks, and part of the borrowing was not productive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The insurance fund had been calculated for at most 1.4 million unemployed; by February 1930 the figure had passed five million, and the governing coalition broke in March over how to pay for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Arnulf Scriba, \"Kunst und Kultur,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/kunst-und-kultur. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Alltagsleben,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/alltagsleben. [Open access]","\"Report of the Commissioner of the Reichsbank (December 10, 1928),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/report-of-the-commissioner-of-the-reichsbank-december-10-1928. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Arbeitslosenversicherung,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/arbeitslosenversicherung-1927. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Der Dawes-Plan,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/dawesplan. [Open access]","\"Sign on a Barbershop Announcing Special Prices for the Unemployed (1927),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/ghdi:image-4099. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Berlin%2C_das_neu_erbaute_Capitol%2C_Portal_mit_viel_Verkehr_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Freiburg_W_134_Nr._001524.jpg/1280px-Berlin%2C_das_neu_erbaute_Capitol%2C_Portal_mit_viel_Verkehr_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Freiburg_W_134_Nr._001524.jpg","title":"The Capitol cinema in Berlin, April 1927","desc":"The newly built Capitol cinema advertising the latest Douglas Fairbanks film, with motor cars drawn up along the kerb and tram rails in the foreground.","credit":"Sammlung Willy Pragher, Landesarchiv Baden-Wuerttemberg, Staatsarchiv Freiburg W 134 Nr. 001524. CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin%2C_das_neu_erbaute_Capitol%2C_Portal_mit_viel_Verkehr_-_LABW_-_Staatsarchiv_Freiburg_W_134_Nr._001524.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a large cinema front lettered CAPITOL, with a painted film hoarding above the entrance, a row of open motor cars parked along the pavement and tram rails in the road."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the name qualified by the people who use it?","a":"Because society remained deeply divided and want was widespread among working families living at the edge of subsistence throughout the same years."},{"q":"Why was the recovery fragile?","a":"Because it was bought on credit: the Reichsbank’s foreign commissioner put the price at an annual interest burden abroad of well over five hundred million reichsmarks, part of it not productively invested."},{"q":"Why did the unemployment insurance break the government?","a":"Because the fund had been calculated for at most 1.4 million unemployed, and by February 1930 the figure had passed five million."}]},
{"id":"ww2-024","num":"24","category":"World War II · History","question":"Thirty countries met in Italy in April 1922 at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to bring Soviet Russia back into European trade, and two of the guests slipped away and signed their own treaty.","questions":["The United States would not attend the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because it refused to deal with the Russian government, which it had not recognised since breaking off relations in 1917.","Fearing they would be left out of whatever the Allies agreed with Moscow, the German delegates to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went 30 kilometres (20 miles) up the coast and made their own deal."],"answer":"Genoa Conference","answerText":"Genoa Conference","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April 1922, some 30 countries at Genoa</span><span class=\"dt-k\">United States</span><span class=\"dt-v\">declined to attend, 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rapallo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 April 1922, Germany and Soviet Russia</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US recognition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 November 1933, after almost 16 years</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","20th century","europe","russia"],"abstract":"The <b>Genoa Conference</b> was an international economic conference of some thirty countries held in Italy in April 1922, called above all to bring Soviet Russia back into the European trading system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Russia had left the war separately at Brest-Litovsk in March 1918, and the Allies had answered the revolution with troops, Japanese, American and other forces occupying parts of northern Russia, Ukraine and Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Bolshevik government had repudiated the Tsar’s debts, ignored existing treaties and seized foreign property, and the United States broke off relations in December 1917.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There had been one chance of a settlement: in March 1919 an American attaché sent to Moscow during the Paris peace conference came back with a Bolshevik offer of an armistice, an end to the blockade and a promise to honour Russia’s debts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Allied leaders let the deadline pass without answering, and that failure delayed American recognition of Soviet Russia for years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Genoa was the British prime minister’s attempt three years later, and the United States refused to attend, in part because it would not deal with the Russian government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German delegation went with modest expectations and a fear of being shut out of the important business, and of any Allied agreement with Russia leaving Germany out in the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the two outcasts dealt with each other: on 16 April, at Rapallo about 30 kilometres (20 miles) along the coast, Germany and Soviet Russia restored relations and waived their war claims against one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Allied powers were surprised and angry, and feared for the whole Versailles order, above all for the existence of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The larger settlement Genoa had been called to make never came, and the United States did not recognise the Soviet Union until 16 November 1933, after almost sixteen years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Bullitt Mission to Soviet Russia, 1919,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/bullitt-mission. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Recognition of the Soviet Union, 1933,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/ussr. [Open access]","\"Cabinet Consultation with the President Regarding the Agenda for Genoa (April 5, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/cabinet-consultation-with-the-president-regarding-the-agenda-for-genoa-april-5-1922. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Vertrag von Rapallo,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/vertrag-von-rapallo. [Open access]","\"German Cabinet Meeting on Diplomatic Effects of the Treaty of Rapallo (April 18, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/german-cabinet-meeting-on-diplomatic-effects-of-the-treaty-of-rapallo-april-18-1922. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Conf%C3%A9rence_de_G%C3%AAnes_-_arriv%C3%A9e_de_M._Lloyd_Georges_aux_c%C3%B4t%C3%A9s_de_M._Pacta_et_de_M._Schlanger_-_btv1b9038062q.jpg/1280px-Conf%C3%A9rence_de_G%C3%AAnes_-_arriv%C3%A9e_de_M._Lloyd_Georges_aux_c%C3%B4t%C3%A9s_de_M._Pacta_et_de_M._Schlanger_-_btv1b9038062q.jpg","title":"Lloyd George arriving at Genoa, April 1922","desc":"The British prime minister, hat in hand at the centre, arriving with other delegates past a line of Italian police. The library dates the press print to 9 April 1922.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, Bibliotheque nationale de France, Meurisse 96567. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conf%C3%A9rence_de_G%C3%AAnes_-_arriv%C3%A9e_de_M._Lloyd_Georges_aux_c%C3%B4t%C3%A9s_de_M._Pacta_et_de_M._Schlanger_-_btv1b9038062q.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of about twenty men in dark overcoats standing in a hall, several holding hats, with uniformed policemen in helmets ranged along the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Soviet Russia outside the trading system at all?","a":"Because the Bolshevik government had repudiated the Tsar’s debts, ignored existing treaties and seized foreign property, and the Allies had answered the revolution with troops."},{"q":"Why did the two outcasts deal with each other?","a":"Because the German delegation feared being shut out of the important business, and that an Allied agreement with Russia would leave Germany in the cold."},{"q":"Why did the agreement alarm the Allied powers?","a":"Because they feared for the whole Versailles order, above all for the existence of Poland, which lay between the two states that had just settled with each other."}]},
{"id":"ww2-025","num":"25","category":"World War II · History","question":"Two isolated states recognised each other in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of April 1922, cancelling their war claims and with them the Versailles article that would have given Russia German reparations.","questions":["The Allied powers feared the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pointed to another partition of Poland, and the German minister who signed it was murdered by nationalist officers ten weeks later.","Believing they were being shut out of the conference down the coast, the German delegates negotiated the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> separately with Soviet Russia in a nearby town."],"answer":"Treaty of Rapallo","answerText":"Treaty of Rapallo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 April 1922, at Rapallo</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Disposed of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Article 116 of the Versailles treaty</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rathenau shot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 June 1922, ten weeks later</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Berlin Treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 April 1926, neutrality promised</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","20th century","europe","germany","russia"],"abstract":"The <b>Treaty of Rapallo</b> was the agreement of 16 April 1922 by which Germany and Soviet Russia restored diplomatic relations, the two most isolated states in Europe recognising one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was signed at Rapallo, about 30 kilometres (20 miles) from Genoa, while the great economic conference there was still sitting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each side gave up its claims on the other: both renounced the costs and damages of the war, and Germany dropped its claim to German property the Soviet state had nationalised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That disposed of Article 116 of the Treaty of Versailles, under which Russia would have been entitled to German reparations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The German foreign minister Walther Rathenau signed for Germany and Georgy Chicherin for Russia, and the treaty also provided for trade between the two countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Germany’s motive was fear as much as ambition, its delegates at Genoa believing they were being shut out of the main business and that any Allied agreement with Russia would leave Germany out in the cold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Allied powers were surprised and angry, and feared for the Versailles order itself, above all that the two might arrange another partition of Poland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The leadership of the Reichswehr under Hans von Seeckt did want joint action against Poland to recover the eastern frontier of 1914, but no such alliance followed from the treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rathenau thought it necessary and knew what it would cost, writing days later that it could not have been done without inflicting wounds and that the storm was not yet over; ultra-nationalist officers murdered him on 24 June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The line held all the same, and after Locarno unsettled Moscow the Berlin Treaty of April 1926 promised each side neutrality if the other were attacked or sanctioned by a third power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"German-Russian Treaty of Rapallo (April 16, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/german-russian-treaty-of-rapallo-april-16-1922. [Open access]","Arnulf Scriba, \"Vertrag von Rapallo,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/aussenpolitik/vertrag-von-rapallo. [Open access]","\"German Cabinet Meeting on Diplomatic Effects of the Treaty of Rapallo (April 18, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/german-cabinet-meeting-on-diplomatic-effects-of-the-treaty-of-rapallo-april-18-1922. [Open access]","\"Foreign Minister Walter Rathenau after Signing the Treaty of Rapallo (April 16, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/foreign-minister-walter-rathenau-after-signing-the-treaty-of-rapallo-april-16-1922. [Open access]","\"Friendship Treaty between Germany and the Soviet Union (Berlin Treaty) (April 24, 1926),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/friendship-treaty-between-germany-and-the-soviet-union-berlin-treaty-april-24-1926. [Open access]","\"German-Russian Agreement; April 16, 1922 (Treaty of Rapallo),\" <i>The Avalon Project</i>, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/rapallo_001.asp. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Joseph_Wirth.jpg","title":"German and Soviet negotiators at Rapallo, 1922","desc":"Chancellor Joseph Wirth talking on a sunlit terrace with the Soviet delegates Leonid Krasin, Georgy Chicherin and Adolf Joffe, one of them carrying a briefcase.","credit":"Unknown photographer, Archiv der sozialen Demokratie der Friedrich-Ebert-Stiftung. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Wirth.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of five men in dark suits standing and talking on a paved terrace with a balustrade and palm leaves behind them, a bentwood stool between them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did mutual renunciation suit both sides?","a":"Because both dropped the costs and damages of the war and Germany its claim to nationalised property, which also disposed of the article entitling Russia to German reparations."},{"q":"Why did Germany sign at all?","a":"Because its delegates at Genoa believed they were being shut out of the main business and that any Allied agreement with Russia would leave Germany in the cold."},{"q":"Why did the signer expect to pay for it?","a":"Because Rathenau wrote days later that it could not have been done without inflicting wounds and that the storm was not yet over; he was murdered on 24 June."}]},
{"id":"ww2-026","num":"26","category":"World War II · History","question":"Nine powers met in the American capital from November 1921 at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which fixed capital-ship tonnage at five to five to three and froze the fortification of Pacific bases.","questions":["Cruisers were left unlimited by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1921 and 1922, so a new building race began at once and the powers were back at the table within five years.","The Nine-Power Treaty signed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised to respect China’s territorial integrity but gave nobody any way to enforce it, as Japan showed in Manchuria."],"answer":"Washington Naval Conference","answerText":"Washington Naval Conference","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 November 1921 to 6 February 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Five-Power ratio</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5:5:3 in capital-ship tonnage</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US released</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1932, after the attack on Shanghai</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Limits expired</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936, extended at London in 1930</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","military","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Washington Naval Conference</b> was a meeting of nine powers held from 12 November 1921 to 6 February 1922 to limit navies and settle the rivalries of the western Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The American secretary of state Charles Evans Hughes invited Britain, Japan, France and Italy to talk about naval reductions, and Belgium, China, Portugal and the Netherlands to talk about the Far East.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Five-Power Treaty was the centrepiece, fixing capital-ship tonnage at 500,000 tons each for the United States and Britain, 300,000 for Japan and 175,000 each for France and Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had asked for ten to ten to seven and the American navy for ten to ten to five; the conference settled on five to five to three, and required the powers to stop building capital ships and scrap old ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Article XIX froze the fortification of Pacific bases, which alarmed American officers who thought it left Hawaii, Guam and the Philippines exposed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Four-Power Treaty replaced the Anglo-Japanese alliance of 1902 with a promise to consult, removing the risk that Britain would be bound to join Japan against the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nine-Power Treaty made the American Open Door policy international, its signatories promising to respect the territorial integrity of China, but it recognised Japanese dominance in Manchuria and provided no way to enforce anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whole classes of warship had been left unlimited, so a cruiser race began at once and the powers returned to the table in 1927 and 1930, when the limits were extended and set to expire in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The good feeling did not last: the American Immigration Act of 1924 excluded Japanese immigrants, which Japan took as a breach of the Gentlemen’s Agreement and protested against, and relations worsened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japan took Manchuria in 1931 and attacked Shanghai in 1932, and Washington answered that breach of the Nine-Power Treaty by declaring itself no longer bound by the naval limits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","Joseph V. Fuller, ed., <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, 1922</i>, vol. 1 (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1938), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1922v01. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The London Naval Conference, 1930,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/london-naval-conf. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Immigration Act of 1924 (The Johnson-Reed Act),\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/immigration-act. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Washington_Naval_Treaty.jpg/1280px-Washington_Naval_Treaty.jpg","title":"The signing of the naval treaty, February 1922","desc":"The delegations ranged round a great U-shaped table under the flags of the signatory powers, with spectators watching from the balconies, at the signing in February 1922.","credit":"Unknown photographer. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_Naval_Treaty.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph looking down on a large hall where about eighty men in dark suits sit and stand around a U-shaped table, national flags and palms along the wall behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the treaty replacing the Anglo-Japanese alliance matter?","a":"Because it removed the risk that Britain would be bound to join Japan against the United States, substituting a promise to consult."},{"q":"Why did a cruiser race begin immediately?","a":"Because whole classes of warship had been left unlimited, so the powers had to return to the table in 1927 and 1930."},{"q":"Why did the Nine-Power Treaty guarantee so little?","a":"Because although its signatories promised to respect China’s territorial integrity, it recognised Japanese dominance in Manchuria and provided no way to enforce anything."}]},
{"id":"ww2-027","num":"27","category":"World War II · History","question":"Opening at Geneva in February 1932, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the League of Nations’ single attempt to disarm the world by treaty, and it collapsed when Germany walked out in 1933.","questions":["Article 8 of the League Covenant promised to cut arms to the lowest point consistent with safety, a pledge the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent three years at Geneva trying to redeem.","Herbert Hoover complained in May 1932 of the ineffectiveness of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose deadlock between French security and German equality outlasted the plan he put forward that June."],"answer":"General Disarmament Conference","answerText":"General Disarmament Conference","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">opened 2 February 1932, at Geneva</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hoover plan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 June 1932, briefly revived talks</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Germany left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 October 1933, and quit the League</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Final phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1934, ended in an arms-traffic treaty</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","military","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>General Disarmament Conference</b>, formally the Conference for the Reduction and Limitation of Armaments, opened at Geneva on 2 February 1932 as the League of Nations’ one attempt to disarm the world by treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its root was Article 8 of the League Covenant, which held that peace required \"the reduction of national armaments to the lowest point consistent with national safety\" and made the League responsible for planning it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Treaty of Versailles pointed the same way: its military clauses opened by saying Germany accepted them \"in order to render possible the initiation of a general limitation of the armaments of all nations\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany read that as a promise, and cited the 100,000-man army the treaty imposed as proof of a bargain the victors had never kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The naval powers had meanwhile tried separately, capping capital ships at Washington in 1922, failing to extend the caps to cruisers at Geneva in 1927, and patching the gap at London in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The 1927 talks collapsed over how cruisers should be counted, after which the United States authorised fifteen new cruisers and a carrier, joining the arms race it had met to stop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The general conference stalled between French demands for security and German demands for equality, and in May 1932 Herbert Hoover told his cabinet of \"the ineffectiveness of accomplishment at the disarmament Conference\", the world then spending five billion dollars a year on arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> His plan of 22 June 1932 revived them briefly, and Germany, which had left, returned that December on a five-power declaration granting equality of rights within a system providing security for all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The concession bought ten months: on 14 October 1933 Germany withdrew from both the conference and the League, and the powers spent 1934 bargaining in parallel rather than together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Japan denounced the Washington treaty that year, the naval ceilings of 1922 and 1930 were due to expire in 1936, and the conference’s last work was an American-sponsored treaty on the arms trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["Gustave A. Nuermberger et al., eds., <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1932, General, Volume I</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1932v01. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" article 8, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","\"The Versailles Treaty, June 28, 1919: Part V, Military, Naval and Air Clauses,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partv.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Geneva Naval Conference, 1927,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/geneva. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The London Naval Conference, 1930,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/london-naval-conf. [Open access]","\"President Hoover to the Secretary of State, Washington, May 24, 1932,\" document 121 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1932, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1932v01/d121. [Open access]","\"The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great Britain, Washington, October 14, 1933,\" document 205 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1933, General, Volume I</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1933v01/d205. [Open access]","Rogers P. Churchill et al., eds., <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1934, General, Volume I</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1951), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1934v01. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Gen%C3%A8ve%2C_2-2-32%2C_ouverture_de_la_conf%C3%A9rence_du_d%C3%A9sarmement_-_btv1b532520413.jpg","title":"The opening session at Geneva, 2 February 1932","desc":"Delegates fill the hall of the Batiment electoral in Geneva on the opening day of the Conference for the Reduction and Limitation of Armaments. A press photograph by the Agence Rol, held by the Bibliotheque nationale de France.","credit":"Agence Rol, via Gallica and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gen%C3%A8ve%2C_2-2-32%2C_ouverture_de_la_conf%C3%A9rence_du_d%C3%A9sarmement_-_btv1b532520413.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a crowded assembly hall, delegates in dark suits seated at tiered desks covered with papers, with a packed public gallery above."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany treat its own disarmament as a bargain?","a":"Because the military clauses of Versailles opened by saying Germany accepted them to render possible a general limitation of the armaments of all nations."},{"q":"Why did the 1927 naval talks make matters worse?","a":"Because they collapsed over how cruisers should be counted, after which the United States authorised fifteen new cruisers and a carrier, joining the race it had met to stop."},{"q":"Why did the concession of equality buy so little?","a":"Because it bought ten months: Germany returned in December 1932 on a five-power declaration and withdrew from both the conference and the League on 14 October 1933."}]},
{"id":"ww2-028","num":"28","category":"World War II · History","question":"In Italy the years 1919 and 1920 became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when strikes, land seizures and the occupation of the factories followed a victory the country could not afford.","questions":["Real wages had fallen and the cost of living stood at 268 against 100 in 1913 when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried Italian workers into the streets and onto the land.","Giovanni Giolitti’s refusal to clear the occupied steelworks by force in September 1920 came at the height of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and cost the liberal state the confidence of property."],"answer":"Biennio Rosso","answerText":"Biennio Rosso","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Biennio rosso</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919 to 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">second half of 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Factories held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parliament</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1919 to 7 April 1921</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","society","europe","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Biennio Rosso</b>, the red two years of 1919 and 1920, was the wave of strikes, land seizures and factory occupations that swept Italy after the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italy had entered that war on 24 May 1915, ten months after it began, against the wishes of the Socialist Party and much of the Catholic world, so the country fought already divided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cost was severe: excess deaths between 1915 and 1918 reached about 1,250,000, of which some 400,000 came from the fighting itself, and a tenth of the men mobilised died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state paid by borrowing and by printing, and paper money in circulation rose from 2,783 million lire in 1913 to 18,552 million in 1919, with the cost-of-living index at 268 against 100 in 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wartime contracts had meanwhile built a small, tightly concentrated industrial bloc around FIAT, Ansaldo, Ilva and Montecatini, while real wages fell for labourers and clerks alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When peace came, protest resumed where the war had interrupted it, in forms the conflict itself had changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second half of 1919 brought an unprecedented wave of rural strikes and land occupations, the summer had already seen violent protests in the cities against the cost of living, and the spring of 1920 brought mass industrial strikes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1920 steelworkers occupied the factories, an act whose symbolic weight exceeded anything it won, and Giovanni Giolitti refused the loud demands that he restore the owners’ property by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The political ground had already moved, the 1919 election being the first fought under proportional representation and the parliament it returned sitting from 1 December 1919 to 7 April 1921.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The liberal leaders would deal with neither of the two new mass parties, the Socialists and the Popular Party founded in January 1919, and the paralysis that followed opened the way for Mussolini.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","Elena Papadia, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10896. [Open access]","Pierluigi Scolè, \"War Losses (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2025), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11650/1.1. [Open access]","Fabio Degli Esposti, \"War Finance (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10722. [Open access]","Roberto Bianchi, \"Social Conflict and Control, Protest and Repression (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10367. [Open access]","Camera dei deputati, \"XXV Legislatura del Regno d’Italia,\" <i>Portale storico della Camera dei deputati</i>, https://storia.camera.it/legislature/leg-regno-XXV. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Biennio_Rosso.jpg","title":"Workers holding an occupied factory, 1920","desc":"Armed workers photographed behind a hammer-and-sickle banner at a factory they had taken over during the occupations of 1920. The photographer is unrecorded; the print reproduced here is the one held by Wikimedia Commons.","credit":"Unknown photographer, 1920, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biennio_Rosso.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of about twenty men in caps and hats, several holding rifles, standing and sitting behind a large banner painted with a hammer and sickle inside a wreath."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Italy come out of the war already divided?","a":"Because it had entered ten months late against the wishes of the Socialist Party and much of the Catholic world, so the intervention itself was contested."},{"q":"Why did prices matter as much as politics?","a":"Because paper money rose from 2,783 million lire in 1913 to 18,552 million in 1919, with the cost-of-living index at 268, while real wages fell for labourers and clerks alike."},{"q":"Why did the factory occupations settle nothing?","a":"Because their symbolic weight exceeded anything they won, and Giolitti refused the demands that he restore the owners’ property by force."}]},
{"id":"ww2-029","num":"29","category":"World War II · History","question":"Italy had won the war and lost the peace, ran the nationalist charge of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after Paris refused it the Adriatic port of Fiume and much of the Dalmatian coast.","questions":["The Pact of London had promised Italy the Alps, Trieste and Dalmatia but left Fiume to Croatia, and the cry of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned that gap into a national grievance.","When Orlando left the Council of Four in April 1919 and did not sit again until 7 May, the phrase <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was already at work on what Italy felt it had been denied."],"answer":"mutilated victory","answerText":"mutilated victory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Wilson’s appeal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 April 1919, to the Italian people</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Italy absent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 April to 7 May 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fiume seized</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1919, by d’Annunzio</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fiume settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1920, made independent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","politics","diplomacy","europe","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>mutilated victory</b> was the nationalist charge that Italy, having won the war at enormous cost, had been cheated of its reward at the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Paris_Peace_Conference_(1919–1920)\">Paris Peace Conference</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Italy had joined the Allies in 1915 on written conditions: the frontier of the Alps, the ports of Trieste and Pola, and much of the Dalmatian coast with many of its islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That bargain, the Pact of London, would move over 200,000 German-speaking Tyrolese and 750,000 Southern Slavs under Italian rule, and it left Fiume to Croatia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States, which had entered as an Associated Power, held that it was not bound by agreements the Allies made before it joined, and Woodrow Wilson opposed the Italian claims on the Adriatic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Italy was meanwhile asking more than the Pact had promised, its proposals of 23 April 1919 demanding Fiume under Italian sovereignty as well as the islands and a mandate over Zara and Sebenico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That day Wilson went over the Italian government’s head with a public appeal to the Italian people, defending it to the other leaders as a friendly message setting out the case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Clemenceau and Lloyd George answered Orlando with a memorandum asking what valid reason there was for adding \"this little city on the Croatian coast\" to Italy \"in the teeth of the Treaty\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Italian delegation then left: Orlando sat with the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Paris_Peace_Conference_(1919–1920)\">Council of Four</span> on 24 April and not again until 7 May, by which time the German treaty was almost done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Neither the demand that the Pact of London be honoured in full nor the claim to Fiume had much hope of being heard, and the expected scaling down of Italian claims let the radical right foster a devastating sense of victimhood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gabriele d’Annunzio seized Fiume with several thousand volunteers in September 1919 and was forced out only in December 1920, and the agitation over the Adriatic gave militants a way to imagine an answer from outside parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","\"Fiume and the Peace Settlement,\" appendix I to \"Notes of a Meeting Held at Mr. Lloyd George’s Residence, Paris, April 24, 1919, 4 p.m.,\" document 19 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d19. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]","\"Notes of a Meeting Held at President Wilson’s House, Paris, April 23, 1919, 11 a.m.,\" document 16 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d16. [Open access]","\"Notes of a Meeting Held at President Wilson’s House, Paris, April 24, 1919, 11 a.m.,\" document 18 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d18. [Open access]","\"Notes of a Meeting Held at President Wilson’s House, Paris, May 7, 1919, 11 a.m.,\" document 48 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume V</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv05/d48. [Open access]","Vanda Wilcox, \"D’Annunzio, Gabriele,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11260. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Council_of_Four_of_the_Peace_Conference._Mr._Lloyd_George%2C_Signor_Orlando%2C_M._Clemenceau%2C_President_Woodrow_Wilson...._-_NARA_-_530791.tif/1280px-Council_of_Four_of_the_Peace_Conference._Mr._Lloyd_George%2C_Signor_Orlando%2C_M._Clemenceau%2C_President_Woodrow_Wilson...._-_NARA_-_530791.tif.jpg","title":"The Council of Four in Paris, 27 May 1919","desc":"Lloyd George and Orlando talking on the left, Clemenceau and Wilson on the right, outside the Hotel Crillon. Orlando had walked out of the conference in late April and returned on 7 May. A United States Signal Corps photograph held by the National Archives as record 530791.","credit":"U.S. Signal Corps, National Archives and Records Administration 530791, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council_of_Four_of_the_Peace_Conference._Mr._Lloyd_George%2C_Signor_Orlando%2C_M._Clemenceau%2C_President_Woodrow_Wilson...._-_NARA_-_530791.tif","alt":"Black and white photograph of four elderly men in dark overcoats on the steps of a building, two of them in conversation on the left and two standing apart on the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the United States hold itself free of the Pact of London?","a":"Because it had entered as an Associated Power and held that it was not bound by agreements the Allies made before it joined."},{"q":"Why did the Italian claim weaken as it grew?","a":"Because Italy asked more than the Pact had promised, demanding Fiume under Italian sovereignty as well, which the Pact had left to Croatia."},{"q":"Why did the disappointment matter more than the terms?","a":"Because the expected scaling down of Italian claims let the radical right foster a devastating sense of victimhood, and the seizure of Fiume showed an answer from outside parliament."}]},
{"id":"ww2-030","num":"30","category":"World War II · History","question":"Germany’s leasehold at Kiaochow passed to Japan rather than back to China at Paris, and the argument over it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, brought Chinese students into the streets.","questions":["Article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles settled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Japan’s favour, handing over the Tsingtao railway and the German rights in a Chinese province.","Secret wartime promises from Britain, France, Italy and Russia, bought with Japanese naval help in the Mediterranean, had largely decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the conference opened."],"answer":"Shandong question","answerText":"Shandong question","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Article 156</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919, Germany renounces to Japan</span><span class=\"dt-k\">May Fourth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919, protests across China</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jinan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">450 Japanese before the war, 25,000 after</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Returned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1922, after the Washington Conference</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","asia","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Shandong question</b>, written Shantung in the diplomacy of the day, was the argument at the Paris Peace Conference over what should become of Germany’s holdings in the Chinese province of Shandong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had joined the Allies in 1914, taken the German leasehold at Kiaochow, and then carried supplies, warships and loans for them while its own economy boomed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It pressed China directly as well: a treaty of 1915 arising from the Twenty-One Demands gave Japan ninety-nine-year leases in south Manchuria, and a second bound China to accept whatever Japan settled with Germany over Shandong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japanese officials and civilians moved into the province’s railways, administration and industries, and the Japanese population of its capital, Jinan, rose from 450 before the war to 25,000 by its end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain, France, Italy and Russia had meanwhile promised in secret wartime treaties to back Japan’s claims there in return for Japanese naval help in the Mediterranean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Washington saw the danger early, a telegram of January 1919 warning that everything the conference might do for China would be futile if Japan’s special right in Shandong were acknowledged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States had entered the war as an Associated Power and held itself unbound by agreements the Allies made before it joined, but those agreements still bound the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Article 156 of the Treaty of Versailles duly made Germany renounce, in favour of Japan, its rights and privileges in Kiaochow and Shandong, the Tsingtao-Tsinanfu railway among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan left Paris one of the Big Five, a founding member of the League of Nations with one of the four permanent seats on its governing body, and mandatory, under the class C terms of the Covenant, for the German island groups of the western Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In China the news that Jiaozhou would not be given back brought students into the streets in the May Fourth Movement, and Japan restored Chinese sovereignty there only in 1922, after the Washington Conference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Rustin Gates, \"War Aims and War Aims Discussions (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11442. [Open access]","\"The Acting Secretary of State to the Commission to Negotiate Peace,\" received January 5, 1919, document 439 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume II</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv02/d439. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Paris Peace Conference and the Treaty of Versailles,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1914-1920/paris-peace. [Open access]","\"The Versailles Treaty, June 28, 1919: Part IV, German Rights and Interests Outside Germany,\" article 156, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/imt/partiv.asp. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" article 22, The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Japanese_delegation_at_the_Paris_Peace_Conference_1919.jpg","title":"The Japanese delegation at Paris, 1919","desc":"Plenipotentiaries, secretaries and military officers of the Japanese delegation to the peace conference, photographed in a courtyard. The print was copied at the Japanese Foreign Ministry archives; the photographer of the 1919 original is unrecorded.","credit":"Unknown photographer, 1919, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_delegation_at_the_Paris_Peace_Conference_1919.jpg","alt":"Black and white group photograph of about sixty men in three rows outside a building, most in dark suits and several in military uniform, the front row seated."},"why":[{"q":"Why could the conference not simply return the province to China?","a":"Because Britain, France, Italy and Russia had promised in secret wartime treaties to back Japan’s claims there in return for Japanese naval help in the Mediterranean."},{"q":"Why had China’s own position been undermined in advance?","a":"Because a treaty of 1915 arising from the Twenty-One Demands bound China to accept whatever Japan settled with Germany over Shandong."},{"q":"Why did the decision matter inside China?","a":"Because the news that Jiaozhou would not be given back brought students into the streets in the May Fourth Movement."}]},
{"id":"ww2-031","num":"31","category":"World War II · History","question":"Japan’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would have written equal treatment for member states’ nationals into the League Covenant, and it won a clear majority of the commission and was ruled not adopted anyway.","questions":["Baron Makino told the plenary session of April 1919 of his government’s poignant regret when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was left out of the Covenant, and promised Japan would press the principle again.","Making no distinction in law or in fact on account of race or nationality was the wording of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the League of Nations Commission declined twice in 1919."],"answer":"racial equality proposal","answerText":"racial equality proposal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Draft reported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 February 1919, without the clause</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second try</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 April 1919, a clear majority</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plenary</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 April 1919, regret placed on record</span><span class=\"dt-k\">US exclusion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924, Japanese immigration barred</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","politics","diplomacy","asia","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>racial equality proposal</b> was Japan’s attempt at the Paris Peace Conference to write a principle of equal treatment for the nationals of member states into the Covenant of the League of Nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its original form, read into the record by Baron Makino Nobuaki, had the parties agree to accord all aliens from member states \"equal and just treatment in every respect, making no distinction, either in law or in fact, on account of their race or nationality\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Makino argued that a man asked to defend the League with his life would naturally demand to stand on an equal footing with the people he was defending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League of Nations Commission did not adopt it, and when the draft Covenant went to a plenary session on 14 February 1919 without it, Japan reserved the right to raise the question again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had reason to expect a hearing, being one of the Big Five at Paris, a founder of the League and one of four powers holding a permanent seat on its governing body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> On 11 April Makino tried a weaker version, a phrase in the Preamble endorsing the equality of nations and the just treatment of their nationals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It won what he called a clear majority of the commission and was still ruled not adopted, for want of unanimity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eight days later the American commissioners in Paris discussed a British-drafted resolution meant to satisfy the Japanese claim \"without exactly stating that recognition in so many words\", and the American Secretary of State doubted it should be inserted at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the plenary of 28 April Makino read the original amendment back into the record and declared his government’s \"poignant regret\", saying Japan would go on pressing the principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Covenant went out with no such clause, and in 1924 the United States barred Japanese immigration outright, breaking the Gentlemen’s Agreement of 1907 and giving great offence in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Preliminary Peace Conference, Protocol No. 5, Plenary Session of April 28, 1919,\" document 7 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv03/d7. [Open access]","Rustin Gates, \"War Aims and War Aims Discussions (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2019), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11442. [Open access]","\"Minutes of the Daily Meetings of the Commissioners Plenipotentiary, Saturday, April 19th, 1919,\" document 53 in <i>Papers Relating to the Foreign Relations of the United States, The Paris Peace Conference, 1919, Volume XI</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1919Parisv11/d53. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" The Avalon Project, Lillian Goldman Law Library, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Immigration Act of 1924 (The Johnson-Reed Act),\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/immigration-act. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/Japanese_peace_delegates_in_1919_with_Makino_Nobuaki.jpg","title":"The Japanese plenipotentiaries at Paris, 1919","desc":"Seated, from the left: Baron Makino Nobuaki, who put the amendment; Marquis Saionji Kinmochi; and Viscount Chinda Sutemi. Standing behind them are Ijuin Hikokichi and Matsui Keishiro, the ambassadors to Italy and France.","credit":"Bain News Service, via the Library of Congress and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_peace_delegates_in_1919_with_Makino_Nobuaki.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of five men in formal dress in a panelled room, three seated on a sofa and two standing behind it."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan connect the principle to military service?","a":"Because Makino argued that a man asked to defend the League with his life would naturally demand to stand on an equal footing with the people he was defending."},{"q":"Why did a proposal with a clear majority fail?","a":"Because it was ruled not adopted for want of unanimity, even in the weaker form that asked only for a phrase in the Preamble."},{"q":"Why did the refusal have consequences beyond Paris?","a":"Because in 1924 the United States barred Japanese immigration outright, breaking the Gentlemen’s Agreement of 1907 and giving great offence in Japan."}]},
{"id":"ww2-032","num":"32","category":"World War II · History","question":"Suspended to pay for the First World War, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was rebuilt through the 1920s, and the rigidity it restored left economies unable to absorb the shocks that came next.","questions":["Britain left the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1931 and the United States in 1933, and by the middle of that year every European debtor but Finland had defaulted on its American loans.","Fixing a currency’s value in gold and letting money move at settled rates, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the frame in which reparations, war debts and American loans all had to circulate."],"answer":"gold standard","answerText":"gold standard","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reparations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">set 1921, 132 billion gold marks</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Allied war debts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over $10 billion owed to America</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Britain off gold</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1931, the United States in 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Defaults</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by mid-1933, all Europe but Finland</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","economics","politics","20th century","europe"],"abstract":"The <b>gold standard</b> fixed a currency’s value in gold and let money move between countries at settled rates, and rebuilding it was the central project of the interwar international economy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system had been suspended to pay for the First World War, and the United States, the European states and Japan spent the 1920s working to restore it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it had to carry was new: Germany owed reparations set in 1921 at 132 billion gold marks, about $31.5 billion, and the Allies owed the United States more than $10 billion lent during the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Washington refused to cancel those debts or to let them be linked to reparations, and in 1922 created a commission to negotiate repayment with the seventeen countries that had borrowed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain answered the same year with the Balfour Note, saying it would seek from its allies and from Germany only as much as it owed the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Dawes Plan of 1924 turned the tangle into a circle: American banks lent Germany enough to pay reparations to France and Britain, which used the money to service their war debts to America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing in that circle earned dollars, and American tariffs saw to it that little else did, the Fordney-McCumber Act of 1922 raising rates and making it harder for Europeans to export to the United States and so earn what they owed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The German cabinet, preparing for the Genoa conference in April 1922, was already discussing debt relief, a working community of central banks and a transition to the gold standard as the way out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When American lending dried up after 1928 the circle stopped: Hoover obtained a one-year moratorium on all debt and reparation payments in 1931, and at Lausanne in 1932 the European powers agreed to cancel their claims on Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain left the gold standard in 1931 and the United States in 1933, and by the middle of that year every European debtor except Finland had defaulted on its American loans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]","\"Cabinet Consultation with the President Regarding the Agenda for Genoa (April 5, 1922),\" <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/cabinet-consultation-with-the-president-regarding-the-agenda-for-genoa-april-5-1922. [Open access]","Gustave A. Nuermberger et al., eds., <i>Foreign Relations of the United States: Diplomatic Papers, 1932, General, Volume I</i> (Washington, DC: Government Printing Office, 1947), https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1932v01. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Lausanne%2C_16-6-1932_%28conf%C3%A9rence%2C_M._Herriot_au_centre%29_-_btv1b53256433g.jpg","title":"Edouard Herriot at Lausanne, 16 June 1932","desc":"The French prime minister, centre, among delegates and reporters on the opening day of the Lausanne conference, at which the European powers agreed to give up their reparation claims on Germany. A press photograph by the Agence Rol, held by the Bibliotheque nationale de France.","credit":"Agence Rol, via Gallica and Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lausanne%2C_16-6-1932_%28conf%C3%A9rence%2C_M._Herriot_au_centre%29_-_btv1b53256433g.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a crowd of men in suits outdoors, one in the middle holding a cigarette and looking towards the camera, roofs and a tower behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the payments form a circle rather than a flow?","a":"Because American banks lent Germany enough to pay reparations to France and Britain, which used the money to service their war debts to America."},{"q":"Why could nothing in that circle earn dollars?","a":"Because American tariffs saw to it that little else did, the Fordney-McCumber Act raising rates and making it harder for Europeans to export to the United States."},{"q":"Why did the system stop when American lending dried up?","a":"Because the circle depended on new loans: within three years there was a moratorium on all payments, the European powers cancelled their claims on Germany, and every debtor but Finland defaulted."}]},
{"id":"ww2-033","num":"33","category":"World War II · History","question":"After a bull market that took the Dow six-fold in eight years, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of October 1929 wiped out almost half that index in under three weeks.","questions":["Buying shares on a tenth down with the stock as collateral was ordinary practice before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why so many small holdings vanished when prices fell.","The Federal Reserve Bank of New York bought securities and lent freely through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and was accused by the Board of exceeding its authority for doing so."],"answer":"Wall Street crash","answerText":"Wall Street crash","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 September 1929, the Dow at 381.17</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Black Monday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 October 1929, down nearly 13 percent</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Black Tuesday</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 October 1929, down nearly 12 percent</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bottom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 July 1932, the Dow at 41.22</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","history","economics","politics","20th century","united states"],"abstract":"The <b>Wall Street crash</b> of October 1929 ended a bull market in which the Dow Jones Industrial Average had risen six-fold since 1921, to a peak of 381.17 on 3 September 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Brokerage houses, investment trusts and margin accounts had let ordinary people buy shares on credit, putting down a tenth of the price and pledging the stock itself as collateral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Federal Reserve was sceptical, holding that its founding act did not contemplate using its resources to extend speculative credit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How to act divided it: the Board wanted reserve banks to refuse credit to members lending to speculators, while George Harrison in New York wanted a higher discount rate, and got 6 percent in August 1929.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because currencies were tied to gold, that tightening pushed foreign central banks to raise their own rates, and the international economy slowed before Wall Street did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> American money had been holding Europe up too, propping the German economy with bank loans until 1928, and when the lending stopped Germany foundered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bankers tried to steady the market in October by buying blocks of shares at high prices in public, and failed; on Black Monday, 28 October, the Dow fell nearly 13 percent, on Black Tuesday nearly 12 more, and by mid-November it had lost almost half its value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Federal Reserve Bank of New York bought government securities, lent freely and cut its discount rate, which other reserve banks thought beyond its authority and which is now credited with keeping the city’s banks open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The crash did not make the Depression: its effect faded within months and by the autumn of 1930 recovery looked close, and it was the banking panics of 1930 and 1931, and the crises after them, that turned a sharp recession into a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tariffs rose as the slump deepened, Hoover having pledged farm protection in 1928 and Congress passing the Tariff Act of 1930; the Dow closed at 41.22 on 8 July 1932, 89 percent below its peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gary Richardson, Alejandro Komai, Michael Gou and Daniel Park, \"Stock Market Crash of 1929,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1929. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","Gary Richardson, \"The Great Depression,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Crowds_gathering_outside_New_York_Stock_Exchange.jpg","title":"Wall Street, 24 October 1929","desc":"The street outside the New York Stock Exchange filled with people during the heavy trading of 24 October, four days before the worst fall. A policeman stands on the steps at the right; Trinity Church closes the view.","credit":"Associated Press, 24 October 1929, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain in the United States. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowds_gathering_outside_New_York_Stock_Exchange.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph looking down a narrow city street packed with hundreds of men in hats, tall stone buildings on both sides and a church spire in the distance."},"why":[{"q":"Why did buying on margin make the fall so steep?","a":"Because ordinary people had bought shares on credit, putting down a tenth of the price and pledging the stock itself as collateral, so falling prices called in the loans."},{"q":"Why did an American rate rise slow the world economy?","a":"Because currencies were tied to gold, so the tightening pushed foreign central banks to raise their own rates, and the international economy slowed before Wall Street did."},{"q":"Why is the crash not the cause of the Depression?","a":"Because its effect faded within months and by the autumn of 1930 recovery looked close; it was the banking panics of 1930 and 1931 that turned a sharp recession into a decade."}]},
{"id":"ww2-034","num":"34","category":"World War II · History","question":"Beginning in August 1929 and ending only with the Second World War, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the longest and deepest downturn in the history of the modern industrial economy.","questions":["Deflation was as much a part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as unemployment, the American money supply falling by nearly 30 percent between 1930 and 1933 and average prices falling with it.","Leaving the gold standard, as Britain did in 1931 and the United States in 1933, was one of the retreats by which governments tried to escape the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its deflation."],"answer":"Great Depression","answerText":"Great Depression","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1929, in the United States</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Money supply</span><span class=\"dt-v\">down nearly 30 percent by 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bottom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1933, a national banking holiday</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World trade</span><span class=\"dt-v\">down about two-thirds, 1929 to 1934</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","history","economics","politics","20th century","united states"],"abstract":"The <b>Great Depression</b> was the longest and deepest downturn in the history of the United States and of the modern industrial economy, beginning in August 1929 and ending during the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not one event but a series: a stock market crash in 1929, regional banking panics in 1930 and 1931, and national and international financial crises from 1931 to 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The worst of it was monetary, the American money supply falling by nearly 30 percent between the autumn of 1930 and the winter of 1933, with average prices falling by as much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That deflation raised the real weight of every debt, distorted decisions, cut consumption, drove unemployment up and pushed banks, firms and households into bankruptcy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Federal Reserve did not stop it, partly because its leaders disagreed: some held that a central bank should lend to solvent banks under run, others that lending should contract when the economy did, and a few that troubled institutions should be left to fail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Defending the gold standard argued for the same tightening, and because currencies were tied to gold, American rate rises pushed recessions onto other countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain left the standard in 1931 and the United States in 1933, and the London Economic Conference of that year broke up without agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Trade was strangled too: the Tariff Act of 1930 entrenched American protection, and world trade fell by about two-thirds between 1929 and 1934.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> American lending abroad had already stopped, and by the middle of 1933 every European debtor except Finland had defaulted on its war loans to the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bottom came in March 1933, when the American banking system collapsed and Roosevelt declared a national banking holiday; reform followed, then a second recession in 1937, and full recovery only with the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gary Richardson, \"The Great Depression,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Dawes Plan, the Young Plan, German Reparations, and Inter-allied War Debts,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/dawes. [Open access]","Gary Richardson, Alejandro Komai, Michael Gou and Daniel Park, \"Stock Market Crash of 1929,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/stock-market-crash-of-1929. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/Unemployed_men_queued_outside_a_depression_soup_kitchen_opened_in_Chicago_by_Al_Capone%2C_02-1931_-_NARA_-_541927.jpg","title":"A soup kitchen queue in Chicago, February 1931","desc":"Men waiting on a wet pavement under a sign reading Free Soup Coffee and Doughnuts for the Unemployed. The kitchen was opened by Al Capone. Held by the National Archives as record 541927.","credit":"Unknown photographer, February 1931, National Archives and Records Administration 541927, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unemployed_men_queued_outside_a_depression_soup_kitchen_opened_in_Chicago_by_Al_Capone%2C_02-1931_-_NARA_-_541927.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a long queue of men in overcoats and caps on a wet city pavement beside a storefront whose sign offers free soup, coffee and doughnuts for the unemployed."},"why":[{"q":"Why did falling prices make the slump worse?","a":"Because deflation raised the real weight of every debt, cut consumption and pushed banks, firms and households into bankruptcy as the money supply fell by nearly 30 per cent."},{"q":"Why did the Federal Reserve not stop it?","a":"Because its leaders disagreed: some held a central bank should lend to solvent banks under run, others that lending should contract with the economy, and a few that troubled institutions should fail."},{"q":"Why did defending gold deepen the trouble abroad?","a":"Because the standard argued for the same tightening, and with currencies tied to gold American rate rises pushed recessions onto other countries."}]},
{"id":"ww2-035","num":"35","category":"World War II · History","question":"Weimar governments after 1930 ruled by emergency decree under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the constitution, which let the president legislate when the parties in the Reichstag could not agree.","questions":["When the last majority coalition fell in March 1930 over half a percentage point of unemployment insurance, government under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the ordinary way Germany was run.","Brüning imposed his austerity against the will of the parliamentary majority through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Hindenburg saw Hitler’s appointment as a continuation of the same policy."],"answer":"Article 48","answerText":"Article 48","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Müller falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 1930, the last majority coalition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Brüning named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 March 1930, by Hindenburg</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unemployed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.3 million, then over 6 million by 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hitler named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1933, in the same policy</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","law","europe","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Article 48</b> of the Weimar constitution let the president govern by emergency decree, and after 1930 that became the ordinary way Germany was run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 27 March 1930 the coalition led by the Social Democrat Hermann Müller broke up over whether to raise unemployment-insurance contributions by half a percentage point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parties could not agree even in the face of a world economic crisis that had begun in the United States and spread around the globe, and President Paul von Hindenburg refused Müller the use of the article.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since late 1929 Hindenburg and his advisers had been planning a government that would rest on his confidence rather than on a majority in parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Heinrich Brüning of the Centre Party had agreed to lead such a cabinet before Müller fell, was appointed chancellor on 29 March, and power began to shift from the Reichstag to the president.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Brüning drove an austerity programme through by emergency decree against the will of the parliamentary majority, raising taxes and cutting spending, salaries, pensions and insurance benefits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The crisis it answered was severe: protectionism spread on both sides of the Atlantic, German exports fell from 13.5 to 5.7 billion Reichsmarks, industrial production by about 40 percent, and unemployment rose from 1.3 million to more than six million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Real incomes fell by a third, and the economic crisis became a crisis of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The National Socialists and the Communists gained at the Reichstag elections of September 1930 and July 1932 while the liberal Democratic Party sank into insignificance, and Brüning’s successors Franz von Papen and Kurt von Schleicher had less parliamentary support than he had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hindenburg understood the appointment of Adolf Hitler as chancellor in 1933 as a continuation of that policy of presidential cabinets, and across the Atlantic the Depression was driving the United States further into isolation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Rebekka von Mallinckrodt, \"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette. [Open access]","Oliver Schweinoch and Arnulf Scriba, \"Die Weltwirtschaftskrise,\" <i>LeMO: Lebendiges Museum Online</i>, Deutsches Historisches Museum, https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/industrie/wirtschaftskrise. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"The Great Depression and U.S. Foreign Policy,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/great-depression. [Open access]","Gary Richardson, \"The Great Depression,\" <i>Federal Reserve History</i>, Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, written as of 22 November 2013, https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/great-depression. [Open access]","United States Department of State, Office of the Historian, \"Protectionism in the Interwar Period,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/protectionism. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ed/Erich_Salomon_Kabinett_Br%C3%BCning_1930.jpg","title":"The Bruning cabinet meeting, 1930","desc":"Ministers of the first Bruning cabinet at a table out of doors, photographed candidly by Erich Salomon. Commons names Kurt Wachsmann, Erich Zweigert, Martin Schiele, Joseph Wirth, Hermann Dietrich, Heinrich Bruning and Hermann Puender among the sitters.","credit":"Erich Salomon, 1930, via Wikimedia Commons, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erich_Salomon_Kabinett_Br%C3%BCning_1930.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of about ten men in suits seated around a long table on grass under trees, several reading papers, white garden chairs in the foreground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a coalition break over half a percentage point?","a":"Because the parties could not agree on unemployment-insurance contributions even in the face of a world economic crisis, and the president refused the chancellor the emergency article."},{"q":"Why was the refusal not simply a constitutional scruple?","a":"Because since late 1929 Hindenburg and his advisers had been planning a government resting on his confidence rather than on a majority in parliament."},{"q":"Why did governing by decree shift power permanently?","a":"Because Brüning drove austerity through against the will of the parliamentary majority, and his successors had less parliamentary support than he had."}]},
{"id":"ww2-036","num":"36","category":"World War II · History","question":"Taking power in Italy in 1922, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave its name to a family of authoritarian nationalist movements and announced the twentieth century as one of authority rather than of liberal individualism.","questions":["A doctrine published under Mussolini’s name held the state to be an absolute beside which individuals count only in relation to it, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> describing itself.","Unlike Nazism, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Italy did not make antisemitism central until 1938, when laws against Jews were passed partly under pressure from Germany."],"answer":"fascism","answerText":"fascism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mussolini named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1922, by Victor Emmanuel III</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One-party state</span><span class=\"dt-v\">built over seven years from 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Doctrine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">published 1932 under Mussolini’s name</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Racial laws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1938, partly under German pressure</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","politics","ideology","europe","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Fascism</b> was the movement that took power in Italy in 1922 and gave its name to a family of authoritarian nationalist movements between the wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its origins lay in the war: committees of public safety had experimented with welfare on hierarchical lines and with the intimidation of political opponents, and right and left, corporativist and syndicalist strands mixed into a new political culture called national radicalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That culture fed fascism and helped keep the fragmented liberal camp from reassembling, the wartime split between neutralists and interventionists persisting afterwards in a more violent form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Gabriele d’Annunzio’s seizure of Fiume in September 1919 and the proto-fascist regency he proclaimed there supplied much of the movement’s style before Mussolini held power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In October 1922 King Victor Emmanuel III appointed Mussolini prime minister, and over the next seven years the Fascists built a one-party dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> It was never quite absolute: the monarchy stayed independent and remained in theory commander-in-chief, and Mussolini’s leadership was nominally subject to the party's supreme body, the Gran Consiglio del Fascismo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In its own account, published under Mussolini’s name, fascism \"conceives of the State as an absolute, in comparison with which all individuals or groups are relative\".<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same text rejects pacifism as cowardice, holds that war alone brings human energy to its highest tension, and calls democracy a regime that gives people the illusion of sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It announced the twentieth century as \"a century of authority\", against the individualism it identified with liberalism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antisemitism was not central to Italian fascism as it was to Nazism: Jews could join the party until 1938, and the antisemitic laws of that year came partly under pressure from Nazi Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","Vanda Wilcox, \"D’Annunzio, Gabriele,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2018), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11260. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","Elena Papadia, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10896. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/MVSN_in_piazza_di_Siena.jpg/1280px-MVSN_in_piazza_di_Siena.jpg","title":"Fascist militia in the piazza di Siena, Rome, 1936","desc":"Ranks of the Voluntary Militia for National Security drawn up in the Villa Borghese, photographed for L'Illustrazione Italiana of 15 March 1936.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, L'Illustrazione Italiana, 15 March 1936. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MVSN_in_piazza_di_Siena.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of several hundred uniformed militiamen standing in ranks on grass among umbrella pines, most with the right arm raised in salute, while a small group of officers walks along the front of the line."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the war leave the ground it grew on?","a":"Because the wartime split between neutralists and interventionists persisted afterwards in a more violent form, keeping the fragmented liberal camp from reassembling."},{"q":"Why was the dictatorship never quite absolute?","a":"Because the monarchy stayed independent and remained in theory commander-in-chief, and the leadership was nominally subject to the party’s supreme body."},{"q":"Why is Italian fascism distinguished from Nazism on antisemitism?","a":"Because Jews could join the party until 1938, and the antisemitic laws of that year came partly under pressure from Nazi Germany."}]},
{"id":"ww2-037","num":"37","category":"World War II · History","question":"Expelled from the Italian Socialist Party for demanding intervention in the war, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded the Fascist movement in 1919 and was appointed prime minister three years later.","questions":["The Acerbo Law and a decree of December 1925 turned the government <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> headed into a dictatorship answerable to the king alone rather than to the Italian parliament.","Dismissed by the king in July 1943 and shot by partisans two years later, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had governed Italy for twenty-one years and taken it into the war beside Germany."],"answer":"Benito Mussolini","answerText":"Benito Mussolini","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 July 1883</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Dovia di Predappio, Italy</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 April 1945, shot by partisans</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In power</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1922 – July 1943</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","history","politics","ideology","italy","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Benito Mussolini</b> founded the Italian Fascist movement and governed Italy from 1922 until 1943.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was born in 1883 at Dovia di Predappio to a blacksmith who named him after the Mexican revolutionary Benito Juárez, and he began his political life as a socialist, editing the party newspaper <i>Avanti!</i> from 1913.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war broke him from the socialists: after an editorial demanding Italian intervention he resigned the paper, and when his own interventionist <i>Il Popolo d’Italia</i> appeared the party expelled him, its funding by industrialists and the French concealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He served seventeen months with the Bersaglieri until a training explosion in February 1917 had him evacuated, and he returned to his paper demanding war until victory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement he inaugurated in March 1919 won only 4,800 votes that November and revived when the strikes of the <i>biennio rosso</i> made its squads useful to the authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its style came out of the war, the squads taking over the symbols and practices of the shock troops and demanding submission to a charismatic leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The king, looking to him for order and stabilisation, appointed him prime minister on 30 October 1922 after some 30,000 Fascists converged on Rome while he waited in Milan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Acerbo Law of 1923 handed the largest party two-thirds of the seats, and a decree of December 1925 made the head of government answerable to the king alone rather than to parliament.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He signed the Pact of Steel with Hitler in May 1939 and took Italy into the war in June 1940; defeat in North Africa and the landing in Sicily brought a vote of no confidence on 25 July 1943, and the king dismissed and arrested him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> German paratroopers freed him to head a puppet Italian state at Salò, and in April 1945 communist partisans caught him near the Swiss border and shot him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Benito Mussolini,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/benito-mussolini-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","Stefano Marcuzzi, \"Victor Emmanuel III, King of Italy,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10977. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Benito_Mussolini_in_divisa_da_bersagliere.jpg/1280px-Benito_Mussolini_in_divisa_da_bersagliere.jpg","title":"Mussolini in Bersaglieri uniform, 1915-1917","desc":"A photograph of Mussolini at the front, inscribed by him to the museum of the Bersaglieri and printed in L'Illustrazione Italiana in 1932.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, published in L'Illustrazione Italiana, 18 September 1932. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benito_Mussolini_in_divisa_da_bersagliere.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a young soldier in a field uniform standing among conifers with a rifle propped beside him, a large handwritten dedication and signature running across the lower part of the print."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the war break him from the socialists?","a":"Because after an editorial demanding Italian intervention he resigned the party newspaper, and when his own interventionist paper appeared the party expelled him."},{"q":"Why did a movement with 4,800 votes revive?","a":"Because the strikes of the biennio rosso made its squads useful to the authorities, so official toleration gave it a place the ballot had not."},{"q":"Why did the king appoint him?","a":"Because he looked to him for order and stabilisation while some 30,000 Fascists converged on Rome and Mussolini himself waited in Milan."}]},
{"id":"ww2-038","num":"38","category":"World War II · History","question":"The insurrection known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Mussolini to office in October 1922, after the king refused to countersign the martial law his own prime minister had ordered.","questions":["Blackshirt columns converged on the capital on the night of 27 and 28 October 1922 in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while Mussolini himself waited in Milan for the king’s invitation.","Fascist Italy counted its own era from 28 October 1922, the date of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and required that year to appear beside the Gregorian date in public documents."],"answer":"March on Rome","answerText":"March on Rome","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Naples congress</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 – 26 October 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Columns move</span><span class=\"dt-v\">night of 27 – 28 October 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Appointed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mussolini, 30 October 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parade in Rome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1922</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","politics","ideology","italy","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>March on Rome</b> was the insurrection of late October 1922 by which the Fascists took power in Italy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mussolini had announced at the end of September that the march would be made, and the party congress held at Naples between 24 and 26 October in effect settled its stages, with the four men later called the quadrumvirs — Balbo, Bianchi, De Bono and De Vecchi — in the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the threat lay two years of squad violence spread across the country, which official toleration had done much to make possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> On the night of 27 and 28 October the columns moved, and some 30,000 armed Fascists converged on the capital while Mussolini himself stayed in Milan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Prime Minister Luigi Facta ordered martial law, and Victor Emmanuel III refused to countersign the decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The king, who looked to Mussolini as the man likeliest to restore order, invited him instead to form a government and appointed him prime minister on 30 October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He came down from Milan by train, and the blackshirts paraded through Rome, ending on 31 October in front of the king and the new prime minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rising was therefore both a threat of force and a handover made by the crown within the constitution, which is why an older account could call it little more than a train journey answering the sovereign’s invitation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More recent work stresses the long preparation and the squad violence of those days, and treats the march as the key to the authoritarian structure that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regime made it a founding myth, counting its own era from 28 October 1922, requiring that year to stand beside the Gregorian date in public acts, and sponsoring an official film of the events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gianmarco Mancosu, \"A Noi! La marcia su Roma tra politica e propaganda,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 53, no. 1 (2023), http://www.studistorici.com/2023/03/29/mancosu_numero_53/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Benito Mussolini,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/benito-mussolini-1. [Open access]","Stefano Marcuzzi, \"Victor Emmanuel III, King of Italy,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10977. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Benito_Mussolini_marching_into_Rome.jpg/1280px-Benito_Mussolini_marching_into_Rome.jpg","title":"Fascists at the Victor Emmanuel monument, Rome, 1922","desc":"A press photograph of the columns filling the piazza below the Victor Emmanuel monument, printed in The Outlook of 29 November 1922.","credit":"Wide World Photos, published in The Outlook 132, no. 13 (29 November 1922), 575. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benito_Mussolini_marching_into_Rome.jpg","alt":"A grainy black-and-white photograph looking down on a Roman piazza, dense columns of people filling the streets and the steps below the colonnaded Victor Emmanuel monument, part of it still under scaffolding."},"why":[{"q":"Why did martial law never take effect?","a":"Because the prime minister ordered it and the king refused to countersign the decree, inviting Mussolini to form a government instead."},{"q":"Why can it be described both as a coup and as a handover?","a":"Because it was a threat of force answered by an appointment made by the crown within the constitution, which is why an older account called it little more than a train journey."},{"q":"Why did the regime make so much of the date?","a":"Because it counted its own era from 28 October 1922, required that year to stand beside the Gregorian date in public acts, and sponsored an official film of the events."}]},
{"id":"ww2-039","num":"39","category":"World War II · History","question":"Passed between 1925 and 1927, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rebuilt Italy as a Fascist state, giving prefects sweeping powers over associations and extending police confinement to political offences.","questions":["A consolidated public security law of 1926, one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, followed an attempt on Mussolini’s life at Bologna and punished those who left the country secretly for political reasons.","Mussolini’s slogan that everything was within the state and nothing against it was given legal form by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which contemporaries saw as ending one regime and beginning another."],"answer":"leggi fascistissime","answerText":"leggi fascistissime","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925 – 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Public security</span><span class=\"dt-v\">consolidated law of 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bologna attempt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 October 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Special Tribunal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">trying cases by 1927</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","politics","law","italy","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>leggi fascistissime</b> were the laws of 1925 to 1927 that turned a government the Fascists led into a Fascist state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their purpose was to realise Mussolini’s slogan that everything was within the state, nothing outside it and nothing against it, and contemporaries understood them as the end of one regime and the beginning of another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were meant to build a new constitutional order rather than simply to remove opponents and put party men in their place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A consolidated public security law was approved in 1926 in the days after an attempt on Mussolini’s life at Bologna on 31 October, and it gave prefects wide discretion over any association held to be acting against the national interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same law extended police confinement to political offences and raised the penalties for leaving the country secretly for political reasons, and a Special Tribunal for the Defence of the State was trying political cases by 1927.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Over the seven years from 1922 the Fascists established and consolidated a one-party dictatorship on these foundations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was never quite absolute: the monarchy stayed independent of the party and remained in theory commander-in-chief, and Mussolini’s leadership was nominally subject to the party’s supreme body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the account published under his name the state leaves the individual a margin of liberty, but the state alone decides how much of that liberty is useful and how much may be taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regime dressed the new order in the memory of the war, its militia taking over the symbols and practices of the wartime shock troops and its ceremonies resting on the cult of the fallen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What was built glorified violence, and it took Italy into a series of wars and finally into a second world conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Francesco Altavilla, \"Il processo di Savona,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 14, no. 2 (2013), http://www.studistorici.com/2013/08/29/altavilla_numero_14/. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Italy,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/italy. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Benito_Mussolini_alla_Camera_dei_Deputati_%2810%29.png/1280px-Benito_Mussolini_alla_Camera_dei_Deputati_%2810%29.png","title":"The Italian Chamber of Deputies in session in the 1920s","desc":"The chamber that passed the laws of 1925 to 1927, seen from the public gallery with the government benches on the left.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 1920s. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benito_Mussolini_alla_Camera_dei_Deputati_%2810%29.png","alt":"A black-and-white photograph looking down into a parliamentary chamber, deputies seated at curved tiers of desks, a long bench of ministers along one wall and four men at a small table on the floor of the house."},"why":[{"q":"Why were these laws more than a purge of opponents?","a":"Because they were meant to build a new constitutional order rather than simply to remove opponents and put party men in their place."},{"q":"Why did an assassination attempt matter to the legislation?","a":"Because the consolidated public security law was approved in the days after the attempt at Bologna, giving prefects wide discretion over any association held to act against the national interest."},{"q":"Why is the liberty the state left not really liberty?","a":"Because the state alone decides how much of that margin is useful and how much may be taken away."}]},
{"id":"ww2-040","num":"40","category":"World War II · History","question":"Fascist Italy replaced free trade unions with state syndicates and banned strikes, building what it called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which it offered as a third way between liberal and communist economies.","questions":["The Carta del Lavoro of 1927 set out the principles of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the order in which people, syndicates and corporations were meant to form one hierarchy under the state.","Corporatism entered fascism through the jurist Alfredo Rocco, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it produced claimed descent from the guilds of medieval Italy while banning strikes under penal sanction."],"answer":"corporate state","answerText":"corporate state","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Syndical laws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">April and July 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ministry created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carta del Lavoro</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council</span><span class=\"dt-v\">powers widened 1930 and 1932</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","economics","italy","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>corporate state</b> was the economic and constitutional order fascist Italy built in the 1920s and 1930s, and one of the defining features of that state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Corporatism entered fascism through the jurist Alfredo Rocco and became the keystone of its economic architecture, offered as a third way between liberal and communist economies and as an answer to the slump of the 1930s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It claimed descent from the guilds of medieval Italy, a lineage that hovers between real history and invented tradition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first fascist programme had already fused revolutionary socialism, syndicalism and extreme nationalism, and corporativist and syndicalist strands ran together in the political culture the movement grew out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two laws of 1926 gave the system its legal form: free trade unions were dissolved and replaced by syndicates authorised and controlled by the state, employers’ organisations were placed under the same tutelage, and a ministry of corporations was created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Strikes and lockouts were forbidden under penal sanction, and collective labour disputes went instead to a labour magistracy that claimed to protect both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Carta del Lavoro of 1927, drafted by Rocco and Carlo Costamagna, set out the principles the order claimed to serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Consiglio Nazionale delle Corporazioni coordinated the syndicates, its powers widened by further legislation in 1930 and 1932, and the liberal chamber of deputies was at last abolished and replaced by an assembly of the fasci and the corporations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People, syndicates, corporations and state formed a single hierarchy, which the regime itself called totalitarian in the sense that no free space was left outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account published under Mussolini’s name says as much, claiming that the state reaches every aspect of national life through its corporative institutions and frames all the economic forces of the nation within them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Mario G. Losano, \"Un modello italiano per l’economia nel Brasile di Getúlio Vargas: la «Carta del Lavoro» del 1927,\" <i>Rechtsgeschichte – Legal History</i> 20 (2012): 274–305, http://data.rg.mpg.de/rechtsgeschichte/rg20_274losano.pdf. [Open access]","Roberta Ferrari, \"Corporativismo fascista e New Deal statunitense. Pianificare tra Stato e business,\" <i>Laboratoire italien</i> 32 (2024), https://doi.org/10.4000/122lg. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","Andrea Baravelli, \"Post-war Societies (Italy),\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10719. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/1927_edition_of_the_Carta_del_Lavoro_commented_by_Giuseppe_Bottai.png/1280px-1927_edition_of_the_Carta_del_Lavoro_commented_by_Giuseppe_Bottai.png","title":"The Carta del Lavoro, Rome, 1927","desc":"The title page of the 1927 Rome edition of the Charter of Labour with Giuseppe Bottai's commentary, dated in the Fascist era as year V.","credit":"Anonymous, Edizioni del Diritto del Lavoro, Rome, 1927. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1927_edition_of_the_Carta_del_Lavoro_commented_by_Giuseppe_Bottai.png","alt":"The title page of a pamphlet, the words La Carta del Lavoro printed large in red between two black rules, with a line below saying it is illustrated by Giuseppe Bottai and an imprint reading Rome, MCMXXVII, Anno V."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the system offered as a third way?","a":"Because it was presented as lying between liberal and communist economies and as an answer to the slump of the 1930s."},{"q":"Why did banning strikes require a new tribunal?","a":"Because with strikes and lockouts forbidden under penal sanction, collective disputes went instead to a labour magistracy that claimed to protect both sides."},{"q":"Why did the regime itself call the order totalitarian?","a":"Because people, syndicates, corporations and state formed a single hierarchy in the sense that no free space was left outside it."}]},
{"id":"ww2-041","num":"41","category":"World War II · History","question":"Fascist Italy built a personal cult around the title of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, treating cinema from the outset as an instrument of consent and remaking its founding myth as its needs changed.","questions":["Ten seconds of a face in half shadow, in the official film of the march on Rome, gave the cult of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the lasting images of its iconography.","Italian propaganda abroad cultivated foreign admirers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the first German life of him was published in Munich in 1924, nine years before Germany had a leader of its own."],"answer":"Duce","answerText":"Duce","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Official film</span><span class=\"dt-v\">A Noi!, 1922</span><span class=\"dt-k\">German life</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Munich, 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A German Führer</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">proclaimed 1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","politics","propaganda","italy","20th century"],"abstract":"The title <b>Duce</b> was the centre of the personal cult that fascist Italy built around Mussolini, and that cult was one of the regime’s chief instruments for manufacturing consent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He came to it as a journalist: the war-diary extracts he published in his own paper already presented him as a Nietzschean superman among submissive comrades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cinema was treated from the beginning as a fundamental instrument of consent, and the official film of the march on Rome gave the cult one of its lasting images, ten seconds of his face in half shadow, staring the audience down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Istituto Luce carried that work on, and the regime’s founding myth was re-articulated as its needs changed, above all around the African empire proclaimed in 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Discipline was the object: the movement demanded complete submission to the party hierarchy and above all to the charismatic leader at the head of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The state described in Mussolini’s own name enforces order, discipline and obedience to the moral code of the country, and holds up the memory of past leaders as an example for future generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of the ritual came out of the war, the militia taking over the symbols of the wartime shock troops and the commemoration of the fallen supplying the regime with a liturgy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cult was exported as well as home-grown: Italian propaganda services abroad cultivated foreign admirers, and the first German life of Mussolini was published in Munich in 1924 by a young journalist of the Nazi party’s press office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From 1933 Germany had a leader of its own, and what German admirers were allowed to say about a second leader sent by providence changed with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is largely what the regime chose to show, since the images of the cult were made, kept and remade by the propaganda apparatus that produced them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Gianmarco Mancosu, \"A Noi! La marcia su Roma tra politica e propaganda,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 53, no. 1 (2023), http://www.studistorici.com/2023/03/29/mancosu_numero_53/. [Open access]","Brian R. Sullivan, \"Mussolini, Benito,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2017), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11178. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","Benito Mussolini, <i>The Political and Social Doctrine of Fascism</i>, authorised translation by Jane Soames (London: Hogarth Press, 1934), https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.217879. [Open access]","Claire Lorenzelli, \"Un Mussolini munichois ? Construction et utilisation du culte du « Duce » dans différents cercles de sociabilité bavarois (1922-1943),\" <i>Laboratoire italien</i> 30 (2023), https://doi.org/10.4000/laboratoireitalien.10074. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Dichiarazione_di_guerra_a_Piazza_Venezia_%2810_giugno_1940%29.jpg/1280px-Dichiarazione_di_guerra_a_Piazza_Venezia_%2810_giugno_1940%29.jpg","title":"The crowd in Piazza Venezia, 10 June 1940","desc":"The square below the balcony of the Palazzo Venezia during the speech announcing Italy's declaration of war on France and Britain.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 10 June 1940, Corriere della Sera. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dichiarazione_di_guerra_a_Piazza_Venezia_%2810_giugno_1940%29.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph taken from above of a Roman square filled edge to edge with a dense crowd, banners held up among them, beside the long plain facade of a palace with a small balcony above its doorway."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the cult treated as an instrument rather than a flourish?","a":"Because it was one of the regime’s chief means of manufacturing consent, cinema being treated from the beginning as a fundamental instrument of it."},{"q":"Why was the leader’s image tied to the memory of the war?","a":"Because the militia took over the symbols of the wartime shock troops and the commemoration of the fallen supplied the regime with a liturgy."},{"q":"Why is the surviving evidence hard to read straight?","a":"Because what survives is largely what the regime chose to show, the images having been made, kept and remade by the propaganda apparatus that produced them."}]},
{"id":"ww2-042","num":"42","category":"World War II · History","question":"Italian troops crossed the Ethiopian frontier in October 1935, opening the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the League of Nations proved unable to stop despite Ethiopia’s membership of it.","questions":["The Hoare-Laval plan of December 1935 would have ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Ethiopia’s expense, and is now read as an early form of the appeasement of the later 1930s.","Defeat at Maychew and the fall of Addis Ababa decided the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which sanctions were wound up and Italy moved away from Britain and France towards Germany."],"answer":"Second Italo-Ethiopian War","answerText":"Second Italo-Ethiopian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Frontier crossed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 October 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hoare-Laval</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rhineland</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 March 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sanctions ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">summer 1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","war","diplomacy","africa","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Second Italo-Ethiopian War</b> was the invasion of Ethiopia by fascist Italy in 1935 and the conquest that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On 2 October 1935 the emperor telegraphed the League of Nations that Italian troops had crossed the frontier south of Mount Mussa Ali and were establishing themselves on Ethiopian soil in preparation for an extensive attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ethiopia was a member of the League, and the organisation’s helplessness before an attack on one of its own members did lasting damage to its authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain and France mattered more to each other and to the Italian government than Ethiopia did, and both preferred an agreement made at Ethiopian expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That preference produced the plan put forward in December 1935 by the French premier and the British foreign secretary, an early form of the appeasement that would govern their policy at the end of the decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> German troops entered the Rhineland on 7 March 1936, which lowered the powers’ interest in the war and raised their interest in bringing Italy back into Europe against Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ethiopia’s attempt to strike the Italian army ended in defeat at Maychew, and the fall of Addis Ababa and the emperor’s departure abroad closed off any negotiated ending.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sanctions were wound up in the summer of 1936: the great powers wanted normal relations with Italy again, and the defence of Ethiopian independence had been treated as a moral duty rather than an interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Italy left the agreements of early 1935 behind and moved towards Germany instead, while at home the new African empire proclaimed in 1936 was worked into the regime’s own founding myth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The war was read racially far beyond Africa, and to many African-Americans Ethiopia stood for sovereignty and achievement, though the help they were able to send was slight beside the sympathy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["T. P. Nesterova, \"France and Attempt to Resolve the Italo-Ethiopian Conflict in Late 1935: the Hoare–Laval Plan,\" <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i>, no. 8 (2020): 398–411, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2020-8-398-411. [Open access]","T. P. Nesterova, \"France, Italy and Diplomatic End of Italo-Ethiopian War in League of Nations (May—July 1936),\" <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i>, no. 8 (2021): 400–413, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2021-8-400-413. [Open access]","The Consul at Geneva (Gilbert) to the Secretary of State, 2 October 1935, in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1935, General, the Near East and Africa</i>, vol. 1, doc. 543, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1935v01/d543. [Open access]","T. P. Nesterova, \"Ethiopia and Great Powers in Final Stage of Italo-Ethiopian War (March — May 1936),\" <i>Nauchnyi dialog</i> 12, no. 8 (2023): 438–454, https://doi.org/10.24224/2227-1295-2023-12-8-438-454. [Open access]","Gianmarco Mancosu, \"A Noi! La marcia su Roma tra politica e propaganda,\" <i>Diacronie. Studi di Storia Contemporanea</i> 53, no. 1 (2023), http://www.studistorici.com/2023/03/29/mancosu_numero_53/. [Open access]","Edward O. Erhagbe and Ehimika A. Ifidon, \"African-Americans and the Italo–Ethiopian Crisis, 1935–1936: The Practical Dimension of Pan-Africanism,\" <i>Aethiopica</i> 11 (2008): 68–84, https://doi.org/10.15460/aethiopica.11.1.187. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Abyssinian-soldiers-1936-142348340618.jpg/1280px-Abyssinian-soldiers-1936-142348340618.jpg","title":"Ethiopian horsemen, 1936","desc":"Mounted troops of the Ethiopian army photographed during the war with Italy.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 1936, Ahlen and Akerlund. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abyssinian-soldiers-1936-142348340618.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a close-packed troop of horsemen on grey horses, rifles slung across their backs and rows of pennants on tall staffs rising behind them."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the attack damage the League so badly?","a":"Because Ethiopia was a member, and the organisation’s helplessness before an attack on one of its own members did lasting damage to its authority."},{"q":"Why did Britain and France prefer a bargain at Ethiopia’s expense?","a":"Because they mattered more to each other and to the Italian government than Ethiopia did, which produced the plan of December 1935."},{"q":"Why were sanctions wound up in the summer of 1936?","a":"Because the great powers wanted normal relations with Italy again, especially after German troops entered the Rhineland, and Ethiopian independence had been treated as a moral duty rather than an interest."}]},
{"id":"ww2-043","num":"43","category":"World War II · History","question":"Founded as a Munich splinter group in 1919 and renamed in 1920, the party of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> published a programme confining citizenship to those of German blood and rejecting the Versailles treaty.","questions":["The doctrine of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divided humanity into races with inherited traits, put Germans at the top of that hierarchy, and demanded living space for them in eastern Europe.","Presenting itself as the heir of the trench soldiers, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> condemned parliament as the source of national weakness and demanded submission to a charismatic leader."],"answer":"Nazism","answerText":"Nazism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Party founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 January 1919, Munich</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hitler joins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">autumn 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 February 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Programme</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 points, 1920</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","politics","ideology","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Nazism</b>, the National Socialism of Hitler’s party, grew out of a small antisemitic and völkisch splinter group founded in Munich in January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hitler joined it that autumn, and at a meeting of two thousand people in February 1920 the German Workers’ Party was renamed the National Socialist German Workers’ Party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The programme published on 24 February 1920 demanded the union of all Germans in a greater Germany and the invalidation of the peace treaties of Versailles and Saint-Germain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It confined citizenship to those of German blood and stated that no Jew could belong to the nation, so racial exclusion stood in the party’s first published aims.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same document demanded land to feed the people, a national army, a press closed to non-Germans and a strong central power, and its authors promised to pursue those points ruthlessly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The movement presented itself as the heir of the trench soldiers, taking the wartime shock troops as the model for a new political soldier and dressing its militia in their symbols and practices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It condemned democracy and parliament as the source of national weakness, branded the parties of the left as anti-national, and demanded submission to the hierarchy and above all to a charismatic leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once Germany had such a leader of its own in 1933, his cult set the terms even for those abroad who admired a different one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its doctrine held that humanity was divided into races whose traits were inherited, that some races were superior to others, and that Germans as ‘Aryans’ stood at the top as a master race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From that came the demand for ‘living space’ in eastern Europe, the plan to remove or dominate the people already there, and the persecution that ended in the murder of six million Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Claire Lorenzelli, \"Un Mussolini munichois ? Construction et utilisation du culte du « Duce » dans différents cercles de sociabilité bavarois (1922-1943),\" <i>Laboratoire italien</i> 30 (2023), https://doi.org/10.4000/laboratoireitalien.10074. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/1920-02-24_25-points-program_NSDAP_page1.jpg/1280px-1920-02-24_25-points-program_NSDAP_page1.jpg","title":"The party programme of 24 February 1920","desc":"The first page of the printed twenty-five point programme, its opening demands set in two columns of blackletter.","credit":"Printed programme of the NSDAP, 24 February 1920. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1920-02-24_25-points-program_NSDAP_page1.jpg","alt":"A printed sheet headed Programm der National-Sozialistischen Deutschen Arbeiter-Partei in heavy blackletter type, with numbered demands running down two columns beneath it."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the 1920 programme matter to what followed?","a":"Because it confined citizenship to those of German blood and stated that no Jew could belong to the nation, so racial exclusion stood in the party’s first published aims."},{"q":"Why did the movement present itself as the heir of the trenches?","a":"Because it took the wartime shock troops as the model for a new political soldier and dressed its militia in their symbols and practices."},{"q":"Why did the racial doctrine require territory?","a":"Because holding Germans to be a master race produced the demand for living space in eastern Europe and the plan to remove or dominate the people already there."}]},
{"id":"ww2-044","num":"44","category":"World War II · History","question":"Appointed chancellor in January 1933 rather than voted into office, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had rebuilt his party around elections after the failed Bavarian coup of November 1923.","questions":["Partially blinded by gas in 1918 and drawn into politics through an army information office, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made his first antisemitic speeches in Munich in August 1919.","A trial for treason in 1924 gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a platform against parliamentary democracy, and the prison term that followed gave him the time to write his book."],"answer":"Adolf Hitler","answerText":"Adolf Hitler","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 April 1889, Austria</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Joins the party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Putsch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1923</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chancellor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 January 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","history","politics","ideology","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Adolf Hitler</b> was the Austrian-born leader of the Nazi party and, from January 1933, the dictator of Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in Austria in 1889, he lived in Vienna from February 1908 until May 1913, drifting and supporting himself by painting watercolours and sketches, and the war gave his life its direction when he left for Munich and joined the German army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was wounded twice, decorated, and partially blinded by mustard gas near Ypres in October 1918; the armistice reached him in a military hospital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Back in Munich he worked for an army information office that gathered intelligence on civilian parties and gave the troops anti-communist instruction, and in August 1919, as a course instructor, he made his first violent antisemitic speeches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the party that October, helped write the 1920 programme of racist antisemitism and expansionist nationalism, and by 1921 was its absolute leader, with membership at 55,000 and more than four thousand men in its militia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The attempt to seize power in Bavaria in November 1923 failed, and he turned his trial for treason into a platform against parliamentary democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Convicted, he served less than a year, and used the imprisonment to begin the book that set out his racial and expansionist programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After his release he rebuilt and reunified the party and chose elections instead of force, campaigning for rearmament and sovereignty and against Versailles, communism and what he called Jewish influence in German life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement he led demanded submission to the party hierarchy and above all to a charismatic leader, which is what he made of himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His party took 2.6 per cent of the vote in 1928 and 37.3 per cent in July 1932, and he was appointed chancellor by the president rather than voted into office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1974-082-44%2C_Adolf_Hitler_im_Ersten_Weltkrieg.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1974-082-44%2C_Adolf_Hitler_im_Ersten_Weltkrieg.jpg","title":"Hitler's regiment in the field, 1914-1918","desc":"A group photograph of German soldiers behind the lines in the First World War; the German Federal Archives identifies Hitler among them.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1974-082-44, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1974-082-44,_Adolf_Hitler_im_Ersten_Weltkrieg.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of eight German soldiers in field caps posed against a brick wall, one lying on the grass in front and a small white dog on a comrade's knee."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a failed coup help rather than finish him?","a":"Because he turned his trial for treason into a platform against parliamentary democracy, served less than a year, and used the imprisonment to begin the book setting out his programme."},{"q":"Why did he choose elections after 1924?","a":"Because force had failed in Bavaria, so he rebuilt the party and campaigned instead for rearmament and sovereignty and against Versailles and communism."},{"q":"Why is it significant that he was appointed rather than elected?","a":"Because his party took 37.3 per cent in July 1932 and never a majority, so the chancellorship came from the president rather than from a vote."}]},
{"id":"ww2-045","num":"45","category":"World War II · History","question":"Founded in Munich in January 1919 and renamed in February 1920, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one of many small völkisch groups before it built a paramilitary wing.","questions":["Several German states banned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1922 as a danger to the republic, and it was dissolved outright after the failed Bavarian coup of 1923.","With about 55,000 members by 1923, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> still took only 2.6 per cent of the national vote in May 1928, while the republic was stable."],"answer":"NSDAP","answerText":"NSDAP","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 January 1919, Munich</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Renamed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 February 1920</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Militia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sturmabteilung, 1921</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925, after the ban</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","history","politics","germany","ideology","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>NSDAP</b>, the National Socialist German Workers’ Party, began as the German Workers’ Party founded in Munich on 5 January 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of many small antisemitic and völkisch groups in a city that had become the centre of right-wing agitation against the Weimar Republic, and it was taken at first for an unimportant debating circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hitler joined in the autumn of 1919 and drew audiences to it, and on 24 February 1920 the party took its new name at a meeting of two thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its published programme of that day demanded a greater Germany and the invalidation of the Versailles treaty, confined citizenship to those of German blood, and was anti-democratic and anti-communist alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the end of 1921 the party had a paramilitary wing, the Sturmabteilung, which fought its street battles and supplied its parades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its language was extreme enough that several German states banned it in 1922 as a danger to the Weimar Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It took the wartime shock troops as its model of a political soldier, and its own militia borrowed their symbols and practices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The party had about 55,000 members and more than 4,000 men in its militia by 1923, when its leaders tried to seize power in Bavaria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Banned and dissolved after that failure, it was permitted again in 1925, rebuilt under one leader, given new formations in the SS that year and the Hitler Youth in the next, and organised from 1928 into a structure matching the electoral districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so it stayed marginal while the Weimar Republic was stable, taking between 1.6 and 2.5 per cent in the state elections of 1926 and 1927 and 2.6 per cent nationally in May 1928, when it had about a hundred thousand members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-04651A%2C_Preu%C3%9Fen%2C_Wahlplakat_Nationalsozialisten.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-04651A%2C_Preu%C3%9Fen%2C_Wahlplakat_Nationalsozialisten.jpg","title":"A party election poster in Prussia, 1932","desc":"Passers-by reading a Nazi election poster pasted round an advertising column, from the presidential campaign of 1932.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-04651A, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-04651A,_Preu%C3%9Fen,_Wahlplakat_Nationalsozialisten.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of five people in coats and caps standing with their backs to the camera, reading a large blackletter poster wrapped around a cylindrical advertising column on a street corner."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Munich where such a party could grow?","a":"Because the city had become the centre of right-wing agitation against the Weimar Republic, so one small antisemitic and völkisch group among many had an audience to draw on."},{"q":"Why did some German states ban it as early as 1922?","a":"Because its language was extreme enough to be judged a danger to the republic, and it already had a paramilitary wing fighting its street battles."},{"q":"Why did it stay marginal for most of the 1920s?","a":"Because the Weimar Republic was stable: it took between 1.6 and 2.5 per cent in state elections of 1926 and 1927 and 2.6 per cent nationally in May 1928."}]},
{"id":"ww2-046","num":"46","category":"World War II · History","question":"Hitler and other Nazi leaders tried to seize Bavaria on 8 and 9 November 1923 in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, meaning to march on Berlin once the state was theirs.","questions":["The trial that followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made Hitler famous in nationalist circles, and the prison term it brought gave him the leisure to write his book.","Bavarian authorities banned the party, its militia and its newspaper after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> collapsed, and it was permitted again only in 1925."],"answer":"Beer Hall Putsch","answerText":"Beer Hall Putsch","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attempted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 – 9 November 1923</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Munich, 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sentence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">five years, under nine served</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Party banned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1923 – 1925</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","politics","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Beer Hall Putsch</b> was the attempt by Hitler and other Nazi leaders to seize power in Bavaria on 8 and 9 November 1923.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The plan was to take the Bavarian state first and then march on Berlin to overthrow the government in Berlin, which the party had refused to compete with at the ballot box.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party had about 55,000 members at the time, and its leaders had decided that power was to be taken by force rather than sought through the elections they despised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rising collapsed quickly against the Bavarian police, and Hitler was arrested and tried for high treason before a Munich court along with the other ringleaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He used the trial as a stage against parliamentary democracy and for xenophobic nationalism, and it carried his name into the German and international press and made him famous in nationalist circles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The court sentenced him to five years, of which he served less than nine months, a leniency that says much about where the sympathies of the Bavarian bench lay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He began writing in Landsberg prison the book that became his best-known work, hoping it would earn money and spread his views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Bavarian authorities answered the attempt by banning and dissolving the party, its militia and its newspaper, so that the movement lost at once its organisation and its voice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party that had grown out of a small Munich splinter group of January 1919 was thus broken within four years of taking its name, and its leader was in prison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When it was allowed again the strategy changed rather than the doctrine, and the movement kept its contempt for parliament and its demand for a leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Adolf_Hitler_m.fl._i_M%C3%BCnchen%2C_1924.jpg/1280px-Adolf_Hitler_m.fl._i_M%C3%BCnchen%2C_1924.jpg","title":"The defendants at Munich, 1924","desc":"The accused photographed at the treason trial that followed the putsch, their names lettered along the foot of the print.","credit":"Photograph by Heinrich Hoffmann, 1924. Bundesarchiv, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolf_Hitler_m.fl._i_M%C3%BCnchen,_1924.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of nine men standing in a line outside a building, most in uniform with swords and medals, two in civilian overcoats, with their surnames handwritten across the bottom of the image."},"why":[{"q":"Why take Bavaria first rather than Berlin?","a":"Because the plan was to seize the state and then march on the national government, which the party had refused to compete with at the ballot box."},{"q":"Why did the sentence say something about the court?","a":"Because he served less than nine months of five years, a leniency that indicates where the sympathies of the Bavarian bench lay."},{"q":"Why did losing the party hurt more than losing the coup?","a":"Because the authorities banned and dissolved the party, its militia and its newspaper, so the movement lost at once its organisation and its voice."}]},
{"id":"ww2-047","num":"47","category":"World War II · History","question":"Begun in Landsberg prison in 1924 and published in two volumes, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> set out a racial view of history, dictatorship at home and expansion in the east.","questions":["Sales of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rose with the party’s vote, reaching almost 230,000 copies by the end of 1932 and more than twelve million printed by the end of 1944.","Bavaria held the copyright of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the war and used it to prevent reprinting in Germany until that copyright expired at the end of 2015."],"answer":"Mein Kampf","answerText":"Mein Kampf","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924, Landsberg prison</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">volume one 1925, volume two 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Printed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 12 million by 1944</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copyright</span><span class=\"dt-v\">expired end of 2015</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["text","history","politics","ideology","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Mein Kampf</b>, meaning My Struggle, is the book Hitler began writing in Landsberg prison in 1924 after his conviction for high treason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had meant to call it a reckoning with four and a half years of lies, stupidity and cowardice, and the title was shortened before publication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party’s own publishing house issued the first volume in 1925 and the second in the following year, and in the summer of 1928 he wrote a further book on foreign policy that was never published and was found only in 1958.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it he set out a racial and social Darwinist view of history, dictatorship at home, military expansion, and the seizure of living space in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That living space was to be taken in eastern Europe, whose peoples were to be removed, dominated or killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The book was not an immediate success: the first edition of ten thousand copies largely sold out and a second was printed, but sales slackened quickly afterwards while the party remained small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They rose with the party’s fortunes, and by the end of 1932, after it had become the largest party in parliament, almost 230,000 copies had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Hitler’s appointment as chancellor in January 1933 made the author a multi-millionaire, with more than 850,000 copies sold in that year alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than twelve million copies had been printed by the end of 1944, most of them after 1939, in editions for newlyweds, in braille, and in translations into more than a dozen languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the war the Allies withdrew it from circulation with the rest of Nazi propaganda, and the copyright passed to Bavaria, which used it to prevent German reprinting until it expired at the end of 2015 and a critical edition appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Lebensraum,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lebensraum. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Mein_Kampf_dust_jacket.jpeg/1280px-Mein_Kampf_dust_jacket.jpeg","title":"The dust jacket of the second volume","desc":"The jacket of the volume subtitled The National Socialist Movement, issued by the party's own publishing house in Munich.","credit":"Dust jacket, Franz Eher Nachfolger, Munich, 1926-1927, New York Public Library. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mein_Kampf_dust_jacket.jpeg","alt":"A book jacket with the title set large in red outlined letters on a cream ground, the author's name in white on black bands above and below."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the book sell so poorly at first?","a":"Because the party remained small: the first edition of ten thousand largely sold out and a second was printed, and sales slackened quickly after that."},{"q":"Why did sales track politics rather than the other way round?","a":"Because almost 230,000 copies had gone by the end of 1932, after the party became the largest in parliament, and more than 850,000 in 1933 alone."},{"q":"Why could Germany prevent reprinting after the war?","a":"Because the copyright passed to Bavaria, which used it to block German reprints until it expired at the end of 2015."}]},
{"id":"ww2-048","num":"48","category":"World War II · History","question":"The promise of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> offered Germans an end to class and confessional division, on condition that membership of the nation was settled by descent.","questions":["Drawing on trench comradeship and older völkisch ideas, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was offered as the answer to every social division of the Weimar Republic.","Since the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was defined by race, the belonging it offered required the exclusion of everyone the party counted as outside the German people."],"answer":"Volksgemeinschaft","answerText":"Volksgemeinschaft","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Idea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">völkisch, and from 1914 – 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Programme</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1920, membership by descent</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In power</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ranking</span><span class=\"dt-v\">races ranked by supposed traits</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","politics","ideology","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Volksgemeinschaft</b>, the people’s community, was the social promise at the centre of Nazi ideology, and the thing the movement offered Germans in exchange for their liberties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It drew on older völkisch ideas of community and on the comradeship men remembered, or believed they remembered, from the trenches of the First World War, where rank and class were held to have counted for nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nazis propagated it as the solution to every political and social antagonism of the Weimar Republic, at a time when those antagonisms had brought the state repeatedly to a standstill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The community was defined by race and modelled on an idealised picture of the ancient Germans, and it was meant to cancel differences of birth, occupation, wealth and education among those counted as members.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Who counted was settled by descent: the party’s own programme confined membership of the nation to those of German blood and excluded Jews by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The doctrine behind it divided humanity into races whose traits were held to be inherited, ranked those races against one another, and placed Germans at the top of the ranking as a supposed master race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Unity of that kind required an enemy, and the movement branded the parties of the left as anti-national and democracy itself as the source of weakness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It also required obedience, and the party demanded complete submission to its hierarchy and above all to the leader at its head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the seizure of power the promise took on a central function in establishing the new dictatorship, since a community defined in this way had no place for parties, unions or private loyalties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it offered ordinary Germans was belonging; what it required was the exclusion, and finally the destruction, of those it defined as outside the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["\"Volksgemeinschaft,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/ns-regime/innenpolitik/volksgemeinschaft.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-020-23%2C_N%C3%BCrnberg%2C_WHW-Werbetafel.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-020-23%2C_N%C3%BCrnberg%2C_WHW-Werbetafel.jpg","title":"A Winter Relief hoarding in Nuremberg","desc":"A street board calling for sacrifice to the Winter Relief campaign, one of the ways the community was made a daily obligation.","credit":"Photograph by Kurt Grimm. Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1984-020-23, CC BY-SA 3.0 DE. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1984-020-23,_N%C3%BCrnberg,_WHW-Werbetafel.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a large blackletter poster on a wooden frame at a street corner, a medieval round tower behind it and a man walking past."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the promise attractive in the Weimar years?","a":"Because it was offered as the solution to every political and social antagonism of the republic, at a time when those antagonisms had brought the state repeatedly to a standstill."},{"q":"Why did the community need an enemy?","a":"Because a unity of that kind was defined by exclusion, so the movement branded the parties of the left as anti-national and democracy itself as the source of weakness."},{"q":"Why did the idea serve the dictatorship after 1933?","a":"Because a community defined by race and obedience had no place for parties, unions or private loyalties."}]},
{"id":"ww2-049","num":"49","category":"World War II · History","question":"The claim that Jews form a biologically separate and inferior race, or <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, dates from the late nineteenth century and became central to Nazi doctrine.","questions":["Older religious hostility was recast as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once theories of race and eugenics gave the prejudice the appearance of science.","Written into the party programme of 1920, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced the demand that Jews be excluded from the nation and finally the murder of six million."],"answer":"racial antisemitism","answerText":"racial antisemitism","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Older hatred</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from antiquity and early Christianity</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Racial form</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 19th century</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Forged plot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">around 1900</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the programme</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1920</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","ideology","religion","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Racial antisemitism</b> is the false claim that Jews are a biologically separate, inferior or parasitic race, and it was a central component of Nazi ideology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hostility to Jews in Europe was far older, reaching back to antiquity and early Christianity, and Christianity played a central part in spreading it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1900 antisemitic stereotypes, misconceptions and myths were well established and widely accepted in Germany and other European societies, which is what later made the Holocaust possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The racial form of it dates from the late nineteenth century, when theories of race and eugenics were given the appearance of science and old religious hostility was recast as a claim about biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political movements built on it demanded the official exclusion of Jews from economic, social and political life, and some demanded their forced emigration; scapegoating of this kind returns in times of upheaval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The most infamous of the conspiracy theories appeared around 1900 in a fabricated publication purporting to reveal a Jewish plot to rule the world, one of a family of lies that cast Jews as hidden masters of press and finance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Nazi party drew on that inheritance and wrote it into its first programme, which confined the nation to those of German blood and excluded Jews by name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hitler made his first violent antisemitic speeches in Munich in 1919 and set the argument down on paper a month later, urging that Jews be removed from Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His book promoted the same view as one of the components of the movement’s doctrine, alongside its racial hierarchy and its foreign policy of conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The doctrine falsely claimed that Jews were an inferior race and an existential threat, and it led to the murder of six million of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Boycot_of_Jewish_shops_april_1_1933.jpeg/1280px-Boycot_of_Jewish_shops_april_1_1933.jpeg","title":"The boycott of 1 April 1933","desc":"Uniformed men outside a shop in Germany during the boycott, beside a placard denouncing its Jewish owners.","credit":"Anonymous photograph, 1 April 1933. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boycot_of_Jewish_shops_april_1_1933.jpeg","alt":"A black-and-white photograph of a shopfront with a large handwritten placard on a post beside it, a uniformed man standing in front of the window and passers-by at the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the racial form dated to the late nineteenth century?","a":"Because theories of race and eugenics were then given the appearance of science, and an older religious hostility was recast as a claim about biology."},{"q":"Why did the older prejudice matter to what came after?","a":"Because by 1900 antisemitic stereotypes and myths were well established and widely accepted across European societies, which is what later made the Holocaust possible."},{"q":"Why do such claims resurface in particular periods?","a":"Because scapegoating of this kind returns in times of upheaval, when movements demand the exclusion of a named group from economic, social and political life."}]},
{"id":"ww2-050","num":"50","category":"World War II · History","question":"Coined by a geographer in 1901 and taken up by the Nazis, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meant the seizure of land in eastern Europe to feed and settle the German people.","questions":["The demand for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was inseparable from racial doctrine, since the land was to be taken on the claim that Germans were a master race.","Germany had held ground as far east as Minsk in the First World War, and the loss of it sharpened the later demand for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the same region."],"answer":"Lebensraum","answerText":"Lebensraum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Coined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1901, by Friedrich Ratzel</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the east</span><span class=\"dt-v\">German advance to Minsk, 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the book</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925 – 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Acted on</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1939</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","politics","ideology","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Lebensraum</b>, or living space, was the demand that Germany take land in the east to feed and settle its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word was coined in 1901 by the geographer Friedrich Ratzel, who held that a nation had to be self-sufficient in resources and territory to be safe from outside threats, and who pointed to the British and French empires as the model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He and others misapplied Darwin to states, arguing that nations struggle for resources and that the fittest expand into other people’s territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans had looked east for such room long before the Nazis, since the German colonisation of the Middle Ages, and by the twentieth century many wrote of eastern lands as rich country wasted on peoples they called inferior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German armies did take that ground in the First World War, reaching Minsk and running a military dictatorship there, and lost it again in defeat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hitler, who had served in that war, insisted Germany must never again be beaten by a shortage of resources, and spoke of the ores of the Urals and the farmland of Ukraine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His book made the seizure of living space in the east one of the party’s central aims, beside its antisemitism and its racial view of the world, and he returned to the theme in the second book he left unpublished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The demand was inseparable from that racial doctrine: the land was to be taken because Germans were held to be a master race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people already living there were to be removed, dominated or murdered, and the occupation of eastern Europe was carried out with extreme brutality.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The movement that made this demand had condemned democracy as weakness and promised instead a nation disciplined enough, and ruthless enough, to take whatever it decided it needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Lebensraum,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/lebensraum. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Mein Kampf,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/mein-kampf. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Greater_German_Reich_in_1942.png/1280px-Greater_German_Reich_in_1942.png","title":"Europe under German control in 1942","desc":"A modern map of the extent of German rule and occupation at its furthest, the demand for living space as it was finally acted on.","credit":"Map by the Wikimedia contributor Mackay 86, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_German_Reich_in_1942.png","alt":"A colour map of Europe with Germany and its annexed territory in dark red, occupied and allied countries in orange, and the Italian sphere in green."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the demand argued from biology?","a":"Because its authors misapplied Darwin to states, holding that nations struggle for resources and that the fittest expand into other people’s territory."},{"q":"Why was the east the direction chosen?","a":"Because Germans had looked there since the medieval colonisation, German armies had held that ground in the First World War and lost it, and its farmland and ores were coveted."},{"q":"Why was the demand inseparable from the racial doctrine?","a":"Because the land was to be taken on the ground that Germans were a master race, and the people living there were to be removed, dominated or murdered."}]},
{"id":"ww2-051","num":"51","category":"World War II · History","question":"Nazi doctrine identified Germans as members of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which it placed at the top of a ranking of peoples whose traits were supposed to be inherited and fixed.","questions":["The blond and blue-eyed ideal of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fitted many Germans badly, and the regime never persecuted anyone for the colour of their hair or eyes.","A national community open to every member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was promised, on the understanding that Jews, Black people and Romani people were excluded from it as outsiders."],"answer":"Aryan race","answerText":"Aryan race","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Programme</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1920, membership by blood</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Doctrine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">races ranked by supposed traits</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In power</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Consequence</span><span class=\"dt-v\">exclusion, then mass murder</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","ideology","history","science","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Aryan race</b> was the invented category at the top of the Nazi racial order, and the standard by which everyone else in Germany was measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doctrine held that humanity was divided into races whose traits passed unchanged from one generation to the next, and that those traits fixed a people’s looks, intelligence, creativity and strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Races so defined were then ranked against one another, and those with the supposedly better traits were said to dominate the rest, which turned an invented biology into a claim about who ought to rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans were identified as Aryans and placed at the top of that ranking, which is why the regime’s propaganda called them a master race and taught schoolchildren to think of themselves in those terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The type was idealised as blond, blue-eyed, tall and athletic, though many Germans the regime counted as Aryan looked nothing like it, and hair and eye colour were never the ground of persecution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state built on the doctrine promised a community open to all Aryan Germans whatever their class, region or religion, and excluded Jews, Black people and Romani people from it as racial outsiders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The claim had been written into the party’s first programme, which allowed membership of the nation only to those of German blood and named Jews as excluded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its antisemitic half was an older prejudice restated as biology: a hatred with roots in antiquity and in early Christianity, recast in the late 19th century as a claim about descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The movement that carried the doctrine also condemned democracy as weakness and branded the parties of the left as enemies of the nation, so that the racial argument and the political one worked as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What followed was not an argument but a policy, since the ranking was used to justify first exclusion from the community, then expulsion from public life, and in the end mass murder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Racism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-racism. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"NSDAP Party Program (1920),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Weimar Germany (1918/19–1933), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/weimar-germany-1918-1933/nsdap-party-program-1920. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitism,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitism. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Volk_und_Rasse_Heft_7_Heuert_Juli_1935_illustrierte_Monatsschrift._People_and_Race_Nazi_Germany_Third_Reich_propaganda_magazine_cover_photo_Reichsarbeitsdienst_RAD_Labour_Service_members_%28Holocaust_exhibition_HL-senteret_Oslo_2021_B.jpg/1280px-Volk_und_Rasse_Heft_7_Heuert_Juli_1935_illustrierte_Monatsschrift._People_and_Race_Nazi_Germany_Third_Reich_propaganda_magazine_cover_photo_Reichsarbeitsdienst_RAD_Labour_Service_members_%28Holocaust_exhibition_HL-senteret_Oslo_2021_B.jpg","title":"Volk und Rasse, July 1935","desc":"The cover of the German race-propaganda monthly Volk und Rasse for July 1935, showing two labour service men in profile as the idealised racial type. Photographed in a Holocaust exhibition at the HL-senteret, Oslo.","credit":"Photograph by Wolfmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volk_und_Rasse_Heft_7_Heuert_Juli_1935_illustrierte_Monatsschrift._People_and_Race_Nazi_Germany_Third_Reich_propaganda_magazine_cover_photo_Reichsarbeitsdienst_RAD_Labour_Service_members_%28Holocaust_exhibition_HL-senteret_Oslo_2021_B.jpg","alt":"Magazine cover in Fraktur type showing two young men in uniform caps in profile, one wearing a swastika armband."},"why":[{"q":"Why does ranking races turn biology into politics?","a":"Because races with supposedly better traits were said to dominate the rest, which converts an invented biology into a claim about who ought to rule."},{"q":"Why was the idealised appearance not the test?","a":"Because many Germans the regime counted as Aryan looked nothing like the blond, blue-eyed type, and hair and eye colour were never the ground of persecution."},{"q":"Why did the racial and the political arguments work as one?","a":"Because the same movement condemned democracy as weakness and branded the left as enemies of the nation, so exclusion by race and by politics ran together."}]},
{"id":"ww2-052","num":"52","category":"World War II · History","question":"Nearly a fifth of German voters went to the Nazis in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the breakthrough that turned a party of 2.6 per cent into a national force.","questions":["Called early after a coalition broke over unemployment insurance, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to build a majority that would keep the left out of office for good.","The party that won its breakthrough in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had rebuilt itself since 1925 as a national organisation combining modern campaigning with street violence."],"answer":"Reichstag election of 1930","answerText":"Reichstag election of 1930","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Coalition falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Election</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1930</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nazi share</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly a fifth of the vote</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July 1932, 37.3 per cent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Reichstag election of 1930</b> was the Nazi electoral breakthrough, and the moment a fringe party became a force in German politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Until then the movement had been marginal, taking between 1.6 and 2.5 per cent in state elections and 2.6 per cent nationally in May 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The governing coalition had broken in March 1930 over half a percentage point on unemployment insurance contributions, which showed that the parties could not agree even in a slump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three middle-class parties then invoked the emergency provisions of the constitution to call an early election, hoping to build a majority that would keep the Social Democrats and the left out of government for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The manoeuvre failed, and in September 1930 the Nazis took nearly a fifth of the vote, drawing in new, unemployed and alienated voters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The party had been rebuilt after 1925 into a national organisation whose structure matched the electoral districts, and its campaigning combined modern technique with street violence for which the leadership could deny responsibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its youth and its distance from every government since 1918 were themselves advantages in a country tired of the parties in office, and it could promise what none of them had delivered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> From 1930 governments ruled by presidential decree rather than by parliamentary consent, which made each election less a choice of government than a test of strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The movement’s programme had not changed with its fortunes: contempt for parliament, a demand for authority, hostility to the left, and the promise of a nation restored to strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two years later, in the elections of July 1932, the party took 37.3 per cent of the vote and became the largest in the Reichstag, though still short of a majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","\"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette.html. [Open access]","\"Die Nationalsozialistische Deutsche Arbeiterpartei (NSDAP),\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/nsdap.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/15/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10313%2C_Reichstagswahl%2C_Propagandawagen_des_Zentrums.jpg","title":"Campaigning for the September 1930 election","desc":"A Centre Party propaganda lorry on a Berlin street in August 1930, at the opening of the campaign for the Reichstag election of 14 September.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-10313, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-10313,_Reichstagswahl,_Propagandawagen_des_Zentrums.jpg","alt":"A lorry carrying a large banner reading Wählt Zentrum, surrounded by cyclists with placards on a crowded street."},"why":[{"q":"Why was an early election called at all?","a":"Because three middle-class parties invoked the emergency provisions hoping to build a majority that would keep the Social Democrats and the left out of government for good."},{"q":"Why did the party’s inexperience help it?","a":"Because its youth and its distance from every government since 1918 were advantages in a country tired of the parties in office, and it could promise what none of them had delivered."},{"q":"Why did elections after 1930 decide less than they seemed to?","a":"Because governments ruled by presidential decree rather than by parliamentary consent, so each election was less a choice of government than a test of strength."}]},
{"id":"ww2-053","num":"53","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 20 July 1932 deposed the Prussian government by emergency decree and ended the last serious Social Democratic share in governing Germany.","questions":["Because Prussia was Germany’s democratic anchor, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> removed the strongest institutional obstacle standing in the way of a national takeover.","Carried out while cabinets already governed by decree rather than by parliament, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came six months before the chancellorship changed hands."],"answer":"Preußenschlag","answerText":"Preußenschlag","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Coup</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 July 1932</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Otto Braun’s Prussian government deposed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commissioner</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Franz von Papen, from 20 July</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Basis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Article 48 of the constitution</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Preußenschlag</b>, the Prussian coup of 20 July 1932, ended the last serious share the Social Democrats had in governing the Weimar Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By emergency decree the president deposed the Prussian government of Otto Braun, who had led the largest German state since 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Braun’s ministry had lost its parliamentary majority at the state election of 24 April 1932 and was still in office only to keep the administration running.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chancellor, Franz von Papen, was appointed Reich commissioner in Prussia, and a new interior minister, the mayor of Essen, ran what its enemies called the red fortress in his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Prussia was two-thirds of Germany and its democratic anchor, so taking its government by decree removed the strongest institutional obstacle in the way of a national takeover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Weimar state had already stopped governing itself in the ordinary way, since from 1930 its cabinets ruled by presidential decree rather than by the consent of a parliamentary majority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That habit began in March 1930, when the last broad coalition collapsed over half a percentage point in unemployment insurance contributions and the parties proved unable to compromise in a slump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each new election then made the parliament less workable rather than more, and by July 1932 the Nazis were the largest party in a chamber that could no longer produce a government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The movement that profited most had never hidden that it meant to be rid of the parliamentary system altogether, and said so in every campaign it fought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Six months after Prussia was taken by decree, the chancellorship itself was handed to the leader of that movement by the same president who had signed the order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["\"Preußenschlag 1932,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/preussenschlag.html. [Open access]","\"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette.html. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-13680%2C_Berlin%2C_Verordnung_%C3%BCber_Ausnahmezustand.jpg","title":"The state of emergency posted in Berlin","desc":"Berliners reading the Reich President's decree of July 1932 declaring a state of emergency over Berlin and the province of Brandenburg, posted on an advertising column.","credit":"Photograph by Georg Pahl. Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-13680, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-13680,_Berlin,_Verordnung_%C3%BCber_Ausnahmezustand.jpg","alt":"People standing at an advertising column reading a printed official notice pasted among commercial posters."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Prussia the state worth taking by decree?","a":"Because it was two-thirds of Germany and its democratic anchor, so its government was the strongest institutional obstacle to a national takeover."},{"q":"Why was the government there vulnerable?","a":"Because it had lost its parliamentary majority at the state election of April 1932 and remained in office only to keep the administration running."},{"q":"Why did each election make the parliament less workable?","a":"Because cabinets had ruled by presidential decree since 1930, and by July 1932 the largest party in the chamber was one that meant to be rid of the system altogether."}]},
{"id":"ww2-054","num":"54","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name given to the appointment of 30 January 1933 and the months in which the German state was turned into a one-party dictatorship.","questions":["Conservatives who arranged the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> believed they could contain the new chancellor in a coalition, and found within months that the arrangement ran the other way.","Because Germany had been governed by decree since 1930, the office handed over in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> already carried powers no chancellor of the 1920s had used."],"answer":"Machtergreifung","answerText":"Machtergreifung","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Appointed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 January 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">By</span><span class=\"dt-v\">President Paul von Hindenburg</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Transformed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in under six months</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by mid-July 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Machtergreifung</b>, the seizure of power, is the name given to Hitler’s appointment as chancellor on 30 January 1933 and the months in which a democracy became a dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was not voted into office: the president, Paul von Hindenburg, appointed him at the head of a coalition in which conservatives believed they could contain him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party behind him was the largest in the Reichstag after July 1932, but it had never won a majority and its vote had fallen back in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germany had been governed by presidential decree since 1930, so the office he was given already carried powers no chancellor of the 1920s had used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In less than six months Germany was transformed from a democratic state into a one-party dictatorship built on racist and authoritarian principles, which is the whole point of the term.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The steps were quick and legal in form: a decree suspending civil rights, an election fought under it, and a law transferring the power to legislate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Political opponents, above all communists and social democrats, were intimidated, arrested and in many cases killed, while the Nazi campaign went on unhindered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement had said for more than a decade that it meant to be rid of parliament and of the parties, so what it did in office surprised nobody who had listened to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The conservatives who thought they were using it found within months that the arrangement ran the other way, and their own parties were dissolved with the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the middle of July the Nazi party was the only one left in Germany, the others outlawed or dissolved under pressure, and the parliament had become a chamber for acclamation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","\"Präsidialkabinette,\" <i>LeMO – Lebendiges Museum Online</i> (Berlin: Deutsches Historisches Museum), https://www.dhm.de/lemo/kapitel/weimarer-republik/innenpolitik/praesidialkabinette.html. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-15347%2C_Berlin%2C_Ernennung_Adolf_Hitlers_zum_Reichskanzler.jpg","title":"Berlin, 30 January 1933","desc":"A crowd in the street outside the Reich chancellery on the day Hitler was appointed chancellor, seen from an upper window.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-15347, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-15347,_Berlin,_Ernennung_Adolf_Hitlers_zum_Reichskanzler.jpg","alt":"A dense crowd in hats fills a street around a parked open car, photographed from above."},"why":[{"q":"Why does the office he received matter as much as the appointment?","a":"Because Germany had been governed by presidential decree since 1930, so the chancellorship already carried powers no chancellor of the 1920s had used."},{"q":"Why were the conservatives wrong about containing him?","a":"Because within months the arrangement ran the other way, and their own parties were dissolved with the rest."},{"q":"Why did what followed surprise nobody who had been listening?","a":"Because the movement had said for more than a decade that it meant to be rid of parliament and of the parties."}]},
{"id":"ww2-055","num":"55","category":"World War II · History","question":"The night the parliament building burned in February 1933 produced the emergency decree that followed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, suspending the civil rights the Weimar constitution guaranteed.","questions":["Thousands of communists and social democrats were arrested under the decree issued the day after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while the governing party campaigned unhindered.","Even with the emergency powers taken after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Nazis won only 43.9 per cent on 5 March and still needed a coalition partner."],"answer":"Reichstag fire","answerText":"Reichstag fire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 February 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Decree</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 February 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suspended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">seven articles of the constitution</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Election</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 March 1933, 43.9 per cent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Reichstag fire</b> of 27 February 1933 burned the German parliament building four weeks after Hitler became chancellor, and gave him the occasion to be rid of his opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next day the president was persuaded to issue a decree for the protection of the people and state, made under the emergency article of the constitution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It suspended the articles of the constitution guaranteeing personal liberty, free expression of opinion including the freedom of the press, the rights of assembly and association, and the privacy of post, telegraph and telephone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It permitted house searches, confiscations and restrictions on property beyond the ordinary legal limits, and let the Reich take over the powers of any state that failed to restore order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thousands of communists and social democrats were arrested in the days that followed, their meetings banned and their newspapers closed, while the National Socialist election campaign went on unhindered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The decree was never lifted, so the rights it suspended stayed suspended for twelve years and Germany became a police state without further legislation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even so the party did not win the election of 5 March: with 43.9 per cent it still needed the German National People’s Party as a coalition partner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the decree had done was to make the campaign one-sided, since the opposition could not meet, print or speak while its own leaders were in custody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is the clearest case of the pattern the seizure of power followed, which was to use the state’s own emergency machinery to destroy German democracy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The movement had promised for years to be done with parliamentary government altogether, and the burnt chamber served it as an emblem of the danger it claimed to be saving the country from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Decree of the Reich President for the Protection of the People and State (Reichstag Fire Decree) (February 28, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/decree-of-the-reich-president-for-the-protection-of-the-people-and-state-reichstag-fire-decree-february-28-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Firemen_work_on_the_burning_Reichstag_Building_in_February%2C_1933%2C_after_fire_broke_out_simultaneously_at_20_places...._-_NARA_-_535790.tif/1280px-Firemen_work_on_the_burning_Reichstag_Building_in_February%2C_1933%2C_after_fire_broke_out_simultaneously_at_20_places...._-_NARA_-_535790.tif.jpg","title":"The Reichstag burning, February 1933","desc":"The German parliament building alight on the night of 27 February 1933, with the words Dem deutschen Volke on the portico and smoke pouring from the dome.","credit":"US National Archives and Records Administration, 535790. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firemen_work_on_the_burning_Reichstag_Building_in_February,_1933,_after_fire_broke_out_simultaneously_at_20_places...._-_NARA_-_535790.tif","alt":"A large classical building at night with flames and smoke rising from its dome and light showing through the windows."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the decree more consequential than the fire?","a":"Because it suspended personal liberty, free expression, assembly, association and the privacy of the post, and it was never lifted, so the rights stayed suspended for twelve years."},{"q":"Why did a one-sided campaign still not produce a majority?","a":"Because with 43.9 per cent the party still needed the German National People’s Party as a coalition partner."},{"q":"Why is this the clearest case of the pattern?","a":"Because it used the state’s own emergency machinery to destroy German democracy, rather than acting against the constitution from outside it."}]},
{"id":"ww2-056","num":"56","category":"World War II · History","question":"Passed on 23 March 1933, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved the power to make law from the German parliament to the cabinet, and allowed laws that broke the constitution.","questions":["The two-thirds majority the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> required was manufactured by excluding the communist deputies, arresting others, and manipulating the rules of the chamber.","Once the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was in force the Reichstag lost its reason for existing and met only to applaud decisions the government had already taken."],"answer":"Enabling Act","answerText":"Enabling Act","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Passed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">23 March 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Effect</span><span class=\"dt-v\">cabinet legislates, even against the constitution</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elected under</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the decree of 28 February 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by mid-July 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","politics","law","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Enabling Act</b> of 23 March 1933 transferred the power to make law from the German parliament to Hitler’s cabinet, and so ended parliamentary government in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Laws made under it could depart from the constitution itself, which meant that the government could legislate against the document it had sworn to uphold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The parliament that passed it had been elected three weeks earlier under the emergency decree that followed the Reichstag fire, with the opposition’s press and meetings banned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Nazis had taken 43.9 per cent at that election and governed in coalition, so the two-thirds majority the act required had to be manufactured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was manufactured by arresting and intimidating elected deputies, by excluding the communist members altogether, and by manipulating the rules of the chamber so that absent deputies counted as present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> With the act in force the Reichstag lost its reason for existing and became a body that met to applaud decisions already taken elsewhere, a rubber stamp for the chancellor’s dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The government used its new power at once, dissolving the state parliaments and appointing governors who answered to the Reich interior ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A law of 7 April then purged the civil service of Jews and of political opponents, requiring proof of ancestry from everyone in public employment, and the professions followed within months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the middle of July every other party had been outlawed or had dissolved itself, and Germany was a one-party state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The movement that did all this had told the electorate for years that it despised parliament and the parties in it, and the act was that promise kept in a single afternoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Decree of the Reich President for the Protection of the People and State (Reichstag Fire Decree) (February 28, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/decree-of-the-reich-president-for-the-protection-of-the-people-and-state-reichstag-fire-decree-february-28-1933. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Enabling_act_of_1933_printed_in_the_Reich_Law_Gazette.jpg","title":"The Enabling Act in the Reich Law Gazette","desc":"The Law to Remedy the Distress of the People and the Reich as printed in the Reichsgesetzblatt of 24 March 1933, with its five articles and the signatures of Hindenburg, Hitler and three ministers.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enabling_act_of_1933_printed_in_the_Reich_Law_Gazette.jpg","alt":"A printed page of German law in Fraktur type headed Reichsgesetzblatt, dated 24 March 1933."},"why":[{"q":"Why was this act more than a delegation of power?","a":"Because laws made under it could depart from the constitution itself, so the government could legislate against the document it had sworn to uphold."},{"q":"Why did the required majority have to be manufactured?","a":"Because the party had taken 43.9 per cent and governed in coalition, so deputies were arrested and intimidated, the communists excluded, and absentees counted as present."},{"q":"Why did the parliament lose its reason for existing?","a":"Because with the power to legislate elsewhere it became a body that met to applaud decisions already taken."}]},
{"id":"ww2-057","num":"57","category":"World War II · History","question":"The regime called the remaking of every German institution in its own image <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a word meaning coordination, and carried it through one field at a time.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the state governments were dissolved, the civil service purged of Jews and opponents, and the trade unions abolished within a few months of 1933.","Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the schools as well, a law of 1934 left the party’s own youth organisation as the only lawful one in Germany."],"answer":"Gleichschaltung","answerText":"Gleichschaltung","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Instrument</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Enabling Act, March 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Civil service</span><span class=\"dt-v\">purged 7 April 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">abolished May 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">One party</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by mid-July 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","politics","history","germany","ideology","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Gleichschaltung</b>, a German word meaning coordination or synchronisation, is the name the regime gave to the process of remaking every German institution to serve its own ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political, social and cultural life were rearranged one field at a time, with the object of turning the country into a single-party state under Hitler and his party.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Enabling Act of March 1933 supplied the instrument, since the cabinet could now legislate without consulting the parliament and against the constitution if it chose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two further laws dissolved the existing state governments and put a Reich governor over each, reporting to the interior minister Wilhelm Frick rather than to any local assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The civil service was purged on 7 April 1933 by a law excluding Jews and political opponents, which reached judges, teachers, university professors and lawyers as well as every rank of administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Trade unions were abolished in May 1933 and their members enrolled with their employers in a single labour organisation controlled by the party, while farmers were gathered into a Reich Food Estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September the propaganda minister created a Reich Culture Chamber over literature, music, theatre, radio, film, the fine arts and the press, so that only its members could go on working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sports teams, music groups and craft associations that had belonged to parties or unions were disbanded, and a law of 1934 left the Hitler Youth as the only lawful youth organisation in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The attempt was made, with less success, on the Christian denominations and their own youth groups, but by mid-July 1933 a law had declared the party the only one permitted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of the work came from below rather than above, as Germans coordinated their own associations unbidden, and the movement that had promised a nation without parties or private loyalties had one within six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service (April 7, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/law-for-the-restoration-of-the-professional-civil-service-april-7-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Der_Weg_des_%22gleichgeschalteten%22_Staatsb%C3%BCrgers.jpg/1280px-Der_Weg_des_%22gleichgeschalteten%22_Staatsb%C3%BCrgers.jpg","title":"The path of the coordinated citizen","desc":"A propaganda chart setting out the life of a German under the regime, from the Jungvolk and the League of German Girls through labour service and military service into the German Labour Front.","credit":"Photograph by Maddriver371, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Der_Weg_des_%22gleichgeschalteten%22_Staatsb%C3%BCrgers.jpg","alt":"A diagram with two horizontal arrows of marching figures, one for women and one for men, labelled by age and by organisation."},"why":[{"q":"Why could the process move field by field so quickly?","a":"Because the Enabling Act let the cabinet legislate without the parliament and against the constitution, so each institution could be remade by law."},{"q":"Why did a culture chamber control who could work?","a":"Because only its members could go on working in literature, music, theatre, radio, film, the fine arts and the press, so membership became a licence to practise."},{"q":"Why is it wrong to picture the process as purely imposed?","a":"Because much of the work came from below, as Germans coordinated their own associations unbidden."}]},
{"id":"ww2-058","num":"58","category":"World War II · History","question":"After the free unions were dissolved in May 1933 their members were enrolled with their employers in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a single body attached to the ruling party.","questions":["Wages and contracts were not settled by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, but by trustees for labour whom the Reich labour ministry appointed.","Propaganda presented the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the end of class conflict, while workers lost every institution through which they had pressed their own interests."],"answer":"German Labour Front","answerText":"German Labour Front","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Unions dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 May 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Parties banned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May to July 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wages set by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">trustees for labour</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Led by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Robert Ley, from 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","politics","history","germany","economics","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>German Labour Front</b> was the single organisation into which German workers and their employers were herded after the free trade unions were destroyed in May 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The slump and years of mass unemployment had already worn down the labour movement’s cohesion and morale and left the unions badly destabilised before the new government touched them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Within a few months of that government taking office the workers had lost every institution that had traditionally represented them, their unions dissolved and the Social Democratic and Communist parties banned between May and July 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new body joined employers and employees in one association attached to the party, under the party leader Robert Ley, and essentially every worker in every sector of the economy belonged to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It did not in fact settle wages, tariffs or contracts, which were decided by trustees for labour appointed by the Reich labour ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Where the old unions had put workers’ rights first, the new one preached national economic goals above personal well-being, and farmers were gathered separately into a Reich Food Estate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Propaganda presented the arrangement as the end of class difference and conflict and the foundation of a national community marked by harmony and solidarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What workers had actually lost were the organs that had carried their interests, along with the right to form organisations of their own and the right to strike, so that control over working conditions passed to employers and trustees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Front also ran the leisure programme Strength through Joy, which offered subsidised holidays, theatre and concert tickets, music and art classes and sport, and so reached into workers’ free time as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same coordination was applied to professional and cultural bodies, and by the middle of July 1933 the party was the only one permitted, so that a movement which had denounced the left absorbed the labour movement rather than argued with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Appeal of the German Workers’ Front after the Dissolution of the Free Trade Unions (May 2, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/appeal-of-the-german-workers-front-after-the-dissolution-of-the-free-trade-unions-then-as-now-we-remain-comrades-may-2-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/FHM-282370_Deutsche_Arbeitsfront_member_paramilitary_uniform_DAF_swastika_flag_Nazi_Germany_propaganda_photo_%28Frihedsmuseet_Nationalmuseet_Denmark%29_No_known_rights.jpg/1280px-FHM-282370_Deutsche_Arbeitsfront_member_paramilitary_uniform_DAF_swastika_flag_Nazi_Germany_propaganda_photo_%28Frihedsmuseet_Nationalmuseet_Denmark%29_No_known_rights.jpg","title":"A German Labour Front man and the Front's flag","desc":"A propaganda photograph of a member of the German Labour Front in uniform beside the organisation's flag, which set a swastika inside a cogwheel.","credit":"Frihedsmuseet, Nationalmuseet, Denmark, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FHM-282370_Deutsche_Arbeitsfront_member_paramilitary_uniform_DAF_swastika_flag_Nazi_Germany_propaganda_photo_%28Frihedsmuseet_Nationalmuseet_Denmark%29_No_known_rights.jpg","alt":"A uniformed man standing on a roof edge beside a flagpole flying a flag with a swastika inside a cogwheel."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the unions already weak when they were destroyed?","a":"Because the slump and years of mass unemployment had worn down the labour movement’s cohesion and morale and left them badly destabilised beforehand."},{"q":"Why was the new body not a union in any sense?","a":"Because it did not settle wages, tariffs or contracts, which were decided by trustees for labour appointed by the Reich labour ministry."},{"q":"Why did it run holidays and concerts as well?","a":"Because the leisure programme reached into workers’ free time, so the organisation covered what the old unions had not as well as replacing what they had."}]},
{"id":"ww2-059","num":"59","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the purge of the party militia’s leadership in June 1934, carried out by shooting and declared lawful by a decree issued afterwards.","questions":["Rumours of a planned coup were spread before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> so that the killings could be presented to Germans as the defence of the state against a second revolution.","With the militia leadership dead after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the elite guard rose in its place and took control of the German police."],"answer":"Night of the Long Knives","answerText":"Night of the Long Knives","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Purge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June – 2 July 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declared lawful</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 July 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ernst Röhm and others</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Aftermath</span><span class=\"dt-v\">SS control of the police</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Night of the Long Knives</b> was the purge of the party’s own paramilitary leadership at the end of June 1934, carried out by shooting rather than by any legal process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rumours of a coup planned by the militia had been spread beforehand on Hitler’s instructions, so that the operation, codenamed Hummingbird, could be presented afterwards as a defence of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The day after the purge the propaganda minister announced that Ernst Röhm and the former chancellor Kurt von Schleicher had been plotting a second revolution, which the party had thwarted and so saved the country from chaos.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party press dwelt on the private life of the militia chief and his circle, and told readers that they no longer presented a moral danger to the German people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On 3 July a law on measures of state self-defence declared the murders lawful after the event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The party newspaper of that day carried pledges of loyalty from across the country, a message from the president thanking the leader for saving Germany from grave danger, and a report of the impression the cleansing action had made abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The militia had been the movement’s street army since 1921 and had grown far larger than the regular forces, whose officers wanted it cut down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With its leadership dead the elite guard rose in its place and took control of the police, which turned the state into a police state in earnest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The killings settled that authority ran downward from one man and was not to be questioned, which was the principle the regime now called its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hindenburg died the following month, and the offices of president and chancellor were merged in the man the purge had confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"The Völkischer Beobachter Justifies the Purge in Response to the ‘Röhm Putsch’ (July 3, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/the-front-page-of-the-voelkischer-beobachter-justifies-the-purge-in-response-to-the-so-called-roehm-putsch-july-3-1934. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14886%2C_Kurt_Daluege%2C_Heinrich_Himmler%2C_Ernst_R%C3%B6hm.jpg","title":"Daluege, Himmler and Röhm, 1933","desc":"The police chief Kurt Daluege, the head of the elite guard Heinrich Himmler and the militia chief Ernst Röhm together in August 1933, less than a year before the purge in which Röhm was shot.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-14886, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14886,_Kurt_Daluege,_Heinrich_Himmler,_Ernst_R%C3%B6hm.jpg","alt":"Three uniformed men standing close together outdoors, the nearest wearing an Iron Cross."},"why":[{"q":"Why were rumours of a coup spread in advance?","a":"Because it let the operation be presented afterwards as a defence of the state rather than as a settling of accounts within the party."},{"q":"Why did a law have to be passed after the killings?","a":"Because they had been carried out by shooting rather than by any legal process, so a law of 3 July declared them lawful after the event."},{"q":"Why did the purge change the shape of the state?","a":"Because with the militia’s leadership dead the elite guard rose in its place and took control of the police, which made the state a police state in earnest."}]},
{"id":"ww2-060","num":"60","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that authority flowed downward from one man through government, party, economy and family, and that it was to be obeyed without question.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the armed forces swore their oath of loyalty to Hitler personally, after the two highest offices were merged on the president’s death in August 1934.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been practised inside the movement for years before it became the working rule of the German state after 1933."],"answer":"Führerprinzip","answerText":"Führerprinzip","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enabling Act</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hindenburg dies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 August 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Offices merged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">leader and Reich chancellor</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Army oath</span><span class=\"dt-v\">sworn to Hitler personally</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","politics","ideology","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Führerprinzip</b>, the leader principle, held that authority flowed downward from Hitler through government, party, economy and family, and was to be obeyed without question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a metaphor but the working rule of the state, since the leader’s will became the foundation of all legislation once the parliament had given up its power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The parliament had done so in March 1933, when it transferred the making of laws to the cabinet, and it thereafter met only to applaud what had been decided elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Hindenburg died in August 1934 the offices of president and chancellor were merged and Hitler took the title of leader and Reich chancellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The armed forces then swore their oath of loyalty to him personally in that capacity, rather than to the constitution or the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> As chancellor his power was still limited by the laws of the German state; as leader it was held to be unlimited and identical with the destiny of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Party officials, Hitler Youth members and labour service leaders had already sworn oaths of their own in February 1934, read out in Munich and broadcast across the country so that the apparatus was sworn to one man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The principle had been practised in the movement long before it was practised in the state, since the party had demanded submission to a charismatic leader from its early years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It had also been advertised: the party told voters for a decade that it wanted authority rather than debate, and once in office it kept that promise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the principle produced was a state in which no office had a standing of its own, and every institution answered upward to a single will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nazi Rise to Power,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nazi-rise-to-power. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Adolf Hitler,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/adolf-hitler. [Open access]","Gustavo Corni, \"Fascism and the Radical Right,\" in <i>1914-1918-online. International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2015), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10715. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-16108A%2C_Vereidigung_von_Reichswehr-Soldaten_auf_Hitler.jpg","title":"The army's oath, August 1934","desc":"Soldiers of the Reichswehr swearing the oath of loyalty to Hitler personally in August 1934, after the offices of president and chancellor were combined.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-16108A, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-16108A,_Vereidigung_von_Reichswehr-Soldaten_auf_Hitler.jpg","alt":"A rank of helmeted soldiers in greatcoats standing with their right hands raised."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the principle a working rule rather than a slogan?","a":"Because the leader’s will became the foundation of all legislation once the parliament had transferred the making of laws to the cabinet."},{"q":"Why did the two titles differ in what they permitted?","a":"Because as chancellor his power was still limited by the laws of the German state, while as leader it was held to be unlimited and identical with the destiny of the nation."},{"q":"Why did the oaths matter as much as the offices?","a":"Because the armed forces swore loyalty to him personally rather than to the constitution or the state, as party and youth officials had already done."}]},
{"id":"ww2-061","num":"61","category":"World War II · History","question":"Hitler’s small bodyguard of 1925 grew into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which by 1936 controlled every police force in Germany and ran the concentration camps as well.","questions":["Candidates for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were admitted only after tests of ancestry and political reliability, and its members needed permission to marry.","For shooting the party militia’s leadership in June 1934 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made an independent organisation answerable to Hitler alone."],"answer":"Schutzstaffel","answerText":"Schutzstaffel","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1925, as a bodyguard</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Himmler</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Reich leader from January 1929</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after the purge of June 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">All police</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","politics","history","germany","warfare","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Schutzstaffel</b>, the protection squads, began in 1925 as a small bodyguard for Hitler and ended as the organisation that ran the police and the camps of the German state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was at first subordinate to the party militia, and its duties were guarding leaders and meetings and selling subscriptions to the party newspaper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In January 1929, when it numbered 280 men, Heinrich Himmler was made its Reich leader, and he rebuilt it by admitting candidates only on tests of ancestry and political reliability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An intelligence branch followed in 1931, which from August 1934 was the only body allowed to gather political intelligence in Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An office set up under a marriage decree came the same year to vet the ancestry of members’ brides, and the expertise it built was later used to decide who counted as German in occupied Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It had more than 52,000 members when the party took power, and for shooting the militia’s leadership in June 1934 it was made an independent organisation answerable to Hitler alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chief had already been given the Prussian political police that April, which let him gather the whole political police apparatus of Germany into one hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1936 he was chief of every police force in the country, and he told the Academy of German Law that law was a matter of higher principles rather than of codes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organisation ran the concentration camps, whose guards it trained and whose regime it set, and it grew into something like a state inside the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The political police it absorbed was joined to its own intelligence service in September 1939 in a single Reich Security Main Office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]","\"Hermann Göring, Chief of the State Secret Police Office, Names Heinrich Himmler Deputy Chief and Inspector of the Prussian Political Police (April 20, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hermann-goering-chief-of-the-state-secret-police-office-names-heinrich-himmler-deputy-chief-and-inspector-of-the-prussian-political-police-april-20-1934. [Open access]","\"Himmler Speech at the Academy of German Law (October 11, 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/himmler-speech-at-the-academy-of-german-law-october-11-1936. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gestapo,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gestapo. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17312%2C_Berlin-Lichterfelde%2C_Hitler_bei_Leibstandarte.jpg","title":"Hitler reviews his guard regiment, December 1935","desc":"Hitler inspecting the SS bodyguard regiment at the former cadet school in Berlin-Lichterfelde, ten years after the organisation began as a small escort.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-17312, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17312,_Berlin-Lichterfelde,_Hitler_bei_Leibstandarte.jpg","alt":"Steel-helmeted troops with shouldered rifles marching past a saluting figure on snow-covered ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did a bodyguard of 280 men end up running Germany's police?","a":"Because Himmler took over in January 1929 and rebuilt it on tests of ancestry and political reliability, then was given the Prussian political police in April 1934, which let him gather the whole political police apparatus of Germany into one hand."},{"q":"Why was it made independent of the party militia in June 1934?","a":"It was rewarded for shooting the militia's own leadership. Having killed its parent organisation for Hitler, it was detached from it and made answerable to Hitler alone."},{"q":"Why did an office set up to vet brides matter beyond marriage?","a":"Vetting members' brides under a marriage decree built expertise in judging ancestry, and that expertise was later used to decide who counted as German in occupied Europe."}]},
{"id":"ww2-062","num":"62","category":"World War II · History","question":"Germany’s political police under the Nazis was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which could send a person to a concentration camp with no lawyer, no appeal and no trial.","questions":["Too small to watch a whole nation, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> depended on denunciations offered by neighbours and colleagues out of belief, spite or self-interest.","The emergency decree of February 1933 freed the police who became the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to open mail, tap telephones and search houses without any warrant."],"answer":"Gestapo","answerText":"Gestapo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Centralised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the end of 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Security Police</span><span class=\"dt-v\">summer 1936, under Heydrich</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Into the RSHA</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Power</span><span class=\"dt-v\">protective custody, without trial</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["institution","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Gestapo</b>, short for the secret state police, was the political police of Nazi Germany, and the instrument through which the regime hunted the people it counted as enemies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political policing was not new: the Weimar Republic had political police in each of its states, but they answered to state and local governments and were bound by a constitution guaranteeing privacy and free speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The emergency decree that followed the Reichstag fire suspended those protections, so that the police could open mail, listen to telephone calls and search houses without a warrant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Centralising the force took several years, and it was only by the end of 1936 that a single political police under Himmler covered the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That summer it was joined with the criminal police in a security police under Reinhard Heydrich, who also ran the party’s own intelligence service.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its most feared power was protective custody, which allowed it to send a person straight to a concentration camp with no lawyer, no appeal and no trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A law of December 1934 made criticism of the party or the regime a crime, so that telling a joke about Hitler could bring an arrest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its agents were mostly trained detectives who had served before 1933, joined by ideologues brought in from the intelligence service with no police training at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was too small to watch a nation and did not try, relying instead on denunciations from neighbours, colleagues and relatives, offered out of belief, spite or self-interest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In September 1939 it became one office of a new Reich Security Main Office under the elite guard, and the state it had helped build no longer troubled to call its power lawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gestapo,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gestapo. [Open access]","\"Hermann Göring, Chief of the State Secret Police Office, Names Heinrich Himmler Deputy Chief and Inspector of the Prussian Political Police (April 20, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hermann-goering-chief-of-the-state-secret-police-office-names-heinrich-himmler-deputy-chief-and-inspector-of-the-prussian-political-police-april-20-1934. [Open access]","\"Himmler Speech at the Academy of German Law (October 11, 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/himmler-speech-at-the-academy-of-german-law-october-11-1936. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183_R97512%2C_Berlin%2C_Geheimes_Staatspolizeihauptamt.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_183_R97512%2C_Berlin%2C_Geheimes_Staatspolizeihauptamt.jpg","title":"The Secret State Police headquarters, Berlin","desc":"The building at 8 Prinz-Albrecht-Strasse that housed the Gestapo's main office, photographed before the war.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183 R97512, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183_R97512,_Berlin,_Geheimes_Staatspolizeihauptamt.jpg","alt":"A large stone office building on a corner behind railings, with a car parked on the empty street."},"why":[{"q":"Why was political policing itself not the novelty here?","a":"The Weimar Republic already had political police in each of its states. What changed was that they answered to state and local governments and were bound by a constitution guaranteeing privacy and free speech, and those protections were suspended."},{"q":"Why did the emergency decree after the Reichstag fire matter so much?","a":"It suspended the constitutional protections, so the police could open mail, listen to telephone calls and search houses without a warrant. The machinery already existed; the decree removed what restrained it."},{"q":"Why did a force too small to watch a nation still terrify one?","a":"It did not try to watch everybody, relying instead on denunciations from neighbours, colleagues and relatives offered out of belief, spite or self-interest. Anyone might inform, so the surveillance was supplied by the population."}]},
{"id":"ww2-063","num":"63","category":"World War II · History","question":"The first Nazi <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened in 1933 to hold political opponents, and Dachau near Munich became the model on which the whole later system was built.","questions":["Anyone could be sent to a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the political police under protective custody, with no charge, no trial and no appeal against the order.","Punishment by humiliation, violence and forced labour, not reform, was the purpose of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the beginning, and deterrence of everyone outside."],"answer":"concentration camp","answerText":"concentration camp","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First camps</span><span class=\"dt-v\">February and March 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dachau opens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 March 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capacity</span><span class=\"dt-v\">five thousand at first</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regulations</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1933, by Theodor Eicke</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","event","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The first Nazi <b>concentration camp</b> system was improvised in the spring of 1933 to hold the thousands of people arrested as political opponents after the party took power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The militia and the police set the earliest camps up locally, all over Germany, wherever a disused factory, castle or barracks could be turned to the purpose, and there were many of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dachau, on the grounds of an abandoned munitions works near Munich, opened in March 1933 and took its first prisoners on the twenty-second of that month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Himmler, then police president of Munich, described it publicly as the first camp for political prisoners, and it was built for five thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its inmates at first were Communists, Social Democrats, trade unionists and other opponents of the new government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The purpose was never reform but punishment by daily humiliation, arbitrary violence and forced labour, and beyond that the deterrence of everybody outside the wire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In October 1933 Dachau’s commandant Theodor Eicke issued regulations prescribing brutal punishments for the smallest offence, and when he was made inspector of the whole system he imposed them everywhere and made his camp the training school for its guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of the early local camps were closed and replaced by a smaller number run centrally by the elite guard, whose death’s head units supplied the guards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The political police could send anyone into this system by protective custody, without charge or trial, which is what made the camps a standing threat rather than a punishment for crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dachau was the only camp opened in 1933 that lasted to 1945, and it became the model on which the rest were built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dachau,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dachau. [Open access]","\"Employment of Prisoners at the Oranienburg Protective Custody Camp near Berlin (April 1, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/employment-of-prisoners-at-the-oranienburg-protective-custody-camp-near-berlin-april-1-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gestapo,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gestapo. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Bundesarchiv_Bild_152-01-14%2C_Dachau%2C_Konzentrationslager_1933.jpg","title":"Prisoners at Dachau, May 1933","desc":"Inmates eating in the mess hall of the Dachau protective-custody camp two months after it opened, in a photograph taken for the regime.","credit":"Photograph by Friedrich Franz Bauer. Bundesarchiv, Bild 152-01-14, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_152-01-14,_Dachau,_Konzentrationslager_1933.jpg","alt":"Rows of men in light uniforms sitting at long trestle tables eating from bowls in a barrack hall."},"why":[{"q":"Why were the earliest camps improvised and scattered across Germany?","a":"The militia and the police set them up locally in the spring of 1933 to hold the thousands arrested after the party took power, using whatever disused factory, castle or barracks could be turned to the purpose."},{"q":"Why did Dachau become the model for the whole system?","a":"Its commandant Theodor Eicke issued regulations in October 1933 prescribing brutal punishments for the smallest offence, and when he was made inspector of the whole system he imposed them everywhere and made his camp the training school for its guards."},{"q":"Why were the camps a standing threat rather than a punishment for crimes?","a":"The political police could send anyone into them by protective custody, without charge or trial. The purpose was never reform but punishment and, beyond that, the deterrence of everybody outside the wire."}]},
{"id":"ww2-064","num":"64","category":"World War II · History","question":"Carried through film, radio, schoolbooks and the press by a ministry set up in 1933, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> prepared Germans for each measure before it was taken.","questions":["The cheap receiver mass-produced under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was meant to bring the poorest German households within reach of the regime’s broadcasts.","Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> presented each new restriction as the government restoring order, much of it aimed at public passivity rather than at enthusiasm."],"answer":"Nazi propaganda","answerText":"Nazi propaganda","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ministry founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Minister</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Joseph Goebbels, 1933 to 1945</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief instrument</span><span class=\"dt-v\">radio, and a cheap receiver</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Timed before</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the race laws of 1935</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","practice","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Nazi propaganda</b> was the regime’s effort to carry its message through every channel a modern state could reach, and it worked on Germans first in a democracy and then in a dictatorship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Reich Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda was set up in March 1933 under Joseph Goebbels, with art, music, theatre, film, books, radio, schoolbooks and the press in its charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its minister told the German press that his object was the spiritual mobilisation of the population, and that achieving it would amount to a revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He treated radio as the most important instrument he had, dissolved the independent broadcasters and had a cheap receiver mass-produced so that the poorest households could be reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The images it used were not invented: the stereotypes of Jews it printed were already familiar to the audience it was aimed at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Campaigns were timed to prepare the ground for measures rather than to explain them afterwards, running hard in 1935 before the race laws of September.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same was done in 1938 before the wave of economic laws that followed the November pogrom, so that each new restriction met a public already prepared for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Much of it aimed at passivity rather than enthusiasm, presenting each measure against Jews as the government stepping in to restore order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Abroad it argued that German demands for territory were reasonable, and at home it dwelt on the treatment of German minorities in the states that had gained land in 1919.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the invasion of the Soviet Union it fused communism and Jewry into one enemy, and that theme may have helped Germans and their auxiliaries fight to the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Propaganda,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-propaganda. [Open access]","\"Joseph Goebbels, Two Speeches on the Tasks of the Reich Ministry for Popular Enlightenment and Propaganda (March 15, March 25, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/joseph-goebbels-two-speeches-on-the-tasks-of-the-reich-ministry-for-popular-enlightenment-and-propaganda-march-15-march-25-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nuremberg Race Laws,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nuremberg-race-laws. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Volksempf%C3%A4nger_VE_301_Dyn%2C_NG-2009-110.jpg/1280px-Volksempf%C3%A4nger_VE_301_Dyn%2C_NG-2009-110.jpg","title":"The People's Receiver","desc":"The cheap radio set mass-produced from 1933 so that the poorest German households could be reached by the ministry's broadcasts.","credit":"Rijksmuseum, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volksempf%C3%A4nger_VE_301_Dyn,_NG-2009-110.jpg","alt":"A small bakelite radio with a fabric-covered speaker, a tuning dial and four knobs."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Goebbels treat radio as his most important instrument?","a":"It reached into the home directly, so he dissolved the independent broadcasters and had a cheap receiver mass-produced in order that even the poorest households could be reached."},{"q":"Why were campaigns run before new measures rather than after them?","a":"They were meant to prepare the ground rather than explain a decision afterwards, running hard in 1935 before the race laws of September and again in 1938 before the economic laws that followed the November pogrom, so each restriction met a prepared public."},{"q":"Why aim at passivity rather than at enthusiasm?","a":"Presenting each measure against Jews as the government stepping in to restore order asked for acquiescence rather than participation, which is a far lower bar and enough for the measures to proceed unopposed."}]},
{"id":"ww2-065","num":"65","category":"World War II · History","question":"Party chief in Berlin from 1926 and Reich propaganda minister from 1933, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought the German press, film, radio and theatre into line with the regime.","questions":["The nationwide anti-Jewish riot of November 1938 was coordinated by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with Hitler’s approval and sold to the world as spontaneous popular anger.","Rejected for war service and holding a doctorate in German literature, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rose through the party as the organiser of its Berlin branch."],"answer":"Joseph Goebbels","answerText":"Joseph Goebbels","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 October 1897, Rheydt</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Berlin party chief</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1926</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Propaganda chief</span><span class=\"dt-v\">party 1930, Reich 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 May 1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["person","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Joseph Goebbels</b> ran the propaganda of the Nazi movement and then of the German state, and was among the most radical antisemites in its leadership.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Born in the Rhineland in 1897 to a strict Catholic family, he was rejected for war service because a club foot hindered his walking, and took a doctorate in German literature at Heidelberg in 1920.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He joined the party in 1924, edited one of its papers, and in 1926 was made its chief in Greater Berlin, where he built its organisation from almost nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He ran his own Berlin newspaper from 1927, under the motto that it spoke for the oppressed against the exploiters, entered the Reichstag in 1928, and became the party’s head of propaganda in 1930.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Berlin he marched the militia into working-class districts held by the left, and made heroes of those killed in the street fighting that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A song named after one such man became the party’s anthem, the movement claimed seventy fallen in the first eight months of 1932 alone, and its Berlin militia grew from 450 men in 1926 to some 32,000 by January 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Made Reich minister for propaganda in 1933, he was charged with bringing the press, art, film, music and literature into line with the regime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He dissolved the independent broadcasters, put a cheap receiver into ordinary homes, and told editors that his aim was the spiritual mobilisation of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In November 1938 he coordinated the nationwide anti-Jewish riot with Hitler’s approval, while presenting it to the world as spontaneous popular anger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He kept both his party and his ministerial offices until 1945, an unconditional follower of a leader whose authority he had spent twenty years advertising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Propaganda,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-propaganda. [Open access]","\"Joseph Goebbels, Two Speeches on the Tasks of the Reich Ministry for Popular Enlightenment and Propaganda (March 15, March 25, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/joseph-goebbels-two-speeches-on-the-tasks-of-the-reich-ministry-for-popular-enlightenment-and-propaganda-march-15-march-25-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The SS,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/ss. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17049%2C_Joseph_Goebbels_spricht.jpg","title":"Goebbels speaking, August 1934","desc":"The Reich propaganda minister in mid-speech, photographed in Berlin in August 1934.","credit":"Photograph by Georg Pahl. Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-17049, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-17049,_Joseph_Goebbels_spricht.jpg","alt":"A man in uniform and peaked cap shouting with his right index finger raised."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Goebbels make heroes of men killed in street fighting?","a":"Marching the militia into working-class districts held by the left produced casualties, and the movement claimed seventy fallen in the first eight months of 1932 alone. A song named after one of them became the party's anthem, so the dead served as recruitment."},{"q":"Why was a doctorate in German literature useful to a propaganda minister?","a":"His ministry was charged with bringing the press, art, film, music and literature into line with the regime, so the culture he was remaking was the one he had been trained to study."},{"q":"Why present the November 1938 riot as spontaneous popular anger?","a":"He had coordinated it nationwide with Hitler's approval, and calling it spontaneous let the regime disclaim responsibility while presenting the violence as what Germans already felt rather than what the party had ordered."}]},
{"id":"ww2-066","num":"66","category":"World War II · History","question":"German students threw tens of thousands of volumes into bonfires in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of May 1933, in more than twenty university towns across the country.","questions":["The students of 1933 fed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with works by Jewish authors, pacifist writing that criticised war, and books praising socialism or communism.","Officials of the party attended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without having organised it, which made plain to everyone that the students acted with the regime’s approval."],"answer":"book burning","answerText":"book burning","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Main burnings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Berlin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 May 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Towns involved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than twenty</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Organised by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the German student union</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","practice","history","germany","art","20th century"],"abstract":"The Nazi <b>book burning</b> of May 1933 destroyed tens of thousands of volumes in bonfires in more than twenty university towns and cities across Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not organised by the government: the students of the national union of student bodies arranged it under the name of an action against the un-German spirit, and the party’s own student group already controlled that union and its chapters at universities across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Local and national officials attended all the same, and their presence made plain that the students had the regime’s approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What was burnt was whatever the students called un-German: works by Jewish authors, pacifist writing that criticised war, and books praising socialism or communism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the burning in Berlin on 10 May a banner read that German students were marching against the un-German spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Remarque’s novel of the Western Front was a favourite target, condemned for the pacifism of its account of the trenches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> German students had been among the movement’s earliest supporters, and secular middle-class student clubs had long been ultra-nationalist and antisemitic, many of them hostile to the Weimar Republic since the end of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The burnings were neither the first nor the last: a party branch in Kaiserslautern had burnt Remarque in March, the youth organisation staged its own at schools, and more followed into the autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They stood at the head of a wider effort to remake German culture, which within months put every writer, musician and artist under a state chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The propaganda ministry founded that spring took charge of that effort, and the man who ran it had himself taken a doctorate in German literature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Book Burning,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/book-burning. [Open access]","\"Book Burnings Across Germany (1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/book-burnings-across-germany-1933. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Propaganda,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-propaganda. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14597%2C_Berlin%2C_Opernplatz%2C_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung.jpg/1280px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14597%2C_Berlin%2C_Opernplatz%2C_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung.jpg","title":"The bonfire on the Opernplatz, May 1933","desc":"Students and onlookers saluting round the burning heap of books on the Opernplatz in Berlin during the burnings of May 1933.","credit":"Photograph by Georg Pahl. Bundesarchiv, Bild 102-14597, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-14597,_Berlin,_Opernplatz,_B%C3%BCcherverbrennung.jpg","alt":"A night crowd with arms raised in salute around a wide bonfire of scattered books and papers."},"why":[{"q":"Why did students rather than the government organise the burnings?","a":"The national union of student bodies arranged them under the name of an action against the un-German spirit, and the party's own student group already controlled that union and its chapters, so the party acted through the students."},{"q":"Why did officials attend an event they had not organised?","a":"Their presence made plain that the students had the regime's approval, which turned a student action into a public statement of government policy without the government having to order it."},{"q":"Why were German students among the movement's earliest supporters?","a":"Secular middle-class student clubs had long been ultra-nationalist and antisemitic, many of them hostile to the Weimar Republic since the end of the First World War, so the ground was prepared well before 1933."}]},
{"id":"ww2-067","num":"67","category":"World War II · History","question":"A clause excluding Jews from an office or profession was called an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it entered national law with the civil service act of April 1933.","questions":["Because private clubs and associations adopted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of their own accord, a man could be expelled from a choir or a sports club by a rule nobody had imposed.","The race laws of 1935 rebuilt the exclusions made under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on descent rather than religion, and gave them one legal definition to work from."],"answer":"Aryan Paragraph","answerText":"Aryan Paragraph","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Boycott</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 April 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Civil service law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 April 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restrictions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 400 by 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Redefined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the race laws of 1935</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","practice","law","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Aryan Paragraph</b> was the clause that excluded Jews from an office, a profession or an association, and it was the device by which Germany was closed to its Jews before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany’s Jews numbered about 525,000 in 1933, under one per cent of the population, and most of them were middle-class townspeople who considered themselves German.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first coordinated action against them was a boycott on 1 April 1933, when Germans were told not to shop in Jewish businesses or visit Jewish doctors and lawyers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Six days later a law on the civil service excluded Jews and the politically unreliable from public employment, and it carried the first such clause in national law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached every rank of administration and judges, teachers, university professors and lawyers with it, and the formula was then copied outwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That April a quota capped Jewish pupils and students, decrees cut Jewish work in medicine and law, and by 1934 Jewish actors could not appear on stage or screen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than four hundred restrictions followed in six years, some national and many issued by state, regional and town officials acting on their own initiative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Private associations adopted the clause voluntarily, so that a man could be expelled from a choir or a sports club by a rule nobody had ordered him to be expelled under.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The race laws of 1935 rebuilt the whole scheme on descent rather than religion, and gave the exclusions a single legal definition to work from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By 1938 Jews were being registered, renamed and stamped, and after the November pogrom they were barred from schools, cinemas, theatres and sports grounds outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Boycott of Jewish Businesses,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-boycott-of-jewish-businesses. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Jews in Prewar Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jews-in-prewar-germany. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nuremberg Race Laws,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nuremberg-race-laws. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Book Burning,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/book-burning. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Judenboykott_1933-04-01_Berlin_Anti-Semitic_Anti-Jewish_Nazi_boycott_of_Jewish_businesses_shops_in_Germany_SA_brownshirt_Poster_warning_sign_window_display_lace_and_crochet_shop_Kauft_nicht_bei_Juden_No_known_copyright.jpg/1280px-Judenboykott_1933-04-01_Berlin_Anti-Semitic_Anti-Jewish_Nazi_boycott_of_Jewish_businesses_shops_in_Germany_SA_brownshirt_Poster_warning_sign_window_display_lace_and_crochet_shop_Kauft_nicht_bei_Juden_No_known_copyright.jpg","title":"The boycott of 1 April 1933","desc":"A militia man posted outside a Berlin lace shop under placards in German and English telling Germans to buy only at German shops.","credit":"Photograph attributed to Georg Pahl. CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Judenboykott_1933-04-01_Berlin_Anti-Semitic_Anti-Jewish_Nazi_boycott_of_Jewish_businesses_shops_in_Germany_SA_brownshirt_Poster_warning_sign_window_display_lace_and_crochet_shop_Kauft_nicht_bei_Juden_No_known_copyright.jpg","alt":"A uniformed man standing before a shop window hung with lace, beside large printed placards."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the clause spread so far beyond the civil service law?","a":"The formula proved copyable: a quota capped Jewish pupils that same April, decrees cut Jewish work in medicine and law, and more than four hundred restrictions followed in six years, many issued by state, regional and town officials on their own initiative."},{"q":"Why did private associations adopt a clause nobody had ordered?","a":"They took it up voluntarily, so a man could be expelled from a choir or a sports club under a rule the state had never imposed. Exclusion spread by imitation as well as by decree."},{"q":"Why did the 1935 race laws change what the paragraph rested on?","a":"They rebuilt the whole scheme on descent rather than religion, which gave the scattered exclusions a single legal definition to work from instead of leaving each office to decide who was Jewish."}]},
{"id":"ww2-068","num":"68","category":"World War II · History","question":"Announced at a party rally in September 1935, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reserved German citizenship to those of German blood and forbade marriage between Jews and other Germans.","questions":["A person with three or four Jewish grandparents was a Jew under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a definition that rested on the religious records of the grandparents.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made citizenship a condition of many professions and courses, they were used to push Jews out of offices and universities."],"answer":"Nuremberg Laws","answerText":"Nuremberg Laws","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Announced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 September 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Laws</span><span class=\"dt-v\">citizenship, and blood and honour</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"dt-v\">regulation of 14 November 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">A Jew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">three or four Jewish grandparents</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","concept","politics","law","history","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>Nuremberg Laws</b> of 15 September 1935 were two measures announced at the party’s rally in Nuremberg that made the exclusion of Jews from German life a matter of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first, a citizenship law, reserved citizenship to those of German or related blood, which made good a promise the party had printed in its programme in 1920, so that Jews became subjects with no political rights.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second, the law for the protection of German blood and honour, forbade marriage and sexual relations between Jews and other Germans on pain of severe punishment, and was meant to isolate them socially as well as racially.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also barred Jewish households from employing German women under 45 as servants, and barred Jews from flying the new national flag.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither law said who a Jew was, and the whole scheme needed that question answered before it could be enforced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A regulation of 14 November supplied the answer: a person with three or four Jewish grandparents was a Jew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A grandparent counted as Jewish if they had belonged to the Jewish religious community, so the racial law rested in the end on church and synagogue records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those with one or two such grandparents were put in a middle category, neither German nor Jewish in the law’s terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germans were then required to prove their ancestry with baptism certificates, community registers and gravestones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the definition settled, the regime used it to push Jews out of professions, offices and courses of study that citizenship had been made to require: judges could no longer cite commentaries by Jewish authors, Jewish officers were expelled from the army, and Jewish students were barred from doctoral examinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Nuremberg Race Laws,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-nuremberg-race-laws. [Open access]","\"The Reich Citizenship Law (September 15, 1935) and the First Regulation to the Reich Citizenship Law (November 14, 1935),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/the-reich-citizenship-law-september-15-1935-and-the-first-regulation-to-the-reich-citizenship-law-november-14-1935. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Jews in Prewar Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jews-in-prewar-germany. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Book Burning,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/book-burning. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Nuremberg_laws_Racial_Chart.jpg/1280px-Nuremberg_laws_Racial_Chart.jpg","title":"The official chart of the race laws","desc":"The government's own chart of 1935 setting out the categories the laws created, from German-blooded through two grades of mixed descent to Jew, by the religion of the grandparents.","credit":"German government chart, 1935. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuremberg_laws_Racial_Chart.jpg","alt":"A printed chart headed Die Nürnberger Gesetze with five columns of white, half-shaded and black circles representing grandparents."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the two laws need a further regulation before they could work?","a":"Neither said who a Jew was, and the whole scheme depended on that question. A regulation of 14 November supplied the answer: three or four Jewish grandparents made a person a Jew."},{"q":"Why did a racial law end up resting on church and synagogue records?","a":"A grandparent counted as Jewish if they had belonged to the Jewish religious community, so ancestry could only be established from religious registers. Germans were then required to prove theirs with baptism certificates and gravestones."},{"q":"Why reserve citizenship to those of German or related blood?","a":"It made good a promise the party had printed in its programme in 1920, and turning Jews into subjects with no political rights supplied the legal footing for pushing them out of professions and offices that citizenship was made to require."}]},
{"id":"ww2-069","num":"69","category":"World War II · History","question":"On 9 and 10 November 1938 the riot called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burnt more than 1,400 synagogues across Germany while the regime called it spontaneous popular anger.","questions":["About 26,000 Jewish men were imprisoned in concentration camps after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, for no reason at all beyond the fact that they were Jews.","The Jewish community was fined a billion marks in an atonement payment after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on top of everything the rioters had destroyed."],"answer":"Kristallnacht","answerText":"Kristallnacht","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Riot</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 – 10 November 1938</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Synagogues burnt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">more than 1,400</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Men imprisoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 26,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">one billion Reichsmark</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["event","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Kristallnacht</b>, the night of broken glass, was the anti-Jewish riot that ran through Germany, Austria and the annexed Sudetenland on 9 and 10 November 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 1,400 synagogues were burnt, thousands of Jewish businesses and homes were wrecked and looted, and Jews were humiliated, assaulted and killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The riot was meant to look like a spontaneous outburst of popular anger, and was in fact coordinated by the propaganda minister with Hitler’s approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its pretext was the shooting of a German diplomat in Paris on 7 November by a seventeen-year-old whose parents were among 17,000 Polish Jews the police had expelled ten days before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The expelled had been left stranded in the strip of land between the two frontiers, where they lived in wretched conditions for weeks, and news of their plight is what moved him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The perpetrators were party officials, the militia, the elite guard and the youth organisation, joined by ordinary Germans who stole from the wrecked houses and made a public show of humiliating their Jewish neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The police then imprisoned about 26,000 Jewish men in concentration camps for no reason but that they were Jews.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost 11,000 of them were sent to Dachau, and most were released within weeks or months, many after proving they had arranged to emigrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The regime then fined the Jewish community a billion marks in what it called an atonement payment, and followed it with a barrage of laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> German officials called it an action; Jews called it a pogrom, the Russian word for the anti-Jewish violence of the previous century, and the fiction of spontaneity did not survive contact with the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]","\"American Consul Samuel Honaker’s Description of Antisemitic Persecution and of Kristallnacht and its Aftereffects in the Stuttgart Region (November 1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/american-consul-samuel-honakers-description-of-antisemitic-persecution-and-kristallnacht-and-its-aftereffects-in-the-stuttgart-region-november-12-and-november-15-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Joseph Goebbels,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/joseph-goebbels-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dachau,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dachau. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Antisemitic Legislation, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/antisemitic-legislation-1933-1939. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/The_day_after_Kristallnacht.jpg/1280px-The_day_after_Kristallnacht.jpg","title":"The morning after, 10 November 1938","desc":"Passers-by in a German street looking away from a Jewish-owned shop whose window was smashed during the night.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_day_after_Kristallnacht.jpg","alt":"A shop front with a large jagged hole in its plate-glass window, with several people walking past."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the riot need to look spontaneous?","a":"It was coordinated by the propaganda minister with Hitler's approval, and calling it popular anger let the regime present the violence as what Germans felt rather than what it had ordered. The fiction did not survive contact with the world."},{"q":"Why did a shooting in Paris serve as the pretext?","a":"The seventeen-year-old who shot the diplomat had parents among 17,000 Polish Jews the police had expelled ten days before and left stranded between the frontiers, so the deed the regime used as its excuse was a response to its own expulsion."},{"q":"Why fine the victims a billion marks after the riot?","a":"The regime called it an atonement payment, which made the community pay for the destruction of its own property and turned a night of violence into a transfer of wealth backed by a barrage of laws."}]},
{"id":"ww2-070","num":"70","category":"World War II · History","question":"Delegates from 32 countries met at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1938 to discuss Jewish refugees, and only the Dominican Republic offered to take a substantial number.","questions":["Roosevelt called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> but sent a businessman friend rather than a senior official, which set the weight of the meeting before it opened.","The German government answered the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with satisfaction that its critics abroad would not open their own doors to the refugees."],"answer":"Evian Conference","answerText":"Evian Conference","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 – 15 July 1938</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Countries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">32 represented</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emigrated by 1938</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 250,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Offered refuge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Dominican Republic</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","politics","history","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Evian Conference</b> of July 1938 brought delegates from 32 countries to a resort on the French shore of Lake Geneva to discuss the refugees fleeing Nazi persecution, and it settled almost nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German policy at that stage was to make Jewish life impossible rather than to kill, so that Jews would leave, and by 1938 about 250,000 had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Leaving meant finding a country that would take you, and that was the difficulty the conference had been called to address.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The American president called it but sent a businessman friend rather than a senior official, which set its weight before it opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over nine days delegate after delegate expressed sympathy for the refugees and then explained why his own country could admit no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Only the Dominican Republic offered to take a substantial number, and the meeting’s one lasting product was an intergovernmental committee that would go on working at the problem without the power to solve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The German government replied with satisfaction that foreign states criticised its treatment of Jews and would not open their own doors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> American quotas fixed in 1924 stayed in place, defended by people who feared competition for work in a depression and by officials who were themselves antisemitic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A bill to admit 20,000 refugee children failed in the American legislature the following year, and a liner carrying nearly a thousand refugees was turned away from Cuba and then from the United States in 1939, and had to sail back to Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four months after the conference the pogrom of November showed the world exactly what the refugees were fleeing, and the doors stayed shut all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Evian Conference,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-evian-conference. [Open access]","\"American Consul Samuel Honaker’s Description of Antisemitic Persecution and of Kristallnacht and its Aftereffects in the Stuttgart Region (November 1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/american-consul-samuel-honakers-description-of-antisemitic-persecution-and-kristallnacht-and-its-aftereffects-in-the-stuttgart-region-november-12-and-november-15-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Voyage of the St. Louis,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/voyage-of-the-st-louis-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Kristallnacht,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/kristallnacht. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Jews in Prewar Germany,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jews-in-prewar-germany. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Myron_Taylor_addresses_the_%C3%89vian_Conference.jpg/1280px-Myron_Taylor_addresses_the_%C3%89vian_Conference.jpg","title":"The American delegate speaks at Évian, July 1938","desc":"Myron Taylor addressing the international conference on refugees at Évian-les-Bains, with the other delegations seated around him.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myron_Taylor_addresses_the_%C3%89vian_Conference.jpg","alt":"A man in a dark suit standing to read from papers at a long table, with seated delegates around him."},"why":[{"q":"Why was finding a country to take them the whole difficulty?","a":"German policy at that stage was to make Jewish life impossible rather than to kill, so that Jews would leave. Leaving required somewhere to go, and about 250,000 had already gone by 1938."},{"q":"Why did sending a businessman rather than an official matter?","a":"The American president had called the conference and then sent a friend from business instead of a senior official, which set its weight before it opened and signalled that no country need expect pressure to raise its limits."},{"q":"Why did the German government welcome the outcome?","a":"It replied with satisfaction that foreign states criticised its treatment of Jews and would not open their own doors, which let it answer criticism by pointing at the refusals of its critics."}]},
{"id":"ww2-071","num":"71","category":"World War II · History","question":"Almost a thousand Jewish refugees sailed from Hamburg aboard the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in May 1939, and were refused entry by Cuba and then by the United States.","questions":["Britain, France, Belgium and the Netherlands took in the passengers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but three of those four countries were overrun by Germany the next year.","Ten months after the Évian conference had declined to raise any refugee limits, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to carry its passengers back across the Atlantic."],"answer":"St. Louis","answerText":"St. Louis","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Sailed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 May 1939, from Hamburg</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Passengers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">almost 1,000 refugees</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Refused by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Cuba, then the United States</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Returned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1939</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>St. Louis</b> was the German liner that carried almost a thousand Jewish refugees out of Hamburg in May 1939 and was made to bring them back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She sailed from Hamburg on 13 May 1939 with Cuba as her destination, carrying passengers who held landing permits bought in good faith and who expected to wait there for American visas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before she arrived the Cuban government revoked those permits, and she lay off Havana for days while a handful of passengers were let ashore and the rest were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States refused them as well, and in June she turned back across the Atlantic towards the continent they had paid to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Britain, France, Belgium and the Netherlands then agreed between them to divide the stranded passengers up and take them in, which is why the voyage is remembered as a rescue at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That looked like a rescue and was only half of one, since three of those four countries were overrun by German forces the following year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the passengers put ashore in western Europe were caught there when the German army arrived and were murdered in the killing programme that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The voyage happened ten months after 32 governments had met at Évian and declined, almost without exception, to raise their limits on refugees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the passengers were fleeing was already plain: a country where Jews had been stripped of citizenship, robbed of their businesses and imprisoned in camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ship became the standing illustration of a refugee crisis in which the danger was obvious, the escape route existed and had been paid for, and the doors were shut anyway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Voyage of the St. Louis,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/voyage-of-the-st-louis-1. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The Evian Conference,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-evian-conference. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Euthanasia Program,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82-%D0%9B%D1%83%D0%B8%D1%81%C2%BB_%282%29.jpg/1280px-%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82-%D0%9B%D1%83%D0%B8%D1%81%C2%BB_%282%29.jpg","title":"Passengers on the St. Louis, 1939","desc":"A group of the ship's Jewish passengers photographed on deck during the voyage, beside a lifebuoy carrying her name.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9D%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B5_%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B9%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B0_%C2%AB%D0%A1%D0%B5%D0%BD%D1%82-%D0%9B%D1%83%D0%B8%D1%81%C2%BB_%282%29.jpg","alt":"Eleven men and women in summer clothes posed on a ship's deck behind a lifebuoy marked St. Louis."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the voyage remembered as only half a rescue?","a":"Britain, France, Belgium and the Netherlands divided the stranded passengers between them, but three of those four countries were overrun the following year, and many of those put ashore in western Europe were caught there and murdered."},{"q":"Why did the passengers hold permits that turned out to be worthless?","a":"They had bought Cuban landing permits in good faith and expected to wait there for American visas, but the Cuban government revoked the permits before the ship arrived and the United States then refused them as well."},{"q":"Why does this voyage stand for the wider refugee crisis?","a":"The danger was already plain, the escape route existed and had been paid for, and the doors were shut anyway, ten months after 32 governments had met at Evian and declined almost without exception to raise their limits."}]},
{"id":"ww2-072","num":"72","category":"World War II · History","question":"The killing of institutionalised disabled patients by German doctors was called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after the Berlin street address of the office that ran it.","questions":["Six gassing installations built under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> killed more than seventy thousand people between January 1940 and August 1941, by the programme’s own count.","A bishop’s sermon in August 1941 led Hitler to halt the gassings of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though the killing of disabled children never stopped at all."],"answer":"Aktion T4","answerText":"Aktion T4","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reporting decree</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 August 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gassings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 1940 – August 1941</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Killed at six centres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70,273</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Halted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late August 1941</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","practice","history","germany","science","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Aktion T4</b> was the secret programme by which German doctors and nurses killed institutionalised patients with disabilities, and it was the regime’s first industrial killing of its own citizens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its name came from the address of its coordinating office in Berlin, Tiergartenstrasse 4, and it was run out of Hitler’s private chancellery rather than through any ministry, which kept it clear of the ordinary machinery of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the doctrine that some lives were unworthy of life, a claim about genetic and financial burden that eugenicists had been making for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A decree of August 1939 required physicians, nurses and midwives to report every newborn and child under three showing signs of severe disability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parents were then encouraged to admit such children to special clinics, which were killing wards where staff used overdoses of medicine or starvation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hitler signed a secret authorisation that autumn to protect the doctors, and had it backdated to 1 September 1939 so that it would look like a wartime measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six gassing installations were then built for adult patients at Bernburg, Brandenburg, Grafeneck, Hadamar, Hartheim and Sonnenstein, and by the programme’s own reckoning they killed 70,273 people between January 1940 and August 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Records were falsified to give natural causes in every case, and it still became an open secret, protested privately and from pulpits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the bishop of Münster denounced the killings in a sermon on 3 August 1941, Hitler ordered the gassings halted, though the killing of children never stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It resumed the next year by injection and starvation and ran to the end of the war, and the men and the methods went on to the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Euthanasia Program,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Persecution of Roma (Gypsies) in Prewar Germany, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-roma-gypsies-in-prewar-germany-1933-1939. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Bus_Hartheim_Foto_Niedernhart_Prozess.jpg","title":"A grey bus at Hartheim, about 1940","desc":"One of the buses run by a bogus ambulance company that carried patients to the killing centre at Hartheim, its windows painted over, photographed with its driver.","credit":"CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bus_Hartheim_Foto_Niedernhart_Prozess.jpg","alt":"A single-decker bus of the 1930s parked outside a large building, a man in uniform standing beside the door."},"why":[{"q":"Why was it run from Hitler's private chancellery rather than a ministry?","a":"That kept it clear of the ordinary machinery of the state, so the killing needed no law, no ministry's approval and no paper trail through the administration that would have made it visible."},{"q":"Why was Hitler's authorisation backdated to 1 September 1939?","a":"Backdating it to the day the war began made a programme of killing German citizens look like a wartime measure, while the document itself existed only to protect the doctors who carried it out."},{"q":"Why did halting the gassings in 1941 not end the killing?","a":"Hitler ordered the gassings stopped after a bishop denounced them publicly, but the killing of children never stopped, it resumed the next year by injection and starvation, and the men and methods went on to the camps."}]},
{"id":"ww2-073","num":"73","category":"World War II · History","question":"German law made sexual relations between men a crime under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the regime rewrote in wider terms in 1935 and enforced with raids and surveillance.","questions":["About 100,000 arrests were made under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the Nazi years, and roughly half of them ended in a conviction and a prison sentence.","Although the regime rewrote and enforced it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not a Nazi invention and had been part of the German criminal code since 1871."],"answer":"Paragraph 175","answerText":"Paragraph 175","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the code</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rewritten</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1935, in wider terms</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrests</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 100,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Convictions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">roughly half of them</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["law","concept","practice","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Paragraph 175</b> was the clause of the German criminal code that made sexual relations between men a crime, and it was the chief instrument of the regime’s campaign against gay men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not a Nazi invention: it had entered the imperial code in 1871 and stayed in German law in one form or another until 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Courts under the empire and the Weimar Republic had read it narrowly, requiring proof of acts resembling intercourse, which made convictions difficult to obtain and kept the numbers low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regime rewrote it in 1935 in wider and harsher terms, and convictions rose sharply once the new wording was in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The police raided the bars, watched the places where gay men met, and closed down their associations and their newspapers, dismantling communities that had grown up in Germany before 1933.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> About 100,000 arrests were made under the statute during the twelve years of the regime, and roughly half of them, some 53,000, ended in a conviction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of those convicted served fixed prison terms, but some were sent on to concentration camps at the end of their sentences, for no fixed period at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other groups were persecuted by the same machinery: Roma were registered by a research office on racial hygiene from 1936, made subject to the race laws, and rounded up into municipal camps in city after city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Before the Berlin Games of 1936 the police cleared the Roma of the city into a field at Marzahn beside a cemetery and a sewage dump.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the camps prisoners wore coloured markings by category, and the men held as asocials, among them a great many Roma, wore a black triangle sewn to the uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Paragraph 175 and the Nazi Campaign against Homosexuality,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/paragraph-175-and-the-nazi-campaign-against-homosexuality. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Persecution of Homosexuals in the Third Reich,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-homosexuals-in-the-third-reich. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Persecution of Roma (Gypsies) in Prewar Germany, 1933–1939,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/persecution-of-roma-gypsies-in-prewar-germany-1933-1939. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Nazi Camps,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nazi-camps. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Euthanasia Program,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/euthanasia-program. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a0/Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1993-051-07%2C_Tafel_mit_KZ-Kennzeichen_%28Winkel%29_retouched.jpg","title":"The camp marking chart, 1936","desc":"The official chart of prisoner markings, showing the pink triangle for men held under Paragraph 175 and the black triangle for those classed as asocial.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 146-1993-051-07, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1993-051-07,_Tafel_mit_KZ-Kennzeichen_%28Winkel%29_retouched.jpg","alt":"A hand-painted chart of coloured triangles in rows, with a striped prisoner's jacket and trousers drawn at the foot."},"why":[{"q":"Why did convictions rise sharply after 1935 under an old law?","a":"The clause had been in the code since 1871, but courts had read it narrowly, requiring proof of acts resembling intercourse. The regime rewrote it in wider and harsher terms, so the same statute suddenly convicted far more men."},{"q":"Why did the police close bars and newspapers as well as prosecute?","a":"Prosecution reached individuals; raiding the meeting places and shutting the associations and papers dismantled the communities that had grown up in Germany before 1933, which no number of convictions on its own would have done."},{"q":"Why did some men stay imprisoned after serving their sentences?","a":"Most of those convicted served fixed prison terms, but some were sent on to concentration camps at the end of them, for no fixed period at all, so the courts handed the police a supply of prisoners the courts had not sentenced."}]},
{"id":"ww2-074","num":"74","category":"World War II · History","question":"Opposition to a pro-Nazi faction inside German Protestantism produced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose founding declaration put the church’s allegiance to scripture above any earthly leader.","questions":["Bonhoeffer and Niemöller belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but neither was typical of a movement mostly concerned with keeping the state out of church affairs.","When a protest was read from the pulpits of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in March 1935, the authorities briefly arrested more than seven hundred pastors."],"answer":"Confessing Church","answerText":"Confessing Church","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Concordat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 July 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Barmen declaration</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pastors arrested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1935, over 700</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Papal encyclical</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 March 1937</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","concept","history","germany","religion","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Confessing Church</b> was the movement within German Protestantism that refused to let the state dictate the church’s doctrine, and it emerged in opposition to a pro-Nazi faction within its own body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of Germany’s sixty million people were Christian in 1933, about forty million Protestant and twenty million Catholic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A movement of German Christians had grown up in the 1920s that embraced the racial and nationalist parts of the new ideology and wanted a single Reich Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its opponents answered with the Barmen declaration, which held that the church owed its allegiance to God and scripture rather than to any earthly leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both groups stayed inside the German Evangelical Church, so what followed was a struggle for control of one body rather than a schism, and it was fought over doctrine and appointments as much as over politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The regime answered dissent forcefully: when a protest was read from Confessing pulpits in March 1935, more than seven hundred pastors were briefly arrested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its two most famous members were Dietrich Bonhoeffer, executed for joining the conspiracy against Hitler, and Martin Niemöller, who spent seven years in the camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither man was typical, and for most of its members the struggle was an internal church affair about keeping the state out of doctrine rather than a fight against the regime itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Catholic Church had meanwhile signed a concordat with the government in July 1933, trading withdrawal from politics for guarantees the regime did not intend to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When those guarantees were broken the pope answered in 1937 with an encyclical read from German pulpits, written in German rather than Latin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Reich Concordat between the Holy See and the German Reich (July 20, 1933),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/reich-concordat-between-the-holy-see-and-the-german-reich-july-20-1933. [Open access]","\"With Deep Anxiety (Mit brennender Sorge): Papal Encyclical on the Situation of the Catholic Church in the German Reich (March 14, 1937),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/with-deep-anxiety-mit-brennender-sorge-papal-encyclical-on-the-situation-of-the-catholic-church-in-the-german-reich-march-14-1937. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dietrich Bonhoeffer,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dietrich-bonhoeffer. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Freie_Evangelische_Synode_1934_%2827818347276%29.jpg/1280px-Freie_Evangelische_Synode_1934_%2827818347276%29.jpg","title":"The free synod at Barmen, January 1934","desc":"Delegates in conversation at the free evangelical synod held at Barmen on 3 and 4 January 1934, one of the meetings out of which the Confessing Church came.","credit":"Archiv der Evangelischen Kirche im Rheinland. CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freie_Evangelische_Synode_1934_%2827818347276%29.jpg","alt":"Two men in suits seated close together in conversation in a plain room."},"why":[{"q":"Why was this a struggle for control rather than a schism?","a":"Both the German Christians and their opponents stayed inside the German Evangelical Church, so what followed was fought over doctrine and appointments within one body rather than by leaving it."},{"q":"Why are Bonhoeffer and Niemoller misleading as examples of it?","a":"Neither man was typical. For most members the struggle was an internal church affair about keeping the state out of doctrine rather than a fight against the regime, which is what the two most famous cases suggest."},{"q":"Why did the Catholic Church end up protesting from German pulpits in 1937?","a":"It had signed a concordat in July 1933 trading withdrawal from politics for guarantees the regime did not intend to keep, and when those were broken the pope answered with an encyclical written in German rather than Latin."}]},
{"id":"ww2-075","num":"75","category":"World War II · History","question":"Germany tore up the military clauses of the Versailles treaty on 16 March 1935 by reintroducing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and rearmament ran openly from that point.","questions":["A credit scheme run through a bogus company hid the cost of the rearmament that followed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, paying for about half of all weapons bought to 1936.","Within a year of reintroducing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Germany sent about 30,000 soldiers into the demilitarised Rhineland, and neither France nor Britain acted."],"answer":"conscription","answerText":"conscription","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conscription</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 March 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Naval treaty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 June 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rhineland</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 March 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Autarky memo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","event","warfare","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"German rearmament began in earnest with the reintroduction of <b>conscription</b> on 16 March 1935, which tore up the military clauses of the Versailles treaty in public.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The announcement was followed the next day by Heroes’ Remembrance Day, a mourning day the regime had renamed to celebrate the glory of the fallen rather than to avoid another war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The open break with the treaty’s military clauses brought sharp criticism abroad, and the government moved at once to divide the powers that might otherwise have answered it together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Britain was offered a naval treaty in June 1935 holding the German fleet to 35 per cent of its own and German submarines to 45 per cent, and it accepted without consulting its allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In March 1936 about 30,000 soldiers marched into the demilitarised Rhineland, breaking both the Versailles treaty and the Locarno pact, which Germany had signed of its own free will.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That was the riskiest step so far and could have been stopped, but France would not act without Britain and Britain saw no international border crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The money came through the Reichsbank, whose president devised a credit scheme of bills drawn on a bogus company, which kept the scale of arms spending out of the published accounts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those bills paid for about half of all weapons bought between 1934 and 1936, and foreign trade was brought under state control to serve the same end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In August 1936 Hitler set the whole country a deadline in a confidential memorandum, and it was expressed in two sentences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The army was to be fit for deployment within four years and the economy fit for war within four years, which is what rearmament had been for all along.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Heroes’ Remembrance Day in Berlin (March 17, 1935),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/heroes-remembrance-day-in-berlin-march-17-1935. [Open access]","\"Joachim von Ribbentrop after the Conclusion of the German-British Naval Treaty in London (June 18, 1935),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/joachim-von-ribbentrop-after-the-conclusion-of-the-german-british-naval-treaty-in-london-june-18-1935. [Open access]","\"Wehrmacht Units Occupy the Demilitarized Rhineland Zone: The First Troops March into Koblenz (March 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/wehrmacht-units-occupy-the-demilitarized-rhineland-zone-the-first-troops-march-into-koblenz-march-1936. [Open access]","\"Hitler with Reichsbank President Hjalmar Schacht (May 5, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-with-reichsbank-president-hjalmar-schacht-may-5-1934. [Open access]","\"Hitler’s Confidential Memo on Autarky (August 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-confidential-memo-on-autarky-august-1936. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H25647%2C_Berlin%2C_Vereidigung_junger_Rekruten.jpg","title":"Swearing in the first conscripts, November 1935","desc":"Recruits of the first class called up under the conscription law of March 1935 taking their oath in Berlin on 7 November of that year.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 183-H25647, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H25647,_Berlin,_Vereidigung_junger_Rekruten.jpg","alt":"Ranks of young soldiers in greatcoats and helmets standing with right hands raised in a city square."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Germany offer Britain a naval treaty in June 1935?","a":"Conscription had torn up the treaty's military clauses in public and brought sharp criticism abroad, so the government moved to divide the powers that might have answered together. Britain accepted without consulting its allies."},{"q":"Why was the Rhineland march the riskiest step so far?","a":"About 30,000 soldiers broke both Versailles and the Locarno pact Germany had signed freely, and it could have been stopped. France would not act without Britain, and Britain saw no international border crossed."},{"q":"Why were arms paid for with bills drawn on a bogus company?","a":"The scheme kept the scale of arms spending out of the published accounts while paying for about half of all weapons bought between 1934 and 1936, so foreign observers could not read the rearmament off the books."}]},
{"id":"ww2-076","num":"76","category":"World War II · History","question":"Rearmament was paid for in its first years by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose bills drawn on a paper company kept about half the arms spending out of the public accounts.","questions":["Foreign trade came under almost complete state control while <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was economics minister, with imports held down to food and the raw materials rearmament needed.","After criticising the pace and the financing of rearmament, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost influence to Göring’s four-year plan and was dismissed from the economics ministry."],"answer":"Hjalmar Schacht","answerText":"Hjalmar Schacht","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reichsbank president</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bogus credit company</span><span class=\"dt-v\">set up April 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Economics minister</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 3 August 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eclipsed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the Four-Year Plan, 1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","economics","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Hjalmar Schacht</b> financed the first years of German rearmament, and his career marks the point at which the regime stopped caring what its bankers thought.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As president of the Reichsbank he devised a credit system of bills drawn on a metallurgical research company that existed only on paper, set up in April 1933 for exactly that purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those bills paid for about half of all weapons purchases between 1934 and 1936 while keeping the spending out of the published accounts, where foreign observers might have read it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He became minister of economics on 3 August 1934, the day after the president’s death, and used the office to drive the country towards self-sufficiency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> German foreign trade came under almost complete state control, with imports held down to foodstuffs and the raw materials rearmament needed, and exports of manufactures pushed hard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His influence began to fall away steadily from 1936, the year a four-year plan was instituted under Hermann Göring and the whole economy was pointed openly at war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plan aimed to centralise the mobilisation of labour, restrict imports, control wages and prices, and synthesise the raw materials Germany lacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also reached into ordinary households, telling Germans to salvage waste and consume less as a duty owed to the racial community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Schacht criticised the pace of rearmament and the way it was being financed, warning that the spending could not go on, and Hitler dismissed him from the economics ministry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party had never held an economic doctrine of its own despite the word socialist in its name, and it left industry in private hands so long as industry produced what a coming war would need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Hitler with Reichsbank President Hjalmar Schacht (May 5, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-with-reichsbank-president-hjalmar-schacht-may-5-1934. [Open access]","\"Hitler’s Confidential Memo on Autarky (August 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-confidential-memo-on-autarky-august-1936. [Open access]","\"Propaganda Poster for the Four-Year Plan (1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/propaganda-poster-for-the-four-year-plan-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Hjalmar_Schacht.jpg","title":"Hjalmar Schacht","desc":"The banker who ran the Reichsbank and, from August 1934, the economics ministry, and who financed the first phase of German rearmament.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hjalmar_Schacht.jpg","alt":"A studio portrait of a man in a high collar and rimless glasses, seated with one arm over a chair back."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Schacht's influence fall away from 1936?","a":"That was the year a four-year plan was instituted under Goring and the economy was pointed openly at war, which took the direction of it out of his hands and made his caution about the pace an obstacle."},{"q":"Why did a party calling itself socialist leave industry in private hands?","a":"It had never held an economic doctrine of its own despite the word in its name, and private ownership was no obstacle so long as industry produced what a coming war would need."},{"q":"Why did state control of foreign trade serve rearmament?","a":"Imports were held down to foodstuffs and the raw materials rearmament needed while exports of manufactures were pushed hard, which drove the country towards self-sufficiency and freed foreign currency for the things arms production could not do without."}]},
{"id":"ww2-077","num":"77","category":"World War II · History","question":"German economic policy was turned openly towards war by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1936, whose charter was a confidential memorandum Hitler had written that August.","questions":["Autarky was the central aim of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that Germany could fight a war without depending on trade for its fuel or its food.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needed households as well as factories, Germans were told to salvage waste as a duty owed to the racial community."],"answer":"Four-Year Plan","answerText":"Four-Year Plan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Memorandum</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plan begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Directed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Hermann Göring</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deadline</span><span class=\"dt-v\">four years, to 1940</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","concept","economics","politics","history","germany"],"abstract":"The <b>Four-Year Plan</b> was the programme begun in October 1936 that turned the German economy openly towards a war the government intended to start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its charter was a confidential memorandum Hitler had written that August, which reviewed Germany’s shortages of raw materials and then set two tasks in two sentences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The army was to be fit for deployment within four years, and the economy fit for war within four years; those were the two tasks, stated in that order and without qualification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hermann Göring was given charge of it, over the head of the economics minister who had financed rearmament so far and whose influence dated from that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The plan centralised the mobilisation of labour, restricted imports, imposed controls on wages and prices, and took charge of allocating the raw materials the country did have.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its central aim was autarky, so that Germany could fight without depending on trade for fuel or food, which meant synthesising in factories what could not be bought or mined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That could not be done by decree alone, so ordinary Germans were told to salvage waste and to curb what they consumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The posters that told them so framed the effort as a duty owed to the racial community rather than as an economic measure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Göring also used the plan’s machinery to systematise the exploitation and expropriation of Jewish property, which was folded into the same drive for resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The party had no economic doctrine of its own to apply, and this was the shape its economics finally took: private industry left in private hands and directed at a war of conquest in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Hitler’s Confidential Memo on Autarky (August 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-s-confidential-memo-on-autarky-august-1936. [Open access]","\"Propaganda Poster for the Four-Year Plan (1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/propaganda-poster-for-the-four-year-plan-1938. [Open access]","\"Hitler with Reichsbank President Hjalmar Schacht (May 5, 1934),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/hitler-with-reichsbank-president-hjalmar-schacht-may-5-1934. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/G%C3%B6ring_Reichsautobahn_1936.jpg/1280px-G%C3%B6ring_Reichsautobahn_1936.jpg","title":"Göring in 1936","desc":"Hermann Göring, who was given charge of the Four-Year Plan that October, saluted by men of the German Labour Front at a motorway opening in April 1936.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%B6ring_Reichsautobahn_1936.jpg","alt":"A uniformed man waving from an open car as ranks of men in working uniform salute beside the road."},"why":[{"q":"Why was autarky the plan's central aim?","a":"So that Germany could fight without depending on trade for fuel or food, which meant synthesising in factories what could not be bought or mined. The point was to make a war of conquest possible rather than to enrich the country."},{"q":"Why give charge of it to Goring rather than the economics minister?","a":"Schacht had financed rearmament so far and doubted the pace it was being pushed at, so putting Goring over his head moved the direction of the economy to somebody who would not argue about how much a coming war should cost."},{"q":"Why frame salvaging waste as a duty to the racial community?","a":"Autarky could not be achieved by decree alone and needed ordinary households to consume less, and an appeal to belonging asked for that as an obligation rather than admitting the shortages the memorandum had described."}]},
{"id":"ww2-078","num":"78","category":"World War II · History","question":"The promise of a German society transcending class and religion was called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it was offered in place of the party politics of the Weimar Republic.","questions":["Open to Aryan Germans of every class, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was closed by definition to Jews, Roma and Black Germans, who were named as outsiders to it.","Appeals to salvage waste and consume less were made in the name of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, as a duty owed to the community rather than an economic necessity."],"answer":"Volksgemeinschaft","answerText":"Volksgemeinschaft","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Promised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Open to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Aryan Germans of every class</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Closed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Jews, Roma and Black Germans</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the associations Germans chose</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","ideology","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Volksgemeinschaft</b>, the people’s community, was the regime’s promise of a German society that transcended the divisions of class and religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was offered in place of the party politics of the Weimar Republic, which the movement had spent a decade denouncing as a system that set Germans against one another for the benefit of nobody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The promise was open to Aryan Germans of every class, region and religion, and closed by definition to Jews, Roma and Black Germans, who were named as outsiders to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its price was individual freedom, since the same programme that promised belonging abolished the associations through which Germans had until then pursued interests of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trade unions went first in May 1933, then the professional and cultural bodies, until almost no organised group in Germany stood outside the party’s reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What replaced them was membership of the community itself, expressed through the party’s own organisations, from the youth movement to the labour front, rather than through anything a person had chosen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The idea did practical work as well as ideological work, and appeals for material sacrifice were made in its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Germans were told to salvage waste and to consume less because they owed it to the community, rather than because the economy required it of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It reached the churches too, where a faction of German Protestants took up its racial and nationalist language and pressed for a single national church built along the same lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The community it described never existed as advertised, but the belonging it offered was real enough to many Germans that they judged it worth the freedoms it cost them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Propaganda Poster for the Four-Year Plan (1938),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/propaganda-poster-for-the-four-year-plan-1938. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Hitler Youth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hitler-youth-2. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/%22Tretet_ein_in_die_Deutsche_Volksgemeinschaft%21%22_-_LABW_-_Hauptstaatsarchiv_Stuttgart_%29J_151_Nr_2712%29.jpeg/1280px-%22Tretet_ein_in_die_Deutsche_Volksgemeinschaft%21%22_-_LABW_-_Hauptstaatsarchiv_Stuttgart_%29J_151_Nr_2712%29.jpeg","title":"Join the German people's community","desc":"A printed appeal in heavy blackletter type inviting Germans to enter the people's community, from the Stuttgart state archive.","credit":"Landesarchiv Baden-Württemberg, Hauptstaatsarchiv Stuttgart, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Tretet_ein_in_die_Deutsche_Volksgemeinschaft%21%22_-_LABW_-_Hauptstaatsarchiv_Stuttgart_%29J_151_Nr_2712%29.jpeg","alt":"A wide printed sheet reading Tretet ein in die Deutsche Volksgemeinschaft in bold German blackletter."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the promise of belonging require dissolving Germans' own associations?","a":"The same programme that offered community abolished the trade unions, professional bodies and cultural groups through which Germans had pursued interests of their own, so belonging replaced choosing rather than sitting beside it."},{"q":"Why was the idea useful for asking Germans to consume less?","a":"It let material sacrifice be demanded as a debt owed to the community rather than explained as something the economy required, which put the request beyond argument about whether the economy really required it."},{"q":"Why did the community have to be closed as well as open?","a":"It was offered to Aryan Germans of every class, region and religion, and Jews, Roma and Black Germans were named as outsiders. The exclusion was what gave the promised unity its content, since a community open to everyone would have described nothing."}]},
{"id":"ww2-079","num":"79","category":"World War II · History","question":"Membership of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made compulsory by a law of December 1936, which turned the party’s youth organisation into an organ of the state.","questions":["From about 100,000 members in January 1933 the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew to 5.4 million by 1937, roughly two thirds of all Germans aged ten to eighteen.","Boys in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were trained in increasingly military ways while the girls’ branch prepared its members to be wives and mothers."],"answer":"Hitler Youth","answerText":"Hitler Youth","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Members</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100,000 in January 1933</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">5.4 million by 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Compulsory</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 December 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7.2 million in 1940</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["institution","practice","politics","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Hitler Youth</b> was the party’s youth organisation, with a branch for boys of that name and a League of German Girls beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It had about 100,000 members when the party took power in January 1933 and more than two million by the end of that same year, roughly a third of German children of the age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Germany had been full of youth movements since the turn of the century, political, religious and social, and the party’s youth leader had nearly all of them dissolved in 1933 and their members drawn into his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Membership reached 5.4 million by 1937, about two thirds of all Germans aged between ten and eighteen, drawn in by enthusiasm, by pressure from other children and by coercion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A law of December 1936 made membership compulsory and turned the organisation from a party body into a state one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Physical training for boys took increasingly paramilitary forms, with an emphasis on strength of will, obedience and the unconditional performance of duty rather than on games.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Girls were prepared instead for the future the regime intended for them, as wives and mothers of the nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1940 the organisation held 7.2 million young Germans, some 82 per cent of them, and in the last months of the war boys in their early teens were put into civil defence and the home guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not every young German went willingly, and some avoided it altogether: the groups who met to listen to banned swing music kept out of it and passed messages to student dissidents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For most of them, though, it was the ordinary shape of growing up, and it was where the party reached children before anyone else could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Hitler Youth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hitler-youth-2. [Open access]","\"Law on the Hitler Youth (December 1, 1936),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/law-on-the-hitler-youth-december-1-1936. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Gleichschaltung: Coordinating the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gleichschaltung-coordinating-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Foundations of the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/foundations-of-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","\"Listening to Swing Music (1937),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/listening-to-swing-music-1937. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/Bundesarchiv_Bild_133-043%2C_Hitlerjugend%2C_Zeltlager.jpg","title":"A Hitler Youth camp, about 1933","desc":"Boys crowded into the mouth of a bell tent at a Hitler Youth camp, with a leader standing beside it.","credit":"Bundesarchiv, Bild 133-043, CC BY-SA 3.0 de, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_133-043,_Hitlerjugend,_Zeltlager.jpg","alt":"A dozen laughing boys packed into the open flap of a large bell tent, one older boy in uniform standing at the right."},"why":[{"q":"Why did membership multiply twentyfold during 1933?","a":"Germany had been full of youth movements since the turn of the century, and the party's youth leader had nearly all of them dissolved that year and their members drawn into his own, so growth came from absorbing rivals as much as from recruitment."},{"q":"Why did a law in December 1936 change what the organisation was?","a":"It made membership compulsory and turned a party body into a state one, so belonging stopped being a political choice and became something a German child was required by law to do."},{"q":"Why were boys and girls trained so differently?","a":"Boys' physical training took increasingly paramilitary forms, stressing strength of will, obedience and unconditional duty rather than games, while girls were prepared for the future the regime intended for them, as wives and mothers of the nation."}]},
{"id":"ww2-080","num":"80","category":"World War II · History","question":"Young Germans who kept listening to banned jazz and swing were called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the regime condemned the music as degenerate.","questions":["Because a person could love the music and still accept the regime, historians argue about whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> amounted to resistance at all.","Some clubs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused the compulsory youth organisation and passed messages to the student group that printed leaflets in Munich."],"answer":"Swing Kids","answerText":"Swing Kids","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Music condemned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Youth service</span><span class=\"dt-v\">compulsory from 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Confessing protest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1935, 700 arrests</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Munich leaflets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1942 to 1943</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["culture","people","practice","history","germany","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Swing Kids</b> were young Germans who went on listening to jazz and swing after the regime condemned the music, and they are the awkward case in any account of dissent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jazz had come to Germany with American goods and American money in the 1920s, under the arrangements that followed the war, and it had taken hold among the young.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After 1933 the regime called it degenerate, tying it to Black American culture and to what it described as a Jewish vice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Young Germans went on dancing to it anyway, in clubs and at private parties, wearing long hair and English clothes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether that was resistance is a real question rather than a rhetorical one, since a person could love the music, frequent the clubs and still believe everything the regime taught about race.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Some of the clubs did become political over time, and their members refused to join the party’s youth organisation, membership of which the law had by then made compulsory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One such network kept up a line of communication with the student group in Munich that printed and distributed leaflets against the regime and was executed for doing so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That group is the reason the question matters, because it shows how far the distance between non-conformity and opposition could be crossed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most dissent before the war never crossed it: the churches argued mainly about their own affairs, and a protest read from Confessing pulpits in 1935 brought some seven hundred arrests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The few who went further, among them a theologian later executed for joining the plot against Hitler, were exceptions in a country that had mostly acquiesced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Listening to Swing Music (1937),\" in <i>German History in Documents and Images</i>, Nazi Germany (1933–1945), https://germanhistorydocs.org/en/nazi-germany-1933-1945/listening-to-swing-music-1937. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"White Rose,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/white-rose. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Hitler Youth,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/hitler-youth-2. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"The German Churches and the Nazi State,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/the-german-churches-and-the-nazi-state. [Open access]","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, \"Dietrich Bonhoeffer,\" <i>Holocaust Encyclopedia</i>, https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/dietrich-bonhoeffer. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is calling them resistance a real question rather than a rhetorical one?","a":"A person could love the music, frequent the clubs and still believe everything the regime taught about race, so listening to condemned music was non-conformity and not by itself opposition to anything the regime was doing."},{"q":"Why did the regime condemn jazz as degenerate at all?","a":"It tied the music to Black American culture and to what it described as a Jewish vice, so a form that had reached Germany with American goods in the 1920s became a racial matter rather than a question of taste."},{"q":"Why does the Munich student group matter to their story?","a":"One swing network kept up a line of communication with the students who printed leaflets against the regime and were executed for it, which shows how far the distance between non-conformity and opposition could be crossed, and that most dissent never crossed it."}]},
{"id":"ww2-081","num":"81","category":"World War II · History","question":"The Meiji Constitution of 1889 created the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a state whose emperor held every executive power and served as commander-in-chief of its armed forces.","questions":["Taiwan was taken from China in 1895 and Korea annexed in 1910 by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had abolished its feudal order only a generation earlier.","In 1914 barely 2.6 percent of the population could vote in the Imperial Diet of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where former samurai from two domains still made the decisions."],"answer":"Empire of Japan","answerText":"Empire of Japan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Constitution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 February 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Diet opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1947</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["state","history","japan","20th century","imperialism"],"abstract":"The <b>Empire of Japan</b> was the state founded on the Meiji Constitution of 1889 and dissolved in 1947, and it is the Japan that fought the Second World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founders overthrew the feudal order in 1868 and built the institutions of a modern country at speed, adding compulsory education in 1872, a cabinet in 1885 and a civil service examination in 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The constitution promulgated on 11 February 1889 gave the country an elected lower house within an Imperial Diet, and the Diet first met the following year, after a long campaign by the Freedom and People’s Rights Movement had pressed the government into conceding both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also vested every executive, legislative and judicial power in the emperor and made him commander-in-chief, which mattered enormously later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Voting was narrow at first, and in 1914 only 1.5 million men paid the ten yen in annual tax that the franchise required, about 2.6 percent of the population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Real decisions rested for decades with a small group of former samurai from the Satsuma and Chōshū domains who had led the reforms, stood close to the throne, and were known as the genrō or elder statesmen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The empire grew outward as it modernised, taking Taiwan from China in 1895, defeating Russia in 1905 and annexing Korea in 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Treaty revision ran alongside that expansion, ending extraterritoriality in 1899 and recovering full control of the tariff in 1911.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The United States and Japan were on cordial terms at the turn of the century, and an American president mediated the peace that ended the Russian war, though relations soured badly over immigration in the two decades afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The structure the constitution created, with an emperor above politics and a military answering to him rather than to the cabinet, is what the 1930s crisis broke open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, \"Evolution of the Meiji State,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha2/index.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10434. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japanese-American Relations at the Turn of the Century, 1900–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/japanese-relations. [Open access]","\"Treaty Regarding the Annexation of Korea to the Empire of Japan,\" Seoul, 22 August 1910, in <i>\"The World and Japan\" Database</i>, Institute for Advanced Studies on Asia, University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/pw/19100822.T1E.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Kenpohapu-chikanobu.jpg/1280px-Kenpohapu-chikanobu.jpg","title":"The promulgation of the Meiji Constitution, 1889","desc":"A woodblock triptych of the ceremony of 11 February 1889 at which the emperor handed the constitution to his prime minister before the assembled court and government.","credit":"Toyohara Chikanobu, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenpohapu-chikanobu.jpg","alt":"Colour woodblock print of a crowded palace hall, the emperor seated at right beneath red drapery while ranks of officials in gold-braided uniforms stand and bow."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the 1889 constitution's structure matter so much in the 1930s?","a":"It vested every executive, legislative and judicial power in the emperor and made him commander-in-chief, so the military answered to him rather than to the cabinet. That arrangement is what the crisis of the 1930s broke open."},{"q":"Why did an elected lower house not mean broad participation?","a":"The franchise required paying ten yen in annual tax, so in 1914 only 1.5 million men could vote, about 2.6 per cent of the population, and real decisions rested with a small group of elder statesmen close to the throne."},{"q":"Why did the country modernise and expand at the same time?","a":"The institutions and the empire were one project: compulsory education, a cabinet and a civil service came in the 1870s and 1880s, and Taiwan, victory over Russia and Korea followed, alongside treaty revision that ended extraterritoriality and recovered the tariff."}]},
{"id":"ww2-082","num":"82","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew from Taiwan in 1895 through the annexation of Korea in 1910 to the German Pacific islands seized in 1914, before postwar treaties tried to contain it.","questions":["Russia was defeated in 1905 and its lease on the Liaotung Peninsula passed into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had been forced to give that same peninsula back ten years earlier.","The Twenty-One Demands presented to China in 1915 were the most sweeping rights ever extracted by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and China did not forget them."],"answer":"Japanese colonial empire","answerText":"Japanese colonial empire","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Taiwan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1895</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Portsmouth</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Korea annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1910</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pacific islands</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1914</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","japan","imperialism","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese colonial empire</b> was the overseas territory Japan gathered between 1895 and 1919, and it was the base from which the country moved into Manchuria in 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan defeated China in 1894 and 1895, took Taiwan, and drove Chinese power out of Korea, only to be pressed by Russia, France and Germany into handing back the Liaotung Peninsula it had won, in exchange for a larger indemnity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia then leased that same peninsula for itself and moved troops into Manchuria, and the collision that followed became the Russo-Japanese War of 1904 and 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Treaty of Portsmouth, mediated by Theodore Roosevelt, gave Japan the southern half of Sakhalin and a free hand in Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korea was made a protectorate in 1905 and annexed outright on 22 August 1910, by a treaty in which its emperor ceded all rights of sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The First World War extended the reach further, as the navy took the German island groups of the western Pacific in 1914 and the army stormed the German fortress at Qingdao that November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Twenty-One Demands of 1915 were the most sweeping set of rights any power had extracted from China, and they were remembered in China long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> More than 50,000 Japanese troops were poured into the Russian Far East by December 1918, in an intervention the Japanese public greeted coldly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of this was unusual among the powers of the day, and the United States had built its own China policy on demanding equal commercial access rather than territory, a position it announced in a set of notes to the other powers in 1899 and 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What changed after 1918 was the mood, and the Washington Conference of 1921 and 1922 tied Japan into naval limits and a treaty guaranteeing Chinese integrity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Siobhan Peeling, \"Russo-Japanese War,\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10050. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Treaty of Portsmouth and the Russo-Japanese War, 1904–1905,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/portsmouth-treaty. [Open access]","\"Treaty Regarding the Annexation of Korea to the Empire of Japan,\" Seoul, 22 August 1910, in <i>\"The World and Japan\" Database</i>, Institute for Advanced Studies on Asia, University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/pw/19100822.T1E.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Secretary of State John Hay and the Open Door in China, 1899–1900,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/hay-and-china. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Empire_of_Japan_%281930%29_territories.svg/1280px-Empire_of_Japan_%281930%29_territories.svg.png","title":"The Japanese empire in 1930","desc":"A globe showing Japan in dark green with its colonies of Korea, Taiwan, Karafuto and the Kuril Islands in lighter green, and the South Seas Mandate outlined to the south-east.","credit":"Colohisto, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empire_of_Japan_%281930%29_territories.svg","alt":"Orthographic globe centred on East Asia with the Japanese home islands in dark green, Korea, Taiwan and Karafuto in pale green, and a dotted boundary over the western Pacific."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the Liaotung Peninsula lead to war with Russia?","a":"Japan won it from China and was pressed by Russia, France and Germany into handing it back, whereupon Russia leased the same peninsula for itself and moved troops into Manchuria. The collision that followed became the war of 1904."},{"q":"Why were the Twenty-One Demands remembered in China so long afterwards?","a":"They were the most sweeping set of rights any power had extracted from China, which made them a standing grievance rather than one more episode among the concessions the powers had been taking."},{"q":"Why does it matter that none of this was unusual among the powers?","a":"The other powers were doing the same, the United States building its China policy on demanding equal commercial access instead. What changed after 1918 was the mood, and the Washington Conference tied Japan into naval limits and a guarantee of Chinese integrity."}]},
{"id":"ww2-083","num":"83","category":"World War II · History","question":"Korea was governed from 1910 to 1945 by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose first decade of rule was enforced by a military gendarmerie with a fearsome reputation.","questions":["A declaration of independence read out across Korea on 1 March 1919 was suppressed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which afterwards eased censorship without loosening its grip.","The slogan naisen ittai, meaning Japan and Korea as one body, was pressed on Koreans by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the late 1930s to make them serve the war."],"answer":"Government-General of Korea","answerText":"Government-General of Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Protectorate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 November 1905</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">22 August 1910</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independence movement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 March 1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","korea","japan","imperialism"],"abstract":"The <b>Government-General of Korea</b> was the colonial administration that governed the Korean peninsula for Japan from 1910 until the empire surrendered in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had taken charge of Korean foreign relations on 17 November 1905, and the American legation in Seoul closed eleven days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The protectorate that followed was a colony in all but name, and its Residency-General built a press-control apparatus through a security act and a press act in 1907, textbook regulations in 1908 and a publication act in 1909.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The annexation treaty signed at Seoul on 22 August 1910 had the Korean emperor make a complete and permanent cession of all rights of sovereignty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first decade of direct rule was run by a military gendarmerie whose harshness in the countryside was notorious even to foreign observers otherwise sympathetic to Japanese administration, and the Korean police force it replaced was dissolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> On 1 March 1919 a declaration of independence was read aloud in towns across the country, and 33 signatories handed themselves in to the police in Seoul.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The demonstrations were unarmed and enormous, spreading from Seoul across the whole country within hours, and the colonial authorities put them down with a violence that foreign witnesses photographed and recorded in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan answered with what it called cultural rule, easing press restrictions and replacing gendarmes with civil police, while leaving the structure of the colony untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From the late 1930s the policy hardened again into assimilation, summed up in the slogan naisen ittai, meaning Japan and Korea as one body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That campaign was meant to turn Koreans into imperial subjects who would work and fight for the war, and it reached into schooling, language, names and family life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["\"Treaty Regarding the Annexation of Korea to the Empire of Japan,\" Seoul, 22 August 1910, in <i>\"The World and Japan\" Database</i>, Institute for Advanced Studies on Asia, University of Tokyo, https://worldjpn.net/documents/texts/pw/19100822.T1E.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"A Guide to the United States’ History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Korea,\" https://history.state.gov/countries/korea. [Open access]","Soyoung Kim, \"Textbook Inspection and Censorship in Korea during the Protectorate Period: A Study of Inspection Copies of Textbooks Compiled by the Young Korean Academy,\" <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 2 (2016): 79–119, https://doi.org/10.22372/ijkh.2016.08.21.2.79. [Open access]","F. A. McKenzie, <i>Korea’s Fight for Freedom</i> (New York: Fleming H. Revell, 1920), https://archive.org/details/koreasfightforfr00mckerich. [Open access]","AhRan Ellie Bae, \"Helen Kim as New Woman and Collaborator: A Comprehensive Assessment of Korean Collaboration under Japanese Colonial Rule,\" <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 1 (2017): 107–37, https://doi.org/10.22372/ijkh.2017.22.1.107. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Japanese_General_Government_Building.jpg/1280px-Japanese_General_Government_Building.jpg","title":"The Government-General building in Seoul, 1929","desc":"The domed headquarters of the colonial administration, built inside the grounds of the Gyeongbokgung palace and finished in 1926.","credit":"Mondaen Bangtaero, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_General_Government_Building.jpg","alt":"Sepia photograph of a large domed neoclassical building in stone, seen across an empty forecourt with hills behind."},"why":[{"q":"Why was the 1905 protectorate a colony in all but name?","a":"Japan had taken charge of Korean foreign relations, the American legation closed eleven days later, and the Residency-General built a press-control apparatus through security, press, textbook and publication acts before annexation was ever signed."},{"q":"Why did Japan switch to cultural rule after March 1919?","a":"Unarmed demonstrations spread across the whole country within hours and were put down with a violence foreign witnesses photographed and recorded, so the press restrictions were eased and gendarmes replaced with civil police while the colony's structure stayed untouched."},{"q":"Why did assimilation reach into names and family life from the late 1930s?","a":"The campaign summed up as Japan and Korea as one body was meant to turn Koreans into imperial subjects who would work and fight for the war, which required more of them than obedience to an administration."}]},
{"id":"ww2-084","num":"84","category":"World War II · History","question":"The period known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Japan its first true party cabinet in 1918 and votes for all adult men in 1925, before the army broke it after 1931.","questions":["Party cabinets governing Japan under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cut military spending from 65 to about 30 percent of the budget, which the Imperial Army never forgave.","The assassination of Prime Minister Inukai Tsuyoshi in May 1932 ended <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and no fully party-based cabinet governed Japan again until after the war."],"answer":"Taishō democracy","answerText":"Taishō democracy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1912 – 1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First party cabinet</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manhood suffrage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1925</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 May 1932</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","japan","politics","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Taishō democracy</b> is the name given to the two decades after 1912 in which elected parties, not oligarchs, came closest to governing Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A coalition of parties toppled an oligarchic cabinet for the first time in February 1913, in what is remembered as the Taishō Political Crisis, after the army had brought down its predecessor by withdrawing its minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The First World War then transformed the country economically, and the rice riots of 1918 brought two million people onto the streets and shook the leadership badly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan got its first genuine party cabinet in September 1918 under Hara Takashi, and party cabinets held office almost continuously from 1924 to 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those governments joined the international disarmament movement, entering the League of Nations at its founding in 1919, signing the Kellogg-Briand Pact in 1928, and taking part in the Washington, Geneva and London naval conferences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Universal Manhood Suffrage Law passed the Imperial Diet in March 1925 and dropped the tax qualification that had limited the vote for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first election held under it, in February 1928, returned the two big parties almost level with each other, despite a government campaign of moving prefectural governors and police chiefs about beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same session passed the Peace Preservation Law, so the widening of the franchise arrived alongside a new instrument for policing opinion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Military spending was cut from 65 percent of the budget in the years to 1922 to about 30 percent by 1932, which the army resented deeply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The end came quickly after 1931: the cabinet could not restrain the army in Manchuria, and the murder of Prime Minister Inukai Tsuyoshi in May 1932 closed the era of party government until after the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, \"Taisho Democracy,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha3/index.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10434. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Adoption of Universal Manhood Suffrage Law and Peace Preservation Law,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha3/description13.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"First Election with Universal Manhood Suffrage,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha3/description14.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"5.15 Incident of 1932,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description02.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Burning_of_the_Okayama_Seimai%2C_1918_rice_riots.jpg/1280px-Burning_of_the_Okayama_Seimai%2C_1918_rice_riots.jpg","title":"The rice riots at Okayama, 1918","desc":"A crowd stands in front of the smoking ruin of a rice-milling business during the disturbances that brought two million people onto the streets that summer.","credit":"Asahi Shimbun, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burning_of_the_Okayama_Seimai,_1918_rice_riots.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a large crowd in straw hats before a burnt-out brick building still smoking behind a low wall."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the army resent the party governments so deeply?","a":"Military spending was cut from 65 per cent of the budget in the years to 1922 to about 30 per cent by 1932, and those cabinets had joined the disarmament movement, entering the League and signing the naval treaties."},{"q":"Why did the widening of the vote arrive with a law policing opinion?","a":"The same Diet session that dropped the tax qualification in March 1925 passed the Peace Preservation Law, so the franchise was extended and a new instrument for controlling what the newly enfranchised might say was created together."},{"q":"Why did the era end so quickly after 1931?","a":"The cabinet could not restrain the army in Manchuria, which exposed how little the elected government controlled, and the murder of Prime Minister Inukai in May 1932 closed party government until after the war."}]},
{"id":"ww2-085","num":"85","category":"World War II · History","question":"A cabinet in Tokyo could be brought down by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> simply by refusing to supply a serving officer as minister, a rule reimposed in 1936 and used at once.","questions":["The Meiji Constitution made the emperor commander-in-chief, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered to the throne rather than to the ministers who were supposed to govern it.","Between 1934 and 1936 the feud between the Imperial Way and Control factions of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> produced a purge, a murdered general and an attempted coup."],"answer":"Imperial Japanese Army","answerText":"Imperial Japanese Army","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1873</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Direct rescript</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1882</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Serving-officer ministers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1900, restored 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dissolved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["institution","history","japan","military","politics"],"abstract":"The <b>Imperial Japanese Army</b> was Japan’s land force from 1873 to 1945, and its constitutional position let it make and unmake governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> National conscription created it, and by 1877 it had put down the last samurai resistance to the new state, while drilling young men from every class and region into something like a common sense of nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tokyo gave it an autonomous General Staff in 1878, and the Imperial Rescript to Soldiers and Sailors of 1882 tied it directly to the emperor rather than to ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Meiji Constitution then named the emperor commander-in-chief, and two ordinances of 1900 required that the army and navy ministers be serving officers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That last rule was the lever, because a cabinet cannot be formed without a full complement of ministers, so refusing to supply one could bring any government down or stop it being made at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was used at once, when the war minister resigned in December 1912 over the army’s demand for two more divisions and brought the cabinet down with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule was abolished in 1913, restored in 1936, and used the following January to prevent Ugaki Kazushige from forming a government at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ugaki wrote that it caused him great regret to have an imperial order obstructed by the emperor’s own army, and the report of his complaint was banned from publication.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The army was also divided against itself, and the feud between its Imperial Way and Control factions produced a purge, an assassination and an attempted coup between 1934 and 1936.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Against all this the parties were weakening, having cut military budgets through the 1920s and then lost their claim to govern once they proved unable to restrain the army in Manchuria after 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Stillbirth of the UGAKI Cabinet,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description08.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Army Factional Infighting,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description05.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Governments, Parliaments and Parties (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2014), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10434. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/The_3rd_Infantry_Regiment_of_Imperial_Japanese_Army_departing_from_Aoyama_Station.jpg/1280px-The_3rd_Infantry_Regiment_of_Imperial_Japanese_Army_departing_from_Aoyama_Station.jpg","title":"An infantry battalion leaving Tokyo, 1894","desc":"The first battalion of the 3rd Regiment drawn up beside its troop train at Aoyama station on 26 September 1894, on its way to the war with China.","credit":"Akiyoshi Zentarō, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_3rd_Infantry_Regiment_of_Imperial_Japanese_Army_departing_from_Aoyama_Station.jpg","alt":"Faded photograph of a long column of soldiers with packs standing beside a line of railway carriages on open ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did requiring serving officers as ministers give the army so much power?","a":"A cabinet cannot be formed without a full complement of ministers, so refusing to supply one could bring any government down or stop it being made at all, as happened in December 1912 and again to Ugaki in January 1937."},{"q":"Why did the army answer to the emperor rather than to ministers?","a":"It was given an autonomous General Staff in 1878, the Rescript of 1882 tied it directly to the emperor, and the constitution then named him commander-in-chief, so the chain of command bypassed the cabinet entirely."},{"q":"Why could Ugaki's complaint not be published?","a":"He had written that it caused him great regret to have an imperial order obstructed by the emperor's own army, which named the contradiction at the centre of the constitutional arrangement, so the report of it was banned."}]},
{"id":"ww2-086","num":"86","category":"World War II · History","question":"Officers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> seized central Tokyo on 26 February 1936 and murdered three senior figures, before the emperor himself demanded that the revolt be crushed.","questions":["Araki Sadao and Masaki Jinzaburō were the senior patrons of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which held that corrupt advisers stood between the emperor and his people.","A lieutenant colonel of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> murdered Nagata Tetsuzan in 1935, after the rival Control Faction forced one of its patrons out of his army post."],"answer":"Imperial Way Faction","answerText":"Imperial Way Faction","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Active</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1931 – 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nagata murdered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 August 1935</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rising</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 February 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Purged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","japan","politics","military"],"abstract":"The <b>Imperial Way Faction</b> was the radical current in the Japanese army that wanted a violent purification of the state around the emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gathered around General Araki Sadao and General Masaki Jinzaburō, and it was opposed inside the army by the more technocratic Control Faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its junior officers held that politicians, financiers and court advisers had come between the emperor and his people, and that only direct action would sweep them away and restore his rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In November 1934 several of them, with five military academy cadets, were arrested for plotting a coup and insisted they had been framed by their rivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Masaki was forced out of his post in July 1935, a lieutenant colonel of the faction answered by murdering Nagata Tetsuzan, the leading figure of the Control Faction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same politics were at work in the campaign of 1935 against the theory of the emperor as an organ of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That theory had underpinned constitutional government for a generation, and nationalist groups, much of the army and a party seeking to topple the cabinet joined in denouncing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The government banned the constitutional scholar Minobe Tatsukichi’s books in April 1935 and ordered corrections printed, which showed how far an academic argument had become a political weapon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The faction’s attempt came on 26 February 1936, when its officers seized the government quarter of central Tokyo and killed a former prime minister, the finance minister and an army inspector general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At first events seemed to run their way, but the emperor declared firmly that the revolt must be suppressed and it collapsed within three days, after which the ringleaders and the two thinkers who had shaped them were sentenced to death and the faction was purged out of the army, leaving the military’s voice in politics stronger than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["National Diet Library, \"Army Factional Infighting,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description05.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"The 2.26 Incident of 1936,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description07.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Dispute over the Emperor as an Organ of Government Theory,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description04.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"5.15 Incident of 1932,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description02.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Rebel_troops_in_February_26_Incident.JPG","title":"Rebel officers and men, 26 February 1936","desc":"A lieutenant of the rising instructs his company in the courtyard of the unfinished Diet building on the morning the rebels seized central Tokyo.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rebel_troops_in_February_26_Incident.JPG","alt":"Sepia photograph of an officer in a long greatcoat addressing several dozen soldiers in caps and coats crowded together in the snow."},"why":[{"q":"Why did junior officers think direct action was the remedy?","a":"They held that politicians, financiers and court advisers had come between the emperor and his people, so on their reading the problem was an obstruction to be swept away rather than a policy to be argued about."},{"q":"Why was an academic theory of the emperor attacked in 1935?","a":"The organ theory had underpinned constitutional government for a generation, so nationalist groups, much of the army and a party seeking to topple the cabinet joined in denouncing it, and the government banned Minobe's books."},{"q":"Why did the failed coup leave the military stronger in politics?","a":"The emperor declared firmly that the revolt must be suppressed, and it collapsed in three days. The ringleaders were sentenced to death and the faction purged, which removed the army's internal rival and left one voice speaking for it."}]},
{"id":"ww2-087","num":"87","category":"World War II · History","question":"An explosion on the South Manchuria Railway on 18 September 1931, known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, gave Japan the pretext it wanted for occupying the whole of Manchuria.","questions":["The League commission that examined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> concluded that the damage was too slight to justify military action and that the response was not legitimate self-defence.","Tokyo declared a policy of non-expansion after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and could not enforce it, which destroyed the cabinet and ended conciliatory diplomacy towards China."],"answer":"Mukden Incident","answerText":"Mukden Incident","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 September 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Place</span><span class=\"dt-v\">near Mukden, Manchuria</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lytton Commission, 1932</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","japan","china","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Mukden Incident</b> was the staged explosion of 18 September 1931 that Japan used as its reason for occupying Manchuria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A short stretch of the South Manchuria Railway near Liutiao Lake, on the outskirts of Mukden, now Shenyang, was blown up between ten and half past ten that night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The line belonged to Japan, and Japanese officers blamed Chinese nationalists for the blast, then attacked the barracks north of the city and captured the walled city itself within hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commission the League of Nations sent to investigate found that the damage did not prevent the punctual arrival of the next train and was not itself enough to justify military action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its verdict was that the night’s operations could not be regarded as measures of legitimate self-defence, though it allowed that officers on the spot may have believed they were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The charge has always been that the bomb was laid by officers of the Kwantung Army, the Japanese force stationed along the railway, in order to manufacture the pretext they wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The cabinet in Tokyo announced a policy of non-expansion at once, and the army in Manchuria simply carried on expanding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That failure destroyed the second Wakatsuki cabinet and ended Foreign Minister Shidehara Kijūrō’s attempt at conciliatory diplomacy, after an effort to form a cross-party government of cooperation backfired into political turmoil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also revived an army whose budgets and standing had been shrinking for a decade under party government, and which had been forced to disband four complete infantry divisions in 1925.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The incident is usually taken as the opening of the fifteen years of war that ran from Manchuria in 1931 through the invasion of China to Japan’s defeat in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Japanese_soldiers_near_Mukden%2C_October_1931.jpg/1280px-Japanese_soldiers_near_Mukden%2C_October_1931.jpg","title":"Japanese soldiers at Mukden, October 1931","desc":"Five infantrymen with rifles and steel helmets outside a shuttered shopfront in the city weeks after the railway explosion.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_soldiers_near_Mukden,_October_1931.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of five helmeted soldiers with rifles resting outside a closed Chinese shopfront in bright sunlight."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the League's commission reject the self-defence claim?","a":"It found that the damage did not prevent the punctual arrival of the next train and was not itself enough to justify military action, though it allowed that officers on the spot may have believed they were defending themselves."},{"q":"Why did Tokyo's policy of non-expansion change nothing?","a":"The cabinet announced it at once and the army in Manchuria simply carried on expanding, which destroyed the Wakatsuki cabinet and ended Shidehara's attempt at conciliatory diplomacy by showing the government could not command its own forces."},{"q":"Why did the incident revive the army's standing at home?","a":"Its budgets and position had been shrinking for a decade under party government, to the point of disbanding four infantry divisions in 1925, and a swift conquest restored what a decade of cuts had taken."}]},
{"id":"ww2-088","num":"88","category":"World War II · History","question":"Manchuria was overrun within months in 1931 by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Japanese force stationed along the South Manchuria Railway, which its own government could not restrain.","questions":["About 10,000 soldiers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were posted in the leased territory at the tip of the Liaotung Peninsula, under rights Japan had held since 1905.","Ishiwara Kanji of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> argued that Manchuria was the crucial strategic stronghold of Japan’s destiny, chiefly as a barrier against Soviet expansion."],"answer":"Kwantung Army","answerText":"Kwantung Army","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1919</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Garrison</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 10,000 in 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied Manchuria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1931 – 1932</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","japan","china","military"],"abstract":"The <b>Kwantung Army</b> was the Japanese force stationed in the leased territory at the tip of the Liaotung Peninsula and along the South Manchuria Railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan had held those rights since its victory over Russia in 1905 and kept about 10,000 soldiers there, with a treaty claim to raise the number to 15,000 if it judged that necessary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its officers engineered the explosion of September 1931 and then overran Manchuria within a few months against an untrained Chinese army that offered next to no resistance, before consolidating their hold on a resource-rich region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ishiwara Kanji, one of the officers behind the plan, had argued that spring that Manchuria was the most crucial strategic stronghold for Japan’s national destiny.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He valued it chiefly as a barrier that would hinder Russian expansion eastward, and only secondarily for its farmland and its coal and iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The League’s commission recorded plainly that a large area of indisputably Chinese territory had been seized and occupied by Japanese armed forces without any declaration of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Manchuria was as large as France and Germany together, and its 400,000 foreign residents included about 230,000 Japanese living along the railway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The army installed Japanese advisers over Chinese officials, and in some districts tax collectors had to submit their books daily to the Japanese gendarmerie and obtain its approval before spending public money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cabinet in Tokyo could not stop any of it, and the episode showed how little control the civil government had over its own forces abroad.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same force went on to make policy for itself for another decade, and the habit of soldiers in the field presenting their government with accomplished facts became one of the standing features of Japanese expansion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","Jaeik Ahn, \"Examining the Manchukuo Issue in the Context of the Asia-Pacific War’s Outbreak,\" <i>The Korean Journal of International Studies</i> 23, no. 1 (2025): 99–121, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2025.04.23.1.99. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Guerre_de_Mandchourie_-_compagnie_japonaise_dans_le_plaine_glac%C3%A9e_-_btv1b90486889.jpg/1280px-Guerre_de_Mandchourie_-_compagnie_japonaise_dans_le_plaine_glac%C3%A9e_-_btv1b90486889.jpg","title":"A Japanese company in Manchuria, 1932","desc":"Infantry with a regimental flag drawn up on a frozen plain during the winter campaign that completed the occupation.","credit":"Agence de presse Meurisse, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerre_de_Mandchourie_-_compagnie_japonaise_dans_le_plaine_glac%C3%A9e_-_btv1b90486889.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a long line of soldiers in padded winter coats standing on broken ice, an officer on horseback at the left."},"why":[{"q":"Why was Manchuria valued chiefly as a barrier rather than for its resources?","a":"Ishiwara had argued that spring that it was the most crucial strategic stronghold for Japan's national destiny, valuing it as a barrier to Russian expansion eastward and only secondarily for its farmland, coal and iron."},{"q":"Why could 10,000 troops overrun a region the size of France and Germany?","a":"The Chinese army opposing them was untrained and offered next to no resistance, so the force stationed along the railway took the whole region within a few months and then consolidated its hold."},{"q":"Why did this episode become a standing feature of Japanese expansion?","a":"The cabinet in Tokyo could not stop any of it, so the same force went on making policy for itself for another decade, and soldiers in the field presenting their government with accomplished facts became the pattern."}]},
{"id":"ww2-089","num":"89","category":"World War II · History","question":"The last Qing emperor Puyi was installed as regent of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the state proclaimed in Manchuria in 1932 and controlled in practice by the Japanese army.","questions":["A League commission concluded that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could not have been formed without Japanese troops and officials, and was therefore no genuine independence movement.","Japan was among the very few governments to recognise <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it was still pressing for wider recognition on the eve of the Pacific war."],"answer":"Manchukuo","answerText":"Manchukuo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Declared</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 February 1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Organic law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regent</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Puyi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","history","japan","china","20th century"],"abstract":"<b>Manchukuo</b> was the state proclaimed in Manchuria in March 1932 and run in fact by the Japanese army that had just conquered the territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A declaration of independence was published on 18 February 1932, signed by the governors of four provinces and by Mongol princes, and it claimed that these men had been chosen by the people to fulfil their wish for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commander of the Kwantung Army gave a dinner that same night for the heads of the new state and assured them of his assistance if they needed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An organic law promulgated in March vested all executive power in a regent who could overrule the state's legislative chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regent was Puyi, the last Qing emperor of China, whom the League’s commission interviewed at Changchun during its enquiry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The commission also met the government’s Japanese officials and advisers, and found that most of the delegations it received had been presented to it by the authorities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its finding was that the presence of Japanese troops and the activity of Japanese officials were the two things without which the state could not have been formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It therefore held that the regime had not been called into existence by a genuine and spontaneous independence movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan recognised it in 1932, one of very few governments in the world to do so, and spent the next decade trying to have that recognition accepted more widely, above all by China itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The refusal of other governments to recognise it was still an obstacle in the negotiations with the United States on the eve of the Pacific war, and Japanese diplomats read the American demands as a proposal to undo it altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","Jaeik Ahn, \"Examining the Manchukuo Issue in the Context of the Asia-Pacific War’s Outbreak,\" <i>The Korean Journal of International Studies</i> 23, no. 1 (2025): 99–121, https://doi.org/10.14731/kjis.2025.04.23.1.99. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Emperor_Hirohito_Handshakes_Emperor_Puyi_of_Manchukuo_26_June_1940.png","title":"Puyi received in Tokyo, 26 June 1940","desc":"The regent, by then styled emperor of Manchukuo, is greeted on the platform at Tokyo Station during a state visit meant to display the new state's standing.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Hirohito_Handshakes_Emperor_Puyi_of_Manchukuo_26_June_1940.png","alt":"Black and white photograph of two uniformed men shaking hands on a railway platform, watched by officials in coats and a naval officer in white."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the commission reject the claim of spontaneous independence?","a":"It found that the presence of Japanese troops and the activity of Japanese officials were the two things without which the state could not have been formed, and that most of the delegations it received had been presented to it by the authorities."},{"q":"Why did an organic law give the regent power over the legislature?","a":"Vesting all executive power in a regent who could overrule the legislative chamber meant one office decided everything, and that office was held by Puyi, whom the army had installed and could direct."},{"q":"Why did recognition of it still matter a decade later?","a":"Japan was one of very few governments to recognise it and spent ten years trying to have that recognition accepted more widely. The refusal of others was still an obstacle in the 1941 negotiations with the United States."}]},
{"id":"ww2-090","num":"90","category":"World War II · History","question":"Signed on 1 October 1932, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held that Japan’s attack in Manchuria had not been self-defence and that the new state there was no spontaneous creation.","questions":["Rather than restoring the position of 1931, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposed an autonomous Manchuria under Chinese sovereignty with Japanese rights recognised by new treaties.","The Japanese delegation walked out of the League Assembly when it adopted the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in February 1933, and Japan gave notice of withdrawal a month later."],"answer":"Lytton Report","answerText":"Lytton Report","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commission appointed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1931</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Manchuria</span><span class=\"dt-v\">spring 1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 October 1932</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted by Assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">February 1933</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","japan","china","diplomacy"],"abstract":"The <b>Lytton Report</b> was the League of Nations commission’s account of the Manchurian crisis, signed on 1 October 1932.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League had asked it, in December 1931, to examine the issues between China and Japan, including their causes and their state at the time of the enquiry, and to suggest a way forward that could reconcile the interests of both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commission spent about six weeks in Manchuria, interviewing the Kwantung Army’s commander, Japanese consular officials and the regent of the new state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It found that the railway explosion of September 1931 had not justified the attack, and that the operations that followed were not legitimate self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It found equally that the new state owed its existence to Japanese troops and officials rather than to any spontaneous movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It did not simply order a return to the position before September 1931, which it thought impossible, and it said so plainly rather than pretending the previous arrangement could be restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead it proposed an autonomous administration for the three provinces under Chinese sovereignty, with Japanese rights recognised and new treaties to settle the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ten principles asked that any solution suit both countries, respect Soviet interests, and conform to the Covenant, the Kellogg-Briand Pact and the Nine-Power Treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> China and Japan were both League members, and the report insisted that each was entitled to the same consideration, on the ground that a solution benefiting only one of them would be no gain for peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the Assembly adopted the report in February 1933 the Japanese delegation walked out, and Japan gave notice of withdrawal from the League the following month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","Thomas W. Burkman, \"League of Nations and Japan,\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2021), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.11571. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Lytton_Commission_in_Shanghai.jpg/1280px-Lytton_Commission_in_Shanghai.jpg","title":"The League commission arrives in Shanghai, 1932","desc":"The members of the commission of enquiry photographed on arrival beneath a banner naming them as representatives of the League of Nations.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lytton_Commission_in_Shanghai.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of about fifteen men in overcoats and hats standing in a row at night beneath a painted banner."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the report refuse to order a return to the position before 1931?","a":"It thought restoring the previous arrangement impossible and said so plainly rather than pretending otherwise, proposing instead an autonomous administration for the three provinces under Chinese sovereignty with Japanese rights recognised."},{"q":"Why did it insist both countries were entitled to equal consideration?","a":"Both were League members, and the commission held that a solution benefiting only one of them would be no gain for peace, so its principles asked that any settlement suit both and respect Soviet interests too."},{"q":"Why did adopting it end Japan's membership of the League?","a":"The report found the attack was not legitimate self-defence and the new state not a genuine independence movement, so when the Assembly adopted it in February 1933 the delegation walked out and notice of withdrawal followed the next month."}]},
{"id":"ww2-091","num":"91","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed the League Assembly’s adoption of the Lytton Report in February 1933, when the delegation walked out and never came back.","questions":["Under Article 1 of the Covenant the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took two years to complete, since a member could leave only after giving that much notice.","Tokyo justified the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by claiming a special position in China and a responsibility for order in East Asia that it would not share."],"answer":"Japanese withdrawal from the League of Nations","answerText":"Japanese withdrawal from the League of Nations","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Assembly vote</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 February 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Notice given</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 1933</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Effective</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 1935</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","japan","diplomacy","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Japanese withdrawal from the League of Nations</b> was announced in February 1933 and completed two years later, and it was the first time a great power walked out of the organisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The League had sent a commission of enquiry to Manchuria, and its report of October 1932 found that the Japanese attack of the previous September had not been legitimate self-defence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also held that the new state of Manchukuo owed its existence to Japanese troops and officials rather than to any movement of the local people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Assembly adopted the report on 24 February 1933 the Japanese delegation walked out of the chamber and never came back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cabinet decided on 17 March to give notice, did so ten days later, and under Article 1 of the Covenant a member could leave only after two years’ notice, so the withdrawal took effect in March 1935.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Japanese government explained itself by saying that Japan had a special position in China and that its views need not agree with those of other nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It would keep peace and order in East Asia on its own responsibility, and would oppose any attempt by China to seek foreign support in resisting it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The League had no way to enforce its finding, and its members had no appetite for economic sanctions in the middle of a worldwide depression.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both countries had also signed the Kellogg-Briand Pact of 1928 renouncing war as an instrument of policy, and appeals based on it proved equally ineffective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The departure left Japan free of the one body that had judged it, and its diplomacy after 1933 was increasingly conducted outside the framework the last war had built, in growing isolation from the other great powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","League of Nations Commission of Enquiry, <i>The Report of the Lytton Commission of Enquiry</i> (Geneva, 1932), https://archive.org/details/service-gdc-gdcwdl-wd-l_-11-60-1-wdl_11601-wdl_11601. [Open access]","\"The Covenant of the League of Nations,\" 1919, The Avalon Project, Yale Law School, https://avalon.law.yale.edu/20th_century/leagcov.asp. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Manchurian Incident of 1931,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description01.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Kellogg-Briand Pact, 1928,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/kellogg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Plenipotentiary_Matsuoka_Yosuke_circa_1933.jpg","title":"Matsuoka Yōsuke, chief delegate at Geneva","desc":"Matsuoka led the Japanese delegation that walked out of the League Assembly in February 1933, and was later foreign minister.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plenipotentiary_Matsuoka_Yosuke_circa_1933.jpg","alt":"Black and white portrait of a moustached man in a dark three-piece suit seated with his hands on his knees."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the withdrawal take two years to complete?","a":"Under Article 1 of the Covenant a member could leave only after two years' notice. The cabinet decided on 17 March 1933 and gave notice ten days later, so the departure took effect in March 1935."},{"q":"Why did the League's finding change nothing on the ground?","a":"It had no way to enforce the finding, and its members had no appetite for economic sanctions in the middle of a worldwide depression, so a judgement was all the organisation could produce."},{"q":"Why did Japan claim its views need not agree with other nations'?","a":"The government explained that Japan had a special position in China and would keep peace and order in East Asia on its own responsibility, opposing any Chinese attempt to seek foreign support in resisting it."}]},
{"id":"ww2-092","num":"92","category":"World War II · History","question":"During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1936, about 22 officers and 1,400 men held central Tokyo for three days and murdered three senior figures of the government.","questions":["The emperor’s own insistence that the revolt be crushed ended the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the purge that followed left the army’s rival faction in command.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the work of officers who wanted a government whose policies would suit the army’s plans for expansion on the Asian continent."],"answer":"February 26 Incident","answerText":"February 26 Incident","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rising</span><span class=\"dt-v\">26 February 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Suppressed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 February 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cabinet fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 March 1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","japan","politics","military"],"abstract":"The <b>February 26 Incident</b> was the attempted coup of 1936 in which young Japanese army officers seized central Tokyo and murdered members of the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some 22 officers and 1,400 men revolted in the early hours of 26 February and terrorised the capital for three and a half days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They took the prime minister’s residence, the Diet building, the war and home ministries, the police headquarters and the General Staff building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They killed the finance minister, the Lord Keeper of the Privy Seal and an army inspector general, and tried to kill the prime minister and the grand chamberlain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rebels belonged to the army’s radical Imperial Way current, which held that corrupt ministers and court advisers stood between the emperor and his people, and they wanted a government whose policies would suit the army’s plans for the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> For a time the rising seemed to be succeeding, and senior officers sympathetic to it drafted a statement urging the men back to their units rather than condemning them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The emperor then declared plainly that the revolt must be suppressed, and it collapsed on 29 February.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The punishment was severe: the ringleaders were sentenced to death, along with the two writers held to have shaped them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The purge that followed removed the Imperial Way faction from the army and left its rivals in charge of a service that now frightened every politician in Tokyo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It came a year after the campaign against the theory of the emperor as an organ of the state, which had already destroyed the constitutional argument for party government, and no party cabinet governed Japan again before the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"The 2.26 Incident of 1936,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description07.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Army Factional Infighting,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description05.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Dispute over the Emperor as an Organ of Government Theory,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description04.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/February_26_Incident%2C_in_Police_Department%2C_01.jpg/1280px-February_26_Incident%2C_in_Police_Department%2C_01.jpg","title":"Rebel troops in the snow, 27 February 1936","desc":"Soldiers of the rising crowd the courtyard of the Metropolitan Police headquarters in Tokyo, their rifles stacked in the snow.","credit":"Kōyō Ishikawa, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:February_26_Incident,_in_Police_Department,_01.jpg","alt":"Aerial black and white photograph of hundreds of soldiers in greatcoats standing about a snow-covered courtyard among stacked rifles."},"why":[{"q":"Why did senior officers draft a statement urging the men back rather than condemning them?","a":"For a time the rising seemed to be succeeding and some of them were sympathetic to it, so the army's first instinct was to bring the rebels in quietly rather than to treat them as enemies of the state."},{"q":"Why did the emperor's declaration end it in three days?","a":"The rebels' whole claim was that they acted for an emperor kept from his people by corrupt ministers, so his plain statement that the revolt must be suppressed removed the only authority they had appealed to."},{"q":"Why did suppressing a coup leave the army more powerful?","a":"The purge removed the Imperial Way faction and left its rivals in charge of a service that now frightened every politician in Tokyo, and no party cabinet governed Japan again before the war."}]},
{"id":"ww2-099","num":"99","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1937 allied China’s Nationalist government with the Communists against Japan, ending a civil war the two had fought for a decade.","questions":["Chiang Kai-shek’s detention at Xi’an in December 1936 set the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in motion, and his release produced an outburst of nationalism across China.","A manifesto from Shaanxi announced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in September 1937, abolishing the soviet governments and putting the Red Army under the national command."],"answer":"Second United Front","answerText":"Second United Front","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Xi’an detention</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12 – 25 December 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Manifesto</span><span class=\"dt-v\">September 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1941</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","china","politics","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Second United Front</b> was the alliance of 1937 between the Chinese Nationalist government and the Chinese Communist Party against the Japanese invasion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two sides had fought a civil war for a decade, and that division was one of the reasons foreign governments doubted whether a country so split against itself could be helped at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The turn came in December 1936, when Chiang Kai-shek was detained at Xi’an by one of his own commanders from the 12th to the 25th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His release on Christmas Day set off what the American ambassador in China described as a spontaneous outburst of nationalism across the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Negotiations between the national government and the Communists in the north-west were already being reported by American diplomats in the spring of 1937, months before the fighting began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The Communist commander Zhu De was telling foreign audiences by then that a proposal of a national front against Japan had brought China’s twenty-year civil war to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The formal announcement came in September 1937, in a manifesto that the Communist central committee issued from Shaanxi and that the American consul general at Hankou reported home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It declared support for Sun Yat-sen’s three principles, cancelled the movements to overthrow Nationalist political power including the forcible confiscation of land, and abolished the existing soviet governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Red Army was reorganised as part of the National Revolutionary Army under the government’s military command and awaited orders for the front.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had spent years trying to detach northern China from the central government with anti-communist slogans, and its invasion produced exactly the unity those slogans had been meant to prevent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["\"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, 24 September 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d609. [Open access]","\"The Ambassador in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of State, 12 January 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1936, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1936v04/d460. [Open access]","\"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, 2 June 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d85. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/Chiang_Kai-shek_after_Xi%27an_incident.jpg","title":"Chiang Kai-shek returns to Nanjing, December 1936","desc":"Chiang and Soong Mei-ling are met at the airfield after his release at Xi’an, the crisis that opened the way to the united front.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiang_Kai-shek_after_Xi%27an_incident.jpg","alt":"Grainy black and white photograph of a crowd of officials and officers in overcoats greeting a couple at an airfield."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the two sides ally after a decade of civil war?","a":"Chiang's detention at Xi'an in December 1936 and his release on Christmas Day set off what the American ambassador described as a spontaneous outburst of nationalism across the whole country, and negotiations were being reported by the spring of 1937."},{"q":"Why did the Communists cancel their own programme in the manifesto?","a":"They declared support for Sun Yat-sen's three principles, abandoned the movements to overthrow Nationalist power including forcible confiscation of land, and abolished the soviet governments, which was the price of the Red Army serving under the government's command."},{"q":"Why did Japan's own policy help produce the alliance?","a":"It had spent years trying to detach northern China from the central government with anti-communist slogans, and its invasion produced exactly the unity between Nationalists and Communists those slogans had been meant to prevent."}]},
{"id":"ww2-093","num":"93","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of August 1936 committed Japan to securing the Asian continent and advancing into the South Seas, and to arming against both Russia and the West.","questions":["A postwar tribunal called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the cornerstone of the whole edifice of Japanese preparation for war, though it began in the service ministries.","The Conference of Five Ministers approved the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on 7 August 1936, six months after a rising by young officers had silenced the political parties."],"answer":"Basis of National Policy","answerText":"Basis of National Policy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Drafted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 June 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Approved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 August 1936</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed statement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11 August 1936</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["text","history","japan","politics","diplomacy"],"abstract":"The <b>Basis of National Policy</b> was the decision of August 1936 in which the Japanese cabinet committed the country to expansion in two directions at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began in the service ministries rather than in the cabinet as a whole, when the war and navy ministers agreed a draft between themselves on 30 June 1936 and laid it before their three colleagues the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their text said Japan should realise the spirit of the imperial way by a consistent policy of overseas expansion and correct the aggressive policies of the great powers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Conference of Five Ministers approved the plan on 7 August and put its decisions into a signed statement four days later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The agreed policy was to secure a firm position on the Asian continent while advancing into and developing the South Seas, and to complete Japan’s armament so that it could do both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That meant preparing against the Soviet Union in the north and against Britain and the United States in the south, which is a programme for two wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The judgment of the postwar tribunal called it the cornerstone of the whole edifice of Japanese preparation for war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came six months after the February rising had frightened the political parties into silence and made the army’s wishes very difficult to refuse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same cabinet restored the rule that the war and navy ministers must be officers on the active list, which handed the army a veto over the formation of any government at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Within a year Japan was at war in China, which absorbed the resources the northern half of the plan required, and the southern half was pursued from 1940 onwards instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Stillbirth of the UGAKI Cabinet,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description08.html. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"The 2.26 Incident of 1936,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/description07.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/K%C5%8Dki_Hirota_Cabinet_19360309.jpg","title":"The Hirota cabinet, March 1936","desc":"Prime Minister Hirota Kōki and his ministers, the government whose Conference of Five Ministers approved the policy five months later.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C5%8Dki_Hirota_Cabinet_19360309.jpg","alt":"Black and white group photograph of about fifteen men in formal dress and uniform on a staircase, with Japanese calligraphy overwritten across it."},"why":[{"q":"Why is committing to both directions a programme for two wars?","a":"Securing a firm position on the Asian continent meant preparing against the Soviet Union in the north, while advancing into the South Seas meant preparing against Britain and the United States, so the plan named two sets of enemies at once."},{"q":"Why did the cabinet find the army's wishes hard to refuse in 1936?","a":"The decision came six months after the February rising had frightened the political parties into silence, and the same cabinet restored the rule that the service ministers be serving officers, which handed the army a veto over forming any government."},{"q":"Why was only the southern half of the plan pursued after 1937?","a":"Within a year Japan was at war in China, which absorbed the resources the northern half required, so the advance southward was taken up from 1940 while the preparation against the Soviet Union went unfunded."}]},
{"id":"ww2-094","num":"94","category":"World War II · History","question":"Japan announced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1940, promising to drive the Western empires out of Asia while securing for itself the raw materials it had been importing from them.","questions":["Planners fixed the ultimate reach of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in July 1940 as everything between eastern India and Burma on one side and Australia and New Zealand on the other.","The territories taken under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were governed for Japan’s benefit rather than freed, which is why the phrase is remembered bitterly across the region."],"answer":"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere","answerText":"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phrase adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Planning meeting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">16 July 1940</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","japan","imperialism","diplomacy"],"abstract":"The <b>Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</b> was the name Japan gave from 1940 to the bloc of Asian territories it meant to take from the Western empires and lead itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It replaced the earlier talk of a new order in East Asia, and the change of phrase followed the German victories in Europe in the summer of 1940.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Britain, France and the Netherlands in eclipse, Japanese leaders decided to seize their possessions in East and Southeast Asia and the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Army, navy and foreign ministry representatives agreed on 16 July 1940 that the ultimate goal ran from eastern India and Burma to Australia and New Zealand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The immediate objective was narrower and still enormous, covering Hong Kong, French Indochina, Thailand, Malaya and the Netherlands East Indies with their oil, rubber and tin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The announced purpose was to drive the Western imperial powers out of Asia, and that promise was made to peoples who had lived under those powers for generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The actual purpose was to secure the raw materials Japan was importing from the West, so that it would no longer depend on suppliers who could cut it off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States was the main source of the oil, steel and iron the Japanese military used, and it was tightening its restrictions through 1940 and 1941.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The order it set out to overturn had been built at Washington in 1921, where the powers agreed naval limits and a treaty guaranteeing the integrity of China, on the older American principle of equal commercial access rather than territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The territories taken under it after 1941 were governed for Japan’s benefit rather than freed, which is why the slogan is remembered bitterly across the region, though the empires it attacked never regained their old authority either.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Secretary of State John Hay and the Open Door in China, 1899–1900,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1899-1913/hay-and-china. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Japanese_Empire_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg/1280px-Japanese_Empire_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg.png","title":"The Japanese empire at its greatest extent","desc":"A globe showing the territory Japan held or occupied in 1942, the reach the Co-Prosperity Sphere had been announced to justify.","credit":"Emok, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Empire_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg","alt":"Orthographic globe centred on East Asia with Japan in dark green and a large swathe of Southeast Asia and the western Pacific in lighter green."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the phrase change in the summer of 1940?","a":"The German victories in Europe left Britain, France and the Netherlands in eclipse, so Japanese leaders decided to seize their possessions in East and Southeast Asia and needed a name for a bloc rather than for a new order in East Asia."},{"q":"Why did the announced purpose differ from the actual one?","a":"The promise to drive the Western powers out of Asia was made to peoples who had lived under them for generations, while the aim was to secure the raw materials Japan was importing from suppliers who could cut it off."},{"q":"Why is the slogan remembered bitterly across the region?","a":"The territories taken after 1941 were governed for Japan's benefit rather than freed, so the promise of liberation was answered with another occupation, though the empires it attacked never regained their old authority either."}]},
{"id":"ww2-095","num":"95","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 7 July 1937 began when Japanese troops on night exercise near Beijing demanded to search a walled town for a missing soldier.","questions":["Within a day of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> large Kwantung Army units were converging from Manchuria, which a postwar tribunal read as proof that the war was chosen.","American opinion swung firmly behind China after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, having been divided while the country was split between Nationalists and Communists."],"answer":"Marco Polo Bridge Incident","answerText":"Marco Polo Bridge Incident","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 July 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Place</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lukouchiao, near Beijing</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Truce proposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 July 1937</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","japan","china","20th century"],"abstract":"The <b>Marco Polo Bridge Incident</b> was the clash of 7 July 1937 outside Beijing that grew within weeks into a full war between Japan and China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japanese garrison troops held an unusual night exercise at Lukouchiao, an old stone crossing on the road south out of the city, and then reported that one of their soldiers was missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They demanded to enter the walled town of Wanping to search for him, and the fighting began while their complaint was still being negotiated between the two sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese issued an ultimatum for the town’s surrender on the following afternoon, took substantial casualties in the battle that followed, and proposed a truce themselves on 10 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The incident might have been regarded as closed at that point, and the postwar tribunal found plainly that this was not what the Japanese army intended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Within a day of the first shots, large units of the Kwantung Army were converging on the scene from Manchuria, and reinforcements followed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese officials told an American diplomat in Nanjing the next morning that the clash was clearly premeditated, and their foreign ministry had already protested to the Japanese embassy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The tribunal read the way a local skirmish was allowed to become a full offensive as evidence that the Army General Staff had chosen war with China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Japan had been working to detach the five provinces of northern China from the central government for two years, building up local self-government there and using anti-communist slogans to justify it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The date is the usual starting point for the war in Asia, and the government in Tokyo was no better able to restrain its army in 1937 than it had been in 1931.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","\"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, 9 July 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d107. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Thomas_Child_-_Marco_Polo_Bridge_%28Lugou_Qiao%29%2C_Peking%2C_1877_NA01-75.jpg","title":"The Marco Polo Bridge, photographed in 1877","desc":"The stone bridge at Lugouqiao south of Beijing, 60 years before the night exercise that turned into a war.","credit":"Thomas Child, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Child_-_Marco_Polo_Bridge_%28Lugou_Qiao%29,_Peking,_1877_NA01-75.jpg","alt":"Sepia photograph of a long many-arched stone bridge with a balustrade of carved posts, crossing a wide shallow riverbed."},"why":[{"q":"Why did the tribunal find the army had chosen war rather than stumbled into it?","a":"The Japanese proposed a truce themselves on 10 July and the incident might have been closed there, but within a day of the first shots large Kwantung Army units were converging from Manchuria with reinforcements behind them."},{"q":"Why did Chinese officials call the clash premeditated at once?","a":"Japan had been working for two years to detach the five northern provinces from the central government, building up local self-government and using anti-communist slogans, so a night exercise outside Beijing fitted a pattern already visible."},{"q":"Why does the incident echo the Manchurian one of 1931?","a":"The government in Tokyo was no better able to restrain its army in 1937 than it had been in 1931, so a local action by officers in the field again decided national policy."}]},
{"id":"ww2-096","num":"96","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began at the Marco Polo Bridge in July 1937 and lasted until 1945, and neither side declared war on the other for years.","questions":["Japan held the cities and railways but never the countryside during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a campaign meant to be short became a drain on everything it had.","The Nationalists and Communists were forced into an alliance by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Communist party announced in September 1937."],"answer":"Second Sino-Japanese War","answerText":"Second Sino-Japanese War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 July 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nanjing fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 December 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1945</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","japan","china","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Second Sino-Japanese War</b> ran from July 1937 to Japan’s surrender in 1945 and was the largest war fought in Asia in the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of a clash at the Marco Polo Bridge, which Chinese officials told American diplomats at once had been premeditated, and out of six years of Japanese encroachment in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Japanese forces took Beijing in July 1937, fought a three-month battle for Shanghai from August, and reached the Chinese capital at Nanjing in December.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chinese government refused the peace terms passed to it through the German ambassador in November and December 1937, and moved its capital inland rather than surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither side declared war on the other, which is why it was called an incident for years and why the neutrality laws of other countries never came into force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The war forced the Nationalists and the Communists into an alliance, and the Communist party announced in September 1937 that it would cooperate with the whole nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Japan held the cities and the railways and could not hold the countryside, and the campaign it expected to finish quickly became a drain on everything it had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The United States was still the main supplier of the oil, steel and iron the Japanese military needed, and it began restricting their sale as the war dragged on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That pressure was one of the reasons Japan turned south in 1940 and 1941 towards the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia, while the same years drew the United States into the quarrel through credits to China and then Lend-Lease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war merged into the wider conflict after the attack on Pearl Harbor in December 1941, and Chinese resistance held down a large part of the Japanese army until the surrender in 1945.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","\"The Consul General at Hankow (Josselyn) to the Secretary of State, 24 September 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d609. [Open access]","\"The Counselor of Embassy in China (Peck) to the Secretary of State, 9 July 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d107. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Japanese_Occupation_of_China_1940.svg/1280px-Japanese_Occupation_of_China_1940.svg.png","title":"Japanese-occupied China in 1940","desc":"The area under Japanese control three years into the war, running along the coast and the railways and leaving the interior to the Chinese government.","credit":"Emilfaro, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Occupation_of_China_1940.svg","alt":"Map of China with the eastern provinces and Manchuria shaded pink for Japanese control and the western interior left pale."},"why":[{"q":"Why did neither side declare war on the other?","a":"It was called an incident for years, which kept the neutrality laws of other countries from coming into force and so left China able to buy arms and Japan able to buy American oil and steel."},{"q":"Why did a war Japan expected to finish quickly become a drain?","a":"It held the cities and the railways and could not hold the countryside, and the Chinese government moved its capital inland rather than accept the peace terms passed through the German ambassador in late 1937."},{"q":"Why did the war in China push Japan southward?","a":"The United States was the main supplier of the oil, steel and iron its military needed and began restricting their sale as the fighting dragged on, which made the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia the alternative."}]},
{"id":"ww2-097","num":"97","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the autumn of 1937 lasted about three months and turned a clash in northern China into a war the whole world was watching.","questions":["Chiang Kai-shek committed his best divisions to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than yield the city, in a deliberate choice to fight where foreigners could see it.","The retreating Chinese armies fell back up the Yangtze after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Japanese forces followed them to the capital within weeks."],"answer":"Battle of Shanghai","answerText":"Battle of Shanghai","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chinese line broke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Followed by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the fall of Nanjing</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","japan","china","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Battle of Shanghai</b> was the three-month fight for China’s largest city in the autumn of 1937, and it turned a northern incident into a national war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fighting followed the killing of a Japanese naval officer named Oyama at a city airfield, which gave Japan the reason it wanted for landing troops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shanghai was where the foreign powers had their concessions and their newspapers, and the American consul general had warned a week beforehand that a clash there was a real danger and that the foreign concessions would not be spared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chiang Kai-shek committed his best divisions rather than yield the city, which was a deliberate choice to fight where foreigners could see it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chinese held on for about three months against naval gunfire from the river and attack from the air before their line finally gave way in November.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> China appealed to the League of Nations while the battle was still going on, and the powers met at Brussels that November under the treaty they had signed in 1921 to guarantee its integrity, without agreeing to do anything effective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain and the United States each offered their good offices and were refused, and the Japanese army preferred that Germany should be asked to act as go-between instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cost to China was enormous, since the divisions lost at Shanghai were the trained and German-equipped core of an army it could not quickly replace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The retreating Chinese armies fell back up the Yangtze towards the capital at Nanjing, and the Japanese army followed them there within a matter of weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the battle bought was time and the attention of the world, and the length of the Chinese resistance that autumn is the reason foreign opinion turned as it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","\"The Consul General at Shanghai (Gauss) to the Secretary of State, 7 August 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v03/d377. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Washington Naval Conference, 1921–1922,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/naval-conference. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Japanese_Special_Naval_Landing_Forces_in_Battle_of_Shanghai_1937.jpg","title":"Japanese naval landing troops at Shanghai, 1937","desc":"Special Naval Landing Forces in gas masks behind a machine gun in the rubble of the city during the autumn battle.","credit":"Ministry of the Navy, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Special_Naval_Landing_Forces_in_Battle_of_Shanghai_1937.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of a dozen helmeted soldiers in gas masks crouched behind a machine gun among broken bricks."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Chiang commit his best divisions to a city he might lose?","a":"Shanghai was where the foreign powers had their concessions and their newspapers, so fighting there was a deliberate choice to resist where foreigners could see it rather than to hold ground he could defend more cheaply."},{"q":"Why was the cost of the battle so hard for China to bear?","a":"The divisions lost at Shanghai were the trained and German-equipped core of an army it could not quickly replace, so a three-month defence spent the best troops China had at the start of an eight-year war."},{"q":"Why did the powers meeting at Brussels achieve nothing?","a":"They met under the 1921 treaty guaranteeing Chinese integrity but agreed on nothing effective, Britain and the United States each had their good offices refused, and the Japanese army preferred Germany as go-between."}]},
{"id":"ww2-098","num":"98","category":"World War II · History","question":"A postwar tribunal put the death toll of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at over 200,000 civilians and prisoners in the first six weeks after the city fell in December 1937.","questions":["Burial societies counted more than 155,000 bodies after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, most of them with their hands tied behind their backs.","Foreigners who stayed through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> organised a safety zone for refugees, and the missionaries among them kept letters, diaries and photographs."],"answer":"Nanjing Massacre","answerText":"Nanjing Massacre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">City fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 December 1937</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Worst period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">first six weeks</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tribunal estimate</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 200,000 killed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","japan","china","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Nanjing Massacre</b> was the mass killing, rape and looting carried out by Japanese forces in the Chinese capital after it fell on 13 December 1937.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Japanese demand for surrender was ignored and the city was bombarded, and an American naval officer on the river reported four days before it fell that the gates were shut, fires were burning on both banks and the bombing had been intensive all day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The postwar tribunal found that prisoners of war were killed in groups by machine-gun fire along the bank of the Yangtze within 72 hours of surrendering, more than 30,000 of them, without even a pretence of trial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its estimate was that over 200,000 civilians and prisoners were murdered in the city and its surroundings in the first six weeks of the occupation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burial societies and other organisations counted more than 155,000 bodies, most of them with their hands tied behind their backs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The tribunal put the number of rapes in the first month at approximately 20,000, and found that women and girls of every age were attacked throughout the city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soldiers stopped unarmed civilians in the road and shot them, looted shops and warehouses and then burned them, and roughly a third of the city was destroyed by fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Foreigners who stayed in the city organised a safety zone for refugees, and a Japanese embassy official warned them that the army meant to make it bad for Nanjing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An international committee ran a safety zone that was still feeding about 250,000 refugees in January, when the Japanese army stopped it selling rice and flour of its own accord.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The American missionaries who stayed kept letters, diaries, reports and photographs that are now an archive at Yale, and opinion in the United States hardened as the news came out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","Yale Divinity Library, \"Nanking Massacre Project,\" Yale University Library, https://web.library.yale.edu/divinity/nanking. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]","\"The Commander of the United States Yangtze Patrol (Marquart) to the Chief of Naval Operations, 9 December 1937\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1937, The Far East, Volume IV</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1937v04/d485. [Open access]","\"The Third Secretary of Embassy in China (Allison) to the Secretary of State, 22 January 1938\", in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, Diplomatic Papers, 1938, The Far East, Volume III</i>, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1938v03/d54. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Minnie_Vautrin_helped_the_refugees_in_1937.jpg/1280px-Minnie_Vautrin_helped_the_refugees_in_1937.jpg","title":"Refugees in Nanjing, 1937","desc":"A column of women and children moving through the city under a flag, among those sheltered by the foreign-run safety zone.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minnie_Vautrin_helped_the_refugees_in_1937.jpg","alt":"Sepia photograph of a long file of women in winter coats walking down a narrow street behind a white flag."},"why":[{"q":"Why is the scale of the killing documented as precisely as it is?","a":"Burial societies and other organisations counted more than 155,000 bodies, most with their hands tied behind their backs, and the postwar tribunal put the total at over 200,000 civilians and prisoners in the first six weeks."},{"q":"Why did foreigners in the city organise a safety zone?","a":"An international committee ran it for refugees and was still feeding about 250,000 of them in January, when the Japanese army stopped it selling rice and flour of its own accord."},{"q":"Why did American opinion harden as a result?","a":"The missionaries who stayed kept letters, diaries, reports and photographs, now an archive at Yale, so the news came out with a documentary record behind it rather than as a distant rumour."}]},
{"id":"ww2-100","num":"100","category":"World War II · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were fought without declarations between 1932 and 1939 along the frontiers of Manchukuo, Mongolia and the Soviet Far East.","questions":["The largest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began at Nomonhan in May 1939 and ended that September with the defeat of the Japanese forces engaged.","A postwar tribunal could not establish whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were ordered from Tokyo or begun by the army in the field on its own account."],"answer":"Soviet-Japanese border conflicts","answerText":"Soviet-Japanese border conflicts","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1932 – 1939</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lake Khasan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">July – August 1938</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nomonhan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May – September 1939</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","japan","russia","warfare"],"abstract":"The <b>Soviet-Japanese border conflicts</b> were the undeclared battles fought along the frontiers of Manchukuo, Mongolia and the Soviet Far East between 1932 and 1939.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Russia had been the army’s first strategic enemy in the national defence plan of 1907, displaced by the United States only in the revision of 1923, and planners kept up their preparations for a Soviet war once Manchuria gave the two countries a common border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In July 1938 Japanese guards were reinforced near Lake Khasan and a diplomat was sent to Moscow to demand that Soviet troops withdraw from a hill of strategic value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war minister and the chief of the General Staff tried to get the emperor’s approval for an attack, misrepresenting the situation to him to obtain it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From January 1939 Japanese armed detachments carried out raids across the Outer Mongolian border on several occasions, as part of a plan to bring that country within Japan’s sphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The largest action began at Nomonhan in May 1939, when Kwantung Army units attacked Soviet forces on the Manchukuoan frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew into a campaign of considerable magnitude and ended in September 1939 with the defeat of the Japanese forces that had been engaged in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tribunal that examined it later could find no evidence of whether the attack was ordered from Tokyo or begun by the army in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That question runs through the whole period, since an army that had staged its own pretext for war in 1931 was very hard for any government in Tokyo to restrain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The defeat weighed heavily in the argument that followed about whether Japan should strike north against the Soviet Union or south towards the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["International Military Tribunal for the Far East, <i>Judgment of 4 November 1948</i> (Tokyo, 1948), https://www.legal-tools.org/doc/8bef6f/pdf/. [Open access]","National Diet Library, \"Crisis in Constitutional Politics,\" <i>Modern Japan in Archives</i>, https://www.ndl.go.jp/modern/e/cha4/index.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"The Mukden Incident of 1931 and the Stimson Doctrine,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1921-1936/mukden-incident. [Open access]","Frederick R. Dickinson, \"Civilian and Military Power (Japan),\" in <i>1914-1918-online: International Encyclopedia of the First World War</i>, ed. Ute Daniel et al. (Berlin: Freie Universität Berlin, 2016), https://doi.org/10.15463/ie1418.10979. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, \"Japan, China, the United States and the Road to Pearl Harbor, 1937–41,\" <i>Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations</i>, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1937-1945/pearl-harbor. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8c/Khalkhin_Gol_Soviet_tanks_1939.jpg","title":"Soviet tanks at Khalkhin Gol, 1939","desc":"Camouflaged Soviet armour on the Mongolian steppe during the summer campaign that ended in the defeat of the Japanese force.","credit":"public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khalkhin_Gol_Soviet_tanks_1939.jpg","alt":"Black and white photograph of five light tanks draped with cut brush standing in long grass on open rolling ground."},"why":[{"q":"Why did Japan keep preparing for a war with the Soviet Union?","a":"Russia had been the army's first strategic enemy in the defence plan of 1907, displaced by the United States only in 1923, and taking Manchuria gave the two countries a common border to plan across."},{"q":"Why could the tribunal not say who ordered the Nomonhan attack?","a":"It found no evidence of whether the attack was ordered from Tokyo or begun by the army in the field, which is the question running through the whole period for a force that had staged its own pretext in 1931."},{"q":"Why did the defeat matter to Japan's later choices?","a":"It weighed heavily in the argument about whether to strike north against the Soviet Union or south towards the oil and rubber of Southeast Asia, and the northern option lost."}]},
{"id":"ko-001","num":1,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Roughly 78 million people live in the two states that divide <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between them, both admitted to the United Nations on the same day in September 1991.","questions":["The armistice that stopped the fighting in 1953 was never replaced by a peace treaty, so the two states dividing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> remain formally at war today.","A people sharing one language and one script had been governed as a single country for centuries when the Americans and the Soviets split <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the 38th parallel in 1945."],"answer":"Korea","answerText":"Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Divided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1945, at the 38th parallel</span><span class=\"dt-k\">War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1950 – 1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Armistice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953, no peace treaty</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Both in UN</span><span class=\"dt-v\">17 September 1991</span></div>","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","country","korea","geography","history","politics","asia"],"abstract":"The word <b>Korea</b> names a peninsula in north-east Asia, the people who have long lived on it, and the two states that today divide it between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together those states hold about 221,000 square kilometres (85,000 square miles) and roughly 78 million people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Republic of Korea, whose capital is Seoul, has the southern 100,401 square kilometres and some 51.7 million inhabitants; the Democratic People's Republic of Korea, whose capital is Pyongyang, has the northern 120,538 and some 26.6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The division is recent, and nothing about the land or the language explains it. In 1945 the United States and the Soviet Union agreed to divide the peninsula temporarily at the 38th parallel so that each could take the surrender of Japanese forces in its half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two governments formed instead of one, the Soviet Union supporting Kim Il-sung in the north and the United States backing Syngman Rhee in the south, and Washington recognised the Republic of Korea, with its capital at Seoul, as an independent state on 1 January 1949.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Kim's army attacked the south in June 1950 and came close to taking the whole peninsula, the People's Republic of China entered the war late that year, and the fighting ended in 1953 in a truce rather than a settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No peace treaty has ever replaced that armistice, so the two states remain formally at war along a fortified line near where they began. Both were nevertheless admitted to the United Nations on the same day, 17 September 1991, under a resolution the General Assembly adopted without a vote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Korea had entered modern diplomacy late and briefly: its first treaty with a Western power was signed in 1882, and in 1905 the American legation in Seoul closed when Japan assumed direction of Korean foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, accessed 6 September 2026, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic People's Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, accessed 6 September 2026, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kp.html. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, “The Korean War, 1950–1953,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Korea,” https://history.state.gov/countries/korea. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Admission of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea and the Republic of Korea to Membership in the United Nations,” resolution A/RES/46/1, adopted 17 September 1991, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/133631. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Korean_Peninsula_at_night_20240124_132602_GMT_Iss-070e080670.jpg/1280px-Korean_Peninsula_at_night_20240124_132602_GMT_Iss-070e080670.jpg","title":"The peninsula after dark","desc":"Photographed from the International Space Station in January 2024. South Korea is a spread of city lights around the bright cluster of Seoul; North Korea, across the middle of the frame, is almost unlit.","credit":"NASA, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_Peninsula_at_night_20240124_132602_GMT_Iss-070e080670.jpg","alt":"A night view from orbit: the lower half of a peninsula covered in scattered points of light, the upper half almost entirely dark."}},
{"id":"ko-002","num":2,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Only its northern edge touches the mainland, so nearly the whole boundary of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is coastline, with a shallow shelf sea on one side and a deep one on the other.","questions":["Forest covers about 64 per cent of the land in the south of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and about half of it in the north, which leaves very little room for crops.","A study of Koreanic speech found the whole <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unusually uniform, with what divisions there are lying along mountain ranges rather than along the old provincial boundaries."],"answer":"Korean Peninsula","answerText":"Korean Peninsula","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["place","korea","geography","nature","asia","climate"],"abstract":"The <b>Korean Peninsula</b> hangs south from the north-east Asian mainland between two very unlike seas, and the states on it hold about 221,000 square kilometres (85,000 square miles) between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the west is the Yellow Sea, a semi-enclosed shelf sea of roughly 400,000 square kilometres whose mean depth is only 44 metres (144 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the east is the East Sea, called the Sea of Japan outside Korea, which is deep and reaches the Pacific only through three straits less than 150 metres (490 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The peninsula's one land frontier is in the north, and every overland route between Korea and the Chinese states ran through the region beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A chain of mountains runs almost its whole length, carries about half of the peaks Koreans regard as sacred, and is treated in the country as its backbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Mountain and forest take up most of the surface: woodland covers about 64 per cent of the land in the south and about 50 per cent in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Cropland is correspondingly scarce, at roughly 14 per cent of the southern land and 19 per cent of the northern, and four fifths of South Koreans now live in towns and cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shelf on the western side is so flat and shallow that even small changes in sea level shift the coast a long way, and through the ice ages the Yellow Sea swung between open water and near-shore deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For its length the peninsula is remarkably uniform in speech, and a study of Koreanic varieties found a high degree of homogeneity across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The divisions that study did find follow the land rather than the old provinces, with barriers along the Sobaek and Taebaek ranges and across the strait to Jeju.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, accessed 6 September 2026, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]","Xuefa Shi, Zhengquan Yao, Jianxing Liu, Shuqing Qiao, Yanguang Liu, Xiaoyan Li, Xisheng Fang and Chaoxin Li, “Dominant Role of Sea Level on the Sedimentary Environmental Evolution in the Bohai and Yellow Seas Over the Last 1 Million Years,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 9 (2021): 638221, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2021.638221. [Open access]","Minkyoung Kim, Jeomshik Hwang, Guebuem Kim, Taehee Na, Tae-Hoon Kim and Jung-Ho Hyun, “Carbon Cycling in the East Sea (Japan Sea): A Review,” <i>Frontiers in Marine Science</i> 9 (2022): 938935, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2022.938935. [Open access]","Peter Yun, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Manchuria and Korea in East Asian History,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 1 (2016): 1–8, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-21-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Mi Yoon Chung, Sungwon Son, Gang Uk Suh, Sonia Herrando-Moraira, Cheul Ho Lee, Jordi López-Pujol and Myong Gi Chung, “The Korean Baekdudaegan Mountains: A Glacial Refugium and a Biodiversity Hotspot That Needs to Be Conserved,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 9 (2018): 489, https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2018.00489. [Open access]","World Bank, “Forest Area (% of Land Area) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/AG.LND.FRST.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]","World Bank, “Arable Land (% of Land Area) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/AG.LND.ARBL.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]","Sean Lee, “A Sketch of Language History in the Korean Peninsula,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0128448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0128448. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Korea_2003-05-01_NASA_MODIS_Terra_250m.jpg/1280px-Korea_2003-05-01_NASA_MODIS_Terra_250m.jpg","title":"The Korean Peninsula from orbit","desc":"A cloud-free satellite view taken on 1 May 2003. The mountainous spine along the eastern side and the wide western lowlands are both plain, as is the sediment-laden shallow water of the Yellow Sea on the left.","credit":"Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Rapid Response Team, NASA/GSFC, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea_2003-05-01_NASA_MODIS_Terra_250m.jpg","alt":"A true-colour satellite image of a green, mountainous peninsula between a pale, silty sea on the west and a deep blue sea on the east."}},
{"id":"ko-003","num":3,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Because the crest of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs close to Korea's east coast, the ground falls steeply on that side and slopes long and gently away to the west.","questions":["Korea's heaviest recorded day of rain, 870.5 millimetres at Gangneung in August 2002, fell on the narrow eastern shore below the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the slope is steepest.","A study of Korean speech found one of the peninsula's three main linguistic divides running along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which have separated the east coast from the rest for a long time."],"answer":"Taebaek Mountains","answerText":"Taebaek Mountains","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","korea","geography","geology","nature","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Taebaek Mountains</b> run down the eastern side of the Korean Peninsula, close to the coast and roughly parallel with it, and they set the shape of the whole country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ground drops sharply from the crest to a narrow eastern shore and slopes long and gently away to the west, a lopsidedness geologists describe as a westward tilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That tilt began in the middle Miocene, when the East Sea started to widen and this strongly asymmetric, high-in-the-east range was rapidly pushed up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lifting has not stopped: a shore platform at sea level on the west coast and marine terraces 18 to 45 metres (59 to 148 feet) above the sea on the east coast were formed at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The range is the main divide of Korean drainage, and because the crest lies so far east it is the westward and southward rivers that are long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its best known summit is Seoraksan, whose main peak Daecheongbong stands at 1,708 metres (5,604 feet) above a massif of some 398 square kilometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The steepness of the eastern flank makes the coast below it one of the wettest places in the country, and Korea's heaviest recorded day of rain, 870.5 millimetres (34.3 inches), fell at Gangneung at its foot on 31 August 2002.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mountains are also a refuge for wild things, and the southern part of the chain they belong to may hold about a third of the flora of the whole peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They have divided people too: a study of Korean speech found one of the peninsula's three principal linguistic barriers running along this range.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Countrywide the result of so much high ground is little farmland and a great deal of woodland, with cropland at about 14 per cent of the land in the South and 19 in the North against forest at 64 and 50 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Cho-Hee Lee, Yeong Bae Seong, John Weber, Sangmin Ha, Dong-Eun Kim and Byung Yong Yu, “Topographic Metrics for Unveiling Fault Segmentation and Tectono-Geomorphic Evolution with Insights into the Impact of Inherited Topography, Ulsan Fault Zone, South Korea,” <i>Earth Surface Dynamics</i> 12, no. 5 (2024): 1091–1120, https://doi.org/10.5194/esurf-12-1091-2024. [Open access]","Mi Yoon Chung, Jordi López-Pujol and Myong Gi Chung, “Is the Baekdudaegan ‘the Southern Appalachians of the East’? A Comparison between These Mountain Systems, Focusing on Their Role as Glacial Refugia,” <i>Korean Journal of Plant Taxonomy</i> 46, no. 4 (2016): 337–47, https://doi.org/10.11110/kjpt.2016.46.4.337. [Open access]","Mi-Hyun Lee, Ju Hyeon Song, Seong Yeob Byeon, Jeong Eun Lee, Ho Jin Kim, Seung-Beom Chae, Chung Weon Yun and Ji-Dong Kim, “The Species Range-Size Patterns for Vascular Plants of Seorak Mountain (Korea),” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 11, no. 18 (2021): 12872–81, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8462172/. [Open access]","Korea Meteorological Administration, “Climate of Korea,” archived 17 January 2024, https://web.archive.org/web/20240117203253/https://www.kma.go.kr/eng/biz/climate_01.jsp. [Open access]","Mi Yoon Chung, Sungwon Son, Gang Uk Suh, Sonia Herrando-Moraira, Cheul Ho Lee, Jordi López-Pujol and Myong Gi Chung, “The Korean Baekdudaegan Mountains: A Glacial Refugium and a Biodiversity Hotspot That Needs to Be Conserved,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 9 (2018): 489, https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2018.00489. [Open access]","Sean Lee, “A Sketch of Language History in the Korean Peninsula,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0128448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0128448. [Open access]","World Bank, “Arable Land (% of Land Area) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/AG.LND.ARBL.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]","World Bank, “Forest Area (% of Land Area) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/AG.LND.FRST.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Seoraksan_National_Park_panorama_3.jpg/1280px-Seoraksan_National_Park_panorama_3.jpg","title":"Granite peaks in Seoraksan","desc":"Seoraksan lies in the southern part of the Taebaek range, near the east coast. Its main peak, Daecheongbong, rises to 1,708 metres (5,604 feet).","credit":"Photograph by kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seoraksan_National_Park_panorama_3.jpg","alt":"Bare grey granite pinnacles rising steeply above a slope of dark pine forest under a blue sky."}},
{"id":"ko-004","num":4,"category":"The land and its people","question":"A walker can follow the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Mount Baekdu in the far north to Mount Jiri in the south without ever crossing a river, because the ridge is the peninsula's watershed.","questions":["The eighteenth-century Korean scheme of mountains treats the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as one great trunk from which a second trunk and thirteen lesser veins branch away.","Ice-age forests survived along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose South Korean section alone may hold about a third of the flowering plants of the whole peninsula."],"answer":"Baekdudaegan","answerText":"Baekdudaegan","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","korea","geography","concept","nature","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Baekdudaegan</b> is the mountain spine of the Korean Peninsula, understood in Korea as one continuous ridge running from Mount Baekdu on the northern border to Mount Jiri at the southern end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates of its length differ with the line taken, from about 1,400 kilometres (870 miles) across some 487 named mountains and hills to about 1,625 kilometres (1,010 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What makes it one thing rather than a list of ranges is that it is a watershed, so the ridgeline can be walked from end to end without crossing a river or a stream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korean geographical writing accordingly treats mountains and rivers as a single system, the ridges being the lines that water does not cross.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The eighteenth-century <i>Sangyeongpyo</i>, a table of the country's mountains compiled by the scholar Sin Gyeongjun, sorts them into fifteen ranges — one great trunk, one main trunk and thirteen veins — and keeps Korean names for peaks that were later renamed under Japanese rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its northern anchor, Mount Baekdu, is a volcano of 2,744 metres (9,003 feet) with a crater lake at the top, and the Yalu, the Tumen and the Sungari all rise on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the geomancy that shaped medieval Korean politics this chain was an arterial system and Baekdu the source of the forces running through it, which is why capitals and tombs were sited by reading the ridges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ridge also carries about half the peaks Koreans hold sacred, whatever their religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is a biological refuge as well: forests survived the last glacial maximum along it, and its South Korean section alone may hold some 1,500 plant species, about a third of the flora of the peninsula, roughly a hundred of them growing nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> South Korea passed an act to protect it in 2003 and designated 260,000 hectares as a protected area in 2005, covering only its own share of a ridge that runs on across the demilitarised zone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Mi Yoon Chung, Jordi López-Pujol and Myong Gi Chung, “Is the Baekdudaegan ‘the Southern Appalachians of the East’? A Comparison between These Mountain Systems, Focusing on Their Role as Glacial Refugia,” <i>Korean Journal of Plant Taxonomy</i> 46, no. 4 (2016): 337–47, https://doi.org/10.11110/kjpt.2016.46.4.337. [Open access]","Peter Hayes, “Building on Baekdudaegan: Peacemaking through Ecological Restoration,” <i>Global Asia</i> 14, no. 4 (2019): 90–95, https://nautilus.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/Hayes-Baekdudegan-Ecological-Security-Korea-Global-Asia-Dec31-2019.pdf. [Open access]","Natalia Chesnokova, “Shin Gyeongjun (1712–1781) and His The Description of Mountains (Sangyeongpyo): The First Korean Proto-Scientific Classification of the Mountains of the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Vostok (Oriens)</i>, no. 4 (2019): 177–90, https://doi.org/10.31857/S086919080005948-9. [Open access]","Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Mi Yoon Chung, Sungwon Son, Gang Uk Suh, Sonia Herrando-Moraira, Cheul Ho Lee, Jordi López-Pujol and Myong Gi Chung, “The Korean Baekdudaegan Mountains: A Glacial Refugium and a Biodiversity Hotspot That Needs to Be Conserved,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 9 (2018): 489, https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2018.00489. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Paektu-san.jpg/1280px-Paektu-san.jpg","title":"The crater of Mount Baekdu","desc":"The volcano at the northern end of the Baekdudaegan, on the frontier with China, photographed from the air. The crater holds the lake Cheonji, and the Amnok, Duman and Sungari rivers all rise on the massif.","credit":"NASA, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paektu-san.jpg","alt":"An aerial view of a snow-streaked volcanic crater with a frozen lake in its floor, ringed by ridges falling away in every direction."}},
{"id":"ko-005","num":5,"category":"The land and its people","question":"More than half of Korea's rain falls in the month or so of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when a stationary front settles across the peninsula and then drifts slowly northwards.","questions":["Winter in Korea is dry, with under a tenth of the year's precipitation, because the wet weather comes with the summer <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the typhoons that follow it.","The date on which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> withdraws has slipped about ten days later since 2000, from an average of 14 July to an average of 24 July."],"answer":"Changma","answerText":"Changma","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","korea","climate","geography","science","nature"],"abstract":"<b>Changma</b> is the Korean name for the summer rainy season, the phase of the East Asian monsoon in which a quasi-stationary front lies across the peninsula and rain falls day after day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the same weather system that China calls Meiyu and Japan calls Baiu, but it arrives roughly ten days later because Korea lies further north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It usually begins in the south of the country in late June, works its way northwards, and lasts about thirty days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than half of Korea's yearly precipitation falls in that period, against less than a tenth of it in the whole of winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Annual totals run from about 1,000 to 1,800 millimetres (39 to 71 inches) in the south of the country and 1,100 to 1,400 (43 to 55) in the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rainy season is not one continuous spell: the Changma proper is followed by a break in early August and then by a second wet phase, whose heavy rain is less regular because typhoons can reshape it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two or three of the roughly 28 typhoons that form each year in the north-west Pacific reach Korea, between June and October.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest of the year is governed by the opposite wind: north-westerlies off the continent bring dry, cold air, and the cold spells behind them depend on the strengthening of the Siberian High.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The timing of the Changma is not fixed, and its withdrawal has moved about ten days later since 2000, from a mean of 14 July over 1985 to 1999 to a mean of 24 July over 2000 to 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korea can measure such shifts unusually far back, because daily rainfall was recorded in Seoul with a standard copper gauge from 1778 to 1907, the longest daily precipitation record anywhere before the industrial age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Korea Meteorological Administration, “Climate of Korea,” archived 17 January 2024, https://web.archive.org/web/20240117203253/https://www.kma.go.kr/eng/biz/climate_01.jsp. [Open access]","Jae-Won Choi, Hae-Dong Kim and Bin Wang, “Interdecadal Variation of Changma (Korean Summer Monsoon Rainy Season) Retreat Date in Korea,” <i>International Journal of Climatology</i> 40, no. 3 (2020): 1348–60, http://www.soest.hawaii.edu/MET/Faculty/bwang/bw/paper/2019pdf/wang405.pdf. [Open access]","Uju Shin, Sang-Hun Park, Yong-Ro Yun and Chimook Oh, “Synoptic Features of August Heavy Rainfall Episodes Accompanied by a Quasi-Stationary Front over the Korean Peninsula and Its Relationship with the Western Pacific Subtropical High,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 10 (2022): 940785, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2022.940785. [Open access]","Anupam Kumar, “A New Approach to Cold Surge Classification in East Asia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 23659, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-02873-0. [Open access]","Jae-Won Lee, Ho-Jeong Shin and Jinkyu Hong, “Precipitation Data in Seoul, Korea during 1778–1907,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 566, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03335-8. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/%EC%B8%A1%EC%9A%B0%EA%B8%B0_%281%29.JPG/1280px-%EC%B8%A1%EC%9A%B0%EA%B8%B0_%281%29.JPG","title":"A cheugugi rain gauge","desc":"A standard Korean rain gauge of the type used from the 15th century, standing on its stone pedestal. Daily rainfall was measured with gauges of this design in Seoul from 1778 to 1907.","credit":"Photograph by Jocelyndurrey, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%B8%A1%EC%9A%B0%EA%B8%B0_%281%29.JPG","alt":"A plain dark cylindrical vessel, taller than it is wide, standing on a square stone slab."}},
{"id":"ko-006","num":6,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Korea's only land frontier faces <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so every overland route between the peninsula and the Chinese states ran through it, and so did most of the armies.","questions":["Gojoseon, Buyeo, Goguryeo and Balhae all held ground in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as well as on the peninsula, which is why its early history is claimed in more than one national account.","China avoids the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for its own north-eastern provinces, because the word is bound up with the Japanese empire that ruled the region in the 1930s."],"answer":"Manchuria","answerText":"Manchuria","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","korea","geography","history","asia","territory"],"abstract":"<b>Manchuria</b> is the region beyond Korea's northern frontier, and before the modern age it was the main route connecting the states of the Korean Peninsula with those of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is used almost everywhere except in China itself, where the region is called Dongbei, the north-east, or the three north-eastern provinces; that avoidance follows from the word's association with Japanese imperialism and the puppet state of Manchukuo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In premodern sources it was usually called Liaodong, the land east of the Liao River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its peoples were not Chinese in language or culture — the Khitans and the Jurchens among them — and empires built there more than once conquered part or all of China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several states counted as Korean held ground on both sides of the frontier, Gojoseon, Buyeo, Goguryeo and Balhae among them, and Goguryeo ruled much of the peninsula and much of the region for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Whoever held the region held an advantage, so Korean courts were repeatedly forced to take sides in wars fought over it, often not by their own choice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goryeo pressed a claim to Liaodong, sending troops across the Amnok in 1356 and again in 1370, and a later expedition ended when its commander Yi Seonggye turned the army round at that river, marched back to the capital and deposed the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korean maps of their own country carried the general lay of the region across the top, and Mount Baekdu on the frontier, where the Amnok, the Duman and the Sungari all rise, was the point from which Korean geography was reckoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The corridor carried continental power in the other direction too: Korea's first treaty with a Western state, in 1882, was negotiated with Chinese help, China having long had influence over Korean foreign relations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chinese forces crossed the Yalu into the Korean War late in 1950 by the same road, and the ancient states of the region, Goguryeo above all, remain the subject of historical dispute between Korea and China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Kishik Noh, “Recent Research Trends on Jurchen-Manchu Studies in Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 1 (2016): 249–58, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-21-1-249.pdf. [Open access]","Peter Yun, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Manchuria and Korea in East Asian History,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 1 (2016): 1–10, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-21-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, “A Guide to the United States' History of Recognition, Diplomatic, and Consular Relations, by Country, since 1776: Korea,” https://history.state.gov/countries/korea. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, U.S. Department of State, “The Korean War, 1950–1953,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/korean-war. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/%EC%95%95%EB%A1%9D%EA%B0%95.jpg/1280px-%EC%95%95%EB%A1%9D%EA%B0%95.jpg","title":"The Amnok river in winter","desc":"The Amnok, known in Chinese as the Yalu, runs along most of Korea's land frontier. Beyond it lies the region called Manchuria in most of the world and Dongbei in China.","credit":"Photograph by Ffggss, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%95%95%EB%A1%9D%EA%B0%95.jpg","alt":"A wide frozen river under a pale sky, with low snow-covered hills on the far bank and dry reeds in the foreground."}},
{"id":"ko-007","num":7,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Cupped between China and Korea and only 44 metres deep on average, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is shallow enough that small changes in sea level move its coastline a long way.","questions":["West of Korea lies the shallow <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, while east of it the sea plunges away and reaches the Pacific only through three straits less than 150 metres deep.","Semi-diurnal tides dominate the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the mean range over parts of it is four to six metres and the largest measured rise and fall is over nine."],"answer":"Yellow Sea","answerText":"Yellow Sea","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","korea","geography","nature","science","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Yellow Sea</b> is the shallow, semi-enclosed sea between the Chinese mainland and the Korean Peninsula, covering roughly 400,000 square kilometres (154,000 square miles) at a mean depth of only 44 metres (144 feet).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the western of the two seas Korea faces, and the difference between them governs the country's two coasts. Its floor is a broad, flat continental shelf, so even a small change in sea level shifts the shoreline a long way, and across the glacial cycles the basin has swung between open water and near-shore deposits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its tides are semi-diurnal and unusually large: the mean range over parts of it is four to six metres (13 to 20 feet), and the greatest range yet measured is more than nine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So great a range over so flat a shelf means broad tidal flats and a coastline that changes with the hour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> East of Korea is a different ocean altogether: the East Sea, called the Sea of Japan outside Korea, is deep and almost closed, joined to the Pacific by three straits less than 150 metres (490 feet) deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Warm water enters it from the south through the Korea Strait, also called the Tsushima Strait, as the Tsushima Warm Current.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Estimates of that inflow vary widely with the method and the period, from 0.7 to 4.6 sverdrups, with a four-year mean over 1999 to 2002 of 2.4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How much water passes the strait is still being measured decades on, because it sets how much heat and salt reach the northern basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Sea level itself travels round the peninsula: fluctuations slower than the tides run along the coast at speeds set by the layering of the water and the shape of the shelf, which are quite different on the two sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Xuefa Shi, Zhengquan Yao, Jianxing Liu, Shuqing Qiao, Yanguang Liu, Xiaoyan Li, Xisheng Fang and Chaoxin Li, “Dominant Role of Sea Level on the Sedimentary Environmental Evolution in the Bohai and Yellow Seas Over the Last 1 Million Years,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 9 (2021): 638221, https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2021.638221. [Open access]","Qian Zhang, Min Su, Peng Yao, Yongping Chen, Marcel J. F. Stive and Zheng B. Wang, “Dynamics of a Tidal Current System in a Marginal Sea: A Case Study of the Yellow Sea, China,” <i>Frontiers in Marine Science</i> 7 (2020): 596388, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2020.596388. [Open access]","Minkyoung Kim, Jeomshik Hwang, Guebuem Kim, Taehee Na, Tae-Hoon Kim and Jung-Ho Hyun, “Carbon Cycling in the East Sea (Japan Sea): A Review,” <i>Frontiers in Marine Science</i> 9 (2022): 938935, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2022.938935. [Open access]","Jae-Hong Moon, Hyunjun Jang, Taekyun Kim, Hyeonsoo Cha and Naoki Hirose, “Non-Linear Long-Term Trend in Volume Transport through the Korea/Tsushima Strait,” <i>Frontiers in Marine Science</i> 10 (2023): 1250452, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2023.1250452. [Open access]","KyungJae Lee, SungHyun Nam and Jae-Hyoung Park, “Alongshore Propagation of Subtidal Sea Level Fluctuations around the Korean Peninsula over Varying Stratification and Shelf Topography,” <i>Frontiers in Marine Science</i> 8 (2022): 802752, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmars.2021.802752. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/%EA%B3%A0%EC%B0%BD%EA%B0%AF%EB%B2%8C.jpg/1280px-%EA%B3%A0%EC%B0%BD%EA%B0%AF%EB%B2%8C.jpg","title":"Tidal flats at Gochang","desc":"The Yellow Sea coast at Gochang, on the south-west of the peninsula, at low water. A tidal range of several metres over a very flat shelf uncovers mudflats on this scale twice a day.","credit":"Photograph by Seungh, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EA%B3%A0%EC%B0%BD%EA%B0%AF%EB%B2%8C.jpg","alt":"A vast expanse of wet mud reaching to a low horizon at sunset, with lines of stakes running into the distance."}},
{"id":"ko-008","num":8,"category":"The land and its people","question":"North Korea's largest mineral endowment is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a magnesium carbonate burned into the brick that lines steel furnaces, and one Tancheon mine alone reports 600 million tonnes of it.","questions":["One South Korean study puts North Korea's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at some six billion tonnes, second in the world, while the United States Geological Survey says such figures cannot be verified.","The mineral that best marks the difference between the two Koreas is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since mining is 11.7 per cent of the northern economy and 0.2 per cent of the southern."],"answer":"magnesite","answerText":"magnesite","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","korea","geology","industry","geography","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Magnesite</b> is a magnesium carbonate mined to be burned into magnesia, the material that lines steel furnaces and kilns, and it is the mineral North Korea is said to have most of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its deposits lie around Tancheon in South Hamgyong Province, where the Ryongyang Mine, the country's largest, reports a reserve base of 600 million tonnes at 45.8 per cent magnesium oxide and the nearby Daeheung Youth Hero Mine another 600 million at 46.8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One South Korean study puts the national total at about six billion tonnes and calls it the second largest in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those numbers cannot be checked from outside: the United States Geological Survey says North Korean reserves are unverifiable, both because the government treats the data as confidential and because the country classifies reserves without asking whether they can be worked at a profit, and its own table of world magnesite reserves lists no figure for the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Output is small beside the claims, an estimated 380,000 tonnes in 2017, because the mines want power, machinery and investment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the peninsula has is split unevenly, and so is the industry built on it. In 2017 mining was 11.7 per cent of North Korea's nominal economy of $32.2 billion, while mining and quarrying in South Korea came to 0.2 per cent of a much larger one, three-quarters of it by value limestone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The South is a buyer of ores rather than a digger of them, and was the world's third-ranked importer of metallic ores, slag and ash that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The North's other holdings look large on paper too — coal, iron, graphite, tungsten, gold — but its biggest iron mine at Musan was working at a fifth to a third of capacity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sales ran almost entirely one way: over 98 per cent of North Korean mineral exports went to China in 2017, four fifths of them by value anthracite, and sanctions cut coal shipments there from 22.5 million tonnes in 2016 to 4.8 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jaewon Chung, “The Mineral Industry of North Korea,” in <i>2017–2018 Minerals Yearbook</i> (Reston, VA: U.S. Geological Survey, 2023), 14.1–14.5, https://pubs.usgs.gov/myb/vol3/2017-18/myb3-2017-18-north-korea.pdf. [Open access]","U.S. Geological Survey, “Magnesium Compounds,” in <i>Mineral Commodity Summaries 2025</i> (Reston, VA: U.S. Geological Survey, 2025), 112–13, https://pubs.usgs.gov/periodicals/mcs2025/mcs2025-magnesium-compounds.pdf. [Open access]","Kyung-soo Choi, “The Mining Industry of North Korea,” <i>Korean Journal of Defense Analysis</i> 23, no. 2 (2011): 211–30, https://www.kida.re.kr/data/kjda/05_Choi%20Kyung-soo.pdf. [Open access]","Jaewon Chung, “The Mineral Industry of the Republic of Korea,” in <i>2017–2018 Minerals Yearbook</i> (Reston, VA: U.S. Geological Survey, 2022), 16.1–16.6, https://pubs.usgs.gov/myb/vol3/2017-18/myb3-2017-18-korea-south.pdf. [Open access]","Edward Yoon, “Recent Activities in the DPRK Minerals Sector,” NAPSNet Special Reports (Berkeley, CA: Nautilus Institute for Security and Sustainability, 3 April 2019), https://nautilus.org/napsnet/napsnet-special-reports/recent-activities-in-the-dprk-minerals-sector/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Magnesite.jpg/1280px-Magnesite.jpg","title":"A magnesite crystal","desc":"Magnesite is magnesium carbonate. It is mined chiefly to be burned into magnesia, the refractory material used to line steel furnaces and kilns.","credit":"Photograph by Didier Descouens, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magnesite.jpg","alt":"A blocky, translucent white crystal with flat faces and stepped edges, photographed against a black ground."}},
{"id":"ko-009","num":9,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Joseon Korea was administered as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each under a governor, and the names of those units still label the country's regions and its dialects.","questions":["The oldest surviving Korean map of Korea, drawn in 1402 for a world map made at court, was a map of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an arrangement Korean atlases kept for centuries.","Mountain lines rather than rivers were treated as the natural divisions between the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because Korean geography read boundaries off the ridges that water does not cross."],"answer":"Eight Provinces","answerText":"Eight Provinces","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","korea","geography","history","province","state"],"abstract":"The <b>Eight Provinces</b> — <i>paldo</i> in Korean — were the standing administrative divisions of Joseon Korea, and their names are still used for the country's regions and dialect areas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement was in place by the start of the 15th century: the oldest surviving Korean map of Korea, drawn in 1402 for a world map made at the new dynasty's court, was called the <i>P'altodo</i>, the Map of the Eight Provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It rested on a reorganisation of counties and prefectures carried through under King Taejong, replacing the ranked and uneven units inherited from Goryeo with one system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each province had a governor, the <i>gwanchalsa</i>, who with the local magistrates held judicial as well as administrative power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The court knew these units in detail: a survey of 1424 and 1425 asked all 334 districts for their borders, populations, distances, terrain and economy, and the answers went into a geographical monograph presented to the throne in 1432.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The eight stayed the frame of Korean geography for the rest of the dynasty, and an American educator in Seoul could still write in 1906 of examination candidates coming to the capital from the eight provinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A project of 1453 set out to map the country as a whole and every province and its 330-odd districts, and the eight provincial maps bound into the sixteenth-century national gazetteer are the earliest that survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Later map albums opened with a general map and then took the provinces one by one, with tables of postal and military stations and figures for population and yearly rice production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their boundaries were read off the land rather than drawn across it, Korean geographical writing treating mountain lines as the natural divisions between one part of the country and the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The names have outlasted the offices, though a study of Korean speech found the real divisions in the language following the Sobaek and Taebaek ranges and the Jeju Strait rather than the old provincial lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sean Lee, “A Sketch of Language History in the Korean Peninsula,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0128448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0128448. [Open access]","Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Jong-rok Oh, “The Characteristics of the Ruling Structure during Early Chosŏn,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 9 (2005): 1–34, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/9_01.pdf. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The Passing of Korea</i> (New York, 1906), 337–38, https://archive.org/details/passingofkorea00hulb. [Open access]","Natalia Chesnokova, “Shin Gyeongjun (1712–1781) and His The Description of Mountains (Sangyeongpyo): The First Korean Proto-Scientific Classification of the Mountains of the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Vostok (Oriens)</i>, no. 4 (2019): 177–90, https://doi.org/10.31857/S086919080005948-9. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/8_Provinces_of_Korea.png/1280px-8_Provinces_of_Korea.png","title":"The eight provinces mapped","desc":"The provinces of Joseon Korea shown on a modern map. Their names — Hamgyong, Pyongan, Hwanghae, Gangwon, Gyeonggi, Chungcheong, Gyeongsang and Jeolla — are still used for the peninsula's regions.","credit":"Map by Anhydride2020, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:8_Provinces_of_Korea.png","alt":"A coloured map of the Korean peninsula divided into eight labelled provinces, four in the north and four in the south."}},
{"id":"ko-010","num":10,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Nearly ten million people live in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself, the capital a new dynasty chose in the 1390s after surveyors had mapped the shapes and forces of its hills.","questions":["Rain was measured daily at the royal palace in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 1778 to 1907, which gives the city the longest daily rainfall record anywhere before the industrial age.","Joseon's founders sited <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by reading the hills around it, since geomancy was held indispensable to choosing where a Korean capital should stand."],"answer":"Seoul","answerText":"Seoul","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["city","korea","capital","geography","history","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Seoul</b> is the capital of the Republic of Korea, and under the name Hanyang it was the capital of the Joseon dynasty from the 1390s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Choosing the site was a geomantic exercise: geomancy was thought indispensable to the selection of a capital, and the records of 1393 mention shapes-and-forces maps of Hanyang and of the ground its principal buildings were to stand on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those maps do not survive, but they were most likely terrain charts of the mountains and streams, with compass readings to fix the orientation of the main features.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The place was not new to importance: it had been one of Goryeo's branch capitals, along with Pyongyang and Gyeongju.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Joseon the city had a magistracy of its own, the Hanseongbu, which sat among the central government's judicial bodies rather than among the provincial ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The city has kept an unusual record of itself: daily rainfall was measured at the royal palace with a standard copper gauge from 1778 to 1907, the longest daily precipitation record anywhere before the industrial age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That gauge, the cheugugi, stood 300 millimetres (12 inches) high and 140 (5.5) across, and the design goes back to 1441.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It drew the country's ambition as well: an American educator living there in 1906 described examination candidates gathered on the grounds behind Gyeongbok Palace, sitting in groups under enormous umbrellas and writing furiously at their essays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was the point from which the country was measured, too, the sixteenth-century national gazetteer giving 1,186 <i>li</i> from Seoul to Uiju on the Yalu and 2,101 to Onsong in the far north-east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Today about 9.96 million people live in the city proper, in a country four fifths of whose population lives in towns and cities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Jong-rok Oh, “The Characteristics of the Ruling Structure during Early Chosŏn,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 9 (2005): 1–34, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/9_01.pdf. [Open access]","Jae-Won Lee, Ho-Jeong Shin and Jinkyu Hong, “Precipitation Data in Seoul, Korea during 1778–1907,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 566, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03335-8. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The Passing of Korea</i> (New York, 1906), 337–38, https://archive.org/details/passingofkorea00hulb. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, accessed 6 September 2026, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]","World Bank, “Urban Population (% of Total Population) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.URB.TOTL.IN.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Seoul_Gyeongbokgung_Blue_House_Bukhansan.jpg/1280px-Seoul_Gyeongbokgung_Blue_House_Bukhansan.jpg","title":"Gyeongbokgung under the northern hills","desc":"The main palace of Joseon Seoul, with the granite ridges behind it. The site was chosen in the 1390s by reading the shapes and forces of these hills.","credit":"Photograph by rabble, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seoul_Gyeongbokgung_Blue_House_Bukhansan.jpg","alt":"A palace of tiled roofs in an open compound, with a wooded granite mountain rising directly behind it."}},
{"id":"ko-011","num":11,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Korea's oldest city stands on the Taedong in the west of the peninsula, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been the seat of the northern government since 1948.","questions":["From the fifth century to the seventh <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was Goguryeo's capital, and schemes to move the Goryeo court there later disturbed that dynasty's peace more than once.","About 3.2 million people live in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a city that dominated the north-west under Joseon through its trade with China and one of the kingdom's strongest provincial governorships."],"answer":"Pyongyang","answerText":"Pyongyang","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["city","korea","capital","geography","history","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Pyongyang</b> stands on the Taedong River in the west of the peninsula's northern half and is by a long way Korea's oldest city, with roots reaching back at least to the last centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the capital of Goguryeo from the 5th century to the 7th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the unification Silla's frontier ran from Pyongyang in the west across to the coast just north of modern Wonsan, so for a time the city stood at the edge of Korean territory rather than inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Goryeo many wanted the capital moved there, and the schemes to do it disturbed the dynasty's peace more than once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Joseon it kept its standing: it dominated the north-west, shared in the trade with China, and carried one of the kingdom's more powerful provincial governorships and one of its more important military commands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It has been the seat of the northern Korean government since the Democratic People's Republic of Korea was established in September 1948.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An American intelligence assessment of 1950 described the Soviet embassy there as heading a mission of four to five thousand advisers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The World Bank's largest-city series puts about 3.2 million people in it and the United Nations 3.06 million in 2025, roughly an eighth of a country of some 26.6 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost nothing of the old city survived the Korean War, and what stands today is a rebuilding of the 1950s and after, laid out around wide avenues and monuments. The General Assembly has twice welcomed a meeting of the two Korean leaders in the city, the summits of 13 to 15 June 2000 and of 2 to 4 October 2007.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","“Memorandum by the Central Intelligence Agency,” document 57 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1950, Korea, Volume VII</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1950v07/d57. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population in the Largest City — Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/EN.URB.LCTY?locations=KP. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic People's Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, accessed 6 September 2026, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kp.html. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Peace, Security and Reunification on the Korean Peninsula,” resolution A/RES/55/11, adopted 31 October 2000, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/425985. [Open access]","United Nations General Assembly, “Peace, Security and Reunification on the Korean Peninsula,” resolution A/RES/62/5, adopted 31 October 2007, https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/610691. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/2016-10-16_Taedong_River_%28Pyongyang%29.jpg/1280px-2016-10-16_Taedong_River_%28Pyongyang%29.jpg","title":"The Taedong River at Pyongyang","desc":"The river runs through the middle of the capital; the city was rebuilt along it after 1953.","credit":"Jan Bockaert, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2016-10-16_Taedong_River_%28Pyongyang%29.jpg","alt":"A riverside walkway on the Taedong at Pyongyang, with the far bank and city buildings beyond."}},
{"id":"ko-012","num":12,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Two branches meeting above Seoul make the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose basin is the largest in Korea and whose water is drunk by some 26 million people.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches the Yellow Sea past Seoul, and its lower course is the essential water resource for close to half of South Korea's population.","Korea's biggest watershed drains 26,219 square kilometres into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Namhan and the Bukhan form where they join upstream of the capital."],"answer":"Han River","answerText":"Han River","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","korea","geography","nature","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Han River</b> is the central river of the Korean Peninsula, made where the Namhan, the South Han, meets the Bukhan, the North Han, above Seoul, and running west past the capital into the Yellow Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its watershed is the largest in Korea, draining 26,219 square kilometres (10,123 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It supplies drinking water to some 26 million people, and its lower reaches are the essential water resource for close to half of South Korea's population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No river in the country carries so much of its life, and none has been so thoroughly engineered. That it runs west at all is a fact about the shape of the peninsula: the high ground lies along the eastern side, tilted that way since the middle Miocene, so the long rivers drain towards the Yellow Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The mouth of the river carried the capital's security as well as its water, and Ganghwa Island at its outlet held a fortified royal refuge for emergencies, so its position and distance from Seoul were things a foreign map could get dangerously wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the founders of Joseon looked for a capital in the 1390s they surveyed the ground at Hanyang on its northern bank, and the city that grew there has depended on the river ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Daily rainfall has been measured beside it in Seoul since 1778, the longest such record anywhere before the industrial age, so the floods of two centuries can be counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Most of the year's water arrives in about a month, more than half of Korea's precipitation falling in the summer rains, and the river rises to match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Old city maps show it running past the southern edge of Seoul and then bending north-west to the sea, which is roughly what it still does beneath the bridges and expressways of a capital of ten million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Jong Kwon Im, Yong Chul Cho, Young Seuk Kim, Soyoung Lee, Taegu Kang and Sang Hun Kim, “Characteristics, Possible Origins, and Health Risk Assessment of Trace Elements in Surface Water of the Han River Watershed,” <i>International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health</i> 19, no. 23 (2022): 15822, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9741419/. [Open access]","Tae-June Choi, Adeel Malik, Hyung-Eun An, Jung-Il Kim, Thinh Dinh Do and Chang-Bae Kim, “Seasonal Diversity of Microeukaryotes in the Han River, Korea through 18S rRNA Gene Metabarcoding,” <i>Evolutionary Bioinformatics</i> 18 (2022): 11769343221074688, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8793432/. [Open access]","Cho-Hee Lee, Yeong Bae Seong, John Weber, Sangmin Ha, Dong-Eun Kim and Byung Yong Yu, “Topographic Metrics for Unveiling Fault Segmentation and Tectono-Geomorphic Evolution with Insights into the Impact of Inherited Topography, Ulsan Fault Zone, South Korea,” <i>Earth Surface Dynamics</i> 12, no. 5 (2024): 1091–1120, https://doi.org/10.5194/esurf-12-1091-2024. [Open access]","Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Jae-Won Lee, Ho-Jeong Shin and Jinkyu Hong, “Precipitation Data in Seoul, Korea during 1778–1907,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 566, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-03335-8. [Open access]","Korea Meteorological Administration, “Climate of Korea,” archived 17 January 2024, https://web.archive.org/web/20240117203253/https://www.kma.go.kr/eng/biz/climate_01.jsp. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Han_River_aerial_view.jpg","title":"The Han River at Seoul","desc":"The lower Han is bridged more than thirty times as it crosses the capital.","credit":"Brit in Seoul, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Han_River_aerial_view.jpg","alt":"An aerial view along the Han River through Seoul, with several road bridges crossing it."}},
{"id":"ko-013","num":13,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Some 78 million <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> live on the peninsula, sharing one language across it, though the idea that they form a single modern nation is barely a century old.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the South now have the lowest fertility of any sovereign state, 0.75 births per woman against a world figure of 2.19.","A study of speech across the peninsula found the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> unusually uniform in language, with the barriers that exist following mountain ranges rather than political lines."],"answer":"Koreans","answerText":"Koreans","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["people","korea","geography","history","asia","concept"],"abstract":"The <b>Koreans</b> are the people of the Korean Peninsula, about 78 million of them, some 51.7 million in the Republic of Korea and 26.6 million in the Democratic People's Republic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are among the most linguistically uniform large populations anywhere: a survey of Koreanic varieties across the peninsula found a high degree of homogeneity, and such barriers as it detected follow the Sobaek and Taebaek ranges and the strait to Jeju rather than any political line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four fifths of southerners and about three fifths of northerners live in towns and cities, a gap that has widened steadily since the two states were founded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two populations are now ageing at very different rates, 19.3 per cent of the South being 65 or older against 12.4 per cent of the North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The southern figure is the leading edge of the sharpest demographic change anywhere in the world. <br><br> In 2024 the Republic of Korea recorded a total fertility rate of 0.748 births per woman, against 1.78 in the North and 2.19 for the world as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Only Macao, a special administrative region rather than a sovereign state, returned a lower figure in the same series.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What has not been continuous is the idea of the Koreans as a single nation in the modern sense, which took shape in the late 19th and early 20th centuries as writers and reformers argued about what the country was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The figure of Dangun was put to work in those decades as a way of giving that nation a common ancestor and a founding date, and had carried quite different meanings before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That the framing is modern says nothing about how long people have lived on the peninsula, which is a separate question with its own evidence.","sources":["United Nations Statistics Division, “Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, accessed 6 September 2026, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kr.html. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Democratic People's Republic of Korea,” UNdata Country Profile, accessed 6 September 2026, https://data.un.org/en/iso/kp.html. [Open access]","Sean Lee, “A Sketch of Language History in the Korean Peninsula,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0128448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0128448. [Open access]","World Bank, “Population Ages 65 and Above (% of Total Population) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.POP.65UP.TO.ZS?locations=KR-KP. [Open access]","World Bank, “Fertility Rate, Total (Births per Woman) — Korea, Rep.; Korea, Dem. People's Rep.; World,” World Development Indicators, https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.DYN.TFRT.IN?locations=KR-KP-1W. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-014","num":14,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Millet farming spread out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> basin in Northeast China, and one much-argued account makes that valley the homeland of the ancestral Korean language.","questions":["Ancient genomes from Korea are modelled as a mixture of a northern source from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Bronze Age and a Late Neolithic population from southern China.","Archaeologists and linguists disagree about when speakers left the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> basin, and geneticists have since shown the peninsula's population is a mixture rather than one stream."],"answer":"West Liao River","answerText":"West Liao River","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","korea","asia","genetics","archaeology","prehistory","migration"],"abstract":"The <b>West Liao River</b> basin, in what is now Northeast China, is where millet was first cultivated in the region, and it has been proposed as the Neolithic homeland of a Transeurasian language family that would include Koreanic and Japonic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that account the ancestor of Korean arrived on the peninsula with millet farmers rather than being there from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genetics tells a partly separate story: ancient DNA from Three Kingdoms period burials in the southeast is modelled as a mixture of a Bronze Age West Liao River population and a Late Neolithic population from southern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of those individuals need no third source, and only two outliers required ancestry related to the Jomon of the Japanese archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The peninsula is therefore not the home of one unmixed lineage, which is what the older accounts of a single founding people assumed. <br><br> A deeper layer is visible further north, where two hunter-gatherers buried at Devil's Gate Cave about 7,700 years ago, on the border between Russia and Korea, are genetically closest to the Amur peoples living there today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their affinity with modern Koreans is close enough to be worth remarking on, and it points to long continuity in the north-east rather than replacement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When any of this happened is exactly what is argued about: one reading of the archaeology has the ancestral Korean speech arriving with Middle Neolithic millet villages in the 4th millennium BCE, another with a pottery tradition moving out of Liaodong in the 5th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The two are five thousand years apart, and no single line of evidence settles them. What the material does support is movement into the peninsula from the north-west, repeatedly, over a very long period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mark J. Hudson and Martine Robbeets, “Archaeolinguistic Evidence for the Farming/Language Dispersal of Koreanic,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e52, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.49. [Open access]","Martine Robbeets, Remco Bouckaert, Matthew Conte, Alexander Savelyev, Tao Li, Deog-Im An et al., “Triangulation Supports Agricultural Spread of the Transeurasian Languages,” <i>Nature</i> 599, no. 7886 (2021): 616–21, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04108-8. [Open access]","Hyoungmin Moon, Daewook Kim, Alina N. Hiss, Don-Nyeong Lee, Juhyeon Lee, Eirini Skourtanioti et al., “Ancient Genomes Reveal an Extensive Kinship Network and Endogamy in a Three-Kingdoms Period Society in Korea,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 15 (2026): eady8614, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13060608/. [Open access]","Veronika Siska, Eppie Ruth Jones, Sungwon Jeon, Youngjune Bhak, Hak-Min Kim, Yun Sung Cho et al., “Genome-Wide Data from Two Early Neolithic East Asian Individuals Dating to 7700 Years Ago,” <i>Science Advances</i> 3, no. 2 (2017): e1601877, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5287702/. [Open access]","Kazuo Miyamoto, “The Emergence of ‘Transeurasian’ Language Families in Northeast Asia as Viewed from Archaeological Evidence,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 4 (2022): e3, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2021.49. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Liaorivermap.png","title":"The Liao river system","desc":"The West Liao is the northern arm, formed by the Xar Moron and the Xilao, and drains the basin where millet was first farmed in the region.","credit":"Kmusser, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liaorivermap.png","alt":"A map of the Liao river system in Northeast China, with the West Liao and its headwaters labelled."}},
{"id":"ko-015","num":15,"category":"The land and its people","question":"The family <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> contains Korean and the speech of Jeju, and whether it is related to Japanese, Turkic and Mongolic at any greater depth is unsettled.","questions":["Because so few relatives survive, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is usually described as an isolate or a very small family, and reconstructing its history leans hard on archaeology.","One much-cited paper places the ancestor of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among millet farmers, spreading onto the peninsula in the 4th millennium BCE from the Liaodong side."],"answer":"Koreanic","answerText":"Koreanic","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","korea","asia","language","history","archaeology"],"abstract":"<b>Koreanic</b> is the language family to which Korean belongs, and it is a very small one: modern Korean, the divergent speech of Jeju, and the fragmentary Old Korean recorded for Silla, Baekje and Kaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some scholars therefore treat Korean as an isolate, since no securely related language survives outside the peninsula and its islands, which leaves its early history to be reconstructed from archaeology and from comparison with neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A widely discussed proposal makes it one branch of a Transeurasian family also containing Japonic, Tungusic, Mongolic and Turkic, spread by millet farmers moving out of the West Liao basin in Northeast China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that archaeolinguistic reading, Proto-Macro-Koreanic separated from Proto-Japonic on the Liaodong peninsula around 3500 BCE and entered Korea with the Middle Neolithic farming villages of the 4th millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It would follow that Korean is an incomer rather than the speech of the peninsula's first inhabitants. <br><br> The proposal is contested at every level, and the disagreement is about method as much as about dates. An archaeologist reading the pottery instead has Proto-Koreanic reaching southern Korea only in the 5th century BCE, carried by a rolled-rim vessel culture moving out of Liaodong as the Chinese state of Yan pushed eastward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A study testing 29 structural features across 28 languages found that at the depth the Transeurasian hypothesis proposes, roughly nine thousand years, the grammatical signal is not strong enough to reconstruct the family's internal branching at all, and the trees it produced were near-complete polytomies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What is not in doubt is the modern picture: a survey of varieties across the peninsula found them strikingly homogeneous, with the sharpest divisions falling at the Sobaek and Taebaek ranges and at the strait to Jeju.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The deep past is argued over; the recent past has left a language that travels the whole country intact.","sources":["Mark J. Hudson and Martine Robbeets, “Archaeolinguistic Evidence for the Farming/Language Dispersal of Koreanic,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e52, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.49. [Open access]","Martine Robbeets, Remco Bouckaert, Matthew Conte, Alexander Savelyev, Tao Li, Deog-Im An et al., “Triangulation Supports Agricultural Spread of the Transeurasian Languages,” <i>Nature</i> 599, no. 7886 (2021): 616–21, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04108-8. [Open access]","Kazuo Miyamoto, “The Emergence of ‘Transeurasian’ Language Families in Northeast Asia as Viewed from Archaeological Evidence,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 4 (2022): e3, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2021.49. [Open access]","Wenchao Li and Haitao Liu, “Structural Phylogenetic Signal Fails at Deep Time: A Bayesian Treebank Analysis of the Transeurasian Languages,” <i>Entropy</i> 28, no. 7 (2026): 816, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13407428/. [Open access]","Sean Lee, “A Sketch of Language History in the Korean Peninsula,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0128448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0128448. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Korean_dialect_map.png/1280px-Korean_dialect_map.png","title":"The dialects of Korean","desc":"The divisions the map shows follow mountain ranges and the strait to Jeju rather than any political line.","credit":"Karaeng Matoaya, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_dialect_map.png","alt":"A colour map of the Korean Peninsula divided into dialect areas, with Jeju shown separately."}},
{"id":"ko-016","num":16,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Completed in 1034 against Khitan and Jurchen raiding, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fixed the northern edge of the kingdom for three centuries across the neck of the peninsula.","questions":["Korea's frontier crept north for six hundred years, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the clearest mark of where that frontier stood in the 11th century.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was finished, campaigns in 1107 pushed far up the east coast and nine fortified towns were built there, all lost within two years."],"answer":"Goryeo long wall","answerText":"Goryeo long wall","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["building","korea","history","warfare","geography","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Goryeo long wall</b>, finished in 1034, ran across the northern neck of the Korean Peninsula and marks where the kingdom's frontier stood against repeated incursions by the Khitan and the Jurchen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It went up in the decade after the Khitan were beaten at Gwiju in 1019, a defeat so complete that a Song official wrote they could not bring home a single horse or cart, and after which the two powers argued instead over fortresses on the Amnok.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korea's northern edge was never fixed but crept upward over six centuries, and the wall is the sharpest surviving statement of one stage in that movement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1107 campaigns led by Yun Gwan drove the Jurchen from a stretch of the east coast as far north as Gyeongseong, where nine towns were built and fortified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jurchen took the ground back two years later, and in the 13th century it passed to the Mongols.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Goryeo did not hold it again until 1356, when King Gongmin sent troops across the Amnok and destroyed the Mongol relay stations, and in 1370 he sent Yi Seonggye into Manchuria with five thousand cavalry and ten thousand foot, forcing the surrender of more than ten thousand households.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By the dynasty's end in 1392 the administered line ran from a point on the Amnok east of Choseong to the coast at Gyeongseong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yi Seonggye, founding Joseon, pushed that coastal strip up to the lower Duman, and by 1441 every part of the land inside the river's northern bend had been added, the farthest north any Korean state had reached since Goguryeo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Holding it was possible partly because early Joseon inherited and strengthened Goryeo's centralised administration rather than replacing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Jurchen along that frontier were handled by treaty, trade and force in turn, and Korean records of them are among the fullest that survive anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Inuk Heo, “An Examination of the Koryŏ-Khitan Relations from the 10th to 12th Century through the Balance of Power System,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 1 (2024): 223–62, https://doi.org/10.22372/ijkh.2024.29.1.223. [Open access]","Peter Yun, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Manchuria and Korea in East Asian History,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 1 (2016): 1–10, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-21-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Jong-rok Oh, “The Characteristics of the Ruling Structure during Early Chosŏn,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 9 (2005): 1–34, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/9_01.pdf. [Open access]","Kishik Noh, “Recent Research Trends on Jurchen-Manchu Studies in Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 1 (2016): 249–58, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-21-1-249.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Cheolli_Jangseong.png","title":"The line of the Goryeo long wall","desc":"The wall ran from the mouth of the Amnok across the peninsula to the east coast.","credit":"Historiographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cheolli_Jangseong.png","alt":"A relief map of Korea and southern Manchuria with the line of the Goryeo long wall drawn in red."}},
{"id":"ko-017","num":17,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Rising on Paektu and running south-west to the Yellow Sea, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has carried Korea's northern boundary with China since the 15th century.","questions":["Korean armies crossed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1356 and again in 1370, and Goryeo and the Khitan had earlier spent years arguing over fortresses built on it.","Where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the Duman rise is a question a Qing and Korean survey party tried to settle on the mountain in 1712, leaving an inscribed stone."],"answer":"Amnok River","answerText":"Amnok River","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["river","korea","geography","asia","history","nature"],"abstract":"The <b>Amnok River</b>, known in Chinese as the Yalu, rises on Mount Paektu and runs south-west to the Yellow Sea, and together with the Duman on the far side of the mountain it carries the whole of Korea's land boundary with China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Duman, the Tumen in Chinese, runs 525 kilometres (326 miles) east from the same massif and touches Russia in its last stretch before the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Neither river was always the border. In the 1030s Goryeo and the Khitan quarrelled for years over fortresses and a bridge built on the Amnok, each treating the crossing as the thing worth holding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Goryeo armies crossed it in 1356 to destroy the Mongol relay stations beyond, and again in 1370 under Yi Seonggye, and the kingdom kept a claim to Liaodong on the far bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Only in the 15th century did a Korean state hold both lower courses and the land inside the Duman's northern bend, and the upper Amnok's terrain defeated an attempt to organise the ground inside its own bend, which proved too rough to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Exactly where on Paektu the two rivers begin mattered enough that in 1712 a joint Qing and Korean party climbed the mountain and cut a boundary inscription on the watershed, whose text was still being copied onto provincial maps a century later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both valleys have stayed thinly settled and heavily forested, and the lower Duman basin now holds some of the best-preserved forest in Northeast Asia, shared between China, North Korea and Russia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Jurchen peoples who lived along the two rivers are the subject of a large Korean scholarly literature, the frontier having been a place of trade and negotiation as much as of raiding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rivers freeze hard in winter, which for most of their history made the boundary they mark a seasonal obstacle rather than a permanent one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Gari Ledyard, “Cartography in Korea,” in <i>The History of Cartography</i>, vol. 2, book 2, ed. J. B. Harley and David Woodward (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1994), 235–344, https://press.uchicago.edu/books/HOC/HOC_V2_B2/HOC_VOLUME2_Book2_chapter10.pdf. [Open access]","Hailong Li, Puneet Pandey, Ying Li, Tianming Wang, Randeep Singh, Yuxi Peng, Hang Lee, Woo-Shin Lee, Weihong Zhu and Chang-Yong Choi, “Transboundary Cooperation in the Tumen River Basin Is the Key to Amur Leopard (<i>Panthera pardus</i>) Population Recovery in the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Animals</i> 14, no. 1 (2023): 59, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10778315/. [Open access]","Inuk Heo, “An Examination of the Koryŏ-Khitan Relations from the 10th to 12th Century through the Balance of Power System,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 1 (2024): 223–62, https://doi.org/10.22372/ijkh.2024.29.1.223. [Open access]","Peter Yun, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Manchuria and Korea in East Asian History,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 1 (2016): 1–10, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-21-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Kishik Noh, “Recent Research Trends on Jurchen-Manchu Studies in Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 21, no. 1 (2016): 249–58, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-21-1-249.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/The_Yalu_River_-_%E9%B8%AD%E7%BB%BF%E6%B1%9F2.jpg/1280px-The_Yalu_River_-_%E9%B8%AD%E7%BB%BF%E6%B1%9F2.jpg","title":"The Amnok River","desc":"The far bank is North Korea; the near bank is China.","credit":"Caitriana Nicholson, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Yalu_River_-_%E9%B8%AD%E7%BB%BF%E6%B1%9F2.jpg","alt":"A wide stretch of the Amnok between wooded hills, with a moored boat at the near bank."}},
{"id":"ko-018","num":18,"category":"The land and its people","question":"About 90 kilometres (56 miles) south of the peninsula, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Korea's largest island, built by volcanoes and crowned by the country's highest mountain.","questions":["The flora of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mixes subtropical, temperate, boreal and arctic-alpine species, which is one reason UNESCO has listed the island three separate ways.","The women divers of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> work without air for a living, and a genetic study found variants among them associated with lower blood pressure and cold tolerance."],"answer":"Jeju","answerText":"Jeju","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","korea","geography","nature","asia","people"],"abstract":"<b>Jeju</b> is the largest island of Korea, lying about 90 kilometres (56 miles) south of the peninsula at the south-western entrance to the Korea Strait, with an area of roughly 1,828 square kilometres (706 square miles) excluding its attached islets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built by volcanic activity between about 1.8 million and 1,000 years ago, and the shield volcano at its centre, Hallasan, reaches 1,950 metres (6,400 feet), the highest point in South Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its slopes carry lava tubes and several hundred parasitic cones called oreum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the island stands where cold and warm floras meet, its plants run from subtropical species through temperate and boreal ones to arctic-alpine survivors near the crater rim, and a single survey of its liverworts and hornworts recorded 222 taxa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of it was made a national park in 1970, a biosphere reserve in 2002, a World Heritage Site in 2007 and a Global Geopark in 2010.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The island is basalt almost throughout, so although it receives between 1,140 and 1,960 millimetres of rain a year, sustained stream flow is rare and about half of that precipitation goes straight into the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its people speak their own variety of Korean, divergent enough that surveys of peninsular speech treat the strait as one of the few real linguistic boundaries in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its best-known working tradition is the haenyeo, women who dive without breathing apparatus for shellfish and seaweed in cold water, often continuing through pregnancy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study of them found pronounced slowing of the heart during dives, largely a training effect, alongside genetic variation associated with lower diastolic blood pressure and with tolerance of cold water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> There are now about 2,623 active divers, more than nine in ten of them over sixty, so the practice is passing out of use as it is being studied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Seung Se Choi, Vadim A. Bakalin, Hyun Min Bum, Seung Jin Park, Dae Shin Kim, Ung San Ahn and Myung-Ok Moon, “The Liverwort and Hornwort Flora of Jeju Island, Republic of Korea: A Volcanic Island with a Unique Flora,” <i>Plants</i> 12, no. 12 (2023): 2384, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10301385/. [Open access]","Hyung-Mi Cho, Tae-Hoon Kim, Jae-Hong Moon, Byung-Chan Song, Dong-Woon Hwang, Taejin Kim and Dong-Hoon Im, “Estimating Submarine Groundwater Discharge in Jeju Volcanic Island (Korea) during a Typhoon (Kong-rey) Using Humic-Fluorescent Dissolved Organic Matter-Si Mass Balance,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 1391, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-79381-0. [Open access]","Sean Lee, “A Sketch of Language History in the Korean Peninsula,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 5 (2015): e0128448, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0128448. [Open access]","Diana Aguilar-Gómez, Jacob Bejder, Jonathan Graae, Yelin Ko, Andrew Vaughn, Kendell Clement et al., “Genetic and Training Adaptations in the Haenyeo Divers of Jeju, Korea,” <i>Cell Reports</i> 44, no. 5 (2025): 115577, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12129667/. [Open access]","Joon Hyuk Park and Ki Woong Kim, “Research on Haenyeo: Insights into Human Adaptations to Extreme Environments,” <i>Psychiatry Investigation</i> 22, no. 7 (2025): 737–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12301677/. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Hallasan_Volcanic_Crater.jpg/1280px-Hallasan_Volcanic_Crater.jpg","title":"The crater of Hallasan","desc":"Baengnokdam, the crater lake at the summit of the shield volcano that built Jeju.","credit":"Nfarag2019, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hallasan_Volcanic_Crater.jpg","alt":"The rocky summit crater of Hallasan on Jeju, partly in cloud, with green slopes below."}},
{"id":"ko-019","num":19,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Two rocky islets and their attendant reefs, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is held by South Korea, claimed by Japan as Takeshima, and covers under a fifth of a square kilometre.","questions":["Japan incorporated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by a Shimane prefectural notice in February 1905, and the San Francisco treaty of 1951 did not name it among the territories returned.","Korean claims to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are argued back to the state of Usan, recorded as submitting to Silla in 512, and to an entry in the Annals of King Sejong."],"answer":"Dokdo","answerText":"Dokdo","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","korea","japan","geography","asia","history","concept"],"abstract":"<b>Dokdo</b>, called Takeshima in Japan and the Liancourt Rocks in neutral usage, is a group of two main islets, Dongdo and Seodo, with attendant rocks, lying in the East Sea 216.8 kilometres (135 miles) from the Korean mainland, 87.4 kilometres (54 miles) from Ulleungdo and 157.6 kilometres (98 miles) from Japan's Oki Islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Volcanic in origin, it covers 187,554 square metres, under a fifth of a square kilometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South Korea holds and garrisons it, Japan claims it, and neither state has agreed to put the question to arbitration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korean argument for an older title runs back to the state of Usan, recorded as submitting to Silla in 512, which is held to have governed both Ulleungdo and the rocks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A chapter of the Annals of King Sejong describing that territory names Usan and Mullung as two places within sight of one another, which is read as a distinction between two islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Whether those names refer to two islands or to one is precisely what the two sides dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The modern case turns on 1905, when Japan incorporated the rocks into Shimane Prefecture by prefectural notice during the years in which it was taking control of Korea, and on whether an act of that period can create a valid title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The San Francisco Peace Treaty of 1951 did not list the rocks among the territories Japan was to give up, which each side reads its own way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Seoul and Tokyo were being pressed towards normalisation, an American memorandum listed sovereignty over the rocks among the outstanding issues between them and dismissed the place as an inconsequential islet, which is roughly the opposite of how either capital saw it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Around them lived the Japanese or Dokdo sea lion, hunted to extinction in the 1950s; a genome study of sixteen bone fragments from Dokdo and Ulleungdo found it the earliest-diverging species of its genus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dong-Yeol Lee, Ilyeong Jeong, Seonmin Kim, Jae Won Choi, Min Hyeok Won, Donguk Kim et al., “Checklist for the Insect Fauna of Two East Sea Islands (Ulleungdo Is. and Dokdo Is.) in the Republic of Korea,” <i>Biodiversity Data Journal</i> 12 (2024): e129360, https://doi.org/10.3897/BDJ.12.e129360. [Open access]","Min Jung Chung, “Analysis of the Territorial Issue regarding the Liancourt Rocks between Korea and Japan,” <i>The Korean Journal of International and Comparative Law</i> 7, no. 1 (2019): 1–66, https://brill.com/view/journals/kjic/7/1/article-p1_1.xml. [Open access]","Yong-ho Ch'oe, “Japan's 1905 Incorporation of Dokdo/Takeshima: A Historical Perspective,” <i>The Asia-Pacific Journal</i> 13 (2015): e1, https://doi.org/10.1017/S1557466015017325. [Open access]","“Memorandum Prepared in the Department of State,” document 261 in <i>Foreign Relations of the United States, 1961–1963, Volume XXII, Northeast Asia</i>, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1961-63v22/d261. [Open access]","Jungeun Kim, Asta Blazyte, Jae-Pil Choi, Changjae Kim, Fedor Sharko, Sungwon Jeon et al., “Dokdo Sea Lion <i>Zalophus japonicus</i> Genome Reveals Its Evolutionary Trajectory before Extinction,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 23 (2025): 234, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12317591/. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/Dokdo_Island.jpg","title":"The Liancourt Rocks","desc":"The two main islets, Dongdo and Seodo, seen from the sea; a South Korean flag flies at the left of the frame.","credit":"Morales9199, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dokdo_Island.jpg","alt":"Two steep rocky islets rising from the sea in mist."}},
{"id":"ko-020","num":20,"category":"The land and its people","question":"Korean historians divide the past into ancient, medieval and modern, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is disputed at every join, with five different starting points proposed for the middle.","questions":["Because it decides what counts as one era, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not a neutral filing system, and Korean scholars have argued about it for the whole life of the discipline.","One <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the language runs Ancient to the 10th century, Medieval to the 16th, Early Modern to the late nineteenth, and Modern after that."],"answer":"Periodisation","answerText":"Periodisation","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"undatable":true,"tags":["concept","korea","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Periodisation</b> is the division of the past into named stretches, and in Korean history the divisions are argued over rather than agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The medieval period is generally taken to coincide with Goryeo, but proposed starting points include the Three Kingdoms era, Unified Silla, the late Silla to early Goryeo transition, the late Goryeo to early Joseon transition, and the mid-Joseon dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scholar who first argued for a Korean medieval archaeology set its span from Unified Silla through Goryeo and into Joseon, partly because he did not want Joseon filed under the modern era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another proposed the narrower Goryeo definition on archaeological grounds, since its tombs, fortresses and pottery differ from what comes before and after, and then used the phrase medieval period rather than the dynasty's name to get out from under a purely dynastic scheme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the difficulty in miniature: an unbroken line of dynasties makes dynastic labels tempting, and a scheme built on them describes the court rather than the country. <br><br> The argument is old and was once explicitly political. Japanese colonial scholarship held that Korean society had been stagnant, and Korean Marxist historians of the 1930s answered by trying to show the peninsula had passed through the same universal stages as everywhere else, which turned the question of which era Joseon belonged to into a national one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Scholars still divide over whether to stress that universality or Korea's particularity, and perceptions of Joseon's standing vary with the topic being studied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the modern era begins is a case in point, since the idea of Koreans as a single nation in the modern sense itself took shape only around the turn of the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Different questions want different divisions: a corpus of Korean texts spanning a thousand years adopts a four-part scheme for the language, running Ancient to 918, Medieval to the 16th century, Early Modern to the late nineteenth and Modern after, joins chosen for changes in writing rather than in politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hyunwoo Kim, “The Medieval Archaeology of Korea: Its Conceptual Framework and Examples,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 71–110, https://doi.org/10.22372/ijkh.2019.24.1.71. [Open access]","Hyongyerl Cho, “The Universal-Particular Debates in Marxist Historiography of Colonial Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 119–56, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-119.pdf. [Open access]","Jong-rok Oh, “The Characteristics of the Ruling Structure during Early Chosŏn,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 9 (2005): 1–34, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/9_01.pdf. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]","Seyoung Song, Nawon Kim, Songeun Chae, Kiwoong Park, Jiho Jin, Haneul Yoo, Kyunghyun Cho and Alice Oh, “Open Korean Historical Corpus: A Millennia-Scale Diachronic Collection of Public Domain Texts,” arXiv:2510.24541 (2026), https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24541. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/%EA%B3%A0%EB%A0%A4%EC%82%AC_%281%29.JPG/1280px-%EA%B3%A0%EB%A0%A4%EC%82%AC_%281%29.JPG","title":"The Goryeosa","desc":"The dynastic history of Goryeo, compiled under Joseon; dynastic titles are the labels a periodisation built on courts uses.","credit":"Jocelyndurrey, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EA%B3%A0%EB%A0%A4%EC%82%AC_%281%29.JPG","alt":"An open woodblock-printed Korean book in classical Chinese characters."}},
{"id":"ko-021","num":21,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Sites in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may reach back 350,000 years, but acid soils destroy bone at open-air sites, so most of what survives is stone.","questions":["The peninsula was never cut off from the mainland as Japan was, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the easternmost ground hominins could walk to.","Human fossils in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> come almost entirely from limestone caves, and most of the important ones lie north of the demilitarised zone."],"answer":"Palaeolithic Korea","answerText":"Palaeolithic Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest claim</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 350,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","evolution"],"abstract":"<b>Palaeolithic Korea</b> is the long stretch of the peninsula's past before pottery, known almost entirely from stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the Korean Peninsula was never severed from the Chinese mainland as the Japanese archipelago was, except during extreme glaciations, it is the easternmost part of Eurasia that hominins restricted to walking could reach, and it is likely that people who reached China in the early or middle Pleistocene reached Korea as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earliest claimed occupation is Geumeunmoru outside Pyongyang, though whether its fractured stones were struck by people or broken by nature is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The next oldest may be a group of open-air sites in the Imjin and Hantan river basins north of Seoul, which have been argued to reach back as far as 350,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those sites yield only stone, because the peninsula's soils are acidic enough that bone rarely survives outside caves and coastal shell middens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Pleistocene human fossils have been reported from eight cave sites, most of them in North Korea, which is one reason the fossil record of the peninsula is little known outside Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modern research began late compared with China and Japan, taking hold only in the 1960s and 1970s at sites such as Seokjang-ri and Amsa-dong, and much of what is now known came from salvage excavations run ahead of very large dam projects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The stone itself is unusual: quartzite and vein quartz dominate, coarse materials that are hard to work finely and that shaped what Korean toolmakers were able to make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Well into the late Pleistocene, around 30,000 years ago, some assemblages remain simple core-and-flake industries of the kind classified as Mode 1, struck by direct hard-hammer percussion with little refined shaping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How those layers are dated — by soil wedges, by volcanic ash, by radiocarbon — is argued about at almost every site, so the chronology is a working one rather than a settled one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Pierre Guyomarc'h, “Potential Contributions of Korean Pleistocene Hominin Fossils to Palaeoanthropology: A View from Ryonggok Cave,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 31–57, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55546. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Chuntaek Seong, “Quartzite and Vein Quartz as Lithic Raw Materials Reconsidered: A View from the Korean Paleolithic,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 73–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17202. [Open access]","Hyeong Woo Lee, “The Persistence of Mode 1 Technology in the Korean Late Paleolithic,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 5 (2013): e64999, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0064999. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Paleolithic_denticulates._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Paleolithic_denticulates._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"Palaeolithic stone tools from Korea","desc":"Denticulate tools in the National Museum of Korea; quartzite and vein quartz dominate Korean Palaeolithic assemblages.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paleolithic_denticulates._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"Four worked stone tools laid out on a museum shelf with numbered labels."}},
{"id":"ko-022","num":22,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Bifacially worked stone tools found in 1978 at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> north of Seoul reopened a long argument about whether East Asia lacked hand axes altogether.","questions":["The stone tools at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lie in Pleistocene deposits on a lava plateau in the Imjin basin, and dating them has proved unusually hard.","Older writing transliterated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as Chongokni, and its discovery is the reason Korean prehistory entered a debate that had been running since 1948."],"answer":"Jeongok-ri","answerText":"Jeongok-ri","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposits</span><span class=\"dt-v\">up to c. 350,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Pleistocene, and disputed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","object"],"abstract":"<b>Jeongok-ri</b>, in Yeoncheon on the Imjin River north of Seoul, is the Korean Palaeolithic site that changed how East Asia's stone tools were discussed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bifacially worked implements were found there in 1978, in deposits clearly of Pleistocene age, and their discovery renewed debate over the validity of the Movius Line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That line, proposed in 1948, divided an Old World in which hand axes were made from an eastern zone in which they supposedly were not, and Korea sat firmly on the eastern side of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Older publications transliterate the site as Chongokni, which is why the same place appears in the literature under two names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It lies on a lava plateau among the Imjin and Hantan river basin sites, some of which have been argued to reach back 350,000 years, and it is the sediments above the basalt that have to be dated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Dating them has proved unusually difficult, and the difficulty is itself part of what the site is known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Radiocarbon results from soil wedges at Jeongok-ri did not come out in stratigraphic order, which has been explained by redeposition or by animals burrowing through the layers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One archaeologist has argued that the site should be dated from how it formed rather than from any single set of measurements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A layer of Aira-Tn volcanic ash, blown from southern Japan, is present at Jeongok-ri and at Juwol-ri in Paju, and some researchers use it as a chronological marker while others doubt that small quantities of ash can date a cultural layer at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the bifaces themselves are remains open: pieces of this kind in Korea are thick and hard-hammer struck, without the refined symmetry of a classic Acheulean hand axe, and they are rare wherever they occur.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Hyeong Woo Lee, “The Persistence of Mode 1 Technology in the Korean Late Paleolithic,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 8, no. 5 (2013): e64999, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0064999. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Pierre Guyomarc'h, “Potential Contributions of Korean Pleistocene Hominin Fossils to Palaeoanthropology: A View from Ryonggok Cave,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 31–57, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55546. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Chuntaek Seong, “Quartzite and Vein Quartz as Lithic Raw Materials Reconsidered: A View from the Korean Paleolithic,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 73–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17202. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5c/Handaxe._Jeongok_ri._Gyeonggi_do._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"A hand axe from Jeongok-ri","desc":"Bifacially worked implements like this one, found from 1978, reopened the argument over the Movius Line.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Handaxe._Jeongok_ri._Gyeonggi_do._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"A pointed bifacially flaked stone tool mounted upright in a museum case."}},
{"id":"ko-023","num":23,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Named after the comb-pattern pottery that defines it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Korea's Neolithic, running from about 10,000 BCE to the arrival of bronze.","questions":["Villages of pit houses appear in west-central Korea during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and small-scale millet cultivation appears with them in the 4th millennium BCE.","Some archaeologists write of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and others simply of the Korean Neolithic, and the two labels cover the same several thousand years."],"answer":"Jeulmun period","answerText":"Jeulmun period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 – 1500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Villages from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["era","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","agriculture"],"abstract":"The <b>Jeulmun period</b> is the Korean Neolithic, named after the comb-patterned pottery that runs through it; older and Western writing usually romanises the word as Chulmun, and many archaeologists simply say Neolithic instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is conventionally divided into Incipient, Early, Middle, Late and Final phases, opening around 10,000 BCE with the Gosan-ni assemblage on Jeju and closing about 1500 BCE with the arrival of plain Mumun pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For most of that span people hunted, fished and gathered, and the coastal shell middens they left behind are among the richest evidence the peninsula has, because bone survives in them where it does not in open ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Villages of semi-subterranean pit houses appear in resource-rich west-central Korea during the Middle phase, on river banks, on the coast and on offshore islands, where fish, shellfish, small game and plants could all be reached without moving far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small-scale cultivation of millet appears with them, in the early 4th millennium BCE. <br><br> What happened next is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Counting pit houses and settlements against radiocarbon dates shows sedentary sites rising sharply through the late 4th millennium and then almost disappearing from central and southern Korea after the late third, which suggests either a return to moving about or a sharp fall in population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That break is why one model treats Bronze Age farming as continuous with Neolithic millet growing and another treats it as a fresh start, and the counts favour the second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A comparable decline is visible in Japan at about the same time and has been linked to a wider Late Neolithic downturn across northern Eurasia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> No environmental crisis or resource shortage has been identified to explain why people settled down in the first place, which is a gap in the account rather than an answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Sung-Mo Ahn, Jangsuk Kim and Jaehoon Hwang, “Sedentism, Settlements, and Radiocarbon Dates of Neolithic Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 113–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55549. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson and Martine Robbeets, “Archaeolinguistic Evidence for the Farming/Language Dispersal of Koreanic,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e52, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.49. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Jar_Coffin%2C_Jeulmun_Period._Jinju._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Jar_Coffin%2C_Jeulmun_Period._Jinju._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"A Jeulmun-period jar coffin","desc":"Found at Jinju and now in the National Museum of Korea.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jar_Coffin%2C_Jeulmun_Period._Jinju._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"A large round-bodied ceramic jar with incised decoration around its upper part, displayed on a stand."}},
{"id":"ko-024","num":24,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Korea's Neolithic takes its name from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, conical jars with pointed or rounded bases whose surfaces carry short slants and herringbones.","questions":["The design on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first laid out in three bands around the vessel, and that division weakened as the style spread south.","Fats absorbed into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its successors show broomcorn millet being cooked in Korea and not in the Japanese pots of the same centuries."],"answer":"comb-pattern pottery","answerText":"comb-pattern pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["object","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Comb-pattern pottery</b> is the ware that gives the Korean Neolithic its Korean name: <i>jeulmun</i> means comb-pattern, and the period is called the Jeulmun, or in older romanisation the Chulmun, accordingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The characteristic vessels are pots and jars with a conical body and a pointed or rounded base, decorated with geometric designs including short slanting strokes and herringbones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decoration was originally arranged in three bands — upper, middle and lower — around the surface of the vessel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As the style spread southward down the peninsula the three-part division was pressed less firmly and regional variants emerged, which is how archaeologists sort sites into phases and regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pottery of this kind is what allows Korean Neolithic sites to be recognised and ordered at all, since so little else survives in acid soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The pots are also a record of what was cooked in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Analysis of the fats absorbed into 260 vessels from Korea and from contemporary sites in northern Kyushu found a lipid biomarker for broomcorn millet in the Korean samples and in none of the Japanese ones, although millet was being grown in Japan by then.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same study found aquatic and ruminant signatures alongside the plant one, so these were general-purpose cooking vessels rather than specialised ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Isotopes read directly from human bone tell a matching story of mixed diets, with some prehistoric Korean communities drawing heavily on the sea and others on land and on cultivated grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pottery appears on the peninsula by about 10,000 BCE, with the Gosan-ni assemblage on Jeju, which places the beginning of the Korean sequence among the earliest anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Oliver E. Craig, Jasmine Lundy, Manon Bondetti, Sesha Nicholson-Lailey, Natsuki Murakami, Miho Suzuki, Chris Stevens and Alexandre Lucquin, “Lipid Residue Analysis Reveals Divergent Culinary Practices in Japan and Korea at the Dawn of Intensive Agriculture,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 30 (2025): e2504414122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12318219/. [Open access]","Kyungcheol Choy, Hee Young Yun, Benjamin T. Fuller and Marcello A. Mannino, “Enhanced Dietary Reconstruction of Korean Prehistoric Populations by Combining δ13C and δ15N Amino Acids of Bone Collagen,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0300068, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0300068. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Bowl%2C_Yeongseon-dong._Jeulmun%2C_Southern_Coastal_region._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Bowl%2C_Yeongseon-dong._Jeulmun%2C_Southern_Coastal_region._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"A comb-patterned bowl","desc":"From Yeongseon-dong on the southern coast, with the incised decoration running over the whole surface.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bowl%2C_Yeongseon-dong._Jeulmun%2C_Southern_Coastal_region._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"A deep ceramic bowl tapering to a narrow base, covered in fine incised cross-hatching."}},
{"id":"ko-025","num":25,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"The standard Neolithic Korean dwelling was a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, dug 30 to 60 cm into the ground with a stone-lined hearth at the centre and four roof posts.","questions":["Counting every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against its radiocarbon date is how archaeologists measure whether Neolithic Koreans were settling down or moving about.","A Middle Neolithic village in Korea usually held three to eight of them, each 4 to 6 metres across, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sheltered a single household."],"answer":"pit house","answerText":"pit house","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 BCE onwards</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","practice"],"abstract":"A <b>pit house</b> is a dwelling whose floor is dug below ground level, and it is the standard house of Neolithic Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Middle Neolithic village typically consisted of three to eight of them, each 4 to 6 metres across and 30 to 50 centimetres deep, with a circular or square living floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four or five posts carried a sloping roof, and every floor had a hearth, usually at the centre and ringed with stones; some of the larger houses had two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Storage pits were dug inside or just outside the house, and at several sites one of them held a large bottomless jar set upside down in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Entrances generally faced south, and open-air hearths and pottery kilns have been found among the houses at Amsa-dong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Because a pit house leaves a clear cut in the ground and can be dated, counting them is how the pace of settling down is measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Doing that across South Korea shows sedentary settlements with small-scale millet cultivation appearing in the central west in the early 4th millennium BCE, rising sharply in the late fourth, and then almost vanishing from central and southern Korea after the late 3rd millennium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is evidence against the view that Bronze Age farming grew smoothly out of Neolithic cultivation, and its authors suggest a worsening environment drove both the rise and the collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The form did not disappear with the Neolithic: rectangular and long rectangular pit dwellings are among the defining features of the Korean Bronze Age, and the shape of the hearth is one of the things that distinguishes its regional styles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the peninsula's acid soils destroy so much else, house floors, hearths and storage pits carry a large share of what can be said about ordinary prehistoric life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Sung-Mo Ahn, Jangsuk Kim and Jaehoon Hwang, “Sedentism, Settlements, and Radiocarbon Dates of Neolithic Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 113–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55549. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Pierre Guyomarc'h, “Potential Contributions of Korean Pleistocene Hominin Fossils to Palaeoanthropology: A View from Ryonggok Cave,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 31–57, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55546. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Amsadong_IMG_2080.jpg/1280px-Amsadong_IMG_2080.jpg","title":"Inside a Neolithic pit house","desc":"A museum reconstruction of the interior of a dwelling at Amsa-dong, with the hearth at the centre.","credit":"G41rn8, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amsadong_IMG_2080.jpg","alt":"A museum diorama of figures seated on the floor of a round sunken dwelling around a central hearth."}},
{"id":"ko-026","num":26,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"On the south bank of the Han within modern Seoul, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds nearly thirty Neolithic dwellings and is the type site for village life in west-central Korea.","questions":["Four of the dwellings at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have radiocarbon dates centring on about 3800 and 3400 BCE, which places the village in the Middle Neolithic.","Along with Seokjang-ri, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one of the sites whose excavation in the 1960s and 1970s began modern Korean prehistoric archaeology."],"answer":"Amsa-dong","answerText":"Amsa-dong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3800 – 3400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory"],"abstract":"<b>Amsa-dong</b> is a Neolithic settlement on the south bank of the Han River, inside the boundaries of modern Seoul, and is treated as one of the primary Middle Neolithic villages of west-central Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It contains nearly thirty dwellings, four of which have radiocarbon dates centring on about 3800 and 3400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The houses were square semi-subterranean pits with rounded corners, each with a stone-lined central hearth and four roof-support posts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Floors ranged from about 3.9 by 3.1 metres to 7.8 by 6.8 metres and were generally 30 to 60 centimetres deep, though at least four were cut a full metre down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The entrances faced south, and open-air hearths and pottery kilns stood among the houses, so the village was making its own vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One dwelling had a storage pit beside its hearth holding a large bottomless jar set upside down, a feature shared with the Gungsan and Jitap-ni sites further north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten more storage pits were found, six of them outside the houses, which is evidence that food was being kept rather than eaten as it was gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Amsa-dong belongs to a group of Middle Neolithic villages set on river banks, coasts and offshore islands, where a community could reach fish, shellfish, small game and plant foods without moving far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its excavation, together with that of Seokjang-ri, is where modern Korean prehistoric research began in the 1960s and 1970s, before the dam-driven salvage projects that produced much of the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Exactly how the Korean Neolithic should be divided into phases is still argued over, so the village's place in the sequence rests on its dates rather than on its pottery alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Sung-Mo Ahn, Jangsuk Kim and Jaehoon Hwang, “Sedentism, Settlements, and Radiocarbon Dates of Neolithic Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 113–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55549. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/20250524_Nakjibibimbap_Archaeological_Site_in_Amsa-dong%2C.jpg/1280px-20250524_Nakjibibimbap_Archaeological_Site_in_Amsa-dong%2C.jpg","title":"The Amsa-dong site","desc":"Reconstructed dwellings at the excavated Neolithic settlement on the south bank of the Han River in Seoul.","credit":"Nakjibibimbap, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20250524_Nakjibibimbap_Archaeological_Site_in_Amsa-dong%2C.jpg","alt":"Two conical thatched huts reconstructed on open ground with trees behind them."}},
{"id":"ko-027","num":27,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"The first crop grown in Korea was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, carbonised grains of which turn up on Middle Neolithic dwelling floors from about 3500 BCE.","questions":["Korea was a secondary region for farming: <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the rice that followed were both domesticated in China and spread onto the peninsula.","Grains of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> AMS-dated to about 3360 BCE were found on a dwelling floor at Tongsam-dong on Korea's south coast."],"answer":"foxtail millet","answerText":"foxtail millet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Korea from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["plant","korea","asia","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology"],"abstract":"<b>Foxtail millet</b>, <i>Setaria italica</i>, is the first crop known to have been grown in Korea, and its appearance marks the beginning of food production on the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbonised grains have been recovered from Middle Neolithic dwelling floors at Jitap-ni and Gungsan in the north-west, dated as early as about 3500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the south coast, foxtail millet from a dwelling floor at Tongsam-dong has been AMS-dated to about 3360 BCE, and broomcorn millet and edible wild chenopods were recovered from the same shell midden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Similar finds come from Masan-ni, Unseo-dong, Asan Shindoshi and Janghangseon, so the practice was spread across the west-central region rather than confined to one settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale was small, and the people growing it went on fishing, hunting and gathering as before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Korea is treated as a secondary region for agriculture rather than a place where crops were domesticated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Foxtail millet farming appears in northern China between about 9,000 and 8,000 years ago, and both millet and later rice spread from China onto the peninsula and from there to Japan, probably carried by people as well as by trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The nearest earlier example was the lower Liao River area of north-eastern China, where farming had begun by about 5000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether this Neolithic millet growing led on to the intensive farming of the Bronze Age is disputed, and a count of settlements and radiocarbon dates suggests that it did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cultivated fields themselves have been identified at Munam-ri in Goseong, and charred seeds turn up in Neolithic deposits elsewhere, which together show that early and middle Holocene foragers were breaking ground rather than merely tending wild stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Sung-Mo Ahn, Jangsuk Kim and Jaehoon Hwang, “Sedentism, Settlements, and Radiocarbon Dates of Neolithic Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 113–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55549. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg/1280px-Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg","title":"Foxtail millet","desc":"Setaria italica, the first crop grown on the Korean Peninsula.","credit":"Krzysztof Ziarnek, Kenraiz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setaria_italica_kz01.jpg","alt":"A stand of tall grass carrying dense bristly seed heads."}},
{"id":"ko-028","num":28,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"By about 1000 BCE Koreans were growing rice, and a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with its channels, dikes and reservoir has been excavated at Majeon-ni in the south-west.","questions":["At Okhyeon in south-eastern Korea a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives with its irrigation channels and dikes, and with human footprints pressed into the dried mud.","Rice reached Korea long after millet, and building a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needed water control that reshaped how Bronze Age villages were organised."],"answer":"paddy field","answerText":"paddy field","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rice in Korea from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fields excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","korea","asia","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","technology"],"abstract":"A <b>paddy field</b> is a levelled, banked plot that can be flooded to grow rice, and the appearance of such fields in Korea marks the arrival of the crop that would become the country's staple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Millet had been grown since about 3500 BCE, but by 1000 BCE, in the Early Mumun period, rice was being cultivated in several regions of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Namgyeong near Pyongyang, carbonised rice, foxtail millet, sorghum and beans are dated to about that time, and the rice there is identifiable as the short-grained <i>japonica</i> type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Hunam-ri in west-central Korea, carbonised <i>japonica</i> rice, foxtail millet, barley and sorghum are dated to about 1000 to 900 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice never arrived alone: Bronze Age sites yield wheat, barley, millets, beans, peas and perilla beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The fields themselves survive from the Middle Mumun period, between about 700 and 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Majeon-ni in the south-west has yielded a paddy field with an irrigation channel, water-control dikes and a reservoir; Gwanchang-ni nearby has fields and channels; and at Okhyeon in the south-east there are fields, channels, dikes and human footprints pressed into dried mud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Songgung-ni, a large site in the same region, produced 395 grams of carbonised <i>japonica</i> rice with radiocarbon dates of about 2665 and 2565 years before present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice and millet were both domesticated in China, rice appearing in the lower Yangtze valley several thousand years before it reached Korea, so both are introductions, and whether the peninsula's rice farmers descended from its Neolithic millet growers or arrived from outside is disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rice did not immediately dominate what people ate: amino-acid isotopes from Mumun-period human bone put C4 plants, which is to say the millets, at the centre of the diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Kyungcheol Choy, Hee Young Yun, Benjamin T. Fuller and Marcello A. Mannino, “Enhanced Dietary Reconstruction of Korean Prehistoric Populations by Combining δ13C and δ15N Amino Acids of Bone Collagen,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0300068, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0300068. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Sung-Mo Ahn, Jangsuk Kim and Jaehoon Hwang, “Sedentism, Settlements, and Radiocarbon Dates of Neolithic Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 113–43, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55549. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Korean_Rice_Paddy_Field.jpg/1280px-Korean_Rice_Paddy_Field.jpg","title":"A rice paddy in Korea","desc":"A modern flooded field; the earliest excavated Korean paddies date to the 1st millennium BCE.","credit":"Salamander724, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_Rice_Paddy_Field.jpg","alt":"Young rice plants standing in rows in a shallow flooded field."}},
{"id":"ko-029","num":29,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Also called the Mumun period after its plain pottery, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from about 1500 to 400 BCE and is when rice farming and dolmens appear.","questions":["Archaeologists divide the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into four subperiods, and some of them doubt whether the earliest of the four exists at all.","Villages move inland and coastal shell middens are abandoned during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is one sign of how completely farming reorganised life."],"answer":"Korean Bronze Age","answerText":"Korean Bronze Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rice from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","agriculture"],"abstract":"The <b>Korean Bronze Age</b>, also called the Mumun period after the plain undecorated pottery that runs through it, covers roughly 1500 to 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is divided into four subperiods — Incipient, Early, Middle and Late — distinguished by house forms, burial types, ceramics, bronzes and stone tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Incipient phase, dated about 1500 to 1300 BCE, rests on fewer than thirty sites, and some archaeologists deny that it exists as a phase at all, treating its material as a minor local element of the Early Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Early phase, about 1300 to 800 BCE, is defined by three regional pottery styles and by long rectangular pit dwellings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those styles have been read as two traditions moving south, from the north-east and the north-west, and meeting in the Han River basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The changes that came with the period were sweeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost every coastal shell midden occupied in the Neolithic was abandoned and villages moved inland; farming tools such as reaping knives, sickles, hoes and querns became common finds; and jade appears more often than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rice was being grown across the peninsula by about 1000 BCE alongside millets, barley, wheat, beans and perilla, and by the Middle phase there were banked paddy fields with irrigation channels and reservoirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Isotopes from human bone of the period nonetheless put C4 plants, the millets, at the centre of the diet, so rice did not displace the older crops at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Dolmen burials appear for the first time and Korea came to hold among the highest densities of them in the world, which together with the rest is read as a more unequal and more organised society than the one before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Kyungcheol Choy, Hee Young Yun, Benjamin T. Fuller and Marcello A. Mannino, “Enhanced Dietary Reconstruction of Korean Prehistoric Populations by Combining δ13C and δ15N Amino Acids of Bone Collagen,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0300068, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0300068. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Example_of_a_northern-style_dolmen_at_Ganghwa_Island.jpg/1280px-Example_of_a_northern-style_dolmen_at_Ganghwa_Island.jpg","title":"A northern-style dolmen on Ganghwa Island","desc":"Dolmen burials appear in Korea with the Bronze Age, and the peninsula holds among the highest densities in the world.","credit":"Taewangkorea, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Example_of_a_northern-style_dolmen_at_Ganghwa_Island.jpg","alt":"A large flat capstone resting on two upright slabs in a grassed enclosure."}},
{"id":"ko-030","num":30,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"The Korean Bronze Age is named for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Korean name means without pattern and which is almost always found alongside farming tools.","questions":["Regional styles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are told apart by their rims — perforated holes, doubled rims, short slanted lines — and each goes with its own house plan.","Where the comb-patterned wares of the Neolithic are decorated all over, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is plain and coarse, and its arrival marks the start of a new age."],"answer":"Mumun pottery","answerText":"Mumun pottery","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["object","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Mumun pottery</b> is the plain coarse ware of the Korean Bronze Age, and its Korean name means simply without pattern, in contrast to the comb-patterned vessels of the Neolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its introduction is taken to mark the beginning of the Bronze Age in Korea, from about 1500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is almost always found together with farming equipment — semi-lunar reaping knives, stone sickles, hoes, querns and digging sticks — which is part of why a change in pottery is read as a change in how people lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Incipient phase is marked by deep bowls with notched clay bands applied below the rim, found with rectangular or square houses whose hearths were ringed with stone slabs or gravel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fewer than thirty sites of that phase are known, which is why some archaeologists doubt it is a phase at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In the Early Bronze Age three regional styles are distinguished, and each is defined by what was done to the rim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yeoksam-dong vessels carry lines of perforated holes; Garak-dong vessels have doubled rims and short slanted strokes; Heunam-ri vessels look like the first but carry the second's motifs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each style is associated with its own dwelling plan and stone-tool assemblage, so the pottery is used to map how material culture moved and where populations met.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lipid residues absorbed into vessels of this period show broomcorn millet being cooked in Korea and not in contemporary pots from northern Kyushu, although millet was grown there too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because so little else survives in the peninsula's acid soils, this pottery carries much of the weight of Bronze Age chronology and of the argument about where its makers came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Oliver E. Craig, Jasmine Lundy, Manon Bondetti, Sesha Nicholson-Lailey, Natsuki Murakami, Miho Suzuki, Chris Stevens and Alexandre Lucquin, “Lipid Residue Analysis Reveals Divergent Culinary Practices in Japan and Korea at the Dawn of Intensive Agriculture,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 30 (2025): e2504414122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12318219/. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Perforated_Mumun_Pottery.National_Museum_of_Korea..jpg/1280px-Perforated_Mumun_Pottery.National_Museum_of_Korea..jpg","title":"Mumun pottery with a perforated rim","desc":"A plain coarse vessel with a line of holes below the rim, the Yeoksam-dong style of the Early Bronze Age.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perforated_Mumun_Pottery.National_Museum_of_Korea..jpg","alt":"A tall undecorated ceramic jar with a row of small holes pierced just below its rim."}},
{"id":"ko-031","num":31,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"At <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the south, greenstone ornaments and long polished daggers were being made by specialists as social differences widened after 700 BCE.","questions":["The excavated settlement of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is where Korean archaeologists trace the growth of craft production alongside the spread of intensive farming.","Large communal granaries at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood one to every four or five dwellings, so harvests were being stored for the group rather than the household."],"answer":"Daepyeong","answerText":"Daepyeong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Site in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Craft phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 850 – 550 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","practice"],"abstract":"<b>Daepyeong</b> is a large Bronze Age settlement in south-central Korea, near Jinju, and it is the site on which arguments about how Korean society became unequal mostly rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Specialised production of greenstone ornaments, ground stone daggers, red-burnished pottery and bronze objects there was contemporary with the onset of intensive agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Early Middle Mumun, about 850 to 700 BCE, that craft work appears to have begun as a supplement to farming, at a time when differences between community members were played down and few prestige objects were consumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Late Middle Mumun, about 700 to 550 BCE, social differences widen sharply, and the evidence suggests full-time leaders used the making and giving of greenstone ornaments and long ground stone daggers to build followings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Craft and inequality grow together rather than one plainly causing the other. <br><br> The settlement is also one of the places where Songgungni-type farming villages can be seen at work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Large above-ground storage buildings raised on posts stood among the houses, and at Daepyeong there was roughly one such granary for every four or five dwellings, which points to harvests being held communally rather than house by house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Similar buildings are known from Yeongdeung-dong, where five stood together in an open space ringed by houses, and from Jangcheon-ni, where one served twelve residences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across the peninsula the same period sees dolmens raised in enormous numbers, the great majority small and without grave goods, a few large and richly furnished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether such monuments are best read as claims to land, as displays by emerging leaders, or as something else is still argued, and the spatial work needed to settle it has not been done; much of what is known about sites of this period comes from salvage excavation ahead of large dam and construction projects rather than from research digs chosen in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Martin T. Bale and Min-jung Ko, “Craft Production and Social Change in Mumun Pottery Period Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 45, no. 2 (2006): 159–87, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17250. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Korea.Bronze_Age-Jinju-Pot-02.jpg/1280px-Korea.Bronze_Age-Jinju-Pot-02.jpg","title":"A Bronze Age jar from Jinju","desc":"Red-burnished pottery of the kind produced at Daepyeong, near Jinju, and found in graves across the south.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea.Bronze_Age-Jinju-Pot-02.jpg","alt":"A large round-bodied earthenware jar with a short neck, standing on a museum mount."}},
{"id":"ko-033","num":33,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Tens of thousands of Bronze Age graves of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> type are known in Korea, most of them small, undecorated and holding nothing at all.","questions":["South Jeolla Province alone has more than 19,000 examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is among the densest concentrations of such monuments anywhere.","Most Korean examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were raised in the transition from the Early to the Middle Bronze Age, and a few of them are very large indeed."],"answer":"dolmen","answerText":"dolmen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["building","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory"],"abstract":"A <b>dolmen</b> is a burial monument built from large stones, and in Korea it is the characteristic grave of the Bronze Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tens of thousands are known across the peninsula, with more than 19,000 identified in South Jeolla Province in the south-west alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The great majority are very small and contain no grave goods, and their sheer numbers show them to be the graves of ordinary people, perhaps the heads of families or lineages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few are impressively large, and both their scale and their contents mark them as the graves of people of high standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two forms are distinguished, a northern table type in which the capstone rests on upright slabs and a southern go-board type in which it sits on low supports over a buried chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Recent work suggests most Korean dolmens were built in the transition from the Early to the Middle Bronze Age, which narrows their span considerably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they meant is less settled: one suggestion is that they were territorial markers by which farming communities claimed land, but that argument rests on distribution maps rather than on spatial analysis and has not been tested against the evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Dolmens appear alongside stone cists, slab-covered pit graves and jar burials, all of which are far less common.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because so many stand in the open and are so plainly monuments, they have carried more than their share of national argument, and Korean archaeologists have long written about how their own field reads them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are also the reason the Bronze Age is read as a more unequal society than the Neolithic before it, and three of the densest concentrations, at Gochang, Hwasun and Ganghwa, were inscribed together on the World Heritage List in 2000, though hundreds of others nearby fall outside that protection and many have been damaged or destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Minjae Zoh, Woojin Shim and Ilhong Ko, “Digital Restoration of the Heritage Landscape through a GIS-Based Approach: Case Study of the Gochang Dolmen Site in South Korea,” <i>The International Archives of the Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing and Spatial Information Sciences</i> XLVIII-M-9-2025 (2025): 1729–34, https://doi.org/10.5194/isprs-archives-XLVIII-M-9-2025-1729-2025. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Example_of_a_southern-style_dolmen_at_Ganghwa_Island.jpg/1280px-Example_of_a_southern-style_dolmen_at_Ganghwa_Island.jpg","title":"A southern-style dolmen on Ganghwa Island","desc":"A capstone resting on low supports, the commonest of the Korean dolmen forms.","credit":"Taewangkorea, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Example_of_a_southern-style_dolmen_at_Ganghwa_Island.jpg","alt":"A broad flat stone slab supported on several upright stones in a field of grass."}},
{"id":"ko-034","num":34,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"The dolmen clusters at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with those at Hwasun and Ganghwa, went on the World Heritage list in 2000 as a single inscribed property.","questions":["Hundreds of dolmens around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fall outside the inscribed boundary, and many of those have been damaged, moved or destroyed outright.","Mapping what has been lost around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an argument that heritage protection favours monuments that are still standing and still impressive."],"answer":"Gochang","answerText":"Gochang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dolmens built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","concept"],"abstract":"<b>Gochang</b>, in North Jeolla Province, holds one of the three Korean dolmen concentrations inscribed together on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2000, the others being at Hwasun and on Ganghwa Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inscription covers the officially recognised clusters, and conservation effort follows the inscription.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Archival and historical records show hundreds of further dolmens around Gochang that lie outside it, many of them damaged, displaced or gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several contemporary Bronze Age settlements in the same area have been excavated and no longer exist above ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Recent work has used geographical information systems to put those lost and undesignated monuments back onto a map of the region, so that the surviving ones can be read as part of a landscape rather than as isolated objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The case is made as a general argument about heritage as much as a study of one place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> World Heritage designation, its authors point out, tends to privilege what is intact and visually striking, so what has been destroyed or never properly recorded falls outside the framework and depends on national law instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> South Korea's Framework Act on Cultural Heritage, in force since 2022, widens the legal definition to include undesignated and spatially diffuse heritage and encourages digital documentation, but practice still favours what can be seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale of what is at stake is clear from the wider count: more than 19,000 dolmens are recorded in South Jeolla Province alone, and tens of thousands across the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korea holds among the highest densities of dolmens anywhere in the world, which is part of why they have been studied as evidence for how monument building, ideology and emerging elites are connected, and how Korean archaeology chooses to present them to the public has itself been debated within the field for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Minjae Zoh, Woojin Shim and Ilhong Ko, “Digital Restoration of the Heritage Landscape through a GIS-Based Approach: Case Study of the Gochang Dolmen Site in South Korea,” <i>The International Archives of the Photogrammetry, Remote Sensing and Spatial Information Sciences</i> XLVIII-M-9-2025 (2025): 1729–34, https://doi.org/10.5194/isprs-archives-XLVIII-M-9-2025-1729-2025. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Gochang_Dolmens%2C_South_Korea.jpg/1280px-Gochang_Dolmens%2C_South_Korea.jpg","title":"Dolmens at Gochang","desc":"Part of one of the three Korean dolmen concentrations inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2000.","credit":"Lance Vanlewen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gochang_Dolmens%2C_South_Korea.jpg","alt":"A large reddish capstone on stone supports, lit from below within a bamboo enclosure."}},
{"id":"ko-035","num":35,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Far rarer than dolmens, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a slab-lined grave, and the richest Korean Bronze Age burials found so far are of this kind.","questions":["Bronze daggers, polished stone daggers, arrowheads and tubular beads of semiprecious stone come mostly from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than from megalithic graves.","Korean Bronze Age burial ran to several forms, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one whose contents show most clearly that some people were being singled out."],"answer":"stone cist","answerText":"stone cist","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","practice"],"abstract":"A <b>stone cist</b> is a grave lined and covered with stone slabs, and in Bronze Age Korea it is one of several burial forms used alongside the far more numerous dolmens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The others are slab-covered pit graves and jar burials, and all three are much less common than dolmen graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes the stone cist important is not its frequency but its contents: the richest Korean Bronze Age burials known are of this type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cist graves such as those at Songgungni have yielded bronze daggers, polished stone daggers and arrowheads, other bronze objects, and tubular beads of semiprecious stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dolmens, by contrast, are mostly empty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two forms are often found together, which is one reason their relationship has been argued over rather than settled; whether they represent different social groups, different periods or different kinds of person is not agreed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a stone slab tomb of this kind, found by accident with a Liaoning-type bronze dagger in it, that led to the excavation of Songgungni and so to the naming of a whole Bronze Age assemblage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Grave goods of polished stone are not incidental: long ground stone daggers were made by specialists and appear to have been used by leaders to build followings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Burial is therefore where Korean Bronze Age inequality is most visible, because settlements of the period differ from one another far less than graves do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of these burials were made between about 1000 and 400 BCE, in the same centuries when dolmens were going up in tens of thousands across the peninsula, so the two forms are contemporaries rather than successive fashions, and the difference between them is one of who was buried in which.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Martin T. Bale and Min-jung Ko, “Craft Production and Social Change in Mumun Pottery Period Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 45, no. 2 (2006): 159–87, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17250. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-036","num":36,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Excavated after a chance find in 1975, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives its name to the assemblage that spread across central and southern Korea after 800 BCE.","questions":["Dwellings, pottery and stone tools of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> type define the Middle Bronze Age, and southern Korea was saturated with them.","The rice, the granaries and the richest cist burials of Bronze Age Korea all come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the sites that share its material."],"answer":"Songgungni","answerText":"Songgungni","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Type site of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","agriculture"],"abstract":"<b>Songgungni</b> is a Bronze Age site at Buyeo in south-western Korea, and its name is attached to the assemblage that defines the Korean Middle Bronze Age, roughly 800 to 400 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Investigation began with the accidental discovery in 1975 of a stone slab tomb containing a Liaoning-type bronze dagger; excavation followed, and over the next four decades sites with the same material were found across central and southern Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The assemblage is a package: a distinctive round dwelling with a central pit, a distinctive pottery, and stone tools including triangular reaping knives, grooved stones and arrowheads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the later Mumun period southern Korea was saturated with dwellings of this type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Large above-ground storage buildings raised on posts stand among the houses at such settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Songgungni itself produced 395 grams of carbonised <i>japonica</i> rice, with radiocarbon dates of about 2665 and 2565 years before present, which is among the clearest evidence anywhere in Korea that rice was being grown at scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its stone cist graves are among the richest Bronze Age burials known in Korea, holding bronze and polished stone daggers, arrowheads and beads of semiprecious stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The spread of the assemblage across central and southern Korea is usually explained either by the movement of people or by the movement of ideas out of the south-west, and the mechanism is argued over as much as the dating is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Craft specialisation in greenstone ornaments and long ground stone daggers belongs to the same centuries and to the same widening of social differences, with full-time leaders apparently using such objects to build followings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Songgungni-type material also reaches northern Kyushu in Japan, which is part of why the Korean Bronze Age matters to the archaeology of the Japanese islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Martin T. Bale and Min-jung Ko, “Craft Production and Social Change in Mumun Pottery Period Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 45, no. 2 (2006): 159–87, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17250. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Stone_Dagger.Bronze_Age_Gojoseon.Songguk-ri%2C_Buyeo.jpg/1280px-Stone_Dagger.Bronze_Age_Gojoseon.Songguk-ri%2C_Buyeo.jpg","title":"A polished stone dagger from Songgungni","desc":"The ground stone dagger found in the slab tomb at Songguk-ri, Buyeo, in 1975.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stone_Dagger.Bronze_Age_Gojoseon.Songguk-ri%2C_Buyeo.jpg","alt":"A long polished stone blade with a flared hilt, displayed upright against a plain background."}},
{"id":"ko-037","num":37,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Iron reached Korea about 300 BCE as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> distributed by the northern Chinese state of Yan, and axes are among the commonest early finds.","questions":["Korean archaeologists date the Early Iron Age from the arrival of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, although mass production of iron objects did not follow for some time.","Because it was poured into moulds rather than hammered, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made tools that could be turned out in numbers but that shattered rather than bending."],"answer":"cast iron","answerText":"cast iron","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Korea from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","korea","asia","technology","archaeology","prehistory"],"abstract":"<b>Cast iron</b> is iron melted and poured into a mould rather than heated and hammered into shape, and it is the form in which iron first reached Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korean archaeologists define the Early Iron Age as the period from 300 to 100 BCE during which cast ironware was distributed by the Yan, a state in what is now north-eastern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cast iron axes are among the commonest early objects, alongside sickles and engraving tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ironware is the material marker of the phase, but mass production of iron objects was not achieved during it, and how much iron was actually in use is easy to overstate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The technique had been developed in China several centuries earlier and arrived in Korea already worked out. <br><br> The arrival was uneven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ironware appeared first in the north-west and around the Geum River, and only later in the south-east, which is one reason the two-hundred-year Early Iron Age is treated as a period of introduction rather than of iron working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Full-scale production in south-eastern Korea began only after the Han Chinese commanderies were installed, which many take as the real beginning of an iron age across the whole peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From there iron became the basis of Korean production and export: by the early centuries of the common era, iron ingots, weapons, armour and tools made in the south were reaching the Japanese islands in quantity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iron tools also changed farming, and it is the increase in agricultural productivity that archaeologists connect to the appearance of more complex societies, though how much weight to give the Chinese written accounts of these centuries against the excavated evidence remains a standing question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Martin T. Bale and Min-jung Ko, “Craft Production and Social Change in Mumun Pottery Period Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 45, no. 2 (2006): 159–87, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17250. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Early_Iron_Age%2C_Knife_and_sickle._National_Museum_of_Korea..jpg/1280px-Early_Iron_Age%2C_Knife_and_sickle._National_Museum_of_Korea..jpg","title":"Iron knife and sickle from Korea","desc":"Early Iron Age ironwork in the National Museum of Korea; the first iron on the peninsula arrived as cast objects.","credit":"Prof. Gary Lee Todd, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Iron_Age%2C_Knife_and_sickle._National_Museum_of_Korea..jpg","alt":"A dark iron knife blade and a curved sickle blade laid on a white museum surface."}},
{"id":"ko-038","num":38,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Running from 300 to 100 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Korea is defined by clay-band-rim pottery and the slender bronze dagger as much as by iron itself.","questions":["Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> deserves to be a period at all is disputed, since two centuries is short beside Korea's other archaeological divisions.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits between the Bronze Age and the proto-Three Kingdoms, and archaeologists disagree about whether to name it for its metal or for its states."],"answer":"Early Iron Age","answerText":"Early Iron Age","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">300 – 100 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["era","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","concept"],"abstract":"The <b>Early Iron Age</b> is the Korean archaeological period from 300 to 100 BCE, defined as the time during which cast ironware distributed by the Chinese state of Yan reached the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits between the Bronze Age and the proto-Three Kingdoms period, and it matters because it is the transition from prehistory to a past with written records in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its material markers are not only iron: the period is identified above all by Jeomtodae pottery, whose rims carry an applied clay band, and by the Korean-style slender bronze dagger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Round-sectioned clay bands come first and triangular ones follow from about 200 BCE, though both traditions run on past the period's end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its cultural character, its area of origin and its relationship to earlier local cultures have all yet to be explained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The name is contested, and the argument is about what a period is for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One position is that the whole six hundred years from 300 BCE to 300 CE should be called the Iron Age, divided into an early phase of introduction in the north and a later phase of full production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another prefers historical names — the Three Han states, the proto-Three Kingdoms — which describe polities rather than materials, and which apply only to the southern part of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A two-hundred-year Early Iron Age is also short beside Korea's other periods, which is a further objection to treating it as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the dispute lies a harder question about how much weight to give Chinese written accounts of Korea against what the ground actually contains, and Korea's other period divisions are argued over in much the same terms; by the end of the period iron was being produced in quantity in the south, and within a few centuries Korean iron was reaching the Japanese islands as ingots, weapons and tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Early_Iron_Age%2C_Korea._Pottery_with_Clay_Stripes._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg/1280px-Early_Iron_Age%2C_Korea._Pottery_with_Clay_Stripes._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg","title":"Early Iron Age pottery from Korea","desc":"A jar with applied clay stripes, Buyeo National Museum, of the kind that defines the period's assemblages.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Iron_Age%2C_Korea._Pottery_with_Clay_Stripes._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg","alt":"A tall reddish-brown earthenware jar with a wide mouth, standing on a white plinth."}},
{"id":"ko-040","num":40,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Korea's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is concentrated on the rivers of the south-east, where carved panels show whales, boats, deer and hunters in fine anatomical detail.","questions":["Whether Korean <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made by Neolithic fishers or by Bronze Age farmers has been argued for decades, and the panels cannot be dated directly.","One Korean panel of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows a boat rowed by about twenty men with a hunter in it throwing a harpoon at a whale."],"answer":"rock art","answerText":"rock art","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 4000 – 500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","art"],"abstract":"<b>Rock art</b> is imagery carved or painted onto natural rock surfaces, and Korea's is concentrated in the south-east of the peninsula, on the rivers that drain to the East Sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best known panel is at Bangudae, on a broad rock ledge beside the lower Taehwa River, where hundreds of figures are carved into the stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of them show whales being hunted from boats: in one scene a substantial boat rowed by about twenty men carries a hunter throwing a spear or harpoon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A number of the whales are rendered in good anatomical detail, closely enough that species have been proposed for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deer, other land animals, boats and human figures share the same surfaces. <br><br> Dating is the difficulty, because a carving cannot be dated directly and these panels lie on open rock.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One argument places them in the Bronze Age, on the grounds that no whaling equipment appears in local Neolithic shell middens and that only log canoes and barges are thought to have existed earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another places them in the Late Neolithic, reading them as the record of hunter-fisher-gatherer life and as part of a fertility ritual, and notes that rising sea level in the Neolithic brought the sea up the Taehwa valley to the panel itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Internationally the Korean panels are cited among the earliest possible evidence for whale hunting anywhere, perhaps six thousand years old, with the caveat that their dates and cultural context remain uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How such material should be read has itself been a recurring question in Korean archaeology, and the panels are among the few classes of Korean prehistoric evidence that speak about belief rather than about subsistence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Krista McGrath, Tatiane Andaluzia Kuss da Silveira Montes, Thiago Fossile, Dione da Rocha Bandeira, Fernanda Mara Borba, Marta J. Cremer, Laura G. van der Sluis and Thomas Higham, “Molecular and Zooarchaeological Identification of 5000 Year Old Whale-Bone Harpoons in Coastal Brazil,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 48, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-67530-w. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/Amlou2518_%EC%9A%B8%EC%A3%BC_%EC%B2%9C%EC%A0%84%EB%A6%AC_%EA%B0%81%EC%84%9D.jpg","title":"The engraved rock at Cheonjeon-ri","desc":"A sloping rock face in Ulju carrying carved figures and later inscriptions, upstream of Bangudae.","credit":"Amlou2518, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amlou2518_%EC%9A%B8%EC%A3%BC_%EC%B2%9C%EC%A0%84%EB%A6%AC_%EA%B0%81%EC%84%9D.jpg","alt":"A large tilted sandstone slab set into a hillside beside a paved path, its surface weathered and streaked."}},
{"id":"ko-032","num":32,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Korea's first bronze weapon was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, called lute-shaped in Korean for its resemblance to a traditional stringed instrument.","questions":["Bronze reached Korea between 800 and 700 BCE in forms linked to Manchuria, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the marker of the Early Bronze Age.","A stone slab tomb containing a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the accidental discovery that opened the excavation of Songgungni in the 1970s."],"answer":"Liaoning bronze dagger","answerText":"Liaoning bronze dagger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 – 400 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["object","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","technology"],"abstract":"The <b>Liaoning bronze dagger</b> is the earliest bronze weapon found in Korea, and Korean archaeologists call it <i>bipa</i>-shaped, after a traditional stringed instrument whose waisted outline the blade resembles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bronze artefacts first appear on the peninsula between 800 and 700 BCE, during the Early Mumun period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their forms link them to the bronzes of the Liaoning peninsula in south-western Manchuria, which differ in origin, typology and use from contemporary Chinese bronzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese assemblages of the time are dominated by ritual vessels; the Liaoning repertoire is daggers, spears, arrowheads, chisels and fan-shaped adzes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> These daggers are the marker of Korea's Early Bronze Age. <br><br> They are found in graves rather than in houses, and the richest are stone cist burials rather than the far commoner dolmens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A stone slab tomb containing one of them, found by accident in 1975, is what led archaeologists to excavate Songgungni, the site that gives its name to the whole later Bronze Age assemblage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The type was succeeded from the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE by the Korean-style slender bronze dagger, a straight blade with a separate handle, named to distinguish it both from the earlier lute-shaped form and from slender daggers found in Liaoning itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bronze spears, axes, chisels, mirrors and, from the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, bells were made alongside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The metal and the forms behind it both arrived from outside, which is why the daggers are read as evidence of contact with the mainland as much as of craft at home; long polished stone daggers were made alongside them by specialists, and both kinds appear to have been used by leaders to gather and hold followings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Martin T. Bale and Min-jung Ko, “Craft Production and Social Change in Mumun Pottery Period Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 45, no. 2 (2006): 159–87, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17250. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Liaoning-type_bronze_dagger.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Liaoning-type_bronze_dagger.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"A Liaoning-type bronze dagger","desc":"The waisted blade Korean archaeologists call bipa-shaped, after a stringed instrument, in the National Museum of Korea.","credit":"Prof. Gary Lee Todd, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liaoning-type_bronze_dagger.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"A corroded bronze dagger blade with a narrow tip and two rounded bulges near its base, mounted upright."}},
{"id":"ko-039","num":39,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Because Korean soils destroy bone, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is where most surviving evidence of Neolithic diet, fishing and burial on the peninsula comes from.","questions":["Along the southern coast the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins to appear from about 8,000 years ago, and some of them contain dwellings and graves as well as refuse.","Bird, fish and deep-sea mammal bones in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show that Neolithic Koreans were working well out from the shore."],"answer":"shell midden","answerText":"shell midden","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 6000 – 1500 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","korea","asia","archaeology","prehistory","nature"],"abstract":"A <b>shell midden</b> is an accumulated deposit of discarded shells and other refuse, and in Korea it is the single most informative kind of prehistoric site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason is chemical: the peninsula's soils are acidic enough that bone rarely survives at open-air sites, while the shells in a midden keep the ground alkaline and preserve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Middens begin to appear along the southern coast in the Early Neolithic, from about 8,000 years ago, at sites such as Sangnodaedo, in the same centuries as the first sedentary villages of pit houses inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some contain the remains of dwellings, as at Tongsam-dong, and some contain burials: seventeen were found at Yondaedo, furnished with pottery, beads and fishing tackle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are middens, but they are also settlements and cemeteries. <br><br> What is in them changed how Korean prehistory is understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Alongside the first evidence of shellfish use in Korea, they contain the bones of birds, fish and deep-sea mammals, and composite bone fishhooks and harpoons, which together show people working well out from the shore rather than merely gathering along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbonised foxtail and broomcorn millet from the Tongsam-dong midden is among the earliest evidence of farming on the peninsula, so the same deposits record both fishing and cultivation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Isotopes read from human bone preserved in such conditions show mixed diets, some communities drawing heavily on the sea and others on the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost every coastal midden was abandoned at the start of the Bronze Age, when villages moved inland to farm, which is one of the sharpest changes in the whole Korean sequence; because the coastal and inland records preserve such different things, comparing Neolithic and Bronze Age land use is harder than it looks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher J. Norton, “Sedentism, Territorial Circumscription, and the Increased Use of Plant Domesticates across Neolithic–Bronze Age Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 1 (2007): 133–65, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17261. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Pierre Guyomarc'h, “Potential Contributions of Korean Pleistocene Hominin Fossils to Palaeoanthropology: A View from Ryonggok Cave,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 31–57, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55546. [Open access]","Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Kyungcheol Choy, Hee Young Yun, Benjamin T. Fuller and Marcello A. Mannino, “Enhanced Dietary Reconstruction of Korean Prehistoric Populations by Combining δ13C and δ15N Amino Acids of Bone Collagen,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 3 (2024): e0300068, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0300068. [Open access]","Bumcheol Kim, “Socioeconomic Development in the Bronze Age: Archaeological Understanding of the Transition from the Early to Middle Bronze Age, South Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 144–84, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55550. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Dongsam-dong_Shell_Midden.jpg/1280px-Dongsam-dong_Shell_Midden.jpg","title":"The Dongsam-dong shell midden","desc":"The coastal site in Busan, one of the longest-occupied shell middens on the southern shore.","credit":"Donggeon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dongsam-dong_Shell_Midden.jpg","alt":"A grassed coastal park above a harbour, with a wooded hill and a town beyond the water."}},
{"id":"ko-041","num":41,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Whales, boats and hunters are carved into a rock ledge above the lower Taehwa River at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the most important prehistoric image field anywhere on the Korean peninsula.","questions":["One boat carved at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is rowed by about twenty men with a hunter standing in it, spear raised, which makes the panel among the earliest whaling scenes known.","Whether the carvings at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were made by Neolithic fishers or by Bronze Age farmers has been argued for decades, and nothing in the rock itself can settle it."],"answer":"Bangudae","answerText":"Bangudae","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3500 – 1000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\"></span><span class=\"dt-v\">range disputed</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","korea","archaeology","art","prehistory","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Bangudae</b> is a rock panel on a broad ledge beside the lower Taehwa River in south-eastern Korea, a river that reaches the East Sea some distance north of Busan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its carved surface is the most important prehistoric image field on the peninsula, and some of its figures show whales being hunted from boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One substantial boat is rowed by about twenty men, and in it stands a hunter throwing a spear or harpoon at a whale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A number of the whales are executed in good anatomical detail, enough for the species to be argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hunting of that kind could only have been carried out by organised and skilled teams, so the panel is read for what it says about society and not only about food, which is the holistic approach Korean prehistoric archaeology has moved towards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When the carvings were made is disputed, and it cannot be settled by measuring them, because dating petroglyphs remains a challenge everywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some argue for a Bronze Age origin, on the grounds that no whaling gear turns up in local Neolithic shell middens and that Neolithic people had only barges and hollowed-out log canoes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others read the scenes as the economic life of Neolithic hunter-fishers, and hold that a higher sea made the valley near the site part of the East Sea, so that whales could be driven into a killing ground by several small boats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Elsewhere a chronology has been proposed for boat carvings by matching their shapes against boats known from other regions, which dates a panel without touching it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Bangudae is one of the sites taken to show people hunting whales as much as 6,000 years ago, and some of its odd human figures have been read as shamans at a ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sook-Chung Shin, Song-Nai Rhee and C. Melvin Aikens, “Chulmun Neolithic Intensification, Complexity, and Emerging Agriculture in Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 68–109, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36659. [Open access]","Meinrat O. Andreae and Tracey W. Andreae, “Archaeometric Studies on Rock Art at Four Sites in the Northeastern Great Basin of North America,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 1 (2022): e0263189, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0263189. [Open access]","Marta Díaz-Guardamino, Boel Bengtsson, Ellie Newton, Ana M. S. Bettencourt, Johan Ling, Juan Latorre-Ruiz, Hugo A. Sampaio and Diogo Marinho, “Boats on the Rocks: Late Prehistoric Nautical Iconography and Landscape, from Northwest Iberia to Scandinavia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 6 (2026): e0349417, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0349417. [Open access]","Krista McGrath, Tatiane Andaluzia Kuss da Silveira Montes, Thiago Fossile, Dione da Rocha Bandeira, Fernanda Mara Borba, Marta J. Cremer, Laura G. van der Sluis and Thomas Higham, “Molecular and Zooarchaeological Identification of 5000 Year Old Whale-Bone Harpoons in Coastal Brazil,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 48, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-67530-w. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Bangudae_Petroglyphs_%28replica%29_IMG_20161005_093305.jpg/1280px-Bangudae_Petroglyphs_%28replica%29_IMG_20161005_093305.jpg","title":"The Bangudae panel, in replica","desc":"A full-size reproduction in the Gyeongju National Museum, on which the whales, boats and figures of the original read clearly.","credit":"G41rn8, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangudae_Petroglyphs_%28replica%29_IMG_20161005_093305.jpg","alt":"A grey slab covered in shallow engraved outlines of animals, boats and human figures."}},
{"id":"ko-042","num":42,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Korean prehistory has never been studied on its own, because the peninsula sits inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its pottery, metals and languages are argued about alongside Manchuria, Japan and the Russian Far East.","questions":["The comparison that Korean archaeologists draw has widened across <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and beyond it, reaching the Mongolian steppe, the Primorye and Siberia in the space of a few decades.","Rice, bronze and iron all reached the peninsula from elsewhere in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and much of what Korea then made travelled on to the Japanese archipelago."],"answer":"Northeast Asia","answerText":"Northeast Asia","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","korea","geography","archaeology","prehistory","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Northeast Asia</b> is the region Korean prehistory belongs to: the peninsula itself, the north-eastern provinces of China, the Russian Far East and the Japanese archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relationship among those areas has been a topic of continued interest since Korean archaeology began, and attention to the sites and artefacts of north-eastern China and of Japan has grown so much over the past few decades that it is no longer comparable with what came before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The area of comparison has widened further still, to the Mongolian steppe, the Russian Primorye and Siberia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not a fashion but a consequence of the evidence, since almost nothing that matters in Korean prehistory begins or ends at a modern border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peninsula is a corridor as well as a place, and the traffic ran in both directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Cast iron reached Korea in the 3rd century BCE through the Chinese state of Yan, and Korean archaeologists date the start of their Early Iron Age by that arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wet rice farming, bronze, iron and the people who knew how to use them crossed in the other direction to Japan, where they underlie the growth of a farming society and eventually of a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Languages have been fitted to the same map, with one large study placing the origins of the Transeurasian family in the West Liao River region and tracing its spread with millet farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That reconstruction is contested, and the archaeology, the genetics and the linguistics do not everywhere agree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the region offers is not a single story of origin but a set of connections that any account of early Korea has to place itself among.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Kazuo Miyamoto, “The Emergence of ‘Transeurasian’ Language Families in Northeast Asia as Viewed from Archaeological Evidence,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 4 (2022): e3, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2021.49. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-043","num":43,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Between the Liao River and the Yalu lies <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the corridor through which bronze, iron and armies reached Korea, and the ground a Yan general campaigned across about 300 BCE.","questions":["Korean archaeologists trace the slender bronze dagger of the peninsula back to the dagger culture of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the country beyond it, carried east by migrant groups.","The frontier that the Qin and then the Han empires tried to hold ran through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and where its outer fortifications stood is still not agreed."],"answer":"Liaodong","answerText":"Liaodong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yan campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Han frontier</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","korea","china","archaeology","geography","prehistory"],"abstract":"<b>Liaodong</b> is the country east of the Liao River, between that river and the Korean peninsula, and it is the ground across which almost everything early Korea received from the continent travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korean archaeologists date the start of their Early Iron Age to about 300 BCE, when a general of the Chinese state of Yan campaigned against Gojoseon and Yan material began to spread east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Korean-style slender bronze dagger is understood to have branched from the Liaoning bronze dagger culture of north-eastern China, carried by migrant groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iron itself arrived as cast objects distributed from Yan rather than as a local invention, and mass production followed only later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The region was not a corridor only: its own borderlands were reorganised in the same centuries, with new groupings of prestige goods marking a realignment of local power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Chinese coins turn up on both sides of that frontier, and knife-shaped bronze coins in the north-west of the peninsula have been connected with people moving east of the Liao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Refugees from Yan, Qi and Zhao are recorded settling on the western edge of Gojoseon, whose king allowed them ground of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Qin destroyed Yan the area was placed under what the sources call the Liaodong outer fortifications, and their exact position is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Early in the Han period the government pulled back to the older Yan line and made a river the border, judging the outer works too distant to hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> South of all this, the earliest iron in the Yŏngnam region of Korea arrives later still, which is a reminder that the peninsula did not receive any of it at one moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Ilhong Ko, “The Earliest Introduction of Iron into the Yŏngnam Region: A Critical Examination of the Evidence,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 1–36, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-1.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-044","num":44,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Wet rice, bronze, iron and the people who knew how to work them crossed from Korea to Japan at the opening of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which begins about 400 BCE.","questions":["Japanese farming, metalworking and eventually kingship are traced by many scholars to arrivals from the peninsula during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the centuries that followed it.","Millet was grown in Korea long before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, and it is the spread of that farming eastward that one large study ties to the spread of a language family."],"answer":"Yayoi period","answerText":"Yayoi period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 BCE – 300 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","japan","korea","archaeology","prehistory","migration","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Yayoi period</b> is the phase of Japanese prehistory that begins with the establishment of wet rice and mixed farming by about 400 BCE and runs to about 300 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its opening is the clearest case in East Asia of a way of life crossing a sea, and what crossed came from the Korean peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Agricultural, technological and organisational skills that carried Japan towards social complexity entered directly from Korea, and they moved with people rather than on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first immigrants crossed under pressure from population growth and social tension at home, driven by the rapid spread of farming in Korea; traders and artisans followed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In later centuries elite families fled across after military defeats, and their descendants served the Yamato court as officials, generals and technicians.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> On the Korean side the background is the Mumun period, when rice, beans, peas and other crops were established and settlements grew large enough to support craft production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Millet had been grown in Korea far earlier, and one large study of language and farming places the origin of the Transeurasian family in the West Liao River region and traces its dispersal with those crops into Korea and Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archaeological version of that argument sets out the routes and the timing by which agricultural populations moved through Northeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A linguistic account of the same problem puts the Koreanic dispersal alongside the farming spread rather than treating language as an independent story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of it is settled, and the relationship between the peninsula, the north-eastern provinces of China and the archipelago remains one of the most argued questions in the archaeology of the whole region, and one whose answers have shifted markedly in the past two decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Martine Robbeets, Remco Bouckaert, Matthew Conte, Alexander Savelyev, Tao Li, Deog-Im An et al., “Triangulation Supports Agricultural Spread of the Transeurasian Languages,” <i>Nature</i> 599, no. 7886 (2021): 616–21, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04108-8. [Open access]","Kazuo Miyamoto, “The Emergence of ‘Transeurasian’ Language Families in Northeast Asia as Viewed from Archaeological Evidence,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 4 (2022): e3, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2021.49. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson and Martine Robbeets, “Archaeolinguistic Evidence for the Farming/Language Dispersal of Koreanic,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e52, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.49. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/D%C5%8Dtaku_a_bronze_vessel_from_Yoshinogari_site.JPG/1280px-D%C5%8Dtaku_a_bronze_vessel_from_Yoshinogari_site.JPG","title":"A Yayoi bronze bell","desc":"A dotaku from the Yoshinogari site in Kyushu; bronze reached Japan with the farming that opens the period.","credit":"Pekachu, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C5%8Dtaku_a_bronze_vessel_from_Yoshinogari_site.JPG","alt":"A tall bronze bell with a looped handle and a ridged body, standing in a museum case."}},
{"id":"ko-045","num":45,"category":"Prehistoric Korea","question":"Much of what is said about early Korea rests on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a device invented in Europe and brought in under colonial rule, and too readily read as a real people.","questions":["When artefact types are found together and given a name, that name is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and reading it as the trace of a people is a step that has to be argued for.","A recent review of the peoples said to have divided central Korea found that the criteria assigning each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to one of them do not survive close reading."],"answer":"archaeological culture","answerText":"archaeological culture","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","korea","history","prehistory","science"],"abstract":"An <b>archaeological culture</b> is a recurring set of artefact types found together in one place and time, and given a name so that it can be compared with others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The concept was devised by European archaeologists early in the 20th century and was brought into Korean archaeology by Japanese colonial scholars at its beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because there is no way of reading the actors of the past directly off their artefacts, the named culture came to stand for past human behaviour in its entirety at a given place and time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The local culture is then readily treated as identical with a real people or ethnic group, differences between assemblages as ethnic or regional variation, and similarity across a distance as migration or diffusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That chain of inference is the one a reader should watch, because each link is a choice rather than an observation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The limitations are practical as well as logical: a culture cannot be measured or quantified, and anyone can see that two assemblages differ without being able to say how or why.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A critical review of the peoples said to have divided central Korea in the centuries around the start of the Common Era found the defining criteria unstable, with one tomb type dated two centuries later than the model required.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It recommended working instead from how objects were used and from institutions people shared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Korean archaeology has in fact moved well beyond typology and genealogy of types, towards environmental and experimental work and towards asking about ideology and ritual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reviews of the field have been saying for decades that the interpretations matter as much as the digging, and that they are where the argument really is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yongwook Yoo, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Alternative Approaches in the Korean Archaeology,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 1–4, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-046","num":46,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The earliest polity the Chinese records place on or beside the Korean peninsula is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, attacked by the state of Yan about 300 BCE and destroyed by the Han in 108 BCE.","questions":["A Yan refugee named Wiman seized power in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> about 195 BCE and turned it into the dominant trading partner of Han China in the region.","Korean archaeologists connect the slender bronze dagger of the peninsula with migrant groups from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though what the polity was and where it lay are both disputed."],"answer":"Gojoseon","answerText":"Gojoseon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yan campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wiman Chosŏn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">195 – 108 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","korea","history","archaeology","china","prehistory"],"abstract":"<b>Gojoseon</b> is the earliest political entity that the Chinese written record places on or beside the Korean peninsula, and almost everything said about it comes from those records rather than from its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese authors list it among the countries they believed existed in that direction, and whether archaeologists should recognise it as a state at all is an open question in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first firm date is about 300 BCE, when a general of the state of Yan campaigned against it and Yan material began to spread eastward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korean archaeologists connect the Korean-style slender bronze dagger with migrant groups from Gojoseon, which is one of the few links between the name and any object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its borderlands in Liaodong and southern Jilin were reorganised over the same centuries, and the polities named in the sources are hard to fix on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> About 195 BCE a refugee from Yan named Wiman took power, and the polity he headed lasted until 108 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A few thousand migrants from the Chinese central plains had already fled there, and its king had let them settle along his western border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wiman Chosŏn made itself the intermediary between Han China and the societies further south, and the Han coins found in its territory are the material trace of that trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Han emperor destroyed it and planted commanderies in its place, which is where the written history of the peninsula properly begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In modern Korea the name carries a second weight, since school histories written from the late 19th century onward opened their account of the country with Kojosŏn founded by Tan'gun in 2333 BCE, and taught that the Korean people descended from him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Korean-type_bronze_dagger_and_mirror.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Korean-type_bronze_dagger_and_mirror.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"A Korean-style bronze dagger and mirror","desc":"The slender dagger that Korean archaeologists connect with migrant groups from Gojoseon, shown with a bronze mirror.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean-type_bronze_dagger_and_mirror.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"A long narrow bronze blade mounted upright beside a small dark bronze disc."}},
{"id":"ko-047","num":47,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"A story that tells how a people or a state began, and is told to say who they are rather than what happened, is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Korea's is the story of Dangun.","questions":["In Korea's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a bear that fasted in a cave for 21 days becomes a woman, and her son by a god descended from heaven founds the first kingdom.","Because a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is usually written down long after the events it claims, dating the story is a different question from dating what it describes."],"answer":"foundation myth","answerText":"foundation myth","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","mythology","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"A <b>foundation myth</b> is a traditional story about how a people, a dynasty or a state began, and it is told to establish who its hearers are rather than to record what happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Myth is the oldest form of story human imagination has made, and it goes on being retold and put to new uses in each generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korea's is the story of Dangun, and Homer Hulbert set it down in English in 1906 as it was then told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A bear and a tiger on a mountainside wished to become human, and were told to eat garlic and fast in a cave for 21 days; the tiger could not endure it and came out, and the bear kept the whole term and stepped forth a woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Her son by Hwanung, himself the son of Hwanin, is the Dangun who is said to have begun to rule some 2,300 years before the Common Era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The chronology of such a story is not the chronology of the events in it, because the text that carries it was written long afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Korea's earliest surviving history, the <i>Samguk sagi</i>, was compiled in 1145 by a Koryŏ official and his colleagues, and even a compiler who claims only to transmit what he received leaves no way of dating when a given tale first appeared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is the standing difficulty with the material: the record of the story is firm and the age of the story is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a foundation myth does is easier to observe, since it is invoked most strongly at moments of crisis and rebuilt to fit them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Korean history textbooks written from the late 19th century opened with it, and it was through such books that the idea of a single people descended from one ancestor took its modern shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Yoonhee Hong, “Return of Myth, Myth Resources, and the Contemporaneity of Mythology in Korea and China Today,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 325–54, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-325.pdf. [Open access]","Tobias Scholl, “Ch'oe Namsŏn and Identity Construction through Negotiation with the Colonizer,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 153–86, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-153.pdf. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The Passing of Korea</i> (New York, 1906), 69–70, https://archive.org/details/passingofkorea00hulb. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Lupa_Capitolina%2C_Rome.jpg/1280px-Lupa_Capitolina%2C_Rome.jpg","title":"The she-wolf of Rome","desc":"Romulus and Remus at the wolf, the foundation myth of a city that told it about itself for a thousand years.","credit":"CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lupa_Capitolina%2C_Rome.jpg","alt":"A bronze statue of a standing wolf suckling two infant boys, on a plain pale ground."}},
{"id":"ko-048","num":48,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Korean tradition makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the founder of the first kingdom in 2333 BCE, a date that reaches print through texts written more than three thousand years after it.","questions":["The two works that carry the story of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were both written under the Mongol invasions of the 13th century, and neither is a record made at the time.","A religion founded in 1909 took <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the object of its faith, and a national holiday still marks the day the first kingdom is said to have begun."],"answer":"Dangun","answerText":"Dangun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional founding</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2333 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Written down</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13th century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","mythology","korea","history","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Dangun</b> is the figure Korean tradition names as the founder of the first kingdom, Gojoseon, conventionally in 2333 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What can be said about him with confidence is textual rather than archaeological: the story survives because two works of the 13th century wrote it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both were made while the Mongols were invading, which is the pattern the tradition repeats, the figure being reached for at moments of danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dynasty that followed took the name Chosŏn in order to claim descent from that first kingdom, and in 1456 a king formally designated Dangun the progenitor of Chosŏn, with rites at Pyongyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A monument on Ganghwa Island was described in 1906 as an altar he had built to worship his own divine father, its age then thought beyond question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The modern use of the figure is the better documented part of the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An era counted from 2333 BCE, a national foundation day, and a religion established in 1909 that made Dangun an object of faith all belong to the decades around the loss of independence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Textbooks of that period introduced him as the founder of Korea who lived in the time of a legendary Chinese king, and taught that Koreans were his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A colonial-era scholar built an account of Korean origins around him while negotiating what he could publish under Japanese rule, which is one reason the subject is difficult to write about calmly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Myth of this kind is still being drawn on today as cultural material, in Korea and elsewhere, and no archaeological finding has established either the 2333 BCE date or the existence of the person it is attached to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The Passing of Korea</i> (New York, 1906), 69–70, https://archive.org/details/passingofkorea00hulb. [Open access]","Tobias Scholl, “Ch'oe Namsŏn and Identity Construction through Negotiation with the Colonizer,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 153–86, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-153.pdf. [Open access]","Yoonhee Hong, “Return of Myth, Myth Resources, and the Contemporaneity of Mythology in Korea and China Today,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 325–54, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-325.pdf. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Portrait_of_Dangun.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_Dangun.jpg","title":"A portrait of Dangun","desc":"Painted by Chae Yong-sin in the decades when Dangun was being made the ancestor of a modern nation.","credit":"Chae Yong-sin (1850-1941), Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Dangun.jpg","alt":"A seated robed figure with a dark beard and a leafy headdress, facing forward in a painted portrait."}},
{"id":"ko-049","num":49,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Most of what is known about Gojoseon comes from one chapter of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Han history whose account of Chosŏn names Wiman, his followers and the war that ended the polity.","questions":["Chinese historians of the Han period had no interest in Korea for its own sake, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> describes the peninsula as a frontier problem and a trading partner.","Where the sources disagree with the archaeology of Korea, it is usually the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its successors that are being weighed against excavated material."],"answer":"Shiji","answerText":"Shiji","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chosŏn account</span><span class=\"dt-v\">chapter 115</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Events covered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">195 – 108 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","china","korea","history","literature","asia"],"abstract":"The <b><i>Shiji</i></b>, the <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i>, is the earliest of the Chinese standard histories and the principal written source for Gojoseon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chapter on Chosŏn records that Wiman was a refugee from the state of Yan, that a few thousand migrants from the central plains fled east, and that the ruler of Chosŏn let them settle along his western border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same work, with the <i>Sanguozhi</i> compiled in the 3rd century CE, is used to argue that the region held migrants from Yan, Qi and Zhao as well as local people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its chapter on the Xiongnu describes the seasonal gifts and border markets by which Han China traded with the powers around it, and the Chosŏn trade is read against that pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also states that after Qin destroyed Yan the territory passed to the Liaodong outer fortifications, and that the early Han drew back and made a river the boundary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> These are the only narrative sources of their period, and they were written from the far side of the frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chinese authors listed the countries they believed lay in that direction, and whether Korean archaeologists should adopt those names as units of their own has been an open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later Korean works add to the difficulty rather than resolving it, since a 12th-century compilation drawing on lost material cannot be dated claim by claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Documents recovered by excavation, such as the household register found in the ground of the Lelang commandery, are the one class of evidence that can be set beside the histories directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Read carefully and against the archaeology, the <i>Shiji</i> is indispensable; read on its own, it gives a frontier official's view of a place he never governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/%E3%80%8E%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98%E3%80%8F%E5%94%90%E5%86%99%E6%9C%AC.jpg/1280px-%E3%80%8E%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98%E3%80%8F%E5%94%90%E5%86%99%E6%9C%AC.jpg","title":"A Tang copy of Sima Qian's history","desc":"A copy made centuries after Sima Qian, and long before the printed editions the work is now read in.","credit":"unknown Tang copyist of Sima Qian, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E3%80%8E%E5%8F%B2%E8%A8%98%E3%80%8F%E5%94%90%E5%86%99%E6%9C%AC.jpg","alt":"A long sheet of aged paper covered in columns of brushed Chinese characters."}},
{"id":"ko-050","num":50,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The argument about where Gojoseon lay turns on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: some place the polity west of it in Liaoning, others in the Taedong basin around Pyongyang.","questions":["Chinese sources describe refugees and armies moving east of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the coins and daggers found on either side of it are read as evidence for both positions.","Liaodong means the country east of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the frontier the Qin and Han empires tried to hold ran somewhere across that ground."],"answer":"Liao River","answerText":"Liao River","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yan expansion</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Han border</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 2nd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["river","china","korea","geography","archaeology","history"],"abstract":"The <b>Liao River</b> runs south through the plain of north-eastern China into the Bohai Sea, and it is the line the argument about early Korea is organised around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The country east of it is Liaodong, whose name says so, and that ground carried almost everything the peninsula received from the continent, including the cast iron by which Korean archaeologists date their Early Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where Gojoseon actually lay is disputed: some scholars place it in the Liaoning region west of the peninsula, others in the Taedong basin around Pyongyang, and a third position has it moving between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dispute is not idle, because a polity's location decides which excavated material belongs to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Korean-style slender bronze dagger is traced to the dagger culture of that region and is held to have been carried east by migrants, which ties the two areas together whatever the answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The written evidence is a set of frontier statements rather than a map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Knife-shaped bronze coins in the north-west of the peninsula have been connected with followers of Wiman moving east of the Liao.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After Qin destroyed Yan the territory is said to have passed to the Liaodong outer fortifications, and scholars generally put those works west of the Yalu on the distribution of Qin weapons and coinage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The early Han then withdrew to the older Yan line, judging the outer works too far off to defend, and fixed the border at a river whose identity is itself argued about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A campaign against Gojoseon about 300 BCE is the fixed point everything else is measured from, and the rest of the frontier remains a question of where, not whether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Christopher J. Bae and Bumcheol Kim, “Korean Prehistory: Current Perspectives,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 1–10, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55544. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/%E7%9B%98%E9%94%A6%E8%8A%A6%E8%8B%87%E8%8D%A1.JPG/1280px-%E7%9B%98%E9%94%A6%E8%8A%A6%E8%8B%87%E8%8D%A1.JPG","title":"The lower Liao River","desc":"The channel running through the reed marshes of the Panjin delta, near where the river reaches the sea.","credit":"吴张帆, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%9B%98%E9%94%A6%E8%8A%A6%E8%8B%87%E8%8D%A1.JPG","alt":"A wide still river running straight between low reed beds under a blue sky."}},
{"id":"ko-051","num":51,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Chinese records give the peoples of the north-eastern peninsula and the country beyond it the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Korean tradition treats them as the population of Gojoseon.","questions":["Trying to match the name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a set of pots or tombs is the point at which the record and the archaeology of early Korea come apart most visibly.","Stone mound tombs were long taken as the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, until they were shown to appear near the end of the period they were supposed to identify."],"answer":"Yemaek","answerText":"Yemaek","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Named in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sanguozhi, 3rd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["people","korea","china","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Yemaek</b> is the name Chinese records give to peoples of the northern and eastern parts of the Korean peninsula and the country beyond it, and it is conventionally attached to the population of Gojoseon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The records that carry it are late and were written from outside: the <i>Sanguozhi</i> of the 3rd century CE and the <i>Hou Hanshu</i> of the 5th describe several distinct groups living south of the Chinese commanderies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of those records notes that a distinction between the local people and the people of Han began to be drawn only after the Han emperor destroyed Chosŏn and set up four commanderies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> So the ethnic map these names imply is partly a product of the administration that recorded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is worth holding on to before any of it is matched against excavated material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The matching has been tried in detail and does not hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the model long used for central Korea the Han people belonged to the Mahan confederation in the west and the Ye or Yemaek lived to the north and east, with stone mound tombs classed as Ye and heated floors and certain rim forms as Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A critical review found that the stone mound tombs do not appear until perhaps 250 to 300 CE, near the end of the period they were supposed to mark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also found a real boundary in a place the conventional map does not put one, between the Han River basin and the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the archaeology supports is a set of societies in contact and changing, and arguments about when and how they turned into the Mahan and the states after them are still open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Early_Iron_Age%2C_Buyeo%2C_Korea._Pottery_with_Horn-shaped_Handles._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg/1280px-Early_Iron_Age%2C_Buyeo%2C_Korea._Pottery_with_Horn-shaped_Handles._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg","title":"Early Iron Age pottery from Korea","desc":"A jar with horn-shaped handles in the Buyeo National Museum, of the kind whose distribution has been mapped onto named peoples.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Iron_Age%2C_Buyeo%2C_Korea._Pottery_with_Horn-shaped_Handles._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg","alt":"A rounded buff earthenware jar with two small upturned handles, on a museum mount."}},
{"id":"ko-052","num":52,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Chosŏn is said to have been governed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which only three survive: death for murder, grain for injury, and enslavement for theft.","questions":["The tradition that Chosŏn lived under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the whole of what is claimed about its law, and five of the articles were never written down.","Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a thief became the slave of the household he robbed, though a heavy payment could buy his freedom back."],"answer":"eight prohibitions","answerText":"eight prohibitions","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ascribed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Chosŏn, before 108 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Articles surviving</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 of 8</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","korea","history","law","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>eight prohibitions</b> are the law code that tradition assigns to Chosŏn, and only three of the eight articles are preserved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Murder was answered with death, inflicted in the manner of the crime; injury done in a brawl was paid for in grain; theft made the offender the slave of the person he had robbed, though he could buy his freedom back with a heavy payment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other five were not recorded, and the guesses made about them are guesses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Short as it is, the list is almost the whole of what the written record claims about how Chosŏn society worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It implies private property worth stealing, a court able to enforce a sentence, and a form of servitude as a punishment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The code reaches us through the Chinese histories rather than from Chosŏn itself, and the Chinese record of this region is the same one that supplies the household and population figures for the commanderies later planted on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Korean tradition attaches the code to Gija, the sage said to have come from China and reformed the country, which makes it part of an argument about origins as well as a piece of legal history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A period English history of Korea set out the three surviving articles in exactly those terms in 1905.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading a society off a handful of clauses written by outsiders is the difficulty the whole of early Korean history runs into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the prohibitions do establish is that the people who wrote about Chosŏn thought of it as a place with law and with property, and that they found its provisions worth setting down beside their own; a society without either would have been described differently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul, 1905), 8–9, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-053","num":53,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Korean tradition holds that a sage of the fallen Shang house named <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came east in 1122 BCE and founded a dynasty of forty generations at Pyongyang.","questions":["Scholar-officials of the Chosŏn dynasty held that Korea had been civilised by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and set his tablet beside Dangun's in the ancestral shrine.","The claim that Korea was reformed by an immigrant Chinese sage made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a difficult figure for a national history built on descent from a single ancestor."],"answer":"Gija","answerText":"Gija","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Traditional arrival</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1122 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Line said to end</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 193 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","korea","china","history","mythology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Gija</b> is the figure Korean tradition names as an uncle of the last Shang emperor of China who came east to Chosŏn about 1122 BCE and founded a line lasting forty generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition credits him with teaching agriculture and writing, dividing the fields on a nine-square plan of which the government took the ninth part, and giving the country its short code of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A period English account calls him the King Arthur of Korea, reports that Koreans resented any suggestion that he was merely legendary, and notes the tomb shown for him at Pyongyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The line is made to end with Gijun, from whom the throne was taken about 195 BCE by a refugee from the Chinese state of Yan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of this is attested by anything excavated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The tradition mattered most to the Chosŏn dynasty, whose scholar-officials held that Korea had been civilised and educated by Gija and set him beside Dangun in the ancestral shrine, Dangun as the first king to receive Heaven’s will and Gija as the first to rule by reformation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That arrangement suited a state that valued its relationship with China, and it became awkward when a national history was wanted instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From the late 19th century Korean historians pressed the opposite case, and by the early 20th they were questioning whether the Chinese records about this period could be relied on at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One influential writer called the passages describing the Han commanderies a false interpolation by scholars of later periods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gija is now generally treated as a tradition about Korea rather than a person in its history, and what the tradition was for is the more answerable question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul, 1905), 4–5, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The Passing of Korea</i> (New York, 1906), 70, https://archive.org/details/passingofkorea00hulb. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/%E4%BA%9C%E7%B4%B0%E4%BA%9C%E5%A4%A7%E8%A6%B3_04_184_%22%E7%AE%95%E5%AD%90%E9%99%B5_%E5%85%B6%E3%81%AE%E4%BA%8C_%EF%BC%88%E5%B9%B3%E5%A3%A4%EF%BC%89%22.jpg/1280px-%E4%BA%9C%E7%B4%B0%E4%BA%9C%E5%A4%A7%E8%A6%B3_04_184_%22%E7%AE%95%E5%AD%90%E9%99%B5_%E5%85%B6%E3%81%AE%E4%BA%8C_%EF%BC%88%E5%B9%B3%E5%A3%A4%EF%BC%89%22.jpg","title":"The tomb said to be Gija's, at Pyongyang","desc":"Photographed in 1927; the enclosure north of the city was rebuilt more than once and holds no verified burial.","credit":"Black Dragon Society, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%9C%E7%B4%B0%E4%BA%9C%E5%A4%A7%E8%A6%B3_04_184_%22%E7%AE%95%E5%AD%90%E9%99%B5_%E5%85%B6%E3%81%AE%E4%BA%8C_%EF%BC%88%E5%B9%B3%E5%A3%A4%EF%BC%89%22.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a walled precinct with a stone stele, an offering table and pines behind."}},
{"id":"ko-054","num":54,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The state that ruled north-western Korea from about 195 to 108 BCE is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it grew rich by standing between Han China and the societies further south.","questions":["Hereditary kings ruled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a bureaucracy and an army, which is why it is usually described as a full state rather than a chiefdom.","Han coins found in its territory are the material trace of what made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> worth destroying: a monopoly on trade with the empire next door."],"answer":"Wiman Joseon","answerText":"Wiman Joseon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 195 – 108 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["state","korea","china","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Wiman Joseon</b> is the polity that ruled north-western Korea from about 195 to 108 BCE, taking its name from the Yan refugee who seized power there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chinese records describe hereditary kings ruling with an elite bureaucracy and a powerful army, exercising authority over other polities and controlling trade networks, which is why it is usually described as a full state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its capital was Wanggeom-seong, at present-day Pyongyang, which is where the Chinese commandery that replaced it was planted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was formed out of an alliance of local people and immigrants rather than by conquest from outside, and that composition is part of what made it work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A few thousand migrants from the Chinese central plains had already been allowed to settle along its western border before Wiman took the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What it did with its position was to make itself the intermediary between Han China and the emerging societies of the peninsula further south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Han China traded with the powers around it through seasonal gifts and border markets, and Wiman Joseon seems to have engaged in the same kind of relations while controlling who else could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coins from Han China found within its territory are the archaeological trace of that traffic, and they are the reason the polity has attracted more attention from archaeologists than its size would suggest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chinese account of it survives in the chapter on Chosŏn in the <i>Shiji</i>, which says only that its ruler had his capital at Wanggeom, while a period Korean history tells the same events as a story of treachery, with Wiman crossing the river in disguise and received by kin already settled there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An independent power of that kind on the north-eastern frontier was not acceptable to the Han empire, and in 108 BCE it was destroyed after more than a year of fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul, 1905), 14–15, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Early_Iron_Age%2C_Korea._Black_Burnished_Pottery._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg/1280px-Early_Iron_Age%2C_Korea._Black_Burnished_Pottery._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg","title":"Black burnished pottery from Korea","desc":"Early Iron Age vessels in the Buyeo National Museum, current on the peninsula while Wiman Joseon held the north-west.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Iron_Age%2C_Korea._Black_Burnished_Pottery._Buyeo_National_Museum.jpg","alt":"Two dark polished jars and a small bowl on white plinths in a museum case."}},
{"id":"ko-055","num":55,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The last ruler of the older Chosŏn line was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who gave a Yan refugee land on his western border and lost his throne to him a few years later.","questions":["Tradition says that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> loaded his treasure onto boats as the usurper entered Pyongyang, and went south to look for a home among the Samhan.","By titling the refugee an Erudite and setting him to guard the frontier, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was doing what rulers commonly did with useful strangers."],"answer":"King Jun","answerText":"King Jun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 195 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Flight south</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 193 BCE, by tradition</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["person","korea","history","asia"],"abstract":"<b>King Jun</b> is the last ruler of the older Chosŏn line, displaced about 195 BCE by Wiman, a refugee from the Chinese state of Yan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disorder of the late Qin and early Han years had sent thousands of fugitives east, and Jun allowed them to settle in his western borderland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He trusted Wiman with that border, titled him Erudite, and accepted his offer to serve as a frontier guard against attack from Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taking in useful strangers and giving them a place was a normal strategy for a ruler who wanted what they knew and what they traded, rather than a lapse of judgement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wiman used the position to gather a following of Yan refugees and local people, and within a few years he had the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The traditional account is more colourful: Wiman is said to have sent word that a great Chinese army was advancing and that he must hurry to the capital to guard the king, and to have taken Pyongyang by that ruse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same telling Jun loaded his treasure onto boats and slipped down the river as the usurper entered the gates, about 193 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where he went is the part that matters for what came after, since the tradition sends him south into the country of the Samhan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Archaeologists debate how far the material culture of late Chosŏn spread into the south-western regions from the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, and what it contributed to the societies that formed there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The displaced king is a convenient figure for that transfer, and the evidence for it is the pottery and the metal rather than the story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul, 1905), 14–15, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]","Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-056","num":56,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The Chinese state of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> campaigned against Gojoseon about 300 BCE, and Korean archaeologists date the start of their Early Iron Age from that moment.","questions":["Cast ironware distributed by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the material by which the Korean Early Iron Age is defined, and the arrival was a consequence of war rather than trade.","When the Qin destroyed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, its frontier works passed to the new empire, and the refugees it sent east changed the politics of the peninsula."],"answer":"Yan","answerText":"Yan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yan campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed by Qin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 3rd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["state","china","korea","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Yan</b> was a Chinese state of the Warring States period whose territory reached north-east to the Liao River, and its dealings with Gojoseon open the historical record of Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About 300 BCE a Yan general campaigned against Gojoseon, and Yan material began to spread eastward in consequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korean archaeologists take that as the start of their Early Iron Age, defining the period as the time during which cast ironware distributed by Yan reached the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Shiji</i> records that the same general had earlier broken the Donghu and driven them back, and that Yan then built a long wall and set up the commanderies of its northern frontier, Liaodong among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A period Korean history has the Yan king pushing the boundary of Chosŏn back as far as the Yalu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What travelled with the armies was not only iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Knife-shaped bronze coins of the kind Yan issued turn up in south-western Manchuria and the north-west of the peninsula, and the Korean-style slender bronze dagger is understood to have branched from the dagger culture of that region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When Qin destroyed Yan the territory passed to what the records call the Liaodong outer fortifications, and the early Han then withdrew to the older Yan line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The collapse of Yan also sent people east: refugees from Yan, Qi and Zhao were allowed to settle on the western border of Chosŏn, and one of them took its throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A neighbour's wars and a neighbour's collapse are between them a large part of why the written history of the peninsula begins where it does, and why the objects that open its Iron Age were made somewhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul, 1905), 13, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Warring_States_Bronze_Dou%2C_Yan_State.jpg/1280px-Warring_States_Bronze_Dou%2C_Yan_State.jpg","title":"A bronze vessel of the state of Yan","desc":"A stemmed dou of the Warring States period; Yan's metalwork and its coinage travelled east with its armies.","credit":"Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Dou%2C_Yan_State.jpg","alt":"A round lidded bronze bowl on a tall foot, with three spikes rising from the lid, in a display case."}},
{"id":"ko-057","num":57,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Bronze coins shaped like blades, known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, are found across southern Manchuria and north-western Korea, and they are the main evidence for Gojoseon's trade.","questions":["Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was issued by the Chinese states, finding it in Korea raises the question of who carried it there and what they were buying.","Where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns up alongside local daggers and pottery, the assemblage is plainly not simply Chinese, which is the point archaeologists argue over."],"answer":"knife money","answerText":"knife money","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Circulating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reaching Korea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the 3rd century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","china","korea","archaeology","history","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Knife money</b> is bronze coinage cast in the shape of a blade, issued by several Chinese states and found in quantity across south-western Manchuria and the north-west of the Korean peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the material centre of the argument about Gojoseon's trade, because it is the one class of object that plainly came from the Chinese economy and can be counted where it was dropped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One reading holds that the coins arrived with Wiman and his followers when they moved east of the Liao; another connects the sites yielding them with the Chosŏn and Wiman Chosŏn polities directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the finds do not show is a Chinese province: the archaeological record of these regions is plainly distinct from that of the central plains, whatever currency circulated in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Coins alone cannot say who used them or for what.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The trade they belong to was structured by position rather than by production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Han China dealt with the powers around it through seasonal gifts and border markets, and Wiman Chosŏn made itself the intermediary between the empire and the societies further south, taking a share of what passed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The distribution of Qin-era weapons and coinage is also what scholars use to place the frontier works of that period, so the same evidence is doing double duty as economics and as geography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the conquest the Chinese commanderies became trading posts in their own right, moving bronze mirrors, lacquerware and long iron swords into the peninsula and the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Early Iron Age of Korea is defined by imported metal, and the coins are the clearest sign of the exchange that brought it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Warring_States_Bronze_Knife_Money_01.jpg/1280px-Warring_States_Bronze_Knife_Money_01.jpg","title":"Bronze knife money","desc":"Warring States coins cast in the shape of a blade, the type found across southern Manchuria and north-western Korea.","credit":"Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Knife_Money_01.jpg","alt":"Five long thin bronze blades with ringed handles, laid side by side in a lit case."}},
{"id":"ko-058","num":58,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"In 109 BCE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sent an army and a fleet against Chosŏn, and when it fell he divided the territory into four commanderies.","questions":["An independent state holding a monopoly on the trade of the north-eastern frontier was not something <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his advisers were prepared to leave standing there.","The forces <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> committed were large by the standards of the frontier, and even so the capital held out for more than a year."],"answer":"Emperor Wu of Han","answerText":"Emperor Wu of Han","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">109 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chosŏn falls</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["person","china","korea","history","warfare","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Emperor Wu of Han</b> conquered Chosŏn at the end of the 2nd century BCE and replaced it with Chinese commanderies, which is the event that opens the documented history of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A period source puts the campaign in the second year of the Yuanfeng era, 109 BCE, and records that he killed Ugŏ, the grandson of Wiman, divided the conquered territory and set up four commanderies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another account gives the fall as 108 BCE and the force as 50,000 soldiers and 7,000 marines, with the capital holding out for more than a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lelang was planted at Pyongyang as the principal one, and a further commandery, Daifang, was added to the south later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chinese administration of the region then lasted some 420 years, until Goguryeo destroyed the two in 313 and 314 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The reason for the war was position rather than territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wiman Chosŏn had made itself the intermediary between Han China and the societies further south and had a monopoly on that traffic, and an independent military power holding it on the empire's north-eastern edge was not acceptable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commanderies that followed were mixed societies of Chinese residents and local people, governed by officials appointed from the capital who chose their subordinates from local elites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They served as trading posts and as channels through which titles and gifts reached local leaders as far as the Japanese archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The phrase four Han commanderies is itself contested in Korean scholarship, which has questioned both the reliability of the passages describing them and the way they have been counted, and later Korean histories add accounts of their own that cannot be checked against anything earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Liu_Che_%28Emperor_Wu_of_Han%29.png/1280px-Liu_Che_%28Emperor_Wu_of_Han%29.png","title":"Emperor Wu of Han","desc":"An ink portrait of Liu Che, the emperor who destroyed Chosŏn and planted four commanderies on its territory.","credit":"Unknown author, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liu_Che_%28Emperor_Wu_of_Han%29.png","alt":"An ink drawing on silk of a standing bearded man in a long robe and a small cap."}},
{"id":"ko-059","num":59,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The capital of Wiman Joseon was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, generally placed at present-day Pyongyang, and it held out against a Han army and fleet for more than a year.","questions":["The <i>Shiji</i> says only that the ruler of Chosŏn had his capital at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why its position has been argued about ever since.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell, the Han planted the Lelang commandery on the site, and the seals and registers found near Pyongyang belong to that administration."],"answer":"Wanggeom-seong","answerText":"Wanggeom-seong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wiman Joseon, c. 195 – 108 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","korea","china","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Wanggeom-seong</b> is the name the Chinese records give to the capital of Wiman Joseon, and it is generally placed at present-day Pyongyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Shiji</i>'s chapter on Chosŏn says of Wiman only that he made Wanggeom his capital, without describing where it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That single clause is the whole of the contemporary evidence for the site, and the identification with Pyongyang rests on what came after it rather than on the sentence itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Han army of 50,000 and a fleet of 7,000 attacked it in 109 BCE, and it fell only after more than a year of fighting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Lelang commandery was then established there as a frontier outpost of the empire, and Chinese administration of the region lasted some four centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the identification strong is the material found around Pyongyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A household register recovered in North Korea records the population of 25 counties of Lelang, and can be compared directly with the figures in the Chinese histories for the same commandery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Official seals have also been found in the area, and they were what settled the question for many scholars when the records themselves were being doubted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The doubt was not idle antiquarianism: locating a Chinese province at the site of the Korean capital has been read as a claim about the country's origins, and some scholars have preferred to place the commanderies elsewhere or to deny them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A period Korean history simply has the last king of the old line loading his treasure onto boats and slipping down the river as the usurper entered the gates, which is the kind of detail a tradition supplies and an excavation cannot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul, 1905), 14–15, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Taedong_River%2C_Pyongyang_%285063216869%29.jpg/1280px-Taedong_River%2C_Pyongyang_%285063216869%29.jpg","title":"The Taedong River at Pyongyang","desc":"The city on the river is where the capital of Wiman Joseon and the Lelang commandery after it are generally placed.","credit":"David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taedong_River%2C_Pyongyang_%285063216869%29.jpg","alt":"A wide river running past a city, with rowing boats in the foreground and a tall tower on the far bank."}},
{"id":"ko-060","num":60,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Korean national history is conventionally written as the story of one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a single people descended from a common ancestor and continuous to the present.","questions":["The idea of a Korean <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took shape in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, in textbooks written while the country was losing its independence.","Because a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must be continuous, evidence of Chinese provinces on the peninsula has been experienced as an injury rather than as a finding."],"answer":"minjok","answerText":"minjok","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","korea","history","asia","nationalism"],"abstract":"<b>Minjok</b> is the Korean term for the nation understood as a single people of common descent, and it is the frame in which the country's history is conventionally written.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The framing is modern: the idea of Koreans as descendants of Dangun spread through history books and newspapers in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, alongside an era counted from 2333 BCE and a national foundation day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was formed during a political crisis and against a colonial power, which is why it is so firmly attached to the earliest part of the past.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A scholar working under Japanese rule built an account of Korean origins around that figure while negotiating what he could publish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The frame is a subject for a history of Korea, not a voice it should speak in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Where it bears hardest is on the archaeology of the north-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Chinese commanderies, Lelang near Pyongyang above all, are attested by both texts and excavated finds, and many Korean scholars have nonetheless regarded them as a blemish on national dignity or denied them outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Early in the 20th century the historian Sin Ch'ae-ho called the passages describing their foundation a false interpolation by scholars of later periods, and the seals later found near Pyongyang were what answered him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Interpretation rather than excavation has been the contested ground in Korean archaeology for as long as it has existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A history written for a nation and a history written from the evidence will not always want the same answers, and being clear about which of the two a given claim belongs to is most of the work of writing about early Korea at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]","Tobias Scholl, “Ch'oe Namsŏn and Identity Construction through Negotiation with the Colonizer,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 153–86, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-153.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Lothar von Falkenhausen, “Hyung Il Pai 裵炯逸 (14 June 1958 – 28 May 2018),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 209–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76779. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Monument_for_Declaration_of_Independence_March_First.jpg/1280px-Monument_for_Declaration_of_Independence_March_First.jpg","title":"The March First declaration monument, Seoul","desc":"The independence movement of 1919 belongs to the decades in which the modern idea of a single Korean people took shape.","credit":"MonasteryInwang, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_for_Declaration_of_Independence_March_First.jpg","alt":"A white stone monument on a lawn in a city park, with a wooded hill and tower blocks behind."}},
{"id":"ko-061","num":61,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"A religion founded at Seoul in 1909 and called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took Dangun as the object of its faith, and its members carried the independence movement into Manchuria.","questions":["When <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was founded its organisers had already tried and failed to stop a treaty, and they concluded that saving the country needed more than a few people.","The movement behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began as Dangun worship, with a ceremony held before the ancestral tablet of the Great Ancestor in February 1909."],"answer":"Taejonggyo","answerText":"Taejonggyo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 February 1909</span><span class=\"dt-k\"></span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Na Ch'ŏl</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["institution","korea","religion","history","asia","nationalism"],"abstract":"<b>Taejonggyo</b> is the religion founded in Korea in 1909 that takes Dangun as its object of faith, and it is the sharpest instance of what the figure was made to do in the modern period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Facing the loss of the country, Dangun became first a national guardian and then a god, and the worship of him was organised into a church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founder was Na Ch'ŏl, a government official who had passed the civil service examination and had tried and failed to have the protectorate treaty of 1905 undone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In exile after 1907 he decided that a movement for national salvation could not be carried by a handful of men, and with several colleagues he began the worship at Hansŏng on 5 February 1909 with a ceremony before the ancestral tablet of the Great Ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was called Dangun worship before it was called anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Scholars, soldiers and officials of the Korean Empire supported the same idea, that the country could be rebuilt with Dangun at the centre of its national spirit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That belongs to the decades in which an era counted from 2333 BCE and a national foundation day were also established, and in which school histories began teaching that Koreans were his descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A colonial-era scholar built an account of Korean origins around the same figure while negotiating what he could publish under Japanese rule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Myth put to this kind of work does not stop when the crisis passes, and it is still drawn on as cultural material today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What such a movement produces is not evidence about the Bronze Age but a record of what a people under threat wanted its past to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]","Tobias Scholl, “Ch'oe Namsŏn and Identity Construction through Negotiation with the Colonizer,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 153–86, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-153.pdf. [Open access]","Yoonhee Hong, “Return of Myth, Myth Resources, and the Contemporaneity of Mythology in Korea and China Today,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 325–54, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-325.pdf. [Open access]","Lothar von Falkenhausen, “Hyung Il Pai 裵炯逸 (14 June 1958 – 28 May 2018),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 209–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76779. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bb/Daejongism.png","title":"The emblem of Taejonggyo","desc":"The symbol of the religion founded in 1909 that takes Dangun as its object of faith.","credit":"Berghendorf, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daejongism.png","alt":"A red triangle on a yellow square inside a blue disc."}},
{"id":"ko-062","num":62,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"North Korea excavated what it declared to be the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1993 and dated the founding of Gojoseon to 5,000 years ago on the strength of it.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was one of three founders' tombs opened or rebuilt in Pyongyang in the early 1990s, the others being Koguryŏ's and Koryŏ's.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> placed the first Korean state at Pyongyang and made it far older, the excavation was announced as a national achievement rather than a finding."],"answer":"Tomb of Dangun","answerText":"Tomb of Dangun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Excavated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1993 – October 1994</span><span class=\"dt-k\"></span><span class=\"dt-v\">north-east of Pyongyang</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","korea","archaeology","history","asia","nationalism"],"abstract":"The <b>Tomb of Dangun</b> is a monument outside Pyongyang that North Korea excavated between October 1993 and October 1994 and declared to be the burial of the founder of Gojoseon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was one of a series: the tomb of Wang Kŏn, who founded Koryŏ, was excavated from September 1992, and the tomb of King Tongmyŏng, who founded Koguryŏ, was rebuilt in May 1993.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The three together were commemorated as a national achievement, the ancestral tombs of the founders of three Korean states all lying within reach of the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Using the tomb as its reference point, North Korean scholarship retroactively dated the formation of Gojoseon to 5,000 years ago, far earlier than the 10th century BCE previously accepted there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavation was therefore a turning point in the North's research on early history, and the direction of the argument ran from the conclusion outwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> None of that is how the figure is treated outside the North.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story of Dangun survives because two works of the 13th century wrote it down, and no excavation has established either the traditional date of 2333 BCE or the person attached to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The division between North and South on early history is not only about Dangun: the Chinese commanderies planted on the peninsula after 108 BCE have been treated as a blemish on national dignity, or denied, in scholarship on both sides at different times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where a state wants a founding it can point to, the archaeology of the north-west is the ground it has to argue over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A claim of this kind is carded here as a claim, which is the only honest way to hold it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jeongbin Lee and Inwook Kim, “Creating P'yŏngyang's Legitimacy: North Korea's Excavation and Reconstruction of the Tomb of King Tongmyŏng,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 87–132, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-87.pdf. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]","Lothar von Falkenhausen, “Hyung Il Pai 裵炯逸 (14 June 1958 – 28 May 2018),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 209–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76779. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Laika_ac_Mausoleum_of_Tangun_%287987312460%29.jpg/1280px-Laika_ac_Mausoleum_of_Tangun_%287987312460%29.jpg","title":"The Tomb of Dangun near Pyongyang","desc":"The stepped monument built over the site North Korea excavated in 1993 and declared to be the founder's burial.","credit":"Laika ac from USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laika_ac_Mausoleum_of_Tangun_%287987312460%29.jpg","alt":"A large white stepped stone pyramid with a carved stone tiger standing before it."}},
{"id":"ko-063","num":63,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The bronze weapon that succeeds the Liaoning type on the peninsula is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a straight blade with a separate handle, and it is the object most often tied to Gojoseon.","questions":["Korean archaeologists hold that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> branched from the dagger culture of north-eastern China and was carried east by migrant groups from Gojoseon.","Because a polity in a Chinese text and a distribution of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> finds are different kinds of evidence, joining them is an argument rather than an observation."],"answer":"slender bronze dagger","answerText":"slender bronze dagger","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\"></span><span class=\"dt-v\">succeeds the Liaoning type</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["object","korea","china","archaeology","prehistory","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>slender bronze dagger</b>, known also as the Korean-style bronze dagger, is the weapon type that follows the Liaoning form on the peninsula from about the 4th century BCE, a narrow straight blade cast separately from its handle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korean archaeologists hold that this dagger culture branched from the Liaoning bronze dagger culture of north-eastern China and was carried east by migrant groups from Gojoseon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That sentence is the main bridge in Korean archaeology between a polity named in Chinese texts and a class of excavated object.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also where the trouble lies, because a distribution of artefacts and a name in a record are different kinds of evidence, and joining them requires an argument that is rarely set out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The daggers themselves are not in doubt; what they are taken to prove is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The bronze repertoire of Korea differs from the Chinese one it is compared with, running to daggers, spears, arrowheads and mirrors rather than to the ritual vessels that dominate contemporary assemblages further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bronze mirrors with multiple knobs and the graves that contain them have been used to argue for ranked society by the number and type of objects buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Early Iron Age that follows is defined by imported cast ironware rather than by anything the peninsula made, which is a reminder of how much of this material arrived rather than developed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the daggers travel, the argument about Gojoseon's location travels with them, since the polity's homeland is disputed between Liaoning and the Taedong basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A type series is not a people, and the same object can belong to several stories at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]","Yongwook Yoo, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Alternative Approaches in the Korean Archaeology,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 1–4, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Korean_Bronze_dagger_and_mirror._Jangsu._Jin_Period._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Korean_Bronze_dagger_and_mirror._Jangsu._Jin_Period._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"A Korean-style bronze dagger and mirror","desc":"The slender blade with a separate handle that follows the Liaoning form, here with a knobbed bronze mirror.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_Bronze_dagger_and_mirror._Jangsu._Jin_Period._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"A long narrow bronze blade mounted upright above a small dark bronze disc."}},
{"id":"ko-064","num":64,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The state on the Songhua river that Chinese records first name in the 2nd century BCE is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and both Goguryeo and Baekje later claimed descent from it.","questions":["<span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept generally peaceful relations with the Chinese states, being far enough from the Central Plain to be a tributary rather than a target.","The Murong Xianbei destroyed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 346 CE, and its people scattered south, north and into the kingdom that took over its heartland."],"answer":"Buyeo","answerText":"Buyeo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">346 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["state","korea","china","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Buyeo</b> was a state centred on the Songhua river in what is now Jilin, and the earliest certain mention of it in Chinese materials dates to the 2nd century BCE, though the context makes clear it existed before that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It grew out of the Bronze Age Xituanshan society of the same region, which occupied the Songhua valley from the 2nd millennium BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its relations with the Chinese states were generally peaceful, helped by distance from the Central Plain and by a tributary relationship with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iron technology and trade with Han China reached it during the Western Han period, and it dealt in turn with the Eastern Han, with the Gongsun house that held Liaodong, and with the Jin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Murong Xianbei destroyed it in 346 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its afterlife matters more to Korean history than its politics did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goguryeo claimed Buyeo in its founding legend and Baekje took the name as the surname of its royal house, so later Korean historians treated it as one of the mainsprings of the Korean people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some Buyeo elites fled to the Chinese Central Plains after the state fell and others went north, while Goguryeo occupied the heartland in the middle of the 4th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Which modern country's history it belongs to has been argued about since China launched its Northeast Project in 2001 and the Republic of Korea answered with a research foundation of its own in 2006.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peoples of this region were described from outside in the same records that describe the Ye and the peninsula's southern polities, and read together they set the frame for everything before the Three Kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Richard D. McBride II, review of <i>The Ancient State of Puyŏ in Northeast Asia: Archeology and Historical Memory</i>, by Mark E. Byington, <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 183–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-183.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Pottery_vessels_excavated_from_Houshishan%2C_Jilin_City.jpg/1280px-Pottery_vessels_excavated_from_Houshishan%2C_Jilin_City.jpg","title":"Pottery from the Buyeo heartland","desc":"Vessels excavated at Houshishan near Jilin City, in the Songhua valley where Buyeo formed.","credit":"Amys eye, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pottery_vessels_excavated_from_Houshishan%2C_Jilin_City.jpg","alt":"Nine round earthenware jars of varying size arranged on two white museum shelves."}},
{"id":"ko-065","num":65,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"When a society is pushed into statehood by the pressure of an existing state next to it, the process is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it is how Buyeo is now explained.","questions":["The archaeology of Buyeo suggests an authoritarian and highly stratified society, which is the sort of outcome <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is invoked to account for.","Reading <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the record means treating migration legends as claims about legitimacy rather than as memories of actual migrations."],"answer":"secondary state formation","answerText":"secondary state formation","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","korea","history","asia","science"],"abstract":"<b>Secondary state formation</b> is the emergence of a state under pressure from an existing one nearby, rather than out of purely local development, and it is the explanation now offered for Buyeo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument is that the incursions of the Chinese states of Yan and Han into southern Manchuria are what turned the societies beyond them into states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Buyeo is read in two phases on this account, social first and then political, with the political phase driven from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the archaeology shows is an authoritarian and highly stratified society, defended by a network of forts and strongholds, whose people were credited even by Han and Wei observers with a keen sense of etiquette.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its territory can be traced from those forts across central Jilin and into parts of north-eastern Liaoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The idea has a second edge that matters more for Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If states in this region formed under external pressure, the migration legends that early Korean states told about their own origins need not record actual migrations or the imposition of foreign rule, and can be read instead as claims to legitimacy made later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the same caution the whole field has been moving towards, since a named culture cannot be read directly as a people and similarity between assemblages does not establish descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the southern peninsula the preferred model is different again, with a multitude of small competing polities gradually coalescing through interaction with each other and with the Chinese commanderies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Both models replace a story about peoples arriving with a story about societies changing under pressure from their neighbours, which is the direction the evidence in this region has pushed for several decades now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Richard D. McBride II, review of <i>The Ancient State of Puyŏ in Northeast Asia: Archeology and Historical Memory</i>, by Mark E. Byington, <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 183–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-183.pdf. [Open access]","Yongwook Yoo, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Alternative Approaches in the Korean Archaeology,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 1–4, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Seung-Og Kim, “Recent Developments and Debates in Korean Prehistoric Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 11–30, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55545. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-066","num":66,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The people of the north-eastern coastal strip between the mountains and the sea were the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, absorbed first by a Chinese commandery and then by Goguryeo.","questions":["A Chinese record says the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became part of Lelang in the 1st century BCE, and that the commandery governing them was later moved north-west.","Neither the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nor their southern neighbours the Dongye ever formed a state, which is why they are known only through other people's records."],"answer":"Okjeo","answerText":"Okjeo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Into Lelang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Sanguozhi, 3rd century CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["people","korea","china","history","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Okjeo</b> were the people of the narrow coastal strip of north-eastern Korea, between the Taebaek mountains and the East Sea, and they are known almost entirely from Chinese records.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Sanguozhi</i>, compiled in the 3rd century CE, records that Okjeo became part of the Lelang commandery in the 1st century BCE and was later subjugated by Goguryeo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commandery arrangement there did not hold: Okjeosŏng was made the seat of Xuantu commandery, which was then relocated to the north-west of Goguryeo after a barbarian invasion, and Okjeo passed back to Lelang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the Han territory was extensive, seven counties east of the mountains were placed under a commandant who led them separately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their neighbours to the south, the Dongye, are described in the same chapter and in much the same terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What these records amount to is an administrative view of peoples who left no writing of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same source notes that a distinction between the local population and the people of Han began to be drawn only after the commanderies were established, so even the ethnic categories are partly an artefact of the administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither group formed a state, and both were eventually absorbed by Goguryeo as it expanded east and south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their material record is one of small polities in loose association rather than a single society, which is the pattern across the whole eastern and northern part of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A later Korean history adds an account of a Lelang kingdom in this region that cannot be dated claim by claim against anything earlier, which is the standing difficulty with every source for the peninsula before the Three Kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Richard D. McBride II, review of <i>The Ancient State of Puyŏ in Northeast Asia: Archeology and Historical Memory</i>, by Mark E. Byington, <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 183–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-183.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-067","num":67,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The three groups of small polities across the southern peninsula were called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and out of them came Baekje, Silla and the Gaya states.","questions":["Chinese records describe the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a large number of minor kingdoms, each made up of a hierarchy of villages rather than of a single centre.","The archaeology of the south fits the picture of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a patchwork of independent polities better than it fits the later Korean histories."],"answer":"Samhan","answerText":"Samhan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\"></span><span class=\"dt-v\">Mahan, Jinhan and Byeonhan</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["people","korea","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Samhan</b>, the Three Han, are the three groupings into which Chinese records divide the southern Korean peninsula in the centuries around the start of the Common Era: Mahan in the west, Jinhan in the south-east and Byeonhan on the south coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Sanguozhi</i> describes each as consisting of a large number of small polities, called <i>soguk</i>, which were themselves made up of hierarchies of villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Further north the Ye are described as a similar set of polities with even less of an overarching framework.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That image of a patchwork of relatively independent units fits the archaeological evidence better than the framework taken from the later Korean histories, and it is compatible with a wider range of explanations for how states eventually formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The period runs roughly from 300 BCE to 300 CE, which Korean archaeology treats as its Iron Age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What replaced the Samhan grew out of them rather than arriving from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small competing polities gradually coalesced from the Mahan, Jinhan and Byeonhan zones into Baekje, Silla and the Gaya groups respectively, and the waning of Han power over its commanderies from the late 2nd century CE is treated as a major spur to that consolidation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contact with China through the commanderies is visible in the graves as Han bronze mirrors and decorated ironwork, though how those objects were made and buried does not fit any single model of contact or acculturation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attempts to divide the same ground between named peoples on the evidence of tomb and pottery types have not held up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The southern peninsula is best described in this period as interdependent local groups sharing a ritual vocabulary and very little political unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Korea-Proto.3.Kingdoms.Bird-shaped_pottery.jpg/1280px-Korea-Proto.3.Kingdoms.Bird-shaped_pottery.jpg","title":"Bird-shaped pottery from southern Korea","desc":"A vessel of the kind buried in the graves of the small polities that made up the Samhan.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Proto.3.Kingdoms.Bird-shaped_pottery.jpg","alt":"A pale ceramic vessel modelled as a long-necked bird with a crest, on a plinth."}},
{"id":"ko-068","num":68,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"The largest of the three southern confederacies, in the west of the peninsula, was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the kingdom of Baekje grew out of it.","questions":["When the historiography of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is argued about, the two questions are when it formed and what its cultural identity was.","One account has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> forming with a distinctive pottery culture; another has it emerging as the material culture of late Gojoseon spread south."],"answer":"Mahan","answerText":"Mahan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\"></span><span class=\"dt-v\">becomes Baekje</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["people","korea","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Mahan</b> is the westernmost and largest of the three Han groupings of southern Korea, occupying the plains of the south-west, and the kingdom of Baekje formed within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like its neighbours it was not a state but a set of small polities, each a hierarchy of villages, as the Chinese records of the 3rd century CE describe them, and there is no sign in the archaeology of a single centre governing the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modern discussion of it has centred on two questions, when it formed and what its cultural identity was, and both are answered mostly from archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One position takes the appearance of a distinctive attached-rim pottery in southern Korea as the beginning of Mahan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another places its formation within the wider spread of late Gojoseon material culture, which begins to appear in the region in the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How that culture reached the area, and what it contributed, is still not well understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tradition that the last king of the older Chosŏn line fled south by boat after losing his throne is the narrative version of the same transfer, and it is a story rather than evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the material shows is contact rather than conquest: Chinese bronze mirrors and decorated iron reached southern graves through the Han commanderies, and local groups used them in their own ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Attempts to assign this ground to named peoples on the evidence of tomb types have been shown to rest on criteria that do not survive examination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mahan's polities consolidated into Baekje over several centuries, a slower and far less tidy process than the precise founding dates given in the later Korean histories suggest, and one that leaves no single moment to point at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Homer B. Hulbert, <i>The History of Korea</i>, vol. 1 (Seoul, 1905), 14–15, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52127. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/Egg_Shaped_Pottery.Proto-three_Kingdoms_Period._Seoul_National_University_Museum.jpg","title":"An egg-shaped jar from southern Korea","desc":"Plain wares of this kind are what most Mahan-period graves and settlements actually yield.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egg_Shaped_Pottery.Proto-three_Kingdoms_Period._Seoul_National_University_Museum.jpg","alt":"A tall plain reddish-brown jar with a slightly flaring rim, on a clear stand."}},
{"id":"ko-069","num":69,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Chinese records call the units the Samhan were made of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, small polities each built from a hierarchy of villages rather than from a capital and a countryside.","questions":["Reading the southern peninsula as dozens of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than as three kingdoms is what makes its archaeology and its written record agree.","Because a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left no writing, what is known of its society comes from graves and from a chapter written by its neighbours."],"answer":"soguk","answerText":"soguk","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","korea","history","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"A <b>soguk</b> is one of the small polities or minor kingdoms into which Chinese records divide the southern Korean peninsula, each composed of a hierarchy of villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Sanguozhi</i> describes the Samhan as consisting of a large number of them, and the Ye further north as a similar set with even less overarching structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That picture matches the archaeology better than the later Korean framework of three early kingdoms, and it leaves room for a wider range of explanations of how states formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives of these societies is mostly mortuary: the goods placed in graves, and how they varied between neighbouring groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two classes of object carry most of the argument about them, Chinese bronze mirrors and iron pieces decorated with bracken-like spiral designs, both found in south-eastern burials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both show substantial contact with Han China through its commanderies, but neither behaves as any standard model of contact predicts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their production varied, the exotic motifs were expressed in local ways, and they were interred in diverse contexts rather than in a single pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What that suggests is a set of interdependent local groups sharing a ritual vocabulary while actively expressing their differences within agreed limits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political unity is not visible even directly before Baekje, Silla and the Gaya polities appear, so the states that follow grew out of competition among these units rather than replacing them from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Attempts to divide the same ground between named peoples using tomb and pottery types have not held up under examination, which is a standing caution against reading any of these units as an ethnic group with a boundary of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Yongwook Yoo, “Guest Editor's Introduction: Alternative Approaches in the Korean Archaeology,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 1–4, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Pottery_with_Raised_Band._Wanju._Jin_Period._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Pottery_with_Raised_Band._Wanju._Jin_Period._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"Pottery with a raised band, from Wanju","desc":"The everyday ware of the small polities of the south, the material from which their society has to be read.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pottery_with_Raised_Band._Wanju._Jin_Period._National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"A brown earthenware jar with a raised horizontal band below the rim, in a museum case."}},
{"id":"ko-070","num":70,"category":"Gojoseon","question":"Almost everything known about Korea's earliest societies comes from one chapter about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the eastern peoples, written by a Chinese historian of the 3rd century.","questions":["Because the chapter on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was compiled by an often adversarial neighbour, its categories are an administrative view rather than a self-description.","Buyeo, Okjeo, Dongye and the Samhan are all described together in the account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why they are so often compared with one another."],"answer":"Dongyi","answerText":"Dongyi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Compiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\"></span><span class=\"dt-v\">Buyeo, Okjeo, Ye, the Samhan</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","china","korea","history","literature","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Dongyi</b>, the eastern peoples, is the Chinese term under which the societies of the Korean peninsula and Manchuria were described, and the account of them in the <i>Sanguozhi</i> of the 3rd century CE is the single most important source for early Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That chapter describes Buyeo on the Songhua, the Okjeo of the north-eastern coast, the Ye, and the three Han groupings of the south, so the comparison between them is built into the record itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It presents the south as a patchwork of small polities and the north-east as looser still, a picture the archaeology broadly supports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The material about Buyeo was assembled by neighbouring and often adversarial peoples, the Chinese states of the Central Plains and Goguryeo to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> These are administrative and diplomatic documents before they are anything like ethnography, written to record who paid tribute and who did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The categories they use are therefore not neutral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same chapter notes that a distinction between the local population and the people of Han began to be drawn only after the four commanderies were established, which means the ethnic map it presents is partly a product of the administration that recorded it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attempts to match its names onto excavated tomb and pottery types have been shown to rest on unstable criteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later Korean histories add accounts of the same period that cannot be checked against anything earlier, so they compound the problem rather than solving it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Read carefully and against the archaeology the chapter is indispensable, since nothing else of its period survives; read on its own it is a frontier administrator's map of peoples he governed, or hoped to govern, at a distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Richard D. McBride II, review of <i>The Ancient State of Puyŏ in Northeast Asia: Archeology and Historical Memory</i>, by Mark E. Byington, <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 183–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-183.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Text_of_the_Wei_Zhi_%28%E9%AD%8F%E5%BF%97%29%2C_297.jpg/1280px-Text_of_the_Wei_Zhi_%28%E9%AD%8F%E5%BF%97%29%2C_297.jpg","title":"A page of the Wei Zhi","desc":"The section of the Sanguozhi that carries the account of the eastern peoples, in a printed edition.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Text_of_the_Wei_Zhi_%28%E9%AD%8F%E5%BF%97%29%2C_297.jpg","alt":"Eight columns of dense Chinese characters printed in black on white, in two rows."}},
{"id":"ko-071","num":71,"category":"The commanderies","question":"After destroying Wiman Joseon in 108 BCE the Han court ruled part of the peninsula through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which only one lasted more than a century.","questions":["Zhenfan, Lintun, Lelang and Xuantu are the four units the <i>Hanshu</i> names as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a fifth was added two centuries later.","Historians describe the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as frontier provinces of a Chinese empire, run by governors the capital appointed and supplied with annual returns of households."],"answer":"Han commanderies","answerText":"Han commanderies","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Daifang added</span><span class=\"dt-v\">204 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lelang lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">314 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","history","korea","china","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Han commanderies</b> were the frontier provinces through which the Han empire governed part of the Korean peninsula and its Manchurian borderlands after the destruction of Wiman Joseon in 108 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Hanshu</i> names four of them: Zhenfan, known in Korean as Chinbeon, Lintun or Imdun, Lelang or Nangnang, and Xuantu or Hyeondo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Of these only Lelang survived on the peninsula into the 1st century CE, and it lasted until Goguryeo took its territory in 314.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The familiar phrase “four commanderies” is therefore a simplification, since a further unit, Daifang or Taebang, was carved out in 204 CE and one source distinguishes a northern from a southern Daifang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting the whole span from the first Han intervention to the fall gives more than four centuries of external administration on Korean soil. <br><br> A commandery was an ordinary administrative division rather than a colony of settlers, governed by an official the central court appointed directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its business was recorded in writing: each commandery submitted annual accounts of households and land to the capital, a system the Han called shangji, and a set of wooden tablets from Pyongyang preserves one such return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where the commanderies actually stood is still argued about, and Zhenfan is the sharpest case, placed by some scholars north of the Gojoseon heartland and by others in southern Hwanghae, with a third view holding that it moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commanderies were not simply an imposition on a blank frontier, since the peninsula already had polities trading with the Chinese states before Han armies arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What they added was a permanent, literate, tax-collecting apparatus at the northern edge of the peninsula, and everything south of it had to deal with that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Four_Commanderies_of_Han%2C_with_English_Names.png/1280px-Four_Commanderies_of_Han%2C_with_English_Names.png","title":"The four commanderies of 108 BCE","desc":"Where Zhenfan, Lintun, Lelang and Xuantu were placed on and around the peninsula at their foundation.","credit":"Stillskywkpd, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Four_Commanderies_of_Han%2C_with_English_Names.png","alt":"A map of the Korean peninsula and southern Manchuria with four coloured administrative areas marked."}},
{"id":"ko-072","num":72,"category":"The commanderies","question":"The commandery centred on modern Pyongyang was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its wooden census tablets record 25 counties and about 400,000 people at its height.","questions":["Of the four commanderies only <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lasted on the peninsula past the 1st century CE, serving as the trading gate between China, the Three Han and Japan.","Korean readers call it Nangnang and Chinese sources call it <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and its cemeteries near Pyongyang are the richest Han-period tombs outside China."],"answer":"Lelang","answerText":"Lelang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Census tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">45 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lost to Goguryeo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">314 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","korea","china","archaeology"],"abstract":"<b>Lelang</b>, called Nangnang in Korean, was the Han commandery established in 108 BCE around what is now Pyongyang, and the only one of the original four to survive on the peninsula for any length of time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the point at which the Chinese empire, the Three Han of the south and the islands of Japan met and traded, and that position made it far more important than its size suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three wooden tablets recovered from a tomb at Jeongbaek-dong preserve its household return for 45 BCE, listing the number of households in each of 25 counties and the change on the previous year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That document shows the central court held a detailed and current picture of the population, which was only possible with an administration working from written records and law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At its peak the commandery held something like 400,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br>  The register also distinguishes the incoming households from the local ones, and the latter, the giho, make up about 86 per cent of the total.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two districts held most of them, with roughly 55,000 people in about 10,000 households in the northern one and just under 30,000 people in the southern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The material record is overwhelmingly funerary: perhaps 3,000 tombs have been excavated around Pyongyang, and almost nothing is known about the settlements where the living actually were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trade through Lelang carried Chinese goods deep into the southern peninsula and on to the Japanese islands, and southern polities used what arrived to mark rank at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Goguryeo took the territory in 314, ending the commandery, though titles carrying its name went on being granted by Chinese courts afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Incense_burner%2C_attributed_to_Nangnang%2C_%28Lelang%29_tomb%2C_Korea%2C_1st_to_3rd_century_AD%2C_ceramic%2C_green_glaze_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC08229.jpg/1280px-Incense_burner%2C_attributed_to_Nangnang%2C_%28Lelang%29_tomb%2C_Korea%2C_1st_to_3rd_century_AD%2C_ceramic%2C_green_glaze_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC08229.jpg","title":"An incense burner from a Lelang tomb","desc":"Green-glazed ceramic of the 1st to 3rd century CE, attributed to a tomb of the Nangnang commandery.","credit":"Daderot, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Incense_burner%2C_attributed_to_Nangnang%2C_%28Lelang%29_tomb%2C_Korea%2C_1st_to_3rd_century_AD%2C_ceramic%2C_green_glaze_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC08229.jpg","alt":"A tall green-glazed ceramic vessel with a domed, pierced lid on a slender stem and flared foot."}},
{"id":"ko-073","num":73,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Cups and boxes of wood coated in tree resin, or <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, are the most striking thing in the Han-period tombs around Pyongyang and are rare elsewhere in Korea.","questions":["Some of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> buried in the Lelang cemeteries is of the grade the imperial household itself used, which is unusual even in tombs excavated in China.","Because wood rots and bronze corrodes, the survival of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in waterlogged Pyongyang tombs gives an unusually direct view of what a frontier elite owned."],"answer":"lacquerware","answerText":"lacquerware","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lelang tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108 BCE – 314 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tomb 364 dug</span><span class=\"dt-v\">early 1990s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","archaeology","korea","china","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Lacquerware</b> is wood or fabric built up in layers of sap from the lacquer tree, which dries to a hard, glossy, water-resistant surface, and it was among the costliest products of the Han world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Korea it is above all associated with the tombs of the Lelang commandery, where bronze vessels, jades and expensive lacquer occur together in a way that is otherwise unusual in Korean archaeology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the pieces are of the same type used by the imperial house, objects valuable enough that their presence at Pyongyang is remarkable even by the standard of tombs excavated in China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their durability matters to what can be known: everyday things were made of wood and clay and have gone, while bronze, jade and lacquer survive, so the record is tilted towards the rich.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roughly 3,000 tombs have been dug around Pyongyang and very little of the living settlement has been touched at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The graves change over time, from log-framed chambers to brick-built ones, and the wealth in them changes with the architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One two-chamber brick tomb needed more than ten thousand bricks by a count taken from the walls at Yangdong-ni, which is a measure of what a local family could command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A wooden-chambered tomb at Jeongbaek-dong, numbered 364, held the commandery's household register on wooden tablets together with two volumes of the <i>Analects</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same cemetery produced a local chief buried with slender bronze daggers of Korean make, so imported luxury and local weaponry went into the ground side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading these tombs as simply Chinese misses that mixture, which is what makes them evidence about the people of the frontier rather than about the empire alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Pantings_on_Lacquar_Basket_Lo-lang.jpg/1280px-Pantings_on_Lacquar_Basket_Lo-lang.jpg","title":"Painted figures on the lacquer basket from Lelang","desc":"Bands of small painted figures from a lacquered basket recovered near Pyongyang, the best known piece of its kind.","credit":"Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pantings_on_Lacquar_Basket_Lo-lang.jpg","alt":"Two long bands of small seated figures in orange and red on a dark purple-blue lacquer ground."}},
{"id":"ko-074","num":74,"category":"The commanderies","question":"A polished disc with a decorated back and a central loop, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travelled from Han workshops through Lelang into southern Korean and Japanese graves.","questions":["Archaeologists read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in an Iron Age tomb south of the commandery as a marker of rank rather than as evidence that its owner had become Chinese.","Together with decorated iron staff-heads, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the object most often used to argue about how far Han material culture reached down the peninsula."],"answer":"bronze mirror","answerText":"bronze mirror","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Korean tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">made in Han workshops, traded through Lelang</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","archaeology","korea","china","technology"],"abstract":"A <b>bronze mirror</b> is a cast disc polished on one face and decorated on the other, usually with a loop at the centre of the back for a cord, and it was among the most widely traded objects of the Han world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Korea it matters because mirrors made in Chinese workshops turn up in Iron Age tombs far to the south of the commandery that supplied them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Together with decorated iron staff-heads, they are the objects most often used to argue about the reach of Han material culture down the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goods of this kind moved along an exchange network that ran through Lelang and on to the Japanese islands, and iron from the southern peninsula moved back along it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The distribution is uneven across the southern polities, and that unevenness is the interesting part of the evidence. <br><br>  The older reading was that such objects show the sinicization of the societies that received them, a one-way process in which local culture was progressively replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Recent work prefers to speak of hybridization, because the same graves that hold imported bronzes also hold locally made daggers, sheaths and pottery, and neither displaced the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A mirror in a southern tomb is better read as something a local leader acquired and used to mark rank at home than as a sign that its owner had adopted a foreign identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Treating “Korean” and “Chinese” as fixed cultural units in this period is itself anachronistic, since the boundary the objects supposedly crossed was not drawn where the modern one is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the mirrors record is contact, and contact between unequal partners does not have to mean absorption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/British_Museum_Han_TLV_Mirror.jpg/1280px-British_Museum_Han_TLV_Mirror.jpg","title":"A Han bronze mirror of TLV pattern","desc":"The back of a cast disc, with the T, L and V shapes that give this common Han design its name.","credit":"CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Han_TLV_Mirror.jpg","alt":"A circular bronze mirror back with a central knob and geometric T, L and V motifs around it."}},
{"id":"ko-075","num":75,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Whether the commandery stood at Pyongyang or far away in Liaoning is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an argument that has run for a century and is about more than geography.","questions":["Colonial surveys from 1909 onwards made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a political question, because a Chinese province on Korean soil suited an argument about Korean dependency.","Most specialists place the commandery around Pyongyang, but the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> continues because the alternative answers carry claims about who Korea has always been."],"answer":"Lelang location debate","answerText":"Lelang location debate","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First surveys</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1909</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Register published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2006</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","history","korea","nationalism","archaeology"],"abstract":"The <b>Lelang location debate</b> is the long argument over where the Han commandery of Lelang actually stood, and it is one of the most contested questions in the study of ancient Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most scholars place it at Pyongyang and its surroundings from its foundation, while most North Korean historians before the 2000s and some in the South have argued for a site in Liaoning, in north-eastern China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The question is old: scholars of the late Joseon period were already trying to fix the commanderies on the map from the geographical treatises of the Chinese histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It became political with the first archaeological surveys, begun in 1909, whose findings served an argument that Korean culture had always depended on China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That framing gave the answer a weight no ordinary question of historical geography carries, and it is why the argument has never settled into a technical one about sites and potsherds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br>  The archaeological evidence for a Chinese or Chinese-influenced population at Pyongyang in the 1st century BCE is substantial, and any argument that the commandery lay elsewhere has to account for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One response has been to attribute the Pyongyang remains to a separate native kingdom of the same name mentioned in the <i>Samguk sagi</i>, a position with a long history running back to the early 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most specialists are sceptical, since nothing outside that one legendary passage attests such a kingdom, and the passage dates its conquest two centuries before any other record does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What drives the dispute is not the map but the difficulty of admitting foreign rule into a national history written as continuous and unbroken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reading the commandery instead as a frontier society of mixed origins takes the question out of that frame altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Lothar von Falkenhausen, “Hyung Il Pai 裵炯逸 (14 June 1958 – 28 May 2018),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 209–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76779. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Taedong-gun_Lelang_Tomb_2.jpg","title":"A colonial survey plate of a Lelang tomb","desc":"Two photographs of the excavation of Tomb 2 at Taedong-gun, from the antiquities album the colonial government published.","credit":"Chōsen Sōtokufu, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taedong-gun_Lelang_Tomb_2.jpg","alt":"A printed album page carrying two sepia photographs above Japanese captions: figures on a grassy burial mound, and two men beside a brick tomb wall."}},
{"id":"ko-077","num":77,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Korean archaeologists call the four centuries between the Han conquest and the rise of Baekje and Silla the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a name that reads the era backwards from what followed.","questions":["Running from about 100 BCE to 300 CE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is also called the Late Iron Age, and neither label satisfies everyone who works on it.","The societies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include many that never became kingdoms at all, which is the strongest objection to naming an age after three that did."],"answer":"proto-Three Kingdoms period","answerText":"proto-Three Kingdoms period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also called</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Late Iron Age, Samhan period</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["era","history","korea","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>proto-Three Kingdoms period</b>, known in Korean as Wonsamguk, is the conventional name for the era on the southern Korean peninsula running from roughly 100 BCE to 300 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It closes with the emergence, in the late 3rd and early 4th centuries, of the kingdoms of Baekje in the Han River basin and Silla in the south-east of the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same span is also called the Late Iron Age, or the Samhan period after the three confederations of Han that occupied most of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of the three names is neutral, and the objection to this one is that it defines an age by what came after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many of the societies it covers never appear in any historical text and never became kingdoms, so naming the period for three that did leaves them out of their own history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  Whatever it is called, it was a time of unusually dense exchange across the whole of north-east Asia. The Lelang commandery in the north was the point of contact between the Chinese empire and the peninsular groups, and goods, techniques and writing moved south from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iron working, which had reached the southern Yeongnam region by the closing centuries BCE, spread and intensified, and iron from the south was carried across the strait towards the Japanese islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Settlements grew, pottery began to be thrown on a wheel and fired hard enough to count as stoneware, and burial goods separate the powerful from everybody else more sharply than before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether those changes amount to states forming, or to chiefdoms getting richer, is precisely the argument the period's name tries to settle in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Ilhong Ko, “The Earliest Introduction of Iron into the Yŏngnam Region: A Critical Examination of the Evidence,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 1–36, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Iron_Tools._1st_c._South_Korea.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg/1280px-Iron_Tools._1st_c._South_Korea.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","title":"Iron tools of the 1st century CE from southern Korea","desc":"A rake, sickles, axeheads and iron bars excavated at Gimhae, Jecheon and Gyeongju, from the centuries this period covers.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iron_Tools._1st_c._South_Korea.National_Museum_of_Korea.jpg","alt":"Eight heavily corroded iron implements laid out on white museum mounts: a three-pronged rake, two curved sickle blades, three broad axeheads and two long bars."}},
{"id":"ko-078","num":78,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Between a village and a state anthropologists put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a society with hereditary rank and a leader but no standing bureaucracy to enforce his will.","questions":["Applying the idea of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Korea lets archaeologists ask when Silla or Baekje stopped being one, rather than assuming a founding date from a chronicle.","Gaya is the clearest Korean case of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that never centralised, staying a set of related polities rather than becoming a single kingdom."],"answer":"chiefdom","answerText":"chiefdom","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"A <b>chiefdom</b> is, in the vocabulary anthropologists use for social scale, a society organised by hereditary rank under a leader whose authority is real but rests on kinship, ritual and wealth rather than on a permanent administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Studies of emerging chiefdoms and states concentrate on the appearance of hierarchical political institutions, economic specialisation and class-based society.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One thing that distinguishes a chiefly elite archaeologically is its command of prestige goods, whose distribution is the main material means by which such an elite builds and holds power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The advent of elites is arguably universal in both chiefdom and state formation, which is why the two are studied together rather than as separate subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The model comes from Western anthropology and reached Korean archaeology from outside it, which is part of why its use there has been argued over. <br><br>  Read against that framework, the polities named in the Korean sources can be asked how far each had actually gone: Joseon, Jin, Goguryeo, the Samhan, Gaya, Baekje and Silla have all been assessed this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gaya is the instructive case, because its several polities grew wealthy and left rich burials without ever combining into one centralised state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The approach has costs, since the categories were built from other parts of the world and a society can be pressed into one of them simply to make it comparable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also has a plain benefit: it lets the question of when a Korean kingdom became a kingdom be asked from excavated evidence rather than settled by a chronicle's founding date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the argument about the centuries after the Han commanderies is really an argument about where on that scale the southern societies stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Terence N. D’Altroy, “Comments: Rethinking Complex Early Societies in Asia,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 351–62, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17053. [Open access]","Laura Lee Junker, “Craft Goods Specialization and Prestige Goods Exchange in Philippine Chiefdoms of the Fifteenth and Sixteenth Centuries,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 32, no. 1 (1993): 1–35, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17018. [Open access]","Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-079","num":79,"category":"The commanderies","question":"The commandery of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, set up in 108 BCE and later shifted westward, is the one historians tie most closely to the formation of Goguryeo.","questions":["Korean sources call it Hyeondo and Chinese ones <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the argument about it is whether Han pressure created the kingdom that eventually destroyed it.","Unlike Lelang, the commandery of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not stay put, moving inland and north-west as the peoples it was meant to administer resisted it."],"answer":"Xuantu","answerText":"Xuantu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">108 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">later reorganised and moved west into Liaoning</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","history","korea","china","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Xuantu</b>, called Hyeondo in Korean, was one of the four commanderies the Han empire set up in 108 BCE after destroying Wiman Joseon, and it was the one placed furthest into the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Unlike Lelang, which stayed on the Taedong River for four centuries, Xuantu did not keep its ground: the sources record its reorganisation and relocation westward into what is now Liaoning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its territory covered the upper Yalu and the valleys where Goguryeo would form, so its history and that kingdom's are hard to separate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Han geographical writing links Xuantu with Zhenfan, one of the other original commanderies, which is part of why the locations of all four are still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The commandery is therefore studied less as a place on a map than as a pressure exerted on the peoples around it, and that is the argument it belongs to. <br><br>  The strongest case made for it is that Goguryeo formed as a state in response to Xuantu, and that the kingdom's early organisation cannot be understood without the commandery beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reading the Han administration supplied both the threat that made political consolidation worth the trouble and the goods and titles that a consolidating leader could distribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The argument is made from written sources read critically together with excavated material from Liaoning, rather than from either alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same pattern has been proposed elsewhere on the frontier, most fully for Buyeo, whose formation is read as a response to Yan and Han incursion in two phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Xuantu's own end came with the rest: Goguryeo, the polity it had helped to call into being, absorbed the commanderies in the early 4th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Richard D. McBride II, review of <i>The Ancient State of Puyŏ in Northeast Asia: Archeology and Historical Memory</i>, by Mark E. Byington, <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 183–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-183.pdf. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-080","num":80,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Imported bronzes, jades and lacquer buried with the powerful are <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and controlling who gets them is how a leader without a bureaucracy builds authority.","questions":["Archaeologists reading early Korean tombs treat <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as evidence of politics, since what an elite could obtain and hand on measures how far its reach extended.","Because they are both markers of rank and stores of wealth that can buy allies, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the material most closely watched in the study of emerging elites."],"answer":"prestige goods","answerText":"prestige goods","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Prestige goods</b> are objects whose value lies in what they signal rather than in what they do: imported bronzes, mirrors, jade, lacquer, fine weapons and ornaments, obtained through long-distance trade or made by specialists working for a leader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Control over their distribution is the principal material means by which an elite in a society without a standing bureaucracy maintains and extends political power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They serve two purposes at once, as unmistakable symbols of rank in a contest for standing, and as stores of wealth that can be handed out to build the alliances centralisation requires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Studies of chiefdom-level societies concentrate on how elites can be distinguished archaeologically and how they exercised power, and the manipulation of such goods is usually the answer to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A key condition is exclusive access, whether through position on a trade route or control of what a region can export.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  In Korea the pattern becomes legible from the Iron Age onwards, and it is legible in three places at once. In the borderlands north of the peninsula, changing assemblages of prestige goods are read as evidence that regional polities and their power structures were being reorganised, rather than as a simple story of new technology arriving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Lelang cemeteries, local elites went into the ground with imported bronze vessels, jades and lacquer beside locally made bronze daggers, which is a claim to both kinds of standing at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Further south, Han mirrors and decorated iron staff-heads reached tombs far from any commandery, and were used to mark rank at home rather than to announce a change of identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Watching what such objects were and who could get them is one of the few ways of tracing politics in societies that wrote nothing down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Laura Lee Junker, “Craft Goods Specialization and Prestige Goods Exchange in Philippine Chiefdoms of the Fifteenth and Sixteenth Centuries,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 32, no. 1 (1993): 1–35, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17018. [Open access]","Terence N. D’Altroy, “Comments: Rethinking Complex Early Societies in Asia,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 33, no. 2 (1994): 351–62, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17053. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-076","num":76,"category":"The commanderies","question":"The Goguryeo king remembered as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took the territory of Lelang in 314, ending more than four centuries of Chinese administration on the peninsula.","questions":["In the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the last commanderies fell, and Chinese records describe local officials leaving with a thousand households for Liaodong instead.","The <i>Samguk sagi</i> credits <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a raid on Lelang in his fourteenth year that carried off more than two thousand men and women."],"answer":"Micheon","answerText":"Micheon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Took Lelang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">314 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Raid recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">his 14th regnal year</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["ruler","person","history","korea","warfare"],"abstract":"<b>Micheon</b>, whose name is also written Michŏn, was the Goguryeo king in whose reign the Han commanderies on the Korean peninsula came to an end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goguryeo took the territory of Lelang in 314, closing a period of external administration that had begun with the Han conquest of Wiman Joseon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Samguk sagi</i> records that in his fourteenth year he attacked the Lelang commandery and captured more than two thousand men and women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Chinese chronicle describes the other side of the same collapse: Zhang Tong of Liaodong and Wang Zun of Lelang, with over a thousand households, submitted to the Murong and moved away to the Liaodong peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most scholars read those two records together as the annexation of the area, one describing the attack and the other the departure of the men who had been running it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The commanderies did not fall to a single blow so much as become unsustainable, and the reasons for that lie well outside the peninsula. The 4th century saw the Chinese-centred order of East Asia break up, and Goguryeo dealt directly with the Former Yan, the Later Zhao and the Eastern Jin in turn, building a sphere of influence of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same decades it opened relations with Baekje and Silla to the south, so the peninsula's politics stopped running through a Chinese province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chinese courts went on conferring titles built on the name Nangnang for a long time afterwards, which suggests the prestige of the place outlasted its administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> North of the peninsula the same century was violent for everyone, and the kingdom of Buyeo, long the strongest power of the Songhua plain, was overrun in 346.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Richard D. McBride II, review of <i>The Ancient State of Puyŏ in Northeast Asia: Archeology and Historical Memory</i>, by Mark E. Byington, <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 183–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-183.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-081","num":81,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Built by piling and beating down soil rather than by stacking stone, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the commonest defended site of the northern frontier in this period.","questions":["Archaeologists argue over whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Hagoseongja was raised by Han officials, by Goguryeo copying them, or by Goguryeo alone.","About seventy Han examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are known across north-east China, and almost none has been excavated in section."],"answer":"earthen fortress","answerText":"earthen fortress","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","archaeology","korea","china","technology"],"abstract":"An <b>earthen fortress</b> is a defended enclosure whose walls are built of soil, laid in layers and compacted, rather than of stacked stone, and it is the commonest fortified form on the Manchurian and northern Korean frontier in this period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roughly seventy of them survive from the Han dynasty across north-east China, but almost none has been investigated in section, so how they were actually built is poorly known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best-studied is Hagoseongja, a flatland site near Huanren in Liaoning, in the valleys where Goguryeo began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was first surveyed in 1978 and properly excavated twenty years later, when a trench was cut through the western wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That wall has a broad core with flanking sections whose soil layers interlock, which suggests core and flanks went up together rather than one against the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  Who built it is genuinely disputed, and the dispute matters because it is a question about the frontier as a whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some scholars hold that the fortress is Han work later taken over by Goguryeo, some that Goguryeo built it using Han techniques, and some that Goguryeo built it with no Han involvement at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether the walls were rammed between wooden shuttering, the method that would most clearly indicate Chinese practice, could not be settled from the trench, since no trace of the frames survived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stone mountain fortresses are the form Goguryeo is famous for, and they have absorbed most of the attention, which is part of why the earthen ones remain obscure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commanderies and the polities around them were separated less by walls than by what the walls were made of and who had the labour to raise them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bonggeun Kim, “Construction Techniques and Significance of the Hagosŏngja Fortress of Koguryŏ,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 2 (2025): 117–51, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-2-117.pdf. [Open access]","HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-082","num":82,"category":"The commanderies","question":"The steppe people known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed Buyeo in 346 and took in the officials who fled the collapsing commanderies a generation earlier.","questions":["Chinese historians filed their account of Korea in a chapter on the Wuhuan, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the eastern peoples, which says how they saw the whole northern frontier.","Goguryeo dealt with states founded by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> throughout the 4th century, among them the Former Yan, whose armies reached deep into Manchuria."],"answer":"Xianbei","answerText":"Xianbei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Overran Buyeo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">346 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">the Former Yan, a state founded by the Murong Xianbei</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["people","history","korea","china","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Xianbei</b> were a horse-riding people of the eastern steppe who descended, with the Wuhuan, from the Donghu, and who came to dominate Manchuria as Han power failed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese historians treated them and Korea's polities as one frontier problem: the chapter of the <i>Sanguozhi</i> that carries the earliest description of the Korean peninsula is headed as memoirs on the Wuhuan, the Xianbei and the eastern peoples together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Donghu had already shaped the peninsula's history indirectly, since the campaign in which the state of Yan drove them back was the same eastward push that struck Gojoseon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yan then built a long wall to keep the Hu out, and Qin's later offensive against Gojoseon ran alongside its own campaign against the Xiongnu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Korea's northern frontier was never only a Chinese one, and the peoples on the far side of it were part of every calculation made there. <br><br>  When the commanderies fell, it was to Goguryeo in the south and the Xianbei in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A Chinese chronicle records the officials of Lelang and Liaodong going over to the Murong, a Xianbei house, with more than a thousand households, and moving away to the Liaodong peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Murong went on to found the Former Yan, one of several states with which Goguryeo had to deal in the 4th century, alongside the Later Zhao and the Eastern Jin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 346 the Murong Xianbei destroyed Buyeo, the oldest of the kingdoms of the north; some of its elite fled south to the Central Plains and others north, and Goguryeo took over its heartland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Steppe goods and steppe styles had been reaching the borderlands for centuries before that, so the arrival was cultural long before it was military.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Richard D. McBride II, review of <i>The Ancient State of Puyŏ in Northeast Asia: Archeology and Historical Memory</i>, by Mark E. Byington, <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 183–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-183.pdf. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Xianbei_warrior_horsemen_armed_with_long_bows._Northern_Qi_dynasty_%28%E5%8C%97%E9%BD%8A_550%E2%80%93577_CE%29%2C_Taiyuan%2C_Shanxi_Province.jpg/1280px-Xianbei_warrior_horsemen_armed_with_long_bows._Northern_Qi_dynasty_%28%E5%8C%97%E9%BD%8A_550%E2%80%93577_CE%29%2C_Taiyuan%2C_Shanxi_Province.jpg","title":"Xianbei horsemen, painted in the 6th century","desc":"A wall painting from Taiyuan of the Northern Qi period, two centuries after the events on this card.","credit":"6th century painting, China, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xianbei_warrior_horsemen_armed_with_long_bows._Northern_Qi_dynasty_%28%E5%8C%97%E9%BD%8A_550%E2%80%93577_CE%29%2C_Taiyuan%2C_Shanxi_Province.jpg","alt":"A wall painting of four men in white, ochre and red robes, each carrying a long bow, on a pale ground."}},
{"id":"ko-083","num":83,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Plates of iron laced together into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are found in Korean tombs of this era, and they cluster in one region rather than spreading evenly.","questions":["The murals of the northern tombs show men in civil dress and no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, which is one reason the frontier's militarisation is argued about.","Because it survives where organic gear does not, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries more of the argument about early Korean warfare than any other class of object."],"answer":"armour","answerText":"armour","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In tomb murals</span><span class=\"dt-v\">357 CE and 408 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Anak tomb 3 and the Deokheung-ri tomb</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","warfare","korea","archaeology","technology"],"abstract":"<b>Armour</b> is protective equipment worn in battle, and in early Korea it survives chiefly as iron: plates cut, pierced and laced together, which endure in a grave where leather, cloth and wood do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That survival makes it carry more of the argument about early Korean warfare than any other class of object, and it also distorts the picture, since what is not iron is largely missing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finds are not evenly spread: most excavated iron armour comes from the tombs of Gaya, in the south-east, which is why studies of it turn quickly into studies of one region's relations with its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its occurrence in ranked burials has been analysed as a measure of who stood where, rather than simply as evidence that fighting happened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Weapons are commoner than armour and are read the same way. <br><br>  In the commandery tombs around Pyongyang, weapons are frequent enough to suggest that being a warrior mattered socially, though that is true of most ancient societies and is not peculiar to a frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is striking there is a decline: the quantity of weapons and military equipment in the tombs falls over time, and the painted figures in the northern tombs of the 4th and 5th centuries wear civil dress rather than armour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A frontier province plainly needed defences more elaborate than an interior one, so the decline is a fact to be explained rather than evidence that the place was peaceful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iron for all of it was available from the southern peninsula, which supplied the commanderies as well as its own polities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fortification is the other half of the subject, and on the northern frontier it is earthwork and stone rather than anything a body could carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Ilhong Ko, “The Earliest Introduction of Iron into the Yŏngnam Region: A Critical Examination of the Evidence,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 1–36, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Bonggeun Kim, “Construction Techniques and Significance of the Hagosŏngja Fortress of Koguryŏ,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 2 (2025): 117–51, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-2-117.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Gaya_Confederacy_Iron_Armor_%26_Helmet.jpg/1280px-Gaya_Confederacy_Iron_Armor_%26_Helmet.jpg","title":"Iron armour and helmet from Gaya","desc":"Plates cut, pierced and laced together, of the kind that dominates the excavated record in the south-east.","credit":"Gary Lee Todd, Ph.D., CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaya_Confederacy_Iron_Armor_%26_Helmet.jpg","alt":"A rust-brown iron cuirass of laced vertical plates on a museum stand, with a domed iron helmet mounted above it."}},
{"id":"ko-084","num":84,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Bars of worked metal that served both as raw material and as money are <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a Chinese account says Korea's south used them exactly as China used coins.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the southern peninsula's iron portable, and the same account records the two commanderies drawing their supply from there.","One 5th-century Korean tomb held 872 examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, buried as an offering, which is a measure of what the metal was worth."],"answer":"iron ingot","answerText":"iron ingot","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron reaches the south</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 – 200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Traded as currency</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 – 300 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","technology","korea","asia","trade"],"abstract":"An <b>iron ingot</b> is a bar or plate of smelted iron, made to be carried, stored and worked later, and in early Korea it was also money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Sanguozhi</i> says of Byeonhan, in the south-east, that iron was produced there and that the Han, the Ye and the Wa all came to get it; that all buying and selling in the markets was done with iron, as coins are used in China; and that it was supplied to the two commanderies as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That single passage has carried an enormous amount of Korean history, since it makes iron the medium connecting the south-east to the wider East Asian world and the motor of Byeonhan's growth into Gaya.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How the technology arrived is contested. The traditional view derived it from the Lelang commandery after 108 BCE, but excavations at Wanju in the early 2000s produced iron that predates the commandery, and the earliest introduction is now put at around the 3rd century BCE in the south-west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The objects themselves changed shape over time, the earlier axe-like forms giving way in the 4th century to thick flat bars made purely for exchange.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were exported across the strait in quantity, and smiths in northern Kyushu worked imported Korean ingots rather than smelting their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One Korean tomb of about 450 CE was furnished with 872 of them, alongside more than a hundred iron tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iron in this form is therefore three things at once: a commodity, a currency and a diplomatic gift, and a hoard of it in a grave says something about all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is also the clearest case in this period of a Korean product on which neighbours depended, rather than the reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ilhong Ko, “The Earliest Introduction of Iron into the Yŏngnam Region: A Critical Examination of the Evidence,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 1–36, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Laura Lee Junker, “Craft Goods Specialization and Prestige Goods Exchange in Philippine Chiefdoms of the Fifteenth and Sixteenth Centuries,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 32, no. 1 (1993): 1–35, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17018. [Open access]","Kisung Yi, “Transition from the Prehistoric Age to the Historic Age: The Early Iron Age on the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 54, no. 1 (2015): 185–206, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/55551. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-085","num":85,"category":"The commanderies","question":"A pit lined and roofed with timber to make a room for the dead is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it is the characteristic grave of Korea in these centuries.","questions":["Where the stone cist held one body, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held a chamber that could take a coffin and a quantity of goods beside it.","The great mounds of Gyeongju were raised over a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the type is found from the Taedong valley to the southern coast."],"answer":"wooden-chamber tomb","answerText":"wooden-chamber tomb","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE – 500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gyeongju mounds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">356 – 514 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","archaeology","korea","asia"],"abstract":"A <b>wooden-chamber tomb</b> is a grave in which a timber-lined and timber-roofed room is built inside a pit, holding a coffin and grave goods, and it is the characteristic burial of the Korean peninsula in the centuries either side of the Common Era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It replaces the stone cist of the Bronze Age, and it makes a difference to what survives: a chamber has room for offerings that a cist has not, so tombs of this kind are where the period's metalwork, lacquer and pottery are found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Lelang the type is present from early on, and the household register of the commandery came out of one of them, at Jeongbaek-dong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same cemetery produced a local chief buried in a timber frame with weapons of Korean make, so the form is not a Chinese import laid over a native population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Timber rots, so what is excavated is the stain and the fill rather than the room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br>  In the south-east the type is the ancestor of something much larger. Around Gyeongju more than 150 mounds cluster near the old Silla palace, and the great ones were built over wooden chambers heaped with stone and then with earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of them went up within a span of about 160 years, from the mid-4th to the early 6th century, which is far too short a period for them all to hold rulers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Differences in size and in what was buried have therefore been read as marks of rank, and the largest have been read as a response to political strain rather than simply as the graves of the greatest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Brick chambers replace timber ones at Lelang and stone chambers do so in the southern kingdoms, and in each case the change in material marks a change in who was being buried and by whom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Sebastian Müller, “Monumental Burial Mounds in Kyŏngju: Remarks on their Socio-political Meaning,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 2 (2019): 133–70, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-2-133.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Daeneungwon-Gyeongju-Korea-2006-11.jpg/1280px-Daeneungwon-Gyeongju-Korea-2006-11.jpg","title":"The mounds of Daereungwon at Gyeongju","desc":"Great earth mounds raised over timber chambers heaped with stone, in the largest such cemetery in Korea.","credit":"riNux from Taipei, Taiwan, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daeneungwon-Gyeongju-Korea-2006-11.jpg","alt":"Two enormous grass-covered domed burial mounds rising against a blue sky, with a path running below them."}},
{"id":"ko-086","num":86,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Bronze mirrors, bells and knives from these graves are usually explained by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on the strength of their resemblance to what Korean ritual specialists still carry.","questions":["The fine-lined mirrors and eight-armed rattles of the southern peninsula are read as the equipment of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, buried with the people who used them.","Archaeologists connect the ritual objects of early Korea to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and to a set of northern steppe practices whose relation to them is argued about."],"answer":"shamanism","answerText":"shamanism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","religion","korea","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Shamanism</b> is the name given to religious practice in which a specialist enters an altered state in order to deal with spirits on the community's behalf, and it is the framework within which the ritual objects of early Korea are usually explained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objects are specific and recognisable: bronze mirrors with fine linear designs, developed out of earlier and coarser Gojoseon types, and bronze rattles, buried with slender daggers in the graves of the Bronze and Early Iron Age south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Specialists interpret those mirrors as ritual equipment tied to a shamanistic belief system, and the daggers beside them as elite weapons standing for political authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two categories are found together, which is the interesting part: whoever conducted the ritual and whoever held the power were often the same person, or at least buried the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Steppe elements travel with them, including bronze ornaments of northern type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The identification has to be handled carefully, because of how it was arrived at. It rests on the resemblance between the bronze mirrors, bells and knives of these graves and the paraphernalia of Korean shamans of the present day, which is a suggestive observation rather than a demonstration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One reviewer of the Korean literature has said as much outright: the affinity is interesting, and more systematically presented evidence is needed before it convinces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the archaeology supplies is a set of objects and a pattern of deposition, and calling that pattern shamanism is an interpretation laid over it, alongside a second claim connecting it to northern steppe practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What can be said without argument is that ritual mattered enough to be equipped, that its equipment was made of the most valuable material available, and that the same graves hold the weapons of power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Yoonhee Hong, “Return of Myth, Myth Resources, and the Contemporaneity of Mythology in Korea and China Today,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 325–54, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-325.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Eight-armed_Bronze_Rattles.jpg/1280px-Eight-armed_Bronze_Rattles.jpg","title":"An eight-armed bronze rattle","desc":"One of the ritual bronzes from Daegok-ri in Hwasun, of the kind read as the equipment of a ritual specialist.","credit":"Leedkmn, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eight-armed_Bronze_Rattles.jpg","alt":"Two flat bronze star shapes, each with eight arms radiating from a centre and ending in a pierced spherical bell."}},
{"id":"ko-087","num":87,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Completed in 1145 by a Goryeo minister and his staff, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest surviving Korean history and the frame every later one was built on.","questions":["Everything known about the early kingdoms from a Korean pen comes through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, written a thousand years after the events it opens with.","Historians treat the early chapters of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with caution, because its account of the 1st century conflicts with everything else on record."],"answer":"Samguk sagi","answerText":"Samguk sagi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Completed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1145</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">compiled by Kim Busik and the Goryeo court</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","literature","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Samguk sagi</b>, the History of the Three Kingdoms, is the oldest surviving Korean historical work, compiled by Kim Busik and officials of the Goryeo court and completed in 1145.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is arranged as annals, one set for each of the three kingdoms, and it is the frame within which the history of the peninsula has been written ever since — the modern scholarly editions still cite it by its Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla annals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its importance is hard to overstate and so is the distance it stands at: the events it opens with are a thousand years older than the pen that recorded them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything it says about the earliest centuries therefore reaches a reader through a 12th-century court's idea of what mattered and what could be believed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where it can be checked against Chinese records the two often disagree, and the disagreements are the most closely studied passages in the book. <br><br>  The clearest case is its account of a place called Lelang, which it says Goguryeo conquered in 32 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Almost everything else on record puts Goguryeo's capture of the Lelang commandery in the early 4th century, and the tale's drum and horn that sound of their own accord are plainly not a report of an event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most scholars have read it instead as a later story, invented after the real conquest and modelled on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That does not make the work unusable; it makes it a source whose layers have to be separated, and the separating is most of what study of the early period consists of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its compiler drew on older Korean records now lost, which is why even a doubted passage is worth reading closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Maurizio Riotto, “The Strange Story of Monk Ariyabalma,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 26, no. 1 (2021): 1–34, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-26-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Lothar von Falkenhausen, “Hyung Il Pai 裵炯逸 (14 June 1958 – 28 May 2018),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 209–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76779. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-088","num":88,"category":"The commanderies","question":"The Buddhist monk <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who lived from 1206 to 1289, put together in five books the collection of tales that preserves the Dangun story.","questions":["Where the court history keeps to annals and treatises, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathered miracles, foundation legends and monks' lives that the official record had left out.","A generation after the Haedong goseung jeon was written to royal order, the monk <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> compiled a very different account of the same centuries."],"answer":"Iryeon","answerText":"Iryeon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1206</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1289</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["person","literature","religion","korea","history"],"abstract":"<b>Iryeon</b>, who lived from 1206 to 1289, was the Buddhist master who compiled the <i>Samguk yusa</i>, the Memorabilia of the Three Kingdoms, in five books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the counterpart of the court history completed a century before it, and it is a different kind of book: a gathering of foundation legends, miracles, monks' lives, poems and local traditions rather than a set of annals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it preserves is largely what the official history had no room for, and the Dangun story survives because he wrote it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was working in a Goryeo on the brink of national crisis, the Mongols having invaded in that century, and his book belongs with the <i>Chewang ungi</i> of the same decades as a product of that moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was also not the first monk to attempt a work of the kind, and knowing what he had in front of him matters. <br><br>  The <i>Haedong goseung jeon</i>, the Lives of Eminent Korean Monks, had been completed by royal order in 1215, some seventy years before him, and he clearly knew it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Comparing the two, and both against the court history and the Chinese records, is how the little that is known about several early figures has been established at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The differences matter as much as the agreements: where he follows a Chinese pilgrim's account rather than the Korean one, that is a decision he made and can be examined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His book is not an archive and does not pretend to be one, which is a reason to read it carefully rather than to discount it, exactly as the court history it stands beside has to be read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of what modern Korea takes as its oldest inheritance comes from these five books rather than from anything older.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Maurizio Riotto, “The Strange Story of Monk Ariyabalma,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 26, no. 1 (2021): 1–34, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-26-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Soo-ja Kim, “The Modern Korean Nation, Tan'gun, and Historical Memory in Late Nineteenth to Early Twentieth Century Korea,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 19, no. 2 (2014): 213–40, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-19-2-213.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Lothar von Falkenhausen, “Hyung Il Pai 裵炯逸 (14 June 1958 – 28 May 2018),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 209–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76779. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"ko-089","num":89,"category":"The commanderies","question":"The oldest description of the Korean peninsula by anyone at all is in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a Chinese dynastic history whose thirtieth chapter covers the eastern peoples.","questions":["Almost everything known about the Samhan comes from one chapter of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lists their names, their customs and the iron they traded.","Compiled in the 3rd century, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> describes Korea's societies within living memory of them, which no Korean source can claim."],"answer":"Sanguozhi","answerText":"Sanguozhi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Compiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">its 30th chapter carries the Korean accounts</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","literature","history","china","korea"],"abstract":"The <b>Sanguozhi</b>, the Records of the Three Kingdoms, is the Chinese dynastic history compiled in the 3rd century whose thirtieth chapter contains the oldest surviving description of the peoples of the Korean peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That chapter is headed as memoirs on the Wuhuan, the Xianbei and the eastern peoples, so Korea's societies are described within a survey of the whole northern and eastern frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its account of the Han is where the three-part division into Mahan, Jinhan and Byeonhan is first set out, and where the small polities that made them up are named.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the source of the passage about iron: that it was produced in Byeonhan, that the Han, the Ye and the Wa all came to get it, that markets used it as China used coins, and that it supplied the two commanderies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No Korean text describes any of this, and none was written for another nine hundred years. <br><br>  Its authority rests on nearness rather than on sympathy. The description is close in time to what it describes, which is exactly what the Korean histories cannot offer, and its statements can sometimes be checked against excavated material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> But it was written from outside and from above, by a court for which these were frontier peoples, and it says what such a court wanted recorded: names, customs, distances, tribute and produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading it as a snapshot of a moment rather than as a description of a stable order is one of the corrections modern scholarship has made to how it is used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two earlier histories, the <i>Shiji</i> and the <i>Hanshu</i>, cover the conquest and the commanderies but say much less about the societies to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]","Ilhong Ko, “The Earliest Introduction of Iron into the Yŏngnam Region: A Critical Examination of the Evidence,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 1–36, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG/1280px-A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG","title":"A manuscript fragment of the Records of the Three Kingdoms","desc":"Part of the biography of Bu Zhi, copied in the official script of the Eastern Jin, the oldest surviving witness to the text.","credit":"猫猫的日记本 (Cat's diary), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG","alt":"A sheet of aged manuscript covered in columns of brushed Chinese characters, torn away along its lower edge."}},
{"id":"ko-090","num":90,"category":"The commanderies","question":"Weighing a text against its date, its author's purpose and every other record is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and early Korea cannot be studied at all without it.","questions":["Applying <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Korean histories shows their earliest passages conflicting with the Chinese records and sometimes with each other.","The compiler of the 13th-century collection practised a rough <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> himself, setting rival accounts of one place side by side and saying he could not choose."],"answer":"source criticism","answerText":"source criticism","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Source criticism</b> is the practice of asking of any record when it was made, by whom, for what purpose and against what else it can be checked, before asking what it says.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For early Korea it is not an optional refinement, because every written source has a disqualifying feature: the Korean histories were compiled a thousand years after the events they open with, and the Chinese ones were written from outside by a power at war with some of their subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The archaeological record is no more neutral, since almost all of it comes from the tombs of the rich, leaving nothing from the people who were ruled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Every account that survives, written or buried, was made by somebody who won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Knowing that does not make the sources useless; what it does is decide which questions may honestly be put to them and which may not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The method is not a modern invention, and the compilers of the Korean histories practised a rough version of it themselves. Writing in the 13th century about where Lelang had been, Iryeon set the rival identifications beside one another and concluded that it was hard to tell who was right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A modern reader can do rather more, because the Chinese records, the Korean ones and the excavated material are three independent bodies of evidence that can be played against each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is how the tale of a 1st-century conquest of Lelang came to be understood as a story shaped by a real conquest three centuries later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also why the least reliable passages are often the most discussed, since a claim that cannot be checked is a claim about the people who made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Maurizio Riotto, “The Strange Story of Monk Ariyabalma,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 26, no. 1 (2021): 1–34, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-26-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Lothar von Falkenhausen, “Hyung Il Pai 裵炯逸 (14 June 1958 – 28 May 2018),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 209–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76779. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-091","num":91,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"Goguryeo, Baekje and Silla are the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a name that has stuck although a fourth power shared the peninsula with them for most of the period.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> take their conventional name from a 12th-century history, and the era it covers ends when two of them are destroyed within eight years.","Historians keep the phrase <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while pointing out that Gaya was a real presence in the south until the 6th century."],"answer":"Three Kingdoms of Korea","answerText":"Three Kingdoms of Korea","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 – 668 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Baekje destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">660 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Goguryeo destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">668 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["era","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>Three Kingdoms of Korea</b> is the conventional name for the era in which Goguryeo in the north, Baekje in the south-west and Silla in the south-east dominated the peninsula, and for those three states together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a convention rather than a description, and its best-known weakness is that it undercounts: the Gaya polities of the south held the lower Nakdong valley into the 6th century without ever combining into a single state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their material culture is indistinguishable from Silla's in the early centuries, which is one reason the boundary between the two is drawn differently by different scholars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each of the three took shape at a different moment, Baekje being reckoned to have become a state in the mid to late 3rd century and Silla's monumental phase beginning only in the mid 4th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The era they name runs, on the usual reckoning, from the late 3rd century to the late 7th. <br><br>  It ends in a war that involved all of East Asia. Tang China, founded in 618, expanded through the 630s and joined with Silla to attack the other two: Baekje was destroyed in 660 and Goguryeo in 668.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Silla then fought its own ally, the Silla-Tang war of 670 to 676 ending Tang power on the peninsula and leaving Silla to rule it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within a decade the three had become one, which is why the period is usually taught as a story with an ending rather than as a long equilibrium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That ending is also why so much of what is known about all three comes from the archives of the state that outlived them and from the epitaphs of the people who fled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]","Junkyu Kim, “An Alternative Approach to the Expansion of Paekche during the Hansŏng Period,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 41–69, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-41.pdf. [Open access]","Sebastian Müller, “Monumental Burial Mounds in Kyŏngju: Remarks on their Socio-political Meaning,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 2 (2019): 133–70, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-2-133.pdf. [Open access]","Kiheinarichika Ueda, “The Genealogy in the Koguryŏ Diaspora’s Epitaph,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 2 (2022): 31–72, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-2-31.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/53/History_of_Korea-476.PNG","title":"The peninsula in 476","desc":"Goguryeo at its greatest extent, with Baekje and Silla to the south and the Gaya polities between them.","credit":"Myself, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:History_of_Korea-476.PNG","alt":"A relief map of the Korean peninsula and Manchuria with several territories outlined and named in English."}},
{"id":"ko-093","num":93,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"A stone <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> buried with a Korean exile in Tang China records a life the Korean histories never mention, and hundreds have been excavated.","questions":["Because it was cut at the moment of death, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is closer to its subject than any Korean chronicle, though it was written to praise rather than to inform.","Modern study of Baekje and Goguryeo has been transformed by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a class of source unknown to the compilers of the Korean histories."],"answer":"epitaph","answerText":"epitaph","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Tang China</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 660 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">hundreds excavated, cut for Korean exiles and their heirs</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","literature","history","korea","china"],"abstract":"An <b>epitaph</b>, in this context a <i>myojimyeong</i>, is an inscribed stone slab buried in a tomb recording the dead person's descent, career and achievements, and it is the most important new source for early Korea to appear in the last century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hundreds have been excavated in China, cut for Korean exiles and their descendants who served the Tang after the fall of Baekje and Goguryeo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Research on Baekje had long been limited by the sheer scarcity of material, and these stones, together with wooden tablets, changed what could be asked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are contemporary in a way no Korean chronicle is, being cut within days of a death that the histories compiled six centuries later do not mention at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That does not make them neutral, and the part of an epitaph a historian most wants is the part most likely to have been shaped. <br><br>  An epitaph was written to praise the family's origin and its ancestors, so the genealogy at the head of it is a claim about status rather than a record of descent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The epitaphs of the Ye family, Baekje men who rose high in Tang service, are the clearest case: whether they were natives of Baekje or of Chinese descent is disputed, and their stated ancestry becomes more specific with each generation of stones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read as a series rather than one at a time, they show a family constructing a past that suited the place it had ended up in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Used carefully they still supply what nothing else does, including names, offices and dates for people the Korean record lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They belong with the other excavated documents of the period rather than with the transmitted histories, and they are read by the same methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Dongmin Lim, “The Native Origins of the Paekche Refugee Ye (禰) Family and the Background of Their Activities in the Tang Dynasty,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 83–114, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-83.pdf. [Open access]","Kiheinarichika Ueda, “The Genealogy in the Koguryŏ Diaspora’s Epitaph,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 2 (2022): 31–72, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-2-31.pdf. [Open access]","Nari Kang, “A Study of Past Research on Sŏngsan Fortress Wooden Tablets and an Examination of Excavated Wooden Tablet Documents,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 115–54, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-115.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Jongrok Lee, “A Reconsideration of the Account of the Lelang Kingdom in <i>Samguk Sagi</i>,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 29, no. 2 (2024): 165–204, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-29-2-165.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-094","num":94,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"Goguryeo's characteristic defence was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a walled enclosure following a ridge line, and its capitals were paired with one for retreat.","questions":["Warfare between the three kingdoms turned on sieges rather than open battles, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the most conspicuous military remain of the period.","The Gwanggaeto stele records its king's conquests mostly as a list of captured strongholds, each of them a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a city."],"answer":"mountain fortress","answerText":"mountain fortress","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","warfare","korea","archaeology","asia"],"abstract":"A <b>mountain fortress</b> is a defended enclosure whose walls follow the contours of a ridge or hilltop, and it is the characteristic fortification of the Korean Three Kingdoms, above all of Goguryeo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goguryeo's capitals were built in pairs, a walled town on flat ground beside a fortress on the height above it to retreat into, and the sites at Ji'an and Huanren preserve both halves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chains of them guarded the approaches to those capitals, and their study is central to understanding how the kingdom held its provinces at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The form spread: Goguryeo's mountain fortresses, defence systems and administrative practices were among the things the southern kingdoms and the Japanese islands took from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earthwork was the older method on this frontier, and stone the one Goguryeo came to be known for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br>  Warfare between the three kingdoms was accordingly a matter of sieges and of holding ground rather than of decisive battles in the open. The inscription on the stele raised for King Gwanggaeto in 414 records his conquests mainly as the names of strongholds taken and of states that submitted, which is what a campaign in this landscape consisted of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fortresses were also administrative places, not merely military ones: the wooden tablets from Seongsan Fortress in Haman are tax labels, so a garrison was a collection point as well as a strongpoint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their positions map the frontier better than any text does, since a wall was built where it was needed rather than where a chronicle says the border ran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war that ended the period took several years of fighting before the combined forces of Silla and Tang destroyed Baekje in 660 and Goguryeo in 668.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Bonggeun Kim, “Construction Techniques and Significance of the Hagosŏngja Fortress of Koguryŏ,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 2 (2025): 117–51, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-2-117.pdf. [Open access]","Jong-Rok Lee, “An Analysis on the Contents of the Stele of Koguryŏ in Ji’an with Regard to Koguryŏ’s Reorganization of Sumyoje,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 47–81, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-47.pdf. [Open access]","Nari Kang, “A Study of Past Research on Sŏngsan Fortress Wooden Tablets and an Examination of Excavated Wooden Tablet Documents,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 115–54, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-115.pdf. [Open access]","Kiheinarichika Ueda, “The Genealogy in the Koguryŏ Diaspora’s Epitaph,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 2 (2022): 31–72, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-2-31.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Korea_Samnyeongsanseong_Fortress_05_%2815344437409%29.jpg/1280px-Korea_Samnyeongsanseong_Fortress_05_%2815344437409%29.jpg","title":"Samnyeon Sanseong, a Silla mountain fortress","desc":"A stone wall of the Three Kingdoms period following the contour of a hill in Boeun, north Chungcheong.","credit":"Republic of Korea from Seoul, Republic of Korea, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea_Samnyeongsanseong_Fortress_05_%2815344437409%29.jpg","alt":"A high wall of small fitted stones running along a hillside, overgrown with creeper at its foot, against a pale sky."}},
{"id":"ko-095","num":95,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"A Chinese court's grant of a title to a foreign ruler was <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the Korean kingdoms sought it from whichever dynasty was nearest and strongest.","questions":["Titles conferred by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlived the things they described, Chinese courts still granting Lelang titles long after that commandery had ceased to exist.","Because it cost the granting court nothing and gave the receiving ruler standing at home, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suited both sides of an unequal relationship."],"answer":"investiture","answerText":"investiture","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["practice","history","korea","china","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Investiture</b> is the formal grant of a title and insignia by a superior court to a ruler who acknowledges its precedence, and it was the ordinary currency of diplomacy between China and its neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Korean kingdoms sought it from whichever dynasty was nearest and strongest, which in the 4th century meant dealing in turn with the Former Yan, the Later Zhao and the Eastern Jin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a ruler bought was standing at home: a title from the imperial court settled arguments about precedence among his own aristocracy that his armies might not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the granting court bought was an acknowledgement, and it cost nothing but a robe, a seal and a form of words.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The asymmetry was real, both sides understood it, and neither party mistook a title for the government of a country. <br><br>  One measure of how far the titles were symbols rather than descriptions is that they could outlive the things they named. Chinese courts went on conferring dignities built on the name of Lelang for a long time after the commandery itself had ceased to exist, because the name carried prestige even when the place had gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Goguryeo used its dealings with the northern dynasties to build a sphere of influence of its own, opening relations with Baekje and Silla and behaving towards them rather as the Chinese courts behaved towards it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After the fall of Baekje and Goguryeo the same machinery absorbed their elites, and Tang ranks and offices are what their epitaphs record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Investiture was therefore not simply submission but a language, and a great many different relationships between very unequal parties could be conducted in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Nguyen Thi My Hanh, “Tributary Activities of Vietnam and Korea with China: Similarities and Differences,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 26, no. 1 (2021): 117–44, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-26-1-117.pdf. [Open access]","HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Andrea de Benedittis, “Discontinuities and Discrepancies in the Hybridization Process of Nangnang Culture,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 1 (2022): 245–87, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-1-245.pdf. [Open access]","Dongmin Lim, “The Native Origins of the Paekche Refugee Ye (禰) Family and the Background of Their Activities in the Tang Dynasty,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 83–114, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-83.pdf. [Open access]","Kiheinarichika Ueda, “The Genealogy in the Koguryŏ Diaspora’s Epitaph,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 2 (2022): 31–72, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-2-31.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-097","num":97,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"Chinese and Korean records call the people of the Japanese islands the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and they appear from the earliest account of the peninsula onwards.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to the southern peninsula to buy iron, and the stele of a Goguryeo king records fighting against them in the 4th century.","Agriculture, ironworking, stoneware and writing all reached the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from Korea across a thousand years of contact, along with the people who carried them."],"answer":"Wa","answerText":"Wa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On the stele</span><span class=\"dt-v\">414 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["people","history","korea","japan","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Wa</b> is the name Chinese and Korean sources of this period give to the people of the Japanese islands, and it is how they appear in every early record that mentions them at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They appear in the oldest surviving description of the Korean peninsula: the account of Byeonhan says that iron was produced there and that the Han, the Ye and the Wa all came to get it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Contact across the strait was continuous and ran in both directions, but the flow of techniques was overwhelmingly from the peninsula eastwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Wet-rice agriculture, ironworking, high-fired stoneware, horse gear and writing all reached the islands from Korea across roughly a thousand years, and so did the people who knew how to use them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Smiths in northern Kyushu worked imported Korean iron bars long before anyone in the islands was smelting the metal for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br>  The relationship was political and military as well as commercial, and it is recorded from both sides. The inscription cut for King Gwanggaeto in 414 records campaigns against the Wa alongside those against Baekje, and what those lines say about relations between the early Korean and Japanese states has been argued over for a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Baekje in particular kept close ties with the islands, and after its destruction in 660 many of its people went there, as others went to Tang China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading this traffic as one country civilising another, in either direction, is the standing temptation and both versions have served modern politics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the material record shows instead is dense, unequal and mutual contact between neighbours who were never a single united thing on either side of the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Ilhong Ko, “The Earliest Introduction of Iron into the Yŏngnam Region: A Critical Examination of the Evidence,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 1–36, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17273. [Open access]","Jong-Rok Lee, “An Analysis on the Contents of the Stele of Koguryŏ in Ji’an with Regard to Koguryŏ’s Reorganization of Sumyoje,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 47–81, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-47.pdf. [Open access]","Kiheinarichika Ueda, “The Genealogy in the Koguryŏ Diaspora’s Epitaph,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 2 (2022): 31–72, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-2-31.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-099","num":99,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"Goguryeo staffed its royal tombs with hereditary custodians under a system called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and a king's own stele sets out the rules for it.","questions":["Of the 330 households assigned under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to guard one king's tomb, two thirds came from territory that king had conquered.","The state legislated against the buying and selling of the custodians it had appointed, which tells us that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was being undermined from within."],"answer":"sumyoje","answerText":"sumyoje","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Stele raised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">414 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Custodian households</span><span class=\"dt-v\">330, of which 220 from conquered land</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"tags":["institution","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Sumyoje</b> was the Goguryeo institution that assigned households to guard and maintain the royal tombs, the custodians being called <i>sumyoin</i>, protectors of the tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is known about it comes mainly from the stele raised for King Gwanggaeto by his son King Jangsu in 414, whose inscription of about 1,775 characters falls into three parts: the kingdom's foundation myth, the king's conquests, and the rules for the custodians of his tomb.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That a third of a royal monument is given to tomb staffing says something about what kingship here consisted of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The households numbered 330 in all, and 220 of them came from territories the king had conquered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Guarding the tomb was therefore also a way of settling captured populations and binding them to the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The system was under strain within a generation of being set out. The inscription reveals that people were buying and selling the custodians, and the regulations it records are the king's own instructions, given while he was alive, to stop it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second Goguryeo stele found at Ji'an carries further rules on the same subject, which is why the institution has been reconsidered in recent years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tombs it served stand at Ji'an still, great stone-piled monuments among which the Tomb of the General and the Tomb of the Great King are the largest, set among the walled capitals and mountain fortresses of the kingdom's heartland with the stele beside the second of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Royal burial was a political resource across the peninsula, the great mounds raised at Gyeongju in the same centuries being the clearest parallel, and after Goguryeo fell its aristocratic families carried their claims about tombs and descent into exile with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Jong-Rok Lee, “An Analysis on the Contents of the Stele of Koguryŏ in Ji’an with Regard to Koguryŏ’s Reorganization of Sumyoje,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 47–81, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-47.pdf. [Open access]","Sebastian Müller, “Monumental Burial Mounds in Kyŏngju: Remarks on their Socio-political Meaning,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 2 (2019): 133–70, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-2-133.pdf. [Open access]","HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Kiheinarichika Ueda, “The Genealogy in the Koguryŏ Diaspora’s Epitaph,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 2 (2022): 31–72, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-2-31.pdf. [Open access]","Bonggeun Kim, “Construction Techniques and Significance of the Hagosŏngja Fortress of Koguryŏ,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 2 (2025): 117–51, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-2-117.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Tomb_of_King_Jangsu_2.jpg/1280px-Tomb_of_King_Jangsu_2.jpg","title":"The Tomb of King Jangsu at Ji'an","desc":"The stepped stone pyramid raised for the Goguryeo king who erected his father's stele in 414.","credit":"EditQ, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_King_Jangsu_2.jpg","alt":"A large stepped pyramid of fitted grey stone blocks on a lawn behind a clipped hedge, with trees and blue sky beyond."}},
{"id":"ko-100","num":100,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"An inscribed strip of wood, or <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is how a Korean kingdom actually kept its accounts, and over 600 have come from one fortress alone.","questions":["Because it was written to be used rather than to be read later, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> records the ordinary business of government that no chronicle bothered with.","Nearly half of every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> known from ancient Korea came out of the drainage works at a single Silla fortress in Haman."],"answer":"wooden tablet","answerText":"wooden tablet","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Seongsan tablets</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 – 700 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">over 600 found, nearly half of the Korean total</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","technology","korea","asia","literature"],"abstract":"A <b>wooden tablet</b>, in Korean a <i>mokgan</i>, is an inscribed wooden strip used for records, labels and correspondence before paper became cheap, and it is the most important documentary source excavated in Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tablets were first confirmed at Anapji, but serious study began with the finds from Seongsan Fortress at Haman, where seventeen excavation campaigns have produced over 600 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is nearly half of all the wooden tablets known from ancient Korea, and most came from the fortress's drainage works, where waterlogging preserved them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all are <i>hachal</i>, labels tied to goods, and they carry a place name, a person's name and the item in that order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The place names cluster along the Nakdong River system, which maps where a 6th-century Silla fortress drew its supplies from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  The value of such a document is that nobody meant it to survive. A chronicle records what a court thought worth remembering; a label records a sack of grain, a village and the man who sent it, which is the machinery of taxation itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They have accordingly reshaped what is known about the Silla tax system and about administration outside the capital in the 6th and 7th centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest documents of this kind on the peninsula are older still, the household register of the Lelang commandery having been written on three such tablets and buried in a tomb near Pyongyang, and Baekje has since produced its own, lifting a shortage of material that long limited study of that kingdom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Together with the epitaphs cut for exiles abroad, they are the class of evidence that has changed early Korean history most in the last half-century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nari Kang, “A Study of Past Research on Sŏngsan Fortress Wooden Tablets and an Examination of Excavated Wooden Tablet Documents,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 115–54, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-115.pdf. [Open access]","Dae-Jae Park, “A New Approach to the Household Register of Lelang Commandery,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 5–46, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-5.pdf. [Open access]","Dongmin Lim, “The Native Origins of the Paekche Refugee Ye (禰) Family and the Background of Their Activities in the Tang Dynasty,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 83–114, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-83.pdf. [Open access]","Junkyu Kim, “An Alternative Approach to the Expansion of Paekche during the Hansŏng Period,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 41–69, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-41.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Silla_Kingdom_Wood_Slips_%2818004662135%29.jpg/1280px-Silla_Kingdom_Wood_Slips_%2818004662135%29.jpg","title":"Silla wooden tablets","desc":"Inscribed wooden strips of the kind used for labels and accounts, in the National Museum of Korea.","credit":"Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silla_Kingdom_Wood_Slips_%2818004662135%29.jpg","alt":"Two narrow pale wooden strips, each carrying a column of brushed characters, mounted against a plain grey ground."}},
{"id":"ko-096","num":96,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"The framework in which a smaller state sent gifts and envoys to a Chinese court in return for recognition is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it organised East Asia for centuries.","questions":["How often a state sent envoys is the measure historians use to rank its place within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Korea sent them more often than anyone.","Premodern East Asian history is largely the history of relations with China, conducted through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than between equals."],"answer":"tributary system","answerText":"tributary system","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","china","korea","asia"],"abstract":"The <b>tributary system</b> is the framework within which states around China sent envoys and gifts to the imperial court and received recognition, titles and trade in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If the modern history of East Asia is the history of its relations with the West, its premodern history is largely the history of relations with China, and this was the form those relations took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Historians rank a state's place within the order by how often it sent embassies, and by that measure Korea stood first: its tributary frequency was greater than any other state's, including Vietnam's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Precedence was visible and mattered, Korean envoys standing ahead of the Vietnamese when both were presented at court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of that made the relationship equal, and none of it meant the smaller state was governed from China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  For the peninsula the arrangement had begun long before there were Korean kingdoms to conduct it. The Han commanderies drew tribute and produce from the peninsula as a matter of administration, and the iron of the south was supplied to them in the same movement that carried it to the Han, the Ye and the Wa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What changed with the Three Kingdoms was that the peninsula's rulers now dealt with the Chinese courts as rulers of their own states rather than as the subjects of a frontier province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Goods, techniques, books and writing itself came back along the same route, so the arrangement was a channel of exchange as much as a hierarchy of rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It outlasted every dynasty that operated it, and it was still the frame within which Korea conducted its foreign relations when the European powers arrived in the 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nguyen Thi My Hanh, “Tributary Activities of Vietnam and Korea with China: Similarities and Differences,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 26, no. 1 (2021): 117–44, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-26-1-117.pdf. [Open access]","Ilhong Ko, “The Earliest Introduction of Iron into the Yŏngnam Region: A Critical Examination of the Evidence,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 1–36, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-1.pdf. [Open access]","HyunSook Kang, review of <i>The History and Archaeology of the Koguryŏ Kingdom</i>, edited by Mark E. Byington, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 316–19, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72096. [Open access]","Sun-Mi Park, “Buffer Zone Trade in Northeast Asia in the Second Century B.C.,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 221–50, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36667. [Open access]","Dayang O and Gimin Kim, “Cultural Fluctuations and the Spatial Realignment of Chin-bŏn in the Northern Liaodong and Southwestern Jilin Borderlands during the Early Iron Age,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 31, no. 1 (2026): 179–217, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-31-1-179.pdf. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-098","num":98,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"The list of ancestors at the head of a tomb inscription is its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and in this period it was the main criterion by which a family's standing was judged.","questions":["Comparing one family's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across three generations of stones shows the ancestry growing more specific, which is evidence about status rather than about descent.","Because office, rank and marriage all followed birth in this period, a family's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was worth writing carefully and, where it fell short, worth improving."],"answer":"genealogy","answerText":"genealogy","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","korea","china","literature"],"abstract":"A <b>genealogy</b> is a record of descent, and in the aristocratic societies of the Korean Three Kingdoms and of Tang China it was a criterion for deciding what a family was entitled to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Office, rank and marriage followed birth, so a line of ancestors was not a private matter but the document on which a claim rested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence comes chiefly from epitaphs, which open by praising the family's origin and its forebears before turning to the life of the person buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That order of presentation is itself the point: the ancestry is the frame within which everything afterwards is meant to be read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the stones were cut for the living quite as much as for the dead, a family's successive ones can be set against each other. <br><br>  The epitaphs of the Ye family, Baekje men who took service under the Tang after their kingdom fell in 660, are the case the argument is usually made on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether they were natives of Baekje or descendants of Chinese immigrants is disputed, and the ancestry stated on their stones becomes more detailed and more Chinese with each generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Koguryeo families in the same position did the same thing, and some of them attached their line to the royal house of the kingdom they had just lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of this is peculiar to Korea, and the practice of improving a pedigree turns up wherever birth decides what a person may hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For the historian the value lies exactly in the distortion, since what a family chose to claim says a good deal more about the rules it was living under than an accurate descent ever could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Kiheinarichika Ueda, “The Genealogy in the Koguryŏ Diaspora’s Epitaph,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 27, no. 2 (2022): 31–72, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-27-2-31.pdf. [Open access]","Dongmin Lim, “The Native Origins of the Paekche Refugee Ye (禰) Family and the Background of Their Activities in the Tang Dynasty,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 22, no. 2 (2017): 83–114, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-22-2-83.pdf. [Open access]","Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]","Sebastian Müller, “Monumental Burial Mounds in Kyŏngju: Remarks on their Socio-political Meaning,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 2 (2019): 133–70, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-2-133.pdf. [Open access]","Sarah M. Nelson, “Korean Interpretations of Korean Archaeology,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 27, no. 2 (1990): 185–91, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/16952. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"ko-092","num":92,"category":"The Three Kingdoms","question":"A centre that governs a conquered district through its own chief, taking tribute rather than appointing officials, is practising <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over it.","questions":["Archaeologists trace Baekje's growth as a shift from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the edges towards direct administration, the frontier of the change moving outward over time.","The distinction between direct government and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the model most often used to describe how a Korean kingdom absorbed the polities around it."],"answer":"indirect rule","answerText":"indirect rule","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hanseong period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">ends 475 CE</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Baekje a state from the later 3rd century</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","history","korea","asia"],"abstract":"<b>Indirect rule</b> is government in which a central authority controls a region through its existing leaders, taking tribute and obedience from them, rather than administering it through officials of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its opposite, direct rule, means regions governed from the centre by appointed officials who answer to it and can be replaced by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pair is the model most widely used to describe how the Korean kingdoms grew, and it has been applied more broadly here than almost anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual picture is that districts near the core were governed directly while the periphery was left to its own chiefs, and that direct rule was gradually extended outward as the kingdom strengthened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Growth on that account is a spatial process, a frontier of administration moving steadily away from the capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>  Baekje is the case on which the argument is usually made, because the written and excavated records are fullest there. Social complexity increased rapidly across the peninsula around the beginning of the Common Era, producing polities of many sizes, and Baekje is reckoned to have become a state in the mid to late 3rd century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From its capital at Hanseong, modern Seoul, it absorbed its neighbours until a Goguryeo invasion forced the court to move south to Ungjin in 475.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the model does not settle is what absorption actually felt like on the ground, since a chief left in place under tribute and a chief replaced by an appointed governor leave very different traces in the record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The small polities being absorbed had their own long histories, and reading them only as material for a kingdom is the error the model makes easiest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Junkyu Kim, “An Alternative Approach to the Expansion of Paekche during the Hansŏng Period,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 24, no. 1 (2019): 41–69, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-24-1-41.pdf. [Open access]","Hari Blackmore, “A Critical Examination of Models Regarding a Han 韓 – Ye 濊 Ethnic Division in Proto-Historic Central Korea, and Further Implications,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 95–122, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76773. [Open access]","Yangjin Pak, review of <i>State Formation in Korea: Historical and Archaeological Perspectives</i>, by Gina L. Barnes, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 43, no. 1 (2004): 151–53, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17205. [Open access]","Taeyang O, “Key Issues and Future Directions in Rewriting the History of Mahan: The Characteristics and Cultural Identity of Early Mahan Society,” <i>International Journal of Korean History</i> 30, no. 1 (2025): 37–77, https://ijkh.khistory.org/upload/pdf/ijkh-30-1-37.pdf. [Open access]","Jack Davey, “Culture Contact and Cultural Boundaries in Iron Age Southern Korea,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 1 (2019): 123–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76774. [Open access]"],"undatable":true},
{"id":"art-001","num":1,"category":"Visual Art","question":"The two abalone shells of red paste found at Blombos Cave are the earliest known kit for preparing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the iron-rich earth used for its colour longer than any other material.","questions":["An earth coloured by iron oxides, ground for 100,000 years and used at Blombos Cave to retouch stone tools as well as to make paste, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the oldest chosen material.","Neanderthals in Crimea carried and applied it, and a South African cave held a 100,000-year-old workshop for liquefying it, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest material chosen for its looks."],"answer":"ochre","answerText":"ochre","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Workshop</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">As a tool</span><span class=\"dt-v\">90,000 – 70,000 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":2,"tags":["object","art","prehistory","archaeology","pigment","africa","south africa"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Ochre</b> is an iron-rich earth, red or yellow according to the mineral in it, and it is the oldest material that people are known to have sought out, worked and kept for the way it looks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Blombos Cave on the southern Cape coast of South Africa, an excavation in 2008 uncovered two abalone shells holding the dried residue of a liquefied ochre-rich mixture, along with the ochre, bone, charcoal, grindstones and hammerstones that had gone into making it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deposit is dated to about 100,000 years ago, and the excavators describe it as a workshop: a place where a compound was made in quantity and then stored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the mixture was for is not known, and the report says so rather than choosing; decoration and skin protection are both offered as possibilities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Storing a substance for later use is the part that matters, because it implies a plan that outlasts the afternoon it was made in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>Ochre at the same cave was also a tool.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seven pieces from levels dated between 90,000 and 70,000 years ago had been deliberately shaped and then used as retouchers to press flakes from stone edges, so one lump could colour a surface and finish a blade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The habit is older and wider than one species: Neanderthals in Crimea collected ochre, carried it and applied it, in contexts their excavators read as symbolic rather than practical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That range is why archaeologists are careful with the word symbolic at all, since a substance can be useful, ornamental and meaningful at once and the ground rarely says which.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What ochre does show, thousands of years before anything was drawn with it, is the habit every later history of art is built on: choosing a material for its appearance and keeping it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher S. Henshilwood, Francesco d'Errico, Karen L. van Niekerk, Yvan Coquinot, Zenobia Jacobs, Stein-Erik Lauritzen, Michel Menu and Renata García-Moreno, \"A 100,000-Year-Old Ochre-Processing Workshop at Blombos Cave, South Africa,\" <i>Science</i> 334, no. 6053 (2011): 219–22 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/21998386.","Elizabeth C. Velliky, Francesco d'Errico, Karen L. van Niekerk and Christopher S. Henshilwood, \"Unveiling the Multifunctional Use of Ochre in the Middle Stone Age: Specialized Ochre Retouchers from Blombos Cave,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 26 (2025): eads2797 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12204176.","Francesco d'Errico, Guilhem Mauran, Africa Pitarch Martí, Ana Majkić and Vadim Stepanchuk, \"Evidence for Symbolic Use of Ochre by Micoquian Neanderthals in Crimea,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 44 (2025): eadx4722 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12571073.","Larissa Mendoza Straffon and Claudio Tennie, \"Art beyond Cognition: Reframing Neanderthal Art through Social Connectivity and Cultural Transmission,\" <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 7 (2025): e38 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12645322.","Christopher S. Henshilwood, Francesco d'Errico and Ian Watts, \"Engraved Ochres from the Middle Stone Age Levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa,\" <i>Journal of Human Evolution</i> 57, no. 1 (2009): 27–47 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/19487016."],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f9/Blombos.jpg","title":"Blombos Cave, southern Cape, South Africa","desc":"The cave in the cliff above the Indian Ocean where the ochre-processing kit was found.","credit":"Photograph by Vincent Mourre / Inrap, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blombos.jpg","alt":"A low cave mouth in a pale cliff face above scrub and the sea, with excavation equipment on the terrace in front of it."}},
{"id":"art-002","num":2,"category":"Visual Art","question":"Because calcite crusts grow over painted walls, the ages of the oldest cave art come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which dates the crust and not the picture.","questions":["A clock that starts when a mineral skin forms, giving a minimum age for anything under it, is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the method behind every claim about the oldest painted cave.","The 40,800-year-old red disk in a Spanish cave and a 67,800-year-old Indonesian hand stencil were both dated by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, not by dating any pigment."],"answer":"uranium-series dating","answerText":"uranium-series dating","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest dated art</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 67,800 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Method in use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">since the 1980s</span></div>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","science","prehistory","art","dating"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Uranium-series dating</b> is the method that gives most of the world's oldest cave art its age, and it never dates the art itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water running over a painted wall leaves a thin crust of calcite, which takes up uranium and no thorium when it forms; the slow decay of the one into the other is a clock that starts at the moment the crust grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A crust lying over a painting therefore gives a minimum age — the picture is older than the skin on top of it, by an unknown margin — while a crust underneath gives a maximum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Applied to eleven Spanish caves, it put a red disk in the Cave of El Castillo at more than 40,800 years old and a hand stencil there at more than 37,300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A later study of three Iberian caves returned ages beyond 64,800 years, which is before modern humans are thought to have reached Europe, and the authors concluded that Neanderthals had painted them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br>The numbers keep moving, and they move in one direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Laser-ablation sampling, which takes the calcite in fine slices instead of drilling out a lump, has since put a hand stencil at Liang Metanduno on Muna Island in Indonesia at a minimum of 67,800 years, the oldest demonstrated age for any cave art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Behind the dating sits a harder question, which is what should be counted at all: a freshwater shell from Trinil in Java carries a zigzag of deliberate scratches and is around half a million years old, far older than our own species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Claims of engraved lines older still, made in a South African cave and attributed to <i>Homo naledi</i>, remain contested by the people reviewing them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The safest reading is that pattern-making is old, widespread and not the property of one kind of human, and that the word art is doing a great deal of work in every sentence it appears in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Alistair W. G. Pike, Dirk L. Hoffmann, Marcos García-Diez, Paul B. Pettitt, José Alcolea, Rodrigo De Balbín, César González-Sainz, Carmen de las Heras, José A. Lasheras, Ramón Montes and João Zilhão, \"U-Series Dating of Paleolithic Art in 11 Caves in Spain,\" <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6087 (2012): 1409–13 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/22700921.","Dirk L. Hoffmann, Christopher D. Standish, Marcos García-Diez, Paul B. Pettitt, James A. Milton, João Zilhão, José J. Alcolea-González, Pedro Cantalejo-Duarte, Hipólito Collado, Rodrigo de Balbín, Michel Lorblanchet, José Ramos-Muñoz, Gerd-Christian Weniger and Alistair W. G. Pike, \"U-Th Dating of Carbonate Crusts Reveals Neandertal Origin of Iberian Cave Art,\" <i>Science</i> 359, no. 6378 (2018): 912–15 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/29472483.","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim, Basran Burhan, Ratno Sardi, Shinatria Adhityatama et al., \"Rock Art from at Least 67,800 Years Ago in Sulawesi,\" <i>Nature</i> 650, no. 8102 (2026): 652–56 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12916292.","Josephine C. A. Joordens, Francesco d'Errico, Frank P. Wesselingh, Stephen Munro, John de Vos, Jakob Wallinga et al., \"<i>Homo erectus</i> at Trinil on Java Used Shells for Tool Production and Engraving,\" <i>Nature</i> 518, no. 7538 (2015): 228–31 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/25470048.","Larissa Mendoza Straffon and Claudio Tennie, \"Art beyond Cognition: Reframing Neanderthal Art through Social Connectivity and Cultural Transmission,\" <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 7 (2025): e38 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12645322.","Lee R. Berger, John Hawks, Agustín Fuentes, Dirk van Rooyen, Mathabela Tsikoane, Maropeng Mpete, Samuel Nkwe and Keneiloe Molopyane, \"An Initial Report of circa 241,000- to 335,000-Year-Old Rock Engravings and Their Relation to <i>Homo naledi</i> in the Rising Star Cave System, South Africa,\" <i>eLife</i> 12 (2025): RP89102 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12283075.","Corijn van Mazijk, \"Opaque Social Instruments: A Cultural Evolutionary Approach to Pleistocene Symbolic Artifacts,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 2 (2026): e70036 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13284629."],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Homo_Erectus_shell_with_geometric_incisions_circa_500%2C000_BP%2C_Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center%2C_Netherlands_%28with_detail%29.jpg","title":"The engraved shell from Trinil, Java","desc":"A freshwater mussel shell scratched with a zigzag about half a million years ago, and a detail of the marks. It is the hardest test case for what counts as art.","credit":"Photograph by Henk Caspers / Naturalis Biodiversity Center, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_Erectus_shell_with_geometric_incisions_circa_500,000_BP,_Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center,_Netherlands_(with_detail).jpg","alt":"A fossil mussel shell photographed beside an enlarged detail showing a shallow zigzag of scratched lines running across its surface."}},
{"id":"art-003","num":3,"category":"Visual Art","question":"Cross-hatched ochre from about 77,000 years ago, and the oldest known drawing, both come from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a cave on the southern Cape coast of South Africa.","questions":["Thirteen engraved ochres, shell beads and a silcrete flake drawn on with an ochre crayon were excavated at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose Middle Stone Age levels span 100,000 to 70,000 years ago.","The claim that pattern-making predates the peopling of Europe rests largely on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a South African cave whose ochre pieces carry deliberate cross-hatching."],"answer":"Blombos Cave","answerText":"Blombos Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Engraved ochres</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100,000 – 75,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ochre drawing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 73,000 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","art","prehistory","archaeology","cave","africa","south africa"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Blombos Cave</b> is a small cave in a limestone cliff above the Indian Ocean, on the southern Cape coast of South Africa, and it holds the oldest deliberate patterns anyone has yet found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two pieces of ochre from its Middle Stone Age levels, published in 2002 and dated to about 77,000 years ago, carry cross-hatched lines ground flat and then incised with a stone point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The excavators argued that the design was made to be looked at, and that a repeated pattern held in mind and executed on purpose is evidence of the kind of thinking language needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two pieces are not isolated: ochre runs all through this cave's deposits, ground, scored, heated and in seven cases shaped into tools for pressing flakes off stone edges, across levels dated from 90,000 to 70,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A material worked over twenty thousand years is a very different claim from a single decorated stone, and it is why this cave is cited in every argument about when image-making began.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br>In 2018 the same team published a flake of silcrete from the 73,000-year-old levels bearing nine lines drawn — not engraved — with the tip of an ochre crayon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Microscopic and chemical analysis showed the pigment had been applied to the ground surface deliberately, which makes it the earliest known drawing, older by some 30,000 years than anything of the kind in Europe or Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same levels have produced shell beads, among the earliest known ornaments, and finely worked stone points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What none of this settles is meaning: a pattern can be a mark of ownership, a habit of the hand, a design or a sign, and the cave floor does not say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The safest statement is the one the finds actually support, which is that people were making marks for their appearance long before anyone painted a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Christopher S. Henshilwood, Francesco d'Errico, Royden Yates, Zenobia Jacobs, Chantal Tribolo, Geoff A. T. Duller, Norbert Mercier, Judith C. Sealy, Helene Valladas, Ian Watts and Ann G. Wintle, \"Emergence of Modern Human Behavior: Middle Stone Age Engravings from South Africa,\" <i>Science</i> 295, no. 5558 (2002): 1278–80 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/11786608.","Christopher S. Henshilwood, Francesco d'Errico, Karen L. van Niekerk, Laure Dayet, Alain Queffelec and Luca Pollarolo, \"An Abstract Drawing from the 73,000-Year-Old Levels at Blombos Cave, South Africa,\" <i>Nature</i> 562, no. 7725 (2018): 115–18 [Paywalled], https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-018-0514-3.","Elizabeth C. Velliky, Francesco d'Errico, Karen L. van Niekerk and Christopher S. Henshilwood, \"Unveiling the Multifunctional Use of Ochre in the Middle Stone Age: Specialized Ochre Retouchers from Blombos Cave,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 26 (2025): eads2797 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12204176.","Larissa Mendoza Straffon and Claudio Tennie, \"Art beyond Cognition: Reframing Neanderthal Art through Social Connectivity and Cultural Transmission,\" <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 7 (2025): e38 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12645322.","Corijn van Mazijk, \"Opaque Social Instruments: A Cultural Evolutionary Approach to Pleistocene Symbolic Artifacts,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 2 (2026): e70036 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13284629.","Francesco d'Errico and Solange Rigaud, \"The Origins of Fashion,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 3 (2026): e70039 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13321133."]},
{"id":"art-005","num":5,"category":"Visual Art","artwork":true,"question":"The warty pig on this wall was painted in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a cave in the Sulawesi karst.","questions":[],"answer":"Leang Tedongnge","answerText":"Leang Tedongnge","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 45,500 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","art","prehistory","cave painting","archaeology","indonesia","asia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Leang Tedongnge</b> is a cave in an enclosed valley in the Maros-Pangkep karst of Sulawesi, reachable only through a narrow gap in the limestone, and on its rear wall is a life-sized painting of a Sulawesi warty pig.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The animal is drawn in dark red ochre, about 136 centimetres long, with the pair of facial warts and the crest of upright bristles that distinguish the species, so the painter recorded a particular kind of pig rather than a generic animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two hand stencils sit above its hindquarters, and traces of two more pigs survive on the same panel, one of them apparently facing the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A calcite growth over the animal's hind foot returned a uranium-series minimum age of 45,500 years, which is a floor rather than a measurement of the paint itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On that number the painting was announced in 2021 as the earliest known representational artwork anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>The title did not last, and the reason is a lesson about how these dates work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Laser-ablation sampling of the same kind of crusts elsewhere in the karst has since dated figurative scenes at 50,200 and 51,200 years, so the pig is no longer the oldest figure known, and it may itself be older than its own minimum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Warty pigs dominate the region's Ice Age art, which suggests animals that mattered to the painters as food, as neighbours or as something else again; the paintings themselves do not say which.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The region has been occupied more or less continuously through the Pleistocene, and its excavated caves yield pigment, tools and ornaments alongside the painted walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> All of it is now at risk from salt weathering, which is stripping the pigment from these panels within a single human lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, Budianto Hakim, Rustan Lebe, Jian-xin Zhao, Priyatno Hadi Sulistyarto, Marlon Ririmasse, Shinatria Adhityatama, Iwan Sumantri and Maxime Aubert, \"Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC7806210.","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim, Basran Burhan, Ratno Sardi, Shinatria Adhityatama et al., \"Narrative Cave Art in Indonesia by 51,200 Years Ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 631, no. 8022 (2024): 814–18 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11269172.","J. Huntley, M. Aubert, A. A. Oktaviana, R. Lebe, B. Hakim, B. Burhan, L. Muhammad Aksa, I. Made Geria, M. Ramli, L. Siagian, H. E. A. Brand and A. Brumm, \"The Effects of Climate Change on the Pleistocene Rock Art of Sulawesi,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 9833 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8119963.","Basran Burhan, Budianto Hakim, Iwan Sumantri, Suryatman, Andi Muhammad Saiful, Adhi Agus Oktaviana et al., \"A Near-Continuous Archaeological Record of Pleistocene Human Occupation at Leang Bulu Bettue, Sulawesi, Indonesia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 12 (2025): e0337993 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12725638.","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim, Basran Burhan, Ratno Sardi, Shinatria Adhityatama et al., \"Rock Art from at Least 67,800 Years Ago in Sulawesi,\" <i>Nature</i> 650, no. 8102 (2026): 652–56 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12916292."],"facts":[["Maker","Unknown"],["Painted","At least 45,500 years ago"],["Medium","Red ochre on limestone"],["Size","136 × 54 cm (54 × 21 inches)"],["Where it is","Maros-Pangkep karst, South Sulawesi"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Leang_Tedongnge_rock_art_panel_credit_Basran_Burhan.jpg","title":"The warty pig of Leang Tedongnge","desc":"The painted panel on the rear wall of the cave, with two hand stencils above the animal's hindquarters.","credit":"Photograph by Basran Burhan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leang_Tedongnge_rock_art_panel_credit_Basran_Burhan.jpg","alt":"A dark red painting of a stocky pig in profile on a pale, weathered limestone wall, with two pale hand shapes stencilled above its back."}},
{"id":"art-004","num":4,"category":"Visual Art","question":"Pigs, dwarf buffalo and part-animal human figures painted at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in the Sulawesi karst, form the oldest scene in art at over 50,000 years old.","questions":["A four-and-a-half-metre panel of therianthropes hunting warty pigs, re-dated in 2024 to at least 50,200 years ago, is on the wall of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in Sulawesi.","The cave whose painted hunt was called the earliest narrative composition when it was published in 2019 is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in the Maros-Pangkep karst of Sulawesi."],"answer":"Leang Bulu' Sipong 4","answerText":"Leang Bulu' Sipong 4","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Painted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 50,200 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":5,"tags":["place","art","prehistory","archaeology","cave painting","indonesia","asia"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Leang Bulu' Sipong 4</b> is a cave high in the limestone towers of the Maros-Pangkep karst, in the southwestern peninsula of Sulawesi, and its back wall carries the oldest scene in the history of pictures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Along a panel some four and a half metres wide, figures of Sulawesi warty pigs and dwarf buffalo are approached by small human-like shapes holding what appear to be spears or ropes, all of it painted in one dark red pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Several of the small figures are not simply people: they carry tails, snouts or beaks, which makes them therianthropes, beings that combine human and animal in a single body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The figures are grouped and oriented so that an action can be read between them, and it is that relation, rather than any single animal, that makes the panel a scene rather than a collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When it was published in 2019 the calcite over the paint gave a minimum age of 43,900 years, which already made it the earliest narrative composition known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>The date has since moved twice, in the same direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sampling the same crusts with a laser instead of a drill, which takes the calcite in fine layers and avoids averaging across them, pushed the minimum age of this panel back to about 50,200 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same study dated a comparable scene at Leang Karampuang to at least 51,200 years, so the record for the oldest known storytelling image now sits a few kilometres away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Older marks still are known on Sulawesi — a hand stencil on Muna Island has a minimum age of 67,800 years — but a stencil is not a scene, and the distinction is the point of this panel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Excavation in the same karst shows people living there through the Pleistocene<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>, and what they left on the walls is now disappearing: salt crystallisation, driven by a warmer and more seasonal climate, is flaking the pigment away faster than it can be recorded.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Maxime Aubert, Rustan Lebe, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Muhammad Tang, Basran Burhan, Hamrullah, Andi Jusdi, Abdullah, Budianto Hakim, Jian-xin Zhao, I Made Geria, Priyatno Hadi Sulistyarto, Ratno Sardi and Adam Brumm, \"Earliest Hunting Scene in Prehistoric Art,\" <i>Nature</i> 576, no. 7787 (2019): 442–45 [Paywalled], https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1806-y.","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim, Basran Burhan, Ratno Sardi, Shinatria Adhityatama et al., \"Narrative Cave Art in Indonesia by 51,200 Years Ago,\" <i>Nature</i> 631, no. 8022 (2024): 814–18 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11269172.","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim, Basran Burhan, Ratno Sardi, Shinatria Adhityatama et al., \"Rock Art from at Least 67,800 Years Ago in Sulawesi,\" <i>Nature</i> 650, no. 8102 (2026): 652–56 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12916292.","Adam Brumm, Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Basran Burhan, Budianto Hakim, Rustan Lebe, Jian-xin Zhao, Priyatno Hadi Sulistyarto, Marlon Ririmasse, Shinatria Adhityatama, Iwan Sumantri and Maxime Aubert, \"Oldest Cave Art Found in Sulawesi,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 3 (2021): eabd4648 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC7806210.","J. Huntley, M. Aubert, A. A. Oktaviana, R. Lebe, B. Hakim, B. Burhan, L. Muhammad Aksa, I. Made Geria, M. Ramli, L. Siagian, H. E. A. Brand and A. Brumm, \"The Effects of Climate Change on the Pleistocene Rock Art of Sulawesi,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 9833 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC8119963.","Basran Burhan, Budianto Hakim, Iwan Sumantri, Suryatman, Andi Muhammad Saiful, Adhi Agus Oktaviana et al., \"A Near-Continuous Archaeological Record of Pleistocene Human Occupation at Leang Bulu Bettue, Sulawesi, Indonesia,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 12 (2025): e0337993 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12725638."]},
{"id":"art-006","num":6,"category":"Visual Art","artwork":true,"question":"These stencilled hands and red disks are on the wall of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, above Puente Viesgo in Cantabria.","questions":[],"answer":"El Castillo","answerText":"El Castillo","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Red disk</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 40,800 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hand stencil</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 37,300 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","art","prehistory","cave painting","archaeology","spain","europe"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The Panel of Hands in <b>El Castillo</b>, a cave in the hill above Puente Viesgo in Cantabria, is a crowd of stencilled hands and red disks blown onto the limestone in wet ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A stencil is made by holding a hand against the wall and spraying pigment around it, so what survives is the shape of the gap; the disks beside them were made by the same mouthful of paint, blown without a hand in the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dating calcite that had grown over the images gave the panel its importance: one red disk is at least 40,800 years old and one hand stencil at least 37,300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those are minimum ages, so the paint is older than the crust by an unknown margin, and the disk is the oldest dated wall painting in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern humans reached northern Spain around the same time, which leaves two readings of the panel — the first art of the newcomers, or work by the Neanderthals already there — and the excavators state both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>Five years later the same method, applied in three other Iberian caves, returned ages beyond 64,800 years, well before modern humans are thought to have arrived, and its authors concluded that Neanderthals had made those marks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That claim is argued about rather than settled, but it has changed the question from whether Neanderthals made images to what kind of images they made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hand stencils in particular turn out to be a worldwide habit, and the oldest one now dated anywhere is not in Europe at all but on Muna Island in Indonesia, at a minimum of 67,800 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cave holds later work too — bison and signs of the Magdalenian, tens of thousands of years after the disks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Why hands recur so widely is unanswered; what can be shown is that people notice figures on a rock face in reliable ways, which is where any explanation has to start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Alistair W. G. Pike, Dirk L. Hoffmann, Marcos García-Diez, Paul B. Pettitt, José Alcolea, Rodrigo De Balbín, César González-Sainz, Carmen de las Heras, José A. Lasheras, Ramón Montes and João Zilhão, \"U-Series Dating of Paleolithic Art in 11 Caves in Spain,\" <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6087 (2012): 1409–13 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/22700921.","Dirk L. Hoffmann, Christopher D. Standish, Marcos García-Diez, Paul B. Pettitt, James A. Milton, João Zilhão et al., \"U-Th Dating of Carbonate Crusts Reveals Neandertal Origin of Iberian Cave Art,\" <i>Science</i> 359, no. 6378 (2018): 912–15 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/29472483.","Larissa Mendoza Straffon and Claudio Tennie, \"Art beyond Cognition: Reframing Neanderthal Art through Social Connectivity and Cultural Transmission,\" <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 7 (2025): e38 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12645322.","Adhi Agus Oktaviana, Renaud Joannes-Boyau, Budianto Hakim, Basran Burhan, Ratno Sardi, Shinatria Adhityatama et al., \"Rock Art from at Least 67,800 Years Ago in Sulawesi,\" <i>Nature</i> 650, no. 8102 (2026): 652–56 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12916292.","María Silva-Gago, Marcos García-Diez, Emiliano Bruner, Luis M. Martínez and Felipe Criado-Boado, \"Figure Recognition and Visual Attention Patterns behind the Observation of Palaeolithic Art,\" <i>Journal of Cultural Cognitive Science</i> 9, no. 2 (2025): 225–41 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12279578.","Corijn van Mazijk, \"Opaque Social Instruments: A Cultural Evolutionary Approach to Pleistocene Symbolic Artifacts,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 2 (2026): e70036 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13284629."],"facts":[["Maker","Unknown"],["Painted","At least 40,800 years ago"],["Medium","Red ochre blown onto limestone"],["Where it is","Cueva de El Castillo, Puente Viesgo, Cantabria"],["Recognition","UNESCO World Heritage, 2008"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Panel_de_manos_de_la_cueva_del_Castillo.jpg","title":"The Panel of Hands","desc":"Hand stencils and red disks on the cave wall. The bison beside them is Magdalenian, tens of thousands of years younger than the disks.","credit":"Photograph by the Gabinete de Prensa del Gobierno de Cantabria, CC BY 3.0 ES, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panel_de_manos_de_la_cueva_del_Castillo.jpg","alt":"A rough limestone wall crowded with pale hand shapes outlined in sprayed red pigment, with several solid red discs among them."}},
{"id":"art-007","num":7,"category":"Visual Art","artwork":true,"question":"This ivory figure, part human and part big cat, is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Swabian Jura.","questions":[],"answer":"Lion-man","answerText":"Lion-man","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 40,000 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","sculpture","prehistory","archaeology","ivory","germany","europe"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Lion-man</b> is a standing figure carved from a single piece of mammoth ivory, about 31 centimetres tall, with the body and legs of a person and the head, ears and forelimbs of a cave lion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It comes from Hohlenstein-Stadel, a cave in the Lone Valley of the Swabian Jura in southern Germany, and belongs to the Aurignacian, the earliest Upper Palaeolithic culture in Europe, at roughly 40,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not a lone experiment: a second lion-headed figure, a waterbird and a horse were carved in the same tradition in a valley a few kilometres away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The carving is not a stylisation of a person or of an animal but a deliberate combination of the two, which is what makes it the oldest known image of something that does not exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries incised marks of its own, among the geometric signs that recur across the ivories of these caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br>The Swabian caves have produced the densest concentration of early figurative art anywhere: mammoths, horses, waterbirds, a second lion-headed figure and the earliest known musical instruments, all within a few valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Analysis of more than two hundred marked objects from these sites shows that their geometric signs are not scattered at random but combine in regular ways, with denser sequences on figurines than on tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stone tools that accompany them are regionally distinctive, so this was a settled local tradition rather than a scatter of one-off objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the figure meant is unknowable, and the honest description is the one the ivory supports: someone shaped a being that had to be imagined before it could be carved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On any account it is evidence that people were sharing ideas that had no referent in the world around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nicholas J. Conard, \"Palaeolithic Ivory Sculptures from Southwestern Germany and the Origins of Figurative Art,\" <i>Nature</i> 426, no. 6968 (2003): 830–32 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/14685236.","Christian Bentz and Ewa Dutkiewicz, \"Humans 40,000 y Ago Developed a System of Conventional Signs,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 9 (2026): e2520385123 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12956821.","Keiko Kitagawa and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Split-Based Points from the Swabian Jura Highlight Aurignacian Regional Signatures,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0239865 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC7654757.","Corijn van Mazijk, \"Opaque Social Instruments: A Cultural Evolutionary Approach to Pleistocene Symbolic Artifacts,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 2 (2026): e70036 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13284629.","Larissa Mendoza Straffon and Claudio Tennie, \"Art beyond Cognition: Reframing Neanderthal Art through Social Connectivity and Cultural Transmission,\" <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 7 (2025): e38 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12645322."],"facts":[["Maker","Unknown"],["Carved","c. 40,000 years ago"],["Medium","Mammoth ivory"],["Size","31 cm (12 inches) tall"],["Found","Hohlenstein-Stadel, Lone Valley"],["Where it is","Museum Ulm, Germany"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Loewenmensch1.jpg","title":"The Lion-man of Hohlenstein-Stadel","desc":"Mammoth ivory, about 31 cm tall, assembled from fragments found in 1939 and later. Museum Ulm.","credit":"Photograph by Dagmar Hollmann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Loewenmensch1.jpg","alt":"A slender standing figure carved in pale, cracked ivory, with an upright human body and the muzzle and rounded ears of a big cat, photographed against a dark ground."}},
{"id":"art-008","num":8,"category":"Visual Art","artwork":true,"question":"These lions were drawn on the wall of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, above the Ardèche gorge in France.","questions":[],"answer":"Chauvet Cave","answerText":"Chauvet Cave","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Drawn</span><span class=\"dt-v\">37,000 – 33,500 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31,000 – 28,000 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","art","prehistory","cave painting","archaeology","france","europe"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Chauvet Cave</b>, above the Ardèche gorge in southern France, holds more than a thousand images, among them some four hundred animals drawn in charcoal and engraved into the soft white wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its subjects are unlike those of the later painted caves: lions, rhinoceroses, mammoths, bears and aurochs outnumber the horses and deer that dominate Lascaux, and predators are drawn as often as prey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The drawing is technically assured — outlines stumped with a finger to model a shoulder, walls scraped pale to make the charcoal read, herds built up from overlapping profiles — and none of it looks like a beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> More than 350 dates from the cave, of which 259 are radiocarbon measurements on charcoal and on the floor, were combined into a single chronological model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That model gives two periods of human presence: one from about 37,000 to 33,500 years ago, and a second from 31,000 to 28,000, after which a rockfall sealed the entrance until 1994.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>The first of those phases makes the drawings among the oldest firmly dated anywhere, and the dating method matters to that claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charcoal can be radiocarbon dated directly, so a black rhinoceros at Chauvet is dated from its own pigment, where a painted disk in Spain can only be bracketed by the calcite that grew over it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cave bears used the same chambers, leaving hollows, scratches and a skull set on a fallen block, and they were still there until about 33,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Why the images were made is not recoverable; what can be studied is how they work on a viewer, and experiments show that people pick figures out of a rock surface in consistent ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cave was closed to visitors from the start, and a full-size replica opened nearby in 2015 so that the drawings could be seen without breathing on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Anita Quiles, Hélène Valladas, Hervé Bocherens, Emmanuelle Delqué-Količ, Evelyne Kaltnecker, Johannes van der Plicht, Jean-Jacques Delannoy, Valérie Feruglio, Carole Fritz, Julien Monney, Michel Philippe, Gilles Tosello, Jean Clottes and Jean-Michel Geneste, \"A High-Precision Chronological Model for the Decorated Upper Paleolithic Cave of Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, Ardèche, France,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 17 (2016): 4670–75 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC4855545.","Ministère de la Culture (France), \"The Decorated Cave of Pont d'Arc, Known as Grotte Chauvet-Pont d'Arc, Ardèche\" [Open access], https://archeologie.culture.gouv.fr/chauvet/en.","Alistair W. G. Pike, Dirk L. Hoffmann, Marcos García-Diez, Paul B. Pettitt, José Alcolea, Rodrigo De Balbín et al., \"U-Series Dating of Paleolithic Art in 11 Caves in Spain,\" <i>Science</i> 336, no. 6087 (2012): 1409–13 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/22700921.","María Silva-Gago, Marcos García-Diez, Emiliano Bruner, Luis M. Martínez and Felipe Criado-Boado, \"Figure Recognition and Visual Attention Patterns behind the Observation of Palaeolithic Art,\" <i>Journal of Cultural Cognitive Science</i> 9, no. 2 (2025): 225–41 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12279578.","Corijn van Mazijk, \"Opaque Social Instruments: A Cultural Evolutionary Approach to Pleistocene Symbolic Artifacts,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 2 (2026): e70036 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13284629."],"facts":[["Maker","Unknown"],["Drawn","37,000 – 33,500 years ago"],["Medium","Charcoal and engraving on limestone"],["Subjects","More than 400 animals"],["Where it is","Ardèche, France"],["Recognition","UNESCO World Heritage, 2014"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Lions_painting%2C_Chauvet_Cave_%28museum_replica%29.jpg","title":"The lion panel, photographed in a full-size replica","desc":"A replica of the panel in the Anthropos Museum, Brno. The originals are closed to visitors, and the maneless heads are drawn from lionesses or from the European cave lion.","credit":"Photograph by HTO, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lions_painting,_Chauvet_Cave_(museum_replica).jpg","alt":"A group of maneless big-cat heads and shoulders drawn in charcoal outline on pale rock, overlapping as if moving together, photographed in a museum replica of the cave wall."}},
{"id":"art-009","num":9,"category":"Visual Art","artwork":true,"question":"This six-centimetre ivory figure is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the oldest known image of a human body.","questions":[],"answer":"Venus of Hohle Fels","answerText":"Venus of Hohle Fels","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 35,000 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":3,"tags":["object","art","sculpture","prehistory","archaeology","ivory","germany","europe"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Venus of Hohle Fels</b> is a woman carved from mammoth ivory, six centimetres tall, from the lowest Aurignacian layer of Hohle Fels cave in the Ach Valley of southwestern Germany.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The body is built around what the carver chose to enlarge: broad hips, a heavy belly, exaggerated breasts and a carefully cut vulva, with the arms reduced to short forearms resting above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no head at all; in its place is a worked ring, worn smooth, so the figure was made to hang on a cord and be carried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was recovered in six fragments in 2008 and is at least 35,000 years old, which put it some five thousand years before the Gravettian figurines that had given the type its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That gap matters, because it showed that the tradition of carving women in the round begins with the first modern humans in Europe rather than with their descendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>The same cave and its neighbours produced the earliest known bird, a lion-headed figure and a horse, all in ivory and all within a few thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mammoth ivory was the working material of this whole tradition, and DNA recovered from the tusk fragments at Hohle Fels shows the animals it came from were largely female, which says something about how the raw material was got.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ivory was also used for tools rather than only for images: a perforated baton from the same cave was made for twisting plant fibre into rope, more than 35,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Calling the figure a Venus is a nineteenth-century habit rather than a description, and nothing about it says goddess, mother or portrait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What it does say is that people carried images of themselves about with them almost as soon as they arrived in Europe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nicholas J. Conard, \"A Female Figurine from the Basal Aurignacian of Hohle Fels Cave in Southwestern Germany,\" <i>Nature</i> 459, no. 7244 (2009): 248–52 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/19444215.","Nicholas J. Conard, \"Palaeolithic Ivory Sculptures from Southwestern Germany and the Origins of Figurative Art,\" <i>Nature</i> 426, no. 6968 (2003): 830–32 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/14685236.","Kelsey N. Moreland, Sibylle Wolf, Dorothée G. Drucker, Arianna Weingarten, Ella Reiter, Maria A. Spyrou, Nicholas J. Conard and Cosimo Posth, \"Ancient DNA from the Upper Paleolithic Mammoth Ivory of Hohle Fels, Germany,\" <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 15181 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13176336.","Nicholas J. Conard and Veerle Rots, \"Rope Making in the Aurignacian of Central Europe More than 35,000 Years Ago,\" <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 5 (2024): eadh5217 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC10830101.","Corijn van Mazijk, \"Opaque Social Instruments: A Cultural Evolutionary Approach to Pleistocene Symbolic Artifacts,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 2 (2026): e70036 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13284629."],"facts":[["Maker","Unknown"],["Carved","At least 35,000 years ago"],["Medium","Mammoth ivory"],["Size","6 cm (2.4 inches) tall"],["Found","Hohle Fels, Ach Valley"],["Where it is","Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/Venus_of_Hohle_Fels_URMU.jpg","title":"The figurine, in the Urgeschichtliches Museum Blaubeuren","desc":"Mammoth ivory, six centimetres tall, recovered in six fragments in 2008.","credit":"Photograph by Catatine, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_of_Hohle_Fels_URMU.jpg","alt":"A small pale ivory carving of a female body with broad hips and heavy breasts, its arms shortened and a worn ring where the head would be."}},
{"id":"art-010","num":10,"category":"Visual Art","artwork":true,"question":"This ivory animal, five centimetres long, is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from a cave in the Lone Valley.","questions":[],"answer":"Vogelherd horse","answerText":"Vogelherd horse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Carved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 35,000 years ago</span></div>","difficulty":4,"tags":["object","art","sculpture","prehistory","archaeology","ivory","germany","europe"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Vogelherd horse</b> is a wild horse carved from mammoth ivory, about five centimetres long, from Vogelherd cave in the Lone Valley of the Swabian Jura.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a complete animal reduced to a few decisions: an arched neck, a muzzle held low, a heavy chest and a spine that runs in one unbroken line from ear to tail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surface is polished from handling, and rows of small incisions were cut into the flank and neck, marks of the kind found across the ivories of these caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It belongs to the Aurignacian, around 35,000 years ago, and comes from the same few valleys as the lion-headed figure and the ivory woman, which makes this small group the oldest figurative sculpture known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The horse is the best known of a group from Vogelherd that includes mammoths, a bison, a cave lion and a small human figure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br>Vogelherd was dug out in a single season in 1931, when excavation was quick and coarse, and later sieving of the old spoil heaps recovered further fragments the first dig had thrown away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The lithic work from the site shows an Aurignacian toolkit made in flexible, planned sequences rather than one fixed routine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across the region those toolkits are distinctive enough to mark out a local tradition, so the ivories are the art of a community and not chance survivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Statistical work on more than two hundred marked objects from these caves finds that their incised signs recur in regular combinations, and more densely on figurines than on tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether those marks meant anything sayable is unknown, and the honest reading is that a system of signs and a carved animal were made by the same hands without either explaining the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nicholas J. Conard, \"Palaeolithic Ivory Sculptures from Southwestern Germany and the Origins of Figurative Art,\" <i>Nature</i> 426, no. 6968 (2003): 830–32 [Paywalled], https://europepmc.org/article/MED/14685236.","Christian Bentz and Ewa Dutkiewicz, \"Humans 40,000 y Ago Developed a System of Conventional Signs,\" <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 9 (2026): e2520385123 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12956821.","Benjamin Schürch, Svenja Schray and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Reconstructing Flexible Pathways of Aurignacian Blade and Bladelet Production at Vogelherd,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 9 (2025): e0331921 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12440199.","Keiko Kitagawa and Nicholas J. Conard, \"Split-Based Points from the Swabian Jura Highlight Aurignacian Regional Signatures,\" <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 11 (2020): e0239865 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC7654757.","Corijn van Mazijk, \"Opaque Social Instruments: A Cultural Evolutionary Approach to Pleistocene Symbolic Artifacts,\" <i>Evolutionary Anthropology</i> 35, no. 2 (2026): e70036 [Open access], https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13284629."],"facts":[["Maker","Unknown"],["Carved","c. 35,000 years ago"],["Medium","Mammoth ivory"],["Size","4.8 cm (1.9 inches) long"],["Found","Vogelherd cave, Lone Valley"],["Where it is","University of Tübingen"]],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/MUT_Wildpferd.JPG","title":"The ivory horse from Vogelherd","desc":"Mammoth ivory, about 4.8 cm long, in the collection of the University of Tübingen.","credit":"Photograph by Mogadir, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MUT_Wildpferd.JPG","alt":"A small polished ivory carving of a stocky animal in profile, with an arched neck, a low-held muzzle and a deep chest, lit against a dark background."}},
{"id":"jp-001","num":1,"category":"Early Japan","question":"Made of five main islands and more than fourteen thousand smaller ones, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been inhabited for at least 38,000 years and now holds about 124 million people.","questions":["A population that sat near thirty million until the 1860s passed a hundred million in 1967 and is now falling, which makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the twelfth most populous country on earth.","American warships forced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a treaty at Kanagawa in 1854, ending two centuries in which its rulers had allowed foreigners almost no access to the islands."],"answer":"Japan","answerText":"Japan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 38,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UN member</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 December 1956</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"日本","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"undatable":true,"tags":["place","geography","history","country","asia","japan"],"abstract":"<b>Japan</b> is an island country off the eastern edge of the Eurasian continent, a crescent of land running from north-east to south-west with the Sea of Japan lying between it and the Asian mainland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is built from five main islands — Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu and Okinawa — together with more than fourteen thousand smaller ones, and its surface area comes to 377,976 square kilometres (145,900 square miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The land is steep and heavily wooded: mountains and hills take about three quarters of it, and forest and open fields cover roughly two thirds of the whole surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> People have lived on these islands for at least 38,000 years, and more than ten thousand sites are known from the long stretch of that occupation before pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first arrivals most probably crossed from the Korean peninsula at a moment when the strait to Kyushu had narrowed to a channel some twenty kilometres (twelve miles) wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Rising seas then cut the archipelago off, and the hunter-gatherers left inside it were joined much later by two further waves of migrants from the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The population sat near thirty million through the 18th century and the first half of the 19th, and rose sharply only once the state set about remaking itself after 1868.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It passed fifty million in 1912 and a hundred million in 1967, and is now going the other way, from 126.15 million at the 2020 census to 123.80 million in 2024.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is still the twelfth largest population in the world, and for two centuries before it began growing the shoguns had allowed foreigners almost no access to the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The closure ended when American warships arrived and a treaty was signed at Kanagawa in 1854, and Japan joined the United Nations on 18 December 1956.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Statistics Bureau of Japan, <i>Statistical Handbook of Japan 2025</i> (Tokyo: Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, 2025), 2–9, https://www.stat.go.jp/english/data/handbook/pdf/2025all.pdf. [Open access]","Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Office of the Historian, “The United States and the Opening to Japan, 1853,” Milestones in the History of U.S. Foreign Relations, U.S. Department of State, https://history.state.gov/milestones/1830-1860/opening-to-japan. [Open access]","United Nations Statistics Division, “Japan,” UNdata Country Profile, https://data.un.org/en/iso/jp.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%A8%E5%B1%85%E3%81%AE%E5%8D%83%E6%9E%9A%E7%94%B0.jpg/1920px-%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%A8%E5%B1%85%E3%81%AE%E5%8D%83%E6%9E%9A%E7%94%B0.jpg","title":"Terraced rice fields at Kamiarii, Kochi prefecture","desc":"Narrow paddies stepped up a valley floor behind dry-stone retaining walls, one of them flooded in the foreground, with wooden houses and steeply wooded hills rising behind.","credit":"Asset utilitist, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A5%9E%E5%9C%A8%E5%B1%85%E3%81%AE%E5%8D%83%E6%9E%9A%E7%94%B0.jpg","alt":"Terraced rice paddies walled with rough stone climbing a valley below dark forested mountains, a flooded field in the foreground and a few houses among the trees"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-002","num":2,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"answer":"Japanese archipelago","answerText":"Japanese archipelago","tags":["place","geography","nature","asia","japan"],"question":"Curving from north-east to south-west off the Asian mainland, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits on the world’s newest mobile belt, which is why its earthquakes and volcanoes are so many.","questions":["Honshu accounts for 231,240 of the 377,976 square kilometres of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose five main islands lie among more than fourteen thousand smaller ones.","The winter monsoon buries the Sea of Japan coast of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in snow while leaving its Pacific side dry, and typhoons arrive from the south each autumn."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Latitude</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20° to 45° north</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">377,976 km² (145,900 sq mi)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cut off</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 – 15,000 years ago</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Japanese archipelago</b> is the chain of islands lying off the eastern seaboard of Eurasia, a crescent running from north-east to south-west roughly parallel to the continental coast with the Sea of Japan between.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stretches between about 20 and 45 degrees north and 123 and 154 degrees east, and its five main islands — Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu and Okinawa — sit among more than fourteen thousand smaller ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Honshu alone accounts for 231,240 square kilometres (89,280 square miles) of a total of 377,976.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain lies on the world’s newest mobile belt, one of the most active seismic and volcanic zones anywhere, so earthquakes are frequent and the share of active volcanoes high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The national weather service catalogues 110 of those volcanoes one by one, from Hokkaido south through the Izu and Bonin chains to the Ryukyus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Mountains and hills, generally steep and cut by ravines, take about three quarters of the land, with hilly ground between the ranges and the plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climate is temperate and maritime, but it differs sharply by region: the winter monsoon dumps heavy snow on the Sea of Japan coast while leaving the Pacific side comparatively dry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are two rainy seasons, one in early summer and one in autumn, and tropical cyclones from the Pacific reach the islands as typhoons between summer and autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has not always been so cut off: during the last glacial maximum the strait between the Korean peninsula and Kyushu narrowed to a channel about twenty kilometres (twelve miles) wide, and the first people crossed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The seas rose between twenty and fifteen thousand years ago, and the isolation that followed left a hunter-gatherer population inside the islands whose genetic separation from the mainland is still measurable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Statistics Bureau of Japan, <i>Statistical Handbook of Japan 2025</i> (Tokyo: Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications, 2025), 2–9, https://www.stat.go.jp/english/data/handbook/pdf/2025all.pdf. [Open access]","Japan Meteorological Agency, <i>National Catalogue of the Active Volcanoes in Japan</i>, 4th ed., English version, https://www.data.jma.go.jp/vois/data/filing/souran_eng/menu.htm. [Open access]","Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Japan Meteorological Agency, “Monitoring of Earthquakes, Tsunamis and Volcanic Activity,” https://www.jma.go.jp/jma/en/Activities/earthquake.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Japan_Relief_Map_of_Land_and_Seabed.png/1920px-Japan_Relief_Map_of_Land_and_Seabed.png","title":"Relief map of the land and seabed of the Japanese archipelago","desc":"The island chain in green and tan against a shallow pale-blue Sea of Japan on one side and, on the other, the deep trench that runs the length of the Pacific coast.","credit":"NASA, National Geophysical Data Center, NOAA, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_Relief_Map_of_Land_and_Seabed.png","alt":"A shaded relief map showing the crescent of the Japanese islands, the shallow shelf between them and Korea, and the deep blue trench along their Pacific side"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-003","num":3,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Japanese Paleolithic","answerText":"Japanese Paleolithic","tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","japan","migration"],"question":"More than ten thousand sites survive from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose toolkits of hafted trapezoids and edge-ground axes look like nothing being made on the Asian mainland at the time.","questions":["Hunters of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dug barrel-shaped pit traps a metre deep, the oldest of them at Tachikiri about 35,000 years ago, and shipped obsidian across open water.","Reaching the islands at the opening of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meant a sea crossing, most likely the strait between Korea and Kyushu, then narrowed by low sea levels to twenty kilometres."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 38,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,000 – 12,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sites known</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 10,000</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Japanese Paleolithic</b> is the archipelago’s occupation before pottery, beginning about 38,000 years ago, and its two earliest known sites are Ishinomoto on Kyushu and Idemaruyama on Honshu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than ten thousand Paleolithic sites are known in Japan, and the earliest stretch of the period alone rests on 354 radiocarbon dates from 61 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Getting there took a boat: the likeliest route is the strait between the Korean peninsula and Kyushu, reduced by low glacial sea levels to a channel about twenty kilometres (twelve miles) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Obsidian from Kozushima, an island 45 kilometres (28 miles) out in open water, was already reaching Idemaruyama at that date, which makes the voyage a planned one rather than an accident.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The toolkits are unlike anything on the mainland, combining small trapezoids designed for hafting, quite possibly as arrowheads, with axes ground along the cutting edge — outside Australia the only such axes of that age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Hunters also dug pit traps, barrel-shaped and about a metre (3 feet) deep and two metres (7 feet) wide, the oldest at Tachikiri dating to roughly 35,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Tanegashima the earliest foragers worked acorns and set such traps between about 35,500 and 34,400 years ago, and by the last cold phase were moving constantly and staying nowhere long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people who made these tools are read as the direct ancestors of the later Jomon, their lineage parting from mainland East Asians about 26,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rising seas closed the archipelago between twenty and fifteen thousand years ago, leaving a small population inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The period ends where pottery begins, which in southern Kyushu is pinned by beds of volcanic ash to between about 14,000 and 12,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]","Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/JapanesePaleolithic_VariousChippedStoneTools.jpg/1920px-JapanesePaleolithic_VariousChippedStoneTools.jpg","title":"Chipped stone tools of the Japanese Paleolithic","desc":"Spear points, borers and a scraper laid out on red mounting board in a museum case, each group under its own Japanese label.","credit":"サフィル (Safiru), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JapanesePaleolithic_VariousChippedStoneTools.jpg","alt":"Nine grey and brown flaked stone tools, some leaf-shaped points and some blunt flakes, arranged in rows on a red display board behind glass"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-004","num":4,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"dual structure model","answerText":"dual structure model","tags":["theory","genetics","prehistory","history","japan","migration"],"question":"Ancient genomes have added a third strand to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which had explained the Japanese population as indigenous hunter-gatherers mixed with farmers arriving later from the continent.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the archipelago’s people are an admixture, and biobank data now put the hunter-gatherer share at 26 percent in Okinawa against under 10 in Kinki.","Acid volcanic soils and a warm wet climate destroy ancient DNA outside northern Japan, which is why testing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against real genomes took so long."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jōmon lineage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 20,000 – 15,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yayoi influx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2,300 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kofun influx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,700 years ago</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>dual structure model</b> is the long-standing account of how the population of Japan was formed: an indigenous Jomon people, joined during the Yayoi period by arrivals from the East Eurasian continent who mixed with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ancient genomes have since revised it into three strands rather than two, on the strength of twelve newly sequenced ancient Japanese genomes spanning eight thousand years alongside five already published.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jomon strand descends from hunter-gatherers whose lineage parts from mainland East Asians about 26,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It emerges as a distinct group between twenty and fifteen thousand years ago, when rising seas cut the islands off, and then held an effective population of roughly a thousand for several millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second strand is Northeast Asian, carried by the people who brought rice agriculture to the islands at least 2,300 years ago, most probably from somewhere around the Liaodong peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The third is East Asian, from a large influx around seventeen hundred years ago, in the era of the great keyhole tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern Japanese carry that mixture in very unequal proportions, and the differences follow geography rather than being spread evenly across the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across 171,287 people in a Japanese biobank the Jomon share averages about 12 percent, but it reaches 26 percent in Okinawa and falls below 10 in the Kinki region around Osaka and Kyoto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Reading any of this is hard, because a warm wet climate and acid volcanic soils destroy ancient DNA everywhere but the far north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The story starts with a sea crossing about 38,000 years ago, and the population it founded rose steeply between six and five thousand years ago before falling away again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]","Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]","Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Jomon_people_-_skeleton.jpg/1920px-Jomon_people_-_skeleton.jpg","title":"Reconstructed Jōmon man and woman shown beside their skeletons","desc":"A museum gallery in which two life-size figures stand each behind a mounted skeleton, surrounded by cord-marked pots and clay figurines.","credit":"Souka Kinmei, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jomon_people_-_skeleton.jpg","alt":"A dark museum room with two reconstructed prehistoric figures, a woman holding a pot and a man with a bow, each standing beside an articulated skeleton, with earthenware jars and small clay figurines on plinths between them"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-005","num":5,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Jōmon period","answerText":"Jōmon period","tags":["era","prehistory","archaeology","history","japan"],"question":"Divided into six phases by the styles of its pottery, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran for some ten thousand years on hunting, fishing and gathering before rice farming reached the islands.","questions":["Nuts from the woods, fish taken with line and net, and deer and boar hunted with the bow fed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose people settled in villages without ever farming.","Counts of radiocarbon dates across eastern Japan climb steeply, hold, and reach a trough about 3,700 years ago, tracing the rise and fall of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> population."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Phases</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Jōmon period</b> is the long age of hunting, fishing and gathering that follows the Paleolithic in the Japanese archipelago, beginning around 13,000 BCE and running for roughly ten thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is divided into six phases — Incipient, Initial, Early, Middle, Late and Final — each defined by the pottery in use, from the plain vessels of the first phase to the finely worked Kamegaoka wares of the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name comes from that pottery, whose wet surfaces were pressed and rolled with twisted cord; the earliest of it in southern Kyushu is fixed by volcanic ash beds to between about 14,000 and 12,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Food came from three directions at once: chestnuts, walnuts and horse chestnuts gathered in the woods, fish taken by line, spear and weighted net, and deer, boar and hare hunted with bows and stone-tipped spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fats soaked into the pots confirm where the emphasis lay, since across 143 vessels spanning nine thousand years a large majority carry markers for food out of the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> People settled: the period brings permanent villages of dwellings and graves, and later regional hubs with heavy pillared buildings, earthen mounds and stone circles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their numbers rose and then fell away, which is not the steady climb a farming population leaves behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across eastern Japan the count of radiocarbon dates climbs steeply between six and five thousand years ago, stays high for a millennium, and drops to a trough around 3,700 years ago before recovering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The regions did not move together, and the Kanto plain was already declining while Aomori and Hokkaido were still growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period closes about 2,400 years ago, when rice agriculture arrived from the Korean peninsula and the Yayoi period begins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “Ancient Lipids Document Continuity in the Use of Early Hunter-Gatherer Pottery through 9,000 Years of Japanese Prehistory,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 15 (2016): 3991–96, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1522908113. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Dogu_with_goggle-shaped_eyes%2C_excavated_at_Kamegaoka%2C_Kizukuri%2C_Tsugaru-shi%2C_Aomori%2C_Jomon_period%2C_1%2C000-400_BC%2C_ceramic_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Ueno_Park%2C_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC09105.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Clay figure with goggle-shaped eyes, Kamegaoka, Aomori, final Jōmon period","desc":"A hollow earthenware figure whose huge slitted discs for eyes fill most of the face, its body and limbs covered in fine incised scrollwork and rows of punched dots.","credit":"Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dogu_with_goggle-shaped_eyes,_excavated_at_Kamegaoka,_Kizukuri,_Tsugaru-shi,_Aomori,_Jomon_period,_1,000-400_BC,_ceramic_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Ueno_Park,_Tokyo,_Japan_-_DSC09105.jpg","alt":"A squat olive-brown clay figure with enormous oval goggle eyes, short arms and a broad decorated body patterned all over with curling incised lines and dotted borders"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-006","num":6,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Jōmon pottery","answerText":"Jōmon pottery","tags":["object","archaeology","art","prehistory","japan","technology"],"question":"Fats soaked into the walls of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> show it was cooking fish and shellfish for nine thousand years, through every change of climate the islands went through.","questions":["The cord-marked surfaces of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> give the whole period its name, and its styles change enough from phase to phase for archaeologists to date sites by them.","Mineral analysis at Sankakuyama I found nine in ten pieces of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made from clays close by, the rest carried at least seventeen kilometres, some of it over open water."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In use for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 years</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jōmon pottery</b> is the earthenware of prehistoric Japan, named for the cord-marked patterns rolled into its wet surfaces, and it gives the whole ten-thousand-year period its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was being made from the very start of that period, and its styles change enough from phase to phase that archaeologists date the layers they come out of by them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the pots were for can be read from the fats soaked into their walls: across 143 vessels spanning nine thousand years, compounds diagnostic of aquatic food appear in a large share whatever the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The link between pots and fishing was made at the very end of the Paleolithic and outlasted every economic change that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It outlasted the climate too, and the warming at the end of the last ice age left the pots being used for the same foods out of rivers and sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Most of it was a local product, made from clays within reach of the settlement that used it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Sankakuyama I in southern Kyushu, mineral analysis of the fabric found roughly nine vessels in ten made from clays near at hand, the rest carried at least 17 to 33 kilometres (11 to 21 miles), some across open water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is a considerable effort to spend on moving pots, and it goes with a wider settling-down: heavy grinding stones, polished axes and arrowheads, and houses dug into the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The quantities involved are large, one site on the island of Tanegashima yielding 14,352 sherds out of a single dense scatter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Later vessels grow elaborate, through the flame-rimmed jars of the middle phases to the finely worked Kamegaoka wares at the end, but the earliest of all are plain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “Ancient Lipids Document Continuity in the Use of Early Hunter-Gatherer Pottery through 9,000 Years of Japanese Prehistory,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 15 (2016): 3991–96, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1522908113. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “The Impact of Environmental Change on the Use of Early Pottery by East Asian Hunter-Gatherers,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 31 (2018): 7931–36, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1803782115. [Open access]","Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Jar_with_cord_marks%2C_Japan%2C_Hokkaido%2C_final_Jomon_period%2C_c._1000_BC%2C_earthenware%2C_iron-red_pigment_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07531.jpg/1920px-Jar_with_cord_marks%2C_Japan%2C_Hokkaido%2C_final_Jomon_period%2C_c._1000_BC%2C_earthenware%2C_iron-red_pigment_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07531.jpg","title":"Jar with cord marks, Hokkaido, final Jōmon period, c. 1000 BCE","desc":"A dark rounded jar with a short everted rim, its body divided by raised bands into panels filled with the fine granular impressions of twisted cord.","credit":"Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jar_with_cord_marks,_Japan,_Hokkaido,_final_Jomon_period,_c._1000_BC,_earthenware,_iron-red_pigment_-_Peabody_Essex_Museum_-_DSC07531.jpg","alt":"A brown earthenware jar on a white plinth, its surface textured all over with the impressions of twisted cord between raised curving ridges"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-007","num":7,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Odai Yamamoto I","answerText":"Odai Yamamoto I","tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","japan","object"],"question":"Plain sherds from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Tsugaru peninsula, dated by burnt food residue to around 13,000 BCE, are the earliest known pottery anywhere in northeast Asia.","questions":["Excavation found no post holes at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and nothing dug into the ground, so the people who left its pots were still carrying their shelters about with them.","The sherds at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came up beside stone arrowheads and tools of late Paleolithic type, and they carry none of the cord marks the following period is named for."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pottery</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Historic Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 March 2013</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Odai Yamamoto I</b> is a site in Sotogahama on the Tsugaru peninsula, at the northern tip of Honshu, that has produced the earliest known pottery in northeast Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sits above the Kanita river at 26 metres (85 feet), and was designated a national Historic Site in March 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sherds came up beside stone arrowheads and stone tools of the kind made in the late Paleolithic, and they are plain, carrying none of the cord marks the Jōmon period is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbonised food residue burnt onto them dates to around 13,000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are no post holes and nothing dug into the ground, so the people who left the pots were still moving, carrying shelters rather than building them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That combination is what makes the site matter, because here pottery arrives before settlement does, at the join between the Paleolithic and the Jōmon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether it is the oldest pottery in the world is a separate question, and the answer is probably no.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Claims from southern China run to between twenty and seventeen thousand years ago, though the dating there is contested, and finds in Transbaikal are of roughly the same age as Japan’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What Japan has that those regions lack is precision, since beds of ash from datable eruptions run through its sequences: the Incipient Jōmon of southern Kyushu is pinned to between about 14,000 and 12,800 years ago, and a model of the Tanegashima dates narrows its start further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Wherever the habit began, it stuck, and from these first plain pots onward the fats in Japanese vessels record the same cooking of food from the water for nine thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Odai Yamamoto Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/odai-yamamoto. [Open access]","Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “Ancient Lipids Document Continuity in the Use of Early Hunter-Gatherer Pottery through 9,000 Years of Japanese Prehistory,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 15 (2016): 3991–96, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1522908113. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “The Impact of Environmental Change on the Use of Early Pottery by East Asian Hunter-Gatherers,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 31 (2018): 7931–36, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1803782115. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Odai_Yamamoto_I_potsherds.jpg/1920px-Odai_Yamamoto_I_potsherds.jpg","title":"Potsherds from Odai Yamamoto I","desc":"Twenty-seven small undecorated fragments of coarse fired clay set out in four rows on a grey ground.","credit":"Mccunicano, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Odai_Yamamoto_I_potsherds.jpg","alt":"Rows of small grey and buff pottery fragments, each a centimetre or two across and none of them decorated, arranged on a dark grey surface"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-008","num":8,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Incipient Jōmon","answerText":"Incipient Jōmon","tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","history","japan"],"question":"First of the six phases the period is cut into, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from about 13,000 to 9,000 BCE and is marked by the arrival of pottery and stone arrowheads.","questions":["Beds of volcanic ash lying directly over the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> layers of southern Kyushu pin them to between about 14,000 and 12,800 years ago, which few regions can match.","The earliest houses dug into the ground belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and one site on Tanegashima yielded five of them from a single excavated block."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Phase</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of 6</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 – 9,000 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Incipient Jōmon</b> is the first of the six phases into which the Jōmon period is divided, running from about 13,000 to 9,000 BCE and marked by the appearance of pottery and of stone arrowheads.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The five that follow it are the Initial, Early, Middle, Late and Final, each named for its pottery and each between one and four thousand years long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scheme is built on ceramic style rather than on absolute dates, which is why its boundaries shift when they are measured against radiocarbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In eastern Japan the Early phase comes out at 6,950 to 5,470 years ago, the Middle at 5,470 to 4,420 and the Late at 4,420 to 3,220.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those figures rest on 1,433 accelerator dates from 187 sites in the Kanto region, Aomori prefecture and Hokkaido.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Dating the first phase is the difficulty, and uncertainty about the geological chronology across East and Northeast Asia has held the question back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Southern Kyushu offers the firmest anchor in the archipelago, because its Incipient Jōmon layers lie directly beneath a bed of ash from a known eruption, placing them between about 14,000 and 12,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A statistical model of the dates from Tanegashima narrows the start to between 14,243 and 13,849 years ago once the least reliable samples are set aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Settled life belongs to this phase as well, since the earliest houses dug into the ground appear in it, five of them from one excavated block on that island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The warming at the close of the last ice age falls inside it too, and the pots go on being used for the same foods across the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “The Impact of Environmental Change on the Use of Early Pottery by East Asian Hunter-Gatherers,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 31 (2018): 7931–36, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1803782115. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg/1920px-Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg","title":"Deep bowls of the Incipient Jōmon from the Hinamiyama site, Kanagawa","desc":"Two tall pale bowls with pointed bases held in rings, one banded with applied cord-like ridges below the rim and the other ribbed to halfway down.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deep_Bowls.Incipient_Jomon.Tokyo_National_Museum.jpg","alt":"Two buff-coloured deep pottery bowls with rounded pointed bottoms standing in supports, their upper walls decorated with horizontal raised ridges"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-009","num":9,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"pit dwelling","answerText":"pit dwelling","tags":["building","archaeology","prehistory","japan","practice"],"question":"Because each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a countable thing dug into the ground, archaeologists use the number of them at a site as a rough measure of population.","questions":["The floor of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is sunk below ground level with the roof carried on posts around the hollow, and it was the standard house of Jōmon Japan.","Five examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came out of one 4,400 square metre block at Onigano on Tanegashima, alongside the first pottery and no trace of anything earlier."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Typical floor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 3 m (10 ft) square</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>pit dwelling</b> is a house whose floor is dug below ground level, with the roof carried on posts set around the hollow, and it was the standard dwelling of Jōmon Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They appear at the very start of the period: five were excavated from a single block of 4,400 square metres at Onigano on the island of Tanegashima, alongside the first pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those houses are modest, roughly three metres (10 feet) square and a quarter of a metre deep, and yet digging one out took at least two thousand kilocalories of work before any roof went on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing of the sort is found in the phases before, where the same landscape yields pit traps for game and no houses at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The change came with everything else: many more tools of many more kinds, and enormous quantities of material left behind, one site giving 3,822 struck flakes and 14,352 sherds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Settlements grew out of these houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Jōmon village is a cluster of dwellings with graves among them, and by the middle of the period some had become regional hubs whose remains include pillar-supported structures, rows of pit graves, storage pits, roads and earthen mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because each house is a countable thing, the number of pit dwellings at a site is used as a measure of how many people were there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a rough measure: around Tokyo Bay a single large settlement can hold most of a period’s dwellings, so dropping one site from the sample can change the whole curve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counts of radiocarbon dates tell a similar story of rise and fall, climbing steeply between six and five thousand years ago and reaching a trough around 3,700.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]","A. Bevan and E. R. Crema, “Modifiable Reporting Unit Problems and Time Series of Long-Term Human Activity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 376, no. 1816 (2021): 20190726, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2019.0726. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Sannai Maruyama Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/sannai-maruyama. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Futatsumori_Kaizuka1.jpg","title":"Reconstructed pit dwelling at the Futatsumori shell midden, Aomori prefecture","desc":"A conical house of layered straw thatch reaching almost to the ground over a sunken floor, standing on grass with a large replica Jōmon pot beside it.","credit":"MChew, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Futatsumori_Kaizuka1.jpg","alt":"A steep cone of pale straw thatch laid in overlapping courses, its eaves resting on the turf, with a huge decorated pot lying on the grass in front of it"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-010","num":10,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Sannai-Maruyama","answerText":"Sannai-Maruyama","tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","history","japan"],"question":"More than two thousand clay figurines have come out of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Aomori City, the largest number recorded from any archaeological site in Japan.","questions":["Chestnut pollen, wood and nuts lie thick around <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, enough to show that the trees were being kept and tended rather than simply gathered from.","Jade, obsidian and asphalt from distant sources reached <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so the settlement stood inside a network of exchange rather than living off its own bay alone."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,900 – 2,200 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Special Historic Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 November 2000</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Sannai-Maruyama</b> is a large Jōmon settlement on a coastal terrace about twenty metres (66 feet) above the sea in Aomori City, at the northern end of Honshu, made a Special Historic Site in November 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The protected area covers an occupation running from roughly 3,900 to 2,200 BCE, with its main phase in the last nine centuries of that span, which is when the settlement was at its largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is a whole village rather than a scatter of finds: pit dwellings, structures raised on massive timber pillars, pit graves in rows, storage pits, roads, artificial earthen mounds and pots buried on purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wet ground preserved wooden objects, bone and antler work, baskets and lacquerware, which almost never last on Japanese sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than two thousand clay figurines have come out of it, the largest number recorded anywhere in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The people there did not live only on whatever the woods happened to provide, and the clearest evidence for that is the chestnut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chestnut pollen, wood and nuts are abundant enough around the settlement to show the trees were being kept, and nut sizes grow across the period until late Jōmon chestnuts match modern cultivated ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jade, obsidian and asphalt reached the site from distant sources, so it stood inside a network of exchange running well beyond its own inlet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs to the middle of the period, when the large hub settlements of northern Japan were at their height, and Aomori’s radiocarbon record keeps climbing while the Kanto plain to the south falls away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting pit dwellings is one of the ways Jōmon population is estimated, and a settlement on this scale dominates whatever count it falls into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Sannai Maruyama Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/sannai-maruyama. [Open access]","Sogo Nishio et al., “Genetic Structure Analysis of Cultivated and Wild Chestnut Populations Reveals Gene Flow from Cultivars to Natural Stands,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 240, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-80696-1. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","A. Bevan and E. R. Crema, “Modifiable Reporting Unit Problems and Time Series of Long-Term Human Activity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 376, no. 1816 (2021): 20190726, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2019.0726. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/140913_Sannai-Maruyama_site_Aomori_Japan01bs6bs6.jpg/1920px-140913_Sannai-Maruyama_site_Aomori_Japan01bs6bs6.jpg","title":"Reconstructions at the Sannai-Maruyama site, Aomori","desc":"The three-storey frame raised on six massive posts stands on mown grass to the left, with a long turf-roofed hall and a row of smaller thatched houses beyond it under a heavy sky.","credit":"663highland, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:140913_Sannai-Maruyama_site_Aomori_Japan01bs6bs6.jpg","alt":"A tall open timber tower on six thick posts beside a long low building with a grassed thatch roof, several smaller thatched huts behind, on a wide green field under grey cloud"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-011","num":11,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Jōmon subsistence","answerText":"Jōmon subsistence","tags":["practice","prehistory","archaeology","nature","japan","history"],"question":"Nuts crushed with stone tools, fish taken by net and spear, and deer hunted with the bow were the three legs of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which never needed a field.","questions":["Chemical traces soaked into 143 pots across nine thousand years put food from the water at the centre of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in every phase of the period.","Isotopes from twenty skeletons at one shell mound show the marine share of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> peaking late and then falling once rice farming arrived."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Farming arrives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,400 years ago</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jōmon subsistence</b> is the food economy of prehistoric Japan, which kept a settled population fed for some ten thousand years without farming, out of the woods, the rivers and the sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gathering brought in nuts above all — chestnuts, walnuts and Japanese horse chestnuts — hard enough to need crushing with stone implements before they could be eaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fish were taken with line and spear and smaller ones with nets weighted by stone sinkers, while deer, boar and hare were hunted with bows and stone-tipped spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pots put it more precisely: across 143 vessels spanning nine thousand years, a large share carry the chemical markers of food out of the water, whatever the phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was not a fixed menu, and the balance moved with time and place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At the Kawaji shell mound on the Atsumi peninsula, twenty skeletons dated between 4,520 and 2,270 years ago show reliance on the sea at its highest in the late and final phases and falling away in the Yayoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plants were more than gathered, and proteins from a soybean and from a nut of the walnut family survive in the charred crust inside a pot from Shimoyakebe about 2,900 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beans were grown as well, azuki and soybean seeds swelling far past wild size in the central highlands between six and four thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Seeds that large are the product of tending rather than of luck, though whether the plants were fully domesticated is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of it is agriculture in the later sense, and the period closes only when rice farming crosses from the Korean peninsula about 2,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “Ancient Lipids Document Continuity in the Use of Early Hunter-Gatherer Pottery through 9,000 Years of Japanese Prehistory,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 15 (2016): 3991–96, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1522908113. [Open access]","Soichiro Kusaka and Yasuhiro Yamada, “Temporal Changes in Diet and Mortuary Practices among the Jomon People Based on Human Skeletal Remains Excavated from the Kawaji Shell Mound in Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 133, no. 2 (2025): 79–88, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.250522. [Open access]","Takumi Nishiuchi et al., “Paleoproteomics of Pottery Food Residues Reveals Plant Cupin Superfamily Proteins Preserved for Thousands of Years,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 5 (2026): 115637, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115637. [Open access]","Yu Takahashi et al., “Domestication of Azuki Bean and Soybean in Japan: From the Insight of Archeological and Molecular Evidence,” <i>Breeding Science</i> 73, no. 2 (2023): 117–31, https://doi.org/10.1270/jsbbs.22074. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/Coprolites%2C_Torihama_shell_mound_-_Niigata_Prefectural_Museum_of_History.jpg","title":"Coprolites from the Torihama shell midden, Early Jōmon","desc":"Five lumps of fossilised human faeces in a glass dish in a museum case, their surfaces full of undigested fragments; the label beneath reads Coprolites / Early Jomon. Such finds are among the most direct evidence there is of what a population actually ate.","credit":"inazakira, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coprolites,_Torihama_shell_mound_-_Niigata_Prefectural_Museum_of_History.jpg","alt":"Five pale brown irregular lumps, each a few centimetres across and visibly full of small hard fragments, lying in a shallow glass dish on a dark wooden display tray"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-012","num":12,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"shell midden","answerText":"shell midden","tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","practice","japan","nature"],"question":"Because the species thrown away on a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be identified one by one, the deposit records the sea and the woods a settlement lived off.","questions":["Clams, oysters and scallops lie at Kitakogane in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the bones of tuna, flounder, fur seals and whales, beside graves and a row of arranged deer skulls.","The dead were buried in and beside the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as well, and one collective grave at Hobi in Aichi holds people whose front teeth had been taken out in life."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kitakogane</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,000 – 2,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kawaji remains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,520 – 2,270 BP</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>shell midden</b> is a place where shells were discarded in such quantity that they built into a deposit, and the Jōmon coasts of Japan are ringed with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The species in one can be told apart down to the last valve, so the heap records the sea and the shore that fed the settlement beside it, and what the surrounding country was like while it stood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Kitakogane on Uchiura Bay in Hokkaido, occupied from about 5,000 BCE on a hill 10 to 20 metres (33 to 66 feet) above the water, the deposits hold common Orient clams, oysters and scallops with the bones of tuna and flounder and of fur seals and whales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same site has a burial area with graves and a row of arranged deer skulls, and a watering place at a spring where grinding stones and milling basins were broken on purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A midden is therefore not simply a rubbish heap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It holds the dead as well, and a collective secondary burial found at the Hobi shell mound in Aichi between 2010 and 2013 contained people whose front teeth had been removed in life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their kinship was tested by measuring the crowns of the teeth that remained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Twenty skeletons from the Kawaji shell mound on the same peninsula, dated between 4,520 and 2,270 years ago, carry the isotopic record of what those people ate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their reliance on marine food climbs to a peak in the late and final Jōmon and drops in the Yayoi, which is the clearest measure there is of what farming changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pottery agrees, since aquatic food had been cooked in Japanese pots from the end of the Paleolithic onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Glossary,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/glossary. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Kitakogane Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/kitakogane. [Open access]","Osamu Kondo et al., “Ritual Tooth Ablation in and Dentometric Assessment of a Newly Discovered Collective Burial at the Hobi Shell-Mound Site, Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 130, no. 1 (2022): 33–38, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.220218. [Open access]","Soichiro Kusaka and Yasuhiro Yamada, “Temporal Changes in Diet and Mortuary Practices among the Jomon People Based on Human Skeletal Remains Excavated from the Kawaji Shell Mound in Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 133, no. 2 (2025): 79–88, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.250522. [Open access]","Alexandre Lucquin et al., “Ancient Lipids Document Continuity in the Use of Early Hunter-Gatherer Pottery through 9,000 Years of Japanese Prehistory,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 113, no. 15 (2016): 3991–96, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1522908113. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Shell_layers_of_Okadaira_Shell_Mounds%2C_Ibaraki_01.jpg/1920px-Shell_layers_of_Okadaira_Shell_Mounds%2C_Ibaraki_01.jpg","title":"A section of the shell layers at the Okadaira shell mounds, Ibaraki","desc":"A slab of the deposit lifted whole and stood upright for display: thousands of clam and scallop valves packed into dark earth, with small labels pinned beside a dozen of the species and a potsherd showing among them.","credit":"Σ64, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shell_layers_of_Okadaira_Shell_Mounds,_Ibaraki_01.jpg","alt":"A vertical cross-section of packed shells and soil behind glass, dense with pale bivalve shells at all angles, with small white identification labels among them"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-013","num":13,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"arboriculture","answerText":"arboriculture","tags":["practice","nature","archaeology","prehistory","japan"],"question":"Pollen at Torihama shows oak and beech giving way to chestnut over thousands of years, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than farming: trees kept and favoured, nothing sown.","questions":["The nuts around Sannai-Maruyama grow steadily larger through the period, and late examples match modern cultivated chestnuts, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> does to a tree over generations.","A woodland worked on by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yields far more than one left alone, which is part of why eastern Japan held its largest prehistoric populations between six and four thousand years ago."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chestnut use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 13,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lacquer tree</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 13,200 BP</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Arboriculture</b> is the tending of trees for what they yield — nuts, sap, timber — without sowing or ploughing anything, and it is how much of Jōmon Japan was fed and supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pollen and wood from the Torihama shell midden in Fukui prefecture record six stages of vegetation between about 14,000 and 5,700 years ago, through which the woodland shifts from oak and beech towards chestnut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Use of the Japanese chestnut begins in the first phase of the period, and the stands on the hill beside the site look to have been maintained deliberately rather than merely visited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lacquer tree is present there from about 13,200 years ago and hemp from about 10,500, and the number of plants being used rises steadily through the sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clearest case is at Sannai-Maruyama in Aomori.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Chestnut pollen, wood and nuts lie so thick around that settlement that the trees are read as having been kept about the village, and nut sizes grow through the period until late Jōmon examples match modern cultivated ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This is not domestication in the strict sense, and the Japanese term for it, <i>hansaibai</i>, names something between gathering and cultivation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Beans went the same way in the central highlands, azuki and soybean seeds swelling far past wild size between six and four thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beans about 6,000 years old from Sannai-Maruyama itself were identified as soybean from their DNA, and at least some of them are still of the wild type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A wood worked on in this way feeds more people than one left alone, and eastern Japan held its largest prehistoric populations across exactly this stretch, between six and four thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Masaaki Yoshikawa et al., “Vegetation History and Use of Plant Resources from the Incipient to Early Jomon Periods at the Torihama Shell Midden Site, Fukui Prefecture, Central Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Historical Botany</i> 24, no. 2 (2016): 69–82, https://doi.org/10.34596/hisbot.24.2_69. [Open access]","Sogo Nishio et al., “Genetic Structure Analysis of Cultivated and Wild Chestnut Populations Reveals Gene Flow from Cultivars to Natural Stands,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 240, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-020-80696-1. [Open access]","Yu Takahashi et al., “Domestication of Azuki Bean and Soybean in Japan: From the Insight of Archeological and Molecular Evidence,” <i>Breeding Science</i> 73, no. 2 (2023): 117–31, https://doi.org/10.1270/jsbbs.22074. [Open access]","Sadao Sakamoto et al., “Species Identification of 6,000-Years-Old Beans from Sannai-Maruyama Site, Aomori, Japan,” <i>Journal of Fossil Research</i> 39, no. 1 (2006): 1–6, https://doi.org/10.60339/kasekiken.39.1_1. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Castanea_crenata_1.jpg/1920px-Castanea_crenata_1.jpg","title":"Japanese chestnut, Castanea crenata, in flower","desc":"Long cream catkins spraying out above narrow saw-edged leaves. This is the tree whose stands the Jōmon kept about their settlements, and whose nuts grow larger through the period.","credit":"bastus917, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castanea_crenata_1.jpg","alt":"A chestnut branch in sunlight, its long pale fuzzy flower spikes standing out above dark green lance-shaped leaves with toothed edges"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-014","num":14,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"azuki bean","answerText":"azuki bean","tags":["plant","archaeology","prehistory","japan","nature"],"question":"Wild seeds of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run to about 3.4 millimetres and archaeological ones from Japan reach 7.0, and that swelling is the evidence that somebody was growing them.","questions":["Carbonised seeds and the hollows left in pot walls by seeds that burnt away are the two records of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the second read from casts taken in silicone.","Seeds of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> enlarge in Japan earlier than in Korea or China, which cuts against the assumption that the crop was domesticated once, on the mainland."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest in Japan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 10,000 BP, Awazu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest seeds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6,000 – 4,000 BP</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>azuki bean</b> is a small pulse of eastern Asia, and the archaeology of Japan holds the earliest evidence anywhere that people were growing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest Japanese find is about 10,000 years old, from the Awazu site in Shiga prefecture; the oldest soybean is older still, about 13,000 years, from Ojiyama in Miyazaki.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What marks cultivation is size: wild seeds run to about 3.4 millimetres and the archaeological ones reach 7.0, while soybeans in the same deposits go from 3.8 to as much as 11.8.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence comes in two forms — seeds charred in hearths, and the impressions seeds left in wet clay before burning away in the kiln, which are read from silicone casts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The impressions have to be handled with care, since the clay takes up water and the seed swells inside it before it is lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The largest seeds cluster in the central highlands of Yamanashi and Nagano between six and four thousand years ago, which is where and when the growing was being done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They fall away there around four to three thousand years ago and reappear in Kyushu, and the whole enlargement happens earlier in Japan than in Korea or China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beans about 6,000 years old from Sannai-Maruyama were identified as soybean by extracting DNA and amplifying chloroplast markers, and at least some are of the wild type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Proteins from a soybean survive in the charred crust inside a pot from Shimoyakebe of about 2,900 years ago, so the beans were being cooked as well as gathered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They belong to a worked woodland rather than to fields: at Torihama the lacquer tree, hemp and chestnut were all in use long before this, and the period remains one without agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yu Takahashi et al., “Domestication of Azuki Bean and Soybean in Japan: From the Insight of Archeological and Molecular Evidence,” <i>Breeding Science</i> 73, no. 2 (2023): 117–31, https://doi.org/10.1270/jsbbs.22074. [Open access]","Sadao Sakamoto et al., “Species Identification of 6,000-Years-Old Beans from Sannai-Maruyama Site, Aomori, Japan,” <i>Journal of Fossil Research</i> 39, no. 1 (2006): 1–6, https://doi.org/10.60339/kasekiken.39.1_1. [Open access]","Takumi Nishiuchi et al., “Paleoproteomics of Pottery Food Residues Reveals Plant Cupin Superfamily Proteins Preserved for Thousands of Years,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 5 (2026): 115637, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115637. [Open access]","Masaaki Yoshikawa et al., “Vegetation History and Use of Plant Resources from the Incipient to Early Jomon Periods at the Torihama Shell Midden Site, Fukui Prefecture, Central Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Historical Botany</i> 24, no. 2 (2016): 69–82, https://doi.org/10.34596/hisbot.24.2_69. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Vigna_angularis_%28Willd.%29_Ohwi_%26_Ohashi_-_adzuki_bean_-_VIAN7_-_Tracey_Slotta_%40_USDA-NRCS_PLANTS_Database.jpg/1920px-Vigna_angularis_%28Willd.%29_Ohwi_%26_Ohashi_-_adzuki_bean_-_VIAN7_-_Tracey_Slotta_%40_USDA-NRCS_PLANTS_Database.jpg","title":"Two seeds of Vigna angularis, the azuki bean, with a scale bar","desc":"A close photograph of two beans against a plain violet ground, each with the pale raised scar where it was attached to the pod, and a 2 mm scale beneath them. Size is what the archaeology of this plant turns on.","credit":"Tracey Slotta, USDA-NRCS PLANTS Database, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vigna_angularis_(Willd.)_Ohwi_%26_Ohashi_-_adzuki_bean_-_VIAN7_-_Tracey_Slotta_%40_USDA-NRCS_PLANTS_Database.jpg","alt":"Two rounded oblong bean seeds photographed close up on a pale purple background, one dark red and one mottled olive, each with a long cream scar along one side, above a two millimetre scale bar"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-015","num":15,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"obsidian","answerText":"obsidian","tags":["object","geology","archaeology","prehistory","japan","technology"],"question":"Because every flow carries its own trace-element signature, a flake of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be matched to the outcrop it came from and the route it travelled read off.","questions":["Over 195 crater-shaped hollows pit the Hoshikuso Pass quarries, and beneath each of them shafts were sunk to reach a vein of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as much as two and a half metres down.","A voyage of 45 kilometres to the island of Kozushima for its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was being made about 38,000 years ago, which makes it the oldest planned sea crossing known."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kozushima voyage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 38,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hoshikuso quarries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Incipient to Late Jōmon</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Obsidian</b> is volcanic glass, and because each flow carries its own trace-element signature a flake can be matched by analysis of the elements it contains to the outcrop it was struck from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the Japanese archipelago that has made it the clearest evidence there is for how far people and their goods moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The habit is older than the Jōmon: obsidian from Kozushima, an island 45 kilometres (28 miles) out in open water, was reaching Idemaruyama on Honshu about 38,000 years ago, which makes that voyage the oldest planned sea crossing known anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the Jōmon the sources were being mined rather than merely picked over, with shafts driven into the ground to follow the good stone down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the Hoshikuso Pass in the central highlands the quarries cover more than 45,000 square metres and are pitted with over 195 crater-shaped hollows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Beneath them run shafts that cross one another and reach as much as 2.5 metres (8 feet) below the modern surface to follow a vein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The workings span the period, with pottery of the later Incipient Jōmon at one tool-working site and quarrying still going on in the Late Jōmon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came out of them travelled, and jade, obsidian and asphalt all reached Sannai-Maruyama in Aomori from distant sources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Other things moved with them: at one site in southern Kyushu about one pot in ten had been carried at least 17 to 33 kilometres (11 to 21 miles), some of it across open channels between islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That kind of carrying is old too, and the foragers of Tanegashima were already exchanging small flaked tools over such distances at the very end of the Paleolithic, which meant navigating open water to do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Masao Ambiru and Kazutaka Shimada, “Excavation and Study of Jomon Obsidian Quarrying Sites at the Hoshikuso Pass,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 8, no. 11 (2001): 123–32, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.8.123. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Sannai Maruyama Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/sannai-maruyama. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Hoshikuso_Pass_Obsidian.jpg/1920px-Hoshikuso_Pass_Obsidian.jpg","title":"Obsidian from the Hoshikuso Pass quarries, Nagano prefecture","desc":"Three lumps of black volcanic glass on a pale museum shelf, their broken faces glossy and shell-like where flakes have come away, the duller surfaces crusted white. Jōmon period, from the Obsidian Experience Museum.","credit":"Fred Cherrygarden, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoshikuso_Pass_Obsidian.jpg","alt":"Three fist-sized pieces of glossy black obsidian on a light surface, their fractured faces catching the light in smooth curved scars and their outsides mottled with pale crust"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-016","num":16,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"answer":"lacquer","answerText":"lacquer","tags":["object","technology","art","archaeology","prehistory","japan"],"question":"Tapped from a tree of the sumac family and laid on in thin coats that harden into a waterproof skin, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was already being worked in Japan nine thousand years ago.","questions":["Two Jōmon combs coated in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, from Ishikawa and Fukui, have been radiocarbon dated to about 7,200 and 6,100 years ago respectively.","Most botanists take the tree that yields <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to be no native of Japan, so a piece of its wood dated to 12,600 years ago puts its arrival remarkably early."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest in Japan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lacquer tree wood</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,600 BP</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Lacquer</b> is a hard glossy coating made from the sap of <i>Toxicodendron vernicifluum</i>, tapped from the living tree and laid on wood or basketry in thin coats that harden into a waterproof skin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Japan it is very old, and lacquered combs are among the objects that most clearly define this side of Jōmon life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One from the Mibiki site in Ishikawa prefecture has been radiocarbon dated to about 7,200 years ago and one from the Torihama shell midden in Fukui to about 6,100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The oldest lacquered object known from the archipelago is older still, from Kakinoshima B in Hokkaido at around 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tree that yields the sap was standing in Japan earlier than any object made from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A piece of <i>Toxicodendron</i> wood from Torihama has been dated to about 12,600 years ago, in the first phase of the Jōmon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That matters because most botanists take the species to be no native of Japan but a plant brought over from the Asian mainland, which would put the crossing extraordinarily early.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pollen at the same site has the lacquer tree present from about 13,200 years ago, beside chestnut from the opening of the period and hemp from about 10,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Kakinoshima is a coastal settlement at Hakodate, occupied between roughly 7,000 and 1,000 BCE, with its houses set apart from a burial ground and long earthen mounds beside them, and it was made a Historic Site in 2011.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What survives of all this is a fraction of what there was, since lacquer, wood and basketry last only where the ground stays wet, so a handful of waterlogged sites is preserving the record for the whole period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yuichiro Kudo and Kasho Yotsuyanagi, “Radiocarbon Dating of the Urushi-Lacquered Combs of the Jomon Period Excavated from the Mibiki Site, Ishikawa Prefecture, and the Torihama Shell Midden, Fukui Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Historical Botany</i> 23, no. 2 (2015): 55–58, https://doi.org/10.34596/hisbot.23.2_55. [Open access]","Mitsuo Suzuki et al., “Radiocarbon Dating of an Excavated Wood Sample of <i>Toxicodendron vernicifluum</i> from the Torihama Shell Midden Site, Fukui Prefecture,” <i>Japanese Journal of Historical Botany</i> 21, no. 2 (2012): 67–71, https://doi.org/10.34596/hisbot.21.2_67. [Open access]","Masaaki Yoshikawa et al., “Vegetation History and Use of Plant Resources from the Incipient to Early Jomon Periods at the Torihama Shell Midden Site, Fukui Prefecture, Central Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Historical Botany</i> 24, no. 2 (2016): 69–82, https://doi.org/10.34596/hisbot.24.2_69. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Kakinoshima Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/kakinoshima. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Sannai Maruyama Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/sannai-maruyama. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Vase_red_lacquer_Jomon_Guimet_EO2891.jpg/1920px-Vase_red_lacquer_Jomon_Guimet_EO2891.jpg","title":"Vessel in terracotta and red lacquer, final Jōmon period","desc":"A shallow wide-mouthed pot with a flaring rim and a band of stamped ornament round its shoulder, its whole surface a deep red where the lacquer survives. Musée Guimet, EO 2891.","credit":"Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vase_red_lacquer_Jomon_Guimet_EO2891.jpg","alt":"A low earthenware bowl with an outward-turning rim, coated in dark red lacquer, with a narrow band of small impressed marks running round it below the neck"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-017","num":17,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"dogū","answerText":"dogū","tags":["object","art","archaeology","prehistory","japan","practice"],"question":"More than two thousand examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> have come out of Sannai-Maruyama alone, the largest number of these clay figures from any site in Japan.","questions":["Almost every surviving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is broken, and the usual explanation is deliberate smashing, which a study of 1,035 of them from Iwate did not bear out.","At the Ōyu stone circles the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns up among clay tablets, animal- and bell-shaped clay objects, stone rods and stone swords."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made through</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Sannai-Maruyama</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 2,000</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>dogū</b> is a small human figure modelled in clay, made in the Japanese archipelago through the Jōmon period and found in enormous numbers all across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sannai-Maruyama alone has produced more than two thousand of them, the largest number recorded from any site in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They turn up at ceremonial sites as one class of object among several, rather than standing alone: around the Ōyu stone circles in Akita they were found with clay tablets, animal- and bell-shaped clay objects, stone rods and stone swords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the highlands of central Japan the middle phases produced figures with pronounced buttocks, which have been read as a record of what those bodies looked like on a diet heavy in wild yams, which carry very little fat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That reading is a proposal rather than a finding, and the figures may be saying something else altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Almost every dogū that survives is broken, and the standard explanation has long been that they were smashed on purpose as part of some rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A study of 1,035 final Jōmon figurines from Iwate prefecture tested that idea and did not find it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The breakage rates track how a figure was made — its size, its shape and whether it was solid or hollow — rather than any pattern of deliberate destruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What they were for is therefore still open, and they come from ordinary settlements and from ceremonial sites alike, across the whole ten thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They were made across the whole span of the period, including the middle phases when the great nucleated settlements of eastern Japan were at their height and its population at its largest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Sannai Maruyama Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/sannai-maruyama. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Oyu Stone Circles,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/oyu. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson et al., “Possible Steatopygia in Prehistoric Central Japan: Evidence from Clay Figurines,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 116, no. 1 (2008): 87–92, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.060317. [Open access]","Akihiko Kaneko, “How Broken Are Jomon Figurines? A Basic Analysis of Final Jomon Figurines from Iwate Prefecture,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 15 (2003): 95–106, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.95. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Figurine_Dogu_Jomon_Mus%C3%A9e_Guimet_70608_3.jpg","title":"Clay figure of the Jōmon period, Musée Guimet","desc":"A standing figure with a broad flattened head, a small applied face, stub arms and short parted legs, its body and thighs covered in raised spiral ornament. A scale marker stands beside it.","credit":"Vassil, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figurine_Dogu_Jomon_Mus%C3%A9e_Guimet_70608_3.jpg","alt":"A brown clay human figure standing on a metal plate, wide-shouldered with a flat mushroom-shaped head, tiny arms and thick legs, its surface worked all over with raised curling scrolls"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-018","num":18,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"answer":"stone circle","answerText":"stone circle","tags":["building","archaeology","prehistory","practice","japan"],"question":"A line drawn between the centres of the two rings at Ōyu points at the midsummer sunset, and each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there has a sundial-like setting at its middle.","questions":["The larger <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Ōyu is 52 metres across and built of river stones carried up to a plateau and set out in groups.","Pit graves lie beneath every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Isedotai, so these rings were burial grounds as much as places of ceremony."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ōyu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,000 – 1,500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Isedotai</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,000 – 1,700 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>stone circle</b> is a ring of stones laid or set out as a monument, and in later Jōmon Japan they were built on a scale that must have taken whole communities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two at Ōyu in Akita prefecture stand on a plateau about 180 metres (590 feet) above sea level beside a river, and measure 52 and 44 metres (171 and 144 feet) across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are made of river stones carried up and arranged in groups, and each is ringed by the remains of pillar-supported buildings, storage pits and pit graves set out concentrically around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the centre of each is a setting of stones like a sundial, and a line drawn between the two centres points at the sunset of the summer solstice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Clay figurines, clay tablets, animal- and bell-shaped clay objects, stone rods and stone swords were found around them in large numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Isedotai, also in Akita, has four rings, each more than 30 metres (98 feet) across and the largest 45, which is the closest grouping of stone circles in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pit graves lie beneath them, so the rings served as burial grounds as well as places of ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both sites belong to roughly the same stretch of the period, between about 2,000 and 1,500 BCE, which on the Kanto plain falls inside an environmental stage running from about 4,500 to 2,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is when the great nucleated villages of eastern Japan were giving way to a larger number of smaller settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is also when populations were falling around the whole northern rim, turning down about 5,000 years ago in northern Hokkaido, about 4,400 in the Kanto region and nearer 4,000 in Aomori.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Oyu Stone Circles,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/oyu. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Isedotai Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/isedotai. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Ben Fitzhugh et al., “Human Paleodemography and Paleoecology of the North Pacific Rim from the Mid to Late Holocene,” <i>Quaternary Research</i> 108 (2022): 123–49, https://doi.org/10.1017/qua.2022.35. [Open access]","Yuichiro Kudo, “The Temporal Correspondences between the Archaeological Chronology and Environmental Changes from 11,500 to 2,800 cal BP on the Kanto Plain, Eastern Japan,” <i>The Quaternary Research (Daiyonki-Kenkyu)</i> 46, no. 3 (2007): 187–94, https://doi.org/10.4116/jaqua.46.187. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Oyu_stone_circles_20180916a.jpg/1920px-Oyu_stone_circles_20180916a.jpg","title":"The Ōyu stone circles, Kazuno, Akita prefecture","desc":"River stones laid in groups sweep across mown grass in a wide ring, with four reconstructed pillar-supported buildings standing beyond it and wooded hills on the skyline.","credit":"掬茶, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oyu_stone_circles_20180916a.jpg","alt":"A broad green field under a blue sky, with dark rounded stones set out in clusters forming a large ring across the foreground and four thatched open-sided timber structures on posts behind"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-019","num":19,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"secondary burial","answerText":"secondary burial","tags":["practice","archaeology","prehistory","japan","history"],"question":"In a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the dead are gathered up and interred again once the flesh has gone, and one such grave at the Hobi shell mound held a whole group together.","questions":["The people in the collective <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at Hobi had had front teeth taken out in life, and their kinship was tested by measuring the crowns of the teeth left.","Kawaji shows which teeth were removed changing by phase, a custom that outlived the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the whole period, carrying on into the Yayoi."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Hobi excavation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2010 – 2013</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kawaji remains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,520 – 2,270 BP</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>secondary burial</b> is one in which the dead are moved and interred again after the flesh has gone, and Jōmon Japan has a distinctive form of it in which many individuals were gathered into a single grave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One was found at the Hobi shell mound in Aichi prefecture during excavations between 2010 and 2013.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people in it had had teeth deliberately removed while they lived, and the kinship among them was tested by measuring the crowns of the teeth that were left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Which teeth were taken changed over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the Kawaji shell mound on the same peninsula the earliest individuals had lost the lateral incisors of both jaws, later ones the two lower canines, and later still four lower incisors and two canines, a pattern that carried on into the Yayoi period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The custom was practised very widely in the late and final phases, and more teeth were taken then, in more complicated patterns, than in the comparable Chinese practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most Jōmon graves, though, are single and primary, dug in a burial area kept apart from the houses of the living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Kitakogane in Hokkaido they lie in their own ground beside the shell mounds, with a row of arranged deer skulls among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Irie on the same bay an adult was buried whose limbs had wasted away, and the bones show the person lived like that for years, which could not have been done unaided, so somebody was caring for them over a decade or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A grave there also held an ornament cut from the canines of a wild boar and worked to look like human teeth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Osamu Kondo et al., “Ritual Tooth Ablation in and Dentometric Assessment of a Newly Discovered Collective Burial at the Hobi Shell-Mound Site, Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 130, no. 1 (2022): 33–38, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.220218. [Open access]","Soichiro Kusaka and Yasuhiro Yamada, “Temporal Changes in Diet and Mortuary Practices among the Jomon People Based on Human Skeletal Remains Excavated from the Kawaji Shell Mound in Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 133, no. 2 (2025): 79–88, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.250522. [Open access]","Han Kangxin and Takahiro Nakahashi, “A Comparative Study of Ritual Tooth Ablation in Ancient China and Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 104, no. 1 (1996): 43–64, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.104.43. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Kitakogane Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/kitakogane. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Irie Site,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/irie. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/Sannai_Jomon._Pottery_used_for_infant_burial.jpg","title":"Pot used for an infant burial, middle Jōmon, from Sannai-Maruyama","desc":"A tall cylindrical vessel with a broken irregular rim, its whole body covered in fine herringbone cord marks and blackened in patches from burial. The museum label beneath names it as pottery used for a child's grave.","credit":"Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sannai_Jomon._Pottery_used_for_infant_burial.jpg","alt":"A tall reddish-brown earthenware cylinder standing in a museum case, its surface textured all over in fine diagonal cord impressions and its uneven rim broken away in places"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-020","num":20,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Late Jōmon","answerText":"Late Jōmon","tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","climate","japan","history"],"question":"Running from about 2,000 to 1,000 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is when settlements broke up into smaller ones and the population of the archipelago stopped growing.","questions":["Counts of radiocarbon dates across eastern Japan drop to a trough about 3,700 years ago, in and after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, before recovering.","Northern Hokkaido turns down about 5,000 years ago, the Kanto plain about 4,400 and Aomori nearer 4,000, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> decline was not one event."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,000 – 1,000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trough</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,700 years ago</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Late Jōmon</b> is the fifth of the six phases of the Jōmon period, running from about 2,000 to 1,000 BCE, when settlements became decentralised across the country and the first great stone circles were raised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also when the long growth of the archipelago’s population came to an end and went into reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Across eastern Japan the count of radiocarbon dates climbs steeply between six and five thousand years ago, holds high for a thousand years, and then falls to a trough about 3,700 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is measured from 1,433 accelerator dates at 187 sites in the Kanto region, Aomori prefecture and Hokkaido.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The great nucleated villages of the middle phases give way to a much larger number of much smaller settlements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The regions did not turn down together: northern Hokkaido about 5,000 years ago, the Kanto plain about 4,400, and Aomori nearer 4,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The explanation offered is ecological rather than catastrophic, with long swings in the productivity of the North Pacific proposed as the driver of the whole rim’s population history, rather than any single event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archaeology is tied to a climate sequence, and on the Kanto plain the stage running from about 4,500 to 2,800 years ago covers the pottery types from Shōmyōji to Angyō 3c.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The stone circles of Ōyu and Isedotai in Akita, the largest monuments the period raised, were built across this stretch, between roughly 2,000 and 1,500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fall was not the end: numbers recover after about 3,300 years ago, the Final Jōmon follows, and the period closes only when rice farming arrives from the Korean peninsula about 2,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Ben Fitzhugh et al., “Human Paleodemography and Paleoecology of the North Pacific Rim from the Mid to Late Holocene,” <i>Quaternary Research</i> 108 (2022): 123–49, https://doi.org/10.1017/qua.2022.35. [Open access]","Yuichiro Kudo, “The Temporal Correspondences between the Archaeological Chronology and Environmental Changes from 11,500 to 2,800 cal BP on the Kanto Plain, Eastern Japan,” <i>The Quaternary Research (Daiyonki-Kenkyu)</i> 46, no. 3 (2007): 187–94, https://doi.org/10.4116/jaqua.46.187. [Open access]","Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Oyu Stone Circles,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-sites/oyu. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Vessel%2C_Tohoku_District%2C_Japan%2C_Late_Jomon_period%2C_2000-1000_BC%2C_ceramic_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC09153.jpg/1920px-Vessel%2C_Tohoku_District%2C_Japan%2C_Late_Jomon_period%2C_2000-1000_BC%2C_ceramic_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo%2C_Japan_-_DSC09153.jpg","title":"Spouted vessel from the Tohoku district, Late Jōmon period","desc":"A rounded pot with a long open spout drawn out to one side, its shoulder worked in fine combed lines and knobs and pierced through in three places around a raised centre. Tokyo National Museum.","credit":"Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vessel,_Tohoku_District,_Japan,_Late_Jomon_period,_2000-1000_BC,_ceramic_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_Tokyo,_Japan_-_DSC09153.jpg","alt":"A grey-brown earthenware pot on a small stand, its body squat and round with a long tapering spout at one side, the upper surface densely incised with fine parallel lines and studded with small raised knobs"}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-021","num":21,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Epi-Jōmon","answerText":"Epi-Jōmon","tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","genetics","japan","history"],"question":"Rice farming never reached Hokkaido, so the island went on hunting and fishing through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while the rest of Japan was already in the Yayoi period.","questions":["Frequencies of one gene in two- to three-thousand-year-old bones differ between Jōmon and Ainu but not between Jōmon and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which reads as continuity.","Hokkaido’s sequence runs Jōmon, then <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, then the Okhotsk and Satsumon cultures, then the Ainu, where the mainland runs Jōmon, Yayoi, Kofun."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 2,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Okhotsk genome</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 600 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Epi-Jōmon</b> is the culture that carried on in Hokkaido from about two thousand years ago, after the Jōmon period had ended everywhere else in the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice agriculture, which is what closes the Jōmon on Honshu, never took hold there, so the island stayed a hunting, fishing and gathering country and stayed open to arrivals from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its sequence therefore runs Jōmon, Epi-Jōmon, then the Okhotsk and Satsumon cultures, and then the Ainu, where the mainland runs Jōmon, Yayoi and Kofun, so the island’s later prehistory contains no Yayoi and no Kofun at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people did not change when the label did, and the evidence for that continuity is read straight off their bones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Frequencies of one gene in human bones two to three thousand years old differ significantly between Jōmon and Ainu but not between Jōmon and Epi-Jōmon, which reads as continuity across that boundary and change after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What arrived later came by sea from the north, and it arrived as people rather than only as objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genome of an Okhotsk individual buried between about 400 and 600 CE is roughly half Jōmon-related and half Kamchatka-related ancestry, the mixing itself dated to around 2,250 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A later Okhotsk individual is about a third Jōmon and two thirds Amur, so the north went on adding to the island afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jōmon population that all this built on was never large: Hokkaido supplies 595 of the 1,433 radiocarbon dates behind the eastern Japanese population curve, and its northern interior turns down about five thousand years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Hokkaido sub-population in one biobank study carries about 31.6 percent Jōmon ancestry, against 26.1 in Okinawa and roughly 12 across mainland Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Takehiro Sato et al., “Genome of an Early Okhotsk Individual Reveals Ancient Admixture between Jomon and Kamchatka Lineages,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 37520, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-21522-4. [Open access]","Hisako Kazuta et al., “Genotype Frequencies of the ABCC11 Gene in 2000–3000-Year-Old Human Bones from the Epi-Jomon and Jomon Sites in Hokkaido, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 119, no. 1 (2011): 81–86, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.100416. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Ben Fitzhugh et al., “Human Paleodemography and Paleoecology of the North Pacific Rim from the Mid to Late Holocene,” <i>Quaternary Research</i> 108 (2022): 123–49, https://doi.org/10.1017/qua.2022.35. [Open access]","Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Epi-J%C5%8Dmon_ceramics%2C_Muroran-shi._Hokkaido._Tokyo_National_Museum.JPG/1920px-Epi-J%C5%8Dmon_ceramics%2C_Muroran-shi._Hokkaido._Tokyo_National_Museum.JPG","title":"Epi-Jōmon vessels from Muroran, Hokkaido","desc":"Three pots of the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE from Muroran in southern Hokkaido, made while the rest of the archipelago was already farming rice.","credit":"Photograph by Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epi-J%C5%8Dmon_ceramics,_Muroran-shi._Hokkaido._Tokyo_National_Museum.JPG","alt":"Three brown earthenware vessels of different sizes on a museum plinth, two of them footed and banded with incised lines."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-022","num":22,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Noguni shell middens","answerText":"Noguni shell middens","tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","japan","animal"],"question":"In use on Okinawa between about 7,200 and 4,400 years ago, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> span the Jōmon of the mainland without belonging to it.","questions":["Pig molars from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are markedly smaller than those of the wild boar on Okinawa today, so the animals were either brought in or far more variable then.","Reaching the island that holds the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> meant 220 kilometres of open water, and it had been done by about 36,000 years ago."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7,200 – 4,400 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Okinawa settled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 36,000 years ago</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Noguni shell middens</b> on the main island of Okinawa were in use between about 7,200 and 4,400 years ago, which is the span of the Jōmon on the mainland, and they are not Jōmon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Ryukyu chain has a prehistory of its own, reached by sea rather than by land and never simply an extension of Kyushu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Okinawa was colonised as early as 36,000 years ago, and getting there took 220 kilometres (137 miles) of open water at the end of a chain of shorter crossings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Layers at Sakitari Cave dated between 23,425 and 19,635 years ago hold the bones of wild pig, which must have been carried to the island deliberately and may be the earliest relocation of an animal anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pigs in the Noguni middens are the same puzzle four thousand years later, and they are the reason the site is argued over at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Their molars are markedly smaller than those of the wild boar living on Okinawa now, so either they were introduced from somewhere else or the island’s prehistoric boar varied far more than the modern one does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the islands do share with the rest of the archipelago is people, and mitochondrial genomes from Ryukyuan burials sit in the same study as those from Hokkaido and Honshu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That study of forty whole mitogenomes finds an east–west split of maternal lineages which drift alone could have produced, rather than one requiring separate migrations into the two halves of the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Okinawans today carry more Jōmon-derived ancestry than any mainland population, about 26 percent against a mainland average near 12.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The islands are not uniform either, and there is measurable population structure between Okinawa and the Miyako islands to its south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Ryohei Takahashi et al., “Morphological and Molecular Phylogenetic Characteristics of Dwarf <i>Sus</i> Specimens from the Noguni Shell Middens in the Ryukyu Islands,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 120, no. 1 (2012): 39–50, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.100929. [Open access]","Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Koki Yoshida et al., “Demographic History of the Jomon People: Insights from Whole-Mitogenome Analysis,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 134, no. 1 (2026): 15–28, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.251024. [Open access]","Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson, Shigeki Nakagome, and John B. Whitman, “The Evolving Japanese: The Dual Structure Hypothesis at 30,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e6, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Hanzanbaru_C.jpg/1920px-Hanzanbaru_C.jpg","title":"A shell-midden section lifted from an Okinawan site","desc":"The layered face of the Hanzanbaru C midden on Okinawa, removed whole and mounted for display, showing the packed shell that gives such deposits their name.","credit":"Photograph by Bérangère444, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanzanbaru_C.jpg","alt":"A wide slab of excavated deposit mounted on a museum wall, its earth densely packed with white shells in horizontal bands."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-023","num":23,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Final Jōmon","answerText":"Final Jōmon","tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","migration","japan","history"],"question":"Rice reached northern Kyushu during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and took several centuries more to cross the country, so the period ends at a different date in every region.","questions":["Direct dates on 294 rice grains from 132 sites show the crop moving above four kilometres a year in western Japan and about one across the Kanto plain, long after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began.","Hokkaido took no rice at all, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no end there and the island’s hunting and fishing life ran on into the Epi-Jōmon."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,000 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rice in Kyushu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1176 – 845 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Final Jōmon</b> is the last of the period’s six phases, running from about 1,000 to 400 BCE, and it is the phase that overlaps the arrival of farming, so its end is not one date but a series of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rice reached northern Kyushu between about 1176 and 845 BCE, brought by migrant communities from the Korean peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did not sweep the country, and the eastern population it was moving towards was recovering by then, its radiocarbon counts climbing again after about 3,300 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Direct dates on 294 grains from 132 sites put local dispersal rates between 0.9 and 2.38 kilometres a year, above four in the Chugoku and Kansai regions and about one across the Kanto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kansai has rice by 946–703 BCE, Chubu by 754–560, and the Kanto plain not until somewhere between 471 and 124.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The gap between Chubu and the Kanto alone is about 375 years, and the crop had already been in Kyushu for centuries by the time it reached the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Northern Tohoku is the exception, taking rice earlier than the regions immediately south of it, which points to transmission along the coast rather than a steady overland advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The delay in the east is read against population, since the eastern Final Jōmon was the more densely settled half of the country and the spread looks like intermarriage and the splitting of groups rather than replacement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Diet did not turn over at once either, and at one shell mound on the Atsumi peninsula reliance on marine food is still at its highest through the late and final phases, dropping only in the Yayoi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hokkaido took no rice at all, and its Jōmon simply continued into the Epi-Jōmon while the rest of the archipelago changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema, Chris J. Stevens, and Shinya Shoda, “Bayesian Analyses of Direct Radiocarbon Dates Reveal Geographic Variations in the Rate of Rice Farming Dispersal in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 38 (2022): eadc9171, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adc9171. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Soichiro Kusaka and Yasuhiro Yamada, “Temporal Changes in Diet and Mortuary Practices among the Jomon People Based on Human Skeletal Remains Excavated from the Kawaji Shell Mound in Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 133, no. 2 (2025): 79–88, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.250522. [Open access]","Takehiro Sato et al., “Genome of an Early Okhotsk Individual Reveals Ancient Admixture between Jomon and Kamchatka Lineages,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 37520, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-21522-4. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Jar%2C_excavated_at_Aomori_prefecture%2C_Jomon_pottery%2C_Final_Jomon_period%2C_ceramic_-_Hakone_Museum_of_Art_-_Hakone%2C_Kanagawa%2C_Japan_-_DSC08203.jpg","title":"A Final Jōmon jar from Aomori","desc":"A small round-bodied jar with a narrow neck and combed decoration over its shoulder, made in northern Honshu in the last centuries of the period.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jar,_excavated_at_Aomori_prefecture,_Jomon_pottery,_Final_Jomon_period,_ceramic_-_Hakone_Museum_of_Art_-_Hakone,_Kanagawa,_Japan_-_DSC08203.jpg","alt":"A small grey spherical earthenware jar with a short flared neck, its shoulder covered in fine concentric combed lines."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-024","num":24,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Funadomari","answerText":"Funadomari","tags":["place","genetics","archaeology","prehistory","japan"],"question":"Burials at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Rebun Island off northern Hokkaido produced the most deeply read ancient genome yet recovered anywhere in the Japanese archipelago.","questions":["A woman buried at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a mutation suiting a diet very high in fat, which is what marine hunting off northern Hokkaido would have supplied.","Modern Japanese, Korean, Ulchi, Taiwanese and Philippine populations are all genetically closer to the woman from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than Han Chinese are."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Burials</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3,800 – 3,500 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mitogenomes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">40 Jōmon individuals</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Funadomari</b> is a site on Rebun Island off the north coast of Hokkaido whose Late Jōmon burials produced the best-read ancient genome from the Japanese archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The people buried there were hunter-gatherers of about 3,500 to 3,800 years ago, living on the sea at the northern edge of the Jōmon world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One woman, known as F23, was sequenced at high depth and a man, F5, at low, which is what makes the evidence from this one site carry so much of the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her lineage separates from other East Eurasians before the ancestors of Native Americans do, though after the forty-thousand-year-old individual from Tianyuan near Beijing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Long runs of shared ancestry in her genome are limited but present, pointing to a small northern Jōmon population, and modelling of other genomes puts the effective size at around a thousand across several millennia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Both individuals carry a mutation in a fat-processing gene that suits a diet very high in fat, which is what marine hunting off Rebun would have supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern Japanese, together with Ulchi, Korean, aboriginal Taiwanese and Philippine populations, are genetically closer to F23 than Han Chinese are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading Jōmon DNA at all is difficult, because a warm wet climate and acid volcanic soils destroy it everywhere but the far north, which is why so much of the evidence comes from Hokkaido.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forty whole mitochondrial genomes from Jōmon burials across Hokkaido, Honshu and the Ryukyus now cover the Initial to the Final phases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They show the population growing at the very start of the period and an east–west split of maternal lineages, which fits the case for structure within the Jōmon rather than one uniform lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hideaki Kanzawa-Kiriyama et al., “Late Jomon Male and Female Genome Sequences from the Funadomari Site in Hokkaido, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 127, no. 2 (2019): 83–108, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.190415. [Open access]","Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]","Koki Yoshida et al., “Demographic History of the Jomon People: Insights from Whole-Mitogenome Analysis,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 134, no. 1 (2026): 15–28, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.251024. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson, Shigeki Nakagome, and John B. Whitman, “The Evolving Japanese: The Dual Structure Hypothesis at 30,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e6, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.6. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Rebun_20140725_110632.jpg/1920px-Rebun_20140725_110632.jpg","title":"The coast of Rebun Island","desc":"A rocky inlet on Rebun, the small island off northern Hokkaido whose Late Jōmon burials produced the archipelago’s best-read ancient genome.","credit":"Photograph by Ka23 13, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rebun_20140725_110632.jpg","alt":"A sheltered bay of clear green water enclosed by low grassy headlands of dark rock, with a shingle beach at its head."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-025","num":25,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Jōmon ancestry","answerText":"Jōmon ancestry","tags":["concept","genetics","history","prehistory","japan"],"question":"Averaging about 12 percent across a Japanese biobank, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaches 26 percent in Okinawa and falls below 10 in the Kinki region around Osaka and Kyoto.","questions":["A study of 10,842 people from 47 prefectures finds <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> highest in Okinawa and the Tohoku prefectures and lowest in Wakayama and Nara.","Regional differences in how many people were already living there, rather than differences in who arrived, are what the uneven map of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thought to record."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yayoi influx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 2,300 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kofun influx</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,700 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mainland average</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 12%</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jōmon ancestry</b> is the share of a person’s genome inherited from the hunter-gatherers who were in the archipelago before farming arrived, and it is spread very unevenly across Japan today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Across 171,287 people in a Japanese biobank the average share is about 12 percent, and the average is the least interesting thing about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs to 26.1 percent in Okinawa and falls to 9.8 in the Kinki region around Osaka and Kyoto, with one Hokkaido sub-population at 31.6.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A separate study of 10,842 people drawn from all 47 prefectures finds the same gradient, from a different sample and by a different method.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its highest scores are Okinawa and then the Tohoku prefectures — Iwate, Aomori, Fukushima — and its lowest are Wakayama and Nara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The explanation offered is not a difference in who arrived but a difference in how many people were already there, which is a claim about the resident population rather than the incoming one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Regional differences in population size between the Final Jōmon and the Yayoi would leave exactly this pattern, incomers diluting a thin population further than a dense one, and the farming frontier itself moved above four kilometres a year in the west and about one across the Kanto.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The framework behind all of it is thirty years old and has largely held, an indigenous population admixed with later arrivals, with Ryukyuans and Ainu retaining most of the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What has been added is a third strand, an East Asian influx around seventeen hundred years ago, and structure inside the Jōmon rather than a single lineage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Higher Jōmon ancestry also tracks a higher body mass index, an association that survived controlling for where people live.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]","Yusuke Watanabe and Jun Ohashi, “Modern Japanese Ancestry-Derived Variants Reveal the Formation Process of the Current Japanese Regional Gradations,” <i>iScience</i> 26, no. 3 (2023): 106130, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2023.106130. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson, Shigeki Nakagome, and John B. Whitman, “The Evolving Japanese: The Dual Structure Hypothesis at 30,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e6, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.6. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema, Chris J. Stevens, and Shinya Shoda, “Bayesian Analyses of Direct Radiocarbon Dates Reveal Geographic Variations in the Rate of Rice Farming Dispersal in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 38 (2022): eadc9171, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adc9171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Jomon_ancestry_of_each_region_of_Japan.jpg/1920px-Jomon_ancestry_of_each_region_of_Japan.jpg","title":"Jōmon ancestry by region","desc":"A published figure mapping the Jōmon ancestry score across the Japanese regions, highest in Okinawa and Tohoku and lowest around Osaka and Kyoto.","credit":"Figure by Yusuke Watanabe and Jun Ohashi, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jomon_ancestry_of_each_region_of_Japan.jpg","alt":"A three-panel scientific figure: a box plot by region, a map of Japan shaded in blues, and a scatter plot with a fitted line."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-026","num":26,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Kamegaoka","answerText":"Kamegaoka","tags":["culture","archaeology","art","prehistory","japan"],"question":"The finest pottery of the whole period comes from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> culture of northern Tohoku, one regional tradition among many in an archipelago that was never uniform.","questions":["Burials of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> culture have been read as evidence of rank and tested against that reading, which they did not bear out.","Two mortuary systems appear in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> culture, matching settlement in open country and in the mountains rather than any hierarchy among the dead."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1,000 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eastern lineage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">25 of 32 individuals</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Kamegaoka</b> is the culture of the Final Jōmon in northern Tohoku, and its pottery is the most elaborate the period produced, which is what the culture is usually named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one regional tradition among many, because the Jōmon was never a single culture spread evenly across the archipelago, and the north was not a poorer copy of anywhere further south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its burials have been read as evidence of emerging rank, and that reading has been tested against the graves themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the graves show instead is two mortuary systems, matching settlement in open country and in the mountains, and not enough to support a society of grades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The regions differ in a great deal more than their pots, and the clearest measure of it is how many people each of them held over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Across eastern Japan the radiocarbon record rises and falls at different times in the Kanto region, in Aomori and in Hokkaido, the Kanto plain declining at the very moment the north was still growing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Aomori, where Kamegaoka is, is one of the places where numbers held up longest, and where the largest settlement of the whole period had stood more than a thousand years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Diet varied by coast as well, and reliance on the sea reaches its peak at different moments in different places, so even the food record refuses to give a single Jōmon story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Maternal lineages divide east from west: one haplogroup dominates 25 of 32 eastern Jōmon individuals sequenced and another 5 of 8 western ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That could be drift rather than separate migrations, and the study that measured it says so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Secretariat for the Promotion of the World Heritage Inscription of Jomon Prehistoric Sites, “Jomon Culture,” Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan, https://jomon-japan.jp/en/learn/jomon-culture. [Open access]","Akihiko Kaneko, “Transegalitarian Society and the Mortuary System of the Kamegaoka Culture,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 12, no. 19 (2005): 1–28, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.12.1. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Soichiro Kusaka and Yasuhiro Yamada, “Temporal Changes in Diet and Mortuary Practices among the Jomon People Based on Human Skeletal Remains Excavated from the Kawaji Shell Mound in Aichi Prefecture, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 133, no. 2 (2025): 79–88, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.250522. [Open access]","Koki Yoshida et al., “Demographic History of the Jomon People: Insights from Whole-Mitogenome Analysis,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 134, no. 1 (2026): 15–28, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.251024. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/%E4%BA%80%E3%83%B6%E5%B2%A1%E5%BC%8F%E5%8F%B0%E4%BB%98%E9%89%A2%E5%BD%A2%E5%9C%9F%E5%99%A8.jpg/1920px-%E4%BA%80%E3%83%B6%E5%B2%A1%E5%BC%8F%E5%8F%B0%E4%BB%98%E9%89%A2%E5%BD%A2%E5%9C%9F%E5%99%A8.jpg","title":"A pedestalled bowl in the Kamegaoka style","desc":"Thin-walled, finely burnished and cut through at the foot, with running curvilinear bands around the body — the ceramic manner the culture is named for.","credit":"Kyushu National Museum, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%80%E3%83%B6%E5%B2%A1%E5%BC%8F%E5%8F%B0%E4%BB%98%E9%89%A2%E5%BD%A2%E5%9C%9F%E5%99%A8.jpg","alt":"A wide earthenware bowl on a flared pedestal foot pierced with openwork, its rim and body carrying fine scrolling incised bands."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-027","num":27,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Koropokkuru","answerText":"Koropokkuru","tags":["theory","history","archaeology","japan","concept"],"question":"A small people of Ainu legend, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were offered for a generation as the answer to who had left the shell mounds of Japan.","questions":["Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had preceded the Ainu was really an argument about whether the Japanese had displaced an earlier people or absorbed them.","The founder of Japanese anthropology made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a people distinct from both the Ainu and the Japanese, and the founder of its physical anthropology argued against him."],"answerDate":"","abstract":"The <b>Koropokkuru</b> were a small people of Ainu legend, and for a generation of scholars in Japan they were the answer to the question of who had left the shell mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That question mattered because it was really a question about who the Japanese are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modern archaeology in the country begins with a paper on the shell mounds at Ōmori, generally taken as the first academic paper published in Japan and as the birth of both archaeology and anthropology there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its author was a zoologist, and he argued that the mounds had been left by a people who came before the Ainu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second foreign scholar took up the legend, holding that a small people called Koropokkuru had lived in Hokkaido while the Ainu held Honshu, until the ancestors of the Japanese drove the Ainu north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The founder of Japanese anthropology combined the two, making the Koropokkuru a people distinct from both the Ainu and the Japanese.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The founder of Japanese physical anthropology argued against him, holding that the Ainu were themselves the indigenous population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Underneath ran a choice between displacement and coexistence — whether one people had replaced another, or whether the Japanese were a mixture — and it was not an academic choice in an empire that was absorbing other peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The modern answer is neither of the two on offer then: the people of the shell mounds are read as ancestral to the Ainu and, in part, to everyone else in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A Late Jōmon genome from Hokkaido sits closer to modern Japanese, Korean, Ulchi, Taiwanese and Philippine populations than to Han Chinese, and the Jōmon share of a Japanese genome now runs from under 10 percent to over 30 depending on where a person comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["David Askew, “The Debate on the ‘Japanese’ Race in Imperial Japan: Displacement or Coexistence?” <i>Japanese Review of Cultural Anthropology</i> 3 (2002): 79–96, https://doi.org/10.14890/jrca.3.0_79. [Open access]","Takashi Gakuhari et al., “Ancient Jomon Genome Sequence Analysis Sheds Light on Migration Patterns of Early East Asian Populations,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 437, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01162-2. [Open access]","Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson, Shigeki Nakagome, and John B. Whitman, “The Evolving Japanese: The Dual Structure Hypothesis at 30,” <i>Evolutionary Human Sciences</i> 2 (2020): e6, https://doi.org/10.1017/ehs.2020.6. [Open access]","Hideaki Kanzawa-Kiriyama et al., “Late Jomon Male and Female Genome Sequences from the Funadomari Site in Hokkaido, Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 127, no. 2 (2019): 83–108, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.190415. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Koroppokuru_Beneath_a_Butterbur_by_Matsuura_Takeshiro_%28Hakodate_City_Museum%29.jpg","title":"Koropokkuru beneath a butterbur leaf","desc":"A 19th-century ink painting of the small people of Ainu legend sheltering under a single butterbur leaf, the image the name is usually attached to.","credit":"Painting by Matsuura Takeshiro, Hakodate City Museum, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koroppokuru_Beneath_a_Butterbur_by_Matsuura_Takeshiro_%28Hakodate_City_Museum%29.jpg","alt":"An ink and colour painting of three tiny figures crouched together beneath the broad grey leaf of a butterbur plant."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-028","num":28,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Japanese Paleolithic hoax","answerText":"Japanese Paleolithic hoax","tags":["event","archaeology","history","japan","theory"],"question":"Stone tools planted at more than 180 sites over a quarter of a century made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one of the largest archaeological frauds of the twentieth century.","questions":["An Early Paleolithic reaching back more than half a million years was widely accepted in Japan until the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was exposed by the press in November 2000.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the field set four conditions for accepting an assemblage, and no site older than about forty thousand years meets all of them."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Planting</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from at least 1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Exposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 2000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sites affected</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 180</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Japanese Paleolithic hoax</b> was the planting of stone tools at more than 180 supposed Paleolithic sites in Miyagi prefecture and elsewhere in eastern Japan, from at least 1976 until the press exposed it in November 2000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The man responsible was an amateur archaeologist who buried the material at sites he was about to dig, and then recovered it in front of his colleagues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finds were spectacular and they kept coming, and on them an Early Paleolithic reaching back more than half a million years became widely accepted in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of it was there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the largest archaeological frauds of the twentieth century, and it ran for a quarter of a century at the centre of a working discipline rather than at its edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How that was possible is the harder question, and one answer offered is the postwar conception of archaeology as the people’s history, which gave the discoveries a reception they were never properly tested against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the exposure the whole Early and Middle Paleolithic of Japan had to be withdrawn, and when people first reached the islands became the field’s central question again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four conditions were set for accepting an assemblage: clear human working of the stone, a stable deposit that could not have made natural lookalikes, a secure stratigraphy, and enough material to judge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No site older than about forty thousand years satisfies all four of them, and claims older than that date are treated as unproven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two earliest now accepted are Ishinomoto on Kyushu and Idemaruyama on Honshu at around thirty-eight thousand years, and the sequences after them are anchored by beds of volcanic ash and by explicit modelling of the dates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mark J. Hudson, “For the People, by the People: Postwar Japanese Archaeology and the Early Paleolithic Hoax,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 113, no. 2 (2005): 131–39, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.040804. [Open access]","Akira Ono, “Frameworks and the Present State of Palaeolithic Studies in Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science (Japanese Series)</i> 119, no. 1 (2011): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1537/asj.119.1. [Open access]","Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]"]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-029","num":29,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Tachikawa Loam","answerText":"Tachikawa Loam","tags":["place","geology","archaeology","prehistory","japan"],"question":"The lowest part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds the earliest occupation Japanese archaeology still accepts, after everything older than it was withdrawn as fabricated.","questions":["Volcanic soil covering the Kanto plain, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the anchor of the record once the planted Early Paleolithic material was taken out of it.","An assemblage below the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would need clear human working, a stable deposit, a secure stratigraphy and enough material, and no such site older than forty thousand years exists."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest accepted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 38,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Conditions set</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Tachikawa Loam</b> is the bed of volcanic soil covering the Kanto plain, and the lowest part of it holds the earliest human occupation Japanese archaeology still accepts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a far later date than the field held through the 1990s, and the reason is a fraud rather than a discovery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Stone tools planted at more than 180 sites between at least 1976 and November 2000 had built an Early Paleolithic reaching back more than half a million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When it was exposed, all of it went, and with it the sites, the sequence and the textbooks built on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The question of when people first reached the islands became the central problem again, and the reception those finds had been given has been traced to a postwar conception of archaeology as the people’s history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Four conditions were set for accepting an assemblage: clear human working of the stone, a stable deposit that cannot have produced natural lookalikes, a secure stratigraphy, and enough material to judge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not one site older than about forty thousand years meets all four, and what evidence there is before that is described as sparse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The discipline that came out of it is one that tries to falsify a claimed occupation rather than to confirm it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is a Paleolithic beginning about thirty-eight thousand years ago at Ishinomoto and Idemaruyama, with more than ten thousand later sites and 354 radiocarbon dates from sixty-one of them for its earliest phase alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Everything since is dated the slow way, by explicit models over series of dates and by counting them site by site — 1,433 of them from 187 sites for the Jōmon of eastern Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Akira Ono, “Frameworks and the Present State of Palaeolithic Studies in Japan,” <i>Anthropological Science (Japanese Series)</i> 119, no. 1 (2011): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1537/asj.119.1. [Open access]","Mark J. Hudson, “For the People, by the People: Postwar Japanese Archaeology and the Early Paleolithic Hoax,” <i>Anthropological Science</i> 113, no. 2 (2005): 131–39, https://doi.org/10.1537/ase.040804. [Open access]","Robin Dennell, “The Japanese Palaeolithic Deserves More Attention in Western Narratives about Human Expansion across Asia,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17 (2026): 6387, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-74116-7. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]"]},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-030","num":30,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"tephrochronology","answerText":"tephrochronology","tags":["concept","geology","archaeology","science","japan"],"question":"Because a large Japanese eruption blankets much of the country at once, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives prehistory there a set of horizons that can be matched from one region to another.","questions":["The earliest pottery of southern Kyushu lies directly beneath a bed of ash from a known eruption, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> doing the work no radiocarbon date could do alone.","Each ash has its own chemistry and physical character, which is what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible at all rather than a matter of counting grey layers."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Incipient Jōmon</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,000 – 12,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eastern dates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,433 from 187 sites</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Tephrochronology</b> is the dating and correlating of deposits by the layers of volcanic ash that fall across them, and in Japan it is the backbone of prehistoric chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archipelago’s calderas produce an eruption large enough to blanket much of the country roughly once in every few tens of thousands of years, and each ash carries its own chemistry and physical character.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once a layer has been identified and dated, everything beneath it is older and everything above it younger, wherever in the country it is found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why the earliest pottery in southern Kyushu can be fixed as tightly as it is, its layers lying directly beneath a bed of ash from a known eruption, between about 14,000 and 12,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Japan’s advantage over the other regions that claim very early pottery is precisely this.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The rest of the framework is pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The six Jōmon phases are defined by ceramic style, and their boundaries shift under calibration: in eastern Japan the Early phase measures 6,950 to 5,470 years ago, the Middle 5,470 to 4,420 and the Late 4,420 to 3,220, on 1,433 accelerator dates from 187 sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the dates are argued over they are modelled explicitly, and a model of the Tanegashima series narrows the start of the first phase to between 14,243 and 13,849 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The environment is dated alongside the archaeology, and on the Kanto plain five stages of environmental change are matched to runs of pottery types between 11,500 and 2,800 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of it rests on a written word, because there is none: ten thousand years of Japanese prehistory are read off ash, pots, bones and the ground they lie in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Takehiko Suzuki, “Tephra Studies on Quaternary Explosive Eruptions in the Japanese Islands,” <i>The Quaternary Research (Daiyonki-Kenkyu)</i> 46, no. 3 (2007): 283–92, https://doi.org/10.4116/jaqua.46.283. [Open access]","Fumie Iizuka, Jeffrey R. Ferguson, and Masami Izuho, “Late Pleistocene Pottery Production and Exchange: Provenance Studies of Hunter-Gatherer Wares from Southern Kyushu, Japan by Neutron Activation Analysis,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 3 (2022): e0265329, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0265329. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema et al., “Summed Probability Distribution of 14C Dates Suggests Regional Divergences in the Population Dynamics of the Jomon Period in Eastern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 4 (2016): e0154809, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0154809. [Open access]","Kazuki Morisaki et al., “More on Mobility and Sedentism: Changes in Adaptation from Upper Paleolithic to Incipient Jomon, Tanegashima Island, Southern Japan,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 1 (2025): e0314311, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0314311. [Open access]","Yuichiro Kudo, “The Temporal Correspondences between the Archaeological Chronology and Environmental Changes from 11,500 to 2,800 cal BP on the Kanto Plain, Eastern Japan,” <i>The Quaternary Research (Daiyonki-Kenkyu)</i> 46, no. 3 (2007): 187–94, https://doi.org/10.4116/jaqua.46.187. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Volcanic_Ash-Fall_Layers_Izu_Oshima_Japan.jpg/1920px-Volcanic_Ash-Fall_Layers_Izu_Oshima_Japan.jpg","title":"Ash-fall layers on Izu Oshima","desc":"A road cutting on the volcanic island of Izu Oshima exposing scores of banded ash falls, each one laid down across the landscape within days of its eruption.","credit":"Photograph by Taiyo Fujii, CC BY 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volcanic_Ash-Fall_Layers_Izu_Oshima_Japan.jpg","alt":"A long roadside cliff of cream and brown volcanic ash in dozens of thin parallel bands curving over a buried hillside."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-031","num":31,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Yayoi period","answerText":"Yayoi period","tags":["era","archaeology","history","prehistory","agriculture","japan"],"question":"Farming, metal, weaving and walled villages all reach Japan within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why its opening date is argued over rather than agreed.","questions":["The end of hunting and gathering as a way of life across most of Japan falls in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though Hokkaido never took the rice that defines it.","Because rice moved east at between about one and four kilometres a year, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins centuries later in the Kanto plain than in Kyushu."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE – 250 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest rice</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 800 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Yayoi period</b> is the age of farming villages that follows the Jōmon in Japan, usually given as running from about 800 BCE to the third century CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is named after a district of Tokyo where its pottery was first recognised, and what defines it is not the pottery but irrigated rice agriculture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Charred rice grains from the Ukikunden site in Saga Prefecture date the first of it to the latter half of the 9th century BCE, which is when the culture is taken to emerge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Others put the beginning later, with the Itazuke pottery style that spread across western Japan in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neither date is a mistake: the period begins with a process rather than an event, and where a scholar cuts it depends on which part of the package is treated as decisive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The package that came with it is large, and almost none of it began in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Paddy fields, new tools, bronze and iron, weaving, dolmens and jar burial all arrive from the Korean peninsula, and with them people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It spread unevenly, moving above four kilometres (2.5 miles) a year in western Japan and about one across the Kanto plain, so the period opens at a different moment in every region and never opens at all in Hokkaido.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Villages grew large and were ringed with ditches, goods travelled the length of the archipelago, and a single settlement on the Japan Sea coast could hold jade from one end of the country and iron from beyond it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By its end the archipelago held ranked societies trading with the Chinese commanderies, and the great mounded tombs of the Kofun period were about to begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Tomomi Nakagawa, Hisashi Nakao, Kohei Tamura, Yui Arimatsu, Naoko Matsumoto, and Takehiko Matsugi, “Violence and Warfare in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Letters on Evolutionary Behavioral Science</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): 8–11, https://doi.org/10.5178/lebs.2017.55. [Open access]","Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]","Miyamoto Kazuo, “The Spread of Rice Agriculture during the Yayoi Period: From the Shandong Peninsula to the Japanese Archipelago via the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2019): 109–24, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-2_109.pdf. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema, Chris J. Stevens, and Shinya Shoda, “Bayesian Analyses of Direct Radiocarbon Dates Reveal Geographic Variations in the Rate of Rice Farming Dispersal in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 38 (2022): eadc9171, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adc9171. [Open access]","Takamuku Hirofumi, Adachi Tatsuro, Nakano Nobuhiko, Tajiri Yoshinori, and Osanai Yasuhito, “Movement during the Yayoi Period: An Examination of the Western Burial Area of the Doigahama Site Using Strontium Isotope and Cranial Morphological Analyses,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 12 (2024): 31–57, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol012/12-1_031-057.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Jar%2CYayoi_period.Takakura-cho.Aichi.TokyoNM.jpg/1920px-Jar%2CYayoi_period.Takakura-cho.Aichi.TokyoNM.jpg","title":"A Yayoi jar from Aichi","desc":"A burnished storage jar with a heavy everted rim and a band of incised zigzags, made in central Honshu in the first three centuries CE.","credit":"Photograph by Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jar,Yayoi_period.Takakura-cho.Aichi.TokyoNM.jpg","alt":"A round buff earthenware jar with a wide flat flaring rim, its shoulder cut with a band of zigzag lines."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-032","num":32,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"wet-rice agriculture","answerText":"wet-rice agriculture","tags":["practice","agriculture","archaeology","migration","japan","history"],"question":"Arriving in northern Kyushu from southern Korea with its paddies, its tools and its dolmens, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the change that ends the Jōmon.","questions":["Rice seeds from a site in Fukuoka were shown by DNA analysis to be the temperate variety, which dates the second wave of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reaching Japan.","Direct dates on 294 grains from 132 sites show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed western Japan several times faster than it crossed the Kanto plain in the east."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reaches Kyushu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 – 800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reaches Kanto</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 471 – 124 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Wet-rice agriculture</b> is rice grown in flooded paddy fields, and its arrival in the Japanese archipelago is the event that closes the Jōmon and opens the Yayoi period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It came from the southern Korean peninsula, the last stage of a spread that had carried irrigated farming north-east from the Shandong peninsula over some two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It arrived as a whole way of working rather than as a crop: paddy fields, wooden and stone agricultural tools, new pottery, dolmens and the people who knew how to use them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charred grains from the Ukikunden site near Karatsu date the earliest of it to the latter half of the 9th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There were two routes and two waves, the first from the Namgang river area to the Karatsu and Itoshima plains, the second from the lower Nakdong to the Fukuoka plain a century or two later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The two waves carried different rice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grains from the Arita site in Fukuoka, dated to the 6th and 5th centuries BCE, were shown by DNA analysis and grain measurement to be temperate Japonica, the variety of the second wave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From Kyushu the crop moved east slowly and unevenly — above four kilometres (2.5 miles) a year in the Chugoku and Kansai regions, about one across the Kanto plain — so the Kanto has no rice until somewhere between 471 and 124 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Whether people moved with it that far is disputed, and one reading of the eastern evidence has rice reaching eastern and northern Japan without migration at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The genetic record shows a real influx from the continent in this period, but it is northern Kyushu that shows it most clearly, and the east is where the argument still runs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Miyamoto Kazuo, “The Spread of Rice Agriculture during the Yayoi Period: From the Shandong Peninsula to the Japanese Archipelago via the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2019): 109–24, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-2_109.pdf. [Open access]","Enrico R. Crema, Chris J. Stevens, and Shinya Shoda, “Bayesian Analyses of Direct Radiocarbon Dates Reveal Geographic Variations in the Rate of Rice Farming Dispersal in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 38 (2022): eadc9171, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adc9171. [Open access]","Gina L. Barnes, “The Jōmon–Yayoi Transition in Eastern Japan: Enquiries from the Kantō Region,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 7 (2019): 33–84, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol007/7-1_033.pdf. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]","Kenichi Yamamoto et al., “Genetic Legacy of Ancient Hunter-Gatherer Jomon in Japanese Populations,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15 (2024): 9780, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-54052-0. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Nabatake_Site_rice_field.JPG/1920px-Nabatake_Site_rice_field.JPG","title":"A paddy replanted at the Nabatake site","desc":"Rice growing in a small bunded plot laid out at the Nabatake site in Karatsu, Saga Prefecture, where excavation found some of the earliest paddy fields in the archipelago.","credit":"Photograph by Pekachu, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabatake_Site_rice_field.JPG","alt":"A small plot of young rice growing in a shallow flooded bed with a narrow channel along one side, edged by grass and trees."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-033","num":33,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Mumun pottery culture","answerText":"Mumun pottery culture","tags":["culture","archaeology","migration","genetics","korea","japan"],"question":"Farmers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the southern Korean peninsula are the people archaeologists have in mind when they speak of migration into Yayoi Japan.","questions":["Rock from across the Tsushima Strait was cut into the polished stone daggers of northern Kyushu by farmers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who settled among Jōmon neighbours.","Skulls from Yayoi cemeteries look continental rather than Jōmon, which is the oldest evidence that people of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> crossed to Japan and stayed."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Crossing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 900 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yayoi span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE – 200 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Mumun pottery culture</b> is the farming culture of the southern Korean peninsula whose people carried irrigated rice agriculture across to northern Kyushu, beginning around the 9th century BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They did not arrive to an empty country and they did not arrive alone: the migrants settled among Jōmon people already living on the Karatsu and Fukuoka plains, so the first Yayoi communities were mixed from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they brought is visible in the ground before it is visible in any text — paddy fields, dolmens, new pottery and polished stone daggers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stone itself points the same way, since tuffite tools from northern Kyushu were cut from rock whose sources lie across the Tsushima Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which parts of the package each region took, and in what order, varied enough that the differences shape Yayoi society for centuries afterwards, which is why the period looks so different in Kyushu, along the Inland Sea and in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The people are visible too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yayoi skeletons are markedly more continental than Jōmon ones, taller and with different faces, and that contrast is one of the oldest observations in Japanese anthropology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strontium isotopes in teeth from the Doigahama cemetery in Yamaguchi have since shown movement at a much smaller scale, identifying two individuals who grew up away from where they were buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient genomes give the same answer at the level of the whole population, with a continental influx in this period that stands as one of three strands in modern Japanese ancestry, beside the Jōmon and a later East Asian one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How many people crossed is still argued over, and the honest position is that the migration is certain while its size is not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Miyamoto Kazuo, “The Spread of Rice Agriculture during the Yayoi Period: From the Shandong Peninsula to the Japanese Archipelago via the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2019): 109–24, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-2_109.pdf. [Open access]","Daisuke Nakamura, “Acceptance of Burial Customs at the Beginning of the Yayoi Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 13, no. 21 (2006): 21–54, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.13.21. [Open access]","Mori Takanori, Yuhara Masaki, Umezaki Keiji, and Kawano Yoshinobu, “Estimating the Sources of Stone Tools Made of Tuffites during the Yayoi Period and Their Archaeological Significance,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 9 (2022): 117–40, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol009/9-2_117-140.pdf. [Open access]","Takamuku Hirofumi, Adachi Tatsuro, Nakano Nobuhiko, Tajiri Yoshinori, and Osanai Yasuhito, “Movement during the Yayoi Period: An Examination of the Western Burial Area of the Doigahama Site Using Strontium Isotope and Cranial Morphological Analyses,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 12 (2024): 31–57, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol012/12-1_031-057.pdf. [Open access]","Niall P. Cooke et al., “Ancient Genomics Reveals Tripartite Origins of Japanese Populations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 7, no. 38 (2021): eabh2419, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abh2419. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Korean-style_plain_coarce_pottery_pieces_from_Yoshinogari_Site.jpg/1920px-Korean-style_plain_coarce_pottery_pieces_from_Yoshinogari_Site.jpg","title":"Korean-style pottery from a Kyushu site","desc":"Fragments of plain coarse pottery of Korean type, excavated at Yoshinogari in Saga Prefecture — the migration visible as a potsherd rather than as an argument.","credit":"Photograph by Pekachu, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean-style_plain_coarce_pottery_pieces_from_Yoshinogari_Site.jpg","alt":"Four undecorated buff pottery sherds on a white museum surface, two of them preserving a thickened rolled rim."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-034","num":34,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Yayoi pottery","answerText":"Yayoi pottery","tags":["object","archaeology","art","prehistory","japan"],"question":"Thinner, plainer and thrown into far more shapes than what came before it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the whole period its name after a Tokyo district.","questions":["Measuring the outlines of pots statistically found two distinct local styles inside a single 65-kilometre stretch of northern Kyushu, which typology had read as one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tradition.","The great jar coffins that hold the dead of northern Kyushu are <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> too, made at a size no domestic vessel ever needed."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Itazuke style</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 400 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE – 250 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Yayoi pottery</b> is the earthenware of farming Japan, and it gave the period its name after the Tokyo district where the first pieces were recognised as something new.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is thinner-walled and far plainer than Jōmon ware, and it is made in a set of specialised shapes — storage jars, cooking pots, pedestalled serving dishes — that answer to a diet built on stored grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One style matters more than the rest for chronology: the Itazuke style of the Fukuoka plain, which spread across western Japan in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE and which some scholars take as the true beginning of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because pots change quickly and are thrown away in quantity, their styles are the framework everything else in the period is dated against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That framework has turned out to be coarser than it looked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Measuring vessel outlines statistically rather than sorting them by eye found two distinct morphological styles inside a single 65-kilometre stretch of northern Kyushu that typology had treated as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The finding is a caution about the method as much as about Kyushu: a style boundary drawn at the scale of a region can hide the potters working at the scale of a valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pottery in this period also carries the dead, which nothing in the Jōmon repertoire had been made to do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Northern Kyushu buried its people in enormous paired jars made for the purpose, a custom taken up from the continent along with the rest of the farming package and adopted differently in each region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Further east the pots record contact rather than replacement, with local traditions taking up Yayoi forms while much of the older way of life continued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Frances Loftus III, “Micro-Regional Complexities in Yayoi Pottery Form as Seen through Morphometric Statistical Analysis,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 9 (2021): 3–31, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol009/9-1_003-031.pdf. [Open access]","Miyamoto Kazuo, “The Spread of Rice Agriculture during the Yayoi Period: From the Shandong Peninsula to the Japanese Archipelago via the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2019): 109–24, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-2_109.pdf. [Open access]","Yoshinogari Historical Park, “The History of Yoshinogari,” Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism, https://www.yoshinogari.jp/en/introduction/history/. [Open access]","Daisuke Nakamura, “Acceptance of Burial Customs at the Beginning of the Yayoi Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 13, no. 21 (2006): 21–54, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.13.21. [Open access]","Gina L. Barnes, “The Jōmon–Yayoi Transition in Eastern Japan: Enquiries from the Kantō Region,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 7 (2019): 33–84, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol007/7-1_033.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/GiantYayoiFuneraryJar.jpg","title":"A jar coffin of the Yayoi period","desc":"One of the enormous funerary jars of northern Kyushu, laid on its side in a museum, made at a size no domestic vessel ever needed.","credit":"Photograph by PHGCOM, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GiantYayoiFuneraryJar.jpg","alt":"A very large plain earthenware jar lying on its side on metal supports, taller than the museum bench beside it."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-035","num":35,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Yayoi metalwork","answerText":"Yayoi metalwork","tags":["object","technology","archaeology","trade","japan"],"question":"Bronze and iron reach Japan together rather than in sequence, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> divides into ritual objects in one metal and working tools in the other.","questions":["A waterlogged site on the Japan Sea coast yielded cast iron axes, chisels, a ring-pommelled sword and fragments of bronze mirrors, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arriving as trade.","Long iron daggers with narrow handles were imported while shorter ones may have been made locally, and telling the two apart is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read at all."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE – 250 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Yayoi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 – 300 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Yayoi metalwork</b> is the bronze and iron of farming Japan, and the archipelago is unusual in receiving both metals at once instead of passing through a bronze age and then an iron one, so there is no Japanese Bronze Age to speak of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because they arrived together the two were sorted by use rather than by date: bronze went to ritual and display, iron to the working edge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clearest picture of the iron comes from waterlogged sites where it survives, above all Aoya Kamijichi on the Japan Sea coast of Tottori, which has yielded cast iron axes, a board-shaped axe, chisels and a sword with a simple ring pommel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same site produced fragments of bronze mirrors, jade beads whose stone came from Itoigawa, jasper from two named quarries and obsidian from the Oki islands, none of it local to Tottori.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Metal was therefore not a local product but a thing that moved, and the routes it moved along are as much the subject as the objects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Iron weapons show the trade in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Daggers and swords vary in size and style and are distributed irregularly from phase to phase, with long narrow-handled daggers apparently imported while some shorter ones may have been made in Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many concentrate on the Sea of Japan side of Honshu, which reads as consumer demand shaping what passed through the transit trade rather than as a simple gradient from the source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Stone did not vanish when metal arrived, and polished stone daggers and woodworking adzes went on being made from rock quarried across the Tsushima Strait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the metal changed fastest was violence, since the earliest weapons and fortifications in Japan belong to this period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]","Kazuhiro Ege, “Iron Daggers and Swords in the Yayoi Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 14, no. 23 (2007): 19–39, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.14.19. [Open access]","Mori Takanori, Yuhara Masaki, Umezaki Keiji, and Kawano Yoshinobu, “Estimating the Sources of Stone Tools Made of Tuffites during the Yayoi Period and Their Archaeological Significance,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 9 (2022): 117–40, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol009/9-2_117-140.pdf. [Open access]","Tomomi Nakagawa, Hisashi Nakao, Kohei Tamura, Yui Arimatsu, Naoko Matsumoto, and Takehiko Matsugi, “Violence and Warfare in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Letters on Evolutionary Behavioral Science</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): 8–11, https://doi.org/10.5178/lebs.2017.55. [Open access]","Yoshinogari Historical Park, “The History of Yoshinogari,” Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism, https://www.yoshinogari.jp/en/introduction/history/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/%E7%A0%B4%E9%8F%A1.jpg/1920px-%E7%A0%B4%E9%8F%A1.jpg","title":"A broken mirror, a bracelet and an arrowhead","desc":"Bronze from the Jūri site in Shiga Prefecture, of the end of the Yayoi and the start of the Kofun period: a fragment of mirror drilled for hanging, a bracelet and an arrowhead.","credit":"Photograph by あずきごはん, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A0%B4%E9%8F%A1.jpg","alt":"Three small bronze objects on red cloth above a Japanese museum label: a drilled mirror fragment, a green corroded bracelet and a thin arrowhead."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-036","num":36,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"dōtaku","answerText":"dōtaku","tags":["object","religion","archaeology","art","japan"],"question":"Cast in bronze, decorated with granaries and deer and then buried on a hillside away from any village, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the strangest object the Yayoi left.","questions":["Nobody is certain what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was for, and the prevailing view is that it served in agricultural rites tied to the rice harvest.","Two hoards in Izumo hold so many of them that the bronzes from one are national treasures, which is what makes a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a political object as well as a ritual one."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made during</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yayoi span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE – 300 CE</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>dōtaku</b> is a bell-shaped bronze casting of the Yayoi period, made across central and western Japan while rice-paddy cultivation was spreading through the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is shaped like a bell and hung like one, and the earliest are small enough to have rung; the later ones grow steadily larger and thinner until they could not have been rung at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they were for is not settled, and the prevailing view is that they were ceremonial implements used in agricultural rites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their decoration is the strongest argument for it, since the scenes cast into them include raised granaries for storing rice and the pounding of grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deer appear more often than any other animal, and the usual reading connects the yearly shedding and regrowth of antlers to the yearly cycle of the crop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How they were treated is stranger still, and it is the part that no reading of the decoration explains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most were found buried, singly or in groups, on isolated hillsides well away from any settlement, and one suggestion is that they were kept in the ground to draw the earth’s force while not in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two hoards in the Izumo region hold so many that they changed the map of Yayoi bronze: the bronzes from one are national treasures and the bells from the other important cultural properties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The metal itself came through the same trade that carried iron tools and Chinese mirror fragments up the Japan Sea coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bells of this kind stop being made around the end of the period and nothing in the graves and mounds that follow takes their place, which is one of the sharper breaks between the Yayoi and what came after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Kyoto National Museum, “Dotaku (Ritual Bronze Bells) and the Yayoi Period,” Museum Dictionary, https://www.kyohaku.go.jp/eng/learn/home/dictio/kouko/45dotaku/. [Open access]","Shimane Museum of Ancient Izumo, “Permanent Exhibition,” https://www.izm.ed.jp/english/. [Open access]","Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]","Miyamoto Kazuo, “The Spread of Rice Agriculture during the Yayoi Period: From the Shandong Peninsula to the Japanese Archipelago via the Korean Peninsula,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2019): 109–24, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-2_109.pdf. [Open access]","Kazuhiro Ege, “Iron Daggers and Swords in the Yayoi Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 14, no. 23 (2007): 19–39, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.14.19. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Dotaku_%28bell-shaped_bronze%29%2C_Yayoi_period%2C_2nd-1st_century_BC%2C_reportedly_from_Kagawa%2C_a_National_Treasure_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_DSC06320.JPG/1920px-Dotaku_%28bell-shaped_bronze%29%2C_Yayoi_period%2C_2nd-1st_century_BC%2C_reportedly_from_Kagawa%2C_a_National_Treasure_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_DSC06320.JPG","title":"A dōtaku with panelled scenes","desc":"A bronze bell of the 2nd or 1st century BCE, reported to come from Kagawa and designated a national treasure, its body divided into panels holding small figures and animals.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dotaku_(bell-shaped_bronze),_Yayoi_period,_2nd-1st_century_BC,_reportedly_from_Kagawa,_a_National_Treasure_-_Tokyo_National_Museum_-_DSC06320.JPG","alt":"A tall dark bronze bell with a broad flat loop on top, its sides ruled into rectangular panels each holding a tiny incised scene."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-037","num":37,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"moated settlement","answerText":"moated settlement","tags":["place","archaeology","warfare","japan","history"],"question":"A village ringed by a dug ditch, sometimes with a bank and towers behind it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the form Yayoi villages take as the period goes on.","questions":["Ditches deep enough to need explaining appear beside the first weapons and the first skeletons with weapon wounds, which is why a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is read as defensive.","By the late Yayoi the largest <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Japan had a V-shaped outer ditch and two enclosures inside it, holding the people who mattered."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yayoi span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest example</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late Yayoi, 1 – 300 CE</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>moated settlement</b> is a Yayoi village enclosed by a dug ditch, and it is the form that farming settlements in Japan take with increasing frequency as the period goes on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ditches are substantial, cut round the edge of a village and in the largest cases doubled, with a bank thrown up behind them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One well-studied Kyushu settlement grew from scattered hamlets into a ditched community, and then in the late Yayoi into the largest such village in the country, with a V-shaped outer ditch and two enclosures within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Whether every ditch was defensive is a real question, since a ditch also drains, marks a boundary and keeps animals out, and the earliest of them are modest enough to be read either way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What tips the reading towards defence is the company the ditches keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest fortifications and the earliest weapons in Japan both belong to this period, and skeletal evidence for violent death is significantly commoner in the Yayoi than in the Jōmon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not every large settlement was walled: Aoya Kamijichi on the Japan Sea coast is known instead for rows of plank pilings, waterlogged deposits and an extraordinary quantity of imported goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Eastern Japan followed its own course, and the settlements of the Kantō take up Yayoi forms without the wholesale replacement seen further west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Much of what a visitor now sees at such a site is reconstruction rather than excavation, built on evidence that is usually reburied for protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A survey of those reconstructions across Japan warns that they are poorly recorded, and that a site with buildings standing on it is read by the public as better understood than one without.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yoshinogari Historical Park, “The History of Yoshinogari,” Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism, https://www.yoshinogari.jp/en/introduction/history/. [Open access]","Tomomi Nakagawa, Hisashi Nakao, Kohei Tamura, Yui Arimatsu, Naoko Matsumoto, and Takehiko Matsugi, “Violence and Warfare in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Letters on Evolutionary Behavioral Science</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): 8–11, https://doi.org/10.5178/lebs.2017.55. [Open access]","Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]","Gina L. Barnes, “The Jōmon–Yayoi Transition in Eastern Japan: Enquiries from the Kantō Region,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 7 (2019): 33–84, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol007/7-1_033.pdf. [Open access]","John Ertl, “Survey of Prehistoric and Ancient Period Architectural Reconstructions in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2021): 157–99, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-2_157.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/%E5%90%89%E9%87%8E%E3%83%B6%E9%87%8C%E9%81%BA%E8%B7%A1%E7%92%B0%E6%BF%A0.JPG/1920px-%E5%90%89%E9%87%8E%E3%83%B6%E9%87%8C%E9%81%BA%E8%B7%A1%E7%92%B0%E6%BF%A0.JPG","title":"A reconstructed ditch and stake barrier","desc":"The V-shaped moat rebuilt at Yoshinogari, with pointed stakes set into the far bank and a palisade above the near one.","credit":"Photograph by 小池 隆, CC BY 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%90%89%E9%87%8E%E3%83%B6%E9%87%8C%E9%81%BA%E8%B7%A1%E7%92%B0%E6%BF%A0.JPG","alt":"A curving earth-cut ditch holding shallow water, with rows of sharpened wooden stakes leaning outward on one bank and a timber palisade on the other."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-038","num":38,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Yoshinogari","answerText":"Yoshinogari","tags":["place","archaeology","warfare","japan","history"],"question":"Ditched, towered and holding both a leaders’ mound and fields of burial jars, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Saga Prefecture is the Yayoi settlement everyone has seen pictures of.","questions":["The earth mound raised for the leaders at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 150 BCE is the oldest large earth fill in Japan built by a layered construction method.","Almost every building standing at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> today is a reconstruction, and the excavated remains beneath them are deliberately buried again under protective fill."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Settlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE – 300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burial mound</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 150 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Yoshinogari</b> is a Yayoi settlement in Saga Prefecture in northern Kyushu, and the most completely excavated and most widely reproduced site of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was not one village but a place lived in for some seven hundred years, growing from scattered hamlets into a ditched settlement and then into the largest moated village in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the late Yayoi it had a V-shaped outer ditch and two inner enclosures, the arrangement usually read as separating those who ruled from those who did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside the village lay its dead: a mound raised for leaders, and cemeteries of the great jars that northern Kyushu buried its people in, set mouth to mouth in pairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mound is the part that has been studied most closely by people who are not archaeologists, a large earth fill being a problem in soil mechanics as well as in prehistory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Dated to about 150 BCE, it is the oldest large earth fill in Japan built mainly by laying and compacting successive layers, a method whose parallels are Chinese rather than Korean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its builders, on that reading, took the technique by sea from the Chinese coast rather than overland through the peninsula, which fits what the imported goods of the period already suggested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The site sits in a period whose weapons, wounds and ditches make its defences look like defences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What a visitor walks through, though, is a reconstruction: the excavated remains are covered by more than 30 centimetres (12 inches) of protective fill with the park built above them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A national survey of such reconstructions warns that they are recorded poorly and that they quietly change what the public believes is known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Yoshinogari Historical Park, “The History of Yoshinogari,” Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism, https://www.yoshinogari.jp/en/introduction/history/. [Open access]","Katsutada Onitsuka and Yutaka Hara, “Roots of Construction Technique of Ancient Yoshinogari Burial Mound and Its Propagation,” <i>Journal of Japan Society of Civil Engineers, Ser. C (Geosphere Engineering)</i> 68, no. 4 (2012): 621–32, https://doi.org/10.2208/jscejge.68.621. [Open access]","Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]","Tomomi Nakagawa, Hisashi Nakao, Kohei Tamura, Yui Arimatsu, Naoko Matsumoto, and Takehiko Matsugi, “Violence and Warfare in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Letters on Evolutionary Behavioral Science</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): 8–11, https://doi.org/10.5178/lebs.2017.55. [Open access]","John Ertl, “Survey of Prehistoric and Ancient Period Architectural Reconstructions in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2021): 157–99, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-2_157.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Yoshinogari_Kitanaikaku_from_Minaminaikaku.jpg/1920px-Yoshinogari_Kitanaikaku_from_Minaminaikaku.jpg","title":"The inner enclosure at Yoshinogari","desc":"The reconstructed north inner enclosure seen from the watchtower of the south, with the raised hall that dominates the site and the hills of Saga behind it.","credit":"Photograph by Pekachu, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshinogari_Kitanaikaku_from_Minaminaikaku.jpg","alt":"Thatched buildings of different sizes behind a pale timber fence on a low rise, with wooded country and blue hills beyond."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-039","num":39,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Yayoi chiefdom","answerText":"Yayoi chiefdom","tags":["concept","history","archaeology","trade","japan"],"question":"Grave goods, imported glass and a mound raised for a few of the dead are the evidence that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had come into being by the middle of the period.","questions":["Lead isotopes showed that glass buried at a northern Kyushu site had been imported from northern China, which is what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent its position on.","Iron weapons were buried overwhelmingly with adult men, and who received one is the sharpest measure of rank inside a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the graves preserve."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Emerges</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 200 – 1 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yoshinogari mound</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 150 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>Yayoi chiefdom</b> is a farming community whose leaders had come to be distinguished from everyone else, and the archaeology of the period is largely the archaeology of that separation appearing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing in the record announces it, so it is read from small differences: who was buried where, with what, and how much of it came from far away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The clearest marker of it is the grave, and it grows clearer as the period goes on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burial customs were taken from the continent unevenly at the start of the period, and the regional differences that produced became the foundation of later imbalances between eastern and western Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the middle of the period a few of the dead in northern Kyushu were raised onto mounds of their own, one of them built about 150 BCE by a method with Chinese parallels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Imported goods are the other half of the argument.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lead isotope analysis of glass from Mikumo in northern Kyushu showed that it had been brought from northern China during this period, which is a very long way for a bead to travel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Iron weapons travelled shorter distances and were distributed just as unequally, buried overwhelmingly with adult men and concentrated in particular regions rather than spread evenly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One coastal settlement on the Japan Sea has produced cast iron tools, Chinese mirror fragments, jade from Itoigawa and jasper from named quarries, which is what a node in such a network looks like from the inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of the period these communities were dealing directly with the Chinese commanderies, and the largest of them are what the Chinese histories would shortly describe as kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]","Daisuke Nakamura, “Acceptance of Burial Customs at the Beginning of the Yayoi Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 13, no. 21 (2006): 21–54, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.13.21. [Open access]","Katsutada Onitsuka and Yutaka Hara, “Roots of Construction Technique of Ancient Yoshinogari Burial Mound and Its Propagation,” <i>Journal of Japan Society of Civil Engineers, Ser. C (Geosphere Engineering)</i> 68, no. 4 (2012): 621–32, https://doi.org/10.2208/jscejge.68.621. [Open access]","Shigenori Maeda, Junko Takase, Yoshinori Sugitani, and Kozo Nagashima, “Determination of Lead Isotope Ratios in Archeological Products by Ion Microanalyzer,” <i>Analytical Sciences</i> 1, no. 3 (1985): 227–30, https://doi.org/10.2116/analsci.1.227. [Open access]","Kazuhiro Ege, “Iron Daggers and Swords in the Yayoi Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 14, no. 23 (2007): 19–39, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.14.19. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/Yoshinogari_Yayoi_Village_b098.jpg","title":"Burial jars from one mound","desc":"Jar coffins recovered from a single burial mound at Yoshinogari, displayed together in the site museum; visitors behind them give the scale.","credit":"Photograph by Michael Gunther, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshinogari_Yayoi_Village_b098.jpg","alt":"A museum hall filled with a dozen waist-high earthenware burial jars standing open on metal cradles, with visitors walking behind them."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-040","num":40,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Yayoi warfare","answerText":"Yayoi warfare","tags":["event","warfare","archaeology","history","japan"],"question":"Ditched villages, the first weapons made in the archipelago and skeletons with wounds are the evidence behind any account of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and none of it is Jōmon.","questions":["Violent death is significantly commoner in Yayoi skeletons than in Jōmon ones, which is the measured basis for saying that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began with farming.","Set beside hunter-gatherer societies elsewhere the rate is low, so the case that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was constant does not survive the bones it rests on."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jōmon violent deaths</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1.8% of adults</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Yayoi span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE – 250 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Yayoi warfare</b> is the organised violence that appears in Japan with farming, and it is the one part of the period where the evidence can be counted rather than described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The traditional account is straightforward: the earliest fortifications and the earliest weapons in the archipelago both belong to this period, and neither has a Jōmon ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An exhaustive survey of Yayoi skeletal remains found violent death significantly commoner than in the Jōmon, which supports that account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jōmon figure it was measured against is 1.8 per cent of adults, twenty-three injured individuals out of 1,275, with no clustering in space or time that would suggest organised conflict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So something did change, and it changed when agriculture arrived — a shift that is measured rather than inferred from the ditches alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What did not happen is the picture of endemic slaughter that the same evidence is sometimes made to carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even in the Yayoi the rate of violent death is far below the figures reported for prehistoric societies elsewhere, and the authors of the survey read it as too low to have driven the evolution of human cooperation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The weapons say the same thing in another register, since iron daggers and swords were scarce, unevenly distributed and buried overwhelmingly with adult men rather than issued to anybody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Stone weapons preceded them, with polished daggers made from rock quarried across the Tsushima Strait, so the earliest fighting in Japan was done with stone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ditches and towers of the largest settlements are the most visible half of it, and they belong to the end of the period rather than its beginning, by which time those villages had been farming for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Tomomi Nakagawa, Hisashi Nakao, Kohei Tamura, Yui Arimatsu, Naoko Matsumoto, and Takehiko Matsugi, “Violence and Warfare in Prehistoric Japan,” <i>Letters on Evolutionary Behavioral Science</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): 8–11, https://doi.org/10.5178/lebs.2017.55. [Open access]","Hisashi Nakao, Kohei Tamura, Yui Arimatsu, Tomomi Nakagawa, Naoko Matsumoto, and Takehiko Matsugi, “Violence in the Prehistoric Period of Japan: The Spatio-temporal Pattern of Skeletal Evidence for Violence in the Jomon Period,” <i>Biology Letters</i> 12, no. 3 (2016): 20160028, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4843228/. [Open access]","Kazuhiro Ege, “Iron Daggers and Swords in the Yayoi Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 14, no. 23 (2007): 19–39, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.14.19. [Open access]","Mori Takanori, Yuhara Masaki, Umezaki Keiji, and Kawano Yoshinobu, “Estimating the Sources of Stone Tools Made of Tuffites during the Yayoi Period and Their Archaeological Significance,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 9 (2022): 117–40, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol009/9-2_117-140.pdf. [Open access]","Yoshinogari Historical Park, “The History of Yoshinogari,” Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism, https://www.yoshinogari.jp/en/introduction/history/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Polished_stone_daggers_from_Yoshinogari_Site.jpg/1920px-Polished_stone_daggers_from_Yoshinogari_Site.jpg","title":"Polished stone daggers","desc":"Blades and hilt fragments of ground stone from Yoshinogari, the weapon type that precedes metal in the archipelago and that has no use but fighting.","credit":"Photograph by Pekachu, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polished_stone_daggers_from_Yoshinogari_Site.jpg","alt":"Nine grey ground-stone pieces laid on white cloth: leaf-shaped blades, long rectangular fragments and two notched hilt ends."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-041","num":41,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Wa","answerText":"Wa","tags":["place","history","china","japan","concept"],"question":"For six centuries the Chinese histories call the archipelago <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a name for a scatter of small countries rather than for anything like a single state.","questions":["A gold seal dug out of a Kyushu field in 1784 reads “King of the Nu state of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of Han”, which matches an embassy the histories date to 57 CE.","A Chinese account of the second century says that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell into great disorder and fought for years with no ruler until a woman was set up as queen."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">57 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold seal found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1784</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Wa</b> is the name the Chinese dynastic histories give to the people and countries of the Japanese archipelago, and for six hundred years it is the only name for Japan in any surviving text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does not describe a state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Hou Han shu</i> records that in 57 CE the country of Nu, which it places at the extreme southern limit of Wa, sent tribute to the Later Han court, its envoy styling himself a grandee, and that the emperor Guangwu gave him a seal and ribbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That seal survives: a gold block a little over two centimetres (0.9 inches) square, weighing 108.7 grams and 95.1 per cent gold, turned up by a farmer on Shikanoshima in 1784 and inscribed with five characters naming the king of the Nu state of Wa of Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 107 CE a king of Wa sent a hundred and sixty people to the same court and asked for an audience.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The later record is fuller and stranger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The <i>Sanguozhi</i> describes Wa as thirty-odd countries reached by sea from the Daifang commandery in Korea, twelve thousand li away, with a great inspector stationed in the north to watch over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between about 146 and 189 CE, the earlier history says, Wa fell into disorder and fought for years with no ruler, until the countries agreed on a woman as queen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is a Chinese one and carries a Chinese judgement, and the countries it lumps together were dealing separately with the commanderies rather than acting as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What Wa meant on the ground is an archaeological question, and the interaction between these communities and their neighbours is now read as central to how a state came into being here at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Fan Ye, <i>Hou Han shu</i>, juan 85, “Dongyi liezhuan” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B785. [Open access]","Fukuoka City Museum, “金印” [The Gold Seal], https://museum.city.fukuoka.jp/gold/. [Open access]","Chen Shou, <i>Sanguozhi</i>, “Wei shu 30: Wuwan Xianbei Dongyi zhuan” (3rd century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97/%E5%8D%B730. [Open access]","Tadashi Nishitani, “Newest Circumstances on Yamataikoku,” <i>Journal of the Japanese Association for Petroleum Technology</i> 75, no. 4 (2010): 277–85, https://doi.org/10.3720/japt.75.277. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/King_of_Na_gold_seal_faces.jpg/1920px-King_of_Na_gold_seal_faces.jpg","title":"The gold seal of the king of Na","desc":"The seal's face on the left and its mirror image on the right, which is how the five characters read when stamped into clay.","credit":"Photograph by PHGCOM, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_of_Na_gold_seal_faces.jpg","alt":"Two views of a small square gold block cut with five archaic Chinese characters, the right-hand view reversed."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-042","num":42,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Hou Han shu","answerText":"Hou Han shu","tags":["text","history","china","japan"],"question":"Two embassies, a gold seal and a civil war are all that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives us of Japan in the first two centuries CE.","questions":["Because the archipelago had no writing of its own yet, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its successors are the only narrative sources for Japanese history before the 5th century.","The seal found on Shikanoshima in 1784 was identified from a single sentence in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why an object and a text confirm one another here."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First Wa embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">57 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second embassy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">107 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Hou Han shu</b> is the standard history of the Later Han dynasty, and its chapter on the eastern peoples carries the earliest narrative account of Japan that survives anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not a small point: the archipelago had no writing of its own, so for several centuries everything written about it was written abroad and by outsiders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chapter is short and its Japanese content shorter still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It records an embassy from the country of Nu in 57 CE and the seal given to it, a second from a king of Wa in 107 CE bringing a hundred and sixty people, and a long civil war in the second half of the 2nd century that ended when the countries jointly made a woman their ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also describes the customs of Wa at some length — tattooing, rice and hemp, divination by scorching bone, and a land without cattle, horses, tigers, leopards, sheep or magpies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The value of such a record is that it can sometimes be checked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The gold seal turned up in a Kyushu field in 1784 and was identified against the single sentence in this history that describes it, which is why the object and the text are usually cited together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the record cannot do is tell us how the countries it names related to one another, which is why its statements are now read beside excavation rather than in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fuller description of Japan belongs to the <i>Sanguozhi</i>, a third-century history whose account of Wa runs to some two thousand characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between them the two texts frame every argument about early Japan, including the one about where its ruling country actually was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Fan Ye, <i>Hou Han shu</i>, juan 85, “Dongyi liezhuan” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B785. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Fukuoka City Museum, “金印” [The Gold Seal], https://museum.city.fukuoka.jp/gold/. [Open access]","Tadashi Nishitani, “Newest Circumstances on Yamataikoku,” <i>Journal of the Japanese Association for Petroleum Technology</i> 75, no. 4 (2010): 277–85, https://doi.org/10.3720/japt.75.277. [Open access]","Chen Shou, <i>Sanguozhi</i>, “Wei shu 30: Wuwan Xianbei Dongyi zhuan” (3rd century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97/%E5%8D%B730. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/%E3%80%8E%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8%E3%80%8F%E6%9D%B1%E5%A4%B7%E4%BC%9D%E3%80%81%E5%80%AD.jpg/1920px-%E3%80%8E%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8%E3%80%8F%E6%9D%B1%E5%A4%B7%E4%BC%9D%E3%80%81%E5%80%AD.jpg","title":"The chapter on Wa, in a woodblock edition","desc":"The opening of the Wa section of the eastern-peoples chapter, in a 17th-century printing held by the National Diet Library, laid open beside a measuring scale.","credit":"National Diet Library Digital Collections, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E3%80%8E%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8%E3%80%8F%E6%9D%B1%E5%A4%B7%E4%BC%9D%E3%80%81%E5%80%AD.jpg","alt":"An open double page of a Japanese woodblock-printed book in columns of Chinese characters, photographed above a ruler."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-043","num":43,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Wajinden","answerText":"Wajinden","tags":["text","history","china","japan","archaeology"],"question":"Some two thousand characters at the end of a third-century Chinese history, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the fullest written description of Japan before its own records begin.","questions":["Tattooed faces, bone divination, markets watched by an official and a country with no horses are all details the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives about third-century Japan.","Every argument about where Japan’s earliest ruling country lay begins with the sailing directions in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which cannot be made to fit the map."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Compiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3rd century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Events described</span><span class=\"dt-v\">238 – 248 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Wajinden</b> is the account of the people of Wa that closes the thirtieth chapter of the <i>Sanguozhi</i>, the Chinese history of the Three Kingdoms, and it is the fullest description of Japan written before Japan wrote about itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs to roughly two thousand characters and reads like a report by someone who had been there or had questioned people who had.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It gives a route from the Daifang commandery in Korea through a chain of small countries, distances in days of sailing and marching, and household counts for each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It describes men who tattoo their faces and bodies, clothing made from a single width of cloth, rice, hemp and silk, weapons of spear, shield and wooden bow, and a country without cattle, horses, tigers, leopards, sheep or magpies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It notes divination by scorching a bone and reading the cracks, markets in every country supervised by an official, and commoners who step off the road into the grass when they meet a person of rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The political content is what has been argued over ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It names the queen Himiko, records the embassies between her court and Wei between 238 and 248, and describes the mound raised over her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its sailing directions are also the reason the argument exists, because followed literally they place the queen’s country in the ocean south of Kyushu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mirrors it says were sent to her have been matched against mirrors found in Japanese tombs, a line of argument that has run for decades without settling anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a source about Japan rather than from it, written by a foreign court with its own reasons for describing tribute, and it is read that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Chen Shou, <i>Sanguozhi</i>, “Wei shu 30: Wuwan Xianbei Dongyi zhuan” (3rd century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97/%E5%8D%B730. [Open access]","Tadashi Nishitani, “Newest Circumstances on Yamataikoku,” <i>Journal of the Japanese Association for Petroleum Technology</i> 75, no. 4 (2010): 277–85, https://doi.org/10.3720/japt.75.277. [Open access]","Toshikatsu Nishikawa, “Sankakubuchi Shinjukyo (Triangular-Rimmed Mirrors) and the Mirrors Presented to Himiko,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 87–99, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_87. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Fan Ye, <i>Hou Han shu</i>, juan 85, “Dongyi liezhuan” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B785. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Text_of_the_Wei_Zhi_%28%E9%AD%8F%E5%BF%97%29%2C_297.jpg/1920px-Text_of_the_Wei_Zhi_%28%E9%AD%8F%E5%BF%97%29%2C_297.jpg","title":"The text of the Wei zhi","desc":"Six pages of the Records of Wei reproduced side by side, the account of the people of Wa running through them in columns.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Text_of_the_Wei_Zhi_(%E9%AD%8F%E5%BF%97),_297.jpg","alt":"Six densely printed pages of classical Chinese laid out in two rows, each ruled into narrow vertical columns of characters."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-044","num":44,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Yamatai","answerText":"Yamatai","tags":["place","history","china","japan","concept"],"question":"Seventy thousand households and thirty-odd countries owing it allegiance make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the largest political thing in third-century Japan that anyone wrote down.","questions":["The country to the south of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a king of its own, would not submit to the queen, and was still fighting her in the 240s.","One reading of Japanese state formation puts its beginning in a third-century consolidation under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than in the fifth century or the seventh."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">238 – 248 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Households</span><span class=\"dt-v\">70,000+</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Yamatai</b> is the country where the queen of Wa held her court, according to the third-century Chinese account that is the only text to describe it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The text gives it more than seventy thousand households, far more than any other country it lists, and names four ranks of official under the queen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roughly thirty countries are said to have belonged to her, from the landing place in northern Kyushu to the limit of her territory, and the account names each in turn before admitting that the more distant ones cannot be described in detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a hegemony rather than a state: the countries keep their own names, officials and household counts, and what binds them is that they acknowledge one ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not all of them did, and the country of Kuna to the south had a king of its own, would not submit, and was still fighting the queen in the 240s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether Yamatai matters depends on where it was and on what followed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One of the three positions in the long argument about when a Japanese state began puts that beginning in a third-century consolidation across a wide stretch of the archipelago under this polity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The competing positions put it in the 5th century, when rulers of Wa were sending tribute to the Chinese Song court, or in the seventh, with written law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The archaeology of the same moment shows standardised keyhole-shaped tombs appearing in the middle of the 3rd century and a network of elite burial tying communities together across a thousand kilometres (620 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether that network is Yamatai, or the thing that replaced it, is the question the location argument is really about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Chen Shou, <i>Sanguozhi</i>, “Wei shu 30: Wuwan Xianbei Dongyi zhuan” (3rd century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97/%E5%8D%B730. [Open access]","Tadashi Nishitani, “Newest Circumstances on Yamataikoku,” <i>Journal of the Japanese Association for Petroleum Technology</i> 75, no. 4 (2010): 277–85, https://doi.org/10.3720/japt.75.277. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Tanaka Yutaka, “Progress in Land Transportation System as a Factor of the State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 5 (2017): 26–43, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol005/5-1_026.pdf. [Open access]","Fan Ye, <i>Hou Han shu</i>, juan 85, “Dongyi liezhuan” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B785. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Four_Commanderies_of_Han%2C_with_English_Names.png/1920px-Four_Commanderies_of_Han%2C_with_English_Names.png","title":"The commanderies the account measures from","desc":"The Han commanderies on the Korean peninsula, including Lelang and Daifang; the Chinese record counts twelve thousand li from Daifang to the queen's country.","credit":"Map by Stillskywkpd, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Four_Commanderies_of_Han,_with_English_Names.png","alt":"A map of the Korean peninsula and Liaodong with four coloured zones labelled Lelang, Xuantu, Lintun and Zhenfan."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-045","num":45,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Himiko","answerText":"Himiko","tags":["person","history","religion","china","japan"],"question":"Set up as ruler to end a war she had not fought, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> governed through a younger brother and was seen by almost nobody.","questions":["A Wei emperor titled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Ruler of Wa Friendly to Wei in 238, giving her a gold seal with a purple ribbon and a hundred bronze mirrors.","The mound raised over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was more than a hundred paces across, and the account says over a hundred attendants were buried with her."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named Ruler of Wa</span><span class=\"dt-v\">238 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 247 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Himiko</b> is the queen of Wa described in the third-century Chinese account of Japan, and the first named person in Japanese history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wa had been ruled by men for seventy or eighty years, the account says, and then fell into years of fighting, until the countries agreed together to make a woman their ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She served the way of spirits and could work on the crowd, she had no husband, and a younger brother helped her govern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A thousand female attendants waited on her, one man brought her food and carried her words in and out, and she lived behind a stockade with towers and armed guards where almost nobody saw her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earlier history places the same figure in the disorder of the later second century, so she was already old when she was made queen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Her dealings with China are dated precisely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 238 she sent an envoy to the Daifang commandery, and an imperial edict of that winter titled her Ruler of Wa Friendly to Wei and gave her a gold seal with a purple ribbon, brocades, swords and a hundred bronze mirrors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those hundred mirrors have been argued about ever since, since one case holds that the mirrors she was given were high-grade pieces kept at her court while the triangular-rimmed mirrors found by the hundred in Japanese tombs went to lesser recipients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> She died after a quarrel with the king of Kuna in 247, and a mound over a hundred paces across was raised for her with more than a hundred attendants buried alongside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A male successor was rejected and a thousand people died in the fighting before her young kinswoman was set up in his place and the country settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Chen Shou, <i>Sanguozhi</i>, “Wei shu 30: Wuwan Xianbei Dongyi zhuan” (3rd century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97/%E5%8D%B730. [Open access]","Fan Ye, <i>Hou Han shu</i>, juan 85, “Dongyi liezhuan” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B785. [Open access]","Toshikatsu Nishikawa, “Sankakubuchi Shinjukyo (Triangular-Rimmed Mirrors) and the Mirrors Presented to Himiko,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 87–99, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_87. [Open access]","Tadashi Nishitani, “Newest Circumstances on Yamataikoku,” <i>Journal of the Japanese Association for Petroleum Technology</i> 75, no. 4 (2010): 277–85, https://doi.org/10.3720/japt.75.277. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/%E5%AF%8C%E9%9B%84%E4%B8%B8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E4%B8%89%E8%A7%92%E7%B8%81%E7%A5%9E%E7%8D%A3%E9%8F%A1-2.JPG/1920px-%E5%AF%8C%E9%9B%84%E4%B8%B8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E4%B8%89%E8%A7%92%E7%B8%81%E7%A5%9E%E7%8D%A3%E9%8F%A1-2.JPG","title":"A triangular-rimmed mirror","desc":"Excavated from the Tomio Maruyama tomb in Nara: a bronze mirror of the type found by the hundred in Japanese tombs and long argued to be the kind sent to the queen.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AF%8C%E9%9B%84%E4%B8%B8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E4%B8%89%E8%A7%92%E7%B8%81%E7%A5%9E%E7%8D%A3%E9%8F%A1-2.JPG","alt":"A green corroded bronze disc with a central boss, ringed by relief figures of deities and beasts and bands of sawtooth pattern."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-046","num":46,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Yamatai debate","answerText":"Yamatai debate","tags":["theory","history","archaeology","japan","china"],"question":"Running for close to three centuries without a decisive find, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asks whether the queen’s country lay in Kyushu or five hundred kilometres east.","questions":["Yoshinogari is the site usually put forward for one side of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Makimuku with the Hashihaka mound for the other.","Whether a Japanese state begins in the third century or two hundred years later depends on the answer to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why it is not merely a puzzle about maps."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Text describes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">238 – 248 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Argued for</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 280 years</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Yamatai debate</b> is the argument over where the country of the queen Himiko actually lay, and it has run for something like 280 years without a decisive piece of evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It exists because the third-century Chinese account gives sailing directions that cannot be followed: taken literally, they put the queen’s country in the sea south of Kyushu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Something in the text is therefore wrong, and which part a scholar chooses to distrust decides the answer — an unusual position for a historical question to be in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Distrust the directions and the country sits in the Kinki region of central Honshu; distrust the distances and it sits in northern Kyushu, near where the envoys landed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A third position has it in Kyushu first and moving east afterwards, which would make both sides right in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Each side has its sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yoshinogari, the great moated settlement in Saga, is the usual exhibit for Kyushu; Makimuku in Nara, with the Hashihaka mound beside it, is the exhibit for the Kinki case, and one reading has that mound as the queen’s own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bronze mirrors were long expected to settle it, but the argument that the hundred mirrors sent from Wei were high-grade pieces rather than the triangular-rimmed type found by the hundred in Japanese tombs cuts the link they were supposed to provide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What makes the question worth arguing is that it is not really about a map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> If a wide consolidation under this polity existed in the third century, a Japanese state begins then; if it did not, the beginning moves to the fifth century or the seventh, and the whole shape of early Japanese history moves with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Tadashi Nishitani, “Newest Circumstances on Yamataikoku,” <i>Journal of the Japanese Association for Petroleum Technology</i> 75, no. 4 (2010): 277–85, https://doi.org/10.3720/japt.75.277. [Open access]","Chen Shou, <i>Sanguozhi</i>, “Wei shu 30: Wuwan Xianbei Dongyi zhuan” (3rd century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97/%E5%8D%B730. [Open access]","Toshikatsu Nishikawa, “Sankakubuchi Shinjukyo (Triangular-Rimmed Mirrors) and the Mirrors Presented to Himiko,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 87–99, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_87. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Tanaka Yutaka, “Progress in Land Transportation System as a Factor of the State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 5 (2017): 26–43, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol005/5-1_026.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Makimuku_Ruins_%28Tsuji_area%29.JPG/1920px-Makimuku_Ruins_%28Tsuji_area%29.JPG","title":"The Makimuku site today","desc":"The Tsuji area of the Makimuku site in Nara, the settlement at the centre of the case for a central Honshu location, now a field beside a railway line.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Makimuku_Ruins_(Tsuji_area).JPG","alt":"A rough grassy field with an information board at one side, houses and a passing two-car train beyond it and low hills behind."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-047","num":47,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Kofun period","answerText":"Kofun period","tags":["era","archaeology","history","japan"],"question":"Named for the tombs that are its most visible remains, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers the three and a half centuries in which a state took shape in Japan.","questions":["Horses were not native to Japan and arrive during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, imported from Korea along with the specialists who knew how to breed and ride them.","One tomb shape, shared by elites from Kyushu to the Kanto, is the clearest evidence for what held the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> together across a thousand kilometres."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 600 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mozu-Furuichi tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 600 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Kofun period</b> runs for about 350 years, from the middle of the 3rd century to the 7th, and it is named after the mounded tombs built through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is the period in which something recognisable as a state comes into being in Japan, and the argument about when exactly that happened is largely an argument about this period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most visible change is a shared burial form: standardised keyhole-shaped mounds appear in the middle of the 3rd century and are then built by elites from Kyushu to the eastern plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That shape ties local communities into a single network stretching a thousand kilometres (620 miles) from west to east, and the tie has never since been broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sharing a tomb plan is not the same as taking orders, and the network is read as weak at first and tightening over time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Two imports show how it tightened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ceramic figures set on the mounds were made to techniques that changed in the central polity and the local polities at the same moment, which argues for direct and continuing contact between the centre and the regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Horses were not native to the archipelago at all: they had to be brought from the Korean peninsula together with the specialists who could breed and ride them, and while the centre took the initiative, local polities invited those specialists themselves and kept their own relations with polities in southern Korea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Land transport barely existed at the start — there were no wheeled vehicles until the fourth century and movement went by water — which is part of why the network took so long to harden.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tombs stop being built on that scale in the 6th century, and the 89 that survive in one Osaka group are what the period left standing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Tanaka Yutaka, “Progress in Land Transportation System as a Factor of the State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 5 (2017): 26–43, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol005/5-1_026.pdf. [Open access]","Sasaki Ken’ichi, “Adoption of the Practice of Horse-Riding in Kofun Period Japan: With Special Reference to the Case of the Central Highlands of Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2018): 23–53, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-1_023.pdf. [Open access]","Hirose Satoru, “The Production of Haniwa and State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 27–77, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_027.pdf. [Open access]","Sakai City, “Profile of Mozu-Furuichi Kofun Group,” https://www.city.sakai.lg.jp/foreign-language/english/visitors/topics/world_heritage_site/Mozu-Furuichi_Kofungun.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Mozu_Kofun_Group_Aerial_photograph_2007.jpg/1920px-Mozu_Kofun_Group_Aerial_photograph_2007.jpg","title":"A group of tombs inside a modern city","desc":"The Mozu tombs in Sakai from the air, moated keyhole mounds of several sizes standing among streets and houses built up to their edges.","credit":"Aerial photograph by the Geospatial Information Authority of Japan, Attribution licence. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mozu_Kofun_Group_Aerial_photograph_2007.jpg","alt":"A vertical aerial view of a dense city in which several wooded keyhole-shaped mounds sit inside green moats."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-048","num":48,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"kofun","answerText":"kofun","tags":["object","archaeology","building","history","japan"],"question":"Ranging from over four hundred metres long to less than ten on a side, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a mounded tomb whose size stated its occupant’s place exactly.","questions":["The keyhole plan of the largest kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was copied by elites a thousand kilometres apart, which is the best evidence for how far the central polity reached.","Because most have never been dug, physicists have begun reading the inside of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with cosmic-ray muons rather than with a trowel."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 600 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest mounds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 400 m long</span></div>","abstract":"A <b>kofun</b> is a mounded tomb of the period named after it, built in Japan from the middle of the 3rd century to the late 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were built in several plans — round, square, and the keyhole shape that joins a round mound to a trapezoidal front — and in every size from over four hundred metres (1,300 feet) long down to squares less than ten metres a side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That range is the point of them, since a tomb’s plan and scale placed its occupant in a hierarchy that everyone building tombs understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The keyhole plan in particular was shared by elites across a thousand kilometres (620 miles), and it is the strongest single piece of evidence that a central polity reached that far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The largest are surrounded by moats and were faced with stone and set about with rows of ceramic cylinders, so a finished mound was a bright artificial hill rather than a green one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What is inside most of them is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The great mounds have never been excavated, and non-invasive methods are now being tried instead, including imaging one mound’s interior with cosmic-ray muons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That silence is old: the earliest written notice of a Japanese tomb is a Chinese account of a mound over a hundred paces across raised for a queen in the 3rd century, with more than a hundred attendants buried alongside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where excavation has happened, it has mostly been on the smaller mounds and on the ground around them, so the period is known best from its least important graves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eighty-nine survive in the one Osaka group that reached the World Heritage list, of a far larger number that once stood there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sakai City, “Profile of Mozu-Furuichi Kofun Group,” https://www.city.sakai.lg.jp/foreign-language/english/visitors/topics/world_heritage_site/Mozu-Furuichi_Kofungun.html. [Open access]","Hirose Satoru, “The Production of Haniwa and State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 27–77, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_027.pdf. [Open access]","Tanaka Yutaka, “Progress in Land Transportation System as a Factor of the State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 5 (2017): 26–43, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol005/5-1_026.pdf. [Open access]","Takefumi Hayashi, Kenji Sumiya, Masaharu Issiki, Gábor Nyitrai, László Oláh, and Hiroyuki K. M. Tanaka, “Interior Exploration of Unexcavated Ancient Burial Mounds by Muography Using MWPC Detector,” <i>Journal of Geography (Chigaku Zasshi)</i> 134, no. 6 (2025): 587–94, https://doi.org/10.5026/jgeography.134.587. [Open access]","Chen Shou, <i>Sanguozhi</i>, “Wei shu 30: Wuwan Xianbei Dongyi zhuan” (3rd century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97/%E5%8D%B730. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Hashihaka-kofun_zenkei.JPG/1920px-Hashihaka-kofun_zenkei.JPG","title":"A kofun from the ground","desc":"The Hashihaka mound in Nara seen across its pond: fifteen centuries on, a great tomb reads as a wooded hill with water round it.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hashihaka-kofun_zenkei.JPG","alt":"A long low wooded mound rising behind a wide still pond under a blue sky, with hills on the left."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-049","num":49,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Daisenryō Kofun","answerText":"Daisenryō Kofun","tags":["place","archaeology","building","history","japan"],"question":"Covering about forty-six hectares of Osaka and closed to everyone, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the largest tomb mound in Japan and has never been excavated.","questions":["Administered as an imperial mausoleum rather than as an archaeological site, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one of 899 such places held in Japan as objects of national reverence.","The group of tombs containing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the World Heritage list in 2019, by which time fewer than half of the mounds around it were still standing."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Mozu group built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 350 – 525 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Area</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 464,000 m²</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Daisenryō Kofun</b> in Sakai, on the plain south of Osaka, is the largest mounded tomb in Japan and one of the largest funerary monuments anywhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its enclosure covers about 464,000 square metres — some forty-six hectares, or a hundred and fifteen acres — and the keyhole-shaped mound at the centre is ringed by moats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is administered as the mausoleum of the emperor Nintoku, one of 899 mausolea and associated sites across 460 locations that the Imperial Household Agency holds as objects of national reverence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That status, rather than any archaeological judgement, is what governs it, and it is why the mound is treated as a place of reverence rather than as a site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has never been excavated, so nothing recovered from inside it identifies who was buried there, and what is known of its interior comes from the surface, from the moat edges and from remote methods now being trialled on unexcavated mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The tombs around it are the context that makes it legible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It stands in the Mozu group, built between the late 4th and the early 6th centuries, where mounds range from giants over four hundred metres (1,300 feet) long to squares under ten metres a side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In all, 89 of those mounds survive across the whole property, and a study of the Mozu group counts forty-four still standing there, the rest lost as Sakai grew over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What saved the survivors was a mixture of direct protection as historic sites and the indirect effect of district planning rules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The group was added to the World Heritage list in 2019, which is recent for a monument that has stood for fifteen centuries in the middle of a city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Imperial Household Agency, “陵墓” [Imperial Mausolea], https://www.kunaicho.go.jp/learn/institution/shisetsu/ryobo.html. [Open access]","Sakai City, “Profile of Mozu-Furuichi Kofun Group,” https://www.city.sakai.lg.jp/foreign-language/english/visitors/topics/world_heritage_site/Mozu-Furuichi_Kofungun.html. [Open access]","Takefumi Hayashi, Kenji Sumiya, Masaharu Issiki, Gábor Nyitrai, László Oláh, and Hiroyuki K. M. Tanaka, “Interior Exploration of Unexcavated Ancient Burial Mounds by Muography Using MWPC Detector,” <i>Journal of Geography (Chigaku Zasshi)</i> 134, no. 6 (2025): 587–94, https://doi.org/10.5026/jgeography.134.587. [Open access]","Yuma Murakami and Hiromu Ito, “The Remaining Process of the Mozu Kofun Group in the Urban Improvement of Sakai City,” <i>Japanese Journal of Landscape Architecture</i> 19, no. 2 (2026): 19–24, https://doi.org/10.5632/jjla.19.2_19. [Open access]","Agency for Cultural Affairs, “List of World Heritage Sites in Japan,” https://www.bunka.go.jp/english/policy/cultural_properties/introduction/world_heritage/list/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/NintokuTomb_Aerial_photograph_2007.jpg","title":"Daisenryō Kofun from above","desc":"The keyhole mound and its moats, with the streets of Sakai running up to the outer bank on every side.","credit":"Aerial photograph by the Geospatial Information Authority of Japan, Attribution licence. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NintokuTomb_Aerial_photograph_2007.jpg","alt":"A vertical aerial photograph of a huge wooded keyhole-shaped mound inside three green moats, surrounded by city blocks."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-050","num":50,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"haniwa","answerText":"haniwa","tags":["object","art","archaeology","religion","japan"],"question":"Standing in rows on the mounds rather than buried inside them, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the one part of a great tomb that was always meant to be seen.","questions":["Because their making changed in the centre and the regions at the same moment, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are used as evidence that a central polity kept direct contact with local elites.","The simplest and commonest kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a plain cylinder, and its changing shape gives archaeologists a clock for the tombs it stands on."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 525 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kofun period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 600 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Haniwa</b> are the unglazed ceramic objects set on and around the mounded tombs of Kofun-period Japan, made from the middle of the 3rd century to the early 6th.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commonest kind is a plain cylinder, planted in rows along the terraces of a mound; others take the form of houses, shields, sunshades, boats, animals and people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were placed on the surface rather than buried with the dead, which makes them the part of a tomb built to be looked at by the living.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their making was a substantial industry, organised by specialists, and it was tied directly to the mortuary ritual of the elite rather than to any household use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why they carry an argument about politics rather than about pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Production techniques changed in the central polity and in the local polities at the same time, which is hard to explain unless the centre was in continuing direct contact with the regions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read that way, the cylinders on a provincial mound are evidence of the reach of the polity in the Nara basin rather than of local fashion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because their forms change steadily they also work as a clock, and a revised chronology of cylindrical haniwa is used to argue for the continuity of the central polity through the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mounds they stand on are the tombs of the highest-ranking elites, the giant keyhole kofun whose builders shared one plan across a thousand kilometres (620 miles).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In all, 89 of those mounds survive in the Osaka group inscribed as World Heritage in 2019, and the rows of cylinders are among the few things about them that can still be examined closely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hirose Satoru, “The Production of Haniwa and State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 27–77, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_027.pdf. [Open access]","Tanaka Yutaka, “Progress in Land Transportation System as a Factor of the State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 5 (2017): 26–43, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol005/5-1_026.pdf. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Sakai City, “Profile of Mozu-Furuichi Kofun Group,” https://www.city.sakai.lg.jp/foreign-language/english/visitors/topics/world_heritage_site/Mozu-Furuichi_Kofungun.html. [Open access]","Agency for Cultural Affairs, “List of World Heritage Sites in Japan,” https://www.bunka.go.jp/english/policy/cultural_properties/introduction/world_heritage/list/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Periodo_kofun%2C_haniwa%2C_guerriero_con_armatura_keiko%2C_VI_sec%2C_02.JPG/1920px-Periodo_kofun%2C_haniwa%2C_guerriero_con_armatura_keiko%2C_VI_sec%2C_02.JPG","title":"A haniwa warrior","desc":"A sixth-century figure in lamellar armour, one of the shaped haniwa that stand alongside the plain cylinders on the later mounds.","credit":"Photograph by sailko, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periodo_kofun,_haniwa,_guerriero_con_armatura_keiko,_VI_sec,_02.JPG","alt":"A reddish clay figure of a standing warrior in plated armour and a helmet, with a sword at the waist, on a museum plinth."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-051","num":51,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Yamato polity","answerText":"Yamato polity","tags":["state","history","japan","kofun period","politics"],"question":"Archaeologists use <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than the word kingdom, because what held the Kofun-period centre together was alliance and prestige rather than borders or officials.","questions":["The largest mounded tombs of every phase stand in the Kinki region, which is the plainest evidence that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outranked every other group in the archipelago.","When the same changes in tomb furniture appear in the Nara basin and a thousand kilometres away in the same decade, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reaching the regions directly."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 250 – 600 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest tombs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 500 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Yamato polity</b> is the name given to the political centre that grew up in the Nara basin and the Kawachi plain during the Kofun period and eventually became the Japanese state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is called a polity rather than a kingdom because almost nothing about it looks like a state with borders, taxes and officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its reach is read out of tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The largest keyhole-shaped mounds of every phase were built in the central Kinki region, although the favoured spot moved twenty or thirty kilometres (12 to 19 miles) between phases, from the southeastern Nara basin to the Kawachi plain and back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That continuity of scale, held for three centuries while everything else changed, is the strongest single argument that one centre stayed on top of a landscape of otherwise self-governing local groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The centre worked through things rather than through orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Techniques of making the ceramic cylinders that stood on a mound changed in the central polity and in distant local polities at the same time, which is hard to explain unless the two were in continuing direct contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roads and coastal routes carried that contact, and the growth of a land transportation system is now treated as one of the conditions of state formation here rather than a consequence of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sea networks along the Japan Sea coast, which had moved goods and information between western communities since Yayoi times, were drawn into the same arrangement and turned towards the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> By the 6th century the leaders of the Kawachi plain were paramount, their monumental tombs at Konda and Daisen the largest ever built here, and the polity was beginning to look like the state that followed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Hirose Satoru, “The Production of Haniwa and State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 27–77, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_027.pdf. [Open access]","Tanaka Yutaka, “Progress in Land Transportation System as a Factor of the State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 5 (2017): 26–43, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol005/5-1_026.pdf. [Open access]","Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Kondagobyoyama_Kofun_aerial_shoot.jpg/1920px-Kondagobyoyama_Kofun_aerial_shoot.jpg","title":"Konda Gobyōyama Kofun","desc":"The second-largest of all the keyhole mounds, in the Furuichi group on the Kawachi plain, with its moat and the modern city built up to the edge of it.","credit":"Photograph by ブルーノ・プラス, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kondagobyoyama_Kofun_aerial_shoot.jpg","alt":"An oblique aerial photograph of a large wooded keyhole-shaped burial mound ringed by a moat, surrounded by suburban housing and roads."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-052","num":52,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"uji","answerText":"uji","tags":["institution","history","japan","kofun period","society"],"question":"A court society organised by name and hereditary duty rather than by office is what the word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> describes, each one supported by the labour of groups called be.","questions":["The ancestor names preserved in later court poetry belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and reciting them was how a group asserted where it stood among the others.","Because a kiln and the tombs of the two or three families who worked it turn out to be one social unit, archaeology can see the working half of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> system."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attested from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th-century compilations</span></div>","abstract":"An <b>uji</b> was a corporate group at the Yamato court that held a name, a hereditary duty and a body of dependent workers, and the ancient political order is usually described as a system of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each uji drew on labour organised in hereditary occupational groups called be, so a group was defined by what it did as much as by whom it descended from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence is awkward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything written about uji and be comes from compilations made in the 8th century, when the system was already being replaced, so its 5th-century shape has to be inferred backwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the ancestor names in those texts really did was regulate relations, turning a story about a founder into a claim about who ruled whom, and thereby stratifying the order under each uji rather than binding all of them to the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Archaeology can see the working end of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At one stoneware site in northern Kyushu the kilns and the small chambered tombs beside them belong to the same social units, with two or three families to a kiln, which is what a hereditary craft group looks like on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Distribution points the same way: the gilt-bronze harnesses and the standard stone chambers of the later period were controlled from the centre, so the people who had them were the people receiving both goods and instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A 5th-century sword records its maker, the king he served and his post as captain of the guard, which is service in a named office rather than a name for the group behind him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Chinese court, asked in 438 to confirm ranks for thirteen named men at once, was being shown the same layered following from the outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Hiroyuki Okada and Shunichi Hara, “Sue Ware Manufacturing Groups in the Kofun Period: A Study of the Sue Sugaura Site, Munakata City, Fukuoka Prefecture,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 25–42, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.25. [Open access]","Hiroaki Ota, “The Distribution of Materials and Information in the Late Kofun Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 13, no. 22 (2006): 127–45, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.13.22_127. [Open access]","Museum of the Sakitama Ancient Burial Mounds, “The Story of the Inariyama Sword,” Google Arts &amp; Culture, https://artsandculture.google.com/story/the-story-of-the-inariyama-sword-museum-of-the-sakitama-ancient-burial-mounds/-wVRNOAGEvhjLQ?hl=en. [Open access]","Shen Yue, <i>Song shu</i>, juan 97, “Yi Man: Wo guo” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%AE%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B797. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/TG61_of_Suemura_Kiln_Site.jpg/1920px-TG61_of_Suemura_Kiln_Site.jpg","title":"A Sue ware kiln","desc":"A sloping tunnel kiln from the Suemura group near Osaka, cut open in section after being moved and rebuilt: the workplace of a hereditary craft group.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TG61_of_Suemura_Kiln_Site.jpg","alt":"A long tunnel-shaped clay kiln built into a grassy slope, sliced lengthways so the arched firing chamber is open to view."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-053","num":53,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"horse-rider theory","answerText":"horse-rider theory","tags":["theory","history","japan","korea","kofun period"],"question":"Horses, armour and harness appear so abruptly in the 5th-century tombs that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> once explained them as the baggage of invaders from the continent.","questions":["What eventually undid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was that local groups turn out to have invited continental specialists themselves, which is the opposite of being overrun by them.","Postwar Japanese archaeology argued for two generations about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the weight of the evidence now favours long peaceful contact instead of conquest."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Explains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Proposed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1948</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>horse-rider theory</b> holds that mounted warriors from the continent crossed to the archipelago in the 4th century, conquered it, and founded the Yamato state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was first put forward in 1948, argued over for the better part of two generations of postwar Japanese archaeology, and the puzzle it sets out to answer is a real one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tombs of the 5th century abruptly fill with horse harness, iron armour and weapons where earlier ones had held mirrors and beads, and horses were not native to the islands at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the theory, a Buyeo-related people moved down the Korean peninsula, took northern Kyushu early in the 4th century, and about a century later seized the Kyoto–Osaka–Nara region and set up a court there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The break in the record was thus explained by new people rather than by new practices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The evidence has moved against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Horses and riding did arrive from Korea together with the specialists who knew how to keep them, but the central polity took the initiative in bringing them, and local polities invited such specialists themselves and kept their own diplomatic dealings with the southern Korean states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is a picture of many parties buying in for their own reasons, not of one party overrun by another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wider 5th-century change is now read as a deliberate shift by an existing centre from importing prestige goods to acquiring technique and knowledge, and even ornaments as small as the carved antler fittings on swords look like gifts used to tie immigrant and travelling craftsmen into local networks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Read across the whole millennium of contact the relationship is one of peaceful interaction for mutual benefit, which is why the conquest version is no longer the standard account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]","Sasaki Ken’ichi, “Adoption of the Practice of Horse-Riding in Kofun Period Japan: With Special Reference to the Case of the Central Highlands of Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2018): 23–53, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-1_023.pdf. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Yamada Shunsuke, “Lineages of Deer Antler Sword Ornaments,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 7 (2019): 3–31, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol007/7-1_003.pdf. [Open access]","Mizumura Naoto, “Importance of Circum-Japan Sea Trade Networks in the Transition from the Yayoi Period to the Kofun Period,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 3 (2015): 3–27, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol003/3-1_003-027.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Periodo_kofun%2C_haniwa%2C_cavallo%2C_VI_sec%2C_03.JPG/1920px-Periodo_kofun%2C_haniwa%2C_cavallo%2C_VI_sec%2C_03.JPG","title":"A haniwa horse","desc":"A 6th-century clay horse modelled with bridle, saddle, stirrup and harness pendants, the equipment whose sudden appearance the theory was built to explain.","credit":"Photograph by sailko, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periodo_kofun%2C_haniwa%2C_cavallo%2C_VI_sec%2C_03.JPG","alt":"A reddish clay figure of a standing horse wearing a modelled bridle, saddle and hanging harness ornaments."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-054","num":54,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Baekje","answerText":"Baekje","tags":["state","history","korea","japan","kofun period"],"question":"Almost everything the archipelago learned from the continent in the 5th century — writing, stoneware, horses, engineering — came through <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than directly from China.","questions":["When Goguryeo destroyed the capital of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 475, officials, scribes and craftsmen scattered, and many of them crossed to a court that already had friendly relations with it.","The Wa kings kept asking the Chinese court to confirm their command over <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the court, which dealt with that kingdom directly, kept refusing."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingdom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 660 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">475 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Baekje</b> was one of the three kingdoms of the Korean peninsula, occupying its south-west, and for two centuries it was the archipelago’s closest partner across the strait, its supplier of technique and its ally against the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relationship was not one of equals in either direction, and it was driven largely by Baekje’s own danger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Goguryeo took 64 of its fortresses between 396 and 407, and in 475 destroyed its capital at Pungnap in the Seoul area altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Leaders, officials, noblemen, scribes and skilled craftsmen fled; most went south to a new capital at Ungjin, but some sailed to Japan, where the Baekje court already had friends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those who came settled mainly in the Kawachi plain and the southern Nara basin, ran the stoneware kilns, and undertook the first large drainage and canal works in the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The traffic in knowledge ran the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing reached the archipelago from China by way of Baekje, and the small steaming vessels and hairpins buried in certain Yamato tombs can be traced through central Baekje back to the Eastern Jin court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Horses arrived with the people who knew how to keep them, and the gold and gilt-bronze ornaments in a Kyushu chieftain’s tomb are read as marks of his dealings with the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Wa kings wanted this acknowledged: from 438 they asked the Chinese court to confirm their military command over a list of countries that included Baekje, and the court struck it from the list every time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Baekje kept an embassy of its own at that court and was nobody’s to command, and the repeated request tells us more about what the Wa kings wanted said of them than about what they held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Sasaki Ken’ichi, “Adoption of the Practice of Horse-Riding in Kofun Period Japan: With Special Reference to the Case of the Central Highlands of Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2018): 23–53, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-1_023.pdf. [Open access]","Tokyo National Museum, “Iron Sword, Eta Funayama Tumulus, Kofun Period,” e-Museum, National Institutes for Cultural Heritage, https://emuseum.nich.go.jp/detail?content_base_id=100199&content_part_id=022&content_pict_id=0&langId=en. [Open access]","Shen Yue, <i>Song shu</i>, juan 97, “Yi Man: Wo guo” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%AE%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B797. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/%EB%AC%B4%EB%A0%B9%EC%99%95%EB%A6%89_%EC%B6%9C%ED%86%A0_%EC%8B%A0%EC%88%98%EB%AC%B8_%EC%B2%AD%EB%8F%99%EA%B1%B0%EC%9A%B8.jpg/1920px-%EB%AC%B4%EB%A0%B9%EC%99%95%EB%A6%89_%EC%B6%9C%ED%86%A0_%EC%8B%A0%EC%88%98%EB%AC%B8_%EC%B2%AD%EB%8F%99%EA%B1%B0%EC%9A%B8.jpg","title":"A mirror from King Muryeong's tomb","desc":"A bronze mirror from the tomb of a Baekje king at Gongju, the southern capital the court moved to after 475; a mirror from the same mould was buried in a mound in eastern Japan.","credit":"Korea Copyright Commission, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EB%AC%B4%EB%A0%B9%EC%99%95%EB%A6%89_%EC%B6%9C%ED%86%A0_%EC%8B%A0%EC%88%98%EB%AC%B8_%EC%B2%AD%EB%8F%99%EA%B1%B0%EC%9A%B8.jpg","alt":"A round green bronze mirror with a central boss, a square panel around it and figures of deities and beasts in relief within banded borders."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-055","num":55,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"toraijin","answerText":"toraijin","tags":["people","history","japan","korea","migration"],"question":"Stoneware, ironworking, horse breeding, engineering and literacy all entered the archipelago in the hands of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the people who came over from the continent.","questions":["Kilns in Kyushu with the graves of their own potters beside them are the plainest archaeological trace of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the crafts they brought with them.","Some among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> married into the ruling families and served the court as scribes and administrators, so the newcomers are not a separate story from the state."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chiefly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 700 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Toraijin</b> is the term later Japanese usage applies to people who crossed from the continent, above all from the Korean peninsula, and settled in the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were not one wave and not one kind of person, and the word covers several centuries of arrivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among those who came in the 5th and 6th centuries were carpenters, ironsmiths, potters, goldsmiths and silversmiths, engineers of public works, scribes, scholars, priests, warriors and aristocrats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they carried was practical: the knowledge to manage estates and offices, to build iron and other craft industries, to breed horses and to move goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of them and their descendants married into the ruling families and became leaders in the making of the state, so they belong to its history rather than beside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest archaeological trace is stoneware.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hard grey vessels fired in sloping kilns were introduced from southern Korea in the 5th century, and at one Kyushu site the kilns and the small tombs of the families who worked them form a single unit, two or three households to a kiln.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Certain small steaming pots and double-pronged hairpins found in tombs around the Yamato palaces mark the same movement, and their design can be followed back through central Baekje to China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Horses came with the handlers who knew how to keep and breed them, and both the central polity and the local ones went out of their way to bring such specialists in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The strait had been crossed this way before: the languages of the archipelago themselves are argued to have spread across it at the opening of the Yayoi period, more than a thousand years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]","Hiroyuki Okada and Shunichi Hara, “Sue Ware Manufacturing Groups in the Kofun Period: A Study of the Sue Sugaura Site, Munakata City, Fukuoka Prefecture,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 25–42, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.25. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Sasaki Ken’ichi, “Adoption of the Practice of Horse-Riding in Kofun Period Japan: With Special Reference to the Case of the Central Highlands of Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2018): 23–53, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-1_023.pdf. [Open access]","Miyamoto Kazuo, “Archaeological Explanation for the Diffusion Theory of the Japonic and Koreanic Languages,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 4 (2016): 53–75, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol004/4-1_053.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Pilgrim_flask%2C_Japan%2C_late_Kofun_or_Asuka_period%2C_c._600_AD%2C_Sue_ware%2C_stoneware_with_ash_glaze_-_Dallas_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC05121.jpg/1920px-Pilgrim_flask%2C_Japan%2C_late_Kofun_or_Asuka_period%2C_c._600_AD%2C_Sue_ware%2C_stoneware_with_ash_glaze_-_Dallas_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC05121.jpg","title":"A Sue ware flask","desc":"Hard grey stoneware, thrown on a wheel and fired in a sloping kiln at a heat no earlier potter in the archipelago could reach.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pilgrim_flask%2C_Japan%2C_late_Kofun_or_Asuka_period%2C_c._600_AD%2C_Sue_ware%2C_stoneware_with_ash_glaze_-_Dallas_Museum_of_Art_-_DSC05121.jpg","alt":"A dark grey round-bodied ceramic flask with a flared neck and two small lugs, standing on a museum plinth."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-056","num":56,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":2,"undatable":true,"answer":"kanji","answerText":"kanji","tags":["concept","history","japan","china","writing"],"question":"Because the archipelago had no script of its own, writing arrived as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Chinese characters brought over by scribes in the service of the court.","questions":["A sword inscribed with 115 characters in gold is the longest thing written in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to survive from the 5th-century archipelago.","Somebody at the Wa king’s court could compose classical prose in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when he sent a formal memorial to a Chinese emperor in 478."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscriptions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 471 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Kanji</b> are the Chinese characters used to write Japanese, and their arrival is the point at which the archipelago enters the written record on its own account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing at all had been written there before, in any script.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The characters came from China by way of Baekje, and the archaeological evidence for the movement of the Baekje and Chinese immigrants who carried them is now good enough to be studied in its own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Scribes are named among the craftsmen and officials who crossed after the fall of the Baekje capital in 475, and they served the court in official capacities once they arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What they brought was not a way of writing Japanese but the ability to read and compose literary Chinese, which is a different and considerably harder thing than writing down one’s own speech.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The earliest results of that learning survive on metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An iron sword from a mound in Saitama carries 115 characters inlaid in gold, naming its maker, his post and the king he served, in a text dated by a cyclical year usually read as 471.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second sword, from a tomb in Kumamoto, is inlaid in silver and is among the oldest written records made anywhere in the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The court could also write outwards: the memorial its king sent to the Chinese emperor in 478 is a piece of accomplished classical prose, with allusions and a formal complaint about a neighbour blocking the sea road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Writing the Japanese language itself, rather than Chinese, took several more centuries of experiment, and the two kana syllabaries were eventually worked out of simplified forms of these same characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]","Museum of the Sakitama Ancient Burial Mounds, “The Story of the Inariyama Sword,” Google Arts &amp; Culture, https://artsandculture.google.com/story/the-story-of-the-inariyama-sword-museum-of-the-sakitama-ancient-burial-mounds/-wVRNOAGEvhjLQ?hl=en. [Open access]","Tokyo National Museum, “Iron Sword, Eta Funayama Tumulus, Kofun Period,” e-Museum, National Institutes for Cultural Heritage, https://emuseum.nich.go.jp/detail?content_base_id=100199&content_part_id=022&content_pict_id=0&langId=en. [Open access]","Shen Yue, <i>Song shu</i>, juan 97, “Yi Man: Wo guo” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%AE%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B797. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/%E6%B1%9F%E7%94%B0%E8%88%B9%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%8A%80%E8%B1%A1%E5%B5%8C%E9%8A%98%E5%A4%A7%E5%88%80.JPG/1920px-%E6%B1%9F%E7%94%B0%E8%88%B9%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%8A%80%E8%B1%A1%E5%B5%8C%E9%8A%98%E5%A4%A7%E5%88%80.JPG","title":"The Eta Funayama sword and its inscription","desc":"The silver-inlaid blade from a mound in Kumamoto, shown with a museum panel giving each of its characters enlarged and in order.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B1%9F%E7%94%B0%E8%88%B9%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E9%8A%80%E8%B1%A1%E5%B5%8C%E9%8A%98%E5%A4%A7%E5%88%80.JPG","alt":"A long corroded iron sword lying in a display case beneath a printed panel showing rows of Chinese characters copied from its blade."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-057","num":57,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Inariyama sword","answerText":"Inariyama sword","tags":["object","history","japan","kofun period","writing"],"question":"Gold inlay on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> preserves 115 characters, the longest inscription to survive from the Kofun period and a national treasure since 1983.","questions":["A man named Wowake had the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made to record his service, and the king he names is thought to be the same ruler the Chinese called Bu.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was buried four hundred kilometres from the Nara basin, it shows how far the central court’s claims on men and service could reach."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 471 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">National Treasure</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1983</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Inariyama sword</b> is an iron sword 73.5 centimetres (29 inches) long, taken from the burial chamber of the oldest great mound in the Saitama cluster, and it carries 115 characters inlaid in gold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is by a wide margin the longest inscription surviving from the Kofun period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The text names the man who had it made, Wowake, sets out his pride in serving his king and assisting the government, and gives his post as captain of the guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is dated only by a year in the sixty-year cycle, so the reading is not certain, but the year is generally taken to be 471.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was designated a national treasure in 1983 and is displayed with the other finds from the same chamber.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its importance is that it joins two bodies of evidence that had never met: the Chinese histories and the archaeology of the mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ruler Wowake names is read as Wakatakeru, a great king, and that ruler is thought to be the same man the Chinese histories call Bu, whose memorial to the emperor of Song is dated 478.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second inscribed sword, from a tomb in Kumamoto at the other end of the country, carries a partly legible royal name read the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saitama is some four hundred kilometres (250 miles) from the Nara basin, so a man there recording service to a king in the west says something about how far the centre’s claims travelled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It matches what the objects say: harness fittings and stone chambers of the standard central design turn up in the same distant regions, changing their form on the same schedule as at the court, distributed by a centre reaching them directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Museum of the Sakitama Ancient Burial Mounds, “The Story of the Inariyama Sword,” Google Arts &amp; Culture, https://artsandculture.google.com/story/the-story-of-the-inariyama-sword-museum-of-the-sakitama-ancient-burial-mounds/-wVRNOAGEvhjLQ?hl=en. [Open access]","Shen Yue, <i>Song shu</i>, juan 97, “Yi Man: Wo guo” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%AE%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B797. [Open access]","Tokyo National Museum, “Iron Sword, Eta Funayama Tumulus, Kofun Period,” e-Museum, National Institutes for Cultural Heritage, https://emuseum.nich.go.jp/detail?content_base_id=100199&content_part_id=022&content_pict_id=0&langId=en. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Hiroaki Ota, “The Distribution of Materials and Information in the Late Kofun Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 13, no. 22 (2006): 127–45, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.13.22_127. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/%E7%A8%B2%E8%8D%B7%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F%E9%89%84%E5%89%A3_%28%E8%A1%A8%E9%9D%A2%29.JPG/1920px-%E7%A8%B2%E8%8D%B7%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F%E9%89%84%E5%89%A3_%28%E8%A1%A8%E9%9D%A2%29.JPG","title":"The inscribed face of the Inariyama sword","desc":"The gold characters run the length of the corroded blade, still legible fifteen centuries after they were hammered into it.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%A8%B2%E8%8D%B7%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%A4%E5%A2%B3%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F%E9%89%84%E5%89%A3_%28%E8%A1%A8%E9%9D%A2%29.JPG","alt":"A long rusted iron sword blade mounted upright, with a column of small gold characters inlaid down its centre."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-058","num":58,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Kofun aristocracy","answerText":"Kofun aristocracy","tags":["concept","history","japan","kofun period","society"],"question":"Rank in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made and remade by burial, each new mark of distinction being copied downwards until the top had to invent another.","questions":["Early mounds in northern Kyushu were furnished much alike whatever their size, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had not yet separated itself from the people beneath it.","Gilt-bronze harness of the central design was not for sale, and owning it placed a man in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and in the flow of news from the court."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 400 – 600 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Kofun aristocracy</b> is the hereditary upper stratum that emerged in the archipelago during the 5th and 6th centuries, and it was built by the practice of burial itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It did not exist at the outset of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the early Kofun period in northern Kyushu, chieftains and lesser people were buried in much the same kind of facility, and there is no significant distinction of status to be read from the tombs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the beginning of the middle period a new type of burial appeared in the chieftains’ tombs, plainly meant to mark them off, and it was then copied in the smaller tombs by people who wanted the same standing, so the chieftains adopted another new form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rank here is a moving target rather than a fixed ladder, and the aristocracy is the residue of that chase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The other half of it was supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Gilt-bronze harness and stone chambers of the standard central design changed their form at regular intervals, and only those in touch with the court could keep pace, so possessing the current type marked a man as receiving materials and information from the centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even minor fittings carried the message: carved antler ornaments on swords fall into distinct lineages, and some appear to have been handed out to bind travelling and immigrant craftsmen into a lord’s following.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Horse-riding was adopted for the same reasons, with local lords fetching in specialists on their own account and keeping their own dealings with the Korean states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What kept the whole display running was an organised industry of specialist makers, tied to elite funerals rather than to any everyday use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Teruyuki Shigefuji and Kenichiro Nishi, “Regionality and Hierarchy Structure of Burial Facilities in Kofun Period Northern Kyushu Region,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 2, no. 2 (1995): 95–117, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.2.95. [Open access]","Hiroaki Ota, “The Distribution of Materials and Information in the Late Kofun Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 13, no. 22 (2006): 127–45, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.13.22_127. [Open access]","Yamada Shunsuke, “Lineages of Deer Antler Sword Ornaments,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 7 (2019): 3–31, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol007/7-1_003.pdf. [Open access]","Sasaki Ken’ichi, “Adoption of the Practice of Horse-Riding in Kofun Period Japan: With Special Reference to the Case of the Central Highlands of Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 6 (2018): 23–53, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol006/6-1_023.pdf. [Open access]","Hirose Satoru, “The Production of Haniwa and State Formation in Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 27–77, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_027.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/KofunTombJewelry.jpg","title":"Gilt-bronze harness fittings","desc":"Pendants and mounts from horse harness of the kind whose design changed on a schedule set at the court, so that owning the current pattern showed who was in touch with it.","credit":"Photograph by PHGCOM, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KofunTombJewelry.jpg","alt":"Ten small gilded bronze harness ornaments, pendants and studs, pinned in rows against a pale cloth backing."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-059","num":59,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Terminal Kofun period","answerText":"Terminal Kofun period","tags":["era","history","japan","kofun period"],"question":"The change that defines the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that keyhole mounds stopped being built while small chambered tombs multiplied in their thousands across the countryside.","questions":["A tomb of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent its effort on the chamber inside rather than on the mound above, and only in the east did the older preference survive.","Ordinary farming families were being buried under mounds of their own by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is not what a monopoly of display looks like."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 550 – 710 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Terminal Kofun period</b> is the last phase of mounded-tomb building in the archipelago, running from the middle of the 6th century until the practice stopped altogether in the early eighth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What ends is the giant keyhole mound; what spreads is the tomb itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Effort moved from the mound to the chamber: in the final stage the type that puts its investment in the mound gives way to a type combining both, everywhere except in and north of the Kanto region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hierarchy in the surviving mounds appears to turn on a threshold of about sixty metres (200 feet) of length of length rather than on the finer gradations of the older mounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chamber that spread was a standard one, and that is the point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A single design of corridor chamber, worked out in the central region, was adopted at every level of society over a wide area, and it changed as one series wherever it was used, so the technique was travelling out from the centre to the men who served it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The goods buried in those chambers moved the same way and under the same control, which is what makes them readable as a map of who was connected to the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Below that level the change was social.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cemeteries of hundreds or thousands of small chambered mounds appear, belonging to family-sized groups within ordinary settlements, so people who could never have built a great mound were now buried under one of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The centre’s own effort was turning elsewhere, from importing prestige goods towards acquiring technique and knowledge, and within a century the labour that had raised mounds was raising Buddhist temples instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Takashi Aoki, “Interrelationships between Mounds and Stone Chambers in Mounded Tombs,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 14, no. 23 (2007): 41–65, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.14.41. [Open access]","Hiroaki Ota, “The Development and Diffusion of the Kinai Type Stone Chamber,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 15 (2003): 35–56, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.35. [Open access]","Hiroaki Ota, “The Distribution of Materials and Information in the Late Kofun Period,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 13, no. 22 (2006): 127–45, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.13.22_127. [Open access]","Hiroyuki Okada and Shunichi Hara, “Sue Ware Manufacturing Groups in the Kofun Period: A Study of the Sue Sugaura Site, Munakata City, Fukuoka Prefecture,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 25–42, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.25. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Ishibutai-kofun_Asuka_Nara_pref05n4592.jpg/1920px-Ishibutai-kofun_Asuka_Nara_pref05n4592.jpg","title":"Ishibutai Kofun","desc":"The chamber of a tomb at Asuka, built of enormous granite blocks; the mound that once covered it has gone, which is how the change of emphasis can be seen at a glance.","credit":"Photograph by 663highland, CC BY 2.5. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishibutai-kofun_Asuka_Nara_pref05n4592.jpg","alt":"A massive megalithic chamber of huge grey granite boulders standing bare on a gravel platform, with wooded hills behind."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-060","num":60,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"five kings of Wa","answerText":"five kings of Wa","tags":["people","history","japan","china","diplomacy"],"question":"Between 421 and 478 the Chinese court recorded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> — Zan, Chin, Sei, Ko and Bu — sending embassies and asking for titles.","questions":["Each of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked to be recognised as military commander over a list of countries, and the Chinese court kept quietly cutting Baekje out of the list.","A memorial from the last of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> claims 55 countries subdued in the east and 95 across the sea to the north."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">421 – 478 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recorded in</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Song shu, 5th century CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>five kings of Wa</b> are the rulers of the archipelago named in the Chinese <i>Song shu</i> as sending embassies to the southern court between 421 and 478, given there as Zan, Chin, Sei, Ko and Bu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The account is short, and it is almost entirely about titles rather than about anything that happened in the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zan was granted an appointment in 421 and sent an envoy in 425; his brother Chin styled himself supreme commander over six countries, including Baekje, and the court granted him only the title of general and king of Wa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chin also secured ranks for thirteen of his own men, and Sei later obtained appointments for 23 more, which shows a following being fitted into a foreign hierarchy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The six-country command Sei was finally granted in 451 quietly dropped Baekje and added Kara instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Bu’s memorial of 478 is the one passage with a voice in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He writes that his forefathers in arms subdued 55 countries of hairy men in the east, 66 of various barbarians in the west, and 95 across the sea to the north, and complains that Goguryeo is blocking his route to the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those figures are a claim rather than a survey, and the whole exchange is best read as a bid for standing in an East Asian order in which titles from a Chinese court were currency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bu is generally identified with the great king Wakatakeru named on two inscribed swords buried at opposite ends of the archipelago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Behind the correspondence lay the real business of the 5th century, which was the flow of iron, technique and people between the archipelago and the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Shen Yue, <i>Song shu</i>, juan 97, “Yi Man: Wo guo” (5th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E5%AE%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B797. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Museum of the Sakitama Ancient Burial Mounds, “The Story of the Inariyama Sword,” Google Arts &amp; Culture, https://artsandculture.google.com/story/the-story-of-the-inariyama-sword-museum-of-the-sakitama-ancient-burial-mounds/-wVRNOAGEvhjLQ?hl=en. [Open access]","Tokyo National Museum, “Iron Sword, Eta Funayama Tumulus, Kofun Period,” e-Museum, National Institutes for Cultural Heritage, https://emuseum.nich.go.jp/detail?content_base_id=100199&content_part_id=022&content_pict_id=0&langId=en. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Eastern_Central_and_Southern_Asia_ca_440_AD.jpg/1920px-Eastern_Central_and_Southern_Asia_ca_440_AD.jpg","title":"East Asia in about 440 CE","desc":"A historical atlas plate of the world the Wa embassies crossed: the Southern dynasty in yellow, Northern Wei in pink, and Goguryeo, Baekje, Silla and Kaya between them and the archipelago.","credit":"Map by Albert Herrmann, Historical and Commercial Atlas of China, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Central_and_Southern_Asia_ca_440_AD.jpg","alt":"A coloured historical map of eastern Asia showing the Chinese dynasties, the Korean kingdoms and the Japanese islands with place names in an older romanisation."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-061","num":61,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Asuka period","answerText":"Asuka period","tags":["era","history","japan","buddhism","politics"],"question":"Everything that made Japan a state arrived within the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> — Buddhism, a written administration, coinage and a capital laid out on a grid.","questions":["A single small valley in the Nara basin gives its name to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because the court kept its palaces there for more than a century.","Roof tiles, workshops and a burnt temple are most of what survives above ground from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and one compound of it still stands."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">592 – 710 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First temple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">588 – 596 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Asuka period</b> is named for a small district at the southern end of the Nara basin where the court kept its palaces from the end of the 6th century until the capital moved to Nara in 710.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a short period, and it changed almost everything about the way the islands were governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Buddhism arrived and was accepted, writing became the ordinary business of government, embassies went to China, and a state built on Chinese models replaced a court of hereditary families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first Buddhist temple in the islands was raised here with craftsmen sent over by the Korean kingdom of Baekje, whose distinctive roof tiles can be traced back through Baekje to Six Dynasties China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A great many more temples followed, so many that the founder of several is still unknown, and they can be sorted by the moulds their eave tiles came out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The ground has been generous to archaeologists here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One valley beside the first temple held about 300 furnaces working gold, silver, copper, glass, iron and lacquer, along with the earliest Japanese coin and the mould it was struck from, and some 8,000 inscribed wooden tablets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Almost nothing stands from the period apart from the western precinct of one temple at Ikaruga and a pagoda beside it, so the architecture is otherwise known from postholes and fallen timbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Palaces moved from reign to reign, which is why the district holds several of them rather than one capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The period closes when the court stops moving and settles at Nara, and the state it had spent a century building is written down as a code of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 117–302, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Akihiro Shimizu, “The Establishment and Development of the Paekche Daetongsa (Daitsuji) Style Roof Tile,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 81–95, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.81. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Village_Asuka_-_panoramio.jpg/1920px-Village_Asuka_-_panoramio.jpg","title":"The Asuka valley","desc":"Rice terraces and wooded hills at the southern end of the Nara basin: the district that gives the period its name and held the court's palaces for a century.","credit":"Photograph by MAKIKO OMOKAWA, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Village_Asuka_-_panoramio.jpg","alt":"A green valley of terraced rice paddies and scattered farmhouses below low wooded hills, with mountains beyond."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-062","num":62,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Asukadera","answerText":"Asukadera","tags":["building","history","japan","buddhism","korea"],"question":"Vowed in the middle of a battle and finished nine years later, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first proper Buddhist temple built anywhere in the Japanese islands.","questions":["Baekje sent the craftsmen who raised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the tiles they laid on its roof carry a lotus design that can be followed back to China.","Relics of the Buddha were laid under the pillar of the pagoda at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 593, which is the earliest such ceremony recorded in Japan."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">588 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Finished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">596 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Asukadera</b>, also called Hōkō-ji, was the first Buddhist temple built in the Japanese islands, raised at Asuka between 588 and 596 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Buddhism itself had arrived earlier, as an object rather than a practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle records that in 552 the king of Baekje sent the court an image of the Buddha in gold and copper, with banners, umbrellas and a number of volumes of scripture, and a memorial saying that the doctrine was the most excellent of all and hard to comprehend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chinese account is blunter and says that the Wa had no writing, only notched wood and knotted cords, until they sought Buddhist scriptures from Baekje and thereby first had letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What made the religion permanent was a building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The temple was vowed during the fighting of 587 by the head of the Soga house, who promised a temple and a pagoda if he won, and building began the next year on the site of a house he had pulled down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Relics were laid in the foundation stone of the pagoda pillar in 593, the pillar was raised the following day, and the temple was finished in 596 with two priests from Goguryeo taking up residence in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Baekje sent the craftsmen, and the eave tiles they made carry a lotus design of a kind developed at a Baekje temple out of techniques taken from Six Dynasties China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same moulds turn up at other temples nearby, which is how the Soga and their branches can be followed across the district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That traffic in skill was two centuries old by then, and the men who came over had been building the archipelago’s industries since the 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 65–67, 112–13, 121–24, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i>, juan 81, “Dongyi: Wo guo” (7th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B781. [Open access]","Akihiro Shimizu, “The Establishment and Development of the Paekche Daetongsa (Daitsuji) Style Roof Tile,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 81–95, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.81. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E5%AF%BA_%E9%8A%85%E9%80%A0%E9%87%88%E8%BF%A6%E5%A6%82%E6%9D%A5%E5%9D%90%E5%83%8F.JPG/1920px-%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E5%AF%BA_%E9%8A%85%E9%80%A0%E9%87%88%E8%BF%A6%E5%A6%82%E6%9D%A5%E5%9D%90%E5%83%8F.JPG","title":"The Asuka Great Buddha","desc":"The bronze Shakyamuni that has sat in the temple since 609, thirteen years after the building was finished, and the oldest Buddhist image in Japan.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E5%AF%BA_%E9%8A%85%E9%80%A0%E9%87%88%E8%BF%A6%E5%A6%82%E6%9D%A5%E5%9D%90%E5%83%8F.JPG","alt":"A large dark bronze seated Buddha with one hand raised, in a plain hall with lotus offerings and a lantern set before it."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-063","num":63,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Mononobe clan","answerText":"Mononobe clan","tags":["institution","history","japan","buddhism","warfare"],"question":"Opposition to the new religion is remembered as the position of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose head was shot out of an elm tree in 587 and whose house never recovered.","questions":["Because their name means the corporation of arms, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> supplied the court its weapons and its guards, which is what made their defeat so complete.","When the court asked in 552 whether the foreign image should be worshipped, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answered that the gods of the country would be angered by it."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">587 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Opposed from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">552 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Mononobe clan</b> was one of the two great service houses at the Yamato court, and its name says what it was: the corporation of arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a house held a hereditary name and a duty, and drew on dependent occupational groups whose members were bound to it by direct personal ties rather than by office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle makes the Mononobe the voice against Buddhism from the first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked in 552 whether the image sent from Baekje should be worshipped, the head of the Soga said that every western land did so and Japan should not stand alone, while the head of the Mononobe answered that the gods of the country would be angered if strange deities were revered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their objection is usually read as political as much as religious, since the rival house was the one profiting from the traffic with the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The quarrel was settled by force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 587 the Soga head drew the princes and ministers into a plot, and an army marched on the Mononobe house at Shibukawa, where its lord built a rice-fort, climbed into the fork of an elm and shot arrows down until a man in the attacking force brought him down and killed him with his children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both leaders on the winning side had vowed temples if they won, and both built them; half of the dead man’s slaves and his own house were given to one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Surviving kin changed their names and titles or disappeared, and the house was finished as a power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temples went up on the ground the quarrel had cleared, and the tiles for them came from Baekje moulds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 65–67, 109–15, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Akihiro Shimizu, “The Establishment and Development of the Paekche Daetongsa (Daitsuji) Style Roof Tile,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 81–95, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.81. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Shitenno-ji%2C_garan.jpg/1920px-Shitenno-ji%2C_garan.jpg","title":"Shitennō-ji","desc":"The temple in Osaka vowed by a young prince during the fighting of 587 and built from the defeated house's own lands; the present buildings are modern replacements on the ancient plan.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shitenno-ji%2C_garan.jpg","alt":"A red and white temple hall with a grey tiled roof, and a five-storey pagoda rising behind it against a clear sky."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-064","num":64,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Soga clan","answerText":"Soga clan","tags":["institution","history","japan","buddhism","politics"],"question":"By marrying daughters to one sovereign after another the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made itself grandfather to the throne, and for sixty years it ran the court.","questions":["A chief minister of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had a reigning sovereign murdered in 592 and put his own great-niece on the throne without any evident objection.","Eave tiles from the same moulds link the temples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the Asuka district, so its branches can be followed in fired clay."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dominant</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 530 – 645 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Overthrown</span><span class=\"dt-v\">645 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Soga clan</b> was the family that dominated the Yamato court from the middle of the 6th century until 645, and it did so by marriage rather than by office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Soga daughters became the consorts of successive sovereigns, so the head of the house was uncle or grandfather to whoever sat on the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also took the side of the new religion from the very first, and profited by it handsomely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a Soga minister who argued in 552 that the image from Baekje should be worshipped, a Soga head who vowed and built the first temple in 588, and Soga sons who were appointed to run it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their power was not gentle: the chronicle records flatly that the chief minister had the reigning sovereign murdered in 592, and that the ministers then asked his great-niece to take the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The archaeology follows the family across the valley by its roofs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eave tiles made in the same moulds as the first temple’s turn up at other temples in the Asuka valley, which places those foundations with the main house or with a collateral line of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The design itself came from Baekje, so the house that controlled the traffic with the peninsula also controlled the look of the new architecture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The workshops beside the first temple, turning out gold, glass, iron and the earliest Japanese coinage, sat in the middle of the ground the family had made its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What they never became was a royal house, and the arrangement they built was a family standing behind a throne rather than on it, which is why one killing in 645 could end it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 65–67, 112–24, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]","Akihiro Shimizu, “The Establishment and Development of the Paekche Daetongsa (Daitsuji) Style Roof Tile,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 81–95, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.81. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E5%AF%BA_%E8%BB%92%E4%B8%B8%E7%93%A6_%28%E8%8A%B1%E7%B5%84%29.JPG/1920px-%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E5%AF%BA_%E8%BB%92%E4%B8%B8%E7%93%A6_%28%E8%8A%B1%E7%B5%84%29.JPG","title":"An eave tile from Asukadera","desc":"A round tile end with a ten-petalled lotus, of the kind whose moulds link the family's temples to one another across the valley.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E5%AF%BA_%E8%BB%92%E4%B8%B8%E7%93%A6_%28%E8%8A%B1%E7%B5%84%29.JPG","alt":"A circular grey clay tile end moulded with a ring of ten broad lotus petals around a small studded boss."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-065","num":65,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Prince Shōtoku","answerText":"Prince Shōtoku","tags":["person","history","japan","buddhism","politics"],"question":"No figure in Japanese history has attracted more legend than <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who is credited with the first law code, the first embassies and the first great temple.","questions":["Born in a stable and able to hear ten petitions at once: the stories told about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began accumulating within a century of his death.","Appointed heir and given control of the government in 593, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> never reigned, and the chronicle records his death in the palace at Ikaruga."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Regent from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">593 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">621 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Prince Shōtoku</b> is the posthumous name of Umayado, a prince of the Yamato court who was made heir apparent in 593 and given control of the government under his aunt, and who died at Ikaruga in 621.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle written a century later already presents him as a sage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It says his mother was delivered of him without effort at the door of the stables, that he could speak at birth, that he could hear the suits of ten men at once and decide them all without error, and that he knew beforehand what was going to happen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also records real work: he studied Buddhist doctrine with a priest from Goguryeo and the Chinese classics with a scholar, he drew up laws in 604, and in 620 he compiled histories of the sovereigns and of the country with the Soga chief minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He appears first as a boy in the war of 587, carving images of the four Heavenly Kings and vowing a temple if his side won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Separating the man from the cult is the difficulty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court that produced the chronicle had reason to want a founder, and by the 20th century the Prince had become the subject of a whole literature of alternative histories, some of it written by academics, which sets itself against the standard scholarly account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is not in doubt is the ground: he moved to Ikaruga in 601 and lived there from 605, and the temple beside his palace became the oldest wooden building complex in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The embassies to China went out during his years in office, and the Chinese record of them describes a court of twelve ranks that matches what the chronicle says he introduced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Asuka temples his generation founded can still be sorted by their tiles, which is a plainer kind of evidence than anything written about him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 121–24, 148, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Micah Auerback, “Review of Orion Klautau, <i>Kakusareta Shōtoku Taishi: Kin-gendai Nihon no gishi to okaruto bunka</i>,” <i>Japanese Religions</i> 46, no. 2 (2025): 208–12, https://doi.org/10.69402/jrxisjp.46.2_208. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i>, juan 81, “Dongyi: Wo guo” (7th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B781. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Portrait_of_Prince_Sh%C5%8Dtoku_and_Two_Princes.jpg/1920px-Portrait_of_Prince_Sh%C5%8Dtoku_and_Two_Princes.jpg","title":"The Prince and two princes","desc":"The best-known image of him, painted a century or more after his death and long kept among the imperial treasures.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Prince_Sh%C5%8Dtoku_and_Two_Princes.jpg","alt":"An ink and colour painting on paper of a bearded man in pink robes holding a tablet, flanked by two smaller youths in yellow."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-066","num":66,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Seventeen-article constitution","answerText":"Seventeen-article constitution","tags":["text","history","japan","politics","buddhism"],"question":"Harmony is to be valued, reverence the three treasures, obey the imperial commands: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opens with maxims rather than with law.","questions":["Dated to 604 and quoting the Analects in its first line, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is addressed to ministers and officials rather than to the people.","A court of hereditary houses is being told to behave like a bureaucracy in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is what makes it a political document."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Promulgated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">604 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Seventeen-article constitution</b> is a set of maxims for ministers and officials that the chronicle says the heir apparent drew up in 604 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a constitution in any modern sense and contains no institutions, no procedure and no penalties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first article opens by quoting the Analects — harmony is to be valued, and wanton opposition avoided — the second orders sincere reverence for the Buddha, the Law and the Priesthood, and the third makes the lord Heaven and the vassal Earth, so that when the lord speaks the vassal listens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Later articles tell officials to behave with decorum, to judge suits without taking bribes, to chastise evil and encourage good, and to employ the people in forced labour only at seasons when they are not farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mixture is deliberate and is the point: Confucian political ethics, Buddhist devotion and a Chinese theory of sovereignty are being laid over a court that ran on hereditary houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That court is what the articles are arguing with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the older order a great house held a name and a duty and commanded people bound to it personally, and the articles insist instead on obedience upwards to a single ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The previous year twelve cap-ranks had been created for individuals, and the Chinese record of the embassies lists the same twelve grades of court rank, so the reform was visible from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether the text is as old as 604, and whether the Prince wrote it, has been argued about for a very long time, and he has since become the centre of a whole literature of alternative histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Read simply as what it says, it is the earliest surviving statement of how the Japanese state wanted to be governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 128–33, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i>, juan 81, “Dongyi: Wo guo” (7th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B781. [Open access]","Micah Auerback, “Review of Orion Klautau, <i>Kakusareta Shōtoku Taishi: Kin-gendai Nihon no gishi to okaruto bunka</i>,” <i>Japanese Religions</i> 46, no. 2 (2025): 208–12, https://doi.org/10.69402/jrxisjp.46.2_208. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a4/Lotus_Sutra_written_by_Prince_Sh%C5%8Dtoku.jpg","title":"The Hokke gisho","desc":"Not the articles, which survive only inside the chronicle, but the commentary on the Lotus Sutra traditionally held to be in the same hand — the other text whose authorship is argued over.","credit":"Photograph by Hachiro Onoue, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lotus_Sutra_written_by_Prince_Sh%C5%8Dtoku.jpg","alt":"The opening columns of a brush-written manuscript in Chinese characters on discoloured paper, with a note in smaller script at the top right."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-067","num":67,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Hōryū-ji","answerText":"Hōryū-ji","tags":["building","history","japan","buddhism","art"],"question":"The oldest wooden buildings anywhere in the world stand at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in a walled precinct beside the ruins of the palace of the prince who founded it.","questions":["A single line in the chronicle says that fire destroyed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 670 and that not one building was left, which contradicted everything visible on the site.","A dig in 1939 found a burnt compound on a different alignment beneath <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that settled an argument that had run since the 1880s."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 601 – 607 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Burned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">670 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Hōryū-ji</b> is a temple at Ikaruga in the Nara basin whose western precinct — a golden hall, a five-storey pagoda, a middle gate and a cloister — contains the oldest wooden buildings standing anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A prince of the court decided in 601 to move to Ikaruga, built a palace there and lived in it from 605, and the temple beside it is the ancestor of the present one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The problem is a single sentence in the chronicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the year 670 it records that after midnight a fire broke out at the temple and not a single building was left, which is hard to reconcile with buildings still standing that look older than that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Argument over whether the precinct was rebuilt ran from the 1880s onward, and it could not be settled by style alone, because so few 7th-century buildings survive that any dating had to reason in a circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Excavation ended it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dig in 1939 found the burnt remains of an earlier compound on a different alignment, with older tiles, which could not have stood alongside the present one, so it is that compound the fire destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The standing buildings are therefore of the later 7th and early 8th centuries, and their details — swelling columns, cloud-shaped bracket arms, saucer blocks, human-figure struts and fret balustrades — belong to a moment when several continental traditions were still in play.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing so old survives on the continent itself, where the oldest wooden hall in China dates from 782 and the oldest in Korea from the 12th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tiles, the craftsmen and the design had all come by way of Baekje, and the temples of the district can still be sorted by the moulds their eave tiles came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 293–94, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Akihiro Shimizu, “The Establishment and Development of the Paekche Daetongsa (Daitsuji) Style Roof Tile,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 81–95, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.81. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Horyu-ji_Kondo_and_Five-story_Pagoda_2022.jpg/1920px-Horyu-ji_Kondo_and_Five-story_Pagoda_2022.jpg","title":"The western precinct","desc":"The golden hall and the five-storey pagoda, with the swelling columns, cloud-shaped brackets and fret balustrades that set them apart from everything built afterwards.","credit":"Photograph by ONO Masanobu, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horyu-ji_Kondo_and_Five-story_Pagoda_2022.jpg","alt":"A two-storeyed timber hall with deep tiled eaves standing beside a five-storey wooden pagoda on a gravelled court."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-068","num":68,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"kenzuishi","answerText":"kenzuishi","tags":["event","history","japan","china","diplomacy"],"question":"A letter carried by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> addressed the Chinese emperor as one Son of Heaven to another, and he told his officials not to show him such things again.","questions":["Sent from 600 onwards, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were the first missions from Japan to China in over a century, and they went to learn as much as to be recognised.","The court sent monks as well as officials with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so the embassies were a way of importing doctrine as well as making a claim."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Embassies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">600 – 614 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>kenzuishi</b> were the embassies the Yamato court sent to the Sui dynasty in China between 600 and 614 CE, the first missions from the archipelago to a Chinese court in more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chinese record of them is unusually frank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It says the first envoys, in 600, explained that their king treated Heaven as elder brother and the sun as younger, holding court before dawn and leaving off business at sunrise, and that the emperor called this quite devoid of reason and ordered it changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 607 the tone had altered: the envoy said his king had heard that the Bodhisattva Son of Heaven in the west was reviving the Buddhist law, and brought several dozen monks to study it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The letter he carried opened by addressing the Son of Heaven in the land where the sun rises to the Son of Heaven in the land where the sun sets, which the emperor read with displeasure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He answered it all the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Chinese envoy was sent back in 608, met at the coast with drums and a guard of honour, and the Japanese chronicle records the same exchange from the other side, along with the men who went out and came back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the embassies were really for was the same thing the archipelago had been getting from the peninsula for two centuries, which is technique and knowledge rather than goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Students and monks stayed behind for years and returned with law, ritual and architecture, and the buildings that went up afterwards show several continental traditions arriving at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The missions stopped when the Sui fell, and were resumed to the Tang a generation later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i>, juan 81, “Dongyi: Wo guo” (7th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B781. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 136–41, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]","Song-Nai Rhee, C. Melvin Aikens, Sung-Rak Choi, and Hyuk-Jin Ro, “Korean Contributions to Agriculture, Technology, and State Formation in Japan: Archaeology and History of an Epochal Thousand Years, 400 B.C.–A.D. 600,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 46, no. 2 (2007): 404–59, https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/afe2b58f-0256-4971-b136-cb41a189affd/download. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Ono_no_Imoko.jpg/1920px-Ono_no_Imoko.jpg","title":"The envoy of 607","desc":"A much later painted portrait of the man who carried the letter to the Chinese court and came back with a Chinese envoy of his own.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ono_no_Imoko.jpg","alt":"A painted hanging-scroll portrait of a seated man in black court robes and a tall black cap, holding a pale tablet."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-069","num":69,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Suiko","answerText":"Suiko","tags":["ruler","history","japan","buddhism","politics"],"question":"After the chief minister had the reigning sovereign murdered, the ministers asked <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> three times before she agreed to take the throne.","questions":["Everything the Asuka period is famous for — the temples, the cap-ranks, the embassies to China — happened in the 35 years <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reigned.","The Chinese record of the embassies describes a king with a wife and a crown prince, which is hard to square with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> being on the throne at the time."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">593 – 628 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died aged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">75</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Suiko</b> is the posthumous name of Toyo-mike Kashikiya-hime, who reigned from 593 to 628 CE and is the first woman the chronicles count as a sovereign of Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She came to the throne out of a crisis that her own family had caused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She was a daughter of one sovereign and consort of another, and when the chief minister of the Soga, her uncle, had the reigning sovereign murdered in 592, the ministers pressed her three times before she consented and was given the imperial seal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Her nephew was made heir the following spring and given control of the government, so authority was shared between a sovereign, an heir and a chief minister who was uncle to both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything the Asuka period is remembered for falls inside her 35 years on the throne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The first temple was finished in 596, twelve cap-ranks were created in 603 and granted the next year, the seventeen articles were drawn up in 604, and the embassies to China went out from 600.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She died in 628 at the age of 75, having reigned longer than anyone before her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One thing does not fit: the Chinese account of the embassies of her reign describes a male king with a wife and a crown prince, and never mentions a queen at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That may be diplomacy, or mistranslation, or a court presenting itself in the terms a Chinese chancery would accept, and the question is not resolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is solid is on the ground — the temple her uncle built, the tiles that link it to its neighbours, and the palace at Ikaruga whose temple still stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 121–22, 127–29, 154, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i>, juan 81, “Dongyi: Wo guo” (7th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B781. [Open access]","Akihiro Shimizu, “The Establishment and Development of the Paekche Daetongsa (Daitsuji) Style Roof Tile,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 17 (2004): 81–95, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.81. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Empress_Suiko_2.jpg","title":"An imagined portrait","desc":"Painted more than a thousand years after her reign, so it records how she was pictured rather than how she looked.","credit":"Painting by Tosa Mitsuyoshi, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empress_Suiko_2.jpg","alt":"A painted portrait of a woman in blue and green court robes with a gold crown, seated behind a striped curtain and holding a fan."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-070","num":70,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Taika Reform","answerText":"Taika Reform","tags":["event","history","japan","politics","law"],"question":"Private estates abolished, governors appointed, a census taken and a uniform tax imposed: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the programme set out in an edict of 646.","questions":["What the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proposed to abolish was the whole basis of the old order, in which great houses owned people rather than holding office.","Historians argue about how much of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was actually carried out in 646 and how much was read back into it by the chronicle a century later."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Edict</span><span class=\"dt-v\">646 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Era name</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Taika, from 645 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Taika Reform</b> is the name given to the programme of government set out in an edict promulgated on the first day of 646 CE, at the beginning of the first Japanese era to be given a name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first article abolishes the private holdings of the great houses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The serfs owned by the court families, the granaries scattered through the provinces and the farmsteads held by the chieftains were all to be given up, and sustenance fiefs granted instead on a descending scale by rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second orders that the capital be regulated for the first time and governors appointed for the home provinces, with barriers, guards, post-horses and bell-tokens for those travelling on official business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The third calls for registers of population and land, and the fourth abolishes the old taxes and forced labour and replaces them with a uniform levy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The target is the arrangement the archipelago had run on for two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In that order a great house held a hereditary name and a duty and commanded people bound to it by direct personal ties, and the edict proposes to make every one of them a subject of the throne instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> How much of this happened in 646 is disputed, since the chronicle was compiled seventy years later by the state the reform was supposed to have created, and some of its language belongs to the later codes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The direction is not in doubt: within fifty years a palace was raised at Fujiwara on a full grid, the first to be roofed in tile throughout, needing ten times the tiles of a temple and kilns in several provinces to supply them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The workshops of the Asuka valley were minting coin and writing on wooden tablets by then, which is what a bureaucracy looks like on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 205–7, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Construction of the Fujiwara Palace and the Source of its Roof Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 111–27, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_111. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]","Nakakubo Tatsuo, “Interregional Interaction Strategies in the Early State Formation of Ancient Japan,” <i>Japanese Journal of Archaeology</i> 8 (2020): 79–124, https://www.jjarchaeology.jp/contents/pdf/vol008/8-1_079.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/210401_One-thousandth_scale_model_of_Fujiwara-kyu.jpg/1920px-210401_One-thousandth_scale_model_of_Fujiwara-kyu.jpg","title":"Fujiwara-kyō in model","desc":"The palace and its streets at one thousandth scale: the first Japanese capital laid out on a full grid, raised within fifty years of the edict.","credit":"Model photographed by 名古屋太郎, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:210401_One-thousandth_scale_model_of_Fujiwara-kyu.jpg","alt":"A large architectural model of an ancient walled palace compound at the centre of a rectangular grid of streets and houses."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-071","num":71,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Isshi incident","answerText":"Isshi incident","tags":["event","history","japan","politics","asuka period"],"question":"Envoys from Korea were presenting tribute in the palace when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began, and the chief minister’s son was cut down at the foot of the throne.","questions":["What the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed was the Soga house, whose head burned the court histories the next day rather than surrender them.","Within days of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a sovereign had abdicated, a new one had taken the throne, and the year had been given the first Japanese era name."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Coup</span><span class=\"dt-v\">645 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Era name</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Taika, from 645 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Isshi incident</b> is the assassination of Soga no Iruka in the palace in 645 CE, and the destruction of the family that had run the court for a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was staged inside a ceremony.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Envoys from the three Korean kingdoms were presenting tribute and their memorials were being read out; the minister doing the reading sweated and stammered, the two swordsmen posted to strike hung back in fear, and the prince who had planned it went in himself and cut Iruka on the head and shoulder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iruka rolled to where the sovereign sat and asked what his crime was; she rose and went inside, the assassins finished him, and rain fell until the court stood in puddles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His father was besieged the next day and, before the end, burned the history of the sovereigns and the history of the country that his house had compiled, one of which a scribe snatched out of the fire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What followed was a change of régime rather than of dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sovereign abdicated, her brother took the throne, the prince who had struck the blow was made heir apparent, his fellow plotter was made inner minister, and the year was renamed Taika, the first Japanese era name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order the coup was aimed at was one of hereditary houses that held a name and a duty and commanded their people by direct personal tie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The new court moved to the coast at Naniwa, and the phasing of the palaces it left behind — which building belongs to which reign — is still argued from potsherds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the Asuka valley the government offices of the old palace lay beside a great terraced ritual site, and the temples the Soga had founded went on standing, their tiles still tying the family’s foundations to one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 191–98, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Takashi Sato, “Reexamination of the Construction Process of the Former Naniwa Palace: Focusing on Comparison with the Asuka Palace Site,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 20 (2022): 21–40, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.20.0_21. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Aihara, “The Results and Significance of Excavations at the Sakafuneishi Site,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 18 (2004): 171–80, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.18_171. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/32/Irukaansatsuzu.jpg","title":"The killing at court","desc":"The scene painted on the Tōnomine scroll in the Edo period: the head struck off at the foot of the throne, with the sovereign seated behind her screen at the top left.","alt":"A painted scroll scene of courtiers in black robes with drawn swords over a fallen man in a green-walled hall, a severed head on the floor and a seated figure behind a screen above.","credit":"Painting by Sumiyoshi Gukei, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-072","num":72,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Tenji","answerText":"Tenji","tags":["ruler","history","japan","politics","asuka period"],"question":"For more than twenty years <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran the government as heir apparent without taking the throne, and reigned as sovereign for only three.","questions":["Fearing invasion from the mainland, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved the capital out of the Asuka valley to the shore of Lake Biwa and repaired the hill forts.","A general household register was drawn up in 670 under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and every later allotment of land in the country rested on registers of that kind."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">668 – 671 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Heir from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">645 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Tenji</b> is the posthumous name of Naka no Ōe, the prince who struck down Soga no Iruka in 645 CE and reigned as sovereign from 668 until his death in 671.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He governed for far longer than he reigned, and the distinction matters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was made heir apparent on the day his uncle took the throne, remained heir under his mother when she reigned a second time, and did not assume the dignity himself for more than twenty years, which is one of the oddities of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His years were anxious ones: the court left the Asuka valley in 667 for Ōtsu on Lake Biwa, hill forts were repaired, and stores of grain and salt were laid in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle also has him refusing a stone sarcophagus for a burial, saying that he had compassion on the people and hoped the refusal would serve as a lesson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His lasting act was a list.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 670 registers of population were prepared, and the household register is one of the two documents — the other being a survey of the fields — on which the whole later system of allotting land depends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That system ran from the Council of State down through provincial governors, and the code that set it out gives the centre more of the work than its Chinese model does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind the politics the old ritual continued: a great terraced stone complex above the Asuka palace was in use from his mother’s reign through to his niece’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even his title is later than he is, since the change from great king to heavenly sovereign belongs to the generation after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 191–93, 285–93, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Aihara, “The Results and Significance of Excavations at the Sakafuneishi Site,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 18 (2004): 171–80, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.18_171. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]","Takashi Sato, “Reexamination of the Construction Process of the Former Naniwa Palace: Focusing on Comparison with the Asuka Palace Site,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 20 (2022): 21–40, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.20.0_21. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Emperor_Tenji_%28Kanou_Tanyuu%29.jpg","title":"An imagined portrait","desc":"Painted by a 17th-century artist about a thousand years after the reign, so it records how he was pictured rather than how he looked.","alt":"An ink and colour painting of a seated man in orange court robes patterned with dragons, wearing a black cap, on a lacquer dais.","credit":"Painting by Kanō Tan'yū, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-073","num":73,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Jinshin War","answerText":"Jinshin War","tags":["event","history","japan","warfare","asuka period"],"question":"Offered the succession on his brother’s sickbed, a prince refused it and withdrew to the mountains, and the next summer the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began with his march on the capital.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was decided at a bridge, where the defenders cut a gap in the span and laid a loose plank over it.","Ancient Japan’s largest civil war, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, put the loser’s uncle on the throne and settled the shape of the state for a century."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">672 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Jinshin War</b> is the succession war fought in 672 CE between Prince Ōama, brother of the dead sovereign, and Prince Ōtomo, that sovereign’s son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The quarrel was set up on a sickbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Summoned in 671 and offered the throne, Ōama said his health had always been bad, asked that the realm be given to the empress and the succession to Ōtomo, took Buddhist orders that same day, deposited his private weapons and withdrew to Yoshino, at which someone remarked that a tiger had been given wings and let go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He assembled his household there and twice told them to leave if they wanted office; half of them stayed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next summer he raised the eastern provinces and marched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It was decided at a bridge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Seta on Lake Biwa the defending army stood with its banners covering the plain and its crossbows discharging arrows like rain, having cut a gap in the bridge and laid a loose plank over it, until one soldier in double armour ran across and cut the rope that held it, after which the line broke and could not be re-formed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ōtomo fled and strangled himself at Yamazaki the following day, and the ministers of the left and right scattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The victor reigned from Asuka rather than from the lakeside capital, and the phasing of those palaces is still argued over from the pottery found in them; above them the great terraced ritual site went on in use across the war and after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His reign then remade the court from the top down — eight new hereditary titles in place of the old crowd of them, commissioners sent out to fix provincial boundaries — and it is in this reign that the change from great king to heavenly sovereign is usually placed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 301–15, 365, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Takashi Sato, “Reexamination of the Construction Process of the Former Naniwa Palace: Focusing on Comparison with the Asuka Palace Site,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 20 (2022): 21–40, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.20.0_21. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Aihara, “The Results and Significance of Excavations at the Sakafuneishi Site,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 18 (2004): 171–80, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.18_171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Seta-no-Karahashi%2C_zenkei-1.jpg/1920px-Seta-no-Karahashi%2C_zenkei-1.jpg","title":"The crossing at Seta","desc":"Where the Seta river leaves Lake Biwa, and where the war was decided; the bridge standing today is a modern one on the ancient crossing.","alt":"A low arched road bridge with yellow railings across a wide calm river, with a large inscribed stone marker in the foreground.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-074","num":74,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Tenmu","answerText":"Tenmu","tags":["ruler","history","japan","politics","asuka period"],"question":"Having won a civil war for the throne, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled for thirteen years without ever appointing a chief minister to stand between himself and the court.","questions":["In 684 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced the whole crowd of old hereditary titles with eight new ones and granted the first of them to thirteen houses in a day.","Commissioners were sent out to fix the boundaries of the provinces under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is what a state does when it means to tax them."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">673 – 686 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eight titles</span><span class=\"dt-v\">684 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Tenmu</b> is the posthumous name of Prince Ōama, who won the civil war of 672 CE and reigned from 673 until 686, and whose reign gave the Japanese state most of the shape it kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He governed alone, without the chief minister who had stood beside every sovereign since the coup of 645 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No chief minister was appointed in his reign, and the chronicle shows him giving orders directly about who should enter service, how they should be tried in the palace guard and how they should then be allotted to office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 684 he abolished the multitude of hereditary titles and instituted eight in their place, from Mabito at the top down to Inaki, granting the first of them to thirteen houses on the day it was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The next day commissioners went out to determine the boundaries of the provinces, which is what a government does when it means to tax and conscript them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Both acts cut across the older order at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In that order a house held its name and its dependants as its own, and rank followed descent; a graded table of titles conferred by the throne puts the ruler at the source of standing instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ritual was rebuilt alongside it, and the great terraced stone complex above the Asuka palace stayed in use through his reign and his widow’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same instinct reached even to carpentry: within a generation the state ran a building bureau that controlled the craftsmen, and temple architecture became noticeably uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is his reign, above all, in which the change from great king to heavenly sovereign is placed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 301–2, 365, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Aihara, “The Results and Significance of Excavations at the Sakafuneishi Site,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 18 (2004): 171–80, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.18_171. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/NoguchiObo_TenmuJito-ryo_201909a.jpg/1920px-NoguchiObo_TenmuJito-ryo_201909a.jpg","title":"The tomb at Noguchi","desc":"The wooded mound in the Asuka valley held to be his burial place and his widow's, fenced and gated as an imperial tomb.","alt":"A wooded mound behind a stone terrace, with a small gate, wooden railing and an information board on the gravel court in front of it.","credit":"Photograph by 8-hachiro, CC BY-SA 4.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-075","num":75,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"ritsuryō","answerText":"ritsuryō","tags":["concept","history","japan","law","politics"],"question":"Penal law and administrative law together make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Chinese-modelled body of code on which the 8th-century Japanese state was built.","questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> registered and taxed every subject directly, it dissolved the older arrangement in which great houses owned people outright.","Land allotment under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran from the Council of State down through provincial governors, with the centre keeping more control than in the Chinese original."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Codified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">701 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In force</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th – 10th centuries CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Ritsuryō</b> is the name for the body of law on which the Japanese state was built from the late 7th century: <i>ritsu</i>, the penal code, and <i>ryō</i>, the administrative code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a borrowing, and like every borrowing of this size it is also a translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The codes were modelled on those of Tang China, and where the Japanese versions depart from their originals the departures are exactly what arguments about the character of the state now turn on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reading is indirect as well, because the text does not survive whole and has to be recovered from later commentaries on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it replaced was personal rather than institutional, and the difference is the whole point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Under the older order a great house held a hereditary name and duty and commanded people bound to it by direct personal ties; the codes made each of those people a registered, taxable subject of the throne instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The state it built works through offices, registers and paper rather than through persons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Allotment of land was controlled at the top by the Council of State and managed locally by provincial governors, and the procedures were refined step by step through the first half of the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The programme had been announced long before it was written down, in an edict of 646 that already orders registers of population and land, governors for the provinces, barriers and post-horses, and a uniform levy in place of the old dues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its reach went well beyond law: within a generation the state ran its own building bureau, controlled the carpenters through it, and temple architecture across the country became markedly uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 205–7, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Ancient_Provinces_of_Japan_Ryoseikoku_Map.png/1920px-Ancient_Provinces_of_Japan_Ryoseikoku_Map.png","title":"The provinces of the codes","desc":"The provinces into which the country was divided under this body of law, which remained the framework of Japanese local administration until 1868.","alt":"A numbered map of the Japanese islands divided into many small provinces, with keyed lists of their names in English and Japanese.","credit":"Map by Artanisen, CC BY-SA 4.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-076","num":76,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Taihō Code","answerText":"Taihō Code","tags":["text","history","japan","law","politics"],"question":"Promulgated in 701, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first complete Japanese law code known to have been put into force across the whole country.","questions":["No manuscript of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives entire, so its clauses are recovered from later commentaries and from the code that replaced it.","Arguments about how the early Japanese state saw its own frontiers turn on a handful of clauses in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and on how they differ from their Chinese models."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Promulgated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">701 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Taihō Code</b>, promulgated in 701 CE, is the first complete Japanese ritsuryō code known to have been enforced throughout the country, and the foundation of the 8th-century state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is known indirectly, and always at second hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No manuscript of it survives whole, so its clauses are reconstructed from the later commentaries that quote them and from the revised code that replaced it in the 8th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That matters for more than philology, because the questions asked of the code are comparative: its provisions were modelled on Tang originals, and the differences are what any argument about the character of the Japanese state has to rest on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One long-standing reading holds that the code drew a firm legal line between the kingdoms of Korea and the unassimilated peoples of the archipelago, and a critical rereading of the commentaries and of the Tang texts finds no basis in the code for that distinction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the code certainly does contain is a scheme of administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country is divided into provinces, provinces into districts and districts into villages, and land is allotted through that hierarchy from the Council of State down to the provincial governors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Below the district the units are hard to see in documents and easier in the ground, where an excavated settlement in eastern Japan can be measured against what the code says a village was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ruler in whose name it all runs is called <i>tennō</i> in it, a Chinese term adopted for a Japanese sovereign within the previous century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its programme is the programme of the reform edict of 646, half a century older and finally written as law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Koichi Aoki, “An Ancient Go and ‘Village’: An Evaluation of the Hachigaya Site, Yamabe-gun, Kazusa Province,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 179–98, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_179. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 205–7, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/%E8%97%A4%E5%8E%9F%E5%AE%AE%E8%B7%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E6%9C%A8%E7%B0%A1%E3%80%8C%E4%B8%8A%E6%8C%9F%E5%9B%BD%E9%98%BF%E6%B3%A2%E8%A9%95%E6%9D%BE%E9%87%8C%E3%80%8D_%28%E8%A4%87%E8%A3%BD%29.JPG","title":"Province, district, village","desc":"A replica of a wooden tag excavated at the Fujiwara palace, labelled with a province, a district and a village — the three units the code prescribes.","alt":"A long narrow wooden tag, notched at the top, with a column of brush-written Chinese characters running down it.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-077","num":77,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"kokugunri system","answerText":"kokugunri system","tags":["institution","history","japan","politics","law"],"question":"Provinces divided into districts and districts into villages: the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is how the ritsuryō state reached down to the fields it meant to tax.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a governor sent out from the capital stood over district officials drawn from the old local families, which is where the arrangement chafed.","Archaeologists can test the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the ground, because an excavated cluster of pillared buildings can be matched to a village named in the documents."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Boundaries fixed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">684 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>kokugunri system</b> is the three-tier local administration of the ritsuryō state: the province, the district within it, and the village within that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was laid out deliberately rather than inherited from anything older, and the record shows when.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Commissioners were sent out in 684 to determine the boundaries of the provinces, and a graded set of hereditary titles conferred by the throne had been instituted the day before, so the map and the pecking order were drawn in the same week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two halves work together: office and income followed court rank, and rank was granted rather than inherited, which is what distinguishes this from the older order of houses commanding their own people by personal tie.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The province was run by a governor sent out from the capital; the district was run by officials drawn from the local families who had held the country before the code did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Land allotment is the clearest thing the arrangement actually did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The procedure was controlled at the top by the Council of State and managed on the ground by those provincial governors, and the Japanese code gives the centre more of that work than its Chinese model does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Below the level of the district the documents thin out and the spade takes over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At one site in Kazusa province in eastern Japan the plans of the pillared buildings have been analysed and compared with the written record, and the settlement is thought to show what a village of this system actually was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The code that prescribed the whole arrangement survives only through later commentaries, so what is being tested against the ground is itself a reconstruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 365, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Koichi Aoki, “An Ancient Go and ‘Village’: An Evaluation of the Hachigaya Site, Yamabe-gun, Kazusa Province,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 179–98, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_179. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Shida-gunga-ato_building-A.JPG/1920px-Shida-gunga-ato_building-A.JPG","title":"A district office","desc":"A modern frame standing over the excavated postholes of a pillared building at the Shida district office in Suruga, one of the local seats of this administration.","alt":"A skeletal cream-coloured frame of posts and beams standing on a paved outline in a gravelled field, marking the plan of a vanished building.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-078","num":78,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"handen","answerText":"handen","tags":["practice","history","japan","law","agriculture"],"question":"Rice land was granted to every registered person and taken back at death under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why the state cared so much about counting people.","questions":["Two documents make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible — a household register and a survey of the fields — and neither had existed before the 7th century.","The reform edict of 646 already announced the principle behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the procedure was not fully worked out for another hundred years."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Principle announced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">646 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First register</span><span class=\"dt-v\">670 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Handen</b> is the system by which the ritsuryō state granted rice land to registered people and took it back when they died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rests on two documents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One is a household register and the other a survey of the fields, and the code’s procedure joins them: the Council of State controlled the allotment at the top and provincial governors managed it locally, with the Japanese version of the code putting more weight on central direction than its Tang model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The machinery was not built in a day, but refined in a series of steps through the first half of the 8th century and probably completed only about the middle of it, when two accounting ledgers were prescribed, one recording the surveys and one the grants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The principle is older than the code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The reform edict of 646 already abolishes the private holdings of the great houses, orders registers of population and of land, and replaces the old dues with a uniform levy; a general household register was actually drawn up in 670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the ground the unit was the village, and one excavated settlement in eastern Japan is read as showing what such a village was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of it works without writing and counting, and both are visible in the Asuka valley before the code was written, at a workshop that has yielded thousands of inscribed wooden tablets along with the earliest Japanese coin and the mould it was struck from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The code itself has to be reconstructed from later commentaries, so the rules being described are known at one remove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What can be said plainly is that a state which allots land must first know who its people are, and that is the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 205–7, 292–93, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Koichi Aoki, “An Ancient Go and ‘Village’: An Evaluation of the Hachigaya Site, Yamabe-gun, Kazusa Province,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 179–98, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_179. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Wooden_tablets_%28copy%29_in_7-8th_c.jpg/1920px-Wooden_tablets_%28copy%29_in_7-8th_c.jpg","title":"Tags from the tax system","desc":"Replicas of wooden labels excavated at Asuka, Fujiwara and Nara, naming a district, a village, a household and a quantity delivered.","alt":"Eight narrow wooden strips inscribed with columns of characters, mounted in a museum case with printed transcription cards beside them.","credit":"Photograph by さぱしあ, CC BY-SA 3.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-079","num":79,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Fujiwara-kyō","answerText":"Fujiwara-kyō","tags":["place","history","japan","politics","architecture"],"question":"The court stopped moving from reign to reign when it settled at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 694, the first Japanese capital laid out on a full grid.","questions":["Tiling the palace and its walls at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took ten times the roof tiles of a temple, from kilns in several provinces at once.","Only sixteen years after the court moved in, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was abandoned for Nara, so the site has never been built over."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">694 – 710 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Fujiwara-kyō</b> was the capital of Japan from 694 to 710 CE, and the first city in the islands to be laid out on a full grid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Until then the court had moved from reign to reign, and had done so for centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A palace was built for a reign and abandoned at its end, which is why the Asuka valley holds several of them rather than one capital, and the chronicle records the sovereign inspecting the site and then removing her residence to the new palace in the twelfth month of 694.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A grid changes the terms: a capital laid out in streets is a permanent thing, and the court never went back to moving with each reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The building of it can be measured in fired clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> It was the first imperial palace whose buildings and surrounding walls were roofed in tile throughout, which meant producing roughly ten times as many tiles as an ancient temple needed, and in a limited time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tiles divide into fifteen groups by fabric and technique, from kilns inside the Yamato basin and from others in Ōmi, Sanuki, Awaji and Izumi, with the outside kilns using a different moulding method and standing where water transport could reach the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is an administrative achievement rather than an architectural one, and it is what a state with provinces and governors is for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The workshops of the old valley had already been minting coin and writing on wooden tablets, so the skills were to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city lasted sixteen years before the court moved again, to Nara, which is why so much of its plan can still be dug.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 414–17, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Construction of the Fujiwara Palace and the Source of its Roof Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 111–27, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_111. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Aihara, “The Results and Significance of Excavations at the Sakafuneishi Site,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 18 (2004): 171–80, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.18_171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/%E8%97%A4%E5%8E%9F%E5%AE%AE%E5%A4%A7%E6%A5%B5%E6%AE%BF%E8%B7%A1.jpg/1920px-%E8%97%A4%E5%8E%9F%E5%AE%AE%E5%A4%A7%E6%A5%B5%E6%AE%BF%E8%B7%A1.jpg","title":"Where the great hall stood","desc":"The site of the audience hall, its pillar positions marked in the open field that the plain has stayed since the court left for Nara in 710.","alt":"Rows of squat red cylindrical markers on white bases standing in a mown grass field under a clear blue sky, with trees along the horizon.","credit":"Photograph by Indiana jo, CC BY-SA 4.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-080","num":80,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"tennō","answerText":"tennō","tags":["title","history","japan","politics","china"],"question":"Before the 7th century the ruler of Japan was styled great king, and the title <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced it as the court remade itself on Chinese lines.","questions":["Written with characters meaning heavenly sovereign, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a term borrowed from Chinese usage and given to a Japanese ruler.","The gold-inlaid sword found in a mound in eastern Japan calls its 5th-century ruler great king, not <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is how the change can be dated at all."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 600 – 700 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the codes</span><span class=\"dt-v\">701 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Tennō</b> is the title of the Japanese sovereign, written with characters meaning heavenly sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has not always been the title, and the moment it became one can be dated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What a ruler is called says a good deal about the state he rules, and the ruler of Japan in the 5th and 6th centuries was called great king, a fact established by the gold-inlaid inscription on an iron sword from a mound in eastern Japan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The change from great king to heavenly sovereign came after the 7th century and in the course of dealings with China, and the term itself is Chinese, so the questions are which Chinese usage it was taken from and what was meant by taking it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 8th-century state was built on a Chinese constitutional model, and its monarch took this special title within it, so the title and the state arrived together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The outside view is a useful check on a chronicle written for the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Chinese record of the embassies of 600 gives the ruler’s style in transcribed Japanese, as Ōkimi, and describes a king with a consort and a crown prince without any hint of a heavenly title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the time the codes were promulgated in 701 the sovereign in them is <i>tennō</i>, and the whole apparatus of provinces, ranks and registers runs in that name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chronicle compiled in 720 then writes the title back into the earliest reigns, so reading it there is reading a later usage into an earlier world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word has outlasted everything else in this story, and it is still the title of the Japanese head of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]","Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i>, juan 81, “Dongyi: Wo guo” (7th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B781. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 109–15, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E6%B1%A0%E9%81%BA%E8%B7%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E5%A4%A9%E7%9A%87%E6%9C%A8%E7%B0%A1_%28%E8%A4%87%E8%A3%BD%29.JPG/1920px-%E9%A3%9B%E9%B3%A5%E6%B1%A0%E9%81%BA%E8%B7%A1%E5%87%BA%E5%9C%9F_%E5%A4%A9%E7%9A%87%E6%9C%A8%E7%B0%A1_%28%E8%A4%87%E8%A3%BD%29.JPG","title":"The word on a wooden tag","desc":"A replica of a tablet from the Asukaike workshop in the Asuka valley whose first two characters are the pair read as this title, with a transcription card beside it.","alt":"A short wooden strip inscribed with a column of brush-written characters, displayed on pale cloth beside a printed white transcription card.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-081","num":81,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Hakusukinoe","answerText":"Hakusukinoe","tags":["event","history","japan","warfare","korea"],"question":"A Tang fleet of 170 ships drawn up in a Korean river estuary destroyed the Japanese expedition at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> over two days of fighting in 663.","questions":["After the defeat at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the court expected an invasion, and stone-walled forts went up along the sea road from Kyushu towards the capital.","Japan lost its foothold on the Korean peninsula for good at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, having sent an army to restore a kingdom that had already fallen."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">663 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Hakusukinoe</b> is the Japanese name for the battle fought in 663 CE at the mouth of a Korean river, where a Japanese fleet sent to restore the kingdom of Paekche was destroyed by the navy of Tang China.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The expedition was large and the alliance was not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some 27,000 men had been sent against Silla the year before, and in the months before the battle the Paekche king had his own best general bound, condemned and executed on a suspicion of treason, which the chronicle reports without approval.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the seventeenth day of the eighth month the Tang generals drew up 170 fighting ships in line of battle in the river; the Japanese ships that arrived first engaged them, had the worst of it and withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ten days later the Japanese commanders agreed among themselves that if they pressed forward the enemy would give way of their own accord, attacked again, were closed upon from both sides, and lost their army in the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What followed at home was a defensive programme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Forts of a Korean type — stone walls carried round a hilltop and its slopes, with gates built up over the springs — went up in northern Kyushu and the western provinces in this century, and where they stand can be read against where the later provincial capitals were placed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court moved its own seat as well, and the phasing of the palaces it built and abandoned on the coast at Naniwa is still argued from their pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The defeat also closed a long association: for two centuries the Yamato court had drawn craftsmen, scribes and prestige from the peninsular kingdoms, and had commanded much of its own population through hereditary houses rather than through offices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the law codes were written two generations later they set out a settled view of who stood outside the realm, which is not the view of a court with allies across the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 278–85, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Susumu Takashige, “Ancient Korean Style Hilltop-Fort and Kohufu,” <i>Geographical Sciences</i> 22 (1975): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.20630/chirikagaku.22.0_1. [Open access]","Takashi Sato, “Reexamination of the Construction Process of the Former Naniwa Palace: Focusing on Comparison with the Asuka Palace Site,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 20 (2022): 21–40, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.20.0_21. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/%E9%AC%BC%E3%83%8E%E5%9F%8E%E8%A5%BF%E9%96%80.jpg/1920px-%E9%AC%BC%E3%83%8E%E5%9F%8E%E8%A5%BF%E9%96%80.jpg","title":"A fort of the Korean kind","desc":"The west gate of Kinojō in Okayama, rebuilt on its excavated foundations: one of the hilltop forts with stone walling raised in the western provinces after the defeat.","alt":"A reconstructed timber gatehouse with a tiled roof and an open upper storey, standing on stone walling at the edge of a hilltop above a wide plain.","credit":"Photograph by 小池 隆, CC BY 3.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-082","num":82,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Jitō","answerText":"Jito","tags":["ruler","person","history","japan","politics"],"question":"Widow of one sovereign and daughter of another, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> governed for four years before taking the throne herself and completed the first gridded capital.","questions":["The 22 volumes of law distributed to the provincial governors in 689 were sent out in the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, three years after her husband’s death.","In 694 the court of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved into the palace at Fujiwara, ending the practice of building a new residence for each reign."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">690 – 697 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fujiwara palace</span><span class=\"dt-v\">694 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jitō</b> is the posthumous name of the Japanese sovereign who reigned from 690 to 697 CE, having been consort to one sovereign and daughter of another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She had been in the war that made the reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle has her following her husband to Yoshino in 671 and into the eastern provinces the next summer, addressing the troops and mingling with the throng while a plan was laid to hold the defensible positions, and it says that from then on her advice on government was constantly of the greatest assistance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When he died in 686 she presided over the court and exercised control without taking the dignity, and within a month a prince accused of treason had been arrested and put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She ruled in that position for four years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Two acts of those years outlast her.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 689 a book of legal enactments in 22 volumes was distributed to all the local governors, and the following year she ordered the registers of population to be made up, which is the pair of documents any allotment of land depends on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> She took the throne on the first day of 690, when the sword, the mirror and the seal were delivered to her and the assembled officials made obeisance and clapped their hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the twelfth month of 694 she removed her residence to the new palace at Fujiwara, the first Japanese capital laid out on a full grid and the first roofed in tile throughout, which took roughly ten times the tiles of a temple and drew on kilns in four provinces beyond the Yamato basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The great terraced ritual site above the old Asuka palace stayed in use through her reign, and the title she is known by is a later usage than her own century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 393–95, 414–17, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Construction of the Fujiwara Palace and the Source of its Roof Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 111–27, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_111. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Aihara, “The Results and Significance of Excavations at the Sakafuneishi Site,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 18 (2004): 171–80, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.18_171. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Empress_Jitou_%28Kanou_Tanyuu%29.jpg/1920px-Empress_Jitou_%28Kanou_Tanyuu%29.jpg","title":"An imagined portrait","desc":"Painted a thousand years after the reign by the same 17th-century artist who imagined her father, so it records how she was pictured rather than how she looked.","alt":"A painted scroll of a court lady seated on a green dais in layered pink and white robes, holding a folding fan before her face.","credit":"Painting by Kanō Tan'yū, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-083","num":83,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"sōjō","answerText":"sojo","tags":["title","institution","history","japan","buddhism"],"question":"A priest who struck his grandfather with an axe in 624 led the court to appoint a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to superintend all the monks and nuns of the country.","questions":["The office of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was given first to a Paekche monk, whose plea had just saved the rest of the clergy from punishment.","Once a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stood over them, the temples were inspected and counted: 46 of them, with 816 monks and 569 nuns."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">624 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Temples counted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">46, in 624 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>sōjō</b> is the senior of the two clerical offices created in Japan in 624 CE to put the Buddhist clergy under the supervision of the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was made in answer to a crime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A priest had taken an axe to his own grandfather, and the sovereign, hearing of it, ordered all the monks and nuns of the temples assembled and examined, with severe punishment for any convicted of an offence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Paekche monk then submitted a memorial arguing that the religion had reached the islands barely a century before, that the clergy had not yet learned its rules, and that all but the offender should be pardoned; the plea was granted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Thirteen days later the decree ran that if the priests themselves offend, the laymen cannot be admonished, and appointed a sōjō and a sōzu over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What follows in the record is a count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the ninth month of the same year an inspection recorded the circumstances of every temple’s building and of every ordination with its year, month and day, and found 46 temples, 816 monks and 569 nuns, 1,385 persons in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the shape the state would keep with religion: a faith patronised by the great houses, and then registered, ranked and audited like anything else it paid for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The temples themselves show the same hand from the other side, since the moulds of their eave tiles tie one foundation to another across the Asuka valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The valley’s workshops were state establishments too, one of them producing metal, glass and the earliest Japanese coin alongside thousands of inscribed wooden tablets, and the temple carpentry of the period is uniform enough to argue for a single transmitted technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 152–54, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Toshiaki Matsuki, “So-mei and the Bemin System: The Structure of Personal Rule in Yamato Government,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 111, no. 3 (2002): 326–50, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.111.3_326. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Gangoji%2C_hondou-3.jpg/1920px-Gangoji%2C_hondou-3.jpg","title":"The main hall of Gangō-ji","desc":"The main hall of Gangō-ji at Nara, the temple that succeeded the first great Asuka foundation; the clergy of houses like it were what the new offices were made to supervise.","alt":"A low wooden temple hall with a broad grey tiled roof and dark timber columns, standing behind flowering shrubs on a gravelled court.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-084","num":84,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Kuratsukuri no Tori","answerText":"Kuratsukuri no Tori","tags":["person","object","history","japan","art"],"question":"Ordered in 605 to cast a Buddha sixteen feet high, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was rewarded with court rank and rice land for getting the finished image through the door.","questions":["The grandfather of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had given the court its first relics, and his aunt had been the first woman in the islands to take Buddhist orders.","Guilds of painters were established in the same years in which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was casting the great bronze images that define the period’s sculpture."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commissioned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">605 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rewarded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">606 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Kuratsukuri no Tori</b> was the sculptor commissioned in 605 CE to make the great bronze image that stands at the head of Japanese Buddhist art.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His family had been in the religion from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court’s own decree recites the line: his grandfather had presented the relics sought for the first temple, his father had taken orders on a sovereign’s behalf, and his aunt had been the first woman in the islands to leave her home and practise the religion of Shakyamuni.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the fourth month of 605 the sovereign, the great minister, the princes and the ministers all vowed together to make images of Buddha sixteen feet high, one of copper and one of embroidery, and named him the engineer of the work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the same month the Kibumi and Yamashiro guilds of painters were first established.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The reward is recorded because of a difficulty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the finished image would not pass into the hall and the workmen were about to break down the doorway, he managed to admit it whole, and for that and the rest he was granted the rank of Dainin and twenty <i>chō</i> of water-fields in Ōmi, out of whose revenue he built a temple that became the nunnery of Sakata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The style of these images is severe and frontal, with long faces and archaic smiles, and it was carried through workshops rather than by single hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The buildings raised around them are the other half of the same import, the swelling columns and cloud-shaped brackets of the oldest surviving precinct arguing for a technique transmitted whole from the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tiles under those roofs, the metal and glass workshops of the valley and the great terraced ritual site above it all belong to the same generation of building.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 133–35, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Founder of the Temple at Okuyama Kume as Seen from Duplicate Eave Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 7 (1999): 59–73, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.59. [Open access]","Hiroshi Hanatani, “The Excavation of the Asuka’ike Workshop Site and its Significance,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 6, no. 8 (1999): 117–26, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.6.8_117. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Aihara, “The Results and Significance of Excavations at the Sakafuneishi Site,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 11, no. 18 (2004): 171–80, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.11.18_171. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Asuka-dera_Asuka_Nara_pref07s5s3900.jpg/1920px-Asuka-dera_Asuka_Nara_pref07s5s3900.jpg","title":"The image of 605","desc":"The seated bronze Shakyamuni at Asukadera, the sixteen-foot image the court vowed to make and named him to build.","alt":"A large dark bronze seated Buddha with one hand raised, in a plain hall with lotus offerings, candles and hanging lanterns before it.","credit":"Photograph by 663highland, CC BY 2.5."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-085","num":85,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"kentōshi","answerText":"kentoshi","tags":["practice","institution","history","japan","china"],"question":"Students and monks who stayed abroad for decades travelled with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the embassies the Japanese court sent across to Tang China.","questions":["One ship of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was wrecked with its ambassadors aboard, which is the ordinary risk of a crossing the court made anyway.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> continued a practice begun with the Sui, whose emperor had found the letter from the islands offensive and told his officers not to report such things again."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First missions</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 630 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 669 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Kentōshi</b> are the embassies the Japanese court sent to Tang China in the 7th, 8th and 9th centuries, and the chief route by which Chinese law, letters and religion reached the islands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They continued something already begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Sui history records the mission of 600 and the letter of 607 from the ruler of the land where the sun rises to the ruler of the land where it sets, at which the emperor told his officer that a barbarian letter wanting in courtesy should not be brought before him again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under the Tang the missions carried an ambassador, an associate ambassador and a body of student priests, several of whom stayed abroad for many years and came home carrying what they had learned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One embassy was lost at sea with its ambassadors aboard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the returning students brought back was a template.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The codes promulgated in Japan in 701 were modelled on Tang originals, and the questions asked of them are comparative ones, because the departures from the model are where the Japanese state can be seen making its own choices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land system is one of those choices, since the Japanese procedure gives the Council of State more of the work of allotment than its Chinese original does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The title of the sovereign is another, taken from Chinese usage in the course of these dealings and put in place of the older style of great king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The court also rebuilt its own seat on Chinese lines in these years, and the phasing of the palaces at Naniwa and in the Asuka valley is still argued from what was found in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 242–46, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Wei Zheng et al., <i>Sui shu</i>, juan 81, “Dongyi: Wo guo” (7th century CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E9%9A%8B%E6%9B%B8/%E5%8D%B781. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Kentoshi_Ship_in_Nara_Palace_Site_Historical_Park.jpg/1920px-Kentoshi_Ship_in_Nara_Palace_Site_Historical_Park.jpg","title":"A ship of the embassies","desc":"A full-size reconstruction standing in the park on the site of the Nara palace, built to show the kind of vessel that made the crossing.","alt":"A large reconstructed wooden ship with a red and white hull and two masts, standing beside a pond with a modern walkway alongside it.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-086","num":86,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":2,"answer":"Nara period","answerText":"Nara period","tags":["era","history","japan","politics","buddhism"],"question":"Japan’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> runs from the founding of a permanent gridded capital in 710 to the court’s departure from it in 794.","questions":["Almost everything written down in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives because the state that produced it wrote on paper and wood and kept what it wrote.","The great bronze Buddha, the first histories and the first anthology of poetry all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a span of under a century."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">710 – 794 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">710 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Nara period</b> is the century of Japanese history that opens with the move to a permanent capital in 710 CE and closes with the court’s removal to Kyoto in 794.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It begins with a decision written down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An edict of 708 recites that the ancient kings had moved their seats five times and three times and had prospered by it, states that the ground at Heijō answers to the four guardian beasts and is defended by three mountains, and says that the tortoise-shell and the milfoil both agree, so a capital should be built there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Officials were appointed to construct it, offerings were sent to Ise to report the work, the ground was ritually quieted in the twelfth month, and in the third month of 710 the capital was moved for the first time to Heijō, a minister of the left being left behind to hold the old seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court then stayed in one place for 84 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The state it ran was the one the codes describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Land was granted to registered people and taken back at death, through provinces, districts and villages, with the Council of State controlling the allotment and the provincial governors managing it on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same codes fixed how the realm regarded what lay beyond it, and their clauses are recovered from later commentaries rather than from any surviving manuscript of the code itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sovereigns of the period travelled their realm as much as they governed it, and a progress could be aimed at demonstrating the legitimacy of a new reign as much as at seeing the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is a short century that produced the first histories, the first great poetry anthology and the largest bronze image in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 5 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 4 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%9B%9B. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","Towao Sakaehara, “Emperor Shomu’s Imperial Visit to Inamino and the Construction of Naniwa Palace,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 13 (2015): 1–9, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.13.0_1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Yakushiji_Toto.jpg/1920px-Yakushiji_Toto.jpg","title":"The east pagoda of Yakushi-ji","desc":"A Nara temple pagoda of three storeys with a lean-to roof under each, which is why it reads at a glance as six.","alt":"A tall wooden pagoda of alternating wide and narrow tiled roofs, with a bronze finial on top, seen against a clear blue sky.","credit":"Photograph by KENPEI, CC BY-SA 3.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-087","num":87,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Heijō-kyō","answerText":"Heijo-kyo","tags":["place","city","history","japan","architecture"],"question":"Laid out on a grid with the palace at its northern end, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the capital of Japan for most of the 8th century.","questions":["Divination was cited when <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was chosen: the tortoise-shell and the milfoil agreed, and the ground answered to the four guardian beasts.","When the court left <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 794 the site was not built over, which is why its plan can still be excavated."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">710 – 784 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chosen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">708 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Heijō-kyō</b> was the capital of Japan from 710 CE, the city on whose site the modern town of Nara stands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founding is an argument as well as an order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The edict of 708 sets out that the capital is the office of all the officials and the place to which the whole realm turns, cites ancient rulers who moved their seats and prospered, and finds that the ground at Heijō answers to the four guardian beasts, is defended by three mountains, and is approved by both forms of divination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Officials were appointed to build it that autumn, the work was reported to the shrine at Ise, the ground was ritually quieted, and the court moved in the third month of 710.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The plan put the palace at the northern end of a rectangular grid of streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That arrangement is imported and can be traced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It belongs to the type whose complete form is the Tang capital of Chang'an, where the palace stands to the north on a north-south axis, and the type reached Japan through the same channels as the law and the writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The building of such a city is an administrative feat rather than an architectural one, as the earlier palace at Fujiwara had already shown, its tiles alone requiring ten times a temple’s output and kilns in four provinces beyond the home basin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The city was fed and staffed by the province and district system that the codes prescribe, which reached down through the allotment of rice land to households counted in registers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The court left in 784 and was in Kyoto ten years later, so much of the plan lay under fields rather than under a later city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 4 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%9B%9B. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 5 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Shuji Funo, “Ancient Chinese Capital Models: Measurement System in Urban Planning,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 93, no. 9 (2017): 724–45, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.93.045. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Construction of the Fujiwara Palace and the Source of its Roof Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 111–27, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_111. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Heijo_Palace%2C_Suzaku-mon_and_Suzaku-oji.jpg/1920px-Heijo_Palace%2C_Suzaku-mon_and_Suzaku-oji.jpg","title":"The Suzaku gate, and the trench","desc":"The reconstructed south gate of the palace, with an excavation open in front of it across the line of the great avenue that ran from it into the city.","alt":"A red and white two-storeyed gate with tiled roofs and gold finials, seen beyond an open archaeological trench with labelled earth sections.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-088","num":88,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Chang'an","answerText":"Chang'an","tags":["place","city","history","china","japan"],"question":"Japanese capitals were laid out after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Tang capital whose palace stood at the northern end of a walled grid of streets.","questions":["The plan of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> belongs to a northern type that reached China with a nomadic dynasty before the Tang made it the model for the whole of East Asia.","Balhae in Manchuria and both of the great Japanese capitals were built to the pattern of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is how far one city plan travelled."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Tang capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">618 – 907 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Copied in Japan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">710 CE onwards</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Chang'an</b> was the capital of Tang China and the model on which the great Japanese capitals were laid out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are two Chinese patterns, and this city is the finished form of one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A capital may put its palace at the centre, as a classical ritual text prescribes, or at the north on a north-south axis, which is the arrangement whose complete form is this city and which is traced back to a northern dynasty of the 5th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That northern type became the plan of a Manchurian capital, of the Japanese capital at Nara and of the one at Kyoto that followed it, so a single arrangement of streets and gates governed cities more than a thousand miles apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some scholars argue that the earlier Japanese capital at Fujiwara followed the other, central-palace pattern instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What was copied is a system of measurement as much as a shape on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A gridded capital has to be set out in units on the ground before anything is built on it, so the plan carries a way of surveying, dividing and recording land along with its streets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Japanese state was assembling exactly that apparatus in the same years, dividing the country into provinces, districts and villages and granting rice land through them from registers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The edict that founded the Japanese capital of 710 makes the borrowing explicit by arguing from Chinese precedent and from Chinese divination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Building on this scale is a claim about the state as much as about the city, which is why the first palace tiled throughout needed ten times a temple’s tiles and kilns in four provinces at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Shuji Funo, “Ancient Chinese Capital Models: Measurement System in Urban Planning,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 93, no. 9 (2017): 724–45, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.93.045. [Open access]","Yoshihiko Ogasawara, “The Construction of the Fujiwara Palace and the Source of its Roof Tiles,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 111–27, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_111. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Koichi Aoki, “An Ancient Go and ‘Village’: An Evaluation of the Hachigaya Site, Yamabe-gun, Kazusa Province,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 179–98, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_179. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 4 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%9B%9B. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Chang%27an_of_Tang.jpg","title":"The plan of the model","desc":"The Tang capital as a walled rectangle of wards, with the palace and the imperial city at the northern end and a great avenue running south from them to the outer gate.","alt":"A drawn plan of a walled rectangular city divided into a grid of wards, with named gates around the wall, two markets, and palace enclosures at the top.","credit":"Map by SY, CC BY-SA 4.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-089","num":89,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Shōmu","answerText":"Shomu","tags":["ruler","person","history","japan","buddhism"],"question":"Faced with an epidemic that emptied whole provinces, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> vowed in 743 to raise a bronze Buddha and asked the country to join in the work.","questions":["The edict of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says outright that the wealth and the power of the realm are his, and then warns that using them may weary the people and move nothing.","Early in the reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the court made progresses to places connected with the imperial house, which is how a new sovereign demonstrated a claim."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">724 – 749 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Buddha vowed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">743 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Shōmu</b> is the posthumous name of the Japanese sovereign who reigned from 724 to 749 CE, in whose reign the great bronze Buddha of Nara was begun.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His early years as sovereign were spent partly on the road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A sovereign of this period travelled to places that carried the imperial house’s own associations, and a progress made soon after an accession was a way of demonstrating that the new reign was the legitimate one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His middle years brought the worst disaster of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The record for the mid-730s reports the pox breaking out through the provinces governed from Kyushu, the common people all lying sick, taxes remitted because the fields had gone unworked, envoys sent with medicine, and a general amnesty proclaimed because calamities were repeated and the pestilence did not stop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The vow he made in 743 is the answer he gave to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The edict states that the wealth of the realm is his and the power of the realm is his, that with such wealth and power the image is easily made and the heart of it hard to reach, and that he fears only to weary the people without moving anything sacred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It asks instead that those who would help come with sincerity, bow three times a day to the Buddha, and make the image each in their own person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is an unusual thing for an autocrat to say, and it makes the project a public one rather than a levy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Behind it stood the ordinary machinery of the state, a country divided into provinces and districts whose registers decided who held land and who owed labour, and it is over the head of that machinery that the appeal was addressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 15 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Towao Sakaehara, “Emperor Shomu’s Imperial Visit to Inamino and the Construction of Naniwa Palace,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 13 (2015): 1–9, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.13.0_1. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 12 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%8C. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Emperor_Shomu.jpg","title":"A later portrait","desc":"Painted centuries after the reign, in the dress of the painter's own day rather than of the 8th century, so it records a memory rather than a likeness.","alt":"A painted hanging scroll of a seated man in dark green court robes and a tall black cap, holding a wooden tablet, on a green dais.","credit":"Public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-090","num":90,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Tōdai-ji","answerText":"Todai-ji","tags":["building","place","history","japan","buddhism"],"question":"The bronze image vowed in 743 was finished nine years later at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the sovereign attended the ceremony that opened its eyes.","questions":["When the great image at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was dedicated in 752, the ranks above the fifth wore ceremonial dress and the rite was ordered as though it were New Year’s Day.","A temple built to hold a single colossal statue, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made the head temple of a network the state maintained in every province."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Image vowed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">743 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eyes opened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">752 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Tōdai-ji</b> is the temple at Nara built to house the colossal bronze Buddha vowed by the court in 743 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vow states its own terms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The edict of the tenth month of that year sets out the wish that the realm should prosper under the protection of the Three Treasures, declares that the wealth and the power of the country belong to the sovereign, and then says that with them the image is easily made and its true meaning hard to reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It warns that the work may only weary people without moving anything sacred, and may even earn resentment, so it asks those who join to do so with sincerity and to bow to the image three times a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The casting took nine years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the fourth month of 752 the great Rushana image was completed and its eyes were opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sovereign went in person to the temple leading the civil and military officials, a great vegetarian assembly was held, and the ceremony was ordered exactly as the rites of New Year’s Day were, with everyone of the fifth rank and above in ceremonial dress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A hall to cover an image of this size is a problem of carpentry, and the technique for building on such a scale had come in with the temple architecture of the previous century, transmitted whole rather than invented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The temple stood inside a capital that was itself laid out on a Chinese plan, so its scale and its city made the same argument in different materials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The court that paid for it travelled its realm and spent on ritual as a way of asserting the standing of a reign, which is the register this whole undertaking belongs to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 15 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 18 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E5%85%AB. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Shuji Funo, “Ancient Chinese Capital Models: Measurement System in Urban Planning,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 93, no. 9 (2017): 724–45, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.93.045. [Open access]","Towao Sakaehara, “Emperor Shomu’s Imperial Visit to Inamino and the Construction of Naniwa Palace,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 13 (2015): 1–9, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.13.0_1. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/%E6%9D%B1%E5%A4%A7%E5%AF%BA_%E5%A4%A7%E4%BB%8F%E6%AE%BF-2.jpg/1920px-%E6%9D%B1%E5%A4%A7%E5%AF%BA_%E5%A4%A7%E4%BB%8F%E6%AE%BF-2.jpg","title":"The hall of the Great Buddha","desc":"The hall built to house the great bronze image; what stands today is a later rebuilding on the ancient site.","alt":"A very large two-storeyed wooden temple hall with sweeping tiled roofs and gold finials, seen across a wide lawn with trees at one side.","credit":"Photograph by Drivephotographer, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-091","num":91,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Vairocana","answerText":"Vairocana","tags":["concept","object","history","japan","buddhism"],"question":"The colossal bronze image at Nara is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> Buddha, the cosmic figure whose body the whole world is said to be contained in.","questions":["The 743 edict vowing the image calls it a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and asks anyone who would help to bow to it three times a day and make it in their own person.","Every province was to raise a sixteen-foot image of its own, but only the capital cast a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of that scale, and it took nine years."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vowed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">743 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Completed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">752 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Vairocana</b> is the cosmic Buddha of whom the great bronze image at Nara was made, vowed in 743 CE and finished nine years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The edict that ordered it says what it is for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It sets out the wish that the realm should prosper under the protection of the Three Treasures, that the blessing should reach animals and plants as well as people, and that on the fifteenth day of the tenth month a great vow was taken to make this image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It then declares that the wealth of the realm is the sovereign’s and the power of the realm is the sovereign’s, and that with them the thing is easily made and its meaning hard to reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It warns that the work may weary people without moving anything sacred, and may even earn resentment and blame, so those who join it are asked to come with sincerity and to bow to the image three times a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The image was finished in the fourth month of 752 and its eyes opened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sovereign attended in person at the head of the civil and military officials, a great assembly was held, and the ceremony was ordered exactly as the rites of New Year’s Day were.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The scale is the point: every province had been told to raise a sixteen-foot image, funded from households granted to the provincial temples, and this one dwarfed all of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A hall to cover it was a problem of carpentry that the temple building of the previous century had prepared, its technique carried in whole from the continent rather than worked out at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The court that paid for it also travelled its realm and spent on ritual to assert the standing of a reign, which is the register the whole undertaking belongs to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 15 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 18 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E5%85%AB. [Open access]","Towao Sakaehara, “Emperor Shomu’s Imperial Visit to Inamino and the Construction of Naniwa Palace,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 13 (2015): 1–9, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.13.0_1. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Shuji Funo, “Ancient Chinese Capital Models: Measurement System in Urban Planning,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 93, no. 9 (2017): 724–45, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.93.045. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/20190121_T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Great_Buddha-2.jpg/1920px-20190121_T%C5%8Ddai-ji_Great_Buddha-2.jpg","title":"The image itself","desc":"The seated bronze figure in its hall at Nara, with the gilded halo behind it carrying small buddhas of its own.","alt":"A colossal dark bronze seated Buddha with one hand raised, before a great gilded halo set with small seated figures, above an altar of lotus offerings.","credit":"Photograph by Balon Greyjoy, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-092","num":92,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Kegon","answerText":"Kegon","tags":["school of thought","concept","history","japan","buddhism"],"question":"Named for the sutra in which Vairocana appears, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the teaching the great temple at Nara was built around.","questions":["The court promoted a lecturer in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to junior clerical superintendent in 756, which is how a school of study appears in the record at all.","A school in 8th-century Japan was a course of study kept up inside a temple rather than a sect, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one the state endowed most heavily."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lectureship</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 756 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Image vowed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">743 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Kegon</b> is the Japanese name of the Buddhist teaching centred on the Flower Garland sutra, in which the cosmic Buddha Vairocana appears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a course of study rather than a sect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The schools of 8th-century Japan were bodies of doctrine kept up by monks who lived in the great temples, several of them at once in the same buildings, and a monk belonged to a temple and to the clergy of the realm rather than to a denomination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The record shows them chiefly through offices, since it is the appointments that the chronicle has reason to note.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 756 the court raised the abbot and a foreign master to senior superintendent and promoted the lecturer in this teaching to junior superintendent, naming the men who had watched over the retired sovereign in his last illness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The teaching mattered to the state because its Buddha did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The image vowed in 743 is a Vairocana, so the temple raised to house it was the house of this doctrine, and the wealth and the labour of the realm went into it on the sovereign’s own declaration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around that centre the state maintained a provincial network of temples with fixed endowments and fixed numbers of monks and nuns, which is a different way of organising a religion from patronage by great families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The buildings themselves were put up by a state bureau that controlled the carpenters, so the architecture of the period is markedly uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the codes had to say about clergy is known only at second hand, since no manuscript of them survives whole and their clauses are recovered from later commentaries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 19 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B9%9D. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 15 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Sudhana_Pilgrimage_55_Kegon_%28Nara_National_Museum%29.jpg/1920px-Sudhana_Pilgrimage_55_Kegon_%28Nara_National_Museum%29.jpg","title":"The pilgrimage of the sutra","desc":"A Heian-period painting of Sudhana, whose journey to 55 teachers is told in the Flower Garland sutra this teaching is named for.","alt":"A worn painting on silk in browns and reds, showing figures seated on a raised veranda and others kneeling on open ground among bare trees.","credit":"Painting by a Heian-period artist, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-093","num":93,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"kokubunji","answerText":"kokubunji","tags":["institution","building","history","japan","buddhism"],"question":"Every province was ordered in 741 to maintain a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a state temple of twenty monks endowed with fifty households and ten <i>chō</i> of paddy.","questions":["The edict creating the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> also ordered a convent of ten nuns in every province, and named both houses after the sutras they were to read.","Sites for each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were to be chosen with care — not so near a town as to be troubled by its smells, not so far that people would be worn out reaching it."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ordered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">741 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>kokubunji</b> is the provincial temple that every Japanese province was ordered to maintain under an edict of 741 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The edict is precise about what it wants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A pagoda temple is to be the flower of its province, so the site must be well chosen and lasting: not so near people that the smells reach it, not so far that those who come are worn out by the journey, and the provincial officials are to keep it richly furnished and clean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each province is to endow the monks’ temple with fifty households and ten <i>chō</i> of paddy and the nuns’ temple with ten <i>chō</i>, and to keep twenty monks in the one and ten nuns in the other, replacing any who are lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two houses are named for what they read — the monks’ for the Golden Light sutra of the four heavenly kings who guard the country, the nuns’ for the Lotus that wipes out sin — and both are to recite on the eighth of every month.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The system is religion organised the way the state organised everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The country was already divided into provinces, districts and villages, with rice land granted to registered people through that hierarchy and the Council of State controlling the allotment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A temple with a fixed endowment, a fixed number of clergy and a fixed calendar of reading is the same administrative habit applied to the sangha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Below the district the documents thin out, and what a village of this system actually looked like has to be read off excavated settlements instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Later the network acquired a head: in 756 the court promoted the abbot of the great temple at the capital and a master newly arrived from China to the senior clerical grade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 14 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E5%9B%9B. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","Koichi Aoki, “An Ancient Go and ‘Village’: An Evaluation of the Hachigaya Site, Yamabe-gun, Kazusa Province,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 179–98, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_179. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 19 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B9%9D. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/%E7%94%B2%E6%96%90%E5%9B%BD%E5%88%86%E5%AF%BA%E8%B7%A1_%E5%A1%94%E8%B7%A1.jpg/1920px-%E7%94%B2%E6%96%90%E5%9B%BD%E5%88%86%E5%AF%BA%E8%B7%A1_%E5%A1%94%E8%B7%A1.jpg","title":"Foundation stones in a field","desc":"The pagoda platform of the provincial temple of Kai, its column bases still in place where the edict of 741 put them.","alt":"A grassy field with a scatter of large flat foundation stones in rows, an information board among them and farm buildings along the horizon.","credit":"Photograph by Saigen Jiro, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-094","num":94,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Gyōki","answerText":"Gyoki","tags":["person","history","japan","buddhism","society"],"question":"Preaching in the streets with thousands following him, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was first restrained by the court and then made its senior cleric in 745.","questions":["People called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a bodhisattva while he was still alive, which the official chronicle records without embarrassment in the notice of his death.","Wherever <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stopped he founded a practice hall, nine of them in the home provinces alone, and his disciples kept them up after his death."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Made daisōjō</span><span class=\"dt-v\">745 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">749 CE, aged 80</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Gyōki</b> was the Japanese monk who preached to ordinary people outside the temples and was made the senior cleric of the realm in 745 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle’s notice of his death is unusually warm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It says he was a monk of Yakushi-ji, of the Koshi family and a man of Izumi province, that he read the Yogācāra treatises when he first left home and grasped their meaning at once, and that he then travelled the capital and the countryside teaching.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those who followed him, it says, were counted in thousands; where he was heard to be coming the lanes emptied of people, who came out to bow to him and were taught according to what each could take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people of the time called him a bodhisattva, and he died at eighty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A preacher with a crowd is a problem for a state that registers everybody.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land system depended on households listed on a register and on the rice fields allotted through them, so a movement of men off their fields and behind a monk was a fiscal matter before it was a religious one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The court dealt with the difficulty by absorbing it: he was made great superintendent of the clergy, and the appointment stands in the record as a bare sentence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reign that appointed him was also raising the great image, and the wealth and labour it needed were exactly what a preacher who could move crowds might supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What survives of him besides the obituary is the network of halls his disciples kept up, and the memory of a monk the state first restrained and then needed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 16 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E5%85%AD. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 17 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Towao Sakaehara, “Emperor Shomu’s Imperial Visit to Inamino and the Construction of Naniwa Palace,” <i>Bulletin of Osaka Museum of History</i> 13 (2015): 1–9, https://doi.org/10.34570/omhbull.13.0_1. [Open access]","Koichi Aoki, “An Ancient Go and ‘Village’: An Evaluation of the Hachigaya Site, Yamabe-gun, Kazusa Province,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 179–98, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_179. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Statue_of_Gyoki_in_Nara_DSCN8896_20100404.JPG","title":"A modern bronze","desc":"He is still a public figure in Nara, where this statue stands outside the station; no image of him from his own century survives.","alt":"A bronze standing statue of a bald monk in robes holding a rosary, on a plinth in front of a modern glass station building.","credit":"Photograph by Ogiyoshisan, CC BY-SA 3.0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-095","num":95,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Ganjin","answerText":"Ganjin","tags":["person","history","japan","buddhism","china"],"question":"After repeated failed crossings, the Chinese monk <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached Japan in 753 with a returning embassy and brought a proper ordination lineage with him.","questions":["The court made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> senior superintendent of the clergy in 756, alongside the abbot of the great temple at the capital.","A monk could not be validly ordained without enough properly ordained masters present, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was fetched across the sea to supply."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arrived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">753 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made daisōzu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">756 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Ganjin</b> was the Chinese monk who reached Japan in 753 CE to give the country a valid line of Buddhist ordination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle records his arrival in a single clause, at the end of a very long attempt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It notes that the associate ambassador to the Tang court came home, and that the Tang monk Ganjin, the monk Fashin and eight others returned with him — the end of an attempt that had taken years and several wrecked crossings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he brought was procedural rather than doctrinal: ordination requires a quorum of masters already ordained in an unbroken line, and without one a Japanese monk was a monk only by the court’s say-so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three years later the court raised him to the senior grade of the clergy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The promotion is worth reading in full, because of what it lists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court exempted 126 monks who had nursed the sick, named the abbot and two other masters and their parents’ households, and then promoted the visiting master and the abbot to senior superintendent and the lecturer in the Flower Garland teaching to junior superintendent, saying that some excelled in learning and some in purity of discipline and that all were fit to guard the reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> This is a clergy ranked, endowed and rewarded like an arm of government, which is what it had been since the first superintendents were appointed in 624.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The temples he worked in were built by a state bureau that controlled the carpenters, so their architecture is unusually uniform.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How the codes themselves regulated clergy is known only through later commentaries, no manuscript of them having survived whole, which is a limit on everything that can be said about how the clergy was governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 19 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B9%9D. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 20 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 2 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 152–54, https://archive.org/details/nihongi2asto. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Toshodaiji_Ganjin.jpg/1920px-Toshodaiji_Ganjin.jpg","title":"The portrait at Tōshōdai-ji","desc":"A dry-lacquer seated figure, made at the temple he founded and traditionally taken to be a likeness of him.","alt":"A monochrome photograph of a lacquer portrait statue of a seated monk with closed eyes and a shaven head, against a black ground.","credit":"Public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-096","num":96,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":4,"answer":"Shōsōin","answerText":"Shosoin","tags":["building","institution","history","japan","art"],"question":"A timber storehouse at Nara, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has kept the possessions of an 8th-century court since they were given away after a sovereign’s death.","questions":["Nothing was excavated to fill the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>: its contents were placed in it deliberately in the 8th century and have simply never left.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came with a written inventory, its objects can be checked against a list drawn up when they were deposited."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8th century CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Donation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">756 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Shōsōin</b> is the timber-framed storehouse at Nara, raised about thirteen hundred years ago, that holds the treasures and everyday objects of an 8th-century Japanese court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not an excavated collection, and that is the whole of its importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The objects were donated to the temple to commemorate a sovereign’s death, listed as they went in, and kept in excellent condition from that day to this, which is why they are evidence of a kind archaeology almost never provides, since what is dug up has usually been thrown away or lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sovereign in question died in the summer of 756, and the chronicle records the death itself, the remission of that year’s land tax and the naming of a successor in his testamentary decree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the storehouse preserves is therefore a household rather than a hoard, and a dated one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its value to a historian is that the written record and the objects have survived together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The collection included about sixty kinds of medicinal substance, stored with a ledger giving their names and their amounts, which shows they were held to be used rather than merely shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The temple it belonged to had just finished casting the largest bronze image in the world, whose eyes were opened four years before the donation with the whole court present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The building technique that made such structures possible had come in with the temple architecture of the previous century, transmitted whole from the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It stood in a capital laid out on a Chinese grid, so the storehouse, the temple and the city all make the same argument in different materials, and all three were raised within a single lifetime.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Michiho Ito, “Pursuing the Identity of a Natural Medicine Licorice by Dr. Shoji Shibata That He Firstly Investigated Half a Century Before,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 101, no. 5 (2025): 239–48, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.101.018. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 19 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B9%9D. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 18 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E5%85%AB. [Open access]","Satoru Unno, “An Essay on the Buildings of the Western Precinct of Hōryūji and the Transmission of Architectural Technique in Ancient East Asia,” <i>Bukkyō Bunka Kenkyū Ronshū</i> 24 (2024): 3–39, https://doi.org/10.34613/todaibusseironshu.24.0_3_1. [Open access]","Shuji Funo, “Ancient Chinese Capital Models: Measurement System in Urban Planning,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 93, no. 9 (2017): 724–45, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.93.045. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/%E6%AD%A3%E5%80%89%E9%99%A2-2.jpg/1920px-%E6%AD%A3%E5%80%89%E9%99%A2-2.jpg","title":"The storehouse","desc":"Raised on posts, with walls of triangular timbers laid log-fashion, and standing behind the great temple at Nara.","alt":"A long dark wooden building with a wide tiled roof, raised well off the ground on rows of thick round pillars, on a lawn behind a low fence.","credit":"Photograph by Drivephotographer, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-097","num":97,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":5,"answer":"Shōsōin medicines","answerText":"Shosoin medicines","tags":["object","practice","history","japan","science"],"question":"About sixty substances kept with a ledger of their names and amounts, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the oldest pharmacy in the world still in its own boxes.","questions":["Scientists were allowed to sample the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1948 and 1949, and published what they thought each of them was in 1955.","A study of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> published in 2003 used genetic sequencing to overturn an identification that had stood for half a century."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Deposited</span><span class=\"dt-v\">756 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First studied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1948 – 1949</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Shōsōin medicines</b> are the roughly sixty kinds of medicinal substance deposited in the Nara storehouse in the 8th century CE and kept there ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They came into the storehouse with a list already made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ledger stored beside them gives each substance’s name and its amount, which is what shows that they were held as a working pharmacy for treating people rather than as curiosities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The repository itself keeps no record of where any of them came from, so the geography of the collection has to be recovered from the substances themselves rather than read off a label.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were examined scientifically for the first time in 1948 and 1949, when a team was allowed to take only small samples and published its identifications in 1955.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One of those identifications did not survive a later re-examination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stored licorice root had been assigned to a species by the shape of the root; a study published in 2003 sequenced a spacer region of its nuclear DNA and assigned it to a different species, correcting a conclusion that had stood for nearly fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is the value of the collection in miniature — objects with a date and a name attached, which later methods can be turned on again whenever the methods improve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court whose possessions these were had just cast the largest bronze image in the world, and the deposit followed the death of the sovereign who vowed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state that kept such a ledger was the one that listed its people in registers and allotted their rice land from them, and the habit is the same one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Michiho Ito, “Pursuing the Identity of a Natural Medicine Licorice by Dr. Shoji Shibata That He Firstly Investigated Half a Century Before,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 101, no. 5 (2025): 239–48, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.101.018. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 18 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E5%85%AB. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 19 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E5%8D%81%E4%B9%9D. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]","Koichi Aoki, “An Ancient Go and ‘Village’: An Evaluation of the Hachigaya Site, Yamabe-gun, Kazusa Province,” <i>Nihon Kokogaku</i> 10, no. 16 (2003): 179–98, https://doi.org/10.11215/nihonkokogaku1994.10.16_179. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Licorice_slices_%2820240123%29.jpg/1920px-Licorice_slices_%2820240123%29.jpg","title":"Licorice root","desc":"Sliced root of the kind stored in the repository, whose species was reassigned in 2003 after its DNA was sequenced; this is a modern sample, not one of the stored pieces.","alt":"A close-up heap of pale sliced woody root with reddish-brown bark at the edges of each slice.","credit":"Photograph by Fumikas Sagisavas, CC0."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-098","num":98,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":2,"answer":"Kojiki","answerText":"Kojiki","tags":["text","history","japan","mythology","literature"],"question":"Completed in 712 and the oldest surviving Japanese book, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was set down from what a court reciter had committed to memory.","questions":["Unlike the chronicle that followed it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries no chronology at all, and begins with the gods rather than with a date.","Chinese characters are used in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> partly for their sound, so the Japanese words behind the text can often be recovered."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Commissioned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">682 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Completed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">712 CE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Kojiki</b> is the oldest surviving Japanese book, completed in 712 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It began as a court project thirty years before it was finished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 682 a number of princes and high officials were formally commissioned to prepare a history of the sovereigns and of matters of high antiquity; nothing is known of what they produced, but the measure led in the end to this work, as its own preface says.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The text was taken down from the recitation of a member of the court named Hieda no Are, by a man with some training in Chinese letters, so the book has two authors of very different kinds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It presents a surface of Chinese characters, but the Chinese construction is interrupted throughout by Japanese words written for their sound, so the Japanese wording behind it can often be restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What it conspicuously does not have is a chronology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicle compiled eight years later fixed dates, months and even days for events centuries before writing was used in the islands; this book gives none, and its account runs from the gods to the sovereigns without that scaffolding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was made by the same court and the same generation that were building a permanent capital at Nara and writing the law codes the country would be governed by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A book of this kind is a claim about where authority comes from, which is why a state that had just remade its offices and its map wanted one of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It survived where an earlier compilation of the same kind did not, and it remains the earliest monument of Japanese literature that anyone can read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), xiii, https://archive.org/details/nihongi1asto. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), xix–xx, https://archive.org/details/nihongi1asto. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 5 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Hirokazu Ohtaka, “The Enactment of the Taiho Ritsuryo Codes and Ancient Japan’s Ideas about its Periphery,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 122, no. 12 (2013): 1985–2018, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.122.12_1985. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/%E5%8F%A4%E4%BA%8B%E8%A8%98_%E5%9B%BD%E7%AB%8B%E5%9B%B3%E6%9B%B8%E9%A4%A8%E3%83%87%E3%82%B8%E3%82%BF%E3%83%AB%E3%82%B3%E3%83%AC%E3%82%AF%E3%82%B7%E3%83%A7%E3%83%B3_020.png","title":"A manuscript of it","desc":"Two openings from a copy in the National Diet Library; no manuscript survives from anywhere near the year the book was finished.","alt":"Two open pages of a woodblock-printed book in columns of Chinese characters, with small annotations between the lines.","credit":"Public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-099","num":99,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Nihon Shoki","answerText":"Nihon Shoki","tags":["text","history","japan","mythology","literature"],"question":"Presented to the throne in 720, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the first of the six official histories and the fuller of the two ancient chronicles.","questions":["Written almost wholly in Chinese, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> quotes variant traditions side by side, introducing each with the words \"in one writing\".","A book of imperial genealogies once belonged to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and is no longer extant, so what survives is 30 books of the original 31."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Presented</span><span class=\"dt-v\">720 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Books</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30, of an original 31</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Nihon Shoki</b> is the chronicle of Japan presented to the throne in 720 CE and the first of the country’s official histories.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It carries neither a title page nor a preface of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is known of its making comes from a commentary written a century later, which says it was completed and laid before the reigning empress by Prince Toneri and Yasumaro, and that besides the thirty books that survive there was a book of imperial genealogies now lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word that commentary uses of the work means selected afresh, which points to compilation from existing materials rather than composition, and the compilers were groups of literati assembling what they had into an arrangement that satisfied their own time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is written almost wholly in Chinese, where the older book keeps breaking into Japanese written for its sound, so the two are as different in language as they are in scope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Its most useful habit, for a modern reader, is that it does not choose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where traditions differed the compilers set the variants down one after another, each introduced as one writing, so a reader can see the alternatives the 8th-century court had in front of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The dates are another matter, since the compilers supplied years, months and days for events long before writing was in use, and the reigns of the early sovereigns are given lengths no human life could reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was made by the court that had just settled a permanent capital and promulgated the law codes, and the sovereign in it bears the title those codes use rather than the older style of great king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For twelve centuries it was the principal source of knowledge of Japanese antiquity, and its pre-eminence there was not contested until modern times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), xiii–xiv, https://archive.org/details/nihongi1asto. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), xvii–xx, https://archive.org/details/nihongi1asto. [Open access]","L. M. Ermakova, “Space and the Gods of Space in Japanese Myths,” <i>Russian Japanology Review</i> 5, no. 1 (2022): 76–96, https://doi.org/10.55105/2658-6444-2022-1-76-96. [Open access]","Sugano no Mamichi et al., comps., <i>Shoku Nihongi</i>, juan 5 (797 CE), Chinese Wikisource, https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E7%BA%8C%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E7%B4%80/%E5%8D%B7%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%94. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Postscript_from_The_Age_of_Gods_chapter%2C_The_Chronicles_of_Japan_%281286%29.jpg","title":"The Age of Gods, copied in 1286","desc":"The opening of the first book in a 13th-century copy, with reading marks and notes added between the columns by later hands.","alt":"A worn scroll of closely written columns of Chinese characters, with small red and black annotation marks beside many of them.","credit":"Copied by Urabe Kanekata, public domain."}},
{"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","id":"jp-100","num":100,"category":"Japan · History","difficulty":3,"answer":"Jinmu","answerText":"Jinmu","tags":["ruler","concept","history","japan","mythology"],"question":"The chronicles date the accession of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a year answering to 660 BCE, which is where the imperial line was given its beginning.","questions":["A palace at Kashihara is where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have taken the dignity, in a passage quoting an old song about pillars set on the bottom-rock.","Writing was not officially used in Japan until the 5th century CE, so the reign dates given for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and his successors were supplied by their compilers."],"answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Accession</span><span class=\"dt-v\">660 BCE, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">720 CE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jinmu</b> is the first sovereign of Japan in the chronicles, whose accession they place in a year answering to 660 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scene is set at Kashihara.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle says that on the first day of the first month he assumed the imperial dignity in the palace there, that this year is reckoned the first of his reign, and it quotes an old saying in praise of the moment, about palace-pillars set mightily on the bottom-rock and roof-timbers reared to the Plain of High Heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dating is the work of the compilers rather than a record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Writing had no official standing in Japan until the 5th century CE and calendar-makers are first mentioned in the 6th, so a complete system of years, months and days reaching back to the 7th century BCE can only have been supplied; the impossible lengths given to the early reigns are the plainest sign of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The two chronicles do not tell the story the same way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They open on different cosmogonies and different first gods, and the fuller of them sets rival traditions side by side rather than choosing between them, which is not what a settled national myth looks like.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the 8th-century court needed was a line running unbroken from the gods to the reigning sovereign, and the chronicles supply exactly that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same generation adopted a Chinese title for the sovereign in place of the older style of great king, and wrote it back into the earliest reigns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It also settled a permanent capital, promulgated the codes and organised the country into provinces and districts, so the myth and the machinery were made together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), 132–33, https://archive.org/details/nihongi1asto. [Open access]","W. G. Aston, trans., <i>Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697</i>, vol. 1 (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trübner, 1896), xvii–xviii, https://archive.org/details/nihongi1asto. [Open access]","L. M. Ermakova, “Space and the Gods of Space in Japanese Myths,” <i>Russian Japanology Review</i> 5, no. 1 (2022): 76–96, https://doi.org/10.55105/2658-6444-2022-1-76-96. [Open access]","Kikushi Hon-iden, “On the Title of the Monarch in Ancient Japan and China: Formation of the Title of ‘Daio’ and ‘Tenno’,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 90, no. 12 (1981): 1747–84, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.90.12_1747. [Open access]","Yoshiyuki Mitani, “The Ritsuryo State and Land Allotment,” <i>Shigaku Zasshi</i> 118, no. 3 (2009): 345–74, https://doi.org/10.24471/shigaku.118.3_345. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b1/Tenn%C5%8D_Jimmu_detail_01.jpg","title":"A Meiji print","desc":"Drawn in the 1880s, some 25 centuries after the date the chronicles give him, in an antiquarian idea of ancient dress.","alt":"A colour woodblock print of a bearded man in a green and red robe over deerskin, holding a long bow, with a quiver of arrows at his back.","credit":"Print by Ginko Adachi, public domain."}},
{"id":"cnh-042","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Grinding slabs from its villages carry the wear and starch of acorns rather than grain, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now read as foraging as much as farming.","questions":["Twenty-three ground stone blades from three of its sites were tested for wear, and only five had cut grasses, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not simply a culture of reapers.","Tanghu, about 20 hectares (49 acres) and the largest village of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, gave up 41 sunken round houses and 169 pits in a single season's digging."],"answer":"Peiligang culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 7000 – 5000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">118, in the Songshan region</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tanghu, about 20 hectares (49 acres)</span></div>","traditional":"裴李崗文化","hanzi":"裴李岗文化","pinyin":"Péilǐgǎng wénhuà","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Peiligang culture</b> is the early Neolithic culture of the middle Yellow River, in what is now Henan, and it runs from about 9,000 to 7,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its people lived in villages, the largest of them Tanghu near Xinzheng, about 20 hectares (49 acres), where one season's digging uncovered 41 round houses half sunk into the ground and 169 pits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the Songshan hills 118 of its settlements are known, standing about 5.6 kilometres (3.5 miles) apart, more than twice the spacing of the Yangshao villages that followed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They fall into nine clusters strung along the foothills and the river valleys, which is what a landscape of small separate communities looks like before any centre pulls them together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The culture is known above all for two tools that look like farming equipment: grinding slabs with their rollers, and long stone blades whose cutting edge is worked into teeth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Both have now been read under the microscope, and neither turns out to be quite what its name says. Twenty-three ground stone blades from Jiahu, Shigu and Egou were examined for wear, twenty of them with toothed edges, and only five carry the polish of harvesting grasses while six were used on tree branches for fruit or nuts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The grinding slabs are harder still on the farming story, since the wear and the starch caught in them show that what they mostly ground was acorns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Crops were grown all the same: at Tanghu the phytoliths put broomcorn millet in the fields 7,800 years ago with rice beside it, and pigs were being managed at Jiahu by about 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So the culture belongs in the middle of the long passage from foraging to farming rather than at the end of it, gathering a great deal of what it ate and cultivating the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Peiligang culture","sources":["Li Liu, Judith Field, Richard Fullagar, Sheahan Bestel, Xingcan Chen, and Xiaolin Ma, “What Did Grinding Stones Grind? New Light on Early Neolithic Subsistence Economy in the Middle Yellow River Valley, China,” <i>Antiquity</i> 84, no. 325 (2010): 816–33, https://hdl.handle.net/10779/uow.27707226.v1. [Open access]","Richard Fullagar, Elspeth Hayes, Xingcan Chen, Xiaolin Ma, and Li Liu, “A Functional Study of Denticulate Sickles and Knives, Ground Stone Tools from the Early Neolithic Peiligang Culture, China,” <i>Archaeological Research in Asia</i> 26 (2021): 100265, https://hdl.handle.net/10779/uow.27819657.v1. [Open access]","Lijie Yan, Xia Wang, Li Zhang, and Fei Teng, “Spatiotemporal Evolution and Clustering Patterns of Settlements from the Neolithic to the Bronze Age (9000–3000 BP) in the Songshan Mountain Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 7 (2026): e0351644, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13390836/. [Open access]","Jianping Zhang et al., “Early Mixed Farming of Millet and Rice 7800 Years Ago in the Middle Yellow River Region, China,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 7, no. 12 (2012): e52146, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0052146. [Open access]","Hitomi Hongo, Hiroki Kikuchi, and Hiroo Nasu, “Beginning of Pig Management in Neolithic China: Comparison of Domestication Processes between Northern and Southern Regions,” <i>Animal Frontiers</i> 11, no. 3 (2021): 30–42, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214443/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","prehistory","archaeology","history","china","agriculture"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Peiligang_Culture_Stone_Roller_%26_Quern%2C_Xinzheng.jpg/1280px-Peiligang_Culture_Stone_Roller_%26_Quern%2C_Xinzheng.jpg","title":"The tool that named a culture","desc":"A grinding slab standing on stubby carved legs, with the roller that worked it, from Xinzheng in Henan; wear and starch on slabs like this one show they were used mostly on acorns.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd of a piece in the Zhengzhou Museum, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peiligang_Culture_Stone_Roller_%26_Quern%2C_Xinzheng.jpg","alt":"An oval stone slab on four short legs, its upper face worn smooth and dished, with a long stone rolling pin lying diagonally across it, on a plain white museum plinth."}},
{"id":"cnh-101","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The heaviest bronze the dynasty ever cast, 875 kilograms (1,929 pounds) of it, was inscribed for a queen mother, which is the scale of the cult owed to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> after death.","questions":["The chief duty of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows in a divination where the king read the cracks for a good birth and got a girl 31 days later.","Marriages into other lineages held the dynasty's relations with peoples outside it, so a daughter was worth as much as a son to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the late Shang court."],"answer":"Shang royal women","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Heaviest bronze</span><span class=\"dt-v\">875 kg, cast for a queen</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dasikong graves</span><span class=\"dt-v\">81 of women, 68 of men</span></div>","traditional":"商代王室女性","hanzi":"商代王室女性","pinyin":"Shāngdài wángshì nǚxìng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang royal women</b> are the consorts, mothers and daughters of the late Shang kings, met in the oracle bones, in the bronzes cast for them and in a handful of graves. The Shang had a vocabulary for them — zi, nü, mu, hou, fu — marking the stage a woman had reached rather than a rank held for life, her standing set throughout by blood or marriage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Daughters were worth having: marriages into other lineages were how the dynasty held its relations with peoples outside it, so a girl was an instrument of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the court wanted of a consort shows in the divinations about childbirth, which put the question as whether the bearing will be good and treat a daughter as the wrong answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In one the king read the cracks and said a birth on a ding day would bring prolonged luck; she gave birth 31 days later, and the verification reads that it was not good, it was a girl.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Death did not end a queen's place at court, since the cycle of offerings that ran through the year was addressed to the high ancestors, the dead kings and the consort queens together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A queen enters it as a Bi, an ancestress carrying one of the ten day names and taking her offerings on that day: Bi Xin was the first queen of Wu Ding, Bi Geng the consort of Xiao Yi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scale of it can be weighed: the largest bronze the Shang ever cast, 133 centimetres (52 inches) tall and 875 kilograms (1,929 pounds), came out of a tomb at Wuguancun inscribed for a queen mother named Wu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which queen is unsettled, one argument dating the vessel later than Wu Ding's reign on its style and giving it to a subsequent king's consort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Below the palace the pattern differs: at the Dasikong cemetery 81 graves of women were dug against 68 of men, and the women's carry the greater labour in their goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang royal women","sources":["Sheri A. Lullo, review of <i>Women in Ancient China</i>, by Bret Hinsch, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 413–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76794. [Open access]","Wm. Theodore de Bary and Irene Bloom, comps., <i>Sources of Chinese Tradition</i>, 2nd ed., vol. 1 (New York: Columbia University Press, 1999), 9, https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/cup/oracle_bone_childbearing.pdf. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 167–68, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 180–82, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Andrew E. MacIver, “Shang Ritual and Social Dynamics at Anyang: An Analysis of Dasikong and Huayuanzhuang East Burials” (MA thesis, University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2017), 67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/62087. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["people","history","archaeology","china","bronze age","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/%E5%8F%B8%E6%AF%8D%E6%88%8A%E6%96%B9%E9%BC%8E%E6%8B%93%E6%9C%AC.jpg/1280px-%E5%8F%B8%E6%AF%8D%E6%88%8A%E6%96%B9%E9%BC%8E%E6%8B%93%E6%9C%AC.jpg","title":"A vessel inscribed for a queen","desc":"An ink rubbing taken from the great tetrapod ding found at Wuguancun near Anyang, with its three-character inscription lifted separately at the top; the reading of the first character is argued over, but the last two name a mother Wu.","credit":"Rubbing in the Shanghai Museum, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8F%B8%E6%AF%8D%E6%88%8A%E6%96%B9%E9%BC%8E%E6%8B%93%E6%9C%AC.jpg","alt":"A black ink rubbing of a four-legged bronze vessel, its walls covered in dense scroll ornament, with a small dark panel of three archaic characters shown above it and a column of Chinese writing down the right side."}},
{"id":"cnh-102","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Told that a sage's heart had seven openings, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had his own kinsman cut open to look, which is the sort of charge the annals lay against the last Shang king.","questions":["Beaten at Muye, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went back into his capital, climbed the treasury tower, put on his jewelled robes and threw himself into the fire he had lit there.","A pool of wine, a forest of hanging meat and a greased beam laid over a brazier are the tradition's picture of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the king the Zhou overthrew."],"answer":"Di Xin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the king list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31st and last</span></div>","traditional":"帝辛","hanzi":"帝辛","pinyin":"Dì Xīn","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Di Xin</b> is the last king of the Shang in the traditional account, thirty-first and final name in the king list and the ruler the Zhou overthrew; his personal name was Shou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nearly everything said of him was written long after the dynasty fell, and the oldest surviving charge is the one the Zhou made on the morning of the battle, that he listened only to his wife.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the longer speeches put into the victor's mouth are among the contested chapters of the Documents, 20 of the 28 surviving Zhou documents being of undisputed genuineness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Grand Historian gives him real gifts turned to bad use: quick of sight and hearing, strong enough to kill wild beasts by hand, and learned and fluent enough to argue down any remonstrance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His favourite was Daji, a captive taken when he defeated the lord of Su, and her words are said to have been obeyed as orders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> He taxed heavily to pile money on the Stag Terrace and grain at Juqiao, stocked the parks at Shaqiu with beasts and birds, made a pool of wine there and hung meat to form a forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For those who murmured there was a metal beam smeared with grease and laid over a brazier, along which the condemned walked until they fell in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He had the marquis of Jiu cut to pieces when that man's daughter refused him, the marquis of E for protesting, and his kinsman Bi Gan opened up to see whether a sage's heart has seven passages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jizi feigned madness and was imprisoned, the music masters carried the sacrificial vessels off to the Zhou, and when his army broke at Muye he climbed the Stag Terrace, put on his jewelled robes and burned himself alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> King Wu cut off his head, hung it from a white banner and put Daji to death; a later legend adds that none of the victors dared strike her until an aged counsellor covered his face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Di Xin","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 199–207, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chou Hsin,” https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ta Chi,” https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 131–32, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 124, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","ruler","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_%28page_4_%E5%A6%B2%E5%B7%B1%E5%AE%B3%E6%94%BF%29.jpg/1280px-%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_%28page_4_%E5%A6%B2%E5%B7%B1%E5%AE%B3%E6%94%BF%29.jpg","title":"The tradition's picture of him","desc":"An 18th-century painted leaf from an album of imperial examples: the king sits above with his favourite while courtiers argue below, and at the left a condemned man is driven along a greased beam laid over a fire.","credit":"Album leaf digitised by 书格, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B8%9D%E9%89%B4%E5%9B%BE%E8%AF%B4.Recueil_Historique_des_Principaux_Traits_de_la_Vie_des_Empereurs_Chinois.95%E5%B8%A7%E5%9B%BE.%E5%BD%A9%E7%BB%98%E5%86%8C%E9%A1%B5.%E7%BA%A618%E4%B8%96%E7%BA%AA_%28page_4_%E5%A6%B2%E5%B7%B1%E5%AE%B3%E6%94%BF%29.jpg","alt":"A Chinese album painting in soft colours: a robed king on a dais with a woman seated beside him, groups of officials on the terraces below, and at the far left a naked figure being made to walk a beam above flames."}},
{"id":"cnh-103","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The insider's account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> likens the dynasty to a man crossing a stream who can find neither ford nor bank, in a speech the Documents preserve.","questions":["The annals open their account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with eight hundred lords gathering at the ford of Meng and being sent home again by the man they had come to follow.","The last king's son was left in charge of the ancestral sacrifices and the Count of Wei was later given Song, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not extinguish its line."],"answer":"fall of the Shang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">At Meng ford</span><span class=\"dt-v\">800 lords, by the annals</span></div>","traditional":"商朝滅亡","hanzi":"商朝灭亡","pinyin":"Shāngcháo mièwáng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>fall of the Shang</b> is the end of its rule over the Central Plain, conventionally put at about 1046 BCE, when a coalition led by the Zhou of the west broke the last king's army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The transmitted account makes it an unravelling rather than a defeat: the Chief of the West grew as Di Xin's authority ebbed, and the lords went over to him in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A minister called Zu Yi warned him that Heaven was ending the mandate, having consulted the great tortoise without one favourable answer, and was told the king's destiny lay with Heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Documents keep an insider's view, a speech by the Count of Wei to two masters of the court: the founder's virtue undone by mad indulgence in spirits, nobles and officers breaking the laws unpunished.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The people were stealing the beasts set apart for the spirits and eating them, multitudes were starving, and Yin was like a man crossing a stream who can find neither ford nor bank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> King Wu of Zhou marched east to the ford of Meng, where eight hundred lords are said to have joined and urged him on; he answered that they did not yet know Heaven's decree, and turned back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The court then broke up: Bi Gan was killed for remonstrating, Jizi feigned madness and was gaoled, and the music masters carried the sacrificial vessels to the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The challengers had been preparing longer than that, since oracle bones from their own centres at Fengchu and Qijiacun carry inscriptions, some of them naming Shang ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the campaign came the Shang army gave way, and the victor is said to have opened the Stag Terrace treasury and the granary at Juqiao and given away what was in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The line was not extinguished: the dead king's son was set over the Shang lands to keep the ancestral offerings, and when he revolted he was killed and the Count of Wei given Song instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"fall of the Shang","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 202–8, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 122–23, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 136, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Rowan K. Flad, “Divination and Power: A Multiregional View of the Development of Oracle Bone Divination in Early China,” <i>Current Anthropology</i> 49, no. 3 (2008): 403–37, https://dash.harvard.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/7312037c-5623-6bd4-e053-0100007fdf3b/content. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chi Tzu,” https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","china","bronze age","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Western_Zhou_%22Li%22_Bronze_Gui.jpg/1280px-Western_Zhou_%22Li%22_Bronze_Gui.jpg","title":"Cast in the reign of the victor","desc":"The Li gui, a bronze food vessel on a square base made in King Wu's reign and found at Lintong in Shaanxi in 1976; the inscription inside it dates his attack on the Shang to a jiazi morning.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd of a piece in the National Museum of China, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Zhou_%22Li%22_Bronze_Gui.jpg","alt":"A dark green bronze bowl with two large handles shaped as beaked animal heads, standing on a deep square base, both bowl and base carved all over with dense scroll and mask ornament."}},
{"id":"cnh-104","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Advance six or seven steps and halt to dress the ranks, strike four blows and halt again: the drill order given at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> survives in the Documents.","questions":["A gold battle-axe in the left hand and a white ensign waved in the right open the Documents' account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, at the grey dawn of a jiazi day.","The defenders are said to have turned their weapons on the ranks behind them and opened a passage, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was decided by defection rather than by fighting."],"answer":"Battle of Muye","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 BCE, at dawn on a jiazi day</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shang strength</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700,000, in the annals</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou strength</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4,000 chariots, in the annals</span></div>","traditional":"牧野之戰","hanzi":"牧野之战","pinyin":"Mùyě zhī zhàn","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Muye</b> is the engagement in which the Zhou and their allies broke the last Shang king's army, fought in open country near his capital and conventionally dated to about 1046 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mu lay south of what is now Qi county in Henan, a stretch of open ground at no great distance from the city itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Documents put the action at the grey dawn of a jiazi day, with King Wu carrying a battle-axe yellow with gold in his left hand and a white ensign in his right, which he waved as he spoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He addressed hereditary rulers of friendly states, the ministers of Instruction, of War and of Works, captains of thousands and of hundreds, and the men of Yong, Shu, Qiang, Mao, Wei, Lu, Peng and Pu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those eight were peoples of the south and west, and their presence has been read as making the conquest an irruption of westerners, which his translator thought too much to claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The speech is a drill order as much as a summons: advance no more than six or seven steps and halt to dress the ranks, strike four to seven blows and halt again, and bear yourselves like tigers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ends by forbidding any rush on men coming over in submission, who were to be received instead to serve the western land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Grand Historian gives the allies four thousand chariots against seven hundred thousand men sent out by the Shang, a figure his own translator sets against Mencius, who allows King Wu three hundred chariots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the accounts agree on is that the defenders did not fight: the front ranks are said to have turned their weapons on those behind and opened a passage, and the king fled to burn himself alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The line the Documents give the day is that the blood flowed until it floated the pestles of the mortars, which later readers found hard to square with an army that had changed sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of Muye","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 131–32, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 136, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 231–34, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 207, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","warfare","history","china","bronze age"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Shang_Bronze_Qi_Axe.jpg/1280px-Shang_Bronze_Qi_Axe.jpg","title":"The weapon of the speech","desc":"A bronze axe of the Shang and Zhou period, cast with a socket for its haft; the address given before the battle opens with the king holding an axe yellow with gold and ends by ordering his men to raise their spears.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd of a piece in the Liaoning Museum, Shenyang, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_Bronze_Qi_Axe.jpg","alt":"A green-patinated bronze axe head seen from the side, its broad flaring blade set at right angles to a tubular socket, mounted on a clear stand against a pale wall."}},
{"id":"cnh-105","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Drunkenness is given as the chief cause of the Xia's fall and then of the Shang's, which is one sign that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a lesson rather than a report.","questions":["The oldest recorded doubt about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is Mencius, who said he believed two or three passages of the Completion of the War and no more.","20 of the 28 surviving Zhou documents are of undisputed genuineness, so even the text of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is unsettled in places."],"answer":"Zhou account of the Shang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou documents</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 of 38 survive</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Undisputed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 of those 28</span></div>","traditional":"周人對商的敘述","hanzi":"周人对商的叙述","pinyin":"Zhōurén duì Shāng de xùshù","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Zhou account of the Shang</b> is the picture of the dynasty left by the people who overthrew it, in the speeches and announcements the Zhou preserved, and the frame every later Chinese history of the dynasty inherited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its argument is a judgement rather than a report: Heaven charged Tang the Successful to set the Xia aside, kept the house of Yin while its kings made their virtue illustrious and kept up the sacrifices, and withdrew when the last did neither.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of the announcements puts that case to the conquered themselves, telling the officers of Yin that the charge laid on the Zhou had been to cut off Yin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same explanation serves twice, drunken debauchery being given as the chief cause of the Xia's fall and then of the Shang's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it leaves out is the Shang's own record, which never speaks of virtue or of mandate and divines instead about a sick tooth, a settlement to be built, the harvest and an enemy attacking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The texts carry the marks of their own transmission: the longest speech given to King Wu before the battle is a contested piece, and another form of it stands in its place in some editions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even a famous line may be an addition, since the four words for six or seven blows in the battle speech are missing from the quotations Han scholars made of the passage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The doubt is old: Mencius said it would be better to be without the Book of History than to give it entire credit, and of the Completion of the War he believed two or three passages only.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His objection was moral rather than textual: the most benevolent prince was fighting the most opposite of men, so how could the blood have floated the pestles of the mortars?<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One habit of it has outlived the account, the rhetoric that a woman brought a dynasty down, now read as a pattern of writing rather than a finding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhou account of the Shang","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 124, 171–72 and 197–98, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 479, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 232, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Wm. Theodore de Bary and Irene Bloom, comps., <i>Sources of Chinese Tradition</i>, 2nd ed., vol. 1 (New York: Columbia University Press, 1999), 8 and 11–12, https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/cup/oracle_bone_general.pdf. [Open access]","Sheri A. Lullo, review of <i>Women in Ancient China</i>, by Bret Hinsch, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 58, no. 2 (2019): 413–17, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/76794. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","china","bronze age","literature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Da_Yu_ding_inscription.jpg/1280px-Da_Yu_ding_inscription.jpg","title":"The Zhou putting it in bronze","desc":"A rubbing of the 291 characters cast inside the Da Yu ding, a Western Zhou vessel of King Kang's reign; among other things the king tells its owner that the Yin lost their kingdom because their officers gave themselves to wine.","credit":"Rubbing photographed by Wikimedia user Mountain, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Da_Yu_ding_inscription.jpg","alt":"A dense black-and-white rubbing of a bronze inscription, nineteen columns of white archaic Chinese characters on a black ground, split into two panels."}},
{"id":"cnh-106","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"An ode tells the last Shang king that the beacon of Yin is not far distant, and it is the fate of the house before his, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins.","questions":["Confucius traced his own descent to that house through the dukes of Song, which is one half of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an honoured ancestry rather than a warning.","The Yin took from the rites of the Xia and added to them, and the Zhou did the same to the Yin, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a link in a chain of precedent."],"answer":"Shang in later memory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Families named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7, from the founder's line</span></div>","traditional":"後世記憶中的商","hanzi":"后世记忆中的商","pinyin":"Hòushì jìyì zhōng de Shāng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang in later memory</b> is the place the dynasty kept in Chinese thought after it fell: a lineage that continued, a link in the chain of ritual precedent, and a standing warning to whoever held power next. Its line was not extinguished: a prince was installed in the state of Song to keep up the ancestral offerings, and the founding ancestor's descendants took the names of their fiefs, giving families called Yin, Lai, Song, Kongtong, Zhi, Beiyin and Muyi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That prince was the Viscount of Wei, a kinsman of the last king who had reproached him and then withdrawn from his court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Confucius traced his own descent to that house through the dukes of Song, though the more moderate genealogies take his ancestry back only to the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He also made the dynasty a link in an argument about continuity: the Yin followed the regulations of the Xia, and what they took away and added can be known, as the Zhou's changes to the Yin can be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The warning was older than the fall and had first been the Shang's own: an ode has King Wen tell the last Shang king that the beacon of Yin is not far distant, lying in the age of the last Xia sovereign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The point is that every house has the one before it to learn from, and it made the fallen Shang the example a later writer reached for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the memory ran in two registers, an honoured ancestry with rites of its own and a byword for the way a dynasty destroys itself. None of it rested on any writing of the dynasty's own, and modern work has had to treat the transmitted picture as evidence of the ages that produced it rather than of the age it describes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is why the argument about early China is an argument about documents, which are not value-free and whose biases and circumstances of making have to be weighed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang in later memory","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), 208, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shih King</i>, in <i>The Sacred Books of China</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 412–13, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects, The Great Learning and The Doctrine of the Mean</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), Analects II.23 and prolegomena, “Life of Confucius,” https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 844, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","china","bronze age","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Confucius_Tang_Dynasty%2C_black_print.jpg/1280px-Confucius_Tang_Dynasty%2C_black_print.jpg","title":"A descendant of the house","desc":"A rubbing taken from a stone engraving of Confucius after a Tang dynasty painting; he traced his own ancestry to the Shang royal house through the dukes of Song, and made the dynasty a link in his argument about ritual precedent.","credit":"Rubbing after a portrait attributed to Wu Daozi, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius_Tang_Dynasty,_black_print.jpg","alt":"A black stone rubbing of a bearded old man in long flowing robes standing with his hands folded, drawn in white line on a black ground, with Chinese characters above and beside him."}},
{"id":"cnh-107","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"More than nine-tenths of the pieces recovered carry no writing at all, which is the first thing to know about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a body of evidence.","questions":["Divination was no great part of the early Shang state's power, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begin where the practice became a bureaucracy rather than where the dynasty began.","One annotated corpus of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counts 93,652 characters and 21,667 further positions where a character is missing or cannot be read."],"answer":"oracle bone archives","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Uninscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 90% of specimens</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Image records</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly 240,000, in one database</span></div>","traditional":"甲骨檔案","hanzi":"甲骨档案","pinyin":"Jiǎgǔ dàng'àn","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>oracle bone archives</b> are the inscribed divination records recovered from the last Shang capital, the only writing the dynasty left, and the source on which most of what can be said about the late Shang state rests. They are the record of one institution in one place rather than of the Shang: burning bone to read the cracks is far older and was long various, and the few oracle bones from the earlier Shang centres suggest divination was no great part of that state's power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What changed at Anyang was elaboration and standardisation, which is the mark of a practice tied to a bureaucracy, since only a centralised administration can hold diviners to one complicated procedure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of what has come out of the ground carries no writing at all, uninscribed pieces making up more than nine-tenths of the known specimens and drawing far less attention than the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor does the practice stop where the dynasty does, since bones from the conquerors' own centres are inscribed too, some of them naming Shang ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Access to what survives is mediated: a database opened in 2019 gathers nearly 240,000 images of them and links the rubbings, facsimiles and transcriptions scattered across separate publications into single entries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The damage is counted along with the text, one annotated corpus of 10,077 pieces recording 93,652 characters and 21,667 further positions where a character is missing or illegible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even a clear patch may defeat a specialist on its own, becoming readable only when the surrounding evidence is brought to bear on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What is written there is also a highly selective record of the concerns of one class of people, so the archive falls silent wherever the court did not think a question worth putting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> And it is one esoteric body of evidence among several, beside bronze inscriptions, transmitted texts with their long commentarial traditions, and the archaeology, which is why a synthesis of the Shang is so hard to make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"oracle bone archives","sources":["Rowan K. Flad, “Divination and Power: A Multiregional View of the Development of Oracle Bone Divination in Early China,” <i>Current Anthropology</i> 49, no. 3 (2008): 403–37, https://dash.harvard.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/7312037c-5623-6bd4-e053-0100007fdf3b/content. [Open access]","Bang Li et al., “OBIMD: A Multi-modal Dataset for Contextual Interpretation of Oracle Bone Inscriptions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 13 (2026): 681, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13128845/. [Open access]","Wm. Theodore de Bary and Irene Bloom, comps., <i>Sources of Chinese Tradition</i>, 2nd ed., vol. 1 (New York: Columbia University Press, 1999), 8 and 11–12, https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/ps/cup/oracle_bone_general.pdf. [Open access]","Pengjie Wang et al., “An Open Dataset for Oracle Bone Character Recognition and Decipherment,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 11 (2024): 976, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11379903/. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","china","bronze age","writing"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Annotated_Oracle_Bone_Inscription_-_Heji_29990_and_30174_-_Rain_Divination.jpg/1280px-Annotated_Oracle_Bone_Inscription_-_Heji_29990_and_30174_-_Rain_Divination.jpg","title":"Two fragments and a join","desc":"A rubbing of a Shang oracle bone made up of two pieces joined by a modern scholar, annotated with the readings: five divinations about whether burning a cripple or making a clay dragon would bring rain.","credit":"Annotated rubbing by Quinlan718, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annotated_Oracle_Bone_Inscription_-_Heji_29990_and_30174_-_Rain_Divination.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white rubbing of a long narrow bone fragment covered in incised archaic characters, with white boxes around it giving each character's modern form and English gloss, and five numbered translations beneath."}},
{"id":"cnh-108","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Three separate sequences have to be pinned to one another before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can give a year at all: the king list, the inscriptions and the dug layers.","questions":["Nineteenth-century reference works put the last king's death in 1122 BCE, which is how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has moved in a century and a half of work.","A recent databank gathers 7,083 published radiocarbon dates, the material on which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now rests where the texts and the inscriptions run out."],"answer":"Shang chronology","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conventional span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Older dating</span><span class=\"dt-v\">fall in 1122 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Radiocarbon dates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7,083, in one databank</span></div>","traditional":"商代年代學","hanzi":"商代年代学","pinyin":"Shāngdài niándàixué","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Shang chronology</b> is the attempt to give the dynasty absolute years, and the familiar span of about 1600 to 1046 BCE is a construction rather than anything the record states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three separate sequences have to be joined to make one: the king list handed down in the texts, the order of the inscriptions themselves, and the layers dug at the sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each is well ordered within itself and none carries a year, so the whole is built by pinning the three to one another and to a handful of datable events.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why the answer has moved so far in a century and a half: the reference works of nineteenth-century sinology put the last king's death in 1122 BCE, and both of the standard English ones give that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The end of the dynasty is nonetheless the firmest part of it and the beginning the softest, since the early reigns are not fixed at all and 1600 is a round convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What has changed the material sequence is radiocarbon, which now supplies the absolute framework Chinese archaeology works in and whose first Chinese laboratory opened in 1965.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its results had been published piecemeal, in several languages and inside separate site reports, until a recent databank gathered 7,083 dates with their archaeological contexts and their measurement details.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A radiocarbon date belongs to a deposit rather than to a reign, and at the eighty to a hundred years of precision those laboratories once had it could bracket the conquest without coming near its year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relative chronology is accordingly the sound part of the subject, the order of reigns, of groups of diviners and of building phases being far better understood than the years attached to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A scheme of this kind is a working hypothesis rather than a result, which is why its dates are best read with the word about in front of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang chronology","sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Menghan Qiu, Linyao Du, Richard Staff, Ziyun Yan, Tingting Yan, Huihui Cao, Chris Gosden, Ruiliang Liu, and Guanghui Dong, “Chronology of Early China: A Radiocarbon Databank for Chinese Archaeology,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12 (2025): 1665, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12540757/. [Open access]","Xianglong Chen, “Radiocarbon Dating and Its Applications in Chinese Archeology: An Overview,” <i>Frontiers in Earth Science</i> 11 (2023): 1064717, https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/feart.2023.1064717/full. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chou Hsin,” https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 849, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","china","bronze age","science"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Oracle_bone_from_the_Couling-Chalfant_collection.jpg/1920px-Oracle_bone_from_the_Couling-Chalfant_collection.jpg","title":"A bone that dates itself","desc":"An inscribed shoulder bone from the Couling-Chalfant collection, recording a ten-day period and a lunar eclipse that can be placed in the calendar","credit":"British Library, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oracle_bone_from_the_Couling-Chalfant_collection.jpg","alt":"An inscribed shoulder bone from the Couling-Chalfant collection, recording a ten-day period and a lunar eclipse that can be placed in the calendar"}},
{"id":"cnh-109","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The first fact about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is that nothing reached the Central Plain from the steppe directly, a belt of mountains and plateaux lying between the two.","questions":["Carnelian beads buried in Sichuan came from sources over 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) north, which is the kind of traffic that places the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among its neighbours.","Raids from the north were long thought to have brought the chariot; the account of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> now puts several regional groups at the capital itself."],"answer":"Shang in the Bronze Age world","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Late Shang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carnelian moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 1,000 km (620 miles)</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Exchange sphere</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 1000 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"青銅時代世界中的商","hanzi":"青铜时代世界中的商","pinyin":"Qīngtóng shídài shìjiè zhōng de Shāng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang in the Bronze Age world</b> is the dynasty seen among its Eurasian neighbours, a setting that shows a state at the end of a chain of intermediaries rather than one meeting the steppe face to face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its contacts ran north for horses and south for metals, and the northern traffic had to cross a belt of high mountains and plateaux wrapped around the two great river basins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That belt reaches from Bohai Bay across northern China and south along the eastern edge of the Tibetan plateau to Yunnan, and everything moving either way went through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Metalworking reached it early in the 2nd millennium BCE as knives, tools and ornaments, and what the Central Plain then did with bronze, casting vessels for offerings to its ancestors, was its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old picture of raids from the north bringing the chariot and new weapons has been reworked: the argument now is that several regional groups were living at or near the capital itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The evidence is a way of burying the dead face down that runs through every level at Anyang, from sacrificial victims to chariot drivers, and isotopes showing that some of them had come from beyond the Shang centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Goods travelled as far as people did: carnelian beads buried in Sichuan between about 1200 and 1000 BCE were cut from stone quarried more than 1,000 kilometres (620 miles) to the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Beads from Gansu, Shaanxi and Beijing carry the same signature, so one exchange sphere seems to have joined the southern Mongolian plateau, the loess lands, the Central Plain and Sichuan between about 1500 and 1000 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even its signature institution was a local elaboration rather than an invention, since divining by heating bone is far older than the Shang and attested across East Asia and beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The comparison has a limit, since a Shang reconstructed from bone inscriptions, bronze epigraphy, texts and excavation is not the same kind of object as a kingdom known from its letters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang in the Bronze Age world","sources":["Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China's Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 136–39, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]","Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China's Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 135 and 161–63, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]","Meiting Yan et al., “Sourcing the Origins of Carnelian in Early Chinese Civilizations,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 7 (2026): e2524563123, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12913004/. [Open access]","Rowan K. Flad, “Divination and Power: A Multiregional View of the Development of Oracle Bone Divination in Early China,” <i>Current Anthropology</i> 49, no. 3 (2008): 403–37, https://dash.harvard.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/7312037c-5623-6bd4-e053-0100007fdf3b/content. [Open access]","Roderick Campbell, review of <i>China in the Early Bronze Age: Shang Civilization</i>, by Robert L. Thorp, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 313–21, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36672. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","archaeology","china","bronze age","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Shang_dynasty_Bronze_ibex-headed_knife_13th-11th_century.jpg/1280px-Shang_dynasty_Bronze_ibex-headed_knife_13th-11th_century.jpg","title":"A knife from the other end of the chain","desc":"A Shang bronze knife of the 13th to 11th centuries BCE with an ibex head worked into its pommel, a shape belonging to the herding country north of the Central Plain rather than to the workshops that cast the ritual vessels.","credit":"Photograph by Ismoon, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_dynasty_Bronze_ibex-headed_knife_13th-11th_century.jpg","alt":"A long curved bronze knife lying on a pale ground, its blade sweeping to a point and its handle ending in the small cast head of a horned goat."}},
{"id":"cnh-110","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Nothing the dynasty itself wrote is a narrative, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answers the questions a court asked and falls silent on almost everything else.","questions":["Because its writing is all divination and short bronze dedications, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can name the ancestors owed a sacrifice and says almost nothing about the countryside.","Every transmitted account of the dynasty was written by the Zhou or later, which is one reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to be read as a composite."],"answer":"Shang record","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Own writing</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1250 – 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First full account</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated years</span><span class=\"dt-v\">none before 841 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商代史料","hanzi":"商代史料","pinyin":"Shāngdài shǐliào","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Shang record</b> is everything the dynasty left behind together with everything written about it afterwards, and the two halves answer different questions and only rarely check each other. Its own writing runs to two kinds: the divination records kept at the royal court of its last capital, and the inscriptions on bronze, which are short and give a clan sign or name the ancestor a vessel was cast for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Neither is a narrative, so nothing survives that the Shang wrote about their own past, their law, their agreements or their accounts. What remains is a record made by a court and for it, and the questions it answers best are the court's own: which ancestor was due a sacrifice, whether the harvest would be good, whether a campaign should go out. Ordinary people enter it chiefly as quantities, in counts of captives, of victims and of men called up, and women almost only as consorts of the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The material record is far wider, but excavation has followed the court as well, and the one village of the period to be nearly completely dug was found by accident ahead of a canal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It turned out to make pottery on a scale its hundred-odd people could never have used and to buy its finest bone tools from workshops at the distant capital, and no divination mentions any of that. The third body of material is the transmitted texts, every one of them composed under the Zhou or later, so they explain the dynasty rather than report it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Grand Historian himself could give the Shang only a list of kings, saying his materials would not let him fix a year for anything before 841 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The tidy succession of three dynasties his account preserves has itself been argued to be a Zhou invention, which is why the safest statements about the Shang are those that two independent kinds of evidence support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Shang record","sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Haicheng Wang, review of <i>Violence, Kinship and the Early Chinese State: The Shang and Their World</i>, by Roderick Campbell, <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 59, no. 1 (2020): 226–32, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/104152. [Open access]","Yanfeng Hou, Roderick Campbell, Zhipeng Li, Yan Zhang, Suting Li, and Yuling He, “The Guandimiao Bone Assemblage (and What It Says about the Shang Economy),” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 57, no. 2 (2018): 281–310, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/72094. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 92–93 and 197–99, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","history","china","bronze age","writing"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/Late_Shang_Jiaguwen_about_horses.png","title":"One divination, read out","desc":"A rubbing of a late Shang bone beside a transcription of its characters and an English rendering: the king fed the horses in the stables.","credit":"Rubbing with modern transcription, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Shang_Jiaguwen_about_horses.png","alt":"On the left a black rubbing of an irregular bone fragment cut with pale archaic characters; on the right the same six characters drawn out singly in two rows with an English sentence set beneath them."}},
{"id":"cnh-111","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Because its kings kept their titles and sacrifices for five centuries after losing power, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlasted every other royal house in Chinese history.","questions":["The house that broke the Shang took its name from a plain below Mount Qi, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is conventionally dated from about 1046 to 256 BCE.","Chinese history acquires its first dated year, 841 BCE, in the middle of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and everything before that is reconstruction from later texts."],"answer":"Zhou dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Western Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eastern Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">770 – 256 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"周朝","hanzi":"周朝","pinyin":"Zhōu cháo","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Zhou dynasty</b> is the royal house that overthrew the Shang and then outlasted every other in Chinese history, conventionally dated from about 1046 to 256 BCE. It takes its name from a place: the grandfather of King Wen moved his people to the plain below Mount Qi in the Wei valley, and the small kingdom founded there was called Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its span divides at 771 BCE, when the western capital was overrun and the court moved east to the Luo valley, so the two halves are named Western and Eastern after where the king sat. Only the first of them was a monarchy in any working sense, and even that had given much away to its lords before it ended. Afterwards the kings kept their sacrifices, their titles and their calendar, and by the age of Confucius the sovereignty had become little more than an empty pageant while the states they had planted made war on one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the dynasty settled nonetheless outlived it, beginning with the argument that Heaven charges a house to rule and takes the charge back when it fails, which was worked out to explain the conquest and served every dynasty that followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The books that became the classics are Zhou books, the Documents and the Odes above all, or else reached under the Zhou the shape they have now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Confucius took its first reigns for his model of good government and said plainly that he followed Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also where Chinese history acquires dated years, since 841 BCE is the earliest year the Grand Historian would put a figure to and everything before it is reconstruction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The end came in 256 BCE, when the last king submitted to Qin 35 years before the empire, so the house had survived the loss of its power by more than five centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhou dynasty","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 209–13 and 316–18, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 319, 101–2, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 92–93 and 197–99, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 24, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["dynasty","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/The_Contending_States--Boundaries_of_350_BC.jpg/1280px-The_Contending_States--Boundaries_of_350_BC.jpg","title":"The dynasty in its long second half","desc":"A 1935 atlas map of China in 350 BCE, when the Zhou kings had held only a ritual primacy for four centuries and the seven great states shown here were fighting for the succession to it","credit":"Albert Herrmann, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Contending_States--Boundaries_of_350_BC.jpg","alt":"A 1935 atlas map of China in 350 BCE, when the Zhou kings had held only a ritual primacy for four centuries and the seven great states shown here were fighting for the succession to it"}},
{"id":"cnh-112","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Since its kings ruled from the Wei valley until 771 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named for where the court sat rather than for anything it did.","questions":["A vessel of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carries a long text where a Shang one carries a clan sign, which is what makes the period legible from its own documents.","Kin and allies planted over walled towns held the east for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an arrangement that worked while the centre could still reward and discipline them."],"answer":"Western Zhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First dated year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">841 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">771 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"西周","hanzi":"西周","pinyin":"Xī Zhōu","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Western Zhou</b> is the first half of the dynasty, running from the conquest of the Shang to 771 BCE, and it is the only stretch of it in which the kings actually governed. The name is a later convenience taken from geography: the royal seat lay west, on the Feng river near modern Xi'an, where King Wen is said to have founded the city that gave the site its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A second centre was built far to the east on the Luo so that the court could hold the country it had just taken, and those two places are the frame of everything the period did. Rule was delegated rather than administered: kinsmen and allies were installed over walled towns across the east and the north, each of them set over a population that was not Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those grants were then recorded in the bronzes the recipients cast, which is the second thing that makes the period legible where the Shang is not. <br><br> A Zhou vessel carries a long text where a Shang one carries a clan sign, and one hoard has yielded a basin whose 372 characters run through eight generations of a family serving the kings from King Wen onward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dynasty's own industry can now be measured too, since coring at the western capital found 29 kilns in a single workshop quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The arrangement held while the centre could reward and discipline, and it was visibly failing by the 9th century, when King Li was driven out in 841 BCE, the first year Chinese history can put a date to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 771 BCE the western capital fell to the Quanrong, called in by a disaffected lord, the king was killed and his heir moved the court east for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survived the move was the ritual order and the language of legitimacy; what did not was the power to command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Western Zhou","sources":["Zhikun Ma, Shu Liu, Xuan Yi, Liya Tang, Zhongyang Fu, Di Wang, and Qingli Sun, “Fuel Types and Use in Late Western Zhou (877–771 BCE) Industrial Contexts in Northwest China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 20166, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-71195-8. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 268–85, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Lai_pan_inscription.jpg","title":"Eight generations in one casting","desc":"A rubbing of the 372 characters cast inside a bronze basin found in a hoard in Shaanxi, in which a man named Lai sets out the service his family had given the Zhou kings from the beginning of the dynasty.","credit":"Rubbing from the Baoji Bronze Ware Museum collection, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lai_pan_inscription.jpg","alt":"A dense rubbing filling the frame: twenty-odd columns of pale archaic Chinese characters standing out against a mottled black ground, the edges of the cast panel visible around them."}},
{"id":"cnh-113","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Because his son conferred the royal title on him after his death, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is counted the founder of a dynasty he never reigned over and did not live to see.","questions":["Denounced to the last Shang ruler and gaoled for two years at Youli, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is said to have spent the captivity working on the judgements of the Changes.","The odes place <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on high and bright in heaven, because the Zhou needed the charge to rule to have settled on him before the conquest."],"answer":"King Wen","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th century BCE, dates disputed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Imprisoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 years, at Youli</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">before 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"周文王","hanzi":"周文王","pinyin":"Zhōu Wén Wáng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>King Wen</b> is the Zhou lord whom the dynasty treated as its founder, although he never reigned as king and did not live to see the Shang overthrown. His name was Chang, and in his own lifetime he was Chief of the West, a Shang office held over the western marches from his family's seat under Mount Qi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The traditional story has him denounced to the last Shang king by a rival marquis, gaoled for two years at Youli in what is now Henan, and released when a beautiful concubine and some fine horses were sent to buy him out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He occupied that captivity with the Changes, the same tradition says, which is why the judgements of that book are attributed to him still, and the same body of legend gives him the counsellor he found fishing on the Wei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The royal title came to him only after his death, conferred by his own son in an act of filial piety that turned a frontier lord into the founder of a house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the dynasty made of him afterwards matters more than any of that, because he is the man on whom Heaven's charge is said to have first settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ode that opens the greater odes has him on high and bright in heaven, and says the appointment lighted on an old country only recently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is the argument the Zhou needed, since a charge given to Wen makes his son's campaign the carrying out of a decision already taken rather than a revolt. The same ode warns the officers of the new dynasty that the great appointment is not easily kept and tells them to look at Yin as a beacon, so the founder is a caution as much as a claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those poems are ascribed to his own son, which is the difficulty with the whole portrait: he survives almost entirely inside the case his descendants were making for themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"King Wen","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), no. 2308, 876–77, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 299 and 377–80, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Yn, 201, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 257, 81–82, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","ruler","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_King_Wen_of_Zhou.jpg/1280px-Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_King_Wen_of_Zhou.jpg","title":"The founder as later ages drew him","desc":"A leaf from an album of imperial portraits made more than 2,500 years after he lived, showing him crowned and robed as a reigning king, which he never was.","credit":"Album leaf by an unknown Chinese artist, Philadelphia Museum of Art, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_King_Wen_of_Zhou.jpg","alt":"A painted half-length portrait on discoloured silk: a bearded man in a green and red crown hung with strings of white beads, wearing a pale robe with a dark collar."}},
{"id":"cnh-114","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Two years after his victory <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell gravely ill, and his brother prayed to be taken in his place and sealed the prayer in a metal-bound chest.","questions":["Rather than the fighting, the Documents dwell on what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did afterwards, freeing the Count of Ji and raising a mound over the grave of Bi Gan.","The heir left by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a boy of 13, so the conquest was only a few years old when an uncle began ruling in a child's name."],"answer":"King Wu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Conquest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned after it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 to 7 years, disputed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Heir aged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13 at accession</span></div>","traditional":"周武王","hanzi":"周武王","pinyin":"Zhōu Wǔ Wáng","translations":"","abstract":"<b>King Wu</b> is the Zhou ruler who actually overthrew the Shang, the son of King Wen and the first of his house to reign as king. His name was Fa; he carried on the operations his father had begun, and when the last Shang army broke he took the throne himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the Documents dwell on is not the fighting but what he did afterwards: he let the Count of Ji out of prison, raised a mound over the grave of Bi Gan, and bowed from the crossbar of his carriage at the gate of a worthy the old court had disgraced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The political settlement was cautious rather than triumphal, since the dead king's son was left over the Shang lands to keep the ancestral offerings up, with two of Wu's own brothers set to administer them alongside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His own seat was at Hao, beside the city his father had founded on the same river, and the pair of settlements stayed the western capital for the rest of the dynasty's ruling centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> He then died very soon, and that single fact shaped everything after him. Two years after the victory he fell gravely ill, and his brother the Duke of Zhou prayed to their ancestors to be taken in his place, sealing the written prayer in a chest bound with metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The king recovered but did not live long afterwards; one old reckoning allows him seven years from the victory, and the heir he left was a boy of 13.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is the hinge of the early dynasty, a conquest a few years old with a child on the throne and an uncle acting for him, and the revolt that followed came close to undoing it. The tradition remembers him as the arm rather than the head, the son who carried out a charge his father had received, which is why the two are almost always named in one breath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"King Wu","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), no. 2353, 891, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 136–37 and 151–55, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxciv, and Annales des Tcheou, 237 and 245–46, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Zhikun Ma, Shu Liu, Xuan Yi, Liya Tang, Zhongyang Fu, Di Wang, and Qingli Sun, “Fuel Types and Use in Late Western Zhou (877–771 BCE) Industrial Contexts in Northwest China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 20166, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-71195-8. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","ruler","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_King_Wu_of_Zhou.jpg/1280px-Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_King_Wu_of_Zhou.jpg","title":"The conqueror in court dress","desc":"A leaf from the same late album of imperial portraits, showing him in the flat-boarded royal cap with its curtains of beads, the dress of a reigning king rather than of a campaigning lord.","credit":"Album leaf by an unknown Chinese artist, Philadelphia Museum of Art, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_King_Wu_of_Zhou.jpg","alt":"A painted half-length portrait on stained silk: a bearded man under a flat black and red board cap fringed with hanging strings of white beads, in a blue-collared robe."}},
{"id":"cnh-115","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Having governed for seven years in his infant nephew's name, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> handed the government back, which is the act the later tradition weighs above all the rest.","questions":["Confucius measured his own decline by saying he no longer dreamed of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose home fief of Lu was the state Confucius was born in.","Slandered by his own younger brothers, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had to leave court until a chest bound with metal was opened and a prayer found inside it."],"answer":"Duke of Zhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Regency</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 years, from c. 1042 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt crushed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 3 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fief</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Lu, held by his eldest son</span></div>","traditional":"周公","hanzi":"周公","pinyin":"Zhōu Gōng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Duke of Zhou</b> is the brother of King Wu who governed for an infant nephew after the conquest and then handed the throne back, and no figure in Chinese tradition except Confucius stands higher.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His name was Dan, and he was the fourth son of King Wen; the title comes from the family's old appanage rather than from any office he held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He first appears in the Documents praying to his ancestors to be taken in his sick brother's place, a prayer he sealed in a chest bound with metal and told nobody about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the king died and left a boy of 13, he took the regency, and his own younger brothers put it about that he meant the throne for himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The suspicion reached the young king, and the duke left the court to live in the east until the chest was opened and what was in it read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What he did in those years is why the tradition made so much of him, beginning with a revolt of his brothers and the surviving Shang heir that took three years to put down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He then settled the conquered east, moving the officers of the fallen house to a new city on the Luo and addressing them in speeches the Documents still carry, a body of text his own house had every reason to preserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The regency is given as seven years, at the end of which he returned the government to the king, and that is the act later ages weighed above everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lu was granted to him for his own, but he stayed at court and sent his eldest son to hold it, so its city became the most distinguished of the eastern states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Confucius was born there, took the duke for the model of a minister, and measured his own decay by saying that he no longer dreamed of him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Duke of Zhou","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 151–55 and 197–99, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxciv, and Annales des Tcheou, 245–46, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I nos. 67 and 545, 20–21 and 169, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 59, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","ruler","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Zhou_Gong.jpg/1280px-Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Zhou_Gong.jpg","title":"A minister, not a king","desc":"A leaf from the same album, which gives him a plain cloth cap and a scholar's robe instead of a crown, the costume the tradition thought fitting for a man who governed and then handed the government back.","credit":"Album leaf by an unknown Chinese artist, Philadelphia Museum of Art, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Zhou_Gong.jpg","alt":"A painted half-length portrait on discoloured silk: an elderly bearded man in a soft blue-grey cap and a pale green robe crossed at the breast, looking to his left."}},
{"id":"cnh-116","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Fought across the whole newly conquered east, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took three years and very nearly ended the Zhou a few years after the conquest.","questions":["Two royal brothers set to watch the Shang heir joined him instead, which is where the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gets its name and why the regent had to fight his own family.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Zhou stopped garrisoning the east and started colonising it, planting the towns that became the great states of the next five centuries."],"answer":"Rebellion of the Three Guards","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Broke out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1042 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1039 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"三監之亂","hanzi":"三监之乱","pinyin":"Sānjiān zhī luàn","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Rebellion of the Three Guards</b> is the revolt that broke out when King Wu died, and it came close to ending the Zhou within a few years of their conquest. The Shang lands had been left to the dead king's own son so the ancestral offerings could go on, with two of King Wu's brothers set to administer them beside him and a third named among the rebels in some accounts, the overseers the revolt is named for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the Duke of Zhou took the regency for a nephew of 13, those brothers put it about that he wanted the throne for himself, and then joined the Shang heir against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tribes of the Huai river came in on the same side, so the fighting ran across the whole of the newly taken east rather than turning on one field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Documents keep the announcement made before the march, in which the young king says over and over that the shell has answered favourably and complains that his princes will not follow him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The war took three years, at the end of which the Shang heir and one royal brother were put to death and another was sent into exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The settlement was harsher than King Wu's: the Shang succession passed from the old royal line to a kinsman who had broken with the last king, seated at Song.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What remained of the Shang people went as a fief to a further brother, and the officers of the fallen court were moved bodily to a new city on the Luo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> This is where the eastern colonies come from, since the campaign turned a conquest into an occupation and the towns planted then grew into the great states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also where the Zhou argument about Heaven was worked out at length, because the announcements addressed to the defeated had to explain why the charge to rule had moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Rebellion of the Three Guards","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 237 and 245–46, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 294, 92, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 156–63, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","china","warfare","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/British_Museum_Kang_Hou_Gui_Front.jpg/1280px-British_Museum_Kang_Hou_Gui_Front.jpg","title":"The settlement, cast in bronze","desc":"A food vessel of the early Western Zhou whose inscription records the campaign against the Shang city and the charge that gave the marquis of Kang the territory of Wei, the fief carved out of the defeated lands.","credit":"Photograph by BabelStone, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_Museum_Kang_Hou_Gui_Front.jpg","alt":"A wide green-patinated bronze bowl on a low foot ring, its body ribbed with vertical flutes below a band of ornament, with a heavy loop handle on each side ending in an animal head."}},
{"id":"cnh-117","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Since a house that has lost it may be replaced, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a licence to rebel as well as a claim to rule, and every later dynasty used it.","questions":["The word behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means a command of the kind a lord issues to a subordinate, which is what makes a king the party being commanded.","The odes warn that the appointment of Heaven is not unchangeable, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is conditional in a way that a claim of descent or divinity is not."],"answer":"Mandate of Heaven","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Argued from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1040 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest texts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Documents and Odes, 11th–9th c. BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2nd century BCE, under the Han</span></div>","traditional":"天命","hanzi":"天命","pinyin":"Tiānmìng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Mandate of Heaven</b> is the Zhou doctrine that Heaven charges one house to rule and takes the charge away when that house fails, and it is the oldest political argument in Chinese history. The word behind it, <i>ming</i>, means a command, and in ordinary Zhou usage it is what a lord issues to a subordinate along with an office or a gift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Turned upward, the same word makes the king the party commanded: the charge is issued to him, so the whole of his authority is a grant that can be recalled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what separates it from a claim of descent or of divinity, since it is conditional and says as much out loud. The odes put the condition bluntly, saying the appointment of Heaven is not unchangeable and telling the officers of the new dynasty to look at Yin as a beacon, because a great appointment is not easily kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The Documents work the case out at length for the audience that most needed persuading, the officers of the house just destroyed. Heaven charged Tang to set the Xia aside, kept the Shang while its kings made their virtue illustrious, and withdrew when the last of them did not, so the Zhou had only done what had been done before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument is then turned on the losers' own records, which are said to show how Yin had superseded the appointment of Xia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because a house that has lost the charge may be replaced, the doctrine is a licence to rebel as much as a title to rule, and every dynasty that afterwards took power by force made the claim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its later life is untidy: Mencius has Heaven confer a throne without speaking, showing its will only through a man's conduct, and the neat version in which Heaven and man are linked is a much later reworking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Mandate of Heaven","sources":["David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 197–99 and 378–80, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 355, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Michael Puett, “Following the Commands of Heaven: The Notion of <i>Ming</i> in Early China,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 49–69, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","china","religion","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e6/Annotated_Edition_of_%E2%80%9CThe_Book_of_Documents%E2%80%9D_WDL11378.jpg","title":"The text the argument lives in","desc":"A page from an annotated edition of the <i>Book of Documents</i>, the collection of speeches and announcements in which the Zhou case for having received the charge to rule is set out at length.","credit":"Annotated edition attributed to Jin Lüxiang, digitised for the World Digital Library, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annotated_Edition_of_%E2%80%9CThe_Book_of_Documents%E2%80%9D_WDL11378.jpg","alt":"A page of a Chinese woodblock book: columns of characters in black ink on cream paper inside a ruled border, with a small red seal impression near the foot of one column."}},
{"id":"cnh-118","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Because the classics exchange it inside a single sentence with the personal name Di, it is hard to say whether <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a person at all.","questions":["The oldest Chinese dictionary explains the graph for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as one joined to great, meaning what is single, undivided and above everything else.","A charge withdrawn for misconduct needs a power that judges conduct, so the mandate argument only works if <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is moral as well as supreme."],"answer":"Tian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhou usage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Graph explained</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 CE, in the Shuowen</span></div>","traditional":"天","hanzi":"天","pinyin":"Tiān","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tian</b> is the Chinese word for the sky and for the power that governs the world from it, and it is the term the Zhou used where the Shang had spoken of Di.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest Chinese dictionary explains its graph as <i>one</i> joined to <i>great</i>, meaning what is single, undivided and great, while a 13th-century lexicographer read the top stroke instead as what is above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the classics the word runs freely between the two senses, and the vague term Heaven is exchanged, sometimes inside one sentence, with the personal names Di and Shangdi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That interchange is why it is so hard to say whether Tian is a person, and translators have divided ever since over whether to render it as God, as the Supreme Ruler, or not at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it is not is remote in the Shang manner, since the Zhou address it directly and expect it to be watching them. <br><br> It is also emphatically moral, or their central argument would not work, because a charge withdrawn for misconduct requires a power that judges conduct.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The odes make that judgement silent as well as certain, saying the doings of high Heaven have neither sound nor smell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The silence became a difficulty rather than a comfort, because a Heaven that does not announce itself has to be read off events instead. Mencius makes the reading explicit: Heaven does not speak, and showed its will for a ruler only through his personal conduct and his conduct of affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the early Confucian texts it is not reliably kind either, the same word covering misfortunes that fall on plainly good people, and reconciling that with a moral Heaven is work the later tradition had to do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tian","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), xxiii–xxv and 378–80, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 355, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Michael Puett, “Following the Commands of Heaven: The Notion of <i>Ming</i> in Early China,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 49–69, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","religion","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/70/Shang_Bronze_and_Oracle_Script.png","title":"The graph and where it comes from","desc":"Eight early Chinese characters drawn in their bronze and oracle-bone forms above their modern shapes; the first, at the top left, is tian, a standing figure whose head is marked as the thing the word is about.","credit":"Character drawings by Wikimedia user Pat457, modelled on surviving examples, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_Bronze_and_Oracle_Script.png","alt":"A chart of eight archaic Chinese characters in three rows each: a thick pictographic bronze form, a thinner oracle-bone form, and the modern character beneath, all in black on white."}},
{"id":"cnh-119","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Land and the people on it went to a kinsman who then ruled there in the king's name, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the arrangement the whole Western Zhou rested on.","questions":["A bronze from a cemetery outside modern Beijing records a king making one man ruler in Yan, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> attested by a document made at the time.","Mencius says the details of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were already lost in his own day, because the princes disliked the arrangement and had destroyed the records of it."],"answer":"fengjian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1040 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ranks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, on a threefold scale of land</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Records lost by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE</span></div>","traditional":"封建","hanzi":"封建","pinyin":"Fēngjiàn","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Fengjian</b> is the Zhou practice of granting land and the people living on it to a kinsman or an ally, who then ruled there in the king's name, and it is the arrangement the whole Western Zhou rested on. After the conquest the court sent members of the royal family out to hold strategic ground, so the grants were a campaign of colonisation rather than the division of an existing map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A grant was recorded on the bronze its holder cast, since the right to cast and inscribe a vessel was itself part of what the king was giving.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One such vessel from a cemetery outside modern Beijing carries the order making a man named Ke ruler in Yan, names the peoples he was to govern, and adds that he arrived and took in its land and its officials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another records the land and people of Yi handed to a lord who brought his own men out from the centre, so the two show different ways of stocking a new state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Documents say the nobles were arranged in five orders with territories on a threefold scale, and Mencius gives the orders as <i>gong</i>, <i>hou</i>, <i>bo</i>, <i>zi</i> and <i>nan</i>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their usual English equivalents are a convenience and no more, and the translator who introduced them warned that duke, marquis, earl, viscount and baron do not answer to the Chinese ranks at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mencius also says the particulars were already lost in his own day, because the princes disliked the arrangement and had made away with the records of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A holder whose domain fell below the smallest size could not appear before the king himself and was attached to a larger neighbour instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The inscriptions distinguish lords of the royal lineage from other affiliated rulers, and that is the point of the system: a grant was a kinship tie as much as a territory, and it decayed as the kinship thinned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"fengjian","sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 136–37, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 373–74, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","institution","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Liulihe_bronze_zun.jpg/1280px-Liulihe_bronze_zun.jpg","title":"A vessel from a planted state","desc":"An inscribed wine vessel of the early Western Zhou dug up at Liulihe near Beijing, the cemetery of the state of Yan, which was founded when a Zhou king made one of his own kin its ruler.","credit":"Photograph by BabelStone, CC BY-SA 3.0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liulihe_bronze_zun.jpg","alt":"A tall green-patinated bronze vessel in a museum case, flaring widely at the mouth and foot, its three registers covered in dense ornament broken by upright flanges."}},
{"id":"cnh-120","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Excavation at Liulihe shows that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took up local vessel shapes rather than simply imposing the metropolitan style on the north.","questions":["Surveys of more than 20 of them found the Zhou forming the top layer of societies that stratified around them, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chiefly exported.","Planted to hold the conquered east for the kings, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the states that fought one another once the kings could no longer command."],"answer":"Zhou colonies in the east","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Planted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1039 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Surveyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 20 states</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Independent by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">770 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"東方封國","hanzi":"东方封国","pinyin":"Dōngfāng fēngguó","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Zhou colonies in the east</b> are the walled towns the dynasty planted across the conquered country after the revolt of its overseers, each with a lord of the royal house set over a population that was not Zhou. Sending kinsmen out to hold strategic ground was a swift campaign rather than a policy worked out over generations, and the grants that founded the towns were recorded on the bronzes their holders cast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What excavation shows is that the settlers did not simply impose themselves: at Liulihe near modern Beijing, the cemetery of the state of Yan, local vessel shapes were taken up and worked into a style belonging to the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The richer graves there carry the mixture plainly, with bronzes of the central-plain kind buried beside gold earrings and bracelets of the northern sort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One of them also shows the other half of the arrangement, holding a set of seven ding of the size and number a high rank was entitled to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Rank, then, was the thing the colonies most reliably exported, rather than a whole way of living, and the graded sets buried in their cemeteries are how that shows up in the ground. Surveys of more than 20 of these states found the Zhou forming the upper level of a society that stratified around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inscriptions keep the distinction the court itself cared about, between rulers who belonged to the royal lineage and rulers of states merely affiliated with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Distance did the rest, since a state as far from the Wei valley as Yan developed a material culture and a set of political attachments of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Documents preserve the charges given to two of these lords, and the towns those charges founded outlasted the dynasty that planted them by five centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhou colonies in the east","sources":["Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 161–72, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 245–46, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty","archaeology"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Liulihe_bronze_ding_2.jpg/1280px-Liulihe_bronze_ding_2.jpg","title":"A colony's own bronze","desc":"A tripod cauldron of the early Western Zhou dug up at Liulihe near Beijing, the cemetery of the state of Yan, one of the towns planted in the north to hold the conquered country.","credit":"Photograph by BabelStone, CC BY-SA 3.0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liulihe_bronze_ding_2.jpg","alt":"A dark bronze cauldron on three tapering legs, its round body banded with dense scrolled ornament and two upright loop handles rising from the rim, in a museum case."}},
{"id":"cnh-121","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The site of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not chosen but divined, the shell being consulted over three stretches of ground before it settled on the Luo.","questions":["Because the officers of the defeated Shang were moved there in a body, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built to hold the country the conquest had won rather than to house a court.","When the western capital fell in 771 BCE the heir moved east to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which his ancestors had built and which served the dynasty for its remaining five centuries."],"answer":"Chengzhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1036 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sole capital from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">770 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on the Luo, near modern Luoyang</span></div>","traditional":"成周","hanzi":"成周","pinyin":"Chéngzhōu","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Chengzhou</b> is the eastern capital the Zhou built on the Luo river, near what is now Luoyang, to hold the country their conquest had won them. King Wu is said to have conceived the city and died too soon to build it, so the work fell to his brother during the minority of the next king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site was not chosen so much as divined: the shell was consulted over the ground north of the Yellow River by the Li water, then east of the Jian and west of the Chan, and twice the answer came back pointing at the Luo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regent then sent his young sovereign a map together with the results of those divinations, which is the sort of detail the Documents keep and nothing else does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Work began in the third month as the moon waned, with people drawn in from five domains of the royal lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the place was for is stated just as plainly, since the officers of the defeated house were moved there in a body and lectured on the spot about why their dynasty had fallen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was a second seat of government as well, one great officer holding the country west of a place called Shen while the regent held everything east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The announcement composed there is addressed to the king himself and turns on the virtue of reverence, telling him to examine the Xia and the Yin and see that both lost the charge for want of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The court nonetheless stayed in the west, so for two and a half centuries the dynasty kept two capitals and used the eastern one to reach ground the western one could not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That arrangement paid for itself in 771 BCE, when the west was lost and the heir simply moved to the city his ancestors had already built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Chengzhou","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 188–91, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), 420–34, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 246–48 and 285–87, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Zhikun Ma, Shu Liu, Xuan Yi, Liya Tang, Zhongyang Fu, Di Wang, and Qingli Sun, “Fuel Types and Use in Late Western Zhou (877–771 BCE) Industrial Contexts in Northwest China,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14, no. 1 (2024): 20166, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-71195-8. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 67, 20–21, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["city","place","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Western_Zhou_Bronze_Gui%2C_Luoyang_-_3.jpg/1280px-Western_Zhou_Bronze_Gui%2C_Luoyang_-_3.jpg","title":"Cast at the eastern capital","desc":"A Western Zhou food vessel excavated at Luoyang, the ground on which the dynasty built its eastern city after divining three stretches of river valley for the site.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, released under CC0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Zhou_Bronze_Gui%2C_Luoyang_-_3.jpg","alt":"A green and grey bronze bowl on a ring foot, its middle ribbed with vertical flutes between two ornamented bands, with a heavy handle on each side."}},
{"id":"cnh-122","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Performed in the ancestral temple before the royal dead, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the setting for nearly everything the dynasty chose to write down.","questions":["Since what a man might own and use was fixed by his standing, the vessels of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stated a rank as much as they served a sacrifice.","A charge given to a lord was part of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a private transaction, because the ancestors were present as witnesses to it."],"answer":"Zhou royal ritual","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ding allowance</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 for the king, 7, 5, 3 or 1 below</span></div>","traditional":"周代王室禮儀","hanzi":"周代王室礼仪","pinyin":"Zhōudài wángshì lǐyí","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zhou royal ritual</b> is what the king and his court did in the ancestral temple, and it is the setting for very nearly everything the dynasty chose to write down. Its core was the offering of food and drink to the royal dead in bronze vessels cast for the purpose and inscribed with the name of the ancestor they were made for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Rank was expressed through those vessels, since the number and type a man might own were fixed by his standing, with the largest allowance kept for the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bells belonged with them, the Chinese phrase for the whole apparatus pairing ritual with music, and sets of chime bells are found buried with the vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Women had a place in it too: vessels were given to noblewomen as dowry or by a husband, and dedicated by sons to their dead mothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The other great ceremony was the appointment, in which the king gave an officer a charge and a gift, and it was performed in the temple in front of the ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is what raised a grant above a transaction, since the dead were witnesses to it and the recipient's proper answer was to cast a vessel saying so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The gift mattered as much as the words, because a ruler who is the focus of distribution keeps his standing only by continuing to distribute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The hymns composed for the temple say what those present were meant to feel, having King Wen ascend and descend on the left and the right of God.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The dependence runs the other way as well: where the ritual stops the record stops, and court life outside the temple is nearly invisible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhou royal ritual","sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Xin Yu, Hailin Liu, Zhe Zhang, Huiqiu Shao, Chunxue Wang, and Dong Wei, “Sacrificial Animals as Evidence for the Disintegration of the Ritual and Music System: A Case Study of the Zhuolu Site (790–410 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0318926, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0318926. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 377–78, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","religion","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Guimet-Bianzhong.jpg/1280px-Guimet-Bianzhong.jpg","title":"The other half of the apparatus","desc":"A graded set of Chinese chime bells with a single large bell beside it; the phrase for the whole system of court observance pairs ritual with music, and bells were buried with the vessels.","credit":"Photograph by Popolon, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guimet-Bianzhong.jpg","alt":"A museum case holding a row of bronze bells of decreasing size raised on thin stands, with one much larger studded bell standing to the right of them."}},
{"id":"cnh-123","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Only about 60 of the roughly 10,000 known <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry a full date, which is why a year in this period is argued over rather than looked up.","questions":["A typical one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has four parts: who made the vessel, for whom, what it is called, and a wish that it be used for ten thousand years.","Because the characters were written into the clay of the mould before the metal went in, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stand raised rather than cut."],"answer":"Western Zhou bronze inscriptions","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cast</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Known</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 10,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fully dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 60 of them</span></div>","traditional":"西周金文","hanzi":"西周金文","pinyin":"Xī Zhōu jīnwén","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Western Zhou bronze inscriptions</b> are the texts cast into ritual vessels between the conquest of the Shang and 771 BCE, and they are the only contemporary written record the period has. They are cast rather than engraved, the characters written into the clay of the mould before the metal went in, and they stand inside the vessel or under its lid where nobody handling it would see them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A typical one has four parts: who made the vessel, for whom it was made, what the vessel is called, and a formula wishing it long use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One from a hoard in Shaanxi does all four in 20 characters, a man named Lai making a treasured vessel for his high ancestor and asking for sons of sons and grandsons of grandsons to use it for ten thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another, of 14 characters, has a king making a pair of vessels for a woman, which is one of the ways a royal marriage enters the record at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What they are not is history writing, since an inscription commemorates an occasion for the ancestors and passes over whatever would not flatter the man paying for the casting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same language of command serves a lord's appointment over a whole new state and a gift of a few shells, so the form tells a reader less than its confidence suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are nonetheless the backbone of the period's chronology, because some carry a date in four elements: the year of a reign, the month, the phase of the moon and the day in the sixty-day cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only about 60 of the roughly 10,000 known carry all four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reign is usually unnamed as well, so which king an inscription belongs to has to be argued from the vessel's shape and the people it names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Western Zhou bronze inscriptions","sources":["Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["object","writing","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/20260520_Inscription_inside_a_Ding_of_the_Guo_Ji_Ding_Set.jpg/1280px-20260520_Inscription_inside_a_Ding_of_the_Guo_Ji_Ding_Set.jpg","title":"Where the writing actually is","desc":"The inscription inside a cauldron from a set cast for a lord of the state of Guo, standing on the inner wall where nobody using the vessel would have seen it.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20260520_Inscription_inside_a_Ding_of_the_Guo_Ji_Ding_Set.jpg","alt":"Looking down into a corroded bronze bowl, several short columns of archaic Chinese characters standing in relief on the curved inner wall below the rim."}},
{"id":"cnh-124","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"By fixing nine ding for the king and seven, five, three or one below him, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned a burial assemblage into a statement of rank.","questions":["Archaeology puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the late 10th or 9th century BCE, roughly a century and a half after the conquest of the Shang.","Scholars who date the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> read it as a consolidation, yet vessels of the same period show unorthodox shapes that argue for continued freedom."],"answer":"Zhou ritual reform","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Solidified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 950 – 800 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King's allowance</span><span class=\"dt-v\">9 ding</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ranks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, on a descending scale</span></div>","traditional":"周代禮制改革","hanzi":"周代礼制改革","pinyin":"Zhōudài lǐzhì gǎigé","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Zhou ritual reform</b> is the reorganisation of bronze ritual assemblages that scholars find in the middle and later Western Zhou, when what a man might own stopped being a matter of wealth and became a matter of declared rank. Its clearest mark is a sumptuary scale built on the ding, the tripod cauldron: the king was entitled to nine, the rank below him to seven, the next to five and the next to three or one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grain servers, liquor containers, water basins and chime bells were specified by number in the same way, and were always fewer than the ding allowance they hung beneath.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Burials bear the scale out: a tomb in the state of Yan of the late 11th century holds six ding with two grain servers and a scatter of drinking vessels, while later graves show the tidier matched sets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The system is dated by the archaeology to the late 10th or 9th century BCE, roughly a century and a half after the conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Calling it a reform is a modern judgement, and the term belongs to a particular reading of the period as one of ritual consolidation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The same reading has been qualified from inside the evidence, because vessels made at the centre in exactly those years take unorthodox shapes with bird-shaped lids and human legs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those pieces belong to aristocratic lineages rather than to the royal house, and their makers were working while the kings were losing their grip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> So the tightening of the rules and the loosening of the politics run together, which is the awkward part of the picture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What came after settles the direction: by the Eastern Zhou the graves of lords carry animal offerings and vessel sets no rule would have allowed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhou ritual reform","sources":["Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Xin Yu, Hailin Liu, Zhe Zhang, Huiqiu Shao, Chunxue Wang, and Dong Wei, “Sacrificial Animals as Evidence for the Disintegration of the Ritual and Music System: A Case Study of the Zhuolu Site (790–410 BC),” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 2 (2025): e0318926, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0318926. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 285–90, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","practice","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/20260408_Bronze_Ding_Set%2C_Gui_Set%2C_and_Li_with_Inscriptions_of_Guo_Ji.jpg/1280px-20260408_Bronze_Ding_Set%2C_Gui_Set%2C_and_Li_with_Inscriptions_of_Guo_Ji.jpg","title":"A rank, laid out","desc":"Matched sets of cauldrons and grain servers cast for one lord of the state of Guo and displayed as they would have been used, the number of each fixed by the standing of the man entitled to them.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20260408_Bronze_Ding_Set%2C_Gui_Set%2C_and_Li_with_Inscriptions_of_Guo_Ji.jpg","alt":"A long museum case holding a row of bronze tripod cauldrons of stepped sizes on the upper shelf and a row of lidded round bowls beneath them, with explanatory panels behind."}},
{"id":"cnh-125","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The odes call the senior line the root and the junior lines the branches, which is the image behind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the reason the two were never equal.","questions":["Because a man could not marry inside his own surname, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bound the Zhou house to the lineages it took its queens from as tightly as to its own kin.","One vessel from a Shaanxi hoard traces eight generations of service to the kings, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> written out by a family that wanted the depth on record."],"answer":"zongfa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 1046 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal surname</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Ji, 1 of several ruling clans</span></div>","traditional":"宗法","hanzi":"宗法","pinyin":"Zōngfǎ","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Zongfa</b> is the Zhou ordering of a noble house by descent, in which one senior line carries the name and the ancestral offerings while the sons who do not inherit found junior lines beneath it. The odes give the image the system is named for, calling the eldest sons by the recognised queen the root and the other sons the branches of one great tree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not a metaphor about families only, because the branches were the chief nobles of the kingdom and the political order was laid over the genealogical one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A grant of land was therefore a statement about kinship as much as about territory, and inscriptions are careful to say whether a lord belonged to the royal lineage or merely to a state affiliated with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The system's memory ran deep: one vessel from a Shaanxi hoard sets out eight generations of a family's service to the kings, beginning with an ancestor who served the dynasty's founders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Marriage was the other half of it, since a man could not take a wife of his own surname and the Zhou royal house therefore married outward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The kings' wives came most often from one particular lineage, and a king's mother could be a daughter of a small eastern state, which tied the house to places its armies never had to reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Rank followed the same logic, so the five orders of nobility and the sizes of their domains were an expression of standing rather than a measure of land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the arrangement could not do was survive its own arithmetic, since every generation made more branches and each was further from the trunk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the time the kings had lost their power the great lineages were acting for themselves, which is the ordinary end of a system that made kinship do the work of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"zongfa","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 378–79, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 373–74, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Elizabeth Childs-Johnson, “Big Ding 鼎 and China Power: Divine Authority and Legitimacy,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 2 (2012): 165–220, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36666. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","institution","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"]},
{"id":"cnh-126","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"A scholar who had hidden his tablets in a wall recovered 29 documents after the Han came in, and that salvage is the core of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> we read.","questions":["Since a chapter is usually one address given on one occasion, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> hands a reader the argument a ruler made rather than an account of what happened.","A second set of chapters said to come from a wall in the Confucian family house has kept the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under suspicion since the Song."],"answer":"Book of Documents","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Composed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">11th – 4th century BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Recovered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 documents, after 191 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cut in stone</span><span class=\"dt-v\">168 – 189 CE</span></div>","traditional":"尚書","hanzi":"尚书","pinyin":"Shàngshū","translations":"","abstract":"The <b><i>Book of Documents</i></b> is the collection of speeches, charges and announcements that Chinese tradition treats as its oldest history, most of them put in the mouths of Zhou rulers and their ministers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is not a narrative: a chapter is usually one address delivered on one occasion, so what a reader gets is the case a ruler made rather than an account of what he did. Its survival was a near thing, since the Qin edict against the ancient books put it under a ban and the sack of the capital in 206 BCE burned the palace copies over three months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One scholar of the fallen dynasty had hidden his tablets in a wall, and coming back for them after the Han were established he recovered 29 documents, which became the text everyone afterwards worked from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That version was written in the script of its own day and cut into the stone tablets of an emperor of the late 2nd century CE, which is why it is called the modern text.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second set of chapters has a different story, having reportedly been found in the wall of the Confucian family's house in a form of the character almost nobody could still read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those extra chapters have been under suspicion since the Song, when a sceptical criticism grew up that judged a document's genuineness chiefly by its style.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Results reached that way vary with the critic, which is the objection the standard English translator raised while defending the disputed books on the history of their transmission instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chapters nobody doubts are the ones the Zhou argument lives in, and they are also the hardest to construe, the Announcements being the most contorted books in the collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They are used as evidence for the period's chronology as well, though the dates in them were long ago tangled with the reign lengths later compilers assigned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Book of Documents","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 5–13, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, 1–44, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxciv–cxcvi, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["text","literature","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Shujing_NCL_1.png/1280px-Shujing_NCL_1.png","title":"An old copy, damaged","desc":"A double page from an early printed edition, the text ruled into columns with commentary set in smaller characters between them, and collectors' seals stamped in red down the outer margin.","credit":"Scan of a National Central Library copy, public domain via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shujing_NCL_1.png","alt":"An open Chinese book showing two pages of columns of black characters inside ruled borders, the paper stained and eaten away along the top edge, with several square red seal impressions."}},
{"id":"cnh-127","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Holding 305 pieces and the bare titles of six more, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the oldest body of Chinese verse there is.","questions":["The four parts of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> run from short songs of the feudal states to hymns for the temple, setting a harvest complaint beside a dynastic hymn.","The Grand Historian says Confucius cut the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> down from more than 3,000 old poems, a claim later editors declined to endorse."],"answer":"Classic of Poetry","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Pieces</span><span class=\"dt-v\">305, plus 6 titles</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 Shang odes, 17th – 12th c. BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Latest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">606 – 586 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"詩經","hanzi":"诗经","pinyin":"Shījīng","translations":"","abstract":"The <b><i>Classic of Poetry</i></b> is the oldest body of Chinese verse, 305 pieces together with the bare titles of six more that were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its latest poems belong to a reign of the early 6th century BCE and its oldest are a group of five sacrificial odes assigned to the Shang, so the collection spans most of a thousand years unevenly, with reigns that left nothing at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into four parts: 160 short pieces gathered from the feudal states, 74 sung when lords came to the royal court, a set of greater odes on the dynasty's own founding, and hymns for the ancestral temple.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That arrangement puts a complaint about the harvest a few pages from a hymn to King Wen, which is why the book is read as evidence for ordinary life as well as for the court.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rhyme runs through nearly all of it, being in Chinese judgement a necessary part of what makes verse verse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How the 305 were chosen is disputed and always has been. The Grand Historian says the old poems came to more than 3,000, that Confucius threw out the repetitions and kept what served propriety and righteousness, and that he sang the survivors over to his lute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The great Song commentator declined to endorse that account, holding only that the poems still extant were put in order once collecting had stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confucius himself, in the <i>Analects</i>, sums the whole collection up in a phrase he takes from one of its own odes, that its design is to have no depraved thoughts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The very first piece celebrates a prince's union with a gentle consort, and the two words it is titled by came to stand for virtuous courtship long after anyone read them as a poem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Classic of Poetry","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 275–80 and 298–99, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, ccxv–ccxvii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1893), 10, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 295, 92–93, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["text","literature","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/%E5%8D%97%E5%AE%8B%E5%A4%AA%E5%AD%A6%E7%9F%B3%E7%BB%8F_%E8%AF%97%E7%BB%8F_%E5%85%B3%E9%9B%8E.jpg","title":"The first ode, cut in stone","desc":"A rubbing from the Southern Song stone classics carrying the opening poem of the collection, the piece about a prince and his consort that later ages made a byword for virtuous courtship.","credit":"Photograph by Wikimedia user 三猎, CC BY-SA 4.0 via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%8D%97%E5%AE%8B%E5%A4%AA%E5%AD%A6%E7%9F%B3%E7%BB%8F_%E8%AF%97%E7%BB%8F_%E5%85%B3%E9%9B%8E.jpg","alt":"A narrow vertical rubbing, pale characters standing out of a black ground in two ragged columns, the stone's chipped edges visible along the sides."}},
{"id":"cnh-128","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Four terms for the phases of the moon give the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> its finest division, and whether they name periods or fixed days is still argued.","questions":["An intercalary month was slipped in at the year's end to keep the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in step with the seasons, because twelve lunations fall short of a solar year.","A recorded double sunrise at Zheng, read as an eclipse, fixes one reign to 899 BCE and so anchors the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at a point that can be calculated."],"answer":"Zhou reckoning of the year","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Day cycle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60, of stems and branches</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anchor date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">21 April 899 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"周曆","hanzi":"周历","pinyin":"Zhōu lì","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Zhou reckoning of the year</b> is the calendar the dynasty dated its documents by, and it is lunisolar: the months follow the moon and an extra month is slipped in at the year's end to keep them in step with the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Days were named by the cycle of 60 the Shang had used, a pairing of ten signs with twelve that was not applied to numbering years until the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between the day and the month sit four terms for the phases of the moon, translated as the first auspiciousness, after the growing brightness, after the full moon and after the dying brightness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Documents date an event by the same reckoning, putting the founding of the eastern capital in the third month, when the moon began to wane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whether those named stretches of the lunation or particular fixed days has been argued both ways, and a study of the late inscriptions came down, tentatively, for stretches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The year was counted from a king's accession, with no firm rule about whether the year he died or the year after it was his successor's first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> None of this was tidy while it was in use, either: early in the dynasty a month began at the first sighting of the new moon and late in it a day earlier, and the first month of the year usually held the winter solstice but sometimes did not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An inscription giving the reign year, the month, the moon phase and the day pins a vessel to a real date, and about 60 of some 10,000 inscriptions do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those are checked against the sky: an entry recording that the sun rose twice at Zheng is read as an eclipse just after sunrise, which calculation places on 21 April 899 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A vessel dated to the first year of that same king, in the sixth month after the full moon, then falls on the 19th day, exactly where the moon phase says it should.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Zhou reckoning of the year","sources":["Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxciii–cxcvi, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 188–89, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 67–97, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 300, 348–49, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","science","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"]},
{"id":"cnh-129","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> eight families were said to farm their own hundred mu apiece and to work a ninth square in common for the lord.","questions":["Everything known about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from a philosopher writing centuries later who was recommending it to a prince, not describing what he had seen.","The whole textual case for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the Zhou rests on one couplet of verse asking the rain to fall on the public field first."],"answer":"well-field system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Attributed to</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the Zhou, c. 1046 – 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Described</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 300 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 square li, 900 mu</span></div>","traditional":"井田制","hanzi":"井田制","pinyin":"Jǐngtián zhì","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>well-field system</b> is a scheme of landholding attributed to the Zhou in which a square of ground was divided into nine, eight families held one square each and worked the ninth together for the lord. Its name comes from the shape: the character for a well is a grid of nine squares, so the arrangement is drawn by the word for it. A square li covered those nine squares and contained 900 mu, giving each family a private hundred, and the public work in the middle square had to be finished before anyone attended to his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The surplus males of a household were allotted 25 mu apiece, and families sharing a square were expected to keep watch together, help one another in sickness and not leave the district.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a description of a whole society as much as of a field, which is the first hint about what kind of document it comes from. <br><br> Everything known about it comes from one passage, written in the 4th century BCE by a philosopher advising a small state on land policy rather than reporting what he had seen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He says so himself, ending with the remark that these are the great outlines and that adapting them is for the prince and his minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His evidence that the Zhou had used it is a single couplet of verse asking the rain to fall on the public field and then on the private ones, from which the existence of a public field is inferred.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The period's own documents do not describe ground this way, a grant of land being recorded by the people and officials who came with it rather than by any grid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the scheme did fit was the story the house told about itself, since the Zhou traced their descent to a Minister of Agriculture remembered as Prince Millet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"well-field system","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 34 and 243–45, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 322–23, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yitzchak Jaffe, “Materializing Identity—A Statistical Analysis of the Western Zhou Liulihe Cemetery,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 51, no. 1 (2012): 47–67, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/36658. [Open access]","David Schaberg, “Command and the Content of Tradition,” in <i>The Magnitude of Ming: Command, Allotment, and Fate in Chinese Culture</i>, ed. Christopher Lupke (Honolulu: University of Hawai‘i Press, 2005), 23–48, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/23071. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 307, 96, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["concept","institution","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"]},
{"id":"cnh-130","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The unit that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was organised around was the four-horse carriage with its crew of three, and a body of foot marched with each one to protect it.","questions":["The infantry of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were husbandmen called off their fields, which is why a campaign opening in the sixth month was by its date alone an announcement of emergency.","A ritual classic allots a hundred men to every carriage in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where a surviving ode counts only thirty thousand foot to a thousand chariots."],"answer":"Zhou warfare","answerText":"Zhou warfare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Practised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chariot crew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 armoured men</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Royal host</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 shi, of 2,500 men each</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Zhou warfare</b> is the campaigning of the Western Zhou king and his lords, and it was organised around the war chariot rather than the man on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vehicle was not a Zhou invention but a Eurasian one that reached China from the north in Shang times, and it was under the Zhou that a team of four abreast became usual.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each carriage carried three armoured men standing: in an ordinary one the driver held the middle, a spearman stood on his right and an archer on his left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the commander's own carriage the driver moved to the left, so that the general could take the centre and beat the drum that drove the ranks forward. A body of foot marched with each carriage to protect it, and the word for chariot came to stand for the whole party, much as lance once did in medieval Europe. <br><br> How many men that made is the oldest argument about the subject: a ritual classic allots each chariot three warriors, 25 footmen at the horses and 72 light-armed men behind, where an ode counts 30,000 foot to a thousand chariots.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Jesuit who first summarised the odes and the missionary who corrected him both thought the ritual figures far too high, and the establishment on paper was in any case never full. The larger formations have names: a shi of 2,500 men attended the king in the field and a lü of 500 a great officer, and the six shi of the odes are the host that followed the king himself. Under the chariots the infantry were husbandmen taken from their labours, so campaigning belonged to the months after harvest, and an ode that opens in the sixth month is announcing an emergency by its date alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A nobleman went out with his ancestors as well as his weapons: scholars disagree about a word cast into certain bronzes, one reading making them portable vessels for sacrifices owed while away on campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","warfare","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 1, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), Prolegomena, 154–56, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics41legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 281–85, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 227–35 and 268–85, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China's Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 144, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]","Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 4–5 and 10–11, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Early_Western_Zhou_Bronze_Ji_Halberd%2C_11th-9th_c._BC.jpg/1280px-Early_Western_Zhou_Bronze_Ji_Halberd%2C_11th-9th_c._BC.jpg","title":"Early Western Zhou bronze halberd","desc":"A bronze halberd of the early Western Zhou, 11th to 9th century BCE, shown beside a museum panel naming the parts of a dagger-axe.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, Palace Museum, Taipei. CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_Western_Zhou_Bronze_Ji_Halberd,_11th-9th_c._BC.jpg","alt":"A dark bronze blade with a long point and a cross-piece, mounted upright in a museum case."}},
{"id":"cnh-131","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"An ode says the war carriages were made ready out of season because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were in blazing force, and the king had ordered the expedition.","questions":["Chinese tradition put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a line of northern neighbours running from the Xunyu to the Xiongnu, which is a genealogy of names rather than of peoples.","After King Li was driven out of his capital the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are said to have overrun the royal lands, and his son’s first year opened with a campaign against them."],"answer":"Xianyun","answerText":"Xianyun","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under the Zhou, c. 1046 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Campaign</span><span class=\"dt-v\">King Xuan’s 1st year, c. 827 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Later called</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Xiongnu, from the 3rd century BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Xianyun</b> is the name the Western Zhou gave the people they fought on their northern and western frontier, known almost wholly from Zhou poetry rather than from anything of their own. The word belongs to a list the Chinese tradition drew up of successive northern neighbours: mountain Rong under Yao and Shun, Chunwei under the Xia, Guifang under the Yin, Xianyun under the Zhou, Xiongnu under the Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a genealogy of names rather than of peoples, and the list’s French translator warned that nomads were probably called after whichever of their tribes held the upper hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> None of it should be read as a settled ethnic identity, and the caution runs both ways, the Zhou heartland itself lying in the north-west within reach of the people it was naming. What archaeology shows along that frontier is not a wall between two worlds but a belt of regional groups living among the Zhou and marrying into them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> At Hengshui in Shanxi the 10th-century lord of the small state of Peng was buried face down with six attendants, a northern rite, and his line took the name Gui.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rulers of Jin, whose border with the north was porous, married women from such groups, and their consorts’ tombs hold jars descended from a northern tradition that no Zhou man’s grave contains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even at the centre the aristocracy was not uniform, so diversity was never confined to the border districts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The fighting is remembered in the odes, where the Xianyun are said to have taken their chance in the disorder after King Li was driven from his capital, overrunning the royal lands to the north of the Jing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Sixth Month tells how the war carriages were made ready out of season, ten of them leading in front, and how the king’s commander Yin Jifu smote them as far as Taiyuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":5,"tags":["people","warfare","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 281–85, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 412–13, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Trois Souverains, 28–29, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China's Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 160–62, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 69, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/27/Duo_You_ding_inscription_%E5%A4%9A%E5%8F%8B%E9%BC%8E%E9%93%AD%E6%96%87%E6%8B%93%E7%89%87_word_Xianyun_%E7%8E%81%E7%8B%81.jpg","title":"The word Xianyun in a Zhou bronze inscription","desc":"The two characters read as Xianyun, cut from a rubbing of the inscription inside the Duo You ding, a vessel recording a campaign against them.","credit":"Rubbing of a 9th-century BCE inscription, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duo_You_ding_inscription_%E5%A4%9A%E5%8F%8B%E9%BC%8E%E9%93%AD%E6%96%87%E6%8B%93%E7%89%87_word_Xianyun_%E7%8E%81%E7%8B%81.jpg","alt":"Two archaic Chinese characters in white on a dark rubbing ground, one above the other."}},
{"id":"cnh-132","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Against a minister’s advice <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marched on the Quan Rong and came home with four white wolves and four white deer, after which the outlying vassals stopped attending court.","questions":["The Travels of the Son of Heaven sends <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to the Kunlun mountains and the Queen Mother of the West behind eight marvellous steeds, and it was compiled long after his death.","A scale on which the five mutilating punishments could be bought off by a fine was set out for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by the Marquis of Lü in the king’s hundredth year."],"answer":"King Mu","answerText":"King Mu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 976 – 922 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Code set out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the king’s 100th year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">55 years, by tradition</span></div>","abstract":"<b>King Mu</b> is the fifth king of the Zhou, remembered less for anything he did than for a journey to the west that later ages fastened on him. He came to the throne already fifty, the Grand Historian says, at a moment when royal conduct had grown slack, and his first act was to order Bojiong to restate the rules of government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then proposed to punish the Quan Rong, and Moufu, Duke of Zhai, argued against it: the early Zhou kings had not been warriors, and King Wu had fought the Shang out of pity rather than any love of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Barbarian vassals, Moufu added, were to be admonished by proclamation and not attacked, and these had not failed the one duty asked of them, which was to appear at court at each new accession. Mu went anyway, brought home four white wolves and four white deer, and from that time the vassals of the outlying lands came to court no more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The other thing the tradition gives him is a law code: in his hundredth year the Marquis of Lü set out for him a scale on which the five mutilating punishments could be redeemed by a fine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Branding and cutting off the nose covered a thousand offences each, cutting off the feet 500, castration 300 and death 200, three thousand crimes in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Grand Historian allows him 55 years on the throne and says nothing whatever about the journey west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That story is preserved in the Travels of King Mu, which sends him to the Kunlun mountains and the Queen Mother of the West behind eight marvellous steeds driven by a charioteer named Zaofu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The book was judged the compilation of a much later age by the 19th-century dictionary that summarised it, and all knowledge of the king is traditional, his reign lying well before the first year Chinese chronology can fix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","person","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 250–61, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 254–63, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), 594–95, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 737, 223–24, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/King_Mu_of_Zhou_%26_Queen_Mother_of_the_West.jpg/1280px-King_Mu_of_Zhou_%26_Queen_Mother_of_the_West.jpg","title":"King Mu visits the Queen Mother of the West","desc":"A Korean Joseon screen painting of the legend that took King Mu west to the Queen Mother, a story the Grand Historian passes over in silence.","credit":"Korean Joseon dynasty screen painting, Gyeonggi Provincial Museum. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Mu_of_Zhou_%26_Queen_Mother_of_the_West.jpg","alt":"A brightly coloured painting of a court gathering before a tiled hall, with figures in robes on a terrace and a crowd below."}},
{"id":"cnh-133","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Told that he had silenced all criticism, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was warned by the Duke of Shao that holding back the mouths of the people is harder than damming water.","questions":["People in the capital of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to exchange only a look as they passed, since anyone the king’s informer denounced was put to death at once.","The flight of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to Zhi is followed by the first year Chinese chronology can fix without dispute, so his expulsion opens the reliable record."],"answer":"King Li","answerText":"King Li","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 877 – 841 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fled to Zhi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">841 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">828 BCE, in exile at Zhi</span></div>","abstract":"<b>King Li</b> is the Zhou king whose own subjects drove him out of his capital, an event that opens the first stretch of Chinese history whose years can be counted without dispute. Thirty years into the reign he grew greedy for wealth and promoted the Duke of Yi, and the Earl of Rui warned him that a commoner who seizes what is useful is called a thief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He made the duke a high officer anyway, and his conduct turned cruel and overbearing. When the people of the capital complained he set a diviner from Wei to find out who was criticising him, and those denounced were put to death at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Criticism grew rare and the lords stopped coming to court, and by the thirty-fourth year nobody dared speak, so that men passing in the road exchanged only a look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The king was pleased and told the Duke of Shao he had put a stop to criticism, and the duke answered that he had built a dam: mouths are harder to hold back than water, and penned water bursts out and drowns many.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three years later men leagued together, attacked him without warning, and he fled the kingdom for Zhi, in what is now Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The crowd surrounded the house where the heir was hiding and the Duke of Shao gave up his own son in the boy’s place, the older annals saying plainly that the substitute was killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four odes are traditionally read as reproaches to this reign, and one modern reconstruction of the chronology makes Li a child of six at his accession who needed a regent of his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whatever the reign was, the fourteen years that followed his flight are where Chinese dates stop being reconstructions and start being a record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","person","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 269–76, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, 153–54, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 495–519, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]","David S. Nivison, “The Key to the Chronology of the Three Dynasties: The ‘Modern Text’ Bamboo Annals,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 93 (January 1999): 23, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp093_bamboo_annals.pdf. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Duo_You_ding_inscription%2C_with_transcription_%E5%A4%9A%E5%8F%8B%E9%BC%8E%E9%93%AD%E6%96%87%E6%8B%93%E7%89%87.jpg/1280px-Duo_You_ding_inscription%2C_with_transcription_%E5%A4%9A%E5%8F%8B%E9%BC%8E%E9%93%AD%E6%96%87%E6%8B%93%E7%89%87.jpg","title":"The Duo You ding inscription","desc":"A rubbing of the inscription inside the Duo You ding, a vessel of this reign recording a campaign, set beside a modern transcription of its characters.","credit":"Rubbing of a 9th-century BCE inscription, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duo_You_ding_inscription,_with_transcription_%E5%A4%9A%E5%8F%8B%E9%BC%8E%E9%93%AD%E6%96%87%E6%8B%93%E7%89%87.jpg","alt":"A dark rubbing covered in columns of archaic Chinese characters, with a printed transcription in modern characters beside it."}},
{"id":"cnh-134","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The first year of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest date on which the sources stop disagreeing, so every year before it is a reconstruction.","questions":["Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was held by two dukes together or by one baron named He depends on which of two surviving accounts is followed.","During the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the exiled king lived on at Zhi for fourteen years, and his son only took the throne after the old man died there."],"answer":"Gonghe regency","answerText":"Gonghe regency","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">841 – 828 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">827 BCE, with King Xuan</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Gonghe regency</b> is the fourteen years between King Li’s flight from his capital and his son’s accession, and it is where Chinese chronology becomes firm. Its first year, 841 BCE, is the earliest date on which the sources stop disagreeing, so every year before it is a reconstruction and every year after it a count.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the word Gonghe means is the oldest problem about the period, because two accounts of the arrangement survive and they cannot both be right. The Grand Historian writes that the two counsellors, the Duke of Shao and the Duke of Zhou, held the government between them, and that the name of their regency was Gonghe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Read that way the two characters mean something like common harmony, and the regency repeats the one an earlier Duke of Zhou and Duke of Shao had exercised for King Cheng.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> say something else: in the king’s thirteenth year, with the king at Zhi, He, Baron of Gong, administered the royal duties, and a note adds that this is styled the Gonghe period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On that reading Gonghe is a fief and a man’s name rather than a description of anything, and the <i>Zhuangzi</i> says the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chinese historians have generally followed the Grand Historian, while a recent Western reconstruction of the chronology lists Gong He flatly as a regent holding office from 841 to 828.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whoever held it, the years were not quiet: the Xianyun are said to have taken their chance in the disorder and overrun the western border, and the annals carry a great drought through five successive years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> King Li died at Zhi in the fourteenth year, the heir Jing came out of hiding grown, and the regency ended with his accession as King Xuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","institution","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 275–77, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, 153–54, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","David S. Nivison, “The Key to the Chronology of the Three Dynasties: The ‘Modern Text’ Bamboo Annals,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 93 (January 1999): 23 and 64, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp093_bamboo_annals.pdf. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 281–82, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"cnh-135","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The tradition calls <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a restorer, but the annals give the end of his reign as a run of defeats and a census his own minister advised against.","questions":["In the first year of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the field levies were restored, war chariots were built, and an ode has Yin Jifu driving the Xianyun back to Taiyuan.","Hidden as a boy in the Duke of Shao’s house, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was named king by the two counsellors only after his father had died in exile at Zhi."],"answer":"King Xuan","answerText":"King Xuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 827 – 782 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">46 years, by tradition</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Beaten at Qianmu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in his 39th year</span></div>","abstract":"<b>King Xuan</b> is the Zhou king the tradition calls a restorer, and the record of his own reign is harder on him than that name suggests. He came to the throne on his father’s death in exile, grown up in the house of the Duke of Shao where he had been hidden as a boy, and the two counsellors named him king by common accord. They helped him and improved his government, the Grand Historian says, he took the principles of Wen, Wu, Cheng and Kang for his rule, and the lords began to respect the Zhou again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> put the practical work in his first year: he restored the field levies, military charges assessed on the quality of the land that had lapsed during his father’s exile, and he made chariots of war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That first year is also the one an ode celebrates, when the king’s commander Yin Jifu drove the Xianyun back as far as Taiyuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What the same annals record for the end of the reign is a run of defeats: the royal forces failed against the hordes of Taiyuan, were routed by the Tiao and the Ben, and were beaten again by the Jiang at Qianmu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the fortieth year the king numbered the people of Taiyuan, a census his own minister had advised against, and the year after that his forces were beaten in Shen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He also had a great officer put to death for a remonstrance, on which the man’s friend killed himself rather than live under such a ruler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He neglected the ceremonial ploughing of the thousand-mu field, for which the Duke Wen of Guo blamed him, and later readers hung the dynasty’s ruin on the census.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He reigned 46 years by the usual count, though a modern reconstruction shortens the reign and moves his death two years earlier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","person","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 276–79, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, 154–56, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 281–85, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]","David S. Nivison, “The Key to the Chronology of the Three Dynasties: The ‘Modern Text’ Bamboo Annals,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 93 (January 1999): 24, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp093_bamboo_annals.pdf. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), 770, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Mao_Gong_ding_inscription.png/1280px-Mao_Gong_ding_inscription.png","title":"The Mao Gong ding inscription","desc":"A rubbing of the inscription inside the Mao Gong ding, a late Western Zhou cauldron usually dated to this reign and carrying the longest bronze text known.","credit":"Rubbing attributed to Chen Jieqi or his pupils, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Gong_ding_inscription.png","alt":"Two curved black panels of a rubbing, each filled with columns of pale archaic Chinese characters."}},
{"id":"cnh-136","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The reign of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened with an earthquake that dried three rivers and brought down part of Mount Qi, which the court astrologer read as the end of the dynasty.","questions":["An eclipse recorded in the sixth year of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the earliest event in Chinese history whose date calculation can put beyond dispute.","Having set aside his queen and her son, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the government to a prince of Guo whom the kingdom thought a flatterer greedy for gain."],"answer":"King You","answerText":"King You","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">781 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earthquake</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in his 2nd year, 780 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Solar eclipse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 August 776 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>King You</b> is the last king of the Western Zhou, killed in 771 BCE at the foot of Mount Li, after which the dynasty never ruled from the Wei valley again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His second year brought an earthquake that dried the Jing, the Wei and the Luo and brought down part of Mount Qi, and the court’s grand astrologer read it as the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the Yi and the Luo dried up the Xia perished, he said, and when the He dried up the Shang perished; a kingdom stands on its mountains and its rivers, and the ruin would come inside ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same year the king began to increase the taxes, which is the first thing the annals think worth entering about his government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In his third year he became attached to the concubine Baosi, and she bore him a son called Bofu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He then degraded Queen Shen, daughter of the Marquis of Shen, and set aside her son Yijiu, making Baosi queen and Bofu heir, on which the grand astrologer read through the annalists’ records and said only that the Zhou were lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gave the government to Shifu, prince of Guo, a man skilled in deceitful speech, given to flattery and greedy for gain, and the kingdom was indignant at the appointment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deposed heir fled to Shen, his mother’s country, which is where the attack that killed the king would come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His sixth year carries the one thing in the reign that can be checked from outside the texts: an eclipse of the sun on the first day of the tenth month, which calculation puts on 29 August 776 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is, in the words of the translator who first verified it against the day and month the ode gives, the earliest date in Chinese history about which there can be no dispute.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["ruler","person","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 279–85, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, 156–57, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 320–21, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]","David S. Nivison, “The Key to the Chronology of the Three Dynasties: The ‘Modern Text’ Bamboo Annals,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 93 (January 1999): 24, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp093_bamboo_annals.pdf. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"cnh-137","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The tradition blames the fall of the Western Zhou on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose laugh the king is said to have bought by lighting the beacons when no enemy was near.","questions":["The Grand Historian traces <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> back to dragon foam kept in a casket through three dynasties, which makes the account a myth rather than a record.","After the queen was degraded and her son banished, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was raised in her place and the child Bofu was named heir to the Zhou."],"answer":"Baosi","answerText":"Baosi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Consort of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">King You, 781 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made queen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 779 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken prisoner</span><span class=\"dt-v\">771 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Baosi</b> is the consort of King You whom the tradition blames for the fall of the Western Zhou, and the story told about her is a myth from its first line. The Grand Historian traces her to two divine dragons that settled in a Xia palace calling themselves princes of the land of Bao, and to the foam they left when the court divined and asked for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The foam was shut in a casket that passed from the Xia to the Shang and from the Shang to the Zhou, and nobody opened it for three dynasties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Opened at the end of King Li’s reign it ran through the palace, turned into a black lizard and entered the harem, where a girl of the age at which milk teeth fall out met it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> She conceived without a husband, took fright and abandoned the child; a fugitive couple picked it up and fled to Bao, whose people later handed the girl over to redeem an offence, which is how she came by her name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In King You’s third year he saw her and loved her and had a son by her called Bofu, whereupon the queen was degraded, her son Yijiu banished and Baosi raised in her place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> She would not laugh, and the king had a beacon that could be lit by day, another for the night, and a great drum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He lit the day beacon as though enemies had come, the lords came running, there was nobody there, and she laughed aloud.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He did it again until nobody believed the signal and the lords stopped coming, which is the whole point of the story, because the same beacon was lit in earnest when the attack came.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of it is evidence: commentators read her into odes she is not in, the account had changed a great deal by the time a Victorian dictionary retold it, and blaming a dynasty’s end on a woman was an old habit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["person","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty","mythology"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 281–85, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, 168–69, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 322–23 and 344–45, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, 156, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), 619–20, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/History_of_King_Yu.jpg/1280px-History_of_King_Yu.jpg","title":"The beacon lit for Baosi","desc":"A later Chinese painting of the story: smoke rising from the beacon tower, armed horsemen riding up to the palace, and the king seated on the terrace with his court.","credit":"Chinese painting, artist unknown, public domain. Scanned by Szilas. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:History_of_King_Yu.jpg","alt":"A coloured painting of a hillside palace with a smoking beacon tower, riders in red and blue climbing towards it, and figures seated on a terrace."}},
{"id":"cnh-138","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended with the king dead at the foot of Mount Li, his heir killed, his consort a captive and the whole treasure of the dynasty carried away.","questions":["A year before the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the prince of Shen had already made his engagement with the western hordes, so the attack was nothing sudden.","After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> two men were declared king at once, the deposed heir by Shen and another son of You by the duke of Guo."],"answer":"sack of Haojing","answerText":"sack of Haojing","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Happened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 11th of King You</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings after it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 at once</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>sack of Haojing</b> is the storming of the Western Zhou capital in 771 BCE by the Marquis of Shen and his allies, in which King You was killed and the dynasty lost its home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Haojing stood in the Wei valley near what is now Xi’an, and it had been the seat of the Zhou since before their conquest of the Shang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war began as a quarrel over the succession: the king had degraded Queen Shen and set aside her son Yijiu, who fled to his mother’s country, and had made Baosi’s son Bofu heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> put the alliance in the ninth year, when the prince of Shen sent to the western hordes and to Zeng and entered into an engagement with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the tenth year the king led his own army against Shen, so what followed was the second move in a war he had begun himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In the spring of the eleventh year the men of Shen and Zeng and the Quan Rong entered the capital and killed the king and Duke Huan of Zheng.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chief of the Quan killed Bofu and took Baosi captive, and the attackers seized the whole treasure of the Zhou and withdrew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Grand Historian adds that the king lit the day beacon to call his troops and that nobody came, which is the payoff of the beacon story rather than the cause of anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His French translator doubted one of the allies outright, an eastern state being an unlikely partner for western barbarians in an attack on the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What followed was not a clean succession: Shen and its allies raised Yijiu, while the duke of Guo declared another son of You king in his own place, so that for a time there were two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","battle","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 285–86, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, 156–58, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), 619–20, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","David S. Nivison, “The Key to the Chronology of the Three Dynasties: The ‘Modern Text’ Bamboo Annals,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 93 (January 1999): 24, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp093_bamboo_annals.pdf. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Chou_Dynasty%2C_11th-9th_Centuries_BC.jpg/1280px-Chou_Dynasty%2C_11th-9th_Centuries_BC.jpg","title":"The Zhou realm before the sack","desc":"Albert Herrmann's map of the Zhou kingdom, whose legend marks the boundaries under King Xuan, the father of the king killed when the capital fell.","credit":"Albert Herrmann, Historical and Commercial Atlas of China, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chou_Dynasty,_11th-9th_Centuries_BC.jpg","alt":"A printed historical map of northern China with coloured patches marking fiefs, rivers drawn in fine lines and a scale bar."}},
{"id":"cnh-139","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"After the western capital fell the court made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the two halves of the dynasty are named Western and Eastern after where the king then sat.","questions":["Four states escorted the king with their troops when the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made, and the chronicle then stops mentioning his accession and narrates the affairs of Jin.","The Odes register the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in their arrangement, filing the royal domain around the new capital among the states as one voice out of fifteen."],"answer":"Zhou move east","answerText":"Zhou move east","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Happened</span><span class=\"dt-v\">770 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King Ping</span><span class=\"dt-v\">reigned 770 – 720 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kings at once</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2, for some years</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Zhou move east</b> is King Ping’s transfer of the royal seat from the Wei valley to the Luo shortly after 771 BCE, and it divides the dynasty into its Western and Eastern halves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The princes and the Marquis of Shen raised the deposed heir Yijiu after his father was killed, and the reason the Grand Historian gives is that somebody had to be charged with the Zhou sacrifices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He went east because the west was no longer safe, the Rong raiding it and the old capital having just been sacked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The city was not new: the Duke of Zhou had built Chengzhou on the Luo nearly three centuries earlier as the dynasty’s second seat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <i>Bamboo Annals</i> have Jin, Wei, Zheng and Qin escort the king there with their troops, and in the same year the prince of Jin was made chief among the princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The succession was contested, the duke of Guo having declared another son of King You king, so that for some years there were two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chronicler’s own habit changes at that point: from the removal eastward he relates the affairs of Jin, and does not mention the king’s accession at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the king kept was his sacrifices, his title and his calendar; what he lost was the west, and with it the power to make anybody do anything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Odes register the demotion in their filing, since the royal domain around the new capital appears among the states as one voice out of fifteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first piece is an officer walking through millet grown up where the palaces had stood and asking Heaven whose doing this was, though the editor who translated it noted that the ode itself never names a capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty","geography"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 285–89, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., “The Annals of the Bamboo Books,” in <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 3, part 1, <i>The Shoo King</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1865), Prolegomena, ch. 4, 157–58, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics31legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 1, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 109–10, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics41legg. [Open access]","David S. Nivison, “The Key to the Chronology of the Three Dynasties: The ‘Modern Text’ Bamboo Annals,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 93 (January 1999): 24, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp093_bamboo_annals.pdf. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Eastern_Zhou_Royal_Six-horse_Chariot_Burial%2C_Luoyang_01.jpg/1280px-Eastern_Zhou_Royal_Six-horse_Chariot_Burial%2C_Luoyang_01.jpg","title":"An Eastern Zhou royal chariot burial at Luoyang","desc":"Horse skeletons and chariot traces in a royal burial pit at Luoyang, the city the Zhou kings moved to and were buried beside.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, Luoyang. CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Zhou_Royal_Six-horse_Chariot_Burial,_Luoyang_01.jpg","alt":"A long excavated pit with rows of horse skeletons lying on their sides in packed earth."}},
{"id":"cnh-140","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The most literal part of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is buried, since the bronzes of the royal heartland come from hoards hidden in a flight nobody returned from.","questions":["Among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are the states themselves, since the kinsmen planted over the east outlived the house that planted them by five centuries.","What is missing from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a working monarchy, since within a lifetime of the move east each lord did what was right in his own eyes."],"answer":"Western Zhou legacy","answerText":"Western Zhou legacy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Western Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1046 – 771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital fell</span><span class=\"dt-v\">771 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hoards hidden</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the flight of 771 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Western Zhou legacy</b> is what the first half of the dynasty left behind after its capital fell in 771 BCE, which is a great deal more than the monarchy it failed to keep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its most literal bequest is buried: the bronzes of the royal heartland come out of hoards rather than graves, and are thought to be temple assemblages hidden in the hasty flight of the royal and aristocratic lineages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nobody came back for them, so the vessels a family cast to record its own service to the kings became the archive by which the period can be read at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The states are the second bequest, since the kinsmen and allies planted over the east and the north outlived the house that planted them and became the players of the next three centuries. The scholarship was Zhou as well: of the 29 chapters of the modern text of the Documents, twenty belong to this dynasty and nine to everything before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What did not survive was the kingship in any working sense, and the change came quickly. Within a lifetime of the move east there was virtually no king in China, in the phrase of the man who translated the chronicle, and the lord of each state did what was right in his own eyes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The larger states grew by eating the smaller, and an officer of Jin could say plainly that eight of the houses it had absorbed carried the royal surname and that Jin’s greatness was owing to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Grand Historian’s account of the aftermath comes to the same thing: Qi, Chu, Qin and Jin began to grow, and power belonged to whoever held the upper hand in his own region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the kings kept was the sacrifices, the calendar and the words, so the vocabulary of Heaven’s charge passed to lords who claimed to act for a king they no longer obeyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty","archaeology"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 74, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 112–14, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cxxxix, and Annales des Tcheou, 285–89, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 92–93 and 197–99, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/%E9%99%95%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%9D%E9%B8%A1%E6%89%B6%E9%A3%8E%E5%8E%BF%E5%BA%84%E7%99%BD%E6%9D%91-%E5%8F%B2%E5%A2%99%E7%9B%98-%E8%A5%BF%E5%91%A8-%E5%AE%9D%E9%B8%A1%E5%91%A8%E5%8E%9F%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%99%A2_01.jpg/1280px-%E9%99%95%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%9D%E9%B8%A1%E6%89%B6%E9%A3%8E%E5%8E%BF%E5%BA%84%E7%99%BD%E6%9D%91-%E5%8F%B2%E5%A2%99%E7%9B%98-%E8%A5%BF%E5%91%A8-%E5%AE%9D%E9%B8%A1%E5%91%A8%E5%8E%9F%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%99%A2_01.jpg","title":"The Shi Qiang pan","desc":"The Shi Qiang pan, a bronze basin of King Gong’s reign from a hoard at Zhuangbai village in Fufeng, Shaanxi; the 284 characters cast inside it praise seven Zhou kings and then record six generations of the family that made it.","credit":"Photograph by Kcx36, Baoji Zhouyuan Museum. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%99%95%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%9D%E9%B8%A1%E6%89%B6%E9%A3%8E%E5%8E%BF%E5%BA%84%E7%99%BD%E6%9D%91-%E5%8F%B2%E5%A2%99%E7%9B%98-%E8%A5%BF%E5%91%A8-%E5%AE%9D%E9%B8%A1%E5%91%A8%E5%8E%9F%E5%8D%9A%E7%89%A9%E9%99%A2_01.jpg","alt":"A wide shallow bronze basin on a museum stand, columns of archaic characters cast into its inner floor."}},
{"id":"cnh-141","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Both halves of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are named after books rather than events, one after a chronicle of Lu and the other after a collection of persuasions.","questions":["When Qin came for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 256 BCE the royal domain had shrunk to 36 towns, which its prince handed over after confessing his guilt.","Through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the kings kept their sacrifices, their calendar and their tripods, and lost the power to make any lord do anything at all."],"answer":"Eastern Zhou","answerText":"Eastern Zhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 770 – 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 500 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">256 BCE, annexed by Qin</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Eastern Zhou</b> is the second half of the dynasty, running from the move to the Luo valley about 770 BCE to the extinction of the house in 256 BCE, and it is the longer half by two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The name is a later convenience taken from geography, as the Western Zhou’s is: the royal seat now lay east, at the city the Duke of Zhou had built on the Luo. What the kings kept was ritual — the sacrifices, the calendar, the title and the tripods — and what they lost was the power to make any lord do anything, their own domain filing among the states as one voice out of fifteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The period is conventionally cut in two, and both halves are named after books rather than after events: the Spring and Autumn after a chronicle of Lu, the Warring States after a collection of persuasions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its record is therefore the states’ record rather than the court’s, the reverse of the Western Zhou, whose bronzes were cast by men reporting to a king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> How small the royal domain had become is measurable at the very end of it. When Qin attacked in 256 BCE the prince of the Western Zhou, one of two branches the domain had by then split into, fled to Qin, confessed and handed over all his towns, 36 of them with thirty thousand people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qin took the nine tripods, the last king died, and seven years later the other branch was annexed as well, at which point the sacrifices of the Zhou ceased.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dynasty as a whole is the longest in Chinese history, and nearly all of that length is this half of it, lived out by kings whom nobody obeyed. What makes the centuries worth recording is what grew in the space the kings left: the presiding chiefs, the covenant meetings, the standing armies and the argument about government that produced the classical schools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","dynasty","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 285–89 and 316–18, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 1–6 and 112–15, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 1, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 109–10, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics41legg. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 74, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","Yun Kuen Lee, “Building the Chronology of Early Chinese History,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 41, no. 1 (2002): 15–42, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/17161. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Xinzheng_Ancient_City_Wall_of_Rammed_Earth%2C_Eastern_Zhou%2C_c._770-230_BC.jpg/1280px-Xinzheng_Ancient_City_Wall_of_Rammed_Earth%2C_Eastern_Zhou%2C_c._770-230_BC.jpg","title":"Rammed-earth wall at Xinzheng","desc":"A roadcut through the rammed-earth wall of the Eastern Zhou city at Xinzheng, showing the yearly layers in which it was built up.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, Xinzheng, Henan. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xinzheng_Ancient_City_Wall_of_Rammed_Earth%2C_Eastern_Zhou%2C_c._770-230_BC.jpg","alt":"A cliff-like cut through packed earth showing many thin horizontal layers, with trees growing above it."}},
{"id":"cnh-142","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Mencius said that in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> there are no righteous wars, since correction is what a supreme authority does and hostile states do not correct one another.","questions":["The order of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was supposed to run on protocol, with lords keeping to their allotments and any unjust attack reported to a king who would take the field.","How many states existed in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a question its Victorian translator refused to answer even approximately, since the smaller kept melting into the larger."],"answer":"Spring and Autumn period","answerText":"Spring and Autumn period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 770 – 476 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chronicle covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">722 – 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dukes of Lu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">12, in that span</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Spring and Autumn period</b> is the first half of the Eastern Zhou, named after the chronicle of the state of Lu whose entries run from 722 to 481 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The era is conventionally opened earlier than the book, at the move of the capital east, so its name is wider than the work it is taken from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The order it describes was supposed to run on protocol: the lords were to keep to their allotments, maintain a good understanding by court visits and visits of compliment, and pass word to one another of any important event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An unjust attack by one state on another was to be reported to the royal court, and the king would then take the field and deal justice on the offender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That arrangement presupposed lords without ambition and a king with overwhelming force, and since neither existed the constitution of the kingdom was torn into shreds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> How many states there were nobody will say: the scholar who translated the whole chronicle declined to guess even approximately, and the number fell throughout as the smaller melted into the larger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No new state arose in the period, and what growth of territory there was came from three that were placed to take it, Jin, Chu and Qin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mencius put the whole thing in a sentence, saying that in the Spring and Autumn there are no righteous wars, because correction is what the supreme authority does to its subjects and hostile states do not correct one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Around and inside the states lived peoples the record calls Rong, Di and Man, who raided, campaigned as allies, married into ruling houses and sometimes settled in the royal domain as subjects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Meanwhile the bronzes of the regional lords grew ambitious in exactly these years, which is the same story told in metal rather than in chronicle entries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 112–16 and 125–26, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 478–79, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 285–89, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 76, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China's Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 160–62, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Dings%2C_Li%2C_%26_Gui%2C_from_Tomb_of_King_of_Zheng_State%2C_Xinzheng%2C_1997.jpg/1280px-Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Dings%2C_Li%2C_%26_Gui%2C_from_Tomb_of_King_of_Zheng_State%2C_Xinzheng%2C_1997.jpg","title":"Ritual bronzes from the tomb of a ruler of Zheng","desc":"A graded set of ritual bronzes — dings, li and gui — from the tomb of a ruler of Zheng at Xinzheng, excavated in 1997.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, Zhengzhou City Museum. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Dings,_Li,_%26_Gui,_from_Tomb_of_King_of_Zheng_State,_Xinzheng,_1997.jpg","alt":"A lit museum case holding a row of three-legged bronze cauldrons of graded sizes, with lidded bowls behind them."}},
{"id":"cnh-143","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Mencius said the subjects of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are Huan of Qi and Wen of Jin, its style historical, and its righteous decisions the sage’s own.","questions":["The name of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was in use for the records of every state before Confucius was born, so it once meant historical records in general.","What the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually contains is a run of the briefest possible notices under twelve dukes, with nothing connecting them and no opinion about any of them."],"answer":"Spring and Autumn Annals","answerText":"Spring and Autumn Annals","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Covers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">722 – 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Span</span><span class=\"dt-v\">242 years, 12 dukes of Lu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commentaries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 early ones survive</span></div>","abstract":"The <b><i>Spring and Autumn Annals</i></b> is the chronicle of the state of Lu covering 722 to 481 BCE, the oldest surviving Chinese work in annalistic form and named for the seasons that order it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its name is not its own: the records of every state appear to have been called by it, and a minister of Jin is described examining the Lu archives and seeing these annals there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tutor was appointed in Jin in 568 BCE on the ground of his acquaintance with them, twenty years before Confucius was born, so the word then meant historical records at large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the book is in itself is a run of the briefest notices under the reigns of twelve dukes, with no attempt to connect them and no opinion expressed about any.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mencius is the earliest witness that Confucius made it, and his claims are enormous: the world had fallen into decay, Confucius was afraid and made the work, and rebellious ministers were terrified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Mencius names the chronicles of Jin and Chu as books of the same character, and reports the sage saying that its subjects are Huan of Qi and Wen of Jin, its style historical, and its decisions his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reconciling that account with the book is the oldest problem in reading it, and the three early commentaries, of Gongyang, of Guliang and of Zuo, are the first attempts at the job.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last of the three is incomparably the most important, since it supplies the events of which the text gives only a shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Doubt about the authorship is old too: an 18th-century Chinese scholar questioned in writing whether Confucius had any hand in it, where the Grand Historian had praised his exactness with the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its Victorian translator went further, calling the notices trivial and colourless and many of them untrue, while a recent study reads the commentary of Zuo as itself adapting stories that circulated on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 1–7, 22–24 and 38–40, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 283 and 327, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, cvii and cxxxiv, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, 104–5, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Story about Qi Xi's Recommendations and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 255 (February 2015): 1–2, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp255_zuozhuan_qi_xi.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Chunqiu_%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%83%D0%BB.jpg/1280px-Chunqiu_%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%83%D0%BB.jpg","title":"The chronicle in translation, 1876","desc":"The title page of the first Russian translation of the chronicle, made by N. I. Monastyrev and printed at St Petersburg in 1876.","credit":"Scanned by Pnkv. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chunqiu_%D1%82%D0%B8%D1%82%D1%83%D0%BB.jpg","alt":"A printed title page in Cyrillic type, with the words Chun-qiu set below a larger heading."}},
{"id":"cnh-144","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"For the many events the chronicle of Lu passes over in silence, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives a full account, and it never says where its author found them.","questions":["Scholars cannot agree whether the author of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was surnamed Zuo or Zuoqiu, and one concluded that the given name is simply lost.","Recent work reads the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a composition rather than an archive, since it adapts stories that also circulate on their own in later collections."],"answer":"Zuo Zhuan","answerText":"Zuo Zhuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Comments on</span><span class=\"dt-v\">722 – 481 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Period covered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">242 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commentaries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 of 3 early ones</span></div>","abstract":"The <b><i>Zuo Zhuan</i></b> is the great narrative of the Spring and Autumn period, transmitted as the first and by far the most important of the three early commentaries on the chronicle of Lu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Beside the chronicle two longer works cover the same years, this one and the <i>Discourses of the States</i>, the first a narrative of deeds and the second a collection of speeches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both have been attributed to a single hand, which is why the commentary of Zuo is often called the inner commentary and the <i>Discourses</i> the outer one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Who that hand was is unsettled at the level of the name: scholars differ over whether the surname was Zuo or Zuoqiu, and a Yuan scholar concluded that the given name is simply lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Grand Historian, listing works written out of adversity, says that Zuoqiu produced the <i>Discourses of the States</i> after losing his sight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> What the book does for the chronicle is supply the events of which the text gives only a shadow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It does a great deal more than that, since for multitudes of things that happened in Lu and elsewhere during the 242 years the chronicle says nothing at all and the commentary gives a full account.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where its author got the information he never says, and a 3rd-century commentator’s guess, that he was himself one of the historiographers of Lu, is still the usual answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was being annotated by the 2nd century CE, when a scholar of Henan wrote the commentary on it that made his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Recent work argues that the book is a composition rather than an archive, adapting stories that also circulate on their own in later collections of didactic anecdotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["text","history","literature","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 22–32, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Story about Qi Xi's Recommendations and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 255 (February 2015): 1–3, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp255_zuozhuan_qi_xi.pdf. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Account of the Death of King Zhao of Chu and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 159 (August 2005): 1–3, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp159_zuozhuan_king_zhao_chu.pdf. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, lviii and cxlvii, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), 580, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/%E6%98%A5%E7%A7%8B%E5%B7%A6%E5%82%B3%E9%9B%86%E8%A7%A3_30%E5%8D%B7.jpg/1280px-%E6%98%A5%E7%A7%8B%E5%B7%A6%E5%82%B3%E9%9B%86%E8%A7%A3_30%E5%8D%B7.jpg","title":"The Zuo commentary in a 1777 printing","desc":"An open copy of the Chunqiu Zuo zhuan jijie, the chronicle printed together with its collected commentary, in a Japanese edition of 1777.","credit":"National Diet Library Digital Collections. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%98%A5%E7%A7%8B%E5%B7%A6%E5%82%B3%E9%9B%86%E8%A7%A3_30%E5%8D%B7.jpg","alt":"An old bound book lying open on a stand, both pages filled with columns of Chinese characters in small type."}},
{"id":"cnh-145","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"The clearest mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a battle in which an earl beat the king, wounded him with an arrow, and then sent to condole with him.","questions":["Through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the forms outlived the power, so a lord who had just defeated a king in the field forbade any pursuit of him.","One measure of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is in the Odes, where the king’s own domain is filed among the states as one voice out of fifteen."],"answer":"decline of Zhou royal authority","answerText":"decline of Zhou royal authority","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 770 – 256 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King wounded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in Duke Huan of Lu’s 5th year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chu’s claim</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the royal title, c. 880 BCE</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>decline of Zhou royal authority</b> is the process by which the kings of the Eastern Zhou kept every form of sovereignty and lost the substance, and it is legible in the metalwork as well as in the chronicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The move east was itself an open acknowledgment of weakness, and the slide had begun long before it, a chief of Chu having taken the royal title for a time about 880 BCE and wished to be independent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sharpest single event is a battle: in the fifth year of Duke Huan of Lu the king led an army against Zheng and was beaten in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An arrow shot by an officer of Zheng wounded him in the shoulder, and he withdrew still fighting ably.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What happened next is the whole period in miniature, since the earl of Zheng forbade any pursuit, saying that a superior man does not wish to be always showing superiority and much less dare he insult the son of Heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That night the earl sent an officer to condole with the king and ask after his attendants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same doubleness runs through the century after it, a king refusing a duke of Zheng the rites due to him at court and Zheng answering by trading away the very field the Son of Heaven used, with nothing done about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The presiding chiefs who kept order professed loyal attachment to the House of Zhou while exercising royal functions, and the kings, sure of no vassal, confirmed unwillingly whoever raised himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The royal domain shrank with the authority, so that the king’s own songs are filed in the Odes among the states, one voice out of fifteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly all of it is known through a commentary that shapes its stories, which is worth remembering before reading the earl’s courtesy as a report rather than a lesson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","event","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1872), 45–46 and Prolegomena, 112–16, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 286–89, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 1, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society's Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 109–10, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics41legg. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 76, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Account of the Death of King Zhao of Chu and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 159 (August 2005): 1–3, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp159_zuozhuan_king_zhao_chu.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/%E9%84%AD%E8%8E%8A%E5%85%AC.jpg","title":"Duke Zhuang of Zheng","desc":"Duke Zhuang of Zheng, the lord who forbade pursuit of the wounded king, in a woodblock illustration from a 1752 edition of the romance of the Eastern Zhou states.","credit":"Illustration from an edition of the romance of the Eastern Zhou states, 1752. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%84%AD%E8%8E%8A%E5%85%AC.jpg","alt":"A line-drawn portrait of a bearded man in a crown and long robes holding a tablet, his name in a cartouche beside him."}},
{"id":"cnh-146","num":0,"category":"China · History","question":"Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one state at a time led all the others and exercised royal functions, while professing loyal attachment to the House of Zhou.","questions":["Small states paid twice under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, owing dues to the Zhou and the requisitions of the leading state as well, sometimes of two at once.","Mencius held that the chiefs of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were sinners against the three kings, because they dragged the princes about to punish other princes."],"answer":"hegemon system","answerText":"hegemon system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 685 – 590 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chiefs named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5, in the usual list</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Foremost held it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">39 years</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>hegemon system</b> is the arrangement by which one state at a time took the lead of all the others and exercised royal functions, while professing loyal attachment to the House of Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was an improvisation against disorder, and its seeds lay in the dynasty’s own beginnings, when princes were made chiefs of regions over their own state and those next to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those appointments had lapsed while the kings felt secure; now no king was sure enough of a vassal to delegate the office, so when a lord raised himself to it they confirmed him unwillingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five presiding chiefs are usually named, of Qi, Jin, Song, Qin and Chu, and their power was real: the foremost of them was the acknowledged head of the confederacy for 39 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whether all five belong on the list is another matter, and the scholar who translated the whole chronicle thought only the first two really represented the system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The system did some good, since Qi and Jin held Chu in check, and Chu would otherwise have advanced quickly to the overthrow of the Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also made things worse, being a rule within a rule that weakened loyalty to the throne: what use were kings who could not govern but had to keep devolving the work on a vassal?<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small states paid twice, owing dues to the Zhou, begged for so irregularly that messengers came from court, and the requisitions of the leading state as well, sometimes of two at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A minister of Zheng told the ministers of Jin that contributions had once been set by rank, that his own state was assessed as a duke’s, and that with no limit set to them they had only to wait for their ruin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mencius was harsher still, holding that the five chiefs were sinners against the three kings because they dragged the princes about to punish other princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["institution","concept","history","china","bronze age","zhou dynasty"],"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch'un Ts'ew, with The Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author's; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 114–17, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 435–36, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 286–89, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 211, 68–69, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Story about Qi Xi's Recommendations and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 255 (February 2015): 1–3, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp255_zuozhuan_qi_xi.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Five_Hegemons.png","title":"The five chiefs and their states","desc":"A modern map of the Spring and Autumn states, marking the royal domain, the five chiefs usually named and the states sometimes put in their place.","credit":"Map by SY. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Hegemons.png","alt":"A shaded relief map of eastern China with state names lettered across it and dashed borders between them."}},
{"id":"cnh-147","num":147,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Raised to the throne of Qi in the disorder after Duke Xiang's murder, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> bound the victim at Kuiqiu and laid the covenant on it without smearing the princes' mouths.","questions":["Three principal wives gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> no son, and the five who then claimed the succession left his body 67 days without a coffin.","A minister of Song, arguing against a human sacrifice, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> preserved three perishing states and drew all the princes to him, and was still thought slight in virtue."],"answer":"Duke Huan of Qi","answerText":"Duke Huan of Qi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled Qi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">685 – 643 BCE, some 43 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Kuiqiu, 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Body uncoffined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">67 nights after his death</span></div>","traditional":"齊桓公","hanzi":"齐桓公","pinyin":"Qí Huán Gōng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Duke Huan of Qi</b> is the lord under whom one state first took the lead of all the others, and the tradition makes him the first and greatest of the chiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His personal name was Xiao Bai; when Duke Xiang of Qi was murdered by his nephew Wuzhi, he and his brother Zijiu returned from exile to quarrel over the succession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He won it with Guan Zhong's help, raised him to chief minister in 685 BCE, and ruled Qi for some 43 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His leadership was assembly and covenant more than conquest: at Kuiqiu in 650 BCE he bound the victim and laid the writing on it, but did not slay it to smear the princes' mouths with blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The injunctions agreed there are a programme of government, opening on family order — slay the unfilial, change not the appointed heir, exalt not a concubine to be the wife — and closing on grain and embankments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He also battled the tribes of the northern and western frontiers, and two years after his death a minister of Song said he had preserved three perishing states and so drawn all the princes to him, though righteous scholars still called his virtue slight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The place was not heritable: when he died his successor became duke of Qi and was not reckoned a chief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His household then undid the state he had built, since his three principal wives bore him no son while six women who were to him as wives bore six.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He and Guan Zhong had put the intended heir in the charge of the duke of Song, but a cook and a eunuch won a promise for another son, and on Guan Zhong's death five of the six begged to be heir.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He died in the tenth month of 643 BCE, his body was not coffined for 67 nights, and it took a Song invasion the next spring to settle who followed him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke He, seventeenth to nineteenth years, 172–77, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 437–38, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 840, 327–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 211, 68–69, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/CADAL02016440_%E7%89%87%E7%92%A7%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%EF%BC%88%E4%B8%80%EF%BC%89_%28%E6%A1%93%E5%85%AC%E8%91%B5%E4%B8%98%E5%A4%A7%E6%9C%83%E7%9B%9F%29.jpg","title":"The assembly at Kuiqiu","desc":"Duke Huan’s great assembly at Kuiqiu, in a woodblock illustration from a Chinese romance of the Eastern Zhou states.","credit":"Peking University, CADAL collection. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CADAL02016440_%E7%89%87%E7%92%A7%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%EF%BC%88%E4%B8%80%EF%BC%89_%28%E6%A1%93%E5%85%AC%E8%91%B5%E4%B8%98%E5%A4%A7%E6%9C%83%E7%9B%9F%29.jpg","alt":"A line-drawn scene of robed figures gathered on a terrace under banners, with an ox standing at the lower right."}},
{"id":"cnh-148","num":148,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Recommended for the chief place by his friend Bao Shuya, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took it in 685 BCE and built up the commerce of Qi by sea and land.","questions":["Asked what had brought a northern army to Chu, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replied that its tribute of three-ribbed rush was unrendered and the king's sacrifices had nothing to strain the spirits with.","Confucius said that but for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> we should now be wearing our hair dishevelled and the lappets of our coats buttoning on the left side."],"answer":"Guan Zhong","answerText":"Guan Zhong","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief minister</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 685 BCE, under Duke Huan of Qi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">645 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">March on Chu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">656 BCE, halted at Zhaoling</span></div>","traditional":"管仲","hanzi":"管仲","pinyin":"Guǎn Zhòng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Guan Zhong</b> is the minister credited with making Qi strong enough to lead the other states, and the man Confucius judged twice over, once harshly and once not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He was a native of Qi and the bosom friend of Bao Shuya, who served the prince who became Duke Huan and recommended him for the chief place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took it in 685 BCE, directed the alliances and campaigns his master headed, and built up Qi's commerce by sea and land until a petty state stood level with far larger rivals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that looked like shows in the expedition of 656 BCE, when the allied army reached Chu and its viscount asked what had brought a lord of the northern sea to the southern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His answer was a lawyer's: the founder of Qi had been charged to punish the guilty among the princes in aid of the House of Zhou, and Chu's tribute of three-ribbed rush was unrendered, leaving the king's sacrifices nothing to strain the spirits with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He added that King Zhao had never come back from his expedition south, and the envoy answered that the tribute was his own lord’s fault but that about King Zhao they should inquire along the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is a hegemon acting as the king’s surrogate rather than against him, which is what separates the office from force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Confucius thought little of the man, saying he kept a screen at his gate and a stand for inverted cups as rulers of states did, and asking who did not know propriety if Guan did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yet when a disciple objected that he had failed to die with the master he first served, Confucius answered that but for him they would be wearing their hair dishevelled and their coats buttoning left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He died in 645 BCE, the work on government that bears his name is a later compilation, and the tradition kept his saying that his parents gave him birth but Bao Shu alone knew his feelings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 1, <i>Confucian Analects</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), 27 and 145–47, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics01legg_0. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke He, fourth year, 140–41, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 1006, 382, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 293, 91, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Qihuangongguanzhong2.jpg","title":"Duke Huan of Qi and Guan Zhong","desc":"Duke Huan of Qi and Guan Zhong on a carved stone slab from the Wu family shrines of the Han dynasty.","credit":"Rubbing of a Wu family shrines relief, Palace Museum. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qihuangongguanzhong2.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white rubbing of a carved stone slab showing standing and kneeling robed figures with captions beside them."}},
{"id":"cnh-149","num":149,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"With a eunuch sent by his father at Li Ji's urging to kill him, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fled the city of Pu in 654 BCE and spent nineteen years among the Di tribes.","questions":["Feasted in Chu during his exile, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> promised that if the two states ever met in war on the plain of the Middle Land he would withdraw three stages before them.","The chronicle says the king held a court of inspection at Heyang, concealing that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had summoned him there and made all the princes do homage."],"answer":"Duke Wen of Jin","answerText":"Duke Wen of Jin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">696 – 628 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In exile</span><span class=\"dt-v\">19 years, from 654 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chengpu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">632 BCE, then Jiantu and Heyang</span></div>","traditional":"晉文公","hanzi":"晋文公","pinyin":"Jìn Wén Gōng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Duke Wen of Jin</b> is the second of the presiding chiefs, and the one whose rule came out of nineteen years in exile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Born Chong'er, a son of Duke Xian of Jin, he fled his city of Pu in 654 BCE when his father's favourite Li Ji, wanting the succession for her own son, sent a eunuch to kill him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He took refuge with the chief of the Di tribes, who gave him his two daughters; he married the elder and gave the younger to one of the followers who had gone out with him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Feasted in Chu on the long way home, he was asked how he would repay the kindness, and answered that Chu had women, gems and silks already, and its own feathers, ivory and hides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he offered instead was a promise: if he recovered Jin and the two states met in war on the plain of the Middle Land, he would withdraw three stages before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> He came back after the nineteen years and took the government, and the promise fell due in 632 BCE at Chengpu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When Jin retreated and its junior officers called it disgraceful for the prince of one state to avoid the minister of another, a commander answered that the retreat repaid Chu and put the right on their own side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chu came on regardless and lost, and later that year the marquis of Jin summoned the king to Jiantu and Heyang and had him hold a court while all the princes did homage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That single act holds the whole ambiguity of the office, the word for a chief being also the word for the elder of a family, and Mencius holding that the five acted on the Son of Heaven’s behalf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The chronicle would not write it down, saying instead that the king by Heaven’s grace held a court of inspection at Heyang — which Legge called a remarkable admission that Confucius related events not according to his knowledge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke He, twenty-third and twenty-eighth years, 186, 200 and 212, and Prolegomena, 116–17, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 294–95, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 448, 527–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 848, 253–54, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 38–39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Duke_Wen_of_Jin_Recovering_His_State_%28%E6%99%89%E6%96%87%E5%85%AC%E5%BE%A9%E5%9C%8B%E5%9C%96%29_by_Li_Tang_%28%E6%9D%8E%E5%94%90%29%2C_1140.jpg/1280px-Duke_Wen_of_Jin_Recovering_His_State_%28%E6%99%89%E6%96%87%E5%85%AC%E5%BE%A9%E5%9C%8B%E5%9C%96%29_by_Li_Tang_%28%E6%9D%8E%E5%94%90%29%2C_1140.jpg","title":"Duke Wen of Jin Recovering His State","desc":"A section of Duke Wen of Jin Recovering His State, a handscroll attributed to the Song painter Li Tang, showing Chong’er’s return from exile.","credit":"Attributed to Li Tang, Metropolitan Museum of Art. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duke_Wen_of_Jin_Recovering_His_State_%28%E6%99%89%E6%96%87%E5%85%AC%E5%BE%A9%E5%9C%8B%E5%9C%96%29_by_Li_Tang_%28%E6%9D%8E%E5%94%90%29%2C_1140.jpg","alt":"A pale silk handscroll section showing a tiled hall on a platform with figures inside, trees around it and an ox in front."}},
{"id":"cnh-150","num":150,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Won at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 632 BCE by tiger skins on the horses and branches dragged behind the chariots to raise dust, the day left Jin at the head of the states.","questions":["Before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the marquis of Jin dreamt the viscount of Chu knelt on him and sucked his brains, and an officer read it as the enemy confessing guilt.","After <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the victors held the enemy camp and ate its provisions for three days, and the defeated commander of the centre killed himself."],"answer":"Battle of Chengpu","answerText":"Battle of Chengpu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">632 BCE, in Duke He of Lu’s 28th year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jin chariots</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700, fully harnessed</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Camp held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 days on the enemy’s provisions</span></div>","traditional":"城濮之戰","hanzi":"城濮之战","pinyin":"Chéngpú zhī Zhàn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Battle of Chengpu</b> is the engagement of 632 BCE in which Jin and its allies broke the army of Chu, making the victor the second of the chiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The marquis of Jin, troubled by the Chu position, heard his own soldiers singing that the field on the plain was beautiful and rich, the old crop removed and the new coming on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was still hesitating when an officer answered him: every Ji state north of the Han had been swallowed by Chu, so he was weighing a small kindness to himself against a great disgrace to his surname.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then dreamt he was wrestling the viscount of Chu, who knelt on him and sucked his brains, until the same officer read it the other way — Chu was kneeling as though confessing guilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chu commander sent to ask for the battle as a game the marquis might watch from his chariot, and had back word to prepare his own chariots for the morning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Jin put 700 chariots in the field, harnessed at back, breast, belly and hips, a vehicle whose draught gear had been developed for centuries into something a crew could hold in line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was won by deception on both wings: one commander covered his horses with tiger skins and broke the Chen and Cai troops on the Chu right, and two more feigned flight, carrying back banners and dragging branches to raise dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Chu centre dashed after them and was taken crosswise while the left was struck in flank, and both wings were scattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only the commander of the centre came off whole, and Jin held his camp for three days before the Zhou king came in person to give the victor the insignia of a chief.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He killed himself afterwards, his ruler refusing to forgive him, and the chronicle records the death as a public execution — an instance, its translator said, of the misrepresentations that abound in the classic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke He, twenty-eighth year, 200 and 209–11, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 294–95, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 448, 527–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 10–11, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"The battle reconstructed","desc":"A museum reconstruction of the battle: infantry with dagger-axes coming on behind their banners past a fallen chariot wheel.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, Shanxi Provincial Museum, Taiyuan. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_at_Chengpu%2C_632_BC_a.jpg","alt":"A museum diorama of armoured figures with long-hafted weapons and tall banners advancing across a painted plain.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Battle_at_Chengpu%2C_632_BC_a.jpg/1280px-Battle_at_Chengpu%2C_632_BC_a.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-151","num":151,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Reviewing his troops on the Zhou border in 606 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked the size and weight of the nine tripods and was told the kingdom's strength lay in virtue.","questions":["Urged to heap the Jin dead into a monument after Bi, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> refused, saying the character for martial prowess is made of the signs for staying and for a spear.","The Jin centre broke at the river before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 597 BCE, its men fighting for the boats until severed fingers could be taken up by the handful."],"answer":"King Zhuang of Chu","answerText":"King Zhuang of Chu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">613 – 591 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Asked the tripods</span><span class=\"dt-v\">606 BCE, on the Luo</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle of Bi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">597 BCE, against Jin</span></div>","traditional":"楚莊王","hanzi":"楚庄王","pinyin":"Chǔ Zhuāng Wáng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>King Zhuang of Chu</b> ruled from 613 to 591 BCE and is the one southern ruler the northern tradition counts among the presiding chiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 606 BCE he attacked the Rong of Luhun, came on to the Luo and reviewed his troops on the Zhou border, and when the king sent an officer with congratulations he asked the size and weight of the nine tripods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tripods were held to be a palladium safeguarding the dynasty, so the question was an offer to take it over, and the officer replied that the strength of the kingdom lies in virtue and not in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He added that bright virtue makes the tripods heavy though small and failing virtue light though large, and that the decree of Heaven had not yet changed, so their weight might not yet be asked about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A modern reading makes the king the counter-case to the northern chiefs, since where they acted as the royal surrogate he used force and marched into the royal domain to show the Son of Heaven had no authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> His great victory came in 597 BCE, when he besieged the capital of Zheng and then broke a Jin army at Bi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rout was at the river: the Jin commander of the centre called out a reward to whoever recrossed first, and his men fought for the boats until the fingers of those clinging to the sides could be taken up by the handful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An officer then urged him to heap the Jin dead into a monument so that later ages should not forget the victory, and he refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The character for martial prowess, he said, is made of the signs for staying and for a spear, and he had left the bones of two states’ soldiers bleaching in the open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He could claim no merit while Jin still stood, which is the sort of answer the commentary likes, and its Chu stories are shaped narratives rather than transcripts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Seuen, third and twelfth years, 293 and 319–20, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 296–97, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Account of the Death of King Zhao of Chu and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 159 (August 2005): 1–3, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp159_zuozhuan_king_zhao_chu.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"King Zhuang of Chu","desc":"King Zhuang of Chu in a woodblock illustration from a Chinese romance of the Eastern Zhou states.","credit":"Woodblock illustration, Nanjing Library. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NJlib-0026095-0001_%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%80%E7%99%BE%E5%85%AB%E5%9B%9E%E5%9C%96%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%B7_%28%E6%A5%9A%E8%8E%8A%E7%8E%8B%29.jpg","alt":"A line-drawn seated figure in a tall cap and layered robes, one hand raised, with columns of text beside him.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/NJlib-0026095-0001_%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%80%E7%99%BE%E5%85%AB%E5%9B%9E%E5%9C%96%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%B7_%28%E6%A5%9A%E8%8E%8A%E7%8E%8B%29.jpg/1280px-NJlib-0026095-0001_%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%80%E7%99%BE%E5%85%AB%E5%9B%9E%E5%9C%96%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%B7_%28%E6%A5%9A%E8%8E%8A%E7%8E%8B%29.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-152","num":152,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Given to King Wu's own preceptor as the first fief handed out, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ran from the sea on the east to the Yellow River on the west across northern Shandong.","questions":["The house of Jiang ruled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until a resident family, the Chen, took the surname Tian and had itself acknowledged marquis in 385 BCE.","Charged to punish the guilty among the princes of the five degrees to help the House of Zhou, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> read that commission as a warrant for leading the states."],"answer":"Qi","answerText":"Qi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enfeoffed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by King Wu, c. 1046 BCE, at Yingqiu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jiang line ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">390 BCE, duke removed to the coast</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tian line</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 385 BCE to 220 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"齊","hanzi":"齐","pinyin":"Qí","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Qi</b> is the eastern state of the Zhou world, holding the north of the Shandong peninsula, and the first seat of the presiding chiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ruling house bore the surname Jiang and claimed descent from Yao’s chief minister, and the Jiang were the lineage that most often married daughters to the Zhou kings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When King Wu handed out fiefs to the ministers who had served him, the first went to his own preceptor, the venerable Shang, who received Yingqiu; the country was called Qi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The tradition makes that man an exile from the last Shang king’s tyranny, found by King Wen fishing on the Wei at eighty and carried off to be his chief counsellor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The state remembered its charter as a police commission: its founder was charged to punish the guilty among the princes of the five degrees and the chiefs of the nine provinces in order to help the House of Zhou.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The land that went with the charge ran from the sea on the east to the He on the west and from Muling in the south to Wudi in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qi led the states under the ruler counted first of the chiefs, and after him remained a great power without ever recovering the presidency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What ended the house of Jiang was not conquest but a resident family, the Chen, who at some point nobody records exchanged that surname for Tian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 390 BCE Tian He moved the reigning duke out of his capital to a town near the sea where he might keep up the ancestral sacrifices, and the old line died out there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tian He then asked the king of Zhou and the other princes to acknowledge him marquis of Qi, which they did, and his first year is dated 385 BCE; his house held the state until it gave itself up to Qin in 220 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 102–11, and Duke He, fourth year, 140–41, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 239, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 1862, 708–9, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Xia Hua and Gideon Shelach-Lavi, “Bronze Art, Cultural Norms, and Group Identity: A Group of Late Western Zhou and Early Spring and Autumn He Vessels Analyzed in Their Temporal and Spatial Contexts,” <i>Asian Perspectives</i> 63, no. 1 (2024): 77, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/113123. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"The old capital at Linzi","desc":"An excavated stone drainage outlet through the wall of the old Qi capital at Linzi, where the ducal city stood.","credit":"Photograph by Inhorw, Linzi, Shandong. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%87%A8%E6%B7%84%E9%BD%8A%E5%9C%8B%E6%95%85%E5%9F%8E%E4%B8%89%E8%99%9F%E6%8E%92%E6%B0%B4%E5%8F%A3%E9%81%BA%E5%9D%80.jpg","alt":"An excavated channel of laid stone blocks running through an earth bank, with young trees along the top.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/%E8%87%A8%E6%B7%84%E9%BD%8A%E5%9C%8B%E6%95%85%E5%9F%8E%E4%B8%89%E8%99%9F%E6%8E%92%E6%B0%B4%E5%8F%A3%E9%81%BA%E5%9D%80.jpg/1280px-%E8%87%A8%E6%B7%84%E9%BD%8A%E5%9C%8B%E6%95%85%E5%9F%8E%E4%B8%89%E8%99%9F%E6%8E%92%E6%B0%B4%E5%8F%A3%E9%81%BA%E5%9D%80.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-153","num":153,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Founded when Tang Shu Yu was invested with Tang, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was divided in 402 BCE among three of its own ministerial houses, Wei, Zhao and Han.","questions":["A branch of the ruling house set up as earls in Quwo fought the main line of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for several reigns and took it outright in 678 BCE.","An officer of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could say that eight of the houses it had swallowed carried the royal surname, and that its greatness was owing to it."],"answer":"Jin","answerText":"Jin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Tang invested 1106 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Quwo takes it</span><span class=\"dt-v\">678 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Partitioned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">402 BCE into 3 marquisates</span></div>","traditional":"晉","hanzi":"晋","pinyin":"Jìn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Jin</b> is the northern state that held the great bend of the Yellow River and produced the second of the presiding chiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its house bore the royal surname Ji: Tang Shu Yu was invested with Tang in 1106 BCE and his son moved the seat to Jin, which gave the state its name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For several reigns the main line fought a losing war against a branch of its own house that had been set up as earls in Quwo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The branch won: in 678 BCE its head put the reigning marquis to death and was acknowledged by the Zhou king as ruler of Jin, and some lists count the Quwo chiefs among the princes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state reached its height a generation later under the marquis who broke Chu and was given the insignia of a chief by the king in person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Jin was one of the three states that grew by taking their neighbours in these centuries, and an officer of its own could say that eight of the houses it had absorbed carried the royal surname.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it could not manage was its own ministers, and the fate of Jin is a theme the great commentary keeps returning to, shaping even a story about an impartial appointment to serve it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By the end the descendants of Tang Shu Yu were puppets, and in 376 BCE the last of them was deprived of his state and his title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three ministerial houses had divided the country between them as the marquisates of Wei, Zhao and Han, whose independent existence dates from 402 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All three later called themselves kingdoms and all three were swallowed by Qin, so the partition of Jin is the event that opens the age after this one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 102–11 and 112–17, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 294–95, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 448, 527–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Story about Qi Xi’s Recommendations and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 255 (February 2015): 1–2, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp255_zuozhuan_qi_xi.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"A ding of a marquis of Jin","desc":"A bronze ding cast for a marquis of Jin, with a shallow bowl on three legs and a pair of upright handles.","credit":"Photograph by Underbar dk, Shanxi Museum, Taiyuan. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shanxi_Museum_-_warm_ding_of_Marquis_of_Jin.JPG","alt":"A round bronze cauldron on three curved legs, banded with ornament, standing on a clear museum plinth.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Shanxi_Museum_-_warm_ding_of_Marquis_of_Jin.JPG/1280px-Shanxi_Museum_-_warm_ding_of_Marquis_of_Jin.JPG"}},
{"id":"cnh-154","num":154,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"A house of the surname Mi ruled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the Lu chronicle calls a viscounty to the end although its rulers had taken the title of king.","questions":["Invested by King Cheng with the country the north called the Jing of the wild south, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had its first capital at Danyang on the gorges of the Yangtze.","Meeting an allied army on its border, the messenger of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked why it had come, the two countries lying by the southern and the northern seas."],"answer":"Chu","answerText":"Chu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Invested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by King Cheng, as a viscounty</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Kingship taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">886 BCE, resumed 703 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 684 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"楚","hanzi":"楚","pinyin":"Chǔ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Chu</b> is the great southern state of the Zhou world, ruled by a house of the surname Mi that the northern record ranked as viscounts and that called itself royal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It claimed descent from the ancient ruler Zhuanxu, though the first of the line actually to bear the surname Mi appears only at the edge of the historical record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Xiong Yi was invested with Chu by King Cheng as a viscount, holding the country the north called Jing Man, the Jing of the wild south, with his capital at Danyang on the gorges of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 886 BCE a successor took the title of king outright; it was dropped again for a time and permanently resumed in 703 BCE by the ruler known as King Wu, who moved the capital to Ying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why the chronicle of Lu goes on calling Chu’s rulers viscounts to the end: the northern record censured the usurpation by declining to write it down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Chu appears in that chronicle for the first time in 684 BCE and is thereafter one of the three states whose growth the period is a record of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only one of its kings is counted among the chiefs of the north’s own list, the ruler who marched to the Luo and asked the weight of the Zhou tripods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> He came within reach of them because the Zhou king had settled a Rong people near the Luo, and a Chu army sent against those Rong arrived on the royal border.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its own messenger met an allied army on its border by asking why it had come, since the two countries lay by the southern and the northern seas, too far apart for their cattle to stray into one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost everything known about its court comes through the great commentary of the north, whose Chu stories are adaptations of tales that also circulate on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 102–11, Duke Chwang, tenth year, 86, and Duke He, fourth year, 140–41, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 296–97, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 448, 527–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Account of the Death of King Zhao of Chu and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 159 (August 2005): 1–3, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp159_zuozhuan_king_zhao_chu.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"A bronze hu of Chu","desc":"A lidded bronze hu of the state of Chu, made after it had grown into the largest of the southern powers.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Hu%2C_Chu_State_%2810161832605%29.jpg","alt":"A dark bronze jar with a rounded body, a narrow neck and a low lid with two small knobs.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Warring_States_Bronze_Hu%2C_Chu_State_%2810161832605%29.jpg/1280px-Warring_States_Bronze_Hu%2C_Chu_State_%2810161832605%29.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-155","num":155,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Begun as a herdsman's holding on the western frontier, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was raised to an earldom for helping the Zhou court escape east and given the dynasty's own abandoned homeland.","questions":["King Xiao of Zhou set a man named Feizi over the royal horse herds between the Qian and the Wei and gave him the small territory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to hold.","An ode of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> laments the three brothers who followed Duke Mu into his grave, each a man above a hundred, and asks the azure Heaven why."],"answer":"Qin","answerText":"Qin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First in the list</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Feizi, 908 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made an earldom</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under Duke Xiang, from 776 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duke Mu died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">621 BCE, with 177 buried after him</span></div>","traditional":"秦","hanzi":"秦","pinyin":"Qín","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Qin</b> is the western state that began as a royal horse-keeper’s holding and ended by swallowing every other one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its house bore the surname Ying and claimed descent from the forester of Shun, though the Grand Historian’s line down through the Xia and the Shang is largely fabulous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is firm is that King Xiao of Zhou was pleased with a man called Feizi for his skill with cattle, set him over the royal horse herds between the Qian and the Wei, and gave him the small territory of Qin to hold as an attached state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Feizi stands first in the list of its princes, dated 908 BCE, and for five reigns the holding was a frontier command rather than a fief of the Zhou order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Horses were the point of it, since the chariot the northern states fought in rested on draught gear and trained teams, and this was the country the animals came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The state was made by the collapse of the Western Zhou, Duke Xiang having given important help in the troubles around King You’s death and the removal of the court east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His rank was raised to earl, Qin took an independent place among the fiefs, and its territory was greatly enlarged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he received was the old home domain of the house of Zhou from Mount Qi westwards, a grant Chinese writers have read as an omen of evil for the dynasty that made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Duke Mu, who succeeded in 659 BCE and died in 621, is counted among the five chiefs of the usual list; he was beaten by Jin at Yaoshan, subdued the Rong and was given golden drums by the king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At his funeral 177 people were buried with him, and an ode of Qin names three of them, asks the azure Heaven why the good must be taken, and offers a hundred lives to ransom each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 102–11, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 1, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society’s Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 198–99, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics41legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 1553, 593, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 10–11, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"The Qin Gong gui","desc":"A lidded bronze food vessel of the Spring and Autumn period whose inscription has a duke of Qin resolve to carry on his predecessors’ work and guard their land.","credit":"Photograph by Cangminzho. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E2%80%9C%E7%A7%A6%E5%85%AC%E2%80%9D%E9%9D%92%E9%93%9C%EF%BC%88%E7%9A%80%E6%AE%B3%EF%BC%8903668.jpg","alt":"A wide lidded bronze bowl on a ring foot with two loop handles, its surface covered in fine interlaced ornament.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/%E2%80%9C%E7%A7%A6%E5%85%AC%E2%80%9D%E9%9D%92%E9%93%9C%EF%BC%88%E7%9A%80%E6%AE%B3%EF%BC%8903668.jpg/1280px-%E2%80%9C%E7%A7%A6%E5%85%AC%E2%80%9D%E9%9D%92%E9%93%9C%EF%BC%88%E7%9A%80%E6%AE%B3%EF%BC%8903668.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-156","num":156,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Granted the royal ceremonies and music because of its founder's services, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one state whose temple hymns stand in the <i>Classic of Poetry</i> beside the Zhou and Shang ones.","questions":["King Wu gave the fief of Qufu to his younger brother the Duke of Zhou, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name the country took, though the duke stayed at court.","Held in miserable subjection by the head of the Ji-sun family, a duke of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tried to break his minister, lost, and fled to Qi."],"answer":"Lu","answerText":"Lu","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enfeoffed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qufu, to the Duke of Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First marquis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Bo Qin, the duke’s eldest son</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Its chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">covers 722 – 481 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"魯","hanzi":"鲁","pinyin":"Lǔ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Lu</b> is the eastern state of the royal surname that gave the Zhou world its chronicle, its ritual reputation and, in the end, an example of what happened to a ruling house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When King Wu handed out fiefs he gave Qufu to his younger brother, the Duke of Zhou, and the country was called Lu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The duke himself stayed at court as counsellor and regent, a man so busy that he is said to have broken off a bath several times holding his wet hair to deal with business, so the state was taken up by his eldest son.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> King Cheng repaid the father’s services by granting that son the use of the royal ceremonies and music, which is why Lu alone among the states had temple hymns of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four of them stand in the <i>Classic of Poetry</i> beside the hymns of Zhou and Shang, and their translator thought Confucius had shown neither discrimination nor moral boldness in letting them keep the name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the state is really known for is its chronicle, kept by its own historiographers and covering 722 to 481 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those historiographers were the trouble as well as the record, since where their own rulers were concerned they dared not look the truth in the face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rulers themselves were held in miserable subjection by the principal noble houses, above all the head of the Ji-sun family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One duke, urged on by his sons, tried to break that minister, lost, and fled to Qi; the chronicle says only that he withdrew, and adds that grackles had come and built nests in the trees.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The fate of a ducal house eaten by its own ministers is a theme the great commentary keeps returning to, and it shapes even the stories it tells about good appointments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 43 and 102–11, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 4, part 2, <i>The She King</i> (Hongkong: London Missionary Society’s Printing Office; London: Henry Frowde, 1871), 610–11, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics42legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 239, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 316, 419, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Story about Qi Xi’s Recommendations and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 255 (February 2015): 1–2, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp255_zuozhuan_qi_xi.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"A jade disc of Lu","desc":"A jade disc of the state of Lu, the flat ringed form used in ritual across the Zhou world.","credit":"Photograph by Windmemories, Zhengzhou Museum. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20210529_The_jade_disc_of_State_Lu_on_display_at_Zhengzhou_Museum.jpg","alt":"A dark green jade ring with a wide central hole, its face carved in small raised spirals and a border pattern.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/20210529_The_jade_disc_of_State_Lu_on_display_at_Zhengzhou_Museum.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-157","num":157,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Held by the descendants of the Shang kings so that their line might continue, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the state whose duke refused to attack Chu until it had crossed the river and formed up.","questions":["The Duke of Zhou summoned the count of Wei to court and invested him with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, there to be the representative of the departed kings of Shang.","A duke of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> said a superior man inflicts no second wound and takes no grey-haired prisoner, and was shamefully beaten and wounded in the thigh."],"answer":"Song","answerText":"Song","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Invested</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1040 BCE, on the count of Wei</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Battle of the Hong</span><span class=\"dt-v\">638 BCE, against Chu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Duke Xiang died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">637 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"宋","hanzi":"宋","pinyin":"Sòng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Song</b> is the state the Zhou set aside for the family they had overthrown, and its house bore the Shang surname Zi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> After the tyrant’s son was put to death for rebellion, the Duke of Zhou summoned the count of Wei to court and invested him with Song in King Cheng’s name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The charge told him to continue the line of his ancestors, observe their ceremonies and take care of their relics, and to come as a guest to the royal house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Guest is the exact word, since a dynasty that had lost the mandate kept its sacrifices and its rank on the sufferance of the house that had taken it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Song was never large, but its duke Xiang is on the usual list of five chiefs, and the modern argument about that list is that the five had very different merits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> His bid began badly: at his first assembly of the princes he used the ruler of a small state as a human sacrifice, and his own minister of War told him it would not get him the presidency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It ended at the river Hong in 638 BCE, where his army was drawn up while the Chu troops were still crossing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The minister asked leave to attack them in the water and was refused, then asked leave to attack them before they had formed and was refused again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Song was shamefully beaten, the duke was wounded in the thigh and his gate-wardens were all killed, and he answered the blame by saying that a superior man inflicts no second wound and takes no grey-haired prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His minister’s reply is the other half of the passage: sharp weapons are what an army uses, an enemy not mortally hurt should be struck again, and to spare the grey-haired is to submit at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 102–11, and Duke He, nineteenth and twenty-second years, 177 and 183, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Shû King</i>, Sacred Books of the East 3 (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1879), 162–63, https://archive.org/details/sacredbooksofchi0003unse. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 239, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 38–39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"Duke Xiang of Song","desc":"Duke Xiang of Song in a woodblock illustration from a Chinese romance of the Eastern Zhou states.","credit":"Woodblock illustration, Nanjing Library. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NJlib-0026095-0001_%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%80%E7%99%BE%E5%85%AB%E5%9B%9E%E5%9C%96%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%B7_%28%E5%AE%8B%E8%A5%84%E5%85%AC%29.jpg","alt":"A line-drawn standing figure in court robes with an attendant behind him and columns of text at the side.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/NJlib-0026095-0001_%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%80%E7%99%BE%E5%85%AB%E5%9B%9E%E5%9C%96%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%B7_%28%E5%AE%8B%E8%A5%84%E5%85%AC%29.jpg/1280px-NJlib-0026095-0001_%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%BF%97%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%B7%E4%B8%80%E7%99%BE%E5%85%AB%E5%9B%9E%E5%9C%96%E4%BA%8C%E5%8D%B7_%28%E5%AE%8B%E8%A5%84%E5%85%AC%29.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-158","num":158,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Held by viscounts of the surname Si who claimed descent from a Xia king, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had its capital at Kuaiji on the coast south of the Yangtze delta.","questions":["Useful to Chu at first as a counterweight to Wu, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> destroyed Wu outright and then became a dangerous enemy of Chu itself.","The chronicle of Lu names <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for the first time only in the 6th century BCE, so late that it enters the northern record as an outside power."],"answer":"Yue","answerText":"Yue","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Kuaiji, in modern Zhejiang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First in the chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Duke Zhao’s 5th year, 537 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed Wu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">473 or 472 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"越","hanzi":"越","pinyin":"Yuè","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Yue</b> is the southernmost of the great states of the Zhou world, lying on the coast below the Yangtze delta in what is now Zhejiang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was held by viscounts of the surname Si who claimed descent from Shaokang, a king of the Xia, and its capital was Kuaiji.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chronicle of Lu names it for the first time only in 537 BCE, which is very late in a record that opens in 722, so it enters the northern account already formed and from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its first use to the north was as a counterweight, Chu finding it helpful against the growing power of Wu, its immediate neighbour up the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That relationship reversed twice over, since Yue destroyed Wu and then became a dangerous antagonist of Chu itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The war with Wu ran about twenty years and turned on two battles, the first of which Wu won decisively at Kuaiji, driving the king of Yue back on the last few thousand men of his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The second ended the other way, and in 473 or 472 BCE, depending on which authority is followed, Yue extinguished Wu and its king killed himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The conqueror then gave in his allegiance to the House of Zhou, which by that date was a courtesy paid to a name rather than a submission to a power.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His chief minister left the court the year after the victory, saying that his prince was a man with whom troubles might be shared but not success enjoyed in fellowship.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lists of the chiefs who led the states differ, and some replace the northern names with the kings of Wu and Yue, which is a fair measure of what the south had become; Yue held it for about a century before Chu absorbed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Ch’aou, fifth year, 602–8, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 980, 373–74, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 197, 39–40, and no. 139, 42, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 102–11, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 38–39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"Yuyue bronze fittings","desc":"Bronze corner fittings for a palanquin, made by the Yuyue people of the Zhejiang coast in the Spring and Autumn period.","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, Zhejiang Provincial Museum, Hangzhou. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuyue_People_Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Palanquin_Components_%2817071255576%29.jpg","alt":"Four angular bronze corner pieces laid out on a museum mount, each drilled with peg holes and cast with scroll ornament.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Yuyue_People_Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Palanquin_Components_%2817071255576%29.jpg/1280px-Yuyue_People_Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Palanquin_Components_%2817071255576%29.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-159","num":159,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Beaten by Wu and left with 5,000 men, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drank daily from a vessel of gall and slept on firewood so that he should not forget the taste of defeat.","questions":["Presented with wine too little to share, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> poured it into a river his soldiers had to ford so that every one of them might have a taste.","The minister who had planned his victory left the court a year after it, saying that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a man with whom trouble could be shared but not success."],"answer":"Goujian","answerText":"Goujian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Came to the throne</span><span class=\"dt-v\">496 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">After the defeat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,000 men left to him</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed Wu</span><span class=\"dt-v\">473 or 472 BCE, after about 20 years</span></div>","traditional":"勾踐","hanzi":"勾践","pinyin":"Gōu Jiàn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Goujian</b> came to the throne of Yue in 496 BCE and is the standing Chinese example of a ruler who lost everything and took twenty years to get it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He opened by rejecting his minister’s advice and attacking Wu, and was totally beaten at the gate of its capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He retreated into his own country, whose capital was Kuaiji on the coast south of the Yangtze, with the 5,000 men left of his army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> From then on he drank daily from a vessel of gall and slept at night on firewood, to keep the bitterness of the defeat in front of him, and the two habits gave Chinese a phrase for patient revenge that is still in use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another story has him presented with wine too little to share, which he poured into a river his soldiers had to ford so that every one of them might have the taste of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The recovery was managed by his minister Fan Li, who is credited with the scheme that ruined the king of Wu through a woman placed in his household.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That king had beaten Yue at the outset, put his own faithful counsellor to death for advising him, and at the end lamented with his last breath that he had not listened.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> After about twenty years of war Yue destroyed Wu, in 473 or 472 BCE depending on which authority is followed, and the beaten king killed himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Goujian annexed the state and gave in his allegiance to the House of Zhou, then still nominally ruling north of the Yangtze.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fan Li left him the following year, carrying his portable treasure and saying that his prince was one with whom troubles might be shared but not victory enjoyed; some lists of the chiefs who led the states put Goujian among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), no. 980, 373–74, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 197, 39–40, and no. 139, 42, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Ch’aou, fifth year, 602–8, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 102–11, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 38–39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]"],"image":{"title":"The Sword of Goujian","desc":"The hilt and lower blade of the Sword of Goujian, two columns of bird-seal characters naming the king of Yue who had it made, with turquoise set into the guard.","credit":"Photograph by Siyuwj, Hubei Provincial Museum. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sword_of_Goujian%2C_2019-06-15_03.jpg","alt":"The base of a bronze sword blade patterned with a lattice, inscribed with archaic characters, above a guard inlaid with blue stone.","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Sword_of_Goujian%2C_2019-06-15_03.jpg/1280px-Sword_of_Goujian%2C_2019-06-15_03.jpg"}},
{"id":"cnh-160","num":160,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"At a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a square pit was dug, the victim killed over it, and everyone present smeared the blood on the sides of his mouth before the writing was buried.","questions":["A minister of Song used a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to try to end the constant wars, and fourteen states sent representatives to his own capital for it.","The presiding chief called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and attendance at it was itself the recognition of who was leading the states that year."],"answer":"covenant meeting","answerText":"covenant meeting","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7th – 5th centuries BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greatest assembly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Kuiqiu, 650 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace conference</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14 states, in the Song capital</span></div>","traditional":"會盟","hanzi":"会盟","pinyin":"huì méng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Covenant meeting</b> is the name for the assemblies at which the lords of the Spring and Autumn states swore agreements, and they are what the period had in place of a government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ceremony was fixed: a square pit was dug, the victim killed over it, its left ear cut off and its blood caught in an ornamented vessel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The president read the articles facing north, as though before the spirits of the sun and moon, after which everyone present smeared the blood on the sides of his mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The victim then went into the pit with the written articles laid on top of it and the whole was covered over, so that the agreement was buried with the animal that sealed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A chief could vary the form to make a point, and at the greatest of them one bound the victim and laid the writing upon it without killing it or smearing any mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the meetings were mostly about was the leadership, since the presiding chief called them and attendance was itself the recognition of who was leading that year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The most ambitious of them was a disarmament conference got up by a minister of Song who thought he would deserve well of the country if he could put an end to the constant wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Jin agreed for a reason it stated plainly: war destroys the people and eats up resources and is the greatest calamity of the small states, and if Jin refused Chu would agree and gain by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fourteen states sent representatives to the Song capital, Qi and Qin were excused the oath, and the compact narrowed to Jin, Chu and their adherents, who were to appear at each other’s courts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nothing was said about the service due to the kings of Zhou, and the peace did not hold; its author was sincere about ending the wars and also wanted a name and something more substantial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 118–22, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 437, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 294–95, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 152, 317, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Wong Weng Pok, “The Way of the Hegemons: Politics and Hegemony in the Sixth Century and Early Seventh Century North China” (PhD diss., University of Hawai‘i at Mānoa, 2024), 38–39, https://hdl.handle.net/10125/110220. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/20250602_Houma_covenants_in_the_Shanxi_Museum_%28111615%29.jpg/1920px-20250602_Houma_covenants_in_the_Shanxi_Museum_%28111615%29.jpg","title":"Houma covenant tablets","desc":"Jade and stone tablets inscribed with covenant oaths, buried at Houma in the Jin state and now in the Shanxi Museum","credit":"Photograph by Yumeto, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20250602_Houma_covenants_in_the_Shanxi_Museum_%28111615%29.jpg","alt":"Jade and stone tablets inscribed with covenant oaths, buried at Houma in the Jin state and now in the Shanxi Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-161","num":161,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"In <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a state's strength was counted in chariots, and the two sides camped, sent messages and formed up on ground both had agreed was suitable.","questions":["The deceptions of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> included tiger skins on the horses, banners carried back to feign a rout, and branches dragged behind chariots to raise its dust.","Looking back on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, Mencius found no righteous wars in it, since correction is what a supreme authority does to subjects and hostile states cannot correct one another."],"answer":"Spring and Autumn warfare","answerText":"Spring and Autumn warfare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">722 – 481 BCE, by the chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Largest force named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">700 chariots, at Chengpu</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Crew</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 to a car, with bow and dagger-axe</span></div>","traditional":"春秋戰爭","hanzi":"春秋战争","pinyin":"Chūnqiū zhànzhēng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Spring and Autumn warfare</b> was fought by aristocrats in chariots, on a scale a modern reader tends to overestimate and under conventions that were already being argued about at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A state’s strength was counted in chariots, and the 700 that Jin brought to its greatest battle were enough to break the largest army in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vehicle was a light two-wheeled car drawn by a team in a yoke-and-pole harness developed over centuries, carrying a driver, an archer and a man with a dagger-axe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chariots cannot work in a defile or a marsh, so a battle had to be arranged: the sides camped, sent messages and formed up on ground both had agreed was suitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The messages could be elaborate, one commander asking for the fight as a game his opposite number might watch leaning on the crossbar of his chariot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Whether the conventions bound anybody was a live question, and the classic case is a duke who would not attack an enemy still crossing a river, nor again before it had drawn up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was badly beaten and blamed for it, and answered that a superior man inflicts no second wound and takes no grey-haired prisoner.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His own minister of War replied that sharp weapons are what an army is for, that an enemy not mortally hurt should be struck again, and that sparing the old is submission under another name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The deceptions were there already in the same generation: tiger skins on the horses to panic an enemy’s, banners carried back to feign a rout, branches dragged behind chariots to raise its dust.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of it reaches us through one commentary whose battle narratives are compositions rather than reports, and Mencius, looking back, found no righteous wars in the period at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke He, twenty-second and twenty-eighth years, and Duke Seuen, twelfth year, 183, 209–11 and 319–20, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), 478–79, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 4–5 and 10–11, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]","Jessica Rawson, Konstantin Chugunov, Yegor Grebnev, and Limin Huan, “Chariotry and Prone Burials: Reassessing Late Shang China’s Relationship with Its Northern Neighbours,” <i>Journal of World Prehistory</i> 33, no. 2 (2020): 160–62, https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:51b8bf31-edcf-408d-a149-4bff736dcd89. [Open access]","Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Story about Qi Xi’s Recommendations and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 255 (February 2015): 1–2, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp255_zuozhuan_qi_xi.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Royal_Ge_Dagger-axe_of_Zhao_Meng.jpg/1920px-Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Royal_Ge_Dagger-axe_of_Zhao_Meng.jpg","title":"Bronze dagger-axe of Zhao Meng","desc":"A Spring and Autumn bronze ge, the weapon of the third man on a war chariot, inscribed for the Jin minister Zhao Meng","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Royal_Ge_Dagger-axe_of_Zhao_Meng.jpg","alt":"A Spring and Autumn bronze ge, the weapon of the third man on a war chariot, inscribed for the Jin minister Zhao Meng"}},
{"id":"cnh-162","num":162,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"After the Zhi chief flooded Jinyang and Han and Wei changed sides in 453 BCE, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carved the greatest northern state into three marquisates the Zhou king later recognised.","questions":["In the year the nine Zhou tripods were struck by lightning, the king ordered Han, Wei and Zhao to become lords, which made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a legal fact rather than a private carve-up.","Of Jin's eight clans only two were native, and after the rest had eaten each other the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left three marquisates where one state had been, the last duke deposed in 376 BCE."],"answer":"partition of Jin","answerText":"partition of Jin","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhi destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">453 BCE, third month</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made lords</span><span class=\"dt-v\">403 BCE, by the Zhou king</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Marquisates</span><span class=\"dt-v\">402 BCE, by Legge’s reckoning</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last duke</span><span class=\"dt-v\">deprived in 376 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"三家分晉","hanzi":"三家分晋","pinyin":"Sān jiā fēn Jìn","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>partition of Jin</b> is the division of the greatest northern state among three of its own ministerial houses, and the conventional break between the Spring and Autumn age and the one that follows. Jin's government had passed to a handful of great families: of its eight powerful clans only the Qi and the Yangshe were natives, so destroying those two left the ducal house without allies at home.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strongest of the survivors was the head of the Zhi, who demanded land of Han and of Wei and was given it, and demanded land of Zhao and was refused. Angered, he took Han and Wei with him against Zhao, whose head shut himself up in the city of Jinyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The three besieged it for more than a year and then turned a river onto the walls, until three courses of rampart stood above the water, pots were slung in the air, and the account says children were exchanged and eaten. <br><br> What broke the siege was a night embassy: the Zhao chief sent his counsellor secretly to Han and Wei, who changed sides, and in the third month of 453 BCE the three destroyed the Zhi and shared out its lands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The victor had his enemy's skull lacquered into a drinking cup, and a retainer of the dead earl spent the rest of his life trying to avenge him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Jin now had three masters, and the annals record that in 403 BCE the nine tripods of the Zhou were struck by lightning and the king ordered Han, Wei and Zhao to become lords.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The independent existence of the three marquisates is dated from 402 BCE, and in 376 BCE they divided what was left of Jin between them and deprived the last duke of his state and his title.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Three of the seven states that fought the next two centuries were made out of one, and none was ruled by the house the Zhou king had enfeoffed.","sources":["Jens Østergaard Petersen, “The Zuozhuan Story about Qi Xi’s Recommendations and Its Sources,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 255 (February 2015): 35, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp255_zuozhuan_qi_xi.pdf. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 43 and 48–49, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Annales des Tcheou, 300 and 304, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Yü Jang,” 957–58, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 108, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Jian%2C_Jin_State.jpg/1920px-Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Jian%2C_Jin_State.jpg","title":"Bronze vessel of Jin","desc":"A Spring and Autumn bronze vessel of the state of Jin, in the Shanxi Provincial Museum at Taiyuan","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spring_%26_Autumn_Bronze_Jian%2C_Jin_State.jpg","alt":"A Spring and Autumn bronze vessel of the state of Jin, in the Shanxi Provincial Museum at Taiyuan"}},
{"id":"cnh-163","num":163,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Between the carving up of Jin and the Qin conquest of 221 BCE lies the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, when the Zhou king counted for nothing and travelling persuaders sold alliances from court to court.","questions":["Qin ate its neighbours from west to east while Chu built leagues from north to south, and the two centuries of that struggle are called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ending with the first Chinese empire.","The chariot gave way to cavalry, the lords took the title of king, and the record of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thin because Qin burnt the chronicles of the states in 213 BCE."],"answer":"Warring States period","answerText":"Warring States period","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Dated variously</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 480, 403 or 376 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE, with the Qin conquest</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Length</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 250 years</span></div>","traditional":"戰國時代","hanzi":"战国时代","pinyin":"Zhànguó shídài","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Warring States period</b> is the age that closes the Zhou dynasty, and it has no agreed beginning. One convention opens it in 403 BCE, when the Zhou king made lords of the three families that had carved up Jin; another waits for 376 BCE, when they finished that state off; a third takes about 480 BCE, where the chronicle of Lu leaves off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Whichever is chosen, the change is the same: the central power weakened by the day, the vassals became independent in fact, and, checked by no higher authority, tore one another apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lords behaved as independent princes and went so far as to take the title of king, and small states were annexed without pause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two powers that had held the middle together were gone: Jin divided, and Qi taken from the Jiang house by the Tian about 391 BCE, and with them went the check they had kept on Qin in the west and Chu in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Those two became the protagonists: Qin pressed east from the west, eating territory, as one description has it, like a silkworm gnawing a mulberry leaf, while Chu built leagues from north to south against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The states between them followed no fixed line, and were visited constantly by clever and unscrupulous men who argued for and against every question and moved from court to court as suited them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their subtle reasonings survive in the Intrigues of the Warring States, which is the chief source for the age because the alternatives were burnt in 213 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The fighting changed with everything else: the chariot's military role fell away sharply as mounted troops came in, and King Wuling of Zhao argued for riding astride and for trousers instead of a chariot crew's robes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Two centuries of this made the first Chinese empire: when a king of Liang asked Mencius what counsels he had brought to profit his kingdom, the answer was benevolence and righteousness, and nobody took it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, CL–CLI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Note préliminaire, 1–3, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 75–76, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 14, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), Bk. I, pt. I, ch. 1, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Two_Tone_Set-bells_of_Marquis_Yi_of_Zeng_%2810166273994%29.jpg/1920px-Two_Tone_Set-bells_of_Marquis_Yi_of_Zeng_%2810166273994%29.jpg","title":"Bells of Marquis Yi of Zeng","desc":"Part of the tuned bronze bell set buried about 433 BCE with Marquis Yi of Zeng, now in the Hubei Provincial Museum","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_Tone_Set-bells_of_Marquis_Yi_of_Zeng_%2810166273994%29.jpg","alt":"Part of the tuned bronze bell set buried about 433 BCE with Marquis Yi of Zeng, now in the Hubei Provincial Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-164","num":164,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Qin in the west, Chu in the south, Qi in the east, Yan in the north and the three heirs of Jin are the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which one was left by 221 BCE.","questions":["Su Qin brought six of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a league against the seventh in 333 BCE, and his fellow student Zhang Yi spent his life pulling that league apart for Qin.","Five of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counted as Middle Kingdoms and two did not, and it was one of the two outsiders that swallowed the rest and ended the Zhou dynasty."],"answer":"seven warring states","answerText":"seven warring states","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Six-state league</span><span class=\"dt-v\">333 BCE, by Su Qin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Su Qin died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">318 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhang Yi died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">310 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Last state taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE, by Qin</span></div>","traditional":"戰國七雄","hanzi":"战国七雄","pinyin":"Zhànguó qīxióng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"The <b>seven warring states</b> are what the Zhou world had been reduced to: Qin in the west, Chu in the south, Qi in the east, Yan in the north, and Zhao, Wei and Han between them. Five of them counted as Middle Kingdoms — the three heirs of Jin, with Qi and Yan — while Qin and Chu were reckoned outside that circle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qin was the least promising of the seven, an insignificant and backward country on the far western border, cut off by a belt of highlands and taking little part in Chinese civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within a century it was stronger than any of the others, and the states it threatened spent the age making combinations against it that bad faith and jealous rivalry broke each time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two policies had names: a vertical league running north to south, and the horizontal extension from west to east by which Qin picked the middle states off one at a time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The men who sold those plans were the celebrities of the age: in 333 BCE Su Qin brought Yan, Zhao, Han, Wei, Qi and Chu into one league against Qin and managed it from one allied court after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His fellow student Zhang Yi took the other side, entering Qin's service and working to bring the allies under his master; he was made foreign minister and led a campaign against his own native Wei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the end Zhang Yi persuaded all six to acknowledge Qin as supreme, and he outlived Su Qin, whom faction and then an assassin caught once the league had broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Both had begun as servants in a school, copying the pupils' exercises onto their palms to learn to read; beaten once on a false charge of theft, Zhang Yi asked his wife only whether he still had his tongue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Qin took the rest one by one and put an end to the Zhou dynasty, so the seven are a list counted backwards from the one that won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Note préliminaire, 1–3, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, CL–CLI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 626, 191–92, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chang I,” 27–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 5, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/EN-WarringStatesAll260BCE.jpg/1920px-EN-WarringStatesAll260BCE.jpg","title":"The seven states about 260 BCE","desc":"A modern political map of China during the Warring States period, showing Qin, Chu, Qi, Yan, Zhao, Wei and Han with the Zhou royal enclave between them","credit":"Map by Philg88, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-WarringStatesAll260BCE.jpg","alt":"A political map of China about 260 BCE with the seven Warring States shown in different colours"}},
{"id":"cnh-165","num":165,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Anyi, then Daliang, was the capital during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which made the first great power of the Warring States out of one of the three shares of Jin.","questions":["A fief given to a chariot fighter in 661 BCE grew into the state whose ascendancy under the marquis called Wen is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and Qin destroyed it in 225 BCE.","When Qin considered attacking, an adviser said the prince honoured wise men and his people praised him, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went unchecked for a generation after 424 BCE."],"answer":"rise of Wei","answerText":"rise of Wei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fief granted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">661 BCE, to Bi Wan</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Marquis Wen</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 424 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made lords</span><span class=\"dt-v\">403 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Destroyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">225 BCE, by Qin</span></div>","traditional":"魏國崛起","hanzi":"魏国崛起","pinyin":"Wèi guó juéqǐ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>rise of Wei</b> is the making of the first great power of the Warring States, and it was built out of one of the three shares of Jin. The house took its name from a fief conferred in 661 BCE on Bi Wan, who had ridden as the right-hand man in the duke of Jin’s chariot on the expedition that destroyed Huo, Geng and Wei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About fifty years later its head moved his seat to Huo, and a successor settled at Anyi, which tradition made the old capital of the Xia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The state’s rise begins with the marquis known as Wen, whose first year was 424 BCE: he walled Shaoliang, took towns from his neighbours, and fought Qin in the west, walling Linjin and Yuanli against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 408 BCE he attacked Zhongshan and left his son to hold it, and five years later Wei, Zhao and Han were placed in the rank of lords by the Zhou king.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The three marquisates are dated from 402 BCE, though Jin itself was not finished off until 376, when they divided what was left of it between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For a time Wei was the strongest state in China, and its rivals said so: when Qin thought of attacking it, an adviser answered that its prince honoured wise men, that his people praised his goodness, and that no plan could yet be laid against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That did not last, because Qin's advance made Anyi untenable, and in 340 BCE the king of Wei moved his capital east to Daliang, after which the kingdom was usually called Liang instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The man who took the first great slice of it for Qin was Zhang Yi, a native of Wei who had gone over to the enemy and who led the campaign against his own country in 328 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wei was destroyed by Qin in 225 BCE, so the state that opened the age did not live to see the end of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Wei, 132–33 and 137, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Wei, 138–43, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 76, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 108, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chang I,” 27–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Warring_States_Spade-shaped_Coins_of_Wei_State.jpg/1920px-Warring_States_Spade-shaped_Coins_of_Wei_State.jpg","title":"Spade coins of Wei","desc":"Bronze spade-shaped coins issued by the state of Wei, in the Eastern Zhou gallery of the Henan Provincial Museum","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Spade-shaped_Coins_of_Wei_State.jpg","alt":"Bronze spade-shaped coins issued by the state of Wei, in the Eastern Zhou gallery of the Henan Provincial Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-166","num":166,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Marquis Wen of Wei took his economics from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who worked out that a peasant family of five ended the year 450 coppers short before any misfortune was counted.","questions":["Grain too dear hurts the buyer and grain too cheap hurts the grower, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose remedy became the ever-normal granary China kept for two thousand years.","The Han imperial catalogue filed the lost book of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the school of law, though his surviving arithmetic is about grain prices and the yield of a hundred mu."],"answer":"Li Kui","answerText":"Li Kui","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Served</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Marquis Wen of Wei, 424 – 387 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Farm reckoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">100 mu at 150 piculs a year</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shortfall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">450 coppers a year</span></div>","traditional":"李悝","hanzi":"李悝","pinyin":"Lǐ Kuī","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Li Kui</b> was the councillor of Marquis Wen of Wei, and the earliest of the men whose reforms made the Warring States a contest of administration as much as of arms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The historian calls him Li Ke; he is credited with a legal code, now lost, and with a book that the Han imperial catalogue filed under the school of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What survives of his thinking is arithmetic: he reckoned that one man must feed five mouths, that 100 mu of land returned about 150 piculs of grain, and that a tenth of that went in tax.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ninety piculs fed the family for a year, leaving 45 to sell at 30 coppers each; 300 coppers went on village obligations and the sacrifices of spring and autumn, and clothing for five cost 1,500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The household therefore ended the year 450 coppers short, before anything at all was allowed for misfortune, illness or a death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His answer was to raise what the land gave and to steady what it fetched. Within a hundred square li there were nine million mu, a third of it mountain, marsh and town; intensive cultivation added three pecks to each of the remaining six million and slackness lost as much, so diligence and neglect stood 1,800,000 piculs apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On price he argued that grain too dear hurts the consumer and grain too cheap hurts the farmer, that a hurt consumer emigrates and a hurt farmer leaves the state poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The remedy was for the government to buy the surplus of good years by grades and sell it back in bad ones, which is the ancestor of the ever-normal granary China kept for two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> His scheme served the people rather than the treasury, and where Wei carried it out the people grew rich and the state strong;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mencius put the same case from the other side, asking a king how shut granaries in a famine differed from stabbing a man.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 43 and 51–52, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 97, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 568–71, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Wei, 143–46, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), Bk. I, pt. I, ch. 3, 132, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"cnh-167","num":167,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Bu Zixia taught him, Duangan Mu refused his salary and Li Kui served him: <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled from 424 to 387 BCE and made Wei the first great Warring States power.","questions":["Told that Wei's prince honoured wise men and that his people praised his goodness, Qin gave up attacking <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose court produced the first reforming government of the age.","Asked to choose between two men for counsellor, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was given five tests by Li Kui instead of a name, and picked the one who gave nine tenths of his salary away."],"answer":"Marquis Wen of Wei","answerText":"Marquis Wen of Wei","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">424 – 387 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhongshan taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">408 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made lord</span><span class=\"dt-v\">403 BCE, by the Zhou king</span></div>","traditional":"魏文侯","hanzi":"魏文侯","pinyin":"Wèi Wén hóu","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Marquis Wen of Wei</b> ruled from 424 to 387 BCE and is the first Warring States prince remembered for the men he gathered rather than the battles he won.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was one of the three ministerial heirs who had divided Jin, and in 403 BCE the Zhou king made the three of them lords; the dukes of Jin were by then puppets in his hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He campaigned steadily, walling Shaoliang, Linjin and Yuanli, fighting Qin in the west, and taking Zhongshan in 408 BCE for his son to garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His court was known for something else: he took the aged Bu Zixia, a disciple of Confucius, as his master, and honoured Duangan Mu, who would accept neither office nor salary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is said to have stood before Duangan Mu until he tired, and to have bowed from his chariot rail whenever he passed the man's gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> When Qin considered attacking him, an adviser answered that the prince of Wei honoured wise men, that his people exalted his goodness, and that prince and subjects being of one mind, no plan could yet be laid against him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Choosing between two candidates for counsellor, he put the question to Li Kui, who declined to judge his betters and gave a rule instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Look, he said, at whom a man loves at home, at what he gives away when rich, at whom he recommends when raised, at what he abstains from without resources, and at what he refuses when poor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The disappointed candidate protested that he had found the governor of the western march, the prefect of Ye and the general who took Zhongshan; the answer was that the man chosen gave nine tenths of his salary away and had thereby found Bu Zixia and Duangan Mu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That court produced the grain policy that made the people rich and the state strong, and every surviving book of the school of law is ascribed to a man of the three Jin or of neighbouring Qin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Wei, 141–46, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Wei, 138–40, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 97, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 568–70, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 1, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Prolegomena, 108, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics51legg. [Open access]"]},
{"id":"cnh-168","num":168,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"The pole at the south gate, the branded tutor and the innkeeper who turned him away all belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the minister who made Qin capable of conquering China.","questions":["A dying Wei minister advised his king either to employ <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or to kill him; the king did neither, and Qin got him instead about 350 BCE.","Fifteen cities at Shang gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the name he is known by, and in 338 BCE the son of his dead patron had him torn apart by chariots."],"answer":"Shang Yang","answerText":"Shang Yang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Entered Qin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 350 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ennobled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">340 BCE, 15 cities at Shang</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE, torn apart by chariots</span></div>","traditional":"商鞅","hanzi":"商鞅","pinyin":"Shāng Yāng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Shang Yang</b> was the minister who made Qin into the state that conquered China, condemned by generations of Confucians and a great statesman even so, caring more for the glory of the state than the good of its people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An illegitimate son of the ruling house of Wei, his real name was Gongsun Yang and he began as assistant to the Wei minister Gongshu Cuo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cuo on his deathbed told his king to give the young man his own office or else kill him, lest those talents serve another state; the king did neither.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Yang went west to Qin about 350 BCE, persuaded Duke Xiao that imperial dignity was within reach, and set about the administration as the only means of getting it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before publishing his code he set a pole at the capital’s south gate and offered ten ounces of gold to whoever would carry it to the north gate; when nobody moved he raised the offer to fifty, and paid the man who did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The crown prince then broke the law, and since an heir could not be executed his tutor was punished and his teacher branded; the next day, the record says, all Qin hurried into the law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ten years on, things dropped in the road went unpicked up and there were no robbers in the mountains; those who came to say the laws were good after all were banished as disorderly persons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 340 BCE he seized the Wei commander at a parley and broke his leaderless army, for which Qin gave him 15 cities at Shang and the title he is known by.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A visitor named Zhao Liang told him he ruled by fear and would fall the moment his patron died; five months later Duke Xiao died.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He was refused a bed at an inn under his own law against lodging an unidentified guest, driven back out of Wei, killed in battle and torn apart by chariots, and his family was exterminated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Wei Yang,” 868–70, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 14–17, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 23–30, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 4 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1901), Maison héréditaire de Tch’ou, 384, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto02chgoog. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 530–31, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/%E5%95%86%E9%9E%85%E6%96%B9%E5%8D%87_163842.jpg/1920px-%E5%95%86%E9%9E%85%E6%96%B9%E5%8D%87_163842.jpg","title":"The Shang Yang measure","desc":"A bronze standard grain measure inscribed with the year of its making, in the Shanghai Museum","credit":"Photograph by Hpppp0527, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%95%86%E9%9E%85%E6%96%B9%E5%8D%87_163842.jpg","alt":"A bronze standard grain measure inscribed with the year of its making, in the Shanghai Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-169","num":169,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Groups of five households answerable for one another, rank by military merit alone and one standard of weights and measures are <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which turned Qin into a machine for war.","questions":["Incomers from the neighbouring states got prepared farms and three generations free of service under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that Qin's own people could be sent to fight abroad.","The old field grid went and private ownership replaced it under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which bought the state an immediate advantage and cost the people the equality that had rested on land."],"answer":"Shang Yang's reforms","answerText":"Shang Yang's reforms","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begun</span><span class=\"dt-v\">356 BCE, under Duke Xiao</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Districts made</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31, about 350 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Field grid ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"商鞅變法","hanzi":"商鞅变法","pinyin":"Shāng Yāng biànfǎ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Shang Yang's reforms</b> were the remaking of Qin from about 356 BCE, and they are the clearest case in Chinese history of a state rebuilt in order to fight. The people were organised into groups of five and ten households answerable for one another: failing to denounce a criminal was punished by being cut in two, denouncing one was rewarded as beheading an enemy was, and hiding one was punished as surrendering was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A household holding two grown men that did not divide paid double tax, and fathers, sons and brothers were forbidden to live under one roof.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Rank came from military merit alone, on a ladder of degrees, and a member of the ruling house who had none could not be counted as belonging to the clan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Tilling and weaving were declared the fundamental occupations, so that whoever produced much grain or silk was exempted from forced labour, while those who followed lesser trades or were poor through idleness were taken as slaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Towns and villages were combined into 31 districts under prefects and their assistants, taxes were equalised, and weights, scales and measures of length and quantity were standardised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The old field grid was abrogated and individual ownership put in its place, the tithing system was introduced, and the capital was moved to Xianyang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He opened the paths and boundaries between the fields for extensive cultivation and let people take as much land as they would work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The point of that was manpower: Qin had more land than people and its neighbours more people than land, so incomers were offered prepared farms and three generations free of military service while natives went to the wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The economic measures were made for military reasons, and what the people lost was the equality that had rested on how land was held;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mencius, in the same years, said a minister who enlarged his master's fields and filled his arsenals used once to be called a robber of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Wei Yang,” 868–70, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 506, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 530–31, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1861), Bk. VI, pt. II, ch. 9, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Eighteen_Laws_of_Qin_on_Qin_Bamboo_Slips_%2810160622885%29.jpg/1920px-Eighteen_Laws_of_Qin_on_Qin_Bamboo_Slips_%2810160622885%29.jpg","title":"Qin statutes on bamboo","desc":"Bamboo slips carrying Qin statutes, excavated at Shuihudi and now in the Hubei Provincial Museum","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eighteen_Laws_of_Qin_on_Qin_Bamboo_Slips_%2810160622885%29.jpg","alt":"Bamboo slips carrying Qin statutes, excavated at Shuihudi and now in the Hubei Provincial Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-170","num":170,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Immigrants got farms and three generations free of military service in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that the men born there could be sent east to fight instead.","questions":["Rank by heads taken and a ladder of 18 honorary degrees outlasted their author by 117 years, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came to conquer the rest of China.","Shang Yang was torn apart by chariots in 338 BCE and not one of his laws was repealed, which is what made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the power that swallowed the other six."],"answer":"Qin state after Shang Yang","answerText":"Qin state after Shang Yang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Qin revives</span><span class=\"dt-v\">364 BCE, noted by the Zhou king</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reformer killed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">338 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Rank ladder</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 degrees, later 20</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"The <b>Qin state after Shang Yang</b> is the machine his laws left behind, which outlived him by 117 years and used them to conquer the rest of China. Qin had been an insignificant and backward country on the far western border, cut off by a belt of highlands and taking little part in the life of Chinese civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its recovery is dated in the annals to 364 BCE, when the Zhou king complimented Duke Xian and Qin began to be powerful again; by 340 BCE it was taking territory from Wei and pushing south into Chu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reformer himself was executed in 338 BCE by the heir whose tutor he had punished, and not one of his laws was repealed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What they had built was a ladder, and its price list was published: one head taken gave a man one degree of rank and an office worth 50 piculs of grain, two heads two degrees and an office worth 100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The ladder ran to 18 degrees, an honorary rank held beside whatever office a man actually filled, and it outlasted the dynasty that made it, since the Han took the whole system over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nothing on it was hereditary: a member of the ruling house without military merit could not be counted as belonging to the clan, and a man without merit, however rich, had no glory whatever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The economy was arranged to feed the armies, so incomers from the crowded states east of the passes were offered prepared farms and three generations free of service while the men born in Qin went to the wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The state then did what it had been built to do, annexing its neighbours one after another until the last of them fell in 221 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What followed made a deep and lasting mark: its first emperor swept the institutions of the past away, and with him the ancient history of China closes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 5–6, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 61–62, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Wei Yang,” 868–70, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 4 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1901), Maison héréditaire de Tch’ou, 384, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto02chgoog. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 506 and 530–31, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Qin_Bronze_Weight_with_Inscribed_Edicts_%2814183835520%29.jpg/1920px-Qin_Bronze_Weight_with_Inscribed_Edicts_%2814183835520%29.jpg","title":"A Qin bronze weight","desc":"A bronze weight cast by the Qin state and inscribed with its edicts, in the Qin Shihuang Museum at Lintong","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_Bronze_Weight_with_Inscribed_Edicts_%2814183835520%29.jpg","alt":"A bronze weight cast by the Qin state and inscribed with its edicts, in the Qin Shihuang Museum at Lintong"}},
{"id":"cnh-171","num":171,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"One head taken bought a degree of rank in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where conscripts in mutually answerable groups of five replaced the aristocrats who had fought from chariots.","questions":["Crossbows cast in matching marked parts and cavalry commanded apart from the cars are what distinguish <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from the chariot fighting of the age before it.","Mencius called men who boast of skill at marshalling troops great criminals, which is his verdict on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fought by standing armies for two centuries."],"answer":"Warring States warfare","answerText":"Warring States warfare","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 480 – 221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Head bounty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 head, 1 degree of rank</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhao’s army</span><span class=\"dt-v\">305 BCE, four commands</span></div>","traditional":"戰國戰爭","hanzi":"战国战争","pinyin":"Zhànguó zhànzhēng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Warring States warfare</b> is what fighting became when the chariot aristocracy gave way to standing armies of commoners, and it is why the age is named after its wars. The military role of the chariot declined sharply as mounted troops came in, and by the end of the period the horse was commanded separately from the cars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhao's attack on Zhongshan in 305 BCE shows the shape of an army: generals of the right, the left and the centre with the king commanding them all, and one officer set over the chariots and the cavalry together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The men were found by conscription and held by law: Qin grouped its households in fives and tens answerable for one another, and the result was said to be a people brave in the state’s wars and timid in private ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Advancement was bought with heads: one head taken gave a man a degree of rank and an office worth 50 piculs of grain, and two heads gave two degrees and an office worth 100.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The weapons were made in quantity and to a standard, and the crossbow, the most complex piece of equipment the period produced, was assembled from separately cast bronze parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their makers marked them, so that matching pieces stayed together and the hand of one workshop can still be traced across a pit of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sieges and long campaigns replaced the single arranged battle, and a frontier could now be a wall: an earlier ruler of Zhao raised a long one barring the difficult ground of the Zhang and the Fu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> War had also become permanent: a king of Zhao told his council that Zhongshan lay on his state’s belly and heart, that the Hu were east of him and the Linhu and Loufan west, and that without brave soldiers the altars of soil and grain would be ruined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mencius, watching it, said that men who boast of being skilful at marshalling troops and conducting a battle are great criminals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 14, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 14–17, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 61, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 70–71 and 85, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Andrew Bevan, Xiuzhen Li, Zhen Zhao, Jianhua Huang, Stuart Laidlaw, Na Xi, Yin Xia, Shengtao Ma, and Marcos Martinón-Torres, “Ink Marks, Bronze Crossbows and Their Implications for the Qin Terracotta Army,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 6, no. 1 (2018): art. 75, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-018-0239-5. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), Bk. VII, pt. II, ch. 4, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Warring_States_Bronze_Halberd_with_3_Ge_%26_Mao_%289873538323%29.jpg/1920px-Warring_States_Bronze_Halberd_with_3_Ge_%26_Mao_%289873538323%29.jpg","title":"Blades of a halberd","desc":"Warring States bronze dagger-axe blades from a halberd, from Suizhou in Hubei","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Halberd_with_3_Ge_%26_Mao_%289873538323%29.jpg","alt":"Warring States bronze dagger-axe blades from a halberd, from Suizhou in Hubei"}},
{"id":"cnh-172","num":172,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Stored energy released by a finger, several cast parts marked to keep them matched, and a stock hiding the marks describe the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the standard arm of the Warring States.","questions":["A man of Chen hid a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at the stable door in 599 BCE and shot his duke dead with it, which is among its earliest appearances in the record.","Men on Han reliefs and pottery watch-towers shoot the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than the bow, a preference that had begun two centuries before the empire."],"answer":"Chinese crossbow","answerText":"Chinese crossbow","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Killing recorded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">599 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Standard arm</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 480 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Qin triggers</span><span class=\"dt-v\">buried c. 210 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"弩","hanzi":"弩","pinyin":"nǔ","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"abstract":"The <b>Chinese crossbow</b> is a bow set crosswise on a stock and held at full draw by a bronze trigger, and it is the weapon that made a missile army out of men who were not archers. Its virtue is that the effort of drawing is separated from the moment of shooting: the force is stored in the bent bow until a finger on the trigger releases it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chinese writers hardly explain the thing because they assumed it, and it appears in their manuals as equipment rather than novelty, shooting fire arrows into an enemy line or waiting in ambush at a camp gate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One turns up in the record well before the Warring States: in 599 BCE a man of Chen hid a crossbow at the stable door and shot his duke dead with it as he came out from drinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the Warring States did was make it the standard arm, and the most complex piece of military equipment the period produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A Qin trigger is not one casting but several, and its parts carry inked and chiselled marks — ordinary characters, numerals, the stems-and-branches notation and symbols nobody has read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those marks were covered by the wooden stock and invisible to the man shooting, so they were made for the workshop and the arsenal rather than for the soldier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Parts cast in the same or similar moulds turn out to group together, which means matching pieces stayed together through assembly and the hand of one workshop cell can be picked out of a pit of weapons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It stayed the Chinese soldier’s weapon: the men on Han reliefs and on the pottery watch-tower models shoot crossbows rather than bows, and their armourers had to reckon with what a bolt would go through.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chariot went out as the crossbow and the mounted archer came in, and by the end of the period the cars were one arm among several rather than the army itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew Bevan, Xiuzhen Li, Zhen Zhao, Jianhua Huang, Stuart Laidlaw, Na Xi, Yin Xia, Shengtao Ma, and Marcos Martinón-Torres, “Ink Marks, Bronze Crossbows and Their Implications for the Qin Terracotta Army,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 6, no. 1 (2018): art. 75, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-018-0239-5. [Open access]","Berthold Laufer, <i>Chinese Clay Figures, Part I: Prolegomena on the History of Defensive Armor</i>, Field Museum of Natural History, Anthropological Series 13, no. 2 (Chicago, 1914), 189–90 and 208, https://archive.org/details/chineseclayfigu13lauf. [Open access]","Lionel Giles, trans., <i>Sun Tzŭ on the Art of War: The Oldest Military Treatise in the World</i> (London: Luzac, 1910), 38 and 149, https://archive.org/details/artofwaroldestmi00suntuoft. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 4 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1901), chap. XXXVI, Maison héréditaire de Tch’en et Ki, under the fifteenth year of Duke Xuan (599 BCE), https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto02chgoog. [Open access]","Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 14, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Warring_States_Bronze_Crossbow_Trigger_Mechanism.jpg/1920px-Warring_States_Bronze_Crossbow_Trigger_Mechanism.jpg","title":"The lock of a crossbow","desc":"A Warring States bronze crossbow trigger mechanism, in the Hebei Museum at Shijiazhuang","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Crossbow_Trigger_Mechanism.jpg","alt":"A Warring States bronze crossbow trigger mechanism, in the Hebei Museum at Shijiazhuang"}},
{"id":"cnh-173","num":173,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Registered households in answerable groups of five, districts under appointed prefects and rank by heads taken are how the Warring States raised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a size the chariot age never saw.","questions":["Qin is recorded as destroying 240,000 men at Yique and more than 400,000 at Shangdang, which is the scale of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the two centuries before the empire.","Mencius said the rulers of Qin and Chu robbed their people of the time to plough and weed, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost the countryside that supplied them."],"answer":"mass infantry armies","answerText":"mass infantry armies","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Yique</span><span class=\"dt-v\">293 BCE, 240,000 lost</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Shangdang</span><span class=\"dt-v\">259 BCE, over 400,000</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Han annexed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">230 BCE, by Qin</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Mass infantry armies</b> are what the Warring States fought with, and the figures the record gives for them are its most startling numbers. In 293 BCE Qin defeated an army of 240,000 that Han had put in the field with help from Zhou and Wei, and took its commander prisoner at Yique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 259 BCE Qin took Shangdang from Zhao and killed more than 400,000 of its soldiers, and the annals of Qi give the same figure for Changping, after which Qin at once besieged the Zhao capital.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Armies of that size need a state that can find the men, so the reforms behind them were administrative: villages and small towns were combined into districts under appointed prefects, taxes were equalised, and weights and measures standardised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Households were registered and grouped in fives and tens answerable for one another's offences, so a levy could be raised off a list instead of from a lord's retinue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Service was the price of holding land, and Qin stated the bargain plainly, offering incomers from the crowded states east of the passes prepared farms and three generations free of service so that the men born there could go to the wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Rank followed from fighting rather than from birth, which gave a peasant the one ladder out of his village that the old order had never offered him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What it cost the countryside was obvious to contemporaries, and Mencius told a king that the rulers of Qin and Chu robbed their people of their time, so that they could not plough and weed their fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> His own remedy was that a well-governed people could be sent with sticks they had cut themselves against strong mail and sharp weapons, and no ruler tried it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The armies went on growing until one state could field enough men to take all the others: Qin annihilated Han in 230 BCE and the rest within a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Han, 219 and 221, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de T’ien King-tchong Wan, 278, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 14–15, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 506, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), Bk. I, pt. I, ch. 5, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Qin_Terracotta_Warriors%2C_Pit_1_13.jpg/1920px-Qin_Terracotta_Warriors%2C_Pit_1_13.jpg","title":"An army in ranks","desc":"Ranked infantry figures in pit 1 of the terracotta army near Xi'an","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_Terracotta_Warriors%2C_Pit_1_13.jpg","alt":"Ranked infantry figures in pit 1 of the terracotta army near Xi'an"}},
{"id":"cnh-174","num":174,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"A man who drives a chariot out of books does not know the temper of horses, said <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who dressed his army like its enemies and taught it to shoot from horseback.","questions":["The assembled ministers opposed <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> when he proposed the coat that fastened on the left, and within two years the Linhu were sending him horses.","Zhongshan lay on the belly and heart of the state <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ruled from 325 to 299 BCE, which is the reason he gave for making his men ride and shoot."],"answer":"King Wuling of Zhao","answerText":"King Wuling of Zhao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Reigned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">325 – 299 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hu dress adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">307 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhongshan taken</span><span class=\"dt-v\">305 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"趙武靈王","hanzi":"赵武灵王","pinyin":"Zhào Wǔlíng wáng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>King Wuling of Zhao</b> ruled from 325 to 299 BCE and is remembered for one order: that his army should dress like its enemies and learn to shoot from horseback. His reason was strategic, and he set it out to his council: Zhongshan lay on his state's belly and heart, Yan was to the north, the Hu to the east, and the Linhu and Loufan to the west with the frontiers of Qin and Han beyond.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without brave soldiers, he said, the altars of soil and grain would be ruined, and a man whose renown rises above his own age is buried under the difficulties made for him in the name of custom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Hu coat fastened on the left where Chinese custom fastened it on the right, and the costume was narrow-waisted and close-fitting where a chariot crew wore robes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One adviser agreed with him and his assembled ministers were unanimously against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His answer became famous: a man who drives a chariot out of books does not know the temper of horses, and one who would govern the present out of antiquity does not understand what circumstances have changed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He adopted the Hu dress and enrolled mounted archers, and the result came quickly: in 306 BCE he toured the north as far as Yuzhong and the king of the Linhu made him a present of horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 305 BCE he took Zhongshan with generals of the right, the left and the centre under his own command and a separate officer over the chariots and the horse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What he created was mounted archery rather than a cavalry arm, since regular cavalry is dated to the Han, and the chariot’s military role fell away across the same two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The debate outlived the reform: the opening argument of the <i>Book of Lord Shang</i>, on whether a law may be changed at all, closely resembles this conversation, and both are preserved in the Intrigues of the Warring States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 46 and 69–71, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 84–85, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Berthold Laufer, <i>Chinese Clay Figures, Part I: Prolegomena on the History of Defensive Armor</i>, Field Museum of Natural History, Anthropological Series 13, no. 2 (Chicago, 1914), 222, https://archive.org/details/chineseclayfigu13lauf. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 146, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Joost H. Crouwel, Gail Brownrigg, and Katheryn Linduff, “From Chariot to Carriage: Wheeled Vehicles and Developments in Draft and Harnessing in Ancient China,” <i>Sino-Platonic Papers</i> 344 (May 2024): 14, http://www.sino-platonic.org/complete/spp344_chariot_to_carriage_in_ancient_china.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Warring_States_Bronze_Horse_Bits.jpg/1920px-Warring_States_Bronze_Horse_Bits.jpg","title":"Bits for riding","desc":"Warring States bronze horse bits, in the Zhongshan state gallery of the Hebei Museum","credit":"Photograph by Gary Lee Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Horse_Bits.jpg","alt":"Warring States bronze horse bits, in the Zhongshan state gallery of the Hebei Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-175","num":175,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"A levy of 480 catties to cast law tripods in 513 BCE and a plough share bought with grain are two records of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which reached the fields before the armouries.","questions":["Confucius said Jin would be ruined by casting its penal laws on tripods, which is a dated levy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and a fact about politics as much as metal.","Salt and iron monopolies, an ordinary plough share bought with grain, and bronze still arming the crossbow describe <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the two centuries before Qin."],"answer":"iron in the Warring States","answerText":"iron in the Warring States","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Jin levy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">513 BCE, 480 catties</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Plough share</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4th century BCE, in Mencius</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Iron armour</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Later Han, 25 – 220 CE</span></div>","traditional":"鐵","hanzi":"铁","pinyin":"tiě","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"abstract":"<b>Iron in the Warring States</b> is the material that changed what a Chinese farmer worked with and, more slowly, what an army was armed with. The clearest single date is 513 BCE, when two ministers of Jin levied on the districts of the state a measure of iron reckoned at 480 catties, in order to cast tripods and inscribe the penal laws on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Confucius said that Jin was going to ruin, because the people would study the tripods instead of honouring their betters, and where noble and mean are not distinguished a state cannot continue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An earlier code in Zheng had been engraved on iron tripods as well, and the man who rewrote it on bamboo tablets was executed by the minister who followed its author.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What both episodes record is a state able to requisition iron by weight, which is a different thing from a smith making a blade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> By the 4th century BCE the metal had reached the fields, and Mencius, arguing that nobody can make everything himself, asked whether a certain farmer ploughed with an iron share.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was told that the man did, and that he got it in exchange for grain, which incidentally records an iron share as an ordinary thing to buy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iron became a fiscal object too, since the salt and iron monopolies are credited to Guan Zhong, and a Confucian critic objected that a monopoly kept for revenue raises prices and is not the same as one kept for the public good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Armour changed last of all: hide could still be said to turn a crossbow bolt, and plates of iron are dated to the Later Han rather than to these two centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor did iron sweep bronze away, since the crossbow triggers buried with the First Emperor’s army a century after this period are bronze castings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 5, part 2, <i>The Ch’un Ts’ëw, with the Tso Chuen</i> (Hongkong: at the author’s; London: Trübner, 1872), Duke Chaou, twenty-ninth year, 732, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics52legg. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), Bk. III, pt. I, ch. 4, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 69–70, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Berthold Laufer, <i>Chinese Clay Figures, Part I: Prolegomena on the History of Defensive Armor</i>, Field Museum of Natural History, Anthropological Series 13, no. 2 (Chicago, 1914), 208, https://archive.org/details/chineseclayfigu13lauf. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 535, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","Andrew Bevan, Xiuzhen Li, Zhen Zhao, Jianhua Huang, Stuart Laidlaw, Na Xi, Yin Xia, Shengtao Ma, and Marcos Martinón-Torres, “Ink Marks, Bronze Crossbows and Their Implications for the Qin Terracotta Army,” <i>Heritage Science</i> 6, no. 1 (2018): art. 75, https://doi.org/10.1186/s40494-018-0239-5. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Warring_States_Iron_Tools_%2810077942803%29.jpg/1920px-Warring_States_Iron_Tools_%2810077942803%29.jpg","title":"Iron for work, not war","desc":"Warring States iron tools in the Hebei Provincial Museum at Shijiazhuang","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Iron_Tools_%2810077942803%29.jpg","alt":"Warring States iron tools in the Hebei Provincial Museum at Shijiazhuang"}},
{"id":"cnh-176","num":176,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Spades, knives, weights and round pieces with a square hole are the four families of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which Qin replaced with a single coin at the empire.","questions":["Bronze pieces nicknamed metallic cowries were issued because real cowries had grown scarce, which is where <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> begins and why its shapes imitate tools and blades.","A peasant budget pricing grain at 30 coppers a picul shows how far <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had reached into ordinary life two centuries before the first empire."],"answer":"Warring States coinage","answerText":"Warring States coinage","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Knife issues</span><span class=\"dt-v\">to 228 BCE, in Zhao</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peasant budget</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 coppers a picul of grain</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unified</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE, by Qin</span></div>","traditional":"戰國貨幣","hanzi":"战国货币","pinyin":"Zhànguó huòbì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Warring States coinage</b> is the bronze money of the seven states, and it falls into four families: weight money, spade money, knife money, and round coins pierced with a square hole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The spade imitated a farm tool and was cast square-footed, round-footed and point-footed, carrying the name of the town that issued it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The knife imitated a blade, and large issues of small knife coins were still being made in Zhao and in Qi down to the Qin conquest of Zhao in 228 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Round coins with a square hole and a raised edge were cast in clusters, several to a mould, which is how a mint kept up with demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Behind the shapes lies a change in what money was: the cowrie shells that had served as currency grew too scarce, and one southern king issued small bean-shaped bronze pieces, inscribed with their weights, that people nicknamed metallic cowries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Money was a matter of state before any of this, and Duke Huan of Qi, advised by Guan Zhong, established a profit on money, fish and salt to relieve the poor and reward capable men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Grain went on serving as a medium of exchange beside metal, and the classical account explains that a government coined more in a bad year precisely because grain could no longer be spared to circulate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The theory it preserves is a two-tier standard in which a dear coin and a cheap one circulate together, the dear called the mother and the cheap the son, with the mix adjusted to move prices.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ordinary life was reckoned in coin by the 4th century BCE: a peasant household budget priced grain at 30 coppers a picul and clothing at 300 a person, and Mencius assumed a farmer bought his tools in exchange for grain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Qin ended the variety along with the states, forbidding gems, pearls, tortoise shells, cowries and tin as currency and issuing one round bronze coin with a square hole, inscribed with its weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Terrien de Lacouperie, <i>Catalogue of Chinese Coins from the VIIth Cent. B.C. to A.D. 621, Including the Series in the British Museum</i>, ed. Reginald Stuart Poole (London: printed by order of the Trustees, 1892), v–viii and xiii–xv, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.110902. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 436–41, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 4 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1901), Maison héréditaire de T’ai kong de Ts’i, 48–49, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto02chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 43, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), Bk. III, pt. I, ch. 4, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Ancient_Chinese_Writing_on_a_Warring_States_Knife-shaped_Coin.jpg/1920px-Ancient_Chinese_Writing_on_a_Warring_States_Knife-shaped_Coin.jpg","title":"A coin shaped like a knife","desc":"A Warring States knife-shaped bronze coin carrying an inscription, in the Chinese Writing Museum at Anyang","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Chinese_Writing_on_a_Warring_States_Knife-shaped_Coin.jpg","alt":"A Warring States knife-shaped bronze coin carrying an inscription, in the Chinese Writing Museum at Anyang"}},
{"id":"cnh-177","num":177,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Handan changed hands twice in two years and Linzi was stripped of its treasures, which is the history of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose walls held mints and markets.","questions":["An inspector over every 20 shops, three trading periods a day and prices set by the government describe the markets of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which the coins name one by one.","Qin combined 31 districts out of the small feudal domains that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been, which is the political problem their independence had posed for centuries."],"answer":"Warring States cities","answerText":"Warring States cities","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Handan</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Zhao’s capital from 386 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Taken by Wei</span><span class=\"dt-v\">353 BCE, returned 351</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Qin’s districts</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31, about 350 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"戰國城市","hanzi":"战国城市","pinyin":"Zhànguó chéngshì","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Warring States cities</b> were walled capitals and market towns, and they are the age’s most visible change on the ground, since a state that had been a scatter of lordships now had a seat that could be named, taxed and taken. A capital could be moved as a matter of policy and then fought over: Zhao took Handan as its seat in 386 BCE, Wei besieged it in 354, took it in 353 and handed it back in 351.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Walls went up everywhere, and the annals of these states record building at named places in almost every reign, on frontiers as well as around towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A city was also a mint, and coins carry the names of the towns that issued them, so a spade or a knife names its own market.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Qi’s capital, Linzi, is known down to its gates, which a geographical commentary lists on the eastern side as the Lu gate and the Wulu and Zhanghua gates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What went on inside was regulated in detail, at least in the ideal accounts that survive, and those accounts describe a market laid out as deliberately as a camp.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Shops were grouped by the goods they sold, and trading ran in three periods: the busiest at noon and called the great market, with merchants dominating the morning and small buyers and sellers the evening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> An inspector was set over every 20 shops to forbid false goods and punish deceit, and prices were controlled by the government.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Politically these towns were the problem the age had to solve, since they had been small feudal domains enjoying a great measure of independence, and Qin’s answer was to combine them into 31 districts under appointed prefects and their assistants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When a city fell, what it held went with it: a general of Yan entered Linzi after Qi’s army had scattered and carried off all the treasures of the state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 55 and 61, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de T’ien King-tchong Wan, 266 and 271, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Chen Huan-Chang, <i>The Economic Principles of Confucius and His School</i>, vol. 2 (New York: Columbia University, 1911), 447, https://archive.org/details/economicprincipl02chen. [Open access]","Terrien de Lacouperie, <i>Catalogue of Chinese Coins from the VIIth Cent. B.C. to A.D. 621, Including the Series in the British Museum</i>, ed. Reginald Stuart Poole (London: printed by order of the Trustees, 1892), v–viii, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.110902. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Linzi_model_2010_06_06.jpg/1920px-Linzi_model_2010_06_06.jpg","title":"Linzi, modelled","desc":"A model of the Qi capital at Linzi, in the Museum of the Qi State","credit":"Photograph by Rolfmueller, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linzi_model_2010_06_06.jpg","alt":"A model of the Qi capital at Linzi, in the Museum of the Qi State"}},
{"id":"cnh-178","num":178,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Because one league ran north to south against Qin and the other ran west to east for it, the doctrines are called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and no middle state kept to either for long.","questions":["Qin devoured territory like a silkworm gnawing a mulberry leaf, which is the horizontal half of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the diplomacy that filled two centuries with broken leagues.","Mencius called the men who made alliances so a master’s battles must succeed robbers of the people, which is his verdict on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a profession."],"answer":"vertical and horizontal alliances","answerText":"vertical and horizontal alliances","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Six-state league</span><span class=\"dt-v\">333 BCE, against Qin</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Its author died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">318 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Qin supreme</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"合縱連橫","hanzi":"合纵连横","pinyin":"Hézòng liánhéng","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":5,"abstract":"<b>Vertical and horizontal alliances</b> are the two rival doctrines of Warring States diplomacy, named for the directions they ran on the map.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The vertical league, <i>he zong</i>, joined the states from north to south against Qin, while the horizontal, <i>lian heng</i>, was Qin’s own method of extending its influence continuously from west to east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chu, the less bold of the two great outsiders, contented itself with soliciting the alliance of the Chinese states to build the north-south league that might hold Qin in check.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qin was the more brutal, advancing gradually eastward and devouring territory, in one description, like a silkworm gnawing a mulberry leaf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The states in between — Zhao, Wei and Han, with Qi and Yan — followed no invariable line, joining now Chu and now Qin as the interest of the moment dictated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That is why the age produced a profession, and clever, unscrupulous men, ready to argue for and against any question, moved from court to court setting out the advantages of one combination or the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their subject had a name of its own, the doctrines of ‘combination and opposition’, which the most famous of them is said to have studied before he put them into practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The results were spectacular and short: a league of six states was formed against Qin in 333 BCE and internal faction destroyed it, and a rival persuader afterwards brought all six to acknowledge Qin as supreme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every such combination was broken at once by bad faith and jealous rivalry, which is what a succession of plots and leagues comes to in practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mencius had a word for the men who made them, saying that those who claim they can form alliances so that their master’s battles must be successful were anciently called robbers of the people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Note préliminaire, 1–3, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, CL–CLI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 626, 191–92, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chang I,” 27–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), Bk. VI, pt. II, ch. 9, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/%E5%86%B0%E9%9B%AA%E5%A0%82%E5%BC%B5%E5%84%80%E7%94%A8%E6%99%BA.jpg/1920px-%E5%86%B0%E9%9B%AA%E5%A0%82%E5%BC%B5%E5%84%80%E7%94%A8%E6%99%BA.jpg","title":"The persuaders, as a later stage saw them","desc":"A woodblock illustration of Zhang Yi using his wits, from a Ming anthology of Yuan plays in which Su Qin returns home in brocade","credit":"Zang Maoxun, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%86%B0%E9%9B%AA%E5%A0%82%E5%BC%B5%E5%84%80%E7%94%A8%E6%99%BA.jpg","alt":"A woodblock illustration of Zhang Yi from a Ming anthology of Yuan plays"}},
{"id":"cnh-179","num":179,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Fellow students who copied their lessons onto their palms, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took opposite sides and between them made and unmade the leagues of a century.","questions":["Beaten on a false charge of theft, one of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asked his wife only whether he still had his tongue, and said that was all he wanted.","The league of 333 BCE and the campaign of 328 belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose story survives as anecdote and speech rather than as record."],"answer":"Su Qin and Zhang Yi","answerText":"Su Qin and Zhang Yi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">League formed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">333 BCE, of six states</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Su Qin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">died 318 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Zhang Yi</span><span class=\"dt-v\">minister 328 BCE, died 310</span></div>","traditional":"蘇秦張儀","hanzi":"苏秦张仪","pinyin":"Sū Qín Zhāng Yí","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"abstract":"<b>Su Qin and Zhang Yi</b> are the two most famous persuaders of the Warring States, fellow students who took opposite sides and between them are credited with the diplomacy of a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both are said to have begun as servants in a school, picking up an education by copying the pupils' exercises onto their palms and legs and writing them out again at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Both then studied under the same master and became itinerant politicians looking for employment with one state or another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Su Qin took the federal cause and in 333 BCE brought Yan, Zhao, Han, Wei, Qi and Chu into a single league against Qin, managing it from one allied court after another until faction pulled it apart, and he was assassinated in 318.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhang Yi, a native of Wei, went into Qin’s service instead, was made foreign minister in 328, led a campaign against his own country and in the end persuaded all six states to acknowledge Qin as supreme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He outlived his old comrade and died in 310 as prime minister of the country he had helped to strip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The anecdotes attached to them are as durable as the policy: Zhang Yi, beaten on a false charge of stealing a gem, asked his wife only whether he still had his tongue, and when she said he had, answered that it was all he wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Su Qin is credited with the proverb that it is better to be a fowl’s beak than the hinder parts of an ox.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How much of any of it happened is another matter, because for these two centuries the historian had little but the speeches collected in the Intrigues of the Warring States and the anecdotes of literary men, tied together by chronological notes he himself found doubtful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A modern study has found the Su Qin story full of contradictions and impossibilities, which is what a romance looks like when it is read as a record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 626, 191–92, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly & Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chang I,” 27–28, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 38, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, CL–CLI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Note préliminaire, 2–3, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/ZhanGuoCe.jpeg","title":"The book that preserves them","desc":"The first page of an old edition of the Intrigues of the Warring States, the collection of speeches on which their story rests","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhanGuoCe.jpeg","alt":"The first page of an old edition of the Intrigues of the Warring States, the collection of speeches on which their story rests"}},
{"id":"cnh-180","num":180,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Wei's heir was warned that a victory would gain him nothing and a defeat cost him everything, and he fought anyway and lost at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where his general died.","questions":["Qi held back until Han had fought five losing battles, then sent Tian Ji and Sun Bin against a worn-out Wei army and destroyed it at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 341 BCE.","A Wei commander pursuing an enemy whose campfires shrank each night was killed at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the heir to the Wei throne was taken prisoner with him."],"answer":"Battle of Maling","answerText":"Battle of Maling","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">341 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or</span><span class=\"dt-v\">343 BCE, by another chronicle</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">disputed, in modern Shandong</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Maling</b> is the defeat that broke Wei, and it was won by a delay as much as by a stratagem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wei had resolved to destroy Han, and when Han appealed for help the king of Qi asked his ministers whether it was better to help early or late.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zou Ji said not to help, Tian Ji said Han would otherwise change sides, and Sun Bin answered that helping before Han and Wei had worn each other out meant taking Wei's blow for Han.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qi therefore made secret promises to Han's envoys, so Han fought five battles counting on help that never came, lost them all, and handed its kingdom eastward into Qi's keeping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only then did Qi take the field, with Tian Ji and Tian Ying as generals and Sun Bin as their director, killing the Wei general Pang Juan at Maling and capturing Shen, the heir apparent of Wei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Wei account adds the warning the heir was given: annexing Qi would make him no richer and no higher than king, and losing would cost his house Wei for ever.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He agreed and asked leave to turn back, and was told he could not, since those who wanted the battle were too many; his charioteer said a general who turns back has as good as been beaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stratagem is the famous part: Sun Bin, knowing Qi had a name for cowardice, showed 100,000 campfires on the first night, 50,000 on the second and 20,000 on the third, until Pang Juan pursued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One version has the pursuer shot down after dark by archers hidden at a stripped tree, and the histories, less dramatically, have him cut his own throat once his army was routed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Little is settled beyond that, the <i>Bamboo Annals</i> dating it to 343 BCE against the histories' 341<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the site being placed in Zhili by some and in Shandong by others;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> the next year Qin, Zhao and Qi combined against Wei.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de T’ien King-tchong Wan, 256–58, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Wei, 155–57, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Lionel Giles, trans., <i>Sun Tzŭ on the Art of War: The Oldest Military Treatise in the World</i> (London: Luzac, 1910), 40, https://archive.org/details/artofwaroldestmi00suntuoft. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 19, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 20 n. 1, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Inscribed_bamboo-slips_of_Sun_Bin%27s_Art_of_War.jpg/1920px-Inscribed_bamboo-slips_of_Sun_Bin%27s_Art_of_War.jpg","title":"The victor's own book","desc":"Bamboo slips inscribed with the military treatise of Sun Bin, recovered from a Han tomb at Yinqueshan","credit":"Photograph by AlexHe34, CC BY-SA 3.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscribed_bamboo-slips_of_Sun_Bin%27s_Art_of_War.jpg","alt":"Bamboo slips inscribed with the military treatise of Sun Bin, recovered from a Han tomb at Yinqueshan"}},
{"id":"cnh-181","num":181,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"A governor who would not hand his province to Qin gave it to Zhao instead, and the war that followed ended at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a surrendered army destroyed.","questions":["Zhao's king replaced a general who would not fight with one who would, and the new commander was surrounded and starved into surrender at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 260 BCE.","The worst recorded slaughter of the Warring States happened at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Qin killed a Zhao army that had already laid down its arms."],"answer":"Battle of Changping","answerText":"Battle of Changping","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">260 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Or</span><span class=\"dt-v\">259 BCE, in the Zhao chapter</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Site</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 li west of Gaoping, Shanxi</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Changping</b> began as a quarrel over a province and ended as the largest killing the record reports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Feng Ting, governing Shangdang for Han, would not give it up to Qin and offered it to Zhao, whose king was advised that seventeen towns could be had by sitting still and should not be let slip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhao's envoy came with marquisates for the governor and his prefects, three degrees of rank for every officer and commoner, and gold for any who kept the peace among themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Feng Ting wept and refused to see him, saying he would fail justice three times over: he had not defended his master's territory to the death, he had handed it away without orders, and he would now profit by the sale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Zhao sent troops to occupy Shangdang all the same, and Lian Po, a general who is remembered for declining battle and staying inside his entrenchments, encamped the army at Changping.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Lian Po was then relieved and Zhao Kuo put at the head of the army instead; the men of Qin surrounded him, he surrendered with more than four hundred thousand men, and all of them were killed, a figure one handbook raises to 450,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The <i>Book of Lord Shang</i> cites the battle to argue that taking territory by cutting off heads gained Qin nothing and cost it men beyond counting, a reference that dates its chapter a century after Shang Yang.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The king afterwards regretted not listening to Zhao Bao, whose advice would have spared him the disaster, and on returning to his capital he refused to submit to Qin, which besieged him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Feng Ting, who had been made prince of Huayang for the gift of Shangdang, died in the battle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Even the year is unsettled, the Zhao chapter placing these events in 259 BCE where the tables and the Qin annals put them in 260, and the site rests on a note fixing it 31 li west of Gaoping in Shanxi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 117–19, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 118 n. 4 and 119 nn. 1–4, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 272 and n. 2, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Chao Kua,” 69, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Lien P’o,” 483, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Warring_States_Bronze_Arrowhead.jpg/1920px-Warring_States_Bronze_Arrowhead.jpg","title":"What the armies shot","desc":"A Warring States bronze arrowhead, in the Chinese Writing Museum at Anyang","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Arrowhead.jpg","alt":"A Warring States bronze arrowhead, in the Chinese Writing Museum at Anyang"}},
{"id":"cnh-182","num":182,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Ennobled as prince of Wu'an for beating Zhao, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was later ordered to fight it again, refused, lost his master's favour and was made to kill himself.","questions":["The Qin general <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is credited with the two worst slaughters in the record, 240,000 allied troops at Yique and 400,000 men of Zhao who had already surrendered.","Sima Qian gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a chapter of his own, and the detailed account of the Changping campaign is in that biography rather than in the annals of any state."],"answer":"Bai Qi","answerText":"Bai Qi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">258 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ennobled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">280 BCE, prince of Wu'an</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Biography</span><span class=\"dt-v\">chapter 73 of the histories</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Bai Qi</b> was the soldier who did most to make Qin's conquest possible, and the record of him is a list of killings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A biographical dictionary compiled from the histories credits him with a campaign against Wei and Han in which he crushed the allies at Yique, where 240,000 of them are said to have been killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It dates that battle to 298 BCE, where the account in the hereditary house of Han puts it in 293, which is the kind of gap the chronology of these two centuries is full of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 280 BCE he defeated the armies of Zhao and was ennobled prince of Wu'an for it, and in 260 he beat them again and put more than 400,000 men to death after accepting their surrender.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then refused a fresh campaign against Zhao, fell out of favour with King Zhaoxiang, and was driven to take his own life in 258.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The same dictionary says he was raised to the command by Wei Yang, which cannot be right, since that reformer had been executed in 338 BCE, forty years before Bai Qi's earliest recorded campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is not in doubt is where his story sits in the sources, because the annals of Zhao break off at the surrender and send the reader to his own biography for the detail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is a mark of standing rather than of notoriety: a general with a chapter to himself is a general the historian treated as a subject rather than as an instrument of somebody else's reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The machinery behind him was the rank ladder, which paid a man one degree and an office for every head taken, so that an army had a direct interest in counting the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether the figures attached to him are counts or claims cannot be settled from the surviving record, and they were reported by the state that did the killing.","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Po Ch’i,” 629–30, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Han, 219 and 221, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 29–30 and 30 n. 4, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 119 n. 4, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 61, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4},
{"id":"cnh-183","num":183,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Qi paid 76 scholars to argue rather than to govern, and the gathering that grew out of it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is named after a gate of the capital.","questions":["The gathering known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> kept nearly a thousand men at Qi's expense to debate the affairs of state without ever taking part in them.","Sima Qian's list of the men King Xuan of Qi housed and ranked, the roll of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, covers the yin-yang, Daoist and Legalist traditions alike."],"answer":"Jixia Academy","answerText":"Jixia Academy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under King Xuan of Qi, 342–324 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Place</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Linzi, capital of Qi</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Jixia Academy</b> is the name given to the scholars Qi kept at its capital, and it is the nearest thing the Warring States had to a paid faculty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> King Xuan of Qi liked learned men who travelled from state to state giving counsel, and the histories name 76 of them, among whom were Zou Yan, Chunyu Kun, Tian Pian, Jie Yu, Shen Dao and Huan Yuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each received a mansion of his own from the king and was made a grand officer of the first rank, and the arrangement is stated exactly: they took no part in the government, but they discussed affairs of state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On those terms the scholars at the foot of the Ji gate became abundant again, counted by hundreds and coming near a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they produced was not one school but several, since the imperial library later classed Zou Yan's 49 chapters with the yin and yang, Tian Pian's 25 with the Way, and Shen Dao's 42 with the laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Even the place is uncertain: Ji was said to be a gate of the Qi capital at whose foot the debaters met, while another authority put it at a Mount Ji with an inn for travelling sophists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The men were valued for their tongues, and the nicknames say so, Zou Yan being called Yan divine in discussion and Tian Pian Pian of the divine mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chunyu Kun, another of the six, is remembered as a wit who talked his prince into a larger present for an ally with a story about a farmer who sacrificed a pig's foot and prayed for full barns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mencius is missing from the list although he talked with the same king repeatedly, which is explained by the speed with which their relations soured into a rupture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What Qi had built was a standing body paid to think about government without governing, and the histories say the scholars became abundant again, so it predates the king credited with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de T’ien King-tchong Wan, 258–60, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de T’ien King-tchong Wan, 258 n. 8, 259 nn. 2, 4–5, and 260 n. 1, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Tsou Yen,” 773, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","James Legge, trans., <i>The Chinese Classics</i>, vol. 2, <i>The Works of Mencius</i>, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1895), prolegomena, 25–29, https://archive.org/details/chineseclassics02legg. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ch’un-yü K’un,” 205–6, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Warring_States_or_Han_Bamboo_Slips_of_Daoist_Text_%289974266303%29.jpg/1920px-Warring_States_or_Han_Bamboo_Slips_of_Daoist_Text_%289974266303%29.jpg","title":"A school's work, on slips","desc":"Bamboo slips carrying a Daoist text of the Warring States or Han period","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_or_Han_Bamboo_Slips_of_Daoist_Text_%289974266303%29.jpg","alt":"Bamboo slips carrying a Daoist text of the Warring States or Han period"}},
{"id":"cnh-184","num":184,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Qi, Wei, Zhao and Chu each had a great noble who kept thousands of retainers, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name later given to the four of them together.","questions":["Stories attached to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> include an escape from Qin managed by one retainer who could imitate a cockcrow and another who was a skilled thief.","When Qin besieged Handan in 258 BCE it was relieved by two of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one fetching help from Chu and the other bringing the army of Wei."],"answer":"Four Lords of the Warring States","answerText":"Four Lords of the Warring States","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">279 BCE, Lord Mengchang of Qi</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">250 BCE, Lord Pingyuan of Zhao</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">244 BCE, Lord Xinling of Wei</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">237 BCE, Lord Chunshen of Chu</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Four Lords of the Warring States</b> are four great nobles of Qi, Wei, Zhao and Chu who kept private followings of thousands and used them against Qin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lord Mengchang of Qi, whose real name was Tian Wen, became minister to Qin in 299 BCE and was imprisoned there on a rumour that he was working for his own state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The escape is the best known thing about the whole group: one retainer stole back a fur robe to buy a favourite's help, and another imitated a cockcrow so the frontier gate, which could not open before dawn, opened early.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is said to have kept three thousand retainers shod in pearl-embroidered shoes, though a second handbook attaches the pearl-booted band to the Chu lord instead, which is how these anecdotes travel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lord Pingyuan of Zhao was Zhao Sheng, younger brother of the reigning prince, who put a favourite concubine to death because a humpbacked retainer had taken offence at her laughing at him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Lord Xinling of Wei was Wu Ji, youngest son of a prince of that state, and his record is the most military of the four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He commanded the Wei army in 258 BCE and marched to relieve Handan, the Zhao capital, which Qin was besieging; in 247 he led the armies of five allied states and drove a Qin general back to the Hangu pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Zhao annals confirm that relief from the other side, recording that Lord Pingyuan went to Chu for help, that Chu came, that the prince of Wei followed, and that the siege was then abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Lord Chunshen of Chu was Huang Xie, a diplomat who became chief minister in 263 BCE, moved the Chu capital eastward in 248 and enlarged the river that runs through modern Shanghai.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of them ended well: Xinling withdrew from public life in disgust, and Chunshen was assassinated by the man who wanted the parentage of the Chu heir kept quiet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Mêng Ch’ang-chün,” 580–81, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Wu Chi,” 878–79, and “Huang Hsieh,” 334–35, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 82, 305, and pt. I no. 563, 175–76, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 80, 26, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Maison héréditaire de Tchao, 120, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/Warring_States_Bronze_Belt_Hook_with_Gold_%26_Silver_Inlay_%2810622432856%29.jpg","title":"What a great house wore","desc":"A Warring States bronze belt hook inlaid with gold and silver","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Warring_States_Bronze_Belt_Hook_with_Gold_%26_Silver_Inlay_%2810622432856%29.jpg","alt":"A Warring States bronze belt hook inlaid with gold and silver"}},
{"id":"cnh-185","num":185,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Liu Xiang gathered the speeches of the travelling persuaders into 33 chapters, and the book he made, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, is where most of the period's talk survives.","questions":["Because the local chronicles of the Warring States were burnt, the historian leaned on a collection of speeches called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for two whole centuries of his narrative.","A twelfth-century editor cut the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 33 chapters into ten, and later editions kept his text while restoring the older table of contents."],"answer":"Zhanguo Ce","answerText":"Zhanguo Ce","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Compiled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Liu Xiang, 76–5 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chapters</span><span class=\"dt-v\">33, later divided into 10</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Oldest text</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the edition of 1146</span></div>","traditional":"戰國策","hanzi":"战国策","pinyin":"Zhànguó Cè","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Zhànguó Cè</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Zhanguo Ce</b>, the Intrigues or Stratagems of the Warring States, collects the speeches with which travelling politicians talked the courts of the period into one alliance or another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not a chronicle, and the distinction runs through all early Chinese history writing, which divides into collections of discourses and local chronicles, the discourses being the older kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is also arranged by state rather than by year, so that one man's career can be split between the chapters on Wei and the chapters on Qin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Liu Xiang was the first to gather the pieces and gave them 33 chapters, and even his dates are unsettled, one handbook giving 80 to 9 BCE where the histories give 76 to 5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He and his son were doing that work as part of a wider effort to arrange the literary remains recovered from the proscription of books ordered by the First Emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That proscription is why the book matters so much, since it fell hardest on exactly these two centuries and left the period thinner in records than the one before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sima Qian used it heavily, framing its speeches in a dry list of towns taken, battles fought and princes enthroned or buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The editing history is a short history of Chinese scholarship in itself: a commentary under the Later Han explained obscure passages, an 11th-century scholar revised the text, and an edition of 1147 cut the 33 chapters down to ten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Another revision was finished in 1325 and published only in 1355 after its author's death, and an edition of 1581 kept the ten chapters while restoring the 33 in its table of contents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What survives is therefore not one book but a family of them, and a passage quoted from it by an anthology of 1743 could not be found at all in the copy a modern translator was working from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 5 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1905), Note préliminaire, 2–5, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto04chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, CLI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Liu Hsiang,” 501–2, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I nos. 404–5, 130–31, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 31 n. 3, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Harvard_drs_25076017_%E6%88%B0%E5%9C%8B%E7%AD%96_v.1.pdf/page6-1920px-Harvard_drs_25076017_%E6%88%B0%E5%9C%8B%E7%AD%96_v.1.pdf.jpg","title":"An edition of the book itself","desc":"A woodblock edition of the Zhanguo Ce opened at its preface, in the Harvard-Yenching Library","credit":"Harvard-Yenching Library, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_25076017_%E6%88%B0%E5%9C%8B%E7%AD%96_v.1.pdf","alt":"A woodblock edition of the Zhanguo Ce opened at its preface, in the Harvard-Yenching Library"}},
{"id":"cnh-186","num":186,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Warned against war with Qin, the king of Chu ignored <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, walked into an ambush and was captured, and the minister who had advised him drowned himself.","questions":["The office <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held was director of the affairs of three clans, Zhao, Qu and Jing, one of which was his own family.","Sima Qian gave <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a chapter and used it to say that rulers, clever or stupid, all want a loyal servant and none of them can pick one."],"answer":"Qu Yuan","answerText":"Qu Yuan","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">332–295 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In office</span><span class=\"dt-v\">under King Huai of Chu, from about 314 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also called</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qu Ping</span></div>","traditional":"屈原","hanzi":"屈原","pinyin":"Qū Yuán","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qū Yuán</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Qu Yuan</b>, also called Qu Ping, is remembered as a poet and was in his own lifetime a minister, and the political story is the one his fame rests on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A native of Ying, he held the office of san lü, director of the affairs of the three great clans of Chu, the Zhao, the Qu and the Jing, one of which was his own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He stood high with King Huai of Chu until rivals worked against him and he was impeached, which is the point at which the tradition has him begin to write.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His central advice was against making war on Qin, and it was rejected: the king went ahead, fell into an ambush and was taken prisoner by his opponents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under Huai's son, who came to the throne as King Xiang, he sank further out of favour still, and it is from that second disgrace that the story of his death is told.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Sima Qian gave him a chapter of his own, and one of the few places in the histories where the writer speaks in his own voice is his comment on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A ruler, he says, whether stupid or clever, worthy or unworthy, cannot help wanting a loyal man to employ and a wise man for his assistant, and yet ruined kingdoms and destroyed houses follow one another without a break.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The reason is that those a prince calls loyal are not loyal and those he thinks wise are not wise, which is the lesson the historian drew from a minister who was right and was not listened to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same idea returns in his own account of why men write, where being exiled is what made Qu Yuan compose, alongside a king imprisoned, a chronicler blinded and a general mutilated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> His nephew Song Yu, writing about 300 BCE, held office as a minister too and is counted among the authors of the elegiac class that grew out of his uncle's work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ch’ü Yüan,” 200–201, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 326, 107, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Introduction, LVI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Appendice I, CCXXXVI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 642, 196–97, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Qu_Yuan.jpg/1920px-Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Qu_Yuan.jpg","title":"The minister as later ages drew him","desc":"An album portrait of Qu Yuan by an unknown Chinese artist, in the Philadelphia Museum of Art","credit":"Unknown Chinese artist, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portraits_of_Famous_Men_-_Qu_Yuan.jpg","alt":"An album portrait of Qu Yuan by an unknown Chinese artist, in the Philadelphia Museum of Art"}},
{"id":"cnh-187","num":187,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Translators cannot agree whether the title of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> means falling into trouble or grief dispelled, which are opposite readings of the same two characters.","questions":["Sima Qian's list of works written out of suffering puts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beside a chronicle made by a blind man and a treatise on war by a general who had lost his feet.","A whole class of elegiac Chinese poetry takes its name from the second character of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is the founding piece of the southern tradition."],"answer":"Li Sao","answerText":"Li Sao","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Written</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after 314 BCE, in exile</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Author</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Qu Yuan, 332–295 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"離騷","hanzi":"离骚","pinyin":"Lí Sāo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Lí Sāo</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Li Sao</b> is the long poem Qu Yuan is said to have written after his impeachment, and the oldest single piece of Chinese literature that anyone has ever tried to read as autobiography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its two characters have been translated in opposite directions, one handbook giving Falling into Trouble and another grief dispelled, so the title alone can be read as a complaint or as a cure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the poem does is allegorical rather than narrative: it describes a search for a prince who will listen to good counsel in government, which is what its author had failed to find.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The other reading of it is didactic, that the writer sought to instruct his sovereign by clothing the lessons of antiquity in lyrical form, and the two accounts are not easy to reconcile.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its landscape is peopled by figures who exist nowhere else, among them Ling Fen, a soothsayer of antiquity known only from these lines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The poem's standing rests on a claim about where writing comes from, and it is the historian who made it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His list runs that a king developed the Changes in prison, that a sage wrote his chronicle in difficulty, that Qu Yuan composed this poem once exiled, that a blind man made the <i>Discourses of the States</i>, and that a general expounded the rules of war after his feet were cut off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All these men, he wrote, had some grief at heart, and being unable to follow the road they had marked out for themselves, they discoursed on past things to set out their thought for posterity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He added that the occasion of the three hundred poems of the <i>Book of Odes</i> was most often indignation breathed out by wise men, which makes this poem the model rather than the exception.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole southern class of elegiac verse took its name from the second half of this title, and later poets of Chu were reckoned as writing in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Appendice I, CCXXXVI, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ch’ü Yüan,” 200, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 326, 107, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ling Fên,” 487, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 642, 196–97, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Illustrated_Poem_of_Li_Sao_WDL4459.jpg/1920px-Illustrated_Poem_of_Li_Sao_WDL4459.jpg","title":"The poem, illustrated","desc":"An illustrated manuscript of the Li Sao, in the World Digital Library","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illustrated_Poem_of_Li_Sao_WDL4459.jpg","alt":"An illustrated manuscript of the Li Sao, in the World Digital Library"}},
{"id":"cnh-188","num":188,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Han fell first and Qi last, and the nine years between them are what the annals call the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which one state held everything.","questions":["Wang Jian, Wang Ben and Meng Wu are the three commanders who between them carried out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taking one king prisoner after another.","A great banquet was held throughout the realm in 222 BCE to celebrate five states destroyed, which left the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> one kingdom short of complete."],"answer":"Qin conquest of the six states","answerText":"Qin conquest of the six states","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">230–221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Order</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Han, Zhao, Wei, Chu, Yan, Qi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE, with Qi</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Qin conquest of the six states</b> took nine years and is recorded in the Qin annals as a plain sequence of campaigns, one state to a paragraph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 230 BCE an officer named Teng attacked Han, seized its King An and took the whole of his territory, which was turned into a commandery called Yingchuan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 228 BCE Wang Jian and Qiang Hui took all of Zhao and captured its king at Dongyang, and the king of Qin entered Handan, where he had been born, and had everyone who had been hostile to his mother put to death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A Zhao prince gathered those who still wanted to fight and withdrew north to Dai, where he proclaimed himself king, so the state was destroyed twice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 225 BCE Wang Ben attacked Wei and turned the Yellow River onto its capital until the rampart of Daliang collapsed and the king asked to submit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Yan was attacked in 226 BCE, and the annals note that its heir had been the instigator of an attempt on the king of Qin's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That heir's own father had already put him to death in a vain effort to conciliate the enemy, and Chu took two campaigns after him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wang Jian was called out again in 224 BCE, and in 223 he and Meng Wu broke the Chu army, after which its commander killed himself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 222 BCE a great levy was raised, Wang Ben took the king of Yan in Liaodong and captured the king of Dai on his way back, and Wang Jian pacified Chu south of the river; a commentator explains the great banquet ordered that year as celebrating five states destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Qi came last, its king breaking off relations and holding his western frontier until Wang Ben marched through the south of Yan and took him prisoner in 221 BCE, at which the annals say Qin for the first time possessed the whole empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 117–20, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 120–23, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ching K’o,” 156–57, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 665, 204, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Map_of_Qin_Conquest_of_the_Six_Warring_States_%2810185929363%29.jpg/1920px-Map_of_Qin_Conquest_of_the_Six_Warring_States_%2810185929363%29.jpg","title":"Nine years, six kingdoms","desc":"A museum map of the Qin conquest of the six warring states","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Qin_Conquest_of_the_Six_Warring_States_%2810185929363%29.jpg","alt":"A museum map of the Qin conquest of the six warring states"}},
{"id":"cnh-189","num":189,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Carrying a map of his own country with a blade rolled inside it, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> got close enough to the king of Qin to strike at him and missed.","questions":["Escorted to the Yi river by mourners, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sang a farewell so bleak that the prince who had sent him wept and the party's hair stood on end.","Qin turned on Yan the year after <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> failed, and the prince who had commissioned the attempt was put to death by his own father."],"answer":"Jing Ke","answerText":"Jing Ke","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">227 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Sent by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Dan, heir apparent of Yan, died 226 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Account</span><span class=\"dt-v\">chapter 86 of the histories</span></div>","traditional":"荊軻","hanzi":"荆轲","pinyin":"Jīng Kē","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Jīng Kē</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Jing Ke</b> is the assassin sent by Yan against the king who would become the First Emperor, and the attempt is the most famous failure in Chinese history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qin had demanded that Yan hand over a deserter and surrender a slice of territory as a fine, and Dan, the heir apparent, looked for a way to strike back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He found a brawling but courageous adventurer and commissioned him as an envoy carrying Yan's submission, along with maps of its territory and, in one account, the head of a commander the king of Qin particularly wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The blade was hidden in the rolled map, which is the detail the story is remembered by, and the prince with a few friends escorted the envoy as far as the Yi river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There he took leave of a companion in a farewell so mournful that the party wept and their hair bristled inside their caps, and the lines are usually rendered as a shrill wind, a chill stream and a champion who is not coming back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> At the Qin capital he was admitted, unfolded his pretended mission and struck, and the two accounts of what happened next do not agree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One has him miss his aim outright and be despatched after a struggle; the other has him wound the king, who fled down a narrow passage and would have been killed had a eunuch not called to him to turn and draw.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The consequences were immediate: Qin turned its armies on Yan, and Dan's father put his own son to death in a vain attempt to conciliate the invader.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Qin annals record the attack on Yan in 226 BCE and note in passing that Dan had been the instigator of the attempt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Yan lasted four more years, its last king being taken in Liaodong in 222 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ching K’o,” 156–57, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 271, 86, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 665, 204, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 121 n. 1, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 122, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3},
{"id":"cnh-190","num":190,"category":"Eastern Zhou","question":"Depending on which of two branches you count, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> falls either in 256 BCE or seven years later, when the second one was annexed too.","questions":["The royal domain had split into two principalities that both called themselves Zhou, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has two dates rather than one.","What Qin carried off at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was a set of nine bronze vessels that a tradition traced to Yu and treated as the dynasty's warrant."],"answer":"end of the Zhou","answerText":"end of the Zhou","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Submission</span><span class=\"dt-v\">256 BCE, the Western Zhou</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Annexation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">249 BCE, both branches</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Dynasty</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from about 1046 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>end of the Zhou</b> has two dates because the royal domain had split into two principalities, a Western Zhou and an Eastern Zhou, each with a prince of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 256 BCE Qin took the village of Fushu from Han, near Yangcheng, and the Western Zhou took fright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They broke with Qin, made a north-south league with the lords, put themselves at the head of picked troops and marched out by Yique to cut Qin off from Yangcheng, which is the last recorded military act of the dynasty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> King Zhao of Qin was angry and sent a general against them; their prince fled into Qin, prostrated himself, admitted his guilt and offered all his towns, 36 of them holding 30,000 people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Qin accepted and sent him home, and then the prince of Zhou and King Nan both died and the people of Zhou fled east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What Qin took away were the nine tripods and the precious vessels, and it moved the duke of the Western Zhou to a village called Danhu.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tripods were the point of the exercise, since a tradition held that the chiefs of the nine provinces had brought metal to Yu, who cast them with figures on their sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One version has those figures teaching the people to know the gods and evil spirits; another makes them a survey, the provinces themselves with records of population and the division of fiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Seven years later King Zhuangxiang of Qin annexed both branches, and the states his successor went on to conquer were still described as owing allegiance to a house that no longer existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Qin chapter tells that second annexation differently, having the Eastern Zhou prince plot with the lords, the minister Lü Buwei charged with killing him and taking his kingdom, and Qin then granting him land at Yangren so that the Zhou sacrifices should not stop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 1 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1895), Les Tcheou, 316–18, https://archive.org/details/lesmemoireshisto0001edou. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Les Ts’in, 96–97, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. II no. 290, 346–47, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Lü Pu-wei,” 558–59, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Bronze_tripods_%28Ding%29%2C_Warring_States%2C_Hunan_Museum.jpg/1920px-Bronze_tripods_%28Ding%29%2C_Warring_States%2C_Hunan_Museum.jpg","title":"What Qin carried off","desc":"Warring States bronze tripods in the Hunan Museum, vessels of the kind the Zhou royal set belonged to","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_tripods_%28Ding%29%2C_Warring_States%2C_Hunan_Museum.jpg","alt":"Warring States bronze tripods in the Hunan Museum, vessels of the kind the Zhou royal set belonged to"}},
{"id":"cnh-191","num":191,"category":"Qin","question":"A founder who abolished posthumous names and numbered his heirs to ten thousand generations left the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with two rulers and fifteen years.","questions":["Almost everything later dynasties took for granted about governing China was set up under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why so short a reign matters so much.","Chinese writers have long enjoyed the irony that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended within a generation of a conquest its ruler thought had settled the future for ever."],"answer":"Qin dynasty","answerText":"Qin dynasty","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ruled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221–206 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emperors</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Capital</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Xianyang, in modern Shaanxi</span></div>","traditional":"秦朝","hanzi":"秦朝","pinyin":"Qín Cháo","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qín Cháo</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Qin dynasty</b> is the shortest of the great Chinese dynasties and the one that decided the shape of the rest, running from the conquest of the last rival kingdom in 221 BCE to its collapse fifteen years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its founder set out his case in an edict listing what each of the six defeated kings had done to deserve it, and ended by asking his ministers for a new title, the one he held being no longer in proportion to his achievement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He abolished the practice of giving a ruler a posthumous name, on the ground that it let sons criticise fathers and subjects their princes, and declared himself the first, his successors to be counted the second generation, the third, and so on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chinese writers have taken pleasure ever since in the irony that a house meant to last ten thousand generations produced exactly two sovereigns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reign lasted long enough to abolish the old order of hereditary lords, divide the empire into commanderies, unify the laws, the weights, the measures, the axle widths and the script, and drive roads and walls across the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What that programme cost is not disputed by any source, including the dynasty's own inscriptions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its ruling doctrine held that the house governed by the virtue of water, whose colour is black, so that severity, deciding every case by law, and using neither kindness nor generosity were held to agree with the numbers of the elements.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The application of the laws was accordingly pressed, and for a long time no pardon was granted at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The regime's most destructive single act was proposed by its chief minister in 213 BCE, when existing literature outside agriculture, medicine and divination was ordered destroyed on pain of branding and four years' forced labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first emperor died in 210 BCE, his chief minister was sawn in two in the market place two years later, and the dynasty went down in rebellion soon afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 123–24 and 129–35, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 128 and n. 4, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652–53, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 464–65, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. III, 369–70, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Qin_Empire_heartland_and_expansion.png/1920px-Qin_Empire_heartland_and_expansion.png","title":"The Qin heartland and what it took","desc":"A map of the Qin heartland in the north-west and the territory added by its expansion","credit":"Map by Ktrinko and पाटलिपुत्र, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_Empire_heartland_and_expansion.png","alt":"A map of the Qin heartland in the north-west and the territory added by its expansion"}},
{"id":"cnh-192","num":192,"category":"Qin","question":"Born at Handan and enthroned at thirteen, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> spent most of his life making war and his last eleven years ruling everything he had taken.","questions":["One story has <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fathered by the merchant-minister who bought his father a throne, which would make the founder of the empire no relation of the house he founded.","Some accounts say <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was buried under a bronze floor mapped with rivers of quicksilver, behind crossbows set to shoot anyone who came in."],"answer":"Qin Shi Huang","answerText":"Qin Shi Huang","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">259–210 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">King of Qin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 247 BCE, aged 13</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Emperor</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 221 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"秦始皇","hanzi":"秦始皇","pinyin":"Qín Shǐ Huáng","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Qín Shǐ Huáng</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Qin Shi Huang</b>, whose personal name was Zheng, was the king of Qin who conquered the other six Chinese kingdoms and then invented the office of emperor to hold them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born at Handan, the capital of Zhao, where his father was living as a hostage in poor circumstances, and came to the Qin throne in 247 BCE at the age of thirteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A persistent story makes him the son not of that father but of the merchant Lü Buwei, who had given the young hostage a wife he is said to have known was already pregnant, and who afterwards ran the state as chief minister.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His early years were spent fighting the states that still nominally owed allegiance to the house of Zhou, and by 221 BCE he was master of the country from the plains of the north to the Yangtze and from the Dongting lake to the eastern sea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He then divided the empire into 36 commanderies, fixed his capital at Xianyang, and took the title that made everything, including literature, begin from his own reign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The measures that followed were practical and remembered as tyrannical in about equal proportion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He introduced a copper coinage so good that cowries went out of circulation, and under his reign the writing brush replaced the old stylus and silk began to replace the bamboo tablet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He also carried his armies far north against the Xiongnu and built the long wall that was meant to keep them out for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He died in 210 BCE at Shaqiu, away from his capital, and was buried in a chamber cut into a mountain which is described as having a bronze floor mapped with the empire in rivers of quicksilver, a roof set with constellations, and machinery to shoot arrows at intruders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The workmen who knew the secret are said to have been shut inside when the outer gate came down, and the entrance was banked with earth and planted over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652–54, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Lü Pu-wei,” 558–59, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 128 and 132–33, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. III, 369–70, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Terrien de Lacouperie, <i>Catalogue of Chinese Coins from the VIIth Cent. B.C. to A.D. 621, Including the Series in the British Museum</i>, ed. Reginald Stuart Poole (London: printed by order of the Trustees, 1892), xiv, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.110902. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/QinShiHuang18thCentury.jpg/1920px-QinShiHuang18thCentury.jpg","title":"The first emperor, as later ages drew him","desc":"A portrait of Qin Shi Huang from an 18th-century album of emperors, the Lidai diwang xiang","credit":"Unknown artist, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QinShiHuang18thCentury.jpg","alt":"A portrait of Qin Shi Huang from an 18th-century album of emperors, the Lidai diwang xiang"}},
{"id":"cnh-193","num":193,"category":"Qin","question":"In the edict announcing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each of the six defeated kings is charged with breaking an agreement he had already made.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is dated from the capture of the last of the six kings, after which the annals say the country was for the first time held whole by one state.","Rather than celebrate the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a triumph, the victor asked his council for a title, saying the one he held no longer matched what he had done."],"answer":"unification of 221 BCE","answerText":"unification of 221 BCE","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Completed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE, with the capture of Qi</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reign year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 26th of the king of Qin</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>unification of 221 BCE</b> is the moment the annals mark by saying Qin for the first time possessed the whole empire, and what they record next is a memorandum rather than a triumph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The king issued an edict going through the six kingdoms one at a time, and the charge against each is the same: it came to terms and then broke the agreement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Han offered its seal and then joined a north-south league; Zhao made a convention, got its hostage prince back and then raised Taiyuan against him; Wei promised to submit and then plotted a surprise attack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chu offered land west of Qingyang and then attacked a Qin commandery; Yan's heir sent an assassin; Qi broke off relations on a counsellor's advice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It closes by saying that with his own feeble person he raised soldiers who punished the cruel and the rebellious, and that the six kings had suffered the penalty of their crimes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its real point comes after: if his title is not changed, nothing will be in proportion to what he has done or carry it to posterity, so the council is told to find an imperial one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reply came from the counsellor Wang Wan, the censor Feng Jie and the judicial officer Li Si, who would end as chief minister of the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In antiquity, they argued, the five emperors held a square of a thousand li, the lords beyond it paid homage or did not, and the Son of Heaven could impose no rule on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Now all within the seas was organised in commanderies and prefectures and the laws issued from one head, which had never happened before and which the five emperors never achieved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What he held ran from the northern plains to the Yangtze and from the Dongting lake east to the sea,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the stones he later set up there date his rule from this 26th year and credit him with equalising the laws and the measures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 123–26, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 145, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 464, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. III, 369–70, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Qin_dynasty_territory.svg/1920px-Qin_dynasty_territory.svg.png","title":"One country, for the first time","desc":"A map of the Qin empire after the unification of 221 BCE, with its commanderies marked","credit":"Map by Ian Remsen, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_dynasty_territory.svg","alt":"A map of the Qin empire after the unification of 221 BCE, with its commanderies marked"}},
{"id":"cnh-194","num":194,"category":"Qin","question":"Offered a title meaning august sovereign, the king of Qin kept half of it, added a word out of deep antiquity and made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which lasted 2,100 years.","questions":["The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a compound of two older words for a ruler, and taking it was a claim to combine the three sovereigns and the five emperors in one person.","Along with <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came a private vocabulary: two words for a ruler's orders and a pronoun that from then on only he could use of himself."],"answer":"huangdi","answerText":"huangdi","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE – 1912 CE</span></div>","traditional":"皇帝","hanzi":"皇帝","pinyin":"huángdì","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">huángdì</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Huangdi</b> is the Chinese word for emperor, and it was made up in 221 BCE for a man who thought the word king no longer covered what he had done.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His ministers reported that they had consulted the learned and found that in antiquity there had been a heavenly sovereign, an earthly sovereign and an august sovereign, of whom the august was the highest, and they proposed he take the last of the three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He rejected august and kept sovereign, adding to it the word for the imperial dignity of the high antiquity, so that the title is a compound of the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The claim behind the compound is that he united in himself the qualities of the three sovereigns and the five emperors alike, the second element being the word borne by legendary rulers such as the Yellow Emperor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The passage is the earliest evidence that the theory of three sovereigns preceding the five emperors already existed, and later writers could not agree on who they were, one list beginning with the Yellow Emperor and another ending elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A private vocabulary came with the title, which is what made it a distinct office rather than a bigger crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His mandates were to be zhi and his ordinances zhao, two words previous dynasties had not used in that sense and which stayed in official style for two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Speaking of himself he was to say zhen, until then an ordinary pronoun anyone might use, which Daoist writing had given the extra sense of a hidden or supernatural influence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A eunuch of the next reign explained the choice by saying that a Son of Heaven says zhen precisely because his voice is not heard and his subjects cannot see his face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Posthumous names went with them: naming a dead ruler after his conduct lets sons judge fathers and subjects judge princes, so he numbered his heirs instead, the second generation, the third, and on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 125–27, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 126 n. 4, 127 n. 1, and 128, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. III, 367–68, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652–53, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Huang Ti,” 338, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c9/%E7%90%85%E7%90%8A%E5%8F%B0%E5%88%BB%E7%9F%B3%E6%8B%93%E7%89%87.jpg","title":"The new title, cut in stone","desc":"A Qing-dynasty rubbing of the Langya terrace inscription, one of the stones the first emperor set up after 221 BCE","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%90%85%E7%90%8A%E5%8F%B0%E5%88%BB%E7%9F%B3%E6%8B%93%E7%89%87.jpg","alt":"A Qing-dynasty rubbing of the Langya terrace inscription, one of the stones the first emperor set up after 221 BCE"}},
{"id":"cnh-195","num":195,"category":"Qin","question":"Having argued the empire out of hereditary lordships and into commanderies, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was accused of treason, tortured into confessing, and sawn in two in the market place.","questions":["A man from Chu who entered a merchant's household in 247 BCE, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rose to be chief minister and is credited with inventing the script the empire wrote in.","The proposal to destroy all books except those on farming, medicine and divination was made in 213 BCE by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who was then at the height of his power."],"answer":"Li Si","answerText":"Li Si","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">208 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Chief minister</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 214 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in the state of Chu</span></div>","traditional":"李斯","hanzi":"李斯","pinyin":"Lǐ Sī","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">Lǐ Sī</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Li Si</b> was the minister who designed the Qin empire, and almost every institution the dynasty is remembered for was either his proposal or carried out under him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A native of Chu, he left a petty post at home and in 247 BCE entered the service of Lü Buwei, then at the head of affairs in Qin, where he caught the sovereign's eye and became senior historiographer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Political advice earned him the office of foreign minister and a long run as a trusted counsellor, and in 214 BCE he was made chief minister, after which his children married into the imperial family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is first heard arguing in 221 BCE, when the whole council proposed making imperial sons kings in the distant east and he alone objected, since the Zhou had enfeoffed their kin in numbers and watched them turn on one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The emperor agreed with him, said that establishing kingdoms again would be planting war, and divided the country into commanderies instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> His second famous proposal, in 213 BCE, was that the claims of antiquity should be blotted out and history begin again with the reigning monarch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All existing literature was to be destroyed except works on agriculture, medicine and divination, and anyone refusing to surrender books was to be branded and set to four years' labour on the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He is also credited with inventing the form of writing known as the lesser seal, and with publishing a book on it, which makes him the author of the empire's script as well as its administration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the emperor died in 210 BCE he joined the conspiracy that put a younger son on the throne, and then tried to restrain the new ruler from his cruelties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That roused the eunuch Zhao Gao, who had him tried and killed after a confession wrung out by torture, in 207 BCE by the annals and a year earlier in the handbooks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 464–65, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 131–32, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 465, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 33, 32, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 209–10, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/%E7%AF%86%E4%B9%A6%E7%90%85%E7%90%8A%E5%8F%B0%E5%88%BB%E7%9F%B3%E8%BD%B4.jpg","title":"The minister's own hand, as tradition has it","desc":"A hanging scroll of the Langya terrace inscription in seal script, attributed to Li Si, in the Shanghai Museum","credit":"Attributed to Li Si, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%AF%86%E4%B9%A6%E7%90%85%E7%90%8A%E5%8F%B0%E5%88%BB%E7%9F%B3%E8%BD%B4.jpg","alt":"A hanging scroll of the Langya terrace inscription in seal script, attributed to Li Si, in the Shanghai Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-196","num":196,"category":"Qin","question":"Every minister but one advised putting imperial sons on thrones in the east, and the emperor took the one, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came about.","questions":["The argument for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was historical: the Zhou had enfeoffed their relatives in numbers and then watched them make war on each other for centuries.","By choosing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the emperor said that re-establishing kingdoms would be planting war, since lords and kings were what the country had just been suffering from."],"answer":"abolition of the fengjian order","answerText":"abolition of the fengjian order","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Replaced</span><span class=\"dt-v\">a system of about 800 years' standing</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>abolition of the fengjian order</b> is the decision, taken in 221 BCE, not to give the conquered territories to members of the imperial family as hereditary kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The proposal came from the chief counsellor, who pointed out that Yan, Qi and Chu lay far off and that without kings placed there they could not be kept in order, and asked the emperor to appoint his own sons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The question went to the ministers and all of them thought it advantageous; one judicial officer objected in the middle of the deliberation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> His argument was entirely historical: the Zhou kings Wen and Wu had given fiefs to sons, younger brothers and kinsmen in great numbers, and with time those close relatives drew apart, attacked one another as enemies, and could not be stopped by the Sons of Heaven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything within the seas was now uniformly reduced to commanderies and prefectures, imperial sons and deserving subjects had been amply paid in titles and revenues, and setting up lords again would not be an advantage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The emperor's reply is the clearest statement of the case in the record: the empire had suffered combats that left it no rest because there were lords and kings, and to establish kingdoms again would be to plant war and seek tranquillity at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He ruled that the judicial officer's opinion was the right one and divided the country into commanderies instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its translator judged this debate quite unlike the more or less artificial speeches of the Zhou period, and a faithful echo of words really spoken at court rather than a rhetorician's exercise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The man who won the argument was Li Si, then only a judicial officer, who would be chief minister within seven years and would use much the same reasoning to propose destroying the books.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What was abolished had stood some eight centuries, and though the next dynasty gave kingdoms back to its own kin, no later Chinese state rested on hereditary lordship again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 131–32, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 131 n. 2, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 464, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader’s Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. III, 369–70, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Terrien de Lacouperie, <i>Catalogue of Chinese Coins from the VIIth Cent. B.C. to A.D. 621, Including the Series in the British Museum</i>, ed. Reginald Stuart Poole (London: printed by order of the Trustees, 1892), xiv, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.110902. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Model_of_Xianyang_Qin_Palace_Site_Made_in_1970s_%289910399875%29.jpg/1920px-Model_of_Xianyang_Qin_Palace_Site_Made_in_1970s_%289910399875%29.jpg","title":"The court where it was argued","desc":"A 1970s model of the Qin palace site at Xianyang, in the Xianyang Museum","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Model_of_Xianyang_Qin_Palace_Site_Made_in_1970s_%289910399875%29.jpg","alt":"A 1970s model of the Qin palace site at Xianyang, in the Xianyang Museum"}},
{"id":"cnh-197","num":197,"category":"Qin","question":"Qin divided the empire into 36 units in 221 BCE, each with a civil administrator, a military governor and an inspector, which is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in outline.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a governor held office at the emperor's pleasure and could not pass it to a son, which is what distinguished it from what it replaced.","The word for the larger unit of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had meant the smaller of the two under the Zhou, so the reform inverted an old pair of terms."],"answer":"commandery-county system","answerText":"commandery-county system","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Established</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Commanderies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">36 at the outset</span></div>","traditional":"郡縣制","hanzi":"郡县制","pinyin":"jùnxiànzhì","translations":"<div class=\"tr-pinline\"><span class=\"tr-pin\">jùnxiànzhì</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>commandery-county system</b> is the administrative frame Qin put in place of hereditary lordships in 221 BCE, and it outlasted the dynasty that invented it by two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The empire was divided into 36 commanderies, each holding a number of prefectures, and into each the emperor put three officers: an administrator, a military governor and a superintendent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The division of powers is the point, since the man who governed did not command the troops and neither of them supervised the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every one of these officers held his post from the throne and could be moved or dismissed, which is precisely what a fief-holder could not be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The units were named after real places and the list survives, running from Sanchuan and Hedong in the centre to Liaodong in the far north-east and Changsha in the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The capital's own territory made up the 36th, and it was known not by a place name but by the title of the officer who administered it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One consequence of the reform was linguistic: a 2nd-century commentator noted that the word Qin used for the larger unit had under the Zhou meant the smaller of the pair, so the reform turned an old distinction upside down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the Zhou rules, he explained, the royal territory was a square of a thousand li divided into a hundred of the larger units, each containing four of the smaller, which is the reverse of the Qin arrangement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With the new divisions came a new word for the population, who were now called the black-headed people, a name one explanation ties to the black that the dynasty had adopted as its colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The shape was not new to Qin itself, whose towns and villages had been combined into districts under prefects a century earlier,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and the officer who won the argument for it rose to be chief minister of the empire it created.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 132–33 and 132 n. 1, 133 n. 2, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), Annales principales de Ts’in Che-hoang, 131–32, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652–53, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 464, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 18–19, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/The_Way_of_Serving_the_Government_on_Qin_Slips_%2810160516084%29.jpg/1920px-The_Way_of_Serving_the_Government_on_Qin_Slips_%2810160516084%29.jpg","title":"How an official was to behave","desc":"Qin bamboo slips carrying a text on the way of serving the government, in the Hubei Provincial Museum at Wuhan","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Way_of_Serving_the_Government_on_Qin_Slips_%2810160516084%29.jpg","alt":"Qin bamboo slips carrying a text on the way of serving the government, in the Hubei Provincial Museum at Wuhan"}},
{"id":"cnh-198","num":198,"category":"Imperial","question":"Bringing weights, measures and axle widths into one system across the whole empire in 221 BCE is what historians mean by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an act commemorated on cast bronze standards.","questions":["The inscription cast on official measures names two counsellors ordered to make the standards identical, which is the founding document of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and dates it to the 26th year.","Because carts had to run in ruts of one gauge and merchants weigh against one set of standards, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> mattered as much to trade and transport as the new commanderies did to government."],"answer":"Qin standardisation","answerText":"Qin standardisation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decreed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Inscribed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">on measures from 221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Restated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">209 BCE, by the Second Emperor</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Qin standardisation</b> is the set of measures taken after the conquest of 221 BCE to make one system out of the units and vehicles of seven kingdoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The annals record it in one sentence: he unified the laws and the rules, the weights and the measures of length, gave carts axles of identical dimensions, and made writing use uniform characters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The programme reached the currency, forbidding gems, pearls, cowries and tin as money and issuing one round bronze coin with a square hole, so good the older kinds went out of use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The axle clause is the most practical, since traffic on unpaved roads cut ruts a cart of the wrong gauge could not follow, and beside it went symbolic numbers: six became the standard, a cart six feet, six feet a pace, a team six horses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The annals' translator warns against reading that as a base-six arithmetic, since weight and capacity were not reduced to multiples of six and counting went on by tens and hundreds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What survives of it is not a decree but the objects it produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A commemorative text was cut into many measuring instruments and rubbings are still read: in the 26th year the Sovereign Emperor gathered the world into his hand, took his new title, and ordered the counsellors Wei Zhuang and Wang Wan to make uniform the rules, the measures of length and capacity, and the standards whose insufficiency left room for doubt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> His successor added a second inscription in 209 BCE saying the measures were entirely his father's work, and had it cut beside the first so no doubt should arise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The stone raised on Mount Tai puts the claim in verse, dating his rule from the 26th year and crediting him with equalising the laws, the measures and the standards for all things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There was a precedent inside Qin itself, where weights and measures were standardised under Shang Yang a century earlier, but that covered a kingdom and this the empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts’ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 129–30, 134–35 and 145, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Chavannes, <i>Mémoires historiques</i>, vol. 2, appendix 3, 549–51, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 19, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Terrien de Lacouperie, <i>Catalogue of Chinese Coins from the VIIth Cent. B.C. to A.D. 621, Including the Series in the British Museum</i>, ed. Reginald Stuart Poole (London: printed by order of the Trustees, 1892), xiv, https://archive.org/details/india.history.resource.110902. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Shih Huang Ti,” 652–53, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Qin_Bronze_Weight_with_2_Imperial_Edicts_%2814367061301%29.jpg/1920px-Qin_Bronze_Weight_with_2_Imperial_Edicts_%2814367061301%29.jpg","title":"A standard carrying both edicts","desc":"A Qin bronze weight inscribed with two imperial edicts on measures, in the Qin Shihuang Museum at Lintong","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_Bronze_Weight_with_2_Imperial_Edicts_%2814367061301%29.jpg","alt":"A Qin bronze weight inscribed with two imperial edicts on measures, in the Qin Shihuang Museum at Lintong"}},
{"id":"cnh-199","num":199,"category":"Imperial","question":"Ordering that writing should everywhere use uniform characters, the Qin carried out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why one written language served kingdoms whose speech differed.","questions":["Two 19th-century handbooks disagree about who carried out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, one crediting the chief minister and the other an official said to have worked it out in prison.","The style promoted by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is called the lesser seal, and it was itself soon simplified into the clerkly hand from which later Chinese writing descends."],"answer":"standardisation of the Chinese script","answerText":"standardisation of the Chinese script","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Ordered</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Style</span><span class=\"dt-v\">lesser seal, then the clerkly hand</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>standardisation of the Chinese script</b> is the last clause of the sentence in which the annals record the Qin reforms of 221 BCE, and it has outlasted every other one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Having unified the laws, the weights and the axle widths, the emperor ordered that in writing uniform characters should be used, which meant suppressing the forms seven kingdoms had developed while writing the same language apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The translator notes that the two words used there are not a doublet: one names the primitive pictographs and the other the characters derived from them, a distinction kept in the full title of the dictionary usually called the Shuowen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The style that resulted is known as the lesser seal, described as a simplified form of an older and more cumbrous one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was itself simplified again into the li script, from which the modern clerkly hand descends, so the seal characters survive today mainly on stamps and on the stones of the period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Who actually did the work is not agreed, and the disagreement is old. Giles credits the official Cheng Miao with inventing the lesser seal under the First Emperor,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while saying under his entry for the chief minister that Li Si is said to have invented it and published a work on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Mayers gives the lesser seal to Li Si outright, and makes Cheng Miao the inventor only of the official hand, which he is said to have worked out during ten years in prison and which won him a pardon and high office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same handbook dates Cheng Miao to the 2nd century BCE, which would put him a century after the emperor it says he served, so the tradition is confused about the man as well as the invention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What is not in doubt is the effect: the empire fell in 206 BCE and its script did not, and a single written standard could afterwards be read across kingdoms whose speech had diverged.","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 135 and 135 n. 1, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Ch‘éng Miao,” 116, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","Giles, <i>Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i>, s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 464–65, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I no. 110, 34, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]","Mayers, <i>Chinese Reader's Manual</i>, no. 364, 123, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/%E5%B3%84%E5%B1%B1%E5%88%BB%E7%9F%B3-%E5%8C%97%E5%AE%8B%E6%B7%B3%E5%8C%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B9%B4%EF%BC%88993%EF%BC%89-%E6%9D%8E%E6%96%AF%E4%B9%A6-%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%89%E7%A2%91%E6%9E%97%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%94%E5%AE%A4_2023-09-29_01.jpg/1920px-%E5%B3%84%E5%B1%B1%E5%88%BB%E7%9F%B3-%E5%8C%97%E5%AE%8B%E6%B7%B3%E5%8C%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B9%B4%EF%BC%88993%EF%BC%89-%E6%9D%8E%E6%96%AF%E4%B9%A6-%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%89%E7%A2%91%E6%9E%97%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%94%E5%AE%A4_2023-09-29_01.jpg","title":"The lesser seal, recut","desc":"A 993 recutting of the Yishan stele, whose text is ascribed to Li Si, in the Forest of Steles at Xi'an","credit":"Photograph by Kcx36, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B3%84%E5%B1%B1%E5%88%BB%E7%9F%B3-%E5%8C%97%E5%AE%8B%E6%B7%B3%E5%8C%96%E5%9B%9B%E5%B9%B4%EF%BC%88993%EF%BC%89-%E6%9D%8E%E6%96%AF%E4%B9%A6-%E8%A5%BF%E5%AE%89%E7%A2%91%E6%9E%97%E7%AC%AC%E4%BA%94%E5%AE%A4_2023-09-29_01.jpg","alt":"A 993 recutting of the Yishan stele, whose text is ascribed to Li Si, in the Forest of Steles at Xi'an"}},
{"id":"cnh-200","num":200,"category":"Imperial","question":"Deciding every case by statute, using neither kindness nor generosity and granting no pardons for years together, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the doctrine the dynasty is chiefly remembered by.","questions":["The dynasty's own stone inscriptions boast that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had removed doubt and fixed the rules so that everyone knew what to avoid, which is the case a code makes for itself.","A Han writer said the founder made punishments pitiless and tyranny the foundation of empire, which is the standard verdict on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and the reason the dynasty lasted fifteen years."],"answer":"Qin law","answerText":"Qin law","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Empire-wide</span><span class=\"dt-v\">221 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Roots in Qin</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from about 356 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Judged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by Han writers after 206 BCE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Qin law</b> is the legal order the first empire was governed by, and the thing the dynasty has been judged on ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The annals tie it to doctrine rather than convenience: because the house ruled by the virtue of water, extreme severity, deciding every affair by law, and using neither kindness nor generosity were held to agree with the numbers of the five virtues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The application of the laws was accordingly pressed, and for a long time no pardon was granted at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its roots were older than the empire, in the Qin of a century before, where households were grouped in fives and tens answerable for one another and not denouncing a criminal meant being cut in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The regime made its own case on stone: one inscription says he removed doubts and fixed the laws so that all know what to avoid, another that wise laws were for the first time put in force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What the penalties were is recorded in a commentary on the sentence passed on the empire's own chief minister, whose end the handbooks call a public death of great barbarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A man condemned to die with his relatives to the third degree was branded, had his nose cut off, then both feet, was beaten to death, and had his head exposed and his bones and flesh displayed in the market place: this was called suffering all five punishments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The verdict of the next dynasty, delivered by writers the historian copied out in full, is unsparing: he forbade writings and books, made punishments and laws pitiless, put deceit and violence first and kindness and justice last, and made tyranny the foundation of empire.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same essay supplies the maxim it turns on, that the methods for taking a thing and for keeping it are not the same.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Under the second emperor the mutilated and the punished are said to have seen one another along the roads, and the empire was soon in rebellion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","sources":["Édouard Chavannes, trans., <i>Les Mémoires historiques de Se-ma Ts'ien</i>, vol. 2 (Paris: Ernest Leroux, 1897), 129–30, 146 and 160, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","J. J. L. Duyvendak, trans., <i>The Book of Lord Shang: A Classic of the Chinese School of Law</i> (London: Arthur Probsthain, 1928), 14 and 60–61, https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.283023. [Open access]","Chavannes, <i>Mémoires historiques</i>, vol. 2, 209 n. 2, 227 and 234, https://archive.org/details/lesmmoireshisto01chgoog. [Open access]","Herbert A. Giles, <i>A Chinese Biographical Dictionary</i> (London: Bernard Quaritch; Shanghai: Kelly &amp; Walsh, 1898), s.v. “Li Ssŭ,” 464–65, https://archive.org/details/chinesebiograph00gile. [Open access]","William Frederick Mayers, <i>The Chinese Reader's Manual</i> (Shanghai: American Presbyterian Mission Press, 1874), pt. I, 122–23, https://archive.org/details/chinesereadersm00maye. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Qin_Bamboo_Slips_Dealing_with_Legal_Questions_%2810160525384%29.jpg/1920px-Qin_Bamboo_Slips_Dealing_with_Legal_Questions_%2810160525384%29.jpg","title":"The law in questions and answers","desc":"Qin bamboo slips dealing with legal questions, in the Hubei Provincial Museum at Wuhan","credit":"Photograph by Gary Todd, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qin_Bamboo_Slips_Dealing_with_Legal_Questions_%2810160525384%29.jpg","alt":"Qin bamboo slips dealing with legal questions, in the Hubei Provincial Museum at Wuhan"}},
{"id":"us-002","num":2,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Footprints at White Sands, tools at Cooper's Ferry and faeces in the Paisley Caves all bear on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which no longer begins with Clovis hunters.","questions":["Genomes date the founding split from east Asia to about 36,000 years ago, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took thousands of years longer than the archaeology alone would suggest.","Because the corridor between the Canadian ice sheets opened too late, the Pacific shore is the likelier road for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though no early coastal camp has been found."],"answer":"settlement of the Americas","answerText":"settlement of the Americas","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earliest claim</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 23,000 – 21,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Branches part</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 17,500 – 14,600 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Clovis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["event","archaeology","prehistory","migration","north america","united states"],"abstract":"The <b>settlement of the Americas</b> carried people out of north-eastern Asia into two continents no human being had ever entered, and it opens every history of the United States. Their ancestors parted from their east Asian relatives about 36,000 years ago, and an isolated founding branch, the Ancient Beringians, broke away between about 22,000 and 18,100 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genome of an infant buried at Upward Sun River in interior Alaska belongs to that branch, while the northern and southern lines most Native Americans descend from parted about 17,500 to 14,600 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For most of the 20th century the Clovis hunters were held to be the first people here, and careful redating now compresses that whole tradition into the years between 13,050 and 12,750 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Sites in the present-day United States broke that model, among them stone tools at Cooper's Ferry in Idaho by about 15,785 years ago and human faeces in the Paisley Caves in Oregon older than any Clovis point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Fossil footprints in the gypsum at White Sands in New Mexico have twice been dated to between roughly 23,000 and 21,000 years ago, putting people south of the ice at the coldest point of the glaciation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One synthesis gathers ten such sites, from Meadowcroft in Pennsylvania to Page-Ladson in Florida, into an American Upper Paleolithic of blades and bifaces resembling the late Upper Palaeolithic of north-east Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Others are unpersuaded, and a study measuring how cleanly the layers of early sites separate found tidy stratigraphy in Alaska and mixing south of the ice, which it read as a late arrival.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The road south is unsettled too, since the corridor between the Canadian ice sheets opened too late for the earliest claims, while no coastal site older than about 12,600 years has been found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The Asian origin is not in doubt, because the genome of the Clovis-age child buried at Anzick in Montana is ancestral to a great many Native Americans alive today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben A. Potter, Lasse Vinner, et al., “Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan genome reveals first founding population of Native Americans,” <i>Nature</i> 553, no. 7687 (2018): 203–207, https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/7887/. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr. and David L. Carlson, “The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Loren G. Davis, David B. Madsen, David A. Sisson, et al., “Dating of a large tool assemblage at the Cooper's Ferry site (Idaho, USA) to ~15,785 cal yr B.P. extends the age of stemmed points in the Americas,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 51 (2022): eade1248, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9788777/. [Open access]","Lisa-Marie Shillito, Helen L. Whelton, John C. Blong, et al., “Pre-Clovis occupation of the Americas identified by human fecal biomarkers in coprolites from Paisley Caves, Oregon,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 29 (2020): eaba6404, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7363456/. [Open access]","Vance T. Holliday, Jason D. Windingstad, Jordon Bright, et al., “Paleolake geochronology supports Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) age for human tracks at White Sands, New Mexico,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 25 (2025): eadv4951, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12175891/. [Open access]","David B. Madsen, Loren G. Davis, Thomas J. Williams, Masami Izuho and Fumie Iizuka, “Characterizing the American Upper Paleolithic,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 43 (2025): eady9545, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12542946/. [Open access]","Todd A. Surovell, Sarah A. Allaun, Barbara A. Crass, et al., “Late date of human arrival to North America: Continental scale differences in stratigraphic integrity of pre-13,000 BP archaeological sites,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0264092, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9020715/. [Open access]","Ben A. Potter, James F. Baichtal, Alwynne B. Beaudoin, et al., “Current evidence allows multiple models for the peopling of the Americas,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 8 (2018): eaat5473, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6082647/. [Open access]","Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., “The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,” <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Human_fossil_tracks_at_White_Sands_New_Mexico.jpg/1920px-Human_fossil_tracks_at_White_Sands_New_Mexico.jpg","title":"Fossil human footprints at White Sands","desc":"Tracks pressed into the muddy margin of an ice age lake in New Mexico, dated by two separate programmes to the height of the last glaciation.","credit":"U.S. Geological Survey, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Human_fossil_tracks_at_White_Sands_New_Mexico.jpg","alt":"Four bare human footprints, toes and heels clearly formed, preserved in pale hardened sediment."}},
{"id":"us-001","num":1,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"The 2020 census counted 9.7 million people who identified as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> alone or with another race, and the federal government recognises 575 tribal governments.","questions":["More than 70 of the languages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were still spoken at home in the United States in 2021, with about half of those speakers using Navajo.","Roughly 227,000 square kilometres (56 million acres) of land is held in trust by the federal government for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and their nations, a remnant of a far larger estate."],"answer":"Native Americans","answerText":"Native Americans","answerDate":"","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["people","history","geography","united states","north america"],"abstract":"<b>Native Americans</b> are the Indigenous peoples of the land that became the United States, and federal statute and the census both call them American Indians and Alaska Natives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2020 census counted 3.7 million people who marked that identity alone and 9.7 million who marked it alone or with another race, the second figure having grown by 87 per cent in a decade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are not one people: the Bureau of Indian Affairs listed 575 tribal entities in January 2026, and each of those is a government rather than a label for an ethnic group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Federal recognition carries the immunities and privileges of a government-to-government relationship with the United States, and with them a body of law that applies nowhere else in the country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bureau holds roughly 227,000 square kilometres (56 million acres) of surface land in trust for those nations and for individual owners.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That estate is the remnant of a much larger one, since the General Allotment Act of 1887 cut reservations into individual parcels, sold what it called 'surplus' and cost communities vast acreage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The history behind the loss is far longer than the loss, and footprints in the gypsum at White Sands in New Mexico have been dated to the height of the last ice age, more than 20,000 years ago, though that age is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> More than 70 Native North American languages were still spoken at home in 2021, by 342,311 people, about six per cent fewer than eight years earlier, and roughly half of those speakers spoke Navajo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Which collective name to use is unsettled, since 'Native American', 'American Indian' and 'Indigenous' are all in current use, and the name of the particular nation is usually the better word. Nations increasingly direct the study of their own past, and the genomic work published on ancestral remains at Picuris Pueblo in 2025 was commissioned by the Pueblo, which holds the data and the decision to publish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Nicholas Jones, Rachel Marks, Roberto Ramirez and Merarys Ríos-Vargas, “2020 Census Illuminates Racial and Ethnic Composition of the Country,” U.S. Census Bureau, August 12, 2021, https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2021/08/improved-race-ethnicity-measures-reveal-united-states-population-much-more-multiracial.html. [Open access]","Bureau of Indian Affairs, “Indian Entities Recognized by and Eligible To Receive Services From the United States Bureau of Indian Affairs,” <i>Federal Register</i> 91, no. 20 (January 30, 2026): 4102–4106, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-01-30/html/2026-01899.htm. [Open access]","Bureau of Indian Affairs, “Office of Trust Services,” U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed September 6, 2026, https://www.bia.gov/bia/ots. [Open access]","National Archives, “Dawes Act (1887),” Milestone Documents, accessed September 6, 2026, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/dawes-act. [Open access]","Vance T. Holliday, Jason D. Windingstad, Jordon Bright, et al., “Paleolake geochronology supports Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) age for human tracks at White Sands, New Mexico,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 25 (2025): eadv4951, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12175891/. [Open access]","Adrienne Griffiths and Daniela Mejía, “How Native North American Language Use Changed in the United States,” U.S. Census Bureau, June 3, 2025, https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2025/06/native-american-language-use.html. [Open access]","Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–132, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Navajo_Nation_Council_Chamber_interior_during_session%2C_April_2024.jpg/1920px-Navajo_Nation_Council_Chamber_interior_during_session%2C_April_2024.jpg","title":"The Navajo Nation Council in session","desc":"The council chamber at Window Rock, Arizona, during a sitting of the legislative body of the Navajo Nation, one of the 575 tribal entities the federal government recognises.","credit":"Photograph by SounderBruce, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navajo_Nation_Council_Chamber_interior_during_session,_April_2024.jpg","alt":"A round chamber with a log-beamed ceiling and painted murals, delegates seated at wooden desks with laptops facing a raised bench."}},
{"id":"us-003","num":3,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Cut-marked horse bones from Bluefish Caves and the burials at Upward Sun River place people in eastern <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the plain that joined Siberia to Alaska.","questions":["A founding branch called the Ancient Beringians split off in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and stayed there, which is why the genetics of the region reads like a homeland rather than a corridor.","Too little snow fell on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for glaciers to form, so it stayed open grassland grazed by mammoth and horse while ice buried the rest of Canada."],"answer":"Beringia","answerText":"Beringia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Land bridge</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 35,700 – 11,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Founding split</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 22,000 – 18,100 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alaskan sites</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from c. 14,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","geography","archaeology","prehistory","migration","north america"],"abstract":"<b>Beringia</b> is the land joining Siberia to Alaska whenever glacial seas fell far enough to bare the shallow floor of the Bering Strait, and its eastern half is now Alaska and the Yukon. It was no causeway but a plain hundreds of kilometres across, and so little snow fell on it that no glacier ever formed there, so it stayed open grassland while ice sheets buried the rest of Canada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sea-floor sediments suggest the strait stayed flooded until surprisingly late, about 35,700 years ago, while separate work on the drowning of the plain puts its end nearer 11,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The grassland carried mammoth, horse, bison and caribou, and butchered bones from Bluefish Caves in the northern Yukon carry cut marks made between roughly 24,000 and 12,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Genetics gives the eastern half a founding population of its own, since the Ancient Beringians split from the ancestors of other Native Americans between about 22,000 and 18,100 years ago and stayed in the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> That branch is known from burials in the Tanana valley of interior Alaska, at a site called Xasaa Na' in the language of the people whose country it is, where three young children were laid in a camp about 11,500 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their genome is what showed the branch had separated early and then gone no further south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The oldest occupations generally accepted in eastern Beringia lie in the same valley, at Swan Point, Broken Mammoth and Mead, and none of them is older than about 14,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A long pause in the north is the standstill hypothesis, which explains a set of mutations carried across the Americas and absent from the nearest Asian relatives by thousands of years of isolation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Whether the region was a corridor or a country is the question underneath all of it, and the archaeology of Alaska now reads less like a crossing than like somewhere people lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["John F. Hoffecker, Scott A. Elias, G. Richard Scott and Dennis H. O'Rourke, “Beringia and the peopling of the Western Hemisphere,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 290, no. 1990 (2023): 20222246, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9832545/. [Open access]","Jesse R. Farmer, Tamara Pico, Ona M. Underwood, et al., “The Bering Strait was flooded 10,000 years before the Last Glacial Maximum,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 1 (2023): e2206742119, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2206742119. [Open access]","Martin Jakobsson, Christof Pearce, Thomas M. Cronin, et al., “Post-glacial flooding of the Bering Land Bridge dated to 11 cal ka BP based on new geophysical and sediment records,” <i>Climate of the Past</i> 13 (2017): 991–1005, https://doi.org/10.5194/cp-13-991-2017. [Open access]","Lauriane Bourgeon, Ariane Burke and Thomas Higham, “Earliest human presence in North America dated to the Last Glacial Maximum: New radiocarbon dates from Bluefish Caves, Canada,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 1 (2017): e0169486, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0169486. [Open access]","J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben A. Potter, Lasse Vinner, et al., “Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan genome reveals first founding population of Native Americans,” <i>Nature</i> 553, no. 7687 (2018): 203–207, https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/7887/. [Open access]","Ben A. Potter, Joel D. Irish and Joshua D. Reuther, “Ancient Burials at Upward Sun River, Central Alaska,” <i>Bioarchaeology International</i> 9, no. 4 (2024), https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/24797/1/BI_Potter%20et%20al%202024%20Accepted%20Version.pdf. [Open access]","Erika Tamm, Toomas Kivisild, Maere Reidla, et al., “Beringian standstill and spread of Native American founders,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 2, no. 9 (2007): e829, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1952074/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Bering_Land_Bridge_NPS_06292023_%2853042106205%29.jpg/1920px-Bering_Land_Bridge_NPS_06292023_%2853042106205%29.jpg","title":"Eastern Beringia today","desc":"Granite tors above the tundra at Serpentine Hot Springs, in the Bering Land Bridge National Preserve on the Seward Peninsula of Alaska.","credit":"Photograph by Gisel Adame for the National Park Service, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bering_Land_Bridge_NPS_06292023_%2853042106205%29.jpg","alt":"Weathered granite outcrops standing above green tundra, with snow-streaked hills behind under a pale sky."}},
{"id":"us-004","num":4,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Kelp forests ring the North Pacific and offer much the same food at every stage, which is the ecology behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the Americas.","questions":["The corridor between the Canadian ice sheets opened only about 13,800 years ago, which is the strongest argument for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> down the Pacific shore instead.","No shoreline site south of Alaska is yet older than about 12,600 years, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is still a hypothesis with good circumstantial support rather than a demonstrated road."],"answer":"coastal migration route","answerText":"coastal migration route","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Coast ice-free</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 17,000 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Best windows</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 24,500 – 22,000 BP and 16,400 – 14,800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Corridor opens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,800 BP</span></div>","undatable":true,"traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","prehistory","migration","geography","north america"],"abstract":"The <b>coastal migration route</b> is the proposal that the first people to reach the Americas came down the Pacific shore rather than overland through the middle of the continent. Its best-known form is the kelp highway hypothesis, which holds that the productive kelp ecosystems ringing the North Pacific could have carried people from north-eastern Asia to the Americas with much the same food available at every stage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any such road depends on the ice going first, and cosmogenic dating in the Alexander Archipelago of south-eastern Alaska shows an open and productive corridor there after about 17,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ice and ocean modelling narrows the opportunity further, naming two windows, roughly 24,500 to 22,000 and 16,400 to 14,800 years ago, when winter sea ice and ice-free summers would both have helped a traveller.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sea ice is not simply a barrier in that reading, since a frozen winter shore is a surface to walk on and a place to hunt seals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The alternative road, the corridor between the Laurentide and Cordilleran ice sheets, is now dated to a final opening about 13,800 years ago, which is too late for the earliest sites south of the ice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is the strongest argument for the coast, because a corridor still under ice cannot have carried the people who were already at Cooper's Ferry in Idaho by roughly 15,785 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cooper's Ferry stands on the Salmon River, reachable up the Columbia from the sea, and its stemmed points resemble the late Pleistocene of north-eastern Asia rather than anything Clovis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What the California islands show is early competence at sea, since a camp on Santa Rosa Island about 11,700 years old yields barbed points, birds, sea mammals and shellfish fetched from distant rocky shores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The awkward fact is that no shoreline site south of Alaska has yet been dated older than about 12,600 years, so the coast remains a hypothesis with good circumstantial support rather than a demonstrated road.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Jon M. Erlandson, Michael H. Graham, Bruce J. Bourque, Debra Corbett, James A. Estes and Robert S. Steneck, “The Kelp Highway Hypothesis: Marine Ecology, the Coastal Migration Theory, and the Peopling of the Americas,” <i>The Journal of Island and Coastal Archaeology</i> 2, no. 2 (2007): 161–174, https://www.elakhaalliance.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/Erlandsonetal.KelpHighwayinJICA.pdf. [Open access]","Alia J. Lesnek, Jason P. Briner, Charlotte Lindqvist, James F. Baichtal and Timothy H. Heaton, “Deglaciation of the Pacific coastal corridor directly preceded the human colonization of the Americas,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 5 (2018): eaar5040, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5976267/. [Open access]","Summer K. Praetorius, Jay R. Alder, Alan Condron, et al., “Ice and ocean constraints on early human migrations into North America along the Pacific coast,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 7 (2023): e2208738120, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9963817/. [Open access]","Jorie Clark, Anders E. Carlson, Alberto V. Reyes, et al., “The age of the opening of the Ice-Free Corridor and implications for the peopling of the Americas,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 14 (2022): e2118558119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9168949/. [Open access]","Loren G. Davis, David B. Madsen, David A. Sisson, et al., “Dating of a large tool assemblage at the Cooper's Ferry site (Idaho, USA) to ~15,785 cal yr B.P. extends the age of stemmed points in the Americas,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 51 (2022): eade1248, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9788777/. [Open access]","Ben A. Potter, James F. Baichtal, Alwynne B. Beaudoin, et al., “Current evidence allows multiple models for the peopling of the Americas,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 8 (2018): eaat5473, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6082647/. [Open access]","Jon M. Erlandson, Todd J. Braje, Amira F. Ainis, et al., “Maritime Paleoindian technology, subsistence, and ecology at an ~11,700 year old Paleocoastal site on California's Northern Channel Islands, USA,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 9 (2020): e0238866, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7498104/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Sanc0023_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/1920px-Sanc0023_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg","title":"A giant kelp canopy off Anacapa Island","desc":"Giant kelp at the surface in the landing cove of Anacapa Island, in the Channel Islands National Marine Sanctuary off southern California.","credit":"Photograph by Claire Fackler, Channel Islands National Marine Sanctuary, NOAA, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanc0023_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg","alt":"Golden fronds of giant kelp floating on clear green water at the foot of a grey rock face."}},
{"id":"us-005","num":5,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"The Carolinas run Clovis into Redstone and then Hardaway-Dalton, one of many regional sequences left by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across North America before about 8000 BCE.","questions":["Fine stone carried hundreds of kilometres and red ochre dug at Powars II in Wyoming show the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moving in a way settled peoples did not.","Whether the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lived chiefly off mammoth and giant bison or off a much broader range of plants, fish and small game is still argued."],"answer":"Paleo-Indians","answerText":"Paleo-Indians","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until c. 8000 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Clovis</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fluted points</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 13,000 – 11,400 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","archaeology","prehistory","north america","united states","migration"],"abstract":"The <b>Paleo-Indians</b> are the first peoples of North America, the hunters and foragers who lived across the continent from the end of the last ice age until the Archaic way of life takes over about 8000 BCE. A Smithsonian survey used the name in 1940 without ever defining it, and it has since come to cover everyone in the Americas before that date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their signature is the fluted point, a spear tip with a channel flake struck from the base up each face so that it could be seated in a socket of bone or ivory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There was never only one kind of them, and the Carolinas alone run Clovis into Redstone and then Hardaway-Dalton between roughly 13,000 and 11,400 years ago, with local Clovis points measurably unlike western ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Further west the assemblage at Cooper's Ferry in Idaho carries stemmed rather than fluted points and is dated to about 15,785 years ago, older than any Clovis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> They moved constantly, and in the Carolinas two large groupings can be traced to the stone they used, one on Piedmont rhyolite and one on chert from the Savannah River, meeting along a line more than 300 kilometres (190 miles) long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They mined as well as hunted, and the Powars II site in Wyoming is the oldest hematite quarry known in the Americas, dug for red ochre from about 12,840 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much of their food came from big animals is the open question, and one hemisphere-wide review reads the bones, the tools and the travelling as specialisation on animals over a tonne.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> On that reading the Anzick child's protein was about 96 per cent megafaunal, but only about a dozen North American sites securely put people among mammoth or mastodon, and several of those have been questioned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> What is settled is that they were here in the ice age, since a point found in 1927 among the ribs of an extinct bison near Folsom, New Mexico, ended that argument for good.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Frank H. H. Roberts Jr., “Developments in the problem of the North American Paleo-Indian,” in <i>Essays in Historical Anthropology of North America</i>, Smithsonian Miscellaneous Collections 100 (Washington, DC: Smithsonian Institution, 1940), 51–116, https://archive.org/details/smithsonianmisce1001940smit. [Open access]","Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr. and David L. Carlson, “The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","I. Randolph Daniel Jr., Albert C. Goodyear, Christopher R. Moore, David K. Thulman and Joseph E. Wilkinson, “Toward a synthesis of Paleoamerican fluted point cultures in the Carolinas,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 28341, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12321997/. [Open access]","Loren G. Davis, David B. Madsen, David A. Sisson, et al., “Dating of a large tool assemblage at the Cooper's Ferry site (Idaho, USA) to ~15,785 cal yr B.P. extends the age of stemmed points in the Americas,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 51 (2022): eade1248, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9788777/. [Open access]","Spencer R. Pelton, Lorena Becerra-Valdivia, Alexander Craib, et al., “In situ evidence for Paleoindian hematite quarrying at the Powars II site (48PL330), Wyoming,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 20 (2022): e2201005119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9171918/. [Open access]","Ben A. Potter, James C. Chatters, Luciano Prates, et al., “Hemisphere-wide evidence of Early Paleoindian megaherbivore specialization,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 27 (2026): eaef9628, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13322243/. [Open access]","Donald K. Grayson, David J. Meltzer and Ryan P. Breslawski, “Overkill and the North American archaeological record—not guilty by association? A comment on Wolfe and Broughton (2020),” <i>Journal of Archaeological Science</i> 128 (2021): 105312, https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/people.smu.edu/dist/5/554/files/2021/08/Grayson-Meltzer-and-Breslawski-2021-JAS-Overkill-and-the-North-American-archaeological-record%E2%80%94not-guilty-by-association.pdf. [Open access]","Linda S. Cordell, “The Folsom site in retrospect,” in <i>Vermejo Park, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook</i> (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 83–86, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.83. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Folsom_Point_on_Sand_%2854429437247%29.jpg/1920px-Folsom_Point_on_Sand_%2854429437247%29.jpg","title":"A Folsom point at Great Sand Dunes","desc":"The base of a fluted Folsom point lying on sand in Great Sand Dunes National Park, Colorado, where hunters took the giant bison at the end of the ice age.","credit":"National Park Service, Great Sand Dunes National Park and Preserve, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Folsom_Point_on_Sand_%2854429437247%29.jpg","alt":"A brown translucent stone point, broken at the tip, with a long channel scar running up its face and finely chipped edges."}},
{"id":"us-006","num":6,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Blackwater Draw in New Mexico gives its name to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose fluted points spread from coast to coast in about three hundred years.","questions":["The only burial known from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an infant boy at Anzick in Montana, laid with more than a hundred ochre-stained tools and reburied in 2014.","Redating compressed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the three centuries between 13,050 and 12,750 years ago, far too late for it to be the first people on the continent."],"answer":"Clovis culture","answerText":"Clovis culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Anzick burial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 12,905 – 12,695 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","archaeology","prehistory","north america","united states"],"abstract":"The <b>Clovis culture</b> is a North American toolmaking tradition of the very end of the ice age, named after Blackwater Draw, a dry lake bed near the town of Clovis in eastern New Mexico. Careful redating now puts the whole of it between 13,050 and 12,750 years ago, three centuries in which one design of fluted point spread from coast to coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is therefore not the work of the first Americans, who were already here, and that reversal is the largest change the subject has undergone in fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its points are not uniform across that range either, and shape analysis finds Carolina examples measurably unlike western ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> These people also buried caches of tools, at East Wenatchee in Washington and at Fenn and Simon further inland, and the pieces in them are often larger and finer than working gear needs to be.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The only Clovis burial known anywhere is at the Anzick site in Montana, where an infant boy was laid with more than a hundred stone and bone objects, every one of them stained with red ochre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dating on the bone itself places the burial between about 12,905 and 12,695 years ago, in agreement with the artefacts beside him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> His genome showed him ancestral to a great many Native Americans alive today, which settled the Asian origin of the founding population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> He is not in a museum: the remains were reburied in a Native American ceremony in June 2014, on the Montana land where he was found, while the tools stayed in state care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What these people ate is still argued, and isotopes from that same child suggest mammoth supplied much of his mother's diet, though the tradition ends within a few generations as the Younger Dryas cold sets in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr. and David L. Carlson, “The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","I. Randolph Daniel Jr., Albert C. Goodyear, Christopher R. Moore, David K. Thulman and Joseph E. Wilkinson, “Toward a synthesis of Paleoamerican fluted point cultures in the Carolinas,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 28341, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12321997/. [Open access]","Richard B. Waitt, “Megafloods and Clovis cache at Wenatchee, Washington,” <i>Quaternary Research</i> 85 (2016): 430–444, https://fop.cascadiageo.org/pacific_northwest_cell/2018/Background_Literature/Waitt2016WenatcheeMegafloods.pdf. [Open access]","Briggs Buchanan, J. David Kilby, Bruce B. Huckell, et al., “A morphometric assessment of the intended function of cached Clovis points,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 7, no. 2 (2012): e30530, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0030530. [Open access]","Lorena Becerra-Valdivia, Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr., et al., “Reassessing the chronology of the archaeological site of Anzick,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 27 (2018): 7000–7003, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6142201/. [Open access]","Morten Rasmussen, Sarah L. Anzick, Michael R. Waters, et al., “The genome of a Late Pleistocene human from a Clovis burial site in western Montana,” <i>Nature</i> 506, no. 7487 (2014): 225–229, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4878442/. [Open access]","James C. Chatters, Ben A. Potter, Stuart J. Fiedel, et al., “Mammoth featured heavily in western Clovis diet,” <i>Science Advances</i> 10, no. 49 (2024): eadr3814, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adr3814. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/DeGraffenried_Clovis_Cache.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-DeGraffenried_Clovis_Cache.tif.jpg","title":"The DeGraffenried Clovis cache","desc":"Four large bifaces and a late-stage point preform of Edwards Plateau chert, buried together in central Texas — the kind of deposit whose pieces are finer than working gear needs to be.","credit":"Photograph by SpringDraw, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DeGraffenried_Clovis_Cache.tif","alt":"Five honey-coloured translucent flaked stone tools laid on black cloth around a five-centimetre scale bar."}},
{"id":"us-007","num":7,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"For most of the 20th century American archaeology held to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the model that the fluted-point hunters were the earliest people on the continent.","questions":["A panel that visited Monte Verde in Chile in 1997 and accepted its early layer is usually taken to be the end of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as an orthodoxy.","Because the corridor between the Canadian ice sheets opened only about 13,800 years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lost the road it had assumed as well as the date."],"answer":"Clovis First","answerText":"Clovis First","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Clovis dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">13,050 – 12,750 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">the 1930s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Overturned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["theory","archaeology","prehistory","history","north america"],"abstract":"<b>Clovis First</b> is the model, orthodox in American archaeology for most of the 20th century, that the Clovis hunters were the first people in the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It rested on a real discovery, since a spear point found in 1927 among the ribs of an extinct bison near Folsom in New Mexico had proved that people were here while ice age animals still lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Clovis was then shown to come earlier than Folsom on the Plains, which put it at the base of the sequence, and for decades it was taken to be the basal horizon of the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The model was strong for good reasons: it was precisely dated, it was testable, and it predicted that nothing older would be found beneath it. Claims for older sites were made steadily, Meadowcroft Rockshelter in Pennsylvania among them, and were as steadily rejected on the grounds that the stratigraphy or the dating would not hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> It broke in 1997, when a panel of archaeologists visited Monte Verde in southern Chile and accepted in print that the main layer there was older than any Clovis point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The evidence since has run one way, with stemmed points older than Clovis in Idaho, human faeces in Oregon and footprints at White Sands dated to the height of the glaciation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Genetics agreed from a different direction, because the founding population had parted from its Asian relatives thousands of years before any Clovis point was struck.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The road went as well as the date, since the corridor between the Canadian ice sheets is now dated to a final opening about 13,800 years ago, later than the sites it was meant to explain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> What has replaced it is an argument rather than a model, one synthesis reading a coherent record back to 20,000 years ago while another finds clean stratigraphy only from Clovis onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Michael R. Waters, Thomas W. Stafford Jr. and David L. Carlson, “The age of Clovis—13,050 to 12,750 cal yr B.P.,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 43 (2020): eaaz0455, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7577710/. [Open access]","Linda S. Cordell, “The Folsom site in retrospect,” in <i>Vermejo Park, New Mexico Geological Society 27th Annual Fall Field Conference Guidebook</i> (Socorro: NMGS, 1976), 83–86, https://doi.org/10.56577/FFC-27.83. [Open access]","David J. Meltzer, Donald K. Grayson, Gerardo Ardila, et al., “On the Pleistocene antiquity of Monte Verde, southern Chile,” <i>American Antiquity</i> 62, no. 4 (1997): 659–663, https://doi.org/10.2307/281884. [Paywalled]","David B. Madsen, Loren G. Davis, Thomas J. Williams, Masami Izuho and Fumie Iizuka, “Characterizing the American Upper Paleolithic,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 43 (2025): eady9545, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12542946/. [Open access]","Todd A. Surovell, Sarah A. Allaun, Barbara A. Crass, et al., “Late date of human arrival to North America: Continental scale differences in stratigraphic integrity of pre-13,000 BP archaeological sites,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0264092, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9020715/. [Open access]","Jorie Clark, Anders E. Carlson, Alberto V. Reyes, et al., “The age of the opening of the Ice-Free Corridor and implications for the peopling of the Americas,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 14 (2022): e2118558119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9168949/. [Open access]","J. Víctor Moreno-Mayar, Ben A. Potter, Lasse Vinner, et al., “Terminal Pleistocene Alaskan genome reveals first founding population of Native Americans,” <i>Nature</i> 553, no. 7687 (2018): 203–207, https://researchonline.ljmu.ac.uk/id/eprint/7887/. [Open access]","Vance T. Holliday, Jason D. Windingstad, Jordon Bright, et al., “Paleolake geochronology supports Last Glacial Maximum (LGM) age for human tracks at White Sands, New Mexico,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 25 (2025): eadv4951, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12175891/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/The_famous_Folsom_Point_found_between_bison_ribs.jpg","title":"The Folsom point among the bison ribs","desc":"The find made near Folsom, New Mexico, in 1927: a stone point in place between the ribs of an extinct bison, which settled whether people had been here during the ice age.","credit":"Unknown photographer, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_famous_Folsom_Point_found_between_bison_ribs.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a leaf-shaped stone point resting in dry sediment between two curved fossil ribs."}},
{"id":"us-008","num":8,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Peat sealed the wood, cordage and seaweed at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in southern Chile, and its acceptance in 1997 reopened the search for early sites in the United States.","questions":["Nine species of marine algae, carried inland from distant beaches for food and medicine, were dated directly at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to between 14,220 and 13,980 years ago.","The stone tools at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are mostly pebble flakes and bola stones with no fluted points at all, which is part of why the site was so hard to accommodate."],"answer":"Monte Verde","answerText":"Monte Verde","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">MV-II</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 14,500 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Seaweed dated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">14,220 – 13,980 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Accepted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1997</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","prehistory","chile","south america"],"abstract":"<b>Monte Verde</b> stands on Chinchihuapi Creek in southern Chile, more than 10,000 kilometres (6,200 miles) from the Bering Strait, and it is the site that broke the belief that Clovis hunters were the first Americans. What makes it exceptional is a blanket of waterlogged peat that sealed the deposit and shut out the oxygen, so wood, cordage and plant remains survived where in that climate they would ordinarily rot away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The upper layer held the foundation of a long tent-like dwelling and of a second structure, hearths, and human footprints pressed into the clay.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its food remains run from grass seeds and forest nuts to the bones of a gomphothere and a palaeocamelid, an inventory that describes foragers rather than specialised big-game hunters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stone tools are mostly expedient pebble flakes and bola stones, and there are no fluted points anywhere in them, which is part of why the site was so hard to accommodate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That upper layer dates to about 14,500 years ago, roughly 1,500 years before Clovis, and it sits at the far southern end of two continents people were supposed to have entered from the north.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nine species of marine algae from its hearths were dated directly to between 14,220 and 13,980 years ago, and the mixture of food and medicinal species describes people who knew the shore as well as the forest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A panel of well-known archaeologists visited the excavation in 1997 and accepted in print that the layer really was older than Clovis, and the Clovis First model never recovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the United States that acceptance licensed a generation of early claims, among them Meadowcroft, Page-Ladson, the Paisley Caves, Cooper's Ferry and White Sands, now gathered into a proposed American Upper Paleolithic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The argument reopened in 2026 with a paper putting the deposit thousands of years younger, and critiques answered within weeks that its samples came from cutbank exposures rather than the excavation itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Tom D. Dillehay, Carlos Ocampo, José Saavedra, et al., “New archaeological evidence for an early human presence at Monte Verde, Chile,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 11 (2015): e0141923, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0141923. [Open access]","Mario Pino and Tom D. Dillehay, “Monte Verde II: an assessment of new radiocarbon dates and their sedimentological context,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 393 (2023): 524–540, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.32. [Open access]","Tom D. Dillehay, C. Ramírez, M. Pino, et al., “Monte Verde: seaweed, food, medicine, and the peopling of South America,” <i>Science</i> 320, no. 5877 (2008): 784–786, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1156533. [Paywalled]","David J. Meltzer, Donald K. Grayson, Gerardo Ardila, et al., “On the Pleistocene antiquity of Monte Verde, southern Chile,” <i>American Antiquity</i> 62, no. 4 (1997): 659–663, https://doi.org/10.2307/281884. [Paywalled]","David B. Madsen, Loren G. Davis, Thomas J. Williams, Masami Izuho and Fumie Iizuka, “Characterizing the American Upper Paleolithic,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 43 (2025): eady9545, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12542946/. [Open access]","Todd A. Surovell, César Méndez, Juan-Luis García, et al., “A mid-Holocene age for Monte Verde challenges the timeline of human colonization of South America,” <i>Science</i> 391, no. 6791 (2026): 1283–1288, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adw9217. [Paywalled]","Michael R. Waters, Jessi J. Halligan, Rolfe D. Mandel, et al., “Geoarchaeological assessment of the suggested middle Holocene age for Monte Verde II, Chile,” eLetter, <i>Science</i>, May 5, 2026, https://fundacionmonteverde.cl/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Waters-et-al.-Rebuttal-to-Surovell-et-al.-2026.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Monte_Verde_13.JPG/1920px-Monte_Verde_13.JPG","title":"The waterlogged ground at Monte Verde","desc":"Peat and nalca along Chinchihuapi Creek in southern Chile; it is this permanently wet ground that preserved the wood, cordage and plants at the site.","credit":"Photograph by Rodolfo Ditzel Lacoa, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Verde_13.JPG","alt":"A low boggy bank of matted grass and broad-leaved plants beside a dark still creek, with bare trees behind."}},
{"id":"us-009","num":9,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Copper tools from the Great Lakes, oyster middens in Georgia and the mounds at Watson Brake all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the long middle of the continent's prehistory.","questions":["Generalists rather than big-game specialists, the peoples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left grinding stones, broad plant use and larger, less mobile settlements.","Goosefoot, sumpweed, little barley and sunflower were domesticated during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, thousands of years before maize mattered anywhere east of the Mississippi."],"answer":"North American Archaic","answerText":"North American Archaic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 8500 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ends</span><span class=\"dt-v\">as late as c. 300 CE in places</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Watson Brake</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 5,400 – 5,000 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":3,"tags":["era","archaeology","prehistory","north america","united states"],"abstract":"The <b>North American Archaic</b> is the long middle of the continent's prehistory, opening around 8500 BCE and closing as late as the first few centuries CE in some places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its people were descendants of the Paleo-Indians but generalists rather than specialists, living off a broad range of wild game and plants and, towards the end, off crops they had domesticated themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Grinding tools such as manos and metates become common on their sites, and by the late Archaic the settlements are larger and less mobile as plant food matters more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the boundary with the earlier world falls depends on the region, and in the Carolinas the fluted-point sequence gives way to Hardaway-Dalton forms by about 11,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phytoliths from a spring section on Tesuque Creek in northern New Mexico show late Archaic foragers using yucca and even a palmetto that does not grow there naturally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> On the Georgia and South Carolina coast oysters were taken from the early Late Archaic onwards, and five thousand years of harvesting did not exhaust the reefs, which industrial fishing managed within decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They worked metal as well, hammering copper from the western Great Lakes into tools between roughly 7,520 and 6,180 years ago, the earliest use of native copper for working tools anywhere in the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That copper travelled, and a band from Isle Royale was buried with cremated bodies in a shell ring on St Catherines Island in Georgia about 4,000 years ago, some 1,850 kilometres (1,150 miles) from its source.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> They built too, raising a complex of earthen mounds at Watson Brake in Louisiana between 5,400 and 5,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> And they domesticated their own plants, cultivating goosefoot, sumpweed, little barley, erect knotweed, squash and sunflower from about 7,000 years ago and holding distinctly domesticated forms by roughly 3,800, long before maize mattered in the east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["National Park Service, “Archaic Period,” accessed September 6, 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/archaic-period.htm. [Open access]","I. Randolph Daniel Jr., Albert C. Goodyear, Christopher R. Moore, David K. Thulman and Joseph E. Wilkinson, “Toward a synthesis of Paleoamerican fluted point cultures in the Carolinas,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 28341, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12321997/. [Open access]","Alison Damick, Arlene Rosen and Scott Ortman, “Palm springs on the Rio Grande: Insight into Archaic forager plant use from phytoliths recovered from a Late Holocene alluvial section in northern New Mexico,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0258231, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8509927/. [Open access]","Victor D. Thompson, Torben Rick, Carey J. Garland, et al., “Ecosystem stability and Native American oyster harvesting along the Atlantic Coast of the United States,” <i>Science Advances</i> 6, no. 28 (2020): eaba9652, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7439446/. [Open access]","Michelle R. Bebber, Briggs Buchanan and Jacob Holland-Lulewicz, “Refining the chronology of North America's copper using traditions: A macroscalar approach via Bayesian modeling,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9041870/. [Open access]","Matthew C. Sanger, Mark A. Hill, Gregory D. Lattanzi, et al., “Early metal use and crematory practices in the American Southeast,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 115, no. 33 (2018): E7672–E7679, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6099914/. [Open access]","Joe W. Saunders, Rolfe D. Mandel, Roger T. Saucier, et al., “A mound complex in Louisiana at 5400-5000 years before the present,” <i>Science</i> 277, no. 5333 (1997): 1796–1799, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.277.5333.1796. [Paywalled]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The sleeping crops of eastern North America: a new synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12079124/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Copper_knife%2C_spearpoints%2C_awls%2C_and_spud%2C_Late_Archaic_period%2C_Wisconsin%2C_3000_BC-1000_BC_-_Wisconsin_Historical_Museum_-_DSC03436.JPG/1920px-Copper_knife%2C_spearpoints%2C_awls%2C_and_spud%2C_Late_Archaic_period%2C_Wisconsin%2C_3000_BC-1000_BC_-_Wisconsin_Historical_Museum_-_DSC03436.JPG","title":"Late Archaic copper from Wisconsin","desc":"Spearpoints, a knife, a spud and awls hammered from native copper of the western Great Lakes, thousands of years before metal was worked for tools anywhere else.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copper_knife,_spearpoints,_awls,_and_spud,_Late_Archaic_period,_Wisconsin,_3000_BC-1000_BC_-_Wisconsin_Historical_Museum_-_DSC03436.JPG","alt":"Green-patinated copper spearpoints, a curved knife, a heavy chisel-like spud and four slender awls arranged in a museum case."}},
{"id":"us-010","num":10,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Made from teosinte in Mexico, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the Tucson Basin by 2100 BCE and was being watered by dug canals within a few centuries.","questions":["Eastern woodland peoples had domesticated goosefoot, sumpweed and sunflower long before <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived, and it took a further thousand years after arriving to matter there.","Skeletons from the midcontinent show mortality rising as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and beans took over, where the earlier native crops had spread while mortality was falling."],"answer":"maize","answerText":"maize","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Domesticated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 9,000 years ago, in Mexico</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Southwest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 2100 BCE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">East, a staple</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 1,100 BP</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":1,"tags":["plant","agriculture","prehistory","archaeology","north america","united states"],"abstract":"<b>Maize</b> is the crop that eventually fed most of Native North America, and it was not domesticated there, having been made out of teosinte, a Mexican grass, about 9,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It travelled slowly and unevenly, and at El Gigante in Honduras, on the road north, maize appears about 4,400 years ago while the turn to field crops comes only after 4,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It reached the south-western United States no later than 2100 BCE, carried north not by farmers migrating but from one community to the next along a chain of related languages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within a few centuries it was worth engineering for, since irrigation canals were being dug in the Tucson Basin between 1750 and 1500 BCE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the eastern woodlands the sequence runs the other way about, because people there had already domesticated crops of their own, cultivating goosefoot, sumpweed, little barley, erect knotweed, squash and sunflower from roughly 7,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Maize reached the north-east by about 2,300 years ago but did not arrive in the heart of that farming country, up the Ohio valley, until about 1,400 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only after about 1,100 years ago did it become important across most of eastern North America, so the crop was in the ground for a thousand years before it mattered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The change brought costs as well as calories, and skeletal populations of the midcontinent show mortality falling as the native crops spread and rising again as maize and beans took over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor did the new order hold, since after about 1300 CE the populations of that region fall away as settlements disperse and diets broaden once more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The indigenous crops did not survive the competition and are now known only from the archaeological record, which is why they are described as sleeping rather than lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Chin Jian Yang, Luis Fernando Samayoa, Peter J. Bradbury, et al., “The genetic architecture of teosinte catalyzed and constrained maize domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 12 (2019): 5643–5652, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1820997116. [Open access]","Douglas J. Kennett, Thomas K. Harper, Amber VanDerwarker, et al., “Trans-Holocene Bayesian chronology for tree and field crop use from El Gigante rockshelter, Honduras,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 6 (2023): e0287195, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0287195. [Open access]","William L. Merrill, Robert J. Hard, Jonathan B. Mabry, et al., “The diffusion of maize to the southwestern United States and its impact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 50 (2009): 21019–21026, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2795521/. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The sleeping crops of eastern North America: a new synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12079124/. [Open access]","George R. Milner and Jesper L. Boldsen, “Population trends and the transition to agriculture: Global processes as seen from North America,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2209478119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9942849/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Early_20th_century_Hopi_corn_%28UBC%29.jpg/1920px-Early_20th_century_Hopi_corn_%28UBC%29.jpg","title":"Hopi maize","desc":"Three preserved ears of early 20th-century Hopi corn, varieties of the crop that reached the Southwest before 2100 BCE.","credit":"Photograph by Adam Jones, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_20th_century_Hopi_corn_%28UBC%29.jpg","alt":"Three ears of maize with mixed dark blue, purple and cream kernels, laid in a museum case."}},
{"id":"us-011","num":11,"category":"United States · Native America","question":"Maize gives the bean its pole, the bean fixes nitrogen and the squash shades out weeds, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are grown in one hill.","questions":["Beans were adopted in eastern North America only about 900 years ago, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are a much younger arrangement than the maize at the centre of them.","Maize lacks lysine and tryptophan and beans supply both, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> feed a household better together than the same three crops grown apart would."],"answer":"Three Sisters","answerText":"Three Sisters","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Beans adopted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 900 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Widespread</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by the mid-1500s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","agriculture","north america","united states","history"],"abstract":"The <b>Three Sisters</b> are maize, beans and squash planted together in one hill, the intercropping system Native nations used across the north-east, the Great Lakes, the south-east, the south-west and the Plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each plant does something the others cannot, since the maize stalk is the pole the bean climbs, the bean fixes nitrogen the other two draw on, and the squash's broad leaves shade out weeds and hold moisture in the soil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nutrition works the same way, because maize is short of lysine and tryptophan and beans supply both, while squash adds sugars, oils and the protein in its seeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At least fifteen nations of the north-eastern woodlands and south-eastern Canada farmed this way, the Wendat and the Haudenosaunee among them, and the maize was usually treated in wood-ash lye before it was eaten.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The claim holds up when it is measured: a field experiment run with Native growers found that intercropping cut salt-extractable nitrate by 54 per cent and raised short-term soil respiration by a quarter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The system is younger than any of its parts, since maize reached the eastern woodlands long before beans did and beans were adopted only about 900 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It became common in the Midwest and the north-east by the middle of the 1500s, so an arrangement most readers take to be ancient is a few generations older than the colonies rather than thousands of years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where it was practised the fields were worked entirely by hand and kept in cultivation for long stretches, with crop residue and other amendments holding the fertility up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was not simply an improvement either, and skeletal populations of the midcontinent show mortality rising as maize and beans took over from the older native crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The system is being farmed again, and that field experiment was designed and run with the Oneida and Menominee nations, Dream of Wild Health and the Nebraska Indian Community College rather than about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["D. G. Kapayou, E. M. Herrighty, C. Gish Hill, et al., “Reuniting the Three Sisters: collaborative science with Native growers to improve soil and community health,” <i>Agriculture and Human Values</i> 40, no. 1 (2022): 65–82, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9288846/. [Open access]","Tania M. Ngapo, Pauline Bilodeau, Yves Arcand, et al., “Historical Indigenous Food Preparation Using Produce of the Three Sisters Intercropping System,” <i>Foods</i> 10, no. 3 (2021): 524, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8001537/. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The sleeping crops of eastern North America: a new synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12079124/. [Open access]","John P. Hart and Robert S. Feranec, “Using Maize δ15N values to assess soil fertility in fifteenth- and sixteenth-century ad Iroquoian agricultural fields,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 15, no. 4 (2020): e0230952, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7141618/. [Open access]","George R. Milner and Jesper L. Boldsen, “Population trends and the transition to agriculture: Global processes as seen from North America,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2209478119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9942849/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Three_Sisters_Garden.jpg/1920px-Three_Sisters_Garden.jpg","title":"A Three Sisters garden","desc":"Maize, climbing beans and squash grown together in one bed, the intercropping system Indigenous farmers in North America developed and still work today.","credit":"Photograph by Myotus, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Sisters_Garden.jpg","alt":"A fenced raised garden bed of dark soil in which tall maize stalks rise above sprawling squash vines in flower."}},
{"id":"us-012","num":12,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Squash, sunflower, marshelder, goosefoot and erect knotweed together make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, five species bred from local wild plants in eastern North America long before maize arrived.","questions":["Charred seeds from rockshelters in Kentucky and Missouri date the first four crops of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to a narrow window around 3800 BP, an independent birthplace of farming.","Every member of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> except sunflower fell out of cultivation as maize spread and colonisation came, so the domesticated goosefoot and erect knotweed are extinct."],"answer":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","answerText":"Eastern Agricultural Complex","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">First crops</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3800 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Declined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">after c. 1000 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Eastern Agricultural Complex</b> is the group of seed crops that people in the river valleys and woodlands of eastern North America bred out of local wild plants, thousands of years before maize reached the region from the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Five species are securely domesticated — squash, sunflower, marshelder, goosefoot and erect knotweed — each carrying the thinner seed coats, larger seeds or altered fruits that mark a plant reshaped by cultivation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest of them turn up as charred seeds in rockshelters and river-bank camps in Kentucky, Tennessee, Missouri and Illinois, where four of the five appear within a narrow window around 3800 BP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That makes eastern North America one of a small handful of places on earth where farming was invented rather than imported, alongside the Fertile Crescent, China, Mesoamerica and the Andes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Why it happened there and then is argued over, and one line of work reads the timing against rising population densities, arguing that people packed into the richest valleys turned to gardening because foraging alone no longer fed them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The crops were grown in floodplain gardens beside a diet still built on nuts, deer and fish, and none of them required a plough or a draught animal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sunflower is the only member that became a world crop, and ancient DNA from archaeological seeds shows that its domestication narrowed the plant's genetic variation, with a second narrowing when modern breeders took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The others were abandoned: maize agriculture spread through the region in the centuries around 1000 CE, colonisation then broke apart the societies that had kept the older gardens, and cultivation stopped.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Goosefoot and erect knotweed still grow wild, but the domesticated forms are gone, which means nobody alive has seen the plants these fields were actually sown with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Archaeologists, farmers and students at more than twenty institutions now grow the wild relatives in experimental plots, and have found that goosefoot and erect knotweed sown together yield more than either sown alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","agriculture","archaeology","north america","united states","prehistory"],"sources":["Bruce D. Smith, “Eastern North America as an independent center of plant domestication,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 103, no. 33 (2006): 12223–28, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1567861/. [Open access]","Bruce D. Smith and Richard A. Yarnell, “Initial formation of an indigenous crop complex in eastern North America at 3800 B.P.,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 106, no. 16 (2009): 6561–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2666091/. [Open access]","Elic M. Weitzel and Brian F. Codding, “Population growth as a driver of initial domestication in Eastern North America,” <i>Royal Society Open Science</i> 3, no. 8 (2016): 160319, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5108960/. [Open access]","Nathan Wales, Melis Akman, Ray H. B. Watson, et al., “Ancient DNA reveals the timing and persistence of organellar genetic bottlenecks over 3,000 years of sunflower domestication and improvement,” <i>Evolutionary Applications</i> 12, no. 1 (2019): 38–53, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6304678/. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The sleeping crops of eastern North America: a new synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12079124/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Chenopodium_berlandieri_1DS-II_1-9974.jpg/1920px-Chenopodium_berlandieri_1DS-II_1-9974.jpg","title":"Goosefoot in flower","desc":"Pitseed goosefoot, one of the five species of the Eastern Agricultural Complex; the domesticated form, with its thin seed coat, no longer exists.","credit":"Photograph by SAplants, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chenopodium_berlandieri_1DS-II_1-9974.jpg","alt":"A dense green flowering spike of a goosefoot plant, its tiny buds crowded along branching stems above toothed leaves."}},
{"id":"us-013","num":13,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Six concentric earthen ridges above the Mississippi floodplain in Louisiana enclose the plaza of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, raised about 3,300 years ago by people who hunted and gathered rather than farmed.","questions":["Millions of hand-moulded baked clay balls, used for cooking in a valley with no stone in it, are the signature artefact of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Louisiana earthwork complex.","Soapstone from the Appalachians and novaculite from Arkansas were carried to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose builders raised the second largest earthen mound in North America without farming or draught animals."],"answer":"Poverty Point","answerText":"Poverty Point","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Earthworks</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 3300 – 3200 BP</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mound A built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in under 90 days</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Poverty Point</b> is a complex of earthworks on Macon Ridge, a low rise above the Mississippi floodplain in northeastern Louisiana, raised about 3,300 years ago by people who hunted, fished and gathered rather than farmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Six concentric, semi-elliptical ridges wrap around a flat plaza, and people lived on those ridges, so at this site the monument and the village are the same construction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its largest mound holds roughly 238,500 cubic metres (312,000 cubic yards) of earth and is the second biggest earthen mound in North America, behind only Monks Mound at Cahokia, raised two thousand years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The valley itself contains no stone at all, being silt and clay for great distances in every direction, so every hard object on the site was carried in from somewhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Soapstone came from the southern Appalachians, novaculite from central Arkansas about 280 kilometres (175 miles) off, and other material from sources up to 1,100 kilometres (700 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> With no stone to heat, cooks moulded balls of loess by hand, baked them hard, and dropped them glowing into earth ovens, and these Poverty Point objects survive in their millions as the site's signature find.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where they cluster suggests cooking on a scale far beyond one household, at gatherings that drew people onto the ridges from a wide area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is what makes the place awkward for a rule long taken for granted, that building on this scale requires farming and a ranked society to command the labour, since neither has been found here.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Careful redating has compressed the earthworks into short bursts of work at the end of a few centuries of occupation, rather than the slow accumulation such mounds are usually taken to record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name is not the builders': it belonged to a 19th-century cotton plantation on the land, and the place is now a state historic site, a national monument and a World Heritage Site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","archaeology","north america","united states","prehistory"],"sources":["Seth B. Grooms, Grace M. V. Ward and Tristram R. Kidder, “Convergence at Poverty Point: A Revised Chronology of the Late Archaic Lower Mississippi Valley,” <i>Antiquity</i> 97, no. 396 (2023): 1453–69, https://doi.org/10.15184/aqy.2023.155. [Open access]","Tristram R. Kidder and Seth B. Grooms, “Chronological Hygiene and Bayesian Modeling of Poverty Point Sites in the Lower Mississippi Valley, circa 4200 to 3200 cal BP,” <i>American Antiquity</i> 89, no. 1 (2024): 98–118, https://doi.org/10.1017/aaq.2023.85. [Open access]","Diana M. Greenlee, “Poverty Point,” <i>64 Parishes</i>, Louisiana Endowment for the Humanities, last updated 26 February 2024, https://64parishes.org/entry/poverty-point. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Poverty Point National Monument,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/popo/index.htm. [Open access]","Christopher T. Hays, “Feasting at Poverty Point with Poverty Point Objects,” <i>Southeastern Archaeology</i> 38, no. 3 (2019): 193–207, https://doi.org/10.1080/0734578X.2018.1496315. [Paywalled]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d4/Poverty_point_1938_USACE-1_retouched.jpg","title":"Poverty Point from the air, 1938","desc":"The Army Corps of Engineers survey photograph in which the six concentric earthen ridges were first recognised from above.","credit":"United States Army Corps of Engineers. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poverty_point_1938_USACE-1_retouched.jpg","alt":"A black-and-white aerial photograph of farmland in which six nested curved ridges show as pale bands across the fields."}},
{"id":"us-014","num":14,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Cone-shaped burial mounds up to 19 metres (63 feet) high, ringed by low earthen sacred circles, were raised along the Ohio River by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, that region's first gardeners.","questions":["Timbered vaults inside the mounds of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held shell beads, tubular pipes and bracelets hammered from Great Lakes copper, laid beside the dead in the Ohio valley.","Grave Creek Mound in West Virginia, the largest known conical mound in the western hemisphere, was raised by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between about three thousand and two thousand years ago."],"answer":"Adena culture","answerText":"Adena culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Adena</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 800 BCE – 500 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Type site burial</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 100 BCE – 100 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Adena culture</b> is the name archaeologists give to the mound-building societies of the Ohio River valley during the Early Woodland period, running from roughly 800 BCE to 500 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Adena people still hunted and gathered as their Archaic ancestors had, but they stayed longer in one place, living beside the rivers in shelters of wood covered with mud, clay and grass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were also the region's first gardeners, planting goosefoot, knotweed, sunflower, sumpweed, maygrass, squash and tobacco alongside the nuts and berries they still collected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their pottery grew sturdier and more decorated than anything Archaic potters had made, and it mattered because storing those harvested seeds through the winter is what a garden is for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the culture is remembered for, though, is earth: conical burial mounds up to 19 metres (63 feet) high, often standing inside a sacred circle of low piled walls and a shallow ditch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The dead were laid in timbered vaults within the mounds, accompanied by shell beads, tubular smoking pipes, copper bracelets and sheets of copper worked into antlers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That copper was native metal from the Great Lakes, hammered cold, and it belonged to a tradition of working the stuff that was already thousands of years old when Adena craftsmen took it up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The biggest surviving example is Grave Creek Mound at Moundsville in West Virginia, about 21 metres (70 feet) tall and 274 metres (900 feet) around, the largest known conical mound in the western hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The culture takes its name from the Adena Mound near Chillicothe in Ohio, whose central grave has been dated by the textile and bark buried with it to about the turn of the era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Grave Creek was dug into and its human remains sent to a museum in Boston before Meriwether Lewis ever saw it, and the mounds remain sacred ground to many Indigenous nations today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","north america","united states","prehistory"],"sources":["National Park Service, “Ohio's Prehistoric Past,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/ohio-s-prehistoric-past.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Grave Creek Mound,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/grave-creek-mound.htm. [Open access]","Bradley T. Lepper, Karen L. Leone, Kathryn A. Jakes, et al., “Radiocarbon Dates on Textile and Bark Samples from the Central Grave of the Adena Mound (33RO1), Chillicothe, Ohio,” <i>Midcontinental Journal of Archaeology</i> 39, no. 3 (2014): 201–21, https://doi.org/10.1179/2327427113Y.0000000008. [Paywalled]","Michelle R. Bebber, Briggs Buchanan and Jacob Holland-Lulewicz, “Refining the chronology of North America's copper using traditions: A macroscalar approach via Bayesian modeling,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9041870/. [Open access]","Natalie G. Mueller, “The sleeping crops of eastern North America: a new synthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1926 (2025): 20240192, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12079124/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Grave_Creek_Mound%2C_Moundsville%2C_West_Virginia.jpg/1920px-Grave_Creek_Mound%2C_Moundsville%2C_West_Virginia.jpg","title":"Grave Creek Mound","desc":"The largest surviving Adena mound, at Moundsville in West Virginia, about 21 metres (70 feet) high and 274 metres (900 feet) around.","credit":"Photograph by Shonebrooks, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_Creek_Mound%2C_Moundsville%2C_West_Virginia.jpg","alt":"A large smooth grass-covered conical mound with a few small trees on its summit, rising from mown lawn under a clear blue sky."}},
{"id":"us-015","num":15,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Earthen enclosures laid out as precise circles, squares and octagons across the valleys of central Ohio are the signature of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which flourished from about 1 to 400 CE.","questions":["Mica, obsidian, copper, marine shells and sharks' teeth were amassed through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose contacts reached from the Atlantic coast to the Rocky Mountains.","A claim that a comet airburst had brought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to an end was refuted by astronomers and archaeologists alike, and the paper making it was retracted."],"answer":"Hopewell tradition","answerText":"Hopewell tradition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 – 400 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2023</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Hopewell tradition</b> is the name given to a body of ceremonial, economic and religious practice shared among Native American societies of the eastern woodlands from about 1 to 400 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was never one people or one state: the word describes beliefs, goods and ways of building held in common by groups who lived far apart and need not have spoken the same language.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its signature is earthwork — enclosures of walls and ditches set out in exact circles, squares and octagons, together with burial mounds of many shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The largest of them enclose hundreds of acres in the river valleys of central Ohio, and eight such complexes there are now protected together as the Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The network of contacts that carried the tradition stretched from the Atlantic coast to the Rocky Mountains, and brought in mica, sharks' teeth, obsidian, copper and marine shells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In 2022 a paper proposed that a comet airburst over the Ohio valley had ended the tradition, citing microscopic metallic spherules and iridium in the soil at several earthwork sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The idea travelled quickly through the press and was answered almost as quickly, astronomers finding the cometary case unsubstantiated and archaeologists finding that the dates and the deposits would not carry it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The paper was retracted the following year, though only one of its seven authors agreed to the retraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nothing catastrophic ended the Hopewell tradition, so far as the evidence goes: earthwork building tailed off and the long-distance network unwound over generations, for reasons still argued about.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The episode is a useful warning that a spectacular explanation can outrun its evidence and stay in circulation long after the scholarship has settled against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Hopewell Culture National Historical Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/hocu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Hopewell Culture National Historical Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/hocu/index.htm. [Open access]","Kenneth Barnett Tankersley, Stephen D. Meyers, Stephanie A. Meyers, et al., “The Hopewell airburst event, 1699–1567 years ago (252–383 CE),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 1706, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-05758-y. [Open access; retracted]","Kenneth Barnett Tankersley, Stephen D. Meyers, Stephanie A. Meyers, et al., “Retraction Note: The Hopewell airburst event, 1699–1567 years ago (252–383 CE),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 14201, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-41237-8. [Open access]","Ralph Neuhäuser and Dagmar L. Neuhäuser, “Arguments for a comet as cause of the Hopewell airburst are unsubstantiated,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 12090, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-16211-5. [Open access]","Kevin C. Nolan, Andrew Weiland, Bradley T. Lepper, et al., “Refuting the sensational claim of a Hopewell-ending cosmic airburst,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 12910, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10412550/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Hopewell_culture_nhp_mounds_chillicothe_ohio_2006.jpg/1920px-Hopewell_culture_nhp_mounds_chillicothe_ohio_2006.jpg","title":"Mounds at Mound City Group","desc":"Burial mounds inside the earthen enclosure at Mound City Group near Chillicothe in Ohio, one of the eight Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks.","credit":"Photograph by Rdikeman, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hopewell_culture_nhp_mounds_chillicothe_ohio_2006.jpg","alt":"A low rounded grassy mound standing in a wide mown field, framed by a large tree in the foreground and woodland behind."}},
{"id":"us-016","num":16,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Obsidian from Yellowstone, mica from North Carolina and copper from Lake Superior all reached the Ohio earthworks through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a web of contacts spanning the continent.","questions":["Almost no Hopewell obsidian turns up between Ohio and Wyoming, which suggests that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> moved goods by direct journeys rather than by passing them neighbour to neighbour.","Ceremonial rather than everyday goods travelled along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which was held together by shared belief, with no state, no market and no writing behind it."],"answer":"Hopewell interaction sphere","answerText":"Hopewell interaction sphere","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Network</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 – 400 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Hopewell interaction sphere</b> is the name for the web of contacts that carried raw materials and ideas across eastern and central North America between about 1 and 400 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It reached from the Atlantic coast to the Rocky Mountains, and what it moved — mica, obsidian, copper, marine shells, sharks' teeth — ends up buried in Ohio earthworks a continent away from where it was got.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Obsidian is the extreme case, since Ohio has no volcanoes, and every piece analysed from the Scioto valley matches Obsidian Cliff in Yellowstone or Bear Gulch in Idaho, more than 2,400 kilometres (1,500 miles) off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between Ohio and Wyoming almost no such obsidian is found, which argues that it was fetched by people making the whole journey rather than passed along a chain of neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mica came from the Blue Ridge Mountains of western North Carolina, over 640 kilometres (400 miles) away, and was cut into raptors' talons, human hands and broad discs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Most of the copper came from around Lake Superior, a journey of nearly 1,300 kilometres (800 miles) one way, and was folded, hammered, drilled and rolled with stone and bone tools into earspools, headdresses, axes and falcon effigies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Native copper had been worked in the Great Lakes country for thousands of years before this, so an old craft was being drawn into a new ceremonial system rather than invented for it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The goods were not ordinary trade stock but material for ceremony, and the obsidian spear points are oversized, with many deliberately snapped before they were buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mound 13 at Mound City Group is known as the Mica Mound: a clay basin lined with mica sheets 15 to 40 centimetres (6 to 16 inches) across, on which the cremated remains of four people were laid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No state, no market and no writing organised any of it, which is why the sphere is described as a body of shared practice rather than as a polity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":5,"tags":["concept","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Sarah Hinkelman, “Hopewell Culture Obsidian,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/hopewell-culture-obsidian.htm. [Open access]","Sarah Hinkelman, “Hopewell Culture Mica,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/hopewell-culture-mica.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Hopewell Culture National Historical Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/hocu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Michelle R. Bebber, Briggs Buchanan and Jacob Holland-Lulewicz, “Refining the chronology of North America's copper using traditions: A macroscalar approach via Bayesian modeling,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9041870/. [Open access]","“Hopewell Culture Copper,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/hopewell-culture-copper.htm. [Open access]","Kevin C. Nolan, Andrew Weiland, Bradley T. Lepper, et al., “Refuting the sensational claim of a Hopewell-ending cosmic airburst,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 12910, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10412550/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Human_face_effigy%2C_Hopewell_culture%2C_Turner_Group%2C_Mound_3%2C_altar%2C_Little_Miami_Valley%2C_Ohio%2C_200_BC_to_500_AD%2C_mica_-_Native_American_collection_-_Peabody_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC06093.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"Mica face from the Turner Group","desc":"A human profile cut from a sheet of mica and buried on an altar in the Little Miami valley in Ohio; the mineral was carried from the Blue Ridge Mountains.","credit":"Photograph by Daderot, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Human_face_effigy%2C_Hopewell_culture%2C_Turner_Group%2C_Mound_3%2C_altar%2C_Little_Miami_Valley%2C_Ohio%2C_200_BC_to_500_AD%2C_mica_-_Native_American_collection_-_Peabody_Museum%2C_Harvard_University_-_DSC06093.jpg","alt":"A pale translucent sheet of mica cut into the profile of a human head, mounted against a dark background."}},
{"id":"us-017","num":17,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Flat-topped earthen platform mounds carrying temples and chiefs' houses around an open plaza are the mark of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose maize-farming towns filled the Southeast.","questions":["Cahokia in Illinois and Emerald Mound in Mississippi were both raised by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose towns spread through the river valleys between about 1000 and 1600 CE.","Modelling of the continent's population puts the towns of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at their height around 1150 CE, with roughly a third of that population gone by 1500."],"answer":"Mississippian culture","answerText":"Mississippian culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 1600 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Population peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Mississippian culture</b> is the name for the maize-farming, town-building societies that spread through the Mississippi valley and the southeastern United States from about 1000 CE onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its towns are laid out around flat-topped earthen platform mounds, which carried temples and the houses of chiefs and stood facing an open plaza.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mounds were raised basket-load by basket-load: soil loosened with digging sticks or bare hands, carried back, tipped out and stamped down, over and over for years on end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Maize grown on rich floodplain soils is what made settlements of this size possible, and the shift to farming is visible in the skeletons of the people who made it as much as in their fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cahokia, on the Illinois side of the Mississippi opposite modern St Louis, is the pre-eminent example, and its central complex of mounds, houses and farms covered more than 4,000 acres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The second largest of the platform mounds is Emerald Mound near Natchez in Mississippi, about 11 metres (35 feet) high on a base measuring 235 by 133 metres (770 by 435 feet) and in use from 1200 to 1600 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Modelling of radiocarbon dates from across the continent puts the Indigenous population of North America at its greatest around 1150 CE, and roughly 30 per cent lower by 1500.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the upper Mississippi watershed the peak comes earlier still, near 1107, and the decline that follows is driven above all by the emptying of Cahokia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A severe and prolonged drought in the middle of the continent, among the worst in more than a thousand years, overlaps the period when the great towns were abandoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That the system was already contracting long before any European reached the interior is one of the firmest results of this work, and it separates what colonisation destroyed from what had changed already.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","archaeology","agriculture","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site: World Heritage Site,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/cahokia-mounds-state-historic-site-world-heritage-site.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Emerald Mound, Milepost 10.3,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/emerald-mound.htm. [Open access]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]","David P. Pompeani, Broxton W. Bird, Jeremy J. Wilson, et al., “Severe Little Ice Age drought in the midcontinental United States during the Mississippian abandonment of Cahokia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 13829, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8257696/. [Open access]","George R. Milner and Jesper L. Boldsen, “Population trends and the transition to agriculture: Global processes as seen from North America,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2209478119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9942849/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Emerald_Mound_%28399314509%29.jpg/1920px-Emerald_Mound_%28399314509%29.jpg","title":"Emerald Mound","desc":"The second largest Mississippian platform mound, near Natchez in Mississippi, with one of its two summit mounds visible on top.","credit":"Photograph by Natalie Maynor, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emerald_Mound_%28399314509%29.jpg","alt":"A broad flat-topped grassy earthwork with a smaller rounded mound on its summit, seen from below against a deep blue sky."}},
{"id":"us-018","num":18,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"With perhaps twenty thousand people inside its palisade and twice that across the bottomlands, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the largest city north of Mexico before European contact.","questions":["Beakers found at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carry the chemical signature of black drink, brewed from a holly that grows no nearer than five hundred kilometres (300 miles) away.","A post circle set to the movements of the sun, more than a hundred mounds and a rebuilt palisade all stood at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the Illinois floodplain."],"answer":"Cahokia","answerText":"Cahokia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flourished</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 1350 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1050 – 1150 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">World Heritage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1982</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Cahokia</b> was the largest city anywhere north of Mexico before European contact, standing on the Mississippi floodplain in what is now southwestern Illinois, across the river from modern St Louis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It flourished between about 1000 and 1350 CE, and its complex of mounds, houses and farmland covered more than 4,000 acres of the bottomland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Estimates put 10,000 to 20,000 people in Cahokia itself and perhaps 40,000 to 50,000 in the wider settlement, which matched the size of many European cities of the same date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At least 120 earthen mounds were raised across the site, 51 of them inside the ground now protected, some serving as foundations for buildings and some as burial places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A palisade of upright posts about 2.8 kilometres (1.7 miles) long enclosed the central precinct, and it was taken down and rebuilt at least four times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> West of the centre stood a great circle of posts, between 73 and 146 metres (240 and 480 feet) across in its successive versions, aligned on the movements of the sun through the year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One mound alone, known as Mound 72, held the remains of more than three hundred people, and what was done there is among the most discussed evidence for rank and violence in the ancient Midwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pottery beakers from the city, dating between 1050 and 1250, carry theobromine, caffeine and ursolic acid together, the chemical signature of black drink brewed from a holly that grows no nearer than 500 kilometres (300 miles) away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A bald cypress post at the nearby Mitchell site, standing at least 18 metres (60 feet) tall, was felled around 1124 and brought from woodland well over 180 kilometres (110 miles) off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The site was inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 1982, and modelling of the continent's population shows the city's rise and fall driving the demographic curve of the whole upper Mississippi watershed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["city","place","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site: World Heritage Site,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/cahokia-mounds-state-historic-site-world-heritage-site.htm. [Open access]","“Periodic Report on the Application of the World Heritage Convention: Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/internationalcooperation/upload/WHSPeriodicRpt-CahokiaMoundsStateHistoricSite.doc. [Open access]","Patricia L. Crown, Thomas E. Emerson, Jiyan Gu, et al., “Ritual Black Drink consumption at Cahokia,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 109, no. 35 (2012): 13944–49, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3435207/. [Open access]","Nicholas V. Kessler, Erin M. Benson, Timothy R. Pauketat, et al., “Age and origin of a Cahokian wooden monument at the Mitchell site, Illinois, USA,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 20, no. 10 (2025): e0333783, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12494245/. [Open access]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Cahokia_Woodhenge_at_dawn.png/1920px-Cahokia_Woodhenge_at_dawn.png","title":"Woodhenge at Cahokia","desc":"The reconstructed circle of posts west of the centre of Cahokia, set to the movements of the sun through the year.","credit":"Photograph by Julia King, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cahokia_Woodhenge_at_dawn.png","alt":"A line of tall bare wooden posts standing in mist over flat grassland at dawn, fading into the haze."}},
{"id":"us-019","num":19,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Rising thirty metres (100 feet) in four terraces and covering six hectares (15 acres) at its base, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the largest earthen structure in the Americas north of Mexico.","questions":["Fourteen successive stages of basket-carried earth, raised without draught animals, wheels or metal tools, made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the dominating structure of the city of Cahokia.","Slumping on its flanks has been excavated both to stabilise <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and to read the internal layering of the greatest mound ever raised north of Mexico."],"answer":"Monks Mound","answerText":"Monks Mound","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cahokia</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1000 – 1350 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Construction</span><span class=\"dt-v\">in 14 stages</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Monks Mound</b> is the largest earthen structure ever raised in the Americas north of Mexico, standing at the centre of the city of Cahokia on the Mississippi floodplain in southwestern Illinois.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was built in fourteen stages, one laid over the last, and now rises about 30 metres (100 feet) in four terraces, covering six hectares (15 acres) at its base.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mounds at Cahokia carried public buildings on their flat summits, and this one looked down on the plaza, the palisade and the whole of the settlement around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every basket of that earth was dug and carried by hand, since the people who built it had no draught animals, no wheels and no metal tools.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The labour it represents is the plainest surviving measure of a city whose influence is reckoned to have reached across some three million square kilometres (1.25 million square miles) of the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Modern slumping on its flanks has been a serious problem, and excavation of the second-terrace slump has served both to stabilise the mound and to read the layers inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A long-standing account held that Cahokia's builders felled so much timber that the uplands eroded, the floodplain flooded, and the city undid itself by its own success.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Testing of the buried ground surface beneath the mounds found that horizon stable right through the occupation and only disturbed by industrial-era development, which does not support that story.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sediment cores from a floodplain lake beside the site tell a related tale: the Mississippi produced no large floods between about 600 and 1200 CE, and big floods returned afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cahokia thus grew during an unusually calm river regime and emptied as that calm ended, which is a claim about the setting rather than a verdict on why the people left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["building","place","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site: World Heritage Site,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/cahokia-mounds-state-historic-site-world-heritage-site.htm. [Open access]","“Periodic Report on the Application of the World Heritage Convention: Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/internationalcooperation/upload/WHSPeriodicRpt-CahokiaMoundsStateHistoricSite.doc. [Open access]","Caitlin G. Rankin, Casey R. Barrier and Timothy J. Horsley, “Evaluating narratives of ecocide with the stratigraphic record at Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site, Illinois, USA,” <i>Geoarchaeology</i> 36, no. 3 (2021): 369–87, https://doi.org/10.1002/gea.21848. [Paywalled]","Samuel E. Munoz, Kristine E. Gruley, Ashtin Massie, et al., “Cahokia's emergence and decline coincided with shifts of flood frequency on the Mississippi River,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 20 (2015): 6319–24, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4443319/. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Emerald Mound, Milepost 10.3,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/emerald-mound.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Monks_Mound_-_Summer_2024.jpg/1920px-Monks_Mound_-_Summer_2024.jpg","title":"Monks Mound","desc":"The largest earthen structure in the Americas north of Mexico, rising in four terraces at the centre of Cahokia in Illinois.","credit":"Photograph by Skubasteve834, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monks_Mound_-_Summer_2024.jpg","alt":"A vast flat-topped grassy mound with a long stairway up its front, rising from level meadow under a clear sky."}},
{"id":"us-020","num":20,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Faecal stanols in lake sediment record tens of thousands of people leaving the American Bottom, a process called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, finished long before Europeans reached the valley.","questions":["Flooding, drought and self-inflicted deforestation have each been offered to explain the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only the last of the three has been clearly ruled out.","Archaeologists call the emptied stretch of the middle Mississippi the Vacant Quarter, a landscape left behind by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and by the fall of the towns around it."],"answer":"abandonment of Cahokia","answerText":"abandonment of Cahokia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decline begins</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Abandoned</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1350 – 1400 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>abandonment of Cahokia</b> is the emptying, across the 13th and 14th centuries, of the largest city that ever stood north of Mexico before European contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Faecal stanols preserved in the sediment of a floodplain lake track the number of people living in the bottomland, and they show a peak around 1100 followed by a long decline to a minimum near 1400.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Modelling of radiocarbon dates from across the continent puts the whole upper Mississippi watershed at its most populous close to 1107, with the emptying of this one city driving the fall that follows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The countryside went with it, leaving what archaeologists call the Vacant Quarter, a broad stretch of the middle Mississippi with no towns left standing in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No single cause is agreed on, and the candidates put forward have been environmental, political and religious by turns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> One proposal reads the river: sediment cores show large Mississippi floods returning after about 1200, after six centuries without them, and the city's decline keeps pace with their return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That was answered at once, on the grounds that correlation is not causation, that the flood's date rests on a thin statistical midpoint, and that no flood deposit has been found in any house basin or storage pit on the site.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second proposal is drought, since lake records show a severe and prolonged dry spell between roughly 1200 and 1400, among the worst in more than a thousand years, overlapping the final departure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A third, that the city stripped its own uplands of timber and brought erosion and flooding on itself, has been tested against the buried soils and is not supported by them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives every version is the shape of the thing: people left gradually, over generations, and the mounds had been standing empty for a long time before any European saw them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","archaeology","north america","united states","history","climate"],"sources":["Samuel E. Munoz, Kristine E. Gruley, Ashtin Massie, et al., “Cahokia's emergence and decline coincided with shifts of flood frequency on the Mississippi River,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 20 (2015): 6319–24, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4443319/. [Open access]","Sarah E. Baires, Melissa R. Baltus and Meghan E. Buchanan, “Correlation does not equal causation: Questioning the Great Cahokia Flood,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 112, no. 29 (2015): E3753, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4517203/. [Open access]","A. J. White, Lora R. Stevens, Varenka Lorenzi, et al., “Fecal stanols show simultaneous flooding and seasonal precipitation change correlate with Cahokia's population decline,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 12 (2019): 5461–66, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6431169/. [Open access]","David P. Pompeani, Broxton W. Bird, Jeremy J. Wilson, et al., “Severe Little Ice Age drought in the midcontinental United States during the Mississippian abandonment of Cahokia,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 11 (2021): 13829, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8257696/. [Open access]","Caitlin G. Rankin, Casey R. Barrier and Timothy J. Horsley, “Evaluating narratives of ecocide with the stratigraphic record at Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site, Illinois, USA,” <i>Geoarchaeology</i> 36, no. 3 (2021): 369–87, https://doi.org/10.1002/gea.21848. [Paywalled]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Cahokia_in_2010.JPG/1920px-Cahokia_in_2010.JPG","title":"The bottomland from Monks Mound","desc":"Grassland and scattered mounds seen from the summit of Monks Mound, with the towers of St Louis on the far horizon.","credit":"Photograph by Caroline, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cahokia_in_2010.JPG","alt":"A wide green landscape of mown grass, trees and low mounds seen from above, with a distant city skyline on the horizon."}},
{"id":"us-021","num":21,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"A group of 29 mounds set around a filled and levelled plaza above the Black Warrior River in Alabama makes up <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a town founded about 1120 CE.","questions":["Copper axes too soft to cut anything, buried with a few adults in the northernmost mounds at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, are read as marks of office rather than as weapons.","By the time Hernando de Soto's expedition crossed Alabama in 1540, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had been emptying for two centuries and served chiefly as a burial ground."],"answer":"Moundville","answerText":"Moundville","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1120 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Mounds disused</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 – 1450 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Moundville</b> is a Mississippian town site on the Black Warrior River in west-central Alabama, about 21 kilometres (13 miles) south of Tuscaloosa, founded around 1120 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It belongs to the same world as Cahokia, some 700 kilometres (430 miles) to the north, and was raised while that city stood at its greatest extent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The site was a planned community: its plaza was artificially filled and levelled, and 29 mounds were placed deliberately around it, ranging from about one to 17 metres (3 to 57 feet) high.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each was raised basket-load by basket-load from soil dug in nearby pits, which are small lakes today, and built up in stages over many decades; a log palisade and earthwork went up early in the site's life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1300 it was the largest town in what is now Alabama, with perhaps a thousand people living behind the walls and more on farmsteads scattered up and down the river.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The arrangement of the mounds is itself the evidence for rank: residential mounds alternate with burial mounds around the plaza, and the largest, holding the richest graves, stand along its northern side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most people were buried beside their own houses with everyday tools, while those laid in the mounds went into the ground with objects of stone, copper and marine shell fetched from a very long way off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several adults in the northernmost burial mounds had copper axes found nowhere else on the site, too soft to serve as tools or weapons and so read as marks of office.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between 1300 and 1450 the mounds gradually fell out of use and most of the population left for smaller single-mound settlements along the river, after which the place was chiefly somewhere to bury the dead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1540, when Hernando de Soto's expedition crossed the region, it probably held few inhabitants — the local shape of a decline that modelling of dates across the continent puts well before any European arrived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["place","city","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["John H. Blitz, “Moundville Archaeological Park,” <i>Encyclopedia of Alabama</i>, Auburn University Outreach and the Alabama Humanities Alliance, last updated 16 September 2025, https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/moundville-archaeological-park/. [Open access]","University of Alabama Museums, “About Moundville,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://moundville.museums.ua.edu/about/. [Open access]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]","George R. Milner and Jesper L. Boldsen, “Population trends and the transition to agriculture: Global processes as seen from North America,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2209478119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9942849/. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site: World Heritage Site,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/cahokia-mounds-state-historic-site-world-heritage-site.htm. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Moundville_Archaeological_Park%2C_Moundville%2C_Alabama_LCCN2010637712.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-Moundville_Archaeological_Park%2C_Moundville%2C_Alabama_LCCN2010637712.tif.jpg","title":"A mound at Moundville","desc":"A platform mound at Moundville seen across one of the flooded borrow pits from which the earth to build it was dug.","credit":"Photograph by Carol M. Highsmith, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moundville_Archaeological_Park%2C_Moundville%2C_Alabama_LCCN2010637712.tif","alt":"A low flat-topped grassy mound at the edge of a wide winter field, seen beyond a shallow reed-filled pond."}},
{"id":"us-022","num":22,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"The farming peoples of the Colorado Plateau who built the great houses of Chaco and the cliff dwellings of Mesa Verde are the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, ancestors of today's Pueblo nations.","questions":["Ancient DNA from Picuris Pueblo, in a study the Pueblo itself began, links its people to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> buried at Pueblo Bonito, a thousand years and 275 kilometres (170 miles) away.","Pithouse villages of about two thousand years ago gave way among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to above-ground masonry houses, farmed streambeds and contacts reaching right across the Colorado Plateau."],"answer":"Ancestral Puebloans","answerText":"Ancestral Puebloans","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Farming from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Four Corners left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1300 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Ancestral Puebloans</b> are the farming peoples of the Colorado Plateau, the Four Corners country where Utah, Colorado, Arizona and New Mexico meet, and their descendants are the Pueblo nations of the Southwest today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The earliest people of that country hunted and gathered and moved with the seasons; about two thousand years ago their descendants began growing maize and living in villages of semi-underground pithouses, a stage archaeologists call Basketmaker.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Out of those farmers the Ancestral Pueblo culture emerged as people came to depend on their fields for most of their food, building above-ground masonry houses and farming the canyon streambeds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their settlements run from single-family roomblocks to the monumental great houses of Chaco Canyon and the cliff dwellings of Mesa Verde, and their contacts reached right across the plateau.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Living rooms are known by their hearths and their bins for grinding maize, granaries by their narrow doorways, and kivas were set aside for communal and ceremonial use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> These were not societies of equals: differences in house size across the northern Southwest show wealth persistently unevenly held from the late 800s to the late 1200s, and sharpest where villages lasted longest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ancient DNA from Picuris Pueblo in New Mexico, in a study the Pueblo itself initiated and controlled, shows genetic continuity across the last thousand years and with the people buried at Pueblo Bonito in Chaco Canyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is direct evidence for what Pueblo people have always said about these places, and against the old idea of a vanished race with no living heirs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Over the 1200s the Four Corners emptied: people shifted away from the old ways, reorganised their world and moved on, the cliff dwellers of Mesa Verde among the last to go south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their descendants are the Hopi, the Zuni and the pueblos of the Rio Grande, for whom the old sites remain living parts of a family history rather than ruins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","culture","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Navajo National Monument,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/nava/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Chaco Culture National Historical Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/chcu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“Cliff Dwellings: Mesa Verde National Park,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/meve/learn/historyculture/cliff_dwellings_home.htm. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler, Darcy Bird, R. Kyle Bocinsky, et al., “Wealth inequality in the prehispanic northern US Southwest: from Malthus to Tyche,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 378, no. 1883 (2023): 20220298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10291424/. [Open access]","Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–32, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Betatakin_Ruin_Navajo_National_Monument.jpg/1920px-Betatakin_Ruin_Navajo_National_Monument.jpg","title":"Betatakin","desc":"An Ancestral Puebloan village of the 13th century set in a sandstone alcove at Navajo National Monument in northern Arizona.","credit":"Photograph by AhwatukeeBauer, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Betatakin_Ruin_Navajo_National_Monument.jpg","alt":"A huge arched alcove in a pale sandstone cliff, with small stone rooms clustered along its floor above a wooded canyon."}},
{"id":"us-023","num":23,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Between the mid-800s and about 1150 CE <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in northwestern New Mexico was the ceremonial and economic centre of a world spanning the San Juan Basin.","questions":["Long winters, a short growing season and marginal rainfall make <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> an unlikely site for monumental stone architecture, which is exactly what its people raised there.","Roads ran out from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to more than 150 great houses across the region, and after 1000 CE its roof beams came from mountains fifty miles off."],"answer":"Chaco Canyon","answerText":"Chaco Canyon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Flowering</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 850 – 1150 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Regional centre</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by 1050 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Chaco Canyon</b>, in northwestern New Mexico, was the centre of an Ancestral Puebloan world that reached across the San Juan Basin from the mid-800s for more than three hundred years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is an unlikely place for one, with long winters, a short growing season and rainfall barely sufficient for maize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its builders raised massive multi-storey stone buildings of hundreds of rooms, planned from the outset rather than added to as need arose, in a masonry unlike anything attempted before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Many were set to solar, lunar and cardinal directions, with lines of sight running between them, and astronomical markers, water-control devices and formal earthen mounds standing around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1050 the canyon had become the ceremonial, administrative and economic centre of the basin, its buildings linked by roads to more than 150 great houses across the region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Pueblo Bonito, the largest and the most thoroughly investigated, was planned and built in stages between 850 and 1150 CE out of quarried sandstone, wooden beams and mud mortar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How many people actually lived there is disputed, with estimates for the height of the building period running between about 2,100 and 2,700, and the great houses may have been public architecture used at intervals for ceremony and trade rather than ordinary villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The canyon's own woodland paid part of the cost: juniper, the dominant tree, was severely reduced by the time the Chacoans left, and after 1000 CE the big roof beams came from ponderosa, Douglas fir and spruce in the Chuska mountains instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Differences in house size across the northern Southwest show wealth persistently unevenly held through these centuries, so whatever Chaco was, it was not a gathering of equals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the 1100s and 1200s building slowed and the centre of gravity moved north to Aztec and Mesa Verde, and Pueblo people today count the canyon a stop on their clans' own migration paths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["place","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Chaco Culture National Historical Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/chcu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“Pueblo Bonito,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/pueblo-bonito.htm. [Open access]","David L. Lentz, Venicia Slotten, Nicholas P. Dunning, et al., “Ecosystem impacts by the Ancestral Puebloans of Chaco Canyon, New Mexico, USA,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0258369, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8550600/. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler, Darcy Bird, R. Kyle Bocinsky, et al., “Wealth inequality in the prehispanic northern US Southwest: from Malthus to Tyche,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 378, no. 1883 (2023): 20220298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10291424/. [Open access]","Henry L. Short, “The deterioration of the Pueblo Bonito Great House in the Chaco Culture National Historical Park, New Mexico, USA,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266099, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8982885/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Pueblo-Bonito-from-far-away.png/1920px-Pueblo-Bonito-from-far-away.png","title":"Pueblo Bonito in its canyon","desc":"The largest of the Chacoan great houses seen along the canyon floor, with the sandstone cliff that stands behind it.","credit":"Photograph by JacobCarr06, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pueblo-Bonito-from-far-away.png","alt":"A long line of low ruined stone walls on scrubby desert flats, with a tall sandstone cliff to the right under heavy cloud."}},
{"id":"us-024","num":24,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"A multi-storey masonry block of hundreds of rooms, planned entire before a stone was laid, is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the building form of Chacoan society in New Mexico.","questions":["Roads ran from Chaco Canyon to more than 150 outlying examples of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> across the San Juan Basin, so the form was never confined to one place.","Because the local pinyon and juniper are too small and crooked to carry a roof, beams for each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came after 1000 CE from mountain forests far away."],"answer":"great house","answerText":"great house","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Built</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 850 – 1150 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>great house</b> is the monumental building form of Chacoan society: a multi-storey masonry block containing hundreds of rooms, far larger than anything its builders had put up before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An ordinary village grows a room at a time as families need them, but a great house was planned from the start, and work on some of them ran across decades and even centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every one is different, yet all share features that make them recognisable as Chacoan wherever they stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pueblo Bonito, Una Vida and Peñasco Blanco went up during the middle and late 800s, and Hungo Pavi, Chetro Ketl and Pueblo Alto followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pueblo Bonito, the largest of them, was built in stages between 850 and 1150 CE out of quarried sandstone, wooden beams and mud mortar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> They were oriented to solar, lunar and cardinal directions, with lines of sight running between them and astronomical markers, water-control works and formal earthen mounds set about them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Roads connected the canyon's own buildings to more than 150 more across the San Juan Basin, so this was a regional form rather than a local one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They may not have been houses in the ordinary sense at all: one reading makes them public architecture, occupied at intervals for ceremony, commerce and gathering, with a resident population far smaller than the room count suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The timber alone was an undertaking, since the local pinyon and juniper have trunks too small and too crooked to carry a roof, and after 1000 CE the main beams came from ponderosa, Douglas fir and spruce growing in the Chuska mountains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Building on this scale went on in societies where wealth was already unevenly held, which is part of what the form records in the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["building","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["“History &amp; Culture: Chaco Culture National Historical Park,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/chcu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“Pueblo Bonito,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/pueblo-bonito.htm. [Open access]","Henry L. Short, “The deterioration of the Pueblo Bonito Great House in the Chaco Culture National Historical Park, New Mexico, USA,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 4 (2022): e0266099, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8982885/. [Open access]","David L. Lentz, Venicia Slotten, Nicholas P. Dunning, et al., “Ecosystem impacts by the Ancestral Puebloans of Chaco Canyon, New Mexico, USA,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 16, no. 10 (2021): e0258369, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8550600/. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler, Darcy Bird, R. Kyle Bocinsky, et al., “Wealth inequality in the prehispanic northern US Southwest: from Malthus to Tyche,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 378, no. 1883 (2023): 20220298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10291424/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Pueblo_Bonito%2C_Chaco_Canyon_New_Mexico.JPG/1920px-Pueblo_Bonito%2C_Chaco_Canyon_New_Mexico.JPG","title":"Chacoan masonry at Pueblo Bonito","desc":"A standing wall of a great house, laid in the thin coursed sandstone that makes Chacoan building recognisable wherever it is found.","credit":"Photograph by Rationalobserver, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pueblo_Bonito%2C_Chaco_Canyon_New_Mexico.JPG","alt":"A tall wall of finely coursed thin sandstone slabs, with square doorways at two levels, standing beneath a cliff."}},
{"id":"us-025","num":25,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Some of the largest and best-preserved cliff dwellings in North America stand in alcoves at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in southwestern Colorado, built between about 1180 and 1300 CE.","questions":["Nearly every dwelling at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits beside a seep spring, which is the best answer archaeologists have to why anyone would build inside a cliff.","Balcony House, with 38 rooms and two kivas for something over thirty people, is a middle-sized dwelling at <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where most hold eight to ten rooms."],"answer":"Mesa Verde","answerText":"Mesa Verde","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cliff dwellings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1180 – 1300 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">National park</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1906</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mesa Verde</b>, in southwestern Colorado, holds some of the largest and best-preserved cliff dwellings in North America, built by Ancestral Pueblo people into natural alcoves in the canyon walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most went up between 1180 and 1300 CE, which makes them among the last structures raised anywhere in the region before it was left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They look like castles in photographs and are mostly nothing of the kind: the average holds eight to ten rooms, and only a few are large enough for public gatherings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Living rooms are known by their hearths and by the bins where maize was ground, narrow spaces were built as granaries for dried food, and kivas were set aside for communal and ceremonial use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Balcony House is a middle-sized example with 38 rooms and two kivas, home to something over thirty people, its balconies reaching the upper rooms by ladder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Why build inside a cliff at all is the question visitors ask, and the answer archaeologists give is water: nearly all of these dwellings stand beside seep springs, among the only permanent water on the mesa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Wood samples record extended droughts through the period, the reservoirs on the mesa tops were becoming unreliable, and people moved their homes to where the water was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were lived in for barely a hundred years, at the end of four centuries in which wealth in these villages had been unevenly and persistently held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The park preserves nearly 5,000 ancestral sites and was established in 1906, the first national park anywhere set aside for cultural heritage rather than scenery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Over the 1200s the whole Four Corners emptied, and the mitochondrial DNA of the turkeys people kept supports a genuine migration southward, whose descendants are the Pueblo nations of New Mexico and Arizona today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","building","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “Cliff Dwellings: Mesa Verde National Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/meve/learn/historyculture/cliff_dwellings_home.htm. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture: Mesa Verde National Park,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/meve/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Brian M. Kemp, Kathleen Judd, Cara Monroe, et al., “Prehistoric mitochondrial DNA of domesticate animals supports a 13th century exodus from the northern US southwest,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0178882, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5528258/. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler, Darcy Bird, R. Kyle Bocinsky, et al., “Wealth inequality in the prehispanic northern US Southwest: from Malthus to Tyche,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 378, no. 1883 (2023): 20220298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10291424/. [Open access]","Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–32, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Cliff_Palace-Colorado-Mesa_Verde_NP.jpg/1920px-Cliff_Palace-Colorado-Mesa_Verde_NP.jpg","title":"Cliff Palace","desc":"The largest cliff dwelling at Mesa Verde, built into an alcove beneath an overhanging ledge; the visitors on the path give its scale.","credit":"Photograph by Tobi 87, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cliff_Palace-Colorado-Mesa_Verde_NP.jpg","alt":"A dense cluster of stone rooms and round kivas filling a wide rock alcove under a great overhang, with a group of people on the path below."}},
{"id":"us-026","num":26,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Over the course of the 1200s the Four Corners country emptied of farmers altogether, an episode called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which ended with people settled far to the south.","questions":["Mitochondrial DNA from turkeys and dogs, animals that travelled with their owners, is what turned the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from an argument about pottery into a testable claim.","Drought, shrinking farmland and a tightening of village life have all been proposed as causes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and no single explanation has settled the matter."],"answer":"Ancestral Puebloan migrations","answerText":"Ancestral Puebloan migrations","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Four Corners left</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1200 – 1300 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Ancestral Puebloan migrations</b> are the movement, over the 13th century, of every farming community out of the Four Corners region of the Southwest, leaving a country that had been settled for a thousand years entirely empty of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is among the most studied episodes in North American archaeology, partly because the scale of it is so hard to explain and partly because the destination was long uncertain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difficulty is that pottery and building styles in the Four Corners differ sharply from those of the places the migrants are supposed to have gone, which made the obvious kind of evidence useless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Human remains cannot be sampled freely, and rightly so, which closed the most direct route to an answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The way round was to test the animals: turkeys and dogs travelled with their owners, and their mitochondrial DNA can be compared between the region people left and the regions they are thought to have reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That comparison supports a real exodus southward rather than a collapse in place, and it puts numbers on a movement previously argued from potsherds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Why it happened is less settled, and the candidates overlap: a long drought recorded in the tree rings, reservoirs on the mesa tops becoming unreliable, and springs in the canyon walls becoming the only dependable water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A second line of argument is social rather than climatic, since wealth in these villages had grown persistently uneven and the unoccupied land a family could leave for was shrinking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Comparative work on transformations of this kind finds that they are usually experienced as hard by the people living through them, whatever the eventual outcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The end of it is not in doubt: genomes from Picuris Pueblo in New Mexico link its people to those buried at Pueblo Bonito, so the migrants arrived and their descendants are there still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","migration","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Brian M. Kemp, Kathleen Judd, Cara Monroe, et al., “Prehistoric mitochondrial DNA of domesticate animals supports a 13th century exodus from the northern US southwest,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0178882, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5528258/. [Open access]","“Cliff Dwellings: Mesa Verde National Park,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/meve/learn/historyculture/cliff_dwellings_home.htm. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler, Darcy Bird, R. Kyle Bocinsky, et al., “Wealth inequality in the prehispanic northern US Southwest: from Malthus to Tyche,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 378, no. 1883 (2023): 20220298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10291424/. [Open access]","Michelle Hegmon, Matthew A. Peeples and the LTVTP-NABO collaboration, “The human experience of social transformation: Insights from comparative archaeology,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 11 (2018): e0208060, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6264852/. [Open access]","Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–32, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Hovenweep_Castle_Structure.jpg/1920px-Hovenweep_Castle_Structure.jpg","title":"Hovenweep Castle","desc":"A stone tower and roomblock left standing on the canyon rim at Hovenweep, in country the farming communities left over the 1200s.","credit":"Photograph by TravelingGamer, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hovenweep_Castle_Structure.jpg","alt":"A tall curved tower of orange sandstone blocks beside lower ruined walls, standing alone in open sagebrush country."}},
{"id":"us-027","num":27,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Red-on-buff pottery, hundreds of miles of canals and more than two hundred ball courts mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> culture of the Sonoran Desert in southern Arizona.","questions":["The word <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an archaeologists' label rather than anyone's name for themselves, and appears to be a mishearing of an O'odham word meaning ancestors.","Turquoise and shell from the Pacific, and copper bells and parrots from Mexico, reached <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> villages in exchange for surplus crops, shell jewellery and pottery."],"answer":"Hohokam","answerText":"Hohokam","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Distinct by</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 450 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Classic period</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1100 – 1450 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Hohokam</b> is the archaeologists' name for the irrigation-farming culture of the Sonoran Desert in what is now southern and central Arizona, recognisable from about 450 CE by its red-on-buff pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a name the descendants do not use: there is no such word in O'odham, Hopi or Zuni, and it seems to be a mishearing of the O'odham 'huhugam', meaning ancestors, so the National Park Service writes instead of the ancestral Sonoran Desert people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They descended from desert foragers, and as the country grew hotter and drier and maize arrived from Mesoamerica they took up farming, diverting mountain runoff and river water onto their fields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Canals were being dug by about 1500 BCE, and hundreds of miles of them have been traced in the Gila, Salt and Santa Cruz valleys.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reconstructing the middle Gila systems hydraulically shows that the labour of running a network rises with its size, so extending one carried a management cost the technology of the day could not meet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The fields carried maize, several kinds of bean, squash, cotton and tobacco, beside cactus fruit, agave hearts and mesquite beans gathered from the desert itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Modelling maize yields against reconstructed river flow shows how narrow the margin was, since a run of dry years in the catchment reaches straight through to the harvest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Trade brought turquoise, obsidian and sea shell from the Gulf of California, and copper bells, pyrite mirrors and parrots from Mexico, paid for with surplus crops, shell jewellery and pottery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than two hundred oval, earthen-sided ball courts have been found in the larger villages, an idea that may have come north from Mesoamerica; from the 1100s they fell out of use and flat-topped platform mounds were raised instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Casa Grande, a great house of puddled adobe built in the 1300s, belongs to that later phase, and in the late 1300s and early 1400s the whole system came apart, in the centuries when the continent's population was falling from its peak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["culture","agriculture","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “The Ancestral Sonoran Desert People,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/cagr/learn/historyculture/the-ancestral-sonoran-desert-people.htm. [Open access]","Zhu Tianduowa, Kyle C. Woodson and Maurits W. Ertsen, “Reconstructing Ancient Hohokam Irrigation Systems in the Middle Gila River Valley, Arizona, United States of America,” <i>Human Ecology</i> 46, no. 5 (2018): 735–46, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6182576/. [Open access]","Tianduowa Zhu, M. W. Ertsen and N. C. van der Giesen, “Long term effects of climate on human adaptation in the middle Gila River Valley, Arizona, America,” <i>Water History</i> 7 (2015): 511–31, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4811293/. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture: Casa Grande Ruins National Monument,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/cagr/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/2021_Casa_Grande_Ruins_1.jpg/1920px-2021_Casa_Grande_Ruins_1.jpg","title":"The Casa Grande","desc":"The four-storey adobe great house at Casa Grande Ruins in Arizona, standing behind the walls of its compound under the steel canopy built to shelter it.","credit":"Photograph by Beyond My Ken, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2021_Casa_Grande_Ruins_1.jpg","alt":"A weathered pale adobe building of several storeys with dark window openings, seen across the low earthen walls of a walled compound."}},
{"id":"us-028","num":28,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"The farming peoples of the mountains and high valleys of southwestern New Mexico and southeastern Arizona are called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by archaeologists, after a nearby range.","questions":["Tree rings put the Gila Cliff Dwellings, built by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> group, at roughly thirty years of use from the 1270s, during a drought that lasted three decades.","Nobody knows what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> people called themselves, since they kept their history by word of mouth and the label comes from an 18th-century Spanish governor."],"answer":"Mogollon","answerText":"Mogollon","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Gila dwellings</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1276 – 1300s CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Drought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1270 – 1300 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Mogollon</b> is the archaeologists' name for the farming peoples of the mountains and high valleys of southwestern New Mexico, southeastern Arizona and northern Mexico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name is borrowed twice over, from the Mogollon range in the Gila Wilderness, which was itself named after Don Juan Ignacio Flores Mogollón, governor of Santa Fe de Nuevo México from 1712 to 1715.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What these people called themselves is not known and probably never will be, since they kept their history by word of mouth rather than in writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their best-known site is the Gila Cliff Dwellings, built into south-facing caves above a stream that runs all year, in a canyon of yucca, piñon, oak and prickly pear with mule deer, elk and bison to hunt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The caves had been in use long before them, soot on their roofs recording thousands of years of occupation and atlatl fragments marking Archaic hunters camping there on their seasonal rounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tree rings date the dwellings closely, with the earliest cutting date at 1276 CE and the whole occupation lasting roughly thirty years, from the 1270s into the early 1300s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those same rings are narrow, the signature of the Great Drought of 1270 to 1300, which is the best answer to why the builders came at all, having moved about 65 kilometres (40 miles) from near present-day Reserve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they left includes corn cobs, pottery, yucca sandals and the feathers of turkeys, owls, roadrunners and a scarlet macaw carried up from far to the south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They did not vanish when they left, but spread out into other pueblos across southwestern New Mexico and into Mexico, in a movement much like the one emptying the Colorado Plateau at that same moment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their country lay between the Ancestral Puebloan plateau to the north and the irrigated Sonoran lowlands to the west, and comparative work finds transformations of this kind hard to live through whatever their outcome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["culture","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “The Mogollon,” Gila Cliff Dwellings National Monument, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/gicl/learn/historyculture/the-mogollon.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “The Ancestral Sonoran Desert People,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/cagr/learn/historyculture/the-ancestral-sonoran-desert-people.htm. [Open access]","Brian M. Kemp, Kathleen Judd, Cara Monroe, et al., “Prehistoric mitochondrial DNA of domesticate animals supports a 13th century exodus from the northern US southwest,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0178882, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5528258/. [Open access]","Michelle Hegmon, Matthew A. Peeples and the LTVTP-NABO collaboration, “The human experience of social transformation: Insights from comparative archaeology,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 11 (2018): e0208060, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6264852/. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler, Darcy Bird, R. Kyle Bocinsky, et al., “Wealth inequality in the prehispanic northern US Southwest: from Malthus to Tyche,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 378, no. 1883 (2023): 20220298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10291424/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Gila_Cliff_Dwellings_National_Monument.jpg/1920px-Gila_Cliff_Dwellings_National_Monument.jpg","title":"The Gila Cliff Dwellings","desc":"Rooms built into south-facing caves above a year-round stream, occupied for about thirty years from the 1270s.","credit":"Photograph by Gorfic, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gila_Cliff_Dwellings_National_Monument.jpg","alt":"Three large caves in a banded cliff face, one holding a small stone building, above a canyon of pines and scrub."}},
{"id":"us-029","num":29,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Hopi, Zuni, Acoma, Taos, Picuris and Ohkay Owingeh are among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, who live in permanent villages of masonry and adobe in New Mexico and Arizona.","questions":["Chaco Culture National Historical Park names 21 of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among the nations traditionally associated with its landscapes, from Acoma and Cochiti to Zia and Zuni.","Genomes from Picuris show the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> descend without a break from those buried at Pueblo Bonito, and give no sign of decline before Europeans arrived."],"answer":"Pueblo peoples","answerText":"Pueblo peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Villages from</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 700 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spanish contact</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1541 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Pueblo peoples</b> are the Native nations of New Mexico and Arizona who live in permanent villages of masonry and adobe, among them Hopi, Zuni, Acoma, Laguna, Taos, Picuris, Jemez, Santa Clara and Ohkay Owingeh.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The list is long and specific: Chaco Culture National Historical Park names 21 of them among the peoples traditionally associated with its landscapes, from Acoma and Cochiti through to Zia and Zuni, and Ysleta del Sur in Texas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their villages continue the Ancestral Puebloan settlements without a break, and Pecos in the upper Pecos valley was farmed and traded from for more than a thousand years by people whose descendants live nearby today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomes from Picuris Pueblo, in a study the Pueblo itself initiated and controlled, show continuity across the last thousand years and with the people buried at Pueblo Bonito in Chaco Canyon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same study finds no sign of population decline before Europeans arrived, which cuts against a long-standing assumption about the Southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Farming is the constant underneath all of it, since maize came into the region about two thousand years ago and the move from foraging to fields is what produced the settled village in the first place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> These were never uniform societies: house sizes across the northern Southwest record persistent differences in wealth from the late 800s to the late 1200s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Spanish colonists reached Pecos with the Coronado expedition in 1541, and after that nothing in the Pueblo world was quite the same again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each nation is a sovereign government today, and several have taken an active part in deciding how their own past is investigated and described.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The old sites are not ruins to the people whose ancestors built them, but stops along their clans' own migration paths, to be honoured and respected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["people","place","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–32, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Chaco Culture National Historical Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/chcu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture: Pecos National Historical Park,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/peco/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Navajo National Monument,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/nava/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Timothy A. Kohler, Darcy Bird, R. Kyle Bocinsky, et al., “Wealth inequality in the prehispanic northern US Southwest: from Malthus to Tyche,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 378, no. 1883 (2023): 20220298, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10291424/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Detail%2C_Taos_Pueblo_NM.jpg/1920px-Detail%2C_Taos_Pueblo_NM.jpg","title":"Taos Pueblo","desc":"Adobe roomblocks with ladders reaching the upper storeys at Taos Pueblo in northern New Mexico, lived in continuously for centuries.","credit":"Photograph by John Phelan, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail%2C_Taos_Pueblo_NM.jpg","alt":"Stacked flat-roofed adobe rooms in warm earth colours, with wooden doors, small windows and ladders leaning to the upper levels."}},
{"id":"us-030","num":30,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Ancestors of the Diné and the Apache nations reached the Southwest from the far northwest of the continent in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose date is still argued over.","questions":["Navajo and Apache are southern outliers of a language family otherwise spoken in interior Alaska and northwestern Canada, which is what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was for.","Ancient genomes from Picuris Pueblo carry no Athabascan ancestry before 1500 CE, which tells against the earlier datings proposed for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into the Southwest."],"answer":"Athabaskan migration","answerText":"Athabaskan migration","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In the Southwest</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 1500 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Athabaskan migration</b> is the movement that brought the ancestors of the Diné, or Navajo, and of the Apache nations from the far northwest of the continent into the American Southwest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their languages belong to the Athabaskan family, whose other members are spoken in interior Alaska and northwestern Canada, so these are the southern outliers of a decidedly northern group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That family is in turn argued to be related to Yeniseian in central Siberia, and phylogenetic work on the two supports a back-migration out of Beringia into Asia rather than a late crossing from Asia into America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genomic work on Native American and Arctic populations likewise treats Athabascan ancestry as a strand of its own, with its own relationships to Arctic and Siberian groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the southern Athabaskans actually arrived is contested, and it is one of the few questions in the region where language, genetics and archaeology can all be brought to bear on a single date.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The sharpest recent evidence comes from Picuris Pueblo, where a study of ancient and living genomes found no Athabascan ancestry in anyone who lived before 1500 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That does not fix the arrival by itself, since it speaks to one valley, but it tells against the earlier hypotheses, which put the newcomers in the region well before the Spanish.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the time written records begin, the Diné were settled between their four sacred mountains, farming and running livestock in canyons the Pueblo farmers had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Canyon de Chelly shows the sequence plainly: Ancestral Pueblo people left it in the mid-1300s, Hopi families came in to plant maize and peach orchards, and Diné families live and farm there now.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chaco Canyon is central to several Navajo clans and ceremonies, and the Jicarilla and Mescalero Apache nations are among the peoples that park names as traditionally associated with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["event","migration","people","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Mark A. Sicoli and Gary Holton, “Linguistic Phylogenies Support Back-Migration from Beringia to Asia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 9, no. 3 (2014): e91722, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3951421/. [Open access]","Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–32, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]","National Park Service, “History &amp; Culture: Canyon de Chelly National Monument,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/cach/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture: Chaco Culture National Historical Park,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/chcu/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Svend V. Nielsen, Andrew H. Vaughn, Kalle Leppälä, et al., “Bayesian inference of admixture graphs on Native American and Arctic populations,” <i>PLOS Genetics</i> 19, no. 2 (2023): e1010410, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9956672/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Hogan_reconstructions%2C_Monument_Valley_Navajo_Tribal_Park.jpg/1920px-Hogan_reconstructions%2C_Monument_Valley_Navajo_Tribal_Park.jpg","title":"Hogans in Monument Valley","desc":"Reconstructed hogans, the traditional Diné dwelling, on the Navajo Nation in the country between the four sacred mountains.","credit":"Photograph by MPSharwood, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hogan_reconstructions%2C_Monument_Valley_Navajo_Tribal_Park.jpg","alt":"Two low domed earth-covered dwellings with timber doorframes standing on red desert ground, with a flat-topped butte beyond."}},
{"id":"us-031","num":31,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Cedar canoes, clan-owned fishing grounds and salmon dried by the tonne for winter belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the peoples of the wet forested shore from Alaska to Washington.","questions":["Ancient DNA from herring bones and thousands of years of salmon remains show the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> harvesting the same runs, in the same places, without exhausting them.","Wealth on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came from the sea rather than from fields, which is why hierarchy and monumental art appeared there without any farming at all."],"answer":"Northwest Coast","answerText":"Northwest Coast","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fisheries</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5,000 years and more</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Russians at Sitka</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1799 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Northwest Coast</b> is the belt of wet, forested shore and islands running from southeastern Alaska down through British Columbia to Washington, and the peoples of it — Tlingit, Haida, Tsimshian, Kwakwaka'wakw, Nuu-chah-nulth, Coast Salish and others — built one of the richest societies on the continent without planting a field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sea did the work of agriculture: cod, halibut and herring were taken with spears and hooks, and salmon running up the shallow rivers in summer were trapped, dried and stored against the winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Canoes up to 18 metres (60 feet) long were carved from single cedar trunks, and each clan held its own fishing grounds, where trespass was grounds for war or compensation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade routes ran along the coast and inland to Athabaskan neighbours, exchanging dried fish, otter furs and Chilkat robes for caribou hides, fox furs, jade and copper.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Winter, with its long darkness, was the ceremonial season, and the great gathering of that season was the potlatch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The archaeology shows the fishery managed rather than merely exploited: at one Coast Salish site the salmon harvest continues for thousands of years without the collapse in fish size or age that overfishing leaves behind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Ancient DNA from herring bones across several centuries shows people drawing on runs that spawned at different times, which spreads the harvest and cushions a bad year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fish bones from middens can even be read as a thermometer, and five thousand years of them record the warming of the catch itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beaches were terraced into sea gardens that raised clam yields for millennia, until commercial fisheries and the law took them from the families who tended them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Russian traders reached Tlingit country in 1799 and Sitka became the capital of Russian America, but the coast's peoples, their clans and their ceremonies are still there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["people","place","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “The Tlingit,” Sitka National Historical Park, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/sitk/learn/historyculture/the-tlingit.htm. [Open access]","Meaghan Efford, Spencer Taft, Jesse Morin, et al., “Archaeology demonstrates sustainable ancestral Coast Salish salmon stewardship over thousands of years,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 8 (2023): e0289797, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10456131/. [Open access]","Eleni L. Petrou, Robert Kopperl, Dana Lepofsky, et al., “Ancient DNA reveals phenological diversity of Coast Salish herring harvests over multiple centuries,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 12 (2022): 13512, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9357025/. [Open access]","Dylan Hillis, Robert Gustas, Daniel Pauly, et al., “A palaeothermometer of ancient Indigenous fisheries reveals increases in mean temperature of the catch over five millennia,” <i>Environmental Biology of Fishes</i> 105, no. 10 (2022): 1381–97, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9592643/. [Open access]","Patrick Hayes, Skye Augustine, Marco Hatch, et al., “Ecological legacy of Indigenous dispossession: disruption of ancestral sea gardens by commercial clam fisheries in British Columbia, 1882–1985,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1930 (2025): 20240277, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12242291/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Unknown_artist_-_Ceremonial_Garment_%28Naaxein%29_%28Chilkat_Blanket%29_-_1981.69_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.jpg/1920px-Unknown_artist_-_Ceremonial_Garment_%28Naaxein%29_%28Chilkat_Blanket%29_-_1981.69_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.jpg","title":"A Chilkat robe","desc":"A ceremonial robe woven from mountain-goat wool and shredded cedar bark, in the formline style of the Northwest Coast.","credit":"Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unknown_artist_-_Ceremonial_Garment_%28Naaxein%29_%28Chilkat_Blanket%29_-_1981.69_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.jpg","alt":"A wide woven robe with a long pale fringe, patterned in black, yellow and white with symmetrical faces and eye forms."}},
{"id":"us-032","num":32,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Names, marriages, titles and the dead are all confirmed by giving wealth away before invited witnesses at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the central institution of the Northwest Coast.","questions":["Canada outlawed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from 1885 to 1951, arresting people for making speeches, dancing or handing out gifts, and seizing the masks used in it.","Because a host who gives away everything gains rank rather than losing it, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was long misread by outsiders as waste rather than as a system of record."],"answer":"potlatch","answerText":"potlatch","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Banned in Canada</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1885 – 1951</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cranmer potlatch</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1921 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"A <b>potlatch</b> is the ceremony at the centre of Northwest Coast society, in which a host invites guests, feeds and houses them for days, and gives away his wealth before them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The word comes from Chinook Jargon, the trade language of the coast, and the giving is not extravagance but procedure: the guests are witnesses, and what they witness becomes law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Names are conferred, marriages made, chiefly titles and hereditary rights transferred, the dead mourned, poles raised and houses opened, each act validated by the people who saw it and were paid to remember.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a society with no writing, this is how a claim is recorded, and the more given the higher the standing of the giver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among the Tlingit it was the great ceremony of the winter, and a host could end it impoverished and greatly raised in rank.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Outsiders read the giving as waste, and worse, as an obstacle to what they called civilisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Canada proclaimed a ban in 1883 that became law on 1 January 1885, making it a misdemeanour to take part in the festival or the associated dances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The prohibition stood for more than sixty years, and after a large potlatch given in 1921 some 45 people were charged, twenty of them sent to prison and hundreds of pieces of regalia surrendered under duress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> People held it in secret and some kept the songs deliberately so that the ceremony could be resumed, and the ban belongs to a wider use of law against Indigenous practice and property on that coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The law was dropped in 1951; a national museum agreed in the 1970s to return its share of the confiscated regalia on condition a building be raised to hold it, and the U'mista Cultural Centre was built for that purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","people","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["U'mista Cultural Society, “Potlatch,” <i>Living Tradition: The Kwakwaka'wakw Potlatch on the Northwest Coast</i>, Virtual Museum of Canada, accessed 6 September 2026, https://umistapotlatch.ca/potlatch-eng.php. [Open access]","“The Tlingit,” National Park Service, Sitka National Historical Park, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/sitk/learn/historyculture/the-tlingit.htm. [Open access]","U'mista Cultural Society, “Potlatch Ban,” <i>Living Tradition: The Kwakwaka'wakw Potlatch on the Northwest Coast</i>, Virtual Museum of Canada, accessed 6 September 2026, https://umistapotlatch.ca/potlatch_interdire-potlatch_ban-eng.php. [Open access]","Patrick Hayes, Skye Augustine, Marco Hatch, et al., “Ecological legacy of Indigenous dispossession: disruption of ancestral sea gardens by commercial clam fisheries in British Columbia, 1882–1985,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 380, no. 1930 (2025): 20240277, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12242291/. [Open access]","“Our Masks Come Home,” <i>Living Tradition: The Kwakwaka'wakw Potlatch on the Northwest Coast</i>, U'mista Cultural Society and the Virtual Museum of Canada, accessed 6 September 2026, https://umistapotlatch.ca/nos_masques_come_home-our_masks_come_home-eng.php. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Kwakiutl_ceremonial_group_VPL_14044.jpg/1920px-Kwakiutl_ceremonial_group_VPL_14044.jpg","title":"A Kwakwaka'wakw dance party at Quatsino","desc":"Dancers in blankets outside a village on northern Vancouver Island; the photographer noted that the host had returned from sealing and was about to hold a potlatch.","credit":"Ben W. Leeson, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kwakiutl_ceremonial_group_VPL_14044.jpg","alt":"A group of people in blankets standing on open ground in front of plank houses and frame buildings beside the water."}},
{"id":"us-033","num":33,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Acorns leached of their tannins, plank canoes of redwood driftwood and country kept open by burning belong to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before contact, where nobody planted a field.","questions":["Pollen, charcoal and tree biomass show that burning by the peoples of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held a mixed conifer forest steady for a thousand years, which climate alone cannot explain.","California supported a high cultural and linguistic diversity of Indigenous peoples, and the genomes of ancient and living <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> communities show a distinctive ancestry held for two thousand years."],"answer":"California","answerText":"California","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Arlington Springs</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 13,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Cabrillo</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1542 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"Before contact the peoples of <b>California</b> fed themselves without planting a field, on acorns, fish, game and a landscape they kept by burning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The acorn was the staple, and it is not a food that can simply be picked: it must be gathered in bulk, cracked, ground and leached of its bitter tannins, and the labour of that fell largely to women, who carried infants while they worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the northern Channel Islands the sea supplied the rest, and the Island Chumash crossed to the mainland in plank canoes called tomols, built from redwood driftwood carried down the coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those islands hold some of the oldest dated human remains on the continent, found at Arlington Springs on Santa Rosa Island and more than 13,000 years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shell and stone were worked into tools and into goods that travelled along the coast and inland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Calling this landscape wild is the commonest mistake made about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A record of pollen, charcoal and tree biomass from the Klamath Mountains shows the structure and composition of a mixed conifer forest holding steady for at least a thousand years, with Native burning alongside lightning fires holding it there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Climate alone cannot account for the forest Europeans found, which means the country they described as untouched was one that had been deliberately kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Tree-ring fire histories record the same practice elsewhere in the West, where generations of Western Apache burning show up in the scars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Depth of time is the other thing the evidence keeps showing: genomes of ancient and present-day Ohlone people from the San Francisco Bay, studied in partnership with the tribe, share a distinctive ancestry across some two thousand years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","people","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “People: Channel Islands National Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/chis/learn/historyculture/people.htm. [Open access]","Alexandra Niclou, Alexandra Greenwald and Cara Ocobock, “Energetic Value of Women's Work: Assessing Maternal Energetic Costs From Acorn Foraging,” <i>American Journal of Biological Anthropology</i> 186, no. 3 (2025): e70023, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11923397/. [Open access]","Clarke A. Knight, Lysanna Anderson, M. Jane Bunting, et al., “Land management explains major trends in forest structure and composition over the last millennium in California's Klamath Mountains,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 12 (2022): e2116264119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8944927/. [Open access]","Christopher I. Roos, J. Mark Kaib, Nicholas C. Laluk, et al., “Tree rings reveal persistent Western Apache (Ndee) fire stewardship and niche construction in the American Southwest,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 32 (2025): e2509169122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12358881/. [Open access]","Alissa L. Severson, Brian F. Byrd, Elizabeth K. Mallott, et al., “Ancient and modern genomics of the Ohlone Indigenous population of California,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 13 (2022): e2111533119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9060455/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/CINMS_-_Chumash_Tomol_%2826919549120%29.jpg/1920px-CINMS_-_Chumash_Tomol_%2826919549120%29.jpg","title":"A Chumash tomol","desc":"A plank canoe under paddle in the Santa Barbara Channel, the craft that carried people and goods between the mainland and the northern Channel Islands.","credit":"National Marine Sanctuaries, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CINMS_-_Chumash_Tomol_%2826919549120%29.jpg","alt":"A narrow wooden plank canoe with several paddlers on calm water under a low sun."}},
{"id":"us-034","num":34,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Brush shelters called wikiups, pinyon nuts, basketry and a country of dry ranges and salt flats belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, there for over 12,000 years.","questions":["Chewed and spat-out fibres of agave and yucca, preserved for millennia in dry caves, still carry the mitochondrial DNA of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> who chewed them.","The Fremont grew maize and squash in small villages before the ancestral Shoshone among the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> arrived about seven hundred years ago, and their descendants remain."],"answer":"Great Basin peoples","answerText":"Great Basin peoples","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Occupied</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 12,000 years</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fremont</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1500 – 700 years ago</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Great Basin peoples</b> live in the dry interior between the Sierra Nevada and the Rockies, a country of parallel ranges and closed valleys whose rivers reach no sea, and it has been occupied for more than 12,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The earliest groups hunted large animals, among them bison and the mammoth and ground sloth that did not outlast the Pleistocene, with big points lashed to spears.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From about 9,000 years ago the Desert Archaic followed, hunting mule deer and antelope, gathering wild onions, wild rye and pinyon nuts, grinding seeds on stone and weaving baskets, mats, hats and sandals from plant fibre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Marine shell beads in their sites show trade reaching the Pacific coast, and continental modelling of radiocarbon dates puts populations across North America rising to a peak around 1150 CE.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Between roughly 1,500 and 700 years ago the Fremont planted maize and squash, built small villages and made a black-on-grey pottery, while still hunting and gathering.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The ancestral Shoshone came in around 700 years ago and their descendants live there still, having moved through the country in brush shelters called wikiups, following game and ripening plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dryness that makes the region hard to live in is what makes its archaeology extraordinary, since fibre, hide and plant remains survive in its caves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Thousands of quids — wads of agave or yucca pulp roasted, chewed and spat out — were dug from one Nevada rockshelter in the 1960s and stored unstudied for fifty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They still hold the chewers' own mitochondrial DNA, so an object made in a moment and thrown away can be dated and its maker's ancestry read from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two of its caves have yielded some of the largest collections of Late Pleistocene perishable technology anywhere, showing how much of that early toolkit was made of things that normally rot, and even the petroglyphs pecked into varnished rock can now be roughly aged from how much varnish has grown back over the cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","place","archaeology","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “People: Great Basin National Park,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/grba/learn/historyculture/people.htm. [Open access]","Scott D. Hamilton-Brehm, Lidia T. Hristova, Susan R. Edwards, et al., “Ancient human mitochondrial DNA and radiocarbon analysis of archived quids from the Mule Spring Rockshelter, Nevada, USA,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 3 (2018): e0194223, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5844571/. [Open access]","Meinrat O. Andreae and Tracey W. Andreae, “Archaeometric studies on rock art at four sites in the northeastern Great Basin of North America,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 17, no. 1 (2022): e0263189, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8791535/. [Open access]","Richard L. Rosencrance, Geoffrey M. Smith, Katelyn N. McDonough, et al., “Complex perishable technologies from the North American Great Basin reveal specialized Late Pleistocene adaptations,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 6 (2026): eaec2916, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12871465/. [Open access]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Basin_and_Range_National_Monument_%2848938948182%29.jpg/1920px-Basin_and_Range_National_Monument_%2848938948182%29.jpg","title":"Petroglyphs in the Basin and Range","desc":"Figures pecked through the dark varnish of a desert rock face in eastern Nevada.","credit":"Matthew Dillon, CC BY 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basin_and_Range_National_Monument_%2848938948182%29.jpg","alt":"Pale abstract and animal figures pecked into a dark brown rock surface."}},
{"id":"us-035","num":35,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Hunting bison on foot, by driving herds over cliffs or into timber pounds, is how people lived on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> for thousands of years before horses.","questions":["A young man in a bison hide would call like a distressed calf to draw a herd towards the drop, a technique of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> yielding tonnes of meat.","Earthlodge villages of the Mandan, Hidatsa and Arikara farmed the Missouri bottoms on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while still hunting bison, and women owned and built the lodges."],"answer":"Great Plains","answerText":"Great Plains","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peopled</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 11,000 years ago</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Earthlodges</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 500 years ago</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"For thousands of years before horses reached them, the peoples of the <b>Great Plains</b> hunted bison on foot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bison themselves are relative newcomers, recolonising the northern grassland after the last glacial maximum, and mitochondrial genomes from dated remains track their numbers rising and falling with climate and with people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a jump, a young man wearing a bison hide worked his way close to a herd and called like a distressed calf to draw it after him, while runners dressed as wolves came up behind and fanned into a V, waving blankets to keep the panicked animals between them until the herd went over a cliff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decoy's place was an honour and went to the fastest runner, and fifty animals over the edge yielded five to nine tonnes of meat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where no cliff was to hand the same skills drove the herd into a timber pound, a corral three to five metres (10 to 15 feet) high with a long chute into it, where the animals were killed with bows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Not everyone on the Plains was mobile: along the upper Missouri the Mandan, Hidatsa and Arikara lived in permanent earthlodge villages, farming the bottomlands and hunting bison from them, in a region peopled for perhaps 11,000 years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The earthlodge was a circular house of posts, split logs, willow, grass and thick sod, nine to eighteen metres (30 to 60 feet) across; Hidatsa women owned them, built them under an older woman who held the knowledge, and rebuilt them about every ten years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ten to twenty people lived in one, usually sisters and their families, around a central fire, with a cache pit sunk in the floor for stored food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those villages drew traders from across the northern Plains long before any European came, and were cosmopolitan places when Lewis and Clark wintered nearby in 1804.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The taste for very large game is old here: on present evidence the earliest hunters of the Americas look like megaherbivore specialists rather than generalists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"tags":["place","people","practice","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “Bison Bellows: Indigenous Hunting Practices,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/bison-bellows-3-31-16.htm. [Open access]","“People: Knife River Indian Villages National Historic Site,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/knri/learn/historyculture/people.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Earthlodge,” Knife River Indian Villages National Historic Site, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/knri/learn/historyculture/earthlodge.htm. [Open access]","Igor V. Ovchinnikov and Blake McCann, “Mitogenomes revealed the history of bison colonization of Northern Plains after the Last Glacial Maximum,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 11097, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10349043/. [Open access]","Ben A. Potter, James C. Chatters, Luciano Prates, et al., “Hemisphere-wide evidence of Early Paleoindian megaherbivore specialization,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 27 (2026): eaef9628, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13322243/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Head-Smashed-In_Buffalo_Jump.jpg/1920px-Head-Smashed-In_Buffalo_Jump.jpg","title":"Head-Smashed-In Buffalo Jump","desc":"The sandstone cliff in southern Alberta over which bison were driven, used for thousands of years.","credit":"Thomas Fuhrmann, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head-Smashed-In_Buffalo_Jump.jpg","alt":"A low sandstone cliff running across dry grassland under a wide sky."}},
{"id":"us-036","num":36,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Five nations — Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca — joined under one council and one longhouse to form the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in what is now New York.","questions":["Fifty chiefs, each raised and held to account by a clan mother, sit on the council of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which took in the Tuscarora as a sixth nation in the 18th century.","Radiocarbon has pulled the village sequence behind the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> decades away from where trade goods had placed it, which changes when its towns grew large."],"answer":"Haudenosaunee Confederacy","answerText":"Haudenosaunee Confederacy","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Villages coalesce</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1350 – 1650 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elective council</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Haudenosaunee Confederacy</b> is the union of the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca nations of what is now upstate New York, joined later by the Tuscarora, who take the longhouse as the symbol of their unity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its own account holds that it has stood since time immemorial, founded when the Peacemaker and Aiionwatha carried the laws of peace from community to community until the chiefs of each nation agreed to join.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifty chiefs sit on its council — nine Mohawk, nine Oneida, fourteen Onondaga, ten Cayuga and eight Seneca — each presented by the clan mothers of his nation and each keeping his own nation's council as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clan mother who presents a chief also keeps him to his moral obligations, and in a matrilineal society the clans through which she does it are led by women.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Membership is by clan rather than by village, and the clans cross the national boundaries, which binds the five together at the level of the family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Archaeology comes at the same history from the other side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between about 1350 and 1650 the villages of what is now southern Ontario and New York grew larger and fewer, and the decoration on their pottery shifts in ways that read as signals of a widening community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fifteenth-century villages at the eastern end of Lake Ontario sat where they could broker between populations to the north and the east, and network analysis of their material shows them doing exactly that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A total of 86 radiocarbon measurements have since moved parts of that sequence decades from where European trade goods had placed it, so the chronology everyone had been using was wrong.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The Confederacy governs today and is still recognised by the Six Nations, alongside the elective system imposed in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","people","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Haudenosaunee Confederacy, “Confederacy's Creation,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.haudenosauneeconfederacy.com/confederacys-creation/. [Open access]","Haudenosaunee Confederacy, “Clan System,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.haudenosauneeconfederacy.com/clan-system/. [Open access]","John P. Hart, Termeh Shafie, Jennifer Birch, et al., “Nation Building and Social Signaling in Southern Ontario: A.D. 1350–1650,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 5 (2016): e0156178, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880188/. [Open access]","John P. Hart, Susan Winchell-Sweeney and Jennifer Birch, “An analysis of network brokerage and geographic location in fifteenth-century AD Northern Iroquoia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 1 (2019): e0209689, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6326549/. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Jennifer Birch, Megan A. Conger, et al., “Radiocarbon re-dating of contact-era Iroquoian history in northeastern North America,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaav0280, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6281431/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/GanondaganRecreatedLonghouse.jpg/1920px-GanondaganRecreatedLonghouse.jpg","title":"Inside a Seneca longhouse","desc":"A recreated longhouse at Ganondagan in western New York, with sleeping platforms along both walls and a line of hearths down the centre.","credit":"DanielPenfield, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GanondaganRecreatedLonghouse.jpg","alt":"The bark-roofed interior of a long building, light falling through smoke holes onto a central hearth between raised platforms."}},
{"id":"us-037","num":37,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"The constitution the Peacemaker is said to have carried from nation to nation, uniting five peoples under one council, is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Haudenosaunee.","questions":["A tree planted at Onondaga with white roots running to the four directions, and weapons buried beneath it, are images from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, recited from wampum.","Bound arrows, an eagle set to watch for danger and fifty antlered chiefs beneath one tree are the symbols in which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was given and is remembered."],"answer":"Great Law of Peace","answerText":"Great Law of Peace","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Towns coalesce</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1350 – 1650 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Elective council</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1924 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"The <b>Great Law of Peace</b> is the constitution of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, and in the Confederacy's own account it was brought by the Peacemaker, sent by the Creator to spread the good mind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With Aiionwatha, commonly called Hiawatha, he travelled from community to community until the chiefs of the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca agreed to join the Great League of Peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The clan mothers of each nation presented their chiefs, and the Peacemaker set deer antlers on each head as the sign of his authority.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Onondaga he planted the Great Tree of Peace, told the chiefs of the council to sit in its shade and keep the fire, and sent its white roots out to the four directions so that any nation willing to keep the law could follow one back and be welcomed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An eagle was set above the tree to watch for danger, weapons were buried beneath it, and an arrow taken from each of the five nations was bound into one bundle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Each law was tied to a string of wampum and its meaning declared as the string was given, which is how a constitution is held and passed on without writing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The provisions are practical as well as symbolic: fifty named chieftainships are distributed among the nations, each title held by a clan and conferred by its clan mother, who also keeps its holder to his obligations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because clans cross the national boundaries, a member of one nation counts as kin in another, which is the family-level binding underneath the political one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Archaeology cannot date a law, but it can date the towns: villages in the region grew larger and fewer between about 1350 and 1650, some of them placed to broker between neighbours, and radiocarbon has since shifted parts of that sequence decades from where trade goods had put it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Confederacy still governs by the Great Law, alongside the elective system established in 1924.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["institution","concept","people","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Haudenosaunee Confederacy, “Confederacy's Creation,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.haudenosauneeconfederacy.com/confederacys-creation/. [Open access]","Haudenosaunee Confederacy, “Clan System,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.haudenosauneeconfederacy.com/clan-system/. [Open access]","John P. Hart, Termeh Shafie, Jennifer Birch, et al., “Nation Building and Social Signaling in Southern Ontario: A.D. 1350–1650,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 5 (2016): e0156178, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880188/. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Jennifer Birch, Megan A. Conger, et al., “Radiocarbon re-dating of contact-era Iroquoian history in northeastern North America,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaav0280, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6281431/. [Open access]","John P. Hart, Susan Winchell-Sweeney and Jennifer Birch, “An analysis of network brokerage and geographic location in fifteenth-century AD Northern Iroquoia,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 14, no. 1 (2019): e0209689, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6326549/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Hiawatha_Wampum_Belt_%281909%29_%2814779431751%29.jpg/1920px-Hiawatha_Wampum_Belt_%281909%29_%2814779431751%29.jpg","title":"The Hiawatha belt","desc":"Four squares and a central emblem worked in white wampum on a purple ground, standing for the nations joined under the Great Law.","credit":"Internet Archive Book Images, no known restrictions. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hiawatha_Wampum_Belt_%281909%29_%2814779431751%29.jpg","alt":"A long beaded belt showing four rectangles and a central tree-like figure in white against a dark ground."}},
{"id":"us-038","num":38,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"From the Wabanaki of Maine to the Powhatan of the Chesapeake, the peoples of the Atlantic woodlands mostly spoke languages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> family.","questions":["Birchbark canoes, seasonal camps by the shore and the first towns the English met all belong to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> peoples of the eastern woodlands.","The English settled at Jamestown in 1607 on the hunting grounds of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> people, and relations with them were mixed from the first season onwards."],"answer":"Algonquian","answerText":"Algonquian","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Champlain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1604 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Jamestown</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1607 CE</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"<b>Algonquian</b> is the language family spoken across most of the Atlantic woodlands of North America when Europeans arrived, from the Wabanaki of Maine and the Maritimes south through southern New England to the Powhatan of the Chesapeake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a language family and not a nation, a unit defined by shared structure, in a hemisphere that is home to nearly half the world’s language families and a very wide range of structural types.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In Maine the four nations known together as the Wabanaki, the People of the Dawnland, are the Maliseet, Mi'kmaq, Passamaquoddy and Penobscot, and they have been in that country since time immemorial.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They travelled overland and in seaworthy birchbark canoes, camping near places such as Somes Sound to hunt, fish, gather berries, harvest clams and trade with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was Wabanaki guides who gave Samuel de Champlain the name Pemetic, the range of mountains, for the island he reached in 1604.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Farther south the same family met the English first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1607 a hundred and four English men and boys founded Jamestown on a site chosen for its deep water and its defensibility, which was Powhatan hunting land, and relations were mixed from the beginning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Algonquian nations of the coast were the ones colonisation reached first, and their neighbours inland were mostly Iroquoian speakers, whose villages were growing larger and fewer over the same centuries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Radiocarbon dating has since moved parts of that northeastern sequence decades away from where European trade goods had placed it, which matters for both families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Wabanaki people met attempts at displacement with resistance and are still in Maine, working with the national park on whose ancestral homeland it sits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"tags":["people","concept","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["National Park Service, “Wabanaki Nations,” Acadia National Park, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/acad/learn/historyculture/wabanaki.htm. [Open access]","“A Short History of Jamestown,” National Park Service, Historic Jamestowne, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/jame/learn/historyculture/a-short-history-of-jamestown.htm. [Open access]","John P. Hart, Termeh Shafie, Jennifer Birch, et al., “Nation Building and Social Signaling in Southern Ontario: A.D. 1350–1650,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 11, no. 5 (2016): e0156178, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880188/. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Jennifer Birch, Megan A. Conger, et al., “Radiocarbon re-dating of contact-era Iroquoian history in northeastern North America,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaav0280, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6281431/. [Open access]","Johanna Nichols, “Founder effects identify languages of the earliest Americans,” <i>American Journal of Biological Anthropology</i> 186, no. 1 (2025): e24923, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11775432/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Wampanoag_Wigwam_Plimoth_Patuxet.jpg/1920px-Wampanoag_Wigwam_Plimoth_Patuxet.jpg","title":"Inside a wetu","desc":"Woven mats, hides and a raised sleeping platform in a reconstructed Wampanoag house in Massachusetts.","credit":"EgorovaSvetlana, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wampanoag_Wigwam_Plimoth_Patuxet.jpg","alt":"The inside of a domed house walled with woven reed mats, with furs spread over a low wooden platform."}},
{"id":"us-039","num":39,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Published figures for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of North America have ranged from a few million to a hundred million, and choosing among them is a political act.","questions":["Summing dated archaeological sites and modelling them continent-wide is one route to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of North America, and it puts the maximum around 1150 CE.","Because so much land went out of cultivation after 1492, carbon drawn down by returning forest gives an independent handle on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the Americas."],"answer":"pre-contact population","answerText":"pre-contact population","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Continental peak</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1150 CE</span><span class=\"dt-k\">By 1500</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 30% lower</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"How many people lived north of Mexico before Europeans came is one of the hardest questions in American history, and the <b>pre-contact population</b> of the continent has been put at figures differing by an order of magnitude.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difficulty is that there was no census, that the epidemics travelled ahead of the people who recorded them, and that any estimate is also an argument about how much was lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One route works from the ground up, summing radiocarbon dates from archaeological sites as a proxy for how many people were leaving traces, then modelling the result across the continent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That approach puts the Indigenous population of North America at its greatest around 1150 CE and roughly thirty per cent lower by 1500, which means a decline was already under way before contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also localises the fall: the upper Mississippi watershed peaks near 1107, driven by the rise and abandonment of one city.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A second route reads the skeletons, since the shift to farming leaves its mark on growth, disease and death, and lets population change be read from cemeteries rather than from counts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A third works backwards from the land: so much ground went out of cultivation after 1492 that regrowing vegetation drew down carbon, and the size of that drawdown constrains how many farmers there had been.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each method has its own bias, and none of them counts people directly, so the honest form of the answer is a range with its assumptions stated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What all of them now agree on is that the continent was populous, that its numbers moved for reasons of its own before 1492, and that what followed was catastrophic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Genomic work at individual pueblos has begun to test these curves community by community, and at Picuris found no decline at all before Europeans arrived, while animal DNA has shown that some apparent disappearances were migrations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","event","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]","George R. Milner and Jesper L. Boldsen, “Population trends and the transition to agriculture: Global processes as seen from North America,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 4 (2023): e2209478119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9942849/. [Open access]","Alexander Koch, Chris Brierley, Mark M. Maslin, et al., “Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492,” <i>Quaternary Science Reviews</i> 207 (2019): 13–36, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.quascirev.2018.12.004. [Open access]","Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–32, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]","Brian M. Kemp, Kathleen Judd, Cara Monroe, et al., “Prehistoric mitochondrial DNA of domesticate animals supports a 13th century exodus from the northern US southwest,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0178882, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5528258/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/George_Catlin_-_Bird%27s-eye_View_of_the_Mandan_Village%2C_1800_Miles_above_St._Louis_-_1985.66.502_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg","title":"A Mandan village on the upper Missouri","desc":"George Catlin painted the earthlodges packed inside their palisade after visiting the Mandan on the upper Missouri in 1832.","credit":"George Catlin, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Catlin_-_Bird%27s-eye_View_of_the_Mandan_Village%2C_1800_Miles_above_St._Louis_-_1985.66.502_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg","alt":"A painted view over dozens of domed earth-covered lodges crowded together, with people on their roofs and open plains beyond."}},
{"id":"us-040","num":40,"category":"Native America before contact","question":"Radiocarbon, tree rings, ancient DNA and the oral histories of descendant nations are the four strands from which <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is reconstructed, each checking the others.","questions":["A set of 86 radiocarbon dates moved a whole village sequence decades from where trade goods had put it, which is a fair summary of how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> keeps being revised.","When a pueblo commissions a study of its own ancestors' genomes and keeps control of the data, how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is investigated has changed as much as what is known."],"answer":"pre-contact America","answerText":"pre-contact America","answerDate":"","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","abstract":"Almost everything known about <b>pre-contact America</b> comes from four kinds of evidence, and the interest lies in how often they correct one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Radiocarbon is the backbone, and it is not a fixed backbone: 86 measurements from Iroquoian villages shifted parts of a well-established sequence decades from where the presence of European trade goods had placed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That matters because those goods had been used as a clock, so a chronology built on them was measuring the wrong thing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Summed across thousands of sites, the same dates become a population curve, which is how the continent's numbers before 1492 are now estimated at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tree rings supply the other clock, dating a roof beam to the year it was cut and recording the droughts and the deliberate burning that shaped the country around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Ancient DNA is the newest strand and the most delicate, because it is read from people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The most instructive recent example is a study begun by Picuris Pueblo itself, which kept control of the data and used genomes to answer questions its own traditional knowledge left open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where sampling human remains is not appropriate, the animals people kept can stand in, and the mitochondrial DNA of turkeys and dogs has been used to test whether a region was abandoned or its people moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Oral history is the fourth strand, and treating it as evidence rather than as folklore is a recent and consequential change in practice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of the four is sufficient alone, which is why the picture keeps moving, and why a confident date in a textbook is worth checking against the dates underneath it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","archaeology","science","north america","united states","history"],"sources":["Sturt W. Manning, Jennifer Birch, Megan A. Conger, et al., “Radiocarbon re-dating of contact-era Iroquoian history in northeastern North America,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaav0280, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6281431/. [Open access]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]","Christopher I. Roos, J. Mark Kaib, Nicholas C. Laluk, et al., “Tree rings reveal persistent Western Apache (Ndee) fire stewardship and niche construction in the American Southwest,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 32 (2025): e2509169122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12358881/. [Open access]","Thomaz Pinotti, Michael A. Adler, Richard Mermejo, et al., “Picuris Pueblo oral history and genomics reveal continuity in US Southwest,” <i>Nature</i> 642, no. 8066 (2025): 125–32, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12137115/. [Open access]","Brian M. Kemp, Kathleen Judd, Cara Monroe, et al., “Prehistoric mitochondrial DNA of domesticate animals supports a 13th century exodus from the northern US southwest,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 7 (2017): e0178882, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5528258/. [Open access]"],"undatable":true,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/North_carolina_algonkin-dorf.jpg","title":"The town of Secoton, 1585","desc":"John White painted a Carolina Algonquian town with its houses, its three fields of maize at different stages and a dance ground, on the first English voyage to Roanoke.","credit":"John White, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_carolina_algonkin-dorf.jpg","alt":"A watercolour looking down on a Native town: barrel-roofed bark houses along a path, plots of maize, people eating and a circle of dancers."}},
{"id":"us-041","num":41,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"The two-way traffic of plants, animals and diseases that followed 1492 is called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it moved maize and potatoes east while bringing cattle, horses and smallpox west.","questions":["Stomach bacteria in the Americas today are mixtures of European and African lineages with almost no pre-1492 input, a molecular trace of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left in living bodies.","American beans do not appear in European painting until the mid-16th century, decades after they crossed, which shows how unevenly the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually moved crops."],"answer":"Columbian exchange","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1492</span><span class=\"dt-k\">First trades</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1492 – 1497</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Beans in Europe</span><span class=\"dt-v\">mid-1500s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Columbian exchange</b> is the movement of people, plants, animals and diseases between the hemispheres that followed 1492, and it reorganised life on both sides of the Atlantic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It began as a series of individual transactions, and a database drawn from the Spanish sources catalogues every recorded handover of an object between Caribbean peoples and Europeans from 1492 to 1497.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Going east were the potato, the tomato, the chile pepper, chocolate, tobacco, pineapple and rubber; going the other way came cattle, donkeys, oranges and bananas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nothing about that traffic was quick, and American beans are not certainly shown in European painting until the middle of the 16th century, decades after the first voyages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Columbus had recorded beans in Cuba in November 1492 by comparing them to crops he knew, calling them cowpeas and broad beans in his journal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The horse went west and was taken up quickly: archaeological and modern North American horses carry strong Iberian ancestry, and horses were in Indigenous hands as far north as Idaho by the first half of the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Disease moved overwhelmingly in one direction, and the pandemics that followed, alongside starvation and enslavement, removed as much as 90 per cent of the Indigenous population of the Americas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The exchange is legible in bacteria, since <i>Helicobacter pylori</i> genomes from North, Central and South America are mixtures of European and African lineages with little surviving pre-Columbian input.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is legible in vegetation too, though not simply: pollen records show that forest regrowth after depopulation varied with region, land use and economy rather than following one pattern.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> And it was never only Atlantic, because the Spanish empire tied Madrid, Mexico City and Manila into a single pan-tropical system that carried American crops on to Asia and the Pacific.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Columbian exchange","sources":["Floris W. M. Keehnen and Angus A. A. Mol, “The roots of the Columbian Exchange: an entanglement and network approach to early Caribbean encounter transactions,” <i>The Journal of Island and Coastal Archaeology</i> 16, no. 2–4 (2021): 261–89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8452148/. [Open access]","James R. Myers, Alice K. Formiga and Jules Janick, “Iconography of Beans and Related Legumes Following the Columbian Exchange,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 13 (2022): 851029, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8964180/. [Open access]","Rebecca Hamilton, Jesse Wolfhagen, Noel Amano, et al., “Non-uniform tropical forest responses to the ‘Columbian Exchange’ in the Neotropics and Asia-Pacific,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 5, no. 8 (2021): 1174–84, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8324576/. [Open access]","Kaisa Thorell, Koji Yahara, Elvire Berthenet, et al., “Rapid evolution of distinct Helicobacter pylori subpopulations in the Americas,” <i>PLOS Genetics</i> 13, no. 2 (2017): e1006546, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5322909/. [Open access]","William Timothy Treal Taylor, Pablo Librado, Mila Hunska Tašunke Icu, et al., “Early dispersal of domestic horses into the Great Plains and northern Rockies,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6639 (2023): 1316–23, https://extension.usu.edu/freesnetwork/files/Tayloretal2023EarlydispersalofhorseinNorthAmerica.pdf. [Open access]","“The Columbian Exchange,” Castillo de San Marcos National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/casa/learn/historyculture/columbian-exchange.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["concept","history","migration","nature","north america","united states"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Badianus_Codex%3B_photograph_of_open_book_Wellcome_L0021273.jpg/1920px-Badianus_Codex%3B_photograph_of_open_book_Wellcome_L0021273.jpg","title":"An Aztec herbal of 1552","desc":"Two facing pages of a manuscript compiled in Mexico, its plants named in Nahuatl and its remedies written out in Latin.","credit":"Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badianus_Codex%3B_photograph_of_open_book_Wellcome_L0021273.jpg","alt":"An open manuscript showing painted plants with roots and flowers above lines of handwritten Latin."}},
{"id":"us-042","num":42,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"An epidemic striking people with no prior exposure and no acquired immunity is called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a phrase that carries a whole explanation of American depopulation with it.","questions":["Fifty exomes from one Northwest Coast community imply a 57 per cent fall in effective population, the sharpest measurement yet of what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did to a people.","Smallpox stops being infectious sooner than an Atlantic crossing took, an awkward objection to the simplest version of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explaining the American catastrophe."],"answer":"virgin soil epidemic","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Named</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1976</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Epidemics</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1782, 1816, 1837</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Measured fall</span><span class=\"dt-v\">57 per cent</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>virgin soil epidemic</b> is one that strikes a population with no previous exposure to the pathogen, and the phrase carries a hypothesis: that Old World diseases met defenceless Americans and did the killing by themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The evidence for it is largely eyewitness testimony from a time when disease was diagnosed by its symptoms and infectious pathology was not characterised at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Against it stands an argument that the collapse was compounded, by lost infrastructure, falling birth rates, war, killing, enslavement, forced movement and famine, with disease one cause among several.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One technical objection is awkward for the simple version, since smallpox stops being infectious sooner than a crossing of the Atlantic then took.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Genetics has been asked to settle the question and has not, because over so few generations a signal of selection is very hard to separate from drift.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The clearest measurement comes from the Northwest Coast, where 50 ancient and modern exomes from one community imply a 57 per cent fall in effective population size after contact.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the same data the immune gene HLA-DQA1 carries a signal of selection before contact and a marked fall in those alleles afterwards, a reversal matching the epidemics of the 1800s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hemisphere-wide, a review of 119 regional estimates puts the loss at about 90 per cent of the Indigenous population in the century after 1492.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Missouri villages show what that meant in one place: smallpox in 1782 broke up settlements, and in 1837 the Mandan lost 93 per cent of their people, leaving 138 alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> On the southern Plains the waves kept coming, with smallpox in 1816, cholera in 1849 that the Kiowa believed took half the tribe, and smallpox again in 1861 and 1862.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"virgin soil epidemic","sources":["Evelyn Jane Collen, Angad Singh Johar, João C. Teixeira and Bastien Llamas, “The immunogenetic impact of European colonization in the Americas,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 13 (2022): 918227, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9388791/. [Open access]","John Lindo, Emilia Huerta-Sánchez, Shigeki Nakagome, et al., “A time transect of exomes from a Native American population before and after European contact,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 7 (2016): 13175, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5116069/. [Open access]","Alexander Koch, Chris Brierley, Mark M. Maslin, et al., “Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492,” <i>Quaternary Science Reviews</i> 207 (2019): 13–36, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.quascirev.2018.12.004. [Open access]","“What Happened to the Earthlodge Builders?,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/what-happened-to-the-earthlodge-builders.htm. [Open access]","“Pandemics on the Plains,” Fort Union National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/foun/learn/historyculture/pandemics-on-the-plains.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","science","north america","united states","genetics"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/39/Aztec_smallpox_victims.jpg","title":"Smallpox in the Florentine Codex","desc":"Nahua drawings from Book XII showing people covered in pustules and a healer attending one of them.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aztec_smallpox_victims.jpg","alt":"A line drawing divided into panels, each showing a blanketed figure spotted with pox lying down."}},
{"id":"us-043","num":43,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"The collapse of Indigenous population across the Americas in the century after 1492 is now called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it is measurable in ice cores as well as in archives.","questions":["Some 55.8 million hectares went out of cultivation and the regrowth drew down carbon, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left a signature in the atmosphere and in Antarctic ice.","A review of 119 regional estimates puts the population of the Americas at 60.5 million in 1491 and the loss during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at about 90 per cent."],"answer":"Great Dying","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Population</span><span class=\"dt-v\">60.5 million, 1492</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Loss</span><span class=\"dt-v\">90 per cent by c. 1600</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Carbon trough</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1610</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Great Dying</b> is the name now given to the collapse of Indigenous population across the Americas in the century after 1492, and it is one of the few historical events large enough to leave a signal in the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A quantitative review of 119 published regional estimates puts the population of the Americas in 1492 at 60.5 million, with an interquartile range of 44.8 to 78.2 million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same review finds that European epidemics removed 90 per cent of those people over the following century, with an interquartile range of 87 to 92 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those are hemispheric figures, and the numbers for North America alone are estimated separately, from summed radiocarbon dates rather than from colonial testimony, and they are contested in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The loss is legible in the land, because at about a hectare of farmland a person some 55.8 million hectares (138 million acres) went out of cultivation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Regrowth on that abandoned ground took up 7.4 petagrams of carbon, the equivalent of about 3.5 parts per million of atmospheric carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ice cores record a fall of 7 to 10 parts per million in the late 1500s and early 1600s with about 0.15 °C (0.27 °F) of cooling, and the trough near 1610 has been proposed as a marker for the start of the Anthropocene.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pollen evidence complicates that story, since afforestation across the Spanish tropics was uneven and depended on what people had been doing with the land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Genomes from one Northwest Coast community imply a 57 per cent fall in effective population size, which is the same collapse measured in a single valley.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The causes are not reducible to microbes, because warfare, enslavement, relocation and famine ran alongside the epidemics, and separating them is the open question.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Great Dying","sources":["Alexander Koch, Chris Brierley, Mark M. Maslin, et al., “Earth system impacts of the European arrival and Great Dying in the Americas after 1492,” <i>Quaternary Science Reviews</i> 207 (2019): 13–36, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.quascirev.2018.12.004. [Open access]","Rebecca Hamilton, Jesse Wolfhagen, Noel Amano, et al., “Non-uniform tropical forest responses to the ‘Columbian Exchange’ in the Neotropics and Asia-Pacific,” <i>Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution</i> 5, no. 8 (2021): 1174–84, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8324576/. [Open access]","John Lindo, Emilia Huerta-Sánchez, Shigeki Nakagome, et al., “A time transect of exomes from a Native American population before and after European contact,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 7 (2016): 13175, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5116069/. [Open access]","Evelyn Jane Collen, Angad Singh Johar, João C. Teixeira and Bastien Llamas, “The immunogenetic impact of European colonization in the Americas,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 13 (2022): 918227, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9388791/. [Open access]","Robert L. Kelly, Madeline E. Mackie, Spencer R. Pelton and Erick Robinson, “Spatiotemporal distribution of the North American Indigenous population prior to European contact,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 6 (2025): e2419454122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11831114/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","climate","science","north america","united states"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Ghgs-lawdome-2000yr-CO2-asof2010.svg/1920px-Ghgs-lawdome-2000yr-CO2-asof2010.svg.png","title":"Carbon dioxide since the year 0","desc":"The Law Dome ice core record, with the dip in the early 1600s that has been attributed to forest regrowth in the Americas.","credit":"DeWikiMan, CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghgs-lawdome-2000yr-CO2-asof2010.svg","alt":"A line graph of carbon dioxide concentration running flat near 280 parts per million, dipping around 1600 and rising steeply after 1850."}},
{"id":"us-044","num":44,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Caused by variola virus and killing at least one in three of those it infected, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the only human disease that has ever been eradicated.","questions":["At one Texas mission in 1739 only 49 of 300 Indigenous inhabitants survived an outbreak of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and on the upper Missouri in 1837 just 138 Mandan were left alive.","An enslaved West African named Onesimus described deliberate infection to Cotton Mather in 1716, which is how inoculation against <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached the American colonies."],"answer":"smallpox","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Inoculation</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Boston, 1721</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Epidemics</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1739, 1782, 1837</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Vaccine</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1796</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Eradicated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1980</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Smallpox</b>, caused by variola virus, killed at least one in three of the people it infected, and it is the only human disease ever eradicated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Molecular dating puts its emergence as a human pathogen earlier than 3,800 years ago, which fits the pockmarked Egyptian mummies and revises younger estimates drawn from ancient genomes alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its lineages drifted apart and went extinct in turn, and a small share of Viking-age ancestry survived in the virus into the 18th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Carried to the Americas by Europeans, it met people who had never encountered it: at Mission San José in Texas in 1739, only 49 of 300 Indigenous inhabitants survived an outbreak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On the upper Missouri it returned again and again, and the epidemic of 1837 left 138 Mandan alive out of a people once estimated at 10,000 to 15,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Survivors were not infected twice, an observation old in Asia and Africa that produced variolation, the deliberate transfer of matter from a smallpox sore into a healthy person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached the American colonies through an enslaved West African named Onesimus, who described the practice in 1716 to Cotton Mather, who argued for it during Boston’s outbreak of 1721.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> During the War of Independence the disease was wrecking the Continental Army, and George Washington made inoculation compulsory for its soldiers in 1777.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 1796 an eight-year-old boy inoculated with matter from a cowpox sore proved resistant to smallpox, and by 1801 testing had established the first vaccine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Compulsory vaccination followed in Britain and parts of the United States in the 1840s and 1850s, yet smallpox was still killing over two million people a year around 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"smallpox","sources":["“History of smallpox vaccination,” World Health Organization, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.who.int/news-room/spotlight/history-of-vaccination/history-of-smallpox-vaccination. [Open access]","Diego Forni, Cristian Molteni, Rachele Cagliani, Mario Clerici and Manuela Sironi, “Analysis of variola virus molecular evolution suggests an old origin of the virus consistent with historical records,” <i>Microbial Genomics</i> 9, no. 1 (2023): 000932, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9973844/. [Open access]","“San Antonio Missions: Spanish Influence in Texas,” Teaching with Historic Places, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/san-antonio-missions-spanish-influence-in-texas-teaching-with-historic-places.htm. [Open access]","“What Happened to the Earthlodge Builders?,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/what-happened-to-the-earthlodge-builders.htm. [Open access]","“Smallpox, Inoculation, and the Revolutionary War,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/smallpox-inoculation-revolutionary-war.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["concept","history","science","north america","united states","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Smallpox_virus_virions_TEM_PHIL_1849.JPG/1920px-Smallpox_virus_virions_TEM_PHIL_1849.JPG","title":"Variola virions","desc":"A transmission electron micrograph of smallpox virus particles in a thin section of infected tissue.","credit":"CDC / Dr. Fred Murphy and Sylvia Whitfield, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smallpox_virus_virions_TEM_PHIL_1849.JPG","alt":"Three dark brick-shaped virus particles against a pale grey background at very high magnification."}},
{"id":"us-045","num":45,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Spanish settlements in the Southwest held herds from the 1500s, and bones now date the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in Indigenous hands as far north as Idaho by the early 17th century.","questions":["Colonial documents had the animal spreading north only after the Pueblo Revolt of 1680, but genomes, isotopes and radiocarbon put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the northern Plains decades earlier.","A foal from Wyoming shows the bone changes of load carrying and a Kansas tooth records winter feeding on maize, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was being managed by Indigenous herders."],"answer":"horse","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Spanish herds</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from the 1500s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Idaho horse</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1597 – 1657</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Comanche horses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 1600s</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>horse</b> came back to North America with the Spanish, whose settlements in the Southwest held herds from the 1500s onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where it went next was long told from colonial documents, which had horses spreading north in numbers only after the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 drove Spanish settlers out of New Mexico.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bones say otherwise, and an analysis combining genomes, isotopes, radiocarbon and skeletal pathology dates horses in Indigenous hands at Paa’ko in New Mexico and American Falls in Idaho to the first half of the 17th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those horses carry strong Iberian ancestry with a later influx from British stock and nothing Norse, and they moved north through Indigenous exchange networks rather than European ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They were being cared for and worked: a foal from Blacks Fork in Wyoming shows bone growth at the back of the skull from carrying loads, and a tooth from the Kaw River in Kansas records winter feeding on maize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The horse changed what was possible on the grassland, above all by letting people leave permanent villages and follow the bison across distances that had defeated hunters on foot, who had to bring a herd to a cliff or into a timber pound to take it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Not every nation reorganised itself around the animal, and of the more than thirty Plains peoples several stayed in their river-valley fields and hunted out from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For the Comanche, who took up horses in the late 17th century, the animal turned grass into bison meat and fed a fast-growing mounted population.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Horses were wealth as well as transport, and they moved through trade networks that also carried captives, weapons, hides and metal goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Lakota account is not one of domestication at all, since Šuŋgwakȟáŋ, the Horse Nation, was neither fenced nor bred to order, and the language has no words for wild and domesticated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"horse","sources":["William Timothy Treal Taylor, Pablo Librado, Mila Hunska Tašunke Icu, et al., “Early dispersal of domestic horses into the Great Plains and northern Rockies,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6639 (2023): 1316–23, https://extension.usu.edu/freesnetwork/files/Tayloretal2023EarlydispersalofhorseinNorthAmerica.pdf. [Open access]","“The Plains Indians,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-plains-indians.htm. [Open access]","“Comanches and Horses,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/comanches-and-horses.htm. [Open access]","Carol A. Lipscomb, “Comanche Indians,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/comanche-indians. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Bison Bellows: Indigenous Hunting Practices,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/bison-bellows-3-31-16.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["animal","history","technology","north america","united states","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Comanche_Feats_of_Horsemanship_SAAM-1985.66.487_1.jpg/1920px-Comanche_Feats_of_Horsemanship_SAAM-1985.66.487_1.jpg","title":"Comanche Feats of Horsemanship","desc":"George Catlin painted riders on the southern Plains in the 1830s, one of them dropped along the far side of his horse.","credit":"George Catlin, Smithsonian American Art Museum, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comanche_Feats_of_Horsemanship_SAAM-1985.66.487_1.jpg","alt":"A painting of four mounted men with lances galloping across open grassland under a pale sky."}},
{"id":"us-046","num":46,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"A Shoshone people of the Great Basin who took up horses and became the dominant power of the southern Plains, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called themselves Nermernuh, meaning simply the People.","questions":["Spanish Texas could not beat them and bought peace instead in 1785, while Texan officials tried to seize a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> peace delegation at San Antonio in 1840 and killed twelve chiefs.","Numbering perhaps 20,000 in the late 1700s, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were reduced to about 2,000 a century later, mostly by disease rather than by any army."],"answer":"Comanche","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Horses</span><span class=\"dt-v\">late 1600s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Texas</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1706</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Council House</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1840</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Census</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1,597 in 1875</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Comanche</b> were a Shoshone people of the Great Basin before they became the dominant power of the southern Plains, and they call themselves Nermernuh, the People.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their language belongs to the Uto-Aztecan family and is close to Northern Shoshone, while the name others used for them comes from a Ute word meaning enemy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taking up horses in the late 17th century remade them, and they left the mountains for the plains of eastern Colorado and western Kansas and then pushed south.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They arrived not as one nation but as bands, of which as many as thirteen were named and five mattered most, from the Penateka in the south to the Yamparika on the Arkansas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Authority was deliberately diffuse, with civil and war chiefs who led by consent and councils whose decisions bound nobody, which European officials repeatedly failed to understand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Horses turned grass into bison meat and let their numbers grow, and horses were themselves a commodity in a trade that also ran in captives, weapons, hides and metal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Spanish Texas could not defeat them and negotiated instead, and the treaty agreed at San Antonio in 1785 held with only minor breaches until the end of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Texan policy after 1836 was harsher, and at the Council House in San Antonio in March 1840 soldiers tried to seize a Penateka peace delegation, killing about 35 Comanches including twelve chiefs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Disease did what armies could not, and a people perhaps 20,000 strong in the late 1700s numbered about 2,000 by the end of the following century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A reservation census in 1875 counted 1,597, and the Comanche Nation’s tribal historian is now a co-author of the study that redated the horse’s arrival on the Plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Comanche","sources":["Carol A. Lipscomb, “Comanche Indians,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/comanche-indians. [Open access]","“Comanches and Horses,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/comanches-and-horses.htm. [Open access]","“Council House Fight,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/council-house-fight. [Open access]","“Pandemics on the Plains,” Fort Union National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/foun/learn/historyculture/pandemics-on-the-plains.htm. [Open access]","William Timothy Treal Taylor, Pablo Librado, Mila Hunska Tašunke Icu, et al., “Early dispersal of domestic horses into the Great Plains and northern Rockies,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6639 (2023): 1316–23, https://extension.usu.edu/freesnetwork/files/Tayloretal2023EarlydispersalofhorseinNorthAmerica.pdf. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["people","history","north america","united states","warfare","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/66/Chief_Quanah_Parker_of_the_Kwahadi_Comanche.jpg","title":"Quanah Parker","desc":"The Kwahadi leader, photographed in a blanket and breastplate outside a tipi after the move to the reservation.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chief_Quanah_Parker_of_the_Kwahadi_Comanche.jpg","alt":"A standing man in a long blanket and bone breastplate, holding feathers, photographed in front of a canvas tipi."}},
{"id":"us-047","num":47,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Spanish and Mexican officials called the country the Comanche controlled <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a territory one historian has called larger than the whole European-held area north of the Rio Grande.","questions":["Bands split to spare the grass and a camp of about 250 people kept a thousand horses and mules, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was actually held together.","Drought in the 1840s set horse herds against bison for forage, hide hunters finished the herds, and by 1875 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had ended in reservations in Indian Territory."],"answer":"Comanchería","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Peace with Spain</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1785</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1850</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Red River War</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1874 – 1875</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Comanchería</b> was the name Spanish and Mexican officials gave to the country the Comanche controlled, a territory one historian has called larger than the whole European-held area north of the Rio Grande.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It ran on horses and grass, so bands stayed small to avoid overgrazing, and a rancheria of roughly 250 people kept about 1,000 horses and mules and moved camp often.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trading and raiding alternated in ways that confused Spanish officials, and over decades the arrangement drained New Mexico and Texas of animals, food and people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Spain answered with a peace bought by gifts and trade, agreed in 1785 and kept until Spanish power could no longer supply what it had promised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Mexican independence in 1821 changed little, though epidemics were already at work and smallpox swept the southern Plains in 1816.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The Republic of Texas would not concede the one thing asked of it, a boundary, and after the killings at the Council House in 1840 a retaliatory raid swept to the coast before being broken at Plum Creek.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What ended the ascendancy was ecological as much as military, since drought in the 1840s and 1850s set the vast horse herds against the bison for forage, water and shelter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Professional hide hunters out of Dodge City then destroyed the herds on the reservation ranges between 1872 and 1874, while the rations promised at Medicine Lodge in 1867 failed to arrive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In June 1874 about 700 Cheyenne, Comanche and Kiowa warriors attacked a buffalo hunters’ camp at Adobe Walls in the Texas Panhandle and were held off by 28 men and one woman.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Red River War followed, and by the spring of 1875 the destruction of horses and supplies rather than any great battle had forced the southern Plains nations onto reservations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Comanchería","sources":["Carol A. Lipscomb, “Comanche Indians,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/comanche-indians. [Open access]","“Red River War,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/red-river-war. [Open access]","“Adobe Walls, Second Battle of,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/adobe-walls-second-battle-of. [Open access]","“Comanches and Horses,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/comanches-and-horses.htm. [Open access]","“Pandemics on the Plains,” Fort Union National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/foun/learn/historyculture/pandemics-on-the-plains.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["place","history","warfare","north america","united states","geography"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d8/Comancheria.png","title":"Comanchería, 1770–1850","desc":"A modern map of the territory and of the raiding routes that ran from it into New Mexico, Texas and northern Mexico.","credit":"Bill Nelson and Wikimedia user Plumber, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comancheria.png","alt":"A greyscale map of the southern Plains with shaded zones and dashed arrows running south into Mexico."}},
{"id":"us-048","num":48,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Emerging from Wind Cave in their own account and pushing onto the northern Plains in the 1700s, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the people of the bison and the Black Hills.","questions":["Tatanka is the word for bison in the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose name pte oyate, the buffalo nation, describes a relationship rather than a food supply.","The horse is called Šuŋgwakȟáŋ, the Horse Nation, an ally rather than a possession, and the language of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has no words for wild or domesticated."],"answer":"Lakota","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Move west</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1700 – 1800</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Bison then</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 30 million</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Horses north</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 1650</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Lakota</b> tell their own beginning at Wind Cave in the Black Hills, where a passage called Oniya Oshoka, where the earth breathes inside, leads to the spirit lodge the first people and bison came out of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The version told at the park comes from the Cheyenne Creek community at Pine Ridge, recorded from a tribal historian and spiritual leader, and there are as many versions as there are bands and families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In it the trickster Iktomi and the double-faced Anog-Ite are punished for letting pride guide them, and the story turns on why people came to be on the surface at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The archaeology of the same country records another kind of history, in which the Badlands were seasonal hunting ground for a succession of peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Excavation read together with oral history shows the Oglala Lakota displacing the Arikara there during the 1700s, as the nation moved west onto the northern Plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Bison were the centre of that life, tatanka in Lakota and also pte, which gives pte oyate, the buffalo nation, out of a herd estimated at some thirty million animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every part of an animal was used for food, clothing, bedding, tools and fuel, and bison run through Lakota oral history and ceremony as well as through the diet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The horse arrived in the same century as the move west, and Lakota accounts describe Šuŋgwakȟáŋ, the Horse Nation, as an ally rather than a possession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lakota tradition also remembers horses in the country long before the Spanish, and environmental DNA now places horses in the far north as late as 5,000 to 6,000 years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That knowledge is not only historical, and at Standing Rock ecological calendars keyed to plants, animals and weather are being used by the community itself to plan for a changing climate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Lakota","sources":["“The Lakota Emergence Story,” Wind Cave National Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/wica/learn/historyculture/the-lakota-emergence-story.htm. [Open access]","“Archaeology: Ancient People of the Badlands,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/archaeology-badl.htm. [Open access]","“Bison, Buffalo, Tatanka: Bovids of the Badlands,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/bison_badl.htm. [Open access]","William Timothy Treal Taylor, Pablo Librado, Mila Hunska Tašunke Icu, et al., “Early dispersal of domestic horses into the Great Plains and northern Rockies,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6639 (2023): 1316–23, https://extension.usu.edu/freesnetwork/files/Tayloretal2023EarlydispersalofhorseinNorthAmerica.pdf. [Open access]","Morgan L. Ruelle, Aubrey Joshua Skye, Evan Collins and Karim-Aly S. Kassam, “Ecological Calendars, Food Sovereignty, and Climate Adaptation in Standing Rock,” <i>GeoHealth</i> 6, no. 12 (2022): e2022GH000621, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9736771/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["people","history","north america","united states","nature","migration"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/Drawings_from_four_winter_counts_-_three_Sioux_and_one_Kiowa_-_representing_the_year_1833.png","title":"Four winter counts for 1833","desc":"Three Sioux counts and one Kiowa, each recording the same meteor storm as that year’s picture in a painted calendar.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drawings_from_four_winter_counts_-_three_Sioux_and_one_Kiowa_-_representing_the_year_1833.png","alt":"Four small drawings of stars falling, one around a tipi, one around a crescent moon, one beside a standing figure."}},
{"id":"us-049","num":49,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Tipis, parfleches, mounted bison hunts and herds counted as wealth are the marks of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which never fitted every nation on the grassland.","questions":["Because everything had to be carried, the art of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> went onto tipis, clothing, cases and pipes rather than onto anything a family could not move.","Fort Union took in 25,000 buffalo robes a year, which is how the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was drawn into a market that would help destroy the herds it depended on."],"answer":"Plains horse culture","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Horses north</span><span class=\"dt-v\">by c. 1650</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Height</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1750 – 1875</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Robe trade</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1828</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Plains horse culture</b> is what the peoples of the grassland made of an animal that reached them only in the 17th century, and it is the image most people now have of the Plains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its foundation was mobility, since a village could now be given up for a life following the bison across distances no one hunting on foot could cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Possessions had to be carried, so the art of these nations went onto the things they travelled with, including tipis, clothing, parfleches, pipes and bundles worked in porcupine quill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A tipi of tanned bison hide over sixteen to eighteen poles was the portable house, and hides also made the clothing, the robes and the containers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Not everyone joined it, and of the more than thirty Plains nations several stayed in their river-valley fields and hunted out from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Horses were wealth as well as transport, and they moved through trade networks that also carried captives, weapons, hides and metal goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Herds had to be fed, so bands split to avoid overgrazing and moved camp often, a camp of about 250 people running roughly 1,000 horses and mules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some horses were foddered through the winter on maize, a practice detectable in the carbon isotopes along a horse’s tooth from the Kaw River in Kansas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mounted hunt did not sweep the older skills away at once, and communal drives over cliffs and into pounds went on being used where they worked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The robe trade then drew the culture into a market, and Fort Union on the upper Missouri took in more than 25,000 buffalo robes a year and sold over $100,000 of imported goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Plains horse culture","sources":["“The Plains Indians,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-plains-indians.htm. [Open access]","William Timothy Treal Taylor, Pablo Librado, Mila Hunska Tašunke Icu, et al., “Early dispersal of domestic horses into the Great Plains and northern Rockies,” <i>Science</i> 379, no. 6639 (2023): 1316–23, https://extension.usu.edu/freesnetwork/files/Tayloretal2023EarlydispersalofhorseinNorthAmerica.pdf. [Open access]","“Comanches and Horses,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/comanches-and-horses.htm. [Open access]","National Park Service, “Bison Bellows: Indigenous Hunting Practices,” accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/bison-bellows-3-31-16.htm. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture,” Fort Union Trading Post National Historic Site, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/fous/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Carol A. Lipscomb, “Comanche Indians,” <i>Handbook of Texas Online</i>, Texas State Historical Association, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/comanche-indians. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["practice","history","north america","united states","technology","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Comanche_Village%2C_Women_Dressing_Robes_and_Drying_Meat_SAAM-1985.66.346_1.jpg/1920px-Comanche_Village%2C_Women_Dressing_Robes_and_Drying_Meat_SAAM-1985.66.346_1.jpg","title":"A Comanche village","desc":"Catlin painted tipis, horses and women pegging out and dressing bison hides, the work that fed the robe trade.","credit":"George Catlin, Smithsonian American Art Museum, CC0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comanche_Village%2C_Women_Dressing_Robes_and_Drying_Meat_SAAM-1985.66.346_1.jpg","alt":"A painting of dozens of tipis across grassland, with people seated in the foreground and a hide pegged out on a frame."}},
{"id":"us-050","num":50,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"European demand for beaver pelts drove the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which moved goods along a 4,800-kilometre canoe route from the Great Lakes into the northwest interior.","questions":["Ojibwe-built birch bark canoes, a 13.7-kilometre portage at Grand Portage and a yearly rendezvous with Montreal were the working machinery of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 18th century.","A beaver population once put at 60 to 400 million was almost extinct in the contiguous United States by 1900, the ecological bill for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and its trapping."],"answer":"North American fur trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand Portage</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1731 – 1804</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Union</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1828 – 1867</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Beaver</span><span class=\"dt-v\">near-extinct by 1900</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>North American fur trade</b> ran on European demand for beaver pelts, a demand worth crossing a continent to satisfy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its main line was the Voyageur’s Highway, a 4,800-kilometre (3,000-mile) canoe route from the Great Lakes to the northwest interior of Canada with some 120 portages, 200 rapids and 50 lakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The canoes were Ojibwe technology of birch bark, cedar, spruce resin and spruce root, about 7.6 metres (25 feet) long and weighing some 140 kilograms (300 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A crew of four to six paddled one, travelling in brigades, and each man was expected to carry two 41-kilogram (90-pound) bales at a time over every portage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> At Grand Portage the Gichi Onigaming, the Great Carrying Place, is a 13.7-kilometre (8.5-mile) trail round the falls of the Pigeon River that had carried trade for thousands of years before any European used it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The North West Company built its depot there, and between 1731 and 1804 thousands of men moved tons of goods and furs across the portage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The depot was the rendezvous, where furs from the interior were exchanged for trade goods brought from Montreal, Europe, Asia and South America.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Further west the trade turned to bison, and Fort Union was built in 1828 at the request of the Assiniboine near the meeting of the Missouri and the Yellowstone, a private post rather than a garrison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Ten nations traded there, among them the Assiniboine, Crow, Plains Cree, Blackfeet, Mandan, Hidatsa, Arikara, Lakota and Dakota, exchanging robes and pelts for goods from eight countries.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ecological bill fell on the beaver, a North American population estimated before the trade at between 60 and 400 million and all but extinct in the contiguous United States by 1900.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"North American fur trade","sources":["“An Economy of Adventure: The Life of a Voyageur,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/economy-adventure-life-of-a-voyageur.htm. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture,” Grand Portage National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/grpo/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture,” Fort Union Trading Post National Historic Site, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/fous/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Jon Dickson, Ellen Wohl, Laura L. Govers, et al., “A review of the historic and present ecological role of aquatic and shoreline wood,” <i>Biological Reviews</i> 101, no. 3 (2026): 1091–1119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13149766/. [Open access]","“The Fur Trade,” Voyageurs National Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/voya/learn/historyculture/the-fur-trade.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["practice","history","north america","united states","nature","geography"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b5/Canoe-Manned-by-Voyageurs-Passing-a-Waterfall-Frances-Anne-Hopkins.webp","title":"Voyageurs passing a waterfall","desc":"Frances Anne Hopkins painted a canot du maître and its crew in 1869, having travelled the fur route herself.","credit":"Frances Anne Hopkins, public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canoe-Manned-by-Voyageurs-Passing-a-Waterfall-Frances-Anne-Hopkins.webp","alt":"A large birch bark canoe with a dozen paddlers on still water below a rock face and a thin waterfall."}},
{"id":"us-051","num":51,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"European hat fashion set off the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a century of fighting over furs in which Haudenosaunee war parties reached the upper Great Lakes with Dutch guns.","questions":["A beaver hat sold for over four pounds in 1663, and that price is most of the explanation for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and for the near-extermination of the animal itself.","The dispersal of the Huron-Wendat in 1649 and the later attacks on the Miami and the Illinois were fought for new beaver grounds during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the 17th century."],"answer":"Beaver Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1640 – 1701</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Huron dispersed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1649</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1701</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Beaver Wars</b> were a long series of 17th-century wars over the fur trade, fought by the Haudenosaunee against the Huron-Wendat, the Algonquian nations of the north and their French allies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The market was European and the price was the engine: a hat made of beaver felt sold for more than four pounds in 1663, and by the 19th century the animal had been hunted almost out of the Adirondacks that had supplied it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch, and after them the English, sold firearms for pelts at Albany, and with them Haudenosaunee war parties ranged as far west as the upper Great Lakes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The routes mattered as much as the guns, and at the Oneida Carrying Place furs went south to the Dutch and English and north-east to the French, with trade goods travelling west in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1649 the Iroquois dispersed the Huron-Wendat, whose survivors eventually settled among Algonquian peoples at Michilimackinac in the straits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The fighting then pressed west and south against the Miami and the Illinois, in an attempt to take new beaver grounds, and the tribes gathered at the Straits of Mackinac lived in fear of being next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Disease ran through the same decades and cannot be separated from them: the Mohawk numbered perhaps 8,000 to 11,000 before contact and 2,000 to 4,500 after it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Captives were adopted rather than only killed, which made good the losses from epidemic, war and migration to the mission towns of the St Lawrence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The archaeology is being redated as this is written, since 86 radiocarbon measurements moved parts of the Iroquoian village sequence decades from where European trade goods had put it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The wars ended at Montreal in 1701, when a general peace closed roughly a century of conflict centred on the fur trade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Beaver Wars","sources":["“Adirondacks: Europeans and American Colonists,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/adirondacks-europeans-and-american-colonists.htm. [Open access]","“Adirondacks: Native Americans,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/adirondacks-native-americans.htm. [Open access]","“Through the Oneida Carrying Place: Travel, Trade, and Conflict,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/teachers/classrooms/through-the-oneida-carrying-place-travel-trade-and-conflict.htm. [Open access]","William N. Fenton, “Kondiaronk,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 2 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/kondiaronk_2E.html. [Open access]","“The Great Peace of Montréal,” Pointe-à-Callière, Montréal Archaeology and History Complex, accessed 6 September 2026, https://pacmusee.qc.ca/en/stories-of-montreal/article/the-great-peace-of-montreal/. [Open access]","“The Covenant Chain,” <i>Chenussio: The Indigenous History of Livingston County</i>, State University of New York at Geneseo, accessed 6 September 2026, https://chenussio.geneseo.sunycreate.cloud/overview-of-seneca-history/the-covenant-chain/. [Open access]","Sturt W. Manning, Jennifer Birch, Megan A. Conger, et al., “Radiocarbon re-dating of contact-era Iroquoian history in northeastern North America,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaav0280, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6281431/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["event","history","warfare","north america","united states","nature"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/North_American_Beaver_%28Castor_canadensis%29_-_Algonquin_Provincial_Park%2C_Ontario_01.jpg/1920px-North_American_Beaver_%28Castor_canadensis%29_-_Algonquin_Provincial_Park%2C_Ontario_01.jpg","title":"A North American beaver","desc":"The animal whose pelt drove the trade at the heart of the wars: a North American beaver in Algonquin Provincial Park, Ontario.","credit":"Photograph by Ryan Hodnett, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_American_Beaver_%28Castor_canadensis%29_-_Algonquin_Provincial_Park,_Ontario_01.jpg","alt":"A brown beaver crouching among grass and cut saplings at the edge of a wood."}},
{"id":"us-052","num":52,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"On 4 August 1701 some 1,300 delegates from 39 nations concluded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with New France, ending the Beaver Wars and committing the Haudenosaunee to neutrality.","questions":["A Huron-Wendat chief’s speech turned the most resistant delegations, and he died the next day, which is why the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is remembered as much for Kondiaronk as for any governor.","By staying out of the wars between France and England after the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the Five Nations could trade at Albany and at Montreal both, and did."],"answer":"Great Peace of Montreal","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Negotiated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1690</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 August 1701</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Alliance held</span><span class=\"dt-v\">until 1760</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Great Peace of Montreal</b> was a general peace concluded on 4 August 1701 between New France, its Indigenous allies and the Five Nations of the Haudenosaunee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More than 1,300 delegates from 39 nations came to Montreal for it, after negotiations that had begun a decade earlier under an earlier governor and had repeatedly broken down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The turn came from Kondiaronk, the Tionontati Huron chief the French called Le Rat, whose speech persuaded the most resistant delegations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He was already ill and died the next day, and the French held his funeral in Montreal during the negotiations he had unlocked.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On 4 August the delegates placed their totemic marks beside the name of Governor Louis-Hector de Callière, who signed for the king of France.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The terms were exact: the Haudenosaunee freed their captives, agreed to stay neutral in wars between France and England, and dropped their opposition to a French post at Detroit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In return they obtained the right to trade freely, the governor promised them goods at lower prices, and the French undertook to arbitrate quarrels between the nations rather than let them run to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The treaty text survives, and its Indigenous signatories are recorded by the animal marks they drew rather than by signatures, so the document itself shows two ways of binding a promise sitting side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Neutrality was the strategic prize, because it let the Five Nations sell furs at Albany and at Montreal at once, and Albany was the hinge of that trade for the next half century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The peace held through later quarrels, it slowed the westward push of the British colonies, and the network of French alliances it consolidated lasted until the conquest of New France in 1760.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Great Peace of Montreal","sources":["“The Great Peace of Montréal,” Pointe-à-Callière, Montréal Archaeology and History Complex, accessed 6 September 2026, https://pacmusee.qc.ca/en/stories-of-montreal/article/the-great-peace-of-montreal/. [Open access]","William N. Fenton, “Kondiaronk,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 2 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/kondiaronk_2E.html. [Open access]","“Document 3: Great Peace of Montreal (1701),” in <i>Open History Seminar: Canadian History</i>, accessed 6 September 2026, https://openhistoryseminar.com/canadianhistory/chapter/document-3-great-peace-of-montreal-1701/. [Open access]","“Through the Oneida Carrying Place: Travel, Trade, and Conflict,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/teachers/classrooms/through-the-oneida-carrying-place-travel-trade-and-conflict.htm. [Open access]","“Adirondacks: Native Americans,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/adirondacks-native-americans.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","north america","united states","warfare","concept"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/1701-08-04_Ratification_de_la_Grande_Paix_de_Montr%C3%A9al_page_01.tif/lossy-page1-1920px-1701-08-04_Ratification_de_la_Grande_Paix_de_Montr%C3%A9al_page_01.tif.jpg","title":"The Great Peace of Montreal, 1701","desc":"The ratification of the Great Peace of Montreal, signed on 4 August 1701; the delegations signed with the marks of their nations rather than with names.","credit":"Bibliothèque et Archives nationales du Québec, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1701-08-04_Ratification_de_la_Grande_Paix_de_Montr%C3%A9al_page_01.tif","alt":"A wide sheet of seventeenth-century manuscript signed along the foot with drawn animal figures used as marks."}},
{"id":"us-053","num":53,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Cylindrical beads of quahog shell strung or woven into belts, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> recorded treaties and laws for the Haudenosaunee and was read back aloud by men trained to remember it.","questions":["The Dutch turned it into currency in the fur trade and set the purple beads at twice the value of the white, but to the Haudenosaunee <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was called onikolha, of the mind.","The Hiawatha belt records the founding of the Confederacy and the George Washington belt the treaty of 1794, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is described as a document rather than an ornament."],"answer":"wampum","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">As currency</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1600 – 1700</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canandaigua belt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1794</span><span class=\"dt-k\">UNDRIP belt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2023</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Wampum</b> is made from the quahog shell, which is white with purple edges and grows only along the Atlantic coast, so the Haudenosaunee obtained the beads by trade from the peoples of Long Island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Strung or woven into belts in particular patterns, it recorded events, carried messages between nations and served as documentation in treaty negotiations, and a belt could be sent to open a discussion as well as to close one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Certain leaders were charged with memorising the event a belt was made for, and could read it back time and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its Haudenosaunee name is onikolha, meaning of the mind, and a belt gives its carrier recognised authority to speak.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Dutch encouraged its use as a currency in the fur trade in the 17th century and valued a purple bead at twice a white one, which is why it is so often mistaken for money.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Hiawatha belt is held to be the original record of the Confederacy’s founding, with the Great Tree of Peace at the centre and two hollow squares on each side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The George Washington belt records the treaty made at Canandaigua on 11 November 1794, showing fifteen figures with clasped hands and a longhouse in the middle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The protocol belts have been repatriated to the Confederacy, which holds them as its own documents rather than as museum pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reading a belt is interpretation, and the belt long said to record William Penn’s treaty has an iconography that will not resolve into a single meaning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The practice is not historical: a belt made in 2023 by Mashpee Wampanoag artists records the United Nations declaration on the rights of Indigenous peoples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"wampum","sources":["Judith L. Jourdan, “Wampum: Our Historical Record,” Oneida Cultural Heritage Department, Oneida Nation, 2013, accessed 6 September 2026, https://oneida-nsn.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/WAMPUM-OUR-HISTORICAL-RECORD-9.13.pdf. [Open access]","Kerr Houston, “Re-reading Wampum: The Penn Treaty Belt and Indeterminate Iconographies,” <i>Panorama: Journal of the Association of Historians of American Art</i> 9, no. 1 (2023), https://doi.org/10.24926/24716839.17266. [Open access]","“Hartman Brings Attention to Indigenous Rights with Wampum Belt,” Mashpee Wampanoag Tribe, accessed 6 September 2026, https://mashpeewampanoagtribe-nsn.gov/hartman-wampum-belt. [Open access]","“Kayanlaˀ Kówa – Great Law of Peace,” Oneida Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://oneida-nsn.gov/our-ways/great-law-of-peace/. [Open access]","William N. Fenton, “Kondiaronk,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 2 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/kondiaronk_2E.html. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["object","history","practice","north america","united states","concept"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Wampum_at_Seneca_Iroquois_National_Museum.jpg/1920px-Wampum_at_Seneca_Iroquois_National_Museum.jpg","title":"Strands of wampum","desc":"Strands of wampum beads, cut and drilled from whelk and quahog shells, at the Seneca-Iroquois National Museum.","credit":"Photograph by Nosferattus, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wampum_at_Seneca_Iroquois_National_Museum.jpg","alt":"Eleven hanging strands of small white and purple cylindrical shell beads."}},
{"id":"us-054","num":54,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"The alliance the Five Nations and the English colonies made at Albany in the 1670s, renewed by wampum and ceremony, was called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by both sides.","questions":["Governor Andros promised protection and supplied arms, and Mohawk raids on the Wampanoag wintering at Hoosick were the first service rendered under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that bound the two.","Because it made Albany the place where colonies had to meet the Five Nations, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave New York control of much of English relations with Native nations."],"answer":"Covenant Chain","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Formalised</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Albany, 1670s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Hoosick raids</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1676</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Canandaigua belt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1794</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Covenant Chain</b> was the alliance between the Five Nations of the Haudenosaunee and the English colonies, formalised at Albany while the colonies were at war in the 1670s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was made in the middle of two wars, King Philip’s War in New England and Bacon’s Rebellion in the Chesapeake, both fought against colonists who had encroached on Native land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sir Edmund Andros, governor of New York, met Iroquois ambassadors at Albany and promised to protect the Five Nations from the French and from their enemies to the north and east.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In exchange he asked the Mohawks to attack the followers of Metacom who had wintered at Hoosick, fifty miles east of Albany, and gave them arms and ammunition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The raids that followed helped break Metacom’s war, and Andros then invited the defeated Algonquians to settle in New York under Mohawk protection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those resettled communities were a buffer against a French invasion and gave the traders at Albany more of the colonists’ business with Native nations, which was the point of the offer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Five Nations, for their part, insisted that any dealings with the Puritan colonies take place at Albany with New York’s governor present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain was not a document but a relationship, kept in wampum and renewed in council, and it bound many nations rather than two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its later life ran through the treaty made at Canandaigua in 1794, for which George Washington had a belt commissioned showing fifteen figures with clasped hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Haudenosaunee were negotiating from strength, since a chain that held the English also held them, and the alliance ran alongside the neutrality they kept towards New France after 1701.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Covenant Chain","sources":["“The Covenant Chain,” <i>Chenussio: The Indigenous History of Livingston County</i>, State University of New York at Geneseo, accessed 6 September 2026, https://chenussio.geneseo.sunycreate.cloud/overview-of-seneca-history/the-covenant-chain/. [Open access]","Judith L. Jourdan, “Wampum: Our Historical Record,” Oneida Cultural Heritage Department, Oneida Nation, 2013, accessed 6 September 2026, https://oneida-nsn.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/WAMPUM-OUR-HISTORICAL-RECORD-9.13.pdf. [Open access]","“Through the Oneida Carrying Place: Travel, Trade, and Conflict,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/teachers/classrooms/through-the-oneida-carrying-place-travel-trade-and-conflict.htm. [Open access]","“Adirondacks: Native Americans,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/adirondacks-native-americans.htm. [Open access]","“The Great Peace of Montréal,” Pointe-à-Callière, Montréal Archaeology and History Complex, accessed 6 September 2026, https://pacmusee.qc.ca/en/stories-of-montreal/article/the-great-peace-of-montreal/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","history","north america","united states","warfare","practice"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Tejonihokarawa.jpg","title":"Tejonihokarawa, painted in London in 1710","desc":"The Mohawk leader Tejonihokarawa, painted by John Verelst during the 1710 embassy to Queen Anne, holding a wampum belt.","credit":"Painting by John Verelst, 1710; Library and Archives Canada, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tejonihokarawa.jpg","alt":"A full-length portrait of a Mohawk man in a red cloak holding a beaded belt, with a wolf beside him."}},
{"id":"us-055","num":55,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Between 1646 and 1675 New England’s missionaries gathered converts into fourteen settlements called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which Natick, founded in 1651, was the first permanent one.","questions":["Residents of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were pressed to take up English farming, houses and social structure as well as Christianity, and the programme was funded from London.","Five months into King Philip’s War the colonists interned the people of every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on Deer Island in Boston Harbour, where cold and hunger killed most of them."],"answer":"praying town","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1646 – 1675</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Natick</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1651</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Deer Island</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1675 – 1676</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"A <b>praying town</b> was a settlement of Native converts in New England, and fourteen of them were founded between 1646 and 1675.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Natick, established in 1651, was the first permanent one, and the programme was run by John Eliot, a minister at Roxbury who had learned enough of the language to preach in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It aimed at more than belief, since residents were expected to take up English-style agriculture, houses and social structure as a condition of the new religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was paid for from London by the Society for Propagating the Gospel in New England, one of several bodies founded for the purpose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its great artefact is the Bible Eliot translated into the Natick dialect and printed at Cambridge between 1660 and 1663, the first complete Bible printed in the Western Hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A thousand copies were ordered, so large a run that the paper had to be shipped specially from England.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the towns had bought their people was tested in 1675, when the hanging of three Wampanoag men and the attack on Swansea began the war the English called King Philip’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Five months into the war they set up an internment camp on Deer Island in Boston Harbour, beginning with Nipmuc people from what is now South Natick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Between 500 and 1,100 people were held there, most of them women and children, because many of the men had been pressed into serving in an English militia against other Native nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Starvation, cold and the lack of shelter killed most of them before the survivors were released in May 1676, and an unknown number were smuggled off the island and sold as slaves in the West Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"praying town","sources":["“Collections of the Massachusetts Historical Society Related to Native American History,” Massachusetts Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.masshist.org/collection-guides/view/fas0546. [Open access]","“The Eliot Indian Bible,” Library of Congress Bible Collection, Library of Congress, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/bibles/interactives/more/other13.html. [Open access]","“Deer Island,” Boston Harbor Islands National Recreation Area, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/deer-island.htm. [Open access]","“The Unexpected Start to Metacom’s War: Investigating the Archeological Remains of Swansea at ‘Nockum Hill’,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-unexpected-start-to-metacom-s-war-investigating-the-archeological-remains-of-swansea-at-nockum-hill.htm. [Open access]","“King Philip’s War (1675–1678),” Roger Williams National Memorial, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/rowi/learn/historyculture/kingphilip.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["place","history","religion","north america","united states","practice"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Houghton_AC6_E%E2%84%93452_663m_-_John_Eliot%2C_1663%2C_title.jpg/1920px-Houghton_AC6_E%E2%84%93452_663m_-_John_Eliot%2C_1663%2C_title.jpg","title":"The title page of the Eliot Bible, 1663","desc":"The title page of the whole Bible translated into Wopanaak and printed at Cambridge in 1663, the book on which the praying towns' teaching rested.","credit":"Houghton Library, Harvard University, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houghton_AC6_E%E2%84%93452_663m_-_John_Eliot,_1663,_title.jpg","alt":"A worn seventeenth-century printed title page in a decorated border, headed in the Wopanaak language."}},
{"id":"us-056","num":56,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Mass and Pueblo dances at one feast day, a Bible printed in Wôpanâak and a Mohawk woman canonised in Rome are all forms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the colonial centuries.","questions":["At Pecos the Franciscans converted many people without stopping the older practice, and the feast days held there today are <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in its most visible form.","Kateri Tekakwitha, orphaned by smallpox at four and baptised at twenty, became the first Indigenous North American canonised by Rome, and one strand of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> among many."],"answer":"Native American Christianity","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Eliot Bible</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1663</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Tekakwitha</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1656 – 1680</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pecos church</span><span class=\"dt-v\">rebuilt 1717</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Native American Christianity</b> is not one thing, because conversion was accepted, refused, adapted and blended, and the mission record shows all four at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Pecos in New Mexico the Franciscans converted many people and did not stop the older practice, and the pueblos of the Rio Grande still hold feast days that include Mass and Pueblo religious dances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What that conversion cost is on the same site, since Pecos people built the mission church and its convento without pay, which is why the labour is now discussed as a form of slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the north the Jesuits worked among the Mohawk after a French expedition burned their towns in 1666 and the Mohawks asked for missionaries as part of the peace.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Kateri Tekakwitha was born at Ossernenon in 1656 to a Christian Algonkin mother and a Mohawk father, and smallpox killed her parents and scarred her face when she was four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> She refused marriage repeatedly, was baptised, moved to the mission settlement near Montreal and died in 1680, and she is the first Indigenous North American to be canonised by the Roman Catholic Church.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In New England conversion was tied to a whole English way of living, and converts were gathered into towns where dress, farming and government were remade together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scripture itself was translated: the Bible printed at Cambridge between 1660 and 1663 was rendered into the Natick dialect and was the first complete Bible printed in the Western Hemisphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It bought its readers less protection than they had been promised, and in 1675 the Christian towns of Massachusetts were interned on an island in Boston Harbour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The useful question is therefore not whether Native people became Christians, but what each community made of a religion it had been given no choice about meeting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Native American Christianity","sources":["“Pecos Mission Church,” Pecos National Historical Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/pecos-mission-church.htm. [Open access]","Henri Béchard, “Tekakwitha, Kateri,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 1 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/tekakwitha_1E.html. [Open access]","“Collections of the Massachusetts Historical Society Related to Native American History,” Massachusetts Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.masshist.org/collection-guides/view/fas0546. [Open access]","“The Eliot Indian Bible,” Library of Congress Bible Collection, Library of Congress, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/bibles/interactives/more/other13.html. [Open access]","“Deer Island,” Boston Harbor Islands National Recreation Area, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/deer-island.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["concept","religion","history","north america","united states","practice"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Kateri_Tekakwitha_-_Claude_Chauchetiere.jpg/1920px-Kateri_Tekakwitha_-_Claude_Chauchetiere.jpg","title":"Kateri Tekakwitha","desc":"Kateri Tekakwitha, the Mohawk convert who died at Kahnawake in 1680, in a devotional painting made more than a century later by the Quebec artist Joseph Légaré.","credit":"Painting by Joseph Légaré (1795–1855), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kateri_Tekakwitha_-_Claude_Chauchetiere.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of a young woman in a dark blanket holding a cross, a river and a church behind her."}},
{"id":"us-057","num":57,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"In August 1680 nearly thirty pueblos rose together in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and drove the Spanish out of New Mexico, which they held for the next twelve years.","questions":["Runners carried knotted deerskin cords from pueblo to pueblo, one knot to be untied each day, so that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> would begin everywhere on the same morning.","Forced tribute, unpaid labour on the missions, an illegal slave trade and the punishment of religious leaders are the causes usually given for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 1680."],"answer":"Pueblo Revolt","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Rose</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1680</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pueblos joined</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly thirty</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Spanish out</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1680 – 1692</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Pueblo Revolt</b> was the rising of August 1680 in which the pueblos of New Mexico drove out the Spanish colony that had been imposed on them since 1598.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The grievances were long and specific: forced tribute in food and cloth, unpaid labour, an illegal slave trade run by officials of the Crown, and floggings for those who kept the old religion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eighty years of that had not made converts of everyone, and the colony answered continued practice by destroying kivas and punishing the men who led the ceremonies, so the rising was fought over religion as much as over labour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1675 the governor convicted 47 Pueblo leaders of sorcery, hanged some of them and had the rest flogged, which produced the men who organised what followed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The coordination was the hard part, and runners carried knotted deerskin cords between the pueblos, one knot to be untied each day until the last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Two of the runners were caught, so word went out to move early and the attacks began on 10 August, two days before the last knot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nearly thirty pueblos took part, missions were burned and the Spanish survivors were besieged at Santa Fe until they were let go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The colony was gone for twelve years, and the Spanish inscriptions cut into the rock at El Morro, which begin in 1605, stop for the whole of that time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some communities left their old sites for good, and the Jémez abandoned Gíusewa in the canyon where they had lived beside an abandoned mission for forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What returned in 1692 was not what had been driven out, since the second colony never suppressed Pueblo practice as the first had tried to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pueblo Revolt","sources":["“The Road to Revolt,” El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail, National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/elca/learn/historyculture/upload/The-Road-To-Revolt-508.pdf. [Open access]","“Pecos Mission Church,” Pecos National Historical Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/pecos-mission-church.htm. [Open access]","“Po’pay Statue,” Architect of the Capitol, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.aoc.gov/explore-capitol-campus/art/popay-statue. [Open access]","“The Spaniards,” El Morro National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/elmo/learn/historyculture/the-spaniards.htm. [Open access]","“San José de los Jémez Mission and Gíusewa Pueblo Site,” Spanish Colonial Missions of the Southwest, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/travelspanishmissions/san-jose-de-los-jemez-mission-and-giusewa-pueblo-site.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"tags":["event","history","religion","north america","united states","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Pecos_Mission_Church_Ruins_1.jpg/1920px-Pecos_Mission_Church_Ruins_1.jpg","title":"The mission church at Pecos","desc":"The ruins of the mission church at Pecos Pueblo, New Mexico; the church built before 1680 was pulled down in the revolt and a smaller one raised on the site afterwards.","credit":"Photograph by Letice Rugula, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pecos_Mission_Church_Ruins_1.jpg","alt":"Thick red adobe walls of a ruined church standing above the low walls of a pueblo, under a deep blue sky."}},
{"id":"us-058","num":58,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Born about 1630 at Ohkay Owingeh and flogged for sorcery in 1675, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> organised the pueblos that rose against Spanish New Mexico five years later.","questions":["The given name Popyn means ripe squash in Tewa, and New Mexico’s second statue in the United States Capitol is of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, carved in marble by a Jemez sculptor.","After the victory of 1680 <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ordered his people to wash away their baptisms with soapweed and to set aside marriages made in the Christian rite."],"answer":"Po’pay","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Born</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1630</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Flogged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1675</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Revolt</span><span class=\"dt-v\">10 August 1680</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statue</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2005</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Po’pay</b> was a religious leader of Ohkay Owingeh, born around 1630, whose given name Popyn means ripe squash in Tewa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As an adult he was responsible for healing and for the spiritual life of his people, which was exactly the work the colony had set out to stamp out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1675 he was one of 47 Pueblo leaders convicted of sorcery by the Spanish governor; some were executed and he was among those publicly flogged in Santa Fe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By 1680 he had moved to Taos, at the northern edge of the colony and well away from Santa Fe, and planned the rising from there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He organised the leaders of the other pueblos, nearly thirty of which joined, and runners carried knotted cords between them so that every village could count down to the same day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Spanish caught two of the runners, the pueblos were told to move early, and the attacks began on 10 August 1680, after which the survivors of the colony were besieged in Santa Fe until their water was cut off and they were allowed to leave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With the colony driven out, he ordered that the stain of baptism be scrubbed away with soapweed, that marriages made in church be set aside, and that couples remarry in the Pueblo rite.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That programme is the clearest statement of what the revolt had been about, and it treated the religion as something that could be washed off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Almost everything known about him was written by his enemies or set down long afterwards, which is why accounts of his later years disagree and why his death is undated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> New Mexico chose him for its second statue in the National Statuary Hall Collection, and Cliff Fragua of Jemez Pueblo carved it in pink marble in 2005.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Po’pay","sources":["“Po’pay Statue,” Architect of the Capitol, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.aoc.gov/explore-capitol-campus/art/popay-statue. [Open access]","“San Geronimo de Taos,” Spanish Colonial Missions of the Southwest, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/travelspanishmissions/san-geronimo-de-taos.htm. [Open access]","“The Road to Revolt,” El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail, National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/elca/learn/historyculture/upload/The-Road-To-Revolt-508.pdf. [Open access]","“The Spaniards,” El Morro National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/elmo/learn/historyculture/the-spaniards.htm. [Open access]","“Pecos Mission Church,” Pecos National Historical Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/pecos-mission-church.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"tags":["person","history","religion","north america","united states","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/U.S._Capitol_Po%E2%80%99pay_Statue.jpg/1920px-U.S._Capitol_Po%E2%80%99pay_Statue.jpg","title":"Po'pay in the National Statuary Hall Collection","desc":"Po'pay, carved in marble by Cliff Fragua of Jemez Pueblo and given by New Mexico to the National Statuary Hall Collection in 2005.","credit":"Photograph by the Architect of the Capitol, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._Capitol_Po%E2%80%99pay_Statue.jpg","alt":"A pale stone statue of a standing man holding a knotted cord, on a black plinth lettered NEW MEXICO, PO' PAY."}},
{"id":"us-060","num":60,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Named for the Wampanoag sachem the English called King Philip, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke out in 1675 and was among the deadliest conflicts per head in American history.","questions":["The hanging of three Wampanoag men and the attack on Swansea in June 1675 began <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which may have started without the sachem’s permission.","Almost a thousand colonial troops attacked the Narragansett winter town in the Great Swamp in December 1675, which brought that nation into <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> against the English."],"answer":"King Philip’s War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1675 – 1676</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Swansea</span><span class=\"dt-v\">June 1675</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Great Swamp</span><span class=\"dt-v\">December 1675</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>King Philip’s War</b> was the war fought in New England in 1675 and 1676 between the English colonies and a coalition of Native nations led by the Wampanoag sachem Metacom, whom the English called King Philip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Fifty years of colonial expansion had produced the tension, and the trigger was the colonists’ hanging of three Wampanoag men, after which the settlement at Swansea was attacked in June 1675.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war may have begun without Metacom’s permission, which is a reminder that these were coalitions of communities rather than armies under a command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sent the old men, women and children of his people across Narragansett Bay to shelter with the Narragansett, and those refugees widened the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Narragansett signed a treaty of neutrality with Massachusetts in October 1675 and then refused to hand the refugees over, answering that the English would not have a Wampanoag nor the paring of a Wampanoag’s nail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Fearing an alliance, the United Colonies raised about a thousand men and attacked the Narragansett winter town in the Great Swamp in December, killing hundreds including women and children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That attack brought the Narragansett into the war, and by the spring of 1676 they had put armies of some 2,000 and 1,500 warriors into the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most English settlements in central Massachusetts were abandoned, and the fighting devastated a region running from Rhode Island to Maine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The English position was saved from the west, where Mohawk raids made under the Covenant Chain broke the winter camp of Metacom’s followers at Hoosick.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Christian Indians were interned on Deer Island whatever their loyalties, and after the war Native captives were sold into slavery in the West Indies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"King Philip’s War","sources":["“The Unexpected Start to Metacom’s War: Investigating the Archeological Remains of Swansea at ‘Nockum Hill’,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-unexpected-start-to-metacom-s-war-investigating-the-archeological-remains-of-swansea-at-nockum-hill.htm. [Open access]","“King Philip’s War (1675–1678),” Roger Williams National Memorial, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/rowi/learn/historyculture/kingphilip.htm. [Open access]","“The Covenant Chain,” <i>Chenussio: The Indigenous History of Livingston County</i>, State University of New York at Geneseo, accessed 6 September 2026, https://chenussio.geneseo.sunycreate.cloud/overview-of-seneca-history/the-covenant-chain/. [Open access]","“Deer Island,” Boston Harbor Islands National Recreation Area, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/deer-island.htm. [Open access]","“Roger Williams: King Philip’s War,” Roger Williams National Memorial, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/rowi/learn/historyculture/philipswar.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"tags":["event","history","warfare","north america","united states","religion"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Phillip_%28sic%29_alias_Metacomet_of_Pokanoket_-_engraved_from_the_original_as_published_by_Church._LCCN89707293.jpg","title":"Metacom, as later engraved","desc":"Metacom, in the engraving published with Benjamin Church's account of the war; no likeness taken from life survives.","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phillip_%28sic%29_alias_Metacomet_of_Pokanoket_-_engraved_from_the_original_as_published_by_Church._LCCN89707293.jpg","alt":"An engraved full-length figure of a man in a beaded sash and blanket holding a musket."}},
{"id":"us-059","num":59,"category":"Contact, disease and the colonial encounter","question":"Diego de Vargas led the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1692 and cut a boast into the rock at El Morro claiming he had conquered the whole province for the faith and the crown.","questions":["Twelve years after the pueblos drove the colony out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> brought Spanish government back, but not the mission regime that had provoked the rising.","The church the Franciscans rebuilt at Pecos in 1717, on the rubble of the one burned in 1680, is noticeably smaller, which is one measure of what the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> restored."],"answer":"reconquest of New Mexico","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vargas returns</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1692</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resettlement</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1693</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Pecos church</span><span class=\"dt-v\">rebuilt 1717</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>reconquest of New Mexico</b> was the Spanish return to the province in 1692, twelve years after the Pueblo Revolt had driven out a colony that had ruled there since 1598.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It was led by Diego de Vargas, who recorded it on the sandstone at El Morro in an inscription saying that he had conquered all New Mexico for our holy faith and for the royal crown, at his own expense, in the year 1692.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rock had been a register of the colony before the revolt, with the earliest dated Spanish inscription cut in 1605, and the return put the colony back into that record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Resettlement followed rather than a conquest in a single season, and the colony was re-established over the next year, with settlers returning to sites their families had abandoned in a night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What came back was a weaker and more cautious establishment, in a province Spain valued less than it had hoped and defended thinly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The mission system returned with it, but not the campaign against Pueblo religion that had provoked the rising, and the Franciscans of the second colony worked in a province that had shown what refusal could do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At Pecos the church burned in 1680 was replaced in 1717 by one built on its rubble and noticeably smaller than the first, while the Jémez, who had left Gíusewa at the revolt, did not go back to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Franciscans went on making converts, and they no longer tried to stop the older practice, which is why the two survive together in the pueblos today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The colony’s attention turned outward, to a frontier war with the Navajo, Apache, Ute and Comanche that grew through the 18th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the end of that century neither church nor crown showed much interest in New Mexico, and few Spanish travellers passed El Morro at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"reconquest of New Mexico","sources":["“The Spaniards,” El Morro National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/elmo/learn/historyculture/the-spaniards.htm. [Open access]","“Pecos Mission Church,” Pecos National Historical Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/places/pecos-mission-church.htm. [Open access]","“The Road to Revolt,” El Camino Real de Tierra Adentro National Historic Trail, National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/elca/learn/historyculture/upload/The-Road-To-Revolt-508.pdf. [Open access]","“San José de los Jémez Mission and Gíusewa Pueblo Site,” Spanish Colonial Missions of the Southwest, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/travelspanishmissions/san-jose-de-los-jemez-mission-and-giusewa-pueblo-site.htm. [Open access]","“San Geronimo de Taos,” Spanish Colonial Missions of the Southwest, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/subjects/travelspanishmissions/san-geronimo-de-taos.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"tags":["event","history","north america","united states","religion","warfare"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/El_Morro_National_Monument_ELMO1460.jpg/1920px-El_Morro_National_Monument_ELMO1460.jpg","title":"El Morro, New Mexico","desc":"El Morro, the sandstone bluff on the road west from the Rio Grande whose face carries the inscription Diego de Vargas cut there in 1692.","credit":"National Park Service, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Morro_National_Monument_ELMO1460.jpg","alt":"A long pale sandstone bluff rising from scrubland under a dark blue sky."}},
{"id":"us-061","num":61,"category":"Contact","question":"The younger son of Massasoit, known to the English as King Philip, became the Pokanoket sachem <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the 1660s and led the coalition that fought New England in 1675.","questions":["A Wampanoag leader who believed the English had poisoned his elder brother, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the sachem of Pokanoket whose name the colonists attached to the deadliest war in New England.","Because no Indigenous account of the war survives, what is known of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from his enemies and from a Pequot minister who eulogised him a century and a half later."],"answer":"Metacom","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1638 – 1676</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August 1676</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Metacom</b> was the Pokanoket sachem, called Philip or King Philip by the English, who led the Native coalition in the war of 1675 and 1676 that carries his name.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A sachem led one of the self-governing Wampanoag towns and had his or her own council, and although leadership usually passed from father to son it could also pass to a daughter, as it did to Weetamoo at Pocasset.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Metacom was the younger son of Ousamequin, the sachem the English called Massasoit, who had made the alliance with Plymouth in 1621 and kept it for forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When Ousamequin died in 1661 the sachemship passed to his elder son Wamsutta, whom the English called Alexander; Wamsutta died suddenly after being summoned to the leaders of Plymouth Colony, and Metacom, who succeeded him, believed his brother had been poisoned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Wampanoag whose towns he led called themselves the People of the First Light, and in the early 17th century they numbered as many as 40,000 in 67 villages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The grievances that followed were land and jurisdiction, and by 1675 they had reached a breaking point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The attack on Swansea that began the war may have been made without Metacom’s permission, which is a reminder that he led a coalition of communities rather than an army under his command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He sent the old men, women and children of his people across Narragansett Bay to shelter with the Narragansett, a decision that helped widen the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> He was seen in Boston on a state visit wearing a broad belt of wampum, and after his death in August 1676 his regalia was taken as a spoil of war by Benjamin Church, whose account describes a belt nine inches wide worked with birds and beasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> No seventeenth-century Indigenous account of the war survives, and the fullest defence of him was written 160 years later by William Apess, a Pequot minister who told a Boston audience in 1836 that Metacom was the greatest man America had produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Metacom","sources":["“The Unexpected Start to Metacom’s War: Investigating the Archeological Remains of Swansea at ‘Nockum Hill’,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-unexpected-start-to-metacom-s-war-investigating-the-archeological-remains-of-swansea-at-nockum-hill.htm. [Open access]","“The Wampanoag,” Roger Williams National Memorial, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/rowi/learn/historyculture/wampanoag.htm. [Open access]","Nancy Eldredge, “Who are the Wampanoag?,” Plimoth Patuxet Museums, accessed 6 September 2026, https://plimoth.org/for-students/homework-help/who-were-the-wampanoag. [Open access]","“Excerpt from <i>A relation of the Indyan Warr</i>, by Mr. Easton of Rhode Island, 1675,” You Are There History, Plimoth Patuxet Museums, accessed 6 September 2026, https://plimoth.org/yath/unit-2/excerpt-from-a-relation-of-the-indyan-warr-by-mr-easton-of-rhode-island-1675. [Open access]","“King Philip’s War (1675–1678),” Roger Williams National Memorial, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/rowi/learn/historyculture/kingphilip.htm. [Open access]","“Wampum Belt,” You Are There History, Plimoth Patuxet Museums, accessed 6 September 2026, https://plimoth.org/yath/unit-2/wampum-belt. [Open access]","“Eulogy of King Philip (Metacom),” You Are There History, Plimoth Patuxet Museums, accessed 6 September 2026, https://plimoth.org/yath/unit-2/eulogy-of-king-philip-metacom. [Open access]","“Wampanoag Government Across Time,” You Are There History, Plimoth Patuxet Museums, accessed 6 September 2026, https://plimoth.org/yath/unit-2/wampanoag-government-across-time. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Philip_King_of_Mount_Hope_by_Paul_Revere_%28cropped%29.jpeg","title":"Metacom, as Paul Revere imagined him in 1772","desc":"Metacom, in Paul Revere's hand-coloured engraving of 1772, made almost a century after his death; no likeness taken from life survives.","credit":"Engraving by Paul Revere, 1772, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_King_of_Mount_Hope_by_Paul_Revere_%28cropped%29.jpeg","alt":"A hand-coloured engraving of a standing man in a blanket and beaded belt holding a musket, with a council seated behind him."}},
{"id":"us-062","num":62,"category":"Contact","question":"Alongside deerskins, colonial Carolina exported captive Native people to the West Indies, a commerce called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that reshaped every nation within reach of Charles Town.","questions":["Between 1670 and 1720 Carolina sent more captive Native people out through Charles Town than it brought Africans in, a traffic known as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and driven by debt and guns.","Because captives taken near home could walk back, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shipped Native people to Barbados and Jamaica instead, and by the 1720s the colony still counted 1,500 of them at home."],"answer":"Indian slave trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chiefly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1670 – 1717</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trade opens</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1674</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Indian slave trade</b> was the colonial traffic in enslaved Native people that ran out of Carolina and the other southern colonies from the 1670s until the 1720s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Official trade with the neighbouring nations began in 1674, when the proprietors sent Henry Woodward to open relations with the Westos, and the visit opened a commerce in skins and in captives at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Settlers traded guns and other manufactured goods for deerskins and for slaves, which drew the nations into a cycle of debt and dependency and gave traders room to abuse it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A nation armed with guns could take captives from a nation that was not, so the trade spread itself: the Yamasee raided the mission towns of Spanish Florida and sold the Apalachee they took to Charles Town merchants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Barbadian settlers were especially active in building the traffic, and much of it ran to the sugar islands rather than to the plantations of the Lowcountry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The direction of the shipping was deliberate, because Native people knew the Carolina landscape and could escape from it, where they could not escape from a West Indian island.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Enslavement was one of the three grievances behind the Tuscarora War of 1711, alongside the practices of white traders and, above all, land encroachment, and the war ended with about a thousand Tuscarora sold into slavery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Four years later the Yamasee and their allies rose against the same system, and the war that followed came closer to destroying an English colony than any other Native rising.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Afterwards Carolina limited the enslavement of Native people and turned to buying Africans through the port of Charles Town instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By the 1720s the colony still counted 1,500 enslaved Native people in a total population of about 17,000, and within a generation the census had stopped distinguishing them from the Africans among whom they lived.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Indian slave trade","sources":["Mary Battle, “American Indian Slavery in Carolina,” <i>African Passages, Lowcountry Adaptations</i>, Lowcountry Digital History Initiative, College of Charleston, accessed 6 September 2026, https://ldhi.library.cofc.edu/exhibits/show/africanpassageslowcountryadapt/sectionii_introduction/american_indian_slavery_caroli. [Open access]","Michael P. Morris, “Indian Trade,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/indian-trade/. [Open access]","Michael P. Morris, “Yamassee War,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/yamassee-war/. [Open access]","Jean B. Anderson et al., “The Tuscarora War: North Carolina Before 1770,” from <i>The Way We Lived in North Carolina</i> (Chapel Hill: University of North Carolina Press, 2003), NCpedia, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.ncpedia.org/waywelived/tuscarora-war. [Open access]","Joshua Howard, “Nooherooka: Site of decisive battle of the Tuscarora War, March 20–23, 1713,” NCpedia, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.ncpedia.org/nooherooka-tuscarora-war. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Native_American_being_enslaved_by_Virginia_colonists_in_the_17th_century.jpg","title":"An Indian sent into Slavery","desc":"A captive being put aboard a boat for a waiting ship, in a wood engraving of the 1880s; the trade itself left almost no images made at the time.","credit":"Wood engraving by G. A. Bogert, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Native_American_being_enslaved_by_Virginia_colonists_in_the_17th_century.jpg","alt":"A wood engraving of armed colonists forcing a bound captive into a boat on a shore, a ship at anchor beyond."}},
{"id":"us-063","num":63,"category":"Contact","question":"Beginning on Good Friday 1715 with the killing of the traders who held their debts, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> came nearer to destroying an English colony than any other Native rising.","questions":["A rebellion of the Yamasee, Creek, Choctaw and Catawba against Carolina, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drove settlers off half the colony’s cultivated land and was only turned by a Cherokee alliance.","Trade debt that would have taken every adult man two years of hunting to clear was among the causes of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which broke out along the Savannah River in April 1715."],"answer":"Yamasee War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1715 – 1717</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">15 April 1715</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Yamasee War</b> was the rising of 1715 in which the Yamasee and many of their neighbours attacked the colony of South Carolina and nearly destroyed it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Yamasee had been Carolina’s closest trading partners, exchanging deerskins and then captives for guns, cloth and rum, but commercial hunting had exhausted the deer and the raiding had exhausted the supply of captives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Traders extended credit far beyond what could be repaid, in the hope of forcing land concessions when the debts came due, and by 1715 the Yamasee debt would have taken every adult man about two years of labour to clear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A board of Indian commissioners had been set up in 1707 to regulate the traders and its agent Thomas Nairne had made little difference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On Good Friday, 15 April 1715, the Yamasee killed the traders living among them, Nairne among them, and struck the plantations along the southern frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Despite the name, the rising was far wider: the Creek, Choctaw, Apalachee, Catawba and other nations killed their own traders in the same weeks, and about ninety per cent of the traders in the interior were dead by June.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Settlers abandoned the countryside for Charles Town, where the governor armed every white man and some enslaved Africans for the defence, and the neighbouring colonies sent little help beyond Virginia’s guns and a North Carolina force under Maurice Moore.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The turning point came in 1716, when Carolina persuaded the Lower Cherokee to take its side against the Creek, opening a quarrel between those two nations that lasted forty years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About four hundred settlers had been killed, farmers had been driven from half the colony’s cultivated land, and the deerskin trade did not recover its former value until 1722.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The war also ended the traffic in enslaved Native people that had helped cause it, and Carolina turned instead to buying Africans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Yamasee War","sources":["Michael P. Morris, “Yamassee War,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/yamassee-war/. [Open access]","Wiley J. Williams, “Yamassee War,” NCpedia, from the <i>Encyclopedia of North Carolina</i>, ed. William S. Powell (Chapel Hill: University of North Carolina Press, 2006), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.ncpedia.org/yamassee-war. [Open access]","Mary Battle, “American Indian Slavery in Carolina,” <i>African Passages, Lowcountry Adaptations</i>, Lowcountry Digital History Initiative, College of Charleston, accessed 6 September 2026, https://ldhi.library.cofc.edu/exhibits/show/africanpassageslowcountryadapt/sectionii_introduction/american_indian_slavery_caroli. [Open access]","Michael P. Morris, “Indian Trade,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/indian-trade/. [Open access]","Mark D. Groover, “Deerskin Trade,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/deerskin-trade/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/YamaseeWarMap01.png","title":"The Yamasee War, 1715–1717","desc":"The nations that rose against South Carolina in 1715, with Charles Town, Pocotaligo and Port Royal, and Spanish Florida to the south.","credit":"Map by Pfly, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YamaseeWarMap01.png","alt":"A map of the colonial southeast naming the Cherokee, Catawba, Creek, Yamasee and other nations, with towns and battle sites marked."}},
{"id":"us-064","num":64,"category":"Contact","question":"Opening in September 1711 with the killing of the surveyor John Lawson, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended two years later at a fortified town in what is now Greene County, North Carolina.","questions":["Fought over trader abuses, enslavement and above all land encroachment, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drove much of a nation north to join the Haudenosaunee as their sixth member.","South Carolina sent two expeditions north to fight the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the second of them destroyed the fort at Nooherooka in March 1713 after a three-day siege."],"answer":"Tuscarora War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1711 – 1713</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Nooherooka</span><span class=\"dt-v\">March 1713</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Tuscarora War</b> was the conflict of 1711 to 1713 between the Tuscarora of the North Carolina coastal plain and the colony, its Native allies and forces sent from South Carolina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Tuscarora were an agricultural people related to the Haudenosaunee who lived, by one early eighteenth-century report, in fifteen villages through the Pamlico and Neuse river basins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The causes were three: the practices of the traders, the enslavement of Native people, and above all the encroachment of settlers, since planters claimed permanent and exclusive ownership of ground that hunters expected to keep using.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The surveyor John Lawson, who had written that the Tuscarora were better to the English than the English were to them, was captured on the Neuse in September 1711 and killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the three days that followed, at least 120 colonists were killed around Bath and their farms destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> North Carolina appealed to its neighbours, and South Carolina sent Colonel John Barnwell with militia and a large force of Yamasee allies, then Carolina’s closest trading partners, who destroyed the towns around Torhunta before agreeing a truce and withdrawing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The colony appealed again, and Colonel James Moore marched a combined force to Nooherooka, a fort of about an acre and a half on Contentnea Creek with palisades and, as excavation has since shown, interconnected bunkers and tunnels stocked with supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Moore mined the outer wall and fired the fort in March 1713, and it took three more days to take it; 392 people were burned or killed inside and another 558 killed or captured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About a thousand Tuscarora were sold into slavery and more than three thousand driven from their homes, many of them travelling north to Pennsylvania and New York.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There they were taken in by the Haudenosaunee and became the sixth nation of the confederacy, while in Carolina the frontier moved west into the ground they had left.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tuscarora War","sources":["Jean B. Anderson et al., “The Tuscarora War: North Carolina Before 1770,” from <i>The Way We Lived in North Carolina</i> (Chapel Hill: University of North Carolina Press, 2003), NCpedia, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.ncpedia.org/waywelived/tuscarora-war. [Open access]","Joshua Howard, “Nooherooka: Site of decisive battle of the Tuscarora War, March 20–23, 1713,” NCpedia, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.ncpedia.org/nooherooka-tuscarora-war. [Open access]","“1768 Boundary Line Treaty of Fort Stanwix,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/1768-boundary-line-treaty-of-fort-stanwix.htm. [Open access]","Mary Battle, “American Indian Slavery in Carolina,” <i>African Passages, Lowcountry Adaptations</i>, Lowcountry Digital History Initiative, College of Charleston, accessed 6 September 2026, https://ldhi.library.cofc.edu/exhibits/show/africanpassageslowcountryadapt/sectionii_introduction/american_indian_slavery_caroli. [Open access]","Michael P. Morris, “Yamassee War,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/yamassee-war/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Christoph_von_Graffenried_%281661-1743%29_and_John_Lawson_%281674-1711%29_as_prisoners_of_the_Tuscarora%2C_1711.jpg/1920px-Christoph_von_Graffenried_%281661-1743%29_and_John_Lawson_%281674-1711%29_as_prisoners_of_the_Tuscarora%2C_1711.jpg","title":"Graffenried and Lawson held by the Tuscarora, 1711","desc":"The captives' trial, drawn by Christoph von Graffenried, who was held with John Lawson on the Neuse in September 1711 and survived it; Lawson did not.","credit":"Drawing by Christoph von Graffenried, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_von_Graffenried_%281661-1743%29_and_John_Lawson_%281674-1711%29_as_prisoners_of_the_Tuscarora,_1711.jpg","alt":"A pen and wash drawing of two seated European prisoners before a fire, with rows of figures and a dancing crowd around them."}},
{"id":"us-065","num":65,"category":"Contact","question":"Shawnee, Delaware, Mingo and Wyandot towns filled the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the 18th century, and the ground between the Appalachians and Lake Erie was fought over for fifty years.","questions":["Britain drew a line along the Appalachians in 1763 to keep colonists out of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and moved it west again five years later at Fort Stanwix.","For the nations living in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the Revolution was not a war of independence but a war for their own survival, since defeat meant losing their towns altogether."],"answer":"Ohio Country","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Boundary line</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1763, at the Appalachians</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Line moved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1768, at Fort Stanwix</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Ohio Country</b> was the region west of the Appalachian Mountains and south of Lake Erie, taking in all of the present state of Ohio and parts of Indiana, Pennsylvania and West Virginia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was too large and too remote for any one power to control, and its towns held people of many nations and languages who received news from the east slowly and at second hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Shawnee, Delaware, Mingo and Wyandot communities settled there through the 18th century, some of them moving west to get clear of colonial settlement, and the Six Nations claimed an interest in the ground as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Before 1763 the nations there dealt with the French and the British in turn as those two fought for the fur trade, and alliances shifted with the balance between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When Britain won the Seven Years’ War and stopped the customary presents of powder and goods, the resentment helped produce the rising of 1763.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In October 1763 the Crown drew a boundary along the Appalachians and forbade settlement beyond it, partly to reduce conflict and partly to keep colonists where they could be taxed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The line was unworkable from the start, since speculators held claims on the far side of it and settlers were already living there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Five years later Sir William Johnson called a council at Fort Stanwix, where the Six Nations ceded interests reaching far down the Ohio, on ground occupied by Shawnee and Delaware towns rather than by their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Revolution that followed was, in this country, a war for land rather than a war of independence, and the sides were not the ones the eastern narrative supplies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the nations there the stakes were also different: colonists beaten could retreat east and the British could sail home, where a defeated nation lost its towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Ohio Country","sources":["“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]","“1768 Boundary Line Treaty of Fort Stanwix,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/1768-boundary-line-treaty-of-fort-stanwix.htm. [Open access]","Louis Chevrette, “Pontiac,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 3 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/pontiac_3E.html. [Open access]","“Native American History in Detroit,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/native-american-history-in-detroit.htm. [Open access]","“Pontiac’s War,” CPN Cultural Heritage Center, Citizen Potawatomi Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.potawatomiheritage.com/encyclopedia/pontiacs-war/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/%28A_trader%27s_map_of_the_Ohio_country_before_1753._LOC_gm71002324.jpg/1920px-%28A_trader%27s_map_of_the_Ohio_country_before_1753._LOC_gm71002324.jpg","title":"A trader's map of the Ohio country","desc":"A manuscript map of the Ohio country made before 1753, showing Lake Erie, the river system and the towns and paths through the interior.","credit":"Manuscript map attributed to John Patten, Library of Congress, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%28A_trader%27s_map_of_the_Ohio_country_before_1753._LOC_gm71002324.jpg","alt":"A hand-drawn map on aged paper showing Lake Erie, a network of rivers and small annotations naming towns and trails."}},
{"id":"us-066","num":66,"category":"Contact","question":"Half of colonial South Carolina’s export earnings came from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around 1700, and in one year alone the colony shipped 121,355 dressed hides to England.","questions":["Catawba, Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw and Chickasaw hunters supplied the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, taking blankets, guns, powder, cloth, axes and brass kettles in exchange at posts along the rivers.","Credit advanced against next season’s hunt made the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a machine for debt, and nations that could not pay were pressed to give up land instead."],"answer":"deerskin trade","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In Carolina</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1670 – 1770s</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Best year</span><span class=\"dt-v\">121,355 skins, 1707</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>deerskin trade</b> was the exchange of dressed deer hides for European manufactured goods that dominated the economy of the colonial southeast for a century after 1670.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the late 1600s and early 1700s it made up at least half of South Carolina’s export earnings, and it stayed among the colony’s leading exports, second behind rice by the 1740s and third behind rice and indigo by the 1760s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charleston was its hub, and merchants there, supplied on credit by factors in England, ran links from Virginia to the Gulf coast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Trade goods went inland along the river systems to posts that looked like small forts, palisaded and holding a trade house and living quarters, where Catawba, Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw and Chickasaw hunters exchanged skins for blankets, firearms, shot, powder, cloth, axes, hoes and brass kettles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The hides were packed into barrels and shipped to England for the growing garment industry; in 1763 a pound of dressed deerskin was worth six shillings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The rhythm of the trade was seasonal and it ran on credit: nations hunted in autumn and winter and settled in spring, buying the coming year’s goods against the next season’s hunt, while the trader in turn owed his own creditor in Charleston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Between 1699 and 1715 Carolina exported about 54,000 skins a year, and the best single year was 1707, when 121,355 went out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The trade eroded self-sufficiency, since iron pots and firearms were better than what they replaced and the older skills were allowed to lapse, and it encouraged hunting far beyond what the deer herds could bear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Traders advanced credit deliberately beyond what could be repaid, and land was the usual settlement: the Creek and the Cherokee gave up millions of acres in the early 1770s to clear their trade debts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Debt of exactly this kind, and the abusive practices that went with it, was among the causes of the rising of 1715, after which the deerskin trade did not recover its former value until 1722.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"deerskin trade","sources":["Mark D. Groover, “Deerskin Trade,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/deerskin-trade/. [Open access]","Michael P. Morris, “Indian Trade,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/indian-trade/. [Open access]","Michael P. Morris, “Yamassee War,” <i>South Carolina Encyclopedia</i>, University of South Carolina, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/yamassee-war/. [Open access]","Mary Battle, “American Indian Slavery in Carolina,” <i>African Passages, Lowcountry Adaptations</i>, Lowcountry Digital History Initiative, College of Charleston, accessed 6 September 2026, https://ldhi.library.cofc.edu/exhibits/show/africanpassageslowcountryadapt/sectionii_introduction/american_indian_slavery_caroli. [Open access]","Wiley J. Williams, “Yamassee War,” NCpedia, from the <i>Encyclopedia of North Carolina</i>, ed. William S. Powell (Chapel Hill: University of North Carolina Press, 2006), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.ncpedia.org/yamassee-war. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg","title":"The Catawba deerskin map","desc":"A copy of a map painted on a deer skin and presented to the governor of South Carolina, showing the nations of the interior, the paths between them and Charlestown at the left.","credit":"Copy of a Catawba original, artist unknown; Library of Congress, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg","alt":"A map on skin showing named circles for Native nations joined by red paths, with a gridded Charlestown at one side."}},
{"id":"us-067","num":67,"category":"Contact","question":"Named for an Odawa war chief at Detroit, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> broke out in 1763 when a Great Lakes confederacy attacked the British forts taken over from the French.","questions":["The preaching of the Lenape prophet Neolin, who told his listeners to give up European goods, helped prepare <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which began with a failed attempt to seize Fort Detroit.","Britain had stopped the French custom of giving powder and goods to its allies, and the resentment that followed produced <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the spring of 1763."],"answer":"Pontiac’s War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1763 – 1766</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Detroit besieged</span><span class=\"dt-v\">May – October 1763</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Pontiac’s War</b> was the rising of 1763 in which a confederacy of Great Lakes and Ohio nations attacked the British posts taken over from France at the end of the Seven Years’ War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under the French, allies had been supplied with powder, munitions and presents as a matter of course; the British commander-in-chief Jeffery Amherst ordered that practice stopped, trade confined to the posts and the rum trade ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By the spring of 1762, at the close of the hunting season, the nations round Detroit were short of powder, lead and rum, and Sir William Johnson’s advice to return to the French practice was refused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many had also heard the Lenape prophet Neolin, who claimed divine instruction and told his listeners to give up European goods and drive the British out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pontiac, an Odawa war chief of the Detroit villages, called a council on the Rivière à l’Écorce on 27 April 1763 that about 460 warriors attended, and a plan was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The plan was to enter Fort Detroit for a council with weapons hidden and rise on a signal, but the commandant had been warned and doubled the guard, so on 7 May Pontiac gave no order.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He besieged the fort instead, from May until the end of October, and the fiercest engagement of the war was fought outside it at Bloody Run on 31 July.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The rising spread far beyond Detroit: Potawatomi warriors took Fort St Joseph by a ruse in May, and posts across the pays d’en haut fell in the same season.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In October the Crown answered by forbidding colonial settlement west of the Appalachians, which was meant to calm the unrest rather than to reward it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Pontiac and Johnson made peace at Fort Ontario in July 1766, and Pontiac was murdered three years later at Cahokia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Pontiac’s War","sources":["“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]","Louis Chevrette, “Pontiac,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 3 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/pontiac_3E.html. [Open access]","“Pontiac’s War,” CPN Cultural Heritage Center, Citizen Potawatomi Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.potawatomiheritage.com/encyclopedia/pontiacs-war/. [Open access]","“Native American History in Detroit,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/native-american-history-in-detroit.htm. [Open access]","“1768 Boundary Line Treaty of Fort Stanwix,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/1768-boundary-line-treaty-of-fort-stanwix.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Indians_giving_a_talk_to_Bouquet.jpg","title":"A council on the Muskingum, October 1764","desc":"The council that ended the fighting in the Ohio country, in an engraving after Benjamin West; the speaker holds a string of wampum.","credit":"Engraving by Charles Grignion after Benjamin West, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indians_giving_a_talk_to_Bouquet.jpg","alt":"A coloured engraving of a standing speaker addressing seated British officers across a fire, with a crowd of listeners behind him."}},
{"id":"us-068","num":68,"category":"Contact","question":"Issued in the autumn after Pontiac’s rising began, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reserved the land west of the colonies for Native nations and forbade governors to take cessions of it.","questions":["Because it says Aboriginal title exists until ceded by treaty, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is sometimes called the Indian Magna Carta and is still cited in Canadian courts today.","Land speculators already held claims beyond the line the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> drew along the Appalachians, so the boundary was unworkable from the day it was published."],"answer":"Royal Proclamation of 1763","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Issued</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7 October 1763</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Canadian law</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Constitution Act, 1982</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Royal Proclamation of 1763</b> was the order issued by George III on 7 October 1763 that set out how Britain would govern the territory won from France and how it would deal with the Native nations living in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two of its provisions matter most: it defined the land west of the existing colonies as Indian territory, where Native people should not be molested or disturbed, and it forbade colonial governors to make grants there or to take cessions of land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Land could pass out of Native hands only by purchase by the Crown at a public meeting, which made the Crown the sole party a nation could sell to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was issued in the autumn of the year Pontiac’s rising began, and its immediate purpose was to calm the unrest by putting a boundary between colonists and the interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second purpose was fiscal, since colonists kept on the eastern seaboard were colonists who could be taxed to pay for the war just ended.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The line was unworkable from the start: speculators held claims on the far side of it, settlers were already living there, and Native people were living east of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within five years Britain was negotiating the line westward again, at Fort Stanwix in 1768 and at Hard Labour with the Cherokee in the same year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The American Revolution ended its force in the new republic, which built its own system of federal purchase in the Indian Intercourse Acts instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In Canada it never lapsed, and it became the basis of the treaty-making that followed, so that it is referred to in section 25 of the Constitution Act of 1982 and is sometimes called the Indian Magna Carta.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Only one English copy of the original survives in Canada, and the French translation printed in the revised statutes contains errors that distort what the provisions say.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Royal Proclamation of 1763","sources":["“Royal Proclamation of 1763,” Crown-Indigenous Relations and Northern Affairs Canada, Government of Canada, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1370355181092/1607905710844. [Open access]","“Royal Proclamation, 1763,” Indigenous Foundations, First Nations and Indigenous Studies, University of British Columbia, accessed 6 September 2026, https://indigenousfoundations.arts.ubc.ca/royal_proclamation_1763/. [Open access]","“1768 Boundary Line Treaty of Fort Stanwix,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/1768-boundary-line-treaty-of-fort-stanwix.htm. [Open access]","“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]","“Pontiac’s War,” CPN Cultural Heritage Center, Citizen Potawatomi Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.potawatomiheritage.com/encyclopedia/pontiacs-war/. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Royal_Proclamation_of_1763.jpg","title":"The Proclamation as printed in 1763","desc":"The Royal Proclamation of 7 October 1763 as printed in London by Mark Baskett, printer to the King.","credit":"Printed by Mark Baskett, London, 1763, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Proclamation_of_1763.jpg","alt":"A printed broadside headed By the King, A Proclamation, in two columns of small type under a royal arms."}},
{"id":"us-069","num":69,"category":"Contact","question":"On 6 August 1777 a relief column marching to Fort Stanwix was ambushed at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where Oneida warriors and Mohawk and Seneca warriors fought each other.","questions":["The Great Law had held the Haudenosaunee together for centuries, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the moment it broke, with the Six Nations divided between the two sides.","Oneida scouts from the village of Oriska marched with the Tryon County militia into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an ambush that killed their general and wrecked the brigade."],"answer":"Battle of Oriskany","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">6 August 1777</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Fort Stanwix</span><span class=\"dt-v\">besieged August 1777</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Oriskany</b> was fought on 6 August 1777, when the Tryon County militia marching to relieve the besieged Fort Stanwix was ambushed a few miles short of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Six Nations had kept out of the Seven Years’ War for most of its length and had then formalised an alliance with Britain, but the Revolution put a pressure on the confederacy that it could not survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most of it stayed with the Crown, while the Oneida and some of the Tuscarora took the American side, in part through the influence of Samuel Kirkland, their missionary since 1766, whose ministry had drawn their attention towards Boston.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Oneida had another reason: Sir William Johnson had overstepped his authority at the 1768 boundary-line council held at Fort Stanwix and worn them down into agreeing to push the line deep into their own land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> About 800 militia under General Nicholas Herkimer assembled at Fort Dayton and set out, joined at the Oneida village of Oriska by a small party of Oneida warriors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Herkimer meant to wait for a signal from the fort, but his officers accused him of cowardice and he ordered the advance in temper; the British commander had already been warned and sent about 700 men to lie in wait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Oneida war chief Han Yerry Tewahangarahken, his wife Two Kettles Together and their son Cornelius fought there, on the opposite side from the Mohawk and Seneca warriors in the ambush.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Herkimer was mortally wounded and the militia brigade was destroyed as a fighting force for the rest of the war, leaving the valley open to raiding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ambushing nations lost heavily too, and their withdrawal from the siege afterwards helped force the British to abandon it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the confederacy the day is remembered as the one on which the council fire at Onondaga was covered and the Great Peace broken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of Oriskany","sources":["“The Battle at Oriska,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-battle-at-oriska.htm. [Open access]","William Sawyer, “The Oneida in the American Revolution,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-oneida-nation-in-the-american-revolution.htm. [Open access]","“1768 Boundary Line Treaty of Fort Stanwix,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/1768-boundary-line-treaty-of-fort-stanwix.htm. [Open access]","“American Revolution,” Historic Timeline, Oneida Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://oneida-nsn.gov/our-ways/historic-timeline/american-revolution/. [Open access]","“The Oneidas — America’s First Allies,” Oneida Indian Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.oneidaindiannation.com/the-oneidas-americas-first-allies. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Oriskany_Battlefield_Monument_2012.jpg/1920px-Oriskany_Battlefield_Monument_2012.jpg","title":"The monument at Oriskany","desc":"The obelisk raised on the ravine where the militia column was ambushed on 6 August 1777.","credit":"Photograph by KrisBellino, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oriskany_Battlefield_Monument_2012.jpg","alt":"A tall pale stone obelisk on a stepped base behind an iron railing, under a blue sky with clouds."}},
{"id":"us-070","num":70,"category":"Contact","question":"A Mohawk war chief, interpreter and Indian Department officer, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fought for the Crown through the Revolution and afterwards led his people to the Grand River.","questions":["Interviewed in London in 1776 and painted there, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> returned convinced that the interests of the Native nations lay in keeping the alliance with the king.","The Mohawk name Thayendanegea means that he sets two bets together, and as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> he tried after 1783 to unite the Six Nations and the western nations into one confederacy."],"answer":"Joseph Brant","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1742 – 1807</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Grand River</span><span class=\"dt-v\">granted 1784</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Joseph Brant</b>, whose Mohawk name Thayendanegea means that he sets or places together two bets, was a Mohawk interpreter, war chief and Indian Department officer of the Revolutionary generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born about March 1742 at Cayahoga, in what is now Ohio, and was a child when his father died; his mother took him and his sister Mary to Canajoharie in the Mohawk valley, where her second husband’s name gave him the surname the English used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He visited England in 1775 and 1776, was interviewed at length for a London magazine, and came home more firmly convinced than before that the welfare of the Native nations lay in the alliance with the Crown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Returning through American-held country in disguise, he urged the Haudenosaunee to abandon the neutrality they had agreed with the Continental Congress and raised a force of about 300 warriors and 100 loyalists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He fought at the siege of Fort Stanwix and at Oriskany in 1777 and raided the New York settlements for the rest of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> He preferred to fight as a war chief and took an official commission only in July 1780, explaining afterwards that the older rank gave him command of more men than a captaincy did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peace of 1783 gave the Crown’s Native allies nothing, and Brant led the Mohawks who would not return to New York into what is now Ontario.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Senecas argued that a settlement on the Bay of Quinte would scatter the Six Nations too widely, and Brant accepted the reasoning and asked instead for ground nearer the old homeland.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the autumn of 1784 they received a large tract along the Grand River, and a census the next year counted 1,843 people there, Mohawk, Cayuga, Onondaga, Seneca, Tuscarora, Delaware, Nanticoke, Tutelo, Creek and Cherokee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He also worked to bring the Six Nations and the western nations into a single confederacy against American expansion, speaking at the councils held near Detroit and at Lower Sandusky in 1783.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Joseph Brant","sources":["Barbara Graymont, “Thayendanegea,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/thayendanegea_5E.html. [Open access]","“The Battle at Oriska,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-battle-at-oriska.htm. [Open access]","“Treaty and Land Transaction of 1784,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/treaty-and-land-transaction-of-1784.htm. [Open access]","“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]","“Native American History in Detroit,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/native-american-history-in-detroit.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Joseph_Brant_painting_by_George_Romney_1776_%282%29.jpg","title":"Joseph Brant, painted in London in 1776","desc":"Thayendanegea, Joseph Brant, painted by George Romney during the visit to England from which he returned resolved to keep the alliance with the Crown.","credit":"Painting by George Romney, 1776, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Brant_painting_by_George_Romney_1776_%282%29.jpg","alt":"An oil portrait of a young man in a plumed headdress, white shirt, silver gorget and sash, holding a blanket about him."}},
{"id":"us-071","num":71,"category":"Contact","question":"Sent against the Six Nations in 1779 with orders to destroy their towns and crops, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> burned more than forty villages and 160,000 bushels of corn.","questions":["Soldiers on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were startled to find framed houses with windows and orchards of apple, peach and cherry rather than the huts they had been told to expect.","Washington committed a large part of the Continental Army to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the hope of forcing the Six Nations either to sue for peace or to stop raiding for good."],"answer":"Sullivan Expedition","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Marched</span><span class=\"dt-v\">August – September 1779</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Newtown</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 August 1779</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Sullivan Expedition</b> was the campaign of 1779 in which the Continental Army invaded the homeland of the Six Nations and destroyed its towns and food supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It answered the raids that Native and loyalist parties had made on the New York and Pennsylvania frontiers through 1778, which had cost the Americans food and manpower and driven settlements to be abandoned; by 1779 the governor of New York was warning that the frontier would be pushed back to the Hudson.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Washington committed about 4,469 Continental troops under General John Sullivan, with General James Clinton second in command, and the declared object was to destroy the principal villages and food of the Cayuga and the Seneca.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two smaller expeditions went with it, one against Seneca settlements in western Pennsylvania and one, in April, against the Onondaga towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Taking prisoners as hostages for good behaviour was a further aim, and in the event almost none were taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The one pitched battle was fought on 29 August at Newtown, where a much smaller loyalist and Native force built breastworks on a ridge and was driven off it by artillery, after which nothing stood in the army’s way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The soldiers were startled by what they burned: not bark huts but ordered rows of hewn timber and framed houses with windows, vegetable fields and orchards of apple, peach and cherry, some of the towns as good as the ones they had come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oneida scouts marched with the army, so the campaign set members of one confederacy against the others as Oriskany had two years before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> By September more than five thousand people had reached Fort Niagara expecting British rations, and the garrison narrowly avoided famine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The raids resumed and grew heavier the following year, but the Six Nations were now dependent on the British for food and clothing, and the peace of 1783 made no provision for them at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Sullivan Expedition","sources":["“The Clinton-Sullivan Campaign of 1779,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-clinton-sullivan-campaign-of-1779.htm. [Open access]","“The Van Schaick Expedition — April 1779,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/the-van-schaick-expedition-april-1779.htm. [Open access]","William Sawyer, “The Oneida in the American Revolution,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-oneida-nation-in-the-american-revolution.htm. [Open access]","Barbara Graymont, “Thayendanegea,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/thayendanegea_5E.html. [Open access]","“Treaty and Land Transaction of 1784,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/treaty-and-land-transaction-of-1784.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ec/SullivanExpeditionMap_%28cropped%29.jpg","title":"The line of Sullivan's march, 1779","desc":"A manuscript map of the march from Easton to the Seneca and Cayuga country, annotated with the settlements destroyed along the way.","credit":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SullivanExpeditionMap_%28cropped%29.jpg","alt":"An aged hand-drawn map of river valleys and the Finger Lakes, with small symbols and notes marking towns."}},
{"id":"us-072","num":72,"category":"Contact","question":"Fought north of the Ohio through the decade after independence, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cost the United States the heaviest defeat any Native force ever inflicted on it.","questions":["A confederacy of Miami, Shawnee, Delaware and other nations fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to hold the Ohio as a boundary, and beat two American armies before losing a third campaign.","The confederacy that fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> wanted an Indian state south of Lake Erie, and Britain hinted at military help that never came."],"answer":"Northwest Indian War","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1785 – 1795</span><span class=\"dt-k\">St Clair's defeat</span><span class=\"dt-v\">November 1791</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Northwest Indian War</b> was fought through the decade after American independence between the United States and a confederacy of nations living north of the Ohio River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The peace of 1783 handed Britain’s claim to the country between the Ohio and the Great Lakes to the new republic without consulting anyone living there, and the nations of the Ohio Country held that the river was the boundary and that no cession made without the confederacy as a whole could bind it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Britain kept its posts in the region and encouraged the confederacy, which drew in Miami, Shawnee, Delaware, Wyandot, Ottawa, Ojibwa and Potawatomi towns.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In October 1790 the Miami war chief Little Turtle broke the forces of Josiah Harmar sent to attack the towns at the head of the Maumee, although Harmar burned 300 houses and 20,000 bushels of corn at the onset of winter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In November 1791 he destroyed Arthur St Clair’s expedition, inflicting 630 killed and 264 wounded — the heaviest losses ever suffered by an American army in a single offensive against Native forces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those victories raised hopes that the United States would accept a limit to its expansion and allow an Indian state south of Lake Erie, and encouraging talk from British officials seemed to promise military help.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Instead the republic raised and drilled a new army under Anthony Wayne, which advanced from the autumn of 1793.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Little Turtle advised negotiating rather than fighting it; his advice was rejected and he handed the command to the Shawnee chief Blue Jacket, keeping only his own Miami warriors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The confederacy was beaten at Fallen Timbers on 20 August 1794, and the British garrison at Fort Miamis shut its gates on the retreating warriors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At Greenville the following summer the nations gave up most of what is now Ohio and part of Indiana, and Little Turtle, having argued unsuccessfully for better terms, signed reluctantly and kept the peace he had signed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Northwest Indian War","sources":["Herbert C. W. Goltz, “Michikinakoua,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/michikinakoua_5E.html. [Open access]","“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]","Reginald Horsman, “Weyapiersenwah,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/weyapiersenwah_5E.html. [Open access]","“Fallen Timbers Battlefield,” Fallen Timbers Battlefield and Fort Miamis National Historic Site, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/fati/learn/historyculture/fallen-timbers-battlefield.htm. [Open access]","“Treaty and Land Transaction of 1784,” Fort Stanwix National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/treaty-and-land-transaction-of-1784.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Treaty_of_Greenville.jpg","title":"The Treaty of Greenville, 1795","desc":"American officers facing the delegates of the confederacy at Greenville, in a painting by an unidentified artist thought to have been present.","credit":"Painting by an unidentified artist, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Greenville.jpg","alt":"An oil painting of uniformed officers standing opposite Native delegates in an open river valley, tents at the left."}},
{"id":"us-073","num":73,"category":"Contact","question":"Named for a stretch of ground where a storm had thrown down the trees, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> ended the confederacy’s resistance north of the Ohio in a single August morning.","questions":["After the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the retreating warriors found the gates of the British fort below the field shut against them, which was the end of the alliance in practice.","Losses were about equal at the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but the confederacy left the field and never re-formed, and the Treaty of Greenville followed within a year."],"answer":"Battle of Fallen Timbers","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">20 August 1794</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Greenville</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1795</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Battle of Fallen Timbers</b> was fought on 20 August 1794 beside the Maumee River in what is now north-western Ohio, between the army of Anthony Wayne and the confederacy of nations that had beaten two American armies before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name comes from the ground: a stretch of woodland thrown down by a storm, in which the warriors took cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Little Turtle, who had commanded at both earlier victories, had told the council that more would be gained by negotiating with Wayne than by fighting him; the council refused, and he gave up the command to the Shawnee chief Blue Jacket.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Losses on the two sides were about equal, but the confederacy abandoned the field, and it was the leaving rather than the casualties that decided the campaign.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The retreating warriors made for the British post at Fort Miamis, a few miles downstream, and found its gates shut against them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That refusal did more damage than the battle, since the confederacy had been fighting on an understanding that Britain would help if it came to war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wayne then burned the towns and fields at the Glaize, and recorded that he had never seen such fields of corn anywhere in America between Canada and Florida.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At Greenville the following summer the nations ceded most of present-day Ohio and a portion of Indiana, and the boundary the confederacy had fought for was gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The National Park Service calls the battle the ending event in the struggle for the Old Northwest, and says plainly that it resulted in the dispossession of the nations who lived there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It did not end the argument: within fifteen years Tecumseh and his brother were rebuilding the same case, that no part of the country could be sold without the consent of all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Battle of Fallen Timbers","sources":["Herbert C. W. Goltz, “Michikinakoua,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/michikinakoua_5E.html. [Open access]","“Fallen Timbers Battlefield,” Fallen Timbers Battlefield and Fort Miamis National Historic Site, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/fati/learn/historyculture/fallen-timbers-battlefield.htm. [Open access]","Reginald Horsman, “Weyapiersenwah,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/weyapiersenwah_5E.html. [Open access]","Herbert C. W. Goltz, “Tecumseh,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/tecumseh_5E.html. [Open access]","“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Fort_Miamis_earthworks.jpg/1920px-Fort_Miamis_earthworks.jpg","title":"The earthworks of Fort Miamis","desc":"What survives of the British post below the battlefield, whose garrison shut its gates against the warriors retreating from Fallen Timbers.","credit":"Photograph by Nyttend, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Miamis_earthworks.jpg","alt":"A grassed ditch and rampart running between bare winter trees."}},
{"id":"us-074","num":74,"category":"Contact","question":"Holding that no single nation could sell land the nations owned in common, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built the last great confederacy of the Ohio Country and died fighting for the British in 1813.","questions":["A Shawnee chief whose name has been read as shooting star, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> told an American agent that the Great Spirit knows no boundaries and his red people would acknowledge none.","The confederacy that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built with his brother the Prophet was the last serious attempt to hold a line against American settlement north of the Ohio."],"answer":"Tecumseh","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1768 – 1813</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">5 October 1813</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tecumseh</b> was the Shawnee chief who built the last confederacy of the Ohio Country and died in British service in the War of 1812.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born about 1768, probably near what is now Springfield, Ohio, the son of a Shawnee chief; his name has been read as shooting star and as panther crouching in wait.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He grew up through the years in which the boundary Britain had drawn was swept away by the Revolution and the Americans pressed settlement north of the Ohio, and in which the confederacies of Little Turtle and others failed because the United States dealt with single nations or parts of nations and absorbed their land piece by piece.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The argument he took up from that failure was that the country was held in common, so that no nation could sell a part of it alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Asked in 1807 to come to Fort Wayne for talks, he answered that the Great Spirit had appointed that place for his people to light their fires and that as to boundaries the Great Spirit knew none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The movement he led was his brother’s as much as his own: Tenskwatawa preached the religious revival that gathered the followers, and Tecumseh carried the political case from nation to nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Americans read British agents behind it, and the governor of Indiana Territory called the Prophet a fool speaking the words of the British; there was some ground for the suspicion, since the brothers did ask the Indian Department at Amherstburg for arms and provisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When war came in 1812 Tecumseh fought alongside the British on the Detroit frontier, where one British officer wrote that he had kept the nations faithful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How far his authority ran is disputed even by sympathetic writers, and other leaders were as prominent on that frontier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was killed on 5 October 1813 at Moraviantown, in what is now Ontario, and the confederacy did not survive him.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tecumseh","sources":["Herbert C. W. Goltz, “Tecumseh,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/tecumseh_5E.html. [Open access]","R. David Edmunds, “Tenskwatawa,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 7 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/tenskwatawa_7E.html. [Open access]","Herbert C. W. Goltz, “Michikinakoua,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/michikinakoua_5E.html. [Open access]","“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]","“Fallen Timbers Battlefield,” Fallen Timbers Battlefield and Fort Miamis National Historic Site, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/fati/learn/historyculture/fallen-timbers-battlefield.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Tecumseh02.jpg","title":"Tecumseh, painted long afterwards","desc":"Tecumseh in a watercolour attributed to Owen Staples and based on a published engraving; it was made more than a century after his death.","credit":"Watercolour attributed to Owen Staples (1866–1949), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tecumseh02.jpg","alt":"A watercolour head and shoulders of a man in a red turban with a feather, a British officer's coat and a silver medal."}},
{"id":"us-075","num":75,"category":"Contact","question":"After visions in 1805 that turned a failed shaman into a prophet, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> preached renunciation of alcohol and of European goods and drew thousands to Prophetstown.","questions":["Born Lalawethika, a name meaning rattle or noisemaker, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> took a new name meaning the open door and became the religious founder of his brother’s confederacy.","The settlement <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> founded in 1808 at the mouth of the Tippecanoe drew more followers than it could feed, and many died in the winters there."],"answer":"Tenskwatawa","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Lived</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1775 – 1836</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Prophetstown</span><span class=\"dt-v\">founded 1808</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Tenskwatawa</b>, known to Americans as the Shawnee Prophet, was the religious leader whose revival gathered the followers of the confederacy his brother Tecumseh built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was born in about 1775 at Old Piqua in what is now Ohio, was named Lalawethika, meaning rattle or noisemaker, lost the sight of one eye as a child and grew up an alcoholic and an outsider beside two admired elder brothers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He was present at Fallen Timbers and probably at the Treaty of Greenville, and the country he came of age in was one where game was failing, the fur trade was collapsing and nations were being pressed into cession after cession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the spring of 1805, after failing as a shaman, he had a series of visions in which he was carried to a heaven and shown a hell where unrepentant drinkers burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He gave up alcohol, took the name Tenskwatawa, meaning the open door, and declared that the Master of Life had chosen him to deliver his people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> His teaching asked for a return to communal life and good relations within family and nation, and for the renunciation of alcohol and of dependence on European goods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Followers came from many nations, and in the spring of 1808, wanting distance from the American frontier and more game, he moved the settlement to Prophetstown at the mouth of the Tippecanoe River.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More arrived than the place could feed, and many died in the winter of 1808 and 1809.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> He asked the British Indian Department for arms and provisions through Tecumseh while assuring the governor of Indiana Territory that he was friendly, and was given American food as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The movement was broken while Tecumseh was away recruiting, and Tenskwatawa lived on until November 1836, dying in what is now Kansas City.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Tenskwatawa","sources":["R. David Edmunds, “Tenskwatawa,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 7 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/tenskwatawa_7E.html. [Open access]","Herbert C. W. Goltz, “Tecumseh,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/tecumseh_5E.html. [Open access]","Herbert C. W. Goltz, “Michikinakoua,” in <i>Dictionary of Canadian Biography</i>, vol. 5 (Toronto: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/michikinakoua_5E.html. [Open access]","“The American Revolution in the Ohio Country,” National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/american-revolution-in-the-ohio-country.htm. [Open access]","“Fallen Timbers Battlefield,” Fallen Timbers Battlefield and Fort Miamis National Historic Site, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/fati/learn/historyculture/fallen-timbers-battlefield.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Ten-sq%C3%BAat-a-way.jpg/1920px-Ten-sq%C3%BAat-a-way.jpg","title":"Tenskwatawa, painted from life in 1830","desc":"Tenskwatawa holding his medicine fire and string of beads, painted by George Catlin in 1830, more than twenty years after Prophetstown.","credit":"Painting by George Catlin, 1830, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ten-sq%C3%BAat-a-way.jpg","alt":"An oil portrait in profile of a seated man in a red headscarf and fur robe, holding a decorated stick."}},
{"id":"us-076","num":76,"category":"Removal","question":"Signed on 28 May 1830, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the president money and authority to negotiate treaties exchanging eastern Native land for territory west of the Mississippi.","questions":["The text of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> says nothing about force: it authorises an exchange of lands, and the removals that followed were carried out under treaties made with it.","Georgia had been promised in 1802 that Native title within the state would be extinguished, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the instrument that finally answered that promise."],"answer":"Indian Removal Act","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 May 1830</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Appropriated</span><span class=\"dt-v\">$500,000</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Indian Removal Act</b> was the statute signed on 28 May 1830 authorising the president to exchange land west of the Mississippi for the land of Native nations living within the eastern states.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its full title describes an exchange rather than a removal by force, and its operative words are permissive: it made it lawful for the president to have territory west of the river divided into districts and offered to nations willing to trade their homes for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It provided $500,000 to lay out those districts, to compensate for improvements left behind, to pay the cost of the journey and to supply one year’s subsistence in the west.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pressure behind it was older than the act: Georgia had ceded its western claims in 1802 in return for a federal promise to extinguish Native title within the state, and gold found in Georgia in the 1820s turned that promise into a demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Jefferson had pressed the same exchange after the Louisiana Purchase and few nations had accepted it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> What the act did was give the removals a legal shape, since with its authority the president could appoint agents to negotiate and then enforce treaties.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Cherokee treaty was made in 1835 at New Echota with a minority party acting against the expressed wishes of the nation, and the Senate ratified it in 1836.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Choctaw went west in 1831 and 1832, the Creek in 1836, the Chickasaw in 1837 and 1838, and the Cherokee in 1838 and 1839 under armed guard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By one account roughly seventy thousand people were moved out of the American South in the first four decades of the century, a process that began before the act and used it once it existed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The land they were sent to became Indian Territory, in what is now Oklahoma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Indian Removal Act","sources":["“Indian Removal Act,” DocsTeach, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.docsteach.org/documents/document/indian-removal-act. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Indian Removal,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN015. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture,” Trail of Tears National Historic Trail, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/trte/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“The History of the Cherokee Nation,” Cherokee Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.cherokee.org/about-the-nation/history/. [Open access]","Dianna Everett, “Indian Territory,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN018. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Register_of_Debates%2C_Senate%2C_21st_Congress%2C_1st_Session_-_%22The_Indians%22.gif","title":"The Senate debating removal, April 1830","desc":"A page of the Register of Debates recording the Senate's amendments to the removal bill and the vote of 28 to 19 that sent it to a third reading.","credit":"Library of Congress, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Register_of_Debates,_Senate,_21st_Congress,_1st_Session_-_%22The_Indians%22.gif","alt":"A printed page of two columns of small type headed Of Debates in Congress, dated April 26, 1830."}},
{"id":"us-077","num":77,"category":"Removal","question":"Asked in 1831 to restrain Georgia from enforcing its laws over Cherokee land, the Supreme Court decided <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by holding that it had no jurisdiction to hear the case at all.","questions":["The phrase domestic dependent nations comes from <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the Court denied that a nation inside American boundaries could sue as a foreign state.","Chief Justice Marshall wrote in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that the relation of such nations to the United States resembled that of a ward to his guardian."],"answer":"Cherokee Nation v. Georgia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1831</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">30 U.S. (5 Pet.) 1</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Cherokee Nation v. Georgia</b> was the case decided by the United States Supreme Court in 1831 in which the Cherokee asked for an injunction to stop Georgia enforcing its laws inside their territory.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bill was brought in the name of the Cherokee Nation as a foreign state owing allegiance to no other power, and was signed by the principal chief John Ross; Georgia had extended its laws over Cherokee land and the nation wanted the Court to say it could not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Constitution gives the Court original jurisdiction in suits between a state and a foreign state, so everything turned on whether the Cherokee were one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chief Justice John Marshall held that they were not: nations living inside the acknowledged boundaries of the United States could not with strict accuracy be called foreign, and might more correctly be denominated domestic dependent nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They occupied a territory to which the United States asserted a title independent of their will, he wrote, and their relation to it resembled that of a ward to his guardian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The phrase has had a long life, since it is the origin of the doctrine that Native nations retain a sovereignty that is real but limited, and of the trust responsibility later built on the guardian image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For the Cherokee in 1831 it meant only that the case was dismissed without any ruling on whether Georgia’s laws were lawful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> John Ross had hired the former attorney general William Wirt to argue for the nation, and the following year Wirt brought the question back in a form the Court could hear, using a missionary imprisoned under the same Georgia laws.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That case, Worcester v. Georgia, was decided for the Cherokee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither judgment stopped the removal, and the Court in 1831 had already framed the relationship in terms that later governments would use to justify deciding for Native nations rather than with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Cherokee Nation v. Georgia","sources":["<i>The Cherokee Nation v. The State of Georgia</i>, 30 U.S. (5 Pet.) 1 (1831), Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/30/1. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Indian Removal,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN015. [Open access]","“The History of the Cherokee Nation,” Cherokee Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.cherokee.org/about-the-nation/history/. [Open access]","<i>Worcester v. The State of Georgia</i>, 31 U.S. (6 Pet.) 515 (1832), Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/31/515. [Open access]","Rennard Strickland, “Cherokee (tribe),” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=CH014. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2a/William_Wirt_by_Henry_Inman_%28frameless%29.jpg","title":"William Wirt","desc":"The former attorney general whom the Cherokee principal chief John Ross engaged to argue the nation's case, first in 1831 and again the following year.","credit":"Painting by Henry Inman, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Wirt_by_Henry_Inman_%28frameless%29.jpg","alt":"An oil portrait of an elderly man in a black coat seated at a desk with a quill and papers."}},
{"id":"us-078","num":78,"category":"Removal","question":"Deciding for a missionary jailed under Georgia law, the judgment in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> declared Cherokee territory a distinct community in which the laws of Georgia could have no force.","questions":["Although the Cherokee won <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outright in 1832, Georgia and the federal government simply ignored the judgment and the removal went ahead.","The ruling in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is still cited as the foundation of tribal sovereignty in American law, which is why its practical failure in 1832 matters."],"answer":"Worcester v. Georgia","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Decided</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1832</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Reported</span><span class=\"dt-v\">31 U.S. (6 Pet.) 515</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Worcester v. Georgia</b> was the case decided by the United States Supreme Court in 1832 in which Georgia’s authority over Cherokee territory was struck down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reached the Court in a form the previous year’s case could not: Samuel Worcester was a missionary imprisoned under a Georgia statute requiring white residents in Cherokee country to hold a state licence, and as a citizen he could bring the question the nation itself had been refused standing to bring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chief Justice John Marshall wrote that the Cherokee nation was a distinct community occupying its own territory, with boundaries accurately described, in which the laws of Georgia could have no force and which the citizens of Georgia had no right to enter without Cherokee assent or the authority of treaties and acts of Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The whole intercourse between the United States and the nation, he held, was committed by the Constitution to the federal government alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a complete victory for the Cherokee case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It changed nothing on the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Georgia ignored the judgment, the federal government declined to enforce it, and neither recognised the sovereignty the Court had just affirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Three years later the president accepted a removal treaty signed at New Echota by a minority faction, and in 1838 soldiers were sent to enforce it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The case is nevertheless the foundation of tribal sovereignty in American law, and is still cited for the proposition that state law does not reach into Indian country, which makes the gap between what it said and what happened part of what it teaches.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A nation can win in the highest court of a republic and still be marched out of its homeland six years later.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Worcester v. Georgia","sources":["<i>Worcester v. The State of Georgia</i>, 31 U.S. (6 Pet.) 515 (1832), Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/31/515. [Open access]","<i>The Cherokee Nation v. The State of Georgia</i>, 30 U.S. (5 Pet.) 1 (1831), Legal Information Institute, Cornell Law School, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/30/1. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Indian Removal,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN015. [Open access]","“The History of the Cherokee Nation,” Cherokee Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.cherokee.org/about-the-nation/history/. [Open access]","Rennard Strickland, “Cherokee (tribe),” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=CH014. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/John_Marshall_%28NPG%29.jpg/1920px-John_Marshall_%28NPG%29.jpg","title":"Chief Justice John Marshall","desc":"Marshall wrote the Court's opinion in both Georgia cases, and in the second of them held that the laws of Georgia had no force in Cherokee territory.","credit":"Painting by James Lambdin after Henry Inman, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Marshall_%28NPG%29.jpg","alt":"An oil portrait of a grey-haired man in a black coat and white stock, resting a hand on a book."}},
{"id":"us-079","num":79,"category":"Removal","question":"Soldiers evicted more than 16,000 Cherokee from their homes in 1838 and marched them west on the journey remembered as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, on which thousands died.","questions":["The name <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> covers the forced journeys of five nations in the 1830s and 1840s, and the route and the losses were different for each of them.","Detachments on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were led by the Cherokee principal chief himself after the first water-borne parties went so badly that the army handed the movement over."],"answer":"Trail of Tears","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Cherokee removal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1838 – 1839</span><span class=\"dt-k\">New Echota</span><span class=\"dt-v\">treaty of 1835</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Trail of Tears</b> is the name given to the forced journeys west made by the nations removed from the American South in the 1830s and 1840s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It covers five removals with different routes, seasons and losses, and the enslaved people taken west with their owners suffered them too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Cherokee removal is the one most often meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It followed the Treaty of New Echota, signed in 1835 by a small faction against the expressed wishes of the nation and ratified by the Senate in 1836, which tore the Cherokee into a treaty party and a large majority who rejected it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In May 1838 troops and state militia moved into the Cherokee country in Tennessee, Alabama, North Carolina and Georgia, evicted more than 16,000 people and held them in round-up and emigration camps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Three detachments were sent west by water that June, and the difficulties were bad enough that General Winfield Scott agreed the principal chief John Ross should take charge of the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ross moved fourteen detachments overland between August and December 1838, and the removal was finished by the end of March 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The National Park Service records that more than a thousand Cherokee died on the journey, chiefly the old, the young and the sick, that hundreds deserted, and that an unknown number, perhaps several thousand, died of its consequences; the Oklahoma Historical Society puts the loss at nearly one Cherokee in four.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The removals of the Choctaw in 1831 and 1832, the Creek in 1836, the Chickasaw in 1837 and 1838 and the Seminole through the same decade were each their own journey of the same kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Cherokee Nation rebuilt itself in the west and is today the largest tribal government in the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Trail of Tears","sources":["“History &amp; Culture,” Trail of Tears National Historic Trail, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/trte/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Trail of Tears (term),” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=TR003. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Indian Removal,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN015. [Open access]","“The History of the Cherokee Nation,” Cherokee Nation, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.cherokee.org/about-the-nation/history/. [Open access]","Rennard Strickland, “Cherokee (tribe),” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=CH014. [Open access]"],"difficulty":1,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Cherokee_Removal_Map.jpg/1920px-Cherokee_Removal_Map.jpg","title":"The routes laid out at a memorial","desc":"The routes of the removal set into the paving at a Trail of Tears memorial, running from the southeastern homelands to Indian Territory.","credit":"Photograph by It's Only Make Believe, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cherokee_Removal_Map.jpg","alt":"A large map of the southern United States inlaid in stone paving, with coloured routes running west, and dark monuments beyond it."}},
{"id":"us-080","num":80,"category":"Removal","question":"Cherokee, Choctaw, Chickasaw, Creek and Seminole are together called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a name that replaced an older phrase carrying a judgement about which nations counted as civilised.","questions":["Americans of the mid-19th century called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> civilised because they appeared to be adopting Christianity, written constitutions and plantation agriculture.","Every one of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resisted removal in its own way, and the Seminole fought a seven-year war rather than leave Florida."],"answer":"Five Tribes","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Removed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1831 – 1842</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Choctaw first</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1831 – 1832</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Five Tribes</b> are the Cherokee, Choctaw, Chickasaw, Creek and Seminole nations, removed from the American South to Indian Territory in the 1830s and 1840s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name in general use until recently was the Five Civilized Tribes, a phrase that came in about the middle of the 19th century and was used most in Indian Territory and Oklahoma, sometimes by the nations themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It called them civilised because they appeared to be assimilating: taking up horticulture and plantation agriculture, Christianity, written constitutions and, in the Cherokee case, a written language of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The judgement inside the phrase is what has made it fall out of use, since it rates nations by their distance from Anglo-American norms and implies the others were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They had cultural, political and economic connections with one another before removal, and the shared name records the shared experience of being moved as much as anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The removals were not one event.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Choctaw went first, under the Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek, in 1831 and 1832; the Creek followed in 1836 after divisions that had already led to the killing of the chief who signed away their Georgia land; the Chickasaw went in 1837 and 1838 and the Cherokee under guard in 1838 and 1839.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Seminole resistance to the treaties forcing them out of Florida produced a war that ran from 1835 until 1842, and those who retreated deep into the Everglades were never wholly defeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Slaveholding was itself among the practices Americans counted as civilised, and the Seminole had long taken others in, adopted whites and people escaping slavery among them, so that African words, stories and dress became part of Seminole life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In Indian Territory each nation rebuilt its government, and their descendants hold five of the largest tribal governments in the United States today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Five Tribes","sources":["Andrew K. Frank, “Five Civilized Tribes,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=FI011. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Indian Removal,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN015. [Open access]","“The Five Civilized Tribes: Who Are They?,” Ocmulgee Mounds National Historical Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/ocmu/learn/historyculture/upload/Accessible-Five-Civilized-Tribes.pdf. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture,” Trail of Tears National Historic Trail, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/trte/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Dianna Everett, “Indian Territory,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN018. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Cherokee_National_Capitol.jpg/1920px-Cherokee_National_Capitol.jpg","title":"The Cherokee National Capitol at Tahlequah","desc":"The capitol built in Indian Territory after the removal, where the Cherokee Nation's government sat from 1869.","credit":"Photograph by Caleb Long, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cherokee_National_Capitol.jpg","alt":"A two-storey red brick building with arched windows and a pedimented porch, behind bare trees and a stone fountain."}},
{"id":"us-081","num":81,"category":"Removal","question":"The land west of Arkansas set aside for the removed nations became known as <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and it was never organised as a territory of the United States in the ordinary way.","questions":["Each nation held its own country inside <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under its own government, laws and courts, which is what made the later opening of the land so hard to justify.","Although <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had no governor, no legislature and no delegate in Congress, the name was in general use for the whole area west of Arkansas."],"answer":"Indian Territory","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">In use</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 1830 – 1907</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land run</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Indian Territory</b> was the name given to the country west of Arkansas and Missouri to which the nations of the American South were removed from the 1830s onwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was a description rather than a legal creation: unlike Kansas or Nebraska it was never organised as a territory of the United States, with a governor, a legislature and a delegate in Congress, and for most of its life it had no federal government of its own at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What it had instead was the governments of the nations themselves, each holding its own country under its own constitution, laws and courts, which is the arrangement the removal treaties had promised in exchange for the eastern homelands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Cherokee, Choctaw, Chickasaw, Creek and Seminole held the eastern part; nations removed from the Great Lakes and the plains were settled in the middle, and the western end was assigned to the southern plains nations after the Civil War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The boundaries shrank steadily as each of those assignments was made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The Civil War was the first great blow, since parts of each of the Five Tribes sided with the Confederacy and the treaties imposed afterwards took land as the price.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The second was the railroads, whose grants through the territory were contingent on Native title being cleared, which gave powerful interests a direct reason to want the nations dissolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 1889 the federal government opened the Unassigned Lands to settlers in a land run, and the following year Congress created Oklahoma Territory out of the western half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The eastern half remained Indian Territory until 1907, when the two were joined and admitted as the state of Oklahoma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nations’ governments were dissolved as part of that settlement, and rebuilding them took most of the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Indian Territory","sources":["Dianna Everett, “Indian Territory,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN018. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture,” Trail of Tears National Historic Trail, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/trte/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Indian Removal,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN015. [Open access]","Clara Sue Kidwell, “Allotment,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=AL011. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Five Civilized Tribes,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=FI011. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/U.S._General_Land_Office_Indian_Territory_1879_UTA.jpg/1920px-U.S._General_Land_Office_Indian_Territory_1879_UTA.jpg","title":"Indian Territory, 1879","desc":"The General Land Office's map of Indian Territory, showing the Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw, Osage, Kiowa, Comanche and Cheyenne and Arapaho lands into which removed nations were resettled.","credit":"United States Department of the Interior. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._General_Land_Office_Indian_Territory_1879_UTA.jpg","alt":"A coloured 1879 government map of Indian Territory, the area of present-day Oklahoma, divided into named tribal nations and reservations."}},
{"id":"us-082","num":82,"category":"Removal","question":"Three wars fought in Florida between 1817 and 1858 over removal are together called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the last of them ended without any treaty being signed.","questions":["Rather than leave Florida under the removal treaties, the Seminole fought the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, of which the second was the longest and costliest of the American Indian wars.","People escaping slavery had joined the Seminole for generations, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turned partly on who among them could be reclaimed as property."],"answer":"Seminole Wars","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Fought</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1817 – 1858</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Second war</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1835 – 1842</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Seminole Wars</b> were the three conflicts fought in Florida between 1817 and 1858 between the United States and the Seminole, over the removal of the Seminole from the peninsula.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Seminole had formed in the 18th century from Muscogee, Hitchiti and probably Yamasee and Yuchi people who separated from the Lower Creek towns, joined by survivors of the older Florida nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They took others in readily, including adopted white people and people escaping slavery in the American colonies, so that African words, stories and dress became part of Seminole life — and so that the question of who could be reclaimed as property ran underneath every negotiation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The first war, in 1817 and 1818, was fought while Florida was still Spanish and ended with the United States taking the province.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> After the Indian Removal Act some chiefs submitted to removal in the hope of avoiding war and others refused outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The resistance of those who refused became the Second Seminole War, fought from 1835 to 1842, which the National Park Service calls one of the deadliest and costliest of the American Indian wars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In November 1837 a party of Seminole leaders and their families came to Fort Peyton under a flag of truce for a parley and were surrounded and taken prisoner; more than 230 were eventually confined in the old Spanish fort at St Augustine, where illness was constant through their two months there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Among them was the war leader Osceola, who was not a chief but was the most influential figure of the war’s early phase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The capture under a truce caused an outcry among Americans as well as among the Seminole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some Seminole retreated deep into the Everglades and were never wholly defeated; others were removed to Indian Territory, where their descendants live beside their Creek relatives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Seminole Wars","sources":["“Seminole Incarceration,” Castillo de San Marcos National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/casa/learn/historyculture/seminole-incarceration.htm. [Open access]","“The Five Civilized Tribes: Who Are They?,” Ocmulgee Mounds National Historical Park, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/ocmu/learn/historyculture/upload/Accessible-Five-Civilized-Tribes.pdf. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Seminole (tribe),” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=SE011. [Open access]","Andrew K. Frank, “Indian Removal,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN015. [Open access]","Dianna Everett, “Indian Territory,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN018. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Osceola_by_Catlin.jpg","title":"Osceola, painted by George Catlin in 1838","desc":"Catlin painted the Seminole war leader at Fort Moultrie in January 1838, weeks after he was seized under a flag of truce and shortly before he died in captivity.","credit":"George Catlin. CC BY 2.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osceola_by_Catlin.jpg","alt":"An oil portrait of a Seminole man in a red turban with a white and black plume, wearing silver gorgets and a beaded sash."}},
{"id":"us-083","num":83,"category":"Removal","question":"The killing and dispossession of California’s Native peoples in the quarter-century after 1848 is now generally called the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the population fell by roughly four fifths.","questions":["What happened in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was not carried out by an unacknowledged policy but by ordinary citizens acting through militias, petitions and votes at every level of government.","Estimates of California’s Native population before the gold rush differ widely, but every one of them agrees that the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> left about 30,000 people alive."],"answer":"California genocide","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Chiefly</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1846 – 1873</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold found</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 1848</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>California genocide</b> is the name now generally given to the killing, enslavement and dispossession of California’s Native peoples in the quarter-century after the American conquest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gold was found at Sutter’s Mill in January 1848, and within a year tens of thousands of miners were crossing the Sierra; California was admitted as a state in 1850.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The National Library of Medicine records that the influx brought diseases that killed thousands and that miners also committed widespread violence against Native villages throughout California and Oregon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Estimates of the population before the gold rush differ: the Muwekma Ohlone Tribe gives about 150,000, one federal source gives 100,000 for 1848 and another gives 310,000 for 1769.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They agree on what was left, which was about 30,000.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The mechanism was not a hidden policy but an open one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Muwekma Ohlone Tribe, whose own country this was, writes that unlike the destruction of European Jewry it was not the work of an unacknowledged government ideology but a sequence of actions by ordinary citizens empowered and placed in authority by a democratic process, and points readers to the historians who have documented it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> State laws allowed the indenture of Native people and public money paid for expeditions against them, and the killing was done by militias, by volunteers and by the army.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last of the California wars was fought in the lava country on the Oregon line from November 1872 to June 1873, when between 50 and 60 Modoc warriors held out against as many as a thousand troops for six months.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> By its end the period the name covers was over, and the survivors held almost none of the country their nations had lived in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"California genocide","sources":["“The Untold Story of Native American Genocide in California,” Muwekma Ohlone Tribe of the San Francisco Bay Area, January 2023, accessed 6 September 2026, https://muwekma.org/blog/2023/january/the-untold-story-of-native-american-genocide-in-california.html. [Open access]","“History &amp; Culture,” California National Historic Trail, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/cali/learn/historyculture/index.htm. [Open access]","“1848: California Gold Rush Brings Miners and Diseases,” <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples’ Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, National Library of Medicine, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/311.html. [Open access]","“1860: California Native Population Plummets after Gold Rush,” <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples’ Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, National Library of Medicine, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/328.html. [Open access]","“Modoc War,” Lava Beds National Monument, National Park Service, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.nps.gov/labe/learn/historyculture/modoc-war.htm. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/%22Protecting_The_Settlers%22_Illustration_by_JR_Browne_for_his_work_%22The_Indians_Of_California%22_1864.jpg","title":"\"Protecting the Settlers\", 1864","desc":"An engraving from J. Ross Browne's The Indians of California, a federal inspector's published indictment of the militia killings the state had paid for.","credit":"John Ross Browne. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Protecting_The_Settlers%22_Illustration_by_JR_Browne_for_his_work_%22The_Indians_Of_California%22_1864.jpg","alt":"A wood engraving of armed men on a ridge firing down by moonlight into a sleeping village of conical dwellings."}},
{"id":"us-084","num":84,"category":"Removal","question":"Land held in trust by the federal government as a permanent homeland for a nation is called a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and title to it is held by the United States rather than the tribe.","questions":["A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be created by treaty, by executive order, by statute or by administrative action, which is why no two of them have quite the same legal history.","The Bureau of Indian Affairs today administers about 56 million acres held in trust, most of it organised as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or several of them."],"answer":"Indian reservation","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Policy era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1850s – 1887</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Held in trust</span><span class=\"dt-v\">c. 56 million acres</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>Indian reservation</b> is an area of land reserved for a tribe or tribes as a permanent homeland, to which the federal government holds title in trust on behalf of the tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is one of the three kinds of reserved federal land in the United States, alongside military and public lands, and it can be created by treaty, by executive order, by federal statute or by administrative action — which is why the legal history of each is different.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Reservations grew out of the treaty system: a nation ceded a large territory and retained a smaller one, so that what a reservation holds is not land given by the government but land never given up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That distinction matters for everything that follows, since rights the treaties did not surrender are held to survive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Bureau of Indian Affairs today administers roughly 56 million acres of trust land for the federally recognised tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> As a policy the reservation was the successor to removal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Once there was no longer an unsettled west to remove nations to, the government confined them instead to defined tracts under the supervision of agents, and the Office of Indian Affairs built a network of local agencies and regional superintendencies to run them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Conditions were often bad, rations and provisions inadequate, and the reservation is the setting of much of what follows in this collection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The policy was itself abandoned within a generation: from 1887 the Dawes Act set about breaking reservations up into individual allotments, on the reasoning that communal land was what kept Native people apart from American life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Reservations survived that attempt, and today the tribes maintain them against encroachment by the states as well as by private interests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Indian reservation","sources":["“Frequently Asked Questions,” Bureau of Indian Affairs, U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","“American Indian Treaties,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/treaties. [Open access]","“Bureau of Indian Affairs,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/bia. [Open access]","Kerry C. Kelly, “Maps of Indian Territory, the Dawes Act, and Will Rogers’ Enrollment Case File,” DocsTeach, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/fed-indian-policy. [Open access]","“Dawes Act (1887),” Milestone Documents, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/dawes-act. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Map_Showing_the_Location_of_the_Indian_Reservations_1885_UTA.jpg/1920px-Map_Showing_the_Location_of_the_Indian_Reservations_1885_UTA.jpg","title":"Indian reservations, 1885","desc":"The Office of Indian Affairs mapped its reservations two years before the Dawes Act began breaking them into individual allotments.","credit":"Paul Brodie. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Showing_the_Location_of_the_Indian_Reservations_1885_UTA.jpg","alt":"An 1885 map of the United States with reservations shaded in orange, most of them west of the Missouri River."}},
{"id":"us-085","num":85,"category":"Removal","question":"The federal officer who lived among a nation and handled its dealings with Washington was the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the office was as open to corruption as any in American government.","questions":["The Office of Indian Affairs ran its business through local field offices, each under an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, with regional superintendencies above them supervising several at once.","Because rations, annuities and land payments all passed through his hands, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> had opportunities that the reformers of the 1860s spent years complaining about."],"answer":"Indian agent","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Office created</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1824</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Superintendencies</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1819 – 1878</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>Indian agent</b> was the federal officer posted to a nation or a group of nations to conduct the government’s business with them, distribute annuities and rations, and report to Washington.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Until 1824 Indian affairs were handled directly by the Secretary of War, apart from the government trading houses run by the Office of Indian Trade between 1806 and 1822.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Bureau of Indian Affairs was established administratively within the War Department on 11 March 1824 by Secretary John C. Calhoun, and Congress created it in law, with the post of Commissioner of Indian Affairs, in 1832.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1849 it moved to the newly created Interior Department, where it remains, and it was known variously as the Indian office, the Indian bureau and the Indian service before the present name was adopted in 1947.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its work on the ground was done through field offices, called agencies, with superintendencies above them covering a territory or state and supervising the agents within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The agent’s position rested on the treaty system, which is what gave him something to administer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A treaty was a negotiated pact between sovereigns, signed by both sides and requiring the approval of Congress, and it typically set a boundary, ceded land, and promised payments and goods in return.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those promised payments were the agent’s to hand out, along with rations, farming equipment and the money owed under land sales, and the opportunities that created were notorious.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The records the agencies generated are now among the largest bodies of material on Native America in the National Archives, and they are organised by tribe and agency rather than by subject, so finding anything means knowing which agent held the ground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The system ended in its treaty form in 1871, though the agencies themselves long outlived it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Indian agent","sources":["“Frequently Asked Questions,” Bureau of Indian Affairs, U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","“Bureau of Indian Affairs,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/bia. [Open access]","Kerry C. Kelly, “Maps of Indian Territory, the Dawes Act, and Will Rogers’ Enrollment Case File,” DocsTeach, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/fed-indian-policy. [Open access]","“American Indian Treaties,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/treaties. [Open access]","Dianna Everett, “Indian Territory,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=IN018. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Fort_Spokane%2C_Agency_Period._Major_Anderson%2C_BIA_Agent%2C_in_his_office._%28c2c9ea84-6a98-4bf4-a571-017ba54b0952%29.jpg/1920px-Fort_Spokane%2C_Agency_Period._Major_Anderson%2C_BIA_Agent%2C_in_his_office._%28c2c9ea84-6a98-4bf4-a571-017ba54b0952%29.jpg","title":"An Indian agent's office, Fort Spokane","desc":"The agent sat between his tribe and the federal government, controlling rations, annuities, schooling and police, and reporting to the Office of Indian Affairs.","credit":"NPS photo. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Spokane,_Agency_Period._Major_Anderson,_BIA_Agent,_in_his_office._%28c2c9ea84-6a98-4bf4-a571-017ba54b0952%29.jpg","alt":"A photograph of a man in a dark suit seated at a desk in a wallpapered office, with a chair and a machine on a table by the window."}},
{"id":"us-086","num":86,"category":"Removal","question":"A rider attached to an appropriations bill in 1871 brought about the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, after which no nation within the United States would be contracted with as an independent power.","questions":["Because the House had no say in ratifying treaties, its refusal to keep funding them forced the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the price of the year’s Indian appropriations.","Nearly a century of agreements had preceded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the existing treaties were left standing, so the change was about the future rather than the past."],"answer":"end of treaty-making","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Enacted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1871</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">nearly 100 years of treaties</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>end of treaty-making</b> came in 1871, when the House of Representatives ceased to recognise the nations within the United States as independent powers with whom the government could contract by treaty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For nearly a century before that the relationship had been conducted in the form of international agreements: negotiated between the parties, nearly identical in form to the Treaty of Paris that ended the Revolutionary War, signed by both sides and requiring the approval of Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those treaties set boundaries and prescribed conditions of behaviour between the parties, and non-tribal citizens were required to hold a passport to cross sovereign Indian land.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> From 1832 the nations had been understood, in the phrase the Supreme Court had supplied, as domestic dependent tribes, and treaties with them still had to go to Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The change of 1871 ended the practice outright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Part of the reason was institutional rather than principled: the Senate ratifies treaties and the House does not, so the House had spent decades voting money for obligations it had no part in making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Attaching the change to an appropriations bill was the way to force it, since the House could refuse to fund obligations it had taken no part in making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The existing treaties were left in force and remain so, which is why treaty rights are still litigated today and why the federal trust responsibility rests on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What ended was the making of new ones, and the agreements that replaced them were statutes and executive orders — instruments the government could write without the other party’s signature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Within sixteen years the same Congress passed the Dawes Act, which broke up the reservations those treaties had reserved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"end of treaty-making","sources":["“American Indian Treaties,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/treaties. [Open access]","“Frequently Asked Questions,” Bureau of Indian Affairs, U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","“Bureau of Indian Affairs,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/bia. [Open access]","“Dawes Act (1887),” Milestone Documents, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/dawes-act. [Open access]","Kerry C. Kelly, “Maps of Indian Territory, the Dawes Act, and Will Rogers’ Enrollment Case File,” DocsTeach, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/fed-indian-policy. [Open access]"],"difficulty":4,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Photograph_of_General_William_T._Sherman_and_Commissioners_in_Council_with_Indian_Chiefs_at_Fort_Laramie%2C_Wyoming%2C_ca._1_-_NARA_-_531079.jpg/1920px-Photograph_of_General_William_T._Sherman_and_Commissioners_in_Council_with_Indian_Chiefs_at_Fort_Laramie%2C_Wyoming%2C_ca._1_-_NARA_-_531079.jpg","title":"The Fort Laramie treaty council, 1868","desc":"General Sherman and the Indian Peace Commission in council with Lakota, Dakota and Arapaho leaders; three years later Congress ended treaty-making altogether.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photograph_of_General_William_T._Sherman_and_Commissioners_in_Council_with_Indian_Chiefs_at_Fort_Laramie,_Wyoming,_ca._1_-_NARA_-_531079.jpg","alt":"A photograph of a council under an open canvas tent, commissioners in dark coats seated facing a group of blanketed men on the ground."}},
{"id":"us-087","num":87,"category":"Removal","question":"Passed in 1887 to break reservations into individual holdings, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> treated Native people as individuals rather than as members of nations.","questions":["Under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a head of a family received a quarter-section, and what was left over after everyone had been allotted was sold as surplus.","The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exempted the Five Tribes and several other nations by name, but later acts extended it to them within a decade."],"answer":"Dawes Act","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Approved</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 February 1887</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">24 Stat. 388–91</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Dawes Act</b>, approved on 8 February 1887, authorised the president to break up reservation land held in common and parcel it out in individual allotments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its formal title is an act to provide for the allotment of lands in severalty to Indians on the various reservations, and severalty is the point of it: it treated Native people as individuals rather than as members of tribes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reasoning was assimilationist and was stated openly — that a person who dressed like a white American and was responsible for his own farm would gradually drop his Indian-ness, and that the government could then stop overseeing Indian welfare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The allotments were a quarter-section to each head of a family, an eighth to each single person over eighteen and to each orphan under eighteen, and a sixteenth to other single people under eighteen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Section 8 exempted by name the Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw, Seminole, Osage, Miami and Peoria, and Sac and Fox in Indian Territory, the Seneca of New York and a strip in Nebraska.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Those exemptions did not last.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1893 President Cleveland appointed a commission under the act’s author, the retired senator Henry Dawes, to negotiate with the Five Tribes, and the acts that followed allotted their common land in exchange for the abolition of their governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To receive an allotment a person had to enrol with the Office of Indian Affairs, and the resulting Dawes Rolls became the definitive record of membership for those nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The land allotted was often desert or near-desert, unsuitable for farming; those who wanted to farm frequently could not afford tools, animals or seed; and children who inherited allotments were often away at boarding schools and could not work them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where allotments were divided among several heirs the holdings became too small to farm at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Dawes Act","sources":["“Dawes Act (1887),” Milestone Documents, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/dawes-act. [Open access]","Clara Sue Kidwell, “Allotment,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=AL011. [Open access]","Kent Carter, “Dawes Commission,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=DA018. [Open access]","“Frequently Asked Questions,” Bureau of Indian Affairs, U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","Kerry C. Kelly, “Maps of Indian Territory, the Dawes Act, and Will Rogers’ Enrollment Case File,” DocsTeach, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/education/lessons/fed-indian-policy. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Dawes_Act_-_First_Page.jpg/1920px-Dawes_Act_-_First_Page.jpg","title":"The Dawes Act, 1887","desc":"The engrossed first page of the General Allotment Act, which authorised the President to survey any reservation and allot it in severalty.","credit":"49th Congress of the U.S.. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawes_Act_-_First_Page.jpg","alt":"The handwritten first page of an 1887 Act of Congress, headed \"Forty-Ninth Congress of the United States of America\"."}},
{"id":"us-089","num":89,"category":"Removal","question":"Between 1819 and 1969 the federal government ran or oversaw 408 institutions of a kind called an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose declared purpose was to assimilate Native children.","questions":["A federal investigation published in 2022 identified burial sites at about 53 of the schools and expected the number to rise as work on the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> system continued.","Children at an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> were separated from their families, forbidden their own languages and given manual labour dressed up as vocational training."],"answer":"Indian boarding school","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">System ran</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1819 – 1969</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Federal schools</span><span class=\"dt-v\">408</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"An <b>Indian boarding school</b> was one of the institutions through which the United States removed Native children from their families in order to assimilate them into the dominant culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A federal investigation ordered by the Secretary of the Interior in June 2021 and published in May 2022 found that between 1819 and 1969 the system consisted of 408 federal schools across 37 states or then-territories, including 21 in Alaska and 7 in Hawaii, some of them operating on more than one site, so that the 408 schools comprised 431 specific sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those were only the federal ones; several hundred Indian boarding schools of various kinds existed across the country, many run by religious bodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The report states its central finding plainly: that the United States directly targeted American Indian, Alaska Native and Native Hawaiian children in pursuit of a policy of cultural assimilation that coincided with Indian territorial dispossession.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also identified marked or unmarked burial sites at approximately 53 of the schools, and expected that number to rise as the investigation continued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The schools grew out of the reservation system, and the government began opening them on reservations during the 1860s and 1870s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reformers of the period presented them as a humane alternative to war, which is how the policy was argued for at the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Children were separated from their families and communities, taught English and forbidden their own languages, given the manual trades and domestic skills the schools called industrial education, and drilled in cultural norms different from those of their kin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The report records that older Indian children were made to punish younger ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 408 schools it identified, about 90 still operate as educational facilities of some kind, though not all of them board children or receive federal support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Indian boarding school","sources":["Bryan Newland, <i>Federal Indian Boarding School Initiative Investigative Report</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Department of the Interior, May 2022), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/sites/default/files/dup/inline-files/bsi_investigative_report_may_2022_508.pdf. [Open access]","“Brief History of the School,” Carlisle Indian School Digital Resource Center, Dickinson College, accessed 6 September 2026, https://carlisleindian.dickinson.edu/page/brief-history-school. [Open access]","“Frequently Asked Questions,” Bureau of Indian Affairs, U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","“Bureau of Indian Affairs,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/bia. [Open access]","“Dawes Act (1887),” Milestone Documents, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/dawes-act. [Open access]"],"difficulty":2,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Girls_dormitory_at_the_Chemawa_Indian_School_near_Salem%2C_Oregon_%283226132221%29.jpg/1920px-Girls_dormitory_at_the_Chemawa_Indian_School_near_Salem%2C_Oregon_%283226132221%29.jpg","title":"The girls' dormitory at Chemawa, Oregon","desc":"Chemawa, opened in 1880, was among the oldest off-reservation boarding schools, where children lived under military discipline far from their families.","credit":"OSU Special Collections & Archives : Commons. No restrictions. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Girls_dormitory_at_the_Chemawa_Indian_School_near_Salem,_Oregon_%283226132221%29.jpg","alt":"A hand-coloured postcard of a large white institutional building with pupils in long dresses gathered on the lawn in front of it."}},
{"id":"us-090","num":90,"category":"Removal","question":"Opened in Pennsylvania in 1879 in a disused army barracks, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was the first federally run boarding school built away from any reservation.","questions":["The argument for placing the school far from any reservation was that distance would speed assimilation, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> became the model for more than two dozen of them.","Of the 7,793 young people who passed through the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, 232 died while enrolled and about 10 per cent were given diplomas."],"answer":"Carlisle Indian Industrial School","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Open</span><span class=\"dt-v\">October 1879 – August 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Passed through</span><span class=\"dt-v\">7,793 people</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"The <b>Carlisle Indian Industrial School</b> operated in Carlisle, Pennsylvania from October 1879 to August 1918, and was the first federally managed off-reservation boarding school for Native children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was run by the Office of Indian Affairs within the Interior Department, and its founder Richard Henry Pratt was superintendent from 1879 until 1904.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Boarding schools already existed on reservations and near Native communities; Pratt’s argument for an off-reservation school was that the greater distance between children and their families would speed assimilation, and that was the centrepiece of his case for opening it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The site he obtained was the Carlisle Barracks, an army installation largely vacant for a decade, which the Army handed to the Interior Department in the summer of 1879.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carlisle became the model for more than two dozen off-reservation schools opened between 1879 and 1910.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The school’s own records, now digitised, allow it to be counted precisely.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In all 7,793 individuals spent time there, drawn from about 200 tribal communities, though half came from just eight — the largest groups Sioux, Chippewa, Oneida and Seneca — with 127 from Alaska, 60 from Puerto Rico and two from the Philippines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Enrolled students arrived at an average age of 16, about 14 per cent at twelve or younger; roughly a fifth stayed less than a year and half stayed more than three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the enrolled students 232 died while in attendance, about three per cent, at an average age of a little over seventeen; 779 received diplomas and another 480 trade certificates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sixteen per cent are recorded as having left without permission at least once, and when the Army reclaimed the barracks as a hospital in 1918 the school was wound up in seven weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"Carlisle Indian Industrial School","sources":["“Brief History of the School,” Carlisle Indian School Digital Resource Center, Dickinson College, accessed 6 September 2026, https://carlisleindian.dickinson.edu/page/brief-history-school. [Open access]","Bryan Newland, <i>Federal Indian Boarding School Initiative Investigative Report</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Department of the Interior, May 2022), accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/sites/default/files/dup/inline-files/bsi_investigative_report_may_2022_508.pdf. [Open access]","“Welcome,” Carlisle Indian School Digital Resource Center, Dickinson College, accessed 6 September 2026, https://carlisleindian.dickinson.edu/. [Open access]","“Frequently Asked Questions,” Bureau of Indian Affairs, U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","“Bureau of Indian Affairs,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/bia. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/Chiricahua_Apaches_Four_Months_After_Arriving_at_Carlisle.jpg","title":"Chiricahua Apache pupils at Carlisle, 1887","desc":"The school's own \"after\" photograph, made four months after the children arrived, with their new English names printed beneath.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiricahua_Apaches_Four_Months_After_Arriving_at_Carlisle.jpg","alt":"A studio photograph of eleven Apache children in school uniform, their names printed below the mount."}},
{"id":"us-088","num":88,"category":"Removal","question":"Breaking commonly held reservation land into individual parcels, a policy called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, cost Native nations most of the land they still held between 1887 and 1934.","questions":["Whatever was left after every enrolled person had been given a parcel was declared surplus, which is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> opened reservation land to settlers.","In Indian Territory alone the commission surveying land for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> appraised 19,525,966 acres of tribal land and allotted 15,794,000 of them to enrolled individuals."],"answer":"allotment","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Policy era</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1887 – 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land run</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1889</span></div>","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","abstract":"<b>Allotment</b> was the federal policy, running from 1887 until 1934, of dividing commonly held reservation land into parcels assigned to individual Native people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The mechanism was the same everywhere: a roll of eligible people was compiled, each was given a parcel of a size fixed by statute or agreement, and whatever land was left over was declared surplus and opened to non-Native settlement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The surplus is where most of the loss happened, since a nation’s land was measured against its enrolled population rather than against what it held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Indian Territory the process ran through a commission appointed in 1893 under the retired senator Henry Dawes, which by 1907 had processed enrolment applications from more than 250,000 people and approved just over 101,000 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It surveyed and appraised 19,525,966 acres of tribal land and allotted 15,794,000, ruling on 10,952 contested claims along the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Resistance was constant and largely unavailing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Five Tribes opposed allotment from the start, in part because railroad grants through their country were contingent on Native title being cleared and would fall in the moment their governments were dissolved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Cherokee refused the first commission’s proposals outright; smaller nations that agreed took 80- or 160-acre allotments and sold hundreds of thousands of surplus acres to the government for sums in the hundreds of thousands of dollars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In 1889 the government opened the Unassigned Lands to settlers in a land run and created Oklahoma Territory the year after, which set the pattern for the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where an individual would not meet the government’s conditions his allotment could be sold to a non-Native buyer, and by these routes the nations lost vast acreages that allotment had been presented as protecting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","citation":"","answerText":"allotment","sources":["“Dawes Act (1887),” Milestone Documents, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/dawes-act. [Open access]","Kent Carter, “Dawes Commission,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=DA018. [Open access]","Clara Sue Kidwell, “Allotment,” <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, Oklahoma Historical Society, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=AL011. [Open access]","“Frequently Asked Questions,” Bureau of Indian Affairs, U.S. Department of the Interior, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","“Bureau of Indian Affairs,” Native American Heritage, National Archives and Records Administration, accessed 6 September 2026, https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/bia. [Open access]"],"difficulty":3,"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/Indian_Land_for_Sale.jpg","title":"\"Indian Land for Sale\", 1911","desc":"The Department of the Interior advertised the surplus left over after allotment, listing acreages and average prices per acre by state.","credit":"United States Department of the Interior. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Land_for_Sale.jpg","alt":"A 1911 poster headed \"INDIAN LAND FOR SALE\", with a photograph of a man in feathered headdress and a table of acreages and prices."}},
{"id":"us-091","num":91,"category":"United States","question":"Hubert Work commissioned an independent survey of Indian affairs whose 1928 report, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, condemned the boarding schools and reshaped federal policy.","questions":["Investigators found children fed on eleven cents a day and working half of every school day, and said so in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, published in 1928.","Congress had never audited what its Indian schools were doing until the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called their provision for children grossly inadequate and pushed policy toward day schools."],"answer":"Meriam Report","answerText":"Meriam Report","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Surveyed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1926 – 1927</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Published</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1928</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Meriam Report</b> is the popular name of <i>The Problem of Indian Administration</i>, the survey of federal Indian policy that Secretary of the Interior Hubert Work commissioned and the Institute for Government Research published in 1928.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A staff of ten specialists, directed by Lewis Meriam, spent seven months visiting agencies, schools and hospitals, and reported on health, education, economic conditions, family life, law and missionary work in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its judgement on the boarding schools was blunt: the provisions for the children in them were, it said, grossly inadequate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Indian Service was trying to feed each child on eleven cents a day, and the diet was deficient in quantity, quality and variety, lacking above all the milk, fruit and green vegetables that protect against tuberculosis and trachoma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Dormitories were crowded materially beyond their capacity, sleeping porches shut light and air out of the rooms behind them, and the medical service given the children was below a reasonable standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The survey also found that the schools were frankly supported in part by the labour of their pupils, who from above the fourth grade worked half of every day and studied the other half.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Much of that work, the report noted, would have been prohibited by the child labour laws of many states, and the long day left almost no free time for recreation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of this was new: the Office of Indian Affairs had been told in 1897 that manual training was all its pupils needed, and by 1903 was ordering health screening because crowded, unventilated dormitories were killing children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What was new was that an independent body had said it in public, with figures, and recommended that young children be taught in day schools near home and sent away only after adolescence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The report supplied much of the argument for the Indian Reorganization Act of 1934, and its findings were confirmed nearly a century later by the Interior Department's own investigation of the school system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"sources":["Lewis Meriam et al., \"General Summary of Findings and Recommendations,\" ch. 1 of <i>The Problem of Indian Administration</i> (Baltimore: Johns Hopkins Press, 1928), https://narf.org/nill/documents/merriam/d_meriam_chapter1_summary_of_findings.pdf. [Open access]","National Indian Law Library, \"Meriam Report: The Problem of Indian Administration (1928),\" Native American Rights Fund, https://narf.org/nill/resources/meriam.html. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1903: Overcrowding, Poor Ventilation Contribute to Deaths in Boarding Schools,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/398.html. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1897: Indian Boarding Schools Teach Manual Labor,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/385.html. [Open access]","Bryan Newland, <i>Federal Indian Boarding School Initiative Investigative Report</i> (Washington, DC: U.S. Department of the Interior, 2022), https://www.bia.gov/sites/default/files/dup/inline-files/bsi_investigative_report_may_2022_508.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Art_Class%2C_Phoenix_Indian_School%2C_Arizona%2C_06-1900_-_NARA_-_518923.jpg/1920px-Art_Class%2C_Phoenix_Indian_School%2C_Arizona%2C_06-1900_-_NARA_-_518923.jpg","title":"The art department, Phoenix Indian School, 1900","desc":"An Indian Service photograph of one of its off-reservation boarding schools, taken 28 years before the Meriam survey reported on how the pupils actually lived.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Art_Class,_Phoenix_Indian_School,_Arizona,_06-1900_-_NARA_-_518923.jpg","alt":"A photograph of seven pupils at easels in a school studio, working on portrait drawings, with plaster casts on a shelf behind them."}},
{"id":"us-092","num":92,"category":"United States","question":"A Paiute called Wovoka had a vision in 1889 that spread across the Plains as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, promising the return of the buffalo and the dead.","questions":["Dancers who joined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fell into trances in which they saw dead relatives living the old life of the years before the reservations.","The army read the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as a rising rather than a religion, and sent troops to the Lakota agencies in November 1890 to suppress it."],"answer":"Ghost Dance","answerText":"Ghost Dance","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Vision</span><span class=\"dt-v\">January 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">On the Plains</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from 1889</span><span class=\"dt-k\">In Oklahoma</span><span class=\"dt-v\">from April 1890 to 1914</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Ghost Dance</b> was a religious movement that began in January 1889, when a Paiute named Wovoka, also called Jack Wilson, had a vision near Walker Lake in western Nevada.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> He prophesied that faithful dancing, clean living, hard work and peace with the whites would hasten the resurrection of dead relatives and the return of the life the Plains peoples had lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was the second such movement, an earlier one having spread from the same region twenty years before, and like the first it sought the revival of ways lost since colonisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> On the Plains it took the form of a four-day round dance in which leaders waved eagle-wing fans before the dancers' faces to induce trance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In trance a dancer was carried to the afterworld and saw departed relatives living the old happy life of the years before the reservations, when the bison were still numerous.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Delegations from Pine Ridge travelled to Nevada in 1889 and 1890 and came back convinced, and Kicking Bear and Short Bull became the movement's most visible Lakota leaders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Agents read the dancing as a rising rather than a religion; by 18 November 1890 the War Department had begun a military occupation of the Lakota reservations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Sitting Bull, who had allowed the dance at Standing Rock, was killed by agency police on 15 December, and about 150 of his followers fled to Big Foot's band.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two weeks later the Seventh Cavalry killed most of that band at Wounded Knee, and the holy man Black Elk, who had danced, rode to the field and saw the dead in the ravine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Elsewhere the dance was left alone and simply continued: in Oklahoma Territory it was still being held in 1914, and it carried old songs, clothing and hand games forward with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"sources":["Gloria A. Young, \"Ghost Dance,\" <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=GH001. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1889: Second Ghost Dance Movement Widespread among Tribes,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/374.html. [Open access]","Richard E. Jensen and Eli Paul, \"Wounded Knee,\" National Register of Historic Places Registration Form (Washington, DC: National Park Service, 1990), https://npgallery.nps.gov/pdfhost/docs/NHLS/Text/66000719.pdf. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Black Elk,\" https://www.nps.gov/people/black-elk.htm. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1890: U.S. Cavalry Massacres Lakota at Wounded Knee,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/377.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Ghost_Dance_at_Pine_Ridge.png","title":"The Ghost Dance at Pine Ridge, 1890","desc":"Frederic Remington drew the round dance at the Pine Ridge Agency from sketches taken on the spot; it was printed in Harper's Weekly a few weeks before the massacre.","credit":"Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghost_Dance_at_Pine_Ridge.png","alt":"A wash drawing of dozens of people in blankets moving in a wide circle on open ground, with tipis in the distance behind them."}},
{"id":"us-093","num":93,"category":"United States","question":"On a December morning in 1890 the Seventh Cavalry surrounded a Lakota camp beside a South Dakota creek, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> followed within half an hour.","questions":["The Medal of Honor went to 28 soldiers for the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, an award Lakota descendants have spent decades asking Congress to revoke.","Four Hotchkiss guns on a hill above the camp opened on tipis holding women and children during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the shooting spreading down a dry ravine."],"answer":"Wounded Knee Massacre","answerText":"Wounded Knee Massacre","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Date</span><span class=\"dt-v\">29 December 1890</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 9.15 a.m.</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lakota dead</span><span class=\"dt-v\">at least 250</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Buried</span><span class=\"dt-v\">3 – 4 January 1891</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Wounded Knee Massacre</b> took place on 29 December 1890 beside Wounded Knee Creek on the Pine Ridge Reservation in South Dakota, and was the bloodiest moment of the army's campaign against the Ghost Dance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Major Samuel Whitside had intercepted the Minneconjou band of Big Foot on 28 December and brought it to camp near the Wounded Knee post office; Colonel James Forsyth arrived that night to take command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the morning of the twenty-ninth about 470 troops surrounded roughly 350 Lakota, of whom some 230 were women and children, and four Hotchkiss rapid-fire guns were trained on the camp from the hill above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The soldiers were disarming the men when a shot was fired, perhaps by accident, and the most intense firing followed within the next thirty minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Much of the killing happened as people fled westward down a dry ravine, where women and children were shot as they ran.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> How many died is not exactly known: the survivor Joseph Horn Cloud compiled a list of 186 Lakota dead, and the landmark study of the site concludes that a total of at least 250 is almost certain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A blizzard closed over the field, and the Dakota physician Charles Eastman reached it three days later with a burial party, finding a handful of survivors still alive in the snow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A mass grave was dug on the hill where the guns had stood, and it was left unmarked until the survivors dedicated a monument there in May 1903.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The Medal of Honor went to 28 soldiers, an award their descendants have campaigned to have withdrawn, and whether soldiers or Lakota fired first has been argued about ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the name is contested, the site's own federal nomination noting that writers call it a massacre or a battle according to the view they take.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":1,"sources":["Richard E. Jensen and Eli Paul, \"Wounded Knee,\" National Register of Historic Places Registration Form (Washington, DC: National Park Service, 1990), https://npgallery.nps.gov/pdfhost/docs/NHLS/Text/66000719.pdf. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1890: U.S. Cavalry Massacres Lakota at Wounded Knee,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/377.html. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1890: Dakota Doctor Witnesses Wounded Knee Aftermath,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/378.html. [Open access]","Michael A. Sievers, \"The Historiography of 'The Bloody Field . . . That Kept the Secret of the Everlasting Word': Wounded Knee,\" <i>South Dakota History</i> 6, no. 1 (1975), https://www.sdhspress.com/journal/south-dakota-history-6-1/the-historiography-of-the-bloody-field-that-kept-the-secret-of-the-everlasting-word-wounded-knee/vol-06-no-1-the-historiography-of-the-bloody-field-that-kept-the-secret-of-the-everlasting-word.pdf. [Open access]","Gloria A. Young, \"Ghost Dance,\" <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=GH001. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Battle_of_Wounded_Knee_14867u.jpg/1920px-Battle_of_Wounded_Knee_14867u.jpg","title":"The mass grave at Wounded Knee, January 1891","desc":"Soldiers and civilian gravediggers at the trench dug on the hill where the guns had stood; the dead of the massacre lie in it. Some readers will find the photograph distressing.","credit":"Trager and Kuhn. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Wounded_Knee_14867u.jpg","alt":"A photograph of a long open trench holding many bodies, with a line of soldiers and workmen standing along its far edge in winter clothing."}},
{"id":"us-094","num":94,"category":"United States","question":"German listeners had broken every Allied cipher by 1918, so an American officer put the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on his field telephones and the messages became unreadable.","questions":["Men who were not citizens of the country they fought for, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> carried orders in a language the War Department had spent forty years punishing children for speaking.","Because their language was neither European nor built on a mathematical progression, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gave the Meuse-Argonne front a code the Germans never broke."],"answer":"Choctaw code talkers","answerText":"Choctaw code talkers","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Served</span><span class=\"dt-v\">autumn 1918</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Unit</span><span class=\"dt-v\">142nd Infantry, 36th Division</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Gold Medal</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2008</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Choctaw code talkers</b> were soldiers of the Choctaw Nation who carried American field-telephone traffic in their own language during the last months of the First World War.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When Congress declared war in April 1917 the members of the Choctaw Nation were not citizens of the United States, and they enlisted anyway, most of them serving in a company of Indian enlistees in the 142nd Infantry of the 36th Division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> By 1918 German forces had deciphered the codes used between Allied commands, were monitoring the radio and telephone lines, and were capturing one runner in four sent between companies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An officer who realised that some of his men spoke a language of their own sought out and recruited Choctaw soldiers to pass messages, and one was placed at each post command.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the language rested on neither a European tongue nor a mathematical progression, the listeners could make nothing of the transmissions, and the Germans never broke it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Words were invented for military things the language had no term for, so that a machine gun became a little gun that shoots fast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The traffic they carried mattered most in the American movements of October 1918, in protecting supplies and gun positions from shelling, and in preparing the last assaults of the autumn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Accounts of how many took part differ: Congress recorded eighteen men recruited, the Oklahoma Military Hall of Fame names nineteen, and the fullest published list gives eight originals joined later by eleven more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Among them was Joseph Oklahombi, of Bokchito, who was decorated by France and became the most celebrated Choctaw soldier of the war.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The high rate of enlistment by men who could not vote helped move Congress to pass the Indian Citizenship Act six years later, and the code talkers themselves were awarded the Congressional Gold Medal in 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"sources":["William C. Meadows, \"Code Talkers,\" <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=CO013. [Open access]","U.S. Senate, <i>Code Talkers Recognition Act of 2003</i>, S. 540, 108th Cong., 1st sess., introduced 6 March 2003, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/BILLS-108s540is/html/BILLS-108s540is.htm. [Open access]","Oklahoma Historical Society, \"Choctaw Code Talkers,\" Oklahoma Military Hall of Fame, https://www.okhistory.org/historycenter/militaryhof/inductee.php?id=19. [Open access]","Corie Delashaw, \"Oklahombi, Joseph,\" <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=OK091. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1924: American Indians Granted U.S. Citizenship,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/431.html. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Choctaw_Nation_Congressional_Gold_Medal_%28reverse%29.jpg","title":"The Choctaw Nation's Congressional Gold Medal","desc":"The reverse of the medal struck under the Code Talkers Recognition Act, showing a bow and arrows crossing a signal fire and naming both world wars.","credit":"Unites States Mint. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Choctaw_Nation_Congressional_Gold_Medal_%28reverse%29.jpg","alt":"The reverse of a gold medal showing a bow, four arrows and a smoking signal fire, lettered \"Act of Congress 2008\", \"World War I\" and \"World War II\"."}},
{"id":"us-095","num":95,"category":"United States","question":"Calvin Coolidge signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in June 1924, making citizens of about 125,000 people who had been born in the country and were still not citizens of it.","questions":["Because voting was left to the states, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not enfranchise anyone, and Arizona, New Mexico and South Dakota went on refusing the ballot for decades.","Tribal membership and tribal property survived the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> untouched, which left officials arguing for years about which rights its new citizens actually held."],"answer":"Indian Citizenship Act","answerText":"Indian Citizenship Act","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 June 1924</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">43 Stat. 253</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Made citizens of</span><span class=\"dt-v\">about 125,000 people</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Indian Citizenship Act</b> of 2 June 1924, also called the Snyder Act, made United States citizens of the roughly 125,000 Native people who were still not citizens, and authorised the Secretary of the Interior to issue them certificates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Citizenship had been arriving piecemeal for half a century, through treaties, through allotment under the Dawes Act, through military service, and through statutes covering particular nations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In Alaska the price had been explicit: an act of 1915 offered citizenship to Alaska Natives who abandoned their tribal customs and produced white witnesses to swear they had done so.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The immediate spur was the First World War, in which Native men had enlisted in large numbers although they were not citizens of the country they served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Congress made the grant unilaterally, without asking the nations concerned, and some of them regarded a citizenship they had not sought as an intrusion on their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The act changed less than its name suggests, because the right to vote is settled by the states rather than by Congress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Arizona, New Mexico and South Dakota went on excluding Native voters, and litigation over the question ran into the second half of the century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also left tribal membership and tribal property untouched, which produced a lasting confusion: later officials read the two statuses as alternatives and concluded that citizens of a tribe could not be full citizens of the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The settled position is the opposite one, that Native people are citizens of the United States, of the states they live in, and of their own nations at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rule now sits in the nationality code, which makes a citizen at birth of every person born in the United States to a member of an Indian, Eskimo, Aleutian or other aboriginal tribe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"sources":["National Archives and Records Administration, \"Indian Citizenship Act,\" DocsTeach, https://www.docsteach.org/documents/document/indian-citizenship-act. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1924: American Indians Granted U.S. Citizenship,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/431.html. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1915: Alaska Natives Must Renounce Cultures to Become Citizens,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/418.html. [Open access]","Legal Information Institute, \"8 U.S. Code sec. 1401 - Nationals and citizens of United States at birth,\" Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/8/1401. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Justice, Office of Tribal Justice, \"Frequently Asked Questions about Native Americans,\" https://www.justice.gov/otj/about-native-americans. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/1924_Indian_Citizenship_Act.jpg/1920px-1924_Indian_Citizenship_Act.jpg","title":"Coolidge with an Osage delegation, 1925","desc":"The president who signed the act, photographed on the White House lawn with four men of the Osage Nation the year after it passed.","credit":"National Photo Company Collection. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1924_Indian_Citizenship_Act.jpg","alt":"A photograph of five men standing on a lawn: four Osage men in beaded and painted regalia and, among them, a man in a dark suit holding a hat."}},
{"id":"us-096","num":96,"category":"United States","question":"The first section of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a single sentence: after 18 June 1934, no land of any Indian reservation shall be allotted in severalty.","questions":["Tribes that adopted a written constitution under the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> could hire lawyers and hold their land in common again, and about a third of them voted it down.","Franklin Roosevelt signed the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, sometimes called the Wheeler-Howard Act, ending nearly fifty years of breaking reservations into individual holdings."],"answer":"Indian Reorganization Act","answerText":"Indian Reorganization Act","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Signed</span><span class=\"dt-v\">18 June 1934</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Statute</span><span class=\"dt-v\">48 Stat. 984</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Also called</span><span class=\"dt-v\">Wheeler-Howard Act, 1934</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Indian Reorganization Act</b>, signed by Franklin Roosevelt on 18 June 1934 and often called the Wheeler-Howard Act, reversed the allotment policy that had governed Indian affairs since 1887.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its first section says the whole of that reversal in one sentence: on and after that date, no land of any reservation shall be allotted in severalty to any Indian.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The argument for it had been assembled six years earlier by the Meriam survey, which found the reservations impoverished, the schools inadequate and the trust lands passing steadily out of Native hands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The commissioner who drove it through, John Collier, also instructed his agents that year to stop suppressing Native religion, ending a policy of forced conversion that had run for decades.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The act offered federal money and legal standing to any tribe that would adopt a written constitution and bylaws, ratified by a majority at an election called by the Secretary of the Interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A tribe that organised in this way gained the right to employ its own legal counsel, to prevent the sale of its land without consent, and to negotiate with federal, state and local governments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Roughly a third of the nations that voted rejected the act outright, and some that accepted it did so narrowly or under pressure from agency staff.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The constitutions themselves were criticised at the time and since for imposing a council model borrowed from American municipal government, without the separation of powers the United States had built into its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Elders quoted in the congressional hearings questioned the format directly, and in several nations the new councils sat uneasily beside older forms of authority that had never stopped functioning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What the act unquestionably did was stop the loss of land, and the tribal governments it created are the ones most federally recognised nations still use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"sources":["Legal Information Institute, \"25 U.S. Code sec. 5101 - Allotment of land on Indian reservations,\" Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/25/5101. [Open access]","Legal Information Institute, \"25 U.S. Code sec. 5123 - Organization of Indian tribes; constitution and bylaws and amendment thereof,\" Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/25/5123. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1934: President Franklin Roosevelt Signs the Indian Reorganization Act,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/452.html. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1934: Commissioner Calls for Religious Freedom for American Indians,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/453.html. [Open access]","Lewis Meriam et al., \"General Summary of Findings and Recommendations,\" ch. 1 of <i>The Problem of Indian Administration</i> (Baltimore: Johns Hopkins Press, 1928), https://narf.org/nill/documents/merriam/d_meriam_chapter1_summary_of_findings.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/U.S._Indian_commissioner_appears_before_Senate_Indian_Affairs_Committee._Washington%2C_D.C.%2C_June_10._Indian_Commissioner_John_Collier_charged_before_the_Senate_Indian_Affairs_Committee_today_LCCN2016877727.jpg/1920px-thumbnail.jpg","title":"John Collier before the Senate Indian Affairs Committee","desc":"The Commissioner of Indian Affairs who drafted the act and pushed it through Congress, testifying at a committee table.","credit":"Harris & Ewing, photographer. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._Indian_commissioner_appears_before_Senate_Indian_Affairs_Committee._Washington,_D.C.,_June_10._Indian_Commissioner_John_Collier_charged_before_the_Senate_Indian_Affairs_Committee_today_LCCN2016877727.jpg","alt":"A photograph of a bespectacled man in a light suit speaking across a committee table strewn with papers, with others seated on either side."}},
{"id":"us-097","num":97,"category":"United States","question":"A 1953 resolution declared that tribes should be freed from federal supervision, and the policy of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that followed ended about 60 of them.","questions":["Under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a tribal roll was closed, the land was sold or divided, the trust relationship ended, and the nation ceased to exist in federal law.","Richard Nixon repudiated <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in 1970, by which time about three million acres of tribal land had been relinquished nationwide."],"answer":"termination","answerText":"termination","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Policy</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1953 – 1970</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Resolution</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1 August 1953</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Land lost</span><span class=\"dt-v\">over 3 million acres</span></div>","abstract":"<b>Termination</b> was the federal policy, pursued from 1953 until about 1970, of ending the legal existence of Indian nations and the trust relationship between them and the United States.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was declared in House Concurrent Resolution 108, adopted on 1 August 1953, which stated the sense of Congress that Indians should be made subject to the same laws as other citizens and their status as wards of the United States should end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The resolution named the tribes to go first: all those in California, Florida, New York and Texas, together with the Flathead, the Klamath, the Menominee, the Potawatomi of Kansas and Nebraska, and the Turtle Mountain Chippewa.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It also directed that Bureau of Indian Affairs offices serving them be abolished, and that the Secretary of the Interior report on the legislation and treaties that would have to be undone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In practice a termination meant closing the tribal roll, liquidating and distributing tribal assets as a single payment to the members on it, and ending the protected status of all land the tribe held.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Congress began about 60 separate terminations, and more than three million acres of tribal land passed out of trust as a result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A companion programme of relocation moved families from reservations to Chicago, Denver, Los Angeles and other cities; some prospered, and many met unemployment, discrimination and the loss of everything their community had supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Klamath of Oregon were terminated in 1954 against the advice of the Bureau of Indian Affairs and over their own representatives' opposition, and 1.8 million acres of reservation were taken by condemnation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many members did not understand what they had agreed to, and several nations later said they had been misinformed about what the statutes meant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Richard Nixon repudiated the policy in 1970, Congress restored the Menominee in 1973 and the Klamath in 1986, and the Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act of 1975 put the opposite principle into law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","difficulty":4,"sources":["U.S. Congress, <i>House Concurrent Resolution 108</i>, 83rd Cong., 1st sess., 1 August 1953, 67 Stat. B132, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-67/pdf/STATUTE-67-PgB132-2.pdf. [Open access]","E. Jethro Gaede, \"Termination and Relocation Programs,\" <i>The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture</i>, https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=TE014. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1953: Congress Seeks to Abolish Tribes, Relocate American Indians,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/488.html. [Open access]","The Klamath Tribes, \"History,\" https://klamathtribes.org/history/. [Open access]","Legal Information Institute, \"25 U.S. Code sec. 5301 - Congressional statement of findings,\" Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/25/5301. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e7/Arthur_V._Watkins%2C_1950.jpg","title":"Senator Arthur V. Watkins, 1950","desc":"The Utah senator who introduced House Concurrent Resolution 108 and drove the termination programme through Congress in the 1950s.","credit":"Harris & Ewing. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_V._Watkins,_1950.jpg","alt":"A studio portrait of an elderly man with white hair and rimless glasses, in a dark suit and tie."}},
{"id":"us-098","num":98,"category":"United States","question":"Two hundred people met in Minneapolis in July 1968 and founded the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, first to patrol the streets against police abuse and then to press treaty claims.","questions":["The <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> occupied the Bureau of Indian Affairs building in 1972, and the FBI answered by making the organisation a target of its COINTELPRO operations.","Founded to help Native people the relocation programme had moved into cities, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grew into a campaign for the full restoration of tribal sovereignty."],"answer":"American Indian Movement","answerText":"American Indian Movement","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Founded</span><span class=\"dt-v\">28 July 1968</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Minneapolis, Minnesota</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Trail of Broken Treaties</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1972</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Wounded Knee</span><span class=\"dt-v\">1973</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>American Indian Movement</b>, usually abbreviated AIM, was founded in Minneapolis on 28 July 1968, when about two hundred Native people answered a call from a group of community activists.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its first constituency was urban: the families that federal relocation had moved from reservations into cities in the previous fifteen years, and who met poverty, discrimination and the police there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> To help the victims of police abuse the founders formed the AIM Patrol, and they went on to establish a legal rights centre offering free representation and a health board providing Native-centred medical care.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In 1972 they founded the Heart of the Earth Survival School, one of the first schools run by Native people for Native children since the boarding-school era.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aims widened as the movement grew, from city services to the full restoration of tribal sovereignty and the treaty rights the United States had signed and set aside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> AIM emerged among a wider wave of action that included the occupation of Alcatraz Island from 1969 to 1971 by activists claiming it under an old treaty provision.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the autumn of 1972 the Trail of Broken Treaties crossed the country from the West Coast to Washington, where its marchers occupied the Bureau of Indian Affairs building for nearly a week.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That action made the organisation a target of COINTELPRO, the FBI programme for disrupting domestic political groups, and the surveillance and prosecutions that followed lasted years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In February 1973 AIM joined traditional Lakota at Wounded Knee, where a siege of 71 days brought the movement worldwide attention and the deaths of two Native men.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Leaders including Dennis Banks, Clyde Bellecourt, Russell Means and the Winnebago chairman Reuben Snake became widely known, though many of those who carried the work were women whose names were not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"sources":["Jon Lurie, \"American Indian Movement (AIM),\" MNopedia, Minnesota Historical Society, https://www.mnhs.org/mnopedia/search/index/group/american-indian-movement-aim. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1972: Reuben A. Snake Jr. Leads American Indian Movement,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/532.html. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1973: American Indian Movement Occupies Wounded Knee,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/535.html. [Open access]","National Park Service, \"Alcatraz Occupation,\" Golden Gate National Recreation Area, https://www.nps.gov/goga/learn/historyculture/alcatraz-occupation.htm. [Open access]","U.S. Department of Justice, Community Relations Service, \"Peaceful End to the Siege at Wounded Knee,\" https://www.justice.gov/crs/timeline-event/peaceful-end-siege-wounded-knee. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Longest_Walk_at_Washington%2C_1978.jpg/1920px-Longest_Walk_at_Washington%2C_1978.jpg","title":"An AIM camp on the Mall, 1978","desc":"A banner and tipis pitched beside the Washington Monument at the end of the Longest Walk, a cross-country march for treaty rights.","credit":"Warren K. Leffler. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Longest_Walk_at_Washington,_1978.jpg","alt":"A photograph of a tipi and a hanging banner bearing the American Indian Movement emblem, with the Washington Monument rising behind them."}},
{"id":"us-099","num":99,"category":"United States","question":"Activists seized the trading post and church at Pine Ridge in February 1973, and the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> held out for 71 days against federal marshals.","questions":["The people who held the village during the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> declared the Independent Oglala Nation and demanded that the United States honour the Fort Laramie treaty of 1868.","Television carried the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> nightly for ten weeks, and journalists have argued ever since about how far the coverage shaped what happened there."],"answer":"Wounded Knee Occupation","answerText":"Wounded Knee Occupation","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Began</span><span class=\"dt-v\">27 February 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Ended</span><span class=\"dt-v\">8 May 1973</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Lasted</span><span class=\"dt-v\">71 days</span><span class=\"dt-k\">Died</span><span class=\"dt-v\">2 people</span></div>","abstract":"The <b>Wounded Knee Occupation</b> began on 27 February 1973, when roughly 250 supporters of the American Indian Movement took over the village of Wounded Knee on the Pine Ridge Reservation in South Dakota.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The dispute that started it was internal: the Oglala Sioux Civil Rights Organization was trying to impeach the tribal chairman Richard Wilson, whom many members regarded as corrupt and as protected by the Bureau of Indian Affairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Traditional families on the reservation said they were being intimidated by Wilson's supporters, and they asked AIM to come.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The occupiers chose the site deliberately, the mass grave of the 1890 massacre lying on the hill above them, and declared the Independent Oglala Nation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They demanded that the Senate hold hearings on the treaties, that the Fort Laramie treaty of 1868 be honoured, and that the Bureau of Indian Affairs be investigated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Federal marshals, the FBI and Bureau police surrounded the village with armoured vehicles and automatic weapons, and exchanges of fire continued for weeks.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The Justice Department's Community Relations Service mediated between the two sides throughout and kept negotiations open when a peaceful end looked impossible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The siege ended on 8 May, after 71 days, when those inside handed over their weapons and submitted to arrest; two Native men, Frank Clearwater and Buddy Lamont, had been killed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of the village burned during the standoff, including the Catholic church and the store, and their ruins are recorded as such in the site's federal landmark file.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> News organisations stayed for the whole of it, and how far the reporting shaped the event has been argued over by historians ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","difficulty":3,"sources":["U.S. Department of Justice, Community Relations Service, \"Peaceful End to the Siege at Wounded Knee,\" https://www.justice.gov/crs/timeline-event/peaceful-end-siege-wounded-knee. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1973: American Indian Movement Occupies Wounded Knee,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/535.html. [Open access]","Jon Lurie, \"American Indian Movement (AIM),\" MNopedia, Minnesota Historical Society, https://www.mnhs.org/mnopedia/search/index/group/american-indian-movement-aim. [Open access]","Rolland Dewing, \"South Dakota Newspaper Coverage of the 1973 Occupation of Wounded Knee,\" <i>South Dakota History</i> 12, no. 1 (1982), https://www.sdhspress.com/journal/south-dakota-history-12-1/south-dakota-newspaper-coverage-of-the-1973-occupation-of-wounded-knee/vol-12-no-1-south-dakota-newspaper-coverage-of-the-1973-occupation-of-wounded-knee.pdf. [Open access]","Richard E. Jensen and Eli Paul, \"Wounded Knee,\" National Register of Historic Places Registration Form (Washington, DC: National Park Service, 1990), https://npgallery.nps.gov/pdfhost/docs/NHLS/Text/66000719.pdf. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Dennis_Banks_speaks_at_Wounded_Knee_church.jpg/1920px-Dennis_Banks_speaks_at_Wounded_Knee_church.jpg","title":"Inside the church at Wounded Knee, 1973","desc":"A meeting during the occupation in the church the occupiers held, one of the buildings that burned before the siege ended.","credit":"Walter Fisk. Public domain. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dennis_Banks_speaks_at_Wounded_Knee_church.jpg","alt":"A grainy newspaper photograph of a crowded room, people standing and seated around a table, one of them wearing a feathered headdress."}},
{"id":"us-100","num":100,"category":"United States","question":"Federal law treats each recognised nation as a government rather than a racial group, and that standing is called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in modern American law.","questions":["Because <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> predates the Constitution rather than being granted by it, a nation retains every power it has never given up by treaty or had taken by Congress.","The Self-Determination Act of 1975 let nations contract to run their own schools, clinics and police, giving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> practical effect after two centuries of federal administration."],"answer":"tribal sovereignty","answerText":"tribal sovereignty","traditional":"","hanzi":"","pinyin":"","translations":"","citation":"","answerDate":"<div class=\"dt\"><span class=\"dt-k\">Self-determination</span><span class=\"dt-v\">4 January 1975</span><span class=\"dt-v dt-sub\">Public Law 93-638</span></div>","undatable":true,"abstract":"<b>Tribal sovereignty</b> is the authority that federally recognised Indian nations exercise over their own members and territory, and in American law it is inherent rather than granted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The doctrine was set out by the Supreme Court in 1832, when it described the Cherokee Nation as a distinct community with its own boundaries in which the laws of Georgia could have no force.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> From that follows the working rule: a nation keeps every power it has not surrendered by treaty and Congress has not expressly taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relationship it produces is political rather than racial, and the services and protections the United States provides flow from membership of a recognised nation rather than from ancestry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A member is therefore a citizen three times over, of the United States, of the state they live in, and of their own nation, and pays federal income tax like anyone else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> There are now well over five hundred federally recognised nations, each with its own criteria for membership, its own government and, in most cases, its own courts and police.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recognition is what makes the trust relationship operate, and a nation that lacks it, whether never acknowledged or terminated in the 1950s, holds no such standing in federal law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The policy that gave the doctrine practical effect is self-determination, announced by the Nixon administration in 1970 as a repudiation of termination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The Indian Self-Determination and Education Assistance Act of 1975 required federal agencies to contract with nations to run the schools, clinics, housing and other services that Washington had run for them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Congress opened that statute by conceding that its own domination of Indian services had denied Native people an effective voice in their own affairs, and by pledging to reverse it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","difficulty":2,"sources":["U.S. Department of Justice, Office of Tribal Justice, \"Frequently Asked Questions about Native Americans,\" https://www.justice.gov/otj/about-native-americans. [Open access]","U.S. Bureau of Indian Affairs, \"Frequently Asked Questions,\" https://www.bia.gov/frequently-asked-questions. [Open access]","Legal Information Institute, \"25 U.S. Code sec. 5301 - Congressional statement of findings,\" Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/25/5301. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, \"1975: Indian Self-Determination Becomes the Law of the Land,\" <i>Native Voices: Native Peoples' Concepts of Health and Illness</i>, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/nativevoices/timeline/539.html. [Open access]","Legal Information Institute, \"Worcester v. Georgia, 31 U.S. 515 (1832),\" Cornell Law School, https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/31/515. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Navajo_Nation_Council_Chamber%2C_January_2019.jpg/1920px-Navajo_Nation_Council_Chamber%2C_January_2019.jpg","title":"The Navajo Nation Council Chamber, Window Rock","desc":"The legislature of the largest federally recognised nation in the United States meets in this sandstone building in Arizona.","credit":"Steven Baltakatei Sandoval. CC BY-SA 4.0. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navajo_Nation_Council_Chamber,_January_2019.jpg","alt":"A photograph of a low sandstone building with projecting log beams and a carved wooden door, lettered \"Navajo Nation Council Chamber\"."}}
];

